if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-155"]="I address these lines—written in India—to my relatives in England.\n\nMy object is to explain the motive which has induced me to refuse the right hand of friendship to my cousin, John Herncastle. The reserve which I have hitherto maintained in this matter has been misinterpreted by members of my family whose good opinion I cannot consent to forfeit. I request them to suspend their decision until they have read my narrative. And I declare, on my word of honour, that what I am now about to write is, strictly and literally, the truth.\n\nThe private difference between my cousin and me took its rise in a great public event in which we were both concerned—the storming of Seringapatam, under General Baird, on the 4th of May, 1799.\n\nIn order that the circumstances may be clearly understood, I must revert for a moment to the period before the assault, and to the stories current in our camp of the treasure in jewels and gold stored up in the Palace of Seringapatam.\n\n# II\n\nOne of the wildest of these stories related to a Yellow Diamond—a famous gem in the native annals of India.\n\nThe earliest known traditions describe the stone as having been set in the forehead of the four-handed Indian god who typifies the Moon. Partly from its peculiar colour, partly from a superstition which represented it as feeling the influence of the deity whom it adorned, and growing and lessening in lustre with the waxing and waning of the moon, it first gained the name by which it continues to be known in India to this day—the name of THE MOONSTONE. A similar superstition was once prevalent, as I have heard, in ancient Greece and Rome; not applying, however (as in India), to a diamond devoted to the service of a god, but to a semi-transparent stone of the inferior order of gems, supposed to be affected by the lunar influences—the moon, in this latter case also, giving the name by which the stone is still known to collectors in our own time.\n\nThe adventures of the Yellow Diamond begin with the eleventh century of the Christian era.\n\nAt that date, the Mohammedan conqueror, Mahmoud of Ghizni, crossed India; seized on the holy city of Somnauth; and stripped of its treasures the famous temple, which had stood for centuries—the shrine of Hindoo pilgrimage, and the wonder of the Eastern world.\n\nOf all the deities worshipped in the temple, the moon-god alone escaped the rapacity of the conquering Mohammedans. Preserved by three Brahmins, the inviolate deity, bearing the Yellow Diamond in its forehead, was removed by night, and was transported to the second of the sacred cities of India—the city of Benares.\n\nHere, in a new shrine—in a hall inlaid with precious stones, under a roof supported by pillars of gold—the moon-god was set up and worshipped. Here, on the night when the shrine was completed, Vishnu the Preserver appeared to the three Brahmins in a dream.\n\nThe deity breathed the breath of his divinity on the Diamond in the forehead of the god. And the Brahmins knelt and hid their faces in their robes. The deity commanded that the Moonstone should be watched, from that time forth, by three priests in turn, night and day, to the end of the generations of men. And the Brahmins heard, and bowed before his will. The deity predicted certain disaster to the presumptuous mortal who laid hands on the sacred gem, and to all of his house and name who received it after him. And the Brahmins caused the prophecy to be written over the gates of the shrine in letters of gold.\n\nOne age followed another—and still, generation after generation, the successors of the three Brahmins watched their priceless Moonstone, night and day. One age followed another until the first years of the eighteenth Christian century saw the reign of Aurungzebe, Emperor of the Moguls. At his command havoc and rapine were let loose once more among the temples of the worship of Brahmah. The shrine of the four-handed god was polluted by the slaughter of sacred animals; the images of the deities were broken in pieces; and the Moonstone was seized by an officer of rank in the army of Aurungzebe.\n\nPowerless to recover their lost treasure by open force, the three guardian priests followed and watched it in disguise. The generations succeeded each other; the warrior who had committed the sacrilege perished miserably; the Moonstone passed (carrying its curse with it) from one lawless Mohammedan hand to another; and still, through all chances and changes, the successors of the three guardian priests kept their watch, waiting the day when the will of Vishnu the Preserver should restore to them their sacred gem. Time rolled on from the first to the last years of the eighteenth Christian century. The Diamond fell into the possession of Tippoo, Sultan of Seringapatam, who caused it to be placed as an ornament in the handle of a dagger, and who commanded it to be kept among the choicest treasures of his armoury. Even then—in the palace of the Sultan himself—the three guardian priests still kept their watch in secret. There were three officers of Tippoo’s household, strangers to the rest, who had won their master’s confidence by conforming, or appearing to conform, to the Mussulman faith; and to those three men report pointed as the three priests in disguise.\n\n# III\n\nSo, as told in our camp, ran the fanciful story of the Moonstone. It made no serious impression on any of us except my cousin—whose love of the marvellous induced him to believe it. On the night before the assault on Seringapatam, he was absurdly angry with me, and with others, for treating the whole thing as a fable. A foolish wrangle followed; and Herncastle’s unlucky temper got the better of him. He declared, in his boastful way, that we should see the Diamond on his finger, if the English army took Seringapatam. The sally was saluted by a roar of laughter, and there, as we all thought that night, the thing ended.\n\nLet me now take you on to the day of the assault.\n\nMy cousin and I were separated at the outset. I never saw him when we forded the river; when we planted the English flag in the first breach; when we crossed the ditch beyond; and, fighting every inch of our way, entered the town. It was only at dusk, when the place was ours, and after General Baird himself had found the dead body of Tippoo under a heap of the slain, that Herncastle and I met.\n\nWe were each attached to a party sent out by the general’s orders to prevent the plunder and confusion which followed our conquest. The camp-followers committed deplorable excesses; and, worse still, the soldiers found their way, by an unguarded door, into the treasury of the Palace, and loaded themselves with gold and jewels. It was in the court outside the treasury that my cousin and I met, to enforce the laws of discipline on our own soldiers. Herncastle’s fiery temper had been, as I could plainly see, exasperated to a kind of frenzy by the terrible slaughter through which we had passed. He was very unfit, in my opinion, to perform the duty that had been entrusted to him.\n\nThere was riot and confusion enough in the treasury, but no violence that I saw. The men (if I may use such an expression) disgraced themselves good-humouredly. All sorts of rough jests and catchwords were bandied about among them; and the story of the Diamond turned up again unexpectedly, in the form of a mischievous joke. “Who’s got the Moonstone?” was the rallying cry which perpetually caused the plundering, as soon as it was stopped in one place, to break out in another. While I was still vainly trying to establish order, I heard a frightful yelling on the other side of the courtyard, and at once ran towards the cries, in dread of finding some new outbreak of the pillage in that direction.\n\nI got to an open door, and saw the bodies of two Indians (by their dress, as I guessed, officers of the palace) lying across the entrance, dead.\n\nA cry inside hurried me into a room, which appeared to serve as an armoury. A third Indian, mortally wounded, was sinking at the feet of a man whose back was towards me. The man turned at the instant when I came in, and I saw John Herncastle, with a torch in one hand, and a dagger dripping with blood in the other. A stone, set like a pommel, in the end of the dagger’s handle, flashed in the torchlight, as he turned on me, like a gleam of fire. The dying Indian sank to his knees, pointed to the dagger in Herncastle’s hand, and said, in his native language—“The Moonstone will have its vengeance yet on you and yours!” He spoke those words, and fell dead on the floor.\n\nBefore I could stir in the matter, the men who had followed me across the courtyard crowded in. My cousin rushed to meet them, like a madman. “Clear the room!” he shouted to me, “and set a guard on the door!” The men fell back as he threw himself on them with his torch and his dagger. I put two sentinels of my own company, on whom I could rely, to keep the door. Through the remainder of the night, I saw no more of my cousin.\n\nEarly in the morning, the plunder still going on, General Baird announced publicly by beat of drum, that any thief detected in the fact, be he whom he might, should be hung. The provost-marshal was in attendance, to prove that the General was in earnest; and in the throng that followed the proclamation, Herncastle and I met again.\n\nHe held out his hand, as usual, and said, “Good morning.”\n\nI waited before I gave him my hand in return.\n\n“Tell me first,” I said, “how the Indian in the armoury met his death, and what those last words meant, when he pointed to the dagger in your hand.”\n\n“The Indian met his death, as I suppose, by a mortal wound,” said Herncastle. “What his last words meant I know no more than you do.”\n\nI looked at him narrowly. His frenzy of the previous day had all calmed down. I determined to give him another chance.\n\n“Is that all you have to tell me?” I asked.\n\nHe answered, “That is all.”\n\nI turned my back on him; and we have not spoken since.\n\n# IV\n\nI beg it to be understood that what I write here about my cousin (unless some necessity should arise for making it public) is for the information of the family only. Herncastle has said nothing that can justify me in speaking to our commanding officer. He has been taunted more than once about the Diamond, by those who recollect his angry outbreak before the assault; but, as may easily be imagined, his own remembrance of the circumstances under which I surprised him in the armoury has been enough to keep him silent. It is reported that he means to exchange into another regiment, avowedly for the purpose of separating himself from me.\n\nWhether this be true or not, I cannot prevail upon myself to become his accuser—and I think with good reason. If I made the matter public, I have no evidence but moral evidence to bring forward. I have not only no proof that he killed the two men at the door; I cannot even declare that he killed the third man inside—for I cannot say that my own eyes saw the deed committed. It is true that I heard the dying Indian’s words; but if those words were pronounced to be the ravings of delirium, how could I contradict the assertion from my own knowledge? Let our relatives, on either side, form their own opinion on what I have written, and decide for themselves whether the aversion I now feel towards this man is well or ill founded.\n\nAlthough I attach no sort of credit to the fantastic Indian legend of the gem, I must acknowledge, before I conclude, that I am influenced by a certain superstition of my own in this matter. It is my conviction, or my delusion, no matter which, that crime brings its own fatality with it. I am not only persuaded of Herncastle’s guilt; I am even fanciful enough to believe that he will live to regret it, if he keeps the Diamond; and that others will live to regret taking it from him, if he gives the Diamond away.\n\nThe Events related by Gabriel Betteredge, house-steward in the service of Julia, Lady Verinder.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nIn the first part of Robinson Crusoe, at page one hundred and twenty-nine, you will find it thus written:\n\n“Now I saw, though too late, the Folly of beginning a Work before we count the Cost, and before we judge rightly of our own Strength to go through with it.”\n\nOnly yesterday, I opened my Robinson Crusoe at that place. Only this morning (May twenty-first, eighteen hundred and fifty), came my lady’s nephew, Mr. Franklin Blake, and held a short conversation with me, as follows:—\n\n“Betteredge,” says Mr. Franklin, “I have been to the lawyer’s about some family matters; and, among other things, we have been talking of the loss of the Indian Diamond, in my aunt’s house in Yorkshire, two years since. Mr. Bruff thinks as I think, that the whole story ought, in the interests of truth, to be placed on record in writing—and the sooner the better.”\n\nNot perceiving his drift yet, and thinking it always desirable for the sake of peace and quietness to be on the lawyer’s side, I said I thought so too. Mr. Franklin went on.\n\n“In this matter of the Diamond,” he said, “the characters of innocent people have suffered under suspicion already—as you know. The memories of innocent people may suffer, hereafter, for want of a record of the facts to which those who come after us can appeal. There can be no doubt that this strange family story of ours ought to be told. And I think, Betteredge, Mr. Bruff and I together have hit on the right way of telling it.”\n\nVery satisfactory to both of them, no doubt. But I failed to see what I myself had to do with it, so far.\n\n“We have certain events to relate,” Mr. Franklin proceeded; “and we have certain persons concerned in those events who are capable of relating them. Starting from these plain facts, the idea is that we should all write the story of the Moonstone in turn—as far as our own personal experience extends, and no farther. We must begin by showing how the Diamond first fell into the hands of my uncle Herncastle, when he was serving in India fifty years since. This prefatory narrative I have already got by me in the form of an old family paper, which relates the necessary particulars on the authority of an eye-witness. The next thing to do is to tell how the Diamond found its way into my aunt’s house in Yorkshire, two years ago, and how it came to be lost in little more than twelve hours afterwards. Nobody knows as much as you do, Betteredge, about what went on in the house at that time. So you must take the pen in hand, and start the story.”\n\nIn those terms I was informed of what my personal concern was with the matter of the Diamond. If you are curious to know what course I took under the circumstances, I beg to inform you that I did what you would probably have done in my place. I modestly declared myself to be quite unequal to the task imposed upon me—and I privately felt, all the time, that I was quite clever enough to perform it, if I only gave my own abilities a fair chance. Mr. Franklin, I imagine, must have seen my private sentiments in my face. He declined to believe in my modesty; and he insisted on giving my abilities a fair chance.\n\nTwo hours have passed since Mr. Franklin left me. As soon as his back was turned, I went to my writing-desk to start the story. There I have sat helpless (in spite of my abilities) ever since; seeing what Robinson Crusoe saw, as quoted above—namely, the folly of beginning a work before we count the cost, and before we judge rightly of our own strength to go through with it. Please to remember, I opened the book by accident, at that bit, only the day before I rashly undertook the business now in hand; and, allow me to ask—if that isn’t prophecy, what is?\n\nI am not superstitious; I have read a heap of books in my time; I am a scholar in my own way. Though turned seventy, I possess an active memory, and legs to correspond. You are not to take it, if you please, as the saying of an ignorant man, when I express my opinion that such a book as Robinson Crusoe never was written, and never will be written again. I have tried that book for years—generally in combination with a pipe of tobacco—and I have found it my friend in need in all the necessities of this mortal life. When my spirits are bad—Robinson Crusoe. When I want advice—Robinson Crusoe. In past times when my wife plagued me; in present times when I have had a drop too much—Robinson Crusoe. I have worn out six stout Robinson Crusoes with hard work in my service. On my lady’s last birthday she gave me a seventh. I took a drop too much on the strength of it; and Robinson Crusoe put me right again. Price four shillings and sixpence, bound in blue, with a picture into the bargain.\n\nStill, this don’t look much like starting the story of the Diamond—does it? I seem to be wandering off in search of Lord knows what, Lord knows where. We will take a new sheet of paper, if you please, and begin over again, with my best respects to you.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nI spoke of my lady a line or two back. Now the Diamond could never have been in our house, where it was lost, if it had not been made a present of to my lady’s daughter; and my lady’s daughter would never have been in existence to have the present, if it had not been for my lady who (with pain and travail) produced her into the world. Consequently, if we begin with my lady, we are pretty sure of beginning far enough back. And that, let me tell you, when you have got such a job as mine in hand, is a real comfort at starting.\n\nIf you know anything of the fashionable world, you have heard tell of the three beautiful Miss Herncastles. Miss Adelaide; Miss Caroline; and Miss Julia—this last being the youngest and the best of the three sisters, in my opinion; and I had opportunities of judging, as you shall presently see. I went into the service of the old lord, their father (thank God, we have got nothing to do with him, in this business of the Diamond; he had the longest tongue and the shortest temper of any man, high or low, I ever met with)—I say, I went into the service of the old lord, as page-boy in waiting on the three honourable young ladies, at the age of fifteen years. There I lived till Miss Julia married the late Sir John Verinder. An excellent man, who only wanted somebody to manage him; and, between ourselves, he found somebody to do it; and what is more, he throve on it and grew fat on it, and lived happy and died easy on it, dating from the day when my lady took him to church to be married, to the day when she relieved him of his last breath, and closed his eyes for ever.\n\nI have omitted to state that I went with the bride to the bride’s husband’s house and lands down here. “Sir John,” she says, “I can’t do without Gabriel Betteredge.” “My lady,” says Sir John, “I can’t do without him, either.” That was his way with her—and that was how I went into his service. It was all one to me where I went, so long as my mistress and I were together.\n\nSeeing that my lady took an interest in the out-of-door work, and the farms, and such like, I took an interest in them too—with all the more reason that I was a small farmer’s seventh son myself. My lady got me put under the bailiff, and I did my best, and gave satisfaction, and got promotion accordingly. Some years later, on the Monday as it might be, my lady says, “Sir John, your bailiff is a stupid old man. Pension him liberally, and let Gabriel Betteredge have his place.” On the Tuesday as it might be, Sir John says, “My lady, the bailiff is pensioned liberally; and Gabriel Betteredge has got his place.” You hear more than enough of married people living together miserably. Here is an example to the contrary. Let it be a warning to some of you, and an encouragement to others. In the meantime, I will go on with my story.\n\nWell, there I was in clover, you will say. Placed in a position of trust and honour, with a little cottage of my own to live in, with my rounds on the estate to occupy me in the morning, and my accounts in the afternoon, and my pipe and my Robinson Crusoe in the evening—what more could I possibly want to make me happy? Remember what Adam wanted when he was alone in the Garden of Eden; and if you don’t blame it in Adam, don’t blame it in me.\n\nThe woman I fixed my eye on, was the woman who kept house for me at my cottage. Her name was Selina Goby. I agree with the late William Cobbett about picking a wife. See that she chews her food well and sets her foot down firmly on the ground when she walks, and you’re all right. Selina Goby was all right in both these respects, which was one reason for marrying her. I had another reason, likewise, entirely of my own discovering. Selina, being a single woman, made me pay so much a week for her board and services. Selina, being my wife, couldn’t charge for her board, and would have to give me her services for nothing. That was the point of view I looked at it from. Economy—with a dash of love. I put it to my mistress, as in duty bound, just as I had put it to myself.\n\n“I have been turning Selina Goby over in my mind,” I said, “and I think, my lady, it will be cheaper to marry her than to keep her.”\n\nMy lady burst out laughing, and said she didn’t know which to be most shocked at—my language or my principles. Some joke tickled her, I suppose, of the sort that you can’t take unless you are a person of quality. Understanding nothing myself but that I was free to put it next to Selina, I went and put it accordingly. And what did Selina say? Lord! how little you must know of women, if you ask that. Of course she said, Yes.\n\nAs my time drew nearer, and there got to be talk of my having a new coat for the ceremony, my mind began to misgive me. I have compared notes with other men as to what they felt while they were in my interesting situation; and they have all acknowledged that, about a week before it happened, they privately wished themselves out of it. I went a trifle further than that myself; I actually rose up, as it were, and tried to get out of it. Not for nothing! I was too just a man to expect she would let me off for nothing. Compensation to the woman when the man gets out of it, is one of the laws of England. In obedience to the laws, and after turning it over carefully in my mind, I offered Selina Goby a feather-bed and fifty shillings to be off the bargain. You will hardly believe it, but it is nevertheless true—she was fool enough to refuse.\n\nAfter that it was all over with me, of course. I got the new coat as cheap as I could, and I went through all the rest of it as cheap as I could. We were not a happy couple, and not a miserable couple. We were six of one and half-a-dozen of the other. How it was I don’t understand, but we always seemed to be getting, with the best of motives, in one another’s way. When I wanted to go upstairs, there was my wife coming down; or when my wife wanted to go down, there was I coming up. That is married life, according to my experience of it.\n\nAfter five years of misunderstandings on the stairs, it pleased an all-wise Providence to relieve us of each other by taking my wife. I was left with my little girl Penelope, and with no other child. Shortly afterwards Sir John died, and my lady was left with her little girl, Miss Rachel, and no other child. I have written to very poor purpose of my lady, if you require to be told that my little Penelope was taken care of, under my good mistress’s own eye, and was sent to school and taught, and made a sharp girl, and promoted, when old enough, to be Miss Rachel’s own maid.\n\nAs for me, I went on with my business as bailiff year after year up to Christmas 1847, when there came a change in my life. On that day, my lady invited herself to a cup of tea alone with me in my cottage. She remarked that, reckoning from the year when I started as page-boy in the time of the old lord, I had been more than fifty years in her service, and she put into my hands a beautiful waistcoat of wool that she had worked herself, to keep me warm in the bitter winter weather.\n\nI received this magnificent present quite at a loss to find words to thank my mistress with for the honour she had done me. To my great astonishment, it turned out, however, that the waistcoat was not an honour, but a bribe. My lady had discovered that I was getting old before I had discovered it myself, and she had come to my cottage to wheedle me (if I may use such an expression) into giving up my hard out-of-door work as bailiff, and taking my ease for the rest of my days as steward in the house. I made as good a fight of it against the indignity of taking my ease as I could. But my mistress knew the weak side of me; she put it as a favour to herself. The dispute between us ended, after that, in my wiping my eyes, like an old fool, with my new woollen waistcoat, and saying I would think about it.\n\nThe perturbation in my mind, in regard to thinking about it, being truly dreadful after my lady had gone away, I applied the remedy which I have never yet found to fail me in cases of doubt and emergency. I smoked a pipe and took a turn at Robinson Crusoe. Before I had occupied myself with that extraordinary book five minutes, I came on a comforting bit (page one hundred and fifty-eight), as follows: “Today we love, what tomorrow we hate.” I saw my way clear directly. Today I was all for continuing to be farm-bailiff; tomorrow, on the authority of Robinson Crusoe, I should be all the other way. Take myself tomorrow while in tomorrow’s humour, and the thing was done. My mind being relieved in this manner, I went to sleep that night in the character of Lady Verinder’s farm-bailiff, and I woke up the next morning in the character of Lady Verinder’s house-steward. All quite comfortable, and all through Robinson Crusoe!\n\nMy daughter Penelope has just looked over my shoulder to see what I have done so far. She remarks that it is beautifully written, and every word of it true. But she points out one objection. She says what I have done so far isn’t in the least what I was wanted to do. I am asked to tell the story of the Diamond and, instead of that, I have been telling the story of my own self. Curious, and quite beyond me to account for. I wonder whether the gentlemen who make a business and a living out of writing books, ever find their own selves getting in the way of their subjects, like me? If they do, I can feel for them. In the meantime, here is another false start, and more waste of good writing-paper. What’s to be done now? Nothing that I know of, except for you to keep your temper, and for me to begin it all over again for the third time.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nThe question of how I am to start the story properly I have tried to settle in two ways. First, by scratching my head, which led to nothing. Second, by consulting my daughter Penelope, which has resulted in an entirely new idea.\n\nPenelope’s notion is that I should set down what happened, regularly day by day, beginning with the day when we got the news that Mr. Franklin Blake was expected on a visit to the house. When you come to fix your memory with a date in this way, it is wonderful what your memory will pick up for you upon that compulsion. The only difficulty is to fetch out the dates, in the first place. This Penelope offers to do for me by looking into her own diary, which she was taught to keep when she was at school, and which she has gone on keeping ever since. In answer to an improvement on this notion, devised by myself, namely, that she should tell the story instead of me, out of her own diary, Penelope observes, with a fierce look and a red face, that her journal is for her own private eye, and that no living creature shall ever know what is in it but herself. When I inquire what this means, Penelope says, “Fiddlesticks!” I say, Sweethearts.\n\nBeginning, then, on Penelope’s plan, I beg to mention that I was specially called one Wednesday morning into my lady’s own sitting-room, the date being the twenty-fourth of May, eighteen hundred and forty-eight.\n\n“Gabriel,” says my lady, “here is news that will surprise you. Franklin Blake has come back from abroad. He has been staying with his father in London, and he is coming to us tomorrow to stop till next month, and keep Rachel’s birthday.”\n\nIf I had had a hat in my hand, nothing but respect would have prevented me from throwing that hat up to the ceiling. I had not seen Mr. Franklin since he was a boy, living along with us in this house. He was, out of all sight (as I remember him), the nicest boy that ever spun a top or broke a window. Miss Rachel, who was present, and to whom I made that remark, observed, in return, that she remembered him as the most atrocious tyrant that ever tortured a doll, and the hardest driver of an exhausted little girl in string harness that England could produce. “I burn with indignation, and I ache with fatigue,” was the way Miss Rachel summed it up, “when I think of Franklin Blake.”\n\nHearing what I now tell you, you will naturally ask how it was that Mr. Franklin should have passed all the years, from the time when he was a boy to the time when he was a man, out of his own country. I answer, because his father had the misfortune to be next heir to a Dukedom, and not to be able to prove it.\n\nIn two words, this was how the thing happened:\n\nMy lady’s eldest sister married the celebrated Mr. Blake—equally famous for his great riches, and his great suit at law. How many years he went on worrying the tribunals of his country to turn out the Duke in possession, and to put himself in the Duke’s place—how many lawyer’s purses he filled to bursting, and how many otherwise harmless people he set by the ears together disputing whether he was right or wrong—is more by a great deal than I can reckon up. His wife died, and two of his three children died, before the tribunals could make up their minds to show him the door and take no more of his money. When it was all over, and the Duke in possession was left in possession, Mr. Blake discovered that the only way of being even with his country for the manner in which it had treated him, was not to let his country have the honour of educating his son. “How can I trust my native institutions,” was the form in which he put it, “after the way in which my native institutions have behaved to me?” Add to this, that Mr. Blake disliked all boys, his own included, and you will admit that it could only end in one way. Master Franklin was taken from us in England, and was sent to institutions which his father could trust, in that superior country, Germany; Mr. Blake himself, you will observe, remaining snug in England, to improve his fellow-countrymen in the Parliament House, and to publish a statement on the subject of the Duke in possession, which has remained an unfinished statement from that day to this.\n\nThere! thank God, that’s told! Neither you nor I need trouble our heads any more about Mr. Blake, senior. Leave him to the Dukedom; and let you and I stick to the Diamond.\n\nThe Diamond takes us back to Mr. Franklin, who was the innocent means of bringing that unlucky jewel into the house.\n\nOur nice boy didn’t forget us after he went abroad. He wrote every now and then; sometimes to my lady, sometimes to Miss Rachel, and sometimes to me. We had had a transaction together, before he left, which consisted in his borrowing of me a ball of string, a four-bladed knife, and seven-and-sixpence in money—the colour of which last I have not seen, and never expect to see again. His letters to me chiefly related to borrowing more. I heard, however, from my lady, how he got on abroad, as he grew in years and stature. After he had learnt what the institutions of Germany could teach him, he gave the French a turn next, and the Italians a turn after that. They made him among them a sort of universal genius, as well as I could understand it. He wrote a little; he painted a little; he sang and played and composed a little—borrowing, as I suspect, in all these cases, just as he had borrowed from me. His mother’s fortune (seven hundred a year) fell to him when he came of age, and ran through him, as it might be through a sieve. The more money he had, the more he wanted; there was a hole in Mr. Franklin’s pocket that nothing would sew up. Wherever he went, the lively, easy way of him made him welcome. He lived here, there, and everywhere; his address (as he used to put it himself) being “Post Office, Europe—to be left till called for.” Twice over, he made up his mind to come back to England and see us; and twice over (saving your presence), some unmentionable woman stood in the way and stopped him. His third attempt succeeded, as you know already from what my lady told me. On Thursday the twenty-fifth of May, we were to see for the first time what our nice boy had grown to be as a man. He came of good blood; he had a high courage; and he was five-and-twenty years of age, by our reckoning. Now you know as much of Mr. Franklin Blake as I did—before Mr. Franklin Blake came down to our house.\n\nThe Thursday was as fine a summer’s day as ever you saw: and my lady and Miss Rachel (not expecting Mr. Franklin till dinner-time) drove out to lunch with some friends in the neighbourhood.\n\nWhen they were gone, I went and had a look at the bedroom which had been got ready for our guest, and saw that all was straight. Then, being butler in my lady’s establishment, as well as steward (at my own particular request, mind, and because it vexed me to see anybody but myself in possession of the key of the late Sir John’s cellar)—then, I say, I fetched up some of our famous Latour claret, and set it in the warm summer air to take off the chill before dinner. Concluding to set myself in the warm summer air next—seeing that what is good for old claret is equally good for old age—I took up my beehive chair to go out into the back court, when I was stopped by hearing a sound like the soft beating of a drum, on the terrace in front of my lady’s residence.\n\nGoing round to the terrace, I found three mahogany-coloured Indians, in white linen frocks and trousers, looking up at the house.\n\nThe Indians, as I saw on looking closer, had small hand-drums slung in front of them. Behind them stood a little delicate-looking light-haired English boy carrying a bag. I judged the fellows to be strolling conjurors, and the boy with the bag to be carrying the tools of their trade. One of the three, who spoke English and who exhibited, I must own, the most elegant manners, presently informed me that my judgment was right. He requested permission to show his tricks in the presence of the lady of the house.\n\nNow I am not a sour old man. I am generally all for amusement, and the last person in the world to distrust another person because he happens to be a few shades darker than myself. But the best of us have our weaknesses—and my weakness, when I know a family plate-basket to be out on a pantry-table, is to be instantly reminded of that basket by the sight of a strolling stranger whose manners are superior to my own. I accordingly informed the Indian that the lady of the house was out; and I warned him and his party off the premises. He made me a beautiful bow in return; and he and his party went off the premises. On my side, I returned to my beehive chair, and set myself down on the sunny side of the court, and fell (if the truth must be owned), not exactly into a sleep, but into the next best thing to it.\n\nI was roused up by my daughter Penelope running out at me as if the house was on fire. What do you think she wanted? She wanted to have the three Indian jugglers instantly taken up; for this reason, namely, that they knew who was coming from London to visit us, and that they meant some mischief to Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nMr. Franklin’s name roused me. I opened my eyes, and made my girl explain herself.\n\nIt appeared that Penelope had just come from our lodge, where she had been having a gossip with the lodge-keeper’s daughter. The two girls had seen the Indians pass out, after I had warned them off, followed by their little boy. Taking it into their heads that the boy was ill-used by the foreigners—for no reason that I could discover, except that he was pretty and delicate-looking—the two girls had stolen along the inner side of the hedge between us and the road, and had watched the proceedings of the foreigners on the outer side. Those proceedings resulted in the performance of the following extraordinary tricks.\n\nThey first looked up the road, and down the road, and made sure that they were alone. Then they all three faced about, and stared hard in the direction of our house. Then they jabbered and disputed in their own language, and looked at each other like men in doubt. Then they all turned to their little English boy, as if they expected him to help them. And then the chief Indian, who spoke English, said to the boy, “Hold out your hand.”\n\nOn hearing those dreadful words, my daughter Penelope said she didn’t know what prevented her heart from flying straight out of her. I thought privately that it might have been her stays. All I said, however, was, “You make my flesh creep.” (Nota bene: Women like these little compliments.)\n\nWell, when the Indian said, “Hold out your hand,” the boy shrunk back, and shook his head, and said he didn’t like it. The Indian, thereupon, asked him (not at all unkindly), whether he would like to be sent back to London, and left where they had found him, sleeping in an empty basket in a market—a hungry, ragged, and forsaken little boy. This, it seems, ended the difficulty. The little chap unwillingly held out his hand. Upon that, the Indian took a bottle from his bosom, and poured out of it some black stuff, like ink, into the palm of the boy’s hand. The Indian—first touching the boy’s head, and making signs over it in the air—then said, “Look.” The boy became quite stiff, and stood like a statue, looking into the ink in the hollow of his hand.\n\n(So far, it seemed to me to be juggling, accompanied by a foolish waste of ink. I was beginning to feel sleepy again, when Penelope’s next words stirred me up.)\n\nThe Indians looked up the road and down the road once more—and then the chief Indian said these words to the boy; “See the English gentleman from foreign parts.”\n\nThe boy said, “I see him.”\n\nThe Indian said, “Is it on the road to this house, and on no other, that the English gentleman will travel today?”\n\nThe boy said, “It is on the road to this house, and on no other, that the English gentleman will travel today.”\n\nThe Indian put a second question—after waiting a little first. He said: “Has the English gentleman got It about him?”\n\nThe boy answered—also, after waiting a little first—“Yes.”\n\nThe Indian put a third and last question: “Will the English gentleman come here, as he has promised to come, at the close of day?”\n\nThe boy said, “I can’t tell.”\n\nThe Indian asked why.\n\nThe boy said, “I am tired. The mist rises in my head, and puzzles me. I can see no more today.”\n\nWith that the catechism ended. The chief Indian said something in his own language to the other two, pointing to the boy, and pointing towards the town, in which (as we afterwards discovered) they were lodged. He then, after making more signs on the boy’s head, blew on his forehead, and so woke him up with a start. After that, they all went on their way towards the town, and the girls saw them no more.\n\nMost things they say have a moral, if you only look for it. What was the moral of this?\n\nThe moral was, as I thought: First, that the chief juggler had heard Mr. Franklin’s arrival talked of among the servants out-of-doors, and saw his way to making a little money by it. Second, that he and his men and boy (with a view to making the said money) meant to hang about till they saw my lady drive home, and then to come back, and foretell Mr. Franklin’s arrival by magic. Third, that Penelope had heard them rehearsing their hocus-pocus, like actors rehearsing a play. Fourth, that I should do well to have an eye, that evening, on the plate-basket. Fifth, that Penelope would do well to cool down, and leave me, her father, to doze off again in the sun.\n\nThat appeared to me to be the sensible view. If you know anything of the ways of young women, you won’t be surprised to hear that Penelope wouldn’t take it. The moral of the thing was serious, according to my daughter. She particularly reminded me of the Indian’s third question, Has the English gentleman got It about him? “Oh, father!” says Penelope, clasping her hands, “don’t joke about this. What does ‘It’ mean?”\n\n“We’ll ask Mr. Franklin, my dear,” I said, “if you can wait till Mr. Franklin comes.” I winked to show I meant that in joke. Penelope took it quite seriously. My girl’s earnestness tickled me. “What on earth should Mr. Franklin know about it?” I inquired. “Ask him,” says Penelope. “And see whether he thinks it a laughing matter, too.” With that parting shot, my daughter left me.\n\nI settled it with myself, when she was gone, that I really would ask Mr. Franklin—mainly to set Penelope’s mind at rest. What was said between us, when I did ask him, later on that same day, you will find set out fully in its proper place. But as I don’t wish to raise your expectations and then disappoint them, I will take leave to warn you here—before we go any further—that you won’t find the ghost of a joke in our conversation on the subject of the jugglers. To my great surprise, Mr. Franklin, like Penelope, took the thing seriously. How seriously, you will understand, when I tell you that, in his opinion, “It” meant the Moonstone.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nI am truly sorry to detain you over me and my beehive chair. A sleepy old man, in a sunny back yard, is not an interesting object, I am well aware. But things must be put down in their places, as things actually happened—and you must please to jog on a little while longer with me, in expectation of Mr. Franklin Blake’s arrival later in the day.\n\nBefore I had time to doze off again, after my daughter Penelope had left me, I was disturbed by a rattling of plates and dishes in the servants’ hall, which meant that dinner was ready. Taking my own meals in my own sitting-room, I had nothing to do with the servants’ dinner, except to wish them a good stomach to it all round, previous to composing myself once more in my chair. I was just stretching my legs, when out bounced another woman on me. Not my daughter again; only Nancy, the kitchen-maid, this time. I was straight in her way out; and I observed, as she asked me to let her by, that she had a sulky face—a thing which, as head of the servants, I never allow, on principle, to pass me without inquiry.\n\n“What are you turning your back on your dinner for?” I asked. “What’s wrong now, Nancy?”\n\nNancy tried to push by, without answering; upon which I rose up, and took her by the ear. She is a nice plump young lass, and it is customary with me to adopt that manner of showing that I personally approve of a girl.\n\n“What’s wrong now?” I said once more.\n\n“Rosanna’s late again for dinner,” says Nancy. “And I’m sent to fetch her in. All the hard work falls on my shoulders in this house. Let me alone, Mr. Betteredge!”\n\nThe person here mentioned as Rosanna was our second housemaid. Having a kind of pity for our second housemaid (why, you shall presently know), and seeing in Nancy’s face, that she would fetch her fellow-servant in with more hard words than might be needful under the circumstances, it struck me that I had nothing particular to do, and that I might as well fetch Rosanna myself; giving her a hint to be punctual in future, which I knew she would take kindly from me.\n\n“Where is Rosanna?” I inquired.\n\n“At the sands, of course!” says Nancy, with a toss of her head. “She had another of her fainting fits this morning, and she asked to go out and get a breath of fresh air. I have no patience with her!”\n\n“Go back to your dinner, my girl,” I said. “I have patience with her, and I’ll fetch her in.”\n\nNancy (who has a fine appetite) looked pleased. When she looks pleased, she looks nice. When she looks nice, I chuck her under the chin. It isn’t immorality—it’s only habit.\n\nWell, I took my stick, and set off for the sands.\n\nNo! it won’t do to set off yet. I am sorry again to detain you; but you really must hear the story of the sands, and the story of Rosanna—for this reason, that the matter of the Diamond touches them both nearly. How hard I try to get on with my statement without stopping by the way, and how badly I succeed! But, there!—Persons and Things do turn up so vexatiously in this life, and will in a manner insist on being noticed. Let us take it easy, and let us take it short; we shall be in the thick of the mystery soon, I promise you!\n\nRosanna (to put the Person before the Thing, which is but common politeness) was the only new servant in our house. About four months before the time I am writing of, my lady had been in London, and had gone over a Reformatory, intended to save forlorn women from drifting back into bad ways, after they had got released from prison. The matron, seeing my lady took an interest in the place, pointed out a girl to her, named Rosanna Spearman, and told her a most miserable story, which I haven’t the heart to repeat here; for I don’t like to be made wretched without any use, and no more do you. The upshot of it was, that Rosanna Spearman had been a thief, and not being of the sort that get up Companies in the City, and rob from thousands, instead of only robbing from one, the law laid hold of her, and the prison and the reformatory followed the lead of the law. The matron’s opinion of Rosanna was (in spite of what she had done) that the girl was one in a thousand, and that she only wanted a chance to prove herself worthy of any Christian woman’s interest in her. My lady (being a Christian woman, if ever there was one yet) said to the matron, upon that, “Rosanna Spearman shall have her chance, in my service.” In a week afterwards, Rosanna Spearman entered this establishment as our second housemaid.\n\nNot a soul was told the girl’s story, excepting Miss Rachel and me. My lady, doing me the honour to consult me about most things, consulted me about Rosanna. Having fallen a good deal latterly into the late Sir John’s way of always agreeing with my lady, I agreed with her heartily about Rosanna Spearman.\n\nA fairer chance no girl could have had than was given to this poor girl of ours. None of the servants could cast her past life in her teeth, for none of the servants knew what it had been. She had her wages and her privileges, like the rest of them; and every now and then a friendly word from my lady, in private, to encourage her. In return, she showed herself, I am bound to say, well worthy of the kind treatment bestowed upon her. Though far from strong, and troubled occasionally with those fainting-fits already mentioned, she went about her work modestly and uncomplainingly, doing it carefully, and doing it well. But, somehow, she failed to make friends among the other women servants, excepting my daughter Penelope, who was always kind to Rosanna, though never intimate with her.\n\nI hardly know what the girl did to offend them. There was certainly no beauty about her to make the others envious; she was the plainest woman in the house, with the additional misfortune of having one shoulder bigger than the other. What the servants chiefly resented, I think, was her silent tongue and her solitary ways. She read or worked in leisure hours when the rest gossiped. And when it came to her turn to go out, nine times out of ten she quietly put on her bonnet, and had her turn by herself. She never quarrelled, she never took offence; she only kept a certain distance, obstinately and civilly, between the rest of them and herself. Add to this that, plain as she was, there was just a dash of something that wasn’t like a housemaid, and that was like a lady, about her. It might have been in her voice, or it might have been in her face. All I can say is, that the other women pounced on it like lightning the first day she came into the house, and said (which was most unjust) that Rosanna Spearman gave herself airs.\n\nHaving now told the story of Rosanna, I have only to notice one of the many queer ways of this strange girl to get on next to the story of the sands.\n\nOur house is high up on the Yorkshire coast, and close by the sea. We have got beautiful walks all round us, in every direction but one. That one I acknowledge to be a horrid walk. It leads, for a quarter of a mile, through a melancholy plantation of firs, and brings you out between low cliffs on the loneliest and ugliest little bay on all our coast.\n\nThe sandhills here run down to the sea, and end in two spits of rock jutting out opposite each other, till you lose sight of them in the water. One is called the North Spit, and one the South. Between the two, shifting backwards and forwards at certain seasons of the year, lies the most horrible quicksand on the shores of Yorkshire. At the turn of the tide, something goes on in the unknown deeps below, which sets the whole face of the quicksand shivering and trembling in a manner most remarkable to see, and which has given to it, among the people in our parts, the name of the Shivering Sand. A great bank, half a mile out, nigh the mouth of the bay, breaks the force of the main ocean coming in from the offing. Winter and summer, when the tide flows over the quicksand, the sea seems to leave the waves behind it on the bank, and rolls its waters in smoothly with a heave, and covers the sand in silence. A lonesome and a horrid retreat, I can tell you! No boat ever ventures into this bay. No children from our fishing-village, called Cobb’s Hole, ever come here to play. The very birds of the air, as it seems to me, give the Shivering Sand a wide berth. That a young woman, with dozens of nice walks to choose from, and company to go with her, if she only said “Come!”, should prefer this place, and should sit and work or read in it, all alone, when it’s her turn out, I grant you, passes belief. It’s true, nevertheless, account for it as you may, that this was Rosanna Spearman’s favourite walk, except when she went once or twice to Cobb’s Hole, to see the only friend she had in our neighbourhood, of whom more anon. It’s also true that I was now setting out for this same place, to fetch the girl in to dinner, which brings us round happily to our former point, and starts us fair again on our way to the sands.\n\nI saw no sign of the girl in the plantation. When I got out, through the sandhills, on to the beach, there she was, in her little straw bonnet, and her plain grey cloak that she always wore to hide her deformed shoulder as much as might be—there she was, all alone, looking out on the quicksand and the sea.\n\nShe started when I came up with her, and turned her head away from me. Not looking me in the face being another of the proceedings, which, as head of the servants, I never allow, on principle, to pass without inquiry—I turned her round my way, and saw that she was crying. My bandanna handkerchief—one of six beauties given to me by my lady—was handy in my pocket. I took it out, and I said to Rosanna, “Come and sit down, my dear, on the slope of the beach along with me. I’ll dry your eyes for you first, and then I’ll make so bold as to ask what you have been crying about.”\n\nWhen you come to my age, you will find sitting down on the slope of a beach a much longer job than you think it now. By the time I was settled, Rosanna had dried her own eyes with a very inferior handkerchief to mine—cheap cambric. She looked very quiet, and very wretched; but she sat down by me like a good girl, when I told her. When you want to comfort a woman by the shortest way, take her on your knee. I thought of this golden rule. But there! Rosanna wasn’t Nancy, and that’s the truth of it!\n\n“Now, tell me, my dear,” I said, “what are you crying about?”\n\n“About the years that are gone, Mr. Betteredge,” says Rosanna quietly. “My past life still comes back to me sometimes.”\n\n“Come, come, my girl,” I said, “your past life is all sponged out. Why can’t you forget it?”\n\nShe took me by one of the lappets of my coat. I am a slovenly old man, and a good deal of my meat and drink gets splashed about on my clothes. Sometimes one of the women, and sometimes another, cleans me of my grease. The day before, Rosanna had taken out a spot for me on the lappet of my coat, with a new composition, warranted to remove anything. The grease was gone, but there was a little dull place left on the nap of the cloth where the grease had been. The girl pointed to that place, and shook her head.\n\n“The stain is taken off,” she said. “But the place shows, Mr. Betteredge—the place shows!”\n\nA remark which takes a man unawares by means of his own coat is not an easy remark to answer. Something in the girl herself, too, made me particularly sorry for her just then. She had nice brown eyes, plain as she was in other ways—and she looked at me with a sort of respect for my happy old age and my good character, as things for ever out of her own reach, which made my heart heavy for our second housemaid. Not feeling myself able to comfort her, there was only one other thing to do. That thing was—to take her in to dinner.\n\n“Help me up,” I said. “You’re late for dinner, Rosanna—and I have come to fetch you in.”\n\n“You, Mr. Betteredge!” says she.\n\n“They told Nancy to fetch you,” I said. “But I thought you might like your scolding better, my dear, if it came from me.”\n\nInstead of helping me up, the poor thing stole her hand into mine, and gave it a little squeeze. She tried hard to keep from crying again, and succeeded—for which I respected her. “You’re very kind, Mr. Betteredge,” she said. “I don’t want any dinner today—let me bide a little longer here.”\n\n“What makes you like to be here?” I asked. “What is it that brings you everlastingly to this miserable place?”\n\n“Something draws me to it,” says the girl, making images with her finger in the sand. “I try to keep away from it, and I can’t. Sometimes,” says she in a low voice, as if she was frightened at her own fancy, “sometimes, Mr. Betteredge, I think that my grave is waiting for me here.”\n\n“There’s roast mutton and suet pudding waiting for you!” says I. “Go in to dinner directly. This is what comes, Rosanna, of thinking on an empty stomach!” I spoke severely, being naturally indignant (at my time of life) to hear a young woman of five-and-twenty talking about her latter end!\n\nShe didn’t seem to hear me: she put her hand on my shoulder, and kept me where I was, sitting by her side.\n\n“I think the place has laid a spell on me,” she said. “I dream of it night after night; I think of it when I sit stitching at my work. You know I am grateful, Mr. Betteredge—you know I try to deserve your kindness, and my lady’s confidence in me. But I wonder sometimes whether the life here is too quiet and too good for such a woman as I am, after all I have gone through, Mr. Betteredge—after all I have gone through. It’s more lonely to me to be among the other servants, knowing I am not what they are, than it is to be here. My lady doesn’t know, the matron at the reformatory doesn’t know, what a dreadful reproach honest people are in themselves to a woman like me. Don’t scold me, there’s a dear good man. I do my work, don’t I? Please not to tell my lady I am discontented—I am not. My mind’s unquiet, sometimes, that’s all.” She snatched her hand off my shoulder, and suddenly pointed down to the quicksand. “Look!” she said “Isn’t it wonderful? isn’t it terrible? I have seen it dozens of times, and it’s always as new to me as if I had never seen it before!”\n\nI looked where she pointed. The tide was on the turn, and the horrid sand began to shiver. The broad brown face of it heaved slowly, and then dimpled and quivered all over. “Do you know what it looks like to me?” says Rosanna, catching me by the shoulder again. “It looks as if it had hundreds of suffocating people under it—all struggling to get to the surface, and all sinking lower and lower in the dreadful deeps! Throw a stone in, Mr. Betteredge! Throw a stone in, and let’s see the sand suck it down!”\n\nHere was unwholesome talk! Here was an empty stomach feeding on an unquiet mind! My answer—a pretty sharp one, in the poor girl’s own interests, I promise you!—was at my tongue’s end, when it was snapped short off on a sudden by a voice among the sandhills shouting for me by my name. “Betteredge!” cries the voice, “where are you?” “Here!” I shouted out in return, without a notion in my mind of who it was. Rosanna started to her feet, and stood looking towards the voice. I was just thinking of getting on my own legs next, when I was staggered by a sudden change in the girl’s face.\n\nHer complexion turned of a beautiful red, which I had never seen in it before; she brightened all over with a kind of speechless and breathless surprise. “Who is it?” I asked. Rosanna gave me back my own question. “Oh! who is it?” she said softly, more to herself than to me. I twisted round on the sand and looked behind me. There, coming out on us from among the hills, was a bright-eyed young gentleman, dressed in a beautiful fawn-coloured suit, with gloves and hat to match, with a rose in his button-hole, and a smile on his face that might have set the Shivering Sand itself smiling at him in return. Before I could get on my legs, he plumped down on the sand by the side of me, put his arm round my neck, foreign fashion, and gave me a hug that fairly squeezed the breath out of my body. “Dear old Betteredge!” says he. “I owe you seven-and-sixpence. Now do you know who I am?”\n\nLord bless us and save us! Here—four good hours before we expected him—was Mr. Franklin Blake!\n\nBefore I could say a word, I saw Mr. Franklin, a little surprised to all appearance, look up from me to Rosanna. Following his lead, I looked at the girl too. She was blushing of a deeper red than ever, seemingly at having caught Mr. Franklin’s eye; and she turned and left us suddenly, in a confusion quite unaccountable to my mind, without either making her curtsey to the gentleman or saying a word to me. Very unlike her usual self: a civiller and better-behaved servant, in general, you never met with.\n\n“That’s an odd girl,” says Mr. Franklin. “I wonder what she sees in me to surprise her?”\n\n“I suppose, sir,” I answered, drolling on our young gentleman’s Continental education, “it’s the varnish from foreign parts.”\n\nI set down here Mr. Franklin’s careless question, and my foolish answer, as a consolation and encouragement to all stupid people—it being, as I have remarked, a great satisfaction to our inferior fellow-creatures to find that their betters are, on occasions, no brighter than they are. Neither Mr. Franklin, with his wonderful foreign training, nor I, with my age, experience, and natural mother-wit, had the ghost of an idea of what Rosanna Spearman’s unaccountable behaviour really meant. She was out of our thoughts, poor soul, before we had seen the last flutter of her little grey cloak among the sandhills. And what of that? you will ask, naturally enough. Read on, good friend, as patiently as you can, and perhaps you will be as sorry for Rosanna Spearman as I was, when I found out the truth.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nThe first thing I did, after we were left together alone, was to make a third attempt to get up from my seat on the sand. Mr. Franklin stopped me.\n\n“There is one advantage about this horrid place,” he said; “we have got it all to ourselves. Stay where you are, Betteredge; I have something to say to you.”\n\nWhile he was speaking, I was looking at him, and trying to see something of the boy I remembered, in the man before me. The man put me out. Look as I might, I could see no more of his boy’s rosy cheeks than of his boy’s trim little jacket. His complexion had got pale: his face, at the lower part was covered, to my great surprise and disappointment, with a curly brown beard and moustachios. He had a lively touch-and-go way with him, very pleasant and engaging, I admit; but nothing to compare with his free-and-easy manners of other times. To make matters worse, he had promised to be tall, and had not kept his promise. He was neat, and slim, and well made; but he wasn’t by an inch or two up to the middle height. In short, he baffled me altogether. The years that had passed had left nothing of his old self, except the bright, straightforward look in his eyes. There I found our nice boy again, and there I concluded to stop in my investigation.\n\n“Welcome back to the old place, Mr. Franklin,” I said. “All the more welcome, sir, that you have come some hours before we expected you.”\n\n“I have a reason for coming before you expected me,” answered Mr. Franklin. “I suspect, Betteredge, that I have been followed and watched in London, for the last three or four days; and I have travelled by the morning instead of the afternoon train, because I wanted to give a certain dark-looking stranger the slip.”\n\nThose words did more than surprise me. They brought back to my mind, in a flash, the three jugglers, and Penelope’s notion that they meant some mischief to Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\n“Who’s watching you, sir,—and why?” I inquired.\n\n“Tell me about the three Indians you have had at the house today,” says Mr. Franklin, without noticing my question. “It’s just possible, Betteredge, that my stranger and your three jugglers may turn out to be pieces of the same puzzle.”\n\n“How do you come to know about the jugglers, sir?” I asked, putting one question on the top of another, which was bad manners, I own. But you don’t expect much from poor human nature—so don’t expect much from me.\n\n“I saw Penelope at the house,” says Mr. Franklin; “and Penelope told me. Your daughter promised to be a pretty girl, Betteredge, and she has kept her promise. Penelope has got a small ear and a small foot. Did the late Mrs. Betteredge possess those inestimable advantages?”\n\n“The late Mrs. Betteredge possessed a good many defects, sir,” says I. “One of them (if you will pardon my mentioning it) was never keeping to the matter in hand. She was more like a fly than a woman: she couldn’t settle on anything.”\n\n“She would just have suited me,” says Mr. Franklin. “I never settle on anything either. Betteredge, your edge is better than ever. Your daughter said as much, when I asked for particulars about the jugglers. ‘Father will tell you, sir. He’s a wonderful man for his age; and he expresses himself beautifully.’ Penelope’s own words—blushing divinely. Not even my respect for you prevented me from—never mind; I knew her when she was a child, and she’s none the worse for it. Let’s be serious. What did the jugglers do?”\n\nI was something dissatisfied with my daughter—not for letting Mr. Franklin kiss her; Mr. Franklin was welcome to that—but for forcing me to tell her foolish story at second hand. However, there was no help for it now but to mention the circumstances. Mr. Franklin’s merriment all died away as I went on. He sat knitting his eyebrows, and twisting his beard. When I had done, he repeated after me two of the questions which the chief juggler had put to the boy—seemingly for the purpose of fixing them well in his mind.\n\n“‘Is it on the road to this house, and on no other, that the English gentleman will travel today?’ ‘Has the English gentleman got It about him?’ I suspect,” says Mr. Franklin, pulling a little sealed paper parcel out of his pocket, “that ‘It’ means this. And ‘this,’ Betteredge, means my uncle Herncastle’s famous Diamond.”\n\n“Good Lord, sir!” I broke out, “how do you come to be in charge of the wicked Colonel’s Diamond?”\n\n“The wicked Colonel’s will has left his Diamond as a birthday present to my cousin Rachel,” says Mr. Franklin. “And my father, as the wicked Colonel’s executor, has given it in charge to me to bring down here.”\n\nIf the sea, then oozing in smoothly over the Shivering Sand, had been changed into dry land before my own eyes, I doubt if I could have been more surprised than I was when Mr. Franklin spoke those words.\n\n“The Colonel’s Diamond left to Miss Rachel!” says I. “And your father, sir, the Colonel’s executor! Why, I would have laid any bet you like, Mr. Franklin, that your father wouldn’t have touched the Colonel with a pair of tongs!”\n\n“Strong language, Betteredge! What was there against the Colonel? He belonged to your time, not to mine. Tell me what you know about him, and I’ll tell you how my father came to be his executor, and more besides. I have made some discoveries in London about my uncle Herncastle and his Diamond, which have rather an ugly look to my eyes; and I want you to confirm them. You called him the ‘wicked Colonel’ just now. Search your memory, my old friend, and tell me why.”\n\nI saw he was in earnest, and I told him.\n\nHere follows the substance of what I said, written out entirely for your benefit. Pay attention to it, or you will be all abroad, when we get deeper into the story. Clear your mind of the children, or the dinner, or the new bonnet, or what not. Try if you can’t forget politics, horses, prices in the City, and grievances at the club. I hope you won’t take this freedom on my part amiss; it’s only a way I have of appealing to the gentle reader. Lord! haven’t I seen you with the greatest authors in your hands, and don’t I know how ready your attention is to wander when it’s a book that asks for it, instead of a person?\n\nI spoke, a little way back, of my lady’s father, the old lord with the short temper and the long tongue. He had five children in all. Two sons to begin with; then, after a long time, his wife broke out breeding again, and the three young ladies came briskly one after the other, as fast as the nature of things would permit; my mistress, as before mentioned, being the youngest and best of the three. Of the two sons, the eldest, Arthur, inherited the title and estates. The second, the Honourable John, got a fine fortune left him by a relative, and went into the army.\n\nIt’s an ill bird, they say, that fouls its own nest. I look on the noble family of the Herncastles as being my nest; and I shall take it as a favour if I am not expected to enter into particulars on the subject of the Honourable John. He was, I honestly believe, one of the greatest blackguards that ever lived. I can hardly say more or less for him than that. He went into the army, beginning in the Guards. He had to leave the Guards before he was two-and-twenty—never mind why. They are very strict in the army, and they were too strict for the Honourable John. He went out to India to see whether they were equally strict there, and to try a little active service. In the matter of bravery (to give him his due), he was a mixture of bull-dog and game-cock, with a dash of the savage. He was at the taking of Seringapatam. Soon afterwards he changed into another regiment, and, in course of time, changed into a third. In the third he got his last step as lieutenant-colonel, and, getting that, got also a sunstroke, and came home to England.\n\nHe came back with a character that closed the doors of all his family against him, my lady (then just married) taking the lead, and declaring (with Sir John’s approval, of course) that her brother should never enter any house of hers. There was more than one slur on the Colonel that made people shy of him; but the blot of the Diamond is all I need mention here.\n\nIt was said he had got possession of his Indian jewel by means which, bold as he was, he didn’t dare acknowledge. He never attempted to sell it—not being in need of money, and not (to give him his due again) making money an object. He never gave it away; he never even showed it to any living soul. Some said he was afraid of its getting him into a difficulty with the military authorities; others (very ignorant indeed of the real nature of the man) said he was afraid, if he showed it, of its costing him his life.\n\nThere was perhaps a grain of truth mixed up with this last report. It was false to say that he was afraid; but it was a fact that his life had been twice threatened in India; and it was firmly believed that the Moonstone was at the bottom of it. When he came back to England, and found himself avoided by everybody, the Moonstone was thought to be at the bottom of it again. The mystery of the Colonel’s life got in the Colonel’s way, and outlawed him, as you may say, among his own people. The men wouldn’t let him into their clubs; the women—more than one—whom he wanted to marry, refused him; friends and relations got too near-sighted to see him in the street.\n\nSome men in this mess would have tried to set themselves right with the world. But to give in, even when he was wrong, and had all society against him, was not the way of the Honourable John. He had kept the Diamond, in flat defiance of assassination, in India. He kept the Diamond, in flat defiance of public opinion, in England. There you have the portrait of the man before you, as in a picture: a character that braved everything; and a face, handsome as it was, that looked possessed by the devil.\n\nWe heard different rumours about him from time to time. Sometimes they said he was given up to smoking opium and collecting old books; sometimes he was reported to be trying strange things in chemistry; sometimes he was seen carousing and amusing himself among the lowest people in the lowest slums of London. Anyhow, a solitary, vicious, underground life was the life the Colonel led. Once, and once only, after his return to England, I myself saw him, face to face.\n\nAbout two years before the time of which I am now writing, and about a year and a half before the time of his death, the Colonel came unexpectedly to my lady’s house in London. It was the night of Miss Rachel’s birthday, the twenty-first of June; and there was a party in honour of it, as usual. I received a message from the footman to say that a gentleman wanted to see me. Going up into the hall, there I found the Colonel, wasted, and worn, and old, and shabby, and as wild and as wicked as ever.\n\n“Go up to my sister,” says he; “and say that I have called to wish my niece many happy returns of the day.”\n\nHe had made attempts by letter, more than once already, to be reconciled with my lady, for no other purpose, I am firmly persuaded, than to annoy her. But this was the first time he had actually come to the house. I had it on the tip of my tongue to say that my mistress had a party that night. But the devilish look of him daunted me. I went upstairs with his message, and left him, by his own desire, waiting in the hall. The servants stood staring at him, at a distance, as if he was a walking engine of destruction, loaded with powder and shot, and likely to go off among them at a moment’s notice.\n\nMy lady had a dash—no more—of the family temper. “Tell Colonel Herncastle,” she said, when I gave her her brother’s message, “that Miss Verinder is engaged, and that I decline to see him.” I tried to plead for a civiller answer than that; knowing the Colonel’s constitutional superiority to the restraints which govern gentlemen in general. Quite useless! The family temper flashed out at me directly. “When I want your advice,” says my lady, “you know that I always ask for it. I don’t ask for it now.” I went downstairs with the message, of which I took the liberty of presenting a new and amended edition of my own contriving, as follows: “My lady and Miss Rachel regret that they are engaged, Colonel; and beg to be excused having the honour of seeing you.”\n\nI expected him to break out, even at that polite way of putting it. To my surprise he did nothing of the sort; he alarmed me by taking the thing with an unnatural quiet. His eyes, of a glittering bright grey, just settled on me for a moment; and he laughed, not out of himself, like other people, but into himself, in a soft, chuckling, horridly mischievous way. “Thank you, Betteredge,” he said. “I shall remember my niece’s birthday.” With that, he turned on his heel, and walked out of the house.\n\nThe next birthday came round, and we heard he was ill in bed. Six months afterwards—that is to say, six months before the time I am now writing of—there came a letter from a highly respectable clergyman to my lady. It communicated two wonderful things in the way of family news. First, that the Colonel had forgiven his sister on his death-bed. Second, that he had forgiven everybody else, and had made a most edifying end. I have myself (in spite of the bishops and the clergy) an unfeigned respect for the Church; but I am firmly persuaded, at the same time, that the devil remained in undisturbed possession of the Honourable John, and that the last abominable act in the life of that abominable man was (saving your presence) to take the clergyman in!\n\nThis was the sum-total of what I had to tell Mr. Franklin. I remarked that he listened more and more eagerly the longer I went on. Also, that the story of the Colonel being sent away from his sister’s door, on the occasion of his niece’s birthday, seemed to strike Mr. Franklin like a shot that had hit the mark. Though he didn’t acknowledge it, I saw that I had made him uneasy, plainly enough, in his face.\n\n“You have said your say, Betteredge,” he remarked. “It’s my turn now. Before, however, I tell you what discoveries I have made in London, and how I came to be mixed up in this matter of the Diamond, I want to know one thing. You look, my old friend, as if you didn’t quite understand the object to be answered by this consultation of ours. Do your looks belie you?”\n\n“No, sir,” I said. “My looks, on this occasion at any rate, tell the truth.”\n\n“In that case,” says Mr. Franklin, “suppose I put you up to my point of view, before we go any further. I see three very serious questions involved in the Colonel’s birthday-gift to my cousin Rachel. Follow me carefully, Betteredge; and count me off on your fingers, if it will help you,” says Mr. Franklin, with a certain pleasure in showing how clear-headed he could be, which reminded me wonderfully of old times when he was a boy. “Question the first: Was the Colonel’s Diamond the object of a conspiracy in India? Question the second: Has the conspiracy followed the Colonel’s Diamond to England? Question the third: Did the Colonel know the conspiracy followed the Diamond; and has he purposely left a legacy of trouble and danger to his sister, through the innocent medium of his sister’s child? That is what I am driving at, Betteredge. Don’t let me frighten you.”\n\nIt was all very well to say that, but he had frightened me.\n\nIf he was right, here was our quiet English house suddenly invaded by a devilish Indian Diamond—bringing after it a conspiracy of living rogues, set loose on us by the vengeance of a dead man. There was our situation as revealed to me in Mr. Franklin’s last words! Who ever heard the like of it—in the nineteenth century, mind; in an age of progress, and in a country which rejoices in the blessings of the British constitution? Nobody ever heard the like of it, and, consequently, nobody can be expected to believe it. I shall go on with my story, however, in spite of that.\n\nWhen you get a sudden alarm, of the sort that I had got now, nine times out of ten the place you feel it in is your stomach. When you feel it in your stomach, your attention wanders, and you begin to fidget. I fidgeted silently in my place on the sand. Mr. Franklin noticed me, contending with a perturbed stomach or mind—which you please; they mean the same thing—and, checking himself just as he was starting with his part of the story, said to me sharply, “What do you want?”\n\nWhat did I want? I didn’t tell him; but I’ll tell you, in confidence. I wanted a whiff of my pipe, and a turn at Robinson Crusoe.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nKeeping my private sentiments to myself, I respectfully requested Mr. Franklin to go on. Mr. Franklin replied, “Don’t fidget, Betteredge,” and went on.\n\nOur young gentleman’s first words informed me that his discoveries, concerning the wicked Colonel and the Diamond, had begun with a visit which he had paid (before he came to us) to the family lawyer, at Hampstead. A chance word dropped by Mr. Franklin, when the two were alone, one day, after dinner, revealed that he had been charged by his father with a birthday present to be taken to Miss Rachel. One thing led to another; and it ended in the lawyer mentioning what the present really was, and how the friendly connexion between the late Colonel and Mr. Blake, senior, had taken its rise. The facts here are really so extraordinary, that I doubt if I can trust my own language to do justice to them. I prefer trying to report Mr. Franklin’s discoveries, as nearly as may be, in Mr. Franklin’s own words.\n\n“You remember the time, Betteredge,” he said, “when my father was trying to prove his title to that unlucky Dukedom? Well! that was also the time when my uncle Herncastle returned from India. My father discovered that his brother-in-law was in possession of certain papers which were likely to be of service to him in his lawsuit. He called on the Colonel, on pretence of welcoming him back to England. The Colonel was not to be deluded in that way. ‘You want something,’ he said, ‘or you would never have compromised your reputation by calling on me.’ My father saw that the one chance for him was to show his hand; he admitted, at once, that he wanted the papers. The Colonel asked for a day to consider his answer. His answer came in the shape of a most extraordinary letter, which my friend the lawyer showed me. The Colonel began by saying that he wanted something of my father, and that he begged to propose an exchange of friendly services between them. The fortune of war (that was the expression he used) had placed him in possession of one of the largest Diamonds in the world; and he had reason to believe that neither he nor his precious jewel was safe in any house, in any quarter of the globe, which they occupied together. Under these alarming circumstances, he had determined to place his Diamond in the keeping of another person. That person was not expected to run any risk. He might deposit the precious stone in any place especially guarded and set apart—like a banker’s or jeweller’s strongroom—for the safe custody of valuables of high price. His main personal responsibility in the matter was to be of the passive kind. He was to undertake either by himself, or by a trustworthy representative—to receive at a prearranged address, on certain prearranged days in every year, a note from the Colonel, simply stating the fact that he was a living man at that date. In the event of the date passing over without the note being received, the Colonel’s silence might be taken as a sure token of the Colonel’s death by murder. In that case, and in no other, certain sealed instructions relating to the disposal of the Diamond, and deposited with it, were to be opened, and followed implicitly. If my father chose to accept this strange charge, the Colonel’s papers were at his disposal in return. That was the letter.”\n\n“What did your father do, sir?” I asked.\n\n“Do?” says Mr. Franklin. “I’ll tell you what he did. He brought the invaluable faculty, called common sense, to bear on the Colonel’s letter. The whole thing, he declared, was simply absurd. Somewhere in his Indian wanderings, the Colonel had picked up with some wretched crystal which he took for a diamond. As for the danger of his being murdered, and the precautions devised to preserve his life and his piece of crystal, this was the nineteenth century, and any man in his senses had only to apply to the police. The Colonel had been a notorious opium-eater for years past; and, if the only way of getting at the valuable papers he possessed was by accepting a matter of opium as a matter of fact, my father was quite willing to take the ridiculous responsibility imposed on him—all the more readily that it involved no trouble to himself. The Diamond and the sealed instructions went into his banker’s strongroom, and the Colonel’s letters, periodically reporting him a living man, were received and opened by our family lawyer, Mr. Bruff, as my father’s representative. No sensible person, in a similar position, could have viewed the matter in any other way. Nothing in this world, Betteredge, is probable unless it appeals to our own trumpery experience; and we only believe in a romance when we see it in a newspaper.”\n\nIt was plain to me from this, that Mr. Franklin thought his father’s notion about the Colonel hasty and wrong.\n\n“What is your own private opinion about the matter, sir?” I asked.\n\n“Let’s finish the story of the Colonel first,” says Mr. Franklin. “There is a curious want of system, Betteredge, in the English mind; and your question, my old friend, is an instance of it. When we are not occupied in making machinery, we are (mentally speaking) the most slovenly people in the universe.”\n\n“So much,” I thought to myself, “for a foreign education! He has learned that way of girding at us in France, I suppose.”\n\nMr. Franklin took up the lost thread, and went on.\n\n“My father,” he said, “got the papers he wanted, and never saw his brother-in-law again from that time. Year after year, on the prearranged days, the prearranged letter came from the Colonel, and was opened by Mr. Bruff. I have seen the letters, in a heap, all of them written in the same brief, business-like form of words: ‘Sir,—This is to certify that I am still a living man. Let the Diamond be. John Herncastle.’ That was all he ever wrote, and that came regularly to the day; until some six or eight months since, when the form of the letter varied for the first time. It ran now: ‘Sir,—They tell me I am dying. Come to me, and help me to make my will.’ Mr. Bruff went, and found him, in the little suburban villa, surrounded by its own grounds, in which he had lived alone, ever since he had left India. He had dogs, cats, and birds to keep him company; but no human being near him, except the person who came daily to do the house-work, and the doctor at the bedside. The will was a very simple matter. The Colonel had dissipated the greater part of his fortune in his chemical investigations. His will began and ended in three clauses, which he dictated from his bed, in perfect possession of his faculties. The first clause provided for the safe keeping and support of his animals. The second founded a professorship of experimental chemistry at a northern university. The third bequeathed the Moonstone as a birthday present to his niece, on condition that my father would act as executor. My father at first refused to act. On second thoughts, however, he gave way, partly because he was assured that the executorship would involve him in no trouble; partly because Mr. Bruff suggested, in Rachel’s interest, that the Diamond might be worth something, after all.”\n\n“Did the Colonel give any reason, sir,” I inquired, “why he left the Diamond to Miss Rachel?”\n\n“He not only gave the reason—he had the reason written in his will,” said Mr. Franklin. “I have got an extract, which you shall see presently. Don’t be slovenly-minded, Betteredge! One thing at a time. You have heard about the Colonel’s Will; now you must hear what happened after the Colonel’s death. It was formally necessary to have the Diamond valued, before the Will could be proved. All the jewellers consulted, at once confirmed the Colonel’s assertion that he possessed one of the largest diamonds in the world. The question of accurately valuing it presented some serious difficulties. Its size made it a phenomenon in the diamond market; its colour placed it in a category by itself; and, to add to these elements of uncertainty, there was a defect, in the shape of a flaw, in the very heart of the stone. Even with this last serious draw-back, however, the lowest of the various estimates given was twenty thousand pounds. Conceive my father’s astonishment! He had been within a hair’s-breadth of refusing to act as executor, and of allowing this magnificent jewel to be lost to the family. The interest he took in the matter now, induced him to open the sealed instructions which had been deposited with the Diamond. Mr. Bruff showed this document to me, with the other papers; and it suggests (to my mind) a clue to the nature of the conspiracy which threatened the Colonel’s life.”\n\n“Then you do believe, sir,” I said, “that there was a conspiracy?”\n\n“Not possessing my father’s excellent common sense,” answered Mr. Franklin, “I believe the Colonel’s life was threatened, exactly as the Colonel said. The sealed instructions, as I think, explain how it was that he died, after all, quietly in his bed. In the event of his death by violence (that is to say, in the absence of the regular letter from him at the appointed date), my father was then directed to send the Moonstone secretly to Amsterdam. It was to be deposited in that city with a famous diamond-cutter, and it was to be cut up into from four to six separate stones. The stones were then to be sold for what they would fetch, and the proceeds were to be applied to the founding of that professorship of experimental chemistry, which the Colonel has since endowed by his Will. Now, Betteredge, exert those sharp wits of yours, and observe the conclusion to which the Colonel’s instructions point!”\n\nI instantly exerted my wits. They were of the slovenly English sort; and they consequently muddled it all, until Mr. Franklin took them in hand, and pointed out what they ought to see.\n\n“Remark,” says Mr. Franklin, “that the integrity of the Diamond, as a whole stone, is here artfully made dependent on the preservation from violence of the Colonel’s life. He is not satisfied with saying to the enemies he dreads, ‘Kill me—and you will be no nearer to the Diamond than you are now; it is where you can’t get at it—in the guarded strongroom of a bank.’ He says instead, ‘Kill me—and the Diamond will be the Diamond no longer; its identity will be destroyed.’ What does that mean?”\n\nHere I had (as I thought) a flash of the wonderful foreign brightness.\n\n“I know,” I said. “It means lowering the value of the stone, and cheating the rogues in that way!”\n\n“Nothing of the sort,” says Mr. Franklin. “I have inquired about that. The flawed Diamond, cut up, would actually fetch more than the Diamond as it now is; for this plain reason—that from four to six perfect brilliants might be cut from it, which would be, collectively, worth more money than the large—but imperfect single stone. If robbery for the purpose of gain was at the bottom of the conspiracy, the Colonel’s instructions absolutely made the Diamond better worth stealing. More money could have been got for it, and the disposal of it in the diamond market would have been infinitely easier, if it had passed through the hands of the workmen of Amsterdam.”\n\n“Lord bless us, sir!” I burst out. “What was the plot, then?”\n\n“A plot organised among the Indians who originally owned the jewel,” says Mr. Franklin—“a plot with some old Hindoo superstition at the bottom of it. That is my opinion, confirmed by a family paper which I have about me at this moment.”\n\nI saw, now, why the appearance of the three Indian jugglers at our house had presented itself to Mr. Franklin in the light of a circumstance worth noting.\n\n“I don’t want to force my opinion on you,” Mr. Franklin went on. “The idea of certain chosen servants of an old Hindoo superstition devoting themselves, through all difficulties and dangers, to watching the opportunity of recovering their sacred gem, appears to me to be perfectly consistent with everything that we know of the patience of Oriental races, and the influence of Oriental religions. But then I am an imaginative man; and the butcher, the baker, and the tax-gatherer, are not the only credible realities in existence to my mind. Let the guess I have made at the truth in this matter go for what it is worth, and let us get on to the only practical question that concerns us. Does the conspiracy against the Moonstone survive the Colonel’s death? And did the Colonel know it, when he left the birthday gift to his niece?”\n\nI began to see my lady and Miss Rachel at the end of it all, now. Not a word he said escaped me.\n\n“I was not very willing, when I discovered the story of the Moonstone,” said Mr. Franklin, “to be the means of bringing it here. But Mr. Bruff reminded me that somebody must put my cousin’s legacy into my cousin’s hands—and that I might as well do it as anybody else. After taking the Diamond out of the bank, I fancied I was followed in the streets by a shabby, dark-complexioned man. I went to my father’s house to pick up my luggage, and found a letter there, which unexpectedly detained me in London. I went back to the bank with the Diamond, and thought I saw the shabby man again. Taking the Diamond once more out of the bank this morning, I saw the man for the third time, gave him the slip, and started (before he recovered the trace of me) by the morning instead of the afternoon train. Here I am, with the Diamond safe and sound—and what is the first news that meets me? I find that three strolling Indians have been at the house, and that my arrival from London, and something which I am expected to have about me, are two special objects of investigation to them when they believe themselves to be alone. I don’t waste time and words on their pouring the ink into the boy’s hand, and telling him to look in it for a man at a distance, and for something in that man’s pocket. The thing (which I have often seen done in the East) is ‘hocus-pocus’ in my opinion, as it is in yours. The present question for us to decide is, whether I am wrongly attaching a meaning to a mere accident? or whether we really have evidence of the Indians being on the track of the Moonstone, the moment it is removed from the safe keeping of the bank?”\n\nNeither he nor I seemed to fancy dealing with this part of the inquiry. We looked at each other, and then we looked at the tide, oozing in smoothly, higher and higher, over the Shivering Sand.\n\n“What are you thinking of?” says Mr. Franklin, suddenly.\n\n“I was thinking, sir,” I answered, “that I should like to shy the Diamond into the quicksand, and settle the question in that way.”\n\n“If you have got the value of the stone in your pocket,” answered Mr. Franklin, “say so, Betteredge, and in it goes!”\n\nIt’s curious to note, when your mind’s anxious, how very far in the way of relief a very small joke will go. We found a fund of merriment, at the time, in the notion of making away with Miss Rachel’s lawful property, and getting Mr. Blake, as executor, into dreadful trouble—though where the merriment was, I am quite at a loss to discover now.\n\nMr. Franklin was the first to bring the talk back to the talk’s proper purpose. He took an envelope out of his pocket, opened it, and handed to me the paper inside.\n\n“Betteredge,” he said, “we must face the question of the Colonel’s motive in leaving this legacy to his niece, for my aunt’s sake. Bear in mind how Lady Verinder treated her brother from the time when he returned to England, to the time when he told you he should remember his niece’s birthday. And read that.”\n\nHe gave me the extract from the Colonel’s Will. I have got it by me while I write these words; and I copy it, as follows, for your benefit:\n\n“Thirdly, and lastly, I give and bequeath to my niece, Rachel Verinder, daughter and only child of my sister, Julia Verinder, widow—if her mother, the said Julia Verinder, shall be living on the said Rachel Verinder’s next Birthday after my death—the yellow Diamond belonging to me, and known in the East by the name of The Moonstone: subject to this condition, that her mother, the said Julia Verinder, shall be living at the time. And I hereby desire my executor to give my Diamond, either by his own hands or by the hands of some trustworthy representative whom he shall appoint, into the personal possession of my said niece Rachel, on her next birthday after my death, and in the presence, if possible, of my sister, the said Julia Verinder. And I desire that my said sister may be informed, by means of a true copy of this, the third and last clause of my Will, that I give the Diamond to her daughter Rachel, in token of my free forgiveness of the injury which her conduct towards me has been the means of inflicting on my reputation in my lifetime; and especially in proof that I pardon, as becomes a dying man, the insult offered to me as an officer and a gentleman, when her servant, by her orders, closed the door of her house against me, on the occasion of her daughter’s birthday.”\n\nMore words followed these, providing if my lady was dead, or if Miss Rachel was dead, at the time of the testator’s decease, for the Diamond being sent to Holland, in accordance with the sealed instructions originally deposited with it. The proceeds of the sale were, in that case, to be added to the money already left by the Will for the professorship of chemistry at the university in the north.\n\nI handed the paper back to Mr. Franklin, sorely troubled what to say to him. Up to that moment, my own opinion had been (as you know) that the Colonel had died as wickedly as he had lived. I don’t say the copy from his Will actually converted me from that opinion: I only say it staggered me.\n\n“Well,” says Mr. Franklin, “now you have read the Colonel’s own statement, what do you say? In bringing the Moonstone to my aunt’s house, am I serving his vengeance blindfold, or am I vindicating him in the character of a penitent and Christian man?”\n\n“It seems hard to say, sir,” I answered, “that he died with a horrid revenge in his heart, and a horrid lie on his lips. God alone knows the truth. Don’t ask me.”\n\nMr. Franklin sat twisting and turning the extract from the Will in his fingers, as if he expected to squeeze the truth out of it in that manner. He altered quite remarkably, at the same time. From being brisk and bright, he now became, most unaccountably, a slow, solemn, and pondering young man.\n\n“This question has two sides,” he said. “An Objective side, and a Subjective side. Which are we to take?”\n\nHe had had a German education as well as a French. One of the two had been in undisturbed possession of him (as I supposed) up to this time. And now (as well as I could make out) the other was taking its place. It is one of my rules in life, never to notice what I don’t understand. I steered a middle course between the Objective side and the Subjective side. In plain English I stared hard, and said nothing.\n\n“Let’s extract the inner meaning of this,” says Mr. Franklin. “Why did my uncle leave the Diamond to Rachel? Why didn’t he leave it to my aunt?”\n\n“That’s not beyond guessing, sir, at any rate,” I said. “Colonel Herncastle knew my lady well enough to know that she would have refused to accept any legacy that came to her from him.”\n\n“How did he know that Rachel might not refuse to accept it, too?”\n\n“Is there any young lady in existence, sir, who could resist the temptation of accepting such a birthday present as The Moonstone?”\n\n“That’s the Subjective view,” says Mr. Franklin. “It does you great credit, Betteredge, to be able to take the Subjective view. But there’s another mystery about the Colonel’s legacy which is not accounted for yet. How are we to explain his only giving Rachel her birthday present conditionally on her mother being alive?”\n\n“I don’t want to slander a dead man, sir,” I answered. “But if he has purposely left a legacy of trouble and danger to his sister, by the means of her child, it must be a legacy made conditional on his sister’s being alive to feel the vexation of it.”\n\n“Oh! That’s your interpretation of his motive, is it? The Subjective interpretation again! Have you ever been in Germany, Betteredge?”\n\n“No, sir. What’s your interpretation, if you please?”\n\n“I can see,” says Mr. Franklin, “that the Colonel’s object may, quite possibly, have been—not to benefit his niece, whom he had never even seen—but to prove to his sister that he had died forgiving her, and to prove it very prettily by means of a present made to her child. There is a totally different explanation from yours, Betteredge, taking its rise in a Subjective-Objective point of view. From all I can see, one interpretation is just as likely to be right as the other.”\n\nHaving brought matters to this pleasant and comforting issue, Mr. Franklin appeared to think that he had completed all that was required of him. He laid down flat on his back on the sand, and asked what was to be done next.\n\nHe had been so clever, and clear-headed (before he began to talk the foreign gibberish), and had so completely taken the lead in the business up to the present time, that I was quite unprepared for such a sudden change as he now exhibited in this helpless leaning upon me. It was not till later that I learned—by assistance of Miss Rachel, who was the first to make the discovery—that these puzzling shifts and transformations in Mr. Franklin were due to the effect on him of his foreign training. At the age when we are all of us most apt to take our colouring, in the form of a reflection from the colouring of other people, he had been sent abroad, and had been passed on from one nation to another, before there was time for any one colouring more than another to settle itself on him firmly. As a consequence of this, he had come back with so many different sides to his character, all more or less jarring with each other, that he seemed to pass his life in a state of perpetual contradiction with himself. He could be a busy man, and a lazy man; cloudy in the head, and clear in the head; a model of determination, and a spectacle of helplessness, all together. He had his French side, and his German side, and his Italian side—the original English foundation showing through, every now and then, as much as to say, “Here I am, sorely transmogrified, as you see, but there’s something of me left at the bottom of him still.” Miss Rachel used to remark that the Italian side of him was uppermost, on those occasions when he unexpectedly gave in, and asked you in his nice sweet-tempered way to take his own responsibilities on your shoulders. You will do him no injustice, I think, if you conclude that the Italian side of him was uppermost now.\n\n“Isn’t it your business, sir,” I asked, “to know what to do next? Surely it can’t be mine?”\n\nMr. Franklin didn’t appear to see the force of my question—not being in a position, at the time, to see anything but the sky over his head.\n\n“I don’t want to alarm my aunt without reason,” he said. “And I don’t want to leave her without what may be a needful warning. If you were in my place, Betteredge, tell me, in one word, what would you do?”\n\nIn one word, I told him: “Wait.”\n\n“With all my heart,” says Mr. Franklin. “How long?”\n\nI proceeded to explain myself.\n\n“As I understand it, sir,” I said, “somebody is bound to put this plaguy Diamond into Miss Rachel’s hands on her birthday—and you may as well do it as another. Very good. This is the twenty-fifth of May, and the birthday is on the twenty-first of June. We have got close on four weeks before us. Let’s wait and see what happens in that time; and let’s warn my lady, or not, as the circumstances direct us.”\n\n“Perfect, Betteredge, as far as it goes!” says Mr. Franklin. “But between this and the birthday, what’s to be done with the Diamond?”\n\n“What your father did with it, to be sure, sir!” I answered. “Your father put it in the safe keeping of a bank in London. You put in the safe keeping of the bank at Frizinghall.” (Frizinghall was our nearest town, and the Bank of England wasn’t safer than the bank there.) “If I were you, sir,” I added, “I would ride straight away with it to Frizinghall before the ladies come back.”\n\nThe prospect of doing something—and, what is more, of doing that something on a horse—brought Mr. Franklin up like lightning from the flat of his back. He sprang to his feet, and pulled me up, without ceremony, on to mine. “Betteredge, you are worth your weight in gold,” he said. “Come along, and saddle the best horse in the stables directly.”\n\nHere (God bless it!) was the original English foundation of him showing through all the foreign varnish at last! Here was the Master Franklin I remembered, coming out again in the good old way at the prospect of a ride, and reminding me of the good old times! Saddle a horse for him? I would have saddled a dozen horses, if he could only have ridden them all!\n\nWe went back to the house in a hurry; we had the fleetest horse in the stables saddled in a hurry; and Mr. Franklin rattled off in a hurry, to lodge the cursed Diamond once more in the strongroom of a bank. When I heard the last of his horse’s hoofs on the drive, and when I turned about in the yard and found I was alone again, I felt half inclined to ask myself if I hadn’t woke up from a dream.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nWhile I was in this bewildered frame of mind, sorely needing a little quiet time by myself to put me right again, my daughter Penelope got in my way (just as her late mother used to get in my way on the stairs), and instantly summoned me to tell her all that had passed at the conference between Mr. Franklin and me. Under present circumstances, the one thing to be done was to clap the extinguisher upon Penelope’s curiosity on the spot. I accordingly replied that Mr. Franklin and I had both talked of foreign politics, till we could talk no longer, and had then mutually fallen asleep in the heat of the sun. Try that sort of answer when your wife or your daughter next worries you with an awkward question at an awkward time, and depend on the natural sweetness of women for kissing and making it up again at the next opportunity.\n\nThe afternoon wore on, and my lady and Miss Rachel came back.\n\nNeedless to say how astonished they were, when they heard that Mr. Franklin Blake had arrived, and had gone off again on horseback. Needless also to say, that they asked awkward questions directly, and that the “foreign politics” and the “falling asleep in the sun” wouldn’t serve a second time over with them. Being at the end of my invention, I said Mr. Franklin’s arrival by the early train was entirely attributable to one of Mr. Franklin’s freaks. Being asked, upon that, whether his galloping off again on horseback was another of Mr. Franklin’s freaks, I said, “Yes, it was;” and slipped out of it—I think very cleverly—in that way.\n\nHaving got over my difficulties with the ladies, I found more difficulties waiting for me when I went back to my own room. In came Penelope—with the natural sweetness of women—to kiss and make it up again; and—with the natural curiosity of women—to ask another question. This time she only wanted me to tell her what was the matter with our second housemaid, Rosanna Spearman.\n\nAfter leaving Mr. Franklin and me at the Shivering Sand, Rosanna, it appeared, had returned to the house in a very unaccountable state of mind. She had turned (if Penelope was to be believed) all the colours of the rainbow. She had been merry without reason, and sad without reason. In one breath she asked hundreds of questions about Mr. Franklin Blake, and in another breath she had been angry with Penelope for presuming to suppose that a strange gentleman could possess any interest for her. She had been surprised, smiling, and scribbling Mr. Franklin’s name inside her workbox. She had been surprised again, crying and looking at her deformed shoulder in the glass. Had she and Mr. Franklin known anything of each other before today? Quite impossible! Had they heard anything of each other? Impossible again! I could speak to Mr. Franklin’s astonishment as genuine, when he saw how the girl stared at him. Penelope could speak to the girl’s inquisitiveness as genuine, when she asked questions about Mr. Franklin. The conference between us, conducted in this way, was tiresome enough, until my daughter suddenly ended it by bursting out with what I thought the most monstrous supposition I had ever heard in my life.\n\n“Father!” says Penelope, quite seriously, “there’s only one explanation of it. Rosanna has fallen in love with Mr. Franklin Blake at first sight!”\n\nYou have heard of beautiful young ladies falling in love at first sight, and have thought it natural enough. But a housemaid out of a reformatory, with a plain face and a deformed shoulder, falling in love, at first sight, with a gentleman who comes on a visit to her mistress’s house, match me that, in the way of an absurdity, out of any story-book in Christendom, if you can! I laughed till the tears rolled down my cheeks. Penelope resented my merriment, in rather a strange way. “I never knew you cruel before, father,” she said, very gently, and went out.\n\nMy girl’s words fell upon me like a splash of cold water. I was savage with myself, for feeling uneasy in myself the moment she had spoken them—but so it was. We will change the subject, if you please. I am sorry I drifted into writing about it; and not without reason, as you will see when we have gone on together a little longer.\n\nThe evening came, and the dressing-bell for dinner rang, before Mr. Franklin returned from Frizinghall. I took his hot water up to his room myself, expecting to hear, after this extraordinary delay, that something had happened. To my great disappointment (and no doubt to yours also), nothing had happened. He had not met with the Indians, either going or returning. He had deposited the Moonstone in the bank—describing it merely as a valuable of great price—and he had got the receipt for it safe in his pocket. I went downstairs, feeling that this was rather a flat ending, after all our excitement about the Diamond earlier in the day.\n\nHow the meeting between Mr. Franklin and his aunt and cousin went off, is more than I can tell you.\n\nI would have given something to have waited at table that day. But, in my position in the household, waiting at dinner (except on high family festivals) was letting down my dignity in the eyes of the other servants—a thing which my lady considered me quite prone enough to do already, without seeking occasions for it. The news brought to me from the upper regions, that evening, came from Penelope and the footman. Penelope mentioned that she had never known Miss Rachel so particular about the dressing of her hair, and had never seen her look so bright and pretty as she did when she went down to meet Mr. Franklin in the drawing-room. The footman’s report was, that the preservation of a respectful composure in the presence of his betters, and the waiting on Mr. Franklin Blake at dinner, were two of the hardest things to reconcile with each other that had ever tried his training in service. Later in the evening, we heard them singing and playing duets, Mr. Franklin piping high, Miss Rachel piping higher, and my lady, on the piano, following them as it were over hedge and ditch, and seeing them safe through it in a manner most wonderful and pleasant to hear through the open windows, on the terrace at night. Later still, I went to Mr. Franklin in the smoking-room, with the soda water and brandy, and found that Miss Rachel had put the Diamond clean out of his head. “She’s the most charming girl I have seen since I came back to England!” was all I could extract from him, when I endeavoured to lead the conversation to more serious things.\n\nTowards midnight, I went round the house to lock up, accompanied by my second in command (Samuel, the footman), as usual. When all the doors were made fast, except the side door that opened on the terrace, I sent Samuel to bed, and stepped out for a breath of fresh air before I too went to bed in my turn.\n\nThe night was still and close, and the moon was at the full in the heavens. It was so silent out of doors, that I heard from time to time, very faint and low, the fall of the sea, as the ground-swell heaved it in on the sand-bank near the mouth of our little bay. As the house stood, the terrace side was the dark side; but the broad moonlight showed fair on the gravel walk that ran along the next side to the terrace. Looking this way, after looking up at the sky, I saw the shadow of a person in the moonlight thrown forward from behind the corner of the house.\n\nBeing old and sly, I forbore to call out; but being also, unfortunately, old and heavy, my feet betrayed me on the gravel. Before I could steal suddenly round the corner, as I had proposed, I heard lighter feet than mine—and more than one pair of them as I thought—retreating in a hurry. By the time I had got to the corner, the trespassers, whoever they were, had run into the shrubbery at the off side of the walk, and were hidden from sight among the thick trees and bushes in that part of the grounds. From the shrubbery, they could easily make their way, over our fence into the road. If I had been forty years younger, I might have had a chance of catching them before they got clear of our premises. As it was, I went back to set a-going a younger pair of legs than mine. Without disturbing anybody, Samuel and I got a couple of guns, and went all round the house and through the shrubbery. Having made sure that no persons were lurking about anywhere in our grounds, we turned back. Passing over the walk where I had seen the shadow, I now noticed, for the first time, a little bright object, lying on the clean gravel, under the light of the moon. Picking the object up, I discovered it was a small bottle, containing a thick sweet-smelling liquor, as black as ink.\n\nI said nothing to Samuel. But, remembering what Penelope had told me about the jugglers, and the pouring of the little pool of ink into the palm of the boy’s hand, I instantly suspected that I had disturbed the three Indians, lurking about the house, and bent, in their heathenish way, on discovering the whereabouts of the Diamond that night.\n\nHere, for one moment, I find it necessary to call a halt.\n\nOn summoning up my own recollections—and on getting Penelope to help me, by consulting her journal—I find that we may pass pretty rapidly over the interval between Mr. Franklin Blake’s arrival and Miss Rachel’s birthday. For the greater part of that time the days passed, and brought nothing with them worth recording. With your good leave, then, and with Penelope’s help, I shall notice certain dates only in this place; reserving to myself to tell the story day by day, once more, as soon as we get to the time when the business of the Moonstone became the chief business of everybody in our house.\n\nThis said, we may now go on again—beginning, of course, with the bottle of sweet-smelling ink which I found on the gravel walk at night.\n\nOn the next morning (the morning of the twenty-sixth) I showed Mr. Franklin this article of jugglery, and told him what I have already told you. His opinion was, not only that the Indians had been lurking about after the Diamond, but also that they were actually foolish enough to believe in their own magic—meaning thereby the making of signs on a boy’s head, and the pouring of ink into a boy’s hand, and then expecting him to see persons and things beyond the reach of human vision. In our country, as well as in the East, Mr. Franklin informed me, there are people who practise this curious hocus-pocus (without the ink, however); and who call it by a French name, signifying something like brightness of sight. “Depend upon it,” says Mr. Franklin, “the Indians took it for granted that we should keep the Diamond here; and they brought their clairvoyant boy to show them the way to it, if they succeeded in getting into the house last night.”\n\n“Do you think they’ll try again, sir?” I asked.\n\n“It depends,” says Mr. Franklin, “on what the boy can really do. If he can see the Diamond through the iron safe of the bank at Frizinghall, we shall be troubled with no more visits from the Indians for the present. If he can’t, we shall have another chance of catching them in the shrubbery, before many more nights are over our heads.”\n\nI waited pretty confidently for that latter chance; but, strange to relate, it never came.\n\nWhether the jugglers heard, in the town, of Mr. Franklin having been seen at the bank, and drew their conclusions accordingly; or whether the boy really did see the Diamond where the Diamond was now lodged (which I, for one, flatly disbelieve); or whether, after all, it was a mere effect of chance, this at any rate is the plain truth—not the ghost of an Indian came near the house again, through the weeks that passed before Miss Rachel’s birthday. The jugglers remained in and about the town plying their trade; and Mr. Franklin and I remained waiting to see what might happen, and resolute not to put the rogues on their guard by showing our suspicions of them too soon. With this report of the proceedings on either side, ends all that I have to say about the Indians for the present.\n\nOn the twenty-ninth of the month, Miss Rachel and Mr. Franklin hit on a new method of working their way together through the time which might otherwise have hung heavy on their hands. There are reasons for taking particular notice here of the occupation that amused them. You will find it has a bearing on something that is still to come.\n\nGentlefolks in general have a very awkward rock ahead in life—the rock ahead of their own idleness. Their lives being, for the most part, passed in looking about them for something to do, it is curious to see—especially when their tastes are of what is called the intellectual sort—how often they drift blindfold into some nasty pursuit. Nine times out of ten they take to torturing something, or to spoiling something—and they firmly believe they are improving their minds, when the plain truth is, they are only making a mess in the house. I have seen them (ladies, I am sorry to say, as well as gentlemen) go out, day after day, for example, with empty pill-boxes, and catch newts, and beetles, and spiders, and frogs, and come home and stick pins through the miserable wretches, or cut them up, without a pang of remorse, into little pieces. You see my young master, or my young mistress, poring over one of their spiders’ insides with a magnifying-glass; or you meet one of their frogs walking downstairs without his head—and when you wonder what this cruel nastiness means, you are told that it means a taste in my young master or my young mistress for natural history. Sometimes, again, you see them occupied for hours together in spoiling a pretty flower with pointed instruments, out of a stupid curiosity to know what the flower is made of. Is its colour any prettier, or its scent any sweeter, when you do know? But there! the poor souls must get through the time, you see—they must get through the time. You dabbled in nasty mud, and made pies, when you were a child; and you dabble in nasty science, and dissect spiders, and spoil flowers, when you grow up. In the one case and in the other, the secret of it is, that you have got nothing to think of in your poor empty head, and nothing to do with your poor idle hands. And so it ends in your spoiling canvas with paints, and making a smell in the house; or in keeping tadpoles in a glass box full of dirty water, and turning everybody’s stomach in the house; or in chipping off bits of stone here, there, and everywhere, and dropping grit into all the victuals in the house; or in staining your fingers in the pursuit of photography, and doing justice without mercy on everybody’s face in the house. It often falls heavy enough, no doubt, on people who are really obliged to get their living, to be forced to work for the clothes that cover them, the roof that shelters them, and the food that keeps them going. But compare the hardest day’s work you ever did with the idleness that splits flowers and pokes its way into spiders’ stomachs, and thank your stars that your head has got something it must think of, and your hands something that they must do.\n\nAs for Mr. Franklin and Miss Rachel, they tortured nothing, I am glad to say. They simply confined themselves to making a mess; and all they spoilt, to do them justice, was the panelling of a door.\n\nMr. Franklin’s universal genius, dabbling in everything, dabbled in what he called “decorative painting.” He had invented, he informed us, a new mixture to moisten paint with, which he described as a “vehicle.” What it was made of, I don’t know. What it did, I can tell you in two words—it stank. Miss Rachel being wild to try her hand at the new process, Mr. Franklin sent to London for the materials; mixed them up, with accompaniment of a smell which made the very dogs sneeze when they came into the room; put an apron and a bib over Miss Rachel’s gown, and set her to work decorating her own little sitting-room—called, for want of English to name it in, her “boudoir.” They began with the inside of the door. Mr. Franklin scraped off all the nice varnish with pumice-stone, and made what he described as a surface to work on. Miss Rachel then covered the surface, under his directions and with his help, with patterns and devices—griffins, birds, flowers, cupids, and such like—copied from designs made by a famous Italian painter, whose name escapes me: the one, I mean, who stocked the world with Virgin Maries, and had a sweetheart at the baker’s. Viewed as work, this decoration was slow to do, and dirty to deal with. But our young lady and gentleman never seemed to tire of it. When they were not riding, or seeing company, or taking their meals, or piping their songs, there they were with their heads together, as busy as bees, spoiling the door. Who was the poet who said that Satan finds some mischief still for idle hands to do? If he had occupied my place in the family, and had seen Miss Rachel with her brush, and Mr. Franklin with his vehicle, he could have written nothing truer of either of them than that.\n\nThe next date worthy of notice is Sunday the fourth of June.\n\nOn that evening we, in the servants’ hall, debated a domestic question for the first time, which, like the decoration of the door, has its bearing on something that is still to come.\n\nSeeing the pleasure which Mr. Franklin and Miss Rachel took in each other’s society, and noting what a pretty match they were in all personal respects, we naturally speculated on the chance of their putting their heads together with other objects in view besides the ornamenting of a door. Some of us said there would be a wedding in the house before the summer was over. Others (led by me) admitted it was likely enough Miss Rachel might be married; but we doubted (for reasons which will presently appear) whether her bridegroom would be Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nThat Mr. Franklin was in love, on his side, nobody who saw and heard him could doubt. The difficulty was to fathom Miss Rachel. Let me do myself the honour of making you acquainted with her; after which, I will leave you to fathom for yourself—if you can.\n\nMy young lady’s eighteenth birthday was the birthday now coming, on the twenty-first of June. If you happen to like dark women (who, I am informed, have gone out of fashion latterly in the gay world), and if you have no particular prejudice in favour of size, I answer for Miss Rachel as one of the prettiest girls your eyes ever looked on. She was small and slim, but all in fine proportion from top to toe. To see her sit down, to see her get up, and specially to see her walk, was enough to satisfy any man in his senses that the graces of her figure (if you will pardon me the expression) were in her flesh and not in her clothes. Her hair was the blackest I ever saw. Her eyes matched her hair. Her nose was not quite large enough, I admit. Her mouth and chin were (to quote Mr. Franklin) morsels for the gods; and her complexion (on the same undeniable authority) was as warm as the sun itself, with this great advantage over the sun, that it was always in nice order to look at. Add to the foregoing that she carried her head as upright as a dart, in a dashing, spirited, thoroughbred way—that she had a clear voice, with a ring of the right metal in it, and a smile that began very prettily in her eyes before it got to her lips—and there behold the portrait of her, to the best of my painting, as large as life!\n\nAnd what about her disposition next? Had this charming creature no faults? She had just as many faults as you have, ma’am—neither more nor less.\n\nTo put it seriously, my dear pretty Miss Rachel, possessing a host of graces and attractions, had one defect, which strict impartiality compels me to acknowledge. She was unlike most other girls of her age, in this—that she had ideas of her own, and was stiff-necked enough to set the fashions themselves at defiance, if the fashions didn’t suit her views. In trifles, this independence of hers was all well enough; but in matters of importance, it carried her (as my lady thought, and as I thought) too far. She judged for herself, as few women of twice her age judge in general; never asked your advice; never told you beforehand what she was going to do; never came with secrets and confidences to anybody, from her mother downwards. In little things and great, with people she loved, and people she hated (and she did both with equal heartiness), Miss Rachel always went on a way of her own, sufficient for herself in the joys and sorrows of her life. Over and over again I have heard my lady say, “Rachel’s best friend and Rachel’s worst enemy are, one and the other—Rachel herself.”\n\nAdd one thing more to this, and I have done.\n\nWith all her secrecy, and self-will, there was not so much as the shadow of anything false in her. I never remember her breaking her word; I never remember her saying No, and meaning Yes. I can call to mind, in her childhood, more than one occasion when the good little soul took the blame, and suffered the punishment, for some fault committed by a playfellow whom she loved. Nobody ever knew her to confess to it, when the thing was found out, and she was charged with it afterwards. But nobody ever knew her to lie about it, either. She looked you straight in the face, and shook her little saucy head, and said plainly, “I won’t tell you!” Punished again for this, she would own to being sorry for saying “won’t;” but, bread and water notwithstanding, she never told you. Self-willed—devilish self-willed sometimes—I grant; but the finest creature, nevertheless, that ever walked the ways of this lower world. Perhaps you think you see a certain contradiction here? In that case, a word in your ear. Study your wife closely, for the next four-and-twenty hours. If your good lady doesn’t exhibit something in the shape of a contradiction in that time, Heaven help you!—you have married a monster.\n\nI have now brought you acquainted with Miss Rachel, which you will find puts us face to face, next, with the question of that young lady’s matrimonial views.\n\nOn June the twelfth, an invitation from my mistress was sent to a gentleman in London, to come and help to keep Miss Rachel’s birthday. This was the fortunate individual on whom I believed her heart to be privately set! Like Mr. Franklin, he was a cousin of hers. His name was Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.\n\nMy lady’s second sister (don’t be alarmed; we are not going very deep into family matters this time)—my lady’s second sister, I say, had a disappointment in love; and taking a husband afterwards, on the neck or nothing principle, made what they call a misalliance. There was terrible work in the family when the Honourable Caroline insisted on marrying plain Mr. Ablewhite, the banker at Frizinghall. He was very rich and very respectable, and he begot a prodigious large family—all in his favour, so far. But he had presumed to raise himself from a low station in the world—and that was against him. However, Time and the progress of modern enlightenment put things right; and the misalliance passed muster very well. We are all getting liberal now; and (provided you can scratch me, if I scratch you) what do I care, in or out of Parliament, whether you are a Dustman or a Duke? That’s the modern way of looking at it—and I keep up with the modern way. The Ablewhites lived in a fine house and grounds, a little out of Frizinghall. Very worthy people, and greatly respected in the neighbourhood. We shall not be much troubled with them in these pages—excepting Mr. Godfrey, who was Mr. Ablewhite’s second son, and who must take his proper place here, if you please, for Miss Rachel’s sake.\n\nWith all his brightness and cleverness and general good qualities, Mr. Franklin’s chance of topping Mr. Godfrey in our young lady’s estimation was, in my opinion, a very poor chance indeed.\n\nIn the first place, Mr. Godfrey was, in point of size, the finest man by far of the two. He stood over six feet high; he had a beautiful red and white colour; a smooth round face, shaved as bare as your hand; and a head of lovely long flaxen hair, falling negligently over the poll of his neck. But why do I try to give you this personal description of him? If you ever subscribed to a Ladies’ Charity in London, you know Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite as well as I do. He was a barrister by profession; a ladies’ man by temperament; and a good Samaritan by choice. Female benevolence and female destitution could do nothing without him. Maternal societies for confining poor women; Magdalen societies for rescuing poor women; strong-minded societies for putting poor women into poor men’s places, and leaving the men to shift for themselves;—he was vice-president, manager, referee to them all. Wherever there was a table with a committee of ladies sitting round it in council there was Mr. Godfrey at the bottom of the board, keeping the temper of the committee, and leading the dear creatures along the thorny ways of business, hat in hand. I do suppose this was the most accomplished philanthropist (on a small independence) that England ever produced. As a speaker at charitable meetings the like of him for drawing your tears and your money was not easy to find. He was quite a public character. The last time I was in London, my mistress gave me two treats. She sent me to the theatre to see a dancing woman who was all the rage; and she sent me to Exeter Hall to hear Mr. Godfrey. The lady did it, with a band of music. The gentleman did it, with a handkerchief and a glass of water. Crowds at the performance with the legs. Ditto at the performance with the tongue. And with all this, the sweetest tempered person (I allude to Mr. Godfrey)—the simplest and pleasantest and easiest to please—you ever met with. He loved everybody. And everybody loved him. What chance had Mr. Franklin—what chance had anybody of average reputation and capacities—against such a man as this?\n\nOn the fourteenth, came Mr. Godfrey’s answer.\n\nHe accepted my mistress’s invitation, from the Wednesday of the birthday to the evening of Friday—when his duties to the Ladies’ Charities would oblige him to return to town. He also enclosed a copy of verses on what he elegantly called his cousin’s “natal day.” Miss Rachel, I was informed, joined Mr. Franklin in making fun of the verses at dinner; and Penelope, who was all on Mr. Franklin’s side, asked me, in great triumph, what I thought of that. “Miss Rachel has led you off on a false scent, my dear,” I replied; “but my nose is not so easily mystified. Wait till Mr. Ablewhite’s verses are followed by Mr. Ablewhite himself.”\n\nMy daughter replied, that Mr. Franklin might strike in, and try his luck, before the verses were followed by the poet. In favour of this view, I must acknowledge that Mr. Franklin left no chance untried of winning Miss Rachel’s good graces.\n\nThough one of the most inveterate smokers I ever met with, he gave up his cigar, because she said, one day, she hated the stale smell of it in his clothes. He slept so badly, after this effort of self-denial, for want of the composing effect of the tobacco to which he was used, and came down morning after morning looking so haggard and worn, that Miss Rachel herself begged him to take to his cigars again. No! he would take to nothing again that could cause her a moment’s annoyance; he would fight it out resolutely, and get back his sleep, sooner or later, by main force of patience in waiting for it. Such devotion as this, you may say (as some of them said downstairs), could never fail of producing the right effect on Miss Rachel—backed up, too, as it was, by the decorating work every day on the door. All very well—but she had a photograph of Mr. Godfrey in her bedroom; represented speaking at a public meeting, with all his hair blown out by the breath of his own eloquence, and his eyes, most lovely, charming the money out of your pockets. What do you say to that? Every morning—as Penelope herself owned to me—there was the man whom the women couldn’t do without, looking on, in effigy, while Miss Rachel was having her hair combed. He would be looking on, in reality, before long—that was my opinion of it.\n\nJune the sixteenth brought an event which made Mr. Franklin’s chance look, to my mind, a worse chance than ever.\n\nA strange gentleman, speaking English with a foreign accent, came that morning to the house, and asked to see Mr. Franklin Blake on business. The business could not possibly have been connected with the Diamond, for these two reasons—first, that Mr. Franklin told me nothing about it; secondly, that he communicated it (when the gentleman had gone, as I suppose) to my lady. She probably hinted something about it next to her daughter. At any rate, Miss Rachel was reported to have said some severe things to Mr. Franklin, at the piano that evening, about the people he had lived among, and the principles he had adopted in foreign parts. The next day, for the first time, nothing was done towards the decoration of the door. I suspect some imprudence of Mr. Franklin’s on the Continent—with a woman or a debt at the bottom of it—had followed him to England. But that is all guesswork. In this case, not only Mr. Franklin, but my lady too, for a wonder, left me in the dark.\n\nOn the seventeenth, to all appearance, the cloud passed away again. They returned to their decorating work on the door, and seemed to be as good friends as ever. If Penelope was to be believed, Mr. Franklin had seized the opportunity of the reconciliation to make an offer to Miss Rachel, and had neither been accepted nor refused. My girl was sure (from signs and tokens which I need not trouble you with) that her young mistress had fought Mr. Franklin off by declining to believe that he was in earnest, and had then secretly regretted treating him in that way afterwards. Though Penelope was admitted to more familiarity with her young mistress than maids generally are—for the two had been almost brought up together as children—still I knew Miss Rachel’s reserved character too well to believe that she would show her mind to anybody in this way. What my daughter told me, on the present occasion, was, as I suspected, more what she wished than what she really knew.\n\nOn the nineteenth another event happened. We had the doctor in the house professionally. He was summoned to prescribe for a person whom I have had occasion to present to you in these pages—our second housemaid, Rosanna Spearman.\n\nThis poor girl—who had puzzled me, as you know already, at the Shivering Sand—puzzled me more than once again, in the interval time of which I am now writing. Penelope’s notion that her fellow-servant was in love with Mr. Franklin (which my daughter, by my orders, kept strictly secret) seemed to be just as absurd as ever. But I must own that what I myself saw, and what my daughter saw also, of our second housemaid’s conduct, began to look mysterious, to say the least of it.\n\nFor example, the girl constantly put herself in Mr. Franklin’s way—very slyly and quietly, but she did it. He took about as much notice of her as he took of the cat; it never seemed to occur to him to waste a look on Rosanna’s plain face. The poor thing’s appetite, never much, fell away dreadfully; and her eyes in the morning showed plain signs of waking and crying at night. One day Penelope made an awkward discovery, which we hushed up on the spot. She caught Rosanna at Mr. Franklin’s dressing-table, secretly removing a rose which Miss Rachel had given him to wear in his button-hole, and putting another rose like it, of her own picking, in its place. She was, after that, once or twice impudent to me, when I gave her a well-meant general hint to be careful in her conduct; and, worse still, she was not over-respectful now, on the few occasions when Miss Rachel accidentally spoke to her.\n\nMy lady noticed the change, and asked me what I thought about it. I tried to screen the girl by answering that I thought she was out of health; and it ended in the doctor being sent for, as already mentioned, on the nineteenth. He said it was her nerves, and doubted if she was fit for service. My lady offered to remove her for change of air to one of our farms, inland. She begged and prayed, with the tears in her eyes, to be let to stop; and, in an evil hour, I advised my lady to try her for a little longer. As the event proved, and as you will soon see, this was the worst advice I could have given. If I could only have looked a little way into the future, I would have taken Rosanna Spearman out of the house, then and there, with my own hand.\n\nOn the twentieth, there came a note from Mr. Godfrey. He had arranged to stop at Frizinghall that night, having occasion to consult his father on business. On the afternoon of the next day, he and his two eldest sisters would ride over to us on horseback, in good time before dinner. An elegant little casket in china accompanied the note, presented to Miss Rachel, with her cousin’s love and best wishes. Mr. Franklin had only given her a plain locket not worth half the money. My daughter Penelope, nevertheless—such is the obstinacy of women—still backed him to win.\n\nThanks be to Heaven, we have arrived at the eve of the birthday at last! You will own, I think, that I have got you over the ground this time, without much loitering by the way. Cheer up! I’ll ease you with another new chapter here—and, what is more, that chapter shall take you straight into the thick of the story.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\nJune twenty-first, the day of the birthday, was cloudy and unsettled at sunrise, but towards noon it cleared up bravely.\n\nWe, in the servants’ hall, began this happy anniversary, as usual, by offering our little presents to Miss Rachel, with the regular speech delivered annually by me as the chief. I follow the plan adopted by the Queen in opening Parliament—namely, the plan of saying much the same thing regularly every year. Before it is delivered, my speech (like the Queen’s) is looked for as eagerly as if nothing of the kind had ever been heard before. When it is delivered, and turns out not to be the novelty anticipated, though they grumble a little, they look forward hopefully to something newer next year. An easy people to govern, in the Parliament and in the Kitchen—that’s the moral of it.\n\nAfter breakfast, Mr. Franklin and I had a private conference on the subject of the Moonstone—the time having now come for removing it from the bank at Frizinghall, and placing it in Miss Rachel’s own hands.\n\nWhether he had been trying to make love to his cousin again, and had got a rebuff—or whether his broken rest, night after night, was aggravating the queer contradictions and uncertainties in his character—I don’t know. But certain it is, that Mr. Franklin failed to show himself at his best on the morning of the birthday. He was in twenty different minds about the Diamond in as many minutes. For my part, I stuck fast by the plain facts as we knew them. Nothing had happened to justify us in alarming my lady on the subject of the jewel; and nothing could alter the legal obligation that now lay on Mr. Franklin to put it in his cousin’s possession. That was my view of the matter; and, twist and turn it as he might, he was forced in the end to make it his view too. We arranged that he was to ride over, after lunch, to Frizinghall, and bring the Diamond back, with Mr. Godfrey and the two young ladies, in all probability, to keep him company on the way home again.\n\nThis settled, our young gentleman went back to Miss Rachel.\n\nThey consumed the whole morning, and part of the afternoon, in the everlasting business of decorating the door, Penelope standing by to mix the colours, as directed; and my lady, as luncheon time drew near, going in and out of the room, with her handkerchief to her nose (for they used a deal of Mr. Franklin’s vehicle that day), and trying vainly to get the two artists away from their work. It was three o’clock before they took off their aprons, and released Penelope (much the worse for the vehicle), and cleaned themselves of their mess. But they had done what they wanted—they had finished the door on the birthday, and proud enough they were of it. The griffins, cupids, and so on, were, I must own, most beautiful to behold; though so many in number, so entangled in flowers and devices, and so topsy-turvy in their actions and attitudes, that you felt them unpleasantly in your head for hours after you had done with the pleasure of looking at them. If I add that Penelope ended her part of the morning’s work by being sick in the back-kitchen, it is in no unfriendly spirit towards the vehicle. No! no! It left off stinking when it dried; and if Art requires these sort of sacrifices—though the girl is my own daughter—I say, let Art have them!\n\nMr. Franklin snatched a morsel from the luncheon-table, and rode off to Frizinghall—to escort his cousins, as he told my lady. To fetch the Moonstone, as was privately known to himself and to me.\n\nThis being one of the high festivals on which I took my place at the side-board, in command of the attendance at table, I had plenty to occupy my mind while Mr. Franklin was away. Having seen to the wine, and reviewed my men and women who were to wait at dinner, I retired to collect myself before the company came. A whiff of—you know what, and a turn at a certain book which I have had occasion to mention in these pages, composed me, body and mind. I was aroused from what I am inclined to think must have been, not a nap, but a reverie, by the clatter of horses’ hoofs outside; and, going to the door, received a cavalcade comprising Mr. Franklin and his three cousins, escorted by one of old Mr. Ablewhite’s grooms.\n\nMr. Godfrey struck me, strangely enough, as being like Mr. Franklin in this respect—that he did not seem to be in his customary spirits. He kindly shook hands with me as usual, and was most politely glad to see his old friend Betteredge wearing so well. But there was a sort of cloud over him, which I couldn’t at all account for; and when I asked how he had found his father in health, he answered rather shortly, “Much as usual.” However, the two Miss Ablewhites were cheerful enough for twenty, which more than restored the balance. They were nearly as big as their brother; spanking, yellow-haired, rosy lasses, overflowing with super-abundant flesh and blood; bursting from head to foot with health and spirits. The legs of the poor horses trembled with carrying them; and when they jumped from their saddles (without waiting to be helped), I declare they bounced on the ground as if they were made of india-rubber. Everything the Miss Ablewhites said began with a large O; everything they did was done with a bang; and they giggled and screamed, in season and out of season, on the smallest provocation. Bouncers—that’s what I call them.\n\nUnder cover of the noise made by the young ladies, I had an opportunity of saying a private word to Mr. Franklin in the hall.\n\n“Have you got the Diamond safe, sir?”\n\nHe nodded, and tapped the breast-pocket of his coat.\n\n“Have you seen anything of the Indians?”\n\n“Not a glimpse.” With that answer, he asked for my lady, and, hearing she was in the small drawing-room, went there straight. The bell rang, before he had been a minute in the room, and Penelope was sent to tell Miss Rachel that Mr. Franklin Blake wanted to speak to her.\n\nCrossing the hall, about half an hour afterwards, I was brought to a sudden standstill by an outbreak of screams from the small drawing-room. I can’t say I was at all alarmed; for I recognised in the screams the favourite large O of the Miss Ablewhites. However, I went in (on pretence of asking for instructions about the dinner) to discover whether anything serious had really happened.\n\nThere stood Miss Rachel at the table, like a person fascinated, with the Colonel’s unlucky Diamond in her hand. There, on either side of her, knelt the two Bouncers, devouring the jewel with their eyes, and screaming with ecstasy every time it flashed on them in a new light. There, at the opposite side of the table, stood Mr. Godfrey, clapping his hands like a large child, and singing out softly, “Exquisite! exquisite!” There sat Mr. Franklin in a chair by the bookcase, tugging at his beard, and looking anxiously towards the window. And there, at the window, stood the object he was contemplating—my lady, having the extract from the Colonel’s Will in her hand, and keeping her back turned on the whole of the company.\n\nShe faced me, when I asked for my instructions; and I saw the family frown gathering over her eyes, and the family temper twitching at the corners of her mouth.\n\n“Come to my room in half an hour,” she answered. “I shall have something to say to you then.”\n\nWith those words, she went out. It was plain enough that she was posed by the same difficulty which had posed Mr. Franklin and me in our conference at the Shivering Sand. Was the legacy of the Moonstone a proof that she had treated her brother with cruel injustice? or was it a proof that he was worse than the worst she had ever thought of him? Serious questions those for my lady to determine, while her daughter, innocent of all knowledge of the Colonel’s character, stood there with the Colonel’s birthday gift in her hand.\n\nBefore I could leave the room in my turn, Miss Rachel, always considerate to the old servant who had been in the house when she was born, stopped me. “Look, Gabriel!” she said, and flashed the jewel before my eyes in a ray of sunlight that poured through the window.\n\nLord bless us! it was a Diamond! As large, or nearly, as a plover’s egg! The light that streamed from it was like the light of the harvest moon. When you looked down into the stone, you looked into a yellow deep that drew your eyes into it so that they saw nothing else. It seemed unfathomable; this jewel, that you could hold between your finger and thumb, seemed unfathomable as the heavens themselves. We set it in the sun, and then shut the light out of the room, and it shone awfully out of the depths of its own brightness, with a moony gleam, in the dark. No wonder Miss Rachel was fascinated: no wonder her cousins screamed. The Diamond laid such a hold on me that I burst out with as large an “O” as the Bouncers themselves. The only one of us who kept his senses was Mr. Godfrey. He put an arm round each of his sisters’ waists, and, looking compassionately backwards and forwards between the Diamond and me, said, “Carbon, Betteredge! mere carbon, my good friend, after all!”\n\nHis object, I suppose, was to instruct me. All he did, however, was to remind me of the dinner. I hobbled off to my army of waiters downstairs. As I went out, Mr. Godfrey said, “Dear old Betteredge, I have the truest regard for him!” He was embracing his sisters, and ogling Miss Rachel, while he honoured me with that testimony of affection. Something like a stock of love to draw on there! Mr. Franklin was a perfect savage by comparison with him.\n\nAt the end of half an hour, I presented myself, as directed, in my lady’s room.\n\nWhat passed between my mistress and me, on this occasion, was, in the main, a repetition of what had passed between Mr. Franklin and me at the Shivering Sand—with this difference, that I took care to keep my own counsel about the jugglers, seeing that nothing had happened to justify me in alarming my lady on this head. When I received my dismissal, I could see that she took the blackest view possible of the Colonel’s motives, and that she was bent on getting the Moonstone out of her daughter’s possession at the first opportunity.\n\nOn my way back to my own part of the house, I was encountered by Mr. Franklin. He wanted to know if I had seen anything of his cousin Rachel. I had seen nothing of her. Could I tell him where his cousin Godfrey was? I didn’t know; but I began to suspect that cousin Godfrey might not be far away from cousin Rachel. Mr. Franklin’s suspicions apparently took the same turn. He tugged hard at his beard, and went and shut himself up in the library with a bang of the door that had a world of meaning in it.\n\nI was interrupted no more in the business of preparing for the birthday dinner till it was time for me to smarten myself up for receiving the company. Just as I had got my white waistcoat on, Penelope presented herself at my toilet, on pretence of brushing what little hair I have got left, and improving the tie of my white cravat. My girl was in high spirits, and I saw she had something to say to me. She gave me a kiss on the top of my bald head, and whispered, “News for you, father! Miss Rachel has refused him.”\n\n“Who’s ‘him’?” I asked.\n\n“The ladies’ committee-man, father,” says Penelope. “A nasty sly fellow! I hate him for trying to supplant Mr. Franklin!”\n\nIf I had had breath enough, I should certainly have protested against this indecent way of speaking of an eminent philanthropic character. But my daughter happened to be improving the tie of my cravat at that moment, and the whole strength of her feelings found its way into her fingers. I never was more nearly strangled in my life.\n\n“I saw him take her away alone into the rose-garden,” says Penelope. “And I waited behind the holly to see how they came back. They had gone out arm-in-arm, both laughing. They came back, walking separate, as grave as grave could be, and looking straight away from each other in a manner which there was no mistaking. I never was more delighted, father, in my life! There’s one woman in the world who can resist Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, at any rate; and, if I was a lady, I should be another!”\n\nHere I should have protested again. But my daughter had got the hair-brush by this time, and the whole strength of her feelings had passed into that. If you are bald, you will understand how she sacrificed me. If you are not, skip this bit, and thank God you have got something in the way of a defence between your hair-brush and your head.\n\n“Just on the other side of the holly,” Penelope went on, “Mr. Godfrey came to a standstill. ‘You prefer,’ says he, ‘that I should stop here as if nothing had happened?’ Miss Rachel turned on him like lightning. ‘You have accepted my mother’s invitation,’ she said; ‘and you are here to meet her guests. Unless you wish to make a scandal in the house, you will remain, of course!’ She went on a few steps, and then seemed to relent a little. ‘Let us forget what has passed, Godfrey,’ she said, ‘and let us remain cousins still.’ She gave him her hand. He kissed it, which I should have considered taking a liberty, and then she left him. He waited a little by himself, with his head down, and his heel grinding a hole slowly in the gravel walk; you never saw a man look more put out in your life. ‘Awkward!’ he said between his teeth, when he looked up, and went on to the house—‘very awkward!’ If that was his opinion of himself, he was quite right. Awkward enough, I’m sure. And the end of it is, father, what I told you all along,” cries Penelope, finishing me off with a last scarification, the hottest of all. “Mr. Franklin’s the man!”\n\nI got possession of the hair-brush, and opened my lips to administer the reproof which, you will own, my daughter’s language and conduct richly deserved.\n\nBefore I could say a word, the crash of carriage-wheels outside struck in, and stopped me. The first of the dinner-company had come. Penelope instantly ran off. I put on my coat, and looked in the glass. My head was as red as a lobster; but, in other respects, I was as nicely dressed for the ceremonies of the evening as a man need be. I got into the hall just in time to announce the two first of the guests. You needn’t feel particularly interested about them. Only the philanthropist’s father and mother—Mr. and Mrs. Ablewhite.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\nOne on the top of the other the rest of the company followed the Ablewhites, till we had the whole tale of them complete. Including the family, they were twenty-four in all. It was a noble sight to see, when they were settled in their places round the dinner-table, and the Rector of Frizinghall (with beautiful elocution) rose and said grace.\n\nThere is no need to worry you with a list of the guests. You will meet none of them a second time—in my part of the story, at any rate—with the exception of two.\n\nThose two sat on either side of Miss Rachel, who, as queen of the day, was naturally the great attraction of the party. On this occasion she was more particularly the centre-point towards which everybody’s eyes were directed; for (to my lady’s secret annoyance) she wore her wonderful birthday present, which eclipsed all the rest—the Moonstone. It was without any setting when it had been placed in her hands; but that universal genius, Mr. Franklin, had contrived, with the help of his neat fingers and a little bit of silver wire, to fix it as a brooch in the bosom of her white dress. Everybody wondered at the prodigious size and beauty of the Diamond, as a matter of course. But the only two of the company who said anything out of the common way about it were those two guests I have mentioned, who sat by Miss Rachel on her right hand and her left.\n\nThe guest on her left was Mr. Candy, our doctor at Frizinghall.\n\nThis was a pleasant, companionable little man, with the drawback, however, I must own, of being too fond, in season and out of season, of his joke, and of his plunging in rather a headlong manner into talk with strangers, without waiting to feel his way first. In society, he was constantly making mistakes, and setting people unintentionally by the ears together. In his medical practice he was a more prudent man; picking up his discretion (as his enemies said) by a kind of instinct, and proving to be generally right where more carefully conducted doctors turned out to be wrong. What he said about the Diamond to Miss Rachel was said, as usual, by way of a mystification or joke. He gravely entreated her (in the interests of science) to let him take it home and burn it. “We will first heat it, Miss Rachel,” says the doctor, “to such and such a degree; then we will expose it to a current of air; and, little by little—puff!—we evaporate the Diamond, and spare you a world of anxiety about the safe keeping of a valuable precious stone!” My lady, listening with rather a careworn expression on her face, seemed to wish that the doctor had been in earnest, and that he could have found Miss Rachel zealous enough in the cause of science to sacrifice her birthday gift.\n\nThe other guest, who sat on my young lady’s right hand, was an eminent public character—being no other than the celebrated Indian traveller, Mr. Murthwaite, who, at risk of his life, had penetrated in disguise where no European had ever set foot before.\n\nThis was a long, lean, wiry, brown, silent man. He had a weary look, and a very steady, attentive eye. It was rumoured that he was tired of the humdrum life among the people in our parts, and longing to go back and wander off on the tramp again in the wild places of the East. Except what he said to Miss Rachel about her jewel, I doubt if he spoke six words or drank so much as a single glass of wine, all through the dinner. The Moonstone was the only object that interested him in the smallest degree. The fame of it seemed to have reached him, in some of those perilous Indian places where his wanderings had lain. After looking at it silently for so long a time that Miss Rachel began to get confused, he said to her in his cool immovable way, “If you ever go to India, Miss Verinder, don’t take your uncle’s birthday gift with you. A Hindoo diamond is sometimes part of a Hindoo religion. I know a certain city, and a certain temple in that city, where, dressed as you are now, your life would not be worth five minutes’ purchase.” Miss Rachel, safe in England, was quite delighted to hear of her danger in India. The Bouncers were more delighted still; they dropped their knives and forks with a crash, and burst out together vehemently, “O! how interesting!” My lady fidgeted in her chair, and changed the subject.\n\nAs the dinner got on, I became aware, little by little, that this festival was not prospering as other like festivals had prospered before it.\n\nLooking back at the birthday now, by the light of what happened afterwards, I am half inclined to think that the cursed Diamond must have cast a blight on the whole company. I plied them well with wine; and being a privileged character, followed the unpopular dishes round the table, and whispered to the company confidentially, “Please to change your mind and try it; for I know it will do you good.” Nine times out of ten they changed their minds—out of regard for their old original Betteredge, they were pleased to say—but all to no purpose. There were gaps of silence in the talk, as the dinner got on, that made me feel personally uncomfortable. When they did use their tongues again, they used them innocently, in the most unfortunate manner and to the worst possible purpose. Mr. Candy, the doctor, for instance, said more unlucky things than I ever knew him to say before. Take one sample of the way in which he went on, and you will understand what I had to put up with at the sideboard, officiating as I was in the character of a man who had the prosperity of the festival at heart.\n\nOne of our ladies present at dinner was worthy Mrs. Threadgall, widow of the late Professor of that name. Talking of her deceased husband perpetually, this good lady never mentioned to strangers that he was deceased. She thought, I suppose, that every able-bodied adult in England ought to know as much as that. In one of the gaps of silence, somebody mentioned the dry and rather nasty subject of human anatomy; whereupon good Mrs. Threadgall straightway brought in her late husband as usual, without mentioning that he was dead. Anatomy she described as the Professor’s favourite recreation in his leisure hours. As ill-luck would have it, Mr. Candy, sitting opposite (who knew nothing of the deceased gentleman), heard her. Being the most polite of men, he seized the opportunity of assisting the Professor’s anatomical amusements on the spot.\n\n“They have got some remarkably fine skeletons lately at the College of Surgeons,” says Mr. Candy, across the table, in a loud cheerful voice. “I strongly recommend the Professor, ma’am, when he next has an hour to spare, to pay them a visit.”\n\nYou might have heard a pin fall. The company (out of respect to the Professor’s memory) all sat speechless. I was behind Mrs. Threadgall at the time, plying her confidentially with a glass of hock. She dropped her head, and said in a very low voice, “My beloved husband is no more.”\n\nUnluckily Mr. Candy, hearing nothing, and miles away from suspecting the truth, went on across the table louder and politer than ever.\n\n“The Professor may not be aware,” says he, “that the card of a member of the College will admit him, on any day but Sunday, between the hours of ten and four.”\n\nMrs. Threadgall dropped her head right into her tucker, and, in a lower voice still, repeated the solemn words, “My beloved husband is no more.”\n\nI winked hard at Mr. Candy across the table. Miss Rachel touched his arm. My lady looked unutterable things at him. Quite useless! On he went, with a cordiality that there was no stopping anyhow. “I shall be delighted,” says he, “to send the Professor my card, if you will oblige me by mentioning his present address.”\n\n“His present address, sir, is the grave,” says Mrs. Threadgall, suddenly losing her temper, and speaking with an emphasis and fury that made the glasses ring again. “The Professor has been dead these ten years.”\n\n“Oh, good Heavens!” says Mr. Candy. Excepting the Bouncers, who burst out laughing, such a blank now fell on the company, that they might all have been going the way of the Professor, and hailing as he did from the direction of the grave.\n\nSo much for Mr. Candy. The rest of them were nearly as provoking in their different ways as the doctor himself. When they ought to have spoken, they didn’t speak; or when they did speak they were perpetually at cross purposes. Mr. Godfrey, though so eloquent in public, declined to exert himself in private. Whether he was sulky, or whether he was bashful, after his discomfiture in the rose-garden, I can’t say. He kept all his talk for the private ear of the lady (a member of our family) who sat next to him. She was one of his committee-women—a spiritually-minded person, with a fine show of collar-bone and a pretty taste in champagne; liked it dry, you understand, and plenty of it. Being close behind these two at the sideboard, I can testify, from what I heard pass between them, that the company lost a good deal of very improving conversation, which I caught up while drawing the corks, and carving the mutton, and so forth. What they said about their Charities I didn’t hear. When I had time to listen to them, they had got a long way beyond their women to be confined, and their women to be rescued, and were disputing on serious subjects. Religion (I understand Mr. Godfrey to say, between the corks and the carving) meant love. And love meant religion. And earth was heaven a little the worse for wear. And heaven was earth, done up again to look like new. Earth had some very objectionable people in it; but, to make amends for that, all the women in heaven would be members of a prodigious committee that never quarrelled, with all the men in attendance on them as ministering angels. Beautiful! beautiful! But why the mischief did Mr. Godfrey keep it all to his lady and himself?\n\nMr. Franklin again—surely, you will say, Mr. Franklin stirred the company up into making a pleasant evening of it?\n\nNothing of the sort! He had quite recovered himself, and he was in wonderful force and spirits, Penelope having informed him, I suspect, of Mr. Godfrey’s reception in the rose-garden. But, talk as he might, nine times out of ten he pitched on the wrong subject, or he addressed himself to the wrong person; the end of it being that he offended some, and puzzled all of them. That foreign training of his—those French and German and Italian sides of him, to which I have already alluded—came out, at my lady’s hospitable board, in a most bewildering manner.\n\nWhat do you think, for instance, of his discussing the lengths to which a married woman might let her admiration go for a man who was not her husband, and putting it in his clear-headed witty French way to the maiden aunt of the Vicar of Frizinghall? What do you think, when he shifted to the German side, of his telling the lord of the manor, while that great authority on cattle was quoting his experience in the breeding of bulls, that experience, properly understood, counted for nothing, and that the proper way to breed bulls was to look deep into your own mind, evolve out of it the idea of a perfect bull, and produce him? What do you say, when our county member, growing hot, at cheese and salad time, about the spread of democracy in England, burst out as follows: “If we once lose our ancient safeguards, Mr. Blake, I beg to ask you, what have we got left?”—what do you say to Mr. Franklin answering, from the Italian point of view: “We have got three things left, sir—Love, Music, and Salad”? He not only terrified the company with such outbreaks as these, but, when the English side of him turned up in due course, he lost his foreign smoothness; and, getting on the subject of the medical profession, said such downright things in ridicule of doctors, that he actually put good-humoured little Mr. Candy in a rage.\n\nThe dispute between them began in Mr. Franklin being led—I forget how—to acknowledge that he had latterly slept very badly at night. Mr. Candy thereupon told him that his nerves were all out of order and that he ought to go through a course of medicine immediately. Mr. Franklin replied that a course of medicine, and a course of groping in the dark, meant, in his estimation, one and the same thing. Mr. Candy, hitting back smartly, said that Mr. Franklin himself was, constitutionally speaking, groping in the dark after sleep, and that nothing but medicine could help him to find it. Mr. Franklin, keeping the ball up on his side, said he had often heard of the blind leading the blind, and now, for the first time, he knew what it meant. In this way, they kept it going briskly, cut and thrust, till they both of them got hot—Mr. Candy, in particular, so completely losing his self-control, in defence of his profession, that my lady was obliged to interfere, and forbid the dispute to go on. This necessary act of authority put the last extinguisher on the spirits of the company. The talk spurted up again here and there, for a minute or two at a time; but there was a miserable lack of life and sparkle in it. The Devil (or the Diamond) possessed that dinner-party; and it was a relief to everybody when my mistress rose, and gave the ladies the signal to leave the gentlemen over their wine.\n\nI had just ranged the decanters in a row before old Mr. Ablewhite (who represented the master of the house), when there came a sound from the terrace which startled me out of my company manners on the instant. Mr. Franklin and I looked at each other; it was the sound of the Indian drum. As I live by bread, here were the jugglers returning to us with the return of the Moonstone to the house!\n\nAs they rounded the corner of the terrace, and came in sight, I hobbled out to warn them off. But, as ill-luck would have it, the two Bouncers were beforehand with me. They whizzed out on to the terrace like a couple of skyrockets, wild to see the Indians exhibit their tricks. The other ladies followed; the gentlemen came out on their side. Before you could say, “Lord bless us!” the rogues were making their salaams; and the Bouncers were kissing the pretty little boy.\n\nMr. Franklin got on one side of Miss Rachel, and I put myself behind her. If our suspicions were right, there she stood, innocent of all knowledge of the truth, showing the Indians the Diamond in the bosom of her dress!\n\nI can’t tell you what tricks they performed, or how they did it. What with the vexation about the dinner, and what with the provocation of the rogues coming back just in the nick of time to see the jewel with their own eyes, I own I lost my head. The first thing that I remember noticing was the sudden appearance on the scene of the Indian traveller, Mr. Murthwaite. Skirting the half-circle in which the gentlefolks stood or sat, he came quietly behind the jugglers and spoke to them on a sudden in the language of their own country.\n\nIf he had pricked them with a bayonet, I doubt if the Indians could have started and turned on him with a more tigerish quickness than they did, on hearing the first words that passed his lips. The next moment they were bowing and salaaming to him in their most polite and snaky way. After a few words in the unknown tongue had passed on either side, Mr. Murthwaite withdrew as quietly as he had approached. The chief Indian, who acted as interpreter, thereupon wheeled about again towards the gentlefolks. I noticed that the fellow’s coffee-coloured face had turned grey since Mr. Murthwaite had spoken to him. He bowed to my lady, and informed her that the exhibition was over. The Bouncers, indescribably disappointed, burst out with a loud “O!” directed against Mr. Murthwaite for stopping the performance. The chief Indian laid his hand humbly on his breast, and said a second time that the juggling was over. The little boy went round with the hat. The ladies withdrew to the drawing-room; and the gentlemen (excepting Mr. Franklin and Mr. Murthwaite) returned to their wine. I and the footman followed the Indians, and saw them safe off the premises.\n\nGoing back by way of the shrubbery, I smelt tobacco, and found Mr. Franklin and Mr. Murthwaite (the latter smoking a cheroot) walking slowly up and down among the trees. Mr. Franklin beckoned to me to join them.\n\n“This,” says Mr. Franklin, presenting me to the great traveller, “is Gabriel Betteredge, the old servant and friend of our family of whom I spoke to you just now. Tell him, if you please, what you have just told me.”\n\nMr. Murthwaite took his cheroot out of his mouth, and leaned, in his weary way, against the trunk of a tree.\n\n“Mr. Betteredge,” he began, “those three Indians are no more jugglers than you and I are.”\n\nHere was a new surprise! I naturally asked the traveller if he had ever met with the Indians before.\n\n“Never,” says Mr. Murthwaite; “but I know what Indian juggling really is. All you have seen tonight is a very bad and clumsy imitation of it. Unless, after long experience, I am utterly mistaken, those men are high-caste Brahmins. I charged them with being disguised, and you saw how it told on them, clever as the Hindoo people are in concealing their feelings. There is a mystery about their conduct that I can’t explain. They have doubly sacrificed their caste—first, in crossing the sea; secondly, in disguising themselves as jugglers. In the land they live in that is a tremendous sacrifice to make. There must be some very serious motive at the bottom of it, and some justification of no ordinary kind to plead for them, in recovery of their caste, when they return to their own country.”\n\nI was struck dumb. Mr. Murthwaite went on with his cheroot. Mr. Franklin, after what looked to me like a little private veering about between the different sides of his character, broke the silence as follows:\n\n“I feel some hesitation, Mr. Murthwaite, in troubling you with family matters, in which you can have no interest and which I am not very willing to speak of out of our own circle. But, after what you have said, I feel bound, in the interests of Lady Verinder and her daughter, to tell you something which may possibly put the clue into your hands. I speak to you in confidence; you will oblige me, I am sure, by not forgetting that?”\n\nWith this preface, he told the Indian traveller all that he had told me at the Shivering Sand. Even the immovable Mr. Murthwaite was so interested in what he heard, that he let his cheroot go out.\n\n“Now,” says Mr. Franklin, when he had done, “what does your experience say?”\n\n“My experience,” answered the traveller, “says that you have had more narrow escapes of your life, Mr. Franklin Blake, than I have had of mine; and that is saying a great deal.”\n\nIt was Mr. Franklin’s turn to be astonished now.\n\n“Is it really as serious as that?” he asked.\n\n“In my opinion it is,” answered Mr. Murthwaite. “I can’t doubt, after what you have told me, that the restoration of the Moonstone to its place on the forehead of the Indian idol, is the motive and the justification of that sacrifice of caste which I alluded to just now. Those men will wait their opportunity with the patience of cats, and will use it with the ferocity of tigers. How you have escaped them I can’t imagine,” says the eminent traveller, lighting his cheroot again, and staring hard at Mr. Franklin. “You have been carrying the Diamond backwards and forwards, here and in London, and you are still a living man! Let us try and account for it. It was daylight, both times, I suppose, when you took the jewel out of the bank in London?”\n\n“Broad daylight,” says Mr. Franklin.\n\n“And plenty of people in the streets?”\n\n“Plenty.”\n\n“You settled, of course, to arrive at Lady Verinder’s house at a certain time? It’s a lonely country between this and the station. Did you keep your appointment?”\n\n“No. I arrived four hours earlier than my appointment.”\n\n“I beg to congratulate you on that proceeding! When did you take the Diamond to the bank at the town here?”\n\n“I took it an hour after I had brought it to this house—and three hours before anybody was prepared for seeing me in these parts.”\n\n“I beg to congratulate you again! Did you bring it back here alone?”\n\n“No. I happened to ride back with my cousins and the groom.”\n\n“I beg to congratulate you for the third time! If you ever feel inclined to travel beyond the civilised limits, Mr. Blake, let me know, and I will go with you. You are a lucky man.”\n\nHere I struck in. This sort of thing didn’t at all square with my English ideas.\n\n“You don’t really mean to say, sir,” I asked, “that they would have taken Mr. Franklin’s life, to get their Diamond, if he had given them the chance?”\n\n“Do you smoke, Mr. Betteredge?” says the traveller.\n\n“Yes, sir.\n\n“Do you care much for the ashes left in your pipe when you empty it?”\n\n“No, sir.”\n\n“In the country those men came from, they care just as much about killing a man, as you care about emptying the ashes out of your pipe. If a thousand lives stood between them and the getting back of their Diamond—and if they thought they could destroy those lives without discovery—they would take them all. The sacrifice of caste is a serious thing in India, if you like. The sacrifice of life is nothing at all.”\n\nI expressed my opinion upon this, that they were a set of murdering thieves. Mr. Murthwaite expressed his opinion that they were a wonderful people. Mr. Franklin, expressing no opinion at all, brought us back to the matter in hand.\n\n“They have seen the Moonstone on Miss Verinder’s dress,” he said. “What is to be done?”\n\n“What your uncle threatened to do,” answered Mr. Murthwaite. “Colonel Herncastle understood the people he had to deal with. Send the Diamond tomorrow (under guard of more than one man) to be cut up at Amsterdam. Make half a dozen diamonds of it, instead of one. There is an end of its sacred identity as The Moonstone—and there is an end of the conspiracy.”\n\nMr. Franklin turned to me.\n\n“There is no help for it,” he said. “We must speak to Lady Verinder tomorrow.”\n\n“What about tonight, sir?” I asked. “Suppose the Indians come back?”\n\nMr. Murthwaite answered me before Mr. Franklin could speak.\n\n“The Indians won’t risk coming back tonight,” he said. “The direct way is hardly ever the way they take to anything—let alone a matter like this, in which the slightest mistake might be fatal to their reaching their end.”\n\n“But suppose the rogues are bolder than you think, sir?” I persisted.\n\n“In that case,” says Mr. Murthwaite, “let the dogs loose. Have you got any big dogs in the yard?”\n\n“Two, sir. A mastiff and a bloodhound.”\n\n“They will do. In the present emergency, Mr. Betteredge, the mastiff and the bloodhound have one great merit—they are not likely to be troubled with your scruples about the sanctity of human life.”\n\nThe strumming of the piano reached us from the drawing-room, as he fired that shot at me. He threw away his cheroot, and took Mr. Franklin’s arm, to go back to the ladies. I noticed that the sky was clouding over fast, as I followed them to the house. Mr. Murthwaite noticed it too. He looked round at me, in his dry, droning way, and said:\n\n“The Indians will want their umbrellas, Mr. Betteredge, tonight!”\n\nIt was all very well for him to joke. But I was not an eminent traveller—and my way in this world had not led me into playing ducks and drakes with my own life, among thieves and murderers in the outlandish places of the earth. I went into my own little room, and sat down in my chair in a perspiration, and wondered helplessly what was to be done next. In this anxious frame of mind, other men might have ended by working themselves up into a fever; I ended in a different way. I lit my pipe, and took a turn at Robinson Crusoe.\n\nBefore I had been at it five minutes, I came to this amazing bit—page one hundred and sixty-one—as follows:\n\n“Fear of Danger is ten thousand times more terrifying than Danger itself, when apparent to the Eyes; and we find the Burthen of Anxiety greater, by much, than the Evil which we are anxious about.”\n\nThe man who doesn’t believe in Robinson Crusoe, after that, is a man with a screw loose in his understanding, or a man lost in the mist of his own self-conceit! Argument is thrown away upon him; and pity is better reserved for some person with a livelier faith.\n\nI was far on with my second pipe, and still lost in admiration of that wonderful book, when Penelope (who had been handing round the tea) came in with her report from the drawing-room. She had left the Bouncers singing a duet—words beginning with a large “O,” and music to correspond. She had observed that my lady made mistakes in her game of whist for the first time in our experience of her. She had seen the great traveller asleep in a corner. She had overheard Mr. Franklin sharpening his wits on Mr. Godfrey, at the expense of Ladies’ Charities in general; and she had noticed that Mr. Godfrey hit him back again rather more smartly than became a gentleman of his benevolent character. She had detected Miss Rachel, apparently engaged in appeasing Mrs. Threadgall by showing her some photographs, and really occupied in stealing looks at Mr. Franklin, which no intelligent lady’s maid could misinterpret for a single instant. Finally, she had missed Mr. Candy, the doctor, who had mysteriously disappeared from the drawing-room, and had then mysteriously returned, and entered into conversation with Mr. Godfrey. Upon the whole, things were prospering better than the experience of the dinner gave us any right to expect. If we could only hold on for another hour, old Father Time would bring up their carriages, and relieve us of them altogether.\n\nEverything wears off in this world; and even the comforting effect of Robinson Crusoe wore off, after Penelope left me. I got fidgety again, and resolved on making a survey of the grounds before the rain came. Instead of taking the footman, whose nose was human, and therefore useless in any emergency, I took the bloodhound with me. His nose for a stranger was to be depended on. We went all round the premises, and out into the road—and returned as wise as we went, having discovered no such thing as a lurking human creature anywhere.\n\nThe arrival of the carriages was the signal for the arrival of the rain. It poured as if it meant to pour all night. With the exception of the doctor, whose gig was waiting for him, the rest of the company went home snugly, under cover, in close carriages. I told Mr. Candy that I was afraid he would get wet through. He told me, in return, that he wondered I had arrived at my time of life, without knowing that a doctor’s skin was waterproof. So he drove away in the rain, laughing over his own little joke; and so we got rid of our dinner company.\n\nThe next thing to tell is the story of the night.\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\nWhen the last of the guests had driven away, I went back into the inner hall and found Samuel at the side-table, presiding over the brandy and soda water. My lady and Miss Rachel came out of the drawing-room, followed by the two gentlemen. Mr. Godfrey had some brandy and soda water, Mr. Franklin took nothing. He sat down, looking dead tired; the talking on this birthday occasion had, I suppose, been too much for him.\n\nMy lady, turning round to wish them good-night, looked hard at the wicked Colonel’s legacy shining in her daughter’s dress.\n\n“Rachel,” she asked, “where are you going to put your Diamond tonight?”\n\nMiss Rachel was in high good spirits, just in that humour for talking nonsense, and perversely persisting in it as if it was sense, which you may sometimes have observed in young girls, when they are highly wrought up, at the end of an exciting day. First, she declared she didn’t know where to put the Diamond. Then she said, “on her dressing-table, of course, along with her other things.” Then she remembered that the Diamond might take to shining of itself, with its awful moony light in the dark—and that would terrify her in the dead of night. Then she bethought herself of an Indian cabinet which stood in her sitting-room; and instantly made up her mind to put the Indian diamond in the Indian cabinet, for the purpose of permitting two beautiful native productions to admire each other. Having let her little flow of nonsense run on as far as that point, her mother interposed and stopped her.\n\n“My dear! your Indian cabinet has no lock to it,” says my lady.\n\n“Good Heavens, mamma!” cried Miss Rachel, “is this an hotel? Are there thieves in the house?”\n\nWithout taking notice of this fantastic way of talking, my lady wished the gentlemen good-night. She next turned to Miss Rachel, and kissed her. “Why not let me keep the Diamond for you tonight?” she asked.\n\nMiss Rachel received that proposal as she might, ten years since, have received a proposal to part her from a new doll. My lady saw there was no reasoning with her that night. “Come into my room, Rachel, the first thing tomorrow morning,” she said. “I shall have something to say to you.” With those last words she left us slowly; thinking her own thoughts, and, to all appearance, not best pleased with the way by which they were leading her.\n\nMiss Rachel was the next to say good-night. She shook hands first with Mr. Godfrey, who was standing at the other end of the hall, looking at a picture. Then she turned back to Mr. Franklin, still sitting weary and silent in a corner.\n\nWhat words passed between them I can’t say. But standing near the old oak frame which holds our large looking-glass, I saw her reflected in it, slyly slipping the locket which Mr. Franklin had given to her, out of the bosom of her dress, and showing it to him for a moment, with a smile which certainly meant something out of the common, before she tripped off to bed. This incident staggered me a little in the reliance I had previously felt on my own judgment. I began to think that Penelope might be right about the state of her young lady’s affections, after all.\n\nAs soon as Miss Rachel left him eyes to see with, Mr. Franklin noticed me. His variable humour, shifting about everything, had shifted about the Indians already.\n\n“Betteredge,” he said, “I’m half inclined to think I took Mr. Murthwaite too seriously, when we had that talk in the shrubbery. I wonder whether he has been trying any of his traveller’s tales on us? Do you really mean to let the dogs loose?”\n\n“I’ll relieve them of their collars, sir,” I answered, “and leave them free to take a turn in the night, if they smell a reason for it.”\n\n“All right,” says Mr. Franklin. “We’ll see what is to be done tomorrow. I am not at all disposed to alarm my aunt, Betteredge, without a very pressing reason for it. Good-night.”\n\nHe looked so worn and pale as he nodded to me, and took his candle to go upstairs, that I ventured to advise his having a drop of brandy-and-water, by way of night-cap. Mr. Godfrey, walking towards us from the other end of the hall, backed me. He pressed Mr. Franklin, in the friendliest manner, to take something, before he went to bed.\n\nI only note these trifling circumstances, because, after all I had seen and heard, that day, it pleased me to observe that our two gentlemen were on just as good terms as ever. Their warfare of words (heard by Penelope in the drawing-room), and their rivalry for the best place in Miss Rachel’s good graces, seemed to have set no serious difference between them. But there! they were both good-tempered, and both men of the world. And there is certainly this merit in people of station, that they are not nearly so quarrelsome among each other as people of no station at all.\n\nMr. Franklin declined the brandy-and-water, and went upstairs with Mr. Godfrey, their rooms being next door to each other. On the landing, however, either his cousin persuaded him, or he veered about and changed his mind as usual. “Perhaps I may want it in the night,” he called down to me. “Send up some brandy-and-water into my room.”\n\nI sent up Samuel with the brandy-and-water; and then went out and unbuckled the dogs’ collars. They both lost their heads with astonishment on being set loose at that time of night, and jumped upon me like a couple of puppies! However, the rain soon cooled them down again: they lapped a drop of water each, and crept back into their kennels. As I went into the house, I noticed signs in the sky which betokened a break in the weather for the better. For the present, it still poured heavily, and the ground was in a perfect sop.\n\nSamuel and I went all over the house, and shut up as usual. I examined everything myself, and trusted nothing to my deputy on this occasion. All was safe and fast when I rested my old bones in bed, between midnight and one in the morning.\n\nThe worries of the day had been a little too much for me, I suppose. At any rate, I had a touch of Mr. Franklin’s malady that night. It was sunrise before I fell off at last into a sleep. All the time I lay awake the house was as quiet as the grave. Not a sound stirred but the splash of the rain, and the sighing of the wind among the trees as a breeze sprang up with the morning.\n\nAbout half-past seven I woke, and opened my window on a fine sunshiny day. The clock had struck eight, and I was just going out to chain up the dogs again, when I heard a sudden whisking of petticoats on the stairs behind me.\n\nI turned about, and there was Penelope flying down after me like mad. “Father!” she screamed, “come upstairs, for God’s sake! The Diamond is gone!”\n\n“Are you out of your mind?” I asked her.\n\n“Gone!” says Penelope. “Gone, nobody knows how! Come up and see.”\n\nShe dragged me after her into our young lady’s sitting-room, which opened into her bedroom. There, on the threshold of her bedroom door, stood Miss Rachel, almost as white in the face as the white dressing-gown that clothed her. There also stood the two doors of the Indian cabinet, wide open. One of the drawers inside was pulled out as far as it would go.\n\n“Look!” says Penelope. “I myself saw Miss Rachel put the Diamond into that drawer last night.” I went to the cabinet. The drawer was empty.\n\n“Is this true, miss?” I asked.\n\nWith a look that was not like herself, with a voice that was not like her own, Miss Rachel answered as my daughter had answered:\n\n“The Diamond is gone!”\n\nHaving said those words, she withdrew into her bedroom, and shut and locked the door.\n\nBefore we knew which way to turn next, my lady came in, hearing my voice in her daughter’s sitting-room, and wondering what had happened. The news of the loss of the Diamond seemed to petrify her. She went straight to Miss Rachel’s bedroom, and insisted on being admitted. Miss Rachel let her in.\n\nThe alarm, running through the house like fire, caught the two gentlemen next.\n\nMr. Godfrey was the first to come out of his room. All he did when he heard what had happened was to hold up his hands in a state of bewilderment, which didn’t say much for his natural strength of mind. Mr. Franklin, whose clear head I had confidently counted on to advise us, seemed to be as helpless as his cousin when he heard the news in his turn. For a wonder, he had had a good night’s rest at last; and the unaccustomed luxury of sleep had, as he said himself, apparently stupefied him. However, when he had swallowed his cup of coffee—which he always took, on the foreign plan, some hours before he ate any breakfast—his brains brightened; the clear-headed side of him turned up, and he took the matter in hand, resolutely and cleverly, much as follows:\n\nHe first sent for the servants, and told them to leave all the lower doors and windows (with the exception of the front door, which I had opened) exactly as they had been left when we locked up over night. He next proposed to his cousin and to me to make quite sure, before we took any further steps, that the Diamond had not accidentally dropped somewhere out of sight—say at the back of the cabinet, or down behind the table on which the cabinet stood. Having searched in both places, and found nothing—having also questioned Penelope, and discovered from her no more than the little she had already told me—Mr. Franklin suggested next extending our inquiries to Miss Rachel, and sent Penelope to knock at her bedroom door.\n\nMy lady answered the knock, and closed the door behind her. The moment after we heard it locked inside by Miss Rachel. My mistress came out among us, looking sorely puzzled and distressed. “The loss of the Diamond seems to have quite overwhelmed Rachel,” she said, in reply to Mr. Franklin. “She shrinks, in the strangest manner, from speaking of it, even to me. It is impossible you can see her for the present.”\n\nHaving added to our perplexities by this account of Miss Rachel, my lady, after a little effort, recovered her usual composure, and acted with her usual decision.\n\n“I suppose there is no help for it?” she said, quietly. “I suppose I have no alternative but to send for the police?”\n\n“And the first thing for the police to do,” added Mr. Franklin, catching her up, “is to lay hands on the Indian jugglers who performed here last night.”\n\nMy lady and Mr. Godfrey (not knowing what Mr. Franklin and I knew) both started, and both looked surprised.\n\n“I can’t stop to explain myself now,” Mr. Franklin went on. “I can only tell you that the Indians have certainly stolen the Diamond. Give me a letter of introduction,” says he, addressing my lady, “to one of the magistrates at Frizinghall—merely telling him that I represent your interests and wishes, and let me ride off with it instantly. Our chance of catching the thieves may depend on our not wasting one unnecessary minute.” (Nota bene: Whether it was the French side or the English, the right side of Mr. Franklin seemed to be uppermost now. The only question was, How long would it last?)\n\nHe put pen, ink, and paper before his aunt, who (as it appeared to me) wrote the letter he wanted a little unwillingly. If it had been possible to overlook such an event as the loss of a jewel worth twenty thousand pounds, I believe—with my lady’s opinion of her late brother, and her distrust of his birthday-gift—it would have been privately a relief to her to let the thieves get off with the Moonstone scot free.\n\nI went out with Mr. Franklin to the stables, and took the opportunity of asking him how the Indians (whom I suspected, of course, as shrewdly as he did) could possibly have got into the house.\n\n“One of them might have slipped into the hall, in the confusion, when the dinner company were going away,” says Mr. Franklin. “The fellow may have been under the sofa while my aunt and Rachel were talking about where the Diamond was to be put for the night. He would only have to wait till the house was quiet, and there it would be in the cabinet, to be had for the taking.” With those words, he called to the groom to open the gate, and galloped off.\n\nThis seemed certainly to be the only rational explanation. But how had the thief contrived to make his escape from the house? I had found the front door locked and bolted, as I had left it at night, when I went to open it, after getting up. As for the other doors and windows, there they were still, all safe and fast, to speak for themselves. The dogs, too? Suppose the thief had got away by dropping from one of the upper windows, how had he escaped the dogs? Had he come provided for them with drugged meat? As the doubt crossed my mind, the dogs themselves came galloping at me round a corner, rolling each other over on the wet grass, in such lively health and spirits that it was with no small difficulty I brought them to reason, and chained them up again. The more I turned it over in my mind, the less satisfactory Mr. Franklin’s explanation appeared to be.\n\nWe had our breakfasts—whatever happens in a house, robbery or murder, it doesn’t matter, you must have your breakfast. When we had done, my lady sent for me; and I found myself compelled to tell her all that I had hitherto concealed, relating to the Indians and their plot. Being a woman of a high courage, she soon got over the first startling effect of what I had to communicate. Her mind seemed to be far more perturbed about her daughter than about the heathen rogues and their conspiracy. “You know how odd Rachel is, and how differently she behaves sometimes from other girls,” my lady said to me. “But I have never, in all my experience, seen her so strange and so reserved as she is now. The loss of her jewel seems almost to have turned her brain. Who would have thought that horrible Diamond could have laid such a hold on her in so short a time?”\n\nIt was certainly strange. Taking toys and trinkets in general, Miss Rachel was nothing like so mad after them as most young girls. Yet there she was, still locked up inconsolably in her bedroom. It is but fair to add that she was not the only one of us in the house who was thrown out of the regular groove. Mr. Godfrey, for instance—though professionally a sort of consoler-general—seemed to be at a loss where to look for his own resources. Having no company to amuse him, and getting no chance of trying what his experience of women in distress could do towards comforting Miss Rachel, he wandered hither and thither about the house and gardens in an aimless uneasy way. He was in two different minds about what it became him to do, after the misfortune that had happened to us. Ought he to relieve the family, in their present situation, of the responsibility of him as a guest, or ought he to stay on the chance that even his humble services might be of some use? He decided ultimately that the last course was perhaps the most customary and considerate course to take, in such a very peculiar case of family distress as this was. Circumstances try the metal a man is really made of. Mr. Godfrey, tried by circumstances, showed himself of weaker metal than I had thought him to be. As for the women-servants excepting Rosanna Spearman, who kept by herself—they took to whispering together in corners, and staring at nothing suspiciously, as is the manner of that weaker half of the human family, when anything extraordinary happens in a house. I myself acknowledge to have been fidgety and ill-tempered. The cursed Moonstone had turned us all upside down.\n\nA little before eleven Mr. Franklin came back. The resolute side of him had, to all appearance, given way, in the interval since his departure, under the stress that had been laid on it. He had left us at a gallop; he came back to us at a walk. When he went away, he was made of iron. When he returned, he was stuffed with cotton, as limp as limp could be.\n\n“Well,” says my lady, “are the police coming?”\n\n“Yes,” says Mr. Franklin; “they said they would follow me in a fly. Superintendent Seegrave, of your local police force, and two of his men. A mere form! The case is hopeless.”\n\n“What! have the Indians escaped, sir?” I asked.\n\n“The poor ill-used Indians have been most unjustly put in prison,” says Mr. Franklin. “They are as innocent as the babe unborn. My idea that one of them was hidden in the house has ended, like all the rest of my ideas, in smoke. It’s been proved,” says Mr. Franklin, dwelling with great relish on his own incapacity, “to be simply impossible.”\n\nAfter astonishing us by announcing this totally new turn in the matter of the Moonstone, our young gentleman, at his aunt’s request, took a seat, and explained himself.\n\nIt appeared that the resolute side of him had held out as far as Frizinghall. He had put the whole case plainly before the magistrate, and the magistrate had at once sent for the police. The first inquiries instituted about the Indians showed that they had not so much as attempted to leave the town. Further questions addressed to the police, proved that all three had been seen returning to Frizinghall with their boy, on the previous night between ten and eleven—which (regard being had to hours and distances) also proved that they had walked straight back after performing on our terrace. Later still, at midnight, the police, having occasion to search the common lodging-house where they lived, had seen them all three again, and their little boy with them, as usual. Soon after midnight I myself had safely shut up the house. Plainer evidence than this, in favour of the Indians, there could not well be. The magistrate said there was not even a case of suspicion against them so far. But, as it was just possible, when the police came to investigate the matter, that discoveries affecting the jugglers might be made, he would contrive, by committing them as rogues and vagabonds, to keep them at our disposal, under lock and key, for a week. They had ignorantly done something (I forget what) in the town, which barely brought them within the operation of the law. Every human institution (justice included) will stretch a little, if you only pull it the right way. The worthy magistrate was an old friend of my lady’s, and the Indians were “committed” for a week, as soon as the court opened that morning.\n\nSuch was Mr. Franklin’s narrative of events at Frizinghall. The Indian clue to the mystery of the lost jewel was now, to all appearance, a clue that had broken in our hands. If the jugglers were innocent, who, in the name of wonder, had taken the Moonstone out of Miss Rachel’s drawer?\n\nTen minutes later, to our infinite relief, Superintendent Seegrave arrived at the house. He reported passing Mr. Franklin on the terrace, sitting in the sun (I suppose with the Italian side of him uppermost), and warning the police, as they went by, that the investigation was hopeless, before the investigation had begun.\n\nFor a family in our situation, the Superintendent of the Frizinghall police was the most comforting officer you could wish to see. Mr. Seegrave was tall and portly, and military in his manners. He had a fine commanding voice, and a mighty resolute eye, and a grand frock-coat which buttoned beautifully up to his leather stock. “I’m the man you want!” was written all over his face; and he ordered his two inferior police men about with a severity which convinced us all that there was no trifling with him.\n\nHe began by going round the premises, outside and in; the result of that investigation proving to him that no thieves had broken in upon us from outside, and that the robbery, consequently, must have been committed by some person in the house. I leave you to imagine the state the servants were in when this official announcement first reached their ears. The Superintendent decided to begin by examining the boudoir, and, that done, to examine the servants next. At the same time, he posted one of his men on the staircase which led to the servants’ bedrooms, with instructions to let nobody in the house pass him, till further orders.\n\nAt this latter proceeding, the weaker half of the human family went distracted on the spot. They bounced out of their corners, whisked upstairs in a body to Miss Rachel’s room (Rosanna Spearman being carried away among them this time), burst in on Superintendent Seegrave, and, all looking equally guilty, summoned him to say which of them he suspected, at once.\n\nMr. Superintendent proved equal to the occasion; he looked at them with his resolute eye, and he cowed them with his military voice.\n\n“Now, then, you women, go downstairs again, every one of you. I won’t have you here. Look!” says Mr. Superintendent, suddenly pointing to a little smear of the decorative painting on Miss Rachel’s door, at the outer edge, just under the lock. “Look what mischief the petticoats of some of you have done already. Clear out! clear out!” Rosanna Spearman, who was nearest to him, and nearest to the little smear on the door, set the example of obedience, and slipped off instantly to her work. The rest followed her out. The Superintendent finished his examination of the room, and, making nothing of it, asked me who had first discovered the robbery. My daughter had first discovered it. My daughter was sent for.\n\nMr. Superintendent proved to be a little too sharp with Penelope at starting. “Now, young woman, attend to me, and mind you speak the truth.” Penelope fired up instantly. “I’ve never been taught to tell lies Mr. Policeman!—and if father can stand there and hear me accused of falsehood and thieving, and my own bedroom shut against me, and my character taken away, which is all a poor girl has left, he’s not the good father I take him for!” A timely word from me put Justice and Penelope on a pleasanter footing together. The questions and answers went swimmingly, and ended in nothing worth mentioning. My daughter had seen Miss Rachel put the Diamond in the drawer of the cabinet the last thing at night. She had gone in with Miss Rachel’s cup of tea at eight the next morning, and had found the drawer open and empty. Upon that, she had alarmed the house—and there was an end of Penelope’s evidence.\n\nMr. Superintendent next asked to see Miss Rachel herself. Penelope mentioned his request through the door. The answer reached us by the same road: “I have nothing to tell the policeman—I can’t see anybody.” Our experienced officer looked equally surprised and offended when he heard that reply. I told him my young lady was ill, and begged him to wait a little and see her later. We thereupon went downstairs again, and were met by Mr. Godfrey and Mr. Franklin crossing the hall.\n\nThe two gentlemen, being inmates of the house, were summoned to say if they could throw any light on the matter. Neither of them knew anything about it. Had they heard any suspicious noises during the previous night? They had heard nothing but the pattering of the rain. Had I, lying awake longer than either of them, heard nothing either? Nothing! Released from examination, Mr. Franklin, still sticking to the helpless view of our difficulty, whispered to me: “That man will be of no earthly use to us. Superintendent Seegrave is an ass.” Released in his turn, Mr. Godfrey whispered to me—“Evidently a most competent person. Betteredge, I have the greatest faith in him!” Many men, many opinions, as one of the ancients said, before my time.\n\nMr. Superintendent’s next proceeding took him back to the “boudoir” again, with my daughter and me at his heels. His object was to discover whether any of the furniture had been moved, during the night, out of its customary place—his previous investigation in the room having, apparently, not gone quite far enough to satisfy his mind on this point.\n\nWhile we were still poking about among the chairs and tables, the door of the bedroom was suddenly opened. After having denied herself to everybody, Miss Rachel, to our astonishment, walked into the midst of us of her own accord. She took up her garden hat from a chair, and then went straight to Penelope with this question:—\n\n“Mr. Franklin Blake sent you with a message to me this morning?”\n\n“Yes, miss.”\n\n“He wished to speak to me, didn’t he?”\n\n“Yes, miss.”\n\n“Where is he now?”\n\nHearing voices on the terrace below, I looked out of window, and saw the two gentlemen walking up and down together. Answering for my daughter, I said, “Mr. Franklin is on the terrace, miss.”\n\nWithout another word, without heeding Mr. Superintendent, who tried to speak to her, pale as death, and wrapped up strangely in her own thoughts, she left the room, and went down to her cousins on the terrace.\n\nIt showed a want of due respect, it showed a breach of good manners, on my part, but, for the life of me, I couldn’t help looking out of window when Miss Rachel met the gentlemen outside. She went up to Mr. Franklin without appearing to notice Mr. Godfrey, who thereupon drew back and left them by themselves. What she said to Mr. Franklin appeared to be spoken vehemently. It lasted but for a short time, and, judging by what I saw of his face from the window, seemed to astonish him beyond all power of expression. While they were still together, my lady appeared on the terrace. Miss Rachel saw her—said a few last words to Mr. Franklin—and suddenly went back into the house again, before her mother came up with her. My lady, surprised herself, and noticing Mr. Franklin’s surprise, spoke to him. Mr. Godfrey joined them, and spoke also. Mr. Franklin walked away a little between the two, telling them what had happened I suppose, for they both stopped short, after taking a few steps, like persons struck with amazement. I had just seen as much as this, when the door of the sitting-room was opened violently. Miss Rachel walked swiftly through to her bedroom, wild and angry, with fierce eyes and flaming cheeks. Mr. Superintendent once more attempted to question her. She turned round on him at her bedroom door. “I have not sent for you!” she cried out vehemently. “I don’t want you. My Diamond is lost. Neither you nor anybody else will ever find it!” With those words she went in, and locked the door in our faces. Penelope, standing nearest to it, heard her burst out crying the moment she was alone again.\n\nIn a rage, one moment; in tears, the next! What did it mean?\n\nI told the Superintendent it meant that Miss Rachel’s temper was upset by the loss of her jewel. Being anxious for the honour of the family, it distressed me to see my young lady forget herself—even with a police-officer—and I made the best excuse I could, accordingly. In my own private mind I was more puzzled by Miss Rachel’s extraordinary language and conduct than words can tell. Taking what she had said at her bedroom door as a guide to guess by, I could only conclude that she was mortally offended by our sending for the police, and that Mr. Franklin’s astonishment on the terrace was caused by her having expressed herself to him (as the person chiefly instrumental in fetching the police) to that effect. If this guess was right, why—having lost her Diamond—should she object to the presence in the house of the very people whose business it was to recover it for her? And how, in Heaven’s name, could she know that the Moonstone would never be found again?\n\nAs things stood, at present, no answer to those questions was to be hoped for from anybody in the house. Mr. Franklin appeared to think it a point of honour to forbear repeating to a servant—even to so old a servant as I was—what Miss Rachel had said to him on the terrace. Mr. Godfrey, who, as a gentleman and a relative, had been probably admitted into Mr. Franklin’s confidence, respected that confidence as he was bound to do. My lady, who was also in the secret no doubt, and who alone had access to Miss Rachel, owned openly that she could make nothing of her. “You madden me when you talk of the Diamond!” All her mother’s influence failed to extract from her a word more than that.\n\nHere we were, then, at a dead-lock about Miss Rachel—and at a dead-lock about the Moonstone. In the first case, my lady was powerless to help us. In the second (as you shall presently judge), Mr. Seegrave was fast approaching the condition of a superintendent at his wits’ end.\n\nHaving ferreted about all over the “boudoir,” without making any discoveries among the furniture, our experienced officer applied to me to know, whether the servants in general were or were not acquainted with the place in which the Diamond had been put for the night.\n\n“I knew where it was put, sir,” I said, “to begin with. Samuel, the footman, knew also—for he was present in the hall, when they were talking about where the Diamond was to be kept that night. My daughter knew, as she has already told you. She or Samuel may have mentioned the thing to the other servants—or the other servants may have heard the talk for themselves, through the side-door of the hall, which might have been open to the back staircase. For all I can tell, everybody in the house may have known where the jewel was, last night.”\n\nMy answer presenting rather a wide field for Mr. Superintendent’s suspicions to range over, he tried to narrow it by asking about the servants’ characters next.\n\nI thought directly of Rosanna Spearman. But it was neither my place nor my wish to direct suspicion against a poor girl, whose honesty had been above all doubt as long as I had known her. The matron at the Reformatory had reported her to my lady as a sincerely penitent and thoroughly trustworthy girl. It was the Superintendent’s business to discover reason for suspecting her first—and then, and not till then, it would be my duty to tell him how she came into my lady’s service. “All our people have excellent characters,” I said. “And all have deserved the trust their mistress has placed in them.” After that, there was but one thing left for Mr. Seegrave to do—namely, to set to work, and tackle the servants’ characters himself.\n\nOne after another, they were examined. One after another, they proved to have nothing to say—and said it (so far as the women were concerned) at great length, and with a very angry sense of the embargo laid on their bedrooms. The rest of them being sent back to their places downstairs, Penelope was then summoned, and examined separately a second time.\n\nMy daughter’s little outbreak of temper in the “boudoir,” and her readiness to think herself suspected, appeared to have produced an unfavourable impression on Superintendent Seegrave. It seemed also to dwell a little on his mind, that she had been the last person who saw the Diamond at night. When the second questioning was over, my girl came back to me in a frenzy. There was no doubt of it any longer—the police-officer had almost as good as told her she was the thief! I could scarcely believe him (taking Mr. Franklin’s view) to be quite such an ass as that. But, though he said nothing, the eye with which he looked at my daughter was not a very pleasant eye to see. I laughed it off with poor Penelope, as something too ridiculous to be treated seriously—which it certainly was. Secretly, I am afraid I was foolish enough to be angry too. It was a little trying—it was, indeed. My girl sat down in a corner, with her apron over her head, quite broken-hearted. Foolish of her, you will say. She might have waited till he openly accused her. Well, being a man of just an equal temper, I admit that. Still Mr. Superintendent might have remembered—never mind what he might have remembered. The devil take him!\n\nThe next and last step in the investigation brought matters, as they say, to a crisis. The officer had an interview (at which I was present) with my lady. After informing her that the Diamond must have been taken by somebody in the house, he requested permission for himself and his men to search the servants’ rooms and boxes on the spot. My good mistress, like the generous high-bred woman she was, refused to let us be treated like thieves. “I will never consent to make such a return as that,” she said, “for all I owe to the faithful servants who are employed in my house.”\n\nMr. Superintendent made his bow, with a look in my direction, which said plainly, “Why employ me, if you are to tie my hands in this way?” As head of the servants, I felt directly that we were bound, in justice to all parties, not to profit by our mistress’s generosity. “We gratefully thank your ladyship,” I said; “but we ask your permission to do what is right in this matter by giving up our keys. When Gabriel Betteredge sets the example,” says I, stopping Superintendent Seegrave at the door, “the rest of the servants will follow, I promise you. There are my keys, to begin with!” My lady took me by the hand, and thanked me with the tears in her eyes. Lord! what would I not have given, at that moment, for the privilege of knocking Superintendent Seegrave down!\n\nAs I had promised for them, the other servants followed my lead, sorely against the grain, of course, but all taking the view that I took. The women were a sight to see, while the police-officers were rummaging among their things. The cook looked as if she could grill Mr. Superintendent alive on a furnace, and the other women looked as if they could eat him when he was done.\n\nThe search over, and no Diamond or sign of a Diamond being found, of course, anywhere, Superintendent Seegrave retired to my little room to consider with himself what he was to do next. He and his men had now been hours in the house, and had not advanced us one inch towards a discovery of how the Moonstone had been taken, or of whom we were to suspect as the thief.\n\nWhile the police-officer was still pondering in solitude, I was sent for to see Mr. Franklin in the library. To my unutterable astonishment, just as my hand was on the door, it was suddenly opened from the inside, and out walked Rosanna Spearman!\n\nAfter the library had been swept and cleaned in the morning, neither first nor second housemaid had any business in that room at any later period of the day. I stopped Rosanna Spearman, and charged her with a breach of domestic discipline on the spot.\n\n“What might you want in the library at this time of day?” I inquired.\n\n“Mr. Franklin Blake dropped one of his rings upstairs,” says Rosanna; “and I have been into the library to give it to him.” The girl’s face was all in a flush as she made me that answer; and she walked away with a toss of her head and a look of self-importance which I was quite at a loss to account for. The proceedings in the house had doubtless upset all the women-servants more or less; but none of them had gone clean out of their natural characters, as Rosanna, to all appearance, had now gone out of hers.\n\nI found Mr. Franklin writing at the library-table. He asked for a conveyance to the railway station the moment I entered the room. The first sound of his voice informed me that we now had the resolute side of him uppermost once more. The man made of cotton had disappeared; and the man made of iron sat before me again.\n\n“Going to London, sir?” I asked.\n\n“Going to telegraph to London,” says Mr. Franklin. “I have convinced my aunt that we must have a cleverer head than Superintendent Seegrave’s to help us; and I have got her permission to despatch a telegram to my father. He knows the Chief Commissioner of Police, and the Commissioner can lay his hand on the right man to solve the mystery of the Diamond. Talking of mysteries, by-the-bye,” says Mr. Franklin, dropping his voice, “I have another word to say to you before you go to the stables. Don’t breathe a word of it to anybody as yet; but either Rosanna Spearman’s head is not quite right, or I am afraid she knows more about the Moonstone than she ought to know.”\n\nI can hardly tell whether I was more startled or distressed at hearing him say that. If I had been younger, I might have confessed as much to Mr. Franklin. But when you are old, you acquire one excellent habit. In cases where you don’t see your way clearly, you hold your tongue.\n\n“She came in here with a ring I dropped in my bedroom,” Mr. Franklin went on. “When I had thanked her, of course I expected her to go. Instead of that, she stood opposite to me at the table, looking at me in the oddest manner—half frightened, and half familiar—I couldn’t make it out. ‘This is a strange thing about the Diamond, sir,’ she said, in a curiously sudden, headlong way. I said, ‘Yes, it was,’ and wondered what was coming next. Upon my honour, Betteredge, I think she must be wrong in the head! She said, ‘They will never find the Diamond, sir, will they? No! nor the person who took it—I’ll answer for that.’ She actually nodded and smiled at me! Before I could ask her what she meant, we heard your step outside. I suppose she was afraid of your catching her here. At any rate, she changed colour, and left the room. What on earth does it mean?”\n\nI could not bring myself to tell him the girl’s story, even then. It would have been almost as good as telling him that she was the thief. Besides, even if I had made a clean breast of it, and even supposing she was the thief, the reason why she should let out her secret to Mr. Franklin, of all the people in the world, would have been still as far to seek as ever.\n\n“I can’t bear the idea of getting the poor girl into a scrape, merely because she has a flighty way with her, and talks very strangely,” Mr. Franklin went on. “And yet if she had said to the Superintendent what she said to me, fool as he is, I’m afraid——” He stopped there, and left the rest unspoken.\n\n“The best way, sir,” I said, “will be for me to say two words privately to my mistress about it at the first opportunity. My lady has a very friendly interest in Rosanna; and the girl may only have been forward and foolish, after all. When there’s a mess of any kind in a house, sir, the women-servants like to look at the gloomy side—it gives the poor wretches a kind of importance in their own eyes. If there’s anybody ill, trust the women for prophesying that the person will die. If it’s a jewel lost, trust them for prophesying that it will never be found again.”\n\nThis view (which I am bound to say, I thought a probable view myself, on reflection) seemed to relieve Mr. Franklin mightily: he folded up his telegram, and dismissed the subject. On my way to the stables, to order the pony-chaise, I looked in at the servants’ hall, where they were at dinner. Rosanna Spearman was not among them. On inquiry, I found that she had been suddenly taken ill, and had gone upstairs to her own room to lie down.\n\n“Curious! She looked well enough when I saw her last,” I remarked.\n\nPenelope followed me out. “Don’t talk in that way before the rest of them, father,” she said. “You only make them harder on Rosanna than ever. The poor thing is breaking her heart about Mr. Franklin Blake.”\n\nHere was another view of the girl’s conduct. If it was possible for Penelope to be right, the explanation of Rosanna’s strange language and behaviour might have been all in this—that she didn’t care what she said, so long as she could surprise Mr. Franklin into speaking to her. Granting that to be the right reading of the riddle, it accounted, perhaps, for her flighty, self-conceited manner when she passed me in the hall. Though he had only said three words, still she had carried her point, and Mr. Franklin had spoken to her.\n\nI saw the pony harnessed myself. In the infernal network of mysteries and uncertainties that now surrounded us, I declare it was a relief to observe how well the buckles and straps understood each other! When you had seen the pony backed into the shafts of the chaise, you had seen something there was no doubt about. And that, let me tell you, was becoming a treat of the rarest kind in our household.\n\nGoing round with the chaise to the front door, I found not only Mr. Franklin, but Mr. Godfrey and Superintendent Seegrave also waiting for me on the steps.\n\nMr. Superintendent’s reflections (after failing to find the Diamond in the servants’ rooms or boxes) had led him, it appeared, to an entirely new conclusion. Still sticking to his first text, namely, that somebody in the house had stolen the jewel, our experienced officer was now of the opinion that the thief (he was wise enough not to name poor Penelope, whatever he might privately think of her!) had been acting in concert with the Indians; and he accordingly proposed shifting his inquiries to the jugglers in the prison at Frizinghall. Hearing of this new move, Mr. Franklin had volunteered to take the Superintendent back to the town, from which he could telegraph to London as easily as from our station. Mr. Godfrey, still devoutly believing in Mr. Seegrave, and greatly interested in witnessing the examination of the Indians, had begged leave to accompany the officer to Frizinghall. One of the two inferior policemen was to be left at the house, in case anything happened. The other was to go back with the Superintendent to the town. So the four places in the pony-chaise were just filled.\n\nBefore he took the reins to drive off, Mr. Franklin walked me away a few steps out of hearing of the others.\n\n“I will wait to telegraph to London,” he said, “till I see what comes of our examination of the Indians. My own conviction is, that this muddle-headed local police-officer is as much in the dark as ever, and is simply trying to gain time. The idea of any of the servants being in league with the Indians is a preposterous absurdity, in my opinion. Keep about the house, Betteredge, till I come back, and try what you can make of Rosanna Spearman. I don’t ask you to do anything degrading to your own self-respect, or anything cruel towards the girl. I only ask you to exercise your observation more carefully than usual. We will make as light of it as we can before my aunt—but this is a more important matter than you may suppose.”\n\n“It is a matter of twenty thousand pounds, sir,” I said, thinking of the value of the Diamond.\n\n“It’s a matter of quieting Rachel’s mind,” answered Mr. Franklin gravely. “I am very uneasy about her.”\n\nHe left me suddenly; as if he desired to cut short any further talk between us. I thought I understood why. Further talk might have let me into the secret of what Miss Rachel had said to him on the terrace.\n\nSo they drove away to Frizinghall. I was ready enough, in the girl’s own interest, to have a little talk with Rosanna in private. But the needful opportunity failed to present itself. She only came downstairs again at tea-time. When she did appear, she was flighty and excited, had what they call an hysterical attack, took a dose of sal-volatile by my lady’s order, and was sent back to her bed.\n\nThe day wore on to its end drearily and miserably enough, I can tell you. Miss Rachel still kept her room, declaring that she was too ill to come down to dinner that day. My lady was in such low spirits about her daughter, that I could not bring myself to make her additionally anxious, by reporting what Rosanna Spearman had said to Mr. Franklin. Penelope persisted in believing that she was to be forthwith tried, sentenced, and transported for theft. The other women took to their Bibles and hymn-books, and looked as sour as verjuice over their reading—a result, which I have observed, in my sphere of life, to follow generally on the performance of acts of piety at unaccustomed periods of the day. As for me, I hadn’t even heart enough to open my Robinson Crusoe. I went out into the yard, and, being hard up for a little cheerful society, set my chair by the kennels, and talked to the dogs.\n\nHalf an hour before dinner-time, the two gentlemen came back from Frizinghall, having arranged with Superintendent Seegrave that he was to return to us the next day. They had called on Mr. Murthwaite, the Indian traveller, at his present residence, near the town. At Mr. Franklin’s request, he had kindly given them the benefit of his knowledge of the language, in dealing with those two, out of the three Indians, who knew nothing of English. The examination, conducted carefully, and at great length, had ended in nothing; not the shadow of a reason being discovered for suspecting the jugglers of having tampered with any of our servants. On reaching that conclusion, Mr. Franklin had sent his telegraphic message to London, and there the matter now rested till tomorrow came.\n\nSo much for the history of the day that followed the birthday. Not a glimmer of light had broken in on us, so far. A day or two after, however, the darkness lifted a little. How, and with what result, you shall presently see.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\nThe Thursday night passed, and nothing happened. With the Friday morning came two pieces of news.\n\nItem the first: the baker’s man declared he had met Rosanna Spearman, on the previous afternoon, with a thick veil on, walking towards Frizinghall by the foot-path way over the moor. It seemed strange that anybody should be mistaken about Rosanna, whose shoulder marked her out pretty plainly, poor thing—but mistaken the man must have been; for Rosanna, as you know, had been all the Thursday afternoon ill upstairs in her room.\n\nItem the second came through the postman. Worthy Mr. Candy had said one more of his many unlucky things, when he drove off in the rain on the birthday night, and told me that a doctor’s skin was waterproof. In spite of his skin, the wet had got through him. He had caught a chill that night, and was now down with a fever. The last accounts, brought by the postman, represented him to be light-headed—talking nonsense as glibly, poor man, in his delirium as he often talked it in his sober senses. We were all sorry for the little doctor; but Mr. Franklin appeared to regret his illness, chiefly on Miss Rachel’s account. From what he said to my lady, while I was in the room at breakfast-time, he appeared to think that Miss Rachel—if the suspense about the Moonstone was not soon set at rest—might stand in urgent need of the best medical advice at our disposal.\n\nBreakfast had not been over long, when a telegram from Mr. Blake, the elder, arrived, in answer to his son. It informed us that he had laid hands (by help of his friend, the Commissioner) on the right man to help us. The name of him was Sergeant Cuff; and the arrival of him from London might be expected by the morning train.\n\nAt reading the name of the new police-officer, Mr. Franklin gave a start. It seems that he had heard some curious anecdotes about Sergeant Cuff, from his father’s lawyer, during his stay in London.\n\n“I begin to hope we are seeing the end of our anxieties already,” he said. “If half the stories I have heard are true, when it comes to unravelling a mystery, there isn’t the equal in England of Sergeant Cuff!”\n\nWe all got excited and impatient as the time drew near for the appearance of this renowned and capable character. Superintendent Seegrave, returning to us at his appointed time, and hearing that the Sergeant was expected, instantly shut himself up in a room, with pen, ink, and paper, to make notes of the Report which would be certainly expected from him. I should have liked to have gone to the station myself, to fetch the Sergeant. But my lady’s carriage and horses were not to be thought of, even for the celebrated Cuff; and the pony-chaise was required later for Mr. Godfrey. He deeply regretted being obliged to leave his aunt at such an anxious time; and he kindly put off the hour of his departure till as late as the last train, for the purpose of hearing what the clever London police-officer thought of the case. But on Friday night he must be in town, having a Ladies’ Charity, in difficulties, waiting to consult him on Saturday morning.\n\nWhen the time came for the Sergeant’s arrival, I went down to the gate to look out for him.\n\nA fly from the railway drove up as I reached the lodge; and out got a grizzled, elderly man, so miserably lean that he looked as if he had not got an ounce of flesh on his bones in any part of him. He was dressed all in decent black, with a white cravat round his neck. His face was as sharp as a hatchet, and the skin of it was as yellow and dry and withered as an autumn leaf. His eyes, of a steely light grey, had a very disconcerting trick, when they encountered your eyes, of looking as if they expected something more from you than you were aware of yourself. His walk was soft; his voice was melancholy; his long lanky fingers were hooked like claws. He might have been a parson, or an undertaker—or anything else you like, except what he really was. A more complete opposite to Superintendent Seegrave than Sergeant Cuff, and a less comforting officer to look at, for a family in distress, I defy you to discover, search where you may.\n\n“Is this Lady Verinder’s?” he asked.\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“I am Sergeant Cuff.”\n\n“This way, sir, if you please.”\n\nOn our road to the house, I mentioned my name and position in the family, to satisfy him that he might speak to me about the business on which my lady was to employ him. Not a word did he say about the business, however, for all that. He admired the grounds, and remarked that he felt the sea air very brisk and refreshing. I privately wondered, on my side, how the celebrated Cuff had got his reputation. We reached the house, in the temper of two strange dogs, coupled up together for the first time in their lives by the same chain.\n\nAsking for my lady, and hearing that she was in one of the conservatories, we went round to the gardens at the back, and sent a servant to seek her. While we were waiting, Sergeant Cuff looked through the evergreen arch on our left, spied out our rosery, and walked straight in, with the first appearance of anything like interest that he had shown yet. To the gardener’s astonishment, and to my disgust, this celebrated policeman proved to be quite a mine of learning on the trumpery subject of rose-gardens.\n\n“Ah, you’ve got the right exposure here to the south and sou’-west,” says the Sergeant, with a wag of his grizzled head, and a streak of pleasure in his melancholy voice. “This is the shape for a rosery—nothing like a circle set in a square. Yes, yes; with walks between all the beds. But they oughtn’t to be gravel walks like these. Grass, Mr. Gardener—grass walks between your roses; gravel’s too hard for them. That’s a sweet pretty bed of white roses and blush roses. They always mix well together, don’t they? Here’s the white musk rose, Mr. Betteredge—our old English rose holding up its head along with the best and the newest of them. Pretty dear!” says the Sergeant, fondling the Musk Rose with his lanky fingers, and speaking to it as if he was speaking to a child.\n\nThis was a nice sort of man to recover Miss Rachel’s Diamond, and to find out the thief who stole it!\n\n“You seem to be fond of roses, Sergeant?” I remarked.\n\n“I haven’t much time to be fond of anything,” says Sergeant Cuff. “But when I have a moment’s fondness to bestow, most times, Mr. Betteredge, the roses get it. I began my life among them in my father’s nursery garden, and I shall end my life among them, if I can. Yes. One of these days (please God) I shall retire from catching thieves, and try my hand at growing roses. There will be grass walks, Mr. Gardener, between my beds,” says the Sergeant, on whose mind the gravel paths of our rosery seemed to dwell unpleasantly.\n\n“It seems an odd taste, sir,” I ventured to say, “for a man in your line of life.”\n\n“If you will look about you (which most people won’t do),” says Sergeant Cuff, “you will see that the nature of a man’s tastes is, most times, as opposite as possible to the nature of a man’s business. Show me any two things more opposite one from the other than a rose and a thief; and I’ll correct my tastes accordingly—if it isn’t too late at my time of life. You find the damask rose a goodish stock for most of the tender sorts, don’t you, Mr. Gardener? Ah! I thought so. Here’s a lady coming. Is it Lady Verinder?”\n\nHe had seen her before either I or the gardener had seen her, though we knew which way to look, and he didn’t. I began to think him rather a quicker man than he appeared to be at first sight.\n\nThe Sergeant’s appearance, or the Sergeant’s errand—one or both—seemed to cause my lady some little embarrassment. She was, for the first time in all my experience of her, at a loss what to say at an interview with a stranger. Sergeant Cuff put her at her ease directly. He asked if any other person had been employed about the robbery before we sent for him; and hearing that another person had been called in, and was now in the house, begged leave to speak to him before anything else was done.\n\nMy lady led the way back. Before he followed her, the Sergeant relieved his mind on the subject of the gravel walks by a parting word to the gardener. “Get her ladyship to try grass,” he said, with a sour look at the paths. “No gravel! no gravel!”\n\nWhy Superintendent Seegrave should have appeared to be several sizes smaller than life, on being presented to Sergeant Cuff, I can’t undertake to explain. I can only state the fact. They retired together; and remained a weary long time shut up from all mortal intrusion. When they came out, Mr. Superintendent was excited, and Mr. Sergeant was yawning.\n\n“The Sergeant wishes to see Miss Verinder’s sitting-room,” says Mr. Seegrave, addressing me with great pomp and eagerness. “The Sergeant may have some questions to ask. Attend the Sergeant, if you please!”\n\nWhile I was being ordered about in this way, I looked at the great Cuff. The great Cuff, on his side, looked at Superintendent Seegrave in that quietly expecting way which I have already noticed. I can’t affirm that he was on the watch for his brother officer’s speedy appearance in the character of an Ass—I can only say that I strongly suspected it.\n\nI led the way upstairs. The Sergeant went softly all over the Indian cabinet and all round the “boudoir;” asking questions (occasionally only of Mr. Superintendent, and continually of me), the drift of which I believe to have been equally unintelligible to both of us. In due time, his course brought him to the door, and put him face to face with the decorative painting that you know of. He laid one lean inquiring finger on the small smear, just under the lock, which Superintendent Seegrave had already noticed, when he reproved the women-servants for all crowding together into the room.\n\n“That’s a pity,” says Sergeant Cuff. “How did it happen?”\n\nHe put the question to me. I answered that the women-servants had crowded into the room on the previous morning, and that some of their petticoats had done the mischief, “Superintendent Seegrave ordered them out, sir,” I added, “before they did any more harm.”\n\n“Right!” says Mr. Superintendent in his military way. “I ordered them out. The petticoats did it, Sergeant—the petticoats did it.”\n\n“Did you notice which petticoat did it?” asked Sergeant Cuff, still addressing himself, not to his brother-officer, but to me.\n\n“No, sir.”\n\nHe turned to Superintendent Seegrave upon that, and said, “You noticed, I suppose?”\n\nMr. Superintendent looked a little taken aback; but he made the best of it. “I can’t charge my memory, Sergeant,” he said, “a mere trifle—a mere trifle.”\n\nSergeant Cuff looked at Mr. Seegrave, as he had looked at the gravel walks in the rosery, and gave us, in his melancholy way, the first taste of his quality which we had had yet.\n\n“I made a private inquiry last week, Mr. Superintendent,” he said. “At one end of the inquiry there was a murder, and at the other end there was a spot of ink on a table cloth that nobody could account for. In all my experience along the dirtiest ways of this dirty little world, I have never met with such a thing as a trifle yet. Before we go a step further in this business we must see the petticoat that made the smear, and we must know for certain when that paint was wet.”\n\nMr. Superintendent—taking his set-down rather sulkily—asked if he should summon the women. Sergeant Cuff, after considering a minute, sighed, and shook his head.\n\n“No,” he said, “we’ll take the matter of the paint first. It’s a question of Yes or No with the paint—which is short. It’s a question of petticoats with the women—which is long. What o’clock was it when the servants were in this room yesterday morning? Eleven o’clock—eh? Is there anybody in the house who knows whether that paint was wet or dry, at eleven yesterday morning?”\n\n“Her ladyship’s nephew, Mr. Franklin Blake, knows,” I said.\n\n“Is the gentleman in the house?”\n\nMr. Franklin was as close at hand as could be—waiting for his first chance of being introduced to the great Cuff. In half a minute he was in the room, and was giving his evidence as follows:\n\n“That door, Sergeant,” he said, “has been painted by Miss Verinder, under my inspection, with my help, and in a vehicle of my own composition. The vehicle dries whatever colours may be used with it, in twelve hours.”\n\n“Do you remember when the smeared bit was done, sir?” asked the Sergeant.\n\n“Perfectly,” answered Mr. Franklin. “That was the last morsel of the door to be finished. We wanted to get it done, on Wednesday last—and I myself completed it by three in the afternoon, or soon after.”\n\n“Today is Friday,” said Sergeant Cuff, addressing himself to Superintendent Seegrave. “Let us reckon back, sir. At three on the Wednesday afternoon, that bit of the painting was completed. The vehicle dried it in twelve hours—that is to say, dried it by three o’clock on Thursday morning. At eleven on Thursday morning you held your inquiry here. Take three from eleven, and eight remains. That paint had been eight hours dry, Mr. Superintendent, when you supposed that the women-servants’ petticoats smeared it.”\n\nFirst knock-down blow for Mr. Seegrave! If he had not suspected poor Penelope, I should have pitied him.\n\nHaving settled the question of the paint, Sergeant Cuff, from that moment, gave his brother-officer up as a bad job—and addressed himself to Mr. Franklin, as the more promising assistant of the two.\n\n“It’s quite on the cards, sir,” he said, “that you have put the clue into our hands.”\n\nAs the words passed his lips, the bedroom door opened, and Miss Rachel came out among us suddenly.\n\nShe addressed herself to the Sergeant, without appearing to notice (or to heed) that he was a perfect stranger to her.\n\n“Did you say,” she asked, pointing to Mr. Franklin, “that he had put the clue into your hands?”\n\n(“This is Miss Verinder,” I whispered, behind the Sergeant.)\n\n“That gentleman, miss,” says the Sergeant—with his steely-grey eyes carefully studying my young lady’s face—“has possibly put the clue into our hands.”\n\nShe turned for one moment, and tried to look at Mr. Franklin. I say, tried, for she suddenly looked away again before their eyes met. There seemed to be some strange disturbance in her mind. She coloured up, and then she turned pale again. With the paleness, there came a new look into her face—a look which it startled me to see.\n\n“Having answered your question, miss,” says the Sergeant, “I beg leave to make an inquiry in my turn. There is a smear on the painting of your door, here. Do you happen to know when it was done? or who did it?”\n\nInstead of making any reply, Miss Rachel went on with her questions, as if he had not spoken, or as if she had not heard him.\n\n“Are you another police-officer?” she asked.\n\n“I am Sergeant Cuff, miss, of the Detective Police.”\n\n“Do you think a young lady’s advice worth having?”\n\n“I shall be glad to hear it, miss.”\n\n“Do your duty by yourself—and don’t allow Mr. Franklin Blake to help you!”\n\nShe said those words so spitefully, so savagely, with such an extraordinary outbreak of ill-will towards Mr. Franklin, in her voice and in her look, that—though I had known her from a baby, though I loved and honoured her next to my lady herself—I was ashamed of Miss Rachel for the first time in my life.\n\nSergeant Cuff’s immovable eyes never stirred from off her face. “Thank you, miss,” he said. “Do you happen to know anything about the smear? Might you have done it by accident yourself?”\n\n“I know nothing about the smear.”\n\nWith that answer, she turned away, and shut herself up again in her bedroom. This time, I heard her—as Penelope had heard her before—burst out crying as soon as she was alone again.\n\nI couldn’t bring myself to look at the Sergeant—I looked at Mr. Franklin, who stood nearest to me. He seemed to be even more sorely distressed at what had passed than I was.\n\n“I told you I was uneasy about her,” he said. “And now you see why.”\n\n“Miss Verinder appears to be a little out of temper about the loss of her Diamond,” remarked the Sergeant. “It’s a valuable jewel. Natural enough! natural enough!”\n\nHere was the excuse that I had made for her (when she forgot herself before Superintendent Seegrave, on the previous day) being made for her over again, by a man who couldn’t have had my interest in making it—for he was a perfect stranger! A kind of cold shudder ran through me, which I couldn’t account for at the time. I know, now, that I must have got my first suspicion, at that moment, of a new light (and horrid light) having suddenly fallen on the case, in the mind of Sergeant Cuff—purely and entirely in consequence of what he had seen in Miss Rachel, and heard from Miss Rachel, at that first interview between them.\n\n“A young lady’s tongue is a privileged member, sir,” says the Sergeant to Mr. Franklin. “Let us forget what has passed, and go straight on with this business. Thanks to you, we know when the paint was dry. The next thing to discover is when the paint was last seen without that smear. You have got a head on your shoulders—and you understand what I mean.”\n\nMr. Franklin composed himself, and came back with an effort from Miss Rachel to the matter in hand.\n\n“I think I do understand,” he said. “The more we narrow the question of time, the more we also narrow the field of inquiry.”\n\n“That’s it, sir,” said the Sergeant. “Did you notice your work here, on the Wednesday afternoon, after you had done it?”\n\nMr. Franklin shook his head, and answered, “I can’t say I did.”\n\n“Did you?” inquired Sergeant Cuff, turning to me.\n\n“I can’t say I did either, sir.”\n\n“Who was the last person in the room, the last thing on Wednesday night?”\n\n“Miss Rachel, I suppose, sir.”\n\nMr. Franklin struck in there, “Or possibly your daughter, Betteredge.” He turned to Sergeant Cuff, and explained that my daughter was Miss Verinder’s maid.\n\n“Mr. Betteredge, ask your daughter to step up. Stop!” says the Sergeant, taking me away to the window, out of earshot. “Your Superintendent here,” he went on, in a whisper, “has made a pretty full report to me of the manner in which he has managed this case. Among other things, he has, by his own confession, set the servants’ backs up. It’s very important to smooth them down again. Tell your daughter, and tell the rest of them, these two things, with my compliments: First, that I have no evidence before me, yet, that the Diamond has been stolen; I only know that the Diamond has been lost. Second, that my business here with the servants is simply to ask them to lay their heads together and help me to find it.”\n\nMy experience of the women-servants, when Superintendent Seegrave laid his embargo on their rooms, came in handy here.\n\n“May I make so bold, Sergeant, as to tell the women a third thing?” I asked. “Are they free (with your compliments) to fidget up and downstairs, and whisk in and out of their bedrooms, if the fit takes them?”\n\n“Perfectly free,” said the Sergeant.\n\n“That will smooth them down, sir,” I remarked, “from the cook to the scullion.”\n\n“Go, and do it at once, Mr. Betteredge.”\n\nI did it in less than five minutes. There was only one difficulty when I came to the bit about the bedrooms. It took a pretty stiff exertion of my authority, as chief, to prevent the whole of the female household from following me and Penelope upstairs, in the character of volunteer witnesses in a burning fever of anxiety to help Sergeant Cuff.\n\nThe Sergeant seemed to approve of Penelope. He became a trifle less dreary; and he looked much as he had looked when he noticed the white musk rose in the flower-garden. Here is my daughter’s evidence, as drawn off from her by the Sergeant. She gave it, I think, very prettily—but, there! she is my child all over: nothing of her mother in her; Lord bless you, nothing of her mother in her!\n\nPenelope examined: Took a lively interest in the painting on the door, having helped to mix the colours. Noticed the bit of work under the lock, because it was the last bit done. Had seen it, some hours afterwards, without a smear. Had left it, as late as twelve at night, without a smear. Had, at that hour, wished her young lady good-night in the bedroom; had heard the clock strike in the “boudoir”; had her hand at the time on the handle of the painted door; knew the paint was wet (having helped to mix the colours, as aforesaid); took particular pains not to touch it; could swear that she held up the skirts of her dress, and that there was no smear on the paint then; could not swear that her dress mightn’t have touched it accidentally in going out; remembered the dress she had on, because it was new, a present from Miss Rachel; her father remembered, and could speak to it, too; could, and would, and did fetch it; dress recognised by her father as the dress she wore that night; skirts examined, a long job from the size of them; not the ghost of a paint-stain discovered anywhere. End of Penelope’s evidence—and very pretty and convincing, too. Signed, Gabriel Betteredge.\n\nThe Sergeant’s next proceeding was to question me about any large dogs in the house who might have got into the room, and done the mischief with a whisk of their tails. Hearing that this was impossible, he next sent for a magnifying-glass, and tried how the smear looked, seen that way. No skin-mark (as of a human hand) printed off on the paint. All the signs visible—signs which told that the paint had been smeared by some loose article of somebody’s dress touching it in going by. That somebody (putting together Penelope’s evidence and Mr. Franklin’s evidence) must have been in the room, and done the mischief, between midnight and three o’clock on the Thursday morning.\n\nHaving brought his investigation to this point, Sergeant Cuff discovered that such a person as Superintendent Seegrave was still left in the room, upon which he summed up the proceedings for his brother-officer’s benefit, as follows:\n\n“This trifle of yours, Mr. Superintendent,” says the Sergeant, pointing to the place on the door, “has grown a little in importance since you noticed it last. At the present stage of the inquiry there are, as I take it, three discoveries to make, starting from that smear. Find out (first) whether there is any article of dress in this house with the smear of the paint on it. Find out (second) who that dress belongs to. Find out (third) how the person can account for having been in this room, and smeared the paint, between midnight and three in the morning. If the person can’t satisfy you, you haven’t far to look for the hand that has got the Diamond. I’ll work this by myself, if you please, and detain you no longer from your regular business in the town. You have got one of your men here, I see. Leave him here at my disposal, in case I want him—and allow me to wish you good morning.”\n\nSuperintendent Seegrave’s respect for the Sergeant was great; but his respect for himself was greater still. Hit hard by the celebrated Cuff, he hit back smartly, to the best of his ability, on leaving the room.\n\n“I have abstained from expressing any opinion, so far,” says Mr. Superintendent, with his military voice still in good working order. “I have now only one remark to offer on leaving this case in your hands. There is such a thing, Sergeant, as making a mountain out of a molehill. Good morning.”\n\n“There is also such a thing as making nothing out of a molehill, in consequence of your head being too high to see it.” Having returned his brother-officer’s compliments in those terms, Sergeant Cuff wheeled about, and walked away to the window by himself.\n\nMr. Franklin and I waited to see what was coming next. The Sergeant stood at the window with his hands in his pockets, looking out, and whistling the tune of “The Last Rose of Summer” softly to himself. Later in the proceedings, I discovered that he only forgot his manners so far as to whistle, when his mind was hard at work, seeing its way inch by inch to its own private ends, on which occasions “The Last Rose of Summer” evidently helped and encouraged him. I suppose it fitted in somehow with his character. It reminded him, you see, of his favourite roses, and, as he whistled it, it was the most melancholy tune going.\n\nTurning from the window, after a minute or two, the Sergeant walked into the middle of the room, and stopped there, deep in thought, with his eyes on Miss Rachel’s bedroom door. After a little he roused himself, nodded his head, as much as to say, “That will do,” and, addressing me, asked for ten minutes’ conversation with my mistress, at her ladyship’s earliest convenience.\n\nLeaving the room with this message, I heard Mr. Franklin ask the Sergeant a question, and stopped to hear the answer also at the threshold of the door.\n\n“Can you guess yet,” inquired Mr. Franklin, “who has stolen the Diamond?”\n\n“Nobody has stolen the Diamond,” answered Sergeant Cuff.\n\nWe both started at that extraordinary view of the case, and both earnestly begged him to tell us what he meant.\n\n“Wait a little,” said the Sergeant. “The pieces of the puzzle are not all put together yet.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\nI found my lady in her own sitting room. She started and looked annoyed when I mentioned that Sergeant Cuff wished to speak to her.\n\n“Must I see him?” she asked. “Can’t you represent me, Gabriel?”\n\nI felt at a loss to understand this, and showed it plainly, I suppose, in my face. My lady was so good as to explain herself.\n\n“I am afraid my nerves are a little shaken,” she said. “There is something in that police-officer from London which I recoil from—I don’t know why. I have a presentiment that he is bringing trouble and misery with him into the house. Very foolish, and very unlike me—but so it is.”\n\nI hardly knew what to say to this. The more I saw of Sergeant Cuff, the better I liked him. My lady rallied a little after having opened her heart to me—being, naturally, a woman of a high courage, as I have already told you.\n\n“If I must see him, I must,” she said. “But I can’t prevail on myself to see him alone. Bring him in, Gabriel, and stay here as long as he stays.”\n\nThis was the first attack of the megrims that I remembered in my mistress since the time when she was a young girl. I went back to the “boudoir.” Mr. Franklin strolled out into the garden, and joined Mr. Godfrey, whose time for departure was now drawing near. Sergeant Cuff and I went straight to my mistress’s room.\n\nI declare my lady turned a shade paler at the sight of him! She commanded herself, however, in other respects, and asked the Sergeant if he had any objection to my being present. She was so good as to add, that I was her trusted adviser, as well as her old servant, and that in anything which related to the household I was the person whom it might be most profitable to consult. The Sergeant politely answered that he would take my presence as a favour, having something to say about the servants in general, and having found my experience in that quarter already of some use to him. My lady pointed to two chairs, and we set in for our conference immediately.\n\n“I have already formed an opinion on this case,” says Sergeant Cuff, “which I beg your ladyship’s permission to keep to myself for the present. My business now is to mention what I have discovered upstairs in Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, and what I have decided (with your ladyship’s leave) on doing next.”\n\nHe then went into the matter of the smear on the paint, and stated the conclusions he drew from it—just as he had stated them (only with greater respect of language) to Superintendent Seegrave. “One thing,” he said, in conclusion, “is certain. The Diamond is missing out of the drawer in the cabinet. Another thing is next to certain. The marks from the smear on the door must be on some article of dress belonging to somebody in this house. We must discover that article of dress before we go a step further.”\n\n“And that discovery,” remarked my mistress, “implies, I presume, the discovery of the thief?”\n\n“I beg your ladyship’s pardon—I don’t say the Diamond is stolen. I only say, at present, that the Diamond is missing. The discovery of the stained dress may lead the way to finding it.”\n\nHer ladyship looked at me. “Do you understand this?” she said.\n\n“Sergeant Cuff understands it, my lady,” I answered.\n\n“How do you propose to discover the stained dress?” inquired my mistress, addressing herself once more to the Sergeant. “My good servants, who have been with me for years, have, I am ashamed to say, had their boxes and rooms searched already by the other officer. I can’t and won’t permit them to be insulted in that way a second time!”\n\n(There was a mistress to serve! There was a woman in ten thousand, if you like!)\n\n“That is the very point I was about to put to your ladyship,” said the Sergeant. “The other officer has done a world of harm to this inquiry, by letting the servants see that he suspected them. If I give them cause to think themselves suspected a second time, there’s no knowing what obstacles they may not throw in my way—the women especially. At the same time, their boxes must be searched again—for this plain reason, that the first investigation only looked for the Diamond, and that the second investigation must look for the stained dress. I quite agree with you, my lady, that the servants’ feelings ought to be consulted. But I am equally clear that the servants’ wardrobes ought to be searched.”\n\nThis looked very like a dead-lock. My lady said so, in choicer language than mine.\n\n“I have got a plan to meet the difficulty,” said Sergeant Cuff, “if your ladyship will consent to it. I propose explaining the case to the servants.”\n\n“The women will think themselves suspected directly,” I said, interrupting him.\n\n“The women won’t, Mr. Betteredge,” answered the Sergeant, “if I can tell them I am going to examine the wardrobes of everybody—from her ladyship downwards—who slept in the house on Wednesday night. It’s a mere formality,” he added, with a side look at my mistress; “but the servants will accept it as even dealing between them and their betters; and, instead of hindering the investigation, they will make a point of honour of assisting it.”\n\nI saw the truth of that. My lady, after her first surprise was over, saw the truth of it also.\n\n“You are certain the investigation is necessary?” she said.\n\n“It’s the shortest way that I can see, my lady, to the end we have in view.”\n\nMy mistress rose to ring the bell for her maid. “You shall speak to the servants,” she said, “with the keys of my wardrobe in your hand.”\n\nSergeant Cuff stopped her by a very unexpected question.\n\n“Hadn’t we better make sure first,” he asked, “that the other ladies and gentlemen in the house will consent, too?”\n\n“The only other lady in the house is Miss Verinder,” answered my mistress, with a look of surprise. “The only gentlemen are my nephews, Mr. Blake and Mr. Ablewhite. There is not the least fear of a refusal from any of the three.”\n\nI reminded my lady here that Mr. Godfrey was going away. As I said the words, Mr. Godfrey himself knocked at the door to say good-bye, and was followed in by Mr. Franklin, who was going with him to the station. My lady explained the difficulty. Mr. Godfrey settled it directly. He called to Samuel, through the window, to take his portmanteau upstairs again, and he then put the key himself into Sergeant Cuff’s hand. “My luggage can follow me to London,” he said, “when the inquiry is over.” The Sergeant received the key with a becoming apology. “I am sorry to put you to any inconvenience, sir, for a mere formality; but the example of their betters will do wonders in reconciling the servants to this inquiry.” Mr. Godfrey, after taking leave of my lady, in a most sympathising manner, left a farewell message for Miss Rachel, the terms of which made it clear to my mind that he had not taken No for an answer, and that he meant to put the marriage question to her once more, at the next opportunity. Mr. Franklin, on following his cousin out, informed the Sergeant that all his clothes were open to examination, and that nothing he possessed was kept under lock and key. Sergeant Cuff made his best acknowledgments. His views, you will observe, had been met with the utmost readiness by my lady, by Mr. Godfrey, and by Mr. Franklin. There was only Miss Rachel now wanting to follow their lead, before we called the servants together, and began the search for the stained dress.\n\nMy lady’s unaccountable objection to the Sergeant seemed to make our conference more distasteful to her than ever, as soon as we were left alone again. “If I send you down Miss Verinder’s keys,” she said to him, “I presume I shall have done all you want of me for the present?”\n\n“I beg your ladyship’s pardon,” said Sergeant Cuff. “Before we begin, I should like, if convenient, to have the washing-book. The stained article of dress may be an article of linen. If the search leads to nothing, I want to be able to account next for all the linen in the house, and for all the linen sent to the wash. If there is an article missing, there will be at least a presumption that it has got the paint-stain on it, and that it has been purposely made away with, yesterday or today, by the person owning it. Superintendent Seegrave,” added the Sergeant, turning to me, “pointed the attention of the women-servants to the smear, when they all crowded into the room on Thursday morning. That may turn out, Mr. Betteredge, to have been one more of Superintendent Seegrave’s many mistakes.”\n\nMy lady desired me to ring the bell, and order the washing-book. She remained with us until it was produced, in case Sergeant Cuff had any further request to make of her after looking at it.\n\nThe washing-book was brought in by Rosanna Spearman. The girl had come down to breakfast that morning miserably pale and haggard, but sufficiently recovered from her illness of the previous day to do her usual work. Sergeant Cuff looked attentively at our second housemaid—at her face, when she came in; at her crooked shoulder, when she went out.\n\n“Have you anything more to say to me?” asked my lady, still as eager as ever to be out of the Sergeant’s society.\n\nThe great Cuff opened the washing-book, understood it perfectly in half a minute, and shut it up again. “I venture to trouble your ladyship with one last question,” he said. “Has the young woman who brought us this book been in your employment as long as the other servants?”\n\n“Why do you ask?” said my lady.\n\n“The last time I saw her,” answered the Sergeant, “she was in prison for theft.”\n\nAfter that, there was no help for it, but to tell him the truth. My mistress dwelt strongly on Rosanna’s good conduct in her service, and on the high opinion entertained of her by the matron at the Reformatory. “You don’t suspect her, I hope?” my lady added, in conclusion, very earnestly.\n\n“I have already told your ladyship that I don’t suspect any person in the house of thieving—up to the present time.”\n\nAfter that answer, my lady rose to go upstairs, and ask for Miss Rachel’s keys. The Sergeant was beforehand with me in opening the door for her. He made a very low bow. My lady shuddered as she passed him.\n\nWe waited, and waited, and no keys appeared. Sergeant Cuff made no remark to me. He turned his melancholy face to the window; he put his lanky hands into his pockets; and he whistled “The Last Rose of Summer” softly to himself.\n\nAt last, Samuel came in, not with the keys, but with a morsel of paper for me. I got at my spectacles, with some fumbling and difficulty, feeling the Sergeant’s dismal eyes fixed on me all the time. There were two or three lines on the paper, written in pencil by my lady. They informed me that Miss Rachel flatly refused to have her wardrobe examined. Asked for her reasons, she had burst out crying. Asked again, she had said: “I won’t, because I won’t. I must yield to force if you use it, but I will yield to nothing else.” I understood my lady’s disinclination to face Sergeant Cuff with such an answer from her daughter as that. If I had not been too old for the amiable weaknesses of youth, I believe I should have blushed at the notion of facing him myself.\n\n“Any news of Miss Verinder’s keys?” asked the Sergeant.\n\n“My young lady refuses to have her wardrobe examined.”\n\n“Ah!” said the Sergeant.\n\nHis voice was not quite in such a perfect state of discipline as his face. When he said “Ah!” he said it in the tone of a man who had heard something which he expected to hear. He half angered and half frightened me—why, I couldn’t tell, but he did it.\n\n“Must the search be given up?” I asked.\n\n“Yes,” said the Sergeant, “the search must be given up, because your young lady refuses to submit to it like the rest. We must examine all the wardrobes in the house or none. Send Mr. Ablewhite’s portmanteau to London by the next train, and return the washing-book, with my compliments and thanks, to the young woman who brought it in.”\n\nHe laid the washing-book on the table, and taking out his penknife, began to trim his nails.\n\n“You don’t seem to be much disappointed,” I said.\n\n“No,” said Sergeant Cuff; “I am not much disappointed.”\n\nI tried to make him explain himself.\n\n“Why should Miss Rachel put an obstacle in your way?” I inquired. “Isn’t it her interest to help you?”\n\n“Wait a little, Mr. Betteredge—wait a little.”\n\nCleverer heads than mine might have seen his drift. Or a person less fond of Miss Rachel than I was, might have seen his drift. My lady’s horror of him might (as I have since thought) have meant that she saw his drift (as the scripture says) “in a glass darkly.” I didn’t see it yet—that’s all I know.\n\n“What’s to be done next?” I asked.\n\nSergeant Cuff finished the nail on which he was then at work, looked at it for a moment with a melancholy interest, and put up his penknife.\n\n“Come out into the garden,” he said, “and let’s have a look at the roses.”\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\nThe nearest way to the garden, on going out of my lady’s sitting-room, was by the shrubbery path, which you already know of. For the sake of your better understanding of what is now to come, I may add to this, that the shrubbery path was Mr. Franklin’s favourite walk. When he was out in the grounds, and when we failed to find him anywhere else, we generally found him here.\n\nI am afraid I must own that I am rather an obstinate old man. The more firmly Sergeant Cuff kept his thoughts shut up from me, the more firmly I persisted in trying to look in at them. As we turned into the shrubbery path, I attempted to circumvent him in another way.\n\n“As things are now,” I said, “if I was in your place, I should be at my wits’ end.”\n\n“If you were in my place,” answered the Sergeant, “you would have formed an opinion—and, as things are now, any doubt you might previously have felt about your own conclusions would be completely set at rest. Never mind for the present what those conclusions are, Mr. Betteredge. I haven’t brought you out here to draw me like a badger; I have brought you out here to ask for some information. You might have given it to me no doubt, in the house, instead of out of it. But doors and listeners have a knack of getting together; and, in my line of life, we cultivate a healthy taste for the open air.”\n\nWho was to circumvent this man? I gave in—and waited as patiently as I could to hear what was coming next.\n\n“We won’t enter into your young lady’s motives,” the Sergeant went on; “we will only say it’s a pity she declines to assist me, because, by so doing, she makes this investigation more difficult than it might otherwise have been. We must now try to solve the mystery of the smear on the door—which, you may take my word for it, means the mystery of the Diamond also—in some other way. I have decided to see the servants, and to search their thoughts and actions, Mr. Betteredge, instead of searching their wardrobes. Before I begin, however, I want to ask you a question or two. You are an observant man—did you notice anything strange in any of the servants (making due allowance, of course, for fright and fluster), after the loss of the Diamond was found out? Any particular quarrel among them? Anyone of them not in his or her usual spirits? Unexpectedly out of temper, for instance? or unexpectedly taken ill?”\n\nI had just time to think of Rosanna Spearman’s sudden illness at yesterday’s dinner—but not time to make any answer—when I saw Sergeant Cuff’s eyes suddenly turn aside towards the shrubbery; and I heard him say softly to himself, “Hullo!”\n\n“What’s the matter?” I asked.\n\n“A touch of the rheumatics in my back,” said the Sergeant, in a loud voice, as if he wanted some third person to hear us. “We shall have a change in the weather before long.”\n\nA few steps further brought us to the corner of the house. Turning off sharp to the right, we entered on the terrace, and went down, by the steps in the middle, into the garden below. Sergeant Cuff stopped there, in the open space, where we could see round us on every side.\n\n“About that young person, Rosanna Spearman?” he said. “It isn’t very likely, with her personal appearance, that she has got a lover. But, for the girl’s own sake, I must ask you at once whether she has provided herself with a sweetheart, poor wretch, like the rest of them?”\n\nWhat on earth did he mean, under present circumstances, by putting such a question to me as that? I stared at him, instead of answering him.\n\n“I saw Rosanna Spearman hiding in the shrubbery as we went by,” said the Sergeant.\n\n“When you said ‘Hullo’?”\n\n“Yes—when I said ‘Hullo!’ If there’s a sweetheart in the case, the hiding doesn’t much matter. If there isn’t—as things are in this house—the hiding is a highly suspicious circumstance, and it will be my painful duty to act on it accordingly.”\n\nWhat, in God’s name, was I to say to him? I knew the shrubbery was Mr. Franklin’s favourite walk; I knew he would most likely turn that way when he came back from the station; I knew that Penelope had over and over again caught her fellow-servant hanging about there, and had always declared to me that Rosanna’s object was to attract Mr. Franklin’s attention. If my daughter was right, she might well have been lying in wait for Mr. Franklin’s return when the Sergeant noticed her. I was put between the two difficulties of mentioning Penelope’s fanciful notion as if it was mine, or of leaving an unfortunate creature to suffer the consequences, the very serious consequences, of exciting the suspicion of Sergeant Cuff. Out of pure pity for the girl—on my soul and my character, out of pure pity for the girl—I gave the Sergeant the necessary explanations, and told him that Rosanna had been mad enough to set her heart on Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nSergeant Cuff never laughed. On the few occasions when anything amused him, he curled up a little at the corners of the lips, nothing more. He curled up now.\n\n“Hadn’t you better say she’s mad enough to be an ugly girl and only a servant?” he asked. “The falling in love with a gentleman of Mr. Franklin Blake’s manners and appearance doesn’t seem to me to be the maddest part of her conduct by any means. However, I’m glad the thing is cleared up: it relieves one’s mind to have things cleared up. Yes, I’ll keep it a secret, Mr. Betteredge. I like to be tender to human infirmity—though I don’t get many chances of exercising that virtue in my line of life. You think Mr. Franklin Blake hasn’t got a suspicion of the girl’s fancy for him? Ah! he would have found it out fast enough if she had been nice-looking. The ugly women have a bad time of it in this world; let’s hope it will be made up to them in another. You have got a nice garden here, and a well-kept lawn. See for yourself how much better the flowers look with grass about them instead of gravel. No, thank you. I won’t take a rose. It goes to my heart to break them off the stem. Just as it goes to your heart, you know, when there’s something wrong in the servants’ hall. Did you notice anything you couldn’t account for in any of the servants when the loss of the Diamond was first found out?”\n\nI had got on very fairly well with Sergeant Cuff so far. But the slyness with which he slipped in that last question put me on my guard. In plain English, I didn’t at all relish the notion of helping his inquiries, when those inquiries took him (in the capacity of snake in the grass) among my fellow-servants.\n\n“I noticed nothing,” I said, “except that we all lost our heads together, myself included.”\n\n“Oh,” says the Sergeant, “that’s all you have to tell me, is it?”\n\nI answered, with (as I flattered myself) an unmoved countenance, “That is all.”\n\nSergeant Cuff’s dismal eyes looked me hard in the face.\n\n“Mr. Betteredge,” he said, “have you any objection to oblige me by shaking hands? I have taken an extraordinary liking to you.”\n\n(Why he should have chosen the exact moment when I was deceiving him to give me that proof of his good opinion, is beyond all comprehension! I felt a little proud—I really did feel a little proud of having been one too many at last for the celebrated Cuff!)\n\nWe went back to the house; the Sergeant requesting that I would give him a room to himself, and then send in the servants (the indoor servants only), one after another, in the order of their rank, from first to last.\n\nI showed Sergeant Cuff into my own room, and then called the servants together in the hall. Rosanna Spearman appeared among them, much as usual. She was as quick in her way as the Sergeant in his, and I suspect she had heard what he said to me about the servants in general, just before he discovered her. There she was, at any rate, looking as if she had never heard of such a place as the shrubbery in her life.\n\nI sent them in, one by one, as desired. The cook was the first to enter the Court of Justice, otherwise my room. She remained but a short time. Report, on coming out: “Sergeant Cuff is depressed in his spirits; but Sergeant Cuff is a perfect gentleman.” My lady’s own maid followed. Remained much longer. Report, on coming out: “If Sergeant Cuff doesn’t believe a respectable woman, he might keep his opinion to himself, at any rate!” Penelope went next. Remained only a moment or two. Report, on coming out: “Sergeant Cuff is much to be pitied. He must have been crossed in love, father, when he was a young man.” The first housemaid followed Penelope. Remained, like my lady’s maid, a long time. Report, on coming out: “I didn’t enter her ladyship’s service, Mr. Betteredge, to be doubted to my face by a low police-officer!” Rosanna Spearman went next. Remained longer than any of them. No report on coming out—dead silence, and lips as pale as ashes. Samuel, the footman, followed Rosanna. Remained a minute or two. Report, on coming out: “Whoever blacks Sergeant Cuff’s boots ought to be ashamed of himself.” Nancy, the kitchen-maid, went last. Remained a minute or two. Report, on coming out: “Sergeant Cuff has a heart; he doesn’t cut jokes, Mr. Betteredge, with a poor hard-working girl.”\n\nGoing into the Court of Justice, when it was all over, to hear if there were any further commands for me, I found the Sergeant at his old trick—looking out of window, and whistling “The Last Rose of Summer” to himself.\n\n“Any discoveries, sir?” I inquired.\n\n“If Rosanna Spearman asks leave to go out,” said the Sergeant, “let the poor thing go; but let me know first.”\n\nI might as well have held my tongue about Rosanna and Mr. Franklin! It was plain enough; the unfortunate girl had fallen under Sergeant Cuff’s suspicions, in spite of all I could do to prevent it.\n\n“I hope you don’t think Rosanna is concerned in the loss of the Diamond?” I ventured to say.\n\nThe corners of the Sergeant’s melancholy mouth curled up, and he looked hard in my face, just as he had looked in the garden.\n\n“I think I had better not tell you, Mr. Betteredge,” he said. “You might lose your head, you know, for the second time.”\n\nI began to doubt whether I had been one too many for the celebrated Cuff, after all! It was rather a relief to me that we were interrupted here by a knock at the door, and a message from the cook. Rosanna Spearman had asked to go out, for the usual reason, that her head was bad, and she wanted a breath of fresh air. At a sign from the Sergeant, I said, Yes. “Which is the servants’ way out?” he asked, when the messenger had gone. I showed him the servants’ way out. “Lock the door of your room,” says the Sergeant; “and if anybody asks for me, say I’m in there, composing my mind.” He curled up again at the corners of the lips, and disappeared.\n\nLeft alone, under those circumstances, a devouring curiosity pushed me on to make some discoveries for myself.\n\nIt was plain that Sergeant Cuff’s suspicions of Rosanna had been roused by something that he had found out at his examination of the servants in my room. Now, the only two servants (excepting Rosanna herself) who had remained under examination for any length of time, were my lady’s own maid and the first housemaid, those two being also the women who had taken the lead in persecuting their unfortunate fellow-servant from the first. Reaching these conclusions, I looked in on them, casually as it might be, in the servants’ hall, and, finding tea going forward, instantly invited myself to that meal. (For, nota bene, a drop of tea is to a woman’s tongue what a drop of oil is to a wasting lamp.)\n\nMy reliance on the tea-pot, as an ally, did not go unrewarded. In less than half an hour I knew as much as the Sergeant himself.\n\nMy lady’s maid and the housemaid, had, it appeared, neither of them believed in Rosanna’s illness of the previous day. These two devils—I ask your pardon; but how else can you describe a couple of spiteful women?—had stolen upstairs, at intervals during the Thursday afternoon; had tried Rosanna’s door, and found it locked; had knocked, and not been answered; had listened, and not heard a sound inside. When the girl had come down to tea, and had been sent up, still out of sorts, to bed again, the two devils aforesaid had tried her door once more, and found it locked; had looked at the keyhole, and found it stopped up; had seen a light under the door at midnight, and had heard the crackling of a fire (a fire in a servant’s bedroom in the month of June!) at four in the morning. All this they had told Sergeant Cuff, who, in return for their anxiety to enlighten him, had eyed them with sour and suspicious looks, and had shown them plainly that he didn’t believe either one or the other. Hence, the unfavourable reports of him which these two women had brought out with them from the examination. Hence, also (without reckoning the influence of the tea-pot), their readiness to let their tongues run to any length on the subject of the Sergeant’s ungracious behaviour to them.\n\nHaving had some experience of the great Cuff’s roundabout ways, and having last seen him evidently bent on following Rosanna privately when she went out for her walk, it seemed clear to me that he had thought it unadvisable to let the lady’s maid and the housemaid know how materially they had helped him. They were just the sort of women, if he had treated their evidence as trustworthy, to have been puffed up by it, and to have said or done something which would have put Rosanna Spearman on her guard.\n\nI walked out in the fine summer afternoon, very sorry for the poor girl, and very uneasy in my mind at the turn things had taken. Drifting towards the shrubbery, some time later, there I met Mr. Franklin. After returning from seeing his cousin off at the station, he had been with my lady, holding a long conversation with her. She had told him of Miss Rachel’s unaccountable refusal to let her wardrobe be examined; and had put him in such low spirits about my young lady that he seemed to shrink from speaking on the subject. The family temper appeared in his face that evening, for the first time in my experience of him.\n\n“Well, Betteredge,” he said, “how does the atmosphere of mystery and suspicion in which we are all living now, agree with you? Do you remember that morning when I first came here with the Moonstone? I wish to God we had thrown it into the quicksand!”\n\nAfter breaking out in that way, he abstained from speaking again until he had composed himself. We walked silently, side by side, for a minute or two, and then he asked me what had become of Sergeant Cuff. It was impossible to put Mr. Franklin off with the excuse of the Sergeant being in my room, composing his mind. I told him exactly what had happened, mentioning particularly what my lady’s maid and the house-maid had said about Rosanna Spearman.\n\nMr. Franklin’s clear head saw the turn the Sergeant’s suspicions had taken, in the twinkling of an eye.\n\n“Didn’t you tell me this morning,” he said, “that one of the tradespeople declared he had met Rosanna yesterday, on the footway to Frizinghall, when we supposed her to be ill in her room?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“If my aunt’s maid and the other woman have spoken the truth, you may depend upon it the tradesman did meet her. The girl’s attack of illness was a blind to deceive us. She had some guilty reason for going to the town secretly. The paint-stained dress is a dress of hers; and the fire heard crackling in her room at four in the morning was a fire lit to destroy it. Rosanna Spearman has stolen the Diamond. I’ll go in directly, and tell my aunt the turn things have taken.”\n\n“Not just yet, if you please, sir,” said a melancholy voice behind us.\n\nWe both turned about, and found ourselves face to face with Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“Why not just yet?” asked Mr. Franklin.\n\n“Because, sir, if you tell her ladyship, her ladyship will tell Miss Verinder.”\n\n“Suppose she does. What then?” Mr. Franklin said those words with a sudden heat and vehemence, as if the Sergeant had mortally offended him.\n\n“Do you think it’s wise, sir,” said Sergeant Cuff, quietly, “to put such a question as that to me—at such a time as this?”\n\nThere was a moment’s silence between them: Mr. Franklin walked close up to the Sergeant. The two looked each other straight in the face. Mr. Franklin spoke first, dropping his voice as suddenly as he had raised it.\n\n“I suppose you know, Mr. Cuff,” he said, “that you are treading on delicate ground?”\n\n“It isn’t the first time, by a good many hundreds, that I find myself treading on delicate ground,” answered the other, as immovable as ever.\n\n“I am to understand that you forbid me to tell my aunt what has happened?”\n\n“You are to understand, if you please, sir, that I throw up the case, if you tell Lady Verinder, or tell anybody, what has happened, until I give you leave.”\n\nThat settled it. Mr. Franklin had no choice but to submit. He turned away in anger—and left us.\n\nI had stood there listening to them, all in a tremble; not knowing whom to suspect, or what to think next. In the midst of my confusion, two things, however, were plain to me. First, that my young lady was, in some unaccountable manner, at the bottom of the sharp speeches that had passed between them. Second, that they thoroughly understood each other, without having previously exchanged a word of explanation on either side.\n\n“Mr. Betteredge,” says the Sergeant, “you have done a very foolish thing in my absence. You have done a little detective business on your own account. For the future, perhaps you will be so obliging as to do your detective business along with me.”\n\nHe took me by the arm, and walked me away with him along the road by which he had come. I dare say I had deserved his reproof—but I was not going to help him to set traps for Rosanna Spearman, for all that. Thief or no thief, legal or not legal, I don’t care—I pitied her.\n\n“What do you want of me?” I asked, shaking him off, and stopping short.\n\n“Only a little information about the country round here,” said the Sergeant.\n\nI couldn’t well object to improve Sergeant Cuff in his geography.\n\n“Is there any path, in that direction, leading to the sea-beach from this house?” asked the Sergeant. He pointed, as he spoke, to the fir-plantation which led to the Shivering Sand.\n\n“Yes,” I said, “there is a path.”\n\n“Show it to me.”\n\nSide by side, in the grey of the summer evening, Sergeant Cuff and I set forth for the Shivering Sand.\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\nThe Sergeant remained silent, thinking his own thoughts, till we entered the plantation of firs which led to the quicksand. There he roused himself, like a man whose mind was made up, and spoke to me again.\n\n“Mr. Betteredge,” he said, “as you have honoured me by taking an oar in my boat, and as you may, I think, be of some assistance to me before the evening is out, I see no use in our mystifying one another any longer, and I propose to set you an example of plain speaking on my side. You are determined to give me no information to the prejudice of Rosanna Spearman, because she has been a good girl to you, and because you pity her heartily. Those humane considerations do you a world of credit, but they happen in this instance to be humane considerations clean thrown away. Rosanna Spearman is not in the slightest danger of getting into trouble—no, not if I fix her with being concerned in the disappearance of the Diamond, on evidence which is as plain as the nose on your face!”\n\n“Do you mean that my lady won’t prosecute?” I asked.\n\n“I mean that your lady can’t prosecute,” said the Sergeant. “Rosanna Spearman is simply an instrument in the hands of another person, and Rosanna Spearman will be held harmless for that other person’s sake.”\n\nHe spoke like a man in earnest—there was no denying that. Still, I felt something stirring uneasily against him in my mind. “Can’t you give that other person a name?” I said.\n\n“Can’t you, Mr. Betteredge?”\n\n“No.”\n\nSergeant Cuff stood stock-still, and surveyed me with a look of melancholy interest.\n\n“It’s always a pleasure to me to be tender towards human infirmity,” he said. “I feel particularly tender at the present moment, Mr. Betteredge, towards you. And you, with the same excellent motive, feel particularly tender towards Rosanna Spearman, don’t you? Do you happen to know whether she has had a new outfit of linen lately?”\n\nWhat he meant by slipping in this extraordinary question unawares, I was at a total loss to imagine. Seeing no possible injury to Rosanna if I owned the truth, I answered that the girl had come to us rather sparely provided with linen, and that my lady, in recompense for her good conduct (I laid a stress on her good conduct), had given her a new outfit not a fortnight since.\n\n“This is a miserable world,” says the Sergeant. “Human life, Mr. Betteredge, is a sort of target—misfortune is always firing at it, and always hitting the mark. But for that outfit, we should have discovered a new nightgown or petticoat among Rosanna’s things, and have nailed her in that way. You’re not at a loss to follow me, are you? You have examined the servants yourself, and you know what discoveries two of them made outside Rosanna’s door. Surely you know what the girl was about yesterday, after she was taken ill? You can’t guess? Oh dear me, it’s as plain as that strip of light there, at the end of the trees. At eleven, on Thursday morning, Superintendent Seegrave (who is a mass of human infirmity) points out to all the women servants the smear on the door. Rosanna has her own reasons for suspecting her own things; she takes the first opportunity of getting to her room, finds the paint-stain on her night-gown, or petticoat, or what not, shams ill and slips away to the town, gets the materials for making a new petticoat or nightgown, makes it alone in her room on the Thursday night, lights a fire (not to destroy it; two of her fellow-servants are prying outside her door, and she knows better than to make a smell of burning, and to have a lot of tinder to get rid of)—lights a fire, I say, to dry and iron the substitute dress after wringing it out, keeps the stained dress hidden (probably on her), and is at this moment occupied in making away with it, in some convenient place, on that lonely bit of beach ahead of us. I have traced her this evening to your fishing village, and to one particular cottage, which we may possibly have to visit, before we go back. She stopped in the cottage for some time, and she came out with (as I believe) something hidden under her cloak. A cloak (on a woman’s back) is an emblem of charity—it covers a multitude of sins. I saw her set off northwards along the coast, after leaving the cottage. Is your sea-shore here considered a fine specimen of marine landscape, Mr. Betteredge?”\n\nI answered, “Yes,” as shortly as might be.\n\n“Tastes differ,” says Sergeant Cuff. “Looking at it from my point of view, I never saw a marine landscape that I admired less. If you happen to be following another person along your sea-coast, and if that person happens to look round, there isn’t a scrap of cover to hide you anywhere. I had to choose between taking Rosanna in custody on suspicion, or leaving her, for the time being, with her little game in her own hands. For reasons which I won’t trouble you with, I decided on making any sacrifice rather than give the alarm as soon as tonight to a certain person who shall be nameless between us. I came back to the house to ask you to take me to the north end of the beach by another way. Sand—in respect of its printing off people’s footsteps—is one of the best detective officers I know. If we don’t meet with Rosanna Spearman by coming round on her in this way, the sand may tell us what she has been at, if the light only lasts long enough. Here is the sand. If you will excuse my suggesting it—suppose you hold your tongue, and let me go first?”\n\nIf there is such a thing known at the doctor’s shop as a detective-fever, that disease had now got fast hold of your humble servant. Sergeant Cuff went on between the hillocks of sand, down to the beach. I followed him (with my heart in my mouth); and waited at a little distance for what was to happen next.\n\nAs it turned out, I found myself standing nearly in the same place where Rosanna Spearman and I had been talking together when Mr. Franklin suddenly appeared before us, on arriving at our house from London. While my eyes were watching the Sergeant, my mind wandered away in spite of me to what had passed, on that former occasion, between Rosanna and me. I declare I almost felt the poor thing slip her hand again into mine, and give it a little grateful squeeze to thank me for speaking kindly to her. I declare I almost heard her voice telling me again that the Shivering Sand seemed to draw her to it against her own will, whenever she went out—almost saw her face brighten again, as it brightened when she first set eyes upon Mr. Franklin coming briskly out on us from among the hillocks. My spirits fell lower and lower as I thought of these things—and the view of the lonesome little bay, when I looked about to rouse myself, only served to make me feel more uneasy still.\n\nThe last of the evening light was fading away; and over all the desolate place there hung a still and awful calm. The heave of the main ocean on the great sandbank out in the bay, was a heave that made no sound. The inner sea lay lost and dim, without a breath of wind to stir it. Patches of nasty ooze floated, yellow-white, on the dead surface of the water. Scum and slime shone faintly in certain places, where the last of the light still caught them on the two great spits of rock jutting out, north and south, into the sea. It was now the time of the turn of the tide: and even as I stood there waiting, the broad brown face of the quicksand began to dimple and quiver—the only moving thing in all the horrid place.\n\nI saw the Sergeant start as the shiver of the sand caught his eye. After looking at it for a minute or so, he turned and came back to me.\n\n“A treacherous place, Mr. Betteredge,” he said; “and no signs of Rosanna Spearman anywhere on the beach, look where you may.”\n\nHe took me down lower on the shore, and I saw for myself that his footsteps and mine were the only footsteps printed off on the sand.\n\n“How does the fishing village bear, standing where we are now?” asked Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“Cobb’s Hole,” I answered (that being the name of the place), “bears as near as may be, due south.”\n\n“I saw the girl this evening, walking northward along the shore, from Cobb’s Hole,” said the Sergeant. “Consequently, she must have been walking towards this place. Is Cobb’s Hole on the other side of that point of land there? And can we get to it—now it’s low water—by the beach?”\n\nI answered, “Yes,” to both those questions.\n\n“If you’ll excuse my suggesting it, we’ll step out briskly,” said the Sergeant. “I want to find the place where she left the shore, before it gets dark.”\n\nWe had walked, I should say, a couple of hundred yards towards Cobb’s Hole, when Sergeant Cuff suddenly went down on his knees on the beach, to all appearance seized with a sudden frenzy for saying his prayers.\n\n“There’s something to be said for your marine landscape here, after all,” remarked the Sergeant. “Here are a woman’s footsteps, Mr. Betteredge! Let us call them Rosanna’s footsteps, until we find evidence to the contrary that we can’t resist. Very confused footsteps, you will please to observe—purposely confused, I should say. Ah, poor soul, she understands the detective virtues of sand as well as I do! But hasn’t she been in rather too great a hurry to tread out the marks thoroughly? I think she has. Here’s one footstep going from Cobb’s Hole; and here is another going back to it. Isn’t that the toe of her shoe pointing straight to the water’s edge? And don’t I see two heel-marks further down the beach, close at the water’s edge also? I don’t want to hurt your feelings, but I’m afraid Rosanna is sly. It looks as if she had determined to get to that place you and I have just come from, without leaving any marks on the sand to trace her by. Shall we say that she walked through the water from this point till she got to that ledge of rocks behind us, and came back the same way, and then took to the beach again where those two heel marks are still left? Yes, we’ll say that. It seems to fit in with my notion that she had something under her cloak, when she left the cottage. No! not something to destroy—for, in that case, where would have been the need of all these precautions to prevent my tracing the place at which her walk ended? Something to hide is, I think, the better guess of the two. Perhaps, if we go on to the cottage, we may find out what that something is?”\n\nAt this proposal, my detective-fever suddenly cooled. “You don’t want me,” I said. “What good can I do?”\n\n“The longer I know you, Mr. Betteredge,” said the Sergeant, “the more virtues I discover. Modesty—oh dear me, how rare modesty is in this world! and how much of that rarity you possess! If I go alone to the cottage, the people’s tongues will be tied at the first question I put to them. If I go with you, I go introduced by a justly respected neighbour, and a flow of conversation is the necessary result. It strikes me in that light; how does it strike you?”\n\nNot having an answer of the needful smartness as ready as I could have wished, I tried to gain time by asking him what cottage he wanted to go to.\n\nOn the Sergeant describing the place, I recognised it as a cottage inhabited by a fisherman named Yolland, with his wife and two grown-up children, a son and a daughter. If you will look back, you will find that, in first presenting Rosanna Spearman to your notice, I have described her as occasionally varying her walk to the Shivering Sand, by a visit to some friends of hers at Cobb’s Hole. Those friends were the Yollands—respectable, worthy people, a credit to the neighbourhood. Rosanna’s acquaintance with them had begun by means of the daughter, who was afflicted with a misshapen foot, and who was known in our parts by the name of Limping Lucy. The two deformed girls had, I suppose, a kind of fellow-feeling for each other. Anyway, the Yollands and Rosanna always appeared to get on together, at the few chances they had of meeting, in a pleasant and friendly manner. The fact of Sergeant Cuff having traced the girl to their cottage, set the matter of my helping his inquiries in quite a new light. Rosanna had merely gone where she was in the habit of going; and to show that she had been in company with the fisherman and his family was as good as to prove that she had been innocently occupied so far, at any rate. It would be doing the girl a service, therefore, instead of an injury, if I allowed myself to be convinced by Sergeant Cuff’s logic. I professed myself convinced by it accordingly.\n\nWe went on to Cobb’s Hole, seeing the footsteps on the sand, as long as the light lasted.\n\nOn reaching the cottage, the fisherman and his son proved to be out in the boat; and Limping Lucy, always weak and weary, was resting on her bed upstairs. Good Mrs. Yolland received us alone in her kitchen. When she heard that Sergeant Cuff was a celebrated character in London, she clapped a bottle of Dutch gin and a couple of clean pipes on the table, and stared as if she could never see enough of him.\n\nI sat quiet in a corner, waiting to hear how the Sergeant would find his way to the subject of Rosanna Spearman. His usual roundabout manner of going to work proved, on this occasion, to be more roundabout than ever. How he managed it is more than I could tell at the time, and more than I can tell now. But this is certain, he began with the Royal Family, the Primitive Methodists, and the price of fish; and he got from that (in his dismal, underground way) to the loss of the Moonstone, the spitefulness of our first house-maid, and the hard behaviour of the women-servants generally towards Rosanna Spearman. Having reached his subject in this fashion, he described himself as making his inquiries about the lost Diamond, partly with a view to find it, and partly for the purpose of clearing Rosanna from the unjust suspicions of her enemies in the house. In about a quarter of an hour from the time when we entered the kitchen, good Mrs. Yolland was persuaded that she was talking to Rosanna’s best friend, and was pressing Sergeant Cuff to comfort his stomach and revive his spirits out of the Dutch bottle.\n\nBeing firmly persuaded that the Sergeant was wasting his breath to no purpose on Mrs. Yolland, I sat enjoying the talk between them, much as I have sat, in my time, enjoying a stage play. The great Cuff showed a wonderful patience; trying his luck drearily this way and that way, and firing shot after shot, as it were, at random, on the chance of hitting the mark. Everything to Rosanna’s credit, nothing to Rosanna’s prejudice—that was how it ended, try as he might; with Mrs. Yolland talking nineteen to the dozen, and placing the most entire confidence in him. His last effort was made, when we had looked at our watches, and had got on our legs previous to taking leave.\n\n“I shall now wish you good-night, ma’am,” says the Sergeant. “And I shall only say, at parting, that Rosanna Spearman has a sincere well-wisher in myself, your obedient servant. But, oh dear me! she will never get on in her present place; and my advice to her is—leave it.”\n\n“Bless your heart alive! she is going to leave it!” cries Mrs. Yolland. (Nota bene—I translate Mrs. Yolland out of the Yorkshire language into the English language. When I tell you that the all-accomplished Cuff was every now and then puzzled to understand her until I helped him, you will draw your own conclusions as to what your state of mind would be if I reported her in her native tongue.)\n\nRosanna Spearman going to leave us! I pricked up my ears at that. It seemed strange, to say the least of it, that she should have given no warning, in the first place, to my lady or to me. A certain doubt came up in my mind whether Sergeant Cuff’s last random shot might not have hit the mark. I began to question whether my share in the proceedings was quite as harmless a one as I had thought it. It might be all in the way of the Sergeant’s business to mystify an honest woman by wrapping her round in a network of lies but it was my duty to have remembered, as a good Protestant, that the father of lies is the Devil—and that mischief and the Devil are never far apart. Beginning to smell mischief in the air, I tried to take Sergeant Cuff out. He sat down again instantly, and asked for a little drop of comfort out of the Dutch bottle. Mrs. Yolland sat down opposite to him, and gave him his nip. I went on to the door, excessively uncomfortable, and said I thought I must bid them good-night—and yet I didn’t go.\n\n“So she means to leave?” says the Sergeant. “What is she to do when she does leave? Sad, sad! The poor creature has got no friends in the world, except you and me.”\n\n“Ah, but she has though!” says Mrs. Yolland. “She came in here, as I told you, this evening; and, after sitting and talking a little with my girl Lucy and me she asked to go upstairs by herself, into Lucy’s room. It’s the only room in our place where there’s pen and ink. ‘I want to write a letter to a friend,’ she says ‘and I can’t do it for the prying and peeping of the servants up at the house.’ Who the letter was written to I can’t tell you: it must have been a mortal long one, judging by the time she stopped upstairs over it. I offered her a postage-stamp when she came down. She hadn’t got the letter in her hand, and she didn’t accept the stamp. A little close, poor soul (as you know), about herself and her doings. But a friend she has got somewhere, I can tell you; and to that friend you may depend upon it, she will go.”\n\n“Soon?” asked the Sergeant.\n\n“As soon as she can,” says Mrs. Yolland.\n\nHere I stepped in again from the door. As chief of my lady’s establishment, I couldn’t allow this sort of loose talk about a servant of ours going, or not going, to proceed any longer in my presence, without noticing it.\n\n“You must be mistaken about Rosanna Spearman,” I said. “If she had been going to leave her present situation, she would have mentioned it, in the first place, to me.”\n\n“Mistaken?” cries Mrs. Yolland. “Why, only an hour ago she bought some things she wanted for travelling—of my own self, Mr. Betteredge, in this very room. And that reminds me,” says the wearisome woman, suddenly beginning to feel in her pocket, “of something I have got it on my mind to say about Rosanna and her money. Are you either of you likely to see her when you go back to the house?”\n\n“I’ll take a message to the poor thing, with the greatest pleasure,” answered Sergeant Cuff, before I could put in a word edgewise.\n\nMrs. Yolland produced out of her pocket, a few shillings and sixpences, and counted them out with a most particular and exasperating carefulness in the palm of her hand. She offered the money to the Sergeant, looking mighty loth to part with it all the while.\n\n“Might I ask you to give this back to Rosanna, with my love and respects?” says Mrs. Yolland. “She insisted on paying me for the one or two things she took a fancy to this evening—and money’s welcome enough in our house, I don’t deny it. Still, I’m not easy in my mind about taking the poor thing’s little savings. And to tell you the truth, I don’t think my man would like to hear that I had taken Rosanna Spearman’s money, when he comes back tomorrow morning from his work. Please say she’s heartily welcome to the things she bought of me—as a gift. And don’t leave the money on the table,” says Mrs. Yolland, putting it down suddenly before the Sergeant, as if it burnt her fingers—“don’t, there’s a good man! For times are hard, and flesh is weak; and I might feel tempted to put it back in my pocket again.”\n\n“Come along!” I said, “I can’t wait any longer: I must go back to the house.”\n\n“I’ll follow you directly,” says Sergeant Cuff.\n\nFor the second time, I went to the door; and, for the second time, try as I might, I couldn’t cross the threshold.\n\n“It’s a delicate matter, ma’am,” I heard the Sergeant say, “giving money back. You charged her cheap for the things, I’m sure?”\n\n“Cheap!” says Mrs. Yolland. “Come and judge for yourself.”\n\nShe took up the candle and led the Sergeant to a corner of the kitchen. For the life of me, I couldn’t help following them. Shaken down in the corner was a heap of odds and ends (mostly old metal), which the fisherman had picked up at different times from wrecked ships, and which he hadn’t found a market for yet, to his own mind. Mrs. Yolland dived into this rubbish, and brought up an old japanned tin case, with a cover to it, and a hasp to hang it up by—the sort of thing they use, on board ship, for keeping their maps and charts, and such-like, from the wet.\n\n“There!” says she. “When Rosanna came in this evening, she bought the fellow to that. ‘It will just do,’ she says, ‘to put my cuffs and collars in, and keep them from being crumpled in my box.’ One and ninepence, Mr. Cuff. As I live by bread, not a halfpenny more!”\n\n“Dirt cheap!” says the Sergeant, with a heavy sigh.\n\nHe weighed the case in his hand. I thought I heard a note or two of “The Last Rose of Summer” as he looked at it. There was no doubt now! He had made another discovery to the prejudice of Rosanna Spearman, in the place of all others where I thought her character was safest, and all through me! I leave you to imagine what I felt, and how sincerely I repented having been the medium of introduction between Mrs. Yolland and Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“That will do,” I said. “We really must go.”\n\nWithout paying the least attention to me, Mrs. Yolland took another dive into the rubbish, and came up out of it, this time, with a dog-chain.\n\n“Weigh it in your hand, sir,” she said to the Sergeant. “We had three of these; and Rosanna has taken two of them. ‘What can you want, my dear, with a couple of dog’s chains?’ says I. ‘If I join them together they’ll go round my box nicely,’ says she. ‘Rope’s cheapest,’ says I. ‘Chain’s surest,’ says she. ‘Who ever heard of a box corded with chain,’ says I. ‘Oh, Mrs. Yolland, don’t make objections!’ says she; ‘let me have my chains!’ A strange girl, Mr. Cuff—good as gold, and kinder than a sister to my Lucy—but always a little strange. There! I humoured her. Three and sixpence. On the word of an honest woman, three and sixpence, Mr. Cuff!”\n\n“Each?” says the Sergeant.\n\n“Both together!” says Mrs. Yolland. “Three and sixpence for the two.”\n\n“Given away, ma’am,” says the Sergeant, shaking his head. “Clean given away!”\n\n“There’s the money,” says Mrs. Yolland, getting back sideways to the little heap of silver on the table, as if it drew her in spite of herself. “The tin case and the dog chains were all she bought, and all she took away. One and ninepence and three and sixpence—total, five and three. With my love and respects—and I can’t find it in my conscience to take a poor girl’s savings, when she may want them herself.”\n\n“I can’t find it in my conscience, ma’am, to give the money back,” says Sergeant Cuff. “You have as good as made her a present of the things—you have indeed.”\n\n“Is that your sincere opinion, sir?” says Mrs. Yolland brightening up wonderfully.\n\n“There can’t be a doubt about it,” answered the Sergeant. “Ask Mr. Betteredge.”\n\nIt was no use asking me. All they got out of me was, “Good-night.”\n\n“Bother the money!” says Mrs. Yolland. With these words, she appeared to lose all command over herself; and, making a sudden snatch at the heap of silver, put it back, holus-bolus, in her pocket. “It upsets one’s temper, it does, to see it lying there, and nobody taking it,” cries this unreasonable woman, sitting down with a thump, and looking at Sergeant Cuff, as much as to say, “It’s in my pocket again now—get it out if you can!”\n\nThis time, I not only went to the door, but went fairly out on the road back. Explain it how you may, I felt as if one or both of them had mortally offended me. Before I had taken three steps down the village, I heard the Sergeant behind me.\n\n“Thank you for your introduction, Mr. Betteredge,” he said. “I am indebted to the fisherman’s wife for an entirely new sensation. Mrs. Yolland has puzzled me.”\n\nIt was on the tip of my tongue to have given him a sharp answer, for no better reason than this—that I was out of temper with him, because I was out of temper with myself. But when he owned to being puzzled, a comforting doubt crossed my mind whether any great harm had been done after all. I waited in discreet silence to hear more.\n\n“Yes,” says the Sergeant, as if he was actually reading my thoughts in the dark. “Instead of putting me on the scent, it may console you to know, Mr. Betteredge (with your interest in Rosanna), that you have been the means of throwing me off. What the girl has done, tonight, is clear enough, of course. She has joined the two chains, and has fastened them to the hasp in the tin case. She has sunk the case, in the water or in the quicksand. She has made the loose end of the chain fast to some place under the rocks, known only to herself. And she will leave the case secure at its anchorage till the present proceedings have come to an end; after which she can privately pull it up again out of its hiding-place, at her own leisure and convenience. All perfectly plain, so far. But,” says the Sergeant, with the first tone of impatience in his voice that I had heard yet, “the mystery is—what the devil has she hidden in the tin case?”\n\nI thought to myself, “The Moonstone!” But I only said to Sergeant Cuff, “Can’t you guess?”\n\n“It’s not the Diamond,” says the Sergeant. “The whole experience of my life is at fault, if Rosanna Spearman has got the Diamond.”\n\nOn hearing those words, the infernal detective-fever began, I suppose, to burn in me again. At any rate, I forgot myself in the interest of guessing this new riddle. I said rashly, “The stained dress!”\n\nSergeant Cuff stopped short in the dark, and laid his hand on my arm.\n\n“Is anything thrown into that quicksand of yours, ever thrown up on the surface again?” he asked.\n\n“Never,” I answered. “Light or heavy, whatever goes into the Shivering Sand is sucked down, and seen no more.”\n\n“Does Rosanna Spearman know that?”\n\n“She knows it as well as I do.”\n\n“Then,” says the Sergeant, “what on earth has she got to do but to tie up a bit of stone in the stained dress and throw it into the quicksand? There isn’t the shadow of a reason why she should have hidden it—and yet she must have hidden it. Query,” says the Sergeant, walking on again, “is the paint-stained dress a petticoat or a night-gown? or is it something else which there is a reason for preserving at any risk? Mr. Betteredge, if nothing occurs to prevent it, I must go to Frizinghall tomorrow, and discover what she bought in the town, when she privately got the materials for making the substitute dress. It’s a risk to leave the house, as things are now—but it’s a worse risk still to stir another step in this matter in the dark. Excuse my being a little out of temper; I’m degraded in my own estimation—I have let Rosanna Spearman puzzle me.”\n\nWhen we got back, the servants were at supper. The first person we saw in the outer yard was the policeman whom Superintendent Seegrave had left at the Sergeant’s disposal. The Sergeant asked if Rosanna Spearman had returned. Yes. When? Nearly an hour since. What had she done? She had gone upstairs to take off her bonnet and cloak—and she was now at supper quietly with the rest.\n\nWithout making any remark, Sergeant Cuff walked on, sinking lower and lower in his own estimation, to the back of the house. Missing the entrance in the dark, he went on (in spite of my calling to him) till he was stopped by a wicket-gate which led into the garden. When I joined him to bring him back by the right way, I found that he was looking up attentively at one particular window, on the bedroom floor, at the back of the house.\n\nLooking up, in my turn, I discovered that the object of his contemplation was the window of Miss Rachel’s room, and that lights were passing backwards and forwards there as if something unusual was going on.\n\n“Isn’t that Miss Verinder’s room?” asked Sergeant Cuff.\n\nI replied that it was, and invited him to go in with me to supper. The Sergeant remained in his place, and said something about enjoying the smell of the garden at night. I left him to his enjoyment. Just as I was turning in at the door, I heard “The Last Rose of Summer” at the wicket-gate. Sergeant Cuff had made another discovery! And my young lady’s window was at the bottom of it this time!\n\nThe latter reflection took me back again to the Sergeant, with a polite intimation that I could not find it in my heart to leave him by himself. “Is there anything you don’t understand up there?” I added, pointing to Miss Rachel’s window.\n\nJudging by his voice, Sergeant Cuff had suddenly risen again to the right place in his own estimation. “You are great people for betting in Yorkshire, are you not?” he asked.\n\n“Well?” I said. “Suppose we are?”\n\n“If I was a Yorkshireman,” proceeded the Sergeant, taking my arm, “I would lay you an even sovereign, Mr. Betteredge, that your young lady has suddenly resolved to leave the house. If I won on that event, I should offer to lay another sovereign, that the idea has occurred to her within the last hour.” The first of the Sergeant’s guesses startled me. The second mixed itself up somehow in my head with the report we had heard from the policeman, that Rosanna Spearman had returned from the sands within the last hour. The two together had a curious effect on me as we went in to supper. I shook off Sergeant Cuff’s arm, and, forgetting my manners, pushed by him through the door to make my own inquiries for myself.\n\nSamuel, the footman, was the first person I met in the passage.\n\n“Her ladyship is waiting to see you and Sergeant Cuff,” he said, before I could put any questions to him.\n\n“How long has she been waiting?” asked the Sergeant’s voice behind me.\n\n“For the last hour, sir.”\n\nThere it was again! Rosanna had come back; Miss Rachel had taken some resolution out of the common; and my lady had been waiting to see the Sergeant—all within the last hour! It was not pleasant to find these very different persons and things linking themselves together in this way. I went on upstairs, without looking at Sergeant Cuff, or speaking to him. My hand took a sudden fit of trembling as I lifted it to knock at my mistress’s door.\n\n“I shouldn’t be surprised,” whispered the Sergeant over my shoulder, “if a scandal was to burst up in the house tonight. Don’t be alarmed! I have put the muzzle on worse family difficulties than this, in my time.”\n\nAs he said the words I heard my mistress’s voice calling to us to come in.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\nWe found my lady with no light in the room but the reading-lamp. The shade was screwed down so as to overshadow her face. Instead of looking up at us in her usual straightforward way, she sat close at the table, and kept her eyes fixed obstinately on an open book.\n\n“Officer,” she said, “is it important to the inquiry you are conducting, to know beforehand if any person now in this house wishes to leave it?”\n\n“Most important, my lady.”\n\n“I have to tell you, then, that Miss Verinder proposes going to stay with her aunt, Mrs. Ablewhite, of Frizinghall. She has arranged to leave us the first thing tomorrow morning.”\n\nSergeant Cuff looked at me. I made a step forward to speak to my mistress—and, feeling my heart fail me (if I must own it), took a step back again, and said nothing.\n\n“May I ask your ladyship when Miss Verinder informed you that she was going to her aunt’s?” inquired the Sergeant.\n\n“About an hour since,” answered my mistress.\n\nSergeant Cuff looked at me once more. They say old people’s hearts are not very easily moved. My heart couldn’t have thumped much harder than it did now, if I had been five-and-twenty again!\n\n“I have no claim, my lady,” says the Sergeant, “to control Miss Verinder’s actions. All I can ask you to do is to put off her departure, if possible, till later in the day. I must go to Frizinghall myself tomorrow morning—and I shall be back by two o’clock, if not before. If Miss Verinder can be kept here till that time, I should wish to say two words to her—unexpectedly—before she goes.”\n\nMy lady directed me to give the coachman her orders, that the carriage was not to come for Miss Rachel until two o’clock. “Have you more to say?” she asked of the Sergeant, when this had been done.\n\n“Only one thing, your ladyship. If Miss Verinder is surprised at this change in the arrangements, please not to mention Me as being the cause of putting off her journey.”\n\nMy mistress lifted her head suddenly from her book as if she was going to say something—checked herself by a great effort—and, looking back again at the open page, dismissed us with a sign of her hand.\n\n“That’s a wonderful woman,” said Sergeant Cuff, when we were out in the hall again. “But for her self-control, the mystery that puzzles you, Mr. Betteredge, would have been at an end tonight.”\n\nAt those words, the truth rushed at last into my stupid old head. For the moment, I suppose I must have gone clean out of my senses. I seized the Sergeant by the collar of his coat, and pinned him against the wall.\n\n“Damn you!” I cried out, “there’s something wrong about Miss Rachel—and you have been hiding it from me all this time!”\n\nSergeant Cuff looked up at me—flat against the wall—without stirring a hand, or moving a muscle of his melancholy face.\n\n“Ah,” he said, “you’ve guessed it at last.”\n\nMy hand dropped from his collar, and my head sunk on my breast. Please to remember, as some excuse for my breaking out as I did, that I had served the family for fifty years. Miss Rachel had climbed upon my knees, and pulled my whiskers, many and many a time when she was a child. Miss Rachel, with all her faults, had been, to my mind, the dearest and prettiest and best young mistress that ever an old servant waited on, and loved. I begged Sergeant’s Cuff’s pardon, but I am afraid I did it with watery eyes, and not in a very becoming way.\n\n“Don’t distress yourself, Mr. Betteredge,” says the Sergeant, with more kindness than I had any right to expect from him. “In my line of life if we were quick at taking offence, we shouldn’t be worth salt to our porridge. If it’s any comfort to you, collar me again. You don’t in the least know how to do it; but I’ll overlook your awkwardness in consideration of your feelings.”\n\nHe curled up at the corners of his lips, and, in his own dreary way, seemed to think he had delivered himself of a very good joke.\n\nI led him into my own little sitting-room, and closed the door.\n\n“Tell me the truth, Sergeant,” I said. “What do you suspect? It’s no kindness to hide it from me now.”\n\n“I don’t suspect,” said Sergeant Cuff. “I know.”\n\nMy unlucky temper began to get the better of me again.\n\n“Do you mean to tell me, in plain English,” I said, “that Miss Rachel has stolen her own Diamond?”\n\n“Yes,” says the Sergeant; “that is what I mean to tell you, in so many words. Miss Verinder has been in secret possession of the Moonstone from first to last; and she has taken Rosanna Spearman into her confidence, because she has calculated on our suspecting Rosanna Spearman of the theft. There is the whole case in a nutshell. Collar me again, Mr. Betteredge. If it’s any vent to your feelings, collar me again.”\n\nGod help me! my feelings were not to be relieved in that way. “Give me your reasons!” That was all I could say to him.\n\n“You shall hear my reasons tomorrow,” said the Sergeant. “If Miss Verinder refuses to put off her visit to her aunt (which you will find Miss Verinder will do), I shall be obliged to lay the whole case before your mistress tomorrow. And, as I don’t know what may come of it, I shall request you to be present, and to hear what passes on both sides. Let the matter rest for tonight. No, Mr. Betteredge, you don’t get a word more on the subject of the Moonstone out of me. There is your table spread for supper. That’s one of the many human infirmities which I always treat tenderly. If you will ring the bell, I’ll say grace. ‘For what we are going to receive——’”\n\n“I wish you a good appetite to it, Sergeant,” I said. “My appetite is gone. I’ll wait and see you served, and then I’ll ask you to excuse me, if I go away, and try to get the better of this by myself.”\n\nI saw him served with the best of everything—and I shouldn’t have been sorry if the best of everything had choked him. The head gardener (Mr. Begbie) came in at the same time, with his weekly account. The Sergeant got on the subject of roses and the merits of grass walks and gravel walks immediately. I left the two together, and went out with a heavy heart. This was the first trouble I remember for many a long year which wasn’t to be blown off by a whiff of tobacco, and which was even beyond the reach of Robinson Crusoe.\n\nBeing restless and miserable, and having no particular room to go to, I took a turn on the terrace, and thought it over in peace and quietness by myself. It doesn’t much matter what my thoughts were. I felt wretchedly old, and worn out, and unfit for my place—and began to wonder, for the first time in my life, when it would please God to take me. With all this, I held firm, notwithstanding, to my belief in Miss Rachel. If Sergeant Cuff had been Solomon in all his glory, and had told me that my young lady had mixed herself up in a mean and guilty plot, I should have had but one answer for Solomon, wise as he was, “You don’t know her; and I do.”\n\nMy meditations were interrupted by Samuel. He brought me a written message from my mistress.\n\nGoing into the house to get a light to read it by, Samuel remarked that there seemed a change coming in the weather. My troubled mind had prevented me from noticing it before. But, now my attention was roused, I heard the dogs uneasy, and the wind moaning low. Looking up at the sky, I saw the rack of clouds getting blacker and blacker, and hurrying faster and faster over a watery moon. Wild weather coming—Samuel was right, wild weather coming.\n\nThe message from my lady informed me, that the magistrate at Frizinghall had written to remind her about the three Indians. Early in the coming week, the rogues must needs be released, and left free to follow their own devices. If we had any more questions to ask them, there was no time to lose. Having forgotten to mention this, when she had last seen Sergeant Cuff, my mistress now desired me to supply the omission. The Indians had gone clean out of my head (as they have, no doubt, gone clean out of yours). I didn’t see much use in stirring that subject again. However, I obeyed my orders on the spot, as a matter of course.\n\nI found Sergeant Cuff and the gardener, with a bottle of Scotch whisky between them, head over ears in an argument on the growing of roses. The Sergeant was so deeply interested that he held up his hand, and signed to me not to interrupt the discussion, when I came in. As far as I could understand it, the question between them was, whether the white moss rose did, or did not, require to be budded on the dog-rose to make it grow well. Mr. Begbie said, Yes; and Sergeant Cuff said, No. They appealed to me, as hotly as a couple of boys. Knowing nothing whatever about the growing of roses, I steered a middle course—just as her Majesty’s judges do, when the scales of justice bother them by hanging even to a hair. “Gentlemen,” I remarked, “there is much to be said on both sides.” In the temporary lull produced by that impartial sentence, I laid my lady’s written message on the table, under the eyes of Sergeant Cuff.\n\nI had got by this time, as nearly as might be, to hate the Sergeant. But truth compels me to acknowledge that, in respect of readiness of mind, he was a wonderful man.\n\nIn half a minute after he had read the message, he had looked back into his memory for Superintendent Seegrave’s report; had picked out that part of it in which the Indians were concerned; and was ready with his answer. A certain great traveller, who understood the Indians and their language, had figured in Mr. Seegrave’s report, hadn’t he? Very well. Did I know the gentleman’s name and address? Very well again. Would I write them on the back of my lady’s message? Much obliged to me. Sergeant Cuff would look that gentleman up, when he went to Frizinghall in the morning.\n\n“Do you expect anything to come of it?” I asked. “Superintendent Seegrave found the Indians as innocent as the babe unborn.”\n\n“Superintendent Seegrave has been proved wrong, up to this time, in all his conclusions,” answered the Sergeant. “It may be worth while to find out tomorrow whether Superintendent Seegrave was wrong about the Indians as well.” With that he turned to Mr. Begbie, and took up the argument again exactly at the place where it had left off. “This question between us is a question of soils and seasons, and patience and pains, Mr. Gardener. Now let me put it to you from another point of view. You take your white moss rose——”\n\nBy that time, I had closed the door on them, and was out of hearing of the rest of the dispute.\n\nIn the passage, I met Penelope hanging about, and asked what she was waiting for.\n\nShe was waiting for her young lady’s bell, when her young lady chose to call her back to go on with the packing for the next day’s journey. Further inquiry revealed to me, that Miss Rachel had given it as a reason for wanting to go to her aunt at Frizinghall, that the house was unendurable to her, and that she could bear the odious presence of a policeman under the same roof with herself no longer. On being informed, half an hour since, that her departure would be delayed till two in the afternoon, she had flown into a violent passion. My lady, present at the time, had severely rebuked her, and then (having apparently something to say, which was reserved for her daughter’s private ear) had sent Penelope out of the room. My girl was in wretchedly low spirits about the changed state of things in the house. “Nothing goes right, father; nothing is like what it used to be. I feel as if some dreadful misfortune was hanging over us all.”\n\nThat was my feeling too. But I put a good face on it, before my daughter. Miss Rachel’s bell rang while we were talking. Penelope ran up the back stairs to go on with the packing. I went by the other way to the hall, to see what the glass said about the change in the weather.\n\nJust as I approached the swing-door leading into the hall from the servants’ offices, it was violently opened from the other side, and Rosanna Spearman ran by me, with a miserable look of pain in her face, and one of her hands pressed hard over her heart, as if the pang was in that quarter. “What’s the matter, my girl?” I asked, stopping her. “Are you ill?” “For God’s sake, don’t speak to me,” she answered, and twisted herself out of my hands, and ran on towards the servants’ staircase. I called to the cook (who was within hearing) to look after the poor girl. Two other persons proved to be within hearing, as well as the cook. Sergeant Cuff darted softly out of my room, and asked what was the matter. I answered, “Nothing.” Mr. Franklin, on the other side, pulled open the swing-door, and beckoning me into the hall, inquired if I had seen anything of Rosanna Spearman.\n\n“She has just passed me, sir, with a very disturbed face, and in a very odd manner.”\n\n“I am afraid I am innocently the cause of that disturbance, Betteredge.”\n\n“You, sir!”\n\n“I can’t explain it,” says Mr. Franklin; “but, if the girl is concerned in the loss of the Diamond, I do really believe she was on the point of confessing everything—to me, of all the people in the world—not two minutes since.”\n\nLooking towards the swing-door, as he said those last words, I fancied I saw it opened a little way from the inner side.\n\nWas there anybody listening? The door fell to, before I could get to it. Looking through, the moment after, I thought I saw the tails of Sergeant Cuff’s respectable black coat disappearing round the corner of the passage. He knew, as well as I did, that he could expect no more help from me, now that I had discovered the turn which his investigations were really taking. Under those circumstances, it was quite in his character to help himself, and to do it by the underground way.\n\nNot feeling sure that I had really seen the Sergeant—and not desiring to make needless mischief, where, Heaven knows, there was mischief enough going on already—I told Mr. Franklin that I thought one of the dogs had got into the house—and then begged him to describe what had happened between Rosanna and himself.\n\n“Were you passing through the hall, sir?” I asked. “Did you meet her accidentally, when she spoke to you?”\n\nMr. Franklin pointed to the billiard-table.\n\n“I was knocking the balls about,” he said, “and trying to get this miserable business of the Diamond out of my mind. I happened to look up—and there stood Rosanna Spearman at the side of me, like a ghost! Her stealing on me in that way was so strange, that I hardly knew what to do at first. Seeing a very anxious expression in her face, I asked her if she wished to speak to me. She answered, ‘Yes, if I dare.’ Knowing what suspicion attached to her, I could only put one construction on such language as that. I confess it made me uncomfortable. I had no wish to invite the girl’s confidence. At the same time, in the difficulties that now beset us, I could hardly feel justified in refusing to listen to her, if she was really bent on speaking to me. It was an awkward position; and I dare say I got out of it awkwardly enough. I said to her, ‘I don’t quite understand you. Is there anything you want me to do?’ Mind, Betteredge, I didn’t speak unkindly! The poor girl can’t help being ugly—I felt that, at the time. The cue was still in my hand, and I went on knocking the balls about, to take off the awkwardness of the thing. As it turned out, I only made matters worse still. I’m afraid I mortified her without meaning it! She suddenly turned away. ‘He looks at the billiard balls,’ I heard her say. ‘Anything rather than look at me!’ Before I could stop her, she had left the hall. I am not quite easy about it, Betteredge. Would you mind telling Rosanna that I meant no unkindness? I have been a little hard on her, perhaps, in my own thoughts—I have almost hoped that the loss of the Diamond might be traced to her. Not from any ill-will to the poor girl: but——” He stopped there, and going back to the billiard-table, began to knock the balls about once more.\n\nAfter what had passed between the Sergeant and me, I knew what it was that he had left unspoken as well as he knew it himself.\n\nNothing but the tracing of the Moonstone to our second housemaid could now raise Miss Rachel above the infamous suspicion that rested on her in the mind of Sergeant Cuff. It was no longer a question of quieting my young lady’s nervous excitement; it was a question of proving her innocence. If Rosanna had done nothing to compromise herself, the hope which Mr. Franklin confessed to having felt would have been hard enough on her in all conscience. But this was not the case. She had pretended to be ill, and had gone secretly to Frizinghall. She had been up all night, making something or destroying something, in private. And she had been at the Shivering Sand, that evening, under circumstances which were highly suspicious, to say the least of them. For all these reasons (sorry as I was for Rosanna) I could not but think that Mr. Franklin’s way of looking at the matter was neither unnatural nor unreasonable, in Mr. Franklin’s position. I said a word to him to that effect.\n\n“Yes, yes!” he said in return. “But there is just a chance—a very poor one, certainly—that Rosanna’s conduct may admit of some explanation which we don’t see at present. I hate hurting a woman’s feelings, Betteredge! Tell the poor creature what I told you to tell her. And if she wants to speak to me—I don’t care whether I get into a scrape or not—send her to me in the library.” With those kind words he laid down the cue and left me.\n\nInquiry at the servants’ offices informed me that Rosanna had retired to her own room. She had declined all offers of assistance with thanks, and had only asked to be left to rest in quiet. Here, therefore, was an end of any confession on her part (supposing she really had a confession to make) for that night. I reported the result to Mr. Franklin, who, thereupon, left the library, and went up to bed.\n\nI was putting the lights out, and making the windows fast, when Samuel came in with news of the two guests whom I had left in my room.\n\nThe argument about the white moss rose had apparently come to an end at last. The gardener had gone home, and Sergeant Cuff was nowhere to be found in the lower regions of the house.\n\nI looked into my room. Quite true—nothing was to be discovered there but a couple of empty tumblers and a strong smell of hot grog. Had the Sergeant gone of his own accord to the bedchamber that was prepared for him? I went upstairs to see.\n\nAfter reaching the second landing, I thought I heard a sound of quiet and regular breathing on my left-hand side. My left-hand side led to the corridor which communicated with Miss Rachel’s room. I looked in, and there, coiled up on three chairs placed right across the passage—there, with a red handkerchief tied round his grizzled head, and his respectable black coat rolled up for a pillow, lay and slept Sergeant Cuff!\n\nHe woke, instantly and quietly, like a dog, the moment I approached him.\n\n“Good-night, Mr. Betteredge,” he said. “And mind, if you ever take to growing roses, the white moss rose is all the better for not being budded on the dog-rose, whatever the gardener may say to the contrary!”\n\n“What are you doing here?” I asked. “Why are you not in your proper bed?”\n\n“I am not in my proper bed,” answered the Sergeant, “because I am one of the many people in this miserable world who can’t earn their money honestly and easily at the same time. There was a coincidence, this evening, between the period of Rosanna Spearman’s return from the Sands and the period when Miss Verinder stated her resolution to leave the house. Whatever Rosanna may have hidden, it’s clear to my mind that your young lady couldn’t go away until she knew that it was hidden. The two must have communicated privately once already tonight. If they try to communicate again, when the house is quiet, I want to be in the way, and stop it. Don’t blame me for upsetting your sleeping arrangements, Mr. Betteredge—blame the Diamond.”\n\n“I wish to God the Diamond had never found its way into this house!” I broke out.\n\nSergeant Cuff looked with a rueful face at the three chairs on which he had condemned himself to pass the night.\n\n“So do I,” he said, gravely.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII\n\nNothing happened in the night; and (I am happy to add) no attempt at communication between Miss Rachel and Rosanna rewarded the vigilance of Sergeant Cuff.\n\nI had expected the Sergeant to set off for Frizinghall the first thing in the morning. He waited about, however, as if he had something else to do first. I left him to his own devices; and going into the grounds shortly after, met Mr. Franklin on his favourite walk by the shrubbery side.\n\nBefore we had exchanged two words, the Sergeant unexpectedly joined us. He made up to Mr. Franklin, who received him, I must own, haughtily enough. “Have you anything to say to me?” was all the return he got for politely wishing Mr. Franklin good morning.\n\n“I have something to say to you, sir,” answered the Sergeant, “on the subject of the inquiry I am conducting here. You detected the turn that inquiry was really taking, yesterday. Naturally enough, in your position, you are shocked and distressed. Naturally enough, also, you visit your own angry sense of your own family scandal upon Me.”\n\n“What do you want?” Mr. Franklin broke in, sharply enough.\n\n“I want to remind you, sir, that I have at any rate, thus far, not been proved to be wrong. Bearing that in mind, be pleased to remember, at the same time, that I am an officer of the law acting here under the sanction of the mistress of the house. Under these circumstances, is it, or is it not, your duty as a good citizen, to assist me with any special information which you may happen to possess?”\n\n“I possess no special information,” says Mr. Franklin.\n\nSergeant Cuff put that answer by him, as if no answer had been made.\n\n“You may save my time, sir, from being wasted on an inquiry at a distance,” he went on, “if you choose to understand me and speak out.”\n\n“I don’t understand you,” answered Mr. Franklin; “and I have nothing to say.”\n\n“One of the female servants (I won’t mention names) spoke to you privately, sir, last night.”\n\nOnce more Mr. Franklin cut him short; once more Mr. Franklin answered, “I have nothing to say.”\n\nStanding by in silence, I thought of the movement in the swing-door on the previous evening, and of the coat-tails which I had seen disappearing down the passage. Sergeant Cuff had, no doubt, just heard enough, before I interrupted him, to make him suspect that Rosanna had relieved her mind by confessing something to Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nThis notion had barely struck me—when who should appear at the end of the shrubbery walk but Rosanna Spearman in her own proper person! She was followed by Penelope, who was evidently trying to make her retrace her steps to the house. Seeing that Mr. Franklin was not alone, Rosanna came to a standstill, evidently in great perplexity what to do next. Penelope waited behind her. Mr. Franklin saw the girls as soon as I saw them. The Sergeant, with his devilish cunning, took on not to have noticed them at all. All this happened in an instant. Before either Mr. Franklin or I could say a word, Sergeant Cuff struck in smoothly, with an appearance of continuing the previous conversation.\n\n“You needn’t be afraid of harming the girl, sir,” he said to Mr. Franklin, speaking in a loud voice, so that Rosanna might hear him. “On the contrary, I recommend you to honour me with your confidence, if you feel any interest in Rosanna Spearman.”\n\nMr. Franklin instantly took on not to have noticed the girls either. He answered, speaking loudly on his side:\n\n“I take no interest whatever in Rosanna Spearman.”\n\nI looked towards the end of the walk. All I saw at the distance was that Rosanna suddenly turned round, the moment Mr. Franklin had spoken. Instead of resisting Penelope, as she had done the moment before, she now let my daughter take her by the arm and lead her back to the house.\n\nThe breakfast-bell rang as the two girls disappeared—and even Sergeant Cuff was now obliged to give it up as a bad job! He said to me quietly, “I shall go to Frizinghall, Mr. Betteredge; and I shall be back before two.” He went his way without a word more—and for some few hours we were well rid of him.\n\n“You must make it right with Rosanna,” Mr. Franklin said to me, when we were alone. “I seem to be fated to say or do something awkward, before that unlucky girl. You must have seen yourself that Sergeant Cuff laid a trap for both of us. If he could confuse me, or irritate her into breaking out, either she or I might have said something which would answer his purpose. On the spur of the moment, I saw no better way out of it than the way I took. It stopped the girl from saying anything, and it showed the Sergeant that I saw through him. He was evidently listening, Betteredge, when I was speaking to you last night.”\n\nHe had done worse than listen, as I privately thought to myself. He had remembered my telling him that the girl was in love with Mr. Franklin; and he had calculated on that, when he appealed to Mr. Franklin’s interest in Rosanna—in Rosanna’s hearing.\n\n“As to listening, sir,” I remarked (keeping the other point to myself), “we shall all be rowing in the same boat if this sort of thing goes on much longer. Prying, and peeping, and listening are the natural occupations of people situated as we are. In another day or two, Mr. Franklin, we shall all be struck dumb together—for this reason, that we shall all be listening to surprise each other’s secrets, and all know it. Excuse my breaking out, sir. The horrid mystery hanging over us in this house gets into my head like liquor, and makes me wild. I won’t forget what you have told me. I’ll take the first opportunity of making it right with Rosanna Spearman.”\n\n“You haven’t said anything to her yet about last night, have you?” Mr. Franklin asked.\n\n“No, sir.”\n\n“Then say nothing now. I had better not invite the girl’s confidence, with the Sergeant on the look-out to surprise us together. My conduct is not very consistent, Betteredge—is it? I see no way out of this business, which isn’t dreadful to think of, unless the Diamond is traced to Rosanna. And yet I can’t, and won’t, help Sergeant Cuff to find the girl out.”\n\nUnreasonable enough, no doubt. But it was my state of mind as well. I thoroughly understood him. If you will, for once in your life, remember that you are mortal, perhaps you will thoroughly understand him too.\n\nThe state of things, indoors and out, while Sergeant Cuff was on his way to Frizinghall, was briefly this:\n\nMiss Rachel waited for the time when the carriage was to take her to her aunt’s, still obstinately shut up in her own room. My lady and Mr. Franklin breakfasted together. After breakfast, Mr. Franklin took one of his sudden resolutions, and went out precipitately to quiet his mind by a long walk. I was the only person who saw him go; and he told me he should be back before the Sergeant returned. The change in the weather, foreshadowed overnight, had come. Heavy rain had been followed soon after dawn, by high wind. It was blowing fresh, as the day got on. But though the clouds threatened more than once, the rain still held off. It was not a bad day for a walk, if you were young and strong, and could breast the great gusts of wind which came sweeping in from the sea.\n\nI attended my lady after breakfast, and assisted her in the settlement of our household accounts. She only once alluded to the matter of the Moonstone, and that was in the way of forbidding any present mention of it between us. “Wait till that man comes back,” she said, meaning the Sergeant. “We must speak of it then: we are not obliged to speak of it now.”\n\nAfter leaving my mistress, I found Penelope waiting for me in my room.\n\n“I wish, father, you would come and speak to Rosanna,” she said. “I am very uneasy about her.”\n\nI suspected what was the matter readily enough. But it is a maxim of mine that men (being superior creatures) are bound to improve women—if they can. When a woman wants me to do anything (my daughter, or not, it doesn’t matter), I always insist on knowing why. The oftener you make them rummage their own minds for a reason, the more manageable you will find them in all the relations of life. It isn’t their fault (poor wretches!) that they act first and think afterwards; it’s the fault of the fools who humour them.\n\nPenelope’s reason why, on this occasion, may be given in her own words. “I am afraid, father,” she said, “Mr. Franklin has hurt Rosanna cruelly, without intending it.”\n\n“What took Rosanna into the shrubbery walk?” I asked.\n\n“Her own madness,” says Penelope; “I can call it nothing else. She was bent on speaking to Mr. Franklin, this morning, come what might of it. I did my best to stop her; you saw that. If I could only have got her away before she heard those dreadful words——”\n\n“There! there!” I said, “don’t lose your head. I can’t call to mind that anything happened to alarm Rosanna.”\n\n“Nothing to alarm her, father. But Mr. Franklin said he took no interest whatever in her—and, oh, he said it in such a cruel voice!”\n\n“He said it to stop the Sergeant’s mouth,” I answered.\n\n“I told her that,” says Penelope. “But you see, father (though Mr. Franklin isn’t to blame), he’s been mortifying and disappointing her for weeks and weeks past; and now this comes on the top of it all! She has no right, of course, to expect him to take any interest in her. It’s quite monstrous that she should forget herself and her station in that way. But she seems to have lost pride, and proper feeling, and everything. She frightened me, father, when Mr. Franklin said those words. They seemed to turn her into stone. A sudden quiet came over her, and she has gone about her work, ever since, like a woman in a dream.”\n\nI began to feel a little uneasy. There was something in the way Penelope put it which silenced my superior sense. I called to mind, now my thoughts were directed that way, what had passed between Mr. Franklin and Rosanna overnight. She looked cut to the heart on that occasion; and now, as ill-luck would have it, she had been unavoidably stung again, poor soul, on the tender place. Sad! sad!—all the more sad because the girl had no reason to justify her, and no right to feel it.\n\nI had promised Mr. Franklin to speak to Rosanna, and this seemed the fittest time for keeping my word.\n\nWe found the girl sweeping the corridor outside the bedrooms, pale and composed, and neat as ever in her modest print dress. I noticed a curious dimness and dullness in her eyes—not as if she had been crying but as if she had been looking at something too long. Possibly, it was a misty something raised by her own thoughts. There was certainly no object about her to look at which she had not seen already hundreds on hundreds of times.\n\n“Cheer up, Rosanna!” I said. “You mustn’t fret over your own fancies. I have got something to say to you from Mr. Franklin.”\n\nI thereupon put the matter in the right view before her, in the friendliest and most comforting words I could find. My principles, in regard to the other sex, are, as you may have noticed, very severe. But somehow or other, when I come face to face with the women, my practice (I own) is not conformable.\n\n“Mr. Franklin is very kind and considerate. Please to thank him.” That was all the answer she made me.\n\nMy daughter had already noticed that Rosanna went about her work like a woman in a dream. I now added to this observation, that she also listened and spoke like a woman in a dream. I doubted if her mind was in a fit condition to take in what I had said to her.\n\n“Are you quite sure, Rosanna, that you understand me?” I asked.\n\n“Quite sure.”\n\nShe echoed me, not like a living woman, but like a creature moved by machinery. She went on sweeping all the time. I took away the broom as gently and as kindly as I could.\n\n“Come, come, my girl!” I said, “this is not like yourself. You have got something on your mind. I’m your friend—and I’ll stand your friend, even if you have done wrong. Make a clean breast of it, Rosanna—make a clean breast of it!”\n\nThe time had been, when my speaking to her in that way would have brought the tears into her eyes. I could see no change in them now.\n\n“Yes,” she said, “I’ll make a clean breast of it.”\n\n“To my lady?” I asked.\n\n“No.”\n\n“To Mr. Franklin?”\n\n“Yes; to Mr. Franklin.”\n\nI hardly knew what to say to that. She was in no condition to understand the caution against speaking to him in private, which Mr. Franklin had directed me to give her. Feeling my way, little by little, I only told her Mr. Franklin had gone out for a walk.\n\n“It doesn’t matter,” she answered. “I shan’t trouble Mr. Franklin, today.”\n\n“Why not speak to my lady?” I said. “The way to relieve your mind is to speak to the merciful and Christian mistress who has always been kind to you.”\n\nShe looked at me for a moment with a grave and steady attention, as if she was fixing what I said in her mind. Then she took the broom out of my hands and moved off with it slowly, a little way down the corridor.\n\n“No,” she said, going on with her sweeping, and speaking to herself; “I know a better way of relieving my mind than that.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Please to let me go on with my work.”\n\nPenelope followed her, and offered to help her.\n\nShe answered, “No. I want to do my work. Thank you, Penelope.” She looked round at me. “Thank you, Mr. Betteredge.”\n\nThere was no moving her—there was nothing more to be said. I signed to Penelope to come away with me. We left her, as we had found her, sweeping the corridor, like a woman in a dream.\n\n“This is a matter for the doctor to look into,” I said. “It’s beyond me.”\n\nMy daughter reminded me of Mr. Candy’s illness, owing (as you may remember) to the chill he had caught on the night of the dinner-party. His assistant—a certain Mr. Ezra Jennings—was at our disposal, to be sure. But nobody knew much about him in our parts. He had been engaged by Mr. Candy under rather peculiar circumstances; and, right or wrong, we none of us liked him or trusted him. There were other doctors at Frizinghall. But they were strangers to our house; and Penelope doubted, in Rosanna’s present state, whether strangers might not do her more harm than good.\n\nI thought of speaking to my lady. But, remembering the heavy weight of anxiety which she already had on her mind, I hesitated to add to all the other vexations this new trouble. Still, there was a necessity for doing something. The girl’s state was, to my thinking, downright alarming—and my mistress ought to be informed of it. Unwilling enough, I went to her sitting-room. No one was there. My lady was shut up with Miss Rachel. It was impossible for me to see her till she came out again.\n\nI waited in vain till the clock on the front staircase struck the quarter to two. Five minutes afterwards, I heard my name called, from the drive outside the house. I knew the voice directly. Sergeant Cuff had returned from Frizinghall.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII\n\nGoing down to the front door, I met the Sergeant on the steps.\n\nIt went against the grain with me, after what had passed between us, to show him that I felt any sort of interest in his proceedings. In spite of myself, however, I felt an interest that there was no resisting. My sense of dignity sank from under me, and out came the words: “What news from Frizinghall?”\n\n“I have seen the Indians,” answered Sergeant Cuff. “And I have found out what Rosanna bought privately in the town, on Thursday last. The Indians will be set free on Wednesday in next week. There isn’t a doubt on my mind, and there isn’t a doubt on Mr. Murthwaite’s mind, that they came to this place to steal the Moonstone. Their calculations were all thrown out, of course, by what happened in the house on Wednesday night; and they have no more to do with the actual loss of the jewel than you have. But I can tell you one thing, Mr. Betteredge—if we don’t find the Moonstone, they will. You have not heard the last of the three jugglers yet.”\n\nMr. Franklin came back from his walk as the Sergeant said those startling words. Governing his curiosity better than I had governed mine, he passed us without a word, and went on into the house.\n\nAs for me, having already dropped my dignity, I determined to have the whole benefit of the sacrifice. “So much for the Indians,” I said. “What about Rosanna next?”\n\nSergeant Cuff shook his head.\n\n“The mystery in that quarter is thicker than ever,” he said. “I have traced her to a shop at Frizinghall, kept by a linen draper named Maltby. She bought nothing whatever at any of the other drapers’ shops, or at any milliners’ or tailors’ shops; and she bought nothing at Maltby’s but a piece of long cloth. She was very particular in choosing a certain quality. As to quantity, she bought enough to make a nightgown.”\n\n“Whose nightgown?” I asked.\n\n“Her own, to be sure. Between twelve and three, on the Thursday morning, she must have slipped down to your young lady’s room, to settle the hiding of the Moonstone while all the rest of you were in bed. In going back to her own room, her nightgown must have brushed the wet paint on the door. She couldn’t wash out the stain; and she couldn’t safely destroy the night-gown without first providing another like it, to make the inventory of her linen complete.”\n\n“What proves that it was Rosanna’s nightgown?” I objected.\n\n“The material she bought for making the substitute dress,” answered the Sergeant. “If it had been Miss Verinder’s nightgown, she would have had to buy lace, and frilling, and Lord knows what besides; and she wouldn’t have had time to make it in one night. Plain long cloth means a plain servant’s nightgown. No, no, Mr. Betteredge—all that is clear enough. The pinch of the question is—why, after having provided the substitute dress, does she hide the smeared nightgown, instead of destroying it? If the girl won’t speak out, there is only one way of settling the difficulty. The hiding-place at the Shivering Sand must be searched—and the true state of the case will be discovered there.”\n\n“How are you to find the place?” I inquired.\n\n“I am sorry to disappoint you,” said the Sergeant—“but that’s a secret which I mean to keep to myself.”\n\n(Not to irritate your curiosity, as he irritated mine, I may here inform you that he had come back from Frizinghall provided with a search-warrant. His experience in such matters told him that Rosanna was in all probability carrying about her a memorandum of the hiding-place, to guide her, in case she returned to it, under changed circumstances and after a lapse of time. Possessed of this memorandum, the Sergeant would be furnished with all that he could desire.)\n\n“Now, Mr. Betteredge,” he went on, “suppose we drop speculation, and get to business. I told Joyce to have an eye on Rosanna. Where is Joyce?”\n\nJoyce was the Frizinghall policeman, who had been left by Superintendent Seegrave at Sergeant Cuff’s disposal. The clock struck two, as he put the question; and, punctual to the moment, the carriage came round to take Miss Rachel to her aunt’s.\n\n“One thing at a time,” said the Sergeant, stopping me as I was about to send in search of Joyce. “I must attend to Miss Verinder first.”\n\nAs the rain was still threatening, it was the close carriage that had been appointed to take Miss Rachel to Frizinghall. Sergeant Cuff beckoned Samuel to come down to him from the rumble behind.\n\n“You will see a friend of mine waiting among the trees, on this side of the lodge gate,” he said. “My friend, without stopping the carriage, will get up into the rumble with you. You have nothing to do but to hold your tongue, and shut your eyes. Otherwise, you will get into trouble.”\n\nWith that advice, he sent the footman back to his place. What Samuel thought I don’t know. It was plain, to my mind, that Miss Rachel was to be privately kept in view from the time when she left our house—if she did leave it. A watch set on my young lady! A spy behind her in the rumble of her mother’s carriage! I could have cut my own tongue out for having forgotten myself so far as to speak to Sergeant Cuff.\n\nThe first person to come out of the house was my lady. She stood aside, on the top step, posting herself there to see what happened. Not a word did she say, either to the Sergeant or to me. With her lips closed, and her arms folded in the light garden cloak which she had wrapped round her on coming into the air, there she stood, as still as a statue, waiting for her daughter to appear.\n\nIn a minute more, Miss Rachel came downstairs—very nicely dressed in some soft yellow stuff, that set off her dark complexion, and clipped her tight (in the form of a jacket) round the waist. She had a smart little straw hat on her head, with a white veil twisted round it. She had primrose-coloured gloves that fitted her hands like a second skin. Her beautiful black hair looked as smooth as satin under her hat. Her little ears were like rosy shells—they had a pearl dangling from each of them. She came swiftly out to us, as straight as a lily on its stem, and as lithe and supple in every movement she made as a young cat. Nothing that I could discover was altered in her pretty face, but her eyes and her lips. Her eyes were brighter and fiercer than I liked to see; and her lips had so completely lost their colour and their smile that I hardly knew them again. She kissed her mother in a hasty and sudden manner on the cheek. She said, “Try to forgive me, mamma”—and then pulled down her veil over her face so vehemently that she tore it. In another moment she had run down the steps, and had rushed into the carriage as if it was a hiding-place.\n\nSergeant Cuff was just as quick on his side. He put Samuel back, and stood before Miss Rachel, with the open carriage-door in his hand, at the instant when she settled herself in her place.\n\n“What do you want?” says Miss Rachel, from behind her veil.\n\n“I want to say one word to you, miss,” answered the Sergeant, “before you go. I can’t presume to stop your paying a visit to your aunt. I can only venture to say that your leaving us, as things are now, puts an obstacle in the way of my recovering your Diamond. Please to understand that; and now decide for yourself whether you go or stay.”\n\nMiss Rachel never even answered him. “Drive on, James!” she called out to the coachman.\n\nWithout another word, the Sergeant shut the carriage-door. Just as he closed it, Mr. Franklin came running down the steps. “Good-bye, Rachel,” he said, holding out his hand.\n\n“Drive on!” cried Miss Rachel, louder than ever, and taking no more notice of Mr. Franklin than she had taken of Sergeant Cuff.\n\nMr. Franklin stepped back thunderstruck, as well he might be. The coachman, not knowing what to do, looked towards my lady, still standing immovable on the top step. My lady, with anger and sorrow and shame all struggling together in her face, made him a sign to start the horses, and then turned back hastily into the house. Mr. Franklin, recovering the use of his speech, called after her, as the carriage drove off, “Aunt! you were quite right. Accept my thanks for all your kindness—and let me go.”\n\nMy lady turned as though to speak to him. Then, as if distrusting herself, waved her hand kindly. “Let me see you, before you leave us, Franklin,” she said, in a broken voice—and went on to her own room.\n\n“Do me a last favour, Betteredge,” says Mr. Franklin, turning to me, with the tears in his eyes. “Get me away to the train as soon as you can!”\n\nHe too went his way into the house. For the moment, Miss Rachel had completely unmanned him. Judge from that, how fond he must have been of her!\n\nSergeant Cuff and I were left face to face, at the bottom of the steps. The Sergeant stood with his face set towards a gap in the trees, commanding a view of one of the windings of the drive which led from the house. He had his hands in his pockets, and he was softly whistling “The Last Rose of Summer” to himself.\n\n“There’s a time for everything,” I said savagely enough. “This isn’t a time for whistling.”\n\nAt that moment, the carriage appeared in the distance, through the gap, on its way to the lodge-gate. There was another man, besides Samuel, plainly visible in the rumble behind.\n\n“All right!” said the Sergeant to himself. He turned round to me. “It’s no time for whistling, Mr. Betteredge, as you say. It’s time to take this business in hand, now, without sparing anybody. We’ll begin with Rosanna Spearman. Where is Joyce?”\n\nWe both called for Joyce, and received no answer. I sent one of the stable-boys to look for him.\n\n“You heard what I said to Miss Verinder?” remarked the Sergeant, while we were waiting. “And you saw how she received it? I tell her plainly that her leaving us will be an obstacle in the way of my recovering her Diamond—and she leaves, in the face of that statement! Your young lady has got a travelling companion in her mother’s carriage, Mr. Betteredge—and the name of it is, the Moonstone.”\n\nI said nothing. I only held on like death to my belief in Miss Rachel.\n\nThe stable-boy came back, followed—very unwillingly, as it appeared to me—by Joyce.\n\n“Where is Rosanna Spearman?” asked Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“I can’t account for it, sir,” Joyce began; “and I am very sorry. But somehow or other——”\n\n“Before I went to Frizinghall,” said the Sergeant, cutting him short, “I told you to keep your eyes on Rosanna Spearman, without allowing her to discover that she was being watched. Do you mean to tell me that you have let her give you the slip?”\n\n“I am afraid, sir,” says Joyce, beginning to tremble, “that I was perhaps a little too careful not to let her discover me. There are such a many passages in the lower parts of this house——”\n\n“How long is it since you missed her?”\n\n“Nigh on an hour since, sir.”\n\n“You can go back to your regular business at Frizinghall,” said the Sergeant, speaking just as composedly as ever, in his usual quiet and dreary way. “I don’t think your talents are at all in our line, Mr. Joyce. Your present form of employment is a trifle beyond you. Good morning.”\n\nThe man slunk off. I find it very difficult to describe how I was affected by the discovery that Rosanna Spearman was missing. I seemed to be in fifty different minds about it, all at the same time. In that state, I stood staring at Sergeant Cuff—and my powers of language quite failed me.\n\n“No, Mr. Betteredge,” said the Sergeant, as if he had discovered the uppermost thought in me, and was picking it out to be answered, before all the rest. “Your young friend, Rosanna, won’t slip through my fingers so easy as you think. As long as I know where Miss Verinder is, I have the means at my disposal of tracing Miss Verinder’s accomplice. I prevented them from communicating last night. Very good. They will get together at Frizinghall, instead of getting together here. The present inquiry must be simply shifted (rather sooner than I had anticipated) from this house, to the house at which Miss Verinder is visiting. In the meantime, I’m afraid I must trouble you to call the servants together again.”\n\nI went round with him to the servants’ hall. It is very disgraceful, but it is not the less true, that I had another attack of the detective-fever, when he said those last words. I forgot that I hated Sergeant Cuff. I seized him confidentially by the arm. I said, “For goodness’ sake, tell us what you are going to do with the servants now?”\n\nThe great Cuff stood stock-still, and addressed himself in a kind of melancholy rapture to the empty air.\n\n“If this man,” said the Sergeant (apparently meaning me), “only understood the growing of roses he would be the most completely perfect character on the face of creation!” After that strong expression of feeling, he sighed, and put his arm through mine. “This is how it stands,” he said, dropping down again to business. “Rosanna has done one of two things. She has either gone direct to Frizinghall (before I can get there), or she has gone first to visit her hiding-place at the Shivering Sand. The first thing to find out is, which of the servants saw the last of her before she left the house.”\n\nOn instituting this inquiry, it turned out that the last person who had set eyes on Rosanna was Nancy, the kitchenmaid.\n\nNancy had seen her slip out with a letter in her hand, and stop the butcher’s man who had just been delivering some meat at the back door. Nancy had heard her ask the man to post the letter when he got back to Frizinghall. The man had looked at the address, and had said it was a roundabout way of delivering a letter directed to Cobb’s Hole, to post it at Frizinghall—and that, moreover, on a Saturday, which would prevent the letter from getting to its destination until Monday morning. Rosanna had answered that the delivery of the letter being delayed till Monday was of no importance. The only thing she wished to be sure of was that the man would do what she told him. The man had promised to do it, and had driven away. Nancy had been called back to her work in the kitchen. And no other person had seen anything afterwards of Rosanna Spearman.\n\n“Well?” I asked, when we were alone again.\n\n“Well,” says the Sergeant. “I must go to Frizinghall.”\n\n“About the letter, sir?”\n\n“Yes. The memorandum of the hiding-place is in that letter. I must see the address at the post-office. If it is the address I suspect, I shall pay our friend, Mrs. Yolland, another visit on Monday next.”\n\nI went with the Sergeant to order the pony-chaise. In the stable-yard we got a new light thrown on the missing girl.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX\n\nThe news of Rosanna’s disappearance had, as it appeared, spread among the out-of-door servants. They too had made their inquiries; and they had just laid hands on a quick little imp, nicknamed “Duffy”—who was occasionally employed in weeding the garden, and who had seen Rosanna Spearman as lately as half-an-hour since. Duffy was certain that the girl had passed him in the fir-plantation, not walking, but running, in the direction of the sea-shore.\n\n“Does this boy know the coast hereabouts?” asked Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“He has been born and bred on the coast,” I answered.\n\n“Duffy!” says the Sergeant, “do you want to earn a shilling? If you do, come along with me. Keep the pony-chaise ready, Mr. Betteredge, till I come back.”\n\nHe started for the Shivering Sand, at a rate that my legs (though well enough preserved for my time of life) had no hope of matching. Little Duffy, as the way is with the young savages in our parts when they are in high spirits, gave a howl, and trotted off at the Sergeant’s heels.\n\nHere again, I find it impossible to give anything like a clear account of the state of my mind in the interval after Sergeant Cuff had left us. A curious and stupefying restlessness got possession of me. I did a dozen different needless things in and out of the house, not one of which I can now remember. I don’t even know how long it was after the Sergeant had gone to the sands, when Duffy came running back with a message for me. Sergeant Cuff had given the boy a leaf torn out of his pocket-book, on which was written in pencil, “Send me one of Rosanna Spearman’s boots, and be quick about it.”\n\nI despatched the first woman-servant I could find to Rosanna’s room; and I sent the boy back to say that I myself would follow him with the boot.\n\nThis, I am well aware, was not the quickest way to take of obeying the directions which I had received. But I was resolved to see for myself what new mystification was going on before I trusted Rosanna’s boot in the Sergeant’s hands. My old notion of screening the girl, if I could, seemed to have come back on me again, at the eleventh hour. This state of feeling (to say nothing of the detective-fever) hurried me off, as soon as I had got the boot, at the nearest approach to a run which a man turned seventy can reasonably hope to make.\n\nAs I got near the shore, the clouds gathered black, and the rain came down, drifting in great white sheets of water before the wind. I heard the thunder of the sea on the sand-bank at the mouth of the bay. A little further on, I passed the boy crouching for shelter under the lee of the sandhills. Then I saw the raging sea, and the rollers tumbling in on the sand-bank, and the driven rain sweeping over the waters like a flying garment, and the yellow wilderness of the beach with one solitary black figure standing on it—the figure of Sergeant Cuff.\n\nHe waved his hand towards the north, when he first saw me. “Keep on that side!” he shouted. “And come on down here to me!”\n\nI went down to him, choking for breath, with my heart leaping as if it was like to leap out of me. I was past speaking. I had a hundred questions to put to him; and not one of them would pass my lips. His face frightened me. I saw a look in his eyes which was a look of horror. He snatched the boot out of my hand, and set it in a footmark on the sand, bearing south from us as we stood, and pointing straight towards the rocky ledge called the South Spit. The mark was not yet blurred out by the rain—and the girl’s boot fitted it to a hair.\n\nThe Sergeant pointed to the boot in the footmark, without saying a word.\n\nI caught at his arm, and tried to speak to him, and failed as I had failed when I tried before. He went on, following the footsteps down and down to where the rocks and the sand joined. The South Spit was just awash with the flowing tide; the waters heaved over the hidden face of the Shivering Sand. Now this way and now that, with an obstinate patience that was dreadful to see, Sergeant Cuff tried the boot in the footsteps, and always found it pointing the same way—straight to the rocks. Hunt as he might, no sign could he find anywhere of the footsteps walking from them.\n\nHe gave it up at last. Still keeping silence, he looked again at me; and then he looked out at the waters before us, heaving in deeper and deeper over the quicksand. I looked where he looked—and I saw his thought in his face. A dreadful dumb trembling crawled all over me on a sudden. I fell upon my knees on the beach.\n\n“She has been back at the hiding-place,” I heard the Sergeant say to himself. “Some fatal accident has happened to her on those rocks.”\n\nThe girl’s altered looks, and words, and actions—the numbed, deadened way in which she listened to me, and spoke to me—when I had found her sweeping the corridor but a few hours since, rose up in my mind, and warned me, even as the Sergeant spoke, that his guess was wide of the dreadful truth. I tried to tell him of the fear that had frozen me up. I tried to say, “The death she has died, Sergeant, was a death of her own seeking.” No! the words wouldn’t come. The dumb trembling held me in its grip. I couldn’t feel the driving rain. I couldn’t see the rising tide. As in the vision of a dream, the poor lost creature came back before me. I saw her again as I had seen her in the past time—on the morning when I went to fetch her into the house. I heard her again, telling me that the Shivering Sand seemed to draw her to it against her will, and wondering whether her grave was waiting for her there. The horror of it struck at me, in some unfathomable way, through my own child. My girl was just her age. My girl, tried as Rosanna was tried, might have lived that miserable life, and died this dreadful death.\n\nThe Sergeant kindly lifted me up, and turned me away from the sight of the place where she had perished.\n\nWith that relief, I began to fetch my breath again, and to see things about me, as things really were. Looking towards the sandhills, I saw the men-servants from out-of-doors, and the fisherman, named Yolland, all running down to us together; and all, having taken the alarm, calling out to know if the girl had been found. In the fewest words, the Sergeant showed them the evidence of the footmarks, and told them that a fatal accident must have happened to her. He then picked out the fisherman from the rest, and put a question to him, turning about again towards the sea: “Tell me,” he said. “Could a boat have taken her off, in such weather as this, from those rocks where her footmarks stop?”\n\nThe fisherman pointed to the rollers tumbling in on the sand-bank, and to the great waves leaping up in clouds of foam against the headlands on either side of us.\n\n“No boat that ever was built,” he answered, “could have got to her through that.”\n\nSergeant Cuff looked for the last time at the foot-marks on the sand, which the rain was now fast blurring out.\n\n“There,” he said, “is the evidence that she can’t have left this place by land. And here,” he went on, looking at the fisherman, “is the evidence that she can’t have got away by sea.” He stopped, and considered for a minute. “She was seen running towards this place, half an hour before I got here from the house,” he said to Yolland. “Some time has passed since then. Call it, altogether, an hour ago. How high would the water be, at that time, on this side of the rocks?” He pointed to the south side—otherwise, the side which was not filled up by the quicksand.\n\n“As the tide makes today,” said the fisherman, “there wouldn’t have been water enough to drown a kitten on that side of the Spit, an hour since.”\n\nSergeant Cuff turned about northward, towards the quicksand.\n\n“How much on this side?” he asked.\n\n“Less still,” answered Yolland. “The Shivering Sand would have been just awash, and no more.”\n\nThe Sergeant turned to me, and said that the accident must have happened on the side of the quicksand. My tongue was loosened at that. “No accident!” I told him. “When she came to this place, she came weary of her life, to end it here.”\n\nHe started back from me. “How do you know?” he asked. The rest of them crowded round. The Sergeant recovered himself instantly. He put them back from me; he said I was an old man; he said the discovery had shaken me; he said, “Let him alone a little.” Then he turned to Yolland, and asked, “Is there any chance of finding her, when the tide ebbs again?” And Yolland answered, “None. What the Sand gets, the Sand keeps for ever.” Having said that, the fisherman came a step nearer, and addressed himself to me.\n\n“Mr. Betteredge,” he said, “I have a word to say to you about the young woman’s death. Four foot out, broadwise, along the side of the Spit, there’s a shelf of rock, about half fathom down under the sand. My question is—why didn’t she strike that? If she slipped, by accident, from off the Spit, she fell in where there’s foothold at the bottom, at a depth that would barely cover her to the waist. She must have waded out, or jumped out, into the Deeps beyond—or she wouldn’t be missing now. No accident, sir! The Deeps of the Quicksand have got her. And they have got her by her own act.”\n\nAfter that testimony from a man whose knowledge was to be relied on, the Sergeant was silent. The rest of us, like him, held our peace. With one accord, we all turned back up the slope of the beach.\n\nAt the sandhillocks we were met by the under-groom, running to us from the house. The lad is a good lad, and has an honest respect for me. He handed me a little note, with a decent sorrow in his face. “Penelope sent me with this, Mr. Betteredge,” he said. “She found it in Rosanna’s room.”\n\nIt was her last farewell word to the old man who had done his best—thank God, always done his best—to befriend her.\n\n“You have often forgiven me, Mr. Betteredge, in past times. When you next see the Shivering Sand, try to forgive me once more. I have found my grave where my grave was waiting for me. I have lived, and died, sir, grateful for your kindness.”\n\nThere was no more than that. Little as it was, I hadn’t manhood enough to hold up against it. Your tears come easy, when you’re young, and beginning the world. Your tears come easy, when you’re old, and leaving it. I burst out crying.\n\nSergeant Cuff took a step nearer to me—meaning kindly, I don’t doubt. I shrank back from him. “Don’t touch me,” I said. “It’s the dread of you, that has driven her to it.”\n\n“You are wrong, Mr. Betteredge,” he answered, quietly. “But there will be time enough to speak of it when we are indoors again.”\n\nI followed the rest of them, with the help of the groom’s arm. Through the driving rain we went back—to meet the trouble and the terror that were waiting for us at the house.\n\n# CHAPTER XX\n\nThose in front had spread the news before us. We found the servants in a state of panic. As we passed my lady’s door, it was thrown open violently from the inner side. My mistress came out among us (with Mr. Franklin following, and trying vainly to compose her), quite beside herself with the horror of the thing.\n\n“You are answerable for this!” she cried out, threatening the Sergeant wildly with her hand. “Gabriel! give that wretch his money—and release me from the sight of him!”\n\nThe Sergeant was the only one among us who was fit to cope with her—being the only one among us who was in possession of himself.\n\n“I am no more answerable for this distressing calamity, my lady, than you are,” he said. “If, in half an hour from this, you still insist on my leaving the house, I will accept your ladyship’s dismissal, but not your ladyship’s money.”\n\nIt was spoken very respectfully, but very firmly at the same time—and it had its effect on my mistress as well as on me. She suffered Mr. Franklin to lead her back into the room. As the door closed on the two, the Sergeant, looking about among the women-servants in his observant way, noticed that while all the rest were merely frightened, Penelope was in tears. “When your father has changed his wet clothes,” he said to her, “come and speak to us, in your father’s room.”\n\nBefore the half-hour was out, I had got my dry clothes on, and had lent Sergeant Cuff such change of dress as he required. Penelope came in to us to hear what the Sergeant wanted with her. I don’t think I ever felt what a good dutiful daughter I had, so strongly as I felt it at that moment. I took her and sat her on my knee and I prayed God bless her. She hid her head on my bosom, and put her arms round my neck—and we waited a little while in silence. The poor dead girl must have been at the bottom of it, I think, with my daughter and with me. The Sergeant went to the window, and stood there looking out. I thought it right to thank him for considering us both in this way—and I did.\n\nPeople in high life have all the luxuries to themselves—among others, the luxury of indulging their feelings. People in low life have no such privilege. Necessity, which spares our betters, has no pity on us. We learn to put our feelings back into ourselves, and to jog on with our duties as patiently as may be. I don’t complain of this—I only notice it. Penelope and I were ready for the Sergeant, as soon as the Sergeant was ready on his side. Asked if she knew what had led her fellow-servant to destroy herself, my daughter answered (as you will foresee) that it was for love of Mr. Franklin Blake. Asked next, if she had mentioned this notion of hers to any other person, Penelope answered, “I have not mentioned it, for Rosanna’s sake.” I felt it necessary to add a word to this. I said, “And for Mr. Franklin’s sake, my dear, as well. If Rosanna has died for love of him, it is not with his knowledge or by his fault. Let him leave the house today, if he does leave it, without the useless pain of knowing the truth.” Sergeant Cuff said, “Quite right,” and fell silent again; comparing Penelope’s notion (as it seemed to me) with some other notion of his own which he kept to himself.\n\nAt the end of the half-hour, my mistress’s bell rang.\n\nOn my way to answer it, I met Mr. Franklin coming out of his aunt’s sitting-room. He mentioned that her ladyship was ready to see Sergeant Cuff—in my presence as before—and he added that he himself wanted to say two words to the Sergeant first. On our way back to my room, he stopped, and looked at the railway time-table in the hall.\n\n“Are you really going to leave us, sir?” I asked. “Miss Rachel will surely come right again, if you only give her time?”\n\n“She will come right again,” answered Mr. Franklin, “when she hears that I have gone away, and that she will see me no more.”\n\nI thought he spoke in resentment of my young lady’s treatment of him. But it was not so. My mistress had noticed, from the time when the police first came into the house, that the bare mention of him was enough to set Miss Rachel’s temper in a flame. He had been too fond of his cousin to like to confess this to himself, until the truth had been forced on him, when she drove off to her aunt’s. His eyes once opened in that cruel way which you know of, Mr. Franklin had taken his resolution—the one resolution which a man of any spirit could take—to leave the house.\n\nWhat he had to say to the Sergeant was spoken in my presence. He described her ladyship as willing to acknowledge that she had spoken over-hastily. And he asked if Sergeant Cuff would consent—in that case—to accept his fee, and to leave the matter of the Diamond where the matter stood now. The Sergeant answered, “No, sir. My fee is paid me for doing my duty. I decline to take it, until my duty is done.”\n\n“I don’t understand you,” says Mr. Franklin.\n\n“I’ll explain myself, sir,” says the Sergeant. “When I came here, I undertook to throw the necessary light on the matter of the missing Diamond. I am now ready, and waiting to redeem my pledge. When I have stated the case to Lady Verinder as the case now stands, and when I have told her plainly what course of action to take for the recovery of the Moonstone, the responsibility will be off my shoulders. Let her ladyship decide, after that, whether she does, or does not, allow me to go on. I shall then have done what I undertook to do—and I’ll take my fee.”\n\nIn those words Sergeant Cuff reminded us that, even in the Detective Police, a man may have a reputation to lose.\n\nThe view he took was so plainly the right one, that there was no more to be said. As I rose to conduct him to my lady’s room, he asked if Mr. Franklin wished to be present. Mr. Franklin answered, “Not unless Lady Verinder desires it.” He added, in a whisper to me, as I was following the Sergeant out, “I know what that man is going to say about Rachel; and I am too fond of her to hear it, and keep my temper. Leave me by myself.”\n\nI left him, miserable enough, leaning on the sill of my window, with his face hidden in his hands and Penelope peeping through the door, longing to comfort him. In Mr. Franklin’s place, I should have called her in. When you are ill-used by one woman, there is great comfort in telling it to another—because, nine times out of ten, the other always takes your side. Perhaps, when my back was turned, he did call her in? In that case it is only doing my daughter justice to declare that she would stick at nothing, in the way of comforting Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nIn the meantime, Sergeant Cuff and I proceeded to my lady’s room.\n\nAt the last conference we had held with her, we had found her not over willing to lift her eyes from the book which she had on the table. On this occasion there was a change for the better. She met the Sergeant’s eye with an eye that was as steady as his own. The family spirit showed itself in every line of her face; and I knew that Sergeant Cuff would meet his match, when a woman like my mistress was strung up to hear the worst he could say to her.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI\n\nThe first words, when we had taken our seats, were spoken by my lady.\n\n“Sergeant Cuff,” she said, “there was perhaps some excuse for the inconsiderate manner in which I spoke to you half an hour since. I have no wish, however, to claim that excuse. I say, with perfect sincerity, that I regret it, if I wronged you.”\n\nThe grace of voice and manner with which she made him that atonement had its due effect on the Sergeant. He requested permission to justify himself—putting his justification as an act of respect to my mistress. It was impossible, he said, that he could be in any way responsible for the calamity, which had shocked us all, for this sufficient reason, that his success in bringing his inquiry to its proper end depended on his neither saying nor doing anything that could alarm Rosanna Spearman. He appealed to me to testify whether he had, or had not, carried that object out. I could, and did, bear witness that he had. And there, as I thought, the matter might have been judiciously left to come to an end.\n\nSergeant Cuff, however, took it a step further, evidently (as you shall now judge) with the purpose of forcing the most painful of all possible explanations to take place between her ladyship and himself.\n\n“I have heard a motive assigned for the young woman’s suicide,” said the Sergeant, “which may possibly be the right one. It is a motive quite unconnected with the case which I am conducting here. I am bound to add, however, that my own opinion points the other way. Some unbearable anxiety in connexion with the missing Diamond, has, I believe, driven the poor creature to her own destruction. I don’t pretend to know what that unbearable anxiety may have been. But I think (with your ladyship’s permission) I can lay my hand on a person who is capable of deciding whether I am right or wrong.”\n\n“Is the person now in the house?” my mistress asked, after waiting a little.\n\n“The person has left the house, my lady.”\n\nThat answer pointed as straight to Miss Rachel as straight could be. A silence dropped on us which I thought would never come to an end. Lord! how the wind howled, and how the rain drove at the window, as I sat there waiting for one or other of them to speak again!\n\n“Be so good as to express yourself plainly,” said my lady. “Do you refer to my daughter?”\n\n“I do,” said Sergeant Cuff, in so many words.\n\nMy mistress had her cheque-book on the table when we entered the room—no doubt to pay the Sergeant his fee. She now put it back in the drawer. It went to my heart to see how her poor hand trembled—the hand that had loaded her old servant with benefits; the hand that, I pray God, may take mine, when my time comes, and I leave my place for ever!\n\n“I had hoped,” said my lady, very slowly and quietly, “to have recompensed your services, and to have parted with you without Miss Verinder’s name having been openly mentioned between us as it has been mentioned now. My nephew has probably said something of this, before you came into my room?”\n\n“Mr. Blake gave his message, my lady. And I gave Mr. Blake a reason——”\n\n“It is needless to tell me your reason. After what you have just said, you know as well as I do that you have gone too far to go back. I owe it to myself, and I owe it to my child, to insist on your remaining here, and to insist on your speaking out.”\n\nThe Sergeant looked at his watch.\n\n“If there had been time, my lady,” he answered, “I should have preferred writing my report, instead of communicating it by word of mouth. But, if this inquiry is to go on, time is of too much importance to be wasted in writing. I am ready to go into the matter at once. It is a very painful matter for me to speak of, and for you to hear.”\n\nThere my mistress stopped him once more.\n\n“I may possibly make it less painful to you, and to my good servant and friend here,” she said, “if I set the example of speaking boldly, on my side. You suspect Miss Verinder of deceiving us all, by secreting the Diamond for some purpose of her own? Is that true?”\n\n“Quite true, my lady.”\n\n“Very well. Now, before you begin, I have to tell you, as Miss Verinder’s mother, that she is absolutely incapable of doing what you suppose her to have done. Your knowledge of her character dates from a day or two since. My knowledge of her character dates from the beginning of her life. State your suspicion of her as strongly as you please—it is impossible that you can offend me by doing so. I am sure, beforehand, that (with all your experience) the circumstances have fatally misled you in this case. Mind! I am in possession of no private information. I am as absolutely shut out of my daughter’s confidence as you are. My one reason for speaking positively, is the reason you have heard already. I know my child.”\n\nShe turned to me, and gave me her hand. I kissed it in silence. “You may go on,” she said, facing the Sergeant again as steadily as ever.\n\nSergeant Cuff bowed. My mistress had produced but one effect on him. His hatchet-face softened for a moment, as if he was sorry for her. As to shaking him in his own conviction, it was plain to see that she had not moved him by a single inch. He settled himself in his chair; and he began his vile attack on Miss Rachel’s character in these words:\n\n“I must ask your ladyship,” he said, “to look this matter in the face, from my point of view as well as from yours. Will you please to suppose yourself coming down here, in my place, and with my experience? and will you allow me to mention very briefly what that experience has been?”\n\nMy mistress signed to him that she would do this. The Sergeant went on:\n\n“For the last twenty years,” he said, “I have been largely employed in cases of family scandal, acting in the capacity of confidential man. The one result of my domestic practice which has any bearing on the matter now in hand, is a result which I may state in two words. It is well within my experience, that young ladies of rank and position do occasionally have private debts which they dare not acknowledge to their nearest relatives and friends. Sometimes, the milliner and the jeweller are at the bottom of it. Sometimes, the money is wanted for purposes which I don’t suspect in this case, and which I won’t shock you by mentioning. Bear in mind what I have said, my lady—and now let us see how events in this house have forced me back on my own experience, whether I liked it or not!”\n\nHe considered with himself for a moment, and went on—with a horrid clearness that obliged you to understand him; with an abominable justice that favoured nobody.\n\n“My first information relating to the loss of the Moonstone,” said the Sergeant, “came to me from Superintendent Seegrave. He proved to my complete satisfaction that he was perfectly incapable of managing the case. The one thing he said which struck me as worth listening to, was this—that Miss Verinder had declined to be questioned by him, and had spoken to him with a perfectly incomprehensible rudeness and contempt. I thought this curious—but I attributed it mainly to some clumsiness on the Superintendent’s part which might have offended the young lady. After that, I put it by in my mind, and applied myself, single-handed, to the case. It ended, as you are aware, in the discovery of the smear on the door, and in Mr. Franklin Blake’s evidence satisfying me, that this same smear, and the loss of the Diamond, were pieces of the same puzzle. So far, if I suspected anything, I suspected that the Moonstone had been stolen, and that one of the servants might prove to be the thief. Very good. In this state of things, what happens? Miss Verinder suddenly comes out of her room, and speaks to me. I observe three suspicious appearances in that young lady. She is still violently agitated, though more than four-and-twenty hours have passed since the Diamond was lost. She treats me as she has already treated Superintendent Seegrave. And she is mortally offended with Mr. Franklin Blake. Very good again. Here (I say to myself) is a young lady who has lost a valuable jewel—a young lady, also, as my own eyes and ears inform me, who is of an impetuous temperament. Under these circumstances, and with that character, what does she do? She betrays an incomprehensible resentment against Mr. Blake, Mr. Superintendent, and myself—otherwise, the very three people who have all, in their different ways, been trying to help her to recover her lost jewel. Having brought my inquiry to that point—then, my lady, and not till then, I begin to look back into my own mind for my own experience. My own experience explains Miss Verinder’s otherwise incomprehensible conduct. It associates her with those other young ladies that I know of. It tells me she has debts she daren’t acknowledge, that must be paid. And it sets me asking myself, whether the loss of the Diamond may not mean—that the Diamond must be secretly pledged to pay them. That is the conclusion which my experience draws from plain facts. What does your ladyship’s experience say against it?”\n\n“What I have said already,” answered my mistress. “The circumstances have misled you.”\n\nI said nothing on my side. Robinson Crusoe—God knows how—had got into my muddled old head. If Sergeant Cuff had found himself, at that moment, transported to a desert island, without a man Friday to keep him company, or a ship to take him off—he would have found himself exactly where I wished him to be! (Nota bene:—I am an average good Christian, when you don’t push my Christianity too far. And all the rest of you—which is a great comfort—are, in this respect, much the same as I am.)\n\nSergeant Cuff went on:\n\n“Right or wrong, my lady,” he said, “having drawn my conclusion, the next thing to do was to put it to the test. I suggested to your ladyship the examination of all the wardrobes in the house. It was a means of finding the article of dress which had, in all probability, made the smear; and it was a means of putting my conclusion to the test. How did it turn out? Your ladyship consented; Mr. Blake consented; Mr. Ablewhite consented. Miss Verinder alone stopped the whole proceeding by refusing point-blank. That result satisfied me that my view was the right one. If your ladyship and Mr. Betteredge persist in not agreeing with me, you must be blind to what happened before you this very day. In your hearing, I told the young lady that her leaving the house (as things were then) would put an obstacle in the way of my recovering her jewel. You saw yourselves that she drove off in the face of that statement. You saw yourself that, so far from forgiving Mr. Blake for having done more than all the rest of you to put the clue into my hands, she publicly insulted Mr. Blake, on the steps of her mother’s house. What do these things mean? If Miss Verinder is not privy to the suppression of the Diamond, what do these things mean?”\n\nThis time he looked my way. It was downright frightful to hear him piling up proof after proof against Miss Rachel, and to know, while one was longing to defend her, that there was no disputing the truth of what he said. I am (thank God!) constitutionally superior to reason. This enabled me to hold firm to my lady’s view, which was my view also. This roused my spirit, and made me put a bold face on it before Sergeant Cuff. Profit, good friends, I beseech you, by my example. It will save you from many troubles of the vexing sort. Cultivate a superiority to reason, and see how you pare the claws of all the sensible people when they try to scratch you for your own good!\n\nFinding that I made no remark, and that my mistress made no remark, Sergeant Cuff proceeded. Lord! how it did enrage me to notice that he was not in the least put out by our silence!\n\n“There is the case, my lady, as it stands against Miss Verinder alone,” he said. “The next thing is to put the case as it stands against Miss Verinder and the deceased Rosanna Spearman taken together. We will go back for a moment, if you please, to your daughter’s refusal to let her wardrobe be examined. My mind being made up, after that circumstance, I had two questions to consider next. First, as to the right method of conducting my inquiry. Second, as to whether Miss Verinder had an accomplice among the female servants in the house. After carefully thinking it over, I determined to conduct the inquiry in, what we should call at our office, a highly irregular manner. For this reason: I had a family scandal to deal with, which it was my business to keep within the family limits. The less noise made, and the fewer strangers employed to help me, the better. As to the usual course of taking people in custody on suspicion, going before the magistrate, and all the rest of it—nothing of the sort was to be thought of, when your ladyship’s daughter was (as I believed) at the bottom of the whole business. In this case, I felt that a person of Mr. Betteredge’s character and position in the house—knowing the servants as he did, and having the honour of the family at heart—would be safer to take as an assistant than any other person whom I could lay my hand on. I should have tried Mr. Blake as well—but for one obstacle in the way. He saw the drift of my proceedings at a very early date; and, with his interest in Miss Verinder, any mutual understanding was impossible between him and me. I trouble your ladyship with these particulars to show you that I have kept the family secret within the family circle. I am the only outsider who knows it—and my professional existence depends on holding my tongue.”\n\nHere I felt that my professional existence depended on not holding my tongue. To be held up before my mistress, in my old age, as a sort of deputy-policeman, was, once again, more than my Christianity was strong enough to bear.\n\n“I beg to inform your ladyship,” I said, “that I never, to my knowledge, helped this abominable detective business, in any way, from first to last; and I summon Sergeant Cuff to contradict me, if he dares!”\n\nHaving given vent in those words, I felt greatly relieved. Her ladyship honoured me by a little friendly pat on the shoulder. I looked with righteous indignation at the Sergeant, to see what he thought of such a testimony as that! The Sergeant looked back like a lamb, and seemed to like me better than ever.\n\nMy lady informed him that he might continue his statement. “I understand,” she said, “that you have honestly done your best, in what you believe to be my interest. I am ready to hear what you have to say next.”\n\n“What I have to say next,” answered Sergeant Cuff, “relates to Rosanna Spearman. I recognised the young woman, as your ladyship may remember, when she brought the washing-book into this room. Up to that time I was inclined to doubt whether Miss Verinder had trusted her secret to anyone. When I saw Rosanna, I altered my mind. I suspected her at once of being privy to the suppression of the Diamond. The poor creature has met her death by a dreadful end, and I don’t want your ladyship to think, now she’s gone, that I was unduly hard on her. If this had been a common case of thieving, I should have given Rosanna the benefit of the doubt just as freely as I should have given it to any of the other servants in the house. Our experience of the Reformatory women is, that when tried in service—and when kindly and judiciously treated—they prove themselves in the majority of cases to be honestly penitent, and honestly worthy of the pains taken with them. But this was not a common case of thieving. It was a case—in my mind—of a deeply planned fraud, with the owner of the Diamond at the bottom of it. Holding this view, the first consideration which naturally presented itself to me, in connection with Rosanna, was this: Would Miss Verinder be satisfied (begging your ladyship’s pardon) with leading us all to think that the Moonstone was merely lost? Or would she go a step further, and delude us into believing that the Moonstone was stolen? In the latter event there was Rosanna Spearman—with the character of a thief—ready to her hand; the person of all others to lead your ladyship off, and to lead me off, on a false scent.”\n\nWas it possible (I asked myself) that he could put his case against Miss Rachel and Rosanna in a more horrid point of view than this? It was possible, as you shall now see.\n\n“I had another reason for suspecting the deceased woman,” he said, “which appears to me to have been stronger still. Who would be the very person to help Miss Verinder in raising money privately on the Diamond? Rosanna Spearman. No young lady in Miss Verinder’s position could manage such a risky matter as that by herself. A go-between she must have, and who so fit, I ask again, as Rosanna Spearman? Your ladyship’s deceased housemaid was at the top of her profession when she was a thief. She had relations, to my certain knowledge, with one of the few men in London (in the money-lending line) who would advance a large sum on such a notable jewel as the Moonstone, without asking awkward questions, or insisting on awkward conditions. Bear this in mind, my lady; and now let me show you how my suspicions have been justified by Rosanna’s own acts, and by the plain inferences to be drawn from them.”\n\nHe thereupon passed the whole of Rosanna’s proceedings under review. You are already as well acquainted with those proceedings as I am; and you will understand how unanswerably this part of his report fixed the guilt of being concerned in the disappearance of the Moonstone on the memory of the poor dead girl. Even my mistress was daunted by what he said now. She made him no answer when he had done. It didn’t seem to matter to the Sergeant whether he was answered or not. On he went (devil take him!), just as steady as ever.\n\n“Having stated the whole case as I understand it,” he said, “I have only to tell your ladyship, now, what I propose to do next. I see two ways of bringing this inquiry successfully to an end. One of those ways I look upon as a certainty. The other, I admit, is a bold experiment, and nothing more. Your ladyship shall decide. Shall we take the certainty first?”\n\nMy mistress made him a sign to take his own way, and choose for himself.\n\n“Thank you,” said the Sergeant. “We’ll begin with the certainty, as your ladyship is so good as to leave it to me. Whether Miss Verinder remains at Frizinghall, or whether she returns here, I propose, in either case, to keep a careful watch on all her proceedings—on the people she sees, on the rides and walks she may take, and on the letters she may write and receive.”\n\n“What next?” asked my mistress.\n\n“I shall next,” answered the Sergeant, “request your ladyship’s leave to introduce into the house, as a servant in the place of Rosanna Spearman, a woman accustomed to private inquiries of this sort, for whose discretion I can answer.”\n\n“What next?” repeated my mistress.\n\n“Next,” proceeded the Sergeant, “and last, I propose to send one of my brother-officers to make an arrangement with that money-lender in London, whom I mentioned just now as formerly acquainted with Rosanna Spearman—and whose name and address, your ladyship may rely on it, have been communicated by Rosanna to Miss Verinder. I don’t deny that the course of action I am now suggesting will cost money, and consume time. But the result is certain. We run a line round the Moonstone, and we draw that line closer and closer till we find it in Miss Verinder’s possession, supposing she decides to keep it. If her debts press, and she decides on sending it away, then we have our man ready, and we meet the Moonstone on its arrival in London.”\n\nTo hear her own daughter made the subject of such a proposal as this, stung my mistress into speaking angrily for the first time.\n\n“Consider your proposal declined, in every particular,” she said. “And go on to your other way of bringing the inquiry to an end.”\n\n“My other way,” said the Sergeant, going on as easy as ever, “is to try that bold experiment to which I have alluded. I think I have formed a pretty correct estimate of Miss Verinder’s temperament. She is quite capable (according to my belief) of committing a daring fraud. But she is too hot and impetuous in temper, and too little accustomed to deceit as a habit, to act the hypocrite in small things, and to restrain herself under all provocations. Her feelings, in this case, have repeatedly got beyond her control, at the very time when it was plainly her interest to conceal them. It is on this peculiarity in her character that I now propose to act. I want to give her a great shock suddenly, under circumstances that will touch her to the quick. In plain English, I want to tell Miss Verinder, without a word of warning, of Rosanna’s death—on the chance that her own better feelings will hurry her into making a clean breast of it. Does your ladyship accept that alternative?”\n\nMy mistress astonished me beyond all power of expression. She answered him on the instant:\n\n“Yes; I do.”\n\n“The pony-chaise is ready,” said the Sergeant. “I wish your ladyship good morning.”\n\nMy lady held up her hand, and stopped him at the door.\n\n“My daughter’s better feelings shall be appealed to, as you propose,” she said. “But I claim the right, as her mother, of putting her to the test myself. You will remain here, if you please; and I will go to Frizinghall.”\n\nFor once in his life, the great Cuff stood speechless with amazement, like an ordinary man.\n\nMy mistress rang the bell, and ordered her waterproof things. It was still pouring with rain; and the close carriage had gone, as you know, with Miss Rachel to Frizinghall. I tried to dissuade her ladyship from facing the severity of the weather. Quite useless! I asked leave to go with her, and hold the umbrella. She wouldn’t hear of it. The pony-chaise came round, with the groom in charge. “You may rely on two things,” she said to Sergeant Cuff, in the hall. “I will try the experiment on Miss Verinder as boldly as you could try it yourself. And I will inform you of the result, either personally or by letter, before the last train leaves for London tonight.”\n\nWith that, she stepped into the chaise, and, taking the reins herself, drove off to Frizinghall.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII\n\nMy mistress having left us, I had leisure to think of Sergeant Cuff. I found him sitting in a snug corner of the hall, consulting his memorandum book, and curling up viciously at the corners of the lips.\n\n“Making notes of the case?” I asked.\n\n“No,” said the Sergeant. “Looking to see what my next professional engagement is.”\n\n“Oh!” I said. “You think it’s all over then, here?”\n\n“I think,” answered Sergeant Cuff, “that Lady Verinder is one of the cleverest women in England. I also think a rose much better worth looking at than a diamond. Where is the gardener, Mr. Betteredge?”\n\nThere was no getting a word more out of him on the matter of the Moonstone. He had lost all interest in his own inquiry; and he would persist in looking for the gardener. An hour afterwards, I heard them at high words in the conservatory, with the dog-rose once more at the bottom of the dispute.\n\nIn the meantime, it was my business to find out whether Mr. Franklin persisted in his resolution to leave us by the afternoon train. After having been informed of the conference in my lady’s room, and of how it had ended, he immediately decided on waiting to hear the news from Frizinghall. This very natural alteration in his plans—which, with ordinary people, would have led to nothing in particular—proved, in Mr. Franklin’s case, to have one objectionable result. It left him unsettled, with a legacy of idle time on his hands, and, in so doing, it let out all the foreign sides of his character, one on the top of another, like rats out of a bag.\n\nNow as an Italian-Englishman, now as a German-Englishman, and now as a French-Englishman, he drifted in and out of all the sitting-rooms in the house, with nothing to talk of but Miss Rachel’s treatment of him; and with nobody to address himself to but me. I found him (for example) in the library, sitting under the map of Modern Italy, and quite unaware of any other method of meeting his troubles, except the method of talking about them. “I have several worthy aspirations, Betteredge; but what am I to do with them now? I am full of dormant good qualities, if Rachel would only have helped me to bring them out!” He was so eloquent in drawing the picture of his own neglected merits, and so pathetic in lamenting over it when it was done, that I felt quite at my wits’ end how to console him, when it suddenly occurred to me that here was a case for the wholesome application of a bit of Robinson Crusoe. I hobbled out to my own room, and hobbled back with that immortal book. Nobody in the library! The map of Modern Italy stared at me; and I stared at the map of Modern Italy.\n\nI tried the drawing-room. There was his handkerchief on the floor, to prove that he had drifted in. And there was the empty room to prove that he had drifted out again.\n\nI tried the dining-room, and discovered Samuel with a biscuit and a glass of sherry, silently investigating the empty air. A minute since, Mr. Franklin had rung furiously for a little light refreshment. On its production, in a violent hurry, by Samuel, Mr. Franklin had vanished before the bell downstairs had quite done ringing with the pull he had given to it.\n\nI tried the morning-room, and found him at last. There he was at the window, drawing hieroglyphics with his finger in the damp on the glass.\n\n“Your sherry is waiting for you, sir,” I said to him. I might as well have addressed myself to one of the four walls of the room; he was down in the bottomless deep of his own meditations, past all pulling up. “How do you explain Rachel’s conduct, Betteredge?” was the only answer I received. Not being ready with the needful reply, I produced Robinson Crusoe, in which I am firmly persuaded some explanation might have been found, if we had only searched long enough for it. Mr. Franklin shut up Robinson Crusoe, and floundered into his German-English gibberish on the spot. “Why not look into it?” he said, as if I had personally objected to looking into it. “Why the devil lose your patience, Betteredge, when patience is all that’s wanted to arrive at the truth? Don’t interrupt me. Rachel’s conduct is perfectly intelligible, if you will only do her the common justice to take the Objective view first, and the Subjective view next, and the Objective-Subjective view to wind up with. What do we know? We know that the loss of the Moonstone, on Thursday morning last, threw her into a state of nervous excitement, from which she has not recovered yet. Do you mean to deny the Objective view, so far? Very well, then—don’t interrupt me. Now, being in a state of nervous excitement, how are we to expect that she should behave as she might otherwise have behaved to any of the people about her? Arguing in this way, from within-outwards, what do we reach? We reach the Subjective view. I defy you to controvert the Subjective view. Very well then—what follows? Good Heavens! the Objective-Subjective explanation follows, of course! Rachel, properly speaking, is not Rachel, but Somebody Else. Do I mind being cruelly treated by Somebody Else? You are unreasonable enough, Betteredge; but you can hardly accuse me of that. Then how does it end? It ends, in spite of your confounded English narrowness and prejudice, in my being perfectly happy and comfortable. Where’s the sherry?”\n\nMy head was by this time in such a condition, that I was not quite sure whether it was my own head, or Mr. Franklin’s. In this deplorable state, I contrived to do, what I take to have been, three Objective things. I got Mr. Franklin his sherry; I retired to my own room; and I solaced myself with the most composing pipe of tobacco I ever remember to have smoked in my life.\n\nDon’t suppose, however, that I was quit of Mr. Franklin on such easy terms as these. Drifting again, out of the morning-room into the hall, he found his way to the offices next, smelt my pipe, and was instantly reminded that he had been simple enough to give up smoking for Miss Rachel’s sake. In the twinkling of an eye, he burst in on me with his cigar-case, and came out strong on the one everlasting subject, in his neat, witty, unbelieving, French way. “Give me a light, Betteredge. Is it conceivable that a man can have smoked as long as I have without discovering that there is a complete system for the treatment of women at the bottom of his cigar-case? Follow me carefully, and I will prove it in two words. You choose a cigar, you try it, and it disappoints you. What do you do upon that? You throw it away and try another. Now observe the application! You choose a woman, you try her, and she breaks your heart. Fool! take a lesson from your cigar-case. Throw her away, and try another!”\n\nI shook my head at that. Wonderfully clever, I dare say, but my own experience was dead against it. “In the time of the late Mrs. Betteredge,” I said, “I felt pretty often inclined to try your philosophy, Mr. Franklin. But the law insists on your smoking your cigar, sir, when you have once chosen it.” I pointed that observation with a wink. Mr. Franklin burst out laughing—and we were as merry as crickets, until the next new side of his character turned up in due course. So things went on with my young master and me; and so (while the Sergeant and the gardener were wrangling over the roses) we two spent the interval before the news came back from Frizinghall.\n\nThe pony-chaise returned a good half hour before I had ventured to expect it. My lady had decided to remain for the present, at her sister’s house. The groom brought two letters from his mistress; one addressed to Mr. Franklin, and the other to me.\n\nMr. Franklin’s letter I sent to him in the library—into which refuge his driftings had now taken him for the second time. My own letter, I read in my own room. A cheque, which dropped out when I opened it, informed me (before I had mastered the contents) that Sergeant Cuff’s dismissal from the inquiry after the Moonstone was now a settled thing.\n\nI sent to the conservatory to say that I wished to speak to the Sergeant directly. He appeared, with his mind full of the gardener and the dog-rose, declaring that the equal of Mr. Begbie for obstinacy never had existed yet, and never would exist again. I requested him to dismiss such wretched trifling as this from our conversation, and to give his best attention to a really serious matter. Upon that he exerted himself sufficiently to notice the letter in my hand. “Ah!” he said in a weary way, “you have heard from her ladyship. Have I anything to do with it, Mr. Betteredge?”\n\n“You shall judge for yourself, Sergeant.” I thereupon read him the letter (with my best emphasis and discretion), in the following words:\n\n“MY GOOD GABRIEL,—I request that you will inform Sergeant Cuff, that I have performed the promise I made to him; with this result, so far as Rosanna Spearman is concerned. Miss Verinder solemnly declares, that she has never spoken a word in private to Rosanna, since that unhappy woman first entered my house. They never met, even accidentally, on the night when the Diamond was lost; and no communication of any sort whatever took place between them, from the Thursday morning when the alarm was first raised in the house, to this present Saturday afternoon, when Miss Verinder left us. After telling my daughter suddenly, and in so many words, of Rosanna Spearman’s suicide—this is what has come of it.”\n\nHaving reached that point, I looked up, and asked Sergeant Cuff what he thought of the letter, so far?\n\n“I should only offend you if I expressed my opinion,” answered the Sergeant. “Go on, Mr. Betteredge,” he said, with the most exasperating resignation, “go on.”\n\nWhen I remembered that this man had had the audacity to complain of our gardener’s obstinacy, my tongue itched to “go on” in other words than my mistress’s. This time, however, my Christianity held firm. I proceeded steadily with her ladyship’s letter:\n\n“Having appealed to Miss Verinder in the manner which the officer thought most desirable, I spoke to her next in the manner which I myself thought most likely to impress her. On two different occasions, before my daughter left my roof, I privately warned her that she was exposing herself to suspicion of the most unendurable and most degrading kind. I have now told her, in the plainest terms, that my apprehensions have been realised.\n\n“Her answer to this, on her own solemn affirmation, is as plain as words can be. In the first place, she owes no money privately to any living creature. In the second place, the Diamond is not now, and never has been, in her possession, since she put it into her cabinet on Wednesday night.\n\n“The confidence which my daughter has placed in me goes no further than this. She maintains an obstinate silence, when I ask her if she can explain the disappearance of the Diamond. She refuses, with tears, when I appeal to her to speak out for my sake. ‘The day will come when you will know why I am careless about being suspected, and why I am silent even to you. I have done much to make my mother pity me—nothing to make my mother blush for me.’ Those are my daughter’s own words.\n\n“After what has passed between the officer and me, I think—stranger as he is—that he should be made acquainted with what Miss Verinder has said, as well as you. Read my letter to him, and then place in his hands the cheque which I enclose. In resigning all further claim on his services, I have only to say that I am convinced of his honesty and his intelligence; but I am more firmly persuaded than ever, that the circumstances, in this case, have fatally misled him.”\n\nThere the letter ended. Before presenting the cheque, I asked Sergeant Cuff if he had any remark to make.\n\n“It’s no part of my duty, Mr. Betteredge,” he answered, “to make remarks on a case, when I have done with it.”\n\nI tossed the cheque across the table to him. “Do you believe in that part of her ladyship’s letter?” I said, indignantly.\n\nThe Sergeant looked at the cheque, and lifted up his dismal eyebrows in acknowledgment of her ladyship’s liberality.\n\n“This is such a generous estimate of the value of my time,” he said, “that I feel bound to make some return for it. I’ll bear in mind the amount in this cheque, Mr. Betteredge, when the occasion comes round for remembering it.”\n\n“What do you mean?” I asked.\n\n“Her ladyship has smoothed matters over for the present very cleverly,” said the Sergeant. “But this family scandal is of the sort that bursts up again when you least expect it. We shall have more detective-business on our hands, sir, before the Moonstone is many months older.”\n\nIf those words meant anything, and if the manner in which he spoke them meant anything—it came to this. My mistress’s letter had proved, to his mind, that Miss Rachel was hardened enough to resist the strongest appeal that could be addressed to her, and that she had deceived her own mother (good God, under what circumstances!) by a series of abominable lies. How other people, in my place, might have replied to the Sergeant, I don’t know. I answered what he said in these plain terms:\n\n“Sergeant Cuff, I consider your last observation as an insult to my lady and her daughter!”\n\n“Mr. Betteredge, consider it as a warning to yourself, and you will be nearer the mark.”\n\nHot and angry as I was, the infernal confidence with which he gave me that answer closed my lips.\n\nI walked to the window to compose myself. The rain had given over; and, who should I see in the court-yard, but Mr. Begbie, the gardener, waiting outside to continue the dog-rose controversy with Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“My compliments to the Sairgent,” said Mr. Begbie, the moment he set eyes on me. “If he’s minded to walk to the station, I’m agreeable to go with him.”\n\n“What!” cries the Sergeant, behind me, “are you not convinced yet?”\n\n“The de’il a bit I’m convinced!” answered Mr. Begbie.\n\n“Then I’ll walk to the station!” says the Sergeant.\n\n“Then I’ll meet you at the gate!” says Mr. Begbie.\n\nI was angry enough, as you know—but how was any man’s anger to hold out against such an interruption as this? Sergeant Cuff noticed the change in me, and encouraged it by a word in season. “Come! come!” he said, “why not treat my view of the case as her ladyship treats it? Why not say, the circumstances have fatally misled me?”\n\nTo take anything as her ladyship took it was a privilege worth enjoying—even with the disadvantage of its having been offered to me by Sergeant Cuff. I cooled slowly down to my customary level. I regarded any other opinion of Miss Rachel, than my lady’s opinion or mine, with a lofty contempt. The only thing I could not do, was to keep off the subject of the Moonstone! My own good sense ought to have warned me, I know, to let the matter rest—but, there! the virtues which distinguish the present generation were not invented in my time. Sergeant Cuff had hit me on the raw, and, though I did look down upon him with contempt, the tender place still tingled for all that. The end of it was that I perversely led him back to the subject of her ladyship’s letter. “I am quite satisfied myself,” I said. “But never mind that! Go on, as if I was still open to conviction. You think Miss Rachel is not to be believed on her word; and you say we shall hear of the Moonstone again. Back your opinion, Sergeant,” I concluded, in an airy way. “Back your opinion.”\n\nInstead of taking offence, Sergeant Cuff seized my hand, and shook it till my fingers ached again.\n\n“I declare to heaven,” says this strange officer solemnly, “I would take to domestic service tomorrow, Mr. Betteredge, if I had a chance of being employed along with You! To say you are as transparent as a child, sir, is to pay the children a compliment which nine out of ten of them don’t deserve. There! there! we won’t begin to dispute again. You shall have it out of me on easier terms than that. I won’t say a word more about her ladyship, or about Miss Verinder—I’ll only turn prophet, for once in a way, and for your sake. I have warned you already that you haven’t done with the Moonstone yet. Very well. Now I’ll tell you, at parting, of three things which will happen in the future, and which, I believe, will force themselves on your attention, whether you like it or not.”\n\n“Go on!” I said, quite unabashed, and just as airy as ever.\n\n“First,” said the Sergeant, “you will hear something from the Yollands—when the postman delivers Rosanna’s letter at Cobb’s Hole, on Monday next.”\n\nIf he had thrown a bucket of cold water over me, I doubt if I could have felt it much more unpleasantly than I felt those words. Miss Rachel’s assertion of her innocence had left Rosanna’s conduct—the making the new nightgown, the hiding the smeared nightgown, and all the rest of it—entirely without explanation. And this had never occurred to me, till Sergeant Cuff forced it on my mind all in a moment!\n\n“In the second place,” proceeded the Sergeant, “you will hear of the three Indians again. You will hear of them in the neighbourhood, if Miss Rachel remains in the neighbourhood. You will hear of them in London, if Miss Rachel goes to London.”\n\nHaving lost all interest in the three jugglers, and having thoroughly convinced myself of my young lady’s innocence, I took this second prophecy easily enough. “So much for two of the three things that are going to happen,” I said. “Now for the third!”\n\n“Third, and last,” said Sergeant Cuff, “you will, sooner or later, hear something of that money-lender in London, whom I have twice taken the liberty of mentioning already. Give me your pocket-book, and I’ll make a note for you of his name and address—so that there may be no mistake about it if the thing really happens.”\n\nHe wrote accordingly on a blank leaf—“Mr. Septimus Luker, Middlesex-place, Lambeth, London.”\n\n“There,” he said, pointing to the address, “are the last words, on the subject of the Moonstone, which I shall trouble you with for the present. Time will show whether I am right or wrong. In the meanwhile, sir, I carry away with me a sincere personal liking for you, which I think does honour to both of us. If we don’t meet again before my professional retirement takes place, I hope you will come and see me in a little house near London, which I have got my eye on. There will be grass walks, Mr. Betteredge, I promise you, in my garden. And as for the white moss rose——”\n\n“The de’il a bit ye’ll get the white moss rose to grow, unless you bud him on the dogue-rose first,” cried a voice at the window.\n\nWe both turned round. There was the everlasting Mr. Begbie, too eager for the controversy to wait any longer at the gate. The Sergeant wrung my hand, and darted out into the court-yard, hotter still on his side. “Ask him about the moss rose, when he comes back, and see if I have left him a leg to stand on!” cried the great Cuff, hailing me through the window in his turn. “Gentlemen, both!” I answered, moderating them again as I had moderated them once already. “In the matter of the moss rose there is a great deal to be said on both sides!” I might as well (as the Irish say) have whistled jigs to a milestone. Away they went together, fighting the battle of the roses without asking or giving quarter on either side. The last I saw of them, Mr. Begbie was shaking his obstinate head, and Sergeant Cuff had got him by the arm like a prisoner in charge. Ah, well! well! I own I couldn’t help liking the Sergeant—though I hated him all the time.\n\nExplain that state of mind, if you can. You will soon be rid, now, of me and my contradictions. When I have reported Mr. Franklin’s departure, the history of the Saturday’s events will be finished at last. And when I have next described certain strange things that happened in the course of the new week, I shall have done my part of the Story, and shall hand over the pen to the person who is appointed to follow my lead. If you are as tired of reading this narrative as I am of writing it—Lord, how we shall enjoy ourselves on both sides a few pages further on!\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII\n\nI had kept the pony-chaise ready, in case Mr. Franklin persisted in leaving us by the train that night. The appearance of the luggage, followed downstairs by Mr. Franklin himself, informed me plainly enough that he had held firm to a resolution for once in his life.\n\n“So you have really made up your mind, sir?” I said, as we met in the hall. “Why not wait a day or two longer, and give Miss Rachel another chance?”\n\nThe foreign varnish appeared to have all worn off Mr. Franklin, now that the time had come for saying good-bye. Instead of replying to me in words, he put the letter which her ladyship had addressed to him into my hand. The greater part of it said over again what had been said already in the other communication received by me. But there was a bit about Miss Rachel added at the end, which will account for the steadiness of Mr. Franklin’s determination, if it accounts for nothing else.\n\n“You will wonder, I dare say” (her ladyship wrote), “at my allowing my own daughter to keep me perfectly in the dark. A Diamond worth twenty thousand pounds has been lost—and I am left to infer that the mystery of its disappearance is no mystery to Rachel, and that some incomprehensible obligation of silence has been laid on her, by some person or persons utterly unknown to me, with some object in view at which I cannot even guess. Is it conceivable that I should allow myself to be trifled with in this way? It is quite conceivable, in Rachel’s present state. She is in a condition of nervous agitation pitiable to see. I dare not approach the subject of the Moonstone again until time has done something to quiet her. To help this end, I have not hesitated to dismiss the police-officer. The mystery which baffles us, baffles him too. This is not a matter in which any stranger can help us. He adds to what I have to suffer; and he maddens Rachel if she only hears his name.\n\n“My plans for the future are as well settled as they can be. My present idea is to take Rachel to London—partly to relieve her mind by a complete change, partly to try what may be done by consulting the best medical advice. Can I ask you to meet us in town? My dear Franklin, you, in your way, must imitate my patience, and wait, as I do, for a fitter time. The valuable assistance which you rendered to the inquiry after the lost jewel is still an unpardoned offence, in the present dreadful state of Rachel’s mind. Moving blindfold in this matter, you have added to the burden of anxiety which she has had to bear, by innocently threatening her secret with discovery, through your exertions. It is impossible for me to excuse the perversity that holds you responsible for consequences which neither you nor I could imagine or foresee. She is not to be reasoned with—she can only be pitied. I am grieved to have to say it, but for the present, you and Rachel are better apart. The only advice I can offer you is, to give her time.”\n\nI handed the letter back, sincerely sorry for Mr. Franklin, for I knew how fond he was of my young lady; and I saw that her mother’s account of her had cut him to the heart. “You know the proverb, sir,” was all I said to him. “When things are at the worst, they’re sure to mend. Things can’t be much worse, Mr. Franklin, than they are now.”\n\nMr. Franklin folded up his aunt’s letter, without appearing to be much comforted by the remark which I had ventured on addressing to him.\n\n“When I came here from London with that horrible Diamond,” he said, “I don’t believe there was a happier household in England than this. Look at the household now! Scattered, disunited—the very air of the place poisoned with mystery and suspicion! Do you remember that morning at the Shivering Sand, when we talked about my uncle Herncastle, and his birthday gift? The Moonstone has served the Colonel’s vengeance, Betteredge, by means which the Colonel himself never dreamt of!”\n\nWith that he shook me by the hand, and went out to the pony-chaise.\n\nI followed him down the steps. It was very miserable to see him leaving the old place, where he had spent the happiest years of his life, in this way. Penelope (sadly upset by all that had happened in the house) came round crying, to bid him good-bye. Mr. Franklin kissed her. I waved my hand as much as to say, “You’re heartily welcome, sir.” Some of the other female servants appeared, peeping after him round the corner. He was one of those men whom the women all like. At the last moment, I stopped the pony-chaise, and begged as a favour that he would let us hear from him by letter. He didn’t seem to heed what I said—he was looking round from one thing to another, taking a sort of farewell of the old house and grounds. “Tell us where you are going to, sir!” I said, holding on by the chaise, and trying to get at his future plans in that way. Mr. Franklin pulled his hat down suddenly over his eyes. “Going?” says he, echoing the word after me. “I am going to the devil!” The pony started at the word, as if he had felt a Christian horror of it. “God bless you, sir, go where you may!” was all I had time to say, before he was out of sight and hearing. A sweet and pleasant gentleman! With all his faults and follies, a sweet and pleasant gentleman! He left a sad gap behind him, when he left my lady’s house.\n\nIt was dull and dreary enough, when the long summer evening closed in, on that Saturday night.\n\nI kept my spirits from sinking by sticking fast to my pipe and my Robinson Crusoe. The women (excepting Penelope) beguiled the time by talking of Rosanna’s suicide. They were all obstinately of opinion that the poor girl had stolen the Moonstone, and that she had destroyed herself in terror of being found out. My daughter, of course, privately held fast to what she had said all along. Her notion of the motive which was really at the bottom of the suicide failed, oddly enough, just where my young lady’s assertion of her innocence failed also. It left Rosanna’s secret journey to Frizinghall, and Rosanna’s proceedings in the matter of the nightgown entirely unaccounted for. There was no use in pointing this out to Penelope; the objection made about as much impression on her as a shower of rain on a waterproof coat. The truth is, my daughter inherits my superiority to reason—and, in respect to that accomplishment, has got a long way ahead of her own father.\n\nOn the next day (Sunday), the close carriage, which had been kept at Mr. Ablewhite’s, came back to us empty. The coachman brought a message for me, and written instructions for my lady’s own maid and for Penelope.\n\nThe message informed me that my mistress had determined to take Miss Rachel to her house in London, on the Monday. The written instructions informed the two maids of the clothing that was wanted, and directed them to meet their mistresses in town at a given hour. Most of the other servants were to follow. My lady had found Miss Rachel so unwilling to return to the house, after what had happened in it, that she had decided on going to London direct from Frizinghall. I was to remain in the country, until further orders, to look after things indoors and out. The servants left with me were to be put on board wages.\n\nBeing reminded, by all this, of what Mr. Franklin had said about our being a scattered and disunited household, my mind was led naturally to Mr. Franklin himself. The more I thought of him, the more uneasy I felt about his future proceedings. It ended in my writing, by the Sunday’s post, to his father’s valet, Mr. Jeffco (whom I had known in former years) to beg he would let me know what Mr. Franklin had settled to do, on arriving in London.\n\nThe Sunday evening was, if possible, duller even than the Saturday evening. We ended the day of rest, as hundreds of thousands of people end it regularly, once a week, in these islands—that is to say, we all anticipated bedtime, and fell asleep in our chairs.\n\nHow the Monday affected the rest of the household I don’t know. The Monday gave me a good shake up. The first of Sergeant Cuff’s prophecies of what was to happen—namely, that I should hear from the Yollands—came true on that day.\n\nI had seen Penelope and my lady’s maid off in the railway with the luggage for London, and was pottering about the grounds, when I heard my name called. Turning round, I found myself face to face with the fisherman’s daughter, Limping Lucy. Bating her lame foot and her leanness (this last a horrid draw-back to a woman, in my opinion), the girl had some pleasing qualities in the eye of a man. A dark, keen, clever face, and a nice clear voice, and a beautiful brown head of hair counted among her merits. A crutch appeared in the list of her misfortunes. And a temper reckoned high in the sum total of her defects.\n\n“Well, my dear,” I said, “what do you want with me?”\n\n“Where’s the man you call Franklin Blake?” says the girl, fixing me with a fierce look, as she rested herself on her crutch.\n\n“That’s not a respectful way to speak of any gentleman,” I answered. “If you wish to inquire for my lady’s nephew, you will please to mention him as Mr. Franklin Blake.”\n\nShe limped a step nearer to me, and looked as if she could have eaten me alive. “Mr. Franklin Blake?” she repeated after me. “Murderer Franklin Blake would be a fitter name for him.”\n\nMy practice with the late Mrs. Betteredge came in handy here. Whenever a woman tries to put you out of temper, turn the tables, and put her out of temper instead. They are generally prepared for every effort you can make in your own defence, but that. One word does it as well as a hundred; and one word did it with Limping Lucy. I looked her pleasantly in the face; and I said—“Pooh!”\n\nThe girl’s temper flamed out directly. She poised herself on her sound foot, and she took her crutch, and beat it furiously three times on the ground. “He’s a murderer! he’s a murderer! he’s a murderer! He has been the death of Rosanna Spearman!” She screamed that answer out at the top of her voice. One or two of the people at work in the grounds near us looked up—saw it was Limping Lucy—knew what to expect from that quarter—and looked away again.\n\n“He has been the death of Rosanna Spearman?” I repeated. “What makes you say that, Lucy?”\n\n“What do you care? What does any man care? Oh! if she had only thought of the men as I think, she might have been living now!”\n\n“She always thought kindly of me, poor soul,” I said; “and, to the best of my ability, I always tried to act kindly by her.”\n\nI spoke those words in as comforting a manner as I could. The truth is, I hadn’t the heart to irritate the girl by another of my smart replies. I had only noticed her temper at first. I noticed her wretchedness now—and wretchedness is not uncommonly insolent, you will find, in humble life. My answer melted Limping Lucy. She bent her head down, and laid it on the top of her crutch.\n\n“I loved her,” the girl said softly. “She had lived a miserable life, Mr. Betteredge—vile people had ill-treated her and led her wrong—and it hadn’t spoiled her sweet temper. She was an angel. She might have been happy with me. I had a plan for our going to London together like sisters, and living by our needles. That man came here, and spoilt it all. He bewitched her. Don’t tell me he didn’t mean it, and didn’t know it. He ought to have known it. He ought to have taken pity on her. ‘I can’t live without him—and, oh, Lucy, he never even looks at me.’ That’s what she said. Cruel, cruel, cruel. I said, ‘No man is worth fretting for in that way.’ And she said, ‘There are men worth dying for, Lucy, and he is one of them.’ I had saved up a little money. I had settled things with father and mother. I meant to take her away from the mortification she was suffering here. We should have had a little lodging in London, and lived together like sisters. She had a good education, sir, as you know, and she wrote a good hand. She was quick at her needle. I have a good education, and I write a good hand. I am not as quick at my needle as she was—but I could have done. We might have got our living nicely. And, oh! what happens this morning? what happens this morning? Her letter comes and tells me that she has done with the burden of her life. Her letter comes, and bids me good-bye for ever. Where is he?” cries the girl, lifting her head from the crutch, and flaming out again through her tears. “Where’s this gentleman that I mustn’t speak of, except with respect? Ha, Mr. Betteredge, the day is not far off when the poor will rise against the rich. I pray Heaven they may begin with him. I pray Heaven they may begin with him.”\n\nHere was another of your average good Christians, and here was the usual break-down, consequent on that same average Christianity being pushed too far! The parson himself (though I own this is saying a great deal) could hardly have lectured the girl in the state she was in now. All I ventured to do was to keep her to the point—in the hope of something turning up which might be worth hearing.\n\n“What do you want with Mr. Franklin Blake?” I asked.\n\n“I want to see him.”\n\n“For anything particular?”\n\n“I have got a letter to give him.”\n\n“From Rosanna Spearman?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Sent to you in your own letter?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nWas the darkness going to lift? Were all the discoveries that I was dying to make, coming and offering themselves to me of their own accord? I was obliged to wait a moment. Sergeant Cuff had left his infection behind him. Certain signs and tokens, personal to myself, warned me that the detective-fever was beginning to set in again.\n\n“You can’t see Mr. Franklin,” I said.\n\n“I must, and will, see him.”\n\n“He went to London last night.”\n\nLimping Lucy looked me hard in the face, and saw that I was speaking the truth. Without a word more, she turned about again instantly towards Cobb’s Hole.\n\n“Stop!” I said. “I expect news of Mr. Franklin Blake tomorrow. Give me your letter, and I’ll send it on to him by the post.”\n\nLimping Lucy steadied herself on her crutch and looked back at me over her shoulder.\n\n“I am to give it from my hands into his hands,” she said. “And I am to give it to him in no other way.”\n\n“Shall I write, and tell him what you have said?”\n\n“Tell him I hate him. And you will tell him the truth.”\n\n“Yes, yes. But about the letter——?”\n\n“If he wants the letter, he must come back here, and get it from Me.”\n\nWith those words she limped off on the way to Cobb’s Hole. The detective-fever burnt up all my dignity on the spot. I followed her, and tried to make her talk. All in vain. It was my misfortune to be a man—and Limping Lucy enjoyed disappointing me. Later in the day, I tried my luck with her mother. Good Mrs. Yolland could only cry, and recommend a drop of comfort out of the Dutch bottle. I found the fisherman on the beach. He said it was “a bad job,” and went on mending his net. Neither father nor mother knew more than I knew. The one way left to try was the chance, which might come with the morning, of writing to Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nI leave you to imagine how I watched for the postman on Tuesday morning. He brought me two letters. One, from Penelope (which I had hardly patience enough to read), announced that my lady and Miss Rachel were safely established in London. The other, from Mr. Jeffco, informed me that his master’s son had left England already.\n\nOn reaching the metropolis, Mr. Franklin had, it appeared, gone straight to his father’s residence. He arrived at an awkward time. Mr. Blake, the elder, was up to his eyes in the business of the House of Commons, and was amusing himself at home that night with the favourite parliamentary plaything which they call “a private bill.” Mr. Jeffco himself showed Mr. Franklin into his father’s study. “My dear Franklin! why do you surprise me in this way? Anything wrong?” “Yes; something wrong with Rachel; I am dreadfully distressed about it.” “Grieved to hear it. But I can’t listen to you now.” “When can you listen?” “My dear boy! I won’t deceive you. I can listen at the end of the session, not a moment before. Good-night.” “Thank you, sir. Good-night.”\n\nSuch was the conversation, inside the study, as reported to me by Mr. Jeffco. The conversation outside the study, was shorter still. “Jeffco, see what time the tidal train starts tomorrow morning.” “At six-forty, Mr. Franklin.” “Have me called at five.” “Going abroad, sir?” “Going, Jeffco, wherever the railway chooses to take me.” “Shall I tell your father, sir?” “Yes; tell him at the end of the session.”\n\nThe next morning Mr. Franklin had started for foreign parts. To what particular place he was bound, nobody (himself included) could presume to guess. We might hear of him next in Europe, Asia, Africa, or America. The chances were as equally divided as possible, in Mr. Jeffco’s opinion, among the four quarters of the globe.\n\nThis news—by closing up all prospects of my bringing Limping Lucy and Mr. Franklin together—at once stopped any further progress of mine on the way to discovery. Penelope’s belief that her fellow-servant had destroyed herself through unrequited love for Mr. Franklin Blake, was confirmed—and that was all. Whether the letter which Rosanna had left to be given to him after her death did, or did not, contain the confession which Mr. Franklin had suspected her of trying to make to him in her life-time, it was impossible to say. It might be only a farewell word, telling nothing but the secret of her unhappy fancy for a person beyond her reach. Or it might own the whole truth about the strange proceedings in which Sergeant Cuff had detected her, from the time when the Moonstone was lost, to the time when she rushed to her own destruction at the Shivering Sand. A sealed letter it had been placed in Limping Lucy’s hand, and a sealed letter it remained to me and to every one about the girl, her own parents included. We all suspected her of having been in the dead woman’s confidence; we all tried to make her speak; we all failed. Now one, and now another, of the servants—still holding to the belief that Rosanna had stolen the Diamond and had hidden it—peered and poked about the rocks to which she had been traced, and peered and poked in vain. The tide ebbed, and the tide flowed; the summer went on, and the autumn came. And the Quicksand, which hid her body, hid her secret too.\n\nThe news of Mr. Franklin’s departure from England on the Sunday morning, and the news of my lady’s arrival in London with Miss Rachel on the Monday afternoon, had reached me, as you are aware, by the Tuesday’s post. The Wednesday came, and brought nothing. The Thursday produced a second budget of news from Penelope.\n\nMy girl’s letter informed me that some great London doctor had been consulted about her young lady, and had earned a guinea by remarking that she had better be amused. Flower-shows, operas, balls—there was a whole round of gaieties in prospect; and Miss Rachel, to her mother’s astonishment, eagerly took to it all. Mr. Godfrey had called; evidently as sweet as ever on his cousin, in spite of the reception he had met with, when he tried his luck on the occasion of the birthday. To Penelope’s great regret, he had been most graciously received, and had added Miss Rachel’s name to one of his Ladies’ Charities on the spot. My mistress was reported to be out of spirits, and to have held two long interviews with her lawyer. Certain speculations followed, referring to a poor relation of the family—one Miss Clack, whom I have mentioned in my account of the birthday dinner, as sitting next to Mr. Godfrey, and having a pretty taste in champagne. Penelope was astonished to find that Miss Clack had not called yet. She would surely not be long before she fastened herself on my lady as usual—and so forth, and so forth, in the way women have of girding at each other, on and off paper. This would not have been worth mentioning, I admit, but for one reason. I hear you are likely to be turned over to Miss Clack, after parting with me. In that case, just do me the favour of not believing a word she says, if she speaks of your humble servant.\n\nOn Friday, nothing happened—except that one of the dogs showed signs of a breaking out behind the ears. I gave him a dose of syrup of buckthorn, and put him on a diet of pot-liquor and vegetables till further orders. Excuse my mentioning this. It has slipped in somehow. Pass it over please. I am fast coming to the end of my offences against your cultivated modern taste. Besides, the dog was a good creature, and deserved a good physicking; he did indeed.\n\nSaturday, the last day of the week, is also the last day in my narrative.\n\nThe morning’s post brought me a surprise in the shape of a London newspaper. The handwriting on the direction puzzled me. I compared it with the money-lender’s name and address as recorded in my pocket-book, and identified it at once as the writing of Sergeant Cuff.\n\nLooking through the paper eagerly enough, after this discovery, I found an ink-mark drawn round one of the police reports. Here it is, at your service. Read it as I read it, and you will set the right value on the Sergeant’s polite attention in sending me the news of the day:\n\n“LAMBETH—Shortly before the closing of the court, Mr. Septimus Luker, the well-known dealer in ancient gems, carvings, intagli, &c., &c., applied to the sitting magistrate for advice. The applicant stated that he had been annoyed, at intervals throughout the day, by the proceedings of some of those strolling Indians who infest the streets. The persons complained of were three in number. After having been sent away by the police, they had returned again and again, and had attempted to enter the house on pretence of asking for charity. Warned off in the front, they had been discovered again at the back of the premises. Besides the annoyance complained of, Mr. Luker expressed himself as being under some apprehension that robbery might be contemplated. His collection contained many unique gems, both classical and Oriental, of the highest value. He had only the day before been compelled to dismiss a skilled workman in ivory carving from his employment (a native of India, as we understood), on suspicion of attempted theft; and he felt by no means sure that this man and the street jugglers of whom he complained, might not be acting in concert. It might be their object to collect a crowd, and create a disturbance in the street, and, in the confusion thus caused, to obtain access to the house. In reply to the magistrate, Mr. Luker admitted that he had no evidence to produce of any attempt at robbery being in contemplation. He could speak positively to the annoyance and interruption caused by the Indians, but not to anything else. The magistrate remarked that, if the annoyance were repeated, the applicant could summon the Indians to that court, where they might easily be dealt with under the Act. As to the valuables in Mr. Luker’s possession, Mr. Luker himself must take the best measures for their safe custody. He would do well perhaps to communicate with the police, and to adopt such additional precautions as their experience might suggest. The applicant thanked his worship, and withdrew.”\n\nOne of the wise ancients is reported (I forget on what occasion) as having recommended his fellow-creatures to “look to the end.” Looking to the end of these pages of mine, and wondering for some days past how I should manage to write it, I find my plain statement of facts coming to a conclusion, most appropriately, of its own self. We have gone on, in this matter of the Moonstone, from one marvel to another; and here we end with the greatest marvel of all—namely, the accomplishment of Sergeant Cuff’s three predictions in less than a week from the time when he had made them.\n\nAfter hearing from the Yollands on the Monday, I had now heard of the Indians, and heard of the money-lender, in the news from London—Miss Rachel herself remember, being also in London at the time. You see, I put things at their worst, even when they tell dead against my own view. If you desert me, and side with the Sergeant, on the evidence before you—if the only rational explanation you can see is, that Miss Rachel and Mr. Luker must have got together, and that the Moonstone must be now in pledge in the money-lender’s house—I own, I can’t blame you for arriving at that conclusion. In the dark, I have brought you thus far. In the dark I am compelled to leave you, with my best respects.\n\nWhy compelled? it may be asked. Why not take the persons who have gone along with me, so far, up into those regions of superior enlightenment in which I sit myself?\n\nIn answer to this, I can only state that I am acting under orders, and that those orders have been given to me (as I understand) in the interests of truth. I am forbidden to tell more in this narrative than I knew myself at the time. Or, to put it plainer, I am to keep strictly within the limits of my own experience, and am not to inform you of what other persons told me—for the very sufficient reason that you are to have the information from those other persons themselves, at first hand. In this matter of the Moonstone the plan is, not to present reports, but to produce witnesses. I picture to myself a member of the family reading these pages fifty years hence. Lord! what a compliment he will feel it, to be asked to take nothing on hear-say, and to be treated in all respects like a Judge on the bench.\n\nAt this place, then, we part—for the present, at least—after long journeying together, with a companionable feeling, I hope, on both sides. The devil’s dance of the Indian Diamond has threaded its way to London; and to London you must go after it, leaving me at the country house. Please to excuse the faults of this composition—my talking so much of myself, and being too familiar, I am afraid, with you. I mean no harm; and I drink most respectfully (having just done dinner) to your health and prosperity, in a tankard of her ladyship’s ale. May you find in these leaves of my writing, what Robinson Crusoe found in his experience on the desert island—namely, “something to comfort yourselves from, and to set in the Description of Good and Evil, on the Credit Side of the Account.”—Farewell.\n\nThe Events related in several Narratives.\n\n# FIRST NARRATIVE.\n\nContributed by Miss Clack; niece of the late Sir John Verinder\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nI am indebted to my dear parents (both now in heaven) for having had habits of order and regularity instilled into me at a very early age.\n\nIn that happy bygone time, I was taught to keep my hair tidy at all hours of the day and night, and to fold up every article of my clothing carefully, in the same order, on the same chair, in the same place at the foot of the bed, before retiring to rest. An entry of the day’s events in my little diary invariably preceded the folding up. The “Evening Hymn” (repeated in bed) invariably followed the folding up. And the sweet sleep of childhood invariably followed the “Evening Hymn.”\n\nIn later life (alas!) the Hymn has been succeeded by sad and bitter meditations; and the sweet sleep has been but ill exchanged for the broken slumbers which haunt the uneasy pillow of care. On the other hand, I have continued to fold my clothes, and to keep my little diary. The former habit links me to my happy childhood—before papa was ruined. The latter habit—hitherto mainly useful in helping me to discipline the fallen nature which we all inherit from Adam—has unexpectedly proved important to my humble interests in quite another way. It has enabled poor Me to serve the caprice of a wealthy member of the family into which my late uncle married. I am fortunate enough to be useful to Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nI have been cut off from all news of my relatives by marriage for some time past. When we are isolated and poor, we are not infrequently forgotten. I am now living, for economy’s sake, in a little town in Brittany, inhabited by a select circle of serious English friends, and possessed of the inestimable advantages of a Protestant clergyman and a cheap market.\n\nIn this retirement—a Patmos amid the howling ocean of popery that surrounds us—a letter from England has reached me at last. I find my insignificant existence suddenly remembered by Mr. Franklin Blake. My wealthy relative—would that I could add my spiritually-wealthy relative!—writes, without even an attempt at disguising that he wants something of me. The whim has seized him to stir up the deplorable scandal of the Moonstone: and I am to help him by writing the account of what I myself witnessed while visiting at Aunt Verinder’s house in London. Pecuniary remuneration is offered to me—with the want of feeling peculiar to the rich. I am to re-open wounds that Time has barely closed; I am to recall the most intensely painful remembrances—and this done, I am to feel myself compensated by a new laceration, in the shape of Mr. Blake’s cheque. My nature is weak. It cost me a hard struggle, before Christian humility conquered sinful pride, and self-denial accepted the cheque.\n\nWithout my diary, I doubt—pray let me express it in the grossest terms!—if I could have honestly earned my money. With my diary, the poor labourer (who forgives Mr. Blake for insulting her) is worthy of her hire. Nothing escaped me at the time I was visiting dear Aunt Verinder. Everything was entered (thanks to my early training) day by day as it happened; and everything, down to the smallest particular, shall be told here. My sacred regard for truth is (thank God) far above my respect for persons. It will be easy for Mr. Blake to suppress what may not prove to be sufficiently flattering in these pages to the person chiefly concerned in them. He has purchased my time, but not even his wealth can purchase my conscience too.*\n\n[*Note. Added by Franklin Blake.—Miss Clack may make her mind quite easy on this point. Nothing will be added, altered or removed, in her manuscript, or in any of the other manuscripts which pass through my hands. Whatever opinions any of the writers may express, whatever peculiarities of treatment may mark, and perhaps in a literary sense, disfigure the narratives which I am now collecting, not a line will be tampered with anywhere, from first to last. As genuine documents they are sent to me—and as genuine documents I shall preserve them, endorsed by the attestations of witnesses who can speak to the facts. It only remains to be added that “the person chiefly concerned” in Miss Clack’s narrative, is happy enough at the present moment, not only to brave the smartest exercise of Miss Clack’s pen, but even to recognise its unquestionable value as an instrument for the exhibition of Miss Clack’s character.]\n\nMy diary informs me, that I was accidentally passing Aunt Verinder’s house in Montagu Square, on Monday, 3rd July, 1848.\n\nSeeing the shutters opened, and the blinds drawn up, I felt that it would be an act of polite attention to knock, and make inquiries. The person who answered the door, informed me that my aunt and her daughter (I really cannot call her my cousin!) had arrived from the country a week since, and meditated making some stay in London. I sent up a message at once, declining to disturb them, and only begging to know whether I could be of any use.\n\nThe person who answered the door, took my message in insolent silence, and left me standing in the hall. She is the daughter of a heathen old man named Betteredge—long, too long, tolerated in my aunt’s family. I sat down in the hall to wait for my answer—and, having always a few tracts in my bag, I selected one which proved to be quite providentially applicable to the person who answered the door. The hall was dirty, and the chair was hard; but the blessed consciousness of returning good for evil raised me quite above any trifling considerations of that kind. The tract was one of a series addressed to young women on the sinfulness of dress. In style it was devoutly familiar. Its title was, “A Word With You On Your Cap-Ribbons.”\n\n“My lady is much obliged, and begs you will come and lunch tomorrow at two.”\n\nI passed over the manner in which she gave her message, and the dreadful boldness of her look. I thanked this young castaway; and I said, in a tone of Christian interest, “Will you favour me by accepting a tract?”\n\nShe looked at the title. “Is it written by a man or a woman, Miss? If it’s written by a woman, I had rather not read it on that account. If it’s written by a man, I beg to inform him that he knows nothing about it.” She handed me back the tract, and opened the door. We must sow the good seed somehow. I waited till the door was shut on me, and slipped the tract into the letter-box. When I had dropped another tract through the area railings, I felt relieved, in some small degree, of a heavy responsibility towards others.\n\nWe had a meeting that evening of the Select Committee of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes-Conversion-Society. The object of this excellent Charity is—as all serious people know—to rescue unredeemed fathers’ trousers from the pawnbroker, and to prevent their resumption, on the part of the irreclaimable parent, by abridging them immediately to suit the proportions of the innocent son. I was a member, at that time, of the select committee; and I mention the Society here, because my precious and admirable friend, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, was associated with our work of moral and material usefulness. I had expected to see him in the boardroom, on the Monday evening of which I am now writing, and had proposed to tell him, when we met, of dear Aunt Verinder’s arrival in London. To my great disappointment he never appeared. On my expressing a feeling of surprise at his absence, my sisters of the Committee all looked up together from their trousers (we had a great pressure of business that night), and asked in amazement, if I had not heard the news. I acknowledged my ignorance, and was then told, for the first time, of an event which forms, so to speak, the starting-point of this narrative. On the previous Friday, two gentlemen—occupying widely-different positions in society—had been the victims of an outrage which had startled all London. One of the gentlemen was Mr. Septimus Luker, of Lambeth. The other was Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.\n\nLiving in my present isolation, I have no means of introducing the newspaper-account of the outrage into my narrative. I was also deprived, at the time, of the inestimable advantage of hearing the events related by the fervid eloquence of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite. All I can do is to state the facts as they were stated, on that Monday evening, to me; proceeding on the plan which I have been taught from infancy to adopt in folding up my clothes. Everything shall be put neatly, and everything shall be put in its place. These lines are written by a poor weak woman. From a poor weak woman who will be cruel enough to expect more?\n\nThe date—thanks to my dear parents, no dictionary that ever was written can be more particular than I am about dates—was Friday, June 30th, 1848.\n\nEarly on that memorable day, our gifted Mr. Godfrey happened to be cashing a cheque at a banking-house in Lombard Street. The name of the firm is accidentally blotted in my diary, and my sacred regard for truth forbids me to hazard a guess in a matter of this kind. Fortunately, the name of the firm doesn’t matter. What does matter is a circumstance that occurred when Mr. Godfrey had transacted his business. On gaining the door, he encountered a gentleman—a perfect stranger to him—who was accidentally leaving the office exactly at the same time as himself. A momentary contest of politeness ensued between them as to who should be the first to pass through the door of the bank. The stranger insisted on making Mr. Godfrey precede him; Mr. Godfrey said a few civil words; they bowed, and parted in the street.\n\nThoughtless and superficial people may say, Here is surely a very trumpery little incident related in an absurdly circumstantial manner. Oh, my young friends and fellow-sinners! beware of presuming to exercise your poor carnal reason. Oh, be morally tidy. Let your faith be as your stockings, and your stockings as your faith. Both ever spotless, and both ready to put on at a moment’s notice!\n\nI beg a thousand pardons. I have fallen insensibly into my Sunday-school style. Most inappropriate in such a record as this. Let me try to be worldly—let me say that trifles, in this case as in many others, led to terrible results. Merely premising that the polite stranger was Mr. Luker, of Lambeth, we will now follow Mr. Godfrey home to his residence at Kilburn.\n\nHe found waiting for him, in the hall, a poorly clad but delicate and interesting-looking little boy. The boy handed him a letter, merely mentioning that he had been entrusted with it by an old lady whom he did not know, and who had given him no instructions to wait for an answer. Such incidents as these were not uncommon in Mr. Godfrey’s large experience as a promoter of public charities. He let the boy go, and opened the letter.\n\nThe handwriting was entirely unfamiliar to him. It requested his attendance, within an hour’s time, at a house in Northumberland Street, Strand, which he had never had occasion to enter before. The object sought was to obtain from the worthy manager certain details on the subject of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes-Conversion-Society, and the information was wanted by an elderly lady who proposed adding largely to the resources of the charity, if her questions were met by satisfactory replies. She mentioned her name, and she added that the shortness of her stay in London prevented her from giving any longer notice to the eminent philanthropist whom she addressed.\n\nOrdinary people might have hesitated before setting aside their own engagements to suit the convenience of a stranger. The Christian Hero never hesitates where good is to be done. Mr. Godfrey instantly turned back, and proceeded to the house in Northumberland Street. A most respectable though somewhat corpulent man answered the door, and, on hearing Mr. Godfrey’s name, immediately conducted him into an empty apartment at the back, on the drawing-room floor. He noticed two unusual things on entering the room. One of them was a faint odour of musk and camphor. The other was an ancient Oriental manuscript, richly illuminated with Indian figures and devices, that lay open to inspection on a table.\n\nHe was looking at the book, the position of which caused him to stand with his back turned towards the closed folding doors communicating with the front room, when, without the slightest previous noise to warn him, he felt himself suddenly seized round the neck from behind. He had just time to notice that the arm round his neck was naked and of a tawny-brown colour, before his eyes were bandaged, his mouth was gagged, and he was thrown helpless on the floor by (as he judged) two men. A third rifled his pockets, and—if, as a lady, I may venture to use such an expression—searched him, without ceremony, through and through to his skin.\n\nHere I should greatly enjoy saying a few cheering words on the devout confidence which could alone have sustained Mr. Godfrey in an emergency so terrible as this. Perhaps, however, the position and appearance of my admirable friend at the culminating period of the outrage (as above described) are hardly within the proper limits of female discussion. Let me pass over the next few moments, and return to Mr. Godfrey at the time when the odious search of his person had been completed. The outrage had been perpetrated throughout in dead silence. At the end of it some words were exchanged, among the invisible wretches, in a language which he did not understand, but in tones which were plainly expressive (to his cultivated ear) of disappointment and rage. He was suddenly lifted from the ground, placed in a chair, and bound there hand and foot. The next moment he felt the air flowing in from the open door, listened, and concluded that he was alone again in the room.\n\nAn interval elapsed, and he heard a sound below like the rustling sound of a woman’s dress. It advanced up the stairs, and stopped. A female scream rent the atmosphere of guilt. A man’s voice below exclaimed “Hullo!” A man’s feet ascended the stairs. Mr. Godfrey felt Christian fingers unfastening his bandage, and extracting his gag. He looked in amazement at two respectable strangers, and faintly articulated, “What does it mean?” The two respectable strangers looked back, and said, “Exactly the question we were going to ask you.”\n\nThe inevitable explanation followed. No! Let me be scrupulously particular. Sal volatile and water followed, to compose dear Mr. Godfrey’s nerves. The explanation came next.\n\nIt appeared from the statement of the landlord and landlady of the house (persons of good repute in the neighbourhood), that their first and second floor apartments had been engaged, on the previous day, for a week certain, by a most respectable-looking gentleman—the same who has been already described as answering the door to Mr. Godfrey’s knock. The gentleman had paid the week’s rent and all the week’s extras in advance, stating that the apartments were wanted for three Oriental noblemen, friends of his, who were visiting England for the first time. Early on the morning of the outrage, two of the Oriental strangers, accompanied by their respectable English friend, took possession of the apartments. The third was expected to join them shortly; and the luggage (reported as very bulky) was announced to follow when it had passed through the Custom-house, late in the afternoon. Not more than ten minutes previous to Mr. Godfrey’s visit, the third foreigner had arrived. Nothing out of the common had happened, to the knowledge of the landlord and landlady downstairs, until within the last five minutes—when they had seen the three foreigners, accompanied by their respectable English friend, all leave the house together, walking quietly in the direction of the Strand. Remembering that a visitor had called, and not having seen the visitor also leave the house, the landlady had thought it rather strange that the gentleman should be left by himself upstairs. After a short discussion with her husband, she had considered it advisable to ascertain whether anything was wrong. The result had followed, as I have already attempted to describe it; and there the explanation of the landlord and the landlady came to an end.\n\nAn investigation was next made in the room. Dear Mr. Godfrey’s property was found scattered in all directions. When the articles were collected, however, nothing was missing; his watch, chain, purse, keys, pocket-handkerchief, note-book, and all his loose papers had been closely examined, and had then been left unharmed to be resumed by the owner. In the same way, not the smallest morsel of property belonging to the proprietors of the house had been abstracted. The Oriental noblemen had removed their own illuminated manuscript, and had removed nothing else.\n\nWhat did it mean? Taking the worldly point of view, it appeared to mean that Mr. Godfrey had been the victim of some incomprehensible error, committed by certain unknown men. A dark conspiracy was on foot in the midst of us; and our beloved and innocent friend had been entangled in its meshes. When the Christian hero of a hundred charitable victories plunges into a pitfall that has been dug for him by mistake, oh, what a warning it is to the rest of us to be unceasingly on our guard! How soon may our own evil passions prove to be Oriental noblemen who pounce on us unawares!\n\nI could write pages of affectionate warning on this one theme, but (alas!) I am not permitted to improve—I am condemned to narrate. My wealthy relative’s cheque—henceforth, the incubus of my existence—warns me that I have not done with this record of violence yet. We must leave Mr. Godfrey to recover in Northumberland Street, and must follow the proceedings of Mr. Luker at a later period of the day.\n\nAfter leaving the bank, Mr. Luker had visited various parts of London on business errands. Returning to his own residence, he found a letter waiting for him, which was described as having been left a short time previously by a boy. In this case, as in Mr. Godfrey’s case, the handwriting was strange; but the name mentioned was the name of one of Mr. Luker’s customers. His correspondent announced (writing in the third person—apparently by the hand of a deputy) that he had been unexpectedly summoned to London. He had just established himself in lodgings in Alfred Place, Tottenham Court Road; and he desired to see Mr. Luker immediately, on the subject of a purchase which he contemplated making. The gentleman was an enthusiastic collector of Oriental antiquities, and had been for many years a liberal patron of the establishment in Lambeth. Oh, when shall we wean ourselves from the worship of Mammon! Mr. Luker called a cab, and drove off instantly to his liberal patron.\n\nExactly what had happened to Mr. Godfrey in Northumberland Street now happened to Mr. Luker in Alfred Place. Once more the respectable man answered the door, and showed the visitor upstairs into the back drawing-room. There, again, lay the illuminated manuscript on a table. Mr. Luker’s attention was absorbed, as Mr. Godfrey’s attention had been absorbed, by this beautiful work of Indian art. He too was aroused from his studies by a tawny naked arm round his throat, by a bandage over his eyes, and by a gag in his mouth. He too was thrown prostrate and searched to the skin. A longer interval had then elapsed than had passed in the experience of Mr. Godfrey; but it had ended as before, in the persons of the house suspecting something wrong, and going upstairs to see what had happened. Precisely the same explanation which the landlord in Northumberland Street had given to Mr. Godfrey, the landlord in Alfred Place now gave to Mr. Luker. Both had been imposed on in the same way by the plausible address and well-filled purse of the respectable stranger, who introduced himself as acting for his foreign friends. The one point of difference between the two cases occurred when the scattered contents of Mr. Luker’s pockets were being collected from the floor. His watch and purse were safe, but (less fortunate than Mr. Godfrey) one of the loose papers that he carried about him had been taken away. The paper in question acknowledged the receipt of a valuable of great price which Mr. Luker had that day left in the care of his bankers. This document would be useless for purposes of fraud, inasmuch as it provided that the valuable should only be given up on the personal application of the owner. As soon as he recovered himself, Mr. Luker hurried to the bank, on the chance that the thieves who had robbed him might ignorantly present themselves with the receipt. Nothing had been seen of them when he arrived at the establishment, and nothing was seen of them afterwards. Their respectable English friend had (in the opinion of the bankers) looked the receipt over before they attempted to make use of it, and had given them the necessary warning in good time.\n\nInformation of both outrages was communicated to the police, and the needful investigations were pursued, I believe, with great energy. The authorities held that a robbery had been planned, on insufficient information received by the thieves. They had been plainly not sure whether Mr. Luker had, or had not, trusted the transmission of his precious gem to another person; and poor polite Mr. Godfrey had paid the penalty of having been seen accidentally speaking to him. Add to this, that Mr. Godfrey’s absence from our Monday evening meeting had been occasioned by a consultation of the authorities, at which he was requested to assist—and all the explanations required being now given, I may proceed with the simpler story of my own little personal experiences in Montagu Square.\n\nI was punctual to the luncheon hour on Tuesday. Reference to my diary shows this to have been a chequered day—much in it to be devoutly regretted, much in it to be devoutly thankful for.\n\nDear Aunt Verinder received me with her usual grace and kindness. But I noticed, after a little while, that something was wrong. Certain anxious looks escaped my aunt, all of which took the direction of her daughter. I never see Rachel myself without wondering how it can be that so insignificant-looking a person should be the child of such distinguished parents as Sir John and Lady Verinder. On this occasion, however, she not only disappointed—she really shocked me. There was an absence of all lady-like restraint in her language and manner most painful to see. She was possessed by some feverish excitement which made her distressingly loud when she laughed, and sinfully wasteful and capricious in what she ate and drank at lunch. I felt deeply for her poor mother, even before the true state of the case had been confidentially made known to me.\n\nLuncheon over, my aunt said: “Remember what the doctor told you, Rachel, about quieting yourself with a book after taking your meals.”\n\n“I’ll go into the library, mamma,” she answered. “But if Godfrey calls, mind I am told of it. I am dying for more news of him, after his adventure in Northumberland Street.” She kissed her mother on the forehead, and looked my way. “Good-bye, Clack,” she said, carelessly. Her insolence roused no angry feeling in me; I only made a private memorandum to pray for her.\n\nWhen we were left by ourselves, my aunt told me the whole horrible story of the Indian Diamond, which, I am happy to know, it is not necessary to repeat here. She did not conceal from me that she would have preferred keeping silence on the subject. But when her own servants all knew of the loss of the Moonstone, and when some of the circumstances had actually found their way into the newspapers—when strangers were speculating whether there was any connection between what had happened at Lady Verinder’s country house, and what had happened in Northumberland Street and Alfred Place—concealment was not to be thought of; and perfect frankness became a necessity as well as a virtue.\n\nSome persons, hearing what I now heard, would have been probably overwhelmed with astonishment. For my own part, knowing Rachel’s spirit to have been essentially unregenerate from her childhood upwards, I was prepared for whatever my aunt could tell me on the subject of her daughter. It might have gone on from bad to worse till it ended in Murder; and I should still have said to myself, The natural result! oh, dear, dear, the natural result! The one thing that did shock me was the course my aunt had taken under the circumstances. Here surely was a case for a clergyman, if ever there was one yet! Lady Verinder had thought it a case for a physician. All my poor aunt’s early life had been passed in her father’s godless household. The natural result again! Oh, dear, dear, the natural result again!\n\n“The doctors recommend plenty of exercise and amusement for Rachel, and strongly urge me to keep her mind as much as possible from dwelling on the past,” said Lady Verinder.\n\n“Oh, what heathen advice!” I thought to myself. “In this Christian country, what heathen advice!”\n\nMy aunt went on, “I do my best to carry out my instructions. But this strange adventure of Godfrey’s happens at a most unfortunate time. Rachel has been incessantly restless and excited since she first heard of it. She left me no peace till I had written and asked my nephew Ablewhite to come here. She even feels an interest in the other person who was roughly used—Mr. Luker, or some such name—though the man is, of course, a total stranger to her.”\n\n“Your knowledge of the world, dear aunt, is superior to mine,” I suggested diffidently. “But there must be a reason surely for this extraordinary conduct on Rachel’s part. She is keeping a sinful secret from you and from everybody. May there not be something in these recent events which threatens her secret with discovery?”\n\n“Discovery?” repeated my aunt. “What can you possibly mean? Discovery through Mr. Luker? Discovery through my nephew?”\n\nAs the word passed her lips, a special providence occurred. The servant opened the door, and announced Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nMr. Godfrey followed the announcement of his name—as Mr. Godfrey does everything else—exactly at the right time. He was not so close on the servant’s heels as to startle us. He was not so far behind as to cause us the double inconvenience of a pause and an open door. It is in the completeness of his daily life that the true Christian appears. This dear man was very complete.\n\n“Go to Miss Verinder,” said my aunt, addressing the servant, “and tell her Mr. Ablewhite is here.”\n\nWe both inquired after his health. We both asked him together whether he felt like himself again, after his terrible adventure of the past week. With perfect tact, he contrived to answer us at the same moment. Lady Verinder had his reply in words. I had his charming smile.\n\n“What,” he cried, with infinite tenderness, “have I done to deserve all this sympathy? My dear aunt! my dear Miss Clack! I have merely been mistaken for somebody else. I have only been blindfolded; I have only been strangled; I have only been thrown flat on my back, on a very thin carpet, covering a particularly hard floor. Just think how much worse it might have been! I might have been murdered; I might have been robbed. What have I lost? Nothing but Nervous Force—which the law doesn’t recognise as property; so that, strictly speaking, I have lost nothing at all. If I could have had my own way, I would have kept my adventure to myself—I shrink from all this fuss and publicity. But Mr. Luker made his injuries public, and my injuries, as the necessary consequence, have been proclaimed in their turn. I have become the property of the newspapers, until the gentle reader gets sick of the subject. I am very sick indeed of it myself. May the gentle reader soon be like me! And how is dear Rachel? Still enjoying the gaieties of London? So glad to hear it! Miss Clack, I need all your indulgence. I am sadly behind-hand with my Committee Work and my dear Ladies. But I really do hope to look in at the Mothers’-Small-Clothes next week. Did you make cheering progress at Monday’s Committee? Was the Board hopeful about future prospects? And are we nicely off for Trousers?”\n\nThe heavenly gentleness of his smile made his apologies irresistible. The richness of his deep voice added its own indescribable charm to the interesting business question which he had just addressed to me. In truth, we were almost too nicely off for Trousers; we were quite overwhelmed by them. I was just about to say so, when the door opened again, and an element of worldly disturbance entered the room, in the person of Miss Verinder.\n\nShe approached dear Mr. Godfrey at a most unladylike rate of speed, with her hair shockingly untidy, and her face, what I should call, unbecomingly flushed.\n\n“I am charmed to see you, Godfrey,” she said, addressing him, I grieve to add, in the off-hand manner of one young man talking to another. “I wish you had brought Mr. Luker with you. You and he (as long as our present excitement lasts) are the two most interesting men in all London. It’s morbid to say this; it’s unhealthy; it’s all that a well-regulated mind like Miss Clack’s most instinctively shudders at. Never mind that. Tell me the whole of the Northumberland Street story directly. I know the newspapers have left some of it out.”\n\nEven dear Mr. Godfrey partakes of the fallen nature which we all inherit from Adam—it is a very small share of our human legacy, but, alas! he has it. I confess it grieved me to see him take Rachel’s hand in both of his own hands, and lay it softly on the left side of his waistcoat. It was a direct encouragement to her reckless way of talking, and her insolent reference to me.\n\n“Dearest Rachel,” he said, in the same voice which had thrilled me when he spoke of our prospects and our trousers, “the newspapers have told you everything—and they have told it much better than I can.”\n\n“Godfrey thinks we all make too much of the matter,” my aunt remarked. “He has just been saying that he doesn’t care to speak of it.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nShe put the question with a sudden flash in her eyes, and a sudden look up into Mr. Godfrey’s face. On his side, he looked down at her with an indulgence so injudicious and so ill-deserved, that I really felt called on to interfere.\n\n“Rachel, darling!” I remonstrated gently, “true greatness and true courage are ever modest.”\n\n“You are a very good fellow in your way, Godfrey,” she said—not taking the smallest notice, observe, of me, and still speaking to her cousin as if she was one young man addressing another. “But I am quite sure you are not great; I don’t believe you possess any extraordinary courage; and I am firmly persuaded—if you ever had any modesty—that your lady-worshippers relieved you of that virtue a good many years since. You have some private reason for not talking of your adventure in Northumberland Street; and I mean to know it.”\n\n“My reason is the simplest imaginable, and the most easily acknowledged,” he answered, still bearing with her. “I am tired of the subject.”\n\n“You are tired of the subject? My dear Godfrey, I am going to make a remark.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“You live a great deal too much in the society of women. And you have contracted two very bad habits in consequence. You have learnt to talk nonsense seriously, and you have got into a way of telling fibs for the pleasure of telling them. You can’t go straight with your lady-worshippers. I mean to make you go straight with me. Come, and sit down. I am brimful of downright questions; and I expect you to be brimful of downright answers.”\n\nShe actually dragged him across the room to a chair by the window, where the light would fall on his face. I deeply feel being obliged to report such language, and to describe such conduct. But, hemmed in, as I am, between Mr. Franklin Blake’s cheque on one side and my own sacred regard for truth on the other, what am I to do? I looked at my aunt. She sat unmoved; apparently in no way disposed to interfere. I had never noticed this kind of torpor in her before. It was, perhaps, the reaction after the trying time she had had in the country. Not a pleasant symptom to remark, be it what it might, at dear Lady Verinder’s age, and with dear Lady Verinder’s autumnal exuberance of figure.\n\nIn the meantime, Rachel had settled herself at the window with our amiable and forbearing—our too forbearing—Mr. Godfrey. She began the string of questions with which she had threatened him, taking no more notice of her mother, or of myself, than if we had not been in the room.\n\n“Have the police done anything, Godfrey?”\n\n“Nothing whatever.”\n\n“It is certain, I suppose, that the three men who laid the trap for you were the same three men who afterwards laid the trap for Mr. Luker?”\n\n“Humanly speaking, my dear Rachel, there can be no doubt of it.”\n\n“And not a trace of them has been discovered?”\n\n“Not a trace.”\n\n“It is thought—is it not?—that these three men are the three Indians who came to our house in the country.”\n\n“Some people think so.”\n\n“Do you think so?”\n\n“My dear Rachel, they blindfolded me before I could see their faces. I know nothing whatever of the matter. How can I offer an opinion on it?”\n\nEven the angelic gentleness of Mr. Godfrey was, you see, beginning to give way at last under the persecution inflicted on him. Whether unbridled curiosity, or ungovernable dread, dictated Miss Verinder’s questions I do not presume to inquire. I only report that, on Mr. Godfrey’s attempting to rise, after giving her the answer just described, she actually took him by the two shoulders, and pushed him back into his chair—Oh, don’t say this was immodest! don’t even hint that the recklessness of guilty terror could alone account for such conduct as I have described! We must not judge others. My Christian friends, indeed, indeed, indeed, we must not judge others!\n\nShe went on with her questions, unabashed. Earnest Biblical students will perhaps be reminded—as I was reminded—of the blinded children of the devil, who went on with their orgies, unabashed, in the time before the Flood.\n\n“I want to know something about Mr. Luker, Godfrey.”\n\n“I am again unfortunate, Rachel. No man knows less of Mr. Luker than I do.”\n\n“You never saw him before you and he met accidentally at the bank?”\n\n“Never.”\n\n“You have seen him since?”\n\n“Yes. We have been examined together, as well as separately, to assist the police.”\n\n“Mr. Luker was robbed of a receipt which he had got from his banker’s—was he not? What was the receipt for?”\n\n“For a valuable gem which he had placed in the safe keeping of the bank.”\n\n“That’s what the newspapers say. It may be enough for the general reader; but it is not enough for me. The banker’s receipt must have mentioned what the gem was?”\n\n“The banker’s receipt, Rachel—as I have heard it described—mentioned nothing of the kind. A valuable gem, belonging to Mr. Luker; deposited by Mr. Luker; sealed with Mr. Luker’s seal; and only to be given up on Mr. Luker’s personal application. That was the form, and that is all I know about it.”\n\nShe waited a moment, after he had said that. She looked at her mother, and sighed. She looked back again at Mr. Godfrey, and went on.\n\n“Some of our private affairs, at home,” she said, “seem to have got into the newspapers?”\n\n“I grieve to say, it is so.”\n\n“And some idle people, perfect strangers to us, are trying to trace a connexion between what happened at our house in Yorkshire and what has happened since, here in London?”\n\n“The public curiosity, in certain quarters, is, I fear, taking that turn.”\n\n“The people who say that the three unknown men who ill-used you and Mr. Luker are the three Indians, also say that the valuable gem——”\n\nThere she stopped. She had become gradually, within the last few moments, whiter and whiter in the face. The extraordinary blackness of her hair made this paleness, by contrast, so ghastly to look at, that we all thought she would faint, at the moment when she checked herself in the middle of her question. Dear Mr. Godfrey made a second attempt to leave his chair. My aunt entreated her to say no more. I followed my aunt with a modest medicinal peace-offering, in the shape of a bottle of salts. We none of us produced the slightest effect on her. “Godfrey, stay where you are. Mamma, there is not the least reason to be alarmed about me. Clack, you’re dying to hear the end of it—I won’t faint, expressly to oblige you.”\n\nThose were the exact words she used—taken down in my diary the moment I got home. But, oh, don’t let us judge! My Christian friends, don’t let us judge!\n\nShe turned once more to Mr. Godfrey. With an obstinacy dreadful to see, she went back again to the place where she had checked herself, and completed her question in these words:\n\n“I spoke to you, a minute since, about what people were saying in certain quarters. Tell me plainly, Godfrey, do they any of them say that Mr. Luker’s valuable gem is—the Moonstone?”\n\nAs the name of the Indian Diamond passed her lips, I saw a change come over my admirable friend. His complexion deepened. He lost the genial suavity of manner which is one of his greatest charms. A noble indignation inspired his reply.\n\n“They do say it,” he answered. “There are people who don’t hesitate to accuse Mr. Luker of telling a falsehood to serve some private interests of his own. He has over and over again solemnly declared that, until this scandal assailed him, he had never even heard of the Moonstone. And these vile people reply, without a shadow of proof to justify them, He has his reasons for concealment; we decline to believe him on his oath. Shameful! shameful!”\n\nRachel looked at him very strangely—I can’t well describe how—while he was speaking. When he had done, she said, “Considering that Mr. Luker is only a chance acquaintance of yours, you take up his cause, Godfrey, rather warmly.”\n\nMy gifted friend made her one of the most truly evangelical answers I ever heard in my life.\n\n“I hope, Rachel, I take up the cause of all oppressed people rather warmly,” he said.\n\nThe tone in which those words were spoken might have melted a stone. But, oh dear, what is the hardness of stone? Nothing, compared to the hardness of the unregenerate human heart! She sneered. I blush to record it—she sneered at him to his face.\n\n“Keep your noble sentiments for your Ladies’ Committees, Godfrey. I am certain that the scandal which has assailed Mr. Luker, has not spared You.”\n\nEven my aunt’s torpor was roused by those words.\n\n“My dear Rachel,” she remonstrated, “you have really no right to say that!”\n\n“I mean no harm, mamma—I mean good. Have a moment’s patience with me, and you will see.”\n\nShe looked back at Mr. Godfrey, with what appeared to be a sudden pity for him. She went the length—the very unladylike length—of taking him by the hand.\n\n“I am certain,” she said, “that I have found out the true reason of your unwillingness to speak of this matter before my mother and before me. An unlucky accident has associated you in people’s minds with Mr. Luker. You have told me what scandal says of him. What does scandal say of you?”\n\nEven at the eleventh hour, dear Mr. Godfrey—always ready to return good for evil—tried to spare her.\n\n“Don’t ask me!” he said. “It’s better forgotten, Rachel—it is, indeed.”\n\n“I will hear it!” she cried out, fiercely, at the top of her voice.\n\n“Tell her, Godfrey!” entreated my aunt. “Nothing can do her such harm as your silence is doing now!”\n\nMr. Godfrey’s fine eyes filled with tears. He cast one last appealing look at her—and then he spoke the fatal words:\n\n“If you will have it, Rachel—scandal says that the Moonstone is in pledge to Mr. Luker, and that I am the man who has pawned it.”\n\nShe started to her feet with a scream. She looked backwards and forwards from Mr. Godfrey to my aunt, and from my aunt to Mr. Godfrey, in such a frantic manner that I really thought she had gone mad.\n\n“Don’t speak to me! Don’t touch me!” she exclaimed, shrinking back from all of us (I declare like some hunted animal!) into a corner of the room. “This is my fault! I must set it right. I have sacrificed myself—I had a right to do that, if I liked. But to let an innocent man be ruined; to keep a secret which destroys his character for life—Oh, good God, it’s too horrible! I can’t bear it!”\n\nMy aunt half rose from her chair, then suddenly sat down again. She called to me faintly, and pointed to a little phial in her work-box.\n\n“Quick!” she whispered. “Six drops, in water. Don’t let Rachel see.”\n\nUnder other circumstances, I should have thought this strange. There was no time now to think—there was only time to give the medicine. Dear Mr. Godfrey unconsciously assisted me in concealing what I was about from Rachel, by speaking composing words to her at the other end of the room.\n\n“Indeed, indeed, you exaggerate,” I heard him say. “My reputation stands too high to be destroyed by a miserable passing scandal like this. It will be all forgotten in another week. Let us never speak of it again.” She was perfectly inaccessible, even to such generosity as this. She went on from bad to worse.\n\n“I must, and will, stop it,” she said. “Mamma! hear what I say. Miss Clack! hear what I say. I know the hand that took the Moonstone. I know—” she laid a strong emphasis on the words; she stamped her foot in the rage that possessed her—“I know that Godfrey Ablewhite is innocent! Take me to the magistrate, Godfrey! Take me to the magistrate, and I will swear it!”\n\nMy aunt caught me by the hand, and whispered, “Stand between us for a minute or two. Don’t let Rachel see me.” I noticed a bluish tinge in her face which alarmed me. She saw I was startled. “The drops will put me right in a minute or two,” she said, and so closed her eyes, and waited a little.\n\nWhile this was going on, I heard dear Mr. Godfrey still gently remonstrating.\n\n“You must not appear publicly in such a thing as this,” he said. “Your reputation, dearest Rachel, is something too pure and too sacred to be trifled with.”\n\n“My reputation!” She burst out laughing. “Why, I am accused, Godfrey, as well as you. The best detective officer in England declares that I have stolen my own Diamond. Ask him what he thinks—and he will tell you that I have pledged the Moonstone to pay my private debts!” She stopped, ran across the room—and fell on her knees at her mother’s feet. “Oh mamma! mamma! mamma! I must be mad—mustn’t I?—not to own the truth now!” She was too vehement to notice her mother’s condition—she was on her feet again, and back with Mr. Godfrey, in an instant. “I won’t let you—I won’t let any innocent man—be accused and disgraced through my fault. If you won’t take me before the magistrate, draw out a declaration of your innocence on paper, and I will sign it. Do as I tell you, Godfrey, or I’ll write it to the newspapers—I’ll go out, and cry it in the streets!”\n\nWe will not say this was the language of remorse—we will say it was the language of hysterics. Indulgent Mr. Godfrey pacified her by taking a sheet of paper, and drawing out the declaration. She signed it in a feverish hurry. “Show it everywhere—don’t think of me,” she said, as she gave it to him. “I am afraid, Godfrey, I have not done you justice, hitherto, in my thoughts. You are more unselfish—you are a better man than I believed you to be. Come here when you can, and I will try and repair the wrong I have done you.”\n\nShe gave him her hand. Alas, for our fallen nature! Alas, for Mr. Godfrey! He not only forgot himself so far as to kiss her hand—he adopted a gentleness of tone in answering her which, in such a case, was little better than a compromise with sin. “I will come, dearest,” he said, “on condition that we don’t speak of this hateful subject again.” Never had I seen and heard our Christian Hero to less advantage than on this occasion.\n\nBefore another word could be said by anybody, a thundering knock at the street door startled us all. I looked through the window, and saw the World, the Flesh, and the Devil waiting before the house—as typified in a carriage and horses, a powdered footman, and three of the most audaciously dressed women I ever beheld in my life.\n\nRachel started, and composed herself. She crossed the room to her mother.\n\n“They have come to take me to the flower-show,” she said. “One word, mamma, before I go. I have not distressed you, have I?”\n\n(Is the bluntness of moral feeling which could ask such a question as that, after what had just happened, to be pitied or condemned? I like to lean towards mercy. Let us pity it.)\n\nThe drops had produced their effect. My poor aunt’s complexion was like itself again. “No, no, my dear,” she said. “Go with our friends, and enjoy yourself.”\n\nHer daughter stooped, and kissed her. I had left the window, and was near the door, when Rachel approached it to go out. Another change had come over her—she was in tears. I looked with interest at the momentary softening of that obdurate heart. I felt inclined to say a few earnest words. Alas! my well-meant sympathy only gave offence. “What do you mean by pitying me?” she asked in a bitter whisper, as she passed to the door. “Don’t you see how happy I am? I’m going to the flower-show, Clack; and I’ve got the prettiest bonnet in London.” She completed the hollow mockery of that address by blowing me a kiss—and so left the room.\n\nI wish I could describe in words the compassion I felt for this miserable and misguided girl. But I am almost as poorly provided with words as with money. Permit me to say—my heart bled for her.\n\nReturning to my aunt’s chair, I observed dear Mr. Godfrey searching for something softly, here and there, in different parts of the room. Before I could offer to assist him he had found what he wanted. He came back to my aunt and me, with his declaration of innocence in one hand, and with a box of matches in the other.\n\n“Dear aunt, a little conspiracy!” he said. “Dear Miss Clack, a pious fraud which even your high moral rectitude will excuse! Will you leave Rachel to suppose that I accept the generous self-sacrifice which has signed this paper? And will you kindly bear witness that I destroy it in your presence, before I leave the house?” He kindled a match, and, lighting the paper, laid it to burn in a plate on the table. “Any trifling inconvenience that I may suffer is as nothing,” he remarked, “compared with the importance of preserving that pure name from the contaminating contact of the world. There! We have reduced it to a little harmless heap of ashes; and our dear impulsive Rachel will never know what we have done! How do you feel? My precious friends, how do you feel? For my poor part, I am as light-hearted as a boy!”\n\nHe beamed on us with his beautiful smile; he held out a hand to my aunt, and a hand to me. I was too deeply affected by his noble conduct to speak. I closed my eyes; I put his hand, in a kind of spiritual self-forgetfulness, to my lips. He murmured a soft remonstrance. Oh the ecstasy, the pure, unearthly ecstasy of that moment! I sat—I hardly know on what—quite lost in my own exalted feelings. When I opened my eyes again, it was like descending from heaven to earth. There was nobody but my aunt in the room. He had gone.\n\nI should like to stop here—I should like to close my narrative with the record of Mr. Godfrey’s noble conduct. Unhappily there is more, much more, which the unrelenting pecuniary pressure of Mr. Blake’s cheque obliges me to tell. The painful disclosures which were to reveal themselves in my presence, during that Tuesday’s visit to Montagu Square, were not at an end yet.\n\nFinding myself alone with Lady Verinder, I turned naturally to the subject of her health; touching delicately on the strange anxiety which she had shown to conceal her indisposition, and the remedy applied to it, from the observation of her daughter.\n\nMy aunt’s reply greatly surprised me.\n\n“Drusilla,” she said (if I have not already mentioned that my Christian name is Drusilla, permit me to mention it now), “you are touching—quite innocently, I know—on a very distressing subject.”\n\nI rose immediately. Delicacy left me but one alternative—the alternative, after first making my apologies, of taking my leave. Lady Verinder stopped me, and insisted on my sitting down again.\n\n“You have surprised a secret,” she said, “which I had confided to my sister Mrs. Ablewhite, and to my lawyer Mr. Bruff, and to no one else. I can trust in their discretion; and I am sure, when I tell you the circumstances, I can trust in yours. Have you any pressing engagement, Drusilla? or is your time your own this afternoon?”\n\nIt is needless to say that my time was entirely at my aunt’s disposal.\n\n“Keep me company then,” she said, “for another hour. I have something to tell you which I believe you will be sorry to hear. And I shall have a service to ask of you afterwards, if you don’t object to assist me.”\n\nIt is again needless to say that, so far from objecting, I was all eagerness to assist her.\n\n“You can wait here,” she went on, “till Mr. Bruff comes at five. And you can be one of the witnesses, Drusilla, when I sign my Will.”\n\nHer Will! I thought of the drops which I had seen in her work-box. I thought of the bluish tinge which I had noticed in her complexion. A light which was not of this world—a light shining prophetically from an unmade grave—dawned on my mind. My aunt’s secret was a secret no longer.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nConsideration for poor Lady Verinder forbade me even to hint that I had guessed the melancholy truth, before she opened her lips. I waited her pleasure in silence; and, having privately arranged to say a few sustaining words at the first convenient opportunity, felt prepared for any duty that could claim me, no matter how painful it might be.\n\n“I have been seriously ill, Drusilla, for some time past,” my aunt began. “And, strange to say, without knowing it myself.”\n\nI thought of the thousands and thousands of perishing human creatures who were all at that moment spiritually ill, without knowing it themselves. And I greatly feared that my poor aunt might be one of the number. “Yes, dear,” I said, sadly. “Yes.”\n\n“I brought Rachel to London, as you know, for medical advice,” she went on. “I thought it right to consult two doctors.”\n\nTwo doctors! And, oh me (in Rachel’s state), not one clergyman! “Yes, dear?” I said once more. “Yes?”\n\n“One of the two medical men,” proceeded my aunt, “was a stranger to me. The other had been an old friend of my husband’s, and had always felt a sincere interest in me for my husband’s sake. After prescribing for Rachel, he said he wished to speak to me privately in another room. I expected, of course, to receive some special directions for the management of my daughter’s health. To my surprise, he took me gravely by the hand, and said, ‘I have been looking at you, Lady Verinder, with a professional as well as a personal interest. You are, I am afraid, far more urgently in need of medical advice than your daughter.’ He put some questions to me, which I was at first inclined to treat lightly enough, until I observed that my answers distressed him. It ended in his making an appointment to come and see me, accompanied by a medical friend, on the next day, at an hour when Rachel would not be at home. The result of that visit—most kindly and gently conveyed to me—satisfied both the physicians that there had been precious time lost, which could never be regained, and that my case had now passed beyond the reach of their art. For more than two years I have been suffering under an insidious form of heart disease, which, without any symptoms to alarm me, has, by little and little, fatally broken me down. I may live for some months, or I may die before another day has passed over my head—the doctors cannot, and dare not, speak more positively than this. It would be vain to say, my dear, that I have not had some miserable moments since my real situation has been made known to me. But I am more resigned than I was, and I am doing my best to set my worldly affairs in order. My one great anxiety is that Rachel should be kept in ignorance of the truth. If she knew it, she would at once attribute my broken health to anxiety about the Diamond, and would reproach herself bitterly, poor child, for what is in no sense her fault. Both the doctors agree that the mischief began two, if not three years since. I am sure you will keep my secret, Drusilla—for I am sure I see sincere sorrow and sympathy for me in your face.”\n\nSorrow and sympathy! Oh, what Pagan emotions to expect from a Christian Englishwoman anchored firmly on her faith!\n\nLittle did my poor aunt imagine what a gush of devout thankfulness thrilled through me as she approached the close of her melancholy story. Here was a career of usefulness opened before me! Here was a beloved relative and perishing fellow-creature, on the eve of the great change, utterly unprepared; and led, providentially led, to reveal her situation to Me! How can I describe the joy with which I now remembered that the precious clerical friends on whom I could rely, were to be counted, not by ones or twos, but by tens and twenties. I took my aunt in my arms—my overflowing tenderness was not to be satisfied, now, with anything less than an embrace. “Oh!” I said to her, fervently, “the indescribable interest with which you inspire me! Oh! the good I mean to do you, dear, before we part!” After another word or two of earnest prefatory warning, I gave her her choice of three precious friends, all plying the work of mercy from morning to night in her own neighbourhood; all equally inexhaustible in exhortation; all affectionately ready to exercise their gifts at a word from me. Alas! the result was far from encouraging. Poor Lady Verinder looked puzzled and frightened, and met everything I could say to her with the purely worldly objection that she was not strong enough to face strangers. I yielded—for the moment only, of course. My large experience (as Reader and Visitor, under not less, first and last, than fourteen beloved clerical friends) informed me that this was another case for preparation by books. I possessed a little library of works, all suitable to the present emergency, all calculated to arouse, convince, prepare, enlighten, and fortify my aunt. “You will read, dear, won’t you?” I said, in my most winning way. “You will read, if I bring you my own precious books? Turned down at all the right places, aunt. And marked in pencil where you are to stop and ask yourself, ‘Does this apply to me?’” Even that simple appeal—so absolutely heathenising is the influence of the world—appeared to startle my aunt. She said, “I will do what I can, Drusilla, to please you,” with a look of surprise, which was at once instructive and terrible to see. Not a moment was to be lost. The clock on the mantelpiece informed me that I had just time to hurry home; to provide myself with a first series of selected readings (say a dozen only); and to return in time to meet the lawyer, and witness Lady Verinder’s Will. Promising faithfully to be back by five o’clock, I left the house on my errand of mercy.\n\nWhen no interests but my own are involved, I am humbly content to get from place to place by the omnibus. Permit me to give an idea of my devotion to my aunt’s interests by recording that, on this occasion, I committed the prodigality of taking a cab.\n\nI drove home, selected and marked my first series of readings, and drove back to Montagu Square, with a dozen works in a carpet-bag, the like of which, I firmly believe, are not to be found in the literature of any other country in Europe. I paid the cabman exactly his fare. He received it with an oath; upon which I instantly gave him a tract. If I had presented a pistol at his head, this abandoned wretch could hardly have exhibited greater consternation. He jumped up on his box, and, with profane exclamations of dismay, drove off furiously. Quite useless, I am happy to say! I sowed the good seed, in spite of him, by throwing a second tract in at the window of the cab.\n\nThe servant who answered the door—not the person with the cap-ribbons, to my great relief, but the footman—informed me that the doctor had called, and was still shut up with Lady Verinder. Mr. Bruff, the lawyer, had arrived a minute since and was waiting in the library. I was shown into the library to wait too.\n\nMr. Bruff looked surprised to see me. He is the family solicitor, and we had met more than once, on previous occasions, under Lady Verinder’s roof. A man, I grieve to say, grown old and grizzled in the service of the world. A man who, in his hours of business, was the chosen prophet of Law and Mammon; and who, in his hours of leisure, was equally capable of reading a novel and of tearing up a tract.\n\n“Have you come to stay here, Miss Clack?” he asked, with a look at my carpet-bag.\n\nTo reveal the contents of my precious bag to such a person as this would have been simply to invite an outburst of profanity. I lowered myself to his own level, and mentioned my business in the house.\n\n“My aunt has informed me that she is about to sign her Will,” I answered. “She has been so good as to ask me to be one of the witnesses.”\n\n“Aye? aye? Well, Miss Clack, you will do. You are over twenty-one, and you have not the slightest pecuniary interest in Lady Verinder’s Will.”\n\nNot the slightest pecuniary interest in Lady Verinder’s Will. Oh, how thankful I felt when I heard that! If my aunt, possessed of thousands, had remembered poor Me, to whom five pounds is an object—if my name had appeared in the Will, with a little comforting legacy attached to it—my enemies might have doubted the motive which had loaded me with the choicest treasures of my library, and had drawn upon my failing resources for the prodigal expenses of a cab. Not the cruellest scoffer of them all could doubt now. Much better as it was! Oh, surely, surely, much better as it was!\n\nI was aroused from these consoling reflections by the voice of Mr. Bruff. My meditative silence appeared to weigh upon the spirits of this worldling, and to force him, as it were, into talking to me against his own will.\n\n“Well, Miss Clack, what’s the last news in the charitable circles? How is your friend Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, after the mauling he got from the rogues in Northumberland Street? Egad! they’re telling a pretty story about that charitable gentleman at my club!”\n\nI had passed over the manner in which this person had remarked that I was more than twenty-one, and that I had no pecuniary interest in my aunt’s Will. But the tone in which he alluded to dear Mr. Godfrey was too much for my forbearance. Feeling bound, after what had passed in my presence that afternoon, to assert the innocence of my admirable friend, whenever I found it called in question—I own to having also felt bound to include in the accomplishment of this righteous purpose, a stinging castigation in the case of Mr. Bruff.\n\n“I live very much out of the world,” I said; “and I don’t possess the advantage, sir, of belonging to a club. But I happen to know the story to which you allude; and I also know that a viler falsehood than that story never was told.”\n\n“Yes, yes, Miss Clack—you believe in your friend. Natural enough. Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite won’t find the world in general quite so easy to convince as a committee of charitable ladies. Appearances are dead against him. He was in the house when the Diamond was lost. And he was the first person in the house to go to London afterwards. Those are ugly circumstances, ma’am, viewed by the light of later events.”\n\nI ought, I know, to have set him right before he went any farther. I ought to have told him that he was speaking in ignorance of a testimony to Mr. Godfrey’s innocence, offered by the only person who was undeniably competent to speak from a positive knowledge of the subject. Alas! the temptation to lead the lawyer artfully on to his own discomfiture was too much for me. I asked what he meant by “later events”—with an appearance of the utmost innocence.\n\n“By later events, Miss Clack, I mean events in which the Indians are concerned,” proceeded Mr. Bruff, getting more and more superior to poor Me, the longer he went on. “What do the Indians do, the moment they are let out of the prison at Frizinghall? They go straight to London, and fix on Mr. Luker. What follows? Mr. Luker feels alarmed for the safety of ‘a valuable of great price,’ which he has got in the house. He lodges it privately (under a general description) in his bankers’ strongroom. Wonderfully clever of him: but the Indians are just as clever on their side. They have their suspicions that the ‘valuable of great price’ is being shifted from one place to another; and they hit on a singularly bold and complete way of clearing those suspicions up. Whom do they seize and search? Not Mr. Luker only—which would be intelligible enough—but Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite as well. Why? Mr. Ablewhite’s explanation is, that they acted on blind suspicion, after seeing him accidentally speaking to Mr. Luker. Absurd! Half-a-dozen other people spoke to Mr. Luker that morning. Why were they not followed home too, and decoyed into the trap? No! no! The plain inference is, that Mr. Ablewhite had his private interest in the ‘valuable’ as well as Mr. Luker, and that the Indians were so uncertain as to which of the two had the disposal of it, that there was no alternative but to search them both. Public opinion says that, Miss Clack. And public opinion, on this occasion, is not easily refuted.”\n\nHe said those last words, looking so wonderfully wise in his own worldly conceit, that I really (to my shame be it spoken) could not resist leading him a little farther still, before I overwhelmed him with the truth.\n\n“I don’t presume to argue with a clever lawyer like you,” I said. “But is it quite fair, sir, to Mr. Ablewhite to pass over the opinion of the famous London police officer who investigated this case? Not the shadow of a suspicion rested upon anybody but Miss Verinder, in the mind of Sergeant Cuff.”\n\n“Do you mean to tell me, Miss Clack, that you agree with the Sergeant?”\n\n“I judge nobody, sir, and I offer no opinion.”\n\n“And I commit both those enormities, ma’am. I judge the Sergeant to have been utterly wrong; and I offer the opinion that, if he had known Rachel’s character as I know it, he would have suspected everybody in the house but her. I admit that she has her faults—she is secret, and self-willed; odd and wild, and unlike other girls of her age. But true as steel, and high-minded and generous to a fault. If the plainest evidence in the world pointed one way, and if nothing but Rachel’s word of honour pointed the other, I would take her word before the evidence, lawyer as I am! Strong language, Miss Clack; but I mean it.”\n\n“Would you object to illustrate your meaning, Mr. Bruff, so that I may be sure I understand it? Suppose you found Miss Verinder quite unaccountably interested in what has happened to Mr. Ablewhite and Mr. Luker? Suppose she asked the strangest questions about this dreadful scandal, and displayed the most ungovernable agitation when she found out the turn it was taking?”\n\n“Suppose anything you please, Miss Clack, it wouldn’t shake my belief in Rachel Verinder by a hair’s-breadth.”\n\n“She is so absolutely to be relied on as that?”\n\n“So absolutely to be relied on as that.”\n\n“Then permit me to inform you, Mr. Bruff, that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was in this house not two hours since, and that his entire innocence of all concern in the disappearance of the Moonstone was proclaimed by Miss Verinder herself, in the strongest language I ever heard used by a young lady in my life.”\n\nI enjoyed the triumph—the unholy triumph, I fear I must admit—of seeing Mr. Bruff utterly confounded and overthrown by a few plain words from Me. He started to his feet, and stared at me in silence. I kept my seat, undisturbed, and related the whole scene as it had occurred. “And what do you say about Mr. Ablewhite now?” I asked, with the utmost possible gentleness, as soon as I had done.\n\n“If Rachel has testified to his innocence, Miss Clack, I don’t scruple to say that I believe in his innocence as firmly as you do. I have been misled by appearances, like the rest of the world; and I will make the best atonement I can, by publicly contradicting the scandal which has assailed your friend wherever I meet with it. In the meantime, allow me to congratulate you on the masterly manner in which you have opened the full fire of your batteries on me at the moment when I least expected it. You would have done great things in my profession, ma’am, if you had happened to be a man.”\n\nWith those words he turned away from me, and began walking irritably up and down the room.\n\nI could see plainly that the new light I had thrown on the subject had greatly surprised and disturbed him. Certain expressions dropped from his lips, as he became more and more absorbed in his own thoughts, which suggested to my mind the abominable view that he had hitherto taken of the mystery of the lost Moonstone. He had not scrupled to suspect dear Mr. Godfrey of the infamy of stealing the Diamond, and to attribute Rachel’s conduct to a generous resolution to conceal the crime. On Miss Verinder’s own authority—a perfectly unassailable authority, as you are aware, in the estimation of Mr. Bruff—that explanation of the circumstances was now shown to be utterly wrong. The perplexity into which I had plunged this high legal authority was so overwhelming that he was quite unable to conceal it from notice. “What a case!” I heard him say to himself, stopping at the window in his walk, and drumming on the glass with his fingers. “It not only defies explanation, it’s even beyond conjecture.”\n\nThere was nothing in these words which made any reply at all needful, on my part—and yet, I answered them! It seems hardly credible that I should not have been able to let Mr. Bruff alone, even now. It seems almost beyond mere mortal perversity that I should have discovered, in what he had just said, a new opportunity of making myself personally disagreeable to him. But—ah, my friends! nothing is beyond mortal perversity; and anything is credible when our fallen natures get the better of us!\n\n“Pardon me for intruding on your reflections,” I said to the unsuspecting Mr. Bruff. “But surely there is a conjecture to make which has not occurred to us yet.”\n\n“Maybe, Miss Clack. I own I don’t know what it is.”\n\n“Before I was so fortunate, sir, as to convince you of Mr. Ablewhite’s innocence, you mentioned it as one of the reasons for suspecting him, that he was in the house at the time when the Diamond was lost. Permit me to remind you that Mr. Franklin Blake was also in the house at the time when the Diamond was lost.”\n\nThe old worldling left the window, took a chair exactly opposite to mine, and looked at me steadily, with a hard and vicious smile.\n\n“You are not so good a lawyer, Miss Clack,” he remarked in a meditative manner, “as I supposed. You don’t know how to let well alone.”\n\n“I am afraid I fail to follow you, Mr. Bruff,” I said, modestly.\n\n“It won’t do, Miss Clack—it really won’t do a second time. Franklin Blake is a prime favourite of mine, as you are well aware. But that doesn’t matter. I’ll adopt your view, on this occasion, before you have time to turn round on me. You’re quite right, ma’am. I have suspected Mr. Ablewhite, on grounds which abstractedly justify suspecting Mr. Blake too. Very good—let’s suspect them together. It’s quite in his character, we will say, to be capable of stealing the Moonstone. The only question is, whether it was his interest to do so.”\n\n“Mr. Franklin Blake’s debts,” I remarked, “are matters of family notoriety.”\n\n“And Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s debts have not arrived at that stage of development yet. Quite true. But there happen to be two difficulties in the way of your theory, Miss Clack. I manage Franklin Blake’s affairs, and I beg to inform you that the vast majority of his creditors (knowing his father to be a rich man) are quite content to charge interest on their debts, and to wait for their money. There is the first difficulty—which is tough enough. You will find the second tougher still. I have it on the authority of Lady Verinder herself, that her daughter was ready to marry Franklin Blake, before that infernal Indian Diamond disappeared from the house. She had drawn him on and put him off again, with the coquetry of a young girl. But she had confessed to her mother that she loved cousin Franklin, and her mother had trusted cousin Franklin with the secret. So there he was, Miss Clack, with his creditors content to wait, and with the certain prospect before him of marrying an heiress. By all means consider him a scoundrel; but tell me, if you please, why he should steal the Moonstone?”\n\n“The human heart is unsearchable,” I said gently. “Who is to fathom it?”\n\n“In other words, ma’am—though he hadn’t the shadow of a reason for taking the Diamond—he might have taken it, nevertheless, through natural depravity. Very well. Say he did. Why the devil——”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr. Bruff. If I hear the devil referred to in that manner, I must leave the room.”\n\n“I beg your pardon, Miss Clack—I’ll be more careful in my choice of language for the future. All I meant to ask was this. Why—even supposing he did take the Diamond—should Franklin Blake make himself the most prominent person in the house in trying to recover it? You may tell me he cunningly did that to divert suspicion from himself. I answer that he had no need to divert suspicion—because nobody suspected him. He first steals the Moonstone (without the slightest reason) through natural depravity; and he then acts a part, in relation to the loss of the jewel, which there is not the slightest necessity to act, and which leads to his mortally offending the young lady who would otherwise have married him. That is the monstrous proposition which you are driven to assert, if you attempt to associate the disappearance of the Moonstone with Franklin Blake. No, no, Miss Clack! After what has passed here today, between us two, the dead-lock, in this case, is complete. Rachel’s own innocence is (as her mother knows, and as I know) beyond a doubt. Mr. Ablewhite’s innocence is equally certain—or Rachel would never have testified to it. And Franklin Blake’s innocence, as you have just seen, unanswerably asserts itself. On the one hand, we are morally certain of all these things. And, on the other hand, we are equally sure that somebody has brought the Moonstone to London, and that Mr. Luker, or his banker, is in private possession of it at this moment. What is the use of my experience, what is the use of any person’s experience, in such a case as that? It baffles me; it baffles you, it baffles everybody.”\n\nNo—not everybody. It had not baffled Sergeant Cuff. I was about to mention this, with all possible mildness, and with every necessary protest against being supposed to cast a slur upon Rachel—when the servant came in to say that the doctor had gone, and that my aunt was waiting to receive us.\n\nThis stopped the discussion. Mr. Bruff collected his papers, looking a little exhausted by the demands which our conversation had made on him. I took up my bag-full of precious publications, feeling as if I could have gone on talking for hours. We proceeded in silence to Lady Verinder’s room.\n\nPermit me to add here, before my narrative advances to other events, that I have not described what passed between the lawyer and me, without having a definite object in view. I am ordered to include in my contribution to the shocking story of the Moonstone a plain disclosure, not only of the turn which suspicion took, but even of the names of the persons on whom suspicion rested, at the time when the Indian Diamond was believed to be in London. A report of my conversation in the library with Mr. Bruff appeared to me to be exactly what was wanted to answer this purpose—while, at the same time, it possessed the great moral advantage of rendering a sacrifice of sinful self-esteem essentially necessary on my part. I have been obliged to acknowledge that my fallen nature got the better of me. In making that humiliating confession, I get the better of my fallen nature. The moral balance is restored; the spiritual atmosphere feels clear once more. Dear friends, we may go on again.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\nThe signing of the Will was a much shorter matter than I had anticipated. It was hurried over, to my thinking, in indecent haste. Samuel, the footman, was sent for to act as second witness—and the pen was put at once into my aunt’s hand. I felt strongly urged to say a few appropriate words on this solemn occasion. But Mr. Bruff’s manner convinced me that it was wisest to check the impulse while he was in the room. In less than two minutes it was all over—and Samuel (unbenefited by what I might have said) had gone downstairs again.\n\nMr. Bruff folded up the Will, and then looked my way; apparently wondering whether I did or did not mean to leave him alone with my aunt. I had my mission of mercy to fulfil, and my bag of precious publications ready on my lap. He might as well have expected to move St. Paul’s Cathedral by looking at it, as to move Me. There was one merit about him (due no doubt to his worldly training) which I have no wish to deny. He was quick at seeing things. I appeared to produce almost the same impression on him which I had produced on the cabman. He too uttered a profane expression, and withdrew in a violent hurry, and left me mistress of the field.\n\nAs soon as we were alone, my aunt reclined on the sofa, and then alluded, with some appearance of confusion, to the subject of her Will.\n\n“I hope you won’t think yourself neglected, Drusilla,” she said. “I mean to give you your little legacy, my dear, with my own hand.”\n\nHere was a golden opportunity! I seized it on the spot. In other words, I instantly opened my bag, and took out the top publication. It proved to be an early edition—only the twenty-fifth—of the famous anonymous work (believed to be by precious Miss Bellows), entitled The Serpent at Home. The design of the book—with which the worldly reader may not be acquainted—is to show how the Evil One lies in wait for us in all the most apparently innocent actions of our daily lives. The chapters best adapted to female perusal are “Satan in the Hair Brush;” “Satan behind the Looking Glass;” “Satan under the Tea Table;” “Satan out of the Window”—and many others.\n\n“Give your attention, dear aunt, to this precious book—and you will give me all I ask.” With those words, I handed it to her open, at a marked passage—one continuous burst of burning eloquence! Subject: Satan among the Sofa Cushions.\n\nPoor Lady Verinder (reclining thoughtlessly on her own sofa cushions) glanced at the book, and handed it back to me looking more confused than ever.\n\n“I’m afraid, Drusilla,” she said, “I must wait till I am a little better, before I can read that. The doctor——”\n\nThe moment she mentioned the doctor’s name, I knew what was coming. Over and over again in my past experience among my perishing fellow-creatures, the members of the notoriously infidel profession of Medicine had stepped between me and my mission of mercy—on the miserable pretence that the patient wanted quiet, and that the disturbing influence of all others which they most dreaded, was the influence of Miss Clack and her Books. Precisely the same blinded materialism (working treacherously behind my back) now sought to rob me of the only right of property that my poverty could claim—my right of spiritual property in my perishing aunt.\n\n“The doctor tells me,” my poor misguided relative went on, “that I am not so well today. He forbids me to see any strangers; and he orders me, if I read at all, only to read the lightest and the most amusing books. ‘Do nothing, Lady Verinder, to weary your head, or to quicken your pulse’—those were his last words, Drusilla, when he left me today.”\n\nThere was no help for it but to yield again—for the moment only, as before. Any open assertion of the infinitely superior importance of such a ministry as mine, compared with the ministry of the medical man, would only have provoked the doctor to practise on the human weakness of his patient, and to threaten to throw up the case. Happily, there are more ways than one of sowing the good seed, and few persons are better versed in those ways than myself.\n\n“You might feel stronger, dear, in an hour or two,” I said. “Or you might wake, tomorrow morning, with a sense of something wanting, and even this unpretending volume might be able to supply it. You will let me leave the book, aunt? The doctor can hardly object to that!”\n\nI slipped it under the sofa cushions, half in, and half out, close by her handkerchief, and her smelling-bottle. Every time her hand searched for either of these, it would touch the book; and, sooner or later (who knows?) the book might touch her. After making this arrangement, I thought it wise to withdraw. “Let me leave you to repose, dear aunt; I will call again tomorrow.” I looked accidentally towards the window as I said that. It was full of flowers, in boxes and pots. Lady Verinder was extravagantly fond of these perishable treasures, and had a habit of rising every now and then, and going to look at them and smell them. A new idea flashed across my mind. “Oh! may I take a flower?” I said—and got to the window unsuspected, in that way. Instead of taking away a flower, I added one, in the shape of another book from my bag, which I left, to surprise my aunt, among the geraniums and roses. The happy thought followed, “Why not do the same for her, poor dear, in every other room that she enters?” I immediately said good-bye; and, crossing the hall, slipped into the library. Samuel, coming up to let me out, and supposing I had gone, went downstairs again. On the library table I noticed two of the “amusing books” which the infidel doctor had recommended. I instantly covered them from sight with two of my own precious publications. In the breakfast-room I found my aunt’s favourite canary singing in his cage. She was always in the habit of feeding the bird herself. Some groundsel was strewed on a table which stood immediately under the cage. I put a book among the groundsel. In the drawing-room I found more cheering opportunities of emptying my bag. My aunt’s favourite musical pieces were on the piano. I slipped in two more books among the music. I disposed of another in the back drawing-room, under some unfinished embroidery, which I knew to be of Lady Verinder’s working. A third little room opened out of the back drawing-room, from which it was shut off by curtains instead of a door. My aunt’s plain old-fashioned fan was on the chimney-piece. I opened my ninth book at a very special passage, and put the fan in as a marker, to keep the place. The question then came, whether I should go higher still, and try the bedroom floor—at the risk, undoubtedly, of being insulted, if the person with the cap-ribbons happened to be in the upper regions of the house, and to find me out. But oh, what of that? It is a poor Christian that is afraid of being insulted. I went upstairs, prepared to bear anything. All was silent and solitary—it was the servants’ tea-time, I suppose. My aunt’s room was in front. The miniature of my late dear uncle, Sir John, hung on the wall opposite the bed. It seemed to smile at me; it seemed to say, “Drusilla! deposit a book.” There were tables on either side of my aunt’s bed. She was a bad sleeper, and wanted, or thought she wanted, many things at night. I put a book near the matches on one side, and a book under the box of chocolate drops on the other. Whether she wanted a light, or whether she wanted a drop, there was a precious publication to meet her eye, or to meet her hand, and to say with silent eloquence, in either case, “Come, try me! try me!” But one book was now left at the bottom of my bag, and but one apartment was still unexplored—the bath-room, which opened out of the bedroom. I peeped in; and the holy inner voice that never deceives, whispered to me, “You have met her, Drusilla, everywhere else; meet her at the bath, and the work is done.” I observed a dressing-gown thrown across a chair. It had a pocket in it, and in that pocket I put my last book. Can words express my exquisite sense of duty done, when I had slipped out of the house, unsuspected by any of them, and when I found myself in the street with my empty bag under my arm? Oh, my worldly friends, pursuing the phantom, Pleasure, through the guilty mazes of Dissipation, how easy it is to be happy, if you will only be good!\n\nWhen I folded up my things that night—when I reflected on the true riches which I had scattered with such a lavish hand, from top to bottom of the house of my wealthy aunt—I declare I felt as free from all anxiety as if I had been a child again. I was so light-hearted that I sang a verse of the Evening Hymn. I was so light-hearted that I fell asleep before I could sing another. Quite like a child again! quite like a child again!\n\nSo I passed that blissful night. On rising the next morning, how young I felt! I might add, how young I looked, if I were capable of dwelling on the concerns of my own perishable body. But I am not capable—and I add nothing.\n\nTowards luncheon time—not for the sake of the creature-comforts, but for the certainty of finding dear aunt—I put on my bonnet to go to Montagu Square. Just as I was ready, the maid at the lodgings in which I then lived looked in at the door, and said, “Lady Verinder’s servant, to see Miss Clack.”\n\nI occupied the parlour-floor, at that period of my residence in London. The front parlour was my sitting-room. Very small, very low in the ceiling, very poorly furnished—but, oh, so neat! I looked into the passage to see which of Lady Verinder’s servants had asked for me. It was the young footman, Samuel—a civil fresh-coloured person, with a teachable look and a very obliging manner. I had always felt a spiritual interest in Samuel, and a wish to try him with a few serious words. On this occasion, I invited him into my sitting-room.\n\nHe came in, with a large parcel under his arm. When he put the parcel down, it appeared to frighten him. “My lady’s love, Miss; and I was to say that you would find a letter inside.” Having given that message, the fresh-coloured young footman surprised me by looking as if he would have liked to run away.\n\nI detained him to make a few kind inquiries. Could I see my aunt, if I called in Montagu Square? No; she had gone out for a drive. Miss Rachel had gone with her, and Mr. Ablewhite had taken a seat in the carriage, too. Knowing how sadly dear Mr. Godfrey’s charitable work was in arrear, I thought it odd that he should be going out driving, like an idle man. I stopped Samuel at the door, and made a few more kind inquiries. Miss Rachel was going to a ball that night, and Mr. Ablewhite had arranged to come to coffee, and go with her. There was a morning concert advertised for tomorrow, and Samuel was ordered to take places for a large party, including a place for Mr. Ablewhite. “All the tickets may be gone, Miss,” said this innocent youth, “if I don’t run and get them at once!” He ran as he said the words—and I found myself alone again, with some anxious thoughts to occupy me.\n\nWe had a special meeting of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes-Conversion Society that night, summoned expressly with a view to obtaining Mr. Godfrey’s advice and assistance. Instead of sustaining our sisterhood, under an overwhelming flow of Trousers which quite prostrated our little community, he had arranged to take coffee in Montagu Square, and to go to a ball afterwards! The afternoon of the next day had been selected for the Festival of the British-Ladies’-Servants’-Sunday-Sweetheart-Supervision Society. Instead of being present, the life and soul of that struggling Institution, he had engaged to make one of a party of worldlings at a morning concert! I asked myself what did it mean? Alas! it meant that our Christian Hero was to reveal himself to me in a new character, and to become associated in my mind with one of the most awful backslidings of modern times.\n\nTo return, however, to the history of the passing day. On finding myself alone in my room, I naturally turned my attention to the parcel which appeared to have so strangely intimidated the fresh-coloured young footman. Had my aunt sent me my promised legacy? and had it taken the form of cast-off clothes, or worn-out silver spoons, or unfashionable jewellery, or anything of that sort? Prepared to accept all, and to resent nothing, I opened the parcel—and what met my view? The twelve precious publications which I had scattered through the house, on the previous day; all returned to me by the doctor’s orders! Well might the youthful Samuel shrink when he brought his parcel into my room! Well might he run when he had performed his miserable errand! As to my aunt’s letter, it simply amounted, poor soul, to this—that she dare not disobey her medical man.\n\nWhat was to be done now? With my training and my principles, I never had a moment’s doubt.\n\nOnce self-supported by conscience, once embarked on a career of manifest usefulness, the true Christian never yields. Neither public nor private influences produce the slightest effect on us, when we have once got our mission. Taxation may be the consequence of a mission; riots may be the consequence of a mission; wars may be the consequence of a mission: we go on with our work, irrespective of every human consideration which moves the world outside us. We are above reason; we are beyond ridicule; we see with nobody’s eyes, we hear with nobody’s ears, we feel with nobody’s hearts, but our own. Glorious, glorious privilege! And how is it earned? Ah, my friends, you may spare yourselves the useless inquiry! We are the only people who can earn it—for we are the only people who are always right.\n\nIn the case of my misguided aunt, the form which pious perseverance was next to take revealed itself to me plainly enough.\n\nPreparation by clerical friends had failed, owing to Lady Verinder’s own reluctance. Preparation by books had failed, owing to the doctor’s infidel obstinacy. So be it! What was the next thing to try? The next thing to try was—Preparation by Little Notes. In other words, the books themselves having been sent back, select extracts from the books, copied by different hands, and all addressed as letters to my aunt, were, some to be sent by post, and some to be distributed about the house on the plan I had adopted on the previous day. As letters they would excite no suspicion; as letters they would be opened—and, once opened, might be read. Some of them I wrote myself. “Dear aunt, may I ask your attention to a few lines?” &c. “Dear aunt, I was reading last night, and I chanced on the following passage,” &c. Other letters were written for me by my valued fellow-workers, the sisterhood at the Mothers’-Small-Clothes. “Dear madam, pardon the interest taken in you by a true, though humble, friend.” “Dear madam, may a serious person surprise you by saying a few cheering words?” Using these and other similar forms of courteous appeal, we reintroduced all my precious passages under a form which not even the doctor’s watchful materialism could suspect. Before the shades of evening had closed around us, I had a dozen awakening letters for my aunt, instead of a dozen awakening books. Six I made immediate arrangements for sending through the post, and six I kept in my pocket for personal distribution in the house the next day.\n\nSoon after two o’clock I was again on the field of pious conflict, addressing more kind inquiries to Samuel at Lady Verinder’s door.\n\nMy aunt had had a bad night. She was again in the room in which I had witnessed her Will, resting on the sofa, and trying to get a little sleep.\n\nI said I would wait in the library, on the chance of seeing her. In the fervour of my zeal to distribute the letters, it never occurred to me to inquire about Rachel. The house was quiet, and it was past the hour at which the musical performance began. I took it for granted that she and her party of pleasure-seekers (Mr. Godfrey, alas! included) were all at the concert, and eagerly devoted myself to my good work, while time and opportunity were still at my own disposal.\n\nMy aunt’s correspondence of the morning—including the six awakening letters which I had posted overnight—was lying unopened on the library table. She had evidently not felt herself equal to dealing with a large mass of letters—and she might be daunted by the number of them, if she entered the library later in the day. I put one of my second set of six letters on the chimney-piece by itself; leaving it to attract her curiosity, by means of its solitary position, apart from the rest. A second letter I put purposely on the floor in the breakfast-room. The first servant who went in after me would conclude that my aunt had dropped it, and would be specially careful to restore it to her. The field thus sown on the basement story, I ran lightly upstairs to scatter my mercies next over the drawing-room floor.\n\nJust as I entered the front room, I heard a double knock at the street-door—a soft, fluttering, considerate little knock. Before I could think of slipping back to the library (in which I was supposed to be waiting), the active young footman was in the hall, answering the door. It mattered little, as I thought. In my aunt’s state of health, visitors in general were not admitted. To my horror and amazement, the performer of the soft little knock proved to be an exception to general rules. Samuel’s voice below me (after apparently answering some questions which I did not hear) said, unmistakably, “Upstairs, if you please, sir.” The next moment I heard footsteps—a man’s footsteps—approaching the drawing-room floor. Who could this favoured male visitor possibly be? Almost as soon as I asked myself the question, the answer occurred to me. Who could it be but the doctor?\n\nIn the case of any other visitor, I should have allowed myself to be discovered in the drawing-room. There would have been nothing out of the common in my having got tired of the library, and having gone upstairs for a change. But my own self-respect stood in the way of my meeting the person who had insulted me by sending me back my books. I slipped into the little third room, which I have mentioned as communicating with the back drawing-room, and dropped the curtains which closed the open doorway. If I only waited there for a minute or two, the usual result in such cases would take place. That is to say, the doctor would be conducted to his patient’s room.\n\nI waited a minute or two, and more than a minute or two. I heard the visitor walking restlessly backwards and forwards. I also heard him talking to himself. I even thought I recognised the voice. Had I made a mistake? Was it not the doctor, but somebody else? Mr. Bruff, for instance? No! an unerring instinct told me it was not Mr. Bruff. Whoever he was, he was still talking to himself. I parted the heavy curtains the least little morsel in the world, and listened.\n\nThe words I heard were, “I’ll do it today!” And the voice that spoke them was Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nMy hand dropped from the curtain. But don’t suppose—oh, don’t suppose—that the dreadful embarrassment of my situation was the uppermost idea in my mind! So fervent still was the sisterly interest I felt in Mr. Godfrey, that I never stopped to ask myself why he was not at the concert. No! I thought only of the words—the startling words—which had just fallen from his lips. He would do it today. He had said, in a tone of terrible resolution, he would do it today. What, oh what, would he do? Something even more deplorably unworthy of him than what he had done already? Would he apostatise from the faith? Would he abandon us at the Mothers’-Small-Clothes? Had we seen the last of his angelic smile in the committee-room? Had we heard the last of his unrivalled eloquence at Exeter Hall? I was so wrought up by the bare idea of such awful eventualities as these in connection with such a man, that I believe I should have rushed from my place of concealment, and implored him in the name of all the Ladies’ Committees in London to explain himself—when I suddenly heard another voice in the room. It penetrated through the curtains; it was loud, it was bold, it was wanting in every female charm. The voice of Rachel Verinder.\n\n“Why have you come up here, Godfrey?” she asked. “Why didn’t you go into the library?”\n\nHe laughed softly, and answered, “Miss Clack is in the library.”\n\n“Clack in the library!” She instantly seated herself on the ottoman in the back drawing-room. “You are quite right, Godfrey. We had much better stop here.”\n\nI had been in a burning fever, a moment since, and in some doubt what to do next. I became extremely cold now, and felt no doubt whatever. To show myself, after what I had heard, was impossible. To retreat—except into the fireplace—was equally out of the question. A martyrdom was before me. In justice to myself, I noiselessly arranged the curtains so that I could both see and hear. And then I met my martyrdom, with the spirit of a primitive Christian.\n\n“Don’t sit on the ottoman,” the young lady proceeded. “Bring a chair, Godfrey. I like people to be opposite to me when I talk to them.”\n\nHe took the nearest seat. It was a low chair. He was very tall, and many sizes too large for it. I never saw his legs to such disadvantage before.\n\n“Well?” she went on. “What did you say to them?”\n\n“Just what you said, dear Rachel, to me.”\n\n“That mamma was not at all well today? And that I didn’t quite like leaving her to go to the concert?”\n\n“Those were the words. They were grieved to lose you at the concert, but they quite understood. All sent their love; and all expressed a cheering belief that Lady Verinder’s indisposition would soon pass away.”\n\n“You don’t think it’s serious, do you, Godfrey?”\n\n“Far from it! In a few days, I feel quite sure, all will be well again.”\n\n“I think so, too. I was a little frightened at first, but I think so too. It was very kind to go and make my excuses for me to people who are almost strangers to you. But why not have gone with them to the concert? It seems very hard that you should miss the music too.”\n\n“Don’t say that, Rachel! If you only knew how much happier I am—here, with you!”\n\nHe clasped his hands, and looked at her. In the position which he occupied, when he did that, he turned my way. Can words describe how I sickened when I noticed exactly the same pathetic expression on his face, which had charmed me when he was pleading for destitute millions of his fellow-creatures on the platform at Exeter Hall!\n\n“It’s hard to get over one’s bad habits, Godfrey. But do try to get over the habit of paying compliments—do, to please me.”\n\n“I never paid you a compliment, Rachel, in my life. Successful love may sometimes use the language of flattery, I admit. But hopeless love, dearest, always speaks the truth.”\n\nHe drew his chair close, and took her hand, when he said “hopeless love.” There was a momentary silence. He, who thrilled everybody, had doubtless thrilled her. I thought I now understood the words which had dropped from him when he was alone in the drawing-room, “I’ll do it today.” Alas! the most rigid propriety could hardly have failed to discover that he was doing it now.\n\n“Have you forgotten what we agreed on, Godfrey, when you spoke to me in the country? We agreed that we were to be cousins, and nothing more.”\n\n“I break the agreement, Rachel, every time I see you.”\n\n“Then don’t see me.”\n\n“Quite useless! I break the agreement every time I think of you. Oh, Rachel! how kindly you told me, only the other day, that my place in your estimation was a higher place than it had ever been yet! Am I mad to build the hopes I do on those dear words? Am I mad to dream of some future day when your heart may soften to me? Don’t tell me so, if I am! Leave me my delusion, dearest! I must have that to cherish, and to comfort me, if I have nothing else!”\n\nHis voice trembled, and he put his white handkerchief to his eyes. Exeter Hall again! Nothing wanting to complete the parallel but the audience, the cheers, and the glass of water.\n\nEven her obdurate nature was touched. I saw her lean a little nearer to him. I heard a new tone of interest in her next words.\n\n“Are you really sure, Godfrey, that you are so fond of me as that?”\n\n“Sure! You know what I was, Rachel. Let me tell you what I am. I have lost every interest in life, but my interest in you. A transformation has come over me which I can’t account for, myself. Would you believe it? My charitable business is an unendurable nuisance to me; and when I see a Ladies’ Committee now, I wish myself at the uttermost ends of the earth!”\n\nIf the annals of apostasy offer anything comparable to such a declaration as that, I can only say that the case in point is not producible from the stores of my reading. I thought of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes. I thought of the Sunday-Sweetheart-Supervision. I thought of the other Societies, too numerous to mention, all built up on this man as on a tower of strength. I thought of the struggling Female Boards, who, so to speak, drew the breath of their business-life through the nostrils of Mr. Godfrey—of that same Mr. Godfrey who had just reviled our good work as a “nuisance”—and just declared that he wished he was at the uttermost ends of the earth when he found himself in our company! My young female friends will feel encouraged to persevere, when I mention that it tried even my discipline before I could devour my own righteous indignation in silence. At the same time, it is only justice to myself to add, that I didn’t lose a syllable of the conversation. Rachel was the next to speak.\n\n“You have made your confession,” she said. “I wonder whether it would cure you of your unhappy attachment to me, if I made mine?”\n\nHe started. I confess I started too. He thought, and I thought, that she was about to divulge the mystery of the Moonstone.\n\n“Would you think, to look at me,” she went on, “that I am the wretchedest girl living? It’s true, Godfrey. What greater wretchedness can there be than to live degraded in your own estimation? That is my life now.”\n\n“My dear Rachel! it’s impossible you can have any reason to speak of yourself in that way!”\n\n“How do you know I have no reason?”\n\n“Can you ask me the question! I know it, because I know you. Your silence, dearest, has never lowered you in the estimation of your true friends. The disappearance of your precious birthday gift may seem strange; your unexplained connection with that event may seem stranger still——”\n\n“Are you speaking of the Moonstone, Godfrey?”\n\n“I certainly thought that you referred——”\n\n“I referred to nothing of the sort. I can hear of the loss of the Moonstone, let who will speak of it, without feeling degraded in my own estimation. If the story of the Diamond ever comes to light, it will be known that I accepted a dreadful responsibility; it will be known that I involved myself in the keeping of a miserable secret—but it will be as clear as the sun at noon-day that I did nothing mean! You have misunderstood me, Godfrey. It’s my fault for not speaking more plainly. Cost me what it may, I will be plainer now. Suppose you were not in love with me? Suppose you were in love with some other woman?”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“Suppose you discovered that woman to be utterly unworthy of you? Suppose you were quite convinced that it was a disgrace to you to waste another thought on her? Suppose the bare idea of ever marrying such a person made your face burn, only with thinking of it?”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“And, suppose, in spite of all that—you couldn’t tear her from your heart? Suppose the feeling she had roused in you (in the time when you believed in her) was not a feeling to be hidden? Suppose the love this wretch had inspired in you——? Oh, how can I find words to say it in! How can I make a man understand that a feeling which horrifies me at myself, can be a feeling that fascinates me at the same time? It’s the breath of my life, Godfrey, and it’s the poison that kills me—both in one! Go away! I must be out of my mind to talk as I am talking now. No! you mustn’t leave me—you mustn’t carry away a wrong impression. I must say what is to be said in my own defence. Mind this! He doesn’t know—he never will know, what I have told you. I will never see him—I don’t care what happens—I will never, never, never see him again! Don’t ask me his name! Don’t ask me any more! Let’s change the subject. Are you doctor enough, Godfrey, to tell me why I feel as if I was stifling for want of breath? Is there a form of hysterics that bursts into words instead of tears? I dare say! What does it matter? You will get over any trouble I have caused you, easily enough now. I have dropped to my right place in your estimation, haven’t I? Don’t notice me! Don’t pity me! For God’s sake, go away!”\n\nShe turned round on a sudden, and beat her hands wildly on the back of the ottoman. Her head dropped on the cushions; and she burst out crying. Before I had time to feel shocked at this, I was horror-struck by an entirely unexpected proceeding on the part of Mr. Godfrey. Will it be credited that he fell on his knees at her feet?—on both knees, I solemnly declare! May modesty mention that he put his arms round her next? And may reluctant admiration acknowledge that he electrified her with two words?\n\n“Noble creature!”\n\nNo more than that! But he did it with one of the bursts which have made his fame as a public speaker. She sat, either quite thunderstruck, or quite fascinated—I don’t know which—without even making an effort to put his arms back where his arms ought to have been. As for me, my sense of propriety was completely bewildered. I was so painfully uncertain whether it was my first duty to close my eyes, or to stop my ears, that I did neither. I attribute my being still able to hold the curtain in the right position for looking and listening, entirely to suppressed hysterics. In suppressed hysterics, it is admitted, even by the doctors, that one must hold something.\n\n“Yes,” he said, with all the fascination of his evangelical voice and manner, “you are a noble creature! A woman who can speak the truth, for the truth’s own sake—a woman who will sacrifice her pride, rather than sacrifice an honest man who loves her—is the most priceless of all treasures. When such a woman marries, if her husband only wins her esteem and regard, he wins enough to ennoble his whole life. You have spoken, dearest, of your place in my estimation. Judge what that place is—when I implore you on my knees, to let the cure of your poor wounded heart be my care. Rachel! will you honour me, will you bless me, by being my wife?”\n\nBy this time I should certainly have decided on stopping my ears, if Rachel had not encouraged me to keep them open, by answering him in the first sensible words I had ever heard fall from her lips.\n\n“Godfrey!” she said, “you must be mad!”\n\n“I never spoke more reasonably, dearest—in your interests, as well as in mine. Look for a moment to the future. Is your happiness to be sacrificed to a man who has never known how you feel towards him, and whom you are resolved never to see again? Is it not your duty to yourself to forget this ill-fated attachment? and is forgetfulness to be found in the life you are leading now? You have tried that life, and you are wearying of it already. Surround yourself with nobler interests than the wretched interests of the world. A heart that loves and honours you; a home whose peaceful claims and happy duties win gently on you day by day—try the consolation, Rachel, which is to be found there! I don’t ask for your love—I will be content with your affection and regard. Let the rest be left, confidently left, to your husband’s devotion, and to Time that heals even wounds as deep as yours.”\n\nShe began to yield already. Oh, what a bringing-up she must have had! Oh, how differently I should have acted in her place!\n\n“Don’t tempt me, Godfrey,” she said; “I am wretched enough and reckless enough as it is. Don’t tempt me to be more wretched and more wreckless still!”\n\n“One question, Rachel. Have you any personal objection to me?”\n\n“I! I always liked you. After what you have just said to me, I should be insensible indeed if I didn’t respect and admire you as well.”\n\n“Do you know many wives, my dear Rachel, who respect and admire their husbands? And yet they and their husbands get on very well. How many brides go to the altar with hearts that would bear inspection by the men who take them there? And yet it doesn’t end unhappily—somehow or other the nuptial establishment jogs on. The truth is, that women try marriage as a Refuge, far more numerously than they are willing to admit; and, what is more, they find that marriage has justified their confidence in it. Look at your own case once again. At your age, and with your attractions, is it possible for you to sentence yourself to a single life? Trust my knowledge of the world—nothing is less possible. It is merely a question of time. You may marry some other man, some years hence. Or you may marry the man, dearest, who is now at your feet, and who prizes your respect and admiration above the love of any other woman on the face of the earth.”\n\n“Gently, Godfrey! you are putting something into my head which I never thought of before. You are tempting me with a new prospect, when all my other prospects are closed before me. I tell you again, I am miserable enough and desperate enough, if you say another word, to marry you on your own terms. Take the warning, and go!”\n\n“I won’t even rise from my knees, till you have said yes!”\n\n“If I say yes you will repent, and I shall repent, when it is too late!”\n\n“We shall both bless the day, darling, when I pressed, and when you yielded.”\n\n“Do you feel as confidently as you speak?”\n\n“You shall judge for yourself. I speak from what I have seen in my own family. Tell me what you think of our household at Frizinghall. Do my father and mother live unhappily together?”\n\n“Far from it—so far as I can see.”\n\n“When my mother was a girl, Rachel (it is no secret in the family), she had loved as you love—she had given her heart to a man who was unworthy of her. She married my father, respecting him, admiring him, but nothing more. Your own eyes have seen the result. Is there no encouragement in it for you and for me?”*\n\n* See Betteredge’s Narrative, chapter viii.\n\n“You won’t hurry me, Godfrey?”\n\n“My time shall be yours.”\n\n“You won’t ask me for more than I can give?”\n\n“My angel! I only ask you to give me yourself.”\n\n“Take me!”\n\nIn those two words, she accepted him!\n\nHe had another burst—a burst of unholy rapture this time. He drew her nearer and nearer to him till her face touched his; and then—No! I really cannot prevail upon myself to carry this shocking disclosure any farther. Let me only say, that I tried to close my eyes before it happened, and that I was just one moment too late. I had calculated, you see, on her resisting. She submitted. To every right-feeling person of my own sex, volumes could say no more.\n\nEven my innocence in such matters began to see its way to the end of the interview now. They understood each other so thoroughly by this time, that I fully expected to see them walk off together, arm in arm, to be married. There appeared, however, judging by Mr. Godfrey’s next words, to be one more trifling formality which it was necessary to observe. He seated himself—unforbidden this time—on the ottoman by her side. “Shall I speak to your dear mother?” he asked. “Or will you?”\n\nShe declined both alternatives.\n\n“Let my mother hear nothing from either of us, until she is better. I wish it to be kept a secret for the present, Godfrey. Go now, and come back this evening. We have been here alone together quite long enough.”\n\nShe rose, and, in rising, looked for the first time towards the little room in which my martyrdom was going on.\n\n“Who has drawn those curtains?” she exclaimed. “The room is close enough, as it is, without keeping the air out of it in that way.”\n\nShe advanced to the curtains. At the moment when she laid her hand on them—at the moment when the discovery of me appeared to be quite inevitable—the voice of the fresh-coloured young footman, on the stairs, suddenly suspended any further proceedings on her side or on mine. It was unmistakably the voice of a man in great alarm.\n\n“Miss Rachel!” he called out, “where are you, Miss Rachel?”\n\nShe sprang back from the curtains, and ran to the door.\n\nThe footman came just inside the room. His ruddy colour was all gone. He said, “Please to come downstairs, Miss! My lady has fainted, and we can’t bring her to again.”\n\nIn a moment more I was alone, and free to go downstairs in my turn, quite unobserved.\n\nMr. Godfrey passed me in the hall, hurrying out, to fetch the doctor. “Go in, and help them!” he said, pointing to the room. I found Rachel on her knees by the sofa, with her mother’s head on her bosom. One look at my aunt’s face (knowing what I knew) was enough to warn me of the dreadful truth. I kept my thoughts to myself till the doctor came in. It was not long before he arrived. He began by sending Rachel out of the room—and then he told the rest of us that Lady Verinder was no more. Serious persons, in search of proofs of hardened scepticism, may be interested in hearing that he showed no signs of remorse when he looked at Me.\n\nAt a later hour I peeped into the breakfast-room, and the library. My aunt had died without opening one of the letters which I had addressed to her. I was so shocked at this, that it never occurred to me, until some days afterwards, that she had also died without giving me my little legacy.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n(1.) “Miss Clack presents her compliments to Mr. Franklin Blake; and, in sending him the fifth chapter of her humble narrative, begs to say that she feels quite unequal to enlarge as she could wish on an event so awful, under the circumstances, as Lady Verinder’s death. She has, therefore, attached to her own manuscripts, copious Extracts from precious publications in her possession, all bearing on this terrible subject. And may those Extracts (Miss Clack fervently hopes) sound as the blast of a trumpet in the ears of her respected kinsman, Mr. Franklin Blake.”\n\n(2.) “Mr. Franklin Blake presents his compliments to Miss Clack, and begs to thank her for the fifth chapter of her narrative. In returning the extracts sent with it, he will refrain from mentioning any personal objection which he may entertain to this species of literature, and will merely say that the proposed additions to the manuscript are not necessary to the fulfilment of the purpose that he has in view.”\n\n(3.) “Miss Clack begs to acknowledge the return of her Extracts. She affectionately reminds Mr. Franklin Blake that she is a Christian, and that it is, therefore, quite impossible for him to offend her. Miss C. persists in feeling the deepest interest in Mr. Blake, and pledges herself, on the first occasion when sickness may lay him low, to offer him the use of her Extracts for the second time. In the meanwhile she would be glad to know, before beginning the final chapters of her narrative, whether she may be permitted to make her humble contribution complete, by availing herself of the light which later discoveries have thrown on the mystery of the Moonstone.”\n\n(4.) “Mr. Franklin Blake is sorry to disappoint Miss Clack. He can only repeat the instructions which he had the honour of giving her when she began her narrative. She is requested to limit herself to her own individual experience of persons and events, as recorded in her diary. Later discoveries she will be good enough to leave to the pens of those persons who can write in the capacity of actual witnesses.”\n\n(5.) “Miss Clack is extremely sorry to trouble Mr. Franklin Blake with another letter. Her Extracts have been returned, and the expression of her matured views on the subject of the Moonstone has been forbidden. Miss Clack is painfully conscious that she ought (in the worldly phrase) to feel herself put down. But, no—Miss C. has learnt Perseverance in the School of Adversity. Her object in writing is to know whether Mr. Blake (who prohibits everything else) prohibits the appearance of the present correspondence in Miss Clack’s narrative? Some explanation of the position in which Mr. Blake’s interference has placed her as an authoress, seems due on the ground of common justice. And Miss Clack, on her side, is most anxious that her letters should be produced to speak for themselves.”\n\n(6.) “Mr. Franklin Blake agrees to Miss Clack’s proposal, on the understanding that she will kindly consider this intimation of his consent as closing the correspondence between them.”\n\n(7.) “Miss Clack feels it an act of Christian duty (before the correspondence closes) to inform Mr. Franklin Blake that his last letter—evidently intended to offend her—has not succeeded in accomplishing the object of the writer. She affectionately requests Mr. Blake to retire to the privacy of his own room, and to consider with himself whether the training which can thus elevate a poor weak woman above the reach of insult, be not worthy of greater admiration than he is now disposed to feel for it. On being favoured with an intimation to that effect, Miss C. solemnly pledges herself to send back the complete series of her Extracts to Mr. Franklin Blake.”\n\n[To this letter no answer was received. Comment is needless.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nThe foregoing correspondence will sufficiently explain why no choice is left to me but to pass over Lady Verinder’s death with the simple announcement of the fact which ends my fifth chapter.\n\nKeeping myself for the future strictly within the limits of my own personal experience, I have next to relate that a month elapsed from the time of my aunt’s decease before Rachel Verinder and I met again. That meeting was the occasion of my spending a few days under the same roof with her. In the course of my visit, something happened, relative to her marriage-engagement with Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, which is important enough to require special notice in these pages. When this last of many painful family circumstances has been disclosed, my task will be completed; for I shall then have told all that I know, as an actual (and most unwilling) witness of events.\n\nMy aunt’s remains were removed from London, and were buried in the little cemetery attached to the church in her own park. I was invited to the funeral with the rest of the family. But it was impossible (with my religious views) to rouse myself in a few days only from the shock which this death had caused me. I was informed, moreover, that the rector of Frizinghall was to read the service. Having myself in past times seen this clerical castaway making one of the players at Lady Verinder’s whist-table, I doubt, even if I had been fit to travel, whether I should have felt justified in attending the ceremony.\n\nLady Verinder’s death left her daughter under the care of her brother-in-law, Mr. Ablewhite the elder. He was appointed guardian by the will, until his niece married, or came of age. Under these circumstances, Mr. Godfrey informed his father, I suppose, of the new relation in which he stood towards Rachel. At any rate, in ten days from my aunt’s death, the secret of the marriage-engagement was no secret at all within the circle of the family, and the grand question for Mr. Ablewhite senior—another confirmed castaway!—was how to make himself and his authority most agreeable to the wealthy young lady who was going to marry his son.\n\nRachel gave him some trouble at the outset, about the choice of a place in which she could be prevailed upon to reside. The house in Montagu Square was associated with the calamity of her mother’s death. The house in Yorkshire was associated with the scandalous affair of the lost Moonstone. Her guardian’s own residence at Frizinghall was open to neither of these objections. But Rachel’s presence in it, after her recent bereavement, operated as a check on the gaieties of her cousins, the Miss Ablewhites—and she herself requested that her visit might be deferred to a more favourable opportunity. It ended in a proposal, emanating from old Mr. Ablewhite, to try a furnished house at Brighton. His wife, an invalid daughter, and Rachel were to inhabit it together, and were to expect him to join them later in the season. They would see no society but a few old friends, and they would have his son Godfrey, travelling backwards and forwards by the London train, always at their disposal.\n\nI describe this aimless flitting about from one place of residence to another—this insatiate restlessness of body and appalling stagnation of soul—merely with the view to arriving at results. The event which (under Providence) proved to be the means of bringing Rachel Verinder and myself together again, was no other than the hiring of the house at Brighton.\n\nMy Aunt Ablewhite is a large, silent, fair-complexioned woman, with one noteworthy point in her character. From the hour of her birth she has never been known to do anything for herself. She has gone through life, accepting everybody’s help, and adopting everybody’s opinions. A more hopeless person, in a spiritual point of view, I have never met with—there is absolutely, in this perplexing case, no obstructive material to work upon. Aunt Ablewhite would listen to the Grand Lama of Thibet exactly as she listens to Me, and would reflect his views quite as readily as she reflects mine. She found the furnished house at Brighton by stopping at an hotel in London, composing herself on a sofa, and sending for her son. She discovered the necessary servants by breakfasting in bed one morning (still at the hotel), and giving her maid a holiday on condition that the girl “would begin enjoying herself by fetching Miss Clack.” I found her placidly fanning herself in her dressing-gown at eleven o’clock. “Drusilla, dear, I want some servants. You are so clever—please get them for me.” I looked round the untidy room. The church-bells were going for a week-day service; they suggested a word of affectionate remonstrance on my part. “Oh, aunt!” I said sadly. “Is this worthy of a Christian Englishwoman? Is the passage from time to eternity to be made in this manner?” My aunt answered, “I’ll put on my gown, Drusilla, if you will be kind enough to help me.” What was to be said after that? I have done wonders with murderesses—I have never advanced an inch with Aunt Ablewhite. “Where is the list,” I asked, “of the servants whom you require?” My aunt shook her head; she hadn’t even energy enough to keep the list. “Rachel has got it, dear,” she said, “in the next room.” I went into the next room, and so saw Rachel again for the first time since we had parted in Montagu Square.\n\nShe looked pitiably small and thin in her deep mourning. If I attached any serious importance to such a perishable trifle as personal appearance, I might be inclined to add that hers was one of those unfortunate complexions which always suffer when not relieved by a border of white next the skin. But what are our complexions and our looks? Hindrances and pitfalls, dear girls, which beset us on our way to higher things! Greatly to my surprise, Rachel rose when I entered the room, and came forward to meet me with outstretched hand.\n\n“I am glad to see you,” she said. “Drusilla, I have been in the habit of speaking very foolishly and very rudely to you, on former occasions. I beg your pardon. I hope you will forgive me.”\n\nMy face, I suppose, betrayed the astonishment I felt at this. She coloured up for a moment, and then proceeded to explain herself.\n\n“In my poor mother’s lifetime,” she went on, “her friends were not always my friends, too. Now I have lost her, my heart turns for comfort to the people she liked. She liked you. Try to be friends with me, Drusilla, if you can.”\n\nTo any rightly-constituted mind, the motive thus acknowledged was simply shocking. Here in Christian England was a young woman in a state of bereavement, with so little idea of where to look for true comfort, that she actually expected to find it among her mother’s friends! Here was a relative of mine, awakened to a sense of her shortcomings towards others, under the influence, not of conviction and duty, but of sentiment and impulse! Most deplorable to think of—but, still, suggestive of something hopeful, to a person of my experience in plying the good work. There could be no harm, I thought, in ascertaining the extent of the change which the loss of her mother had wrought in Rachel’s character. I decided, as a useful test, to probe her on the subject of her marriage-engagement to Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.\n\nHaving first met her advances with all possible cordiality, I sat by her on the sofa, at her own request. We discussed family affairs and future plans—always excepting that one future plan which was to end in her marriage. Try as I might to turn the conversation that way, she resolutely declined to take the hint. Any open reference to the question, on my part, would have been premature at this early stage of our reconciliation. Besides, I had discovered all I wanted to know. She was no longer the reckless, defiant creature whom I had heard and seen, on the occasion of my martyrdom in Montagu Square. This was, of itself, enough to encourage me to take her future conversion in hand—beginning with a few words of earnest warning directed against the hasty formation of the marriage tie, and so getting on to higher things. Looking at her, now, with this new interest—and calling to mind the headlong suddenness with which she had met Mr. Godfrey’s matrimonial views—I felt the solemn duty of interfering with a fervour which assured me that I should achieve no common results. Rapidity of proceeding was, as I believed, of importance in this case. I went back at once to the question of the servants wanted for the furnished house.\n\n“Where is the list, dear?”\n\nRachel produced it.\n\n“Cook, kitchen-maid, housemaid, and footman,” I read. “My dear Rachel, these servants are only wanted for a term—the term during which your guardian has taken the house. We shall have great difficulty in finding persons of character and capacity to accept a temporary engagement of that sort, if we try in London. Has the house in Brighton been found yet?”\n\n“Yes. Godfrey has taken it; and persons in the house wanted him to hire them as servants. He thought they would hardly do for us, and came back having settled nothing.”\n\n“And you have no experience yourself in these matters, Rachel?”\n\n“None whatever.”\n\n“And Aunt Ablewhite won’t exert herself?”\n\n“No, poor dear. Don’t blame her, Drusilla. I think she is the only really happy woman I have ever met with.”\n\n“There are degrees in happiness, darling. We must have a little talk, some day, on that subject. In the meantime I will undertake to meet the difficulty about the servants. Your aunt will write a letter to the people of the house——”\n\n“She will sign a letter, if I write it for her, which comes to the same thing.”\n\n“Quite the same thing. I shall get the letter, and I will go to Brighton tomorrow.”\n\n“How extremely kind of you! We will join you as soon as you are ready for us. And you will stay, I hope, as my guest. Brighton is so lively; you are sure to enjoy it.”\n\nIn those words the invitation was given, and the glorious prospect of interference was opened before me.\n\nIt was then the middle of the week. By Saturday afternoon the house was ready for them. In that short interval I had sifted, not the characters only, but the religious views as well, of all the disengaged servants who applied to me, and had succeeded in making a selection which my conscience approved. I also discovered, and called on two serious friends of mine, residents in the town, to whom I knew I could confide the pious object which had brought me to Brighton. One of them—a clerical friend—kindly helped me to take sittings for our little party in the church in which he himself ministered. The other—a single lady, like myself—placed the resources of her library (composed throughout of precious publications) entirely at my disposal. I borrowed half-a-dozen works, all carefully chosen with a view to Rachel. When these had been judiciously distributed in the various rooms she would be likely to occupy, I considered that my preparations were complete. Sound doctrine in the servants who waited on her; sound doctrine in the minister who preached to her; sound doctrine in the books that lay on her table—such was the treble welcome which my zeal had prepared for the motherless girl! A heavenly composure filled my mind, on that Saturday afternoon, as I sat at the window waiting the arrival of my relatives. The giddy throng passed and repassed before my eyes. Alas! how many of them felt my exquisite sense of duty done? An awful question. Let us not pursue it.\n\nBetween six and seven the travellers arrived. To my indescribable surprise, they were escorted, not by Mr. Godfrey (as I had anticipated), but by the lawyer, Mr. Bruff.\n\n“How do you do, Miss Clack?” he said. “I mean to stay this time.”\n\nThat reference to the occasion on which I had obliged him to postpone his business to mine, when we were both visiting in Montagu Square, satisfied me that the old worldling had come to Brighton with some object of his own in view. I had prepared quite a little Paradise for my beloved Rachel—and here was the Serpent already!\n\n“Godfrey was very much vexed, Drusilla, not to be able to come with us,” said my Aunt Ablewhite. “There was something in the way which kept him in town. Mr. Bruff volunteered to take his place, and make a holiday of it till Monday morning. By-the-bye, Mr. Bruff, I’m ordered to take exercise, and I don’t like it. That,” added Aunt Ablewhite, pointing out of window to an invalid going by in a chair on wheels, drawn by a man, “is my idea of exercise. If it’s air you want, you get it in your chair. And if it’s fatigue you want, I am sure it’s fatigue enough to look at the man.”\n\nRachel stood silent, at a window by herself, with her eyes fixed on the sea.\n\n“Tired, love?” I inquired.\n\n“No. Only a little out of spirits,” she answered. “I have often seen the sea, on our Yorkshire coast, with that light on it. And I was thinking, Drusilla, of the days that can never come again.”\n\nMr. Bruff remained to dinner, and stayed through the evening. The more I saw of him, the more certain I felt that he had some private end to serve in coming to Brighton. I watched him carefully. He maintained the same appearance of ease, and talked the same godless gossip, hour after hour, until it was time to take leave. As he shook hands with Rachel, I caught his hard and cunning eyes resting on her for a moment with a peculiar interest and attention. She was plainly concerned in the object that he had in view. He said nothing out of the common to her or to anyone on leaving. He invited himself to luncheon the next day, and then he went away to his hotel.\n\nIt was impossible the next morning to get my Aunt Ablewhite out of her dressing-gown in time for church. Her invalid daughter (suffering from nothing, in my opinion, but incurable laziness, inherited from her mother) announced that she meant to remain in bed for the day. Rachel and I went alone together to church. A magnificent sermon was preached by my gifted friend on the heathen indifference of the world to the sinfulness of little sins. For more than an hour his eloquence (assisted by his glorious voice) thundered through the sacred edifice. I said to Rachel, when we came out, “Has it found its way to your heart, dear?” And she answered, “No; it has only made my head ache.” This might have been discouraging to some people; but, once embarked on a career of manifest usefulness, nothing discourages Me.\n\nWe found Aunt Ablewhite and Mr. Bruff at luncheon. When Rachel declined eating anything, and gave as a reason for it that she was suffering from a headache, the lawyer’s cunning instantly saw, and seized, the chance that she had given him.\n\n“There is only one remedy for a headache,” said this horrible old man. “A walk, Miss Rachel, is the thing to cure you. I am entirely at your service, if you will honour me by accepting my arm.”\n\n“With the greatest pleasure. A walk is the very thing I was longing for.”\n\n“It’s past two,” I gently suggested. “And the afternoon service, Rachel, begins at three.”\n\n“How can you expect me to go to church again,” she asked, petulantly, “with such a headache as mine?”\n\nMr. Bruff officiously opened the door for her. In another minute more they were both out of the house. I don’t know when I have felt the solemn duty of interfering so strongly as I felt it at that moment. But what was to be done? Nothing was to be done but to interfere at the first opportunity, later in the day.\n\nOn my return from the afternoon service I found that they had just got back. One look at them told me that the lawyer had said what he wanted to say. I had never before seen Rachel so silent and so thoughtful. I had never before seen Mr. Bruff pay her such devoted attention, and look at her with such marked respect. He had (or pretended that he had) an engagement to dinner that day—and he took an early leave of us all; intending to go back to London by the first train the next morning.\n\n“Are you sure of your own resolution?” he said to Rachel at the door.\n\n“Quite sure,” she answered—and so they parted.\n\nThe moment his back was turned, Rachel withdrew to her own room. She never appeared at dinner. Her maid (the person with the cap-ribbons) was sent downstairs to announce that her headache had returned. I ran up to her and made all sorts of sisterly offers through the door. It was locked, and she kept it locked. Plenty of obstructive material to work on here! I felt greatly cheered and stimulated by her locking the door.\n\nWhen her cup of tea went up to her the next morning, I followed it in. I sat by her bedside and said a few earnest words. She listened with languid civility. I noticed my serious friend’s precious publications huddled together on a table in a corner. Had she chanced to look into them?—I asked. Yes—and they had not interested her. Would she allow me to read a few passages of the deepest interest, which had probably escaped her eye? No, not now—she had other things to think of. She gave these answers, with her attention apparently absorbed in folding and refolding the frilling on her nightgown. It was plainly necessary to rouse her by some reference to those worldly interests which she still had at heart.\n\n“Do you know, love,” I said, “I had an odd fancy, yesterday, about Mr. Bruff? I thought, when I saw you after your walk with him, that he had been telling you some bad news.”\n\nHer fingers dropped from the frilling of her nightgown, and her fierce black eyes flashed at me.\n\n“Quite the contrary!” she said. “It was news I was interested in hearing—and I am deeply indebted to Mr. Bruff for telling me of it.”\n\n“Yes?” I said, in a tone of gentle interest.\n\nHer fingers went back to the frilling, and she turned her head sullenly away from me. I had been met in this manner, in the course of plying the good work, hundreds of times. She merely stimulated me to try again. In my dauntless zeal for her welfare, I ran the great risk, and openly alluded to her marriage engagement.\n\n“News you were interested in hearing?” I repeated. “I suppose, my dear Rachel, that must be news of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite?”\n\nShe started up in the bed, and turned deadly pale. It was evidently on the tip of her tongue to retort on me with the unbridled insolence of former times. She checked herself—laid her head back on the pillow—considered a minute—and then answered in these remarkable words:\n\n“I shall never marry Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.”\n\nIt was my turn to start at that.\n\n“What can you possibly mean?” I exclaimed. “The marriage is considered by the whole family as a settled thing!”\n\n“Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite is expected here today,” she said doggedly. “Wait till he comes—and you will see.”\n\n“But my dear Rachel——”\n\nShe rang the bell at the head of her bed. The person with the cap-ribbons appeared.\n\n“Penelope! my bath.”\n\nLet me give her her due. In the state of my feelings at that moment, I do sincerely believe that she had hit on the only possible way of forcing me to leave the room.\n\nBy the mere worldly mind my position towards Rachel might have been viewed as presenting difficulties of no ordinary kind. I had reckoned on leading her to higher things by means of a little earnest exhortation on the subject of her marriage. And now, if she was to be believed, no such event as her marriage was to take place at all. But ah, my friends! a working Christian of my experience (with an evangelising prospect before her) takes broader views than these. Supposing Rachel really broke off the marriage, on which the Ablewhites, father and son, counted as a settled thing, what would be the result? It could only end, if she held firm, in an exchanging of hard words and bitter accusations on both sides. And what would be the effect on Rachel when the stormy interview was over? A salutary moral depression would be the effect. Her pride would be exhausted, her stubbornness would be exhausted, by the resolute resistance which it was in her character to make under the circumstances. She would turn for sympathy to the nearest person who had sympathy to offer. And I was that nearest person—brimful of comfort, charged to overflowing with seasonable and reviving words. Never had the evangelising prospect looked brighter, to my eyes, than it looked now.\n\nShe came down to breakfast, but she ate nothing, and hardly uttered a word.\n\nAfter breakfast she wandered listlessly from room to room—then suddenly roused herself, and opened the piano. The music she selected to play was of the most scandalously profane sort, associated with performances on the stage which it curdles one’s blood to think of. It would have been premature to interfere with her at such a time as this. I privately ascertained the hour at which Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was expected, and then I escaped the music by leaving the house.\n\nBeing out alone, I took the opportunity of calling upon my two resident friends. It was an indescribable luxury to find myself indulging in earnest conversation with serious persons. Infinitely encouraged and refreshed, I turned my steps back again to the house, in excellent time to await the arrival of our expected visitor. I entered the dining-room, always empty at that hour of the day, and found myself face to face with Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite!\n\nHe made no attempt to fly the place. Quite the contrary. He advanced to meet me with the utmost eagerness.\n\n“Dear Miss Clack, I have been only waiting to see you! Chance set me free of my London engagements today sooner than I had expected, and I have got here, in consequence, earlier than my appointed time.”\n\nNot the slightest embarrassment encumbered his explanation, though this was his first meeting with me after the scene in Montagu Square. He was not aware, it is true, of my having been a witness of that scene. But he knew, on the other hand, that my attendances at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes, and my relations with friends attached to other charities, must have informed me of his shameless neglect of his Ladies and of his Poor. And yet there he was before me, in full possession of his charming voice and his irresistible smile!\n\n“Have you seen Rachel yet?” I asked.\n\nHe sighed gently, and took me by the hand. I should certainly have snatched my hand away, if the manner in which he gave his answer had not paralysed me with astonishment.\n\n“I have seen Rachel,” he said with perfect tranquillity. “You are aware, dear friend, that she was engaged to me? Well, she has taken a sudden resolution to break the engagement. Reflection has convinced her that she will best consult her welfare and mine by retracting a rash promise, and leaving me free to make some happier choice elsewhere. That is the only reason she will give, and the only answer she will make to every question that I can ask of her.”\n\n“What have you done on your side?” I inquired. “Have you submitted?”\n\n“Yes,” he said with the most unruffled composure, “I have submitted.”\n\nHis conduct, under the circumstances, was so utterly inconceivable, that I stood bewildered with my hand in his. It is a piece of rudeness to stare at anybody, and it is an act of indelicacy to stare at a gentleman. I committed both those improprieties. And I said, as if in a dream, “What does it mean?”\n\n“Permit me to tell you,” he replied. “And suppose we sit down?”\n\nHe led me to a chair. I have an indistinct remembrance that he was very affectionate. I don’t think he put his arm round my waist to support me—but I am not sure. I was quite helpless, and his ways with ladies were very endearing. At any rate, we sat down. I can answer for that, if I can answer for nothing more.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n“I have lost a beautiful girl, an excellent social position, and a handsome income,” Mr. Godfrey began; “and I have submitted to it without a struggle. What can be the motive for such extraordinary conduct as that? My precious friend, there is no motive.”\n\n“No motive?” I repeated.\n\n“Let me appeal, my dear Miss Clack, to your experience of children,” he went on. “A child pursues a certain course of conduct. You are greatly struck by it, and you attempt to get at the motive. The dear little thing is incapable of telling you its motive. You might as well ask the grass why it grows, or the birds why they sing. Well! in this matter, I am like the dear little thing—like the grass—like the birds. I don’t know why I made a proposal of marriage to Miss Verinder. I don’t know why I have shamefully neglected my dear Ladies. I don’t know why I have apostatised from the Mothers’ Small-Clothes. You say to the child, Why have you been naughty? And the little angel puts its finger into its mouth, and doesn’t know. My case exactly, Miss Clack! I couldn’t confess it to anybody else. I feel impelled to confess it to you!”\n\nI began to recover myself. A mental problem was involved here. I am deeply interested in mental problems—and I am not, it is thought, without some skill in solving them.\n\n“Best of friends, exert your intellect, and help me,” he proceeded. “Tell me—why does a time come when these matrimonial proceedings of mine begin to look like something done in a dream? Why does it suddenly occur to me that my true happiness is in helping my dear Ladies, in going my modest round of useful work, in saying my few earnest words when called on by my Chairman? What do I want with a position? I have got a position! What do I want with an income? I can pay for my bread and cheese, and my nice little lodging, and my two coats a year. What do I want with Miss Verinder? She has told me with her own lips (this, dear lady, is between ourselves) that she loves another man, and that her only idea in marrying me is to try and put that other man out of her head. What a horrid union is this! Oh, dear me, what a horrid union is this! Such are my reflections, Miss Clack, on my way to Brighton. I approach Rachel with the feeling of a criminal who is going to receive his sentence. When I find that she has changed her mind too—when I hear her propose to break the engagement—I experience (there is no sort of doubt about it) a most overpowering sense of relief. A month ago I was pressing her rapturously to my bosom. An hour ago, the happiness of knowing that I shall never press her again, intoxicates me like strong liquor. The thing seems impossible—the thing can’t be. And yet there are the facts, as I had the honour of stating them when we first sat down together in these two chairs. I have lost a beautiful girl, an excellent social position, and a handsome income; and I have submitted to it without a struggle. Can you account for it, dear friend? It’s quite beyond me.”\n\nHis magnificent head sank on his breast, and he gave up his own mental problem in despair.\n\nI was deeply touched. The case (if I may speak as a spiritual physician) was now quite plain to me. It is no uncommon event, in the experience of us all, to see the possessors of exalted ability occasionally humbled to the level of the most poorly-gifted people about them. The object, no doubt, in the wise economy of Providence, is to remind greatness that it is mortal and that the power which has conferred it can also take it away. It was now—to my mind—easy to discern one of these salutary humiliations in the deplorable proceedings on dear Mr. Godfrey’s part, of which I had been the unseen witness. And it was equally easy to recognise the welcome reappearance of his own finer nature in the horror with which he recoiled from the idea of a marriage with Rachel, and in the charming eagerness which he showed to return to his Ladies and his Poor.\n\nI put this view before him in a few simple and sisterly words. His joy was beautiful to see. He compared himself, as I went on, to a lost man emerging from the darkness into the light. When I answered for a loving reception of him at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes, the grateful heart of our Christian Hero overflowed. He pressed my hands alternately to his lips. Overwhelmed by the exquisite triumph of having got him back among us, I let him do what he liked with my hands. I closed my eyes. I felt my head, in an ecstasy of spiritual self-forgetfulness, sinking on his shoulder. In a moment more I should certainly have swooned away in his arms, but for an interruption from the outer world, which brought me to myself again. A horrid rattling of knives and forks sounded outside the door, and the footman came in to lay the table for luncheon.\n\nMr. Godfrey started up, and looked at the clock on the mantelpiece.\n\n“How time flies with you!” he exclaimed. “I shall barely catch the train.”\n\nI ventured on asking why he was in such a hurry to get back to town. His answer reminded me of family difficulties that were still to be reconciled, and of family disagreements that were yet to come.\n\n“I have heard from my father,” he said. “Business obliges him to leave Frizinghall for London today, and he proposes coming on here, either this evening or tomorrow. I must tell him what has happened between Rachel and me. His heart is set on our marriage—there will be great difficulty, I fear, in reconciling him to the breaking-off of the engagement. I must stop him, for all our sakes, from coming here till he is reconciled. Best and dearest of friends, we shall meet again!”\n\nWith those words he hurried out. In equal haste on my side, I ran upstairs to compose myself in my own room before meeting Aunt Ablewhite and Rachel at the luncheon-table.\n\nI am well aware—to dwell for a moment yet on the subject of Mr. Godfrey—that the all-profaning opinion of the world has charged him with having his own private reasons for releasing Rachel from her engagement, at the first opportunity she gave him. It has also reached my ears, that his anxiety to recover his place in my estimation has been attributed in certain quarters, to a mercenary eagerness to make his peace (through me) with a venerable committee-woman at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes, abundantly blessed with the goods of this world, and a beloved and intimate friend of my own. I only notice these odious slanders for the sake of declaring that they never had a moment’s influence on my mind. In obedience to my instructions, I have exhibited the fluctuations in my opinion of our Christian Hero, exactly as I find them recorded in my diary. In justice to myself, let me here add that, once reinstated in his place in my estimation, my gifted friend never lost that place again. I write with the tears in my eyes, burning to say more. But no—I am cruelly limited to my actual experience of persons and things. In less than a month from the time of which I am now writing, events in the money-market (which diminished even my miserable little income) forced me into foreign exile, and left me with nothing but a loving remembrance of Mr. Godfrey which the slander of the world has assailed, and assailed in vain.\n\nLet me dry my eyes, and return to my narrative.\n\nI went downstairs to luncheon, naturally anxious to see how Rachel was affected by her release from her marriage engagement.\n\nIt appeared to me—but I own I am a poor authority in such matters—that the recovery of her freedom had set her thinking again of that other man whom she loved, and that she was furious with herself for not being able to control a revulsion of feeling of which she was secretly ashamed. Who was the man? I had my suspicions—but it was needless to waste time in idle speculation. When I had converted her, she would, as a matter of course, have no concealments from Me. I should hear all about the man; I should hear all about the Moonstone. If I had had no higher object in stirring her up to a sense of spiritual things, the motive of relieving her mind of its guilty secrets would have been enough of itself to encourage me to go on.\n\nAunt Ablewhite took her exercise in the afternoon in an invalid chair. Rachel accompanied her. “I wish I could drag the chair,” she broke out, recklessly. “I wish I could fatigue myself till I was ready to drop.”\n\nShe was in the same humour in the evening. I discovered in one of my friend’s precious publications—the Life, Letters, and Labours of Miss Jane Ann Stamper, forty-fourth edition—passages which bore with a marvellous appropriateness on Rachel’s present position. Upon my proposing to read them, she went to the piano. Conceive how little she must have known of serious people, if she supposed that my patience was to be exhausted in that way! I kept Miss Jane Ann Stamper by me, and waited for events with the most unfaltering trust in the future.\n\nOld Mr. Ablewhite never made his appearance that night. But I knew the importance which his worldly greed attached to his son’s marriage with Miss Verinder—and I felt a positive conviction (do what Mr. Godfrey might to prevent it) that we should see him the next day. With his interference in the matter, the storm on which I had counted would certainly come, and the salutary exhaustion of Rachel’s resisting powers would as certainly follow. I am not ignorant that old Mr. Ablewhite has the reputation generally (especially among his inferiors) of being a remarkably good-natured man. According to my observation of him, he deserves his reputation as long as he has his own way, and not a moment longer.\n\nThe next day, exactly as I had foreseen, Aunt Ablewhite was as near to being astonished as her nature would permit, by the sudden appearance of her husband. He had barely been a minute in the house, before he was followed, to my astonishment this time, by an unexpected complication in the shape of Mr. Bruff.\n\nI never remember feeling the presence of the lawyer to be more unwelcome than I felt it at that moment. He looked ready for anything in the way of an obstructive proceeding—capable even of keeping the peace with Rachel for one of the combatants!\n\n“This is a pleasant surprise, sir,” said Mr. Ablewhite, addressing himself with his deceptive cordiality to Mr. Bruff. “When I left your office yesterday, I didn’t expect to have the honour of seeing you at Brighton today.”\n\n“I turned over our conversation in my mind, after you had gone,” replied Mr. Bruff. “And it occurred to me that I might perhaps be of some use on this occasion. I was just in time to catch the train, and I had no opportunity of discovering the carriage in which you were travelling.”\n\nHaving given that explanation, he seated himself by Rachel. I retired modestly to a corner—with Miss Jane Ann Stamper on my lap, in case of emergency. My aunt sat at the window; placidly fanning herself as usual. Mr. Ablewhite stood up in the middle of the room, with his bald head much pinker than I had ever seen it yet, and addressed himself in the most affectionate manner to his niece.\n\n“Rachel, my dear,” he said, “I have heard some very extraordinary news from Godfrey. And I am here to inquire about it. You have a sitting-room of your own in this house. Will you honour me by showing me the way to it?”\n\nRachel never moved. Whether she was determined to bring matters to a crisis, or whether she was prompted by some private sign from Mr. Bruff, is more than I can tell. She declined doing old Mr. Ablewhite the honour of conducting him into her sitting-room.\n\n“Whatever you wish to say to me,” she answered, “can be said here—in the presence of my relatives, and in the presence” (she looked at Mr. Bruff) “of my mother’s trusted old friend.”\n\n“Just as you please, my dear,” said the amiable Mr. Ablewhite. He took a chair. The rest of them looked at his face—as if they expected it, after seventy years of worldly training, to speak the truth. I looked at the top of his bald head; having noticed on other occasions that the temper which was really in him had a habit of registering itself there.\n\n“Some weeks ago,” pursued the old gentleman, “my son informed me that Miss Verinder had done him the honour to engage herself to marry him. Is it possible, Rachel, that he can have misinterpreted—or presumed upon—what you really said to him?”\n\n“Certainly not,” she replied. “I did engage myself to marry him.”\n\n“Very frankly answered!” said Mr. Ablewhite. “And most satisfactory, my dear, so far. In respect to what happened some weeks since, Godfrey has made no mistake. The error is evidently in what he told me yesterday. I begin to see it now. You and he have had a lovers’ quarrel—and my foolish son has interpreted it seriously. Ah! I should have known better than that at his age.”\n\nThe fallen nature in Rachel—the mother Eve, so to speak—began to chafe at this.\n\n“Pray let us understand each other, Mr. Ablewhite,” she said. “Nothing in the least like a quarrel took place yesterday between your son and me. If he told you that I proposed breaking off our marriage engagement, and that he agreed on his side—he told you the truth.”\n\nThe self-registering thermometer at the top of Mr. Ablewhite’s bald head began to indicate a rise of temper. His face was more amiable than ever—but there was the pink at the top of his face, a shade deeper already!\n\n“Come, come, my dear!” he said, in his most soothing manner, “now don’t be angry, and don’t be hard on poor Godfrey! He has evidently said some unfortunate thing. He was always clumsy from a child—but he means well, Rachel, he means well!”\n\n“Mr. Ablewhite, I have either expressed myself very badly, or you are purposely mistaking me. Once for all, it is a settled thing between your son and myself that we remain, for the rest of our lives, cousins and nothing more. Is that plain enough?”\n\nThe tone in which she said those words made it impossible, even for old Mr. Ablewhite, to mistake her any longer. His thermometer went up another degree, and his voice when he next spoke, ceased to be the voice which is appropriate to a notoriously good-natured man.\n\n“I am to understand, then,” he said, “that your marriage engagement is broken off?”\n\n“You are to understand that, Mr. Ablewhite, if you please.”\n\n“I am also to take it as a matter of fact that the proposal to withdraw from the engagement came, in the first instance, from you?”\n\n“It came, in the first instance, from me. And it met, as I have told you, with your son’s consent and approval.”\n\nThe thermometer went up to the top of the register. I mean, the pink changed suddenly to scarlet.\n\n“My son is a mean-spirited hound!” cried this furious old worldling. “In justice to myself as his father—not in justice to him—I beg to ask you, Miss Verinder, what complaint you have to make of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite?”\n\nHere Mr. Bruff interfered for the first time.\n\n“You are not bound to answer that question,” he said to Rachel.\n\nOld Mr. Ablewhite fastened on him instantly.\n\n“Don’t forget, sir,” he said, “that you are a self-invited guest here. Your interference would have come with a better grace if you had waited until it was asked for.”\n\nMr. Bruff took no notice. The smooth varnish on his wicked old face never cracked. Rachel thanked him for the advice he had given to her, and then turned to old Mr. Ablewhite—preserving her composure in a manner which (having regard to her age and her sex) was simply awful to see.\n\n“Your son put the same question to me which you have just asked,” she said. “I had only one answer for him, and I have only one answer for you. I proposed that we should release each other, because reflection had convinced me that I should best consult his welfare and mine by retracting a rash promise, and leaving him free to make his choice elsewhere.”\n\n“What has my son done?” persisted Mr. Ablewhite. “I have a right to know that. What has my son done?”\n\nShe persisted just as obstinately on her side.\n\n“You have had the only explanation which I think it necessary to give to you, or to him,” she answered.\n\n“In plain English, it’s your sovereign will and pleasure, Miss Verinder, to jilt my son?”\n\nRachel was silent for a moment. Sitting close behind her, I heard her sigh. Mr. Bruff took her hand, and gave it a little squeeze. She recovered herself, and answered Mr. Ablewhite as boldly as ever.\n\n“I have exposed myself to worse misconstruction than that,” she said. “And I have borne it patiently. The time has gone by, when you could mortify me by calling me a jilt.”\n\nShe spoke with a bitterness of tone which satisfied me that the scandal of the Moonstone had been in some way recalled to her mind. “I have no more to say,” she added, wearily, not addressing the words to anyone in particular, and looking away from us all, out of the window that was nearest to her.\n\nMr. Ablewhite got upon his feet, and pushed away his chair so violently that it toppled over and fell on the floor.\n\n“I have something more to say on my side,” he announced, bringing down the flat of his hand on the table with a bang. “I have to say that if my son doesn’t feel this insult, I do!”\n\nRachel started, and looked at him in sudden surprise.\n\n“Insult?” she repeated. “What do you mean?”\n\n“Insult!” reiterated Mr. Ablewhite. “I know your motive, Miss Verinder, for breaking your promise to my son! I know it as certainly as if you had confessed it in so many words. Your cursed family pride is insulting Godfrey, as it insulted me when I married your aunt. Her family—her beggarly family—turned their backs on her for marrying an honest man, who had made his own place and won his own fortune. I had no ancestors. I wasn’t descended from a set of cut-throat scoundrels who lived by robbery and murder. I couldn’t point to the time when the Ablewhites hadn’t a shirt to their backs, and couldn’t sign their own names. Ha! ha! I wasn’t good enough for the Herncastles, when I married. And now it comes to the pinch, my son isn’t good enough for you. I suspected it, all along. You have got the Herncastle blood in you, my young lady! I suspected it all along.”\n\n“A very unworthy suspicion,” remarked Mr. Bruff. “I am astonished that you have the courage to acknowledge it.”\n\nBefore Mr. Ablewhite could find words to answer in, Rachel spoke in a tone of the most exasperating contempt.\n\n“Surely,” she said to the lawyer, “this is beneath notice. If he can think in that way, let us leave him to think as he pleases.”\n\nFrom scarlet, Mr. Ablewhite was now becoming purple. He gasped for breath; he looked backwards and forwards from Rachel to Mr. Bruff in such a frenzy of rage with both of them that he didn’t know which to attack first. His wife, who had sat impenetrably fanning herself up to this time, began to be alarmed, and attempted, quite uselessly, to quiet him. I had, throughout this distressing interview, felt more than one inward call to interfere with a few earnest words, and had controlled myself under a dread of the possible results, very unworthy of a Christian Englishwoman who looks, not to what is meanly prudent, but to what is morally right. At the point at which matters had now arrived, I rose superior to all considerations of mere expediency. If I had contemplated interposing any remonstrance of my own humble devising, I might possibly have still hesitated. But the distressing domestic emergency which now confronted me, was most marvellously and beautifully provided for in the Correspondence of Miss Jane Ann Stamper—Letter one thousand and one, on “Peace in Families.” I rose in my modest corner, and I opened my precious book.\n\n“Dear Mr. Ablewhite,” I said, “one word!”\n\nWhen I first attracted the attention of the company by rising, I could see that he was on the point of saying something rude to me. My sisterly form of address checked him. He stared at me in heathen astonishment.\n\n“As an affectionate well-wisher and friend,” I proceeded, “and as one long accustomed to arouse, convince, prepare, enlighten, and fortify others, permit me to take the most pardonable of all liberties—the liberty of composing your mind.”\n\nHe began to recover himself; he was on the point of breaking out—he would have broken out, with anybody else. But my voice (habitually gentle) possesses a high note or so, in emergencies. In this emergency, I felt imperatively called upon to have the highest voice of the two.\n\nI held up my precious book before him; I rapped the open page impressively with my forefinger. “Not my words!” I exclaimed, in a burst of fervent interruption. “Oh, don’t suppose that I claim attention for My humble words! Manna in the wilderness, Mr. Ablewhite! Dew on the parched earth! Words of comfort, words of wisdom, words of love—the blessed, blessed, blessed words of Miss Jane Ann Stamper!”\n\nI was stopped there by a momentary impediment of the breath. Before I could recover myself, this monster in human form shouted out furiously,\n\n“Miss Jane Ann Stamper be ——!”\n\nIt is impossible for me to write the awful word, which is here represented by a blank. I shrieked as it passed his lips; I flew to my little bag on the side table; I shook out all my tracts; I seized the one particular tract on profane swearing, entitled, “Hush, for Heaven’s Sake!”; I handed it to him with an expression of agonised entreaty. He tore it in two, and threw it back at me across the table. The rest of them rose in alarm, not knowing what might happen next. I instantly sat down again in my corner. There had once been an occasion, under somewhat similar circumstances, when Miss Jane Ann Stamper had been taken by the two shoulders and turned out of a room. I waited, inspired by her spirit, for a repetition of her martyrdom.\n\nBut no—it was not to be. His wife was the next person whom he addressed. “Who—who—who,” he said, stammering with rage, “who asked this impudent fanatic into the house? Did you?”\n\nBefore Aunt Ablewhite could say a word, Rachel answered for her.\n\n“Miss Clack is here,” she said, “as my guest.”\n\nThose words had a singular effect on Mr. Ablewhite. They suddenly changed him from a man in a state of red-hot anger to a man in a state of icy-cold contempt. It was plain to everybody that Rachel had said something—short and plain as her answer had been—which gave him the upper hand of her at last.\n\n“Oh?” he said. “Miss Clack is here as your guest—in my house?”\n\nIt was Rachel’s turn to lose her temper at that. Her colour rose, and her eyes brightened fiercely. She turned to the lawyer, and, pointing to Mr. Ablewhite, asked haughtily, “What does he mean?”\n\nMr. Bruff interfered for the third time.\n\n“You appear to forget,” he said, addressing Mr. Ablewhite, “that you took this house as Miss Verinder’s guardian, for Miss Verinder’s use.”\n\n“Not quite so fast,” interposed Mr. Ablewhite. “I have a last word to say, which I should have said some time since, if this——” He looked my way, pondering what abominable name he should call me—“if this Rampant Spinster had not interrupted us. I beg to inform you, sir, that, if my son is not good enough to be Miss Verinder’s husband, I cannot presume to consider his father good enough to be Miss Verinder’s guardian. Understand, if you please, that I refuse to accept the position which is offered to me by Lady Verinder’s will. In your legal phrase, I decline to act. This house has necessarily been hired in my name. I take the entire responsibility of it on my shoulders. It is my house. I can keep it, or let it, just as I please. I have no wish to hurry Miss Verinder. On the contrary, I beg her to remove her guest and her luggage, at her own entire convenience.” He made a low bow, and walked out of the room.\n\nThat was Mr. Ablewhite’s revenge on Rachel, for refusing to marry his son!\n\nThe instant the door closed, Aunt Ablewhite exhibited a phenomenon which silenced us all. She became endowed with energy enough to cross the room!\n\n“My dear,” she said, taking Rachel by the hand, “I should be ashamed of my husband, if I didn’t know that it is his temper which has spoken to you, and not himself. You,” continued Aunt Ablewhite, turning on me in my corner with another endowment of energy, in her looks this time instead of her limbs—“you are the mischievous person who irritated him. I hope I shall never see you or your tracts again.” She went back to Rachel and kissed her. “I beg your pardon, my dear,” she said, “in my husband’s name. What can I do for you?”\n\nConsistently perverse in everything—capricious and unreasonable in all the actions of her life—Rachel melted into tears at those commonplace words, and returned her aunt’s kiss in silence.\n\n“If I may be permitted to answer for Miss Verinder,” said Mr. Bruff, “might I ask you, Mrs. Ablewhite, to send Penelope down with her mistress’s bonnet and shawl. Leave us ten minutes together,” he added, in a lower tone, “and you may rely on my setting matters right, to your satisfaction as well as to Rachel’s.”\n\nThe trust of the family in this man was something wonderful to see. Without a word more, on her side, Aunt Ablewhite left the room.\n\n“Ah!” said Mr. Bruff, looking after her. “The Herncastle blood has its drawbacks, I admit. But there is something in good breeding after all!”\n\nHaving made that purely worldly remark, he looked hard at my corner, as if he expected me to go. My interest in Rachel—an infinitely higher interest than his—riveted me to my chair.\n\nMr. Bruff gave it up, exactly as he had given it up at Aunt Verinder’s, in Montagu Square. He led Rachel to a chair by the window, and spoke to her there.\n\n“My dear young lady,” he said, “Mr. Ablewhite’s conduct has naturally shocked you, and taken you by surprise. If it was worth while to contest the question with such a man, we might soon show him that he is not to have things all his own way. But it isn’t worth while. You were quite right in what you said just now; he is beneath our notice.”\n\nHe stopped, and looked round at my corner. I sat there quite immovable, with my tracts at my elbow and with Miss Jane Ann Stamper on my lap.\n\n“You know,” he resumed, turning back again to Rachel, “that it was part of your poor mother’s fine nature always to see the best of the people about her, and never the worst. She named her brother-in-law your guardian because she believed in him, and because she thought it would please her sister. I had never liked Mr. Ablewhite myself, and I induced your mother to let me insert a clause in the will, empowering her executors, in certain events, to consult with me about the appointment of a new guardian. One of those events has happened today; and I find myself in a position to end all these dry business details, I hope agreeably, with a message from my wife. Will you honour Mrs. Bruff by becoming her guest? And will you remain under my roof, and be one of my family, until we wise people have laid our heads together, and have settled what is to be done next?”\n\nAt those words, I rose to interfere. Mr. Bruff had done exactly what I had dreaded he would do, when he asked Mrs. Ablewhite for Rachel’s bonnet and shawl.\n\nBefore I could interpose a word, Rachel had accepted his invitation in the warmest terms. If I suffered the arrangement thus made between them to be carried out—if she once passed the threshold of Mr. Bruff’s door—farewell to the fondest hope of my life, the hope of bringing my lost sheep back to the fold! The bare idea of such a calamity as this quite overwhelmed me. I cast the miserable trammels of worldly discretion to the winds, and spoke with the fervour that filled me, in the words that came first.\n\n“Stop!” I said—“stop! I must be heard. Mr. Bruff! you are not related to her, and I am. I invite her—I summon the executors to appoint me guardian. Rachel, dearest Rachel, I offer you my modest home; come to London by the next train, love, and share it with me!”\n\nMr. Bruff said nothing. Rachel looked at me with a cruel astonishment which she made no effort to conceal.\n\n“You are very kind, Drusilla,” she said. “I shall hope to visit you whenever I happen to be in London. But I have accepted Mr. Bruff’s invitation, and I think it will be best, for the present, if I remain under Mr. Bruff’s care.”\n\n“Oh, don’t say so!” I pleaded. “I can’t part with you, Rachel—I can’t part with you!”\n\nI tried to fold her in my arms. But she drew back. My fervour did not communicate itself; it only alarmed her.\n\n“Surely,” she said, “this is a very unnecessary display of agitation? I don’t understand it.”\n\n“No more do I,” said Mr. Bruff.\n\nTheir hardness—their hideous, worldly hardness—revolted me.\n\n“Oh, Rachel! Rachel!” I burst out. “Haven’t you seen yet, that my heart yearns to make a Christian of you? Has no inner voice told you that I am trying to do for you, what I was trying to do for your dear mother when death snatched her out of my hands?”\n\nRachel advanced a step nearer, and looked at me very strangely.\n\n“I don’t understand your reference to my mother,” she said. “Miss Clack, will you have the goodness to explain yourself?”\n\nBefore I could answer, Mr. Bruff came forward, and offering his arm to Rachel, tried to lead her out of the room.\n\n“You had better not pursue the subject, my dear,” he said. “And Miss Clack had better not explain herself.”\n\nIf I had been a stock or a stone, such an interference as this must have roused me into testifying to the truth. I put Mr. Bruff aside indignantly with my own hand, and, in solemn and suitable language, I stated the view with which sound doctrine does not scruple to regard the awful calamity of dying unprepared.\n\nRachel started back from me—I blush to write—with a scream of horror.\n\n“Come away!” she said to Mr. Bruff. “Come away, for God’s sake, before that woman can say any more! Oh, think of my poor mother’s harmless, useful, beautiful life! You were at the funeral, Mr. Bruff; you saw how everybody loved her; you saw the poor helpless people crying at her grave over the loss of their best friend. And that wretch stands there, and tries to make me doubt that my mother, who was an angel on earth, is an angel in heaven now! Don’t stop to talk about it! Come away! It stifles me to breathe the same air with her! It frightens me to feel that we are in the same room together!”\n\nDeaf to all remonstrance, she ran to the door.\n\nAt the same moment, her maid entered with her bonnet and shawl. She huddled them on anyhow. “Pack my things,” she said, “and bring them to Mr. Bruff’s.” I attempted to approach her—I was shocked and grieved, but, it is needless to say, not offended. I only wished to say to her, “May your hard heart be softened! I freely forgive you!” She pulled down her veil, and tore her shawl away from my hand, and, hurrying out, shut the door in my face. I bore the insult with my customary fortitude. I remember it now with my customary superiority to all feeling of offence.\n\nMr. Bruff had his parting word of mockery for me, before he too hurried out, in his turn.\n\n“You had better not have explained yourself, Miss Clack,” he said, and bowed, and left the room.\n\nThe person with the cap-ribbons followed.\n\n“It’s easy to see who has set them all by the ears together,” she said. “I’m only a poor servant—but I declare I’m ashamed of you!” She too went out, and banged the door after her.\n\nI was left alone in the room. Reviled by them all, deserted by them all, I was left alone in the room.\n\nIs there more to be added to this plain statement of facts—to this touching picture of a Christian persecuted by the world? No! my diary reminds me that one more of the many chequered chapters in my life ends here. From that day forth, I never saw Rachel Verinder again. She had my forgiveness at the time when she insulted me. She has had my prayerful good wishes ever since. And when I die—to complete the return on my part of good for evil—she will have the Life, Letters, and Labours of Miss Jane Ann Stamper left her as a legacy by my will.\n\n# SECOND NARRATIVE.\n\nContributed by Mathew Bruff, Solicitor, of Gray’s Inn Square.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nMy fair friend, Miss Clack, having laid down the pen, there are two reasons for my taking it up next, in my turn.\n\nIn the first place, I am in a position to throw the necessary light on certain points of interest which have thus far been left in the dark. Miss Verinder had her own private reason for breaking her marriage engagement—and I was at the bottom of it. Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite had his own private reason for withdrawing all claim to the hand of his charming cousin—and I discovered what it was.\n\nIn the second place, it was my good or ill fortune, I hardly know which, to find myself personally involved—at the period of which I am now writing—in the mystery of the Indian Diamond. I had the honour of an interview, at my own office, with an Oriental stranger of distinguished manners, who was no other, unquestionably, than the chief of the three Indians. Add to this, that I met with the celebrated traveller, Mr. Murthwaite, the day afterwards, and that I held a conversation with him on the subject of the Moonstone, which has a very important bearing on later events. And there you have the statement of my claims to fill the position which I occupy in these pages.\n\nThe true story of the broken marriage engagement comes first in point of time, and must therefore take the first place in the present narrative. Tracing my way back along the chain of events, from one end to the other, I find it necessary to open the scene, oddly enough as you will think, at the bedside of my excellent client and friend, the late Sir John Verinder.\n\nSir John had his share—perhaps rather a large share—of the more harmless and amiable of the weaknesses incidental to humanity. Among these, I may mention as applicable to the matter in hand, an invincible reluctance—so long as he enjoyed his usual good health—to face the responsibility of making his will. Lady Verinder exerted her influence to rouse him to a sense of duty in this matter; and I exerted my influence. He admitted the justice of our views—but he went no further than that, until he found himself afflicted with the illness which ultimately brought him to his grave. Then, I was sent for at last, to take my client’s instructions on the subject of his will. They proved to be the simplest instructions I had ever received in the whole of my professional career.\n\nSir John was dozing, when I entered the room. He roused himself at the sight of me.\n\n“How do you do, Mr. Bruff?” he said. “I sha’n’t be very long about this. And then I’ll go to sleep again.” He looked on with great interest while I collected pens, ink, and paper. “Are you ready?” he asked. I bowed and took a dip of ink, and waited for my instructions.\n\n“I leave everything to my wife,” said Sir John. “That’s all.” He turned round on his pillow, and composed himself to sleep again.\n\nI was obliged to disturb him.\n\n“Am I to understand,” I asked, “that you leave the whole of the property, of every sort and description, of which you die possessed, absolutely to Lady Verinder?”\n\n“Yes,” said Sir John. “Only, I put it shorter. Why can’t you put it shorter, and let me go to sleep again? Everything to my wife. That’s my Will.”\n\nHis property was entirely at his own disposal, and was of two kinds. Property in land (I purposely abstain from using technical language), and property in money. In the majority of cases, I am afraid I should have felt it my duty to my client to ask him to reconsider his Will. In the case of Sir John, I knew Lady Verinder to be, not only worthy of the unreserved trust which her husband had placed in her (all good wives are worthy of that)—but to be also capable of properly administering a trust (which, in my experience of the fair sex, not one in a thousand of them is competent to do). In ten minutes, Sir John’s Will was drawn, and executed, and Sir John himself, good man, was finishing his interrupted nap.\n\nLady Verinder amply justified the confidence which her husband had placed in her. In the first days of her widowhood, she sent for me, and made her Will. The view she took of her position was so thoroughly sound and sensible, that I was relieved of all necessity for advising her. My responsibility began and ended with shaping her instructions into the proper legal form. Before Sir John had been a fortnight in his grave, the future of his daughter had been most wisely and most affectionately provided for.\n\nThe Will remained in its fireproof box at my office, through more years than I like to reckon up. It was not till the summer of eighteen hundred and forty-eight that I found occasion to look at it again under very melancholy circumstances.\n\nAt the date I have mentioned, the doctors pronounced the sentence on poor Lady Verinder, which was literally a sentence of death. I was the first person whom she informed of her situation; and I found her anxious to go over her Will again with me.\n\nIt was impossible to improve the provisions relating to her daughter. But, in the lapse of time, her wishes in regard to certain minor legacies, left to different relatives, had undergone some modification; and it became necessary to add three or four Codicils to the original document. Having done this at once, for fear of accident, I obtained her ladyship’s permission to embody her recent instructions in a second Will. My object was to avoid certain inevitable confusions and repetitions which now disfigured the original document, and which, to own the truth, grated sadly on my professional sense of the fitness of things.\n\nThe execution of this second Will has been described by Miss Clack, who was so obliging as to witness it. So far as regarded Rachel Verinder’s pecuniary interests, it was, word for word, the exact counterpart of the first Will. The only changes introduced related to the appointment of a guardian, and to certain provisions concerning that appointment, which were made under my advice. On Lady Verinder’s death, the Will was placed in the hands of my proctor to be “proved” (as the phrase is) in the usual way.\n\nIn about three weeks from that time—as well as I can remember—the first warning reached me of something unusual going on under the surface. I happened to be looking in at my friend the proctor’s office, and I observed that he received me with an appearance of greater interest than usual.\n\n“I have some news for you,” he said. “What do you think I heard at Doctors’ Commons this morning? Lady Verinder’s Will has been asked for, and examined, already!”\n\nThis was news indeed! There was absolutely nothing which could be contested in the Will; and there was nobody I could think of who had the slightest interest in examining it. (I shall perhaps do well if I explain in this place, for the benefit of the few people who don’t know it already, that the law allows all Wills to be examined at Doctors’ Commons by anybody who applies, on the payment of a shilling fee.)\n\n“Did you hear who asked for the Will?” I asked.\n\n“Yes; the clerk had no hesitation in telling me. Mr. Smalley, of the firm of Skipp and Smalley, asked for it. The Will has not been copied yet into the great Folio Registers. So there was no alternative but to depart from the usual course, and to let him see the original document. He looked it over carefully, and made a note in his pocket-book. Have you any idea of what he wanted with it?”\n\nI shook my head. “I shall find out,” I answered, “before I am a day older.” With that I went back at once to my own office.\n\nIf any other firm of solicitors had been concerned in this unaccountable examination of my deceased client’s Will, I might have found some difficulty in making the necessary discovery. But I had a hold over Skipp and Smalley which made my course in this matter a comparatively easy one. My common-law clerk (a most competent and excellent man) was a brother of Mr. Smalley’s; and, owing to this sort of indirect connection with me, Skipp and Smalley had, for some years past, picked up the crumbs that fell from my table, in the shape of cases brought to my office, which, for various reasons, I did not think it worth while to undertake. My professional patronage was, in this way, of some importance to the firm. I intended, if necessary, to remind them of that patronage, on the present occasion.\n\nThe moment I got back I spoke to my clerk; and, after telling him what had happened, I sent him to his brother’s office, “with Mr. Bruff’s compliments, and he would be glad to know why Messrs. Skipp and Smalley had found it necessary to examine Lady Verinder’s will.”\n\nThis message brought Mr. Smalley back to my office in company with his brother. He acknowledged that he had acted under instructions received from a client. And then he put it to me, whether it would not be a breach of professional confidence on his part to say more.\n\nWe had a smart discussion upon that. He was right, no doubt; and I was wrong. The truth is, I was angry and suspicious—and I insisted on knowing more. Worse still, I declined to consider any additional information offered me, as a secret placed in my keeping: I claimed perfect freedom to use my own discretion. Worse even than that, I took an unwarrantable advantage of my position. “Choose, sir,” I said to Mr. Smalley, “between the risk of losing your client’s business and the risk of losing Mine.” Quite indefensible, I admit—an act of tyranny, and nothing less. Like other tyrants, I carried my point. Mr. Smalley chose his alternative, without a moment’s hesitation.\n\nHe smiled resignedly, and gave up the name of his client:\n\nMr. Godfrey Ablewhite.\n\nThat was enough for me—I wanted to know no more.\n\nHaving reached this point in my narrative, it now becomes necessary to place the reader of these lines—so far as Lady Verinder’s Will is concerned—on a footing of perfect equality, in respect of information, with myself.\n\nLet me state, then, in the fewest possible words, that Rachel Verinder had nothing but a life-interest in the property. Her mother’s excellent sense, and my long experience, had combined to relieve her of all responsibility, and to guard her from all danger of becoming the victim in the future of some needy and unscrupulous man. Neither she, nor her husband (if she married), could raise sixpence, either on the property in land, or on the property in money. They would have the houses in London and in Yorkshire to live in, and they would have the handsome income—and that was all.\n\nWhen I came to think over what I had discovered, I was sorely perplexed what to do next.\n\nHardly a week had passed since I had heard (to my surprise and distress) of Miss Verinder’s proposed marriage. I had the sincerest admiration and affection for her; and I had been inexpressibly grieved when I heard that she was about to throw herself away on Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite. And now, here was the man—whom I had always believed to be a smooth-tongued impostor—justifying the very worst that I had thought of him, and plainly revealing the mercenary object of the marriage, on his side! And what of that?—you may reply—the thing is done every day. Granted, my dear sir. But would you think of it quite as lightly as you do, if the thing was done (let us say) with your own sister?\n\nThe first consideration which now naturally occurred to me was this. Would Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite hold to his engagement, after what his lawyer had discovered for him?\n\nIt depended entirely on his pecuniary position, of which I knew nothing. If that position was not a desperate one, it would be well worth his while to marry Miss Verinder for her income alone. If, on the other hand, he stood in urgent need of realising a large sum by a given time, then Lady Verinder’s Will would exactly meet the case, and would preserve her daughter from falling into a scoundrel’s hands.\n\nIn the latter event, there would be no need for me to distress Miss Rachel, in the first days of her mourning for her mother, by an immediate revelation of the truth. In the former event, if I remained silent, I should be conniving at a marriage which would make her miserable for life.\n\nMy doubts ended in my calling at the hotel in London, at which I knew Mrs. Ablewhite and Miss Verinder to be staying. They informed me that they were going to Brighton the next day, and that an unexpected obstacle prevented Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite from accompanying them. I at once proposed to take his place. While I was only thinking of Rachel Verinder, it was possible to hesitate. When I actually saw her, my mind was made up directly, come what might of it, to tell her the truth.\n\nI found my opportunity, when I was out walking with her, on the day after my arrival.\n\n“May I speak to you,” I asked, “about your marriage engagement?”\n\n“Yes,” she said, indifferently, “if you have nothing more interesting to talk about.”\n\n“Will you forgive an old friend and servant of your family, Miss Rachel, if I venture on asking whether your heart is set on this marriage?”\n\n“I am marrying in despair, Mr. Bruff—on the chance of dropping into some sort of stagnant happiness which may reconcile me to my life.”\n\nStrong language! and suggestive of something below the surface, in the shape of a romance. But I had my own object in view, and I declined (as we lawyers say) to pursue the question into its side issues.\n\n“Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite can hardly be of your way of thinking,” I said. “His heart must be set on the marriage at any rate?”\n\n“He says so, and I suppose I ought to believe him. He would hardly marry me, after what I have owned to him, unless he was fond of me.”\n\nPoor thing! the bare idea of a man marrying her for his own selfish and mercenary ends had never entered her head. The task I had set myself began to look like a harder task than I had bargained for.\n\n“It sounds strangely,” I went on, “in my old-fashioned ears——”\n\n“What sounds strangely?” she asked.\n\n“To hear you speak of your future husband as if you were not quite sure of the sincerity of his attachment. Are you conscious of any reason in your own mind for doubting him?”\n\nHer astonishing quickness of perception, detected a change in my voice, or my manner, when I put that question, which warned her that I had been speaking all along with some ulterior object in view. She stopped, and taking her arm out of mine, looked me searchingly in the face.\n\n“Mr. Bruff,” she said, “you have something to tell me about Godfrey Ablewhite. Tell it.”\n\nI knew her well enough to take her at her word. I told it.\n\nShe put her arm again into mine, and walked on with me slowly. I felt her hand tightening its grasp mechanically on my arm, and I saw her getting paler and paler as I went on—but, not a word passed her lips while I was speaking. When I had done, she still kept silence. Her head drooped a little, and she walked by my side, unconscious of my presence, unconscious of everything about her; lost—buried, I might almost say—in her own thoughts.\n\nI made no attempt to disturb her. My experience of her disposition warned me, on this, as on former occasions, to give her time.\n\nThe first instinct of girls in general, on being told of anything which interests them, is to ask a multitude of questions, and then to run off, and talk it all over with some favourite friend. Rachel Verinder’s first instinct, under similar circumstances, was to shut herself up in her own mind, and to think it over by herself. This absolute self-dependence is a great virtue in a man. In a woman it has a serious drawback of morally separating her from the mass of her sex, and so exposing her to misconstruction by the general opinion. I strongly suspect myself of thinking as the rest of the world think in this matter—except in the case of Rachel Verinder. The self-dependence in her character, was one of its virtues in my estimation; partly, no doubt, because I sincerely admired and liked her; partly, because the view I took of her connexion with the loss of the Moonstone was based on my own special knowledge of her disposition. Badly as appearances might look, in the matter of the Diamond—shocking as it undoubtedly was to know that she was associated in any way with the mystery of an undiscovered theft—I was satisfied nevertheless that she had done nothing unworthy of her, because I was also satisfied that she had not stirred a step in the business, without shutting herself up in her own mind, and thinking it over first.\n\nWe had walked on, for nearly a mile I should say, before Rachel roused herself. She suddenly looked up at me with a faint reflection of her smile of happier times—the most irresistible smile I have ever seen on a woman’s face.\n\n“I owe much already to your kindness,” she said. “And I feel more deeply indebted to it now than ever. If you hear any rumours of my marriage when you get back to London, contradict them at once, on my authority.”\n\n“Have you resolved to break your engagement?” I asked.\n\n“Can you doubt it?” she returned proudly, “after what you have told me!”\n\n“My dear Miss Rachel, you are very young—and you may find more difficulty in withdrawing from your present position than you anticipate. Have you no one—I mean a lady, of course—whom you could consult?”\n\n“No one,” she answered.\n\nIt distressed me, it did indeed distress me, to hear her say that. She was so young and so lonely—and she bore it so well! The impulse to help her got the better of any sense of my own unfitness which I might have felt under the circumstances; and I stated such ideas on the subject as occurred to me on the spur of the moment, to the best of my ability. I have advised a prodigious number of clients, and have dealt with some exceedingly awkward difficulties, in my time. But this was the first occasion on which I had ever found myself advising a young lady how to obtain her release from a marriage engagement. The suggestion I offered amounted briefly to this. I recommended her to tell Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite—at a private interview, of course—that he had, to her certain knowledge, betrayed the mercenary nature of the motive on his side. She was then to add that their marriage, after what she had discovered, was a simple impossibility—and she was to put it to him, whether he thought it wisest to secure her silence by falling in with her views, or to force her, by opposing them, to make the motive under which she was acting generally known. If he attempted to defend himself, or to deny the facts, she was, in that event, to refer him to me.\n\nMiss Verinder listened attentively till I had done. She then thanked me very prettily for my advice, but informed me at the same time that it was impossible for her to follow it.\n\n“May I ask,” I said, “what objection you see to following it?”\n\nShe hesitated—and then met me with a question on her side.\n\n“Suppose you were asked to express your opinion of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s conduct?” she began.\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“What would you call it?”\n\n“I should call it the conduct of a meanly deceitful man.”\n\n“Mr. Bruff! I have believed in that man. I have promised to marry that man. How can I tell him he is mean, how can I tell him he has deceived me, how can I disgrace him in the eyes of the world after that? I have degraded myself by ever thinking of him as my husband. If I say what you tell me to say to him—I am owning that I have degraded myself to his face. I can’t do that. After what has passed between us, I can’t do that! The shame of it would be nothing to him. But the shame of it would be unendurable to me.”\n\nHere was another of the marked peculiarities in her character disclosing itself to me without reserve. Here was her sensitive horror of the bare contact with anything mean, blinding her to every consideration of what she owed to herself, hurrying her into a false position which might compromise her in the estimation of all her friends! Up to this time, I had been a little diffident about the propriety of the advice I had given to her. But, after what she had just said, I had no sort of doubt that it was the best advice that could have been offered; and I felt no sort of hesitation in pressing it on her again.\n\nShe only shook her head, and repeated her objection in other words.\n\n“He has been intimate enough with me to ask me to be his wife. He has stood high enough in my estimation to obtain my consent. I can’t tell him to his face that he is the most contemptible of living creatures, after that!”\n\n“But, my dear Miss Rachel,” I remonstrated, “it’s equally impossible for you to tell him that you withdraw from your engagement without giving some reason for it.”\n\n“I shall say that I have thought it over, and that I am satisfied it will be best for both of us if we part.”\n\n“No more than that?”\n\n“No more.”\n\n“Have you thought of what he may say, on his side?”\n\n“He may say what he pleases.”\n\nIt was impossible not to admire her delicacy and her resolution, and it was equally impossible not to feel that she was putting herself in the wrong. I entreated her to consider her own position. I reminded her that she would be exposing herself to the most odious misconstruction of her motives. “You can’t brave public opinion,” I said, “at the command of private feeling.”\n\n“I can,” she answered. “I have done it already.”\n\n“What do you mean?”\n\n“You have forgotten the Moonstone, Mr. Bruff. Have I not braved public opinion, there, with my own private reasons for it?”\n\nHer answer silenced me for the moment. It set me trying to trace the explanation of her conduct, at the time of the loss of the Moonstone, out of the strange avowal which had just escaped her. I might perhaps have done it when I was younger. I certainly couldn’t do it now.\n\nI tried a last remonstrance before we returned to the house. She was just as immovable as ever. My mind was in a strange conflict of feelings about her when I left her that day. She was obstinate; she was wrong. She was interesting; she was admirable; she was deeply to be pitied. I made her promise to write to me the moment she had any news to send. And I went back to my business in London, with a mind exceedingly ill at ease.\n\nOn the evening of my return, before it was possible for me to receive my promised letter, I was surprised by a visit from Mr. Ablewhite the elder, and was informed that Mr. Godfrey had got his dismissal—and had accepted it—that very day.\n\nWith the view I already took of the case, the bare fact stated in the words that I have underlined, revealed Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s motive for submission as plainly as if he had acknowledged it himself. He needed a large sum of money; and he needed it by a given time. Rachel’s income, which would have helped him to anything else, would not help him here; and Rachel had accordingly released herself, without encountering a moment’s serious opposition on his part. If I am told that this is a mere speculation, I ask, in my turn, what other theory will account for his giving up a marriage which would have maintained him in splendour for the rest of his life?\n\nAny exultation I might otherwise have felt at the lucky turn which things had now taken, was effectually checked by what passed at my interview with old Mr. Ablewhite.\n\nHe came, of course, to know whether I could give him any explanation of Miss Verinder’s extraordinary conduct. It is needless to say that I was quite unable to afford him the information he wanted. The annoyance which I thus inflicted, following on the irritation produced by a recent interview with his son, threw Mr. Ablewhite off his guard. Both his looks and his language convinced me that Miss Verinder would find him a merciless man to deal with, when he joined the ladies at Brighton the next day.\n\nI had a restless night, considering what I ought to do next. How my reflections ended, and how thoroughly well founded my distrust of Mr. Ablewhite proved to be, are items of information which (as I am told) have already been put tidily in their proper places, by that exemplary person, Miss Clack. I have only to add—in completion of her narrative—that Miss Verinder found the quiet and repose which she sadly needed, poor thing, in my house at Hampstead. She honoured us by making a long stay. My wife and daughters were charmed with her; and, when the executors decided on the appointment of a new guardian, I feel sincere pride and pleasure in recording that my guest and my family parted like old friends, on either side.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\nThe next thing I have to do, is to present such additional information as I possess on the subject of the Moonstone, or, to speak more correctly, on the subject of the Indian plot to steal the Diamond. The little that I have to tell is (as I think I have already said) of some importance, nevertheless, in respect of its bearing very remarkably on events which are still to come.\n\nAbout a week or ten days after Miss Verinder had left us, one of my clerks entered the private room at my office, with a card in his hand, and informed me that a gentleman was below, who wanted to speak to me.\n\nI looked at the card. There was a foreign name written on it, which has escaped my memory. It was followed by a line written in English at the bottom of the card, which I remember perfectly well:\n\n“Recommended by Mr. Septimus Luker.”\n\nThe audacity of a person in Mr. Luker’s position presuming to recommend anybody to me, took me so completely by surprise, that I sat silent for the moment, wondering whether my own eyes had not deceived me. The clerk, observing my bewilderment, favoured me with the result of his own observation of the stranger who was waiting downstairs.\n\n“He is rather a remarkable-looking man, sir. So dark in the complexion that we all set him down in the office for an Indian, or something of that sort.”\n\nAssociating the clerk’s idea with the line inscribed on the card in my hand, I thought it possible that the Moonstone might be at the bottom of Mr. Luker’s recommendation, and of the stranger’s visit at my office. To the astonishment of my clerk, I at once decided on granting an interview to the gentleman below.\n\nIn justification of the highly unprofessional sacrifice to mere curiosity which I thus made, permit me to remind anybody who may read these lines, that no living person (in England, at any rate) can claim to have had such an intimate connexion with the romance of the Indian Diamond as mine has been. I was trusted with the secret of Colonel Herncastle’s plan for escaping assassination. I received the Colonel’s letters, periodically reporting himself a living man. I drew his Will, leaving the Moonstone to Miss Verinder. I persuaded his executor to act, on the chance that the jewel might prove to be a valuable acquisition to the family. And, lastly, I combated Mr. Franklin Blake’s scruples, and induced him to be the means of transporting the Diamond to Lady Verinder’s house. If anyone can claim a prescriptive right of interest in the Moonstone, and in everything connected with it, I think it is hardly to be denied that I am the man.\n\nThe moment my mysterious client was shown in, I felt an inner conviction that I was in the presence of one of the three Indians—probably of the chief. He was carefully dressed in European costume. But his swarthy complexion, his long lithe figure, and his grave and graceful politeness of manner were enough to betray his Oriental origin to any intelligent eyes that looked at him.\n\nI pointed to a chair, and begged to be informed of the nature of his business with me.\n\nAfter first apologising—in an excellent selection of English words—for the liberty which he had taken in disturbing me, the Indian produced a small parcel the outer covering of which was of cloth of gold. Removing this and a second wrapping of some silken fabric, he placed a little box, or casket, on my table, most beautifully and richly inlaid in jewels, on an ebony ground.\n\n“I have come, sir,” he said, “to ask you to lend me some money. And I leave this as an assurance to you that my debt will be paid back.”\n\nI pointed to his card. “And you apply to me,” I rejoined, “at Mr. Luker’s recommendation?”\n\nThe Indian bowed.\n\n“May I ask how it is that Mr. Luker himself did not advance the money that you require?”\n\n“Mr. Luker informed me, sir, that he had no money to lend.”\n\n“And so he recommended you to come to me?”\n\nThe Indian, in his turn, pointed to the card. “It is written there,” he said.\n\nBriefly answered, and thoroughly to the purpose! If the Moonstone had been in my possession, this Oriental gentleman would have murdered me, I am well aware, without a moment’s hesitation. At the same time, and barring that slight drawback, I am bound to testify that he was the perfect model of a client. He might not have respected my life. But he did what none of my own countrymen had ever done, in all my experience of them—he respected my time.\n\n“I am sorry,” I said, “that you should have had the trouble of coming to me. Mr. Luker is quite mistaken in sending you here. I am trusted, like other men in my profession, with money to lend. But I never lend it to strangers, and I never lend it on such a security as you have produced.”\n\nFar from attempting, as other people would have done, to induce me to relax my own rules, the Indian only made me another bow, and wrapped up his box in its two coverings without a word of protest. He rose—this admirable assassin rose to go, the moment I had answered him!\n\n“Will your condescension towards a stranger, excuse my asking one question,” he said, “before I take my leave?”\n\nI bowed on my side. Only one question at parting! The average in my experience was fifty.\n\n“Supposing, sir, it had been possible (and customary) for you to lend me the money,” he said, “in what space of time would it have been possible (and customary) for me to pay it back?”\n\n“According to the usual course pursued in this country,” I answered, “you would have been entitled to pay the money back (if you liked) in one year’s time from the date at which it was first advanced to you.”\n\nThe Indian made me a last bow, the lowest of all—and suddenly and softly walked out of the room.\n\nIt was done in a moment, in a noiseless, supple, cat-like way, which a little startled me, I own. As soon as I was composed enough to think, I arrived at one distinct conclusion in reference to the otherwise incomprehensible visitor who had favoured me with a call.\n\nHis face, voice, and manner—while I was in his company—were under such perfect control that they set all scrutiny at defiance. But he had given me one chance of looking under the smooth outer surface of him, for all that. He had not shown the slightest sign of attempting to fix anything that I had said to him in his mind, until I mentioned the time at which it was customary to permit the earliest repayment, on the part of a debtor, of money that had been advanced as a loan. When I gave him that piece of information, he looked me straight in the face, while I was speaking, for the first time. The inference I drew from this was—that he had a special purpose in asking me his last question, and a special interest in hearing my answer to it. The more carefully I reflected on what had passed between us, the more shrewdly I suspected the production of the casket, and the application for the loan, of having been mere formalities, designed to pave the way for the parting inquiry addressed to me.\n\nI had satisfied myself of the correctness of this conclusion—and was trying to get on a step further, and penetrate the Indian’s motives next—when a letter was brought to me, which proved to be from no less a person that Mr. Septimus Luker himself. He asked my pardon in terms of sickening servility, and assured me that he could explain matters to my satisfaction, if I would honour him by consenting to a personal interview.\n\nI made another unprofessional sacrifice to mere curiosity. I honoured him by making an appointment at my office, for the next day.\n\nMr. Luker was, in every respect, such an inferior creature to the Indian—he was so vulgar, so ugly, so cringing, and so prosy—that he is quite unworthy of being reported, at any length, in these pages. The substance of what he had to tell me may be fairly stated as follows:\n\nThe day before I had received the visit of the Indian, Mr. Luker had been favoured with a call from that accomplished gentleman. In spite of his European disguise, Mr. Luker had instantly identified his visitor with the chief of the three Indians, who had formerly annoyed him by loitering about his house, and who had left him no alternative but to consult a magistrate. From this startling discovery he had rushed to the conclusion (naturally enough I own) that he must certainly be in the company of one of the three men, who had blindfolded him, gagged him, and robbed him of his banker’s receipt. The result was that he became quite paralysed with terror, and that he firmly believed his last hour had come.\n\nOn his side, the Indian preserved the character of a perfect stranger. He produced the little casket, and made exactly the same application which he had afterwards made to me. As the speediest way of getting rid of him, Mr. Luker had at once declared that he had no money. The Indian had thereupon asked to be informed of the best and safest person to apply to for the loan he wanted. Mr. Luker had answered that the best and safest person, in such cases, was usually a respectable solicitor. Asked to name some individual of that character and profession, Mr. Luker had mentioned me—for the one simple reason that, in the extremity of his terror, mine was the first name which occurred to him. “The perspiration was pouring off me like rain, sir,” the wretched creature concluded. “I didn’t know what I was talking about. And I hope you’ll look over it, Mr. Bruff, sir, in consideration of my having been really and truly frightened out of my wits.”\n\nI excused the fellow graciously enough. It was the readiest way of releasing myself from the sight of him. Before he left me, I detained him to make one inquiry.\n\nHad the Indian said anything noticeable, at the moment of quitting Mr. Luker’s house?\n\nYes! The Indian had put precisely the same question to Mr. Luker, at parting, which he had put to me; receiving of course, the same answer as the answer which I had given him.\n\nWhat did it mean? Mr. Luker’s explanation gave me no assistance towards solving the problem. My own unaided ingenuity, consulted next, proved quite unequal to grapple with the difficulty. I had a dinner engagement that evening; and I went upstairs, in no very genial frame of mind, little suspecting that the way to my dressing-room and the way to discovery, meant, on this particular occasion, one and the same thing.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nThe prominent personage among the guests at the dinner party I found to be Mr. Murthwaite.\n\nOn his appearance in England, after his wanderings, society had been greatly interested in the traveller, as a man who had passed through many dangerous adventures, and who had escaped to tell the tale. He had now announced his intention of returning to the scene of his exploits, and of penetrating into regions left still unexplored. This magnificent indifference to placing his safety in peril for the second time, revived the flagging interest of the worshippers in the hero. The law of chances was clearly against his escaping on this occasion. It is not every day that we can meet an eminent person at dinner, and feel that there is a reasonable prospect of the news of his murder being the news that we hear of him next.\n\nWhen the gentlemen were left by themselves in the dining-room, I found myself sitting next to Mr. Murthwaite. The guests present being all English, it is needless to say that, as soon as the wholesome check exercised by the presence of the ladies was removed, the conversation turned on politics as a necessary result.\n\nIn respect to this all-absorbing national topic, I happen to be one of the most un-English Englishmen living. As a general rule, political talk appears to me to be of all talk the most dreary and the most profitless. Glancing at Mr. Murthwaite, when the bottles had made their first round of the table, I found that he was apparently of my way of thinking. He was doing it very dexterously—with all possible consideration for the feelings of his host—but it is not the less certain that he was composing himself for a nap. It struck me as an experiment worth attempting, to try whether a judicious allusion to the subject of the Moonstone would keep him awake, and, if it did, to see what he thought of the last new complication in the Indian conspiracy, as revealed in the prosaic precincts of my office.\n\n“If I am not mistaken, Mr. Murthwaite,” I began, “you were acquainted with the late Lady Verinder, and you took some interest in the strange succession of events which ended in the loss of the Moonstone?”\n\nThe eminent traveller did me the honour of waking up in an instant, and asking me who I was.\n\nI informed him of my professional connection with the Herncastle family, not forgetting the curious position which I had occupied towards the Colonel and his Diamond in the bygone time.\n\nMr. Murthwaite shifted round in his chair, so as to put the rest of the company behind him (Conservatives and Liberals alike), and concentrated his whole attention on plain Mr. Bruff, of Gray’s Inn Square.\n\n“Have you heard anything, lately, of the Indians?” he asked.\n\n“I have every reason to believe,” I answered, “that one of them had an interview with me, in my office, yesterday.”\n\nMr. Murthwaite was not an easy man to astonish; but that last answer of mine completely staggered him. I described what had happened to Mr. Luker, and what had happened to myself, exactly as I have described it here. “It is clear that the Indian’s parting inquiry had an object,” I added. “Why should he be so anxious to know the time at which a borrower of money is usually privileged to pay the money back?”\n\n“Is it possible that you don’t see his motive, Mr. Bruff?”\n\n“I am ashamed of my stupidity, Mr. Murthwaite—but I certainly don’t see it.”\n\nThe great traveller became quite interested in sounding the immense vacuity of my dulness to its lowest depths.\n\n“Let me ask you one question,” he said. “In what position does the conspiracy to seize the Moonstone now stand?”\n\n“I can’t say,” I answered. “The Indian plot is a mystery to me.”\n\n“The Indian plot, Mr. Bruff, can only be a mystery to you, because you have never seriously examined it. Shall we run it over together, from the time when you drew Colonel Herncastle’s Will, to the time when the Indian called at your office? In your position, it may be of very serious importance to the interests of Miss Verinder, that you should be able to take a clear view of this matter in case of need. Tell me, bearing that in mind, whether you will penetrate the Indian’s motive for yourself? or whether you wish me to save you the trouble of making any inquiry into it?”\n\nIt is needless to say that I thoroughly appreciated the practical purpose which I now saw that he had in view, and that the first of the two alternatives was the alternative I chose.\n\n“Very good,” said Mr. Murthwaite. “We will take the question of the ages of the three Indians first. I can testify that they all look much about the same age—and you can decide for yourself, whether the man whom you saw was, or was not, in the prime of life. Not forty, you think? My idea too. We will say not forty. Now look back to the time when Colonel Herncastle came to England, and when you were concerned in the plan he adopted to preserve his life. I don’t want you to count the years. I will only say, it is clear that these present Indians, at their age, must be the successors of three other Indians (high caste Brahmins all of them, Mr. Bruff, when they left their native country!) who followed the Colonel to these shores. Very well. These present men of ours have succeeded to the men who were here before them. If they had only done that, the matter would not have been worth inquiring into. But they have done more. They have succeeded to the organisation which their predecessors established in this country. Don’t start! The organisation is a very trumpery affair, according to our ideas, I have no doubt. I should reckon it up as including the command of money; the services, when needed, of that shady sort of Englishman, who lives in the byways of foreign life in London; and, lastly, the secret sympathy of such few men of their own country, and (formerly, at least) of their own religion, as happen to be employed in ministering to some of the multitudinous wants of this great city. Nothing very formidable, as you see! But worth notice at starting, because we may find occasion to refer to this modest little Indian organisation as we go on. Having now cleared the ground, I am going to ask you a question; and I expect your experience to answer it. What was the event which gave the Indians their first chance of seizing the Diamond?”\n\nI understood the allusion to my experience.\n\n“The first chance they got,” I replied, “was clearly offered to them by Colonel Herncastle’s death. They would be aware of his death, I suppose, as a matter of course?”\n\n“As a matter of course. And his death, as you say, gave them their first chance. Up to that time the Moonstone was safe in the strongroom of the bank. You drew the Colonel’s Will leaving his jewel to his niece; and the Will was proved in the usual way. As a lawyer, you can be at no loss to know what course the Indians would take (under English advice) after that.”\n\n“They would provide themselves with a copy of the Will from Doctors’ Commons,” I said.\n\n“Exactly. One or other of those shady Englishmen to whom I have alluded, would get them the copy you have described. That copy would inform them that the Moonstone was bequeathed to the daughter of Lady Verinder, and that Mr. Blake the elder, or some person appointed by him, was to place it in her hands. You will agree with me that the necessary information about persons in the position of Lady Verinder and Mr. Blake, would be perfectly easy information to obtain. The one difficulty for the Indians would be to decide whether they should make their attempt on the Diamond when it was in course of removal from the keeping of the bank, or whether they should wait until it was taken down to Yorkshire to Lady Verinder’s house. The second way would be manifestly the safest way—and there you have the explanation of the appearance of the Indians at Frizinghall, disguised as jugglers, and waiting their time. In London, it is needless to say, they had their organisation at their disposal to keep them informed of events. Two men would do it. One to follow anybody who went from Mr. Blake’s house to the bank. And one to treat the lower men servants with beer, and to hear the news of the house. These commonplace precautions would readily inform them that Mr. Franklin Blake had been to the bank, and that Mr. Franklin Blake was the only person in the house who was going to visit Lady Verinder. What actually followed upon that discovery, you remember, no doubt, quite as correctly as I do.”\n\nI remembered that Franklin Blake had detected one of the spies, in the street—that he had, in consequence, advanced the time of his arrival in Yorkshire by some hours—and that (thanks to old Betteredge’s excellent advice) he had lodged the Diamond in the bank at Frizinghall, before the Indians were so much as prepared to see him in the neighbourhood. All perfectly clear so far. But the Indians being ignorant of the precautions thus taken, how was it that they had made no attempt on Lady Verinder’s house (in which they must have supposed the Diamond to be) through the whole of the interval that elapsed before Rachel’s birthday?\n\nIn putting this difficulty to Mr. Murthwaite, I thought it right to add that I had heard of the little boy, and the drop of ink, and the rest of it, and that any explanation based on the theory of clairvoyance was an explanation which would carry no conviction whatever with it, to my mind.\n\n“Nor to mine either,” said Mr. Murthwaite. “The clairvoyance in this case is simply a development of the romantic side of the Indian character. It would be refreshment and an encouragement to those men—quite inconceivable, I grant you, to the English mind—to surround their wearisome and perilous errand in this country with a certain halo of the marvellous and the supernatural. Their boy is unquestionably a sensitive subject to the mesmeric influence—and, under that influence, he has no doubt reflected what was already in the mind of the person mesmerising him. I have tested the theory of clairvoyance—and I have never found the manifestations get beyond that point. The Indians don’t investigate the matter in this way; the Indians look upon their boy as a Seer of things invisible to their eyes—and, I repeat, in that marvel they find the source of a new interest in the purpose that unites them. I only notice this as offering a curious view of human character, which must be quite new to you. We have nothing whatever to do with clairvoyance, or with mesmerism, or with anything else that is hard of belief to a practical man, in the inquiry that we are now pursuing. My object in following the Indian plot, step by step, is to trace results back, by rational means, to natural causes. Have I succeeded to your satisfaction so far?”\n\n“Not a doubt of it, Mr. Murthwaite! I am waiting, however, with some anxiety, to hear the rational explanation of the difficulty which I have just had the honour of submitting to you.”\n\nMr. Murthwaite smiled. “It’s the easiest difficulty to deal with of all,” he said. “Permit me to begin by admitting your statement of the case as a perfectly correct one. The Indians were undoubtedly not aware of what Mr. Franklin Blake had done with the Diamond—for we find them making their first mistake, on the first night of Mr. Blake’s arrival at his aunt’s house.”\n\n“Their first mistake?” I repeated.\n\n“Certainly! The mistake of allowing themselves to be surprised, lurking about the terrace at night, by Gabriel Betteredge. However, they had the merit of seeing for themselves that they had taken a false step—for, as you say, again, with plenty of time at their disposal, they never came near the house for weeks afterwards.”\n\n“Why, Mr. Murthwaite? That’s what I want to know! Why?”\n\n“Because no Indian, Mr. Bruff, ever runs an unnecessary risk. The clause you drew in Colonel Herncastle’s Will, informed them (didn’t it?) that the Moonstone was to pass absolutely into Miss Verinder’s possession on her birthday. Very well. Tell me which was the safest course for men in their position? To make their attempt on the Diamond while it was under the control of Mr. Franklin Blake, who had shown already that he could suspect and outwit them? Or to wait till the Diamond was at the disposal of a young girl, who would innocently delight in wearing the magnificent jewel at every possible opportunity? Perhaps you want a proof that my theory is correct? Take the conduct of the Indians themselves as the proof. They appeared at the house, after waiting all those weeks, on Miss Verinder’s birthday; and they were rewarded for the patient accuracy of their calculations by seeing the Moonstone in the bosom of her dress! When I heard the story of the Colonel and the Diamond, later in the evening, I felt so sure about the risk Mr. Franklin Blake had run (they would have certainly attacked him, if he had not happened to ride back to Lady Verinder’s in the company of other people); and I was so strongly convinced of the worse risk still, in store for Miss Verinder, that I recommended following the Colonel’s plan, and destroying the identity of the gem by having it cut into separate stones. How its extraordinary disappearance that night, made my advice useless, and utterly defeated the Hindoo plot—and how all further action on the part of the Indians was paralysed the next day by their confinement in prison as rogues and vagabonds—you know as well as I do. The first act in the conspiracy closes there. Before we go on to the second, may I ask whether I have met your difficulty, with an explanation which is satisfactory to the mind of a practical man?”\n\nIt was impossible to deny that he had met my difficulty fairly; thanks to his superior knowledge of the Indian character—and thanks to his not having had hundreds of other Wills to think of since Colonel Herncastle’s time!\n\n“So far, so good,” resumed Mr. Murthwaite. “The first chance the Indians had of seizing the Diamond was a chance lost, on the day when they were committed to the prison at Frizinghall. When did the second chance offer itself? The second chance offered itself—as I am in a condition to prove—while they were still in confinement.”\n\nHe took out his pocket-book, and opened it at a particular leaf, before he went on.\n\n“I was staying,” he resumed, “with some friends at Frizinghall, at the time. A day or two before the Indians were set free (on a Monday, I think), the governor of the prison came to me with a letter. It had been left for the Indians by one Mrs. Macann, of whom they had hired the lodging in which they lived; and it had been delivered at Mrs. Macann’s door, in ordinary course of post, on the previous morning. The prison authorities had noticed that the postmark was ‘Lambeth,’ and that the address on the outside, though expressed in correct English, was, in form, oddly at variance with the customary method of directing a letter. On opening it, they had found the contents to be written in a foreign language, which they rightly guessed at as Hindustani. Their object in coming to me was, of course, to have the letter translated to them. I took a copy in my pocket-book of the original, and of my translation—and there they are at your service.”\n\nHe handed me the open pocket-book. The address on the letter was the first thing copied. It was all written in one paragraph, without any attempt at punctuation, thus: “To the three Indian men living with the lady called Macann at Frizinghall in Yorkshire.” The Hindoo characters followed; and the English translation appeared at the end, expressed in these mysterious words:\n\n“In the name of the Regent of the Night, whose seat is on the Antelope, whose arms embrace the four corners of the earth.\n\n“Brothers, turn your faces to the south, and come to me in the street of many noises, which leads down to the muddy river.\n\n“The reason is this.\n\n“My own eyes have seen it.”\n\nThere the letter ended, without either date or signature. I handed it back to Mr. Murthwaite, and owned that this curious specimen of Hindoo correspondence rather puzzled me.\n\n“I can explain the first sentence to you,” he said; “and the conduct of the Indians themselves will explain the rest. The god of the moon is represented, in the Hindoo mythology, as a four-armed deity, seated on an antelope; and one of his titles is the regent of the night. Here, then, to begin with, is something which looks suspiciously like an indirect reference to the Moonstone. Now, let us see what the Indians did, after the prison authorities had allowed them to receive their letter. On the very day when they were set free they went at once to the railway station, and took their places in the first train that started for London. We all thought it a pity at Frizinghall that their proceedings were not privately watched. But, after Lady Verinder had dismissed the police-officer, and had stopped all further inquiry into the loss of the Diamond, no one else could presume to stir in the matter. The Indians were free to go to London, and to London they went. What was the next news we heard of them, Mr. Bruff?”\n\n“They were annoying Mr. Luker,” I answered, “by loitering about the house at Lambeth.”\n\n“Did you read the report of Mr. Luker’s application to the magistrate?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“In the course of his statement he referred, if you remember, to a foreign workman in his employment, whom he had just dismissed on suspicion of attempted theft, and whom he also distrusted as possibly acting in collusion with the Indians who had annoyed him. The inference is pretty plain, Mr. Bruff, as to who wrote that letter which puzzled you just now, and as to which of Mr. Luker’s Oriental treasures the workman had attempted to steal.”\n\nThe inference (as I hastened to acknowledge) was too plain to need being pointed out. I had never doubted that the Moonstone had found its way into Mr. Luker’s hands, at the time Mr. Murthwaite alluded to. My only question had been, How had the Indians discovered the circumstance? This question (the most difficult to deal with of all, as I had thought) had now received its answer, like the rest. Lawyer as I was, I began to feel that I might trust Mr. Murthwaite to lead me blindfold through the last windings of the labyrinth, along which he had guided me thus far. I paid him the compliment of telling him this, and found my little concession very graciously received.\n\n“You shall give me a piece of information in your turn before we go on,” he said. “Somebody must have taken the Moonstone from Yorkshire to London. And somebody must have raised money on it, or it would never have been in Mr. Luker’s possession. Has there been any discovery made of who that person was?”\n\n“None that I know of.”\n\n“There was a story (was there not?) about Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite. I am told he is an eminent philanthropist—which is decidedly against him, to begin with.”\n\nI heartily agreed in this with Mr. Murthwaite. At the same time, I felt bound to inform him (without, it is needless to say, mentioning Miss Verinder’s name) that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite had been cleared of all suspicion, on evidence which I could answer for as entirely beyond dispute.\n\n“Very well,” said Mr. Murthwaite, quietly, “let us leave it to time to clear the matter up. In the meanwhile, Mr. Bruff, we must get back again to the Indians, on your account. Their journey to London simply ended in their becoming the victims of another defeat. The loss of their second chance of seizing the Diamond is mainly attributable, as I think, to the cunning and foresight of Mr. Luker—who doesn’t stand at the top of the prosperous and ancient profession of usury for nothing! By the prompt dismissal of the man in his employment, he deprived the Indians of the assistance which their confederate would have rendered them in getting into the house. By the prompt transport of the Moonstone to his banker’s, he took the conspirators by surprise before they were prepared with a new plan for robbing him. How the Indians, in this latter case, suspected what he had done, and how they contrived to possess themselves of his banker’s receipt, are events too recent to need dwelling on. Let it be enough to say that they know the Moonstone to be once more out of their reach; deposited (under the general description of ‘a valuable of great price’) in a banker’s strong room. Now, Mr. Bruff, what is their third chance of seizing the Diamond? and when will it come?”\n\nAs the question passed his lips, I penetrated the motive of the Indian’s visit to my office at last!\n\n“I see it!” I exclaimed. “The Indians take it for granted, as we do, that the Moonstone has been pledged; and they want to be certainly informed of the earliest period at which the pledge can be redeemed—because that will be the earliest period at which the Diamond can be removed from the safe keeping of the bank!”\n\n“I told you you would find it out for yourself, Mr. Bruff, if I only gave you a fair chance. In a year from the time when the Moonstone was pledged, the Indians will be on the watch for their third chance. Mr. Luker’s own lips have told them how long they will have to wait, and your respectable authority has satisfied them that Mr. Luker has spoken the truth. When do we suppose, at a rough guess, that the Diamond found its way into the money-lender’s hands?”\n\n“Towards the end of last June,” I answered, “as well as I can reckon it.”\n\n“And we are now in the year ’forty-eight. Very good. If the unknown person who has pledged the Moonstone can redeem it in a year, the jewel will be in that person’s possession again at the end of June, ’forty-nine. I shall be thousands of miles from England and English news at that date. But it may be worth your while to take a note of it, and to arrange to be in London at the time.”\n\n“You think something serious will happen?” I said.\n\n“I think I shall be safer,” he answered, “among the fiercest fanatics of Central Asia than I should be if I crossed the door of the bank with the Moonstone in my pocket. The Indians have been defeated twice running, Mr. Bruff. It’s my firm belief that they won’t be defeated a third time.”\n\nThose were the last words he said on the subject. The coffee came in; the guests rose, and dispersed themselves about the room; and we joined the ladies of the dinner-party upstairs.\n\nI made a note of the date, and it may not be amiss if I close my narrative by repeating that note here:\n\nJune, ’forty-nine. Expect news of the Indians, towards the end of the month.\n\nAnd that done, I hand the pen, which I have now no further claim to use, to the writer who follows me next.\n\nContributed by Franklin Blake.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nIn the spring of the year eighteen hundred and forty-nine I was wandering in the East, and had then recently altered the travelling plans which I had laid out some months before, and which I had communicated to my lawyer and my banker in London.\n\nThis change made it necessary for me to send one of my servants to obtain my letters and remittances from the English consul in a certain city, which was no longer included as one of my resting-places in my new travelling scheme. The man was to join me again at an appointed place and time. An accident, for which he was not responsible, delayed him on his errand. For a week I and my people waited, encamped on the borders of a desert. At the end of that time the missing man made his appearance, with the money and the letters, at the entrance of my tent.\n\n“I am afraid I bring you bad news, sir,” he said, and pointed to one of the letters, which had a mourning border round it, and the address on which was in the handwriting of Mr. Bruff.\n\nI know nothing, in a case of this kind, so unendurable as suspense. The letter with the mourning border was the letter that I opened first.\n\nIt informed me that my father was dead, and that I was heir to his great fortune. The wealth which had thus fallen into my hands brought its responsibilities with it, and Mr. Bruff entreated me to lose no time in returning to England.\n\nBy daybreak the next morning, I was on my way back to my own country.\n\nThe picture presented of me, by my old friend Betteredge, at the time of my departure from England, is (as I think) a little overdrawn. He has, in his own quaint way, interpreted seriously one of his young mistress’s many satirical references to my foreign education; and has persuaded himself that he actually saw those French, German, and Italian sides to my character, which my lively cousin only professed to discover in jest, and which never had any real existence, except in our good Betteredge’s own brain. But, barring this drawback, I am bound to own that he has stated no more than the truth in representing me as wounded to the heart by Rachel’s treatment, and as leaving England in the first keenness of suffering caused by the bitterest disappointment of my life.\n\nI went abroad, resolved—if change and absence could help me—to forget her. It is, I am persuaded, no true view of human nature which denies that change and absence do help a man under these circumstances; they force his attention away from the exclusive contemplation of his own sorrow. I never forgot her; but the pang of remembrance lost its worst bitterness, little by little, as time, distance, and novelty interposed themselves more and more effectually between Rachel and me.\n\nOn the other hand, it is no less certain that, with the act of turning homeward, the remedy which had gained its ground so steadily, began now, just as steadily, to drop back. The nearer I drew to the country which she inhabited, and to the prospect of seeing her again, the more irresistibly her influence began to recover its hold on me. On leaving England she was the last person in the world whose name I would have suffered to pass my lips. On returning to England, she was the first person I inquired after, when Mr. Bruff and I met again.\n\nI was informed, of course, of all that had happened in my absence; in other words, of all that has been related here in continuation of Betteredge’s narrative—one circumstance only being excepted. Mr. Bruff did not, at that time, feel himself at liberty to inform me of the motives which had privately influenced Rachel and Godfrey Ablewhite in recalling the marriage promise, on either side. I troubled him with no embarrassing questions on this delicate subject. It was relief enough to me, after the jealous disappointment caused by hearing that she had ever contemplated being Godfrey’s wife, to know that reflection had convinced her of acting rashly, and that she had effected her own release from her marriage engagement.\n\nHaving heard the story of the past, my next inquiries (still inquiries after Rachel!) advanced naturally to the present time. Under whose care had she been placed after leaving Mr. Bruff’s house? and where was she living now?\n\nShe was living under the care of a widowed sister of the late Sir John Verinder—one Mrs. Merridew—whom her mother’s executors had requested to act as guardian, and who had accepted the proposal. They were reported to me as getting on together admirably well, and as being now established, for the season, in Mrs. Merridew’s house in Portland Place.\n\nHalf an hour after receiving this information, I was on my way to Portland Place—without having had the courage to own it to Mr. Bruff!\n\nThe man who answered the door was not sure whether Miss Verinder was at home or not. I sent him upstairs with my card, as the speediest way of setting the question at rest. The man came down again with an impenetrable face, and informed me that Miss Verinder was out.\n\nI might have suspected other people of purposely denying themselves to me. But it was impossible to suspect Rachel. I left word that I would call again at six o’clock that evening.\n\nAt six o’clock I was informed for the second time that Miss Verinder was not at home. Had any message been left for me? No message had been left for me. Had Miss Verinder not received my card? The servant begged my pardon—Miss Verinder had received it.\n\nThe inference was too plain to be resisted. Rachel declined to see me.\n\nOn my side, I declined to be treated in this way, without making an attempt, at least, to discover a reason for it. I sent up my name to Mrs. Merridew, and requested her to favour me with a personal interview at any hour which it might be most convenient to her to name.\n\nMrs. Merridew made no difficulty about receiving me at once. I was shown into a comfortable little sitting-room, and found myself in the presence of a comfortable little elderly lady. She was so good as to feel great regret and much surprise, entirely on my account. She was at the same time, however, not in a position to offer me any explanation, or to press Rachel on a matter which appeared to relate to a question of private feeling alone. This was said over and over again, with a polite patience that nothing could tire; and this was all I gained by applying to Mrs. Merridew.\n\nMy last chance was to write to Rachel. My servant took a letter to her the next day, with strict instructions to wait for an answer.\n\nThe answer came back, literally in one sentence.\n\n“Miss Verinder begs to decline entering into any correspondence with Mr. Franklin Blake.”\n\nFond as I was of her, I felt indignantly the insult offered to me in that reply. Mr. Bruff came in to speak to me on business, before I had recovered possession of myself. I dismissed the business on the spot, and laid the whole case before him. He proved to be as incapable of enlightening me as Mrs. Merridew herself. I asked him if any slander had been spoken of me in Rachel’s hearing. Mr. Bruff was not aware of any slander of which I was the object. Had she referred to me in any way while she was staying under Mr. Bruff’s roof? Never. Had she not so much as asked, during all my long absence, whether I was living or dead? No such question had ever passed her lips. I took out of my pocket-book the letter which poor Lady Verinder had written to me from Frizinghall, on the day when I left her house in Yorkshire. And I pointed Mr. Bruff’s attention to these two sentences in it:\n\n“The valuable assistance which you rendered to the inquiry after the lost jewel is still an unpardoned offence, in the present dreadful state of Rachel’s mind. Moving blindfold in this matter, you have added to the burden of anxiety which she has had to bear, by innocently threatening her secret with discovery through your exertions.”\n\n“Is it possible,” I asked, “that the feeling towards me which is there described, is as bitter as ever against me now?”\n\nMr. Bruff looked unaffectedly distressed.\n\n“If you insist on an answer,” he said, “I own I can place no other interpretation on her conduct than that.”\n\nI rang the bell, and directed my servant to pack my portmanteau, and to send out for a railway guide. Mr. Bruff asked, in astonishment, what I was going to do.\n\n“I am going to Yorkshire,” I answered, “by the next train.”\n\n“May I ask for what purpose?”\n\n“Mr. Bruff, the assistance I innocently rendered to the inquiry after the Diamond was an unpardoned offence, in Rachel’s mind, nearly a year since; and it remains an unpardoned offence still. I won’t accept that position! I am determined to find out the secret of her silence towards her mother, and her enmity towards me. If time, pains, and money can do it, I will lay my hand on the thief who took the Moonstone!”\n\nThe worthy old gentleman attempted to remonstrate—to induce me to listen to reason—to do his duty towards me, in short. I was deaf to everything that he could urge. No earthly consideration would, at that moment, have shaken the resolution that was in me.\n\n“I shall take up the inquiry again,” I went on, “at the point where I dropped it; and I shall follow it onwards, step by step, till I come to the present time. There are missing links in the evidence, as I left it, which Gabriel Betteredge can supply, and to Gabriel Betteredge I go!”\n\nTowards sunset that evening I stood again on the well-remembered terrace, and looked once more at the peaceful old country house. The gardener was the first person whom I saw in the deserted grounds. He had left Betteredge, an hour since, sunning himself in the customary corner of the back yard. I knew it well; and I said I would go and seek him myself.\n\nI walked round by the familiar paths and passages, and looked in at the open gate of the yard.\n\nThere he was—the dear old friend of the happy days that were never to come again—there he was in the old corner, on the old beehive chair, with his pipe in his mouth, and his Robinson Crusoe on his lap, and his two friends, the dogs, dozing on either side of him! In the position in which I stood, my shadow was projected in front of me by the last slanting rays of the sun. Either the dogs saw it, or their keen scent informed them of my approach; they started up with a growl. Starting in his turn, the old man quieted them by a word, and then shaded his failing eyes with his hand, and looked inquiringly at the figure at the gate.\n\nMy own eyes were full of tears. I was obliged to wait a moment before I could trust myself to speak to him.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n“Betteredge!” I said, pointing to the well-remembered book on his knee, “has Robinson Crusoe informed you, this evening, that you might expect to see Franklin Blake?”\n\n“By the lord Harry, Mr. Franklin!” cried the old man, “that’s exactly what Robinson Crusoe has done!”\n\nHe struggled to his feet with my assistance, and stood for a moment, looking backwards and forwards between Robinson Crusoe and me, apparently at a loss to discover which of us had surprised him most. The verdict ended in favour of the book. Holding it open before him in both hands, he surveyed the wonderful volume with a stare of unutterable anticipation—as if he expected to see Robinson Crusoe himself walk out of the pages, and favour us with a personal interview.\n\n“Here’s the bit, Mr. Franklin!” he said, as soon as he had recovered the use of his speech. “As I live by bread, sir, here’s the bit I was reading, the moment before you came in! Page one hundred and fifty-six as follows:—‘I stood like one Thunderstruck, or as if I had seen an Apparition.’ If that isn’t as much as to say: ‘Expect the sudden appearance of Mr. Franklin Blake’—there’s no meaning in the English language!” said Betteredge, closing the book with a bang, and getting one of his hands free at last to take the hand which I offered him.\n\nI had expected him, naturally enough under the circumstances, to overwhelm me with questions. But no—the hospitable impulse was the uppermost impulse in the old servant’s mind, when a member of the family appeared (no matter how!) as a visitor at the house.\n\n“Walk in, Mr. Franklin,” he said, opening the door behind him, with his quaint old-fashioned bow. “I’ll ask what brings you here afterwards—I must make you comfortable first. There have been sad changes, since you went away. The house is shut up, and the servants are gone. Never mind that! I’ll cook your dinner; and the gardener’s wife will make your bed—and if there’s a bottle of our famous Latour claret left in the cellar, down your throat, Mr. Franklin, that bottle shall go. I bid you welcome, sir! I bid you heartily welcome!” said the poor old fellow, fighting manfully against the gloom of the deserted house, and receiving me with the sociable and courteous attention of the bygone time.\n\nIt vexed me to disappoint him. But the house was Rachel’s house, now. Could I eat in it, or sleep in it, after what had happened in London? The commonest sense of self-respect forbade me—properly forbade me—to cross the threshold.\n\nI took Betteredge by the arm, and led him out into the garden. There was no help for it. I was obliged to tell him the truth. Between his attachment to Rachel, and his attachment to me, he was sorely puzzled and distressed at the turn things had taken. His opinion, when he expressed it, was given in his usual downright manner, and was agreeably redolent of the most positive philosophy I know—the philosophy of the Betteredge school.\n\n“Miss Rachel has her faults—I’ve never denied it,” he began. “And riding the high horse, now and then, is one of them. She has been trying to ride over you—and you have put up with it. Lord, Mr. Franklin, don’t you know women by this time better than that? You have heard me talk of the late Mrs. Betteredge?”\n\nI had heard him talk of the late Mrs. Betteredge pretty often—invariably producing her as his one undeniable example of the inbred frailty and perversity of the other sex. In that capacity he exhibited her now.\n\n“Very well, Mr. Franklin. Now listen to me. Different women have different ways of riding the high horse. The late Mrs. Betteredge took her exercise on that favourite female animal whenever I happened to deny her anything that she had set her heart on. So sure as I came home from my work on these occasions, so sure was my wife to call to me up the kitchen stairs, and to say that, after my brutal treatment of her, she hadn’t the heart to cook me my dinner. I put up with it for some time—just as you are putting up with it now from Miss Rachel. At last my patience wore out. I went downstairs, and I took Mrs. Betteredge—affectionately, you understand—up in my arms, and carried her, holus-bolus, into the best parlour where she received her company. I said ‘That’s the right place for you, my dear,’ and so went back to the kitchen. I locked myself in, and took off my coat, and turned up my shirt-sleeves, and cooked my own dinner. When it was done, I served it up in my best manner, and enjoyed it most heartily. I had my pipe and my drop of grog afterwards; and then I cleared the table, and washed the crockery, and cleaned the knives and forks, and put the things away, and swept up the hearth. When things were as bright and clean again, as bright and clean could be, I opened the door and let Mrs. Betteredge in. ‘I’ve had my dinner, my dear,’ I said; ‘and I hope you will find that I have left the kitchen all that your fondest wishes can desire.’ For the rest of that woman’s life, Mr. Franklin, I never had to cook my dinner again! Moral: You have put up with Miss Rachel in London; don’t put up with her in Yorkshire. Come back to the house!”\n\nQuite unanswerable! I could only assure my good friend that even his powers of persuasion were, in this case, thrown away on me.\n\n“It’s a lovely evening,” I said. “I shall walk to Frizinghall, and stay at the hotel, and you must come tomorrow morning and breakfast with me. I have something to say to you.”\n\nBetteredge shook his head gravely.\n\n“I am heartily sorry for this,” he said. “I had hoped, Mr. Franklin, to hear that things were all smooth and pleasant again between you and Miss Rachel. If you must have your own way, sir,” he continued, after a moment’s reflection, “there is no need to go to Frizinghall tonight for a bed. It’s to be had nearer than that. There’s Hotherstone’s Farm, barely two miles from here. You can hardly object to that on Miss Rachel’s account,” the old man added slily. “Hotherstone lives, Mr. Franklin, on his own freehold.”\n\nI remembered the place the moment Betteredge mentioned it. The farm-house stood in a sheltered inland valley, on the banks of the prettiest stream in that part of Yorkshire: and the farmer had a spare bedroom and parlour, which he was accustomed to let to artists, anglers, and tourists in general. A more agreeable place of abode, during my stay in the neighbourhood, I could not have wished to find.\n\n“Are the rooms to let?” I inquired.\n\n“Mrs. Hotherstone herself, sir, asked for my good word to recommend the rooms, yesterday.”\n\n“I’ll take them, Betteredge, with the greatest pleasure.”\n\nWe went back to the yard, in which I had left my travelling-bag. After putting a stick through the handle, and swinging the bag over his shoulder, Betteredge appeared to relapse into the bewilderment which my sudden appearance had caused, when I surprised him in the beehive chair. He looked incredulously at the house, and then he wheeled about, and looked more incredulously still at me.\n\n“I’ve lived a goodish long time in the world,” said this best and dearest of all old servants—“but the like of this, I never did expect to see. There stands the house, and here stands Mr. Franklin Blake—and, Damme, if one of them isn’t turning his back on the other, and going to sleep in a lodging!”\n\nHe led the way out, wagging his head and growling ominously. “There’s only one more miracle that can happen,” he said to me, over his shoulder. “The next thing you’ll do, Mr. Franklin, will be to pay me back that seven-and-sixpence you borrowed of me when you were a boy.”\n\nThis stroke of sarcasm put him in a better humour with himself and with me. We left the house, and passed through the lodge gates. Once clear of the grounds, the duties of hospitality (in Betteredge’s code of morals) ceased, and the privileges of curiosity began.\n\nHe dropped back, so as to let me get on a level with him. “Fine evening for a walk, Mr. Franklin,” he said, as if we had just accidentally encountered each other at that moment. “Supposing you had gone to the hotel at Frizinghall, sir?”\n\n“Yes?”\n\n“I should have had the honour of breakfasting with you, tomorrow morning.”\n\n“Come and breakfast with me at Hotherstone’s Farm, instead.”\n\n“Much obliged to you for your kindness, Mr. Franklin. But it wasn’t exactly breakfast that I was driving at. I think you mentioned that you had something to say to me? If it’s no secret, sir,” said Betteredge, suddenly abandoning the crooked way, and taking the straight one, “I’m burning to know what’s brought you down here, if you please, in this sudden way.”\n\n“What brought me here before?” I asked.\n\n“The Moonstone, Mr. Franklin. But what brings you now, sir?”\n\n“The Moonstone again, Betteredge.”\n\nThe old man suddenly stood still, and looked at me in the grey twilight as if he suspected his own ears of deceiving him.\n\n“If that’s a joke, sir,” he said, “I’m afraid I’m getting a little dull in my old age. I don’t take it.”\n\n“It’s no joke,” I answered. “I have come here to take up the inquiry which was dropped when I left England. I have come here to do what nobody has done yet—to find out who took the Diamond.”\n\n“Let the Diamond be, Mr. Franklin! Take my advice, and let the Diamond be! That cursed Indian jewel has misguided everybody who has come near it. Don’t waste your money and your temper—in the fine spring time of your life, sir—by meddling with the Moonstone. How can you hope to succeed (saving your presence), when Sergeant Cuff himself made a mess of it? Sergeant Cuff!” repeated Betteredge, shaking his forefinger at me sternly. “The greatest policeman in England!”\n\n“My mind is made up, my old friend. Even Sergeant Cuff doesn’t daunt me. By-the-bye, I may want to speak to him, sooner or later. Have you heard anything of him lately?”\n\n“The Sergeant won’t help you, Mr. Franklin.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“There has been an event, sir, in the police-circles, since you went away. The great Cuff has retired from business. He has got a little cottage at Dorking; and he’s up to his eyes in the growing of roses. I have it in his own handwriting, Mr. Franklin. He has grown the white moss rose, without budding it on the dog-rose first. And Mr. Begbie the gardener is to go to Dorking, and own that the Sergeant has beaten him at last.”\n\n“It doesn’t much matter,” I said. “I must do without Sergeant Cuff’s help. And I must trust to you, at starting.”\n\nIt is likely enough that I spoke rather carelessly.\n\nAt any rate, Betteredge seemed to be piqued by something in the reply which I had just made to him. “You might trust to worse than me, Mr. Franklin—I can tell you that,” he said a little sharply.\n\nThe tone in which he retorted, and a certain disturbance, after he had spoken, which I detected in his manner, suggested to me that he was possessed of some information which he hesitated to communicate.\n\n“I expect you to help me,” I said, “in picking up the fragments of evidence which Sergeant Cuff has left behind him. I know you can do that. Can you do no more?”\n\n“What more can you expect from me, sir?” asked Betteredge, with an appearance of the utmost humility.\n\n“I expect more—from what you said just now.”\n\n“Mere boasting, Mr. Franklin,” returned the old man obstinately. “Some people are born boasters, and they never get over it to their dying day. I’m one of them.”\n\nThere was only one way to take with him. I appealed to his interest in Rachel, and his interest in me.\n\n“Betteredge, would you be glad to hear that Rachel and I were good friends again?”\n\n“I have served your family, sir, to mighty little purpose, if you doubt it!”\n\n“Do you remember how Rachel treated me, before I left England?”\n\n“As well as if it was yesterday! My lady herself wrote you a letter about it; and you were so good as to show the letter to me. It said that Miss Rachel was mortally offended with you, for the part you had taken in trying to recover her jewel. And neither my lady, nor you, nor anybody else could guess why.\n\n“Quite true, Betteredge! And I come back from my travels, and find her mortally offended with me still. I knew that the Diamond was at the bottom of it, last year, and I know that the Diamond is at the bottom of it now. I have tried to speak to her, and she won’t see me. I have tried to write to her, and she won’t answer me. How, in Heaven’s name, am I to clear the matter up? The chance of searching into the loss of the Moonstone, is the one chance of inquiry that Rachel herself has left me.”\n\nThose words evidently put the case before him, as he had not seen it yet. He asked a question which satisfied me that I had shaken him.\n\n“There is no ill-feeling in this, Mr. Franklin, on your side—is there?”\n\n“There was some anger,” I answered, “when I left London. But that is all worn out now. I want to make Rachel come to an understanding with me—and I want nothing more.”\n\n“You don’t feel any fear, sir—supposing you make any discoveries—in regard to what you may find out about Miss Rachel?”\n\nI understood the jealous belief in his young mistress which prompted those words.\n\n“I am as certain of her as you are,” I answered. “The fullest disclosure of her secret will reveal nothing that can alter her place in your estimation, or in mine.”\n\nBetteredge’s last-left scruples vanished at that.\n\n“If I am doing wrong to help you, Mr. Franklin,” he exclaimed, “all I can say is—I am as innocent of seeing it as the babe unborn! I can put you on the road to discovery, if you can only go on by yourself. You remember that poor girl of ours—Rosanna Spearman?”\n\n“Of course!”\n\n“You always thought she had some sort of confession in regard to this matter of the Moonstone, which she wanted to make to you?”\n\n“I certainly couldn’t account for her strange conduct in any other way.”\n\n“You may set that doubt at rest, Mr. Franklin, whenever you please.”\n\nIt was my turn to come to a standstill now. I tried vainly, in the gathering darkness, to see his face. In the surprise of the moment, I asked a little impatiently what he meant.\n\n“Steady, sir!” proceeded Betteredge. “I mean what I say. Rosanna Spearman left a sealed letter behind her—a letter addressed to you.”\n\n“Where is it?”\n\n“In the possession of a friend of hers, at Cobb’s Hole. You must have heard tell, when you were here last, sir, of Limping Lucy—a lame girl with a crutch.”\n\n“The fisherman’s daughter?”\n\n“The same, Mr. Franklin.”\n\n“Why wasn’t the letter forwarded to me?”\n\n“Limping Lucy has a will of her own, sir. She wouldn’t give it into any hands but yours. And you had left England before I could write to you.”\n\n“Let’s go back, Betteredge, and get it at once!”\n\n“Too late, sir, tonight. They’re great savers of candles along our coast; and they go to bed early at Cobb’s Hole.”\n\n“Nonsense! We might get there in half an hour.”\n\n“You might, sir. And when you did get there, you would find the door locked.” He pointed to a light, glimmering below us; and, at the same moment, I heard through the stillness of the evening the bubbling of a stream. “There’s the Farm, Mr. Franklin! Make yourself comfortable for tonight, and come to me tomorrow morning—if you’ll be so kind?”\n\n“You will go with me to the fisherman’s cottage?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Early?”\n\n“As early, Mr. Franklin, as you like.”\n\nWe descended the path that led to the Farm.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\nI have only the most indistinct recollection of what happened at Hotherstone’s Farm.\n\nI remember a hearty welcome; a prodigious supper, which would have fed a whole village in the East; a delightfully clean bedroom, with nothing in it to regret but that detestable product of the folly of our forefathers—a feather-bed; a restless night, with much kindling of matches, and many lightings of one little candle; and an immense sensation of relief when the sun rose, and there was a prospect of getting up.\n\nIt had been arranged over-night with Betteredge, that I was to call for him, on our way to Cobb’s Hole, as early as I liked—which, interpreted by my impatience to get possession of the letter, meant as early as I could. Without waiting for breakfast at the Farm, I took a crust of bread in my hand, and set forth, in some doubt whether I should not surprise the excellent Betteredge in his bed. To my great relief he proved to be quite as excited about the coming event as I was. I found him ready, and waiting for me, with his stick in his hand.\n\n“How are you this morning, Betteredge?”\n\n“Very poorly, sir.”\n\n“Sorry to hear it. What do you complain of?”\n\n“I complain of a new disease, Mr. Franklin, of my own inventing. I don’t want to alarm you, but you’re certain to catch it before the morning is out.”\n\n“The devil I am!”\n\n“Do you feel an uncomfortable heat at the pit of your stomach, sir? and a nasty thumping at the top of your head? Ah! not yet? It will lay hold of you at Cobb’s Hole, Mr. Franklin. I call it the detective-fever; and I first caught it in the company of Sergeant Cuff.”\n\n“Aye! aye! and the cure in this instance is to open Rosanna Spearman’s letter, I suppose? Come along, and let’s get it.”\n\nEarly as it was, we found the fisherman’s wife astir in her kitchen. On my presentation by Betteredge, good Mrs. Yolland performed a social ceremony, strictly reserved (as I afterwards learnt) for strangers of distinction. She put a bottle of Dutch gin and a couple of clean pipes on the table, and opened the conversation by saying, “What news from London, sir?”\n\nBefore I could find an answer to this immensely comprehensive question, an apparition advanced towards me, out of a dark corner of the kitchen. A wan, wild, haggard girl, with remarkably beautiful hair, and with a fierce keenness in her eyes, came limping up on a crutch to the table at which I was sitting, and looked at me as if I was an object of mingled interest and horror, which it quite fascinated her to see.\n\n“Mr. Betteredge,” she said, without taking her eyes off me, “mention his name again, if you please.”\n\n“This gentleman’s name,” answered Betteredge (with a strong emphasis on gentleman), “is Mr. Franklin Blake.”\n\nThe girl turned her back on me, and suddenly left the room. Good Mrs. Yolland—as I believe—made some apologies for her daughter’s odd behaviour, and Betteredge (probably) translated them into polite English. I speak of this in complete uncertainty. My attention was absorbed in following the sound of the girl’s crutch. Thump-thump, up the wooden stairs; thump-thump across the room above our heads; thump-thump down the stairs again—and there stood the apparition at the open door, with a letter in its hand, beckoning me out!\n\nI left more apologies in course of delivery behind me, and followed this strange creature—limping on before me, faster and faster—down the slope of the beach. She led me behind some boats, out of sight and hearing of the few people in the fishing-village, and then stopped, and faced me for the first time.\n\n“Stand there,” she said, “I want to look at you.”\n\nThere was no mistaking the expression on her face. I inspired her with the strongest emotions of abhorrence and disgust. Let me not be vain enough to say that no woman had ever looked at me in this manner before. I will only venture on the more modest assertion that no woman had ever let me perceive it yet. There is a limit to the length of the inspection which a man can endure, under certain circumstances. I attempted to direct Limping Lucy’s attention to some less revolting object than my face.\n\n“I think you have got a letter to give me,” I began. “Is it the letter there, in your hand?”\n\n“Say that again,” was the only answer I received.\n\nI repeated the words, like a good child learning its lesson.\n\n“No,” said the girl, speaking to herself, but keeping her eyes still mercilessly fixed on me. “I can’t find out what she saw in his face. I can’t guess what she heard in his voice.” She suddenly looked away from me, and rested her head wearily on the top of her crutch. “Oh, my poor dear!” she said, in the first soft tones which had fallen from her, in my hearing. “Oh, my lost darling! what could you see in this man?” She lifted her head again fiercely, and looked at me once more. “Can you eat and drink?” she asked.\n\nI did my best to preserve my gravity, and answered, “Yes.”\n\n“Can you sleep?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“When you see a poor girl in service, do you feel no remorse?”\n\n“Certainly not. Why should I?”\n\nShe abruptly thrust the letter (as the phrase is) into my face.\n\n“Take it!” she exclaimed furiously. “I never set eyes on you before. God Almighty forbid I should ever set eyes on you again.”\n\nWith those parting words she limped away from me at the top of her speed. The one interpretation that I could put on her conduct has, no doubt, been anticipated by everybody. I could only suppose that she was mad.\n\nHaving reached that inevitable conclusion, I turned to the more interesting object of investigation which was presented to me by Rosanna Spearman’s letter. The address was written as follows:—“For Franklin Blake, Esq. To be given into his own hands (and not to be trusted to anyone else), by Lucy Yolland.”\n\nI broke the seal. The envelope contained a letter: and this, in its turn, contained a slip of paper. I read the letter first:—\n\n“Sir,—If you are curious to know the meaning of my behaviour to you, whilst you were staying in the house of my mistress, Lady Verinder, do what you are told to do in the memorandum enclosed with this—and do it without any person being present to overlook you. Your humble servant,\n\n“ROSANNA SPEARMAN.”\n\nI turned to the slip of paper next. Here is the literal copy of it, word for word:\n\n“Memorandum:—To go to the Shivering Sand at the turn of the tide. To walk out on the South Spit, until I get the South Spit Beacon, and the flagstaff at the Coast-guard station above Cobb’s Hole in a line together. To lay down on the rocks, a stick, or any straight thing to guide my hand, exactly in the line of the beacon and the flagstaff. To take care, in doing this, that one end of the stick shall be at the edge of the rocks, on the side of them which overlooks the quicksand. To feel along the stick, among the seaweed (beginning from the end of the stick which points towards the beacon), for the Chain. To run my hand along the Chain, when found, until I come to the part of it which stretches over the edge of the rocks, down into the quicksand. And then, to pull the chain.”\n\nJust as I had read the last words—underlined in the original—I heard the voice of Betteredge behind me. The inventor of the detective-fever had completely succumbed to that irresistible malady. “I can’t stand it any longer, Mr. Franklin. What does her letter say? For mercy’s sake, sir, tell us, what does her letter say?”\n\nI handed him the letter, and the memorandum. He read the first without appearing to be much interested in it. But the second—the memorandum—produced a strong impression on him.\n\n“The Sergeant said it!” cried Betteredge. “From first to last, sir, the Sergeant said she had got a memorandum of the hiding-place. And here it is! Lord save us, Mr. Franklin, here is the secret that puzzled everybody, from the great Cuff downwards, ready and waiting, as one may say, to show itself to you! It’s the ebb now, sir, as anybody may see for themselves. How long will it be till the turn of the tide?” He looked up, and observed a lad at work, at some little distance from us, mending a net. “Tammie Bright!” he shouted at the top of his voice.\n\n“I hear you!” Tammie shouted back.\n\n“When’s the turn of the tide?”\n\n“In an hour’s time.”\n\nWe both looked at our watches.\n\n“We can go round by the coast, Mr. Franklin,” said Betteredge; “and get to the quicksand in that way with plenty of time to spare. What do you say, sir?”\n\n“Come along!”\n\nOn our way to the Shivering Sand, I applied to Betteredge to revive my memory of events (as affecting Rosanna Spearman) at the period of Sergeant Cuff’s inquiry. With my old friend’s help, I soon had the succession of circumstances clearly registered in my mind. Rosanna’s journey to Frizinghall, when the whole household believed her to be ill in her own room—Rosanna’s mysterious employment of the night-time with her door locked, and her candle burning till the morning—Rosanna’s suspicious purchase of the japanned tin case, and the two dog’s chains from Mrs. Yolland—the Sergeant’s positive conviction that Rosanna had hidden something at the Shivering Sand, and the Sergeant’s absolute ignorance as to what that something might be—all these strange results of the abortive inquiry into the loss of the Moonstone were clearly present to me again, when we reached the quicksand, and walked out together on the low ledge of rocks called the South Spit.\n\nWith Betteredge’s help, I soon stood in the right position to see the Beacon and the Coast-guard flagstaff in a line together. Following the memorandum as our guide, we next laid my stick in the necessary direction, as neatly as we could, on the uneven surface of the rocks. And then we looked at our watches once more.\n\nIt wanted nearly twenty minutes yet of the turn of the tide. I suggested waiting through this interval on the beach, instead of on the wet and slippery surface of the rocks. Having reached the dry sand, I prepared to sit down; and, greatly to my surprise, Betteredge prepared to leave me.\n\n“What are you going away for?” I asked.\n\n“Look at the letter again, sir, and you will see.”\n\nA glance at the letter reminded me that I was charged, when I made my discovery, to make it alone.\n\n“It’s hard enough for me to leave you, at such a time as this,” said Betteredge. “But she died a dreadful death, poor soul—and I feel a kind of call on me, Mr. Franklin, to humour that fancy of hers. Besides,” he added, confidentially, “there’s nothing in the letter against your letting out the secret afterwards. I’ll hang about in the fir-plantation, and wait till you pick me up. Don’t be longer than you can help, sir. The detective-fever isn’t an easy disease to deal with, under these circumstances.”\n\nWith that parting caution, he left me.\n\nThe interval of expectation, short as it was when reckoned by the measure of time, assumed formidable proportions when reckoned by the measure of suspense. This was one of the occasions on which the invaluable habit of smoking becomes especially precious and consolatory. I lit a cigar, and sat down on the slope of the beach.\n\nThe sunlight poured its unclouded beauty on every object that I could see. The exquisite freshness of the air made the mere act of living and breathing a luxury. Even the lonely little bay welcomed the morning with a show of cheerfulness; and the bared wet surface of the quicksand itself, glittering with a golden brightness, hid the horror of its false brown face under a passing smile. It was the finest day I had seen since my return to England.\n\nThe turn of the tide came, before my cigar was finished. I saw the preliminary heaving of the Sand, and then the awful shiver that crept over its surface—as if some spirit of terror lived and moved and shuddered in the fathomless deeps beneath. I threw away my cigar, and went back again to the rocks.\n\nMy directions in the memorandum instructed me to feel along the line traced by the stick, beginning with the end which was nearest to the beacon.\n\nI advanced, in this manner, more than half way along the stick, without encountering anything but the edges of the rocks. An inch or two further on, however, my patience was rewarded. In a narrow little fissure, just within reach of my forefinger, I felt the chain. Attempting, next, to follow it, by touch, in the direction of the quicksand, I found my progress stopped by a thick growth of seaweed—which had fastened itself into the fissure, no doubt, in the time that had elapsed since Rosanna Spearman had chosen her hiding-place.\n\nIt was equally impossible to pull up the seaweed, or to force my hand through it. After marking the spot indicated by the end of the stick which was placed nearest to the quicksand, I determined to pursue the search for the chain on a plan of my own. My idea was to “sound” immediately under the rocks, on the chance of recovering the lost trace of the chain at the point at which it entered the sand. I took up the stick, and knelt down on the brink of the South Spit.\n\nIn this position, my face was within a few feet of the surface of the quicksand. The sight of it so near me, still disturbed at intervals by its hideous shivering fit, shook my nerves for the moment. A horrible fancy that the dead woman might appear on the scene of her suicide, to assist my search—an unutterable dread of seeing her rise through the heaving surface of the sand, and point to the place—forced itself into my mind, and turned me cold in the warm sunlight. I own I closed my eyes at the moment when the point of the stick first entered the quicksand.\n\nThe instant afterwards, before the stick could have been submerged more than a few inches, I was free from the hold of my own superstitious terror, and was throbbing with excitement from head to foot. Sounding blindfold, at my first attempt—at that first attempt I had sounded right! The stick struck the chain.\n\nTaking a firm hold of the roots of the seaweed with my left hand, I laid myself down over the brink, and felt with my right hand under the overhanging edges of the rock. My right hand found the chain.\n\nI drew it up without the slightest difficulty. And there was the japanned tin case fastened to the end of it.\n\nThe action of the water had so rusted the chain, that it was impossible for me to unfasten it from the hasp which attached it to the case. Putting the case between my knees and exerting my utmost strength, I contrived to draw off the cover. Some white substance filled the whole interior when I looked in. I put in my hand, and found it to be linen.\n\nIn drawing out the linen, I also drew out a letter crumpled up with it. After looking at the direction, and discovering that it bore my name, I put the letter in my pocket, and completely removed the linen. It came out in a thick roll, moulded, of course, to the shape of the case in which it had been so long confined, and perfectly preserved from any injury by the sea.\n\nI carried the linen to the dry sand of the beach, and there unrolled and smoothed it out. There was no mistaking it as an article of dress. It was a nightgown.\n\nThe uppermost side, when I spread it out, presented to view innumerable folds and creases, and nothing more. I tried the undermost side, next—and instantly discovered the smear of the paint from the door of Rachel’s boudoir!\n\nMy eyes remained riveted on the stain, and my mind took me back at a leap from present to past. The very words of Sergeant Cuff recurred to me, as if the man himself was at my side again, pointing to the unanswerable inference which he drew from the smear on the door.\n\n“Find out whether there is any article of dress in this house with the stain of paint on it. Find out who that dress belongs to. Find out how the person can account for having been in the room, and smeared the paint between midnight and three in the morning. If the person can’t satisfy you, you haven’t far to look for the hand that took the Diamond.”\n\nOne after another those words travelled over my memory, repeating themselves again and again with a wearisome, mechanical reiteration. I was roused from what felt like a trance of many hours—from what was really, no doubt, the pause of a few moments only—by a voice calling to me. I looked up, and saw that Betteredge’s patience had failed him at last. He was just visible between the sandhills, returning to the beach.\n\nThe old man’s appearance recalled me, the moment I perceived it, to my sense of present things, and reminded me that the inquiry which I had pursued thus far still remained incomplete. I had discovered the smear on the nightgown. To whom did the nightgown belong?\n\nMy first impulse was to consult the letter in my pocket—the letter which I had found in the case.\n\nAs I raised my hand to take it out, I remembered that there was a shorter way to discovery than this. The nightgown itself would reveal the truth, for, in all probability, the nightgown was marked with its owner’s name.\n\nI took it up from the sand, and looked for the mark.\n\nI found the mark, and read—MY OWN NAME.\n\nThere were the familiar letters which told me that the nightgown was mine. I looked up from them. There was the sun; there were the glittering waters of the bay; there was old Betteredge, advancing nearer and nearer to me. I looked back again at the letters. My own name. Plainly confronting me—my own name.\n\n“If time, pains, and money can do it, I will lay my hand on the thief who took the Moonstone.”—I had left London, with those words on my lips. I had penetrated the secret which the quicksand had kept from every other living creature. And, on the unanswerable evidence of the paint-stain, I had discovered Myself as the Thief.\n\nI have not a word to say about my own sensations.\n\nMy impression is that the shock inflicted on me completely suspended my thinking and feeling power. I certainly could not have known what I was about when Betteredge joined me—for I have it on his authority that I laughed, when he asked what was the matter, and putting the nightgown into his hands, told him to read the riddle for himself.\n\nOf what was said between us on the beach, I have not the faintest recollection. The first place in which I can now see myself again plainly is the plantation of firs. Betteredge and I are walking back together to the house; and Betteredge is telling me that I shall be able to face it, and he will be able to face it, when we have had a glass of grog.\n\nThe scene shifts from the plantation, to Betteredge’s little sitting-room. My resolution not to enter Rachel’s house is forgotten. I feel gratefully the coolness and shadiness and quiet of the room. I drink the grog (a perfectly new luxury to me, at that time of day), which my good old friend mixes with icy-cold water from the well. Under any other circumstances, the drink would simply stupefy me. As things are, it strings up my nerves. I begin to “face it,” as Betteredge has predicted. And Betteredge, on his side, begins to “face it,” too.\n\nThe picture which I am now presenting of myself, will, I suspect, be thought a very strange one, to say the least of it. Placed in a situation which may, I think, be described as entirely without parallel, what is the first proceeding to which I resort? Do I seclude myself from all human society? Do I set my mind to analyse the abominable impossibility which, nevertheless, confronts me as an undeniable fact? Do I hurry back to London by the first train to consult the highest authorities, and to set a searching inquiry on foot immediately? No. I accept the shelter of a house which I had resolved never to degrade myself by entering again; and I sit, tippling spirits and water in the company of an old servant, at ten o’clock in the morning. Is this the conduct that might have been expected from a man placed in my horrible position? I can only answer that the sight of old Betteredge’s familiar face was an inexpressible comfort to me, and that the drinking of old Betteredge’s grog helped me, as I believe nothing else would have helped me, in the state of complete bodily and mental prostration into which I had fallen. I can only offer this excuse for myself; and I can only admire that invariable preservation of dignity, and that strictly logical consistency of conduct which distinguish every man and woman who may read these lines, in every emergency of their lives from the cradle to the grave.\n\n“Now, Mr. Franklin, there’s one thing certain, at any rate,” said Betteredge, throwing the nightgown down on the table between us, and pointing to it as if it was a living creature that could hear him. “He’s a liar, to begin with.”\n\nThis comforting view of the matter was not the view that presented itself to my mind.\n\n“I am as innocent of all knowledge of having taken the Diamond as you are,” I said. “But there is the witness against me! The paint on the nightgown, and the name on the nightgown are facts.”\n\nBetteredge lifted my glass, and put it persuasively into my hand.\n\n“Facts?” he repeated. “Take a drop more grog, Mr. Franklin, and you’ll get over the weakness of believing in facts! Foul play, sir!” he continued, dropping his voice confidentially. “That is how I read the riddle. Foul play somewhere—and you and I must find it out. Was there nothing else in the tin case, when you put your hand into it?”\n\nThe question instantly reminded me of the letter in my pocket. I took it out, and opened it. It was a letter of many pages, closely written. I looked impatiently for the signature at the end. “Rosanna Spearman.”\n\nAs I read the name, a sudden remembrance illuminated my mind, and a sudden suspicion rose out of the new light.\n\n“Stop!” I exclaimed. “Rosanna Spearman came to my aunt out of a reformatory? Rosanna Spearman had once been a thief?”\n\n“There’s no denying that, Mr. Franklin. What of it now, if you please?”\n\n“What of it now? How do we know she may not have stolen the Diamond after all? How do we know she may not have smeared my nightgown purposely with the paint?”\n\nBetteredge laid his hand on my arm, and stopped me before I could say any more.\n\n“You will be cleared of this, Mr. Franklin, beyond all doubt. But I hope you won’t be cleared in that way. See what the letter says, sir. In justice to the girl’s memory, see what it says.”\n\nI felt the earnestness with which he spoke—felt it as a friendly rebuke to me. “You shall form your own judgment on her letter,” I said. “I will read it out.”\n\nI began—and read these lines:\n\n“Sir—I have something to own to you. A confession which means much misery, may sometimes be made in very few words. This confession can be made in three words. I love you.”\n\nThe letter dropped from my hand. I looked at Betteredge. “In the name of Heaven,” I said, “what does it mean?”\n\nHe seemed to shrink from answering the question.\n\n“You and Limping Lucy were alone together this morning, sir,” he said. “Did she say nothing about Rosanna Spearman?”\n\n“She never even mentioned Rosanna Spearman’s name.”\n\n“Please to go back to the letter, Mr. Franklin. I tell you plainly, I can’t find it in my heart to distress you, after what you have had to bear already. Let her speak for herself, sir. And get on with your grog. For your own sake, get on with your grog.”\n\nI resumed the reading of the letter.\n\n“It would be very disgraceful to me to tell you this, if I was a living woman when you read it. I shall be dead and gone, sir, when you find my letter. It is that which makes me bold. Not even my grave will be left to tell of me. I may own the truth—with the quicksand waiting to hide me when the words are written.\n\n“Besides, you will find your nightgown in my hiding-place, with the smear of the paint on it; and you will want to know how it came to be hidden by me? and why I said nothing to you about it in my life-time? I have only one reason to give. I did these strange things, because I loved you.\n\n“I won’t trouble you with much about myself, or my life, before you came to my lady’s house. Lady Verinder took me out of a reformatory. I had gone to the reformatory from the prison. I was put in the prison, because I was a thief. I was a thief, because my mother went on the streets when I was quite a little girl. My mother went on the streets, because the gentleman who was my father deserted her. There is no need to tell such a common story as this, at any length. It is told quite often enough in the newspapers.\n\n“Lady Verinder was very kind to me, and Mr. Betteredge was very kind to me. Those two, and the matron at the reformatory, are the only good people I have ever met with in all my life. I might have got on in my place—not happily—but I might have got on, if you had not come visiting. I don’t blame you, sir. It’s my fault—all my fault.\n\n“Do you remember when you came out on us from among the sandhills, that morning, looking for Mr. Betteredge? You were like a prince in a fairy-story. You were like a lover in a dream. You were the most adorable human creature I had ever seen. Something that felt like the happy life I had never led yet, leapt up in me at the instant I set eyes on you. Don’t laugh at this if you can help it. Oh, if I could only make you feel how serious it is to me!\n\n“I went back to the house, and wrote your name and mine in my work-box, and drew a true lovers’ knot under them. Then, some devil—no, I ought to say some good angel—whispered to me, ‘Go and look in the glass.’ The glass told me—never mind what. I was too foolish to take the warning. I went on getting fonder and fonder of you, just as if I was a lady in your own rank of life, and the most beautiful creature your eyes ever rested on. I tried—oh, dear, how I tried—to get you to look at me. If you had known how I used to cry at night with the misery and the mortification of your never taking any notice of me, you would have pitied me perhaps, and have given me a look now and then to live on.\n\n“It would have been no very kind look, perhaps, if you had known how I hated Miss Rachel. I believe I found out you were in love with her, before you knew it yourself. She used to give you roses to wear in your button-hole. Ah, Mr. Franklin, you wore my roses oftener than either you or she thought! The only comfort I had at that time, was putting my rose secretly in your glass of water, in place of hers—and then throwing her rose away.\n\n“If she had been really as pretty as you thought her, I might have borne it better. No; I believe I should have been more spiteful against her still. Suppose you put Miss Rachel into a servant’s dress, and took her ornaments off? I don’t know what is the use of my writing in this way. It can’t be denied that she had a bad figure; she was too thin. But who can tell what the men like? And young ladies may behave in a manner which would cost a servant her place. It’s no business of mine. I can’t expect you to read my letter, if I write it in this way. But it does stir one up to hear Miss Rachel called pretty, when one knows all the time that it’s her dress does it, and her confidence in herself.\n\n“Try not to lose patience with me, sir. I will get on as fast as I can to the time which is sure to interest you—the time when the Diamond was lost.\n\n“But there is one thing which I have got it on my mind to tell you first.\n\n“My life was not a very hard life to bear, while I was a thief. It was only when they had taught me at the reformatory to feel my own degradation, and to try for better things, that the days grew long and weary. Thoughts of the future forced themselves on me now. I felt the dreadful reproach that honest people—even the kindest of honest people—were to me in themselves. A heart-breaking sensation of loneliness kept with me, go where I might, and do what I might, and see what persons I might. It was my duty, I know, to try and get on with my fellow-servants in my new place. Somehow, I couldn’t make friends with them. They looked (or I thought they looked) as if they suspected what I had been. I don’t regret, far from it, having been roused to make the effort to be a reformed woman—but, indeed, indeed it was a weary life. You had come across it like a beam of sunshine at first—and then you too failed me. I was mad enough to love you; and I couldn’t even attract your notice. There was great misery—there really was great misery in that.\n\n“Now I am coming to what I wanted to tell you. In those days of bitterness, I went two or three times, when it was my turn to go out, to my favourite place—the beach above the Shivering Sand. And I said to myself, ‘I think it will end here. When I can bear it no longer, I think it will end here.’ You will understand, sir, that the place had laid a kind of spell on me before you came. I had always had a notion that something would happen to me at the quicksand. But I had never looked at it, with the thought of its being the means of my making away with myself, till the time came of which I am now writing. Then I did think that here was a place which would end all my troubles for me in a moment or two—and hide me for ever afterwards.\n\n“This is all I have to say about myself, reckoning from the morning when I first saw you, to the morning when the alarm was raised in the house that the Diamond was lost.\n\n“I was so aggravated by the foolish talk among the women servants, all wondering who was to be suspected first; and I was so angry with you (knowing no better at that time) for the pains you took in hunting for the jewel, and sending for the police, that I kept as much as possible away by myself, until later in the day, when the officer from Frizinghall came to the house.\n\n“Mr. Seegrave began, as you may remember, by setting a guard on the women’s bedrooms; and the women all followed him upstairs in a rage, to know what he meant by the insult he had put on them. I went with the rest, because if I had done anything different from the rest, Mr. Seegrave was the sort of man who would have suspected me directly. We found him in Miss Rachel’s room. He told us he wouldn’t have a lot of women there; and he pointed to the smear on the painted door, and said some of our petticoats had done the mischief, and sent us all downstairs again.\n\n“After leaving Miss Rachel’s room, I stopped a moment on one of the landings, by myself, to see if I had got the paint-stain by any chance on my gown. Penelope Betteredge (the only one of the women with whom I was on friendly terms) passed, and noticed what I was about.\n\n“‘You needn’t trouble yourself, Rosanna,’ she said. ‘The paint on Miss Rachel’s door has been dry for hours. If Mr. Seegrave hadn’t set a watch on our bedrooms, I might have told him as much. I don’t know what you think—I was never so insulted before in my life!’\n\n“Penelope was a hot-tempered girl. I quieted her, and brought her back to what she had said about the paint on the door having been dry for hours.\n\n“‘How do you know that?’ I asked.\n\n“‘I was with Miss Rachel, and Mr. Franklin, all yesterday morning,’ Penelope said, ‘mixing the colours, while they finished the door. I heard Miss Rachel ask whether the door would be dry that evening, in time for the birthday company to see it. And Mr. Franklin shook his head, and said it wouldn’t be dry in less than twelve hours. It was long past luncheon-time—it was three o’clock before they had done. What does your arithmetic say, Rosanna? Mine says the door was dry by three this morning.’\n\n“‘Did some of the ladies go upstairs yesterday evening to see it?’ I asked. ‘I thought I heard Miss Rachel warning them to keep clear of the door.’\n\n“‘None of the ladies made the smear,’ Penelope answered. ‘I left Miss Rachel in bed at twelve last night. And I noticed the door, and there was nothing wrong with it then.’\n\n“‘Oughtn’t you to mention this to Mr. Seegrave, Penelope?’\n\n“‘I wouldn’t say a word to help Mr. Seegrave for anything that could be offered to me!’\n\n“She went to her work, and I went to mine.\n\n“My work, sir, was to make your bed, and to put your room tidy. It was the happiest hour I had in the whole day. I used to kiss the pillow on which your head had rested all night. No matter who has done it since, you have never had your clothes folded as nicely as I folded them for you. Of all the little knick-knacks in your dressing-case, there wasn’t one that had so much as a speck on it. You never noticed it, any more than you noticed me. I beg your pardon; I am forgetting myself. I will make haste, and go on again.\n\n“Well, I went in that morning to do my work in your room. There was your nightgown tossed across the bed, just as you had thrown it off. I took it up to fold it—and I saw the stain of the paint from Miss Rachel’s door!\n\n“I was so startled by the discovery that I ran out with the nightgown in my hand, and made for the back stairs, and locked myself into my own room, to look at it in a place where nobody could intrude and interrupt me.\n\n“As soon as I got my breath again, I called to mind my talk with Penelope, and I said to myself, ‘Here’s the proof that he was in Miss Rachel’s sitting-room between twelve last night, and three this morning!’\n\n“I shall not tell you in plain words what was the first suspicion that crossed my mind, when I had made that discovery. You would only be angry—and, if you were angry, you might tear my letter up and read no more of it.\n\n“Let it be enough, if you please, to say only this. After thinking it over to the best of my ability, I made it out that the thing wasn’t likely, for a reason that I will tell you. If you had been in Miss Rachel’s sitting-room, at that time of night, with Miss Rachel’s knowledge (and if you had been foolish enough to forget to take care of the wet door) she would have reminded you—she would never have let you carry away such a witness against her, as the witness I was looking at now! At the same time, I own I was not completely certain in my own mind that I had proved my own suspicion to be wrong. You will not have forgotten that I have owned to hating Miss Rachel. Try to think, if you can, that there was a little of that hatred in all this. It ended in my determining to keep the nightgown, and to wait, and watch, and see what use I might make of it. At that time, please to remember, not the ghost of an idea entered my head that you had stolen the Diamond.”\n\nThere, I broke off in the reading of the letter for the second time.\n\nI had read those portions of the miserable woman’s confession which related to myself, with unaffected surprise, and, I can honestly add, with sincere distress. I had regretted, truly regretted, the aspersion which I had thoughtlessly cast on her memory, before I had seen a line of her letter. But when I had advanced as far as the passage which is quoted above, I own I felt my mind growing bitterer and bitterer against Rosanna Spearman as I went on. “Read the rest for yourself,” I said, handing the letter to Betteredge across the table. “If there is anything in it that I must look at, you can tell me as you go on.”\n\n“I understand you, Mr. Franklin,” he answered. “It’s natural, sir, in you. And, God help us all!” he added, in a lower tone, “it’s no less natural in her.”\n\nI proceed to copy the continuation of the letter from the original, in my own possession:—\n\n“Having determined to keep the nightgown, and to see what use my love, or my revenge (I hardly know which) could turn it to in the future, the next thing to discover was how to keep it without the risk of being found out.\n\n“There was only one way—to make another nightgown exactly like it, before Saturday came, and brought the laundry-woman and her inventory to the house.\n\n“I was afraid to put it off till next day (the Friday); being in doubt lest some accident might happen in the interval. I determined to make the new nightgown on that same day (the Thursday), while I could count, if I played my cards properly, on having my time to myself. The first thing to do (after locking up your nightgown in my drawer) was to go back to your bedroom—not so much to put it to rights (Penelope would have done that for me, if I had asked her) as to find out whether you had smeared off any of the paint-stain from your nightgown, on the bed, or on any piece of furniture in the room.\n\n“I examined everything narrowly, and at last, I found a few streaks of the paint on the inside of your dressing-gown—not the linen dressing-gown you usually wore in that summer season, but a flannel dressing-gown which you had with you also. I suppose you felt chilly after walking to and fro in nothing but your nightdress, and put on the warmest thing you could find. At any rate, there were the stains, just visible, on the inside of the dressing-gown. I easily got rid of these by scraping away the stuff of the flannel. This done, the only proof left against you was the proof locked up in my drawer.\n\n“I had just finished your room when I was sent for to be questioned by Mr. Seegrave, along with the rest of the servants. Next came the examination of all our boxes. And then followed the most extraordinary event of the day—to me—since I had found the paint on your nightgown. This event came out of the second questioning of Penelope Betteredge by Superintendent Seegrave.\n\n“Penelope returned to us quite beside herself with rage at the manner in which Mr. Seegrave had treated her. He had hinted, beyond the possibility of mistaking him, that he suspected her of being the thief. We were all equally astonished at hearing this, and we all asked, Why?\n\n“‘Because the Diamond was in Miss Rachel’s sitting-room,’ Penelope answered. ‘And because I was the last person in the sitting-room at night!’\n\n“Almost before the words had left her lips, I remembered that another person had been in the sitting-room later than Penelope. That person was yourself. My head whirled round, and my thoughts were in dreadful confusion. In the midst of it all, something in my mind whispered to me that the smear on your nightgown might have a meaning entirely different to the meaning which I had given to it up to that time. ‘If the last person who was in the room is the person to be suspected,’ I thought to myself, ‘the thief is not Penelope, but Mr. Franklin Blake!’\n\n“In the case of any other gentleman, I believe I should have been ashamed of suspecting him of theft, almost as soon as the suspicion had passed through my mind.\n\n“But the bare thought that you had let yourself down to my level, and that I, in possessing myself of your nightgown, had also possessed myself of the means of shielding you from being discovered, and disgraced for life—I say, sir, the bare thought of this seemed to open such a chance before me of winning your good will, that I passed blindfold, as one may say, from suspecting to believing. I made up my mind, on the spot, that you had shown yourself the busiest of anybody in fetching the police, as a blind to deceive us all; and that the hand which had taken Miss Rachel’s jewel could by no possibility be any other hand than yours.\n\n“The excitement of this new discovery of mine must, I think, have turned my head for a while. I felt such a devouring eagerness to see you—to try you with a word or two about the Diamond, and to make you look at me, and speak to me, in that way—that I put my hair tidy, and made myself as nice as I could, and went to you boldly in the library where I knew you were writing.\n\n“You had left one of your rings upstairs, which made as good an excuse for my intrusion as I could have desired. But, oh, sir! if you have ever loved, you will understand how it was that all my courage cooled, when I walked into the room, and found myself in your presence. And then, you looked up at me so coldly, and you thanked me for finding your ring in such an indifferent manner, that my knees trembled under me, and I felt as if I should drop on the floor at your feet. When you had thanked me, you looked back, if you remember, at your writing. I was so mortified at being treated in this way, that I plucked up spirit enough to speak. I said, ‘This is a strange thing about the Diamond, sir.’ And you looked up again, and said, ‘Yes, it is!’ You spoke civilly (I can’t deny that); but still you kept a distance—a cruel distance between us. Believing, as I did, that you had got the lost Diamond hidden about you, while you were speaking, your coolness so provoked me that I got bold enough, in the heat of the moment, to give you a hint. I said, ‘They will never find the Diamond, sir, will they? No! nor the person who took it—I’ll answer for that.’ I nodded, and smiled at you, as much as to say, ‘I know!’ This time, you looked up at me with something like interest in your eyes; and I felt that a few more words on your side and mine might bring out the truth. Just at that moment, Mr. Betteredge spoilt it all by coming to the door. I knew his footstep, and I also knew that it was against his rules for me to be in the library at that time of day—let alone being there along with you. I had only just time to get out of my own accord, before he could come in and tell me to go. I was angry and disappointed; but I was not entirely without hope for all that. The ice, you see, was broken between us—and I thought I would take care, on the next occasion, that Mr. Betteredge was out of the way.\n\n“When I got back to the servants’ hall, the bell was going for our dinner. Afternoon already! and the materials for making the new nightgown were still to be got! There was but one chance of getting them. I shammed ill at dinner; and so secured the whole of the interval from then till tea-time to my own use.\n\n“What I was about, while the household believed me to be lying down in my own room; and how I spent the night, after shamming ill again at tea-time, and having been sent up to bed, there is no need to tell you. Sergeant Cuff discovered that much, if he discovered nothing more. And I can guess how. I was detected (though I kept my veil down) in the draper’s shop at Frizinghall. There was a glass in front of me, at the counter where I was buying the longcloth; and—in that glass—I saw one of the shopmen point to my shoulder and whisper to another. At night again, when I was secretly at work, locked into my room, I heard the breathing of the women servants who suspected me, outside my door.\n\n“It didn’t matter then; it doesn’t matter now. On the Friday morning, hours before Sergeant Cuff entered the house, there was the new nightgown—to make up your number in place of the nightgown that I had got—made, wrung out, dried, ironed, marked, and folded as the laundry woman folded all the others, safe in your drawer. There was no fear (if the linen in the house was examined) of the newness of the nightgown betraying me. All your underclothing had been renewed, when you came to our house—I suppose on your return home from foreign parts.\n\n“The next thing was the arrival of Sergeant Cuff; and the next great surprise was the announcement of what he thought about the smear on the door.\n\n“I had believed you to be guilty (as I have owned), more because I wanted you to be guilty than for any other reason. And now, the Sergeant had come round by a totally different way to the same conclusion (respecting the nightgown) as mine! And I had got the dress that was the only proof against you! And not a living creature knew it—yourself included! I am afraid to tell you how I felt when I called these things to mind—you would hate my memory for ever afterwards.”\n\nAt that place, Betteredge looked up from the letter.\n\n“Not a glimmer of light so far, Mr. Franklin,” said the old man, taking off his heavy tortoiseshell spectacles, and pushing Rosanna Spearman’s confession a little away from him. “Have you come to any conclusion, sir, in your own mind, while I have been reading?”\n\n“Finish the letter first, Betteredge; there may be something to enlighten us at the end of it. I shall have a word or two to say to you after that.”\n\n“Very good, sir. I’ll just rest my eyes, and then I’ll go on again. In the meantime, Mr. Franklin—I don’t want to hurry you—but would you mind telling me, in one word, whether you see your way out of this dreadful mess yet?”\n\n“I see my way back to London,” I said, “to consult Mr. Bruff. If he can’t help me——”\n\n“Yes, sir?”\n\n“And if the Sergeant won’t leave his retirement at Dorking——”\n\n“He won’t, Mr. Franklin!”\n\n“Then, Betteredge—as far as I can see now—I am at the end of my resources. After Mr. Bruff and the Sergeant, I don’t know of a living creature who can be of the slightest use to me.”\n\nAs the words passed my lips, some person outside knocked at the door of the room.\n\nBetteredge looked surprised as well as annoyed by the interruption.\n\n“Come in,” he called out, irritably, “whoever you are!”\n\nThe door opened, and there entered to us, quietly, the most remarkable-looking man that I had ever seen. Judging him by his figure and his movements, he was still young. Judging him by his face, and comparing him with Betteredge, he looked the elder of the two. His complexion was of a gipsy darkness; his fleshless cheeks had fallen into deep hollows, over which the bone projected like a penthouse. His nose presented the fine shape and modelling so often found among the ancient people of the East, so seldom visible among the newer races of the West. His forehead rose high and straight from the brow. His marks and wrinkles were innumerable. From this strange face, eyes, stranger still, of the softest brown—eyes dreamy and mournful, and deeply sunk in their orbits—looked out at you, and (in my case, at least) took your attention captive at their will. Add to this a quantity of thick closely-curling hair, which, by some freak of Nature, had lost its colour in the most startlingly partial and capricious manner. Over the top of his head it was still of the deep black which was its natural colour. Round the sides of his head—without the slightest gradation of grey to break the force of the extraordinary contrast—it had turned completely white. The line between the two colours preserved no sort of regularity. At one place, the white hair ran up into the black; at another, the black hair ran down into the white. I looked at the man with a curiosity which, I am ashamed to say, I found it quite impossible to control. His soft brown eyes looked back at me gently; and he met my involuntary rudeness in staring at him, with an apology which I was conscious that I had not deserved.\n\n“I beg your pardon,” he said. “I had no idea that Mr. Betteredge was engaged.” He took a slip of paper from his pocket, and handed it to Betteredge. “The list for next week,” he said. His eyes just rested on me again—and he left the room as quietly as he had entered it.\n\n“Who is that?” I asked.\n\n“Mr. Candy’s assistant,” said Betteredge. “By-the-bye, Mr. Franklin, you will be sorry to hear that the little doctor has never recovered that illness he caught, going home from the birthday dinner. He’s pretty well in health; but he lost his memory in the fever, and he has never recovered more than the wreck of it since. The work all falls on his assistant. Not much of it now, except among the poor. They can’t help themselves, you know. They must put up with the man with the piebald hair, and the gipsy complexion—or they would get no doctoring at all.”\n\n“You don’t seem to like him, Betteredge?”\n\n“Nobody likes him, sir.”\n\n“Why is he so unpopular?”\n\n“Well, Mr. Franklin, his appearance is against him, to begin with. And then there’s a story that Mr. Candy took him with a very doubtful character. Nobody knows who he is—and he hasn’t a friend in the place. How can you expect one to like him, after that?”\n\n“Quite impossible, of course! May I ask what he wanted with you, when he gave you that bit of paper?”\n\n“Only to bring me the weekly list of the sick people about here, sir, who stand in need of a little wine. My lady always had a regular distribution of good sound port and sherry among the infirm poor; and Miss Rachel wishes the custom to be kept up. Times have changed! times have changed! I remember when Mr. Candy himself brought the list to my mistress. Now it’s Mr. Candy’s assistant who brings the list to me. I’ll go on with the letter, if you will allow me, sir,” said Betteredge, drawing Rosanna Spearman’s confession back to him. “It isn’t lively reading, I grant you. But, there! it keeps me from getting sour with thinking of the past.” He put on his spectacles, and wagged his head gloomily. “There’s a bottom of good sense, Mr. Franklin, in our conduct to our mothers, when they first start us on the journey of life. We are all of us more or less unwilling to be brought into the world. And we are all of us right.”\n\nMr. Candy’s assistant had produced too strong an impression on me to be immediately dismissed from my thoughts. I passed over the last unanswerable utterance of the Betteredge philosophy; and returned to the subject of the man with the piebald hair.\n\n“What is his name?” I asked.\n\n“As ugly a name as need be,” Betteredge answered gruffly. “Ezra Jennings.”\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\nHaving told me the name of Mr. Candy’s assistant, Betteredge appeared to think that we had wasted enough of our time on an insignificant subject. He resumed the perusal of Rosanna Spearman’s letter.\n\nOn my side, I sat at the window, waiting until he had done. Little by little, the impression produced on me by Ezra Jennings—it seemed perfectly unaccountable, in such a situation as mine, that any human being should have produced an impression on me at all!—faded from my mind. My thoughts flowed back into their former channel. Once more, I forced myself to look my own incredible position resolutely in the face. Once more, I reviewed in my own mind the course which I had at last summoned composure enough to plan out for the future.\n\nTo go back to London that day; to put the whole case before Mr. Bruff; and, last and most important, to obtain (no matter by what means or at what sacrifice) a personal interview with Rachel—this was my plan of action, so far as I was capable of forming it at the time. There was more than an hour still to spare before the train started. And there was the bare chance that Betteredge might discover something in the unread portion of Rosanna Spearman’s letter, which it might be useful for me to know before I left the house in which the Diamond had been lost. For that chance I was now waiting.\n\nThe letter ended in these terms:\n\n“You have no need to be angry, Mr. Franklin, even if I did feel some little triumph at knowing that I held all your prospects in life in my own hands. Anxieties and fears soon came back to me. With the view Sergeant Cuff took of the loss of the Diamond, he would be sure to end in examining our linen and our dresses. There was no place in my room—there was no place in the house—which I could feel satisfied would be safe from him. How to hide the nightgown so that not even the Sergeant could find it? and how to do that without losing one moment of precious time?—these were not easy questions to answer. My uncertainties ended in my taking a way that may make you laugh. I undressed, and put the nightgown on me. You had worn it—and I had another little moment of pleasure in wearing it after you.\n\n“The next news that reached us in the servants’ hall showed that I had not made sure of the nightgown a moment too soon. Sergeant Cuff wanted to see the washing-book.\n\n“I found it, and took it to him in my lady’s sitting-room. The Sergeant and I had come across each other more than once in former days. I was certain he would know me again—and I was not certain of what he might do when he found me employed as servant in a house in which a valuable jewel had been lost. In this suspense, I felt it would be a relief to me to get the meeting between us over, and to know the worst of it at once.\n\n“He looked at me as if I was a stranger, when I handed him the washing-book; and he was very specially polite in thanking me for bringing it. I thought those were both bad signs. There was no knowing what he might say of me behind my back; there was no knowing how soon I might not find myself taken in custody on suspicion, and searched. It was then time for your return from seeing Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite off by the railway; and I went to your favourite walk in the shrubbery, to try for another chance of speaking to you—the last chance, for all I knew to the contrary, that I might have.\n\n“You never appeared; and, what was worse still, Mr. Betteredge and Sergeant Cuff passed by the place where I was hiding—and the Sergeant saw me.\n\n“I had no choice, after that, but to return to my proper place and my proper work, before more disasters happened to me. Just as I was going to step across the path, you came back from the railway. You were making straight for the shrubbery, when you saw me—I am certain, sir, you saw me—and you turned away as if I had got the plague, and went into the house.*\n\n* NOTE; by Franklin Blake.—The writer is entirely mistaken, poor creature. I never noticed her. My intention was certainly to have taken a turn in the shrubbery. But, remembering at the same moment that my aunt might wish to see me, after my return from the railway, I altered my mind, and went into the house.\n\n“I made the best of my way indoors again, returning by the servants’ entrance. There was nobody in the laundry-room at that time; and I sat down there alone. I have told you already of the thoughts which the Shivering Sand put into my head. Those thoughts came back to me now. I wondered in myself which it would be harder to do, if things went on in this manner—to bear Mr. Franklin Blake’s indifference to me, or to jump into the quicksand and end it for ever in that way?\n\n“It’s useless to ask me to account for my own conduct, at this time. I try—and I can’t understand it myself.\n\n“Why didn’t I stop you, when you avoided me in that cruel manner? Why didn’t I call out, ‘Mr. Franklin, I have got something to say to you; it concerns yourself, and you must, and shall, hear it?’ You were at my mercy—I had got the whip-hand of you, as they say. And better than that, I had the means (if I could only make you trust me) of being useful to you in the future. Of course, I never supposed that you—a gentleman—had stolen the Diamond for the mere pleasure of stealing it. No. Penelope had heard Miss Rachel, and I had heard Mr. Betteredge, talk about your extravagance and your debts. It was plain enough to me that you had taken the Diamond to sell it, or pledge it, and so to get the money of which you stood in need. Well! I could have told you of a man in London who would have advanced a good large sum on the jewel, and who would have asked no awkward questions about it either.\n\n“Why didn’t I speak to you! why didn’t I speak to you!\n\n“I wonder whether the risks and difficulties of keeping the nightgown were as much as I could manage, without having other risks and difficulties added to them? This might have been the case with some women—but how could it be the case with me? In the days when I was a thief, I had run fifty times greater risks, and found my way out of difficulties to which this difficulty was mere child’s play. I had been apprenticed, as you may say, to frauds and deceptions—some of them on such a grand scale, and managed so cleverly, that they became famous, and appeared in the newspapers. Was such a little thing as the keeping of the nightgown likely to weigh on my spirits, and to set my heart sinking within me, at the time when I ought to have spoken to you? What nonsense to ask the question! The thing couldn’t be.\n\n“Where is the use of my dwelling in this way on my own folly? The plain truth is plain enough, surely? Behind your back, I loved you with all my heart and soul. Before your face—there’s no denying it—I was frightened of you; frightened of making you angry with me; frightened of what you might say to me (though you had taken the Diamond) if I presumed to tell you that I had found it out. I had gone as near to it as I dared when I spoke to you in the library. You had not turned your back on me then. You had not started away from me as if I had got the plague. I tried to provoke myself into feeling angry with you, and to rouse up my courage in that way. No! I couldn’t feel anything but the misery and the mortification of it. ‘You’re a plain girl; you have got a crooked shoulder; you’re only a housemaid—what do you mean by attempting to speak to Me?’ You never uttered a word of that, Mr. Franklin; but you said it all to me, nevertheless! Is such madness as this to be accounted for? No. There is nothing to be done but to confess it, and let it be.\n\n“I ask your pardon, once more, for this wandering of my pen. There is no fear of its happening again. I am close at the end now.\n\n“The first person who disturbed me by coming into the empty room was Penelope. She had found out my secret long since, and she had done her best to bring me to my senses—and done it kindly too.\n\n“‘Ah!’ she said, ‘I know why you’re sitting here, and fretting, all by yourself. The best thing that can happen for your advantage, Rosanna, will be for Mr. Franklin’s visit here to come to an end. It’s my belief that he won’t be long now before he leaves the house.”\n\n“In all my thoughts of you I had never thought of your going away. I couldn’t speak to Penelope. I could only look at her.\n\n“‘I’ve just left Miss Rachel,’ Penelope went on. ‘And a hard matter I have had of it to put up with her temper. She says the house is unbearable to her with the police in it; and she’s determined to speak to my lady this evening, and to go to her Aunt Ablewhite tomorrow. If she does that, Mr. Franklin will be the next to find a reason for going away, you may depend on it!’\n\n“I recovered the use of my tongue at that. ‘Do you mean to say Mr. Franklin will go with her?’ I asked.\n\n“‘Only too gladly, if she would let him; but she won’t. He has been made to feel her temper; he is in her black books too—and that after having done all he can to help her, poor fellow! No! no! If they don’t make it up before tomorrow, you will see Miss Rachel go one way, and Mr. Franklin another. Where he may betake himself to I can’t say. But he will never stay here, Rosanna, after Miss Rachel has left us.’\n\n“I managed to master the despair I felt at the prospect of your going away. To own the truth, I saw a little glimpse of hope for myself if there was really a serious disagreement between Miss Rachel and you. ‘Do you know,’ I asked, ‘what the quarrel is between them?’\n\n“‘It is all on Miss Rachel’s side,’ Penelope said. ‘And, for anything I know to the contrary, it’s all Miss Rachel’s temper, and nothing else. I am loth to distress you, Rosanna; but don’t run away with the notion that Mr. Franklin is ever likely to quarrel with her. He’s a great deal too fond of her for that!’\n\n“She had only just spoken those cruel words when there came a call to us from Mr. Betteredge. All the indoor servants were to assemble in the hall. And then we were to go in, one by one, and be questioned in Mr. Betteredge’s room by Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“It came to my turn to go in, after her ladyship’s maid and the upper housemaid had been questioned first. Sergeant Cuff’s inquiries—though he wrapped them up very cunningly—soon showed me that those two women (the bitterest enemies I had in the house) had made their discoveries outside my door, on the Tuesday afternoon, and again on the Thursday night. They had told the Sergeant enough to open his eyes to some part of the truth. He rightly believed me to have made a new nightgown secretly, but he wrongly believed the paint-stained nightgown to be mine. I felt satisfied of another thing, from what he said, which it puzzled me to understand. He suspected me, of course, of being concerned in the disappearance of the Diamond. But, at the same time, he let me see—purposely, as I thought—that he did not consider me as the person chiefly answerable for the loss of the jewel. He appeared to think that I had been acting under the direction of somebody else. Who that person might be, I couldn’t guess then, and can’t guess now.\n\n“In this uncertainty, one thing was plain—that Sergeant Cuff was miles away from knowing the whole truth. You were safe as long as the nightgown was safe—and not a moment longer.\n\n“I quite despair of making you understand the distress and terror which pressed upon me now. It was impossible for me to risk wearing your nightgown any longer. I might find myself taken off, at a moment’s notice, to the police court at Frizinghall, to be charged on suspicion, and searched accordingly. While Sergeant Cuff still left me free, I had to choose—and at once—between destroying the nightgown, or hiding it in some safe place, at some safe distance from the house.\n\n“If I had only been a little less fond of you, I think I should have destroyed it. But oh! how could I destroy the only thing I had which proved that I had saved you from discovery? If we did come to an explanation together, and if you suspected me of having some bad motive, and denied it all, how could I win upon you to trust me, unless I had the nightgown to produce? Was it wronging you to believe, as I did and do still, that you might hesitate to let a poor girl like me be the sharer of your secret, and your accomplice in the theft which your money-troubles had tempted you to commit? Think of your cold behaviour to me, sir, and you will hardly wonder at my unwillingness to destroy the only claim on your confidence and your gratitude which it was my fortune to possess.\n\n“I determined to hide it; and the place I fixed on was the place I knew best—the Shivering Sand.\n\n“As soon as the questioning was over, I made the first excuse that came into my head, and got leave to go out for a breath of fresh air. I went straight to Cobb’s Hole, to Mr. Yolland’s cottage. His wife and daughter were the best friends I had. Don’t suppose I trusted them with your secret—I have trusted nobody. All I wanted was to write this letter to you, and to have a safe opportunity of taking the nightgown off me. Suspected as I was, I could do neither of those things with any sort of security, at the house.\n\n“And now I have nearly got through my long letter, writing it alone in Lucy Yolland’s bedroom. When it is done, I shall go downstairs with the nightgown rolled up, and hidden under my cloak. I shall find the means I want for keeping it safe and dry in its hiding-place, among the litter of old things in Mrs. Yolland’s kitchen. And then I shall go to the Shivering Sand—don’t be afraid of my letting my footmarks betray me!—and hide the nightgown down in the sand, where no living creature can find it without being first let into the secret by myself.\n\n“And, when that’s done, what then?\n\n“Then, Mr. Franklin, I shall have two reasons for making another attempt to say the words to you which I have not said yet. If you leave the house, as Penelope believes you will leave it, and if I haven’t spoken to you before that, I shall lose my opportunity forever. That is one reason. Then, again, there is the comforting knowledge—if my speaking does make you angry—that I have got the nightgown ready to plead my cause for me as nothing else can. That is my other reason. If these two together don’t harden my heart against the coldness which has hitherto frozen it up (I mean the coldness of your treatment of me), there will be the end of my efforts—and the end of my life.\n\n“Yes. If I miss my next opportunity—if you are as cruel as ever, and if I feel it again as I have felt it already—good-bye to the world which has grudged me the happiness that it gives to others. Good-bye to life, which nothing but a little kindness from you can ever make pleasurable to me again. Don’t blame yourself, sir, if it ends in this way. But try—do try—to feel some forgiving sorrow for me! I shall take care that you find out what I have done for you, when I am past telling you of it myself. Will you say something kind of me then—in the same gentle way that you have when you speak to Miss Rachel? If you do that, and if there are such things as ghosts, I believe my ghost will hear it, and tremble with the pleasure of it.\n\n“It’s time I left off. I am making myself cry. How am I to see my way to the hiding-place if I let these useless tears come and blind me?\n\n“Besides, why should I look at the gloomy side? Why not believe, while I can, that it will end well after all? I may find you in a good humour tonight—or, if not, I may succeed better tomorrow morning. I sha’n’t improve my plain face by fretting—shall I? Who knows but I may have filled all these weary long pages of paper for nothing? They will go, for safety’s sake (never mind now for what other reason) into the hiding-place along with the nightgown. It has been hard, hard work writing my letter. Oh! if we only end in understanding each other, how I shall enjoy tearing it up!\n\n“I beg to remain, sir, your true lover and humble servant,\n\n“ROSANNA SPEARMAN.”\n\nThe reading of the letter was completed by Betteredge in silence. After carefully putting it back in the envelope, he sat thinking, with his head bowed down, and his eyes on the ground.\n\n“Betteredge,” I said, “is there any hint to guide me at the end of the letter?”\n\nHe looked up slowly, with a heavy sigh.\n\n“There is nothing to guide you, Mr. Franklin,” he answered. “If you take my advice you will keep the letter in the cover till these present anxieties of yours have come to an end. It will sorely distress you, whenever you read it. Don’t read it now.”\n\nI put the letter away in my pocket-book.\n\nA glance back at the sixteenth and seventeenth chapters of Betteredge’s Narrative will show that there really was a reason for my thus sparing myself, at a time when my fortitude had been already cruelly tried. Twice over, the unhappy woman had made her last attempt to speak to me. And twice over, it had been my misfortune (God knows how innocently!) to repel the advances she had made to me. On the Friday night, as Betteredge truly describes it, she had found me alone at the billiard-table. Her manner and language suggested to me and would have suggested to any man, under the circumstances—that she was about to confess a guilty knowledge of the disappearance of the Diamond. For her own sake, I had purposely shown no special interest in what was coming; for her own sake, I had purposely looked at the billiard-balls, instead of looking at her—and what had been the result? I had sent her away from me, wounded to the heart! On the Saturday again—on the day when she must have foreseen, after what Penelope had told her, that my departure was close at hand—the same fatality still pursued us. She had once more attempted to meet me in the shrubbery walk, and she had found me there in company with Betteredge and Sergeant Cuff. In her hearing, the Sergeant, with his own underhand object in view, had appealed to my interest in Rosanna Spearman. Again for the poor creature’s own sake, I had met the police-officer with a flat denial, and had declared—loudly declared, so that she might hear me too—that I felt “no interest whatever in Rosanna Spearman.” At those words, solely designed to warn her against attempting to gain my private ear, she had turned away and left the place: cautioned of her danger, as I then believed; self-doomed to destruction, as I know now. From that point, I have already traced the succession of events which led me to the astounding discovery at the quicksand. The retrospect is now complete. I may leave the miserable story of Rosanna Spearman—to which, even at this distance of time, I cannot revert without a pang of distress—to suggest for itself all that is here purposely left unsaid. I may pass from the suicide at the Shivering Sand, with its strange and terrible influence on my present position and future prospects, to interests which concern the living people of this narrative, and to events which were already paving my way for the slow and toilsome journey from the darkness to the light.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\nI walked to the railway station accompanied, it is needless to say, by Gabriel Betteredge. I had the letter in my pocket, and the nightgown safely packed in a little bag—both to be submitted, before I slept that night, to the investigation of Mr. Bruff.\n\nWe left the house in silence. For the first time in my experience of him, I found old Betteredge in my company without a word to say to me. Having something to say on my side, I opened the conversation as soon as we were clear of the lodge gates.\n\n“Before I go to London,” I began, “I have two questions to ask you. They relate to myself, and I believe they will rather surprise you.”\n\n“If they will put that poor creature’s letter out of my head, Mr. Franklin, they may do anything else they like with me. Please to begin surprising me, sir, as soon as you can.”\n\n“My first question, Betteredge, is this. Was I drunk on the night of Rachel’s Birthday?”\n\n“You drunk!” exclaimed the old man. “Why it’s the great defect of your character, Mr. Franklin that you only drink with your dinner, and never touch a drop of liquor afterwards!”\n\n“But the birthday was a special occasion. I might have abandoned my regular habits, on that night of all others.”\n\nBetteredge considered for a moment.\n\n“You did go out of your habits, sir,” he said. “And I’ll tell you how. You looked wretchedly ill—and we persuaded you to have a drop of brandy and water to cheer you up a little.”\n\n“I am not used to brandy and water. It is quite possible——”\n\n“Wait a bit, Mr. Franklin. I knew you were not used, too. I poured you out half a wineglass-full of our fifty year old Cognac; and (more shame for me!) I drowned that noble liquor in nigh on a tumbler-full of cold water. A child couldn’t have got drunk on it—let alone a grown man!”\n\nI knew I could depend on his memory, in a matter of this kind. It was plainly impossible that I could have been intoxicated. I passed on to the second question.\n\n“Before I was sent abroad, Betteredge, you saw a great deal of me when I was a boy? Now tell me plainly, do you remember anything strange of me, after I had gone to bed at night? Did you ever discover me walking in my sleep?”\n\nBetteredge stopped, looked at me for a moment, nodded his head, and walked on again.\n\n“I see your drift now, Mr. Franklin!” he said “You’re trying to account for how you got the paint on your nightgown, without knowing it yourself. It won’t do, sir. You’re miles away still from getting at the truth. Walk in your sleep? You never did such a thing in your life!”\n\nHere again, I felt that Betteredge must be right. Neither at home nor abroad had my life ever been of the solitary sort. If I had been a sleep-walker, there were hundreds on hundreds of people who must have discovered me, and who, in the interest of my own safety, would have warned me of the habit, and have taken precautions to restrain it.\n\nStill, admitting all this, I clung—with an obstinacy which was surely natural and excusable, under the circumstances—to one or other of the only two explanations that I could see which accounted for the unendurable position in which I then stood. Observing that I was not yet satisfied, Betteredge shrewdly adverted to certain later events in the history of the Moonstone; and scattered both my theories to the wind at once and for ever.\n\n“Let’s try it another way, sir,” he said. “Keep your own opinion, and see how far it will take you towards finding out the truth. If we are to believe the nightgown—which I don’t for one—you not only smeared off the paint from the door, without knowing it, but you also took the Diamond without knowing it. Is that right, so far?”\n\n“Quite right. Go on.”\n\n“Very good, sir. We’ll say you were drunk, or walking in your sleep, when you took the jewel. That accounts for the night and morning, after the birthday. But how does it account for what has happened since that time? The Diamond has been taken to London, since that time. The Diamond has been pledged to Mr. Luker, since that time. Did you do those two things, without knowing it, too? Were you drunk when I saw you off in the pony-chaise on that Saturday evening? And did you walk in your sleep to Mr. Luker’s, when the train had brought you to your journey’s end? Excuse me for saying it, Mr. Franklin, but this business has so upset you, that you’re not fit yet to judge for yourself. The sooner you lay your head alongside Mr. Bruff’s head, the sooner you will see your way out of the dead-lock that has got you now.”\n\nWe reached the station, with only a minute or two to spare.\n\nI hurriedly gave Betteredge my address in London, so that he might write to me, if necessary; promising, on my side, to inform him of any news which I might have to communicate. This done, and just as I was bidding him farewell, I happened to glance towards the book-and-newspaper stall. There was Mr. Candy’s remarkable-looking assistant again, speaking to the keeper of the stall! Our eyes met at the same moment. Ezra Jennings took off his hat to me. I returned the salute, and got into a carriage just as the train started. It was a relief to my mind, I suppose, to dwell on any subject which appeared to be, personally, of no sort of importance to me. At all events, I began the momentous journey back which was to take me to Mr. Bruff, wondering—absurdly enough, I admit—that I should have seen the man with the piebald hair twice in one day!\n\nThe hour at which I arrived in London precluded all hope of my finding Mr. Bruff at his place of business. I drove from the railway to his private residence at Hampstead, and disturbed the old lawyer dozing alone in his dining-room, with his favourite pug-dog on his lap, and his bottle of wine at his elbow.\n\nI shall best describe the effect which my story produced on the mind of Mr. Bruff by relating his proceedings when he had heard it to the end. He ordered lights, and strong tea, to be taken into his study; and he sent a message to the ladies of his family, forbidding them to disturb us on any pretence whatever. These preliminaries disposed of, he first examined the nightgown, and then devoted himself to the reading of Rosanna Spearman’s letter.\n\nThe reading completed, Mr. Bruff addressed me for the first time since we had been shut up together in the seclusion of his own room.\n\n“Franklin Blake,” said the old gentleman, “this is a very serious matter, in more respects than one. In my opinion, it concerns Rachel quite as nearly as it concerns you. Her extraordinary conduct is no mystery now. She believes you have stolen the Diamond.”\n\nI had shrunk from reasoning my own way fairly to that revolting conclusion. But it had forced itself on me, nevertheless. My resolution to obtain a personal interview with Rachel, rested really and truly on the ground just stated by Mr. Bruff.\n\n“The first step to take in this investigation,” the lawyer proceeded, “is to appeal to Rachel. She has been silent all this time, from motives which I (who know her character) can readily understand. It is impossible, after what has happened, to submit to that silence any longer. She must be persuaded to tell us, or she must be forced to tell us, on what grounds she bases her belief that you took the Moonstone. The chances are, that the whole of this case, serious as it seems now, will tumble to pieces, if we can only break through Rachel’s inveterate reserve, and prevail upon her to speak out.”\n\n“That is a very comforting opinion for me,” I said. “I own I should like to know——”\n\n“You would like to know how I can justify it,” interposed Mr. Bruff. “I can tell you in two minutes. Understand, in the first place, that I look at this matter from a lawyer’s point of view. It’s a question of evidence, with me. Very well. The evidence breaks down, at the outset, on one important point.”\n\n“On what point?”\n\n“You shall hear. I admit that the mark of the name proves the nightgown to be yours. I admit that the mark of the paint proves the nightgown to have made the smear on Rachel’s door. But what evidence is there to prove that you are the person who wore it, on the night when the Diamond was lost?”\n\nThe objection struck me, all the more forcibly that it reflected an objection which I had felt myself.\n\n“As to this,” pursued the lawyer taking up Rosanna Spearman’s confession, “I can understand that the letter is a distressing one to you. I can understand that you may hesitate to analyse it from a purely impartial point of view. But I am not in your position. I can bring my professional experience to bear on this document, just as I should bring it to bear on any other. Without alluding to the woman’s career as a thief, I will merely remark that her letter proves her to have been an adept at deception, on her own showing; and I argue from that, that I am justified in suspecting her of not having told the whole truth. I won’t start any theory, at present, as to what she may or may not have done. I will only say that, if Rachel has suspected you on the evidence of the nightgown only, the chances are ninety-nine to a hundred that Rosanna Spearman was the person who showed it to her. In that case, there is the woman’s letter, confessing that she was jealous of Rachel, confessing that she changed the roses, confessing that she saw a glimpse of hope for herself, in the prospect of a quarrel between Rachel and you. I don’t stop to ask who took the Moonstone (as a means to her end, Rosanna Spearman would have taken fifty Moonstones)—I only say that the disappearance of the jewel gave this reclaimed thief who was in love with you, an opportunity of setting you and Rachel at variance for the rest of your lives. She had not decided on destroying herself, then, remember; and, having the opportunity, I distinctly assert that it was in her character, and in her position at the time, to take it. What do you say to that?”\n\n“Some such suspicion,” I answered, “crossed my own mind, as soon as I opened the letter.”\n\n“Exactly! And when you had read the letter, you pitied the poor creature, and couldn’t find it in your heart to suspect her. Does you credit, my dear sir—does you credit!”\n\n“But suppose it turns out that I did wear the nightgown? What then?”\n\n“I don’t see how the fact can be proved,” said Mr. Bruff. “But assuming the proof to be possible, the vindication of your innocence would be no easy matter. We won’t go into that, now. Let us wait and see whether Rachel hasn’t suspected you on the evidence of the nightgown only.”\n\n“Good God, how coolly you talk of Rachel suspecting me!” I broke out. “What right has she to suspect Me, on any evidence, of being a thief?”\n\n“A very sensible question, my dear sir. Rather hotly put—but well worth considering for all that. What puzzles you, puzzles me too. Search your memory, and tell me this. Did anything happen while you were staying at the house—not, of course, to shake Rachel’s belief in your honour—but, let us say, to shake her belief (no matter with how little reason) in your principles generally?”\n\nI started, in ungovernable agitation, to my feet. The lawyer’s question reminded me, for the first time since I had left England, that something had happened.\n\nIn the eighth chapter of Betteredge’s Narrative, an allusion will be found to the arrival of a foreigner and a stranger at my aunt’s house, who came to see me on business. The nature of his business was this.\n\nI had been foolish enough (being, as usual, straitened for money at the time) to accept a loan from the keeper of a small restaurant in Paris, to whom I was well known as a customer. A time was settled between us for paying the money back; and when the time came, I found it (as thousands of other honest men have found it) impossible to keep my engagement. I sent the man a bill. My name was unfortunately too well known on such documents: he failed to negotiate it. His affairs had fallen into disorder, in the interval since I had borrowed of him; bankruptcy stared him in the face; and a relative of his, a French lawyer, came to England to find me, and to insist upon the payment of my debt. He was a man of violent temper; and he took the wrong way with me. High words passed on both sides; and my aunt and Rachel were unfortunately in the next room, and heard us. Lady Verinder came in, and insisted on knowing what was the matter. The Frenchman produced his credentials, and declared me to be responsible for the ruin of a poor man, who had trusted in my honour. My aunt instantly paid him the money, and sent him off. She knew me better of course than to take the Frenchman’s view of the transaction. But she was shocked at my carelessness, and justly angry with me for placing myself in a position, which, but for her interference, might have become a very disgraceful one. Either her mother told her, or Rachel heard what passed—I can’t say which. She took her own romantic, high-flown view of the matter. I was “heartless”; I was “dishonourable”; I had “no principle”; there was “no knowing what I might do next”—in short, she said some of the severest things to me which I had ever heard from a young lady’s lips. The breach between us lasted for the whole of the next day. The day after, I succeeded in making my peace, and thought no more of it. Had Rachel reverted to this unlucky accident, at the critical moment when my place in her estimation was again, and far more seriously, assailed? Mr. Bruff, when I had mentioned the circumstances to him, answered the question at once in the affirmative.\n\n“It would have its effect on her mind,” he said gravely. “And I wish, for your sake, the thing had not happened. However, we have discovered that there was a predisposing influence against you—and there is one uncertainty cleared out of our way, at any rate. I see nothing more that we can do now. Our next step in this inquiry must be the step that takes us to Rachel.”\n\nHe rose, and began walking thoughtfully up and down the room. Twice, I was on the point of telling him that I had determined on seeing Rachel personally; and twice, having regard to his age and his character, I hesitated to take him by surprise at an unfavourable moment.\n\n“The grand difficulty is,” he resumed, “how to make her show her whole mind in this matter, without reserve. Have you any suggestions to offer?”\n\n“I have made up my mind, Mr. Bruff, to speak to Rachel myself.”\n\n“You!” He suddenly stopped in his walk, and looked at me as if he thought I had taken leave of my senses. “You, of all the people in the world!” He abruptly checked himself, and took another turn in the room. “Wait a little,” he said. “In cases of this extraordinary kind, the rash way is sometimes the best way.” He considered the question for a moment or two, under that new light, and ended boldly by a decision in my favour. “Nothing venture, nothing have,” the old gentleman resumed. “You have a chance in your favour which I don’t possess—and you shall be the first to try the experiment.”\n\n“A chance in my favour?” I repeated, in the greatest surprise.\n\nMr. Bruff’s face softened, for the first time, into a smile.\n\n“This is how it stands,” he said. “I tell you fairly, I don’t trust your discretion, and I don’t trust your temper. But I do trust in Rachel’s still preserving, in some remote little corner of her heart, a certain perverse weakness for you. Touch that—and trust to the consequences for the fullest disclosures that can flow from a woman’s lips! The question is—how are you to see her?”\n\n“She has been a guest of yours at this house,” I answered. “May I venture to suggest—if nothing was said about me beforehand—that I might see her here?”\n\n“Cool!” said Mr. Bruff. With that one word of comment on the reply that I had made to him, he took another turn up and down the room.\n\n“In plain English,” he said, “my house is to be turned into a trap to catch Rachel; with a bait to tempt her, in the shape of an invitation from my wife and daughters. If you were anybody else but Franklin Blake, and if this matter was one atom less serious than it really is, I should refuse point-blank. As things are, I firmly believe Rachel will live to thank me for turning traitor to her in my old age. Consider me your accomplice. Rachel shall be asked to spend the day here; and you shall receive due notice of it.”\n\n“When? Tomorrow?”\n\n“Tomorrow won’t give us time enough to get her answer. Say the day after.”\n\n“How shall I hear from you?”\n\n“Stay at home all the morning and expect me to call on you.”\n\nI thanked him for the inestimable assistance which he was rendering to me, with the gratitude that I really felt; and, declining a hospitable invitation to sleep that night at Hampstead, returned to my lodgings in London.\n\nOf the day that followed, I have only to say that it was the longest day of my life. Innocent as I knew myself to be, certain as I was that the abominable imputation which rested on me must sooner or later be cleared off, there was nevertheless a sense of self-abasement in my mind which instinctively disinclined me to see any of my friends. We often hear (almost invariably, however, from superficial observers) that guilt can look like innocence. I believe it to be infinitely the truer axiom of the two that innocence can look like guilt. I caused myself to be denied all day, to every visitor who called; and I only ventured out under cover of the night.\n\nThe next morning, Mr. Bruff surprised me at the breakfast-table. He handed me a large key, and announced that he felt ashamed of himself for the first time in his life.\n\n“Is she coming?”\n\n“She is coming today, to lunch and spend the afternoon with my wife and my girls.”\n\n“Are Mrs. Bruff, and your daughters, in the secret?”\n\n“Inevitably. But women, as you may have observed, have no principles. My family don’t feel my pangs of conscience. The end being to bring you and Rachel together again, my wife and daughters pass over the means employed to gain it, as composedly as if they were Jesuits.”\n\n“I am infinitely obliged to them. What is this key?”\n\n“The key of the gate in my back-garden wall. Be there at three this afternoon. Let yourself into the garden, and make your way in by the conservatory door. Cross the small drawing-room, and open the door in front of you which leads into the music-room. There, you will find Rachel—and find her, alone.”\n\n“How can I thank you!”\n\n“I will tell you how. Don’t blame me for what happens afterwards.”\n\nWith those words, he went out.\n\nI had many weary hours still to wait through. To while away the time, I looked at my letters. Among them was a letter from Betteredge.\n\nI opened it eagerly. To my surprise and disappointment, it began with an apology warning me to expect no news of any importance. In the next sentence the everlasting Ezra Jennings appeared again! He had stopped Betteredge on the way out of the station, and had asked who I was. Informed on this point, he had mentioned having seen me to his master Mr. Candy. Mr. Candy hearing of this, had himself driven over to Betteredge, to express his regret at our having missed each other. He had a reason for wishing particularly to speak to me; and when I was next in the neighbourhood of Frizinghall, he begged I would let him know. Apart from a few characteristic utterances of the Betteredge philosophy, this was the sum and substance of my correspondent’s letter. The warm-hearted, faithful old man acknowledged that he had written “mainly for the pleasure of writing to me.”\n\nI crumpled up the letter in my pocket, and forgot it the moment after, in the all-absorbing interest of my coming interview with Rachel.\n\nAs the clock of Hampstead church struck three, I put Mr. Bruff’s key into the lock of the door in the wall. When I first stepped into the garden, and while I was securing the door again on the inner side, I own to having felt a certain guilty doubtfulness about what might happen next. I looked furtively on either side of me; suspicious of the presence of some unexpected witness in some unknown corner of the garden. Nothing appeared, to justify my apprehensions. The walks were, one and all, solitudes; and the birds and the bees were the only witnesses.\n\nI passed through the garden; entered the conservatory; and crossed the small drawing-room. As I laid my hand on the door opposite, I heard a few plaintive chords struck on the piano in the room within. She had often idled over the instrument in this way, when I was staying at her mother’s house. I was obliged to wait a little, to steady myself. The past and present rose side by side, at that supreme moment—and the contrast shook me.\n\nAfter the lapse of a minute, I roused my manhood, and opened the door.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\nAt the moment when I showed myself in the doorway, Rachel rose from the piano.\n\nI closed the door behind me. We confronted each other in silence, with the full length of the room between us. The movement she had made in rising appeared to be the one exertion of which she was capable. All use of every other faculty, bodily or mental, seemed to be merged in the mere act of looking at me.\n\nA fear crossed my mind that I had shown myself too suddenly. I advanced a few steps towards her. I said gently, “Rachel!”\n\nThe sound of my voice brought the life back to her limbs, and the colour to her face. She advanced, on her side, still without speaking. Slowly, as if acting under some influence independent of her own will, she came nearer and nearer to me; the warm dusky colour flushing her cheeks, the light of reviving intelligence brightening every instant in her eyes. I forgot the object that had brought me into her presence; I forgot the vile suspicion that rested on my good name; I forgot every consideration, past, present, and future, which I was bound to remember. I saw nothing but the woman I loved coming nearer and nearer to me. She trembled; she stood irresolute. I could resist it no longer—I caught her in my arms, and covered her face with kisses.\n\nThere was a moment when I thought the kisses were returned; a moment when it seemed as if she, too, might have forgotten. Almost before the idea could shape itself in my mind, her first voluntary action made me feel that she remembered. With a cry which was like a cry of horror—with a strength which I doubt if I could have resisted if I had tried—she thrust me back from her. I saw merciless anger in her eyes; I saw merciless contempt on her lips. She looked me over, from head to foot, as she might have looked at a stranger who had insulted her.\n\n“You coward!” she said. “You mean, miserable, heartless coward!”\n\nThose were her first words! The most unendurable reproach that a woman can address to a man, was the reproach that she picked out to address to Me.\n\n“I remember the time, Rachel,” I said, “when you could have told me that I had offended you, in a worthier way than that. I beg your pardon.”\n\nSomething of the bitterness that I felt may have communicated itself to my voice. At the first words of my reply, her eyes, which had been turned away the moment before, looked back at me unwillingly. She answered in a low tone, with a sullen submission of manner which was quite new in my experience of her.\n\n“Perhaps there is some excuse for me,” she said. “After what you have done, is it a manly action, on your part, to find your way to me as you have found it today? It seems a cowardly experiment, to try an experiment on my weakness for you. It seems a cowardly surprise, to surprise me into letting you kiss me. But that is only a woman’s view. I ought to have known it couldn’t be your view. I should have done better if I had controlled myself, and said nothing.”\n\nThe apology was more unendurable than the insult. The most degraded man living would have felt humiliated by it.\n\n“If my honour was not in your hands,” I said, “I would leave you this instant, and never see you again. You have spoken of what I have done. What have I done?”\n\n“What have you done! You ask that question of me?”\n\n“I ask it.”\n\n“I have kept your infamy a secret,” she answered. “And I have suffered the consequences of concealing it. Have I no claim to be spared the insult of your asking me what you have done? Is all sense of gratitude dead in you? You were once a gentleman. You were once dear to my mother, and dearer still to me——”\n\nHer voice failed her. She dropped into a chair, and turned her back on me, and covered her face with her hands.\n\nI waited a little before I trusted myself to say any more. In that moment of silence, I hardly know which I felt most keenly—the sting which her contempt had planted in me, or the proud resolution which shut me out from all community with her distress.\n\n“If you will not speak first,” I said, “I must. I have come here with something serious to say to you. Will you do me the common justice of listening while I say it?”\n\nShe neither moved, nor answered. I made no second appeal to her; I never advanced an inch nearer to her chair. With a pride which was as obstinate as her pride, I told her of my discovery at the Shivering Sand, and of all that had led to it. The narrative, of necessity, occupied some little time. From beginning to end, she never looked round at me, and she never uttered a word.\n\nI kept my temper. My whole future depended, in all probability, on my not losing possession of myself at that moment. The time had come to put Mr. Bruff’s theory to the test. In the breathless interest of trying that experiment, I moved round so as to place myself in front of her.\n\n“I have a question to ask you,” I said. “It obliges me to refer again to a painful subject. Did Rosanna Spearman show you the nightgown? Yes, or No?”\n\nShe started to her feet; and walked close up to me of her own accord. Her eyes looked me searchingly in the face, as if to read something there which they had never read yet.\n\n“Are you mad?” she asked.\n\nI still restrained myself. I said quietly, “Rachel, will you answer my question?”\n\nShe went on, without heeding me.\n\n“Have you some object to gain which I don’t understand? Some mean fear about the future, in which I am concerned? They say your father’s death has made you a rich man. Have you come here to compensate me for the loss of my Diamond? And have you heart enough left to feel ashamed of your errand? Is that the secret of your pretence of innocence, and your story about Rosanna Spearman? Is there a motive of shame at the bottom of all the falsehood, this time?”\n\nI stopped her there. I could control myself no longer.\n\n“You have done me an infamous wrong!” I broke out hotly. “You suspect me of stealing your Diamond. I have a right to know, and I will know, the reason why!”\n\n“Suspect you!” she exclaimed, her anger rising with mine. “You villain, I saw you take the Diamond with my own eyes!”\n\nThe revelation which burst upon me in those words, the overthrow which they instantly accomplished of the whole view of the case on which Mr. Bruff had relied, struck me helpless. Innocent as I was, I stood before her in silence. To her eyes, to any eyes, I must have looked like a man overwhelmed by the discovery of his own guilt.\n\nShe drew back from the spectacle of my humiliation and of her triumph. The sudden silence that had fallen upon me seemed to frighten her. “I spared you, at the time,” she said. “I would have spared you now, if you had not forced me to speak.” She moved away as if to leave the room—and hesitated before she got to the door. “Why did you come here to humiliate yourself?” she asked. “Why did you come here to humiliate me?” She went on a few steps, and paused once more. “For God’s sake, say something!” she exclaimed, passionately. “If you have any mercy left, don’t let me degrade myself in this way! Say something—and drive me out of the room!”\n\nI advanced towards her, hardly conscious of what I was doing. I had possibly some confused idea of detaining her until she had told me more. From the moment when I knew that the evidence on which I stood condemned in Rachel’s mind, was the evidence of her own eyes, nothing—not even my conviction of my own innocence—was clear to my mind. I took her by the hand; I tried to speak firmly and to the purpose. All I could say was, “Rachel, you once loved me.”\n\nShe shuddered, and looked away from me. Her hand lay powerless and trembling in mine. “Let go of it,” she said faintly.\n\nMy touch seemed to have the same effect on her which the sound of my voice had produced when I first entered the room. After she had said the word which called me a coward, after she had made the avowal which branded me as a thief—while her hand lay in mine I was her master still!\n\nI drew her gently back into the middle of the room. I seated her by the side of me. “Rachel,” I said, “I can’t explain the contradiction in what I am going to tell you. I can only speak the truth as you have spoken it. You saw me—with your own eyes, you saw me take the Diamond. Before God who hears us, I declare that I now know I took it for the first time! Do you doubt me still?”\n\nShe had neither heeded nor heard me. “Let go of my hand,” she repeated faintly. That was her only answer. Her head sank on my shoulder; and her hand unconsciously closed on mine, at the moment when she asked me to release it.\n\nI refrained from pressing the question. But there my forbearance stopped. My chance of ever holding up my head again among honest men depended on my chance of inducing her to make her disclosure complete. The one hope left for me was the hope that she might have overlooked something in the chain of evidence—some mere trifle, perhaps, which might nevertheless, under careful investigation, be made the means of vindicating my innocence in the end. I own I kept possession of her hand. I own I spoke to her with all that I could summon back of the sympathy and confidence of the bygone time.\n\n“I want to ask you something,” I said. “I want you to tell me everything that happened, from the time when we wished each other good-night, to the time when you saw me take the Diamond.”\n\nShe lifted her head from my shoulder, and made an effort to release her hand. “Oh, why go back to it!” she said. “Why go back to it!”\n\n“I will tell you why, Rachel. You are the victim, and I am the victim, of some monstrous delusion which has worn the mask of truth. If we look at what happened on the night of your birthday together, we may end in understanding each other yet.”\n\nHer head dropped back on my shoulder. The tears gathered in her eyes, and fell slowly over her cheeks. “Oh!” she said, “have I never had that hope? Have I not tried to see it, as you are trying now?”\n\n“You have tried by yourself,” I answered. “You have not tried with me to help you.”\n\nThose words seemed to awaken in her something of the hope which I felt myself when I uttered them. She replied to my questions with more than docility—she exerted her intelligence; she willingly opened her whole mind to me.\n\n“Let us begin,” I said, “with what happened after we had wished each other good-night. Did you go to bed? or did you sit up?”\n\n“I went to bed.”\n\n“Did you notice the time? Was it late?”\n\n“Not very. About twelve o’clock, I think.”\n\n“Did you fall asleep?”\n\n“No. I couldn’t sleep that night.”\n\n“You were restless?”\n\n“I was thinking of you.”\n\nThe answer almost unmanned me. Something in the tone, even more than in the words, went straight to my heart. It was only after pausing a little first that I was able to go on.\n\n“Had you any light in your room?” I asked.\n\n“None—until I got up again, and lit my candle.”\n\n“How long was that, after you had gone to bed?”\n\n“About an hour after, I think. About one o’clock.”\n\n“Did you leave your bedroom?”\n\n“I was going to leave it. I had put on my dressing-gown; and I was going into my sitting-room to get a book——”\n\n“Had you opened your bedroom door?”\n\n“I had just opened it.”\n\n“But you had not gone into the sitting-room?”\n\n“No—I was stopped from going into it.”\n\n“What stopped you?\n\n“I saw a light, under the door; and I heard footsteps approaching it.”\n\n“Were you frightened?”\n\n“Not then. I knew my poor mother was a bad sleeper; and I remembered that she had tried hard, that evening, to persuade me to let her take charge of my Diamond. She was unreasonably anxious about it, as I thought; and I fancied she was coming to me to see if I was in bed, and to speak to me about the Diamond again, if she found that I was up.”\n\n“What did you do?”\n\n“I blew out my candle, so that she might think I was in bed. I was unreasonable, on my side—I was determined to keep my Diamond in the place of my own choosing.”\n\n“After blowing out the candle, did you go back to bed?”\n\n“I had no time to go back. At the moment when I blew the candle out, the sitting-room door opened, and I saw——”\n\n“You saw?”\n\n“You.”\n\n“Dressed as usual?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“In my nightgown?”\n\n“In your nightgown—with your bedroom candle in your hand.”\n\n“Alone?”\n\n“Alone.”\n\n“Could you see my face?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Plainly?”\n\n“Quite plainly. The candle in your hand showed it to me.”\n\n“Were my eyes open?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did you notice anything strange in them? Anything like a fixed, vacant expression?”\n\n“Nothing of the sort. Your eyes were bright—brighter than usual. You looked about in the room, as if you knew you were where you ought not to be, and as if you were afraid of being found out.”\n\n“Did you observe one thing when I came into the room—did you observe how I walked?”\n\n“You walked as you always do. You came in as far as the middle of the room—and then you stopped and looked about you.”\n\n“What did you do, on first seeing me?”\n\n“I could do nothing. I was petrified. I couldn’t speak, I couldn’t call out, I couldn’t even move to shut my door.”\n\n“Could I see you, where you stood?”\n\n“You might certainly have seen me. But you never looked towards me. It’s useless to ask the question. I am sure you never saw me.”\n\n“How are you sure?”\n\n“Would you have taken the Diamond? would you have acted as you did afterwards? would you be here now—if you had seen that I was awake and looking at you? Don’t make me talk of that part of it! I want to answer you quietly. Help me to keep as calm as I can. Go on to something else.”\n\nShe was right—in every way, right. I went on to other things.\n\n“What did I do, after I had got to the middle of the room, and had stopped there?”\n\n“You turned away, and went straight to the corner near the window—where my Indian cabinet stands.”\n\n“When I was at the cabinet, my back must have been turned towards you. How did you see what I was doing?”\n\n“When you moved, I moved.”\n\n“So as to see what I was about with my hands?”\n\n“There are three glasses in my sitting-room. As you stood there, I saw all that you did, reflected in one of them.”\n\n“What did you see?”\n\n“You put your candle on the top of the cabinet. You opened, and shut, one drawer after another, until you came to the drawer in which I had put my Diamond. You looked at the open drawer for a moment. And then you put your hand in, and took the Diamond out.”\n\n“How do you know I took the Diamond out?”\n\n“I saw your hand go into the drawer. And I saw the gleam of the stone between your finger and thumb, when you took your hand out.”\n\n“Did my hand approach the drawer again—to close it, for instance?”\n\n“No. You had the Diamond in your right hand; and you took the candle from the top of the cabinet with your left hand.”\n\n“Did I look about me again, after that?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did I leave the room immediately?”\n\n“No. You stood quite still, for what seemed a long time. I saw your face sideways in the glass. You looked like a man thinking, and dissatisfied with his own thoughts.”\n\n“What happened next?”\n\n“You roused yourself on a sudden, and you went straight out of the room.”\n\n“Did I close the door after me?”\n\n“No. You passed out quickly into the passage, and left the door open.”\n\n“And then?”\n\n“Then, your light disappeared, and the sound of your steps died away, and I was left alone in the dark.”\n\n“Did nothing happen—from that time, to the time when the whole house knew that the Diamond was lost?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“Are you sure of that? Might you not have been asleep a part of the time?”\n\n“I never slept. I never went back to my bed. Nothing happened until Penelope came in, at the usual time in the morning.”\n\nI dropped her hand, and rose, and took a turn in the room. Every question that I could put had been answered. Every detail that I could desire to know had been placed before me. I had even reverted to the idea of sleep-walking, and the idea of intoxication; and, again, the worthlessness of the one theory and the other had been proved—on the authority, this time, of the witness who had seen me. What was to be said next? what was to be done next? There rose the horrible fact of the Theft—the one visible, tangible object that confronted me, in the midst of the impenetrable darkness which enveloped all besides! Not a glimpse of light to guide me, when I had possessed myself of Rosanna Spearman’s secret at the Shivering Sand. And not a glimpse of light now, when I had appealed to Rachel herself, and had heard the hateful story of the night from her own lips.\n\nShe was the first, this time, to break the silence.\n\n“Well?” she said, “you have asked, and I have answered. You have made me hope something from all this, because you hoped something from it. What have you to say now?”\n\nThe tone in which she spoke warned me that my influence over her was a lost influence once more.\n\n“We were to look at what happened on my birthday night, together,” she went on; “and we were then to understand each other. Have we done that?”\n\nShe waited pitilessly for my reply. In answering her I committed a fatal error—I let the exasperating helplessness of my situation get the better of my self-control. Rashly and uselessly, I reproached her for the silence which had kept me until that moment in ignorance of the truth.\n\n“If you had spoken when you ought to have spoken,” I began; “if you had done me the common justice to explain yourself——”\n\nShe broke in on me with a cry of fury. The few words I had said seemed to have lashed her on the instant into a frenzy of rage.\n\n“Explain myself!” she repeated. “Oh! is there another man like this in the world? I spare him, when my heart is breaking; I screen him when my own character is at stake; and he—of all human beings, he—turns on me now, and tells me that I ought to have explained myself! After believing in him as I did, after loving him as I did, after thinking of him by day, and dreaming of him by night—he wonders I didn’t charge him with his disgrace the first time we met: ‘My heart’s darling, you are a Thief! My hero whom I love and honour, you have crept into my room under cover of the night, and stolen my Diamond!’ That is what I ought to have said. You villain, you mean, mean, mean villain, I would have lost fifty diamonds, rather than see your face lying to me, as I see it lying now!”\n\nI took up my hat. In mercy to her—yes! I can honestly say it—in mercy to her, I turned away without a word, and opened the door by which I had entered the room.\n\nShe followed, and snatched the door out of my hand; she closed it, and pointed back to the place that I had left.\n\n“No!” she said. “Not yet! It seems that I owe a justification of my conduct to you. You shall stay and hear it. Or you shall stoop to the lowest infamy of all, and force your way out.”\n\nIt wrung my heart to see her; it wrung my heart to hear her. I answered by a sign—it was all I could do—that I submitted myself to her will.\n\nThe crimson flush of anger began to fade out of her face, as I went back, and took my chair in silence. She waited a little, and steadied herself. When she went on, but one sign of feeling was discernible in her. She spoke without looking at me. Her hands were fast clasped in her lap, and her eyes were fixed on the ground.\n\n“I ought to have done you the common justice to explain myself,” she said, repeating my own words. “You shall see whether I did try to do you justice, or not. I told you just now that I never slept, and never returned to my bed, after you had left my sitting-room. It’s useless to trouble you by dwelling on what I thought—you would not understand my thoughts—I will only tell you what I did, when time enough had passed to help me to recover myself. I refrained from alarming the house, and telling everybody what had happened—as I ought to have done. In spite of what I had seen, I was fond enough of you to believe—no matter what!—any impossibility, rather than admit it to my own mind that you were deliberately a thief. I thought and thought—and I ended in writing to you.”\n\n“I never received the letter.”\n\n“I know you never received it. Wait a little, and you shall hear why. My letter would have told you nothing openly. It would not have ruined you for life, if it had fallen into some other person’s hands. It would only have said—in a manner which you yourself could not possibly have mistaken—that I had reason to know you were in debt, and that it was in my experience and in my mother’s experience of you, that you were not very discreet, or very scrupulous about how you got money when you wanted it. You would have remembered the visit of the French lawyer, and you would have known what I referred to. If you had read on with some interest after that, you would have come to an offer I had to make to you—the offer, privately (not a word, mind, to be said openly about it between us!), of the loan of as large a sum of money as I could get.—And I would have got it!” she exclaimed, her colour beginning to rise again, and her eyes looking up at me once more. “I would have pledged the Diamond myself, if I could have got the money in no other way! In those words I wrote to you. Wait! I did more than that. I arranged with Penelope to give you the letter when nobody was near. I planned to shut myself into my bedroom, and to have the sitting-room left open and empty all the morning. And I hoped—with all my heart and soul I hoped!—that you would take the opportunity, and put the Diamond back secretly in the drawer.”\n\nI attempted to speak. She lifted her hand impatiently, and stopped me. In the rapid alternations of her temper, her anger was beginning to rise again. She got up from her chair, and approached me.\n\n“I know what you are going to say,” she went on. “You are going to remind me again that you never received my letter. I can tell you why. I tore it up.\n\n“For what reason?” I asked.\n\n“For the best of reasons. I preferred tearing it up to throwing it away upon such a man as you! What was the first news that reached me in the morning? Just as my little plan was complete, what did I hear? I heard that you—you!!!—were the foremost person in the house in fetching the police. You were the active man; you were the leader; you were working harder than any of them to recover the jewel! You even carried your audacity far enough to ask to speak to me about the loss of the Diamond—the Diamond which you yourself had stolen; the Diamond which was all the time in your own hands! After that proof of your horrible falseness and cunning, I tore up my letter. But even then—even when I was maddened by the searching and questioning of the policeman, whom you had sent in—even then, there was some infatuation in my mind which wouldn’t let me give you up. I said to myself, ‘He has played his vile farce before everybody else in the house. Let me try if he can play it before me.’ Somebody told me you were on the terrace. I went down to the terrace. I forced myself to look at you; I forced myself to speak to you. Have you forgotten what I said?”\n\nI might have answered that I remembered every word of it. But what purpose, at that moment, would the answer have served?\n\nHow could I tell her that what she had said had astonished me, had distressed me, had suggested to me that she was in a state of dangerous nervous excitement, had even roused a moment’s doubt in my mind whether the loss of the jewel was as much a mystery to her as to the rest of us—but had never once given me so much as a glimpse at the truth? Without the shadow of a proof to produce in vindication of my innocence, how could I persuade her that I knew no more than the veriest stranger could have known of what was really in her thoughts when she spoke to me on the terrace?\n\n“It may suit your convenience to forget; it suits my convenience to remember,” she went on. “I know what I said—for I considered it with myself, before I said it. I gave you one opportunity after another of owning the truth. I left nothing unsaid that I could say—short of actually telling you that I knew you had committed the theft. And all the return you made, was to look at me with your vile pretence of astonishment, and your false face of innocence—just as you have looked at me today; just as you are looking at me now! I left you, that morning, knowing you at last for what you were—for what you are—as base a wretch as ever walked the earth!”\n\n“If you had spoken out at the time, you might have left me, Rachel, knowing that you had cruelly wronged an innocent man.”\n\n“If I had spoken out before other people,” she retorted, with another burst of indignation, “you would have been disgraced for life! If I had spoken out to no ears but yours, you would have denied it, as you are denying it now! Do you think I should have believed you? Would a man hesitate at a lie, who had done what I saw you do—who had behaved about it afterwards, as I saw you behave? I tell you again, I shrank from the horror of hearing you lie, after the horror of seeing you thieve. You talk as if this was a misunderstanding which a few words might have set right! Well! the misunderstanding is at an end. Is the thing set right? No! the thing is just where it was. I don’t believe you now! I don’t believe you found the nightgown, I don’t believe in Rosanna Spearman’s letter, I don’t believe a word you have said. You stole it—I saw you! You affected to help the police—I saw you! You pledged the Diamond to the money-lender in London—I am sure of it! You cast the suspicion of your disgrace (thanks to my base silence!) on an innocent man! You fled to the Continent with your plunder the next morning! After all that vileness, there was but one thing more you could do. You could come here with a last falsehood on your lips—you could come here, and tell me that I have wronged you!”\n\nIf I had stayed a moment more, I know not what words might have escaped me which I should have remembered with vain repentance and regret. I passed by her, and opened the door for the second time. For the second time—with the frantic perversity of a roused woman—she caught me by the arm, and barred my way out.\n\n“Let me go, Rachel” I said. “It will be better for both of us. Let me go.”\n\nThe hysterical passion swelled in her bosom—her quickened convulsive breathing almost beat on my face, as she held me back at the door.\n\n“Why did you come here?” she persisted, desperately. “I ask you again—why did you come here? Are you afraid I shall expose you? Now you are a rich man, now you have got a place in the world, now you may marry the best lady in the land—are you afraid I shall say the words which I have never said yet to anybody but you? I can’t say the words! I can’t expose you! I am worse, if worse can be, than you are yourself.” Sobs and tears burst from her. She struggled with them fiercely; she held me more and more firmly. “I can’t tear you out of my heart,” she said, “even now! You may trust in the shameful, shameful weakness which can only struggle against you in this way!” She suddenly let go of me—she threw up her hands, and wrung them frantically in the air. “Any other woman living would shrink from the disgrace of touching him!” she exclaimed. “Oh, God! I despise myself even more heartily than I despise him!”\n\nThe tears were forcing their way into my eyes in spite of me—the horror of it was to be endured no longer.\n\n“You shall know that you have wronged me, yet,” I said. “Or you shall never see me again!”\n\nWith those words, I left her. She started up from the chair on which she had dropped the moment before: she started up—the noble creature!—and followed me across the outer room, with a last merciful word at parting.\n\n“Franklin!” she said, “I forgive you! Oh, Franklin, Franklin! we shall never meet again. Say you forgive me!”\n\nI turned, so as to let my face show her that I was past speaking—I turned, and waved my hand, and saw her dimly, as in a vision, through the tears that had conquered me at last.\n\nThe next moment, the worst bitterness of it was over. I was out in the garden again. I saw her, and heard her, no more.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\nLate that evening, I was surprised at my lodgings by a visit from Mr. Bruff.\n\nThere was a noticeable change in the lawyer’s manner. It had lost its usual confidence and spirit. He shook hands with me, for the first time in his life, in silence.\n\n“Are you going back to Hampstead?” I asked, by way of saying something.\n\n“I have just left Hampstead,” he answered. “I know, Mr. Franklin, that you have got at the truth at last. But, I tell you plainly, if I could have foreseen the price that was to be paid for it, I should have preferred leaving you in the dark.”\n\n“You have seen Rachel?”\n\n“I have come here after taking her back to Portland Place; it was impossible to let her return in the carriage by herself. I can hardly hold you responsible—considering that you saw her in my house and by my permission—for the shock that this unlucky interview has inflicted on her. All I can do is to provide against a repetition of the mischief. She is young—she has a resolute spirit—she will get over this, with time and rest to help her. I want to be assured that you will do nothing to hinder her recovery. May I depend on your making no second attempt to see her—except with my sanction and approval?”\n\n“After what she has suffered, and after what I have suffered,” I said, “you may rely on me.”\n\n“I have your promise?”\n\n“You have my promise.”\n\nMr. Bruff looked relieved. He put down his hat, and drew his chair nearer to mine.\n\n“That’s settled!” he said. “Now, about the future—your future, I mean. To my mind, the result of the extraordinary turn which the matter has now taken is briefly this. In the first place, we are sure that Rachel has told you the whole truth, as plainly as words can tell it. In the second place—though we know that there must be some dreadful mistake somewhere—we can hardly blame her for believing you to be guilty, on the evidence of her own senses; backed, as that evidence has been, by circumstances which appear, on the face of them, to tell dead against you.”\n\nThere I interposed. “I don’t blame Rachel,” I said. “I only regret that she could not prevail on herself to speak more plainly to me at the time.”\n\n“You might as well regret that Rachel is not somebody else,” rejoined Mr. Bruff. “And even then, I doubt if a girl of any delicacy, whose heart had been set on marrying you, could have brought herself to charge you to your face with being a thief. Anyhow, it was not in Rachel’s nature to do it. In a very different matter to this matter of yours—which placed her, however, in a position not altogether unlike her position towards you—I happen to know that she was influenced by a similar motive to the motive which actuated her conduct in your case. Besides, as she told me herself, on our way to town this evening, if she had spoken plainly, she would no more have believed your denial then than she believes it now. What answer can you make to that? There is no answer to be made to it. Come, come, Mr. Franklin! my view of the case has been proved to be all wrong, I admit—but, as things are now, my advice may be worth having for all that. I tell you plainly, we shall be wasting our time, and cudgelling our brains to no purpose, if we attempt to try back, and unravel this frightful complication from the beginning. Let us close our minds resolutely to all that happened last year at Lady Verinder’s country house; and let us look to what we can discover in the future, instead of to what we can not discover in the past.”\n\n“Surely you forget,” I said, “that the whole thing is essentially a matter of the past—so far as I am concerned?”\n\n“Answer me this,” retorted Mr. Bruff. “Is the Moonstone at the bottom of all the mischief—or is it not?”\n\n“It is—of course.”\n\n“Very good. What do we believe was done with the Moonstone, when it was taken to London?”\n\n“It was pledged to Mr. Luker.”\n\n“We know that you are not the person who pledged it. Do we know who did?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Where do we believe the Moonstone to be now?”\n\n“Deposited in the keeping of Mr. Luker’s bankers.”\n\n“Exactly. Now observe. We are already in the month of June. Towards the end of the month (I can’t be particular to a day) a year will have elapsed from the time when we believe the jewel to have been pledged. There is a chance—to say the least—that the person who pawned it, may be prepared to redeem it when the year’s time has expired. If he redeems it, Mr. Luker must himself—according to the terms of his own arrangement—take the Diamond out of his banker’s hands. Under these circumstances, I propose setting a watch at the bank, as the present month draws to an end, and discovering who the person is to whom Mr. Luker restores the Moonstone. Do you see it now?”\n\nI admitted (a little unwillingly) that the idea was a new one, at any rate.\n\n“It’s Mr. Murthwaite’s idea quite as much as mine,” said Mr. Bruff. “It might have never entered my head, but for a conversation we had together some time since. If Mr. Murthwaite is right, the Indians are likely to be on the lookout at the bank, towards the end of the month too—and something serious may come of it. What comes of it doesn’t matter to you and me except as it may help us to lay our hands on the mysterious Somebody who pawned the Diamond. That person, you may rely on it, is responsible (I don’t pretend to know how) for the position in which you stand at this moment; and that person alone can set you right in Rachel’s estimation.”\n\n“I can’t deny,” I said, “that the plan you propose meets the difficulty in a way that is very daring, and very ingenious, and very new. But——”\n\n“But you have an objection to make?”\n\n“Yes. My objection is, that your proposal obliges us to wait.”\n\n“Granted. As I reckon the time, it requires you to wait about a fortnight—more or less. Is that so very long?”\n\n“It’s a life-time, Mr. Bruff, in such a situation as mine. My existence will be simply unendurable to me, unless I do something towards clearing my character at once.”\n\n“Well, well, I understand that. Have you thought yet of what you can do?”\n\n“I have thought of consulting Sergeant Cuff.”\n\n“He has retired from the police. It’s useless to expect the Sergeant to help you.”\n\n“I know where to find him; and I can but try.”\n\n“Try,” said Mr. Bruff, after a moment’s consideration. “The case has assumed such an extraordinary aspect since Sergeant Cuff’s time, that you may revive his interest in the inquiry. Try, and let me hear the result. In the meanwhile,” he continued, rising, “if you make no discoveries between this, and the end of the month, am I free to try, on my side, what can be done by keeping a lookout at the bank?”\n\n“Certainly,” I answered—“unless I relieve you of all necessity for trying the experiment in the interval.”\n\nMr. Bruff smiled, and took up his hat.\n\n“Tell Sergeant Cuff,” he rejoined, “that I say the discovery of the truth depends on the discovery of the person who pawned the Diamond. And let me hear what the Sergeant’s experience says to that.”\n\nSo we parted.\n\nEarly the next morning, I set forth for the little town of Dorking—the place of Sergeant Cuff’s retirement, as indicated to me by Betteredge.\n\nInquiring at the hotel, I received the necessary directions for finding the Sergeant’s cottage. It was approached by a quiet by-road, a little way out of the town, and it stood snugly in the middle of its own plot of garden ground, protected by a good brick wall at the back and the sides, and by a high quickset hedge in front. The gate, ornamented at the upper part by smartly-painted trellis-work, was locked. After ringing at the bell, I peered through the trellis-work, and saw the great Cuff’s favourite flower everywhere; blooming in his garden, clustering over his door, looking in at his windows. Far from the crimes and the mysteries of the great city, the illustrious thief-taker was placidly living out the last Sybarite years of his life, smothered in roses!\n\nA decent elderly woman opened the gate to me, and at once annihilated all the hopes I had built on securing the assistance of Sergeant Cuff. He had started, only the day before, on a journey to Ireland.\n\n“Has he gone there on business?” I asked.\n\nThe woman smiled. “He has only one business now, sir,” she said; “and that’s roses. Some great man’s gardener in Ireland has found out something new in the growing of roses—and Mr. Cuff’s away to inquire into it.”\n\n“Do you know when he will be back?”\n\n“It’s quite uncertain, sir. Mr. Cuff said he should come back directly, or be away some time, just according as he found the new discovery worth nothing, or worth looking into. If you have any message to leave for him, I’ll take care, sir, that he gets it.”\n\nI gave her my card, having first written on it in pencil: “I have something to say about the Moonstone. Let me hear from you as soon as you get back.” That done, there was nothing left but to submit to circumstances, and return to London.\n\nIn the irritable condition of my mind, at the time of which I am now writing, the abortive result of my journey to the Sergeant’s cottage simply aggravated the restless impulse in me to be doing something. On the day of my return from Dorking, I determined that the next morning should find me bent on a new effort at forcing my way, through all obstacles, from the darkness to the light.\n\nWhat form was my next experiment to take?\n\nIf the excellent Betteredge had been present while I was considering that question, and if he had been let into the secret of my thoughts, he would, no doubt, have declared that the German side of me was, on this occasion, my uppermost side. To speak seriously, it is perhaps possible that my German training was in some degree responsible for the labyrinth of useless speculations in which I now involved myself. For the greater part of the night, I sat smoking, and building up theories, one more profoundly improbable than another. When I did get to sleep, my waking fancies pursued me in dreams. I rose the next morning, with Objective-Subjective and Subjective-Objective inextricably entangled together in my mind; and I began the day which was to witness my next effort at practical action of some kind, by doubting whether I had any sort of right (on purely philosophical grounds) to consider any sort of thing (the Diamond included) as existing at all.\n\nHow long I might have remained lost in the mist of my own metaphysics, if I had been left to extricate myself, it is impossible for me to say. As the event proved, accident came to my rescue, and happily delivered me. I happened to wear, that morning, the same coat which I had worn on the day of my interview with Rachel. Searching for something else in one of the pockets, I came upon a crumpled piece of paper, and, taking it out, found Betteredge’s forgotten letter in my hand.\n\nIt seemed hard on my good old friend to leave him without a reply. I went to my writing-table, and read his letter again.\n\nA letter which has nothing of the slightest importance in it, is not always an easy letter to answer. Betteredge’s present effort at corresponding with me came within this category. Mr. Candy’s assistant, otherwise Ezra Jennings, had told his master that he had seen me; and Mr. Candy, in his turn, wanted to see me and say something to me, when I was next in the neighbourhood of Frizinghall. What was to be said in answer to that, which would be worth the paper it was written on? I sat idly drawing likenesses from memory of Mr. Candy’s remarkable-looking assistant, on the sheet of paper which I had vowed to dedicate to Betteredge—until it suddenly occurred to me that here was the irrepressible Ezra Jennings getting in my way again! I threw a dozen portraits, at least, of the man with the piebald hair (the hair in every case, remarkably like), into the waste-paper basket—and then and there, wrote my answer to Betteredge. It was a perfectly commonplace letter—but it had one excellent effect on me. The effort of writing a few sentences, in plain English, completely cleared my mind of the cloudy nonsense which had filled it since the previous day.\n\nDevoting myself once more to the elucidation of the impenetrable puzzle which my own position presented to me, I now tried to meet the difficulty by investigating it from a plainly practical point of view. The events of the memorable night being still unintelligible to me, I looked a little farther back, and searched my memory of the earlier hours of the birthday for any incident which might prove of some assistance to me in finding the clue.\n\nHad anything happened while Rachel and I were finishing the painted door? or, later, when I rode over to Frizinghall? or afterwards, when I went back with Godfrey Ablewhite and his sisters? or, later again, when I put the Moonstone into Rachel’s hands? or, later still, when the company came, and we all assembled round the dinner-table? My memory disposed of that string of questions readily enough, until I came to the last. Looking back at the social event of the birthday dinner, I found myself brought to a standstill at the outset of the inquiry. I was not even capable of accurately remembering the number of the guests who had sat at the same table with me.\n\nTo feel myself completely at fault here, and to conclude, thereupon, that the incidents of the dinner might especially repay the trouble of investigating them, formed parts of the same mental process, in my case. I believe other people, in a similar situation, would have reasoned as I did. When the pursuit of our own interests causes us to become objects of inquiry to ourselves, we are naturally suspicious of what we don’t know. Once in possession of the names of the persons who had been present at the dinner, I resolved—as a means of enriching the deficient resources of my own memory—to appeal to the memory of the rest of the guests; to write down all that they could recollect of the social events of the birthday; and to test the result, thus obtained, by the light of what had happened afterwards, when the company had left the house.\n\nThis last and newest of my many contemplated experiments in the art of inquiry—which Betteredge would probably have attributed to the clear-headed, or French, side of me being uppermost for the moment—may fairly claim record here, on its own merits. Unlikely as it may seem, I had now actually groped my way to the root of the matter at last. All I wanted was a hint to guide me in the right direction at starting. Before another day had passed over my head, that hint was given me by one of the company who had been present at the birthday feast!\n\nWith the plan of proceeding which I now had in view, it was first necessary to possess the complete list of the guests. This I could easily obtain from Gabriel Betteredge. I determined to go back to Yorkshire on that day, and to begin my contemplated investigation the next morning.\n\nIt was just too late to start by the train which left London before noon. There was no alternative but to wait, nearly three hours, for the departure of the next train. Was there anything I could do in London, which might usefully occupy this interval of time?\n\nMy thoughts went back again obstinately to the birthday dinner.\n\nThough I had forgotten the numbers, and, in many cases, the names of the guests, I remembered readily enough that by far the larger proportion of them came from Frizinghall, or from its neighbourhood. But the larger proportion was not all. Some few of us were not regular residents in the country. I myself was one of the few. Mr. Murthwaite was another. Godfrey Ablewhite was a third. Mr. Bruff—no: I called to mind that business had prevented Mr. Bruff from making one of the party. Had any ladies been present, whose usual residence was in London? I could only remember Miss Clack as coming within this latter category. However, here were three of the guests, at any rate, whom it was clearly advisable for me to see before I left town. I drove off at once to Mr. Bruff’s office; not knowing the addresses of the persons of whom I was in search, and thinking it probable that he might put me in the way of finding them.\n\nMr. Bruff proved to be too busy to give me more than a minute of his valuable time. In that minute, however, he contrived to dispose—in the most discouraging manner—of all the questions I had to put to him.\n\nIn the first place, he considered my newly-discovered method of finding a clue to the mystery as something too purely fanciful to be seriously discussed. In the second, third, and fourth places, Mr. Murthwaite was now on his way back to the scene of his past adventures; Miss Clack had suffered losses, and had settled, from motives of economy, in France; Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite might, or might not, be discoverable somewhere in London. Suppose I inquired at his club? And suppose I excused Mr. Bruff, if he went back to his business and wished me good morning?\n\nThe field of inquiry in London, being now so narrowed as only to include the one necessity of discovering Godfrey’s address, I took the lawyer’s hint, and drove to his club.\n\nIn the hall, I met with one of the members, who was an old friend of my cousin’s, and who was also an acquaintance of my own. This gentleman, after enlightening me on the subject of Godfrey’s address, told me of two recent events in his life, which were of some importance in themselves, and which had not previously reached my ears.\n\nIt appeared that Godfrey, far from being discouraged by Rachel’s withdrawal from her engagement to him had made matrimonial advances soon afterwards to another young lady, reputed to be a great heiress. His suit had prospered, and his marriage had been considered as a settled and certain thing. But, here again, the engagement had been suddenly and unexpectedly broken off—owing, it was said, on this occasion, to a serious difference of opinion between the bridegroom and the lady’s father, on the question of settlements.\n\nAs some compensation for this second matrimonial disaster, Godfrey had soon afterwards found himself the object of fond pecuniary remembrance, on the part of one of his many admirers. A rich old lady—highly respected at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes-Conversion-Society, and a great friend of Miss Clack’s (to whom she left nothing but a mourning ring)—had bequeathed to the admirable and meritorious Godfrey a legacy of five thousand pounds. After receiving this handsome addition to his own modest pecuniary resources, he had been heard to say that he felt the necessity of getting a little respite from his charitable labours, and that his doctor prescribed “a run on the Continent, as likely to be productive of much future benefit to his health.” If I wanted to see him, it would be advisable to lose no time in paying my contemplated visit.\n\nI went, then and there, to pay my visit.\n\nThe same fatality which had made me just one day too late in calling on Sergeant Cuff, made me again one day too late in calling on Godfrey. He had left London, on the previous morning, by the tidal train, for Dover. He was to cross to Ostend; and his servant believed he was going on to Brussels. The time of his return was rather uncertain; but I might be sure he would be away at least three months.\n\nI went back to my lodgings a little depressed in spirits. Three of the guests at the birthday dinner—and those three all exceptionally intelligent people—were out of my reach, at the very time when it was most important to be able to communicate with them. My last hopes now rested on Betteredge, and on the friends of the late Lady Verinder whom I might still find living in the neighbourhood of Rachel’s country house.\n\nOn this occasion, I travelled straight to Frizinghall—the town being now the central point in my field of inquiry. I arrived too late in the evening to be able to communicate with Betteredge. The next morning, I sent a messenger with a letter, requesting him to join me at the hotel, at his earliest convenience.\n\nHaving taken the precaution—partly to save time, partly to accommodate Betteredge—of sending my messenger in a fly, I had a reasonable prospect, if no delays occurred, of seeing the old man within less than two hours from the time when I had sent for him. During this interval, I arranged to employ myself in opening my contemplated inquiry, among the guests present at the birthday dinner who were personally known to me, and who were easily within my reach. These were my relatives, the Ablewhites, and Mr. Candy. The doctor had expressed a special wish to see me, and the doctor lived in the next street. So to Mr. Candy I went first.\n\nAfter what Betteredge had told me, I naturally anticipated finding traces in the doctor’s face of the severe illness from which he had suffered. But I was utterly unprepared for such a change as I saw in him when he entered the room and shook hands with me. His eyes were dim; his hair had turned completely grey; his face was wizen; his figure had shrunk. I looked at the once lively, rattlepated, humorous little doctor—associated in my remembrance with the perpetration of incorrigible social indiscretions and innumerable boyish jokes—and I saw nothing left of his former self, but the old tendency to vulgar smartness in his dress. The man was a wreck; but his clothes and his jewellery—in cruel mockery of the change in him—were as gay and as gaudy as ever.\n\n“I have often thought of you, Mr. Blake,” he said; “and I am heartily glad to see you again at last. If there is anything I can do for you, pray command my services, sir—pray command my services!”\n\nHe said those few commonplace words with needless hurry and eagerness, and with a curiosity to know what had brought me to Yorkshire, which he was perfectly—I might say childishly—incapable of concealing from notice.\n\nWith the object that I had in view, I had of course foreseen the necessity of entering into some sort of personal explanation, before I could hope to interest people, mostly strangers to me, in doing their best to assist my inquiry. On the journey to Frizinghall I had arranged what my explanation was to be—and I seized the opportunity now offered to me of trying the effect of it on Mr. Candy.\n\n“I was in Yorkshire, the other day, and I am in Yorkshire again now, on rather a romantic errand,” I said. “It is a matter, Mr. Candy, in which the late Lady Verinder’s friends all took some interest. You remember the mysterious loss of the Indian Diamond, now nearly a year since? Circumstances have lately happened which lead to the hope that it may yet be found—and I am interesting myself, as one of the family, in recovering it. Among the obstacles in my way, there is the necessity of collecting again all the evidence which was discovered at the time, and more if possible. There are peculiarities in this case which make it desirable to revive my recollection of everything that happened in the house, on the evening of Miss Verinder’s birthday. And I venture to appeal to her late mother’s friends who were present on that occasion, to lend me the assistance of their memories——”\n\nI had got as far as that in rehearsing my explanatory phrases, when I was suddenly checked by seeing plainly in Mr. Candy’s face that my experiment on him was a total failure.\n\nThe little doctor sat restlessly picking at the points of his fingers all the time I was speaking. His dim watery eyes were fixed on my face with an expression of vacant and wistful inquiry very painful to see. What he was thinking of, it was impossible to divine. The one thing clearly visible was that I had failed, after the first two or three words, in fixing his attention. The only chance of recalling him to himself appeared to lie in changing the subject. I tried a new topic immediately.\n\n“So much,” I said, gaily, “for what brings me to Frizinghall! Now, Mr. Candy, it’s your turn. You sent me a message by Gabriel Betteredge——”\n\nHe left off picking at his fingers, and suddenly brightened up.\n\n“Yes! yes! yes!” he exclaimed eagerly. “That’s it! I sent you a message!”\n\n“And Betteredge duly communicated it by letter,” I went on. “You had something to say to me, the next time I was in your neighbourhood. Well, Mr. Candy, here I am!”\n\n“Here you are!” echoed the doctor. “And Betteredge was quite right. I had something to say to you. That was my message. Betteredge is a wonderful man. What a memory! At his age, what a memory!”\n\nHe dropped back into silence, and began picking at his fingers again. Recollecting what I had heard from Betteredge about the effect of the fever on his memory, I went on with the conversation, in the hope that I might help him at starting.\n\n“It’s a long time since we met,” I said. “We last saw each other at the last birthday dinner my poor aunt was ever to give.”\n\n“That’s it!” cried Mr. Candy. “The birthday dinner!” He started impulsively to his feet, and looked at me. A deep flush suddenly overspread his faded face, and he abruptly sat down again, as if conscious of having betrayed a weakness which he would fain have concealed. It was plain, pitiably plain, that he was aware of his own defect of memory, and that he was bent on hiding it from the observation of his friends.\n\nThus far he had appealed to my compassion only. But the words he had just said—few as they were—roused my curiosity instantly to the highest pitch. The birthday dinner had already become the one event in the past, at which I looked back with strangely-mixed feelings of hope and distrust. And here was the birthday dinner unmistakably proclaiming itself as the subject on which Mr. Candy had something important to say to me!\n\nI attempted to help him out once more. But, this time, my own interests were at the bottom of my compassionate motive, and they hurried me on a little too abruptly, to the end I had in view.\n\n“It’s nearly a year now,” I said, “since we sat at that pleasant table. Have you made any memorandum—in your diary, or otherwise—of what you wanted to say to me?”\n\nMr. Candy understood the suggestion, and showed me that he understood it, as an insult.\n\n“I require no memorandum, Mr. Blake,” he said, stiffly enough. “I am not such a very old man, yet—and my memory (thank God) is to be thoroughly depended on!”\n\nIt is needless to say that I declined to understand that he was offended with me.\n\n“I wish I could say the same of my memory,” I answered. “When I try to think of matters that are a year old, I seldom find my remembrance as vivid as I could wish it to be. Take the dinner at Lady Verinder’s, for instance——”\n\nMr. Candy brightened up again, the moment the allusion passed my lips.\n\n“Ah! the dinner, the dinner at Lady Verinder’s!” he exclaimed, more eagerly than ever. “I have got something to say to you about that.”\n\nHis eyes looked at me again with the painful expression of inquiry, so wistful, so vacant, so miserably helpless to see. He was evidently trying hard, and trying in vain, to recover the lost recollection. “It was a very pleasant dinner,” he burst out suddenly, with an air of saying exactly what he wanted to say. “A very pleasant dinner, Mr. Blake, wasn’t it?” He nodded and smiled, and appeared to think, poor fellow, that he had succeeded in concealing the total failure of his memory, by a well-timed exertion of his own presence of mind.\n\nIt was so distressing that I at once shifted the talk—deeply as I was interested in his recovering the lost remembrance—to topics of local interest.\n\nHere, he got on glibly enough. Trumpery little scandals and quarrels in the town, some of them as much as a month old, appeared to recur to his memory readily. He chattered on, with something of the smooth gossiping fluency of former times. But there were moments, even in the full flow of his talkativeness, when he suddenly hesitated—looked at me for a moment with the vacant inquiry once more in his eyes—controlled himself—and went on again. I submitted patiently to my martyrdom (it is surely nothing less than martyrdom to a man of cosmopolitan sympathies, to absorb in silent resignation the news of a country town?) until the clock on the chimney-piece told me that my visit had been prolonged beyond half an hour. Having now some right to consider the sacrifice as complete, I rose to take leave. As we shook hands, Mr. Candy reverted to the birthday festival of his own accord.\n\n“I am so glad we have met again,” he said. “I had it on my mind—I really had it on my mind, Mr. Blake, to speak to you. About the dinner at Lady Verinder’s, you know? A pleasant dinner—really a pleasant dinner now, wasn’t it?”\n\nOn repeating the phrase, he seemed to feel hardly as certain of having prevented me from suspecting his lapse of memory, as he had felt on the first occasion. The wistful look clouded his face again; and, after apparently designing to accompany me to the street door, he suddenly changed his mind, rang the bell for the servant, and remained in the drawing-room.\n\nI went slowly down the doctor’s stairs, feeling the disheartening conviction that he really had something to say which it was vitally important to me to hear, and that he was morally incapable of saying it. The effort of remembering that he wanted to speak to me was, but too evidently, the only effort that his enfeebled memory was now able to achieve.\n\nJust as I reached the bottom of the stairs, and had turned a corner on my way to the outer hall, a door opened softly somewhere on the ground floor of the house, and a gentle voice said behind me:—\n\n“I am afraid, sir, you find Mr. Candy sadly changed?”\n\nI turned round, and found myself face to face with Ezra Jennings.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\nThe doctor’s pretty housemaid stood waiting for me, with the street door open in her hand. Pouring brightly into the hall, the morning light fell full on the face of Mr. Candy’s assistant when I turned, and looked at him.\n\nIt was impossible to dispute Betteredge’s assertion that the appearance of Ezra Jennings, speaking from a popular point of view, was against him. His gipsy-complexion, his fleshless cheeks, his gaunt facial bones, his dreamy eyes, his extraordinary parti-coloured hair, the puzzling contradiction between his face and figure which made him look old and young both together—were all more or less calculated to produce an unfavourable impression of him on a stranger’s mind. And yet—feeling this as I certainly did—it is not to be denied that Ezra Jennings made some inscrutable appeal to my sympathies, which I found it impossible to resist. While my knowledge of the world warned me to answer the question which he had put, acknowledging that I did indeed find Mr. Candy sadly changed, and then to proceed on my way out of the house—my interest in Ezra Jennings held me rooted to the place, and gave him the opportunity of speaking to me in private about his employer, for which he had been evidently on the watch.\n\n“Are you walking my way, Mr. Jennings?” I said, observing that he held his hat in his hand. “I am going to call on my aunt, Mrs. Ablewhite.”\n\nEzra Jennings replied that he had a patient to see, and that he was walking my way.\n\nWe left the house together. I observed that the pretty servant girl—who was all smiles and amiability, when I wished her good morning on my way out—received a modest little message from Ezra Jennings, relating to the time at which he might be expected to return, with pursed-up lips, and with eyes which ostentatiously looked anywhere rather than look in his face. The poor wretch was evidently no favourite in the house. Out of the house, I had Betteredge’s word for it that he was unpopular everywhere. “What a life!” I thought to myself, as we descended the doctor’s doorsteps.\n\nHaving already referred to Mr. Candy’s illness on his side, Ezra Jennings now appeared determined to leave it to me to resume the subject. His silence said significantly, “It’s your turn now.” I, too, had my reasons for referring to the doctor’s illness: and I readily accepted the responsibility of speaking first.\n\n“Judging by the change I see in him,” I began, “Mr. Candy’s illness must have been far more serious than I had supposed?”\n\n“It is almost a miracle,” said Ezra Jennings, “that he lived through it.”\n\n“Is his memory never any better than I have found it today? He has been trying to speak to me——”\n\n“Of something which happened before he was taken ill?” asked the assistant, observing that I hesitated.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“His memory of events, at that past time, is hopelessly enfeebled,” said Ezra Jennings. “It is almost to be deplored, poor fellow, that even the wreck of it remains. While he remembers dimly plans that he formed—things, here and there, that he had to say or do before his illness—he is perfectly incapable of recalling what the plans were, or what the thing was that he had to say or do. He is painfully conscious of his own deficiency, and painfully anxious, as you must have seen, to hide it from observation. If he could only have recovered in a complete state of oblivion as to the past, he would have been a happier man. Perhaps we should all be happier,” he added, with a sad smile, “if we could but completely forget!”\n\n“There are some events surely in all men’s lives,” I replied, “the memory of which they would be unwilling entirely to lose?”\n\n“That is, I hope, to be said of most men, Mr. Blake. I am afraid it cannot truly be said of all. Have you any reason to suppose that the lost remembrance which Mr. Candy tried to recover—while you were speaking to him just now—was a remembrance which it was important to you that he should recall?”\n\nIn saying those words, he had touched, of his own accord, on the very point upon which I was anxious to consult him. The interest I felt in this strange man had impelled me, in the first instance, to give him the opportunity of speaking to me; reserving what I might have to say, on my side, in relation to his employer, until I was first satisfied that he was a person in whose delicacy and discretion I could trust. The little that he had said, thus far, had been sufficient to convince me that I was speaking to a gentleman. He had what I may venture to describe as the unsought self-possession, which is a sure sign of good breeding, not in England only, but everywhere else in the civilised world. Whatever the object which he had in view, in putting the question that he had just addressed to me, I felt no doubt that I was justified—so far—in answering him without reserve.\n\n“I believe I have a strong interest,” I said, “in tracing the lost remembrance which Mr. Candy was unable to recall. May I ask whether you can suggest to me any method by which I might assist his memory?”\n\nEzra Jennings looked at me, with a sudden flash of interest in his dreamy brown eyes.\n\n“Mr. Candy’s memory is beyond the reach of assistance,” he said. “I have tried to help it often enough since his recovery, to be able to speak positively on that point.”\n\nThis disappointed me; and I owned it.\n\n“I confess you led me to hope for a less discouraging answer than that,” I said.\n\nEzra Jennings smiled. “It may not, perhaps, be a final answer, Mr. Blake. It may be possible to trace Mr. Candy’s lost recollection, without the necessity of appealing to Mr. Candy himself.”\n\n“Indeed? Is it an indiscretion, on my part, to ask how?”\n\n“By no means. My only difficulty in answering your question, is the difficulty of explaining myself. May I trust to your patience, if I refer once more to Mr. Candy’s illness: and if I speak of it this time without sparing you certain professional details?”\n\n“Pray go on! You have interested me already in hearing the details.”\n\nMy eagerness seemed to amuse—perhaps, I might rather say, to please him. He smiled again. We had by this time left the last houses in the town behind us. Ezra Jennings stopped for a moment, and picked some wild flowers from the hedge by the roadside. “How beautiful they are!” he said, simply, showing his little nosegay to me. “And how few people in England seem to admire them as they deserve!”\n\n“You have not always been in England?” I said.\n\n“No. I was born, and partly brought up, in one of our colonies. My father was an Englishman; but my mother—We are straying away from our subject, Mr. Blake; and it is my fault. The truth is, I have associations with these modest little hedgeside flowers—It doesn’t matter; we were speaking of Mr. Candy. To Mr. Candy let us return.”\n\nConnecting the few words about himself which thus reluctantly escaped him, with the melancholy view of life which led him to place the conditions of human happiness in complete oblivion of the past, I felt satisfied that the story which I had read in his face was, in two particulars at least, the story that it really told. He had suffered as few men suffer; and there was the mixture of some foreign race in his English blood.\n\n“You have heard, I dare say, of the original cause of Mr. Candy’s illness?” he resumed. “The night of Lady Verinder’s dinner-party was a night of heavy rain. My employer drove home through it in his gig, and reached the house wetted to the skin. He found an urgent message from a patient, waiting for him; and he most unfortunately went at once to visit the sick person, without stopping to change his clothes. I was myself professionally detained, that night, by a case at some distance from Frizinghall. When I got back the next morning, I found Mr. Candy’s groom waiting in great alarm to take me to his master’s room. By that time the mischief was done; the illness had set in.”\n\n“The illness has only been described to me, in general terms, as a fever,” I said.\n\n“I can add nothing which will make the description more accurate,” answered Ezra Jennings. “From first to last the fever assumed no specific form. I sent at once to two of Mr. Candy’s medical friends in the town, both physicians, to come and give me their opinion of the case. They agreed with me that it looked serious; but they both strongly dissented from the view I took of the treatment. We differed entirely in the conclusions which we drew from the patient’s pulse. The two doctors, arguing from the rapidity of the beat, declared that a lowering treatment was the only treatment to be adopted. On my side, I admitted the rapidity of the pulse, but I also pointed to its alarming feebleness as indicating an exhausted condition of the system, and as showing a plain necessity for the administration of stimulants. The two doctors were for keeping him on gruel, lemonade, barley-water, and so on. I was for giving him champagne, or brandy, ammonia, and quinine. A serious difference of opinion, as you see! A difference between two physicians of established local repute, and a stranger who was only an assistant in the house. For the first few days, I had no choice but to give way to my elders and betters; the patient steadily sinking all the time. I made a second attempt to appeal to the plain, undeniably plain, evidence of the pulse. Its rapidity was unchecked, and its feebleness had increased. The two doctors took offence at my obstinacy. They said, ‘Mr. Jennings, either we manage this case, or you manage it. Which is it to be?’ I said, ‘Gentlemen, give me five minutes to consider, and that plain question shall have a plain reply.’ When the time expired, I was ready with my answer. I said, ‘You positively refuse to try the stimulant treatment?’ They refused in so many words. ‘I mean to try it at once, gentlemen.’—‘Try it, Mr. Jennings, and we withdraw from the case.’ I sent down to the cellar for a bottle of champagne; and I administered half a tumbler-full of it to the patient with my own hand. The two physicians took up their hats in silence, and left the house.”\n\n“You had assumed a serious responsibility,” I said. “In your place, I am afraid I should have shrunk from it.”\n\n“In my place, Mr. Blake, you would have remembered that Mr. Candy had taken you into his employment, under circumstances which made you his debtor for life. In my place, you would have seen him sinking, hour by hour; and you would have risked anything, rather than let the one man on earth who had befriended you, die before your eyes. Don’t suppose that I had no sense of the terrible position in which I had placed myself! There were moments when I felt all the misery of my friendlessness, all the peril of my dreadful responsibility. If I had been a happy man, if I had led a prosperous life, I believe I should have sunk under the task I had imposed on myself. But I had no happy time to look back at, no past peace of mind to force itself into contrast with my present anxiety and suspense—and I held firm to my resolution through it all. I took an interval in the middle of the day, when my patient’s condition was at its best, for the repose I needed. For the rest of the four-and-twenty hours, as long as his life was in danger, I never left his bedside. Towards sunset, as usual in such cases, the delirium incidental to the fever came on. It lasted more or less through the night; and then intermitted, at that terrible time in the early morning—from two o’clock to five—when the vital energies even of the healthiest of us are at their lowest. It is then that Death gathers in his human harvest most abundantly. It was then that Death and I fought our fight over the bed, which should have the man who lay on it. I never hesitated in pursuing the treatment on which I had staked everything. When wine failed, I tried brandy. When the other stimulants lost their influence, I doubled the dose. After an interval of suspense—the like of which I hope to God I shall never feel again—there came a day when the rapidity of the pulse slightly, but appreciably, diminished; and, better still, there came also a change in the beat—an unmistakable change to steadiness and strength. Then, I knew that I had saved him; and then I own I broke down. I laid the poor fellow’s wasted hand back on the bed, and burst out crying. An hysterical relief, Mr. Blake—nothing more! Physiology says, and says truly, that some men are born with female constitutions—and I am one of them!”\n\nHe made that bitterly professional apology for his tears, speaking quietly and unaffectedly, as he had spoken throughout. His tone and manner, from beginning to end, showed him to be especially, almost morbidly, anxious not to set himself up as an object of interest to me.\n\n“You may well ask, why I have wearied you with all these details?” he went on. “It is the only way I can see, Mr. Blake, of properly introducing to you what I have to say next. Now you know exactly what my position was, at the time of Mr. Candy’s illness, you will the more readily understand the sore need I had of lightening the burden on my mind by giving it, at intervals, some sort of relief. I have had the presumption to occupy my leisure, for some years past, in writing a book, addressed to the members of my profession—a book on the intricate and delicate subject of the brain and the nervous system. My work will probably never be finished; and it will certainly never be published. It has none the less been the friend of many lonely hours; and it helped me to while away the anxious time—the time of waiting, and nothing else—at Mr. Candy’s bedside. I told you he was delirious, I think? And I mentioned the time at which his delirium came on?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, I had reached a section of my book, at that time, which touched on this same question of delirium. I won’t trouble you at any length with my theory on the subject—I will confine myself to telling you only what it is your present interest to know. It has often occurred to me in the course of my medical practice, to doubt whether we can justifiably infer—in cases of delirium—that the loss of the faculty of speaking connectedly, implies of necessity the loss of the faculty of thinking connectedly as well. Poor Mr. Candy’s illness gave me an opportunity of putting this doubt to the test. I understand the art of writing in shorthand; and I was able to take down the patient’s ‘wanderings’, exactly as they fell from his lips.—Do you see, Mr. Blake, what I am coming to at last?”\n\nI saw it clearly, and waited with breathless interest to hear more.\n\n“At odds and ends of time,” Ezra Jennings went on, “I reproduced my shorthand notes, in the ordinary form of writing—leaving large spaces between the broken phrases, and even the single words, as they had fallen disconnectedly from Mr. Candy’s lips. I then treated the result thus obtained, on something like the principle which one adopts in putting together a child’s ‘puzzle.’ It is all confusion to begin with; but it may be all brought into order and shape, if you can only find the right way. Acting on this plan, I filled in each blank space on the paper, with what the words or phrases on either side of it suggested to me as the speaker’s meaning; altering over and over again, until my additions followed naturally on the spoken words which came before them, and fitted naturally into the spoken words which came after them. The result was, that I not only occupied in this way many vacant and anxious hours, but that I arrived at something which was (as it seemed to me) a confirmation of the theory that I held. In plainer words, after putting the broken sentences together I found the superior faculty of thinking going on, more or less connectedly, in my patient’s mind, while the inferior faculty of expression was in a state of almost complete incapacity and confusion.”\n\n“One word!” I interposed eagerly. “Did my name occur in any of his wanderings?”\n\n“You shall hear, Mr. Blake. Among my written proofs of the assertion which I have just advanced—or, I ought to say, among the written experiments, tending to put my assertion to the proof—there is one, in which your name occurs. For nearly the whole of one night, Mr. Candy’s mind was occupied with something between himself and you. I have got the broken words, as they dropped from his lips, on one sheet of paper. And I have got the links of my own discovering which connect those words together, on another sheet of paper. The product (as the arithmeticians would say) is an intelligible statement—first, of something actually done in the past; secondly, of something which Mr. Candy contemplated doing in the future, if his illness had not got in the way, and stopped him. The question is whether this does, or does not, represent the lost recollection which he vainly attempted to find when you called on him this morning?”\n\n“Not a doubt of it!” I answered. “Let us go back directly, and look at the papers!”\n\n“Quite impossible, Mr. Blake.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Put yourself in my position for a moment,” said Ezra Jennings. “Would you disclose to another person what had dropped unconsciously from the lips of your suffering patient and your helpless friend, without first knowing that there was a necessity to justify you in opening your lips?”\n\nI felt that he was unanswerable, here; but I tried to argue the question, nevertheless.\n\n“My conduct in such a delicate matter as you describe,” I replied, “would depend greatly on whether the disclosure was of a nature to compromise my friend or not.”\n\n“I have disposed of all necessity for considering that side of the question, long since,” said Ezra Jennings. “Wherever my notes included anything which Mr. Candy might have wished to keep secret, those notes have been destroyed. My manuscript experiments at my friend’s bedside, include nothing, now, which he would have hesitated to communicate to others, if he had recovered the use of his memory. In your case, I have every reason to suppose that my notes contain something which he actually wished to say to you.”\n\n“And yet, you hesitate?”\n\n“And yet, I hesitate. Remember the circumstances under which I obtained the information which I possess! Harmless as it is, I cannot prevail upon myself to give it up to you, unless you first satisfy me that there is a reason for doing so. He was so miserably ill, Mr. Blake! and he was so helplessly dependent upon Me! Is it too much to ask, if I request you only to hint to me what your interest is in the lost recollection—or what you believe that lost recollection to be?”\n\nTo have answered him with the frankness which his language and his manner both claimed from me, would have been to commit myself to openly acknowledging that I was suspected of the theft of the Diamond. Strongly as Ezra Jennings had intensified the first impulsive interest which I had felt in him, he had not overcome my unconquerable reluctance to disclose the degrading position in which I stood. I took refuge once more in the explanatory phrases with which I had prepared myself to meet the curiosity of strangers.\n\nThis time I had no reason to complain of a want of attention on the part of the person to whom I addressed myself. Ezra Jennings listened patiently, even anxiously, until I had done.\n\n“I am sorry to have raised your expectations, Mr. Blake, only to disappoint them,” he said. “Throughout the whole period of Mr. Candy’s illness, from first to last, not one word about the Diamond escaped his lips. The matter with which I heard him connect your name has, I can assure you, no discoverable relation whatever with the loss or the recovery of Miss Verinder’s jewel.”\n\nWe arrived, as he said those words, at a place where the highway along which we had been walking branched off into two roads. One led to Mr. Ablewhite’s house, and the other to a moorland village some two or three miles off. Ezra Jennings stopped at the road which led to the village.\n\n“My way lies in this direction,” he said. “I am really and truly sorry, Mr. Blake, that I can be of no use to you.”\n\nHis voice told me that he spoke sincerely. His soft brown eyes rested on me for a moment with a look of melancholy interest. He bowed, and went, without another word, on his way to the village.\n\nFor a minute or more I stood and watched him, walking farther and farther away from me; carrying farther and farther away with him what I now firmly believed to be the clue of which I was in search. He turned, after walking on a little way, and looked back. Seeing me still standing at the place where we had parted, he stopped, as if doubting whether I might not wish to speak to him again. There was no time for me to reason out my own situation—to remind myself that I was losing my opportunity, at what might be the turning point of my life, and all to flatter nothing more important than my own self-esteem! There was only time to call him back first, and to think afterwards. I suspect I am one of the rashest of existing men. I called him back—and then I said to myself, “Now there is no help for it. I must tell him the truth!”\n\nHe retraced his steps directly. I advanced along the road to meet him.\n\n“Mr. Jennings,” I said. “I have not treated you quite fairly. My interest in tracing Mr. Candy’s lost recollection is not the interest of recovering the Moonstone. A serious personal matter is at the bottom of my visit to Yorkshire. I have but one excuse for not having dealt frankly with you in this matter. It is more painful to me than I can say, to mention to anybody what my position really is.”\n\nEzra Jennings looked at me with the first appearance of embarrassment which I had seen in him yet.\n\n“I have no right, Mr. Blake, and no wish,” he said, “to intrude myself into your private affairs. Allow me to ask your pardon, on my side, for having (most innocently) put you to a painful test.”\n\n“You have a perfect right,” I rejoined, “to fix the terms on which you feel justified in revealing what you heard at Mr. Candy’s bedside. I understand and respect the delicacy which influences you in this matter. How can I expect to be taken into your confidence if I decline to admit you into mine? You ought to know, and you shall know, why I am interested in discovering what Mr. Candy wanted to say to me. If I turn out to be mistaken in my anticipations, and if you prove unable to help me when you are really aware of what I want, I shall trust to your honour to keep my secret—and something tells me that I shall not trust in vain.”\n\n“Stop, Mr. Blake. I have a word to say, which must be said before you go any farther.”\n\nI looked at him in astonishment. The grip of some terrible emotion seemed to have seized him, and shaken him to the soul. His gipsy complexion had altered to a livid greyish paleness; his eyes had suddenly become wild and glittering; his voice had dropped to a tone—low, stern, and resolute—which I now heard for the first time. The latent resources in the man, for good or for evil—it was hard, at that moment, to say which—leapt up in him and showed themselves to me, with the suddenness of a flash of light.\n\n“Before you place any confidence in me,” he went on, “you ought to know, and you must know, under what circumstances I have been received into Mr. Candy’s house. It won’t take long. I don’t profess, sir, to tell my story (as the phrase is) to any man. My story will die with me. All I ask, is to be permitted to tell you, what I have told Mr. Candy. If you are still in the mind, when you have heard that, to say what you have proposed to say, you will command my attention and command my services. Shall we walk on?”\n\nThe suppressed misery in his face silenced me. I answered his question by a sign. We walked on.\n\nAfter advancing a few hundred yards, Ezra Jennings stopped at a gap in the rough stone wall which shut off the moor from the road, at this part of it.\n\n“Do you mind resting a little, Mr. Blake?” he asked. “I am not what I was—and some things shake me.”\n\nI agreed, of course. He led the way through the gap to a patch of turf on the heathy ground, screened by bushes and dwarf trees on the side nearest to the road, and commanding in the opposite direction a grandly desolate view over the broad brown wilderness of the moor. The clouds had gathered, within the last half hour. The light was dull; the distance was dim. The lovely face of Nature met us, soft and still colourless—met us without a smile.\n\nWe sat down in silence. Ezra Jennings laid aside his hat, and passed his hand wearily over his forehead, wearily through his startling white and black hair. He tossed his little nosegay of wild flowers away from him, as if the remembrances which it recalled were remembrances which hurt him now.\n\n“Mr. Blake!” he said, suddenly. “You are in bad company. The cloud of a horrible accusation has rested on me for years. I tell you the worst at once. I am a man whose life is a wreck, and whose character is gone.”\n\nI attempted to speak. He stopped me.\n\n“No,” he said. “Pardon me; not yet. Don’t commit yourself to expressions of sympathy which you may afterwards wish to recall. I have mentioned an accusation which has rested on me for years. There are circumstances in connexion with it that tell against me. I cannot bring myself to acknowledge what the accusation is. And I am incapable, perfectly incapable, of proving my innocence. I can only assert my innocence. I assert it, sir, on my oath, as a Christian. It is useless to appeal to my honour as a man.”\n\nHe paused again. I looked round at him. He never looked at me in return. His whole being seemed to be absorbed in the agony of recollecting, and in the effort to speak.\n\n“There is much that I might say,” he went on, “about the merciless treatment of me by my own family, and the merciless enmity to which I have fallen a victim. But the harm is done; the wrong is beyond all remedy. I decline to weary or distress you, sir, if I can help it. At the outset of my career in this country, the vile slander to which I have referred struck me down at once and for ever. I resigned my aspirations in my profession—obscurity was the only hope left for me. I parted with the woman I loved—how could I condemn her to share my disgrace? A medical assistant’s place offered itself, in a remote corner of England. I got the place. It promised me peace; it promised me obscurity, as I thought. I was wrong. Evil report, with time and chance to help it, travels patiently, and travels far. The accusation from which I had fled followed me. I got warning of its approach. I was able to leave my situation voluntarily, with the testimonials that I had earned. They got me another situation in another remote district. Time passed again; and again the slander that was death to my character found me out. On this occasion I had no warning. My employer said, ‘Mr. Jennings, I have no complaint to make against you; but you must set yourself right, or leave me.’ I had but one choice—I left him. It’s useless to dwell on what I suffered after that. I am only forty years old now. Look at my face, and let it tell for me the story of some miserable years. It ended in my drifting to this place, and meeting with Mr. Candy. He wanted an assistant. I referred him, on the question of capacity, to my last employer. The question of character remained. I told him what I have told you—and more. I warned him that there were difficulties in the way, even if he believed me. ‘Here, as elsewhere,’ I said ‘I scorn the guilty evasion of living under an assumed name: I am no safer at Frizinghall than at other places from the cloud that follows me, go where I may.’ He answered, ‘I don’t do things by halves—I believe you, and I pity you. If you will risk what may happen, I will risk it too.’ God Almighty bless him! He has given me shelter, he has given me employment, he has given me rest of mind—and I have the certain conviction (I have had it for some months past) that nothing will happen now to make him regret it.”\n\n“The slander has died out?” I said.\n\n“The slander is as active as ever. But when it follows me here, it will come too late.”\n\n“You will have left the place?”\n\n“No, Mr. Blake—I shall be dead. For ten years past I have suffered from an incurable internal complaint. I don’t disguise from you that I should have let the agony of it kill me long since, but for one last interest in life, which makes my existence of some importance to me still. I want to provide for a person—very dear to me—whom I shall never see again. My own little patrimony is hardly sufficient to make her independent of the world. The hope, if I could only live long enough, of increasing it to a certain sum, has impelled me to resist the disease by such palliative means as I could devise. The one effectual palliative in my case, is—opium. To that all-potent and all-merciful drug I am indebted for a respite of many years from my sentence of death. But even the virtues of opium have their limit. The progress of the disease has gradually forced me from the use of opium to the abuse of it. I am feeling the penalty at last. My nervous system is shattered; my nights are nights of horror. The end is not far off now. Let it come—I have not lived and worked in vain. The little sum is nearly made up; and I have the means of completing it, if my last reserves of life fail me sooner than I expect. I hardly know how I have wandered into telling you this. I don’t think I am mean enough to appeal to your pity. Perhaps, I fancy you may be all the readier to believe me, if you know that what I have said to you, I have said with the certain knowledge in me that I am a dying man. There is no disguising, Mr. Blake, that you interest me. I have attempted to make my poor friend’s loss of memory the means of bettering my acquaintance with you. I have speculated on the chance of your feeling a passing curiosity about what he wanted to say, and of my being able to satisfy it. Is there no excuse for my intruding myself on you? Perhaps there is some excuse. A man who has lived as I have lived has his bitter moments when he ponders over human destiny. You have youth, health, riches, a place in the world, a prospect before you. You, and such as you, show me the sunny side of human life, and reconcile me with the world that I am leaving, before I go. However this talk between us may end, I shall not forget that you have done me a kindness in doing that. It rests with you, sir, to say what you proposed saying, or to wish me good morning.”\n\nI had but one answer to make to that appeal. Without a moment’s hesitation I told him the truth, as unreservedly as I have told it in these pages.\n\nHe started to his feet, and looked at me with breathless eagerness as I approached the leading incident of my story.\n\n“It is certain that I went into the room,” I said; “it is certain that I took the Diamond. I can only meet those two plain facts by declaring that, do what I might, I did it without my own knowledge——”\n\nEzra Jennings caught me excitedly by the arm.\n\n“Stop!” he said. “You have suggested more to me than you suppose. Have you ever been accustomed to the use of opium?”\n\n“I never tasted it in my life.”\n\n“Were your nerves out of order, at this time last year? Were you unusually restless and irritable?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did you sleep badly?”\n\n“Wretchedly. Many nights I never slept at all.”\n\n“Was the birthday night an exception? Try, and remember. Did you sleep well on that one occasion?”\n\n“I do remember! I slept soundly.”\n\nHe dropped my arm as suddenly as he had taken it—and looked at me with the air of a man whose mind was relieved of the last doubt that rested on it.\n\n“This is a marked day in your life, and in mine,” he said, gravely. “I am absolutely certain, Mr. Blake, of one thing—I have got what Mr. Candy wanted to say to you this morning, in the notes that I took at my patient’s bedside. Wait! that is not all. I am firmly persuaded that I can prove you to have been unconscious of what you were about, when you entered the room and took the Diamond. Give me time to think, and time to question you. I believe the vindication of your innocence is in my hands!”\n\n“Explain yourself, for God’s sake! What do you mean?”\n\nIn the excitement of our colloquy, we had walked on a few steps, beyond the clump of dwarf trees which had hitherto screened us from view. Before Ezra Jennings could answer me, he was hailed from the high road by a man, in great agitation, who had been evidently on the look-out for him.\n\n“I am coming,” he called back; “I am coming as fast as I can!” He turned to me. “There is an urgent case waiting for me at the village yonder; I ought to have been there half an hour since—I must attend to it at once. Give me two hours from this time, and call at Mr. Candy’s again—and I will engage to be ready for you.”\n\n“How am I to wait!” I exclaimed, impatiently. “Can’t you quiet my mind by a word of explanation before we part?”\n\n“This is far too serious a matter to be explained in a hurry, Mr. Blake. I am not wilfully trying your patience—I should only be adding to your suspense, if I attempted to relieve it as things are now. At Frizinghall, sir, in two hours’ time!”\n\nThe man on the high road hailed him again. He hurried away, and left me.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\nHow the interval of suspense in which I was now condemned might have affected other men in my position, I cannot pretend to say. The influence of the two hours’ probation upon my temperament was simply this. I felt physically incapable of remaining still in any one place, and morally incapable of speaking to any one human being, until I had first heard all that Ezra Jennings had to say to me.\n\nIn this frame of mind, I not only abandoned my contemplated visit to Mrs. Ablewhite—I even shrank from encountering Gabriel Betteredge himself.\n\nReturning to Frizinghall, I left a note for Betteredge, telling him that I had been unexpectedly called away for a few hours, but that he might certainly expect me to return towards three o’clock in the afternoon. I requested him, in the interval, to order his dinner at the usual hour, and to amuse himself as he pleased. He had, as I well knew, hosts of friends in Frizinghall; and he would be at no loss how to fill up his time until I returned to the hotel.\n\nThis done, I made the best of my way out of the town again, and roamed the lonely moorland country which surrounds Frizinghall, until my watch told me that it was time, at last, to return to Mr. Candy’s house.\n\nI found Ezra Jennings ready and waiting for me.\n\nHe was sitting alone in a bare little room, which communicated by a glazed door with a surgery. Hideous coloured diagrams of the ravages of hideous diseases decorated the barren buff-coloured walls. A bookcase filled with dingy medical works, and ornamented at the top with a skull, in place of the customary bust; a large deal table copiously splashed with ink; wooden chairs of the sort that are seen in kitchens and cottages; a threadbare drugget in the middle of the floor; a sink of water, with a basin and waste-pipe roughly let into the wall, horribly suggestive of its connection with surgical operations—comprised the entire furniture of the room. The bees were humming among a few flowers placed in pots outside the window; the birds were singing in the garden, and the faint intermittent jingle of a tuneless piano in some neighbouring house forced itself now and again on the ear. In any other place, these everyday sounds might have spoken pleasantly of the everyday world outside. Here, they came in as intruders on a silence which nothing but human suffering had the privilege to disturb. I looked at the mahogany instrument case, and at the huge roll of lint, occupying places of their own on the bookshelves, and shuddered inwardly as I thought of the sounds, familiar and appropriate to the everyday use of Ezra Jennings’ room.\n\n“I make no apology, Mr. Blake, for the place in which I am receiving you,” he said. “It is the only room in the house, at this hour of the day, in which we can feel quite sure of being left undisturbed. Here are my papers ready for you; and here are two books to which we may have occasion to refer, before we have done. Bring your chair to the table, and we shall be able to consult them together.”\n\nI drew up to the table; and Ezra Jennings handed me his manuscript notes. They consisted of two large folio leaves of paper. One leaf contained writing which only covered the surface at intervals. The other presented writing, in red and black ink, which completely filled the page from top to bottom. In the irritated state of my curiosity, at that moment, I laid aside the second sheet of paper in despair.\n\n“Have some mercy on me!” I said. “Tell me what I am to expect, before I attempt to read this.”\n\n“Willingly, Mr. Blake! Do you mind my asking you one or two more questions?”\n\n“Ask me anything you like!”\n\nHe looked at me with the sad smile on his lips, and the kindly interest in his soft brown eyes.\n\n“You have already told me,” he said, “that you have never—to your knowledge—tasted opium in your life.”\n\n“To my knowledge,” I repeated.\n\n“You will understand directly why I speak with that reservation. Let us go on. You are not aware of ever having taken opium. At this time, last year, you were suffering from nervous irritation, and you slept wretchedly at night. On the night of the birthday, however, there was an exception to the rule—you slept soundly. Am I right, so far?”\n\n“Quite right!”\n\n“Can you assign any cause for the nervous suffering, and your want of sleep?”\n\n“I can assign no cause. Old Betteredge made a guess at the cause, I remember. But that is hardly worth mentioning.”\n\n“Pardon me. Anything is worth mentioning in such a case as this. Betteredge attributed your sleeplessness to something. To what?”\n\n“To my leaving off smoking.”\n\n“Had you been an habitual smoker?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did you leave off the habit suddenly?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Betteredge was perfectly right, Mr. Blake. When smoking is a habit a man must have no common constitution who can leave it off suddenly without some temporary damage to his nervous system. Your sleepless nights are accounted for, to my mind. My next question refers to Mr. Candy. Do you remember having entered into anything like a dispute with him—at the birthday dinner, or afterwards—on the subject of his profession?”\n\nThe question instantly awakened one of my dormant remembrances in connection with the birthday festival. The foolish wrangle which took place, on that occasion, between Mr. Candy and myself, will be found described at much greater length than it deserves in the tenth chapter of Betteredge’s Narrative. The details there presented of the dispute—so little had I thought of it afterwards—entirely failed to recur to my memory. All that I could now recall, and all that I could tell Ezra Jennings was, that I had attacked the art of medicine at the dinner-table with sufficient rashness and sufficient pertinacity to put even Mr. Candy out of temper for the moment. I also remembered that Lady Verinder had interfered to stop the dispute, and that the little doctor and I had “made it up again,” as the children say, and had become as good friends as ever, before we shook hands that night.\n\n“There is one thing more,” said Ezra Jennings, “which it is very important I should know. Had you any reason for feeling any special anxiety about the Diamond, at this time last year?”\n\n“I had the strongest reasons for feeling anxiety about the Diamond. I knew it to be the object of a conspiracy; and I was warned to take measures for Miss Verinder’s protection, as the possessor of the stone.”\n\n“Was the safety of the Diamond the subject of conversation between you and any other person, immediately before you retired to rest on the birthday night?”\n\n“It was the subject of a conversation between Lady Verinder and her daughter——”\n\n“Which took place in your hearing?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nEzra Jennings took up his notes from the table, and placed them in my hands.\n\n“Mr. Blake,” he said, “if you read those notes now, by the light which my questions and your answers have thrown on them, you will make two astounding discoveries concerning yourself. You will find:—First, that you entered Miss Verinder’s sitting-room and took the Diamond, in a state of trance, produced by opium. Secondly, that the opium was given to you by Mr. Candy—without your own knowledge—as a practical refutation of the opinions which you had expressed to him at the birthday dinner.”\n\nI sat with the papers in my hand completely stupefied.\n\n“Try and forgive poor Mr. Candy,” said the assistant gently. “He has done dreadful mischief, I own; but he has done it innocently. If you will look at the notes, you will see that—but for his illness—he would have returned to Lady Verinder’s the morning after the party, and would have acknowledged the trick that he had played you. Miss Verinder would have heard of it, and Miss Verinder would have questioned him—and the truth which has laid hidden for a year would have been discovered in a day.”\n\nI began to regain my self-possession. “Mr. Candy is beyond the reach of my resentment,” I said angrily. “But the trick that he played me is not the less an act of treachery, for all that. I may forgive, but I shall not forget it.”\n\n“Every medical man commits that act of treachery, Mr. Blake, in the course of his practice. The ignorant distrust of opium (in England) is by no means confined to the lower and less cultivated classes. Every doctor in large practice finds himself, every now and then, obliged to deceive his patients, as Mr. Candy deceived you. I don’t defend the folly of playing you a trick under the circumstances. I only plead with you for a more accurate and more merciful construction of motives.”\n\n“How was it done?” I asked. “Who gave me the laudanum, without my knowing it myself?”\n\n“I am not able to tell you. Nothing relating to that part of the matter dropped from Mr. Candy’s lips, all through his illness. Perhaps your own memory may point to the person to be suspected.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“It is useless, in that case, to pursue the inquiry. The laudanum was secretly given to you in some way. Let us leave it there, and go on to matters of more immediate importance. Read my notes, if you can. Familiarise your mind with what has happened in the past. I have something very bold and very startling to propose to you, which relates to the future.”\n\nThose last words roused me.\n\nI looked at the papers, in the order in which Ezra Jennings had placed them in my hands. The paper which contained the smaller quantity of writing was the uppermost of the two. On this, the disconnected words, and fragments of sentences, which had dropped from Mr. Candy in his delirium, appeared as follows:\n\n“... Mr. Franklin Blake ... and agreeable ... down a peg ... medicine ... confesses ... sleep at night ... tell him ... out of order ... medicine ... he tells me ... and groping in the dark mean one and the same thing ... all the company at the dinner-table ... I say ... groping after sleep ... nothing but medicine ... he says ... leading the blind ... know what it means ... witty ... a night’s rest in spite of his teeth ... wants sleep ... Lady Verinder’s medicine chest ... five-and-twenty minims ... without his knowing it ... tomorrow morning ... Well, Mr. Blake ... medicine today ... never ... without it ... out, Mr. Candy ... excellent ... without it ... down on him ... truth ... something besides ... excellent ... dose of laudanum, sir ... bed ... what ... medicine now.”\n\nThere, the first of the two sheets of paper came to an end. I handed it back to Ezra Jennings.\n\n“That is what you heard at his bedside?” I said.\n\n“Literally and exactly what I heard,” he answered—“except that the repetitions are not transferred here from my short-hand notes. He reiterated certain words and phrases a dozen times over, fifty times over, just as he attached more or less importance to the idea which they represented. The repetitions, in this sense, were of some assistance to me in putting together those fragments. Don’t suppose,” he added, pointing to the second sheet of paper, “that I claim to have reproduced the expressions which Mr. Candy himself would have used if he had been capable of speaking connectedly. I only say that I have penetrated through the obstacle of the disconnected expression, to the thought which was underlying it connectedly all the time. Judge for yourself.”\n\nI turned to the second sheet of paper, which I now knew to be the key to the first.\n\nOnce more, Mr. Candy’s wanderings appeared, copied in black ink; the intervals between the phrases being filled up by Ezra Jennings in red ink. I reproduce the result here, in one plain form; the original language and the interpretation of it coming close enough together in these pages to be easily compared and verified.\n\n“... Mr. Franklin Blake is clever and agreeable, but he wants taking down a peg when he talks of medicine. He confesses that he has been suffering from want of sleep at night. I tell him that his nerves are out of order, and that he ought to take medicine. He tells me that taking medicine and groping in the dark mean one and the same thing. This before all the company at the dinner-table. I say to him, you are groping after sleep, and nothing but medicine can help you to find it. He says to me, I have heard of the blind leading the blind, and now I know what it means. Witty—but I can give him a night’s rest in spite of his teeth. He really wants sleep; and Lady Verinder’s medicine chest is at my disposal. Give him five-and-twenty minims of laudanum tonight, without his knowing it; and then call tomorrow morning. ‘Well, Mr. Blake, will you try a little medicine today? You will never sleep without it.’—‘There you are out, Mr. Candy: I have had an excellent night’s rest without it.’ Then, come down on him with the truth! ‘You have had something besides an excellent night’s rest; you had a dose of laudanum, sir, before you went to bed. What do you say to the art of medicine, now?’”\n\nAdmiration of the ingenuity which had woven this smooth and finished texture out of the ravelled skein was naturally the first impression that I felt, on handing the manuscript back to Ezra Jennings. He modestly interrupted the first few words in which my sense of surprise expressed itself, by asking me if the conclusion which he had drawn from his notes was also the conclusion at which my own mind had arrived.\n\n“Do you believe as I believe,” he said, “that you were acting under the influence of the laudanum in doing all that you did, on the night of Miss Verinder’s birthday, in Lady Verinder’s house?”\n\n“I am too ignorant of the influence of laudanum to have an opinion of my own,” I answered. “I can only follow your opinion, and feel convinced that you are right.”\n\n“Very well. The next question is this. You are convinced; and I am convinced—how are we to carry our conviction to the minds of other people?”\n\nI pointed to the two manuscripts, lying on the table between us. Ezra Jennings shook his head.\n\n“Useless, Mr. Blake! Quite useless, as they stand now for three unanswerable reasons. In the first place, those notes have been taken under circumstances entirely out of the experience of the mass of mankind. Against them, to begin with! In the second place, those notes represent a medical and metaphysical theory. Against them, once more! In the third place, those notes are of my making; there is nothing but my assertion to the contrary, to guarantee that they are not fabrications. Remember what I told you on the moor—and ask yourself what my assertion is worth. No! my notes have but one value, looking to the verdict of the world outside. Your innocence is to be vindicated; and they show how it can be done. We must put our conviction to the proof—and You are the man to prove it!”\n\n“How?” I asked.\n\nHe leaned eagerly nearer to me across the table that divided us.\n\n“Are you willing to try a bold experiment?”\n\n“I will do anything to clear myself of the suspicion that rests on me now.”\n\n“Will you submit to some personal inconvenience for a time?”\n\n“To any inconvenience, no matter what it may be.”\n\n“Will you be guided implicitly by my advice? It may expose you to the ridicule of fools; it may subject you to the remonstrances of friends whose opinions you are bound to respect.”\n\n“Tell me what to do!” I broke out impatiently. “And, come what may, I’ll do it.”\n\n“You shall do this, Mr. Blake,” he answered. “You shall steal the Diamond, unconsciously, for the second time, in the presence of witnesses whose testimony is beyond dispute.”\n\nI started to my feet. I tried to speak. I could only look at him.\n\n“I believe it can be done,” he went on. “And it shall be done—if you will only help me. Try to compose yourself—sit down, and hear what I have to say to you. You have resumed the habit of smoking; I have seen that for myself. How long have you resumed it.”\n\n“For nearly a year.”\n\n“Do you smoke more or less than you did?”\n\n“More.”\n\n“Will you give up the habit again? Suddenly, mind!—as you gave it up before.”\n\nI began dimly to see his drift. “I will give it up, from this moment,” I answered.\n\n“If the same consequences follow, which followed last June,” said Ezra Jennings—“if you suffer once more as you suffered then, from sleepless nights, we shall have gained our first step. We shall have put you back again into something assimilating to your nervous condition on the birthday night. If we can next revive, or nearly revive, the domestic circumstances which surrounded you; and if we can occupy your mind again with the various questions concerning the Diamond which formerly agitated it, we shall have replaced you, as nearly as possible in the same position, physically and morally, in which the opium found you last year. In that case we may fairly hope that a repetition of the dose will lead, in a greater or lesser degree, to a repetition of the result. There is my proposal, expressed in a few hasty words. You shall now see what reasons I have to justify me in making it.”\n\nHe turned to one of the books at his side, and opened it at a place marked by a small slip of paper.\n\n“Don’t suppose that I am going to weary you with a lecture on physiology,” he said. “I think myself bound to prove, in justice to both of us, that I am not asking you to try this experiment in deference to any theory of my own devising. Admitted principles, and recognised authorities, justify me in the view that I take. Give me five minutes of your attention; and I will undertake to show you that Science sanctions my proposal, fanciful as it may seem. Here, in the first place, is the physiological principle on which I am acting, stated by no less a person than Dr. Carpenter. Read it for yourself.”\n\nHe handed me the slip of paper which had marked the place in the book. It contained a few lines of writing, as follows:—\n\n“There seems much ground for the belief, that every sensory impression which has once been recognised by the perceptive consciousness, is registered (so to speak) in the brain, and may be reproduced at some subsequent time, although there may be no consciousness of its existence in the mind during the whole intermediate period.”\n\n“Is that plain, so far?” asked Ezra Jennings.\n\n“Perfectly plain.”\n\nHe pushed the open book across the table to me, and pointed to a passage, marked by pencil lines.\n\n“Now,” he said, “read that account of a case, which has—as I believe—a direct bearing on your own position, and on the experiment which I am tempting you to try. Observe, Mr. Blake, before you begin, that I am now referring you to one of the greatest of English physiologists. The book in your hand is Doctor Elliotson’s Human Physiology; and the case which the doctor cites rests on the well-known authority of Mr. Combe.”\n\nThe passage pointed out to me was expressed in these terms:—\n\n“Dr. Abel informed me,” says Mr. Combe, “of an Irish porter to a warehouse, who forgot, when sober, what he had done when drunk; but, being drunk, again recollected the transactions of his former state of intoxication. On one occasion, being drunk, he had lost a parcel of some value, and in his sober moments could give no account of it. Next time he was intoxicated, he recollected that he had left the parcel at a certain house, and there being no address on it, it had remained there safely, and was got on his calling for it.”\n\n“Plain again?” asked Ezra Jennings.\n\n“As plain as need be.”\n\nHe put back the slip of paper in its place, and closed the book.\n\n“Are you satisfied that I have not spoken without good authority to support me?” he asked. “If not, I have only to go to those bookshelves, and you have only to read the passages which I can point out to you.”\n\n“I am quite satisfied,” I said, “without reading a word more.”\n\n“In that case, we may return to your own personal interest in this matter. I am bound to tell you that there is something to be said against the experiment as well as for it. If we could, this year, exactly reproduce, in your case, the conditions as they existed last year, it is physiologically certain that we should arrive at exactly the same result. But this—there is no denying it—is simply impossible. We can only hope to approximate to the conditions; and if we don’t succeed in getting you nearly enough back to what you were, this venture of ours will fail. If we do succeed—and I am myself hopeful of success—you may at least so far repeat your proceedings on the birthday night, as to satisfy any reasonable person that you are guiltless, morally speaking, of the theft of the Diamond. I believe, Mr. Blake, I have now stated the question, on both sides of it, as fairly as I can, within the limits that I have imposed on myself. If there is anything that I have not made clear to you, tell me what it is—and if I can enlighten you, I will.”\n\n“All that you have explained to me,” I said, “I understand perfectly. But I own I am puzzled on one point, which you have not made clear to me yet.”\n\n“What is the point?”\n\n“I don’t understand the effect of the laudanum on me. I don’t understand my walking downstairs, and along corridors, and my opening and shutting the drawers of a cabinet, and my going back again to my own room. All these are active proceedings. I thought the influence of opium was first to stupefy you, and then to send you to sleep.”\n\n“The common error about opium, Mr. Blake! I am, at this moment, exerting my intelligence (such as it is) in your service, under the influence of a dose of laudanum, some ten times larger than the dose Mr. Candy administered to you. But don’t trust to my authority—even on a question which comes within my own personal experience. I anticipated the objection you have just made: and I have again provided myself with independent testimony which will carry its due weight with it in your own mind, and in the minds of your friends.”\n\nHe handed me the second of the two books which he had by him on the table.\n\n“There,” he said, “are the far-famed Confessions of an English Opium Eater! Take the book away with you, and read it. At the passage which I have marked, you will find that when De Quincey had committed what he calls ‘a debauch of opium,’ he either went to the gallery at the Opera to enjoy the music, or he wandered about the London markets on Saturday night, and interested himself in observing all the little shifts and bargainings of the poor in providing their Sunday’s dinner. So much for the capacity of a man to occupy himself actively, and to move about from place to place under the influence of opium.”\n\n“I am answered so far,” I said; “but I am not answered yet as to the effect produced by the opium on myself.”\n\n“I will try to answer you in a few words,” said Ezra Jennings. “The action of opium is comprised, in the majority of cases, in two influences—a stimulating influence first, and a sedative influence afterwards. Under the stimulating influence, the latest and most vivid impressions left on your mind—namely, the impressions relating to the Diamond—would be likely, in your morbidly sensitive nervous condition, to become intensified in your brain, and would subordinate to themselves your judgment and your will exactly as an ordinary dream subordinates to itself your judgment and your will. Little by little, under this action, any apprehensions about the safety of the Diamond which you might have felt during the day would be liable to develop themselves from the state of doubt to the state of certainty—would impel you into practical action to preserve the jewel—would direct your steps, with that motive in view, into the room which you entered—and would guide your hand to the drawers of the cabinet, until you had found the drawer which held the stone. In the spiritualised intoxication of opium, you would do all that. Later, as the sedative action began to gain on the stimulant action, you would slowly become inert and stupefied. Later still you would fall into a deep sleep. When the morning came, and the effect of the opium had been all slept off, you would wake as absolutely ignorant of what you had done in the night as if you had been living at the Antipodes. Have I made it tolerably clear to you so far?”\n\n“You have made it so clear,” I said, “that I want you to go farther. You have shown me how I entered the room, and how I came to take the Diamond. But Miss Verinder saw me leave the room again, with the jewel in my hand. Can you trace my proceedings from that moment? Can you guess what I did next?”\n\n“That is the very point I was coming to,” he rejoined. “It is a question with me whether the experiment which I propose as a means of vindicating your innocence, may not also be made a means of recovering the lost Diamond as well. When you left Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, with the jewel in your hand, you went back in all probability to your own room——”\n\n“Yes? and what then?”\n\n“It is possible, Mr. Blake—I dare not say more—that your idea of preserving the Diamond led, by a natural sequence, to the idea of hiding the Diamond, and that the place in which you hid it was somewhere in your bedroom. In that event, the case of the Irish porter may be your case. You may remember, under the influence of the second dose of opium, the place in which you hid the Diamond under the influence of the first.”\n\nIt was my turn, now, to enlighten Ezra Jennings. I stopped him, before he could say any more.\n\n“You are speculating,” I said, “on a result which cannot possibly take place. The Diamond is, at this moment, in London.”\n\nHe started, and looked at me in great surprise.\n\n“In London?” he repeated. “How did it get to London from Lady Verinder’s house?”\n\n“Nobody knows.”\n\n“You removed it with your own hand from Miss Verinder’s room. How was it taken out of your keeping?”\n\n“I have no idea how it was taken out of my keeping.”\n\n“Did you see it, when you woke in the morning?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Has Miss Verinder recovered possession of it?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Mr. Blake! there seems to be something here which wants clearing up. May I ask how you know that the Diamond is, at this moment, in London?”\n\nI had put precisely the same question to Mr. Bruff when I made my first inquiries about the Moonstone, on my return to England. In answering Ezra Jennings, I accordingly repeated what I had myself heard from the lawyer’s own lips—and what is already familiar to the readers of these pages.\n\nHe showed plainly that he was not satisfied with my reply.\n\n“With all deference to you,” he said, “and with all deference to your legal adviser, I maintain the opinion which I expressed just now. It rests, I am well aware, on a mere assumption. Pardon me for reminding you, that your opinion also rests on a mere assumption as well.”\n\nThe view he took of the matter was entirely new to me. I waited anxiously to hear how he would defend it.\n\n“I assume,” pursued Ezra Jennings, “that the influence of the opium—after impelling you to possess yourself of the Diamond, with the purpose of securing its safety—might also impel you, acting under the same influence and the same motive, to hide it somewhere in your own room. You assume that the Hindoo conspirators could by no possibility commit a mistake. The Indians went to Mr. Luker’s house after the Diamond—and, therefore, in Mr. Luker’s possession the Diamond must be! Have you any evidence to prove that the Moonstone was taken to London at all? You can’t even guess how, or by whom, it was removed from Lady Verinder’s house! Have you any evidence that the jewel was pledged to Mr. Luker? He declares that he never heard of the Moonstone; and his bankers’ receipt acknowledges nothing but the deposit of a valuable of great price. The Indians assume that Mr. Luker is lying—and you assume again that the Indians are right. All I say, in differing with you, is—that my view is possible. What more, Mr. Blake, either logically, or legally, can be said for yours?”\n\nIt was put strongly; but there was no denying that it was put truly as well.\n\n“I confess you stagger me,” I replied. “Do you object to my writing to Mr. Bruff, and telling him what you have said?”\n\n“On the contrary, I shall be glad if you will write to Mr. Bruff. If we consult his experience, we may see the matter under a new light. For the present, let us return to our experiment with the opium. We have decided that you leave off the habit of smoking from this moment.”\n\n“From this moment.”\n\n“That is the first step. The next step is to reproduce, as nearly as we can, the domestic circumstances which surrounded you last year.”\n\nHow was this to be done? Lady Verinder was dead. Rachel and I, so long as the suspicion of theft rested on me, were parted irrevocably. Godfrey Ablewhite was away travelling on the Continent. It was simply impossible to reassemble the people who had inhabited the house, when I had slept in it last. The statement of this objection did not appear to embarrass Ezra Jennings. He attached very little importance, he said, to reassembling the same people—seeing that it would be vain to expect them to reassume the various positions which they had occupied towards me in the past times. On the other hand, he considered it essential to the success of the experiment, that I should see the same objects about me which had surrounded me when I was last in the house.\n\n“Above all things,” he said, “you must sleep in the room which you slept in, on the birthday night, and it must be furnished in the same way. The stairs, the corridors, and Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, must also be restored to what they were when you saw them last. It is absolutely necessary, Mr. Blake, to replace every article of furniture in that part of the house which may now be put away. The sacrifice of your cigars will be useless, unless we can get Miss Verinder’s permission to do that.”\n\n“Who is to apply to her for permission?” I asked.\n\n“Is it not possible for you to apply?”\n\n“Quite out of the question. After what has passed between us on the subject of the lost Diamond, I can neither see her, nor write to her, as things are now.”\n\nEzra Jennings paused, and considered for a moment.\n\n“May I ask you a delicate question?” he said.\n\nI signed to him to go on.\n\n“Am I right, Mr. Blake, in fancying (from one or two things which have dropped from you) that you felt no common interest in Miss Verinder, in former times?”\n\n“Quite right.”\n\n“Was the feeling returned?”\n\n“It was.”\n\n“Do you think Miss Verinder would be likely to feel a strong interest in the attempt to prove your innocence?”\n\n“I am certain of it.”\n\n“In that case, I will write to Miss Verinder—if you will give me leave.”\n\n“Telling her of the proposal that you have made to me?”\n\n“Telling her of everything that has passed between us today.”\n\nIt is needless to say that I eagerly accepted the service which he had offered to me.\n\n“I shall have time to write by today’s post,” he said, looking at his watch. “Don’t forget to lock up your cigars, when you get back to the hotel! I will call tomorrow morning and hear how you have passed the night.”\n\nI rose to take leave of him; and attempted to express the grateful sense of his kindness which I really felt.\n\nHe pressed my hand gently. “Remember what I told you on the moor,” he answered. “If I can do you this little service, Mr. Blake, I shall feel it like a last gleam of sunshine, falling on the evening of a long and clouded day.”\n\nWe parted. It was then the fifteenth of June. The events of the next ten days—every one of them more or less directly connected with the experiment of which I was the passive object—are all placed on record, exactly as they happened, in the Journal habitually kept by Mr. Candy’s assistant. In the pages of Ezra Jennings nothing is concealed, and nothing is forgotten. Let Ezra Jennings tell how the venture with the opium was tried, and how it ended.\n\nExtracted from the Journal of Ezra Jennings.\n\n1849.—June 15.... With some interruption from patients, and some interruption from pain, I finished my letter to Miss Verinder in time for today’s post. I failed to make it as short a letter as I could have wished. But I think I have made it plain. It leaves her entirely mistress of her own decision. If she consents to assist the experiment, she consents of her own free will, and not as a favour to Mr. Franklin Blake or to me.\n\nJune 16th.—Rose late, after a dreadful night; the vengeance of yesterday’s opium, pursuing me through a series of frightful dreams. At one time I was whirling through empty space with the phantoms of the dead, friends and enemies together. At another, the one beloved face which I shall never see again, rose at my bedside, hideously phosphorescent in the black darkness, and glared and grinned at me. A slight return of the old pain, at the usual time in the early morning, was welcome as a change. It dispelled the visions—and it was bearable because it did that.\n\nMy bad night made it late in the morning, before I could get to Mr. Franklin Blake. I found him stretched on the sofa, breakfasting on brandy and soda water, and a dry biscuit.\n\n“I am beginning, as well as you could possibly wish,” he said. “A miserable, restless night; and a total failure of appetite this morning. Exactly what happened last year, when I gave up my cigars. The sooner I am ready for my second dose of laudanum, the better I shall be pleased.”\n\n“You shall have it on the earliest possible day,” I answered. “In the meantime, we must be as careful of your health as we can. If we allow you to become exhausted, we shall fail in that way. You must get an appetite for your dinner. In other words, you must get a ride or a walk this morning, in the fresh air.”\n\n“I will ride, if they can find me a horse here. By-the-bye, I wrote to Mr. Bruff, yesterday. Have you written to Miss Verinder?”\n\n“Yes—by last night’s post.”\n\n“Very good. We shall have some news worth hearing, to tell each other tomorrow. Don’t go yet! I have a word to say to you. You appeared to think, yesterday, that our experiment with the opium was not likely to be viewed very favourably by some of my friends. You were quite right. I call old Gabriel Betteredge one of my friends; and you will be amused to hear that he protested strongly when I saw him yesterday. ‘You have done a wonderful number of foolish things in the course of your life, Mr. Franklin, but this tops them all!’ There is Betteredge’s opinion! You will make allowance for his prejudices, I am sure, if you and he happen to meet?”\n\nI left Mr. Blake, to go my rounds among my patients; feeling the better and the happier even for the short interview that I had had with him.\n\nWhat is the secret of the attraction that there is for me in this man? Does it only mean that I feel the contrast between the frankly kind manner in which he has allowed me to become acquainted with him, and the merciless dislike and distrust with which I am met by other people? Or is there really something in him which answers to the yearning that I have for a little human sympathy—the yearning, which has survived the solitude and persecution of many years; which seems to grow keener and keener, as the time comes nearer and nearer when I shall endure and feel no more? How useless to ask these questions! Mr. Blake has given me a new interest in life. Let that be enough, without seeking to know what the new interest is.\n\nJune 17th.—Before breakfast, this morning, Mr. Candy informed me that he was going away for a fortnight, on a visit to a friend in the south of England. He gave me as many special directions, poor fellow, about the patients, as if he still had the large practice which he possessed before he was taken ill. The practice is worth little enough now! Other doctors have superseded him; and nobody who can help it will employ me.\n\nIt is perhaps fortunate that he is to be away just at this time. He would have been mortified if I had not informed him of the experiment which I am going to try with Mr. Blake. And I hardly know what undesirable results might not have happened, if I had taken him into my confidence. Better as it is. Unquestionably, better as it is.\n\nThe post brought me Miss Verinder’s answer, after Mr. Candy had left the house.\n\nA charming letter! It gives me the highest opinion of her. There is no attempt to conceal the interest that she feels in our proceedings. She tells me, in the prettiest manner, that my letter has satisfied her of Mr. Blake’s innocence, without the slightest need (so far as she is concerned) of putting my assertion to the proof. She even upbraids herself—most undeservedly, poor thing!—for not having divined at the time what the true solution of the mystery might really be. The motive underlying all this proceeds evidently from something more than a generous eagerness to make atonement for a wrong which she has innocently inflicted on another person. It is plain that she has loved him, throughout the estrangement between them. In more than one place the rapture of discovering that he has deserved to be loved, breaks its way innocently through the stoutest formalities of pen and ink, and even defies the stronger restraint still of writing to a stranger. Is it possible (I ask myself, in reading this delightful letter) that I, of all men in the world, am chosen to be the means of bringing these two young people together again? My own happiness has been trampled under foot; my own love has been torn from me. Shall I live to see a happiness of others, which is of my making—a love renewed, which is of my bringing back? Oh merciful Death, let me see it before your arms enfold me, before your voice whispers to me, “Rest at last!”\n\nThere are two requests contained in the letter. One of them prevents me from showing it to Mr. Franklin Blake. I am authorised to tell him that Miss Verinder willingly consents to place her house at our disposal; and, that said, I am desired to add no more.\n\nSo far, it is easy to comply with her wishes. But the second request embarrasses me seriously.\n\nNot content with having written to Mr. Betteredge, instructing him to carry out whatever directions I may have to give, Miss Verinder asks leave to assist me, by personally superintending the restoration of her own sitting-room. She only waits a word of reply from me to make the journey to Yorkshire, and to be present as one of the witnesses on the night when the opium is tried for the second time.\n\nHere, again, there is a motive under the surface; and, here again, I fancy that I can find it out.\n\nWhat she has forbidden me to tell Mr. Franklin Blake, she is (as I interpret it) eager to tell him with her own lips, before he is put to the test which is to vindicate his character in the eyes of other people. I understand and admire this generous anxiety to acquit him, without waiting until his innocence may, or may not, be proved. It is the atonement that she is longing to make, poor girl, after having innocently and inevitably wronged him. But the thing cannot be done. I have no sort of doubt that the agitation which a meeting between them would produce on both sides—reviving dormant feelings, appealing to old memories, awakening new hopes—would, in their effect on the mind of Mr. Blake, be almost certainly fatal to the success of our experiment. It is hard enough, as things are, to reproduce in him the conditions as they existed, or nearly as they existed, last year. With new interests and new emotions to agitate him, the attempt would be simply useless.\n\nAnd yet, knowing this, I cannot find it in my heart to disappoint her. I must try if I can discover some new arrangement, before post-time, which will allow me to say Yes to Miss Verinder, without damage to the service which I have bound myself to render to Mr. Franklin Blake.\n\nTwo o’clock.—I have just returned from my round of medical visits; having begun, of course, by calling at the hotel.\n\nMr. Blake’s report of the night is the same as before. He has had some intervals of broken sleep, and no more. But he feels it less today, having slept after yesterday’s dinner. This after-dinner sleep is the result, no doubt, of the ride which I advised him to take. I fear I shall have to curtail his restorative exercise in the fresh air. He must not be too well; he must not be too ill. It is a case (as a sailor would say) of very fine steering.\n\nHe has not heard yet from Mr. Bruff. I found him eager to know if I had received any answer from Miss Verinder.\n\nI told him exactly what I was permitted to tell, and no more. It was quite needless to invent excuses for not showing him the letter. He told me bitterly enough, poor fellow, that he understood the delicacy which disinclined me to produce it. “She consents, of course, as a matter of common courtesy and common justice,” he said. “But she keeps her own opinion of me, and waits to see the result.” I was sorely tempted to hint that he was now wronging her as she had wronged him. On reflection, I shrank from forestalling her in the double luxury of surprising and forgiving him.\n\nMy visit was a very short one. After the experience of the other night, I have been compelled once more to give up my dose of opium. As a necessary result, the agony of the disease that is in me has got the upper hand again. I felt the attack coming on, and left abruptly, so as not to alarm or distress him. It only lasted a quarter of an hour this time, and it left me strength enough to go on with my work.\n\nFive o’clock.—I have written my reply to Miss Verinder.\n\nThe arrangement I have proposed reconciles the interests on both sides, if she will only consent to it. After first stating the objections that there are to a meeting between Mr. Blake and herself, before the experiment is tried, I have suggested that she should so time her journey as to arrive at the house privately, on the evening when we make the attempt. Travelling by the afternoon train from London, she would delay her arrival until nine o’clock. At that hour, I have undertaken to see Mr. Blake safely into his bedchamber; and so to leave Miss Verinder free to occupy her own rooms until the time comes for administering the laudanum. When that has been done, there can be no objection to her watching the result, with the rest of us. On the next morning, she shall show Mr. Blake (if she likes) her correspondence with me, and shall satisfy him in that way that he was acquitted in her estimation, before the question of his innocence was put to the proof.\n\nIn that sense, I have written to her. This is all that I can do today. Tomorrow I must see Mr. Betteredge, and give the necessary directions for re-opening the house.\n\nJune 18th.—Late again, in calling on Mr. Franklin Blake. More of that horrible pain in the early morning; followed, this time, by complete prostration, for some hours. I foresee, in spite of the penalties which it exacts from me, that I shall have to return to the opium for the hundredth time. If I had only myself to think of, I should prefer the sharp pains to the frightful dreams. But the physical suffering exhausts me. If I let myself sink, it may end in my becoming useless to Mr. Blake at the time when he wants me most.\n\nIt was nearly one o’clock before I could get to the hotel today. The visit, even in my shattered condition, proved to be a most amusing one—thanks entirely to the presence on the scene of Gabriel Betteredge.\n\nI found him in the room, when I went in. He withdrew to the window and looked out, while I put my first customary question to my patient. Mr. Blake had slept badly again, and he felt the loss of rest this morning more than he had felt it yet.\n\nI asked next if he had heard from Mr. Bruff.\n\nA letter had reached him that morning. Mr. Bruff expressed the strongest disapproval of the course which his friend and client was taking under my advice. It was mischievous—for it excited hopes that might never be realised. It was quite unintelligible to his mind, except that it looked like a piece of trickery, akin to the trickery of mesmerism, clairvoyance, and the like. It unsettled Miss Verinder’s house, and it would end in unsettling Miss Verinder herself. He had put the case (without mentioning names) to an eminent physician; and the eminent physician had smiled, had shaken his head, and had said—nothing. On these grounds, Mr. Bruff entered his protest, and left it there.\n\nMy next inquiry related to the subject of the Diamond. Had the lawyer produced any evidence to prove that the jewel was in London?\n\nNo, the lawyer had simply declined to discuss the question. He was himself satisfied that the Moonstone had been pledged to Mr. Luker. His eminent absent friend, Mr. Murthwaite (whose consummate knowledge of the Indian character no one could deny), was satisfied also. Under these circumstances, and with the many demands already made on him, he must decline entering into any disputes on the subject of evidence. Time would show; and Mr. Bruff was willing to wait for time.\n\nIt was quite plain—even if Mr. Blake had not made it plainer still by reporting the substance of the letter, instead of reading what was actually written—that distrust of me was at the bottom of all this. Having myself foreseen that result, I was neither mortified nor surprised. I asked Mr. Blake if his friend’s protest had shaken him. He answered emphatically, that it had not produced the slightest effect on his mind. I was free after that to dismiss Mr. Bruff from consideration—and I did dismiss him accordingly.\n\nA pause in the talk between us, followed—and Gabriel Betteredge came out from his retirement at the window.\n\n“Can you favour me with your attention, sir?” he inquired, addressing himself to me.\n\n“I am quite at your service,” I answered.\n\nBetteredge took a chair and seated himself at the table. He produced a huge old-fashioned leather pocket-book, with a pencil of dimensions to match. Having put on his spectacles, he opened the pocket-book, at a blank page, and addressed himself to me once more.\n\n“I have lived,” said Betteredge, looking at me sternly, “nigh on fifty years in the service of my late lady. I was page-boy before that, in the service of the old lord, her father. I am now somewhere between seventy and eighty years of age—never mind exactly where! I am reckoned to have got as pretty a knowledge and experience of the world as most men. And what does it all end in? It ends, Mr. Ezra Jennings, in a conjuring trick being performed on Mr. Franklin Blake, by a doctor’s assistant with a bottle of laudanum—and by the living jingo, I’m appointed, in my old age, to be conjurer’s boy!”\n\nMr. Blake burst out laughing. I attempted to speak. Betteredge held up his hand, in token that he had not done yet.\n\n“Not a word, Mr. Jennings!” he said, “It don’t want a word, sir, from you. I have got my principles, thank God. If an order comes to me, which is own brother to an order come from Bedlam, it don’t matter. So long as I get it from my master or mistress, as the case may be, I obey it. I may have my own opinion, which is also, you will please to remember, the opinion of Mr. Bruff—the Great Mr. Bruff!” said Betteredge, raising his voice, and shaking his head at me solemnly. “It don’t matter; I withdraw my opinion, for all that. My young lady says, ‘Do it.’ And I say, ‘Miss, it shall be done.’ Here I am, with my book and my pencil—the latter not pointed so well as I could wish, but when Christians take leave of their senses, who is to expect that pencils will keep their points? Give me your orders, Mr. Jennings. I’ll have them in writing, sir. I’m determined not to be behind ’em, or before ’em, by so much as a hair’s breadth. I’m a blind agent—that’s what I am. A blind agent!” repeated Betteredge, with infinite relish of his own description of himself.\n\n“I am very sorry,” I began, “that you and I don’t agree——”\n\n“Don’t bring me, into it!” interposed Betteredge. “This is not a matter of agreement, it’s a matter of obedience. Issue your directions, sir—issue your directions!”\n\nMr. Blake made me a sign to take him at his word. I “issued my directions” as plainly and as gravely as I could.\n\n“I wish certain parts of the house to be re-opened,” I said, “and to be furnished, exactly as they were furnished at this time last year.”\n\nBetteredge gave his imperfectly-pointed pencil a preliminary lick with his tongue. “Name the parts, Mr. Jennings!” he said loftily.\n\n“First, the inner hall, leading to the chief staircase.”\n\n“‘First, the inner hall,’” Betteredge wrote. “Impossible to furnish that, sir, as it was furnished last year—to begin with.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because there was a stuffed buzzard, Mr. Jennings, in the hall last year. When the family left, the buzzard was put away with the other things. When the buzzard was put away—he burst.”\n\n“We will except the buzzard then.”\n\nBetteredge took a note of the exception. “‘The inner hall to be furnished again, as furnished last year. A burst buzzard alone excepted.’ Please to go on, Mr. Jennings.”\n\n“The carpet to be laid down on the stairs, as before.”\n\n“‘The carpet to be laid down on the stairs, as before.’ Sorry to disappoint you, sir. But that can’t be done either.”\n\n“Why not?”\n\n“Because the man who laid that carpet down is dead, Mr. Jennings—and the like of him for reconciling together a carpet and a corner, is not to be found in all England, look where you may.”\n\n“Very well. We must try the next best man in England.”\n\nBetteredge took another note; and I went on issuing my directions.\n\n“Miss Verinder’s sitting-room to be restored exactly to what it was last year. Also, the corridor leading from the sitting-room to the first landing. Also, the second corridor, leading from the second landing to the best bedrooms. Also, the bedroom occupied last June by Mr. Franklin Blake.”\n\nBetteredge’s blunt pencil followed me conscientiously, word by word. “Go on, sir,” he said, with sardonic gravity. “There’s a deal of writing left in the point of this pencil yet.”\n\nI told him that I had no more directions to give. “Sir,” said Betteredge, “in that case, I have a point or two to put on my own behalf.” He opened the pocket-book at a new page, and gave the inexhaustible pencil another preliminary lick.\n\n“I wish to know,” he began, “whether I may, or may not, wash my hands——”\n\n“You may decidedly,” said Mr. Blake. “I’ll ring for the waiter.”\n\n“——of certain responsibilities,” pursued Betteredge, impenetrably declining to see anybody in the room but himself and me. “As to Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, to begin with. When we took up the carpet last year, Mr. Jennings, we found a surprising quantity of pins. Am I responsible for putting back the pins?”\n\n“Certainly not.”\n\nBetteredge made a note of that concession, on the spot.\n\n“As to the first corridor next,” he resumed. “When we moved the ornaments in that part, we moved a statue of a fat naked child—profanely described in the catalogue of the house as ‘Cupid, god of Love.’ He had two wings last year, in the fleshy part of his shoulders. My eye being off him, for the moment, he lost one of them. Am I responsible for Cupid’s wing?”\n\nI made another concession, and Betteredge made another note.\n\n“As to the second corridor,” he went on. “There having been nothing in it, last year, but the doors of the rooms (to every one of which I can swear, if necessary), my mind is easy, I admit, respecting that part of the house only. But, as to Mr. Franklin’s bedroom (if that is to be put back to what it was before), I want to know who is responsible for keeping it in a perpetual state of litter, no matter how often it may be set right—his trousers here, his towels there, and his French novels everywhere. I say, who is responsible for untidying the tidiness of Mr. Franklin’s room, him or me?”\n\nMr. Blake declared that he would assume the whole responsibility with the greatest pleasure. Betteredge obstinately declined to listen to any solution of the difficulty, without first referring it to my sanction and approval. I accepted Mr. Blake’s proposal; and Betteredge made a last entry in the pocket-book to that effect.\n\n“Look in when you like, Mr. Jennings, beginning from tomorrow,” he said, getting on his legs. “You will find me at work, with the necessary persons to assist me. I respectfully beg to thank you, sir, for overlooking the case of the stuffed buzzard, and the other case of the Cupid’s wing—as also for permitting me to wash my hands of all responsibility in respect of the pins on the carpet, and the litter in Mr. Franklin’s room. Speaking as a servant, I am deeply indebted to you. Speaking as a man, I consider you to be a person whose head is full of maggots, and I take up my testimony against your experiment as a delusion and a snare. Don’t be afraid, on that account, of my feelings as a man getting in the way of my duty as a servant! You shall be obeyed. The maggots notwithstanding, sir, you shall be obeyed. If it ends in your setting the house on fire, Damme if I send for the engines, unless you ring the bell and order them first!”\n\nWith that farewell assurance, he made me a bow, and walked out of the room.\n\n“Do you think we can depend on him?” I asked.\n\n“Implicitly,” answered Mr. Blake. “When we go to the house, we shall find nothing neglected, and nothing forgotten.”\n\nJune 19th.—Another protest against our contemplated proceedings! From a lady this time.\n\nThe morning’s post brought me two letters. One from Miss Verinder, consenting, in the kindest manner, to the arrangement that I have proposed. The other from the lady under whose care she is living—one Mrs. Merridew.\n\nMrs. Merridew presents her compliments, and does not pretend to understand the subject on which I have been corresponding with Miss Verinder, in its scientific bearings. Viewed in its social bearings, however, she feels free to pronounce an opinion. I am probably, Mrs. Merridew thinks, not aware that Miss Verinder is barely nineteen years of age. To allow a young lady, at her time of life, to be present (without a “chaperone”) in a house full of men among whom a medical experiment is being carried on, is an outrage on propriety which Mrs. Merridew cannot possibly permit. If the matter is allowed to proceed, she will feel it to be her duty—at a serious sacrifice of her own personal convenience—to accompany Miss Verinder to Yorkshire. Under these circumstances, she ventures to request that I will kindly reconsider the subject; seeing that Miss Verinder declines to be guided by any opinion but mine. Her presence cannot possibly be necessary; and a word from me, to that effect, would relieve both Mrs. Merridew and myself of a very unpleasant responsibility.\n\nTranslated from polite commonplace into plain English, the meaning of this is, as I take it, that Mrs. Merridew stands in mortal fear of the opinion of the world. She has unfortunately appealed to the very last man in existence who has any reason to regard that opinion with respect. I won’t disappoint Miss Verinder; and I won’t delay a reconciliation between two young people who love each other, and who have been parted too long already. Translated from plain English into polite commonplace, this means that Mr. Jennings presents his compliments to Mrs. Merridew, and regrets that he cannot feel justified in interfering any farther in the matter.\n\nMr. Blake’s report of himself, this morning, was the same as before. We determined not to disturb Betteredge by overlooking him at the house today. Tomorrow will be time enough for our first visit of inspection.\n\nJune 20th.—Mr. Blake is beginning to feel his continued restlessness at night. The sooner the rooms are refurnished, now, the better.\n\nOn our way to the house, this morning, he consulted me, with some nervous impatience and irresolution, about a letter (forwarded to him from London) which he had received from Sergeant Cuff.\n\nThe Sergeant writes from Ireland. He acknowledges the receipt (through his housekeeper) of a card and message which Mr. Blake left at his residence near Dorking, and announces his return to England as likely to take place in a week or less. In the meantime, he requests to be favoured with Mr. Blake’s reasons for wishing to speak to him (as stated in the message) on the subject of the Moonstone. If Mr. Blake can convict him of having made any serious mistake, in the course of his last year’s inquiry concerning the Diamond, he will consider it a duty (after the liberal manner in which he was treated by the late Lady Verinder) to place himself at that gentleman’s disposal. If not, he begs permission to remain in his retirement, surrounded by the peaceful horticultural attractions of a country life.\n\nAfter reading the letter, I had no hesitation in advising Mr. Blake to inform Sergeant Cuff, in reply, of all that had happened since the inquiry was suspended last year, and to leave him to draw his own conclusions from the plain facts.\n\nOn second thoughts I also suggested inviting the Sergeant to be present at the experiment, in the event of his returning to England in time to join us. He would be a valuable witness to have, in any case; and, if I proved to be wrong in believing the Diamond to be hidden in Mr. Blake’s room, his advice might be of great importance, at a future stage of the proceedings over which I could exercise no control. This last consideration appeared to decide Mr. Blake. He promised to follow my advice.\n\nThe sound of the hammer informed us that the work of refurnishing was in full progress, as we entered the drive that led to the house.\n\nBetteredge, attired for the occasion in a fisherman’s red cap, and an apron of green baize, met us in the outer hall. The moment he saw me, he pulled out the pocket-book and pencil, and obstinately insisted on taking notes of everything that I said to him. Look where we might, we found, as Mr. Blake had foretold, that the work was advancing as rapidly and as intelligently as it was possible to desire. But there was still much to be done in the inner hall, and in Miss Verinder’s room. It seemed doubtful whether the house would be ready for us before the end of the week.\n\nHaving congratulated Betteredge on the progress that he had made (he persisted in taking notes every time I opened my lips; declining, at the same time, to pay the slightest attention to anything said by Mr. Blake); and having promised to return for a second visit of inspection in a day or two, we prepared to leave the house, going out by the back way. Before we were clear of the passages downstairs, I was stopped by Betteredge, just as I was passing the door which led into his own room.\n\n“Could I say two words to you in private?” he asked, in a mysterious whisper.\n\nI consented of course. Mr. Blake walked on to wait for me in the garden, while I accompanied Betteredge into his room. I fully anticipated a demand for certain new concessions, following the precedent already established in the cases of the stuffed buzzard, and the Cupid’s wing. To my great surprise, Betteredge laid his hand confidentially on my arm, and put this extraordinary question to me:\n\n“Mr. Jennings, do you happen to be acquainted with Robinson Crusoe?”\n\nI answered that I had read Robinson Crusoe when I was a child.\n\n“Not since then?” inquired Betteredge.\n\n“Not since then.”\n\nHe fell back a few steps, and looked at me with an expression of compassionate curiosity, tempered by superstitious awe.\n\n“He has not read Robinson Crusoe since he was a child,” said Betteredge, speaking to himself—not to me. “Let’s try how Robinson Crusoe strikes him now!”\n\nHe unlocked a cupboard in a corner, and produced a dirty and dog’s-eared book, which exhaled a strong odour of stale tobacco as he turned over the leaves. Having found a passage of which he was apparently in search, he requested me to join him in the corner; still mysteriously confidential, and still speaking under his breath.\n\n“In respect to this hocus-pocus of yours, sir, with the laudanum and Mr. Franklin Blake,” he began. “While the workpeople are in the house, my duty as a servant gets the better of my feelings as a man. When the workpeople are gone, my feelings as a man get the better of my duty as a servant. Very good. Last night, Mr. Jennings, it was borne in powerfully on my mind that this new medical enterprise of yours would end badly. If I had yielded to that secret Dictate, I should have put all the furniture away again with my own hand, and have warned the workmen off the premises when they came the next morning.”\n\n“I am glad to find, from what I have seen upstairs,” I said, “that you resisted the secret Dictate.”\n\n“Resisted isn’t the word,” answered Betteredge. “Wrostled is the word. I wrostled, sir, between the silent orders in my bosom pulling me one way, and the written orders in my pocket-book pushing me the other, until (saving your presence) I was in a cold sweat. In that dreadful perturbation of mind and laxity of body, to what remedy did I apply? To the remedy, sir, which has never failed me yet for the last thirty years and more—to This Book!”\n\nHe hit the book a sounding blow with his open hand, and struck out of it a stronger smell of stale tobacco than ever.\n\n“What did I find here,” pursued Betteredge, “at the first page I opened? This awful bit, sir, page one hundred and seventy-eight, as follows:—‘Upon these, and many like Reflections, I afterwards made it a certain rule with me, That whenever I found those secret Hints or Pressings of my Mind, to doing, or not doing any Thing that presented; or to going this Way, or that Way, I never failed to obey the secret Dictate.’ As I live by bread, Mr. Jennings, those were the first words that met my eye, exactly at the time when I myself was setting the secret Dictate at defiance! You don’t see anything at all out of the common in that, do you, sir?”\n\n“I see a coincidence—nothing more.”\n\n“You don’t feel at all shaken, Mr. Jennings, in respect to this medical enterprise of yours?\n\n“Not the least in the world.”\n\nBetteredge stared hard at me, in dead silence. He closed the book with great deliberation; he locked it up again in the cupboard with extraordinary care; he wheeled round, and stared hard at me once more. Then he spoke.\n\n“Sir,” he said gravely, “there are great allowances to be made for a man who has not read Robinson Crusoe since he was a child. I wish you good morning.”\n\nHe opened his door with a low bow, and left me at liberty to find my own way into the garden. I met Mr. Blake returning to the house.\n\n“You needn’t tell me what has happened,” he said. “Betteredge has played his last card: he has made another prophetic discovery in Robinson Crusoe. Have you humoured his favourite delusion? No? You have let him see that you don’t believe in Robinson Crusoe? Mr. Jennings! you have fallen to the lowest possible place in Betteredge’s estimation. Say what you like, and do what you like, for the future. You will find that he won’t waste another word on you now.”\n\nJune 21st.—A short entry must suffice in my journal today.\n\nMr. Blake has had the worst night that he has passed yet. I have been obliged, greatly against my will, to prescribe for him. Men of his sensitive organisation are fortunately quick in feeling the effect of remedial measures. Otherwise, I should be inclined to fear that he will be totally unfit for the experiment when the time comes to try it.\n\nAs for myself, after some little remission of my pains for the last two days I had an attack this morning, of which I shall say nothing but that it has decided me to return to the opium. I shall close this book, and take my full dose—five hundred drops.\n\nJune 22nd.—Our prospects look better today. Mr. Blake’s nervous suffering is greatly allayed. He slept a little last night. My night, thanks to the opium, was the night of a man who is stunned. I can’t say that I woke this morning; the fitter expression would be, that I recovered my senses.\n\nWe drove to the house to see if the refurnishing was done. It will be completed tomorrow—Saturday. As Mr. Blake foretold, Betteredge raised no further obstacles. From first to last, he was ominously polite, and ominously silent.\n\nMy medical enterprise (as Betteredge calls it) must now, inevitably, be delayed until Monday next. Tomorrow evening the workmen will be late in the house. On the next day, the established Sunday tyranny which is one of the institutions of this free country, so times the trains as to make it impossible to ask anybody to travel to us from London. Until Monday comes, there is nothing to be done but to watch Mr. Blake carefully, and to keep him, if possible, in the same state in which I find him today.\n\nIn the meanwhile, I have prevailed on him to write to Mr. Bruff, making a point of it that he shall be present as one of the witnesses. I especially choose the lawyer, because he is strongly prejudiced against us. If we convince him, we place our victory beyond the possibility of dispute.\n\nMr. Blake has also written to Sergeant Cuff; and I have sent a line to Miss Verinder. With these, and with old Betteredge (who is really a person of importance in the family) we shall have witnesses enough for the purpose—without including Mrs. Merridew, if Mrs. Merridew persists in sacrificing herself to the opinion of the world.\n\nJune 23rd.—The vengeance of the opium overtook me again last night. No matter; I must go on with it now till Monday is past and gone.\n\nMr. Blake is not so well again today. At two this morning, he confesses that he opened the drawer in which his cigars are put away. He only succeeded in locking it up again by a violent effort. His next proceeding, in case of temptation, was to throw the key out of window. The waiter brought it in this morning, discovered at the bottom of an empty cistern—such is Fate! I have taken possession of the key until Tuesday next.\n\nJune 24th.—Mr. Blake and I took a long drive in an open carriage. We both felt beneficially the blessed influence of the soft summer air. I dined with him at the hotel. To my great relief—for I found him in an over-wrought, over-excited state this morning—he had two hours’ sound sleep on the sofa after dinner. If he has another bad night, now—I am not afraid of the consequences.\n\nJune 25th, Monday.—The day of the experiment! It is five o’clock in the afternoon. We have just arrived at the house.\n\nThe first and foremost question, is the question of Mr. Blake’s health.\n\nSo far as it is possible for me to judge, he promises (physically speaking) to be quite as susceptible to the action of the opium tonight as he was at this time last year. He is, this afternoon, in a state of nervous sensitiveness which just stops short of nervous irritation. He changes colour readily; his hand is not quite steady; and he starts at chance noises, and at unexpected appearances of persons and things.\n\nThese results have all been produced by deprivation of sleep, which is in its turn the nervous consequence of a sudden cessation in the habit of smoking, after that habit has been carried to an extreme. Here are the same causes at work again, which operated last year; and here are, apparently, the same effects. Will the parallel still hold good, when the final test has been tried? The events of the night must decide.\n\nWhile I write these lines, Mr. Blake is amusing himself at the billiard table in the inner hall, practising different strokes in the game, as he was accustomed to practise them when he was a guest in this house in June last. I have brought my journal here, partly with a view to occupying the idle hours which I am sure to have on my hands between this and tomorrow morning; partly in the hope that something may happen which it may be worth my while to place on record at the time.\n\nHave I omitted anything, thus far? A glance at yesterday’s entry shows me that I have forgotten to note the arrival of the morning’s post. Let me set this right before I close these leaves for the present, and join Mr. Blake.\n\nI received a few lines then, yesterday, from Miss Verinder. She has arranged to travel by the afternoon train, as I recommended. Mrs. Merridew has insisted on accompanying her. The note hints that the old lady’s generally excellent temper is a little ruffled, and requests all due indulgence for her, in consideration of her age and her habits. I will endeavour, in my relations with Mrs. Merridew, to emulate the moderation which Betteredge displays in his relations with me. He received us today, portentously arrayed in his best black suit, and his stiffest white cravat. Whenever he looks my way, he remembers that I have not read Robinson Crusoe since I was a child, and he respectfully pities me.\n\nYesterday, also, Mr. Blake had the lawyer’s answer. Mr. Bruff accepts the invitation—under protest. It is, he thinks, clearly necessary that a gentleman possessed of the average allowance of common sense, should accompany Miss Verinder to the scene of, what we will venture to call, the proposed exhibition. For want of a better escort, Mr. Bruff himself will be that gentleman.—So here is poor Miss Verinder provided with two “chaperones.” It is a relief to think that the opinion of the world must surely be satisfied with this!\n\nNothing has been heard of Sergeant Cuff. He is no doubt still in Ireland. We must not expect to see him tonight.\n\nBetteredge has just come in, to say that Mr. Blake has asked for me. I must lay down my pen for the present.\n\nSeven o’clock.—We have been all over the refurnished rooms and staircases again; and we have had a pleasant stroll in the shrubbery, which was Mr. Blake’s favourite walk when he was here last. In this way, I hope to revive the old impressions of places and things as vividly as possible in his mind.\n\nWe are now going to dine, exactly at the hour at which the birthday dinner was given last year. My object, of course, is a purely medical one in this case. The laudanum must find the process of digestion, as nearly as may be, where the laudanum found it last year.\n\nAt a reasonable time after dinner I propose to lead the conversation back again—as inartificially as I can—to the subject of the Diamond, and of the Indian conspiracy to steal it. When I have filled his mind with these topics, I shall have done all that it is in my power to do, before the time comes for giving him the second dose.\n\nHalf-past eight.—I have only this moment found an opportunity of attending to the most important duty of all; the duty of looking in the family medicine chest, for the laudanum which Mr. Candy used last year.\n\nTen minutes since, I caught Betteredge at an unoccupied moment, and told him what I wanted. Without a word of objection, without so much as an attempt to produce his pocket-book, he led the way (making allowances for me at every step) to the store-room in which the medicine chest is kept.\n\nI discovered the bottle, carefully guarded by a glass stopper tied over with leather. The preparation which it contained was, as I had anticipated, the common Tincture of Opium. Finding the bottle still well filled, I have resolved to use it, in preference to employing either of the two preparations with which I had taken care to provide myself, in case of emergency.\n\nThe question of the quantity which I am to administer presents certain difficulties. I have thought it over, and have decided on increasing the dose.\n\nMy notes inform me that Mr. Candy only administered twenty-five minims. This is a small dose to have produced the results which followed—even in the case of a person so sensitive as Mr. Blake. I think it highly probable that Mr. Candy gave more than he supposed himself to have given—knowing, as I do, that he has a keen relish of the pleasures of the table, and that he measured out the laudanum on the birthday, after dinner. In any case, I shall run the risk of enlarging the dose to forty minims. On this occasion, Mr. Blake knows beforehand that he is going to take the laudanum—which is equivalent, physiologically speaking, to his having (unconsciously to himself) a certain capacity in him to resist the effects. If my view is right, a larger quantity is therefore imperatively required, this time, to repeat the results which the smaller quantity produced, last year.\n\nTen o’clock.—The witnesses, or the company (which shall I call them?) reached the house an hour since.\n\nA little before nine o’clock, I prevailed on Mr. Blake to accompany me to his bedroom; stating, as a reason, that I wished him to look round it, for the last time, in order to make quite sure that nothing had been forgotten in the refurnishing of the room. I had previously arranged with Betteredge, that the bedchamber prepared for Mr. Bruff should be the next room to Mr. Blake’s, and that I should be informed of the lawyer’s arrival by a knock at the door. Five minutes after the clock in the hall had struck nine, I heard the knock; and, going out immediately, met Mr. Bruff in the corridor.\n\nMy personal appearance (as usual) told against me. Mr. Bruff’s distrust looked at me plainly enough out of Mr. Bruff’s eyes. Being well used to producing this effect on strangers, I did not hesitate a moment in saying what I wanted to say, before the lawyer found his way into Mr. Blake’s room.\n\n“You have travelled here, I believe, in company with Mrs. Merridew and Miss Verinder?” I said.\n\n“Yes,” answered Mr. Bruff, as drily as might be.\n\n“Miss Verinder has probably told you, that I wish her presence in the house (and Mrs. Merridew’s presence of course) to be kept a secret from Mr. Blake, until my experiment on him has been tried first?”\n\n“I know that I am to hold my tongue, sir!” said Mr. Bruff, impatiently. “Being habitually silent on the subject of human folly, I am all the readier to keep my lips closed on this occasion. Does that satisfy you?”\n\nI bowed, and left Betteredge to show him to his room. Betteredge gave me one look at parting, which said, as if in so many words, “You have caught a Tartar, Mr. Jennings—and the name of him is Bruff.”\n\nIt was next necessary to get the meeting over with the two ladies. I descended the stairs—a little nervously, I confess—on my way to Miss Verinder’s sitting-room.\n\nThe gardener’s wife (charged with looking after the accommodation of the ladies) met me in the first-floor corridor. This excellent woman treats me with an excessive civility which is plainly the offspring of down-right terror. She stares, trembles, and curtseys, whenever I speak to her. On my asking for Miss Verinder, she stared, trembled, and would no doubt have curtseyed next, if Miss Verinder herself had not cut that ceremony short, by suddenly opening her sitting-room door.\n\n“Is that Mr. Jennings?” she asked.\n\nBefore I could answer, she came out eagerly to speak to me in the corridor. We met under the light of a lamp on a bracket. At the first sight of me, Miss Verinder stopped, and hesitated. She recovered herself instantly, coloured for a moment—and then, with a charming frankness, offered me her hand.\n\n“I can’t treat you like a stranger, Mr. Jennings,” she said. “Oh, if you only knew how happy your letters have made me!”\n\nShe looked at my ugly wrinkled face, with a bright gratitude so new to me in my experience of my fellow-creatures, that I was at a loss how to answer her. Nothing had prepared me for her kindness and her beauty. The misery of many years has not hardened my heart, thank God. I was as awkward and as shy with her, as if I had been a lad in my teens.\n\n“Where is he now?” she asked, giving free expression to her one dominant interest—the interest in Mr. Blake. “What is he doing? Has he spoken of me? Is he in good spirits? How does he bear the sight of the house, after what happened in it last year? When are you going to give him the laudanum? May I see you pour it out? I am so interested; I am so excited—I have ten thousand things to say to you, and they all crowd together so that I don’t know what to say first. Do you wonder at the interest I take in this?”\n\n“No,” I said. “I venture to think that I thoroughly understand it.”\n\nShe was far above the paltry affectation of being confused. She answered me as she might have answered a brother or a father.\n\n“You have relieved me of indescribable wretchedness; you have given me a new life. How can I be ungrateful enough to have any concealment from you? I love him,” she said simply, “I have loved him from first to last—even when I was wronging him in my own thoughts; even when I was saying the hardest and the cruellest words to him. Is there any excuse for me, in that? I hope there is—I am afraid it is the only excuse I have. When tomorrow comes, and he knows that I am in the house, do you think——”\n\nShe stopped again, and looked at me very earnestly.\n\n“When tomorrow comes,” I said, “I think you have only to tell him what you have just told me.”\n\nHer face brightened; she came a step nearer to me. Her fingers trifled nervously with a flower which I had picked in the garden, and which I had put into the button-hole of my coat.\n\n“You have seen a great deal of him lately,” she said. “Have you, really and truly, seen that?”\n\n“Really and truly,” I answered. “I am quite certain of what will happen tomorrow. I wish I could feel as certain of what will happen tonight.”\n\nAt that point in the conversation, we were interrupted by the appearance of Betteredge with the tea-tray. He gave me another significant look as he passed on into the sitting-room. “Aye! aye! make your hay while the sun shines. The Tartar’s upstairs, Mr. Jennings—the Tartar’s upstairs!”\n\nWe followed him into the room. A little old lady, in a corner, very nicely dressed, and very deeply absorbed over a smart piece of embroidery, dropped her work in her lap, and uttered a faint little scream at the first sight of my gipsy complexion and my piebald hair.\n\n“Mrs. Merridew,” said Miss Verinder, “this is Mr. Jennings.”\n\n“I beg Mr. Jennings’s pardon,” said the old lady, looking at Miss Verinder, and speaking at me. “Railway travelling always makes me nervous. I am endeavouring to quiet my mind by occupying myself as usual. I don’t know whether my embroidery is out of place, on this extraordinary occasion. If it interferes with Mr. Jennings’s medical views, I shall be happy to put it away of course.”\n\nI hastened to sanction the presence of the embroidery, exactly as I had sanctioned the absence of the burst buzzard and the Cupid’s wing. Mrs. Merridew made an effort—a grateful effort—to look at my hair. No! it was not to be done. Mrs. Merridew looked back again at Miss Verinder.\n\n“If Mr. Jennings will permit me,” pursued the old lady, “I should like to ask a favour. Mr. Jennings is about to try a scientific experiment tonight. I used to attend scientific experiments when I was a girl at school. They invariably ended in an explosion. If Mr. Jennings will be so very kind, I should like to be warned of the explosion this time. With a view to getting it over, if possible, before I go to bed.”\n\nI attempted to assure Mrs. Merridew that an explosion was not included in the programme on this occasion.\n\n“No,” said the old lady. “I am much obliged to Mr. Jennings—I am aware that he is only deceiving me for my own good. I prefer plain dealing. I am quite resigned to the explosion—but I do want to get it over, if possible, before I go to bed.”\n\nHere the door opened, and Mrs. Merridew uttered another little scream. The advent of the explosion? No: only the advent of Betteredge.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr. Jennings,” said Betteredge, in his most elaborately confidential manner. “Mr. Franklin wishes to know where you are. Being under your orders to deceive him, in respect to the presence of my young lady in the house, I have said I don’t know. That, you will please to observe, was a lie. Having one foot already in the grave, sir, the fewer lies you expect me to tell, the more I shall be indebted to you, when my conscience pricks me and my time comes.”\n\nThere was not a moment to be wasted on the purely speculative question of Betteredge’s conscience. Mr. Blake might make his appearance in search of me, unless I went to him at once in his own room. Miss Verinder followed me out into the corridor.\n\n“They seem to be in a conspiracy to persecute you,” she said. “What does it mean?”\n\n“Only the protest of the world, Miss Verinder—on a very small scale—against anything that is new.”\n\n“What are we to do with Mrs. Merridew?”\n\n“Tell her the explosion will take place at nine tomorrow morning.”\n\n“So as to send her to bed?”\n\n“Yes—so as to send her to bed.”\n\nMiss Verinder went back to the sitting-room, and I went upstairs to Mr. Blake.\n\nTo my surprise I found him alone; restlessly pacing his room, and a little irritated at being left by himself.\n\n“Where is Mr. Bruff?” I asked.\n\nHe pointed to the closed door of communication between the two rooms. Mr. Bruff had looked in on him, for a moment; had attempted to renew his protest against our proceedings; and had once more failed to produce the smallest impression on Mr. Blake. Upon this, the lawyer had taken refuge in a black leather bag, filled to bursting with professional papers. “The serious business of life,” he admitted, “was sadly out of place on such an occasion as the present. But the serious business of life must be carried on, for all that. Mr. Blake would perhaps kindly make allowance for the old-fashioned habits of a practical man. Time was money—and, as for Mr. Jennings, he might depend on it that Mr. Bruff would be forthcoming when called upon.” With that apology, the lawyer had gone back to his own room, and had immersed himself obstinately in his black bag.\n\nI thought of Mrs. Merridew and her embroidery, and of Betteredge and his conscience. There is a wonderful sameness in the solid side of the English character—just as there is a wonderful sameness in the solid expression of the English face.\n\n“When are you going to give me the laudanum?” asked Mr. Blake impatiently.\n\n“You must wait a little longer,” I said. “I will stay and keep you company till the time comes.”\n\nIt was then not ten o’clock. Inquiries which I had made, at various times, of Betteredge and Mr. Blake, had led me to the conclusion that the dose of laudanum given by Mr. Candy could not possibly have been administered before eleven. I had accordingly determined not to try the second dose until that time.\n\nWe talked a little; but both our minds were preoccupied by the coming ordeal. The conversation soon flagged—then dropped altogether. Mr. Blake idly turned over the books on his bedroom table. I had taken the precaution of looking at them, when we first entered the room. The Guardian; The Tatler; Richardson’s Pamela; Mackenzie’s Man of Feeling; Roscoe’s Lorenzo de’ Medici; and Robertson’s Charles the Fifth—all classical works; all (of course) immeasurably superior to anything produced in later times; and all (from my present point of view) possessing the one great merit of enchaining nobody’s interest, and exciting nobody’s brain. I left Mr. Blake to the composing influence of Standard Literature, and occupied myself in making this entry in my journal.\n\nMy watch informs me that it is close on eleven o’clock. I must shut up these leaves once more.\n\nTwo o’clock A.M.—The experiment has been tried. With what result, I am now to describe.\n\nAt eleven o’clock, I rang the bell for Betteredge, and told Mr. Blake that he might at last prepare himself for bed.\n\nI looked out of the window at the night. It was mild and rainy, resembling, in this respect, the night of the birthday—the twenty-first of June, last year. Without professing to believe in omens, it was at least encouraging to find no direct nervous influences—no stormy or electric perturbations—in the atmosphere. Betteredge joined me at the window, and mysteriously put a little slip of paper into my hand. It contained these lines:\n\n“Mrs. Merridew has gone to bed, on the distinct understanding that the explosion is to take place at nine tomorrow morning, and that I am not to stir out of this part of the house until she comes and sets me free. She has no idea that the chief scene of the experiment is my sitting-room—or she would have remained in it for the whole night! I am alone, and very anxious. Pray let me see you measure out the laudanum; I want to have something to do with it, even in the unimportant character of a mere looker-on.—R.V.”\n\nI followed Betteredge out of the room, and told him to remove the medicine-chest into Miss Verinder’s sitting-room.\n\nThe order appeared to take him completely by surprise. He looked as if he suspected me of some occult medical design on Miss Verinder! “Might I presume to ask,” he said, “what my young lady and the medicine-chest have got to do with each other?”\n\n“Stay in the sitting-room, and you will see.”\n\nBetteredge appeared to doubt his own unaided capacity to superintend me effectually, on an occasion when a medicine-chest was included in the proceedings.\n\n“Is there any objection, sir” he asked, “to taking Mr. Bruff into this part of the business?”\n\n“Quite the contrary! I am now going to ask Mr. Bruff to accompany me downstairs.”\n\nBetteredge withdrew to fetch the medicine-chest, without another word. I went back into Mr. Blake’s room, and knocked at the door of communication. Mr. Bruff opened it, with his papers in his hand—immersed in Law; impenetrable to Medicine.\n\n“I am sorry to disturb you,” I said. “But I am going to prepare the laudanum for Mr. Blake; and I must request you to be present, and to see what I do.”\n\n“Yes?” said Mr. Bruff, with nine-tenths of his attention riveted on his papers, and with one-tenth unwillingly accorded to me. “Anything else?”\n\n“I must trouble you to return here with me, and to see me administer the dose.”\n\n“Anything else?”\n\n“One thing more. I must put you to the inconvenience of remaining in Mr. Blake’s room, and of waiting to see what happens.”\n\n“Oh, very good!” said Mr. Bruff. “My room, or Mr. Blake’s room—it doesn’t matter which; I can go on with my papers anywhere. Unless you object, Mr. Jennings, to my importing that amount of common sense into the proceedings?”\n\nBefore I could answer, Mr. Blake addressed himself to the lawyer, speaking from his bed.\n\n“Do you really mean to say that you don’t feel any interest in what we are going to do?” he asked. “Mr. Bruff, you have no more imagination than a cow!”\n\n“A cow is a very useful animal, Mr. Blake,” said the lawyer. With that reply he followed me out of the room, still keeping his papers in his hand.\n\nWe found Miss Verinder, pale and agitated, restlessly pacing her sitting-room from end to end. At a table in a corner stood Betteredge, on guard over the medicine-chest. Mr. Bruff sat down on the first chair that he could find, and (emulating the usefulness of the cow) plunged back again into his papers on the spot.\n\nMiss Verinder drew me aside, and reverted instantly to her one all-absorbing interest—her interest in Mr. Blake.\n\n“How is he now?” she asked. “Is he nervous? is he out of temper? Do you think it will succeed? Are you sure it will do no harm?”\n\n“Quite sure. Come, and see me measure it out.”\n\n“One moment! It is past eleven now. How long will it be before anything happens?”\n\n“It is not easy to say. An hour perhaps.”\n\n“I suppose the room must be dark, as it was last year?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“I shall wait in my bedroom—just as I did before. I shall keep the door a little way open. It was a little way open last year. I will watch the sitting-room door; and the moment it moves, I will blow out my light. It all happened in that way, on my birthday night. And it must all happen again in the same way, musn’t it?”\n\n“Are you sure you can control yourself, Miss Verinder?”\n\n“In his interests, I can do anything!” she answered fervently.\n\nOne look at her face told me that I could trust her. I addressed myself again to Mr. Bruff.\n\n“I must trouble you to put your papers aside for a moment,” I said.\n\n“Oh, certainly!” He got up with a start—as if I had disturbed him at a particularly interesting place—and followed me to the medicine-chest. There, deprived of the breathless excitement incidental to the practice of his profession, he looked at Betteredge—and yawned wearily.\n\nMiss Verinder joined me with a glass jug of cold water, which she had taken from a side-table. “Let me pour out the water,” she whispered. “I must have a hand in it!”\n\nI measured out the forty minims from the bottle, and poured the laudanum into a medicine glass. “Fill it till it is three parts full,” I said, and handed the glass to Miss Verinder. I then directed Betteredge to lock up the medicine chest; informing him that I had done with it now. A look of unutterable relief overspread the old servant’s countenance. He had evidently suspected me of a medical design on his young lady!\n\nAfter adding the water as I had directed, Miss Verinder seized a moment—while Betteredge was locking the chest, and while Mr. Bruff was looking back to his papers—and slyly kissed the rim of the medicine glass. “When you give it to him,” said the charming girl, “give it to him on that side!”\n\nI took the piece of crystal which was to represent the Diamond from my pocket, and gave it to her.\n\n“You must have a hand in this, too,” I said. “You must put it where you put the Moonstone last year.”\n\nShe led the way to the Indian cabinet, and put the mock Diamond into the drawer which the real Diamond had occupied on the birthday night. Mr. Bruff witnessed this proceeding, under protest, as he had witnessed everything else. But the strong dramatic interest which the experiment was now assuming, proved (to my great amusement) to be too much for Betteredge’s capacity of self-restraint. His hand trembled as he held the candle, and he whispered anxiously, “Are you sure, miss, it’s the right drawer?”\n\nI led the way out again, with the laudanum and water in my hand. At the door, I stopped to address a last word to Miss Verinder.\n\n“Don’t be long in putting out the lights,” I said.\n\n“I will put them out at once,” she answered. “And I will wait in my bedroom, with only one candle alight.”\n\nShe closed the sitting-room door behind us. Followed by Mr. Bruff and Betteredge, I went back to Mr. Blake’s room.\n\nWe found him moving restlessly from side to side of the bed, and wondering irritably whether he was to have the laudanum that night. In the presence of the two witnesses, I gave him the dose, and shook up his pillows, and told him to lie down again quietly and wait.\n\nHis bed, provided with light chintz curtains, was placed, with the head against the wall of the room, so as to leave a good open space on either side of it. On one side, I drew the curtains completely—and in the part of the room thus screened from his view, I placed Mr. Bruff and Betteredge, to wait for the result. At the bottom of the bed I half drew the curtains—and placed my own chair at a little distance, so that I might let him see me or not see me, speak to me or not speak to me, just as the circumstances might direct. Having already been informed that he always slept with a light in the room, I placed one of the two lighted candles on a little table at the head of the bed, where the glare of the light would not strike on his eyes. The other candle I gave to Mr. Bruff; the light, in this instance, being subdued by the screen of the chintz curtains. The window was open at the top, so as to ventilate the room. The rain fell softly, the house was quiet. It was twenty minutes past eleven, by my watch, when the preparations were completed, and I took my place on the chair set apart at the bottom of the bed.\n\nMr. Bruff resumed his papers, with every appearance of being as deeply interested in them as ever. But looking towards him now, I saw certain signs and tokens which told me that the Law was beginning to lose its hold on him at last. The suspended interest of the situation in which we were now placed was slowly asserting its influence even on his unimaginative mind. As for Betteredge, consistency of principle and dignity of conduct had become, in his case, mere empty words. He forgot that I was performing a conjuring trick on Mr. Franklin Blake; he forgot that I had upset the house from top to bottom; he forgot that I had not read Robinson Crusoe since I was a child. “For the Lord’s sake, sir,” he whispered to me, “tell us when it will begin to work.”\n\n“Not before midnight,” I whispered back. “Say nothing, and sit still.”\n\nBetteredge dropped to the lowest depth of familiarity with me, without a struggle to save himself. He answered by a wink!\n\nLooking next towards Mr. Blake, I found him as restless as ever in his bed; fretfully wondering why the influence of the laudanum had not begun to assert itself yet. To tell him, in his present humour, that the more he fidgeted and wondered, the longer he would delay the result for which we were now waiting, would have been simply useless. The wiser course to take was to dismiss the idea of the opium from his mind, by leading him insensibly to think of something else.\n\nWith this view, I encouraged him to talk to me; contriving so to direct the conversation, on my side, as to lead it back again to the subject which had engaged us earlier in the evening—the subject of the Diamond. I took care to revert to those portions of the story of the Moonstone, which related to the transport of it from London to Yorkshire; to the risk which Mr. Blake had run in removing it from the bank at Frizinghall; and to the unexpected appearance of the Indians at the house, on the evening of the birthday. And I purposely assumed, in referring to these events, to have misunderstood much of what Mr. Blake himself had told me a few hours since. In this way, I set him talking on the subject with which it was now vitally important to fill his mind—without allowing him to suspect that I was making him talk for a purpose. Little by little, he became so interested in putting me right that he forgot to fidget in the bed. His mind was far away from the question of the opium, at the all-important time when his eyes first told me that the opium was beginning to lay its hold on his brain.\n\nI looked at my watch. It wanted five minutes to twelve, when the premonitory symptoms of the working of the laudanum first showed themselves to me.\n\nAt this time, no unpractised eyes would have detected any change in him. But, as the minutes of the new morning wore away, the swiftly-subtle progress of the influence began to show itself more plainly. The sublime intoxication of opium gleamed in his eyes; the dew of a stealthy perspiration began to glisten on his face. In five minutes more, the talk which he still kept up with me, failed in coherence. He held steadily to the subject of the Diamond; but he ceased to complete his sentences. A little later, the sentences dropped to single words. Then, there was an interval of silence. Then, he sat up in bed. Then, still busy with the subject of the Diamond, he began to talk again—not to me, but to himself. That change told me that the first stage in the experiment was reached. The stimulant influence of the opium had got him.\n\nThe time, now, was twenty-three minutes past twelve. The next half hour, at most, would decide the question of whether he would, or would not, get up from his bed, and leave the room.\n\nIn the breathless interest of watching him—in the unutterable triumph of seeing the first result of the experiment declare itself in the manner, and nearly at the time, which I had anticipated—I had utterly forgotten the two companions of my night vigil. Looking towards them now, I saw the Law (as represented by Mr. Bruff’s papers) lying unheeded on the floor. Mr. Bruff himself was looking eagerly through a crevice left in the imperfectly-drawn curtains of the bed. And Betteredge, oblivious of all respect for social distinctions, was peeping over Mr. Bruff’s shoulder.\n\nThey both started back, on finding that I was looking at them, like two boys caught out by their schoolmaster in a fault. I signed to them to take off their boots quietly, as I was taking off mine. If Mr. Blake gave us the chance of following him, it was vitally necessary to follow him without noise.\n\nTen minutes passed—and nothing happened. Then, he suddenly threw the bed-clothes off him. He put one leg out of bed. He waited.\n\n“I wish I had never taken it out of the bank,” he said to himself. “It was safe in the bank.”\n\nMy heart throbbed fast; the pulses at my temples beat furiously. The doubt about the safety of the Diamond was, once more, the dominant impression in his brain! On that one pivot, the whole success of the experiment turned. The prospect thus suddenly opened before me was too much for my shattered nerves. I was obliged to look away from him—or I should have lost my self-control.\n\nThere was another interval of silence.\n\nWhen I could trust myself to look back at him he was out of his bed, standing erect at the side of it. The pupils of his eyes were now contracted; his eyeballs gleamed in the light of the candle as he moved his head slowly to and fro. He was thinking; he was doubting—he spoke again.\n\n“How do I know?” he said. “The Indians may be hidden in the house.”\n\nHe stopped, and walked slowly to the other end of the room. He turned—waited—came back to the bed.\n\n“It’s not even locked up,” he went on. “It’s in the drawer of her cabinet. And the drawer doesn’t lock.”\n\nHe sat down on the side of the bed. “Anybody might take it,” he said.\n\nHe rose again restlessly, and reiterated his first words.\n\n“How do I know? The Indians may be hidden in the house.”\n\nHe waited again. I drew back behind the half curtain of the bed. He looked about the room, with a vacant glitter in his eyes. It was a breathless moment. There was a pause of some sort. A pause in the action of the opium? a pause in the action of the brain? Who could tell? Everything depended, now, on what he did next.\n\nHe laid himself down again on the bed!\n\nA horrible doubt crossed my mind. Was it possible that the sedative action of the opium was making itself felt already? It was not in my experience that it should do this. But what is experience, where opium is concerned? There are probably no two men in existence on whom the drug acts in exactly the same manner. Was some constitutional peculiarity in him, feeling the influence in some new way? Were we to fail on the very brink of success?\n\nNo! He got up again abruptly. “How the devil am I to sleep,” he said, “with this on my mind?”\n\nHe looked at the light, burning on the table at the head of his bed. After a moment, he took the candle in his hand.\n\nI blew out the second candle, burning behind the closed curtains. I drew back, with Mr. Bruff and Betteredge, into the farthest corner by the bed. I signed to them to be silent, as if their lives had depended on it.\n\nWe waited—seeing and hearing nothing. We waited, hidden from him by the curtains.\n\nThe light which he was holding on the other side of us moved suddenly. The next moment he passed us, swift and noiseless, with the candle in his hand.\n\nHe opened the bedroom door, and went out.\n\nWe followed him along the corridor. We followed him down the stairs. We followed him along the second corridor. He never looked back; he never hesitated.\n\nHe opened the sitting-room door, and went in, leaving it open behind him.\n\nThe door was hung (like all the other doors in the house) on large old-fashioned hinges. When it was opened, a crevice was opened between the door and the post. I signed to my two companions to look through this, so as to keep them from showing themselves. I placed myself—outside the door also—on the opposite side. A recess in the wall was at my left hand, in which I could instantly hide myself, if he showed any signs of looking back into the corridor.\n\nHe advanced to the middle of the room, with the candle still in his hand: he looked about him—but he never looked back.\n\nI saw the door of Miss Verinder’s bedroom, standing ajar. She had put out her light. She controlled herself nobly. The dim white outline of her summer dress was all that I could see. Nobody who had not known it beforehand would have suspected that there was a living creature in the room. She kept back, in the dark: not a word, not a movement escaped her.\n\nIt was now ten minutes past one. I heard, through the dead silence, the soft drip of the rain and the tremulous passage of the night air through the trees.\n\nAfter waiting irresolute, for a minute or more, in the middle of the room, he moved to the corner near the window, where the Indian cabinet stood.\n\nHe put his candle on the top of the cabinet. He opened, and shut, one drawer after another, until he came to the drawer in which the mock Diamond was put. He looked into the drawer for a moment. Then he took the mock Diamond out with his right hand. With the other hand, he took the candle from the top of the cabinet.\n\nHe walked back a few steps towards the middle of the room, and stood still again.\n\nThus far, he had exactly repeated what he had done on the birthday night. Would his next proceeding be the same as the proceeding of last year? Would he leave the room? Would he go back now, as I believed he had gone back then, to his bedchamber? Would he show us what he had done with the Diamond, when he had returned to his own room?\n\nHis first action, when he moved once more, proved to be an action which he had not performed, when he was under the influence of the opium for the first time. He put the candle down on a table, and wandered on a little towards the farther end of the room. There was a sofa there. He leaned heavily on the back of it, with his left hand—then roused himself, and returned to the middle of the room. I could now see his eyes. They were getting dull and heavy; the glitter in them was fast dying out.\n\nThe suspense of the moment proved too much for Miss Verinder’s self-control. She advanced a few steps—then stopped again. Mr. Bruff and Betteredge looked across the open doorway at me for the first time. The prevision of a coming disappointment was impressing itself on their minds as well as on mine.\n\nStill, so long as he stood where he was, there was hope. We waited, in unutterable expectation, to see what would happen next.\n\nThe next event was decisive. He let the mock Diamond drop out of his hand.\n\nIt fell on the floor, before the doorway—plainly visible to him, and to every one. He made no effort to pick it up: he looked down at it vacantly, and, as he looked, his head sank on his breast. He staggered—roused himself for an instant—walked back unsteadily to the sofa—and sat down on it. He made a last effort; he tried to rise, and sank back. His head fell on the sofa cushions. It was then twenty-five minutes past one o’clock. Before I had put my watch back in my pocket, he was asleep.\n\nIt was all over now. The sedative influence had got him; the experiment was at an end.\n\nI entered the room, telling Mr. Bruff and Betteredge that they might follow me. There was no fear of disturbing him. We were free to move and speak.\n\n“The first thing to settle,” I said, “is the question of what we are to do with him. He will probably sleep for the next six or seven hours, at least. It is some distance to carry him back to his own room. When I was younger, I could have done it alone. But my health and strength are not what they were—I am afraid I must ask you to help me.”\n\nBefore they could answer, Miss Verinder called to me softly. She met me at the door of her room, with a light shawl, and with the counterpane from her own bed.\n\n“Do you mean to watch him while he sleeps?” she asked.\n\n“Yes, I am not sure enough of the action of the opium in his case to be willing to leave him alone.”\n\nShe handed me the shawl and the counterpane.\n\n“Why should you disturb him?” she whispered. “Make his bed on the sofa. I can shut my door, and keep in my room.”\n\nIt was infinitely the simplest and the safest way of disposing of him for the night. I mentioned the suggestion to Mr. Bruff and Betteredge—who both approved of my adopting it. In five minutes I had laid him comfortably on the sofa, and had covered him lightly with the counterpane and the shawl. Miss Verinder wished us good-night, and closed the door. At my request, we three then drew round the table in the middle of the room, on which the candle was still burning, and on which writing materials were placed.\n\n“Before we separate,” I began, “I have a word to say about the experiment which has been tried tonight. Two distinct objects were to be gained by it. The first of these objects was to prove, that Mr. Blake entered this room, and took the Diamond, last year, acting unconsciously and irresponsibly, under the influence of opium. After what you have both seen, are you both satisfied, so far?”\n\nThey answered me in the affirmative, without a moment’s hesitation.\n\n“The second object,” I went on, “was to discover what he did with the Diamond, after he was seen by Miss Verinder to leave her sitting-room with the jewel in his hand, on the birthday night. The gaining of this object depended, of course, on his still continuing exactly to repeat his proceedings of last year. He has failed to do that; and the purpose of the experiment is defeated accordingly. I can’t assert that I am not disappointed at the result—but I can honestly say that I am not surprised by it. I told Mr. Blake from the first, that our complete success in this matter depended on our completely reproducing in him the physical and moral conditions of last year—and I warned him that this was the next thing to a downright impossibility. We have only partially reproduced the conditions, and the experiment has been only partially successful in consequence. It is also possible that I may have administered too large a dose of laudanum. But I myself look upon the first reason that I have given, as the true reason why we have to lament a failure, as well as to rejoice over a success.”\n\nAfter saying those words, I put the writing materials before Mr. Bruff, and asked him if he had any objection—before we separated for the night—to draw out, and sign, a plain statement of what he had seen. He at once took the pen, and produced the statement with the fluent readiness of a practised hand.\n\n“I owe you this,” he said, signing the paper, “as some atonement for what passed between us earlier in the evening. I beg your pardon, Mr. Jennings, for having doubted you. You have done Franklin Blake an inestimable service. In our legal phrase, you have proved your case.”\n\nBetteredge’s apology was characteristic of the man.\n\n“Mr. Jennings,” he said, “when you read Robinson Crusoe again (which I strongly recommend you to do), you will find that he never scruples to acknowledge it, when he turns out to have been in the wrong. Please to consider me, sir, as doing what Robinson Crusoe did, on the present occasion.” With those words he signed the paper in his turn.\n\nMr. Bruff took me aside, as we rose from the table.\n\n“One word about the Diamond,” he said. “Your theory is that Franklin Blake hid the Moonstone in his room. My theory is, that the Moonstone is in the possession of Mr. Luker’s bankers in London. We won’t dispute which of us is right. We will only ask, which of us is in a position to put his theory to the test?”\n\n“The test, in my case,” I answered, “has been tried tonight, and has failed.”\n\n“The test, in my case,” rejoined Mr. Bruff, “is still in process of trial. For the last two days I have had a watch set for Mr. Luker at the bank; and I shall cause that watch to be continued until the last day of the month. I know that he must take the Diamond himself out of his bankers’ hands—and I am acting on the chance that the person who has pledged the Diamond may force him to do this by redeeming the pledge. In that case I may be able to lay my hand on the person. If I succeed, I clear up the mystery, exactly at the point where the mystery baffles us now! Do you admit that, so far?”\n\nI admitted it readily.\n\n“I am going back to town by the morning train,” pursued the lawyer. “I may hear, when I return, that a discovery has been made—and it may be of the greatest importance that I should have Franklin Blake at hand to appeal to, if necessary. I intend to tell him, as soon as he wakes, that he must return with me to London. After all that has happened, may I trust to your influence to back me?”\n\n“Certainly!” I said.\n\nMr. Bruff shook hands with me, and left the room. Betteredge followed him out.\n\nI went to the sofa to look at Mr. Blake. He had not moved since I had laid him down and made his bed—he lay locked in a deep and quiet sleep.\n\nWhile I was still looking at him, I heard the bedroom door softly opened. Once more, Miss Verinder appeared on the threshold, in her pretty summer dress.\n\n“Do me a last favour?” she whispered. “Let me watch him with you.”\n\nI hesitated—not in the interests of propriety; only in the interest of her night’s rest. She came close to me, and took my hand.\n\n“I can’t sleep; I can’t even sit still, in my own room,” she said. “Oh, Mr. Jennings, if you were me, only think how you would long to sit and look at him. Say, yes! Do!”\n\nIs it necessary to mention that I gave way? Surely not!\n\nShe drew a chair to the foot of the sofa. She looked at him in a silent ecstasy of happiness, till the tears rose in her eyes. She dried her eyes, and said she would fetch her work. She fetched her work, and never did a single stitch of it. It lay in her lap—she was not even able to look away from him long enough to thread her needle. I thought of my own youth; I thought of the gentle eyes which had once looked love at me. In the heaviness of my heart I turned to my Journal for relief, and wrote in it what is written here.\n\nSo we kept our watch together in silence. One of us absorbed in his writing; the other absorbed in her love.\n\nHour after hour he lay in his deep sleep. The light of the new day grew and grew in the room, and still he never moved.\n\nTowards six o’clock, I felt the warning which told me that my pains were coming back. I was obliged to leave her alone with him for a little while. I said I would go upstairs, and fetch another pillow for him out of his room. It was not a long attack, this time. In a little while I was able to venture back, and let her see me again.\n\nI found her at the head of the sofa, when I returned. She was just touching his forehead with her lips. I shook my head as soberly as I could, and pointed to her chair. She looked back at me with a bright smile, and a charming colour in her face. “You would have done it,” she whispered, “in my place!”\n\nIt is just eight o’clock. He is beginning to move for the first time.\n\nMiss Verinder is kneeling by the side of the sofa. She has so placed herself that when his eyes first open, they must open on her face.\n\nShall I leave them together?\n\nYes!\n\nEleven o’clock.—The house is empty again. They have arranged it among themselves; they have all gone to London by the ten o’clock train. My brief dream of happiness is over. I have awakened again to the realities of my friendless and lonely life.\n\nI dare not trust myself to write down the kind words that have been said to me—especially by Miss Verinder and Mr. Blake. Besides, it is needless. Those words will come back to me in my solitary hours, and will help me through what is left of the end of my life. Mr. Blake is to write, and tell me what happens in London. Miss Verinder is to return to Yorkshire in the autumn (for her marriage, no doubt); and I am to take a holiday, and be a guest in the house. Oh me, how I felt, as the grateful happiness looked at me out of her eyes, and the warm pressure of her hand said, “This is your doing!”\n\nMy poor patients are waiting for me. Back again, this morning, to the old routine! Back again, tonight, to the dreadful alternative between the opium and the pain!\n\nGod be praised for His mercy! I have seen a little sunshine—I have had a happy time.\n\nThe Story resumed by Franklin Blake.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\nBut few words are needed, on my part, to complete the narrative that has been presented in the Journal of Ezra Jennings.\n\nOf myself, I have only to say that I awoke on the morning of the twenty-sixth, perfectly ignorant of all that I had said and done under the influence of the opium—from the time when the drug first laid its hold on me, to the time when I opened my eyes, in Rachel’s sitting-room.\n\nOf what happened after my waking, I do not feel called upon to render an account in detail. Confining myself merely to results, I have to report that Rachel and I thoroughly understood each other, before a single word of explanation had passed on either side. I decline to account, and Rachel declines to account, for the extraordinary rapidity of our reconciliation. Sir and Madam, look back at the time when you were passionately attached to each other—and you will know what happened, after Ezra Jennings had shut the door of the sitting-room, as well as I know it myself.\n\nI have, however, no objection to add, that we should have been certainly discovered by Mrs. Merridew, but for Rachel’s presence of mind. She heard the sound of the old lady’s dress in the corridor, and instantly ran out to meet her; I heard Mrs. Merridew say, “What is the matter?” and I heard Rachel answer, “The explosion!” Mrs. Merridew instantly permitted herself to be taken by the arm, and led into the garden, out of the way of the impending shock. On her return to the house, she met me in the hall, and expressed herself as greatly struck by the vast improvement in Science, since the time when she was a girl at school. “Explosions, Mr. Blake, are infinitely milder than they were. I assure you, I barely heard Mr. Jennings’s explosion from the garden. And no smell afterwards, that I can detect, now we have come back to the house! I must really apologise to your medical friend. It is only due to him to say that he has managed it beautifully!”\n\nSo, after vanquishing Betteredge and Mr. Bruff, Ezra Jennings vanquished Mrs. Merridew herself. There is a great deal of undeveloped liberal feeling in the world, after all!\n\nAt breakfast, Mr. Bruff made no secret of his reasons for wishing that I should accompany him to London by the morning train. The watch kept at the bank, and the result which might yet come of it, appealed so irresistibly to Rachel’s curiosity, that she at once decided (if Mrs. Merridew had no objection) on accompanying us back to town—so as to be within reach of the earliest news of our proceedings.\n\nMrs. Merridew proved to be all pliability and indulgence, after the truly considerate manner in which the explosion had conducted itself; and Betteredge was accordingly informed that we were all four to travel back together by the morning train. I fully expected that he would have asked leave to accompany us. But Rachel had wisely provided her faithful old servant with an occupation that interested him. He was charged with completing the refurnishing of the house, and was too full of his domestic responsibilities to feel the “detective-fever” as he might have felt it under other circumstances.\n\nOur one subject of regret, in going to London, was the necessity of parting, more abruptly than we could have wished, with Ezra Jennings. It was impossible to persuade him to accompany us. I could only promise to write to him—and Rachel could only insist on his coming to see her when she returned to Yorkshire. There was every prospect of our meeting again in a few months—and yet there was something very sad in seeing our best and dearest friend left standing alone on the platform, as the train moved out of the station.\n\nOn our arrival in London, Mr. Bruff was accosted at the terminus by a small boy, dressed in a jacket and trousers of threadbare black cloth, and personally remarkable in virtue of the extraordinary prominence of his eyes. They projected so far, and they rolled about so loosely, that you wondered uneasily why they remained in their sockets. After listening to the boy, Mr. Bruff asked the ladies whether they would excuse our accompanying them back to Portland Place. I had barely time to promise Rachel that I would return, and tell her everything that had happened, before Mr. Bruff seized me by the arm, and hurried me into a cab. The boy with the ill-secured eyes took his place on the box by the driver, and the driver was directed to go to Lombard Street.\n\n“News from the bank?” I asked, as we started.\n\n“News of Mr. Luker,” said Mr. Bruff. “An hour ago, he was seen to leave his house at Lambeth, in a cab, accompanied by two men, who were recognised by my men as police officers in plain clothes. If Mr. Luker’s dread of the Indians is at the bottom of this precaution, the inference is plain enough. He is going to take the Diamond out of the bank.”\n\n“And we are going to the bank to see what comes of it?”\n\n“Yes—or to hear what has come of it, if it is all over by this time. Did you notice my boy—on the box, there?”\n\n“I noticed his eyes.”\n\nMr. Bruff laughed. “They call the poor little wretch ‘Gooseberry’ at the office,” he said. “I employ him to go on errands—and I only wish my clerks who have nicknamed him were as thoroughly to be depended on as he is. Gooseberry is one of the sharpest boys in London, Mr. Blake, in spite of his eyes.”\n\nIt was twenty minutes to five when we drew up before the bank in Lombard Street. Gooseberry looked longingly at his master, as he opened the cab door.\n\n“Do you want to come in too?” asked Mr. Bruff kindly. “Come in then, and keep at my heels till further orders. He’s as quick as lightning,” pursued Mr. Bruff, addressing me in a whisper. “Two words will do with Gooseberry, where twenty would be wanted with another boy.”\n\nWe entered the bank. The outer office—with the long counter, behind which the cashiers sat—was crowded with people; all waiting their turn to take money out, or to pay money in, before the bank closed at five o’clock.\n\nTwo men among the crowd approached Mr. Bruff, as soon as he showed himself.\n\n“Well,” asked the lawyer. “Have you seen him?”\n\n“He passed us here half an hour since, sir, and went on into the inner office.”\n\n“Has he not come out again yet?”\n\n“No, sir.”\n\nMr. Bruff turned to me. “Let us wait,” he said.\n\nI looked round among the people about me for the three Indians. Not a sign of them was to be seen anywhere. The only person present with a noticeably dark complexion was a tall man in a pilot coat, and a round hat, who looked like a sailor. Could this be one of them in disguise? Impossible! The man was taller than any of the Indians; and his face, where it was not hidden by a bushy black beard, was twice the breadth of any of their faces at least.\n\n“They must have their spy somewhere,” said Mr. Bruff, looking at the dark sailor in his turn. “And he may be the man.”\n\nBefore he could say more, his coat-tail was respectfully pulled by his attendant sprite with the gooseberry eyes. Mr. Bruff looked where the boy was looking. “Hush!” he said. “Here is Mr. Luker!”\n\nThe money-lender came out from the inner regions of the bank, followed by his two guardian policemen in plain clothes.\n\n“Keep your eye on him,” whispered Mr. Bruff. “If he passes the Diamond to anybody, he will pass it here.”\n\nWithout noticing either of us, Mr. Luker slowly made his way to the door—now in the thickest, now in the thinnest part of the crowd. I distinctly saw his hand move, as he passed a short, stout man, respectably dressed in a suit of sober grey. The man started a little, and looked after him. Mr. Luker moved on slowly through the crowd. At the door his guard placed themselves on either side of him. They were all three followed by one of Mr. Bruff’s men—and I saw them no more.\n\nI looked round at the lawyer, and then looked significantly towards the man in the suit of sober grey. “Yes!” whispered Mr. Bruff, “I saw it too!” He turned about, in search of his second man. The second man was nowhere to be seen. He looked behind him for his attendant sprite. Gooseberry had disappeared.\n\n“What the devil does it mean?” said Mr. Bruff angrily. “They have both left us at the very time when we want them most.”\n\nIt came to the turn of the man in the grey suit to transact his business at the counter. He paid in a cheque—received a receipt for it—and turned to go out.\n\n“What is to be done?” asked Mr. Bruff. “We can’t degrade ourselves by following him.”\n\n“I can!” I said. “I wouldn’t lose sight of that man for ten thousand pounds!”\n\n“In that case,” rejoined Mr. Bruff, “I wouldn’t lose sight of you, for twice the money. A nice occupation for a man in my position,” he muttered to himself, as we followed the stranger out of the bank. “For Heaven’s sake don’t mention it. I should be ruined if it was known.”\n\nThe man in the grey suit got into an omnibus, going westward. We got in after him. There were latent reserves of youth still left in Mr. Bruff. I assert it positively—when he took his seat in the omnibus, he blushed!\n\nThe man in the grey suit stopped the omnibus, and got out in Oxford Street. We followed him again. He went into a chemist’s shop.\n\nMr. Bruff started. “My chemist!” he exclaimed. “I am afraid we have made a mistake.”\n\nWe entered the shop. Mr. Bruff and the proprietor exchanged a few words in private. The lawyer joined me again, with a very crestfallen face.\n\n“It’s greatly to our credit,” he said, as he took my arm, and led me out—“that’s one comfort!”\n\n“What is to our credit?” I asked.\n\n“Mr. Blake! you and I are the two worst amateur detectives that ever tried their hands at the trade. The man in the grey suit has been thirty years in the chemist’s service. He was sent to the bank to pay money to his master’s account—and he knows no more of the Moonstone than the babe unborn.”\n\nI asked what was to be done next.\n\n“Come back to my office,” said Mr. Bruff. “Gooseberry, and my second man, have evidently followed somebody else. Let us hope that they had their eyes about them at any rate!”\n\nWhen we reached Gray’s Inn Square, the second man had arrived there before us. He had been waiting for more than a quarter of an hour.\n\n“Well!” asked Mr. Bruff. “What’s your news?”\n\n“I am sorry to say, sir,” replied the man, “that I have made a mistake. I could have taken my oath that I saw Mr. Luker pass something to an elderly gentleman, in a light-coloured paletot. The elderly gentleman turns out, sir, to be a most respectable master iron-monger in Eastcheap.”\n\n“Where is Gooseberry?” asked Mr. Bruff resignedly.\n\nThe man stared. “I don’t know, sir. I have seen nothing of him since I left the bank.”\n\nMr. Bruff dismissed the man. “One of two things,” he said to me. “Either Gooseberry has run away, or he is hunting on his own account. What do you say to dining here, on the chance that the boy may come back in an hour or two? I have got some good wine in the cellar, and we can get a chop from the coffee-house.”\n\nWe dined at Mr. Bruff’s chambers. Before the cloth was removed, “a person” was announced as wanting to speak to the lawyer. Was the person Gooseberry? No: only the man who had been employed to follow Mr. Luker when he left the bank.\n\nThe report, in this case, presented no feature of the slightest interest. Mr. Luker had gone back to his own house, and had there dismissed his guard. He had not gone out again afterwards. Towards dusk, the shutters had been put up, and the doors had been bolted. The street before the house, and the alley behind the house, had been carefully watched. No signs of the Indians had been visible. No person whatever had been seen loitering about the premises. Having stated these facts, the man waited to know whether there were any further orders. Mr. Bruff dismissed him for the night.\n\n“Do you think Mr. Luker has taken the Moonstone home with him?” I asked.\n\n“Not he,” said Mr. Bruff. “He would never have dismissed his two policemen, if he had run the risk of keeping the Diamond in his own house again.”\n\nWe waited another half-hour for the boy, and waited in vain. It was then time for Mr. Bruff to go to Hampstead, and for me to return to Rachel in Portland Place. I left my card, in charge of the porter at the chambers, with a line written on it to say that I should be at my lodgings at half past ten, that night. The card was to be given to the boy, if the boy came back.\n\nSome men have a knack of keeping appointments; and other men have a knack of missing them. I am one of the other men. Add to this, that I passed the evening at Portland Place, on the same seat with Rachel, in a room forty feet long, with Mrs. Merridew at the further end of it. Does anybody wonder that I got home at half past twelve instead of half past ten? How thoroughly heartless that person must be! And how earnestly I hope I may never make that person’s acquaintance!\n\nMy servant handed me a morsel of paper when he let me in.\n\nI read, in a neat legal handwriting, these words—“If you please, sir, I am getting sleepy. I will come back tomorrow morning, between nine and ten.” Inquiry proved that a boy, with very extraordinary-looking eyes, had called, and presented my card and message, had waited an hour, had done nothing but fall asleep and wake up again, had written a line for me, and had gone home—after gravely informing the servant that “he was fit for nothing unless he got his night’s rest.”\n\nAt nine, the next morning, I was ready for my visitor. At half past nine, I heard steps outside my door. “Come in, Gooseberry!” I called out. “Thank you, sir,” answered a grave and melancholy voice. The door opened. I started to my feet, and confronted—Sergeant Cuff!\n\n“I thought I would look in here, Mr. Blake, on the chance of your being in town, before I wrote to Yorkshire,” said the Sergeant.\n\nHe was as dreary and as lean as ever. His eyes had not lost their old trick (so subtly noticed in Betteredge’s Narrative) of “looking as if they expected something more from you than you were aware of yourself.” But, so far as dress can alter a man, the great Cuff was changed beyond all recognition. He wore a broad-brimmed white hat, a light shooting jacket, white trousers, and drab gaiters. He carried a stout oak stick. His whole aim and object seemed to be to look as if he had lived in the country all his life. When I complimented him on his Metamorphosis, he declined to take it as a joke. He complained, quite gravely, of the noises and the smells of London. I declare I am far from sure that he did not speak with a slightly rustic accent! I offered him breakfast. The innocent countryman was quite shocked. His breakfast hour was half-past six—and he went to bed with the cocks and hens!\n\n“I only got back from Ireland last night,” said the Sergeant, coming round to the practical object of his visit, in his own impenetrable manner. “Before I went to bed, I read your letter, telling me what has happened since my inquiry after the Diamond was suspended last year. There’s only one thing to be said about the matter on my side. I completely mistook my case. How any man living was to have seen things in their true light, in such a situation as mine was at the time, I don’t profess to know. But that doesn’t alter the facts as they stand. I own that I made a mess of it. Not the first mess, Mr. Blake, which has distinguished my professional career! It’s only in books that the officers of the detective force are superior to the weakness of making a mistake.”\n\n“You have come in the nick of time to recover your reputation,” I said.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr. Blake,” rejoined the Sergeant. “Now I have retired from business, I don’t care a straw about my reputation. I have done with my reputation, thank God! I am here, sir, in grateful remembrance of the late Lady Verinder’s liberality to me. I will go back to my old work—if you want me, and if you will trust me—on that consideration, and on no other. Not a farthing of money is to pass, if you please, from you to me. This is on honour. Now tell me, Mr. Blake, how the case stands since you wrote to me last.”\n\nI told him of the experiment with the opium, and of what had occurred afterwards at the bank in Lombard Street. He was greatly struck by the experiment—it was something entirely new in his experience. And he was particularly interested in the theory of Ezra Jennings, relating to what I had done with the Diamond, after I had left Rachel’s sitting-room, on the birthday night.\n\n“I don’t hold with Mr. Jennings that you hid the Moonstone,” said Sergeant Cuff. “But I agree with him, that you must certainly have taken it back to your own room.”\n\n“Well?” I asked. “And what happened then?”\n\n“Have you no suspicion yourself of what happened, sir?”\n\n“None whatever.”\n\n“Has Mr. Bruff no suspicion?”\n\n“No more than I have.”\n\nSergeant Cuff rose, and went to my writing-table. He came back with a sealed envelope. It was marked “Private;” it was addressed to me; and it had the Sergeant’s signature in the corner.\n\n“I suspected the wrong person, last year,” he said: “and I may be suspecting the wrong person now. Wait to open the envelope, Mr. Blake, till you have got at the truth. And then compare the name of the guilty person, with the name that I have written in that sealed letter.”\n\nI put the letter into my pocket—and then asked for the Sergeant’s opinion of the measures which we had taken at the bank.\n\n“Very well intended, sir,” he answered, “and quite the right thing to do. But there was another person who ought to have been looked after besides Mr. Luker.”\n\n“The person named in the letter you have just given to me?”\n\n“Yes, Mr. Blake, the person named in the letter. It can’t be helped now. I shall have something to propose to you and Mr. Bruff, sir, when the time comes. Let’s wait, first, and see if the boy has anything to tell us that is worth hearing.”\n\nIt was close on ten o’clock, and the boy had not made his appearance. Sergeant Cuff talked of other matters. He asked after his old friend Betteredge, and his old enemy the gardener. In a minute more, he would no doubt have got from this, to the subject of his favourite roses, if my servant had not interrupted us by announcing that the boy was below.\n\nOn being brought into the room, Gooseberry stopped at the threshold of the door, and looked distrustfully at the stranger who was in my company. I told the boy to come to me.\n\n“You may speak before this gentleman,” I said. “He is here to assist me; and he knows all that has happened. Sergeant Cuff,” I added, “this is the boy from Mr. Bruff’s office.”\n\nIn our modern system of civilisation, celebrity (no matter of what kind) is the lever that will move anything. The fame of the great Cuff had even reached the ears of the small Gooseberry. The boy’s ill-fixed eyes rolled, when I mentioned the illustrious name, till I thought they really must have dropped on the carpet.\n\n“Come here, my lad,” said the Sergeant, “and let’s hear what you have got to tell us.”\n\nThe notice of the great man—the hero of many a famous story in every lawyer’s office in London—appeared to fascinate the boy. He placed himself in front of Sergeant Cuff, and put his hands behind him, after the approved fashion of a neophyte who is examined in his catechism.\n\n“What is your name?” said the Sergeant, beginning with the first question in the catechism.\n\n“Octavius Guy,” answered the boy. “They call me Gooseberry at the office because of my eyes.”\n\n“Octavius Guy, otherwise Gooseberry,” pursued the Sergeant, with the utmost gravity, “you were missed at the bank yesterday. What were you about?”\n\n“If you please, sir, I was following a man.”\n\n“Who was he?”\n\n“A tall man, sir, with a big black beard, dressed like a sailor.”\n\n“I remember the man!” I broke in. “Mr. Bruff and I thought he was a spy employed by the Indians.”\n\nSergeant Cuff did not appear to be much impressed by what Mr. Bruff and I had thought. He went on catechising Gooseberry.\n\n“Well?” he said—“and why did you follow the sailor?”\n\n“If you please, sir, Mr. Bruff wanted to know whether Mr. Luker passed anything to anybody on his way out of the bank. I saw Mr. Luker pass something to the sailor with the black beard.”\n\n“Why didn’t you tell Mr. Bruff what you saw?”\n\n“I hadn’t time to tell anybody, sir, the sailor went out in such a hurry.”\n\n“And you ran out after him—eh?”\n\n“Yes, sir.”\n\n“Gooseberry,” said the Sergeant, patting his head, “you have got something in that small skull of yours—and it isn’t cotton-wool. I am greatly pleased with you, so far.”\n\nThe boy blushed with pleasure. Sergeant Cuff went on.\n\n“Well? and what did the sailor do, when he got into the street?”\n\n“He called a cab, sir.”\n\n“And what did you do?”\n\n“Held on behind, and run after it.”\n\nBefore the Sergeant could put his next question, another visitor was announced—the head clerk from Mr. Bruff’s office.\n\nFeeling the importance of not interrupting Sergeant Cuff’s examination of the boy, I received the clerk in another room. He came with bad news of his employer. The agitation and excitement of the last two days had proved too much for Mr. Bruff. He had awoke that morning with an attack of gout; he was confined to his room at Hampstead; and, in the present critical condition of our affairs, he was very uneasy at being compelled to leave me without the advice and assistance of an experienced person. The chief clerk had received orders to hold himself at my disposal, and was willing to do his best to replace Mr. Bruff.\n\nI wrote at once to quiet the old gentleman’s mind, by telling him of Sergeant Cuff’s visit: adding that Gooseberry was at that moment under examination; and promising to inform Mr. Bruff, either personally, or by letter, of whatever might occur later in the day. Having despatched the clerk to Hampstead with my note, I returned to the room which I had left, and found Sergeant Cuff at the fireplace, in the act of ringing the bell.\n\n“I beg your pardon, Mr. Blake,” said the Sergeant. “I was just going to send word by your servant that I wanted to speak to you. There isn’t a doubt on my mind that this boy—this most meritorious boy,” added the Sergeant, patting Gooseberry on the head, “has followed the right man. Precious time has been lost, sir, through your unfortunately not being at home at half past ten last night. The only thing to do, now, is to send for a cab immediately.”\n\nIn five minutes more, Sergeant Cuff and I (with Gooseberry on the box to guide the driver) were on our way eastward, towards the City.\n\n“One of these days,” said the Sergeant, pointing through the front window of the cab, “that boy will do great things in my late profession. He is the brightest and cleverest little chap I have met with, for many a long year past. You shall hear the substance, Mr. Blake, of what he told me while you were out of the room. You were present, I think, when he mentioned that he held on behind the cab, and ran after it?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Well, sir, the cab went from Lombard Street to the Tower Wharf. The sailor with the black beard got out, and spoke to the steward of the Rotterdam steamboat, which was to start next morning. He asked if he could be allowed to go on board at once, and sleep in his berth over-night. The steward said, No. The cabins, and berths, and bedding were all to have a thorough cleaning that evening, and no passenger could be allowed to come on board, before the morning. The sailor turned round, and left the wharf. When he got into the street again, the boy noticed for the first time, a man dressed like a respectable mechanic, walking on the opposite side of the road, and apparently keeping the sailor in view. The sailor stopped at an eating-house in the neighbourhood, and went in. The boy—not being able to make up his mind, at the moment—hung about among some other boys, staring at the good things in the eating-house window. He noticed the mechanic waiting, as he himself was waiting—but still on the opposite side of the street. After a minute, a cab came by slowly, and stopped where the mechanic was standing. The boy could only see plainly one person in the cab, who leaned forward at the window to speak to the mechanic. He described that person, Mr. Blake, without any prompting from me, as having a dark face, like the face of an Indian.”\n\nIt was plain, by this time, that Mr. Bruff and I had made another mistake. The sailor with the black beard was clearly not a spy in the service of the Indian conspiracy. Was he, by any possibility, the man who had got the Diamond?\n\n“After a little,” pursued the Sergeant, “the cab moved on slowly down the street. The mechanic crossed the road, and went into the eating-house. The boy waited outside till he was hungry and tired—and then went into the eating-house, in his turn. He had a shilling in his pocket; and he dined sumptuously, he tells me, on a black-pudding, an eel-pie, and a bottle of ginger-beer. What can a boy not digest? The substance in question has never been found yet.”\n\n“What did he see in the eating-house?” I asked.\n\n“Well, Mr. Blake, he saw the sailor reading the newspaper at one table, and the mechanic reading the newspaper at another. It was dusk before the sailor got up, and left the place. He looked about him suspiciously when he got out into the street. The boy—being a boy—passed unnoticed. The mechanic had not come out yet. The sailor walked on, looking about him, and apparently not very certain of where he was going next. The mechanic appeared once more, on the opposite side of the road. The sailor went on, till he got to Shore Lane, leading into Lower Thames Street. There he stopped before a public-house, under the sign of ‘The Wheel of Fortune,’ and, after examining the place outside, went in. Gooseberry went in too. There were a great many people, mostly of the decent sort, at the bar. ‘The Wheel of Fortune’ is a very respectable house, Mr. Blake; famous for its porter and pork-pies.”\n\nThe Sergeant’s digressions irritated me. He saw it; and confined himself more strictly to Gooseberry’s evidence when he went on.\n\n“The sailor,” he resumed, “asked if he could have a bed. The landlord said ‘No; they were full.’ The barmaid corrected him, and said ‘Number Ten was empty.’ A waiter was sent for to show the sailor to Number Ten. Just before that, Gooseberry had noticed the mechanic among the people at the bar. Before the waiter had answered the call, the mechanic had vanished. The sailor was taken off to his room. Not knowing what to do next, Gooseberry had the wisdom to wait and see if anything happened. Something did happen. The landlord was called for. Angry voices were heard upstairs. The mechanic suddenly made his appearance again, collared by the landlord, and exhibiting, to Gooseberry’s great surprise, all the signs and tokens of being drunk. The landlord thrust him out at the door, and threatened him with the police if he came back. From the altercation between them, while this was going on, it appeared that the man had been discovered in Number Ten, and had declared with drunken obstinacy that he had taken the room. Gooseberry was so struck by this sudden intoxication of a previously sober person, that he couldn’t resist running out after the mechanic into the street. As long as he was in sight of the public-house, the man reeled about in the most disgraceful manner. The moment he turned the corner of the street, he recovered his balance instantly, and became as sober a member of society as you could wish to see. Gooseberry went back to ‘The Wheel of Fortune’ in a very bewildered state of mind. He waited about again, on the chance of something happening. Nothing happened; and nothing more was to be heard, or seen, of the sailor. Gooseberry decided on going back to the office. Just as he came to this conclusion, who should appear, on the opposite side of the street as usual, but the mechanic again! He looked up at one particular window at the top of the public-house, which was the only one that had a light in it. The light seemed to relieve his mind. He left the place directly. The boy made his way back to Gray’s Inn—got your card and message—called—and failed to find you. There you have the state of the case, Mr. Blake, as it stands at the present time.”\n\n“What is your own opinion of the case, Sergeant?”\n\n“I think it’s serious, sir. Judging by what the boy saw, the Indians are in it, to begin with.”\n\n“Yes. And the sailor is evidently the person to whom Mr. Luker passed the Diamond. It seems odd that Mr. Bruff, and I, and the man in Mr. Bruff’s employment, should all have been mistaken about who the person was.”\n\n“Not at all, Mr. Blake. Considering the risk that person ran, it’s likely enough that Mr. Luker purposely misled you, by previous arrangement between them.”\n\n“Do you understand the proceedings at the public-house?” I asked. “The man dressed like a mechanic was acting of course in the employment of the Indians. But I am as much puzzled to account for his sudden assumption of drunkenness as Gooseberry himself.”\n\n“I think I can give a guess at what it means, sir,” said the Sergeant. “If you will reflect, you will see that the man must have had some pretty strict instructions from the Indians. They were far too noticeable themselves to risk being seen at the bank, or in the public-house—they were obliged to trust everything to their deputy. Very good. Their deputy hears a certain number named in the public-house, as the number of the room which the sailor is to have for the night—that being also the room (unless our notion is all wrong) which the Diamond is to have for the night, too. Under those circumstances, the Indians, you may rely on it, would insist on having a description of the room—of its position in the house, of its capability of being approached from the outside, and so on. What was the man to do, with such orders as these? Just what he did! He ran upstairs to get a look at the room, before the sailor was taken into it. He was found there, making his observations—and he shammed drunk, as the easiest way of getting out of the difficulty. That’s how I read the riddle. After he was turned out of the public-house, he probably went with his report to the place where his employers were waiting for him. And his employers, no doubt, sent him back to make sure that the sailor was really settled at the public-house till the next morning. As for what happened at ‘The Wheel of Fortune,’ after the boy left—we ought to have discovered that last night. It’s eleven in the morning, now. We must hope for the best, and find out what we can.”\n\nIn a quarter of an hour more, the cab stopped in Shore Lane, and Gooseberry opened the door for us to get out.\n\n“All right?” asked the Sergeant.\n\n“All right,” answered the boy.\n\nThe moment we entered “The Wheel of Fortune” it was plain even to my inexperienced eyes that there was something wrong in the house.\n\nThe only person behind the counter at which the liquors were served, was a bewildered servant girl, perfectly ignorant of the business. One or two customers, waiting for their morning drink, were tapping impatiently on the counter with their money. The barmaid appeared from the inner regions of the parlour, excited and preoccupied. She answered Sergeant Cuff’s inquiry for the landlord, by telling him sharply that her master was upstairs, and was not to be bothered by anybody.\n\n“Come along with me, sir,” said Sergeant Cuff, coolly leading the way upstairs, and beckoning to the boy to follow him.\n\nThe barmaid called to her master, and warned him that strangers were intruding themselves into the house. On the first floor we were encountered by the landlord, hurrying down, in a highly irritated state, to see what was the matter.\n\n“Who the devil are you? and what do you want here?” he asked.\n\n“Keep your temper,” said the Sergeant, quietly. “I’ll tell you who I am to begin with. I am Sergeant Cuff.”\n\nThe illustrious name instantly produced its effect. The angry landlord threw open the door of a sitting-room, and asked the Sergeant’s pardon.\n\n“I am annoyed and out of sorts, sir—that’s the truth,” he said. “Something unpleasant has happened in the house this morning. A man in my way of business has a deal to upset his temper, Sergeant Cuff.”\n\n“Not a doubt of it,” said the Sergeant. “I’ll come at once, if you will allow me, to what brings us here. This gentleman and I want to trouble you with a few inquiries, on a matter of some interest to both of us.”\n\n“Relating to what, sir?” asked the landlord.\n\n“Relating to a dark man, dressed like a sailor, who slept here last night.”\n\n“Good God! that’s the man who is upsetting the whole house at this moment!” exclaimed the landlord. “Do you, or does this gentleman know anything about him?”\n\n“We can’t be certain till we see him,” answered the Sergeant.\n\n“See him?” echoed the landlord. “That’s the one thing that nobody has been able to do since seven o’clock this morning. That was the time when he left word, last night, that he was to be called. He was called—and there was no getting an answer from him, and no opening his door to see what was the matter. They tried again at eight, and they tried again at nine. No use! There was the door still locked—and not a sound to be heard in the room! I have been out this morning—and I only got back a quarter of an hour ago. I have hammered at the door myself—and all to no purpose. The potboy has gone to fetch a carpenter. If you can wait a few minutes, gentlemen, we will have the door opened, and see what it means.”\n\n“Was the man drunk last night?” asked Sergeant Cuff.\n\n“Perfectly sober, sir—or I would never have let him sleep in my house.”\n\n“Did he pay for his bed beforehand?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Could he leave the room in any way, without going out by the door?”\n\n“The room is a garret,” said the landlord. “But there’s a trap-door in the ceiling, leading out on to the roof—and a little lower down the street, there’s an empty house under repair. Do you think, Sergeant, the blackguard has got off in that way, without paying?”\n\n“A sailor,” said Sergeant Cuff, “might have done it—early in the morning, before the street was astir. He would be used to climbing, and his head wouldn’t fail him on the roofs of the houses.”\n\nAs he spoke, the arrival of the carpenter was announced. We all went upstairs, at once, to the top story. I noticed that the Sergeant was unusually grave, even for him. It also struck me as odd that he told the boy (after having previously encouraged him to follow us), to wait in the room below till we came down again.\n\nThe carpenter’s hammer and chisel disposed of the resistance of the door in a few minutes. But some article of furniture had been placed against it inside, as a barricade. By pushing at the door, we thrust this obstacle aside, and so got admission to the room. The landlord entered first; the Sergeant second; and I third. The other persons present followed us.\n\nWe all looked towards the bed, and all started.\n\nThe man had not left the room. He lay, dressed, on the bed—with a white pillow over his face, which completely hid it from view.\n\n“What does that mean?” said the landlord, pointing to the pillow.\n\nSergeant Cuff led the way to the bed, without answering, and removed the pillow.\n\nThe man’s swarthy face was placid and still; his black hair and beard were slightly, very slightly, discomposed. His eyes stared wide-open, glassy and vacant, at the ceiling. The filmy look and the fixed expression of them horrified me. I turned away, and went to the open window. The rest of them remained, where Sergeant Cuff remained, at the bed.\n\n“He’s in a fit!” I heard the landlord say.\n\n“He’s dead,” the Sergeant answered. “Send for the nearest doctor, and send for the police.”\n\nThe waiter was despatched on both errands. Some strange fascination seemed to hold Sergeant Cuff to the bed. Some strange curiosity seemed to keep the rest of them waiting, to see what the Sergeant would do next.\n\nI turned again to the window. The moment afterwards, I felt a soft pull at my coat-tails, and a small voice whispered, “Look here, sir!”\n\nGooseberry had followed us into the room. His loose eyes rolled frightfully—not in terror, but in exultation. He had made a detective-discovery on his own account. “Look here, sir,” he repeated—and led me to a table in the corner of the room.\n\nOn the table stood a little wooden box, open, and empty. On one side of the box lay some jewellers’ cotton. On the other side, was a torn sheet of white paper, with a seal on it, partly destroyed, and with an inscription in writing, which was still perfectly legible. The inscription was in these words:\n\n“Deposited with Messrs. Bushe, Lysaught, and Bushe, by Mr. Septimus Luker, of Middlesex Place, Lambeth, a small wooden box, sealed up in this envelope, and containing a valuable of great price. The box, when claimed, to be only given up by Messrs. Bushe and Co. on the personal application of Mr. Luker.”\n\nThose lines removed all further doubt, on one point at least. The sailor had been in possession of the Moonstone, when he had left the bank on the previous day.\n\nI felt another pull at my coat-tails. Gooseberry had not done with me yet.\n\n“Robbery!” whispered the boy, pointing, in high delight, to the empty box.\n\n“You were told to wait downstairs,” I said. “Go away!”\n\n“And Murder!” added Gooseberry, pointing, with a keener relish still, to the man on the bed.\n\nThere was something so hideous in the boy’s enjoyment of the horror of the scene, that I took him by the two shoulders and put him out of the room.\n\nAt the moment when I crossed the threshold of the door, I heard Sergeant Cuff’s voice, asking where I was. He met me, as I returned into the room, and forced me to go back with him to the bedside.\n\n“Mr. Blake!” he said. “Look at the man’s face. It is a face disguised—and here’s the proof of it!”\n\nHe traced with his finger a thin line of livid white, running backward from the dead man’s forehead, between the swarthy complexion, and the slightly-disturbed black hair. “Let’s see what is under this,” said the Sergeant, suddenly seizing the black hair, with a firm grip of his hand.\n\nMy nerves were not strong enough to bear it. I turned away again from the bed.\n\nThe first sight that met my eyes, at the other end of the room, was the irrepressible Gooseberry, perched on a chair, and looking with breathless interest, over the heads of his elders, at the Sergeant’s proceedings.\n\n“He’s pulling off his wig!” whispered Gooseberry, compassionating my position, as the only person in the room who could see nothing.\n\nThere was a pause—and then a cry of astonishment among the people round the bed.\n\n“He’s pulled off his beard!” cried Gooseberry.\n\nThere was another pause—Sergeant Cuff asked for something. The landlord went to the wash-hand-stand, and returned to the bed with a basin of water and a towel.\n\nGooseberry danced with excitement on the chair. “Come up here, along with me, sir! He’s washing off his complexion now!”\n\nThe Sergeant suddenly burst his way through the people about him, and came, with horror in his face, straight to the place where I was standing.\n\n“Come back to the bed, sir!” he began. He looked at me closer, and checked himself “No!” he resumed. “Open the sealed letter first—the letter I gave you this morning.”\n\nI opened the letter.\n\n“Read the name, Mr. Blake, that I have written inside.”\n\nI read the name that he had written. It was—Godfrey Ablewhite.\n\n“Now,” said the Sergeant, “come with me, and look at the man on the bed.”\n\nI went with him, and looked at the man on the bed.\n\nContributed by Sergeant Cuff.\n\n# I\n\nDorking, Surrey, July 30th, 1849. To Franklin Blake, Esq. Sir,—I beg to apologise for the delay that has occurred in the production of the Report, with which I engaged to furnish you. I have waited to make it a complete Report; and I have been met, here and there, by obstacles which it was only possible to remove by some little expenditure of patience and time.\n\nThe object which I proposed to myself has now, I hope, been attained. You will find, in these pages, answers to the greater part—if not all—of the questions, concerning the late Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, which occurred to your mind when I last had the honour of seeing you.\n\nI propose to tell you—in the first place—what is known of the manner in which your cousin met his death; appending to the statement such inferences and conclusions as we are justified (according to my opinion) in drawing from the facts.\n\nI shall then endeavour—in the second place—to put you in possession of such discoveries as I have made, respecting the proceedings of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, before, during and after the time, when you and he met as guests at the late Lady Verinder’s country house.\n\nAs to your cousin’s death, then, first.\n\nIt appears to be established, beyond any reasonable doubt, that he was killed (while he was asleep, or immediately on his waking) by being smothered with a pillow from his bed—that the persons guilty of murdering him are the three Indians—and that the object contemplated (and achieved) by the crime, was to obtain possession of the diamond, called the Moonstone.\n\nThe facts from which this conclusion is drawn, are derived partly from an examination of the room at the tavern; and partly from the evidence obtained at the Coroner’s Inquest.\n\nOn forcing the door of the room, the deceased gentleman was discovered, dead, with the pillow of the bed over his face. The medical man who examined him, being informed of this circumstance, considered the post-mortem appearances as being perfectly compatible with murder by smothering—that is to say, with murder committed by some person, or persons, pressing the pillow over the nose and mouth of the deceased, until death resulted from congestion of the lungs.\n\nNext, as to the motive for the crime.\n\nA small box, with a sealed paper torn off from it (the paper containing an inscription) was found open, and empty, on a table in the room. Mr. Luker has himself personally identified the box, the seal, and the inscription. He has declared that the box did actually contain the diamond, called the Moonstone; and he has admitted having given the box (thus sealed up) to Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite (then concealed under a disguise), on the afternoon of the twenty-sixth of June last. The fair inference from all this is, that the stealing of the Moonstone was the motive of the crime.\n\nNext, as to the manner in which the crime was committed.\n\nOn examination of the room (which is only seven feet high), a trap-door in the ceiling, leading out on to the roof of the house, was discovered open. The short ladder, used for obtaining access to the trap-door (and kept under the bed), was found placed at the opening, so as to enable any person or persons, in the room, to leave it again easily. In the trap-door itself was found a square aperture cut in the wood, apparently with some exceedingly sharp instrument, just behind the bolt which fastened the door on the inner side. In this way, any person from the outside could have drawn back the bolt, and opened the door, and have dropped (or have been noiselessly lowered by an accomplice) into the room—its height, as already observed, being only seven feet. That some person, or persons, must have got admission in this way, appears evident from the fact of the aperture being there. As to the manner in which he (or they) obtained access to the roof of the tavern, it is to be remarked that the third house, lower down in the street, was empty, and under repair—that a long ladder was left by the workmen, leading from the pavement to the top of the house—and that, on returning to their work, on the morning of the 27th, the men found the plank which they had tied to the ladder, to prevent anyone from using it in their absence, removed, and lying on the ground. As to the possibility of ascending by this ladder, passing over the roofs of the houses, passing back, and descending again, unobserved—it is discovered, on the evidence of the night policeman, that he only passes through Shore Lane twice in an hour, when out on his beat. The testimony of the inhabitants also declares, that Shore Lane, after midnight, is one of the quietest and loneliest streets in London. Here again, therefore, it seems fair to infer that—with ordinary caution, and presence of mind—any man, or men, might have ascended by the ladder, and might have descended again, unobserved. Once on the roof of the tavern, it has been proved, by experiment, that a man might cut through the trap-door, while lying down on it, and that in such a position, the parapet in front of the house would conceal him from the view of anyone passing in the street.\n\nLastly, as to the person, or persons, by whom the crime was committed.\n\nIt is known (1) that the Indians had an interest in possessing themselves of the Diamond. (2) It is at least probable that the man looking like an Indian, whom Octavius Guy saw at the window of the cab, speaking to the man dressed like a mechanic, was one of the three Hindoo conspirators. (3) It is certain that this same man dressed like a mechanic, was seen keeping Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite in view, all through the evening of the 26th, and was found in the bedroom (before Mr. Ablewhite was shown into it) under circumstances which lead to the suspicion that he was examining the room. (4) A morsel of torn gold thread was picked up in the bedroom, which persons expert in such matters, declare to be of Indian manufacture, and to be a species of gold thread not known in England. (5) On the morning of the 27th, three men, answering to the description of the three Indians, were observed in Lower Thames Street, were traced to the Tower Wharf, and were seen to leave London by the steamer bound for Rotterdam.\n\nThere is here, moral, if not legal, evidence, that the murder was committed by the Indians.\n\nWhether the man personating a mechanic was, or was not, an accomplice in the crime, it is impossible to say. That he could have committed the murder alone, seems beyond the limits of probability. Acting by himself, he could hardly have smothered Mr. Ablewhite—who was the taller and stronger man of the two—without a struggle taking place, or a cry being heard. A servant girl, sleeping in the next room, heard nothing. The landlord, sleeping in the room below, heard nothing. The whole evidence points to the inference that more than one man was concerned in this crime—and the circumstances, I repeat, morally justify the conclusion that the Indians committed it.\n\nI have only to add, that the verdict at the Coroner’s Inquest was Wilful Murder against some person, or persons, unknown. Mr. Ablewhite’s family have offered a reward, and no effort has been left untried to discover the guilty persons. The man dressed like a mechanic has eluded all inquiries. The Indians have been traced. As to the prospect of ultimately capturing these last, I shall have a word to say to you on that head, when I reach the end of the present Report.\n\nIn the meanwhile, having now written all that is needful on the subject of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s death, I may pass next to the narrative of his proceedings before, during, and after the time, when you and he met at the late Lady Verinder’s house.\n\n# III\n\nWith regard to the subject now in hand, I may state, at the outset, that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s life had two sides to it.\n\nThe side turned up to the public view, presented the spectacle of a gentleman, possessed of considerable reputation as a speaker at charitable meetings, and endowed with administrative abilities, which he placed at the disposal of various Benevolent Societies, mostly of the female sort. The side kept hidden from the general notice, exhibited this same gentleman in the totally different character of a man of pleasure, with a villa in the suburbs which was not taken in his own name, and with a lady in the villa, who was not taken in his own name, either.\n\nMy investigations in the villa have shown me several fine pictures and statues; furniture tastefully selected, and admirably made; and a conservatory of the rarest flowers, the match of which it would not be easy to find in all London. My investigation of the lady has resulted in the discovery of jewels which are worthy to take rank with the flowers, and of carriages and horses which have (deservedly) produced a sensation in the Park, among persons well qualified to judge of the build of the one, and the breed of the others.\n\nAll this is, so far, common enough. The villa and the lady are such familiar objects in London life, that I ought to apologise for introducing them to notice. But what is not common and not familiar (in my experience), is that all these fine things were not only ordered, but paid for. The pictures, the statues, the flowers, the jewels, the carriages, and the horses—inquiry proved, to my indescribable astonishment, that not a sixpence of debt was owing on any of them. As to the villa, it had been bought, out and out, and settled on the lady.\n\nI might have tried to find the right reading of this riddle, and tried in vain—but for Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s death, which caused an inquiry to be made into the state of his affairs.\n\nThe inquiry elicited these facts:—\n\nThat Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was entrusted with the care of a sum of twenty thousand pounds—as one of two Trustees for a young gentleman, who was still a minor in the year eighteen hundred and forty-eight. That the Trust was to lapse, and that the young gentleman was to receive the twenty thousand pounds on the day when he came of age, in the month of February, eighteen hundred and fifty. That, pending the arrival of this period, an income of six hundred pounds was to be paid to him by his two Trustees, half-yearly—at Christmas and Midsummer Day. That this income was regularly paid by the active Trustee, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite. That the twenty thousand pounds (from which the income was supposed to be derived) had every farthing of it been sold out of the Funds, at different periods, ending with the end of the year eighteen hundred and forty-seven. That the power of attorney, authorising the bankers to sell out the stock, and the various written orders telling them what amounts to sell out, were formally signed by both the Trustees. That the signature of the second Trustee (a retired army officer, living in the country) was a signature forged, in every case, by the active Trustee—otherwise Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.\n\nIn these facts lies the explanation of Mr. Godfrey’s honourable conduct, in paying the debts incurred for the lady and the villa—and (as you will presently see) of more besides.\n\nWe may now advance to the date of Miss Verinder’s birthday (in the year eighteen hundred and forty-eight)—the twenty-first of June.\n\nOn the day before, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite arrived at his father’s house, and asked (as I know from Mr. Ablewhite, senior, himself) for a loan of three hundred pounds. Mark the sum; and remember at the same time, that the half-yearly payment to the young gentleman was due on the twenty-fourth of the month. Also, that the whole of the young gentleman’s fortune had been spent by his Trustee, by the end of the year ’forty-seven.\n\nMr. Ablewhite, senior, refused to lend his son a farthing.\n\nThe next day Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite rode over, with you, to Lady Verinder’s house. A few hours afterwards, Mr. Godfrey (as you yourself have told me) made a proposal of marriage to Miss Verinder. Here, he saw his way no doubt—if accepted—to the end of all his money anxieties, present and future. But, as events actually turned out, what happened? Miss Verinder refused him.\n\nOn the night of the birthday, therefore, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s pecuniary position was this. He had three hundred pounds to find on the twenty-fourth of the month, and twenty thousand pounds to find in February eighteen hundred and fifty. Failing to raise these sums, at these times, he was a ruined man.\n\nUnder those circumstances, what takes place next?\n\nYou exasperate Mr. Candy, the doctor, on the sore subject of his profession; and he plays you a practical joke, in return, with a dose of laudanum. He trusts the administration of the dose, prepared in a little phial, to Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite—who has himself confessed the share he had in the matter, under circumstances which shall presently be related to you. Mr. Godfrey is all the readier to enter into the conspiracy, having himself suffered from your sharp tongue in the course of the evening. He joins Betteredge in persuading you to drink a little brandy and water before you go to bed. He privately drops the dose of laudanum into your cold grog. And you drink the mixture.\n\nLet us now shift the scene, if you please to Mr. Luker’s house at Lambeth. And allow me to remark, by way of preface, that Mr. Bruff and I, together, have found a means of forcing the money-lender to make a clean breast of it. We have carefully sifted the statement he has addressed to us; and here it is at your service.\n\nLate on the evening of Friday, the twenty-third of June (’forty-eight), Mr. Luker was surprised by a visit from Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite. He was more than surprised, when Mr. Godfrey produced the Moonstone. No such Diamond (according to Mr. Luker’s experience) was in the possession of any private person in Europe.\n\nMr. Godfrey Ablewhite had two modest proposals to make, in relation to this magnificent gem. First, Would Mr. Luker be so good as to buy it? Secondly, Would Mr. Luker (in default of seeing his way to the purchase) undertake to sell it on commission, and to pay a sum down, on the anticipated result?\n\nMr. Luker tested the Diamond, weighed the Diamond and estimated the value of the Diamond, before he answered a word. His estimate (allowing for the flaw in the stone) was thirty thousand pounds.\n\nHaving reached that result, Mr. Luker opened his lips, and put a question: “How did you come by this?” Only six words! But what volumes of meaning in them!\n\nMr. Godfrey Ablewhite began a story. Mr. Luker opened his lips again, and only said three words, this time. “That won’t do!”\n\nMr. Godfrey Ablewhite began another story. Mr. Luker wasted no more words on him. He got up, and rang the bell for the servant to show the gentleman out.\n\nUpon this compulsion, Mr. Godfrey made an effort, and came out with a new and amended version of the affair, to the following effect.\n\nAfter privately slipping the laudanum into your brandy and water, he wished you good-night, and went into his own room. It was the next room to yours; and the two had a door of communication between them. On entering his own room Mr. Godfrey (as he supposed) closed his door. His money troubles kept him awake. He sat, in his dressing-gown and slippers, for nearly an hour, thinking over his position. Just as he was preparing to get into bed, he heard you, talking to yourself, in your own room, and going to the door of communication, found that he had not shut it as he supposed.\n\nHe looked into your room to see what was the matter. He discovered you with the candle in your hand, just leaving your bedchamber. He heard you say to yourself, in a voice quite unlike your own voice, “How do I know? The Indians may be hidden in the house.”\n\nUp to that time, he had simply supposed himself (in giving you the laudanum) to be helping to make you the victim of a harmless practical joke. It now occurred to him, that the laudanum had taken some effect on you, which had not been foreseen by the doctor, any more than by himself. In the fear of an accident happening he followed you softly to see what you would do.\n\nHe followed you to Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, and saw you go in. You left the door open. He looked through the crevice thus produced, between the door and the post, before he ventured into the room himself.\n\nIn that position, he not only detected you in taking the Diamond out of the drawer—he also detected Miss Verinder, silently watching you from her bedroom, through her open door. His own eyes satisfied him that she saw you take the Diamond, too.\n\nBefore you left the sitting-room again, you hesitated a little. Mr. Godfrey took advantage of this hesitation to get back again to his bedroom before you came out, and discovered him. He had barely got back, before you got back too. You saw him (as he supposes) just as he was passing through the door of communication. At any rate, you called to him in a strange, drowsy voice.\n\nHe came back to you. You looked at him in a dull sleepy way. You put the Diamond into his hand. You said to him, “Take it back, Godfrey, to your father’s bank. It’s safe there—it’s not safe here.” You turned away unsteadily, and put on your dressing-gown. You sat down in the large arm-chair in your room. You said, “I can’t take it back to the bank. My head’s like lead—and I can’t feel my feet under me.” Your head sank on the back of the chair—you heaved a heavy sigh—and you fell asleep.\n\nMr. Godfrey Ablewhite went back, with the Diamond, into his own room. His statement is, that he came to no conclusion, at that time—except that he would wait, and see what happened in the morning.\n\nWhen the morning came, your language and conduct showed that you were absolutely ignorant of what you had said and done overnight. At the same time, Miss Verinder’s language and conduct showed that she was resolved to say nothing (in mercy to you) on her side. If Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite chose to keep the Diamond, he might do so with perfect impunity. The Moonstone stood between him and ruin. He put the Moonstone into his pocket.\n\n# V\n\nThis was the story told by your cousin (under pressure of necessity) to Mr. Luker.\n\nMr. Luker believed the story to be, as to all main essentials, true—on this ground, that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was too great a fool to have invented it. Mr. Bruff and I agree with Mr. Luker, in considering this test of the truth of the story to be a perfectly reliable one.\n\nThe next question, was the question of what Mr. Luker would do in the matter of the Moonstone. He proposed the following terms, as the only terms on which he would consent to mix himself up with, what was (even in his line of business) a doubtful and dangerous transaction.\n\nMr. Luker would consent to lend Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite the sum of two thousand pounds, on condition that the Moonstone was to be deposited with him as a pledge. If, at the expiration of one year from that date, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite paid three thousand pounds to Mr. Luker, he was to receive back the Diamond, as a pledge redeemed. If he failed to produce the money at the expiration of the year, the pledge (otherwise the Moonstone) was to be considered as forfeited to Mr. Luker—who would, in this latter case, generously make Mr. Godfrey a present of certain promissory notes of his (relating to former dealings) which were then in the money-lender’s possession.\n\nIt is needless to say, that Mr. Godfrey indignantly refused to listen to these monstrous terms. Mr. Luker thereupon, handed him back the Diamond, and wished him good-night.\n\nYour cousin went to the door, and came back again. How was he to be sure that the conversation of that evening would be kept strictly secret between his friend and himself?\n\nMr. Luker didn’t profess to know how. If Mr. Godfrey had accepted his terms, Mr. Godfrey would have made him an accomplice, and might have counted on his silence as on a certainty. As things were, Mr. Luker must be guided by his own interests. If awkward inquiries were made, how could he be expected to compromise himself, for the sake of a man who had declined to deal with him?\n\nReceiving this reply, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite did, what all animals (human and otherwise) do, when they find themselves caught in a trap. He looked about him in a state of helpless despair. The day of the month, recorded on a neat little card in a box on the money-lender’s chimney-piece, happened to attract his eye. It was the twenty-third of June. On the twenty-fourth he had three hundred pounds to pay to the young gentleman for whom he was trustee, and no chance of raising the money, except the chance that Mr. Luker had offered to him. But for this miserable obstacle, he might have taken the Diamond to Amsterdam, and have made a marketable commodity of it, by having it cut up into separate stones. As matters stood, he had no choice but to accept Mr. Luker’s terms. After all, he had a year at his disposal, in which to raise the three thousand pounds—and a year is a long time.\n\nMr. Luker drew out the necessary documents on the spot. When they were signed, he gave Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite two cheques. One, dated June 23rd, for three hundred pounds. Another, dated a week on, for the remaining balance—seventeen hundred pounds.\n\nHow the Moonstone was trusted to the keeping of Mr. Luker’s bankers, and how the Indians treated Mr. Luker and Mr. Godfrey (after that had been done) you know already.\n\nThe next event in your cousin’s life refers again to Miss Verinder. He proposed marriage to her for the second time—and (after having being accepted) he consented, at her request, to consider the marriage as broken off. One of his reasons for making this concession has been penetrated by Mr. Bruff. Miss Verinder had only a life interest in her mother’s property—and there was no raising the twenty thousand pounds on that.\n\nBut you will say, he might have saved the three thousand pounds, to redeem the pledged Diamond, if he had married. He might have done so certainly—supposing neither his wife, nor her guardians and trustees, objected to his anticipating more than half of the income at his disposal, for some unknown purpose, in the first year of his marriage. But even if he got over this obstacle, there was another waiting for him in the background. The lady at the Villa, had heard of his contemplated marriage. A superb woman, Mr. Blake, of the sort that are not to be trifled with—the sort with the light complexion and the Roman nose. She felt the utmost contempt for Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite. It would be silent contempt, if he made a handsome provision for her. Otherwise, it would be contempt with a tongue to it. Miss Verinder’s life interest allowed him no more hope of raising the “provision” than of raising the twenty thousand pounds. He couldn’t marry—he really couldn’t marry, under all the circumstances.\n\nHow he tried his luck again with another lady, and how that marriage also broke down on the question of money, you know already. You also know of the legacy of five thousand pounds, left to him shortly afterwards, by one of those many admirers among the soft sex whose good graces this fascinating man had contrived to win. That legacy (as the event has proved) led him to his death.\n\nI have ascertained that when he went abroad, on getting his five thousand pounds, he went to Amsterdam. There he made all the necessary arrangements for having the Diamond cut into separate stones. He came back (in disguise), and redeemed the Moonstone, on the appointed day. A few days were allowed to elapse (as a precaution agreed to by both parties) before the jewel was actually taken out of the bank. If he had got safe with it to Amsterdam, there would have been just time between July ’forty-nine, and February ’fifty (when the young gentleman came of age) to cut the Diamond, and to make a marketable commodity (polished or unpolished) of the separate stones. Judge from this, what motives he had to run the risk which he actually ran. It was “neck or nothing” with him—if ever it was “neck or nothing” with a man yet.\n\nI have only to remind you, before closing this Report, that there is a chance of laying hands on the Indians, and of recovering the Moonstone yet. They are now (there is every reason to believe) on their passage to Bombay, in an East Indiaman. The ship (barring accidents) will touch at no other port on her way out; and the authorities at Bombay (already communicated with by letter, overland) will be prepared to board the vessel, the moment she enters the harbour.\n\nI have the honour to remain, dear sir, your obedient servant, RICHARD CUFF (late sergeant in the Detective Force, Scotland Yard, London).*\n\n* NOTE.—Wherever the Report touches on the events of the birthday, or of the three days that followed it, compare with Betteredge’s Narrative, chapters viii. to xiii.\n\nIn a Letter from Mr. Candy.\n\nFrizinghall, Wednesday, September 26th, 1849.—Dear Mr. Franklin Blake, you will anticipate the sad news I have to tell you, on finding your letter to Ezra Jennings returned to you, unopened, in this enclosure. He died in my arms, at sunrise, on Wednesday last.\n\nI am not to blame for having failed to warn you that his end was at hand. He expressly forbade me to write to you. “I am indebted to Mr. Franklin Blake,” he said, “for having seen some happy days. Don’t distress him, Mr. Candy—don’t distress him.”\n\nHis sufferings, up to the last six hours of his life, were terrible to see. In the intervals of remission, when his mind was clear, I entreated him to tell me of any relatives of his to whom I might write. He asked to be forgiven for refusing anything to me. And then he said—not bitterly—that he would die as he had lived, forgotten and unknown. He maintained that resolution to the last. There is no hope now of making any discoveries concerning him. His story is a blank.\n\nThe day before he died, he told me where to find all his papers. I brought them to him on his bed. There was a little bundle of old letters which he put aside. There was his unfinished book. There was his Diary—in many locked volumes. He opened the volume for this year, and tore out, one by one, the pages relating to the time when you and he were together. “Give those,” he said, “to Mr. Franklin Blake. In years to come, he may feel an interest in looking back at what is written there.” Then he clasped his hands, and prayed God fervently to bless you, and those dear to you. He said he should like to see you again. But the next moment he altered his mind. “No,” he answered when I offered to write. “I won’t distress him! I won’t distress him!”\n\nAt his request I next collected the other papers—that is to say, the bundle of letters, the unfinished book and the volumes of the Diary—and enclosed them all in one wrapper, sealed with my own seal. “Promise,” he said, “that you will put this into my coffin with your own hand; and that you will see that no other hand touches it afterwards.”\n\nI gave him my promise. And the promise has been performed.\n\nHe asked me to do one other thing for him—which it cost me a hard struggle to comply with. He said, “Let my grave be forgotten. Give me your word of honour that you will allow no monument of any sort—not even the commonest tombstone—to mark the place of my burial. Let me sleep, nameless. Let me rest, unknown.” When I tried to plead with him to alter his resolution, he became for the first, and only time, violently agitated. I could not bear to see it; and I gave way. Nothing but a little grass mound marks the place of his rest. In time, the tombstones will rise round it. And the people who come after us will look and wonder at the nameless grave.\n\nAs I have told you, for six hours before his death his sufferings ceased. He dozed a little. I think he dreamed. Once or twice he smiled. A woman’s name, as I suppose—the name of “Ella”—was often on his lips at this time. A few minutes before the end he asked me to lift him on his pillow, to see the sun rise through the window. He was very weak. His head fell on my shoulder. He whispered, “It’s coming!” Then he said, “Kiss me!” I kissed his forehead. On a sudden he lifted his head. The sunlight touched his face. A beautiful expression, an angelic expression, came over it. He cried out three times, “Peace! peace! peace!” His head sank back again on my shoulder, and the long trouble of his life was at an end.\n\nSo he has gone from us. This was, as I think, a great man—though the world never knew him. He had the sweetest temper I have ever met with. The loss of him makes me feel very lonely. Perhaps I have never been quite myself since my illness. Sometimes, I think of giving up my practice, and going away, and trying what some of the foreign baths and waters will do for me.\n\nIt is reported here, that you and Miss Verinder are to be married next month. Please to accept my best congratulations.\n\nThe pages of my poor friend’s Journal are waiting for you at my house—sealed up, with your name on the wrapper. I was afraid to trust them to the post.\n\nMy best respects and good wishes attend Miss Verinder. I remain, dear Mr. Franklin Blake, truly yours,\n\nContributed by Gabriel Betteredge.\n\nI am the person (as you remember no doubt) who led the way in these pages, and opened the story. I am also the person who is left behind, as it were, to close the story up.\n\nLet nobody suppose that I have any last words to say here concerning the Indian Diamond. I hold that unlucky jewel in abhorrence—and I refer you to other authority than mine, for such news of the Moonstone as you may, at the present time, be expected to receive. My purpose, in this place, is to state a fact in the history of the family, which has been passed over by everybody, and which I won’t allow to be disrespectfully smothered up in that way. The fact to which I allude is—the marriage of Miss Rachel and Mr. Franklin Blake. This interesting event took place at our house in Yorkshire, on Tuesday, October ninth, eighteen hundred and forty-nine. I had a new suit of clothes on the occasion. And the married couple went to spend the honeymoon in Scotland.\n\nFamily festivals having been rare enough at our house, since my poor mistress’s death, I own—on this occasion of the wedding—to having (towards the latter part of the day) taken a drop too much on the strength of it.\n\nIf you have ever done the same sort of thing yourself you will understand and feel for me. If you have not, you will very likely say, “Disgusting old man! why does he tell us this?” The reason why is now to come.\n\nHaving, then, taken my drop (bless you! you have got your favourite vice, too; only your vice isn’t mine, and mine isn’t yours), I next applied the one infallible remedy—that remedy being, as you know, Robinson Crusoe. Where I opened that unrivalled book, I can’t say. Where the lines of print at last left off running into each other, I know, however, perfectly well. It was at page three hundred and eighteen—a domestic bit concerning Robinson Crusoe’s marriage, as follows:\n\n“With those Thoughts, I considered my new Engagement, that I had a Wife”—(Observe! so had Mr. Franklin!)—“one Child born”—(Observe again! that might yet be Mr. Franklin’s case, too!)—“and my Wife then”—What Robinson Crusoe’s wife did, or did not do, “then,” I felt no desire to discover. I scored the bit about the Child with my pencil, and put a morsel of paper for a mark to keep the place; “Lie you there,” I said, “till the marriage of Mr. Franklin and Miss Rachel is some months older—and then we’ll see!”\n\nThe months passed (more than I had bargained for), and no occasion presented itself for disturbing that mark in the book. It was not till this present month of November, eighteen hundred and fifty, that Mr. Franklin came into my room, in high good spirits, and said, “Betteredge! I have got some news for you! Something is going to happen in the house, before we are many months older.”\n\n“Does it concern the family, sir?” I asked.\n\n“It decidedly concerns the family,” says Mr. Franklin.\n\n“Has your good lady anything to do with it, if you please, sir?”\n\n“She has a great deal to do with it,” says Mr. Franklin, beginning to look a little surprised.\n\n“You needn’t say a word more, sir,” I answered. “God bless you both! I’m heartily glad to hear it.”\n\nMr. Franklin stared like a person thunderstruck. “May I venture to inquire where you got your information?” he asked. “I only got mine (imparted in the strictest secrecy) five minutes since.”\n\nHere was an opportunity of producing Robinson Crusoe! Here was a chance of reading that domestic bit about the child which I had marked on the day of Mr. Franklin’s marriage! I read those miraculous words with an emphasis which did them justice, and then I looked him severely in the face. “Now, sir, do you believe in Robinson Crusoe?” I asked, with a solemnity suitable to the occasion.\n\n“Betteredge!” says Mr. Franklin, with equal solemnity, “I’m convinced at last.” He shook hands with me—and I felt that I had converted him.\n\nWith the relation of this extraordinary circumstance, my reappearance in these pages comes to an end. Let nobody laugh at the unique anecdote here related. You are welcome to be as merry as you please over everything else I have written. But when I write of Robinson Crusoe, by the Lord it’s serious—and I request you to take it accordingly!\n\nWhen this is said, all is said. Ladies and gentlemen, I make my bow, and shut up the story.\n\nOn the twenty-seventh of June last, I received instructions from Sergeant Cuff to follow three men; suspected of murder, and described as Indians. They had been seen on the Tower Wharf that morning, embarking on board the steamer bound for Rotterdam.\n\nI left London by a steamer belonging to another company, which sailed on the morning of Thursday the twenty-eighth. Arriving at Rotterdam, I succeeded in finding the commander of the Wednesday’s steamer. He informed me that the Indians had certainly been passengers on board his vessel—but as far as Gravesend only. Off that place, one of the three had inquired at what time they would reach Calais. On being informed that the steamer was bound to Rotterdam, the spokesman of the party expressed the greatest surprise and distress at the mistake which he and his two friends had made. They were all willing (he said) to sacrifice their passage money, if the commander of the steamer would only put them ashore. Commiserating their position, as foreigners in a strange land, and knowing no reason for detaining them, the commander signalled for a shore boat, and the three men left the vessel.\n\nThis proceeding of the Indians having been plainly resolved on beforehand, as a means of preventing their being traced, I lost no time in returning to England. I left the steamer at Gravesend, and discovered that the Indians had gone from that place to London. Thence, I again traced them as having left for Plymouth. Inquiries made at Plymouth proved that they had sailed, forty-eight hours previously, in the Bewley Castle, East Indiaman, bound direct to Bombay.\n\nOn receiving this intelligence, Sergeant Cuff caused the authorities at Bombay to be communicated with, overland—so that the vessel might be boarded by the police immediately on her entering the port. This step having been taken, my connection with the matter came to an end. I have heard nothing more of it since that time.\n\nI am requested by Sergeant Cuff to set in writing certain facts, concerning three men (believed to be Hindoos) who were passengers, last summer, in the ship Bewley Castle, bound for Bombay direct, under my command.\n\nThe Hindoos joined us at Plymouth. On the passage out I heard no complaint of their conduct. They were berthed in the forward part of the vessel. I had but few occasions myself of personally noticing them.\n\nIn the latter part of the voyage, we had the misfortune to be becalmed for three days and nights, off the coast of India. I have not got the ship’s journal to refer to, and I cannot now call to mind the latitude and longitude. As to our position, therefore, I am only able to state generally that the currents drifted us in towards the land, and that when the wind found us again, we reached our port in twenty-four hours afterwards.\n\nThe discipline of a ship (as all seafaring persons know) becomes relaxed in a long calm. The discipline of my ship became relaxed. Certain gentlemen among the passengers got some of the smaller boats lowered, and amused themselves by rowing about, and swimming, when the sun at evening time was cool enough to let them divert themselves in that way. The boats when done with ought to have been slung up again in their places. Instead of this they were left moored to the ship’s side. What with the heat, and what with the vexation of the weather, neither officers nor men seemed to be in heart for their duty while the calm lasted.\n\nOn the third night, nothing unusual was heard or seen by the watch on deck. When the morning came, the smallest of the boats was missing—and the three Hindoos were next reported to be missing, too.\n\nIf these men had stolen the boat shortly after dark (which I have no doubt they did), we were near enough to the land to make it vain to send in pursuit of them, when the discovery was made in the morning. I have no doubt they got ashore, in that calm weather (making all due allowance for fatigue and clumsy rowing), before day-break.\n\nOn reaching our port, I there learnt, for the first time, the reason these passengers had for seizing their opportunity of escaping from the ship. I could only make the same statement to the authorities which I have made here. They considered me to blame for allowing the discipline of the vessel to be relaxed. I have expressed my regret on this score to them, and to my owners.\n\nSince that time, nothing has been heard to my knowledge of the three Hindoos. I have no more to add to what is here written.\n\n(In a letter to Mr. Bruff.)\n\nHave you any recollection, my dear sir, of a semi-savage person whom you met out at dinner, in London, in the autumn of ’forty-eight? Permit me to remind you that the person’s name was Murthwaite, and that you and he had a long conversation together after dinner. The talk related to an Indian Diamond, called the Moonstone, and to a conspiracy then in existence to get possession of the gem.\n\nSince that time, I have been wandering in Central Asia. Thence I have drifted back to the scene of some of my past adventures in the north and north-west of India. About a fortnight since, I found myself in a certain district or province (but little known to Europeans) called Kattiawar.\n\nHere an adventure befell me, in which (incredible as it may appear) you are personally interested.\n\nIn the wild regions of Kattiawar (and how wild they are, you will understand, when I tell you that even the husbandmen plough the land, armed to the teeth), the population is fanatically devoted to the old Hindoo religion—to the ancient worship of Bramah and Vishnu. The few Mahometan families, thinly scattered about the villages in the interior, are afraid to taste meat of any kind. A Mahometan even suspected of killing that sacred animal, the cow, is, as a matter of course, put to death without mercy in these parts by the pious Hindoo neighbours who surround him. To strengthen the religious enthusiasm of the people, two of the most famous shrines of Hindoo pilgrimage are contained within the boundaries of Kattiawar. One of them is Dwarka, the birthplace of the god Krishna. The other is the sacred city of Somnauth—sacked, and destroyed as long since as the eleventh century, by the Mahometan conqueror, Mahmoud of Ghizni.\n\nFinding myself, for the second time, in these romantic regions, I resolved not to leave Kattiawar, without looking once more on the magnificent desolation of Somnauth. At the place where I planned to do this, I was (as nearly as I could calculate it) some three days distant, journeying on foot, from the sacred city.\n\nI had not been long on the road, before I noticed that other people—by twos and threes—appeared to be travelling in the same direction as myself.\n\nTo such of these as spoke to me, I gave myself out as a Hindoo-Boodhist, from a distant province, bound on a pilgrimage. It is needless to say that my dress was of the sort to carry out this description. Add, that I know the language as well as I know my own, and that I am lean enough and brown enough to make it no easy matter to detect my European origin—and you will understand that I passed muster with the people readily: not as one of themselves, but as a stranger from a distant part of their own country.\n\nOn the second day, the number of Hindoos travelling in my direction had increased to fifties and hundreds. On the third day, the throng had swollen to thousands; all slowly converging to one point—the city of Somnauth.\n\nA trifling service which I was able to render to one of my fellow-pilgrims, during the third day’s journey, proved the means of introducing me to certain Hindoos of the higher caste. From these men I learnt that the multitude was on its way to a great religious ceremony, which was to take place on a hill at a little distance from Somnauth. The ceremony was in honour of the god of the Moon; and it was to be held at night.\n\nThe crowd detained us as we drew near to the place of celebration. By the time we reached the hill the moon was high in the heaven. My Hindoo friends possessed some special privileges which enabled them to gain access to the shrine. They kindly allowed me to accompany them. When we arrived at the place, we found the shrine hidden from our view by a curtain hung between two magnificent trees. Beneath the trees a flat projection of rock jutted out, and formed a species of natural platform. Below this, I stood, in company with my Hindoo friends.\n\nLooking back down the hill, the view presented the grandest spectacle of Nature and Man, in combination, that I have ever seen. The lower slopes of the eminence melted imperceptibly into a grassy plain, the place of the meeting of three rivers. On one side, the graceful winding of the waters stretched away, now visible, now hidden by trees, as far as the eye could see. On the other, the waveless ocean slept in the calm of the night. People this lovely scene with tens of thousands of human creatures, all dressed in white, stretching down the sides of the hill, overflowing into the plain, and fringing the nearer banks of the winding rivers. Light this halt of the pilgrims by the wild red flames of cressets and torches, streaming up at intervals from every part of the innumerable throng. Imagine the moonlight of the East, pouring in unclouded glory over all—and you will form some idea of the view that met me when I looked forth from the summit of the hill.\n\nA strain of plaintive music, played on stringed instruments and flutes, recalled my attention to the hidden shrine.\n\nI turned, and saw on the rocky platform the figures of three men. In the central figure of the three I recognised the man to whom I had spoken in England, when the Indians appeared on the terrace at Lady Verinder’s house. The other two who had been his companions on that occasion were no doubt his companions also on this.\n\nOne of the spectators, near whom I was standing, saw me start. In a whisper, he explained to me the apparition of the three figures on the platform of rock.\n\nThey were Brahmins (he said) who had forfeited their caste in the service of the god. The god had commanded that their purification should be the purification by pilgrimage. On that night, the three men were to part. In three separate directions, they were to set forth as pilgrims to the shrines of India. Never more were they to look on each other’s faces. Never more were they to rest on their wanderings, from the day which witnessed their separation, to the day which witnessed their death.\n\nAs those words were whispered to me, the plaintive music ceased. The three men prostrated themselves on the rock, before the curtain which hid the shrine. They rose—they looked on one another—they embraced. Then they descended separately among the people. The people made way for them in dead silence. In three different directions I saw the crowd part, at one and the same moment. Slowly the grand white mass of the people closed together again. The track of the doomed men through the ranks of their fellow mortals was obliterated. We saw them no more.\n\nA new strain of music, loud and jubilant, rose from the hidden shrine. The crowd around me shuddered, and pressed together.\n\nThe curtain between the trees was drawn aside, and the shrine was disclosed to view.\n\nThere, raised high on a throne—seated on his typical antelope, with his four arms stretching towards the four corners of the earth—there, soared above us, dark and awful in the mystic light of heaven, the god of the Moon. And there, in the forehead of the deity, gleamed the yellow Diamond, whose splendour had last shone on me in England, from the bosom of a woman’s dress!\n\nYes! after the lapse of eight centuries, the Moonstone looks forth once more, over the walls of the sacred city in which its story first began. How it has found its way back to its wild native land—by what accident, or by what crime, the Indians regained possession of their sacred gem, may be in your knowledge, but is not in mine. You have lost sight of it in England, and (if I know anything of this people) you have lost sight of it for ever.\n\nSo the years pass, and repeat each other; so the same events revolve in the cycles of time. What will be the next adventures of the Moonstone? Who can tell?";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-155"]="(어느 가문의 문서에서 발췌한 것이다.)\n\n인도에서 쓰는 이 글을 나는 영국에 있는 내 친척들에게 부친다.\n내 목적은, 사촌 존 헌캐슬에게 우정의 손을 내밀기를 거부하게 된 그 동기를 밝히려는 데 있다. 지금까지 내가 이 문제에 관해 지켜 온 침묵은, 그 호의를 차마 잃고 싶지 않은 가족들에게 오해를 사 왔다. 나는 그들에게, 내 이야기를 다 읽기 전에는 판단을 미루어 달라고 청한다. 그리고 나는 명예를 걸고 선언하거니와, 이제부터 쓰려는 것은 엄밀히, 그리고 글자 그대로 진실이다.\n\n사촌과 나 사이의 사사로운 불화는, 우리 둘 다 얽혀 있던 큰 공적 사건에서 비롯되었다—1799년 5월 4일, 베어드 장군의 지휘 아래 감행된 세링가파탐 공략이 그것이다.\n\n그 정황을 분명히 이해시키기 위해, 나는 잠시 공격 이전의 시기로, 그리고 세링가파탐 궁전에 쌓여 있다는 보석과 황금의 보물에 관하여 우리 진영에 떠돌던 이야기들로 거슬러 올라가야겠다.\n\n# II\n\n그 이야기들 가운데 가장 터무니없는 것 하나가 어느 노란 다이아몬드에 관한 것이었다—인도 토착의 연대기에 이름 높은 보석이었다.\n\n알려진 가장 오랜 전승은, 그 돌이 달을 상징하는 네 팔 달린 인도 신의 이마에 박혀 있었다고 전한다. 한편으로는 그 독특한 빛깔 때문에, 또 한편으로는 그것이 제 몸을 장식한 신의 감응을 받아 달이 차고 이지러짐에 따라 광채가 더해지고 스러진다고 여긴 미신 때문에, 그 돌은 오늘날까지도 인도에서 통용되는 이름을 처음으로 얻었다—바로 '월장석(月長石)'이라는 이름이다. 내가 듣기로는, 이와 비슷한 미신이 옛 그리스와 로마에도 한때 널리 퍼져 있었다. 다만 그것은 (인도에서처럼) 신을 섬기는 데 바쳐진 다이아몬드가 아니라, 하급 보석에 속하는 어느 반투명한 돌에 관한 것이었으니, 그 돌이 달의 감응을 받는다고들 믿었던 것이다—이 경우에도 역시 달이, 오늘날 수집가들 사이에서 여전히 통하는 그 돌의 이름을 지어 주었다.\n\n이 노란 다이아몬드의 기구한 내력은 기독교 기원 11세기부터 시작된다.\n\n그 무렵, 이슬람의 정복자 가즈니의 마무드가 인도를 가로질러 쳐들어와, 성도(聖都) 솜나트를 함락하고, 여러 세기 동안 서 있던 그 이름난 신전에서 보물을 남김없이 약탈했다—힌두 순례의 성소요, 동방 세계의 경이이던 신전이었다.\n\n그 신전에서 섬겨지던 모든 신들 가운데, 오직 달의 신만이 정복자 이슬람교도들의 탐욕을 면했다. 세 명의 브라만이 지켜 낸 그 침범할 수 없는 신상은, 이마에 노란 다이아몬드를 박은 채 밤을 틈타 옮겨져, 인도의 두 번째 성도(聖都)—베나레스 시로 운반되었다.\n\n이곳, 새로운 성소에서—보석으로 아로새긴 전당, 황금 기둥이 떠받친 지붕 아래—달의 신은 모셔지고 섬겨졌다. 이곳에서, 성소가 완성되던 날 밤, 수호신 비슈누가 세 브라만의 꿈에 나타났다.\n\n그 신은 신의 이마에 박힌 다이아몬드에 자신의 신성한 숨결을 불어넣었다. 그러자 브라만들은 무릎 꿇고 옷자락에 얼굴을 파묻었다. 신은 명하기를, 그때부터 세 사제가 번갈아, 밤낮으로, 인간의 대(代)가 다하는 그날까지 월장석을 지키라 하였다. 브라만들은 그 말씀을 듣고 그 뜻 앞에 머리를 조아렸다. 신은 예언하기를, 그 신성한 보석에 감히 손을 대는 오만한 인간에게, 그리고 그 뒤로 그것을 물려받는 그 집안과 이름의 모든 자에게 반드시 재앙이 내리리라 하였다. 브라만들은 그 예언을 성소의 문 위에 황금 글자로 새겨 두게 하였다.\n\n한 시대가 가고 또 한 시대가 왔다—그러고도 여전히, 대를 이어 가며, 세 브라만의 후예들은 값을 매길 수 없는 그들의 월장석을 밤낮으로 지켰다. 한 시대가 가고 또 한 시대가 오기를 거듭하여, 마침내 기독교 기원 18세기 초엽에 이르러 무굴의 황제 아우랑제브의 치세를 맞았다. 그의 명령으로, 브라마를 섬기는 신전들에 또다시 파괴와 약탈이 풀려났다. 네 팔 달린 신의 성소는 신성한 짐승들을 도살함으로써 더럽혀졌고, 신들의 상은 산산이 부서졌으며, 월장석은 아우랑제브 군대의 어느 지체 높은 장교의 손에 빼앗겼다.\n\n잃어버린 보물을 드러내 놓고 힘으로 되찾을 길이 없었으므로, 세 수호 사제는 변장한 채 그것을 뒤쫓으며 지켜보았다. 대가 거듭 바뀌었다. 그 신성모독을 저지른 장수는 비참하게 죽었고, 월장석은 (제 저주를 지닌 채) 무법한 이슬람교도의 손에서 손으로 넘어갔다. 그러면서도 여전히, 온갖 우여곡절을 겪는 동안, 세 수호 사제의 후예들은 수호신 비슈누의 뜻이 그들의 신성한 보석을 되돌려 줄 그날을 기다리며 파수를 늦추지 않았다. 세월은 기독교 기원 18세기의 첫 해로부터 마지막 해로 흘러갔다. 다이아몬드는 세링가파탐의 술탄 티푸의 손에 들어갔으니, 그는 그것을 단검 자루에 장식으로 박게 하고, 자기 병기고의 가장 귀한 보물들 틈에 간수하라 명하였다. 그때에도—바로 그 술탄의 궁전 안에서—세 수호 사제는 여전히 남몰래 파수를 서고 있었다. 티푸의 가솔 가운데 세 사람이 있었는데, 다른 이들과는 낯선 자들로서, 이슬람교로 개종하거나 개종한 체함으로써 주인의 신임을 얻은 자들이었다. 소문은 바로 그 세 사람을 변장한 세 사제라 지목하였다.\n\n# III\n\n우리 진영에서 전해진 월장석의 허황된 이야기는 대략 이러하였다. 그 이야기는 내 사촌 하나를 빼고는 우리 누구에게도 대수로운 인상을 주지 못했다—그런데 기이한 것을 좋아하던 그 사촌은 그것을 곧이곧대로 믿었다. 세링가파탐 공격 전날 밤, 그는 온통 그 일을 한낱 옛이야기로 치부한다고 하여 나와 다른 이들에게 어처구니없이 화를 냈다. 어리석은 언쟁이 뒤따랐고, 헌캐슬은 그 못된 성미에 그만 자신을 잃고 말았다. 그는 제 자랑삼는 투로, 영국군이 세링가파탐을 함락하면 우리가 그의 손가락에 낀 그 다이아몬드를 보게 되리라 큰소리쳤다. 그 호언은 한바탕 폭소로 맞이되었고, 그날 밤 우리 모두가 여긴 대로, 그것으로 일은 끝난 듯싶었다.\n\n이제 나는 당신들을 그 공격의 날로 데려가려 한다.\n\n사촌과 나는 처음부터 서로 떨어졌다. 우리가 강을 건널 때에도, 첫 번째 돌파구에 영국 국기를 꽂을 때에도, 그 너머의 참호를 건널 때에도, 한 걸음 한 걸음 싸워 가며 시내로 들어설 때에도, 나는 그를 보지 못했다. 그곳이 우리 손에 들어오고, 베어드 장군이 몸소 시체 더미 아래에서 티푸의 주검을 찾아낸 뒤, 땅거미가 질 무렵에야 비로소 헌캐슬과 나는 마주쳤다.\n\n우리는 각기, 우리의 정복에 뒤이은 약탈과 혼란을 막으라는 장군의 명으로 파견된 부대에 배속되어 있었다. 종군하던 무리는 개탄스러운 난동을 부렸고, 더욱 나쁜 것은, 병사들이 지키는 이 없는 문으로 궁전의 보물고에 들어가 황금과 보석을 잔뜩 챙긴 일이었다. 사촌과 내가 마주친 곳은 바로 그 보물고 바깥뜰, 우리 편 병사들에게 군율을 세우려던 자리였다. 내가 똑똑히 보건대, 헌캐슬의 불같은 성미는 우리가 지나온 그 끔찍한 살육으로 하여 일종의 광기에 이를 만큼 격앙되어 있었다. 내 생각에 그는 자기에게 맡겨진 임무를 수행하기에 지극히 부적당한 자였다.\n\n보물고 안에는 소란과 혼란이 넘쳤으나, 내가 본 바로는 폭행은 없었다. 병사들은 (이런 표현을 써도 좋다면) 흥청거리며 스스로를 욕되게 하고 있었다. 온갖 상스러운 농지거리와 후렴 같은 말들이 그들 사이에 오갔고, 다이아몬드 이야기가 뜻밖에도 짓궂은 농담의 꼴로 다시 튀어나왔다. \"월장석은 누가 차지했나?\"—이것이 그 함성이었으니, 한곳에서 약탈이 그치기가 무섭게 다른 곳에서 다시 터지게 만들곤 하였다. 내가 여전히 부질없이 질서를 잡으려 애쓰고 있을 때, 안뜰 저편에서 소름 끼치는 고함이 들려왔고, 나는 그쪽에서 또 무슨 새로운 약탈이 벌어졌을까 두려워 곧장 그 소리를 향해 달려갔다.\n\n나는 열린 문 앞에 이르러, 인도인 둘의 주검이 (그 복색으로 미루어 짐작건대 궁전의 관리들이었다) 입구를 가로질러 죽어 널브러져 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n안에서 터진 비명에 나는 서둘러 어느 방으로 들어섰는데, 병기고로 쓰이는 듯한 곳이었다. 치명상을 입은 세 번째 인도인이, 등을 내 쪽으로 돌린 한 사내의 발치에 스러져 가고 있었다. 그 사내는 내가 들어서는 순간 몸을 돌렸고, 나는 존 헌캐슬을, 한 손에는 횃불을, 다른 손에는 피가 뚝뚝 듣는 단검을 든 그를 보았다. 단검 자루 끝에 손잡이 머리처럼 박힌 돌 하나가, 그가 나를 향해 돌아설 때 횃불빛을 받아 불꽃처럼 번쩍였다. 죽어 가던 인도인은 무릎을 꿇으며, 헌캐슬의 손에 든 단검을 가리키고는, 제 나라 말로 이렇게 말했다—\"월장석은 반드시 너와 네 핏줄에게 그 원한을 갚으리라!\" 그는 그 말을 남기고 바닥에 쓰러져 숨졌다.\n\n내가 이 일에 손쓸 겨를도 없이, 안뜰을 가로질러 나를 뒤따라온 병사들이 우르르 몰려들었다. 사촌은 미친 사람처럼 그들에게 달려들었다. \"이 방을 비워라!\" 그가 내게 소리쳤다. \"그리고 문에 파수를 세워라!\" 그가 횃불과 단검을 휘두르며 덤벼들자 병사들은 물러섰다. 나는 내가 믿을 만한 우리 중대의 보초 둘을 세워 문을 지키게 했다. 그날 밤이 다하도록 나는 다시는 사촌을 보지 못했다.\n\n이튿날 이른 아침, 약탈이 여전히 이어지는 가운데, 베어드 장군은 북을 울려, 현장에서 붙잡힌 도둑은 그가 누구이든 교수형에 처하겠노라 공공연히 포고했다. 장군의 진심을 증명하려는 듯 헌병대장이 배석했다. 그 포고를 뒤따른 인파 속에서 헌캐슬과 나는 다시 마주쳤다.\n\n그는 여느 때처럼 손을 내밀며 \"안녕하시오\" 하고 말했다.\n\n나는 답으로 손을 내밀기 전에 잠시 기다렸다.\n\n\"먼저 말해 주시오.\" 내가 말했다. \"병기고의 그 인도인이 어떻게 죽었는지, 그리고 그가 당신 손의 단검을 가리키며 남긴 그 마지막 말이 무슨 뜻이었는지를.\"\n\n\"그 인도인은, 내 짐작으로는, 치명상을 입어 죽은 것이오.\" 헌캐슬이 말했다. \"그의 마지막 말이 무슨 뜻인지는 나도 당신만큼밖에 모르오.\"\n\n나는 그를 유심히 뜯어보았다. 전날의 광기는 온통 가라앉아 있었다. 나는 그에게 한 번 더 기회를 주기로 마음먹었다.\n\n\"내게 할 말이 그것뿐이오?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n그는 \"그것뿐이오\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n나는 그에게 등을 돌렸다. 그리고 우리는 그 뒤로 말을 나눈 일이 없다.\n\n# IV\n\n내가 여기 사촌에 관하여 적는 것은 (그것을 공개해야 할 어떤 필요가 생기지 않는 한) 오직 집안에 알리기 위함임을 밝혀 두고자 한다. 헌캐슬은 내가 우리 지휘관에게 고할 만한 어떤 말도 하지 않았다. 공격 전의 그 격분을 기억하는 이들이 다이아몬드를 두고 한두 번 그를 비웃은 적은 있으나, 능히 짐작할 수 있듯이, 내가 병기고에서 그를 불시에 맞닥뜨린 그 정황에 대한 그 자신의 기억만으로도 그의 입을 다물게 하기에 충분했다. 들리는 말로는, 그는 다른 연대로 옮길 작정이라는데, 대놓고 내세우는 이유가 바로 나에게서 떨어지려는 것이라 한다.\n\n이것이 사실이든 아니든, 나는 도저히 그를 고발하는 자가 될 마음이 나지 않는다—그리고 거기에는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다고 여긴다. 이 일을 공개한다 한들, 나로서는 정황상의 심증 말고는 내놓을 증거가 없다. 그가 문간의 두 사람을 죽였다는 증거가 없을뿐더러, 안에 있던 세 번째 사람을 죽였다고조차 단언할 수 없다—내 두 눈으로 그 행위가 저질러지는 것을 보았다고 말할 수 없기 때문이다. 죽어 가던 인도인의 말을 내가 들은 것은 사실이나, 만일 그 말이 헛것에 들린 자의 광언(狂言)이었다고 판정된다면, 내가 무슨 수로 내 아는 바를 들어 그 단정을 반박하겠는가? 양쪽 우리 친척들은 내가 적은 바에 대하여 저마다 나름의 판단을 내리고, 지금 내가 이 사람에게 느끼는 혐오가 근거 있는 것인지 아닌지를 스스로 정하기 바란다.\n\n그 보석에 얽힌 황당한 인도 전설을 나는 조금도 믿지 않지만, 이 글을 맺기 전에 고백해 두어야겠다. 이 일에 관하여 나 역시 나름의 어떤 미신에 사로잡혀 있음을. 그것이 내 확신이든 내 망상이든—어느 쪽이라도 상관없다—죄는 제 안에 제 몫의 재앙을 품고 온다는 것이 그 미신이다. 나는 헌캐슬이 죄를 지었음을 확신할 뿐 아니라, 그가 그 다이아몬드를 계속 지닌다면 살아생전에 그것을 뉘우치게 되리라 믿고, 또 그가 그 다이아몬드를 남에게 넘긴다면 그것을 받은 다른 이들이 살아생전에 그것을 뉘우치게 되리라 믿을 만큼 공상에 빠져 있기까지 하다.\n\n여기 실린 사건들은, 줄리아 곧 레이디 버린더를 섬기는 집사(執事) 게이브리얼 베터리지가 이야기한 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n「로빈슨 크루소」제1부 백스물아홉 쪽을 펼치면, 여러분은 이렇게 적힌 대목을 보게 될 것이다.\n\n\"이제야 나는, 너무 늦게나마 깨달았다. 치를 대가를 미리 헤아려 보지도 않고, 그 일을 끝까지 해낼 제 힘을 옳게 가늠해 보지도 않은 채 일을 벌이는 것이 얼마나 어리석은 짓인가를.\"\n\n바로 어제, 나는 내 「로빈슨 크루소」를 하필 그 대목에서 펼쳤다. 그리고 바로 오늘 아침(1850년 5월 스무하룻날), 마님의 조카이신 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이 오셔서, 나와 이런 짤막한 이야기를 나누셨다.\n\n\"베터리지,\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말씀하셨다. \"집안일로 변호사한테 다녀오는 길인데, 이런저런 얘기 끝에 이 년 전 요크셔의 이모님 댁에서 인도 다이아몬드를 잃어버린 일까지 나왔다네. 브러프 씨도 나와 같은 생각이야—진실을 위해서라도 그 이야기를 죄다 글로 적어 남겨 두어야 한다는 거지. 그것도 이를수록 좋고.\"\n\n아직 그 속뜻을 알아채지 못한 데다, 무릇 조용하고 편안하게 지내자면 변호사 편을 드는 게 상책이라 여겨, 나도 그렇게 생각한다고 대답했다. 프랭클린 도련님은 말을 이으셨다.\n\n\"이 다이아몬드 일로 말하자면,\" 그가 말씀하셨다. \"자네도 알다시피, 죄 없는 사람들이 벌써 의심을 받아 명예가 상했네. 앞으로도, 뒷사람들이 기댈 만한 사실의 기록이 없어서 죄 없는 사람들의 기억이 욕을 볼지도 몰라. 우리 집안의 이 야릇한 이야기를 마땅히 남겨 두어야 한다는 건 두말할 나위가 없지. 그런데 베터리지, 브러프 씨와 나 둘이서 그걸 남기는 옳은 방법을 찾아낸 것 같단 말이야.\"\n\n두 분한테야 더할 나위 없이 흡족한 방법이었겠지. 하지만 나로서는, 여태 그게 나하고 무슨 상관이 있는지 도무지 알 수가 없었다.\n\n\"우리한테는 이야기해야 할 사건들이 있고,\" 프랭클린 도련님이 계속하셨다. \"그 사건들에 얽힌, 그것을 이야기할 만한 사람들도 있지. 이 뻔한 사실에서 출발해서, 우리 모두가 돌아가며 월장석 이야기를 쓰자는 거야—저마다 제가 몸소 겪은 데까지만, 그 이상은 말고. 우선 오십 년 전 헌캐슬 외삼촌이 인도에서 복무할 적에 그 다이아몬드가 어떻게 처음 그분 손에 들어왔는지부터 보여야 해. 이 머리말 격의 이야기는 내가 이미 오래된 가문 문서 형태로 갖고 있네—목격자의 증언에 기대어 필요한 사정을 죄다 적어 둔 것이지. 그다음 할 일은, 이 년 전 그 다이아몬드가 어떻게 요크셔의 이모님 댁으로 흘러들었고, 그러고 나서 열두 시간 남짓 만에 어떻게 없어졌는지를 이야기하는 거야. 그때 그 집 안에서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 자네만큼 잘 아는 사람이 없어, 베터리지. 그러니 자네가 펜을 들고 이야기의 첫머리를 떼야겠네.\"\n\n이런 식으로 나는 그 다이아몬드 일에 내가 무슨 관계가 있는지를 통고받은 셈이었다. 그런 처지에서 내가 어떻게 처신했는지 궁금하시다면, 여러분이 내 자리에 있었어도 아마 그리했을 법한 그대로 했다고 아뢰겠다. 나는 겸손하게, 나한테 지워진 그 일을 감당할 재간이 도무지 없노라 잡아뗐다—그러면서도 속으로는 내내, 내 능력에 한 번 공평한 기회만 준다면 그쯤이야 넉넉히 해낼 만큼 영리하다고 여기고 있었다. 짐작건대 프랭클린 도련님은 내 속마음을 얼굴에서 읽으셨던 모양이다. 그분은 내 겸손을 곧이듣기를 마다하시고, 기어이 내 능력에 공평한 기회를 주겠노라 우기셨다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님이 나를 떠나신 지 두 시간이 흘렀다. 그분이 등을 돌리기가 무섭게, 나는 이야기를 시작하려고 책상 앞에 앉았다. 그러고는 (내 능력이 무색하게도) 여태껏 속수무책으로 앉아만 있다. 앞에서 옮긴 대로, 로빈슨 크루소가 깨친 그것—곧 치를 대가를 미리 헤아려 보지도 않고, 그 일을 끝까지 해낼 제 힘을 옳게 가늠해 보지도 않은 채 일을 벌이는 어리석음을 나 역시 눈앞에 보면서 말이다. 부디 잊지 마시라, 내가 지금 벌인 이 일을 경솔하게 떠맡기 바로 전날, 나는 우연히 그 책을 하필 그 대목에서 펼쳤던 것이다. 그러니 감히 여쭙거니와—그게 예언이 아니라면, 대체 무엇이 예언이란 말인가?\n\n나는 미신을 믿는 사람이 아니다. 살아오면서 책도 산더미같이 읽었고, 내 나름으로는 학식깨나 갖춘 사람이다. 일흔을 넘겼어도 기억력은 여태 팔팔하고, 다리도 그에 걸맞게 성하다. 그러니 「로빈슨 크루소」같은 책은 여태 쓰인 적도 없고 앞으로도 다시는 쓰이지 못하리라는 게 내 소견이라 하여, 부디 무식쟁이의 헛소리로 듣지는 마시라. 나는 여러 해 동안 그 책을 시험 삼아 읽어 왔는데—대개는 담배 한 대와 곁들여서였다—이 덧없는 인생의 온갖 고비마다 그 책이 곤경 속의 벗임을 알았다. 심사가 사나울 때면—「로빈슨 크루소」. 조언이 아쉬울 때면—「로빈슨 크루소」. 지난날 아내가 나를 들볶을 때에도, 요즈음 술이 한 잔 과했을 때에도—「로빈슨 크루소」. 나는 여태 부려 온 세월 동안 튼튼한 「로빈슨 크루소」를 여섯 권이나 닳도록 읽어 없앴다. 지난번 마님 생신에 마님은 내게 일곱 번째 것을 주셨다. 나는 그게 반가워 술을 한 잔 과하게 걸쳤고—그러자 「로빈슨 크루소」가 또다시 나를 바로잡아 주었다. 값은 네 실링 육 펜스, 파란 표지에, 덤으로 그림 한 장까지 딸린 책이다.\n\n그건 그렇고, 이래서야 다이아몬드 이야기를 시작하는 꼴이 영 아니지 않은가? 나는 어디서 무엇을 찾겠다는 건지 저도 모르게 자꾸 딴 데로 새고 있는 성싶다. 자, 괜찮으시다면 새 종이를 한 장 꺼내 들고, 여러분께 삼가 예를 갖추어 처음부터 다시 시작하기로 하자.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n두어 줄 앞에서 나는 마님 이야기를 꺼냈다. 그런데 그 다이아몬드가, 잃어버린 그 장소인 우리 집에 있게 된 것은, 애초에 그것이 마님의 따님에게 선물로 주어진 덕분이요, 마님의 따님이 그 선물을 받으려고 세상에 나와 있게 된 것은, (산고와 진통을 겪어 가며) 그분을 이 세상에 낳아 주신 마님이 계셨던 덕분이다. 그러니 마님에서부터 시작한다면, 우리는 넉넉히 거슬러 올라간 데서 시작하는 셈이 틀림없다. 그리고 말이지, 나 같은 일감을 손에 쥐어 보면 알겠지만, 그게 바로 첫머리를 떼는 데 여간 큰 위안이 아니다.\n\n상류 사교계를 조금이라도 아신다면, 여러분은 아름다운 헌캐슬 세 자매 아가씨에 관해 들어 보셨을 것이다. 애덜레이드 아가씨, 캐럴라인 아가씨, 그리고 줄리아 아가씨—이 막내가 세 자매 가운데 가장 어리면서도, 내 소견으로는 가장 훌륭했다. 그리고 그 점을 가늠할 기회가 내게 있었음은, 곧 보시게 될 것이다. 나는 그분들의 부친인 노(老)영주 밑에서 일하게 되었는데(다행히도 이 다이아몬드 사건에서 그분과 우리는 아무 상관이 없다. 그분으로 말할 것 같으면, 지체가 높든 낮든 내가 여태 만나 본 어느 누구보다도 혀가 길고 성미가 짧은 양반이었다)—아무튼, 나는 열다섯 나이에, 그 세 귀하신 아가씨들의 시중을 드는 시동(侍童)으로 노영주 밑에 들어갔다. 그러고는 줄리아 아가씨가 지금은 고인이 되신 존 버린더 경과 혼인하실 때까지 그곳에서 지냈다. 존 경은 훌륭한 분이었으되, 다만 자기를 다스려 줄 누군가가 있어야 할 분이었다. 그리고 우리끼리 하는 말이지만, 그분은 그 누군가를 찾아내셨다. 뿐만 아니라 그분은 그 덕에 잘되고 그 덕에 살이 올랐으며, 마님이 그분을 성당으로 데려가 혼인하시던 날부터, 마님이 그분의 마지막 숨을 거두어 주고 그 눈을 영영 감겨 드리던 날까지, 그 덕에 행복하게 살다 편안하게 돌아가셨다.\n\n내가 신부를 따라, 신랑 되는 분의 이 아랫녘 저택과 땅으로 함께 내려왔다는 이야기를 빼먹었다. \"존 경,\" 아가씨가 말씀하셨다. \"저는 게이브리얼 베터리지 없이는 못 살아요.\" \"마님,\" 존 경이 말씀하셨다. \"나도 저 사람 없이는 못 살겠소.\" 그것이 아가씨를 대하는 그분의 방식이었다—그리고 그렇게 해서 나는 그분 밑에서 일하게 되었다. 마님과 나만 함께 있을 수 있다면, 내가 어디로 가든 내게는 매한가지였다.\n\n마님이 바깥일이며 농장이며 그런 일들에 관심을 두시는 것을 보고, 나도 덩달아 관심을 두게 되었다—더구나 나 자신이 자그마한 소작농의 일곱째 아들이었으니 관심을 둘 까닭이야 충분했다. 마님은 나를 마름 밑에 딸리게 해 주셨고, 나는 힘껏 애를 써서 만족을 드렸으며, 그에 따라 승진도 했다. 몇 해 뒤, 이를테면 어느 월요일에, 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"존 경, 당신네 마름은 어리석은 늙은이예요. 후하게 연금을 주어 내보내고, 게이브리얼 베터리지에게 그 자리를 주도록 하세요.\" 이를테면 그 화요일에, 존 경이 말씀하셨다. \"마님, 마름은 후하게 연금을 받아 물러났고, 게이브리얼 베터리지가 그 자리를 차지했소.\" 부부가 함께 살며 서로 못 잡아먹어 안달인 이야기라면 여러분도 지겹도록 들으셨을 것이다. 여기 그와는 정반대의 본보기가 있다. 이것을 어떤 이에게는 경계로, 또 어떤 이에게는 격려로 삼으시기 바란다. 그동안 나는 내 이야기나 계속하겠다.\n\n자, 그쯤 되면 팔자가 늘어졌구나, 여러분은 그렇게 말씀하실 것이다. 믿음직하고 명예로운 자리에 앉아, 몸 뉠 조그만 오두막을 하나 얻고, 아침이면 영지를 도는 순찰로, 오후면 장부 정리로, 저녁이면 담뱃대와 「로빈슨 크루소」로 소일하니—대체 무엇이 더 있어야 내가 행복해지겠는가? 아담이 에덴동산에 홀로 있을 적에 무엇을 아쉬워했는지 떠올려 보시라. 그것을 두고 아담을 나무라지 않으신다면, 나 또한 나무라지 마시라.\n\n내가 눈독을 들인 여자는, 내 오두막에서 살림을 돌봐 주던 여자였다. 이름은 셀리나 고비였다. 아내를 고르는 일에 관해서라면 나는 지금은 고인이 된 윌리엄 코빗의 말에 동의한다. 음식을 잘 씹어 먹는지, 걸을 때 땅을 야무지게 딛는지만 보면 그것으로 됐다는 것이다. 셀리나 고비는 그 두 가지 점에서 다 나무랄 데가 없었으니, 그것이 그녀와 혼인한 한 가지 까닭이었다. 나한테는 또 다른 까닭도 있었는데, 이건 순전히 나 혼자 궁리해 낸 것이었다. 셀리나가 처녀로 있을 때는, 그녀의 식비며 품삯이며 하여 내가 그녀에게 주급을 얼마씩 치러야 했다. 그런데 셀리나가 내 아내가 되면, 식비를 청구할 수도 없거니와, 품도 공짜로 내게 바쳐야 할 터였다. 내가 그 일을 바라본 관점이 바로 그러했다. 절약—거기에 사랑도 한 자밤. 나는 마땅히 그래야 할 도리대로, 나 자신에게 내놓았던 그대로 그 이야기를 마님께도 아뢰었다.\n\n\"셀리나 고비 문제를 곰곰이 굴려 보았습니다만,\" 하고 나는 아뢰었다. \"마님, 아무래도 그 여자를 데리고 사는 것보다 아예 혼인하는 편이 싸게 먹힐 성싶습니다.\"\n\n마님은 웃음을 터뜨리시더니, 내 말투에 더 놀라야 할지 내 소신에 더 놀라야 할지 모르겠다고 하셨다. 짐작건대, 지체 높은 분이 아니고서는 알아듣지 못할 종류의 어떤 우스갯거리가 마님의 심사를 간질였던 모양이다. 나로 말하면, 이제 셀리나한테 그 말을 꺼내도 좋다는 것 말고는 아무것도 알아듣지 못한 채, 냉큼 가서 그대로 말을 꺼냈다. 그래서 셀리나가 뭐라고 했느냐고? 원, 그런 걸 물으시다니, 여러분은 여자에 대해 어지간히도 모르시는구려. 물론 그녀는 \"네\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n그날이 가까워 오고, 예식에 입을 새 외투를 마련해야 한다는 이야기가 나오자, 내 마음에 슬며시 불안이 일기 시작했다. 나는 나와 같은 흥미로운 처지에 놓였던 다른 사내들과 심경을 견주어 본 적이 있는데, 그들은 하나같이, 그 일이 있기 한 주 전쯤에는 속으로 이 일에서 발을 빼고 싶어 했노라 실토했다. 나 자신은 거기서 한 발짝 더 나아갔다. 나는 실제로, 이를테면 벌떡 일어나서, 이 일에서 빠져나오려고 애를 썼다. 물론 거저는 아니었다! 나는 그녀가 나를 거저 놓아주리라 기대할 만큼 뻔뻔한 사람은 못 되었다. 사내가 발을 뺄 적에 여자에게 위자료를 물어 주는 것은, 영국 법도의 하나다. 그 법도를 좇아, 그리고 마음속으로 신중히 저울질한 끝에, 나는 셀리나 고비에게 이 혼약을 무르는 대가로 깃털 이불 하나와 오십 실링을 내놓았다. 여러분은 좀처럼 믿지 못하시겠지만, 그래도 이것은 엄연한 사실이다—그녀는 어리석게도 그것을 마다했다.\n\n그다음엔, 물론, 나는 만사 끝장이었다. 나는 새 외투를 되도록 싸게 장만했고, 나머지 일도 죄다 되도록 싸게 치렀다. 우리는 행복한 부부도 아니요, 불행한 부부도 아니었다. 이것이나 저것이나 그게 그거인, 도토리 키 재기였다. 어찌 된 노릇인지 나도 모르겠으나, 우리는 더없이 좋은 뜻을 품고서도 늘 서로의 앞길을 가로막기 일쑤였다. 내가 위층으로 올라가려 하면 아내가 내려오고, 아내가 아래층으로 내려가려 하면 내가 올라가는 식이었다. 내 겪어 본 바로는, 그게 바로 부부살이라는 것이다.\n\n계단에서 오 년 동안 엇갈린 끝에, 전지(全知)하신 섭리께서 기꺼이 내 아내를 거두어 가심으로써 우리를 서로에게서 놓아주셨다. 내게는 어린 딸 페넬로피 하나가 남고, 다른 자식은 없었다. 그 얼마 뒤 존 경이 돌아가시니, 마님께는 어린 따님 레이철 아가씨 하나가 남고, 다른 자식은 없었다. 우리 어진 마님의 손수 보살핌 아래 내 어린 페넬로피가 자랐고, 학교에 보내져 배웠고, 야무진 처녀로 컸으며, 나이가 차자 레이철 아가씨의 몸종으로 발탁되었다는 것을—여러분께 굳이 일러 드려야 한다면, 나는 마님에 관해 참으로 형편없이 적어 온 셈이 될 것이다.\n\n나로 말하면, 해가 바뀌고 또 바뀌도록 마름 일을 이어 가다가, 1847년 성탄절에 이르러 내 삶에 한 변화가 찾아왔다. 그날, 마님이 몸소 내 오두막을 찾아 나와 단둘이 차 한 잔을 청하셨다. 마님은, 노영주 시절에 내가 시동으로 첫발을 디딘 그해부터 헤아리면, 내가 오십 년 넘도록 당신을 섬겨 왔노라 이르시고는, 살을 에는 겨울 추위에 나를 따뜻이 감싸라고, 손수 짜신 아름다운 양모 조끼를 내 손에 쥐여 주셨다.\n\n나는 이 훌륭한 선물을 받고서, 마님이 베풀어 주신 그 영광에 무슨 말로 감사를 올려야 할지 몰라 그저 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다. 그런데 놀랍게도, 알고 보니 그 조끼는 영광이 아니라 뇌물이었다. 마님은 내가 미처 깨닫기도 전에 내가 늙어 간다는 것을 알아채셨고, (이런 표현을 써도 좋다면) 나를 살살 구슬려, 고된 바깥일인 마름 노릇을 그만두고 남은 날들일랑 저택의 집사로서 편히 지내게 하시려고 내 오두막을 찾아오셨던 것이다. 나는 편히 지내야 한다는 그 치욕에 맞서 있는 힘껏 버텨 보았다. 하지만 마님은 내 약한 구석을 아셨으니, 그것을 당신을 위한 청으로 돌려 말씀하셨다. 그렇게 해서 우리 사이의 실랑이는, 내가 늙은 바보처럼 새 양모 조끼로 눈물을 훔치며 한번 생각해 보겠노라 말하는 것으로 끝이 났다.\n\n마님이 돌아가신 뒤, 그 일을 생각해 본다는 것을 두고 내 마음이 심란하기가 이루 말할 수 없이 끔찍했으므로, 나는 의혹과 위급의 순간마다 여태 한 번도 나를 저버린 적이 없는 그 처방을 썼다. 담뱃대를 한 대 피우고 「로빈슨 크루소」를 한 대목 들춘 것이다. 그 비범한 책에 파묻힌 지 오 분도 채 지나지 않아, 나는 마음을 달래 주는 한 구절(백쉰여덟 쪽)을 만났으니, 이러하였다. \"오늘 우리가 사랑하는 것을, 내일 우리는 미워한다.\" 나는 대뜸 갈 길이 훤히 보였다. 오늘의 나는 한사코 농장 마름으로 남으려 한다. 그러나 내일의 나는, 「로빈슨 크루소」의 권위를 빌리자면, 정반대로 마음이 기울 터였다. 내일 내일의 기분에 잠겼을 때의 나를 붙잡으면, 일은 다 된 것이었다. 이렇게 마음을 놓은 나는, 그날 밤 레이디 버린더의 농장 마름이라는 신분으로 잠이 들었고, 이튿날 아침 레이디 버린더의 집사라는 신분으로 잠에서 깨었다. 만사가 아주 편안하게, 그것도 죄다 「로빈슨 크루소」 덕분에!\n\n방금 내 딸 페넬로피가 내 어깨너머로 여태 내가 써 놓은 것을 들여다보았다. 이 애 말이, 글이 참으로 곱게 쓰였고 한 마디 한 마디가 죄다 사실이라고 한다. 그러면서도 한 가지 트집을 잡는다. 여태 내가 써 놓은 것이, 나한테 시킨 일과는 전혀 딴판이라는 것이다. 나는 다이아몬드 이야기를 하라고 부탁받았는데, 그 대신 내 신세타령이나 늘어놓고 있었다는 게다. 희한한 노릇이요, 나로서는 도무지 까닭을 알 길이 없다. 책 쓰는 것으로 밥벌이하고 생업을 삼는 양반들도, 나처럼 저 자신이 제 주제(主題)를 가로막고 나서는 일을 겪는 적이 있을까 궁금하다. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 그분들의 심정을 헤아릴 수 있겠다. 그건 그렇고, 여기 또 한 번의 헛발질과, 아까운 원고지 낭비가 있다. 이제 어쩐다? 여러분은 부디 성을 삭이시고, 나는 세 번째로 처음부터 모조리 다시 시작하는 수밖에, 내가 아는 도리로는 달리 방도가 없다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n이야기를 제대로 시작하려면 어떻게 해야 하나, 이 문제를 나는 두 가지 방법으로 풀어 보려 했다. 첫째는 머리통을 긁적이는 것이었는데, 이것은 아무 소득이 없었다. 둘째는 내 딸 페넬로피와 의논하는 것이었는데, 이것이 아주 새로운 생각으로 열매를 맺었다.\n\n페넬로피의 생각인즉, 일어난 일을 날마다 차례차례 적어 나가되, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 우리 집에 오시기로 되어 있다는 소식을 받은 그날부터 시작하라는 것이다. 이런 식으로 날짜를 딱 박아 기억을 붙들어 매 놓으면, 그 날짜에 걸려 기억이 무엇무엇을 끌어올려 주는지 참으로 놀라울 지경이다. 다만 한 가지 어려움은, 애당초 그 날짜들을 캐내는 일이다. 이 일을 페넬로피가 제 일기장을 들춰 대신해 주겠다고 나선다. 그 애는 학교에 다닐 적에 일기 쓰는 법을 배웠고, 그때부터 지금껏 줄곧 써 오고 있다. 이 생각을 한층 더 낫게 손질한답시고, 나 스스로—그러니까 나 대신 그 애가 제 일기장을 밑천 삼아 이야기를 하면 어떻겠느냐고—궁리해 내놓았더니, 페넬로피는 눈을 사납게 치뜨고 얼굴을 붉히며 하는 말이, 제 일기는 저 혼자 보려고 쓴 것이요 그 안에 무엇이 적혔는지는 살아 있는 그 어떤 것도 오직 저 말고는 결코 알지 못하리라는 것이다. 그게 무슨 소리냐고 내가 물으니, 페넬로피는 \"쓸데없는 소리!\" 한다. 나는 \"아이고, 요 귀여운 것.\" 한다.\n\n그러면, 페넬로피의 방식대로 시작하기로 하고, 나는 어느 수요일 아침에 특별히 마님의 거실로 불려 들어갔음을 아뢰는 바이다. 날짜는 1848년 5월 24일이었다.\n\n\"게이브리얼.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"자네가 놀랄 만한 소식이 있네. 프랭클린 블레이크가 외국에서 돌아왔어. 런던에서 제 아버지와 함께 지내다가, 내일 우리한테 와서 다음 달까지 묵으며 레이철의 생일을 함께 쇨 참이라네.\"\n\n만일 그때 내 손에 모자가 들려 있었던들, 오직 예의 하나가 나를 말렸을 뿐, 나는 그 모자를 천장까지 냅다 던져 올렸을 것이다. 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 소년이던 시절, 우리와 함께 이 집에서 살던 그 뒤로 그분을 뵌 적이 없었다. 내 기억으로 그분은, 팽이를 돌리거나 유리창을 깨뜨린 어느 사내아이보다도 단연코 가장 사랑스러운 아이였다. 마침 그 자리에 있던 레이철 아가씨는, 내가 그 말을 건네자 되받아 이르시기를, 아가씨 당신은 그분을, 인형을 못살게 군 것으로 치면 세상에 둘도 없는 흉악한 폭군이요 노끈 마구를 씌워 지쳐 빠진 어린 계집아이를 몰아댄 것으로 치면 온 영국이 낳을 수 있는 가장 모진 마부로 기억한다고 하셨다. \"나는 분해서 몸이 타오르고, 고단해서 삭신이 쑤신다니까.\" 레이철 아가씨는 프랭클린 블레이크를 떠올릴 때의 심경을 그렇게 요약하셨다.\n\n지금 내가 하는 이야기를 듣고서, 여러분은 자연히 물으실 것이다. 프랭클린 도련님이 소년이던 때부터 어른이 된 때까지 그 세월을 어찌하여 죄다 제 나라 밖에서 보내게 되었느냐고. 나는 이렇게 답하겠다. 그분의 부친께서 공작 작위의 다음 상속자가 되고서도 그것을 증명해 내지 못하는 불운을 겪으셨기 때문이라고.\n\n두 마디로 하자면, 일은 이렇게 된 것이다.\n\n마님의 맏언니께서 그 유명한 블레이크 씨와 혼인하셨다—어마어마한 재산으로도 유명하고 어마어마한 소송으로도 똑같이 유명한 분이었다. 그분이 작위를 차지하고 있는 공작을 몰아내고 그 자리에 자기가 들어앉겠노라며 나라의 법정들을 몇 해나 들볶았는지, 변호사들의 주머니를 몇 개나 터지도록 채워 주었는지, 그리고 그분이 옳으니 그르니 다투게 만들어 서로 멱살을 잡게 한 애먼 사람들이 몇이나 되는지는—내가 헤아릴 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많다. 법정이 마침내 그분을 문밖으로 내치고 그 돈을 더는 받지 않기로 마음을 정하기도 전에, 그분의 아내가 죽고 세 자녀 가운데 둘이 죽었다. 만사가 다 끝나 작위를 차지한 공작이 그대로 작위를 지니게 되자, 블레이크 씨는 자기를 그런 식으로 대접한 조국에 앙갚음하는 유일한 길이, 조국으로 하여금 제 아들을 교육하는 영예를 누리지 못하게 하는 것임을 깨달았다. \"내 나라의 제도가 나한테 이 따위로 굴었는데, 내가 어찌 그것을 믿을 수 있겠는가?\" 그분은 그런 식으로 말했다. 여기에다, 블레이크 씨가 제 자식을 포함해 사내아이라면 죄다 질색했다는 점까지 보태면, 여러분도 일이 한 가지 결말로밖에 끝날 수 없었음을 인정하시리라. 프랭클린 도련님은 영국의 우리 곁에서 떨어져 나가, 그 잘난 나라 독일, 즉 부친께서 믿을 수 있는 제도들이 있는 곳으로 보내졌다. 정작 블레이크 씨 본인은, 보시다시피, 영국에 아늑히 눌러앉아 의사당에서 동포들을 계도하고 작위를 차지한 공작에 관한 성명서를 펴내는 일에 몰두하셨으니, 그 성명서는 그날 이후 오늘날까지 미완성인 채로 남아 있다.\n\n자! 하느님 감사합니다, 그 이야기는 다 했다! 여러분도 나도 이제 블레이크 씨, 그 어른에 관해서는 더 골머리를 앓을 것이 없다. 그분은 공작 작위에나 맡겨 두고, 여러분과 나는 다이아몬드에나 매달리기로 하자.\n\n다이아몬드 이야기가 나오니 다시 프랭클린 도련님께로 돌아가게 되는데, 그 불길한 보석을 이 집으로 들여온 죄 없는 장본인이 바로 그분이었다.\n\n우리 착한 도련님은 외국으로 떠난 뒤에도 우리를 잊지 않았다. 그분은 이따금 편지를 보내오셨다. 어떤 때는 마님께, 어떤 때는 레이철 아가씨께, 또 어떤 때는 내게. 그분이 떠나기 전에 우리 사이에는 거래가 하나 있었으니, 그분이 내게서 노끈 한 뭉치와 날이 넷 달린 칼 한 자루, 그리고 돈 칠 실링 육 펜스를 꾸어 간 것이었다—그 돈으로 말할 것 같으면, 나는 그 빛깔을 여태 다시 본 적이 없고 앞으로도 다시 보리라 기대하지 않는다. 내게 오는 그분의 편지는 주로 더 꾸어 달라는 내용이었다. 그렇지만 그분이 나이와 키가 자라 감에 따라 외국에서 어찌 지내는지는 마님께 전해 들었다. 그분은 독일의 제도가 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 것을 다 배우고 나서, 다음으로는 프랑스를 한 차례 겪고, 그다음으로는 이탈리아를 한 차례 겪었다. 내가 이해한 바로는, 그 나라들이 그분을 이를테면 만능 천재 비슷한 것으로 만들어 놓은 모양이었다. 그분은 글도 좀 쓰고, 그림도 좀 그리고, 노래도 좀 하고 연주도 좀 하고 작곡도 좀 했다—짐작건대 이 모든 경우에도 나한테서 꾸어 갔듯 여기저기서 꾸어다 한 것이렷다. 그분이 성년이 되자 어머니의 유산(연 700파운드)이 그분 몫으로 떨어졌는데, 그것은 마치 체를 빠져나가듯 그분을 술술 빠져나갔다. 돈이 많아질수록 그분은 더 많이 바랐다. 프랭클린 도련님의 주머니에는 무엇으로도 기워지지 않는 구멍이 하나 뚫려 있었던 것이다. 어디를 가든 그분의 발랄하고 스스럼없는 됨됨이가 그분을 반갑게 만들었다. 그분은 여기저기, 어디에서나 살았다. 그분의 주소는 (본인이 즐겨 이르던 대로 하면) \"유럽 우체국—찾으러 올 때까지 보관 요망\"이었다. 두 차례나 그분은 영국으로 돌아와 우리를 보러 오리라 마음먹었으나, 두 차례 다 (외람된 말씀이오나) 입에 올리지 못할 어떤 여자가 앞을 가로막아 그분을 붙들어 세웠다. 세 번째 시도가 성공했다는 것은, 마님이 내게 이르신 바로 여러분도 이미 아시는 대로다. 5월 25일 목요일에, 우리는 우리 착한 도련님이 어른이 되어 어떤 모습으로 자랐는지 처음으로 보게 될 참이었다. 그분은 좋은 혈통을 타고났고, 드높은 용기를 지녔으며, 우리 셈으로 스물다섯 살이었다. 자, 이제 여러분은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에 관해, 그분이 우리 집에 내려오기 전의 내가 알던 만큼은 아시게 된 셈이다.\n\n그 목요일은 여러분이 여태 본 그 어떤 날 못지않게 화창한 여름날이었다. 그리고 마님과 레이철 아가씨는 (프랭클린 도련님이 저녁 식사 때나 되어야 오시리라 여기고서) 이웃에 사는 벗들과 점심을 들러 마차를 몰고 나가셨다.\n\n두 분이 떠나신 뒤, 나는 손님을 맞으려고 채비해 둔 침실을 둘러보러 가서, 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있는지 살폈다. 그러고 나서, 마님 댁에서 마름일 뿐 아니라 집사 노릇도 겸하고 있던 터라—이건 (똑똑히 새겨 두시라) 순전히 내 특별한 청에 따른 것으로, 지금은 고인이 되신 존 경의 술 창고 열쇠를 나 아닌 다른 누가 쥐고 있는 꼴을 보면 심사가 뒤틀렸기 때문이다—아무튼 내 말이지, 나는 우리 집의 그 유명한 라투르 클라레 포도주를 몇 병 꺼내 와, 저녁 식사 전에 냉기를 가시게 하려고 따뜻한 여름 공기 속에 놓아두었다. 오래 묵은 클라레에 좋은 것은 늙은이한테도 매한가지로 좋으렷다 싶어, 다음은 나 자신을 그 따뜻한 여름 공기 속에 놓아두기로 하고, 뒤뜰로 나가려 벌집 모양 등받이 의자를 집어 드는데, 마님 저택 앞 테라스에서 마치 북을 나직이 두드리는 듯한 소리가 들려 나는 멈칫 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n테라스 쪽으로 돌아가 보니, 흰 아마포 웃옷과 바지를 걸친 마호가니 빛 살갗의 인도인 셋이 우리 집을 올려다보고 서 있었다.\n\n가까이서 보니 그 인도인들은 저마다 조그만 손북을 앞에 매달고 있었다. 그들 뒤에는 가방을 든, 여리여리하고 살결이 고운 금발의 영국 소년 하나가 서 있었다. 나는 이 사내들을 떠돌이 요술쟁이로, 가방을 든 그 소년을 그들 장사 밑천을 나르는 아이로 짐작했다. 셋 가운데 영어를 하는 한 명은, 실토하건대 여간 우아한 태도를 지닌 자였는데, 이내 내 짐작이 옳았음을 일러 주었다. 그는 이 집 마님 앞에서 자기 재주를 보여 드릴 수 있게 허락해 달라고 청했다.\n\n자, 나는 심술궂은 늙은이가 아니다. 나는 웬만하면 다 흥겨운 것을 좋아하는 사람이요, 누군가가 나보다 살빛이 몇 겹 검다는 까닭만으로 그 사람을 못 믿을 인간은 결코 아니다. 하지만 우리 중 아무리 잘난 사람에게도 약점은 있는 법이라—내 약점인즉, 집안의 은 접시 바구니가 식기실 탁자에 나와 있는 줄 알고 있을 적에, 나보다 태도가 점잖은 떠돌이 낯선 자를 보기만 하면 그 바구니가 대뜸 떠오른다는 것이다. 그리하여 나는 그 인도인에게 이 집 마님은 출타 중이라 이르고, 그와 그 일행을 이 집 안에서 나가라 경고했다. 그는 답례로 내게 아름다운 절을 한 번 하더니, 자기 일행과 함께 집 밖으로 나갔다. 나는 나대로 벌집 모양 의자로 돌아가 뜰의 양지바른 쪽에 자리를 잡고 앉아, (사실대로 털어놓자면) 잠이라고까지는 할 수 없으되 그다음으로 좋은 상태에 빠져들었다.\n\n나를 흔들어 깨운 것은, 집에 불이라도 난 듯 내게로 뛰쳐나온 내 딸 페넬로피였다. 이 애가 무엇을 바랐는지 여러분은 아시겠는가? 이 애는 그 인도인 요술쟁이 셋을 당장 붙잡아 가두기를 바랐다. 그 까닭인즉, 그자들이 런던에서 누가 우리를 찾아오는지를 알고 있으며 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 무슨 해코지를 하려 든다는 것이었다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님의 이름에 나는 정신이 번쩍 들었다. 나는 눈을 뜨고, 내 딸에게 무슨 소린지 똑똑히 말해 보라 했다.\n\n알고 보니 페넬로피는 방금 우리 문지기 오두막에서 오는 길이었는데, 거기서 문지기 딸과 수다를 떨고 있었더란다. 두 처녀는, 내가 인도인들을 쫓아낸 뒤에 그자들이 어린 소년을 뒤딸린 채 나가는 것을 보았다. 그 소년이 외국인들에게 학대받고 있다고 지레짐작하고—내가 알아낼 수 있는 까닭이라곤 그저 그 아이가 곱상하고 여리여리해 보인다는 것뿐이었지만—두 처녀는 우리 집과 한길 사이의 산울타리 안쪽을 따라 몰래 다가가서는, 그 바깥쪽에서 외국인들이 하는 짓을 지켜보았다. 그자들이 한 짓이란 다음과 같은 괴상한 재주로 이어졌다.\n\n그자들은 먼저 한길 위쪽을 보고 아래쪽을 보며, 자기들뿐임을 확인했다. 그러고는 셋이 다 몸을 돌려, 우리 집 쪽을 뚫어져라 노려보았다. 그러고는 저희끼리 저희 말로 지껄이며 옥신각신하더니, 갈피를 못 잡는 사람들처럼 서로를 쳐다보았다. 그러고는 셋이 다 저희 어린 영국 소년에게로 돌아서서, 마치 그 아이가 저희를 도와주기를 바라는 듯했다. 그러더니 영어를 하는 우두머리 인도인이 소년에게 말했다. \"손을 내밀어라.\"\n\n그 무시무시한 말을 듣는 순간, 내 딸 페넬로피는 제 심장이 곧장 몸 밖으로 튀어나가지 않은 게 무슨 조화인지 모르겠더란다. 나는 속으로, 그거야 그 애가 조인 코르셋 덕이었으려니 했다. 그러나 내가 입 밖에 낸 말은 그저 \"네 말을 들으니 소름이 다 끼치는구나.\"뿐이었다. (참고삼아 이르는 말이오나, 여자들은 이런 자잘한 치사를 좋아한다.)\n\n자, 그 인도인이 \"손을 내밀어라.\" 하자, 소년은 움찔 뒤로 물러나 고개를 젓고는 그게 싫다고 했다. 그러자 인도인은 (조금도 모질지 않게) 아이에게 물었다. 다시 런던으로 돌려보내져, 저희가 처음 아이를 주워 왔던 곳, 그러니까 저잣거리의 빈 바구니 속에서 잠자던 그곳, 굶주리고 누더기를 걸친 채 버림받은 어린아이로 되돌아가고 싶으냐고. 이것으로 실랑이는 끝난 모양이었다. 어린 녀석은 마지못해 손을 내밀었다. 그러자 인도인은 품에서 병을 하나 꺼내, 그 속에서 잉크 같은 검은 것을 소년의 손바닥에 부었다. 인도인은—먼저 소년의 머리에 손을 대고 그 위 허공에다 무슨 표시를 그리더니—이윽고 말했다. \"보아라.\" 소년은 아주 뻣뻣하게 굳어, 조각상처럼 서서 제 손바닥 오목한 곳의 잉크를 들여다보았다.\n\n(여기까지는, 내가 보기에 어리석게 잉크나 축내는 요술로만 여겨졌다. 나는 다시 슬슬 졸음이 오려는 참이었는데, 페넬로피의 다음 말이 나를 흔들어 깨웠다.)\n\n인도인들은 한 번 더 한길 위쪽을 보고 아래쪽을 보았다—그러고는 우두머리 인도인이 소년에게 이런 말을 했다. \"외국에서 오는 그 영국 신사를 보아라.\"\n\n소년이 말했다. \"보입니다.\"\n\n인도인이 말했다. \"그 영국 신사가 오늘 여행할 길이, 다른 어느 길도 아닌 바로 이 집으로 오는 길이더냐?\"\n\n소년이 말했다. \"그 영국 신사가 오늘 여행할 길은, 다른 어느 길도 아닌 바로 이 집으로 오는 길입니다.\"\n\n인도인은—먼저 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤에—두 번째 물음을 던졌다. 그가 말했다. \"그 영국 신사가 그것을 몸에 지니고 있더냐?\"\n\n소년은—역시 먼저 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤에—대답했다. \"예.\"\n\n인도인이 세 번째이자 마지막 물음을 던졌다. \"그 영국 신사가, 약속한 대로, 해 질 무렵에 이리로 오겠더냐?\"\n\n소년이 말했다. \"그건 모르겠습니다.\"\n\n인도인이 까닭을 물었다.\n\n소년이 말했다. \"저는 지쳤습니다. 머릿속에 안개가 피어올라 저를 어지럽힙니다. 오늘은 더 볼 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n그것으로 그 문답은 끝났다. 우두머리 인도인은 소년을 가리키고, 또 그들이 (뒤에 우리가 알아낸 바로는) 묵고 있던 읍내 쪽을 가리키며, 저희 말로 다른 두 사람에게 무어라 일렀다. 그러고는 소년의 머리에 다시 몇 가지 표시를 한 뒤, 그 이마에 입김을 훅 불어 소년을 화들짝 깨웠다. 그런 다음 그들은 다 함께 읍내 쪽으로 제 갈 길을 갔고, 처녀들은 그들을 더는 보지 못했다.\n\n사람들이 하는 대개의 일에는, 찾아보려고만 들면 교훈이 담겨 있는 법이다. 그렇다면 이 일의 교훈은 무엇이었을까?\n\n교훈인즉, 내 생각으로는 이러했다. 첫째, 그 우두머리 요술쟁이가 바깥의 하인들 사이에서 프랭클린 도련님의 도착 이야기가 오가는 것을 듣고 그것으로 돈푼깨나 벌 궁리를 했다는 것. 둘째, 그자가 (그 돈을 벌 셈으로) 제 사내들과 소년을 데리고, 마님이 마차를 몰고 집으로 돌아오시는 것을 볼 때까지 근처를 어정거리다가, 그런 다음 다시 와서 프랭클린 도련님의 도착을 요술로 알아맞히려 했다는 것. 셋째, 페넬로피가 들은 것은, 배우들이 연극을 연습하듯 그자들이 저희 요술 눈속임을 미리 익히는 소리였다는 것. 넷째, 나는 그날 저녁에 그 은 접시 바구니를 잘 지켜보는 편이 좋으리라는 것. 다섯째, 페넬로피는 흥분을 가라앉히고 제 아비인 나를 두고 물러가, 다시 볕 아래서 졸게 내버려 두는 편이 좋으리라는 것.\n\n내가 보기에는 그것이 사리에 맞는 견해였다. 젊은 여자들의 성미를 조금이라도 아신다면, 페넬로피가 그 견해를 받아들이지 않았다는 이야기를 들으셔도 놀라지 않으실 것이다. 내 딸의 말로는, 이 일의 교훈은 심상치 않은 것이었다. 그 애는 특히 인도인의 세 번째 물음, 곧 그 영국 신사가 그것을 몸에 지니고 있더냐, 하던 것을 내게 새삼 일깨웠다. \"아, 아버지!\" 페넬로피가 두 손을 마주 잡으며 말했다. \"이 일로 농담하지 마세요. '그것'이 무슨 뜻일까요?\"\n\n\"프랭클린 도련님께 여쭤보자꾸나, 얘야.\" 나는 말했다. \"네가 프랭클린 도련님이 오실 때까지 기다릴 수만 있다면 말이다.\" 나는 그게 농담이라는 뜻으로 눈을 찡긋해 보였다. 페넬로피는 그것을 아주 진지하게 받아들였다. 내 딸의 그 진지함이 나를 우습게 간질였다. \"대체 프랭클린 도련님이 그 일에 대해 무엇을 아신단 말이냐?\" 내가 물었다. \"여쭤보세요.\" 페넬로피가 말했다. \"그러곤 그분도 이걸 웃음거리로 여기시는지 두고 보자고요.\" 그 한마디를 이별의 화살처럼 쏘아 놓고, 내 딸은 나를 떠났다.\n\n그 애가 가고 난 뒤, 나는 정말로 프랭클린 도련님께 여쭤보리라고 혼자 마음을 정했다—주로 페넬로피의 마음을 가라앉혀 주기 위해서였다. 그날 늦게 내가 실제로 여쭈었을 때 우리 사이에 오간 말은, 마땅한 자리에 이르러 낱낱이 적어 놓은 것을 보시게 될 것이다. 그러나 나는 여러분의 기대를 잔뜩 부풀려 놓았다가 저버리고 싶지는 않으므로, 여기서—더 나아가기 전에—미리 일러두는 것을 허락받고자 한다. 그 요술쟁이들에 관한 우리 대화에서는 농담의 그림자조차 찾아보지 못하시리라는 것을. 참으로 놀랍게도, 프랭클린 도련님은 페넬로피와 마찬가지로 그 일을 진지하게 받아들이셨다. 얼마나 진지했는가 하면, 그분의 소견으로는 '그것'이 곧 월장석을 뜻했다고 내가 말씀드리면 여러분도 아시게 되리라.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n여러분을 나와 내 벌집 의자 곁에 이렇게 붙들어 두어 참으로 미안하다. 볕바른 뒤뜰에서 꾸벅거리는 늙은이란 흥미로운 구경거리가 못 된다는 것쯤은 나도 잘 안다. 그러나 일이란 실제로 일어난 그대로 마땅한 제자리에 적어 두어야 하는 법이니—부디 조금만 더 나와 함께 터덜터덜 걸으시며, 이날 늦게 있을 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 도착을 기다려 주시기 바란다.\n\n내 딸 페넬로피가 나를 떠난 뒤, 미처 다시 졸음에 빠져들 새도 없이, 나는 하인들 식당에서 접시와 그릇이 달그락거리는 소리에 방해를 받았으니, 그것은 곧 저녁 식사가 준비되었다는 뜻이었다. 나는 내 식사를 내 거실에서 따로 드는지라, 하인들 저녁과는 아무 상관이 없었고, 그저 다시 의자에 몸을 편안히 누이기 전에 다들 맛있게 잘 먹으라고 빌어 주는 것이 고작이었다. 막 다리를 쭉 뻗으려는데, 또 다른 여자 하나가 내게로 튀어나왔다. 이번엔 딸이 아니라, 부엌데기 낸시였다. 나는 마침 그녀가 나가는 길목에 딱 서 있었는데, 지나가게 비켜 달라는 그녀의 얼굴을 보니 뾰로통한 낯빛이었다—하인들의 우두머리인 나로서는, 원칙상 그런 낯빛을 캐묻지 않고 그냥 지나쳐 보내는 법이 결코 없다.\n\n\"저녁밥을 마다하고 등을 돌리다니 무슨 일이냐?\" 나는 물었다. \"지금 무엇이 못마땅한 게냐, 낸시?\"\n\n낸시는 대답도 없이 나를 밀치고 지나가려 했다. 그래서 나는 몸을 일으켜 그녀의 귀를 붙잡았다. 그녀는 통통하니 잘생긴 젊은 처녀인데, 나는 한 계집아이가 개인적으로 마음에 든다는 표시로 그런 식을 취하는 것이 예사다.\n\n\"지금 무엇이 못마땅하냐고?\" 나는 다시 한번 말했다.\n\n\"로재나가 또 저녁 시간에 늦었어요.\" 낸시가 말한다. \"그래서 제가 그 애를 데리러 가라고 시킴을 당했지 뭐예요. 이 집에선 힘든 일이 죄다 제 어깨에 떨어진다니까요. 놓아주세요, 베터리지 씨!\"\n\n여기서 로재나라고 일컬은 사람은 우리 집 둘째 하녀였다. 우리 둘째 하녀가 나는 어쩐지 가엾게 여겨지던 터라 (그 까닭은 이제 곧 아시게 될 것이다), 그리고 낸시의 낯빛을 보아하니 형편상 필요한 것보다 더 모진 말을 퍼부으며 제 동료를 데려올 성싶기에, 문득 나는 딱히 할 일도 없겠다 로재나를 내가 손수 데려오는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 들었다. 앞으로는 시간을 지키라고 넌지시 일러 줄 참이었는데, 그 말이 다른 사람 아닌 나한테서 나온다면 그 애가 고이 받아들이리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었다.\n\n\"로재나는 어디 있느냐?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"그야 물론 모래펄이죠!\" 낸시가 고개를 홱 젖히며 말한다. \"오늘 아침에도 그 기절 발작을 또 일으키더니, 바깥에 나가 바람 좀 쐬고 오게 해 달라지 뭐예요. 저는 정말이지 그 애한테 진절머리가 나요!\"\n\n\"저녁밥이나 먹으러 돌아가거라, 얘야.\" 나는 말했다. \"나는 그 애한테 진절머리가 나지 않으니, 내가 데려오마.\"\n\n낸시는 (식성이 좋은 아이라) 흐뭇한 낯빛이 되었다. 그 애가 흐뭇한 낯빛이 되면, 예뻐 보인다. 그 애가 예뻐 보이면, 나는 그 턱을 살짝 쓸어 준다. 이건 부도덕이 아니라—그저 버릇일 뿐이다.\n\n자, 나는 지팡이를 챙겨 들고 모래펄로 길을 나섰다.\n\n아니! 아직 나서서는 안 되겠다. 또 여러분을 붙들어 두어 미안하다. 하지만 여러분은 정말이지 이 모래펄 이야기와 로재나 이야기를 들으셔야 한다—그 까닭인즉, 다이아몬드 사건이 그 둘 다와 밀접히 얽혀 있기 때문이다. 곁길로 새지 않고 내 이야기를 밀고 나가려고 내가 얼마나 애를 쓰는지, 그리고 얼마나 번번이 실패하는지! 그러나 어쩌겠는가!—사람이며 사물이며 이 세상살이에서 어찌나 성가시게 불쑥불쑥 튀어나와, 이를테면 저를 좀 봐 달라고 기어이 우겨 대는 것을. 우리 느긋이, 그리고 짤막하게 넘어가기로 하자. 곧 수수께끼 한복판에 들어서게 될 테니, 내가 약속하리다!\n\n로재나로 말할 것 같으면 (사물보다 사람을 앞세우는 것이 그저 인지상정이니), 우리 집의 유일한 새 하인이었다. 내가 지금 적고 있는 시점으로부터 넉 달쯤 전에, 마님이 런던에 가셨다가 한 갱생원을 둘러보셨다. 그곳은 감옥에서 풀려난 가엾은 여자들이 다시 나쁜 길로 떠내려가지 않도록 구하려는 뜻으로 세워진 곳이었다. 원장은 마님이 그곳에 관심을 두시는 것을 보고, 로재나 스피어먼이라는 이름의 한 처녀를 가리켜 보이며, 참으로 애처로운 사연을 들려주었다. 그 사연을 나는 여기서 되풀이할 엄두가 나지 않는다. 아무 소용도 없이 마음이 비참해지는 것을 나는 좋아하지 않으며, 여러분도 그러시리라. 그 사연의 요지인즉, 로재나 스피어먼이 도둑이었다는 것이다. 다만 그녀는, 도시에서 회사를 차려 한 사람이 아니라 수천 명에게서 훔쳐 내는 그런 부류가 못 되었던 탓에, 법에 붙들렸고, 감옥과 갱생원이 그 법의 뒤를 따랐다. 로재나에 대한 원장의 소견은 (그녀가 저지른 일에도 불구하고) 이 처녀가 천에 하나 나올까 말까 한 아이요, 그저 어떤 기독교인 여성의 관심을 받을 만한 사람임을 스스로 증명해 보일 기회 하나가 필요할 뿐이라는 것이었다. 마님은 (세상에 참된 기독교인 여성이 있다면 바로 그분이셨으니) 그 말에, 원장에게 이르셨다. \"로재나 스피어먼에게 내 밑에서 그 기회를 주겠어요.\" 그로부터 일주일 뒤, 로재나 스피어먼은 우리 둘째 하녀로 이 집에 들어왔다.\n\n레이철 아가씨와 나 말고는, 이 처녀의 사연을 아는 이가 한 사람도 없었다. 마님은 대개의 일을 내게 물어 의논하시는 영예를 베풀어 주셨는데, 로재나에 관해서도 내게 물으셨다. 요즈음 들어 나는, 지금은 고인이 되신 존 경의, 매사에 마님 뜻을 따르는 방식에 제법 물들어 있던 터라, 로재나 스피어먼에 관해서도 마님 뜻에 진심으로 동의했다.\n\n이 가엾은 우리 처녀에게 주어진 것보다 더 공평한 기회를 누린 처녀는 없었을 것이다. 하인들 가운데 아무도 그녀의 지난 삶을 들먹여 면박할 수 없었으니, 그것이 어떠했는지 아는 하인이 하나도 없었기 때문이다. 그녀는 다른 이들과 마찬가지로 제 품삯과 제 특혜를 받았고, 이따금 마님이 남몰래 건네주시는 다정한 격려의 말도 받았다. 그 답례로 그녀는, 나로서는 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없는데, 저에게 베풀어진 그 친절한 대접을 받을 만한 사람임을 넉넉히 보여 주었다. 몸은 결코 튼튼하지 못하여 이따금 앞서 말한 그 기절 발작에 시달리면서도, 그녀는 제 일을 얌전하고 군말 없이, 정성껏, 그리고 훌륭하게 해냈다. 그러나 어쩐 일인지, 그녀는 다른 여자 하인들 사이에서는 좀처럼 벗을 사귀지 못했다. 다만 내 딸 페넬로피만은 예외였으니, 그 애는 로재나와 각별히 친하게 지내지는 않았어도 늘 그녀에게 다정했다.\n\n그 처녀가 대체 무엇으로 그들의 비위를 거슬렀는지 나는 도무지 모르겠다. 남들의 시샘을 살 만한 미모가 그녀에게 있었던 것도 아니다. 그녀는 이 집에서 가장 못생긴 여자였고, 게다가 한쪽 어깨가 다른 쪽보다 크다는 불운까지 겹쳐 있었다. 하인들이 무엇보다 못마땅해한 것은, 내 생각에, 그녀의 과묵한 혀와 외톨이 같은 처신이었다. 그녀는 남들이 수다를 떠는 틈틈이 책을 읽거나 일을 했다. 그리고 제 차례가 되어 바깥나들이를 할 때면, 열에 아홉은 조용히 챙 모자를 눌러쓰고 혼자서 그 차례를 보냈다. 그녀는 결코 다투는 법이 없었고, 결코 성을 내는 법이 없었다. 다만 남들과 저 사이에, 어떤 일정한 거리를, 고집스럽게, 그러나 예의 바르게 두었을 뿐이다. 여기에다, 그토록 못생겼으면서도 그녀에게는 하녀 같지 않고 오히려 귀부인 같은 무언가가 살짝 감돌았다는 점까지 보태자. 그것은 그녀의 목소리에 있었는지도 모르고, 그녀의 얼굴에 있었는지도 모른다. 내가 말할 수 있는 것은 다만, 그녀가 이 집에 들어온 첫날부터 다른 여자들이 번갯불처럼 그 점을 낚아채더니, (참으로 부당하게도) 로재나 스피어먼이 잘난 체를 한다고 수군거렸다는 것뿐이다.\n\n이제 로재나 이야기를 했으니, 이 별난 처녀의 여러 괴상한 버릇 가운데 하나만 짚어 두고서 다음으로 모래펄 이야기로 넘어가면 되겠다.\n\n우리 집은 요크셔 해안 높다란 곳, 바다에 바싹 붙어 있다. 우리 집 사방으로는, 딱 한 방향만 빼고, 어느 쪽으로든 아름다운 산책길이 나 있다. 그 한 방향만은, 나도 인정하거니와, 지긋지긋한 길이다. 그 길은 사분의 일 마일가량 음산한 전나무 조림지를 뚫고 나아가다가, 나지막한 벼랑 사이로 여러분을 데려내어, 우리 해안을 통틀어 가장 외지고 가장 볼썽사나운 조그만 만(灣)에 이르게 한다.\n\n여기 모래언덕들은 바다까지 흘러내리다가, 서로 마주 보며 삐죽 튀어나온 두 갈래 바위 곶으로 끝나는데, 그 곶들은 물속에서 시야를 벗어날 때까지 뻗어 나간다. 하나는 북쪽 곶, 하나는 남쪽 곶이라 불린다. 그 둘 사이에, 해마다 철에 따라 앞뒤로 자리를 옮겨 가며, 요크셔 해안을 통틀어 가장 무시무시한 유사(流沙)가 자리하고 있다. 물때가 바뀔 무렵이면, 저 아래 알 수 없는 깊은 곳에서 무언가가 일어나, 유사의 온 얼굴을 보기에도 참으로 놀랍도록 부르르 떨고 부들부들 흔들리게 만든다. 그 때문에 우리 고장 사람들 사이에서 그곳은 떠는 모래펄이라는 이름을 얻었다. 만 어귀 가까이, 반 마일쯤 나간 곳에 커다란 모래톱이 하나 있어, 먼바다에서 밀려 들어오는 큰 물결의 힘을 꺾어 준다. 겨울이나 여름이나, 밀물이 그 유사 위로 흘러들 때면, 바다는 제 물결들을 그 모래톱에 남겨 둔 채, 물을 넘실넘실 매끄럽게 밀어 넣어, 소리 없이 모래를 덮어 버리는 듯하다. 외롭고도 지긋지긋한 피난처라고 내 장담하리다! 이 만에는 배 한 척 감히 들어오지 않는다. 코브스 홀이라 불리는 우리 어촌의 아이들도 여기 놀러 오는 법이 없다. 내가 보기엔 하늘을 나는 새들조차 이 떠는 모래펄을 멀찍이 에돌아 간다. 골라 걸을 근사한 산책길이 수십 군데요, \"같이 가!\" 한마디만 하면 함께 가 줄 동무도 있는 젊은 여자가, 하필 이런 곳을 좋아하여, 제 나들이 차례가 오면 여기 홀로 앉아 일을 하거나 책을 읽는다는 것은—나도 인정하거니와, 도무지 믿기지 않는 노릇이다. 그러나 여러분이 어떻게 갖다 붙이든 간에, 이것이 로재나 스피어먼이 가장 좋아하는 산책길이었다는 것만은 엄연한 사실이다. 다만 그녀가 한두 번 코브스 홀에 간 적은 있었으니, 우리 동네에서 그녀에게 있는 단 하나뿐인 벗을 만나러 간 것이었는데, 그에 관해서는 이따가 더 이야기하겠다. 그리고 내가 지금 그 처녀를 저녁 먹으러 데려오려고 바로 그 같은 곳으로 나서고 있었다는 것도 엄연한 사실이니, 이로써 우리는 다행히 아까 그 대목으로 되돌아와, 다시 모래펄로 가는 길에 산뜻이 오르게 되었다.\n\n조림지에서는 그 처녀의 그림자도 보이지 않았다. 모래언덕을 지나 바닷가로 나서니, 거기 그녀가 있었다. 조그만 밀짚 챙 모자를 쓰고, 기형인 어깨를 되도록 감추려고 늘 걸치던 그 수수한 잿빛 망토를 두른 채—거기 그녀가, 오롯이 홀로 서서, 유사와 바다를 내다보고 있었다.\n\n내가 다가서자 그녀는 흠칫 놀라, 나에게서 고개를 돌려 버렸다. 사람의 얼굴을 마주 보지 않는 것 역시, 하인들의 우두머리인 내가 원칙상 캐묻지 않고 그냥 지나쳐 보내는 법이 결코 없는 처사이기에—나는 그녀를 내 쪽으로 돌려세웠고, 그녀가 울고 있음을 알았다. 마님이 내게 주신 여섯 장의 고운 손수건 가운데 하나인 반다나 손수건이 마침 내 주머니에 있었다. 나는 그것을 꺼내며 로재나에게 말했다. \"이리 와서 앉으렴, 얘야, 나와 함께 여기 비탈진 바닷가에. 우선 내가 네 눈물부터 닦아 주고, 그러고 나서 무엇 때문에 울고 있었는지 주제넘게 한번 물어보마.\"\n\n여러분도 내 나이가 되어 보면, 비탈진 바닷가에 앉는 일이 지금 생각하시는 것보다 훨씬 오래 걸리는 일임을 아시게 되리라. 내가 자리를 다 잡았을 무렵, 로재나는 내 것보다 한참 못한 손수건—값싼 무명—으로 제 눈물을 다 훔친 뒤였다. 그녀는 몹시 조용하고 몹시 가엾어 보였다. 그러나 내가 말하니 착한 아이처럼 내 곁에 앉았다. 여자를 가장 빠른 길로 달래고 싶거든, 무릎 위에 앉히라. 나는 이 금과옥조를 떠올렸다. 그러나 이런! 로재나는 낸시가 아니었으니, 그것이 사실이렷다!\n\n\"자, 말해 보렴, 얘야.\" 나는 말했다. \"무엇 때문에 울고 있느냐?\"\n\n\"흘러가 버린 세월 때문이에요, 베터리지 씨.\" 로재나가 조용히 말한다. \"제 지난 삶이 아직도 이따금 제게 되돌아와요.\"\n\n\"이런, 이런, 얘야.\" 나는 말했다. \"네 지난 삶은 죄다 말끔히 씻겨 나갔단다. 어째서 그걸 잊지 못하는 게냐?\"\n\n그녀는 내 외투 자락 한쪽을 붙잡았다. 나는 칠칠치 못한 늙은이라, 먹고 마시는 것이 옷에 곧잘 튄다. 이 여자 저 여자가 번갈아 내 몸의 기름때를 닦아 준다. 바로 전날, 로재나는 무엇이든 지워 준다고 장담하는 새 약제로 내 외투 자락의 얼룩 하나를 빼 주었다. 기름때는 사라졌으나, 기름이 묻었던 자리에 천 보풀이 살짝 칙칙해진 데가 남아 있었다. 그 처녀는 그 자리를 가리키며 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"얼룩은 빠졌어요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 자국은 남아요, 베터리지 씨—자국은 남는다고요!\"\n\n제 외투를 빌미로 삼아 사람의 허를 찌르는 그런 말에는 대꾸하기가 쉽지 않다. 게다가 그 처녀 자신의 무언가가, 바로 그 순간 그녀를 유난히 가엾게 여기게 만들었다. 다른 점으로는 그토록 못생겼어도 그녀에게는 고운 갈색 눈이 있었는데—그 눈으로 그녀는, 내 행복한 노년과 내 훌륭한 인품을, 마치 저 자신에게는 영영 닿을 수 없는 것을 바라보듯, 어떤 존경 어린 눈길로 나를 바라보았으니, 그것이 우리 둘째 하녀를 생각하는 내 마음을 무겁게 했다. 내가 스스로 그녀를 달랠 능력이 없다고 느껴지니, 남은 것은 오직 한 가지뿐이었다. 그것은—그녀를 데리고 저녁을 먹으러 들어가는 일이었다.\n\n\"나 좀 일으켜다오.\" 나는 말했다. \"너 저녁 시간에 늦었다, 로재나—그래서 내가 데리러 온 게야.\"\n\n\"당신이요, 베터리지 씨!\" 그녀가 말한다.\n\n\"낸시더러 너를 데려오라 했더랬다.\" 나는 말했다. \"하지만 어차피 꾸중을 들을 바에야, 얘야, 그게 내 입에서 나오는 편이 네게 나으리라 싶었지.\"\n\n가엾은 것은 나를 일으켜 주기는커녕, 제 손을 슬며시 내 손안에 밀어 넣고 살짝 쥐어 왔다. 그녀는 다시 울지 않으려고 무진 애를 썼고, 마침내 참아 냈으니—나는 그 점을 갸륵히 여겼다. \"참 다정하세요, 베터리지 씨.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 오늘 저녁은 먹고 싶지 않아요—여기 조금만 더 있게 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"무엇이 그리 이곳을 좋아하게 하느냐?\" 나는 물었다. \"대체 무엇이 너를 이 처량한 곳으로 노상 끌어들이는 게냐?\"\n\n\"무언가가 저를 이곳으로 끌어당겨요.\" 그 처녀가 손가락으로 모래에 그림을 그리며 말한다. \"여기서 떨어져 있으려고 애를 써도, 그럴 수가 없어요. 이따금,\" 그녀가, 마치 제 공상에 저 스스로 겁이라도 먹은 듯, 나직한 목소리로 말한다. \"이따금, 베터리지 씨, 저는 제 무덤이 여기서 저를 기다리고 있는 것만 같아요.\"\n\n\"널 기다리는 건 양고기 구이와 쇠기름 푸딩이니라!\" 하고 나는 말한다. \"당장 들어가 저녁을 먹어라. 로재나야, 빈속에 생각에 잠기면 바로 이 꼴이 나는 게다!\" 나는 준엄하게 말했으니, (내 나잇살에) 스물다섯 젊은 여자가 제 마지막을 입에 올리는 소리를 들으매 자연히 부아가 치밀었던 것이다!\n\n그녀는 내 말이 들리지도 않는 듯했다. 그녀는 내 어깨에 손을 얹어, 나를 제 곁에 앉은 그대로 붙들어 두었다.\n\n\"이곳이 제게 마법을 걸어 놓은 것만 같아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"밤이면 밤마다 이곳 꿈을 꾸어요. 앉아서 바느질을 할 때도 이곳을 생각해요. 제가 고마워하는 줄은 아시잖아요, 베터리지 씨—제가 당신의 친절과, 저를 믿어 주시는 마님의 신임에 값하려 애쓰는 줄은 아시잖아요. 하지만 이따금 저는, 제가 겪어 온 그 모든 일을 겪고 난 저 같은 여자한테는 여기 삶이 너무 조용하고 너무 과분한 게 아닐까 싶어요, 베터리지 씨—제가 겪어 온 그 모든 일을 겪고 나서요. 제가 저들과 같은 사람이 아님을 알면서 다른 하인들 틈에 끼어 있는 것이, 제게는 이곳에 있는 것보다 더 외로워요. 마님도 모르시고, 갱생원 원장님도 모르세요, 정직한 사람들이 저 같은 여자한테는 그저 그 존재만으로도 얼마나 무서운 꾸짖음이 되는지를요. 저를 꾸짖지 마세요, 다정하고 착한 어른이시니. 저는 제 할 일은 하잖아요, 그렇죠? 부디 제가 불만스러워한다고 마님께 이르지 마세요—저는 불만스럽지 않아요. 그저 이따금 마음이 뒤숭숭할 뿐이에요.\" 그녀는 내 어깨에서 제 손을 홱 떼더니, 느닷없이 유사를 손가락으로 가리켰다. \"보세요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"놀랍지 않아요? 무섭지 않아요? 벌써 수십 번은 봤는데도, 마치 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 것처럼 볼 때마다 늘 새로워요!\"\n\n나는 그녀가 가리키는 곳을 보았다. 물때가 바뀌는 참이라, 그 지긋지긋한 모래가 부르르 떨기 시작했다. 그 널따란 갈색 얼굴이 서서히 부풀어 오르더니, 이내 온통 옴폭옴폭 패며 부들부들 떨었다. \"저것이 제 눈엔 어떻게 보이는지 아세요?\" 로재나가 다시 내 어깨를 붙잡으며 말한다. \"마치 그 밑에 숨 막혀 죽어 가는 사람이 수백 명이나 있어서—죄다 수면 위로 올라오려고 발버둥 치면서, 죄다 저 무서운 심연 속으로 점점 더 깊이 가라앉는 것만 같아요! 돌을 하나 던져 보세요, 베터리지 씨! 돌을 하나 던져서, 모래가 그걸 쭉 빨아들이는 걸 봐요!\"\n\n이 무슨 해괴한 소리인가! 빈속이 뒤숭숭한 마음을 파먹고 있는 꼴이라니! 내 대답이—장담하건대, 이 가엾은 처녀 자신을 위한 제법 매서운 대답이!—혀끝에서 막 나오려는 참에, 모래언덕 사이에서 내 이름을 부르며 나를 찾는 목소리에 그 대답이 별안간 뚝 끊겨 버렸다. \"베터리지!\" 목소리가 외친다. \"어디 있나?\" \"여기요!\" 나도 마주 외쳤으나, 그게 누구인지는 짐작조차 가지 않았다. 로재나가 벌떡 일어서더니, 그 목소리 쪽을 바라보고 섰다. 나도 이번엔 내 두 다리로 일어서 볼까 하던 참인데, 그 처녀의 얼굴에 별안간 일어난 변화에 나는 그만 얼떨떨해졌다.\n\n그녀의 낯빛이, 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 아름다운 붉은빛으로 물들었다. 그녀는 말도 숨도 못 이룰 듯한 놀라움에 사로잡혀 온몸이 환히 밝아졌다. \"누구지?\" 내가 물었다. 로재나는 내 물음을 도로 내게 되돌려주었다. \"아! 누구지?\" 그녀는, 내게라기보다 저 자신에게, 나직이 말했다. 나는 모래 위에서 몸을 비틀어 뒤를 돌아보았다. 거기, 언덕 사이에서 우리에게로 나오고 있는 것은, 눈빛이 초롱초롱한 젊은 신사였으니, 아름다운 담황색 양복에 그에 어울리는 장갑과 모자를 갖추고, 단춧구멍에는 장미 한 송이를 꽂고, 얼굴에는 저 떠는 모래펄마저도 마주 웃게 만들 법한 미소를 띠고 있었다. 내가 미처 일어서기도 전에, 그는 내 곁 모래 위로 털썩 주저앉더니, 외국식으로 제 팔을 내 목에 두르고, 내 몸에서 숨이 다 빠져나갈 만큼 나를 와락 끌어안았다. \"그리운 베터리지 영감!\" 그가 말한다. \"내가 자네한테 칠 실링 육 펜스 빚이 있지. 자, 내가 누군지 알겠나?\"\n\n우리를 굽어살피사 지켜 주소서! 여기—우리가 그분을 기다리던 때보다 실히 네 시간이나 앞서—프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 와 있었던 것이다!\n\n내가 미처 한마디 하기도 전에, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이, 겉보기에 조금 놀란 낯으로, 나에게서 로재나에게로 눈길을 들어 올리는 것을 보았다. 그분을 따라, 나도 그 처녀를 보았다. 그녀는, 프랭클린 도련님과 눈이 마주친 탓인 듯, 그 어느 때보다 더 짙은 붉은빛으로 얼굴을 붉히더니, 몸을 돌려 홀연 우리 곁을 떠나갔다. 그 신사에게 절을 올리지도, 내게 한마디 건네지도 않은 채, 내 머리로는 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 당황함에 휩싸여서. 여느 때의 그녀와는 영 딴판이었다. 대체로 그녀보다 더 예의 바르고 몸가짐 반듯한 하인은 여러분도 좀처럼 만나 보지 못하셨을 것이다.\n\n\"별난 아가씨로군.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"저 사람이 내게서 무얼 보았기에 저리 놀라는 걸까?\"\n\n\"제 짐작으로는, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 우리 젊은 신사의 대륙풍 교육을 살짝 놀리며 대답했다. \"외국물이 든 그 광택이 아닐는지요.\"\n\n나는 프랭클린 도련님의 무심한 물음과 나의 어리석은 대답을 여기 적어 두거니와, 이는 모든 아둔한 사람들에게 위안과 격려가 되라는 뜻에서다—앞서도 말했듯이, 저보다 나은 사람들도 때로는 저희만큼이나 별수 없다는 것을 알게 되는 것이, 우리네 못난 동류들에게는 크나큰 만족이 되는 법이니 말이다. 그 놀라운 외국 수련을 쌓은 프랭클린 도련님도, 나이와 경험과 타고난 어수룩한 잔꾀를 지닌 나도, 로재나 스피어먼의 그 까닭 모를 행동이 실제로 무엇을 뜻하는지는 눈곱만큼도 짐작하지 못했다. 그 가엾은 것은, 우리가 모래언덕 사이로 그 조그만 잿빛 망토가 마지막으로 나풀거리는 것을 미처 다 보기도 전에, 우리 생각에서 잊히고 말았다. 그래서 그게 어쨌다는 거냐? 여러분은 자연히 그렇게 물으실 것이다. 계속 읽어 나가시라, 착한 벗이여, 되도록 참을성 있게. 그러면 여러분도 아마, 내가 진상을 알아냈을 때 그러했듯이, 로재나 스피어먼을 가엾이 여기게 되시리라.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n우리 둘만 남게 된 뒤, 내가 맨 처음 한 일은, 모래 위 내 자리에서 일어나 보려고 세 번째로 시도해 본 것이었다. 프랭클린 도련님이 나를 말렸다.\n\n\"이 지긋지긋한 곳에도 한 가지 이점은 있군.\" 그가 말했다. \"이곳을 온통 우리끼리 차지했으니 말이야. 그대로 있게, 베터리지. 자네한테 할 말이 있어.\"\n\n그가 말하는 동안, 나는 그를 바라보며, 내 앞에 선 이 사내에게서 내가 기억하는 그 소년의 모습을 조금이나마 찾아보려 애썼다. 그런데 이 사내가 나를 어리둥절하게 했다. 아무리 뜯어보아도, 소년 시절 그 말쑥한 조그만 저고리를 찾을 수 없듯 소년 시절 그 발그레한 뺨도 찾아볼 수가 없었다. 그의 낯빛은 창백해져 있었다. 얼굴 아래쪽은, 나로서는 여간 놀랍고 실망스럽게도, 곱슬곱슬한 갈색 턱수염과 콧수염으로 덮여 있었다. 그에게는 활기차고 시원시원한 태도가 있어, 인정하거니와 여간 유쾌하고 사람을 끄는 데가 있었다. 그러나 옛 시절의 그 스스럼없고 편안하던 몸가짐에는 견줄 바가 못 되었다. 설상가상으로, 그는 키가 커지겠거니 싶더니만 그 약속을 지키지 않았다. 그는 말끔하고 호리호리하고 몸매가 좋았으나, 중키에서 한두 치는 모자랐다. 한마디로, 그는 나를 온통 갈피 못 잡게 했다. 흘러간 세월은 그의 옛 모습이라곤 아무것도 남겨 두지 않았다. 다만 그 눈에 어린 밝고 솔직한 눈빛만은 예외였다. 거기서 나는 우리 착한 도련님을 다시 찾아냈고, 거기서 내 살펴보기를 그만 멈추기로 했다.\n\n\"옛집으로 돌아오신 것을 환영합니다, 프랭클린 도련님.\" 나는 말했다. \"더구나, 도련님, 우리가 기다리던 것보다 몇 시간이나 일찍 오셨으니 그만큼 더 반갑습니다요.\"\n\n\"자네들이 기다리던 것보다 일찍 온 데는 까닭이 있네.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"베터리지, 아무래도 나는 지난 사나흘 동안 런던에서 미행을 당하고 감시를 받은 것 같아. 그래서 오후 기차 대신 아침 기차로 온 걸세. 어떤 거무튀튀한 낯선 자를 따돌리고 싶었거든.\"\n\n그 말은 나를 놀라게 하는 데 그치지 않았다. 그 말은 내 머릿속에, 번개처럼, 그 요술쟁이 셋과, 그들이 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 무슨 해코지를 하려 든다던 페넬로피의 짐작을 되살려 놓았다.\n\n\"누가 도련님을 감시한단 말입니까—또 어째서요?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"오늘 이 집에 왔었다는 그 인도인 셋에 대해 말해 주게.\" 프랭클린 도련님이, 내 물음에는 아랑곳없이 말한다. \"베터리지, 어쩌면 내 그 낯선 자와 자네네 그 요술쟁이 셋이, 한 수수께끼의 조각들로 밝혀질지도 모르는 일이야.\"\n\n\"도련님은 어떻게 그 요술쟁이들에 대해 아십니까?\" 나는, 물음 위에 또 물음을 얹으며 물었으니, 실토하건대 이건 무례한 짓이었다. 그러나 여러분도 가련한 인간 본성에서 너무 많은 것을 기대하지는 않으실 터이니—나한테서도 너무 많은 것을 기대하지는 마시라.\n\n\"이 집에서 페넬로피를 보았지.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"그리고 페넬로피가 내게 말해 주었어. 베터리지, 자네 딸은 예쁜 처녀가 되겠거니 싶더니 그 약속을 지켰더군. 페넬로피는 조그만 귀와 조그만 발을 지녔어. 지금은 고인이 된 베터리지 부인도 그런 값진 장점을 지녔었나?\"\n\n\"지금은 고인이 된 베터리지 부인은 흠이라면 여럿 지녔었지요, 도련님.\" 하고 나는 말한다. \"그중 하나가 (이런 말씀을 드려도 용서하신다면) 도무지 당면한 일에 붙어 있질 못하는 것이었습니다요. 그 사람은 여자라기보다 파리에 가까웠어요. 무엇 하나에도 진득이 내려앉질 못했으니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 그 사람은 딱 내게 어울렸겠군.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"나 역시 무엇 하나에도 진득이 눌러앉질 못하거든. 베터리지, 자네 그 재치는 여전하다 못해 예전보다 더 날이 섰군그래. 자네 딸도 내가 요술쟁이 이야기를 자세히 물었을 때 꼭 그렇게 말하더군. '아버지가 말씀해 주실 거예요, 도련님. 연세에 비해 참으로 대단한 분이시고, 말씀도 참 곱게 하시거든요.' 페넬로피가 한 말 그대로일세—고운 얼굴을 발그레 붉히면서. 내가 자네를 우러르는 마음조차 나를 말리지 못했으니—아니, 됐네. 나는 그 애가 어린애일 적부터 알았고, 그 애도 그만한 일로 나빠질 것은 없지. 자, 진지해지세. 그래, 그 요술쟁이들이 무슨 짓을 했나?\"\n\n나는 내 딸이 조금 못마땅했다—프랭클린 도련님이 그 애에게 입 맞추게 놔두어서가 아니라 (그거야 프랭클린 도련님이라면 얼마든지 환영이었다)—그 애의 어리석은 이야기를 나더러 건너 전해 옮기게 만들었기 때문이다. 그러나 이제 와서는 그 일을 이야기하는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다. 내가 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 프랭클린 도련님의 그 유쾌함은 죄다 스러졌다. 그는 눈썹을 잔뜩 찌푸리고 턱수염을 배배 꼬며 앉아 있었다. 내 이야기가 끝나자, 그는 우두머리 요술쟁이가 소년에게 던졌던 물음 가운데 둘을 나를 따라 되뇌었다—아무래도 그것들을 제 머릿속에 단단히 박아 두려는 속셈인 듯했다.\n\n\"'그 영국 신사가 오늘 여행할 길이, 다른 어느 길도 아닌 바로 이 집으로 오는 길이더냐?' '그 영국 신사가 그것을 몸에 지니고 있더냐?' 아무래도,\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제 주머니에서 봉인된 조그만 종이 꾸러미를 하나 꺼내며 말한다. \"그 '그것'이란 바로 이것을 뜻하는 것 같아. 그리고 '이것'이란, 베터리지, 내 외숙부 헌캐슬의 그 유명한 다이아몬드를 뜻하지.\"\n\n\"아이고 맙소사, 도련님!\" 나는 불쑥 내뱉었다. \"어쩌다 도련님이 그 사악한 대령의 다이아몬드를 맡게 되셨답니까?\"\n\n\"그 사악한 대령의 유언이, 그 다이아몬드를 내 사촌 레이철에게 생일 선물로 남겼다네.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"그리고 그 사악한 대령의 유언 집행인인 내 아버지가, 그것을 이리로 가져오라고 내게 맡기신 게지.\"\n\n그때 떠는 모래펄 위로 매끄럽게 스며들던 그 바다가, 내 눈앞에서 마른 땅으로 변했던들, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 그 말을 했을 때보다 더 놀랄 수 있었을까 싶다.\n\n\"대령의 다이아몬드가 레이철 아가씨께 남겨졌다!\" 하고 나는 말한다. \"게다가 도련님 아버님이, 도련님, 그 대령의 유언 집행인이시라니! 아니, 프랭클린 도련님, 저는 도련님 아버님이 그 대령을 부젓가락으로도 건드리려 하지 않으실 거라는 데 무슨 내기든 걸었을 겁니다요!\"\n\n\"험한 소리로군, 베터리지! 그 대령한테 무슨 흠이 있었기에? 그 사람은 내 시절이 아니라 자네 시절 사람이잖나. 자네가 그에 대해 아는 것을 내게 말해 주게. 그러면 나도 내 아버지가 어떻게 그의 유언 집행인이 되셨는지, 그 밖의 것들도 자네한테 말해 주지. 나는 런던에서 내 외숙부 헌캐슬과 그 다이아몬드에 관해 몇 가지를 알아냈는데, 내 눈에는 그게 꽤나 흉하게 보인단 말이야. 자네가 그걸 확인해 주었으면 하네. 자네 방금 그를 '사악한 대령'이라고 불렀지. 기억을 더듬어 보게, 이 사람아, 그리고 그 까닭을 내게 말해 주게.\"\n\n그가 진지하다는 것을 알았기에, 나는 그에게 말해 주었다.\n\n이하는 내가 한 이야기의 골자로, 순전히 여러분을 위해 죄다 적어 놓은 것이다. 여기에 주의를 기울이시라. 그러지 않으면 이야기가 깊어졌을 때 여러분은 온통 갈피를 잃고 헤매게 될 것이다. 아이들이며 저녁밥이며 새 챙 모자며 하는 것들을 머릿속에서 말끔히 비워 내시라. 정치며 말(馬)이며 도시의 시세며 클럽에서의 불평 따위를 잊을 수 있거든 어디 한번 잊어 보시라. 내 이런 허물없음을 부디 언짢게 여기지 마시기를. 이건 그저 내가 점잖으신 독자께 호소하는 한 방식일 뿐이다. 원 참! 나는 여러분이 손에 그 위대한 작가들의 책을 들고 있는 것을 보아 왔거니와, 사람이 아니라 책이 주의를 청할 때면 여러분의 주의가 얼마나 냉큼 딴 데로 새어 나가려 하는지를 내가 어찌 모르겠는가?\n\n나는 조금 앞에서, 마님의 부친이신, 성미는 짧고 혀는 긴 그 노영주 이야기를 했다. 그분에게는 자녀가 모두 다섯 있었다. 우선 아들이 둘. 그러고는 한참 뒤에, 그 아내가 다시 아이를 배기 시작하더니, 세 아가씨가, 이치가 허락하는 한 부지런히, 잇달아 태어났다. 앞서 말했듯이 내 여주인이 그 셋 가운데 가장 어리고 가장 훌륭했다. 두 아들 중 맏이 아서는 작위와 영지를 물려받았다. 둘째인 존 도련님은 한 친척이 남긴 넉넉한 재산을 얻어, 군에 들어갔다.\n\n제 둥지를 더럽히는 새는 못된 새라는 말이 있다. 나는 저 고귀한 헌캐슬 가문을 내 둥지로 여기고 있다. 그러니 존 도련님에 관한 이야기를 시시콜콜 늘어놓으라고 나한테 바라지 않으신다면, 나는 그것을 고맙게 여기겠다. 그는, 내가 정직히 믿기로, 세상에 나온 가장 몹쓸 망나니 가운데 하나였다. 그에 대해서라면 나는 그 이상도 그 이하도 좀처럼 말하기 어렵다. 그는 근위대에서 시작하여 군에 들어갔다. 그는 스물두 살도 되기 전에 근위대를 떠나야 했다—까닭은 묻지 마시라. 군은 몹시 엄격한데, 존 도련님한테는 지나치게 엄격했던 것이다. 그는 인도로 나가, 거기서도 똑같이 엄격한지 알아보고, 실전(實戰)을 조금 겪어 볼 셈이었다. 용맹으로 말하면 (그의 몫은 돌려주자면), 그는 불도그와 싸움닭을 섞고 거기에 야만의 기운을 한 자밤 뿌린 사내였다. 그는 세링가파탐 공략에 참가했다. 얼마 뒤 그는 다른 연대로 옮겼고, 세월이 흐르는 사이에 세 번째 연대로 또 옮겼다. 그 세 번째 연대에서 그는 중령으로 마지막 진급을 했고, 그것을 얻으면서 일사병도 얻어, 영국으로 돌아왔다.\n\n그는 온 집안의 문을 죄다 제게서 닫아 걸게 만드는 평판을 안고 돌아왔으니, (그때 막 혼인하신) 마님이 앞장서서, 당신 오라비는 결코 당신 집 어느 문도 들어서지 못하리라 선언하셨다 (물론 존 경의 동의를 얻어). 대령에게는 사람들이 그를 꺼리게 만드는 오점이 한둘이 아니었으나, 여기서는 그 다이아몬드의 오점 하나만 짚어 두면 되겠다.\n\n들리는 말로는, 그가 그 인도 보석을, 아무리 대담한 그일지언정 감히 실토하지 못할 방법으로 손에 넣었다고 했다. 그는 결코 그것을 팔려 하지 않았다—돈이 궁하지도 않았거니와, (다시 그의 몫을 돌려주자면) 돈을 목적으로 삼지도 않았으니까. 그는 결코 그것을 남에게 주지 않았고, 살아 있는 그 어떤 사람에게도 그것을 보여 준 적조차 없었다. 어떤 이들은 그가, 그것 때문에 군 당국과 곤란한 일에 얽힐까 봐 두려워한다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 (그 사내의 본바탕을 참으로 몰라도 한참 몰랐던 자들이었는데) 그가, 그것을 보여 주었다가는 목숨을 잃을까 봐 두려워한다고 했다.\n\n이 마지막 소문에는 어쩌면 한 톨의 진실이 섞여 있었는지도 모른다. 그가 두려워한다는 것은 거짓말이었으나, 인도에서 그의 목숨이 두 번이나 위협당했다는 것은 사실이었다. 그리고 그 밑바닥에 월장석이 있다는 것이 굳게 믿어졌다. 그가 영국으로 돌아와, 모든 사람이 저를 피하는 것을 알게 되었을 때도, 그 밑바닥에 또 월장석이 있다고들 여겼다. 대령의 삶에 드리운 수수께끼가 대령의 앞길을 가로막아, 이를테면 그를 제 겨레붙이 사이에서 무법자로 내몰았다. 사내들은 그를 저희 클럽에 들이려 하지 않았고, 그가 혼인하고자 했던 여자들은—한둘이 아니었는데—그를 거절했으며, 벗과 친척들은 거리에서 그를 알아보지 못할 만큼 눈이 어두워졌다.\n\n이런 곤경에 처한 사내라면 세상과 화해하려고 애써 볼 법도 하다. 그러나 제가 그르고 온 사회가 저를 적대할 때조차 굽히고 드는 것은, 존 도련님의 방식이 아니었다. 그는 인도에서, 암살에 대놓고 맞서며 그 다이아몬드를 지켰다. 그는 영국에서, 세상 여론에 대놓고 맞서며 그 다이아몬드를 지켰다. 여기, 한 폭의 그림처럼, 그 사내의 초상이 여러분 앞에 놓여 있다. 모든 것에 맞서는 성정, 그리고 잘생겼으되 악마에게 들린 듯한 얼굴.\n\n우리는 이따금 그에 관한 갖가지 소문을 들었다. 어떤 때는 그가 아편을 피우고 고서(古書)를 모으는 일에 빠져 있다고 했고, 어떤 때는 화학으로 괴상한 것들을 시험해 보고 있다고 전해졌으며, 또 어떤 때는 런던의 가장 낮은 빈민굴에서 가장 낮은 사람들 틈에 끼어 진탕 마시고 흥청거리는 것이 목격되었다고도 했다. 아무튼 외롭고 사악하고 음침한 삶이, 그 대령이 살아간 삶이었다. 영국으로 돌아온 뒤 단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 나 자신이 그를 얼굴을 맞대고 본 적이 있다.\n\n지금 내가 적고 있는 시점으로부터 두 해쯤 전, 그리고 그가 죽기 한 해 반쯤 전에, 대령이 뜻밖에도 런던에 있는 마님 댁에 찾아왔다. 그날은 레이철 아가씨의 생일밤, 6월 21일이었고, 여느 때처럼 그것을 기리는 잔치가 열리고 있었다. 나는 하인에게서, 어떤 신사분이 나를 보고자 한다는 전갈을 받았다. 현관으로 올라가 보니, 거기 대령이 있었다. 여위고, 초췌하고, 늙고, 남루한 데다, 여전히 사납고 여전히 사악한 모습으로.\n\n\"내 누이한테 올라가게.\" 그가 말한다. \"그리고 내 조카에게 생일을 두고두고 경하하고 싶어 내가 찾아왔다고 전하게.\"\n\n그는 이미 여러 차례 편지로, 마님과 화해해 보려고 시도한 바 있었으나, 내가 굳게 믿기로, 그것은 오직 마님을 성가시게 하려는 것 말고는 다른 목적이 없었다. 그러나 실제로 이 집에 몸소 찾아온 것은 이번이 처음이었다. 나는 오늘 밤 내 여주인께서 잔치를 열고 계신다고 말하려고 혀끝까지 그 말이 나와 있었다. 그러나 그의 그 악마 같은 낯빛이 나를 주춤하게 했다. 나는 그의 전갈을 들고 위층으로 올라갔고, 그 자신이 바라는 대로 그를 현관에 세워 둔 채 기다리게 했다. 하인들은 멀찍이 떨어져 서서 그를 빤히 쳐다보았으니, 마치 그가 화약과 탄환을 잔뜩 채워 넣은 걸어 다니는 파괴 병기라서, 언제라도 대뜸 저희 틈에서 터져 버릴 것만 같다는 듯했다.\n\n마님께는 그 집안 성미가 한 가닥—그 이상은 아니고—있었다. \"헌캐슬 대령한테 이르게.\" 내가 그 오라비의 전갈을 전해 드리니 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"버린더 아가씨는 볼일이 있고, 나는 그를 만나기를 거절한다고.\" 나는 그보다 좀 더 점잖은 대답을 올려 보시라고 아뢰어 보려 했으니, 여느 신사들을 다스리는 예법 따위를 대령이 천성적으로 대수롭잖게 여긴다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다. 아주 헛일이었다! 그 집안 성미가 대뜸 내게로 번쩍 튀었다. \"내가 자네 조언이 필요할 때는,\" 마님이 말씀하신다. \"늘 자네한테 청한다는 걸 알지 않나. 지금은 청하지 않네.\" 나는 그 전갈을 들고 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 주제넘게도 내 딴에 새로 손질한 개정판을 하나 지어내어 이렇게 올렸다. \"마님과 레이철 아가씨께서는 볼일이 있어 유감스러워하시며, 대령님, 뵙는 영광을 누리지 못함을 부디 양해해 주십사 하십니다.\"\n\n그토록 점잖게 둘러 말했는데도, 나는 그가 벌컥 화를 내리라 여겼다. 놀랍게도 그는 전혀 그러지 않았다. 그는 그 일을 부자연스러울 만큼 잠잠하게 받아들여 나를 오싹하게 했다. 번뜩이는 밝은 잿빛 눈이 잠깐 내게 머무는가 싶더니, 그는 웃었다. 남들처럼 밖으로 터뜨려 웃는 것이 아니라, 안으로 삼켜, 나직이 킥킥거리며, 소름 끼치도록 심술궂게. \"고맙네, 베터리지.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 조카의 생일을 잊지 않으마.\" 그러고는 발꿈치를 홱 돌려, 집을 걸어 나갔다.\n\n이듬해 생일이 돌아왔을 때, 우리는 그가 병들어 자리에 누웠다는 소식을 들었다. 여섯 달 뒤에—그러니까 지금 내가 적고 있는 시점으로부터 여섯 달 전에—더없이 지체 높은 어느 목사가 마님께 보낸 편지 한 통이 당도했다. 그것은 집안 소식 삼아 두 가지 놀라운 일을 알려 왔다. 첫째, 대령이 임종의 자리에서 제 누이를 용서했다는 것. 둘째, 그가 그 밖의 모든 사람도 용서했으며, 더없이 교화적인 최후를 맞았다는 것. 나 자신은 (주교들과 성직자들에도 불구하고) 교회에 대해 거짓 없는 존경을 품고 있다. 그러나 동시에 나는, 악마가 여전히 그 존 도련님을 아무 방해도 받지 않고 차지하고 있었으며, 그 몹쓸 사내의 삶에서 마지막 몹쓸 짓이란 (외람된 말씀이오나) 그 목사를 감쪽같이 속여 넘긴 것이었다고 굳게 믿는다!\n\n이것이 내가 프랭클린 도련님께 들려줄 이야기의 전부였다. 나는 이야기가 길어질수록 그가 점점 더 열심히 귀 기울이는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 또한, 대령이 제 조카의 생일을 맞아 누이의 문전에서 쫓겨났다는 그 대목이, 프랭클린 도련님을 마치 과녁에 명중한 총알처럼 후려치는 듯한 것도 눈여겨보았다. 그는 인정하지는 않았으나, 내가 그를 불안하게 만들었음을 그의 얼굴에서 똑똑히 읽을 수 있었다.\n\n\"자네는 할 말을 다 했군, 베터리지.\" 그가 말했다. \"이제 내 차례일세. 하지만, 내가 런던에서 무엇을 알아냈는지, 그리고 내가 어쩌다 이 다이아몬드 사건에 얽히게 되었는지 자네한테 말하기 전에, 한 가지 알고 싶은 게 있어. 이 사람아, 자네 얼굴을 보아하니, 우리의 이 의논으로 대체 무엇을 밝히려는 건지 통 갈피를 못 잡은 것 같군그래. 자네 얼굴이 거짓을 말하고 있는 건가?\"\n\n\"아닙니다요, 도련님.\" 나는 말했다. \"제 얼굴은, 적어도 이번만큼은, 사실을 말하고 있습니다요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"더 나아가기 전에, 자네한테 내 관점을 일러 주기로 하지. 나는 대령이 내 사촌 레이철에게 남긴 그 생일 선물에, 아주 중대한 물음이 셋 걸려 있다고 보네. 잘 따라오게, 베터리지. 그리고 도움이 되겠거든, 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 헤아려 보게.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제가 얼마나 명석해질 수 있는지 뽐내는 데서 어떤 즐거움을 느끼며 말하는데, 그것이 나로 하여금 그가 소년이던 옛 시절을 신기하리만치 떠오르게 했다. \"첫 번째 물음. 대령의 다이아몬드가 인도에서 어떤 음모의 표적이었는가? 두 번째 물음. 그 음모가 대령의 다이아몬드를 따라 영국까지 왔는가? 세 번째 물음. 대령은 그 음모가 다이아몬드를 뒤쫓는 줄 알고 있었는가—그리하여, 제 누이의 자식이라는 죄 없는 매개를 통해, 누이에게 골칫거리와 위험이라는 유산을 일부러 남긴 것인가? 바로 그것이 내가 겨누고 있는 바일세, 베터리지. 내가 자네를 겁주게 두진 말게.\"\n\n그렇게 말하는 거야 좋았지만, 그는 이미 나를 겁주고 만 터였다.\n\n만일 그의 말이 옳다면, 여기 우리의 조용한 영국 집이 별안간 악마 같은 인도 다이아몬드에 침범당한 셈이었다—죽은 자의 복수가 우리에게 풀어놓은, 시퍼렇게 살아 있는 악당들의 음모를 뒤에 거느린 채로. 프랭클린 도련님의 마지막 말이 내게 드러내 보인 우리의 처지가 바로 그러했다! 이런 일을 대체 누가 들어 본 적 있단 말인가—그것도, 새겨 두시라, 십구 세기에. 진보의 시대에, 그리고 영국 헌정의 축복을 누리며 기뻐하는 나라에서? 이런 일은 여태 아무도 들어 본 적이 없고, 따라서 아무에게도 이것을 믿으라고 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이다. 그렇지만 나는 그럼에도 내 이야기를 계속해 나가겠다.\n\n지금 내가 당한 그런 종류의 갑작스러운 놀라움이 닥치면, 열에 아홉은 그것을 느끼는 자리가 배 속이다. 배 속에서 그것을 느끼면, 주의가 산만해지고, 안절부절못하기 시작한다. 나는 모래 위 내 자리에서 소리 없이 안절부절못했다. 프랭클린 도련님은 내가 뒤숭숭한 배—아니면 마음, 어느 쪽이든 좋으시다. 그게 그거니까—와 씨름하고 있는 것을 눈치채고는, 막 제 몫의 이야기를 시작하려다 스스로 멈추고, 내게 퉁명스레 말했다. \"자네 뭐가 필요한가?\"\n\n내가 무엇이 필요했느냐고? 그에게는 말하지 않았다. 하지만 여러분께는, 은밀히, 말씀드리겠다. 나는 내 담뱃대 한 모금과, 「로빈슨 크루소」 한 대목이 필요했다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n내 속마음은 나 혼자 간직한 채, 나는 프랭클린 도련님께 공손히 계속하시라 청했다. 프랭클린 도련님은 \"안절부절못하지 말게, 베터리지.\" 하고 대꾸하더니, 이야기를 계속했다.\n\n우리 젊은 신사가 맨 처음 한 말로 나는 알게 되었으니, 그 사악한 대령과 다이아몬드에 관한 그의 알아냄은, 그가 (우리에게 오기 전에) 햄스테드에 사는 집안 변호사를 찾아간 데서 비롯되었다는 것이다. 어느 날 저녁 식사 뒤 둘만 있을 때 프랭클린 도련님이 무심코 흘린 한마디가, 그가 제 아버지로부터 레이철 아가씨께 전할 생일 선물을 맡았다는 사실을 드러냈다. 한 가지가 다른 한 가지로 이어졌고, 마침내 변호사가, 그 선물이 실제로 무엇인지, 그리고 지금은 고인이 되신 대령과 블레이크 씨, 그 어른 사이의 그 화기로운 인연이 어떻게 시작되었는지를 이야기하기에 이르렀다. 여기 그 사실들은 참으로 하도 기이하여, 나는 그것들을 온당히 옮겨 낼 만큼 내 말솜씨를 믿을 수 있을지 의심스럽다. 나는 차라리 프랭클린 도련님이 알아낸 것을, 되도록 프랭클린 도련님 자신의 말 그대로 전해 보려 한다.\n\n\"자네도 기억하겠지, 베터리지.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 아버지가 그 불운한 공작 작위에 대한 당신 권리를 증명하려 애쓰던 그 무렵을? 그런데! 바로 그 무렵이, 내 외숙부 헌캐슬이 인도에서 돌아온 때이기도 했어. 내 아버지는, 처남이 당신 소송에 도움이 될 법한 어떤 문서들을 가지고 있다는 것을 알아냈지. 그래서 아버지는, 영국으로 돌아온 것을 환영한다는 구실로 대령을 찾아갔어. 대령은 그런 식으로 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니었지. '자네 뭔가 바라는 게 있군.' 그가 말했어. '아니면 나 같은 사람을 찾아와 자네 체면을 구길 리가 없지.' 내 아버지는, 자기에게 남은 단 하나의 승산이 패를 까 보이는 것임을 알았어. 그래서 대뜸, 그 문서들을 원한다고 인정했지. 대령은 대답을 궁리할 하루 말미를 청했어. 그의 대답은 더없이 기이한 편지 한 통의 형태로 왔는데, 내 벗인 그 변호사가 내게 그 편지를 보여 주었어. 대령은 먼저, 자기도 내 아버지에게서 바라는 것이 있으며, 두 사람 사이에 서로 돕는 우호적 교환을 제안하고 싶다는 말로 시작했지. 전쟁의 운(그가 쓴 표현이 그러했다네)이 자기를 세상에서 가장 큰 다이아몬드 가운데 하나의 임자로 만들어 주었노라고. 그런데 자기든 그 귀한 보석이든, 그 둘이 함께 머무는 집이라면 지구상 어느 구석에 있든 안전하지 못하다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 있노라고. 이 불안한 형편에서, 자기는 그 다이아몬드를 다른 사람의 보관에 맡기기로 마음먹었다고. 그 사람은 아무 위험도 무릅쓸 필요가 없다고. 그저 그 귀한 보석을, 은행 금고나 보석상 금고처럼—값비싼 귀중품을 안전히 보관하도록 각별히 지키고 따로 마련해 둔—그런 아무 곳에나 맡겨 두면 된다고. 이 일에서 그 사람이 몸소 질 주된 책임이란 소극적인 종류의 것이라고. 그 사람은, 몸소든 아니면 자기가 세운 믿을 만한 대리인을 통해서든—미리 정한 주소로, 해마다 미리 정한 며칠에, 대령이 그 날짜에 살아 있는 사람임을 그저 알리는 쪽지 한 장을 받기만 하면 된다고. 만일 그 쪽지를 받지 못한 채 그 날짜가 넘어가면, 대령의 침묵은 대령이 살해되어 죽었다는 확실한 표지로 여겨도 좋다고. 그런 경우에, 오직 그런 경우에만, 다이아몬드의 처분에 관한, 그것과 함께 맡겨 둔 어떤 봉인된 지시서를 열어, 한 치도 어김없이 따르라고. 만일 내 아버지가 이 기이한 소임을 맡기로 한다면, 그 답례로 대령의 문서들을 아버지 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 하겠노라고. 그 편지가 그러했어.\"\n\n\"도련님 아버님은 어찌하셨습니까요?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"어찌했느냐고?\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"무얼 하셨는지 말해 주지. 아버지는, 상식이라 불리는 그 값을 매길 수 없는 능력을 대령의 편지에 들이대셨어. 이 모든 게 그저 터무니없는 소리라고 아버지는 단언하셨지. 대령이 인도를 떠돌던 어디쯤에서, 다이아몬드로 착각한 어떤 형편없는 수정 조각을 하나 주워 왔으리라는 게야. 살해당할 위험이니, 제 목숨과 제 수정 조각을 지키려고 궁리해 낸 방비책이니 하는 것으로 말하면, 지금은 십구 세기요, 제정신인 사람이라면 그저 경찰에 신고하기만 하면 될 노릇이라고. 대령은 여러 해 전부터 소문난 아편쟁이였으니, 그가 가진 그 귀중한 문서들을 손에 넣는 단 하나의 길이 아편쟁이의 헛소리를 엄연한 사실인 양 받아들이는 것이라면, 내 아버지는 자기에게 지워진 그 우스꽝스러운 책임을 기꺼이 떠맡을 용의가 있었어—더구나 그것이 자기한테는 아무 수고도 끼치지 않는 일이었으니 더욱 선뜻. 다이아몬드와 그 봉인된 지시서는 아버지 거래 은행의 금고로 들어갔고, 대령이 살아 있는 사람임을 주기적으로 알려 오는 그 편지들은, 아버지 대리인인 우리 집안 변호사 브러프 씨가 받아 열어 보았지. 비슷한 처지에 놓인 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구도 이 일을 달리 볼 수는 없었을 게야. 베터리지, 이 세상 무엇도 우리 자신의 하찮은 경험에 들어맞지 않는 한 그럴듯해 보이지 않는 법이고, 우리는 신문에서 읽어야 비로소 어떤 기담(奇談)을 믿는 법이니까.\"\n\n이로써 나는, 프랭클린 도련님이 그 대령에 관한 제 아버지의 생각을 성급하고 그릇된 것으로 여긴다는 것을 뻔히 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"그럼 이 일에 대한 도련님 자신의 속생각은 어떻습니까요?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"우선 대령 이야기부터 마저 끝내세.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"베터리지, 영국인의 머리에는 묘하게 체계가 없단 말이야. 그리고 이 사람아, 자네 그 물음이 바로 그 한 본보기일세. 우리는 기계를 만드느라 바쁘지 않을 때면, (정신으로 말하자면) 온 우주에서 가장 칠칠치 못한 사람들이거든.\"\n\n\"외국 교육이란 게 고작 이 모양이로군.\" 나는 속으로 생각했다. \"우리를 이렇게 빈정대는 법을 프랑스에서 배워 오셨겠지, 아마.\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 놓쳤던 이야기의 실마리를 도로 집어 들고 계속했다.\n\n\"내 아버지는,\" 그가 말했다. \"원하던 문서들을 얻고서, 그때부터 다시는 처남을 만나지 않으셨어. 해마다, 미리 정한 며칠에, 미리 정한 그 편지가 대령에게서 왔고, 브러프 씨가 그것을 열어 보았지. 나도 그 편지들을 한 무더기로 본 적이 있는데, 죄다 똑같이 짧고 사무적인 문투로 쓰여 있었어. '귀하—본인이 아직 살아 있는 사람임을 이로써 증명하는 바입니다. 다이아몬드는 그대로 두시오. 존 헌캐슬.' 그가 쓴 것이라곤 늘 그게 다였고, 그게 날짜에 맞춰 어김없이 왔지. 그러다 여섯 달인가 여덟 달쯤 전에, 그 편지의 문투가 처음으로 달라졌어. 이번엔 이랬지. '귀하—사람들이 내가 죽어 간다고 하오. 내게 와서, 유언장 작성을 도와주시오.' 브러프 씨가 갔고, 대령이, 인도를 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 저 혼자 살아온, 제 땅에 둘러싸인 그 조그만 교외 별장에 있는 것을 보았어. 그에게는 벗 삼을 개와 고양이와 새들은 있었으나, 곁에 가까이 있는 사람이라곤 날마다 와서 집안일을 해 주는 사람과 머리맡의 의사뿐이었지. 유언장은 아주 단순한 것이었어. 대령은 제 재산의 대부분을 화학 연구로 탕진해 버렸거든. 그의 유언은 세 조항으로 시작해서 세 조항으로 끝났는데, 정신을 온전히 지닌 채 침상에서 그것을 받아쓰게 했어. 첫째 조항은 제 짐승들을 안전히 건사하고 부양하는 데 관한 것이었어. 둘째 조항은 북부의 한 대학에 실험화학 강좌를 하나 세우는 것이었지. 셋째 조항은, 내 아버지가 유언 집행인 노릇을 한다는 조건으로, 월장석을 제 조카에게 생일 선물로 물려주는 것이었어. 내 아버지는 처음에는 맡기를 거절하셨어. 그러나 다시 생각한 끝에 뜻을 굽히셨는데, 한편으로는 그 집행인 노릇이 자기한테 아무 골칫거리도 끼치지 않으리라는 다짐을 받았기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 브러프 씨가, 레이철을 위해서 하는 말이라며, 그 다이아몬드가 그래도 얼마간 값어치가 있을지도 모른다고 넌지시 일렀기 때문이야.\"\n\n\"대령이, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"어째서 그 다이아몬드를 레이철 아가씨께 남겼는지 무슨 까닭이라도 댔습니까요?\"\n\n\"까닭을 댔을 뿐 아니라—그 까닭을 유언장에 적어 놓기까지 했어.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말했다. \"그 발췌본을 내가 하나 갖고 있으니, 이따가 자네도 보게 될 걸세. 칠칠치 못하게 굴지 말게, 베터리지! 한 번에 하나씩. 자네는 대령의 유언장에 관해 들었으니, 이제 대령이 죽은 뒤에 무슨 일이 있었는지 들어야 하네. 유언장을 검인받으려면, 먼저 그 다이아몬드의 값을 매겨 두는 것이 절차상 필요했어. 자문을 구한 보석상들은 하나같이, 세상에서 가장 큰 다이아몬드 가운데 하나를 지녔다던 대령의 장담을 대뜸 확인해 주었지. 그것의 값을 정확히 매기는 문제에는 여간 심각한 어려움이 따랐어. 그 크기는 그것을 다이아몬드 시장의 진기한 물건으로 만들었고, 그 빛깔은 그것을 그것만의 부류에 따로 세워 두었으며, 게다가 이 불확실한 요소들에 더하여, 그 돌 한복판에 흠 모양의 결함이 하나 있었거든. 그렇지만 이 마지막의 심각한 흠에도 불구하고, 매겨진 여러 감정가 가운데 가장 낮은 것이 이만 파운드였어. 내 아버지가 얼마나 놀라셨을지 헤아려 보게! 아버지는 하마터면 집행인 맡기를 거절하여, 이 굉장한 보석을 집안에서 잃어버리게 할 뻔했으니까. 이제 이 일에 품게 된 관심이, 아버지로 하여금 그 다이아몬드와 함께 맡겨 두었던 봉인된 지시서를 열어 보게 했어. 브러프 씨가 다른 문서들과 함께 이 서류를 내게 보여 주었는데, 그것은 (내 보기엔) 대령의 목숨을 위협하던 그 음모의 정체에 대한 한 실마리를 넌지시 던져 주고 있어.\"\n\n\"그럼 도련님은, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"정말로 음모가 있었다고 믿으시는 겁니까요?\"\n\n\"내 아버지의 그 훌륭한 상식을 지니지 못한지라,\" 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"나는 대령의 목숨이, 바로 대령이 말한 그대로, 위협당했다고 믿네. 그 봉인된 지시서가, 내 생각엔, 그가 그럼에도 결국 제 침상에서 조용히 죽은 것이 어찌 된 일인지를 설명해 주지. 그가 만일 폭력으로 죽는 경우에는(그러니까, 정한 날짜에 그에게서 정례 편지가 오지 않는 경우에는), 내 아버지더러 그 월장석을 은밀히 암스테르담으로 보내라고 지시해 두었어. 그 도시의 어느 이름난 다이아몬드 세공사에게 그것을 맡겨, 넷에서 여섯 개의 낱낱 돌로 쪼개게 하라고. 그런 다음 그 돌들을 받을 수 있는 값에 팔아, 그 판 돈을, 대령이 그 뒤 유언장으로 기부한 바로 그 실험화학 강좌를 세우는 데 쓰라고 했지. 자, 베터리지, 그 날카로운 재치를 어디 좀 부려서, 대령의 지시가 가리키는 결론을 살펴보게나!\"\n\n나는 대뜸 내 재치를 부렸다. 그런데 그것은 칠칠치 못한 영국식이라, 결국 온통 뒤죽박죽으로 만들어 놓고 말았으니, 프랭클린 도련님이 그것을 붙들어 마땅히 보아야 할 바를 짚어 주고서야 겨우 정리가 되었다.\n\n\"잘 보게.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"여기서 그 다이아몬드가 온전한 한 돌로서 지니는 완전함이, 대령의 목숨이 폭력에서 지켜지느냐에 교묘히 매여 있단 말이야. 그는 자기가 두려워하는 적들에게 이렇게 말하는 것으로 만족하지 않았어. '나를 죽여라—그래 봐야 너희는 지금보다 그 다이아몬드에 조금도 더 가까워지지 못한다. 그것은 너희가 손댈 수 없는 곳, 삼엄히 지키는 은행 금고 안에 있으니까.' 그는 그 대신 이렇게 말한 거지. '나를 죽여라—그러면 그 다이아몬드는 더 이상 그 다이아몬드가 아니게 된다. 그 정체가 파괴되고 말 테니까.' 그게 무슨 뜻이겠나?\"\n\n여기서 나는 (내 딴에는) 그 놀라운 외국식 명민함이 한 줄기 번쩍하는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"알겠습니다.\" 나는 말했다. \"그 돌의 값어치를 떨어뜨려서, 그런 식으로 그 악당들을 골탕 먹이겠다는 뜻이군요!\"\n\n\"전혀 그렇지 않아.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"내가 그 점을 알아봤거든. 흠 있는 그 다이아몬드는, 쪼개 놓으면, 지금 그대로일 때보다 실제로 더 값을 받을 걸세. 그 뻔한 까닭인즉—거기서 넷에서 여섯 개의 완벽한 광채석을 잘라 낼 수 있는데, 그것들을 다 합치면 크되 흠 있는 낱돌 하나보다 더 많은 돈이 될 테니까. 만일 이득을 노린 강탈이 그 음모의 밑바닥에 있었다면, 대령의 지시는 그야말로 그 다이아몬드를 훔칠 값어치가 더 나가도록 만든 셈이야. 그것으로 더 많은 돈을 받을 수 있었을 테고, 암스테르담 직공들의 손을 거쳤더라면 그것을 다이아몬드 시장에서 처분하기도 한없이 더 쉬웠을 테니까.\"\n\n\"아이고 맙소사, 도련님!\" 나는 불쑥 내뱉었다. \"그럼 대체 그 흉계가 무엇이었단 말입니까요?\"\n\n\"애초에 그 보석을 소유했던 인도인들 사이에서 꾸며진 흉계지.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다—\"그 밑바닥에 어떤 오래된 힌두교 미신이 깔린 흉계 말일세. 그게 내 생각인데, 지금 이 순간 내가 몸에 지니고 있는 한 집안 문서가 그것을 뒷받침해 주고 있어.\"\n\n나는 이제야, 우리 집에 그 인도인 요술쟁이 셋이 나타난 것이 어째서 프랭클린 도련님에게 눈여겨볼 만한 정황으로 비쳤는지 알 수 있었다.\n\n\"내 생각을 자네한테 억지로 들이밀 생각은 없네.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 계속했다. \"어떤 오래된 힌두교 미신을 받드는, 뽑힌 종복들이, 온갖 어려움과 위험을 무릅쓰고, 저희 성스러운 보석을 되찾을 기회를 엿보는 데 몸을 바친다는 발상은, 내가 보기엔, 동방 민족의 그 끈기와 동방 종교의 그 위력에 대해 우리가 아는 모든 것과 완벽하게 들어맞는단 말이야. 하지만 뭐, 나는 상상력이 풍부한 사람이니까. 그리고 내 머릿속에서는, 푸주한이며 빵집 주인이며 세금 걷는 이만이 믿을 만한 현실인 것도 아니고. 이 일의 진상에 대해 내가 해 본 짐작은 그저 그 값어치대로 여기기로 하고, 우리와 관계된 단 하나의 실제적인 물음으로 넘어가세. 월장석을 노린 그 음모가 대령의 죽음 뒤에도 살아남아 있는가? 그리고 대령은, 제 조카에게 그 생일 선물을 남길 때, 그 사실을 알고 있었는가?\"\n\n나는 이제야, 이 모든 것의 끄트머리에 마님과 레이철 아가씨가 계신 것을 보기 시작했다. 그가 하는 말은 한마디도 내게서 놓치지 않았다.\n\n\"나는, 월장석의 그 내력을 알아냈을 때,\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말했다. \"그것을 이리로 가져오는 노릇을 맡고 싶은 마음이 그리 없었어. 하지만 브러프 씨가, 누군가는 내 사촌의 유산을 내 사촌의 손에 쥐여 주어야 하는데—그 일을 남이 하나 내가 하나 매한가지 아니겠느냐고 일깨워 주더군. 은행에서 그 다이아몬드를 찾아 나온 뒤, 나는 거리에서 남루하고 거무튀튀한 사내 하나에게 미행당하는 것 같았어. 짐을 챙기러 아버지 댁에 갔더니 거기 편지가 한 통 있었는데, 그게 뜻밖에 나를 런던에 붙들어 두었지. 나는 그 다이아몬드를 도로 은행에 갖다 두었는데, 그 남루한 사내를 또 본 것 같았어. 오늘 아침 다시 한번 은행에서 그 다이아몬드를 찾아 나오다가, 나는 그 사내를 세 번째로 보았고, 그를 따돌린 뒤, (그가 내 자취를 되찾기 전에) 오후 기차 대신 아침 기차로 출발한 거야. 이렇게 나는, 다이아몬드를 멀쩡히 잘 간수한 채 여기 와 있어—그런데 나를 맞이한 첫 소식이 무엇인 줄 아나? 알고 보니 떠돌이 인도인 셋이 이 집에 다녀갔고, 내가 런던에서 오는 일과, 내가 몸에 지니고 있으리라 여겨지는 어떤 것이, 저희끼리만 있다고 믿을 때 그자들이 캐고 드는 두 가지 특별한 표적이라지 뭔가. 그자들이 소년의 손에 잉크를 붓고, 그 속을 들여다보아 멀리 있는 사람과 그 사람 주머니 속 무언가를 찾아보라고 시켰다는 것에는, 나도 굳이 시간과 말을 허비하지 않겠네. 그런 짓은 (동방에서 여러 번 행해지는 것을 봤지만) 내 생각에도, 자네 생각과 마찬가지로, '눈속임'이니까. 지금 우리가 판가름해야 할 물음은 이걸세. 내가 한낱 우연에 부질없이 어떤 의미를 갖다 붙이고 있는 건가? 아니면 우리는, 그 다이아몬드가 은행의 안전한 보관에서 벗어나는 바로 그 순간 인도인들이 월장석의 자취를 뒤쫓고 있다는 진짜 증거를 쥔 건가?\"\n\n그도 나도, 이 조사의 이 대목을 다루고 싶은 마음은 없어 보였다. 우리는 서로를 마주 보았고, 그러고는, 떠는 모래펄 위로 점점 더 높이 매끄럽게 스며드는 밀물을 바라보았다.\n\n\"무슨 생각을 하고 있나?\" 프랭클린 도련님이 불쑥 말한다.\n\n\"저는 생각하고 있었습니다요, 도련님.\" 나는 대답했다. \"그 다이아몬드를 저 유사에 던져 넣어, 그런 식으로 이 문제를 매듭지어 버리면 좋겠다고요.\"\n\n\"그 돌의 값어치를 자네 주머니에서 물어 낼 수 있거든,\" 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"그렇다고 말하게, 베터리지. 그러면 냅다 던져 넣지!\"\n\n마음이 불안할 때, 아주 조그만 농담 하나가 마음을 덜어 주는 데 얼마나 멀리까지 가 닿는지는 눈여겨볼 만한 노릇이다. 우리는 그때, 레이철 아가씨의 정당한 재산을 없애 버려 유언 집행인인 블레이크 씨를 끔찍한 곤경에 빠뜨린다는 생각에 한바탕 신명이 났으니—대체 그 신명 날 거리가 어디 있었는지는, 지금 와서는 나도 도무지 찾아낼 길이 없다만.\n\n이야기를 그 본래 목적으로 도로 끌어온 것은 프랭클린 도련님이 먼저였다. 그는 제 주머니에서 봉투를 하나 꺼내 열더니, 그 안에 든 종이를 내게 건넸다.\n\n\"베터리지.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리는 내 이모님을 위해서라도, 대령이 제 조카에게 이 유산을 남긴 동기라는 물음을 마주해야 하네. 대령이 영국으로 돌아온 때부터, 제 조카의 생일을 잊지 않겠노라 자네한테 말한 때까지, 레이디 버린더가 그 오라비를 어떻게 대했는지 되새겨 보게. 그러고 나서 이걸 읽어 보게.\"\n\n그는 대령의 유언장에서 뽑은 발췌본을 내게 주었다. 나는 이 글을 쓰는 지금도 그것을 곁에 두고 있으며, 여러분을 위해 그것을 아래와 같이 옮겨 적는다.\n\n\"셋째로, 그리고 마지막으로, 나는 내 조카 레이철 버린더, 곧 내 누이인 미망인 줄리아 버린더의 딸이자 외동 자식인 그에게—만일 그 어머니, 곧 앞서 말한 줄리아 버린더가 내가 죽은 뒤 앞서 말한 레이철 버린더의 다음 생일에 살아 있다면—나에게 속한, 그리고 동방에서 월장석이라는 이름으로 알려진 그 노란 다이아몬드를 주고 물려준다. 다만 그 어머니, 곧 앞서 말한 줄리아 버린더가 그때에 살아 있어야 한다는 이 조건에 따른다. 그리고 나는 이로써 내 유언 집행인에게, 내가 죽은 뒤 다음 생일에, 되도록이면 내 누이, 곧 앞서 말한 줄리아 버린더가 지켜보는 가운데, 내 다이아몬드를 제 손으로든 아니면 제가 세운 어떤 믿을 만한 대리인의 손으로든, 앞서 말한 내 조카 레이철의 몸소 소유로 넘겨줄 것을 바란다. 또한 나는, 내가 그 딸 레이철에게 이 다이아몬드를 주는 것이, 그의 행실이 내 생전에 내 명예에 입힌 상처를 내가 기꺼이 용서하는 표시임을—특히, 그 딸의 생일을 맞아 그가 제 하인을 시켜 제 집 문을 내 앞에서 닫아걸었을 때, 한 사람의 장교이자 신사인 나에게 가한 그 모욕을, 죽어 가는 사람답게 내가 용서한다는 증거임을—내 유언의 이 셋째이자 마지막 조항의 진정한 사본을 통해, 앞서 말한 내 누이에게 알려 주기를 바란다.\"\n\n이 뒤로 몇 마디가 더 이어졌는데, 만일 유언자가 죽을 때 마님이 이미 죽었거나 레이철 아가씨가 이미 죽은 경우에는, 애초에 그 다이아몬드와 함께 맡겨 둔 봉인된 지시서에 따라 그것을 네덜란드로 보내라는 내용이었다. 그 경우, 판 돈은 이미 유언장으로 북부 대학의 화학 강좌에 남긴 돈에 보태도록 되어 있었다.\n\n나는 그 종이를 프랭클린 도련님께 도로 건네주며, 그에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몹시 난감했다. 그 순간까지, 내 나름의 생각은 (여러분도 아시다시피) 대령이 살아온 그대로 사악하게 죽었으리라는 것이었다. 그 유언장 사본이 그 생각에서 나를 실제로 돌아서게 했다고까지는 말하지 않겠다. 다만 그것이 나를 휘청하게 했다고만 말하겠다.\n\n\"자.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"이제 자네도 대령 자신의 진술을 읽었으니, 어떻게 생각하나? 그 월장석을 내 이모님 댁으로 가져오는 것으로, 나는 눈을 가린 채 그의 복수에 이바지하고 있는 건가, 아니면 뉘우치는 기독교인이라는 사람됨으로 그의 명예를 되세우고 있는 건가?\"\n\n\"말하기 어려워 보입니다요, 도련님.\" 나는 대답했다. \"그가 가슴에는 끔찍한 복수심을, 입술에는 끔찍한 거짓말을 담은 채 죽었다고요. 진상은 오직 하느님만이 아십니다. 저한테는 묻지 마십시오.\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님은, 마치 그런 식으로 진상을 쥐어짜 낼 수 있으리라 여기는 듯, 그 유언장 발췌본을 손가락으로 배배 돌리고 뒤집으며 앉아 있었다. 그와 동시에 그는 사뭇 눈에 띄게 달라졌다. 활기차고 명민하던 데서, 이제 그는, 참으로 알 수 없게도, 굼뜨고 근엄하고 골똘한 젊은이가 되어 버렸다.\n\n\"이 물음에는 두 측면이 있어.\" 그가 말했다. \"객관적 측면과, 주관적 측면 말이야. 우리는 어느 쪽을 택할까?\"\n\n그는 프랑스식뿐 아니라 독일식 교육도 받은 터였다. (내 짐작으로는) 그 둘 중 하나가 이때까지 그를 아무 방해 없이 차지하고 있었다. 그런데 이제 (내가 짐작할 수 있는 한) 다른 하나가 그 자리를 대신 차지하는 참이었다. 내가 이해하지 못하는 것에는 결코 아랑곳하지 않는 것이, 내 인생의 규칙 가운데 하나다. 나는 객관적 측면과 주관적 측면 사이에서 중도를 잡았다. 쉬운 우리말로 하면, 나는 눈만 멀뚱멀뚱 뜨고 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n\"이것의 속뜻을 뽑아내 보세.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"어째서 내 외숙부는 그 다이아몬드를 레이철에게 남겼을까? 어째서 그것을 내 이모님께 남기지 않았을까?\"\n\n\"그거야 적어도 짐작 못 할 일은 아니지요, 도련님.\" 나는 말했다. \"헌캐슬 대령은, 그에게서 오는 유산이라면 무엇이든 마님이 받기를 거절하시리라는 것을 알 만큼 마님을 잘 알고 있었으니까요.\"\n\n\"레이철도 그것을 받기를 거절할지 모른다는 걸, 그는 어떻게 알았을까?\"\n\n\"세상에, 도련님, 월장석 같은 생일 선물을 받고 싶은 유혹을 이겨 낼 수 있는 젊은 아가씨가 어디 있겠습니까요?\"\n\n\"그게 바로 주관적 관점일세.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"주관적 관점을 취할 줄 알다니, 베터리지, 자네 참 대단하군그래. 하지만 대령의 그 유산에는 아직 설명되지 않은 또 다른 수수께끼가 있어. 그가 레이철에게 생일 선물을 주되 오직 그 어머니가 살아 있어야 한다는 조건을 단 것을, 우리는 어떻게 설명해야 할까?\"\n\n\"죽은 사람을 헐뜯고 싶지는 않습니다요, 도련님.\" 나는 대답했다. \"하지만 그가 만일, 제 누이의 자식을 매개 삼아, 골칫거리와 위험이라는 유산을 누이에게 일부러 남긴 것이라면, 그건 틀림없이 누이가 살아서 그 속을 썩일 수 있어야 한다는 조건을 단 유산이겠지요.\"\n\n\"오! 그게 그의 동기에 대한 자네 풀이란 말이지? 또 그 주관적 풀이로군! 자네 독일에 가 본 적 있나, 베터리지?\"\n\n\"없습니다요, 도련님. 그럼 도련님의 풀이는 어떻습니까, 괜찮으시다면?\"\n\n\"내가 보기엔,\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"대령의 목적이, 얼마든지 이럴 수도 있어—얼굴 한번 본 적조차 없는 제 조카에게 은혜를 베풀려던 게 아니라, 제가 누이를 용서하며 죽었음을 누이에게 증명해 보이려던 것, 그것도 누이의 자식에게 준 선물을 통해 아주 곱게 증명해 보이려던 것 말이야. 이건 자네 것과는 전혀 다른 설명일세, 베터리지. 주관적-객관적 관점에서 비롯된 설명이지. 내가 볼 수 있는 한, 어느 한쪽 풀이나 다른 한쪽 풀이나 옳을 법하기는 매한가지야.\"\n\n이렇게 일을 이 유쾌하고 위안이 되는 결말로 이끌어 놓고서, 프랭클린 도련님은 제게 요구된 바를 죄다 마쳤다고 여기는 듯했다. 그는 모래 위에 벌렁 나자빠져 등을 대고 눕더니, 다음엔 무엇을 하면 되느냐고 물었다.\n\n그는 (그 외국식 횡설수설을 늘어놓기 전까지는) 어찌나 영리하고 명민했던지, 또 지금껏 이 일에서 어찌나 완전히 앞장서 왔던지, 이제 이렇게 나한테 속수무책으로 기대는 이 갑작스러운 변화에 나는 전혀 마음의 채비가 되어 있지 않았다. 프랭클린 도련님의 이 갈피 못 잡을 변덕과 뒤바뀜이 그가 받은 외국 수련이 그에게 미친 영향 탓임을—레이철 아가씨의 도움으로 알게 되었는데, 이 사실을 맨 먼저 알아낸 이가 아가씨였다—내가 알게 된 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다. 우리가 저마다 가장 쉽게, 남들의 물이 비치듯 그 물을 받아 제 물을 들이는 그 나이에, 그는 외국으로 보내졌고, 어느 한 가지 물이 다른 물보다 그에게 단단히 자리 잡을 겨를도 없이 이 나라에서 저 나라로 넘겨졌다. 그 탓에 그는, 저마다 서로 얼마간 어긋나는 하도 여러 다른 면을 제 됨됨이에 지닌 채 돌아왔으니, 마치 저 자신과 끝없이 모순을 이루는 상태로 평생을 사는 듯했다. 그는 부지런한 사람일 수도, 게으른 사람일 수도 있었다. 머리가 흐릿할 수도, 머리가 맑을 수도 있었다. 결단의 본보기일 수도, 속수무책의 볼거리일 수도 있었으니, 그 모든 게 한꺼번에였다. 그에게는 프랑스식 면이 있고, 독일식 면이 있고, 이탈리아식 면이 있었다—그리고 그 밑바탕의 본디 영국식이 이따금 비쳐 나와, 이를테면 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"나 여기 있소. 보다시피 지독히 뒤바뀌긴 했지만, 그래도 이 사람 밑바닥엔 아직 내 것이 얼마쯤 남아 있다오.\" 레이철 아가씨는 늘 말씀하시기를, 그가 뜻밖에 굽히고 들며 그 곱고 상냥한 투로 제 책임을 남의 어깨에 지워 달라 청할 때면, 그의 이탈리아식 면이 가장 위로 올라온 것이라 하셨다. 지금 그의 이탈리아식 면이 가장 위로 올라와 있었다고 여기신들, 내 생각엔, 여러분이 그를 부당하게 여기는 것은 아니리라.\n\n\"다음에 무엇을 할지 아는 게, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"도련님이 하실 일 아닙니까요? 설마 그게 제 일일 리는 없겠지요?\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 내 물음의 무게를 알아채지 못하는 듯했다—그때 그는 제 머리 위 하늘 말고는 아무것도 볼 처지가 아니었으니까.\n\n\"까닭 없이 이모님을 놀라게 해 드리고 싶진 않아.\" 그가 말했다. \"그렇다고 어쩌면 꼭 필요할지도 모를 경고 없이 이모님을 내버려 두고 싶지도 않고. 자네가 내 처지라면, 베터리지, 한마디로 말해 보게, 자네라면 어찌하겠나?\"\n\n한마디로, 나는 그에게 말했다. \"기다리시지요.\"\n\n\"내 진심으로 그러겠네.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"얼마나?\"\n\n나는 내 뜻을 풀어 설명해 나갔다.\n\n\"제가 이해하기로는, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"누군가는 이 성가신 다이아몬드를 레이철 아가씨의 생일에 아가씨 손에 쥐여 드려야 하는데—그 일을 남이 하나 도련님이 하나 매한가지지요. 아주 좋습니다. 오늘이 5월 25일이고, 생일은 6월 21일입니다. 우리한테는 넉 주 가까운 시간이 있어요. 그동안 무슨 일이 일어나는지 기다려 보시지요. 그리고 마님께 경고를 드리든 안 드리든, 형편이 이끄는 대로 하시고요.\"\n\n\"완벽해, 베터리지, 거기까지는!\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"하지만 지금부터 그 생일까지, 그 다이아몬드는 어떻게 해 둔다지?\"\n\n\"도련님 아버님이 그것으로 하신 대로 하셔야지요, 그야!\" 나는 대답했다. \"도련님 아버님은 그것을 런던의 한 은행에 안전히 맡겨 두셨어요. 도련님은 그것을 프리징홀의 은행에 안전히 맡겨 두시고요.\" (프리징홀은 우리 집에서 가장 가까운 읍이었고, 잉글랜드 은행이라 한들 거기 은행보다 더 안전할 것도 없었다.) \"제가 도련님이라면요, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 덧붙였다. \"숙녀분들이 돌아오시기 전에 그것을 가지고 곧장 프리징홀로 말을 달려가겠습니다요.\"\n\n무언가를 한다는 것—더구나, 그것도 말을 타고 한다는 것—의 전망이, 프랭클린 도련님을 등을 대고 누운 자리에서 번갯불처럼 벌떡 일으켜 세웠다. 그는 두 발로 벌떡 뛰어 일어나더니, 다짜고짜 나를 붙들어 내 두 발로 일으켜 세웠다. \"베터리지, 자네는 제 몸무게만큼 금값을 하는 사람이야.\" 그가 말했다. \"자, 어서 가서 마구간에서 가장 좋은 말에 당장 안장을 얹게.\"\n\n여기 (하느님 감사합니다!) 그 온갖 외국물의 광택을 뚫고 마침내 그의 그 본디 영국식 밑바탕이 비쳐 나오고 있었다! 여기, 내가 기억하는 그 프랭클린 도련님이, 말을 탄다는 전망에 그 정겨운 옛 모습으로 다시 튀어나와, 내게 그 정겨운 옛 시절을 떠올려 주고 있었다! 그에게 말 한 필에 안장을 얹어 주라고? 그가 그 말을 죄다 탈 수만 있다면, 나는 열두 필에라도 안장을 얹어 주었으리라!\n\n우리는 부랴부랴 집으로 돌아갔다. 우리는 부랴부랴 마구간에서 가장 날랜 말에 안장을 얹었다. 그리고 프랭클린 도련님은, 그 저주받은 다이아몬드를 또 한 번 은행 금고에 맡겨 두려고, 부랴부랴 말을 달려 떠나갔다. 그의 말발굽 소리가 마찻길에서 마지막으로 잦아드는 것을 듣고, 마당에서 몸을 돌려 다시 나 홀로임을 알았을 때, 나는 내가 혹 꿈에서 깨어난 것은 아닌지 나 자신에게 물어보고 싶은 심정이 반쯤 들었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n이렇게 얼떨떨한 심경에 잠겨, 나 자신을 도로 추스르려면 나 혼자 조용한 시간이 좀 절실히 필요하던 참에, 내 딸 페넬로피가 내 앞을 가로막고서 (꼭 제 죽은 어미가 계단에서 내 앞을 가로막곤 하던 것처럼) 대뜸, 프랭클린 도련님과 나 사이의 그 담판에서 오간 이야기를 죄다 저한테 들려 달라고 졸랐다. 지금 형편에서 할 수 있는 단 한 가지는, 페넬로피의 호기심에 그 자리에서 뚜껑을 덮어 버리는 것이었다. 그리하여 나는, 프랭클린 도련님과 내가 둘 다 외국 정치 이야기를 하도 오래 나누다가 더는 할 말이 없어져서, 그다음엔 볕이 뜨거워 서로 잠이 들어 버렸다고 대답했다. 다음번에 여러분의 아내나 딸이 곤란한 때에 곤란한 물음으로 여러분을 들볶거든 그런 식의 대답을 한번 써 보시라. 그리고 다음 기회에 입 맞추며 다시 화해하는 일일랑 여자의 그 타고난 상냥함에 맡겨 두시라.\n\n오후가 저물어 가고, 마님과 레이철 아가씨가 돌아오셨다.\n\n프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 왔다가 다시 말을 타고 떠나 버렸다는 말을 들었을 때 두 분이 얼마나 놀라셨는지는 굳이 말할 것도 없다. 또한, 그 두 분이 대뜸 곤란한 물음들을 던지셨다는 것도, 그리고 \"외국 정치\"니 \"볕에 잠이 들었다\"느니 하는 것이 그 두 분한테는 두 번씩이나 통하지 않았다는 것도, 굳이 말할 것도 없다. 내 꾀가 바닥이 나서, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 이른 기차로 온 것은 순전히 프랭클린 도련님 특유의 변덕 탓이라고 둘러댔다. 그러자, 그가 다시 말을 타고 내달려 간 것도 프랭클린 도련님의 또 다른 변덕이냐고 물으시기에, 나는 \"예, 그렇습니다.\" 하고는—내 생각엔 아주 영리하게—그런 식으로 슬쩍 빠져나갔다.\n\n숙녀분들과의 곤경을 넘기고 나니, 내 방으로 돌아왔을 때 더 많은 곤경이 나를 기다리고 있었다. 페넬로피가 들어왔다—여자의 그 타고난 상냥함으로 다시 입 맞추며 화해하려고, 그리고—여자의 그 타고난 호기심으로 또 하나의 물음을 던지려고. 이번엔 그저, 우리 둘째 하녀 로재나 스피어먼이 대체 무슨 일이냐고 내게 말해 달라는 것이었다.\n\n떠는 모래펄에서 프랭클린 도련님과 나를 두고 떠난 뒤, 로재나는, 알고 보니, 참으로 알 수 없는 심경으로 집에 돌아왔더란다. 그녀는 (페넬로피의 말을 믿는다면) 무지개 빛깔을 죄다 띠었다. 까닭 없이 들떴다가, 까닭 없이 시무룩했다. 한 숨결로는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에 관해 수백 가지 물음을 던지다가, 또 한 숨결로는, 낯선 신사가 저한테 무슨 관심거리가 될 수 있으리라 지레짐작한다고 페넬로피에게 화를 냈다. 놀란 얼굴로 미소를 지으며 제 반짇고리 속에 프랭클린 도련님의 이름을 끄적거리기도 했다. 또 놀란 얼굴로 울면서 거울에 비친 제 기형인 어깨를 들여다보기도 했다. 그녀와 프랭클린 도련님이 오늘 이전에 서로에 대해 무언가 알고 있었을까? 도무지 있을 수 없는 일이었다! 서로에 대해 무언가 들은 적이 있었을까? 이 또한 있을 수 없는 일이었다! 나는, 그 처녀가 저를 빤히 쳐다보는 것을 보았을 때 프랭클린 도련님이 지은 놀라움이 진짜였다고 증언할 수 있다. 페넬로피는, 그녀가 프랭클린 도련님에 관해 물었을 때 그 캐묻는 마음이 진짜였다고 증언할 수 있었다. 이런 식으로 이어진 우리 사이의 담판은 여간 지겨운 것이 아니었는데, 마침내 내 딸이, 내 평생 들어 본 것 중 가장 터무니없다 싶은 억측을 불쑥 내뱉으며 그것을 끝장내 버렸다.\n\n\"아버지!\" 페넬로피가, 자못 진지하게 말한다. \"이건 설명이 딱 하나뿐이에요. 로재나가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨한테 첫눈에 반해 버린 거라고요!\"\n\n아름다운 젊은 아가씨가 첫눈에 사랑에 빠졌다는 이야기라면 여러분도 들어 보셨을 테고, 그것을 제법 자연스러운 일로 여기셨을 것이다. 그러나 갱생원 출신에, 못생긴 얼굴에, 기형인 어깨를 가진 하녀가, 제 여주인 집에 손님으로 온 신사한테 첫눈에 사랑에 빠졌다니—이만한 황당함을, 온 기독교 세계의 그 어떤 이야기책에서든 어디 한번 나한테 견주어 대 보시라, 하실 수 있거든! 나는 두 뺨에 눈물이 흘러내리도록 웃었다. 페넬로피는 내 웃음을, 자못 묘한 식으로 언짢아했다. \"아버지가 이렇게 모진 분인 줄은 여태 몰랐어요.\" 그 애는 아주 나직이 말하고는, 나가 버렸다.\n\n내 딸의 말이 마치 찬물 한 바가지처럼 내게 끼얹어졌다. 그 애가 그 말을 내뱉은 순간 내 속이 어쩐지 편치 않아지는 것에, 나는 나 자신에게 화가 났다—그러나 사실이 그러했다. 여러분이 괜찮으시다면, 우리 이 이야기는 그만 접기로 하자. 내가 어쩌다 이 이야기를 쓰게 되어 미안하다. 그리고 그것이 까닭 없는 미안함이 아님은, 우리가 조금 더 함께 나아가 보면 여러분도 아시게 될 것이다.\n\n저녁이 되었고, 프랭클린 도련님이 프리징홀에서 돌아오기 전에 저녁 식사를 알리는 옷 갈아입을 종이 울렸다. 나는 그의 세숫물을 손수 그의 방으로 들고 올라가며, 이 유별난 지체 끝에, 무슨 일이라도 일어났다는 말을 듣게 되려니 여겼다. 나로서는 여간 실망스럽게도 (그리고 틀림없이 여러분에게도), 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 그는 갈 때도 올 때도 그 인도인들과 마주치지 않았다. 그는 그 월장석을 은행에 맡겼고—그저 값비싼 귀중품이라고만 이르고서—그 보관증을 제 주머니에 안전히 챙겨 넣었다. 나는 아래층으로 내려가며, 낮에 그 다이아몬드를 두고 그토록 들떴던 것에 비하면 이건 어지간히 맥 빠지는 결말이라는 생각이 들었다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님과 그 이모, 그리고 사촌 사이의 만남이 어떻게 흘러갔는지는, 내가 여러분께 말씀드릴 수 있는 바를 넘어선다.\n\n그날 식탁 시중을 들 수만 있었다면 나는 무엇이라도 내놓았을 것이다. 그러나 이 집에서 내 자리로 말하면, 저녁 식사 시중을 드는 일은 (집안의 큰 잔치가 아니고서는) 다른 하인들 눈에 내 위엄을 떨어뜨리는 짓이었다—마님이 여기시기로, 내가 굳이 그럴 기회를 찾아 나서지 않아도 이미 넉넉히 그러기 쉬운 사람이었으니 말이다. 그날 저녁 위쪽 세상에서 내게 전해진 소식은, 페넬로피와 하인에게서 온 것이었다. 페넬로피의 말이, 레이철 아가씨가 그토록 머리 손질에 까다롭게 구시는 것은 여태 본 적이 없고, 응접실로 프랭클린 도련님을 맞으러 내려가실 때만큼 그토록 환하고 어여쁘신 것도 여태 본 적이 없다는 것이었다. 하인의 보고인즉, 윗분들 앞에서 공손한 침착함을 지키는 일과, 저녁 식탁에서 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 시중을 드는 일, 이 둘을 서로 아우르는 것이야말로 여태껏 제 시중 수련을 시험한 가장 어려운 일 축에 든다는 것이었다. 저녁이 이슥해지자, 우리는 그분들이 노래하고 이중주를 연주하는 소리를 들었으니, 프랭클린 도련님은 높이 소리를 뽑고, 레이철 아가씨는 더 높이 뽑고, 마님은 피아노로 그분들을 이를테면 산울타리와 도랑을 넘도록 뒤따르며, 밤의 테라스에서 열린 창을 통해 듣기에 더없이 놀랍고 유쾌하게, 그분들을 무사히 그 곡을 헤쳐 나가게 이끄셨다. 그보다 더 이슥해서, 나는 소다수와 브랜디를 들고 흡연실의 프랭클린 도련님께 갔는데, 레이철 아가씨가 그 다이아몬드를 그의 머릿속에서 말끔히 몰아내 버린 것을 알았다. 내가 이야기를 좀 더 진지한 쪽으로 이끌어 보려 애썼을 때 그에게서 끄집어낼 수 있었던 것이라곤 \"그녀는 내가 영국에 돌아온 뒤로 본 가장 매혹적인 아가씨야!\"라는 말뿐이었다.\n\n자정 무렵, 나는 여느 때처럼 내 부관(하인 새뮤얼)을 데리고, 문단속을 하러 집을 한 바퀴 돌았다. 테라스로 통하는 옆문만 빼고 모든 문을 단단히 잠근 뒤, 나는 새뮤얼을 자러 보내고, 나도 내 차례로 잠자리에 들기 전에 바깥바람을 좀 쐬려고 밖으로 나섰다.\n\n밤은 고요하고 후텁지근했으며, 하늘에는 보름달이 걸려 있었다. 바깥이 어찌나 괴괴하던지, 나는 이따금, 아주 희미하고 나직하게, 너울이 우리 작은 만 어귀 가까이 모래톱으로 물을 밀어 올릴 때 바닷물이 내려앉는 소리를 들었다. 집이 놓인 모양새로 보면, 테라스 쪽이 어두운 쪽이었으나, 넓은 달빛이 테라스 다음 쪽을 따라 난 자갈길 위에 훤히 비치고 있었다. 하늘을 올려다본 뒤 이쪽을 바라보니, 집 모퉁이 뒤에서 앞으로 드리워진 사람 그림자 하나가 달빛 속에 보였다.\n\n나이 든 데다 능글맞은지라, 나는 소리쳐 부르는 것을 참았다. 그러나 불행히도 나이 든 데다 몸도 무거운지라, 자갈길에서 내 발이 나를 배신했다. 내가 애초 작정한 대로 그 모퉁이를 슬며시 돌아 나가기도 전에, 나는 내 것보다 가벼운 발소리가—게다가 내 생각엔 한 사람이 아닌 여럿의 발소리가—부랴부랴 물러나는 것을 들었다. 내가 그 모퉁이에 이르렀을 무렵, 그 무단 침입자들은, 그게 누구든 간에, 길 저쪽 관목숲으로 뛰어들어, 영지 그 언저리의 우거진 나무와 덤불 사이로 자취를 감춘 뒤였다. 그 관목숲에서라면, 그들은 우리 울타리를 넘어 손쉽게 한길로 빠져나갈 수 있었다. 내가 마흔 살만 젊었던들, 그들이 우리 집 경내를 벗어나기 전에 붙잡을 승산이 있었을지도 모른다. 실상은 그러하여, 나는 내 것보다 젊은 두 다리를 부리려고 되돌아갔다. 아무도 깨우지 않은 채, 새뮤얼과 나는 총을 두 자루 챙겨 들고, 집을 한 바퀴 돌고 관목숲을 헤집었다. 우리 영지 어디에도 숨어 있는 자가 없음을 확인하고서, 우리는 발길을 돌렸다. 아까 그 그림자를 보았던 길을 지나다가, 나는 이제야 처음으로, 달빛 아래 깨끗한 자갈 위에 놓인 조그맣고 반짝이는 물건 하나를 알아보았다. 그 물건을 집어 들어 보니, 그것은 잉크처럼 새까맣고 걸쭉하며 달콤한 냄새가 나는 액체가 담긴 조그만 병이었다.\n\n나는 새뮤얼에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그러나 페넬로피가 그 요술쟁이들에 관해, 그리고 소년의 손바닥에 조그맣게 잉크를 고이도록 부었다던 이야기를 내게 들려준 것을 떠올리며, 나는 대뜸 의심했다. 내가, 집 언저리에 숨어, 저희 이교도다운 방식으로 그날 밤 그 다이아몬드가 어디 있는지 알아내려 벼르던 그 인도인 셋을 방해한 것이라고.\n\n여기서, 잠깐, 나는 한 번 멈춰 서야겠다.\n\n내 나름의 기억을 더듬어 보고—그리고 페넬로피더러 제 일기를 뒤져 나를 돕게 한 결과—나는, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 도착과 레이철 아가씨의 생일 사이의 그 사이 기간은 제법 빠르게 지나쳐도 되겠다는 것을 알았다. 그 시간의 대부분은 날들이 그저 지나갔을 뿐, 적어 둘 만한 것을 하나도 데려오지 않았다. 그러니 여러분이 허락하신다면, 그리고 페넬로피의 도움을 받아, 나는 이 대목에서는 몇몇 날짜만 짚어 두려 한다. 월장석 사건이 우리 집 모든 사람의 첫째가는 큰일이 된 그 시점에 이르면, 다시 한번 날마다 하나하나 이야기해 나가는 몫은 나 자신을 위해 남겨 두기로 하고.\n\n이 말을 해 두었으니, 이제 우리 다시 나아가도 되겠다—물론, 내가 밤에 자갈길에서 주운 그 달콤한 냄새 나는 잉크병에서부터 시작하기로 하고.\n\n이튿날 아침(26일 아침)에 나는 프랭클린 도련님께 이 요술 물건을 보여 주며, 내가 이미 여러분께 들려드린 것을 그에게 이야기했다. 그의 생각인즉, 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 노려 언저리에 숨어 있었을 뿐 아니라, 실제로 어리석게도 저희 요술을 스스로 믿을 만큼이었다는 것이다—여기서 요술이란, 소년의 머리에 표시를 하고 소년의 손에 잉크를 붓고서, 그런 다음 그 아이가 사람의 눈이 미치지 못하는 곳의 사람과 사물을 보아 주기를 기대하는 것을 말한다. 프랭클린 도련님이 일러 주기로, 동방뿐 아니라 우리나라에도 이 괴상한 눈속임을 부리는 사람들이 있는데 (다만 잉크는 없이), 그들은 그것을 프랑스말로 부르며, 그것은 이를테면 밝은 눈이라는 뜻이라고 했다. \"틀림없어.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"인도인들은 우리가 그 다이아몬드를 여기 둘 것이라고 당연시했어. 그래서 어젯밤 집 안에 들어드는 데 성공하면 그 투시하는 소년더러 다이아몬드로 가는 길을 알려 달라고 데려온 거지.\"\n\n\"그자들이 또 시도할 것 같습니까요, 도련님?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"그거야 달렸지.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"그 소년이 실제로 무엇을 할 수 있느냐에. 만일 그 애가 프리징홀 은행의 그 철제 금고를 뚫고 다이아몬드를 볼 수 있다면, 우리는 당분간 인도인들의 방문에 더는 시달리지 않을 걸세. 만일 못 본다면, 우리 머리 위로 여러 밤이 더 지나가기 전에, 관목숲에서 그자들을 붙잡을 또 한 번의 기회가 있을 게고.\"\n\n나는 그 뒤엣것, 곧 붙잡을 기회를 제법 자신 있게 기다렸다. 그러나, 이상하게도, 그것은 끝내 오지 않았다.\n\n요술쟁이들이 읍내에서 프랭클린 도련님이 은행에 나타났다는 말을 듣고 그에 맞춰 결론을 내렸는지, 아니면 그 소년이 정말로 그 다이아몬드가 지금 맡겨져 있는 곳에서 그것을 보았는지(나로 말하면 이것은 딱 잘라 못 믿겠다만), 아니면 결국 한낱 우연의 조화였는지—어쨌거나 이것만은 엄연한 사실이다. 레이철 아가씨의 생일에 앞선 그 몇 주 동안, 인도인의 그림자조차 다시는 집 근처에 얼씬하지 않았다는 것 말이다. 요술쟁이들은 읍내와 그 언저리에 머물며 저희 장사를 이어 갔고, 프랭클린 도련님과 나는, 무슨 일이 일어날지 두고 보며 기다리되, 우리의 의심을 너무 일찍 내비쳐 그 악당들에게 경계심을 심어 주지 않기로 굳게 마음먹고 있었다. 양쪽에서 벌어진 일에 대한 이 보고를 끝으로, 인도인들에 관해 당분간 내가 할 이야기는 다 마친다.\n\n그달 29일에, 레이철 아가씨와 프랭클린 도련님은, 그러지 않았으면 손안에서 지루하게 늘어졌을 그 시간을 함께 헤쳐 나갈 새로운 방편을 하나 찾아냈다. 여기서 그 두 사람을 재미나게 한 그 소일거리를 각별히 눈여겨 두어야 할 까닭이 있다. 그것이 아직 오지 않은 어떤 일과 관련이 있음을 여러분은 알게 되실 것이다.\n\n대체로 지체 높은 분들에게는 인생에 아주 성가신 암초가 하나 앞에 놓여 있다—바로 저 자신의 무료함이라는 암초다. 그분들의 삶은 대개, 무언가 할 일이 없나 하고 주위를 두리번거리며 지나가는지라, 보고 있노라면 참으로 묘하다—더구나 그 취향이 이른바 지적이라는 부류일 때면—얼마나 자주 그분들이 눈을 가린 채 어떤 고약한 소일거리로 떠내려가는지 말이다. 열에 아홉은 무언가를 못살게 굴거나 무언가를 망가뜨리는 데 손을 대고서는—그러면서 저희가 제 정신을 갈고닦고 있다고 굳게 믿는데, 엄연한 사실인즉 그저 집안을 어지럽히고 있을 따름이다. 나는 그분들이 (숙녀분들도, 이런 말씀 드리기 안됐습니다만, 신사분들 못지않게) 이를테면 날마다 빈 알약통을 들고 나가, 영원(蠑螈)이며 딱정벌레며 거미며 개구리를 잡아 와서는, 그 가엾은 것들의 몸에 핀을 꿰거나, 눈곱만한 뉘우침도 없이 조각조각 잘라 놓는 것을 보아 왔다. 우리 젊은 도련님이나 우리 젊은 아가씨가 확대경을 들이대고 그분들 거미 중 하나의 뱃속을 들여다보고 있는 꼴을 여러분은 보게 되고, 아니면 그분들 개구리 중 하나가 제 머리도 없이 계단을 걸어 내려오는 것과 마주치게 된다—그리고 이 잔인한 고약함이 무슨 뜻이냐고 여러분이 의아해하면, 그것이 우리 젊은 도련님이나 우리 젊은 아가씨의 박물학 취미를 뜻하는 것이라는 대답을 듣게 된다. 또 어떤 때는, 그 꽃이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 알고 싶다는 어리석은 호기심에서, 뾰족한 연장으로 예쁜 꽃 한 송이를 망가뜨리며 몇 시간이고 매달려 있는 것을 보게 된다. 막상 알고 나면 그 빛깔이 조금이라도 더 예뻐지던가, 그 향기가 조금이라도 더 향긋해지던가? 하지만 어쩌겠는가! 가엾은 분들은, 보시다시피, 그 시간을 어떻게든 때워야 하는 것을—그 시간을 어떻게든 때워야 하는 것을. 여러분도 어릴 적엔 고약한 진흙을 주무르며 흙떡을 빚었고, 어른이 되어서는 고약한 과학을 주무르며 거미를 해부하고 꽃을 망가뜨리는 것이다. 이 경우나 저 경우나, 그 속내인즉, 여러분의 가엾고 텅 빈 머리에는 생각할 것이 아무것도 없고, 여러분의 가엾고 한가한 손에는 할 것이 아무것도 없다는 것이다. 그리하여 그것은 결국, 물감으로 화폭을 망치며 집안에 냄새를 피우는 것으로, 아니면 더러운 물이 가득한 유리 상자에 올챙이를 길러 온 집안 식구의 속을 뒤집어 놓는 것으로, 아니면 여기저기, 아무 데서나 돌 조각을 쪼아 내어 집안의 온갖 먹을거리에 모래알을 떨어뜨리는 것으로, 아니면 사진 놀음을 좇느라 손가락에 얼룩을 묻히고 온 집안 식구의 얼굴을 사정없이 있는 그대로 박아 내는 것으로 끝이 난다. 제 밥벌이를 정말로 해야만 하는 사람들, 곧 제 몸을 덮을 옷과 제 몸을 가릴 지붕과 제 목숨을 이어 줄 밥을 위해 억지로 일해야만 하는 사람들에게는, 그 일이 흔히 어지간히 고되게 내리누르는 것도 틀림없다. 그러나 여러분이 여태 해 본 가장 고된 하루 노동을, 꽃을 쪼개고 거미 뱃속을 쑤셔 대는 그 무료함과 견주어 보고서, 여러분의 머리에는 반드시 생각해야만 할 무언가가 있고 여러분의 손에는 반드시 해야만 할 무언가가 있음을 여러분의 별들에게 감사하시라.\n\n프랭클린 도련님과 레이철 아가씨로 말하면, 다행히도 그분들은 아무것도 못살게 굴지 않았다. 그분들은 그저 어지르는 것으로 그쳤고, 그분들이 망가뜨린 것이라곤, 공평히 말하자면, 문짝 하나의 널판때기뿐이었다.\n\n무엇에나 손을 대는 프랭클린 도련님의 그 만능 재주가, 이번엔 그가 \"장식 그림\"이라 부르는 것에 손을 댔다. 그는, 물감을 개는 데 쓰는 새로운 혼합물을 발명했노라 우리에게 일러 주며, 그것을 \"매재(媒材)\"라고 일컬었다. 그것이 무엇으로 만들어졌는지 나는 모른다. 그것이 무슨 일을 했는지는 두 마디로 말씀드릴 수 있다—고약한 악취를 풍겼다. 레이철 아가씨가 그 새 기법에 손수 도전해 보고 싶어 안달이 나자, 프랭클린 도련님은 런던으로 재료를 주문했다. 그러고는 방에 들어오는 개들마저 재채기하게 만드는 냄새를 곁들여 그것들을 개어, 레이철 아가씨의 겉옷 위에 앞치마와 턱받이를 두르게 하고, 아가씨를 앉혀 아가씨 자신의 조그만 거실을—우리말로 부를 마땅한 말이 없어 그저 아가씨의 \"부두아르(규방)\"라고 불렀는데—꾸미는 일을 시켰다. 그분들은 문 안쪽부터 시작했다. 프랭클린 도련님은 속돌로 그 고운 광택칠을 죄다 벗겨 내어, 그가 이르기로 작업할 바탕을 만들었다. 그러자 레이철 아가씨는, 그의 지시에 따라 그의 도움을 받아 가며, 그 바탕을 온갖 무늬와 도안으로—그리핀이며 새며 꽃이며 큐피드며 그런 것들로—뒤덮었으니, 이름은 잊었으나 어느 유명한 이탈리아 화가가 그린 도안을 본뜬 것이었다. 내 말은, 세상을 성모 마리아상으로 가득 채우고 빵집에 애인을 두었다던 바로 그 사람 말이다. 일로 보자면, 이 장식은 하는 데 더디고 다루기에 지저분했다. 그러나 우리 젊은 아가씨와 신사분은 도무지 그것에 물릴 줄을 몰랐다. 말을 타거나, 손님을 보거나, 끼니를 들거나, 노래를 뽑는 때가 아니면, 그분들은 늘 거기서 머리를 맞대고, 꿀벌처럼 부지런히, 그 문을 망가뜨리고 있었다. 게으른 손에게는 사탄이 여전히 무슨 못된 짓거리를 마련해 준다고 말한 시인이 누구였던가? 그가 만일 이 집에서 내 자리에 있어, 붓을 든 레이철 아가씨와 매재를 든 프랭클린 도련님을 보았던들, 그 두 사람 어느 쪽에 대해서도 그보다 더 참된 것은 쓸 수 없었으리라.\n\n눈여겨 둘 만한 다음 날짜는 6월 4일 일요일이다.\n\n그날 저녁 우리는, 하인들 식당에서, 한 집안일을 놓고 처음으로 갑론을박했는데, 그것 또한 그 문 장식과 마찬가지로, 아직 오지 않은 어떤 일과 관련이 있다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님과 레이철 아가씨가 서로의 벗이 되어 즐거워하는 것을 보고, 또 그분들이 됨됨이의 온갖 면에서 얼마나 어울리는 한 쌍인지를 눈여겨보고서, 우리는 자연히, 그분들이 문 하나 꾸미는 것 말고 다른 목적을 염두에 두고 머리를 맞댈 가망은 없을까 어림해 보았다. 우리 중 더러는 여름이 가기 전에 이 집에 혼례가 있으리라 했다. 다른 이들은 (내가 앞장서서) 레이철 아가씨가 혼인하실 법하다는 데는 동의하면서도, 다만 그 신랑이 프랭클린 블레이크 씨일지는 (이제 곧 드러날 까닭들로) 미심쩍어했다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님이, 그 나름으로는, 사랑에 빠져 있다는 것은, 그를 보고 그의 말을 들은 이라면 아무도 의심할 수 없었다. 어려운 것은 레이철 아가씨의 속을 헤아리는 일이었다. 여러분께 아가씨를 소개하는 영예를 내가 누리게 해 주시라. 그러고 나서는, 여러분 스스로 헤아려 보시라고—하실 수 있거든—맡겨 두겠다.\n\n우리 젊은 아가씨의 열여덟 번째 생일이, 6월 21일에 이제 곧 다가올 그 생일이었다. 혹 여러분이 살빛 검은 여자를 좋아하시고 (그런 여자들은 요즈음 화려한 사교계에서 유행이 지났다고 들었습니다만), 그리고 몸집에 대해 딱히 이렇다 할 편애가 없으시다면, 나는 레이철 아가씨가 여러분 눈이 여태 본 가장 어여쁜 처녀 가운데 하나임을 보증하겠다. 아가씨는 자그맣고 호리호리했으나, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 죄다 고른 균형을 이루고 있었다. 아가씨가 앉는 것을 보고, 일어서는 것을 보고, 특히 걷는 것을 보노라면, 제정신인 사내라면 누구든, (이런 표현을 용서하신다면) 그 몸매의 우아함이 옷이 아니라 살에 깃들어 있음에 넉넉히 흡족해할 만했다. 아가씨의 머리칼은 내가 여태 본 것 중 가장 검었다. 아가씨의 눈은 그 머리칼과 짝을 이루었다. 코는, 인정하거니와, 그리 크지는 못했다. 입과 턱은 (프랭클린 도련님의 말을 빌리자면) 신들이 탐낼 만한 진미였고, 낯빛은 (똑같이 부인할 수 없는 그 권위에 따르면) 해 그 자체만큼이나 따뜻했으되, 해보다 이 큰 이점이 있었으니, 언제 보아도 늘 곱게 갈무리되어 있다는 점이었다. 앞엣것에다, 아가씨가 그 머리를 화살처럼 꼿꼿이, 늠름하고 활기차고 순종(純種) 같은 태로 이고 다녔다는 것—맑은 목소리에는 제대로 된 쇠붙이의 울림이 서려 있었다는 것—그리고 입술에 이르기 전에 먼저 눈에서 아주 예쁘게 피어나는 미소가 있었다는 것을 보태면—자, 여기, 내 그림 솜씨가 미치는 데까지 실물만 한 크기로 그린 아가씨의 초상이 있소이다!\n\n그럼 다음으로 아가씨의 성품은 어떠했느냐고? 이 매혹적인 존재에게는 흠이 하나도 없었느냐고? 아가씨에게도 흠은 꼭 여러분만큼 있었소이다, 부인—더도 덜도 아니고.\n\n진지하게 말하자면, 나의 사랑스럽고 어여쁜 레이철 아가씨는, 수많은 우아함과 매력을 지녔으되, 한 가지 결함이 있었으니, 엄정한 공평함이 나로 하여금 그것을 인정하게 만든다. 아가씨는 또래의 다른 여느 처녀들과 이 점에서 달랐으니—제 나름의 생각을 지녔고, 유행이 제 소견에 맞지 않으면 유행 그 자체마저 거스를 만큼 고집이 셌다는 것이다. 사소한 일에서는 이 독립심이 다 좋았다. 그러나 중대한 일에서는, 그것이 아가씨를 (마님이 생각하시기에도, 또 내가 생각하기에도) 지나치게 멀리 몰아갔다. 아가씨는, 제 나이의 곱절 되는 여자들도 좀처럼 그러지 못하는 만큼, 제 스스로 판단했다. 결코 남의 조언을 구하지 않았고, 무엇을 할 참인지 미리 일러 주는 법이 없었으며, 제 어머니에서부터 그 아래로 그 누구에게도 비밀이며 속마음을 털어놓으러 오는 법이 없었다. 크고 작은 일에서, 사랑하는 사람에게나 미워하는 사람에게나 (아가씨는 그 둘 다 똑같이 진심으로 했다), 레이철 아가씨는 언제나 제 나름의 길을 갔으니, 제 삶의 기쁨과 슬픔 속에서 스스로에게 족했다. 나는 마님이 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 몇 번이고 거듭 들었다. \"레이철의 가장 좋은 벗과 레이철의 가장 나쁜 원수는, 이것도 저것도 다—레이철 자신이야.\"\n\n여기에 한 가지만 더 보태면, 내 할 말은 다 한다.\n\n그 온갖 은밀함과 고집에도 불구하고, 아가씨에게는 거짓된 것이라곤 그 그림자조차 없었다. 나는 아가씨가 약속을 어기는 것을 본 기억이 없다. 아가씨가 아니오, 라고 말하면서 속으로 예, 라고 여기는 것을 본 기억이 없다. 어린 시절, 그 착한 어린것이 제가 사랑하는 놀이동무가 저지른 잘못을 두고 대신 탓을 뒤집어쓰고 벌을 받은 일이 한두 번이 아니었음을 나는 떠올릴 수 있다. 그 일이 들통나서 나중에 아가씨가 추궁을 받았을 때, 아가씨가 그것을 자백하는 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그러나 아가씨가 그것을 두고 거짓말하는 것을 본 사람 또한 아무도 없었다. 아가씨는 여러분의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며, 그 앙큼한 조그만 머리를 젓고는, 딱 잘라 말했다. \"말 안 할래요!\" 이 일로 또 벌을 받으면, 아가씨는 \"안 할래요\"라고 말한 것은 미안하다고 인정했다. 그러나 빵과 물만 주는 벌에도 아랑곳없이, 끝내 말하지 않았다. 고집이 세고—때로는 지독히도 고집이 세고—그건 인정한다. 그러나 그럼에도 아가씨는, 이 낮은 세상의 길을 걸어 본 이들 가운데 가장 훌륭한 존재였다. 여러분은 여기서 어떤 모순을 보시는 듯싶은가? 그렇다면, 귓속말 한마디 하겠다. 앞으로 스물네 시간 동안 여러분의 아내를 찬찬히 살펴보시라. 만일 여러분의 어지신 부인께서 그동안 모순이랄 만한 것을 조금도 내보이지 않으신다면, 하느님이 여러분을 도우시기를!—여러분은 괴물과 혼인하신 것이니.\n\n이제 나는 여러분께 레이철 아가씨를 소개해 드렸으니, 이로써 우리는 다음으로, 그 젊은 아가씨의 혼삿길에 관한 물음과 마주 서게 되었음을 여러분도 아시게 될 것이다.\n\n6월 12일에, 내 여주인의 초대장이 런던의 한 신사분께 보내졌으니, 오셔서 레이철 아가씨의 생일 쇠는 것을 도와 달라는 것이었다. 이분이 바로, 아가씨가 남몰래 마음을 두고 있다고 내가 믿었던 그 복 받은 사람이었다! 프랭클린 도련님과 마찬가지로, 그도 아가씨의 사촌이었다. 이름은 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨였다.\n\n마님의 둘째 언니께서는 (놀라지 마시라. 이번엔 집안 사정을 아주 깊이까지 파고들 것은 아니니)—마님의 둘째 언니, 내 말은, 그분께서 사랑에 실연하시고, 그 뒤 될 대로 되라는 심정으로 남편을 얻어, 이른바 격에 안 맞는 혼인을 하셨다. 캐럴라인 아가씨께서 그저 평범한 에이블화이트 씨, 곧 프리징홀의 그 은행가와 혼인하겠다고 우기셨을 때, 집안에는 한바탕 난리가 났다. 그는 매우 부유하고 매우 점잖았으며, 엄청나게 많은 자식을 두었다—여기까지는 죄다 그에게 유리한 점이다. 그러나 그는 세상의 낮은 신분에서 감히 제 몸을 끌어올린 사람이었다—그리고 그 점이 그에게 불리했다. 그렇지만 세월과 근대 계몽의 진보가 일을 바로잡아, 그 격에 안 맞는 혼인은 아주 무난히 받아들여졌다. 이제 우리는 다들 자유주의자가 되어 가고 있으니, (여러분이 내 등을 긁어 주면 나도 여러분 등을 긁어 준다는 조건 아래) 의회 안에서든 밖에서든, 여러분이 청소부이든 공작이든 내가 무슨 상관이겠는가? 그것이 요즈음 세상의 관점이요—나도 요즈음 세상에 발맞추고 있다. 에이블화이트 집안은 프리징홀에서 조금 벗어난, 훌륭한 저택과 정원에서 살았다. 매우 훌륭한 사람들이요, 이웃에서 크게 존경받았다. 이 지면에서 그들 때문에 우리가 크게 시달릴 일은 없을 것이다—다만 에이블화이트 씨의 둘째 아들인 고드프리 씨만은 예외이니, 그는, 여러분이 괜찮으시다면, 레이철 아가씨를 위해서라도 여기서 제 마땅한 자리를 차지해야 한다.\n\n그 온갖 총명함과 영리함과 두루 좋은 자질에도 불구하고, 우리 젊은 아가씨의 눈에 프랭클린 도련님이 고드프리 씨를 능가할 가망은, 내 생각에, 참으로 지극히 희박한 가망이었다.\n\n우선 첫째로, 고드프리 씨는, 몸집으로 치면, 두 사람 가운데 단연코 더 훤칠한 사내였다. 그는 키가 6피트를 넘었고, 아름다운 붉고 흰 낯빛에, 여러분 손바닥처럼 말끔히 면도한 매끈하고 둥근 얼굴에, 목덜미 위로 아무렇게나 흘러내리는 고운 긴 아마빛 머리칼을 지녔다. 그런데 내가 어째서 그의 이런 생김새를 여러분께 굳이 그려 드리려 애쓰는가? 여러분이 혹 런던에서 어느 부인 자선회에 기부해 보신 적이 있다면, 여러분도 나만큼이나 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 잘 아신다. 그는 직업으로는 법정 변호사요, 기질로는 여자들의 사내요, 스스로 택하기로는 착한 사마리아인이었다. 여성의 자선과 여성의 궁핍은 그가 없이는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 가난한 여자들의 해산을 돕는 산모회, 가난한 여자들을 구제하는 막달레나회, 가난한 여자들을 가난한 남자들의 자리에 앉히고 남자들일랑 저 알아서 살라고 내버려 두자는 억센 마음의 모임들—그는 그 모든 것의 부회장이요, 간사요, 심판이었다. 어디든 숙녀 위원들이 빙 둘러앉아 의논하는 탁자가 있는 곳이면, 거기 그 판의 맨 끄트머리에 고드프리 씨가 있어, 위원회의 성미를 다독이며, 그 어여쁜 분들을 모자를 손에 들고 사무의 가시밭길로 이끌었다. 나는 이 사람이야말로 (얼마 안 되는 제 재산으로) 영국이 여태 낳은 가장 완숙한 박애가라고 생각한다. 자선 모임의 연설가로서, 여러분의 눈물과 여러분의 돈을 끌어내는 데 그만한 사람은 좀처럼 찾기 어려웠다. 그는 그야말로 공인이었다. 지난번 내가 런던에 있을 때, 내 여주인이 내게 두 가지 대접을 베푸셨다. 마님은 나를 극장에 보내 장안의 화제이던 어느 춤추는 여자를 보게 하셨고, 나를 엑서터 홀에 보내 고드프리 씨의 연설을 듣게 하셨다. 그 여자는 악단을 곁들여 그 일을 했다. 그 신사는 손수건 한 장과 물 한 잔을 곁들여 그 일을 했다. 다리로 하는 그 공연에도 인파가 몰렸다. 혀로 하는 그 공연에도 마찬가지였다. 그리고 이 모든 것에 더해, 그는 (내 말은 고드프리 씨 말이다) 여러분이 여태 만나 본 가장 상냥한 성미의 사람—가장 소탈하고 가장 유쾌하고 가장 비위 맞추기 쉬운 사람이었다. 그는 모든 사람을 사랑했다. 그리고 모든 사람이 그를 사랑했다. 프랭클린 도련님한테—평범한 평판과 재간을 지닌 그 누구한테—이런 사람에 맞설 무슨 가망이 있었겠는가?\n\n14일에, 고드프리 씨의 답장이 왔다.\n\n그는 내 여주인의 초대를, 생일인 수요일부터 금요일 저녁까지—그때가 되면 부인 자선회에 대한 그의 소임이 그를 도로 도시로 불러들일 참이라며—받아들였다. 그는 또한 그가 우아하게 제 사촌의 \"탄신일\"이라 일컬은 것을 노래한 시 한 편의 사본을 동봉했다. 내가 전해 들으니, 레이철 아가씨는 저녁 식탁에서 프랭클린 도련님과 어울려 그 시를 놀림감으로 삼았다고 한다. 그리고 죄다 프랭클린 도련님 편이던 페넬로피가, 자못 의기양양하게, 그걸 어떻게 생각하시느냐고 내게 물었다. \"레이철 아가씨가 너를 헛다리 짚게 끌고 갔구나, 얘야.\" 나는 대꾸했다. \"하지만 내 코는 그리 쉽사리 홀리지 않는단다. 에이블화이트 씨의 시 뒤에 에이블화이트 씨 본인이 따라올 때까지 기다려 보렴.\"\n\n내 딸은, 그 시 뒤에 그 시인이 따라오기 전에, 프랭클린 도련님이 끼어들어 제 운을 시험해 볼지도 모른다고 대꾸했다. 이 견해에 유리하게, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 레이철 아가씨의 총애를 얻으려고 시도해 보지 않은 기회가 하나도 없었음을 인정하지 않을 수 없다.\n\n내가 여태 만나 본 가장 골수 골초 가운데 하나였으면서도, 그는 시가를 끊었다. 어느 날 아가씨가, 그의 옷에서 나는 그 케케묵은 담배 냄새가 싫다고 말했기 때문이다. 이 극기의 노력 뒤로, 그는, 익숙해져 있던 담배의 그 마음을 가라앉히는 효험이 없으니 잠을 하도 설쳐, 아침마다 그토록 초췌하고 지친 몰골로 내려오기에, 급기야 레이철 아가씨가 몸소 그에게 시가를 다시 피우라고 청할 지경이었다. 아니 될 말! 그는 아가씨에게 한순간이라도 언짢음을 끼칠 수 있는 것이라면 무엇도 다시는 손대지 않으려 했다. 그는 그것을 결연히 견뎌 내어, 이르든 늦든, 그것을 기다리는 참을성의 순전한 힘으로 제 잠을 되찾으려 했다. 이만한 헌신이라면 (아래층의 더러가 그렇게 말했듯이) 레이철 아가씨에게 마땅한 효과를 내지 못할 리 없다고 여러분은 말씀하실 것이다—더구나 날마다 그 문에 하는 장식 일까지 뒷받침해 주고 있었으니 말이다. 다 좋다—그러나 아가씨는 제 침실에 고드프리 씨의 사진을 한 장 두고 있었다. 어느 공개 모임에서 연설하는 모습으로, 제 웅변의 입김에 머리칼이 죄다 흩날리고, 그 더없이 사랑스러운 눈은 여러분 주머니에서 돈을 홀려 내는 사진이었다. 여러분은 그것에 대해 뭐라 하시겠는가? 아침마다—페넬로피 자신이 내게 실토한 바로—레이철 아가씨가 머리를 빗기는 동안, 여자들이 없이는 못 사는 그 사내가, 초상이 되어, 지켜보고 있었던 것이다. 머잖아 그는 실물로 지켜보게 되리라—그것이 그 일에 대한 내 생각이었다.\n\n6월 16일에는, 내 보기에 프랭클린 도련님의 가망을 그 어느 때보다 더 나빠 보이게 만든 한 사건이 일어났다.\n\n영어를 외국식 억양으로 하는 어떤 낯선 신사가, 그날 아침 집에 찾아와, 볼일이 있어 프랭클린 블레이크 씨를 뵙고 싶다 청했다. 그 볼일은 도무지 그 다이아몬드와는 관계가 있을 수 없었으니, 그 까닭은 두 가지다—첫째, 프랭클린 도련님이 그것에 관해 내게 아무 말도 하지 않았다는 것. 둘째, 그가 그것을 (그 신사가 돌아간 뒤, 짐작건대) 마님께 알렸다는 것. 마님은 아마 그다음에 그것에 관해 무언가를 당신 따님께 넌지시 비치셨을 것이다. 어쨌든, 그날 저녁 피아노 앞에서 레이철 아가씨가 프랭클린 도련님에게, 그가 함께 어울려 지낸 사람들과 그가 외국에서 받아들인 신조에 관해 몇 가지 매서운 말을 했다고 전해졌다. 이튿날, 처음으로, 그 문 장식을 향해서는 아무것도 이루어지지 않았다. 나는 대륙에서의 프랭클린 도련님의 어떤 경솔한 짓이—그 밑바닥에 여자 아니면 빚을 깔고서—그를 따라 영국까지 온 것이 아닐까 의심한다. 그러나 그것은 죄다 짐작일 뿐이다. 이번 경우엔, 프랭클린 도련님뿐 아니라 마님까지도, 웬일로, 나를 캄캄한 채로 내버려 두셨다.\n\n17일에는, 겉보기로는, 그 먹구름이 다시 걷혔다. 그분들은 문에 하는 장식 일로 돌아갔고, 여느 때처럼 좋은 벗으로 보였다. 페넬로피의 말을 믿는다면, 프랭클린 도련님이 그 화해의 기회를 붙들어 레이철 아가씨에게 청혼을 했는데, 받아들여지지도 거절당하지도 않았다는 것이다. 내 딸은 (여러분을 굳이 성가시게 할 것 없는 이런저런 낌새와 표지로 미루어) 확신하기를, 제 젊은 여주인이 그가 진심이라는 것을 믿기를 거부하는 식으로 프랭클린 도련님을 물리쳐 놓고는, 그 뒤 그런 식으로 그를 대한 것을 남몰래 후회했다는 것이었다. 페넬로피가 여느 하녀들보다 제 젊은 여주인과 더 허물없이 지내는 것이 허락되어 있기는 했으나—그 둘은 어린 시절 거의 함께 자라다시피 했으므로—그래도 나는, 아가씨가 그런 식으로 제 속마음을 누구에게든 내보이리라 믿기에는 레이철 아가씨의 그 내성적인 성품을 너무 잘 알고 있었다. 이번 경우에 내 딸이 내게 한 말은, 내가 의심한 대로, 그 애가 실제로 아는 것이라기보다 그 애가 바라는 것에 가까웠다.\n\n19일에는 또 다른 사건이 일어났다. 우리는 의사를 진료차 집에 들였다. 그는, 내가 이 지면에서 여러분께 소개할 기회가 있었던 한 사람—우리 둘째 하녀 로재나 스피어먼을 진찰하러 불려 왔다.\n\n이 가엾은 처녀는—여러분도 이미 아시다시피 떠는 모래펄에서 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 그 처녀는—지금 내가 적고 있는 그 사이 기간에도, 다시 한두 번 나를 어리둥절하게 했다. 제 동료 하인이 프랭클린 도련님을 사랑한다는 페넬로피의 생각은 (내 명에 따라 내 딸이 철저히 비밀에 부친 것이었는데) 여전히 예전만큼이나 터무니없어 보였다. 그러나 나 자신이 본 것, 그리고 내 딸도 본, 우리 둘째 하녀의 처신이, 아무리 좋게 보아도 알쏭달쏭해 보이기 시작했음은 나도 인정해야겠다.\n\n이를테면, 그 처녀는 끊임없이 프랭클린 도련님의 앞길에 제 몸을 갖다 놓았다—아주 슬며시 그리고 조용히 그랬으나, 아무튼 그랬다. 그는 고양이한테 기울일 만큼의 관심을 그녀에게 기울였을 뿐이다. 로재나의 못생긴 얼굴에 눈길 한 번 허비할 생각은 도무지 떠오르지 않는 듯했다. 그 가엾은 것의, 본디 많지도 않던 입맛이 무섭게 떨어졌고, 아침이면 그 눈이, 밤에 잠 못 이루고 운 것이 뚜렷한 흔적을 드러냈다. 어느 날 페넬로피가 난처한 발견을 하나 했는데, 우리는 그것을 그 자리에서 쉬쉬해 두었다. 그 애가, 프랭클린 도련님의 화장대에서, 레이철 아가씨가 그더러 단춧구멍에 꽂으라고 준 장미 한 송이를 로재나가 남몰래 빼내고, 그 자리에 제가 손수 꺾은 그와 비슷한 다른 장미를 꽂아 놓는 것을 붙잡은 것이다. 그 뒤로 그녀는, 내가 처신을 조심하라고 좋은 뜻에서 두루뭉술하게 넌지시 일러 줄 때, 한두 번 내게 건방지게 굴었다. 그리고 더 나쁘게는, 레이철 아가씨가 어쩌다 그녀에게 말을 거는 그 몇 안 되는 경우에도, 이제 그리 공손하지가 않았다.\n\n마님이 그 변화를 눈치채시고, 그것에 대해 어찌 생각하느냐고 내게 물으셨다. 나는 그 처녀를 감싸 주려고, 그녀가 몸이 편찮은 것 같다고 대답했다. 그리고 그것은 앞서 말했듯이 19일에 의사를 부르는 것으로 이어졌다. 의사는 그녀의 신경 탓이라며, 그녀가 일할 만한 몸인지 미심쩍어했다. 마님은 바람이라도 쐬게 그녀를 내륙에 있는 우리 농장 중 하나로 옮겨 주겠다고 하셨다. 그녀는 눈에 눈물을 담고서, 여기 머물게 해 달라고 빌고 또 빌었다. 그리고 불행한 시각에, 나는 마님께 그녀를 좀 더 두고 보시라 권했다. 뒤에 일이 드러낸 대로, 그리고 여러분도 곧 보시게 될 대로, 이것은 내가 할 수 있었던 가장 나쁜 권고였다. 내가 앞날을 조금이라도 내다볼 수만 있었던들, 나는 로재나 스피어먼을 그 자리에서 당장, 내 손으로 이 집에서 내보냈으리라.\n\n20일에는, 고드프리 씨에게서 쪽지가 하나 왔다. 그는 볼일이 있어 제 아버지와 의논할 일이 생겨, 그날 밤은 프리징홀에 묵기로 정했다고 했다. 이튿날 오후에, 그와 그의 손위 두 누이가 저녁 식사에 넉넉히 앞서 우리에게 말을 타고 오리라는 것이었다. 그 쪽지에는 우아한 도자기 상자 하나가 딸려 왔는데, 사촌의 사랑과 더없는 축원을 담아 레이철 아가씨께 드리는 것이었다. 프랭클린 도련님은 그저, 그 돈의 반값도 안 되는 밋밋한 로켓 하나를 아가씨께 드렸을 뿐이다. 그렇지만 내 딸 페넬로피는—여자의 고집이란 그런 것이라—여전히 그가 이기는 데 걸었다.\n\n하늘에 감사하게도, 우리는 마침내 그 생일의 전야에 이르렀다! 이번엔 내가 여러분을, 길에서 그리 어정거리지 않고 이 대목까지 넘어오게 해 드렸다고, 여러분도 아마 인정하시리라. 기운을 내시라! 여기서 또 하나 새 장(章)으로 여러분의 짐을 덜어 드리겠다—더구나, 그 장은 여러분을 곧장 이야기 한복판으로 데려다줄 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\n생일날인 6월 21일은, 해가 뜰 무렵엔 흐리고 변덕스러웠으나, 정오 무렵엔 씩씩하게 개었다.\n\n우리는, 하인들 식당에서, 여느 때처럼 이 기쁜 기념일을 시작했으니, 우리네 조촐한 선물을 레이철 아가씨께 바치고, 우두머리인 내가 해마다 하는 그 판에 박힌 연설을 올리는 것이었다. 나는 여왕께서 의회를 개회하실 때 취하시는 방식을 따른다—곧, 해마다 판에 박힌 듯 거의 똑같은 이야기를 하는 방식 말이다. 그 연설이 나오기 전에는, 내 연설도 (여왕의 연설과 마찬가지로) 마치 그런 것을 여태 한 번도 들어 본 적 없다는 듯 잔뜩 기다려진다. 그것이 나오고 나서, 기대하던 새로운 것이 아님이 드러나면, 다들 조금 투덜대기는 해도, 이듬해엔 무언가 더 새로운 것이 있으려니 하고 희망차게 고대한다. 의회에서든 부엌에서든, 다스리기 쉬운 백성이라—그것이 그 교훈이다.\n\n아침 식사 뒤, 프랭클린 도련님과 나는 월장석을 두고 남몰래 담판을 벌였다—이제 그것을 프리징홀 은행에서 찾아 내어 레이철 아가씨 손에 직접 쥐여 드릴 때가 되었기 때문이다.\n\n그가 다시 제 사촌에게 사랑을 고백해 보려다 퇴짜를 맞은 것인지—아니면 밤이면 밤마다 설친 잠이 그 됨됨이의 그 묘한 모순과 종잡을 수 없음을 더 부추긴 것인지—나는 모른다. 그러나 분명한 것은, 프랭클린 도련님이 생일날 아침에 제 가장 나은 모습을 보여 주지 못했다는 것이다. 그는 몇 분 사이에 그 다이아몬드를 두고 스무 가지나 다른 마음을 먹었다. 나로 말하면, 우리가 아는 대로의 그 엄연한 사실들에 딱 붙어 있었다. 그 보석을 두고 마님을 놀라게 해 드릴 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았고, 이제 그것을 제 사촌의 소유로 넘겨줄 프랭클린 도련님에게 지워진 그 법적 의무를 바꿀 수 있는 것도 아무것도 없었다. 그것이 이 일에 대한 내 견해였고, 그가 아무리 이리 비틀고 저리 돌려 보아도, 그는 결국 그것을 제 견해로도 삼지 않을 수 없었다. 우리는 그가 점심 뒤에 프리징홀로 말을 달려가, 그 다이아몬드를 가지고 오되, 아마도 고드프리 씨와 두 젊은 아가씨를 데리고 와 집으로 돌아오는 길에 벗을 삼기로 정했다.\n\n이렇게 매듭짓고서, 우리 젊은 신사는 레이철 아가씨께로 돌아갔다.\n\n그분들은 아침 내내, 그리고 오후의 얼마간을, 그 끝없는 문 장식 일로 다 써 버렸다. 페넬로피는 곁에 서서 지시받은 대로 물감을 개었고, 마님은 점심때가 가까워지자, 손수건으로 코를 막은 채 (그날 그분들이 프랭클린 도련님의 그 매재를 어지간히 많이 썼기 때문이다) 방을 들락날락하시며, 그 두 화가를 일에서 떼어 내려고 헛되이 애쓰셨다. 그분들이 앞치마를 벗고, (그 매재 탓에 훨씬 더 축난) 페넬로피를 놓아주고, 제 몸에 묻은 지저분한 것을 씻어 낸 것은 세 시가 되어서였다. 그러나 그분들은 원하던 것을 해낸 터였다—그분들은 생일날 그 문을 완성했고, 그 일을 여간 자랑스러워하지 않았다. 그리핀이며 큐피드며 그런 것들은, 인정하거니와, 바라보기에 더없이 아름다웠다. 다만 그 수가 하도 많고, 꽃과 도안 속에 하도 뒤엉켜 있으며, 그 몸짓과 자세가 하도 뒤죽박죽이라, 그것들을 바라보는 즐거움을 다한 뒤에도 몇 시간이나 그것들이 머릿속에서 불쾌하게 어른거렸다. 페넬로피가 그날 아침 일에서 제 몫을 뒤쪽 부엌에서 토하는 것으로 끝맺었다고 내가 덧붙인다면, 그것은 그 매재에 대한 아무 악의 없이 하는 말이다. 아니! 아니고말고! 그것은 마르고 나니 악취가 가셨다. 그리고 예술이 이런 종류의 희생을 요구한다면—그 애가 내 딸이긴 하다만—나는 말하겠다, 예술더러 그것을 가져가라고!\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 점심 식탁에서 한 입 낚아채 먹고는, 프리징홀로 말을 달려갔다—마님께 이르기로는, 제 사촌들을 데려오려고. 그러나 그 월장석을 가지러 간다는 것은, 그 자신과 나만 남몰래 아는 바였다.\n\n그날은 내가 식기대 곁에 자리 잡고 식탁 시중을 지휘하는 그 큰 잔치 가운데 하나였던지라, 프랭클린 도련님이 나가 있는 동안 나는 마음을 쏟을 일이 넉넉했다. 포도주를 살피고, 저녁 식탁에서 시중들 내 사내와 여자들을 점검하고 나서, 나는 손님들이 오기 전에 마음을 추스르려고 물러났다. 그—여러분도 아시는 것—한 모금과, 이 지면에서 내가 언급할 기회가 있었던 어떤 책 한 대목이, 내 몸과 마음을 가라앉혀 주었다. 나는, 내 생각에 아무래도 낮잠이 아니라 공상이었지 싶은 그 상태에서, 바깥에서 나는 말발굽 소리에 깨어났다. 그리고 문간으로 가서, 프랭클린 도련님과 그의 세 사촌으로 이루어진 마상(馬上) 행렬을, 노(老)에이블화이트 씨의 마부 한 사람이 딸려 온 그 행렬을 맞이했다.\n\n고드프리 씨가, 참 이상하게도, 이 점에서 프랭클린 도련님과 닮았다는 인상을 내게 주었다—그도 여느 때의 기운을 차리지 못한 듯 보였다는 것이다. 그는 여느 때처럼 다정하게 내게 악수를 청하고, 옛 벗 베터리지가 그토록 정정한 것을 뵈니 더없이 반갑다고 정중히 말했다. 그러나 그에게는 내가 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 어떤 먹구름 같은 것이 드리워 있었고, 부친께서 강녕하시더냐고 내가 묻자, 그는 자못 짧게 \"여느 때와 다름없으시네.\" 하고 대답했다. 그렇지만 두 에이블화이트 아가씨는 스무 사람 몫으로 명랑하여, 그 균형을 넉넉히 되돌려 놓고도 남았다. 그들은 제 오라비만큼이나 덩치가 컸다. 팔팔하고, 머리가 노랗고, 발그레한 처녀들로, 넘쳐 나는 살과 피가 그득하고, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 건강과 기운이 터질 듯했다. 그들을 태운 가엾은 말들은 다리가 후들거렸다. 그리고 그들이 (부축을 기다리지도 않고) 안장에서 뛰어내릴 때, 정말이지 그들은 마치 인도 고무로 만들어지기라도 한 듯 땅에서 통통 튀었다. 에이블화이트 아가씨들이 하는 말은 죄다 커다란 \"오!\"로 시작했고, 그들이 하는 짓은 죄다 쾅 소리와 함께였으며, 그들은 때를 가리지 않고, 아주 사소한 자극에도 킥킥거리고 꺅꺅거렸다. 통통이들—그것이 내가 그들을 부르는 이름이다.\n\n젊은 아가씨들이 내는 소란을 틈타, 나는 현관에서 프랭클린 도련님께 남몰래 한마디 건넬 기회를 얻었다.\n\n\"그 다이아몬드는 안전히 챙겨 오셨습니까요, 도련님?\"\n\n그는 고개를 끄덕이며, 제 외투 가슴 주머니를 톡톡 쳤다.\n\n\"인도인들은 뭐 좀 보이던가요?\"\n\n\"그림자도 없었어.\" 그 대답과 함께, 그는 마님을 찾았고, 마님이 작은 응접실에 계시다는 말을 듣자 곧장 그리로 갔다. 그가 그 방에 든 지 일 분도 채 되기 전에 종이 울렸고, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 이야기를 나누고자 하신다는 것을 레이철 아가씨께 알리라고 페넬로피가 보내졌다.\n\n그로부터 반 시간쯤 뒤, 현관을 가로지르다가, 나는 작은 응접실에서 터져 나온 비명 소리에 우뚝 걸음을 멈췄다. 내가 조금이라도 놀랐다고는 할 수 없다. 그 비명 속에서 나는 에이블화이트 아가씨들이 즐겨 내는 그 커다란 \"오!\"를 알아차렸으니까. 그렇지만 나는 (저녁 식사에 관한 분부를 여쭙는다는 구실로) 안으로 들어가, 정말로 무슨 심상찮은 일이 일어났는지 알아보았다.\n\n거기, 레이철 아가씨가 탁자 곁에, 마치 홀린 사람처럼, 대령의 그 불길한 다이아몬드를 손에 들고 서 있었다. 거기, 아가씨 양옆에는, 두 통통이가 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 그 보석을 눈으로 집어삼킬 듯 바라보며, 그것이 새로운 빛으로 저희에게 번쩍일 때마다 황홀하여 비명을 질렀다. 거기, 탁자 맞은편에는, 고드프리 씨가 커다란 어린애처럼 손뼉을 치며, \"빼어나! 빼어나!\" 하고 나직이 노래하듯 외치고 서 있었다. 거기, 책장 옆 의자에는 프랭클린 도련님이 앉아, 제 턱수염을 잡아당기며, 근심스레 창 쪽을 바라보고 있었다. 그리고 거기, 그 창가에는, 그가 바라보던 그 대상이 서 있었으니—마님이, 대령의 유언장 발췌본을 손에 든 채, 온 좌중에 등을 돌리고 계셨다.\n\n내가 분부를 여쭙자 마님이 내 쪽으로 돌아서셨다. 그리고 나는, 그 집안 특유의 찌푸림이 마님의 눈 위로 모여드는 것과, 그 집안 특유의 성미가 마님의 입가에서 씰룩거리는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"반 시간 뒤에 내 방으로 오게.\" 마님이 대답하셨다. \"그때 자네한테 할 말이 있을 게야.\"\n\n그 말을 남기고, 마님은 나가셨다. 마님이, 떠는 모래펄에서 프랭클린 도련님과 나를 담판 중에 난감하게 했던 바로 그 똑같은 어려움에 난감해하고 계심은 뻔했다. 월장석의 유증(遺贈)은, 마님이 당신 오라비를 잔인하고 부당하게 대했다는 증거였을까? 아니면 그가, 마님이 여태껏 그에 대해 생각해 온 그 가장 나쁜 것보다도 더 나빴다는 증거였을까? 대령의 사람됨에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는 마님의 따님이, 대령의 생일 선물을 손에 들고 거기 서 있는 동안, 마님이 판가름해야 할 그 물음들은 심각한 것이었다.\n\n내 차례로 그 방을 나서기도 전에, 늘 제가 태어날 적부터 이 집에 있던 늙은 하인을 살뜰히 챙기는 레이철 아가씨가 나를 붙들었다. \"보세요, 게이브리얼!\" 아가씨가 말하며, 창으로 쏟아져 드는 한 줄기 햇살 속에 그 보석을 내 눈앞에서 번쩍 비춰 보였다.\n\n우리를 굽어살피소서! 그것은 정말이지 다이아몬드였다! 물떼새 알만큼, 아니 거의 그만큼 큰! 그것에서 흘러나오는 빛은 마치 한가위 보름달 빛 같았다. 그 돌 속을 들여다보면, 여러분은 여러분의 눈을 그 속으로 끌어들여 다른 아무것도 보이지 않게 만드는 어떤 노란 심연을 들여다보는 것이었다. 그것은 헤아릴 길이 없어 보였다. 여러분이 엄지와 검지 사이에 쥘 수 있는 이 보석이, 하늘 그 자체만큼이나 헤아릴 길이 없어 보였다. 우리가 그것을 햇빛 속에 두었다가 방 안의 빛을 차단하니, 그것은 어둠 속에서, 달빛 같은 미광(微光)으로, 제 자신의 밝음의 심연에서 무시무시하게 빛을 뿜었다. 레이철 아가씨가 홀린 것도 무리가 아니었다. 아가씨의 사촌들이 비명을 지른 것도 무리가 아니었다. 그 다이아몬드가 나까지 어찌나 사로잡던지, 나도 그 통통이들 못지않게 커다란 \"오!\"를 터뜨리고 말았다. 우리 중 홀로 제정신을 지킨 이는 고드프리 씨뿐이었다. 그는 제 누이 둘의 허리에 한 팔씩 두르고서, 그 다이아몬드와 나를 번갈아 딱하다는 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"탄소일세, 베터리지! 그저 탄소야, 이 사람아, 따지고 보면!\"\n\n그의 뜻은, 짐작건대, 나를 깨우쳐 주려는 것이었으리라. 그러나 그가 한 일이라곤, 내게 저녁 식사를 떠올려 준 것뿐이었다. 나는 아래층의 내 시중꾼 군대를 향해 절뚝절뚝 걸어갔다. 내가 나가는데, 고드프리 씨가 말했다. \"그리운 베터리지 영감, 나는 그를 진심으로 아낀다니까!\" 그는 그 애정의 증언으로 나를 치켜세우면서, 제 누이들을 얼싸안고, 레이철 아가씨에게 추파를 던지고 있었다. 저기 저 사람한테는 끌어다 쓸 사랑이 무슨 밑천처럼 그득한 모양이지! 그에 견주면 프랭클린 도련님은 그야말로 야만인이었다.\n\n반 시간이 지나, 나는 분부받은 대로 마님의 방에 나타났다.\n\n이 자리에서 내 여주인과 나 사이에 오간 것은, 대체로, 떠는 모래펄에서 프랭클린 도련님과 나 사이에 오간 것의 되풀이였다—다만 이 차이가 있었으니, 나는 그 요술쟁이들에 관해서는 내 속내를 감추어 두려 조심했다는 것이다. 이 대목을 두고 마님을 놀라게 해 드릴 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았음을 알고 있었으니까. 물러가라는 분부를 받았을 때, 나는 마님이 대령의 동기에 대해 있을 수 있는 가장 새까만 견해를 취하고 계심을, 그리고 마님이 첫 기회가 오는 대로 그 월장석을 당신 따님의 소유에서 빼내기로 작정하셨음을 알 수 있었다.\n\n내 쪽 집으로 돌아가는 길에, 나는 프랭클린 도련님과 마주쳤다. 그는 제 사촌 레이철을 뭐 좀 보았느냐고 알고 싶어 했다. 나는 아가씨를 전혀 보지 못했다. 제 사촌 고드프리가 어디 있는지 일러 줄 수 있느냐고. 나는 몰랐다. 다만 나는 사촌 고드프리가 사촌 레이철에게서 그리 멀지 않은 데 있을지도 모른다고 의심하기 시작했다. 프랭클린 도련님의 의심도 겉보기에 같은 쪽으로 돌아간 모양이었다. 그는 제 턱수염을 세게 잡아당기더니, 가서 서재에 틀어박히며 문을, 온갖 뜻이 담긴 그 문을, 쾅 닫아 버렸다.\n\n생일 저녁 식사 채비하는 일에서 나는 더는 방해받지 않았고, 마침내 손님들을 맞으려고 내 몸을 말끔히 단장할 때가 되었다. 내가 막 흰 조끼를 입었을 때, 페넬로피가, 내 몇 오라기 안 남은 머리칼을 빗기고 내 흰 넥타이 매듭을 손봐 준다는 구실로, 내 화장대 앞에 나타났다. 내 딸은 잔뜩 들떠 있었고, 나는 그 애가 내게 할 말이 있음을 알았다. 그 애는 내 벗어진 머리 꼭대기에 입을 맞추고는 속삭였다. \"아버지, 소식이 있어요! 레이철 아가씨가 그 사람을 거절했대요.\"\n\n\"그 '사람'이 누구냐?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"그 부인회 위원 나리요, 아버지.\" 페넬로피가 말한다. \"고약하고 능글맞은 작자예요! 프랭클린 도련님 자리를 가로채려 들다니, 저는 그 사람이 미워요!\"\n\n내게 숨이 넉넉했더라면, 나는 틀림없이, 저명한 박애가를 두고 이토록 무엄하게 말하는 것에 항의했을 것이다. 그러나 그 순간 마침 내 딸이 내 넥타이 매듭을 손보고 있었고, 그 감정의 온 힘이 그 애 손가락으로 쏠려 들었다. 내 평생 그토록 목이 졸릴 뻔한 적은 없었다.\n\n\"그 사람이 아가씨를 혼자 장미 정원으로 데리고 가는 걸 봤어요.\" 페넬로피가 말한다. \"그래서 저는 그분들이 어떻게 돌아오나 보려고 호랑가시나무 뒤에서 기다렸죠. 나갈 땐 둘이 팔짱을 끼고, 둘 다 웃으면서 갔어요. 돌아올 땐, 따로따로 걸으며, 더없이 근엄하게, 그리고 서로에게서 고개를 딱 돌린 채로였는데, 그건 오해할 여지가 없는 모양새였어요. 저는 제 평생 그토록 기뻤던 적이 없어요, 아버지! 세상에 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 이겨 낼 수 있는 여자가 적어도 하나는 있는 거예요. 그리고 제가 만일 숙녀라면, 저도 또 하나가 될 거고요!\"\n\n여기서 나는 또 항의했어야 마땅하다. 그러나 이때쯤 내 딸은 머리빗을 들고 있었고, 그 감정의 온 힘이 그리로 옮아가 있었다. 여러분이 대머리라면, 그 애가 나를 어떻게 제물 삼았는지 아시리라. 대머리가 아니시라면, 이 대목은 건너뛰시고, 여러분의 머리빗과 여러분의 머리 사이에 방패 노릇을 할 무언가가 있음을 하느님께 감사하시라.\n\n\"바로 그 호랑가시나무 저쪽 편에서,\" 페넬로피가 계속했다. \"고드프리 씨가 우뚝 멈춰 섰어요. '그럼 넌,' 하고 그가 말해요. '내가 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 여기 이대로 머물기를 바라는 거니?' 레이철 아가씨가 번갯불처럼 그에게 홱 돌아섰어요. '오라버니는 우리 어머니의 초대를 받아들였잖아요.' 아가씨가 말했어요. '그리고 어머니의 손님들을 맞으러 여기 온 거고요. 이 집안에 추문을 일으키고 싶은 게 아니라면, 물론 머무셔야죠!' 아가씨가 몇 걸음 더 가더니, 조금 누그러진 듯했어요. '지난 일은 잊기로 해요, 고드프리.' 아가씨가 말했어요. '그리고 우린 여전히 사촌으로 남기로 해요.' 아가씨가 그에게 손을 내밀었어요. 그가 그 손에 입을 맞췄는데, 저 같으면 그걸 주제넘은 짓으로 여겼을 거예요. 그러고 나서 아가씨가 그를 두고 떠났어요. 그는 고개를 떨군 채, 발꿈치로 자갈길에 천천히 구멍을 갈아 파며, 잠시 혼자 서 있었어요. 그렇게 낭패한 얼굴을 한 사내는 평생 보신 적 없으실 거예요. '난처하군!' 그가 고개를 들며 이 사이로 중얼거리더니, 집 쪽으로 가면서—'아주 난처해!' 그게 자기 자신에 대한 그의 생각이었다면, 그는 아주 옳았어요. 여간 난처하지 않았을 테니까요, 틀림없이. 그리고 그 결말은, 아버지, 제가 내내 아버지께 말씀드린 그대로예요.\" 하고 페넬로피가, 마지막 생채기를, 그중에서도 가장 뜨거운 것을 내며 나를 끝장내며 외친다. \"프랭클린 도련님이 바로 그 사람이라니까요!\"\n\n나는 머리빗을 손에 넣고서, 여러분도 인정하시겠지만 내 딸의 말과 처신이 넉넉히 받아 마땅한 그 꾸지람을 내리려고 입술을 열었다.\n\n내가 미처 한마디 하기도 전에, 바깥에서 마차 바퀴가 요란하게 굴러드는 소리가 끼어들어 나를 멈춰 세웠다. 저녁 손님의 첫 무리가 온 것이다. 페넬로피가 대뜸 달아났다. 나는 외투를 걸치고 거울을 들여다보았다. 내 머리는 바닷가재처럼 새빨갰다. 그러나 그 밖의 점으로는, 나는 그 저녁 예식을 위해 사내가 갖춰 입어야 할 만큼 말끔히 차려입은 모습이었다. 나는 마침맞게 현관에 들어서서 첫 두 손님을 알렸다. 그 두 분에 대해서는 여러분이 딱히 관심을 두실 것 없다. 그저 그 박애가의 아버지와 어머니—에이블화이트 씨 부부일 뿐이니.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\n에이블화이트 씨네 뒤로 나머지 손님들이 꼬리를 물고 잇달아 들어와, 마침내 그들 무리가 죄다 채워졌다. 집안 식구까지 넣어, 그들은 모두 스물넷이었다. 그들이 저녁 식탁을 빙 둘러 제자리에 자리 잡고, 프리징홀 교구 목사가 (고운 낭독 솜씨로) 일어나 감사 기도를 올리는 광경은, 보기에 훌륭한 것이었다.\n\n손님 명단으로 여러분을 성가시게 할 것은 없다. 그들 가운데 여러분이 두 번째로 다시 만나실 이는—적어도 내 몫의 이야기에서는—단 둘을 빼고는 아무도 없을 테니 말이다.\n\n그 둘은 레이철 아가씨의 양옆에 앉았는데, 그날의 여왕인 아가씨는 당연히 그 잔치의 큰 볼거리였다. 이 자리에서 아가씨는 특히나 모든 이의 눈길이 쏠리는 중심점이었으니, (마님이 남몰래 언짢아하시게도) 아가씨가 그 놀라운 생일 선물, 곧 나머지 모든 것을 무색하게 하는 그 월장석을 걸치고 있었기 때문이다. 그것은 아가씨 손에 쥐여졌을 때는 아무 테두리 장식이 없었으나, 저 만능 재주꾼 프랭클린 도련님이, 제 야무진 손가락과 은철사 한 오라기의 도움으로, 그것을 아가씨의 흰 드레스 가슴에 브로치로 고정해 놓았다. 모두가 그 다이아몬드의 엄청난 크기와 아름다움에 감탄한 것은 물론이었다. 그러나 그것을 두고 예사롭지 않은 말을 한 사람은 좌중에서 오직 둘뿐이었으니, 곧 내가 언급한, 레이철 아가씨의 오른편과 왼편에 앉은 그 두 손님이었다.\n\n아가씨 왼편의 손님은 캔디 씨, 곧 프리징홀에 있는 우리 의사였다.\n\n이 사람은 유쾌하고 붙임성 있는 자그마한 사내였으나, 다만 내가 인정해야 할 흠이 있었으니, 때를 가리지 않고 제 농담을 지나치게 즐기고, 우선 조심스레 실마리를 더듬어 볼 것도 없이 자못 무모하게 낯선 이들과의 이야기에 뛰어드는 것이었다. 사교장에서 그는 끊임없이 실수를 저지르고, 뜻하지 않게 사람들을 서로 다투게 만들었다. 의술을 펼 때 그는 더 신중한 사람이었다. (그의 적들의 말로는) 일종의 본능으로 분별을 주워 담아, 더 조심스레 진료하는 의사들이 그르다고 드러날 자리에서 대개는 옳다고 판명되곤 했다. 그가 레이철 아가씨께 그 다이아몬드에 대해 한 말은, 여느 때처럼, 어리둥절하게 하거나 농담하는 식으로 한 것이었다. 그는 (과학을 위해서라며) 그것을 제집으로 가져가 태우게 해 달라고 짐짓 엄숙히 아가씨께 청했다. \"우선 그것을 이만저만한 온도로 달구겠습니다, 레이철 아가씨.\" 하고 의사가 말한다. \"그런 다음 그것을 공기의 흐름에 쐬는 겁니다. 그러면, 조금씩 조금씩—확!—우리는 그 다이아몬드를 증발시켜, 값비싼 귀한 돌을 안전히 간수하는 일에 대한 온갖 근심에서 아가씨를 벗어나게 해 드리는 겁니다요!\" 얼굴에 자못 근심스러운 빛을 띠고 듣고 계시던 마님은, 그 의사가 진심이었더라면, 그리고 레이철 아가씨가 제 생일 선물을 희생할 만큼 과학의 대의에 열성적임을 그가 알아냈더라면 좋았으련만, 하고 바라시는 듯했다.\n\n우리 젊은 아가씨의 오른편에 앉은 다른 손님은 저명한 공인이었으니—다름 아닌 그 유명한 인도 여행가 머스웨이트 씨였다. 그는, 제 목숨을 걸고, 여태 어느 유럽인도 발을 들인 적 없는 곳에 변장하고 뚫고 들어간 인물이었다.\n\n이 사람은 키가 크고, 여위고, 근골이 질기고, 살빛이 검은, 과묵한 사내였다. 그에게는 지친 기색과, 아주 흔들림 없고 주의 깊은 눈이 있었다. 소문으로는, 그가 우리 고장 사람들 사이의 단조로운 삶에 진력이 나서, 다시 돌아가 동방의 거친 땅을 정처 없이 떠돌고 싶어 못 견딘다는 것이었다. 그가 레이철 아가씨께 그 보석에 대해 한 말을 빼면, 나는 그가 저녁 식사 내내 여섯 마디나 했는지, 포도주를 한 잔이라도 마셨는지조차 의심스럽다. 월장석만이 그가 조금이라도 관심을 둔 유일한 대상이었다. 그것의 명성이, 그의 방랑길이 뻗어 있던 그 위험한 인도의 어느 곳들에서, 그의 귀에까지 닿았던 모양이다. 그가 하도 오래 말없이 그것을 바라보아 레이철 아가씨가 거북해지기 시작할 무렵, 그는 그 차갑고 요지부동한 투로 아가씨께 말했다. \"혹 인도에 가시거든, 버린더 아가씨, 외숙부의 생일 선물은 지니고 가지 마십시오. 힌두교 다이아몬드는 때로 힌두교 종교의 일부입니다. 나는 어떤 도시를, 그리고 그 도시의 어떤 사원을 알고 있는데, 거기서라면 지금 아가씨 차림 그대로는 아가씨 목숨이 오 분어치도 못 될 겁니다.\" 영국에 안전히 있는 레이철 아가씨는, 인도에서의 제 위험 이야기를 듣고 자못 신이 났다. 통통이들은 한술 더 떠 신이 났다. 그들은 나이프와 포크를 쨍그랑 떨어뜨리며, 한목소리로 격하게 터뜨렸다. \"오! 어쩜 그렇게 흥미로울까!\" 마님은 의자에서 안절부절못하시며, 화제를 바꾸셨다.\n\n저녁 식사가 진행되면서, 나는 조금씩 조금씩, 이 잔치가 그 전의 여느 비슷한 잔치들이 잘 흘러간 것처럼 잘 흘러가고 있지 않음을 알아차렸다.\n\n이제 와서, 그 뒤에 일어난 일에 비추어 그 생일을 되돌아보면, 나는, 그 저주받은 다이아몬드가 온 좌중에 무슨 마름병을 드리운 것이 틀림없다는 생각이 반쯤 든다. 나는 그들에게 포도주를 넉넉히 권했고, 특권을 지닌 사람인지라, 인기 없는 요리를 들고 식탁을 돌며 좌중에게 은밀히 속삭였다. \"부디 마음을 바꾸어 이걸 좀 드셔 보십시오. 몸에 좋을 줄 제가 아니까요.\" 열에 아홉은 마음을 바꾸었으나—저희 오래되고 한결같은 베터리지를 봐서 그런다고 기꺼이 말해 주면서—죄다 아무 소용이 없었다. 저녁 식사가 진행되면서, 이야기에 침묵의 틈이 생겨, 나 자신이 다 거북해질 지경이었다. 그들이 다시 혀를 놀릴 때면, 아무 악의 없이, 더없이 불운한 방식으로, 있을 수 있는 가장 나쁜 목적으로 그것을 놀렸다. 이를테면 의사 캔디 씨는, 내가 그가 여태껏 하는 것을 본 것보다 더 불길한 소리들을 했다. 그가 어떤 식으로 굴었는지 한 가지 본보기만 들어 보시면, 잔치의 흥성을 마음에 두고 그 소임을 맡은 사람으로서 내가 식기대 곁에서 무엇을 견뎌 내야 했는지 여러분도 아시게 될 것이다.\n\n저녁 식사에 자리한 우리 숙녀분들 가운데, 지금은 고인이 된 같은 이름의 교수의 미망인, 훌륭한 스레드골 부인이 있었다. 이 어지신 부인은 돌아가신 남편 이야기를 노상 하면서도, 낯선 이들에게는 그가 고인이라는 것을 결코 입에 올리지 않았다. 짐작건대 부인은, 영국의 성한 몸을 가진 성인이라면 누구나 그쯤은 알고 있어야 마땅하다고 여겼던 모양이다. 침묵의 틈 하나에서, 누군가가 인체 해부라는 무미건조하고 자못 께름칙한 화제를 꺼냈다. 그러자 어지신 스레드골 부인은 여느 때처럼 대뜸 돌아가신 남편을 끌어들였는데, 그가 죽은 사람이라는 말은 하지 않았다. 부인은 해부학을 두고, 교수가 여가에 즐기던 취미라고 묘사했다. 불운하게도, 맞은편에 앉은 캔디 씨는 (그 돌아가신 신사에 대해 아무것도 몰랐는데) 부인의 말을 들었다. 더없이 예의 바른 사람인지라, 그는 그 자리에서 그 교수의 해부학적 취미를 거들 기회를 냉큼 붙잡았다.\n\n\"요즈음 외과 의사 학교에 유난히 훌륭한 골격 표본이 몇 점 들어왔답니다.\" 하고 캔디 씨가 식탁 너머로, 크고 명랑한 목소리로 말한다. \"부인, 교수님께 강력히 권합니다, 다음번에 한 시간 짬이 나거든 꼭 한번 가서 그것들을 구경하시라고요.\"\n\n핀 떨어지는 소리라도 들렸을 것이다. 좌중은 (교수의 넋을 기려) 죄다 말없이 앉아 있었다. 나는 그때 스레드골 부인 뒤에서, 부인께 백포도주 한 잔을 은밀히 권하고 있었다. 부인은 고개를 떨구고, 아주 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 남편은 이미 세상에 없어요.\"\n\n불운하게도 캔디 씨는, 아무것도 못 듣고, 진상을 짐작하기에는 십만 리나 떨어져 있는지라, 식탁 너머로 그 어느 때보다 더 크고 더 정중하게 이어 갔다.\n\n\"교수님은 아마 모르실 겁니다.\" 하고 그가 말한다. \"학교 회원의 명함이 있으면, 일요일만 빼고 아무 날에나, 열 시부터 네 시 사이에 입장하실 수 있다는 것을요.\"\n\n스레드골 부인은 고개를 제 옷깃 안으로 푹 떨구고, 더욱 나직한 목소리로, 그 엄숙한 말을 되풀이했다. \"내 사랑하는 남편은 이미 세상에 없어요.\"\n\n나는 식탁 너머로 캔디 씨에게 눈을 힘껏 찡긋했다. 레이철 아가씨는 그의 팔을 건드렸다. 마님은 그에게 이루 말 못 할 눈길을 보내셨다. 아주 헛일이었다! 그는, 도무지 멈출 수 없는 다정함으로, 계속해 나갔다. \"기꺼이 교수님께 제 명함을 보내 드리겠습니다.\" 하고 그가 말한다. \"지금 계신 주소를 일러 주시는 은혜를 베풀어 주신다면요.\"\n\n\"그이가 지금 있는 주소는요, 선생, 바로 무덤이랍니다.\" 하고 스레드골 부인이, 별안간 성을 내며, 술잔들이 다시 쨍 울릴 만큼 힘주어 노기 띤 목소리로 말한다. \"그 교수는 죽은 지 십 년이 되었어요.\"\n\n\"오, 맙소사!\" 하고 캔디 씨가 말한다. 웃음을 터뜨린 통통이들만 빼고, 이제 좌중에 어찌나 허탈한 정적이 내려앉던지, 그들 모두가 그 교수의 뒤를 따라, 그처럼 무덤 쪽에서 부름을 받고 있기라도 한 것 같았다.\n\n캔디 씨 이야기는 이쯤 해 두자. 나머지 사람들도 저마다 다른 식으로 그 의사 못지않게 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓았다. 마땅히 말해야 할 때 그들은 말하지 않았고, 말을 할 때면 노상 서로 딴 데를 겨누었다. 고드프리 씨는, 사람들 앞에서는 그토록 능변이면서도, 사석에서는 애써 나서기를 마다했다. 장미 정원에서 낭패를 본 뒤 그가 뾰로통한 것인지, 아니면 수줍은 것인지는 나로선 알 수 없다. 그는 제 곁에 앉은 부인(우리 집안 사람이었다)의 귀에만 대고 온갖 이야기를 도맡아 했다. 부인은 그의 위원회 여인 중 하나로—신앙심이 도타운 사람이었는데, 쇄골을 곱게 드러내고, 샴페인에 제법 취미가 있었다. 아시겠지만, 달지 않은 것으로, 그것도 넉넉히 좋아했다. 식기대 곁에서 이 두 사람 바로 뒤에 있던지라, 나는 그들 사이에 오간 것을 들은 바로 증언할 수 있으니, 좌중은 여간 유익한 대화를 많이 놓친 것이 아니었다. 나는 그것을, 코르크 마개를 뽑고 양고기를 저미고 하는 사이에 주워들었다. 저희 자선회에 대해 그들이 무슨 말을 했는지는 나는 못 들었다. 내가 그들 말에 귀 기울일 짬이 났을 때는, 그들은 저희가 해산을 도울 여자들이며 구제할 여자들 이야기에서 한참 멀리 나아가, 자못 진지한 주제를 놓고 갑론을박하고 있었다. (코르크와 저미기 사이에서 고드프리 씨가 하는 말을 내가 알아들은 바로는) 종교란 사랑을 뜻했다. 그리고 사랑이란 종교를 뜻했다. 그리고 이 땅은 조금 낡은 천국이었다. 그리고 천국은 새것처럼 다시 매만진 이 땅이었다. 이 땅에는 여간 아니꼬운 사람들이 좀 있었으나, 그것을 벌충하느라, 천국의 모든 여자는 결코 다투는 법 없는 어마어마한 위원회의 회원이 될 터이고, 모든 남자는 시중드는 천사가 되어 그들을 곁에서 받들 터였다. 아름답구려! 아름답구려! 그런데 대체 무엇 때문에 고드프리 씨는 그 이야기를 죄다 제 부인과 저 자신한테만 담아 두었을까?\n\n그럼 프랭클린 도련님은—여러분은 필시 이렇게 말씀하시리라, 프랭클린 도련님이 좌중을 부추겨 그 저녁을 유쾌하게 만들었으리라고?\n\n천만에! 그는 아주 기운을 되찾았고, 놀랍도록 힘차고 기운이 넘쳤으니, 짐작건대 페넬로피가 그에게 장미 정원에서의 고드프리 씨의 대접에 관해 일러 주었기 때문이다. 그러나 아무리 이야기를 해도, 열에 아홉은 그가 엉뚱한 화제를 골랐거나, 엉뚱한 사람에게 말을 건넸으니, 그 끝은 그가 몇몇의 비위를 거스르고 모두를 어리둥절하게 만드는 것이었다. 앞서 내가 언급한 그의 그 외국 수련—그의 프랑스식 면과 독일식 면과 이탈리아식 면이, 마님의 넉넉한 식탁에서, 더없이 얼떨떨한 방식으로 튀어나왔다.\n\n이를테면, 여러분은 어떻게 생각하시는가, 그가 유부녀가 제 남편 아닌 사내에게 얼마만큼까지 흠모를 품어도 되느냐 하는 정도를 논하며, 그것을 그 명석하고 재치 있는 프랑스식 투로, 프리징홀 교구 부목사의 노처녀 이모께 늘어놓은 것을? 여러분은 어떻게 생각하시는가, 그가 독일식 면으로 옮아가, 소에 관한 그 대단한 권위자인 이 영지의 지주가 황소 사육에 관한 제 경험을 늘어놓는 동안, 그에게, 경험이란 것은 제대로 이해하면 아무것도 아니며, 황소를 사육하는 올바른 길은 제 마음속을 깊이 들여다보아 거기서 완벽한 황소의 관념을 뽑아내어 그놈을 낳는 것이라고 말한 것을? 여러분은 뭐라 하시겠는가, 치즈와 샐러드가 나올 무렵, 영국에서의 민주주의 확산을 놓고 열이 오른 우리 고을 의원이 이렇게 터뜨렸을 때—\"우리가 우리의 유서 깊은 방벽을 한번 잃고 나면, 블레이크 씨, 감히 여쭙습니다만, 우리에게 대체 무엇이 남습니까?\"—프랭클린 도련님이 이탈리아식 관점에서 이렇게 대답한 것에 대해서 말이오. \"우리한텐 세 가지가 남지요, 의원님—사랑, 음악, 그리고 샐러드요.\" 그는 이런 돌출로 좌중을 질겁하게 했을 뿐 아니라, 때가 되어 그의 영국식 면이 튀어나오자, 그 외국식의 매끈함을 잃어버렸다. 그리고 의술이라는 주제에 들어서서는, 의사들을 조롱하는 그런 노골적인 말들을 해 대는 통에, 급기야 붙임성 좋고 자그마한 캔디 씨를 발끈하게 만들고 말았다.\n\n그 둘 사이의 언쟁은, 프랭클린 도련님이—어쩌다 그리 되었는지는 잊었으나—요즈음 밤에 잠을 몹시 설친다고 인정하도록 이끌린 데서 비롯됐다. 그러자 캔디 씨는 그에게, 신경이 죄다 어긋나 있으니 당장 약물 치료를 한 차례 받아야 한다고 일렀다. 프랭클린 도련님은, 약물 치료 한 차례나 어둠 속을 더듬는 것 한 차례나, 자기가 보기엔 그게 그거라고 대꾸했다. 캔디 씨는 날렵하게 되받아, 프랭클린 도련님 자신이야말로, 체질로 말하자면, 어둠 속에서 잠을 더듬고 있는 것이며, 약물 말고는 무엇도 그가 그것을 찾도록 도와줄 수 없다고 말했다. 프랭클린 도련님은, 제 쪽에서 공을 계속 받아넘기며, 소경이 소경을 인도한다는 말은 여러 번 들었으나, 그것이 무슨 뜻인지는 이제 처음으로 알겠다고 말했다. 이런 식으로 그들은, 치고 찌르며, 활발하게 그것을 이어 가다가, 둘 다 열이 올랐다—특히 캔디 씨는, 제 직업을 옹호하느라 어찌나 완전히 자제심을 잃던지, 마님이 끼어들어 그 언쟁을 그만두게 하지 않을 수 없었다. 이 부득이한 권위의 행사가 좌중의 흥에 마지막 불끄개를 덮어 버렸다. 이야기가 여기저기서 한두 번씩 잠깐 다시 솟구치기는 했으나, 거기엔 생기와 반짝임이 처량하리만치 없었다. 그 저녁 잔치를 악마가 (아니면 그 다이아몬드가) 사로잡고 있었다. 그리고 내 여주인이 일어서서, 숙녀분들에게 신사분들을 포도주 곁에 남겨 두고 물러나자는 신호를 보냈을 때, 모두에게 그것은 하나의 안도였다.\n\n내가 막 노에이블화이트 씨(집주인을 대신하고 있었다) 앞에 유리병들을 한 줄로 늘어놓았을 때, 테라스에서 소리가 하나 들려와, 그 즉시 나를 손님 앞의 예법에서 화들짝 벗어나게 했다. 프랭클린 도련님과 나는 서로를 마주 보았다. 그것은 인도 북소리였다. 내가 빵을 먹고 사는 것이 확실하듯, 여기, 월장석이 이 집으로 돌아온 것과 때를 맞추어 요술쟁이들이 우리에게 돌아오고 있었던 것이다!\n\n그들이 테라스 모퉁이를 돌아 시야에 들어오자, 나는 그들을 쫓아 보내려고 절뚝절뚝 걸어 나갔다. 그러나 불운하게도, 두 통통이가 나보다 한발 앞섰다. 그들은 인도인들이 재주 부리는 것을 보고 싶어 안달이 나서, 한 쌍의 쏘아 올린 불꽃처럼 테라스로 쌩 뛰쳐나갔다. 다른 숙녀분들이 뒤를 따랐고, 신사분들도 그들 쪽에서 나왔다. \"우리를 굽어살피소서!\" 소리를 채 하기도 전에, 그 악당들은 저희 인사(살람)를 올리고 있었고, 통통이들은 그 예쁘장한 어린 소년에게 입을 맞추고 있었다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 레이철 아가씨의 한쪽 곁에 붙어 섰고, 나는 아가씨 뒤에 자리 잡았다. 우리의 의심이 옳다면, 거기 아가씨가, 진상은 아무것도 모른 채, 제 드레스 가슴의 그 다이아몬드를 인도인들에게 내보이며 서 있는 셈이었다!\n\n그들이 무슨 재주를 부렸는지, 어떻게 했는지는 여러분께 말씀드릴 수 없다. 저녁 식사에 대한 언짢음에다, 그 악당들이 하필 절묘한 순간에 돌아와 제 눈으로 그 보석을 보게 된 것에 대한 부아까지 겹쳐, 나는, 실토하건대, 정신을 놓아 버렸다. 내가 알아차린 것으로 맨 처음 기억나는 것은, 그 자리에 인도 여행가 머스웨이트 씨가 별안간 나타난 것이었다. 지체 높은 분들이 서고 앉아 있던 그 반원을 빙 돌아, 그는 요술쟁이들 뒤로 조용히 다가가더니, 별안간 저희 나라 말로 그들에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n그가 그들을 총검으로 찔렀던들, 인도인들이, 그의 입에서 나온 첫마디를 들었을 때 그리한 것보다 더 호랑이 같은 날램으로 흠칫 놀라 그에게 홱 돌아설 수 있었을까 싶다. 다음 순간 그들은, 더없이 정중하고 뱀 같은 방식으로 그에게 절하고 인사를 올리고 있었다. 그 알 수 없는 말로 양쪽에서 몇 마디가 오간 뒤, 머스웨이트 씨는 다가올 때와 마찬가지로 조용히 물러났다. 통역 노릇을 하던 우두머리 인도인은, 그러자 다시 지체 높은 분들 쪽으로 홱 돌아섰다. 나는 그 사내의 커피빛 얼굴이, 머스웨이트 씨가 그에게 말을 건넨 뒤로 잿빛이 된 것을 알아차렸다. 그는 마님께 절하고, 공연이 끝났다고 아뢰었다. 통통이들은, 이루 말 못 하게 실망하여, 공연을 중단시킨 머스웨이트 씨를 겨냥해 큰 소리로 \"오!\"를 터뜨렸다. 우두머리 인도인은 제 가슴에 겸손히 손을 얹고, 요술이 끝났다고 두 번째로 말했다. 어린 소년이 모자를 들고 돌아다녔다. 숙녀분들은 응접실로 물러났고, 신사분들은 (프랭클린 도련님과 머스웨이트 씨만 빼고) 저희 포도주로 돌아갔다. 나와 하인은 인도인들을 따라가, 그들이 무사히 경내를 벗어나는 것을 지켜보았다.\n\n관목숲을 지나 돌아오다가, 나는 담배 냄새를 맡고, 프랭클린 도련님과 머스웨이트 씨가 (후자는 여송연을 피우며) 나무들 사이를 천천히 오르내리며 거니는 것을 보았다. 프랭클린 도련님이 내게 저희와 합류하라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"이 사람이,\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 그 위대한 여행가에게 나를 소개하며 말한다. \"게이브리얼 베터리지, 방금 제가 말씀드린 우리 집안의 오랜 하인이자 벗입니다. 부디, 방금 저한테 말씀하신 것을 이 사람한테도 말씀해 주시지요.\"\n\n머스웨이트 씨는 여송연을 입에서 떼고, 그 지친 투로, 나무 둥치에 기댔다.\n\n\"베터리지 씨.\" 그가 입을 열었다. \"저 인도인 셋은 당신이나 나만큼이나 요술쟁이가 아니올시다.\"\n\n이거야말로 새로운 놀라움이었다! 나는 자연히 그 여행가에게, 전에 그 인도인들을 만난 적이 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"전혀 없소.\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말한다. \"하지만 나는 인도의 요술이 실제로 어떤 것인지 아오. 오늘 밤 당신이 본 것은 죄다 그것을 아주 서투르고 어설프게 흉내 낸 것일 뿐이오. 오랜 경험 끝의 내가 아주 크게 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 저 사내들은 높은 계급의 브라만이오. 내가 그들에게 변장하고 있다고 몰아붙였더니, 힌두 사람들이 제 감정을 감추는 데 그토록 능한데도, 그것이 그들에게 어떻게 작용했는지 당신도 보셨소. 그들의 행실에는 내가 설명할 수 없는 수수께끼가 있소. 그들은 두 겹으로 제 계급을 희생했으니—첫째, 바다를 건넌 것이요, 둘째, 요술쟁이로 변장한 것이오. 그들이 사는 땅에서 그것은 어마어마한 희생이오. 그 밑바닥에는 여간 심각한 동기가 있는 게 틀림없고, 그들이 제 나라로 돌아가 제 계급을 되찾을 때 그들을 변호할 예사 아닌 어떤 명분이 있는 게 틀림없소.\"\n\n나는 벙어리가 되었다. 머스웨이트 씨는 제 여송연을 계속 피웠다. 프랭클린 도련님은, 내 보기엔 제 됨됨이의 서로 다른 면들 사이에서 잠시 남몰래 방향을 트는 듯싶더니, 이렇게 침묵을 깼다.\n\n\"저는 좀 망설여집니다, 머스웨이트 씨, 당신께 아무 흥미도 없으실, 그리고 제가 우리 울타리 밖에서는 그리 이야기하고 싶지 않은 집안일로 당신을 성가시게 해 드리는 것이요. 하지만 당신 말씀을 듣고 나니, 저는, 레이디 버린더와 그 따님을 위해서, 어쩌면 당신 손에 실마리를 쥐여 드릴지도 모를 무언가를 당신께 말씀드려야 한다는 의무를 느낍니다. 저는 은밀히 말씀드리는 것이니, 부디 그 점을 잊지 말아 주시는 은혜를 베풀어 주시리라 믿습니다.\"\n\n이 서두와 함께, 그는 떠는 모래펄에서 내게 들려주었던 모든 것을 그 인도 여행가에게 이야기했다. 그 요지부동한 머스웨이트 씨조차 듣는 이야기에 어찌나 흥미를 느꼈던지, 제 여송연이 꺼지도록 내버려 두었다.\n\n\"자.\" 하고 이야기를 마친 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"당신의 경험은 뭐라고 합니까?\"\n\n\"내 경험은,\" 하고 그 여행가가 대답했다. \"당신이, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨, 내가 내 목숨을 아슬아슬하게 건진 것보다 더 여러 번 당신 목숨을 아슬아슬하게 건졌다고 말하는구려. 그리고 그건 여간한 말이 아니오.\"\n\n이제 놀랄 차례는 프랭클린 도련님이었다.\n\n\"정말로 그렇게까지 심각합니까?\" 그가 물었다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 그렇소.\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 대답했다. \"당신이 내게 말한 것을 듣고 나니, 나는 그 월장석을 인도 우상의 이마에 있던 제자리로 되돌려 놓는 것이, 방금 내가 언급한 그 계급 희생의 동기이자 명분임을 의심할 수가 없소. 저 사내들은 고양이의 끈기로 제 기회를 기다릴 것이며, 호랑이의 사나움으로 그것을 쓸 것이오. 당신이 어떻게 그들에게서 벗어났는지 나는 짐작이 안 가는구려.\" 하고 그 저명한 여행가가, 제 여송연에 다시 불을 붙이고, 프랭클린 도련님을 뚫어져라 쳐다보며 말한다. \"당신은 그 다이아몬드를 여기와 런던에서 이리저리 가지고 다녔는데도, 아직 살아 있는 사람이오! 어디 한번 그 까닭을 대 봅시다. 런던에서 그 보석을 은행에서 찾아 나올 때, 두 번 다, 낮이었겠지요?\"\n\n\"훤한 대낮이었지요.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다.\n\n\"그리고 거리엔 사람이 많았고요?\"\n\n\"많았지요.\"\n\n\"당신은, 물론, 레이디 버린더의 집에 어느 시각에 도착하기로 정해 두었겠지요? 여기와 역 사이는 인적 드문 벌판이잖소. 그 약속을 지켰소?\"\n\n\"아니요. 저는 약속보다 네 시간 일찍 도착했지요.\"\n\n\"그 처사에 축하를 드리는 바요! 여기 이 읍내 은행에는 그 다이아몬드를 언제 가져갔소?\"\n\n\"이 집에 그것을 가져온 지 한 시간 뒤에 가져갔지요—그리고 이 근방에서 누가 저를 볼 채비를 하기 세 시간 전에요.\"\n\n\"다시 한번 축하를 드리는 바요! 그것을 여기로 도로 가져올 때는 혼자였소?\"\n\n\"아니요. 마침 제 사촌들과 마부와 함께 말을 타고 왔지요.\"\n\n\"세 번째로 축하를 드리는 바요! 혹 문명의 경계 너머로 여행하고 싶은 마음이 들거든, 블레이크 씨, 내게 알려 주시오. 내가 함께 가리다. 당신은 운 좋은 사람이오.\"\n\n여기서 나는 끼어들었다. 이런 식의 이야기는 내 영국식 생각과는 도무지 맞아떨어지지 않았다.\n\n\"정말로, 선생,\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"그자들이 저희 다이아몬드를 손에 넣으려고, 프랭클린 도련님이 틈을 주었더라면, 도련님 목숨을 빼앗았으리라는 말씀입니까?\"\n\n\"당신은 담배를 피우시오, 베터리지 씨?\" 하고 그 여행가가 말한다.\n\n\"예, 그렇습니다요.\"\n\n\"담뱃대를 비울 때 그 속에 남은 재를 당신은 대수롭게 여기시오?\"\n\n\"아닙니다요, 선생.\"\n\n\"저 사내들이 온 나라에서는, 사람을 죽이는 일을, 당신이 담뱃대에서 재를 털어 내는 일만큼이나 대수롭게 여긴다오. 만일 천 명의 목숨이 그들과 저희 다이아몬드를 되찾는 일 사이를 가로막고 있다면—그리고 만일 그들이 발각되지 않고 그 목숨들을 없앨 수 있다고 여긴다면—그들은 그것을 죄다 빼앗을 것이오. 계급의 희생은, 정 그러시다면, 인도에서 심각한 일이오. 목숨의 희생은 아무것도 아니오.\"\n\n나는 이에 대해 내 의견을 밝혔으니, 그들이 사람 죽이는 도둑 떼라는 것이었다. 머스웨이트 씨는 그들이 놀라운 민족이라는 자기 의견을 밝혔다. 아무 의견도 밝히지 않은 프랭클린 도련님이, 우리를 당면한 문제로 도로 데려왔다.\n\n\"그들은 버린더 아가씨의 드레스에서 그 월장석을 봤습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"어찌해야 합니까?\"\n\n\"당신 외숙부가 하겠다고 으름장 놓은 대로 하시오.\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 대답했다. \"헌캐슬 대령은 자기가 상대해야 할 사람들을 잘 알고 있었소. 그 다이아몬드를 내일 (한 사람 이상의 호위를 붙여) 암스테르담으로 보내 쪼개게 하시오. 그것을 하나 대신 여섯 개의 다이아몬드로 만드시오. 그러면 월장석으로서의 그 성스러운 정체도 끝이 나고—그 음모도 끝이 나는 것이오.\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님이 나를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"달리 도리가 없군.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리는 내일 레이디 버린더께 말씀드려야 하네.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은요, 도련님?\" 나는 물었다. \"만일 인도인들이 다시 온다면요?\"\n\n머스웨이트 씨가, 프랭클린 도련님이 미처 말하기 전에, 내게 대답했다.\n\n\"인도인들은 오늘 밤 다시 오는 모험은 하지 않을 것이오.\" 그가 말했다. \"곧장 가는 길은 그들이 무슨 일에든 좀처럼 택하지 않는 길이오—더구나 이런 일에서야, 아주 사소한 실수 하나가 저희 목적에 이르는 데 치명적일 수 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 그 악당들이 선생 생각보다 더 대담하다면요?\" 나는 물고 늘어졌다.\n\n\"그럴 경우엔,\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말한다. \"개들을 풀어놓으시오. 마당에 큰 개가 있소?\"\n\n\"둘 있습니다요, 선생. 마스티프 한 마리와 블러드하운드 한 마리요.\"\n\n\"그거면 되겠소. 지금 이 위급한 때에, 베터리지 씨, 그 마스티프와 블러드하운드에게는 큰 장점이 하나 있소—그놈들은 사람 목숨의 신성함에 대한 당신의 그 거리낌 따위로 골머리를 앓을 것 같지가 않다는 점 말이오.\"\n\n그가 그 화살을 내게 쏘아 붙일 때, 응접실에서 피아노 퉁기는 소리가 우리에게 들려왔다. 그는 제 여송연을 내던지고, 숙녀분들에게로 돌아가려고 프랭클린 도련님의 팔을 잡았다. 그들을 따라 집으로 가는데, 나는 하늘이 빠르게 흐려지고 있음을 알아차렸다. 머스웨이트 씨도 그것을 알아차렸다. 그는 그 무미건조하고 웅얼거리는 투로 나를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"인도인들은 오늘 밤 우산이 필요하겠구려, 베터리지 씨!\"\n\n그야 그로서는 농담해도 좋았다. 그러나 나는 저명한 여행가가 아니었고—이 세상에서 내가 걸어온 길은, 땅끝의 낯선 곳들에서 도둑과 살인자들 틈에 끼어 내 목숨을 물수제비 뜨듯 하는 데로 나를 이끌지 않았다. 나는 내 조그만 방으로 들어가, 땀에 젖은 채 의자에 주저앉아, 다음엔 무엇을 해야 하나 하고 속수무책으로 골똘했다. 이런 근심스러운 심경이면, 다른 사내들은 결국 저 스스로를 부추겨 열병이 나고 말았을지도 모른다. 나는 다른 식으로 끝을 맺었다. 나는 담뱃대에 불을 붙이고, 「로빈슨 크루소」를 한 대목 들췄다.\n\n그것에 매달린 지 오 분도 채 되기 전에, 나는 이 놀라운 대목—백예순한 쪽—에 이르렀으니, 이러하였다.\n\n\"위험에 대한 두려움은, 눈에 뻔히 보이는 위험 그 자체보다 만 곱절이나 더 무섭다. 그리고 우리는 근심의 짐이, 우리가 근심하는 그 화(禍)보다 훨씬 더 무거움을 알게 된다.\"\n\n이러고도 「로빈슨 크루소」를 믿지 않는 사람은, 그 머릿속 나사 하나가 풀린 사람이거나, 제 자만의 안개 속에서 길을 잃은 사람이다! 그런 사람에게 논변은 부질없는 것이요, 딱한 마음일랑 좀 더 발랄한 믿음을 지닌 다른 사람을 위해 아껴 두는 편이 낫다.\n\n내가 두 번째 담뱃대에 한창 빠져, 여전히 그 놀라운 책에 감탄하여 넋을 잃고 있는데, (차를 돌리던) 페넬로피가 응접실에서의 보고를 들고 들어왔다. 그 애는 통통이들이 이중창을 부르는 것을 두고 나왔는데—커다란 \"오!\"로 시작하는 노랫말에, 거기에 맞춘 곡이었다. 그 애가 눈여겨본 바로, 마님이 우리가 여태껏 모신 경험으로는 처음으로, 휘스트 놀이에서 실수를 하셨다. 그 애는 그 위대한 여행가가 한구석에서 잠든 것을 보았다. 그 애는 프랭클린 도련님이, 대체로 부인 자선회를 도마에 올려 고드프리 씨에게 제 재치를 벼리는 것을 어깨너머로 들었다. 그리고 고드프리 씨가, 제 그 자비로운 성품에 어울리는 것보다는 자못 날렵하게 그에게 되받아치는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그 애는 레이철 아가씨가, 겉으로는 스레드골 부인께 사진 몇 장을 보여 주며 부인을 달래는 척하면서, 실은 프랭클린 도련님에게 몰래 눈길을 훔치는 일에 골몰해 있는 것을 알아챘으니, 그것은 총명한 하녀라면 단 한순간도 잘못 해석할 수 없는 눈길이었다. 끝으로, 그 애는 의사 캔디 씨가 안 보이는 것을 알아챘는데, 그는 응접실에서 알 수 없이 사라졌다가, 그러고는 알 수 없이 돌아와, 고드프리 씨와 이야기에 들어간 것이었다. 대체로, 일은 저녁 식사의 경험이 우리에게 기대할 자격을 준 것보다는 더 잘 흘러가고 있었다. 우리가 한 시간만 더 버틸 수 있다면, 늙은 시간 노인이 그들의 마차를 몰고 와, 그들을 죄다 우리에게서 덜어 줄 터였다.\n\n이 세상 무엇이나 닳아 없어지는 법이니, 「로빈슨 크루소」의 그 위안이 되는 효험조차, 페넬로피가 나를 두고 나간 뒤로는 닳아 없어졌다. 나는 다시 안절부절못하게 되어, 비가 오기 전에 영지를 한 바퀴 살펴보기로 마음먹었다. 코가 사람의 것이라 어떤 위급한 때에도 쓸모없는 그 하인을 데려가는 대신, 나는 블러드하운드를 데리고 갔다. 낯선 자를 알아채는 그놈의 코라면 믿을 만했다. 우리는 경내를 죄다 돌고, 한길로 나갔다가—숨어 있는 사람 따위는 어디서도 찾아내지 못한 채, 나갈 때만큼이나 아는 것 없이 돌아왔다.\n\n마차들의 도착은 곧 비의 도착을 알리는 신호였다. 밤새 퍼부을 작정인 듯 비가 쏟아졌다. 이륜마차가 기다리고 있던 그 의사만 빼고, 나머지 손님들은 지붕 있는 밀폐 마차를 타고 아늑하게 집으로 갔다. 나는 캔디 씨에게 흠뻑 젖으실까 걱정된다고 말했다. 그는 그 답례로, 의사의 살갗이 방수라는 것도 모르고 내가 이 나잇살을 먹은 것이 놀랍다고 내게 말했다. 그러고는 제 조그만 농담에 껄껄 웃으며 빗속으로 마차를 몰아갔다. 그리하여 우리는 저녁 손님들을 다 치웠다.\n\n다음으로 할 이야기는 그 밤의 이야기다.\n\n# CHAPTER XI\n\n마지막 손님이 마차를 몰고 떠난 뒤, 나는 안쪽 현관으로 돌아가, 새뮤얼이 곁 탁자에서 브랜디와 소다수를 맡아 지키고 있는 것을 보았다. 마님과 레이철 아가씨가 응접실에서 나오셨고, 두 신사가 그 뒤를 따랐다. 고드프리 씨는 브랜디에 소다수를 타 마셨고, 프랭클린 도련님은 아무것도 들지 않았다. 그는 죽도록 지친 얼굴로 앉았다. 짐작건대 이 생일 자리에서의 이야기가 그에게 너무 벅찼던 모양이다.\n\n마님이 그분들께 잘 자라는 인사를 하려고 돌아서시다가, 당신 따님의 드레스에서 빛나고 있는 그 사악한 대령의 유산을 뚫어져라 바라보셨다.\n\n\"레이철.\" 마님이 물으셨다. \"네 다이아몬드를 오늘 밤 어디에 둘 참이냐?\"\n\n레이철 아가씨는 잔뜩 들뜬 기분이었고, 마침 허튼소리를 늘어놓으며 그것을 사리에 맞는 말인 양 짐짓 우기고 드는 그런 심사였으니, 여러분도 젊은 처녀들이 신나는 하루의 끝에 잔뜩 흥분했을 때 이따금 그런 모습을 보신 적이 있으리라. 처음에 아가씨는 그 다이아몬드를 어디 둬야 할지 모르겠다고 잘라 말했다. 그러더니 \"그야 물론 다른 것들과 함께 제 화장대에\" 두겠다고 했다. 그러더니 그 다이아몬드가 어둠 속에서 그 무시무시한 달빛으로 저 혼자 빛을 내기 시작할지도 모른다는 것을—그리고 그것이 한밤중에 저를 질겁하게 하리라는 것을 떠올렸다. 그러더니 제 거실에 놓인 인도식 캐비닛을 생각해 냈다. 그러고는 대뜸, 두 아름다운 토산품이 서로 감탄하도록 해 주려는 뜻에서, 그 인도 다이아몬드를 그 인도식 캐비닛에 넣기로 마음먹었다. 아가씨의 그 자그마한 허튼소리의 물줄기가 거기까지 흘러가도록 놔두시던 그 어머니가, 끼어들어 아가씨를 멈추셨다.\n\n\"얘야! 네 인도식 캐비닛은 자물쇠가 없잖니.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하신다.\n\n\"어머, 엄마!\" 하고 레이철 아가씨가 외쳤다. \"여기가 무슨 여관이에요? 이 집에 도둑이라도 있어요?\"\n\n이 엉뚱한 말투에는 아랑곳없이, 마님은 신사분들께 잘 자라는 인사를 하셨다. 그러고는 레이철 아가씨께 돌아서서, 아가씨에게 입을 맞추셨다. \"오늘 밤 그 다이아몬드를 내가 대신 맡아 주면 안 되겠니?\" 하고 물으셨다.\n\n레이철 아가씨는 그 제안을, 십 년 전이었다면 제게서 새 인형을 떼어 놓겠다는 제안을 받았을 법한 그 심정으로 받아들였다. 마님은 그날 밤 아가씨와는 이치를 따질 수 없음을 아셨다. \"내일 아침 맨 먼저 내 방으로 오너라, 레이철.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"너한테 할 말이 있을 게다.\" 그 마지막 말을 남기고 마님은 천천히 우리를 떠나셨다. 당신 나름의 생각에 잠긴 채, 그리고 겉보기에, 그 생각이 당신을 이끌어 가는 그 방향이 영 마뜩잖으신 채로.\n\n다음으로 잘 자라는 인사를 한 것은 레이철 아가씨였다. 아가씨는 먼저, 현관 저쪽 끝에서 그림 한 점을 보고 서 있던 고드프리 씨와 악수했다. 그러고는 여전히 한구석에 지치고 말없이 앉아 있는 프랭클린 도련님께로 돌아섰다.\n\n그 두 사람 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔는지는 나는 알 수 없다. 그러나 우리 큰 거울을 끼운 그 낡은 참나무 틀 가까이 서 있던 나는, 거울에 비친 아가씨가, 프랭클린 도련님이 아가씨께 준 그 로켓을 제 드레스 가슴에서 슬며시 빼내어, 잠시 그에게 내보이는 것을 보았다. 잠자리로 총총 떠나기 전에, 분명 예사롭지 않은 무언가를 뜻하는 미소와 함께. 이 일은, 내가 여태 내 판단에 품고 있던 그 믿음을 조금 휘청하게 했다. 나는, 어쩌면 제 젊은 여주인의 마음 상태에 관해 페넬로피가 결국 옳았을지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n레이철 아가씨가 그의 볼 눈을 남겨 두고 떠나자마자, 프랭클린 도련님이 나를 알아보았다. 무엇에나 이리저리 옮아 다니는 그의 변덕스러운 기분이, 이미 그 인도인들 쪽으로도 옮아가 있었다.\n\n\"베터리지.\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 관목숲에서 그 이야기를 나눌 때, 내가 머스웨이트 씨를 너무 진지하게 받아들인 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 반쯤 드는군. 그가 우리한테 제 여행담 나부랭이를 시험해 본 게 아닌가 싶어. 자네 정말로 개들을 풀어놓을 참인가?\"\n\n\"그놈들 목줄을 벗겨 주겠습니다요, 도련님.\" 나는 대답했다. \"그러고는 그놈들이 무슨 낌새를 맡거든 밤중에 한 바퀴 돌 수 있게 풀어 두겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋아.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"무엇을 할지는 내일 보기로 하지. 나는, 베터리지, 아주 절박한 까닭이 없이는 이모님을 놀라게 해 드릴 마음이 조금도 없네. 잘 자게.\"\n\n그가 내게 고개를 끄덕이고 위층으로 가려고 제 촛불을 집어 들 때 어찌나 초췌하고 창백해 보이던지, 나는 자기 전에 잠자리 술로 브랜디에 물을 조금 타 드시라고 주제넘게 권해 보았다. 현관 저쪽 끝에서 우리 쪽으로 걸어오던 고드프리 씨가 내 편을 들었다. 그는 더없이 다정한 태도로, 자기 전에 뭐라도 좀 들라고 프랭클린 도련님을 채근했다.\n\n내가 이 자질구레한 정황들을 굳이 적어 두는 것은, 그날 내가 보고 들은 모든 것에도 불구하고, 우리 두 신사가 여느 때와 다름없이 좋은 사이임을 보게 되어 흐뭇했기 때문이다. (페넬로피가 응접실에서 들은) 그들의 말싸움과, 레이철 아가씨의 총애 속에서 가장 좋은 자리를 차지하려는 그들의 경쟁도, 그들 사이에 이렇다 할 골을 남기지는 않은 듯했다. 하지만 어쩌겠는가! 그들은 둘 다 성미가 좋았고, 둘 다 세상 물정에 밝은 사람들이었다. 그리고 지체 있는 사람들에게는 분명 이 장점이 있으니, 그들은 지체라곤 전혀 없는 사람들만큼 저희끼리 그리 잘 다투지 않는다는 것이다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 브랜디 물을 마다하고, 고드프리 씨와 함께 위층으로 올라갔으니, 두 사람 방이 서로 나란히 붙어 있었다. 그러나 층계참에서, 사촌이 그를 설득했든지, 아니면 그가 여느 때처럼 방향을 틀어 마음을 바꿨든지 했다. \"어쩌면 밤중에 그게 당길지도 모르지.\" 그가 내게 아래로 대고 외쳤다. \"브랜디에 물 좀 타서 내 방으로 올려 보내 주게.\"\n\n나는 새뮤얼 편에 브랜디 물을 올려 보냈다. 그러고는 밖으로 나가 개들의 목줄을 끌렀다. 그 두 놈은 그런 밤 시각에 풀려나자 놀라움에 정신을 잃고, 한 쌍의 강아지처럼 내게 껑충 뛰어올랐다! 그렇지만 비가 곧 그놈들을 다시 가라앉혔다. 그놈들은 저마다 물 한 모금씩을 핥아 마시고는, 슬금슬금 제 개집으로 기어들어 갔다. 집으로 들어가면서, 나는 날씨가 개는 쪽으로 바뀔 조짐을 하늘에서 알아차렸다. 당장은, 여전히 비가 세차게 퍼붓고, 땅은 온통 질척였다.\n\n새뮤얼과 나는 집을 죄다 돌며 여느 때처럼 문단속을 했다. 나는 모든 것을 손수 살폈고, 이번만큼은 내 부관에게 아무것도 맡기지 않았다. 내가 자정과 새벽 한 시 사이에 늙은 뼈를 침상에 뉘었을 때, 모든 것이 안전하고 단단했다.\n\n짐작건대, 그날의 온갖 근심이 내게 조금 벅찼던 모양이다. 아무튼, 나는 그날 밤 프랭클린 도련님의 그 병을 조금 앓았다. 마침내 잠에 빠져든 것은 해가 뜰 무렵이었다. 내가 깨어 누워 있는 동안 내내 집은 무덤처럼 고요했다. 빗물이 철벅이는 소리와, 아침과 함께 산들바람이 일면서 나무들 사이로 바람이 한숨짓는 소리 말고는, 아무 소리도 움직이지 않았다.\n\n일곱 시 반쯤에 나는 깨어, 화창한 햇살이 비치는 날에 창을 열었다. 시계가 여덟 시를 쳤고, 나는 막 다시 개들을 사슬에 매러 나가려던 참인데, 내 뒤 계단에서 별안간 페티코트가 휙휙 스치는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n내가 돌아서니, 거기 페넬로피가 미친 듯이 나를 뒤쫓아 뛰어 내려오고 있었다. \"아버지!\" 그 애가 비명을 질렀다. \"제발 위층으로 올라오세요! 다이아몬드가 사라졌어요!\"\n\n\"너 정신이 나갔니?\" 나는 그 애에게 물었다.\n\n\"사라졌어요!\" 하고 페넬로피가 말한다. \"어떻게 된 건지 아무도 몰라요! 올라와서 보세요.\"\n\n그 애가 나를 끌고 우리 젊은 아가씨의 거실로 들어갔는데, 그 방은 아가씨의 침실로 통해 있었다. 거기, 침실 문지방에, 레이철 아가씨가, 제가 걸친 흰 실내복만큼이나 낯빛이 하얗게 질려 서 있었다. 거기 또, 인도식 캐비닛의 두 문짝이 활짝 열려 있었다. 그 안의 서랍 하나가 끝까지 잡아 빼내진 채였다.\n\n\"보세요!\" 하고 페넬로피가 말한다. \"어젯밤 레이철 아가씨가 그 서랍에 다이아몬드를 넣으시는 걸 제가 이 눈으로 봤다니까요.\" 나는 캐비닛으로 갔다. 서랍은 텅 비어 있었다.\n\n\"이게 사실입니까, 아가씨?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n제 자신 같지 않은 표정으로, 제 자신 같지 않은 목소리로, 레이철 아가씨는 내 딸이 대답했던 그대로 대답했다.\n\n\"다이아몬드가 사라졌어요!\"\n\n그 말을 하고서, 아가씨는 제 침실로 물러나, 문을 닫고 잠갔다.\n\n우리가 다음엔 어느 쪽으로 몸을 돌려야 할지 알기도 전에, 당신 따님의 거실에서 나는 내 목소리를 듣고 무슨 일인가 싶으셨던 마님이 들어오셨다. 다이아몬드를 잃어버렸다는 소식이 마님을 돌처럼 굳게 하는 듯했다. 마님은 곧장 레이철 아가씨의 침실로 가시더니, 들여보내 달라고 우기셨다. 레이철 아가씨가 마님을 들여보냈다.\n\n불길처럼 온 집안을 내달린 그 놀라움이, 다음으로 두 신사에게 옮아붙었다.\n\n고드프리 씨가 제 방에서 맨 먼저 나왔다. 무슨 일이 있었는지 듣고서 그가 한 일이라곤, 어리둥절하여 두 손을 치켜드는 것뿐이었으니, 그것은 그의 타고난 심지의 굳음을 그리 대변해 주지 못했다. 우리에게 조언을 해 주리라 내가 자신 있게 기대던 그 맑은 머리의 프랭클린 도련님도, 제 차례로 그 소식을 듣고서는, 제 사촌만큼이나 속수무책인 듯했다. 웬일로, 그는 마침내 하룻밤 푹 잤던 것이다. 그리고 그 낯선 잠의 호사가, 제 말로는, 그를 얼떨떨하게 만들어 놓은 모양이었다. 그렇지만 그가 커피 한 잔을 마시고 나자—그는 외국식으로 늘 아침을 먹기 몇 시간 전에 커피를 마셨다—그의 머리가 환해졌다. 그의 명민한 면이 튀어나왔고, 그는 다음과 같이, 결연하고 영리하게, 그 일을 손에 잡았다.\n\n그는 먼저 하인들을 불러, 아래층의 모든 문과 창을 (내가 열어 둔 현관문만 빼고) 밤에 우리가 문단속했을 때 그대로 놔두라고 일렀다. 그다음으로 그는, 우리가 더 나아간 조처를 취하기에 앞서, 그 다이아몬드가 어쩌다 어딘가 눈에 안 띄는 데로 떨어진 것은 아닌지—이를테면 캐비닛 뒤쪽이나, 캐비닛이 놓인 탁자 뒤로—확실히 해 두자고 제 사촌과 내게 제안했다. 그 두 군데를 다 뒤져 보고 아무것도 찾지 못한 뒤—또한 페넬로피에게 물어보았으나 그 애가 이미 내게 말한 그 얼마 말고는 더 알아내지 못한 뒤—프랭클린 도련님은 다음으로 우리 조사를 레이철 아가씨께로 넓히자고 제안하며, 페넬로피를 보내 아가씨 침실 문을 두드리게 했다.\n\n마님이 그 두드림에 대답하시고, 뒤로 문을 닫으셨다. 그다음 순간 우리는 레이철 아가씨가 안에서 그것을 잠그는 소리를 들었다. 내 여주인이 우리 가운데로 나오셨는데, 몹시 난감하고 괴로운 낯빛이셨다. \"다이아몬드를 잃은 것이 레이철을 아주 압도해 버린 모양이야.\" 하고 마님이 프랭클린 도련님께 대답하여 말씀하셨다. \"그 애가, 참으로 이상하게도, 나한테조차 그 이야기를 하기를 움츠리는구나. 지금은 도저히 그 애를 볼 수가 없겠어.\"\n\n레이철 아가씨에 관한 이 이야기로 우리의 곤혹스러움을 더해 놓으시고서, 마님은, 잠시 애쓴 끝에, 여느 때의 침착함을 되찾아, 여느 때의 결단으로 움직이셨다.\n\n\"달리 도리가 없겠지?\" 하고 마님이 조용히 말씀하셨다. \"경찰을 부르는 것 말고는 내게 다른 방도가 없겠지?\"\n\n\"그리고 경찰이 맨 먼저 할 일은,\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 마님의 말을 받아 덧붙였다. \"어젯밤 여기서 재주를 부린 그 인도인 요술쟁이들을 잡아들이는 것입니다.\"\n\n마님과 고드프리 씨는 (프랭클린 도련님과 내가 아는 것을 알지 못한지라) 둘 다 흠칫 놀라, 둘 다 어리둥절한 낯빛이 되었다.\n\n\"지금은 제가 다 설명해 드릴 겨를이 없습니다.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 이어 갔다. \"다만, 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 훔친 게 틀림없다는 것만은 말씀드릴 수 있어요. 소개장을 하나 써 주십시오.\" 하고 그가 마님께 말한다. \"프리징홀의 치안판사 중 한 사람 앞으로—그저, 제가 이모님의 이익과 뜻을 대신한다고만 적어서요. 그러면 제가 그것을 들고 당장 말을 달려가겠습니다. 도둑을 잡을 우리 승산이, 쓸데없는 일 분도 허비하지 않는 데 달렸을지도 모르니까요.\" (참고삼아 이르는 말이오나, 그것이 프랑스식 면이었든 영국식 면이었든, 프랭클린 도련님의 옳은 면이 이제 가장 위로 올라온 듯했다. 다만 물음은 하나, 그것이 얼마나 오래갈 것인가였다.)\n\n그는 이모 앞에 펜과 잉크와 종이를 내놓았고, 마님은 (내가 보기엔) 그가 원하는 편지를 자못 마지못해 쓰셨다. 만일 이만 파운드짜리 보석을 잃어버린 그런 일을 못 본 척 넘길 수만 있었던들, 나는 믿거니와—돌아가신 오라비에 대한 마님의 생각과, 그의 생일 선물에 대한 마님의 불신을 헤아리면—그 도둑들이 월장석을 가지고 아무 벌도 안 받고 달아나게 놔두는 것이 마님께는 남몰래 오히려 안도가 되었을 것이다.\n\n나는 프랭클린 도련님과 함께 마구간으로 나가, 그 인도인들이 (물론 나도 그만큼이나 예리하게 의심하고 있었다) 대체 어떻게 집 안에 들어올 수 있었겠느냐고 그에게 물어볼 기회를 잡았다.\n\n\"저녁 손님들이 돌아갈 때 그 혼란을 틈타, 그중 하나가 현관으로 슬쩍 들어왔을 수 있지.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"이모님과 레이철이 그 다이아몬드를 밤에 어디에 둘지 이야기하는 동안, 그 녀석이 소파 밑에 숨어 있었을 수도 있고. 그러면 집이 조용해질 때까지 기다리기만 하면 되는 거지. 그러면 그게 캐비닛 안에, 그냥 집어 가기만 하면 되게 놓여 있을 테니까.\" 그 말과 함께, 그는 마부에게 문을 열라고 외치고는, 말을 달려 떠났다.\n\n이것이 분명 유일하게 이치에 닿는 설명인 듯했다. 그러나 그 도둑은 어떻게 집에서 빠져나갈 수 있었을까? 나는 아침에 일어나 현관문을 열러 갔을 때, 그것이 밤에 내가 놔둔 그대로 잠기고 빗장까지 걸려 있는 것을 보았다. 다른 문과 창들로 말하면, 거기 그것들이 여전히, 죄다 안전하고 단단히 잠긴 채, 스스로 증언하고 있었다. 개들 또한? 그 도둑이 위층 창 하나에서 뛰어내려 달아났다 치자, 그럼 그가 어떻게 개들에게서 벗어났단 말인가? 그가 개들을 위해 약을 탄 고기를 마련해 왔던가? 그 의심이 내 머리를 스치는 바로 그때, 개들 자신이 모퉁이를 돌아 내게로 껑충껑충 달려오더니, 젖은 풀 위에서 서로 뒹굴며, 어찌나 팔팔하게 건강과 기운이 넘치던지, 나는 여간 애를 먹고서야 그놈들을 얌전하게 만들어 다시 사슬에 맬 수 있었다. 그것을 마음속으로 곱씹으면 곱씹을수록, 프랭클린 도련님의 설명은 점점 더 미덥지 못해 보였다.\n\n우리는 아침을 먹었다—집에서 무슨 일이 일어나든, 강도든 살인이든, 그런 건 아무래도 좋고, 아침은 먹어야 하는 법이다. 우리가 다 먹고 나자, 마님이 나를 부르셨다. 그리고 나는, 그 인도인들과 그들의 흉계에 관해 여태 내가 감추어 온 모든 것을 마님께 말씀드리지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 담이 큰 여인인지라, 마님은 내가 알린 것의 그 첫 충격을 곧 이겨 내셨다. 마님의 마음은 그 이교도 악당들과 그들의 음모보다도 당신 따님을 두고 훨씬 더 어지러운 듯했다. \"자네도 레이철이 얼마나 별난지, 그리고 이따금 다른 처녀들과 얼마나 다르게 구는지 알잖나.\" 마님이 내게 말씀하셨다. \"하지만 나는, 여태 겪은 모든 경험을 통틀어도, 그 애가 지금처럼 그렇게 이상하고 그렇게 속을 닫는 것을 본 적이 없어. 제 보석을 잃은 것이 그 애 머리를 거의 돌게 만든 것 같아. 그 끔찍한 다이아몬드가 그토록 짧은 사이에 그 애를 그토록 사로잡을 줄 누가 생각이나 했겠나?\"\n\n그것은 분명 이상한 일이었다. 대체로 장난감이며 노리개로 말하면, 레이철 아가씨는 여느 젊은 처녀들만큼 그런 것에 사족을 못 쓰는 편은 결코 아니었다. 그런데도 아가씨는, 여전히 제 침실에 틀어박혀 달랠 길 없이 있었다. 공평을 위해 덧붙이자면, 이 집에서 여느 궤도 밖으로 튕겨 나간 사람이 아가씨만은 아니었다. 이를테면 고드프리 씨는—직업상 이를테면 만인의 위로자 같은 사람이면서도—제 밑천을 어디서 찾아야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 듯했다. 즐겁게 해 줄 친구도 없고, 곤경에 처한 여자들에 관한 제 경험이 레이철 아가씨를 달래는 데 무엇을 할 수 있을지 시험해 볼 기회도 얻지 못한지라, 그는 목적 없이 불안한 걸음으로 집과 정원 이곳저곳을 배회했다. 우리에게 닥친 그 불행 뒤에, 자기가 어떻게 처신해야 마땅한지를 두고 그는 두 마음이었다. 지금 이 형편에서, 손님으로서의 자기라는 짐을 이 집안에서 덜어 드려야 할까, 아니면 제 하찮은 시중이나마 얼마간 쓸모가 있을지 모른다는 가망을 걸고 눌러 있어야 할까? 그는 마침내, 이렇게 여간 유별난 집안의 곤경에서는 아마 마지막 쪽이 가장 관례에 맞고 사려 깊은 처사이리라고 결정했다. 정황은 사내가 실제로 어떤 쇠붙이로 만들어졌는지를 시험한다. 정황에 시험받은 고드프리 씨는, 내가 여겼던 것보다 더 무른 쇠붙이임을 스스로 드러냈다. 여자 하인들로 말하면—홀로 지내던 로재나 스피어먼만 빼고—그들은 구석에서 서로 수군거리며, 아무것도 아닌 것을 미심쩍게 노려보기 시작했으니, 집에 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 일이 일어났을 때 인간이라는 무리의 그 여린 절반이 으레 그러하듯 말이다. 나 자신도 안절부절못하고 심사가 사나웠음을 인정한다. 그 저주받은 월장석이 우리 모두를 발칵 뒤집어 놓았던 것이다.\n\n열한 시가 조금 못 되어 프랭클린 도련님이 돌아왔다. 그가 떠난 뒤로 그 사이에, 겉보기에, 그의 결연한 면이, 거기에 지워진 중압 아래 무너져 버린 터였다. 그는 말을 달려 우리를 떠났으나, 걸음걸이로 우리에게 돌아왔다. 떠날 때 그는 쇠로 만들어져 있었다. 돌아올 때 그는 솜으로 채워져, 더없이 흐늘흐늘했다.\n\n\"그래.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하신다. \"경찰이 오고 있느냐?\"\n\n\"예.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"삯마차로 저를 뒤따라오겠다고 했습니다. 이곳 관할 경찰의 시그레이브 경정과 그 부하 둘이요. 그저 형식일 뿐입니다! 이 사건은 가망이 없어요.\"\n\n\"아니! 인도인들이 달아났답니까, 도련님?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"그 가엾은, 억울한 누명을 쓴 인도인들은 더없이 부당하게 감옥에 갇혔다네.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"그들은 아직 태어나지 않은 갓난아이만큼이나 결백해. 그들 중 하나가 집 안에 숨어 있었다던 내 생각은, 내 다른 모든 생각들과 마찬가지로, 연기처럼 스러졌지. 그건 그야말로 불가능한 일임이 밝혀졌어.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제 무능함을 자못 흡족해하며 곱씹으며 말한다.\n\n월장석 사건에서 이 전혀 새로운 국면을 알려 우리를 놀라게 하고서, 우리 젊은 신사는, 이모의 청에 따라, 자리에 앉아 사정을 설명했다.\n\n겉보기에 그의 결연한 면은 프리징홀까지는 버텼던 모양이다. 그는 치안판사 앞에 그 사건 전부를 낱낱이 펼쳐 놓았고, 치안판사는 대뜸 경찰을 불렀다. 인도인들에 관해 처음 벌인 조사에서, 그들이 읍내를 떠나려는 시도조차 하지 않았음이 드러났다. 경찰에 던진 더 자세한 물음들로, 셋 다 지난밤 열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 제 소년을 데리고 프리징홀로 돌아가는 것이 목격되었음이 밝혀졌으니—이는 (시각과 거리를 헤아려 보면) 그들이 우리 테라스에서 재주를 부린 뒤 곧장 걸어 돌아갔음도 밝혀 주는 것이었다. 그보다 더 늦게, 자정에, 경찰이 그들이 사는 공동 숙박소를 뒤질 일이 있어 갔을 때, 셋을 다시, 그리고 그들과 함께 있는 어린 소년을, 여느 때처럼 다 보았다. 자정 조금 뒤에 나 자신이 집을 안전히 잠갔다. 인도인들에게 유리한 이보다 더 명백한 증거는 좀처럼 있을 수 없었다. 치안판사는 여태껏 그들에게 의심을 둘 건덕지조차 없다고 말했다. 그러나, 경찰이 이 일을 조사하러 오면 그 요술쟁이들에게 걸리는 무언가가 드러날 수도 있는 만큼, 그는 그들을 부랑아이자 뜨내기로 구금하는 식으로 하여, 일주일 동안 우리 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 자물쇠를 채워 붙들어 두도록 손을 쓰겠다고 했다. 그들은 읍내에서 뭔가 (뭔지는 잊었다) 멋모르고 저지른 것이 있어, 간신히 법의 적용 범위에 들었다. 인간의 온갖 제도는 (정의도 포함해서) 올바른 쪽으로 잡아당기기만 하면 얼마간 늘어나는 법이다. 그 훌륭한 치안판사는 마님의 오랜 벗이었고, 그리하여 인도인들은 그날 아침 법정이 열리자마자 일주일간 \"구금\"되었다.\n\n프리징홀에서 있었던 일에 대한 프랭클린 도련님의 이야기는 그러했다. 잃어버린 보석의 수수께끼로 가는 인도인이라는 실마리는, 이제 겉보기에, 우리 손안에서 끊어져 버린 실마리였다. 만일 그 요술쟁이들이 결백하다면, 대체 세상에 누가 레이철 아가씨의 서랍에서 그 월장석을 가져갔단 말인가?\n\n십 분 뒤, 우리로서는 이루 말 못 하게 안도스럽게도, 시그레이브 경정이 집에 도착했다. 그는 테라스에서 (짐작건대 이탈리아식 면이 가장 위로 올라온 채) 볕을 쬐며 앉아 있던 프랭클린 도련님을 지나쳐 왔는데, 그가 지나가는 경찰에게, 조사가 시작되기도 전에 그 조사는 가망이 없다고 경고하더라고 보고했다.\n\n우리 같은 처지의 집안에게, 프리징홀 경찰 경정은 여러분이 보고 싶어 할 만한 가장 든든한 관리였다. 시그레이브 씨는 키가 크고 풍채가 좋았으며, 태도가 군인다웠다. 그에게는 우렁차게 호령하는 목소리와, 여간 결연한 눈과, 가죽 목깃까지 곱게 단추를 잠근 훌륭한 프록코트가 있었다. \"당신들이 찾던 사람이 바로 나요!\"라는 말이 그의 얼굴 곳곳에 쓰여 있었다. 그리고 그는 그 아래 두 순경을 어찌나 준엄하게 부리던지, 우리 모두는 그와는 함부로 장난칠 수 없음을 뼈저리게 느꼈다.\n\n그는 먼저 경내를, 밖과 안을, 한 바퀴 도는 것으로 시작했다. 그 조사의 결과로 그에게 드러난 바, 바깥에서 도둑이 우리에게 침입한 것이 아니며, 따라서 그 절도는 집 안의 누군가가 저지른 것이 틀림없다는 것이었다. 이 관(官)의 선언이 처음 그들 귀에 닿았을 때 하인들이 어떤 꼴이었는지는 여러분 상상에 맡기겠다. 경정은 먼저 그 부두아르를 살펴보고, 그것이 끝나면 다음으로 하인들을 심문하기로 정했다. 그와 동시에 그는 하인들 침실로 이어지는 계단에 제 부하 하나를 배치하여, 다음 지시가 있을 때까지 집안 누구도 저를 지나쳐 가지 못하게 하라고 일렀다.\n\n이 뒤엣 조처에, 인간이라는 무리의 그 여린 절반이 그 자리에서 이성을 잃었다. 그들은 저희 구석에서 튀어나와, 떼를 지어 위층 레이철 아가씨의 방으로 휙 몰려가서는 (이번엔 로재나 스피어먼도 그들 틈에 휩쓸려 갔다), 시그레이브 경정에게 들이닥쳐, 죄다 똑같이 죄지은 얼굴로, 그가 누구를 의심하는지 당장 말하라고 다그쳤다.\n\n경정 나리는 그 자리에 걸맞은 사람임을 입증했다. 그는 제 결연한 눈으로 그들을 바라보고, 제 군인다운 목소리로 그들을 주눅 들게 했다.\n\n\"자, 그럼, 여자들, 다들 다시 아래층으로 내려가시오. 하나도 빠짐없이. 여기 있는 걸 두고 볼 수 없소. 보시오!\" 하고 경정 나리가, 별안간 레이철 아가씨 문의 바깥쪽 가장자리, 자물쇠 바로 밑에 묻은 장식 그림의 조그만 얼룩을 가리키며 말한다. \"당신들 중 누군가의 페티코트가 벌써 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 보시오. 나가시오! 나가!\" 그에게서 가장 가깝고, 문에 묻은 그 조그만 얼룩에서도 가장 가깝던 로재나 스피어먼이, 복종의 본을 보이며, 대뜸 제 일터로 슬쩍 빠져나갔다. 나머지가 그 뒤를 따라 나갔다. 경정은 그 방 조사를 마치고, 아무 소득도 얻지 못하자, 누가 그 절도를 맨 처음 발견했느냐고 내게 물었다. 내 딸이 맨 먼저 발견했다. 내 딸이 불려 왔다.\n\n경정 나리는 처음부터 페넬로피에게 좀 지나치게 날카롭게 굴었다. \"자, 젊은 처자, 내 말 잘 듣고, 진실을 말하도록 하시오.\" 페넬로피가 대뜸 발끈했다. \"저는 거짓말하라고 배운 적 없어요, 순경 나리!—그리고 아버지가 저기 서서 제가 거짓과 도둑질로 몰리는 걸 듣고도, 제 침실이 저한테 잠기고, 가난한 계집한테 남은 전부인 제 인격이 짓밟히는 걸 두고 보신다면, 제가 여긴 그 좋은 아버지가 아니시죠!\" 내가 때맞춰 한마디 하여 정의와 페넬로피를 좀 더 유쾌한 사이로 만들어 놓았다. 물음과 대답이 술술 나아가, 언급할 만한 것 없이 끝났다. 내 딸은 레이철 아가씨가 밤에 맨 마지막으로 그 다이아몬드를 캐비닛 서랍에 넣는 것을 보았다. 그 애는 이튿날 아침 여덟 시에 레이철 아가씨의 찻잔을 들고 들어갔다가, 서랍이 열려 텅 빈 것을 발견했다. 그러자 그 애가 집안에 알린 것이었다—그것으로 페넬로피의 증언은 끝이었다.\n\n경정 나리는 다음으로 레이철 아가씨를 직접 보게 해 달라 청했다. 페넬로피가 문 너머로 그 청을 전했다. 대답이 같은 길로 우리에게 왔다. \"그 순경한테 할 말은 아무것도 없어요—누구도 볼 수 없어요.\" 우리의 노련한 관리는 그 대답을 듣고 놀라기도 하고 언짢기도 한 낯빛이 되었다. 나는 그에게 우리 젊은 아가씨가 편찮으시다고 말하고, 조금 기다렸다가 이따 뵙기를 청했다. 그리하여 우리는 다시 아래층으로 내려갔고, 현관을 가로지르던 고드프리 씨와 프랭클린 도련님을 만났다.\n\n두 신사는 집에 묵는 사람인지라, 이 일에 무슨 실마리라도 던져 줄 수 있는지 말하라고 불려 왔다. 둘 다 그것에 관해 아무것도 몰랐다. 지난밤 무슨 수상한 소리라도 들었는가? 그들은 빗소리 말고는 아무것도 듣지 못했다. 그 둘 어느 쪽보다 더 오래 깨어 누워 있던 나도, 아무것도 못 들었는가? 아무것도! 심문에서 풀려나며, 여전히 우리 곤경을 속수무책으로 보는 프랭클린 도련님이 내게 속삭였다. \"저 사람은 우리한테 눈곱만큼도 쓸모가 없겠어. 시그레이브 경정은 얼간이야.\" 제 차례로 풀려나며, 고드프리 씨가 내게 속삭였다—\"보아하니 여간 유능한 사람이야. 베터리지, 나는 그를 크게 믿네!\" 옛사람 하나가 내 시절보다 앞서 말했듯이, 사람이 여럿이면 의견도 여럿이다.\n\n경정 나리의 다음 조처는 그를 다시 그 \"부두아르\"로 데려갔고, 내 딸과 나도 그 뒤를 바싹 따랐다. 그의 목적은, 밤사이에 가구 가운데 무엇이 여느 제자리에서 옮겨진 것은 없는지 알아내는 것이었다—겉보기에, 그 방에서의 앞선 조사가 이 점에 대해 그의 마음을 흡족하게 할 만큼 충분히 나아가지 못했던 모양이다.\n\n우리가 아직 의자며 탁자 사이를 쑤시고 다니는데, 별안간 침실 문이 열렸다. 모두에게 저를 안 보이겠다고 하던 레이철 아가씨가, 우리를 놀라게도, 제 발로 우리 한가운데로 걸어 들어왔다. 아가씨는 의자에서 제 정원용 모자를 집어 들고, 그러고는 곧장 페넬로피에게 가서 이렇게 물었다.—\n\n\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 오늘 아침 너를 내게 전갈을 들려 보냈지?\"\n\n\"예, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"나와 이야기하고 싶어 하셨어, 그렇지?\"\n\n\"예, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"그분 지금 어디 계시니?\"\n\n아래 테라스에서 목소리가 들리기에, 나는 창밖을 내다보았고, 두 신사가 함께 오르내리며 거니는 것을 보았다. 내 딸을 대신하여, 나는 말했다. \"프랭클린 도련님은 테라스에 계십니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n더는 한마디도 없이, 저에게 말을 건네려는 경정 나리에는 아랑곳없이, 죽은 사람처럼 창백하게, 그리고 이상하게 제 생각에 파묻힌 채, 아가씨는 방을 나가, 테라스의 사촌들에게로 내려갔다.\n\n그것은 마땅한 예의를 저버린 짓이요, 예법을 어긴 짓이었으나, 내 쪽에서 말이오, 나는 죽어도, 레이철 아가씨가 밖에서 신사들을 만날 때 창밖을 내다보지 않을 수가 없었다. 아가씨는 고드프리 씨는 알아채지도 못한 채 프랭클린 도련님께 다가갔고, 그러자 고드프리 씨는 물러나 두 사람을 저희끼리 있게 했다. 아가씨가 프랭클린 도련님께 하는 말은 격하게 하는 듯했다. 그것은 잠깐밖에 이어지지 않았고, 창에서 내가 본 그의 얼굴로 미루어 보건대, 그를 이루 표현할 길 없이 놀라게 하는 듯했다. 그들이 아직 함께 있는데, 마님이 테라스에 나타나셨다. 레이철 아가씨가 마님을 보고—프랭클린 도련님께 마지막 몇 마디를 하고는—제 어머니가 다다르기 전에, 별안간 다시 집 안으로 돌아갔다. 당신도 놀라시고, 프랭클린 도련님의 놀람을 알아채신 마님이, 그에게 말을 거셨다. 고드프리 씨가 그들에게 합류하여, 또 말을 했다. 프랭클린 도련님이 두 사람 사이에서 조금 걸어가며, 짐작건대 무슨 일이 있었는지 그들에게 이야기하는 듯했으니, 그들 둘 다 몇 걸음 옮기다가, 놀라움에 얻어맞은 사람들처럼 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 내가 딱 이만큼 보았을 때, 거실 문이 거칠게 열렸다. 레이철 아가씨가 침실로 잰걸음으로 걸어갔으니, 사납고 성난 채, 눈에는 불꽃이 일고 뺨은 타올랐다. 경정 나리가 다시 한번 아가씨에게 물으려 했다. 아가씨는 제 침실 문에서 그에게 홱 돌아섰다. \"나는 당신을 부른 적 없어요!\" 아가씨가 격하게 외쳤다. \"나는 당신이 필요 없어요. 내 다이아몬드는 사라졌어요. 당신도 그 누구도 그걸 영영 못 찾을 거예요!\" 그 말과 함께 아가씨는 안으로 들어가, 우리 코앞에서 문을 잠갔다. 그 문에서 가장 가까이 섰던 페넬로피가, 아가씨가 다시 혼자가 되자마자 울음을 터뜨리는 소리를 들었다.\n\n한순간엔 노발대발, 그다음 순간엔 눈물바다! 그게 무슨 뜻이었을까?\n\n나는 경정에게, 그것은 레이철 아가씨의 성미가 제 보석을 잃어 뒤틀린 것을 뜻한다고 말했다. 집안의 명예가 걱정스러운지라, 우리 젊은 아가씨가—경찰관 앞에서까지—제 몸가짐을 잊는 것을 보매 나는 괴로웠고, 그래서 나는 그에 맞춰 되도록 그럴싸한 변명을 지어냈다. 내 속마음으로는, 레이철 아가씨의 그 유별난 말과 행동에, 말로 다 할 수 없이 어리둥절했다. 아가씨가 제 침실 문에서 한 말을 짐작의 실마리로 삼으면, 나는 다만, 아가씨가 우리가 경찰을 부른 것에 죽도록 언짢아하며, 테라스에서의 프랭클린 도련님의 놀람이, 아가씨가 그에게 (경찰을 데려오는 데 주로 앞장선 사람으로서) 그런 뜻을 내비쳤기 때문에 일어난 것이라고 결론지을 수밖에 없었다. 만일 이 짐작이 옳다면, 어째서—제 다이아몬드를 잃고서—그것을 저를 위해 되찾아 주는 게 소임인 바로 그 사람들이 집에 있는 것을 아가씨는 마다한단 말인가? 그리고 대체 어떻게 아가씨가, 그 월장석을 영영 다시 못 찾으리라는 것을 알 수 있었단 말인가?\n\n지금 형편으로는, 그 물음들에 대한 대답을 집안 누구에게서도 바랄 수 없었다. 프랭클린 도련님은, 레이철 아가씨가 테라스에서 그에게 한 말을 하인에게—나만큼이나 오래된 하인에게까지도—되풀이하지 않는 것을 명예로운 일로 여기는 듯했다. 신사이자 친척으로서, 아마 프랭클린 도련님의 속내에 끼워졌을 고드프리 씨는, 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로 그 속내를 지켰다. 마님 역시 틀림없이 그 비밀을 아셨고, 홀로 레이철 아가씨께 드나들 수 있었으나, 그 애를 도무지 어떻게 할 수 없다고 솔직히 인정하셨다. \"다이아몬드 이야기를 하면 네가 나를 미치게 하는구나!\" 어머니의 온갖 힘도 아가씨에게서 그 이상의 말은 한마디도 끌어내지 못했다.\n\n그리하여 우리는, 레이철 아가씨에 관해서도 꽉 막혔고—월장석에 관해서도 꽉 막혔다. 앞엣것에서는, 마님이 우리를 도우실 힘이 없으셨다. 뒤엣것에서는 (여러분도 곧 판단하시겠지만), 시그레이브 씨가 재간이 다한 경정의 처지에 빠르게 다가가고 있었다.\n\n그 \"부두아르\" 곳곳을 샅샅이 뒤지고도 가구 가운데서 아무것도 발견하지 못한 우리의 노련한 관리는, 하인들이 대체로 그 다이아몬드를 밤에 넣어 둔 자리를 알고 있었는지 아닌지 내게 물어 왔다.\n\n\"저는 그게 어디에 놓였는지 알았습니다요, 선생.\" 나는 말했다. \"우선 저부터가요. 하인 새뮤얼도 알았지요—그날 밤 다이아몬드를 어디 둘지 이야기할 때 그가 현관에 있었으니까요. 제 딸도, 이미 선생께 말씀드린 대로, 알았고요. 그 애나 새뮤얼이 다른 하인들한테 그 일을 말했을 수도 있고—아니면 다른 하인들이, 뒤쪽 계단으로 열려 있었을지도 모를 현관의 옆문을 통해, 그 이야기를 저희끼리 들었을 수도 있습니다. 제가 아는 한, 어젯밤 그 보석이 어디 있는지 이 집 사람 누구나 알고 있었을 수 있어요.\"\n\n내 대답이 경정 나리의 의심이 뻗칠 자못 넓은 벌판을 내놓은지라, 그는 다음으로 하인들의 인품에 대해 물으며 그것을 좁혀 보려 했다.\n\n나는 대뜸 로재나 스피어먼이 떠올랐다. 그러나 내가 그녀를 알아 온 내내 그 정직함이 온갖 의심을 넘어서 있던 가엾은 처녀에게 의심을 돌리는 것은, 내 자리도 내 뜻도 아니었다. 갱생원의 원장은 그녀를 진심으로 뉘우치고 더없이 믿음직한 처녀로 마님께 보고한 바 있었다. 그녀를 먼저 의심할 까닭을 찾아내는 것은 경정의 소임이었다—그러고 나서, 그러기 전에는 아니고, 그녀가 어떻게 마님의 시중을 들게 되었는지 그에게 말하는 것이 내 도리일 터였다. \"우리 사람들은 죄다 인품이 훌륭합니다요.\" 나는 말했다. \"그리고 죄다, 여주인이 저희에게 둔 그 믿음을 받을 만합니다.\" 그 뒤로, 시그레이브 씨가 할 수 있는 일은 딱 하나 남아 있었다—곧, 팔을 걷어붙이고 몸소 하인들의 인품과 씨름하는 것이었다.\n\n하나씩 하나씩, 그들이 심문받았다. 하나씩 하나씩, 그들은 할 말이 아무것도 없음이 드러났다—그리고 (여자들로 말하면) 아주 길게, 그리고 제 침실에 놓인 그 통행 금지에 대한 여간한 노기를 담아, 그것을 말했다. 나머지가 아래층 제 자리로 돌려보내진 뒤, 다음으로 페넬로피가 불려 나와, 따로 두 번째로 심문받았다.\n\n그 \"부두아르\"에서 내 딸이 조금 발끈했던 것과, 저를 의심받는다고 냉큼 여기던 것이, 시그레이브 경정에게 좋지 않은 인상을 남긴 모양이었다. 또한, 그 애가 밤에 그 다이아몬드를 본 마지막 사람이라는 것도 그의 마음에 조금 걸리는 듯했다. 두 번째 심문이 끝나자, 내 딸이 광분하여 내게 돌아왔다. 이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없었다—그 경찰관이 대놓고 그 애더러 도둑이라고 말한 것이나 진배없었던 것이다! 나는 (프랭클린 도련님의 견해를 좇아) 그가 그토록까지 얼간이라고는 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다. 그러나 그가 아무 말도 하지 않았어도, 그가 내 딸을 바라보던 그 눈은 그리 유쾌히 볼 만한 눈이 아니었다. 나는 가엾은 페넬로피와 함께 그것을, 진지하게 다루기엔 너무 우스꽝스러운 것으로—실제로 그러했다—웃어넘겼다. 남몰래, 나도 어리석게도 화가 났던 것 같아 걱정이다. 그것은 좀 사람을 시험하는 일이었다—정말이지 그러했다. 내 딸은 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓴 채 구석에 앉아, 완전히 상심하여 있었다. 그 애가 어리석다고 여러분은 말씀하시리라. 그가 대놓고 저를 고발할 때까지 기다렸어도 됐을 것이라고. 그야, 나도 그만큼 침착한 사람인지라, 그건 인정한다. 그래도 경정 나리는 떠올렸어야 했다—그가 무엇을 떠올렸어야 했는지는 아무래도 좋다. 그를 악마가 잡아가기를!\n\n조사의 다음이자 마지막 단계가, 이른바, 일을 위기로 몰고 갔다. 그 관리가 (내가 배석한 자리에서) 마님과 면담을 했다. 그 다이아몬드가 집 안의 누군가에게 틀림없이 훔쳐졌음을 마님께 알린 뒤, 그는 저와 제 부하들이 하인들의 방과 상자를 그 자리에서 뒤질 수 있게 허락해 달라 청했다. 나의 어지신 여주인은, 본디 그 관대하고 지체 높은 여인답게, 우리가 도둑처럼 취급받게 놔두기를 거절하셨다. \"나는 결코 그런 갚음을 하는 데 동의하지 않겠어요.\" 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"내 집에서 일하는 그 충실한 하인들에게 내가 진 모든 것에 대해서 말이에요.\"\n\n경정 나리는 절을 하며, 내 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌는데, 그것은 뻔히 이렇게 말하고 있었다. \"이런 식으로 내 손을 묶으실 거면, 나를 왜 부르셨소?\" 하인들의 우두머리로서, 나는 대뜸, 모든 당사자를 위한 정의를 생각해, 우리가 여주인의 관대함을 이용해서는 안 된다고 느꼈다. \"마님의 은혜에 감사드립니다요.\" 나는 말했다. \"하지만 저희 열쇠를 내드려, 이 일에서 옳은 바를 행하도록 허락해 주십사 청합니다. 게이브리얼 베터리지가 본을 보이면,\" 하고 나는, 문간에서 시그레이브 경정을 붙들어 세우며 말한다. \"나머지 하인들도 따를 겁니다요, 제가 약속하지요. 우선, 여기 제 열쇠올시다!\" 마님이 내 손을 잡고, 눈에 눈물을 담아 내게 고마워하셨다. 아이고! 그 순간, 시그레이브 경정을 때려눕히는 특권을 위해서라면 내가 무엇인들 내놓지 않았으랴!\n\n내가 그들을 대신해 약속한 대로, 다른 하인들도 내 본을 따랐으니, 물론 몹시 마지못해서였으나, 죄다 내가 취한 견해를 취했다. 경찰관들이 저희 물건을 뒤지는 동안, 그 여자들은 볼만한 구경거리였다. 요리사는 마치 경정 나리를 화덕에 산 채로 구울 수 있을 듯한 얼굴이었고, 다른 여자들은 마치 그가 다 구워지면 그를 먹어 치울 수 있을 듯한 얼굴이었다.\n\n수색이 끝나고, 물론, 어디서도 다이아몬드나 다이아몬드의 흔적이 나오지 않자, 시그레이브 경정은 다음엔 무엇을 할지 홀로 궁리하려고 내 조그만 방으로 물러났다. 그와 그 부하들은 이제 몇 시간이나 집에 있었으면서도, 그 월장석이 어떻게 훔쳐졌는지, 아니면 우리가 누구를 도둑으로 의심해야 하는지를 밝히는 데는 우리를 한 치도 나아가게 하지 못했다.\n\n그 경찰관이 아직 홀로 골똘히 궁리하는 동안, 나는 서재에서 프랭클린 도련님을 뵈러 오라는 부름을 받았다. 이루 말 못 하게 놀랍게도, 내 손이 막 문에 닿았을 때, 그것이 별안간 안에서 열리더니, 로재나 스피어먼이 걸어 나왔다!\n\n아침에 서재를 쓸고 닦은 뒤로는, 첫째 하녀든 둘째 하녀든 그날 그 뒤 시각에 그 방에 볼일이 있을 리 없었다. 나는 로재나 스피어먼을 붙들어 세우고, 그 자리에서 집안 규율을 어겼다고 나무랐다.\n\n\"이 시각에 서재에서 대체 무슨 볼일이냐?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 위층에서 반지 하나를 떨어뜨리셔서요.\" 하고 로재나가 말한다. \"그걸 갖다드리려고 서재에 들어갔던 거예요.\" 그렇게 대답하는 그 처녀의 얼굴은 온통 붉게 달아올라 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 고개를 홱 젖히며, 내가 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 잘난 체하는 낯빛으로 걸어가 버렸다. 집안에서 벌어진 일들이 여자 하인들을 죄다 얼마쯤은 뒤흔들어 놓은 것이 틀림없었으나, 겉보기에 로재나가 지금 제 타고난 성품에서 벗어난 것만큼 완전히 제 타고난 성품에서 벗어난 이는 아무도 없었다.\n\n나는 프랭클린 도련님이 서재 탁자에서 글을 쓰고 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 내가 방에 들어서기가 무섭게 기차역까지 갈 탈것을 청했다. 그 목소리의 첫 울림이 내게 일러 주기로, 이제 우리에게는 그의 결연한 면이 다시 한번 가장 위로 올라와 있었다. 솜으로 만든 사내는 사라졌고, 쇠로 만든 사내가 다시 내 앞에 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"런던으로 가십니까, 도련님?\" 나는 물었다.\n\n\"런던에 전보를 치러 가는 걸세.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"나는 이모님께, 우리한테는 시그레이브 경정보다 더 영리한 머리가 도와주어야 한다고 납득시켰네. 그리고 내 아버지께 전보를 보낼 허락을 받았지. 아버지는 경찰 총감을 알고, 그 총감은 이 다이아몬드의 수수께끼를 풀 적임자를 짚어 낼 수 있어. 그런데, 수수께끼 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데,\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 목소리를 낮추며 말한다. \"자네가 마구간에 가기 전에 한마디 더 할 게 있네. 아직은 아무한테도 한마디도 흘리지 말게. 로재나 스피어먼의 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 게 아니라면, 아무래도 그녀가 그 월장석에 대해 마땅히 알아야 할 것보다 더 많이 아는 것 같아서 걱정이야.\"\n\n그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 들으매 내가 놀랐는지 괴로웠는지는 나도 좀처럼 가리기 어렵다. 내가 더 젊었더라면, 나도 그만큼을 프랭클린 도련님께 털어놓았을지도 모른다. 그러나 늙으면 훌륭한 버릇을 하나 익히게 된다. 갈 길이 뚜렷이 보이지 않는 경우엔, 혀를 놀리지 않는 것이다.\n\n\"그녀가 내가 침실에서 떨어뜨린 반지를 들고 여기 들어왔어.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 이어 갔다. \"내가 고맙다고 했으니, 물론 나는 그녀가 나가려니 했지. 그런데 그러기는커녕, 그녀가 탁자를 사이에 두고 내 맞은편에 서서, 더없이 이상한 태로 나를 바라보는 게야—반쯤 겁먹고, 반쯤 허물없이—나는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었어. '이 다이아몬드는 참 이상한 일이에요, 도련님.' 그녀가 묘하게 별안간, 무모한 투로 말하더군. 나는 '그래, 이상하지.' 하고는, 다음에 무슨 말이 나올까 싶었어. 내 명예를 걸고, 베터리지, 나는 그녀의 머리가 어떻게 된 게 틀림없다고 생각해! 그녀가 이러는 게야. '그들은 그 다이아몬드를 영영 못 찾을 거예요, 도련님, 그렇죠? 그럼요! 그걸 가져간 사람도 못 찾을 거고요—제가 장담해요.' 그녀가 실제로 내게 고개를 끄덕이며 웃어 보이더군! 내가 무슨 뜻이냐고 미처 묻기도 전에, 우리는 바깥에서 자네 발소리를 들었어. 짐작건대 그녀는 자네가 저를 여기서 붙잡을까 봐 겁이 났던 게지. 아무튼, 그녀는 낯빛이 변하더니, 방을 나갔어. 대체 그게 무슨 뜻일까?\"\n\n나는, 그때조차도, 차마 그에게 그 처녀의 사연을 말할 수가 없었다. 그것은 그에게 그녀가 도둑이라고 말하는 것이나 거의 진배없었을 것이다. 게다가, 설령 내가 그것을 죄다 털어놓았다 한들, 또 설령 그녀가 도둑이라 한들, 하필 세상 모든 사람 가운데 프랭클린 도련님에게 그녀가 제 비밀을 흘려야 할 까닭은 여전히 오리무중이었을 것이다.\n\n\"그 가엾은 처녀가 그저 들뜬 데가 있고 아주 이상하게 말한다는 이유만으로 그녀를 곤경에 빠뜨린다는 건 견딜 수가 없어.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 이어 갔다. \"그렇지만 만일 그녀가 나한테 한 말을 그 경정한테 했다면, 그가 아무리 얼간이라도, 아무래도——\" 그는 거기서 멈추고, 나머지는 말하지 않은 채로 두었다.\n\n\"가장 좋은 방법은요, 도련님,\" 나는 말했다. \"첫 기회가 오는 대로 제가 그것에 대해 여주인께 남몰래 두어 마디 아뢰는 겁니다. 마님은 로재나에게 아주 다정한 관심을 두고 계시니까요. 그리고 그 처녀는 결국 그저 주제넘고 어리석었던 것뿐인지도 모르고요. 집에 무슨 소동이 나면요, 도련님, 여자 하인들은 어두운 쪽을 보고 싶어 합니다—그게 그 가엾은 것들에게 제 눈에 일종의 무게를 실어 주거든요. 누가 병들면, 여자들이 그 사람이 죽으리라 예언하는 데 걸어 보세요. 보석을 잃으면, 그것을 영영 다시 못 찾으리라 예언하는 데 걸어 보시고요.\"\n\n이 견해가 (거듭 생각하니 나 스스로도 있음 직한 견해라 여겼음을 밝혀 둔다) 프랭클린 도련님을 대단히 안심시키는 듯했다. 그는 제 전보를 접어 넣고, 그 이야기를 접었다. 조랑말 이륜마차를 대령시키러 마구간으로 가는 길에, 나는 하인들 식당을 들여다보았는데, 그들이 저녁을 먹고 있었다. 로재나 스피어먼은 그들 틈에 없었다. 물어보니, 그녀가 별안간 앓아누워, 눕겠다고 제 방으로 위층에 올라갔다는 것이었다.\n\n\"이상하네! 내가 마지막으로 봤을 땐 멀쩡해 보였는데.\" 나는 한마디 했다.\n\n페넬로피가 나를 따라 나왔다. \"다른 사람들 앞에서 그런 식으로 말씀하지 마세요, 아버지.\" 그 애가 말했다. \"아버지는 그저 그들이 로재나한테 더 모질게 굴게 만들 뿐이에요. 그 가엾은 것은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨 때문에 애간장이 다 타고 있다고요.\"\n\n여기 그 처녀의 행동에 대한 또 다른 견해가 있었다. 만일 페넬로피가 옳을 수 있다면, 로재나의 그 이상한 말과 행동에 대한 설명은 죄다 이런 것이었을지도 모른다—프랭클린 도련님을 놀래어 저한테 말을 걸게 할 수만 있다면, 그녀는 제가 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 않았다는 것 말이다. 그것이 그 수수께끼의 옳은 풀이라 치면, 그것은 아마, 그녀가 현관에서 나를 지나칠 때 그 들뜨고 잘난 체하던 태를 설명해 주었다. 그가 겨우 세 마디밖에 하지 않았어도, 어쨌든 그녀는 제 목적을 이룬 것이었으니, 프랭클린 도련님이 그녀에게 말을 걸었던 것이다.\n\n나는 조랑말에 마구가 채워지는 것을 손수 지켜보았다. 이제 우리를 에워싼 그 지옥 같은 수수께끼와 종잡을 수 없음의 그물 속에서, 정말이지, 그 죔쇠와 가죽끈이 서로를 그토록 잘 알아듣는 것을 보는 것은 하나의 안도였다! 조랑말이 이륜마차의 채 안으로 뒷걸음쳐 들어가는 것을 보고 나면, 여러분은 의심할 여지없는 무언가를 본 것이었다. 그리고 그것은, 내 말하건대, 우리 집안에서 더없이 진귀한 종류의 대접이 되어 가고 있었다.\n\n이륜마차를 몰고 현관으로 돌아가 보니, 프랭클린 도련님뿐 아니라 고드프리 씨와 시그레이브 경정까지도 층계에서 나를 기다리고 있었다.\n\n경정 나리의 궁리는 (하인들의 방과 상자에서 그 다이아몬드를 찾는 데 실패한 뒤) 그를 전혀 새로운 결론으로 이끈 모양이었다. 여전히 제 첫 명제, 곧 집 안의 누군가가 그 보석을 훔쳤다는 것에 매달린 채, 우리의 노련한 관리는, 이제 그 도둑이 (그가 페넬로피를 속으로 어찌 여기든, 그 가엾은 페넬로피의 이름을 대지 않을 만큼은 지혜로웠다!) 인도인들과 짜고 움직였다는 견해였다. 그리하여 그는 제 조사를 프리징홀 감옥의 요술쟁이들에게로 옮기자고 제안했다. 이 새 움직임을 듣고, 프랭클린 도련님이, 그 경정을 읍내로 다시 데려다주겠다고 자원했으니, 거기서라면 우리 역에서만큼이나 손쉽게 런던에 전보를 칠 수 있었다. 여전히 시그레이브 씨를 독실히 믿고, 인도인들의 심문을 참관하는 데 크게 흥미를 느낀 고드프리 씨는, 그 관리를 따라 프리징홀에 가게 해 달라 청했다. 두 하급 순경 중 하나는, 무슨 일이 생길 경우에 대비해 집에 남기로 했다. 다른 하나는 경정과 함께 읍내로 돌아가기로 했다. 그리하여 조랑말 이륜마차의 네 자리가 딱 채워졌다.\n\n고삐를 잡고 몰아 떠나기 전에, 프랭클린 도련님이 나를 다른 이들에게 안 들리는 데로 몇 걸음 데리고 갔다.\n\n\"나는 런던에 전보 치는 것을, 우리의 인도인 심문이 어찌 되는지 볼 때까지 미루겠네.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 나름의 확신으로는, 이 얼빠진 시골 경찰관은 여전히 오리무중이고, 그저 시간을 벌려는 것뿐이야. 하인 중 누군가가 인도인들과 한패라는 발상은, 내 생각에, 얼토당토않은 헛소리일세. 내가 돌아올 때까지 집 언저리에 있게, 베터리지. 그리고 로재나 스피어먼에게서 무엇을 알아낼 수 있는지 해 보게. 나는 자네더러 자네 자존심을 깎는 짓이나, 그 처녀에게 모진 짓을 하라고 청하는 게 아니야. 다만 여느 때보다 더 조심스레 자네 관찰력을 부려 보라고 청하는 걸세. 이모님 앞에서는 되도록 대수롭잖게 넘기겠지만—이건 자네가 짐작하는 것보다 더 중요한 문제야.\"\n\n\"그건 이만 파운드짜리 문제입지요, 도련님.\" 나는 그 다이아몬드의 값어치를 생각하며 말했다.\n\n\"그건 레이철의 마음을 가라앉히는 문제일세.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 근엄하게 대답했다. \"나는 그 애가 몹시 걱정스러워.\"\n\n그는 별안간 나를 떠났다. 마치 우리 사이의 더 이상의 이야기를 잘라 버리고 싶어 하는 듯이. 나는 그 까닭을 알 것 같았다. 더 이야기하다 보면, 레이철 아가씨가 테라스에서 그에게 한 말의 비밀을 내가 알게 될 수도 있었으니까.\n\n그리하여 그들은 프리징홀로 마차를 몰아 떠났다. 나는 그 처녀 자신을 위해서라도, 로재나와 남몰래 조금 이야기를 나눌 마음이 넉넉했다. 그러나 그럴 만한 기회가 좀처럼 나지 않았다. 그녀는 찻시간에야 다시 아래층으로 내려왔다. 막상 나타났을 때, 그녀는 들떠 흥분해 있었고, 이른바 히스테리 발작을 일으켜, 마님의 분부로 각성제를 한 모금 먹고, 다시 잠자리로 보내졌다.\n\n그날은, 내 말하건대, 여간 음울하고 비참하게 끝을 향해 저물어 갔다. 레이철 아가씨는 여전히 제 방을 지키며, 몸이 너무 아파 그날은 저녁 식사에 내려올 수 없다고 잘라 말했다. 마님은 당신 따님을 두고 어찌나 심기가 가라앉으셨던지, 나는 차마, 로재나 스피어먼이 프랭클린 도련님께 한 말을 알려 마님을 한층 더 근심스럽게 해 드릴 수가 없었다. 페넬로피는, 그녀가 이제 곧 절도죄로 재판받고, 선고받고, 유배당하리라 굳게 믿고 있었다. 다른 여자들은 저희 성경과 찬송가책을 집어 들고, 그것을 읽으며 시큼한 풋과일처럼 뚱한 얼굴을 하고 있었다—이는, 내가 내 처지에서 관찰한 바로, 하루 중 익숙지 않은 시각에 경건한 짓을 행하면 대개 뒤따르는 결과다. 나로 말하면, 나는 「로빈슨 크루소」를 펼칠 마음조차 없었다. 나는 마당으로 나가, 조금 즐거운 벗이 아쉬운지라, 개집 곁에 내 의자를 놓고, 개들에게 말을 걸었다.\n\n저녁 식사 시간 반 시간 전에, 두 신사가 프리징홀에서 돌아왔는데, 시그레이브 경정과, 그가 이튿날 우리에게 돌아오기로 약조를 해 둔 터였다. 그들은 인도 여행가 머스웨이트 씨를, 읍내 가까운 그의 현 거처로 찾아갔었다. 프랭클린 도련님의 청에 따라, 그는 친절히, 셋 중 영어를 전혀 모르는 그 두 인도인을 상대하는 데, 제 언어 지식의 도움을 그들에게 베풀어 주었다. 조심스레, 그리고 아주 길게 진행된 그 심문은 아무 소득 없이 끝났다. 그 요술쟁이들이 우리 하인 누구와 손을 댔다고 의심할 만한 까닭은 그림자조차 발견되지 않았다. 그 결론에 이르자, 프랭클린 도련님은 제 전보를 런던으로 보냈고, 이제 그 일은 내일이 올 때까지 그렇게 걸려 있었다.\n\n생일 다음 날의 이야기는 이쯤 해 두자. 여태껏, 우리에게 한 줄기 빛도 새어 들지 않았다. 그러나 하루 이틀 뒤에, 그 어둠이 조금 걷혔다. 어떻게, 그리고 어떤 결과로였는지는, 여러분도 곧 보시게 될 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XII\n\n목요일 밤이 지나갔으나, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 금요일 아침과 함께 두 가지 소식이 들어왔다.\n\n첫 번째 소식. 빵집 사내가 딱 잘라 말하기를, 자기가 전날 오후에 로재나 스피어먼을 만났다는 것이다. 두툼한 베일을 쓰고, 황야를 가로지르는 오솔길을 따라 프리징홀 쪽으로 걸어가고 있더라는 것이었다. 로재나로 말할 것 같으면, 그 딱한 것의 어깨가 그 사람됨을 제법 뚜렷이 드러내 주는 터라, 누군가 그를 잘못 알아본다는 것은 이상한 노릇으로 보였다—그러나 그 사내가 잘못 본 것임에 틀림없었다. 여러분도 아시다시피, 로재나는 목요일 오후 내내 위층 제 방에서 앓아누워 있었으니 말이다.\n\n두 번째 소식은 우편배달부를 통해 왔다. 그 착한 캔디 씨는, 생일날 밤 빗속으로 마차를 몰고 떠나며 나더러 의사의 살갗은 방수라고 말했을 때, 제 그 숱한 실없는 소리에 한마디를 더 보탠 셈이었다. 그 살갗이 무색하게도, 빗물이 그를 뚫고 스며들었으니 말이다. 그는 그날 밤 오한이 들었고, 이제 열병으로 앓아누웠다. 우편배달부가 가져온 가장 최근의 전갈에 따르면, 그는 정신이 혼미하다는 것이었다—그 딱한 양반이, 헛소리 속에서도, 맑은 정신일 때 곧잘 지껄이던 그 허튼소리를 어찌나 술술 늘어놓던지. 우리는 다들 그 자그마한 의사를 딱하게 여겼다. 그러나 프랭클린 도련님은, 주로 레이철 아가씨 때문에, 그의 병을 안타까워하는 눈치였다. 아침 식사 때 내가 방에 있는 동안 그가 마님께 한 말로 미루어 보건대, 그는 이렇게 생각하는 듯했다—만일 월장석을 둘러싼 이 애타는 마음이 곧 가라앉지 않으면, 레이철 아가씨가 우리가 마련할 수 있는 최선의 의술을 다급히 필요로 하게 될지도 모른다고.\n\n아침 식사가 끝난 지 얼마 되지 않아, 아버지 되는 블레이크 씨가 제 아들에게 답으로 보낸 전보가 도착했다. 그 전보는 우리에게 알리기를, 그가 (제 친구인 경찰국장의 도움으로) 우리를 도와줄 적임자를 붙잡았다는 것이었다. 그 사람의 이름은 커프 부장이며, 그가 런던에서 오전 기차 편으로 도착할 것으로 예상하면 되리라는 것이었다.\n\n그 새 경찰관의 이름을 읽고, 프랭클린 도련님은 흠칫했다. 아무래도 그는 런던에 머무는 동안, 제 아버지의 변호사에게서 커프 부장에 관한 기이한 일화를 몇 가지 들은 모양이었다.\n\n\"우리 근심의 끝이 벌써 보이기 시작하는 것 같아 슬며시 기대가 생기는군.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"내가 들은 이야기의 절반만 사실이라 해도, 수수께끼를 푸는 일에 관한 한, 온 잉글랜드를 통틀어 커프 부장에 견줄 자가 없다는 거야!\"\n\n이 이름난 유능한 인물이 나타날 시각이 가까워질수록 우리는 모두 들뜨고 조바심이 났다. 정해진 시각에 우리에게 돌아온 시그레이브 경정은, 그 부장이 오기로 되어 있다는 말을 듣자, 곧바로 방 하나에 틀어박혀 펜과 잉크와 종이를 갖추고서, 저에게서 분명 요구될 그 보고서의 메모를 작성하기 시작했다. 나로서는 내가 직접 역으로 나가 그 부장을 모셔 오고 싶었다. 그러나 그 이름난 커프를 위해서라 한들, 마님의 마차와 말은 언감생심이었고, 조랑말 이륜마차는 나중에 고드프리 씨께 필요했다. 그분은 이런 애타는 때에 이모님 곁을 떠나야 하는 것을 몹시 안타까워했다. 그래서 그 영리한 런던 경찰관이 이 사건을 어찌 보는지 들어 보려는 뜻에서, 친절하게도 제 출발 시각을 막차 시각까지 늦추어 놓았다. 그러나 금요일 밤에는 시내에 있어야만 했으니, 어려움에 처한 어느 부인 자선회가 토요일 아침에 그와 의논하려고 기다리고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그 부장이 도착할 시각이 되자, 나는 그를 맞으러 대문께로 내려갔다.\n\n내가 문지기 오두막에 이르렀을 때 역에서 온 삯마차 한 대가 다가와 섰다. 그리고 백발이 희끗한 나이 든 사내가 내렸는데, 어찌나 딱하게 여위었던지 온몸 어디에도 뼈에 살점 한 냥 붙어 있지 않은 듯 보였다. 그는 온통 점잖은 검은 옷차림에, 목에는 흰 크라바트를 두르고 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 손도끼처럼 날카로웠고, 그 살갗은 가을 낙엽처럼 누렇고 메마르고 시들어 있었다. 강철빛이 도는 옅은 잿빛 눈은, 여러분의 눈과 마주칠 때, 여러분 자신이 미처 깨닫지 못한 무언가를 여러분에게서 더 바라는 듯 보이는, 몹시 당혹스러운 버릇이 있었다. 그의 걸음은 나긋했고, 목소리는 우울했으며, 길고 여윈 손가락은 갈퀴처럼 구부러져 있었다. 그는 목사일 수도, 장의사일 수도—아니, 여러분 좋으실 대로 그 무엇이라 해도 좋았으나, 오직 그가 정말로 그것인 바로 그것만은 아닌 듯했다. 커프 부장보다 시그레이브 경정과 더 완벽하게 상반된 인물, 그리고 곤경에 처한 집안이 바라보기에 이보다 덜 위안이 되는 관리는, 아무리 이 잡듯 뒤진들 결코 찾아내지 못하리라고 나는 장담한다.\n\n\"여기가 버린더 마님 댁이오?\" 하고 그가 물었다.\n\n\"예, 그렇습니다.\"\n\n\"나는 커프 부장이오.\"\n\n\"이리로 오시지요, 나리.\"\n\n집으로 가는 길에, 나는 마님이 그를 부린 그 용건에 관해 그가 내게 이야기해도 좋다는 것을 그에게 납득시키려고, 내 이름과 이 집안에서의 내 위치를 밝혔다. 그러나 그럼에도 그는 그 용건에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그는 이 저택 부지를 감탄해 마지않았고, 바닷바람이 아주 상쾌하고 시원하게 느껴진다고 말했다. 나는 나대로, 이 이름난 커프가 대체 어떻게 그런 명성을 얻었는지 속으로 의아해했다. 우리는 마치 난생처음 한 사슬에 함께 묶인 낯선 개 두 마리 같은 심사로 집에 다다랐다.\n\n마님을 찾으니 온실 중 한 곳에 계시다는 대답이라, 우리는 집 뒤편 정원으로 돌아가서, 하인 하나를 보내 마님을 모셔 오게 했다. 기다리는 동안, 커프 부장은 우리 왼편의 상록수 아치 너머를 들여다보다가, 우리 장미원을 발견하고는, 여태 그가 보인 것 중 처음으로 관심 비슷한 기색을 띠며 곧장 그 안으로 걸어 들어갔다. 정원사가 놀라고 내가 못마땅해하게도, 이 이름난 경찰관은 장미원이라는 그 하찮은 주제에 관한 한 그야말로 지식의 보고임이 드러났다.\n\n\"아, 여기는 남쪽과 남서쪽으로 향한 방향이 딱 알맞구려.\" 하고 부장이 그 희끗한 머리를 끄덕이며, 우울한 목소리에 한 줄기 기쁨을 실어 말한다. \"이게 바로 장미원에 걸맞은 모양이지—네모 안에 동그라미를 앉힌 것만 한 게 없다오. 그렇지, 그렇지. 화단마다 사이로 길을 낸 것도 그렇고. 하지만 이렇게 자갈길이어서는 안 되지. 잔디요, 정원사 양반—장미 사이로는 잔디 길을 내야지. 자갈은 장미한테 너무 딱딱하니까. 저기 저 흰 장미와 연분홍 장미를 심은 화단은 참 곱기도 하구려. 이 둘은 늘 잘 어울리지 않소? 여기 이건 흰 사향장미로군, 베터리지 양반—우리네 오래된 잉글랜드 장미가 저 가장 빼어나고 가장 새로운 것들 틈에서도 고개를 꼿꼿이 쳐들고 있구려. 어여쁜 것!\" 하고 부장은 그 여윈 손가락으로 사향장미를 어루만지며, 마치 어린아이에게 말하듯 그것에게 말을 건넨다.\n\n이런 자가 레이철 아가씨의 다이아몬드를 되찾고, 그것을 훔친 도둑을 알아낼 위인이라니, 참으로 그럴싸한 노릇이었다!\n\n\"장미를 무척 좋아하시나 봅니다, 부장님?\" 하고 내가 말했다.\n\n\"나는 무언가를 좋아할 겨를이 그리 많지 않소.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"허나 내게 잠시라도 쏟을 애정이 생기면, 베터리지 양반, 그건 대개 장미 차지가 되지. 나는 아버지의 묘목밭에서 장미 틈에 파묻혀 삶을 시작했고, 할 수만 있다면 장미 틈에서 삶을 끝맺을 작정이오. 그렇소. 언젠가는 (하느님 뜻이라면) 도둑 잡는 일에서 물러나, 장미 기르는 일에 손을 대 볼 참이지. 내 화단 사이에는 잔디 길을 낼 거요, 정원사 양반.\" 하고 부장은 말하는데, 우리 장미원의 자갈길이 그의 마음에 못내 거슬리게 남아 있는 모양이었다.\n\n\"그거 좀 별난 취향으로 보입니다, 나리.\" 하고 나는 조심스레 말을 꺼냈다. \"나리 같은 직업에 몸담은 분치고는 말입니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 주위를 한번 둘러보기만 한다면 (하기야 사람들 대부분은 그러지 않지만),\" 하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"사람의 취향이라는 것이, 대개는, 그 사람의 생업과 될 수 있는 한 정반대라는 걸 알게 될 거요. 장미와 도둑보다 서로 더 정반대되는 두 가지를 내게 대 보시오. 그러면 그에 맞춰 내 취향을 바로잡으리다—이 나이에 너무 늦은 게 아니라면 말이지. 정원사 양반, 다마스크 장미가 저 여린 품종 대부분한테 제법 쓸 만한 대목(臺木)이라는 걸 아시지 않소? 아! 그럴 줄 알았소. 여기 한 부인이 오고 있구려. 저분이 버린더 마님이오?\"\n\n그는, 나나 정원사보다 먼저 마님을 알아보았다. 우리는 어느 쪽을 봐야 하는지 알고 있었고 그는 몰랐는데도 말이다. 나는 그가 첫눈에 보이는 것보다는 꽤나 눈치 빠른 사람이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다.\n\n그 부장의 겉모습이었는지, 아니면 그의 용무였는지—그 하나 때문인지 둘 다 때문인지—마님은 다소 당혹스러워하시는 듯했다. 마님은, 내가 마님을 겪어 온 그 오랜 세월에 처음으로, 낯선 이와의 면담에서 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 어쩔 줄 몰라 하셨다. 커프 부장은 곧바로 마님의 마음을 편하게 해 드렸다. 그는 우리가 저를 부르기 전에 이 도난 사건에 다른 사람이 쓰인 적이 있는지를 물었다. 그리고 다른 사람이 불려 와 지금 이 집에 있다는 말을 듣고는, 다른 무슨 일에 착수하기 전에 먼저 그 사람과 이야기하게 해 달라고 청했다.\n\n마님이 앞장서 돌아가셨다. 부장은 마님을 뒤따르기 전에, 정원사에게 작별의 말 한마디를 남겨 그 자갈길에 관한 제 마음속 응어리를 풀었다. \"마님을 구슬려 잔디를 한번 써 보시게.\" 하고 그는 그 길들을 못마땅하게 쏘아보며 말했다. \"자갈은 안 돼! 자갈은 안 된다니까!\"\n\n커프 부장에게 소개되었을 때, 어째서 시그레이브 경정이 제 실물보다 몇 치수는 작아 보였는지, 나로서는 나서서 설명할 재간이 없다. 그저 그 사실만을 말할 수 있을 뿐이다. 두 사람은 함께 물러갔고, 사람이라면 누구도 끼어들 수 없게 문을 걸어 잠근 채 지겹도록 오랜 시간을 머물렀다. 그들이 나왔을 때, 경정 나리는 잔뜩 흥분해 있었고, 부장 나리는 하품을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"부장께서 버린더 아가씨의 거실을 보고 싶어 하시네.\" 하고 시그레이브 씨가 잔뜩 위엄을 부리며 열을 올려 내게 이른다. \"부장께서 물으실 것이 좀 있으실 게야. 부장을 잘 모시게, 알겠나!\"\n\n이런 식으로 내가 이래라저래라 부림을 받는 동안, 나는 그 위대한 커프를 바라보았다. 그 위대한 커프는, 저대로, 내가 앞서 눈여겨본 그 조용히 무언가를 기다리는 눈길로 시그레이브 경정을 바라보고 있었다. 그가 제 동료 관리가 머잖아 얼간이 꼴을 드러내기를 노리고 있었다고 딱 잘라 말할 수는 없다—다만 나는 그러리라고 강하게 짐작했다고는 말할 수 있다.\n\n나는 앞장서 위층으로 올라갔다. 부장은 그 인도식 캐비닛 구석구석과 \"규방\" 사방을 나긋나긋 훑어보며, (경정 나리께는 이따금씩만, 내게는 끊임없이) 이런저런 것을 물었는데, 그 물음의 갈피는 우리 둘 다에게 똑같이 알 수 없는 것이었다고 나는 생각한다. 이윽고 그의 발길은 그 문께에 이르러, 여러분도 아시는 그 장식 그림과 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다. 그는 여윈 손가락 하나를 캐묻듯이, 자물쇠 바로 아래의 그 작은 얼룩에 갖다 댔는데, 그것은 시그레이브 경정이 여자 하인들이 죄다 방 안으로 몰려든 것을 나무랄 때 이미 눈여겨보았던 바로 그 얼룩이었다.\n\n\"그거 안됐군.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"어쩌다 이리됐소?\"\n\n그는 그 물음을 내게 던졌다. 나는, 여자 하인들이 전날 아침 그 방으로 몰려들었고, 그 가운데 몇몇의 속치마가 이런 낭패를 저질렀다고 대답했다. \"시그레이브 경정께서 그 여자들을 내보내셨습니다, 나리.\" 하고 나는 덧붙였다. \"더 해코지를 하기 전에 말입니다.\"\n\n\"옳거니!\" 하고 경정 나리가 그 군인다운 투로 말한다. \"내가 그들을 내보냈지. 속치마들이 저지른 짓이오, 부장—속치마들이 저지른 짓이라오.\"\n\n\"어느 속치마가 저지른 짓인지 눈여겨보셨소?\" 하고 커프 부장이 물었는데, 여전히 그 동료 관리가 아니라 내게 말을 건네고 있었다.\n\n\"아닙니다, 나리.\"\n\n그러자 그는 시그레이브 경정께로 돌아서서 말했다. \"경정께서는 눈여겨보셨겠지요?\"\n\n경정 나리는 좀 당황한 낯빛이었으나, 애써 태연한 척했다. \"내 기억을 다 짜낼 수야 없지 않소, 부장.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그깟 하찮은 일—그저 하찮은 일이니 말이오.\"\n\n커프 부장은, 장미원의 자갈길을 바라보았을 때처럼 시그레이브 씨를 바라보았고, 그 우울한 투로, 우리가 여태 맛본 적 없던 그의 진면목을 처음으로 우리에게 슬쩍 보여 주었다.\n\n\"지난주에 나는 은밀히 한 사건을 조사했소, 경정 나리.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그 사건의 한쪽 끝에는 살인이 있었고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 아무도 그 까닭을 대지 못하는, 식탁보에 묻은 잉크 한 방울이 있었지. 이 더러운 조그만 세상의 더없이 지저분한 길바닥을 헤매고 다닌 내 온 경험을 통틀어, 나는 여태 하찮은 일 따위란 것과 마주친 적이 한 번도 없소. 이 일에서 한 걸음이라도 더 나아가기 전에, 우리는 그 얼룩을 낸 속치마를 봐야 하고, 그 페인트가 언제 젖어 있었는지를 확실히 알아야 하오.\"\n\n경정 나리는—면박당한 것을 다소 뾰로통하게 받아들이며—그 여자들을 불러올까요, 하고 물었다. 커프 부장은 잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤, 한숨을 쉬고는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"페인트 문제를 먼저 다룹시다. 페인트로 말하면 예냐 아니냐의 문제라—간단하지. 여자들로 말하면 속치마의 문제라—길고 말이오. 어제 아침 하인들이 이 방에 있었던 게 몇 시였소? 열한 시—응? 어제 아침 열한 시에 그 페인트가 젖어 있었는지 말라 있었는지 아는 사람이 이 집에 누구 없소?\"\n\n\"마님의 조카 되시는 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이 아십니다.\" 하고 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그 신사분이 이 집에 계시오?\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 더할 나위 없이 가까이 있었다—그 위대한 커프에게 소개될 첫 기회를 기다리면서 말이다. 반 분도 안 되어 그는 방 안에 들어와, 다음과 같이 제 증언을 하고 있었다.\n\n\"저 문은요, 부장님.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"버린더 아가씨가, 제 감독 아래, 제 도움을 받아, 제가 손수 조제한 용매로 칠한 것입니다. 그 용매는 그것과 함께 쓴 물감이 어떤 것이든 열두 시간이면 말립니다.\"\n\n\"그 얼룩진 부분을 언제 칠하셨는지 기억하시오, 나리?\" 하고 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"똑똑히 기억합니다.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"거기가 그 문에서 맨 마지막으로 마무리한 자리였지요. 우리는 지난 수요일에 그걸 끝내고 싶었고—제가 손수 오후 세 시 무렵, 아니 그 조금 뒤에 마무리했습니다.\"\n\n\"오늘이 금요일이오.\" 하고 커프 부장이 시그레이브 경정께 말을 건네며 말했다. \"거슬러 셈해 봅시다, 나리. 수요일 오후 세 시에, 그 그림의 그 부분이 마무리되었소. 용매가 열두 시간이면 그걸 말렸으니—다시 말해, 목요일 새벽 세 시면 말랐다는 얘기지. 목요일 아침 열한 시에 경정께서는 여기서 조사를 벌였고. 열한에서 셋을 빼면, 여덟이 남소. 그 페인트는 이미 여덟 시간을 마른 상태였던 거요, 경정 나리, 여자 하인들의 속치마가 그걸 문질렀다고 짐작하셨을 그때 말이오.\"\n\n시그레이브 씨가 얻어맞은 첫 번째 결정타였다! 그가 그 딱한 페넬로피를 의심하지만 않았던들, 나는 그를 가엾이 여겼으련만.\n\n페인트 문제를 매듭짓고 나자, 커프 부장은 그 순간부터, 제 동료 관리를 가망 없는 자로 아주 단념해 버리고—둘 중 더 미더운 조력자인 프랭클린 도련님께로 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"충분히 있을 법한 일입니다, 나리.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"도련님이 그 실마리를 우리 손에 쥐여 주셨다는 것이 말입니다.\"\n\n그 말이 그의 입에서 떨어지기가 무섭게, 침실 문이 열리고, 레이철 아가씨가 불쑥 우리 가운데로 나왔다.\n\n아가씨는, 그가 저와는 생판 낯선 사람이라는 것을 알아채는 (혹은 개의하는) 기색도 없이, 부장에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"방금 말씀하셨나요,\" 하고 아가씨가 프랭클린 도련님을 가리키며 물었다. \"저 사람이 그 실마리를 당신 손에 쥐여 주었다고요?\"\n\n(\"이분이 버린더 아가씨입니다.\" 하고 나는 부장의 등 뒤에서 속삭였다.)\n\n\"저 신사분이 말이지요, 아가씨,\" 하고 부장이—그 강철빛 잿빛 눈으로 우리 아가씨의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살피며—말한다. \"어쩌면 그 실마리를 우리 손에 쥐여 주셨을지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n아가씨는 한순간 돌아서서, 프랭클린 도련님을 바라보려 했다. 바라보려 했다고 말하는 것은, 두 사람의 눈길이 마주치기 전에 아가씨가 별안간 다시 시선을 돌려 버렸기 때문이다. 아가씨의 마음속에 무언가 야릇한 동요가 이는 듯했다. 아가씨는 얼굴이 붉어졌다가, 이내 다시 파리해졌다. 그 파리함과 함께, 아가씨의 얼굴에 새로운 기색이 떠올랐다—보고 있자니 나를 흠칫하게 하는 그런 기색이었다.\n\n\"아가씨의 물음에 답해 드렸으니, 아가씨,\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"이번에는 제가 여쭙게 해 주시지요. 여기, 아가씨 문의 그림에 얼룩이 하나 있습니다. 혹 언제 그리되었는지 아십니까? 아니면 누가 그랬는지요?\"\n\n아무런 대답도 하지 않고, 레이철 아가씨는, 그가 말을 하지 않았다는 듯, 아니면 아가씨가 그 말을 듣지 못했다는 듯, 제 물음을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"당신도 또 한 사람의 경찰관인가요?\" 하고 아가씨가 물었다.\n\n\"저는 커프 부장입니다, 아가씨, 형사과 소속이지요.\"\n\n\"젊은 처녀의 충고도 새겨들을 만하다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"기꺼이 듣겠습니다, 아가씨.\"\n\n\"당신 혼자 힘으로 당신 할 일을 하세요—그리고 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 당신을 돕게 놔두지 마세요!\"\n\n아가씨는 그 말을 어찌나 앙심 서리게, 어찌나 사납게, 그 목소리와 그 눈빛에 프랭클린 도련님을 향한 그토록 유별난 적의를 터뜨리며 내뱉던지—비록 내가 아가씨를 갓난아기 적부터 알아 왔고, 비록 내가 마님 바로 다음으로 아가씨를 아끼고 우러러 왔건만—나는 난생처음으로 레이철 아가씨가 부끄러웠다.\n\n커프 부장의 꿈쩍 않는 눈은 아가씨의 얼굴에서 조금도 떨어지지 않았다. \"고맙습니다, 아가씨.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"혹 그 얼룩에 대해 뭔가 아시는 게 있습니까? 아가씨 자신이 실수로 그리한 건 아니고요?\"\n\n\"저는 그 얼룩에 대해 아무것도 몰라요.\"\n\n그 대답을 남기고, 아가씨는 돌아서서, 다시 제 침실에 틀어박혔다. 이번에는 나도 들었다—앞서 페넬로피가 들었던 것처럼—아가씨가 다시 혼자가 되자마자 왈칵 울음을 터뜨리는 소리를.\n\n나는 차마 부장을 바라볼 수가 없었다—나는 내게서 가장 가까이 서 있던 프랭클린 도련님을 바라보았다. 그는 방금 일어난 일에 나보다도 더 애타게 괴로워하는 듯 보였다.\n\n\"내가 아가씨 일로 마음이 편치 않다고 자네한테 말했잖나.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이제 그 까닭을 알겠지.\"\n\n\"버린더 아가씨는 다이아몬드를 잃은 일로 심기가 좀 상하신 모양이군요.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"값진 보석이니 말입니다. 그럴 만도 하지요! 그럴 만도 하고말고요!\"\n\n여기, 내가 (전날 아가씨가 시그레이브 경정 앞에서 자제심을 잃었을 때) 아가씨를 위해 둘러댔던 바로 그 변명이, 나 같은 사심을 품었을 리 없는 사람의 입에서—그도 그럴 것이 그는 생판 낯선 이였으니!—다시 한번 아가씨를 위해 둘러대지고 있었다. 일종의 차가운 전율이 나를 훑고 지나갔는데, 그 까닭을 그때는 알 수 없었다. 이제 와서 나는 안다, 바로 그 순간 나는 처음으로 낌새를 챘음이 틀림없다는 것을—커프 부장의 마음속에 이 사건을 비추는 어떤 새로운 (그리고 소름 끼치는) 빛이 별안간 드리웠다는 낌새를 말이다. 그것은 오로지, 그리고 온전히, 그가 그 첫 면담에서 레이철 아가씨에게서 본 것과 레이철 아가씨에게서 들은 것의 결과였다.\n\n\"젊은 처녀의 혀란 것은 특권을 누리는 지체이지요, 나리.\" 하고 부장이 프랭클린 도련님께 말한다. \"방금 일은 잊어버리고, 이 일을 곧장 밀고 나갑시다. 도련님 덕분에, 우리는 페인트가 언제 말랐는지를 알지요. 다음으로 알아내야 할 것은, 그 얼룩이 없는 페인트를 마지막으로 본 것이 언제냐 하는 겁니다. 도련님은 어깨 위에 제대로 된 머리를 이고 계시니—제 말뜻을 아실 겁니다.\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 마음을 가다듬고, 애를 써서 레이철 아가씨 생각에서 벗어나 지금 눈앞의 일로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"알 것 같습니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"시간의 문제를 좁히면 좁힐수록, 조사의 범위도 그만큼 좁혀지는 거로군요.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다, 나리.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"수요일 오후에, 이 일을 끝낸 뒤에, 여기 이 손질한 자리를 눈여겨보셨습니까?\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님은 고개를 저으며 대답했다. \"봤다고는 할 수 없군요.\"\n\n\"자네는 봤나?\" 하고 커프 부장이 내게 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n\"저 역시 봤다고는 할 수 없습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"수요일 밤, 맨 마지막으로 그 방에 있었던 사람은 누구요?\"\n\n\"레이철 아가씨일 겁니다, 나리.\"\n\n거기서 프랭클린 도련님이 끼어들었다. \"아니면 어쩌면 자네 딸일지도 모르지, 베터리지.\" 그는 커프 부장께로 돌아서서, 내 딸이 버린더 아가씨의 몸종이라고 설명했다.\n\n\"베터리지 양반, 따님더러 올라오라 하시오. 잠깐!\" 하고 부장이, 남의 귀에 들리지 않게 나를 창가로 데려가며 말한다. \"여기 이 댁 경정께서는,\" 하고 그가 속삭임으로 말을 이었다. \"이 사건을 어떻게 다뤄 왔는지를 내게 제법 소상히 보고했소. 그중에서도, 그는 제 입으로 실토한 대로, 하인들의 심기를 잔뜩 건드려 놨더군. 그들을 다시 다독여 놓는 것이 아주 중요하오. 따님한테, 그리고 나머지 사람들한테도, 내 인사와 함께 이 두 가지를 전하시오. 첫째, 나는 아직 내 앞에 다이아몬드가 도둑맞았다는 증거가 하나도 없다는 것. 나는 다만 다이아몬드가 없어졌다는 것만 알 뿐이오. 둘째, 여기서 하인들을 상대로 하는 내 볼일이란, 그저 그들더러 다 같이 머리를 맞대어 그것을 찾도록 나를 도와 달라고 청하는 것뿐이라는 것.\"\n\n시그레이브 경정이 여자 하인들의 방에 출입 금지령을 내렸을 적에 겪은 그 경험이, 여기서 요긴하게 쓰였다.\n\n\"부장님, 제가 외람되나마 그 여자들한테 세 번째 것을 하나 더 일러도 될까요?\" 하고 내가 물었다. \"그들이 (부장님 인사와 함께) 마음 내키는 대로 위아래층을 들락날락 부산을 떨고, 제 침실을 휙휙 드나들어도 좋다고 말입니다.\"\n\n\"얼마든지 마음대로.\" 하고 부장이 말했다.\n\n\"그 말이면 그들을 다독여 놓을 겁니다, 나리.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"요리사에서 부엌데기에 이르기까지 말입니다.\"\n\n\"가서, 당장 그리하시오, 베터리지 양반.\"\n\n나는 그 일을 오 분도 안 되어 해치웠다. 침실 대목에 이르렀을 때 딱 한 가지 어려움이 있었다. 여자 식솔 전부가, 커프 부장을 돕겠다는 안달로 애가 타서, 자원 증인 노릇을 하겠다며 나와 페넬로피를 뒤따라 위층으로 올라오려는 것을 막느라, 우두머리로서 내 권위를 제법 뻣뻣하게 부려야만 했다.\n\n부장은 페넬로피가 마음에 드는 눈치였다. 그는 조금은 덜 음침해졌고, 화단에서 흰 사향장미를 눈여겨보았을 때와 사뭇 비슷한 낯빛이 되었다. 여기, 부장이 내 딸에게서 이끌어 낸 그 증언이 있다. 딸아이는, 내 생각에, 아주 어여쁘게 그것을 진술했다—허나, 저런! 그 아이는 어디로 보나 영락없는 내 자식이다. 제 어미를 닮은 구석이라곤 하나도 없으니. 아이고 맙소사, 제 어미를 닮은 데라곤 눈곱만큼도 없다니까!\n\n페넬로피 신문: 물감 섞는 일을 거들었던 터라, 문의 그 그림에 생생한 관심을 두었음. 자물쇠 아래의 그 손질한 자리를 눈여겨보았으니, 그것이 맨 마지막으로 칠한 자리였기 때문임. 그로부터 몇 시간 뒤에, 얼룩 없는 그것을 보았음. 밤 열두 시나 되어서까지도, 얼룩 없는 그대로 두고 나왔음. 그 시각에, 침실에서 제 아가씨께 잘 자라는 인사를 드렸음. \"규방\"에서 시계가 치는 소리를 들었음. 그때 제 손을 그 칠한 문의 손잡이에 얹고 있었음. 그 페인트가 젖어 있음을 알고 있었음 (앞서 말한 대로 물감 섞는 일을 거들었으므로). 그것에 닿지 않으려고 각별히 조심했음. 제 드레스 자락을 치켜들고 있었으며 그때 페인트에 아무 얼룩도 없었음을 맹세할 수 있음. 나가는 길에 제 드레스가 어쩌다 그것에 닿지 않았노라고는 맹세할 수 없음. 제가 입고 있던 드레스를 기억하니, 그것이 새것이요 레이철 아가씨께 받은 선물이었기 때문임. 제 아비도 그것을 기억하며, 그에 대해 증언할 수 있음. 그것을 가져올 수 있고, 가져오려 했고, 실제로 가져왔음. 제 아비가 그것을 그날 밤 딸아이가 입었던 드레스로 알아봄. 그 자락을 살폈으니, 크기가 큰 탓에 오래 걸리는 일이었음. 그 어디에서도 페인트 얼룩의 그림자조차 발견되지 않음. 페넬로피의 증언 끝—그리고 아주 어여쁘고, 게다가 아주 설득력 있는 것이었음. 서명, 게이브리얼 베터리지.\n\n부장이 다음으로 한 일은, 혹 그 방에 들어가 꼬리를 휙 쳐서 이런 낭패를 저질렀을 법한 큰 개가 이 집에 있는지를 내게 캐묻는 것이었다. 그런 일은 있을 수 없다는 말을 듣고, 그는 다음으로 확대경을 가져오라 하여, 그것으로 들여다보면 그 얼룩이 어찌 보이는지를 살폈다. (사람 손 같은) 살갗 자국은 페인트에 찍혀 있지 않았다. 눈에 보이는 온갖 흔적들은—누군가의 드레스에 달린 어떤 헐거운 것이 지나가다 스치는 바람에 페인트가 문질러졌음을 말해 주는 흔적들이었다. 그 누군가는 (페넬로피의 증언과 프랭클린 도련님의 증언을 맞추어 보건대) 목요일 새벽 자정과 세 시 사이에 그 방에 있었고, 이 낭패를 저지른 것이 틀림없었다.\n\n조사를 이 지점까지 끌고 온 뒤에야, 커프 부장은 시그레이브 경정이라는 위인이 아직 방 안에 남아 있음을 알아챘고, 그리하여 그 동료 관리를 위해 지금까지의 경과를 다음과 같이 간추려 주었다.\n\n\"경정 나리의 이 하찮은 일이 말입니다,\" 하고 부장이 문의 그 자리를 가리키며 말한다. \"지난번 나리가 눈여겨본 이래로 그 중요함이 조금 자라났습니다. 지금 이 조사 단계에서는, 내가 보기에, 그 얼룩에서 출발하여 밝혀내야 할 것이 세 가지 있소. (첫째) 이 집에 페인트 얼룩이 묻은 옷가지가 있는지를 알아낼 것. (둘째) 그 옷이 누구 것인지를 알아낼 것. (셋째) 그 사람이 자정과 새벽 세 시 사이에 이 방에 있으면서 페인트를 문지른 일을 어떻게 해명하는지를 알아낼 것. 만일 그 사람이 나리를 흡족하게 대지 못한다면, 다이아몬드를 손에 넣은 그 손을 찾느라 멀리 헤맬 것 없소. 괜찮으시다면 이 일은 나 혼자 해 나가리다. 그러니 나리를 시내에서의 본연의 업무에서 더는 붙들어 두지 않겠소. 보아하니 나리의 부하 하나가 여기 있구려. 혹 내가 그가 필요할 때를 대비해, 그를 여기 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 남겨 두시오—그럼 나리께 좋은 아침 인사를 드리게 해 주시오.\"\n\n시그레이브 경정이 그 부장에게 품은 존경은 컸다. 그러나 저 자신에게 품은 존경은 그보다도 더 컸다. 그 이름난 커프에게 호되게 얻어맞은 그는, 방을 나서면서, 제 딴에 있는 재간을 다해 야무지게 되받아쳤다.\n\n\"나는 여태 아무런 의견도 내지 않고 삼가 왔소.\" 하고 경정 나리가, 그 군인다운 목소리를 여전히 멀쩡히 굴리며 말한다. \"이제 이 사건을 당신 손에 넘기며 딱 한마디만 하겠소. 부장, 두더지 흙두둑을 가지고 산을 만드는 일이란 게 세상엔 있는 법이오. 좋은 아침이오.\"\n\n\"제 머리가 너무 높이 있어 그것이 눈에 뵈지 않는 통에, 두더지 흙두둑을 가지고 아무것도 만들어 내지 못하는 일이란 것도 세상엔 있는 법이지요.\" 그런 말로 제 동료 관리의 인사를 되돌려 준 뒤, 커프 부장은 홱 돌아서서, 혼자 창가로 걸어갔다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님과 나는 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질지 기다렸다. 부장은 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고 창가에 서서, 밖을 내다보며, \"여름날의 마지막 장미\" 가락을 혼자 나직이 휘파람으로 불고 있었다. 뒷날 일이 진행되는 가운데 나는 알게 되었는데, 그가 예의를 잊고 휘파람을 부는 것은 오직 그의 머리가 저만의 은밀한 목적을 향해 한 치 한 치 길을 더듬으며 골똘히 돌아갈 때뿐이었고, 그럴 때면 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"가 그를 거드는 것이 분명했다. 짐작건대 그것이 어떻게든 그의 성미에 들어맞았던 모양이다. 아시다시피 그것은 그에게 가장 아끼는 장미를 떠올리게 했고, 게다가 그가 그것을 휘파람으로 불면, 그것은 세상에 다시없이 우울한 가락이 되었다.\n\n일이 분쯤 뒤, 부장은 창가에서 돌아서서, 방 한복판으로 걸어 들어가 거기 멈춰 서더니, 레이철 아가씨의 침실 문에 눈을 둔 채 깊은 생각에 잠겼다. 조금 있다가 그는 정신을 차리고, \"그만하면 됐다\"는 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 내게 말을 건네어, 마님이 형편 닿는 대로 되도록 빨리 십 분간 이야기를 나눌 수 있게 해 달라고 청했다.\n\n이 전갈을 안고 방을 나서던 나는, 프랭클린 도련님이 부장에게 무언가 묻는 소리를 듣고, 나 역시 그 대답을 들으려고 문지방께에 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"이제 짐작이 가시오,\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 물었다. \"누가 그 다이아몬드를 훔쳤는지 말이오?\"\n\n\"아무도 그 다이아몬드를 훔치지 않았습니다.\" 하고 커프 부장이 대답했다.\n\n우리 둘 다 그 사건에 대한 그 놀라운 견해에 흠칫했고, 둘 다 그게 무슨 뜻인지 말해 달라고 간곡히 청했다.\n\n\"좀 기다려 보시오.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"그 수수께끼의 조각들이 아직 다 맞춰지지 않았으니 말이오.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XIII\n\n나는 마님을 마님의 거실에서 뵈었다. 커프 부장이 마님과 이야기하고 싶어 한다고 아뢰자, 마님은 흠칫하시며 언짢은 낯빛이 되셨다.\n\n\"내가 꼭 그 사람을 만나야 하느냐?\" 하고 마님이 물으셨다. \"네가 나를 대신할 수는 없겠니, 게이브리얼?\"\n\n나는 이 말을 어찌 헤아려야 할지 몰라 난감했고, 짐작건대 그 난감함이 내 얼굴에 고스란히 드러났던 모양이다. 마님은 고맙게도 당신 마음을 풀어 설명해 주셨다.\n\n\"아무래도 내 신경이 좀 곤두선 것 같구나.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"저 런던에서 온 경찰관에게는 어쩐지 내가 움츠러들게 하는 무언가가 있어—왜인지는 나도 모르겠다. 그가 이 집에 근심과 불행을 안고 들어오고 있다는 예감이 드는구나. 참으로 어리석고, 나답지 않은 노릇이다만—그래도 그런 것을 어쩌겠느냐.\"\n\n나는 이 말에 무어라 대답해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 커프 부장을 볼수록, 나는 그가 더욱 마음에 들었다. 마님은 내게 당신 속내를 털어놓고 나자 조금 기운을 차리셨다—앞서 여러분께 말씀드린 대로, 본디 담대한 분이시니 말이다.\n\n\"만나야 한다면, 만나야겠지.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"허나 그를 혼자서 대면할 엄두가 도무지 나지 않는구나. 그를 들여보내고, 게이브리얼, 그가 머무는 동안 너도 여기 함께 있거라.\"\n\n이것이, 마님이 아직 어린 처녀였던 시절 이래로 내가 기억하는, 마님이 겪으신 첫 신경 발작이었다. 나는 \"규방\"으로 돌아갔다. 프랭클린 도련님은 정원으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나가, 이제 떠날 시각이 가까워 오던 고드프리 씨와 어울렸다. 커프 부장과 나는 곧장 마님의 방으로 갔다.\n\n단언컨대 마님은 그를 보시자 안색이 한 겹 더 파리해지셨다! 그러나 그 밖의 점에서는 마님은 자제하셨고, 부장에게 내가 자리에 있어도 괜찮겠냐고 물으셨다. 마님은 고맙게도, 내가 당신의 오랜 하인일 뿐 아니라 믿음직한 조언자이기도 하며, 집안일에 관한 것이라면 상의하기에 가장 이로울 사람이 바로 나라고 덧붙이셨다. 부장은 정중히 대답하기를, 하인들 전반에 대해 할 말이 있고 또 그 방면에서 내 경험이 이미 제게 다소 도움이 되었던 터라, 내가 자리에 있는 것을 오히려 고맙게 여기겠노라고 했다. 마님이 의자 두 개를 가리키셨고, 우리는 곧바로 회의에 들어갔다.\n\n\"저는 이미 이 사건에 대해 한 가지 의견을 세웠습니다.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"그것은 마님께서 허락해 주신다면 당분간은 제 안에 담아 두고자 합니다. 지금 제 볼일은, 제가 위층 버린더 아가씨의 거실에서 알아낸 것과, 그리고 (마님의 윤허 아래) 다음에 하기로 정한 것을 말씀드리는 것입니다.\"\n\n이어 그는 페인트에 묻은 얼룩 문제로 들어가, 거기서 이끌어 낸 결론들을 진술했다—시그레이브 경정에게 진술했던 그대로였다 (다만 말씨는 한결 정중했다). \"한 가지는,\" 하고 그가 결론지어 말했다. \"확실합니다. 다이아몬드가 캐비닛 서랍에서 없어졌다는 것이지요. 또 한 가지는 확실한 것이나 다름없습니다. 문의 그 얼룩에서 묻어난 자국이, 이 집 누군가에게 딸린 어떤 옷가지에 남아 있는 게 틀림없다는 것입니다. 우리는 한 걸음 더 나아가기 전에 그 옷가지를 찾아내야 합니다.\"\n\n\"그리고 그것을 찾아낸다는 것은,\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"짐작건대, 도둑을 찾아낸다는 뜻이겠지요?\"\n\n\"송구하오나 마님—저는 다이아몬드가 도둑맞았다고 말씀드리지 않습니다. 지금으로서는, 다만 다이아몬드가 없어졌다고만 말씀드릴 뿐입니다. 그 얼룩진 옷을 찾아내는 일이, 그것을 찾는 길로 이어질지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n마님이 나를 바라보셨다. \"너는 이 말을 알아듣겠느냐?\" 하고 물으셨다.\n\n\"커프 부장께서 알아들으시는 겁니다, 마님.\" 하고 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"그 얼룩진 옷을 어떻게 찾아낼 작정이시오?\" 하고 마님이 다시 한번 부장에게 말을 건네며 물으셨다. \"부끄러운 말이오만, 오랜 세월 나와 함께해 온 내 선량한 하인들은, 이미 저 다른 관리에게 제 상자와 방을 뒤짐당했소. 나는 그들이 그런 식으로 두 번씩이나 모욕당하는 것을 허락할 수도 없고, 하지도 않겠소!\"\n\n(이 얼마나 섬길 만한 마님인가! 이야말로 만 사람 가운데 하나 나올까 말까 한 여인이 아닌가!)\n\n\"그것이 바로 제가 마님께 여쭈려던 요점입니다.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"저 다른 관리는, 하인들에게 제가 그들을 의심한다는 것을 내보임으로써, 이 조사에 이만저만 해를 끼친 게 아닙니다. 만일 제가 그들에게 두 번째로 의심받는다고 여길 빌미를 준다면, 그들이 제 앞길에 어떤 훼방을 놓을지 알 수 없지요—특히나 여자들이 그렇습니다. 하나 그와 동시에, 그들의 상자는 반드시 다시 뒤져야 합니다—그야말로 뻔한 이유에서지요. 첫 번째 조사는 다이아몬드만 찾았지만, 두 번째 조사는 그 얼룩진 옷을 찾아야 하니 말입니다. 하인들의 심정을 헤아려야 한다는 데는 저도 전적으로 마님과 뜻을 같이합니다, 마님. 허나 하인들의 옷장을 뒤져야 한다는 것 또한 저는 못지않게 분명히 압니다.\"\n\n이것은 영락없이 막다른 골목처럼 보였다. 마님도, 나보다 한결 고른 말씨로, 그렇게 말씀하셨다.\n\n\"이 난처함을 풀 방책이 제게 하나 있습니다.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"마님께서 승낙만 해 주신다면요. 저는 하인들에게 이 사정을 설명하려 합니다.\"\n\n\"여자들은 대뜸 제가 의심받는다고 여길 겁니다.\" 하고 나는 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다.\n\n\"여자들은 그러지 않을 겁니다, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"제가 그들에게, 수요일 밤 이 집에서 잔 사람이라면 누구든—마님으로부터 아래로 죄다—그 옷장을 살펴볼 참이라고 말할 수 있다면 말이지요. 그저 형식일 뿐입니다.\" 하고 그는 마님을 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다. \"허나 하인들은 그것을 저희와 저희 윗분들 사이의 공평한 처사로 받아들일 것이고, 그리하여 조사를 훼방하기는커녕, 그것을 돕는 것을 명예로 삼으려 들 것입니다.\"\n\n나는 그 말이 옳음을 알아보았다. 마님도, 처음의 놀라움이 가시고 나자, 역시 그 말이 옳음을 알아보셨다.\n\n\"그 조사가 꼭 필요하다고 확신하시오?\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다.\n\n\"제가 보기에는, 우리가 눈앞에 둔 목적에 이르는 가장 빠른 길입니다, 마님.\"\n\n마님이 몸종을 부르는 종을 울리려고 자리에서 일어나셨다. \"그대는 하인들에게 이야기하시오.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"내 옷장 열쇠를 손에 쥐고서 말이오.\"\n\n커프 부장이 아주 뜻밖의 물음으로 마님을 멈춰 세웠다.\n\n\"먼저 확실히 해 두는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까,\" 하고 그가 물었다. \"이 집의 다른 숙녀분들과 신사분들도 함께 승낙하실지를 말입니다.\"\n\n\"이 집에 다른 숙녀라고는 버린더 아가씨뿐이오.\" 하고 마님이 놀란 낯빛으로 대답하셨다. \"신사라고는 내 조카들인 블레이크 도련님과 에이블화이트 조카뿐이고. 그 셋 중 누구에게서도 거절이 나올 염려는 조금도 없소.\"\n\n나는 여기서 마님께 고드프리 씨가 떠나려던 참임을 일깨워 드렸다. 내가 그 말을 하는 참에, 고드프리 씨 본인이 작별 인사를 하려고 문을 두드렸고, 그를 역까지 배웅하려던 프랭클린 도련님이 뒤따라 들어왔다. 마님이 그 난처함을 설명하셨다. 고드프리 씨는 곧바로 그것을 매듭지었다. 그는 창문 너머로 새뮤얼을 불러 제 여행 가방을 다시 위층으로 올려 두라 이르고는, 손수 그 열쇠를 커프 부장의 손에 쥐여 주었다. \"제 짐은 이 조사가 끝나면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저를 따라 런던으로 부치면 되겠지요.\" 부장은 격에 맞는 사과와 함께 그 열쇠를 받아 들었다. \"한낱 형식 때문에 폐를 끼치게 되어 송구합니다, 나리. 하나 윗분들이 보이시는 본보기는, 하인들이 이 조사를 순순히 받아들이게 하는 데 놀라운 힘을 낼 것입니다.\" 고드프리 씨는, 더없이 마음을 헤아리는 태도로 마님께 작별을 고한 뒤, 레이철 아가씨께 작별의 전언을 남겼는데, 그 말투로 미루어 내 마음속에는 그가 거절을 순순히 받아들이지 않았으며, 다음 기회에 아가씨께 그 혼담을 한 번 더 꺼낼 작정임이 분명해졌다. 프랭클린 도련님은, 제 사촌을 따라 나서면서, 부장에게 제 옷은 죄다 살펴봐도 좋으며, 제가 지닌 것 중에 자물쇠로 잠가 둔 것이라곤 하나도 없다고 일러 주었다. 커프 부장은 더없이 정중히 사의를 표했다. 여러분도 보시다시피, 그의 뜻은 마님과, 고드프리 씨와, 프랭클린 도련님에게서 더할 나위 없이 흔쾌하게 받아들여진 셈이었다. 이제 하인들을 불러 모아 그 얼룩진 옷을 찾기 시작하기에 앞서, 그들의 본을 따르는 데 남은 이라고는 오직 레이철 아가씨 한 사람뿐이었다.\n\n부장에 대한 마님의 그 알 수 없는 거부감은, 우리 둘만 다시 남게 되자마자, 이 회의를 마님께 그 어느 때보다 더 언짢은 것으로 만드는 듯했다. \"내가 버린더 아가씨의 열쇠를 그대에게 내려보내면,\" 하고 마님이 그에게 말씀하셨다. \"짐작건대 당분간 내게 바라는 바는 다 이룬 셈이겠지요?\"\n\n\"송구하오나 마님.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"시작하기에 앞서, 괜찮으시다면, 세탁 장부를 봤으면 합니다. 그 얼룩진 옷가지가 아마포 종류일 수도 있으니까요. 만일 이 수색이 아무 소득 없이 끝난다면, 저는 다음으로 이 집에 있는 모든 아마포와, 세탁에 내보낸 모든 아마포를 낱낱이 셈할 수 있기를 바랍니다. 만일 없어진 물건이 하나라도 있다면, 적어도 그것에 페인트 얼룩이 묻어 있었으며, 그 임자 되는 사람이 어제나 오늘, 일부러 그것을 없애 버렸으리라는 짐작은 서게 될 테지요. 시그레이브 경정께서는,\" 하고 부장은 나를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"목요일 아침 여자 하인들이 죄다 방으로 몰려들었을 때, 그들의 주의를 그 얼룩으로 이끌었지요. 그것이, 베터리지 양반, 시그레이브 경정의 그 숱한 실수에 하나 더 보태진 것으로 드러날지도 모릅니다.\"\n\n마님은 내게 종을 울려 세탁 장부를 가져오라 이르셨다. 마님은 그것이 나올 때까지 우리와 함께 계셨는데, 커프 부장이 그것을 살펴본 뒤 마님께 또 다른 청을 할 경우에 대비해서였다.\n\n세탁 장부는 로재나 스피어먼이 들여왔다. 그 아이는 그날 아침 딱하게도 파리하고 초췌한 낯으로 아침 식사에 내려왔으나, 전날의 병에서는 제 여느 일을 할 만큼 웬만큼 회복되어 있었다. 커프 부장은 우리 집 둘째 하녀를 유심히 바라보았다—들어올 때는 그 얼굴을, 나갈 때는 그 굽은 어깨를.\n\n\"내게 더 할 말이 있소?\" 하고 마님이, 여전히 부장의 곁을 벗어나고 싶어 안달하며 물으셨다.\n\n그 위대한 커프는 세탁 장부를 펼쳐, 반 분 만에 그것을 완벽히 파악하고는, 다시 덮었다. \"마님께 감히 마지막 질문 하나로 폐를 끼치겠습니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"우리에게 이 장부를 가져다준 저 젊은 여자는, 다른 하인들만큼 오래 마님 밑에서 일해 왔습니까?\"\n\n\"어째서 그것을 묻소?\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다.\n\n\"제가 그를 마지막으로 보았을 때,\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"그는 절도죄로 감옥에 있었습니다.\"\n\n그다음에는, 그에게 사실대로 이르는 수밖에 도리가 없었다. 마님은 로재나가 당신 밑에서 지내며 보인 착실한 품행과, 갱생원 여감독관이 그를 높이 평가했다는 사실을 힘주어 말씀하셨다. \"설마 그 아이를 의심하는 건 아니겠지요?\" 하고 마님은 끝으로 아주 간곡히 덧붙이셨다.\n\n\"저는 이미 마님께 말씀드렸습니다, 이 집의 그 누구도 도둑질을 저질렀다고 의심하지 않는다고요—지금 이 순간까지는 말입니다.\"\n\n그 대답을 듣고, 마님은 위층으로 올라가 레이철 아가씨의 열쇠를 청하려고 자리에서 일어나셨다. 부장은 나보다 앞질러 마님을 위해 문을 열었다. 그는 아주 깊이 허리를 굽혀 절했다. 마님은 그의 곁을 지나치시며 몸서리를 치셨다.\n\n우리는 기다리고, 또 기다렸으나, 열쇠는 나타나지 않았다. 커프 부장은 내게 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 그 우울한 얼굴을 창 쪽으로 돌리고, 여윈 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고는, \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 혼자 나직이 휘파람으로 불었다.\n\n마침내 새뮤얼이 들어왔는데, 열쇠가 아니라, 내게 전할 쪽지 한 조각을 들고 있었다. 나는 부장의 그 음침한 눈이 내내 나를 지켜보고 있음을 느끼며, 얼마쯤 더듬거리고 애를 먹은 끝에 안경을 찾아 썼다. 그 쪽지에는 마님이 연필로 적으신 두어 줄이 있었다. 그 글은 내게 알리기를, 레이철 아가씨가 제 옷장을 살펴보게 하는 것을 딱 잘라 거절했다는 것이었다. 그 까닭을 묻자, 아가씨는 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다. 다시 묻자, 아가씨는 이렇게 말했다는 것이었다. \"싫으니까 싫은 거예요. 강제로 하신다면 그 힘에는 굽히겠지만, 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 저는 굽히지 않겠어요.\" 나는 마님이 당신 따님에게서 나온 그런 대답을 들고 커프 부장을 대면하기 꺼리시는 심정을 헤아렸다. 만일 내가 젊은이의 사랑스러운 약점을 지니기에는 너무 늙어 버린 처지가 아니었던들, 나 역시 그를 대면할 생각에 얼굴을 붉혔으리라 여겨진다.\n\n\"버린더 아가씨의 열쇠에 대해 무슨 소식이 있소?\" 하고 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"우리 아가씨께서 제 옷장을 살펴보게 하는 것을 거절하십니다.\"\n\n\"아!\" 하고 부장이 말했다.\n\n그의 목소리는 그의 얼굴만큼 그리 완벽하게 다스려진 상태는 아니었다. 그가 \"아!\" 하고 말했을 때, 그것은 듣게 되리라 예상하던 무언가를 들은 사람의 어조였다. 그것은 나를 반은 골나게 하고 반은 겁나게 했다—왜인지는 나도 알 수 없었으나, 그가 그렇게 만들었다.\n\n\"수색을 그만두어야 합니까?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"수색은 그만두어야 하오. 아가씨가 나머지 사람들처럼 그것에 응하기를 거절하시니 말이오. 우리는 이 집의 옷장을 죄다 살펴보든지, 아니면 하나도 살펴보지 말든지 해야 하오. 에이블화이트 씨의 여행 가방은 다음 기차 편으로 런던에 부치고, 세탁 장부는 그것을 가져온 젊은 여자에게, 내 인사와 감사와 함께 돌려주시오.\"\n\n그는 세탁 장부를 탁자 위에 내려놓고는, 주머니칼을 꺼내 제 손톱을 다듬기 시작했다.\n\n\"그리 실망하신 것 같지 않군요.\" 하고 내가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"나는 그리 실망하지 않았소.\"\n\n나는 그에게 속내를 털어놓게 하려고 애썼다.\n\n\"어째서 레이철 아가씨가 당신 앞길에 훼방을 놓으시는 걸까요?\" 하고 내가 캐물었다. \"당신을 돕는 것이 아가씨께 이로운 일 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"좀 기다려 보시오, 베터리지 양반—좀 기다려 보시오.\"\n\n나보다 영리한 머리라면 그의 속셈을 알아보았을지도 모른다. 아니면 나만큼 레이철 아가씨를 아끼지 않는 사람이라면 그의 속셈을 알아보았을지도 모른다. 그에 대한 마님의 그 두려움은 (그 뒤로 내가 생각해 온 바로는) 마님께서는 그의 속셈을 (성경 말씀처럼) \"희미하게 거울로 보듯\" 알아보셨음을 뜻하는 것일 수도 있었다. 나는 아직 그것을 알아보지 못했다—내가 아는 것은 그뿐이다.\n\n\"이제 다음엔 무얼 해야 합니까?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n커프 부장은 그때 다듬고 있던 손톱을 마저 다듬고는, 그것을 우울한 흥미로 잠시 들여다보다가, 주머니칼을 도로 넣었다.\n\n\"정원으로 나갑시다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 그 장미나 한번 보러 갑시다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER XIV\n\n마님의 거실을 나서서 정원으로 가는 가장 가까운 길은, 여러분도 이미 아시는 그 관목 숲 오솔길이었다. 앞으로 벌어질 일을 여러분이 한결 잘 헤아리시도록, 여기에 한 가지 덧붙이자면, 그 관목 숲 오솔길은 프랭클린 도련님이 즐겨 걷는 산책길이었다. 그가 부지에 나와 있을 때, 다른 어디서도 그를 찾지 못하면, 우리는 대개 여기서 그를 찾아냈다.\n\n부끄러운 말이나, 나는 아무래도 좀 고집스러운 늙은이임을 인정해야겠다. 커프 부장이 제 속생각을 내게서 굳게 걸어 잠글수록, 나는 더욱 굳게 그 속을 들여다보려 애썼다. 관목 숲 오솔길로 접어들면서, 나는 또 다른 수로 그를 넘겨짚어 보려 했다.\n\n\"지금 사정이 이러니,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"제가 만일 나리 처지라면, 저는 도무지 갈피를 못 잡았을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"당신이 만일 내 처지라면,\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"당신은 이미 한 가지 의견을 세웠을 것이오—그리고 지금 사정이 이러하니, 당신이 앞서 제 결론에 대해 품었을 법한 어떤 의심도 말끔히 가셨을 테지. 그 결론이 무엇인지는 당분간 신경 쓸 것 없소, 베터리지 양반. 나는 당신을 여기로 데려온 것이 나를 오소리 잡듯 캐내라는 것이 아니오. 나는 얼마쯤 알아둘 것이 있어 당신을 여기로 데려온 거요. 물론 당신은 그것을 집 밖이 아니라 집 안에서도 내게 일러 줄 수 있었겠지. 허나 문과 엿듣는 귀란 것은 곧잘 한데 어울리는 재간이 있고, 우리네 직업에서는, 바깥 공기에 대한 건전한 취향을 기르는 법이라오.\"\n\n이런 사람을 누가 넘겨짚을 수 있으랴? 나는 두 손 들었다—그리고 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 되도록 참을성 있게 기다렸다.\n\n\"당신 아가씨의 속뜻에까지 파고들지는 맙시다.\" 하고 부장이 말을 이었다. \"다만 아가씨가 나를 돕기를 마다하시니 딱한 노릇이라고만 해 둡시다. 그리함으로써, 아가씨는 이 조사를 본디보다 한결 더 어렵게 만드셨으니 말이오. 우리는 이제 그 문의 얼룩이라는 수수께끼를—내 말을 믿으시오, 그것은 곧 다이아몬드의 수수께끼이기도 하니—다른 어떤 방도로 풀어 보아야 하오. 나는 하인들의 옷장을 뒤지는 대신, 그들을 만나 그들의 생각과 행동을 살피기로 정했소, 베터리지 양반. 그러나 시작하기 전에, 당신에게 한두 가지 묻고 싶은 것이 있소. 당신은 눈썰미 있는 사람이니—다이아몬드가 없어진 것이 밝혀진 뒤에, (물론 놀라고 허둥댔을 것은 감안해서) 하인들 가운데 누구에게서든 무언가 이상한 낌새를 눈치챈 것이 있소? 그들 사이에 무슨 유별난 다툼이 있었다든가? 누구 하나 여느 때 같지 않은 기색이었다든가? 이를테면, 뜻밖에 심기가 상했다든가? 아니면 뜻밖에 몸져누웠다든가?\"\n\n나는 어제 저녁 식사 때 로재나 스피어먼이 별안간 앓았던 일을 떠올릴 겨를은 있었으나—미처 아무 대답도 하기 전에—커프 부장의 눈이 별안간 관목 숲 쪽으로 돌아가는 것을 보았다. 그리고 그가 혼자 나직이 \"이런!\" 하고 말하는 소리를 들었다.\n\n\"무슨 일입니까?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"등에 신경통이 좀 도지는군.\" 하고 부장이, 마치 제삼자가 우리 말을 들어 주기를 바라기라도 하듯, 큰 소리로 말했다. \"머잖아 날씨가 바뀔 모양이오.\"\n\n몇 걸음 더 가자 우리는 집 모퉁이에 이르렀다. 오른편으로 홱 꺾어, 우리는 테라스로 들어섰고, 한복판의 계단을 따라, 아래쪽 정원으로 내려갔다. 커프 부장은 거기, 사방을 둘러볼 수 있는 툭 트인 자리에서 멈춰 섰다.\n\n\"그 젊은 사람 말인데, 로재나 스피어먼 말이오.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그 생김새로 보아, 정인이 있을 성싶지는 않소. 허나, 그 아이 자신을 위해서라도, 나는 당장 당신에게 묻지 않을 수 없구려. 그 딱한 것도, 나머지 여자들처럼, 제게 사랑하는 이를 하나 마련해 두었소?\"\n\n지금 이런 마당에, 대체 무슨 뜻으로 그가 내게 그런 물음을 던지는지 알 수가 없었다. 나는 그에게 대답하는 대신 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"우리가 지나갈 때, 로재나 스피어먼이 관목 숲에 숨어 있는 것을 보았소.\" 하고 부장이 말했다.\n\n\"'이런!' 하고 말씀하셨을 때 말입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소—내가 '이런!' 하고 말했을 때 말이오. 만일 이 일에 사랑하는 이가 얽혀 있다면, 그 숨은 것은 그리 대수롭지 않소. 만일 그렇지 않다면—이 집 사정이 이러한 마당에—그 숨은 것은 몹시 수상쩍은 정황이며, 그에 따라 손을 쓰는 것이 나의 괴로운 임무가 될 것이오.\"\n\n맙소사, 나는 그에게 무어라 말해야 했을까? 나는 그 관목 숲이 프랭클린 도련님이 즐겨 걷는 산책길임을 알고 있었다. 나는 그가 역에서 돌아오면 십중팔구 그리로 향하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 나는 페넬로피가 제 동료 하인이 그곳을 서성이는 것을 몇 번이고 붙잡았으며, 로재나의 속셈이 프랭클린 도련님의 눈길을 끄는 데 있다고 내게 늘 딱 잘라 말해 왔음을 알고 있었다. 만일 내 딸의 말이 옳다면, 부장이 그를 눈여겨보았을 때 그는 프랭클린 도련님이 돌아오기를 숨어 기다리고 있었던 것일 수도 있었다. 나는 두 갈래 곤경 사이에 놓였다—페넬로피의 그 지어낸 억측을 마치 내 것인 양 말할 것인가, 아니면 한 불행한 것을 내버려 두어 커프 부장의 의심을 산 결과를, 그것도 아주 중대한 결과를 고스란히 겪게 할 것인가. 그 아이가 순전히 가여운 마음에서—내 영혼과 내 인격을 걸고, 순전히 그 아이가 가여운 마음에서—나는 부장에게 필요한 설명을 해 주었고, 로재나가 어리석게도 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 마음을 두었다고 일러 주었다.\n\n커프 부장은 결코 소리 내어 웃는 법이 없었다. 무언가가 그를 즐겁게 하는 드문 경우에도, 그는 입아귀를 조금 말아 올릴 뿐, 그 이상은 없었다. 그가 이제 입아귀를 말아 올렸다.\n\n\"차라리, 그 아이가 어리석게도 못생긴 데다 한낱 하인 신세라고 말하는 편이 낫지 않겠소?\" 하고 그가 물었다. \"프랭클린 블레이크 도련님 같은 몸가짐과 풍채를 지닌 신사에게 반한 것이, 내가 보기엔 그 아이의 처신 중 결코 가장 어리석은 대목은 아닌 듯싶소. 아무튼, 이 일이 밝혀져서 다행이오. 일이란 밝혀지고 나면 마음이 놓이는 법이지. 그렇소, 이건 비밀로 해 두리다, 베터리지 양반. 나는 사람의 약함에 너그럽고 싶은 사람이오—하기야 우리네 직업에서 그 덕을 발휘할 기회가 그리 많지는 않소만. 당신은 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이 그 아이가 저를 마음에 둔 것을 눈치조차 못 챘다고 여기시오? 아! 그 아이가 곱상했더라면 그는 냉큼 알아챘을 거요. 못생긴 여자들은 이 세상에서 고달픈 신세지—저세상에서라도 그 값을 치러 받기를 바라 봅시다. 여기 정원이 참 좋고, 잔디도 잘 손질돼 있구려. 자갈 대신 잔디를 둘러 놓으니 꽃들이 얼마나 더 고와 보이는지 당신 눈으로 직접 보시오. 아니, 됐소. 나는 장미를 꺾지 않으리다. 그것을 줄기에서 꺾어 내는 것은 내 마음을 아프게 하니 말이오. 마치, 아시다시피, 하인 방에서 무언가 잘못된 일이 있을 때 당신 마음이 아픈 것처럼 말이오. 다이아몬드가 없어진 것이 처음 밝혀졌을 때, 하인들 가운데 누구에게서든 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 무언가를 눈치챈 것이 있소?\"\n\n여기까지는 나도 커프 부장과 제법 무난히 지내 왔다. 그러나 그가 그 마지막 물음을 슬며시 끼워 넣는 그 능청스러움이 나를 경계하게 만들었다. 쉬운 말로 하자면, 나는 그의 조사를 돕는다는 생각이 도무지 내키지 않았다. 그 조사가 그를 (풀숲에 도사린 뱀의 자격으로) 내 동료 하인들 틈으로 끌고 들어갈 때는 말이다.\n\n\"저는 아무것도 눈치채지 못했습니다.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"우리 모두가, 저까지 포함해서, 한꺼번에 넋을 잃은 것 말고는요.\"\n\n\"허,\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"내게 일러 줄 것이 그게 전부요?\"\n\n나는 (스스로 우쭐하기로는) 태연한 낯빛으로 대답했다. \"그게 전부입니다.\"\n\n커프 부장의 그 음침한 눈이 내 얼굴을 뚫어져라 들여다보았다.\n\n\"베터리지 양반,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"미안하지만 나와 악수 한번 해 주지 않겠소? 나는 당신이 유별나게 마음에 드오.\"\n\n(하필 내가 그를 속이고 있던 바로 그 순간을 골라, 나를 좋게 본다는 그런 증거를 내게 보이다니, 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 노릇이다! 나는 조금 우쭐했다—마침내 저 이름난 커프에게 한 수 앞섰다는 생각에 정말이지 조금 우쭐했다!)\n\n우리는 집으로 돌아갔다. 부장은 제게 방 하나를 따로 내어 주고, 그런 다음 하인들을 (집 안 하인들만) 지위 순서대로, 위에서부터 아래까지, 하나씩 차례로 들여보내 달라고 청했다.\n\n나는 커프 부장을 내 방으로 안내했고, 그런 다음 하인들을 큰방에 불러 모았다. 로재나 스피어먼도 그들 틈에 여느 때와 별반 다름없는 모습으로 나타났다. 그는 제 나름대로 부장이 제 나름으로 그러하듯 눈치가 빨랐고, 짐작건대 부장이 그를 발견하기 바로 전에 하인들 전반에 관해 내게 한 말을 엿들었던 것 같다. 아무튼 그는 거기, 마치 평생 관목 숲이라는 데가 있다는 소리조차 들어 본 적 없는 얼굴로 서 있었다.\n\n나는 청한 대로 그들을 하나씩 들여보냈다. 요리사가 그 재판정, 다시 말해 내 방에 맨 먼저 들어갔다. 그는 잠깐 머물렀을 뿐이다. 나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장은 기가 죽어 있긴 하지만, 완벽한 신사이십니다.\" 다음은 마님의 몸종이 뒤를 이었다. 훨씬 오래 머물렀다. 나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장이 점잖은 여자를 못 믿겠다면, 하다못해 그 속내는 혼자 담아 두시는 게 좋겠어요!\" 다음은 페넬로피가 들어갔다. 겨우 한두 순간 머물렀다. 나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장은 참 딱한 분이에요. 아버지, 저분은 젊었을 적에 사랑에 실패하셨던 게 틀림없어요.\" 첫째 하녀가 페넬로피의 뒤를 이었다. 마님의 몸종처럼, 오래 머물렀다. 나오면서 하는 보고. \"저는 한낱 하급 경찰관에게 대놓고 의심받으려고 마님 밑에 들어온 게 아니에요, 베터리지 씨!\" 다음은 로재나 스피어먼이 들어갔다. 그들 누구보다도 오래 머물렀다. 나오면서 아무 보고도 없었다—죽은 듯한 침묵과, 잿빛처럼 파리한 입술뿐이었다. 하인 새뮤얼이 로재나의 뒤를 이었다. 한두 순간 머물렀다. 나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장의 구두를 닦는 사람이 누구든 제 신세를 부끄러워해야 마땅합니다.\" 부엌데기 낸시가 맨 마지막으로 들어갔다. 한두 순간 머물렀다. 나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장은 마음이 따뜻한 분이에요. 저분은, 베터리지 씨, 고되게 일하는 가엾은 계집아이를 두고 농담 따위 하지 않으세요.\"\n\n일이 죄다 끝난 뒤, 내게 더 이를 분부가 있는지 들으러 그 재판정에 들어가 보니, 부장이 그 옛 버릇대로—창밖을 내다보며 혼자 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 휘파람으로 불고 있었다.\n\n\"뭐 좀 알아내셨습니까, 나리?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"로재나 스피어먼이 밖에 나가게 해 달라 청하면,\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"그 딱한 것을 내보내 주시오. 다만 먼저 내게 알려 주고.\"\n\n로재나와 프랭클린 도련님 이야기 따위, 차라리 입을 다물고 있을 것을! 이제 뻔했다. 그 불행한 아이는, 그것을 막으려고 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 짓을 다 했건만, 기어이 커프 부장의 의심 아래 떨어지고 만 것이다.\n\n\"설마 로재나가 다이아몬드 실종에 얽혀 있다고 여기시는 건 아니겠지요?\" 하고 나는 조심스레 말을 꺼냈다.\n\n부장의 그 우울한 입아귀가 말려 올라갔고, 그는, 정원에서 그러했던 것과 똑같이, 내 얼굴을 뚫어져라 들여다보았다.\n\n\"당신에게는 말하지 않는 편이 나을 것 같소, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은, 아시다시피, 두 번째로 또 넋을 잃을지도 모르니 말이오.\"\n\n나는 과연 내가 저 이름난 커프에게 한 수 앞섰던 것인지 새삼 의심스러워지기 시작했다! 여기서 문 두드리는 소리와 요리사가 전하는 전갈에 우리 이야기가 끊긴 것이, 나로서는 오히려 다행이었다. 로재나 스피어먼이, 여느 때와 같은 이유로, 곧 머리가 아프고 바깥바람을 좀 쐬고 싶다며, 밖에 나가게 해 달라 청했던 것이다. 부장이 눈짓하기에, 나는 그러라고 했다. \"하인들이 드나드는 문이 어느 쪽이오?\" 하고 그가 심부름꾼이 가고 난 뒤에 물었다. 나는 그에게 하인들이 드나드는 문을 일러 주었다. \"당신 방문을 잠가 두시오.\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"그리고 누가 나를 찾거든, 내가 그 안에서 마음을 가다듬고 있다고 하시오.\" 그는 입아귀를 다시 말아 올리고는, 사라졌다.\n\n그런 처지에 혼자 남겨지니, 좀이 쑤시는 호기심이 나를 부추겨 나 스스로 무언가를 알아내 보게끔 몰아갔다.\n\n커프 부장의 로재나에 대한 의심이, 내 방에서 하인들을 신문하는 동안 그가 알아낸 무언가에서 비롯되었음은 뻔했다. 그런데 (로재나 자신을 빼고) 얼마쯤 긴 시간 신문 아래 머문 하인은 오직 둘뿐이었으니, 곧 마님의 몸종과 첫째 하녀였다. 그리고 이 둘은 또한, 처음부터 그 불행한 동료 하인을 못살게 구는 데 앞장선 여자들이기도 했다. 이런 결론에 다다른 나는, 짐짓 별일 아닌 척, 하인 방에 있는 그들을 들여다보았고, 마침 차 시간이 벌어지고 있는 것을 보고, 냉큼 그 자리에 나 자신을 끼워 넣었다. (왜냐하면, 눈여겨 두시라, 차 한 방울이 여자의 혀에 하는 노릇은 기름 한 방울이 사위어 가는 등잔에 하는 노릇과 같으니 말이다.)\n\n동맹군으로서의 찻주전자에 건 내 기대는 헛되지 않았다. 반 시간도 채 안 되어 나는 부장 자신만큼이나 많은 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n마님의 몸종과 하녀는, 알고 보니, 둘 다 전날 로재나가 앓았다는 것을 믿지 않았다. 이 두 악마는—용서를 구하나, 심술궂은 두 여자를 달리 어찌 이르겠는가?—목요일 오후 동안 틈틈이 위층으로 살금살금 올라가, 로재나의 문을 밀어 보고는 잠겨 있음을 알아냈고, 두드려도 대답이 없었으며, 귀 기울여도 안에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다. 그 아이가 차를 마시러 내려왔다가, 여전히 몸이 안 좋아 다시 잠자리에 들라고 올려 보내진 뒤, 앞서 말한 그 두 악마는 그 문을 다시 한번 밀어 보고 잠겨 있음을 알아냈고, 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보고는 그것이 틀어막혀 있음을 알아냈으며, 자정에 문 아래로 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았고, 새벽 네 시에는 불이 타닥거리는 소리를 (유월에 하인 침실에 불이라니!) 들었다. 이 모든 것을 그들은 커프 부장에게 일러바쳤는데, 부장은 저를 깨우쳐 주려는 그들의 안달에 대한 보답으로, 시큰둥하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 그들을 훑어보며, 이쪽도 저쪽도 도무지 믿지 않는다는 것을 그들에게 뻔히 내보였다. 그리하여, 이 두 여자가 신문을 마치고 나오며 내놓은 그에 대한 그 언짢은 보고가 나온 것이다. 그리하여, 또한 (찻주전자의 영향력은 셈에 넣지 않더라도) 그들에게 부장이 보인 그 무례한 처사를 두고 실컷 혀를 놀려 대는 그 신바람도 나온 것이다.\n\n저 위대한 커프의 에두르는 수법을 얼마쯤 겪어 본 데다, 로재나가 산책하러 나갈 때 그를 남몰래 뒤밟을 작정임이 뚜렷한 그를 마지막으로 본 터라, 그가 몸종과 하녀에게 그들이 얼마나 결정적으로 저를 도왔는지 알리는 것을 마땅치 않게 여겼음이 내게는 분명해 보였다. 만일 그가 그들의 증언을 미덥게 여기는 척했다면, 그들은 그것에 우쭐하여 로재나 스피어먼이 경계하게끔 만들 무슨 말이나 짓을 하고도 남을, 딱 그런 부류의 여자들이었으니 말이다.\n\n나는 그 화창한 여름 오후에 밖으로 나갔다. 그 딱한 아이가 몹시 가엾고, 일이 흘러가는 판국에 마음이 몹시 편치 않은 채로였다. 얼마쯤 뒤 관목 숲 쪽으로 발길이 흘러갔는데, 거기서 나는 프랭클린 도련님을 만났다. 역에서 제 사촌을 배웅하고 돌아온 뒤, 그는 마님과 함께 오래도록 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 마님은 레이철 아가씨가 도무지 까닭 모를 이유로 제 옷장을 살펴보게 하기를 거절한 일을 그에게 이르셨고, 그리하여 그를 우리 아가씨에 대해 어찌나 풀 죽게 만드셨던지 그는 그 이야기를 꺼내는 것조차 꺼리는 듯했다. 그 집안 특유의 성미가 그날 저녁 그의 얼굴에 처음으로—내가 그를 겪어 온 이래 처음으로—드러났다.\n\n\"저런, 베터리지,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"지금 우리 모두가 그 속에서 살아가고 있는 이 수수께끼와 의심의 공기가, 자네한테는 어떤가? 내가 월장석을 안고 여기 처음 왔던 그 아침을 기억하나? 맙소사, 우리가 그것을 그 유사(流沙) 속에 던져 버렸더라면 좋았을 것을!\"\n\n그런 식으로 터뜨리고 나서, 그는 마음을 가라앉힐 때까지 다시 입을 열지 않았다. 우리는 나란히, 한두 순간 말없이 걸었고, 그런 다음 그는 내게 커프 부장이 어찌 되었느냐고 물었다. 프랭클린 도련님을 부장이 내 방에서 마음을 가다듬고 있다는 핑계로 따돌릴 수는 없는 노릇이었다. 나는 그에게 일어난 일을 있는 그대로 일러 주었는데, 특히 마님의 몸종과 하녀가 로재나 스피어먼에 대해 한 말을 짚어 이야기했다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님의 명석한 머리는, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 부장의 의심이 향한 방향을 알아보았다.\n\n\"자네, 오늘 아침에 내게 말하지 않았나,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"우리가 로재나를 제 방에서 앓고 있으려니 여기던 어제, 그를 프리징홀로 가는 오솔길에서 만났노라고 어느 장사꾼이 딱 잘라 말하더라고 말이야?\"\n\n\"예, 그렇습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"내 이모님의 몸종과 그 다른 여자가 사실을 말한 것이라면, 그 장사꾼이 그를 만난 것이 틀림없다고 믿어도 좋아. 그 아이가 앓았던 것은 우리를 속이려는 눈속임이었어. 그는 무언가 켕기는 이유가 있어 몰래 시내로 갔던 거야. 그 페인트 얼룩진 옷은 그의 옷이고, 새벽 네 시에 그 방에서 타닥거리는 소리가 들렸다는 그 불은 그것을 태워 없애려고 지핀 불이야. 로재나 스피어먼이 그 다이아몬드를 훔친 거야. 내 곧장 들어가서, 이모님께 일이 이렇게 돌아갔다고 아뢰야겠어.\"\n\n\"괜찮으시다면, 아직은 하지 마시지요, 나리.\" 하고 우리 등 뒤에서 우울한 목소리가 말했다.\n\n우리 둘 다 홱 돌아섰고, 커프 부장과 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다.\n\n\"어째서 아직은 안 된다는 거요?\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 물었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면, 나리, 나리가 마님께 아뢰면, 마님은 버린더 아가씨께 이르실 테니까요.\"\n\n\"이르신다 칩시다. 그러면 어떻단 말이오?\" 프랭클린 도련님은, 마치 부장이 자기를 죽도록 모욕하기라도 한 듯, 별안간 발끈 성을 내어 그 말을 내뱉었다.\n\n\"나리, 그것이 현명한 일이라고 여기십니까,\" 하고 커프 부장이 나직이 말했다. \"지금 이런 마당에—내게 그런 물음을 던지시는 것이 말입니다?\"\n\n두 사람 사이에 한순간 침묵이 흘렀다. 프랭클린 도련님이 부장에게 바짝 다가섰다. 두 사람은 서로의 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 보았다. 프랭클린 도련님이, 목소리를 높였을 때만큼이나 별안간 그것을 낮추며, 먼저 입을 열었다.\n\n\"짐작건대 당신도 아시겠지, 커프 씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"지금 당신이 아슬아슬한 자리를 밟고 있다는 것을 말이오?\"\n\n\"내가 아슬아슬한 자리를 밟고 있음을 깨닫는 것이, 이번이 처음은 아니오—몇백 번은 족히 되지.\" 하고 상대가, 여전히 꿈쩍도 않고 대답했다.\n\n\"그러니까 당신은, 무슨 일이 있었는지 이모님께 이르는 것을 내게 금하는 거요?\"\n\n\"괜찮으시다면 이렇게 알아 두시지요, 나리. 만일 나리가, 내가 허락하기 전에, 버린더 마님께든 그 누구에게든, 무슨 일이 있었는지 이른다면, 나는 이 사건을 손 떼겠습니다.\"\n\n그것으로 판가름이 났다. 프랭클린 도련님으로서는 따르는 수밖에 도리가 없었다. 그는 성이 나서 홱 돌아서더니—우리를 두고 가 버렸다.\n\n나는 온몸을 떨며 거기 서서 그들의 말을 듣고 있었다—누구를 의심해야 할지, 다음엔 무엇을 생각해야 할지도 모른 채로. 그러나 내 혼란의 한복판에서도, 두 가지는 내게 뚜렷했다. 첫째, 우리 아가씨가, 도무지 알 수 없는 어떤 식으로, 그들 사이에 오간 그 날 선 말다툼의 밑바닥에 있다는 것. 둘째, 그들이, 앞서 어느 쪽도 설명이랄 것을 한마디도 주고받은 적 없이, 서로를 속속들이 알아듣고 있다는 것.\n\n\"베터리지 양반,\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"당신은 내가 없는 동안 아주 어리석은 짓을 저질렀소. 당신 딴에 조그만 탐정 노릇을 좀 한 거요. 앞으로는, 부디, 당신 탐정 노릇을 나와 함께해 주는 아량을 베풀어 주시겠소.\"\n\n그는 내 팔을 잡고, 제가 왔던 길을 따라 나를 데리고 걸었다. 그의 나무람을 들어 마땅했을지도 모른다—그렇다 해도, 나는 로재나 스피어먼에게 덫을 놓는 일에 그를 도울 생각은 없었다. 도둑이든 아니든, 법에 걸리든 안 걸리든, 나는 아랑곳없다—나는 그가 가여웠다.\n\n\"제게 무얼 바라시는 겁니까?\" 하고 나는 그를 뿌리치고 우뚝 멈춰 서며 물었다.\n\n\"이 근방 고장에 대해 얼마쯤 알고 싶을 뿐이오.\" 하고 부장이 말했다.\n\n커프 부장의 지리 지식을 넓혀 주는 것을 굳이 마다할 수도 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n\"저쪽 방향으로, 이 집에서 바닷가로 이어지는 길이 어디 있소?\" 하고 부장이 물었다. 그는 말하면서, 그 흔들리는 모래로 이어지는 전나무 숲을 가리켰다.\n\n\"예,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"길이 하나 있습니다.\"\n\n\"내게 보여 주시오.\"\n\n여름 저녁의 어스름 속에서, 나란히, 커프 부장과 나는 그 흔들리는 모래를 향해 길을 나섰다.\n\n# CHAPTER XV\n\n부장은 저만의 생각에 잠긴 채, 우리가 그 유사(流沙)로 이어지는 전나무 숲에 들어설 때까지 잠자코 있었다. 거기서 그는, 마음을 굳힌 사람처럼 정신을 차리더니, 다시 내게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"베터리지 양반,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 내 배에서 노 한 자루를 잡아 주는 영광을 베풀었고, 또 내 생각에 오늘 저녁이 다 가기 전에 당신이 내게 얼마쯤 도움이 될 성싶으니, 우리가 더 이상 서로를 헷갈리게 하는 것은 아무 소용이 없다고 보오. 그러니 내 쪽에서는 당신에게 솔직히 털어놓는 본을 보이려 하오. 당신은 로재나 스피어먼에게 해가 될 정보는 내게 한 조각도 주지 않기로 마음을 굳혔소. 그 아이가 당신에게는 착한 아이였고, 또 당신이 진심으로 그를 가여워하니 말이오. 그런 인정 어린 헤아림은 당신에게 이만저만한 명예가 아니오만, 이 경우에는 공교롭게도 그것이 헛되이 내던져진 인정 어린 헤아림이 되고 말았소. 로재나 스피어먼이 곤경에 빠질 위험은 조금도 없소—그렇소, 내가 그 아이를, 당신 코가 얼굴에 붙어 있는 것만큼이나 뻔한 증거로, 다이아몬드 실종에 연루된 자로 못 박는다 해도 말이오!\"\n\n\"마님께서 그 아이를 고발하지 않으시리라는 말씀입니까?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"마님께서 고발하실 수가 없다는 말이오.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"로재나 스피어먼은 그저 다른 사람 손에 쥐인 도구일 뿐이고, 로재나 스피어먼은 그 다른 사람을 위해 화를 면하게 될 것이오.\"\n\n그는 진심인 사람처럼 말했다—그것은 부인할 수 없었다. 그래도, 내 마음속에서는 무언가가 그를 향해 께름칙하게 꿈틀거리는 것이 느껴졌다. \"그 다른 사람에게 이름을 붙여 주실 수는 없습니까?\" 하고 내가 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 못 붙이겠소, 베터리지 양반?\"\n\n\"못 붙입니다.\"\n\n커프 부장은 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 우울한 흥미가 어린 눈길로 나를 찬찬히 훑어보았다.\n\n\"사람의 약함에 너그럽게 구는 것은 내게는 늘 즐거운 일이오.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"지금 이 순간, 나는 당신에게 유별나게 너그러운 마음이 드는구려, 베터리지 양반. 그리고 당신은, 그와 똑같은 갸륵한 동기에서, 로재나 스피어먼에게 유별나게 너그러운 마음이 들지, 그렇지 않소? 혹 그 아이가 요즘 아마포 옷 한 벌을 새로 장만한 적이 있는지 아시오?\"\n\n이 뜬금없는 물음을 슬며시 끼워 넣은 그의 속뜻이 무엇인지, 나는 도무지 짐작할 길이 없었다. 사실대로 밝힌들 로재나에게 해가 될 리 없다고 보아, 나는 그 아이가 아마포 옷을 제법 변변찮게 갖춘 채 우리에게 왔으며, 마님이 그 착실한 품행에 대한 보답으로 (나는 그 착실한 품행에 힘을 주어 말했다) 채 보름도 안 되어 새 옷 한 벌을 내려 주셨다고 대답했다.\n\n\"참으로 서글픈 세상이오.\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"사람의 목숨이란, 베터리지 양반, 일종의 과녁 같은 것이지—불운이 늘 그것을 향해 쏘아 대고, 늘 명중시키니 말이오. 그 새 옷만 아니었다면, 우리는 로재나의 물건 틈에서 새 잠옷이나 속치마를 발견하고, 그런 식으로 그 아이를 못 박았을 거요. 내 말을 못 따라오는 건 아니겠지? 당신은 손수 하인들을 신문했으니, 그중 둘이 로재나의 문밖에서 무엇을 알아냈는지 알고 있소. 그러니 그 아이가 몸져누운 뒤 어제 무슨 짓을 했는지도 분명 알겠지? 짐작이 안 가시오? 아이고 저런, 저기 나무들 끝에 비치는 저 한 줄기 빛만큼이나 뻔한 일이오. 목요일 아침 열한 시에, (사람의 약함이 뭉쳐진 덩어리 같은) 시그레이브 경정이 여자 하인들 모두에게 그 문의 얼룩을 가리켜 보였소. 로재나는 제 물건을 의심할 저만의 이유가 있었고, 제 방으로 갈 첫 기회를 붙잡아, 제 잠옷이나 속치마나 뭐 그런 것에서 페인트 얼룩을 발견하고는, 앓는 시늉을 하며 슬며시 시내로 빠져나가, 새 속치마나 잠옷을 지을 감을 구해, 목요일 밤 제 방에서 혼자 그것을 짓고, 불을 지폈소 (그것을 태우려는 게 아니오. 제 동료 하인 둘이 문밖에서 엿보고 있었으니, 타는 냄새를 피우고 없애야 할 잿더미를 잔뜩 남기는 어리석은 짓은 안 할 만큼 그 아이는 영리했지)—불을 지폈다는 것은, 짜낸 대체 옷을 말리고 다림질하려던 것이고, 그 얼룩진 옷은 (아마도 제 몸에) 숨겨 둔 채, 지금 이 순간 우리 앞 저 외진 바닷가 어딘가 편한 자리에서 그것을 없애느라 바쁜 것이오. 나는 오늘 저녁 그 아이를 당신네 어촌까지, 그리고 어느 한 오두막까지 뒤밟았는데, 그 오두막은 우리가 돌아가기 전에 어쩌면 들러야 할지도 모르오. 그 아이는 그 오두막에 얼마간 머물렀다가, (내가 믿기로는) 무언가를 제 망토 아래 감춘 채 나왔소. 망토란 (여자의 등에 걸치면) 자비의 상징이지—숱한 죄를 덮어 주니까 말이오. 나는 그 아이가 오두막을 나선 뒤 해안을 따라 북쪽으로 떠나는 것을 보았소. 여기 당신네 바닷가는 훌륭한 해양 경관의 본보기로 쳐 주는 곳이오, 베터리지 양반?\"\n\n나는 될 수 있는 대로 짤막하게 \"예.\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"취향이란 제각각이지.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"내 관점에서 보자면, 이보다 덜 감탄스러운 해양 경관은 여태 본 적이 없소. 만일 당신이 이런 바닷가를 따라 어떤 사람을 뒤밟고 있는데, 그 사람이 마침 뒤를 돌아본다면, 당신을 숨겨 줄 가림막이라곤 어디에도 한 조각 없으니 말이오. 나는 로재나를 혐의로 붙잡아 두느냐, 아니면 당분간 그 아이가 제 잔꾀를 제 손에 쥔 채로 내버려 두느냐, 그 둘 중 하나를 골라야 했소. 당신을 성가시게 하지 않으려고 이유는 접어 두겠소만, 나는 우리 사이에서 이름을 밝히지 않을 어떤 사람에게 당장 오늘 밤 경종을 울리느니, 차라리 어떤 희생이라도 치르는 편이 낫겠다고 마음먹었소. 나는 당신에게 다른 길로 나를 바닷가 북쪽 끝까지 데려가 달라고 청하러 집으로 돌아왔던 거요. 모래란—사람의 발자국을 찍어 내는 점에서는—내가 아는 가장 뛰어난 형사 나리 중 하나요. 이런 식으로 그 아이를 에둘러 덮쳐도 로재나 스피어먼과 마주치지 못한다면, 빛만 넉넉히 남아 있어 준다면, 그 모래가 그 아이가 무슨 짓을 했는지 우리에게 일러 줄지도 모르오. 자, 여기가 그 모래요. 내 이런 제안을 하는 것을 너그러이 봐 주신다면—당신은 입을 다물고, 내가 먼저 가게 해 주는 것이 어떻겠소?\"\n\n만일 의원(醫院)에 탐정 열병이라는 것이 알려져 있다면, 그 병이 이제 이 미천한 종을 단단히 사로잡은 셈이었다. 커프 부장은 모래 언덕들 사이로 나아가, 바닷가로 내려갔다. 나는 (가슴이 목구멍까지 치받쳐 오른 채) 그를 뒤따랐고, 조금 떨어진 자리에서 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질지 기다렸다.\n\n알고 보니, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 런던에서 우리 집에 당도하여 우리 앞에 별안간 나타났을 때, 로재나 스피어먼과 내가 함께 이야기를 나누던 바로 그 자리 가까이에 서 있었다. 내 눈은 부장을 지켜보고 있었으나, 내 마음은 나도 모르게 그 지난번에 로재나와 나 사이에 오갔던 일로 흘러갔다. 단언컨대 나는 그 딱한 것이 다시 제 손을 내 손에 밀어 넣어, 제게 다정하게 말해 준 것을 고마워하며 살짝 감사의 뜻으로 쥐던 것을 거의 느끼는 듯했다. 단언컨대 나는 그 아이의 목소리가, 밖에 나갈 때마다 흔들리는 모래가 제 뜻과는 달리 저를 끌어당기는 것 같다고 다시 내게 말하는 것을 거의 듣는 듯했다—프랭클린 도련님이 언덕들 사이에서 우리 쪽으로 성큼 나오는 것을 처음 보았을 때 환해졌던 것처럼, 그 얼굴이 다시 환해지는 것을 거의 보는 듯했다. 이런 일들을 떠올리자 내 마음은 갈수록 가라앉았다—그리고 정신을 차리려 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 그 쓸쓸한 조그만 만(灣)의 풍경은 나를 한층 더 불안하게 만들 뿐이었다.\n\n저녁 빛의 마지막 자락이 스러지고 있었다. 그리고 그 황량한 곳 전체 위로 고요하고 무시무시한 정적이 드리워 있었다. 만 저편 큰 모래톱에 부딪는 넓은 바다의 너울은, 아무 소리도 내지 않는 너울이었다. 안쪽 바다는 그것을 흔들 바람 한 점 없이, 아득하고 흐릿하게 누워 있었다. 역겨운 진창 덩어리들이, 누르스름한 흰빛으로, 죽은 듯한 물 표면에 떠 있었다. 바다로 남북으로 삐져나온 두 큰 바위 곶 위에, 마지막 남은 빛이 여태 걸려 있는 어떤 자리들에서는, 물때와 점액이 어렴풋이 번들거렸다. 이제 물때가 바뀔 무렵이었다. 그리하여 내가 거기 서서 기다리는 바로 그 순간에도, 유사의 넓적한 갈색 얼굴이 잔물결을 일으키며 떨리기 시작했다—그 소름 끼치는 곳 전체에서 유일하게 움직이는 것이었다.\n\n나는 그 모래의 떨림이 부장의 눈에 들자 그가 흠칫하는 것을 보았다. 일 분쯤 그것을 바라보다가, 그는 돌아서서 내게로 왔다.\n\n\"믿을 수 없는 곳이오, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 아무리 둘러봐도, 바닷가 어디에도 로재나 스피어먼의 흔적이라곤 없구려.\"\n\n그는 나를 바닷가 더 낮은 데로 데려갔고, 나는 그의 발자국과 내 발자국만이 모래에 찍혀 있는 것을 내 눈으로 직접 보았다.\n\n\"지금 우리가 선 자리에서 보면, 그 어촌이 어느 방향이오?\" 하고 커프 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"코브스 홀은,\" 하고 나는 (그것이 그곳의 이름이었으므로) 대답했다. \"될 수 있는 대로 가까이 잡아, 정남쪽에 있습니다.\"\n\n\"나는 오늘 저녁 그 아이가 코브스 홀에서부터 바닷가를 따라 북쪽으로 걸어가는 것을 보았소.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"그러니, 그 아이는 이곳을 향해 걸어오고 있었던 게 틀림없소. 코브스 홀이 저기 저 곶 반대편에 있소? 그리고 우리가—지금은 썰물이니—바닷가를 따라 거기 갈 수 있소?\"\n\n나는 그 두 물음에 다 \"예.\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"내 이런 제안을 하는 것을 너그러이 봐 주신다면, 걸음을 서둘러 봅시다.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"날이 어두워지기 전에, 그 아이가 바닷가를 벗어난 자리를 찾고 싶소.\"\n\n우리가, 대충 잡아, 코브스 홀 쪽으로 이백 야드쯤 걸었을 때, 커프 부장이 별안간 바닷가에 무릎을 꿇었는데, 겉보기로는 마치 느닷없이 기도를 올리고 싶은 광기에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯했다.\n\n\"여기 당신네 해양 경관도 그리 나쁘게만 볼 것은 아니로군, 결국은 말이오.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"여기 여자의 발자국이 있소, 베터리지 양반! 도저히 거스를 수 없는 반대 증거를 찾기 전까지는, 이것을 로재나의 발자국이라 부릅시다. 아주 어지럽혀진 발자국이라는 걸 부디 눈여겨보시오—일부러 어지럽힌 것이라고 나는 보오. 아, 딱한 것, 그 아이는 모래의 그 형사다운 재간을 나만큼이나 잘 아는군! 허나 그 자국을 말끔히 밟아 지우기에는 너무 서둘렀던 게 아닐까? 나는 그렇게 보오. 여기 코브스 홀에서 멀어지는 발자국이 하나 있고, 여기 다시 그리로 돌아가는 발자국이 또 하나 있소. 저것이 곧장 물가를 가리키는 그 아이 신발 앞코가 아니오? 그리고 바닷가 더 아래, 역시 물가 바로 곁에, 발뒤꿈치 자국 둘이 내 눈에 보이지 않소? 당신 심기를 상하게 하고 싶지는 않소만, 아무래도 로재나는 잔꾀가 있는 아이 같소. 마치 그 아이가, 저를 뒤밟을 아무 자국도 모래에 남기지 않고, 당신과 내가 방금 다녀온 그 자리에 이르기로 작정했던 것처럼 보이오. 그 아이가 이 지점에서부터 물속을 걸어 우리 뒤편 저 바위 턱에 이르렀다가, 같은 길로 되돌아와, 저 발뒤꿈치 자국 둘이 아직 남아 있는 그 자리에서 다시 바닷가로 올라섰다고—그렇게 봐도 되겠소? 그렇소, 그렇게 봅시다. 그 아이가 오두막을 나설 때 제 망토 아래 무언가를 지니고 있었다는 내 짐작과 아귀가 맞는 것 같소. 아니! 없애 버릴 무언가는 아니오—만일 그런 것이라면, 제 걸음이 끝난 자리를 내가 뒤밟지 못하게 하려고 이 온갖 조심을 다 부릴 까닭이 어디 있었겠소? 숨길 무언가라는 것이, 내 생각엔, 그 둘 중 더 나은 짐작이오. 어쩌면, 우리가 그 오두막까지 가 보면, 그 무언가가 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있을지도 모르겠소?\"\n\n이 제안에, 내 탐정 열병이 별안간 식어 버렸다. \"저는 필요 없으시잖습니까.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"제가 무슨 도움이 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"당신을 오래 알수록, 베터리지 양반,\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"나는 더 많은 미덕을 발견하는구려. 겸손이라니—아이고 저런, 이 세상에 겸손이란 게 얼마나 드문 것인데! 그리고 당신은 그 드문 것을 얼마나 많이 지녔는지! 만일 내가 혼자 그 오두막에 간다면, 사람들은 내가 던지는 첫 물음에 그만 입을 다물어 버릴 거요. 만일 내가 당신과 함께 간다면, 나는 마땅히 존경받는 이웃의 소개를 받고 가는 셈이니, 술술 이야기가 흘러나오는 것이 그 당연한 결과지. 내게는 그렇게 보이오. 당신에게는 어떻게 보이오?\"\n\n내가 바라던 만큼 재치 있는 대답이 얼른 준비되어 있지 않아, 나는 그가 가려는 오두막이 어느 것인지 물어 시간을 벌어 보려 했다.\n\n부장이 그곳을 묘사하자, 나는 그것이 욜랜드라는 어부가 제 아내와 다 자란 두 아이, 곧 아들 하나 딸 하나와 함께 사는 오두막임을 알아보았다. 앞을 돌이켜 보시면, 내가 로재나 스피어먼을 처음 여러분께 소개할 때, 그가 이따금 흔들리는 모래로 가는 산책길을 바꾸어 코브스 홀에 사는 제 벗 몇을 찾아가곤 했다고 묘사한 것을 아실 것이다. 그 벗들이 바로 욜랜드네였다—점잖고 훌륭한 사람들로, 그 이웃의 자랑거리였다. 로재나가 그들과 알게 된 것은 그 딸을 통해서였는데, 그 딸은 발이 기형이라, 우리 고장에서는 절름발이 루시라는 이름으로 통했다. 짐작건대 그 두 불구의 처녀는 서로에게 일종의 동병상련을 느꼈던 모양이다. 아무튼 욜랜드네와 로재나는, 어쩌다 만날 몇 안 되는 기회에, 늘 화기애애하고 다정하게 어울리는 듯 보였다. 커프 부장이 그 아이를 그들의 오두막까지 뒤밟았다는 사실은, 그의 조사를 돕는다는 문제를 사뭇 새로운 빛 아래 놓았다. 로재나는 그저 늘 다니던 곳에 갔을 뿐이었다. 그리고 그가 그 어부와 그 식구들과 함께 있었음을 밝히는 것은, 적어도 거기까지는 그가 아무 죄 없이 지냈음을 증명하는 것이나 다름없었다. 그러니 내가 커프 부장의 논리에 설득당하기로 한다면, 그것은 그 아이에게 해를 끼치는 것이 아니라 오히려 도움을 주는 일이 될 터였다. 그리하여 나는 그 논리에 설득당했노라고 표명했다.\n\n우리는 코브스 홀로 나아갔고, 빛이 남아 있는 동안은 모래 위의 발자국을 보며 갔다.\n\n오두막에 다다르니, 어부와 그 아들은 배를 타고 나가 있었고, 늘 약하고 지쳐 있는 절름발이 루시는 위층 제 침대에서 쉬고 있었다. 마음씨 좋은 욜랜드 부인이 제 부엌에서 우리를 혼자 맞았다. 커프 부장이 런던에서 이름난 인물이라는 말을 듣자, 부인은 네덜란드 진 한 병과 깨끗한 담뱃대 두 개를 탁자에 탁 내려놓고는, 아무리 봐도 성이 안 찬다는 듯 그를 뚫어져라 쳐다보았다.\n\n나는 부장이 로재나 스피어먼이라는 화제로 어떻게 길을 터 갈지 들으려고 한구석에 잠자코 앉아 있었다. 그가 일에 착수하는 그 여느 에두르는 방식이, 이번만은, 그 어느 때보다도 더 에둘렀다. 그가 그것을 어떻게 해냈는지는 그때도 내가 이를 수 없는 노릇이었고, 지금도 이를 수 없는 노릇이다. 허나 이것만은 분명하다. 그는 왕실 이야기와, 원시감리교도와, 생선값으로 말문을 열었다. 그리고 거기서 (그 음침하고 땅속 같은 방식으로) 월장석의 실종과, 우리 집 첫째 하녀의 심술과, 여자 하인들이 로재나 스피어먼에게 보인 대체로 모진 처사로 옮겨 갔다. 이런 식으로 제 화제에 이른 그는, 자기가 저 잃어버린 다이아몬드에 관해 조사하는 것이, 한편으로는 그것을 찾으려는 뜻에서요, 다른 한편으로는 그 집안에서 로재나를 원수처럼 여기는 자들의 부당한 의심에서 그를 벗겨 주려는 뜻에서라고 스스로를 소개했다. 우리가 그 부엌에 들어선 지 십오 분쯤 되었을 무렵, 마음씨 좋은 욜랜드 부인은 자기가 로재나의 가장 좋은 벗과 이야기하고 있다고 굳게 믿게 되었고, 커프 부장에게 그 네덜란드 병으로 뱃속을 달래고 기운을 되살리라고 자꾸 권하고 있었다.\n\n부장이 욜랜드 부인에게 아무 소득도 없이 헛기운을 쓰고 있다고 굳게 믿은 나는, 왕년에 무대극 한 편을 즐기며 앉아 있었던 것과 사뭇 비슷하게, 두 사람의 이야기를 즐기며 앉아 있었다. 저 위대한 커프는 놀라운 참을성을 보였다. 이리 찔러 보고 저리 찔러 보며 처량하게 운을 시험했고, 이를테면 과녁을 맞힐 요행을 바라며 아무렇게나 총알을 연방 쏘아 댔다. 죄다 로재나에게 이로운 것뿐, 로재나에게 해로운 것이라곤 하나도 없었다—아무리 애를 써도 결국은 그렇게 끝났다. 욜랜드 부인은 쉴 새 없이 나불거리며, 그에게 더할 나위 없는 믿음을 두었다. 그의 마지막 시도는, 우리가 시계를 보고, 작별을 고하려고 자리에서 일어섰을 때 이루어졌다.\n\n\"이제 그만 안녕히 주무시라 인사드리겠소, 부인.\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"그리고 헤어지면서 딱 한마디만 하자면, 로재나 스피어먼에게는 저, 그대의 충직한 종이라는 진심 어린 후원자가 하나 있다는 거요. 허나, 아이고 저런! 그 아이는 지금 있는 그 자리에서는 결코 잘 풀리지 못할 거요. 그러니 그 아이에게 주는 내 조언은—거길 떠나라, 이거요.\"\n\n\"어머 저런, 세상에나! 그 애가 바로 떠나려는 참인걸요!\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 외친다. (눈여겨 두시라—나는 욜랜드 부인의 말을 요크셔 말에서 표준 영어로 옮겨 전하는 것이다. 그 온갖 재주를 다 갖춘 커프조차 내가 거들어 주기 전까지는 이따금 그의 말을 알아듣지 못해 쩔쩔맸다고 내가 이르면, 만일 내가 그의 말을 그 본디 말투 그대로 전한다면 여러분의 정신 상태가 어찌 될지에 대해 여러분 나름의 결론을 내리실 것이다.)\n\n로재나 스피어먼이 우리를 떠나려 한다니! 나는 그 말에 귀가 쫑긋해졌다. 아무리 좋게 봐도, 그 아이가 애당초 마님께든 내게든 아무런 언질조차 주지 않았다는 것이 이상했다. 커프 부장의 그 마지막 아무렇게나 쏜 총알이 어쩌면 과녁을 맞힌 게 아닐까 하는 일말의 의심이 내 마음속에 떠올랐다. 나는 이 일에서 내가 맡은 몫이 과연 내가 여기던 만큼 그리 해롭지 않은 것이었는지 새삼 의심하기 시작했다. 정직한 여자를 거짓의 그물로 칭칭 둘러 헷갈리게 하는 것이야 죄다 부장의 일 처리 방식일 수 있겠으나, 착실한 신교도로서 나는, 거짓의 아비는 마귀이며—또 재앙과 마귀는 결코 멀리 떨어져 있는 법이 없음을 마땅히 기억했어야 했다. 공기 중에서 재앙의 냄새가 나기 시작하는 것을 느낀 나는, 커프 부장을 밖으로 데리고 나가려 해 보았다. 그는 곧바로 도로 앉더니, 그 네덜란드 병으로 위안 한 모금을 청했다. 욜랜드 부인은 그의 맞은편에 앉아, 그에게 한 잔을 따라 주었다. 나는 몹시 불편한 마음으로 문께로 갔고, 이제 그만 안녕히 주무시라 인사드려야겠다고 말했다—그러면서도 정작 나가지는 못했다.\n\n\"그럼 그 아이가 떠날 작정이라 이거요?\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"떠나면 무얼 하려고? 딱하기도 하지, 딱하기도 해! 그 가여운 것은 이 세상에 벗이라곤, 당신과 나 말고는 하나도 없는데.\"\n\n\"아이고, 그렇지가 않다니까요!\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다. \"말씀드렸다시피, 그 애가 오늘 저녁 여기 왔었어요. 그리고 우리 딸 루시랑 저랑 앉아서 얘기 좀 나누다가, 혼자 위층으로, 루시 방에 좀 올라가게 해 달라더군요. 우리 집에서 펜이랑 잉크가 있는 방은 거기뿐이거든요. '벗한테 편지를 한 장 쓰고 싶은데,' 그 애가 그러더라고요, '저 위 집에선 하인들이 엿보고 훔쳐보고 하니 도무지 쓸 수가 없어요.' 편지가 누구한테 쓴 건지는 말씀 못 드리겠어요. 그걸 붙들고 위층에 머문 시간으로 미루어 보건대, 엄청나게 긴 편지였던 게 틀림없어요. 내려왔을 때 제가 우표 한 장을 건네줬어요. 손에 편지는 안 들려 있었고, 우표도 안 받더군요. 그 딱한 것이, (아시다시피) 제 일이랑 제 하는 짓에 대해선 좀 입이 무거운 애라서요. 하지만 어디엔가 벗이 하나 있는 건 분명해요, 장담해요. 그리고 그 벗한테, 틀림없이, 그 애가 갈 거예요.\"\n\n\"곧 말이오?\" 하고 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"될 수 있는 대로 빨리요.\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다.\n\n여기서 나는 다시 문께에서 걸어 들어갔다. 마님 살림의 우두머리로서, 우리 하인 하나가 떠나느니 안 떠나느니 하는 이런 실없는 이야기가 내가 있는 자리에서 더 이상 오가는 것을, 아무 말 없이 그냥 둘 수는 없는 노릇이었다.\n\n\"당신이 로재나 스피어먼에 대해 잘못 아신 게 틀림없습니다.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"만일 그 아이가 지금 있는 자리를 떠나려 했다면, 애당초 그 말을 내게 했을 겁니다.\"\n\n\"잘못 알아요?\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 외친다. \"아니, 바로 한 시간 전에 그 애가 여행에 필요한 물건 몇 가지를 샀는걸요—다른 사람도 아닌 저한테서, 베터리지 씨, 바로 이 방에서요. 그러고 보니 생각나네요,\" 하고 이 성가신 여자가, 별안간 제 주머니를 뒤지기 시작하며 말한다. \"로재나랑 그 애 돈에 대해 마음에 걸려 하던 게 하나 있었어요. 두 분 중 누구든 집에 돌아가서 그 애를 볼 것 같으세요?\"\n\n\"내가 그 딱한 것에게 더없이 기꺼이 전갈을 전해 주리다.\" 하고 커프 부장이, 내가 한마디 비집고 넣기도 전에 대답했다.\n\n욜랜드 부인은 제 주머니에서 실링과 육펜스 몇 닢을 꺼내, 더없이 꼼꼼하고 사람 애태우는 조심스러움으로 제 손바닥 위에 세어 놓았다. 부인은 그 돈을 부장에게 내밀었는데, 그러는 내내 그것을 죄다 내놓기가 여간 아까운 낯이 아니었다.\n\n\"부디 이걸 로재나한테, 내 사랑과 안부와 함께 돌려주시겠어요?\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다. \"그 애가 오늘 저녁 마음에 든 물건 한두 가지 값을 기어이 치르겠다고 우겨서요—돈이야 우리 집엔 반갑기 그지없죠, 그건 부정 안 해요. 그래도, 그 딱한 것의 쥐꼬리만 한 저금을 받는 게 영 마음이 편치가 않아요. 그리고 사실대로 말씀드리면, 우리 양반이 내일 아침 일 마치고 돌아와서, 내가 로재나 스피어먼의 돈을 받았다는 얘길 들으면 좋아하지 않을 것 같아요. 그 애가 저한테서 산 물건은—선물로—얼마든지 가지라고 부디 전해 주세요. 그리고 그 돈을 탁자에 두고 가진 마세요,\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이, 마치 그것이 제 손가락을 데기라도 하는 듯, 별안간 그것을 부장 앞에 내려놓으며 말한다. \"제발요, 착한 양반! 세상살이는 팍팍하고 사람 마음은 여리니까요. 안 그럼 저도 모르게 다시 그걸 주머니에 도로 넣고 싶어질지도 모르잖아요.\"\n\n\"자, 갑시다!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"더는 못 기다리겠습니다. 집으로 돌아가야 해요.\"\n\n\"내 곧 따라가리다.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말한다.\n\n두 번째로, 나는 문께로 갔다. 그리고 두 번째로, 아무리 애를 써도, 나는 그 문지방을 넘지 못했다.\n\n\"돈을 돌려준다는 게, 부인, 미묘한 일이구려.\" 하고 부장이 말하는 것이 들렸다. \"그 물건들 값을 싸게 받으신 게 틀림없죠?\"\n\n\"싸게요!\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다. \"와서 직접 판단해 보세요.\"\n\n부인은 촛불을 집어 들고 부장을 부엌 한구석으로 데려갔다. 나는 도무지 그들을 따라가지 않고는 못 배겼다. 그 구석에는 잡동사니 한 무더기(거의가 낡은 쇠붙이였다)가 쌓여 있었는데, 그것은 어부가 이런저런 때에 난파선에서 주워 온 것으로, 그의 마음에 차게 팔아 치울 자리를 아직 못 찾은 물건들이었다. 욜랜드 부인은 이 잡동사니 속으로 파고들더니, 뚜껑이 달리고 걸어 두는 걸쇠가 붙은 낡은 옻칠 양철 상자 하나를 꺼내 올렸다—뱃사람들이 배 위에서 제 지도며 해도(海圖)며 그런 것들을 물기에서 지키느라 쓰는 그런 물건이었다.\n\n\"자!\" 하고 부인이 말한다. \"로재나가 오늘 저녁 왔을 때, 이거랑 똑같은 걸 하나 샀어요. '이거면 딱 좋겠어요,' 그 애가 그러더라고요, '제 소맷동이랑 깃을 넣어서, 상자 안에서 구겨지지 않게 하는 데요.' 일 실링 구 펜스예요, 커프 씨. 밥 먹고 사는 목숨을 걸고, 반 페니도 더 안 받았어요!\"\n\n\"거저나 다름없구려!\" 하고 부장이 무거운 한숨과 함께 말한다.\n\n그는 그 상자를 손으로 가늠해 보았다. 그가 그것을 들여다보며 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 한두 소절 흥얼거리는 소리를 들은 것 같았다. 이제 의심할 여지가 없었다! 그는 로재나 스피어먼에게 해가 될 또 하나의 사실을, 내가 그 아이의 결백이 가장 안전하리라 여기던 바로 그곳에서, 그것도 죄다 내 탓으로 알아낸 것이다! 내가 무슨 심정이었는지, 그리고 욜랜드 부인과 커프 부장 사이를 잇는 매개가 되어 준 것을 얼마나 진심으로 후회했는지는, 여러분의 상상에 맡기겠다.\n\n\"이만하면 됐습니다.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"우린 정말 가야 해요.\"\n\n내게는 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳하지 않고, 욜랜드 부인은 그 잡동사니 속으로 또 한 번 파고들더니, 이번에는 개 사슬 하나를 꺼내 올렸다.\n\n\"손으로 무게를 한번 가늠해 보세요, 나리.\" 하고 부인이 부장에게 말했다. \"우리 집에 이게 세 개 있었는데, 로재나가 그중 둘을 가져갔어요. '아니 얘야, 개 사슬을 두 개나 뭐에 쓰려고?' 하고 제가 그랬죠. '둘을 이으면 제 상자를 딱 알맞게 두를 수 있거든요,' 하고 그 애가 그러더군요. '밧줄이 제일 싼데,' 하고 제가 그랬죠. '사슬이 제일 튼튼해요,' 하고 그 애가 그러고요. '아니 세상에 사슬로 상자를 동여맨다는 소리를 누가 들어 봤다니,' 하고 제가 그랬죠. '어머, 욜랜드 아주머니, 자꾸 토 달지 마세요!' 하고 그 애가 그러더군요. '그냥 사슬 주세요!' 참 별난 애예요, 커프 씨—금덩이처럼 착하고, 우리 루시한테는 친자매보다 더 다정하고—그래도 늘 어딘가 좀 별나요. 자! 제가 그 애 비위를 맞춰 줬죠. 삼 실링 육 펜스요. 정직한 여자의 말을 걸고, 삼 실링 육 펜스예요, 커프 씨!\"\n\n\"하나에 말이오?\" 하고 부장이 말한다.\n\n\"둘 합쳐서요!\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다. \"둘 다 해서 삼 실링 육 펜스요.\"\n\n\"거저 주신 셈이오, 부인.\" 하고 부장이 고개를 저으며 말한다. \"아주 거저 주신 셈이오!\"\n\n\"돈은 여기 있어요,\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이, 마치 그것이 제 뜻과는 달리 저를 끌어당기기라도 하는 듯, 탁자 위 그 조그만 은화 무더기 쪽으로 옆걸음으로 도로 다가가며 말한다. \"양철 상자랑 개 사슬이 그 애가 산 전부고, 가져간 전부예요. 일 실링 구 펜스에 삼 실링 육 펜스—합해서 오 실링 삼 펜스죠. 내 사랑과 안부와 함께요—그리고 가여운 계집애가 저 쓸 데 있을지도 모르는 저금을, 제 양심상 도저히 못 받겠어요.\"\n\n\"부인, 나도 내 양심상 도저히 그 돈을 돌려줄 수가 없구려.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"그 물건들을 그 아이에게 선물로 주신 것이나 진배없소—정말이지 그렇소.\"\n\n\"그게 진심이세요, 나리?\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 놀랍도록 낯빛이 환해지며 말한다.\n\n\"의심할 여지가 있을 리가 있소.\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"베터리지 양반에게 물어보시구려.\"\n\n내게 물어봐야 아무 소용 없었다. 그들이 내게서 얻어 낸 것이라곤 \"안녕히 주무십시오.\"뿐이었다.\n\n\"돈이고 뭐고!\" 하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다. 이 말과 함께, 부인은 제 자제심을 죄다 잃은 듯했다. 그러고는 그 은화 무더기를 별안간 낚아채, 몽땅 제 주머니에 도로 넣었다. \"저기 저러고 놓여 있는데 아무도 안 가져가는 걸 보고 있자니, 사람 성미가 뒤집힌다니까요, 정말이지,\" 하고 이 도무지 말이 안 통하는 여자가, 털썩 주저앉으며, 마치 \"이제 다시 내 주머니에 들어갔으니—꺼낼 수 있으면 어디 꺼내 봐요!\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯, 커프 부장을 바라보며 외친다.\n\n이번에는, 나는 문께로 갔을 뿐 아니라, 아주 돌아가는 길로 나와 버렸다. 어떻게 설명하든, 나는 그 둘 중 하나가, 아니 둘 다가 나를 죽도록 모욕하기라도 한 것 같은 심정이었다. 내가 마을을 세 걸음도 채 내려가기 전에, 등 뒤에서 부장의 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"소개해 줘서 고맙소, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저 어부의 아내 덕분에 나는 아주 새로운 느낌 하나를 얻었소. 욜랜드 부인이 나를 헷갈리게 했구려.\"\n\n내 혀끝에는 그에게 쏘아붙일 대꾸가 걸려 있었으니, 그 까닭이란 다름 아니라—내가 나 자신에게 심기가 상해 있는 통에 그에게도 심기가 상해 있었기 때문이었다. 그러나 그가 헷갈렸다고 실토하자, 결국 별 큰 해는 없었던 게 아닐까 하는 위안이 되는 의심이 내 마음을 스쳤다. 나는 더 들으려고 조심스레 잠자코 기다렸다.\n\n\"그렇소,\" 하고 부장이, 마치 어둠 속에서 내 속생각을 정말로 읽어 내기라도 하듯 말한다. \"내게 냄새를 맡게 해 주기는커녕, (로재나에게 마음을 쓰는) 당신에게는 이 말이 위안이 될지도 모르겠소, 베터리지 양반, 당신이 나를 그 자취에서 벗어나게 하는 구실을 했다는 것이 말이오. 그 아이가 오늘 밤 무슨 짓을 했는지야, 물론, 뻔하기 그지없소. 그 아이는 두 사슬을 이어, 그것을 그 양철 상자 걸쇠에 붙들어 맸소. 그리고 그 상자를 물속이나 유사 속에 가라앉혔지. 사슬의 풀린 쪽 끝은 저만 아는 바위 아래 어딘가에 단단히 붙들어 맸고. 그리고 이번 일이 다 끝날 때까지 그 상자를 그 닻자리에 안전하게 놔둘 거요. 그 뒤에는 저 좋을 때 편할 때, 남몰래 그것을 숨은 자리에서 도로 끌어 올릴 수 있지. 여기까지는 죄다 더없이 뻔하오. 허나,\" 하고 부장이, 내가 여태 들어 본 중 처음으로 그 목소리에 조바심의 기색을 실어 말한다. \"수수께끼는—그 아이가 대체 그 양철 상자에 무엇을 숨겼느냐, 이거요.\"\n\n나는 속으로 \"월장석!\" 하고 생각했다. 그러나 커프 부장에게는 그저 \"짐작이 안 가십니까?\" 하고만 말했다.\n\n\"그건 다이아몬드가 아니오.\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"로재나 스피어먼이 그 다이아몬드를 가졌다면, 내 평생의 온 경험이 다 틀린 것이지.\"\n\n그 말을 듣자, 그 지긋지긋한 탐정 열병이, 짐작건대, 내 안에서 다시 타오르기 시작했다. 아무튼 나는 이 새 수수께끼를 알아맞히는 재미에 그만 나를 잊었다. 나는 경솔하게도 \"그 얼룩진 옷!\" 하고 말했다.\n\n커프 부장이 어둠 속에서 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 내 팔에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"저 당신네 유사에 무언가를 던져 넣으면, 그것이 다시 표면으로 떠오른 적이 있소?\" 하고 그가 물었다.\n\n\"결코 없습니다.\" 하고 내가 대답했다. \"가볍든 무겁든, 저 흔들리는 모래에 들어가는 것은 무엇이든 빨려 들어가, 다시는 보이지 않습니다.\"\n\n\"로재나 스피어먼도 그것을 아오?\"\n\n\"저만큼이나 잘 압니다.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"대체 그 아이가, 그 얼룩진 옷에 돌멩이 하나를 싸서 유사에 던져 넣는 것 말고, 달리 무슨 할 일이 있단 말이오? 그 아이가 그것을 숨겼어야 할 이유는 그림자만큼도 없소—그런데도 그 아이는 그것을 숨긴 게 틀림없소. 문제는,\" 하고 부장이, 다시 걸으며 말한다. \"그 페인트 얼룩진 옷이 속치마냐 잠옷이냐—아니면 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 간직할 이유가 있는 다른 무언가냐, 이거요. 베터리지 양반, 훼방 놓을 일이 생기지 않는 한, 나는 내일 프리징홀에 가서, 그 아이가 대체 옷을 지을 감을 남몰래 구할 때 시내에서 무엇을 샀는지를 알아내야겠소. 지금 이런 마당에 집을 비우는 것은 모험이오만—이 일에서 캄캄한 채로 한 걸음 더 내딛는 것은 그보다 더한 모험이지. 내 심기가 좀 상한 것을 너그러이 봐 주시오. 나는 내 스스로 보기에 체면을 구겼소—로재나 스피어먼이 나를 헷갈리게 하도록 내버려 뒀으니 말이오.\"\n\n우리가 돌아왔을 때, 하인들은 저녁을 먹고 있었다. 바깥뜰에서 우리가 처음 마주친 사람은, 시그레이브 경정이 부장의 마음대로 쓰라고 남겨 둔 그 순경이었다. 부장은 로재나 스피어먼이 돌아왔는지 물었다. 그렇다고. 언제? 거의 한 시간 전에. 무얼 했나? 위층으로 올라가 보닛과 망토를 벗었고—지금은 나머지 사람들과 조용히 저녁을 먹고 있다고.\n\n아무 말도 없이, 커프 부장은, 제 스스로 보기에 갈수록 더 체면을 구긴 채, 집 뒤편으로 걸어갔다. 어둠 속에서 입구를 놓친 그는, (내가 불러 세우는데도) 계속 걸어가다가, 정원으로 이어지는 쪽문에 막혀서야 멈춰 섰다. 내가 그를 바른길로 데려오려고 다가가 보니, 그는 집 뒤편 침실 층의 어느 한 창문을 유심히 올려다보고 있었다.\n\n나도 이번에는 따라 올려다보고서, 그가 골똘히 바라보던 대상이 레이철 아가씨 방의 창문이며, 마치 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 벌어지고 있기라도 하듯 그곳에서 불빛이 이리저리 오가고 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n\"저것이 버린더 아가씨의 방 아니오?\" 하고 커프 부장이 물었다.\n\n나는 그렇다고 대꾸하고, 나와 함께 안으로 들어가 저녁을 들자고 청했다. 부장은 제자리에 그대로 서서, 밤중 정원의 냄새를 즐기는 것에 대해 무어라 말했다. 나는 그를 그 즐거움에 맡겨 두었다. 막 문 안으로 들어서려던 참에, 나는 그 쪽문께에서 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 들었다. 커프 부장이 또 하나를 알아낸 것이다! 그리고 이번에는 우리 아가씨의 창문이 그 밑바닥에 있었다!\n\n그 뒤엣것에 생각이 미치자 나는, 차마 그를 혼자 내버려 둘 마음이 나지 않는다는 정중한 뜻과 함께, 다시 부장에게로 돌아갔다. \"저 위에 무언가 이해가 안 가는 게 있으십니까?\" 하고 나는 레이철 아가씨의 창문을 가리키며 덧붙였다.\n\n그 목소리로 미루어, 커프 부장은 별안간 다시 제 스스로 보기에 걸맞은 자리로 올라선 모양이었다. \"요크셔 사람들은 내기를 대단히 좋아하지 않소?\" 하고 그가 물었다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"우리가 그렇다 칩시다?\"\n\n\"내가 만일 요크셔 사람이라면,\" 하고 부장이 내 팔을 잡으며 말을 이었다. \"당신에게 소버린 금화 한 닢을 대등하게 걸겠소, 베터리지 양반, 당신 아가씨가 별안간 이 집을 떠나기로 마음먹으셨다는 데 말이오. 만일 그 내기에서 이긴다면, 나는 소버린 금화 한 닢을 더 걸겠다고 나서겠소, 그 생각이 아가씨께 지난 한 시간 안에 떠올랐다는 데 말이오.\" 부장의 첫 번째 짐작이 나를 흠칫하게 했다. 두 번째 짐작은, 로재나 스피어먼이 지난 한 시간 안에 그 모래밭에서 돌아왔다는, 우리가 순경에게서 들은 그 보고와 내 머릿속에서 어쩐지 뒤섞였다. 우리가 저녁을 들러 들어갈 때, 그 둘이 함께 내게 야릇한 영향을 미쳤다. 나는 커프 부장의 팔을 뿌리치고는, 예의도 잊은 채, 내 나름으로 직접 알아보려고 그를 밀치고 문을 지나갔다.\n\n하인 새뮤얼이, 복도에서 내가 처음 마주친 사람이었다.\n\n\"마님께서 어르신과 커프 부장을 뵙자고 기다리고 계십니다.\" 하고 그가, 내가 무어라 물어보기도 전에 말했다.\n\n\"얼마나 오래 기다리셨나?\" 하고 내 등 뒤에서 부장의 소리가 물었다.\n\n\"지난 한 시간째입니다, 나리.\"\n\n또 그거였다! 로재나가 돌아왔고, 레이철 아가씨가 예사롭지 않은 무슨 결심을 했으며, 마님이 부장을 뵈려고 기다리고 계셨다—죄다 지난 한 시간 안에 말이다! 이토록 서로 다른 사람들과 일들이 이런 식으로 서로 얽혀 드는 것을 보는 것은 유쾌한 노릇이 아니었다. 나는 커프 부장을 쳐다보지도, 그에게 말을 건네지도 않고, 위층으로 올라갔다. 마님의 방문을 두드리려고 손을 들어 올리는데, 내 손이 별안간 부들부들 떨리는 발작을 일으켰다.\n\n\"놀랄 일도 아니오,\" 하고 부장이 내 어깨 너머로 속삭였다. \"오늘 밤 이 집에서 추문이 터진다 해도 말이오. 놀라지 마시오! 나는 왕년에 이보다 더한 집안의 골칫거리도 입막음시켜 본 사람이오.\"\n\n그가 그 말을 하는데, 마님이 우리더러 들어오라 부르시는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVI\n\n우리는 방에 독서등 말고는 아무 불도 없는 채로 마님을 뵈었다. 갓은 마님의 얼굴에 그늘이 지도록 아래로 돌려 내려져 있었다. 여느 때처럼 우리를 똑바로 올려다보시는 대신, 마님은 탁자에 바짝 붙어 앉아, 펼쳐 놓은 책 한 권에 눈길을 고집스레 박고 계셨다.\n\n\"관리 양반,\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그대가 벌이고 있는 조사에 있어, 지금 이 집에 있는 누군가가 이곳을 떠나고자 하는지를 미리 아는 것이 중요하오?\"\n\n\"더없이 중요합니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 그대에게 이르지 않을 수 없구려. 버린더 아가씨가 제 이모 되는 프리징홀의 에이블화이트 부인 댁에 가 지내겠다 하오. 내일 아침 일찍 우리를 떠나기로 정했소.\"\n\n커프 부장이 나를 바라보았다. 나는 마님께 말씀드리려고 한 걸음 앞으로 나아갔다가—(굳이 인정하자면) 가슴이 오그라드는 것을 느끼고, 다시 한 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 아무 말도 하지 못했다.\n\n\"버린더 아가씨가 이모 댁에 가시겠다고 마님께 언제 아뢰었는지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\" 하고 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"한 시간쯤 전에 말이오.\" 하고 마님이 대답하셨다.\n\n커프 부장이 다시 한번 나를 바라보았다. 늙은이의 심장은 그리 쉽게 흔들리지 않는다고들 한다. 내가 다시 스물다섯이 되었다 한들, 내 심장이 지금보다 더 세차게 쿵쾅거릴 수는 없었으리라!\n\n\"마님, 저에게는,\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"버린더 아가씨의 행동을 좌우할 아무런 권한도 없습니다. 제가 마님께 청할 수 있는 것이라곤, 될 수 있으면, 아가씨의 출발을 그날 좀 더 늦은 시각으로 미루어 달라는 것뿐입니다. 저는 내일 아침 직접 프리징홀에 가야 합니다—그리고 더 이르지 않더라도, 두 시까지는 돌아오겠습니다. 만일 버린더 아가씨를 그때까지 여기 붙들어 둘 수 있다면, 저는 아가씨가 떠나시기 전에—뜻밖에—두어 마디 여쭙고 싶습니다.\"\n\n마님은 내게, 두 시가 되기 전에는 마차를 레이철 아가씨께 대령하지 말라는 분부를 마부에게 전하라 이르셨다. \"더 할 말이 있소?\" 하고 이 일이 끝나자 마님이 부장에게 물으셨다.\n\n\"딱 한 가지입니다, 마님. 만일 버린더 아가씨가 이 채비의 변경에 놀라시거든, 부디 아가씨의 여정을 미룬 원인으로 저를 들먹이지 말아 주십시오.\"\n\n마님은 무슨 말씀을 하시려는 듯 별안간 책에서 고개를 드셨다가—크게 애를 써서 스스로를 억누르시고는—다시 그 펼쳐진 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리시며, 손짓으로 우리를 물러가게 하셨다.\n\n\"참으로 놀라운 여인이오.\" 하고 우리가 다시 큰방으로 나왔을 때 커프 부장이 말했다. \"저 자제심만 아니었다면, 당신을 헷갈리게 하는 그 수수께끼는, 베터리지 양반, 오늘 밤 끝장이 났을 거요.\"\n\n그 말에, 마침내 그 진실이 나의 우둔한 늙은 머릿속으로 왈칵 밀려들었다. 그 순간, 짐작건대 나는 제정신을 아주 놓아 버렸던 게 틀림없다. 나는 부장의 저고리 깃을 움켜쥐고, 그를 벽에 밀어붙였다.\n\n\"이런 빌어먹을!\" 하고 나는 소리쳤다. \"레이철 아가씨한테 뭔가 잘못된 게 있고—당신은 여태 그걸 내게 숨겨 온 거요!\"\n\n커프 부장은—벽에 납작하게 밀린 채—손 하나 까딱하지 않고, 그 우울한 얼굴의 근육 하나 움직이지 않고, 나를 올려다보았다.\n\n\"아,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"마침내 알아채셨구려.\"\n\n내 손이 그의 깃에서 떨어졌고, 내 고개가 가슴께로 떨궈졌다. 내가 그런 식으로 발끈한 데 대한 얼마간의 변명으로, 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다. 나는 이 집안을 오십 년이나 섬겨 왔다는 것을. 레이철 아가씨는 어린 시절 몇 번이고, 또 몇 번이고 내 무릎에 기어올라, 내 구레나룻을 잡아당기곤 했다. 레이철 아가씨는, 그 온갖 흠에도 불구하고, 내 마음속에서, 늙은 하인이 여태 시중들고 사랑한 그 어떤 아가씨보다도 가장 애틋하고 가장 어여쁘고 가장 훌륭한 젊은 여주인이었다. 나는 커프 부장에게 용서를 빌었으나, 아무래도 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채, 그리 격에 맞지 않는 꼴로 빌었던 것 같다.\n\n\"너무 상심 마시오, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 부장이, 내가 그에게 바랄 자격이라곤 없는 다정함으로 말한다. \"우리네 직업에서, 걸핏하면 발끈하는 사람이라면, 죽 한 그릇에 칠 소금값어치도 못 되지. 위로가 좀 된다면, 다시 내 멱살을 잡으시오. 당신은 그걸 도무지 할 줄 모르오만, 당신 심정을 헤아려 그 서투름은 눈감아 주리다.\"\n\n그는 입아귀를 말아 올리고는, 제 나름의 그 처량한 방식으로, 아주 그럴듯한 농담 한마디를 던졌다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n나는 그를 내 조그만 거실로 데리고 들어가, 문을 닫았다.\n\n\"사실대로 말해 주시오, 부장.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"무엇을 의심하는 거요? 이제 와서 그걸 내게 숨기는 건 친절도 아니오.\"\n\n\"나는 의심하는 게 아니오.\" 하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"나는 아는 거요.\"\n\n내 그 불운한 성미가 다시 나를 이기기 시작했다.\n\n\"그러니까 당신은, 쉬운 말로,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"레이철 아가씨가 당신 다이아몬드를 손수 훔쳤다고 내게 말하려는 거요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"그것이, 이만큼 여러 말로, 내가 당신에게 하려는 말이오. 버린더 아가씨는 처음부터 끝까지 남몰래 월장석을 지니고 계셨소. 그리고 로재나 스피어먼을 제 심복으로 끌어들이셨지. 우리가 로재나 스피어먼을 그 절도의 범인으로 의심하리라 셈하셨기 때문이오. 이 사건의 전모가 여기 한마디로 다 들었소. 다시 멱살을 잡으시오, 베터리지 양반. 당신 심정을 풀어 주는 데 조금이라도 도움이 된다면, 다시 멱살을 잡으시오.\"\n\n하느님 저를 도우소서! 내 심정은 그런 식으로 풀릴 것이 아니었다. \"그 이유를 대 보시오!\" 그것이 내가 그에게 할 수 있는 말의 전부였다.\n\n\"내 이유는 내일 들으실 거요.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"만일 버린더 아가씨가 이모 댁 방문을 미루기를 거절하신다면 (아가씨가 그러시리라는 것을 당신은 알게 될 거요), 나는 내일 이 사건의 전모를 마님 앞에 늘어놓지 않을 수 없소. 그리고 그 결과가 어찌 될지 나로서는 알 수 없으니, 당신에게 그 자리에 있어, 양편에서 오가는 말을 들어 달라고 청하겠소. 이 일은 오늘 밤은 접어 둡시다. 아니오, 베터리지 양반, 당신은 월장석에 관한 한 나에게서 더는 한마디도 못 얻어 낼 거요. 저기 당신 탁자에 저녁상이 차려져 있구려. 저것이 내가 늘 너그러이 대하는 그 숱한 사람의 약함 가운데 하나요. 종을 울려 주시면, 내 감사 기도를 올리리다. '이제 저희가 받으려 하는 이 음식을 위하여—'\"\n\n\"부장, 맛있게 드시길 비오.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"내 입맛은 가셨소. 나는 당신 상 차려지는 것까지 지켜본 다음, 자리를 뜨는 것을 양해해 달라 청하고, 이 심정을 나 혼자 다스려 보려 하오.\"\n\n나는 그에게 온갖 것 중 가장 좋은 것으로 상이 차려지는 것을 지켜보았다—그리고 그 온갖 가장 좋은 것이 그의 목을 막히게 했더라도 나는 조금도 애석하지 않았으리라. 마침 그때 우두머리 정원사(벡비 씨)가 제 주간 장부를 들고 들어왔다. 부장은 곧바로 장미 이야기와 잔디 길과 자갈 길의 우열에 관한 화제로 접어들었다. 나는 그 둘을 내버려 두고, 무거운 마음으로 나갔다. 이것은 내가 여러 해 동안 기억하는 근심 가운데 처음으로, 담배 한 모금으로 날려 버릴 수 없는 것이었고, 심지어 『로빈슨 크루소』의 손길로도 미치지 못하는 것이었다.\n\n안절부절못하고 비참한 데다, 딱히 갈 방도 없어, 나는 테라스를 한 바퀴 돌며, 혼자 조용하고 고요하게 그 일을 곰곰이 되씹었다. 내 생각이 무엇이었는지는 그리 대수롭지 않다. 나는 처량하게 늙고, 지쳐 빠지고, 내 자리에 걸맞지 않은 처지라는 것을 느꼈다—그리고 평생 처음으로, 하느님께서 언제쯤 나를 데려가 주실지를 헤아려 보기 시작했다. 이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 나는 레이철 아가씨에 대한 내 믿음을 굳게 지켰다. 커프 부장이 그 온갖 영화를 누리던 솔로몬이었고, 또 우리 아가씨가 비열하고 죄스러운 음모에 발을 담갔다고 내게 말했다 한들, 나는 아무리 지혜로운 솔로몬에게라도 단 하나의 대답밖에 없었으리라. \"당신은 아가씨를 모르오. 나는 알고 말이오.\"\n\n내 묵상은 새뮤얼에게 끊겼다. 그는 내게 마님의 서면 전갈을 가져왔다.\n\n그것을 읽을 불을 얻으려고 집 안으로 들어가면서, 새뮤얼이 날씨가 바뀔 것 같다고 말했다. 심란한 마음 탓에 나는 그 전까지 그것을 눈치채지 못했던 것이다. 그러나 이제 주의가 일깨워지고 보니, 개들이 불안해하는 소리와, 바람이 낮게 신음하는 소리가 들렸다. 하늘을 올려다보니, 조각구름이 갈수록 시커메지며, 물기 어린 달 위로 갈수록 빠르게 휘몰려 가는 것이 보였다. 사나운 날씨가 오고 있었다—새뮤얼의 말이 옳았다, 사나운 날씨가 오고 있었다.\n\n마님의 전갈은 내게 알리기를, 프리징홀의 치안판사가 그 인도인 셋에 관해 마님께 상기시키는 편지를 보내왔다는 것이었다. 다가오는 주 초에는, 그 악당들을 부득이 풀어 주어, 저희 마음대로 하게끔 놓아주어야 했다. 만일 우리가 그들에게 더 물어볼 것이 있다면, 지체할 겨를이 없었다. 마님이 지난번 커프 부장을 뵈었을 때 이 이야기를 하는 것을 잊으셨던 터라, 이제 내게 그 빠뜨린 것을 채우라 이르신 것이다. 그 인도인들은 내 머릿속에서 깨끗이 사라져 있었다 (틀림없이 여러분의 머릿속에서도 깨끗이 사라졌으리라). 그 화제를 다시 들쑤셔 무슨 소용이 있을까 싶었다. 그래도 나는, 당연한 노릇으로, 그 자리에서 분부에 따랐다.\n\n나는 커프 부장과 정원사가, 그 둘 사이에 스카치 위스키 한 병을 놓고, 장미 재배를 두고 벌인 논쟁에 귀밑까지 푹 빠져 있는 것을 보았다. 부장은 어찌나 깊이 빠져 있던지, 내가 들어오자 손을 들어, 그 토론에 끼어들지 말라는 시늉을 했다. 내가 알아들은 바로는, 그들 사이의 문제란, 흰 이끼장미를 잘 자라게 하려면 그것을 들장미에 접붙여야 하느냐 마느냐 하는 것이었다. 벡비 씨는 그래야 한다고 하고, 커프 부장은 그럴 것 없다고 했다. 그들은 사내아이 둘처럼 열을 올리며 내게 판정을 청했다. 장미 재배에 관해서라곤 아무것도 모르는 나는, 정의의 저울이 머리카락 한 올만큼도 기울지 않고 팽팽하여 여왕 폐하의 재판관들을 애먹일 때 그들이 하는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 가운뎃길을 잡았다. \"두 분,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"양편 다 할 말이 많은 노릇입니다.\" 그 공평무사한 판결이 자아낸 잠시의 소강상태를 틈타, 나는 마님의 서면 전갈을 커프 부장의 눈앞, 탁자 위에 내려놓았다.\n\n이 무렵 나는, 될 수 있는 대로 가깝게 말하자면, 그 부장을 미워하기에 이르러 있었다. 그러나 진실을 위해 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없으니, 기지의 민첩함이라는 점에서, 그는 놀라운 사람이었다.\n\n그 전갈을 읽고 반 분도 안 되어, 그는 제 기억 속에서 시그레이브 경정의 보고를 되짚어, 그중 인도인들이 얽힌 대목을 골라냈고, 답을 낼 채비가 되어 있었다. 인도인들과 그들의 말을 아는 어떤 대단한 여행가가 시그레이브 씨의 보고에 등장했었지, 그렇지 않소? 좋소. 그 신사분의 이름과 주소를 아시오? 그것도 좋소. 그것을 마님 전갈 뒷면에 적어 주시겠소? 참으로 고맙소. 커프 부장은 아침에 프리징홀에 가면 그 신사분을 찾아볼 참이었다.\n\n\"거기서 뭐라도 나올 걸로 보시오?\" 하고 내가 물었다. \"시그레이브 경정은 그 인도인들이 갓난아기처럼 결백하다고 봤소만.\"\n\n\"시그레이브 경정은, 지금까지, 제 온갖 결론에서 죄다 틀렸음이 드러났소.\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"시그레이브 경정이 그 인도인들에 대해서도 틀렸는지 아닌지, 내일 알아볼 만한 노릇일지도 모르지.\" 그러고는 벡비 씨에게로 돌아서서, 논쟁이 끊겼던 바로 그 대목에서 다시 그것을 이어 갔다. \"우리 사이의 이 문제는 흙과 철에 관한 문제요, 또 참을성과 공에 관한 문제지, 정원사 양반. 자, 다른 관점에서 그것을 짚어 봅시다. 당신이 흰 이끼장미를 든다 치면—\"\n\n그 무렵 나는, 그들에게 문을 닫아, 나머지 논쟁이 들리지 않는 데로 나와 있었다.\n\n복도에서, 나는 페넬로피가 서성거리는 것을 만나, 무엇을 기다리느냐고 물었다.\n\n그 아이는 제 아가씨의 종을 기다리고 있었다. 아가씨가 다음 날 여정을 위한 짐 꾸리기를 마저 하러 저를 도로 부르실 때 울릴 그 종을 말이다. 더 물어보고서야 나는, 레이철 아가씨가 프리징홀의 이모 댁에 가고 싶어 하는 이유로, 이 집이 당신께 견딜 수 없는 곳이 되었고, 한 지붕 아래 경찰관이 있다는 그 역겨운 존재를 더는 참을 수 없노라고 댄 것을 알게 되었다. 반 시간 전에 출발이 오후 두 시로 늦춰지리라는 말을 듣고, 아가씨는 격렬한 노여움을 터뜨렸다. 그때 그 자리에 계시던 마님이 아가씨를 호되게 꾸짖으신 다음, (아무래도 따님만의 귀에 남겨 둘 무슨 하실 말씀이 있었던 듯) 페넬로피를 방에서 내보내셨다. 내 딸아이는 집안이 이렇게 뒤바뀐 형편에 처량하게 풀이 죽어 있었다. \"만사가 어그러져요, 아버지. 아무것도 예전 같지 않아요. 무슨 무시무시한 불행이 우리 모두 위에 드리워져 있는 것만 같아요.\"\n\n그것은 나의 심정이기도 했다. 그러나 나는 딸 앞에서는 태연한 낯을 지어 보였다. 우리가 이야기하는 동안 레이철 아가씨의 종이 울렸다. 페넬로피는 짐 꾸리기를 마저 하러 뒤쪽 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다. 나는 다른 길로 큰방으로 가서, 날씨 변화에 대해 청우계가 뭐라 하는지 보려 했다.\n\n하인들의 방에서 큰방으로 이어지는 그 회전문에 막 다가서던 참에, 그것이 반대편에서 거칠게 열리더니, 로재나 스피어먼이 내 곁을 지나 뛰어갔다. 얼굴에는 딱한 고통의 빛이 서려 있고, 한 손은, 마치 그 쥐어짜는 아픔이 그 어름에 있기라도 하듯, 제 가슴을 꽉 누른 채였다. \"무슨 일이냐, 얘야?\" 하고 나는 그를 붙잡으며 물었다. \"어디 아프냐?\" \"제발, 저한테 말 걸지 마세요.\" 하고 그가 대답하고는, 몸을 비틀어 내 손을 빠져나와, 하인용 계단 쪽으로 뛰어갔다. 나는 (들을 만한 데 있던) 요리사를 불러 그 딱한 아이를 살펴 달라 했다. 요리사 말고도 다른 두 사람이 들을 만한 데 있었음이 드러났다. 커프 부장이 내 방에서 소리 없이 튀어나와, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 나는 \"아무것도 아니오.\" 하고 대답했다. 다른 편에서는, 프랭클린 도련님이 그 회전문을 홱 열어젖히고, 나를 큰방으로 손짓해 부르며, 로재나 스피어먼을 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"방금 제 곁을 지나갔습니다, 나리. 몹시 심란한 얼굴로, 아주 이상한 모양으로요.\"\n\n\"아무래도 내가 죄 없이 그 심란함의 원인인 것 같네, 베터리지.\"\n\n\"나리께서요!\"\n\n\"나도 그걸 설명할 수가 없어.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"하지만, 만일 그 아이가 다이아몬드 실종에 얽혀 있는 것이라면, 나는 정말이지, 그 아이가 채 이 분도 안 되기 전에, 세상 모든 사람 중에서도 하필 나에게—모든 것을 털어놓기 직전이었다고 믿네.\"\n\n그가 그 마지막 말을 하면서 회전문 쪽을 바라보는데, 나는 그것이 안쪽에서 조금 열리는 것을 본 듯한 착각이 들었다.\n\n누가 엿듣고 있었던 걸까? 내가 거기 다다르기도 전에 그 문은 도로 닫혔다. 그 바로 다음 순간 안을 들여다보니, 커프 부장의 그 점잖은 검은 저고리 자락이 복도 모퉁이를 돌아 사라지는 것을 본 듯싶었다. 그는, 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었다. 그의 조사가 실은 어느 방향으로 향하고 있는지를 내가 알아챈 지금, 그가 나에게서 더는 도움을 바랄 수 없다는 것을. 그런 처지에서, 스스로 알아서 하되, 그것을 땅속 같은 은밀한 방식으로 하는 것은 그야말로 그다운 노릇이었다.\n\n내가 정말로 부장을 본 것인지 확신이 서지 않는 데다—하늘도 아시다시피, 이미 재앙이 넘치도록 벌어지고 있는 마당에 공연히 재앙을 더 만들고 싶지도 않아, 나는 프랭클린 도련님에게 개 한 마리가 집 안에 들어온 것 같다고 말했다—그러고는 그에게 로재나와 그 자신 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 이야기해 달라고 청했다.\n\n\"큰방을 지나가시던 중이었습니까, 나리?\" 하고 내가 물었다. \"그 아이가 나리께 말을 걸었을 때, 우연히 마주치신 겁니까?\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님이 당구대를 가리켰다.\n\n\"나는 공이나 이리저리 쳐 대며,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이 지긋지긋한 다이아몬드 일을 머릿속에서 떨쳐 내려 하고 있었네. 마침 고개를 들었는데—내 곁에 로재나 스피어먼이 유령처럼 서 있지 뭔가! 그런 식으로 내게 살금살금 다가온 것이 어찌나 야릇하던지, 처음엔 뭘 어째야 할지 도무지 알 수가 없었어. 그 얼굴에 몹시 안달하는 기색이 서린 것을 보고, 나는 그 아이에게 내게 할 말이 있느냐고 물었지. 그 아이가 대답하기를, '예, 감히 그래도 된다면요.' 하더군. 그 아이에게 어떤 혐의가 걸려 있는지 아는 터라, 나는 그런 말을 오직 한 가지로밖에 새길 수 없었어. 솔직히 그 말이 나를 불편하게 했네. 나는 그 아이의 속내를 청해 들일 마음이 없었어. 그와 동시에, 지금 우리를 에워싼 이 곤경 속에서, 만일 그 아이가 정말로 내게 말하기로 작정한 것이라면, 그것을 들어 주기를 거절하는 것도 도무지 떳떳할 수가 없었지. 난처한 처지였네. 그리고 아마 나는 그 처지를 제법 서투르게 빠져나왔을 거야. 나는 그 아이에게, '네 말을 잘 모르겠구나. 내가 해 주었으면 하는 게 뭐라도 있느냐?' 하고 말했어. 이보게, 베터리지, 나는 매정하게 말한 게 아니었네! 그 딱한 아이가 못생긴 거야 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이지—그때 나는 그걸 느꼈어. 큐대가 여전히 내 손에 들려 있어서, 나는 이 일의 어색함을 덜어 보려고 계속 공을 쳐 댔지. 그런데 알고 보니, 나는 사태를 한층 더 나쁘게 만들었을 뿐이었어. 아무래도 나는 뜻하지 않게 그 아이의 자존심을 상하게 한 것 같아! 그 아이가 별안간 돌아서더군. '저이는 당구공을 보네,' 하고 그 아이가 말하는 것이 들렸어. '나를 보느니 뭐든 다른 걸 보는 게 낫다는 거지!' 내가 그를 붙잡기도 전에, 그 아이는 큰방을 떠나 버렸어. 나는 그게 영 마음이 편치 않네, 베터리지. 미안하지만 로재나에게 내가 매정하게 굴 뜻은 없었다고 전해 주겠나? 내 속생각으로는, 어쩌면 내가 그 아이에게 좀 모질었는지도 몰라—나는 그 다이아몬드의 실종이 그 아이에게로 거슬러 밝혀지기를 은근히 바라기까지 했으니 말이야. 그 딱한 아이에게 무슨 악의가 있어서가 아니라, 다만—\" 그는 거기서 말을 멈추고, 당구대로 돌아가, 다시 한번 공을 이리저리 쳐 대기 시작했다.\n\n부장과 나 사이에 오간 일을 겪은 뒤라, 나는 그가 입 밖에 내지 않고 남겨 둔 것이 무엇인지를 그 자신이 아는 것만큼이나 잘 알았다.\n\n이제 오직 월장석이 우리 집 둘째 하녀에게로 거슬러 밝혀지는 것만이, 커프 부장의 마음속에서 레이철 아가씨를 짓누르고 있는 그 치욕스러운 혐의 위로 아가씨를 끌어올릴 수 있었다. 이제 그것은 우리 아가씨의 초조한 흥분을 가라앉히는 문제가 아니었다. 그것은 아가씨의 결백을 증명하는 문제였다. 만일 로재나가 제 몸에 혐의를 씌울 아무 짓도 하지 않았더라면, 프랭클린 도련님이 품었노라 실토한 그 바람은, 양심에 비추어 그 아이에게 이만저만 모진 것이 아니었으리라. 그러나 사정은 그렇지가 않았다. 그 아이는 앓는 척했고, 남몰래 프리징홀에 다녀왔다. 그 아이는 밤을 꼬박 새우며, 남모르게 무언가를 짓거나 무언가를 없애 버렸다. 그리고 그 아이는 그날 저녁, 아무리 좋게 봐도 몹시 수상쩍은 정황 아래, 그 흔들리는 모래에 가 있었다. 이 모든 이유에서 (로재나가 딱하기는 했으나) 나는, 이 일을 바라보는 프랭클린 도련님의 방식이, 프랭클린 도련님의 처지에서는, 부자연스럽지도 이치에 어긋나지도 않는다고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다. 나는 그런 취지의 말을 그에게 한마디 했다.\n\n\"그래, 그래!\" 하고 그가 대꾸했다. \"하지만 딱 한 가닥 가망은 있어—물론 아주 희박한 것이지만—로재나의 처신에, 지금 우리 눈에는 안 보이는 무슨 설명이 있을지도 모른다는 가망 말이야. 나는 여자의 마음을 아프게 하는 게 질색이네, 베터리지! 그 딱한 것에게, 내가 자네더러 전하라 한 그 말을 전해 주게. 그리고 만일 그 아이가 내게 말하고 싶어 하거든—내가 곤경에 빠지든 말든 나는 아랑곳없으니—서재로 그를 보내 주게.\" 이 다정한 말과 함께 그는 큐대를 내려놓고 나를 떠났다.\n\n하인들의 방에 물어보니, 로재나가 제 방으로 물러갔다는 것이었다. 그 아이는 도와주겠다는 온갖 제안을 고마워하며 물리쳤고, 그저 조용히 쉬게 내버려 달라고만 청했다는 것이었다. 그러니, 여기서 그 아이 쪽에서 나올 어떤 고백도 (설령 정말로 할 고백이 있었다손 치더라도) 그날 밤은 끝난 셈이었다. 나는 그 결과를 프랭클린 도련님께 알렸고, 그는 그러자 서재를 떠나, 잠자리에 들러 올라갔다.\n\n내가 불을 끄고 창문을 단단히 잠그고 있는데, 새뮤얼이 내가 내 방에 남겨 둔 두 손님에 관한 소식을 가지고 들어왔다.\n\n흰 이끼장미에 관한 논쟁은 아무래도 마침내 끝난 모양이었다. 정원사는 집으로 돌아갔고, 커프 부장은 집 아래층 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다.\n\n나는 내 방을 들여다보았다. 과연 그러했다—거기서 발견할 수 있는 것이라곤 빈 텀블러 두 개와 뜨거운 그로그 술의 짙은 냄새뿐이었다. 부장이 제 발로 저를 위해 마련된 침실로 간 것일까? 나는 알아보러 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n두 번째 층계참에 이른 뒤, 나는 왼편에서 조용하고 고른 숨소리가 들리는 것 같았다. 내 왼편은 레이철 아가씨의 방과 통하는 복도로 이어져 있었다. 안을 들여다보니, 거기, 복도를 가로질러 나란히 놓인 의자 세 개 위에 몸을 웅크린 채—거기, 희끗한 머리에 붉은 손수건을 둘러 매고, 제 점잖은 검은 저고리를 말아 베개로 삼은 채, 커프 부장이 누워 자고 있는 게 아닌가!\n\n내가 그에게 다가서는 그 순간, 그는 개처럼, 곧바로 조용히 잠에서 깼다.\n\n\"안녕히 주무시오, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 명심하시오, 만일 당신이 언젠가 장미를 기르게 되거든, 흰 이끼장미는 들장미에 접붙이지 않는 편이 도리어 낫소, 정원사가 아무리 그 반대로 말하더라도 말이오!\"\n\n\"여기서 뭘 하는 거요?\" 하고 내가 물었다. \"어째서 당신의 제 침대에 있지 않고?\"\n\n\"내가 내 제 침대에 있지 않은 것은,\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"내가, 이 서글픈 세상에서, 제 밥벌이를 정직하게, 그러면서 동시에 편안하게 할 수는 없는 그 숱한 사람 가운데 하나이기 때문이오. 오늘 저녁, 로재나 스피어먼이 그 모래밭에서 돌아온 무렵과, 버린더 아가씨가 이 집을 떠나기로 한 결심을 밝힌 무렵이 우연히 겹쳤소. 로재나가 무엇을 숨겼든 간에, 당신 아가씨는 그것이 숨겨졌다는 것을 알기 전에는 떠나실 수 없었으리라는 것이 내게는 뻔하오. 그 둘은 오늘 밤 이미 한 번 남몰래 통했음이 틀림없소. 만일 그들이 집이 조용해졌을 때 다시 통하려 한다면, 나는 그 길목에 있다가, 그것을 막고 싶은 거요. 당신 잠자리 채비를 어질러 놓았다고 나를 탓하지 마시오, 베터리지 양반—그 다이아몬드를 탓하시오.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 그 다이아몬드가 이 집에 발을 들여놓는 일이 애초에 없었더라면 좋았을 것을!\" 하고 나는 터뜨렸다.\n\n커프 부장은 제가 하룻밤을 지새우도록 스스로를 처넣은 그 의자 셋을 서글픈 얼굴로 바라보았다.\n\n\"나도 그렇소.\" 하고 그가 무겁게 말했다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVII\n\n밤에는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 그리고 (기쁘게 덧붙이자면) 레이철 아가씨와 로재나 사이에 통하려는 어떤 시도도 커프 부장의 경계에 걸려들지 않았다.\n\n나는 부장이 아침에 맨 먼저 프리징홀로 떠날 줄 알았다. 그러나 그는, 마치 먼저 할 다른 일이라도 있는 듯, 이리저리 서성거렸다. 나는 그를 제 마음대로 하게 내버려 두었다. 그러고는 얼마 뒤 부지로 나갔다가, 관목 숲 곁 그 즐겨 걷는 산책길에서 프랭클린 도련님을 만났다.\n\n우리가 두 마디도 채 나누기 전에, 부장이 뜻밖에 우리에게 합류했다. 그는 프랭클린 도련님께 다가갔는데, 도련님은, 인정하건대, 제법 도도하게 그를 맞았다. \"내게 할 말이라도 있소?\" 하는 것이, 프랭클린 도련님께 정중히 좋은 아침 인사를 건넨 것에 대해 부장이 받은 대꾸의 전부였다.\n\n\"나리께 드릴 말씀이 있습니다, 나리.\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"제가 여기서 벌이고 있는 조사에 관해서 말입니다. 나리는 어제 그 조사가 실은 어느 방향으로 향하고 있는지를 알아채셨지요. 나리의 처지에서는, 지극히 당연하게도, 충격을 받고 괴로우실 겁니다. 또한 지극히 당연하게도, 나리는 나리 집안의 추문에 대한 나리 자신의 노여운 심정을 저에게 쏟아 내시는 거고요.\"\n\n\"뭘 바라는 거요?\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제법 날카롭게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"나리께 일깨워 드리고 싶습니다, 나리. 어쨌거나, 지금까지, 제가 틀렸다고 증명된 적은 없다는 것을요. 그것을 마음에 새기시되, 동시에, 부디 기억해 주십시오. 저는 이 댁 마님의 재가 아래 여기서 일하는 법의 관리라는 것을 말입니다. 이런 처지에서, 나리가 마침 지니고 계실지도 모르는 어떤 특별한 정보로 저를 돕는 것이, 선량한 시민으로서 나리의 의무입니까, 아닙니까?\"\n\n\"나는 아무런 특별한 정보도 지니고 있지 않소.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다.\n\n커프 부장은, 마치 아무 대답도 나오지 않았다는 듯, 그 대답을 옆으로 밀쳐 두었다.\n\n\"나리는 제 시간이 먼 데서의 조사에 헛되이 쓰이는 것을 아껴 주실 수 있습니다, 나리.\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"만일 나리가 제 뜻을 알아차리고 툭 터놓기로 하신다면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"나는 당신 뜻을 못 알아듣겠소.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"그리고 나는 할 말이 없소.\"\n\n\"여자 하인 하나가 (이름은 대지 않겠습니다) 어젯밤 나리께 남몰래 말을 걸었지요, 나리.\"\n\n또 한 번 프랭클린 도련님이 그의 말을 잘랐다. 또 한 번 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"나는 할 말이 없소.\"\n\n잠자코 곁에 서서, 나는 전날 저녁 그 회전문이 움직이던 것과, 복도 저편으로 사라지던 것을 내가 본 그 저고리 자락을 떠올렸다. 커프 부장은, 틀림없이, 내가 그의 말을 가로막기 직전에, 로재나가 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 무언가를 고백함으로써 제 마음의 짐을 덜었으리라 의심할 만큼은 충분히 엿들었던 것이다.\n\n이 생각이 내 머리를 스치기가 무섭게—관목 숲 산책길 끝에 나타난 것이 다름 아닌 로재나 스피어먼 본인이지 뭔가! 그 뒤로는 페넬로피가 따라오고 있었는데, 아무래도 그를 집으로 되돌려 세우려 애쓰는 눈치였다. 프랭클린 도련님이 혼자가 아닌 것을 보고, 로재나는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 이제 어찌해야 할지 몰라 크게 당황한 기색이 역력했다. 페넬로피는 그 뒤에서 기다렸다. 프랭클린 도련님도 내가 그 아이들을 본 것과 동시에 그들을 보았다. 부장은, 그 악마 같은 교활함으로, 그들을 아예 눈치채지 못한 척했다. 이 모든 일이 순식간에 벌어졌다. 프랭클린 도련님이나 나나 미처 한마디 하기도 전에, 커프 부장이, 앞서의 대화를 이어 가는 척하며, 매끄럽게 끼어들었다.\n\n\"그 아이에게 해를 끼칠까 봐 두려워하실 것 없습니다, 나리.\" 하고 그가, 로재나가 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 프랭클린 도련님께 말했다. \"오히려, 나리가 로재나 스피어먼에게 조금이라도 관심이 있으시다면, 저를 믿고 털어놓는 영광을 베풀어 주시기를 권합니다.\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님도 곧바로 그 아이들을 눈치채지 못한 척했다. 그도 제 쪽에서 큰 소리로 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 로재나 스피어먼에게 아무런 관심도 없소.\"\n\n나는 산책길 끝 쪽을 바라보았다. 그 거리에서 내가 본 것이라곤, 프랭클린 도련님이 말을 마치는 그 순간 로재나가 별안간 홱 돌아서는 것뿐이었다. 바로 조금 전에 그러했던 것처럼 페넬로피에게 버티는 대신, 그는 이제 내 딸이 제 팔을 잡고 저를 집으로 도로 데려가도록 순순히 맡겼다.\n\n두 아이가 사라지는 것과 동시에 아침 식사를 알리는 종이 울렸다—그리고 이제는 커프 부장마저 이것을 가망 없는 일로 단념하지 않을 수 없었다! 그는 내게 나직이 말했다. \"나는 프리징홀에 가겠소, 베터리지 양반. 그리고 두 시 전에 돌아오겠소.\" 그는 더 말 한마디 없이 제 갈 길을 갔다—그리하여 몇 시간 동안 우리는 그를 잘 떨쳐 낸 셈이었다.\n\n\"자네가 로재나와의 일을 바로잡아 줘야겠네.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 우리 둘만 남았을 때 내게 말했다. \"나는 저 불운한 아이 앞에서는 무슨 서툰 말이나 짓을 하도록 운명 지어진 것만 같아. 커프 부장이 우리 둘 다에게 덫을 놓은 것을, 자네도 직접 봤을 거야. 만일 그가 나를 헷갈리게 하거나, 그 아이를 자극해 무언가 터뜨리게 할 수 있었다면, 그 아이든 나든 그의 목적에 들어맞을 무슨 말을 했을지도 몰라. 그 순간의 기지로는, 내가 택한 그 방도 말고는 거기서 빠져나갈 더 나은 길이 보이지 않았어. 그것이 그 아이가 무슨 말을 하는 것을 막았고, 또 부장에게는 내가 그 속을 꿰뚫어 봤다는 것을 보여 줬지. 그가 엿듣고 있었던 게 분명해, 베터리지, 내가 어젯밤 자네에게 말할 때 말이야.\"\n\n그는 엿듣는 것보다 더한 짓을 했다고, 나는 속으로 생각했다. 그는 그 아이가 프랭클린 도련님께 반해 있다고 내가 그에게 말한 것을 기억해 두었고, 로재나가 듣는 데서 프랭클린 도련님의 로재나에 대한 관심에 호소할 때, 바로 그것을 셈에 넣었던 것이다.\n\n\"엿듣는 것으로 말하자면, 나리,\" 하고 나는 (그 다른 대목은 속에 묻어 둔 채) 말했다. \"이런 일이 이대로 더 오래 이어진다면, 우리 모두가 한배를 젓는 신세가 될 겁니다. 훔쳐보고, 엿보고, 엿듣는 것이 우리 같은 처지에 놓인 사람들의 자연스러운 소일거리지요. 하루 이틀만 더 지나면, 프랭클린 도련님, 우리는 다 함께 벙어리가 되고 말 겁니다—그 까닭인즉, 우리가 다들 서로의 비밀을 불시에 캐내려고 엿듣고 있을 것이요, 또 다들 그것을 알고 있을 테니까요. 제가 발끈한 것을 용서하십시오, 나리. 이 집에 우리 위로 드리워진 그 소름 끼치는 수수께끼가 술처럼 제 머릿속에 스며들어, 저를 사납게 만듭니다. 나리가 제게 이르신 것을 잊지 않겠습니다. 첫 기회를 잡아 로재나 스피어먼과의 일을 바로잡겠습니다.\"\n\n\"자네, 어젯밤 일에 대해서는 아직 그 아이에게 아무 말도 안 했지, 그렇지?\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 물었다.\n\n\"안 했습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"그럼 지금은 아무 말도 말게. 부장이 우리 둘을 함께 덮치려고 지켜보는 마당에, 나는 그 아이의 속내를 청해 듣지 않는 편이 낫겠어. 내 처신이 그리 앞뒤가 맞지는 않지, 베터리지—안 그런가? 나는 이 일에서, 생각만 해도 끔찍하지 않은 빠져나갈 길이라곤, 그 다이아몬드가 로재나에게로 거슬러 밝혀지는 것 말고는 도무지 안 보여. 그러면서도 나는 커프 부장이 그 아이를 캐내는 것을 도울 수가 없고, 돕지도 않겠어.\"\n\n의심할 여지 없이, 어지간히 이치에 안 맞는 노릇이었다. 그러나 그것은 나의 심정이기도 했다. 나는 그를 속속들이 이해했다. 만일 여러분이, 평생에 한 번만이라도, 여러분도 죽을 목숨임을 기억한다면, 아마 여러분도 그를 속속들이 이해하시리라.\n\n커프 부장이 프리징홀로 가는 동안, 집 안팎의 형편은, 간추리자면 이러했다.\n\n레이철 아가씨는, 여전히 고집스레 제 방에 틀어박힌 채, 마차가 저를 이모 댁으로 데려갈 시각을 기다렸다. 마님과 프랭클린 도련님은 함께 아침을 들었다. 아침 식사 뒤, 프랭클린 도련님은 그 느닷없는 결심 가운데 하나를 내리고는, 오래 걸으며 마음을 달래려고 부랴부랴 밖으로 나갔다. 그가 나가는 것을 본 사람은 나뿐이었다. 그리고 그는 내게 부장이 돌아오기 전에 돌아오겠다고 말했다. 밤사이 예고되었던 날씨의 변화가 찾아왔다. 새벽 직후 세찬 비가 내리더니, 뒤이어 거센 바람이 불었다. 날이 밝아 갈수록 바람은 상쾌하게 불었다. 그러나 구름이 몇 번이고 을러댔음에도, 비는 여전히 참고 있었다. 젊고 튼튼하여, 바다에서 휘몰아쳐 오는 그 큰 돌풍을 가슴으로 맞받을 수 있는 사람이라면, 산책하기에 나쁜 날은 아니었다.\n\n나는 아침 식사 뒤 마님을 모시고, 집안 살림 장부를 셈하시는 것을 거들었다. 마님은 월장석 이야기를 딱 한 번 넌지시 꺼내셨는데, 그것도 우리 사이에서 지금은 그 이야기를 하지 못하게 하려는 뜻에서였다. \"저 사람이 돌아올 때까지 기다려라.\" 하고 마님은, 부장을 두고 말씀하셨다. \"그때는 그것을 이야기해야만 할 게다. 지금은 그것을 이야기할 까닭이 없느니라.\"\n\n마님 곁을 떠난 뒤, 나는 페넬로피가 내 방에서 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"아버지, 아버지가 좀 와서 로재나한테 말 좀 걸어 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"그 애 때문에 마음이 몹시 안 좋아요.\"\n\n무슨 일인지 나는 냉큼 짐작이 갔다. 그러나 사내란 (우월한 존재이니만큼) 여자를 나아지게 할 의무가 있다는 것이—할 수만 있다면 말이다—나의 격언이다. 여자가 내게 무언가를 해 주기를 바랄 때는 (내 딸이든 아니든 상관없다), 나는 늘 그 까닭을 아는 것을 고집한다. 여자들에게 까닭을 찾느라 제 마음속을 더 자주 뒤지게 만들수록, 삶의 온갖 관계에서 그들을 더 다루기 쉬워지는 것을 알게 되리라. 그들이 (딱한 것들!) 먼저 저지르고 나중에 생각하는 것은 그들의 잘못이 아니다. 그들의 비위를 맞춰 주는 바보들의 잘못이다.\n\n이번 경우에 페넬로피가 댄 까닭은 그 아이 자신의 말로 옮겨 볼 수 있다. \"아버지, 저는요,\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"프랭클린 도련님이 뜻하지 않게 로재나를 모질게 아프게 하신 것 같아 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"무엇 때문에 로재나가 관목 숲 산책길로 갔던 거냐?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"제 광기 때문이죠.\" 하고 페넬로피가 말한다. \"달리 뭐라 부를 수가 없어요. 그 앤 오늘 아침, 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 기어이 프랭클린 도련님께 말하려고 작정했어요. 저는 그 앨 막으려고 애를 다 썼어요. 아버지도 그건 보셨잖아요. 그 애가 그 무시무시한 말을 듣기 전에 제가 그 앨 데리고 가기만 했더라도—\"\n\n\"저런! 저런!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"정신 놓지 마라. 로재나를 놀래킬 무슨 일이 있었는지 나는 도무지 떠오르질 않는구나.\"\n\n\"놀래킬 건 없었어요, 아버지. 하지만 프랭클린 도련님이 그 애한테 아무런 관심도 없다고 하셨잖아요—게다가, 아, 어찌나 모진 목소리로 그러시던지요!\"\n\n\"그건 부장의 입을 막으려고 하신 말이다.\" 하고 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"그건 저도 그 애한테 말해 줬어요.\" 하고 페넬로피가 말한다. \"하지만 보세요, 아버지 (프랭클린 도련님을 탓할 건 아니지만), 도련님은 몇 주 몇 주째 그 애 자존심을 상하게 하고 실망시켜 오셨어요. 그런데 이제 그 모든 것 위에 이 일까지 겹친 거예요! 물론 그 애가 도련님이 저한테 관심을 가져 주기를 바랄 자격은 없죠. 그 애가 그런 식으로 저 자신과 제 신분을 잊는다는 건 정말 터무니없는 일이에요. 하지만 그 앤 자존심이며, 온당한 분별이며, 죄다 잃어버린 것 같아요. 프랭클린 도련님이 그 말을 하셨을 때, 아버지, 그 애가 저를 겁나게 했어요. 그 말이 그 애를 돌덩이로 바꿔 놓는 것 같았어요. 별안간 그 애한테 고요함이 내려앉더니, 그때부터 죽 그 앤 마치 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 제 일을 해요.\"\n\n나는 조금 마음이 편치 않아지기 시작했다. 페넬로피가 그것을 늘어놓는 그 태도에는, 나의 우월한 분별을 잠재우는 무언가가 있었다. 이제 내 생각이 그리로 향하고 보니, 나는 밤사이 프랭클린 도련님과 로재나 사이에 오간 일을 떠올렸다. 그때 그 아이는 가슴이 도려내진 듯한 얼굴이었다. 그리고 이제, 불운하게도, 그 딱한 것은 어쩔 수 없이 그 여린 자리를 또 한 번 찔린 것이다. 딱하기도 하지! 딱하기도 해!—그 아이에게 저를 정당화할 아무런 까닭도 없고, 그것을 서러워할 아무런 권리도 없다는 데서, 한층 더 딱한 노릇이었다.\n\n나는 프랭클린 도련님께 로재나에게 말을 걸겠다고 약속한 터였고, 지금이 그 약속을 지키기에 가장 알맞은 때인 듯했다.\n\n우리는 그 아이가 침실들 바깥 복도를 쓸고 있는 것을 보았는데, 파리하고 차분한 데다, 그 수수한 무명 드레스 차림이 여느 때처럼 말끔했다. 나는 그 눈에 어린 야릇한 흐릿함과 멍함을 눈여겨보았다—운 것 같은 기색이 아니라, 무언가를 너무 오래 바라본 것 같은 기색이었다. 어쩌면 그것은 제 생각이 자아낸 어떤 안개 같은 것이었는지도 모른다. 그 언저리에는 확실히, 그 아이가 이미 수백 번 수백 번 보지 않은, 바라볼 만한 대상이라곤 하나도 없었으니 말이다.\n\n\"기운 내라, 로재나!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"네 부질없는 공상으로 애태우면 못써. 프랭클린 도련님께서 전하라 하신 말씀이 있단다.\"\n\n그리하여 나는, 찾아낼 수 있는 가장 다정하고 가장 위로가 되는 말로, 그 일을 그 아이 앞에 바르게 펼쳐 놓았다. 이성(異性)에 관한 한, 여러분도 눈치채셨겠지만, 나의 원칙은 아주 엄격하다. 그러나 어쩐 일인지, 막상 여자들과 얼굴을 맞대고 보면, 나의 실천은 (인정하건대) 그에 걸맞지가 않다.\n\n\"프랭클린 도련님은 참 자상하고 사려 깊으세요. 부디 도련님께 고맙다고 전해 주세요.\" 그것이 그 아이가 내게 한 대답의 전부였다.\n\n내 딸은 이미 로재나가 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 제 일을 한다고 눈여겨본 터였다. 나는 이제 이 관찰에, 그 아이가 또한 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 듣고 말한다는 것을 덧붙였다. 나는 그 아이의 정신이 내가 그에게 한 말을 받아들일 만한 상태인지 미심쩍었다.\n\n\"정말 확실하냐, 로재나, 내 말을 알아들었어?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"정말 확실해요.\"\n\n그 아이는 내 말을 되뇌었으나, 살아 있는 여자처럼이 아니라, 기계 장치로 움직이는 존재처럼 그리했다. 그러는 내내 그 아이는 계속 비질을 했다. 나는 될 수 있는 대로 부드럽고 다정하게 그 빗자루를 치웠다.\n\n\"자, 자, 얘야!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"이건 네 답지가 않구나. 뭔가 마음에 걸리는 게 있지. 나는 네 벗이다—그리고 나는, 네가 설령 잘못을 저질렀더라도, 네 벗으로 있어 주마. 죄다 털어놓으렴, 로재나—죄다 털어놓으렴!\"\n\n내가 그런 식으로 말하면 그 아이의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해지던 때가 있었다. 이제 그 눈에서 나는 아무 변화도 볼 수 없었다.\n\n\"예,\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"죄다 털어놓을게요.\"\n\n\"마님께?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"프랭클린 도련님께?\"\n\n\"예, 프랭클린 도련님께요.\"\n\n나는 거기에 무어라 말해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다. 그 아이는 프랭클린 도련님이 내게 그에게 주라고 이르신, 그와 은밀히 이야기하지 말라는 그 주의를 알아들을 상태가 아니었다. 나는 조금씩 조심조심 더듬어 가며, 그저 프랭클린 도련님이 산책하러 나가셨다고만 일러 주었다.\n\n\"괜찮아요.\" 하고 그 아이가 대답했다. \"오늘은 프랭클린 도련님을 성가시게 하지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"어째서 마님께 말씀드리지 않고?\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"네 마음을 더는 길은, 늘 네게 다정하셨던 그 자비롭고 신앙심 깊은 마님께 말씀드리는 거란다.\"\n\n그 아이는 잠시, 마치 내 말을 제 마음속에 새겨 두려는 듯, 진지하고 흔들림 없는 눈길로 나를 바라보았다. 그러더니 내 손에서 빗자루를 빼앗아 들고, 그것을 든 채 복도를 따라 조금 떨어진 데로 천천히 옮겨 갔다.\n\n\"아니요,\" 하고 그 아이가, 비질을 이어 가며, 저 혼자 말했다. \"저는 그것보다 제 마음을 더는 더 나은 길을 알아요.\"\n\n\"그게 뭐냐?\"\n\n\"부디 제 일을 계속하게 놔둬 주세요.\"\n\n페넬로피가 그를 따라가, 도와주겠다고 나섰다.\n\n그 아이가 대답했다. \"아니. 나는 내 일을 하고 싶어. 고마워, 페넬로피.\" 그러고는 나를 돌아보았다. \"고맙습니다, 베터리지 씨.\"\n\n그 아이를 움직일 도리가 없었다—더는 할 말이 없었다. 나는 페넬로피에게 나와 함께 물러가자고 손짓했다. 우리는 그 아이를, 우리가 발견했을 때 그대로, 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 복도를 쓸고 있는 채로 두고 떠났다.\n\n\"이건 의사가 들여다봐야 할 일이다.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"내 힘으로는 어림없어.\"\n\n내 딸은 캔디 씨의 병을 내게 일깨워 주었는데, 그것은 (여러분도 기억하시겠지만) 그가 만찬 날 밤 걸린 오한 탓이었다. 그의 조수—에즈라 제닝스라는 어떤 사람—를 부를 수야 있었다, 물론. 그러나 우리 고장에서는 아무도 그에 대해 아는 것이 별로 없었다. 그는 자못 별난 사정 아래 캔디 씨에게 고용된 터였고, 옳든 그르든, 우리 중 누구도 그를 좋아하지도 미더워하지도 않았다. 프리징홀에는 다른 의사들도 있었다. 그러나 그들은 우리 집에 낯선 이들이었다. 그리고 페넬로피는, 로재나가 지금 그런 상태에서, 낯선 이들이 그에게 득보다 해를 끼치지나 않을지 미심쩍어했다.\n\n나는 마님께 말씀드릴까 생각했다. 그러나 마님이 이미 마음에 짊어지고 계신 그 근심의 무거운 짐을 떠올리니, 그 온갖 다른 시름에 이 새로운 골칫거리를 더하기가 망설여졌다. 그래도, 무언가를 하지 않을 수 없었다. 그 아이의 상태는, 내 보기에, 여간 걱정스러운 것이 아니었다—그러니 마님께 그것을 알려 드려야 마땅했다. 어지간히 내키지 않는 걸음으로, 나는 마님의 거실로 갔다. 거기엔 아무도 없었다. 마님은 레이철 아가씨와 함께 틀어박혀 계셨다. 마님이 다시 나오시기 전에는 내가 마님을 뵐 도리가 없었다.\n\n나는 앞쪽 층계의 시계가 두 시 십오 분 전을 칠 때까지 부질없이 기다렸다. 그로부터 오 분 뒤, 나는 집 밖 진입로에서 내 이름을 부르는 소리를 들었다. 나는 그 목소리를 대번에 알아챘다. 커프 부장이 프리징홀에서 돌아온 것이다.\n\n# CHAPTER XVIII\n\n현관으로 내려가다가, 나는 층계에서 부장을 만났다.\n\n우리 사이에 오간 일을 겪은 뒤라, 그에게 내가 그의 일 처리에 무슨 관심이라도 있는 티를 내는 것이 나로서는 영 마음에 거슬렸다. 그러나 나도 어쩔 수 없이, 도무지 거스를 수 없는 관심이 일었다. 내 위신은 발밑에서 무너져 내렸고, 이런 말이 튀어나왔다. \"프리징홀에서는 무슨 소식이오?\"\n\n\"인도인들을 만나 봤소.\" 하고 커프 부장이 대답했다. \"그리고 로재나가 지난 목요일 시내에서 남몰래 무엇을 샀는지도 알아냈고. 인도인들은 다음 주 수요일에 풀려날 거요. 그들이 월장석을 훔치러 이곳에 왔다는 것은, 내 마음속에도 한 점 의심이 없고, 머스웨이트 씨의 마음속에도 한 점 의심이 없소. 물론, 그들의 셈은 수요일 밤 이 집에서 일어난 일로 죄다 어그러졌지. 그러니 그 보석의 실제 실종에 대해서는 그들도 당신만큼이나 아무 상관이 없소. 허나 한 가지는 일러 둘 수 있소, 베터리지 양반—만일 우리가 월장석을 찾지 못하면, 저들이 찾아낼 거요. 당신은 그 세 요술쟁이의 이야기를 아직 다 들은 게 아니오.\"\n\n부장이 그 섬뜩한 말을 하는 참에 프랭클린 도련님이 산책에서 돌아왔다. 내가 내 호기심을 다스린 것보다 제 호기심을 더 잘 다스리며, 그는 한마디 없이 우리를 지나쳐, 집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n나로 말하자면, 이미 위신을 떨어뜨린 터라, 그 희생의 값어치를 온전히 뽑아내기로 마음먹었다. \"인도인 이야기는 그쯤 하고.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"그다음 로재나는 어찌 됐소?\"\n\n커프 부장이 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"그쪽 수수께끼는 그 어느 때보다 더 짙어졌소.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 그 아이를 프리징홀의 한 가게까지 뒤밟았소. 몰트비라는 아마포 상인이 하는 가게였지. 그 아이는 다른 어느 포목점에서도, 어느 모자점이나 양복점에서도 아무것도 사지 않았소. 그리고 몰트비네에서 산 것이라곤 롱클로스 한 필뿐이었소. 그 아이는 어떤 특정한 품질을 고르는 데 아주 까다로웠소. 양으로 말하자면, 잠옷 한 벌을 지을 만큼 샀지.\"\n\n\"누구 잠옷 말이오?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"제 것이지, 물론. 목요일 새벽 열두 시에서 세 시 사이에, 그 아이는 당신네 나머지 사람들이 죄다 잠자리에 든 사이, 월장석을 숨기는 일을 매듭지으려고 당신 아가씨의 방으로 살며시 내려간 게 틀림없소. 제 방으로 돌아가는 길에, 그 아이의 잠옷이 문의 젖은 페인트를 스쳤을 테고. 그 아이는 그 얼룩을 씻어 낼 수도 없었고, 제 아마포 목록을 온전히 채워 둘, 그것과 똑같은 다른 것을 먼저 마련하지 않고서는 그 잠옷을 안전하게 없애 버릴 수도 없었던 거요.\"\n\n\"그것이 로재나의 잠옷이었다는 걸 무엇으로 증명하오?\" 하고 내가 이의를 달았다.\n\n\"그 아이가 대체 옷을 지으려고 산 그 감이지.\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"만일 그것이 버린더 아가씨의 잠옷이었다면, 그 아이는 레이스며, 주름 장식이며, 그 밖에 뭔지 모를 것들을 사야 했을 테고, 그것을 하룻밤에 지을 시간도 없었을 거요. 소박한 롱클로스는 소박한 하인의 잠옷을 뜻하지. 아니, 아니오, 베터리지 양반—그 모든 게 어지간히 뻔하오. 이 문제의 요점은—어째서, 대체 옷을 마련해 놓고서도, 그 아이는 그 얼룩진 잠옷을 없애 버리는 대신 숨기느냐 하는 거요. 만일 그 아이가 툭 터놓지 않는다면, 이 난제를 매듭지을 길은 하나뿐이오. 흔들리는 모래의 그 숨긴 자리를 뒤져야 하오—그러면 이 사건의 참된 실상이 거기서 드러날 거요.\"\n\n\"그 자리를 어떻게 찾아낼 셈이오?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"당신을 실망시켜 미안하오만,\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"그건 내 혼자만 간직할 작정인 비밀이오.\"\n\n(그가 내 호기심을 애태웠던 것처럼 여러분의 호기심을 애태우지 않으려고, 여기서 여러분께 알려 드릴 수 있으니, 그는 수색 영장을 갖추어 프리징홀에서 돌아온 터였다. 그런 일에 대한 그의 경험이 그에게 일러 주기를, 로재나는 십중팔구 그 숨긴 자리를 적은 비망록을 몸에 지니고 다니고 있으리라는 것이었다. 형편이 바뀌고 얼마쯤 시간이 흐른 뒤에 다시 그리로 돌아갈 경우에 저를 인도해 줄 그런 비망록 말이다. 이 비망록을 손에 넣으면, 부장은 저가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추게 될 터였다.)\n\n\"자, 베터리지 양반,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"이제 억측은 그만 접고, 일에 착수합시다. 나는 조이스에게 로재나를 지켜보라고 일러 뒀소. 조이스는 어디 있소?\"\n\n조이스는, 시그레이브 경정이 커프 부장의 마음대로 쓰라고 남겨 둔 그 프리징홀 순경이었다. 그가 그 물음을 던지는 참에 시계가 두 시를 쳤다. 그리고 시각에 딱 맞추어, 레이철 아가씨를 이모 댁으로 데려갈 마차가 돌아 나왔다.\n\n\"한 번에 한 가지씩.\" 하고 부장이, 내가 조이스를 찾으러 사람을 보내려던 참에 나를 멈춰 세우며 말했다. \"먼저 버린더 아가씨부터 챙겨야 하오.\"\n\n비가 여전히 을러대고 있던 터라, 레이철 아가씨를 프리징홀로 데려가기로 정해진 것은 지붕 있는 마차였다. 커프 부장이 뒤쪽 하인석에 있던 새뮤얼에게 저에게로 내려오라고 손짓했다.\n\n\"문지기 대문 이쪽 편, 나무들 사이에 내 친구 하나가 기다리고 있는 것이 보일 거요.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"내 친구는, 마차를 세우지 않고, 자네와 함께 그 하인석에 올라탈 거요. 자네가 할 일이라곤 입을 다물고 눈을 감는 것뿐이오. 안 그러면, 자네가 곤경에 처하게 될 테니까.\"\n\n이런 당부와 함께, 그는 하인을 제자리로 돌려보냈다. 새뮤얼이 어찌 생각했는지는 나도 모른다. 내 마음에는, 레이철 아가씨가 우리 집을 떠나는 그 순간부터—정말로 떠난다면—은밀히 감시받으리라는 것이 뻔했다. 우리 아가씨께 감시가 붙다니! 제 어머니 마차 하인석에, 등 뒤에 첩자가 붙다니! 나는 커프 부장에게 말을 걸 만큼 나를 잊었던 것에 내 혀를 잘라 버리고 싶은 심정이었다.\n\n집에서 맨 처음 나온 사람은 마님이었다. 마님은 맨 위 층계 한쪽으로 비켜서서, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보려고 거기 자리를 잡으셨다. 부장에게든 내게든, 마님은 한마디도 하지 않으셨다. 입술을 굳게 다물고, 바깥공기를 쐬러 나오며 몸에 두르신 그 가벼운 정원용 망토 속에 두 팔을 낀 채, 거기 마님은, 조각상처럼 미동도 없이, 당신 따님이 나타나기를 기다리며 서 계셨다.\n\n다시 일 분쯤 뒤, 레이철 아가씨가 아래층으로 내려왔다—아가씨의 가무스름한 살빛을 돋보이게 하고, (재킷 모양으로) 허리께를 꼭 죄어 주는 어떤 부드러운 노란 감으로 아주 곱게 차려입은 채였다. 아가씨는 흰 베일을 둘러 감은 맵시 있는 조그만 밀짚모자를 머리에 쓰고 있었다. 앵초 빛깔 장갑이 마치 또 하나의 살갗처럼 아가씨 손에 꼭 맞았다. 아가씨의 아름다운 검은 머리칼은 모자 아래에서 공단처럼 매끄러워 보였다. 아가씨의 조그만 귀는 장밋빛 조개껍데기 같았다—그 각각에 진주가 하나씩 달랑거렸다. 아가씨는, 줄기 위의 백합처럼 꼿꼿하게, 그리고 하는 몸짓마다 어린 고양이처럼 나긋나긋하고 매끄럽게, 우리 쪽으로 쏜살같이 나왔다. 아가씨의 그 어여쁜 얼굴에서 내가 알아볼 수 있는 것 가운데 달라진 것이라곤, 그 눈과 그 입술뿐이었다. 아가씨의 눈은 내가 보고 싶은 것보다 더 밝고 더 사나웠다. 그리고 아가씨의 입술은 그 빛깔과 그 미소를 어찌나 남김없이 잃어버렸던지 나는 그것을 좀처럼 다시 알아보지 못했다. 아가씨는 서두르는 느닷없는 몸짓으로 제 어머니의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다. 아가씨는 \"저를 용서하려 애써 주세요, 엄마.\" 하고 말했다—그러고는 제 베일을 얼굴 위로 어찌나 거세게 끌어내렸던지 그것을 찢고 말았다. 다시 한순간에 아가씨는 층계를 뛰어 내려가, 마치 그것이 숨을 자리라도 되는 듯 마차 안으로 뛰어들었다.\n\n커프 부장도 저대로 꼭 그만큼 재발랐다. 그는 새뮤얼을 뒤로 물리고, 아가씨가 제자리에 앉는 바로 그 순간, 열린 마차 문을 손에 쥔 채 레이철 아가씨 앞에 섰다.\n\n\"뭘 바라죠?\" 하고 레이철 아가씨가 제 베일 뒤에서 말한다.\n\n\"아가씨께 한마디 여쭙고 싶습니다, 아가씨.\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"떠나시기 전에요. 저는 감히 아가씨가 이모 댁을 방문하시는 것을 막을 수 없습니다. 다만 이 말씀만은 감히 드리지요, 지금 이런 마당에 아가씨가 우리를 떠나시는 것은, 제가 아가씨의 다이아몬드를 되찾는 길에 걸림돌을 놓는 것이라고요. 부디 그것을 헤아려 주십시오. 그러고 나서, 가실지 머무실지는 아가씨 스스로 정하십시오.\"\n\n레이철 아가씨는 그에게 대꾸조차 하지 않았다. \"출발해요, 제임스!\" 하고 아가씨가 마부에게 외쳤다.\n\n더 말 한마디 없이, 부장은 마차 문을 닫았다. 그가 그것을 막 닫는 참에, 프랭클린 도련님이 층계를 뛰어 내려왔다. \"잘 가, 레이철.\" 하고 그가 손을 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"출발해요!\" 하고 레이철 아가씨가, 그 어느 때보다 큰 소리로 외쳤다. 커프 부장에게 그랬던 것 이상으로 프랭클린 도련님께도 아랑곳하지 않고 말이다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님은, 그럴 만도 했으니, 벼락 맞은 듯 뒷걸음질 쳤다. 마부는, 어찌해야 할지 몰라, 여전히 맨 위 층계에 꿈쩍 않고 서 계시던 마님 쪽을 바라보았다. 마님은, 노여움과 서러움과 부끄러움이 얼굴에서 죄다 뒤엉키는 채로, 마부에게 말을 몰라는 신호를 보내시고는, 부랴부랴 집 안으로 돌아 들어가셨다. 프랭클린 도련님은, 말문을 되찾고, 마차가 떠나갈 때 마님을 향해 뒤에서 외쳤다. \"이모님! 이모님 말씀이 정말 옳았습니다. 이모님의 그 온갖 자상함에 대한 제 감사를 받아 주십시오—그리고 저를 떠나게 해 주십시오.\"\n\n마님이 그에게 무슨 말씀을 하시려는 듯 돌아서셨다. 그러더니, 마치 당신 자신을 못 미더워하듯, 다정하게 손을 흔드셨다. \"떠나기 전에 나를 보고 가거라, 프랭클린.\" 하고 마님이 잠긴 목소리로 말씀하시고는—당신 방으로 가셨다.\n\n\"내게 마지막 부탁 하나만 들어주게, 베터리지.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 내게 돌아서며 말한다. \"될 수 있는 대로 빨리 나를 기차 편으로 떠나보내 주게!\"\n\n그도 제 갈 길로 집 안에 들어갔다. 그 순간, 레이철 아가씨가 그의 사내다움을 아주 무너뜨려 놓은 것이다. 그것으로 미루어 보시라, 그가 얼마나 아가씨를 아꼈던 것인지를!\n\n커프 부장과 나만이, 층계 밑에서 얼굴을 마주하고 남겨졌다. 부장은, 집에서 뻗어 나온 그 진입로의 굽이 하나가 내다보이는 나무들 사이의 틈을 향해 얼굴을 두고 서 있었다. 그는 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고, 혼자 나직이 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 휘파람으로 불고 있었다.\n\n\"매사에는 때가 있는 법이오.\" 하고 나는 제법 사납게 말했다. \"지금은 휘파람 불 때가 아니오.\"\n\n바로 그 순간, 마차가 그 틈 사이로, 저 멀리, 문지기 대문을 향해 가는 모습으로 나타났다. 뒤쪽 하인석에는, 새뮤얼 말고도, 또 한 사내가 뚜렷이 보였다.\n\n\"됐군!\" 하고 부장이 혼자 말했다. 그가 내게로 돌아섰다. \"당신 말대로, 휘파람 불 때가 아니오, 베터리지 양반. 이제, 누구도 봐주지 않고, 이 일에 착수할 때요. 로재나 스피어먼부터 시작합시다. 조이스는 어디 있소?\"\n\n우리 둘 다 조이스를 불렀으나, 아무 대답도 없었다. 나는 마구간지기 아이 하나를 보내 그를 찾게 했다.\n\n\"내가 버린더 아가씨께 뭐라 했는지 들었지요?\" 하고 부장이, 우리가 기다리는 동안 말했다. \"그리고 아가씨가 그것을 어떻게 받아들이는지 봤고? 나는 아가씨께, 우리를 떠나시는 것이 내가 아가씨 다이아몬드를 되찾는 길에 걸림돌이 되리라고 뻔히 말했소—그런데도 아가씨는 그 말을 뻔히 듣고서 떠나셨소! 당신 아가씨는 제 어머니 마차에 길동무를 하나 두셨소, 베터리지 양반—그리고 그 이름은, 월장석이오.\"\n\n나는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 나는 그저 죽기 살기로 레이철 아가씨에 대한 내 믿음을 붙들고 있었다.\n\n마구간지기 아이가 돌아왔는데, 그 뒤로—내 보기엔 아주 마지못한 걸음으로—조이스가 따라왔다.\n\n\"로재나 스피어먼은 어디 있소?\" 하고 커프 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"영문을 모르겠습니다, 나리.\" 하고 조이스가 말문을 열었다. \"정말 죄송합니다. 하지만 어쩌다 보니—\"\n\n\"내가 프리징홀에 가기 전에,\" 하고 부장이 그의 말을 자르며 말했다. \"나는 자네에게, 로재나 스피어먼이 감시받고 있다는 것을 눈치채지 못하게 하면서, 그를 지켜보라고 일렀소. 그러니까 자네는, 그가 자네를 따돌리게 놔뒀다고 내게 말하려는 거요?\"\n\n\"아무래도, 나리,\" 하고 조이스가 떨기 시작하며 말한다. \"제가 그에게 들키지 않으려고 어쩌면 좀 지나치게 조심했던 것 같습니다. 이 집 아래층에는 통로가 어찌나 많은지—\"\n\n\"그를 놓친 지 얼마나 됐소?\"\n\n\"거의 한 시간쯤 됐습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"자네는 프리징홀에서 자네 본연의 업무로 돌아가도 좋소.\" 하고 부장이, 그 여느 조용하고 처량한 투로, 여전히 꼭 그대로 태연하게 말했다. \"내 보기에 자네 재능은 우리 쪽 일과는 도무지 맞지 않소, 조이스 씨. 지금 자네가 맡은 이 일은 자네에게 조금 벅차구려. 좋은 아침이오.\"\n\n그 사내는 슬금슬금 물러갔다. 로재나 스피어먼이 사라졌다는 사실에 내가 어떤 심정이었는지는 참으로 묘사하기 어렵다. 나는 그것에 대해, 죄다 한꺼번에, 쉰 가지 서로 다른 마음이 든 듯싶었다. 그런 상태로, 나는 커프 부장을 빤히 쳐다보며 서 있었다—그리고 나의 말재간은 아주 나를 저버렸다.\n\n\"아니오, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 부장이, 마치 내 안의 맨 위에 있는 생각을 알아채고, 그것을 나머지 모두에 앞서 답해 주려고 골라낸 듯 말했다. \"당신의 젊은 벗, 로재나는, 당신이 여기는 것처럼 그리 쉽게 내 손가락 사이로 빠져나가지 못할 거요. 버린더 아가씨가 어디 계신지를 내가 아는 한, 나는 버린더 아가씨의 공범을 뒤밟을 방도를 손에 쥐고 있는 셈이오. 나는 어젯밤 그들이 통하는 것을 막았소. 아주 좋소. 그들은 여기서 만나는 대신, 프리징홀에서 만날 거요. 지금 이 조사는 그저 (내가 예상했던 것보다는 다소 이르게) 이 집에서, 버린더 아가씨가 방문하고 계신 그 집으로 옮겨 가야 하는 것뿐이오. 그러는 동안, 미안하오만 당신에게 하인들을 다시 불러 모으는 수고를 끼쳐야겠소.\"\n\n나는 그와 함께 하인 방으로 돌아갔다. 참으로 창피한 노릇이나, 그렇다고 덜 참된 것도 아니니, 그가 그 마지막 말을 했을 때 나는 또 한 번 탐정 열병 발작을 일으켰다. 나는 내가 커프 부장을 미워한다는 것을 잊었다. 나는 은밀히 그의 팔을 움켜쥐었다. 나는 말했다. \"제발 부탁이오, 이제 하인들을 데리고 뭘 하려는 건지 우리에게 말해 주시오.\"\n\n그 위대한 커프는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 일종의 우울한 황홀경에 잠겨 텅 빈 허공을 향해 말했다.\n\n\"이 사람이,\" 하고 부장이 (아무래도 나를 두고 하는 말이었다) \"장미 재배만 이해한다면 천지 만물의 얼굴 위에서 가장 완벽하게 온전한 인물이 될 텐데!\" 그런 강렬한 감정 표현을 하고 나서, 그는 한숨을 쉬고, 제 팔을 내 팔에 꿰었다. \"사정은 이렇소.\" 하고 그가, 다시 일 이야기로 내려앉으며 말했다. \"로재나는 둘 중 하나를 한 거요. 곧장 프리징홀로 갔든지 (내가 거기 도착하기 전에), 아니면 먼저 흔들리는 모래에 있는 제 숨긴 자리를 살피러 갔든지. 맨 먼저 알아내야 할 것은, 그 아이가 집을 떠나기 전에 하인들 중 누가 그를 마지막으로 봤느냐 하는 거요.\"\n\n이 조사를 벌이고 보니, 로재나를 마지막으로 눈에 담은 사람은 부엌데기 낸시임이 드러났다.\n\n낸시는 그가 손에 편지 한 장을 들고 살며시 나가, 마침 뒷문으로 고기를 얼마쯤 배달하고 있던 푸줏간 사내를 붙잡는 것을 보았다. 낸시는 그가 그 사내에게 프리징홀에 돌아가거든 그 편지를 부쳐 달라고 청하는 소리를 들었다. 그 사내는 겉봉을 보더니, 코브스 홀로 가는 편지를 프리징홀에서 부친다는 건 에두르는 배달 방식이라고—게다가, 토요일이니, 이 편지가 월요일 아침에나 목적지에 닿게 될 거라고 말했다. 로재나는 편지 배달이 월요일까지 늦어지는 것은 대수롭지 않다고 대답했다. 그가 확실히 해 두고 싶은 단 한 가지는, 그 사내가 저가 이른 대로 해 주리라는 것뿐이었다. 그 사내는 그러겠다고 약속하고, 마차를 몰고 떠났다. 낸시는 부엌의 제 일로 도로 불려 갔다. 그리고 그 뒤로 다른 어느 누구도 로재나 스피어먼을 다시 보지 못했다.\n\n\"그래서요?\" 하고 나는 우리 둘만 다시 남았을 때 물었다.\n\n\"그래서,\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"나는 프리징홀에 가야 하오.\"\n\n\"그 편지 때문에요, 나리?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그 숨긴 자리의 비망록이 그 편지 안에 있소. 나는 우체국에서 그 겉봉을 봐야 하오. 만일 그것이 내가 짐작하는 그 겉봉이라면, 나는 다음 월요일에 우리 벗 욜랜드 부인을 다시 한번 찾아갈 거요.\"\n\n나는 부장과 함께 조랑말 이륜마차를 대령하라 이르러 갔다. 마구간 뜰에서 우리는 그 사라진 아이에 대한 새로운 실마리를 얻었다.\n\n# CHAPTER XIX\n\n로재나가 사라졌다는 소식은, 보아하니, 바깥일을 하는 하인들 사이에도 이미 퍼져 있었다. 그들 역시 나름대로 수소문을 해 본 터라, 마침 \"더피\"라는 별명으로 불리는 잽싼 꼬마 녀석 하나를 붙들어 온 참이었다—이따금 정원의 잡초를 뽑는 일에 부려지곤 하던 아이로, 불과 반 시간 전에 로재나 스피어먼을 본 아이였다. 더피는 그 아이가 전나무 숲에서 저를 지나쳤는데, 걷는 것이 아니라 바닷가 쪽으로 뛰어가고 있었다고 딱 잘라 말했다.\n\n\"이 아이가 이 근방 해안을 아오?\" 하고 커프 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"이 해안에서 나고 자란 아이오.\" 하고 내가 대답했다.\n\n\"더피!\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"너, 한 실링 벌고 싶으냐? 그러거든, 나를 따라오너라. 베터리지 양반, 내가 돌아올 때까지 조랑말 이륜마차를 대기시켜 두시오.\"\n\n그는 흔들리는 모래를 향해, 내 다리로는 (내 나이치고는 제법 잘 간수해 온 다리이건만) 도무지 따라잡을 가망이 없는 속도로 내달렸다. 꼬마 더피는, 우리 고장의 어린 야만족들이 신이 났을 때 흔히 그러하듯, 한바탕 괴성을 지르고는 부장의 발뒤꿈치를 좇아 종종걸음으로 달려 나갔다.\n\n여기서도 또, 커프 부장이 우리를 떠난 뒤의 그 동안 내 마음이 어떤 상태였는지를 조금이라도 또렷이 적어 낸다는 것은 나로서는 도저히 못 할 노릇이다. 야릇하고 얼을 빼는 안절부절이 나를 사로잡았다. 나는 집 안팎에서 열두 가지도 넘는 부질없는 짓들을 했는데, 그중 단 하나도 지금은 떠올릴 수가 없다. 부장이 모래펄로 떠난 지 얼마나 지났을 때 더피가 내게 전할 말을 들고 뛰어 돌아왔는지조차 나는 모른다. 커프 부장은 그 아이에게 제 수첩에서 찢어 낸 종잇장 하나를 주었는데, 거기에는 연필로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"로재나 스피어먼의 장화 한 짝을 내게 보내 주시오, 그리고 서두르시오.\"\n\n나는 맨 처음 눈에 띈 하녀를 로재나의 방으로 급히 보냈다. 그리고 그 아이는, 내가 몸소 그 장화를 들고 뒤따라가겠다는 말을 전하라고 돌려보냈다.\n\n이것이, 나도 잘 알거니와, 내가 받은 지시를 따르는 데 가장 빠른 길은 아니었다. 그러나 나는 로재나의 장화를 부장의 손에 맡기기 전에, 이번에는 또 무슨 알쏭달쏭한 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 내 눈으로 직접 보리라 마음먹었다. 할 수만 있다면 그 아이를 감싸 주려던 내 오랜 심산이, 이 막판에 이르러 다시금 내게로 되돌아온 듯싶었다. 이런 심사가 (탐정 열병은 말할 것도 없고) 나로 하여금, 장화를 손에 넣기가 무섭게, 일흔 고개를 넘긴 사람이 그런대로 바랄 수 있는 가장 뜀박질에 가까운 걸음으로 부랴부랴 떠나게 했다.\n\n해안 가까이 다다르자, 구름이 시커멓게 몰려들었고, 비가 쏟아져 내리며, 바람 앞에서 거대한 흰 물의 장막이 되어 흩날렸다. 만 어귀의 모래톱에 부딪는 바다의 우렛소리가 들려왔다. 조금 더 나아가서, 나는 모래 언덕의 바람 그늘에 웅크려 비를 피하고 있는 그 아이를 지나쳤다. 그러고 나서 나는 미쳐 날뛰는 바다와, 모래톱으로 밀려 굴러드는 큰 물결과, 흩날리는 옷자락처럼 물 위를 휩쓸어 가는 몰아치는 빗줄기와, 그 위에 홀로 검은 형상 하나가 서 있는 그 누런 황무지 같은 백사장을 보았다—커프 부장의 형상이었다.\n\n그는 나를 처음 알아보고는, 북쪽을 향해 손을 내저었다. \"그쪽으로 오시오!\" 하고 그가 소리쳤다. \"그리고 여기 내게로 내려오시오!\"\n\n나는, 숨이 턱턱 막히고, 심장이 마치 몸 밖으로 튀어나올 듯 펄떡거리는 채로, 그에게로 내려갔다. 나는 말이 나오지 않는 지경이었다. 그에게 물을 것이 백 가지나 있었건만, 그중 단 하나도 내 입 밖으로 나오려 하지 않았다. 그의 얼굴이 나를 오싹하게 했다. 나는 그의 눈에서 어떤 표정을 보았는데, 그것은 공포의 표정이었다. 그는 내 손에서 장화를 낚아채더니, 우리가 선 자리에서 남쪽으로 뻗어, 사우스 스핏이라 불리는 그 바위 턱을 곧장 가리키고 있는 모래 위의 한 발자국에 그것을 맞추어 놓았다. 그 자국은 아직 빗물에 지워지지 않은 채였다—그리고 그 아이의 장화가 거기에 털끝만큼도 어긋남 없이 꼭 들어맞았다.\n\n부장은, 한마디 말도 없이, 그 발자국에 놓인 장화를 손으로 가리켰다.\n\n나는 그의 팔을 붙들고, 그에게 말을 걸어 보려 했으나, 아까 애썼을 때 그러했던 것처럼 또 뜻을 이루지 못했다. 그는, 그 발자국을 좇아, 바위와 모래가 맞닿는 곳까지 자꾸자꾸 내려갔다. 사우스 스핏은 밀려드는 밀물에 막 잠기려는 참이었다. 물결이 흔들리는 모래의 그 숨은 낯 위로 넘실거렸다. 이쪽으로 갔다 저쪽으로 갔다, 보기에도 소름 끼치는 그 끈질긴 참을성으로, 커프 부장은 그 발자국마다 장화를 맞추어 보았고, 그때마다 그것이 한결같이 같은 방향을—곧장 바위 쪽을 가리키고 있음을 알아냈다. 아무리 뒤져 보아도, 그 바위에서 걸어 나온 발자국의 자취는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었다.\n\n그는 마침내 단념했다. 여전히 잠자코, 그는 다시 나를 바라보았다. 그러고 나서, 우리 앞에서 유사 위로 점점 더 깊이 넘실거리며 밀려드는 바닷물을 내다보았다. 나는 그가 보는 곳을 보았다—그리고 나는 그의 얼굴에서 그의 생각을 읽었다. 소름 끼치는 말없는 떨림이 별안간 내 온몸을 타고 기어올랐다. 나는 백사장 위에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다.\n\n\"그 아이가 숨긴 자리로 되돌아갔던 거로군.\" 하고 부장이 혼잣말하는 소리가 내게 들렸다. \"저 바위에서 무슨 끔찍한 변을 당한 게야.\"\n\n불과 몇 시간 전 복도를 쓸고 있던 그 아이를 찾아냈을 때의, 그 달라진 낯빛과, 말과, 몸짓—얼이 빠지고 넋이 나간 듯 내 말을 듣고 내게 말하던 그 모양이 내 마음속에 떠올라, 부장이 그 말을 하는 바로 그 순간에도, 그의 짐작이 그 끔찍한 진실과는 한참 어긋나 있음을 내게 일러 주었다. 나는 나를 얼어붙게 한 그 두려움을 그에게 말하려 했다. 나는 \"그 아이가 맞은 죽음은, 부장, 제 스스로 찾아 나선 죽음이오.\" 하고 말하려 했다. 아니! 말이 나오지 않았다. 그 말없는 떨림이 나를 그 손아귀에 옭아쥐고 있었다. 나는 몰아치는 비를 느낄 수도 없었다. 나는 차오르는 밀물을 볼 수도 없었다. 마치 꿈속의 환영처럼, 그 가엾이 스러진 것이 내 앞에 되돌아왔다. 나는 지난 시절 보았던 그대로 그 아이를 다시 보았다—내가 그 아이를 집으로 데려오려고 갔던 그 아침의 모습을. 나는, 흔들리는 모래가 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 저를 그리로 끌어당기는 것만 같다고, 그리고 제 무덤이 저기서 저를 기다리고 있는 것은 아닐까 하던 그 아이의 말을 다시 들었다. 그 소름 끼침이, 어떤 헤아릴 길 없는 방식으로, 내 제 자식을 통해 나를 후려쳤다. 내 딸도 꼭 그 아이 나이였다. 내 딸도, 로재나가 시달린 것처럼 시달렸더라면, 그 비참한 삶을 살고, 이 끔찍한 죽음을 맞았을지도 모를 일이었다.\n\n부장은 다정하게 나를 일으켜 세우고는, 그 아이가 스러진 그 자리가 보이지 않도록 나를 돌려세웠다.\n\n그 덕에 한숨 돌리자, 나는 다시 숨을 고르고, 내 둘레의 것들을, 있는 그대로 보기 시작했다. 모래 언덕 쪽을 바라보니, 바깥일을 하는 사내 하인들과, 욜랜드라는 이름의 그 어부가, 다 함께 우리 쪽으로 뛰어 내려오는 것이 보였다. 그리고 다들, 소동에 놀라, 그 아이를 찾았느냐고 외쳐 묻고 있었다. 아주 짧은 몇 마디로, 부장은 그들에게 그 발자국이라는 증거를 보여 주고, 그 아이가 끔찍한 변을 당한 게 틀림없다고 일러 주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 그 나머지 가운데서 그 어부를 골라내어, 다시 바다 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 그에게 물음 하나를 던졌다. \"말해 보시오.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이런 날씨에, 그 아이의 발자국이 멎은 저 바위에서, 배 한 척이 그 아이를 태워 갈 수 있었겠소?\"\n\n그 어부는 모래톱으로 밀려 굴러드는 큰 물결과, 우리 양편의 곶에 부딪혀 물거품 구름을 일으키며 솟구쳐 오르는 커다란 파도를 손으로 가리켰다.\n\n\"여태 만들어진 그 어떤 배도,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"저것을 뚫고 그 아이에게 다다르지는 못했을 겁니다.\"\n\n커프 부장은, 비가 이제 빠르게 지워 가고 있는 모래 위의 그 발자국을 마지막으로 한 번 바라보았다.\n\n\"저기,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저것이 그 아이가 뭍으로는 이곳을 떠날 수 없었다는 증거요. 그리고 여기,\" 하고 그가, 그 어부를 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"여기 있는 것이 그 아이가 바다로는 빠져나갈 수 없었다는 증거고.\" 그는 말을 멈추고, 잠시 헤아려 보았다. \"그 아이가 이곳으로 뛰어오는 것이 목격된 것이, 내가 집에서 여기 다다르기 반 시간 전이오.\" 하고 그가 욜랜드에게 말했다. \"그 뒤로 얼마쯤 시간이 흘렀고. 다 쳐서, 한 시간 전이라고 칩시다. 그때, 이쪽 바위 편의 물이 얼마나 높았겠소?\" 그는 남쪽 편을—다시 말해, 유사에 메워지지 않은 그 편을 가리켰다.\n\n\"오늘 밀물 드는 형편으로 보아서는,\" 하고 그 어부가 말했다. \"한 시간 전이라면, 스핏 그쪽 편에는 새끼 고양이 한 마리 빠뜨려 죽일 만한 물도 없었을 겁니다.\"\n\n커프 부장은 북쪽으로, 유사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n\"이쪽 편은 얼마나 됐겠소?\" 하고 그가 물었다.\n\n\"그보다 더 적었지요.\" 하고 욜랜드가 대답했다. \"흔들리는 모래는 그저 물에 겨우 잠길락 말락 했을 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었을 겁니다.\"\n\n부장은 내게로 돌아서서, 그 변고가 유사 편에서 일어난 게 틀림없다고 말했다. 그 말에 내 혀가 풀렸다. \"변고가 아니오!\" 하고 내가 그에게 말했다. \"그 아이가 이곳에 왔을 때, 그 아이는 제 삶에 지쳐, 여기서 그것을 끝내려고 온 거요.\"\n\n그는 나로부터 흠칫 물러섰다. \"어떻게 아시오?\" 하고 그가 물었다. 나머지 사람들이 우르르 둘러쌌다. 부장은 이내 마음을 다잡았다. 그는 그들을 내게서 물러서게 하고, 나더러 늙은이라고 했고, 이 발견이 나를 뒤흔들어 놓은 거라고 했고, \"잠시 이 사람을 가만 놔두시오.\" 하고 말했다. 그러고 나서 그는 욜랜드에게로 돌아서서 물었다. \"밀물이 다시 빠지면, 그 아이를 찾아낼 가망이 조금이라도 있소?\" 그러자 욜랜드가 대답했다. \"없습니다. 저 모래가 삼킨 것은, 저 모래가 영영 간직하지요.\" 그 말을 하고 나서, 그 어부는 한 걸음 더 가까이 다가와, 내게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"베터리지 양반,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그 젊은 여자의 죽음에 대해 한마디 드릴 말씀이 있소. 스핏 옆으로, 옆으로 나란히 네 자쯤 나간 곳에, 모래 밑으로 반 길쯤 되는 데에 바위 선반이 하나 있소. 내 물음은 이거요—어째서 그 여자는 거기에 부딪지 않았느냐 하는 거요. 만일 그 여자가, 어쩌다, 스핏에서 미끄러졌다면, 밑바닥에 발 디딜 데가 있는 곳으로, 기껏해야 허리께나 잠길 깊이에 빠졌을 거요. 그 여자는 그 너머의 깊은 데로 걸어 들어갔거나, 뛰어들었음이 틀림없소—안 그랬다면 지금 이렇게 자취가 없을 리가 없지. 변고가 아니오, 양반! 유사의 깊은 데가 그 여자를 삼킨 거요. 그리고 제 스스로 한 짓으로 그리된 거요.\"\n\n그 방면의 앎을 믿고 기댈 만한 사람의 그런 증언이 있고 나자, 부장은 말이 없었다. 우리 나머지도, 그와 마찬가지로, 입을 다물었다. 한마음으로, 우리는 다 함께 백사장 비탈을 되짚어 올라갔다.\n\n그 조그만 모래 언덕들께에서 우리는, 집에서 우리 쪽으로 뛰어오는 아래 마부와 마주쳤다. 그 젊은이는 착한 젊은이로, 나를 참되게 우러르는 아이였다. 그는, 얼굴에 얌전한 슬픔을 띤 채, 내게 조그만 쪽지 하나를 건넸다. \"페넬로피가 이걸 저더러 갖다 드리라 했습니다, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"로재나의 방에서 찾아냈다고요.\"\n\n그것은, 그 아이의 벗이 되어 주려고 제 힘을 다한—하느님 고맙게도, 늘 제 힘을 다한—그 늙은이에게 남긴 그 아이의 마지막 작별 인사였다.\n\n\"지난날, 베터리지 양반은 저를 여러 번 용서해 주셨지요. 다음번에 흔들리는 모래를 보시거든, 부디 한 번만 더 저를 용서해 주세요. 저는 제 무덤이 저를 기다리고 있던 그 자리에서 제 무덤을 찾았습니다. 저는, 양반의 그 자상함에 고마워하며, 살았고, 또 죽습니다.\"\n\n그것뿐, 더는 없었다. 그 짧은 것에도, 나는 견뎌 낼 만한 사내다움이 없었다. 젊어서, 세상을 막 시작할 적에는, 눈물이 쉬이 나는 법이다. 늙어서, 세상을 떠나갈 적에도, 눈물이 쉬이 나는 법이다. 나는 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다.\n\n커프 부장이 내게로 한 걸음 다가섰다—다정한 마음에서였음을, 나는 의심치 않는다. 나는 그로부터 움츠러들어 물러섰다. \"내게 손대지 마시오.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"그 아이를 이 지경으로 내몬 것은, 바로 당신에 대한 두려움이오.\"\n\n\"당신이 틀렸소, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 그가 조용히 대답했다. \"허나 그 이야기는 우리가 다시 집 안에 들거든 할 겨를이 넉넉할 거요.\"\n\n나는, 그 마부의 팔에 의지하여, 나머지 사람들을 뒤따랐다. 몰아치는 빗속을 뚫고 우리는 되돌아갔다—집에서 우리를 기다리고 있던 그 근심과 그 공포를 맞이하러.\n\n# CHAPTER XX\n\n앞서 간 사람들이 우리보다 먼저 그 소식을 퍼뜨려 놓은 터였다. 우리가 돌아와 보니 하인들은 겁에 질려 우왕좌왕하고 있었다. 우리가 마님의 방문 앞을 지나는데, 그 문이 안쪽에서 거칠게 벌컥 열렸다. 마님이 (프랭클린 도련님이 뒤따르며 헛되이 마님을 진정시키려 애쓰는 채로) 우리 가운데로 나오셨는데, 이 일의 그 끔찍함에 아주 제정신이 아니셨다.\n\n\"이 일은 그대에게 책임이 있소!\" 하고 마님이, 손으로 부장을 사납게 위협하며 외치셨다. \"게이브리얼! 저 몹쓸 사람에게 삯을 치러 주게—그리고 저 사람 꼴을 내 눈앞에서 치워 주게!\"\n\n우리 가운데 마님을 감당할 만한 사람은 부장뿐이었다—우리 가운데 저를 온전히 다스리고 있는 사람이 그뿐이었으니.\n\n\"이 애처로운 참사에 대해 제게 책임이 없기로는, 마님, 마님께 책임이 없는 것과 매한가지입니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"만일, 지금부터 반 시간 뒤에도, 마님께서 여전히 제가 이 집을 떠나기를 고집하신다면, 저는 마님의 해고는 받아들이겠으나, 마님의 돈은 받지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n그것은 아주 공손하게, 그러나 동시에 아주 단호하게 뱉어진 말이었다—그리고 그것은 나에게 그러하였듯 마님께도 효험을 냈다. 마님은 프랭클린 도련님이 당신을 방 안으로 도로 이끌어 들이도록 순순히 따르셨다. 그 둘 위로 문이 닫히자, 부장은, 그 눈여겨보는 버릇으로 하녀들 사이를 둘러보다가, 나머지가 모두 그저 겁을 먹고 있을 뿐인데 페넬로피만은 눈물을 흘리고 있음을 알아챘다. \"네 아버지가 젖은 옷을 갈아입거든,\" 하고 그가 그 아이에게 말했다. \"네 아버지 방으로 와서, 우리와 이야기 좀 하자.\"\n\n반 시간이 다 가기 전에, 나는 마른 옷을 갈아입었고, 커프 부장에게도 그가 필요로 하는 만큼 갈아입을 옷을 빌려주었다. 페넬로피가, 부장이 저에게 무슨 볼일이 있는지 들으러, 우리에게로 들어왔다. 나는 그 순간만큼 내가 얼마나 착하고 효성스러운 딸을 두었는지를 그토록 사무치게 느껴 본 적이 없는 듯싶다. 나는 그 아이를 데려다 내 무릎에 앉히고, 하느님께서 그 아이에게 복 내리시기를 빌었다. 그 아이는 내 가슴에 제 머리를 파묻고, 두 팔로 내 목을 감았다—그리고 우리는 잠시 말없이 기다렸다. 그 가엾이 죽은 아이가, 내 딸과 내 마음 밑바닥에 함께 자리하고 있었으리라, 나는 생각한다. 부장은 창가로 가서, 거기 서서 바깥을 내다보았다. 나는 그가 이렇게 우리 둘을 헤아려 준 것에 대해 고마움을 표하는 것이 도리라 여겼다—그래서 그리했다.\n\n지체 높은 사람들은 온갖 호사를 저희끼리만 누린다—그중에서도, 제 감정을 마음껏 풀어 놓는 호사 말이다. 지체 낮은 사람들에게는 그런 특권이 없다. 우리보다 나은 이들은 봐주는 그 필연이라는 것이, 우리에게는 도무지 자비가 없다. 우리는 제 감정을 도로 제 안으로 밀어 넣고, 될 수 있는 대로 참을성 있게 제 소임을 꾸역꾸역 해 나가는 법을 배운다. 나는 이를 두고 불평하는 것이 아니다—다만 이를 짚어 둘 뿐이다. 페넬로피와 나는, 부장이 저쪽 채비를 갖추기가 무섭게, 부장을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었다. 무엇이 제 동료 하인을 스스로 목숨을 끊게 했는지 아느냐는 물음에, 내 딸은 (여러분도 미리 짐작하시겠지만) 그것이 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님에 대한 사랑 때문이었다고 대답했다. 이어서, 이 제 생각을 다른 누구에게 말한 적이 있느냐는 물음에, 페넬로피는 \"저는 로재나를 생각해서, 말하지 않았습니다.\" 하고 대답했다. 나는 여기에 한마디 보태는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈다. 나는 말했다. \"그리고 프랭클린 도련님을 생각해서도 그런 거지, 얘야. 만일 로재나가 정말로 그분을 사랑해서 죽은 거라 해도, 그것은 그분이 아셔서도 아니고 그분 잘못으로도 아니다. 그분이 오늘 이 집을 떠나신다면, 그 진실을 아는 부질없는 아픔 없이 떠나시게 하자꾸나.\" 커프 부장은 \"지당한 말이오.\" 하고 말하고는, 다시 잠잠해졌다. 페넬로피의 생각을 (내 보기에) 저 혼자만 간직한 어떤 딴 생각과 견주어 보면서 말이다.\n\n반 시간이 다 될 무렵, 마님의 초인종이 울렸다.\n\n그것에 응하러 가는 길에, 나는 이모님의 거실에서 나오는 프랭클린 도련님과 마주쳤다. 그는 마님께서 커프 부장을 만날—전처럼 내가 배석한 가운데—채비가 되셨다고 알려 주었고, 그러면서 저 자신도 먼저 부장에게 두어 마디 하고 싶다고 덧붙였다. 내 방으로 되돌아가는 길에, 그는 걸음을 멈추고, 현관에 걸린 기차 시각표를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"정말로 저희를 떠나시렵니까, 도련님?\" 하고 내가 물었다. \"시간만 좀 드리면, 레이철 아가씨도 분명 다시 마음을 돌리실 텐데요?\"\n\n\"레이철은 다시 마음을 돌릴 걸세.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"내가 떠나 버렸다는 것을, 그리고 다시는 나를 볼 일이 없다는 것을 그녀가 듣게 되면 말이야.\"\n\n나는 그가 우리 아가씨가 저를 대한 처사에 대한 노여움에서 그리 말하는 줄로 알았다. 그러나 그렇지가 않았다. 마님은, 경찰이 처음 이 집에 든 그때부터, 그의 이름을 그저 입에 올리는 것만으로도 레이철 아가씨의 성미를 불같이 돋우기에 넉넉하다는 것을 눈여겨보아 오셨다. 그는 제 사촌 누이를 너무나 아꼈던 터라, 아가씨가 이모 댁으로 마차를 몰고 떠나 그 진실이 억지로 그에게 들이밀어질 때까지는, 이것을 저 자신에게 털어놓기를 꺼렸다. 여러분도 아시는 그 모진 방식으로 그의 눈이 한번 뜨이고 나자, 프랭클린 도련님은 결심을 굳혔다—조금이라도 기개 있는 사내라면 능히 내릴 수 있는 그 유일한 결심을—이 집을 떠나겠다는 결심을.\n\n그가 부장에게 하고자 한 말은 내가 배석한 가운데 뱉어졌다. 그는 마님께서 당신이 너무 성급하게 말씀하셨음을 기꺼이 인정하려 하신다고 전했다. 그리고 그는 커프 부장이—그렇다면—제 삯을 받고, 다이아몬드 일은 지금 그대로 놓아두는 데 동의해 주겠느냐고 물었다. 부장이 대답했다. \"아니오, 도련님. 제 삯은 제 소임을 다한 값으로 치러지는 겁니다. 제 소임이 끝나기 전에는, 저는 그것을 받기를 사양하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\" 하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다.\n\n\"제 뜻을 풀어 드리지요, 도련님.\" 하고 부장이 말한다. \"제가 이곳에 왔을 때, 저는 사라진 다이아몬드 일에 필요한 빛을 비추어 밝히겠노라 떠맡았습니다. 저는 이제 채비가 되었고, 제 약조를 지키려고 기다리고 있는 참입니다. 지금 사정이 놓인 그대로 이 사건을 레이디 버린더께 아뢰고, 월장석을 되찾으려면 어떤 행보를 취해야 하는지를 마님께 명백히 말씀드리고 나면, 그 책임은 제 어깨에서 벗어납니다. 그 뒤에, 제가 계속하도록 두실지 마실지는, 마님께서 정하시게 하십시오. 그러면 저는 제가 떠맡은 일을 다한 것이 됩니다—그리고 저는 제 삯을 받겠습니다.\"\n\n그 말로써 커프 부장은, 탐정 경찰에게조차, 사내에게는 잃을 명성이라는 것이 있을 수 있음을 우리에게 일깨워 주었다.\n\n그가 취한 견해가 어찌나 뻔히 옳은 것이었던지, 더 할 말이 없었다. 내가 그를 마님의 방으로 안내하려고 일어서자, 그는 프랭클린 도련님이 배석하기를 바라느냐고 물었다. 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"레이디 버린더께서 바라지 않으신다면, 그럴 것 없소.\" 그는, 내가 부장을 따라 나가려는 참에, 나직이 내 귀에 대고 덧붙였다. \"저 사람이 레이철에 대해 무슨 말을 할지 나는 알아. 그런데 나는 그녀를 너무 아껴서, 그 말을 듣고도 성미를 다스릴 수가 없어. 나를 혼자 있게 놔두게.\"\n\n나는 그를, 넉넉히 비참한 채로, 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 내 창턱에 기대어 있게 두고 나왔다. 페넬로피는 문틈으로 엿보며, 그를 위로해 주고 싶어 안달이었다. 내가 프랭클린 도련님 처지였다면, 나는 그 아이를 불러들였을 것이다. 한 여인에게 모진 대접을 받았을 때는, 그것을 또 다른 여인에게 털어놓는 데 큰 위안이 있는 법이다—열 번에 아홉 번은, 그 또 다른 여인이 늘 여러분 편을 들어 주니까 말이다. 어쩌면, 내가 등을 돌린 사이, 그가 그 아이를 불러들였을까? 그렇다고 하면, 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님을 위로하는 일에 있어서라면 그 아이가 무슨 짓이든 마다하지 않았으리라고 선언해 두는 것이야말로 내 딸에게 마땅한 정을 베푸는 일이렷다.\n\n그러는 동안, 커프 부장과 나는 마님의 방으로 나아갔다.\n\n마님과 마지막으로 가졌던 그 대담에서, 우리는 마님이 탁자 위에 놓아둔 책에서 눈을 좀처럼 들려 하지 않는 것을 보았었다. 이번에는 좀 나아진 쪽으로 변화가 있었다. 마님은 부장의 눈을, 그의 눈만큼이나 흔들림 없는 눈으로 마주하셨다. 그 가문의 기개가 마님 얼굴의 주름 하나하나에서 드러났다. 그리고 나는, 우리 마님 같은 여인이 저에게 퍼부어질 수 있는 가장 나쁜 말을 들으려고 잔뜩 벼르고 있는 마당에는, 커프 부장도 제 맞수를 만나게 되리라는 것을 알았다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXI\n\n우리가 자리를 잡고 앉았을 때, 첫마디를 뗀 것은 마님이었다.\n\n\"커프 부장.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"반 시간 전에 내가 그대에게 함부로 말한 것에는, 어쩌면 얼마쯤 변명거리가 있을지도 모르오. 허나 나는 그 변명거리를 내세우고 싶지 않소. 나는, 아주 진심으로 말하거니와, 만일 내가 그대를 억울하게 했다면, 그것을 뉘우치오.\"\n\n마님이 그에게 그런 사죄를 건네는 그 목소리와 몸가짐의 기품은 부장에게 마땅한 효험을 냈다. 그는 저를 변호할 것을 청했다—제 변호를 마님께 바치는 예의의 행위로 삼으면서 말이다. 그가 우리 모두를 충격에 빠뜨린 그 참사에 조금이라도 책임이 있다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고, 그가 말했다. 이런 넉넉한 이유에서였다. 곧, 그가 제 조사를 마땅한 결말에 이르게 하는 데 성공하려면, 로재나 스피어먼을 놀라게 할 만한 어떤 말도, 어떤 행동도 하지 않는 데 달려 있었기 때문이라는 것이었다. 그는 그가 그 목적을 지켜 냈는지 어쨌는지를 증언해 달라고 내게 호소했다. 나는, 그가 그리했노라 능히 증언할 수 있었고, 또 그리했다. 그리고 거기서, 내 생각으로는, 이 일이 슬기롭게 마무리되도록 놓아둘 수도 있었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 커프 부장은, (여러분도 이제 판단하시게 되겠지만) 있을 수 있는 온갖 해명 가운데 가장 고통스러운 해명이 마님과 저 사이에 벌어지도록 몰아갈 심산으로, 그것을 한 걸음 더 끌고 나갔다.\n\n\"그 젊은 여자의 자살에 대해 어떤 동기가 붙여진 것을 저도 들었습니다.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"그것이 어쩌면 옳은 동기일 수도 있지요. 그것은 제가 여기서 맡아 하고 있는 사건과는 도무지 이어지지 않는 동기입니다. 허나, 이 말은 덧붙여야겠군요, 제 나름의 견해는 그 반대쪽을 가리키고 있다고요. 사라진 다이아몬드와 관련된 어떤 견딜 수 없는 근심이, 제가 믿기로는, 그 가엾은 것을 제 손으로 목숨을 끊는 데로 내몬 것입니다. 그 견딜 수 없는 근심이 대체 무엇이었을지, 저는 안다고 꾸며 대지 않겠습니다. 허나 저는 (마님의 허락하에) 제가 옳은지 그른지를 능히 판가름할 수 있는 사람에게 손을 얹을 수 있으리라 생각합니다.\"\n\n\"그 사람이 지금 이 집 안에 있소?\" 하고 마님이, 잠시 뜸을 들이신 뒤에 물으셨다.\n\n\"그 사람은 이 집을 떠났습니다, 마님.\"\n\n그 대답은 더할 나위 없이 곧장 레이철 아가씨를 가리키고 있었다. 결코 끝날 것 같지 않은 침묵이 우리 위로 내려앉았다. 아이고! 내가 거기 앉아 그 둘 가운데 누군가가 다시 입을 열기를 기다리는 사이, 바람은 어찌 그리 울부짖고, 비는 어찌 그리 창문을 두들겨 대던지!\n\n\"부디 분명하게 말해 주시오.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"내 딸을 두고 하는 말이오?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\" 하고 커프 부장이, 딱 그 몇 마디로 말했다.\n\n우리가 방에 들어섰을 때 마님은 탁자 위에 수표책을 놓아두고 계셨다—의심할 것 없이 부장에게 삯을 치르려는 것이었다. 마님은 이제 그것을 서랍 속에 도로 넣으셨다. 그 가엾은 손이 어찌나 떨리던지 보고 있자니 내 가슴이 미어졌다—당신의 늙은 하인에게 온갖 은혜를 베풀어 주신 그 손이. 하느님께 비오니, 나의 때가 와서 내가 내 자리를 영영 떠날 적에, 부디 나의 손을 잡아 주었으면 하는 그 손이 말이다.\n\n\"나는 바랐소.\" 하고 마님이, 아주 천천히 그리고 조용히 말씀하셨다. \"그대의 수고에 값을 치르고, 지금 이렇게 오간 것처럼 버린더 아가씨의 이름이 우리 사이에 드러내 놓고 오르는 일 없이 그대와 헤어지기를 말이오. 아마 내 조카가, 그대가 내 방에 들어오기 전에, 이에 대해 무슨 말을 했겠지?\"\n\n\"블레이크 도련님께서 전할 말씀을 주셨습니다, 마님. 그리고 제가 블레이크 도련님께 한 가지 이유를 말씀드렸고요—\"\n\n\"그대의 이유는 내게 말할 것 없소. 방금 그대가 한 말을 듣고 나니, 그대도 나만큼이나 잘 알 것이오, 그대가 이미 되돌아가기에는 너무 멀리 나아갔다는 것을. 나는 나 자신에 대한 도리로서도, 내 자식에 대한 도리로서도, 그대가 여기 남아 있기를 고집하고, 그대가 툭 터놓고 말하기를 고집해야겠소.\"\n\n부장은 제 회중시계를 들여다보았다.\n\n\"시간이 있었더라면, 마님.\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"저는 입으로 아뢰는 대신, 제 보고서를 써서 올리는 편이 나았을 겁니다. 허나, 만일 이 조사가 계속되어야 한다면, 시간이라는 것이 글을 쓰느라 허비하기에는 너무나 소중합니다. 저는 당장이라도 이 문제로 들어갈 채비가 되어 있습니다. 그것은 제가 말하기에도, 마님이 들으시기에도, 아주 고통스러운 문제입니다.\"\n\n거기서 마님이 다시 한번 그를 멈춰 세우셨다.\n\n\"내가 내 쪽에서 대범하게 말하는 본을 보이면, 어쩌면 그것이 그대에게도, 여기 있는 나의 착한 하인이자 벗에게도, 덜 고통스러워질지 모르겠소.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그대는 버린더 아가씨가, 제 나름의 어떤 속셈으로 다이아몬드를 감춤으로써, 우리 모두를 속이고 있다고 의심하는 것이오? 그것이 사실이오?\"\n\n\"틀림없는 사실입니다, 마님.\"\n\n\"좋소. 자, 그대가 시작하기에 앞서, 나는 버린더 아가씨의 어미로서 그대에게 일러 두어야겠소, 그 아이는 그대가 짐작하는 그런 짓을 저지를 능력이 전혀 없다는 것을. 그 아이의 됨됨이에 대한 그대의 앎은 하루 이틀 전으로 거슬러 올라갈 뿐이오. 그 아이의 됨됨이에 대한 나의 앎은 그 아이가 태어난 처음으로 거슬러 올라가오. 그 아이에 대한 그대의 의심을 좋을 대로 얼마든지 세게 말해 보시오—그런다고 그대가 나를 언짢게 할 수는 없을 것이오. 나는, 미리부터, (그대의 그 온갖 경험에도 불구하고) 이 사건에서는 정황이 그대를 돌이킬 수 없이 그르쳐 놓았다고 확신하오. 명심하시오! 나는 어떤 은밀한 정보도 쥐고 있지 않소. 나는 그대만큼이나 내 딸의 속내에서 온전히 밀려나 있소. 내가 단언하여 말하는 그 한 가지 근거는, 그대가 이미 들은 그 근거요. 나는 내 자식을 아오.\"\n\n마님은 내게로 돌아서서, 당신 손을 내게 내미셨다. 나는 말없이 그 손에 입을 맞추었다. \"이어서 하시오.\" 하고 마님이, 여느 때처럼 흔들림 없이 부장을 다시 마주하며 말씀하셨다.\n\n커프 부장은 고개를 숙였다. 마님은 그에게 단 한 가지 효험만을 내셨다. 그의 손도끼 같은 얼굴이 한순간 누그러졌다, 마치 마님이 딱하다는 듯이. 그러나 그 자신의 확신을 흔들어 놓는 데 있어서는, 마님이 그를 한 치도 움직이지 못했음이 뻔히 보였다. 그는 제 의자에 자리를 고쳐 앉았다. 그리고 그는 레이철 아가씨의 됨됨이에 대한 그 비열한 공격을 이런 말로 시작했다.\n\n\"저는 마님께 청하는 바입니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이 문제를, 마님의 관점에서뿐만 아니라 제 관점에서도 정면으로 바라봐 주시기를요. 마님께서 제 처지가 되어, 제 경험을 지니고 이곳에 내려오셨다고 여겨 봐 주시겠습니까? 그리고 그 경험이 어떤 것이었는지를 제가 아주 간략히 말씀드리는 것을 허락해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n마님은 그리하겠노라는 뜻을 그에게 보이셨다. 부장은 말을 이었다.\n\n\"지난 이십 년 동안,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 은밀한 심부름꾼 격으로 나서서, 집안의 추문에 얽힌 사건들에 크게 부려져 왔습니다. 지금 다루고 있는 이 문제와 조금이라도 관련이 있는, 제 그 집안일 경험의 한 가지 결론은, 제가 딱 두 마디로 말씀드릴 수 있는 결론입니다. 곧, 지체 있고 신분 있는 젊은 아가씨들이 이따금 제 가장 가까운 친척이나 벗들에게도 감히 털어놓지 못하는 사사로운 빚을 진다는 것은, 넉넉히 제 경험 안에 있는 일입니다. 어떤 때는, 양장점 여자와 보석상이 그 밑바탕에 있지요. 어떤 때는, 이번 사건에서는 제가 의심하지 않는, 그리고 입에 올려 마님을 놀라게 하고 싶지 않은 그런 용도에 그 돈이 필요한 것이고요. 제가 한 말을 마음에 새겨 두십시오, 마님—그리고 이제, 이 집에서 벌어진 일들이 어떻게, 제가 좋든 싫든, 저를 제 경험으로 되돌려 놓았는지 함께 봅시다!\"\n\n그는 잠시 혼자 헤아려 보고는, 말을 이어 갔다—여러분으로 하여금 그를 이해하지 않을 수 없게 하는 그 소름 끼치는 또렷함으로, 아무도 편들지 않는 그 얄미운 공평함으로.\n\n\"월장석의 분실에 관한 저의 첫 정보는,\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"시그레이브 경정에게서 제게로 왔습니다. 그는 저로 하여금 그가 이 사건을 다룰 능력이 도무지 없음을 아주 흡족하게 납득하게 해 주었지요. 그가 한 말 가운데 제가 귀담아들을 만하다 여긴 단 한 가지는 이것이었습니다—버린더 아가씨가 그의 신문을 받기를 거절했고, 도무지 알 수 없는 무례함과 경멸로 그에게 말했다는 것이었지요. 저는 이것을 야릇하게 여겼습니다—허나 저는 그것을, 그 젊은 아가씨의 비위를 거슬렀을지도 모를 경정 쪽의 어떤 서투름 탓으로 주로 돌렸습니다. 그러고는, 저는 그것을 마음속에 밀쳐 두고, 혼자 힘으로 이 사건에 매달렸습니다. 그것은, 마님도 아시다시피, 문에 묻은 얼룩을 발견하는 것으로, 그리고 이 똑같은 얼룩과 다이아몬드의 분실이 같은 수수께끼의 조각들이라는 것을 저로 하여금 흡족히 납득하게 한 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님의 증언으로 끝났습니다. 거기까지는, 제가 무언가를 의심했다면, 저는 월장석이 도둑맞았고, 하인들 가운데 하나가 그 도둑으로 드러날지 모른다고 의심했지요. 아주 좋습니다. 이런 형편에서, 무슨 일이 벌어졌겠습니까? 버린더 아가씨가 별안간 제 방에서 나와, 제게 말을 겁니다. 저는 그 젊은 아가씨에게서 세 가지 수상쩍은 낌새를 알아챕니다. 다이아몬드를 잃은 지 스물네 시간도 넘게 지났건만, 아가씨는 여전히 격렬하게 흥분해 있습니다. 아가씨는 이미 시그레이브 경정을 대했던 것처럼 저를 대합니다. 그리고 아가씨는 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님에게 죽도록 골이 나 있습니다. 다시 아주 좋습니다. 여기 (하고 저는 혼잣말합니다) 값진 보석을 잃은 젊은 아가씨가 있다—또한, 제 눈과 귀가 제게 일러 주는 바로는, 성미가 불같은 젊은 아가씨가. 이런 정황에서, 그런 됨됨이를 지닌 아가씨가, 무엇을 합니까? 아가씨는 블레이크 도련님과, 경정과, 저에게—다시 말해, 저마다 서로 다른 방식으로 아가씨가 잃은 보석을 되찾도록 도우려 애써 온 바로 그 세 사람에게—도무지 알 수 없는 원망을 드러냅니다. 제 조사를 그 지점까지 끌고 오고 나서야—그때, 마님, 그때에 이르러서야 비로소, 저는 제 경험을 찾아 제 마음속을 되돌아보기 시작합니다. 제 경험이 버린더 아가씨의 달리 이해할 길 없는 행동을 풀어 줍니다. 그것이 아가씨를 제가 아는 그 다른 젊은 아가씨들과 하나로 묶어 줍니다. 그것이 제게 이르기를, 아가씨에게는 감히 털어놓지 못하는, 갚아야만 하는 빚이 있다고 합니다. 그리고 그것이 저로 하여금 스스로에게 묻게 합니다, 다이아몬드의 분실이 뜻하는 바가—그 빚을 갚기 위해 다이아몬드를 남몰래 저당 잡혀야만 한다는 것은 아닐지를 말입니다. 그것이 바로 제 경험이 명백한 사실들로부터 이끌어 낸 결론입니다. 마님의 경험은 그에 맞서 무어라 하십니까?\"\n\n\"내가 이미 한 말대로요.\" 하고 마님이 대답하셨다. \"정황이 그대를 그르쳐 놓았소.\"\n\n나는 내 쪽에서 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 「로빈슨 크루소」가—하느님만 아시게 어찌 된 노릇인지—내 어리둥절한 늙은 머릿속에 들어와 있었다. 만일 커프 부장이, 바로 그 순간, 저와 벗해 줄 프라이데이 하나 없이, 저를 태워 갈 배 한 척 없이, 어느 무인도로 실려 갔다면—그는 딱 내가 그를 있게 하고 싶은 그 자리에 놓인 셈이었으련만! (부기: 나는, 여러분이 내 기독교 신앙을 너무 몰아붙이지만 않는다면, 여느 만큼은 착한 기독교인이다. 그리고 여러분 나머지도 모두—이것이 큰 위안이거니와—이 점에 있어서는, 나와 어지간히 매한가지렷다.)\n\n커프 부장은 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"옳든 그르든, 마님.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"제 결론을 이끌어 냈으니, 그다음에 할 일은 그것을 시험대에 올리는 것이었습니다. 저는 마님께 이 집 안의 옷장을 죄다 조사할 것을 제안했지요. 그것은, 십중팔구, 그 얼룩을 낸 옷가지를 찾아내는 방편이었고, 또한 제 결론을 시험대에 올리는 방편이었습니다. 그것이 어떻게 되었습니까? 마님도 승낙하셨고, 블레이크 도련님도 승낙하셨고, 에이블화이트 씨도 승낙하셨습니다. 오직 버린더 아가씨만이 딱 잘라 거절함으로써 그 온 절차를 멈춰 세웠습니다. 그 결말이 제게 제 견해가 옳다는 것을 납득시켰습니다. 마님과 베터리지 양반이 저와 뜻을 같이하지 않기를 고집하신다면, 바로 오늘 두 분 눈앞에서 벌어진 일에 눈을 감고 계신 것임에 틀림없습니다. 두 분이 듣는 데서, 저는 그 젊은 아가씨께, (그때 형편으로) 아가씨가 이 집을 떠나시는 것이 제가 아가씨의 보석을 되찾는 길에 걸림돌을 놓는 것이라고 말했습니다. 두 분은 아가씨가 그 말에도 아랑곳없이 마차를 몰고 떠나는 것을 몸소 보셨습니다. 두 분은 아가씨가, 두 분 모두를 합친 것보다 더 애써 그 실마리를 제 손에 쥐여 준 블레이크 도련님을 용서하기는커녕, 제 어머니 집 층계에서 드러내 놓고 블레이크 도련님을 모욕하는 것을 몸소 보셨습니다. 이런 일들이 무엇을 뜻합니까? 만일 버린더 아가씨가 다이아몬드를 감춘 일에 관여하고 있지 않다면, 이런 일들이 대체 무엇을 뜻한단 말입니까?\"\n\n이번에는 그가 내 쪽을 바라보았다. 그가 레이철 아가씨에게 불리한 증거를 하나하나 쌓아 올리는 것을 듣고 있자니, 그리고 그 아가씨를 감싸 주고 싶어 애가 타는 와중에도, 그가 하는 말의 진실됨에는 다툴 여지가 없음을 알고 있자니, 그야말로 소름이 끼쳤다. 나는 (하느님 고맙게도!) 타고나기를 이치를 넘어서게 태어났다. 이 덕에 나는, 나의 견해이기도 한 마님의 견해를 굳게 붙들 수 있었다. 이것이 내 기개를 북돋아, 커프 부장 앞에서 뻔뻔한 낯을 지을 수 있게 했다. 착한 벗들이여, 부디, 나의 본을 보고 덕을 보시라. 그것이 여러분을 성가신 부류의 숱한 근심에서 구해 줄 것이다. 이치를 넘어서는 기질을 갈고닦으시라, 그리고 그 온갖 사리 밝은 사람들이 여러분의 이로움을 위한답시고 여러분을 할퀴려 들 때 그들의 발톱을 어떻게 잘라 내는지 보시라!\n\n내가 아무 말도 하지 않고, 마님도 아무 말씀도 하지 않으심을 알고서, 커프 부장은 계속했다. 아이고! 그가 우리의 침묵에 조금도 당황하지 않는 것을 알아채는 것이 어찌나 나를 화나게 하던지!\n\n\"버린더 아가씨 한 사람에게만 불리하게 놓여 있는, 지금 그대로의 사건은 그러합니다, 마님.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그다음은, 버린더 아가씨와 죽은 로재나 스피어먼을 한데 묶었을 때 놓이는 그대로의 사건을 아뢰는 것입니다. 괜찮으시다면, 잠시 아가씨가 제 옷장을 조사하도록 하기를 거절한 그 대목으로 되돌아가지요. 그 정황이 있고 나서 제 마음이 정해지자, 저는 그다음으로 두 가지 물음을 헤아려야 했습니다. 첫째는, 제 조사를 이끌어 갈 옳은 방법에 관한 것이었습니다. 둘째는, 버린더 아가씨에게 이 집 안의 하녀들 가운데 공범이 있느냐 하는 것이었습니다. 그것을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 저는, 우리 사무소에서 이르는 말로 하자면, 아주 격식에 어긋난 방식으로 이 조사를 이끌어 가기로 마음먹었습니다. 이런 까닭에서였습니다. 저는 다루어야 할 집안 추문이 있었는데, 그것을 집안의 울타리 안에 묶어 두는 것이 제 소임이었지요. 소란은 덜 일으킬수록, 돕는 데 부려지는 외부인은 적을수록 좋았습니다. 혐의로 사람들을 붙잡아 가두고, 치안판사 앞에 세우고, 그 밖의 온갖 일을 벌이는 여느 절차로 말하자면—마님의 따님이 (제가 믿기로는) 이 온 일의 밑바탕에 있는 마당에, 그런 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었습니다. 이 경우에, 저는 베터리지 양반 같은 됨됨이와, 이 집에서의 지위를 지닌 사람이라면—하인들을 그이만큼 잘 알고, 집안의 명예를 마음에 두고 있으니—제가 손댈 수 있는 다른 어떤 사람보다 조수로 삼기에 더 미더우리라 느꼈습니다. 저는 블레이크 도련님도 시험해 보려 했습니다—허나 길을 가로막는 한 가지 걸림돌이 있었지요. 그이는 아주 이른 때에 제 하는 일의 낌새를 알아챘습니다. 그리고, 버린더 아가씨에 대한 그이의 마음으로 보아, 그이와 저 사이에 어떤 서로 간의 양해도 있을 수 없었습니다. 제가 이런 자잘한 것들로 마님을 성가시게 하는 것은, 제가 이 집안의 비밀을 집안의 테두리 안에 지켜 왔음을 보여 드리기 위해서입니다. 그것을 아는 외부인은 저 하나뿐이고요—그리고 제 입을 다무는 데에 저의 직업적 목숨이 달려 있습니다.\"\n\n여기서 나는, 나의 직업적 목숨은 나의 입을 다물지 않는 데에 달려 있음을 느꼈다. 이 늘그막에, 마님 앞에서, 일종의 보조 순경 노릇을 한 사람으로 치켜세워지는 것은, 다시 한번, 나의 기독교 신앙이 견뎌 낼 만큼 굳세지 못한 노릇이었다.\n\n\"마님께 아뢰옵기를 청하옵니다.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"저는, 제가 아는 한, 처음부터 끝까지 이 얄미운 탐정질을 어떤 식으로도 결코 돕지 않았사옵니다. 그리고 저는 커프 부장에게 요구하는 바입니다, 감히 그럴 배짱이 있거든, 저를 반박해 보라고요!\"\n\n그 말로써 속을 후련히 풀고 나니, 나는 크게 홀가분해졌다. 마님은 내 어깨를 다정하게 살짝 두드려 주심으로써 나를 높여 주셨다. 나는, 부장이 그런 증언을 어찌 여기는지 보려고, 정의로운 노여움에 차 그를 바라보았다! 부장은 어린 양처럼 마주 바라보았고, 그 어느 때보다 나를 더 마음에 들어 하는 듯싶었다.\n\n마님은 그가 진술을 계속해도 좋다고 그에게 이르셨다. \"나는 이해하오.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그대가, 내 이로움이라 믿는 바를 위해, 정직하게 그대의 최선을 다했다는 것을. 나는 그대가 다음에 할 말을 들을 채비가 되어 있소.\"\n\n\"제가 다음에 아뢸 것은,\" 하고 커프 부장이 대답했다. \"로재나 스피어먼에 관한 것입니다. 마님도 어쩌면 기억하시겠지만, 저는 그 젊은 여자가 이 방에 세탁 장부를 가지고 들어왔을 때 그를 알아보았습니다. 그때까지 저는 버린더 아가씨가 제 비밀을 누구에게든 맡겼을지 어떨지를 미심쩍어하는 쪽이었지요. 로재나를 보았을 때, 저는 마음을 고쳐먹었습니다. 저는 대뜸 그가 다이아몬드를 감춘 일에 관여하고 있다고 의심했습니다. 그 가엾은 것이 끔찍한 종말로 제 죽음을 맞았으니, 저는 마님께서, 이제 그가 갔다고 해서, 제가 그를 지나치게 모질게 대했다고 여기시기를 바라지 않습니다. 만일 이것이 여느 도둑질 사건이었다면, 저는 이 집의 다른 어느 하인에게든 그리했을 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 선선히 로재나에게 의심의 이로움을 베풀었을 것입니다. 감화원 출신 여자들에 대한 우리의 경험은, 그들이 하인살이로 시험받을 때—그리고 다정하고 슬기롭게 다루어질 때—태반의 경우에 정직하게 뉘우치고, 그들에게 들인 수고에 정직하게 값하는 사람임을 스스로 증명해 보인다는 것입니다. 허나 이것은 여느 도둑질 사건이 아니었습니다. 그것은—제 마음속으로는—다이아몬드의 주인이 그 밑바탕에 있는, 깊이 꾸며진 사기 사건이었습니다. 이런 견해를 지니고 보니, 로재나와 관련하여 제게 자연스레 떠오른 첫 헤아림은 이것이었습니다. 곧, 버린더 아가씨는 (마님, 송구하오나) 우리 모두로 하여금 월장석이 그저 없어졌을 뿐이라고 여기게 하는 것으로 만족했을까요? 아니면 아가씨는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 우리를 홀려 월장석이 도둑맞았다고 믿게 했을까요? 뒤엣것이라면, 거기에 로재나 스피어먼이—도둑이라는 이력을 지닌 채—아가씨 손안에 마련되어 있었지요. 다른 누구보다도, 마님을 그릇된 자취로 이끌고, 저를 그릇된 자취로 이끌기에 알맞은 사람이 말입니다.\"\n\n(하고 나는 스스로에게 물었다) 그가 레이철 아가씨와 로재나에게 불리한 그의 사건을 이보다 더 소름 끼치는 관점으로 세울 수 있을까? 세울 수 있었다, 여러분도 이제 보시게 되겠지만.\n\n\"저는 그 죽은 여자를 의심할 또 다른 이유가 있었습니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"제게는 그것이 오히려 더 강력했던 것으로 여겨집니다. 버린더 아가씨가 다이아몬드로 남몰래 돈을 마련하는 것을 도울, 바로 그 적임자가 누구였겠습니까? 로재나 스피어먼입니다. 버린더 아가씨 같은 처지의 젊은 아가씨는 그처럼 위험한 일을 혼자 힘으로 꾸려 나갈 수 없습니다. 아가씨에게는 중개인이 하나 있어야만 했는데, 다시 여쭙거니와, 로재나 스피어먼만큼 알맞은 이가 어디 있겠습니까? 마님의 죽은 하녀는 도둑이던 시절에 제 직업의 정점에 있던 사람입니다. 그는, 제가 확실히 아는 바로는, 월장석 같은 이름난 보석에 대해 껄끄러운 질문을 던지거나 껄끄러운 조건을 내걸지 않고 큰돈을 내어줄, 런던의 (돈놀이 쪽) 몇 안 되는 사내들 가운데 하나와 왕래가 있었습니다. 이것을 마음에 새겨 두십시오, 마님. 그리고 이제 제 의심이 로재나 자신의 행위로써, 그리고 그로부터 이끌어 낼 수 있는 명백한 추론으로써 어떻게 정당화되었는지 보여 드리겠습니다.\"\n\n그러고서 그는 로재나의 온갖 행적을 낱낱이 훑어 냈다. 여러분은 그 행적들을 이미 나만큼이나 잘 알고 계신다. 그러니 여러분은 그의 보고의 이 대목이 어떻게, 월장석의 실종에 관여했다는 죄를 그 가엾이 죽은 아이의 기억 위에 반박할 길 없이 못 박아 놓았는지 아실 것이다. 마님조차 그가 이제 하는 말에 기가 꺾이셨다. 마님은 그가 말을 마쳤을 때 그에게 아무 대꾸도 하지 않으셨다. 대꾸를 받든 못 받든 부장에게는 대수롭지 않은 듯싶었다. 그는 계속 나아갔다 (빌어먹을 인간 같으니!), 여느 때처럼 꼭 그대로 태연하게.\n\n\"제가 이해하는 그대로 온 사건을 아뢰었으니,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 이제 마님께, 제가 다음에 무엇을 하려는지만 말씀드리면 됩니다. 저는 이 조사를 성공리에 끝맺을 두 가지 길이 보입니다. 그 두 길 가운데 하나를 저는 확실한 것으로 봅니다. 다른 하나는, 인정하거니와, 대담한 시도일 뿐, 그 이상은 아닙니다. 마님께서 정하시게 됩니다. 확실한 것부터 취할까요?\"\n\n마님은 그에게, 그가 제 방식대로 하고, 스스로 고르라는 뜻을 보이셨다.\n\n\"고맙습니다.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"마님께서 이렇게 제게 맡겨 주시니, 확실한 것부터 시작하지요. 버린더 아가씨가 프리징홀에 머무시든, 아니면 이곳으로 돌아오시든, 저는 어느 경우에든, 아가씨의 온갖 행보를—아가씨가 만나는 사람들과, 아가씨가 나서는 승마와 산책과, 아가씨가 쓰고 받는 편지들을 낱낱이 세심하게 지켜볼 작정입니다.\"\n\n\"그다음은?\" 하고 마님이 물으셨다.\n\n\"저는 그다음으로,\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"로재나 스피어먼의 자리를 대신하는 하인으로서, 이런 종류의 은밀한 조사에 익은, 제가 그 신중함을 보증할 수 있는 여자 하나를 이 집에 들이는 것을 허락해 주십사 마님께 청하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"그다음은?\" 하고 마님이 되물으셨다.\n\n\"그다음으로,\" 하고 부장이 이어 갔다. \"그리고 마지막으로, 저는 제 동료 하나를 보내어, 방금 로재나 스피어먼과 예전에 안면이 있다고 말씀드린 그 런던의 돈놀이꾼과 약조를 맺게 할 작정입니다—그리고 그자의 이름과 주소는, 마님, 로재나가 버린더 아가씨께 알려 준 것이 틀림없다고 믿으셔도 좋습니다. 제가 지금 제안하는 이 행보가 돈이 들고 시간이 걸리리라는 것을 저는 부인하지 않습니다. 허나 그 결과는 확실합니다. 우리는 월장석 둘레에 한 줄을 두르고, 그 줄을 점점 더 바싹 조여, 마침내 그것을 버린더 아가씨가 지니고 계신 데서 찾아내는 것입니다, 아가씨가 그것을 간직하기로 정하신다면 말입니다. 만일 빚이 아가씨를 몰아붙여, 아가씨가 그것을 부쳐 보내기로 정하신다면, 그때는 우리 사람이 대기하고 있다가, 월장석이 런던에 다다르는 것을 맞이하는 것이지요.\"\n\n당신의 친딸이 그런 제안의 대상이 되는 것을 듣자, 마님은 처음으로 노기 띤 말씀을 하시게 되었다.\n\n\"그대의 제안은, 낱낱이, 거절된 것으로 아시오.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그리고 조사를 끝맺을 그대의 다른 길로 넘어가시오.\"\n\n\"제 다른 길은,\" 하고 부장이, 여느 때처럼 태연하게 이어 가며 말했다. \"제가 넌지시 말씀드린 그 대담한 시도를 해 보는 것입니다. 저는 버린더 아가씨의 성미에 대해 제법 정확한 어림을 지니게 되었다고 생각합니다. 아가씨는 (제 믿음으로는) 대담한 사기를 저지를 만한 사람이 넉넉히 됩니다. 허나 아가씨는 성미가 너무 뜨겁고 불같으며, 속임수를 몸에 밴 버릇으로 삼는 데 너무 익지 않아, 자잘한 일에까지 위선을 부리고 온갖 부아 돋우는 일에도 저를 억누르지는 못합니다. 아가씨의 감정은, 이번 사건에서, 그것을 감추는 것이 뻔히 아가씨의 이로움이던 바로 그 순간에, 거듭거듭 아가씨의 다스림을 벗어났습니다. 제가 이제 발판으로 삼으려는 것은 아가씨의 됨됨이에 있는 바로 이 별난 점입니다. 저는 아가씨의 아픈 데를 찌를 정황 아래, 별안간 아가씨에게 큰 충격을 주고 싶습니다. 쉬운 말로 하자면, 저는 버린더 아가씨께, 아무런 미리 알림도 없이, 로재나의 죽음을 말하고 싶습니다—아가씨 자신의 더 나은 심성이 아가씨를 부추겨 죄다 툭 털어놓게 할지 모른다는 요행을 노리고 말입니다. 마님께서는 그 대안을 받아들이시겠습니까?\"\n\n마님은 이루 말할 수 없이 나를 놀라게 하셨다. 마님은 그 자리에서 대뜸 그에게 대답하셨다.\n\n\"그러겠소. 받아들이겠소.\"\n\n\"조랑말 이륜마차가 준비되어 있습니다.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"마님, 안녕히 계십시오.\"\n\n마님은 손을 들어, 문가에서 그를 멈춰 세우셨다.\n\n\"내 딸의 더 나은 심성에, 그대가 제안한 대로 호소해 보겠소.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"허나 나는, 그 아이의 어미로서, 그 아이를 몸소 시험대에 올릴 권리를 내세우겠소. 괜찮다면, 그대는 여기 남아 있으시오. 그리고 프리징홀에는 내가 가겠소.\"\n\n평생에 단 한 번, 그 위대한 커프가 여느 사람처럼 넋을 잃고 말문이 막힌 채 서 있었다.\n\n마님은 초인종을 울려, 당신의 방수 우비를 가져오라 이르셨다. 여전히 비가 억수같이 쏟아지고 있었다. 그리고 그 지붕 있는 마차는, 여러분도 아시다시피, 레이철 아가씨를 태우고 프리징홀로 가 버린 터였다. 나는 마님께 그 모진 날씨를 무릅쓰지 마시라 말리려 했다. 아주 부질없었다! 나는 함께 가서 우산을 받쳐 들게 해 달라 청했다. 마님은 들으려 하지 않으셨다. 조랑말 이륜마차가, 마부가 맡아 몰고, 돌아 나왔다. \"그대는 두 가지를 믿어도 좋소.\" 하고 마님이 현관에서 커프 부장에게 말씀하셨다. \"나는 그대 자신이 능히 시험해 볼 수 있을 만큼 대담하게 버린더 아가씨에게 그 시도를 해 보겠소. 그리고 그 결과를, 몸소든 편지로든, 오늘 밤 런던행 마지막 기차가 떠나기 전에 그대에게 알리겠소.\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 마님은 이륜마차에 올라, 몸소 고삐를 잡고, 프리징홀로 몰고 떠나셨다.\n\n# CHAPTER XXII\n\n마님이 우리를 떠나시고 나자, 나는 커프 부장을 생각할 겨를이 생겼다. 나는 그가 현관의 아늑한 구석에 앉아, 제 비망록을 들여다보며, 입가를 짓궂게 말아 올리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"사건에 대한 기록을 하시오?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"아니오.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"내 다음번 일거리가 무엇인지 살펴보고 있소.\"\n\n\"허!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"그럼 여기 일은 죄다 끝났다고 여기는 거요?\"\n\n\"내 생각에는,\" 하고 커프 부장이 대답했다. \"레이디 버린더는 잉글랜드에서 가장 영리한 여인 가운데 하나요. 그리고 내 생각에는, 다이아몬드보다 장미 한 송이가 훨씬 더 들여다볼 값어치가 있고. 정원사는 어디 있소, 베터리지 양반?\"\n\n월장석 일에 대해서는 그에게서 더는 한마디도 끌어낼 수가 없었다. 그는 제 조사에 대한 온갖 흥미를 잃어버렸고, 한사코 정원사를 찾겠다는 것이었다. 한 시간 뒤, 나는 그 둘이 온실에서 언성을 높이는 소리를 들었는데, 그 다툼의 밑바탕에는 다시 한번 들장미가 자리하고 있었다.\n\n그러는 동안, 프랭클린 도련님이 오후 기차로 우리를 떠나겠다는 결심을 여전히 굳게 지키고 있는지 알아보는 것이 내 일이었다. 마님의 방에서 있었던 그 대담과, 그것이 어떻게 끝났는지에 대해 전해 듣고 나자, 그는 대뜸 프리징홀에서 오는 소식을 기다려 듣기로 마음먹었다. 계획에 있어 이 아주 자연스러운 변경은—여느 사람이라면 딱히 아무 일로도 이어지지 않았을 것이나—프랭클린 도련님의 경우에는, 한 가지 달갑잖은 결과를 낳는 것으로 드러났다. 그것은 그를 어수선하게 붙들어 놓아, 손에 한가한 시간이라는 유산을 안겼고, 그렇게 함으로써, 그의 됨됨이의 온갖 외국물 든 면모를, 자루에서 쏟아져 나오는 쥐 떼처럼, 하나 위에 또 하나씩, 죄다 풀어놓았다.\n\n이탈리아식 잉글랜드인이 되었다가, 독일식 잉글랜드인이 되었다가, 또 프랑스식 잉글랜드인이 되어, 그는 레이철 아가씨가 저를 대한 처사 말고는 이야기할 거리라곤 없이, 또 나 말고는 하소연할 상대라곤 없이, 집 안의 온갖 거실을 들락날락 떠돌았다. 나는 (이를테면) 서재에서, 근대 이탈리아 지도 아래 앉아, 제 근심을 마주하는 데에 그 근심에 대해 이야기하는 방법 말고는 다른 어떤 방법도 도무지 알지 못하는 그를 보았다. \"나는 여러 가지 훌륭한 포부가 있어, 베터리지. 그런데 이제 그것들을 가지고 어쩌란 말인가? 나는 잠들어 있는 좋은 자질들로 가득 차 있는데, 레이철이 그것들을 끌어내는 것을 도와주기만 했더라면!\" 그는 제 등한시된 미덕을 그려 보이는 데에 어찌나 청산유수던지, 그리고 그것을 다 그리고 나서 그것을 두고 한탄하는 데에 어찌나 애처롭던지, 그를 어떻게 위로해야 할지 나는 그야말로 어찌할 바를 몰랐는데, 문득 여기야말로 「로빈슨 크루소」한 대목을 몸에 이롭게 발라 줄 사례라는 생각이 떠올랐다. 나는 절뚝거리며 내 방으로 나가, 그 불멸의 책을 들고 절뚝거리며 돌아왔다. 서재에는 아무도 없었다! 근대 이탈리아 지도가 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그리고 나는 근대 이탈리아 지도를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n나는 응접실을 살펴보았다. 마룻바닥에 그의 손수건이 떨어져 있어, 그가 그리로 떠들어 왔음을 증명해 주었다. 그리고 텅 빈 방이 있어, 그가 다시 떠들어 나갔음을 증명해 주었다.\n\n나는 식당을 살펴보았다가, 새뮤얼이 비스킷 한 조각과 셰리주 한 잔을 든 채, 말없이 텅 빈 허공을 살피고 있는 것을 발견했다. 조금 전, 프랭클린 도련님이 가벼운 요깃거리를 가져오라고 사납게 초인종을 울렸다. 그것이, 새뮤얼에 의해, 몹시 부산스럽게 마련되어 나오자, 프랭클린 도련님은, 그가 잡아당긴 그 초인종이 아래층에서 채 다 울리기도 전에, 사라져 버렸다는 것이었다.\n\n나는 아침 방을 살펴보았고, 마침내 그를 찾아냈다. 그는 거기 창가에서, 유리에 서린 김에 손가락으로 상형 문자를 그리고 있었다.\n\n\"셰리주가 기다리고 있습니다, 도련님.\" 하고 나는 그에게 말했다. 방의 네 벽 가운데 하나에 대고 말하는 편이 차라리 나았으리라. 그는 제 명상의 바닥 모를 깊은 데 잠겨, 도무지 끌어 올릴 길이 없었다. \"자네는 레이철의 행동을 어떻게 설명하나, 베터리지?\"가 내가 받은 유일한 대답이었다. 마땅한 대꾸를 얼른 내놓지 못한 채, 나는 「로빈슨 크루소」를 꺼내 들었는데, 그 안에서라면, 우리가 그것을 넉넉히 오래 찾기만 했더라면, 어떤 설명이든 찾아낼 수 있었으리라고 나는 굳게 믿는다. 프랭클린 도련님은 「로빈슨 크루소」를 탁 덮고는, 그 자리에서 그의 독일식 잉글랜드 횡설수설로 허우적거리며 빠져들었다. \"왜 그것을 파고들어 보지 않는 건가?\" 하고 그가, 마치 내가 몸소 그것을 파고드는 데 반대하기라도 한 듯 말했다. \"빌어먹을, 진실에 다다르는 데 필요한 것이라곤 참을성뿐인데, 어째서 참을성을 잃는단 말인가, 베터리지? 내 말을 끊지 말게. 레이철의 행동은, 자네가 그저 그녀에게 마땅한 공정을 베풀어, 먼저 객관적 관점을 취하고, 그다음에 주관적 관점을 취하고, 그리고 마무리로 객관적-주관적 관점을 취하기만 한다면, 아주 또렷이 이해될 수 있어. 우리가 무엇을 아는가? 우리는 지난 목요일 아침의 월장석 분실이 그녀를, 아직도 헤어나지 못한 신경 흥분 상태에 빠뜨렸다는 것을 알지. 자네는 여기까지의 객관적 관점을 부인하려는 건가? 좋아, 그렇다면—내 말을 끊지 말게. 자, 신경 흥분 상태에 있으니, 그녀가 제 둘레의 어느 누구에게든 달리 처신했을 법하게 처신하기를 우리가 어찌 기대할 수 있겠나? 이런 식으로, 안에서 바깥으로 따져 나가면, 우리는 무엇에 다다르는가? 우리는 주관적 관점에 다다르지. 자네가 주관적 관점을 뒤집을 수 있거든 어디 해 보게. 좋아, 그렇다면—무엇이 뒤따르는가? 맙소사! 물론, 객관적-주관적 설명이 뒤따르지! 레이철은, 엄밀히 말하면, 레이철이 아니라, 다른 어떤 사람이야. 다른 어떤 사람에게 모질게 대접받는 것을 내가 마음에 두겠나? 자네는 넉넉히 억지스럽네, 베터리지. 허나 자네가 나더러 그렇다고 나무랄 수는 없지. 그러면 그것은 어떻게 끝나는가? 그것은, 자네의 그 지긋지긋한 잉글랜드식 옹졸함과 편견에도 불구하고, 내가 아주 행복하고 편안해지는 것으로 끝난다네. 셰리주는 어디 있나?\"\n\n이때쯤 내 머리는, 그것이 내 머리인지 프랭클린 도련님의 머리인지 도무지 확신이 서지 않을 지경이 되었다. 이 딱한 처지에서, 나는, 내가 세 가지 객관적인 일이라 여기는 것을 용케 해냈다. 나는 프랭클린 도련님께 셰리주를 갖다 드렸고, 내 방으로 물러갔고, 그리고 내 평생 피워 본 것 가운데 가장 마음을 가라앉혀 주는 담배 한 대로 내 마음을 달랬다.\n\n허나, 내가 이토록 손쉬운 조건으로 프랭클린 도련님에게서 벗어났으리라고는 여기지 마시라. 다시 떠돌아, 아침 방에서 현관으로 나온 그는, 이번에는 하인방 쪽으로 길을 잡아, 내 담배 냄새를 맡고는, 대뜸 저가 레이철 아가씨를 위해 담배를 끊는 어리석음을 부렸었다는 것을 떠올렸다. 눈 깜짝할 새, 그는 제 여송연갑을 들고 내게 들이닥쳐, 그의 그 말쑥하고, 재치 있고, 곧이듣지 않는, 프랑스식 태도로, 그 하나뿐인 영원한 화제를 힘차게 늘어놓았다. \"불 좀 붙여 주게, 베터리지. 사내가 나만큼 오래 담배를 피우고서도, 제 여송연갑 밑바탕에 여자를 다루는 완벽한 체계가 들어 있다는 것을 알아채지 못했다는 게 있을 법한 일인가? 내 말을 잘 따라오게, 그러면 두 마디로 그것을 증명해 보이지. 자네는 여송연 하나를 고르고, 그것을 피워 보는데, 그것이 자네를 실망시켜. 그러면 자네는 어찌하나? 자네는 그것을 던져 버리고 다른 것을 피워 보지. 자, 이 적용을 눈여겨보게! 자네는 여자 하나를 고르고, 그녀를 시험해 보는데, 그녀가 자네의 가슴을 찢어 놓아. 이 어리석은 사람아! 자네 여송연갑에서 교훈을 얻게. 그녀를 던져 버리고, 다른 여자를 시험해 보라고!\"\n\n나는 그 말에 고개를 저었다. 놀랍도록 영리하렷다, 허나 내 나름의 경험은 딱 그와 어긋났다. \"돌아가신 베터리지 안사람이 살아 있던 시절에는,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"저도 도련님의 그 철학을 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 꽤나 자주 들었습지요, 프랭클린 도련님. 허나 법이라는 것이, 한번 여송연을 골랐거든, 그것을 끝까지 피우라고 우기지 뭡니까, 도련님.\" 나는 그 소견에 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하는 것으로 방점을 찍었다. 프랭클린 도련님은 왈칵 웃음을 터뜨렸다—그리고 우리는, 이윽고 그의 됨됨이의 그다음 새 면모가 순서대로 튀어나올 때까지, 귀뚜라미처럼 흥겨웠다. 나의 젊은 주인과 나 사이의 일은 그렇게 흘러갔다. 그리고 그렇게 (부장과 정원사가 장미를 두고 옥신각신하는 동안) 우리 둘은 프리징홀에서 소식이 돌아오기 전의 그 사이를 보냈다.\n\n조랑말 이륜마차는 내가 감히 예상했던 것보다 족히 반 시간은 일찍 돌아왔다. 마님은 당분간, 언니 되시는 분의 집에 머물기로 정하신 터였다. 마부가 제 여주인에게서 편지 두 통을 가져왔는데, 하나는 프랭클린 도련님 앞으로, 다른 하나는 내 앞으로 온 것이었다.\n\n프랭클린 도련님의 편지는 서재로 그에게 보냈다—그의 떠돌이 걸음이 이제 두 번째로 그를 피신시켜 놓은 그 은신처로 말이다. 내 편지는, 내 방에서 읽었다. 그것을 뜯었을 때 툭 떨어진 수표 한 장이, (내가 그 내용을 다 헤아리기도 전에) 월장석을 좇는 조사에서 커프 부장을 해임하는 것이 이제 정해진 일임을 내게 알려 주었다.\n\n나는 온실로 사람을 보내, 부장에게 곧바로 할 말이 있다고 전했다. 그는, 정원사와 들장미로 머릿속이 가득한 채, 베그비 씨만큼 고집스러운 자는 여태 존재한 적도 없고, 앞으로도 결코 존재하지 않으리라고 선언하며 나타났다. 나는 그에게, 이런 하잘것없는 시시한 소리는 우리 이야기에서 물리치고, 정말로 진지한 문제에 온 정신을 쏟아 달라고 청했다. 그러자 그는, 내 손에 든 편지를 알아챌 만큼은 애를 썼다. \"아!\" 하고 그가 지친 투로 말했다. \"마님에게서 기별이 왔구려. 나와 무슨 상관이 있는 거요, 베터리지 양반?\"\n\n\"직접 판단해 보시오, 부장.\" 그러고서 나는 그에게 그 편지를 (내 나름 가장 잘 힘주고 신중하게) 다음과 같은 말로 읽어 주었다.\n\n\"나의 착한 게이브리얼에게,—커프 부장에게 알려 주기를 부탁하오, 내가 그에게 한 약속을 이행했으며, 로재나 스피어먼에 관한 한 이런 결과가 나왔다고 말이오. 버린더 아가씨는, 그 불행한 여자가 처음 내 집에 들어온 이래, 로재나와 단둘이서 단 한마디도 나눈 적이 없다고 엄숙히 단언하오. 그들은, 다이아몬드를 잃은 그 밤에도, 우연히라도 마주친 적이 없소. 그리고 이 집에서 처음 소동이 벌어진 목요일 아침부터, 버린더 아가씨가 우리를 떠난 오늘 이 토요일 오후까지, 그들 사이에는 어떤 종류의 왕래도 전혀 없었소. 내 딸에게 별안간, 그 몇 마디로, 로재나 스피어먼의 자살을 말한 뒤에—이런 결과가 나온 것이오.\"\n\n거기까지 다다르자, 나는 눈을 들어, 커프 부장에게 여기까지의 편지를 어찌 여기느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"내가 내 의견을 밝힌다면 그저 당신을 언짢게 할 뿐이겠지.\" 하고 부장이 대답했다. \"계속하시오, 베터리지 양반.\" 하고 그가, 더할 나위 없이 부아 돋우는 체념으로 말했다. \"계속하시오.\"\n\n이 사내가 뻔뻔스럽게도 우리 정원사의 고집을 불평했던 것을 떠올리자, 내 혀는 마님의 말씀 아닌 다른 말로 \"계속하고\" 싶어 근질거렸다. 허나, 이번에는, 나의 기독교 신앙이 굳게 버텨 주었다. 나는 마님의 편지를 꾸준히 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그 형사가 가장 바람직하다 여긴 방식으로 버린더 아가씨에게 호소한 뒤, 나는 이어서 나 자신이 그 아이에게 가장 감명을 줄 성싶다 여긴 방식으로 그 아이에게 말했소. 두 차례에 걸쳐, 내 딸이 내 지붕 아래를 떠나기 전에, 나는 그 아이에게, 가장 견딜 수 없고 가장 수치스러운 종류의 의심에 스스로를 내맡기고 있노라고 남몰래 일렀소. 나는 이제 그 아이에게, 더없이 명백한 말로, 나의 우려가 현실이 되었다고 말했소.\n\n\"이에 대한 그 아이의 대답은, 그 아이 자신의 엄숙한 확언으로써, 더할 나위 없이 분명하오. 첫째로, 그 아이는 살아 있는 그 어떤 이에게도 남몰래 진 빚이 없소. 둘째로, 다이아몬드는, 그 아이가 수요일 밤 그것을 제 장롱에 넣어 둔 이래, 지금 그 아이가 지니고 있지도 않고, 여태 지닌 적도 없소.\n\n\"내 딸이 내게 준 속내는 이 이상으로 더 나아가지 않소. 다이아몬드의 실종을 설명할 수 있느냐고 내가 물으면, 그 아이는 고집스러운 침묵을 지키오. 나를 위해 툭 터놓고 말해 달라 내가 호소하면, 그 아이는 눈물을 흘리며 거절하오. '제가 어째서 의심받는 것에 개의치 않는지, 그리고 어째서 엄마에게조차 입을 다무는지, 엄마가 알게 될 날이 올 거예요. 저는 제 어머니가 저를 가엾이 여기게 할 일은 많이 했어도—제 어머니가 저 때문에 낯을 붉히게 할 일은 아무것도 하지 않았어요.' 그것이 내 딸 자신의 말이오.\n\n\"그 형사와 나 사이에 오간 일로 미루어, 나는—그가 외부인이긴 하나—그에게도 그대에게처럼 버린더 아가씨가 한 말을 알려 주어야 마땅하다고 생각하오. 내 편지를 그에게 읽어 주고, 그런 뒤에 내가 동봉하는 수표를 그의 손에 쥐여 주시오. 그의 수고에 대한 이후의 모든 청구를 그만두면서, 나는 다만 이 말만 하겠소, 나는 그의 정직함과 그의 총명함을 확신한다고. 허나 나는 그 어느 때보다 더 굳게 믿게 되었소, 이 사건에서는, 정황이 그를 돌이킬 수 없이 그르쳐 놓았다고 말이오.\"\n\n거기서 편지가 끝났다. 수표를 건네기 전에, 나는 커프 부장에게 무슨 할 말이 있느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"사건을 다 끝낸 마당에, 그 사건에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵하는 것은, 베터리지 양반,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"내 소임의 몫이 아니오.\"\n\n나는 탁자 너머로 그에게 수표를 툭 던졌다. \"마님 편지의 그 대목을 당신은 믿소?\" 하고 내가 분개하여 말했다.\n\n부장은 수표를 들여다보더니, 마님의 후함을 인정하는 뜻으로 제 침울한 눈썹을 치켜올렸다.\n\n\"이것은 내 시간의 값어치를 어찌나 후하게 쳐준 것인지,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나로서는 그에 얼마쯤 보답을 하지 않을 수 없다는 느낌이 드는구려. 이 수표의 액수를 마음에 새겨 두겠소, 베터리지 양반, 그것을 떠올릴 때가 돌아오거든 말이오.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이오?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"마님은 당장은 일을 아주 영리하게 얼버무려 놓으셨소.\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"허나 이런 집안 추문이라는 것은 당신이 가장 뜻하지 않을 때 다시 터져 나오는 종류의 것이오. 우리는 손에 더 많은 탐정 일거리를 쥐게 될 거요, 양반, 월장석이 몇 달 더 나이를 먹기 전에 말이오.\"\n\n그 말에 무슨 뜻이 있다면, 그리고 그가 그 말을 하는 태도에 무슨 뜻이 있다면—그것은 이런 것이 되었다. 마님의 편지는, 그의 마음속에, 레이철 아가씨가 저에게 던져질 수 있는 가장 강력한 호소에도 버틸 만큼 모질어졌으며, 일련의 얄미운 거짓말로 (아이고 하느님, 어떤 정황 아래서!) 제 친어머니를 속였음을 증명해 주었다는 것이었다. 다른 사람들이라면, 내 처지에서, 그 부장에게 어떻게 대꾸했을지 나는 모른다. 나는 그가 한 말에 이런 명백한 말로 대답했다.\n\n\"커프 부장, 나는 당신의 그 마지막 소견을 마님과 그 따님에 대한 모욕으로 여기겠소!\"\n\n\"베터리지 양반, 그것을 당신 자신에 대한 경고로 여긴다면, 당신은 정곡에 더 가까워질 거요.\"\n\n내가 아무리 달아오르고 노여웠어도, 그가 그 대답을 내놓는 그 지옥 같은 자신감은 내 입을 다물게 했다.\n\n나는 마음을 가라앉히려 창가로 걸어갔다. 비가 그친 터였다. 그런데 뜰에서 내가 본 것은, 다름 아닌, 커프 부장과 들장미 논쟁을 이어 가려고 밖에서 기다리고 있는 정원사 베그비 씨였다.\n\n\"부장 나리께 안부 여쭙습니다요.\" 하고 베그비 씨가, 나를 보자마자 말했다. \"나리가 역까지 걸어갈 마음이 있으시다면, 지도 기꺼이 함께 가겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"뭐요!\" 하고 내 뒤에서 부장이 소리친다. \"당신 아직도 납득이 안 됐소?\"\n\n\"납득은 무슨 얼어 죽을!\" 하고 베그비 씨가 대답했다.\n\n\"그럼 나는 역까지 걸어가겠소!\" 하고 부장이 말한다.\n\n\"그럼 지는 대문에서 나리를 맞겠습니다요!\" 하고 베그비 씨가 말한다.\n\n여러분도 아시다시피, 나는 넉넉히 노여웠다—허나 이런 훼방에 어느 누구의 노여움인들 어찌 버텨 낼 수 있었으랴? 커프 부장은 내 안에 인 변화를 알아채고는, 때맞춘 한마디로 그것을 부추겼다. \"자! 자!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"어째서 이 사건에 대한 내 견해를, 마님이 그것을 대하시듯 대하지 않는 거요? 어째서, 정황이 나를 돌이킬 수 없이 그르쳐 놓았다고 말하지 않는 거요?\"\n\n무엇이든 마님이 그것을 대하시듯 대하는 것은 누릴 값어치가 있는 특권이었다—그것이 커프 부장에게서 내게 건네진 것이라는 흠이 있음에도 말이다. 나는 서서히 여느 때 상태로 식어 갔다. 나는 레이철 아가씨에 대한, 마님의 견해나 나의 견해 아닌 다른 어떤 견해도, 도도한 경멸로 여겼다. 내가 도무지 할 수 없었던 단 한 가지는, 월장석이라는 화제에서 손을 떼는 것이었다! 나도 알거니와, 나의 분별력이 마땅히 나를 일깨워 그 일을 그냥 놓아두게 했어야 했다—허나, 저런! 요즘 세대를 돋보이게 하는 그 미덕들은 내 시대에는 아직 발명되지 않았던 것이다. 커프 부장이 내 생살을 건드려 놓은 터라, 내가 그를 경멸로 내려다보긴 했어도, 그럼에도 그 여린 데는 여전히 얼얼했다. 그 끝은, 내가 심술궂게 그를 마님 편지라는 화제로 도로 이끌었다는 것이다. \"나 자신은 아주 흡족하오.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"허나 그건 아무래도 좋소! 마치 내가 아직 설득에 열려 있기라도 한 듯 계속해 보시오. 당신은 레이철 아가씨의 말을 곧이들을 수 없다고 여기지. 그리고 당신은 우리가 월장석에 대해 다시 듣게 되리라고 말하고. 당신 견해를 뒷받침해 보시오, 부장.\" 하고 나는, 가벼운 투로 맺었다. \"당신 견해를 뒷받침해 보시오.\"\n\n부장은 언짢아하기는커녕, 내 손을 움켜쥐고, 내 손가락이 다시 아파 올 때까지 흔들어 댔다.\n\n\"하늘에 대고 맹세하거니와,\" 하고 이 별난 형사가 엄숙히 말한다. \"당신과 함께 부려질 기회만 있다면, 나는 당장 내일이라도 하인살이에 나서겠소, 베터리지 양반! 당신더러 아이처럼 속이 훤히 들여다보인다고 말하는 것은, 열에 아홉은 그런 찬사를 받을 자격도 없는 아이들에게 찬사를 바치는 셈이오. 저런! 저런! 우리 다시 다투지는 맙시다. 그보다 손쉬운 조건으로 내게서 그것을 들려 드리지. 마님에 대해서도, 버린더 아가씨에 대해서도 더는 한마디도 하지 않겠소—그저, 이번 한 번만, 그리고 당신을 위해, 예언자 노릇을 해 보겠소. 나는 이미 당신에게 당신이 아직 월장석 일을 끝낸 게 아니라고 일러 두었소. 아주 좋소. 이제 나는 헤어지는 마당에, 앞으로 일어날, 그리고 내가 믿기로는, 당신이 좋든 싫든 억지로 당신의 눈길을 끌어당길 세 가지 일을 당신에게 일러 주겠소.\"\n\n\"계속해 보시오!\" 하고 나는, 조금도 기죽지 않고, 여느 때처럼 가볍게 말했다.\n\n\"첫째,\" 하고 부장이 말했다. \"당신은 욜랜드네에서 무슨 소식을 듣게 될 거요—우체부가 다음 월요일에 코브스 홀로 로재나의 편지를 배달할 때 말이오.\"\n\n그가 내게 찬물 한 통을 끼얹었다 한들, 그 말을 느꼈던 것보다 훨씬 더 언짢게 느낄 수 있었을지 의심스럽다. 레이철 아가씨가 제 결백을 단언한 탓에, 로재나의 행동은—새 잠옷을 지은 것, 얼룩진 잠옷을 숨긴 것, 그리고 그 밖의 온갖 것들은—온전히 설명되지 않은 채 남아 있었다. 그런데 이것이, 커프 부장이 순식간에 그것을 내 마음에 들이밀 때까지, 내게는 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었던 것이다!\n\n\"둘째로,\" 하고 부장이 이어 갔다. \"당신은 그 인도인 셋에 대해 다시 듣게 될 거요. 레이철 아가씨가 이 근방에 머무신다면, 당신은 이 근방에서 그들에 대해 듣게 될 거요. 레이철 아가씨가 런던으로 가신다면, 당신은 런던에서 그들에 대해 듣게 될 거고.\"\n\n그 세 요술쟁이에 대한 온갖 흥미를 잃어버렸고, 우리 아가씨의 결백을 나 자신에게 철저히 납득시킨 터라, 나는 이 두 번째 예언을 넉넉히 대수롭잖게 받아들였다. \"일어날 세 가지 일 가운데 두 가지는 그쯤 됐고.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"이제 세 번째요!\"\n\n\"셋째로, 그리고 마지막으로,\" 하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"당신은, 이르든 늦든, 내가 이미 두 번이나 주제넘게 입에 올린 그 런던의 돈놀이꾼에 대해 무슨 소식을 듣게 될 거요. 당신 수첩을 이리 주시오, 그러면 당신을 위해 그자의 이름과 주소를 적어 두겠소—그래야 그 일이 정말로 일어날 때 착오가 없을 테니.\"\n\n그리하여 그는 빈 종잇장에 이렇게 적었다—\"셉티머스 루커 씨, 미들섹스플레이스, 램버스, 런던.\"\n\n\"자,\" 하고 그가, 그 주소를 가리키며 말했다. \"이것이 당분간 월장석이라는 화제에 대해 내가 당신을 성가시게 할 마지막 말이오. 내가 옳은지 그른지는 시간이 보여 줄 거요. 그러는 동안, 양반, 나는 당신에 대한 진심 어린 개인적 호감을 지니고 떠나오, 그리고 그것이 우리 둘 다에게 명예로운 일이라고 나는 생각하오. 만일 내 직업적 은퇴가 이루어지기 전에 우리가 다시 만나지 못한다면, 부디 내가 점찍어 둔 런던 근교의 조그만 집으로 나를 보러 와 주시오. 풀밭 오솔길이 있을 거요, 베터리지 양반, 내 장담하리다, 나의 정원에는 말이오. 그리고 그 흰 이끼장미로 말하자면——\"\n\n\"흰 이끼장미는, 먼저 들장미에 접붙이지 않고서는, 어림 반 푼어치도 자라게 못 할 겁니다요!\" 하고 창가에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다.\n\n우리는 둘 다 돌아보았다. 거기에는 그 지긋지긋한 베그비 씨가, 논쟁이 하고 싶어 안달이 나 더는 대문에서 기다리지 못하고 서 있었다. 부장은 내 손을 힘껏 쥐었다가, 제 쪽에서 한층 더 달아올라, 뜰로 뛰쳐나갔다. \"저 사람이 돌아오거든 이끼장미에 대해 물어보시오, 그리고 내가 저 사람에게 딛고 설 다리 한쪽이라도 남겨 뒀는지 보시오!\" 하고 그 위대한 커프가, 이번에는 제가 창문 너머로 나를 부르며 외쳤다. \"두 분 다, 신사분들이오!\" 하고 나는, 이미 한 번 그들을 진정시켰던 것처럼 다시 그들을 진정시키며 대답했다. \"이끼장미 문제에 있어서는 양쪽 다 할 말이 많소이다!\" (아일랜드 사람들 말마따나) 이정표에 대고 지그 가락을 휘파람으로 부는 편이 차라리 나았으리라. 그들은 함께 떠나갔다, 양쪽 어느 편도 봐주기를 청하지도 베풀지도 않으며 장미 전쟁을 벌이면서 말이다. 내가 그들을 마지막으로 본 모습은, 베그비 씨가 제 고집스러운 머리를 절레절레 흔들고 있고, 커프 부장은 죄인을 붙든 간수처럼 그의 팔을 붙들고 있는 것이었다. 아, 저런! 저런! 나는 인정하거니와 그 부장을 좋아하지 않을 수가 없었다—내내 그를 미워하면서도 말이다.\n\n할 수 있거든, 그런 마음의 상태를 설명해 보시라. 여러분은 이제, 곧, 나와 나의 모순들에게서 벗어나게 될 것이다. 프랭클린 도련님의 출발을 아뢰고 나면, 그 토요일에 있었던 일들의 기록은 마침내 끝날 것이다. 그리고 새로운 한 주 사이에 일어난 어떤 기이한 일들을 그다음에 이야기하고 나면, 나는 이 이야기의 내 몫을 다한 것이 되며, 내 뒤를 잇도록 정해진 사람에게 붓을 넘기게 될 것이다. 만일 여러분이 이 이야기를 읽는 데에 내가 그것을 쓰는 데에 지친 것만큼 지쳐 있다면—아이고, 몇 쪽 더 나아가면 우리 양쪽 다 얼마나 신이 날 것인가!\n\n# CHAPTER XXIII\n\n나는, 프랭클린 도련님이 그날 밤 기차로 우리를 떠나겠다는 결심을 굳게 지킬 경우에 대비하여, 조랑말 이륜마차를 대기시켜 두었다. 짐이 나타나고, 그 뒤로 프랭클린 도련님이 몸소 층계를 내려오는 것을 보니, 그가 평생에 단 한 번 어떤 결심을 굳게 지켰음이 넉넉히 뻔했다.\n\n\"그럼 정말로 마음을 굳히신 겁니까, 도련님?\" 하고 우리가 현관에서 마주쳤을 때 내가 말했다. \"하루 이틀 더 기다리셔서, 레이철 아가씨께 다시 한번 기회를 드리시는 게 어떻습니까?\"\n\n이제 작별을 고할 때가 되고 보니, 그 외국물 든 겉치레는 프랭클린 도련님에게서 죄다 벗겨진 듯싶었다. 그는 내게 말로 대답하는 대신, 마님이 그에게 보내신 편지를 내 손에 쥐여 주었다. 그 대부분은 내게 온 다른 편지에서 이미 이야기된 것을 다시 이야기하고 있었다. 허나 끝에 레이철 아가씨에 대한 대목이 얼마쯤 덧붙어 있었는데, 그것은, 다른 아무것도 설명해 주지 못한다 해도, 프랭클린 도련님의 그 흔들림 없는 결심만은 설명해 줄 것이다.\n\n\"자네는 아마 의아해하겠지\" (하고 마님은 쓰셨다) \"내가 내 친딸이 나를 온전히 깜깜한 데 두도록 내버려 두는 것을 말이야. 이만 파운드 값어치의 다이아몬드가 없어졌는데—나는 그 실종의 수수께끼가 레이철에게는 수수께끼가 아니라는 것, 그리고 내게는 전혀 알려지지 않은 어떤 사람 혹은 사람들에 의해, 내가 짐작조차 할 수 없는 어떤 목적을 위해, 무언가 알 수 없는 침묵의 의무가 그 아이에게 지워졌다는 것을 미루어 헤아릴 수밖에 없는 처지에 남겨졌네. 내가 이런 식으로 놀림감이 되는 것을 스스로 용납한다는 것이 있을 법한 일인가? 지금 레이철의 상태로 보아서는, 넉넉히 있을 법한 일이지. 그 아이는 보기에 딱한 신경 흥분 상태에 있어. 시간이 그 아이를 얼마쯤 가라앉혀 놓을 때까지, 나는 감히 월장석이라는 화제에 다시 다가갈 수가 없네. 이 목적을 돕고자, 나는 서슴없이 그 경찰관을 해임했어. 우리를 쩔쩔매게 하는 그 수수께끼는, 그 사람도 쩔쩔매게 하니까. 이것은 어떤 외부인도 우리를 도울 수 있는 일이 아니야. 그 사람은 내가 겪어야 할 고통에 짐을 더할 뿐이고, 레이철은 그 사람 이름만 들어도 미쳐 날뛴다네.\n\n\"앞날에 대한 나의 계획은 정해질 수 있는 만큼은 정해졌네. 지금 나의 생각은 레이철을 런던으로 데려가는 것이야—한편으로는 온전한 환경의 변화로 그 아이의 마음을 풀어 주려는 것이고, 한편으로는 가장 뛰어난 의료 조언을 구하면 무엇을 할 수 있을지 시험해 보려는 것이지. 자네에게 도회지에서 우리를 만나 달라고 청해도 되겠나? 나의 사랑하는 프랭클린, 자네도, 자네 나름의 방식으로, 나의 참을성을 본받아, 나처럼, 더 알맞은 때를 기다려야 하네. 자네가 그 잃어버린 보석을 좇는 조사에 베푼 그 값진 도움은, 지금 레이철의 이 끔찍한 마음 상태에서는, 여전히 용서받지 못한 죄야. 이 일에서 눈을 가린 채 움직이면서, 자네는, 그 애씀을 통해, 순진하게도 그 아이의 비밀을 발각의 위협에 빠뜨림으로써, 그 아이가 짊어져야 했던 근심의 짐을 더해 놓았네. 자네도 나도 상상하거나 내다볼 수 없었던 결과에 대해 자네에게 책임을 지우는 그 억지를, 나로서는 도무지 두둔할 수가 없어. 그 아이는 사리로 타이를 수 있는 상태가 아니야—그저 가엾이 여길 수 있을 뿐이지. 이런 말을 해야 하는 것이 서글프네만, 당분간은, 자네와 레이철은 떨어져 있는 편이 나아. 내가 자네에게 건넬 수 있는 유일한 조언은, 그 아이에게 시간을 주라는 것이야.\"\n\n나는 프랭클린 도련님이 진심으로 딱해서, 그 편지를 도로 건넸다. 나는 그가 우리 아가씨를 얼마나 아끼는지 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 그 어머니가 아가씨에 대해 전한 그 사연이 그의 가슴을 도려냈음을 보았다. \"속담을 아시지요, 도련님.\" 하는 것이 내가 그에게 한 말의 전부였다. \"일이 가장 나쁠 때는, 반드시 나아지게 마련이라잖습니까. 일이 지금보다 훨씬 더 나빠질 수야 없겠지요, 프랭클린 도련님.\"\n\n프랭클린 도련님은, 내가 감히 그에게 건네 본 그 소견에 별로 위로를 받은 기색도 없이, 이모님의 편지를 접었다.\n\n\"내가 그 끔찍한 다이아몬드를 가지고 런던에서 이곳으로 왔을 때,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"잉글랜드에 이 집보다 더 행복한 가정은 없었으리라 나는 믿네. 지금 이 가정을 보게! 뿔뿔이 흩어지고, 갈라져서—이곳의 공기마저 수수께끼와 의심으로 독이 올랐어! 우리가 흔들리는 모래에서, 내 외숙부 헌캐슬과, 그의 생일 선물에 대해 이야기하던 그날 아침을 기억하나? 월장석은 대령 자신도 결코 꿈에도 그리지 못한 방식으로, 대령의 복수를 이루어 주었네, 베터리지!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 내 손을 잡고 흔들고는, 조랑말 이륜마차로 나갔다.\n\n나는 층계를 내려 그를 따라갔다. 그가 제 인생의 가장 행복한 시절을 보낸 그 오래된 집을, 이런 식으로 떠나는 것을 보는 것은 몹시 서글펐다. 페넬로피가 (이 집에서 벌어진 온갖 일로 몹시 심란해져서) 그에게 작별을 고하려고 울면서 돌아 나왔다. 프랭클린 도련님은 그 아이에게 입을 맞추었다. 나는 \"진심으로 반가운 인사이옵니다, 도련님.\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯 손을 흔들었다. 다른 하녀들 몇몇이 나타나, 모퉁이를 돌아 그를 배웅하며 엿보았다. 그는 여자들이 모두 좋아하는 그런 사내들 가운데 하나였다. 마지막 순간에, 나는 조랑말 이륜마차를 멈춰 세우고, 편지로나마 그의 소식을 듣게 해 달라고 청을 넣었다. 그는 내 말을 귀담아듣는 기색이 아니었다—그는 이것저것 둘러보며, 그 오래된 집과 뜰에 일종의 작별을 고하고 있었다. \"어디로 가시는지 저희에게 말씀해 주십시오, 도련님!\" 하고 나는, 이륜마차를 붙든 채, 그런 식으로나마 그의 앞날 계획을 캐내 보려 애쓰며 말했다. 프랭클린 도련님은 별안간 제 모자를 눈 위로 푹 눌러 내렸다. \"가느냐고?\" 하고 그가, 내 말을 따라 되뇌며 말한다. \"나는 지옥으로 가네!\" 조랑말이 그 말에 흠칫했다, 마치 그 말에 기독교인다운 공포를 느끼기라도 한 듯이. \"하느님께서 축복하시기를, 도련님, 어디로 가시든지요!\" 하는 것이, 그가 눈에서도 귀에서도 사라지기 전에 내가 할 수 있었던 말의 전부였다. 다정하고 유쾌한 신사분! 그 온갖 흠과 어리석음에도 불구하고, 다정하고 유쾌한 신사분! 그는 마님의 집을 떠나면서, 뒤에 서글픈 빈자리를 남겼다.\n\n그 토요일 밤, 긴 여름 저녁이 저물어 들 무렵에는, 넉넉히 따분하고 을씨년스러웠다.\n\n나는 내 담뱃대와 내 「로빈슨 크루소」에 바싹 붙어 있음으로써 내 기운이 처지지 않게 붙들었다. (페넬로피만 빼고) 여자들은 로재나의 자살에 대해 이야기하며 시간을 보냈다. 그들은 하나같이, 그 가엾은 아이가 월장석을 훔쳤으며, 발각될까 두려워 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 의견을 고집스럽게 붙들고 있었다. 내 딸은, 물론, 저 혼자 내내 해 온 말을 굳게 지켰다. 그 자살의 정말 밑바탕에 있던 동기에 대한 그 아이의 생각은, 참으로 묘하게도, 우리 아가씨가 제 결백을 단언한 그것이 어그러지는 바로 그 지점에서 똑같이 어그러졌다. 그것은 로재나가 프리징홀로 남몰래 다녀온 일과, 잠옷 문제에 있어서의 로재나의 행적을 온전히 설명되지 않은 채로 남겨 두었다. 페넬로피에게 이것을 짚어 주어 봐야 소용이 없었다. 그 반론은 방수 외투에 소나기 한 줄기가 미치는 만큼의 감명밖에 그 아이에게 주지 못했다. 사실인즉, 내 딸은 이치를 넘어서는 나의 기질을 물려받았다—그리고, 그 재간에 있어서는, 제 친아비를 한참 앞질러 있다.\n\n그 이튿날 (일요일), 에이블화이트 씨 댁에 두었던 지붕 있는 마차가 우리에게로 텅 빈 채 돌아왔다. 마부가 나에게 전할 말과, 마님의 몸종과 페넬로피에게 줄 문서로 된 지시를 가져왔다.\n\n그 전언은, 마님이 월요일에 레이철 아가씨를 런던에 있는 당신 집으로 데려가기로 정하셨음을 내게 알려 주었다. 그 문서로 된 지시는, 두 하녀에게 어떤 옷가지가 필요한지를 알리고, 정해진 시각에 도회지에서 제 여주인들을 만나라고 일러 두었다. 다른 하인들도 대부분 뒤따르기로 되어 있었다. 마님은, 이 집에서 그런 일이 벌어진 뒤로, 레이철 아가씨가 이 집으로 돌아오기를 어찌나 꺼리는지를 아시고, 프리징홀에서 곧장 런던으로 가기로 정하신 터였다. 나는, 안팎의 일들을 돌보기 위해, 다음 분부가 있을 때까지 시골에 남아 있기로 되어 있었다. 나와 함께 남은 하인들은 식대만 받는 형편에 놓이게 되었다.\n\n이 모든 것으로 인해, 우리가 뿔뿔이 흩어지고 갈라진 가정이 되었다던 프랭클린 도련님의 말이 떠오르자, 내 마음은 자연히 프랭클린 도련님 그 자신에게로 이끌렸다. 그를 생각하면 할수록, 나는 그의 앞날 행보에 대해 더욱 마음이 편치 않았다. 그것은, 내가 일요일 우편으로, 그의 아버지의 시종인 제프코 씨에게 (내가 지난날 알고 지내던 사람이다) 편지를 써서, 프랭클린 도련님이 런던에 다다르거든 무엇을 하기로 정했는지 알려 달라고 청하는 것으로 끝났다.\n\n일요일 저녁은, 그럴 수만 있다면, 토요일 저녁보다도 더 따분했다. 우리는 이 섬나라에서 수십만 사람이 일주일에 한 번씩 어김없이 안식일을 끝맺듯, 그렇게 그 쉬는 날을 끝맺었다—다시 말해, 우리는 모두 잠자리에 들 때를 앞질러, 제 의자에 앉은 채 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n월요일이 나머지 식구들에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지 나는 모른다. 월요일은 나를 단단히 뒤흔들어 놓았다. 앞으로 일어날 일에 대한 커프 부장의 예언들 가운데 첫 번째—곧, 내가 욜랜드네에서 소식을 듣게 되리라는 것이—그날 들어맞았다.\n\n나는 페넬로피와 마님의 몸종을 런던행 짐과 함께 기차로 떠나보내고, 뜰을 어정거리고 있었는데, 그때 내 이름을 부르는 소리가 들렸다. 돌아보니, 나는 그 어부의 딸, 절름발이 루시와 얼굴을 마주하고 있었다. 그 아이의 저는 발과 야윈 몸(이 뒤엣것은, 내 생각에는, 여자에게 끔찍한 흠이다)을 빼면, 그 아이는 사내의 눈에 들 만한 몇 가지 어여쁜 데가 있었다. 가무잡잡하고, 예리하고, 영리한 얼굴과, 곱고 맑은 목소리와, 아름다운 갈색 머릿결이 그 아이의 미덕에 꼽혔다. 목발이 그 아이의 불운 목록에 나타났다. 그리고 성깔이 그 아이의 결점 총합에서 큰 몫을 차지했다.\n\n\"그래, 얘야,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"내게 무슨 볼일이냐?\"\n\n\"당신들이 프랭클린 블레이크라고 부르는 그 사내는 어디 있어요?\" 하고 그 아이가, 목발에 몸을 기댄 채, 사나운 눈길로 나를 쏘아보며 말한다.\n\n\"어느 신사분을 두고 그리 말하는 것은 예의 바른 말투가 아니다.\" 하고 내가 대답했다. \"마님의 조카분에 대해 알고 싶거든, 그분을 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이라 일컫는 것이 좋을 게다.\"\n\n그 아이가 내게로 절뚝절뚝 한 걸음 다가서더니, 나를 산 채로 잡아먹을 듯한 낯빛을 지었다. \"프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이라고요?\" 하고 그 아이가 내 말을 되뇌었다. \"살인자 프랭클린 블레이크가 그자에게 더 알맞은 이름이겠죠.\"\n\n돌아가신 베터리지 안사람과 겪은 내 경험이 여기서 요긴하게 쓰였다. 여자가 여러분의 성미를 돋우려 들 때는, 언제든지 판을 뒤집어, 대신 그 여자의 성미를 돋우어 놓으시라. 그들은 대체로 여러분이 제 방어를 위해 할 수 있는 온갖 애씀에는 대비가 되어 있지만, 그것만은 아니다. 한 마디가 백 마디만큼이나 효험을 낸다. 그리고 절름발이 루시에게는 한 마디가 효험을 냈다. 나는 그 아이의 얼굴을 유쾌하게 들여다보았다. 그리고 나는 말했다—\"흥!\"\n\n그 아이의 성깔이 대뜸 활활 타올랐다. 그 아이는 제 성한 발로 몸을 가누고, 제 목발을 들어, 땅바닥을 세 번 사납게 내리쳤다. \"저자는 살인자예요! 저자는 살인자예요! 저자는 살인자라고요! 저자가 로재나 스피어먼을 죽인 거예요!\" 그 아이는 그 대답을 목청껏 소리 질러 댔다. 우리 가까이 뜰에서 일하던 사람 한둘이 고개를 들어—그것이 절름발이 루시임을 보고는—저쪽에서 무엇을 예상해야 할지 알아채고는—다시 눈길을 돌렸다.\n\n\"저 도련님이 로재나 스피어먼을 죽였다고?\" 하고 내가 되뇌었다. \"무엇 때문에 그런 말을 하느냐, 루시?\"\n\n\"당신이 무슨 상관이에요? 사내가 무슨 상관이냐고요? 아! 그 애가 나처럼 사내들을 생각하기만 했어도, 지금 살아 있었을지 몰라요!\"\n\n\"그 아이는 늘 나를 다정하게 생각해 주었단다, 가엾은 것.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"그리고, 내 힘닿는 한, 나는 늘 그 아이에게 다정하게 대하려 애썼고.\"\n\n나는 그 말을 될 수 있는 대로 위로가 되게끔 뱉었다. 사실인즉, 나는 또 한 번 내 약삭빠른 대꾸로 그 아이를 성나게 할 마음이 없었다. 처음에 나는 그 아이의 성깔만을 알아챘었다. 이제 나는 그 아이의 비참함을 알아챘다—그리고 비참함이라는 것은, 여러분도 아시게 되겠지만, 미천한 살림에서는 드물지 않게 무례한 법이다. 나의 대답이 절름발이 루시를 누그러뜨렸다. 그 아이는 고개를 숙여, 그것을 제 목발 꼭대기에 얹었다.\n\n\"저는 그 애를 사랑했어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 나직이 말했다. \"그 애는 비참한 삶을 살았어요, 베터리지 아저씨—몹쓸 사람들이 그 애를 학대하고 그릇된 길로 이끌었지만—그것이 그 애의 다정한 성품을 망쳐 놓지는 못했어요. 그 애는 천사였어요. 그 애는 저와 함께 행복할 수도 있었어요. 저는 우리 둘이 자매처럼 함께 런던으로 가서, 바느질로 먹고살 계획을 세웠었죠. 그 사내가 이곳에 와서, 그것을 죄다 망쳐 놓았어요. 그자가 그 애를 홀렸어요. 그자가 그럴 뜻이 없었다느니, 몰랐다느니 하는 말은 마세요. 그자는 마땅히 알았어야 했어요. 그자는 마땅히 그 애를 가엾이 여겼어야 했어요. '그이 없이는 살 수가 없어—그런데, 아, 루시, 그이는 나를 쳐다보지도 않아.' 그 애가 한 말이 그거예요. 잔인해요, 잔인해요, 잔인해요. 저는 말했죠, '그런 식으로 애태울 값어치가 있는 사내는 없어.' 그러니까 그 애가 말했어요, '목숨을 바칠 값어치가 있는 사내들도 있어, 루시, 그리고 그이가 그런 사람이야.' 저는 돈을 조금 모아 두었었어요. 저는 아버지 어머니와 이야기를 매듭지어 놓았고요. 저는 그 애를 이곳에서 겪고 있던 그 굴욕에서 데리고 떠날 작정이었어요. 우리는 런던에 조그만 셋방을 얻어, 자매처럼 함께 살았을 거예요. 그 애는, 아저씨도 아시다시피, 배움이 넉넉했고, 글씨도 잘 썼어요. 바느질도 잽쌌고요. 저도 배움이 넉넉하고, 글씨도 잘 써요. 저는 그 애만큼 바느질이 잽싸지는 못해도—그럭저럭 해낼 수 있었을 거예요. 우리는 그런대로 먹고살 수 있었을 거예요. 그런데, 아! 오늘 아침에 무슨 일이 일어났나요? 오늘 아침에 무슨 일이 일어났느냐고요? 그 애의 편지가 와서 제게 이르기를, 그 애가 제 삶의 짐을 끝냈다는 거예요. 그 애의 편지가 와서, 제게 영영 작별을 고하는 거예요. 그자는 어디 있죠?\" 하고 그 아이가, 목발에서 고개를 들어, 눈물 사이로 다시 활활 타오르며 외친다. \"제가 예의를 갖추지 않고서는 입에 올려서도 안 된다는 그 신사분은 어디 있느냐고요? 하, 베터리지 아저씨, 가난한 자들이 부유한 자들에게 들고일어날 날이 머지않았어요. 부디 하늘이 그자부터 시작하게 하시기를. 부디 하늘이 그자부터 시작하게 하시기를.\"\n\n여기 또 하나의 여느 만큼 착한 기독교인이 있었고, 여기 바로 그 여느 만큼의 기독교 신앙이 너무 몰아붙여진 데서 비롯된 그 흔한 무너짐이 있었다! 목사님 자신도 (이렇게 말하는 것이 대단한 말임을 나도 인정하지만) 지금 그 아이가 놓인 상태로는 그 아이를 좀처럼 타이를 수 없었으리라. 내가 감히 할 수 있었던 것이라곤, 무언가 들을 값어치가 있는 것이 튀어나오리라는 바람으로, 그 아이를 요점에 붙들어 두는 것뿐이었다.\n\n\"프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 무슨 볼일이냐?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그자를 만나고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 특별한 일로?\"\n\n\"그자에게 건넬 편지가 있어요.\"\n\n\"로재나 스피어먼에게서 온?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"네 편지에 함께 부쳐 온?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n어둠이 걷히려는 것인가? 내가 몹시도 알아내고 싶던 그 온갖 것들이, 제 발로 다가와 내게 제 몸을 내맡기려는 것인가? 나는 잠시 뜸을 들여야 했다. 커프 부장이 제 병균을 뒤에 남겨 놓고 간 것이다. 나 자신에게만 나타나는 어떤 조짐과 낌새가, 탐정 열병이 다시 도지기 시작했음을 내게 일러 주었다.\n\n\"프랭클린 도련님은 만날 수 없다.\" 하고 내가 말했다.\n\n\"저는 반드시, 기어이, 그자를 만날 거예요.\"\n\n\"그분은 어젯밤 런던으로 가셨다.\"\n\n절름발이 루시가 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보더니, 내가 진실을 말하고 있음을 알아챘다. 더 말 한마디 없이, 그 아이는 대뜸 다시 코브스 홀 쪽으로 돌아섰다.\n\n\"멈춰라!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"내일 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님의 소식을 받게 될 게다. 네 편지를 내게 주면, 우편으로 그분께 부쳐 주마.\"\n\n절름발이 루시가 제 목발에 몸을 가누고, 어깨 너머로 나를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"저는 이걸 제 손에서 그자의 손으로 직접 건네야 해요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 이걸 그자에게 다른 어떤 식으로도 건네지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"내가 편지를 써서, 네가 한 말을 그분께 전해 줄까?\"\n\n\"제가 그자를 미워한다고 전해요. 그럼 당신은 진실을 전하는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래. 그런데 편지 말이다만——?\"\n\n\"그자가 편지를 원하거든, 이곳으로 돌아와서, 저에게서 받아 가야 해요.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그 아이는 코브스 홀로 가는 길로 절뚝절뚝 떠나갔다. 탐정 열병이 그 자리에서 내 온갖 위신을 태워 버렸다. 나는 그 아이를 뒤따라가, 그 아이를 말하게 하려 애썼다. 죄다 헛일이었다. 사내라는 것이 내 불운이었다—그리고 절름발이 루시는 나를 실망시키는 것을 즐겼다. 그날 늦게, 나는 그 아이의 어머니에게 운을 걸어 보았다. 착한 욜랜드 부인은 그저 울면서, 그 네덜란드 병에서 나오는 위안 한 방울을 권할 뿐이었다. 나는 그 어부를 바닷가에서 찾아냈다. 그는 \"고약한 일\"이라고 말하고는, 제 그물을 계속 손질했다. 아비도 어미도 나보다 더 아는 것이 없었다. 시험해 볼 남은 한 가지 길은, 아침과 함께 올지도 모를 요행—곧, 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 편지를 쓰는 것이었다.\n\n내가 화요일 아침에 우체부를 어떻게 지켜보았을지는 여러분의 상상에 맡기겠다. 그는 내게 편지 두 통을 가져왔다. 하나는, 페넬로피에게서 온 것으로 (나는 그것을 읽어 낼 참을성도 거의 없었다), 마님과 레이철 아가씨가 런던에 무사히 자리 잡았음을 알렸다. 다른 하나는, 제프코 씨에게서 온 것으로, 그 주인의 아드님이 이미 잉글랜드를 떠났음을 내게 알려 주었다.\n\n그 대도회지에 다다르자, 프랭클린 도련님은, 보아하니, 곧장 제 아버지의 저택으로 갔다. 그는 곤란한 때에 다다랐다. 아버지 되는 블레이크 씨는 하원 일에 눈코 뜰 새가 없었고, 그날 밤에는 집에서 그들이 \"개인 법안\"이라 부르는 그 가장 좋아하는 의회 장난감을 가지고 소일하고 있었다. 제프코 씨 자신이 프랭클린 도련님을 제 아버지의 서재로 안내해 들였다. \"얘야 프랭클린! 어째서 이런 식으로 나를 놀라게 하느냐? 무슨 일이 잘못됐느냐?\" \"네, 레이철에게 무언가 잘못된 일이 있어요. 저는 그것 때문에 몹시 괴롭습니다.\" \"그 말을 들으니 서글프구나. 허나 나는 지금 네 말을 들어 줄 수가 없다.\" \"언제쯤 들어 주실 수 있어요?\" \"얘야! 너를 속이지는 않으마. 나는 회기가 끝나야 들어 줄 수 있다, 한순간도 그 전에는 안 되고. 잘 자거라.\" \"고맙습니다, 아버지. 안녕히 주무세요.\"\n\n제프코 씨가 내게 전해 준 바로는, 서재 안에서 오간 대화는 그러했다. 서재 밖에서 오간 대화는, 그보다도 더 짧았다. \"제프코, 내일 아침 조수 맞춰 떠나는 기차가 몇 시에 출발하는지 알아봐 주게.\" \"여섯 시 사십 분입니다, 프랭클린 도련님.\" \"다섯 시에 나를 깨워 주게.\" \"외국으로 가시는 겁니까, 도련님?\" \"가네, 제프코, 철도가 나를 데려가겠다는 곳이 어디든 말이야.\" \"아버님께 말씀드릴까요, 도련님?\" \"그래, 회기가 끝나거든 말씀드리게.\"\n\n이튿날 아침 프랭클린 도련님은 외국 땅을 향해 떠났다. 그가 어느 특정한 곳으로 향하는지는 아무도 (그 자신을 포함하여) 감히 짐작할 수 없었다. 우리는 그다음번에 유럽에서, 아시아에서, 아프리카에서, 아니면 아메리카에서 그의 소식을 들을지 몰랐다. 제프코 씨의 견해로는, 그 승산이 지구의 네 방위에 걸쳐 될 수 있는 한 고르게 나뉘어 있었다.\n\n이 소식은—절름발이 루시와 프랭클린 도련님을 한자리에 모을 온갖 가망을 막아 버림으로써—발견으로 나아가는 내 걸음의 모든 진전을 대뜸 멈춰 세웠다. 제 동료 하인이 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님을 향한 짝사랑 때문에 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 페넬로피의 믿음은 굳어졌다—그리고 그것이 전부였다. 로재나가 제 죽음 뒤에 그에게 건네지도록 남긴 그 편지가, 그가 그 아이의 생전에 그 아이가 그에게 하려 애썼으리라 의심한 그 고백을 담고 있었는지 아닌지는, 도무지 알 길이 없었다. 그것은 그저, 제 손이 닿지 않는 사람을 향한 불행한 연정의 비밀 말고는 아무것도 이르지 않는, 작별의 말일 뿐일지도 몰랐다. 아니면 그것은, 월장석을 잃은 때부터 흔들리는 모래에서 제 스스로의 파멸로 달려든 때까지, 커프 부장이 그 아이에게서 알아챈 그 기이한 행적들에 대한 온전한 진실을 털어놓은 것일지도 몰랐다. 그것은 봉인된 편지로 절름발이 루시의 손에 놓였고, 나에게도, 그 아이의 친부모를 포함하여 그 아이 둘레의 모든 이에게도, 봉인된 편지로 남았다. 우리는 모두 그 아이가 그 죽은 여자의 속내를 알고 있었다고 의심했다. 우리는 모두 그 아이를 말하게 하려 애썼다. 우리는 모두 실패했다. 하인들이, 이번엔 이 사람이, 이번엔 저 사람이—여전히 로재나가 다이아몬드를 훔쳐 숨겨 두었다는 믿음을 붙든 채—그 아이의 자취가 이어졌던 그 바위들을 들여다보고 쑤셔 보았으나, 들여다보고 쑤셔 봐도 헛일이었다. 밀물이 빠지고, 밀물이 밀려들었다. 여름이 흘러가고, 가을이 왔다. 그리고 그 아이의 몸을 감춘 유사는, 그 아이의 비밀도 함께 감추었다.\n\n일요일 아침 프랭클린 도련님이 잉글랜드를 떠났다는 소식과, 월요일 오후 마님이 레이철 아가씨와 함께 런던에 다다랐다는 소식은, 여러분도 아시다시피, 화요일 우편으로 내게 이르렀다. 수요일이 와서는, 아무것도 가져오지 않았다. 목요일이 페넬로피에게서 온 두 번째 소식 꾸러미를 내놓았다.\n\n내 딸의 편지는, 어떤 대단한 런던 의사가 우리 아가씨에 대해 진찰을 받았는데, 아가씨를 즐겁게 해 드리는 편이 낫겠다고 소견을 낸 값으로 일 기니를 벌어 갔다고 내게 알려 주었다. 꽃 전시회, 오페라, 무도회—온갖 흥겨운 놀이가 줄줄이 앞에 놓여 있었다. 그리고 레이철 아가씨는, 그 어머니가 놀랍게도, 그 모든 것에 열심히 빠져들었다. 고드프리 씨가 다녀갔는데, 생일 때 운을 걸어 보았다가 겪은 그 홀대에도 불구하고, 뻔히 제 사촌 누이에게 여느 때처럼 다정했다. 페넬로피가 몹시 아쉬워하게도, 그는 더할 나위 없이 정중하게 맞아들여졌고, 그 자리에서 제가 맡은 부인 자선회 가운데 하나에 레이철 아가씨의 이름을 올렸다. 마님은 기운이 없으시고, 당신 변호사와 두 차례 긴 면담을 가지셨다고 전해졌다. 이어서 어떤 억측들이 뒤따랐는데, 그것은 그 집안의 어느 가난한 친척—내가 생일 만찬에 대한 내 이야기에서, 고드프리 씨 옆자리에 앉아, 샴페인에 제법 취향이 있다고 언급한 바 있는 어느 클랙 양에 관한 것이었다. 페넬로피는 클랙 양이 아직 찾아오지 않은 것을 알고 놀랐다. 그 여자는 분명 오래지 않아 여느 때처럼 마님에게 찰싹 달라붙을 터였다—어쩌고저쩌고, 이러쿵저러쿵, 여자들이 종이 위에서나 밖에서나 서로 헐뜯는 그 방식으로 말이다. 나도 인정하거니와, 이것은 한 가지 이유가 아니었다면 언급할 값어치가 없었으리라. 듣자 하니 여러분은 나와 헤어진 뒤에 클랙 양에게 넘겨질 성싶다. 그렇게 되거든, 그 여자가 여러분의 이 미천한 하인에 대해 말하거든, 부디 그 여자 말은 한마디도 믿지 말아 주는 호의를 내게 베풀어 주시라.\n\n금요일에는, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다—개 한 마리가 귀 뒤에 무언가 헐기 시작하는 낌새를 보인 것만 빼고. 나는 그놈에게 갈매나무 시럽 한 첩을 먹이고, 다음 분부가 있을 때까지 고깃국물과 채소로 식이를 조절해 주었다. 이것을 언급하는 것을 용서하시라. 어쩌다 보니 슬그머니 끼어들었다. 부디 그냥 넘어가 주시라. 나는 여러분의 세련된 현대적 취향에 대한 나의 죄과의 끝에 빠르게 다다르고 있다. 게다가, 그 개는 착한 짐승이었고, 정성 어린 치료를 받을 만했다. 정말로 그러했다.\n\n한 주의 마지막 날인 토요일은, 나의 이야기에서도 마지막 날이다.\n\n그날 아침 우편이 런던 신문 한 부의 모습으로 내게 뜻밖의 것을 가져왔다. 그 겉봉의 글씨가 나를 어리둥절하게 했다. 나는 그것을 내 수첩에 적힌 그 돈놀이꾼의 이름과 주소와 견주어 보고는, 대뜸 그것이 커프 부장의 글씨임을 알아냈다.\n\n이 발견 뒤에, 넉넉히 열심히 그 신문을 훑어보다가, 나는 경찰 보도 가운데 하나에 잉크로 두른 표시가 그어져 있는 것을 발견했다. 여기, 여러분께 바친다. 내가 읽은 대로 그것을 읽어 보시라, 그러면 여러분은 그날의 소식을 내게 보내 준 그 부장의 정중한 배려에 마땅한 값을 매기게 될 것이다.\n\n\"램버스—법정이 문을 닫기 조금 전, 고대 보석과 조각과 음각 세공 등등의 이름난 거래상인 셉티머스 루커 씨가, 재판 중인 치안판사에게 조언을 구했다. 신청인이 진술하기로는, 그는 온종일 틈틈이, 거리를 들끓게 하는 그 떠돌이 인도인 몇몇의 행패로 시달렸다는 것이다. 하소연 대상이 된 자들은 셋이었다. 경찰에 의해 쫓겨난 뒤에도, 그들은 몇 번이고 다시 돌아와, 자선을 청하는 척하며 집 안에 들어오려 시도했다. 앞쪽에서 쫓겨나면, 그들은 다시 그 건물 뒤쪽에서 발견되곤 했다. 하소연한 그 행패 말고도, 루커 씨는 절도가 꾀해지고 있을지 모른다는 얼마간의 우려에 사로잡혀 있다고 밝혔다. 그의 소장품에는 최고 값어치의 고전적이고 동양적인 진귀한 보석이 많이 들어 있었다. 그는 바로 그 전날, 절도 미수 혐의로 상아 조각에 숙련된 직공 하나를 (우리가 이해하기로는, 인도 태생이었다) 마지못해 제 밑에서 내보내야 했다. 그리고 그는 이 사내와 그가 하소연한 그 거리의 요술쟁이들이 서로 짜고 움직이고 있지는 않은지 결코 확신할 수 없다고 느꼈다. 사람들을 모아, 거리에서 소동을 일으키고, 그렇게 빚어진 혼란을 틈타, 집 안에 발을 들이는 것이 그들의 목적일지도 몰랐다. 치안판사에게 답하며, 루커 씨는 절도가 꾀해지고 있다는 어떤 시도에 대해서도 내놓을 증거가 없음을 인정했다. 그는 그 인도인들이 일으킨 행패와 훼방에 대해서는 딱 잘라 말할 수 있어도, 그 밖의 어떤 것에 대해서도 그럴 수는 없었다. 치안판사는, 만일 그 행패가 되풀이되거든, 신청인이 그 인도인들을 그 법정으로 소환할 수 있으며, 거기서 그들은 법령에 따라 손쉽게 다루어질 수 있으리라고 소견을 밝혔다. 루커 씨가 지닌 귀중품에 대해서는, 루커 씨 자신이 그것들의 안전한 보관을 위한 최선의 방책을 강구해야 할 것이었다. 그는 아마 경찰과 연락을 취하고, 그들의 경험이 일러 줄 만한 추가적인 대비책을 채택하는 것이 좋으리라. 신청인은 판사에게 감사하고, 물러갔다.\"\n\n옛 현인 가운데 하나가 (무슨 자리에서였는지는 잊었다) 제 동료 인간들에게 \"끝을 바라보라\"고 권한 것으로 전해진다. 나의 이 여러 쪽의 끝을 바라보며, 그리고 며칠 전부터 그것을 어떻게 써낼까 궁리하며, 나는 나의 이 담담한 사실 진술이, 더없이 알맞게, 저절로 결말에 다다르고 있음을 알아챈다. 우리는, 이 월장석 일에 있어서, 하나의 경이에서 또 하나의 경이로 나아왔다. 그리고 여기서 우리는 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 큰 경이로 끝맺는다—곧, 커프 부장이 그 세 가지 예언을 한 때로부터 일주일도 채 안 되어 그것들이 이루어졌다는 것이다.\n\n월요일에 욜랜드네에서 소식을 들은 뒤, 나는 이제 그 인도인들에 대해 들었고, 런던발 소식에서 그 돈놀이꾼에 대해 들었다—레이철 아가씨 자신도, 기억하시라, 그때 마침 런던에 있었다는 것을. 보시다시피, 나는, 그것이 나 자신의 견해에 딱 어긋나게 말할 때조차, 일들을 가장 나쁘게 놓는다. 만일 여러분이 나를 저버리고, 여러분 앞에 놓인 증거로 보아 부장 편을 든다면—만일 여러분이 볼 수 있는 유일하게 사리에 맞는 설명이, 레이철 아가씨와 루커 씨가 서로 손을 잡았음이 틀림없고, 월장석이 지금 그 돈놀이꾼의 집에 저당 잡혀 있음이 틀림없다는 것이라면—인정하거니와, 나는 여러분이 그 결론에 다다르는 것을 나무랄 수가 없다. 어둠 속에서, 나는 여러분을 이만큼 데려왔다. 어둠 속에서 나는, 최고의 경의를 담아, 여러분을 남겨 두고 떠날 수밖에 없다.\n\n어째서 그럴 수밖에 없느냐고, 물음이 나올 법하다. 어째서 이만큼 나와 함께 걸어온 사람들을, 내가 몸소 앉아 있는 그 더 높은 깨달음의 경지로 데려가지 않느냐고 말이다.\n\n이에 대한 답으로, 나는 그저, 내가 명령을 받아 움직이고 있으며, 그 명령이 (내가 이해하기로는) 진실을 위하여 내게 내려졌다는 것만 말할 수 있다. 나는 이 이야기에서 그 당시 나 자신이 알던 것 이상을 이야기하는 것이 금지되어 있다. 다시 말해, 더 쉽게 이르자면, 나는 나 자신의 경험의 테두리 안에 엄격히 머물러야 하며, 다른 사람들이 내게 말해 준 것을 여러분에게 알려서는 안 된다—여러분이 그 정보를 그 다른 사람들 자신에게서, 직접, 얻게 되어 있다는 아주 넉넉한 이유에서다. 이 월장석 일에 있어서 그 방침은, 보고를 내놓는 것이 아니라, 증인을 내세우는 것이다. 나는 이 집안의 한 사람이 오십 년 뒤에 이 쪽들을 읽는 모습을 마음속에 그려 본다. 아이고! 아무것도 전해 들은 대로 받아들이지 말라는 청을 받고, 모든 점에서 법정의 판사처럼 대접받는 것을, 그가 얼마나 큰 영예로 느낄 것인가.\n\n그러면, 이 자리에서, 우리는 헤어진다—적어도, 당분간은—오래 함께 여행한 끝에, 부디, 양쪽 다 벗다운 정을 품고서 말이다. 그 인도 다이아몬드의 악마의 춤은 제 길을 더듬어 런던까지 다다랐다. 그리고 여러분은 그것을 좇아 런던으로 가야 하며, 나는 시골집에 남는다. 이 글의 흠들을—내가 나 자신에 대해 너무 많이 지껄인 것과, 여러분에게 너무 허물없이 군 것이 아닌가 싶은 것을—부디 용서하시라. 나는 해코지할 뜻이 없다. 그리고 나는 (막 저녁을 마친 참이라) 마님의 맥주 한 잔으로, 더없이 정중하게, 여러분의 건강과 번영을 위해 마신다. 부디 여러분이 나의 이 글줄들 속에서, 「로빈슨 크루소」가 그 무인도에서의 제 경험 속에서 찾아낸 것을 찾아내기를—곧, \"스스로를 위로할 무언가를, 그리고 선과 악의 명세 가운데 계정의 대변(貸邊) 쪽에 적어 넣을 무언가를.\"—안녕히.\n\n여러 편의 이야기로 서술된 사건들.\n\n# FIRST NARRATIVE.\n\n고(故) 존 버린더 경의 조카딸, 클랙 양이 기고함.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n나는 나의 사랑하는 부모님(두 분 다 이제는 천국에 계신다)께, 아주 어린 나이에 질서와 규칙의 습관을 내 안에 심어 주신 데 대해 큰 은혜를 입고 있다.\n\n그 행복했던 지난 시절에, 나는 낮이든 밤이든 어느 때고 머리를 단정히 하도록, 그리고 잠자리에 들기 전에 내 옷가지를 하나하나, 똑같은 순서로, 똑같은 의자 위에, 침대 발치의 똑같은 자리에, 정성껏 개어 두도록 배웠다. 나의 작은 일기장에 그날의 일들을 적는 것이 언제나 그 개는 일에 앞섰다. (잠자리에서 되뇌는) '저녁 찬송'이 언제나 그 개는 일 뒤에 따랐다. 그리고 어린 시절의 그 달콤한 잠이 언제나 '저녁 찬송' 뒤에 따랐다.\n\n훗날의 삶에서 (아아!) 그 찬송은 서글프고 쓰라린 명상으로 이어졌다. 그리고 그 달콤한 잠은 근심으로 편치 못한 베개를 맴도는 그 끊긴 선잠과 고약하게 맞바꾸어졌을 뿐이다. 다른 한편으로, 나는 내 옷을 개고, 나의 작은 일기를 적는 일을 계속해 왔다. 앞엣것의 습관은 나를 나의 행복했던 어린 시절로—아빠가 파산하시기 전으로 이어 준다. 뒤엣것의 습관은—여태껏은 주로 우리 모두가 아담에게서 물려받은 그 타락한 본성을 다스리는 데 도움이 되어 왔거니와—전혀 다른 방식으로, 뜻밖에도 나의 미천한 이익에 중요한 것으로 드러났다. 그것이 이 가엾은 나로 하여금, 내 돌아가신 외숙부가 혼인으로 맺어진 그 집안의 어느 부유한 일원의 변덕에 봉사할 수 있게 해 준 것이다. 나는 다행스럽게도 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 쓸모가 있게 되었다.\n\n나는 지난 얼마 동안 혼인으로 맺어진 친척들의 온갖 소식에서 끊겨 있었다. 우리가 외따로 떨어지고 가난할 때, 우리는 드물지 않게 잊힌다. 나는 지금, 아껴 쓰기 위하여, 브르타뉴의 어느 작은 마을에 살고 있는데, 그곳에는 진지한 영국인 벗들의 선택받은 무리가 거주하며, 개신교 목사님 한 분과 값싼 장터라는 값을 매길 수 없는 이점을 지니고 있다.\n\n이 은거지에서—우리를 에워싼 저 울부짖는 교황 숭배의 대양 한가운데 있는 파트모스에서—마침내 잉글랜드에서 온 편지 한 통이 내게 다다랐다. 나는 나의 보잘것없는 존재가 별안간 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 기억되었음을 알게 되었다. 나의 부유한 친척은—그가 영적으로 부유한 친척이라고 덧붙일 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으랴!—내게 무언가 바라는 바가 있음을 숨기려는 시도조차 없이 편지를 쓴다. 월장석이라는 그 개탄스러운 추문을 들쑤셔 보려는 변덕이 그를 사로잡았다. 그리하여 나는, 런던의 버린더 이모님 댁에 머무는 동안 나 자신이 목격한 것에 대한 기록을 씀으로써 그를 도와야 한다. 내게 금전적 보상이 제시된다—부유한 자들 특유의 무정함과 더불어 말이다. 나는 세월이 겨우 아물려 놓은 상처들을 다시 열어야 한다. 나는 더없이 사무치게 고통스러운 기억들을 떠올려야 한다—그리고 이 일을 마치고 나면, 나는 블레이크 씨의 수표라는 모양을 한 새로운 생채기로 보상받는 셈이 된다. 나의 천성은 약하다. 기독교인다운 겸손이 죄 많은 자존심을 이기고, 극기가 그 수표를 받아들이기까지, 그것은 내게 힘겨운 씨름을 치르게 했다.\n\n나의 일기가 없었더라면, 나는—부디 가장 노골적인 말로 그것을 표현하게 해 다오!—과연 정직하게 내 돈을 벌 수 있었을지 의심스럽다. 나의 일기와 더불어, 이 가엾은 품팔이꾼은 (자기를 모욕한 블레이크 씨를 용서하는 이 여자는) 제 삯을 받을 자격이 있다. 사랑하는 버린더 이모님 댁에 머물던 그 시절, 그 무엇도 나를 벗어나지 못했다. 모든 것이 (나의 어린 시절 훈련 덕분에) 날마다 일어나는 그대로 적혔다. 그리고 모든 것이, 가장 사소한 세부까지, 여기에 이야기될 것이다. 진실에 대한 나의 성스러운 존중은 (하느님 고맙게도) 사람에 대한 나의 존중보다 훨씬 위에 있다. 이 쪽들 가운데, 여기에 주로 관련된 사람에게 충분히 아첨이 되지 못할 만한 것을 억누르는 일은, 블레이크 씨에게 손쉬운 일이리라. 그는 나의 시간은 샀으나, 그의 그 재물조차 나의 양심까지 살 수는 없다.*\n\n[*주. 프랭클린 블레이크가 덧붙임.—클랙 양은 이 점에 대해 마음을 아주 편히 놓아도 좋다. 그녀의 원고에서든, 내 손을 거치는 다른 어느 원고에서든, 아무것도 덧붙여지거나, 고쳐지거나, 지워지지 않을 것이다. 어느 필자가 어떤 견해를 밝히든, 어떤 별난 처리 방식이 내가 지금 모으고 있는 이야기들을 특징짓든, 그리고 어쩌면 문학적 의미에서 그것들을 망가뜨리든, 처음부터 끝까지 어느 한 줄도 어디에서도 손대어지지 않을 것이다. 진짜 문서로서 그것들은 내게 보내진다—그리고 진짜 문서로서 나는 그것들을, 사실을 증언할 수 있는 증인들의 확인을 곁들여, 보존할 것이다. 다만 덧붙여 둘 것은, 클랙 양의 이야기에 \"주로 관련된 사람\"은, 지금 이 순간, 클랙 양의 붓이 부리는 가장 매서운 놀림을 꿋꿋이 견뎌 낼 뿐만 아니라, 심지어 그것을 클랙 양의 됨됨이를 드러내 보이는 도구로서 의심할 여지 없는 값어치를 지녔음을 알아볼 만큼 충분히 행복하다는 것이다.]\n\n나의 일기는, 내가 1848년 7월 3일 월요일에, 몬터규 스퀘어에 있는 버린더 이모님 댁을 우연히 지나가고 있었음을 내게 일러 준다.\n\n덧문이 열리고, 차양이 걷혀 올라간 것을 보고, 나는 문을 두드려 안부를 여쭙는 것이 예의 바른 배려의 행위이리라 느꼈다. 문을 열어 준 사람이 내게 알려 주기로는, 이모님과 그 따님이 (나는 정말이지 그 아이를 내 사촌이라고 부를 수가 없다!) 일주일 전에 시골에서 올라와, 런던에 얼마간 머물 작정을 하고 있다는 것이었다. 나는 대뜸 위층으로 전언을 올려, 그들을 방해하기를 사양하고, 다만 내가 무슨 도움이 될 수 있을지만 알고 싶다고 청했다.\n\n문을 열어 준 그 사람은, 무례한 침묵 속에 내 전언을 받아 들고, 나를 현관에 세워 둔 채 가 버렸다. 그 여자는 베터리지라는 이름의 이교도 늙은이의 딸로—오래도록, 너무도 오래도록, 이모님 집안에서 참아 준 자다. 나는 대답을 기다리려고 현관에 앉았다—그리고, 언제나 가방에 소책자 몇 부를 지니고 다니는 터라, 나는 문을 열어 준 그 사람에게 참으로 하느님의 섭리처럼 꼭 들어맞는 것으로 드러난 한 부를 골라냈다. 현관은 지저분했고, 의자는 딱딱했다. 허나 악을 선으로 갚는다는 그 복된 자각이 나를 그런 하잘것없는 헤아림 따위로부터 넉넉히 높이 들어 올려 주었다. 그 소책자는 젊은 여자들에게 옷치레의 죄스러움에 대해 이르는 한 연작 가운데 하나였다. 문체에 있어서 그것은 경건하게 친근했다. 그 제목은 '그대의 모자 리본에 관하여 한 말씀'이었다.\n\n\"마님께서 대단히 고마워하시며, 내일 두 시에 점심 드시러 오시라 청하십니다.\"\n\n나는 그 여자가 그 전언을 건네는 태도와, 그 끔찍하게 뻔뻔한 눈초리를 그냥 넘겼다. 나는 이 젊은 버림받은 자에게 감사했다. 그리고 나는 기독교인다운 관심의 어조로 말했다. \"소책자 한 부를 받아 주는 호의를 베풀어 주겠니?\"\n\n그 여자는 제목을 들여다보았다. \"이거 남자가 썼나요, 여자가 썼나요, 아가씨? 여자가 쓴 거라면, 그런 이유로 저는 읽지 않는 편이 낫겠어요. 남자가 쓴 거라면, 그 사람에게 아무것도 모르면서 썼다고 전해 주시길 청합니다.\" 그 여자는 소책자를 내게 도로 건네고는, 문을 열었다. 우리는 어떻게든 좋은 씨앗을 뿌려야 한다. 나는 문이 내 뒤로 닫힐 때까지 기다렸다가, 소책자를 우편함에 슬며시 밀어 넣었다. 또 한 부의 소책자를 지하실 난간 사이로 떨어뜨리고 나자, 나는 타인들을 향한 무거운 책임에서, 얼마쯤이나마, 홀가분해진 느낌이 들었다.\n\n그날 저녁 우리는 '어머니회 아동복 개조 협회'의 선정 위원회 모임이 있었다. 이 훌륭한 자선회의 목적은—진지한 사람이라면 다 알거니와—구원받지 못한 아버지들의 바지를 전당포에서 구해 내고, 그것을 즉시 죄 없는 아들의 몸에 맞도록 줄임으로써, 그 개심할 길 없는 어버이 편에서 그것을 되찾아 가는 일을 막는 것이다. 나는, 그 무렵, 그 선정 위원회의 일원이었다. 그리고 내가 여기서 그 협회를 언급하는 것은, 나의 소중하고 감탄스러운 벗, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 우리의 도덕적이고 물질적인 유익의 일에 함께하고 있었기 때문이다. 나는 지금 내가 적고 있는 그 월요일 저녁에 이사회 방에서 그를 보리라 기대하고 있었고, 우리가 만나거든, 사랑하는 버린더 이모님이 런던에 도착하셨음을 그에게 알리려 작정하고 있었다. 나의 큰 실망스럽게도 그는 끝내 나타나지 않았다. 그의 부재에 놀라움의 심정을 내비치자, 위원회의 자매들이 다 함께 저마다의 바지에서 (그날 밤 우리는 일감이 크게 밀려 있었다) 고개를 들고는, 놀라며, 내가 그 소식을 듣지 못했느냐고 물었다. 나는 나의 무지를 인정했고, 그러자 나는, 이를테면, 이 이야기의 출발점을 이루는 어떤 사건에 대해, 처음으로 전해 들었다. 그 전 금요일에, 두 신사—사회에서 크게 다른 지위를 차지하고 있는—가 온 런던을 깜짝 놀라게 한 어떤 폭행의 희생자가 되었다는 것이었다. 그 신사들 가운데 하나는 램버스의 셉티머스 루커 씨였다. 다른 하나는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨였다.\n\n지금 이 외딴 처지에 살고 있는 터라, 나는 그 폭행에 대한 신문 기사를 나의 이야기에 들여올 방도가 없다. 나는 또한, 그 당시, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 그 열띤 웅변으로 이야기되는 사건들을 들을 값을 매길 수 없는 이점을 누리지도 못했다. 내가 할 수 있는 것이라곤, 그 월요일 저녁 내게 이야기된 그대로 사실들을 적는 것뿐이다. 어려서부터 옷을 개는 데에 취하도록 배워 온 그 방침에 따라 나아가면서 말이다. 모든 것이 단정히 놓일 것이며, 모든 것이 제자리에 놓일 것이다. 이 글줄들은 가엾고 연약한 한 여자에 의해 쓰인다. 가엾고 연약한 한 여자에게서 누가 그 이상을 기대할 만큼 모질겠는가?\n\n그 날짜는—나의 사랑하는 부모님 덕분에, 여태껏 쓰인 그 어떤 사전도 날짜에 관한 한 나보다 더 꼼꼼할 수는 없거니와—1848년 6월 30일 금요일이었다.\n\n그 잊지 못할 날의 이른 시각에, 우리의 재능 있는 고드프리 씨는 마침 롬바드 스트리트의 어느 은행에서 수표 한 장을 현금으로 바꾸고 있었다. 그 회사의 이름은 나의 일기에서 어쩌다 얼룩져 지워졌고, 진실에 대한 나의 성스러운 존중이 나로 하여금 이런 종류의 일에서 함부로 짐작을 무릅쓰는 것을 금한다. 다행히, 그 회사의 이름은 대수롭지 않다. 대수로운 것은 고드프리 씨가 제 볼일을 마쳤을 때 일어난 어떤 정황이다. 문에 다다르자, 그는 어느 신사와—그에게는 전혀 낯선 이와—맞닥뜨렸는데, 그이는 마침 그와 꼭 같은 순간에 그 사무소를 나서고 있었다. 은행 문을 누가 먼저 지날 것이냐를 두고 그들 사이에 한순간의 예의 겨루기가 벌어졌다. 그 낯선 이는 고드프리 씨를 먼저 지나게 하겠다고 우겼다. 고드프리 씨가 정중한 말 몇 마디를 건넸다. 그들은 고개 숙여 인사하고, 거리에서 헤어졌다.\n\n생각 없고 얄팍한 사람들은 말할지 모른다, 여기 참으로 하잘것없는 조그만 일 하나가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 시시콜콜하게 이야기되고 있다고. 오, 나의 젊은 벗들이여, 그리고 함께 죄 지은 이들이여! 그대들의 가엾은 육신의 이성을 감히 부리려 들지 않도록 조심하라. 오, 도덕적으로 단정하라. 그대들의 믿음이 그대들의 양말과 같게 하고, 그대들의 양말이 그대들의 믿음과 같게 하라. 둘 다 언제나 티끌 하나 없이, 그리고 둘 다 한순간의 통지에도 걸칠 채비가 되어 있게!\n\n수천 번 용서를 빈다. 나는 나도 모르게 나의 주일학교 문체에 빠져들고 말았다. 이런 기록에는 더없이 어울리지 않는 것을. 세속적이 되어 보도록 애써 보리라—다른 많은 경우에서와 마찬가지로 이 경우에도, 사소한 것들이 끔찍한 결과로 이어졌다고 말해 보리라. 그 정중한 낯선 이가 램버스의 루커 씨였다는 것만 미리 밝혀 두고, 우리는 이제 고드프리 씨를 따라 킬번에 있는 그의 저택으로 가 보리라.\n\n그는, 현관에서, 초라한 옷차림이지만 곱상하고 흥미로워 보이는 어린 사내아이 하나가 자기를 기다리고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그 아이는 그에게 편지 한 통을 건네며, 다만 자기가 알지 못하는 어느 노부인에게서 그것을 맡았는데, 그분이 답장을 기다리라는 아무 지시도 주지 않았다고만 말했다. 이런 일들은 공공 자선의 후원자로서 고드프리 씨의 넓은 경험에서 드물지 않았다. 그는 그 아이를 보내고, 편지를 뜯었다.\n\n그 필적은 그에게 전혀 낯익지 않았다. 그것은 한 시간 안에, 그가 여태 들어가 볼 일이 없었던 스트랜드의 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에 있는 어느 집으로 와 달라고 청하고 있었다. 구하는 바는, 그 훌륭한 관리자에게서 '어머니회 아동복 개조 협회'라는 화제에 대한 어떤 세부 사항들을 얻는 것이었고, 그 정보를 원하는 이는, 만일 제 물음들이 흡족한 답으로 채워진다면, 그 자선회의 재원에 크게 보태겠다는 어느 나이 지긋한 부인이었다. 그녀는 제 이름을 밝히고, 런던에 머무는 기간이 짧아 자기가 편지를 보내는 그 저명한 자선가에게 더 길게 미리 알릴 수가 없었다고 덧붙였다.\n\n여느 사람들이라면 낯선 이의 편의를 봐주려고 제 볼일을 제쳐 두기 전에 망설였을지 모른다. 기독교적 영웅은 선을 행할 일이 있는 곳에서 결코 망설이지 않는다. 고드프리 씨는 대뜸 발길을 돌려, 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에 있는 그 집으로 나아갔다. 더없이 점잖으나 다소 뚱뚱한 사내가 문을 열어 주었고, 고드프리 씨의 이름을 듣고는, 즉시 그를 응접실 층 뒤편의 텅 빈 방으로 안내해 들였다. 그는 그 방에 들어서면서 예사롭지 않은 두 가지를 알아챘다. 그 하나는 사향과 장뇌의 희미한 냄새였다. 다른 하나는, 인도풍의 인물과 문양으로 화려하게 장식되어, 탁자 위에 들여다보라는 듯 펼쳐져 있는 어느 고대 동양 필사본이었다.\n\n그는 그 책을 들여다보고 있었는데, 그 책이 놓인 위치 탓에 그는 앞방으로 통하는 닫힌 접이문에 등을 돌린 채 서 있게 되었고, 그때, 그를 일깨울 아무런 낌새의 소리도 앞서 없이, 그는 뒤에서 별안간 제 목이 휘감기는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 제 목을 휘감은 그 팔이 벌거벗겨 있고 황갈색을 띠고 있음을 알아챌 겨를밖에 없었는데, 그 순간 그의 눈이 가리어지고, 그의 입에 재갈이 물리고, 그는 (그가 헤아리기로는) 두 사내에 의해 속절없이 바닥에 내동댕이쳐졌다. 세 번째 자가 그의 호주머니를 털어—그리고, 숙녀로서 감히 이런 표현을 써도 된다면—격식도 없이, 그의 살갗에 이르기까지 샅샅이 그를 뒤졌다.\n\n여기서 나는 이토록 끔찍한 위급함 속에서 고드프리 씨를 홀로 지탱해 줄 수 있었을 그 경건한 믿음에 대해 몇 마디 격려의 말을 하는 것을 크게 즐길 만하다. 허나 어쩌면, (위에서 묘사한 바) 그 폭행의 절정기에 있어서의 나의 감탄스러운 벗의 자세와 몰골은, 여자가 논할 마땅한 테두리 안에 좀처럼 들지 않는 것이리라. 그다음 몇 순간은 그냥 넘기고, 그의 몸에 대한 그 역겨운 수색이 끝난 때의 고드프리 씨에게로 돌아가리라. 그 폭행은 처음부터 끝까지 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 저질러졌다. 그것이 끝날 무렵 그 보이지 않는 몹쓸 자들 사이에, 그가 알아듣지 못하는 어떤 언어로, 허나 (그의 교양 있는 귀에는) 실망과 분노를 뚜렷이 나타내는 어조로, 몇 마디 말이 오갔다. 그는 별안간 바닥에서 들어 올려져, 의자에 앉혀지고, 거기에 손발이 묶였다. 그다음 순간 그는 열린 문에서 공기가 흘러 들어오는 것을 느끼고, 귀 기울였고, 자기가 그 방에 다시 혼자 남았다고 결론지었다.\n\n한동안이 흐르고, 그는 아래층에서 여자의 옷자락이 스치는 소리 같은 것을 들었다. 그것이 층계를 올라와, 멈췄다. 여자의 비명이 그 죄스러운 공기를 찢었다. 아래층에서 사내의 목소리가 \"어이!\" 하고 외쳤다. 사내의 발이 층계를 올라왔다. 고드프리 씨는 기독교인의 손가락이 제 눈가리개를 풀고, 제 재갈을 빼내는 것을 느꼈다. 그는 놀라며 점잖은 두 낯선 이를 바라보고, 힘없이 또렷이 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻입니까?\" 그 점잖은 두 낯선 이가 마주 바라보며 말했다. \"바로 우리가 당신에게 물으려던 물음이오.\"\n\n피할 수 없는 해명이 뒤따랐다. 아니! 나로 하여금 빈틈없이 꼼꼼하게 하도록 해 다오. 사랑하는 고드프리 씨의 신경을 가라앉히기 위한 각성용 암모니아수와 물이 뒤따랐다. 해명은 그다음에 왔다.\n\n그 집의 남녀 주인(이웃에서 평판 좋은 사람들)의 진술로 미루어 드러난 바는, 그들의 이층과 삼층 방이, 그 전날, 확실히 일주일간, 더없이 점잖아 보이는 어느 신사에 의해—고드프리 씨의 문 두드림에 문을 열어 준 자로 이미 묘사된 바로 그 자에 의해—세내어졌다는 것이었다. 그 신사는 일주일 치 방세와 일주일 치 온갖 별도 비용을 미리 치르면서, 그 방들이 잉글랜드를 처음으로 방문하는 제 친구인 세 동양 귀족을 위해 필요하다고 밝혔다. 그 폭행의 날 이른 아침에, 그 동양 낯선 이들 가운데 둘이, 그들의 점잖은 영국인 벗을 대동하고, 그 방들을 차지했다. 세 번째 자는 오래지 않아 그들에게 합류할 것으로 예상되었다. 그리고 그 짐은 (아주 부피가 크다고 전해졌다) 그날 오후 늦게 세관을 통과하거든 뒤따라오는 것으로 알려졌다. 고드프리 씨가 방문하기 채 십 분도 전에, 그 세 번째 외국인이 도착했다. 아래층의 남녀 주인이 아는 한, 지난 오 분 사이까지는—그 세 외국인이, 그들의 점잖은 영국인 벗을 대동하고, 죄다 함께 그 집을 나서, 스트랜드 방향으로 조용히 걸어가는 것을 그들이 본 그때까지는—예사롭지 않은 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았다. 방문객 하나가 다녀갔음을 기억하고, 그 방문객이 함께 그 집을 나서는 것은 보지 못한 터라, 그 안주인은 그 신사가 위층에 홀로 남겨졌다는 것이 다소 이상하다고 여겼다. 제 남편과 잠깐 의논한 뒤, 그녀는 무언가 잘못된 것은 아닌지 확인해 보는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그 결과가, 내가 이미 묘사하려 애쓴 대로, 뒤따랐다. 그리고 거기서 남녀 주인의 해명이 끝났다.\n\n그다음으로 그 방에서 조사가 이루어졌다. 사랑하는 고드프리 씨의 소지품이 사방에 흩어져 있는 것이 발견되었다. 허나, 그 물건들을 모아 보니, 없어진 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그의 회중시계, 시곗줄, 지갑, 열쇠, 손수건, 수첩, 그리고 낱장의 종이들이 죄다 꼼꼼히 살펴진 뒤, 주인이 되찾도록 손상 없이 남겨져 있었다. 마찬가지로, 그 집 주인들에게 딸린 소지품은 아주 조그만 부스러기 하나도 훔쳐지지 않았다. 그 동양 귀족들은 저희 자신의 장식된 필사본을 치워 갔을 뿐, 그 밖에는 아무것도 치워 가지 않았다.\n\n그것은 무슨 뜻이었을까? 세속적 관점을 취하자면, 그것은 고드프리 씨가 어떤 알 수 없는 자들이 저지른 어떤 이해할 수 없는 착오의 희생자가 되었다는 뜻인 듯싶었다. 어두운 음모가 우리 한복판에서 진행되고 있었다. 그리고 우리의 사랑스럽고 죄 없는 벗이 그 그물코에 얽혀들었던 것이다. 백 번의 자선의 승리를 거둔 기독교적 영웅이, 착오로 파인 함정에 곤두박질칠 때, 오, 그것은 우리 나머지에게 끊임없이 경계하라는 얼마나 큰 경고인가! 우리 자신의 사악한 정념이 얼마나 이내 방심한 우리를 덮치는 동양 귀족으로 드러날 수 있는가!\n\n나는 이 한 가지 주제에 대해 애정 어린 경고를 몇 쪽이고 쓸 수 있으련만, (아아!) 나는 교화하는 것이 허락되지 않는다—나는 서술하도록 선고받았다. 나의 부유한 친척의 수표가—이제부터, 나의 존재를 짓누르는 악몽이거니와—내게 아직 이 폭력의 기록을 끝내지 못했음을 일깨운다. 우리는 고드프리 씨를 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서 회복하도록 남겨 두고, 그날 늦은 시각의 루커 씨의 행적을 따라가야 한다.\n\n은행을 나선 뒤, 루커 씨는 볼일로 런던의 여러 곳을 다녔다. 제 저택으로 돌아오니, 그는 자기를 기다리는 편지 한 통을 발견했는데, 그것은 얼마 전 어느 사내아이가 두고 갔다고 전해졌다. 이 경우에도, 고드프리 씨의 경우에서와 마찬가지로, 그 필적은 낯설었다. 허나 거기 적힌 이름은 루커 씨의 고객 가운데 하나의 이름이었다. 그의 편지 상대는 (삼인칭으로 쓰면서—보아하니 대필자의 손을 빌려) 자기가 뜻밖에 런던으로 불려 왔다고 알렸다. 그는 방금 토트넘 코트 로드의 앨프리드 플레이스에 숙소를 정한 참이었다. 그리고 그는, 자기가 하려는 어떤 구매라는 화제에 관하여, 루커 씨를 즉시 만나고 싶어 했다. 그 신사는 동양 골동품의 열렬한 수집가였고, 여러 해 동안 램버스의 그 상점의 후한 단골이었다. 오, 우리는 언제쯤 맘몬 숭배에서 젖을 뗄 것인가! 루커 씨는 삯마차를 불러, 대뜸 제 후한 단골에게로 몰고 갔다.\n\n노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서 고드프리 씨에게 일어났던 바로 그 일이 이제 앨프리드 플레이스에서 루커 씨에게 일어났다. 다시 한번 그 점잖은 사내가 문을 열어 주고, 방문객을 위층 뒤편 응접실로 안내했다. 거기에, 또, 그 장식된 필사본이 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다. 루커 씨의 관심은, 고드프리 씨의 관심이 사로잡혔던 것처럼, 이 아름다운 인도 예술품에 사로잡혔다. 그도 제 목을 휘감는 황갈색의 벌거벗은 팔에, 제 눈을 덮는 눈가리개에, 그리고 제 입에 물리는 재갈에 제 궁리에서 화들짝 깨어났다. 그도 엎어뜨려져 살갗까지 뒤져졌다. 그런 뒤 고드프리 씨의 경험에서 지났던 것보다 더 긴 동안이 흘렀다. 허나 그것도 앞서처럼, 그 집 사람들이 무언가 잘못되었다고 의심하고, 무슨 일이 일어났는지 보려고 위층으로 올라가는 것으로 끝났다. 노섬벌랜드 스트리트의 주인이 고드프리 씨에게 했던 바로 그 해명을, 앨프리드 플레이스의 주인이 이제 루커 씨에게 했다. 둘 다, 제 외국인 벗들을 대신해 행세한다고 자기를 소개한 그 점잖은 낯선 이의 그럴듯한 언사와 두둑한 지갑에 똑같은 식으로 속아 넘어간 것이었다. 두 경우 사이의 단 하나의 차이점은 루커 씨의 호주머니에서 쏟아진 것들이 바닥에서 그러모아질 때 나타났다. 그의 회중시계와 지갑은 무사했으나, (고드프리 씨보다는 운이 덜 좋게도) 그가 몸에 지니고 다니던 낱장의 종이 하나가 없어졌다. 문제의 그 종이는 루커 씨가 그날 제 은행에 맡겨 둔, 값이 대단히 비싼 어느 귀중품의 수령을 증명하는 것이었다. 이 문서는, 그 귀중품이 오직 주인 본인이 직접 청할 때에만 내어진다고 규정하고 있었으므로, 사기의 목적으로는 쓸모가 없을 터였다. 정신을 차리자마자, 루커 씨는, 자기를 턴 그 도둑들이 아무것도 모르고 그 수령증을 들고 나타날지 모른다는 요행을 노리고, 은행으로 서둘러 갔다. 그가 그 은행에 다다랐을 때 그들의 자취는 보이지 않았고, 그 뒤로도 그들의 자취는 보이지 않았다. 그들의 점잖은 영국인 벗이 (은행 사람들의 견해로는) 그들이 그것을 써먹으려 시도하기 전에 그 수령증을 훑어보고, 제때에 그들에게 필요한 경고를 해 준 것이었다.\n\n두 폭행에 대한 정보가 경찰에 전해졌고, 필요한 조사가, 내가 믿기로는, 아주 힘차게 이루어졌다. 당국은 그 도둑들이 불충분한 정보를 받아 절도를 꾸몄다고 보았다. 그들은 루커 씨가 제 귀한 보석의 이송을 다른 사람에게 맡겼는지 아닌지를 뻔히 확신하지 못했던 것이다. 그리고 가엾은 정중한 고드프리 씨는 우연히 그와 이야기하는 것이 목격된 대가를 치른 것이었다. 여기에 덧붙이자면, 고드프리 씨가 우리의 월요일 저녁 모임에 나오지 못한 것은 당국의 협의 탓이었는데, 그는 그 자리에서 협조해 달라는 청을 받았던 것이다—그리고 필요한 온갖 해명을 이제 다 했으니, 나는 몬터규 스퀘어에서의 나 자신의 조그만 개인적 경험이라는 더 단순한 이야기로 나아가도 되겠다.\n\n나는 화요일 점심 시각에 어김없이 갔다. 나의 일기를 살펴보니 이날은 이런저런 일이 뒤섞인 날이었음을 알 수 있다—그 안에 경건히 뉘우칠 일도 많고, 그 안에 경건히 감사할 일도 많은 날이었다.\n\n사랑하는 버린더 이모님은 여느 때의 우아함과 다정함으로 나를 맞으셨다. 허나 나는, 잠시 뒤, 무언가 잘못되었음을 알아챘다. 이모님에게서 어떤 근심 어린 눈길이 새어 나오곤 했는데, 그 모두가 당신 따님 쪽을 향했다. 나는 레이철을 볼 때마다 저토록 보잘것없어 보이는 사람이 어떻게 존 버린더 경과 레이디 버린더 같은 그토록 저명한 부모의 자식일 수 있는지 의아해하지 않은 적이 없다. 허나 이번에는, 그 아이가 나를 실망시켰을 뿐만 아니라—정말로 나를 충격에 빠뜨렸다. 그 아이의 말과 몸가짐에는 숙녀다운 절제라곤 죄다 없어서 보기에 더없이 고통스러웠다. 그 아이는 어떤 열병 같은 흥분에 사로잡혀 있어, 웃을 때는 딱하게도 목소리가 컸고, 점심에 먹고 마시는 것에 있어서는 죄스럽게도 헤프고 변덕스러웠다. 나는, 이 일의 참된 실상이 내게 은밀히 알려지기도 전에, 그 아이의 가엾은 어머니를 위해 깊이 마음 아파했다.\n\n점심이 끝나자, 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"레이철, 의사가 네게 이른 말을 기억하렴, 밥 먹은 뒤에는 책으로 마음을 가라앉히라던 그 말 말이야.\"\n\n\"서재로 갈게요, 엄마.\" 하고 그 아이가 대답했다. \"그런데 고드프리가 오거든, 제게 꼭 알려 주세요. 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서의 그 봉변 뒤로, 저는 그이의 소식을 더 듣고 싶어 죽겠어요.\" 그 아이는 제 어머니의 이마에 입을 맞추고, 내 쪽을 바라보았다. \"잘 있어요, 클랙.\" 하고 그 아이가 아무렇지도 않게 말했다. 그 아이의 무례함은 내 안에 아무런 노여운 심정도 불러일으키지 않았다. 나는 다만 그 아이를 위해 기도하리라고 남몰래 마음속에 적어 두었다.\n\n우리 둘만 남겨지자, 이모님은 내게 그 인도 다이아몬드에 대한 그 온갖 끔찍한 이야기를 들려주셨는데, 나는 그것을 여기서 되풀이할 필요가 없다는 것을 알아 기쁘다. 이모님은 그 화제에 대해 침묵을 지키는 편을 좋아하셨으리라는 것을 내게 숨기지 않으셨다. 허나 당신 하인들이 죄다 월장석의 분실을 알고 있고, 그 정황 가운데 얼마쯤이 실제로 신문에까지 흘러들어 간 마당에—낯선 이들이, 레이디 버린더의 시골집에서 일어난 일과 노섬벌랜드 스트리트와 앨프리드 플레이스에서 일어난 일 사이에 무슨 관련이 있는지 억측하고 있는 마당에—감추는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 그리고 온전한 솔직함이 미덕이자 필요가 되었다.\n\n지금 내가 들은 것을 들었다면, 어떤 사람들은 아마 놀라움에 압도되었으리라. 나로 말하자면, 레이철의 성정이 어린 시절부터 죽 본질적으로 거듭나지 못한 것임을 알고 있는 터라, 나는 이모님이 당신 따님이라는 화제에 대해 내게 이야기할 수 있는 그 무엇에도 마음의 채비가 되어 있었다. 그것이 나쁜 데서 더 나쁜 데로 나아가 마침내 살인으로 끝났을 수도 있으련만, 그래도 나는 여전히 혼잣말했으리라, 당연한 결과지! 오, 저런, 저런, 당연한 결과야! 정말로 나를 충격에 빠뜨린 단 한 가지는 그 정황 아래 이모님이 취한 방침이었다. 여기야말로, 여태껏 그런 경우가 있었다면, 참으로 목사님이 필요한 경우가 아니겠는가! 레이디 버린더는 그것을 의사가 필요한 경우로 여기셨다. 나의 가엾은 이모님의 어린 시절이 죄다 당신 아버지의 신을 모르는 집안에서 지나갔던 것이다. 또다시 당연한 결과! 오, 저런, 저런, 또다시 당연한 결과!\n\n\"의사들은 레이철에게 운동과 놀이를 많이 하라고 권하고, 그 아이의 마음이 될 수 있는 대로 지난 일에 머물지 않게 하라고 내게 강하게 당부하는구나.\" 하고 레이디 버린더가 말씀하셨다.\n\n\"오, 이 무슨 이교도 같은 조언인가!\" 하고 나는 혼잣말했다. \"이 기독교의 나라에서, 이 무슨 이교도 같은 조언인가!\"\n\n이모님이 말을 이으셨다. \"나는 그 당부를 지키려고 최선을 다한단다. 그런데 고드프리의 이 기이한 봉변이 더없이 불운한 때에 일어났어. 레이철은 그 소식을 처음 들은 뒤로 쉼 없이 안절부절못하고 흥분해 있어. 그 아이는 내가 조카 에이블화이트에게 편지를 써서 이리로 와 달라 청할 때까지 나를 가만두지 않았지. 그 아이는 심지어 거칠게 다루어진 다른 사람에게도—루커 씨라던가, 그런 이름의 사람에게도—관심을 느껴, 물론 그 사내가 그 아이에게는 전혀 낯선 사람인데도 말이야.\"\n\n\"세상에 대한 이모님의 앎이 저의 앎보다 나으시지요, 사랑하는 이모님.\" 하고 나는 조심스레 넌지시 말했다. \"허나 레이철 편의 이 예사롭지 않은 행동에는 분명 무슨 까닭이 있을 거예요. 그 아이는 이모님과 모두에게 죄스러운 비밀을 감추고 있어요. 이 최근의 일들 가운데, 그 아이의 비밀을 발각의 위협에 빠뜨리는 무언가가 있는 것은 아닐까요?\"\n\n\"발각이라고?\" 하고 이모님이 되뇌셨다. \"대체 무슨 뜻이니? 루커 씨를 통한 발각? 내 조카를 통한 발각?\"\n\n그 말이 이모님의 입술을 지나는 순간, 특별한 섭리가 일어났다. 하인이 문을 열고, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 방문을 알렸다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n고드프리 씨는 제 이름이 불리는 것을 뒤따라—고드프리 씨가 다른 모든 일을 하듯이—꼭 알맞은 때에 들어섰다. 그는 우리를 놀라게 할 만큼 하인의 발뒤꿈치에 바싹 붙어 있지도 않았다. 그는 우리에게 멈칫하는 순간과 열린 문이라는 이중의 불편을 끼칠 만큼 뒤처져 있지도 않았다. 참된 기독교인이 드러나는 것은 그 나날의 삶의 완벽함에서다. 이 사랑스러운 사람은 아주 완벽했다.\n\n\"버린더 아가씨께 가서,\" 하고 이모님이 하인에게 이르셨다. \"에이블화이트 씨가 오셨다고 전하렴.\"\n\n우리는 둘 다 그의 건강을 물었다. 우리는 둘 다 함께, 지난주의 그 끔찍한 봉변 뒤로, 그가 다시 제 몸 같은지를 물었다. 더없는 재치로, 그는 용케 우리 둘에게 동시에 답했다. 레이디 버린더는 그의 대답을 말로 받으셨다. 나는 그의 매혹적인 미소를 받았다.\n\n\"제가,\" 하고 그가, 한없는 다정함으로 외쳤다. \"이 온갖 동정을 받을 무슨 일을 했다는 겁니까? 사랑하는 이모님! 사랑하는 클랙 양! 저는 그저 다른 누군가로 잘못 여겨졌을 뿐입니다. 저는 그저 눈이 가려졌을 뿐이고, 저는 그저 목이 졸렸을 뿐이고, 저는 그저 유난히 딱딱한 바닥을 덮은 아주 얇은 양탄자 위에 벌렁 나자빠졌을 뿐입니다. 그것이 얼마나 더 나빴을 수 있었을지 한번 생각해 보세요! 저는 살해되었을 수도 있고, 저는 강도를 당했을 수도 있습니다. 제가 무엇을 잃었나요? 신경의 기운 말고는 아무것도 잃지 않았지요—그리고 그것은 법이 재산으로 인정하지 않으니, 엄밀히 말하면, 저는 아무것도 잃지 않은 셈입니다. 제 마음대로 할 수 있었다면, 저는 제 봉변을 저 혼자만 간직했을 겁니다—저는 이 온갖 소동과 세상의 이목이 질색이거든요. 허나 루커 씨가 제 피해를 세상에 알렸고, 그리하여 제 피해도, 그 당연한 결과로, 제 차례가 되어 알려진 것이지요. 저는 점잖은 독자들이 이 화제에 물릴 때까지, 신문의 소유물이 되었습니다. 저 자신도 참으로 그것에 물렸고요. 부디 점잖은 독자들도 어서 저처럼 되기를! 그런데 사랑하는 레이철은 어떻습니까? 여전히 런던의 흥겨움을 즐기고 있고요? 그 말을 들으니 참 기쁩니다! 클랙 양, 저는 당신의 온갖 너그러움이 필요합니다. 저는 제 위원회 일과 제 사랑하는 부인들 일에 서글프게도 뒤처져 있어요. 허나 저는 다음 주에는 정말 '어머니회 아동복' 모임에 들러 볼 셈입니다. 월요일 위원회에서는 기쁜 진전이 있었습니까? 이사회는 앞날의 전망에 대해 희망적이었고요? 그리고 우리는 바지 형편이 넉넉한가요?\"\n\n그의 미소의 그 천상 같은 부드러움이 그의 사과를 거스를 수 없게 만들었다. 그의 굵은 목소리의 풍성함이, 그가 방금 내게 건넨 그 흥미로운 볼일에 관한 물음에 저 나름의 형언할 수 없는 매력을 더했다. 사실인즉, 우리는 바지 형편이 거의 지나칠 만큼 넉넉했다. 우리는 그것들에 아주 파묻힐 지경이었다. 나는 막 그렇게 말하려던 참이었는데, 그때 문이 다시 열리고, 세속적 소란의 한 요소가, 버린더 아가씨라는 사람의 모습으로, 방에 들어왔다.\n\n그 아이는 더없이 숙녀답지 못한 속도로 사랑하는 고드프리 씨에게 다가갔는데, 머리는 충격적으로 헝클어져 있고, 얼굴은, 내가 말하자면, 볼썽사납게 달아올라 있었다.\n\n\"만나서 반가워요, 고드프리.\" 하고 그 아이가, 덧붙이기 서글프거니와, 한 젊은 사내가 다른 젊은 사내에게 말하는 그 스스럼없는 태도로 그에게 말했다. \"루커 씨를 함께 데려왔으면 좋았을걸. 당신하고 그이는 (지금 우리의 이 흥분이 이어지는 한) 온 런던에서 가장 흥미로운 두 사내예요. 이런 말을 하는 건 병적이지요. 건강하지 못하고요. 클랙 양 같은 반듯한 마음이 본능적으로 몸서리치는 그 모든 것이고요. 그런 건 아무래도 좋아요. 노섬벌랜드 스트리트 이야기를 죄다 당장 들려줘요. 신문들이 그중 얼마쯤을 빼놓았다는 걸 저는 알거든요.\"\n\n사랑하는 고드프리 씨조차 우리 모두가 아담에게서 물려받은 그 타락한 본성을 나누어 지녔다—그것은 우리 인간 유산의 아주 작은 몫이지만, 아아! 그도 그것을 지녔다. 그가 레이철의 손을 제 두 손으로 잡아, 제 조끼 왼편에 부드럽게 갖다 대는 것을 보고 마음이 아팠음을 나는 고백한다. 그것은 그 아이의 무모한 말투와, 나를 향한 무례한 언급에 대한 직접적인 부추김이었다.\n\n\"더없이 사랑하는 레이철,\" 하고 그가, 우리의 전망과 우리의 바지에 대해 말할 때 나를 전율케 했던 바로 그 목소리로 말했다. \"신문들이 당신에게 죄다 이야기했고—내가 할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 이야기했어요.\"\n\n\"고드프리는 우리 모두가 이 일을 너무 크게 여긴다고 생각한단다.\" 하고 이모님이 짚으셨다. \"그 애는 방금 그것을 이야기하고 싶지 않다고 말하던 참이야.\"\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n그 아이는 눈에 별안간 번득임을 띠고, 별안간 고드프리 씨의 얼굴을 올려다보며 그 물음을 던졌다. 그의 쪽에서는, 어찌나 분별없고 어찌나 받을 자격 없는 너그러움으로 그 아이를 내려다보던지, 나는 정말이지 끼어들어야겠다는 느낌이 들었다.\n\n\"레이철, 얘야!\" 하고 나는 부드럽게 타일렀다. \"참된 위대함과 참된 용기는 언제나 겸손하단다.\"\n\n\"당신은 당신 나름으로 아주 좋은 사람이에요, 고드프리.\" 하고 그 아이가—보시라, 나는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고, 여전히 한 젊은 사내가 다른 젊은 사내에게 말하듯 제 사촌에게 말하며—말했다. \"하지만 당신이 위대하지 않다는 건 제가 아주 확실히 알아요. 당신이 무슨 대단한 용기를 지녔다고는 믿지 않고요. 그리고 저는 굳게 믿어요—당신에게 겸손이라는 게 조금이라도 있었다면—당신의 그 부인 숭배자들이 여러 해 전에 그 미덕을 당신에게서 벗겨 갔으리라고요. 당신은 노섬벌랜드 스트리트의 봉변을 이야기하지 않을 무슨 남모를 까닭이 있어요. 그리고 저는 그걸 알아낼 셈이에요.\"\n\n\"제 까닭은 상상할 수 있는 가장 단순한 것이고, 가장 손쉽게 인정할 수 있는 것이에요.\" 하고 그가, 여전히 그 아이를 참아 주며 대답했다. \"저는 그 화제에 물렸어요.\"\n\n\"당신이 그 화제에 물렸다고요? 사랑하는 고드프리, 제가 소견을 하나 말할게요.\"\n\n\"뭔데요?\"\n\n\"당신은 너무 지나치게 여자들 무리 속에서 살아요. 그리고 그 결과로 아주 나쁜 버릇 두 가지가 들었고요. 당신은 헛소리를 진지하게 하는 걸 익혔고, 재미 삼아 거짓말을 늘어놓는 버릇이 들었어요. 당신은 그 부인 숭배자들에게는 곧이곧대로 갈 수가 없죠. 저는 당신이 저에게만은 곧이곧대로 가게 만들 셈이에요. 이리 와서, 앉아요. 저는 대놓고 묻는 물음들로 가득 차 있고, 당신은 대놓고 답하는 대답들로 가득 차 있기를 바라요.\"\n\n그 아이는 실제로 그를 방 저편, 빛이 그의 얼굴에 떨어질 창가의 의자로 끌고 갔다. 나는 이런 말을 옮기고, 이런 행동을 묘사해야 하는 것을 깊이 마음 아파한다. 허나, 한쪽에는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 수표가, 다른 쪽에는 진실에 대한 나 자신의 성스러운 존중이 있어, 이렇게 옴짝달싹 못하게 끼여 있는 마당에, 나로서는 어쩌란 말인가? 나는 이모님을 바라보았다. 이모님은 꿈쩍도 않고 앉아 계셨다. 보아하니 조금도 끼어들 뜻이 없으신 듯했다. 나는 여태껏 이모님에게서 이런 종류의 무기력을 본 적이 없었다. 그것은, 어쩌면, 시골에서 겪으신 그 힘겨운 시절 뒤의 반작용이었으리라. 그것이 무엇이든 간에, 사랑하는 레이디 버린더의 연세에, 그리고 사랑하는 레이디 버린더의 그 무르익은 풍만한 몸매로 보아, 짚어 두기에 유쾌한 징후는 아니었다.\n\n그러는 동안, 레이철은 우리의 상냥하고 잘 참아 주는—너무 잘 참아 주는—고드프리 씨와 함께 창가에 자리를 잡았다. 그 아이는, 마치 우리가 그 방에 있지도 않은 듯 제 어머니나 나를 더는 아랑곳하지 않고, 그를 위협했던 그 줄줄이 이어지는 물음들을 시작했다.\n\n\"경찰이 뭐라도 했어요, 고드프리?\"\n\n\"아무것도 못 했어요.\"\n\n\"당신에게 덫을 놓은 그 세 사내가, 나중에 루커 씨에게 덫을 놓은 바로 그 세 사내라는 건, 틀림없겠죠?\"\n\n\"사람의 앎으로 말하자면, 사랑하는 레이철, 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요.\"\n\n\"그런데 그들의 자취라곤 하나도 발견되지 않았고요?\"\n\n\"자취 하나 없어요.\"\n\n\"그 세 사내가 시골 우리 집에 왔던 그 인도인 셋이라고—여겨지죠, 안 그래요?\"\n\n\"그리 여기는 사람들도 있어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그리 여겨요?\"\n\n\"사랑하는 레이철, 그들은 제가 그들 얼굴을 보기도 전에 제 눈을 가렸어요. 저는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라요. 제가 어떻게 그것에 대해 의견을 낼 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n보시다시피, 고드프리 씨의 그 천사 같은 부드러움조차, 그에게 가해지는 그 시달림 아래 마침내 무너지기 시작하고 있었다. 고삐 풀린 호기심이 버린더 아가씨의 물음을 몰았는지, 아니면 다스릴 수 없는 두려움이 몰았는지, 나는 감히 캐묻지 않으련다. 나는 다만, 방금 묘사한 그 대답을 그 아이에게 준 뒤 고드프리 씨가 일어서려 하자, 그 아이가 실제로 그의 두 어깨를 붙들어, 그를 의자에 도로 밀어 넣었다는 것만 옮긴다—오, 이것이 정숙하지 못했다고 말하지 마시라! 죄지은 자의 공포에서 오는 무모함만이 내가 묘사한 그런 행동을 설명할 수 있으리라고 넌지시라도 말하지 마시라! 우리는 남을 판단해서는 안 된다. 나의 기독교인 벗들이여, 정말로, 정말로, 정말로, 우리는 남을 판단해서는 안 된다!\n\n그 아이는 기죽지 않고 제 물음을 이어 갔다. 진지한 성경 학도라면 어쩌면 떠올릴 것이다—나도 떠올렸듯이—대홍수 이전 시절에, 기죽지 않고 제 광란의 잔치를 이어 가던 그 눈먼 악마의 자식들을 말이다.\n\n\"루커 씨에 대해 좀 알고 싶어요, 고드프리.\"\n\n\"저는 또다시 운이 없군요, 레이철. 저보다 루커 씨를 덜 아는 사람은 없어요.\"\n\n\"당신과 그이가 은행에서 우연히 마주치기 전에는 그이를 본 적이 없어요?\"\n\n\"한 번도 없어요.\"\n\n\"그 뒤로는 그이를 봤고요?\"\n\n\"네. 경찰을 돕느라, 우리는 따로도, 함께도 조사를 받았어요.\"\n\n\"루커 씨는 제 은행에서 받은 수령증을 빼앗겼어요—그렇죠? 그 수령증은 무엇에 대한 거였죠?\"\n\n\"그가 은행에 안전하게 맡겨 둔 어느 값진 보석에 대한 것이었어요.\"\n\n\"그건 신문들이 하는 말이에요. 그건 여느 독자에게는 넉넉할지 모르지만, 저에게는 넉넉하지 않아요. 그 은행 수령증에는 분명 그 보석이 무엇인지 적혀 있었을 텐데요?\"\n\n\"그 은행 수령증에는, 레이철—내가 들어 묘사된 바로는—그런 건 아무것도 적혀 있지 않았어요. 루커 씨에게 딸린 값진 보석 하나. 루커 씨가 맡김. 루커 씨의 봉인으로 봉해짐. 그리고 오직 루커 씨 본인이 직접 청할 때에만 내어짐. 그것이 그 양식이었고, 그것이 내가 그에 대해 아는 전부예요.\"\n\n그가 그 말을 하고 나서, 그 아이는 잠시 뜸을 들였다. 그 아이는 제 어머니를 바라보고, 한숨지었다. 그 아이는 다시 고드프리 씨를 바라보고, 말을 이었다.\n\n\"우리 집안의 사사로운 일 가운데 얼마쯤이,\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"신문에 들어간 것 같은데요?\"\n\n\"그렇다고 말하기 서글프지만, 그래요.\"\n\n\"그리고 우리에게는 전혀 낯선 어떤 한가한 사람들이, 요크셔의 우리 집에서 일어난 일과 그 뒤 여기 런던에서 일어난 일 사이의 관련을 캐내려 하고 있고요?\"\n\n\"세상의 호기심이, 어떤 부류에서는, 그렇게 방향을 트는 것 같아 걱정이에요.\"\n\n\"당신과 루커 씨를 험하게 다룬 그 낯선 세 사내가 그 인도인 셋이라고 말하는 사람들은, 또한 그 값진 보석이——\"\n\n거기서 그 아이는 멈췄다. 그 아이는 지난 몇 순간 사이에 점점 더, 얼굴이 하얘지고 하얘졌다. 그 아이의 유별나게 검은 머리칼이 이 창백함을, 그 대비로, 어찌나 섬뜩하게 보이게 하던지, 우리는 모두 그 아이가, 제 물음 한복판에서 스스로 말을 멈춘 그 순간, 기절하리라 여겼다. 사랑하는 고드프리 씨가 두 번째로 의자를 떠나려 시도했다. 이모님이 그 아이에게 더는 말하지 말라고 애원하셨다. 나는 각성제 한 병이라는 모양을 한 겸손한 약물의 화해 예물을 들고 이모님을 뒤따랐다. 우리 가운데 누구도 그 아이에게 조금의 효험도 내지 못했다. \"고드프리, 그 자리에 그대로 있어요. 엄마, 저에 대해선 조금도 놀라실 까닭이 없어요. 클랙, 당신은 이 끝을 듣고 싶어 죽겠지요—당신 비위를 딱히 맞춰 주려고, 저는 기절하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n그것이 그 아이가 쓴 바로 그 말이었다—내가 집에 다다르는 순간 나의 일기에 받아 적은 것이다. 허나, 오, 우리 판단하지 말자! 나의 기독교인 벗들이여, 우리 판단하지 말자!\n\n그 아이는 다시 한번 고드프리 씨에게로 몸을 돌렸다. 보기에 소름 끼치는 고집으로, 그 아이는 스스로 말을 멈추었던 그 자리로 도로 돌아가, 이런 말로 제 물음을 마무리했다.\n\n\"조금 전에, 어떤 부류에서 사람들이 뭐라 하는지에 대해 당신에게 말했죠. 분명하게 말해 줘요, 고드프리, 그들 중 누구든, 루커 씨의 그 값진 보석이—월장석이라고 말하나요?\"\n\n그 인도 다이아몬드의 이름이 그 아이의 입술을 지나는 순간, 나는 나의 감탄스러운 벗에게 변화가 이는 것을 보았다. 그의 낯빛이 짙어졌다. 그는 그의 가장 큰 매력 가운데 하나인 그 상냥하고 부드러운 태도를 잃었다. 고결한 분노가 그의 대답을 불러일으켰다.\n\n\"그들은 정말 그리 말해요.\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"루커 씨가 저 나름의 어떤 남모를 잇속을 위해 거짓을 말하고 있다고 서슴없이 몰아붙이는 사람들이 있어요. 그는 몇 번이고 거듭, 이 추문이 자기를 덮치기 전까지는 월장석에 대해 들어 본 적조차 없다고 엄숙히 단언했어요. 그런데 이 몹쓸 사람들은, 저희를 정당화할 증거의 그림자 하나 없이, 이렇게 대꾸하지요, 그가 감출 까닭이 있는 거다, 우리는 그의 맹세를 믿기를 거부한다고. 수치스러워요! 수치스러워요!\"\n\n레이철은 그가 말하는 동안 그를 아주 이상하게—어떻게라고 잘 묘사할 수가 없는데—바라보았다. 그가 말을 마치자, 그 아이가 말했다. \"루커 씨가 그저 당신의 우연한 지인일 뿐임을 헤아려 보면, 당신은 그이의 편을, 고드프리, 제법 뜨겁게 드는군요.\"\n\n나의 재능 있는 벗은 그 아이에게, 내가 내 평생 들어 본 가장 참으로 복음적인 대답 가운데 하나를 건넸다.\n\n\"저는, 레이철, 억눌린 모든 사람들의 편을 제법 뜨겁게 들기를 바라요.\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n그 말이 뱉어진 그 어조는 돌이라도 녹였으리라. 허나, 아 저런, 돌의 굳음이 무엇인가? 거듭나지 못한 인간 마음의 굳음에 견주면, 아무것도 아니다! 그 아이는 비웃었다. 이것을 적기가 낯 뜨겁지만—그 아이는 그의 얼굴에 대고 비웃었다.\n\n\"당신의 고결한 감정은 당신의 부인 위원회들을 위해 간직해 둬요, 고드프리. 루커 씨를 덮친 그 추문이, 당신도 봐주지 않았다는 걸 저는 확신해요.\"\n\n이모님의 무기력조차 그 말에 일깨워졌다.\n\n\"얘 레이철,\" 하고 이모님이 타이르셨다. \"너는 정말이지 그런 말을 할 자격이 없다!\"\n\n\"해코지할 뜻은 없어요, 엄마—좋은 뜻이에요. 저에게 잠깐만 참을성을 가져 주세요, 그럼 아시게 될 거예요.\"\n\n그 아이는, 별안간 그가 딱해진 듯한 낯빛으로, 고드프리 씨를 다시 바라보았다. 그 아이는 그의 손을 잡는—더없이 숙녀답지 못한—지경에까지 이르렀다.\n\n\"저는 확신해요,\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"제 어머니 앞에서, 그리고 제 앞에서 이 일을 이야기하지 않으려는 당신의 마음의 참된 까닭을 알아냈다고요. 어떤 불운한 우연이 사람들의 마음속에서 당신을 루커 씨와 엮어 놓았어요. 당신은 그이에 대해 추문이 뭐라 하는지 저에게 말했죠. 당신에 대해서는 추문이 뭐라 하나요?\"\n\n이 막판에 이르러서까지, 사랑하는 고드프리 씨는—언제나 악을 선으로 갚을 채비가 되어 있는 그는—그 아이를 봐주려 애썼다.\n\n\"제게 묻지 말아요!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그건 잊는 편이 나아요, 레이철—정말로 그래요.\"\n\n\"저는 기어이 그걸 듣겠어요!\" 하고 그 아이가, 목청껏, 사납게 외쳤다.\n\n\"말해 주게, 고드프리!\" 하고 이모님이 애원하셨다. \"지금 자네의 침묵이 저 아이에게 끼치는 만큼의 해를 끼칠 수 있는 건 아무것도 없네!\"\n\n고드프리 씨의 고운 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였다. 그는 마지막으로 한 번 애원하는 눈길을 그 아이에게 던지고는—그러고 나서 그 운명적인 말을 했다.\n\n\"정 그러겠다면, 레이철—추문은 월장석이 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혀 있고, 내가 그것을 저당 잡힌 사내라고 말해요.\"\n\n그 아이는 비명과 함께 벌떡 일어섰다. 그 아이는 고드프리 씨에게서 이모님으로, 이모님에게서 고드프리 씨로, 어찌나 미친 듯이 앞뒤로 눈길을 옮기던지 나는 정말로 그 아이가 미쳐 버린 줄 알았다.\n\n\"저에게 말 걸지 마세요! 저를 건드리지 마세요!\" 하고 그 아이가, (선언하거니와 마치 어떤 쫓기는 짐승처럼!) 우리 모두에게서 움츠러들어 물러나며, 방 한구석으로 외쳤다. \"이건 제 잘못이에요! 제가 바로잡아야 해요. 저는 저 자신을 희생했어요—원한다면 그럴 권리가 저에겐 있었죠. 하지만 죄 없는 사내가 파멸하도록 두다니. 그이의 됨됨이를 평생 무너뜨리는 비밀을 지키다니—오, 하느님 맙소사, 너무 끔찍해요! 저는 견딜 수 없어요!\"\n\n이모님이 의자에서 반쯤 일어나셨다가, 별안간 다시 앉으셨다. 이모님은 힘없이 나를 부르시고, 당신 반짇고리 안의 작은 약병 하나를 가리키셨다.\n\n\"어서!\" 하고 이모님이 속삭이셨다. \"여섯 방울, 물에. 레이철이 못 보게 하렴.\"\n\n다른 정황이었다면, 나는 이것을 이상하게 여겼으리라. 지금은 생각할 겨를이 없었다—그저 약을 드릴 겨를밖에 없었다. 사랑하는 고드프리 씨는, 방 저편에서 그 아이에게 마음을 가라앉히는 말을 건넴으로써, 내가 하는 일을 레이철에게서 감추도록 저도 모르게 나를 도왔다.\n\n\"정말이지, 정말이지, 당신은 과장하고 있어요.\" 하는 그의 말이 들렸다. \"제 평판은 이런 지나가는 비참한 추문 따위에 무너지기에는 너무 높이 서 있어요. 그건 한 주만 더 지나면 죄다 잊힐 거예요. 우리 다시는 그것을 이야기하지 맙시다.\" 그 아이는, 이런 너그러움에조차, 도무지 다가갈 수가 없었다. 그 아이는 나쁜 데서 더 나쁜 데로 나아갔다.\n\n\"저는 반드시, 기어이, 그걸 멈추겠어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"엄마! 제 말을 들어 주세요. 클랙 양! 제 말을 들어 줘요. 저는 월장석을 가져간 손을 알아요. 저는 알아요—\" 그 아이는 그 말에 세게 힘을 주었다. 그 아이는 저를 사로잡은 격분에 발을 굴렀다—\"저는 고드프리 에이블화이트가 죄 없음을 알아요! 저를 치안판사에게 데려가 줘요, 고드프리! 저를 치안판사에게 데려가 줘요, 그럼 제가 그것을 맹세하겠어요!\"\n\n이모님이 내 손을 붙드시고, 속삭이셨다. \"일이 분만 우리 사이에 서 있으렴. 레이철이 나를 못 보게 하고.\" 나는 이모님 얼굴에 나를 놀라게 하는 푸르스름한 빛이 도는 것을 알아챘다. 이모님은 내가 놀란 것을 보셨다. \"그 방울이 일이 분이면 나를 낫게 할 거야.\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하시고는, 그렇게 눈을 감으시고, 잠시 기다리셨다.\n\n이 일이 벌어지는 동안, 사랑하는 고드프리 씨가 여전히 부드럽게 타이르는 소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"당신은 이런 일에 세상 사람들 앞에 나서서는 안 돼요.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 평판은, 더없이 사랑하는 레이철, 함부로 다루기에는 너무 순결하고 너무 성스러운 것이에요.\"\n\n\"제 평판이요!\" 그 아이는 왈칵 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"아니, 저도, 고드프리, 당신처럼 고발당했어요. 잉글랜드 최고의 형사가 제가 제 다이아몬드를 훔쳤다고 선언한다고요. 그이에게 뭐라 생각하는지 물어봐요—그럼 그이는 제가 제 사사로운 빚을 갚으려고 월장석을 저당 잡혔다고 말할 거예요!\" 그 아이는 멈추더니, 방을 가로질러 달려가—제 어머니 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다. \"오 엄마! 엄마! 엄마! 지금 진실을 털어놓지 않다니—저는 미친 게 틀림없어요—그렇지 않아요?\" 그 아이는 너무 격해서 제 어머니의 상태를 알아채지 못했다—그 아이는 어느새 다시 일어나, 순식간에 고드프리 씨에게 도로 가 있었다. \"저는 당신이—어떤 죄 없는 사내가—제 잘못으로 고발당하고 망신당하도록 두지 않겠어요. 당신이 저를 치안판사 앞에 데려가지 않겠다면, 당신의 결백을 종이에 선언서로 적어 줘요, 그럼 제가 그것에 서명하겠어요. 제가 이르는 대로 해요, 고드프리, 안 그러면 제가 그걸 신문에 써 보내겠어요—제가 밖으로 나가, 그걸 거리에서 외치겠어요!\"\n\n이것이 뉘우침의 말이었다고는 말하지 않으련다—우리는 그것이 히스테리의 말이었다고 말하련다. 너그러운 고드프리 씨가 종이 한 장을 꺼내, 그 선언서를 적어 내려감으로써 그 아이를 달랬다. 그 아이는 열병 같은 다급함으로 그것에 서명했다. \"그걸 어디에나 보여 줘요—저는 생각하지 말고요.\" 하고 그 아이가 그것을 그에게 주며 말했다. \"제가, 고드프리, 여태껏, 제 마음속에서 당신을 정당하게 대하지 못한 것 같아 걱정이에요. 당신은 제가 믿었던 것보다 더 사심이 없어요—더 나은 사람이에요. 올 수 있을 때 이리 와요, 그럼 제가 당신에게 저지른 잘못을 바로잡으려 애써 볼게요.\"\n\n그 아이는 그에게 제 손을 내밀었다. 아아, 우리의 타락한 본성이여! 아아, 고드프리 씨여! 그는 그 아이의 손에 입 맞출 만큼 스스로를 잊었을 뿐만 아니라—이런 경우에는 죄와의 타협보다 나을 것이 거의 없는 부드러운 어조를 취해 그 아이에게 답했다. \"가겠어요, 더없이 사랑하는 이.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"우리가 이 밉살스러운 화제를 다시는 이야기하지 않는다는 조건으로요.\" 나는 우리의 기독교적 영웅을 이때만큼 못한 모습으로 보고 들은 적이 없었다.\n\n누구든 한마디를 더 할 수 있기도 전에, 현관문을 우레같이 두드리는 소리가 우리 모두를 놀라게 했다. 나는 창밖을 내다보았고, 세상과 육신과 악마가 그 집 앞에서 기다리는 것을 보았다—마차와 말들과, 분을 바른 하인과, 내 평생 본 가운데 가장 뻔뻔스럽게 차려입은 세 여자로 표상된 채로 말이다.\n\n레이철은 흠칫하더니, 마음을 다잡았다. 그 아이는 방을 가로질러 제 어머니에게로 갔다.\n\n\"저를 꽃 전시회에 데려가려고 왔어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"가기 전에 한마디만요, 엄마. 제가 엄마를 괴롭히진 않았죠, 그렇죠?\"\n\n(방금 있었던 일 뒤에, 그런 물음을 던질 수 있는 그 도덕적 감각의 무딤은, 가엾이 여겨야 할 것인가 나무라야 할 것인가? 나는 자비 쪽으로 기우는 편이 좋다. 그것을 가엾이 여기자.)\n\n그 방울들이 효험을 냈다. 나의 가엾은 이모님의 낯빛이 다시 본디대로 돌아와 있었다. \"아니, 아니야, 얘야.\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"우리 벗들과 함께 가서, 즐겁게 지내렴.\"\n\n그 따님은 몸을 굽혀, 이모님께 입 맞추었다. 나는 창가를 떠나, 문 가까이에 있었는데, 그때 레이철이 나가려고 그 문으로 다가왔다. 그 아이에게 또 한 번 변화가 일었다—그 아이는 눈물을 흘리고 있었다. 나는 그 완고한 마음의 순간적인 누그러짐을 흥미롭게 바라보았다. 나는 간곡한 말 몇 마디를 하고 싶은 마음이 들었다. 아아! 나의 선의의 동정이 그저 비위를 거스르고 말았다. \"저를 가엾이 여긴다니 무슨 뜻이에요?\" 하고 그 아이가, 문으로 지나가며, 쓰라린 속삭임으로 물었다. \"제가 얼마나 행복한지 안 보여요? 저는 꽃 전시회에 가요, 클랙. 그리고 저는 런던에서 가장 예쁜 보닛을 썼고요.\" 그 아이는 내게 손 키스를 날려 보냄으로써 그 속 빈 조롱의 인사를 마무리했다—그러고는 방을 나섰다.\n\n나는 이 비참하고 그릇 인도된 아이에게 느낀 그 연민을 말로 묘사할 수 있으면 좋겠다. 허나 나는 돈만큼이나 말도 넉넉지 못하다. 이 말만은 하게 해 다오—나의 가슴이 그 아이를 위해 피 흘렸다.\n\n이모님의 의자로 돌아오다가, 나는 사랑하는 고드프리 씨가 방의 여기저기, 이곳저곳에서 무언가를 부드럽게 찾고 있는 것을 보았다. 내가 그를 돕겠다고 나서기도 전에 그는 원하는 것을 찾아냈다. 그는 한 손에는 제 결백의 선언서를, 다른 손에는 성냥갑을 든 채, 이모님과 내게로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"사랑하는 이모님, 조그만 모의 하나!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"사랑하는 클랙 양, 당신의 그 높은 도덕적 올곧음조차 눈감아 줄 경건한 속임수 하나! 레이철이, 이 종이에 서명한 그 갸륵한 자기희생을 제가 받아들이는 줄로 여기도록 놓아두시겠습니까? 그리고 제가 이 집을 떠나기 전에, 두 분 앞에서 이것을 없애는 것을 친절히 증언해 주시겠습니까?\" 그는 성냥을 켜, 그 종이에 불을 붙여, 탁자 위의 접시에 담아 태웠다. \"제가 겪을 수 있는 그 어떤 사소한 불편도,\" 하고 그가 짚었다. \"저 순결한 이름을 세상의 오염시키는 접촉으로부터 지켜 내는 일의 중대함에 견주면, 아무것도 아닙니다. 자! 우리는 그것을 조그맣고 해롭잖은 잿더미로 만들어 버렸습니다. 그리고 우리의 사랑스러운 충동적인 레이철은 우리가 무슨 일을 했는지 결코 알지 못할 겁니다! 기분이 어떠십니까? 나의 소중한 벗들이여, 기분이 어떠십니까? 이 가엾은 저로 말하자면, 저는 소년처럼 마음이 가볍습니다!\"\n\n그는 그 아름다운 미소로 우리를 향해 환히 빛냈다. 그는 한 손을 이모님께, 한 손을 내게 내밀었다. 나는 그의 고결한 행동에 너무 깊이 감동해 말을 할 수가 없었다. 나는 눈을 감았다. 나는, 일종의 영적인 자기 망각 속에서, 그의 손을 내 입술에 갖다 대었다. 그가 부드럽게 나무라는 말을 웅얼거렸다. 오 그 순간의 황홀함, 그 순결하고, 이 세상 것이 아닌 황홀함이여! 나는 앉아—무엇에 앉았는지도 잘 모르겠다—나 자신의 드높여진 감정에 아주 넋을 잃었다. 다시 눈을 떴을 때, 그것은 천국에서 땅으로 내려오는 것과 같았다. 방에는 이모님밖에 없었다. 그는 가고 없었다.\n\n나는 여기서 멈추고 싶다—나는 고드프리 씨의 그 고결한 행동의 기록으로 나의 이야기를 맺고 싶다. 불행히도 더, 훨씬 더 많은 것이 있어, 블레이크 씨의 수표라는 그 가차 없는 금전적 압박이 나로 하여금 이야기하지 않을 수 없게 한다. 그 화요일의 몬터규 스퀘어 방문 동안, 내 앞에서 저를 드러내게 될 그 고통스러운 폭로들은 아직 끝나지 않은 것이었다.\n\n레이디 버린더와 단둘이 남은 나 자신을 발견하고, 나는 자연스레 이모님의 건강이라는 화제로 돌아섰다. 이모님이 당신의 병환과, 그에 쓴 약을 당신 따님의 눈에서 감추려 보이신 그 이상한 근심을 조심스레 건드리면서 말이다.\n\n이모님의 대답은 나를 크게 놀라게 했다.\n\n\"드루실라,\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다 (내 세례명이 드루실라라는 것을 아직 언급하지 않았다면, 지금 그것을 언급하게 해 다오). \"너는 아주 순진하게, 나도 알거니와—더없이 마음 아픈 화제를 건드리고 있구나.\"\n\n나는 즉시 일어섰다. 조심성이 내게는 오직 하나의 선택지를 남겼다—먼저 사과를 드린 뒤, 물러가는 선택지 말이다. 레이디 버린더는 나를 멈춰 세우시고, 다시 앉으라고 우기셨다.\n\n\"너는 어떤 비밀을 뜻하지 않게 알아냈구나,\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"내가 내 언니 에이블화이트 부인과, 내 변호사 브러프 씨에게만 털어놓고, 그 밖에는 아무에게도 털어놓지 않은 비밀을 말이야. 나는 그들의 신중함을 믿을 수 있다. 그리고 네게 그 정황을 이야기하면, 나는 너의 신중함도 믿을 수 있으리라 확신한다. 무슨 급한 볼일이라도 있니, 드루실라? 아니면 오늘 오후 시간은 네 마음대로 쓸 수 있고?\"\n\n내 시간이 온전히 이모님 뜻에 달려 있었음은 말할 것도 없다.\n\n\"그럼 나와 함께 있어 다오,\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"한 시간만 더. 나는 네게, 네가 듣기 서글퍼하리라 믿는 무언가를 이야기해야겠다. 그리고 그 뒤에, 네가 나를 돕기를 마다하지 않는다면, 네게 부탁할 일이 하나 있을 게다.\"\n\n마다하기는커녕, 내가 이모님을 도우려는 열의로 가득했음은 다시 말할 것도 없다.\n\n\"너는 여기서 기다리면 된다,\" 하고 이모님이 말을 이으셨다. \"다섯 시에 브러프 씨가 올 때까지 말이야. 그리고 너는, 드루실라, 내가 내 유언장에 서명할 때, 증인 가운데 하나가 되어 주면 된다.\"\n\n이모님의 유언장이라니! 나는 이모님 반짇고리에서 본 그 방울들을 떠올렸다. 나는 이모님 낯빛에서 알아챈 그 푸르스름한 빛을 떠올렸다. 이 세상 것이 아닌 빛이—아직 만들어지지 않은 무덤에서 예언처럼 비쳐 오는 빛이—내 마음에 밝아 왔다. 이모님의 비밀은 더는 비밀이 아니었다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n가엾은 레이디 버린더에 대한 배려가, 이모님이 입을 여시기 전에 내가 그 서글픈 진실을 짐작했다는 것을 넌지시라도 비치는 것을 내게 금했다. 나는 말없이 이모님의 뜻을 기다렸다. 그리고, 첫 알맞은 기회에 몇 마디 지탱해 주는 말을 하리라 남몰래 마련해 둔 채, 그것이 아무리 고통스러울지라도, 나를 부를 수 있는 그 어떤 소임에도 마음의 채비가 된 느낌이었다.\n\n\"나는 지난 얼마 동안, 드루실라, 심하게 앓아 왔단다.\" 하고 이모님이 말문을 여셨다. \"그런데, 이상하게도, 나 자신은 그것을 모른 채 말이야.\"\n\n나는 바로 그 순간 저 자신은 그것을 모른 채 영적으로 앓고 있는 수천수만의 스러져 가는 인간들을 떠올렸다. 그리고 나는 나의 가엾은 이모님도 그 무리 가운데 하나일까 봐 몹시 두려웠다. \"네, 이모님.\" 하고 나는 서글피 말했다. \"네.\"\n\n\"너도 알다시피, 나는 의료 조언을 구하러 레이철을 런던으로 데려왔지.\" 하고 이모님이 말을 이으셨다. \"나는 의사 둘에게 진찰받는 것이 옳다고 여겼어.\"\n\n의사 둘! 그리고, 오 저런, (레이철의 상태에서) 목사님은 한 분도 없이! \"네, 이모님?\" 하고 나는 다시 한번 말했다. \"네?\"\n\n\"그 두 의사 가운데 하나는,\" 하고 이모님이 이어 가셨다. \"내게 낯선 사람이었어. 다른 하나는 내 남편의 오랜 벗이었고, 언제나 내 남편을 봐서 나에게 진심 어린 관심을 느껴 온 사람이었지. 레이철에게 처방을 내린 뒤, 그는 다른 방에서 나와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다고 했어. 나는, 물론, 내 딸의 건강을 돌보는 데 대한 어떤 특별한 지시를 받으리라 예상했지. 놀랍게도, 그는 진지하게 내 손을 잡고, 이렇게 말했어. '저는, 레이디 버린더, 직업적 관심으로도 개인적 관심으로도 마님을 살펴보고 있었습니다. 마님은, 유감스럽게도, 따님보다 훨씬 더 절박하게 의료 조언이 필요하십니다.' 그는 내게 몇 가지 물음을 던졌는데, 나는 처음에는 그것들을 넉넉히 가볍게 다루려는 편이었어, 내 대답이 그를 괴롭게 하는 것을 알아채기 전까지는. 그것은 그가 이튿날, 레이철이 집에 없을 시각에, 어느 의사 벗을 대동하고 나를 보러 오겠다는 약속을 잡는 것으로 끝났지. 그 방문의 결과는—더없이 친절하고 부드럽게 내게 전해졌거니와—두 의사 모두에게, 결코 되찾을 수 없는 귀중한 시간이 허비되었으며, 내 병이 이제 그들의 의술이 미치는 데를 지나 버렸음을 납득시켰어. 이 년 넘게 나는 은밀한 형태의 심장병을 앓아 왔는데, 그것은, 나를 놀라게 할 아무 증상도 없이, 조금씩 조금씩, 나를 돌이킬 수 없이 허물어뜨려 왔지. 나는 몇 달을 더 살 수도 있고, 아니면 내 머리 위로 하루가 더 지나기 전에 죽을 수도 있어—의사들도 이 이상으로 딱 잘라 말할 수는, 또 감히 그럴 수는 없다는구나. 내 실상이 내게 알려진 뒤로 내가 얼마간 비참한 순간을 겪지 않았다고 말하는 것은, 얘야, 부질없는 노릇이겠지. 허나 나는 예전보다는 마음을 다잡았고, 내 세속의 일들을 정리하는 데 최선을 다하고 있어. 나의 한 가지 큰 근심은 레이철이 그 진실을 모른 채로 있어야 한다는 거야. 만일 그 아이가 그것을 안다면, 그 아이는 대뜸 내 허물어진 건강을 다이아몬드에 대한 근심 탓으로 돌리고, 결코 그 아이 잘못이 아닌 일로, 가엾은 것, 저 자신을 쓰라리게 나무랄 테니까. 두 의사 모두 그 화근이 이 년, 어쩌면 삼 년 전에 시작되었다는 데 뜻을 같이해. 나는 네가 내 비밀을 지켜 주리라 확신한다, 드루실라—네 얼굴에서 나를 향한 진심 어린 슬픔과 동정이 보인다고 확신하니 말이야.\"\n\n슬픔과 동정이라니! 오, 제 믿음에 굳게 닻을 내린 기독교인 영국 여자에게서 기대하기에 이 무슨 이교도 같은 감정인가!\n\n나의 가엾은 이모님은, 당신의 그 서글픈 이야기의 끝에 다가가시는 동안 얼마나 벅찬 경건한 감사가 나를 전율케 하는지 조금도 짐작하지 못하셨다. 여기 쓸모의 길이 내 앞에 열려 있었던 것이다! 여기, 사랑하는 친척이자 스러져 가는 동료 인간이, 그 큰 변화의 문턱에서, 전혀 채비가 되지 않은 채 있었고—섭리의 인도로 이끌려, 제 처지를 내게 드러낸 것이다! 이제 내가, 내가 믿고 기댈 수 있는 그 소중한 성직자 벗들이, 하나둘로가 아니라, 열, 스물로 헤아려진다는 것을 떠올린 그 기쁨을 어찌 묘사할 수 있으랴. 나는 이모님을 내 품에 안았다—넘쳐 흐르는 내 다정함이, 이제는, 포옹 아닌 무엇으로도 흡족해질 수 없었다. \"오!\" 하고 나는 뜨겁게 이모님께 말했다. \"이모님이 제 안에 불러일으키는 그 형언할 수 없는 관심이라니! 오! 우리가 헤어지기 전에, 이모님, 제가 이모님께 하려는 그 선이라니!\" 몇 마디 더 간곡한 서두의 경고를 건넨 뒤, 나는 이모님께 세 소중한 벗 가운데 골라잡으시게 했는데, 다들 이모님의 이웃에서 아침부터 밤까지 자비의 일에 힘쓰고, 다들 똑같이 지칠 줄 모르고 권면하며, 다들 나의 한마디에 다정하게 제 은사를 베풀 채비가 되어 있는 이들이었다. 아아! 그 결과는 도무지 신통치 못했다. 가엾은 레이디 버린더는 어리둥절하고 겁먹은 낯빛으로, 내가 이모님께 할 수 있는 온갖 말을, 낯선 이들을 마주할 만큼 기운이 넉넉지 못하다는 순전히 세속적인 이의로 받으셨다. 나는 물러섰다—물론, 그저 그때만이다. (여태껏 열넷도 넘는 사랑하는 성직자 벗들 밑에서, 낭독자이자 방문자로서 쌓은) 나의 넓은 경험이, 이것이 또 하나의 책으로 준비할 경우임을 내게 일러 주었다. 나는 저작들의 조그만 서고를 지니고 있었는데, 죄다 지금의 이 위급함에 알맞고, 죄다 이모님을 일깨우고, 납득시키고, 준비시키고, 깨우치고, 굳세게 하도록 헤아려진 것들이었다. \"읽으실 거죠, 이모님, 그렇죠?\" 하고 나는 더없이 사근사근하게 말했다. \"제가 저의 소중한 책들을 가져다드리면, 읽으실 거죠? 알맞은 데는 죄다 페이지 귀퉁이를 접어서요, 이모님. 그리고 '이것이 내게 해당되는가?' 하고 멈춰 서서 스스로에게 물어야 할 데는 연필로 표시해서요.\" 그런 소박한 호소조차—세상의 영향이 어찌나 철저히 사람을 이교화하는지—이모님을 놀라게 하는 듯싶었다. 이모님은 \"내가 할 수 있는 것을 하마, 드루실라, 너를 기쁘게 하려고.\" 하고, 놀란 듯한 낯빛으로 말씀하셨는데, 그것은 교훈적이면서도 동시에 보기에 끔찍했다. 한순간도 허비할 수 없었다. 벽난로 위의 시계가, 내가 서둘러 집에 가서, 골라낸 읽을거리의 첫 묶음을 (이를테면 열두어 부만) 마련해 오고, 변호사를 만나 레이디 버린더의 유언장을 증언하기에 딱 알맞을 겨를이 있음을 내게 일러 주었다. 다섯 시까지 반드시 돌아오겠노라 약속하고, 나는 자비의 심부름을 나서 그 집을 떠났다.\n\n나 자신의 잇속밖에 걸려 있지 않을 때는, 나는 겸손히 합승 마차로 이곳에서 저곳으로 다니는 것에 만족한다. 이번에는, 삯마차를 타는 낭비를 저질렀음을 적음으로써, 이모님의 잇속에 대한 나의 헌신을 여러분이 짐작하게 해 다오.\n\n나는 마차로 집에 가서, 읽을거리의 첫 묶음을 골라 표시하고, 융단 가방에 열두어 부의 저작을 담아 몬터규 스퀘어로 다시 몰고 왔는데, 그런 것들은, 내가 굳게 믿거니와, 유럽의 다른 어느 나라 문헌에서도 찾아볼 수 없는 것들이다. 나는 마부에게 삯을 꼭 그만큼만 치렀다. 그는 그것을 욕설과 함께 받았다. 그래서 나는 대뜸 그에게 소책자 한 부를 주었다. 내가 그의 머리에 권총을 겨누었던들, 이 막된 몹쓸 자는 그보다 더 큰 경악을 좀처럼 드러내지 못했으리라. 그는 제 마부석에 뛰어올라, 낭패의 불경한 외침과 함께, 사납게 몰아 떠나갔다. 아주 부질없었다, 말할 수 있어 기쁘거니와! 나는 그에게도 아랑곳없이, 마차 창 안으로 두 번째 소책자를 던져 넣음으로써, 좋은 씨앗을 뿌렸다.\n\n문을 열어 준 하인은—그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 아니라, 나의 큰 안도스럽게도, 그 남자 하인이었는데—의사가 다녀갔고, 여전히 레이디 버린더와 함께 방에 들어앉아 있다고 내게 알려 주었다. 변호사 브러프 씨가 조금 전에 도착해 서재에서 기다리고 있었다. 나도 기다리도록 서재로 안내되었다.\n\n브러프 씨는 나를 보고 놀란 듯했다. 그는 그 집안의 사무 변호사로, 우리는 예전에 레이디 버린더의 지붕 아래서 여러 번 만난 적이 있었다. 말하기 서글프거니와, 세상을 섬기다 늙어 반백이 된 사내다. 볼일을 보는 시간에는, 법과 맘몬의 뽑힌 예언자였고, 한가한 시간에는, 소설을 읽는 것도 소책자를 찢는 것도 똑같이 능히 해내는 사내였다.\n\n\"여기 머무르러 오셨습니까, 클랙 양?\" 하고 그가, 내 융단 가방을 흘긋 보며 물었다.\n\n이런 사람에게 나의 소중한 가방의 내용물을 드러내는 것은 그저 불경의 폭발을 부르는 노릇이었으리라. 나는 그 자신의 수준으로 나를 낮추어, 이 집에서의 내 볼일을 언급했다.\n\n\"이모님이 곧 유언장에 서명하실 거라고 제게 알려 주셨어요.\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"고맙게도 저더러 증인 가운데 하나가 되어 달라 청하셨고요.\"\n\n\"그래요? 그래요? 음, 클랙 양, 당신이면 되겠군요. 당신은 스물한 살이 넘었고, 레이디 버린더의 유언장에 조금의 금전적 이해관계도 없으니 말입니다.\"\n\n레이디 버린더의 유언장에 조금의 금전적 이해관계도 없다니. 오, 그 말을 들었을 때 나는 얼마나 감사했던지! 만일 수천 파운드를 지닌 이모님이, 오 파운드도 큰돈인 이 가엾은 나를 기억하셨더라면—만일 내 이름이, 조그마한 위안이 될 유산과 함께, 그 유언장에 나타났더라면—나의 원수들은 나로 하여금 내 서고의 가장 빼어난 보물들을 짊어지게 하고, 삯마차의 헤픈 비용을 위해 내 쪼그라드는 살림을 축내게 한 그 동기를 의심했을지 모른다. 이제는 그들 가운데 가장 모진 조롱꾼도 의심할 수 없었다. 이대로가 훨씬 나았다! 오, 분명, 분명, 이대로가 훨씬 나았다!\n\n나는 브러프 씨의 목소리에 이 위안이 되는 상념에서 깨어났다. 나의 명상에 잠긴 침묵이 이 속물의 마음을 짓눌러, 이를테면, 그 자신의 뜻과는 어긋나게 나에게 말을 걸도록 그를 몰아붙이는 듯싶었다.\n\n\"음, 클랙 양, 자선계의 최근 소식은 무엇입니까? 당신의 벗 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서 그 악당들에게 두들겨 맞은 뒤로 어떻습니까? 이런! 우리 클럽에서는 그 자선가 신사분에 대해 제법 재미난 이야기를 하고들 있답니다!\"\n\n나는 이 사람이 내가 스물한 살이 넘었다는 것과, 이모님 유언장에 금전적 이해관계가 없다는 것을 짚은 그 태도는 그냥 넘겼다. 허나 그가 사랑하는 고드프리 씨를 넌지시 이르는 그 어조는 내 참을성으로 감당하기에 너무 지나쳤다. 그날 오후 내 앞에서 벌어진 일 뒤로, 나의 감탄스러운 벗의 결백이 의문시되는 것을 볼 때마다 그것을 주장할 의무가 있다고 느끼는 터라—나는 또한 이 정의로운 목적을 이루는 데에, 브러프 씨의 경우에는 따끔한 벌을 곁들일 의무가 있다고도 느꼈음을 인정한다.\n\n\"저는 아주 세상 밖에서 삽니다.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그리고, 선생, 저는 클럽에 속하는 이점을 지니고 있지 않아요. 하지만 저는 마침 선생이 넌지시 이르는 그 이야기를 알고 있어요. 그리고 저는 또한, 그 이야기보다 더 비열한 거짓은 여태껏 있은 적이 없다는 것도 알고요.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요, 클랙 양—당신은 당신 벗을 믿는군요. 넉넉히 자연스러운 일이지요. 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 자선하는 부인들의 위원회만큼 세상 일반을 그리 쉽게 납득시키지는 못할 겁니다. 겉모습이 죄다 그에게 불리하거든요. 그는 다이아몬드를 잃었을 때 그 집에 있었어요. 그리고 그 뒤 그 집에서 맨 처음 런던으로 간 사람이 그였고요. 그것들은 고약한 정황입니다, 부인, 나중의 일들에 비추어 보자면 말이지요.\"\n\n나도 알거니와, 나는 그가 더 나아가기 전에 그를 바로잡았어야 했다. 나는 그에게, 그가 이 화제에 대한 확실한 앎으로부터 말할 수 있음이 부인할 수 없는 유일한 사람이 내놓은, 고드프리 씨의 결백에 대한 증언을 모른 채 말하고 있다고 일러 주었어야 했다. 아아! 그 변호사를 교묘히 이끌어 그 자신의 낭패로 몰아넣으려는 유혹이 나로서는 너무 지나쳤다. 나는 그가 \"나중의 일들\"로 무엇을 뜻하느냐고—더없이 순진한 낯빛으로—물었다.\n\n\"나중의 일들로, 클랙 양, 저는 그 인도인들이 관련된 일들을 뜻합니다.\" 하고 브러프 씨가, 말을 이어 갈수록 이 가엾은 나보다 점점 더 잘난 체하며 이어 갔다. \"그 인도인들이 프리징홀 감옥에서 풀려나는 그 순간 무엇을 합니까? 그들은 곧장 런던으로 가서, 루커 씨를 점찍습니다. 무엇이 뒤따르나요? 루커 씨는 제가 집에 지닌 '값이 대단히 비싼 귀중품'의 안전을 걱정합니다. 그는 그것을 (뭉뚱그린 묘사로) 제 은행의 금고실에 남몰래 맡깁니다. 놀랍도록 영리하지요. 허나 인도인들도 저희 편에서 꼭 그만큼 영리합니다. 그들은 그 '값이 대단히 비싼 귀중품'이 이곳에서 저곳으로 옮겨지고 있다는 의심을 품습니다. 그리고 그들은 그 의심을 풀 유난히 대담하고 빈틈없는 방도를 찾아냅니다. 그들이 누구를 붙잡아 뒤지나요? 루커 씨만이 아니라—그거라면 넉넉히 알아들을 만하지요—고드프리 에이블화이트 씨까지입니다. 왜죠? 에이블화이트 씨의 해명은, 그가 우연히 루커 씨와 이야기하는 것을 본 뒤, 그들이 눈먼 의심에서 그리했다는 겁니다. 어림없어요! 그날 아침 예닐곱 명의 다른 사람들도 루커 씨와 이야기했어요. 어째서 그들은 집까지 미행당하고 덫으로 꾀어지지 않았나요? 아니! 아니에요! 명백한 추론은, 에이블화이트 씨도 루커 씨처럼 그 '귀중품'에 남모를 잇속이 있었으며, 인도인들이 그 둘 중 누가 그것을 처분할 수 있는지 어찌나 확신하지 못했던지, 둘 다 뒤지는 것 말고는 다른 방도가 없었다는 겁니다. 여론이 그리 말합니다, 클랙 양. 그리고 여론은, 이 경우에, 쉽게 반박되지 않지요.\"\n\n그는 그 마지막 말을, 저 나름의 세속적 자만 속에서 놀랍도록 현명해 보이는 낯빛으로 뱉었는데, 그래서 나는 정말로 (낯 뜨겁게도 하는 말이지만) 진실로 그를 압도하기 전에 그를 조금 더 이끌고 나가는 것을 참을 수가 없었다.\n\n\"저는 선생 같은 영리한 변호사와 논쟁할 주제가 못 됩니다.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"하지만, 선생, 이 사건을 조사한 그 이름난 런던 경찰관의 견해를 그냥 넘기는 것이 에이블화이트 씨에게 온당한 일일까요? 커프 부장의 마음속에는, 버린더 아가씨 말고는 그 누구에게도 의심의 그림자 하나 드리워지지 않았어요.\"\n\n\"당신 지금 나더러, 클랙 양, 당신이 그 부장에게 동의한다고 말하는 겁니까?\"\n\n\"저는 누구도 판단하지 않아요, 선생, 그리고 저는 아무 의견도 내지 않고요.\"\n\n\"그런데 나는 그 두 가지 죄악을 다 저지릅니다, 부인. 나는 그 부장이 완전히 틀렸다고 판단합니다. 그리고 나는, 만일 그가 내가 아는 만큼 레이철의 됨됨이를 알았더라면, 그 집에서 그 아이만 빼고 모두를 의심했으리라는 의견을 냅니다. 나는 그 아이에게 흠이 있음을 인정해요—그 아이는 속을 감추고, 고집이 세지요. 별나고 사납고, 제 또래의 다른 아이들과 다르고요. 하지만 강철처럼 참되고, 흠잡을 데 없이 고결하고 너그럽습니다. 만일 세상에서 가장 명백한 증거가 한쪽을 가리키고, 오직 레이철의 명예를 건 말만이 다른 쪽을 가리킨다 해도, 나는, 변호사인 나로서도, 그 증거보다 그 아이의 말을 택하겠습니다! 센 말이지요, 클랙 양. 허나 나는 진심입니다.\"\n\n\"선생의 뜻을 예를 들어 설명해 주시겠어요, 브러프 씨, 제가 그것을 확실히 이해하도록요? 만일 선생이 버린더 아가씨가 에이블화이트 씨와 루커 씨에게 일어난 일에 아주 영문 모르게 관심을 두는 것을 발견했다고 해 봐요. 만일 그 아이가 이 끔찍한 추문에 대해 더없이 이상한 물음들을 던지고, 그것이 트는 방향을 알아냈을 때 더없이 다스릴 수 없는 흥분을 드러냈다고 해 봐요.\"\n\n\"당신 좋을 대로 무엇이든 가정해 보세요, 클랙 양, 그것이 레이철 버린더에 대한 내 믿음을 털끝만큼도 흔들지 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇게까지 온전히 믿고 기댈 만하다고요?\"\n\n\"그렇게까지 온전히 믿고 기댈 만합니다.\"\n\n\"그럼 선생께 알려 드리게 해 주세요, 브러프 씨, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 채 두 시간도 안 되어 이 집에 있었고, 월장석의 실종과 관련한 온갖 혐의에 대한 그의 온전한 결백이, 내 평생 젊은 아가씨가 쓴 것을 들어 본 가운데 가장 센 말로, 버린더 아가씨 자신에 의해 선언되었다는 것을요.\"\n\n나는 이 가엾은 나의 몇 마디 명백한 말에 브러프 씨가 완전히 당황하고 무너지는 것을 보는 승리감을—낯 뜨겁게도 인정하거니와, 그 거룩하지 못한 승리감을—맛보았다. 그는 벌떡 일어서서, 말없이 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 나는 흔들림 없이 내 자리를 지키고, 그 온 장면을 일어난 그대로 이야기했다. \"그럼 이제 에이블화이트 씨에 대해 뭐라 하시겠어요?\" 하고 나는, 이야기를 마치자마자, 될 수 있는 대로 더없이 부드럽게 물었다.\n\n\"레이철이 그의 결백을 증언했다면, 클랙 양, 나는 서슴없이 말하겠습니다, 나도 당신만큼 굳게 그의 결백을 믿는다고요. 나도 세상 나머지처럼 겉모습에 속아 넘어갔던 겁니다. 그리고 나는 할 수 있는 최선의 속죄를, 당신 벗을 덮친 그 추문을 어디서 마주치든 드러내 놓고 반박함으로써 하겠습니다. 그러는 동안, 당신이 내가 가장 뜻하지 않은 순간에 나를 향해 당신 포대의 전면적인 포화를 열어젖힌 그 능란한 솜씨에 대해 축하하게 해 주세요. 당신이 마침 사내였다면, 부인, 당신은 내 직업에서 대단한 일을 해냈을 겁니다.\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 나에게서 몸을 돌려, 짜증스럽게 방 안을 오락가락 걷기 시작했다.\n\n내가 그 화제에 던진 새 빛이 그를 크게 놀라게 하고 심란하게 했음을 나는 뻔히 알 수 있었다. 그가 그 자신의 생각에 점점 더 빠져들면서, 어떤 표현들이 그의 입술에서 새어 나왔는데, 그것들은 그가 여태껏 잃어버린 월장석의 수수께끼에 대해 취해 온 그 얄미운 견해를 내 마음에 넌지시 알려 주었다. 그는 서슴없이 사랑하는 고드프리 씨를 다이아몬드를 훔친 파렴치의 혐의로 의심하고, 레이철의 행동을 그 죄를 감추려는 갸륵한 결심 탓으로 돌린 것이었다. 버린더 아가씨 자신의 확언으로—여러분도 아시다시피, 브러프 씨의 평가에서는 더없이 반박할 수 없는 확언으로—그 정황에 대한 그 설명이 이제 완전히 틀린 것으로 드러났다. 내가 이 높은 법률적 권위자를 빠뜨려 넣은 그 당혹감이 어찌나 압도적이던지 그는 그것을 남의 눈에서 도무지 감출 수가 없었다. \"이 무슨 사건인가!\" 하고 그가, 오락가락하다 창가에 멈춰 서서, 손가락으로 유리를 두드리며 혼잣말하는 소리가 들렸다. \"이건 설명을 거스를 뿐만 아니라, 억측조차 넘어서는군.\"\n\n이 말들에는 내 쪽에서 어떤 대꾸도 조금도 필요하게 만드는 것이 없었다—그런데도, 나는 그 말들에 대꾸했다! 이제 와서까지도 내가 브러프 씨를 가만 놓아둘 수 없었다는 것은 좀처럼 믿기지 않는 노릇인 듯싶다. 그가 방금 한 말 속에서, 그에게 나 자신을 개인적으로 밉살스럽게 굴 새로운 기회를 발견했다는 것은 한낱 인간의 심술을 거의 넘어서는 노릇인 듯싶다. 허나—아, 나의 벗들이여! 무엇도 인간의 심술을 넘어서지 못한다. 그리고 우리의 타락한 본성이 우리를 이길 때는 무엇이든 믿을 만하다!\n\n\"선생의 상념을 방해해서 죄송해요.\" 하고 나는 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 브러프 씨에게 말했다. \"하지만 우리에게 아직 떠오르지 않은, 해 봄 직한 억측이 하나 분명 있어요.\"\n\n\"어쩌면요, 클랙 양. 그게 무엇인지 나는 모르겠습니다만.\"\n\n\"제가, 선생, 다행히도 선생께 에이블화이트 씨의 결백을 납득시키기 전에, 선생은 그를 의심하는 까닭 가운데 하나로, 다이아몬드를 잃었을 때 그가 그 집에 있었다는 것을 언급하셨어요. 프랭클린 블레이크 씨도 다이아몬드를 잃었을 때 그 집에 있었다는 것을 상기시켜 드리게 해 주세요.\"\n\n그 늙은 속물은 창가를 떠나, 내 맞은편에 딱 마주 보이는 의자를 잡아 앉고는, 딱딱하고 짓궂은 미소로 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신은 내가 생각했던 것만큼, 클랙 양,\" 하고 그가 명상에 잠긴 투로 짚었다. \"훌륭한 변호사는 못 되는군요. 당신은 좋은 것을 그냥 놓아둘 줄을 몰라요.\"\n\n\"선생 말씀을 못 따라가겠어요, 브러프 씨.\" 하고 나는 겸손히 말했다.\n\n\"그건 안 통해요, 클랙 양—두 번째로는 정말 안 통합니다. 프랭클린 블레이크는, 당신도 잘 알다시피, 내가 아주 아끼는 사람입니다. 하지만 그건 대수롭지 않아요. 나는 이번에는, 당신이 나를 향해 돌아설 겨를도 없이, 당신 견해를 받아들이겠습니다. 당신 말이 아주 옳아요, 부인. 나는 에이블화이트 씨를, 원리로 따지면 블레이크 씨도 의심하는 것이 정당해지는 근거에서 의심했습니다. 아주 좋아요—그 둘을 함께 의심합시다. 그가 월장석을 훔칠 만하다는 것은, 우리가 말하자면, 그의 됨됨이에 넉넉히 들어맞습니다. 유일한 물음은, 그리하는 것이 그의 잇속이었느냐 하는 겁니다.\"\n\n\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 빚은,\" 하고 나는 짚었다. \"집안에 널리 알려진 일이지요.\"\n\n\"그런데 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 빚은 아직 그런 단계에 이르지 않았지요. 아주 옳은 말입니다. 하지만 당신 이론에는 마침 두 가지 걸림돌이 있어요, 클랙 양. 나는 프랭클린 블레이크의 일을 관리하는데, 당신께 알려 드리지요, 그의 채권자 대다수가 (그의 아버지가 부자임을 아는 터라) 제 빚에 이자를 물리고 제 돈을 기다리는 것에 아주 만족한다고요. 거기 첫 번째 걸림돌이 있어요—이것도 넉넉히 벅차지요. 두 번째는 더 벅차다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다. 나는 레이디 버린더 자신의 확언으로, 그 지긋지긋한 인도 다이아몬드가 집에서 사라지기 전에, 그 따님이 프랭클린 블레이크와 혼인할 채비가 되어 있었다는 것을 알고 있습니다. 그 아이는 젊은 아가씨의 요염함으로 그를 끌어들였다 다시 밀어냈다 했지요. 하지만 그 아이는 사촌 프랭클린을 사랑한다고 제 어머니에게 털어놓았고, 그 어머니는 사촌 프랭클린에게 그 비밀을 맡겼습니다. 그러니 거기 그가 있었던 겁니다, 클랙 양, 채권자들은 기다리는 것에 만족하고, 앞에는 상속녀와 혼인할 확실한 전망이 놓인 채로 말이지요. 얼마든지 그를 악당으로 여기세요. 하지만 말해 주세요, 부디, 어째서 그가 월장석을 훔쳐야 한단 말입니까?\"\n\n\"사람의 마음은 헤아릴 수 없지요.\" 하고 나는 부드럽게 말했다. \"누가 그것을 가늠하겠어요?\"\n\n\"다시 말해, 부인—그가 다이아몬드를 가져갈 이유의 그림자 하나 없었을지라도—그럼에도 그가, 타고난 사악함으로, 그것을 가져갔을 수도 있다는 거군요. 아주 좋아요. 그가 그랬다고 칩시다. 그럼 대체 어째서——\"\n\n\"죄송합니다만, 브러프 씨. 악마가 그런 식으로 입에 오르는 것을 들으면, 저는 이 방을 떠나야겠어요.\"\n\n\"죄송합니다, 클랙 양—앞으로는 말을 고르는 데 더 조심하지요. 내가 묻고자 한 것은 다만 이것입니다. 어째서—그가 다이아몬드를 가져갔다고 가정하더라도—프랭클린 블레이크가 그것을 되찾는 데에 그 집에서 가장 두드러진 사람으로 나섰겠느냐 하는 겁니다. 당신은 나더러, 그가 저 자신에게서 의심을 돌리려고 교활하게 그랬다고 말할지 모르지요. 나는 그가 의심을 돌릴 필요가 없었다고 답합니다—아무도 그를 의심하지 않았으니까요. 그는 먼저 (조금의 이유도 없이) 타고난 사악함으로 월장석을 훔치고, 그런 다음 그 보석의 분실과 관련하여 조금도 할 필요가 없는 역할을 연기하는데, 그 역할이 그로 하여금, 안 그랬다면 그와 혼인했을 그 젊은 아가씨의 비위를 죽도록 거스르게 만듭니다. 그것이, 당신이 월장석의 실종을 프랭클린 블레이크와 엮으려 시도한다면, 당신이 어쩔 수 없이 주장하게 되는 그 터무니없는 명제입니다. 아니, 아니에요, 클랙 양! 오늘 여기서, 우리 둘 사이에 오간 일 뒤로, 이 사건에서, 그 교착은 완전합니다. 레이철 자신의 결백은 (그 어머니가 알고, 내가 알듯이) 의심할 여지가 없어요. 에이블화이트 씨의 결백도 똑같이 확실하고요—안 그랬다면 레이철이 결코 그것을 증언하지 않았을 겁니다. 그리고 프랭클린 블레이크의 결백은, 당신이 방금 보았듯이, 반박할 수 없이 스스로를 주장합니다. 한편으로, 우리는 이 모든 것을 도덕적으로 확신합니다. 그리고, 다른 한편으로, 우리는 누군가가 월장석을 런던으로 가져왔으며, 루커 씨나 그의 은행이 바로 이 순간 그것을 남몰래 지니고 있다는 것도 똑같이 확신하고요. 그런 사건에서, 내 경험이 무슨 소용입니까, 어느 누구의 경험인들 무슨 소용이고요? 그것은 나를 쩔쩔매게 합니다. 그것은 당신을 쩔쩔매게 하고, 그것은 모두를 쩔쩔매게 하지요.\"\n\n아니—모두는 아니었다. 그것은 커프 부장을 쩔쩔매게 하지 못했다. 나는, 될 수 있는 대로 더없이 부드럽게, 그리고 레이철에게 흠집을 내려는 것으로 여겨지는 데 대한 온갖 필요한 항변과 더불어, 이것을 언급하려던 참이었는데—그때 하인이 들어와, 의사가 갔으며, 이모님이 우리를 맞으려 기다리신다고 전했다.\n\n이것이 그 논쟁을 멈추게 했다. 브러프 씨는, 우리 대화가 그에게 요구한 것들에 다소 지친 듯한 낯빛으로, 제 서류를 그러모았다. 나는 소중한 간행물로 가득 찬 내 가방을 집어 들었는데, 몇 시간이라도 계속 이야기할 수 있을 것 같은 느낌이었다. 우리는 말없이 레이디 버린더의 방으로 나아갔다.\n\n나의 이야기가 다른 일들로 나아가기 전에, 여기에 덧붙이게 해 다오, 내가 그 변호사와 나 사이에 오간 일을 묘사한 것이 뚜렷한 목적 없이 한 것이 아님을. 나는 월장석의 그 충격적인 이야기에 대한 나의 기고에, 그 인도 다이아몬드가 런던에 있다고 여겨지던 그때, 의심이 튼 방향뿐만 아니라, 의심이 드리워진 사람들의 이름까지 명백히 밝히도록 명령받았다. 서재에서의 브러프 씨와의 대화에 대한 기록이, 이 목적에 답하기에 딱 필요한 것으로 내게 여겨졌다—그러면서, 동시에, 그것은 나의 편에서 죄스러운 자존심의 희생을 본질적으로 필요하게 만드는 그 큰 도덕적 이점을 지녔다. 나는 나의 타락한 본성이 나를 이겼음을 인정할 수밖에 없었다. 그 창피한 고백을 함으로써, 나는 나의 타락한 본성을 이긴다. 도덕의 저울이 회복된다. 영적인 공기가 다시 한번 맑아지는 느낌이다. 사랑하는 벗들이여, 우리는 다시 나아가도 되겠다.\n\n# CHAPTER IV\n\n유언장에 서명하는 일은 내가 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 짧게 끝났다. 그것은, 내 생각으로는, 볼썽사나울 만큼 서둘러 후딱 해치워졌다. 두 번째 증인 노릇을 하도록 남자 하인 새뮤얼이 불려 왔다—그리고 펜이 대뜸 이모님 손에 쥐어졌다. 나는 이 엄숙한 자리에 몇 마디 알맞은 말을 하고 싶은 강한 충동을 느꼈다. 허나 브러프 씨의 태도가 나로 하여금, 그가 방에 있는 동안에는 그 충동을 억누르는 것이 가장 슬기롭다고 확신하게 했다. 채 이 분도 안 되어 그것은 죄다 끝났다—그리고 새뮤얼은 (내가 했을 법한 말의 덕은 보지 못한 채) 다시 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n브러프 씨는 유언장을 접고는, 내 쪽을 바라보았다. 보아하니 내가 그를 이모님과 단둘이 남겨 둘 뜻이 있는지 없는지 궁금해하는 듯했다. 나에게는 이루어야 할 자비의 사명이 있었고, 소중한 간행물이 든 내 가방이 무릎 위에 준비되어 있었다. 그가 나를 움직이려 하느니, 세인트 폴 대성당을 바라봄으로써 그것을 움직이려 기대하는 편이 나았으리라. 그에게는 (의심할 것 없이 그의 세속적 훈련 덕분인) 한 가지 장점이 있었는데, 나는 그것을 부인할 뜻이 없다. 그는 사물을 재빨리 알아챘다. 나는 그에게, 내가 그 마부에게 냈던 것과 거의 똑같은 인상을 내는 듯싶었다. 그도 불경한 표현을 뱉고, 몹시 부산스럽게 물러가, 나를 이 싸움터의 주인으로 남겨 두었다.\n\n우리 둘만 남자마자, 이모님은 소파에 기대어 누우시고는, 얼마쯤 당혹스러운 낯빛으로, 당신 유언장이라는 화제를 넌지시 이르셨다.\n\n\"네가 소홀히 대접받았다고 여기지 않았으면 좋겠구나, 드루실라.\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"나는 너의 그 조그만 유산을, 얘야, 내 손으로 직접 주려는 거란다.\"\n\n여기 황금 같은 기회가 있었다! 나는 그 자리에서 그것을 붙잡았다. 다시 말해, 나는 대뜸 내 가방을 열어, 맨 위의 간행물을 꺼냈다. 그것은 「집 안의 뱀」이라는 제목의 그 이름난 익명 저작(소중한 벨로스 양의 것으로 여겨진다)의 초기 판—겨우 스물다섯 번째 판—으로 드러났다. 그 책의 취지는—세속적 독자는 익숙하지 않을지 모르나—악한 자가 우리의 나날의 삶에서 겉보기에 가장 죄 없어 보이는 온갖 행동 속에 어떻게 우리를 노리고 도사리는지를 보여 주는 것이다. 여자가 읽기에 가장 알맞은 장들은 '머리빗 속의 사탄', '거울 뒤의 사탄', '찻상 밑의 사탄', '창밖의 사탄'—그 밖에도 여럿이다.\n\n\"이 소중한 책에 정신을 쏟아 주세요, 사랑하는 이모님—그러면 이모님은 제가 청하는 모든 것을 제게 주시는 거예요.\" 그 말과 함께, 나는 그것을, 표시해 둔 대목에 펼쳐서 이모님께 건넸다—타오르는 웅변의 끊임없는 폭발 한 줄기였다! 주제는, 소파 방석 사이의 사탄.\n\n가엾은 레이디 버린더는 (당신 자신의 소파 방석에 아무 생각 없이 기대어 누우신 채) 그 책을 흘긋 보시고는, 그 어느 때보다 더 당혹스러운 낯빛으로 그것을 내게 도로 건네셨다.\n\n\"미안하다만, 드루실라,\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"그것을 읽으려면 내가 조금 더 나아질 때까지 기다려야겠구나. 의사가——\"\n\n이모님이 의사의 이름을 언급하시는 그 순간, 나는 무엇이 올지 알았다. 스러져 가는 동료 인간들 사이에서 겪은 나의 지난 경험 속에서 몇 번이고 거듭, 그 악명 높이 불신앙적인 직업인 의료의 종사자들이 나와 나의 자비의 사명 사이에 끼어들곤 했다—환자에게 안정이 필요하며, 그들이 다른 무엇보다 가장 두려워하는 그 방해가 되는 영향이란 곧 클랙 양과 그녀의 책들의 영향이라는 비참한 핑계로 말이다. 바로 그 똑같은 눈먼 유물론이 (내 등 뒤에서 음험하게 작동하며) 이제 나의 가난이 내세울 수 있는 유일한 소유권을—나의 스러져 가는 이모님에 대한 나의 영적 소유권을—내게서 빼앗으려 들었다.\n\n\"의사가 내게 이르기를,\" 하고 나의 가엾고 그릇 인도된 친척이 말을 이으셨다. \"오늘은 내가 그리 좋지 않다는구나. 그는 내가 낯선 이를 만나는 것을 금하고, 만일 읽는다면, 가장 가볍고 가장 재미난 책만 읽으라고 이르는구나. '아무것도 하지 마십시오, 레이디 버린더, 머리를 지치게 하거나, 맥박을 빠르게 할 만한 것은요'—그것이, 드루실라, 그가 오늘 나를 떠날 때 남긴 마지막 말이었어.\"\n\n다시 물러서는 수밖에 없었다—전처럼, 그저 그때만이다. 의료인의 봉직에 견주어, 나의 봉직 같은 것이 한없이 더 중대하다고 드러내 놓고 주장하는 것은, 그저 그 의사를 부추겨 제 환자의 인간적 나약함을 이용하게 하고, 그 사건을 내던지겠다고 으름장 놓게 할 뿐이었으리라. 다행히도, 좋은 씨앗을 뿌리는 데는 한 가지 이상의 길이 있고, 그 길들에 나보다 더 밝은 사람은 드물다.\n\n\"한두 시간 뒤면 기운이 나실지도 몰라요, 이모님.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"아니면 내일 아침, 무언가 부족한 느낌으로 깨어나실지도 모르고요, 그때는 이 수수한 책 한 권이 그것을 채워 드릴 수 있을지도 몰라요. 책을 두고 가게 해 주실 거죠, 이모님? 의사도 그것만은 좀처럼 반대하지 못할 거예요!\"\n\n나는 그것을, 반은 안으로 반은 밖으로, 이모님의 손수건과 냄새 맡는 약병 바로 곁, 소파 방석 밑에 슬며시 밀어 넣었다. 이모님 손이 그 둘 중 하나를 찾을 때마다, 그것은 그 책에 닿을 터였다. 그리고, 이르든 늦든 (누가 알랴?) 그 책이 이모님께 닿을지도 몰랐다. 이렇게 마련해 놓고, 나는 물러가는 것이 슬기롭다고 여겼다. \"쉬시게 두고 가겠어요, 사랑하는 이모님. 내일 다시 들르겠어요.\" 나는 그 말을 하며 우연히 창 쪽을 바라보았다. 그것은 상자와 화분에 담긴 꽃들로 가득했다. 레이디 버린더는 이 스러지기 쉬운 보물들을 유난히 좋아하셔서, 이따금 일어나, 그것들을 보고 냄새 맡으러 가는 버릇이 있으셨다. 새로운 생각 하나가 내 마음을 스쳤다. \"오! 꽃 한 송이 가져가도 될까요?\" 하고 나는 말했다—그리고 그런 식으로, 의심받지 않고 창가로 갔다. 꽃 한 송이를 가져가는 대신, 나는 하나를 보탰는데, 내 가방에서 꺼낸 또 다른 책이라는 모양으로, 이모님을 놀래 드리려고, 그것을 제라늄과 장미 사이에 남겨 두었다. 이런 기특한 생각이 뒤따랐다, \"어째서 이모님이, 가엾은 이가, 들어가는 다른 모든 방에서도 똑같이 해 드리지 않으랴?\" 나는 즉시 작별을 고했다. 그리고, 현관을 가로질러, 서재로 슬며시 들어갔다. 나를 내보내 주려고 올라온 새뮤얼은, 내가 갔다고 여기고, 다시 아래층으로 내려갔다. 서재 탁자 위에서 나는 그 불신앙적인 의사가 권한 '재미난 책' 두 권을 알아챘다. 나는 즉시 그것들을 나 자신의 소중한 간행물 두 권으로 보이지 않게 덮었다. 아침 식사방에서 나는 이모님이 가장 아끼는 카나리아가 새장 안에서 노래하는 것을 발견했다. 이모님은 언제나 그 새에게 손수 모이를 주는 버릇이 있으셨다. 새장 바로 아래 놓인 탁자 위에 개쑥갓이 뿌려져 있었다. 나는 그 개쑥갓 사이에 책 한 권을 놓았다. 응접실에서 나는 내 가방을 비울 더 기쁜 기회들을 발견했다. 이모님이 가장 아끼는 악보들이 피아노 위에 있었다. 나는 그 악보 사이에 책 두 권을 더 슬며시 끼워 넣었다. 나는 또 한 권을, 레이디 버린더의 솜씨임을 내가 아는 어떤 끝나지 않은 자수 밑, 뒤편 응접실에 두었다. 뒤편 응접실에서 셋째 방이 이어졌는데, 그것은 문 대신 커튼으로 가려져 있었다. 이모님의 소박한 구식 부채가 벽난로 선반 위에 있었다. 나는 나의 아홉 번째 책을 아주 특별한 대목에 펼쳐, 그 자리를 표시해 두려고, 그 부채를 책갈피로 끼워 넣었다. 그다음 물음이 왔으니, 내가 더 높이 올라가, 침실 층까지 시험해 볼 것인가—만일 그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 마침 이 집의 위층에 있다가 나를 발견한다면, 의심할 것 없이, 모욕당할 위험을 무릅쓰고 말이다. 허나 오, 그것이 무슨 대수랴? 모욕당하는 것을 두려워한다면 가엾은 기독교인이다. 나는 무엇이든 견딜 채비를 하고 위층으로 올라갔다. 온통 고요하고 인적이 없었다—하인들의 차 마시는 시간이었으리라. 이모님의 방이 앞쪽에 있었다. 내 돌아가신 사랑하는 외숙부 존 경의 소형 초상화가 침대 맞은편 벽에 걸려 있었다. 그것은 내게 미소 짓는 듯했다. 그것은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다, \"드루실라! 책을 하나 두렴.\" 이모님 침대 양편에 탁자가 있었다. 이모님은 잠을 잘 못 이루셔서, 밤에 이것저것 필요로 하시거나, 필요로 한다고 여기셨다. 나는 한쪽에는 성냥 곁에 책을 하나, 다른 쪽에는 초콜릿 방울 상자 밑에 책을 하나 두었다. 이모님이 불을 원하시든, 방울을 원하시든, 이모님 눈에 들거나, 이모님 손에 닿을, 그리고 어느 경우에든 말없는 웅변으로 \"자, 저를 시험해 보세요! 저를 시험해 보세요!\" 하고 말할 소중한 간행물이 있었다. 허나 이제 내 가방 밑바닥에는 책 한 권만이 남았고, 아직 답사되지 않은 방은 하나뿐이었다—침실에서 이어지는 그 욕실 말이다. 나는 안을 엿보았다. 그리고 결코 속이지 않는 그 거룩한 내면의 목소리가 내게 속삭였다, \"너는 다른 모든 곳에서 이모님을 만났다, 드루실라. 욕실에서 이모님을 만나면, 그 일이 다 이루어진다.\" 나는 의자에 걸쳐진 실내복을 보았다. 그것에는 주머니가 하나 있었고, 그 주머니에 나는 나의 마지막 책을 넣었다. 내가, 그들 가운데 누구에게도 의심받지 않고, 그 집을 슬며시 빠져나와, 빈 가방을 팔에 끼고 거리에 서 있는 나 자신을 발견했을 때의, 소임을 다했다는 그 더없는 느낌을 어찌 말로 표현할 수 있으랴? 오, 방탕의 죄스러운 미로를 지나, 쾌락이라는 환영을 좇는 나의 세속적 벗들이여, 그저 착하게만 굴려 한다면, 행복해지기가 얼마나 쉬운가!\n\n그날 밤 내 옷들을 갤 때—내가 나의 부유한 이모님 집 위에서 아래까지 그토록 아낌없는 손으로 흩뿌려 놓은 그 참된 재물을 되새길 때—나는 선언하거니와 마치 다시 어린아이가 된 듯 온갖 근심에서 벗어난 느낌이었다. 나는 어찌나 마음이 가볍던지 '저녁 찬송'의 한 절을 불렀다. 나는 어찌나 마음이 가볍던지 또 한 절을 부르기도 전에 잠이 들었다. 아주 다시 어린아이가 된 듯! 아주 다시 어린아이가 된 듯!\n\n그렇게 나는 그 더없이 행복한 밤을 보냈다. 이튿날 아침 일어날 때, 나는 얼마나 젊은 느낌이었던지! 내가 나 자신의 스러지기 쉬운 몸의 일들에 마음을 쏟을 수 있는 사람이라면, 내가 얼마나 젊어 보였던지도 덧붙일 수 있으련만. 허나 나는 그럴 수 있는 사람이 못 된다—그리고 나는 아무것도 덧붙이지 않는다.\n\n점심 시각 무렵—몸의 안락을 위해서가 아니라, 사랑하는 이모님을 확실히 뵙기 위해—나는 몬터규 스퀘어로 가려고 보닛을 썼다. 막 채비가 되었을 때, 그 무렵 내가 살던 셋집의 하녀가 문 안을 들여다보며 말했다, \"레이디 버린더의 하인이, 클랙 양을 뵈러 왔습니다.\"\n\n나는 그 무렵 런던 거주 시절에, 응접실 층을 쓰고 있었다. 앞쪽 응접실이 나의 거실이었다. 아주 작고, 천장이 아주 낮고, 세간이 아주 초라한—허나, 오, 어찌나 말끔한지! 나는 레이디 버린더의 하인 가운데 누가 나를 찾는지 보려고 통로를 내다보았다. 그것은 그 젊은 남자 하인 새뮤얼이었다—배우려는 낯빛과 아주 싹싹한 태도를 지닌, 얌전하고 혈색 좋은 사람이었다. 나는 언제나 새뮤얼에게 영적 관심을 느껴 왔고, 몇 마디 진지한 말로 그를 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 있었다. 이번에는, 나는 그를 내 거실로 청해 들였다.\n\n그는 큰 꾸러미를 팔에 끼고 들어왔다. 그가 그 꾸러미를 내려놓을 때, 그것이 그를 겁먹게 하는 듯했다. \"마님께서 안부 전하십니다, 아가씨. 그리고 안에 편지가 있다고 말씀드리라 하셨습니다.\" 그 전언을 건네고 나서, 그 혈색 좋은 젊은 하인은 마치 달아나고 싶은 듯한 낯빛을 지어 나를 놀라게 했다.\n\n나는 몇 마디 다정한 질문을 하려고 그를 붙들었다. 몬터규 스퀘어에 들르면 이모님을 뵐 수 있을까? 아니, 이모님은 마차 산책을 나가셨다. 레이철 아가씨도 함께 가셨고, 에이블화이트 씨도 마차에 자리를 잡으셨다. 사랑하는 고드프리 씨의 자선 일이 얼마나 서글프게 밀려 있는지 아는 터라, 나는 그가 한가한 사내처럼 마차 산책을 나간다는 것이 이상하다고 여겼다. 나는 문가에서 새뮤얼을 멈춰 세우고, 몇 마디 더 다정한 질문을 했다. 레이철 아가씨는 그날 밤 무도회에 가실 참이었고, 에이블화이트 씨는 커피를 마시러 와서 그 아이와 함께 가기로 약속되어 있었다. 내일 오전 음악회가 광고되어 있었고, 새뮤얼은 에이블화이트 씨의 자리를 포함해 여럿의 자리를 잡으라는 분부를 받았다. \"표가 죄다 나가 버릴지도 모릅니다, 아가씨,\" 하고 이 순진한 젊은이가 말했다. \"제가 얼른 달려가 당장 그것들을 구하지 않으면요!\" 그는 그 말을 하며 달려갔다—그리고 나는, 나를 사로잡을 몇 가지 근심스러운 생각과 함께, 다시 혼자가 되었다.\n\n그날 밤 우리는 고드프리 씨의 조언과 도움을 얻으려는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 특별히 소집된, '어머니회 아동복 개조 협회'의 특별 모임이 있었다. 우리의 작은 공동체를 아주 짓눌러 버리는 그 압도적인 바지의 물결 아래 우리 자매회를 떠받치는 대신, 그는 몬터규 스퀘어에서 커피를 마시고, 그다음에 무도회에 가기로 약속해 놓은 것이었다! 이튿날 오후는 '영국 부인회 하인 주일 애인 감독 협회'의 축제일로 잡혀 있었다. 그 고전하는 기관의 생명이자 영혼으로 참석하는 대신, 그는 오전 음악회에서 세속적인 자들의 무리 가운데 하나가 되기로 약조한 것이었다! 나는 스스로에게 물었다, 이것이 무슨 뜻인가? 아아! 그것은 우리의 기독교적 영웅이 새로운 됨됨이로 내게 저를 드러내리라는 뜻이었고, 내 마음속에서 현대의 가장 끔찍한 타락 가운데 하나와 엮이게 되리라는 뜻이었다.\n\n허나, 지나가는 그날의 이야기로 돌아가자. 내 방에 혼자 남은 나 자신을 발견하고, 나는 자연히 그 혈색 좋은 젊은 하인을 그토록 이상하게 겁먹게 한 듯한 그 꾸러미에 관심을 돌렸다. 이모님이 내게 약속하신 유산을 보내신 걸까? 그리고 그것이 헌 옷가지나, 닳은 은수저나, 유행 지난 장신구나, 그런 종류의 것이라는 모양을 취한 걸까? 무엇이든 받아들이고, 무엇에도 발끈하지 않을 채비를 하고, 나는 그 꾸러미를 열었다—그러자 무엇이 내 눈에 들어왔던가? 내가 그 전날 온 집 안에 흩뿌려 놓았던 그 열두 권의 소중한 간행물이, 의사의 분부로 죄다 내게 되돌려진 것이었다! 젊은 새뮤얼이 제 꾸러미를 내 방으로 들여올 때 움츠러들 만도 했구나! 그 비참한 심부름을 마치고 나서 그가 달아날 만도 했구나! 이모님의 편지로 말하자면, 그것은 그저, 가엾은 이가, 이런 것에 이르렀다—당신이 감히 당신 의료인의 말을 거스를 수 없다는 것이었다.\n\n이제 어찌해야 했던가? 나의 훈련과 나의 원칙으로 보아, 나는 한순간의 망설임도 없었다.\n\n한번 양심에 의해 스스로 지탱되고, 한번 명백한 쓸모의 길에 나서면, 참된 기독교인은 결코 굴하지 않는다. 우리가 한번 우리의 사명을 얻으면, 공적 영향도 사적 영향도 우리에게 조금의 효험도 내지 못한다. 과세가 사명의 결과일 수 있고, 폭동이 사명의 결과일 수 있고, 전쟁이 사명의 결과일 수 있다. 우리는, 우리 바깥의 세상을 움직이는 온갖 인간적 헤아림과 상관없이, 우리의 일을 이어 간다. 우리는 이치를 넘어서 있고, 우리는 조롱을 넘어서 있다. 우리는 그 누구의 눈으로도 보지 않고, 그 누구의 귀로도 듣지 않고, 그 누구의 마음으로도 느끼지 않는다, 우리 자신의 것 말고는. 영광스럽고, 영광스러운 특권이여! 그리고 그것은 어떻게 얻어지는가? 아, 나의 벗들이여, 그 부질없는 물음은 삼가도 좋다! 그것을 얻을 수 있는 것은 오직 우리뿐이다—우리야말로 언제나 옳은 유일한 사람들이니까.\n\n나의 그릇 인도된 이모님의 경우에, 경건한 끈기가 다음으로 취할 모양이 넉넉히 뻔하게 내게 드러났다.\n\n성직자 벗들에 의한 준비는, 레이디 버린더 자신의 꺼림 탓에, 실패했다. 책들에 의한 준비는, 의사의 불신앙적인 고집 탓에, 실패했다. 그렇다면 좋다! 다음으로 시험해 볼 것은 무엇이었나? 다음으로 시험해 볼 것은—작은 쪽지들에 의한 준비였다. 다시 말해, 책 자체는 되돌려졌으니, 그 책들에서 골라낸 발췌문을, 서로 다른 손으로 베껴, 죄다 이모님께 보내는 편지로 부친 뒤, 그중 얼마쯤은 우편으로 보내고, 얼마쯤은 내가 그 전날 취한 방침대로 온 집 안에 뿌리는 것이었다. 편지로서 그것들은 아무 의심도 일으키지 않을 터였다. 편지로서 그것들은 뜯길 터였다—그리고, 한번 뜯기면, 읽힐지도 몰랐다. 그중 얼마쯤은 내가 직접 썼다. \"사랑하는 이모님, 몇 줄에 이모님의 관심을 청해도 될까요?\" 어쩌고. \"사랑하는 이모님, 저는 어젯밤 책을 읽다가, 마침 다음 대목을 만났어요,\" 어쩌고. 다른 편지들은 나의 소중한 동료들, '어머니회 아동복'의 자매들이 나를 대신해 써 주었다. \"친애하는 부인, 참되지만 미천한 벗이 부인께 두는 관심을 용서하소서.\" \"친애하는 부인, 진지한 사람이 몇 마디 기운 나는 말씀을 드려 부인을 놀래도 될까요?\" 이러한, 그리고 그 밖의 비슷한 정중한 호소의 양식을 써서, 우리는 나의 그 온갖 소중한 대목들을, 의사의 그 방심 없는 유물론조차 의심할 수 없는 모양으로 다시 들여왔다. 저녁 어스름이 우리 둘레에 닫히기 전에, 나는 이모님을 위해 열두 권의 일깨우는 책 대신, 열두 통의 일깨우는 편지를 마련했다. 여섯 통은 즉시 우편으로 보내도록 마련하고, 여섯 통은 이튿날 집에서 직접 뿌리려고 내 주머니에 간직했다.\n\n두 시 조금 지나 나는 다시, 레이디 버린더의 문에서 새뮤얼에게 더 다정한 질문을 건네며, 경건한 싸움의 마당에 나섰다.\n\n이모님은 밤을 나쁘게 지내셨다. 이모님은 다시, 내가 당신 유언장을 증언했던 그 방에서, 소파에 기대어, 잠을 좀 청해 보려 하고 계셨다.\n\n나는 이모님을 뵐 요행을 노리고, 서재에서 기다리겠다고 말했다. 편지를 뿌리려는 열의의 뜨거움 속에서, 나는 레이철에 대해 물어볼 생각을 도무지 하지 못했다. 집은 조용했고, 음악 공연이 시작되는 시각이 지나 있었다. 나는 그 아이와 그 아이의 쾌락 좇는 무리가 (아아! 고드프리 씨도 포함하여) 죄다 음악회에 있으려니 여기고, 시간과 기회가 아직 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 동안, 나의 좋은 일에 열심히 매달렸다.\n\n그날 아침의 이모님 앞으로 온 편지들이—내가 간밤에 부친 그 여섯 통의 일깨우는 편지를 포함하여—서재 탁자 위에 뜯기지 않은 채 놓여 있었다. 이모님은 뻔히 많은 편지 더미를 감당할 만한 기운이 없다고 느끼신 듯했다—그리고 그날 늦게 서재에 들어오시면 그 수에 기가 꺾이실지도 몰랐다. 나는 나의 두 번째 여섯 통 가운데 하나를 벽난로 선반 위에 따로 놓았다. 나머지와 떨어진 그 외로운 자리로써, 이모님의 호기심을 끌도록 그것을 남겨 두면서 말이다. 두 번째 편지는 일부러 아침 식사방 바닥에 놓았다. 나 다음으로 들어가는 첫 하인이 이모님이 그것을 떨어뜨렸다고 여기고, 그것을 이모님께 도로 갖다드리려고 각별히 조심할 터였다. 이렇게 지하층에 씨를 뿌리고, 나는 다음으로 응접실 층에 나의 자비를 흩뿌리려고 가볍게 위층으로 달려 올라갔다.\n\n막 앞쪽 방에 들어서는데, 현관문을 두 번 두드리는 소리가 들렸다—부드럽고, 파닥거리는 듯한, 조심스러운 조그만 두드림이었다. 내가 (내가 기다리고 있으리라 여겨지는) 서재로 슬며시 돌아갈 생각을 하기도 전에, 그 재빠른 젊은 하인이 현관에서 문을 열고 있었다. 그건 대수롭지 않다고, 나는 생각했다. 이모님의 건강 상태로 보아, 방문객은 대체로 들이지 않았으니까. 나의 두려움과 놀라움에도, 그 부드러운 조그만 두드림을 한 자가 여느 규칙의 예외로 드러났다. 내 아래쪽에서 새뮤얼의 목소리가 (내가 듣지 못한 어떤 질문에 답한 뒤인 듯) 틀림없이 말했다, \"위층으로 올라가시지요, 나리.\" 다음 순간 나는 발소리를—사내의 발소리를—응접실 층으로 다가오는 것을 들었다. 이 총애받는 남자 방문객이 대체 누구일 수 있었나? 내가 스스로에게 그 물음을 던지자마자, 거의 그와 동시에, 대답이 떠올랐다. 그 의사 말고 누구일 수 있었으랴?\n\n다른 어느 방문객의 경우였다면, 나는 나 자신이 응접실에서 발견되도록 놓아두었으리라. 내가 서재에 싫증이 나, 기분 전환으로 위층에 올라왔다는 데는 예사롭지 않을 것이 아무것도 없었을 테니까. 허나 나 자신의 자존심이, 내게 내 책들을 되돌려 보냄으로써 나를 모욕한 그 사람을 만나는 것을 가로막았다. 나는, 뒤편 응접실과 통한다고 언급한 그 조그만 셋째 방으로 슬며시 들어가, 그 열린 문간을 가리는 커튼을 늘어뜨렸다. 내가 거기서 일이 분만 기다린다면, 그런 경우의 여느 결과가 벌어질 터였다. 다시 말해, 그 의사는 제 환자의 방으로 안내될 터였다.\n\n나는 일이 분, 그리고 일이 분 이상을 기다렸다. 나는 그 방문객이 안절부절 앞뒤로 걷는 소리를 들었다. 나는 또한 그가 혼잣말하는 소리도 들었다. 나는 심지어 그 목소리를 알아보는 것도 같았다. 내가 착각한 걸까? 그것은 의사가 아니라, 다른 누군가였나? 이를테면, 브러프 씨? 아니! 어긋남 없는 본능이 내게 그것이 브러프 씨가 아니라고 일러 주었다. 그가 누구든, 그는 여전히 혼잣말하고 있었다. 나는 그 무거운 커튼을 세상에서 가장 조그만 부스러기만큼 벌리고, 귀 기울였다.\n\n내가 들은 말은, \"오늘 그것을 하겠어!\"였다. 그리고 그 말을 한 목소리는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 것이었다.\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n내 손이 커튼에서 떨어졌다. 허나 여기지 마시라—오, 여기지 마시라—내 처지의 그 끔찍한 곤혹스러움이 내 마음속의 가장 앞선 생각이었다고는! 고드프리 씨에게 느끼는 자매다운 관심이 여전히 어찌나 뜨겁던지, 나는 그가 어째서 음악회에 있지 않은지 스스로에게 물어볼 겨를도 없었다. 아니! 나는 오직 그 말들만을—그의 입술에서 방금 떨어진 그 놀라운 말들만을 생각했다. 그는 오늘 그것을 하겠다고 했다. 그는, 끔찍한 결의의 어조로, 오늘 그것을 하겠다고 말했다. 무엇을, 오 무엇을, 그는 하려는 걸까? 그가 이미 한 것보다도 더 개탄스럽게 그답지 못한 무언가를? 그가 신앙을 저버리려는 걸까? 그가 '어머니회 아동복'에서 우리를 저버리려는 걸까? 우리는 위원회 방에서 그의 천사 같은 미소를 마지막으로 본 걸까? 우리는 엑서터 홀에서 그의 견줄 데 없는 웅변을 마지막으로 들은 걸까? 나는 그런 사람과 관련하여 이런 끔찍한 사태를 떠올리는 것만으로도 어찌나 격해졌던지, 나는 내 숨은 자리에서 뛰쳐나가, 런던의 모든 부인 위원회의 이름으로 그에게 해명하기를 애원했으리라—그때 나는 별안간 그 방에서 또 다른 목소리를 들었다. 그것은 커튼을 뚫고 들어왔다. 그것은 크고, 대담하고, 온갖 여자다운 매력이 없었다. 레이철 버린더의 목소리였다.\n\n\"어째서 여기 올라온 거예요, 고드프리?\" 하고 그 아이가 물었다. \"어째서 서재로 들어가지 않고요?\"\n\n그는 부드럽게 웃고, 대답했다. \"클랙 양이 서재에 있어요.\"\n\n\"클랙이 서재에요!\" 그 아이는 대뜸 뒤편 응접실의 등받이 없는 긴 의자에 앉았다. \"당신 말이 아주 옳아요, 고드프리. 우리는 여기 머무는 편이 훨씬 낫겠어요.\"\n\n나는, 조금 전만 해도, 타는 듯한 열병에 시달리며, 다음에 무엇을 할지 얼마쯤 갈피를 못 잡고 있었다. 나는 이제 몹시 싸늘해졌고, 조금의 갈피도 못 잡을 것이 없었다. 내가 들은 것을 들은 뒤에, 내 모습을 드러내는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 물러나는 것은—벽난로 속으로가 아니고서는—똑같이 생각할 수 없었다. 순교가 내 앞에 놓여 있었다. 나 자신을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 나는 볼 수도 들을 수도 있도록 소리 없이 커튼을 매만졌다. 그리고 나서 나는, 초기 기독교인의 정신으로, 나의 순교를 맞았다.\n\n\"등받이 없는 의자에 앉지 마세요.\" 하고 그 젊은 아가씨가 이어 갔다. \"의자를 가져와요, 고드프리. 저는 사람들과 이야기할 때 그들이 제 맞은편에 있는 게 좋아요.\"\n\n그는 가장 가까운 자리를 잡았다. 그것은 낮은 의자였다. 그는 아주 키가 컸고, 그것에 비해 몇 치수나 너무 컸다. 나는 여태껏 그의 다리가 그토록 볼썽사나운 것을 본 적이 없었다.\n\n\"자?\" 하고 그 아이가 이어 갔다. \"그들에게 뭐라고 말했어요?\"\n\n\"당신이 나에게 말한 그대로예요, 사랑하는 레이철.\"\n\n\"엄마가 오늘 도무지 좋지 않다고요? 그리고 제가 그런 엄마를 두고 음악회에 가는 게 썩 내키지 않는다고요?\"\n\n\"그 말 그대로였어요. 그들은 음악회에서 당신을 잃는 것을 서글퍼했지만, 아주 이해했어요. 다들 안부를 전했고요. 그리고 다들 레이디 버린더의 병환이 곧 나으리라는 기운 나는 믿음을 내비쳤어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 그게 심각하다고 여기진 않죠, 그렇죠, 고드프리?\"\n\n\"천만에요! 며칠이면, 저는 아주 확신하는데, 죄다 다시 괜찮아질 거예요.\"\n\n\"저도 그렇게 여겨요. 처음엔 조금 겁이 났지만, 저도 그렇게 여겨요. 당신에게 거의 낯선 사람들에게 저를 대신해 변명해 주러 간 건 아주 친절한 일이었어요. 그런데 어째서 그들과 함께 음악회에 가지 않았어요? 당신까지 음악을 놓치다니 아주 안됐어요.\"\n\n\"그런 말 말아요, 레이철! 제가 얼마나 더 행복한지 당신이 알기만 한다면요—여기, 당신과 함께!\"\n\n그는 두 손을 맞잡고, 그 아이를 바라보았다. 그가 그리할 때 그가 있던 자세로, 그는 내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다. 엑서터 홀 연단에서 그가 헐벗은 수백만 동료 인간들을 위해 호소할 때 나를 매혹했던 바로 그 애처로운 표정을 그의 얼굴에서 알아챘을 때 내가 얼마나 역겨웠는지 어찌 말로 묘사할 수 있으랴!\n\n\"나쁜 버릇을 고치기란 어렵지요, 고드프리. 하지만 부디 그 아첨하는 버릇은 고쳐 봐요—부디, 저를 기쁘게 해 주려면요.\"\n\n\"저는 여태껏 당신에게, 레이철, 제 평생 아첨을 한 적이 없어요. 이루어진 사랑은 때로 아첨의 말을 쓸 수 있음을, 저도 인정해요. 하지만 가망 없는 사랑은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 언제나 진실을 말하지요.\"\n\n그는 \"가망 없는 사랑\"이라고 말할 때, 제 의자를 바싹 당기고, 그 아이의 손을 잡았다. 한순간의 침묵이 있었다. 모두를 전율케 하는 그가, 의심할 것 없이 그 아이도 전율케 했던 것이다. 나는 이제야 그가 응접실에서 혼자 있을 때 그에게서 떨어진 그 말, \"오늘 그것을 하겠어\"를 이해한 듯싶었다. 아아! 더없이 엄격한 예절인들, 그가 지금 그것을 하고 있음을 좀처럼 알아채지 못할 리 없었으리라.\n\n\"우리가 합의한 것을 잊었어요, 고드프리, 당신이 시골에서 저에게 말했을 때요? 우리는 사촌일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니기로 합의했잖아요.\"\n\n\"저는 그 합의를 깨요, 레이철, 당신을 볼 때마다요.\"\n\n\"그럼 저를 보지 마세요.\"\n\n\"아주 부질없어요! 저는 그 합의를 깨요, 당신을 생각할 때마다요. 오, 레이철! 바로 며칠 전에, 당신은 저에 대한 당신의 평가에서 제 자리가 여태껏 있었던 것보다 더 높은 자리라고 얼마나 다정하게 말해 주었던가요! 제가 그 사랑스러운 말에 이런 희망을 걸다니 미친 걸까요? 언젠가 당신의 마음이 저에게 누그러질 어느 앞날을 꿈꾸다니 미친 걸까요? 만일 그렇다면, 그렇다고 저에게 말하지 말아요! 저에게 저의 망상을 남겨 주세요, 더없이 사랑하는 이여! 저에게 다른 아무것도 없다면, 저는 그것만은 소중히 간직하고, 저를 위로할 그것만은 가져야 해요!\"\n\n그의 목소리가 떨렸고, 그는 제 흰 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다. 또다시 엑서터 홀이었다! 그 대비를 완성하는 데에 청중과, 갈채와, 물 한 잔밖에 빠진 것이 없었다.\n\n그 아이의 완고한 성정조차 감동했다. 나는 그 아이가 그에게 조금 더 가까이 기대는 것을 보았다. 나는 그 아이의 다음 말에서 새로운 관심의 어조를 들었다.\n\n\"정말 확실해요, 고드프리, 당신이 그토록 저를 아낀다는 게?\"\n\n\"확실하고말고요! 당신은 제가 어떤 사람이었는지 알지요, 레이철. 제가 어떤 사람인지 당신에게 말하게 해 주세요. 저는 삶의 온갖 흥미를 잃었어요, 당신에 대한 제 관심만 빼고요. 저 자신도 설명할 수 없는 변모가 저에게 일어났어요. 믿어지겠어요? 제 자선 일이 저에게 견딜 수 없는 성가심이 되었어요. 그리고 이제 부인 위원회를 보면, 저는 제가 땅끝에 가 있기를 바라요!\"\n\n만일 배교(背敎)의 기록에 그런 선언에 견줄 만한 것이 조금이라도 있다면, 나는 다만 그 사례가 나의 독서의 창고에서 내놓을 수 없는 것이라고밖에 말할 수 없다. 나는 '어머니회 아동복'을 떠올렸다. 나는 '주일 애인 감독'을 떠올렸다. 나는, 일일이 언급하기에 너무 많은, 죄다 이 사내를 힘의 탑으로 삼아 세워진 그 밖의 협회들을 떠올렸다. 나는, 이를테면 고드프리 씨의 콧구멍을 통해 제 사업적 생명의 숨을 들이쉬던 그 고전하는 부인 이사회들을—우리의 좋은 일을 방금 \"성가심\"이라 헐뜯고—우리와 함께 있는 저 자신을 발견하면 땅끝에 가 있기를 바란다고 방금 선언한 바로 그 고드프리 씨를 떠올렸다! 나의 젊은 여자 벗들은, 내가 나 자신의 정의로운 분노를 말없이 삼켜 낼 수 있기까지 그것이 나의 수양조차 시험했다고 말하면, 견뎌 나갈 용기를 얻으리라. 동시에, 나 자신을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 내가 그 대화의 한 음절도 놓치지 않았음을 덧붙여 둘 뿐이다. 다음으로 말한 것은 레이철이었다.\n\n\"당신은 고백을 했어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"제가 제 고백을 하면, 그게 저에 대한 당신의 그 불행한 애착을 낫게 할지 궁금하네요.\"\n\n그는 흠칫했다. 나도 흠칫했음을 고백한다. 그도, 나도, 그 아이가 월장석의 수수께끼를 털어놓으려 한다고 생각했다.\n\n\"저를 보고,\" 하고 그 아이가 이어 갔다. \"제가 살아 있는 가장 비참한 아이라고 여기겠어요? 정말이에요, 고드프리. 제 자신의 평가에서 격이 떨어져 사는 것보다 더 큰 비참함이 어디 있겠어요? 그게 지금 제 삶이에요.\"\n\n\"사랑하는 레이철! 당신이 자신에 대해 그런 식으로 말할 무슨 이유가 있을 리 없어요!\"\n\n\"제게 이유가 없다는 걸 당신이 어떻게 알아요?\"\n\n\"어떻게 저에게 그런 물음을 던질 수 있어요! 저는 알아요, 저는 당신을 아니까요. 당신의 침묵은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 당신의 참된 벗들의 평가에서 결코 당신을 낮춘 적이 없어요. 당신의 소중한 생일 선물이 사라진 것은 이상해 보일지 모르지요. 그 사건과 당신의 설명되지 않은 관련은 더 이상해 보일지 모르고요——\"\n\n\"당신 지금 월장석 이야기를 하는 거예요, 고드프리?\"\n\n\"저는 분명 당신이 이르는 것이——\"\n\n\"저는 그런 걸 이른 게 아니에요. 저는 누가 그것을 이야기하든, 월장석의 분실에 대해 들어도, 제 자신의 평가에서 격이 떨어지는 느낌 없이 들을 수 있어요. 만일 그 다이아몬드의 사연이 언젠가 드러난다면, 제가 끔찍한 책임을 떠맡았음이 알려질 거예요. 제가 비참한 비밀을 지키는 일에 저를 얽어 넣었음이 알려질 거예요—하지만 제가 비열한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았음이 한낮의 태양처럼 환히 드러날 거예요! 당신은 저를 오해했어요, 고드프리. 더 분명하게 말하지 않은 제 잘못이에요. 무슨 대가를 치르든, 저는 이제 더 분명하게 말하겠어요. 당신이 저를 사랑하지 않는다고 해 봐요. 당신이 어떤 다른 여자를 사랑한다고 해 봐요.\"\n\n\"네?\"\n\n\"당신이 그 여자가 도무지 당신에게 어울리지 않는다는 걸 알아냈다고 해 봐요. 그 여자에게 또 한 번 생각을 허비하는 것이 당신에게 수치라고 아주 확신했다고 해 봐요. 그런 사람과 언젠가 혼인한다는 생각만으로도, 그저 그것을 생각하는 것만으로도 당신의 얼굴이 화끈거렸다고 해 봐요.\"\n\n\"네?\"\n\n\"그리고, 그 모든 것에도 불구하고—당신이 그 여자를 당신의 마음에서 떼어 낼 수 없었다고 해 봐요. 그 여자가 (당신이 그 여자를 믿던 시절에) 당신 안에 불러일으킨 감정이 감출 수 있는 감정이 아니었다고 해 봐요. 이 몹쓸 여자가 당신 안에 불러일으킨 사랑이——? 오, 그것을 말할 무슨 말을 제가 어찌 찾겠어요! 저 자신에게 소름 끼치는 감정이 동시에 저를 매혹하는 감정일 수 있다는 것을 어찌 사내에게 이해시킬 수 있겠어요? 그건 제 삶의 숨결이에요, 고드프리, 그리고 그건 저를 죽이는 독이에요—둘이 하나로! 가세요! 지금 제가 말하듯 말하다니 저는 제정신이 아닌 게 틀림없어요. 아니에요! 당신은 저를 떠나면 안 돼요—당신은 잘못된 인상을 안고 가면 안 돼요. 저는 제 자신을 변호하기 위해 할 말은 해야겠어요. 이걸 명심해요! 그이는 몰라요—그이는 결코 알지 못할 거예요, 제가 당신에게 말한 것을요. 저는 결코 그이를 보지 않겠어요—무슨 일이 일어나든 상관없어요—저는 결코, 결코, 결코 그이를 다시 보지 않겠어요! 저에게 그이의 이름을 묻지 마세요! 저에게 더는 묻지 마세요! 화제를 바꿔요. 당신은, 고드프리, 제가 어째서 숨이 막혀 죽을 것 같은 느낌인지 말해 줄 만큼 의사인가요? 눈물 대신 말로 터져 나오는 히스테리의 형태가 있나요? 아마 있겠죠! 그게 무슨 대수예요? 당신은 이제 제가 당신에게 끼친 어떤 괴로움도 넉넉히 쉽게 이겨 낼 거예요. 저는 당신의 평가에서 제 마땅한 자리로 떨어졌죠, 안 그래요? 저를 아랑곳하지 마세요! 저를 가엾이 여기지 마세요! 제발 부탁이에요, 가세요!\"\n\n그 아이는 별안간 돌아서서, 두 손으로 그 긴 의자의 등받이를 사납게 두들겼다. 그 아이의 머리가 방석 위로 떨어졌다. 그리고 그 아이는 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다. 내가 이것에 충격받을 겨를도 없이, 나는 고드프리 씨 편의 전혀 뜻밖의 행동에 소름이 끼쳤다. 그가 그 아이 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다면 믿어지겠는가?—두 무릎을 다, 나는 엄숙히 선언한다! 그가 그다음에 그 아이에게 두 팔을 둘렀음을 정숙함이 언급해도 될까? 그리고 그가 두 마디로 그 아이를 감전시켰음을 마지못한 감탄이 인정해도 될까?\n\n\"고귀한 사람이여!\"\n\n그것뿐이었다! 허나 그는 그를 대중 연설가로서 이름나게 한 그 격정의 폭발 가운데 하나로 그것을 했다. 그 아이는, 아주 넋을 잃었는지, 아주 매혹되었는지—어느 쪽인지 모르겠으나—그의 팔을 마땅히 있어야 할 자리로 도로 밀어내려는 애조차 쓰지 않고 앉아 있었다. 나로 말하자면, 나의 예절 감각이 완전히 어리둥절해졌다. 눈을 감는 것이 나의 첫 의무인지, 아니면 귀를 막는 것이 나의 첫 의무인지 어찌나 괴롭게도 갈피를 못 잡던지, 나는 둘 다 하지 못했다. 내가 여전히 보고 듣기에 알맞은 자리에 커튼을 붙들고 있을 수 있었던 것을, 나는 온전히 억눌린 히스테리 탓으로 돌린다. 억눌린 히스테리에 있어서는, 의사들조차 인정하거니와, 사람이 무언가를 붙들고 있어야 한다.\n\n\"그래요,\" 하고 그가, 그의 복음적인 목소리와 태도의 온갖 매혹으로 말했다. \"당신은 고귀한 사람이에요! 진실을, 진실 그 자체를 위해 말할 수 있는 여자—자기를 사랑하는 정직한 사내를 희생하느니, 차라리 제 자존심을 희생할 여자는—온갖 보물 가운데 가장 값을 매길 수 없는 것이에요. 그런 여자가 혼인할 때, 그 남편이 그 여자의 존경과 존중만 얻어도, 그는 제 온 삶을 고귀하게 할 만큼을 얻는 겁니다. 당신은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 저의 평가에서 당신의 자리에 대해 말했지요. 그 자리가 어떤 자리인지 헤아려 봐요—제가 무릎 꿇고, 당신의 가엾은 상처 입은 마음을 치유하는 일을 저의 몫으로 삼게 해 달라 애원할 때 말이에요. 레이철! 저의 아내가 됨으로써, 저를 영광되게 해 주시겠어요, 저를 축복해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n이때쯤 나는 분명 귀를 막기로 마음먹었으련만, 레이철이, 내가 여태껏 그 아이의 입술에서 떨어지는 것을 들어 본 가운데 가장 분별 있는 말로 그에게 대답함으로써, 내가 귀를 열어 두도록 부추기지 않았더라면 말이다.\n\n\"고드프리!\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"당신 미친 게 틀림없어요!\"\n\n\"저는 여태껏 이보다 더 사리에 맞게 말한 적이 없어요, 더없이 사랑하는 이여—저의 잇속뿐만 아니라, 당신의 잇속으로도요. 잠깐 앞날을 바라봐요. 당신의 행복이, 당신이 자기에게 어떤 감정을 품는지 결코 알지 못했고, 당신이 결코 다시 보지 않기로 결심한 사내에게 희생되어야 하나요? 이 기구한 애착을 잊는 것이 당신 자신에 대한 당신의 의무가 아닌가요? 그리고 당신이 지금 이끄는 삶에서 그 잊음을 찾을 수 있나요? 당신은 그 삶을 시험해 봤고, 벌써 그것에 싫증 내고 있어요. 세상의 그 비참한 잇속보다 더 고귀한 관심들로 당신을 에워싸요. 당신을 사랑하고 존경하는 마음, 그 평화로운 요구와 행복한 소임이 날마다 부드럽게 당신을 사로잡는 가정—레이철, 거기서 찾을 수 있는 그 위안을 시험해 봐요! 저는 당신의 사랑을 청하는 게 아니에요—저는 당신의 애정과 존중으로 만족하겠어요. 나머지는, 당신의 상처만큼 깊은 상처조차 낫게 하는 세월과, 당신 남편의 헌신에, 믿고 맡겨 두어요.\"\n\n그 아이는 벌써 넘어가기 시작했다. 오, 그 아이가 어떤 양육을 받았기에! 오, 그 아이 처지였다면 나는 얼마나 다르게 처신했으랴!\n\n\"저를 유혹하지 마세요, 고드프리.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"저는 이대로도 넉넉히 비참하고 넉넉히 무모해요. 저를 더 비참하고 더 무모해지도록 유혹하지 마세요!\"\n\n\"한 가지 물음이에요, 레이철. 당신은 저에 대해 개인적으로 못마땅한 것이 있나요?\"\n\n\"저요! 저는 언제나 당신을 좋아했어요. 당신이 방금 저에게 한 말 뒤로는, 제가 당신을 존경하고 감탄하기까지 하지 않는다면 저는 정말이지 무딘 사람이겠죠.\"\n\n\"당신은, 사랑하는 레이철, 제 남편을 존경하고 감탄하는 아내를 많이 아나요? 그런데도 그들과 그들의 남편은 아주 잘 지내요. 얼마나 많은 신부가, 저를 그리로 데려가는 사내들의 검사를 견뎌 낼 만한 마음을 지니고 제단으로 가나요? 그런데도 그것이 불행하게 끝나지는 않아요—어떻게든 그 혼인 살림은 꾸역꾸역 굴러가지요. 사실인즉, 여자들은, 저희가 기꺼이 인정하려는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이, 혼인을 피난처로 삼아 시험해요. 그리고, 더욱이, 저희는 혼인이 그에 대한 저희의 믿음을 정당화해 주었음을 알게 되지요. 당신 자신의 경우를 다시 한번 봐요. 당신의 나이에, 당신의 매력으로, 당신이 자신에게 독신의 삶을 선고하는 것이 있을 법한 일인가요? 세상에 대한 제 앎을 믿어요—그보다 덜 있을 법한 일은 없어요. 그건 그저 시간 문제예요. 당신은 몇 해 뒤에, 어떤 다른 사내와 혼인할지도 모르지요. 아니면 당신은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 지금 당신 발치에 있는, 그리고 이 땅 위의 다른 어떤 여자의 사랑보다 당신의 존경과 감탄을 더 값지게 여기는 이 사내와 혼인할지도 모르고요.\"\n\n\"천천히요, 고드프리! 당신은 제가 여태껏 생각해 본 적 없는 무언가를 제 머릿속에 넣고 있어요. 당신은, 저의 다른 온갖 앞날이 제 앞에서 닫혔을 때, 새로운 앞날로 저를 유혹하고 있어요. 다시 말하지만, 저는, 당신이 한마디만 더 하면, 당신 조건대로 당신과 혼인할 만큼 넉넉히 비참하고 넉넉히 절박해요. 그 경고를 받아들이고, 가세요!\"\n\n\"당신이 그러겠다고 말하기 전까지는, 저는 무릎에서 일어서지도 않겠어요!\"\n\n\"제가 그러겠다고 말하면 당신은 뉘우칠 거예요, 그리고 저도 뉘우칠 거예요, 너무 늦었을 때요!\"\n\n\"우리는 둘 다 그날을 축복할 거예요, 사랑하는 이여, 제가 밀어붙이고, 당신이 넘어간 그날을요.\"\n\n\"당신은 말하는 만큼 확신하나요?\"\n\n\"직접 판단해 봐요. 저는 제 자신의 집안에서 본 것을 말하는 거예요. 프리징홀의 우리 가정을 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 봐요. 제 아버지와 어머니가 함께 불행하게 사시나요?\"\n\n\"천만에요—제가 보기에는요.\"\n\n\"제 어머니가 아가씨였을 때, 레이철 (집안에 비밀도 아니지만), 그분은 당신이 사랑하듯 사랑했어요—그분은 자기에게 어울리지 않는 사내에게 마음을 주었지요. 그분은 제 아버지와, 그분을 존경하고, 감탄하며, 하지만 그 이상은 없이 혼인했어요. 당신 자신의 눈이 그 결과를 봤고요. 그 안에 당신과 저에게 아무 용기도 없나요?*\n\n* 베터리지의 이야기, 8장 참조.\n\n\"저를 서두르게 하지 않을 거죠, 고드프리?\"\n\n\"제 시간은 당신의 것이에요.\"\n\n\"제가 줄 수 있는 것보다 더는 청하지 않을 거죠?\"\n\n\"저의 천사여! 저는 다만 당신 자신을 저에게 달라고 청할 뿐이에요.\"\n\n\"저를 가져요!\"\n\n그 두 마디로, 그 아이는 그를 받아들였다!\n\n그는 또 한 번 격정을 터뜨렸다—이번에는 거룩하지 못한 환희의 폭발이었다. 그는 그 아이의 얼굴이 제 얼굴에 닿을 때까지 그 아이를 점점 더 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그리고 나서는—아니! 나는 정말이지 이 충격적인 폭로를 더 이어 갈 마음을 나 자신에게서 도무지 끌어낼 수가 없다. 다만, 나는 그 일이 벌어지기 전에 눈을 감으려 했으나, 딱 한순간 너무 늦었다는 것만 말하겠다. 나는, 보시다시피, 그 아이가 저항하리라 셈했던 것이다. 그 아이는 순순히 따랐다. 나와 같은 성(性)의 올바르게 느끼는 모든 사람에게라면, 여러 권의 책인들 이 이상 말할 수 없으리라.\n\n이런 일에 대한 나의 순진함조차 이제 그 면담의 끝으로 가는 길이 보이기 시작했다. 그들은 이때쯤 서로를 어찌나 철저히 알아버렸던지, 나는 그들이 혼인하러 팔짱을 끼고 함께 걸어 나가는 것을 보게 되리라 넉넉히 예상했다. 허나, 고드프리 씨의 다음 말로 미루어 보건대, 지켜야 할 사소한 격식이 하나 더 있는 듯했다. 그는—이번에는 금지되지 않은 채—그 아이 곁 긴 의자에 앉았다. \"제가 당신의 사랑하는 어머니께 말할까요?\" 하고 그가 물었다. \"아니면 당신이 하겠어요?\"\n\n그 아이는 두 가지 다 마다했다.\n\n\"제 어머니가 나으실 때까지는, 우리 둘 중 누구에게서도 아무것도 듣지 않게 해요. 저는 당분간 그것을 비밀로 하고 싶어요, 고드프리. 이제 가요, 그리고 오늘 저녁에 돌아와요. 우리는 여기 단둘이서 넉넉히 오래 있었어요.\"\n\n그 아이는 일어섰고, 일어서면서, 처음으로 나의 순교가 벌어지고 있는 그 조그만 방 쪽을 바라보았다.\n\n\"누가 저 커튼을 쳐 놨지?\" 하고 그 아이가 외쳤다. \"이 방은 이대로도 넉넉히 답답한데, 그런 식으로 공기를 막다니.\"\n\n그 아이는 커튼으로 다가갔다. 그 아이가 커튼에 손을 얹은 순간—내가 발각되는 것이 아주 피할 수 없어 보이던 순간—층계에서 들려온 그 혈색 좋은 젊은 하인의 목소리가, 별안간 그 아이 편의 그리고 내 편의 어떤 다음 행동도 멈춰 세웠다. 그것은 틀림없이 몹시 놀란 사내의 목소리였다.\n\n\"레이철 아가씨!\" 하고 그가 소리쳐 불렀다. \"어디 계세요, 레이철 아가씨?\"\n\n그 아이는 커튼에서 물러나 뛰어, 문으로 달려갔다.\n\n그 하인이 방 안쪽으로 막 들어섰다. 그의 불그레한 혈색이 죄다 사라져 있었다. 그가 말했다. \"아래층으로 내려오세요, 아가씨! 마님이 기절하셨는데, 저희가 도무지 깨워 드릴 수가 없습니다.\"\n\n한 순간 더 지나 나는 혼자가 되었고, 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않고, 내 차례로 아래층에 내려가도 되었다.\n\n고드프리 씨가 의사를 부르러 서둘러 나가며, 현관에서 나를 지나쳤다. \"들어가서, 그들을 도와요!\" 하고 그가, 그 방을 가리키며 말했다. 나는 레이철이 소파 곁에 무릎 꿇고, 제 어머니의 머리를 제 가슴에 안고 있는 것을 발견했다. 이모님의 얼굴을 한 번 보는 것으로 (내가 아는 것을 아는 터라) 그 끔찍한 진실을 알아채기에 넉넉했다. 나는 의사가 들어올 때까지 내 생각을 내 안에 간직했다. 그가 도착하기까지는 오래지 않았다. 그는 레이철을 방에서 내보내는 것으로 시작했다—그러고 나서 그는 우리 나머지에게 레이디 버린더가 세상을 떠났다고 말했다. 완고한 회의(懷疑)의 증거를 찾는 진지한 사람들은, 그가 나를 바라볼 때 아무런 뉘우침의 기색도 보이지 않았다는 것을 들으면 흥미로워할지 모른다.\n\n한참 뒤 나는 아침 식사방과 서재를 엿보았다. 이모님은 내가 이모님 앞으로 쓴 편지들 가운데 한 통도 뜯지 않은 채 돌아가신 것이었다. 나는 이것에 어찌나 충격받았던지, 이모님이 또한 나에게 나의 조그만 유산을 주지 않은 채 돌아가셨다는 것은, 며칠 뒤에야 비로소 내게 떠올랐다.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n(1.) \"클랙 양은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨께 문안 인사를 올리며, 그에게 자신의 미천한 이야기의 다섯째 장을 보내면서, 이런 정황에서 레이디 버린더의 죽음처럼 그토록 참혹한 사건에 대해서는 자신이 바라는 만큼 상세히 서술할 능력이 도무지 없다고 느낀다는 말씀을 감히 드립니다. 그리하여 클랙 양은 자신의 원고에, 자신이 지닌 소중한 간행물에서 뽑은, 죄다 이 끔찍한 주제와 관련된 넉넉한 발췌문을 붙였습니다. 그리고 부디 그 발췌문이 (클랙 양은 뜨겁게 바랍니다) 자신이 존경하는 친척 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 귀에 나팔의 울림처럼 울려 퍼지기를 빕니다.\"\n\n(2.) \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 클랙 양께 문안 인사를 올리며, 그녀의 이야기 다섯째 장에 대해 감사드리는 바입니다. 그것과 함께 보내진 발췌문을 돌려보내면서, 그는 이런 종류의 문헌에 대해 자신이 품을 수 있는 어떤 개인적 반감도 언급하기를 삼가고, 다만 그 원고에 덧붙이자고 제안된 것들이 그가 뜻하는 목적을 이루는 데에는 필요하지 않다고만 말씀드리겠습니다.\"\n\n(3.) \"클랙 양은 자신의 발췌문이 반송되었음을 확인하는 바입니다. 그녀는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게, 자신이 기독교인이며, 따라서 그가 자신의 비위를 거스르는 것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일임을 다정하게 상기시켜 드립니다. 클랙 양은 여전히 블레이크 씨에게 더없이 깊은 관심을 느끼며, 병환이 그를 눕혀 놓게 될 첫 기회에, 자신의 발췌문을 두 번째로 그에게 쓰시도록 내드릴 것을 스스로 다짐합니다. 그러는 동안 그녀는, 자신의 이야기의 마지막 장들을 시작하기에 앞서, 나중의 발견들이 월장석의 수수께끼에 던진 그 빛을 이용하여, 자신의 미천한 기고를 온전하게 만들어도 되는지 알고 싶습니다.\"\n\n(4.) \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 클랙 양을 실망시켜 유감스럽습니다. 그는 그녀가 이야기를 시작할 때 그녀에게 삼가 드린 지시를 되풀이할 수 있을 뿐입니다. 그녀는, 자신의 일기에 기록된 대로, 사람과 사건에 대한 자기 자신의 개인적 경험에만 국한하기를 청받습니다. 나중의 발견들은 실제 증인의 자격으로 쓸 수 있는 사람들의 붓에 맡겨 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\"\n\n(5.) \"클랙 양은 또 한 통의 편지로 프랭클린 블레이크 씨를 성가시게 해 몹시 유감스럽습니다. 그녀의 발췌문은 반송되었고, 월장석이라는 주제에 대한 그녀의 무르익은 견해를 표하는 것은 금지되었습니다. 클랙 양은 자신이 (세속적 표현으로) 묵살당했다고 느껴야 마땅함을 고통스럽게 자각합니다. 허나, 아니지요—클랙 양은 역경의 학교에서 인내를 배웠습니다. 그녀가 편지를 쓰는 뜻은, 블레이크 씨가 (다른 모든 것을 금하시는 그가) 이 서신 왕복을 클랙 양의 이야기에 싣는 것도 금하시는지를 알기 위함입니다. 블레이크 씨의 간섭이 저술가로서의 그녀를 놓은 그 처지에 대한 얼마쯤의 해명이, 마땅한 공정의 견지에서 필요한 듯싶습니다. 그리고 클랙 양은, 그녀 편에서, 자신의 편지들이 스스로 말하도록 내놓이기를 더없이 간절히 바랍니다.\"\n\n(6.) \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 클랙 양의 제안에 동의하는 바이며, 다만 그녀가 이 동의의 통지를 두 사람 사이의 서신 왕복을 끝맺는 것으로 친절히 여겨 주시리라는 양해하에서입니다.\"\n\n(7.) \"클랙 양은 (서신 왕복이 끝나기 전에) 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게, 그의 마지막 편지가—뻔히 그녀의 비위를 거스르려는 뜻이었던 그 편지가—그 필자의 목적을 이루는 데 성공하지 못했음을 알리는 것이 기독교인의 의무라고 느낍니다. 그녀는 블레이크 씨에게, 자기 방의 조용한 데로 물러가, 이렇듯 가엾고 연약한 한 여자를 모욕이 미치는 데 너머로 높여 줄 수 있는 그 수양이, 그가 지금 그것에 대해 느끼려는 것보다 더 큰 감탄을 받을 만하지 않은지 스스로 헤아려 보시기를 다정하게 청합니다. 그런 뜻의 통지를 받게 되면, 클랙 양은 자신의 발췌문 온전한 한 벌을 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 되돌려 보낼 것을 엄숙히 다짐합니다.\"\n\n[이 편지에 대해서는 아무 답장도 받지 못했다. 논평은 부질없다. (서명) 드루실라 클랙.]\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n앞의 서신 왕복이, 어째서 내게 나의 다섯째 장을 끝맺는 그 사실의 담담한 알림만으로 레이디 버린더의 죽음을 넘길 수밖에 다른 선택지가 없는지를 넉넉히 설명해 줄 것이다.\n\n앞으로 나 자신의 개인적 경험의 테두리 안에 엄격히 머물면서, 나는 다음으로, 이모님이 돌아가신 때로부터 레이철 버린더와 내가 다시 만나기까지 한 달이 흘렀음을 이야기해야겠다. 그 만남은 내가 그 아이와 한 지붕 아래서 며칠을 보내는 계기가 되었다. 그 방문 중에, 그 아이의 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와의 혼약과 관련하여 어떤 일이 일어났는데, 그것은 이 쪽들에서 각별히 다룰 만큼 넉넉히 중요하다. 이 많은 고통스러운 집안 정황 가운데 마지막이 밝혀지고 나면, 나의 소임은 완수될 것이다. 그때는 내가, 사건들의 실제 (그리고 더없이 마지못한) 증인으로서, 내가 아는 모든 것을 이야기한 셈이 될 테니까.\n\n이모님의 유해는 런던에서 옮겨져, 당신 자신의 정원에 딸린 교회의 그 조그만 묘지에 묻혔다. 나는 나머지 식구들과 함께 장례에 초대받았다. 허나 (나의 종교적 견해로 보아) 겨우 며칠 만에 이 죽음이 내게 일으킨 그 충격에서 나 자신을 추스르기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 게다가, 나는 프리징홀의 교구 목사가 그 예배를 집전하기로 되어 있다고 전해 들었다. 지난날 이 성직에서 버림받은 자가 레이디 버린더의 휘스트 탁자에서 노름꾼 가운데 하나가 되어 있는 것을 몸소 본 터라, 나는, 설령 내가 여행할 만한 상태였더라도, 그 의식에 참석하는 것이 정당하다고 느꼈을지 의심스럽다.\n\n레이디 버린더의 죽음은 그 따님을 형부 되는 노(老) 에이블화이트 씨의 보살핌 아래 남겼다. 그는 유언장에 의해, 조카딸이 혼인하거나 성년이 될 때까지, 후견인으로 임명되었다. 이런 정황에서, 고드프리 씨는, 내 짐작으로는, 저가 레이철에 대해 놓인 그 새로운 관계를 제 아버지에게 알렸다. 어쨌든, 이모님이 돌아가신 지 열흘 만에, 그 혼약의 비밀은 집안 테두리 안에서는 도무지 비밀이 아니게 되었고, 노 에이블화이트 씨에게—또 한 사람의 판박이 버림받은 자!—큰 물음은, 어떻게 저 자신과 제 권위를 제 아들과 혼인할 그 부유한 젊은 아가씨에게 더없이 마음에 들게 만드느냐 하는 것이었다.\n\n레이철은 처음부터, 그 아이가 살도록 설득될 만한 곳을 고르는 일로 그에게 얼마간 애를 먹였다. 몬터규 스퀘어의 집은 제 어머니의 죽음이라는 참사와 엮여 있었다. 요크셔의 집은 잃어버린 월장석이라는 추문거리와 엮여 있었다. 프리징홀에 있는 후견인 자신의 저택은 이 두 가지 반론 어느 쪽에도 걸리지 않았다. 허나 최근의 사별 뒤 그곳에 있는 레이철의 존재가, 그 아이의 사촌들인 에이블화이트 아가씨들의 흥겨움에 제동을 걸었다—그리고 그 아이 자신이 제 방문을 더 알맞은 기회로 미루자고 청했다. 그것은 노 에이블화이트 씨에게서 나온, 브라이턴에 가구 딸린 집을 얻어 보자는 제안으로 끝났다. 그의 아내와, 병약한 딸과, 레이철이 함께 그 집에 살고, 그가 철이 좀 지나 그들에게 합류하기를 기다리기로 했다. 그들은 몇몇 오랜 벗 말고는 아무 교제도 하지 않을 터였고, 런던행 기차로 오락가락하는 그의 아들 고드프리를 언제든 마음대로 부릴 수 있을 터였다.\n\n내가 이 목적 없는, 이 거처에서 저 거처로의 옮겨 다님을—이 채울 길 없는 몸의 안절부절과 소름 끼치는 영혼의 정체를—묘사하는 것은, 그저 결과에 이르기 위함이다. (섭리 아래) 레이철 버린더와 나를 다시 한자리에 모으는 방편이 된 그 사건은, 다름 아닌 그 브라이턴 집을 얻는 일이었다.\n\n나의 에이블화이트 이모는 몸집 크고, 말 없고, 살빛 흰 여자로, 그 됨됨이에 눈여겨볼 만한 점이 하나 있다. 태어난 그 시각부터 그녀는 여태껏 무엇 하나 제 손으로 한 것으로 알려진 적이 없다. 그녀는 모든 이의 도움을 받아들이고, 모든 이의 의견을 취하며 삶을 지나왔다. 영적인 관점에서, 나는 여태껏 더 가망 없는 사람을 만나 본 적이 없다—이 난감한 경우에는, 붙들고 씨름할 저항하는 재료가 전혀 없다. 에이블화이트 이모는 티베트의 달라이 라마가 하는 말을 딱 나의 말을 듣듯 들을 것이고, 그의 견해를 나의 견해를 비추는 것만큼이나 선선히 비출 것이다. 그녀는 런던의 어느 호텔에 묵으며, 소파에 몸을 뉘고, 제 아들을 부름으로써, 브라이턴의 그 가구 딸린 집을 찾아냈다. 그녀는 어느 아침 (여전히 그 호텔에서) 침대에서 아침을 들면서, 제 하녀에게 \"클랙 양을 데려오는 것으로 즐거움을 시작하는\" 조건으로 휴가를 줌으로써, 필요한 하인들을 알아냈다. 나는 열한 시에 그녀가 실내복 차림으로 태평하게 부채질하고 있는 것을 보았다. \"드루실라, 얘야, 나는 하인이 몇 필요하구나. 너는 어찌나 영리한지—부디 나를 위해 그들을 구해 다오.\" 나는 어수선한 방을 둘러보았다. 평일 예배를 알리는 교회 종이 울리고 있었다. 그것이 내게 다정한 훈계 한마디를 넌지시 일러 주었다. \"오, 이모님!\" 하고 나는 서글피 말했다. \"이것이 기독교인 영국 여자에게 어울리는 일인가요? 시간에서 영원으로 건너가는 것을 이런 식으로 해야 하나요?\" 이모는 대답했다. \"가운을 입으마, 드루실라, 네가 친절히 도와준다면.\" 그 뒤에 무슨 말을 할 수 있었으랴? 나는 살인한 여자들을 상대로 놀라운 일들을 해냈다—나는 에이블화이트 이모를 상대로는 한 치도 나아가지 못했다. \"이모님이 필요로 하시는 하인들의,\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"명단은 어디 있어요?\" 이모는 고개를 저었다. 그녀에게는 명단을 간수할 기운조차 없었다. \"레이철이 갖고 있단다, 얘야,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"옆방에.\" 나는 옆방으로 들어갔고, 그렇게 우리가 몬터규 스퀘어에서 헤어진 뒤 처음으로 레이철을 다시 보았다.\n\n그 아이는 짙은 상복 차림으로 딱하리만치 작고 여위어 보였다. 만일 내가 용모 같은 그토록 스러지기 쉬운 하찮은 것에 조금이라도 진지한 무게를 둔다면, 그 아이의 살빛이, 살갗에 잇닿은 흰 테두리로 돋보이지 않을 때는 언제나 손해를 보는 그 불운한 살빛 가운데 하나라고 덧붙일 마음이 들지도 모른다. 허나 우리의 살빛과 우리의 용모가 무엇인가? 더 높은 것들로 가는 우리 길목에 도사린 걸림돌이자 함정이다, 사랑하는 아이들이여! 나의 큰 놀라움에도, 레이철은 내가 방에 들어서자 일어나, 손을 내밀고 나를 맞으러 앞으로 나왔다.\n\n\"만나서 반가워요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"드루실라, 저는 예전에 여러 번, 당신에게 아주 어리석고 아주 무례하게 말하는 버릇이 있었죠. 용서를 빌어요. 부디 저를 용서해 주면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n내 얼굴이, 짐작건대, 이것에 내가 느낀 놀라움을 드러냈으리라. 그 아이는 한순간 얼굴을 붉히고는, 이어서 제 뜻을 설명했다.\n\n\"제 가엾은 어머니가 살아 계실 때는,\" 하고 그 아이가 이어 갔다. \"그분의 벗들이 늘 제 벗이기도 했던 건 아니었어요. 이제 그분을 잃고 나니, 제 마음이 위안을 얻으려고 그분이 좋아하던 사람들에게로 돌아서네요. 그분은 당신을 좋아하셨죠. 저와 벗이 되어 주려 애써 줘요, 드루실라, 그럴 수 있다면요.\"\n\n올바르게 짜인 마음이라면 누구에게든, 이렇게 인정된 그 동기는 그저 충격적이었다. 여기 기독교의 잉글랜드에, 사별의 처지에 놓인 젊은 여자가, 참된 위안을 어디서 찾아야 할지에 대한 생각이 어찌나 없던지, 그것을 실제로 제 어머니의 벗들 사이에서 찾으리라 예상하다니! 여기 나의 친척이, 타인들에 대한 제 모자람의 자각에 눈떴는데, 확신과 의무의 영향 아래서가 아니라, 감상과 충동의 영향 아래서였다니! 생각하기에 더없이 개탄스럽거니와—허나, 그럼에도, 좋은 일에 힘쓰는 나 같은 경험 있는 사람에게는, 무언가 희망적인 것을 넌지시 알려 주는 노릇이었다. 나는, 그 어머니의 죽음이 레이철의 됨됨이에 일으킨 그 변화의 정도를 확인하는 데 해로울 것이 없다고 생각했다. 나는, 쓸모 있는 시험으로, 그 아이의 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와의 혼약이라는 화제에 대해 그 아이를 떠보기로 마음먹었다.\n\n먼저 그 아이의 다가옴을 될 수 있는 대로 더없이 살갑게 맞아 준 뒤, 나는 그 아이 자신의 청에 따라, 소파에서 그 아이 곁에 앉았다. 우리는 집안일과 앞날의 계획을 논했다—그 아이의 혼인으로 끝나게 될 그 한 가지 앞날의 계획만은 언제나 빼고서. 아무리 그쪽으로 이야기를 돌리려 애써도, 그 아이는 그 낌새를 알아채기를 한사코 마다했다. 그 물음을 내 쪽에서 드러내 놓고 이르는 것은, 우리 화해의 이 이른 단계에서는 너무 성급한 노릇이었으리라. 게다가, 나는 알고 싶은 것을 죄다 알아냈다. 그 아이는 더는, 몬터규 스퀘어에서의 나의 순교 때 내가 듣고 본 그 무모하고 반항적인 것이 아니었다. 이것만으로도, 그 아이의 앞날의 개심을 손에 맡을 만큼—혼인의 매듭을 성급히 짓는 데 맞선 몇 마디 간곡한 경고로 시작하여, 그렇게 더 높은 것들로 나아갈 만큼—넉넉히 나를 부추겼다. 이제, 이 새로운 관심으로 그 아이를 바라보며—그리고 그 아이가 고드프리 씨의 혼인 견해를 맞은 그 곤두박질 같은 느닷없음을 떠올리며—나는, 내가 예사롭지 않은 결과를 이루어 내리라 확신케 하는 뜨거움으로, 끼어드는 그 엄숙한 의무를 느꼈다. 이 경우에는, 내가 믿기로는, 일을 빨리 처리하는 것이 중요했다. 나는 대뜸 그 가구 딸린 집에 필요한 하인들이라는 물음으로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"명단이 어디 있니, 얘야?\"\n\n레이철이 그것을 내놓았다.\n\n\"요리사, 부엌 하녀, 하녀, 그리고 남자 하인이라.\" 하고 나는 읽었다. \"얘 레이철, 이 하인들은 한 기간 동안만—네 후견인이 그 집을 얻은 기간 동안만 필요한 거지. 런던에서 구하려 들면, 우리는 그런 종류의 한시적 자리를 받아들일, 됨됨이와 능력을 갖춘 사람들을 찾는 데 큰 어려움을 겪을 게다. 브라이턴 집은 벌써 구해졌니?\"\n\n\"네. 고드프리가 얻었어요. 그리고 그 집 사람들이 그더러 저희를 하인으로 고용해 달라 했고요. 그는 그들이 우리에게 좀처럼 어울리지 않으리라 여기고, 아무것도 정하지 않은 채 돌아왔어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 너 자신은 이런 일에 아무 경험도 없고, 레이철?\"\n\n\"전혀 없어요.\"\n\n\"그리고 에이블화이트 이모는 몸을 움직이려 하지 않으실 테고?\"\n\n\"안 하시죠, 가엾은 이. 그분을 나무라지 마세요, 드루실라. 제 생각에 그분은 제가 여태껏 만나 본 가운데 정말로 유일하게 행복한 여자예요.\"\n\n\"행복에도 정도가 있단다, 얘야. 우리 언젠가 그 화제에 대해 좀 이야기 나눠야겠구나. 그러는 동안 하인들에 대한 그 어려움은 내가 맡으마. 네 이모가 그 집 사람들에게 편지를 쓰시면——\"\n\n\"제가 그분을 위해 써 드리면 그분이 서명하실 거예요, 그게 같은 얘기죠.\"\n\n\"딱 같은 얘기지. 내가 그 편지를 받아서, 내일 브라이턴으로 가마.\"\n\n\"어쩌면 그렇게 친절하세요! 저희도 당신 채비가 되는 대로 곧 당신과 합류할게요. 그리고 부디, 제 손님으로 머물러 주세요. 브라이턴은 아주 활기차거든요. 당신도 분명 즐거우실 거예요.\"\n\n그 말로 초대가 건네졌고, 끼어듦의 영광스러운 앞날이 내 앞에 열렸다.\n\n그때는 주중이었다. 토요일 오후면 그 집은 그들을 맞을 채비가 되었다. 그 짧은 사이에 나는, 내게 지원한 일 없는 하인들 죄다의 됨됨이뿐만 아니라 종교적 견해까지 가려내어, 내 양심이 승인하는 선택을 해내는 데 성공했다. 나는 또한, 브라이턴에 사는 나의 진지한 벗 둘을 알아내어 찾아갔는데, 나는 그들에게 나를 브라이턴으로 데려온 그 경건한 목적을 털어놓을 수 있음을 알고 있었다. 그중 하나는—성직자 벗이었는데—우리 작은 무리를 위해 저 자신이 봉직하는 교회에 자리를 잡는 것을 친절히 도와주었다. 다른 하나는—나처럼 미혼의 부인이었는데—제 서고(온통 소중한 간행물로 이루어진)의 자원을 온전히 내 뜻대로 쓰게 해 주었다. 나는 예닐곱 권의 저작을, 죄다 레이철을 염두에 두고 정성껏 골라, 빌렸다. 이것들을 그 아이가 있음 직한 여러 방에 슬기롭게 뿌려 놓고 나서, 나는 나의 준비가 완료되었다고 여겼다. 그 아이를 시중드는 하인들 안의 건전한 교리, 그 아이에게 설교하는 목사 안의 건전한 교리, 그 아이 탁자에 놓인 책들 안의 건전한 교리—그것이, 나의 열의가 그 어미 없는 아이를 위해 마련한 세 겹의 환영이었다! 그 토요일 오후, 내가 창가에 앉아 나의 친척들이 도착하기를 기다리는 동안, 천상의 평온이 내 마음을 채웠다. 어지러운 무리가 내 눈앞을 지나갔다 다시 지나갔다 했다. 아아! 그들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 소임을 다했다는 나의 더없는 느낌을 느꼈던가? 끔찍한 물음이다. 우리 그것을 파고들지 말자.\n\n여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이에 그 여행자들이 도착했다. 나의 이루 말할 수 없는 놀라움에도, 그들을 호위한 것은 (내가 예상했던 대로) 고드프리 씨가 아니라, 변호사 브러프 씨였다.\n\n\"안녕하십니까, 클랙 양?\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이번에는 머무를 작정입니다.\"\n\n우리 둘 다 몬터규 스퀘어에 방문했을 때 내가 그로 하여금 제 볼일을 내 볼일 뒤로 미루게 했던 그 일을 이르는 그 말이, 그 늙은 속물이 저 나름의 어떤 목적을 품고 브라이턴에 왔음을 내게 납득시켰다. 나는 나의 사랑하는 레이철을 위해 넉넉히 조그만 낙원을 마련해 두었는데—여기 벌써 뱀이 있었던 것이다!\n\n\"고드프리가 우리와 함께 오지 못해 아주 속상해했어, 드루실라.\" 하고 에이블화이트 이모가 말했다. \"그를 도회지에 붙들어 놓는 무언가가 있었나 봐. 브러프 씨가 자청해서 그의 자리를 대신하고, 월요일 아침까지 휴가로 삼겠다고 했지. 그런데 참, 브러프 씨, 나는 운동을 하라는 분부를 받았는데, 나는 그게 싫어요. 저것이,\" 하고 에이블화이트 이모가, 창밖으로 사내가 끄는 바퀴 달린 의자에 실려 가는 어느 병자를 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"내가 생각하는 운동이에요. 바람을 쐬고 싶으면, 의자에 앉아 쐬면 되죠. 그리고 고단해지고 싶으면, 저 사내를 보는 것만으로도 넉넉히 고단하고요.\"\n\n레이철은 저 혼자 어느 창가에, 두 눈을 바다에 고정한 채, 말없이 서 있었다.\n\n\"고단하니, 얘야?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"아니요. 그저 기운이 좀 없어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 대답했다. \"저는 우리 요크셔 해안에서, 저런 빛이 어린 바다를 자주 봤어요. 그리고 저는, 드루실라, 결코 다시 올 수 없는 그 나날을 생각하고 있었어요.\"\n\n브러프 씨는 저녁 식사에 남았고, 저녁 내내 머물렀다. 그를 볼수록, 나는 그가 브라이턴에 오면서 봉사할 어떤 남모를 목적이 있다고 더욱 확신했다. 나는 그를 유심히 지켜보았다. 그는 떠날 때가 될 때까지, 시간이 가고 또 가도, 똑같은 태연한 모양새를 유지하고, 똑같은 신을 모르는 잡담을 늘어놓았다. 그가 레이철과 악수할 때, 나는 그의 딱딱하고 교활한 눈이 한순간 유별난 관심과 주의로 그 아이에게 머무는 것을 포착했다. 그 아이는 뻔히 그가 품은 그 목적에 관련되어 있었다. 그는 떠나면서 그 아이에게든 누구에게든 예사롭지 않은 말은 아무것도 하지 않았다. 그는 이튿날 점심에 저 자신을 초대하고는, 제 호텔로 갔다.\n\n이튿날 아침 에이블화이트 이모를 교회에 갈 시간에 맞춰 실내복에서 벗어나게 하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 그 병약한 딸은 (내 생각에는, 제 어머니에게서 물려받은 낫지 않는 게으름 말고는 아무것도 앓지 않으면서) 그날은 침대에 있을 작정이라고 알렸다. 레이철과 나는 단둘이서 교회에 갔다. 나의 재능 있는 벗이 작은 죄들의 죄스러움에 대한 세상의 이교도 같은 무관심에 대해 훌륭한 설교를 했다. 한 시간 넘게 그의 웅변이 (그의 영광스러운 목소리에 도움받아) 그 성스러운 건물에 우렁차게 울려 퍼졌다. 나는 우리가 나올 때 레이철에게 말했다. \"그것이 네 마음에 가닿았니, 얘야?\" 그러자 그 아이가 대답했다. \"아니요. 그저 머리가 아플 뿐이에요.\" 이것은 어떤 사람들에게는 맥 빠지는 노릇이었을지 모른다. 허나, 한번 명백한 쓸모의 길에 나서면, 아무것도 나를 맥 빠지게 하지 못한다.\n\n우리는 에이블화이트 이모와 브러프 씨가 점심을 들고 있는 것을 보았다. 레이철이 아무것도 먹기를 마다하며, 머리가 아파 그런다고 이유를 대자, 그 변호사의 교활함이 대뜸, 그 아이가 그에게 준 그 기회를 보고, 붙잡았다.\n\n\"머리 아픈 데는 약이 하나뿐입니다.\" 하고 이 끔찍한 늙은이가 말했다. \"산책이, 레이철 아가씨, 아가씨를 낫게 할 것입니다. 제 팔을 받아 주는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 저는 온전히 아가씨의 뜻에 따르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"더없이 기쁘게요. 산책이야말로 제가 바로 갈망하던 거예요.\"\n\n\"두 시가 지났어.\" 하고 나는 부드럽게 넌지시 일렀다. \"그리고 오후 예배가, 레이철, 세 시에 시작한단다.\"\n\n\"이런 두통을 가지고 어떻게 저더러 또 교회에 가라는 거예요?\" 하고 그 아이가 짜증스레 물었다.\n\n브러프 씨가 주제넘게 그 아이를 위해 문을 열어 주었다. 한 순간 더 지나 그들은 둘 다 집 밖에 있었다. 나는 그 순간 느낀 것만큼 끼어드는 엄숙한 의무를 그토록 강하게 느껴 본 적이 언제였는지 모르겠다. 허나 어찌해야 했던가? 그날 늦게, 첫 기회에 끼어드는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없었다.\n\n오후 예배에서 돌아오니 나는 그들이 막 돌아온 것을 알았다. 그들을 한 번 보는 것으로 나는 그 변호사가 하고 싶은 말을 했음을 알았다. 나는 여태껏 레이철이 그토록 말이 없고 그토록 생각에 잠긴 것을 본 적이 없었다. 나는 여태껏 브러프 씨가 그 아이에게 그토록 헌신적인 주의를 기울이고, 그토록 두드러진 존경으로 그 아이를 바라보는 것을 본 적이 없었다. 그는 그날 저녁 식사 약속이 있었다 (또는 있는 척했다)—그리고 그는 우리 모두에게 이른 작별을 고했다. 이튿날 아침 첫 기차로 런던에 돌아갈 작정으로 말이다.\n\n\"당신 자신의 결심을 확신합니까?\" 하고 그가 문가에서 레이철에게 말했다.\n\n\"아주 확신해요.\" 하고 그 아이가 대답했다—그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다.\n\n그가 등을 돌리는 순간, 레이철은 제 방으로 물러갔다. 그 아이는 저녁 식사에 도무지 나타나지 않았다. 그 아이의 하녀 (그 모자 리본을 단 사람)가 아래층으로 보내져, 그 아이의 두통이 도졌다고 알렸다. 나는 그 아이에게로 뛰어 올라가, 문 너머로 온갖 자매다운 도움을 내밀었다. 문은 잠겨 있었고, 그 아이는 그것을 잠근 채 두었다. 여기 붙들고 씨름할 저항하는 재료가 넉넉했다! 나는 그 아이가 문을 잠근 것에 크게 기운이 나고 북돋아졌다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그 아이의 차 한 잔이 위층으로 올라갈 때, 나는 그것을 뒤따라 들어갔다. 나는 그 아이 침대맡에 앉아 몇 마디 간곡한 말을 했다. 그 아이는 나른한 정중함으로 들었다. 나는 나의 진지한 벗의 소중한 간행물들이 한구석 탁자 위에 뒤섞여 쌓여 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그 아이가 우연히 그것들을 들여다본 적이 있는지?—나는 물었다. 그렇다—그리고 그것들은 그 아이의 흥미를 끌지 못했다. 그 아이 눈을 아마 스쳐 지나갔을, 더없이 깊은 흥미로운 대목 몇 개를 내가 읽어 주는 것을 허락하겠는지? 아니, 지금은 아니에요—그 아이는 생각할 다른 일들이 있었다. 그 아이는, 뻔히 제 잠옷의 주름 장식을 접었다 다시 접었다 하는 데 정신을 팔며, 이 대답들을 했다. 그 아이가 여전히 마음에 두고 있는 그 세속적 잇속에 대한 어떤 언급으로 그 아이를 일깨우는 것이 뻔히 필요했다.\n\n\"그거 아니, 얘야,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 어제, 브러프 씨에 대해 이상한 생각이 들었단다. 나는, 네가 그와 산책한 뒤에 너를 봤을 때, 그가 네게 무슨 나쁜 소식을 전한 거라고 생각했지.\"\n\n그 아이의 손가락이 제 잠옷 주름 장식에서 떨어졌고, 그 아이의 사나운 검은 눈이 나를 향해 번득였다.\n\n\"정반대예요!\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"그건 제가 듣기에 관심 있던 소식이었어요—그리고 저는 그것을 제게 전해 준 브러프 씨에게 깊이 감사하고 있고요.\"\n\n\"그래?\" 하고 나는 부드러운 관심의 어조로 말했다.\n\n그 아이의 손가락이 주름 장식으로 돌아갔고, 그 아이는 뾰로통하게 나에게서 고개를 돌렸다. 좋은 일에 힘쓰는 동안, 나는 이런 식으로 수백 번 마주쳐졌다. 그것은 그저 나로 하여금 다시 시도하도록 북돋았을 뿐이다. 그 아이의 안녕을 위한 나의 굴하지 않는 열의로, 나는 큰 위험을 무릅쓰고, 그 아이의 혼약을 드러내 놓고 넌지시 일렀다.\n\n\"네가 듣기에 관심 있던 소식이라?\" 하고 나는 되뇌었다. \"짐작건대, 얘 레이철, 그건 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 소식임이 틀림없겠구나?\"\n\n그 아이는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 새파랗게 질렸다. 지난날의 고삐 풀린 무례함으로 나에게 쏘아붙이려는 것이 뻔히 그 아이 혀끝에 걸려 있었다. 그 아이는 스스로를 억눌렀다—제 머리를 베개에 도로 뉘고—잠시 헤아리고는—그런 다음 이 놀라운 말로 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 결코 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와 혼인하지 않겠어요.\"\n\n이번에는 내가 흠칫할 차례였다.\n\n\"대체 무슨 뜻이니?\" 하고 나는 외쳤다. \"그 혼인은 온 집안이 정해진 일로 여기는데!\"\n\n\"고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 오늘 여기 오기로 되어 있어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 고집스레 말했다. \"그가 올 때까지 기다려요—그럼 알게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 얘 레이철——\"\n\n그 아이는 제 침대 머리맡의 초인종을 울렸다. 그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 나타났다.\n\n\"페넬로피! 내 목욕물.\"\n\n그 아이에게 마땅한 값을 쳐주자. 그 순간의 내 심정의 상태로 보아, 나는 그 아이가 나로 하여금 그 방을 떠나게 만들 유일하게 가능한 방도를 찾아냈다고 진심으로 믿는다.\n\n한낱 세속적 마음으로 보자면 레이철에 대한 나의 처지는 예사롭지 않은 종류의 어려움을 드러내는 것으로 보였을지 모른다. 나는 그 아이의 혼인이라는 화제에 대한 조그마한 간곡한 훈계로 그 아이를 더 높은 것들로 이끌 셈이었다. 그런데 이제, 그 아이의 말을 믿는다면, 그 아이의 혼인 같은 사건은 도무지 벌어지지 않을 것이었다. 허나 아, 나의 벗들이여! 나 같은 경험 있는 일하는 기독교인은 (전도의 앞날을 앞에 두고) 이런 것보다 더 넓은 견해를 취한다. 만일 레이철이, 에이블화이트 부자가 정해진 일로 셈하던 그 혼인을 정말로 깨뜨린다고 하면, 그 결과는 어떻게 될까? 그 아이가 굳게 버틴다면, 그것은 양편에서 모진 말과 쓰라린 비난을 주고받는 것으로밖에 끝날 수 없었다. 그리고 그 폭풍 같은 면담이 끝났을 때 레이철에게 미칠 영향은 어떤 것일까? 이로운 도덕적 침체가 그 영향일 터였다. 그 아이의 자존심은 지쳐 버릴 것이고, 그 아이의 고집은, 그런 정황에서 그 아이의 됨됨이가 부릴 그 완강한 저항으로 인해, 지쳐 버릴 것이었다. 그 아이는 동정을 베풀 가장 가까운 사람에게 동정을 얻으려 돌아설 것이었다. 그리고 내가 그 가장 가까운 사람이었다—위안으로 가득 차고, 때맞고 되살리는 말로 넘치도록 채워진. 전도의 앞날이 나의 눈에, 지금 보이는 것만큼 밝아 보인 적은 없었다.\n\n그 아이는 아침 식사에 내려왔으나, 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 한마디도 거의 하지 않았다.\n\n아침 식사 뒤 그 아이는 맥없이 이 방 저 방을 어정거렸다—그러다 별안간 스스로를 추스르고, 피아노를 열었다. 그 아이가 골라 친 그 곡은 더없이 추문거리로 불경한 종류의 것으로, 생각만 해도 피가 얼어붙는 무대 공연과 엮인 것이었다. 이런 때에 그 아이에게 끼어드는 것은 너무 성급한 노릇이었으리라. 나는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 오기로 되어 있는 시각을 남몰래 확인하고는, 집을 떠남으로써 그 음악에서 벗어났다.\n\n혼자 나온 김에, 나는 나의 그곳에 사는 두 벗을 찾아가는 기회를 잡았다. 진지한 사람들과 간곡한 대화에 빠져 있는 나 자신을 발견하는 것은 이루 말할 수 없는 호사였다. 한없이 북돋아지고 상쾌해져서, 나는, 우리가 기다리는 방문객의 도착을 기다리기에 아주 알맞은 때에, 집으로 발길을 돌렸다. 나는, 하루 중 그 시각에는 언제나 비어 있는 식당에 들어갔고, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와 얼굴을 마주하고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다!\n\n그는 그 자리를 피하려는 시도를 조금도 하지 않았다. 정반대였다. 그는 더없는 열의로 나를 맞으러 나아왔다.\n\n\"사랑하는 클랙 양, 저는 다만 당신을 보려고 기다리고 있었어요! 뜻밖의 우연이 오늘 저를 런던 약속에서 예상보다 일찍 풀어 주었고, 그 결과 저는 정해진 시각보다 일찍 여기 다다랐답니다.\"\n\n몬터규 스퀘어의 그 장면 뒤 이것이 그와 나의 첫 만남이었건만, 조금의 곤혹스러움도 그의 해명을 짓누르지 않았다. 그가 그 장면의 증인이 나였음을 몰랐던 것은 사실이다. 허나 그는, 다른 한편으로, 내가 '어머니회 아동복'에 나가는 것과, 다른 자선회에 딸린 벗들과의 관계로 미루어, 그가 제 부인들과 제 가난한 이들을 파렴치하게 등한시한 것을 내가 알았음이 틀림없다는 것은 알고 있었다. 그런데도 거기 그가 내 앞에 있었다, 그의 매혹적인 목소리와 그의 거스를 수 없는 미소를 온전히 지닌 채로!\n\n\"레이철은 벌써 봤나요?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n그는 부드럽게 한숨짓고, 내 손을 잡았다. 그가 제 대답을 하는 태도가 나를 놀라움으로 마비시키지 않았더라면, 나는 분명 내 손을 홱 잡아 뺐으리라.\n\n\"레이철을 봤어요.\" 하고 그가 더없는 평온함으로 말했다. \"당신도 아시지요, 사랑하는 벗, 그 아이가 저와 약혼했다는 것을요? 그런데, 그 아이가 별안간 그 약혼을 깨기로 결심했어요. 곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에 그 아이는, 성급한 약속을 물리고, 제가 다른 데서 더 행복한 선택을 할 수 있도록 자유롭게 놓아주는 것이, 제 안녕과 저의 안녕을 가장 잘 살피는 길이라고 확신하게 되었지요. 그것이 그 아이가 댈 유일한 이유이고, 제가 그 아이에게 던질 수 있는 온갖 물음에 그 아이가 할 유일한 대답이에요.\"\n\n\"당신은 당신 편에서 무엇을 했나요?\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"순순히 따랐나요?\"\n\n\"네.\" 하고 그가 더없이 흐트러지지 않은 평온함으로 말했다. \"저는 순순히 따랐어요.\"\n\n그 정황 아래 그의 처신이 어찌나 도무지 상상할 수 없는 것이던지, 나는 내 손을 그의 손에 맡긴 채 어리둥절해 서 있었다. 누구를 빤히 쳐다보는 것은 무례한 짓이고, 신사를 빤히 쳐다보는 것은 정숙하지 못한 짓이다. 나는 그 두 가지 무례를 다 저질렀다. 그리고 나는, 마치 꿈속에서인 듯, 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻인가요?\"\n\n\"당신에게 말씀드리게 해 주세요.\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"그리고 우리 앉을까요?\"\n\n그는 나를 의자로 이끌었다. 나는 그가 아주 다정했다는 어렴풋한 기억이 있다. 나를 부축하려고 그가 내 허리에 팔을 둘렀던 것 같지는 않다—허나 확실치는 않다. 나는 아주 속수무책이었고, 부인들을 대하는 그의 태도는 아주 사랑스러웠다. 어쨌든, 우리는 앉았다. 다른 것은 아무것도 장담할 수 없어도, 그것만은 장담할 수 있다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n\"저는 아름다운 아가씨와, 훌륭한 사회적 지위와, 두둑한 수입을 잃었어요.\" 하고 고드프리 씨가 말문을 열었다. \"그리고 저는 아무 다툼도 없이 그것에 순순히 따랐고요. 그런 예사롭지 않은 처신의 동기가 무엇일 수 있겠어요? 나의 소중한 벗, 동기라는 게 없어요.\"\n\n\"동기가 없다고요?\" 하고 나는 되뇌었다.\n\n\"저의 사랑하는 클랙 양, 아이들에 대한 당신의 경험에 호소하게 해 주세요.\" 하고 그가 이어 갔다. \"아이가 어떤 행동을 해요. 당신은 그것에 크게 마음이 끌려, 그 동기를 캐내려 하고요. 그 사랑스러운 조그만 것은 당신에게 제 동기를 말할 능력이 없어요. 풀에게 왜 자라느냐고, 새에게 왜 노래하느냐고 묻는 편이 차라리 나을 거예요. 자! 이 일에서, 저는 그 사랑스러운 조그만 것과 같아요—풀과 같고—새와 같고요. 저는 제가 왜 버린더 아가씨에게 청혼했는지 모르겠어요. 저는 제가 왜 부끄럽게도 저의 사랑하는 부인들을 등한시했는지 모르겠어요. 저는 제가 왜 '어머니회 아동복'에서 저버렸는지 모르겠어요. 당신이 아이에게 물어요, 왜 말을 안 들었니? 그러면 그 조그만 천사는 제 손가락을 입에 넣고, 모른다고 해요. 딱 제 경우예요, 클랙 양! 저는 다른 누구에게도 그것을 고백할 수 없었어요. 저는 당신에게 그것을 고백하지 않을 수 없다는 느낌이 들어요!\"\n\n나는 정신을 차리기 시작했다. 여기에는 마음의 문제 하나가 걸려 있었다. 나는 마음의 문제에 깊이 관심이 있다—그리고 나는, 그것을 푸는 데에 얼마쯤 솜씨가 없지 않다고들 여겨진다.\n\n\"더없이 좋은 벗이여, 당신의 지력을 발휘해서, 저를 도와줘요.\" 하고 그가 이어 갔다. \"말해 줘요—어째서 저의 이 혼인 절차가 꿈속에서 한 무언가처럼 보이기 시작하는 때가 오는 걸까요? 어째서 저의 참된 행복이 저의 사랑하는 부인들을 돕는 데에, 쓸모 있는 일의 수수한 순회를 도는 데에, 저의 의장께서 부르실 때 몇 마디 간곡한 말을 하는 데에 있다는 생각이 별안간 드는 걸까요? 제게 지위가 무슨 소용인가요? 저는 지위가 있는걸요! 제게 수입이 무슨 소용인가요? 저는 제 빵과 치즈와, 제 아담한 조그만 셋방과, 한 해 두 벌의 외투 값을 치를 수 있는걸요. 제게 버린더 아가씨가 무슨 소용인가요? 그 아이는 제 입으로 저에게 (이건, 사랑하는 부인, 우리끼리 얘긴데요) 다른 사내를 사랑하며, 저와 혼인하려는 유일한 생각은 그 다른 사내를 제 머릿속에서 몰아내 보려는 것이라고 말했어요. 이 얼마나 끔찍한 결합인가요! 오, 저런, 이 얼마나 끔찍한 결합인가요! 그것이, 클랙 양, 브라이턴으로 오는 길에 든 저의 상념이에요. 저는 판결을 받으러 가는 죄인의 심정으로 레이철에게 다가가요. 그 아이도 마음을 바꿨다는 것을 알아냈을 때—그 아이가 약혼을 깨자고 제안하는 것을 들었을 때—저는 (그것에 대해서는 어떤 종류의 의심도 없이) 더없이 압도적인 안도감을 느껴요. 한 달 전에 저는 황홀하게 그 아이를 제 가슴에 끌어안고 있었어요. 한 시간 전에는, 다시는 그 아이를 끌어안지 않으리라는 것을 아는 그 행복이, 독한 술처럼 저를 취하게 해요. 그 일은 있을 수 없어 보여요—그 일은 있을 수 없어요. 그런데도 사실들이 저기 있어요, 우리가 이 두 의자에 처음 함께 앉았을 때 제가 삼가 아뢴 그대로요. 저는 아름다운 아가씨와, 훌륭한 사회적 지위와, 두둑한 수입을 잃었어요. 그리고 저는 아무 다툼도 없이 그것에 순순히 따랐고요. 당신은 그것을 설명할 수 있어요, 사랑하는 벗? 그건 도무지 저로서는 넘어서는 일이에요.\"\n\n그의 훌륭한 머리가 제 가슴으로 떨어졌고, 그는 절망 속에서 제 자신의 마음의 문제를 포기했다.\n\n나는 깊이 감동했다. 그 경우는 (내가 영적 의사로서 말해도 된다면) 이제 내게 아주 명백했다. 뛰어난 재능을 지닌 이들이 이따금 제 둘레의 가장 재능이 빈약한 사람들의 수준으로 낮추어지는 것을 보는 것은, 우리 모두의 경험에서 드물지 않은 일이다. 그 뜻은, 의심할 것 없이, 섭리의 슬기로운 경륜 속에서, 위대함에게 그것이 죽을 것이며 그것을 부여한 그 힘이 또한 그것을 거두어 갈 수도 있음을 일깨우는 것이다. 이제—내 마음으로는—사랑하는 고드프리 씨 편의 그 개탄스러운 행동, 내가 그 보이지 않는 증인이었던 그 행동 속에서, 이런 이로운 낮추어짐 가운데 하나를 알아보기가 쉬웠다. 그리고 그가 레이철과의 혼인이라는 생각에서 움츠러들며 물러선 그 소름 끼침 속에서, 그리고 제 부인들과 제 가난한 이들에게로 돌아가려고 그가 보인 그 매혹적인 열의 속에서, 그 자신의 더 고운 본성의 반가운 재등장을 알아보기도 꼭 그만큼 쉬웠다.\n\n나는 몇 마디 소박하고 자매다운 말로 이 견해를 그의 앞에 내놓았다. 그의 기쁨은 보기에 아름다웠다. 그는, 내가 말을 이어 가는 동안, 저 자신을 어둠에서 빛으로 나오는 길 잃은 사람에 견주었다. 내가 '어머니회 아동복'에서 그를 다정하게 맞아 주리라 장담하자, 우리의 기독교적 영웅의 감사에 찬 마음이 넘쳐 흘렀다. 그는 내 두 손을 번갈아 제 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그를 우리에게로 도로 데려왔다는 그 더없는 승리감에 압도되어, 나는 그가 내 손을 마음대로 하도록 놓아두었다. 나는 눈을 감았다. 나는 나의 머리가, 영적인 자기 망각의 황홀 속에서, 그의 어깨로 떨어지는 것을 느꼈다. 한 순간 더 지나면 나는 분명 그의 팔 안에서 까무러쳤으련만, 바깥세상에서 온 훼방이 나를 다시 정신 차리게 했다. 문밖에서 나이프와 포크가 끔찍하게 덜그럭거리는 소리가 났고, 그 남자 하인이 점심 상을 차리러 들어왔다.\n\n고드프리 씨는 벌떡 일어나, 벽난로 선반의 시계를 보았다.\n\n\"당신과 함께 있으니 시간이 어찌나 잘 가는지!\" 하고 그가 외쳤다. \"저는 기차를 겨우 잡겠어요.\"\n\n나는 그가 어째서 그토록 서둘러 도회지로 돌아가려는지 감히 물어보았다. 그의 대답은 내게, 아직 풀려야 할 집안의 어려움들과, 아직 올 집안의 불화들을 떠올리게 했다.\n\n\"저는 아버지에게서 기별을 받았어요.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"볼일 때문에 아버지가 오늘 프리징홀을 떠나 런던으로 가시게 되었는데, 오늘 저녁이나 내일 이곳으로 오시겠다는 거예요. 저는 레이철과 저 사이에 있었던 일을 아버지께 말씀드려야 해요. 아버지는 우리 혼인에 마음을 쏟고 계세요—약혼이 깨진 것을 받아들이시게 하는 데에 큰 어려움이 있을까 봐 걱정이에요. 저는, 우리 모두를 위해, 아버지가 받아들이시기 전에는 이곳으로 오시는 것을 막아야 해요. 더없이 좋고 사랑하는 벗이여, 우리 다시 만나요!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 그는 서둘러 나갔다. 내 쪽에서도 똑같이 서둘러, 나는 점심 상에서 에이블화이트 이모와 레이철을 만나기 전에 마음을 가라앉히려고 내 방으로 뛰어 올라갔다.\n\n나는 잘 안다—고드프리 씨라는 화제에 잠깐 더 머물자면—온갖 것을 더럽히는 세상의 견해가 그를, 레이철이 그에게 준 첫 기회에 그 아이를 약혼에서 풀어 준 데에 저 나름의 남모를 이유가 있었다고 몰아붙였음을. 또한 내 귀에 들어오기로는, 나의 평가에서 제 자리를 되찾으려는 그의 안달이, 어떤 부류에서는, 이 세상 재물로 넉넉히 복 받은, 그리고 나 자신의 사랑하고 가까운 벗인, '어머니회 아동복'의 어느 존경받는 위원회 부인과 (나를 통해) 화해하려는 잇속 밝은 열의 탓으로 돌려졌다는 것이다. 내가 이 얄미운 비방을 짚는 것은 오로지, 그것이 결코 한순간도 내 마음에 영향을 미치지 못했음을 선언하기 위해서다. 나의 지시에 따라, 나는 우리의 기독교적 영웅에 대한 나의 견해의 흔들림을, 나의 일기에 기록된 그대로 꼭 그렇게 드러내 보였다. 나 자신을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 여기에 덧붙이게 해 다오, 나의 평가에서 한번 제 자리를 되찾은 뒤로, 나의 재능 있는 벗은 결코 다시 그 자리를 잃지 않았음을. 나는 눈에 눈물을 담고, 더 말하고 싶어 애타며 쓴다. 허나 아니—나는 사람과 사물에 대한 나의 실제 경험에 모질게도 국한되어 있다. 내가 지금 쓰고 있는 그때로부터 채 한 달도 안 되어, 금융 시장의 사건들이 (나의 그 비참한 조그만 수입마저 줄여 놓아) 나를 외국 유배로 몰아넣었고, 세상의 비방이 덮쳤으나 헛되이 덮친 고드프리 씨에 대한 다정한 기억 말고는 아무것도 내게 남기지 않았다.\n\n나의 눈물을 닦고, 나의 이야기로 돌아가자.\n\n나는 점심을 들러 아래층으로 내려갔다, 자연히 레이철이 제 혼약에서 풀려난 것에 어떻게 영향받았는지 보고 싶어 마음 졸이며.\n\n내게 보이기로는—허나 나는 이런 일에 서투른 권위자임을 인정한다—제 자유를 되찾은 것이 그 아이로 하여금 다시 그 사랑하던 다른 사내를 생각하게 만들었고, 그 아이가 남몰래 부끄러워하는 그 감정의 반전을 다스리지 못하는 것에 저 자신에게 몹시 화가 나 있는 듯했다. 그 사내는 누구였을까? 나는 나름의 의심이 있었다—허나 부질없는 억측으로 시간을 허비할 것 없었다. 내가 그 아이를 개심시키면, 그 아이는, 당연히, 나에게 아무 감춤도 없을 터였다. 나는 그 사내에 대해 죄다 듣게 될 것이었다. 나는 월장석에 대해 죄다 듣게 될 것이었다. 그 아이를 일깨워 영적인 것들을 자각하게 하는 데에 내게 더 높은 목적이 없었더라도, 그 아이의 마음을 죄스러운 비밀에서 풀어 준다는 동기만으로도 나로 하여금 계속하도록 넉넉히 북돋았으리라.\n\n에이블화이트 이모는 오후에 병자용 의자에서 운동을 했다. 레이철이 함께했다. \"제가 그 의자를 끌 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 무모하게 터뜨렸다. \"쓰러질 지경이 될 때까지 저 자신을 고단하게 만들 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\"\n\n그 아이는 저녁에도 똑같은 심기였다. 나는 나의 벗의 소중한 간행물 가운데 하나—「제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 생애, 편지, 그리고 노고」 사십사 판—에서 레이철의 지금 처지에 놀랍도록 꼭 들어맞는 대목들을 찾아냈다. 내가 그것들을 읽어 주자고 제안하자, 그 아이는 피아노로 갔다. 나의 참을성이 그런 식으로 지쳐 버리리라 여겼다면, 그 아이가 진지한 사람들을 얼마나 몰랐음이 틀림없을지 헤아려 보시라! 나는 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양을 내 곁에 두고, 앞날에 대한 더없이 흔들림 없는 믿음으로 사건들을 기다렸다.\n\n노 에이블화이트 씨는 그날 밤 끝내 나타나지 않았다. 허나 나는 그의 세속적 탐욕이 제 아들과 버린더 아가씨의 혼인에 두는 그 중대함을 알고 있었다—그리고 나는 (고드프리 씨가 그것을 막으려 무엇을 하든) 우리가 이튿날 그를 보게 되리라는 확실한 확신이 들었다. 그가 그 일에 끼어들면, 내가 셈했던 그 폭풍이 분명 올 것이고, 레이철의 저항력의 이로운 소진이 꼭 그만큼 분명 뒤따를 터였다. 나는 노 에이블화이트 씨가 (특히 제 아랫사람들 사이에서) 놀랍도록 사람 좋은 사내라는 평판을 지녔다는 것을 모르지 않는다. 그에 대한 나의 관찰로 보아, 그는 제 뜻대로 되는 동안에는 제 평판에 값하고, 그 이상은 한순간도 아니다.\n\n이튿날, 딱 내가 내다본 대로, 에이블화이트 이모는 제 남편의 별안간 나타남에, 그녀의 성정이 허락하는 한 놀라움에 가까운 상태가 되었다. 그가 그 집에 든 지 겨우 일 분 만에, 이번에는 나의 놀라움에도, 브러프 씨라는 모양을 한 뜻밖의 복잡함이 그를 뒤따랐다.\n\n나는 그 순간 느낀 것만큼 그 변호사의 존재를 달갑잖게 느낀 적이 여태껏 있었는지 기억나지 않는다. 그는 방해하는 행동이라면 무엇이든 할 채비가 된 듯 보였다—싸움꾼 가운데 하나를 위해 레이철과 평화를 지키는 것마저 능히 할 만하게!\n\n\"이거 반가운 뜻밖의 일이군요, 선생.\" 하고 에이블화이트 씨가, 제 속임수 같은 살가움으로 브러프 씨에게 말했다. \"어제 당신 사무소를 떠날 때, 저는 오늘 브라이턴에서 당신을 뵙는 영광을 누리리라곤 예상 못 했어요.\"\n\n\"당신이 가신 뒤, 저는 우리 대화를 마음속에서 되짚어 보았습니다.\" 하고 브러프 씨가 대답했다. \"그리고 제가 이번 일에 어쩌면 얼마쯤 쓸모가 있을지 모른다는 생각이 들었지요. 저는 기차를 겨우 맞춰 잡았고, 당신이 타고 계신 객차를 찾아낼 기회가 없었습니다.\"\n\n그 해명을 하고 나서, 그는 레이철 곁에 앉았다. 나는—만약을 대비하여 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양을 무릎에 얹고—겸손히 한구석으로 물러났다. 이모는 창가에 앉아, 여느 때처럼 태평하게 부채질했다. 에이블화이트 씨는 방 한가운데 서서, 그의 벗어진 머리를 내가 여태껏 본 것보다 훨씬 더 분홍빛으로 물들인 채, 더없이 다정한 태도로 제 조카딸에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"레이철, 얘야,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 고드프리에게서 아주 예사롭지 않은 소식을 들었다. 그래서 그것에 대해 물어보러 여기 왔지. 너는 이 집에 네 전용 거실이 있잖니. 나를 그리로 안내하는 영광을 베풀어 주겠니?\"\n\n레이철은 도무지 움직이지 않았다. 그 아이가 일을 위기로 몰아갈 작정이었는지, 아니면 브러프 씨에게서 온 어떤 남모를 신호에 부추겨졌는지는 내가 알 수 있는 바가 아니다. 그 아이는 노 에이블화이트 씨를 제 거실로 안내하는 영광을 베풀기를 마다했다.\n\n\"저에게 하실 말씀이 무엇이든,\" 하고 그 아이가 대답했다. \"여기서 하실 수 있어요—제 친척들 앞에서, 그리고\" (그 아이는 브러프 씨를 바라보았다) \"제 어머니가 믿던 오랜 벗 앞에서요.\"\n\n\"네 좋을 대로 하렴, 얘야.\" 하고 상냥한 에이블화이트 씨가 말했다. 그는 의자를 잡았다. 나머지 사람들은 그의 얼굴을 바라보았다—마치 칠십 년의 세속적 훈련 뒤에, 그것이 진실을 말하기를 기대하기라도 하듯이. 나는 그의 벗어진 머리 꼭대기를 바라보았다. 다른 경우에, 정말로 그의 안에 있는 성미가 바로 거기에 저를 기록하는 버릇이 있음을 알아챈 터라서.\n\n\"몇 주 전에,\" 하고 그 늙은 신사가 이어 갔다. \"내 아들이 나에게, 버린더 아가씨가 저와 혼인하기로 약혼하는 영광을 베풀었다고 알렸다. 있을 수 있는 일이냐, 레이철, 그 아이가 네가 정말로 저에게 한 말을 잘못 해석했거나—넘겨짚었을 수가?\"\n\n\"결코 아니에요.\" 하고 그 아이가 대답했다. \"저는 정말로 그와 혼인하기로 약혼했어요.\"\n\n\"아주 솔직한 대답이구나!\" 하고 에이블화이트 씨가 말했다. \"그리고 더없이 흡족하다, 얘야, 거기까지는. 몇 주 전에 있었던 일에 관해서는, 고드프리가 아무 잘못을 하지 않았어. 그 착오는 뻔히 그 애가 어제 나에게 말한 데에 있구나. 나는 이제 그것이 보이기 시작한다. 너와 그 애가 연인끼리의 다툼을 한 거야—그리고 나의 어리석은 아들이 그것을 진지하게 해석한 거고. 아! 나라면 그 애 나이에 그보다는 잘 알았을 텐데.\"\n\n레이철 안의 타락한 본성이—이를테면, 이브 어머니가—이것에 발끈하기 시작했다.\n\n\"부디 우리 서로를 분명히 이해해요, 에이블화이트 씨.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"어제 당신 아들과 저 사이에 다툼 비슷한 것은 조금도 없었어요. 만일 그가 당신에게, 제가 우리 혼약을 깨자고 제안했고, 그가 제 편에서 동의했다고 말했다면—그는 당신에게 진실을 말한 거예요.\"\n\n에이블화이트 씨의 벗어진 머리 꼭대기의 그 스스로 기록하는 온도계가 성미의 상승을 나타내기 시작했다. 그의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 다정했으나—바로 거기에, 그의 얼굴 꼭대기의 그 분홍빛이, 벌써 한 단계 더 짙어져 있었다!\n\n\"자, 자, 얘야!\" 하고 그가 더없이 달래는 태도로 말했다. \"자, 화내지 말고, 가엾은 고드프리에게 모질게 굴지 마라! 그 애가 뻔히 무슨 불운한 말을 한 게로구나. 그 애는 어려서부터 늘 서툴렀지—하지만 뜻은 좋단다, 레이철, 뜻은 좋아!\"\n\n\"에이블화이트 씨, 제가 제 뜻을 아주 서투르게 표현했거나, 아니면 당신이 일부러 저를 오해하시는 거예요. 단번에 말하지만, 우리가 남은 평생, 사촌으로, 그 이상은 아닌 채로 남는다는 것은 당신 아들과 저 사이에 정해진 일이에요. 그건 충분히 분명한가요?\"\n\n그 아이가 그 말을 한 그 어조가, 노 에이블화이트 씨조차도, 더는 그 아이를 오해하는 것을 있을 수 없게 만들었다. 그의 온도계가 한 눈금 더 올라갔고, 그가 다음에 말할 때의 그 목소리는, 소문난 사람 좋은 사내에게 어울리는 그 목소리이기를 그쳤다.\n\n\"그럼, 나는 이렇게 이해해야겠구나,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"네 혼약이 깨졌다고?\"\n\n\"그렇게 이해하시면 돼요, 에이블화이트 씨, 괜찮으시다면요.\"\n\n\"나는 또한, 약혼에서 물러나자는 제안이, 처음에, 너에게서 왔다는 것도 사실로 받아들여야 하고?\"\n\n\"그것은, 처음에, 저에게서 왔어요. 그리고 그것은, 제가 말씀드렸듯이, 당신 아들의 동의와 승인을 받았고요.\"\n\n그 온도계가 눈금 꼭대기까지 올라갔다. 다시 말해, 그 분홍빛이 별안간 진홍빛으로 바뀌었다.\n\n\"내 아들은 비열한 개다!\" 하고 이 격분한 늙은 속물이 외쳤다. \"그 애 아비로서의 나 자신을 위한 공정으로—그 애를 위한 공정이 아니라—나는 너에게 묻는 바다, 버린더 아가씨, 고드프리 에이블화이트에게 무슨 불만이 있느냐?\"\n\n여기서 브러프 씨가 처음으로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"당신은 그 물음에 답할 의무가 없습니다.\" 하고 그가 레이철에게 말했다.\n\n노 에이블화이트 씨가 대뜸 그에게 달려들었다.\n\n\"잊지 마시오, 선생.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은 여기 스스로 자청한 손님이오. 당신의 끼어듦은 그것이 청해질 때까지 기다렸더라면 더 볼품 있게 왔을 거요.\"\n\n브러프 씨는 아랑곳하지 않았다. 그의 그 사악한 늙은 얼굴의 매끄러운 겉치레는 도무지 금이 가지 않았다. 레이철은 그가 자기에게 준 조언에 감사하고는, 노 에이블화이트 씨에게로 돌아섰다—(그 아이의 나이와 성을 헤아리면) 그저 소름 끼치는 방식으로 제 평온을 지키면서.\n\n\"당신 아들이 저에게 방금 당신이 하신 그 똑같은 물음을 던졌어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"저는 그에게 줄 대답이 하나뿐이었고, 당신에게 줄 대답도 하나뿐이에요. 저는 우리가 서로를 놓아주자고 제안했어요, 왜냐하면 곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에, 성급한 약속을 물리고, 그가 다른 데서 제 선택을 할 수 있도록 자유롭게 놓아주는 것이, 그의 안녕과 저의 안녕을 가장 잘 살피는 길이라고 확신하게 되었기 때문이에요.\"\n\n\"내 아들이 무엇을 했느냐?\" 하고 에이블화이트 씨가 우겼다. \"나는 그것을 알 권리가 있다. 내 아들이 무엇을 했느냐?\"\n\n그 아이도 제 편에서 꼭 그만큼 고집스럽게 우겼다.\n\n\"당신에게든, 그에게든, 제가 드릴 필요가 있다고 여기는 유일한 해명을 당신은 들으셨어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 대답했다.\n\n\"쉬운 말로 하면, 내 아들을 차는 것이 너의 지엄하신 뜻이자 마음이렷다, 버린더 아가씨?\"\n\n레이철은 한순간 잠잠했다. 그 아이 바로 뒤에 앉아, 나는 그 아이가 한숨짓는 소리를 들었다. 브러프 씨가 그 아이의 손을 잡고, 그것을 살짝 쥐어 주었다. 그 아이는 스스로를 추스르고, 그 어느 때보다 대담하게 에이블화이트 씨에게 대답했다.\n\n\"저는 그보다 더 고약한 오해에 저를 내맡겨 왔어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 그것을 참을성 있게 견뎌 왔고요. 당신이 저를 차는 여자라 불러 저를 굴욕스럽게 할 수 있던 때는 이미 지났어요.\"\n\n그 아이는 어조에 쓰라림을 담아 말했는데, 그것이 나로 하여금 월장석의 추문이 어떤 식으로 그 아이의 마음에 떠올랐음을 납득하게 했다. \"저는 더 할 말이 없어요.\" 하고 그 아이가, 딱히 누구에게랄 것 없이, 그리고 우리 모두에게서 눈길을 돌려, 저에게 가장 가까운 창밖을 바라보며, 지친 듯 덧붙였다.\n\n에이블화이트 씨는 일어서서, 제 의자를 어찌나 사납게 밀쳐 냈던지 그것이 넘어가 바닥에 쓰러졌다.\n\n\"내 편에서 더 할 말이 있다.\" 하고 그가, 제 손바닥을 탁자에 쾅 내리치며 알렸다. \"내 아들이 이 모욕을 느끼지 못한다면, 나는 느낀다고 말해야겠다!\"\n\n레이철은 흠칫하며, 별안간 놀라 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"모욕이라고요?\" 하고 그 아이가 되뇌었다. \"무슨 뜻이에요?\"\n\n\"모욕이다!\" 하고 에이블화이트 씨가 되풀이했다. \"나는 안다, 버린더 아가씨, 네가 내 아들에게 한 약속을 깨는 그 동기를! 나는 네가 그 몇 마디로 그것을 고백하기라도 한 것처럼 확실히 안다. 너의 그 저주받은 집안 자존심이 고드프리를 모욕하고 있다, 내가 네 이모와 혼인했을 때 나를 모욕했던 것처럼! 그녀의 집안이—그녀의 거지 같은 집안이—제 자리를 스스로 만들고 제 재산을 스스로 얻은 정직한 사내와 혼인했다는 이유로 그녀에게 등을 돌렸지. 나는 조상이 없었다. 나는 강도와 살인으로 먹고산 목 베는 악당 무리의 후손이 아니었어. 나는 에이블화이트 집안이 등에 걸칠 셔츠 한 장 없고 제 이름도 못 쓰던 때를 짚어 보일 수가 없었지. 하! 하! 내가 혼인했을 때 나는 헌캐슬 집안에는 어울릴 만큼 훌륭하지 못했다. 그런데 이제 막판에 이르니, 내 아들이 너에게는 어울릴 만큼 훌륭하지 못하구나. 나는 그것을, 내내, 의심했다. 너에게는 헌캐슬 피가 흐르고 있어, 젊은 아가씨! 나는 그것을 내내 의심했다.\"\n\n\"아주 못난 의심이군요.\" 하고 브러프 씨가 짚었다. \"당신이 그것을 인정할 배짱이 있다니 놀랍습니다.\"\n\n에이블화이트 씨가 대답할 말을 찾기도 전에, 레이철이 더없이 부아 돋우는 경멸의 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"분명,\" 하고 그 아이가 그 변호사에게 말했다. \"이건 상대할 가치도 없어요. 저이가 그런 식으로 생각할 수 있다면, 저이가 좋을 대로 생각하도록 내버려 둬요.\"\n\n진홍빛에서, 에이블화이트 씨는 이제 자줏빛이 되어 가고 있었다. 그는 숨을 헐떡였다. 그는 레이철에서 브러프 씨로, 앞뒤로, 그 둘 다에게 어찌나 격분해 미쳐 날뛰던지 누구를 먼저 공격해야 할지 몰랐다. 그의 아내는, 이때까지 뚫을 수 없이 부채질하며 앉아 있다가, 놀라기 시작해, 아주 부질없게도, 그를 진정시키려 했다. 나는, 이 괴로운 면담 내내, 몇 마디 간곡한 말로 끼어들라는 안으로부터의 부름을 한 번 이상 느꼈고, 있을 수 있는 결과에 대한 두려움 아래 나 자신을 억눌러 왔는데, 그것은 비열하게 신중한 것이 아니라 도덕적으로 옳은 것을 바라보는 기독교인 영국 여자에게는 아주 못난 노릇이었다. 이제 일이 이른 그 지점에서, 나는 한낱 편의의 온갖 헤아림 위로 높이 일어섰다. 만일 내가 나의 미천한 궁리로 무슨 훈계를 끼워 넣으려 생각했다면, 나는 어쩌면 여전히 망설였을지 모른다. 허나 이제 나를 마주한 그 괴로운 집안의 위급함은, 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 서신집—'가정의 평화'에 관한 천한 번째 편지—속에 더없이 놀랍고 아름답게 마련되어 있었다. 나는 나의 수수한 한구석에서 일어섰고, 나의 소중한 책을 펼쳤다.\n\n\"사랑하는 에이블화이트 씨,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"한마디만요!\"\n\n내가 일어섬으로써 처음 그 자리에 있는 이들의 주의를 끌었을 때, 나는 그가 나에게 무슨 무례한 말을 하려는 참임을 알 수 있었다. 나의 자매다운 부름의 형식이 그를 멈춰 세웠다. 그는 이교도 같은 놀라움으로 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다.\n\n\"다정하게 잘되기를 비는 이이자 벗으로서,\" 하고 나는 이어 갔다. \"그리고 오래도록 타인들을 일깨우고, 납득시키고, 준비시키고, 깨우치고, 굳세게 하는 데 익은 이로서, 온갖 무례 가운데 가장 용서받을 만한 무례를—당신의 마음을 가라앉히는 무례를 저지르게 해 주세요.\"\n\n그는 정신을 차리기 시작했다. 그는 터뜨릴 참이었다—다른 누구에게라면 그는 터뜨렸으리라. 허나 나의 목소리는 (습관적으로 부드럽지만) 위급할 때는, 얼마쯤 높은 음을 지니고 있다. 이 위급함에서, 나는 그 둘 가운데 더 높은 목소리를 내라는 절박한 부름을 받은 느낌이었다.\n\n나는 나의 소중한 책을 그의 앞에 들어 올렸다. 나는 펼쳐진 쪽을 나의 집게손가락으로 인상 깊게 톡톡 두드렸다. \"나의 말이 아니에요!\" 하고 나는 뜨거운 훼방의 폭발로 외쳤다. \"오, 이 미천한 나의 말에 주의를 청한다고 여기지 마세요! 광야의 만나예요, 에이블화이트 씨! 메마른 땅 위의 이슬이에요! 위안의 말, 지혜의 말, 사랑의 말—제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 복되고, 복되고, 복된 말이에요!\"\n\n나는 거기서 한순간 숨이 막혀 멈추었다. 내가 정신을 차리기도 전에, 인간의 모습을 한 이 괴물이 격분해 소리쳤다.\n\n\"제인 앤 스탬퍼 양은 ——이나 먹으라지!\"\n\n여기서 빈칸으로 나타낸 그 끔찍한 말을 나로서는 쓸 수가 없다. 나는 그것이 그의 입술을 지날 때 비명을 질렀다. 나는 곁상 위의 내 조그만 가방으로 날아갔다. 나는 내 소책자를 죄다 털어냈다. 나는 불경한 욕설에 관한 그 한 특정한 소책자, '제발, 조용히 하세요!'라는 제목의 그것을 붙잡았다. 나는 그것을 애타는 애원의 낯빛으로 그에게 건넸다. 그는 그것을 둘로 찢어, 탁자 너머로 내게 도로 던졌다. 나머지 사람들이 다음에 무슨 일이 일어날지 몰라, 놀라 일어섰다. 나는 대뜸 다시 내 구석에 앉았다. 예전에, 얼마쯤 비슷한 정황에서, 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양이 두 어깨를 잡힌 채 어느 방에서 쫓겨난 일이 한 번 있었다. 나는, 그녀의 정신에 힘입어, 그녀의 순교의 되풀이를 기다렸다.\n\n허나 아니—그렇게 되지는 않았다. 그가 다음으로 말을 건넨 것은 제 아내였다. \"누가—누가—누가,\" 하고 그가 격분에 말을 더듬으며 말했다. \"누가 이 뻔뻔한 광신자를 집에 들였소? 당신이오?\"\n\n에이블화이트 이모가 한마디 하기도 전에, 레이철이 그녀를 대신해 대답했다.\n\n\"클랙 양은 여기,\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"제 손님으로 있어요.\"\n\n그 말이 에이블화이트 씨에게 유별난 효과를 냈다. 그것은 별안간 그를, 벌겋게 달아오른 분노의 상태에 있던 사내에서, 얼음처럼 싸늘한 경멸의 상태에 있는 사내로 바꾸어 놓았다. 레이철이 무언가—그 아이의 대답이 짧고 명백했음에도—마침내 그로 하여금 그 아이보다 우위에 서게 하는 무언가를 말했음이 모두에게 뻔했다.\n\n\"오?\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"클랙 양이 여기 너의 손님으로 있다—나의 집에?\"\n\n이번에는 레이철이 성미를 잃을 차례였다. 그 아이의 낯빛이 달아올랐고, 그 아이의 눈이 사납게 빛났다. 그 아이는 그 변호사에게 돌아서서, 에이블화이트 씨를 가리키며, 거만하게 물었다. \"저이가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말이죠?\"\n\n브러프 씨가 세 번째로 끼어들었다.\n\n\"당신은 잊으신 듯합니다.\" 하고 그가 에이블화이트 씨에게 말했다. \"당신이 이 집을 버린더 아가씨의 후견인으로서, 버린더 아가씨가 쓰도록 얻었다는 것을요.\"\n\n\"그렇게 빨리는 안 되지.\" 하고 에이블화이트 씨가 끼어들었다. \"나에게는 마지막 할 말이 있소, 얼마 전에 했어야 할 말인데, 만일 이——\" 그는 나를 나더러 무슨 얄미운 이름으로 부를까 궁리하며 바라보았다—\"만일 이 설쳐대는 노처녀가 우리를 훼방하지 않았더라면 말이오. 당신께 알려 드리는 바요, 선생, 만일 내 아들이 버린더 아가씨의 남편이 될 만큼 훌륭하지 못하다면, 나는 감히 그 아비가 버린더 아가씨의 후견인이 될 만큼 훌륭하다고 여길 수가 없소. 부디, 이렇게 이해하시오, 나는 레이디 버린더의 유언장이 나에게 제시한 그 지위를 받기를 거부하겠소. 당신네 법률 용어로, 나는 직무를 맡기를 사양하오. 이 집은 마땅히 내 이름으로 얻어졌소. 나는 그것의 온전한 책임을 내 어깨에 지겠소. 이건 내 집이오. 나는 그것을 간직하든, 세놓든, 내 좋을 대로 할 수 있소. 나는 버린더 아가씨를 서두르게 할 뜻이 없소. 오히려, 나는 그녀에게 청하는 바요, 그녀의 온전한 편의에 따라, 그녀의 손님과 그녀의 짐을 치워 달라고.\" 그는 깊이 고개 숙여 절하고는, 방을 나갔다.\n\n그것이, 제 아들과 혼인하기를 마다한 데 대한, 레이철에 대한 에이블화이트 씨의 복수였다!\n\n문이 닫히는 순간, 에이블화이트 이모가 우리 모두를 잠잠하게 하는 현상을 드러내 보였다. 그녀는 방을 가로지를 만큼의 기운을 부여받게 된 것이다!\n\n\"얘야,\" 하고 그녀가 레이철의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"저것이 네게 말한 것이 그이 자신이 아니라 그이의 성미라는 것을 내가 몰랐다면, 나는 내 남편이 부끄러웠을 게다. 당신은,\" 하고 에이블화이트 이모가, 이번에는 팔다리가 아니라 눈빛에 또 한 번의 기운을 담아, 내 구석의 나를 향해 돌아서며 이어 갔다. \"당신은 그이를 성나게 한 그 심술궂은 사람이에요. 나는 당신도 당신의 소책자도 다시는 안 봤으면 좋겠어요.\" 그녀는 레이철에게로 돌아가 그 아이에게 입 맞추었다. \"미안하다, 얘야,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"내 남편을 대신해서. 내가 너를 위해 무엇을 해 줄까?\"\n\n무엇에서나 한결같이 심술궂고—제 삶의 온갖 행동에서 변덕스럽고 불합리한—레이철이 그 흔해 빠진 말에 눈물을 쏟으며 녹아, 말없이 제 이모의 입맞춤에 응했다.\n\n\"제가 버린더 아가씨를 대신해 답해도 된다면,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. \"에이블화이트 부인, 페넬로피를 아래로 보내 그 여주인의 보닛과 숄을 가져오게 해 주시겠습니까? 우리 둘이 십 분만 있게 해 주세요.\" 하고 그가 더 낮은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그러면 제가 일들을, 레이철뿐만 아니라 당신도 흡족하게, 바로잡으리라 믿으셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n이 사내에 대한 그 집안의 믿음은 보기에 놀라운 것이었다. 제 편에서 더 말 한마디 없이, 에이블화이트 이모는 방을 떠났다.\n\n\"아!\" 하고 브러프 씨가, 그녀를 눈으로 좇으며 말했다. \"헌캐슬 피에는 흠이 있지요, 인정합니다. 하지만 좋은 혈통에는 결국 무언가가 있긴 있어요!\"\n\n그 순전히 세속적인 소견을 내고 나서, 그는, 마치 내가 가기를 기대하는 듯, 내 구석을 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 레이철에 대한 나의 관심—그의 것보다 한없이 더 높은 관심—이 나를 내 의자에 붙들어 매었다.\n\n브러프 씨는, 몬터규 스퀘어의 버린더 이모님 댁에서 그랬던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그것을 단념했다. 그는 레이철을 창가의 의자로 이끌어, 거기서 그 아이에게 말했다.\n\n\"나의 사랑하는 젊은 아가씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"에이블화이트 씨의 처신은 자연히 당신을 충격에 빠뜨리고, 놀라게 했어요. 만일 그런 사내와 그 문제를 다툴 만한 가치가 있다면, 우리는 이내 그에게 그가 만사를 제 뜻대로 할 수는 없다는 것을 보여 줄 수 있을 겁니다. 하지만 그럴 만한 가치가 없어요. 당신이 방금 한 말은 아주 옳았어요. 그는 우리가 상대할 가치도 없어요.\"\n\n그는 멈추고, 내 구석을 둘러보았다. 나는 소책자를 팔꿈치에 두고 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양을 무릎에 얹은 채, 도무지 꿈쩍 않고 거기 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"당신도 알잖아요,\" 하고 그가, 다시 레이철에게로 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"제 둘레의 사람들에게서 언제나 가장 좋은 점을 보고, 결코 가장 나쁜 점은 보지 않는 것이 당신의 가엾은 어머니의 고운 본성의 일부였다는 것을요. 그녀는 형부를 당신의 후견인으로 지명했는데, 그를 믿었기 때문이고, 그것이 제 언니를 기쁘게 하리라 여겼기 때문이지요. 나 자신은 여태껏 에이블화이트 씨를 좋아한 적이 없었고, 나는 당신 어머니를 설득해, 어떤 사건이 생길 경우, 새 후견인의 임명에 대해 나와 상의할 권한을 그녀의 유언 집행인들에게 부여하는 조항을 유언장에 넣게 했지요. 그 사건들 가운데 하나가 오늘 일어났어요. 그리고 나는, 이 온갖 무미건조한 사무적 세부를, 부디 기분 좋게, 내 아내의 전언과 함께 끝맺을 수 있는 처지에 있게 되었고요. 브러프 부인의 손님이 되어 주는 영광을 베풀어 주겠어요? 그리고 우리 슬기로운 사람들이 머리를 맞대고, 다음에 무엇을 할지 정할 때까지, 내 지붕 아래 머물러, 내 식구 가운데 하나가 되어 주겠어요?\"\n\n그 말에, 나는 끼어들려고 일어섰다. 브러프 씨는, 에이블화이트 부인에게 레이철의 보닛과 숄을 청했을 때 내가 그가 하리라 두려워했던 바로 그것을 한 것이었다.\n\n내가 한마디 끼워 넣기도 전에, 레이철이 더없이 따뜻한 말로 그의 초대를 받아들였다. 만일 내가 그들 사이에 그렇게 맺어진 그 약정이 이루어지도록 놓아둔다면—만일 그 아이가 한번 브러프 씨의 문지방을 넘는다면—내 삶의 가장 애틋한 희망, 나의 길 잃은 양을 우리로 도로 데려오려는 그 희망은 안녕이었다! 이런 참사라는 생각만으로도 나는 아주 압도되었다. 나는 세속적 신중함이라는 그 비참한 굴레를 바람에 내던지고, 나를 채운 그 뜨거움으로, 처음 떠오르는 말로 말했다.\n\n\"멈춰요!\" 하고 나는 말했다—\"멈춰요! 제 말을 들어야 해요. 브러프 씨! 당신은 그 아이의 친척이 아니고, 저는 친척이에요. 제가 그 아이를 청해요—저는 유언 집행인들에게 저를 후견인으로 임명하라 요구해요. 레이철, 더없이 사랑하는 레이철, 저는 너에게 나의 수수한 집을 내주마. 다음 기차로 런던으로 오렴, 얘야, 그리고 그것을 나와 함께 나누자!\"\n\n브러프 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 레이철은, 감추려는 애조차 하지 않는 모진 놀라움으로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"참 친절하시네요, 드루실라.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"저는 런던에 있게 될 때마다 당신을 찾아뵙기를 바랄게요. 하지만 저는 브러프 씨의 초대를 받아들였고, 당분간은, 제가 브러프 씨의 보살핌 아래 머무는 것이 가장 좋으리라 생각해요.\"\n\n\"오, 그런 말 말아라!\" 하고 나는 간청했다. \"나는 너와 헤어질 수 없어, 레이철—나는 너와 헤어질 수 없어!\"\n\n나는 그 아이를 내 품에 안으려 했다. 허나 그 아이는 물러섰다. 나의 뜨거움은 옮겨 가지 않았다. 그것은 그저 그 아이를 놀라게 했을 뿐이다.\n\n\"분명,\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"이건 아주 부질없는 흥분의 과시죠? 저는 이해가 안 돼요.\"\n\n\"나도 마찬가지예요.\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다.\n\n그들의 모짊이—그들의 소름 끼치는, 세속적인 모짊이—나를 역겹게 했다.\n\n\"오, 레이철! 레이철!\" 하고 나는 터뜨렸다. \"너는 아직도 못 봤니, 나의 마음이 너를 기독교인으로 만들고자 애타는 것을? 죽음이 네 사랑하는 어머니를 내 손에서 낚아채 갔을 때 내가 그분을 위해 하려던 것을, 내가 너를 위해 하려 애쓰고 있다고, 어떤 내면의 목소리도 너에게 일러 주지 않았니?\"\n\n레이철이 한 걸음 더 가까이 나아와, 나를 아주 이상하게 바라보았다.\n\n\"저는 제 어머니에 대한 당신의 언급을 이해할 수 없어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"클랙 양, 부디 당신 뜻을 설명해 주시겠어요?\"\n\n내가 대답하기도 전에, 브러프 씨가 앞으로 나와, 레이철에게 제 팔을 내밀며, 그 아이를 방 밖으로 이끌어 내려 했다.\n\n\"당신은 그 화제를 더 파고들지 않는 편이 낫습니다, 얘야.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 클랙 양도 제 뜻을 설명하지 않는 편이 낫고요.\"\n\n만일 내가 나무토막이나 돌덩이였다면, 이런 끼어듦은 분명 나를 일깨워 진실을 증언하게 했으리라. 나는 나의 손으로 브러프 씨를 분개하여 밀쳐 내고, 엄숙하고 알맞은 말로, 채비 없이 죽는다는 그 끔찍한 참사를 건전한 교리가 서슴없이 여기는 그 견해를 밝혔다.\n\n레이철이 나에게서 흠칫 물러섰다—적기가 낯 뜨겁거니와—소름 끼치는 비명과 함께.\n\n\"가요!\" 하고 그 아이가 브러프 씨에게 말했다. \"가요, 제발, 저 여자가 더 말할 수 있기 전에요! 오, 제 가엾은 어머니의 해롭잖고, 쓸모 있고, 아름다운 삶을 생각해요! 당신은 장례에 있었죠, 브러프 씨. 당신은 모두가 그분을 얼마나 사랑했는지 보았죠. 당신은 그 가엾고 힘없는 사람들이 제 가장 좋은 벗을 잃은 것을 두고 그분 무덤에서 우는 것을 보았죠. 그런데 저 몹쓸 사람이 저기 서서, 이 땅 위의 천사였던 제 어머니가 지금 천국의 천사인지를 제가 의심하게 만들려 해요! 그것에 대해 이야기하느라 멈추지 말아요! 가요! 저 여자와 같은 공기를 마시는 게 저를 숨 막히게 해요! 우리가 같은 방에 함께 있다는 걸 느끼는 게 저를 겁나게 해요!\"\n\n온갖 훈계에 귀먹은 채, 그 아이는 문으로 달려갔다.\n\n바로 그 순간, 그 아이의 하녀가 그 아이의 보닛과 숄을 들고 들어왔다. 그 아이는 그것들을 아무렇게나 걸쳤다. \"제 짐을 꾸려요,\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"그리고 그것들을 브러프 씨 댁으로 가져와요.\" 나는 그 아이에게 다가가려 했다—나는 충격받고 마음 아팠으나, 다시 말할 것도 없이, 비위가 상하지는 않았다. 나는 그저 그 아이에게 말하고 싶었을 뿐이다, \"부디 너의 모진 마음이 누그러지기를! 나는 기꺼이 너를 용서한다!\" 그 아이는 제 베일을 끌어내리고, 내 손에서 제 숄을 홱 잡아채고는, 서둘러 나가며, 내 얼굴에 대고 문을 닫았다. 나는 여느 때의 꿋꿋함으로 그 모욕을 견뎠다. 나는 그것을 지금 온갖 비위 상하는 느낌을 넘어서는 여느 때의 우월함으로 떠올린다.\n\n브러프 씨도, 제 차례로 서둘러 나가기 전에, 나에게 건넬 작별의 조롱의 말이 있었다.\n\n\"당신은 제 뜻을 설명하지 않는 편이 나았어요, 클랙 양.\" 하고 그가 말하고는, 고개 숙여 절하고, 방을 나갔다.\n\n그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 뒤따랐다.\n\n\"저들을 다 서로 아웅다웅하게 만든 게 누군지는 뻔히 보이네요.\" 하고 그 여자가 말했다. \"저는 그저 가엾은 하녀일 뿐이지만—당신이 부끄럽다고 선언해요!\" 그 여자도 나가, 제 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫았다.\n\n나는 그 방에 혼자 남겨졌다. 그들 모두에게 욕먹고, 그들 모두에게 버림받고, 나는 그 방에 혼자 남겨졌다.\n\n이 담담한 사실 진술에—세상에 박해받는 한 기독교인의 이 가슴 뭉클한 그림에—덧붙일 것이 더 있을까? 아니! 나의 일기가 내게, 나의 삶의 그 숱한 얼룩덜룩한 장들 가운데 또 하나가 여기서 끝난다는 것을 일깨운다. 그날 이후로, 나는 레이철 버린더를 다시 보지 못했다. 그 아이는 나를 모욕한 그때 나의 용서를 받았다. 그 아이는 그 뒤로 죽 나의 기도 어린 축복을 받아 왔다. 그리고 내가 죽을 때—악을 선으로 갚는 나의 편의 그 되갚음을 완성하고자—그 아이는 나의 유언에 의해 「제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 생애, 편지, 그리고 노고」를 유산으로 남겨 받을 것이다.\n\n# SECOND NARRATIVE.\n\n그레이스 인 스퀘어의 사무 변호사, 매슈 브러프가 기고함.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n나의 어여쁜 벗 클랙 양이 붓을 내려놓았으니, 다음으로 내가 내 차례로 그것을 집어 드는 데는 두 가지 이유가 있다.\n\n첫째로, 나는 여태껏 어둠 속에 남겨져 온 몇몇 흥미로운 점에 필요한 빛을 비출 처지에 있다. 버린더 아가씨에게는 제 혼약을 깨뜨릴 저 나름의 남모를 이유가 있었다—그리고 내가 그 밑바탕에 있었다. 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게는 제 매혹적인 사촌 누이의 손에 대한 온갖 청구를 물릴 저 나름의 남모를 이유가 있었다—그리고 나는 그것이 무엇인지 알아냈다.\n\n둘째로, 내가 지금 쓰고 있는 그때에, 나 자신이 그 인도 다이아몬드의 수수께끼에 몸소 얽혀들게 된 것이 나의 행운이었는지 불운이었는지, 나는 좀처럼 모르겠다. 나는 나 자신의 사무소에서, 품위 있는 태도를 지닌 어느 동양 낯선 이와 면담하는 영광을 누렸는데, 그는 의심할 것 없이, 다름 아닌 그 인도인 셋의 우두머리였다. 여기에 덧붙이자면, 나는 그 이튿날 이름난 여행가 머스웨이트 씨를 만났고, 그와 월장석이라는 화제에 대해 대화를 나누었는데, 그것은 나중의 일들에 아주 중요하게 관련된다. 그리고 그것으로 여러분은, 내가 이 쪽들에서 차지하는 자리를 채우는 데 대한 나의 자격의 진술을 갖게 되는 셈이다.\n\n깨진 혼약의 참된 이야기가 시간상 먼저 오니, 따라서 그것이 지금의 이야기에서 첫 자리를 차지해야 한다. 사건들의 사슬을 따라, 한쪽 끝에서 다른 쪽 끝으로, 내 길을 되짚어 가다 보니, 나는, 여러분이 생각하기에 참으로 이상하게도, 나의 훌륭한 의뢰인이자 벗인 고(故) 존 버린더 경의 병상에서 그 장면을 열 필요를 느낀다.\n\n존 경은 인간에게 딸린 약점 가운데 더 해롭잖고 상냥한 것들을 제 몫으로—어쩌면 다소 큰 몫으로—지니고 있었다. 그중에서, 지금 다루는 일에 해당하는 것으로 내가 언급할 만한 것은, 그가—여느 때의 건강을 누리는 동안에는—제 유언장을 만드는 책임을 마주하기를 이길 수 없이 꺼렸다는 것이다. 레이디 버린더가 이 일에서 그를 일깨워 의무를 자각하게 하려고 제 영향력을 발휘했다. 그리고 나도 내 영향력을 발휘했다. 그는 우리 견해의 옳음을 인정했다—허나 결국 그를 무덤으로 데려간 그 병에 걸린 자신을 발견하기 전까지는, 그 이상으로 나아가지 않았다. 그때에야, 마침내, 나는 유언장이라는 화제에 대한 내 의뢰인의 지시를 받으러 불려 갔다. 그것은 내 직업 이력 전체에서 여태껏 받아 본 가장 단순한 지시로 드러났다.\n\n내가 방에 들어섰을 때 존 경은 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다. 그는 나를 보고 정신을 차렸다.\n\n\"안녕하시오, 브러프 씨?\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이 일에 그리 오래 걸리지는 않을 거요. 그러고 나면 나는 다시 잠들겠소.\" 그는 내가 펜과 잉크와 종이를 챙기는 동안 큰 관심을 가지고 지켜보았다. \"준비됐소?\" 하고 그가 물었다. 나는 고개 숙여 인사하고 잉크를 찍고는, 그의 지시를 기다렸다.\n\n\"모든 것을 내 아내에게 남기오.\" 하고 존 경이 말했다. \"그게 다요.\" 그는 베개 위에서 돌아누워, 다시 잠들 채비를 했다.\n\n나는 그를 방해할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"이렇게 이해해야 합니까,\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"경께서 돌아가실 때 지니고 계신, 온갖 종류와 종별의 재산 전부를, 온전히 레이디 버린더께 남기신다고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\" 하고 존 경이 말했다. \"다만, 나는 그것을 더 짧게 말했을 뿐이오. 당신은 어째서 그것을 더 짧게 말하고, 나를 다시 잠들게 해 주지 못하오? 모든 것을 내 아내에게. 그것이 나의 유언이오.\"\n\n그의 재산은 온전히 그 자신의 뜻대로 처분할 수 있는 것이었고, 두 가지 종류였다. 토지 재산(나는 일부러 전문 용어를 쓰기를 삼간다)과, 금전 재산이었다. 태반의 경우에, 나는 내 의뢰인에게 유언장을 다시 헤아려 보라 청하는 것이 그에 대한 나의 의무라고 느꼈으리라. 존 경의 경우에는, 나는 레이디 버린더가, 그 남편이 그녀에게 둔 그 아낌없는 신뢰에 값할 만할 (모든 좋은 아내가 그럴 만하다) 뿐만 아니라, 신탁을 제대로 운영할 능력도 있음을 (내가 여자들을 겪은 바로는, 그럴 능력이 있는 이는 천에 하나도 없다) 알고 있었다. 십 분 만에, 존 경의 유언장은 작성되고, 집행되었으며, 존 경 그 자신은, 착한 이여, 그 끊긴 선잠을 마저 자고 있었다.\n\n레이디 버린더는 그 남편이 그녀에게 둔 그 믿음에 넉넉히 값했다. 과부가 된 첫 며칠에, 그녀는 나를 불러, 제 유언장을 만들었다. 그녀가 제 처지에 대해 취한 견해가 어찌나 철저히 건전하고 분별 있던지, 나는 그녀에게 조언할 온갖 필요에서 벗어났다. 나의 책임은 그녀의 지시를 알맞은 법적 형식으로 다듬는 데서 시작되어 거기서 끝났다. 존 경이 무덤에 든 지 채 두 주도 되기 전에, 그 딸의 앞날이 더없이 슬기롭고 더없이 다정하게 마련되었다.\n\n그 유언장은 내 사무소의 방화 상자 안에, 내가 헤아려 보고 싶지 않을 만큼 여러 해 동안 머물러 있었다. 내가 다시 그것을 들여다볼 일을 찾은 것은, 아주 서글픈 정황 아래, 1848년 여름이 되어서였다.\n\n내가 언급한 그 시기에, 의사들은 가엾은 레이디 버린더에게 선고를 내렸는데, 그것은 문자 그대로 죽음의 선고였다. 나는 그녀가 제 처지를 알린 첫 사람이었다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 나와 다시 제 유언장을 살펴보고 싶어 안달임을 알았다.\n\n그 딸에 관한 조항들은 손볼 수가 없었다. 허나, 세월이 흐르며, 여러 친척에게 남긴 몇몇 자잘한 유산에 관한 그녀의 뜻이 얼마쯤 바뀌었다. 그리하여 원래 문서에 서너 개의 부칙을 덧붙일 필요가 생겼다. 만일의 사고를 염려하여 이것을 대뜸 해 놓고 나서, 나는 그녀의 최근 지시를 두 번째 유언장에 담아도 된다는 마님의 허락을 얻었다. 나의 목적은, 이제 원래 문서를 볼썽사납게 하는, 그리고, 진실을 인정하자면, 사물의 알맞음에 대한 나의 직업적 감각을 서글피 거스르는, 어떤 피할 수 없는 혼란과 되풀이를 없애는 것이었다.\n\n이 두 번째 유언장의 집행은 클랙 양이 묘사했는데, 그녀는 친절하게도 그것을 증언해 주었다. 레이철 버린더의 금전적 이익에 관한 한, 그것은, 한 자 한 자, 첫 번째 유언장의 꼭 그대로의 짝이었다. 들여온 유일한 변경은 후견인의 임명과, 그 임명에 관한 어떤 조항들—나의 조언 아래 만들어진 것들—에 관련되었다. 레이디 버린더가 죽자, 그 유언장은 여느 방식대로 (그 말마따나) \"검인\"되도록 나의 검인 대리인의 손에 놓였다.\n\n그때로부터 세 주쯤 지나—내가 기억할 수 있는 한—무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 겉면 아래 진행되고 있다는 첫 경고가 내게 다다랐다. 나는 마침 내 벗인 그 검인 대리인의 사무소에 들렀는데, 나는 그가 여느 때보다 더 큰 관심의 낯빛으로 나를 맞는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n\"당신께 드릴 소식이 좀 있습니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"제가 오늘 아침 닥터스 코먼스에서 무슨 소리를 들었는지 아세요? 레이디 버린더의 유언장이 벌써 조회되고, 열람되었답니다!\"\n\n이것은 참으로 소식이었다! 그 유언장에는 다툴 만한 것이라곤 도무지 아무것도 없었다. 그리고 그것을 열람하는 데 조금이라도 관심이 있을 만한 사람으로 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 이는 아무도 없었다. (아직 그것을 모르는 몇몇 사람을 위해, 법이 모든 유언장을 닥터스 코먼스에서, 일 실링의 수수료를 내면 누구든 신청하는 이가 열람할 수 있게 허용한다는 것을 여기서 설명해 두는 것이 좋겠다.)\n\n\"누가 그 유언장을 조회했는지 들었소?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"네. 서기가 나에게 서슴없이 말해 주더군요. 스킵 앤드 스몰리 회사의 스몰리 씨가 그것을 조회했답니다. 그 유언장은 아직 그 큰 이절판 등록부에 옮겨 적히지 않았지요. 그래서 여느 절차에서 벗어나, 그에게 원래 문서를 보여 주는 수밖에 없었답니다. 그는 그것을 꼼꼼히 훑어보고, 제 수첩에 무언가 적었다는군요. 그가 그것으로 무엇을 하려는지 짐작이 가세요?\"\n\n나는 고개를 저었다. \"알아내겠소.\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"하루가 더 가기 전에.\" 그 말과 함께 나는 대뜸 나 자신의 사무소로 돌아갔다.\n\n만일 다른 어떤 사무 변호사 회사가 나의 죽은 의뢰인의 유언장에 대한 이 영문 모를 열람에 관련되었다면, 나는 필요한 발견을 하는 데 얼마쯤 어려움을 겪었을지 모른다. 허나 나는 스킵 앤드 스몰리에 대해, 이 일에서 나의 행보를 견주어 손쉽게 만드는 어떤 쥔 것이 있었다. 나의 관습법 서기(더없이 유능하고 훌륭한 사람)가 스몰리 씨의 형제였다. 그리고 나와의 이런 종류의 간접적 연줄 덕분에, 스킵 앤드 스몰리는, 지난 몇 해 동안, 여러 이유로 내가 맡을 값어치가 없다고 여긴, 내 사무소에 들어온 사건이라는 모양으로, 내 상에서 떨어지는 부스러기를 주워 왔다. 나의 직업적 후원이, 이런 식으로, 그 회사에 얼마쯤 중요했다. 나는, 필요하다면, 이번 경우에 그들에게 그 후원을 상기시킬 작정이었다.\n\n돌아오는 즉시 나는 내 서기에게 말했다. 그리고, 그에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 이른 뒤, 나는 그를 제 형제의 사무소로 보냈다, \"브러프 씨의 안부와 함께, 스킵 앤드 스몰리 씨들이 어째서 레이디 버린더의 유언장을 열람할 필요가 있다고 여겼는지 알고 싶다\"고.\n\n이 전언이 스몰리 씨를 제 형제와 함께 내 사무소로 데려왔다. 그는 어느 의뢰인에게서 받은 지시에 따라 움직였음을 인정했다. 그러고 나서 그는 내게, 더 말하는 것이 제 편에서 직업적 비밀을 저버리는 것이 아니겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n우리는 그것을 두고 날카로운 논쟁을 벌였다. 그가 옳았다, 의심할 것 없이. 그리고 내가 틀렸다. 사실인즉, 나는 화가 나고 미심쩍었다—그래서 나는 더 알기를 우겼다. 더 나쁘게도, 나는 내게 제시되는 어떤 추가 정보도 내가 간직하도록 맡겨진 비밀로 여기기를 마다했다. 나는 나 자신의 재량을 쓸 온전한 자유를 주장했다. 그보다 더 나쁘게도, 나는 내 처지를 부당하게 이용했다. \"고르시오, 선생,\" 하고 나는 스몰리 씨에게 말했다. \"당신 의뢰인의 일감을 잃을 위험과, 나의 일감을 잃을 위험 사이에서.\" 아주 변명할 여지가 없다, 인정하거니와—폭정의 행위요, 그 이하가 아니다. 다른 폭군들처럼, 나는 내 뜻을 관철했다. 스몰리 씨는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 제 선택지를 골랐다.\n\n그는 체념한 듯 미소 짓고, 제 의뢰인의 이름을 내주었다.\n\n고드프리 에이블화이트 씨였다.\n\n그것으로 내게는 넉넉했다—나는 더 알 필요가 없었다.\n\n나의 이야기에서 이 지점에 다다랐으니, 이제—레이디 버린더의 유언장에 관한 한—이 글줄들을 읽는 이를, 정보에 관해, 나 자신과 온전히 대등한 처지에 놓을 필요가 생긴다.\n\n그러면, 될 수 있는 대로 가장 적은 말로 밝히자면, 레이철 버린더는 그 재산에 대해 종신 수익권밖에 없었다. 그녀의 어머니의 훌륭한 분별과, 나의 오랜 경험이 어우러져, 그녀를 온갖 책임에서 벗어나게 하고, 앞으로 궁핍하고 파렴치한 어떤 사내의 희생물이 될 온갖 위험에서 그녀를 지켰다. 그녀도, 그녀의 남편도 (그녀가 혼인한다면), 토지 재산으로도, 금전 재산으로도, 육 펜스 한 푼 마련할 수 없었다. 그들은 런던과 요크셔의 집을 살 곳으로 갖게 되고, 두둑한 수입을 갖게 될 것이었다—그리고 그것이 전부였다.\n\n내가 알아낸 것을 곰곰이 헤아려 보게 되자, 나는 다음에 무엇을 할지 몹시 갈피를 못 잡았다.\n\n내가 (놀라움과 괴로움 속에서) 버린더 아가씨의 예정된 혼인에 대해 들은 지 채 한 주도 지나지 않은 터였다. 나는 그녀를 진심으로 감탄하고 아꼈다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 저를 내던지려 한다는 것을 들었을 때 이루 말할 수 없이 마음 아팠다. 그런데 이제, 여기, 그 사내가—내가 언제나 매끄러운 혀를 놀리는 사기꾼이라 믿어 온 그 사내가—내가 그에 대해 생각한 바로 그 가장 나쁜 것을 입증하며, 제 편에서 그 혼인의 잇속 밝은 목적을 뻔히 드러내고 있었던 것이다! 그래서 그것이 어떻다는 거냐?—여러분은 대꾸할지 모른다—그런 일은 날마다 일어난다고. 그렇다 치자, 나의 선생. 허나 만일 그 일이 (이를테면) 여러분 자신의 누이에게 벌어진다면, 여러분은 그것을 지금 여기는 만큼 가볍게 여기겠는가?\n\n이제 자연히 내게 떠오른 첫 헤아림은 이것이었다. 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 제 변호사가 저를 위해 알아낸 것 뒤에도, 제 약혼을 지킬 것인가?\n\n그것은 온전히 그의 금전적 처지에 달려 있었는데, 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다. 만일 그 처지가 절박한 것이 아니라면, 오직 그 수입만을 위해서라도 버린더 아가씨와 혼인하는 것이 그로서는 넉넉히 값어치 있는 일일 터였다. 만일, 다른 한편으로, 그가 정해진 때까지 큰돈을 마련해야 할 절박한 필요에 처해 있다면, 그때는 레이디 버린더의 유언장이 딱 그 경우에 들어맞아, 그 딸이 악당의 손에 떨어지는 것을 막아 줄 터였다.\n\n뒤엣경우라면, 내가 레이철 아가씨를, 제 어머니를 애도하는 첫 며칠에, 즉각적인 진실의 폭로로 괴롭힐 필요가 없을 터였다. 앞엣경우라면, 만일 내가 잠자코 있으면, 나는 그녀를 평생 비참하게 만들 혼인을 눈감아 주는 셈이 될 터였다.\n\n나의 망설임은, 에이블화이트 부인과 버린더 아가씨가 머물고 있음을 내가 아는 런던의 그 호텔에 들르는 것으로 끝났다. 그들은 내게, 이튿날 브라이턴으로 갈 것이며, 뜻밖의 걸림돌이 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그들과 동행하는 것을 막는다고 알려 주었다. 나는 대뜸 그의 자리를 대신하겠다고 제안했다. 내가 레이철 버린더를 생각하기만 할 때는, 망설이는 것이 가능했다. 내가 실제로 그녀를 보자, 나의 마음은 대뜸, 그것으로 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 그녀에게 진실을 말하기로 정해졌다.\n\n나는 도착한 이튿날, 그녀와 산책을 나갔을 때 나의 기회를 찾았다.\n\n\"당신에게 말해도 되겠소,\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"당신의 혼약에 대해?\"\n\n\"네,\" 하고 그녀가 무심하게 말했다. \"더 흥미로운 이야깃거리가 없으시다면요.\"\n\n\"당신 집안의 오랜 벗이자 하인을 용서해 주겠소, 레이철 아가씨, 만일 내가 감히, 당신의 마음이 이 혼인에 쏠려 있는지 물어본다면?\"\n\n\"저는 절망 속에서 혼인하는 거예요, 브러프 씨—저를 제 삶과 화해시켜 줄지 모를 어떤 종류의 정체된 행복에 빠져들 요행을 노리고요.\"\n\n센 말이었다! 그리고 어떤 사랑 이야기라는 모양으로, 겉면 아래 무언가가 있음을 넌지시 알려 주는 말이었다. 허나 나에게는 나 나름의 목적이 있었고, 나는 (우리 변호사들 말마따나) 그 물음을 그 곁가지들로 좇기를 마다했다.\n\n\"고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 좀처럼 당신과 같은 생각일 수 없겠구려.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그의 마음은 어쨌든 그 혼인에 쏠려 있음이 틀림없겠지요?\"\n\n\"그이는 그렇다고 하고, 저는 그이를 믿어야 마땅하겠죠. 제가 그이에게 털어놓은 것 뒤에도, 저를 아끼지 않는다면 그이는 좀처럼 저와 혼인하지 않을 테니까요.\"\n\n가엾은 것! 어떤 사내가 저 자신의 이기적이고 잇속 밝은 목적으로 그녀와 혼인한다는 생각만은 그녀의 머릿속에 도무지 들어온 적이 없었던 것이다. 내가 나 자신에게 지운 그 과업이 내가 어림했던 것보다 더 벅찬 과업으로 보이기 시작했다.\n\n\"이상하게 들리는구려,\" 하고 나는 이어 갔다. \"나의 구식 귀에는——\"\n\n\"뭐가 이상하게 들려요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"당신이 당신의 앞날의 남편에 대해, 마치 그의 애정의 진심을 아주 확신하지는 못하는 듯 말하는 것을 듣는 것이. 당신은 당신 자신의 마음속에서 그를 미심쩍어할 무슨 이유라도 자각하고 있소?\"\n\n그녀의 놀라운 지각의 예리함이, 내가 그 물음을 던질 때, 나의 목소리, 또는 나의 태도에 인 변화를 알아챘고, 그것이 그녀에게 내가 내내 어떤 숨은 목적을 품고 말해 왔음을 일깨웠다. 그녀는 멈추더니, 제 팔을 내 팔에서 빼고, 나의 얼굴을 살피듯 들여다보았다.\n\n\"브러프 씨,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 고드프리 에이블화이트에 대해 저에게 할 말이 있어요. 말해 줘요.\"\n\n나는 그녀의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들일 만큼 그녀를 잘 알았다. 나는 그것을 말했다.\n\n그녀는 제 팔을 다시 내 팔에 끼고, 나와 함께 천천히 걸어 나갔다. 나는 그녀의 손이 기계적으로 내 팔을 붙드는 힘을 조이는 것을 느꼈고, 내가 말을 이어 가는 동안 그녀가 점점 더 창백해지는 것을 보았다—허나, 내가 말하는 동안 그녀의 입술에서는 한마디도 나오지 않았다. 내가 말을 마쳤을 때도, 그녀는 여전히 침묵을 지켰다. 그녀의 머리가 조금 수그러졌고, 그녀는 내 존재도, 제 둘레의 모든 것도 의식하지 못한 채, 내 곁에서 걸었다. 제 자신의 생각에 잠긴—거의 파묻힌, 이라고 해도 되겠다.\n\n나는 그녀를 방해하려는 시도를 하지 않았다. 그녀의 성향에 대한 나의 경험이, 이번에도, 예전 경우들에서처럼, 그녀에게 시간을 주라고 내게 일러 주었다.\n\n여자들 일반이, 저희에게 흥미로운 무언가를 들었을 때의 첫 본능은, 숱한 물음을 던지고, 그런 다음 달려가, 어떤 마음에 드는 벗과 그것을 죄다 이야기하는 것이다. 비슷한 정황에서 레이철 버린더의 첫 본능은, 제 자신의 마음속에 틀어박혀, 저 혼자 그것을 곰곰이 헤아리는 것이었다. 이 온전한 자립은 사내에게는 큰 미덕이다. 여자에게 그것은, 그녀를 도덕적으로 제 성(性)의 무리에서 갈라놓고, 그리하여 그녀를 세상 일반의 견해에 의한 오해에 내맡기는, 심각한 흠을 지닌다. 나는 이 일에서 세상 나머지가 생각하는 대로 생각하는 나 자신을 강하게 의심한다—레이철 버린더의 경우만은 빼고. 그녀의 됨됨이에 있는 그 자립은, 나의 평가에서는, 그 미덕 가운데 하나였다. 얼마쯤은, 의심할 것 없이, 내가 그녀를 진심으로 감탄하고 좋아했기 때문이고, 얼마쯤은, 월장석의 분실에 대한 그녀의 관련에 대해 내가 취한 견해가 그녀의 성향에 대한 나 자신의 각별한 앎에 바탕을 두었기 때문이다. 그 다이아몬드 문제에서 겉모습이 아무리 고약해 보여도—그녀가 어떤 식으로든 발각되지 않은 도둑질의 수수께끼에 엮여 있음을 아는 것이 의심할 것 없이 아무리 충격적이어도—그럼에도 나는 그녀가 저답지 못한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았다고 흡족히 여겼는데, 나는 또한 그녀가 그 일에서 한 걸음도 움직이지 않되, 먼저 제 자신의 마음속에 틀어박혀, 그것을 곰곰이 헤아리지 않고서는 그리하지 않았다고 흡족히 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n우리는, 내가 말하기로 거의 일 마일을 걸어왔을 때에야, 레이철이 스스로를 추슬렀다. 그녀는 별안간 나를 올려다보았는데, 더 행복했던 시절의 미소가 희미하게 어렸다—내가 여태껏 여자의 얼굴에서 본 가장 거스를 수 없는 미소였다.\n\n\"저는 이미 당신의 친절에 많은 빚을 졌어요.\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 저는 이제 그 어느 때보다 더 깊이 그것에 빚졌음을 느껴요. 만일 당신이 런던에 돌아가서 제 혼인에 대한 어떤 소문이라도 듣거든, 제 이름을 내세워, 대뜸 그것을 반박해 줘요.\"\n\n\"당신은 당신의 약혼을 깨기로 결심했소?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"의심할 수 있어요?\" 하고 그녀가 자랑스레 되받았다. \"당신이 저에게 말해 준 것 뒤에!\"\n\n\"나의 사랑하는 레이철 아가씨, 당신은 아주 젊소—그리고 당신은 당신의 지금 처지에서 물러나는 데에 당신이 어림하는 것보다 더 큰 어려움을 겪을지 모르오. 당신에게는 아무도 없소—물론 부인 말이오—당신이 상의할 수 있는?\"\n\n\"아무도 없어요.\" 하고 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n그녀가 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣는 것이 나를 괴롭혔다, 정말로 나를 괴롭혔다. 그녀는 어찌나 젊고 어찌나 외로운지—그리고 그것을 어찌나 잘 견디는지! 그녀를 도우려는 충동이, 그 정황에서 내가 느꼈을 법한 나 자신의 부적격의 느낌을 이겼다. 그리고 나는 그 화제에 대해 그 순간 문득 떠오르는 생각들을 내 힘닿는 한 밝혔다. 나는 내 시절에 엄청난 수의 의뢰인에게 조언했고, 몇몇 지극히 껄끄러운 어려움을 다루어 왔다. 허나 이것은 젊은 아가씨에게 혼약에서 벗어날 방도를 조언하는 나 자신을 발견한 첫 경우였다. 내가 내놓은 제안은 간단히 이런 것이었다. 나는 그녀에게, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게—물론, 단둘의 면담에서—그가, 그녀가 확실히 아는 바로, 제 편의 그 잇속 밝은 동기의 본색을 드러냈다고 말하라 권했다. 그런 다음 그녀는, 그녀가 알아낸 것 뒤에, 그들의 혼인은 그저 있을 수 없는 일이라고 덧붙이고—그에게, 그녀의 견해에 따름으로써 그녀의 침묵을 확보하는 것이 가장 슬기롭다고 여기는지, 아니면 그것에 맞섬으로써 그녀로 하여금 그녀가 따르는 그 동기를 세상에 널리 알리게 하는 것이 가장 슬기롭다고 여기는지 물어야 했다. 만일 그가 저를 변호하려 하거나, 그 사실들을 부인하려 하면, 그녀는, 그 경우에, 그를 나에게 보내야 했다.\n\n버린더 아가씨는 내가 마칠 때까지 주의 깊게 들었다. 그런 다음 그녀는 나의 조언에 아주 어여쁘게 감사했으나, 동시에 그것을 따르기가 저로서는 있을 수 없는 일이라고 내게 알렸다.\n\n\"물어봐도 되겠소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그것을 따르는 데에 무슨 반대되는 점이 보이는지?\"\n\n그녀는 망설였다—그런 다음 제 편에서 물음으로 나를 맞았다.\n\n\"만일 당신이 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 처신에 대해 당신의 의견을 밝히라는 청을 받는다고 해 봐요.\" 하고 그녀가 말문을 열었다.\n\n\"그래서요?\"\n\n\"당신은 그것을 뭐라 부르겠어요?\"\n\n\"나는 그것을 비열하게 남을 속이는 사내의 처신이라 부르겠소.\"\n\n\"브러프 씨! 저는 그 사내를 믿었어요. 저는 그 사내와 혼인하겠다고 약속했고요. 그런데 어떻게 제가 그이에게 비열하다고 말하겠어요, 어떻게 그이가 저를 속였다고 말하겠어요, 어떻게 제가 그 뒤에 세상의 눈앞에서 그이를 망신시키겠어요? 저는 그이를 제 남편으로 생각한 것만으로도 저 자신을 격 떨어뜨렸어요. 만일 제가 당신이 그이에게 말하라는 것을 말한다면—저는 제가 저 자신을 그이의 면전에서 격 떨어뜨렸음을 인정하는 거예요. 저는 그럴 수 없어요. 우리 사이에 오간 것 뒤에, 저는 그럴 수 없어요! 그것의 수치는 그이에게는 아무것도 아니겠죠. 하지만 그것의 수치는 저로서는 견딜 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n여기 그녀의 됨됨이에 있는 그 두드러진 별난 점들 가운데 또 하나가 거리낌 없이 내게 저를 드러내고 있었다. 여기 비열한 무엇과의 그저 스치는 접촉에 대한 그녀의 예민한 소름 끼침이, 그녀가 자신에게 진 것에 대한 온갖 헤아림에 그녀를 눈멀게 하고, 그녀를 그녀의 온 벗들의 평가에서 그녀에게 해가 될지 모를 그릇된 처지로 몰아넣고 있었다! 이때까지, 나는 내가 그녀에게 준 조언의 온당함에 대해 조금 자신이 없었다. 허나, 그녀가 방금 한 말 뒤로, 나는 그것이 내놓을 수 있었던 가장 좋은 조언이라는 데 아무런 의심도 없었다. 그리고 나는 그것을 그녀에게 다시 밀어붙이는 데 아무런 망설임도 느끼지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 그저 고개를 젓고, 다른 말로 제 반대를 되풀이했다.\n\n\"그이는 저에게 제 아내가 되어 달라 청할 만큼 저와 가까웠어요. 그이는 제 승낙을 얻을 만큼 제 평가에서 높이 서 있었고요. 그런데 그 뒤에 그이의 면전에서, 그이가 살아 있는 것들 가운데 가장 경멸스러운 자라고 말할 수는 없어요!\"\n\n\"하지만, 나의 사랑하는 레이철 아가씨,\" 하고 나는 타일렀다. \"그것에 대한 아무 이유도 대지 않고 당신의 약혼에서 물러나겠다고 그에게 말하는 것도 꼭 그만큼 있을 수 없는 일이오.\"\n\n\"저는 곰곰이 헤아려 보았으며, 우리가 헤어지는 것이 우리 둘 다에게 가장 좋으리라 흡족히 여긴다고 말하겠어요.\"\n\n\"그 이상은 아니고?\"\n\n\"그 이상은 아니고요.\"\n\n\"그가 제 편에서 무슨 말을 할지 생각해 봤소?\"\n\n\"그이는 제 좋을 대로 말하면 돼요.\"\n\n그녀의 섬세함과 그녀의 결의에 감탄하지 않기란 있을 수 없는 일이었고, 그녀가 자신을 그릇된 처지에 놓고 있음을 느끼지 않기도 꼭 그만큼 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 나는 그녀에게 그녀 자신의 처지를 헤아려 보라고 간청했다. 나는 그녀에게, 그녀가 자신의 동기에 대한 더없이 얄미운 오해에 저를 내맡기게 되리라는 것을 상기시켰다. \"당신은 세상의 견해에 맞설 수 없소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"사사로운 감정의 명령에 따라서는.\"\n\n\"할 수 있어요.\" 하고 그녀가 대답했다. \"저는 이미 그리했는걸요.\"\n\n\"무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n\"당신은 월장석을 잊으셨어요, 브러프 씨. 제가 거기서, 그것에 대한 저 나름의 남모를 이유를 품고, 세상의 견해에 맞서지 않았던가요?\"\n\n그녀의 대답이 그 순간 나를 잠잠하게 했다. 그것은 나로 하여금, 방금 그녀에게서 새어 나온 그 이상한 실토에서, 월장석 분실 때의 그녀의 처신에 대한 설명을 더듬어 찾으려 애쓰게 했다. 나는 더 젊었을 때라면 어쩌면 그것을 해냈으리라. 나는 이제는 분명 그것을 해낼 수 없었다.\n\n나는 우리가 집으로 돌아가기 전에 마지막 훈계를 시도했다. 그녀는 그 어느 때처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다. 그날 내가 그녀를 떠날 때 나의 마음은 그녀에 대한 이상한 감정의 갈등 속에 있었다. 그녀는 고집스러웠다. 그녀는 틀렸다. 그녀는 흥미로웠다. 그녀는 감탄스러웠다. 그녀는 깊이 가엾이 여길 만했다. 나는 그녀에게, 보낼 소식이 생기는 즉시 나에게 편지를 쓰겠다고 약속하게 했다. 그리고 나는 마음이 지극히 편치 못한 채, 런던의 내 볼일로 돌아갔다.\n\n내가 돌아온 그날 저녁, 내가 약속받은 편지를 받는 것이 가능하기도 전에, 나는 노 에이블화이트 씨의 방문에 놀랐고, 고드프리 씨가 바로 그날 해고당했으며—그것을 받아들였다는 것을 전해 들었다.\n\n내가 그 경우에 대해 이미 취한 견해로 보아, 내가 밑줄 친 그 말로 밝혀진 그 담담한 사실은, 마치 그가 몸소 그것을 인정한 것처럼 뻔하게, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 순순히 따른 동기를 드러냈다. 그는 큰돈이 필요했다. 그리고 그는 그것이 정해진 때까지 필요했다. 다른 무엇에라면 그를 도왔을 레이철의 수입이, 여기서는 그를 돕지 못할 터였다. 그리하여 레이철은, 그의 편에서 한순간의 진지한 반대도 마주치지 않고, 스스로를 풀어 준 것이었다. 만일 누군가 이것이 한낱 억측이라고 나에게 말한다면, 나는, 내 차례로, 다른 어떤 이론이 그가 제 남은 평생을 호화롭게 지탱해 주었을 혼인을 포기한 것을 설명해 주겠느냐고 묻는다.\n\n일들이 이제 취한 그 다행스러운 방향 전환에 내가 안 그랬다면 느꼈을 법한 어떤 득의양양함도, 노 에이블화이트 씨와의 면담에서 오간 것으로 인해 효과 있게 억눌러졌다.\n\n그는, 물론, 버린더 아가씨의 예사롭지 않은 처신에 대해 내가 무슨 설명이라도 해 줄 수 있는지 알러 왔다. 내가 그가 원하는 정보를 도무지 줄 수 없었음은 말할 것도 없다. 내가 이렇게 끼친 언짢음이, 제 아들과의 최근 면담이 자아낸 짜증에 뒤이어, 에이블화이트 씨의 경계를 무너뜨렸다. 그의 낯빛도 그의 말도, 버린더 아가씨가 이튿날 브라이턴에서 그 부인들과 합류하면 그를 상대하기에 무자비한 사내로 알게 되리라는 것을 나에게 납득시켰다.\n\n나는 다음에 내가 무엇을 해야 할지 헤아리며, 편치 못한 밤을 보냈다. 나의 상념이 어떻게 끝났는지, 그리고 에이블화이트 씨에 대한 나의 불신이 얼마나 철저히 근거 있는 것으로 드러났는지는 (내가 듣기로는) 그 모범적인 사람 클랙 양에 의해 이미 알맞은 자리에 말끔히 놓인 정보의 항목들이다. 나는 다만—그녀의 이야기를 마무리하며—버린더 아가씨가 제 서글피 필요로 하던 고요와 안식을, 가엾은 것, 햄스테드의 내 집에서 찾았음을 덧붙일 뿐이다. 그녀는 오래 머묾으로써 우리를 영예롭게 했다. 나의 아내와 딸들은 그녀에게 매혹되었다. 그리고 유언 집행인들이 새 후견인의 임명을 정했을 때, 나는 나의 손님과 나의 식구가 양편에서 오랜 벗처럼 헤어졌음을 적는 데에 진심 어린 자랑과 기쁨을 느낀다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n내가 다음으로 해야 할 일은, 월장석이라는 화제에 대해—더 정확히 말하자면, 그 다이아몬드를 훔치려는 인도인의 음모라는 화제에 대해—내가 지닌 그런 추가 정보를 내놓는 것이다. 내가 이야기할 것은 비록 얼마 안 되나 (내 생각에 이미 말했듯이), 그럼에도, 아직 다가올 일들에 아주 두드러지게 관련된다는 점에서, 얼마쯤 중요하다.\n\n버린더 아가씨가 우리를 떠난 지 한 주쯤, 또는 열흘쯤 지났을 때, 내 서기 하나가 손에 명함 한 장을 들고 내 사무소의 개인실로 들어와, 아래층에 나와 이야기하고 싶어 하는 신사 한 분이 와 있다고 알렸다.\n\n나는 그 명함을 보았다. 그 위에는 어느 외국 이름이 적혀 있었는데, 그것은 내 기억에서 사라졌다. 그 뒤로, 명함 아래쪽에 영어로 적힌 한 줄이 이어졌는데, 그것을 나는 아주 또렷이 기억한다.\n\n\"셉티머스 루커 씨의 추천으로.\"\n\n루커 씨 같은 처지의 사람이 감히 누군가를 나에게 추천하다니, 그 뻔뻔함이 나를 어찌나 완전히 놀라게 했던지, 나는 잠시 잠자코 앉아, 내 두 눈이 나를 속인 것은 아닌가 의아해했다. 그 서기는, 나의 어리둥절함을 알아채고, 아래층에서 기다리는 그 낯선 이에 대한 제 나름의 관찰의 결과를 나에게 베풀어 주었다.\n\n\"제법 눈에 띄는 생김새의 분입니다, 나리. 낯빛이 어찌나 거무스름한지 우리는 사무소에서 죄다 그를 인도인이거나, 그 비슷한 무엇으로 여겼습니다.\"\n\n내 손에 든 명함에 적힌 그 줄에 그 서기의 생각을 이어 붙여, 나는 월장석이 루커 씨의 추천과, 그 낯선 이의 내 사무소 방문의 밑바탕에 있을지도 모른다고 생각했다. 내 서기가 깜짝 놀라게도, 나는 대뜸 아래층의 그 신사에게 면담을 허락하기로 정했다.\n\n내가 이렇듯 한낱 호기심에 바친 그 지극히 비직업적인 희생을 변명하고자, 이 글줄들을 읽을지 모를 누구에게든 상기시키는 것을 허락해 주기 바란다. 살아 있는 어떤 사람도 (적어도 잉글랜드에서는) 그 인도 다이아몬드의 기담과 나만큼 그리도 가까운 연을 맺었다고 주장할 수 없다는 것을. 나는 헌캐슬 대령의 암살 모면 계획의 비밀을 맡았다. 나는 대령이 제가 살아 있는 사람임을 주기적으로 알려 오는 편지들을 받았다. 나는 월장석을 버린더 아가씨에게 남기는 그의 유언장을 작성했다. 나는 그 보석이 그 집안에 값진 소득으로 드러날 요행을 노려, 그 유언 집행인을 움직이도록 설득했다. 그리고, 끝으로, 나는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 망설임에 맞서, 그가 그 다이아몬드를 레이디 버린더의 집으로 옮기는 수단이 되게끔 이끌었다. 만일 누군가 월장석에 대해, 그리고 그것에 얽힌 모든 것에 대해 시효로 얻은 관심의 권리를 주장할 수 있다면, 그것이 나라는 것은 좀처럼 부인될 수 없으리라고 나는 생각한다.\n\n나의 수수께끼 같은 의뢰인이 안으로 안내되어 든 그 순간, 나는 내가 그 인도인 셋 가운데 하나—아마도 그 우두머리—의 앞에 있다는 속으로부터의 확신을 느꼈다. 그는 유럽식 복장을 정성 들여 차려입고 있었다. 허나 그의 거무스름한 낯빛, 그의 길고 나긋한 몸매, 그리고 그의 무겁고도 우아한 몸가짐의 정중함은, 그를 바라보는 어떤 지각 있는 눈에게든 그의 동양 태생을 드러내기에 넉넉했다.\n\n나는 의자 하나를 가리키고, 그가 나와 볼 일의 성격을 알려 주기를 청했다.\n\n먼저—더없이 잘 고른 영어 낱말들로—나를 방해하는 무람없음을 저질렀음을 사과한 뒤, 그 인도인은 겉을 금실 천으로 감싼 작은 꾸러미 하나를 꺼냈다. 이것과, 어떤 비단 천으로 된 두 번째 싸개를 벗기고서, 그는 내 탁자 위에 작은 상자, 곧 함(函) 하나를 놓았는데, 그것은 흑단 바탕에 보석이 더없이 아름답고 화려하게 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"저는 왔습니다, 선생.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신께 얼마간의 돈을 빌려 달라고 청하러요. 그리고 저는 제 빚이 갚아지리라는 보증으로 이것을 당신께 맡겨 두겠습니다.\"\n\n나는 그의 명함을 가리켰다. \"그래서 당신은 나에게 청하는 거요,\" 하고 나는 되받았다. \"루커 씨의 추천으로?\"\n\n그 인도인은 고개 숙여 인사했다.\n\n\"물어봐도 되겠소, 어찌하여 루커 씨 자신은 당신이 필요로 하는 돈을 내주지 않았는지?\"\n\n\"루커 씨는 저에게, 선생, 빌려줄 돈이 없다고 알렸습니다.\"\n\n\"그래서 그가 당신더러 나에게 오라고 추천했다?\"\n\n그 인도인은, 제 차례로, 그 명함을 가리켰다. \"거기에 적혀 있습니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n짧게 대답하되, 더없이 요점에 들어맞았다! 만일 월장석이 내 수중에 있었더라면, 이 동양 신사는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 나를 죽였으리라는 것을, 나는 잘 안다. 동시에, 그 자잘한 흠 하나만 빼면, 나는 그가 완벽한 의뢰인의 본보기였음을 증언하지 않을 수 없다. 그는 나의 목숨은 존중하지 않았을지 모른다. 허나 그는, 내가 그들을 겪은 온 경험에서 내 동포 가운데 아무도 여태껏 하지 않은 일을 했다—그는 나의 시간을 존중했다.\n\n\"미안하오,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신이 나에게 오는 수고를 겪어야 했다니. 루커 씨가 당신을 여기로 보낸 것은 아주 잘못한 일이오. 나는, 내 직업의 다른 사람들처럼, 빌려줄 돈을 맡고 있소. 허나 나는 결코 그것을 낯선 이에게 빌려주지 않으며, 결코 당신이 내놓은 그런 담보로 빌려주지 않소.\"\n\n다른 사람들이라면 그랬을 법하게, 내가 내 규칙을 늦추도록 꾀려 애쓰기는커녕, 그 인도인은 나에게 다시 한 번 고개 숙여 인사했을 뿐이고, 한마디 항변도 없이 제 상자를 그 두 싸개로 도로 쌌다. 그는 일어섰다—이 감탄스러운 자객은, 내가 그에게 대답한 그 순간, 떠나려고 일어섰다!\n\n\"낯선 이에게 베푸시는 당신의 너그러움이, 제가 한 가지 물음을 여쭙는 것을 용서해 주겠습니까,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"제가 물러가기 전에?\"\n\n나도 내 편에서 고개 숙여 인사했다. 헤어질 때 물음이 오직 하나라니! 내 경험에서 그 평균은 쉰이었다.\n\n\"이를테면, 선생, 당신이 저에게 그 돈을 빌려주는 것이 가능했다(그리고 관례였다)고 한다면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"제가 그것을 갚는 것이 가능한(그리고 관례인) 것은 얼마만 한 기간 안이겠습니까?\"\n\n\"이 나라에서 밟는 통상의 절차에 따르면,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"당신은 그 돈이 당신에게 처음 내주어진 날로부터 한 해 안에 (당신이 원한다면) 그것을 갚을 권리가 있게 될 거요.\"\n\n그 인도인은 나에게 마지막 인사를, 그중 가장 깊은 인사를 하고는—별안간, 그리고 소리 없이 방을 걸어 나갔다.\n\n그것은 한순간에, 소리 없고, 나긋하고, 고양이 같은 몸놀림으로 이루어졌는데, 그것이 나를 조금 놀라게 했음을 나는 인정한다. 생각할 만큼 마음을 추스르자마자, 나는 그러지 않고서는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는, 나를 찾아 준 그 방문객에 관해 한 가지 또렷한 결론에 다다랐다.\n\n그의 얼굴, 목소리, 그리고 몸가짐은—내가 그와 함께 있는 동안—어찌나 완벽히 통제되어 있던지, 온갖 뜯어봄을 무색게 했다. 허나 그럼에도 그는 나에게 그의 매끄러운 겉면 아래를 들여다볼 기회를 한 번 주었다. 그는, 빚진 이 편에서 대여로 내준 돈을 가장 이르게 갚는 것을 허락하는 것이 관례인 때를 내가 언급하기 전까지는, 내가 그에게 한 말을 무엇 하나 제 마음에 새겨 두려 애쓰는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았다. 내가 그에게 그 정보를 주었을 때, 그는, 내가 말하는 동안, 처음으로 나의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다. 이것에서 내가 이끌어 낸 추론은—그가 그 마지막 물음을 나에게 던진 데에 각별한 목적이 있었고, 그 답을 듣는 데에 각별한 관심이 있었다는 것이다. 우리 사이에 오간 것을 더 꼼꼼히 곱씹을수록, 나는 그 함을 꺼낸 것과, 그 대여의 청이, 나에게 던져진 그 이별의 물음으로 가는 길을 닦으려고 꾀한 한낱 형식에 지나지 않았음을 더 날카롭게 의심했다.\n\n나는 이 결론의 옳음을 스스로 흡족히 여겼고—한 걸음 더 나아가, 다음으로 그 인도인의 동기를 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰고 있었는데—그때 나에게 편지 한 통이 왔고, 그것은 다름 아닌 셉티머스 루커 씨 자신에게서 온 것으로 드러났다. 그는 역겨우리만치 굽실거리는 말투로 나의 용서를 구했고, 만일 내가 직접 만나는 데 동의하는 영예를 베풀어 준다면 자기가 나를 흡족하게끔 일들을 설명할 수 있다고 장담했다.\n\n나는 한낱 호기심에 또 한 번 비직업적인 희생을 바쳤다. 나는 이튿날 내 사무소에서 약속을 잡음으로써 그에게 영예를 베풀었다.\n\n루커 씨는, 온갖 면에서, 그 인도인에 견주면 어찌나 못난 인간이던지—어찌나 상스럽고, 어찌나 흉하고, 어찌나 굽실대고, 어찌나 따분하던지—그는 이 쪽들에서 조금이라도 길게 전할 값어치가 도무지 없다. 그가 나에게 이야기해야 했던 것의 알맹이는 대체로 다음과 같이 밝힐 수 있다.\n\n내가 그 인도인의 방문을 받기 전날, 루커 씨는 그 능란한 신사의 방문을 받았었다. 그의 유럽식 변장에도, 루커 씨는 대뜸 제 방문객을, 예전에 제 집 둘레를 어슬렁거리며 그를 성가시게 했고, 그로 하여금 치안판사에게 상의하는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없게 만든 그 인도인 셋의 우두머리로 알아보았다. 이 소스라칠 발견에서 그는 (당연하게도, 나는 인정한다) 자기가 틀림없이, 저의 눈을 가리고, 저의 입을 틀어막고, 저의 은행 영수증을 빼앗은 그 세 사내 가운데 하나와 함께 있는 것이 분명하다는 결론으로 내달았다. 그 결과 그는 공포로 아주 얼이 빠졌고, 저의 마지막 시각이 왔다고 굳게 믿었다.\n\n제 편에서, 그 인도인은 온전한 낯선 이의 모습을 지켰다. 그는 그 작은 함을 꺼냈고, 뒤에 나에게 한 것과 꼭 같은 청을 했다. 그를 떼어 내는 가장 빠른 방편으로, 루커 씨는 대뜸 자기에게는 돈이 없다고 잘라 말했다. 그러자 그 인도인은 자기가 원하는 대여를 청하기에 가장 좋고 가장 안전한 사람을 알려 달라 청했다. 루커 씨는, 그런 경우에 가장 좋고 가장 안전한 사람은 대개 점잖은 사무 변호사라고 대답했다. 그런 성격과 직업의 어떤 개인의 이름을 대라는 청을 받자, 루커 씨는 나를 언급했다—오직 한 가지 단순한 이유에서, 곧 그의 공포의 극단에서, 나의 이름이 그에게 맨 처음 떠오른 이름이었기 때문이다. \"땀이 저에게서 비처럼 쏟아지고 있었습니다, 선생.\" 하고 그 가련한 인간이 말을 맺었다. \"저는 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 몰랐지요. 그리고 저는 당신께서, 브러프 씨, 선생, 제가 정말이지 참으로 넋이 빠지도록 겁에 질렸던 것을 헤아리시어, 그것을 눈감아 주시기를 바랍니다.\"\n\n나는 그 친구를 제법 너그러이 용서해 주었다. 그것이 그의 꼴에서 나 자신을 풀어내는 가장 손쉬운 방편이었다. 그가 나를 떠나기 전에, 나는 한 가지 물음을 던지려고 그를 붙들었다.\n\n그 인도인이 루커 씨의 집을 떠나던 그 순간에, 무언가 눈에 띄는 말을 했던가?\n\n그랬다! 그 인도인은, 헤어질 때, 나에게 던진 것과 정확히 같은 물음을 루커 씨에게 던졌고, 물론, 내가 그에게 준 답과 같은 답을 받았다.\n\n그것은 무엇을 뜻했는가? 루커 씨의 설명은 그 수수께끼를 푸는 데에 나에게 아무런 도움도 주지 못했다. 다음으로 기댄 나 자신의 홀로의 재간도, 그 어려움과 맞붙기에 아주 모자란 것으로 드러났다. 나는 그날 저녁 만찬 약속이 있었다. 그리고 나는 그리 유쾌하지 못한 심사로 위층으로 올라갔는데, 나의 화장실로 가는 길과 발견으로 가는 길이, 바로 이 경우에는, 하나의 같은 것을 뜻한다고는 좀처럼 의심하지 못한 채였다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n그 만찬 자리의 손님들 가운데 가장 두드러진 인물은, 알고 보니 머스웨이트 씨였다.\n\n떠돌이 방랑을 마치고 잉글랜드에 모습을 드러냈을 때, 사교계는 그 여행가에게 지대한 관심을 보였다—숱한 위험한 모험을 헤치고 지나와, 그 이야기를 전하려 살아 돌아온 사람으로서 말이다. 그는 이제 자기의 그 무용담의 무대로 돌아가, 아직도 미답으로 남은 지역들 속으로 파고들 작정임을 밝힌 터였다. 두 번째로 제 안전을 위험에 내던지는 이 장엄한 무심함이, 그 영웅을 흠모하는 이들의 시들해진 관심을 되살려 놓았다. 이번만은 그가 무사히 벗어나기가 확률의 법칙상 명백히 어려웠다. 저명한 인물과 만찬에서 마주 앉아, 그에 관해 우리가 다음번에 듣게 될 소식이 바로 그의 피살 소식일 상당한 개연성이 있다고 느끼는 일은, 날마다 있는 일이 아니다.\n\n신사들끼리만 식당에 남게 되었을 때, 나는 어느새 머스웨이트 씨의 옆자리에 앉아 있었다. 자리한 손님들이 모두 잉글랜드 사람이었으니, 숙녀들의 임석이 발휘하던 그 건전한 제어가 사라지자마자 화제가 필연적인 결과로 정치로 흘렀다는 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다.\n\n이 온 나라를 사로잡는 화제에 관한 한, 나는 세상에 살아 있는 가장 잉글랜드인답지 않은 잉글랜드인의 하나임을 자인한다. 대체로 나에게 정치 이야기는 온갖 이야기 가운데 가장 따분하고 가장 무익한 것으로 여겨진다. 병들이 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌고 났을 때 머스웨이트 씨를 흘긋 보니, 그도 겉보기에 나와 같은 생각을 지닌 이였다. 그는 아주 능란하게 그리하고 있었다—주인의 심기를 헤아릴 수 있는 온갖 배려를 다하면서—허나 그가 한숨 자려고 몸을 가다듬고 있다는 것만은 못지않게 분명했다. 월장석이라는 화제를 지혜롭게 넌지시 꺼내면 그를 깨어 있게 할 수 있을지, 그리고 만일 그리된다면, 나의 사무소의 그 무미건조한 울타리 안에서 드러난 그 인도인 음모의 마지막 새 얽힘을 그가 어찌 생각하는지 보아 두는 것이, 해 볼 만한 실험으로 나에게 떠올랐다.\n\n\"제가 잘못 안 것이 아니라면, 머스웨이트 씨,\" 하고 나는 말을 뗐다. \"당신은 고인이 되신 레이디 버린더와 아는 사이셨고, 월장석의 분실로 끝난 그 기이한 사건들의 잇따름에 얼마쯤 관심을 두셨지요?\"\n\n그 저명한 여행가는 그 자리에서 대뜸 깨어나, 내가 누구냐고 물어 오는 영예를 나에게 베풀었다.\n\n나는 헌캐슬 집안과의 나의 직업상의 연을 그에게 알렸는데, 지난 시절 그 대령과 그의 다이아몬드를 향해 내가 차지했던 그 묘한 처지도 잊지 않고 곁들였다.\n\n머스웨이트 씨는 나머지 일행(보수당원이든 자유당원이든 매한가지로)을 제 등 뒤로 돌리려는 듯 의자에서 몸을 틀어, 그레이스 인 스퀘어의 한낱 평범한 브러프 씨에게 온 정신을 쏟았다.\n\n\"근래에 그 인도인들에 관해 무언가 들으신 것이 있소?\" 하고 그가 물었다.\n\n\"믿을 만한 온갖 근거가 있소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"그들 가운데 하나가 어제, 내 사무소에서, 나와 면담을 가졌다고 여길 만한 근거가요.\"\n\n머스웨이트 씨는 좀처럼 놀라게 하기 어려운 사람이었다. 허나 나의 그 마지막 대답은 그를 완전히 휘청거리게 했다. 나는 루커 씨에게 일어난 일과, 나 자신에게 일어난 일을, 바로 여기 적어 온 그대로 그에게 이야기해 주었다. \"그 인도인이 헤어지며 던진 물음에 목적이 있었던 것은 분명하오,\" 하고 나는 덧붙였다. \"어찌하여 그가, 돈을 빌린 이가 통상 그 돈을 되갚도록 허락받는 시기를 그리도 애타게 알고 싶어 했겠소?\"\n\n\"당신은 정녕 그의 동기를 모르시겠소, 브러프 씨?\"\n\n\"나의 아둔함이 부끄럽소, 머스웨이트 씨—허나 나는 정말로 그것을 모르겠소.\"\n\n그 위대한 여행가는 나의 우둔함의 그 광대한 공허를 가장 밑바닥까지 재어 보는 데에 자못 흥미가 동한 참이었다.\n\n\"당신에게 한 가지 물음을 던지게 해 주시오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"월장석을 손에 넣으려는 음모는 지금 어떤 처지에 놓여 있소?\"\n\n\"나는 알 수 없소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"그 인도인의 음모는 나에게 하나의 수수께끼요.\"\n\n\"그 인도인의 음모가, 브러프 씨, 당신에게 수수께끼일 수 있는 것은 오직 당신이 그것을 진지하게 살펴본 적이 없기 때문이오. 우리 그것을 함께 한번 훑어봅시다, 당신이 헌캐슬 대령의 유언장을 작성한 때로부터, 그 인도인이 당신의 사무소를 찾아온 때까지 말이오. 당신의 처지에서는, 필요할 때에 이 문제를 또렷이 내다볼 수 있다는 것이, 버린더 아가씨의 이해(利害)에 아주 막중하게 중요할지도 모르오. 그것을 마음에 두고서, 나에게 말해 보시오, 당신 스스로 그 인도인의 동기를 꿰뚫어 보시겠소? 아니면 내가 당신을 대신해 그것을 캐어 보는 수고를 덜어 드리기를 바라시오?\"\n\n이제 그가 품고 있음을 내가 알아본 그 실질적인 목적을 내가 낱낱이 헤아렸다는 것과, 그 두 갈래 가운데 첫째가 내가 고른 갈래였다는 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다.\n\n\"아주 좋소,\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말했다. \"우리는 먼저 그 인도인 셋의 나이 문제를 다루겠소. 나는 그들이 모두 엇비슷한 나이로 보인다고 증언할 수 있소—그리고 당신이 본 그 사내가 한창 나이였는지 아니었는지는 당신 스스로 판단할 수 있소. 마흔은 안 되었다, 그리 여기시오? 나의 짐작도 그렇소. 마흔은 안 되었다고 해 둡시다. 자, 헌캐슬 대령이 잉글랜드로 왔던 때, 그리고 당신이 그가 제 목숨을 지키려 채택한 계획에 관여했던 때로 거슬러 봅시다. 나는 당신더러 햇수를 세라는 것이 아니오. 나는 다만 이렇게만 말하겠소, 지금의 이 인도인들이, 그 나이로 보아, 대령을 뒤따라 이 해안까지 온 다른 인도인 셋(모두 제 고국을 떠날 때는 높은 계급의 브라만이었소, 브러프 씨!)의 후계자들임에 틀림없다는 것은 명백하다고 말이오. 아주 좋소. 지금 우리의 이 사내들은 그들에 앞서 여기 있던 사내들의 자리를 이어받았소. 만일 그들이 그것만 했다면, 이 일은 캐어 볼 값어치조차 없었을 거요. 허나 그들은 그 이상을 했소. 그들은 그들의 선대가 이 나라에 세운 조직을 이어받았소. 놀라지 마시오! 그 조직은, 나는 조금도 의심치 않소만, 우리의 관념으로는 아주 하찮은 물건이오. 나라면 그것을 이렇게 헤아리겠소—돈을 부리는 힘, 필요할 때 부릴 수 있는, 런던의 외국 삶의 뒷골목에 사는 그 수상쩍은 부류의 잉글랜드 사람의 도움, 그리고, 끝으로, 이 큰 도시의 그 숱한 필요 가운데 얼마쯤을 채워 주는 일에 우연히 종사하는, 저희 나라 사람이자 (적어도 예전에는) 저희 종교를 지닌 몇 안 되는 사람들의 은밀한 동조 말이오. 보시다시피, 그리 대단할 것은 없소! 허나 처음에 눈여겨 둘 값어치는 있소, 우리가 나아가는 동안 이 조촐하고 자그마한 인도인 조직을 다시 언급할 계제를 만날지도 모르기 때문이오. 이제 바탕을 닦았으니, 나는 당신에게 한 가지 물음을 던지려 하오. 그리고 나는 당신의 경험이 그것에 답해 주기를 바라오. 그 인도인들에게 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 첫 기회를 준 사건은 무엇이었소?\"\n\n나는 나의 경험을 가리키는 그 말뜻을 알아들었다.\n\n\"그들이 얻은 첫 기회는,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"명백히 헌캐슬 대령의 죽음이 그들에게 안겨 준 것이오. 짐작건대 그들은 당연한 일로 그의 죽음을 알았겠지요?\"\n\n\"당연한 일로 알았소. 그리고 그의 죽음은, 당신이 말하듯, 그들에게 그 첫 기회를 주었소. 그때까지 월장석은 은행의 금고실에 안전히 있었소. 당신은 대령의 보석을 그의 조카딸에게 남기는 그 유언장을 작성했소. 그리고 그 유언장은 통상의 절차로 검인되었소. 변호사로서, 당신은 그 뒤에 인도인들이 (잉글랜드 사람의 조언을 받아) 어떤 방도를 취할지를 어렵잖게 알 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n\"그들은 닥터스 코먼스에서 그 유언장의 사본을 마련하겠지요,\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 내가 넌지시 말한 그 수상쩍은 잉글랜드 사람들 가운데 이런저런 이가 그들에게 당신이 방금 말한 그 사본을 구해다 줄 거요. 그 사본은 그들에게, 월장석이 레이디 버린더의 딸에게 유증되었다는 것과, 손위 블레이크 씨, 또는 그가 지명한 어떤 이가 그것을 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주기로 되어 있다는 것을 알려 줄 거요. 레이디 버린더와 블레이크 씨 같은 처지의 사람들에 관해 필요한 정보를 얻는 것이 더없이 손쉬운 일이라는 데에는 당신도 나와 뜻을 같이할 거요. 인도인들에게 오직 하나의 어려움은, 그 다이아몬드를 은행의 보관에서 옮기는 도중에 노릴지, 아니면 그것이 요크셔의 레이디 버린더의 집으로 내려가기까지 기다릴지를 정하는 일이었을 거요. 둘째 방도가 명백히 가장 안전한 방도였을 것이오—그리고 바로 거기에, 요술쟁이로 변장하고 프리징홀에 나타나 제 때를 기다린 그 인도인들의 출현에 대한 설명이 있소. 런던에서, 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 그들은 사건의 소식을 계속 얻으려고 제 조직을 부릴 수 있었소. 두 사람이면 될 일이었소. 하나는 블레이크 씨의 집에서 은행으로 가는 이는 누구든 뒤따르는 일, 그리고 하나는 아랫사람 하인들에게 맥주를 사 대며 그 집의 소식을 듣는 일. 이 흔해빠진 방비만으로도 그들은, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 은행에 다녀왔다는 것과, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 그 집에서 레이디 버린더를 찾아가려는 유일한 사람이라는 것을 어렵잖게 알았을 거요. 그 발견에 뒤이어 실제로 일어난 일은, 당신도 나만큼이나 아주 또렷이 기억하고 있을 것이 틀림없소.\"\n\n나는 기억했다. 프랭클린 블레이크가 그 밀정 가운데 하나를 길거리에서 알아챈 것, 그리하여 그가 요크셔에 다다르는 시각을 몇 시간 앞당긴 것, 그리고 (늙은 베터리지의 훌륭한 조언 덕분에) 인도인들이 그를 그 근방에서 볼 채비를 채 갖추기도 전에 그가 프리징홀의 은행에 그 다이아몬드를 맡겨 둔 것을 말이다. 여기까지는 모두 더없이 또렷했다. 허나 인도인들이 이렇듯 취해진 방비를 몰랐다고 한들, 어찌하여 그들은 레이철의 생일에 앞서 흘러간 그 온 기간 내내 (그들이 다이아몬드가 있으리라 여겼음이 틀림없는) 레이디 버린더의 집에 아무런 시도도 하지 않았단 말인가?\n\n이 난제를 머스웨이트 씨에게 내놓으면서, 나는 그 어린 소년과, 잉크 한 방울과, 그 나머지의 이야기를 들은 바 있다는 것, 그리고 천리안이라는 이론에 바탕을 둔 어떤 설명도 나의 마음에는 조금도 확신을 주지 못하는 설명이라는 것을 덧붙이는 것이 옳다고 여겼다.\n\n\"나의 마음에도 마찬가지요,\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말했다. \"이 경우의 천리안이란 그저 인도인 기질의 낭만적인 면이 뻗어 나온 것에 지나지 않소. 그들에게는—당신네 잉글랜드 사람의 마음으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 일임을 나도 인정하오만—이 나라에서 벌이는 저희의 지루하고 위태로운 심부름을 어떤 경이롭고 초자연한 후광으로 두르는 것이, 하나의 위안이자 격려일 것이오. 그들의 소년은 두말할 것 없이 최면술의 영향에 예민한 대상이오—그리고, 그 영향 아래에서, 그는 그에게 최면을 거는 사람의 마음속에 이미 들어 있던 것을 비추어 냈을 뿐임에 틀림없소. 나는 천리안이라는 이론을 시험해 보았소—그리고 나는 그 나타남이 그 지점을 넘어서는 것을 결코 본 적이 없소. 인도인들은 이런 식으로 그 일을 캐지 않소. 인도인들은 그들의 소년을 저희 눈에 보이지 않는 것들을 보는 예언자로 우러러보오—그리고, 거듭 말하거니와, 그 경이 속에서 그들은 저희를 하나로 묶는 목적에 대한 새로운 관심의 원천을 찾소. 나는 다만 이것을, 당신에게는 아주 새로울 것이 틀림없는, 인간 기질의 한 흥미로운 면을 보여 주는 것으로서 짚어 둘 뿐이오. 우리가 지금 좇고 있는 이 탐구에서는, 천리안도, 최면술도, 실질적인 사람으로서는 믿기 어려운 그 무엇도, 우리와는 아무런 상관이 없소. 그 인도인의 음모를 한 걸음 한 걸음 좇는 나의 목적은, 결과를 이성적인 방편으로 자연스러운 원인들로 거슬러 밝히는 것이오. 여기까지 내가 당신을 흡족하게 해 드렸소?\"\n\n\"조금도 의심할 바 없소, 머스웨이트 씨! 다만 나는, 얼마쯤 애를 태우며, 내가 방금 당신께 내놓는 영예를 누린 그 난제에 대한 이성적인 설명을 듣기를 기다리고 있소.\"\n\n머스웨이트 씨는 미소 지었다. \"그것은 온갖 난제 가운데 다루기 가장 쉬운 것이오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"먼저 이 사건에 대한 당신의 진술이 더없이 옳다는 것을 인정하는 데서 시작하도록 허락해 주시오. 인도인들은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지를 두말할 것 없이 몰랐소—그들이 블레이크 씨가 이모의 집에 다다른 그 첫날 밤에 첫 실수를 저지르는 것을 우리가 보게 되니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그들의 첫 실수라니요?\" 하고 나는 되물었다.\n\n\"물론이오! 밤에 그 테라스 둘레를 어슬렁대다가 게이브리얼 베터리지에게 들키는 낭패를 자초한 실수 말이오. 그러나 그들에게는 스스로 자기들이 잘못된 걸음을 내디뎠음을 알아차리는 미덕이 있었소—당신이 다시 말하듯, 손에 넉넉한 시간을 쥐고서도, 그들은 그 뒤로 몇 주 동안 그 집 근처에 얼씬도 하지 않았으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"어찌하여요, 머스웨이트 씨? 그것이 바로 내가 알고 싶은 것이오! 어찌하여?\"\n\n\"어떤 인도인도, 브러프 씨, 결코 쓸데없는 위험을 무릅쓰지 않기 때문이오. 당신이 헌캐슬 대령의 유언장에 작성한 그 조항은 그들에게, 월장석이 버린더 아가씨의 생일에 그녀의 소유로 완전히 넘어가리라는 것을 알려 주었소 (그렇지 않소?). 아주 좋소. 그들의 처지에 놓인 사람들에게 가장 안전한 방도가 어느 쪽이었는지 나에게 말해 보시오. 이미 저희를 의심하고 따돌릴 수 있음을 보여 준 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 손아귀에 그 다이아몬드가 있는 동안에 노리는 것이겠소? 아니면 그 화려한 보석을 틈만 나면 몸에 지니려 천진하게 즐거워할 어린 아가씨의 손에 그 다이아몬드가 놓일 때까지 기다리는 것이겠소? 어쩌면 당신은 나의 이론이 옳다는 증거를 원할지도 모르오? 그 인도인들 자신의 처신을 그 증거로 삼으시오. 그들은 그 여러 주를 다 기다린 끝에, 버린더 아가씨의 생일에 그 집에 나타났소. 그리고 그들은 그녀의 드레스 가슴께에 있는 월장석을 봄으로써 저희 계산의 참을성 있는 정확함에 대한 보답을 받았소! 그날 저녁 늦게 그 대령과 그 다이아몬드의 이야기를 들었을 때, 나는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 무릅쓴 그 위험을 어찌나 확신했던지 (만일 그가 마침 다른 사람들과 함께 무리 지어 레이디 버린더의 집으로 말을 몰아 돌아오지 않았더라면, 그들은 틀림없이 그를 덮쳤을 것이오), 그리고 버린더 아가씨에게 앞으로 닥칠 더한 위험을 어찌나 굳게 믿었던지, 나는 그 대령의 계획을 따라, 그 보석을 여러 개의 돌로 쪼개어 그 정체를 없애 버리기를 권했소. 그날 밤 그것의 그 기이한 사라짐이 어찌하여 나의 조언을 쓸모없게 만들고 그 힌두인의 음모를 완전히 무너뜨렸는지—그리고 인도인들 편의 온갖 다음 행동이 어찌하여 이튿날 그들이 부랑자이자 무뢰한으로 옥에 갇힘으로써 마비되었는지—당신도 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있소. 그 음모의 첫 막(幕)은 거기서 닫히오. 우리가 둘째 막으로 넘어가기 전에, 내가 당신의 난제를, 실질적인 사람의 마음에 흡족한 설명으로써 풀어 드렸는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n그가 나의 난제를 정정당당히 풀어 주었음은 부인할 도리가 없었다. 인도인 기질에 대한 그의 뛰어난 식견 덕분에—그리고 헌캐슬 대령의 시절 이후로 그가 생각해야 할 다른 유언장을 수백 건이나 떠안지 않은 덕분에 말이다!\n\n\"여기까지는 잘되었소,\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말을 이었다. \"인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 첫 기회는, 그들이 프리징홀의 옥에 갇히던 날 잃어버린 기회였소. 둘째 기회는 언제 찾아왔소? 그 둘째 기회는—내가 증명할 수 있는 처지에 있소만—그들이 아직 갇혀 있는 동안에 찾아왔소.\"\n\n그는 말을 잇기에 앞서 제 수첩을 꺼내어 어느 한 낱장을 펼쳤다.\n\n\"나는 그때,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"프리징홀의 몇몇 벗과 함께 묵고 있었소. 그 인도인들이 풀려나기 하루나 이틀 전에 (내 생각에 월요일이었소), 그 옥의 소장이 편지 한 통을 들고 나를 찾아왔소. 그것은 그 인도인들이 세 들어 살던 숙소의 주인인 어떤 매캔 부인이라는 이가 그들 앞으로 남겨 둔 것이었소. 그리고 그것은 그 전날 아침, 통상의 우편 절차로 매캔 부인의 집 문 앞에 배달된 것이었소. 옥의 당국은 소인이 '램버스'인 것과, 겉면의 주소가, 비록 올바른 영어로 적혀 있기는 하나, 그 꼴이 편지 겉봉을 쓰는 관례의 방식과는 묘하게 어긋난다는 것을 눈여겨보았소. 그것을 뜯어 보고, 그들은 그 내용이 어떤 외국어로 적혀 있음을 알아냈는데, 그들은 그것을 힌두스타니어라고 옳게 짐작했소. 그들이 나를 찾아온 목적은, 물론, 그 편지를 그들에게 번역해 주도록 하는 것이었소. 나는 그 원문과 나의 번역을 내 수첩에 베껴 두었소—그리고 그것들이 여기 당신을 위해 있소.\"\n\n그는 그 펼쳐진 수첩을 나에게 건넸다. 그 편지의 주소가 맨 먼저 베껴져 있었다. 그것은 온통 한 문단에, 구두점을 찍으려는 아무런 시도도 없이, 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"요크셔의 프리징홀에서 매캔이라 불리는 부인과 함께 사는 세 명의 인도 사내에게.\" 뒤이어 힌두 글자들이 있었고, 그 영어 번역이 끝에 나타났는데, 다음의 알쏭달쏭한 말로 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"밤의 섭정, 그 자리가 영양(羚羊) 위에 있고, 그 팔이 땅의 네 귀퉁이를 끌어안는 이의 이름으로.\n\n\"형제들이여, 그대들의 얼굴을 남쪽으로 돌려, 진흙탕 강으로 내려가는 소란한 거리로 나에게 오라.\n\n\"까닭은 이러하다.\n\n\"내 두 눈이 그것을 보았노라.\"\n\n거기서 그 편지는, 날짜도 서명도 없이, 끝나 있었다. 나는 그것을 머스웨이트 씨에게 도로 건네며, 이 기묘한 힌두 서신의 표본이 나를 자못 어리둥절하게 한다고 털어놓았다.\n\n\"나는 당신에게 그 첫 문장을 풀이해 드릴 수 있소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 나머지는 그 인도인들 자신의 처신이 풀이해 줄 거요. 달의 신은, 힌두 신화에서, 영양 위에 앉은, 팔이 넷 달린 신으로 그려지오. 그리고 그의 칭호 가운데 하나가 밤의 섭정이오. 그러니, 우선 여기에, 월장석에 대한 에두른 언급인 양 수상쩍게 보이는 무언가가 있는 셈이오. 자, 옥의 당국이 그들에게 그 편지를 받도록 허락한 뒤에 그 인도인들이 무엇을 했는지 봅시다. 그들은 풀려난 바로 그날, 곧장 철도역으로 가서 런던으로 떠나는 첫 기차에 자리를 잡았소. 우리는 프리징홀에서 그들의 행동이 은밀히 감시되지 않은 것을 모두 애석하게 여겼소. 허나 레이디 버린더가 그 경찰관을 물리치고, 그 다이아몬드의 분실에 대한 온갖 더 이상의 조사를 그만두게 한 뒤로는, 아무도 감히 그 일에 나설 수 없었소. 인도인들은 마음대로 런던으로 갈 수 있었고, 런던으로 그들은 갔소. 우리가 다음으로 그들에 관해 들은 소식은 무엇이었소, 브러프 씨?\"\n\n\"그들은 루커 씨를 성가시게 하고 있었소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"램버스의 그 집 둘레를 어슬렁거리며 말이오.\"\n\n\"당신은 루커 씨가 치안판사에게 낸 신청의 보고서를 읽어 보았소?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그의 진술 가운데, 당신도 기억한다면, 그는 자기가 부리던 어느 외국인 일꾼을 언급했소—그가 절도 미수의 혐의로 방금 내쫓은, 그리고 자기를 성가시게 한 그 인도인들과 어쩌면 짜고 놀아났을지 모른다고 미심쩍어한 그 일꾼을 말이오. 방금 당신을 어리둥절하게 한 그 편지를 누가 썼는지, 그리고 루커 씨의 동양 보물들 가운데 어느 것을 그 일꾼이 훔치려 했는지에 대해서는, 브러프 씨, 그 미루어 짐작함이 자못 뚜렷하오.\"\n\n그 미루어 짐작함은 (내가 서둘러 인정했듯) 굳이 짚어 줄 것도 없이 너무나 뚜렷했다. 나는 머스웨이트 씨가 넌지시 말한 그 무렵에 월장석이 루커 씨의 손에 흘러 들어갔다는 것을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었다. 나의 유일한 물음은, 어떻게 그 인도인들이 그 정황을 알아냈는가 하는 것이었다. 이 물음은 (내 생각에 온갖 것 가운데 다루기 가장 어려운 것이었는데) 이제 나머지처럼 그 답을 얻은 셈이었다. 변호사인 나였으나, 나는 머스웨이트 씨가 여기까지 나를 이끌어 온 그 미로의 마지막 굽이들을 지나 눈먼 나를 데려가 주리라 믿어도 좋겠다고 느끼기 시작했다. 나는 그에게 이렇게 말하는 찬사를 바쳤고, 나의 그 자그마한 양보가 아주 정중하게 받아들여지는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"우리가 나아가기 전에 이번에는 당신이 나에게 한 가지 정보를 주어야겠소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"누군가 그 월장석을 요크셔에서 런던으로 가져갔을 것이오. 그리고 누군가 그것을 담보로 돈을 마련했을 것이오, 그러지 않았다면 그것이 결코 루커 씨의 수중에 있지 않았을 테니 말이오. 그 사람이 누구였는지에 대해 무슨 밝혀진 것이 있소?\"\n\n\"내가 아는 바로는 아무것도 없소.\"\n\n\"고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 관한 소문이 (있지 않았소?) 있었소. 나는 그가 저명한 자선가라고 들었소—그것부터가, 우선 그에게 결정적으로 불리한 점이오.\"\n\n나는 이 점에서 머스웨이트 씨에게 진심으로 뜻을 같이했다. 동시에, 나는 그에게 (굳이 말할 것도 없이, 버린더 아가씨의 이름은 들먹이지 않고) 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가, 내가 전적으로 다툼의 여지가 없다고 장담할 수 있는 증거로써, 온갖 혐의를 벗었음을 알려 줄 의무를 느꼈다.\n\n\"아주 좋소,\" 하고 머스웨이트 씨가 조용히 말했다. \"그 일은 시간에 맡겨 밝혀지게 둡시다. 그동안, 브러프 씨, 우리는 당신을 위해 다시 그 인도인들에게로 돌아가야 하오. 그들의 런던행은 그저 그들이 또 한 번의 낭패의 희생물이 되는 것으로 끝나고 말았소. 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 그들의 둘째 기회를 잃은 것은, 내 생각에, 주로 루커 씨의 교활함과 앞을 내다보는 눈 덕분이오—그가 공연히 그 번창하고 유서 깊은 고리대업의 꼭대기에 앉아 있는 것이 아니오! 자기가 부리던 그 사내를 즉각 내쫓음으로써, 그는 인도인들에게서 그들의 공범이 그 집으로 들어가는 데에 베풀었을 도움을 앗아 버렸소. 월장석을 즉각 제 은행가에게로 옮김으로써, 그는 그 음모자들이 자기를 털 새 계획을 채 갖추기도 전에 그들의 허를 찔렀소. 이 뒤엣일에서, 인도인들이 어떻게 그가 한 일을 미심쩍어했고, 어떻게 그들이 그의 은행가 영수증을 손에 넣으려 꾀했는지는, 너무나 근래의 일이라 새삼 늘어놓을 것도 없소. 그들이 이제 월장석이 다시 한 번 저희 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있음을—('큰 값어치의 귀중품'이라는 두루뭉술한 표현으로) 어느 은행가의 금고실에 맡겨져 있음을 안다는 것만 말해 두면 넉넉하오. 자, 브러프 씨, 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 그들의 셋째 기회는 무엇이오? 그리고 그것은 언제 오겠소?\"\n\n그 물음이 그의 입술을 스쳐 나오는 순간, 나는 마침내 그 인도인이 내 사무소를 찾아온 동기를 꿰뚫어 보았다!\n\n\"알겠소!\" 하고 나는 외쳤다. \"그 인도인들은, 우리처럼, 월장석이 저당 잡혔음을 당연한 일로 여기고 있소. 그리고 그들은 그 저당물을 되찾을 수 있는 가장 이른 시기를 틀림없이 알고 싶어 하오—그것이 곧 그 다이아몬드가 은행의 안전한 보관에서 나올 수 있는 가장 이른 시기일 테니 말이오!\"\n\n\"내가 당신에게 기회만 정당히 주면 당신 스스로 알아내리라고 말했지요, 브러프 씨. 그 월장석이 저당 잡힌 때로부터 한 해가 지나면, 인도인들은 그들의 셋째 기회를 노리고 지켜볼 것이오. 그들이 얼마나 오래 기다려야 하는지는 루커 씨 자신의 입이 그들에게 일러 주었고, 당신의 존경할 만한 권위가 루커 씨의 말이 참임을 그들에게 확인해 주었소. 우리가 얼추 어림잡아, 그 다이아몬드가 그 돈놀이꾼의 손에 흘러 들어간 것이 언제라고 보오?\"\n\n\"지난 유월 말경이오,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"내가 헤아릴 수 있는 한으로는 말이오.\"\n\n\"그리고 지금 우리는 사십팔 년에 있소. 아주 좋소. 만일 그 월장석을 저당 잡힌 그 알 수 없는 사람이 한 해 안에 그것을 되찾을 수 있다면, 그 보석은 사십구 년 유월 말에 다시 그 사람의 수중에 있게 될 것이오. 그 무렵 나는 잉글랜드와 잉글랜드 소식에서 수천 마일 떨어진 곳에 있을 거요. 허나 그것을 적어 두고, 그때 런던에 있도록 채비하는 것이 당신에게는 애써 볼 값어치가 있을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"당신은 무언가 심상찮은 일이 일어나리라 여기시오?\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"나는 내가,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"그 월장석을 주머니에 넣고 은행의 문을 넘느니, 차라리 중앙아시아의 가장 사나운 광신자들 틈에 있는 편이 더 안전하리라 여기오. 인도인들은 잇달아 두 번 낭패를 보았소, 브러프 씨. 그들이 세 번째로는 낭패를 보지 않으리라는 것이 나의 굳은 믿음이오.\"\n\n그것이 그 화제에 관해 그가 한 마지막 말이었다. 커피가 들어왔다. 손님들은 자리에서 일어나 방 안 이곳저곳으로 흩어졌다. 그리고 우리는 위층에서 그 만찬 자리의 숙녀들과 어울렸다.\n\n나는 그 날짜를 적어 두었다. 그리고 그 적어 둔 것을 여기 다시 옮기며 나의 이야기를 맺더라도 그리 나쁘지는 않으리라.\n\n사십구 년 유월. 유월 말경, 인도인들의 소식을 기다릴 것.\n\n그리고 그것을 마쳤으니, 나는 이제 더는 쓸 자격이 없는 이 펜을, 나의 다음을 잇는 글쓴이에게 넘긴다.\n\n프랭클린 블레이크가 기고함.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n천팔백사십구 년 봄에 나는 동방을 떠돌고 있었는데, 그 무렵 나는 몇 달 전에 세워 두었던, 그리고 런던의 내 변호사와 내 은행가에게 알려 두었던 여행 계획을 얼마 전 바꾼 참이었다.\n\n이 변경으로 인해 나는 내 하인 하나를 어느 도시의 잉글랜드 영사에게 보내어 내 편지와 송금을 받아 오게 할 필요가 생겼는데, 그 도시는 나의 새 여행 일정에서는 더 이상 내가 머무를 곳의 하나로 들어 있지 않았다. 그 사내는 정해진 곳과 정해진 때에 나와 다시 합류하기로 되어 있었다. 그의 탓이 아닌 사고 하나가 그 심부름길에서 그를 지체시켰다. 일주일 동안 나와 내 사람들은 어느 사막의 언저리에 진을 치고 기다렸다. 그 기간이 끝날 무렵, 그 사라졌던 사내가 그 돈과 편지들을 지니고, 내 천막 어귀에 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"안됐지만 나쁜 소식을 가져온 것 같습니다, 나리.\" 하고 그가 말하며, 편지 하나를 가리켰는데, 그것은 둘레에 상장(喪章)의 검은 테가 둘려 있었고, 그 위의 주소는 브러프 씨의 필적이었다.\n\n이런 경우에, 나는 마음 졸이는 조바심만큼 견디기 어려운 것을 알지 못한다. 그 검은 테가 둘린 편지가 내가 맨 먼저 뜯은 편지였다.\n\n그것은 나에게, 내 아버지가 돌아가셨다는 것과, 내가 그의 막대한 재산의 상속인이라는 것을 알려 주었다. 이렇듯 내 손에 떨어진 그 재물은 그와 더불어 그 책임을 함께 가져왔고, 브러프 씨는 나에게 지체 없이 잉글랜드로 돌아오기를 간청했다.\n\n이튿날 동틀 무렵, 나는 내 조국으로 돌아가는 길에 올라 있었다.\n\n내가 잉글랜드를 떠날 무렵의 나를 그려 낸, 내 옛 벗 베터리지의 그 초상은 (내 생각에) 조금 과장되어 있다. 그는, 제 나름의 예스럽고 별난 투로, 그의 젊은 여주인이 내 외국 물든 교육을 두고 던진 그 숱한 빈정거림 가운데 하나를 진지하게 새겨듣고 말았다. 그리하여 그는, 내 활달한 사촌이 다만 우스개로 찾아냈다고 짐짓 말했을 뿐이고, 우리의 착한 베터리지 자신의 머릿속 말고는 실은 아무런 실체도 없었던, 내 성품의 그 프랑스식·독일식·이탈리아식 면들을 자기가 정말로 보았노라고 스스로 믿어 버렸다. 허나, 이 흠만 빼면, 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없다—그가 나를, 레이철의 처사에 가슴 깊이 상처 입고, 내 생애의 가장 쓰라린 실망이 안긴 고통이 가장 날카로운 그 첫머리에 잉글랜드를 떠나는 사람으로 그려 낸 것은, 진실을 넘어선 무엇도 말한 것이 아니라는 것을.\n\n나는, 마음먹고—만일 변화와 부재가 나에게 도움이 될 수 있다면—그녀를 잊으려, 외국으로 갔다. 이런 처지에 놓인 사람에게 변화와 부재가 정녕 도움이 된다는 것을 부인하는 것은, 나는 확신하거니와, 인간 본성에 대한 참된 견해가 아니다. 그것들은 그의 주의를 오직 제 슬픔만을 곱씹는 데에서 억지로 떼어 놓는다. 나는 결코 그녀를 잊지 못했다. 허나 기억의 그 아릿한 아픔은, 시간과 거리와 새로움이 레이철과 나 사이에 점점 더 효과적으로 끼어들면서, 조금씩 그 가장 모진 쓰라림을 잃어 갔다.\n\n한편으로, 못지않게 분명한 것은, 고향으로 발길을 돌리는 그 행위와 더불어, 그토록 꾸준히 자리를 넓혀 오던 그 약이, 이제 꼭 그만큼 꾸준히 도로 물러나기 시작했다는 것이다. 그녀가 사는 그 나라에, 그리고 그녀를 다시 볼 전망에 가까이 다가갈수록, 그녀의 영향력은 그만큼 더 뿌리치기 어렵게 나에 대한 제 손아귀를 되찾기 시작했다. 잉글랜드를 떠날 적에 그녀는 내가 그 이름이 내 입술을 스치도록 허락했을 세상에서 가장 마지막 사람이었다. 잉글랜드로 돌아오자, 브러프 씨와 내가 다시 만났을 때, 그녀는 내가 맨 먼저 안부를 물은 사람이었다.\n\n나는, 물론, 내가 없는 동안에 일어난 온갖 일을 전해 들었다. 다시 말해, 베터리지의 이야기에 이어 여기 적혀 온 모든 것을 말이다—오직 한 가지 정황만은 예외로 하고. 브러프 씨는, 그때는, 레이철과 고드프리 에이블화이트로 하여금 양쪽에서 그 혼약을 물리게끔 은밀히 움직인 그 동기들을 나에게 알려 줄 자유가 자기에게 있다고 여기지 않았다. 나는 이 미묘한 화제에 관해 그를 난처하게 할 물음으로 그를 괴롭히지 않았다. 그녀가 한때 고드프리의 아내가 될 것을 생각했다는 것을 듣고 일어난 질투 어린 실망 뒤에, 곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에 그녀 자신이 경솔하게 처신했음을 깨달았다는 것과, 그녀가 스스로 제 혼약에서 풀려났다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 나에게는 넉넉한 위안이었다.\n\n지난 일의 이야기를 들었으니, 나의 다음 물음은 (여전히 레이철에 대한 물음이었다!) 자연스럽게 현재로 나아갔다. 그녀는 브러프 씨의 집을 떠난 뒤 누구의 보살핌에 맡겨졌는가? 그리고 그녀는 지금 어디에 살고 있는가?\n\n그녀는 고인이 된 존 버린더 경의, 홀로된 누이 되는 어느 분—메리듀 부인이라는 이의 보살핌 아래 살고 있었는데, 그녀의 어머니의 유언 집행인들이 그 부인에게 후견인 노릇을 해 주기를 청했고, 그 부인이 그 제안을 받아들였던 것이다. 그들은 서로 더없이 잘 지내고 있으며, 지금은 사교철을 나려고 포틀랜드 플레이스의 메리듀 부인의 집에 자리 잡고 있다고 나에게 전해졌다.\n\n이 소식을 받은 지 반 시간 만에, 나는 포틀랜드 플레이스로 가는 길에 올라 있었다—브러프 씨에게 그것을 털어놓을 용기는 차마 내지 못한 채로!\n\n문을 열어 준 사내는 버린더 아가씨가 집에 있는지 없는지 확실히 알지 못했다. 나는 그 물음을 매듭짓는 가장 빠른 방편으로, 내 명함을 들려 그를 위층으로 올려 보냈다. 그 사내는 속을 헤아릴 수 없는 낯빛으로 다시 내려와, 버린더 아가씨가 외출 중이라고 나에게 알렸다.\n\n다른 사람들 같으면 나에게 일부러 없는 척한 것이 아닌가 의심했을지도 모른다. 허나 레이철을 의심하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다. 나는 그날 저녁 여섯 시에 다시 찾아오겠다는 말을 남겼다.\n\n여섯 시에, 나는 두 번째로 버린더 아가씨가 집에 없다는 말을 들었다. 나에게 남겨진 전갈이 있는가? 나에게 남겨진 전갈은 없었다. 버린더 아가씨가 내 명함을 받지 못했는가? 그 하인은 나에게 용서를 빌었다—버린더 아가씨는 그것을 받으셨다고.\n\n그 미루어 짐작함은 너무나 뚜렷하여 물리칠 수 없었다. 레이철은 나를 만나기를 마다한 것이다.\n\n내 편에서, 나는 적어도 그 까닭을 알아내려는 시도조차 없이 이런 대접을 받기를 마다했다. 나는 메리듀 부인에게 내 이름을 올려 보내, 그녀가 편한 시각을 아무 때나 정하여 나에게 직접 면담의 은혜를 베풀어 주기를 청했다.\n\n메리듀 부인은 나를 곧바로 맞아들이는 데에 아무 어려움을 두지 않았다. 나는 아늑하고 자그마한 응접실로 안내되어 들었고, 아늑하고 자그마한 늙은 부인 앞에 서 있는 나 자신을 보았다. 그녀는 오로지 나를 위하여 큰 유감과 적잖은 놀라움을 느낄 만큼 마음씨가 고왔다. 허나 그녀는 동시에, 나에게 아무런 설명을 해 줄 처지도, 오로지 개인의 감정 문제에만 관련되는 듯싶은 사안을 두고 레이철을 다그칠 처지도 아니었다. 이것은, 무엇으로도 지치게 할 수 없는 예의 바른 참을성으로, 거듭거듭 되풀이되었다. 그리고 이것이 내가 메리듀 부인에게 청하여 얻은 전부였다.\n\n나의 마지막 방편은 레이철에게 편지를 쓰는 것이었다. 내 하인은 이튿날 답장을 기다리라는 엄한 분부와 함께 그녀에게 편지를 가져갔다.\n\n답장은, 글자 그대로 단 한 문장으로, 돌아왔다.\n\n\"버린더 아가씨는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨와 어떠한 서신 왕래도 나누기를 삼가고자 합니다.\"\n\n그녀를 그토록 사랑했으면서도, 나는 그 답장에 담긴 나에 대한 모욕을 분노에 차 느꼈다. 내가 채 제정신을 되찾기도 전에, 브러프 씨가 볼일로 나에게 이야기하러 들어왔다. 나는 그 볼일을 그 자리에서 물리치고, 그 온 사연을 그의 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다. 그는 메리듀 부인 자신과 다름없이 나를 밝혀 줄 능력이 없는 것으로 드러났다. 나는 그에게 레이철이 듣는 데에서 나에 대한 어떤 험담이 나온 적이 있는지 물었다. 브러프 씨는 내가 그 표적이 된 어떤 험담도 아는 바가 없었다. 그녀가 브러프 씨의 지붕 아래 머무는 동안 어떤 식으로든 나를 언급한 적이 있는가? 한 번도 없었다. 나의 그 오랜 부재 내내, 그녀가 내가 살아 있는지 죽었는지조차 물은 적이 있는가? 그런 물음은 그녀의 입술을 한 번도 스친 적이 없었다. 나는 내 수첩에서, 가엾은 레이디 버린더가 내가 요크셔의 그녀의 집을 떠나던 날 프리징홀에서 나에게 써 보낸 그 편지를 꺼냈다. 그리고 나는 그 안의 이 두 문장에 브러프 씨의 주의를 돌렸다.\n\n\"그 잃어버린 보석의 조사에 자네가 베푼 그 값진 도움은, 지금 레이철의 마음의 이 끔찍한 상태에서는, 아직도 용서받지 못한 잘못일세. 이 일에서 눈을 가린 채 움직이며, 자네는 자네의 애씀을 통해 그 아이의 비밀이 드러날 위협을 저도 모르게 가함으로써, 그 아이가 짊어져야 했던 그 근심의 무게를 더해 놓았네.\"\n\n\"있을 수 있는 일이오,\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"거기 그려진 나에 대한 그 감정이, 지금도 나에 대해 여전히 그토록 쓰라리다는 것이?\"\n\n브러프 씨는 꾸밈없이 괴로워하는 낯빛이었다.\n\n\"당신이 굳이 대답을 바란다면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 그녀의 처신에 그것 말고 다른 해석을 붙일 수 없음을 인정하오.\"\n\n나는 종을 울려, 내 하인에게 내 여행 가방을 꾸리고 철도 안내서를 사 오도록 분부했다. 브러프 씨가, 놀라움에 차, 내가 무엇을 하려는 것이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"나는 요크셔로 가려 하오,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"다음 기차로 말이오.\"\n\n\"무슨 목적으로인지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"브러프 씨, 그 다이아몬드의 조사에 내가 저도 모르게 베푼 그 도움은, 거의 한 해 전에, 레이철의 마음속에서 용서받지 못한 잘못이었소. 그리고 그것은 지금도 여전히 용서받지 못한 잘못으로 남아 있소. 나는 그 처지를 받아들이지 않겠소! 나는 그녀가 제 어머니에게 침묵한 그 비밀과, 나에 대한 그 적의의 비밀을 기어이 알아내고야 말겠소. 만일 시간과 애씀과 돈으로 그것을 할 수 있다면, 나는 월장석을 훔친 그 도둑에게 내 손을 얹고야 말겠소!\"\n\n그 인품 있는 노신사는 나를 타이르려—나에게 이치에 귀 기울이게 하려—요컨대, 나를 향한 제 도리를 다하려 애썼다. 나는 그가 다그칠 수 있는 온갖 말에 귀먹은 채였다. 그 순간, 이 세상의 어떤 사려도 내 안에 든 그 결심을 흔들어 놓지 못했을 것이다.\n\n\"나는 그 조사를 다시 이어 나가겠소,\" 하고 나는 말을 이었다. \"내가 그것을 놓아 버린 그 지점에서 말이오. 그리고 나는 그것을 한 걸음 한 걸음 앞으로 좇아, 현재에 다다르기까지 나아가겠소. 내가 남겨 둔 그대로의 그 증거에는 빠진 고리들이 있는데, 그것을 게이브리얼 베터리지가 채워 줄 수 있소. 그러니 게이브리얼 베터리지에게로 나는 가오!\"\n\n그날 저녁 해 질 녘에, 나는 다시 그 잊지 못할 테라스에 서서, 그 평화롭고 오래된 시골집을 한 번 더 바라보았다. 그 인적 없는 뜰에서 내가 맨 처음 본 사람은 정원사였다. 그는 한 시간 전에, 뒤뜰의 늘 그 자리에서 볕을 쬐고 있는 베터리지를 두고 온 참이었다. 나는 그곳을 잘 알았다. 그래서 나는 몸소 가서 그를 찾겠노라고 말했다.\n\n나는 그 낯익은 오솔길과 통로를 돌아 걸어, 그 뜰의 열린 문 안을 들여다보았다.\n\n그가 거기 있었다—다시는 돌아오지 않을 그 행복한 날들의, 정다운 옛 벗이—그가 거기, 그 옛 모퉁이에, 그 옛 벌통 모양 의자에, 입에 파이프를 물고, 무릎에 그의 「로빈슨 크루소」를 얹고, 그의 두 벗인 개들을 양옆에 두어 꾸벅꾸벅 졸게 한 채로 있었다! 내가 서 있던 그 자리에서, 저무는 해의 마지막 비스듬한 빛살이 내 그림자를 내 앞으로 드리웠다. 그 개들이 그것을 보았거나, 아니면 그것들의 날카로운 후각이 내가 다가옴을 그것들에게 알렸거나 했다. 그것들은 으르렁거리며 벌떡 일어섰다. 제 차례로 소스라친 그 노인은 한마디 말로 그것들을 달래고는, 흐릿해진 제 눈을 손으로 가려 그늘을 지우고, 그 문가의 사람 모습을 미심쩍은 듯 바라보았다.\n\n내 두 눈은 눈물로 가득했다. 나는 그에게 말을 건넬 만큼 나 자신을 다잡을 수 있기까지 잠시 기다려야만 했다.\n\n# CHAPTER II\n\n\"베터리지!\" 하고 나는 그의 무릎 위의 그 잊지 못할 책을 가리키며 말했다. \"「로빈슨 크루소」가 오늘 저녁 자네에게, 프랭클린 블레이크를 보게 되리라 여겨도 좋다고 일러 주던가?\"\n\n\"어이쿠 맙소사, 프랭클린 나리!\" 하고 그 노인이 부르짖었다. \"바로 그것이 「로빈슨 크루소」가 한 일입지요!\"\n\n그는 내 부축을 받아 간신히 일어서서, 잠시 「로빈슨 크루소」와 나 사이를 이리저리 번갈아 보며 서 있었는데, 겉보기에 우리 둘 가운데 어느 쪽이 자기를 가장 놀라게 했는지 가려내지 못하는 눈치였다. 그 판정은 그 책 편을 들어 주는 것으로 끝났다. 그것을 두 손으로 제 앞에 펼쳐 든 채, 그는 그 놀라운 책을 이루 말할 수 없는 기대에 찬 뚫어진 눈길로 살폈다—마치 로빈슨 크루소 그 사람이 책장 밖으로 걸어 나와, 우리에게 몸소 면담의 영예를 베풀어 주기라도 기대하는 것처럼.\n\n\"바로 이 대목입니다, 프랭클린 나리!\" 하고 그는 말할 힘을 되찾자마자 말했다. \"이 몸이 빵을 먹고 사는 것이 진실이듯, 나리, 이것이 나리께서 들어오시기 바로 그 순간에 제가 읽고 있던 대목입지요! 백쉰여섯 쪽에 이렇게 나와 있습니다요—'나는 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼, 또는 유령이라도 본 사람처럼 서 있었다.' 만일 그것이 '프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 느닷없는 출현을 기다리라'는 말이나 진배없는 것이 아니라면—영어라는 말에는 아무 뜻도 없는 것입지요!\" 하고 베터리지는 그 책을 탁 소리 나게 덮고, 마침내 한 손을 자유롭게 놓아 내가 그에게 내민 손을 잡으며 말했다.\n\n나는, 그런 상황에서라면 지극히 당연하게도, 그가 나에게 물음을 퍼부어 댈 줄 알았다. 허나 아니었다—그 집안의 한 사람이 (어찌 되었든 간에!) 그 집의 방문객으로 나타났을 때, 그 늙은 하인의 마음속에서 가장 위에 있는 충동은 손님을 대접하려는 충동이었다.\n\n\"들어오시지요, 프랭클린 나리.\" 하고 그는 제 뒤의 문을 열며, 그 예스럽고 별난 몸짓의 인사와 함께 말했다. \"무엇이 나리를 여기까지 오게 했는지는 나중에 여쭙겠습니다—우선 나리를 편히 모셔야지요. 나리께서 떠나신 뒤로 서글픈 변화가 있었습니다. 집은 닫혔고, 하인들은 떠났습지요. 그런 건 마음 쓰지 마십시오! 제가 나리의 저녁을 지어 올리겠습니다. 그리고 정원사의 아내가 나리의 잠자리를 봐 드리지요—그리고 지하실에 우리의 그 이름난 라투르 클라레 한 병이 남아 있다면, 나리, 그 병은 나리의 목구멍으로 넘어갈 것입니다. 나리를 환영합니다요, 나리! 진심으로 환영합니다요!\" 하고 그 가엾은 노인은, 그 인적 없는 집의 음울함에 맞서 사내답게 싸우며, 지난 시절의 그 정겹고 정중한 대접으로 나를 맞으며 말했다.\n\n그를 실망시키는 것이 나에게는 괴로웠다. 허나 그 집은, 이제, 레이철의 집이었다. 런던에서 일어난 일 뒤에, 내가 그 안에서 밥을 먹거나 잠을 잘 수 있단 말인가? 자존이라는 지극히 흔한 분별이 나에게—마땅히도—그 문지방을 넘는 것을 금했다.\n\n나는 베터리지의 팔을 잡고, 그를 이끌어 정원으로 나갔다. 달리 도리가 없었다. 나는 그에게 진실을 말해 주지 않을 수 없었다. 레이철에 대한 애착과 나에 대한 애착 사이에서, 그는 일이 이렇게 돌아간 데에 몹시 어리둥절해하고 괴로워했다. 그의 견해는, 그가 그것을 내놓았을 때, 그의 여느 거침없는 투로 주어졌고, 내가 아는 가장 단정적인 철학—베터리지 학파의 철학의 향취를 기분 좋게 물씬 풍겼다.\n\n\"레이철 아가씨께는 흠이 있습지요—저는 그것을 한 번도 부인한 적이 없습니다.\" 하고 그가 말을 뗐다. \"그리고 이따금 콧대를 세우고 내닫는 것이 그중 하나입니다요. 그 아가씨는 나리를 타 넘으려 애써 오셨습니다—그리고 나리는 그것을 참아 오셨지요. 원 참, 프랭클린 나리, 이 나이 되도록 여자를 그보다는 잘 아셔야지요? 고인이 된 베터리지 마누라 이야기를 제가 하는 걸 들으신 적 있으시지요?\"\n\n나는 그가 고인이 된 베터리지 부인 이야기를 하는 것을 꽤 자주 들었었다—그는 어김없이 그녀를, 그 다른 성(性)의 타고난 무름과 어깃장의, 그의 부인할 수 없는 유일한 본보기로 내세우곤 했다. 그 자격으로 그는 이제 그녀를 다시 끄집어냈다.\n\n\"자, 프랭클린 나리. 이제 제 말을 들어 보십시오. 여자마다 콧대 세우고 내닫는 방식이 다 다릅니다요. 고인이 된 베터리지 마누라는, 제가 마침 그 여편네가 마음에 둔 무언가를 안 들어줄 때면 언제든, 바로 그 여자들이 즐겨 타는 짐승 위에 올라 그 운동을 즐겼습지요. 이럴 때 제가 일을 마치고 집에 돌아오기가 무섭게, 어김없이 제 마누라는 부엌 계단 위로 저를 불러, 제가 저를 그리 모질게 다루었으니 저에게 저녁을 지어 줄 마음이 없다고 말하곤 했습니다요. 저는 한동안 그것을 참았습지요—꼭 나리께서 지금 레이철 아가씨한테 참고 계신 것처럼요. 마침내 제 인내가 바닥났습니다. 저는 아래층으로 내려가, 베터리지 마누라를—애정을 담아서요, 아시겠지요—두 팔로 안아 들고, 통째로 번쩍, 그 여자가 손님을 맞던 가장 좋은 응접실로 데려갔습지요. 저는 '이곳이 임자에게 딱 맞는 자리요, 여보,' 하고는, 그길로 부엌으로 돌아갔습니다. 저는 안에서 문을 걸어 잠그고, 웃옷을 벗고, 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올리고, 제 저녁을 손수 지었습지요. 다 되자, 저는 제가 할 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 솜씨로 그것을 차려 내어, 더없이 흐뭇하게 즐겼습니다. 뒤이어 저는 제 파이프와 저의 그로그 한 모금을 즐겼지요. 그러고는 상을 치우고, 그릇을 씻고, 나이프와 포크를 닦고, 물건들을 제자리에 넣고, 벽난로 아궁이를 쓸었습니다. 온갖 것이 다시, 반들반들 깨끗할 수 있는 만큼 반들반들 깨끗해졌을 때, 저는 문을 열어 베터리지 마누라를 들였습지요. '나는 저녁을 다 먹었소, 여보,' 하고 저는 말했습니다. '그리고 임자의 가장 살가운 소원이 바랄 수 있는 그 모든 대로 부엌을 남겨 둔 것을 임자가 알아주기 바라오.' 그 여자가 사는 남은 평생 동안, 프랭클린 나리, 저는 다시는 제 저녁을 지을 일이 없었습지요! 교훈인즉—나리는 런던에서 레이철 아가씨를 참으셨습니다. 요크셔에서는 참지 마십시오. 집으로 돌아오시지요!\"\n\n도무지 대꾸할 수 없었다! 나는 다만 나의 그 착한 벗에게, 이 경우에는 그의 그 설득의 힘조차 나에게 헛되이 쓰인 것이라고 다짐해 줄 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"참으로 좋은 저녁일세.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 프리징홀까지 걸어가, 그 호텔에 묵겠네. 그러니 자네는 내일 아침 와서 나와 함께 아침을 들게. 자네에게 할 이야기가 있네.\"\n\n베터리지는 무겁게 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"이것 참 진심으로 안됐습니다요.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저는, 프랭클린 나리, 나리와 레이철 아가씨 사이가 다시 모두 매끄럽고 즐겁게 되었다는 말을 듣기를 바랐습지요. 나리께서 굳이 나리 뜻대로 하셔야겠다면, 나리,\" 하고 그는 잠시 곰곰 생각하고서 말을 이었다. \"잠자리 때문에 오늘 밤 프리징홀까지 가실 것은 없습니다. 그보다 더 가까운 데서 얻을 수 있습지요. 여기서 채 이 마일도 안 되는 곳에 호더스톤 농장이 있습니다. 그거라면 나리께서 레이철 아가씨 때문에 마다하시기도 어려울 겁니다요,\" 하고 그 노인은 능청스레 덧붙였다. \"호더스톤은요, 프랭클린 나리, 제 소유의 자유보유지에서 삽지요.\"\n\n베터리지가 그곳을 입에 올리는 순간 나는 그 자리를 떠올렸다. 그 농가는 요크셔의 그 지역에서 가장 어여쁜 개울가의, 바람 자는 내륙의 골짜기에 서 있었다. 그리고 그 농부에게는 남는 침실과 응접실이 하나씩 있었는데, 그는 그것을 화가와 낚시꾼과 여느 나그네들에게 세놓곤 했다. 그 근방에 머무는 동안 그보다 더 마음에 드는 거처를, 나는 도무지 찾기를 바랄 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n\"그 방들을 세놓는가?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"바로 어제, 나리, 호더스톤 부인이 몸소 그 방들을 추천해 달라고 제 좋은 말을 청했습지요.\"\n\n\"그 방들을 얻겠네, 베터리지, 더없이 기쁜 마음으로.\"\n\n우리는, 내가 내 여행 가방을 두고 온 그 뜰로 돌아갔다. 손잡이에 막대기를 꿰어 그 가방을 어깨에 둘러멘 뒤, 베터리지는, 내가 그 벌통 의자에서 그를 놀라게 했을 때 나의 느닷없는 출현이 자아냈던 그 어리둥절함으로 도로 빠져드는 듯했다. 그는 못 믿겠다는 듯 그 집을 보았고, 그러고는 홱 돌아서, 못 믿겠다는 눈길로 나를 한층 더 오래 보았다.\n\n\"이 몸이 세상을 제법 오래 살아 왔습니다요.\" 하고 온 늙은 하인들 가운데 가장 착하고 가장 정다운 이 사람이 말했다. \"허나 이런 일은, 저는 여태 보게 되리라 여겨 본 적이 없습지요. 저기 집이 서 있고, 여기 프랭클린 블레이크 나리가 서 있는데—에잇, 빌어먹을, 그중 하나가 다른 하나에 등을 돌리고, 셋방에 자러 간다니 말입니다!\"\n\n그는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 불길하게 투덜대면서 앞장서 나갔다. \"이제 일어날 수 있는 기적이 딱 하나 더 남았습지요.\" 하고 그가 어깨 너머로 나에게 말했다. \"나리께서 다음으로 하실 일은, 프랭클린 나리, 어릴 적에 저한테서 꾸어 가신 그 칠 실링 육 펜스를 저에게 갚으시는 것일 겁니다요.\"\n\n이 빈정거림의 한 방이 그를 그 자신에 대해서도, 나에 대해서도 한결 나은 기분으로 만들어 놓았다. 우리는 그 집을 떠나, 관리인 오두막의 문을 지나갔다. 일단 그 뜰을 벗어나자, (베터리지의 도덕률에서는) 손님 대접의 의무가 그치고, 호기심의 특권이 시작되었다.\n\n그는, 내가 그와 나란히 서게끔, 뒤로 처졌다. \"산책하기 좋은 저녁입니다요, 프랭클린 나리.\" 하고 그는, 마치 우리가 방금 그 순간 우연히 서로 마주치기라도 한 것처럼 말했다. \"가령 나리께서 프리징홀의 그 호텔로 가셨다면 말입니다, 나리?\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"저는 내일 아침 나리와 함께 아침을 드는 영예를 누렸겠지요.\"\n\n\"그러지 말고 호더스톤 농장으로 와서 나와 아침을 들게.\"\n\n\"친절히 대해 주시니 참으로 고맙습니다요, 프랭클린 나리. 허나 제가 노린 것이 딱히 아침은 아니었습지요. 나리께서 저에게 하실 이야기가 있다고 말씀하신 것 같은데요? 만일 비밀이 아니라면, 나리,\" 하고 베터리지는, 별안간 그 에두르는 길을 버리고 곧은 길로 들어서며 말했다. \"무엇이 나리를, 부디 말씀해 주십시오, 이리 느닷없이 여기까지 내려오게 했는지 저는 몹시 궁금해 죽겠습니다요.\"\n\n\"전에는 무엇이 나를 여기 오게 했던가?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"월장석입지요, 프랭클린 나리. 허나 지금은 무엇이 나리를 오게 했습니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"다시 월장석일세, 베터리지.\"\n\n그 노인은 별안간 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 그 잿빛 땅거미 속에서, 마치 제 두 귀가 저를 속이는 것은 아닌가 의심하는 듯 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"만일 그것이 농담이라면, 나리,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 이 늙은 나이에 머리가 좀 둔해지나 봅니다요. 저는 그걸 못 알아듣겠습니다.\"\n\n\"농담이 아닐세.\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"나는 내가 잉글랜드를 떠날 때 놓아 버린 그 조사를 다시 이어 가려고 여기 왔네. 나는 아직 아무도 하지 못한 일을 하려고—누가 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔는지 알아내려고 여기 왔네.\"\n\n\"그 다이아몬드는 내버려 두십시오, 프랭클린 나리! 제 말을 들으시고, 그 다이아몬드는 내버려 두십시오! 그 저주받은 인도 보석은 그것에 가까이 다가간 누구든 죄 그르쳐 놓았습지요. 나리의 삶의 그 고운 봄철에, 나리—월장석에 참견하다가 나리의 돈과 나리의 심기를 허비하지 마십시오. 커프 부장 자신이 그 일을 죽 쒀 놨는데, 어찌 나리가 (외람되오나) 성공하기를 바라시겠습니까? 커프 부장이요!\" 하고 베터리지는, 나를 향해 집게손가락을 엄히 흔들며 되뇌었다. \"잉글랜드에서 가장 위대한 경관 말입니다!\"\n\n\"내 마음은 정해졌네, 나의 오랜 벗이여. 커프 부장조차 나를 주눅 들게 하지 못하네. 그러고 보니, 나는 조만간 그와 이야기해야 할지도 모르겠군. 근래에 그에 관해 무슨 들은 것이 있는가?\"\n\n\"그 부장은 나리를 돕지 못합니다요, 프랭클린 나리.\"\n\n\"어째서?\"\n\n\"나리께서 떠나신 뒤로, 나리, 경찰 바닥에 한 사건이 있었습지요. 그 위대한 커프가 일에서 물러났습니다. 그는 도킹에 자그마한 오두막 하나를 얻었습지요. 그리고 그는 장미 기르는 일에 눈코 뜰 새가 없습니다. 저는 그것을 그 사람 자신의 필적으로 받았습니다요, 프랭클린 나리. 그는 흰 이끼장미를, 먼저 들장미에 접붙이지 않고 길러 냈습지요. 그리고 정원사 베그비 씨가 도킹으로 가서, 그 부장이 마침내 자기를 이겼음을 인정하기로 되어 있습니다.\"\n\n\"그것은 그리 대수롭지 않네.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 커프 부장의 도움 없이 해내야 하네. 그리고 나는, 우선, 자네를 믿어야 하네.\"\n\n내가 다소 무심하게 말했을 법도 하다.\n\n어쨌든, 베터리지는 내가 방금 그에게 한 그 대답 속의 무언가에 마음이 상한 듯했다. \"저보다 못한 이를 믿으실 수도 있습지요, 프랭클린 나리—그것만은 제가 말씀드릴 수 있습니다,\" 하고 그가 다소 날카롭게 말했다.\n\n그가 되받은 그 어조와, 말을 마친 뒤 그의 태도에서 내가 알아챈 어떤 동요가, 나에게 그가 전하기를 망설이는 어떤 정보를 지니고 있음을 넌지시 일러 주었다.\n\n\"나는 자네가 나를 도와주기를 바라네.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"커프 부장이 뒤에 남기고 간 그 증거의 조각들을 주워 모으는 데에 말일세. 나는 자네가 그것을 할 수 있음을 아네. 자네는 그 이상은 못 하는가?\"\n\n\"저에게서 그 이상 무엇을 바라실 수 있겠습니까, 나리?\" 하고 베터리지는 더없이 겸손한 낯빛으로 물었다.\n\n\"나는 그 이상을 바라네—자네가 방금 한 말로 보아서 말일세.\"\n\n\"한낱 허풍입지요, 프랭클린 나리,\" 하고 그 노인은 고집스레 되받았다. \"어떤 사람들은 허풍쟁이로 태어나, 죽는 날까지 그것을 못 고칩니다요. 저도 그중 하납니다.\"\n\n그에게 취할 방도는 오직 하나였다. 나는 레이철에 대한 그의 마음과 나에 대한 그의 마음에 호소했다.\n\n\"베터리지, 레이철과 내가 다시 좋은 벗이 되었다는 말을 들으면 자네는 기쁘겠는가?\"\n\n\"만일 그것을 의심하신다면, 나리, 제가 나리의 집안을 섬긴 것이 참으로 하찮은 보람밖에 없는 것입지요!\"\n\n\"내가 잉글랜드를 떠나기 전에 레이철이 나를 어찌 대했는지 기억하는가?\"\n\n\"바로 어제 일인 양 똑똑히요! 마님께서 몸소 그것에 관해 나리께 편지를 쓰셨습지요. 그리고 나리는 친절하게도 그 편지를 저에게 보여 주셨습니다. 그 편지에는, 레이철 아가씨가 그 보석을 되찾으려 애쓴 데에 나리께서 맡은 몫 때문에 나리께 죽도록 마음이 상하셨다고 적혀 있었습니다. 그리고 마님도, 나리도, 다른 그 누구도 어째서인지 짐작할 수 없었습지요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇네, 베터리지! 그런데 나는 여행에서 돌아와, 그녀가 여전히 나에게 죽도록 마음 상해 있는 것을 보네. 나는 작년에 그 다이아몬드가 그 밑바탕에 있음을 알았고, 지금도 그 다이아몬드가 그 밑바탕에 있음을 아네. 나는 그녀에게 말을 걸어 보려 했으나, 그녀는 나를 만나려 하지 않네. 나는 그녀에게 편지를 써 보려 했으나, 그녀는 나에게 답하려 하지 않네. 대체, 도무지, 내가 어떻게 이 일을 밝혀낸단 말인가? 월장석의 분실을 캐어 볼 그 기회가, 레이철 자신이 나에게 남겨 준 유일한 조사의 기회일세.\"\n\n그 말들은 분명, 그가 아직 보지 못했던 대로, 그 사건을 그의 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다. 그는 내가 그를 흔들어 놓았음을 나에게 흡족히 확인시켜 주는 물음 하나를 던졌다.\n\n\"이 일에, 프랭클린 나리, 나리 편에는 아무 나쁜 감정이 없는 것입지요—그렇지요?\"\n\n\"약간의 노여움은 있었네.\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"런던을 떠날 적에 말일세. 허나 그것은 이제 죄 닳아 없어졌네. 나는 레이철이 나와 서로 이해에 이르게 되기를 바라네—그리고 나는 그 이상 아무것도 바라지 않네.\"\n\n\"나리는 아무 두려움도 느끼지 않으십니까, 나리—가령 나리께서 무언가 알아내신다 치고—레이철 아가씨에 관해 나리께서 알아내실지 모를 것에 대해서요?\"\n\n나는 그 말들을 자아낸, 그의 젊은 여주인에 대한 그 질투 어린 믿음을 헤아렸다.\n\n\"나는 자네만큼이나 그녀를 굳게 믿네.\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"그녀의 비밀이 낱낱이 드러난들, 자네의 평가에서든 나의 평가에서든 그녀의 자리를 바꿔 놓을 만한 것은 아무것도 드러나지 않을 걸세.\"\n\n베터리지의 마지막 남은 망설임이 그 말에 사라졌다.\n\n\"만일 제가 나리를 돕는 것이 잘못하는 것이라면, 프랭클린 나리,\" 하고 그가 외쳤다. \"제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은 오직—저는 그것을 아직 세상에 나지 않은 갓난아기만큼이나 알아채지 못하고 있다는 것뿐입니다요! 나리께서 홀로 나아가시기만 한다면, 저는 나리를 그 발견으로 가는 길에 올려 드릴 수 있습니다. 우리의 그 가엾은 아이—로재나 스피어먼을 기억하시지요?\"\n\n\"물론이지!\"\n\n\"나리는 늘, 그 아이가 이 월장석의 일에 관해 나리께 털어놓고 싶어 하는 어떤 고백 같은 것을 지녔다고 여기셨지요?\"\n\n\"나는 정녕 그녀의 그 이상한 처신을 달리는 도무지 설명할 수 없었네.\"\n\n\"나리는 그 의심을, 프랭클린 나리, 언제든 나리가 원하실 때 쉬게 하실 수 있습니다요.\"\n\n이제는 내가 우뚝 멈춰 설 차례였다. 나는, 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서, 그의 얼굴을 보려 했으나 헛일이었다. 그 순간의 놀라움에, 나는 다소 조바심을 내며 그것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다.\n\n\"진정하십시오, 나리!\" 하고 베터리지가 말을 이었다. \"저는 제가 말하는 그대로를 뜻합니다. 로재나 스피어먼은 봉인된 편지 한 통을 뒤에 남겼습지요—나리 앞으로 된 편지 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그것이 어디 있는가?\"\n\n\"코브스 홀에 있는 그 아이의 벗 하나가 지니고 있습니다요. 나리께서 지난번 여기 계실 때, 나리, 절름발이 루시 이야기를 들으셨을 겁니다—목발을 짚은 저는 아이 말입니다.\"\n\n\"그 어부의 딸 말인가?\"\n\n\"바로 그 아이입지요, 프랭클린 나리.\"\n\n\"어째서 그 편지가 나에게 부쳐지지 않았는가?\"\n\n\"절름발이 루시는 제 나름의 고집이 있습니다, 나리. 그 아이는 그것을 나리의 손이 아닌 어떤 손에도 내주려 하지 않았습지요. 그리고 나리는 제가 나리께 편지를 쓸 수 있기도 전에 잉글랜드를 떠나셨습니다.\"\n\n\"돌아가세, 베터리지, 지금 당장 그것을 받으러 가세!\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 너무 늦었습니다요, 나리. 우리 해안 사람들은 초를 무척 아끼는지라, 코브스 홀에서는 일찍 잠자리에 듭지요.\"\n\n\"당치 않은 소리! 우리는 반 시간이면 거기 갈 수 있을 걸세.\"\n\n\"나리라면 그럴 수 있겠지요, 나리. 그리고 나리가 거기 다다르셨을 때, 나리는 문이 잠긴 것을 보시게 될 겁니다.\" 그는 우리 아래쪽에서 가물거리는 불빛 하나를 가리켰다. 그리고, 바로 그 순간, 나는 그 저녁의 고요를 뚫고 개울이 졸졸 흐르는 소리를 들었다. \"저기 그 농장입니다, 프랭클린 나리! 오늘 밤은 편히 쉬시고, 내일 아침 저에게로 오십시오—그리 해 주시겠지요?\"\n\n\"자네가 나와 함께 그 어부의 오두막에 가 주겠는가?\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n\"이른 아침에?\"\n\n\"나리가 원하시는 만큼 이른 아침에요, 프랭클린 나리.\"\n\n우리는 그 농장으로 이어지는 그 오솔길을 내려갔다.\n\n# CHAPTER III\n\n호더스톤 농장에서 일어난 일에 대해 나는 그저 더없이 흐릿한 기억밖에 없다.\n\n나는 기억한다—정성 어린 환대, 동방에서라면 온 마을을 다 먹였을 어마어마한 저녁, 더없이 정갈한 침실—그 안에 아쉬운 것이라고는 우리 조상들의 어리석음이 낳은 저 몹쓸 산물, 곧 깃털 침대뿐이었던, 성냥을 여러 번 그어 대고 작은 초 하나에 여러 번 불을 붙이며 지새운 뒤척인 밤, 그리고 해가 떠올라 이제 일어날 전망이 생겼을 때의 그 어마어마한 안도감을.\n\n밤사이 베터리지와, 코브스 홀로 가는 길에 내가 원하는 만큼 이른 아침에 그를 부르러 들르기로 정해 두었었는데—그것은 그 편지를 손에 넣으려는 나의 조바심으로 헤아리면, 내가 그럴 수 있는 한 가장 이른 아침을 뜻했다. 농장에서 아침을 기다리지 않고, 나는 빵 한 조각을 손에 쥐고 길을 나섰는데, 그 훌륭한 베터리지를 잠자리에서 깜짝 놀라게 하는 것은 아닐까 다소 미심쩍었다. 나의 큰 안도로, 그는 나만큼이나 다가올 일에 들떠 있는 것으로 드러났다. 나는 그가 채비를 갖추고, 손에 지팡이를 든 채 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"오늘 아침 몸은 어떤가, 베터리지?\"\n\n\"아주 형편없습니다요, 나리.\"\n\n\"안됐군. 무엇이 괴로운가?\"\n\n\"저는 새로운 병 하나로 괴로워하고 있습지요, 프랭클린 나리, 제가 몸소 지어낸 병 말입니다. 나리를 놀라게 하고 싶지는 않습니다만, 나리도 아침이 다 가기 전에 틀림없이 그것에 걸리실 겁니다요.\"\n\n\"내가 그런단 말인가, 원 참!\"\n\n\"명치께에 거북한 열이 느껴지십니까, 나리? 그리고 정수리께가 고약하게 쿵쿵 뛰는 것이? 아! 아직 아니시라고요? 그것은 코브스 홀에서 나리를 덮칠 겁니다요, 프랭클린 나리. 저는 그것을 탐정 열병이라 부릅지요. 그리고 이 몸은 커프 부장과 함께 있으면서 그것에 처음 걸렸습니다.\"\n\n\"옳거니! 옳거니! 그런데 이 경우의 치료약은, 짐작건대, 로재나 스피어먼의 편지를 여는 것이렷다? 자 가세, 그것을 손에 넣도록 하세.\"\n\n이른 시각이었건만, 우리는 그 어부의 아내가 제 부엌에서 부산히 움직이고 있는 것을 보았다. 베터리지가 나를 소개하자, 착한 욜랜드 부인은 (내가 나중에 알게 된 바로는) 오직 지체 높은 손님을 위해서만 엄격히 아껴 두는 사교의 의례를 치렀다. 그녀는 네덜란드 진 한 병과 깨끗한 파이프 두 개를 탁자에 올려놓고, \"런던에서는 무슨 소식이 있습니까, 나리?\" 하고 말하며 대화의 문을 열었다.\n\n이 어마어마하게 두루뭉술한 물음에 미처 답을 찾기도 전에, 그 부엌의 어두운 구석에서 한 유령 같은 형체가 나를 향해 다가왔다. 파리하고, 사납고, 초췌한 소녀가, 유난히 아름다운 머리칼을 하고, 두 눈에는 매서운 날카로움을 담은 채, 목발을 짚고 절뚝이며 내가 앉아 있는 탁자로 올라와, 마치 내가 그녀로서는 보는 것만으로도 자못 홀리는, 관심과 공포가 뒤섞인 대상이라도 되는 양 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"베터리지 씨,\" 하고 그녀가 나에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. \"부디, 저이의 이름을 다시 한 번 대 주세요.\"\n\n\"이 신사분의 이름은,\" 하고 베터리지가 (신사라는 낱말에 힘을 잔뜩 주어) 대답했다. \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨란다.\"\n\n그 소녀는 나에게 등을 돌리고, 별안간 방을 나가 버렸다. 착한 욜랜드 부인은—내가 믿기로는—제 딸의 그 괴이한 행동에 대해 얼마쯤 사과를 했고, 베터리지는 (아마도) 그것을 정중한 영어로 옮겼다. 나는 이것을 온전히 확신 없이 말한다. 나의 주의는 그 소녀의 목발 소리를 좇는 데에 온통 쏠려 있었다. 쿵-쿵, 나무 계단을 오르고, 쿵-쿵, 우리 머리 위의 방을 가로지르고, 쿵-쿵, 다시 계단을 내려오고—그러더니 그 유령 같은 형체가 열린 문가에, 손에 편지 한 통을 든 채, 나에게 밖으로 나오라 손짓하며 서 있는 것이었다!\n\n나는 미처 다 전해지지 못한 사과들을 뒤에 남겨 두고, 이 이상한 존재를 따라갔다—내 앞에서 절뚝이며, 점점 더 빠르게—그 해변의 비탈을 내려갔다. 그녀는 나를 몇 척의 배 뒤로 이끌어, 그 어촌의 몇 안 되는 사람들의 눈과 귀가 닿지 않는 곳으로 데려가더니, 걸음을 멈추고, 처음으로 나를 마주 보았다.\n\n\"거기 서세요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신을 보고 싶어요.\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정은 잘못 볼 여지가 없었다. 나는 그녀에게 혐오와 역겨움의 가장 강렬한 감정을 불러일으켰다. 여태 어떤 여자도 나를 이런 식으로 본 적이 없었노라고 말할 만큼 내가 우쭐하지는 않으련다. 나는 다만, 여태 어떤 여자도 나로 하여금 그것을 알아채게 한 적이 없었노라는, 좀 더 겸손한 주장에 그치련다. 어떤 상황에서는, 한 사내가 견딜 수 있는 뜯어봄의 길이에도 한계가 있다. 나는 절름발이 루시의 주의를 내 얼굴보다 덜 역겨운 어떤 대상으로 돌려 보려 했다.\n\n\"자네가 나에게 줄 편지를 가지고 있는 것 같은데,\" 하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"저기, 자네 손에 든 그 편지가 그것인가?\"\n\n\"그 말을 다시 해 보세요,\" 하는 것이 내가 받은 유일한 대답이었다.\n\n나는, 제 공부를 익히는 착한 아이처럼, 그 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n\"아니야,\" 하고 그 소녀는 혼잣말로 말했으나, 그 눈은 여전히 인정사정없이 나에게 못 박힌 채였다. \"나는 그 아이가 저이의 얼굴에서 무엇을 보았는지 도무지 알 수가 없어. 나는 그 아이가 저이의 목소리에서 무엇을 들었는지 도무지 짐작할 수가 없어.\" 그녀는 별안간 나에게서 눈을 돌려, 지친 듯 제 목발 끝에 머리를 기댔다. \"오, 나의 가엾은 벗아!\" 하고 그녀는, 내가 듣는 데에서 그녀에게서 흘러나온 첫 부드러운 어조로 말했다. \"오, 나의 잃어버린 님아! 대체 이 사내에게서 무엇을 볼 수 있었단 말이냐?\" 그녀는 다시 사납게 머리를 들어, 나를 한 번 더 바라보았다. \"당신은 먹고 마실 수 있나요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n나는 애써 근엄함을 지키며, \"그렇네,\" 하고 대답했다.\n\n\"당신은 잠잘 수 있나요?\"\n\n\"그렇네.\"\n\n\"당신은 하인살이하는 가엾은 소녀를 볼 때, 아무런 뉘우침도 느끼지 않나요?\"\n\n\"물론 느끼지 않네. 어째서 내가 느껴야 하는가?\"\n\n그녀는 별안간 그 편지를 (흔히 하는 말대로) 내 얼굴에 들이밀었다.\n\n\"받아요!\" 하고 그녀가 노기등등하게 외쳤다. \"나는 이제껏 당신을 본 적이 없어요. 전능하신 하느님이시여, 제가 다시는 이 사람을 보게 되지 않게 하소서.\"\n\n그 이별의 말과 함께 그녀는 있는 힘껏 빠르게 나에게서 절뚝이며 멀어져 갔다. 그녀의 처신에 내가 붙일 수 있는 그 한 가지 해석은, 두말할 것 없이, 누구나 이미 짐작했을 것이다. 나는 다만 그녀가 미쳤으리라고 여길 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그 피할 수 없는 결론에 이르러, 나는 로재나 스피어먼의 편지가 나에게 내놓은, 더 흥미로운 조사의 대상으로 눈을 돌렸다. 그 주소는 다음과 같이 적혀 있었다—\"프랭클린 블레이크 님께. 루시 욜랜드가, (다른 누구에게도 맡기지 말고) 그분 본인의 손에 직접 전할 것.\"\n\n나는 그 봉인을 뜯었다. 그 봉투 안에는 편지 한 통이 들어 있었다. 그리고 이것은, 그 안에 또다시, 종이쪽지 한 장을 담고 있었다. 나는 먼저 그 편지를 읽었다—\n\n\"선생님—만일 선생님께서, 저의 주인이신 레이디 버린더의 집에 머무시는 동안 제가 선생님께 한 행동의 뜻을 궁금해하신다면, 이것에 동봉된 각서에 적힌 대로 하십시오—그리고 선생님을 지켜볼 어떤 사람도 곁에 없이 그것을 하십시오. 선생님의 미천한 하녀,\n\n로재나 스피어먼.\"\n\n나는 다음으로 그 종이쪽지로 눈을 돌렸다. 다음은 그것을 한 글자 한 글자 그대로 옮긴 것이다.\n\n\"각서—조수(潮水)가 바뀔 무렵 흔들리는 모래로 갈 것. 남쪽 사주(砂洲) 위로 걸어 나가, 남쪽 사주 등대와, 코브스 홀 위쪽 해안 경비대 초소의 깃대가 한 줄로 나란히 놓일 때까지 갈 것. 그 바위 위에, 막대기든 무엇이든 곧은 물건 하나를, 내 손을 이끌도록, 꼭 그 등대와 그 깃대의 선에 맞추어 놓을 것. 이것을 할 때, 그 막대기의 한쪽 끝이, 그 바위의 늪 모래를 내려다보는 쪽 가장자리에 오도록 조심할 것. 그 막대기를 따라, 해초 사이로 (등대 쪽을 가리키는 막대기 끝에서부터 시작하여), 쇠사슬을 더듬어 찾을 것. 찾거든, 그 쇠사슬을 따라 손을 옮겨, 그것이 바위 가장자리를 넘어 늪 모래 속으로 뻗어 내린 부분에 이를 때까지 갈 것. 그러고 나서, 그 사슬을 잡아당길 것.\"\n\n내가 그 마지막 말들—원문에는 밑줄이 쳐진—을 다 읽은 바로 그때, 나는 내 뒤에서 베터리지의 목소리를 들었다. 그 탐정 열병의 창안자는 그 뿌리칠 수 없는 병에 완전히 굴복해 버린 참이었다. \"저는 더는 못 참겠습니다, 프랭클린 나리. 그 아이의 편지에 뭐라 적혀 있습니까? 제발, 나리, 저희에게 말씀해 주십시오, 그 아이의 편지에 뭐라 적혀 있습니까?\"\n\n나는 그에게 그 편지와 그 각서를 건넸다. 그는 그 첫째 것을 그리 흥미로워하는 기색 없이 읽었다. 허나 둘째 것—그 각서—은 그에게 강한 인상을 남겼다.\n\n\"부장이 그리 말했습지요!\" 하고 베터리지가 부르짖었다. \"처음부터 끝까지, 나리, 부장은 그 아이가 그 은닉처의 각서를 가지고 있다고 말했습니다. 그리고 여기 그것이 있습니다! 맙소사, 프랭클린 나리, 여기, 그 위대한 커프를 비롯해 온 사람을 어리둥절하게 했던 그 비밀이, 이를테면, 나리께 제 모습을 드러내려고 채비를 갖추고 기다리고 있습니다요! 지금은 썰물입니다, 나리, 누구든 스스로 보면 알 수 있듯이요. 조수가 바뀌기까지 얼마나 남았을까요?\" 그는 고개를 들어, 우리에게서 얼마쯤 떨어진 데서 그물을 손질하고 있는 젊은이 하나를 눈여겨보았다. \"태미 브라이트!\" 하고 그가 목청껏 소리쳤다.\n\n\"들려요!\" 하고 태미가 되받아 소리쳤다.\n\n\"조수가 언제 바뀌지?\"\n\n\"한 시간 뒤에요.\"\n\n우리는 둘 다 우리의 시계를 보았다.\n\n\"해안을 돌아서 갈 수 있습니다, 프랭클린 나리,\" 하고 베터리지가 말했다. \"그리고 그렇게 하면 시간이 넉넉히 남는 채로 그 늪 모래에 다다를 수 있습지요. 어떻게 하시겠습니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"자 가세!\"\n\n흔들리는 모래로 가는 길에, 나는 베터리지에게 청하여 커프 부장의 조사 무렵의 (로재나 스피어먼에 관련되는 한에서의) 사건들에 대한 나의 기억을 되살렸다. 나의 옛 벗의 도움으로, 나는 곧 그 정황들의 잇따름을 또렷이 내 마음에 새겨 넣었다. 온 집안이 그녀가 제 방에서 앓고 있는 줄로 믿었을 때의 로재나의 프리징홀행—문을 걸어 잠근 채, 초를 아침까지 태우며 밤 시간을 보낸 로재나의 알 수 없는 일—로재나가 욜랜드 부인에게서 옻칠한 양철상자와 개사슬 두 개를 수상쩍게 사들인 것—로재나가 흔들리는 모래에 무언가를 숨겼다는 부장의 단정적인 확신과, 그 무언가가 무엇인지에 대한 부장의 전적인 무지—월장석의 분실을 캐다 만 그 조사의 이 온갖 이상한 결과들이, 우리가 그 늪 모래에 다다라, 남쪽 사주라 불리는 그 낮은 바위 턱 위로 함께 걸어 나갔을 때, 다시 또렷이 내 앞에 있었다.\n\n베터리지의 도움으로, 나는 곧 그 등대와 해안 경비대의 깃대가 한 줄로 나란히 보이는 알맞은 자리에 섰다. 그 각서를 우리의 길잡이로 삼아, 우리는 다음으로 내 막대기를 그 바위의 울퉁불퉁한 바닥 위에, 우리가 할 수 있는 한 정확히, 필요한 방향으로 놓았다. 그러고 나서 우리는 우리의 시계를 한 번 더 보았다.\n\n조수가 바뀌기까지 아직 이십 분쯤이 모자랐다. 나는 이 사이의 시간을, 젖고 미끄러운 바위 바닥 위가 아니라, 해변에서 기다리자고 했다. 마른 모래에 이르러, 나는 앉을 채비를 했다. 그런데 나의 큰 놀라움으로, 베터리지는 나를 떠날 채비를 하는 것이었다.\n\n\"어디 가려는 겐가?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"편지를 다시 보십시오, 나리, 그러면 아실 겁니다.\"\n\n편지를 흘긋 보니, 내가 나의 발견을 할 때에 그것을 홀로 하도록 당부받았음이 떠올랐다.\n\n\"이런 때에 나리를 두고 가는 것이 저에게는 참으로 괴롭습니다요,\" 하고 베터리지가 말했다. \"허나 그 아이는 끔찍한 죽음을 맞았습지요, 가엾은 것이—그리고 저는, 프랭클린 나리, 그 아이의 그 소원을 들어주라는 어떤 부름 같은 것을 저에게 느낍니다. 게다가,\" 하고 그는 은근히 덧붙였다. \"그 편지에는 나리께서 나중에 그 비밀을 털어놓으시는 것을 막는 것은 아무것도 없습니다. 저는 전나무 숲 언저리에서 어정거리며, 나리께서 저를 데리러 오실 때까지 기다리겠습니다. 되도록 오래 걸리지 않으시길, 나리. 탐정 열병은, 이런 상황에서는, 다루기 쉬운 병이 아닙지요.\"\n\n그 이별의 당부와 함께, 그는 나를 떠났다.\n\n그 기다림의 시간은, 시간의 잣대로 재면 짧았건만, 조바심의 잣대로 재면 어마어마한 크기를 띠었다. 이것은 담배 피우는 그 더없이 값진 버릇이 각별히 소중하고 위안이 되는 그런 경우의 하나였다. 나는 여송연 하나에 불을 붙이고, 그 해변의 비탈에 앉았다.\n\n햇빛은 내가 볼 수 있는 온갖 대상 위에 그 구름 한 점 없는 아름다움을 쏟아부었다. 더없이 상쾌한 공기가 그저 살아 숨 쉬는 행위마저 하나의 호사로 만들었다. 그 외로운 자그마한 만(灣)조차 명랑한 낯빛으로 아침을 맞았다. 그리고 그 늪 모래 자체의 드러난 젖은 표면마저, 황금빛 밝음으로 반짝이며, 그 지나가는 미소 아래 제 거짓된 갈색 얼굴의 공포를 감추었다. 그것은 내가 잉글랜드로 돌아온 뒤로 본 가장 화창한 날이었다.\n\n조수는 내 여송연이 다 타기 전에 바뀌었다. 나는 그 모래의 예비적인 들썩임을 보았고, 뒤이어 그 표면 위로 기어드는 그 무시무시한 떨림을 보았다—마치 어떤 공포의 정령이 그 밑의 헤아릴 수 없는 심연 속에서 살아 움직이며 몸서리치기라도 하는 듯이. 나는 여송연을 던져 버리고, 다시 그 바위로 돌아갔다.\n\n그 각서 속의 나에 대한 지시는, 등대에 가장 가까운 끝에서 시작하여, 그 막대기가 그은 선을 따라 더듬으라고 나에게 이르고 있었다.\n\n나는, 이런 식으로, 그 바위의 가장자리 말고는 아무것도 만나지 못한 채, 그 막대기를 따라 절반을 넘어 나아갔다. 허나 한두 치 더 나아가자, 나의 참을성이 보답을 받았다. 내 집게손가락이 겨우 닿을락 말락 한, 좁고 자그마한 틈 속에서, 나는 그 쇠사슬을 만졌다. 다음으로, 촉감으로, 그것을 늪 모래 쪽으로 따라가려 하자, 나는 나의 나아감이 두툼하게 자란 해초에 가로막힌 것을 알았다—그것은, 두말할 것 없이, 로재나 스피어먼이 제 은닉처를 고른 뒤로 흘러간 시간 동안, 그 틈 속에 스스로 얽혀 든 것이었다.\n\n그 해초를 뽑아내는 것도, 그 사이로 내 손을 밀어 넣는 것도 똑같이 불가능했다. 늪 모래에 가장 가까이 놓인 막대기 끝이 가리키는 지점을 표시해 둔 뒤, 나는 나 나름의 방책으로 그 쇠사슬 찾기를 이어 가기로 정했다. 나의 생각은, 그 쇠사슬이 모래로 들어가는 지점에서 그 잃어버린 자취를 되찾을 요행을 노려, 그 바위 바로 아래를 곧장 \"재어\" 보는 것이었다. 나는 그 막대기를 집어 들고, 그 남쪽 사주의 벼랑 끝에 무릎을 꿇었다.\n\n이 자세에서, 내 얼굴은 그 늪 모래의 표면에서 몇 자 안 되는 거리에 있었다. 그 흉측한 떨림의 발작으로 이따금 여전히 요동치는 그것이 나에게 그토록 가까이 있는 광경이, 그 순간 나의 신경을 뒤흔들었다. 그 죽은 여인이, 나의 찾기를 도우려, 제 자살의 현장에 나타날지도 모른다는 끔찍한 상상—그녀가 그 들썩이는 모래의 표면을 뚫고 솟아올라, 그 자리를 가리키는 것을 보게 될지도 모른다는 이루 말할 수 없는 공포가—내 마음속에 억지로 밀고 들어와, 그 따스한 햇빛 속에서 나를 오싹 얼어붙게 했다. 나는 인정한다, 그 막대기 끝이 처음으로 그 늪 모래로 들어가던 그 순간, 내가 두 눈을 감았음을.\n\n그 바로 다음 순간, 그 막대기가 채 몇 치도 잠기기 전에, 나는 나 자신의 미신적인 공포의 손아귀에서 벗어나, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 흥분으로 두근거리고 있었다. 눈을 감고 재어 보아, 나의 첫 시도에—그 첫 시도에 나는 바로 맞게 재었던 것이다! 그 막대기가 그 쇠사슬에 부딪쳤다.\n\n왼손으로 그 해초의 뿌리를 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 나는 그 벼랑 끝 너머로 몸을 눕히고, 오른손으로 그 튀어나온 바위 가장자리 아래를 더듬었다. 나의 오른손이 그 쇠사슬을 찾아냈다.\n\n나는 그것을 조금의 어려움도 없이 끌어 올렸다. 그리고 거기, 그 끝에 그 옻칠한 양철상자가 매달려 있었다.\n\n물의 작용이 그 쇠사슬을 어찌나 녹슬게 했던지, 나는 그것을 그 상자에 매어 두는 걸쇠에서 풀어낼 수가 없었다. 그 상자를 두 무릎 사이에 끼우고 나의 온 힘을 다하여, 나는 간신히 그 뚜껑을 벗겨 냈다. 안을 들여다보니 어떤 흰 물체가 그 속을 온통 채우고 있었다. 나는 손을 넣어, 그것이 아마포임을 알았다.\n\n그 아마포를 끌어내면서, 나는 그것과 함께 구겨진 편지 한 통도 끌어냈다. 그 겉면을 보고, 그것이 내 이름을 지니고 있음을 알아채고서, 나는 그 편지를 주머니에 넣고, 그 아마포를 온전히 끄집어냈다. 그것은, 물론, 그토록 오래 갇혀 있던 그 상자의 모양에 맞추어 빚어진, 두툼한 두루마리 꼴로 나왔고, 바닷물로 인한 어떤 상함으로부터도 완벽히 지켜져 있었다.\n\n나는 그 아마포를 그 해변의 마른 모래로 가져가, 거기서 그것을 펼쳐 반반히 폈다. 그것이 하나의 옷가지라는 것은 잘못 볼 여지가 없었다. 그것은 잠옷이었다.\n\n내가 그것을 펼쳤을 때, 그 위로 향한 면은, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 주름과 구김살을 눈에 드러낼 뿐, 그 이상은 아무것도 없었다. 나는 다음으로 그 아래로 향한 면을 살폈다—그러자 대뜸 레이철의 규방 문에서 묻은 그 페인트 얼룩을 알아챈 것이다!\n\n내 두 눈은 그 얼룩에 못 박힌 채였고, 나의 마음은 나를 단숨에 현재에서 과거로 데려갔다. 커프 부장의 바로 그 말들이, 마치 그 사람 자신이 다시 내 곁에서, 그가 그 문의 얼룩에서 이끌어 낸 그 반박할 수 없는 추론을 가리키기라도 하는 듯, 나에게 되살아났다.\n\n\"이 집에 페인트 얼룩이 묻은 옷가지가 있는지 알아내시오. 그 옷이 누구의 것인지 알아내시오. 그 사람이 자정과 새벽 세 시 사이에 그 방에 들어가 그 페인트를 묻힌 것을 어떻게 해명하는지 알아내시오. 만일 그 사람이 당신을 흡족하게 하지 못한다면, 그 다이아몬드를 가져간 손을 찾으려 멀리 볼 것도 없소.\"\n\n그 말들이 하나하나 나의 기억 위를 지나가며, 지겹고 기계적인 되풀이로 몇 번이고 거듭 저를 되뇌었다. 나는, 여러 시간의 무아지경처럼 느껴진 것에서—실은, 두말할 것 없이, 겨우 몇 순간의 멈춤에 지나지 않았던 것에서—나를 부르는 목소리에 깨어났다. 나는 고개를 들어, 베터리지의 참을성이 마침내 바닥나 버렸음을 보았다. 그는 그 모래언덕 사이로 겨우 보일락 말락 하게, 해변으로 돌아오고 있었다.\n\n그 노인의 모습은, 내가 그것을 알아챈 순간, 나를 현재의 일들에 대한 감각으로 불러들였고, 여기까지 내가 좇아 온 그 조사가 아직 마무리되지 않은 채 남아 있음을 나에게 일깨웠다. 나는 그 잠옷의 얼룩을 발견했다. 그 잠옷은 누구의 것이었던가?\n\n나의 첫 충동은 내 주머니 속의 편지—내가 그 상자 속에서 찾은 그 편지를 살펴보는 것이었다.\n\n그것을 꺼내려 손을 드는데, 나는 이보다 발견으로 가는 더 빠른 길이 있음을 떠올렸다. 그 잠옷 자체가 진실을 드러내 줄 터였다, 십중팔구, 그 잠옷에 그 주인의 이름이 새겨져 있을 것이므로.\n\n나는 그것을 모래에서 집어 들고, 그 표시를 찾았다.\n\n나는 그 표시를 찾아, 읽었다—바로 나 자신의 이름을.\n\n거기 그 잠옷이 나의 것임을 나에게 일러 주는 그 낯익은 글자들이 있었다. 나는 그것들에서 눈을 들었다. 거기 해가 있었다. 거기 그 만의 반짝이는 물이 있었다. 거기 늙은 베터리지가, 점점 더 가까이 나에게 다가오고 있었다. 나는 다시 그 글자들을 돌아보았다. 바로 나 자신의 이름. 뚜렷이 나를 마주하고 있는—바로 나 자신의 이름을.\n\n\"만일 시간과 애씀과 돈으로 그것을 할 수 있다면, 나는 월장석을 훔친 그 도둑에게 내 손을 얹고야 말겠소.\"—나는 그 말들을 입에 담고 런던을 떠났었다. 나는 그 늪 모래가 다른 온갖 살아 있는 존재에게서 감추어 온 그 비밀을 꿰뚫어 보았다. 그리고, 그 페인트 얼룩의 반박할 수 없는 증거로써, 나는 바로 나 자신을 그 도둑으로 발견한 것이다.\n\n나 자신의 심경에 대해서는 한마디도 할 말이 없다.\n\n내 느낌으로는, 나에게 가해진 그 충격이 나의 생각하고 느끼는 힘을 완전히 멎게 했던 것 같다. 베터리지가 나에게 합류했을 때 나는 정녕 내가 무엇을 하고 있는지 알 수 없었을 것이다—무슨 일이냐고 그가 물었을 때 내가 웃었고, 그 잠옷을 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 그 수수께끼를 스스로 읽어 보라고 그에게 말했다는 것을, 나는 그의 증언에 기대어 알고 있으니 말이다.\n\n그 해변에서 우리 사이에 오간 말에 대해서는, 나에게 아주 흐릿한 기억조차 없다. 이제 내가 다시 나 자신을 또렷이 볼 수 있는 첫 장소는 그 전나무 숲이다. 베터리지와 나는 함께 그 집으로 걸어 돌아가고 있다. 그리고 베터리지는 나에게, 우리가 그로그 한 잔을 마시고 나면, 내가 그것에 맞설 수 있을 것이고, 그도 그것에 맞설 수 있을 것이라고 말하고 있다.\n\n장면이 그 숲에서, 베터리지의 자그마한 응접실로 옮겨 간다. 레이철의 집에 들지 않겠다던 나의 결심은 잊혔다. 나는 그 방의 서늘함과 그늘짐과 고요함을 고맙게 느낀다. 나는 그로그를 (그 시각에는 나에게 아주 새로운 호사였다) 마신다—나의 착한 옛 벗이 우물에서 길어 온 얼음처럼 찬 물에 타 주는 그것을. 여느 다른 상황에서라면, 그 술은 그저 나를 멍하게 만들었을 것이다. 지금 사정이 사정인지라, 그것은 나의 신경을 팽팽히 죈다. 나는 베터리지가 예언한 대로, \"그것에 맞서기\" 시작한다. 그리고 베터리지도, 제 편에서, \"그것에 맞서기\" 시작한다.\n\n내가 지금 나 자신에 대해 내놓고 있는 이 초상은, 짐작건대, 아무리 좋게 말해도, 아주 이상한 것으로 여겨지리라. 내 생각에 전혀 유례가 없다고 이를 만한 처지에 놓여, 내가 맨 먼저 취하는 조치는 무엇인가? 나는 온갖 인간의 사회로부터 나 자신을 격리하는가? 나는, 그럼에도 부인할 수 없는 사실로 나를 마주하는 그 가증스러운 불가능을 분석하는 데에 나의 마음을 쏟는가? 나는 최고의 권위자들과 상의하고 당장 철저한 조사에 착수하려, 첫 기차로 서둘러 런던으로 돌아가는가? 아니다. 나는 다시는 들어가 나 자신을 욕되게 하지 않겠다고 결심했던 그 집의 비호를 받아들인다. 그리고 나는, 아침 열 시에, 늙은 하인과 함께, 술과 물을 홀짝이며 앉아 있다. 이것이 나의 그 끔찍한 처지에 놓인 사람에게서 기대되었을 법한 처신인가? 나는 다만, 늙은 베터리지의 낯익은 얼굴을 보는 것이 나에게 이루 말할 수 없는 위안이었다는 것과, 늙은 베터리지의 그로그를 마시는 것이, 내가 빠져든 그 온전한 몸과 마음의 탈진 상태에서, 다른 어떤 것도 나를 돕지 못했으리라 믿는 만큼 나를 도왔다는 것으로써 답할 수밖에 없다. 나는 다만 나 자신을 위해 이 변명을 내놓을 수밖에 없다. 그리고 나는, 이 글줄을 읽을지 모를 온갖 남녀가, 요람에서 무덤에 이르기까지 그들 삶의 온갖 위급한 때에 보여 주는, 그 한결같은 위엄의 지킴과, 그 엄격히 논리적인 처신의 일관됨을 그저 감탄할 수밖에 없다.\n\n\"자, 프랭클린 나리, 어쨌든 한 가지만은 확실합니다요,\" 하고 베터리지가, 그 잠옷을 우리 사이의 탁자 위에 툭 던지고, 마치 그것이 제 말을 들을 수 있는 살아 있는 존재이기라도 한 양 그것을 가리키며 말했다. \"우선, 저놈은 거짓말쟁이입니다.\"\n\n이 위안이 되는 견해는 나의 마음에 떠오른 견해가 아니었다.\n\n\"나는 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔다는 온갖 앎에 대해 자네만큼이나 결백하네.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"허나 저기 나에게 불리한 증인이 있네! 그 잠옷의 페인트와, 그 잠옷의 이름은 엄연한 사실이야.\"\n\n베터리지는 내 잔을 들어, 구슬리듯 그것을 내 손에 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"사실이라고요?\" 하고 그가 되뇌었다. \"그로그를 한 모금 더 드십시오, 프랭클린 나리, 그러면 사실 따위를 믿는 그 약한 마음을 이겨 내실 겁니다요! 흉계입니다, 나리!\" 하고 그는 은근히 목소리를 낮추며 말을 이었다. \"그것이 제가 이 수수께끼를 읽어 내는 방식입지요. 어딘가에 흉계가 있습니다—그리고 나리와 저는 그것을 밝혀내야 합니다. 그 양철상자에 손을 넣으셨을 때, 그 안에 다른 것은 아무것도 없었습니까?\"\n\n그 물음은 대뜸 나에게 내 주머니 속의 편지를 떠올리게 했다. 나는 그것을 꺼내어 폈다. 그것은 빽빽이 적힌, 여러 쪽에 걸친 편지였다. 나는 조바심을 내며 그 끝의 서명을 찾았다. \"로재나 스피어먼.\"\n\n그 이름을 읽자, 별안간 떠오른 기억이 내 마음을 밝혔고, 그 새로운 빛에서 별안간 하나의 의심이 일었다.\n\n\"멈추게!\" 하고 나는 외쳤다. \"로재나 스피어먼은 감화원에서 나와 내 이모에게 온 것이지? 로재나 스피어먼은 한때 도둑이었지?\"\n\n\"그것은 부인할 수 없습지요, 프랭클린 나리. 그런데 그것이 이제 와서 어쨌다는 겁니까, 부디?\"\n\n\"그것이 이제 와서 어쨌냐고? 결국 그녀가 그 다이아몬드를 훔치지 않았다고 우리가 어찌 아는가? 그녀가 일부러 내 잠옷에 그 페인트를 묻히지 않았다고 우리가 어찌 아는가?\"\n\n베터리지는 내 팔에 손을 얹어, 내가 더 말하기 전에 나를 멈추게 했다.\n\n\"나리는 이 일에서 벗으실 겁니다, 프랭클린 나리, 조금의 의심할 여지도 없이요. 허나 저는 나리가 그런 식으로 벗으시지는 않기를 바랍니다요. 그 편지가 뭐라 하는지 보십시오, 나리. 그 아이의 넋에 대한 도리로라도, 그것이 뭐라 하는지 보십시오.\"\n\n나는 그가 말하는 그 간곡함을 느꼈다—그것을 나에 대한 다정한 꾸짖음으로 느꼈다. \"자네가 그녀의 편지에 대해 자네 나름의 판단을 세우게.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"내가 그것을 소리 내어 읽겠네.\"\n\n나는 시작했다—그리고 이 글줄들을 읽었다.\n\n\"선생님—저는 선생님께 털어놓을 것이 있습니다. 큰 비참을 뜻하는 고백이, 때로는 아주 몇 마디 말로 이루어질 수 있습니다. 이 고백은 세 마디로 이루어질 수 있습니다. 저는 당신을 사랑합니다.\"\n\n그 편지가 내 손에서 떨어졌다. 나는 베터리지를 보았다. \"맙소사,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"이것이 무슨 뜻인가?\"\n\n그는 그 물음에 답하기를 꺼리는 듯했다.\n\n\"나리는 오늘 아침 절름발이 루시와 단둘이 있으셨지요, 나리,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그 아이가 로재나 스피어먼에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않던가요?\"\n\n\"그녀는 로재나 스피어먼의 이름조차 입에 올리지 않았네.\"\n\n\"부디 그 편지로 돌아가 주십시오, 프랭클린 나리. 솔직히 말씀드리는데, 나리께서 이미 겪으셔야 했던 일 뒤에, 저는 도무지 나리를 괴롭힐 마음이 나지 않습니다요. 그 아이가 스스로 말하게 두십시오, 나리. 그리고 그로그를 마저 드십시오. 나리 자신을 위해서라도, 그로그를 마저 드십시오.\"\n\n나는 그 편지 읽기를 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n\"선생님께서 이것을 읽으실 때 제가 살아 있는 여자라면, 이런 말씀을 드리는 것이 저에게 몹시 부끄러운 일이겠지요. 선생님께서 제 편지를 찾으실 때, 저는, 선생님, 죽어 사라지고 없을 것입니다. 그것이 저를 대담하게 만듭니다. 저에 대해 말해 줄 무덤조차 저에게는 남지 않을 것입니다. 이 말들이 다 적히면 저를 감추려 기다리는 그 늪 모래와 더불어—저는 진실을 털어놓아도 되겠지요.\n\n\"게다가, 선생님께서는 저의 은닉처에서, 페인트 얼룩이 묻은 채로, 선생님의 잠옷을 찾으실 것입니다. 그러면 선생님께서는, 그것이 어쩌다 저에게 감추어지게 되었는지, 그리고 어째서 제가 살아생전 그것에 대해 선생님께 아무 말도 하지 않았는지 알고 싶으실 것입니다. 저에게는 내놓을 이유가 오직 하나 있습니다. 저는 이 이상한 일들을, 선생님을 사랑했기에 했습니다.\n\n\"선생님께서 저의 마님 댁에 오시기 전의, 저 자신에 대해서나, 저의 삶에 대해서 많은 것으로 선생님을 성가시게 하지는 않겠습니다. 레이디 버린더께서는 저를 감화원에서 데려오셨습니다. 저는 옥에서 감화원으로 갔습니다. 저는 옥에 갇혔는데, 제가 도둑이었기 때문입니다. 저는 도둑이었는데, 제가 아주 어린 계집아이일 때 제 어머니가 거리로 나섰기 때문입니다. 제 어머니가 거리로 나선 것은, 제 아버지였던 그 신사가 그녀를 버렸기 때문입니다. 이처럼 흔한 이야기를 길게 늘어놓을 것은 없습니다. 그것은 신문에 아주 자주 실립니다.\n\n\"레이디 버린더께서는 저에게 아주 다정하셨고, 베터리지 씨도 저에게 아주 다정하셨습니다. 그 두 분과, 감화원의 그 사감님이, 제 온 생애에서 제가 만난 유일한 좋은 분들입니다. 저는 제 자리에서 그럭저럭 지낼 수 있었을지도 모릅니다—행복하지는 않게라도—선생님께서 오셔서 머물지만 않으셨다면, 저는 그럭저럭 지낼 수 있었을지도 모릅니다. 저는 선생님을 탓하지 않습니다, 선생님. 그것은 제 잘못입니다—죄다 제 잘못입니다.\n\n\"그날 아침, 선생님께서 베터리지 씨를 찾으시며 그 모래언덕 사이에서 저희 앞으로 나오셨을 때를 기억하십니까? 선생님은 마치 동화 속의 왕자님 같았습니다. 선생님은 마치 꿈속의 연인 같았습니다. 선생님은 제가 여태 본 가장 사랑스러운 인간이었습니다. 제가 여태 살아 본 적 없는 그 행복한 삶처럼 느껴지는 무언가가, 제가 선생님께 눈길을 준 그 순간 제 안에서 솟구쳤습니다. 하실 수만 있다면 이것을 비웃지 말아 주십시오. 오, 이것이 저에게 얼마나 진지한 것인지 선생님께 느끼게 해 드릴 수만 있다면!\n\n\"저는 그 집으로 돌아가, 제 반짇고리에 선생님의 이름과 저의 이름을 적고, 그 아래에 진실한 연인의 매듭을 그렸습니다. 그러자 어떤 악마가—아니, 어떤 착한 천사라고 해야겠지요—저에게 속삭였습니다, '가서 거울을 보아라.' 그 거울이 저에게 일러 주었습니다—무엇을 일러 주었는지는 아무래도 좋습니다. 저는 그 경고를 새겨듣기에는 너무 어리석었습니다. 저는, 마치 제가 선생님과 같은 신분의 귀부인이자, 선생님의 눈이 여태 머문 가장 아름다운 존재이기라도 한 양, 선생님을 점점 더 사랑하게 되었습니다. 저는 애썼습니다—오, 아아, 제가 얼마나 애썼는지—선생님이 저를 보시게 하려고요. 선생님이 저를 조금도 알아봐 주지 않으시는 그 비참과 굴욕으로 제가 밤마다 어찌 울곤 했는지 아셨더라면, 선생님은 어쩌면 저를 가엾이 여겨, 제가 그것으로 살아갈 눈길을 이따금 저에게 주셨을지도 모릅니다.\n\n\"제가 레이철 아가씨를 얼마나 미워했는지 아셨더라면, 그것은 아마 그리 다정한 눈길은 아니었겠지요. 저는 선생님이 그녀를 사랑하고 계심을, 선생님 자신이 아시기도 전에 알아챘다고 믿습니다. 그녀는 선생님께 단춧구멍에 꽂으실 장미를 주곤 했습니다. 아, 프랭클린 선생님, 선생님은 선생님도 그녀도 여기신 것보다 더 자주 저의 장미를 꽂으셨답니다! 그 무렵 제가 가진 유일한 위안은, 그녀의 것 대신 저의 장미를 몰래 선생님의 물잔에 꽂아 두고—그런 다음 그녀의 장미를 던져 버리는 것이었습니다.\n\n\"만일 그녀가 정말로 선생님이 여기신 만큼 어여뻤다면, 저는 그것을 더 잘 견뎠을지도 모릅니다. 아니요. 저는 그녀에게 더욱더 앙심을 품었으리라 믿습니다. 레이철 아가씨에게 하녀의 옷을 입히고, 그녀의 장신구를 벗겨 놓았다고 쳐 보세요? 제가 어째서 이런 식으로 쓰고 있는지 모르겠습니다. 그녀의 몸매가 볼품없었다는 것은 부인할 수 없습니다. 그녀는 너무 말랐습니다. 허나 사내들이 무엇을 좋아하는지 누가 알겠습니까? 그리고 젊은 아가씨는 하녀 같으면 제 자리를 잃을 만한 행실을 부려도 됩니다. 그것은 저와 상관없는 일이지요. 제가 이런 식으로 쓴다면, 선생님이 제 편지를 읽어 주시기를 바랄 수 없습니다. 허나, 내내 그것이 그녀의 옷과, 그녀의 제 자신에 대한 자신감이 만든 것임을 알면서도, 레이철 아가씨가 어여쁘다고 불리는 것을 듣는 것은 정말이지 사람의 속을 뒤집어 놓습니다.\n\n\"저에게 참을성을 잃지 말아 주십시오, 선생님. 저는 선생님께서 틀림없이 관심 두실 그때—그 다이아몬드가 없어진 그때로 되도록 빨리 나아가겠습니다.\n\n\"허나 먼저 선생님께 말씀드려야겠다고 마음먹은 것이 한 가지 있습니다.\n\n\"제가 도둑이던 동안에는, 제 삶이 견디기에 그리 힘든 삶이 아니었습니다. 감화원에서 저에게 제 자신의 타락을 느끼도록, 그리고 더 나은 것을 좇도록 가르치고 나서야, 비로소 나날이 길고 고달파졌습니다. 이제 미래에 대한 생각들이 저에게 밀려들었습니다. 저는 정직한 사람들이—가장 다정한 정직한 사람들조차—그저 그 자체로 저에게 얼마나 무서운 꾸짖음인지를 느꼈습니다. 어디를 가든, 무엇을 하든, 어떤 사람들을 보든, 가슴이 미어지는 외로움이 저에게서 떠나지 않았습니다. 새 자리에서 제 동료 하인들과 그럭저럭 어울려 지내려 애쓰는 것이 저의 도리임을, 저도 압니다. 어쩐지 저는 그들과 벗이 될 수 없었습니다. 그들은 (또는 그렇게 보인다고 제가 여겼습니다) 제가 무엇이었는지를 의심하는 듯 보였습니다. 저는, 개과천선한 여자가 되려는 애씀에 눈뜬 것을 후회하지 않습니다, 결코 후회하지 않습니다—허나, 정말이지, 정말이지 그것은 고달픈 삶이었습니다. 선생님은 처음에는 한 줄기 햇살처럼 그 삶을 가로질러 오셨습니다—그러고는 선생님마저 저를 저버리셨습니다. 저는 선생님을 사랑할 만큼 미쳐 있었습니다. 그런데 저는 선생님의 눈길조차 끌 수 없었습니다. 거기에는 큰 비참이 있었습니다—정말로 거기에는 큰 비참이 있었습니다.\n\n\"이제 저는 제가 말씀드리고 싶었던 것에 이릅니다. 그 쓰라린 나날에, 저는 외출할 차례가 되면, 저의 가장 좋아하는 곳—흔들리는 모래 위쪽의 그 해변으로 두세 번 갔습니다. 그리고 저는 스스로에게 말했습니다, '나는 이것이 여기서 끝나리라 생각해. 내가 더는 견딜 수 없게 되면, 나는 이것이 여기서 끝나리라 생각해.' 아시겠지만, 선생님, 그곳은 선생님이 오시기 전부터 저에게 일종의 마법을 걸어 두었습니다. 저는 언제나 그 늪 모래에서 저에게 무슨 일이 일어나리라는 생각을 품고 있었습니다. 허나 저는, 지금 제가 쓰고 있는 그때가 오기까지는, 그것을 제 자신을 없애는 수단이 되리라는 생각으로 바라본 적은 없었습니다. 그때 저는 정녕, 여기가 저의 온갖 괴로움을 한두 순간에 끝내 줄—그리고 그 뒤로 영영 저를 감추어 줄 곳이라고 생각했습니다.\n\n\"이것이, 제가 선생님을 처음 본 그 아침부터, 그 다이아몬드가 없어졌다는 소동이 그 집에서 일어난 그 아침까지 헤아려, 저 자신에 대해 드릴 말씀의 전부입니다.\n\n\"저는 누가 맨 먼저 의심받을까 죄 궁금해하는 하녀들 사이의 그 어리석은 수다에 몹시 짜증이 났습니다. 그리고 저는 (그때는 더 잘 알지 못한 채) 선생님이 그 보석을 찾느라 애쓰고 경찰을 부르시는 것에 몹시 화가 나서, 그날 늦게 프리징홀에서 그 경관이 그 집에 올 때까지, 되도록 저 혼자 떨어져 있었습니다.\n\n\"기억하시겠지만, 시그레이브 씨는 먼저 하녀들의 침실에 파수를 세우는 것으로 시작했습니다. 그러자 하녀들은 그가 자기들에게 준 그 모욕이 무슨 뜻인지 따지려, 죄 성이 나서 그를 따라 위층으로 올라갔습니다. 저도 나머지와 함께 갔는데, 만일 제가 나머지와 다른 무언가를 했다면, 시그레이브 씨는 대뜸 저를 의심했을 그런 부류의 사람이었기 때문입니다. 우리는 그가 레이철 아가씨의 방에 있는 것을 보았습니다. 그는 여자들을 잔뜩 그곳에 두지 않겠다고 우리에게 말했습니다. 그리고 그는 그 칠해진 문의 얼룩을 가리키며, 우리 페티코트 자락 가운데 하나가 그 사달을 냈다고 말하고는, 우리를 죄 다시 아래층으로 보냈습니다.\n\n\"레이철 아가씨의 방을 나온 뒤, 저는 혹시 저의 옷에 그 페인트 얼룩이 묻지는 않았는지 보려고, 층계참 하나에서 혼자 잠시 멈춰 섰습니다. 페넬로피 베터리지가 (제가 사이좋게 지낸 유일한 하녀였습니다) 지나가다, 제가 무엇을 하는지 알아챘습니다.\n\n\"'애태울 것 없어, 로재나,' 하고 그녀가 말했습니다. '레이철 아가씨 방의 그 페인트는 몇 시간 전에 말랐는걸. 시그레이브 씨가 우리 침실에 파수를 세우지만 않았어도, 내가 그에게 그만큼 말해 줬을 텐데. 네 생각은 모르겠지만—나는 평생 이렇게 모욕당한 적이 없어!'\n\n\"페넬로피는 성미가 급한 아이였습니다. 저는 그녀를 달래, 그 문의 페인트가 몇 시간 전에 말랐다고 한 그녀의 말로 그녀를 되돌렸습니다.\n\n\"'그걸 어떻게 알아?' 하고 저는 물었습니다.\n\n\"'나는 어제 아침 내내 레이철 아가씨랑 프랭클린 나리랑 함께 있었잖아,' 하고 페넬로피가 말했습니다. '그분들이 그 문을 마저 칠하시는 동안 물감을 개면서 말이야. 레이철 아가씨가 그 문이 그날 저녁, 생일 손님들이 볼 수 있게 제때 마를지 여쭙는 걸 나는 들었어. 그러자 프랭클린 나리가 고개를 저으시며, 열두 시간 안에는 마르지 않을 거라고 하셨지. 점심때가 한참 지나서였어—그분들이 마치신 게 세 시였으니까. 네 셈으로는 어떻게 나오니, 로재나? 내 셈으로는 그 문이 오늘 아침 세 시쯤엔 말랐어.'\n\n\"'어제 저녁 그걸 보러 위층에 올라간 부인들이 있었어?' 하고 저는 물었습니다. '레이철 아가씨가 그 문에 가까이 가지 말라고 그분들께 이르시는 걸 들은 것 같은데.'\n\n\"'그 얼룩을 낸 부인은 아무도 없어,' 하고 페넬로피가 대답했습니다. '나는 어젯밤 열두 시에 레이철 아가씨를 잠자리에 뉘어 드리고 나왔어. 그리고 그 문을 눈여겨봤는데, 그때는 그것에 아무 이상도 없었어.'\n\n\"'이걸 시그레이브 씨에게 말해야 하지 않겠니, 페넬로피?'\n\n\"'나한테 무엇을 주더라도 시그레이브 씨를 돕는 말은 한마디도 안 할 거야!'\n\n\"그녀는 제 일을 하러 갔고, 저는 제 일을 하러 갔습니다.\n\n\"저의 일은, 선생님, 선생님의 잠자리를 봐 드리고, 선생님의 방을 정돈하는 것이었습니다. 그것은 온종일 중에 제가 가진 가장 행복한 시간이었습니다. 저는 밤새 선생님의 머리가 놓였던 그 베개에 입을 맞추곤 했습니다. 그 뒤로 누가 그 일을 했든, 선생님은 제가 선생님을 위해 갠 것만큼 곱게 옷이 개어진 적이 없으셨을 것입니다. 선생님의 화장 상자 안의 온갖 자잘한 소품들 가운데, 티끌 하나라도 앉은 것은 없었습니다. 선생님은 저를 알아보지 않으신 것과 마찬가지로, 그것도 알아보지 않으셨습니다. 용서하십시오, 제가 저를 잊고 있습니다. 서둘러, 다시 나아가겠습니다.\n\n\"자, 그날 아침 저는 선생님의 방에서 제 일을 하러 들어갔습니다. 거기, 선생님이 벗어 던지신 그대로, 선생님의 잠옷이 침대에 걸쳐져 있었습니다. 저는 그것을 개려고 집어 들었습니다—그리고 저는 레이철 아가씨의 문에서 묻은 그 페인트 얼룩을 보았습니다!\n\n\"저는 그 발견에 어찌나 소스라쳤던지, 그 잠옷을 손에 든 채 뛰쳐나가, 뒤쪽 계단으로 가서, 아무도 끼어들어 저를 방해할 수 없는 곳에서 그것을 보려고, 제 방에 저를 가두었습니다.\n\n\"다시 숨을 돌리자마자, 저는 페넬로피와의 그 이야기를 떠올리고, 스스로에게 말했습니다, '여기 그분이 어젯밤 열두 시와 오늘 아침 세 시 사이에 레이철 아가씨의 응접실에 계셨다는 증거가 있어!'\n\n\"제가 그 발견을 했을 때 제 마음을 스친 첫 의심이 무엇이었는지는, 대놓고 말씀드리지 않겠습니다. 선생님은 그저 화만 나실 테니까요—그리고, 화가 나시면, 선생님은 제 편지를 찢어 버리시고 더는 읽지 않으실지도 모릅니다.\n\n\"부디, 오직 이것만 말씀드리는 것으로 넉넉하다고 해 주십시오. 제 능력껏 그것을 곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에, 저는 그 일이 있음 직하지 않다고, 제가 말씀드릴 한 가지 이유에서, 결론지었습니다. 만일 선생님이 그 밤 시각에, 레이철 아가씨가 아시는 채로 그녀의 응접실에 계셨다면 (그리고 만일 선생님이 그 젖은 문을 조심할 것을 잊을 만큼 어리석으셨다면) 그녀가 선생님께 일러 드렸을 것입니다—그녀가 결코, 지금 제가 보고 있는 그 증인 같은, 자신에게 불리한 증인을 선생님이 가지고 나가시게 두지 않았을 것입니다! 동시에, 저는 제 의심이 틀렸음을 증명했다고 제 마음속으로 온전히 확신한 것은 아니었음을 털어놓습니다. 제가 레이철 아가씨를 미워했다고 털어놓은 것을 잊지 않으셨겠지요. 하실 수 있다면, 이 모든 것에 그 미움이 얼마쯤 섞여 있었다고 생각해 주십시오. 그것은, 제가 그 잠옷을 간직하고, 기다리며, 지켜보며, 그것으로 제가 무슨 쓸모를 얻을 수 있을지 보기로 마음먹는 것으로 끝났습니다. 그때, 부디 기억해 주십시오, 선생님이 그 다이아몬드를 훔치셨으리라는 생각은 그 유령조차 제 머릿속에 들어오지 않았습니다.\"\n\n거기서, 나는 그 편지 읽기를 두 번째로 멈추었다.\n\n나는 그 가엾은 여인의 고백 가운데 나 자신과 관련된 부분들을, 꾸밈없는 놀라움과, 정직하게 덧붙이자면, 진심 어린 괴로움을 안고 읽었다. 나는, 그녀의 편지를 한 줄도 보기 전에 내가 무심히 그녀의 넋에 던진 그 험담을, 참으로 뉘우쳤다. 허나 위에 인용된 그 대목까지 나아갔을 때, 나는 인정한다, 읽어 나갈수록 로재나 스피어먼에 대한 내 마음이 점점 더 쓰라려지는 것을 느꼈음을. \"나머지는 자네가 스스로 읽게.\" 하고 나는, 그 편지를 탁자 너머로 베터리지에게 건네며 말했다. \"그 안에 내가 꼭 보아야 할 것이 있거든, 자네가 읽어 가며 나에게 일러 주게.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 프랭클린 나리,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"그것은 나리로서는 당연한 일입지요. 그리고, 우리 모두를 하느님이 도우시기를!\" 하고 그는 한결 낮은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그것은 그 아이로서도 못지않게 당연한 일입니다.\"\n\n나는 내가 지니고 있는 원본에서 그 편지의 이어지는 부분을 옮겨 적어 나간다—\n\n\"그 잠옷을 간직하고, 저의 사랑, 또는 저의 복수가 (어느 쪽인지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다) 앞으로 그것을 무슨 쓸모로 돌릴 수 있을지 보기로 마음먹었으니, 다음으로 알아내야 할 것은 들킬 위험 없이 그것을 간직할 방도였습니다.\n\n\"길은 오직 하나였습니다—토요일이 와서 세탁하는 여자와 그녀의 물목 명세를 그 집에 데려오기 전에, 그것과 꼭 같은 잠옷을 또 하나 만드는 것이었습니다.\n\n\"저는 그것을 이튿날(금요일)까지 미루기가 두려웠습니다. 그 사이에 무슨 사고가 일어날까 봐 걱정스러웠기 때문입니다. 저는, 제가 패를 제대로 놀리기만 하면 제 시간을 저 자신에게 쓸 수 있으리라 셈할 수 있는, 바로 그날(목요일)에 그 새 잠옷을 만들기로 마음먹었습니다. 맨 먼저 할 일은 (선생님의 잠옷을 제 서랍에 넣고 잠근 뒤) 선생님의 침실로 돌아가는 것이었는데—그것을 정돈하기 위해서라기보다는 (제가 청했다면 페넬로피가 저를 위해 그것을 해 주었을 것입니다) 선생님이 선생님의 잠옷에서 그 페인트 얼룩을 침대나 방 안의 어떤 가구에 묻혀 놓지는 않으셨는지 알아내기 위해서였습니다.\n\n\"저는 온갖 것을 샅샅이 살폈고, 마침내, 선생님의 실내복 안쪽에서 페인트 몇 줄기를 찾아냈습니다—그해 여름철에 선생님이 늘 입으시던 그 아마포 실내복이 아니라, 선생님이 함께 가지고 계셨던 플란넬 실내복이었습니다. 짐작건대 선생님은 잠옷만 입고 이리저리 걸으신 뒤 으슬으슬 추위를 느끼시어, 찾을 수 있는 가장 따뜻한 것을 걸치셨겠지요. 어쨌든, 거기, 그 실내복 안쪽에, 그 얼룩이 겨우 보일락 말락 있었습니다. 저는 그 플란넬의 보풀을 긁어내어 이것들을 쉽게 없앴습니다. 이렇게 하고 나니, 선생님에게 불리하게 남은 유일한 증거는 제 서랍에 넣고 잠근 그 증거뿐이었습니다.\n\n\"제가 막 선생님의 방을 다 정돈했을 때, 저는 나머지 하인들과 함께 시그레이브 씨에게 신문받으라는 부름을 받았습니다. 다음으로 우리 모두의 상자에 대한 검사가 이어졌습니다. 그러고는, 제가 선생님의 잠옷에서 그 페인트를 찾아낸 뒤로, 그날의—저에게—가장 놀라운 사건이 뒤따랐습니다. 이 사건은 시그레이브 슈퍼린텐던트의 페넬로피 베터리지에 대한 두 번째 신문에서 나왔습니다.\n\n\"페넬로피는 시그레이브 씨가 자기를 다룬 그 태도에 아주 넋이 나갈 만큼 성이 나서 우리에게 돌아왔습니다. 그는, 잘못 알아들을 여지가 조금도 없이, 그녀가 그 도둑이 아닐까 의심한다고 넌지시 비쳤던 것입니다. 우리는 이 말을 듣고 죄 똑같이 놀라, 죄 물었습니다, 어째서냐고.\n\n\"'그 다이아몬드가 레이철 아가씨의 응접실에 있었으니까,' 하고 페넬로피가 대답했습니다. '그리고 내가 밤에 그 응접실에 있었던 마지막 사람이었으니까!'\n\n\"그 말이 그녀의 입술을 채 떠나기도 전에, 저는 또 다른 사람이 페넬로피보다 더 늦게 그 응접실에 있었음을 떠올렸습니다. 그 사람은 바로 선생님이었습니다. 제 머리가 핑핑 돌았고, 제 생각은 끔찍한 혼란에 빠졌습니다. 그 온갖 것의 한복판에서, 제 마음속의 무언가가 저에게, 선생님의 잠옷에 묻은 그 얼룩이 그때까지 제가 그것에 부여해 온 뜻과는 전혀 다른 뜻을 지녔을지도 모른다고 속삭였습니다. '만일 그 방에 있었던 마지막 사람이 의심받을 사람이라면,' 하고 저는 속으로 생각했습니다, '그 도둑은 페넬로피가 아니라, 프랭클린 블레이크 나리야!'\n\n\"여느 다른 신사의 경우라면, 저는 그를 절도로 의심한 것을, 그 의심이 제 마음을 스치기가 무섭게 부끄러워했으리라 믿습니다.\n\n\"허나 선생님이 저의 수준으로 몸을 낮추셨다는, 그리고 제가, 선생님의 잠옷을 손에 넣음으로써, 선생님이 발각되어 평생 욕되게 되는 것으로부터 선생님을 지켜 드릴 수단도 손에 넣었다는—그 헐벗은 생각만으로도, 선생님의 호의를 얻을 그런 기회가 제 앞에 열리는 듯싶어, 저는, 이를테면, 눈을 감은 채 의심에서 믿음으로 건너갔습니다. 저는 그 자리에서, 선생님이 우리 모두를 속이려는 눈가림으로, 경찰을 부르는 데에 누구보다 부산히 나서는 모습을 보이셨다고, 그리고 레이철 아가씨의 보석을 가져간 그 손은 도저히 선생님의 손 말고 다른 손일 수 없다고 마음을 굳혔습니다.\n\n\"저의 이 새로운 발견의 흥분이, 제 생각에, 한동안 제 머리를 돌게 했음이 틀림없습니다. 저는 선생님을 보고 싶은—그 다이아몬드에 관해 한두 마디로 선생님을 떠보고, 선생님이 저를 그런 식으로 보시고, 저에게 말을 거시게 만들고 싶은 어찌나 애타는 열망을 느꼈던지—저는 제 머리를 매만지고, 되도록 곱게 저를 꾸미고, 선생님이 글을 쓰고 계신 줄 아는 그 서재로 대담하게 선생님께 갔습니다.\n\n\"선생님은 반지 하나를 위층에 두고 오셨는데, 그것이 제가 바랐을 만한 좋은 침입의 핑계가 되어 주었습니다. 허나, 오, 선생님! 만일 선생님이 사랑을 해 보신 적이 있다면, 제가 그 방으로 걸어 들어가 선생님 앞에 서게 되었을 때, 어찌하여 저의 온 용기가 식어 버렸는지 이해하실 것입니다. 그러고는, 선생님이 저를 어찌나 차갑게 올려다보시고, 어찌나 무심한 태도로 반지를 찾아 준 것에 대해 저에게 고마워하셨던지, 제 무릎이 저를 지탱하지 못하고 떨렸고, 저는 마치 선생님 발치의 바닥에 쓰러질 것만 같았습니다. 저에게 고마워하시고서, 기억하신다면, 선생님은 다시 선생님의 글로 눈을 돌리셨습니다. 저는 이런 식으로 대접받은 것이 어찌나 굴욕스러웠던지, 말할 만큼의 기운을 가까스로 냈습니다. 저는 말했습니다, '이 다이아몬드 일은 참 이상하네요, 나리.' 그러자 선생님은 다시 올려다보시며, '그래, 그렇지!' 하고 말씀하셨습니다. 선생님은 점잖게 말씀하셨습니다 (그것은 부인할 수 없습니다). 허나 그럼에도 선생님은 거리를—우리 사이에 잔인한 거리를 두셨습니다. 선생님이 말씀하시는 동안, 선생님이 그 잃어버린 다이아몬드를 몸에 감추고 계신다고 믿었기에, 선생님의 그 차가움이 저를 어찌나 부아 나게 했던지, 저는 그 북받침에 겨워 대담해져서 선생님께 넌지시 비쳤습니다. 저는 말했습니다, '그들은 결코 그 다이아몬드를 못 찾을 거예요, 나리, 그렇지요? 아무렴요! 그것을 가져간 사람도 못 찾을 거예요—그건 제가 장담해요.' 저는 고개를 끄덕이고, 마치 '난 알아요!' 하고 말하는 것처럼 선생님께 미소 지었습니다. 이번에는, 선생님이 눈에 얼마쯤 관심 같은 것을 담고 저를 올려다보셨습니다. 그리고 저는 선생님 편과 제 편의 몇 마디만 더 있으면 진실이 드러날지도 모른다고 느꼈습니다. 바로 그 순간, 베터리지 씨가 그 문으로 옴으로써 그 모든 것을 망쳐 놓았습니다. 저는 그의 발소리를 알았고, 또한 그 시각에 제가 서재에 있는 것이—선생님과 함께 거기 있는 것은 말할 것도 없이—그의 규율에 어긋난다는 것도 알았습니다. 저는 그가 들어와 저더러 가라고 이르기 전에, 제 발로 나올 겨우 그만큼의 짬밖에 없었습니다. 저는 화가 나고 실망했습니다. 허나 그래도 저는 아주 희망이 없지는 않았습니다. 아시겠지만, 우리 사이의 얼음이 깨진 것이었습니다—그리고 저는 다음번에는, 베터리지 씨가 자리에 없도록 조심하리라 생각했습니다.\n\n\"제가 하인 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 우리의 저녁을 알리는 종이 울리고 있었습니다. 벌써 오후였습니다! 그런데 그 새 잠옷을 만들 재료는 아직 마련해야 했습니다! 그것을 마련할 기회는 오직 하나였습니다. 저는 저녁 때 앓는 시늉을 했습니다. 그리하여 그때부터 다과 시간까지의 사이 전부를 저 자신의 몫으로 확보했습니다.\n\n\"온 집안이 제가 제 방에 누워 있는 줄로 믿는 동안 제가 무엇을 했는지, 그리고 다과 시간에 다시 앓는 시늉을 하고 잠자리로 올려 보내진 뒤 제가 밤을 어떻게 보냈는지는, 선생님께 말씀드릴 것도 없습니다. 커프 부장은, 더는 알아내지 못했더라도, 그만큼은 알아냈습니다. 그리고 저는 어떻게인지 짐작할 수 있습니다. 저는 (베일을 내리고 있었건만) 프리징홀의 포목점에서 들켰습니다. 제가 옥양목을 사고 있던 그 계산대 앞에 거울이 하나 있었습니다. 그리고—그 거울 속에서—저는 점원 하나가 제 어깨를 가리키며 다른 점원에게 소곤대는 것을 보았습니다. 밤에 다시, 제가 방에 갇혀 몰래 일하고 있을 때, 저는 저를 의심하는 하녀들이 제 문밖에서 숨 쉬는 소리를 들었습니다.\n\n\"그때는 그것이 대수롭지 않았고, 지금도 그것은 대수롭지 않습니다. 금요일 아침, 커프 부장이 그 집에 들기 몇 시간 전에, 거기 그 새 잠옷이—제가 손에 넣은 잠옷 대신 선생님의 벌수를 채우려고—만들어지고, 짜지고, 말려지고, 다려지고, 표시되고, 세탁하는 여자가 나머지 온갖 것을 갠 대로 개어져, 선생님의 서랍에 안전히 있었습니다. (그 집의 아마포가 검사되더라도) 그 잠옷의 새것 티가 저를 배신할 두려움은 없었습니다. 선생님의 온갖 속옷은, 선생님이 우리 집에 오셨을 때 죄 새것으로 바꾸어진 터였습니다—짐작건대 외국 땅에서 귀국하시면서요.\n\n\"다음 일은 커프 부장의 도착이었습니다. 그리고 다음의 큰 놀라움은 그 문의 얼룩에 대해 그가 어찌 생각하는지의 발표였습니다.\n\n\"저는 (털어놓았듯이) 선생님이 죄지으셨다고 믿었는데, 다른 어떤 이유에서라기보다 제가 선생님이 죄지으셨기를 바랐기 때문이었습니다. 그런데 이제, 그 부장이 전혀 다른 길을 돌아, (그 잠옷에 관하여) 저와 같은 결론에 이른 것이었습니다! 그리고 저는 선생님에게 불리한 유일한 증거인 그 옷을 가지고 있었습니다! 그리고 살아 있는 어떤 존재도—선생님 자신을 포함해—그것을 알지 못했습니다! 이것들을 떠올렸을 때 제가 어떤 심정이었는지 선생님께 말씀드리기가 두렵습니다—선생님은 그 뒤로 영영 저의 넋을 미워하실 테니까요.\"\n\n그 대목에서, 베터리지는 그 편지에서 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"여기까지는 빛 한 줄기 없습니다요, 프랭클린 나리,\" 하고 그 노인은, 그의 두툼한 대모갑 안경을 벗고, 로재나 스피어먼의 고백을 제게서 조금 밀어 놓으며 말했다. \"제가 읽는 동안, 나리는 나리 마음속으로 어떤 결론에 이르셨습니까, 나리?\"\n\n\"먼저 그 편지를 끝내게, 베터리지. 그 끝에 우리를 밝혀 줄 무언가가 있을지도 모르네. 그다음에 자네에게 한두 마디 할 말이 있겠네.\"\n\n\"알겠습니다, 나리. 잠깐 눈을 좀 쉬고, 그런 다음 다시 나아가겠습니다. 그동안, 프랭클린 나리—나리를 재촉하고 싶지는 않습니다만—한마디로, 나리께서 이 끔찍한 난장판을 빠져나갈 길을 아직 보시는지 저에게 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"나는 런던으로 돌아갈 길이 보이네.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"브러프 씨와 상의하러 말일세. 만일 그가 나를 돕지 못한다면——\"\n\n\"예, 나리?\"\n\n\"그리고 만일 그 부장이 도킹의 은거를 떠나려 하지 않는다면——\"\n\n\"떠나지 않을 겁니다, 프랭클린 나리!\"\n\n\"그렇다면, 베터리지—지금 내가 보는 한으로는—나는 나의 방책이 다한 것일세. 브러프 씨와 그 부장 다음으로는, 나는 나에게 조금이라도 쓸모가 있을 만한 살아 있는 존재를 알지 못하네.\"\n\n그 말이 내 입술을 스칠 때, 밖에서 누군가가 그 방의 문을 두드렸다.\n\n베터리지는 그 방해에 짜증이 나기도 하고 놀라기도 한 낯빛이었다.\n\n\"들어오시오,\" 하고 그가 짜증스레 소리쳤다. \"누구시든 간에!\"\n\n문이 열리고, 우리에게로, 조용히, 내가 여태 본 가장 눈에 띄는 생김새의 사내가 들어왔다. 그의 몸매와 그의 몸놀림으로 판단하면, 그는 아직 젊었다. 그의 얼굴로 판단하고, 그를 베터리지와 견주면, 그는 둘 중 더 나이 든 쪽으로 보였다. 그의 낯빛은 집시처럼 거무스름했다. 그의 살 없는 두 뺨은 깊은 우묵함으로 꺼져 있었고, 그 위로 뼈가 차양처럼 튀어나와 있었다. 그의 코는 동방의 옛 종족들 사이에서는 그토록 자주 보이나, 서방의 더 새로운 인종들 사이에서는 그토록 드물게 보이는, 그 고운 모양새와 조형을 지니고 있었다. 그의 이마는 눈썹에서부터 높고 곧게 솟아 있었다. 그의 반점과 주름은 헤아릴 수 없이 많았다. 이 이상한 얼굴에서, 더욱더 이상한, 더없이 부드러운 갈색 눈이—꿈꾸는 듯하고 애처로운, 그 안와에 깊이 꺼진 눈이—당신을 내다보며, (적어도 나의 경우에는) 제멋대로 당신의 주의를 사로잡았다. 여기에다, 자연의 어떤 변덕으로, 더없이 놀랍도록 얼룩덜룩하고 제멋대로인 방식으로 그 빛깔을 잃어버린, 빽빽하고 촘촘히 곱슬거리는 숱 많은 머리칼을 더하라. 그의 머리 꼭대기 위로는 그것이 여전히, 그 본래의 빛깔인 짙은 검정이었다. 그의 머리 옆으로 빙 둘러서는—그 놀라운 대비의 기세를 누그러뜨릴 회색의 옅은 층 하나 없이—그것이 완전히 하얗게 세어 있었다. 그 두 빛깔 사이의 경계는 아무런 규칙도 지키지 않았다. 어느 곳에서는 흰 머리칼이 검은 머리칼 속으로 치달아 올랐고, 다른 곳에서는 검은 머리칼이 흰 머리칼 속으로 흘러내렸다. 나는, 부끄럽지만 도무지 억누를 수 없다고 느낀 호기심을 안고, 그 사내를 바라보았다. 그의 부드러운 갈색 눈이 다정하게 나를 마주 보았다. 그리고 그는, 그를 빤히 쳐다보는 나의 무심결의 무례함을, 내가 받을 자격이 없다고 자각한 사과로써 맞이했다.\n\n\"용서하십시오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"베터리지 씨에게 손님이 계신 줄 몰랐습니다.\" 그는 주머니에서 종이쪽지 하나를 꺼내어, 베터리지에게 건넸다. \"다음 주 목록입니다,\" 하고 그가 말했다. 그의 눈이 다시 나에게 잠시 머물렀다—그러고는 그는 그가 들어온 것과 똑같이 조용히 방을 나갔다.\n\n\"저 사람은 누구인가?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"캔디 씨의 조수입지요,\" 하고 베터리지가 말했다. \"그러고 보니, 프랭클린 나리, 나리께서 들으시면 안타까우실 텐데, 그 자그마한 의사 선생은 생일 만찬에서 돌아가는 길에 걸린 그 병에서 끝내 회복하지 못했습니다. 건강은 제법 괜찮습니다요. 허나 그는 그 열병에 기억을 잃었고, 그 뒤로 그것의 잔해 이상은 끝내 되찾지 못했습니다. 일이 죄 그의 조수에게 떨어집지요. 지금은 그리 많지도 않습니다, 가난한 사람들 사이에서 말고는요. 그들은, 아시다시피, 어쩔 도리가 없지요. 그들은 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼과 집시 같은 낯빛의 사내로 참을 수밖에 없습니다—그러지 않으면 진료를 아예 못 받을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"자네는 그를 좋아하지 않는 듯하군, 베터리지?\"\n\n\"아무도 그를 좋아하지 않습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"어째서 그가 그리 인기가 없는가?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 프랭클린 나리, 우선, 그의 생김새가 그에게 불리합니다. 그리고 캔디 씨가 그를 아주 미심쩍은 신원으로 받아들였다는 소문이 있습지요. 아무도 그가 누구인지 모릅니다—그리고 그는 이곳에 벗 하나 없습니다. 그러고서야, 어찌 누가 그를 좋아하기를 바라겠습니까?\"\n\n\"물론, 아주 불가능하지! 그가 자네에게 그 종잇조각을 줄 때, 자네에게 무슨 볼일이 있었는지 물어봐도 되겠는가?\"\n\n\"그저 이 근방의, 포도주가 좀 필요한 병자들의 주간 목록을 저에게 가져다주는 것뿐입니다, 나리. 마님께서는 늘 병약한 가난한 이들에게 좋고 실한 포트와 셰리를 정기적으로 나눠 주셨습니다. 그리고 레이철 아가씨는 그 관례가 이어지기를 바라십니다. 세월이 변했습니다! 세월이 변했어요! 저는 캔디 씨가 몸소 그 목록을 저의 여주인께 가져오던 때를 기억합니다. 이제는 캔디 씨의 조수가 그 목록을 저에게 가져옵니다. 허락하신다면, 그 편지를 이어 나가겠습니다, 나리,\" 하고 베터리지는 로재나 스피어먼의 고백을 제게로 도로 끌어당기며 말했다. \"명랑한 읽을거리가 아닌 것은 인정합니다요. 허나, 원 참! 그것이 저를 지난 일 생각으로 심사가 뒤틀리지 않게 지켜 줍니다.\" 그는 안경을 쓰고, 침울하게 고개를 저었다. \"우리가 태어날 때, 우리를 처음으로 삶의 여정에 내보내는 우리 어머니들을 향한 우리의 처신에는, 프랭클린 나리, 밑바탕에 깔린 지혜가 있습지요. 우리는 죄, 다소간 이 세상에 나오기를 꺼립니다. 그리고 우리는 죄 옳습니다.\"\n\n캔디 씨의 조수는 나에게 너무나 강한 인상을 남겨, 당장 내 생각에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 나는 베터리지 철학의 그 마지막 반박할 수 없는 발언을 지나쳐 흘려버리고, 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼의 사내라는 화제로 돌아갔다.\n\n\"그의 이름은 무엇인가?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"더없이 흉한 이름입지요,\" 하고 베터리지가 퉁명스레 대답했다. \"에즈라 제닝스입니다.\"\n\n# CHAPTER V\n\n캔디 씨의 조수의 이름을 나에게 말해 주고서, 베터리지는 우리가 하찮은 화제에 시간을 넉넉히 허비했다고 여기는 듯했다. 그는 로재나 스피어먼의 편지 읽기를 다시 이어 갔다.\n\n내 편에서, 나는 그가 다 읽기를 기다리며 창가에 앉아 있었다. 조금씩, 에즈라 제닝스가 나에게 남긴 그 인상은—나 같은 처지에서, 어떤 인간 존재가 대체 나에게 인상을 남겼다는 것 자체가 도무지 설명할 수 없는 일로 보였다!—내 마음에서 바래져 갔다. 나의 생각은 도로 그 이전의 물길로 흘러들었다. 다시 한 번, 나는 나 자신의 그 믿기 어려운 처지를 단호히 똑바로 마주하도록 나를 다잡았다. 다시 한 번, 나는 마침내 미래를 위해 짜낼 만큼의 침착함을 그러모아 세운 그 방책을 내 마음속으로 되짚었다.\n\n그날 런던으로 돌아가는 것, 그 온 사건을 브러프 씨 앞에 펼쳐 놓는 것, 그리고, 마지막이자 가장 중요하게, (어떤 수단으로든 어떤 희생을 치르든) 레이철과의 직접 면담을 얻어 내는 것—이것이, 그때 내가 짤 수 있는 한으로는, 나의 행동 방책이었다. 기차가 떠나기까지는 아직 한 시간 넘게 남아 있었다. 그리고 내가 그 다이아몬드가 없어졌던 집을 떠나기 전에 알아 두면 쓸모가 있을 만한 무언가를, 베터리지가 로재나 스피어먼의 편지의 아직 안 읽은 부분에서 찾아낼지도 모를 실낱같은 요행이 있었다. 그 요행을 나는 이제 기다리고 있었다.\n\n그 편지는 이런 말로 끝났다.\n\n\"화내실 것 없습니다, 프랭클린 선생님, 제가 선생님의 온 앞날을 제 손에 쥐고 있음을 아는 데에서 약간의 승리감을 얼마쯤 느꼈다 하더라도요. 근심과 두려움이 이내 저에게 돌아왔습니다. 커프 부장이 그 다이아몬드의 분실을 보는 그 시각으로 보아, 그는 틀림없이 결국 우리의 아마포와 우리의 옷을 검사하게 될 터였습니다. 제 방에는—그 집에는—그로부터 안전하리라고 제가 흡족히 여길 만한 곳이 없었습니다. 그 부장조차 찾아내지 못하도록 그 잠옷을 어떻게 감출까? 그리고 그것을, 그 소중한 시간을 한 순간도 잃지 않고서 어떻게 할까?—이것들은 답하기 쉬운 물음이 아니었습니다. 저의 갈팡질팡은, 선생님을 웃게 만들지도 모를 방도를 취하는 것으로 끝났습니다. 저는 옷을 벗고, 그 잠옷을 제 몸에 걸쳤습니다. 선생님이 그것을 입으셨었지요—그리고 저는 선생님 뒤에 그것을 입는 데에서 또 한 번 자그마한 기쁨의 순간을 누렸습니다.\n\n\"하인 방에 다음으로 우리에게 다다른 소식은, 제가 그 잠옷을 챙긴 것이 한순간도 이르지 않았음을 보여 주었습니다. 커프 부장이 세탁 장부를 보고 싶어 했던 것입니다.\n\n\"저는 그것을 찾아, 마님의 응접실에 있는 그에게 가져갔습니다. 그 부장과 저는 지난날 한 번 넘게 서로 마주친 적이 있었습니다. 저는 그가 저를 다시 알아보리라 확신했습니다—그리고 저는 그가, 값진 보석이 없어진 집에 제가 하녀로 일하고 있는 것을 알았을 때 무엇을 할지 확신할 수 없었습니다. 이 마음 졸임 속에서, 저는 우리 사이의 만남을 끝내 버리고, 그 최악의 것을 대뜸 아는 것이 저에게 차라리 안도가 되리라 느꼈습니다.\n\n\"제가 그에게 세탁 장부를 건넸을 때, 그는 마치 제가 낯선 사람이기라도 한 양 저를 보았습니다. 그리고 그는 그것을 가져다준 것에 대해 저에게 각별히 아주 정중하게 고마워했습니다. 저는 그 둘 다 나쁜 징조라고 생각했습니다. 그가 제 등 뒤에서 저에 대해 무슨 말을 할지 알 수 없었습니다. 제가 얼마나 곧 의심을 받아 붙들려, 몸수색을 당하게 될지 알 수 없었습니다. 그때는 선생님이 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 철도역까지 배웅하고 돌아오실 무렵이었습니다. 그래서 저는 선생님께 말을 걸 또 한 번의 기회를—제가 아는 한, 제가 가질 수 있는 마지막 기회를 노리고, 선생님이 즐겨 거니시는 관목숲으로 갔습니다.\n\n\"선생님은 끝내 나타나지 않으셨습니다. 그리고, 더 나쁘게도, 베터리지 씨와 커프 부장이 제가 숨어 있던 그곳을 지나갔습니다—그리고 그 부장이 저를 보았습니다.\n\n\"그 뒤로, 저에게 더 많은 재앙이 닥치기 전에, 저는 제 마땅한 자리와 제 마땅한 일로 돌아가는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없었습니다. 제가 막 그 오솔길을 가로질러 발을 내디디려는 참에, 선생님이 철도역에서 돌아오셨습니다. 선생님은 곧장 그 관목숲으로 향하시다가, 저를 보셨습니다—틀림없이, 선생님, 선생님은 저를 보셨습니다—그리고 선생님은 마치 제가 흑사병에라도 걸린 양 몸을 돌리시어, 그 집으로 들어가셨습니다.*\n\n* 주(註); 프랭클린 블레이크가 붙임.—글쓴이는 온전히 잘못 알고 있다, 가엾은 사람이. 나는 결코 그녀를 알아채지 못했다. 나의 뜻은 정녕 그 관목숲을 한 바퀴 거닐려는 것이었다. 허나 그와 동시에, 철도역에서 돌아온 뒤 이모가 나를 보고 싶어 할지도 모른다는 것이 떠올라, 나는 마음을 바꾸어, 그 집으로 들어갔다.\n\n\"저는 하인용 출입구로 돌아, 다시 서둘러 안으로 들어갔습니다. 그때 세탁방에는 아무도 없었습니다. 그래서 저는 거기 홀로 앉았습니다. 흔들리는 모래가 제 머릿속에 넣어 준 그 생각들을 저는 이미 선생님께 말씀드렸습니다. 그 생각들이 이제 저에게 돌아왔습니다. 저는 속으로, 일이 이런 식으로 계속된다면—프랭클린 블레이크 나리의 저에 대한 무심함을 견디는 것과, 그 늪 모래로 뛰어들어 그런 식으로 그것을 영영 끝내는 것 가운데 어느 쪽이 하기 더 어려울까 궁금해했습니다.\n\n\"이때의 제 처신을 해명하라고 저에게 청해 봐야 헛일입니다. 저는 애써 봅니다—그리고 저 자신도 그것을 이해할 수 없습니다.\n\n\"선생님이 그 잔인한 태도로 저를 피하셨을 때, 어째서 저는 선생님을 멈춰 세우지 않았을까요? 어째서 저는 '프랭클린 나리, 저는 나리께 드릴 말씀이 있어요. 그것은 나리 자신에 관한 것이니, 나리는 그것을 들으셔야 하고, 또 들으실 거예요' 하고 외치지 않았을까요? 선생님은 제 손아귀에 있었습니다—흔히 하는 말로, 저는 선생님을 쥐고 있었습니다. 그리고 그보다 나은 것으로, 저는 (제가 선생님을 저를 믿게 만들 수만 있다면) 앞으로 선생님께 쓸모가 될 수단을 지니고 있었습니다. 물론, 저는 선생님이—신사이신 선생님이—그저 훔치는 재미로 그 다이아몬드를 훔치셨다고는 결코 여기지 않았습니다. 아니요. 페넬로피는 레이철 아가씨가, 저는 베터리지 씨가, 선생님의 낭비와 선생님의 빚에 대해 이야기하는 것을 들었습니다. 선생님이 그 다이아몬드를 팔거나 저당 잡혀, 그리하여 선생님이 필요로 하시는 돈을 마련하려고 그것을 가져가셨다는 것은 저에게 아주 뚜렷했습니다. 그렇지요! 저는 선생님께, 그 보석에 상당히 큰 금액을 내줄, 그리고 그것에 대해 거북한 물음도 하지 않을, 런던의 한 사내를 일러 드릴 수 있었을 것입니다.\n\n\"어째서 저는 선생님께 말하지 않았을까요! 어째서 저는 선생님께 말하지 않았을까요!\n\n\"그 잠옷을 간직하는 위험과 어려움이, 다른 위험과 어려움이 거기 더해지는 것을 감당할 수 없을 만큼 제가 겨우 감당할 수 있는 것이었을까 궁금합니다. 어떤 여자들에게는 이것이 사실이었을지도 모릅니다—허나 어떻게 그것이 저에게 사실일 수 있겠습니까? 제가 도둑이던 시절에, 저는 쉰 배는 더 큰 위험을 무릅썼고, 이 어려움 따위는 한낱 애들 장난인 그런 어려움에서 빠져나오는 길을 찾았습니다. 저는, 이를테면, 사기와 속임수의 도제 노릇을 했습니다—그중 어떤 것은 어찌나 큰 규모로, 어찌나 교묘하게 부려졌던지, 이름이 나서 신문에까지 실렸습니다. 그 잠옷을 간직하는 것 같은 그런 자잘한 일이, 제가 선생님께 말했어야 할 그때에, 제 마음을 짓누르고 제 가슴을 제 안에서 내려앉게 할 만한 것이었을까요? 그런 물음을 하다니 얼마나 어이없는 일입니까! 그럴 리 없었습니다.\n\n\"제 자신의 어리석음을 이런 식으로 곱씹은들 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까? 분명한 진실은, 정말이지, 충분히 분명하지 않은가요? 선생님의 등 뒤에서, 저는 온 마음과 온 넋으로 선생님을 사랑했습니다. 선생님의 얼굴 앞에서는—그것은 부인할 수 없습니다—저는 선생님이 무서웠습니다. 선생님을 저에게 화나시게 할까 무서웠습니다. 제가 감히 그것을 알아냈다고 선생님께 말씀드린다면 선생님이 저에게 무슨 말씀을 하실지 (선생님이 그 다이아몬드를 가져가셨는데도) 무서웠습니다. 저는 서재에서 선생님께 말했을 때 감히 그럴 수 있는 만큼 그것에 가까이 갔었습니다. 그때는 선생님이 저에게 등을 돌리지 않으셨습니다. 그때는 선생님이 마치 제가 흑사병에 걸린 양 저에게서 소스라쳐 물러나지 않으셨습니다. 저는 선생님께 화가 나도록 저를 부추기고, 그런 식으로 제 용기를 일으켜 세우려 애썼습니다. 아니요! 저는 그것의 비참과 굴욕밖에는 아무것도 느낄 수 없었습니다. '너는 못난 계집이야. 너는 어깨가 굽었어. 너는 한낱 하녀일 뿐이야—무슨 낯으로 감히 나에게 말을 걸려 드는 게냐?' 선생님은 그런 말을 한마디도 입에 담지 않으셨습니다, 프랭클린 나리. 허나 선생님은 그럼에도 그 모든 것을 저에게 말씀하셨습니다! 이런 미친 짓이 해명될 수 있는 것일까요? 아니요. 그것을 고백하고, 그대로 두는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없습니다.\n\n\"제 펜의 이 헤맴에 대해, 다시 한 번 선생님의 용서를 구합니다. 그것이 다시 일어날 두려움은 없습니다. 저는 이제 끝에 다다랐습니다.\n\n\"그 빈방에 들어와 저를 방해한 첫 사람은 페넬로피였습니다. 그녀는 오래전에 제 비밀을 알아챘고, 저를 제정신으로 돌려놓으려 애를 다했습니다—그것도 다정하게 그리했습니다.\n\n\"'아!' 하고 그녀가 말했습니다. '나는 네가 왜 여기 혼자 앉아, 애태우는지 알아. 로재나, 너에게 가장 좋은 일은 프랭클린 나리의 이곳 방문이 끝나는 걸 거야. 내 믿기로는 그분이 이제 곧 그 집을 떠나실 거야.'\n\n\"선생님에 대한 저의 온갖 생각 중에서 저는 선생님이 떠나신다는 것을 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었습니다. 저는 페넬로피에게 말을 할 수 없었습니다. 저는 그저 그녀를 바라볼 수밖에 없었습니다.\n\n\"'나는 방금 레이철 아가씨 곁을 떠나왔어,' 하고 페넬로피가 말을 이었습니다. '그런데 그분 성미를 견디느라 참 애를 먹었지. 그분은 경찰이 든 이 집이 견딜 수 없다고 하셔. 그리고 그분은 오늘 저녁 마님께 말씀드리고, 내일 에이블화이트 이모님께 가시기로 마음을 굳히셨어. 그분이 그리하시면, 프랭클린 나리가 다음으로 떠날 이유를 찾으실 거야, 그건 틀림없어!'\n\n\"저는 그 말에 다시 혀를 놀릴 수 있게 되었습니다. '프랭클린 나리가 그분과 함께 가신다는 말이야?' 하고 저는 물었습니다.\n\n\"'그분이 허락만 하시면야 더없이 기꺼이 그러시겠지. 허나 그분은 허락 안 하실 거야. 그분도 그분의 성미를 겪으셨어. 그분도 그분의 미움을 사고 계셔—그것도 그분을 도우려 할 수 있는 걸 다 하시고서 말이야, 가엾은 분! 아니! 아니야! 그분들이 내일 전에 화해하지 않으시면, 너는 레이철 아가씨가 이 길로 가시고, 프랭클린 나리가 저 길로 가시는 걸 보게 될 거야. 그분이 어디로 몸을 두실지 나는 말할 수 없어. 허나 레이철 아가씨가 우리를 떠나신 뒤에, 그분은 결코 여기 머무르지 않으실 거야, 로재나.'\n\n\"저는 선생님이 떠나신다는 전망에 제가 느낀 절망을 가까스로 억눌렀습니다. 진실을 털어놓자면, 만일 레이철 아가씨와 선생님 사이에 정말로 심각한 불화가 있다면, 저는 저 자신을 위한 희망의 실낱같은 빛을 보았습니다. '너는,' 하고 저는 물었습니다. '그분들 사이의 다툼이 무엇인지 아니?'\n\n\"'그건 죄 레이철 아가씨 쪽에서 비롯된 거야,' 하고 페넬로피가 말했습니다. '그리고, 내가 달리 아는 게 없는 한, 그건 죄 레이철 아가씨 성미일 뿐, 다른 아무것도 아니야. 나는 너를 괴롭게 하고 싶지 않아, 로재나. 허나 프랭클린 나리가 언젠가 그분과 다투실 것 같다는 생각일랑 품지 마. 그러기엔 그분이 그분을 너무나도 좋아하시니까!'\n\n\"그녀가 그 잔인한 말을 막 마쳤을 때, 베터리지 씨에게서 우리에게 부름이 왔습니다. 온 안살림 하인들은 현관홀에 모이라는 것이었습니다. 그런 다음 우리는 하나씩 들어가, 베터리지 씨의 방에서 커프 부장에게 신문받기로 되어 있었습니다.\n\n\"마님의 몸종과 웃길 하녀가 먼저 신문받은 뒤, 제가 들어갈 차례가 되었습니다. 커프 부장의 물음은—그가 그것을 아주 교묘하게 감쌌건만—곧 저에게, 그 두 여자가 (그 집에서 제가 가진 가장 지독한 원수들이었습니다) 화요일 오후에, 그리고 다시 목요일 밤에 제 문밖에서 저의 일을 알아챘음을 보여 주었습니다. 그들은 그 부장에게, 그의 눈이 진실의 얼마쯤에 뜨일 만큼 충분히 일러바쳤습니다. 그는 제가 몰래 새 잠옷을 만들었다고 옳게 믿었으나, 페인트 얼룩진 잠옷이 저의 것이라고 그릇되게 믿었습니다. 저는 그가 한 말에서, 이해하기에 저를 어리둥절하게 하는 또 한 가지를 흡족히 느꼈습니다. 그는, 물론, 제가 그 다이아몬드의 사라짐에 관련되어 있다고 의심했습니다. 허나 동시에 그는—일부러, 라고 저는 생각했습니다—저를 그 보석의 분실에 주로 책임 있는 사람으로는 여기지 않음을 저에게 내비쳤습니다. 그는 제가 다른 누군가의 지시 아래 움직여 왔다고 여기는 듯했습니다. 그 사람이 누구일지, 저는 그때도 짐작할 수 없었고, 지금도 짐작할 수 없습니다.\n\n\"이 갈팡질팡 속에서, 한 가지는 뚜렷했습니다—커프 부장이 온전한 진실을 아는 데에서 아득히 멀리 있다는 것이었습니다. 그 잠옷이 안전한 한 선생님은 안전했습니다—그리고 그 이상 한 순간도 안전하지 않았습니다.\n\n\"이제 저를 짓누르던 그 괴로움과 공포를 선생님이 이해하시게 만드는 것은 정녕 가망 없는 일입니다. 저로서는 더 이상 선생님의 잠옷을 입는 위험을 무릅쓰는 것이 불가능했습니다. 저는 당장이라도, 의심으로 고발되어 그에 따라 몸수색을 당하려, 프리징홀의 경찰서로 끌려가게 될지도 몰랐습니다. 커프 부장이 아직 저를 자유롭게 놓아둔 동안, 저는 골라야 했습니다—그것도 당장에—그 잠옷을 없애 버리는 것과, 그 집에서 안전한 거리만큼 떨어진 어느 안전한 곳에 그것을 감추는 것 가운데서 말입니다.\n\n\"만일 제가 선생님을 조금만 덜 사랑했더라면, 저는 그것을 없애 버렸으리라 생각합니다. 허나 오! 제가 선생님을 발각에서 구했음을 증명하는, 제가 가진 유일한 그것을 어찌 제가 없앨 수 있겠습니까? 만일 우리가 정말로 함께 해명에 이르게 되고, 만일 선생님이 저에게 무슨 나쁜 동기가 있다고 의심하시어 그 모든 것을 부인하신다면, 제가 내놓을 그 잠옷을 가지고 있지 않고서야, 어찌 제가 선생님이 저를 믿게 만들 수 있겠습니까? 선생님의 돈 걱정이 저지르도록 선생님을 꾄 그 절도의, 저 같은 가엾은 계집이 선생님의 비밀을 함께 나누는 자, 선생님의 공범이 되게 하는 것을 선생님이 망설이실지도 모른다고, 제가 그때 믿었고 지금도 믿듯이 믿는 것이, 선생님께 잘못하는 일일까요? 선생님의 저에 대한 그 차가운 처신을 생각해 보십시오, 선생님, 그러면 선생님은, 제가 지니게 된 행운인 그 유일한, 선생님의 믿음과 선생님의 고마움에 대한 권리를 없애기를 제가 꺼린 것에 좀처럼 놀라지 않으실 것입니다.\n\n\"저는 그것을 감추기로 마음먹었습니다. 그리고 제가 정한 곳은 제가 가장 잘 아는 곳—흔들리는 모래였습니다.\n\n\"신문이 끝나자마자, 저는 제 머리에 떠오른 첫 핑계를 대고, 바깥바람을 좀 쐬러 나가도 좋다는 허락을 얻었습니다. 저는 곧장 코브스 홀로, 욜랜드 씨의 오두막으로 갔습니다. 그의 아내와 딸은 제가 가진 가장 좋은 벗이었습니다. 제가 그들에게 선생님의 비밀을 맡겼다고는 여기지 마십시오—저는 아무에게도 맡기지 않았습니다. 제가 원한 것은 오직 이 편지를 선생님께 쓰는 것과, 그 잠옷을 제 몸에서 벗을 안전한 기회를 갖는 것뿐이었습니다. 의심받고 있는 처지라, 저는 그 집에서는 그 두 가지 중 어느 것도 조금도 안전하게 할 수 없었습니다.\n\n\"그리고 이제 저는 루시 욜랜드의 침실에서 홀로 이 편지를 쓰며, 저의 긴 편지를 거의 다 마쳤습니다. 그것이 끝나면, 저는 그 잠옷을 둘둘 말아 제 망토 아래 감추고 아래층으로 내려가겠습니다. 저는 욜랜드 부인의 부엌에 있는 낡은 잡동사니 사이의 그 은닉처에, 그것을 안전하고 마른 채로 간직하는 데 필요한 수단을 찾을 것입니다. 그런 다음 저는 흔들리는 모래로 가서—제 발자국이 저를 배신하도록 두지 않을 테니 두려워 마십시오!—그 잠옷을, 제가 먼저 그 비밀을 알려 주지 않고서는 살아 있는 어떤 존재도 찾을 수 없는, 그 모래 속에 감출 것입니다.\n\n\"그리고, 그것이 끝나면, 그다음은?\n\n\"그다음, 프랭클린 나리, 저는 아직 하지 못한 그 말들을 선생님께 하려는 또 한 번의 시도를 할 두 가지 이유를 갖게 될 것입니다. 만일 선생님이, 페넬로피가 믿는 대로, 그 집을 떠나신다면, 그리고 만일 제가 그 전에 선생님께 말하지 못한다면, 저는 제 기회를 영영 잃을 것입니다. 그것이 한 가지 이유입니다. 그리고, 또, 만일 저의 말함이 선생님을 화나시게 만든다면—다른 어떤 것도 할 수 없는 방식으로 저를 위해 제 사정을 변호해 줄 그 잠옷을 제가 채비해 두었다는, 그 위안이 되는 앎이 있습니다. 그것이 저의 다른 이유입니다. 만일 이 둘이 함께, 여태 저를 얼어붙게 해 온 그 차가움(선생님의 저에 대한 처신의 차가움을 말합니다)에 맞서 제 마음을 굳게 만들지 못한다면, 거기서 저의 애씀은 끝날 것입니다—그리고 저의 삶도 끝날 것입니다.\n\n\"그렇습니다. 만일 제가 다음 기회를 놓친다면—만일 선생님이 여느 때처럼 잔인하시고, 만일 제가 이미 느낀 대로 그것을 다시 느낀다면—남들에게는 베푸는 그 행복을 저에게는 인색하게 굴어 온 이 세상이여, 안녕히. 오직 선생님의 작은 다정함만이 다시 저에게 즐거운 것으로 만들어 줄 수 있는 그 삶이여, 안녕히. 만일 그것이 이런 식으로 끝나거든, 선생님, 선생님 자신을 탓하지 마십시오. 허나 애써—부디 애써—저를 위해 얼마쯤 용서하는 슬픔을 느껴 주십시오! 저는, 제가 선생님께 직접 말할 수 없게 되었을 때, 선생님이 제가 선생님을 위해 한 일을 알아내시도록 마음 쓰겠습니다. 그때 선생님은 저에 대해 뭔가 다정한 말씀을 해 주시겠습니까—선생님이 레이철 아가씨께 말씀하실 때의 그 다감한 투로요? 만일 선생님이 그리하신다면, 그리고 만일 유령 같은 것이 있다면, 저는 저의 유령이 그것을 듣고, 그 기쁨에 떨리라 믿습니다.\n\n\"이제 그만 멈춰야 할 때입니다. 제가 저를 울리고 있습니다. 이 쓸데없는 눈물이 나와 저를 멀게 하도록 둔다면, 어떻게 제가 그 은닉처로 가는 길을 보겠습니까?\n\n\"게다가, 어째서 제가 그 음울한 쪽을 보아야 합니까? 할 수 있는 동안, 결국 그것이 잘 끝나리라고 믿지 못할 것이 무엇입니까? 저는 오늘 밤 기분 좋으신 선생님을 뵐지도 모릅니다—또는, 그렇지 않다면, 저는 내일 아침에 더 잘 해낼지도 모릅니다. 저는 애태운다고 저의 못난 얼굴을 나아지게 하지는 못할 겁니다—그렇지요? 제가 이 고달프고 긴 종이쪽들을 죄 헛되이 채웠을지 누가 알겠습니까? 그것들은, 안전을 위하여 (다른 무슨 이유에서인지는 이제 아무래도 좋습니다) 그 잠옷과 함께 그 은닉처로 들어갈 것입니다. 제 편지를 쓰는 것은 고되고, 고된 일이었습니다. 오! 우리가 서로 이해하는 것으로 끝나기만 한다면, 제가 이것을 찢어 버리는 것을 얼마나 즐기겠습니까!\n\n\"삼가, 선생님, 선생님의 진실한 연인이자 미천한 하녀로 남습니다,\n\n로재나 스피어먼.\"\n\n그 편지 읽기는 베터리지에 의해 말없이 마쳐졌다. 그것을 조심스레 봉투에 도로 넣고서, 그는 고개를 숙이고, 눈을 바닥에 둔 채, 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"베터리지,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그 편지 끝에 나를 이끌어 줄 무슨 실마리가 있는가?\"\n\n그는 무거운 한숨과 함께, 천천히 눈을 들었다.\n\n\"나리를 이끌어 줄 것은 아무것도 없습니다, 프랭클린 나리,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"제 말을 들으신다면 나리는 나리의 지금 이 근심들이 끝날 때까지 그 편지를 봉투 안에 넣어 두시는 게 좋겠습니다. 그것은 나리가 읽으실 때마다 나리를 몹시 괴롭게 할 것입니다. 지금은 그것을 읽지 마십시오.\"\n\n나는 그 편지를 내 수첩에 넣어 두었다.\n\n베터리지 이야기의 열여섯째와 열일곱째 장을 되돌아보면, 이미 나의 꿋꿋함이 잔인하게 시험받은 그때에 내가 이렇듯 나 자신을 아낀 데에 정녕 이유가 있었음을 알 수 있으리라. 두 번에 걸쳐, 그 불행한 여인은 나에게 말을 걸려는 마지막 시도를 했었다. 그리고 두 번에 걸쳐, 그녀가 나에게 건넨 그 다가섬을 밀어낸 것이 (하느님은 그것이 얼마나 무심결이었는지 아신다!) 나의 불운이었다. 금요일 밤에, 베터리지가 참되이 그리는 대로, 그녀는 내가 당구대에 홀로 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀의 태도와 말은 나에게, 그리고 그런 상황에서라면 어떤 사내에게든—그녀가 그 다이아몬드의 사라짐에 대한 죄지은 앎을 고백하려는 참임을 넌지시 일러 주었다. 그녀 자신을 위하여, 나는 일부러 다가오는 것에 아무 각별한 관심도 보이지 않았다. 그녀 자신을 위하여, 나는 일부러 그녀를 보는 대신 당구공들을 보았다—그리고 그 결과는 어떠했던가? 나는 그녀를, 가슴 깊이 상처 입은 채, 나에게서 떠나보냈던 것이다! 토요일에 다시—페넬로피가 그녀에게 말해 준 뒤로, 나의 떠남이 임박했음을 그녀가 내다보았음이 틀림없는 그날에—똑같은 숙명이 여전히 우리를 좇았다. 그녀는 다시 한 번 그 관목숲 길에서 나를 만나려 했고, 그녀는 내가 베터리지와 커프 부장과 함께 있는 그곳에서 나를 보았다. 그녀가 듣는 데에서, 그 부장은, 제 나름의 은밀한 목적을 품고, 로재나 스피어먼에 대한 나의 관심에 호소했다. 다시 그 가엾은 존재 자신을 위하여, 나는 그 경관을 딱 잘라 부인함으로써 맞았고—그녀도 나를 듣도록—큰 소리로 선언했다, 내가 \"로재나 스피어먼에게 아무런 관심도 없다\"고. 그녀가 나의 사적인 귀를 얻으려 시도하는 것을 막으려는 뜻으로만 던진 그 말에, 그녀는 몸을 돌려 그곳을 떠났다—그때 내가 믿기로는, 제 위험을 경계한 채로. 지금 내가 아는 대로는, 파멸로 스스로를 몰아넣은 채로. 그 지점에서부터, 나는 나를 그 늪 모래에서의 그 아연할 발견으로 이끈 사건들의 잇따름을 이미 좇아 왔다. 그 회고는 이제 마무리되었다. 나는 로재나 스피어먼의 그 비참한 이야기를—이만한 세월이 흐른 지금도 괴로움의 아릿함 없이는 돌이킬 수 없는 그 이야기를—여기 일부러 말하지 않은 채 남겨 둔 온갖 것을 스스로 넌지시 일러 주도록 둘 수 있으리라. 나는 흔들리는 모래에서의 그 자살을, 나의 현재 처지와 미래 전망에 대한 그 이상하고도 무서운 영향과 더불어 지나쳐, 이 이야기의 살아 있는 사람들에 관련되는 관심사로, 그리고 어둠에서 빛으로 가는 그 더디고 고된 여정을 위해 이미 나의 길을 닦고 있던 사건들로 넘어갈 수 있으리라.\n\n# CHAPTER VI\n\n나는, 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 게이브리얼 베터리지를 동무 삼아, 그 철도역까지 걸었다. 나는 그 편지를 주머니에 지녔고, 그 잠옷을 자그마한 가방에 안전히 챙겼다—둘 다, 그날 밤 내가 잠들기 전에, 브러프 씨의 조사에 부쳐질 것이었다.\n\n우리는 말없이 그 집을 떠났다. 내가 그를 겪은 이래 처음으로, 나는 늙은 베터리지가 나에게 할 말 한마디 없이 내 곁에 있는 것을 보았다. 내 편에서는 할 말이 있었으므로, 나는 우리가 그 관리인 문을 벗어나자마자 대화의 문을 열었다.\n\n\"내가 런던으로 가기 전에,\" 하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"자네에게 물을 것이 두 가지 있네. 그것들은 나 자신에 관한 것인데, 아마 자네를 자못 놀라게 할 걸세.\"\n\n\"그것들이 그 가엾은 것의 편지를 제 머리에서 몰아내 준다면, 프랭클린 나리, 그것들이 저를 어찌하든 좋습니다. 부디 되도록 빨리 저를 놀라게 하기 시작해 주십시오, 나리.\"\n\n\"나의 첫 물음은, 베터리지, 이것일세. 레이철의 생일 밤에 내가 취해 있었는가?\"\n\n\"나리가 취하셨다니요!\" 하고 그 노인이 외쳤다. \"원 참, 나리는 저녁에만 술을 드시고 그 뒤로는 술 한 방울 입에 대지 않으신다는 것이야말로 나리의 성품의 큰 흠입지요, 프랭클린 나리!\"\n\n\"허나 그 생일은 특별한 날이었네. 다른 어느 밤도 아닌 그 밤에, 내가 나의 여느 버릇을 저버렸을지도 모르지.\"\n\n베터리지는 잠시 곰곰 생각했다.\n\n\"나리는 정녕 나리의 버릇에서 벗어나셨습니다, 나리,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 제가 어찌 된 일인지 말씀드리지요. 나리가 몹시 편찮아 보이셨습니다—그래서 우리는 나리를 조금 기운 나게 해 드리려고 브랜디 물을 좀 드시라고 나리를 구슬렸습니다.\"\n\n\"나는 브랜디 물에 익숙하지 않아. 아주 있을 법한 일이지——\"\n\n\"잠깐만요, 프랭클린 나리. 저도 나리가 익숙하지 않으신 걸 알았습니다. 저는 나리께 우리의 오십 년 묵은 코냑을 포도주잔으로 반 잔 따라 드렸습니다. 그리고 (더 부끄러운 일이지요!) 저는 그 고귀한 술을 거의 큰 잔 하나 가득한 찬물에 빠뜨려 놓았습니다. 어린아이도 그것으로는 취할 수 없었을 겁니다—다 큰 어른은 말할 것도 없이요!\"\n\n나는 이런 종류의 일에서는 그의 기억에 기대어도 좋음을 알았다. 내가 취해 있었을 수는 명백히 없었다. 나는 두 번째 물음으로 넘어갔다.\n\n\"내가 외국으로 보내지기 전에, 베터리지, 자네는 내가 소년이었을 때 나를 많이 보았지? 자, 솔직히 말해 주게, 밤에 내가 잠자리에 든 뒤에 나에게서 무슨 이상한 것을 기억하는가? 자네는 언젠가 내가 잠결에 걷는 것을 본 적이 있는가?\"\n\n베터리지는 걸음을 멈추고, 잠시 나를 바라보고, 고개를 끄덕이고, 다시 걸어갔다.\n\n\"이제 나리의 속셈을 알겠습니다, 프랭클린 나리!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나리는 나리 자신도 모르게 어찌하여 나리의 잠옷에 그 페인트가 묻었는지를 해명하려 하고 계십니다. 그건 안 됩니다, 나리. 나리는 진실에 다다르는 데에서 아직 아득히 멀리 계십니다. 잠결에 걷는다고요? 나리는 평생 그런 짓을 하신 적이 없습니다!\"\n\n여기서도 다시, 나는 베터리지가 옳음이 틀림없다고 느꼈다. 집에서든 외국에서든 나의 삶은 여태 혼자만의 부류였던 적이 없었다. 만일 내가 몽유병자였다면, 나를 발견했음이 틀림없는, 그리고 나 자신의 안전을 위하여 그 버릇을 나에게 알려 주고 그것을 막을 방비를 취했을, 수백에 수백 명의 사람들이 있었을 것이다.\n\n그럼에도, 이 모든 것을 인정하면서도, 나는—그런 상황에서라면 정녕 당연하고 용서받을 만한 고집으로—그때 내가 놓여 있던 그 견딜 수 없는 처지를 설명해 주는, 내가 볼 수 있는 오직 두 가지 설명 가운데 이런저런 것에 매달렸다. 내가 아직 만족하지 않았음을 알아채고, 베터리지는 월장석의 내력에서 어떤 나중의 사건들을 슬기롭게 들추었다. 그리하여 나의 두 이론을 대뜸, 그리고 영영, 바람에 날려 버렸다.\n\n\"이걸 다른 식으로 해 봅시다, 나리,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나리 나름의 견해를 지키시고, 그것이 진실을 알아내는 데로 나리를 얼마나 멀리 데려가는지 보십시오. 만일 우리가 그 잠옷을 믿는다면—저는 조금도 안 믿습니다만—나리는 나리 자신도 모르게 그 문에서 페인트를 묻히셨을 뿐 아니라, 나리 자신도 모르게 그 다이아몬드도 가져가신 것입니다. 여기까지 맞습니까?\"\n\n\"아주 맞네. 계속하게.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 나리. 나리가 그 보석을 가져가셨을 때 취해 계셨거나, 잠결에 걸으셨다고 해 둡시다. 그것이 그 생일 뒤의 밤과 아침을 설명해 줍니다. 허나 그것이 그때 이후로 일어난 일을 어떻게 설명합니까? 그때 이후로 그 다이아몬드는 런던으로 가져가졌습니다. 그때 이후로 그 다이아몬드는 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔습니다. 나리는 그 두 가지 일도, 나리 자신도 모르게 하셨습니까? 그 토요일 저녁에 제가 나리를 조랑말 마차에 태워 배웅했을 때 나리는 취해 계셨습니까? 그리고 기차가 나리를 여정의 끝에 데려다주었을 때, 나리는 루커 씨의 집으로 잠결에 걸어가셨습니까? 이런 말씀 드리는 걸 용서하십시오, 프랭클린 나리, 허나 이 일이 나리를 어찌나 심란하게 했던지, 나리는 아직 나리 스스로 판단하실 만한 상태가 못 됩니다. 나리의 머리를 브러프 씨의 머리 곁에 나란히 대실수록, 나리는 지금 나리를 옭아맨 그 막다른 처지에서 빠져나갈 길을 더 빨리 보시게 될 것입니다.\"\n\n우리는 겨우 일이 분을 남기고 그 역에 다다랐다.\n\n나는, 필요하다면 그가 나에게 편지할 수 있도록, 서둘러 베터리지에게 런던의 내 주소를 주었다. 내 편에서는, 내가 전할 만한 무슨 소식이든 그에게 알리겠다고 약속하면서. 이것을 마치고, 막 그에게 작별을 고하려는데, 나는 우연히 그 책과 신문 가판대 쪽을 흘긋 보았다. 거기, 다시 캔디 씨의 그 눈에 띄는 생김새의 조수가, 그 가판대 주인에게 이야기하고 있는 것이었다! 우리의 눈이 같은 순간에 마주쳤다. 에즈라 제닝스는 나에게 모자를 벗어 인사했다. 나는 그 인사에 답하고, 기차가 막 떠나는 참에 객차에 올랐다. 짐작건대, 나에게 개인적으로 아무런 중요성도 없어 보이는 어떤 화제에 마음을 두는 것이, 나의 마음에 안도가 되었다. 어쨌든, 나는—꽤나 어이없게도, 인정하거니와—내가 하루에 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼의 사내를 두 번이나 보았다는 것을 의아해하며, 나를 브러프 씨에게로 데려갈 그 중대한 귀로에 올랐다!\n\n내가 런던에 다다른 시각은 브러프 씨를 그의 일터에서 볼 온갖 희망을 앗아 갔다. 나는 철도역에서 햄스테드의 그의 사저로 마차를 몰았고, 그 늙은 변호사가 무릎에는 그가 아끼는 퍼그 개를, 팔꿈치께에는 포도주 병을 두고, 홀로 그의 식당에서 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 것을 방해했다.\n\n나의 이야기가 브러프 씨의 마음에 남긴 그 효과는, 그가 그것을 끝까지 듣고 나서 취한 조치들을 이야기하는 것으로 가장 잘 그려 낼 수 있으리라. 그는 그의 서재로 불과 진한 차를 들이라 일렀다. 그리고 그는 그의 가족의 부인들에게, 그 무슨 핑계로도 우리를 방해하지 말라는 전갈을 보냈다. 이 사전 채비를 마치고, 그는 먼저 그 잠옷을 살폈고, 그런 다음 로재나 스피어먼의 편지 읽기에 몰두했다.\n\n그 읽기가 끝나자, 브러프 씨는, 우리가 그의 방의 은밀함 속에 함께 들어앉은 이래 처음으로 나에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"프랭클린 블레이크,\" 하고 그 노신사가 말했다. \"이것은 한 가지 이상의 면에서 아주 중대한 일이오. 내 생각으로는, 이것은 당신에게 관련되는 것 못지않게 레이철에게도 가까이 관련되오. 그녀의 그 유별난 처신은 이제 수수께끼가 아니오. 그녀는 당신이 그 다이아몬드를 훔쳤다고 믿는 것이오.\"\n\n나는 나 자신의 논리로 그 역겨운 결론에 정직하게 이르기를 움츠려 피해 왔다. 허나 그것은 그럼에도 나에게 억지로 밀고 들어와 있었다. 레이철과의 직접 면담을 얻으려는 나의 결심은, 참으로 정녕, 브러프 씨가 방금 밝힌 그 근거 위에 놓여 있었다.\n\n\"이 조사에서 취할 첫걸음은,\" 하고 그 변호사가 말을 이었다. \"레이철에게 호소하는 것이오. 그녀는 여태 내내, 내가 (그녀의 성품을 아는 사람으로서) 어렵잖게 이해할 수 있는 동기에서, 침묵해 왔소. 이런 일이 일어난 뒤에는, 그 침묵을 더 이상 참는 것이 불가능하오. 그녀가, 당신이 월장석을 가져갔다는 믿음을 어떤 근거 위에 두고 있는지, 우리에게 말하도록 설득되든지, 아니면 억지로라도 말하게 되든지 해야 하오. 만일 우리가 레이철의 그 뿌리 깊은 과묵함을 뚫고, 그녀가 털어놓도록 이겨 낼 수만 있다면, 지금은 이토록 중대해 보이는 이 온 사건이, 산산조각으로 무너져 내릴 공산이 크오.\"\n\n\"그것은 나에게 아주 위안이 되는 견해요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 정녕 알고 싶소——\"\n\n\"당신은 내가 그것을 어떻게 정당화할 수 있는지 알고 싶겠지,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 끼어들었다. \"이 분 안에 말해 줄 수 있소. 우선, 내가 이 일을 변호사의 관점에서 본다는 것을 이해하시오. 나에게 이것은 증거의 문제요. 좋소. 그 증거는, 첫머리에서, 한 가지 중요한 점에서 무너지오.\"\n\n\"어떤 점에서요?\"\n\n\"들어 보시오. 나는 그 이름 표시가 그 잠옷이 당신의 것임을 증명한다는 것을 인정하오. 나는 그 페인트 자국이 그 잠옷이 레이철의 문에 그 얼룩을 냈음을 증명한다는 것을 인정하오. 허나 그 다이아몬드가 없어진 그 밤에 그것을 입은 사람이 당신이라는 것을 증명할 증거가 어디 있소?\"\n\n그 반론은, 그것이 나 자신도 느꼈던 반론을 되비치는 것이었던 만큼, 한층 더 세게 나를 때렸다.\n\n\"이것에 관해서는,\" 하고 그 변호사는 로재나 스피어먼의 고백을 집어 들며 말을 이었다. \"나는 그 편지가 당신에게 괴로운 편지임을 이해할 수 있소. 나는 당신이 그것을 순전히 공정한 관점에서 분석하기를 망설일 수 있음을 이해할 수 있소. 허나 나는 당신의 처지에 있지 않소. 나는 나의 직업적 경험을, 다른 어떤 문서에든 적용하듯, 바로 이 문서에 적용할 수 있소. 그 여인의 도둑으로서의 이력은 들먹이지 않고, 나는 다만 그녀의 편지가 그녀 자신이 내보인 대로 그녀가 속임수에 능한 사람이었음을 증명한다는 것만 짚어 두겠소. 그리고 나는 그것으로부터, 그녀가 온전한 진실을 말하지 않았다고 의심하는 것이 정당하다고 논하오. 나는 지금으로서는, 그녀가 무엇을 했을지 안 했을지에 대해 어떤 이론도 세우지 않겠소. 나는 다만 이렇게 말하겠소, 만일 레이철이 오직 그 잠옷의 증거만으로 당신을 의심했다면, 로재나 스피어먼이 그것을 그녀에게 보여 준 사람일 공산이 백에 아흔아홉이라고. 그런 경우, 여기 그 여인의 편지가 있소, 자기가 레이철을 질투했음을 고백하고, 자기가 그 장미를 바꿔치기했음을 고백하고, 레이철과 당신 사이의 다툼의 전망에서 자기 자신을 위한 희망의 실낱같은 빛을 보았음을 고백하는 편지 말이오. 나는 누가 월장석을 가져갔는지 물으려 멈추지 않겠소 (제 목적의 수단으로라면, 로재나 스피어먼은 월장석 쉰 개라도 가져갔을 거요)—나는 다만, 그 보석의 사라짐이, 당신을 사랑한 이 개과천선한 도둑에게, 당신과 레이철을 남은 평생 갈라놓을 기회를 주었다고 말하는 것이오. 기억하시오, 그녀는 그때는 아직 자기 자신을 없애기로 정하지 않았소. 그리고, 그 기회를 가진 채로, 그것을 붙잡는 것이 그녀의 성품에, 그리고 그때 그녀의 처지에 있었다고 나는 또렷이 단언하오. 그것에 대해 당신은 뭐라 하겠소?\"\n\n\"그와 비슷한 의심이,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"그 편지를 열자마자 내 마음도 스쳤소.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소! 그리고 그 편지를 다 읽었을 때, 당신은 그 가엾은 존재를 가엾이 여겨, 도무지 그녀를 의심할 마음이 나지 않았지. 당신을 돋보이게 하오, 나의 친애하는 이여—당신을 돋보이게 하오!\"\n\n\"허나 결국 내가 그 잠옷을 입었던 것으로 드러난다고 쳐 봅시다? 그러면 어찌 되오?\"\n\n\"나는 그 사실이 어떻게 증명될 수 있는지 모르겠소,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. \"허나 그 증명이 가능하다고 가정하면, 당신의 결백을 밝히는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아닐 거요. 지금은 그 문제에 들어가지 맙시다. 레이철이 오직 그 잠옷의 증거만으로 당신을 의심한 것은 아닌지 기다려 봅시다.\"\n\n\"맙소사, 레이철이 나를 의심한다는 이야기를 어찌 그리 태연히 하시오!\" 하고 나는 터뜨렸다. \"그녀가 무슨 권리로, 그 무슨 증거로, 나를 도둑으로 의심한단 말이오?\"\n\n\"아주 분별 있는 물음이오, 나의 친애하는 이여. 다소 격하게 던져졌으나—그래도 곰곰이 새겨 볼 값어치가 넉넉하오. 당신을 어리둥절하게 하는 것이, 나도 어리둥절하게 하오. 당신의 기억을 뒤져, 나에게 이것을 말해 보시오. 당신이 그 집에 머무는 동안 무슨 일이 일어났소—물론, 당신의 명예에 대한 레이철의 믿음을 뒤흔들 만한 것이 아니라—이를테면, (그 근거가 아무리 하찮든) 당신의 사람됨 전반에 대한 그녀의 믿음을 뒤흔들 만한 무슨 일이?\"\n\n나는, 걷잡을 수 없는 동요에 휩싸여, 벌떡 일어섰다. 그 변호사의 물음은, 내가 잉글랜드를 떠난 이래 처음으로, 정녕 무슨 일이 일어났었음을 나에게 일깨웠다.\n\n베터리지 이야기의 여덟째 장에, 볼일로 나를 보러 온, 어느 외국인이자 낯선 이의 내 이모 집 방문에 대한 언급이 있을 것이다. 그의 볼일의 성격은 이러했다.\n\n나는 어리석게도 (여느 때처럼 그 무렵 돈에 쪼들려) 파리의 어느 작은 식당 주인에게서 빚을 받아들였는데, 나는 그에게 손님으로 잘 알려져 있었다. 그 돈을 되갚을 때가 우리 사이에 정해졌다. 그리고 그때가 오자, 나는 (다른 수천의 정직한 사내들이 그러했듯이) 나의 약속을 지키는 것이 불가능함을 알았다. 나는 그 사내에게 어음 한 장을 보냈다. 안타깝게도 그런 문서에 나의 이름은 너무나 잘 알려져 있었다. 그는 그것을 유통하는 데에 실패했다. 내가 그에게서 빌린 이래 흘러간 사이에 그의 형편은 어지러워졌다. 파산이 그의 눈앞에 닥쳤다. 그리고 그의 친척 하나인 어느 프랑스인 변호사가, 나를 찾아 나의 빚의 상환을 강경히 요구하러 잉글랜드로 왔다. 그는 성미가 사나운 사내였다. 그리고 그는 나에게 잘못된 방도를 취했다. 양쪽에서 거친 말이 오갔다. 그리고 안타깝게도 내 이모와 레이철이 옆방에 있어, 우리 소리를 들었다. 레이디 버린더가 들어와, 무슨 일인지 알아야겠다고 다그쳤다. 그 프랑스인은 제 신임장을 내보이며, 내가, 나의 명예를 믿었던 한 가엾은 사내의 파멸에 책임이 있다고 선언했다. 내 이모는 대뜸 그에게 그 돈을 갚아, 그를 돌려보냈다. 그녀는 물론 그 거래에 대해 그 프랑스인의 견해를 취할 만큼 나를 몰랐던 것이 아니다. 허나 그녀는 나의 부주의에 충격을 받았고, 그녀의 개입이 아니었다면 아주 욕된 처지가 될 수도 있었던 처지에 나 자신을 몰아넣은 것에 대해 나에게 마땅히 화를 냈다. 그녀의 어머니가 그녀에게 말했든지, 아니면 레이철이 오간 말을 들었든지—어느 쪽인지 나는 말할 수 없다. 그녀는 그 일에 대해 제 나름의 낭만적이고 드높은 견해를 취했다. 나는 \"무정\"했고, 나는 \"명예를 모르는\" 사람이었고, 나는 \"지조가 없\"었으며, \"내가 다음에 무슨 짓을 할지 알 수 없\"었다—요컨대, 그녀는 젊은 아가씨의 입술에서 내가 여태 들은 가장 모진 말들을 나에게 했다. 우리 사이의 그 틈은 이튿날 온종일 이어졌다. 그다음 날, 나는 가까스로 화해를 이루었고, 그것을 더 마음에 두지 않았다. 나의 그녀의 평가 속의 자리가 다시, 그것도 훨씬 더 심각하게 공격받은 그 위태로운 순간에, 레이철이 이 불운한 사건으로 되돌아갔던 것일까? 브러프 씨는, 내가 그 정황을 그에게 이야기하자, 대뜸 그 물음에 그렇다고 답했다.\n\n\"그것은 그녀의 마음에 영향을 미쳤을 거요,\" 하고 그가 무겁게 말했다. \"그리고 나는, 당신을 위해, 그 일이 일어나지 않았기를 바라오. 어쨌든, 우리는 정녕 당신에게 불리하게 미리 마음을 기울인 영향이 있었음을 알아냈소—그리고 어쨌든 한 가지 불확실함이 우리 길에서 치워졌소. 이제 우리가 할 수 있는 것이 더는 보이지 않소. 이 조사에서 우리의 다음 걸음은 우리를 레이철에게로 데려가는 걸음이어야 하오.\"\n\n그는 일어서서, 생각에 잠긴 채 방 안을 오르내리며 걷기 시작했다. 두 번, 나는 그에게 레이철을 직접 만나기로 마음먹었다고 말하려는 참이었다. 그리고 두 번, 그의 나이와 그의 성품을 헤아려, 나는 좋지 않은 순간에 그를 깜짝 놀라게 하기를 망설였다.\n\n\"큰 어려움은,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"그녀가 이 일에서 제 온 마음을 감춤 없이 드러내도록 어떻게 만드느냐 하는 것이오. 당신은 내놓을 무슨 제안이 있소?\"\n\n\"나는 마음을 굳혔소, 브러프 씨, 내가 몸소 레이철에게 말하기로.\"\n\n\"당신이!\" 그는 별안간 걸음을 멈추고, 마치 내가 제정신을 놓았다고 여기는 듯 나를 바라보았다. \"세상 온갖 사람 가운데 하필 당신이!\" 그는 갑자기 스스로를 다잡고, 방 안을 한 바퀴 더 돌았다. \"잠깐만,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이런 유별난 종류의 경우에는, 무모한 방도가 때로 가장 좋은 방도이기도 하오.\" 그는 그 새로운 빛 아래에서 그 문제를 한두 순간 곰곰이 헤아리고는, 대담하게 나에게 유리한 결정으로 끝맺었다. \"부딪쳐 보지 않으면 얻는 것도 없지,\" 하고 그 노신사가 말을 이었다. \"당신에게는 내가 지니지 못한, 당신에게 유리한 기회가 있소—그러니 당신이 먼저 그 실험을 해 보시오.\"\n\n\"나에게 유리한 기회라니요?\" 하고 나는 크나큰 놀라움에 되뇌었다.\n\n브러프 씨의 얼굴이, 처음으로, 미소로 부드러워졌다.\n\n\"사정은 이렇소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신에게 솔직히 말하는데, 나는 당신의 신중함을 믿지 않고, 당신의 성미를 믿지 않소. 허나 나는, 레이철이 제 마음의 어느 외진 자그마한 구석에 여전히 당신에 대한 어떤 삐딱한 약함을 간직하고 있음은 믿소. 그것을 건드리시오—그리고 여인의 입술에서 흘러나올 수 있는 가장 온전한 털어놓음을 그 결과에 맡기시오! 문제는—어떻게 그녀를 만나느냐 하는 것이오.\"\n\n\"그녀는 이 집에서 당신의 손님이었소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"감히 제안해도 되겠소—만일 미리 나에 대해 아무 말도 없다면—내가 여기서 그녀를 만날 수 있으리라는 것을?\"\n\n\"뻔뻔하군!\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. 내가 그에게 한 그 대답에 대한 그 한마디 논평과 함께, 그는 방 안을 한 바퀴 더 오르내렸다.\n\n\"쉬운 말로 하면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"내 집이 레이철을 잡는 덫으로 바뀌는 것이오. 그녀를 꾈 미끼로, 내 아내와 딸들의 초대라는 모양을 하고서 말이오. 만일 당신이 프랭클린 블레이크 아닌 다른 누구였다면, 그리고 만일 이 일이 실제보다 티끌만큼이라도 덜 중대했다면, 나는 딱 잘라 거절했을 거요. 사정이 사정인지라, 나는 레이철이 내가 늘그막에 그녀를 배신한 것에 대해 언젠가 나에게 고마워하며 살리라 굳게 믿소. 나를 당신의 공범으로 여기시오. 레이철은 여기서 하루를 보내도록 청함을 받을 것이오. 그리고 당신은 그에 대한 마땅한 통지를 받을 것이오.\"\n\n\"언제요? 내일이오?\"\n\n\"내일은 그녀의 답을 받을 만한 시간을 주지 못하오. 모레라고 해 둡시다.\"\n\n\"당신에게서 어떻게 소식을 듣소?\"\n\n\"오전 내내 집에 계시고, 내가 당신을 찾아오기를 기다리시오.\"\n\n나는 그가 나에게 베풀고 있는 그 값을 헤아릴 수 없는 도움에 대해, 내가 참으로 느낀 고마움으로 그에게 감사했다. 그리고 그날 밤 햄스테드에서 자고 가라는 후한 초대를 사양하고, 런던의 내 하숙으로 돌아갔다.\n\n뒤이은 그날에 대해, 나는 그것이 내 생애에서 가장 긴 하루였다고 말할 수밖에 없다. 내가 스스로 결백함을 알았고, 나에게 씌워진 그 가증스러운 혐의가 조만간 반드시 벗겨지리라 확신했건만, 그럼에도 나의 마음에는, 본능적으로 나로 하여금 나의 어떤 벗도 만나고 싶지 않게 만드는 자기 비하의 느낌이 있었다. 우리는 죄가 결백처럼 보일 수 있다는 말을 흔히 듣는다 (허나, 거의 어김없이, 겉만 보는 이들에게서다). 나는 그 둘 가운데, 결백이 죄처럼 보일 수 있다는 것이 한없이 더 참된 격언이라 믿는다. 나는 온종일, 찾아오는 온갖 방문객에게 없다는 말을 하게 했다. 그리고 나는 오직 밤의 장막 아래에서만 감히 밖에 나갔다.\n\n이튿날 아침, 브러프 씨가 아침 식탁에서 나를 깜짝 놀라게 했다. 그는 나에게 큼직한 열쇠 하나를 건네며, 제 생애에서 처음으로 스스로가 부끄럽게 느껴진다고 알렸다.\n\n\"그녀가 오는 것이오?\"\n\n\"그녀는 오늘 오오, 점심을 들고 내 아내와 내 딸들과 함께 오후를 보내려고.\"\n\n\"브러프 부인과 당신의 딸들은 그 비밀을 아오?\"\n\n\"불가피하게도. 허나 당신도 알아챘겠지만, 여자들은 지조가 없소. 내 식구들은 내가 느끼는 양심의 아릿함을 느끼지 않소. 당신과 레이철을 다시 함께 맺어 주는 것이 목적이니, 내 아내와 딸들은 그것을 이루려 쓰이는 수단을, 마치 예수회 수사라도 되는 양 태연히 눈감아 버리오.\"\n\n\"그들에게 한없이 고맙소. 이 열쇠는 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"내 뒷마당 담의 문 열쇠요. 오늘 오후 세 시에 거기 가 계시오. 그 문으로 마당에 드시어, 온실 문으로 안에 들어가시오. 작은 응접실을 가로질러, 당신 앞의, 음악실로 이어지는 문을 여시오. 거기서 당신은 레이철을 보게 될 것이오—그리고 그녀를, 홀로 있는 그녀를 보게 될 것이오.\"\n\n\"어찌 당신에게 감사하리까!\"\n\n\"어찌 감사할지 내가 말해 주겠소. 그다음에 일어나는 일에 대해 나를 탓하지 마시오.\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 그는 나갔다.\n\n나는 아직 지새워야 할 고달픈 시간이 많았다. 시간을 때우려, 나는 내 편지들을 보았다. 그중에 베터리지에게서 온 편지가 있었다.\n\n나는 그것을 간절히 뜯었다. 나의 놀라움과 실망으로, 그것은 아무런 중요한 소식도 기대하지 말라 미리 이르는 사과로 시작했다. 그다음 문장에서 그 지긋지긋한 에즈라 제닝스가 다시 나타났다! 그는 역을 나서는 길에 베터리지를 붙들어, 내가 누구냐고 물었던 것이다. 이 점에 대해 알게 되자, 그는 자기 주인인 캔디 씨에게 나를 본 것을 언급했다. 이것을 들은 캔디 씨는, 우리가 서로 엇갈린 것에 대한 유감을 나타내려, 몸소 베터리지에게 마차를 몰고 왔다. 그는 각별히 나에게 이야기하고 싶은 이유가 있었다. 그리고 내가 다음에 프리징홀 근방에 오게 되면, 자기에게 알려 주기를 청했다. 베터리지 철학의 몇몇 특유한 발언들 말고는, 이것이 나의 편지 상대의 편지의 알맹이의 전부였다. 그 따뜻한 마음씨의, 충직한 노인은 자기가 \"주로 나에게 편지하는 즐거움 때문에\" 그것을 썼음을 인정했다.\n\n나는 그 편지를 주머니에 구겨 넣고, 곧이어 레이철과의 다가올 면담에 대한 온통 마음을 사로잡는 관심 속에, 그것을 잊어버렸다.\n\n햄스테드 교회의 시계가 세 시를 칠 때, 나는 그 담의 문 자물쇠에 브러프 씨의 열쇠를 넣었다. 내가 처음 그 마당으로 발을 들이고, 안쪽에서 다시 그 문을 잠그는 동안, 나는 그다음에 무슨 일이 일어날지에 대해 어떤 죄지은 듯한 미심쩍음을 느꼈음을 인정한다. 나는 내 양옆을 몰래 흘긋거렸다. 그 마당의 어느 알 수 없는 구석에, 뜻밖의 어떤 증인이 있지 않을까 미심쩍어하면서. 나의 염려를 정당화해 줄 것은 아무것도 나타나지 않았다. 그 길들은, 하나같이 죄, 인적 없었다. 그리고 새들과 벌들만이 유일한 증인이었다.\n\n나는 그 마당을 지나, 온실에 들어, 작은 응접실을 가로질렀다. 내가 맞은편 문에 손을 얹었을 때, 나는 안쪽 방의 피아노에서 몇몇 애처로운 화음이 울리는 것을 들었다. 그녀는 내가 그녀의 어머니의 집에 머물 때, 이런 식으로 그 악기를 만지작거리며 자주 시간을 보내곤 했다. 나는 나를 다잡으려, 잠시 기다리지 않을 수 없었다. 그 지고한 순간에, 과거와 현재가 나란히 솟아올랐다—그리고 그 대비가 나를 뒤흔들었다.\n\n일 분이 지난 뒤, 나는 나의 사내다움을 일깨워, 그 문을 열었다.\n\n# CHAPTER VII\n\n내가 그 문간에 모습을 드러낸 그 순간, 레이철이 피아노에서 일어섰다.\n\n나는 내 뒤의 문을 닫았다. 우리는, 방의 온 길이를 사이에 두고, 말없이 서로를 마주했다. 그녀가 일어서며 한 그 동작이, 그녀가 할 수 있는 유일한 안간힘인 듯했다. 몸이든 마음이든, 다른 온갖 능력의 온갖 쓰임이, 그저 나를 바라보는 행위 하나에 녹아든 것 같았다.\n\n내가 너무 느닷없이 모습을 드러낸 것은 아닌가 하는 두려움이 내 마음을 스쳤다. 나는 그녀를 향해 몇 걸음 나아갔다. 나는 부드럽게 말했다, \"레이철!\"\n\n내 목소리의 울림이 그녀의 팔다리에 생기를, 그녀의 얼굴에 핏기를 되돌려 놓았다. 그녀는, 제 편에서, 여전히 말없이 나아왔다. 천천히, 마치 제 뜻과는 별개의 어떤 영향 아래 움직이는 듯, 그녀는 나에게 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다. 따스한 거뭇한 홍조가 그녀의 두 뺨을 붉혔고, 되살아나는 지각의 빛이 순간순간 그녀의 눈 속에서 밝아졌다. 나는 나를 그녀 앞에 데려온 그 목적을 잊었다. 나는 나의 명예에 얹힌 그 비열한 의심을 잊었다. 나는 내가 마땅히 기억해야 할 온갖 사려를, 과거의 것도, 현재의 것도, 미래의 것도 잊었다. 나는 내가 사랑하는 그 여인이 나에게 점점 더 가까이 다가오는 것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그녀는 떨었다. 그녀는 망설이며 섰다. 나는 더는 그것을 견딜 수 없었다—나는 그녀를 내 품에 끌어안고, 그녀의 얼굴을 입맞춤으로 뒤덮었다.\n\n그 입맞춤이 되돌려지는 것 같은 순간이 있었다. 그녀도, 잊었을지 모른다고 여겨지는 순간이. 그 생각이 내 마음속에서 미처 꼴을 갖추기도 전에, 그녀의 첫 자발적인 행동이 나로 하여금 그녀가 기억하고 있음을 느끼게 했다. 공포의 외침 같은 외침과 함께—내가 애를 썼더라도 저항할 수 있었을지 의심스러운 힘과 함께—그녀는 나를 제게서 밀쳐 냈다. 나는 그녀의 눈에서 인정사정없는 노여움을 보았다. 나는 그녀의 입술에서 인정사정없는 경멸을 보았다. 그녀는, 마치 자기를 모욕한 낯선 이를 훑어보듯, 나를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보았다.\n\n\"이 비겁한 사람!\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"이 치사하고, 비참하고, 무정한 비겁한 사람!\"\n\n그것이 그녀의 첫마디였다! 여자가 남자에게 던질 수 있는 가장 견디기 어려운 꾸짖음이, 그녀가 나에게 던지려 골라낸 그 꾸짖음이었다.\n\n\"나는 그때를 기억하오, 레이철,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신이 나에게, 내가 당신의 마음을 상하게 했다고, 그것보다 더 값진 방식으로 말해 줄 수 있었던 그때를 말이오. 용서하시오.\"\n\n내가 느낀 쓰라림의 얼마쯤이 내 목소리에 옮아 갔을지도 모른다. 내 대답의 첫마디에, 조금 전 딴 데로 돌려져 있던 그녀의 눈이, 마지못해 나를 되돌아보았다. 그녀는, 내가 그녀를 겪은 중에 아주 새로운, 부루퉁하게 순응하는 태도로, 낮은 어조로 대답했다.\n\n\"어쩌면 저에게도 얼마쯤 변명거리가 있을지 몰라요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 한 일을 겪고서, 오늘 당신이 찾아온 그런 식으로 저에게 오는 것이, 당신 편에서 사내다운 짓인가요? 당신에 대한 저의 약함을 시험하려 실험을 하다니, 그것은 비겁한 실험 같아요. 저를 깜짝 놀라게 하여 당신이 입 맞추도록 두게 만들다니, 그것은 비겁한 기습 같아요. 허나 그것은 그저 한 여자의 견해일 뿐이지요. 그것이 당신의 견해일 수 없음을 저는 알았어야 했어요. 제가 저를 다잡고 아무 말도 하지 않았더라면 더 나았을 거예요.\"\n\n그 사과는 그 모욕보다 더 견디기 어려웠다. 살아 있는 가장 비천한 사람이라도 그것에 굴욕을 느꼈으리라.\n\n\"만일 나의 명예가 당신 손에 있지 않다면,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 이 순간 당신을 떠나, 다시는 당신을 보지 않을 것이오. 당신은 내가 한 일에 대해 말했소. 내가 무엇을 했소?\"\n\n\"당신이 무엇을 했느냐고요! 당신이 그 물음을 저에게 하는 건가요?\"\n\n\"내가 그것을 묻소.\"\n\n\"저는 당신의 그 파렴치함을 비밀로 지켜 왔어요,\" 하고 그녀가 대답했다. \"그리고 저는 그것을 감춘 대가를 치러 왔어요. 당신이 한 일이 무엇이냐고 저에게 묻는 그 모욕만은 면할 자격이 저에게 없단 말인가요? 온갖 고마움의 마음이 당신 안에서 다 죽어 버린 건가요? 당신은 한때 신사였어요. 당신은 한때 저의 어머니께 소중한 사람이었고, 저에게는 더욱더 소중한 사람이었어요——\"\n\n그녀의 목소리가 그녀를 저버렸다. 그녀는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 나에게 등을 돌리고, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다.\n\n나는 더 무슨 말을 할 만큼 나 자신을 믿기 전에 잠시 기다렸다. 그 침묵의 순간에, 나는 어느 쪽을 가장 날카롭게 느꼈는지 좀처럼 알 수 없다—그녀의 경멸이 내 안에 심어 놓은 그 가시인지, 아니면 나를 그녀의 괴로움과의 온갖 교감에서 몰아낸 그 오만한 결의인지.\n\n\"만일 당신이 먼저 말하지 않겠다면,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"내가 말해야겠소. 나는 당신에게 할 중대한 이야기를 지니고 여기 왔소. 내가 그것을 말하는 동안 들어 주는, 그 예사로운 도리라도 나에게 베풀어 주겠소?\"\n\n그녀는 움직이지도, 대답하지도 않았다. 나는 그녀에게 두 번 청하지 않았다. 나는 그녀의 의자로 한 치도 더 다가가지 않았다. 그녀의 오만만큼이나 고집스러운 오만을 안고, 나는 그녀에게 흔들리는 모래에서의 나의 발견과, 그것에 이르게 한 온갖 것을 이야기했다. 그 이야기는, 필연적으로, 얼마쯤의 시간이 걸렸다. 처음부터 끝까지, 그녀는 결코 나를 돌아보지 않았고, 결코 한마디도 입에 담지 않았다.\n\n나는 성미를 다스렸다. 나의 온 앞날은, 십중팔구, 그 순간 내가 나 자신을 잃지 않는 데에 달려 있었다. 브러프 씨의 이론을 시험대에 올릴 때가 온 것이다. 그 실험을 해 보려는 숨죽인 관심 속에, 나는 나 자신을 그녀 앞에 두려 빙 돌아 움직였다.\n\n\"당신에게 물을 것이 있소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그것은 내가 다시 괴로운 화제를 들추지 않을 수 없게 하오. 로재나 스피어먼이 당신에게 그 잠옷을 보여 주었소? 그렇소, 아니오?\"\n\n그녀는 벌떡 일어섰다. 그리고 제 발로 나에게 바싹 다가와 걸어왔다. 그녀의 눈이, 마치 여태 읽어 본 적 없는 무언가를 거기서 읽으려는 듯, 나의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보았다.\n\n\"당신 미쳤어요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n나는 여전히 나를 억눌렀다. 나는 조용히 말했다, \"레이철, 내 물음에 답해 주겠소?\"\n\n그녀는, 나에게 아랑곳없이, 말을 이어 갔다.\n\n\"제가 이해하지 못하는 무슨 얻을 목적이 있나요? 제가 관련된, 미래에 대한 무슨 치사한 두려움이? 당신 아버지의 죽음이 당신을 부자로 만들었다더군요. 당신은 저의 다이아몬드의 손실을 저에게 보상해 주려고 여기 온 건가요? 그리고 당신은 당신의 볼일이 부끄럽다고 느낄 만큼의 마음이 아직 남아 있나요? 그것이 당신의 결백한 척과, 로재나 스피어먼에 대한 당신의 이야기의 비밀인가요? 이번에는, 그 온갖 거짓의 밑바탕에 부끄러움의 동기가 있는 건가요?\"\n\n나는 거기서 그녀를 멈추게 했다. 나는 더는 나를 다스릴 수 없었다.\n\n\"당신은 나에게 파렴치한 잘못을 저질렀소!\" 하고 나는 격하게 터뜨렸다. \"당신은 내가 당신의 다이아몬드를 훔쳤다고 의심하고 있소. 나는 그 까닭을 알 권리가 있고, 나는 기어이 알고야 말겠소!\"\n\n\"당신을 의심한다고요!\" 하고 그녀가 외쳤다, 그녀의 노여움이 나의 노여움과 함께 치솟으며. \"이 악당아, 나는 내 두 눈으로 당신이 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 보았어요!\"\n\n그 말과 함께 나에게 터져 나온 그 폭로, 그것들이 브러프 씨가 기대었던 그 온 사건관을 대뜸 뒤엎어 버린 그 전복이, 나를 속수무책으로 때렸다. 결백했건만, 나는 그녀 앞에 말없이 서 있었다. 그녀의 눈에는, 어떤 눈에든, 나는 제 자신의 죄의 발각에 압도된 사내처럼 보였음이 틀림없다.\n\n그녀는 나의 굴욕과 그녀의 승리의 그 광경에서 물러났다. 나에게 내려앉은 그 별안간의 침묵이 그녀를 겁먹게 하는 듯했다. \"저는 그때 당신을 봐줬어요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"만일 당신이 저더러 말하도록 몰아붙이지만 않았다면, 저는 지금도 당신을 봐주었을 거예요.\" 그녀는 마치 방을 나가려는 듯 물러났다—그리고 문에 다다르기 전에 망설였다. \"어째서 당신은 여기 와서 당신 자신을 욕되게 하나요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다. \"어째서 당신은 여기 와서 저를 욕되게 하나요?\" 그녀는 몇 걸음 더 가더니, 다시 한 번 멈춰 섰다. \"제발, 무슨 말이든 좀 해요!\" 하고 그녀가 격정에 차 외쳤다. \"당신에게 자비가 조금이라도 남았다면, 제가 이런 식으로 저 자신을 깎아내리게 두지 말아요! 무슨 말이든 해요—그리고 저를 이 방에서 몰아내요!\"\n\n나는, 내가 무엇을 하는지도 거의 자각하지 못한 채, 그녀를 향해 나아갔다. 나는 아마도, 그녀가 나에게 더 말해 줄 때까지 그녀를 붙들어 두려는 어떤 어렴풋한 생각을 품었던 것 같다. 레이철의 마음속에서 나를 단죄하는 그 증거가 그녀 자신의 눈의 증거임을 안 그 순간부터, 아무것도—심지어 나 자신의 결백에 대한 나의 확신조차—내 마음에 또렷하지 않았다. 나는 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 나는 굳게, 그리고 요점에 맞게 말하려 애썼다. 내가 할 수 있었던 말은 오직, \"레이철, 당신은 한때 나를 사랑했소,\" 하는 것뿐이었다.\n\n그녀는 몸서리치며, 나에게서 눈을 돌렸다. 그녀의 손이 힘없이, 떨며 내 손 안에 놓여 있었다. \"그것을 놓아요,\" 하고 그녀가 가냘프게 말했다.\n\n나의 손길은, 내가 처음 그 방에 들어섰을 때 내 목소리의 울림이 자아냈던 것과 같은 효과를 그녀에게 미치는 듯했다. 나를 비겁한 사람이라 부른 그 말을 하고 나서도, 나를 도둑으로 낙인찍은 그 폭로를 하고 나서도—그녀의 손이 내 손 안에 놓여 있는 동안 나는 여전히 그녀의 주인이었다!\n\n나는 그녀를 부드럽게 방 한가운데로 도로 이끌었다. 나는 그녀를 내 곁에 앉혔다. \"레이철,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 하려는 말 속의 그 모순을 나는 설명할 수 없소. 나는 다만 당신이 진실을 말한 대로 진실을 말할 수 있을 뿐이오. 당신은 나를 보았소—당신 자신의 눈으로, 당신은 내가 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 보았소. 우리 말을 듣고 계신 하느님 앞에서, 나는 선언하오, 내가 그것을 가져갔음을 지금 처음으로 안다는 것을! 당신은 아직도 나를 의심하오?\"\n\n그녀는 나에게 아랑곳하지도, 나를 듣지도 않았다. \"내 손을 놓아요,\" 하고 그녀가 가냘프게 되뇌었다. 그것이 그녀의 유일한 대답이었다. 그녀의 머리가 내 어깨에 내려앉았다. 그리고 그녀의 손은, 나에게 그것을 놓아 달라고 청하는 그 순간에, 나도 모르게 내 손을 꼭 쥐었다.\n\n나는 그 물음을 다그치기를 삼갔다. 허나 나의 참음은 거기서 그쳤다. 다시 정직한 사람들 사이에서 고개를 들 나의 가망은, 그녀가 그 털어놓음을 온전히 하도록 이끌 나의 가망에 달려 있었다. 나에게 남은 유일한 희망은, 그녀가 그 증거의 사슬에서 무언가를—어쩌면 한낱 사소한 것을, 그럼에도 꼼꼼히 살피면 끝내 나의 결백을 밝히는 수단이 될 수 있을 무언가를 놓쳤을지도 모른다는 희망이었다. 나는 인정한다, 내가 그녀의 손을 계속 쥐고 있었음을. 나는 인정한다, 내가 지난 시절의 교감과 믿음 중에서 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 것을 담아 그녀에게 말했음을.\n\n\"당신에게 뭔가 묻고 싶소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 서로 잘 자라 인사한 그때부터, 당신이 내가 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 본 그때까지, 일어난 모든 것을 나에게 말해 주었으면 하오.\"\n\n그녀는 내 어깨에서 머리를 들고, 제 손을 빼려 애썼다. \"오, 어째서 그것으로 돌아가나요!\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"어째서 그것으로 돌아가나요!\"\n\n\"어째서인지 내가 말해 주겠소, 레이철. 당신은 희생자요, 나도 희생자요, 진실의 가면을 쓴 어떤 괴물 같은 착각의. 만일 우리가 당신의 생일 밤에 일어난 일을 함께 본다면, 우리는 아직 서로 이해하는 것으로 끝날 수 있을지도 모르오.\"\n\n그녀의 머리가 내 어깨에 도로 내려앉았다. 눈물이 그녀의 눈에 고여, 천천히 그녀의 뺨 위로 흘러내렸다. \"오!\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그 희망을 한 번도 품지 않았을 것 같아요? 지금 당신이 애쓰듯, 제가 그것을 보려 애쓰지 않았을 것 같아요?\"\n\n\"당신은 혼자서 애썼소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"당신은 당신을 도울 나와 함께 애쓰지는 않았소.\"\n\n그 말은, 내가 그것을 입에 담을 때 나 자신이 느낀 그 희망의 얼마쯤을 그녀 안에서 일깨우는 듯했다. 그녀는 나의 물음에 고분고분함 이상으로 답했다—그녀는 제 지각을 발휘했다. 그녀는 기꺼이 제 온 마음을 나에게 열었다.\n\n\"시작합시다,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 서로 잘 자라 인사하고 나서 일어난 일부터. 당신은 잠자리에 들었소? 아니면 앉아 있었소?\"\n\n\"저는 잠자리에 들었어요.\"\n\n\"시각을 눈여겨보았소? 늦었소?\"\n\n\"아주 늦지는 않았어요. 열두 시쯤이었던 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"잠이 들었소?\"\n\n\"아니요. 그날 밤 저는 잠들 수 없었어요.\"\n\n\"뒤척였소?\"\n\n\"당신을 생각하고 있었어요.\"\n\n그 대답은 나를 거의 무너뜨렸다. 그 말보다도 더, 그 어조 속의 무언가가 곧장 내 가슴에 와닿았다. 나는 먼저 잠시 멈추고 나서야 비로소 계속할 수 있었다.\n\n\"당신 방에 무슨 불이 있었소?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"없었어요—제가 다시 일어나 초에 불을 붙이기 전까지는.\"\n\n\"그것은, 당신이 잠자리에 든 지 얼마 뒤였소?\"\n\n\"한 시간쯤 뒤였던 것 같아요. 한 시쯤.\"\n\n\"당신은 침실을 떠났소?\"\n\n\"떠나려던 참이었어요. 저는 실내복을 걸쳤고, 책을 가지러 응접실로 들어가려던 참이었어요——\"\n\n\"침실 문을 열었소?\"\n\n\"막 열었어요.\"\n\n\"허나 응접실로는 들어가지 않았소?\"\n\n\"아니요—저는 그리 들어가는 것을 저지당했어요.\"\n\n\"무엇이 당신을 저지했소?\"\n\n\"저는 문 아래로 불빛을 보았어요. 그리고 그것으로 다가오는 발소리를 들었어요.\"\n\n\"겁이 났소?\"\n\n\"그때는 아니었어요. 저는 저의 가엾은 어머니가 잠귀가 여림을 알았어요. 그리고 저는 그분이 그날 저녁, 제 다이아몬드를 자기가 맡게 해 달라고 저를 몹시 설득하려 애쓰신 것을 떠올렸어요. 제 생각에 그분은 그것에 대해 지나치게 걱정하셨어요. 그래서 저는 그분이, 제가 잠자리에 들었는지 보려고, 그리고 제가 안 자고 있으면 다시 그 다이아몬드에 대해 저에게 말씀하려고, 저에게 오시는 것이라고 짐작했어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 어찌했소?\"\n\n\"저는 그분이 제가 잠자리에 든 줄로 여기시도록, 제 초를 불어 껐어요. 저도, 제 편에서 지나쳤지요—저는 제 다이아몬드를 제가 고른 곳에 두기로 마음을 굳혔어요.\"\n\n\"초를 불어 끄고 나서, 당신은 잠자리로 돌아갔소?\"\n\n\"돌아갈 짬이 없었어요. 제가 초를 불어 끈 그 순간에, 응접실 문이 열렸고, 저는 보았어요——\"\n\n\"당신은 보았소?\"\n\n\"당신을요.\"\n\n\"여느 때처럼 옷을 입고?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"내 잠옷 차림으로?\"\n\n\"당신의 잠옷 차림으로—당신의 침실 초를 손에 들고서요.\"\n\n\"혼자서?\"\n\n\"혼자서요.\"\n\n\"내 얼굴을 볼 수 있었소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"또렷이?\"\n\n\"아주 또렷이요. 당신 손의 그 초가 그것을 저에게 보여 줬어요.\"\n\n\"내 눈이 떠져 있었소?\"\n\n\"네.\"\n\n\"그 눈에서 무슨 이상한 것을 눈여겨보았소? 뭔가 고정된, 텅 빈 표정 같은 것을?\"\n\n\"그런 것은 없었어요. 당신의 눈은 밝았어요—여느 때보다 더 밝았어요. 당신은, 마치 자기가 있어서는 안 될 곳에 있음을 아는 듯, 그리고 마치 들킬까 두려워하는 듯, 방 안을 두리번거렸어요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 방에 들어왔을 때 한 가지를 눈여겨보았소—내가 어떻게 걸었는지 눈여겨보았소?\"\n\n\"당신은 늘 걷는 대로 걸었어요. 당신은 방 한가운데까지 들어왔어요—그러고는 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러봤어요.\"\n\n\"나를 처음 보았을 때, 당신은 어찌했소?\"\n\n\"저는 아무것도 할 수 없었어요. 저는 돌처럼 굳어 버렸어요. 저는 말할 수도, 소리칠 수도, 심지어 제 문을 닫으러 움직일 수도 없었어요.\"\n\n\"당신이 서 있던 그곳에서, 내가 당신을 볼 수 있었겠소?\"\n\n\"당신은 틀림없이 저를 볼 수 있었을 거예요. 허나 당신은 결코 제 쪽을 보지 않았어요. 그 물음은 물어봐야 헛일이에요. 저는 당신이 결코 저를 보지 못했음을 확신해요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 확신하오?\"\n\n\"만일 당신이, 제가 깨어서 당신을 보고 있음을 보았다면—당신이 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔겠어요? 당신이 그 뒤에 한 대로 처신했겠어요? 당신이 지금 여기 있겠어요? 그 부분에 대해 저더러 말하게 하지 말아요! 저는 당신에게 조용히 답하고 싶어요. 제가 될 수 있는 한 침착하게 있도록 도와줘요. 다른 것으로 넘어가요.\"\n\n그녀가 옳았다—온갖 면에서, 옳았다. 나는 다른 것들로 넘어갔다.\n\n\"내가 방 한가운데에 이르러, 거기서 멈춰 선 뒤에, 나는 무엇을 했소?\"\n\n\"당신은 몸을 돌려, 곧장 창가 모퉁이로 갔어요—제 인도식 장식장이 서 있는 곳으로요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 장식장에 있었을 때, 내 등이 당신 쪽으로 돌려져 있었음이 틀림없소. 당신은 내가 무엇을 하는지 어떻게 보았소?\"\n\n\"당신이 움직이면, 저도 움직였어요.\"\n\n\"내가 내 손으로 무엇을 하는지 볼 수 있도록?\"\n\n\"제 응접실에는 거울이 세 개 있어요. 당신이 거기 서 있는 동안, 저는 그중 하나에 비친, 당신이 한 모든 것을 봤어요.\"\n\n\"당신은 무엇을 보았소?\"\n\n\"당신은 당신의 초를 그 장식장 위에 놓았어요. 당신은 서랍을 하나씩 열었다 닫았다 하며, 제가 제 다이아몬드를 넣어 둔 그 서랍에 이르렀어요. 당신은 잠시 그 열린 서랍을 들여다봤어요. 그러고는 손을 넣어, 그 다이아몬드를 꺼냈어요.\"\n\n\"내가 그 다이아몬드를 꺼낸 것을 당신이 어떻게 아오?\"\n\n\"저는 당신 손이 그 서랍으로 들어가는 것을 봤어요. 그리고 저는 당신이 손을 뺄 때, 당신의 검지와 엄지 사이에서 그 돌이 반짝이는 것을 봤어요.\"\n\n\"내 손이 다시 그 서랍으로 다가갔소—이를테면, 그것을 닫으려고?\"\n\n\"아니요. 당신은 오른손에 그 다이아몬드를 쥐고 있었어요. 그리고 당신은 왼손으로 그 장식장 위에서 그 초를 집었어요.\"\n\n\"그 뒤에 내가 다시 주위를 둘러보았소?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"내가 곧바로 방을 떠났소?\"\n\n\"아니요. 당신은, 긴 시간처럼 느껴진 동안, 아주 가만히 서 있었어요. 저는 그 거울 속에서 당신의 옆얼굴을 봤어요. 당신은 무언가 생각하는, 그리고 제 자신의 생각에 흡족해하지 않는 사내처럼 보였어요.\"\n\n\"그다음에 무슨 일이 있었소?\"\n\n\"당신은 별안간 정신을 차리더니, 곧장 방을 걸어 나갔어요.\"\n\n\"내가 나가면서 문을 닫았소?\"\n\n\"아니요. 당신은 재빨리 복도로 나가, 문을 열어 둔 채였어요.\"\n\n\"그러고는?\"\n\n\"그러고는, 당신의 불빛이 사라졌고, 당신의 발소리가 잦아들었고, 저는 어둠 속에 홀로 남겨졌어요.\"\n\n\"아무 일도 없었소—그때부터, 온 집이 그 다이아몬드가 없어졌음을 안 그때까지?\"\n\n\"아무 일도요.\"\n\n\"그것을 확신하오? 그 사이 얼마쯤 잠들지는 않았소?\"\n\n\"저는 결코 잠들지 않았어요. 저는 결코 제 잠자리로 돌아가지 않았어요. 아침의 여느 때에 페넬로피가 들어올 때까지 아무 일도 없었어요.\"\n\n나는 그녀의 손을 놓고, 일어나, 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌았다. 내가 던질 수 있는 온갖 물음에 답이 주어졌다. 내가 알고 싶어 할 수 있는 온갖 세세한 것이 내 앞에 놓였다. 나는 심지어 몽유의 생각과 취중의 생각으로 되돌아가 보았다. 그리고, 다시, 그 두 이론의 무가치함이 증명되었다—이번에는, 나를 본 그 증인의 권위로써. 다음으로 무슨 말을 해야 하는가? 다음으로 무엇을 해야 하는가? 거기 그 절도라는 끔찍한 사실이 솟아 있었다—다른 온갖 것을 뒤덮은 그 뚫을 수 없는 어둠의 한복판에서 나를 마주하는, 유일하게 눈에 보이고 만질 수 있는 대상이! 흔들리는 모래에서 로재나 스피어먼의 비밀을 손에 넣었을 때에도 나를 이끌어 줄 빛 한 줄기 없었다. 그리고 지금, 레이철 자신에게 호소하여, 그녀 자신의 입술에서 그 밤의 가증스러운 이야기를 들었을 때에도 빛 한 줄기 없었다.\n\n이번에는, 그 침묵을 먼저 깬 것이 그녀였다.\n\n\"자?\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 물었고, 저는 답했어요. 당신이 이 모든 것에서 무언가를 바랐기에, 당신은 저로 하여금 이 모든 것에서 무언가를 바라게 만들었어요. 이제 당신은 할 말이 뭐가 있나요?\"\n\n그녀가 말하는 그 어조가, 그녀에 대한 나의 영향력이 다시 한 번 잃어버린 영향력임을 나에게 일러 주었다.\n\n\"우리는 나의 생일 밤에 일어난 일을 함께 보기로 했지요,\" 하고 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 우리는 그때 서로를 이해하기로 했지요. 우리가 그것을 했나요?\"\n\n그녀는 인정사정없이 나의 대답을 기다렸다. 그녀에게 답하면서 나는 치명적인 잘못을 저질렀다—나는 내 처지의 그 부아 나는 속수무책함이 나의 자제심을 이기도록 두었다. 경솔하고 쓸데없이, 나는 그 순간까지 나를 진실에 대한 무지에 붙들어 둔 그 침묵에 대해 그녀를 나무랐다.\n\n\"만일 당신이 말했어야 할 때에 말했다면,\" 하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"만일 당신이 스스로 해명하는, 그 예사로운 도리를 나에게 베풀었다면——\"\n\n그녀가 격노의 외침으로 나의 말을 끊었다. 내가 한 그 몇 마디가 그 자리에서 그녀를 격분의 광란으로 몰아넣은 듯했다.\n\n\"스스로 해명한다고요!\" 하고 그녀가 되뇌었다. \"오! 세상에 이런 남자가 또 있을까요? 내 가슴이 찢어질 때 나는 그를 봐줬어요. 내 자신의 명예가 걸렸을 때 나는 그를 감싸 줬어요. 그런데 그가—온갖 인간 가운데 하필 그가—이제 나에게 등을 돌려, 나더러 스스로 해명했어야 한다고 말하는군요! 그를 그토록 믿고서, 그를 그토록 사랑하고서, 낮에는 그를 생각하고, 밤에는 그를 꿈꾸고서—그는 어째서 내가 우리가 처음 만났을 때 그의 그 파렴치를 그에게 들이대지 않았는지 의아해하네요. '내 마음의 님이여, 당신은 도둑이에요! 내가 사랑하고 우러르는 나의 영웅이여, 당신은 밤을 틈타 내 방에 숨어들어, 내 다이아몬드를 훔쳤어요!' 그것이 내가 말했어야 할 것이라니. 이 악당아, 이 치사하고, 치사하고, 치사한 악당아, 나는 다이아몬드 쉰 개를 잃을지언정, 지금 내가 보고 있듯 당신의 얼굴이 나에게 거짓말하는 것을 보지는 않았을 거예요!\"\n\n나는 내 모자를 집어 들었다. 그녀에 대한 자비로—그렇다! 나는 정직하게 그리 말할 수 있다—그녀에 대한 자비로, 나는 한마디 없이 몸을 돌려, 내가 그 방에 들어온 그 문을 열었다.\n\n그녀가 뒤따라와, 내 손에서 그 문을 낚아챘다. 그녀는 그것을 닫고, 내가 떠나온 그 자리를 도로 가리켰다.\n\n\"안 돼요!\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"아직은! 아무래도 내가 당신에게 내 처신에 대한 해명을 빚진 것 같군요. 당신은 남아서 그것을 들어요. 아니면 당신은 온갖 것 가운데 가장 낮은 파렴치로 몸을 굽혀, 억지로 나가 버리든가요.\"\n\n그녀를 보는 것이 내 가슴을 쥐어짰다. 그녀를 듣는 것이 내 가슴을 쥐어짰다. 나는 하나의 몸짓으로 답했다—그것이 내가 할 수 있는 전부였다—내가 그녀의 뜻에 따르겠노라는.\n\n내가 돌아가, 말없이 내 의자를 잡자, 노여움의 진홍빛 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에서 바래기 시작했다. 그녀는 잠시 기다리며, 자신을 다잡았다. 그녀가 말을 이었을 때, 그녀에게서 알아볼 수 있는 감정의 표시는 단 하나뿐이었다. 그녀는 나를 보지 않고 말했다. 그녀의 두 손은 무릎에 꼭 맞잡혀 있었고, 그녀의 눈은 바닥에 못 박혀 있었다.\n\n\"나는 당신에게 스스로 해명하는, 그 예사로운 도리를 베풀었어야 했어요,\" 하고 그녀가, 내 자신의 말을 되뇌며 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 도리를 다하려 애썼는지 아닌지 당신이 보게 될 거예요. 나는 방금 당신에게, 당신이 내 응접실을 떠난 뒤로 내가 결코 잠들지 않았고, 결코 내 잠자리로 돌아가지 않았다고 말했어요. 내가 무엇을 생각했는지를 곱씹어 당신을 괴롭히는 것은 헛일이에요—당신은 내 생각을 이해하지 못할 테니까요—나는 다만, 나를 추스르는 데 도움이 될 만큼 시간이 충분히 지났을 때, 내가 무엇을 했는지만 당신에게 말하겠어요. 나는—마땅히 그랬어야 했듯이—온 집을 깨워 무슨 일이 일어났는지 모두에게 알리기를 삼갔어요. 내가 본 것에도 불구하고, 나는, 당신이 일부러 도둑이라고 내 마음속에 인정하느니, 차라리—무엇이든!—어떤 불가능이든 믿을 만큼 당신을 사랑했어요. 나는 생각하고 또 생각했어요—그리고 나는 당신에게 편지 쓰는 것으로 끝냈어요.\"\n\n\"나는 그 편지를 결코 받지 못했소.\"\n\n\"당신이 그것을 결코 받지 못한 것을 나는 알아요. 잠시 기다려요, 그러면 어째서인지 듣게 될 거예요. 내 편지는 당신에게 아무것도 드러내 놓고 말하지 않았을 거예요. 그것이 다른 어떤 사람의 손에 들어갔더라도, 그것은 당신을 평생 파멸시키지 않았을 거예요. 그것은 다만—당신 자신은 도저히 잘못 알 수 없는 방식으로—내가 당신이 빚졌음을 알 만한 근거가 있다는 것과, 당신이 돈이 필요할 때 그것을 어떻게 마련하는지에 대해 그리 신중하지도, 그리 조심스럽지도 않다는 것이 나의, 그리고 나의 어머니의 당신에 대한 경험이라는 것을 말했을 뿐이에요. 당신은 그 프랑스인 변호사의 방문을 떠올리고, 내가 무엇을 가리키는지 알았을 거예요. 만일 당신이 그다음에 얼마쯤 관심을 가지고 계속 읽었다면, 당신은 내가 당신에게 할 제안에 이르렀을 거예요—사사로이 (명심해요, 우리 사이에 그것에 대해 드러내 놓고 한마디도 말하지 않기로 하고!), 내가 마련할 수 있는 만큼 큰 금액을 빌려주겠다는 제안에요.—그리고 나는 그것을 마련했을 거예요!\" 하고 그녀가 외쳤다, 그녀의 핏기가 다시 오르기 시작하고, 그녀의 눈이 다시 한 번 나를 올려다보며. \"만일 다른 어떤 방도로도 그 돈을 마련할 수 없었다면, 나는 그 다이아몬드를 내 손으로 저당 잡혔을 거예요! 그런 말로 나는 당신에게 편지했어요. 잠깐! 나는 그 이상을 했어요. 나는 아무도 가까이 없을 때 당신에게 그 편지를 주도록 페넬로피와 짰어요. 나는 내 침실에 나를 가두고, 응접실을 오전 내내 열어 둔 채 비워 두기로 짰어요. 그리고 나는 바랐어요—온 마음과 온 넋으로 나는 바랐어요!—당신이 그 기회를 붙잡아, 그 다이아몬드를 몰래 그 서랍에 도로 넣기를.\"\n\n나는 말하려 했다. 그녀가 조바심을 내며 손을 들어, 나를 멈추게 했다. 그녀의 성미의 빠른 오르내림 속에서, 그녀의 노여움이 다시 치솟기 시작하고 있었다. 그녀는 제 의자에서 일어나, 나에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"나는 당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 알아요,\" 하고 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 다시 나에게, 당신이 내 편지를 결코 받지 못했음을 일깨우려는 거지요. 어째서인지 내가 말해 줄게요. 나는 그것을 찢어 버렸어요.\"\n\n\"무슨 이유로요?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"더없이 마땅한 이유로요. 나는 그것을 당신 같은 사내에게 던져 버리느니 찢어 버리는 편을 택했어요! 아침에 나에게 다다른 첫 소식이 무엇이었을까요? 나의 그 자그마한 계획이 막 완성되었을 때, 내가 무엇을 들었을까요? 나는 당신이—당신이!!!—경찰을 부르는 데에 그 집에서 가장 앞장선 사람이라는 것을 들었어요. 당신이 그 발 벗고 나선 사내였어요. 당신이 그 주동자였어요. 당신이 그 보석을 되찾으려 그들 중 누구보다 더 열심히 애쓰고 있었어요! 당신은 심지어 뻔뻔함을 한껏 부려, 그 다이아몬드의 분실에 대해 나에게 이야기하기를 청하기까지 했어요—당신 자신이 훔친 그 다이아몬드, 내내 당신 자신의 손안에 있던 그 다이아몬드에 대해서요! 당신의 그 끔찍한 거짓됨과 교활함의 그 증거를 겪고서, 나는 내 편지를 찢어 버렸어요. 허나 그때조차—당신이 들여보낸 그 경찰관의 뒤짐과 신문에 내가 미쳐 버릴 지경이었을 때조차—그때조차, 내 마음속에는 당신을 포기하지 못하게 하는 어떤 홀림이 있었어요. 나는 스스로에게 말했어요, '그는 집안의 다른 모든 사람 앞에서 그의 그 비열한 광대극을 벌였어. 그가 내 앞에서 그것을 벌일 수 있는지 시험해 보자.' 누군가 나에게 당신이 그 테라스에 있다고 말했어요. 나는 그 테라스로 내려갔어요. 나는 억지로 당신을 보았어요. 나는 억지로 당신에게 말했어요. 당신은 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 잊었나요?\"\n\n나는 그 한마디 한마디를 다 기억한다고 답할 수도 있었으리라. 허나 그 순간, 그 답이 무슨 소용에 닿았겠는가?\n\n그녀가 한 말이 나를 놀라게 했고, 나를 괴롭게 했고, 그녀가 위태로운 신경의 흥분 상태에 있음을 나에게 넌지시 일러 주었고, 심지어 그 보석의 분실이 우리 나머지에게만큼이나 그녀에게도 수수께끼인지 나의 마음에 한순간의 의심마저 불러일으켰다는 것을—허나 진실에 대한 실낱같은 엿봄조차 나에게 한 번도 준 적이 없었다는 것을, 내가 어찌 그녀에게 말할 수 있었겠는가? 나의 결백을 밝히려 내놓을 증거의 그림자 하나 없이, 그녀가 그 테라스에서 나에게 말했을 때 그녀의 생각 속에 정말로 무엇이 있었는지를, 내가 그야말로 온전한 낯선 이가 알 수 있었을 것보다 조금도 더 알지 못했음을, 내가 어찌 그녀를 설득할 수 있었겠는가?\n\n\"잊는 것이 당신 편할지 모르지요. 기억하는 것이 나는 편해요,\" 하고 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"나는 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 알아요—그것을 말하기 전에 나 스스로 곰곰이 헤아렸으니까요. 나는 당신에게 진실을 털어놓을 기회를 하나 또 하나 주었어요. 나는 내가 할 수 있는 말은 하나도 안 한 채 남겨 두지 않았어요—당신이 그 절도를 저질렀음을 내가 안다고 실제로 말하는 것만 빼고요. 그런데 당신이 한 보답이라고는, 당신의 그 비열한 놀란 척과, 당신의 그 거짓된 결백의 얼굴로 나를 바라보는 것뿐이었어요—꼭 당신이 오늘 나를 바라본 것처럼요. 꼭 당신이 지금 나를 바라보고 있는 것처럼요! 나는 그날 아침, 마침내 당신이 어떤 사람인지를—당신이 어떤 사람인지를—여태 이 땅 위를 걸은 가장 비열한 몹쓸 것임을 알고서, 당신을 떠났어요!\"\n\n\"만일 당신이 그때 털어놓았다면, 당신은 자기가 결백한 사람에게 잔인하게 잘못했음을 알고서, 나를 떠날 수 있었을 것이오, 레이철.\"\n\n\"만일 내가 다른 사람들 앞에서 털어놓았다면,\" 하고 그녀가 또 한 차례의 분노의 터짐과 함께 되받았다. \"당신은 평생 욕되게 되었을 거예요! 만일 내가 당신 귀에만 털어놓았다면, 당신은, 지금 부인하고 있듯, 그것을 부인했을 거예요! 내가 당신을 믿었을 것 같아요? 내가 당신이 하는 것을 본 짓을 한 사내가—그 뒤에 그것에 대해 내가 당신이 처신하는 것을 본 대로 처신한 사내가—거짓말 앞에 망설였을까요? 다시 말하지만, 나는 당신이 훔치는 것을 본 그 끔찍함 뒤에, 당신이 거짓말하는 것을 듣는 그 끔찍함이 무서워 움츠러들었어요. 당신은 마치 이것이 몇 마디 말로 바로잡힐 수 있었을 오해이기라도 한 양 말하는군요! 그래요! 그 오해는 끝났어요. 그것이 바로잡혔나요? 아니요! 그것은 여전히 그대로예요. 나는 지금도 당신을 안 믿어요! 나는 당신이 그 잠옷을 찾았다는 것을 안 믿어요. 나는 로재나 스피어먼의 편지를 안 믿어요. 나는 당신이 한 말 한마디도 안 믿어요. 당신이 그것을 훔쳤어요—나는 당신을 봤어요! 당신이 경찰을 돕는 척했어요—나는 당신을 봤어요! 당신이 런던의 그 돈놀이꾼에게 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡혔어요—나는 그것을 확신해요! 당신이 당신의 파렴치의 그 의심을 (나의 비열한 침묵 덕분에!) 결백한 사람에게 씌웠어요! 당신이 이튿날 아침 당신의 노획물을 가지고 대륙으로 달아났어요! 그 온갖 비열함을 저지르고, 당신이 할 수 있는 것이 딱 한 가지 더 남았지요. 당신은 입술에 마지막 거짓을 담고 여기 올 수 있었어요—당신은 여기 와서, 내가 당신에게 잘못했다고 나에게 말할 수 있었어요!\"\n\n만일 내가 한순간 더 머물렀다면, 나는 뒤에 헛된 뉘우침과 후회로 기억했을 그 무슨 말이 내 입에서 새어 나왔을지 알지 못한다. 나는 그녀를 지나쳐, 두 번째로 그 문을 열었다. 두 번째로—격앙된 여자의 광란 어린 어깃장으로—그녀가 내 팔을 붙들어, 내가 나가는 길을 막았다.\n\n\"나를 가게 해 주오, 레이철,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그것이 우리 둘 다에게 더 나을 것이오. 나를 가게 해 주오.\"\n\n발작적인 격정이 그녀의 가슴속에서 부풀어 올랐다—그녀가 그 문에서 나를 막아서는 동안, 그녀의 가빠진 경련하는 숨결이 거의 내 얼굴에 부딪쳤다.\n\n\"어째서 당신은 여기 왔나요?\" 하고 그녀가 필사적으로 다그쳤다. \"다시 물을게요—어째서 당신은 여기 왔나요? 내가 당신을 폭로할까 두려운 건가요? 이제 당신은 부자가 되었고, 이제 당신은 세상에서 자리를 얻었고, 이제 당신은 이 나라에서 가장 좋은 규수와 혼인할 수 있어요—내가 여태 당신 말고는 아무에게도 하지 않은 그 말을 할까 두려운 건가요? 나는 그 말을 못 해요! 나는 당신을 폭로할 수 없어요! 나는, 더 나쁠 수 있다면, 당신 자신보다 더 나빠요.\" 흐느낌과 눈물이 그녀에게서 터져 나왔다. 그녀는 그것들과 사납게 싸웠다. 그녀는 나를 점점 더 굳게 붙들었다. \"나는 당신을 내 마음에서 뜯어낼 수 없어요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"지금조차도요! 오직 이런 식으로만 당신에게 맞서 발버둥 칠 수 있는 이 부끄럽고, 부끄러운 약함을 믿어도 좋아요!\" 그녀가 별안간 나를 놓았다—그녀는 두 손을 치켜들어, 허공에 대고 미친 듯이 비틀었다. \"살아 있는 다른 어떤 여자라도 그를 만지는 그 수치를 꺼렸을 거예요!\" 하고 그녀가 외쳤다. \"오, 하느님! 나는 그를 경멸하는 것보다 나 자신을 더욱더 진심으로 경멸해요!\"\n\n눈물이, 나도 모르게, 내 눈으로 밀고 들어오고 있었다—그 끔찍함은 더는 견딜 수 없었다.\n\n\"당신은 아직, 당신이 나에게 잘못했음을 알게 될 것이오,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"아니면 당신은 다시는 나를 보지 못할 것이오!\"\n\n그 말과 함께, 나는 그녀를 떠났다. 그녀는 조금 전 털썩 주저앉았던 그 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나—그 고귀한 존재는!—바깥방을 가로질러 나를 뒤따라오며, 헤어질 때의 마지막 자비로운 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"프랭클린!\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 당신을 용서해요! 오, 프랭클린, 프랭클린! 우리는 다시는 만나지 못할 거예요. 당신도 나를 용서한다고 말해 줘요!\"\n\n나는, 내가 말할 수 없는 지경임을 내 얼굴이 그녀에게 보이도록 몸을 돌렸다—나는 몸을 돌려, 손을 흔들었고, 마침내 나를 이겨 버린 그 눈물 너머로, 마치 환영 속에서인 양, 흐릿하게 그녀를 보았다.\n\n그다음 순간, 그것의 가장 모진 쓰라림이 지나갔다. 나는 다시 그 정원에 나와 있었다. 나는 그녀를 다시는 보지도, 듣지도 못했다.\n\n# CHAPTER VIII\n\n그날 저녁 늦게, 나는 내 하숙에서 브러프 씨의 방문에 놀랐다.\n\n그 변호사의 태도에는 눈에 띄는 변화가 있었다. 그것은 여느 때의 자신감과 기운을 잃은 채였다. 그는, 제 생애에서 처음으로, 말없이 나와 악수했다.\n\n\"햄스테드로 돌아가는 길이오?\" 하고 나는 무슨 말이든 하려는 셈으로 물었다.\n\n\"나는 방금 햄스테드를 떠나왔소,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"나는 아오, 프랭클린 씨, 당신이 마침내 진실에 다다랐음을. 허나, 솔직히 말하는데, 만일 그것을 위해 치러질 대가를 내가 미리 내다볼 수 있었다면, 나는 차라리 당신을 어둠 속에 남겨 두는 편을 택했을 거요.\"\n\n\"당신은 레이철을 보았소?\"\n\n\"나는 그녀를 포틀랜드 플레이스로 데려다주고서 여기 왔소. 그녀를 마차에 혼자 태워 돌려보내기란 불가능했소. 나는 좀처럼 당신에게 책임을 물을 수 없소—당신이 내 집에서, 내 허락으로 그녀를 보았음을 헤아리면 말이오—이 불운한 면담이 그녀에게 가한 그 충격에 대해서. 내가 할 수 있는 것은 다만 그 화의 되풀이를 막는 것뿐이오. 그녀는 젊소—그녀는 굳센 기상이 있소—그녀는, 시간과 안식이 그녀를 도우면, 이것을 이겨 낼 것이오. 나는 당신이 그녀의 회복을 방해할 아무 짓도 하지 않으리라는 것을 다짐받고 싶소. 내가 당신이, 나의 재가와 승인이 있는 경우 말고는, 그녀를 만나려는 두 번째 시도를 하지 않으리라 믿어도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"그녀가 겪은 일 뒤에, 그리고 내가 겪은 일 뒤에,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신은 나를 믿어도 좋소.\"\n\n\"당신의 약속을 받은 것이오?\"\n\n\"나의 약속을 받은 것이오.\"\n\n브러프 씨는 안도한 낯빛이었다. 그는 제 모자를 내려놓고, 제 의자를 내 의자 가까이 끌어당겼다.\n\n\"그것은 정해졌소!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이제, 미래에 대해서인데—당신의 미래 말이오. 내 생각으로는, 이 일이 이제 취한 그 유별난 전환의 결과는 간추리면 이렇소. 우선, 우리는 레이철이, 말로 할 수 있는 한 또렷이, 당신에게 온 진실을 말했음을 확신하오. 둘째로—어딘가에 무슨 끔찍한 잘못이 있음이 틀림없음을 우리가 알기는 하나—우리는 그녀가, 제 자신의 감각의 증거로, 그 증거가, 겉으로 보기에 당신에게 완전히 불리하게 말하는 정황들로 뒷받침되었으니, 당신을 죄지었다고 믿는 것을 좀처럼 탓할 수 없소.\"\n\n거기서 나는 끼어들었다. \"나는 레이철을 탓하지 않소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 다만 그녀가 그때 나에게 더 또렷이 말하도록 스스로를 이겨 내지 못한 것이 안타까울 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"그것은 레이철이 다른 누군가가 아닌 것을 안타까워하는 것이나 마찬가지요,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다. \"그리고 그렇다 한들, 당신과 혼인하는 데에 제 마음을 쏟았던, 얼마쯤이라도 섬세한 아가씨가, 당신의 면전에 대고 당신이 도둑이라고 들이댈 마음을 스스로 낼 수 있었을지 나는 의심스럽소. 어쨌든, 그것은 레이철의 천성에 없는 일이었소. 이 당신의 일과는 아주 다른 어떤 일에서—허나 그것은 그녀를, 당신을 향한 처지와 아주 다르지만은 않은 처지에 놓았소—나는 마침, 그녀가 당신의 경우에 그녀의 처신을 움직인 동기와 비슷한 동기에 영향받았음을 아오. 게다가, 그녀 자신이 오늘 저녁 시내로 오는 길에 나에게 말했듯이, 만일 그녀가 정녕 또렷이 말했더라도, 그녀는 그때 당신의 부인을, 지금 그것을 믿지 않는 것보다 조금도 더 믿지 않았을 거요. 그것에 당신은 무슨 대답을 할 수 있소? 그것에는 할 대답이 없소. 자, 자, 프랭클린 씨! 이 사건에 대한 나의 견해가 죄 틀린 것으로 증명되었음을 나는 인정하오—허나, 사정이 지금 이러하니, 나의 조언은 그럼에도 들어 둘 값어치가 있을지 모르오. 솔직히 말하는데, 만일 우리가 거꾸로 되짚어, 이 무시무시한 얽힘을 처음부터 풀어내려 한다면, 우리는 우리의 시간을 허비하고, 아무 소용 없이 우리의 머리를 쥐어짜는 것이 될 거요. 지난해에 레이디 버린더의 시골집에서 일어난 온갖 것에 우리의 마음을 단호히 닫읍시다. 그리고 우리가 과거에서 알아낼 수 없는 것 대신에, 우리가 미래에서 알아낼 수 있는 것을 봅시다.\"\n\n\"당신은 정녕 잊고 있소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"이 온 일이, 나에게 관련되는 한, 본질적으로 과거의 일이라는 것을?\"\n\n\"이것에 답해 보시오,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다. \"월장석이 이 온갖 화의 밑바탕에 있소—아니오?\"\n\n\"있소—물론이오.\"\n\n\"좋소. 그 월장석이 런던으로 가져가졌을 때, 그것이 어찌되었다고 우리는 믿소?\"\n\n\"그것은 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔소.\"\n\n\"우리는 그것을 저당 잡힌 사람이 당신이 아님을 아오. 우리는 누가 그리했는지 아오?\"\n\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"우리는 그 월장석이 지금 어디 있다고 믿소?\"\n\n\"루커 씨의 은행가의 보관에 맡겨져 있소.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 자 눈여겨보시오. 우리는 이미 유월에 들어와 있소. 이달 말경이면 (하루까지 꼭 집을 수는 없소) 우리가 그 보석이 저당 잡혔다고 믿는 그때로부터 한 해가 흘러가 있을 거요. 실낱같은 가능성이나마—적어도—그것을 저당 잡힌 사람이, 그 한 해의 기한이 다하면 그것을 되찾을 채비를 하고 있을 가능성이 있소. 만일 그가 그것을 되찾는다면, 루커 씨는 몸소—제 자신의 약정 조건에 따라—그 다이아몬드를 제 은행가의 손에서 꺼내야 하오. 이런 상황에서, 나는 이달이 끝나 갈 무렵 그 은행에 파수를 세워, 루커 씨가 그 월장석을 되돌려 주는 사람이 누구인지 알아낼 것을 제안하오. 이제 그것이 보이오?\"\n\n나는 (다소 마지못해) 그 생각이 어쨌든 새로운 것임을 인정했다.\n\n\"그것은 나의 생각인 것 못지않게 머스웨이트 씨의 생각이기도 하오,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. \"얼마 전 우리가 함께 나눈 대화가 아니었다면, 그것은 결코 내 머리에 떠오르지 않았을지도 모르오. 만일 머스웨이트 씨가 옳다면, 그 인도인들도 이달 말경 그 은행을 노리고 지켜볼 공산이 크오—그리고 거기서 무언가 심상찮은 일이 나올지도 모르오. 거기서 무엇이 나오든, 그것이 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡힌 그 알 수 없는 누군가에게 우리 손을 얹는 데에 도움이 되는 한에서 말고는, 당신과 나에게 대수롭지 않소. 그 사람이, 믿어도 좋소, 당신이 이 순간 처한 그 처지에 (나는 어떻게인지 안다고 짐짓 말하지는 않겠소) 책임이 있소. 그리고 오직 그 사람만이 레이철의 평가 속에서 당신을 바로잡아 줄 수 있소.\"\n\n\"나는 부인할 수 없소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신이 제안하는 그 계획이 아주 대담하고, 아주 기발하고, 아주 새로운 방식으로 그 어려움에 맞선다는 것을. 허나——\"\n\n\"허나 당신은 내놓을 반론이 있군?\"\n\n\"그렇소. 나의 반론은, 당신의 제안이 우리로 하여금 기다리게 한다는 것이오.\"\n\n\"인정하오. 내가 시간을 헤아리기로는, 그것은 당신에게 두 주쯤—더도 덜도—기다리기를 요구하오. 그것이 그리 오래오?\"\n\n\"그것은 나 같은 처지에서는, 브러프 씨, 한평생이오. 나는 당장 나의 명예를 씻는 무언가를 하지 않고서는, 나의 삶이 나에게 그저 견딜 수 없을 것이오.\"\n\n\"그래, 그래, 나도 그것을 이해하오. 당신이 무엇을 할 수 있을지 아직 생각해 보았소?\"\n\n\"나는 커프 부장과 상의할 생각을 했소.\"\n\n\"그는 경찰에서 물러났소. 그 부장이 당신을 돕기를 바라는 것은 헛일이오.\"\n\n\"나는 그를 어디서 찾을지 아오. 그리고 나는 해 볼 수밖에 없소.\"\n\n\"해 보시오,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 잠시 생각하고 나서 말했다. \"이 사건은 커프 부장의 시절 이래 어찌나 유별난 양상을 띠었던지, 당신이 정녕 그 조사에 대한 그의 관심을 되살릴 수 있을지도 모르오. 해 보시고, 나에게 그 결과를 들려주시오. 그동안,\" 하고 그는 일어서며 말을 이었다. \"만일 당신이 지금과 이달 말 사이에 아무것도 알아내지 못한다면, 나는 내 편에서, 그 은행을 지켜봄으로써 무엇을 할 수 있을지 해 봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"물론이오,\" 하고 나는 대답했다—\"내가 그 사이에 당신이 그 실험을 해 볼 온갖 필요를 덜어 주지 않는 한 말이오.\"\n\n브러프 씨는 미소 지으며, 제 모자를 집어 들었다.\n\n\"커프 부장에게 전하시오,\" 하고 그가 되받았다. \"진실의 발견이 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡힌 사람의 발견에 달려 있다고 내가 말한다고. 그리고 그것에 대해 그 부장의 경험이 뭐라 하는지 나에게 들려주시오.\"\n\n그리하여 우리는 헤어졌다.\n\n이튿날 아침 일찍, 나는 도킹이라는 작은 읍으로 길을 나섰다—베터리지가 나에게 일러 준 대로, 커프 부장의 은거지였다.\n\n그 호텔에서 물어, 나는 그 부장의 오두막을 찾는 데 필요한 안내를 받았다. 그것은 읍에서 조금 벗어난, 조용한 샛길로 다가가게 되어 있었고, 그것은 뒤와 옆으로는 튼튼한 벽돌담에, 앞으로는 높은 산울타리에 둘러싸여, 제 나름의 정원 뙈기 한가운데에 아늑히 서 있었다. 위쪽이 산뜻하게 칠해진 격자 세공으로 꾸며진 그 문은, 잠겨 있었다. 종을 울리고서, 나는 그 격자 세공 사이로 들여다보았고, 그 위대한 커프가 아끼는 꽃이 곳곳에 있는 것을 보았다. 그의 정원에 피어나고, 그의 문 위로 무리 지고, 그의 창을 들여다보며. 그 큰 도시의 범죄와 수수께끼에서 멀리 떨어져, 그 이름난 도둑잡이는 장미에 파묻혀, 제 생애의 마지막 향락의 나날을 고요히 살아 내고 있었다!\n\n점잖은 나이 든 여자가 나에게 그 문을 열어 주었고, 대뜸 커프 부장의 도움을 얻는 데에 내가 쌓아 올린 온갖 희망을 무너뜨렸다. 그는, 겨우 그 전날, 아일랜드로 가는 여행길에 올랐던 것이다.\n\n\"그가 볼일로 거기 갔소?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n그 여자는 미소 지었다. \"그분에게는 이제 볼일이 딱 하나뿐입니다, 나리,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 장미지요. 아일랜드의 어느 대단한 분의 정원사가 장미 기르는 데에서 무언가 새로운 것을 알아냈답니다—그래서 커프 씨는 그것을 알아보러 떠나셨어요.\"\n\n\"그가 언제 돌아올지 아오?\"\n\n\"아주 알 수 없습니다, 나리. 커프 씨는, 그 새 발견이 아무 값어치가 없다고 보시는지, 아니면 살펴볼 값어치가 있다고 보시는지에 꼭 따라, 곧 돌아오시거나 한동안 떠나 계시거나 하겠다고 하셨어요. 만일 그분께 남길 전갈이 있으시면, 제가 그분이 그것을 받으시도록 마음 쓰겠습니다, 나리.\"\n\n나는, 먼저 그 위에 연필로 이렇게 써서, 그녀에게 내 명함을 주었다. \"월장석에 관해 드릴 말씀이 있습니다. 돌아오시는 대로 연락 주십시오.\" 그것을 마치니, 상황에 따르는 것과, 런던으로 돌아가는 것 말고는 남은 것이 아무것도 없었다.\n\n지금 내가 쓰고 있는 그 무렵의, 나의 마음의 짜증스러운 상태에서, 그 부장의 오두막으로의 나의 여행의 헛된 결과는 그저 무언가를 하려는 내 안의 그 안절부절못하는 충동을 부추길 뿐이었다. 도킹에서 돌아온 날, 나는 이튿날 아침에는 내가, 온갖 장애를 뚫고 어둠에서 빛으로 억지로 나아가려는 새로운 애씀에 마음을 쏟고 있게끔 하리라 마음먹었다.\n\n나의 다음 실험은 무슨 꼴을 띠어야 하는가?\n\n그 물음을 헤아리는 동안 그 훌륭한 베터리지가 곁에 있었더라면, 그리고 그가 내 생각의 비밀에 들어왔더라면, 그는 두말할 것 없이, 이번에는 나의 독일식 면이 나의 가장 위에 있는 면이라고 선언했으리라. 진지하게 말하자면, 나의 독일식 훈련이, 내가 이제 나 자신을 얽어 넣은 그 쓸데없는 사변의 미로에 얼마쯤 책임이 있었는지도 모른다. 밤의 태반 동안, 나는 앉아서 담배를 피우며, 하나가 다른 하나보다 더 깊이 있음 직하지 않은 이론들을 쌓아 올렸다. 정작 잠이 들자, 나의 깨어 있을 적의 공상이 꿈속에서 나를 좇았다. 나는 이튿날 아침, 객관-주관과 주관-객관이 내 마음속에 풀 수 없이 뒤엉킨 채로 일어났다. 그리고 나는, 무슨 종류든 실질적인 행동을 향한 나의 다음 애씀을 지켜볼 그날을, 내가 (순전히 철학적인 근거에서) 어떤 종류의 것이든 (그 다이아몬드를 포함하여) 도무지 존재하는 것으로 여길 무슨 권리가 있는지 의심하는 것으로 시작했다.\n\n만일 나 스스로 헤쳐 나오도록 내버려 두어졌더라면, 내가 얼마나 오래 나 자신의 형이상학의 안개 속에 길 잃은 채 있었을지, 나는 말할 수 없다. 결과가 증명했듯이, 우연이 나를 구하러 와서, 다행히 나를 건져 냈다. 나는 그날 아침, 마침 레이철과 면담한 그날 입었던 그 웃옷을 입고 있었다. 주머니 하나에서 다른 무언가를 찾다가, 나는 구겨진 종잇조각 하나에 손이 닿았고, 그것을 꺼내니, 내 손에 잊고 있던 베터리지의 편지가 있었다.\n\n나의 착한 옛 벗을 답장 없이 두는 것은 그에게 야박한 일로 여겨졌다. 나는 내 필기 탁자로 가서, 그의 편지를 다시 읽었다.\n\n조금도 중요할 것이 없는 편지가, 늘 답하기 쉬운 편지인 것은 아니다. 나와 편지를 주고받으려는 베터리지의 이번 애씀은 이 부류에 들었다. 캔디 씨의 조수, 다시 말해 에즈라 제닝스가 제 주인에게 나를 보았다고 말했고, 캔디 씨는, 제 차례로, 내가 다음에 프리징홀 근방에 오면 나를 보고 나에게 무언가 말하고 싶어 했다. 그것에 대한 답으로, 그것이 적힌 종이 값어치라도 할 무슨 말을 할 수 있단 말인가? 나는 베터리지에게 바치기로 다짐한 그 종이 위에, 캔디 씨의 눈에 띄는 생김새의 조수의 얼굴을 기억에서 이리저리 그리며 한가로이 앉아 있었다—문득, 여기 그 억누를 수 없는 에즈라 제닝스가 다시 내 길을 가로막고 있다는 것이 떠오르기까지! 나는 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼의 사내의 초상을 (그 머리칼은 어느 경우에나 놀랍도록 비슷했다) 적어도 열두 장은 휴지통에 던져 넣었다—그러고는 그 자리에서, 베터리지에게 내 답장을 썼다. 그것은 더없이 평범한 편지였다—허나 그것은 나에게 한 가지 훌륭한 효과를 냈다. 몇 문장을, 쉬운 영어로 쓰는 그 애씀이, 전날부터 내 마음을 채웠던 그 안개 낀 헛소리를 완전히 걷어 냈다.\n\n나 자신의 처지가 나에게 내놓은 그 뚫을 수 없는 수수께끼의 해명에 다시 한 번 몰두하며, 나는 이제 그것을 명백히 실질적인 관점에서 살핌으로써 그 어려움에 맞서려 했다. 그 잊지 못할 밤의 사건들이 여전히 나에게 알쏭달쏭했으므로, 나는 조금 더 뒤로 돌아가, 그 실마리를 찾는 데에 나에게 얼마쯤 도움이 될 만한 어떤 사건이든 찾으려, 그 생일의 이른 시각들에 대한 나의 기억을 뒤졌다.\n\n레이철과 내가 그 칠한 문을 마무리하는 동안 무슨 일이 일어났던가? 또는, 나중에, 내가 프리징홀로 말을 몰고 갔을 때? 또는 그 뒤에, 내가 고드프리 에이블화이트와 그의 누이들과 함께 돌아왔을 때? 또는, 다시 나중에, 내가 그 월장석을 레이철의 손에 쥐여 주었을 때? 또는, 더 나중에, 손님들이 오고, 우리 모두가 만찬 식탁에 둘러 모였을 때? 나의 기억은 그 일련의 물음들을, 마지막 것에 이르기까지, 어렵잖게 처리했다. 그 생일 만찬이라는 그 사교 행사를 되돌아보니, 나는 그 조사의 첫머리에서 우뚝 멈추어 섰다. 나는 심지어 나와 같은 식탁에 앉았던 손님들의 수조차 정확히 기억할 수 없었다.\n\n여기서 내가 완전히 막혔음을 느끼는 것과, 그리하여 그 만찬의 사건들이 각별히 그것들을 조사하는 수고를 갚아 줄지 모른다고 결론짓는 것은, 나의 경우에, 같은 사고 과정의 부분을 이루었다. 나는 비슷한 처지에 놓인 다른 사람들도 나처럼 헤아렸으리라 믿는다. 우리 자신의 이해를 좇는 것이 우리로 하여금 우리 자신에게 조사의 대상이 되게 할 때, 우리는 자연히 우리가 모르는 것을 미심쩍어한다. 그 만찬에 있었던 사람들의 이름을 일단 손에 넣으면, 나는—나 자신의 부족한 기억의 밑천을 넉넉히 하는 수단으로—나머지 손님들의 기억에 호소하기로, 그들이 그 생일의 사교적 사건들에 대해 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것을 적어 두기로, 그리고 이렇게 얻은 결과를, 손님들이 그 집을 떠난 뒤에 일어난 일의 빛으로 검증하기로 마음먹었다.\n\n조사의 기술에서 내가 궁리한 그 숱한 실험 가운데 이 마지막이자 가장 새로운 것은—베터리지라면 아마 이 순간 나의 명석한, 곧 프랑스식 면이 가장 위에 있는 탓으로 돌렸을 것이다—그 자체의 값어치로, 여기 기록될 자격이 넉넉하다. 있음 직하지 않아 보일지 모르나, 나는 이제 마침내 정녕 그 일의 뿌리로 더듬어 나아간 것이었다. 내가 원한 것은 다만 시작에서 나를 옳은 방향으로 이끌어 줄 실마리 하나뿐이었다. 하루가 더 내 머리 위로 지나가기 전에, 그 실마리가 그 생일 잔치에 있었던 손님 가운데 하나에 의해 나에게 주어졌다!\n\n내가 이제 염두에 둔 그 진행의 계획으로는, 먼저 그 손님들의 온전한 명단을 지녀야 했다. 이것을 나는 게이브리얼 베터리지에게서 쉽게 얻을 수 있었다. 나는 그날 요크셔로 돌아가, 이튿날 아침 내가 궁리한 조사를 시작하기로 마음먹었다.\n\n정오 전에 런던을 떠나는 그 기차로 출발하기에는 딱 너무 늦었다. 다음 기차의 출발까지 거의 세 시간을 기다리는 것 말고는 다른 방도가 없었다. 이 사이의 시간을 쓸모 있게 채울 만한, 내가 런던에서 할 수 있는 무언가가 있었던가?\n\n나의 생각은 다시 고집스레 그 생일 만찬으로 돌아갔다.\n\n나는 그 수를, 그리고, 많은 경우, 손님들의 이름을 잊었건만, 그들 가운데 훨씬 더 큰 비율이 프리징홀이나 그 근방에서 왔다는 것은 어렵잖게 기억했다. 허나 그 더 큰 비율이 전부는 아니었다. 우리 중 몇몇은 그 지방의 상주자가 아니었다. 나 자신이 그 몇몇 중 하나였다. 머스웨이트 씨가 또 하나였다. 고드프리 에이블화이트가 셋째였다. 브러프 씨는—아니다. 나는 볼일이 브러프 씨가 그 자리의 하나가 되는 것을 막았음을 떠올렸다. 여느 거처가 런던인 부인이 있었던가? 나는 오직 클랙 양만이 이 뒤엣 부류에 드는 것으로 기억할 수 있었다. 어쨌든, 여기, 어쨌든, 내가 시내를 떠나기 전에 만나는 것이 명백히 이로울 손님이 셋 있었다. 나는 대뜸 브러프 씨의 사무소로 마차를 몰았다. 내가 찾는 그 사람들의 주소를 알지 못했고, 그가 나에게 그들을 찾는 길을 일러 줄지도 모른다고 여겨서.\n\n브러프 씨는 그의 값진 시간을 나에게 일 분 넘게 내주기에는 너무 바쁜 것으로 드러났다. 허나 그 일 분 안에, 그는—더없이 맥 빠지게 하는 방식으로—내가 그에게 물을 온갖 물음을 처리해 냈다.\n\n우선, 그는 그 수수께끼의 실마리를 찾는 나의 새로 발견한 방법을, 진지하게 논하기에는 너무나 순전히 공상적인 무엇으로 여겼다. 둘째로, 셋째로, 넷째로, 머스웨이트 씨는 이제 제 지난 모험의 무대로 돌아가는 길에 있었다. 클랙 양은 손실을 겪었고, 절약의 동기에서, 프랑스에 자리 잡았다. 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 런던 어딘가에서 찾을 수 있을지도, 없을지도 몰랐다. 그의 클럽에서 물어보면 어떻겠소? 그리고 만일 브러프 씨가 제 볼일로 돌아가 나에게 안녕을 고한다면 양해해 주겠소?\n\n런던에서의 조사의 마당이, 이제 오직 고드프리의 주소를 알아내는 그 한 가지 필요만을 담게끔 그토록 좁혀졌으므로, 나는 그 변호사의 귀띔을 따라, 그의 클럽으로 마차를 몰았다.\n\n그 현관홀에서, 나는 내 사촌의 옛 벗이자 나 자신과도 아는 사이인 회원 하나와 마주쳤다. 이 신사는, 고드프리의 주소에 관해 나를 밝혀 준 뒤, 그의 삶에서의 최근의 두 사건에 대해 나에게 이야기해 주었는데, 그것들은 그 자체로 얼마쯤 중요했고, 그때껏 나의 귀에 다다르지 않은 것이었다.\n\n알고 보니 고드프리는, 레이철이 그에 대한 혼약에서 물러난 것에 낙심하기는커녕, 그 뒤 곧 크나큰 상속녀로 알려진 다른 젊은 아가씨에게 혼담을 건넸던 것이다. 그의 구애는 순조로웠고, 그의 혼인은 정해지고 확실한 일로 여겨졌다. 허나, 여기서도 다시, 그 혼약은 별안간 뜻밖에 깨지고 말았다—이번에는, 재산 분배의 문제를 두고 신랑과 그 아가씨의 아버지 사이의 심각한 견해차 때문이었다는 소문이었다.\n\n이 두 번째 혼인의 낭패에 대한 얼마쯤의 보상으로, 고드프리는 그 뒤 곧 제 숱한 흠모자 가운데 하나의, 다정한 금전적 기억의 대상이 된 것을 알았다. 어느 부유한 노부인이—'어머니회 어린이옷 개조 협회'에서 크게 존경받고, 클랙 양의 좋은 벗이었던 (그 클랙 양에게는 상장 반지 하나 말고는 아무것도 남기지 않았다) 부인이—그 감탄스럽고 갸륵한 고드프리에게 오천 파운드의 유산을 유증했던 것이다. 제 자신의 조촐한 금전 밑천에 이 큼직한 보탬을 받고서, 그는 자기가 자선 사업에서 잠시 벗어나 숨 돌릴 필요를 느낀다고, 그리고 자기 의사가 \"앞으로 그의 건강에 크게 이로울 법한 것으로서, 대륙 여행\"을 처방했다고 말하는 것이 들렸다. 만일 내가 그를 보고 싶다면, 지체 없이 내가 궁리한 방문을 하는 것이 이로울 터였다.\n\n나는, 그 자리에서, 나의 방문을 하러 갔다.\n\n내가 커프 부장을 찾아가는 데에 딱 하루 너무 늦게 했던 그 똑같은 숙명이, 내가 고드프리를 찾아가는 데에도 다시 딱 하루 너무 늦게 했다. 그는, 그 전날 아침, 조석(潮汐) 기차로, 도버로 떠났다. 그는 오스텐트로 건너가기로 되어 있었다. 그리고 그의 하인은 그가 브뤼셀로 계속 가는 것이라 믿었다. 그가 돌아올 때는 자못 알 수 없었다. 허나 그가 적어도 석 달은 떠나 있으리라는 것만은 확실했다.\n\n나는 다소 풀 죽은 심사로 내 하숙으로 돌아갔다. 그 생일 만찬의 손님 셋이—그것도 그 셋이 죄 유달리 총명한 사람들이—그들과 연락할 수 있는 것이 가장 중요한 바로 그때에, 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었다. 나의 마지막 희망은 이제 베터리지에게, 그리고 레이철의 시골집 근방에서 아직 살아 있는 것을 볼 수 있을 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 벗들에게 놓였다.\n\n이번에는, 나는 곧장 프리징홀로 갔다—그 읍이 이제 내 조사의 마당에서 중심점이었으므로. 나는 저녁에 너무 늦게 다다라 베터리지와 연락할 수 없었다. 이튿날 아침, 나는 편지를 든 심부름꾼을 보내어, 되도록 편한 때에 그 호텔로 나에게 와 달라고 청했다.\n\n시간을 아끼려, 또 얼마쯤은 베터리지를 배려하려, 내 심부름꾼을 삯마차로 보내는 조심을 했으므로, 나는, 아무 지체도 없다면, 그를 부르러 보낸 때로부터 두 시간이 채 못 되어 그 노인을 볼 만한 상당한 전망이 있었다. 이 사이에, 나는 내가 몸소 아는, 그리고 내 손이 쉽게 닿는, 그 생일 만찬에 있었던 손님들 사이에서 내가 궁리한 조사를 시작하는 데에 나 자신을 쓰기로 정했다. 이들은 나의 친척인 에이블화이트 집안 사람들과, 캔디 씨였다. 그 의사는 나를 보고 싶다는 각별한 바람을 나타낸 터였고, 그 의사는 다음 거리에 살았다. 그래서 나는 먼저 캔디 씨에게로 갔다.\n\n베터리지가 나에게 말해 준 것을 겪고서, 나는 자연히 그 의사의 얼굴에서 그가 앓은 그 심한 병의 자취를 볼 것을 예상했다. 허나 나는 그가 방에 들어와 나와 악수했을 때 그에게서 본 그런 변화에는 전혀 채비가 되어 있지 않았다. 그의 눈은 흐릿했고, 그의 머리칼은 완전히 세었고, 그의 얼굴은 쭈그러들었고, 그의 몸은 오그라들었다. 나는, 나의 기억 속에서 고칠 수 없는 사교적 경솔과 헤아릴 수 없는 어린애 같은 농담을 저지르는 것과 결부된, 한때 활달하고, 수다스럽고, 익살맞던 그 자그마한 의사를 보았다—그리고 나는 그의 이전 자신에게서 남은 것을, 그의 옷차림의 저속한 멋에 대한 옛 성벽 말고는, 아무것도 보지 못했다. 그 사람은 잔해였다. 허나 그의 옷과 그의 장신구는—그에게 일어난 변화에 대한 잔인한 조롱인 양—여느 때와 다름없이 화사하고 야했다.\n\n\"나는 당신을 자주 생각했소, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 마침내 당신을 다시 보게 되어 진심으로 기쁘오. 만일 내가 당신을 위해 할 수 있는 것이 있다면, 부디 내 도움을 부리시오, 선생—부디 내 도움을 부리시오!\"\n\n그는 그 몇 마디 평범한 말을 쓸데없이 서두르며 안달하는 태도로, 그리고 무엇이 나를 요크셔로 데려왔는지 알고 싶어 하는—그가 완전히, 어린애처럼이라 이를 만큼, 남의 눈에서 감출 수 없는—호기심을 안고 말했다.\n\n내가 염두에 둔 그 목적으로, 나는 물론, 대개 나에게 낯선 그 사람들이 나의 조사를 돕는 데에 최선을 다하도록 관심을 두게 하기를 바라기 전에, 얼마쯤의 개인적 설명에 들어갈 필요를 미리 내다본 터였다. 프리징홀로 오는 여행길에 나는 나의 설명이 어떠할지를 짜 두었다—그리고 나는 이제 나에게 주어진 그 기회를 붙잡아, 그것의 효과를 캔디 씨에게 시험해 보았다.\n\n\"나는 요전 날 요크셔에 있었고, 지금 다시 요크셔에 있소, 자못 낭만적인 볼일로,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그것은, 캔디 씨, 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 벗들이 죄 얼마쯤 관심을 둔 일이오. 이제 거의 한 해 전인, 그 인도 다이아몬드의 알 수 없는 분실을 기억하시오? 근래에, 그것이 아직 찾아질지도 모른다는 희망으로 이어지는 정황들이 일어났소—그리고 나는, 그 집안의 한 사람으로서, 그것을 되찾는 데에 나 자신의 관심을 두고 있소. 내 길의 장애물 가운데, 그때 발견된 온갖 증거를, 그리고 가능하다면 그 이상을, 다시 모아야 할 필요가 있소. 이 사건에는, 버린더 아가씨의 생일 저녁에 그 집에서 일어난 모든 것에 대한 나의 기억을 되살리는 것을 바람직하게 만드는 특이한 점들이 있소. 그리고 나는 감히, 그 자리에 있었던 그녀의 고인이 된 어머니의 벗들에게, 그들의 기억의 도움을 나에게 빌려주기를 호소하오——\"\n\n나의 설명하는 말들을 되뇌는 데에 그만큼 나아갔을 때, 나는 캔디 씨의 얼굴에서 그에 대한 나의 실험이 완전한 실패임을 또렷이 보고 별안간 멈추었다.\n\n그 자그마한 의사는 내가 말하는 내내 안절부절못하며 제 손끝을 만지작거리며 앉아 있었다. 그의 흐릿하고 물기 어린 눈은 보기에 아주 애처로운, 텅 비고 아쉬워하는 물음의 표정을 담고 나의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다. 그가 무엇을 생각하는지는 헤아릴 도리가 없었다. 또렷이 보이는 한 가지는, 내가 처음 두세 마디 뒤로, 그의 주의를 붙드는 데에 실패했다는 것이었다. 그를 제정신으로 불러들일 유일한 가망은 화제를 바꾸는 데에 있는 듯했다. 나는 대뜸 새 화제를 시도했다.\n\n\"무엇이 나를 프리징홀로 데려왔는지는,\" 하고 나는 명랑하게 말했다. \"그쯤 해 둡시다! 자, 캔디 씨, 이번엔 당신 차례요. 당신은 게이브리얼 베터리지를 통해 나에게 전갈을 보냈지요——\"\n\n그는 제 손가락 만지작거리기를 그치고, 별안간 환해졌다.\n\n\"그렇소! 그렇소! 그렇소!\" 하고 그가 간절히 외쳤다. \"바로 그거요! 내가 당신에게 전갈을 보냈소!\"\n\n\"그리고 베터리지가 그것을 편지로 어김없이 전했소,\" 하고 나는 말을 이었다. \"당신은 내가 다음에 당신 근방에 오면 나에게 할 말이 있었소. 자, 캔디 씨, 내가 여기 왔소!\"\n\n\"당신이 여기 왔군!\" 하고 그 의사가 되뇌었다. \"그리고 베터리지가 아주 옳았소. 나는 당신에게 할 말이 있었소. 그것이 나의 전갈이었소. 베터리지는 놀라운 사람이오. 그 무슨 기억력이오! 그 나이에, 그 무슨 기억력이오!\"\n\n그는 도로 침묵에 빠져, 다시 제 손가락을 만지작거리기 시작했다. 그 열병이 그의 기억에 미친 효과에 대해 베터리지에게서 들은 것을 떠올리며, 나는 그가 시작하는 것을 도울 수 있을까 하는 희망으로 대화를 이어 갔다.\n\n\"우리가 만난 지 오래되었소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"우리는 나의 가엾은 이모가 여태 열 마지막 생일 만찬에서 서로를 마지막으로 보았소.\"\n\n\"바로 그거요!\" 하고 캔디 씨가 부르짖었다. \"그 생일 만찬!\" 그는 충동적으로 벌떡 일어나, 나를 바라보았다. 깊은 홍조가 별안간 그의 바랜 얼굴에 번졌고, 그는, 마치 기꺼이 감추었을 약함을 드러냈음을 자각한 듯, 갑자기 다시 앉았다. 그가 제 자신의 기억의 결함을 알고 있다는 것, 그리고 그가 그것을 벗들의 눈에서 감추려 애쓰고 있다는 것이, 뚜렷했다, 가엾도록 뚜렷했다.\n\n여기까지 그는 오직 나의 연민에만 호소했다. 허나 그가 방금 한 말은—몇 마디 안 되었건만—대뜸 나의 호기심을 가장 높은 정점으로 일으켜 세웠다. 그 생일 만찬은 이미, 내가 희망과 불신의 이상하게 뒤섞인 느낌으로 되돌아보는, 과거의 그 유일한 사건이 되어 있었다. 그런데 여기 그 생일 만찬이, 캔디 씨가 나에게 할 중요한 말이 있는 그 화제임을 잘못 볼 여지 없이 선언하고 있었던 것이다!\n\n나는 다시 한 번 그를 거들려 했다. 허나, 이번에는, 나 자신의 이해가 나의 연민 어린 동기의 밑바탕에 있었고, 그것들이 나를, 내가 염두에 둔 그 끝으로, 조금 너무 갑작스레 재촉했다.\n\n\"이제 거의 한 해가 되었소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 그 즐거운 식탁에 앉은 지. 당신은 나에게 하고 싶었던 말을—당신의 일기에나, 아니면 달리—무슨 각서로 적어 두었소?\"\n\n캔디 씨는 그 제안을 이해했고, 그가 그것을 하나의 모욕으로 이해했음을 나에게 보였다.\n\n\"나는 아무 각서도 필요치 않소, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그가 자못 뻣뻣하게 말했다. \"나는 아직 그리 늙은이가 아니오—그리고 나의 기억은 (하느님께 감사하게도) 완전히 믿어도 좋소!\"\n\n그가 나에게 언짢아졌음을 내가 이해하기를 마다한 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다.\n\n\"나의 기억에 대해서도 그리 말할 수 있으면 좋으련만,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"내가 한 해 묵은 일들을 생각하려 하면, 나는 좀처럼 나의 기억이 내가 바랄 만큼 생생함을 보지 못하오. 이를테면 레이디 버린더의 그 만찬을 들어 봅시다——\"\n\n캔디 씨는, 그 말이 내 입술을 스치는 순간, 다시 환해졌다.\n\n\"아! 그 만찬, 레이디 버린더의 그 만찬!\" 하고 그가 여느 때보다 더 간절히 외쳤다. \"나는 그것에 대해 당신에게 할 말이 있소.\"\n\n그의 눈이 다시 그 애처로운 물음의 표정을 담고 나를 보았다, 그토록 아쉬워하고, 그토록 텅 비고, 그토록 비참하도록 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며. 그는 뚜렷이, 그 잃어버린 기억을 되찾으려 애쓰고 있었으나, 헛되이 애쓰고 있었다. \"그것은 아주 즐거운 만찬이었소,\" 하고 그가, 꼭 하고 싶은 말을 하는 척하는 태도로, 별안간 터뜨렸다. \"아주 즐거운 만찬이었소, 블레이크 씨, 그렇지 않소?\" 그는 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며, 가엾게도, 자기가 제 침착함을 알맞게 발휘함으로써 제 기억의 완전한 실패를 감추는 데에 성공했다고 여기는 듯했다.\n\n그것이 어찌나 괴로웠던지 나는—그가 그 잃어버린 기억을 되찾는 데에 그토록 깊이 관심을 두었건만—대뜸 이야기를 그 고장의 관심사로 옮겼다.\n\n여기서, 그는 자못 술술 나아갔다. 그 읍의 하찮은 자잘한 뒷공론과 다툼이, 그중 얼마쯤은 한 달이나 묵은 것이었건만, 어렵잖게 그의 기억에 되살아나는 듯했다. 그는, 지난 시절의 매끄러운 수다의 유창함을 얼마쯤 담고, 재잘거려 나갔다. 허나, 그의 수다스러움이 한창 흐를 때조차, 그가 별안간 망설이는—다시 한 번 그 눈에 텅 빈 물음을 담고 잠시 나를 보고—자신을 다잡고—다시 나아가는 순간들이 있었다. 나는 참을성 있게 나의 순교를 견뎠다 (온 세상에 마음이 열린 사람에게, 어느 시골 읍의 소식을 말없는 체념 속에 빨아들이는 것은, 정녕 순교나 다름없지 않은가?)—벽난로 위의 시계가 나의 방문이 반 시간을 넘어 늘어졌음을 나에게 일러 줄 때까지. 이제 그 희생을 온전한 것으로 여길 얼마쯤의 권리가 생겼으니, 나는 작별하려 일어섰다. 우리가 악수할 때, 캔디 씨는 제 발로 그 생일 잔치로 되돌아갔다.\n\n\"우리가 다시 만나 참으로 기쁘오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 그것을 마음에 두고 있었소—나는 정말이지 그것을 마음에 두고 있었소, 블레이크 씨, 당신에게 말하려고. 레이디 버린더의 그 만찬에 대해서 말이오, 아시겠소? 즐거운 만찬—정말이지 즐거운 만찬이었소, 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n그 말을 되뇌면서, 그는 제 기억의 실수를 내가 의심하는 것을 자기가 막았다는 것을, 처음 때만큼 확신하는 것 같지는 않았다. 그 아쉬워하는 표정이 다시 그의 얼굴을 흐렸다. 그리고, 겉보기에 나를 현관문까지 배웅하려 하다가, 그는 별안간 마음을 바꾸어, 하인을 부르는 종을 울리고, 그 응접실에 남았다.\n\n나는 그 의사의 계단을 천천히 내려왔다, 그가 정녕 내가 듣는 것이 나에게 아주 중요한 무언가를 말할 것이 있으나, 그가 도덕적으로 그것을 말할 능력이 없다는, 맥 빠지는 확신을 느끼면서. 그가 나에게 말하고 싶어 했음을 기억하는 그 애씀이, 너무나 뚜렷이, 그의 약해진 기억이 이제 이룰 수 있는 유일한 애씀이었다.\n\n내가 막 그 계단 밑에 다다라, 바깥 현관홀로 가는 길에 모퉁이를 돌았을 때, 그 집의 아래층 어딘가에서 문 하나가 사뿐히 열리고, 부드러운 목소리가 내 뒤에서 말했다—\n\n\"안됐지만, 선생, 당신은 캔디 씨가 서글피 변한 것을 보시겠지요?\"\n\n나는 돌아섰고, 에즈라 제닝스와 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다.\n\n# CHAPTER IX\n\n그 의사의 어여쁜 하녀가, 현관문을 손에 연 채, 나를 기다리며 서 있었다. 그 현관홀로 환히 쏟아져 드는 아침 빛이, 내가 돌아서서 그를 보았을 때, 캔디 씨의 조수의 얼굴에 가득 떨어졌다.\n\n에즈라 제닝스의 모습이, 세간의 관점에서 말하자면, 그에게 불리하다는 베터리지의 주장에는 반박할 도리가 없었다. 그의 집시 같은 낯빛, 그의 살 없는 두 뺨, 그의 앙상한 얼굴뼈, 그의 꿈꾸는 듯한 눈, 그의 유별난 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼, 그를 늙어 보이는 동시에 젊어 보이게 하는 그의 얼굴과 몸매 사이의 어리둥절한 모순—이 모든 것이 다소간, 낯선 이의 마음에 그에 대한 좋지 않은 인상을 자아내게끔 되어 있었다. 그럼에도—내가 정녕 이것을 느끼면서도—에즈라 제닝스가 나의 연민에 어떤 헤아릴 수 없는 호소를 했고, 내가 그것에 저항하기가 불가능했음은 부인할 수 없다. 세상에 대한 나의 앎이, 그가 던진 물음에 답하여, 내가 정녕 캔디 씨가 서글피 변한 것을 보았음을 인정하고, 그런 다음 그 집을 나서는 내 길을 가라고 나에게 일렀건만—에즈라 제닝스에 대한 나의 관심이 나를 그 자리에 붙박아 두어, 그가 뚜렷이 노리고 있던, 그의 고용주에 대해 나에게 사사로이 이야기할 기회를 그에게 주었다.\n\n\"내 길과 같은 길을 걸으시오, 제닝스 씨?\" 하고 나는, 그가 모자를 손에 든 것을 보고 말했다. \"나는 내 이모 에이블화이트 부인을 찾아가는 길이오.\"\n\n에즈라 제닝스는 자기가 볼 환자가 있으며, 자기가 내 길과 같은 길을 걷는다고 답했다.\n\n우리는 함께 그 집을 떠났다. 나는 그 어여쁜 하녀가—내가 나가는 길에 안녕을 빌었을 때는 온통 미소와 상냥함이었건만—입술을 오므린 채, 그의 얼굴을 보느니 차라리 어디든 딴 데를 보란 듯 보는 눈으로, 그가 언제쯤 돌아올지에 관련된 에즈라 제닝스의 조촐한 자그마한 전갈을 받는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그 가엾은 몹쓸 사람은 뚜렷이 그 집에서 아무에게도 사랑받지 못했다. 그 집 밖에서는, 그가 어디서든 인기가 없다는 베터리지의 말이 있었다. \"이 무슨 삶인가!\" 하고 나는, 우리가 그 의사의 현관 계단을 내려오면서, 속으로 생각했다.\n\n이미 제 편에서 캔디 씨의 병을 언급했으므로, 에즈라 제닝스는 이제 그 화제를 다시 꺼내는 것을 나에게 맡기기로 정한 듯했다. 그의 침묵은 뜻깊게 말하고 있었다, \"이제 당신 차례요.\" 나도, 그 의사의 병을 언급할 나 나름의 이유가 있었다. 그래서 나는 먼저 말할 책임을 기꺼이 받아들였다.\n\n\"그에게서 내가 보는 그 변화로 판단하건대,\" 하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"캔디 씨의 병은 내가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 더 심각했음이 틀림없소?\"\n\n\"그가 그것을 견뎌 내고 살아남은 것은,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"거의 기적이오.\"\n\n\"그의 기억은 오늘 내가 본 것보다 조금도 더 나아진 적이 없소? 그가 나에게 무언가 말하려 애쓰고 있었소——\"\n\n\"그가 병에 걸리기 전에 일어난 무언가에 대해서요?\" 하고 그 조수가, 내가 망설이는 것을 보고 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"그 지난 시절의 사건에 대한 그의 기억은, 가망 없이 약해졌소,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"가엾은 사람, 그것의 잔해라도 남아 있다는 것이 오히려 애석할 지경이오. 그는 자기가 세운 계획들—병에 걸리기 전에 이러저러하게 말하거나 해야 했던 것들을 어렴풋이 기억하면서도—그 계획이 무엇이었는지, 또는 그가 말하거나 해야 했던 그 일이 무엇이었는지를 떠올리는 것은 완전히 불가능하오. 그는 제 자신의 결함을 아프도록 자각하고, 당신도 보았겠지만, 그것을 남의 눈에서 감추려 아프도록 안달하오. 만일 그가 과거에 대한 온전한 망각의 상태로 회복될 수만 있었다면, 그는 더 행복한 사람이었을 거요. 어쩌면 우리 모두가 더 행복할지도 모르오,\" 하고 그는 서글픈 미소와 함께 덧붙였다. \"우리가 완전히 잊을 수만 있다면!\"\n\n\"모든 사람의 삶에는 정녕 어떤 사건들이 있지 않겠소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"그것의 기억을 온전히 잃기를 꺼릴 만한?\"\n\n\"그것은, 바라건대, 대부분의 사람에 대해 할 수 있는 말이겠지요, 블레이크 씨. 안됐지만 그것이 모든 사람에 대해 참되이 할 수 있는 말은 아닐 것이오. 캔디 씨가 되찾으려 애쓴 그 잃어버린 기억이—방금 당신이 그와 이야기하는 동안 말이오—그가 그것을 떠올리는 것이 당신에게 중요한 기억이었다고 여길 무슨 근거가 당신에게 있소?\"\n\n그 말을 하면서, 그는, 제 발로, 내가 그와 상의하고 싶어 하던 바로 그 점을 건드린 것이었다. 이 이상한 사람에게 내가 느낀 관심이, 나로 하여금, 우선, 그에게 나에게 이야기할 기회를 주도록 몰아붙였다—그의 고용주에 관해 내 편에서 할 말이 있으면, 그가 내가 그 섬세함과 신중함을 믿을 수 있는 사람임을 먼저 흡족히 여기기까지 미뤄 두면서 말이다. 그가 여태 한 그 얼마 안 되는 말이, 내가 신사와 이야기하고 있음을 나에게 확신시키기에 넉넉했다. 그에게는, 잉글랜드에서뿐 아니라 문명 세계의 다른 어디에서든, 좋은 가문의 확실한 표시인, 내가 감히 구하지 않은 침착함이라 이를 만한 것이 있었다. 그가 방금 나에게 던진 그 물음을 던짐에 있어 무슨 목적을 염두에 두었든, 나는 내가—여기까지는—감춤 없이 그에게 답하는 것이 정당하다고 조금도 의심하지 않았다.\n\n\"나는 내가 강한 관심을 지녔다고 믿소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"캔디 씨가 떠올리지 못한 그 잃어버린 기억을 좇는 데에. 내가 그의 기억을 도울 만한 무슨 방법을 당신이 나에게 일러 줄 수 있는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n에즈라 제닝스는, 그의 꿈꾸는 듯한 갈색 눈에 별안간 관심의 번뜩임을 담고, 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"캔디 씨의 기억은 도움의 손길이 닿을 수 없는 곳에 있소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 그의 회복 이래 그것을 자못 자주 도우려 애써 왔소, 그 점에 대해 단정적으로 말할 수 있을 만큼.\"\n\n이것이 나를 실망시켰다. 그리고 나는 그것을 털어놓았다.\n\n\"솔직히 당신은 나로 하여금 그것보다 덜 맥 빠지는 답을 바라게 했소,\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n에즈라 제닝스는 미소 지었다. \"그것이, 어쩌면, 최종의 답은 아닐지도 모르오, 블레이크 씨. 캔디 씨 자신에게 호소할 필요 없이, 캔디 씨의 잃어버린 기억을 좇는 것이 가능할지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"정말이오? 어떻게인지 묻는 것이, 내 편에서 무람없는 일이오?\"\n\n\"결코 아니오. 당신의 물음에 답하는 데에 나의 유일한 어려움은, 나 자신을 설명하는 어려움이오. 만일 내가 다시 한 번 캔디 씨의 병을 언급하되, 이번에는 어떤 전문적인 세세한 것들을 당신에게 아끼지 않고 말한다면, 당신의 참을성에 기대어도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"부디 계속하시오! 당신은 그 세세한 것들을 듣는 데에 이미 나의 관심을 끌었소.\"\n\n나의 간절함이 그를 재미있어하게—어쩌면, 차라리, 기뻐하게 하는 듯했다. 그는 다시 미소 지었다. 우리는 이때쯤 그 읍의 마지막 집들을 뒤로한 터였다. 에즈라 제닝스는 잠시 걸음을 멈추고, 길가의 산울타리에서 들꽃 몇 송이를 꺾었다. \"얼마나 아름다운가!\" 하고 그는, 그의 자그마한 꽃다발을 나에게 보이며, 소박하게 말했다. \"그리고 잉글랜드에서 얼마나 적은 사람들만이 이것들을 마땅한 만큼 감탄하는 것 같은가!\"\n\n\"당신이 늘 잉글랜드에 있었던 것은 아니오?\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"아니오. 나는 우리 식민지의 하나에서 태어나, 얼마쯤 거기서 자랐소. 내 아버지는 잉글랜드 사람이었소. 허나 내 어머니는—우리가 우리 화제에서 벗어나고 있소, 블레이크 씨. 그리고 그것은 나의 잘못이오. 실은, 나는 이 조촐하고 자그마한 산울타리 꽃들과 얽힌 추억이 있소—아무래도 좋소. 우리는 캔디 씨에 대해 이야기하던 참이었소. 캔디 씨에게로 돌아갑시다.\"\n\n이렇게 마지못해 그에게서 새어 나온, 그 자신에 대한 그 몇 마디를, 과거에 대한 온전한 망각 속에 인간 행복의 조건을 두게 한 그의 우울한 인생관과 이어 보고, 나는 그의 얼굴에서 내가 읽은 그 이야기가, 적어도 두 가지 점에서는, 정녕 그것이 말하는 이야기임을 흡족히 여겼다. 그는 좀처럼 없을 만큼 괴로움을 겪은 사람이었다. 그리고 그의 잉글랜드 피에는 어떤 이방 종족의 섞임이 있었다.\n\n\"당신은, 짐작건대, 캔디 씨의 병의 애초의 원인을 들었겠지요?\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"레이디 버린더의 만찬 밤은 비가 억수같이 쏟아지는 밤이었소. 나의 고용주는 그것을 뚫고 이륜마차로 집에 돌아와, 살갗까지 젖은 채로 그 집에 다다랐소. 그는 어느 환자에게서 온 급한 전갈이 자기를 기다리고 있음을 알았소. 그리고 그는 더없이 안타깝게도, 옷을 갈아입으러 멈추지 않고, 대뜸 그 병자를 찾아갔소. 나 자신은, 그날 밤, 프리징홀에서 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳의 어느 환자에게 볼일로 붙들려 있었소. 이튿날 아침 돌아오니, 나는 캔디 씨의 마부가 크게 놀라, 나를 제 주인의 방으로 데려가려 기다리고 있는 것을 보았소. 그때는 이미 화가 저질러진 뒤였소. 병이 시작되어 있었소.\"\n\n\"그 병은 나에게 그저, 두루뭉술하게, 열병으로만 이야기되었소,\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그 설명을 더 정확하게 만들 것을 아무것도 덧붙일 수 없소,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 대답했다. \"처음부터 끝까지 그 열병은 어떤 특정한 꼴도 띠지 않았소. 나는 대뜸 그 읍의 캔디 씨의 의사 벗 둘, 둘 다 내과의였는데, 그들에게 와서 그 병에 대한 그들의 견해를 달라고 사람을 보냈소. 그들은 그것이 심각해 보인다는 데에는 나와 뜻을 같이했소. 허나 그들은 둘 다, 그 치료에 대해 내가 취한 견해에는 강하게 반대했소. 우리는 그 환자의 맥에서 우리가 이끌어 낸 결론에서 완전히 갈렸소. 그 두 의사는, 그 박동의 빠름으로 논하여, 열을 내리는 치료가 채택할 유일한 치료라고 선언했소. 내 편에서는, 나는 그 맥의 빠름은 인정했으나, 나는 또한 그것의 놀라운 미약함을 몸의 지친 상태를 가리키는 것으로, 그리고 흥분제의 투여의 명백한 필요를 보여 주는 것으로 짚었소. 그 두 의사는 그를 죽과 레모네이드와 보리차 따위로 지키려 했소. 나는 그에게 샴페인이나 브랜디, 암모니아와 키니네를 주려 했소. 보다시피, 심각한 견해차였소! 그 고장의 확고한 명성을 지닌 두 내과의와, 그 집의 한낱 조수일 뿐인 낯선 이 사이의 차이였소. 처음 며칠 동안, 나는 나의 어른이자 윗사람에게 양보하는 것 말고는 도리가 없었소. 그동안 환자는 꾸준히 가라앉았소. 나는 그 명백한, 부인할 수 없이 명백한 맥의 증거에 호소하려 두 번째 시도를 했소. 그것의 빠름은 억눌리지 않았고, 그것의 미약함은 더해 있었소. 그 두 의사는 나의 고집에 언짢아했소. 그들은 말했소, '제닝스 씨, 우리가 이 병을 다루든지, 당신이 다루든지 하시오. 어느 쪽이오?' 나는 말했소, '선생들, 나에게 헤아릴 오 분을 주시오, 그러면 그 명백한 물음에 명백한 대답이 있을 것이오.' 그 시간이 다하자, 나는 내 대답을 준비하고 있었소. 나는 말했소, '당신들은 흥분제 치료를 해 보기를 단호히 거절하오?' 그들은 그야말로 거절했소. '나는 그것을 당장 해 보겠소, 선생들.'—'해 보시오, 제닝스 씨, 그러면 우리는 이 병에서 손을 떼겠소.' 나는 지하실로 샴페인 한 병을 가져오라고 보냈소. 그리고 나는 내 손으로 그것을 큰 잔의 반쯤 환자에게 투여했소. 그 두 내과의는 말없이 제 모자를 집어 들고, 그 집을 떠났소.\"\n\n\"당신은 심각한 책임을 떠맡았소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신 처지였다면, 안됐지만 나는 그것을 움츠려 피했을 것 같소.\"\n\n\"나의 처지였다면, 블레이크 씨, 당신은 캔디 씨가, 당신을 평생 그의 은인의 빚진 자로 만든 상황에서, 당신을 제 밑에 거두었음을 떠올렸을 것이오. 나의 처지였다면, 당신은 그가 시시각각 가라앉는 것을 보았을 것이오. 그리고 당신은, 이 땅에서 당신을 도운 유일한 사람이 당신의 눈앞에서 죽도록 두느니, 차라리 무엇이든 무릅썼을 것이오. 내가 나 자신을 놓아둔 그 끔찍한 처지에 대한 아무 감각도 없었다고 여기지 마시오! 나의 벗 없음의 온갖 비참을, 나의 그 끔찍한 책임의 온갖 위태로움을 느낀 순간들이 있었소. 만일 내가 행복한 사람이었다면, 만일 내가 번창한 삶을 살아왔다면, 나는 내가 나 자신에게 지운 그 과업 아래 가라앉았으리라 믿소. 허나 나에게는 되돌아볼 행복한 시절이 없었고, 나의 현재의 근심과 마음 졸임에 억지로 대비될 지난 마음의 평안이 없었소—그리고 나는 그 모든 것을 견디며 나의 결의를 굳게 지켰소. 나는 내 환자의 상태가 가장 좋은 한낮에, 내가 필요로 하는 안식을 위해 짬을 냈소. 남은 스물네 시간 동안은, 그의 목숨이 위태로운 한, 나는 결코 그의 잠자리를 떠나지 않았소. 해 질 녘에, 그런 경우에 흔히 그렇듯, 그 열병에 딸린 섬망이 왔소. 그것은 밤새 다소간 이어지다가, 이른 아침의 그 끔찍한 시각—두 시부터 다섯 시까지, 우리 중 가장 건강한 이의 생명력조차 가장 낮은 그때에—뜸해졌소. 죽음이 제 인간의 수확을 가장 넉넉히 거두어들이는 것이 바로 그때요. 죽음과 내가, 그 위에 누운 사람을 누가 차지할지, 그 침상을 두고 우리의 싸움을 벌인 것이 바로 그때요. 나는 내가 온갖 것을 건 그 치료를 밀고 나감에 있어 결코 망설이지 않았소. 포도주가 듣지 않으면, 나는 브랜디를 시도했소. 다른 흥분제가 그 힘을 잃으면, 나는 그 양을 두 배로 했소. 마음 졸임의 사이—다시는 결코 느끼지 않기를 하느님께 바라는 그런—뒤에, 그 맥의 빠름이 조금이나마, 허나 알아볼 만하게 줄어든 날이 왔소. 그리고, 더 좋게도, 그 박동에도 변화가 왔소—꾸준함과 힘으로의, 잘못 볼 여지 없는 변화가. 그때, 나는 내가 그를 구했음을 알았소. 그리고 그때 나는, 인정하거니와, 무너졌소. 나는 그 가엾은 사람의 여윈 손을 침상에 도로 뉘고, 울음을 터뜨렸소. 발작적인 안도였소, 블레이크 씨—그 이상 아무것도 아니었소! 생리학은 말하오, 그리고 참되이 말하오, 어떤 사람들은 여성의 체질을 타고난다고—그리고 나는 그중 하나요!\"\n\n그는 그의 눈물에 대해 그 씁쓸하게 전문적인 변명을, 내내 말해 온 대로, 조용하고 꾸밈없이 말했다. 그의 어조와 태도는, 처음부터 끝까지, 그가 각별히, 거의 병적으로, 나에게 관심의 대상으로 자신을 내세우지 않으려 안달함을 보여 주었다.\n\n\"당신은 정녕, 내가 어째서 당신을 이 온갖 세세한 것들로 지치게 했는지 물을 만하오,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"그것이, 블레이크 씨, 내가 다음으로 할 말을 당신에게 제대로 소개할, 내가 보는 유일한 길이오. 이제 당신은 캔디 씨의 병 무렵에 나의 처지가 정확히 어떠했는지 아니, 당신은, 이따금, 내 마음의 짐에 무슨 안도를 줌으로써 그것을 덜어야 할 그 절실한 필요를 더 어렵잖게 이해할 것이오. 나는 몇 해 전부터, 나의 여가를, 나의 직업의 사람들에게 부치는 책—뇌와 신경계라는 그 복잡하고 섬세한 주제에 대한 책을 쓰는 데에 쓰는 주제넘음을 부려 왔소. 나의 저작은 아마 결코 끝나지 않을 것이오. 그리고 그것은 확실히 결코 출판되지 않을 것이오. 그럼에도 그것은 숱한 외로운 시간의 벗이었소. 그리고 그것은 내가 캔디 씨의 잠자리에서 그 애타는 시간—기다림의, 그 밖의 아무것도 아닌 시간을 때우는 것을 도왔소. 내가 그가 섬망 상태였다고 당신에게 말한 것 같소? 그리고 내가 그의 섬망이 온 시각을 언급했지요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"자, 나는 그때, 나의 책에서 바로 이 섬망의 문제를 건드린 한 부분에 이른 참이었소. 그 주제에 대한 나의 이론으로 당신을 길게 성가시게 하지는 않겠소—나는 나 자신을, 오직 당신이 지금 알 관심이 있는 것만 당신에게 말하는 데에 국한하겠소. 나는 나의 의료 행위 가운데 자주, 섬망의 경우에—조리 있게 말하는 능력의 상실이 필연적으로 조리 있게 생각하는 능력의 상실도 뜻한다고, 우리가 정당하게 미루어 짐작할 수 있는지 의심하곤 했소. 가엾은 캔디 씨의 병이 이 의심을 시험대에 올릴 기회를 나에게 주었소. 나는 속기의 기술을 아오. 그리고 나는 그 환자의 '헛소리'를, 그것이 그의 입술에서 떨어지는 그대로 정확히 받아 적을 수 있었소.—보이오, 블레이크 씨, 내가 마침내 무엇에 이르고 있는지?\"\n\n나는 그것을 또렷이 보았고, 더 듣기를 숨죽인 관심으로 기다렸다.\n\n\"자투리 시간에,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말을 이었다. \"나는 나의 속기 기록을, 여느 글자의 꼴로 옮겼소—캔디 씨의 입술에서 조각조각 떨어진 그대로, 끊어진 어구들 사이에, 심지어 낱낱의 낱말들 사이에도, 큰 빈칸을 남겨 두면서. 그런 다음 나는 이렇게 얻은 결과를, 아이의 '조각 맞추기'를 맞출 때 쓰는 것과 비슷한 원리로 다루었소. 처음에는 죄 뒤죽박죽이오. 허나 당신이 옳은 길만 찾을 수 있다면, 그것은 죄 질서와 꼴로 이끌어질 수 있소. 이 방책에 따라, 나는 종이 위의 빈칸 하나하나를, 그 양편의 낱말이나 어구가 나에게 말하는 이의 뜻으로 넌지시 일러 준 것으로 채웠소. 나의 보탬이, 그것들 앞에 오는 말해진 낱말들에 자연스레 이어지고, 그것들 뒤에 오는 말해진 낱말들에 자연스레 들어맞기까지, 몇 번이고 고치면서. 그 결과는, 내가 이런 식으로 숱한 텅 비고 애타는 시간을 채웠을 뿐 아니라, 내가 (나에게 보이기로는) 내가 지닌 이론의 확증인 무언가에 이르렀다는 것이었소. 더 쉬운 말로 하면, 그 끊어진 문장들을 맞춘 뒤, 나는 내 환자의 마음속에서, 생각하는 그 우월한 능력이 다소간 조리 있게 이어지고 있는 것을 보았소, 표현하는 그 열등한 능력은 거의 완전한 무능과 혼란의 상태에 있는 동안에 말이오.\"\n\n\"한마디만!\" 하고 나는 간절히 끼어들었다. \"그의 헛소리 어딘가에 내 이름이 나왔소?\"\n\n\"들어 보시오, 블레이크 씨. 내가 방금 내놓은 그 주장의 나의 글로 된 증거들 가운데—또는, 내 주장을 증명하는 데로 향하는, 글로 된 실험들이라 해야겠소만—당신의 이름이 나오는 것이 하나 있소. 거의 온 하룻밤 동안, 캔디 씨의 마음은 그 자신과 당신 사이의 무언가에 사로잡혀 있었소. 나는 그 끊어진 낱말들을, 그것들이 그의 입술에서 떨어진 그대로, 한 장의 종이에 지니고 있소. 그리고 나는 그 낱말들을 이어 주는, 나 자신이 발견한 그 고리들을 다른 한 장의 종이에 지니고 있소. 그 곱셈의 결과는 (산술가들이라면 그리 말하겠지요) 알아들을 수 있는 진술이오—첫째로, 과거에 실제로 이루어진 무언가에 대한, 둘째로, 만일 그의 병이 길을 막아 그를 멈추게 하지 않았다면 캔디 씨가 미래에 하려고 궁리한 무언가에 대한. 문제는, 이것이, 당신이 오늘 아침 그를 찾아왔을 때 그가 헛되이 찾으려 애쓴 그 잃어버린 기억을 나타내느냐, 아니냐 하는 것이오.\"\n\n\"조금도 의심할 바 없소!\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"당장 돌아가, 그 종이들을 봅시다!\"\n\n\"아주 불가능하오, 블레이크 씨.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"잠시 내 처지에 서 보시오,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"당신이라면, 당신의 입을 여는 것을 정당화할 무슨 필요가 있는지를 먼저 알지도 못한 채, 당신의 앓는 환자이자 어쩔 도리 없는 벗의 입술에서 무심결에 떨어진 것을 다른 사람에게 털어놓겠소?\"\n\n나는 그가 여기서 반박할 여지가 없음을 느꼈다. 허나 나는 그럼에도 그 문제를 따져 보려 했다.\n\n\"당신이 말한 그런 섬세한 일에서의 나의 처신은,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"그 털어놓음이 나의 벗을 위태롭게 할 성질의 것인지 아닌지에 크게 달렸을 것이오.\"\n\n\"나는 그 문제의 그 면을 헤아릴 온갖 필요를, 오래전에 처리했소,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"나의 기록이 캔디 씨가 비밀로 지키고 싶어 했을 무언가를 담은 곳은 어디든, 그 기록은 없애 버렸소. 내 벗의 잠자리에서의 나의 원고 실험은, 이제, 그가 제 기억의 쓰임을 되찾았다면 남들에게 전하기를 망설였을 것을 아무것도 담고 있지 않소. 당신의 경우에는, 나의 기록이 그가 정녕 당신에게 말하고 싶어 했던 무언가를 담고 있다고 여길 온갖 근거가 나에게 있소.\"\n\n\"그런데도, 당신은 망설이오?\"\n\n\"그런데도, 나는 망설이오. 내가 지닌 그 정보를 얻은 상황을 기억하시오! 그것이 아무리 해롭지 않다 한들, 나는, 당신이 먼저 그리할 이유가 있음을 나에게 흡족히 보여 주지 않는 한, 그것을 당신에게 내줄 마음을 나 자신에게 내키게 할 수 없소. 그는 어찌나 비참하게 앓았던지, 블레이크 씨! 그리고 그는 어찌나 어쩔 도리 없이 나에게 의지했던지! 만일 내가 당신에게, 그 잃어버린 기억에 당신의 관심이 무엇인지—또는 당신이 그 잃어버린 기억을 무엇이라 믿는지 그저 넌지시 일러 주기만을 청한다면, 그것이 너무한 부탁이오?\"\n\n그의 말과 그의 태도가 둘 다 나에게 요구하는 그 솔직함으로 그에게 답하는 것은, 내가 그 다이아몬드의 절도로 의심받고 있음을 드러내 놓고 인정하는 데에 나 자신을 매어 두는 것이었으리라. 에즈라 제닝스가 내가 그에게 느낀 그 첫 충동적 관심을 아무리 강하게 북돋웠어도, 그는 내가 놓여 있는 그 욕된 처지를 털어놓기를 꺼리는 나의 이겨 낼 수 없는 마음을 이겨 내지는 못했다. 나는 다시 한 번, 낯선 이들의 호기심을 맞으려 나 자신을 채비시킨 그 설명하는 말들에 몸을 숨겼다.\n\n이번에는, 나는 내가 말을 건네는 그 사람 편에서 주의가 모자라다고 불평할 이유가 없었다. 에즈라 제닝스는, 내가 마칠 때까지, 참을성 있게, 애타게까지 들었다.\n\n\"당신의 기대를 부풀려 놓고, 그것을 실망시키기만 하게 되어 안됐소, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"캔디 씨의 병의 온 기간 내내, 처음부터 끝까지, 그 다이아몬드에 대해서는 한마디도 그의 입술에서 새어 나오지 않았소. 내가 당신의 이름과 이어지는 것을 들은 그 일은, 장담하건대, 버린더 아가씨의 보석의 분실이나 되찾음과는 조금도 알아볼 만한 관련이 없소.\"\n\n우리는, 그가 그 말을 하면서, 우리가 걸어오던 그 큰길이 두 갈래 길로 갈라지는 곳에 다다랐다. 하나는 에이블화이트 씨의 집으로 이어졌고, 다른 하나는 두세 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 황야의 마을로 이어졌다. 에즈라 제닝스는 그 마을로 이어지는 길에서 멈추었다.\n\n\"나의 길은 이 방향에 있소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 정말이지 참으로 안됐소, 블레이크 씨, 내가 당신에게 아무 쓸모도 될 수 없어서.\"\n\n그의 목소리는 나에게 그가 진심으로 말함을 일러 주었다. 그의 부드러운 갈색 눈이 우울한 관심의 눈길로 잠시 나에게 머물렀다. 그는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 한마디 없이, 그 마을로 가는 제 길을 갔다.\n\n일 분 남짓, 나는 서서 그가 나에게서 점점 더 멀리 걸어가는 것을 지켜보았다. 내가 이제 굳게 찾고 있는 그 실마리라 믿는 것을 그와 함께 점점 더 멀리 가져가는 것을. 그는 얼마쯤 걸어가고 나서, 돌아서, 뒤돌아보았다. 내가 여전히 우리가 헤어진 그 자리에 서 있는 것을 보고, 그는, 마치 내가 그와 다시 이야기하고 싶어 하는 것은 아닌가 미심쩍어하듯, 걸음을 멈추었다. 나 자신의 처지를 따져 볼—내 생애의 전환점일지도 모를 때에 내가 나의 기회를 잃고 있으며, 그것이 죄 나 자신의 자존심보다 조금도 더 중요할 것 없는 것을 만족시키려는 것임을 나 자신에게 일깨울 짬은 없었다! 먼저 그를 도로 부르고, 나중에 생각할 짬밖에 없었다. 나는 살아 있는 사람 가운데 가장 경솔한 이의 하나인 것 같다. 나는 그를 도로 불렀다—그러고는 나 자신에게 말했다, \"이제 어쩔 도리가 없다. 나는 그에게 진실을 말해야 한다!\"\n\n그는 대뜸 제 걸음을 되짚어 왔다. 나는 그를 맞으러 그 길을 따라 나아갔다.\n\n\"제닝스 씨,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 당신을 아주 공정하게 대하지 않았소. 캔디 씨의 잃어버린 기억을 좇는 데에 대한 나의 관심은 그 월장석을 되찾는 관심이 아니오. 심각한 개인적 일이 나의 요크셔 방문의 밑바탕에 있소. 이 일에서 당신에게 솔직히 대하지 않은 데에 나는 오직 한 가지 변명거리가 있소. 나의 처지가 정말로 어떠한지를 누구에게든 말하는 것이, 나에게는 이루 말할 수 없이 괴롭소.\"\n\n에즈라 제닝스는, 내가 여태 그에게서 본 처음의 당혹한 기색을 띠고, 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"나에게는 아무 권리도 없고, 아무 바람도 없소, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 사사로운 일에 나를 밀어 넣을. 내 편에서, (더없이 무심결에) 당신을 괴로운 시험에 들게 한 데에 대해, 당신의 용서를 구하도록 허락해 주시오.\"\n\n\"당신에게는 온전한 권리가 있소,\" 하고 나는 되받았다. \"캔디 씨의 잠자리에서 들은 것을 드러내는 것이 정당하다고 느낄 조건을 정할. 나는 이 일에서 당신을 움직이는 그 섬세함을 이해하고 존중하오. 내가 당신을 나의 믿음 안에 들이기를 마다한다면, 어찌 내가 당신의 믿음 안에 받아들여지기를 바라겠소? 당신은 마땅히 알아야 하고, 알게 될 것이오, 어째서 내가 캔디 씨가 나에게 말하고 싶어 한 것을 알아내는 데에 관심을 두는지. 만일 내가 나의 예상에서 틀린 것으로 드러나고, 만일 당신이 내가 원하는 것을 정말로 알게 되었을 때 나를 도울 수 없는 것으로 드러난다면, 나는 나의 비밀을 지켜 줄 당신의 명예를 믿겠소—그리고 무언가가 나에게, 내가 헛되이 믿는 것이 아니리라고 일러 주오.\"\n\n\"멈추시오, 블레이크 씨. 나는 당신이 조금이라도 더 나아가기 전에 해야만 할 말이 있소.\"\n\n나는 놀라움에 그를 바라보았다. 어떤 끔찍한 감정의 움켜쥠이 그를 사로잡아, 그를 넋까지 뒤흔들어 놓은 듯했다. 그의 집시 같은 낯빛은 검푸르뎅뎅한 잿빛 창백함으로 변해 있었다. 그의 눈은 별안간 사납고 번들거리게 되었다. 그의 목소리는 어떤 어조로—낮고, 준엄하고, 단호한, 내가 이제 처음으로 듣는 어조로 떨어졌다. 그 사람 안의 잠재된 힘이, 선을 향한 것인지 악을 향한 것인지—그 순간에는 어느 쪽인지 말하기 어려웠다—그의 안에서 솟구쳐, 한 줄기 섬광의 느닷없음으로 나에게 제 모습을 드러냈다.\n\n\"당신이 나에게 조금이라도 믿음을 두기 전에,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 마땅히 알아야 하고, 알아야만 하오, 내가 어떤 상황에서 캔디 씨의 집에 거두어졌는지. 오래 걸리지 않을 것이오. 나는, 선생, (흔히 하는 말로) 어떤 사람에게든 나의 이야기를 하겠다고 짐짓 나서지 않소. 나의 이야기는 나와 함께 죽을 것이오. 내가 청하는 것은 다만, 내가 캔디 씨에게 말한 것을 당신에게 말하도록 허락받는 것뿐이오. 만일 당신이, 그것을 듣고 나서도, 당신이 하겠다고 한 말을 할 마음이면, 당신은 나의 주의를 부리고 나의 도움을 부릴 것이오. 걸으면서 이야기할까요?\"\n\n그의 얼굴의 억눌린 비참함이 나를 잠잠하게 했다. 나는 하나의 몸짓으로 그의 물음에 답했다. 우리는 걸어 나아갔다.\n\n몇백 야드 나아가고서, 에즈라 제닝스는, 이 부분에서 그 황야를 길과 갈라놓는 거친 돌담의 터진 곳에서 멈추었다.\n\n\"조금 쉬어도 괜찮겠소, 블레이크 씨?\" 하고 그가 물었다. \"나는 예전의 내가 아니오—그리고 어떤 것들은 나를 뒤흔드오.\"\n\n나는, 물론, 그러자고 했다. 그는 그 터진 곳을 지나, 길과 가장 가까운 쪽으로는 덤불과 왜소한 나무들에 가려지고, 맞은편 방향으로는 그 황야의 드넓은 갈색 벌판 너머로 장엄하도록 황량한 경치가 내다보이는, 그 히스 우거진 땅의 잔디밭 한 뙈기로 앞장서 갔다. 지난 반 시간 사이에 구름이 몰려든 터였다. 빛은 흐렸다. 먼 곳은 어슴푸레했다. 자연의 그 사랑스러운 얼굴이, 부드럽고 여전히 빛깔 없이, 우리를 맞았다—미소 없이 우리를 맞았다.\n\n우리는 말없이 앉았다. 에즈라 제닝스는 제 모자를 벗어 놓고, 지친 듯 제 이마를, 지친 듯 제 놀라운 흑백의 머리칼을 손으로 쓸었다. 그는, 마치 그것이 되살리는 추억이 이제 그를 아프게 하는 추억이기라도 한 양, 그의 자그마한 들꽃 꽃다발을 제게서 던져 버렸다.\n\n\"블레이크 씨!\" 하고 그가 별안간 말했다. \"당신은 나쁜 벗과 함께 있소. 어떤 끔찍한 혐의의 먹구름이 여러 해 동안 나에게 얹혀 있었소. 나는 대뜸 최악을 당신에게 말하오. 나는 삶이 잔해이고, 명예가 사라진 사람이오.\"\n\n나는 말하려 했다. 그가 나를 멈추게 했다.\n\n\"아니오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"용서하시오. 아직은. 당신이 나중에 도로 거두고 싶어 할지 모를 동정의 표현에 당신 자신을 매어 두지 마시오. 나는 여러 해 동안 나에게 얹혀 있는 혐의를 언급했소. 그것과 이어진, 나에게 불리하게 말하는 상황들이 있소. 나는 그 혐의가 무엇인지 인정할 마음을 나 자신에게 낼 수 없소. 그리고 나는 나의 결백을 증명할 능력이 없소, 완전히 없소. 나는 다만 나의 결백을 주장할 수 있을 뿐이오. 나는 그것을, 선생, 그리스도인으로서, 나의 맹세를 걸고 주장하오. 사내로서의 나의 명예에 호소하는 것은 헛일이오.\"\n\n그는 다시 멈추었다. 나는 그를 돌아보았다. 그는 답으로 결코 나를 보지 않았다. 그의 온 존재가, 떠올리는 고통에, 그리고 말하려는 애씀에 온통 사로잡힌 듯했다.\n\n\"내가 말할 수 있는 것이 많소,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"나 자신의 가족의 나에 대한 인정사정없는 처사와, 내가 그 희생물로 떨어진 그 인정사정없는 적의에 대해. 허나 해는 저질러졌소. 그 잘못은 온갖 바로잡음의 너머에 있소. 나는, 할 수만 있다면, 당신을 지치게 하거나 괴롭히기를 마다하오, 선생. 이 나라에서 나의 이력의 첫머리에, 내가 언급한 그 비열한 중상이 나를 대뜸, 그리고 영영 쓰러뜨렸소. 나는 나의 직업에서의 나의 포부를 버렸소—무명이 나에게 남은 유일한 희망이었소. 나는 내가 사랑하는 여인과 헤어졌소—어찌 내가 그녀에게 나의 치욕을 함께 나누라고 선고할 수 있었겠소? 잉글랜드의 어느 외진 구석에, 의사 조수의 자리 하나가 났소. 나는 그 자리를 얻었소. 그것은 나에게 평안을 약속했소. 그것은, 내 생각으로는, 나에게 무명을 약속했소. 나는 틀렸소. 나쁜 소문은, 시간과 우연이 그것을 도우면, 참을성 있게, 그리고 멀리 나아가오. 내가 달아난 그 혐의가 나를 뒤따라왔소. 나는 그것이 다가옴을 경고받았소. 나는 내가 얻은 추천장과 함께, 나의 자리를 제 발로 떠날 수 있었소. 그것들이 다른 외진 지역의 다른 자리를 나에게 구해 주었소. 다시 세월이 흘렀소. 그리고 다시 나의 명예에 죽음인 그 중상이 나를 찾아냈소. 이번에는 나에게 아무 경고가 없었소. 나의 고용주가 말했소, '제닝스 씨, 나는 당신에게 아무 불만이 없소. 허나 당신은 당신의 결백을 밝히든지, 나를 떠나든지 해야 하오.' 나에게는 오직 한 가지 선택뿐이었소—나는 그를 떠났소. 그 뒤에 내가 겪은 것을 곱씹어 봐야 헛일이오. 나는 이제 겨우 마흔 살이오. 내 얼굴을 보시오, 그리고 그것이 나를 대신해 어떤 비참한 세월의 이야기를 말하게 두시오. 그것은 내가 이곳으로 흘러들어, 캔디 씨와 만나는 것으로 끝났소. 그는 조수를 원했소. 나는 그를, 능력의 문제에 대해서는, 나의 지난 고용주에게 알아보게 했소. 명예의 문제가 남았소. 나는 그에게 내가 당신에게 말한 것을—그리고 그 이상을 말했소. 나는 그에게, 그가 나를 믿더라도, 길에 어려움이 있음을 경고했소. '여기서도, 다른 데서처럼,' 하고 나는 말했소, '나는 가명으로 사는 그 죄지은 회피를 경멸하오. 나는 프리징홀에서도, 어디를 가든 나를 뒤따르는 그 먹구름으로부터, 다른 곳에서보다 조금도 더 안전하지 않소.' 그가 대답했소, '나는 일을 어중간하게 하지 않소—나는 당신을 믿고, 당신을 가엾이 여기오. 만일 당신이 무슨 일이 일어날지를 무릅쓰겠다면, 나도 그것을 무릅쓰겠소.' 전능하신 하느님이 그를 축복하시기를! 그는 나에게 비호를 주었고, 나에게 일자리를 주었고, 나에게 마음의 안식을 주었소—그리고 나는 이제 (지난 몇 달 동안 지녀 온) 확실한 확신이 있소, 그가 그것을 후회하게 만들 아무 일도 이제 일어나지 않으리라는.\"\n\n\"그 중상은 사그라졌소?\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"그 중상은 여느 때와 다름없이 살아 있소. 허나 그것이 여기로 나를 뒤따라올 때에는, 너무 늦게 올 것이오.\"\n\n\"당신이 그 자리를 떠나 있을 테니?\"\n\n\"아니오, 블레이크 씨—나는 죽어 있을 것이오. 지난 십 년 동안 나는 고칠 수 없는 몸속의 병으로 앓아 왔소. 나는 당신에게 숨기지 않소, 나의 삶을 아직 나에게 얼마쯤 중요하게 만드는, 삶에 대한 한 가지 마지막 관심이 아니었다면, 나는 그것의 고통이 오래전에 나를 죽이도록 두었으리라는 것을. 나는 한 사람을—나에게 아주 소중한, 내가 다시는 보지 못할 사람을 돌보아 주고 싶소. 나 자신의 자그마한 유산은 그녀를 세상으로부터 독립하게 하기에 좀처럼 넉넉지 않소. 내가 오래 살 수만 있다면 그것을 어떤 액수까지 불릴 그 희망이, 나로 하여금 내가 궁리할 수 있는 완화 수단으로 그 병에 맞서게 몰아붙였소. 나의 경우에 그 하나의 효과 있는 완화제는—아편이오. 그 전능하고 자비로운 약에, 나는 나의 사형 선고로부터 여러 해의 유예를 빚졌소. 허나 아편의 미덕조차 그 한계가 있소. 그 병의 진행이 나를 점점, 아편의 쓰임에서 그것의 남용으로 억지로 몰아넣었소. 나는 마침내 그 벌을 받고 있소. 나의 신경계는 산산조각 났소. 나의 밤은 공포의 밤이오. 이제 그 끝이 멀지 않소. 그것이 오게 두시오—나는 헛되이 살고 일한 것이 아니오. 그 자그마한 액수는 거의 마련되었소. 그리고 나에게는, 나의 마지막 생명의 밑천이 내가 예상하는 것보다 더 빨리 나를 저버리더라도, 그것을 채울 수단이 있소. 나는 내가 어쩌다 이것을 당신에게 말하는 데로 헤매어 들었는지 좀처럼 모르겠소. 나는 내가 당신의 연민에 호소할 만큼 비열하다고는 생각지 않소. 어쩌면, 내가 당신에게 한 말이, 내가 죽어 가는 사람임을 내 안에 확실히 알고서 한 말임을 당신이 안다면, 당신은 그만큼 더 어렵잖게 나를 믿어 줄지도 모른다고 나는 짐작하오. 숨길 것도 없소, 블레이크 씨, 당신이 나의 관심을 끈다는 것을. 나는 나의 가엾은 벗의 기억 상실을, 당신과의 사귐을 나아지게 하는 수단으로 삼으려 했소. 나는 당신이 그가 말하고 싶어 한 것에 대해 지나가는 호기심을 느끼고, 내가 그것을 만족시킬 수 있을 요행을 노렸소. 내가 나 자신을 당신에게 밀어 넣은 데에 아무 변명거리가 없소? 어쩌면 얼마쯤 변명거리가 있소. 나처럼 살아온 사람은 인간의 운명을 곰곰이 헤아릴 때 쓰라린 순간들이 있소. 당신에게는 젊음, 건강, 부, 세상에서의 자리, 앞에 놓인 전망이 있소. 당신과, 당신 같은 이들이, 나에게 인간 삶의 볕 드는 쪽을 보여 주고, 내가 떠나기 전에 내가 떠나는 그 세상과 나를 화해시켜 주오. 우리 사이의 이 이야기가 어떻게 끝나든, 나는 당신이 그리함으로써 나에게 친절을 베풀었음을 잊지 않을 것이오. 이제 당신에게 달렸소, 선생, 당신이 하겠다고 한 말을 하든지, 아니면 나에게 안녕을 고하든지.\"\n\n나는 그 호소에 할 대답이 오직 하나뿐이었다. 한순간의 망설임도 없이 나는 그에게 진실을, 내가 이 쪽들에서 그것을 말해 온 것만큼 감춤 없이 말했다.\n\n그는 내가 나의 이야기의 그 핵심 사건에 다가서자, 벌떡 일어나, 숨죽인 간절함으로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내가 그 방에 들어간 것은 확실하오,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"내가 그 다이아몬드를 가져간 것은 확실하오. 나는 그 두 명백한 사실을, 내가 무엇을 했든, 나는 그것을 나 자신도 모르게 했다고 선언함으로써 맞설 수밖에 없소——\"\n\n에즈라 제닝스가 흥분하여 내 팔을 붙들었다.\n\n\"멈추시오!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은 당신이 짐작하는 것보다 더 많은 것을 나에게 넌지시 일러 주었소. 당신은 여태 아편의 쓰임에 익숙했던 적이 있소?\"\n\n\"나는 평생 그것을 맛본 적이 없소.\"\n\n\"이때쯤 지난해에 당신의 신경이 어긋나 있었소? 당신은 유달리 안절부절못하고 짜증스러웠소?\"\n\n\"그랬소.\"\n\n\"당신은 잠을 잘 못 잤소?\"\n\n\"형편없었소. 숱한 밤 나는 아예 잠들지 못했소.\"\n\n\"그 생일 밤은 예외였소? 애써, 기억해 보시오. 당신은 그 한 번은 잘 잤소?\"\n\n\"정녕 기억하오! 나는 곤히 잤소.\"\n\n그는 내 팔을 잡았던 것만큼 별안간 놓았다—그리고 제 마음에 얹혀 있던 마지막 의심을 던 사람의 태도로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"이것은 당신의 삶에서, 그리고 나의 삶에서 특기할 날이오,\" 하고 그가 무겁게 말했다. \"나는 한 가지를 절대적으로 확신하오, 블레이크 씨—나는 캔디 씨가 오늘 아침 당신에게 말하고 싶어 한 것을, 내 환자의 잠자리에서 받아 적은 그 기록 속에 지니고 있소. 잠깐! 그것이 전부가 아니오. 나는, 당신이 그 방에 들어가 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔을 때 당신이 당신 자신이 무엇을 하는지 자각하지 못하고 있었음을 내가 증명할 수 있다고 굳게 믿소. 나에게 생각할 시간과, 당신에게 물을 시간을 주시오. 나는 당신의 결백을 밝히는 것이 내 손안에 있다고 믿소!\"\n\n\"제발, 당신 자신을 설명하시오! 무슨 뜻이오?\"\n\n우리의 이야기의 흥분 속에서, 우리는 그때껏 우리를 남의 눈에서 가려 주던 그 왜소한 나무들의 무리 너머로, 몇 걸음 걸어 나온 터였다. 에즈라 제닝스가 나에게 답하기도 전에, 그는 그 큰길에서, 뚜렷이 그를 지켜보고 있던 한 사내에 의해, 크게 안달하며 불렸다.\n\n\"가오,\" 하고 그가 되받아 소리쳤다. \"되도록 빨리 가오!\" 그는 나에게로 돌아섰다. \"저기 저 마을에 나를 기다리는 급한 환자가 있소. 나는 반 시간 전에 거기 있어야 했소—나는 당장 그것을 돌봐야 하오. 지금부터 두 시간을 나에게 주고, 캔디 씨에게 다시 들르시오—그러면 나는 당신을 위해 채비하고 있을 것을 약속하오.\"\n\n\"내가 어찌 기다린단 말이오!\" 하고 나는 조바심을 내며 외쳤다. \"우리가 헤어지기 전에 한마디 설명으로 내 마음을 잠재워 줄 수 없소?\"\n\n\"이것은 서둘러 설명하기에는 너무나 심각한 일이오, 블레이크 씨. 나는 일부러 당신의 참을성을 시험하는 것이 아니오—사정이 지금 이러하니, 내가 당신의 마음 졸임을 덜려 한다면 나는 그것을 더할 뿐일 것이오. 프리징홀에서, 선생, 두 시간 뒤에!\"\n\n그 큰길의 사내가 그를 다시 불렀다. 그는 서둘러 가 버리고, 나를 남겼다.\n\n# CHAPTER X\n\n내가 이제 처해진 그 마음 졸임의 사이가, 나의 처지에 놓인 다른 사람들에게 어찌 영향을 미쳤을지, 나는 짐짓 말할 수 없다. 그 두 시간의 유예가 나의 기질에 미친 영향은 그저 이러했다. 나는, 에즈라 제닝스가 나에게 할 말을 죄 먼저 듣기까지는, 어느 한 자리에 가만히 있는 것이 몸으로 불가능함을, 그리고 어떤 한 인간에게든 말하는 것이 마음으로 불가능함을 느꼈다.\n\n이런 심사로, 나는 궁리했던 에이블화이트 부인에 대한 방문을 버렸을 뿐 아니라—심지어 게이브리얼 베터리지 자신과 마주치는 것조차 움츠려 피했다.\n\n프리징홀로 돌아와, 나는 베터리지에게 쪽지 하나를 남겨, 내가 뜻밖에 몇 시간 불려 나갔으나, 오후 세 시경에는 틀림없이 내가 돌아오기를 기대해도 좋다고 일렀다. 나는 그에게, 그 사이에, 여느 때에 저녁을 시키고, 마음껏 즐기라고 청했다. 그에게는, 내가 잘 알거니와, 프리징홀에 벗들이 숱했다. 그리고 그는 내가 그 호텔로 돌아올 때까지 제 시간을 채우는 데에 조금도 막힘이 없을 터였다.\n\n이것을 마치고, 나는 다시 서둘러 그 읍을 나서서, 나의 시계가 마침내 캔디 씨의 집으로 돌아갈 때임을 나에게 일러 줄 때까지, 프리징홀을 둘러싼 그 외로운 황야의 지방을 헤맸다.\n\n나는 에즈라 제닝스가 채비를 갖추고 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n그는, 유리를 낀 문으로 진료실과 통하는, 휑뎅그렁한 자그마한 방에 홀로 앉아 있었다. 흉측한 병들이 앗아 간 참상을 그린 흉측한 채색 도해들이 그 삭막한 담황색 벽을 장식하고 있었다. 칙칙한 의학서들로 가득 차고, 그 위쪽에 여느 흉상 대신 두개골로 꾸며진 책장 하나, 잉크가 흥건히 튄 큰 전나무 탁자 하나, 부엌과 오두막에서 보이는 부류의 나무 의자들, 그 방바닥 한가운데의 올이 다 드러난 융단 한 장, 대야와 배수관이 벽에 거칠게 박혀, 그것이 외과 수술과 이어져 있음을 끔찍하게 넌지시 일러 주는 물이 든 개수대 하나—가 그 방의 온 세간을 이루었다. 벌들이 그 창밖 화분에 놓인 몇 송이 꽃 사이에서 붕붕거렸다. 새들이 그 정원에서 노래했다. 그리고 어느 이웃집의 가락 없는 피아노의 희미하고 이따금씩의 짤랑거림이 이따금 귀에 억지로 밀려들었다. 여느 다른 곳에서라면, 이 나날의 소리들이 바깥의 나날의 세상을 즐겁게 이야기했을지도 모른다. 여기서, 그것들은, 오직 인간의 괴로움만이 어지럽힐 특권을 가진 그 고요에 침입자로 들어왔다. 나는, 그 책장에 제 나름의 자리를 차지한 그 마호가니 기구 상자와 그 커다란 붕대 두루마리를 보았고, 에즈라 제닝스의 방의 나날의 쓰임에 익숙하고 걸맞은 그 소리들을 생각하며 속으로 몸서리쳤다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 맞는 이 자리에 대해, 블레이크 씨, 나는 아무 변명도 하지 않겠소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이것이 그 집에서, 하루의 이 시각에, 우리가 방해받지 않고 있을 것을 아주 확신할 수 있는 유일한 방이오. 여기 당신을 위해 채비된 나의 종이들이 있소. 그리고 여기 우리가 마치기 전에 참조할 계제가 있을지도 모를 책 두 권이 있소. 당신의 의자를 탁자로 가져오시오, 그러면 우리가 함께 그것들을 참조할 수 있을 것이오.\"\n\n나는 탁자로 다가갔다. 그리고 에즈라 제닝스는 나에게 그의 원고 기록을 건넸다. 그것들은 두 장의 큰 이절판 종이로 이루어져 있었다. 한 장은 오직 이따금씩만 표면을 덮은 글씨를 담고 있었다. 다른 한 장은, 붉은 잉크와 검은 잉크로 된, 위에서 아래까지 그 쪽을 완전히 채운 글씨를 내보였다. 그 순간, 나의 호기심의 애태워진 상태에서, 나는 절망하여 그 둘째 장을 옆으로 밀어 놓았다.\n\n\"나에게 좀 자비를 베푸시오!\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"내가 이것을 읽으려 하기 전에, 내가 무엇을 예상해야 할지 나에게 말해 주시오.\"\n\n\"기꺼이, 블레이크 씨! 내가 당신에게 한두 가지 물음을 더 물어도 괜찮겠소?\"\n\n\"당신이 원하는 것은 무엇이든 나에게 물으시오!\"\n\n그는 입술에 그 서글픈 미소를, 부드러운 갈색 눈에 다정한 관심을 담고 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"당신은 이미 나에게 말했소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 여태껏—당신이 아는 한—평생 아편을 맛본 적이 없다고.\"\n\n\"내가 아는 한,\" 하고 나는 되뇌었다.\n\n\"당신은 내가 어째서 그 유보를 달아 말하는지 곧 이해할 것이오. 계속합시다. 당신은 아편을 먹은 적이 있음을 자각하지 못하오. 이때쯤, 지난해에, 당신은 신경의 짜증으로 괴로워했고, 밤에 잠을 형편없이 잤소. 허나 그 생일 밤에는, 그 상례에 예외가 있었소—당신은 곤히 잤소. 여기까지 내가 옳소?\"\n\n\"아주 옳소!\"\n\n\"그 신경의 괴로움과, 당신의 잠 못 이룸에 대해 무슨 원인이든 댈 수 있소?\"\n\n\"나는 아무 원인도 댈 수 없소. 늙은 베터리지가 그 원인을 짐작해 본 것이, 기억나오. 허나 그것은 좀처럼 언급할 값어치가 없소.\"\n\n\"용서하시오. 이런 경우에는 무엇이든 언급할 값어치가 있소. 베터리지가 당신의 잠 못 이룸을 무언가의 탓으로 돌렸소. 무엇의?\"\n\n\"내가 담배를 끊은 것의.\"\n\n\"당신은 습관적인 흡연가였소?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"당신은 그 버릇을 별안간 끊었소?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"베터리지가 완전히 옳았소, 블레이크 씨. 흡연이 버릇일 때, 그것을 별안간 끊고도 제 신경계에 얼마쯤의 일시적 손상이 없을 수 있는 사람은 여느 체질이 아님이 틀림없소. 당신의 잠 못 이룬 밤은, 내 생각으로는, 설명되오. 나의 다음 물음은 캔디 씨에 관한 것이오. 당신은 그와—그 생일 만찬에서든, 그 뒤든—그의 직업이라는 주제로 다툼 같은 것을 벌인 것을 기억하오?\"\n\n그 물음은 대뜸 그 생일 잔치와 이어진 나의 잠든 기억 하나를 일깨웠다. 그 자리에서 캔디 씨와 나 사이에 일어난 그 어리석은 언쟁은, 베터리지 이야기의 열째 장에 그것이 마땅한 것보다 훨씬 더 길게 그려진 것으로 나올 것이다. 거기 내놓인 그 다툼의 세세한 것들은—그 뒤에 내가 그것을 어찌나 조금밖에 생각하지 않았던지—나의 기억에 온전히 되살아나지 않았다. 이제 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것, 그리고 내가 에즈라 제닝스에게 말할 수 있는 온갖 것은, 내가 그 만찬 식탁에서, 캔디 씨조차 그 순간 발끈하게 할 만큼의 경솔함과 끈질김으로 의술을 공격했다는 것이었다. 나는 또한, 레이디 버린더가 그 다툼을 멈추려 끼어들었다는 것과, 그 자그마한 의사와 내가, 아이들 말로 하듯, \"다시 화해\"하여, 그날 밤 우리가 악수하기 전에 여느 때와 다름없이 좋은 벗이 되었다는 것을 기억했다.\n\n\"한 가지가 더 있소,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"내가 아는 것이 아주 중요한. 이때쯤 지난해에, 당신은 그 다이아몬드에 대해 어떤 각별한 걱정을 느낄 무슨 이유가 있었소?\"\n\n\"나는 그 다이아몬드에 대해 걱정을 느낄 더없이 강한 이유가 있었소. 나는 그것이 어떤 음모의 대상임을 알았소. 그리고 나는 그 돌의 소유자인 버린더 아가씨를 지킬 방책을 취하라고 경고받았소.\"\n\n\"그 다이아몬드의 안전이, 당신이 그 생일 밤에 잠자리에 들기 바로 전에, 당신과 다른 어떤 사람 사이의 대화의 주제였소?\"\n\n\"그것은 레이디 버린더와 그녀의 딸 사이의 대화의 주제였소——\"\n\n\"그것이 당신이 듣는 데에서 일어났소?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n에즈라 제닝스는 탁자에서 그의 기록을 집어 들어, 그것을 내 손에 쥐여 주었다.\n\n\"블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"만일 당신이 지금, 나의 물음과 당신의 대답이 그것들 위에 던진 그 빛으로, 그 기록을 읽는다면, 당신은 당신 자신에 관한 두 가지 아연할 발견을 하게 될 것이오. 당신은 알게 될 것이오—첫째로, 당신이 아편이 자아낸 무아지경의 상태로, 버린더 아가씨의 응접실에 들어가 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔음을. 둘째로, 그 아편이 캔디 씨에 의해 당신에게—당신 자신도 모르게—당신이 그 생일 만찬에서 그에게 내뱉은 견해에 대한 실질적인 반박으로 주어졌음을.\"\n\n나는 그 종이들을 손에 든 채 완전히 얼이 빠져 앉아 있었다.\n\n\"가엾은 캔디 씨를 부디 용서하시오,\" 하고 그 조수가 부드럽게 말했다. \"그가 끔찍한 화를 저지른 것은 나도 인정하오. 허나 그는 그것을 무심결에 저질렀소. 만일 당신이 그 기록을 본다면, 당신은 알 것이오—그의 병이 아니었다면—그가 그 잔치 이튿날 아침 레이디 버린더의 집으로 돌아가, 그가 당신에게 부린 그 장난을 인정했으리라는 것을. 버린더 아가씨가 그것을 들었을 것이고, 버린더 아가씨가 그를 캐물었을 것이고—한 해 동안 숨겨져 있던 그 진실이 하루 만에 밝혀졌을 것이오.\"\n\n나는 나의 침착함을 되찾기 시작했다. \"캔디 씨는 나의 원한이 닿을 수 없는 곳에 있소,\" 하고 나는 성이 나서 말했다. \"허나 그가 나에게 부린 그 장난은, 그럼에도, 하나의 배신 행위임에 조금도 다름이 없소. 나는 용서할지언정, 그것을 잊지는 않겠소.\"\n\n\"모든 의사가 제 의료 행위 가운데 그 배신 행위를 저지르오, 블레이크 씨. (잉글랜드에서) 아편에 대한 무지한 불신은 결코 하층의, 덜 교양 있는 계급에 국한되지 않소. 크게 개업한 모든 의사가, 이따금, 캔디 씨가 당신을 속인 것처럼, 제 환자를 속이지 않을 수 없음을 알게 되오. 나는 그 상황에서 당신에게 장난을 부린 그 어리석음을 두둔하지 않소. 나는 다만 당신에게 동기에 대한 더 정확하고 더 자비로운 해석을 청할 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"그것이 어떻게 이루어졌소?\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"누가, 나 자신도 모르게, 나에게 그 아편팅크를 주었소?\"\n\n\"나는 당신에게 말할 수 없소. 그 일의 그 부분에 관련되는 것은 아무것도, 그의 온 병 내내, 캔디 씨의 입술에서 새어 나오지 않았소. 어쩌면 당신 자신의 기억이 의심받을 사람을 가리킬지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"그런 경우, 그 조사를 좇는 것은 헛일이오. 그 아편팅크는 어떤 식으로든 몰래 당신에게 주어졌소. 그것은 거기 두고, 더 당장 중요한 일로 넘어갑시다. 할 수 있거든, 나의 기록을 읽으시오. 과거에 일어난 일에 당신의 마음을 익히시오. 나에게는 미래에 관련되는, 당신에게 제안할 아주 대담하고 아주 놀라운 무언가가 있소.\"\n\n그 마지막 말이 나를 일깨웠다.\n\n나는 에즈라 제닝스가 그것들을 내 손에 쥐여 준 순서대로 그 종이들을 보았다. 더 적은 양의 글씨를 담은 종이가 그 둘 중 맨 위에 있었다. 여기에, 캔디 씨가 그의 섬망 속에서 떨어뜨린, 이어지지 않는 낱말들과 문장의 조각들이, 다음과 같이 나타났다.\n\n\"…프랭클린 블레이크 씨…그리고 상냥한…한 등급 낮추어…약…털어놓는다…밤에 잠…그에게 말해…어긋난…약…그가 나에게 말한다…그리고 어둠 속을 더듬는 것은 하나의 같은 것을 뜻한다…만찬 식탁의 온 손님들…내가 말한다…잠을 더듬어 찾는…약 말고는 아무것도…그가 말한다…눈먼 이를 이끄는…무슨 뜻인지 안다…재치 있는…제 고집에도 하룻밤 안식을…잠을 원한다…레이디 버린더의 약장…스물다섯 미님…그가 모르게…내일 아침…자, 블레이크 씨…오늘 약…결코…그것 없이…틀렸소, 캔디 씨…훌륭한…그것 없이…그를 몰아붙여…진실…그 밖에 무언가…훌륭한…아편팅크 한 모금, 선생…잠자리…무슨…이제 약.\"\n\n거기서, 그 두 장의 종이 가운데 첫째 장이 끝났다. 나는 그것을 에즈라 제닝스에게 도로 건넸다.\n\n\"그것이 당신이 그의 잠자리에서 들은 것이오?\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"글자 그대로, 정확히 내가 들은 것이오,\" 하고 그가 대답했다—\"다만 그 되풀이들이 나의 속기 기록에서 여기 옮겨지지 않았을 뿐이오. 그는 어떤 낱말들과 어구들을, 그것들이 나타내는 생각에 얼마쯤 더, 또는 덜 중요성을 두는 데에 꼭 따라, 열두 번씩, 쉰 번씩 되뇌었소. 그 되풀이들은, 이런 뜻에서, 그 조각들을 맞추는 데에 나에게 얼마쯤 도움이 되었소. 여기지 마시오,\" 하고 그는 그 둘째 장의 종이를 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"내가 캔디 씨 자신이, 조리 있게 말할 능력이 있었다면 썼을 그 표현들을 되살렸다고 짐짓 주장하는 것으로. 나는 다만, 내가 그 이어지지 않는 표현의 장애를 뚫고, 내내 그 밑에 조리 있게 깔려 있던 그 생각에 이르렀다고 말할 뿐이오. 당신 스스로 판단하시오.\"\n\n나는 이제 첫째 장의 열쇠임을 알게 된 그 둘째 장의 종이로 눈을 돌렸다.\n\n다시 한 번, 캔디 씨의 헛소리가, 검은 잉크로 옮겨져 나타났다. 그 어구들 사이의 빈틈은 에즈라 제닝스가 붉은 잉크로 채운 채였다. 나는 그 결과를 여기 하나의 평이한 꼴로 옮긴다. 원래의 말과 그것의 해석이 이 쪽들에서 쉽게 견주어 확인될 만큼 충분히 가까이 오도록.\n\n\"…프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 영리하고 상냥하나, 그가 약 이야기를 할 때는 한 등급 낮출 필요가 있다. 그는 자기가 밤에 잠이 모자라 괴로워해 왔음을 털어놓는다. 나는 그에게, 그의 신경이 어긋났으니, 약을 먹어야 한다고 말한다. 그는 나에게, 약을 먹는 것과 어둠 속을 더듬는 것은 하나의 같은 것을 뜻한다고 말한다. 이것을 만찬 식탁의 온 손님들 앞에서 한다. 나는 그에게 말한다, 당신은 잠을 더듬어 찾고 있으며, 약 말고는 아무것도 당신이 그것을 찾도록 도울 수 없다고. 그는 나에게 말한다, 나는 눈먼 이가 눈먼 이를 이끈다는 말을 들은 적이 있는데, 이제 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 안다고. 재치 있는 말이다—허나 나는 그의 고집에도 그에게 하룻밤 안식을 줄 수 있다. 그는 정말로 잠을 원한다. 그리고 레이디 버린더의 약장이 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 있다. 오늘 밤, 그가 모르게, 그에게 아편팅크 스물다섯 미님을 주자. 그런 다음 내일 아침 찾아가자. '자, 블레이크 씨, 오늘 약을 좀 드셔 보시겠소? 그것 없이는 결코 잠들지 못할 것이오.'—'거기서 틀렸소, 캔디 씨. 나는 그것 없이도 훌륭히 하룻밤 안식을 취했소.' 그러거든, 진실로 그를 몰아붙여라! '당신은 훌륭한 하룻밤 안식 말고 그 밖에 무언가를 취했소. 당신은 잠자리에 들기 전에, 선생, 아편팅크 한 모금을 취했소. 이제 의술에 대해 뭐라 하겠소?'\"\n\n이 엉킨 실타래에서 이 매끄럽고 다듬어진 짜임을 짜 낸 그 기발함에 대한 감탄이, 그 원고를 에즈라 제닝스에게 도로 건네면서 내가 느낀, 자연스럽게 첫 인상이었다. 그는 나의 놀라움의 느낌이 스스로를 나타낸 그 처음 몇 마디를, 그가 그의 기록에서 이끌어 낸 결론이, 나 자신의 마음이 이른 결론이기도 한지 나에게 물음으로써, 겸손히 끊었다.\n\n\"당신은 나처럼 믿소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 레이디 버린더의 집에서, 버린더 아가씨의 생일 밤에, 당신이 한 그 온갖 것을 하면서, 그 아편팅크의 영향 아래 움직이고 있었다고?\"\n\n\"나는 아편팅크의 영향에 너무 무지하여 나 자신의 견해를 가질 수 없소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"나는 다만 당신의 견해를 따르고, 당신이 옳음을 확신할 수 있을 뿐이오.\"\n\n\"좋소. 다음 물음은 이것이오. 당신은 확신하오. 그리고 나도 확신하오—우리는 어떻게 우리의 확신을 다른 사람들의 마음에 전할 것이오?\"\n\n나는 우리 사이의 탁자 위에 놓인 그 두 원고를 가리켰다. 에즈라 제닝스는 고개를 저었다.\n\n\"헛일이오, 블레이크 씨! 그것들이 지금 있는 그대로로는, 세 가지 반박할 수 없는 이유에서, 아주 헛일이오. 우선, 그 기록은 인류 대중의 경험에서 온전히 벗어난 상황에서 받아 적혔소. 우선 그것부터가 그것들에게 불리하오! 둘째로, 그 기록은 하나의 의학적이고 형이상학적인 이론을 나타내오. 다시 한 번 그것들에게 불리하오! 셋째로, 그 기록은 나의 손으로 만든 것이오. 그것들이 날조가 아님을 보장할, 그렇지 않다는 나의 주장 말고는 아무것도 없소. 내가 그 황야에서 당신에게 말한 것을 기억하시오—그리고 나의 주장이 무슨 값어치가 있는지 스스로에게 물어보시오. 아니오! 나의 기록은, 바깥 세상의 평결을 보자면, 오직 한 가지 값어치가 있소. 당신의 결백이 밝혀져야 하오. 그리고 그것들은 그것이 어떻게 이루어질 수 있는지 보여 주오. 우리는 우리의 확신을 증명해야 하오—그리고 당신이 그것을 증명할 사람이오!\"\n\n\"어떻게요?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n그는 우리를 갈라놓은 그 탁자 너머로 간절히 나에게 더 가까이 몸을 기울였다.\n\n\"당신은 대담한 실험을 해 볼 마음이 있소?\"\n\n\"나는 이제 나에게 얹혀 있는 그 의심에서 나를 씻기 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠소.\"\n\n\"당신은 한동안 얼마쯤의 개인적 불편을 감수하겠소?\"\n\n\"어떤 불편이든, 그것이 무엇이든 간에.\"\n\n\"당신은 나의 조언에 온전히 따르겠소? 그것은 당신을 바보들의 비웃음에 드러낼지도 모르오. 그것은 당신을, 당신이 마땅히 존중해야 할 견해를 지닌 벗들의 항변에 처하게 할지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"무엇을 할지 나에게 말하시오!\" 하고 나는 조바심을 내며 터뜨렸다. \"그리고, 무슨 일이 있어도, 나는 그것을 하겠소.\"\n\n\"당신은 이것을 할 것이오, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"당신은, 두 번째로, 무의식적으로, 그 증언이 다툼의 여지가 없는 증인들의 앞에서, 그 다이아몬드를 훔칠 것이오.\"\n\n나는 벌떡 일어섰다. 나는 말하려 했다. 나는 그를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"나는 그것이 정녕 이루어질 수 있다고 믿소,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그것은 정녕 이루어질 것이오—당신이 나를 돕기만 한다면. 진정하려 애쓰시오—앉으시오, 그리고 내가 당신에게 할 말을 들으시오. 당신은 담배 피우는 버릇을 다시 들였소. 나는 그것을 내 눈으로 보았소. 언제부터 그것을 다시 들였소?\"\n\n\"거의 한 해 전부터.\"\n\n\"당신은 예전보다 더 많이 피우오, 덜 피우오?\"\n\n\"더 많이.\"\n\n\"당신은 그 버릇을 다시 끊겠소? 별안간, 명심하시오!—당신이 전에 그것을 끊었듯이.\"\n\n나는 그의 속셈을 어렴풋이 보기 시작했다. \"나는 이 순간부터 그것을 끊겠소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다.\n\n\"만일 지난 유월에 뒤따른 것과 같은 결과가 뒤따른다면,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다—\"만일 당신이 그때 괴로워한 것처럼, 잠 못 이룬 밤으로 다시 한 번 괴로워한다면, 우리는 우리의 첫걸음을 이룬 것이오. 우리는 당신을 그 생일 밤의 당신의 신경 상태와 비슷한 무언가로 다시 되돌려 놓은 것이오. 만일 우리가 다음으로, 당신을 둘러쌌던 그 집안의 상황을 되살리거나, 거의 되살릴 수 있다면, 그리고 만일 우리가 예전에 당신의 마음을 어지럽혔던 그 다이아몬드에 관한 갖가지 물음으로 당신의 마음을 다시 사로잡을 수 있다면, 우리는 당신을, 몸으로도 마음으로도, 지난해에 그 아편이 당신을 찾아냈던 그 처지와 되도록 가까운 처지에 도로 놓은 것이오. 그런 경우 우리는, 그 약을 되풀이하는 것이, 더하든 덜하든, 그 결과의 되풀이로 이어지리라고 정녕 바랄 수 있소. 이것이, 몇 마디 서두른 말로 나타낸 나의 제안이오. 이제 당신은 내가 그것을 하는 데에 나를 정당화할 무슨 이유가 있는지 보게 될 것이오.\"\n\n그는 그의 곁의 책 하나로 눈을 돌려, 작은 종이쪽지로 표시된 곳에서 그것을 폈다.\n\n\"내가 당신을 생리학 강의로 지치게 하려는 것으로 여기지 마시오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 우리 둘 다에 대한 도리로, 내가 나 자신이 궁리한 어떤 이론에 대한 존중에서 이 실험을 해 보라고 당신에게 청하는 것이 아님을 증명해야겠다고 여기오. 인정된 원리들과, 널리 알려진 권위들이, 내가 취하는 그 견해에 나를 정당화하오. 나에게 당신의 주의의 오 분을 주시오. 그러면 나는, 그것이 아무리 공상적으로 보일지라도, 과학이 나의 제안을 승인함을 당신에게 보여 드리겠소. 여기, 우선, 내가 따라 움직이는 그 생리학적 원리가, 다른 아닌 카펜터 박사만 한 사람에 의해 진술되어 있소. 당신 스스로 그것을 읽으시오.\"\n\n그는 나에게 그 책에서 그 곳을 표시했던 그 종이쪽지를 건넸다. 그것은 몇 줄의 글씨를 담고 있었는데, 다음과 같았다—\n\n\"지각 의식에 의해 한 번 인식된 모든 감각 인상은 (이를테면) 뇌에 등록되며, 비록 그 사이의 온 기간 동안 그것의 존재에 대한 의식이 마음에 없을지라도, 그 뒤 어느 때에 되살아날 수 있다는 믿음에 상당한 근거가 있는 듯하다.\"\n\n\"여기까지 평이하오?\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 물었다.\n\n\"완전히 평이하오.\"\n\n그는 그 펼쳐진 책을 탁자 너머로 나에게 밀고, 연필 줄로 표시된 한 대목을 가리켰다.\n\n\"자,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신 자신의 처지와, 그리고 내가 당신에게 해 보라고 꾀고 있는 그 실험에—내가 믿기로는—직접 관련되는 어떤 경우에 대한 그 서술을 읽으시오. 시작하기 전에 눈여겨보시오, 블레이크 씨, 내가 지금 당신에게 잉글랜드의 가장 위대한 생리학자 가운데 하나를 가리키고 있음을. 당신 손에 든 그 책은 엘리엇슨 박사의 《인체 생리학》이오. 그리고 그 박사가 인용한 그 경우는 콤 씨의 널리 알려진 권위에 기대고 있소.\"\n\n나에게 가리켜진 그 대목은 이런 말로 나타나 있었다—\n\n\"에이벌 박사가 나에게 알려 주었다,\" 하고 콤 씨는 말한다. \"어느 창고의 아일랜드인 짐꾼에 대해, 그는 술이 깨면, 술 취했을 때 자기가 한 일을 잊었으나, 다시 술 취하면, 자기의 이전 취한 상태의 일들을 도로 떠올렸다. 한번은, 술 취해, 그가 얼마쯤 값나가는 꾸러미 하나를 잃어버렸고, 그의 술 깬 순간에는 그것에 대해 아무것도 댈 수 없었다. 다음에 그가 취했을 때, 그는 자기가 그 꾸러미를 어느 집에 두었음을 떠올렸고, 그 위에 주소가 없었던 터라, 그것은 거기 안전히 남아 있어, 그가 그것을 찾으러 가매 되찾아졌다.\"\n\n\"다시 평이하오?\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 물었다.\n\n\"더없이 평이하오.\"\n\n그는 그 종이쪽지를 제자리에 도로 넣고, 그 책을 덮었다.\n\n\"내가 나를 뒷받침할 좋은 권위 없이 말한 것이 아님을 흡족히 여기오?\" 하고 그가 물었다. \"만일 그렇지 않다면, 나는 저 책장으로 가기만 하면 되고, 당신은 내가 당신에게 가리켜 줄 수 있는 대목들을 읽기만 하면 되오.\"\n\n\"나는 아주 흡족하오,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"한마디 더 읽지 않아도.\"\n\n\"그런 경우, 우리는 이 일에서의 당신 자신의 개인적 이해로 돌아가도 되겠소. 나는 당신에게, 그 실험에 대해 찬성뿐 아니라 반대해서도 할 말이 있음을 말해야겠소. 만일 우리가, 올해, 당신의 경우에, 지난해에 있었던 그대로의 조건을 정확히 되살릴 수 있다면, 우리가 정확히 같은 결과에 이르리라는 것은 생리학적으로 확실하오. 허나 이것은—부인할 수 없거니와—그저 불가능하오. 우리는 다만 그 조건에 가까이 다가가기를 바랄 수 있을 뿐이오. 그리고 만일 우리가 당신을 당신이던 상태에 충분히 가까이 되돌려 놓는 데에 성공하지 못한다면, 우리의 이 모험은 실패할 것이오. 만일 우리가 성공한다면—그리고 나 자신은 성공을 바라고 있소—당신은 적어도, 어떤 분별 있는 사람에게든 당신이 그 다이아몬드의 절도에 대해, 도덕적으로 말하여, 죄 없음을 흡족히 여기게 할 만큼은, 그 생일 밤의 당신의 행동을 되풀이할 수 있을 것이오. 나는, 블레이크 씨, 내가 이제 그 문제를, 내가 나 자신에게 지운 한계 안에서, 그 양편으로, 되도록 공정하게 진술한 것 같소. 만일 내가 당신에게 또렷이 하지 못한 무언가가 있다면, 그것이 무엇인지 나에게 말하시오—그리고 내가 당신을 밝혀 줄 수 있다면, 그리하겠소.\"\n\n\"당신이 나에게 설명한 온갖 것을,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 완전히 이해하오. 허나 나는, 당신이 아직 나에게 또렷이 하지 못한 한 가지 점에서 어리둥절함을 인정하오.\"\n\n\"그 점이 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"나는 그 아편팅크가 나에게 미친 영향을 이해하지 못하오. 나는 내가 아래층으로, 그리고 복도를 따라 걸은 것과, 내가 장식장의 서랍을 열고 닫은 것과, 내가 다시 내 방으로 돌아간 것을 이해하지 못하오. 이 모든 것은 능동적인 행동이오. 나는 아편의 영향이 먼저 당신을 멍하게 하고, 그런 다음 당신을 잠재우는 것이라고 생각했소.\"\n\n\"아편에 대한 흔한 오해요, 블레이크 씨! 나는, 이 순간, 캔디 씨가 당신에게 투여한 양보다 열 배쯤 더 많은 아편팅크 한 모금의 영향 아래, 당신을 위해 나의 (그런 정도의) 지력을 발휘하고 있소. 허나 나의 권위에 기대지 마시오—나 자신의 개인적 경험에 드는 물음에 대해서조차. 나는 당신이 방금 한 그 반론을 미리 내다보았소. 그리고 나는 다시, 당신 자신의 마음에서, 그리고 당신의 벗들의 마음에서 마땅한 무게를 지닐, 독립적인 증언을 나 자신에게 마련해 두었소.\"\n\n그는 나에게 그가 탁자 위에 두었던 그 두 책 가운데 둘째 것을 건넸다.\n\n\"저기,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그 널리 이름난 《어느 잉글랜드 아편 상용자의 고백》이오! 그 책을 가져가서, 읽으시오. 내가 표시한 그 대목에서, 당신은 드퀸시가 자기가 '아편의 방탕'이라 부르는 것을 저질렀을 때, 그가 음악을 즐기러 오페라의 특별석에 가든지, 아니면 그가 토요일 밤 런던의 장터를 돌아다니며, 가난한 이들이 저희 일요일 저녁을 마련하는 온갖 자잘한 궁리와 흥정을 살피는 데에 관심을 두든지 했음을 알게 될 것이오. 아편의 영향 아래 능동적으로 스스로를 쓰고, 이곳저곳으로 옮겨 다니는 사람의 능력에 대해서는 그쯤 해 둡시다.\"\n\n\"거기까지는 답을 얻었소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"허나 나는 그 아편이 나 자신에게 미친 그 영향에 대해서는 아직 답을 얻지 못했소.\"\n\n\"내가 몇 마디로 당신에게 답하려 애써 보겠소,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"아편의 작용은, 대부분의 경우, 두 가지 영향에 담겨 있소—먼저 흥분시키는 영향, 그리고 뒤이어 진정시키는 영향. 그 흥분시키는 영향 아래, 당신의 마음에 남겨진 가장 나중의, 가장 생생한 인상들—곧 그 다이아몬드에 관련되는 인상들이, 당신의 병적으로 예민한 신경 상태에서, 당신의 뇌 속에서 격해져, 꼭 여느 꿈이 당신의 판단과 당신의 의지를 제 아래 둠과 똑같이, 당신의 판단과 당신의 의지를 제 아래 두었을 공산이 크오. 조금씩, 이 작용 아래, 당신이 낮 동안 느꼈을지 모를 그 다이아몬드의 안전에 대한 어떤 염려든 의심의 상태에서 확신의 상태로 발전하기 쉬웠을 것이고—당신을 그 보석을 지키려는 실질적인 행동으로 몰아붙였을 것이고—그 동기를 염두에 두고, 당신의 걸음을 당신이 들어간 그 방으로 이끌었을 것이고—당신이 그 돌을 담은 서랍을 찾기까지, 당신의 손을 그 장식장의 서랍으로 인도했을 것이오. 아편의 그 영적으로 고양된 취기 속에서, 당신은 그 온갖 것을 했을 것이오. 나중에, 그 진정시키는 작용이 그 흥분시키는 작용을 이기기 시작하면서, 당신은 서서히 무기력하고 멍해졌을 것이오. 더 나중에 당신은 깊은 잠에 빠졌을 것이오. 아침이 오고, 그 아편의 영향이 죄 잠으로 씻겨 나갔을 때, 당신은 마치 당신이 지구 반대편에서 살고 있었기라도 한 양, 밤에 당신이 한 일에 대해 절대적으로 무지한 채로 깨어났을 것이오. 여기까지 내가 당신에게 그것을 웬만큼 또렷이 했소?\"\n\n\"당신은 그것을 어찌나 또렷이 했던지,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 당신이 더 나아가기를 바라오. 당신은 나에게 내가 어떻게 그 방에 들어갔고, 어떻게 그 다이아몬드를 가져가게 되었는지 보여 주었소. 허나 버린더 아가씨는 내가 그 보석을 손에 들고 다시 그 방을 떠나는 것을 보았소. 당신은 그 순간부터의 나의 행동을 좇을 수 있소? 당신은 내가 다음에 무엇을 했는지 짐작할 수 있소?\"\n\n\"그것이 바로 내가 이르려던 그 점이오,\" 하고 그가 되받았다. \"나에게는, 당신의 결백을 밝히는 수단으로 내가 제안하는 그 실험이, 그 잃어버린 다이아몬드를 되찾는 수단으로도 될 수 있지 않을까 하는 물음이 있소. 당신이 그 보석을 손에 들고 버린더 아가씨의 응접실을 떠났을 때, 당신은 십중팔구 당신 자신의 방으로 돌아갔을 것이오——\"\n\n\"그래서요? 그러고는요?\"\n\n\"있을 법한 일이오, 블레이크 씨—나는 감히 더 말하지 못하겠소만—그 다이아몬드를 지키려는 당신의 생각이, 자연스러운 잇따름으로, 그 다이아몬드를 감추려는 생각으로 이어졌고, 당신이 그것을 감춘 그 자리가 당신의 침실 어딘가였다는 것이. 그런 경우, 그 아일랜드인 짐꾼의 경우가 당신의 경우일지도 모르오. 당신은, 그 둘째 번 아편의 영향 아래, 당신이 첫째 번 영향 아래 그 다이아몬드를 감춘 그 자리를 떠올릴지도 모르오.\"\n\n이제, 에즈라 제닝스를 밝혀 줄 차례는 나였다. 나는 그가 더 말하기 전에 그를 멈추게 했다.\n\n\"당신은 짐작하고 있소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"도저히 일어날 수 없는 결과를. 그 다이아몬드는, 이 순간, 런던에 있소.\"\n\n그는 소스라치며, 크나큰 놀라움에 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"런던에?\" 하고 그가 되뇌었다. \"그것이 레이디 버린더의 집에서 어떻게 런던으로 갔소?\"\n\n\"아무도 모르오.\"\n\n\"당신은 당신 자신의 손으로 그것을 버린더 아가씨의 방에서 옮겼소. 그것이 어떻게 당신의 보관에서 빠져나갔소?\"\n\n\"나는 그것이 어떻게 나의 보관에서 빠져나갔는지 짐작조차 못 하오.\"\n\n\"당신은 아침에 깨어났을 때 그것을 보았소?\"\n\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"버린더 아가씨가 그것을 도로 손에 넣었소?\"\n\n\"아니오.\"\n\n\"블레이크 씨! 여기 밝혀져야 할 무언가가 있는 듯하오. 당신이, 그 다이아몬드가 이 순간 런던에 있음을 어떻게 아는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n나는, 내가 잉글랜드로 돌아와 그 월장석에 대한 나의 첫 조사를 했을 때, 바로 똑같은 물음을 브러프 씨에게 던진 터였다. 에즈라 제닝스에게 답하면서, 나는 그에 따라, 내가 그 변호사 자신의 입술에서 들은 것을—그리고 이 쪽들의 독자들에게 이미 익숙한 것을 되뇌었다.\n\n그는 그가 나의 대답에 흡족하지 않음을 또렷이 내보였다.\n\n\"당신에 대한 온갖 존중과,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 법률 고문에 대한 온갖 존중을 담아, 나는 방금 내가 나타낸 그 견해를 고수하오. 그것은, 내가 잘 아는 바로는, 한낱 가정에 기대고 있소. 당신의 견해도 마찬가지로 한낱 가정에 기대고 있음을 당신에게 일깨워 드리는 것을 용서하시오.\"\n\n그가 그 일에 대해 취한 그 견해는 나에게 온전히 새로운 것이었다. 나는 그가 그것을 어떻게 옹호할지 애태우며 들으려 기다렸다.\n\n\"나는 가정하오,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말을 이었다. \"그 아편의 영향이—당신을, 그것의 안전을 확보할 목적으로, 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣도록 몰아붙인 뒤에—또한 당신을, 같은 영향과 같은 동기 아래 움직여, 그것을 당신 자신의 방 어딘가에 감추도록 몰아붙였을지도 모른다고. 당신은 가정하오, 그 힌두인 음모자들이 도저히 잘못을 저지를 수 없다고. 그 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 찾아 루커 씨의 집으로 갔고—따라서, 그 다이아몬드가 루커 씨의 소유에 있음이 틀림없다고! 당신은 그 월장석이 애초에 런던으로 가져가지기라도 했다는 것을 증명할 무슨 증거라도 있소? 당신은 그것이 어떻게, 또는 누구에 의해 레이디 버린더의 집에서 옮겨졌는지 짐작조차 못 하오! 당신은 그 보석이 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔다는 무슨 증거라도 있소? 그는 자기가 그 월장석에 대해 들은 적이 없다고 선언하오. 그리고 그의 은행가 영수증은 큰 값어치의 귀중품의 예치 말고는 아무것도 인정하지 않소. 그 인도인들은 루커 씨가 거짓말하고 있다고 가정하오—그리고 당신은 다시 그 인도인들이 옳다고 가정하오. 당신과 견해를 달리하면서, 내가 하는 말의 전부는—나의 견해가 가능하다는 것이오. 그 이상, 블레이크 씨, 논리적으로든 법률적으로든, 당신의 견해를 위해 무슨 말을 할 수 있소?\"\n\n그것은 강하게 던져졌다. 허나 그것이 마찬가지로 참되이 던져졌음도 부인할 수 없었다.\n\n\"솔직히 당신이 나를 휘청거리게 하오,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"내가 브러프 씨에게 편지하여, 당신이 말한 것을 그에게 이야기하는 것에 반대하오?\"\n\n\"오히려, 당신이 브러프 씨에게 편지한다면 나는 기쁘겠소. 만일 우리가 그의 경험을 참조한다면, 우리는 그 일을 새로운 빛 아래 볼지도 모르오. 지금으로서는, 우리의 아편 실험으로 돌아갑시다. 우리는 당신이 이 순간부터 담배 피우는 버릇을 끊기로 정했소.\"\n\n\"이 순간부터.\"\n\n\"그것이 첫걸음이오. 다음 걸음은, 우리가 할 수 있는 한 가까이, 지난해에 당신을 둘러쌌던 그 집안의 상황을 되살리는 것이오.\"\n\n이것이 어떻게 이루어질 것인가? 레이디 버린더는 죽었다. 레이철과 나는, 절도의 의심이 나에게 얹혀 있는 한, 돌이킬 수 없이 갈라져 있었다. 고드프리 에이블화이트는 대륙으로 여행을 떠나 있었다. 내가 지난번 그 집에서 잤을 때 그것에 살던 사람들을 다시 불러 모으는 것은 그저 불가능했다. 이 반론을 진술해도 에즈라 제닝스는 난처해하는 듯싶지 않았다. 그는 같은 사람들을 다시 모으는 데에는 아주 조금밖에 중요성을 두지 않는다고 말했다—그들이 지난 시절 나를 향해 차지했던 갖가지 처지를 다시 취하기를 기대하는 것은 헛되리라고 보았으므로. 다른 한편으로, 그는 그 실험의 성공에, 내가 지난번 그 집에 있었을 때 나를 둘러쌌던 것과 같은 물건들을 내 둘레에서 보는 것이 필수라고 여겼다.\n\n\"무엇보다도,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은 당신이 그 생일 밤에 잔 그 방에서 자야 하오. 그리고 그것은 같은 식으로 갖추어져야 하오. 그 계단과, 복도와, 버린더 아가씨의 응접실도, 당신이 지난번 그것들을 보았을 때의 그대로로 되돌려져야 하오. 블레이크 씨, 그 집의 그 부분에서 이제 치워졌을지도 모를 온갖 세간을 도로 놓는 것이 절대적으로 필요하오. 당신의 여송연의 희생은, 우리가 버린더 아가씨의 허락을 얻어 그것을 할 수 없다면, 헛될 것이오.\"\n\n\"누가 그녀에게 허락을 청할 것이오?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"당신이 청하는 것이 가능하지 않소?\"\n\n\"아주 당치 않소. 그 잃어버린 다이아몬드라는 주제로 우리 사이에 오간 일을 겪고서, 나는, 사정이 지금 이러하니, 그녀를 만날 수도, 그녀에게 편지할 수도 없소.\"\n\n에즈라 제닝스는 멈추어, 잠시 곰곰이 생각했다.\n\n\"내가 당신에게 섬세한 물음을 하나 해도 되겠소?\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 그에게 계속하라고 몸짓했다.\n\n\"내가 옳소, 블레이크 씨, (당신에게서 새어 나온 한두 가지로) 당신이 지난 시절 버린더 아가씨에게 여느 것 아닌 관심을 느꼈다고 짐작하는 것이?\"\n\n\"아주 옳소.\"\n\n\"그 감정이 되돌려졌소?\"\n\n\"그랬소.\"\n\n\"당신은 버린더 아가씨가 당신의 결백을 증명하려는 그 시도에 강한 관심을 느낄 공산이 크다고 생각하오?\"\n\n\"나는 그것을 확신하오.\"\n\n\"그런 경우, 내가 버린더 아가씨에게 편지하겠소—당신이 나에게 허락한다면.\"\n\n\"당신이 나에게 한 그 제안을 그녀에게 이야기하고?\"\n\n\"오늘 우리 사이에 오간 모든 것을 그녀에게 이야기하고.\"\n\n내가 그가 나에게 내놓은 그 도움을 간절히 받아들인 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다.\n\n\"오늘 우편으로 편지할 짬이 있겠소,\" 하고 그가 그의 시계를 보며 말했다. \"호텔로 돌아가거든, 당신의 여송연을 넣고 잠그는 것을 잊지 마시오! 나는 내일 아침 찾아와 당신이 밤을 어떻게 보냈는지 듣겠소.\"\n\n나는 그에게 작별하려 일어섰다. 그리고 내가 정말로 느낀, 그의 친절에 대한 고마운 느낌을 나타내려 했다.\n\n그는 부드럽게 내 손을 쥐었다. \"그 황야에서 내가 당신에게 말한 것을 기억하시오,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"만일 내가 당신에게 이 자그마한 도움이 될 수 있다면, 블레이크 씨, 나는 그것을, 길고 흐렸던 하루의 저녁에 떨어지는 마지막 한 줄기 햇살처럼 느낄 것이오.\"\n\n우리는 헤어졌다. 그때는 유월 십오일이었다. 뒤이은 열흘의 사건들은—그 하나하나가 다소간 곧바로, 내가 그 수동적인 대상이었던 그 실험과 이어진—캔디 씨의 조수가 늘 써 온 일기에, 그것들이 일어난 그대로 정확히, 죄 기록되어 있다. 에즈라 제닝스의 쪽들에는 아무것도 숨겨지지 않고, 아무것도 잊히지 않는다. 그 아편의 모험이 어떻게 시도되었고, 어떻게 끝났는지, 에즈라 제닝스가 이야기하게 두자.\n\n에즈라 제닝스의 일기에서 발췌함.\n\n1849년.—유월 15일.… 환자들로 인한 얼마쯤의 방해와, 통증으로 인한 얼마쯤의 방해에도, 나는 오늘 우편에 대도록 버린더 아가씨에게 보내는 나의 편지를 마쳤다. 나는 그것을 내가 바랐을 만큼 짧은 편지로 만드는 데에 실패했다. 허나 나는 그것을 평이하게 만든 것 같다. 그것은 그녀를 온전히 자기 자신의 결정의 주인으로 남겨 둔다. 만일 그녀가 그 실험을 돕는 데에 동의한다면, 그녀는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨나 나에 대한 호의로가 아니라, 제 자유로운 뜻으로 동의하는 것이다.\n\n유월 16일.—끔찍한 밤을 지새운 뒤, 늦게 일어났다. 어제의 아편의 앙갚음이, 일련의 무시무시한 꿈을 통해 나를 좇았다. 어느 때는 내가 죽은 이들의, 벗과 원수가 함께 있는 환영들과 더불어 텅 빈 허공을 소용돌이치며 날았다. 다른 때는, 내가 다시는 보지 못할 그 하나의 사랑하는 얼굴이, 내 잠자리 곁에 솟아올라, 그 캄캄한 어둠 속에서 흉측하게 인광을 뿜으며, 나를 노려보고 나를 향해 이를 드러내며 웃었다. 이른 아침의 여느 때에 그 옛 통증이 조금 돌아온 것이, 변화로서 반가웠다. 그것은 그 환영들을 흩어 놓았다—그리고 그것이 그리했기에 견딜 만했다.\n\n나의 나쁜 밤이, 내가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 갈 수 있기 전에, 아침을 늦게 만들었다. 나는 그가 소파에 몸을 뻗고, 브랜디와 소다수와 마른 비스킷으로 아침을 들고 있는 것을 보았다.\n\n\"당신이 정녕 바랄 만큼 잘, 나는 시작하고 있소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"비참하고, 안절부절못한 밤. 그리고 오늘 아침 완전한 입맛의 실종. 지난해에 내가 여송연을 끊었을 때 일어난 것과 정확히 같소. 내가 나의 둘째 번 아편팅크를 위해 채비될수록, 나는 그만큼 더 흡족할 것이오.\"\n\n\"당신은 되도록 이른 날에 그것을 얻을 것이오,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"그동안, 우리는 되도록 당신의 건강을 조심해야 하오. 만일 우리가 당신을 지치게 둔다면, 우리는 그런 식으로 실패할 것이오. 당신은 당신의 저녁을 위한 입맛을 얻어야 하오. 다시 말해, 당신은 오늘 아침, 맑은 공기 속에서 승마나 산책을 해야 하오.\"\n\n\"여기서 나에게 말을 구해 줄 수 있다면, 나는 말을 타겠소. 그러고 보니, 나는 어제 브러프 씨에게 편지했소. 당신은 버린더 아가씨에게 편지했소?\"\n\n\"그렇소—지난밤 우편으로.\"\n\n\"아주 좋소. 우리는 내일 서로에게 이야기할, 들을 값어치 있는 소식을 얼마쯤 갖게 될 것이오. 아직 가지 마시오! 당신에게 할 말이 있소. 당신은 어제, 우리의 아편 실험이 나의 어떤 벗들에게 그리 좋게 여겨질 성싶지 않다고 생각하는 듯했소. 당신은 아주 옳았소. 나는 늙은 게이브리얼 베터리지를 나의 벗들 가운데 하나로 부르오. 그리고 당신은, 내가 어제 그를 보았을 때 그가 강하게 항변한 것을 들으면 재미있어할 것이오. '나리는 나리의 생애 가운데 놀라울 만큼 숱한 어리석은 일을 하셨습니다요, 프랭클린 나리, 허나 이것은 그 모두의 으뜸입니다!' 그것이 베터리지의 견해요! 만일 당신과 그가 마주치게 된다면, 당신은 틀림없이 그의 편견을 헤아려 주겠지요?\"\n\n나는, 내 환자들 사이의 나의 왕진을 돌러, 블레이크 씨를 떠났다. 내가 그와 가진 그 짧은 면담만으로도 더 나아지고 더 행복해진 것을 느끼면서.\n\n이 사람에게 내가 느끼는 그 끌림의 비밀은 무엇인가? 그것은 그저, 그가 나로 하여금 그와 사귀게 해 준 그 솔직하고 다정한 태도와, 다른 사람들이 나를 맞는 그 인정사정없는 미움과 불신 사이의 대비를 내가 느낀다는 것을 뜻할 뿐인가? 아니면 그에게, 내가 얼마쯤의 인간적 교감에 대해 지닌 그 갈망에—여러 해의 고독과 박해를 견디고 살아남은, 내가 더는 견디지도 느끼지도 않게 될 그때가 점점 더 가까이 다가올수록 더욱더 날카로워지는 듯한 그 갈망에 응답하는 무언가가 정말로 있는가? 이 물음들을 하는 것이 얼마나 헛된가! 블레이크 씨는 나에게 삶에 대한 새로운 관심을 주었다. 그 새로운 관심이 무엇인지 알려 하지 않고, 그것으로 넉넉하다 하자.\n\n유월 17일.—오늘 아침, 아침 식사 전에, 캔디 씨가 나에게, 자기가 잉글랜드 남부의 어느 벗을 찾아, 두 주 동안 떠나 있겠다고 알렸다. 그는 나에게, 가엾은 사람, 마치 그가 아직도 그가 병에 걸리기 전에 지녔던 그 큰 개업을 하고 있기라도 한 양, 그 환자들에 대해 숱한 각별한 지시를 주었다. 그 개업은 이제 값어치가 얼마 안 된다! 다른 의사들이 그를 밀어냈다. 그리고 어쩔 수 있는 사람이면 아무도 나를 부리지 않을 것이다.\n\n그가 바로 이때에 떠나 있게 되는 것이 어쩌면 다행이다. 만일 내가 그에게 내가 블레이크 씨와 해 보려는 그 실험을 알리지 않았다면 그는 언짢아했을 것이다. 그리고 만일 내가 그를 나의 믿음 안에 들였다면 무슨 바람직하지 못한 결과가 일어났을지 나는 좀처럼 모르겠다. 지금 이대로가 더 낫다. 두말할 것 없이, 지금 이대로가 더 낫다.\n\n우편이, 캔디 씨가 그 집을 떠난 뒤에, 나에게 버린더 아가씨의 답장을 가져다주었다.\n\n매혹적인 편지! 그것은 나로 하여금 그녀를 더없이 높이 여기게 한다. 우리의 일에 그녀가 느끼는 관심을 감추려는 아무 시도도 없다. 그녀는, 더없이 어여쁜 태도로, 나에게, 나의 편지가 (그녀에 관한 한) 나의 주장을 증명에 부칠 조금의 필요도 없이, 그녀에게 블레이크 씨의 결백을 흡족히 여기게 했다고 말한다. 그녀는 심지어—더없이 부당하게도, 가엾은 것!—그 수수께끼의 참된 해답이 정말로 무엇일지를 그때 알아채지 못한 데에 대해 자신을 나무란다. 이 모든 것의 밑에 깔린 동기는 뚜렷이, 그녀가 무심결에 다른 사람에게 가한 잘못을 속죄하려는 너그러운 간절함 이상의 무언가에서 비롯한다. 그녀가, 그들 사이의 소원함 내내, 그를 사랑해 온 것이 뚜렷하다. 한 곳 넘게에서, 그가 사랑받을 자격이 있었음을 발견한 그 황홀함이, 펜과 잉크의 더없이 굳센 격식을 뚫고 무심결에 제 길을 내고, 심지어 낯선 이에게 편지하는 더 강한 제약마저 무릅쓴다. 가능한 일인가 (나는 이 기쁨 어린 편지를 읽으며 스스로에게 묻는다), 세상 온갖 사람 가운데 하필 내가, 이 두 젊은이를 다시 함께 맺어 주는 수단으로 뽑힌 것이? 나 자신의 행복은 발밑에 짓밟혔다. 나 자신의 사랑은 나에게서 뜯겨 나갔다. 나는, 내가 만드는 남들의 행복을—내가 되돌리는, 되살아난 사랑을 살아서 보게 될 것인가? 오 자비로운 죽음이여, 그대의 팔이 나를 감싸기 전에, 그대의 목소리가 나에게 \"마침내 쉬어라!\" 하고 속삭이기 전에, 나로 하여금 그것을 보게 하라.\n\n그 편지에는 두 가지 청이 담겨 있다. 그중 하나가 나로 하여금 그것을 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 보이지 못하게 한다. 나는 버린더 아가씨가 기꺼이 자기 집을 우리 마음대로 쓰게 하는 데에 동의한다고 그에게 말할 권한이 있고—그것을 말하고 나서, 나는 더는 아무것도 덧붙이지 말라는 청을 받았다.\n\n여기까지는, 그녀의 바람에 따르기가 쉽다. 허나 그 둘째 청이 나를 심각하게 난처하게 한다.\n\n베터리지 씨에게 편지하여, 내가 줄 어떤 지시든 이행하라고 이른 것으로 만족하지 못하고, 버린더 아가씨는 제 응접실의 복원을 몸소 감독함으로써 나를 돕게 해 달라고 청한다. 그녀는 다만 나의 한마디 답을 기다려, 요크셔로 여행하여, 그 아편이 두 번째로 시도되는 그 밤에 증인 가운데 하나로 자리하려 한다.\n\n여기서도, 다시, 표면 아래 동기가 있다. 그리고, 여기서도, 다시, 나는 그것을 알아낼 수 있을 것 같다.\n\n그녀가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 말하는 것을 나에게 금한 것을, 그녀는 (내가 그것을 해석하기로는) 그가, 다른 사람들의 눈에 그의 명예를 밝힐 그 시험에 부쳐지기 전에, 제 입술로 그에게 말하기를 간절히 바란다. 나는, 그의 결백이 증명되든 안 되든, 그것을 기다리지 않고 그를 무죄로 여기려는 이 너그러운 안달을 이해하고 감탄한다. 그것은, 그녀가, 무심결에, 그리고 어쩔 수 없이 그에게 잘못한 뒤에, 하기를 갈망하는 속죄다, 가엾은 아가씨. 허나 그 일은 이루어질 수 없다. 나는, 그들 사이의 만남이 양편에 자아낼 그 동요가—잠든 감정을 되살리고, 옛 기억에 호소하고, 새 희망을 일깨우며—블레이크 씨의 마음에 미치는 그 효과에 있어, 우리 실험의 성공에 거의 틀림없이 치명적이리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않는다. 사정이 이러하니, 지난해에 있었던, 또는 거의 있었던 그대로의 조건을 그에게 되살리는 것만으로도 충분히 어렵다. 그를 뒤흔들 새 관심과 새 감정이 있다면, 그 시도는 그저 헛될 것이다.\n\n그럼에도, 이것을 알면서도, 나는 그녀를 실망시킬 마음이 도무지 나지 않는다. 나는, 우편 시간 전에, 내가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 하기로 나를 맨 그 도움에 해를 끼치지 않고, 버린더 아가씨에게 그렇다고 말할 수 있게 해 줄 어떤 새로운 방안을 찾아낼 수 있을지 애써 봐야 한다.\n\n두 시.—나는 방금 나의 왕진을 돌고 돌아왔다—물론, 그 호텔에 들르는 것으로 시작하여.\n\n블레이크 씨의 그 밤에 대한 보고는 이전과 같다. 그는 얼마쯤의 끊긴 잠의 사이가 있었을 뿐, 그 이상은 없었다. 허나 그는 어제 저녁을 든 뒤 잤으므로, 오늘은 그것을 덜 느낀다. 이 저녁 식사 뒤의 잠은, 두말할 것 없이, 내가 그에게 권한 그 승마의 결과다. 안됐지만 나는 맑은 공기 속에서의 그의 회복 운동을 줄여야 할 것 같다. 그는 너무 건강해서도 안 되고, 너무 아파서도 안 된다. 이것은 (뱃사람이라면 그리 말하듯) 아주 섬세한 키잡이가 필요한 경우다.\n\n그는 아직 브러프 씨에게서 소식을 듣지 못했다. 나는 그가 내가 버린더 아가씨에게서 무슨 답을 받았는지 알고 싶어 안달하는 것을 보았다.\n\n나는 그에게, 내가 말하도록 허락받은 것을 정확히, 그 이상은 아무것도 말하지 않았다. 그에게 그 편지를 보이지 않는 데에 대한 핑계를 지어낼 것은 아주 없었다. 그는, 가엾은 사람, 자기가 내가 그것을 내놓기를 꺼리게 하는 그 섬세함을 이해한다고, 자못 쓰라리게 나에게 말했다. \"그녀는, 물론, 예사로운 예의와 예사로운 도리에서 동의하는 것이오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"허나 그녀는 나에 대한 제 견해를 지킨 채, 그 결과를 보려 기다리는 것이오.\" 나는 그에게, 그가 이제 그녀가 그에게 잘못한 것처럼 그녀에게 잘못하고 있다고 넌지시 비칠 심한 유혹을 느꼈다. 곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 나는 그를 놀라게 하고 용서하는 그 이중의 호사에서 그녀를 앞지르기를 움츠려 피했다.\n\n나의 방문은 아주 짧았다. 요전 날 밤의 겪음 뒤에, 나는 다시 한 번 나의 아편 한 모금을 끊지 않을 수 없었다. 필연적인 결과로, 내 안의 그 병의 고통이 다시 우위를 차지했다. 나는 그 발작이 다가옴을 느끼고, 그를 놀라게 하거나 괴롭히지 않으려, 갑자기 떠났다. 이번에는 그것이 겨우 십오 분 이어졌고, 나에게 나의 일을 계속할 만큼의 힘을 남겼다.\n\n다섯 시.—나는 버린더 아가씨에게 나의 답장을 썼다.\n\n내가 제안한 그 방안은, 그녀가 그것에 동의하기만 한다면, 양편의 이해를 조화시킨다. 먼저 그 실험이 시도되기 전에 블레이크 씨와 그녀 자신 사이의 만남에 있는 반론들을 진술하고서, 나는 그녀가, 우리가 그 시도를 하는 그 저녁에 남몰래 그 집에 다다르도록 여행 시간을 잡을 것을 제안했다. 런던에서 오후 기차로 여행하면, 그녀는 그녀의 다다름을 아홉 시까지 늦출 것이다. 그 시각에, 나는 블레이크 씨를 그의 침실로 안전히 들이기로 했다. 그리하여 그 아편팅크를 투여할 때가 올 때까지 버린더 아가씨가 자유로이 제 방을 쓰도록 남겨 두기로. 그것이 이루어지고 나면, 그녀가 우리 나머지와 함께 그 결과를 지켜보는 데에 아무 반론도 있을 수 없다. 이튿날 아침, 그녀는 (원한다면) 블레이크 씨에게 나와의 그녀의 편지 왕래를 보여, 그가 그의 결백의 문제가 증명에 부쳐지기 전에 그녀의 평가에서 무죄로 여겨졌음을 그런 식으로 그에게 흡족히 여기게 할 것이다.\n\n그런 뜻으로, 나는 그녀에게 편지했다. 이것이 오늘 내가 할 수 있는 전부다. 내일 나는 베터리지 씨를 보고, 그 집을 다시 여는 데에 필요한 지시를 주어야 한다.\n\n유월 18일.—다시 늦게, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨를 찾아갔다. 이른 아침의 그 끔찍한 통증이 더 있었고, 이번에는, 몇 시간 동안의 완전한 탈진이 뒤따랐다. 나는, 그것이 나에게 물리는 벌에도, 백 번째로 그 아편으로 돌아가야 할 것을 내다본다. 만일 나 자신만 생각하면 된다면, 나는 그 무시무시한 꿈보다 그 날카로운 통증을 택하련다. 허나 그 몸의 괴로움이 나를 지치게 한다. 만일 내가 나 자신을 가라앉게 둔다면, 그것은 블레이크 씨가 나를 가장 필요로 하는 그때에 내가 그에게 쓸모없게 되는 것으로 끝날지도 모른다.\n\n오늘 내가 그 호텔에 갈 수 있기 전에 거의 한 시가 되었다. 그 방문은, 나의 산산조각 난 상태에서조차, 더없이 재미있는 것으로 드러났다—온전히 게이브리얼 베터리지가 그 자리에 있어 준 덕분에.\n\n내가 들어갔을 때, 나는 그가 방에 있는 것을 보았다. 내가 나의 환자에게 나의 첫 여느 물음을 던지는 동안, 그는 창가로 물러나 밖을 내다보았다. 블레이크 씨는 다시 잠을 잘 못 잤고, 그는 오늘 아침 그 안식의 손실을 여태껏보다 더 느꼈다.\n\n나는 다음으로 그가 브러프 씨에게서 소식을 들었는지 물었다.\n\n그날 아침 그에게 편지 한 통이 다다랐다. 브러프 씨는 그의 벗이자 의뢰인이 나의 조언에 따라 취하고 있는 그 방책에 더없이 강한 반대를 나타냈다. 그것은 해로웠다—결코 이루어지지 않을지도 모를 희망을 부추기니 말이다. 그것은, 그것이 최면술, 천리안 따위의 술수와 비슷한 한 조각 술수처럼 보인다는 것 말고는, 그의 마음에는 아주 알쏭달쏭했다. 그것은 버린더 아가씨의 집을 어수선하게 했고, 그것은 버린더 아가씨 자신을 어수선하게 하는 것으로 끝날 터였다. 그는 그 경우를 (이름은 대지 않고) 어느 저명한 내과의에게 내놓았다. 그리고 그 저명한 내과의는 미소 짓고, 고개를 젓고, 말했다—아무것도. 이런 근거에서, 브러프 씨는 그의 항변을 제기하고, 그것을 거기 두었다.\n\n나의 다음 물음은 그 다이아몬드라는 주제에 관련되었다. 그 변호사가 그 보석이 런던에 있음을 증명할 무슨 증거라도 내놓았던가?\n\n아니, 그 변호사는 그저 그 문제를 논하기를 마다했다. 그 자신은 그 월장석이 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔음을 흡족히 여겼다. 그의 저명한 자리를 비운 벗, 머스웨이트 씨(그의 인도인 기질에 대한 더없이 완숙한 앎을 아무도 부인할 수 없었다)도 마찬가지로 흡족히 여겼다. 이런 상황에서, 그리고 이미 그에게 걸린 숱한 요구가 있으니, 그는 증거라는 주제에 대한 어떤 다툼에도 들기를 마다해야 했다. 시간이 밝혀 줄 터였다. 그리고 브러프 씨는 기꺼이 시간을 기다렸다.\n\n그것은 아주 뚜렷했다—블레이크 씨가, 실제로 적힌 것을 읽는 대신 그 편지의 알맹이를 전함으로써 그것을 한층 더 뚜렷이 하지 않았더라도—나에 대한 불신이 이 모든 것의 밑바탕에 있음이. 나 자신이 그 결과를 미리 내다본 터라, 나는 언짢지도 놀랍지도 않았다. 나는 블레이크 씨에게 그의 벗의 항변이 그를 흔들어 놓았는지 물었다. 그는 힘주어, 그것이 그의 마음에 조금의 영향도 미치지 않았다고 대답했다. 나는 그 뒤로 브러프 씨를 헤아림에서 물릴 수 있었다—그리고 나는 그에 따라 그를 물렸다.\n\n우리 사이의 이야기에 잠시 멈춤이 뒤따랐다—그리고 게이브리얼 베터리지가 창가의 그의 은둔에서 나왔다.\n\n\"저에게 주의를 좀 베풀어 주시겠습니까, 선생?\" 하고 그가 나에게 말을 건네며 물었다.\n\n\"나는 아주 당신을 위해 있소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다.\n\n베터리지는 의자 하나를 가져와 그 탁자에 앉았다. 그는 그에 걸맞은 크기의 연필과 함께, 커다란 예스러운 가죽 수첩을 꺼냈다. 안경을 쓰고, 그는 빈 쪽에서 그 수첩을 펴고, 다시 한 번 나에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"저는 살아왔습니다,\" 하고 베터리지가 나를 엄히 보며 말했다. \"고인이 되신 저의 마님을 섬기며 거의 오십 년을. 그 전에는, 그분의 아버님이신 옛 나리를 섬기며 시동이었습지요. 저는 이제 일흔과 여든 사이 어딘가입니다—정확히 어디인지는 아무래도 좋습니다! 저는 대부분의 사람만큼 세상에 대한 제법 훌륭한 앎과 경험을 얻은 것으로 여겨집니다. 그런데 그 모든 것이 무엇으로 끝나나요? 그것은, 에즈라 제닝스 씨, 프랭클린 블레이크 나리에게, 아편팅크 한 병을 든 의사의 조수에 의해 요술 부리기가 행해지는 것으로 끝납니다—그리고 살아 계신 하느님을 걸고, 저는, 이 늙은 나이에, 요술쟁이의 조수 노릇을 하도록 임명됩니다!\"\n\n블레이크 씨가 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 나는 말하려 했다. 베터리지가 아직 안 끝났다는 표시로 손을 들었다.\n\n\"한마디도 마십시오, 제닝스 씨!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그건 당신에게서 한마디도 필요치 않습니다, 선생. 저에게는, 하느님 덕분에, 저의 원칙이 있습니다. 만일 저에게, 정신병원에서 나온 명령과 친형제뻘인 명령이 온다 해도, 그건 상관없습니다. 제가 그것을, 경우에 따라, 저의 주인이나 여주인에게서 받는 한, 저는 그것에 복종합니다. 저에게는 저 나름의 견해가 있을지도 모릅니다, 그것은 또한, 기억해 주시기 바랍니다만, 브러프 씨의—그 위대하신 브러프 씨의 견해이기도 합지요!\" 하고 베터리지가, 목소리를 높이고, 엄숙하게 나를 향해 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그건 상관없습니다. 저는 그럼에도 저의 견해를 거둡니다. 저의 젊은 아가씨께서 말씀하십니다, '그것을 하라.' 그리고 저는 말합니다, '아가씨, 그것은 이루어질 것입니다.' 여기 제가, 저의 수첩과 저의 연필을 들고 있습니다—뒤엣것은 제가 바랄 만큼 잘 뾰족하지는 않습니다만, 기독교인들이 제정신을 놓을 때, 연필이 그 뾰족함을 지키기를 누가 기대하겠습니까? 저에게 당신의 명령을 주십시오, 제닝스 씨. 저는 그것을 글로 받겠습니다, 선생. 저는 그것들에 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼도 뒤처지거나 앞서지 않기로 마음먹었습니다. 저는 눈먼 대리인입니다—그것이 바로 저입니다. 눈먼 대리인!\" 하고 베터리지가, 제 자신에 대한 제 서술을 한없이 흡족해하며 되뇌었다.\n\n\"참으로 안됐소,\" 하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"당신과 내가 뜻을 같이하지 못해서——\"\n\n\"저를 거기 끌어들이지 마십시오!\" 하고 베터리지가 끼어들었다. \"이것은 뜻을 같이하고 말고의 문제가 아니라, 복종의 문제입니다. 당신의 지시를 내리십시오, 선생—당신의 지시를 내리십시오!\"\n\n블레이크 씨가 나에게 그의 말을 곧이곧대로 받으라고 몸짓했다. 나는 되도록 평이하고 무겁게 \"나의 지시를 내렸다.\"\n\n\"나는 그 집의 어떤 부분들이 다시 열리기를 바라오,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그리고 이때쯤 지난해에 갖추어졌던 그대로 정확히 갖추어지기를.\"\n\n베터리지는 그의 불완전하게 뾰족한 연필을 그의 혀로 한 번 예비적으로 핥았다. \"그 부분들을 대시오, 제닝스 씨!\" 하고 그가 거만하게 말했다.\n\n\"먼저, 그 중앙 계단으로 이어지는 안쪽 현관홀.\"\n\n\"'먼저, 그 안쪽 현관홀,'\" 하고 베터리지가 적었다. \"우선, 그것을 지난해에 갖추어졌던 대로 갖추기란 불가능합니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"어째서요?\"\n\n\"지난해에 그 현관홀에 박제된 말똥가리가 있었기 때문입지요, 제닝스 씨. 그 집안이 떠날 때, 그 말똥가리는 다른 물건들과 함께 치워졌습니다. 그 말똥가리가 치워질 때—그것이 터졌습니다.\"\n\n\"그러면 그 말똥가리는 제외하겠소.\"\n\n베터리지는 그 예외를 적어 두었다. \"'그 안쪽 현관홀은 지난해에 갖추어진 대로 다시 갖출 것. 터진 말똥가리 하나만 제외함.' 계속하시지요, 제닝스 씨.\"\n\n\"그 계단에 융단을 전처럼 깔 것.\"\n\n\"'그 계단에 융단을 전처럼 깔 것.' 실망시켜 드려 안됐습니다, 선생. 허나 그것도 할 수 없습니다.\"\n\n\"어째서 안 되오?\"\n\n\"그 융단을 깐 그 사람이 죽었기 때문입지요, 제닝스 씨—그리고 융단과 모퉁이를 서로 잘 맞추는 데에 그만한 사람은, 어디를 보든, 온 잉글랜드에 없습니다.\"\n\n\"좋소. 우리는 잉글랜드에서 그다음으로 가장 뛰어난 사람을 시켜야겠소.\"\n\n베터리지는 또 하나를 적어 두었다. 그리고 나는 나의 지시를 계속 내렸다.\n\n\"버린더 아가씨의 응접실은 지난해의 그대로 정확히 되돌릴 것. 또한, 그 응접실에서 첫째 층계참으로 이어지는 복도. 또한, 둘째 층계참에서 그 가장 좋은 침실들로 이어지는 둘째 복도. 또한, 지난 유월 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 쓴 침실.\"\n\n베터리지의 뭉툭한 연필이 한 자 한 자 성실히 나를 뒤따랐다. \"계속하시지요, 선생,\" 하고 그가 빈정대는 무거움으로 말했다. \"이 연필 촉에는 아직 쓸 것이 잔뜩 남아 있습니다.\"\n\n나는 그에게 더는 내릴 지시가 없다고 말했다. \"선생,\" 하고 베터리지가 말했다. \"그런 경우, 저에게는 저 나름으로 짚어 둘 점이 한둘 있습니다.\" 그는 새 쪽에서 그 수첩을 펴고, 그 다함 없는 연필을 다시 한 번 예비적으로 핥았다.\n\n\"저는 알고 싶습니다,\" 하고 그가 입을 뗐다. \"제가, 어떤 책임들에서 손을 씻어도 되는지 아닌지를——\"\n\n\"당신은 정녕 그래도 되오,\" 하고 블레이크 씨가 말했다. \"내가 급사를 부르러 종을 울리겠소.\"\n\n\"——어떤 책임들에서 말입니다,\" 하고 베터리지가, 그 방에 자기와 나 말고는 아무도 없는 척, 흔들림 없이 이어 갔다. \"우선, 버린더 아가씨의 응접실에 관해서요. 우리가 지난해에 그 융단을 걷었을 때, 제닝스 씨, 우리는 놀라운 양의 핀을 발견했습니다. 제가 그 핀을 도로 놓는 데에 책임이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"물론 아니오.\"\n\n베터리지는 그 자리에서 그 양보를 적어 두었다.\n\n\"다음으로 그 첫째 복도에 관해서요,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"우리가 그 부분의 장식물을 옮겼을 때, 우리는 살진 벌거벗은 아이의 상 하나를 옮겼습니다—그 집의 목록에는 불경하게도 '큐피드, 사랑의 신'이라 적혀 있었습지요. 그는 지난해에 그의 어깨의 살진 부분에 날개가 둘 있었습니다. 제 눈이 잠시 그에게서 떨어진 사이, 그는 그중 하나를 잃었습니다. 제가 큐피드의 날개에 책임이 있습니까?\"\n\n나는 또 하나의 양보를 했고, 베터리지는 또 하나를 적어 두었다.\n\n\"그 둘째 복도에 관해서는,\" 하고 그가 계속했다. \"지난해에 거기에 그 방들의 문 말고는 (그 하나하나에 대해, 필요하다면, 제가 맹세할 수 있습니다) 아무것도 없었던 터라, 저는, 인정하거니와, 그 집의 그 부분에 관해서만은 마음이 편합니다. 허나, 프랭클린 나리의 침실에 관해서는 (만일 그것이 전의 그대로로 되돌려져야 한다면), 저는 알고 싶습니다, 그것이 아무리 자주 정돈되더라도, 그것을 영구히 어질러진 상태로 지키는 데에 누가 책임이 있는지를—그의 바지는 여기, 그의 수건은 저기, 그리고 그의 프랑스 소설은 어디에나 널려 있으니 말입니다. 저는 묻습니다, 프랭클린 나리의 방의 정돈됨을 어지럽히는 데에 누가 책임이 있는지, 그입니까 저입니까?\"\n\n블레이크 씨는 자기가 더없이 기쁜 마음으로 그 온 책임을 떠맡겠다고 선언했다. 베터리지는, 먼저 그것을 나의 재가와 승인에 부치지 않고서는, 그 어려움의 어떤 해결도 들으려 고집스레 마다했다. 나는 블레이크 씨의 제안을 받아들였다. 그리고 베터리지는 그 취지로 그 수첩에 마지막 항목을 적어 넣었다.\n\n\"내일부터, 원하실 때 들르십시오, 제닝스 씨,\" 하고 그가 다리를 세워 일어서며 말했다. \"당신은 제가, 저를 도울 필요한 사람들과 함께, 일하고 있는 것을 보실 겁니다. 저는 삼가 당신께 감사드립니다, 선생, 그 박제된 말똥가리의 경우와, 그 큐피드의 날개의 다른 경우를 눈감아 주신 데에 대해—또한 그 융단의 핀과, 프랭클린 나리의 방의 어질러짐에 관한 온갖 책임에서 제가 손을 씻도록 허락해 주신 데에 대해서도. 하인으로서 말하자면, 저는 당신께 깊이 빚졌습니다. 사람으로서 말하자면, 저는 당신을 머릿속이 구더기로 가득한 사람으로 여기며, 저는 당신의 실험이 하나의 미혹이자 덫이라는 저의 증언을 제기합니다. 그렇다고 해서, 하인으로서의 저의 의무를 사람으로서의 저의 감정이 가로막을까 두려워 마십시오! 당신은 복종받으실 겁니다. 그 구더기에도 불구하고, 선생, 당신은 복종받으실 겁니다. 만일 그것이 당신이 그 집에 불을 지르는 것으로 끝난다면, 당신이 먼저 종을 울려 그것들을 부르지 않는 한, 제가 소방차를 부른다면 빌어먹을 놈입니다!\"\n\n그 작별의 다짐과 함께, 그는 나에게 고개 숙여 인사하고, 그 방을 걸어 나갔다.\n\n\"우리가 그를 믿어도 될 것 같소?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"온전히,\" 하고 블레이크 씨가 대답했다. \"우리가 그 집에 가면, 우리는 아무것도 소홀히 되지 않고, 아무것도 잊히지 않은 것을 볼 것이오.\"\n\n유월 19일.—우리가 궁리한 일에 대한 또 하나의 항변! 이번에는 한 부인에게서.\n\n그날 아침 우편이 나에게 편지 두 통을 가져다주었다. 하나는 버린더 아가씨에게서 온 것으로, 더없이 다정하게, 내가 제안한 그 방안에 동의하는 것이었다. 다른 하나는 그녀가 그 보살핌 아래 살고 있는 그 부인—어느 메리듀 부인에게서 온 것이었다.\n\n메리듀 부인은 인사를 전하며, 내가 버린더 아가씨와 편지를 주고받아 온 그 주제를, 그것의 과학적 측면에서 이해하는 척하지 않는다. 허나, 그것의 사교적 측면에서 보면, 그녀는 마음 놓고 견해를 밝힌다. 짐작건대 나는, 메리듀 부인이 생각하기로, 버린더 아가씨가 겨우 열아홉 살임을 자각하지 못한다. 젊은 아가씨를, 그 나이에, 의학 실험이 행해지고 있는 사내들로 가득한 집에 (\"샤프롱\" 없이) 자리하게 두는 것은, 메리듀 부인이 도저히 허락할 수 없는 예절에 대한 능욕이다. 만일 그 일이 진행되도록 허락된다면, 그녀는—제 자신의 개인적 편의의 심각한 희생을 무릅쓰고—버린더 아가씨를 요크셔로 동행하는 것을 자기의 도리로 느낄 것이다. 이런 상황에서, 그녀는 감히, 내가 부디 그 주제를 다시 헤아려 주기를 청한다. 버린더 아가씨가 나의 견해 말고는 어떤 견해에도 이끌리기를 마다하는 것을 보아서 말이다. 그녀의 자리함은 도저히 필요할 수 없다. 그리고 그런 취지의, 나로부터의 한마디가, 메리듀 부인과 나 자신을 아주 불쾌한 책임에서 덜어 줄 것이다.\n\n정중한 상투어에서 쉬운 영어로 옮기면, 이것의 뜻은, 내가 그것을 새기기로는, 메리듀 부인이 세상의 견해를 죽도록 두려워한다는 것이다. 그녀는 안타깝게도, 그 견해를 존중으로 여길 아무 이유도 없는, 살아 있는 그야말로 마지막 사람에게 호소한 것이다. 나는 버린더 아가씨를 실망시키지 않겠다. 그리고 나는 서로 사랑하고, 이미 너무 오래 갈라져 있던 두 젊은이 사이의 화해를 미루지 않겠다. 쉬운 영어에서 정중한 상투어로 옮기면, 이것의 뜻은, 제닝스 씨가 메리듀 부인에게 인사를 전하며, 그가 그 일에 조금이라도 더 끼어드는 것이 정당하다고 느낄 수 없어 유감이라는 것이다.\n\n블레이크 씨의 오늘 아침 제 자신에 대한 보고는 이전과 같았다. 우리는 오늘 그 집에서 베터리지를 살펴봄으로써 그를 방해하지 않기로 정했다. 내일이면 우리의 첫 점검 방문에 넉넉히 이를 것이다.\n\n유월 20일.—블레이크 씨가 밤의 그의 계속되는 안절부절못함을 느끼기 시작하고 있다. 이제, 그 방들이 다시 갖추어질수록, 그만큼 좋다.\n\n오늘 아침, 그 집으로 가는 길에, 그는, 얼마쯤의 신경질적인 조바심과 우유부단함을 안고, (런던에서 그에게 부쳐진) 그가 커프 부장에게서 받은 편지에 대해 나와 상의했다.\n\n그 부장은 아일랜드에서 쓴다. 그는 블레이크 씨가 도킹 근처의 그의 거처에 남긴 명함과 전갈의 (그의 가정부를 통한) 수령을 인정하며, 그의 잉글랜드로의 귀환이 일주일이나 그 이내에 이루어질 공산이 크다고 알린다. 그동안, 그는 블레이크 씨가 (그 전갈에 진술된 대로) 그 월장석이라는 주제로 그와 이야기하고 싶어 하는 이유를 알려 주기를 청한다. 만일 블레이크 씨가, 그의 지난해의 그 다이아몬드에 관한 조사 과정에서, 그가 무슨 심각한 잘못을 저질렀음을 입증할 수 있다면, 그는 (고인이 된 레이디 버린더에게 후하게 대접받은 뒤로) 그 신사가 마음대로 부리도록 자신을 내놓는 것을 도리로 여길 것이다. 그렇지 않다면, 그는 시골 삶의 평화로운 원예의 매력에 둘러싸여, 그의 은거에 남아 있기를 청한다.\n\n그 편지를 읽고서, 나는 블레이크 씨에게, 답으로, 지난해에 그 조사가 중단된 이래 일어난 온갖 것을 커프 부장에게 알리고, 그로 하여금 그 명백한 사실에서 스스로 결론을 이끌어 내도록 두라고 조언하는 데에 아무 망설임이 없었다.\n\n곰곰이 다시 생각한 끝에 나는 또한, 그 부장이 우리와 합류할 만큼 제때 잉글랜드로 돌아올 경우에, 그를 그 실험에 자리하도록 청할 것을 제안했다. 그는, 어쨌든, 두어 값진 증인이 될 터였다. 그리고, 만일 그 다이아몬드가 블레이크 씨의 방에 감춰져 있다고 믿는 데에서 내가 틀린 것으로 드러난다면, 내가 아무런 통제도 발휘할 수 없는, 그 일의 나중 단계에서 그의 조언이 크게 중요할지도 몰랐다. 이 마지막 헤아림이 블레이크 씨를 결심하게 하는 듯했다. 그는 나의 조언을 따르겠다고 약속했다.\n\n망치 소리가, 우리가 그 집으로 이어지는 그 진입로에 들어서면서, 다시 갖추는 일이 한창임을 우리에게 알렸다.\n\n이 날을 위해 어부의 붉은 모자와 초록 나사 앞치마 차림을 한 베터리지가, 그 바깥 현관홀에서 우리를 맞았다. 그가 나를 본 순간, 그는 그 수첩과 연필을 꺼내어, 내가 그에게 말하는 온갖 것을 적어 두겠다고 고집스레 우겼다. 어디를 보든, 우리는, 블레이크 씨가 예언한 대로, 그 일이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 빠르고 지혜롭게 진척되고 있는 것을 보았다. 허나 그 안쪽 현관홀과, 버린더 아가씨의 방에는 아직 할 일이 많았다. 그 집이 이번 주말 전에 우리를 위해 채비될지는 미심쩍은 듯했다.\n\n베터리지에게 그가 이룬 진척에 대해 축하하고 (그는 내가 입을 열 때마다 적어 두기를 고집했으며, 동시에, 블레이크 씨가 하는 말에는 조금의 주의도 기울이기를 마다했다), 하루나 이틀 안에 둘째 점검 방문을 하러 돌아오겠다고 약속하고서, 우리는 뒷길로 나가, 그 집을 떠날 채비를 했다. 우리가 아래층 통로를 미처 벗어나기 전에, 나는 막 그 자신의 방으로 이어지는 문을 지나가는데, 베터리지에게 붙들렸다.\n\n\"당신께 사사로이 두어 마디 드려도 될까요?\" 하고 그가, 알쏭달쏭한 속삭임으로 물었다.\n\n나는 물론 그러자고 했다. 블레이크 씨는 정원에서 나를 기다리려 걸어갔고, 나는 베터리지를 따라 그의 방으로 들어갔다. 나는, 그 박제된 말똥가리와 큐피드의 날개의 경우에 이미 세워진 선례를 좇아, 어떤 새로운 양보에 대한 요구를 온전히 예상했다. 나의 큰 놀라움으로, 베터리지는 은근히 내 팔에 손을 얹고, 이 유별난 물음을 나에게 던졌다.\n\n\"제닝스 씨, 당신은 혹시 「로빈슨 크루소」를 아십니까?\"\n\n나는 어렸을 때 「로빈슨 크루소」를 읽었다고 대답했다.\n\n\"그 뒤로는 안 읽으셨고요?\" 하고 베터리지가 물었다.\n\n\"그 뒤로는 안 읽었소.\"\n\n그는 몇 걸음 물러나, 미신적인 경외로 누그러진, 가엾이 여기는 호기심의 표정으로 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"그는 어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽었어,\" 하고 베터리지가, 나에게가 아니라 자기 자신에게 말했다. \"「로빈슨 크루소」가 이제 그에게 어떻게 와닿는지 시험해 보자!\"\n\n그는 한 모퉁이의 찬장을 열고, 더럽고 귀퉁이가 접힌 책 한 권을 꺼냈는데, 그것은 그가 책장을 넘길 때 케케묵은 담배의 강한 냄새를 뿜었다. 겉보기에 그가 찾던 한 대목을 찾고서, 그는 나에게 그 모퉁이에서 그와 합류하기를 청했다. 여전히 알쏭달쏭하게 은근하고, 여전히 숨죽여 말하며.\n\n\"당신의, 아편팅크와 프랭클린 블레이크 나리에 관한 이 요술에 관해서 말입니다, 선생,\" 하고 그가 입을 뗐다. \"일꾼들이 그 집에 있는 동안은, 하인으로서의 저의 의무가 사람으로서의 저의 감정을 이깁니다. 일꾼들이 가고 나면, 사람으로서의 저의 감정이 하인으로서의 저의 의무를 이깁지요. 좋습니다. 지난밤에, 제닝스 씨, 이 당신의 새로운 의학적 사업이 나쁘게 끝나리라는 것이 저의 마음에 세차게 밀려들었습니다. 만일 제가 그 은밀한 명령에 굴복했다면, 저는 제 손으로 온갖 세간을 다시 치워 버리고, 이튿날 아침 일꾼들이 왔을 때 그들을 그 집 밖으로 내쫓았을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"내가 위층에서 본 것으로 보아,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신이 그 은밀한 명령에 맞선 것을 알게 되어 기쁘오.\"\n\n\"맞섰다는 것은 옳은 말이 아닙니다,\" 하고 베터리지가 대답했다. \"씨름했다가 옳은 말입지요. 저는 씨름했습니다, 선생, 제 가슴속에서 저를 한쪽으로 당기는 그 말 없는 명령과, 제 수첩 속에서 저를 다른 쪽으로 미는 그 글로 된 명령 사이에서, (외람되오나) 제가 식은땀이 날 때까지요. 그 끔찍한 마음의 소란과 몸의 늘어짐 속에서, 저는 무슨 약에 기댔을까요? 지난 삼십 년 넘게 여태 저를 저버린 적이 없는 그 약에요, 선생—이 책에요!\"\n\n그는 그 책을 편 손으로 소리 나게 한 대 치더니, 그것에서 여느 때보다 더 강한 케케묵은 담배 냄새를 쳐 냈다.\n\n\"제가 여기서 무엇을 찾았을까요,\" 하고 베터리지가 이어 갔다. \"제가 편 첫 쪽에서? 이 무시무시한 대목입니다, 선생, 백일흔여덟 쪽에, 이렇게—'이러한, 그리고 이와 비슷한 숱한 성찰에 따라, 나는 그 뒤로 이것을 나의 확고한 규칙으로 삼았다, 곧 내가 눈앞에 나타난 어떤 일을 하거나 안 하도록, 또는 이 길로 가거나 저 길로 가도록 하는 그 은밀한 암시나 마음의 재촉을 느낄 때는 언제든, 나는 어김없이 그 은밀한 명령에 복종했다.' 이 몸이 빵을 먹고 사는 것이 진실이듯, 제닝스 씨, 그것이 바로 제가 그 은밀한 명령을 거스르던 그때에 정확히, 제 눈에 든 첫마디였습니다! 당신은 거기서 조금이라도 예사롭지 않은 무언가가 안 보이시나요, 선생?\"\n\n\"나는 우연의 일치가 보이오—그 이상은 아무것도.\"\n\n\"당신은 이 당신의 의학적 사업에 관해, 조금도, 제닝스 씨, 흔들리지 않으시나요?\"\n\n\"세상없어도 조금도.\"\n\n베터리지는 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에, 나를 뚫어지게 노려보았다. 그는 아주 신중하게 그 책을 덮었다. 그는 유별난 조심으로 그것을 다시 찬장에 넣고 잠갔다. 그는 홱 돌아서, 다시 한 번 나를 뚫어지게 노려보았다. 그러고는 그가 말했다.\n\n\"선생,\" 하고 그가 무겁게 말했다. \"어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽은 사람에게는 크게 헤아려 줄 것이 있지요. 저는 당신께 안녕을 빕니다.\"\n\n그는 나직이 고개 숙여 인사하며 그의 문을 열고, 내가 정원으로 가는 나의 길을 스스로 찾도록 놓아주었다. 나는 그 집으로 돌아오는 블레이크 씨와 마주쳤다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 있었는지 나에게 말할 것 없소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"베터리지가 그의 마지막 패를 내밀었소. 그는 「로빈슨 크루소」에서 또 하나의 예언적 발견을 한 것이오. 당신은 그가 아끼는 그 미혹을 받아 주었소? 아니라고? 당신은 그로 하여금 당신이 「로빈슨 크루소」를 믿지 않음을 알게 했소? 제닝스 씨! 당신은 베터리지의 평가에서 있을 수 있는 가장 낮은 자리로 떨어졌소. 앞으로는, 원하는 대로 말하고, 원하는 대로 하시오. 당신은 그가 이제 당신에게 한마디도 더 허비하지 않으리라는 것을 알게 될 것이오.\"\n\n유월 21일.—오늘은 나의 일기에 짧은 항목으로 넉넉해야겠다.\n\n블레이크 씨는 여태 지낸 중 가장 나쁜 밤을 지냈다. 나는, 몹시 내키지 않았으나, 그에게 약을 처방하지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 예민한 체질의 사람들은 다행히 약의 효과를 빨리 느낀다. 그렇지 않았다면, 나는 그것을 시도할 때가 왔을 때 그가 그 실험에 온전히 부적합할까 두려워하는 쪽으로 기울었을 것이다.\n\n나 자신으로 말하자면, 지난 이틀 동안 나의 통증이 얼마쯤 뜸해진 뒤, 나는 오늘 아침 발작이 있었는데, 그것에 대해서는, 그것이 나로 하여금 그 아편으로 돌아가기로 정하게 했다는 것 말고는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다. 나는 이 책을 덮고, 나의 온전한 양—오백 방울을 취하겠다.\n\n유월 22일.—우리의 전망이 오늘 더 나아 보인다. 블레이크 씨의 신경의 괴로움이 크게 가라앉았다. 그는 지난밤 조금 잤다. 나의 밤은, 그 아편 덕분에, 얼이 빠진 사람의 밤이었다. 나는 오늘 아침 깨어났다고 말할 수 없다. 더 걸맞은 표현은, 내가 나의 지각을 되찾았다는 것이겠다.\n\n우리는 그 다시 갖추기가 끝났는지 보러 그 집으로 마차를 몰았다. 그것은 내일—토요일이면 마쳐질 것이다. 블레이크 씨가 예언한 대로, 베터리지는 더는 아무 장애도 세우지 않았다. 처음부터 끝까지, 그는 불길하게 정중했고, 불길하게 말이 없었다.\n\n나의 의학적 사업은 (베터리지가 그것을 그리 부른다) 이제, 어쩔 수 없이, 다음 월요일까지 미뤄져야 한다. 내일 저녁 일꾼들이 그 집에서 늦게까지 있을 것이다. 그 이튿날은, 이 자유로운 나라의 제도의 하나인 그 굳어진 일요일의 폭정이, 기차 시간을 어찌나 잡아 놓았던지 누구에게든 런던에서 우리에게로 여행해 달라고 청하기가 불가능하다. 월요일이 올 때까지, 블레이크 씨를 조심스레 지켜보고, 가능하다면, 그를 오늘 내가 그를 보는 것과 같은 상태로 지키는 것 말고는 할 일이 없다.\n\n그동안, 나는 그를 설득하여, 브러프 씨에게, 그가 증인 가운데 하나로 자리하는 것을 요점으로 삼아 편지하게 했다. 나는 그 변호사를 각별히 고르는데, 그가 우리에게 강하게 편견을 지녔기 때문이다. 만일 우리가 그를 납득시킨다면, 우리는 우리의 승리를 다툼의 여지 너머에 두는 것이다.\n\n블레이크 씨는 커프 부장에게도 편지했다. 그리고 나는 버린더 아가씨에게 몇 줄을 보냈다. 이들과, 그리고 늙은 베터리지(그는 정말로 그 집안에서 중요한 사람이다)와 함께, 우리는 그 목적에 넉넉한 증인을 갖게 될 것이다—메리듀 부인이, 만일 메리듀 부인이 세상의 견해에 자신을 희생하기를 고집한다면, 그녀를 넣지 않고서라도.\n\n유월 23일.—그 아편의 앙갚음이 지난밤 다시 나를 덮쳤다. 아무래도 좋다. 나는 월요일이 지나 갈 때까지 이제 그것을 계속해야 한다.\n\n블레이크 씨가 오늘 다시 그리 좋지 않다. 오늘 아침 두 시에, 그는 자기가 여송연을 넣어 둔 서랍을 열었음을 털어놓는다. 그는 격렬한 안간힘으로 겨우 그것을 다시 잠갔다. 유혹에 대비한 그의 다음 조치는, 그 열쇠를 창밖으로 던지는 것이었다. 급사가 오늘 아침 그것을, 빈 물통 바닥에서 찾아내어 들고 왔다—운명이란 이런 것이다! 나는 다음 화요일까지 그 열쇠를 맡았다.\n\n유월 24일.—블레이크 씨와 나는 뚜껑 없는 마차로 오래 마차를 탔다. 우리는 둘 다 그 부드러운 여름 공기의 축복 어린 영향을 이롭게 느꼈다. 나는 그 호텔에서 그와 저녁을 들었다. 나의 큰 안도로—나는 오늘 아침 그가 과로하고 과도하게 흥분한 상태에 있음을 보았으므로—그는 저녁 뒤에 소파에서 두 시간 곤히 잤다. 만일 그가 이제 또 한 번 나쁜 밤을 지낸다면—나는 그 결과가 두렵지 않다.\n\n유월 25일, 월요일.—그 실험의 날! 오후 다섯 시다. 우리는 방금 그 집에 다다랐다.\n\n첫째이자 무엇보다도 앞선 물음은, 블레이크 씨의 건강의 물음이다.\n\n내가 판단할 수 있는 한으로는, 그는 (몸으로 말하여) 오늘 밤 이때쯤 지난해에 그랬던 만큼이나 아편의 작용에 예민하리라 약속한다. 그는, 오늘 오후, 신경의 짜증에 딱 못 미친 신경의 예민함의 상태에 있다. 그는 어렵잖게 낯빛이 변한다. 그의 손은 아주 흔들림 없지는 않다. 그리고 그는 우연한 소리에, 그리고 사람과 물건의 뜻밖의 나타남에 소스라친다.\n\n이 결과들은 죄 잠의 결핍으로 자아내진 것인데, 그것은 다시, 그 버릇이 극단으로 밀린 뒤의, 담배 피우는 버릇의 별안간의 멈춤의 신경적 결과다. 여기 지난해에 작용한 것과 같은 원인들이 다시 작용하고 있다. 그리고 여기, 겉보기에, 같은 결과들이 있다. 그 최종의 시험이 시도되었을 때, 그 나란함이 여전히 들어맞을 것인가? 그 밤의 사건들이 결정해야 한다.\n\n내가 이 글줄들을 쓰는 동안, 블레이크 씨는 그 안쪽 현관홀의 당구대에서, 지난 유월 그가 이 집의 손님이었을 때 그것들을 연습하던 대로, 그 놀이의 갖가지 타법을 연습하며, 스스로를 즐기고 있다. 나는 나의 일기를 여기 가져왔는데, 얼마쯤은 내가 지금부터 내일 아침 사이에 틀림없이 손에 갖게 될 그 한가한 시간을 채울 셈에서, 얼마쯤은 그때에 내가 기록해 둘 값어치가 있을 무언가가 일어날지도 모른다는 희망에서다.\n\n여기까지 내가 무언가 빠뜨렸는가? 어제의 항목을 흘긋 보니, 내가 아침 우편의 다다름을 적어 두는 것을 잊었음을 알겠다. 지금으로서는 이 낱장들을 덮고 블레이크 씨와 합류하기 전에, 이것을 바로잡아 두자.\n\n그러니, 나는 어제, 버린더 아가씨에게서 몇 줄을 받았다. 그녀는, 내가 권한 대로, 오후 기차로 여행하기로 했다. 메리듀 부인이 그녀를 동행하겠다고 우겼다. 그 쪽지는, 그 노부인의 대체로 훌륭한 성미가 조금 뒤틀렸음을 넌지시 일러 주며, 그녀의 나이와 그녀의 버릇을 헤아려, 그녀에게 온갖 마땅한 너그러움을 베풀어 주기를 청한다. 나는, 메리듀 부인과의 나의 관계에서, 베터리지가 나와의 그의 관계에서 내보이는 그 절제를 본받으려 애쓰겠다. 그는 오늘, 불길하게도 그의 가장 좋은 검은 정장과, 그의 가장 뻣뻣한 흰 넥타이 차림으로 우리를 맞았다. 그는 내 쪽을 볼 때마다, 내가 어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽었음을 떠올리고, 삼가 나를 가엾이 여긴다.\n\n어제, 또한, 블레이크 씨는 그 변호사의 답을 받았다. 브러프 씨는 그 초대를—항변과 함께—받아들인다. 그가 생각하기로, 예사로운 분별의 평균치를 지닌 신사가, 우리가 감히 그리 부르자면, 그 제안된 구경거리의 현장으로 버린더 아가씨를 동행하는 것이 명백히 필요하다. 더 나은 호위가 없어, 브러프 씨 자신이 그 신사가 될 것이다.—그리하여 여기 가엾은 버린더 아가씨에게 두 \"샤프롱\"이 갖추어졌다. 세상의 견해가 이것으로는 틀림없이 만족되리라 생각하니 안도가 된다!\n\n커프 부장에게서는 아무 소식도 들리지 않았다. 그는 두말할 것 없이 아직 아일랜드에 있다. 우리는 오늘 밤 그를 볼 것을 기대하지 말아야 한다.\n\n베터리지가 방금 들어와, 블레이크 씨가 나를 찾는다고 말했다. 나는 지금으로서는 나의 펜을 내려놓아야 한다.\n\n일곱 시.—우리는 다시 그 새로 갖춘 방들과 계단들을 죄 둘러보았다. 그리고 우리는, 블레이크 씨가 지난번 여기 있었을 때 그가 즐기던 산책이었던 그 관목숲에서 기분 좋은 산책을 했다. 이런 식으로, 나는 그의 마음속에 장소와 물건들의 옛 인상을 되도록 생생하게 되살리기를 바란다.\n\n우리는 이제, 지난해에 그 생일 만찬이 열린 바로 그 시각에, 저녁을 들려 한다. 나의 목적은, 물론, 이 경우에는 순전히 의학적인 것이다. 그 아편팅크는, 되도록 가까이, 그것이 지난해에 소화 과정을 찾았던 그 지점에서 소화 과정을 찾아야 한다.\n\n저녁 뒤의 알맞은 때에 나는 그 대화를 다시—되도록 꾸밈없이—그 다이아몬드라는 주제로, 그리고 그것을 훔치려는 인도인의 음모라는 주제로 도로 이끌 작정이다. 내가 그의 마음을 이 화제들로 채우고 나면, 나는 그에게 그 둘째 번 약을 주는 때가 오기 전에 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 온갖 것을 다한 셈일 것이다.\n\n여덟 시 반.—나는 바로 이 순간에야 가장 중요한 그 온갖 의무에 마음 쓸 기회를 찾았다. 곧, 지난해에 캔디 씨가 쓴 그 아편팅크를 찾아, 그 집안의 약장을 들여다볼 의무를.\n\n십 분 전에, 나는 손 비어 있는 순간의 베터리지를 붙들어, 그에게 내가 원하는 것을 말했다. 한마디 반대도 없이, 그의 수첩을 꺼내려는 시도조차 없이, 그는 (걸음마다 나를 헤아려 주며) 그 약장이 보관된 그 저장실로 앞장서 갔다.\n\n나는 그 병을, 가죽으로 덮어 묶인 유리 마개로 조심스레 지켜진 채 찾아냈다. 그것이 담은 그 조제약은, 내가 예상한 대로, 여느 아편팅크였다. 그 병이 아직 잘 차 있는 것을 보고, 나는, 위급에 대비하여 내가 마련해 둔 그 두 조제약 중 어느 것을 쓰기보다, 그것을 쓰기로 정했다.\n\n내가 투여할 그 양의 물음은 어떤 어려움을 내놓는다. 나는 그것을 곰곰이 헤아려, 그 양을 늘리기로 정했다.\n\n나의 기록은 나에게 캔디 씨가 겨우 스물다섯 미님을 투여했음을 알려 준다. 이것은 그 뒤따른 결과를—블레이크 씨처럼 예민한 사람의 경우에조차—자아냈다기엔 적은 양이다. 나는, 내가 아는 대로, 그가 식탁의 즐거움을 예리하게 좋아하고, 그가 그 생일에 저녁 뒤에 그 아편팅크를 재어 냈음을 아니, 캔디 씨가 자기가 준 것으로 여긴 것보다 더 주었을 공산이 크다고 생각한다. 어쨌든, 나는 그 양을 마흔 미님으로 늘리는 위험을 무릅쓰겠다. 이번에는, 블레이크 씨가 자기가 그 아편팅크를 취하려 함을 미리 아는데—그것은, 생리학적으로 말하여, 그가 (그 자신은 자각하지 못한 채) 그 효과에 저항할 어떤 능력을 제 안에 지니는 것과 맞먹는다. 만일 나의 견해가 옳다면, 따라서, 이번에는, 지난해에 그 적은 양이 자아낸 결과를 되풀이하려면 더 많은 양이 반드시 필요하다.\n\n열 시.—그 증인들, 또는 그 손님들이 (나는 그들을 무엇이라 부를까?) 한 시간 전에 그 집에 다다랐다.\n\n아홉 시 조금 전에, 나는 블레이크 씨를 설득하여 나와 함께 그의 침실로 가게 했다. 이유로, 그 방을 다시 갖추는 데에 아무것도 잊히지 않았음을 아주 확실히 하려고, 그가 그것을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보기를 바란다고 대며. 나는 미리 베터리지와, 브러프 씨를 위해 채비된 그 침실이 블레이크 씨의 방 옆방이어야 한다는 것과, 문을 두드림으로써 나에게 그 변호사의 다다름이 알려져야 한다는 것을 짜 두었다. 그 현관홀의 시계가 아홉 시를 친 지 오 분 뒤에, 나는 그 두드림을 들었다. 그리고 대뜸 나가서, 그 복도에서 브러프 씨와 마주쳤다.\n\n나의 겉모습이 (여느 때처럼) 나에게 불리하게 작용했다. 브러프 씨의 불신이 브러프 씨의 눈에서 자못 또렷이 나를 바라보았다. 낯선 이들에게 이 효과를 자아내는 데에 익숙한지라, 나는 그 변호사가 블레이크 씨의 방으로 들어가기 전에, 내가 하고 싶은 말을 하는 데에 한순간도 망설이지 않았다.\n\n\"당신은, 내가 믿기로, 메리듀 부인과 버린더 아가씨와 함께 여기 여행해 왔지요?\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"그렇소,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 되도록 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다.\n\n\"버린더 아가씨가 아마 당신에게, 내가 그녀의 그 집에 있음이 (그리고 물론 메리듀 부인의 있음이), 그에 대한 나의 실험이 먼저 시도되기까지, 블레이크 씨에게 비밀로 지켜지기를 바란다고 말했겠지요?\"\n\n\"나는 내가 입을 다물어야 함을 아오, 선생!\" 하고 브러프 씨가 조바심을 내며 말했다. \"인간의 어리석음이라는 주제에 대해서는 늘 입을 다무는지라, 나는 이 경우에 그만큼 더 어렵잖게 나의 입술을 다문 채로 지킬 수 있소. 그것이 당신을 만족시키오?\"\n\n나는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 베터리지에게 그를 그의 방으로 안내하도록 두었다. 베터리지는 헤어질 때 나에게 한 눈길을 주었는데, 그것은, 마치 그야말로 이런 말로 하듯, 이렇게 말했다, \"당신은 만만찮은 사람을 붙들었소, 제닝스 씨—그리고 그의 이름은 브러프요.\"\n\n다음으로 그 두 부인과의 만남을 끝내야 했다. 나는—인정하거니와, 조금 신경이 곤두선 채로—버린더 아가씨의 응접실로 가는 길에 그 계단을 내려갔다.\n\n(그 부인들의 시중을 맡은) 정원사의 아내가 그 이 층 복도에서 나를 맞았다. 이 훌륭한 여자는 나를, 뚜렷이 그야말로 공포에서 태어난 지나친 정중함으로 대한다. 그녀는 내가 말할 때마다 빤히 쳐다보고, 떨고, 무릎을 굽혀 인사한다. 내가 버린더 아가씨를 청하자, 그녀는 빤히 쳐다보고, 떨고, 두말할 것 없이 다음으로 무릎을 굽혀 인사했을 것이나, 버린더 아가씨 자신이 별안간 제 응접실 문을 엶으로써 그 의례를 짧게 끊지 않았다면 말이다.\n\n\"제닝스 씨인가요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n내가 미처 답하기도 전에, 그녀는 그 복도에서 나에게 이야기하려 간절히 나왔다. 우리는 벽걸이 등의 불빛 아래에서 마주했다. 나를 처음 본 순간, 버린더 아가씨는 멈추어, 망설였다. 그녀는 대뜸 자신을 다잡고, 잠시 낯을 붉혔다—그러고는, 매혹적인 솔직함으로, 나에게 그녀의 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"나는 당신을 낯선 이처럼 대할 수 없어요, 제닝스 씨,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"오, 당신의 편지가 나를 얼마나 행복하게 했는지 당신이 알기만 한다면!\"\n\n그녀는 나의 못난 주름진 얼굴을, 내 동료 인간들에 대한 나의 경험에서 그토록 새로운 밝은 고마움을 담고 바라보았는데, 나는 그녀에게 어찌 답해야 할지 막막했다. 그 무엇도 나를 그녀의 친절과 그녀의 아름다움에 대해 채비시켜 놓지 않았었다. 여러 해의 비참함이 나의 마음을 굳히지는 못했다, 하느님께 감사하게도. 나는, 마치 내가 십 대의 소년이기라도 한 양, 그녀와 함께 있으니 어색하고 수줍었다.\n\n\"그가 지금 어디 있어요?\" 하고 그녀가, 그녀의 하나의 압도적인 관심—블레이크 씨에 대한 관심을 거리낌 없이 나타내며 물었다. \"그가 무엇을 하고 있어요? 그가 나에 대해 말했어요? 그가 기분이 좋아요? 그가 지난해에 그 집에서 일어난 일을 겪고서, 그 집을 보는 것을 어떻게 견뎌요? 언제 그에게 그 아편팅크를 줄 거예요? 당신이 그것을 따르는 것을 내가 봐도 될까요? 나는 어찌나 관심이 이는지, 어찌나 흥분되는지—당신에게 할 말이 만 가지나 있는데, 그것들이 죄 한데 몰려서 나는 무엇을 먼저 말해야 할지 모르겠어요. 내가 이것에 두는 관심에 당신은 놀라나요?\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 감히 내가 그것을 낱낱이 이해한다고 생각하오.\"\n\n그녀는 어리둥절해하는 척하는 그 하찮은 꾸밈보다 훨씬 위에 있었다. 그녀는 마치 오빠나 아버지에게 답하듯 나에게 답했다.\n\n\"당신은 나를 이루 말할 수 없는 비참함에서 덜어 주었어요. 당신은 나에게 새 삶을 주었어요. 어찌 내가, 당신에게 무언가를 감출 만큼 은혜를 모를 수 있겠어요? 나는 그를 사랑해요,\" 하고 그녀가 소박하게 말했다. \"나는 처음부터 끝까지 그를 사랑해 왔어요—내가 내 자신의 생각 속에서 그에게 잘못하고 있을 때조차, 내가 그에게 가장 모질고 가장 잔인한 말을 하고 있을 때조차요. 거기에, 나에게 무슨 변명거리가 있을까요? 나는 있기를 바라요—안됐지만 그것이 내가 가진 유일한 변명거리 같아요. 내일이 와서, 그가 내가 그 집에 있음을 알면, 당신은 생각하나요——\"\n\n그녀는 다시 멈추어, 아주 간절히 나를 바라보았다.\n\n\"내일이 오면,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신은 방금 나에게 말한 것을 그에게 말하기만 하면 된다고 나는 생각하오.\"\n\n그녀의 얼굴이 환해졌다. 그녀는 나에게 한 걸음 더 다가왔다. 그녀의 손가락이, 내가 정원에서 꺾어 내 웃옷의 단춧구멍에 꽂았던 꽃 한 송이를 신경질적으로 만지작거렸다.\n\n\"당신은 근래에 그를 많이 봤어요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은, 정말이지 참으로, 그것을 봤나요?\"\n\n\"정말이지 참으로,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"나는 내일 무슨 일이 일어날지 아주 확신하오. 나는 오늘 밤 무슨 일이 일어날지도 그만큼 확신할 수 있으면 좋으련만.\"\n\n그 대화의 그 지점에서, 우리는 다과 쟁반을 든 베터리지의 나타남에 방해받았다. 그는 그 응접실로 들어가면서 나에게 또 한 번 뜻깊은 눈길을 주었다. \"옳지! 옳지! 해가 비칠 때 건초를 만드시오. 그 만만찮은 사람이 위층에 있소, 제닝스 씨—그 만만찮은 사람이 위층에 있소!\"\n\n우리는 그를 따라 그 방으로 들어갔다. 한 모퉁이의, 아주 곱게 차려입고, 세련된 자수 한 조각에 아주 깊이 빠져 있던 자그마한 노부인이, 나의 집시 같은 낯빛과 나의 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼을 처음 본 순간, 무릎에 제 일감을 떨어뜨리고, 희미하고 자그마한 비명을 내질렀다.\n\n\"메리듀 부인,\" 하고 버린더 아가씨가 말했다. \"이분이 제닝스 씨예요.\"\n\n\"제닝스 씨의 용서를 빕니다,\" 하고 그 노부인이, 버린더 아가씨를 보며, 나에게 말했다. \"기차 여행은 늘 저를 초조하게 만듭니다. 저는 여느 때처럼 스스로를 바쁘게 함으로써 제 마음을 잠재우려 애쓰는 중이에요. 이 유별난 자리에서, 제 자수가 어울리지 않는 것인지 저는 모르겠어요. 만일 그것이 제닝스 씨의 의학적 견해를 방해한다면, 저는 물론 기꺼이 그것을 치우겠어요.\"\n\n나는, 꼭 내가 그 터진 말똥가리와 그 큐피드의 날개의 부재를 승인했듯이, 서둘러 그 자수의 자리함을 승인했다. 메리듀 부인은 나의 머리칼을 보려 한 번 애를 썼다—고마운 애씀을. 아니! 그것은 될 일이 아니었다. 메리듀 부인은 다시 버린더 아가씨를 돌아보았다.\n\n\"만일 제닝스 씨가 저에게 허락해 주신다면,\" 하고 그 노부인이 이어 갔다. \"저는 청 하나를 하고 싶어요. 제닝스 씨는 오늘 밤 과학 실험을 하려 하십니다. 저는 학교 다니는 계집아이였을 때 과학 실험에 참관하곤 했어요. 그것들은 어김없이 폭발로 끝났지요. 만일 제닝스 씨가 아주 친절히 해 주신다면, 저는 이번에는 그 폭발을 미리 경고받고 싶어요. 가능하다면, 제가 잠자리에 들기 전에 그것을 끝내 버릴 셈으로요.\"\n\n나는 메리듀 부인에게, 이번에는 폭발이 그 순서에 들어 있지 않다고 다짐하려 했다.\n\n\"아니요,\" 하고 그 노부인이 말했다. \"저는 제닝스 씨께 아주 고마워요—저는 그가 오직 저 자신의 좋음을 위해 저를 속이고 있음을 알아요. 저는 솔직한 대함을 좋아해요. 저는 그 폭발에 아주 체념했어요—허나 저는 정말로, 가능하다면, 제가 잠자리에 들기 전에 그것을 끝내 버리고 싶어요.\"\n\n여기서 그 문이 열렸고, 메리듀 부인이 또 한 번 자그마한 비명을 질렀다. 그 폭발의 도래인가? 아니, 그저 베터리지의 도래일 뿐.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 제닝스 씨,\" 하고 베터리지가, 그의 더없이 공들여 은근한 태도로 말했다. \"프랭클린 나리가 당신이 어디 계신지 알고 싶어 하십니다. 저의 젊은 아가씨가 그 집에 있음에 관하여 그를 속이라는 당신의 명령을 받은지라, 저는 모른다고 말했습니다. 그것은, 눈여겨봐 주시기 바랍니다만, 거짓말이었습지요. 이미 한 발을 무덤에 들여놓은지라, 선생, 당신이 저에게 하기를 바라시는 거짓말이 적을수록, 저의 양심이 저를 찌르고 저의 때가 올 때, 저는 그만큼 당신께 빚지게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n베터리지의 양심이라는 순전히 사변적인 물음에 허비할 순간은 없었다. 만일 내가 대뜸 그의 방에서 그에게 가지 않는다면, 블레이크 씨가 나를 찾아 나타날지도 몰랐다. 버린더 아가씨가 나를 따라 그 복도로 나왔다.\n\n\"그들이 당신을 못살게 굴려고 짜기라도 한 것 같아요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그게 무슨 뜻일까요?\"\n\n\"그저 세상의 항변이에요, 버린더 아가씨—아주 작은 규모의—새로운 것은 무엇이든에 대한.\"\n\n\"우리가 메리듀 부인을 어떻게 하죠?\"\n\n\"그녀에게 그 폭발이 내일 아침 아홉 시에 일어날 거라고 말하시오.\"\n\n\"그녀를 잠자리로 보내려고요?\"\n\n\"그렇소—그녀를 잠자리로 보내려고.\"\n\n버린더 아가씨는 그 응접실로 돌아갔고, 나는 블레이크 씨에게로 위층으로 올라갔다.\n\n나의 놀라움으로 나는 그가 홀로 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 안절부절못하며 그의 방을 서성이고 있었고, 홀로 남겨진 것에 조금 짜증이 나 있었다.\n\n\"브러프 씨는 어디 있소?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n그는 그 두 방 사이의 닫힌 통용문을 가리켰다. 브러프 씨가 잠시 그를 들여다보았고, 우리 일에 대한 그의 항변을 되풀이하려 했고, 다시 한 번 블레이크 씨에게 조금의 인상도 남기는 데에 실패했다. 이에, 그 변호사는 직업상의 서류로 터질 듯 채워진 검은 가죽 가방에 몸을 숨겼다. \"삶의 진지한 일\"이, 그가 인정하기를, \"현재 같은 자리에는 서글피 어울리지 않소. 허나 삶의 진지한 일은 그럼에도 계속되어야 하오. 블레이크 씨는 어쩌면 친절히, 실질적인 사람의 예스러운 버릇을 헤아려 줄 거요. 시간은 돈이오—그리고, 제닝스 씨로 말하자면, 그는 브러프 씨가 부름을 받으면 나서리라 믿어도 좋소.\" 그 변명과 함께, 그 변호사는 제 방으로 돌아가, 그의 검은 가방에 고집스레 파묻혔다.\n\n나는 메리듀 부인과 그녀의 자수를, 그리고 베터리지와 그의 양심을 생각했다. 잉글랜드인의 성품의 그 굳건한 면에는 놀라운 한결같음이 있다—꼭 잉글랜드인의 얼굴의 그 굳건한 표정에 놀라운 한결같음이 있듯이.\n\n\"언제 나에게 그 아편팅크를 줄 것이오?\" 하고 블레이크 씨가 조바심을 내며 물었다.\n\n\"당신은 조금 더 기다려야 하오,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 남아서 그때가 올 때까지 당신의 벗이 되어 드리겠소.\"\n\n그때는 아직 열 시가 아니었다. 내가 여러 때에, 베터리지와 블레이크 씨에게 한 물음들이, 나로 하여금 캔디 씨가 준 그 아편팅크 약이 도저히 열한 시 전에 투여되었을 수 없다는 결론에 이르게 했다. 나는 그에 따라 그때까지 그 둘째 번 약을 시도하지 않기로 정한 터였다.\n\n우리는 조금 이야기했다. 허나 우리 둘의 마음 모두 다가올 시련에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그 대화는 곧 시들해졌다—그러고는 아예 그쳤다. 블레이크 씨는 그의 침실 탁자 위의 책들을 한가로이 뒤적였다. 나는 우리가 처음 그 방에 들어섰을 때, 그것들을 보아 두는 조심을 한 터였다. 《가디언》, 《태틀러》, 리처드슨의 《패멀라》, 매켄지의 《감정의 사람》, 로스코의 《로렌초 데 메디치》, 그리고 로버트슨의 《카를 오세》—죄 고전 작품들이다. 죄 (물론) 나중 시대에 나온 무엇보다 헤아릴 수 없이 뛰어나다. 그리고 죄 (나의 지금 관점에서) 아무의 관심도 사로잡지 않고, 아무의 머리도 흥분시키지 않는다는 그 하나의 큰 장점을 지녔다. 나는 블레이크 씨를 그 표준 문학의 진정시키는 영향에 맡겨 두고, 나의 일기에 이 항목을 적는 데에 나를 썼다.\n\n나의 시계가 나에게 열한 시가 다 되었음을 알린다. 나는 다시 한 번 이 낱장들을 덮어야 한다.\n\n오전 두 시.—그 실험이 시도되었다. 무슨 결과로 그러했는지, 나는 이제 서술하려 한다.\n\n열한 시에, 나는 베터리지를 부르는 종을 울리고, 블레이크 씨에게 그가 마침내 잠자리에 들 채비를 해도 좋다고 말했다.\n\n나는 창밖으로 그 밤을 내다보았다. 그것은 부드럽고 비가 왔는데, 이 점에서, 그 생일 밤—지난해 유월 이십일일과 닮았다. 조짐을 믿는 척하지 않고도, 적어도 그 대기 속에 아무 직접적인 신경적 영향—아무 사나운, 또는 전기적인 소란도 없음을 알게 된 것은 격려가 되었다. 베터리지가 그 창가에서 나에게 합류하여, 알쏭달쏭하게 자그마한 종잇조각 하나를 내 손에 넣어 주었다. 그것은 이 글줄들을 담고 있었다.\n\n\"메리듀 부인은, 그 폭발이 내일 아침 아홉 시에 일어난다는 것과, 그녀가 와서 나를 놓아줄 때까지 내가 그 집의 이 부분을 벗어나 움직이지 않는다는 뚜렷한 이해로, 잠자리에 들었어요. 그녀는 그 실험의 주된 무대가 나의 응접실이라는 것을 알지 못해요—그렇지 않았다면 그녀는 온 밤 내내 그 안에 남아 있었을 거예요! 나는 홀로 있고, 아주 애가 타요. 부디 당신이 그 아편팅크를 재어 내는 것을 내가 보게 해 줘요. 나는, 한낱 구경꾼이라는 대수롭지 않은 자격으로라도, 그것에 무언가 관여하고 싶어요.—R.V.\"\n\n나는 베터리지를 따라 그 방을 나가, 그에게 그 약장을 버린더 아가씨의 응접실로 옮기라고 말했다.\n\n그 명령은 그를 완전히 깜짝 놀라게 하는 듯했다. 그는 마치 내가 버린더 아가씨에게 어떤 은밀한 의학적 속셈을 품었다고 의심하는 것 같은 낯빛이었다! \"감히 여쭤봐도 될까요,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저의 젊은 아가씨와 그 약장이 서로 무슨 상관이 있는지를?\"\n\n\"그 응접실에 있어 보시오, 그러면 알게 될 것이오.\"\n\n베터리지는, 약장이 그 일에 들어 있는 자리에서, 나를 효과적으로 감독할 제 혼자만의 능력을 미심쩍어하는 듯했다.\n\n\"반대가 있으신가요, 선생,\" 하고 그가 물었다. \"브러프 씨를 이 부분의 일에 끌어들이는 것에?\"\n\n\"오히려 정반대요! 나는 이제 브러프 씨에게 나와 함께 아래층으로 가기를 청하려 하오.\"\n\n베터리지는 한마디 없이 그 약장을 가지러 물러났다. 나는 블레이크 씨의 방으로 돌아가, 그 통용문을 두드렸다. 브러프 씨가, 손에 그의 서류를 들고 그것을 열었다—법률에 파묻혀, 의술에는 뚫리지 않는 채로.\n\n\"방해해서 안됐소,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"허나 나는 블레이크 씨를 위해 그 아편팅크를 채비하려 하오. 그리고 나는 당신이 자리하여, 내가 무엇을 하는지 보기를 청해야겠소.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 하고 브러프 씨가, 그의 주의의 십 분의 구를 그의 서류에 못 박고, 십 분의 일을 마지못해 나에게 내주며 말했다. \"그 밖에 또 있소?\"\n\n\"나는 당신에게 나와 함께 여기로 돌아와, 내가 그 약을 투여하는 것을 보는 수고를 끼쳐야겠소.\"\n\n\"그 밖에 또 있소?\"\n\n\"한 가지 더. 나는 당신에게 블레이크 씨의 방에 남아, 무슨 일이 일어나는지 보려 기다리는 불편을 끼쳐야겠소.\"\n\n\"오, 아주 좋소!\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. \"내 방이든, 블레이크 씨의 방이든—어느 쪽이든 상관없소. 나는 어디서든 나의 서류를 계속할 수 있소. 제닝스 씨, 당신이 내가 그만한 양의 예사로운 분별을 이 일에 들여오는 것에 반대하지 않는다면 말이오?\"\n\n내가 미처 답하기도 전에, 블레이크 씨가 그의 침상에서 말하며 그 변호사에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"당신은 정말로 우리가 하려는 것에 아무 관심도 느끼지 않는다는 말이오?\" 하고 그가 물었다. \"브러프 씨, 당신은 소만큼의 상상력도 없구려!\"\n\n\"소는 아주 쓸모 있는 짐승이오, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그 변호사가 말했다. 그 대답과 함께 그는, 여전히 그의 서류를 손에 든 채, 나를 따라 그 방을 나왔다.\n\n우리는 버린더 아가씨가, 창백하고 동요한 채, 안절부절못하며 제 응접실을 이 끝에서 저 끝으로 서성이는 것을 보았다. 한 모퉁이의 탁자에 베터리지가, 그 약장을 지키며 서 있었다. 브러프 씨는 그가 찾을 수 있는 첫 의자에 앉아, (그 소의 쓸모 있음을 본받아) 그 자리에서 다시 그의 서류에 파묻혔다.\n\n버린더 아가씨는 나를 한쪽으로 끌어, 대뜸 그녀의 하나의 온통 마음을 사로잡는 관심—블레이크 씨에 대한 관심으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n\"그가 지금 어때요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다. \"그가 초조해해요? 그가 성이 났어요? 당신은 그것이 성공할 거라 생각해요? 당신은 그것이 아무 해도 끼치지 않을 것을 확신해요?\"\n\n\"아주 확신하오. 자, 와서 내가 그것을 재어 내는 것을 보시오.\"\n\n\"잠깐만요! 이제 열한 시가 지났어요. 무슨 일이 일어나기까지 얼마나 걸릴까요?\"\n\n\"말하기 쉽지 않소. 어쩌면 한 시간.\"\n\n\"짐작건대 그 방은, 지난해에 그랬듯이, 어두워야겠지요?\"\n\n\"물론이오.\"\n\n\"나는 내 침실에서 기다릴게요—꼭 전에 그랬듯이. 나는 문을 조금 열어 둘게요. 지난해에 그것이 조금 열려 있었어요. 나는 그 응접실 문을 지켜볼게요. 그리고 그것이 움직이는 순간, 나는 내 불을 불어 끌게요. 그것이 죄 그런 식으로 일어났어요, 나의 생일 밤에. 그리고 그것이 죄 같은 식으로 다시 일어나야 해요, 그렇지 않아요?\"\n\n\"당신은 스스로를 다잡을 수 있음을 확신하오, 버린더 아가씨?\"\n\n\"그를 위해서라면, 나는 무엇이든 할 수 있어요!\" 하고 그녀가 열렬히 대답했다.\n\n그녀의 얼굴을 한 번 보니 나는 그녀를 믿어도 좋음을 알았다. 나는 다시 브러프 씨에게 말을 건넸다.\n\n\"나는 당신에게 잠시 당신의 서류를 옆에 놓는 수고를 끼쳐야겠소,\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"오, 물론이오!\" 그는 소스라치며 일어났다—마치 내가 그를 각별히 흥미로운 대목에서 방해하기라도 한 양—그리고 나를 따라 그 약장으로 왔다. 거기서, 그의 직업의 행위에 딸린 그 숨죽인 흥분을 빼앗긴 채, 그는 베터리지를 보았다—그리고 지친 듯 하품했다.\n\n버린더 아가씨가, 곁상에서 집어 온 찬물 유리 주전자를 들고 나에게 합류했다. \"물은 내가 따르게 해 줘요,\" 하고 그녀가 속삭였다. \"나는 정말로 그것에 손을 대고 싶어요!\"\n\n나는 그 병에서 마흔 미님을 재어 내어, 그 아편팅크를 약 잔에 따랐다. \"그것이 사분의 삼쯤 차도록 채우시오,\" 하고 나는 말하며, 그 잔을 버린더 아가씨에게 건넸다. 그런 다음 나는 베터리지에게 그 약장을 넣고 잠그라고 지시했다. 그에게 이제 그것으로 볼일을 다했다고 알리며. 이루 말할 수 없는 안도의 표정이 그 늙은 하인의 낯에 번졌다. 그는 뚜렷이 내가 그의 젊은 아가씨에게 어떤 의학적 속셈을 품었다고 의심했던 것이다!\n\n내가 지시한 대로 그 물을 더한 뒤, 버린더 아가씨는 한순간을 붙잡아—베터리지가 그 약장을 잠그는 동안, 그리고 브러프 씨가 그의 서류를 돌아보는 동안—슬며시 그 약 잔의 가장자리에 입을 맞추었다. \"그것을 그에게 줄 때,\" 하고 그 매혹적인 아가씨가 말했다. \"그것을 저쪽으로 그에게 줘요!\"\n\n나는 그 다이아몬드를 나타낼 그 수정 조각을 내 주머니에서 꺼내어, 그것을 그녀에게 주었다.\n\n\"당신은 이것에도 손을 대야 하오,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신은 그것을 지난해에 그 월장석을 놓았던 곳에 놓아야 하오.\"\n\n그녀는 그 인도식 장식장으로 앞장서 가, 그 진짜 다이아몬드가 그 생일 밤에 차지했던 그 서랍에 그 가짜 다이아몬드를 놓았다. 브러프 씨는, 그가 다른 온갖 것을 지켜본 것과 같이, 항변과 함께, 이 일을 지켜보았다. 허나 그 실험이 이제 띠어 가는 그 강렬한 극적 관심이, (나의 큰 재미로) 베터리지의 자제 능력에 벅찬 것으로 드러났다. 그는 그 초를 든 채 손을 떨며, 애타게 속삭였다, \"아가씨, 그게 옳은 서랍이 확실한가요?\"\n\n나는 그 아편팅크와 물을 손에 들고, 다시 앞장서 나갔다. 그 문에서, 나는 버린더 아가씨에게 마지막 한마디를 건네려 멈추었다.\n\n\"불을 끄는 것을 오래 끌지 마시오,\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"나는 그것들을 대뜸 끌게요,\" 하고 그녀가 대답했다. \"그리고 나는 오직 초 하나만 켜 둔 채, 내 침실에서 기다릴게요.\"\n\n그녀는 우리 뒤로 그 응접실 문을 닫았다. 브러프 씨와 베터리지를 뒤에 거느리고, 나는 블레이크 씨의 방으로 돌아갔다.\n\n우리는 그가 안절부절못하며 침상의 이쪽저쪽으로 움직이며, 자기가 그날 밤 그 아편팅크를 받게 되는지 짜증스레 궁금해하는 것을 보았다. 그 두 증인의 앞에서, 나는 그에게 그 약을 주고, 그의 베개를 흔들어 매만지고, 다시 조용히 누워 기다리라고 그에게 말했다.\n\n가벼운 사라사 커튼을 두른 그의 침상은, 그 방의 벽에 머리를 대고, 그 양옆으로 넉넉히 트인 공간을 남기게끔 놓여 있었다. 한쪽으로, 나는 그 커튼을 완전히 쳤다—그리고 그의 눈에서 이렇게 가려진 그 방의 부분에, 나는 그 결과를 기다리도록 브러프 씨와 베터리지를 두었다. 그 침상의 발치에 나는 그 커튼을 반쯤 쳤다—그리고 나 자신의 의자를 조금 떨어진 곳에 두어, 상황이 지시하는 대로, 그가 나를 보게 하거나 못 보게 하고, 나에게 말을 걸게 하거나 못 걸게 할 수 있도록. 그가 늘 방에 불을 켠 채 잔다는 것을 이미 들은 터라, 나는 그 두 켜진 초 중 하나를, 그 불빛의 번쩍임이 그의 눈에 부딪치지 않을, 그 침상 머리맡의 자그마한 탁자에 놓았다. 다른 초는 브러프 씨에게 주었다. 그 불빛은, 이 경우에, 그 사라사 커튼의 가림으로 누그러진 채였다. 그 창은, 방을 환기하려, 위쪽이 열려 있었다. 비가 부드럽게 내렸고, 그 집은 고요했다. 그 채비가 마쳐지고, 내가 그 침상 발치에 따로 놓인 그 의자에 나의 자리를 잡았을 때, 나의 시계로 열한 시 이십 분이었다.\n\n브러프 씨는, 여느 때와 다름없이 그것들에 깊이 관심을 둔 온갖 기색과 함께, 그의 서류를 다시 이어 갔다. 허나 이제 그를 향해 보니, 나는 그 법률이 마침내 그에 대한 손아귀를 잃기 시작하고 있음을 나에게 일러 주는 어떤 표시와 조짐들을 보았다. 우리가 이제 놓인 그 처지의 그 멎은 관심이, 그의 상상력 없는 마음에조차 서서히 그 영향을 발휘하고 있었다. 베터리지로 말하자면, 원칙의 일관됨과 처신의 위엄이, 그의 경우에, 한낱 빈말이 되어 있었다. 그는 내가 프랭클린 블레이크 나리에게 요술을 부리고 있음을 잊었다. 그는 내가 그 집을 위아래로 뒤집어 놓았음을 잊었다. 그는 내가 어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽었음을 잊었다. \"제발, 선생,\" 하고 그가 나에게 속삭였다. \"그것이 언제 듣기 시작할지 우리에게 말해 주십시오.\"\n\n\"자정 전에는 아니오,\" 하고 나는 속삭여 되받았다. \"아무 말도 말고, 가만히 앉아 있으시오.\"\n\n베터리지는, 자신을 지키려는 안간힘 하나 없이, 나와의 스스럼없음의 가장 낮은 밑바닥으로 떨어졌다. 그는 눈을 찡긋함으로써 답했다!\n\n다음으로 블레이크 씨를 향해 보니, 나는 그가 여느 때와 다름없이 그의 침상에서 안절부절못하며, 어째서 그 아편팅크의 영향이 아직 발휘되기 시작하지 않았는지 안달하며 궁금해하는 것을 보았다. 그의 지금 심사에, 그가 안달하고 궁금해할수록, 우리가 이제 기다리는 그 결과를 그가 그만큼 더 늦추게 되리라고 그에게 말하는 것은 그저 헛되었을 것이다. 취할 더 슬기로운 방도는, 그로 하여금 어느새 다른 무언가를 생각하게 이끎으로써, 그의 마음에서 그 아편의 생각을 몰아내는 것이었다.\n\n이 뜻으로, 나는 그가 나에게 이야기하도록 부추겼다. 내 편에서, 그것을 그날 저녁 더 이르게 우리를 사로잡았던 그 주제—그 다이아몬드라는 주제로 도로 이끌도록 그 대화를 이끌면서. 나는, 그 월장석의 이야기에서, 그것을 런던에서 요크셔로 옮긴 것에, 블레이크 씨가 그것을 프리징홀의 은행에서 옮기면서 무릅쓴 그 위험에, 그리고 그 생일 저녁에 그 집에 인도인들이 뜻밖에 나타난 것에 관련된 부분들로 되돌아가는 데에 마음 썼다. 그리고 나는, 이 사건들을 언급하면서, 블레이크 씨 자신이 몇 시간 전에 나에게 말해 준 것 중 많은 것을 잘못 알아들은 척 일부러 꾸몄다. 이런 식으로, 나는 그로 하여금, 이제 그의 마음을 채우는 것이 아주 중요한 그 주제로 이야기하게 만들었다—그가, 내가 어떤 목적으로 그를 이야기하게 만들고 있음을 의심하도록 두지 않으면서. 조금씩, 그는 나를 바로잡는 데에 어찌나 관심이 이는지 그 침상에서 안달하는 것을 잊었다. 그의 눈이 처음으로 나에게 그 아편이 그의 뇌에 손을 얹기 시작하고 있음을 일러 준 그 더없이 중요한 때에, 그의 마음은 그 아편의 물음에서 멀리 떨어져 있었다.\n\n나는 나의 시계를 보았다. 그 아편팅크가 듣는 것의 전조 증상이 처음으로 나에게 제 모습을 드러냈을 때, 열두 시까지 오 분이 모자랐다.\n\n이때, 훈련되지 않은 눈이라면 그에게서 아무 변화도 알아채지 못했을 것이다. 허나, 그 새 아침의 순간들이 흘러가면서, 그 영향의 빠르고도 미묘한 진행이 더 또렷이 제 모습을 드러내기 시작했다. 아편의 그 숭고한 취기가 그의 눈에서 어른거렸다. 은밀한 땀의 이슬이 그의 얼굴에서 반짝이기 시작했다. 오 분이 더 지나자, 그가 여전히 나와 이어 가던 그 이야기가, 조리를 잃었다. 그는 그 다이아몬드라는 주제를 꾸준히 붙들었다. 허나 그는 그의 문장들을 마무리하기를 그쳤다. 조금 뒤, 그 문장들이 낱낱의 낱말로 떨어졌다. 그러고는, 침묵의 사이가 있었다. 그러고는, 그가 침상에서 일어나 앉았다. 그러고는, 여전히 그 다이아몬드라는 주제로 바쁜 채, 그가 다시 이야기하기 시작했다—나에게가 아니라, 자기 자신에게. 그 변화가 나에게 그 실험의 첫 단계에 이르렀음을 일러 주었다. 그 아편의 흥분시키는 영향이 그를 사로잡은 것이다.\n\n그때, 시각은 열두 시 이십삼 분이었다. 기껏해야 그다음 반 시간이, 그가 그의 침상에서 일어나 그 방을 떠날 것인지 아닌지의 물음을 결정할 터였다.\n\n그를 지켜보는 그 숨죽인 관심 속에서—그 실험의 첫 결과가, 내가 예상한 그 방식으로, 그리고 거의 그 때에 스스로를 드러내는 것을 보는 그 이루 말할 수 없는 승리 속에서—나는 나의 밤샘의 그 두 동무를 온전히 잊고 있었다. 이제 그들을 향해 보니, 나는 (브러프 씨의 서류로 나타나는) 그 법률이 바닥에 아랑곳없이 놓인 것을 보았다. 브러프 씨 자신은 그 불완전하게 쳐진 침상의 커튼에 남겨진 틈으로 간절히 들여다보고 있었다. 그리고 베터리지는, 신분의 차이에 대한 온갖 존중을 잊은 채, 브러프 씨의 어깨 너머로 엿보고 있었다.\n\n그들은 둘 다, 내가 그들을 보고 있음을 알아채고, 마치 잘못을 저지르다 훈장에게 들킨 두 소년처럼 움찔 물러났다. 나는, 내가 내 것을 벗고 있듯, 그들에게 조용히 그들의 부츠를 벗으라고 몸짓했다. 만일 블레이크 씨가 우리에게 그를 뒤따를 기회를 준다면, 소리 없이 그를 뒤따르는 것이 아주 중요했다.\n\n십 분이 지났다—그리고 아무 일도 없었다. 그러고는, 그가 별안간 그의 침구를 벗어 던졌다. 그는 한 다리를 침상 밖으로 내밀었다. 그는 기다렸다.\n\n\"내가 그것을 그 은행에서 꺼내지 말았어야 했는데,\" 하고 그가 자기 자신에게 말했다. \"그것은 그 은행에서 안전했는데.\"\n\n내 심장이 빠르게 뛰었다. 내 관자놀이의 맥이 세차게 뛰었다. 그 다이아몬드의 안전에 대한 그 의심이, 다시 한 번, 그의 뇌 속의 압도적인 인상이었다! 그 하나의 축(軸) 위에, 그 실험의 온 성공이 돌았다. 이렇게 별안간 내 앞에 열린 그 전망이 나의 산산조각 난 신경에 벅찼다. 나는 그에게서 눈을 돌리지 않을 수 없었다—그러지 않았다면 나는 나의 자제심을 잃었을 것이다.\n\n또 한 번 침묵의 사이가 있었다.\n\n내가 그를 도로 볼 만큼 나 자신을 믿을 수 있게 되었을 때 그는 그의 침상 밖에 나와, 그 곁에 곧추서 있었다. 그의 눈동자는 이제 오그라들어 있었다. 그가 그의 머리를 천천히 이리저리 움직이자 그의 눈알이 그 초의 불빛 속에서 어른거렸다. 그는 생각하고 있었다. 그는 의심하고 있었다—그가 다시 말했다.\n\n\"내가 어찌 알겠어?\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그 인도인들이 그 집에 숨어 있을지도 몰라.\"\n\n그는 멈추어, 그 방의 다른 쪽 끝으로 천천히 걸어갔다. 그는 돌아섰다—기다렸다—그 침상으로 돌아왔다.\n\n\"그건 잠겨 있지도 않아,\" 하고 그가 이어 갔다. \"그건 그녀의 장식장 서랍에 있어. 그리고 그 서랍은 잠기지 않아.\"\n\n그는 그 침상 곁에 앉았다. \"누구든 그것을 가져갈 수 있어,\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n그는 다시 안절부절못하며 일어나, 그의 첫마디를 되뇌었다.\n\n\"내가 어찌 알겠어? 그 인도인들이 그 집에 숨어 있을지도 몰라.\"\n\n그는 다시 기다렸다. 나는 그 침상의 반쯤 친 커튼 뒤로 물러났다. 그는 그의 눈에 텅 빈 어른거림을 담고, 그 방을 둘러보았다. 그것은 숨죽인 순간이었다. 어떤 종류의 멈춤이 있었다. 그 아편의 작용의 멈춤인가? 그 뇌의 작용의 멈춤인가? 누가 알 수 있었으랴? 모든 것이, 이제, 그가 다음에 무엇을 하는지에 달렸다.\n\n그가 다시 그 침상에 몸을 눕혔다!\n\n끔찍한 의심이 내 마음을 스쳤다. 그 아편의 진정시키는 작용이 이미 느껴지고 있다는 것이 있을 수 있는 일인가? 그것이 이리하는 것은 나의 경험에 없었다. 허나, 아편에 관한 한, 경험이 무엇이랴? 그 약이 정확히 같은 방식으로 작용하는 두 사람은 세상에 아마 없을 것이다. 그의 어떤 체질적 특이함이, 그 영향을 어떤 새로운 방식으로 느끼고 있는 것인가? 우리가 성공의 그야말로 벼랑에서 실패해야 하는가?\n\n아니! 그가 다시 갑자기 일어났다. \"내가 어찌 잠들겠어,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이것을 마음에 두고서?\"\n\n그는 그의 침상 머리맡 탁자에서 타는 그 불빛을 보았다. 잠시 뒤, 그가 그 초를 손에 들었다.\n\n나는 그 닫힌 커튼 뒤에서 타는 그 둘째 초를 불어 껐다. 나는, 브러프 씨와 베터리지와 함께, 그 침상 곁의 가장 먼 모퉁이로 물러났다. 나는 그들에게, 마치 그들의 목숨이 거기 달리기라도 한 양, 조용히 하라고 몸짓했다.\n\n우리는 기다렸다—아무것도 보지도 듣지도 못한 채. 우리는 기다렸다, 그 커튼에 그에게서 가려진 채.\n\n그가 우리 저쪽에서 들고 있던 그 불빛이 별안간 움직였다. 다음 순간 그가, 손에 그 초를 들고, 재빠르고 소리 없이, 우리를 지나쳤다.\n\n그는 그 침실 문을 열고, 나갔다.\n\n우리는 그를 따라 그 복도를 지나갔다. 우리는 그를 따라 그 계단을 내려갔다. 우리는 그를 따라 그 둘째 복도를 지나갔다. 그는 결코 뒤돌아보지 않았다. 그는 결코 망설이지 않았다.\n\n그는 그 응접실 문을 열고, 그 뒤에 그것을 열어 둔 채, 들어갔다.\n\n그 문은 (그 집의 다른 온갖 문처럼) 큰 예스러운 돌쩌귀에 달려 있었다. 그것이 열리자, 그 문과 문설주 사이에 틈이 열렸다. 나는 나의 두 동무에게, 그들 자신이 드러나지 않게, 이것을 통해 들여다보라고 몸짓했다. 나 자신은—역시 그 문 밖에서—그 맞은편에 자리했다. 벽의 우묵한 곳이 나의 왼편에 있었는데, 만일 그가 그 복도를 뒤돌아볼 어떤 기색이라도 보이면, 나는 그 안에 대뜸 나를 숨길 수 있었다.\n\n그는 여전히 손에 그 초를 든 채, 그 방 한가운데로 나아갔다. 그는 주위를 둘러보았다—허나 그는 결코 뒤돌아보지 않았다.\n\n나는 버린더 아가씨의 침실 문이 빠끔히 열려 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 그녀의 불을 껐다. 그녀는 고귀하게 스스로를 다잡았다. 그녀의 여름 옷의 어슴푸레한 흰 윤곽이 내가 볼 수 있는 전부였다. 그것을 미리 알지 못한 사람이라면 그 방에 살아 있는 존재가 있음을 의심하지 않았을 것이다. 그녀는 어둠 속에 물러나 있었다. 한마디 말도, 한 번의 움직임도 그녀에게서 새어 나오지 않았다.\n\n이제 한 시 십 분이었다. 나는, 그 죽은 듯한 침묵을 뚫고, 그 비의 부드러운 떨어짐과, 그 나무들 사이를 지나는 그 밤 공기의 떨리는 지나감을 들었다.\n\n그 방 한가운데에서, 일 분 남짓, 망설이며 기다린 뒤, 그는 그 인도식 장식장이 서 있는 그 창가 모퉁이로 움직였다.\n\n그는 그의 초를 그 장식장 위에 놓았다. 그는 서랍을 하나씩 열었다 닫았다 하며, 그 가짜 다이아몬드가 놓인 그 서랍에 이르렀다. 그는 잠시 그 서랍을 들여다보았다. 그러고는 그의 오른손으로 그 가짜 다이아몬드를 꺼냈다. 다른 손으로, 그는 그 장식장 위에서 그 초를 집었다.\n\n그는 그 방 한가운데를 향해 몇 걸음 걸어 돌아와, 다시 가만히 섰다.\n\n여기까지, 그는 그 생일 밤에 그가 한 것을 정확히 되풀이한 터였다. 그의 다음 조치가 지난해의 조치와 같을 것인가? 그가 그 방을 떠날 것인가? 그가 이제, 내가 그때 그가 돌아갔다고 믿는 대로, 그의 침실로 돌아갈 것인가? 그가 그 자신의 방으로 돌아갔을 때 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지 우리에게 보여 줄 것인가?\n\n그가 다시 한 번 움직였을 때 그의 첫 행동은, 그가 처음으로 그 아편의 영향 아래 있었을 때 그가 하지 않은 행동으로 드러났다. 그는 그 초를 탁자에 내려놓고, 그 방의 더 먼 쪽 끝을 향해 조금 헤매어 갔다. 거기 소파가 하나 있었다. 그는 왼손으로 그 등받이에 무겁게 기댔다—그러고는 정신을 차리고, 그 방 한가운데로 돌아왔다. 나는 이제 그의 눈을 볼 수 있었다. 그것들은 흐릿하고 무거워지고 있었다. 그것들 속의 그 어른거림이 빠르게 스러져 가고 있었다.\n\n그 순간의 마음 졸임이 버린더 아가씨의 자제심에 벅찬 것으로 드러났다. 그녀는 몇 걸음 나아갔다—그러고는 다시 멈추었다. 브러프 씨와 베터리지가 처음으로 그 열린 문간 너머로 나를 건너다보았다. 다가오는 실망에 대한 예감이, 나의 마음에서와 마찬가지로 그들의 마음에서도 새겨지고 있었다.\n\n그럼에도, 그가 그가 있는 곳에 서 있는 한, 희망이 있었다. 우리는, 이루 말할 수 없는 기대 속에, 다음에 무슨 일이 일어나는지 보려 기다렸다.\n\n다음 사건이 결정적이었다. 그가 그 가짜 다이아몬드를 그의 손에서 떨어뜨렸다.\n\n그것은 바닥에, 그 문간 앞에 떨어졌다—그에게도, 모두에게도 또렷이 보이게. 그는 그것을 집으려 아무 애도 쓰지 않았다. 그는 그것을 멍하니 내려다보았고, 내려다보면서, 그의 머리가 그의 가슴에 내려앉았다. 그는 비틀거렸다—한순간 정신을 차렸다—불안하게 그 소파로 걸어 돌아왔다—그리고 그 위에 앉았다. 그는 마지막 안간힘을 썼다. 그는 일어서려 했고, 도로 주저앉았다. 그의 머리가 그 소파 쿠션에 떨어졌다. 그때는 한 시 이십오 분이었다. 내가 나의 시계를 주머니에 도로 넣기 전에, 그는 잠들어 있었다.\n\n이제 그것은 죄 끝났다. 그 진정시키는 영향이 그를 사로잡았다. 그 실험은 끝난 것이다.\n\n나는, 브러프 씨와 베터리지에게 그들이 나를 뒤따라도 좋다고 말하며, 그 방으로 들어갔다. 그를 방해할 두려움은 없었다. 우리는 자유로이 움직이고 말할 수 있었다.\n\n\"먼저 정할 일은,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 그를 어떻게 하느냐 하는 물음이오. 그는 아마 적어도 그다음 여섯이나 일곱 시간을 잘 것이오. 그를 그 자신의 방으로 도로 나르기에는 얼마쯤 거리가 있소. 내가 더 젊었을 때는, 나는 그것을 혼자 할 수 있었을 것이오. 허나 나의 건강과 힘은 예전 같지 않소—안됐지만 나는 당신들에게 나를 돕기를 청해야겠소.\"\n\n그들이 미처 답하기도 전에, 버린더 아가씨가 나를 나직이 불렀다. 그녀는 그녀의 방 문에서, 가벼운 숄과, 그녀 자신의 침상에서 가져온 침대보를 들고 나를 맞았다.\n\n\"그가 자는 동안 그를 지켜볼 셈인가요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 나는 그의 경우에 그 아편의 작용을 그를 혼자 두어도 될 만큼 확신하지 못하오.\"\n\n그녀는 나에게 그 숄과 그 침대보를 건넸다.\n\n\"어째서 그를 방해해야 하죠?\" 하고 그녀가 속삭였다. \"그의 잠자리를 그 소파에 봐 줘요. 나는 내 문을 닫고, 내 방에 있을 수 있어요.\"\n\n그것은 한없이, 그날 밤 그를 처리하는 가장 간단하고 가장 안전한 방도였다. 나는 그 제안을 브러프 씨와 베터리지에게 말했다—둘 다 내가 그것을 채택하는 것에 찬성했다. 오 분 만에 나는 그를 그 소파에 편안히 뉘었고, 그 침대보와 그 숄로 그를 가볍게 덮었다. 버린더 아가씨는 우리에게 잘 자라 인사하고, 그 문을 닫았다. 나의 청으로, 우리 셋은 그런 다음, 그 초가 여전히 타고 있고 필기도구가 놓인, 그 방 한가운데의 그 탁자에 둘러 모였다.\n\n\"우리가 헤어지기 전에,\" 하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"나는 오늘 밤 시도된 그 실험에 대해 한마디 하고 싶소. 그것으로 얻어야 할 두 가지 뚜렷한 목적이 있었소. 이 목적 가운데 첫째는, 블레이크 씨가 지난해에 이 방에 들어와 그 다이아몬드를 가져간 것이, 아편의 영향 아래, 무의식적으로, 책임 없이 한 것임을 증명하는 것이었소. 당신들 둘이 본 것을 겪고서, 당신들 둘은, 여기까지, 흡족하오?\"\n\n그들은 한순간의 망설임도 없이, 나에게 그렇다고 답했다.\n\n\"그 둘째 목적은,\" 하고 나는 이어 갔다. \"그 생일 밤에, 그가 손에 그 보석을 들고 그녀의 응접실을 떠나는 것을 버린더 아가씨가 본 뒤, 그가 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지 알아내는 것이었소. 이 목적을 얻는 것은, 물론, 그가 여전히 지난해의 그의 행동을 정확히 계속 되풀이하는 것에 달려 있었소. 그는 그것을 하는 데에 실패했소. 그리고 그에 따라 그 실험의 목적은 좌절되었소. 나는 내가 그 결과에 실망하지 않았다고 우기지는 못하겠소—허나 나는 내가 그것에 놀라지는 않는다고 정직하게 말할 수 있소. 나는 처음부터 블레이크 씨에게, 이 일에서의 우리의 온전한 성공이, 우리가 지난해의 몸과 마음의 조건을 그에게 온전히 되살리는 것에 달렸다고 말했소—그리고 나는 그에게 이것이 그야말로 불가능한 것에 가깝다고 경고했소. 우리는 다만 그 조건을 부분적으로만 되살렸고, 그 결과로 그 실험은 다만 부분적으로만 성공했소. 내가 그 아편팅크를 너무 많이 투여했을지도 모른다는 것도 있을 법하오. 허나 나 자신은, 우리가 성공을 기뻐할 뿐 아니라 실패를 슬퍼해야 하는 참된 이유로, 내가 든 그 첫 이유를 보오.\"\n\n그 말을 하고서, 나는 브러프 씨 앞에 그 필기도구를 놓고, 그에게—우리가 그날 밤 헤어지기 전에—그가 본 것에 대한 평이한 진술을 작성하고 서명하는 것에 반대가 있는지 물었다. 그는 대뜸 그 펜을 들어, 익숙한 손의 유창한 거침없음으로 그 진술을 내놓았다.\n\n\"나는 당신에게 이것을 빚졌소,\" 하고 그가 그 종이에 서명하며 말했다. \"그날 저녁 더 이르게 우리 사이에 오간 일에 대한 얼마쯤의 속죄로. 당신을 의심한 데 대해 당신의 용서를 비오, 제닝스 씨. 당신은 프랭클린 블레이크에게 값을 헤아릴 수 없는 도움을 베풀었소. 우리의 법률 용어로, 당신은 당신의 주장을 증명했소.\"\n\n베터리지의 사과는 그 사람다웠다.\n\n\"제닝스 씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 「로빈슨 크루소」를 다시 읽으시면 (제가 강하게 권하는 바입니다만), 당신은 그가 자기가 틀렸던 것으로 드러날 때, 결코 그것을 인정하기를 망설이지 않는 것을 알게 될 것입니다. 부디, 선생, 이 경우에 저를, 로빈슨 크루소가 한 것을 하는 사람으로 여겨 주십시오.\" 그 말과 함께 그는 제 차례로 그 종이에 서명했다.\n\n브러프 씨가, 우리가 그 탁자에서 일어설 때, 나를 한쪽으로 끌었다.\n\n\"그 다이아몬드에 대해 한마디,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 이론은 프랭클린 블레이크가 그 월장석을 그의 방에 감췄다는 것이오. 나의 이론은, 그 월장석이 런던의 루커 씨의 은행가의 소유에 있다는 것이오. 우리 중 어느 쪽이 옳은지는 다투지 맙시다. 우리는 다만, 우리 중 어느 쪽이 제 이론을 증명에 부칠 처지에 있는지 물읍시다.\"\n\n\"나의 경우의 그 증명은,\" 하고 나는 대답했다. \"오늘 밤 시도되어, 실패했소.\"\n\n\"나의 경우의 그 증명은,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다. \"아직 시도되는 과정에 있소. 지난 이틀 동안 나는 그 은행에 루커 씨를 위한 파수를 세워 두었소. 그리고 나는 그 파수를 이달의 마지막 날까지 계속하게 하겠소. 나는 그가 몸소 그 다이아몬드를 그의 은행가의 손에서 꺼내야 함을 아오—그리고 나는, 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡힌 사람이 그 저당물을 되찾음으로써 그로 하여금 이것을 하게 만들지도 모른다는 요행에 따라 움직이고 있소. 그런 경우 나는 그 사람에게 내 손을 얹을 수 있을지 모르오. 만일 내가 성공한다면, 나는 바로 그 수수께끼가 지금 우리를 어리둥절하게 하는 그 지점에서, 그 수수께끼를 밝히는 것이오! 여기까지 당신은 인정하오?\"\n\n나는 그것을 어렵잖게 인정했다.\n\n\"나는 아침 기차로 시내로 돌아가오,\" 하고 그 변호사가 이어 갔다. \"돌아가면 나는, 어떤 발견이 이루어졌다는 소식을 들을지도 모르오—그리고, 필요하다면, 프랭클린 블레이크를 곁에 두어 그에게 물을 수 있는 것이 더없이 중요할지도 모르오. 나는 그가 깨어나는 대로, 그가 나와 함께 런던으로 돌아가야 한다고 그에게 말할 작정이오. 일어난 온갖 것을 겪은 뒤에, 내가 당신의 영향력에 기대어 나를 뒷받침해 주기를 믿어도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"물론이오!\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n브러프 씨는 나와 악수하고, 그 방을 떠났다. 베터리지가 그를 따라 나갔다.\n\n나는 블레이크 씨를 보려 그 소파로 갔다. 그는 내가 그를 뉘고 그의 잠자리를 봐 준 이래 움직이지 않은 채였다—그는 깊고 고요한 잠에 잠겨 누워 있었다.\n\n내가 아직 그를 보고 있는 동안, 나는 그 침실 문이 사뿐히 열리는 것을 들었다. 다시 한 번, 버린더 아가씨가, 그녀의 어여쁜 여름 옷 차림으로, 그 문지방에 나타났다.\n\n\"나에게 마지막 은혜를 베풀어 주겠어요?\" 하고 그녀가 속삭였다. \"당신과 함께 그를 지켜보게 해 줘요.\"\n\n나는 망설였다—예절을 위해서가 아니라, 오직 그녀의 밤의 안식을 위해서. 그녀는 나에게 바싹 다가와, 내 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"나는 잠들 수 없어요. 나는 내 방에서 가만히 앉아 있을 수조차 없어요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"오, 제닝스 씨, 만일 당신이 나라면, 당신이 앉아서 그를 바라보기를 얼마나 갈망할지 생각해 봐요. 그렇다고 말해 줘요! 부디!\"\n\n내가 굴복했음을 언급할 필요가 있는가? 정녕 없으리라!\n\n그녀는 그 소파 발치로 의자 하나를 끌어왔다. 그녀는 눈에 눈물이 고이기까지, 말없는 행복의 황홀함 속에 그를 바라보았다. 그녀는 눈물을 닦고, 자기의 일감을 가져오겠다고 말했다. 그녀는 자기의 일감을 가져왔고, 그것을 한 땀도 뜨지 않았다. 그것은 그녀의 무릎에 놓여 있었다—그녀는 자기 바늘에 실을 꿰기에 넉넉할 만큼도 그에게서 눈을 뗄 수 없었다. 나는 나 자신의 젊음을 생각했다. 나는 한때 나를 향해 사랑을 담아 바라보던 그 다정한 눈을 생각했다. 마음의 무거움 속에서 나는 위안을 얻으려 나의 일기로 눈을 돌려, 그것에 여기 적힌 것을 적었다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 말없이 함께 우리의 지킴을 이어 갔다. 우리 중 하나는 그의 글쓰기에 빠져, 다른 하나는 그녀의 사랑에 빠져.\n\n시간이 흐르고 흘러도 그는 그 깊은 잠에 누워 있었다. 그 새 날의 빛이 그 방에서 자라고 자랐으나, 그는 여전히 결코 움직이지 않았다.\n\n여섯 시경, 나는 나의 통증이 돌아오고 있음을 나에게 일러 주는 그 경고를 느꼈다. 나는 잠시 그녀를 그와 홀로 남겨 두지 않을 수 없었다. 나는 위층으로 가서, 그의 방에서 그를 위한 베개 하나를 더 가져오겠다고 말했다. 이번에는, 긴 발작이 아니었다. 조금 뒤 나는 감히 돌아와, 그녀가 나를 다시 볼 수 있게 할 수 있었다.\n\n내가 돌아왔을 때, 나는 그녀가 그 소파 머리맡에 있는 것을 보았다. 그녀는 막 그녀의 입술로 그의 이마에 닿는 참이었다. 나는 되도록 근엄하게 고개를 젓고, 그녀의 의자를 가리켰다. 그녀는 밝은 미소와, 그녀의 얼굴에 매혹적인 홍조를 담고 나를 돌아보았다. \"당신도 그리했을 거예요,\" 하고 그녀가 속삭였다. \"내 처지였다면!\"\n\n이제 막 여덟 시다. 그가 처음으로 움직이기 시작하고 있다.\n\n버린더 아가씨가 그 소파 곁에 무릎 꿇고 있다. 그녀는, 그의 눈이 처음 떠질 때, 그것이 그녀의 얼굴에 떠지도록 스스로 그렇게 자리를 잡았다.\n\n내가 그들을 함께 두고 떠날까?\n\n그래!\n\n열한 시.—그 집이 다시 비었다. 그들은 자기들끼리 그것을 정했다. 그들은 죄 열 시 기차로 런던으로 갔다. 나의 짧은 행복의 꿈이 끝났다. 나는 다시, 벗 없고 외로운 나의 삶의 현실로 깨어났다.\n\n나는 감히, 나에게 건네진 그 다정한 말들을—각별히 버린더 아가씨와 블레이크 씨에게서 건네진 말들을 적어 둘 만큼 나 자신을 믿지 못하겠다. 게다가, 그것은 부질없다. 그 말들은 나의 외로운 시간에 나에게 돌아올 것이고, 나의 삶의 끝의 남은 것을 헤쳐 나가는 것을 도울 것이다. 블레이크 씨는 편지하여, 런던에서 무슨 일이 일어나는지 나에게 이야기하기로 되어 있다. 버린더 아가씨는 가을에 요크셔로 돌아오기로 (두말할 것 없이, 그녀의 혼인을 위해) 되어 있다. 그리고 나는 휴가를 얻어, 그 집의 손님이 되기로 되어 있다. 오 이런, 그 고마운 행복이 그녀의 눈에서 나를 바라보고, 그녀의 손의 따뜻한 쥠이 \"이것은 당신이 한 일이에요!\" 하고 말했을 때, 내가 어떤 심정이었던가.\n\n나의 가엾은 환자들이 나를 기다리고 있다. 오늘 아침, 다시 그 옛 상례로! 오늘 밤, 다시 그 아편과 그 통증 사이의 끔찍한 선택으로!\n\n그분의 자비에 하느님을 찬미하라! 나는 얼마쯤의 햇살을 보았다—나는 행복한 시간을 가졌다.\n\n프랭클린 블레이크가 이어 쓰는 이야기.\n\n# CHAPTER I\n\n허나 에즈라 제닝스의 일기에 내놓인 그 이야기를 마무리하는 데에는, 내 편에서, 몇 마디밖에 필요치 않다.\n\n나 자신에 대해서는, 나는 다만, 내가 이십육일 아침에, 그 아편의 영향 아래 내가 말하고 한 온갖 것에 대해—그 약이 처음 나에게 손을 얹은 때로부터, 내가 레이철의 응접실에서 눈을 뜬 때까지—완전히 무지한 채로 깨어났다는 것만 말할 수밖에 없다.\n\n나의 깨어남 뒤에 일어난 일에 대해서는, 나는 세세하게 이야기할 의무가 있다고 느끼지 않는다. 오직 결과에만 나 자신을 국한하여, 나는, 양편에서 한마디의 설명도 오가기 전에, 레이철과 내가 서로를 낱낱이 이해했다고 전하는 바다. 나는 우리 화해의 그 유별난 빠름을 설명하기를 마다하며, 레이철도 설명하기를 마다한다. 선생과 부인이여, 그대들이 서로에게 열렬히 매여 있던 그때를 되돌아보시라—그러면 그대들은, 에즈라 제닝스가 그 응접실의 문을 닫은 뒤에 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 나 자신이 아는 것만큼 잘 알게 되리라.\n\n허나, 나는, 레이철의 침착함이 아니었다면 우리가 틀림없이 메리듀 부인에게 들켰으리라는 것을 덧붙이는 데에 반대가 없다. 그녀는 그 복도에서 그 노부인의 옷 소리를 듣고, 대뜸 뛰쳐나가 그녀를 맞았다. 나는 메리듀 부인이 \"무슨 일이야?\" 하고 말하는 것을 들었고, 나는 레이철이 \"그 폭발이에요!\" 하고 답하는 것을 들었다. 메리듀 부인은 대뜸 팔을 잡히도록 스스로를 내맡겨, 그 임박한 충격의 길에서 벗어나, 정원으로 이끌려 갔다. 그 집으로 돌아오면서, 그녀는 그 현관홀에서 나와 마주쳐, 자기가 학교 다니는 계집아이였을 때 이래 과학이 크게 나아진 것에 아주 놀랐다고 밝혔다. \"폭발은, 블레이크 씨, 예전보다 한없이 순해요. 장담하는데, 저는 정원에서 제닝스 씨의 폭발을 겨우 들었어요. 그리고 그 뒤로, 우리가 그 집으로 돌아온 지금, 제가 알아챌 수 있는 아무 냄새도 없어요! 저는 정말로 당신의 의사 벗께 사과해야겠어요. 그가 그것을 훌륭히 해냈다고 말해 주는 것이 그에게 마땅한 도리예요!\"\n\n그리하여, 베터리지와 브러프 씨를 무찌른 뒤, 에즈라 제닝스는 메리듀 부인 자신마저 무찔렀다. 결국, 세상에는 아직 피어나지 않은 너그러운 정이 자못 많이 있다!\n\n아침 식사에서, 브러프 씨는 내가 아침 기차로 그와 함께 런던으로 가기를 바라는 그의 이유를 조금도 감추지 않았다. 그 은행에 세운 파수와, 그것에서 아직 나올지 모를 그 결과가, 레이철의 호기심에 어찌나 뿌리칠 수 없이 호소했던지, 그녀는 대뜸 (메리듀 부인이 반대하지 않는다면) 우리와 함께 시내로 돌아가기로 정했다—우리 일의 가장 이른 소식이 닿는 곳에 있으려고.\n\n메리듀 부인은, 그 폭발이 진정 사려 깊게 처신한 그 태도 뒤에, 온통 유순하고 너그러운 것으로 드러났다. 그리고 그에 따라 베터리지는 우리 넷이 죄 아침 기차로 함께 돌아가리라는 것을 전해 들었다. 나는 그가 우리를 동행하게 해 달라고 청할 줄로 온전히 예상했다. 허나 레이철은 슬기롭게 그녀의 충직한 늙은 하인에게 그의 관심을 끄는 일거리를 마련해 준 터였다. 그는 그 집의 다시 갖추기를 마무리하는 것을 맡았고, 그의 집안 책임으로 어찌나 벅찼던지 다른 상황에서라면 느꼈을 법한 \"탐정 열병\"을 느끼지 못했다.\n\n런던으로 가면서, 우리의 한 가지 아쉬움의 화제는, 우리가 바랐을 것보다 더 갑작스레, 에즈라 제닝스와 헤어지는 필요였다. 그를 우리와 동행하도록 설득하기란 불가능했다. 나는 다만 그에게 편지하겠다고 약속할 수밖에 없었고—레이철은 다만 그녀가 요크셔로 돌아오면 그가 그녀를 보러 오기를 우길 수밖에 없었다. 우리가 몇 달 안에 다시 만날 온갖 전망이 있었다—그럼에도 기차가 역에서 나갈 때, 우리의 가장 좋고 가장 정다운 벗이 그 승강장에 홀로 남아 서 있는 것을 보는 데에는 아주 서글픈 무언가가 있었다.\n\n우리가 런던에 다다르자, 브러프 씨는 그 종착역에서, 올이 다 드러난 검은 천으로 된 재킷과 바지 차림의, 그리고 그의 눈의 유별난 튀어나옴으로 개인적으로 눈에 띄는 어린 소년 하나에게 말을 걸렸다. 그것들은 어찌나 멀리 튀어나오고, 어찌나 헐겁게 이리저리 굴렀던지, 그것들이 어째서 그 눈구멍에 남아 있는지 불안스레 의아했다. 그 소년의 말을 듣고서, 브러프 씨는 그 부인들에게, 우리가 그들을 포틀랜드 플레이스로 배웅하지 못하는 것을 양해해 주겠는지 물었다. 나는 겨우, 브러프 씨가 내 팔을 붙들어 나를 삯마차로 서둘러 밀어 넣기 전에, 레이철에게 내가 돌아와, 일어난 모든 것을 그녀에게 이야기하겠다고 약속할 짬밖에 없었다. 그 잘못 박힌 눈의 소년이 마부 곁의 마부석에 제 자리를 잡았고, 마부는 롬바드가로 가라는 지시를 받았다.\n\n\"그 은행에서 온 소식이오?\" 하고 나는 우리가 출발하면서 물었다.\n\n\"루커 씨의 소식이오,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. \"한 시간 전에, 그가 두 사람을 데리고, 삯마차로 램버스의 그의 집을 떠나는 것이 목격되었소. 그 두 사람은 내 사람들에게 사복 경관으로 알아보아졌소. 만일 인도인들에 대한 루커 씨의 두려움이 이 방비의 밑바탕에 있다면, 그 미루어 짐작함은 자못 뚜렷하오. 그는 그 다이아몬드를 그 은행에서 꺼내려는 것이오.\"\n\n\"그리고 우리는 그것에서 무엇이 나오는지 보러 그 은행으로 가는 것이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소—또는, 만일 그것이 이때쯤 죄 끝났다면, 그것에서 무엇이 나왔는지 들으러 가는 것이오. 당신은 내 소년을 눈여겨봤소—저기, 마부석의?\"\n\n\"나는 그의 눈을 눈여겨봤소.\"\n\n브러프 씨가 웃었다. \"그 사무소에서는 그 가엾은 자그마한 몹쓸 것을 '구스베리'라 부른다오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 그를 심부름에 부리오—그리고 나는 다만 그에게 별명을 붙인 내 서기들이 그만큼이나 낱낱이 믿을 만하면 좋으련만 하오. 구스베리는, 블레이크 씨, 그의 눈에도 불구하고, 런던에서 가장 예리한 소년의 하나요.\"\n\n우리가 롬바드가의 그 은행 앞에 마차를 세웠을 때는 다섯 시 이십 분 전이었다. 구스베리는 그 삯마차 문을 열면서, 갈망하는 눈으로 제 주인을 보았다.\n\n\"너도 들어오고 싶으냐?\" 하고 브러프 씨가 다정하게 물었다. \"그럼 들어와서, 다음 명령이 있을 때까지 내 뒤꿈치에 붙어 있어라. 그는 번개처럼 빠르오,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 나에게 속삭이며 말을 이었다. \"다른 소년이라면 스무 마디가 필요할 데서, 구스베리에게는 두 마디면 되오.\"\n\n우리는 그 은행에 들어갔다. 그 바깥 사무실은—긴 계산대와, 그 뒤에 출납원들이 앉은—사람들로 붐볐다. 죄 그 은행이 다섯 시에 닫기 전에, 돈을 찾거나 돈을 넣으려 제 차례를 기다리며.\n\n그 무리 가운데 두 사람이, 브러프 씨가 모습을 드러내자마자, 그에게 다가왔다.\n\n\"자,\" 하고 그 변호사가 물었다. \"그를 봤소?\"\n\n\"그가 반 시간 전에 여기서 우리를 지나쳐, 안쪽 사무실로 들어갔습니다, 나리.\"\n\n\"그가 아직 다시 나오지 않았소?\"\n\n\"예, 나리.\"\n\n브러프 씨가 나에게로 돌아섰다. \"기다립시다,\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n나는 내 둘레의 사람들 사이에서 그 인도인 셋을 찾아 둘러보았다. 그들의 기색은 어디서도 보이지 않았다. 자리한 사람들 가운데 눈에 띄게 거무스름한 낯빛의 유일한 사람은, 뱃사람처럼 보이는, 두꺼운 외투와 둥근 모자 차림의 키 큰 사내였다. 이것이 변장한 그들 중 하나일 수 있을까? 있을 수 없었다! 그 사내는 인도인들 누구보다 키가 컸다. 그리고 그의 얼굴은, 그 텁수룩한 검은 수염에 가려지지 않은 데서, 적어도 그들 누구의 얼굴보다 너비가 두 배였다.\n\n\"그들은 어딘가에 그들의 밀정을 두었음이 틀림없소,\" 하고 브러프 씨가, 제 차례로 그 거무스름한 뱃사람을 보며 말했다. \"그리고 그가 그 사람일지도 모르오.\"\n\n그가 미처 더 말하기 전에, 그의 웃옷 자락이, 구스베리 눈을 한 그의 시중드는 요정에게 삼가 잡아당겨졌다. 브러프 씨는 그 소년이 보고 있는 곳을 보았다. \"쉿!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"여기 루커 씨가 있소!\"\n\n그 돈놀이꾼이, 사복 차림의 그의 두 호위 경관을 거느리고, 그 은행의 안쪽 구역에서 나왔다.\n\n\"그에게서 눈을 떼지 마시오,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 속삭였다. \"만일 그가 누구에게든 그 다이아몬드를 건넨다면, 그는 그것을 여기서 건넬 것이오.\"\n\n우리 둘 어느 쪽도 알아채지 못한 채, 루커 씨는—이제는 그 무리의 가장 빽빽한 곳에서, 이제는 가장 성긴 곳에서—천천히 그 문으로 나아갔다. 나는 그가, 수수한 회색 정장을 점잖게 차려입은 키 작고 다부진 사내를 지나칠 때, 그의 손이 움직이는 것을 또렷이 보았다. 그 사내는 조금 소스라치며, 그의 뒤를 보았다. 루커 씨는 그 무리 사이로 천천히 나아갔다. 그 문에서 그의 호위가 그의 양옆에 자리했다. 그들 셋은 죄 브러프 씨의 사람 하나에게 뒤따라졌다—그리고 나는 그들을 다시 보지 못했다.\n\n나는 그 변호사를 돌아보았고, 그런 다음 그 수수한 회색 정장의 사내를 향해 뜻깊게 보았다. \"그렇소!\" 하고 브러프 씨가 속삭였다. \"나도 그것을 봤소!\" 그는 그의 둘째 사람을 찾아 돌아섰다. 그 둘째 사람은 어디에도 보이지 않았다. 그는 그의 시중드는 요정을 찾아 그의 뒤를 보았다. 구스베리가 사라져 있었다.\n\n\"이게 대체 무슨 뜻이오?\" 하고 브러프 씨가 성이 나서 말했다. \"그들이 둘 다, 우리가 그들을 가장 필요로 하는 바로 그때에 우리를 떠났소.\"\n\n그 회색 정장의 사내가 그 계산대에서 그의 볼일을 볼 차례가 되었다. 그는 수표 한 장을 넣었다—그것에 대한 영수증을 받았다—그리고 나가려 돌아섰다.\n\n\"어찌해야 하오?\" 하고 브러프 씨가 물었다. \"우리는 그를 뒤따르는 것으로 우리 자신을 욕되게 할 수 없소.\"\n\n\"나는 할 수 있소!\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 만 파운드를 준대도 저 사내에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠소!\"\n\n\"그런 경우,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다. \"나는 그 두 배의 돈을 준대도 당신에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠소. 나 같은 처지의 사람에게 참 좋은 일거리로군,\" 하고 그는, 우리가 그 낯선 이를 뒤따라 그 은행을 나서면서, 자기 자신에게 중얼거렸다. \"제발 그것을 언급하지 마시오. 그것이 알려지면 나는 파멸이오.\"\n\n그 회색 정장의 사내는 서쪽으로 가는 승합마차에 올랐다. 우리는 그를 따라 올랐다. 브러프 씨에게는 아직 잠재된 젊음의 밑천이 남아 있었다. 나는 그것을 단언한다—그가 그 승합마차에 그의 자리를 잡았을 때, 그는 낯을 붉혔다!\n\n그 회색 정장의 사내는 그 승합마차를 세우고, 옥스퍼드가에서 내렸다. 우리는 다시 그를 뒤따랐다. 그는 약제사의 가게로 들어갔다.\n\n브러프 씨가 소스라쳤다. \"내 약제사요!\" 하고 그가 외쳤다. \"안됐지만 우리가 잘못한 것 같소.\"\n\n우리는 그 가게로 들어갔다. 브러프 씨와 그 주인이 사사로이 몇 마디를 나누었다. 그 변호사는 아주 풀 죽은 얼굴로 다시 나에게 합류했다.\n\n\"그것이 크게 우리의 명예요,\" 하고 그가, 내 팔을 잡고 나를 이끌어 내며 말했다. \"그것이 하나의 위안이오!\"\n\n\"무엇이 우리의 명예요?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"블레이크 씨! 당신과 나는 여태 이 장사에 손을 댄 가장 형편없는 두 아마추어 탐정이오. 그 회색 정장의 사내는 그 약제사를 삼십 년 섬겼소. 그는 제 주인의 계좌에 돈을 넣으러 그 은행에 보내진 것이오—그리고 그는 아직 세상에 나지 않은 갓난아기만큼도 그 월장석에 대해 아는 것이 없소.\"\n\n나는 다음으로 어찌해야 하는지 물었다.\n\n\"내 사무소로 돌아갑시다,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. \"구스베리와, 내 둘째 사람은, 뚜렷이 다른 누군가를 뒤따랐소. 어쨌든 그들이 눈을 밝히고 있었기를 바랍시다!\"\n\n우리가 그레이스 인 스퀘어에 다다르자, 그 둘째 사람이 우리보다 먼저 거기 다다라 있었다. 그는 십오 분 넘게 기다린 터였다.\n\n\"자!\" 하고 브러프 씨가 물었다. \"무슨 소식이오?\"\n\n\"안됐지만, 나리,\" 하고 그 사람이 대답했다. \"제가 잘못을 저질렀습니다. 저는 루커 씨가, 밝은 빛깔의 외투 차림의 나이 든 신사에게 무언가를 건네는 것을 봤다고 맹세할 수 있었습니다. 그 나이 든 신사는, 나리, 이스트칩의 더없이 점잖은 철물상으로 드러났습니다.\"\n\n\"구스베리는 어디 있소?\" 하고 브러프 씨가 체념하여 물었다.\n\n그 사람이 빤히 쳐다보았다. \"모르겠습니다, 나리. 저는 그 은행을 떠난 이래 그를 전혀 못 봤습니다.\"\n\n브러프 씨가 그 사람을 물렸다. \"둘 중 하나요,\" 하고 그가 나에게 말했다. \"구스베리가 달아났든지, 아니면 그가 제 나름으로 쫓고 있든지. 그 소년이 한두 시간 안에 돌아올지 모른다는 요행을 노려, 여기서 저녁을 드는 것에 대해 어떻게 생각하오? 나에게 지하실에 좋은 포도주가 얼마쯤 있고, 우리는 그 커피점에서 고기 한 점을 얻을 수 있소.\"\n\n우리는 브러프 씨의 방에서 저녁을 들었다. 상보가 걷히기 전에, \"어떤 사람\"이 그 변호사와 이야기하고 싶어 한다고 알려졌다. 그 사람이 구스베리였을까? 아니, 그저 루커 씨가 그 은행을 떠날 때 그를 뒤따르도록 부려진 그 사람일 뿐이었다.\n\n이 경우의 그 보고는 조금도 관심 있는 면을 내놓지 않았다. 루커 씨는 그 자신의 집으로 돌아가, 거기서 그의 호위를 물렸다. 그는 그 뒤로 다시 나가지 않았다. 땅거미 무렵, 덧문이 세워지고, 문들이 빗장 질러졌다. 그 집 앞의 거리와, 그 집 뒤의 골목이 조심스레 지켜졌다. 인도인들의 기색은 보이지 않았다. 그 어떤 사람도 그 집 둘레를 어슬렁거리는 것이 보이지 않았다. 이 사실들을 진술하고서, 그 사람은 무슨 더 이상의 명령이 있는지 알려 기다렸다. 브러프 씨는 그 밤은 그를 물렸다.\n\n\"당신은 루커 씨가 그 월장석을 자기 집으로 가져갔다고 생각하오?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"그럴 리 없소,\" 하고 브러프 씨가 말했다. \"만일 그가 그 다이아몬드를 다시 자기 집에 두는 위험을 무릅썼다면, 그는 결코 그의 두 경관을 물리지 않았을 것이오.\"\n\n우리는 그 소년을 반 시간 더 기다렸으나, 헛되이 기다렸다. 그때는 브러프 씨가 햄스테드로 갈, 그리고 내가 포틀랜드 플레이스의 레이철에게로 돌아갈 때였다. 나는 그 방의 문지기에게 맡겨, 그날 밤 열 시 반에 내 하숙에 있겠다는 한 줄을 적은 내 명함을 남겼다. 그 명함은, 만일 그 소년이 돌아오면, 그 소년에게 주어질 것이었다.\n\n어떤 사람들은 약속을 지키는 재주가 있고, 다른 사람들은 그것을 어기는 재주가 있다. 나는 그 다른 사람들 중 하나다. 여기에다, 내가 그 저녁을, 저 끝에 메리듀 부인을 둔 마흔 자 길이의 방에서, 레이철과 같은 자리에서 보냈음을 더하라. 내가 열 시 반이 아니라 열두 시 반에 집에 다다랐다고 누가 의아해하겠는가? 그 사람은 얼마나 낱낱이 무정함이 틀림없으랴! 그리고 나는 내가 결코 그 사람과 아는 사이가 되지 않기를 얼마나 간절히 바라는가!\n\n내 하인이 나를 들이면서 나에게 종잇조각 하나를 건넸다.\n\n나는, 단정한 법률가풍 필적으로, 이 말들을 읽었다—\"죄송하지만, 나리, 저는 졸리고 있습니다. 저는 내일 아침, 아홉 시와 열 시 사이에 다시 오겠습니다.\" 물어보니, 아주 유별난 생김새의 눈을 한 소년 하나가 찾아와, 내 명함과 전갈을 내놓고, 한 시간 기다리고, 잠들었다 다시 깨는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않고, 나에게 한 줄을 적고, 집으로 갔음이 드러났다—그가 하인에게, \"제 밤의 안식을 얻지 못하면 저는 아무 쓸모가 없습니다\" 하고 근엄하게 알린 뒤에.\n\n이튿날 아침 아홉 시에, 나는 내 방문객을 맞을 채비를 했다. 아홉 시 반에, 나는 내 문밖의 발소리를 들었다. \"들어와라, 구스베리!\" 하고 나는 소리쳤다. \"고맙습니다, 선생,\" 하고 근엄하고 우울한 목소리가 답했다. 그 문이 열렸다. 나는 벌떡 일어나, 마주했다—커프 부장을!\n\n\"당신이 시내에 있을지 모른다는 요행을 노려, 요크셔로 편지하기 전에, 여기 들여다볼까 했소, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그 부장이 말했다.\n\n그는 여느 때와 다름없이 음울하고 여위었다. 그의 눈은 (베터리지 이야기에서 그토록 미묘하게 눈여겨보아진) \"당신 자신이 자각하는 것보다 당신에게서 무언가를 더 기대하는 듯 보이는\" 그 옛 버릇을 잃지 않은 채였다. 허나, 옷차림이 사람을 바꿀 수 있는 한으로는, 그 위대한 커프는 알아볼 수 없게 변해 있었다. 그는 챙 넓은 흰 모자, 가벼운 사냥 재킷, 흰 바지, 그리고 담갈색 각반을 걸치고 있었다. 그는 튼튼한 참나무 지팡이를 들었다. 그의 온 목표와 목적은 마치 그가 평생 시골에서 산 것처럼 보이는 것인 듯했다. 내가 그의 변신을 칭찬하자, 그는 그것을 농담으로 받기를 마다했다. 그는, 자못 근엄하게, 런던의 소음과 냄새에 대해 불평했다. 나는 정녕 그가 조금 촌스러운 억양으로 말하지 않았다고 결코 장담하지 못하겠다! 나는 그에게 아침 식사를 권했다. 그 순진한 시골 사람은 자못 충격을 받았다. 그의 아침 시각은 여섯 시 반이었다—그리고 그는 닭들과 함께 잠자리에 들었다!\n\n\"나는 겨우 지난밤 아일랜드에서 돌아왔소,\" 하고 그 부장이, 그 자신의 뚫을 수 없는 태도로, 그의 방문의 실질적인 목적으로 돌아오며 말했다. \"잠자리에 들기 전에, 나는 지난해에 그 다이아몬드에 대한 내 조사가 중단된 이래 일어난 일을 나에게 이야기하는 당신의 편지를 읽었소. 그 일에 대해 내 편에서 할 말은 오직 하나뿐이오. 나는 내 사건을 완전히 잘못 알았소. 그때의 나 같은 상황에서, 살아 있는 어떤 사람이 사물을 그 참된 빛으로 볼 수 있었을지, 나는 안다고 짐짓 나서지 않소. 허나 그것이 지금 그대로의 사실을 바꾸지는 않소. 나는 내가 그것을 죽 쒀 놨음을 인정하오. 내 직업 이력을 두드러지게 한 첫 죽은 아니오, 블레이크 씨! 탐정대의 관원이 잘못을 저지르는 약함보다 위에 있는 것은 오직 책 속에서뿐이오.\"\n\n\"당신은 당신의 명성을 되찾을 딱 좋은 때에 왔소,\" 하고 나는 말했다.\n\n\"용서하시오, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그 부장이 되받았다. \"이제 나는 일에서 물러났으니, 나는 내 명성에 대해 지푸라기만큼도 개의치 않소. 나는 내 명성과 볼일을 다했소, 하느님께 감사하게도! 나는 여기, 선생, 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 나에 대한 후함에 대한 고마운 기억에서 왔소. 나는 나의 옛 일로 돌아가겠소—당신이 나를 원하고, 당신이 나를 믿는다면—그 헤아림에서, 그리고 다른 어떤 것에서도 아니게. 부디, 당신에게서 나에게로 한 푼의 돈도 오가지 않기로. 이것은 명예를 걸고 하는 것이오. 자 나에게 말해 보시오, 블레이크 씨, 당신이 나에게 마지막으로 편지한 이래 그 사건이 어떤 상태인지.\"\n\n나는 그에게 그 아편 실험에 대해, 그리고 그 뒤에 롬바드가의 그 은행에서 일어난 일에 대해 말했다. 그는 그 실험에 크게 놀랐다—그것은 그의 경험에 온전히 새로운 무엇이었다. 그리고 그는, 내가 그 생일 밤에 레이철의 응접실을 떠난 뒤 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지에 관련된, 에즈라 제닝스의 이론에 각별히 관심을 두었다.\n\n\"나는 당신이 그 월장석을 감췄다는 제닝스 씨의 견해에는 동의하지 않소,\" 하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"허나 나는, 당신이 틀림없이 그것을 당신 자신의 방으로 도로 가져갔음이 분명하다는 데에는 그와 뜻을 같이하오.\"\n\n\"그래서요?\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"그러고는 무슨 일이 일어났소?\"\n\n\"당신 자신은 무슨 일이 일어났는지 아무 짐작도 없소, 선생?\"\n\n\"전혀 없소.\"\n\n\"브러프 씨는 아무 짐작도 없소?\"\n\n\"나와 마찬가지로 없소.\"\n\n커프 부장은 일어나, 내 필기 탁자로 갔다. 그는 봉인된 봉투 하나를 들고 돌아왔다. 그것은 \"친전\"이라 표시되어 있었다. 그것은 나에게 부쳐진 것이었다. 그리고 그것은 모퉁이에 그 부장의 서명을 지니고 있었다.\n\n\"나는 지난해에 엉뚱한 사람을 의심했소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 지금 엉뚱한 사람을 의심하고 있을지도 모르오. 당신이 진실에 다다르기까지, 블레이크 씨, 그 봉투를 여는 것을 기다리시오. 그런 다음 그 죄지은 사람의 이름을, 내가 그 봉인된 편지에 적어 둔 이름과 견주어 보시오.\"\n\n나는 그 편지를 내 주머니에 넣었다—그런 다음 그 은행에서 우리가 취한 그 방책에 대한 그 부장의 견해를 물었다.\n\n\"아주 좋은 뜻이었소, 선생,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"그리고 아주 옳게 할 일이었소. 허나 루커 씨 말고도 살펴봤어야 할 다른 사람이 있었소.\"\n\n\"당신이 방금 나에게 준 그 편지에 이름이 적힌 그 사람이오?\"\n\n\"그렇소, 블레이크 씨, 그 편지에 이름이 적힌 그 사람이오. 이제 어쩔 수 없소. 나는 때가 오면, 선생, 당신과 브러프 씨에게 제안할 무언가가 있을 것이오. 먼저 기다려, 그 소년이 우리에게 들려줄 값어치 있는 무언가가 있는지 봅시다.\"\n\n거의 열 시가 다 되었으나, 그 소년은 나타나지 않은 채였다. 커프 부장은 다른 일들을 이야기했다. 그는 그의 옛 벗 베터리지와, 그의 옛 원수인 그 정원사의 안부를 물었다. 조금만 더 있었으면, 만일 내 하인이 그 소년이 아래에 있다고 알림으로써 우리를 끊지 않았다면, 그는 두말할 것 없이 이것에서 그가 아끼는 장미라는 주제로 넘어갔을 것이다.\n\n그 방으로 데려와지자, 구스베리는 그 문지방에서 멈추어, 나와 함께 있는 그 낯선 이를 미심쩍게 보았다. 나는 그 소년에게 나에게 오라고 말했다.\n\n\"너는 이 신사 앞에서 말해도 좋다,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그는 나를 돕기 위해 여기 있다. 그리고 그는 일어난 온갖 것을 안다. 커프 부장,\" 하고 나는 덧붙였다. \"이 아이가 브러프 씨의 사무소에서 온 그 소년이오.\"\n\n우리의 근대 문명 체계에서, 명성은 (그것이 어떤 종류이든) 무엇이든 움직일 지렛대다. 그 위대한 커프의 명성은 그 자그마한 구스베리의 귀에마저 다다른 터였다. 그 소년의 잘못 박힌 눈은, 내가 그 이름난 이름을 언급하자, 정녕 그것들이 그 융단에 떨어졌음이 틀림없다고 내가 생각할 만큼 굴렀다.\n\n\"이리 오너라, 얘야,\" 하고 그 부장이 말했다. \"그리고 네가 우리에게 들려줄 것을 들어 보자.\"\n\n그 위대한 사람—런던의 모든 변호사 사무소에서 숱한 이름난 이야기의 주인공인 이의 눈여겨봄이 그 소년을 홀리는 듯했다. 그는 커프 부장 앞에 자리하고, 제 교리문답을 시험받는 초심자의 정해진 방식대로, 그의 손을 뒤로 두었다.\n\n\"네 이름이 무엇이냐?\" 하고 그 부장이, 그 교리문답의 첫 물음으로 시작하며 말했다.\n\n\"옥타비어스 가이입니다,\" 하고 그 소년이 대답했다. \"그들은 제 눈 때문에 사무소에서 저를 구스베리라 부릅니다.\"\n\n\"옥타비어스 가이, 다시 말해 구스베리,\" 하고 그 부장이 더없는 근엄함으로 이어 갔다. \"너는 어제 그 은행에서 사라졌다. 무엇을 하고 있었느냐?\"\n\n\"죄송하지만, 선생, 저는 한 사내를 뒤따르고 있었습니다.\"\n\n\"그가 누구였느냐?\"\n\n\"뱃사람처럼 차려입은, 큰 검은 수염의 키 큰 사내였습니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"나는 그 사내를 기억하오!\" 하고 나는 끼어들었다. \"브러프 씨와 나는 그가 인도인들에게 부려진 밀정이라고 생각했소.\"\n\n커프 부장은 브러프 씨와 내가 생각한 것에 그리 놀라는 듯싶지 않았다. 그는 구스베리를 계속 문답했다.\n\n\"그래서?\" 하고 그가 말했다—\"그리고 너는 어째서 그 뱃사람을 뒤따랐느냐?\"\n\n\"죄송하지만, 선생, 브러프 씨가 루커 씨가 그 은행을 나서는 길에 누구에게든 무언가를 건네는지 알고 싶어 했습니다. 저는 루커 씨가 그 검은 수염의 뱃사람에게 무언가를 건네는 것을 봤습니다.\"\n\n\"어째서 네가 본 것을 브러프 씨에게 말하지 않았느냐?\"\n\n\"저는 아무에게도 말할 짬이 없었습니다, 선생, 그 뱃사람이 어찌나 서둘러 나갔던지요.\"\n\n\"그래서 너는 그를 뒤쫓아 뛰어나갔다—응?\"\n\n\"예, 선생.\"\n\n\"구스베리,\" 하고 그 부장이 그의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"너는 그 자그마한 머릿속에 무언가를 지녔구나—그리고 그것은 솜뭉치가 아니다. 나는, 여기까지, 너에게 크게 흡족하다.\"\n\n그 소년이 기뻐 낯을 붉혔다. 커프 부장이 이어 갔다.\n\n\"그래서? 그리고 그 뱃사람이 거리로 나갔을 때 무엇을 했느냐?\"\n\n\"그가 삯마차를 불렀습니다, 선생.\"\n\n\"그리고 너는 무엇을 했느냐?\"\n\n\"뒤에 매달려, 그것을 뒤쫓아 뛰었습니다.\"\n\n그 부장이 그의 다음 물음을 던지기 전에, 또 하나의 방문객이 알려졌다—브러프 씨의 사무소에서 온 수석 서기였다.\n\n커프 부장의 그 소년에 대한 문답을 끊지 않는 것의 중요함을 느끼고, 나는 다른 방에서 그 서기를 맞았다. 그는 그의 고용주에 대한 나쁜 소식을 가지고 왔다. 지난 이틀의 동요와 흥분이 브러프 씨에게 벅찬 것으로 드러났다. 그는 그날 아침 통풍의 발작과 함께 깨어났다. 그는 햄스테드의 그의 방에 갇혀 있었다. 그리고, 우리 일의 지금의 위태로운 상태에서, 그는 나를 경험 있는 사람의 조언과 도움 없이 두어야 하게 된 것에 아주 불안해했다. 그 수석 서기는 나의 뜻대로 대기하라는 명령을 받았고, 기꺼이 브러프 씨를 대신하려 최선을 다하려 했다.\n\n나는 대뜸, 커프 부장의 방문을 알림으로써, 그 노신사의 마음을 잠재우려 편지했다. 구스베리가 그 순간 문답받고 있음을 덧붙이며, 그리고 그날 늦게 일어날 무엇이든, 몸소, 또는 편지로, 브러프 씨에게 알리겠다고 약속하며. 그 서기를 내 쪽지와 함께 햄스테드로 보내고서, 나는 내가 떠났던 그 방으로 돌아가, 커프 부장이 그 벽난로에서, 막 종을 울리는 참인 것을 보았다.\n\n\"용서하시오, 블레이크 씨,\" 하고 그 부장이 말했다. \"나는 막 당신의 하인을 통해, 내가 당신과 이야기하고 싶다는 말을 보내려던 참이었소. 이 소년이—이 더없이 갸륵한 소년이,\" 하고 그 부장이 구스베리의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 덧붙였다. \"옳은 사내를 뒤따랐다는 데에는 내 마음에 아무 의심도 없소. 소중한 시간이, 선생, 당신이 안타깝게도 지난밤 열 시 반에 집에 없었던 탓에 잃어졌소. 이제 할 유일한 일은, 대뜸 삯마차를 부르는 것이오.\"\n\n오 분이 더 지나, 커프 부장과 나는 (마부를 안내하러 마부석에 구스베리를 태우고) 동쪽으로, 시티를 향해 가는 길에 있었다.\n\n\"머잖아,\" 하고 그 부장이 그 삯마차의 앞창을 통해 가리키며 말했다. \"저 소년은 나의 옛 직업에서 큰일을 할 것이오. 그는 여러 해 만에 내가 만난 가장 총명하고 가장 영리한 자그마한 녀석이오. 당신은, 블레이크 씨, 당신이 그 방을 나가 있는 동안 그가 나에게 들려준 것의 알맹이를 듣게 될 것이오. 내 생각에 당신은, 그가 그 삯마차 뒤에 매달려, 그것을 뒤쫓아 뛰었다고 언급했을 때 자리에 있었지요?\"\n\n\"그렇소.\"\n\n\"자, 선생, 그 삯마차는 롬바드가에서 타워 부두로 갔소. 그 검은 수염의 뱃사람이 내려, 이튿날 아침 떠나기로 되어 있던 그 로테르담 증기선의 사무장에게 이야기했소. 그는 자기가 대뜸 승선하여, 하룻밤 자기 침상에서 잘 수 있게 허락받을 수 있는지 물었소. 그 사무장은 아니라고 했소. 선실과, 침상과, 침구가 그날 저녁 죄 철저히 청소되어야 하고, 어떤 승객도 아침 전에 승선하도록 허락될 수 없다고. 그 뱃사람은 돌아서서, 그 부두를 떠났소. 그가 다시 거리로 나갔을 때, 그 소년은 처음으로, 점잖은 직공처럼 차려입은 사내 하나가, 그 길 맞은편에서 걸으며, 겉보기에 그 뱃사람을 눈에 두고 있음을 알아챘소. 그 뱃사람은 그 근방의 어느 식당에서 멈추어, 들어갔소. 그 소년은—그 순간 마음을 정할 수 없어—다른 소년들 몇몇 사이에서 어정거리며, 그 식당 창의 맛난 것들을 빤히 쳐다보았소. 그는 그 직공이, 그 자신이 기다리고 있듯, 기다리는 것을—허나 여전히 그 길 맞은편에서—알아챘소. 잠시 뒤, 삯마차 한 대가 천천히 지나와, 그 직공이 서 있는 곳에서 멈추었소. 그 소년은 그 삯마차에서 오직 한 사람만 또렷이 볼 수 있었는데, 그가 그 직공에게 이야기하려 그 창에서 앞으로 몸을 기울였소. 그는 그 사람을, 블레이크 씨, 나에게서 아무 재촉도 없이, 인도인의 얼굴 같은, 거무스름한 얼굴을 지녔다고 서술했소.\"\n\n이때쯤, 브러프 씨와 내가 또 하나의 잘못을 저질렀음이 뚜렷했다. 그 검은 수염의 뱃사람은 명백히 그 인도인 음모에 부려진 밀정이 아니었다. 그가, 혹시나, 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣은 사내였을까?\n\n\"조금 뒤,\" 하고 그 부장이 이어 갔다. \"그 삯마차가 그 거리를 천천히 따라 나아갔소. 그 직공은 그 길을 건너, 그 식당으로 들어갔소. 그 소년은 그가 배고프고 지칠 때까지 밖에서 기다렸소—그런 다음, 제 차례로 그 식당으로 들어갔소. 그에게는 주머니에 실링 하나가 있었소. 그리고 그는, 그가 나에게 말하기로, 선지 순대와, 뱀장어 파이와, 생강 맥주 한 병으로 진수성찬을 들었소. 소년이 소화 못 할 것이 무엇이랴? 그 문제의 알맹이는 아직 찾아지지 않았소.\"\n\n\"그가 그 식당에서 무엇을 봤소?\" 하고 나는 물었다.\n\n\"자, 블레이크 씨, 그는 그 뱃사람이 한 탁자에서 신문을 읽고, 그 직공이 다른 탁자에서 신문을 읽는 것을 봤소. 그 뱃사람이 일어나 그곳을 떠난 것은 땅거미가 진 뒤였소. 그는 거리로 나갔을 때 미심쩍게 그의 둘레를 둘러보았소. 그 소년은—소년인지라—눈에 띄지 않고 지나쳤소. 그 직공은 아직 나오지 않은 채였소. 그 뱃사람은, 그의 둘레를 둘러보며, 겉보기에 그가 다음에 어디로 가는지 그리 확실하지 않은 채로 걸어 나아갔소. 그 직공이 다시 한 번, 그 길 맞은편에 나타났소. 그 뱃사람은, 그가 로어 템스가로 이어지는 쇼어 레인에 이르기까지 나아갔소. 거기서 그는 '운명의 수레바퀴'라는 간판 아래의 어느 술집 앞에서 멈추어, 그곳을 밖에서 살핀 뒤, 들어갔소. 구스베리도 들어갔소. 그 술청에는 아주 많은 사람이, 대개 점잖은 부류가 있었소. '운명의 수레바퀴'는 아주 점잖은 집이오, 블레이크 씨. 그 흑맥주와 돼지고기 파이로 이름났소.\"\n\n그 부장의 곁길로 새는 것이 나를 부아 나게 했다. 그가 그것을 보았다. 그리고 그는 이어 갈 때 더 엄격히 구스베리의 증언에 자신을 국한했다.\n\n\"그 뱃사람은,\" 하고 그가 다시 이어 갔다. \"잠자리를 얻을 수 있는지 물었소. 그 주인은 '아니오, 다 찼소' 하고 말했소. 그 술청 아가씨가 그를 바로잡아, '십 호가 비었어요' 하고 말했소. 그 뱃사람을 십 호로 안내하러 급사가 불려졌소. 바로 그 전에, 구스베리는 그 술청의 사람들 사이에서 그 직공을 알아챘소. 그 급사가 그 부름에 답하기 전에, 그 직공이 사라졌소. 그 뱃사람은 그의 방으로 데려가졌소. 다음에 어찌할지 몰라, 구스베리는 슬기롭게 기다려 무슨 일이 일어나는지 보았소. 무언가가 정녕 일어났소. 그 주인이 불려졌소. 성난 목소리가 위층에서 들렸소. 그 직공이 별안간 다시 나타났는데, 그 주인에게 멱살이 잡히고, 구스베리의 큰 놀라움으로, 술 취한 온갖 표시와 조짐을 내보였소. 그 주인은 그를 문밖으로 밀쳐내고, 그가 다시 오면 경찰을 부르겠다고 으름장을 놓았소. 이것이 벌어지는 동안 그들 사이의 언쟁으로 보아, 그 사내가 십 호에서 발각되었고, 술 취한 고집으로 자기가 그 방을 잡았다고 우겼음이 드러났소. 구스베리는 앞서 말짱하던 사람의 이 별안간의 취기에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그 직공을 뒤쫓아 거리로 뛰어나가는 것을 참을 수 없었소. 그가 그 술집이 보이는 데 있는 한, 그 사내는 더없이 창피한 태로 비틀거렸소. 그가 그 거리 모퉁이를 도는 순간, 그는 대뜸 그의 균형을 되찾고, 당신이 바랄 만큼 말짱한 사회의 일원이 되었소. 구스베리는 아주 어리둥절한 심사로 '운명의 수레바퀴'로 돌아갔소. 그는 무언가가 일어날 요행을 노려 다시 어정거렸소. 아무 일도 일어나지 않았소. 그리고 그 뱃사람에 대해 더는 들리거나 보이는 것이 없었소. 구스베리는 그 사무소로 돌아가기로 정했소. 그가 이 결론에 이르렀을 때, 여느 때처럼 그 길 맞은편에, 다름 아닌 그 직공이 다시 나타난 것이었소! 그는 그 술집 꼭대기의, 유일하게 불이 켜진 한 특정한 창을 올려다보았소. 그 불빛이 그의 마음을 놓이게 하는 듯했소. 그는 대뜸 그곳을 떠났소. 그 소년은 그레이스 인으로 돌아가는 길을 갔소—당신의 명함과 전갈을 받았소—찾아갔소—그리고 당신을 찾는 데에 실패했소. 거기에 그 사건의 상태가, 블레이크 씨, 지금 그대로 있소.\"\n\n\"그 사건에 대한 당신 자신의 견해는 무엇이오, 부장?\"\n\n\"나는 그것이 심각하다고 생각하오, 선생. 그 소년이 본 것으로 판단하건대, 우선, 인도인들이 거기 끼여 있소.\"\n\n\"그렇소. 그리고 그 뱃사람은 명백히 루커 씨가 그 다이아몬드를 건넨 그 사람이오. 브러프 씨와, 나와, 브러프 씨에게 부려진 그 사람이 죄 그 사람이 누구인지에 대해 잘못 안 것은 이상해 보이오.\"\n\n\"조금도 이상하지 않소, 블레이크 씨. 그 사람이 무릅쓴 위험을 헤아리면, 루커 씨가 그들 사이의 미리 짠 약속으로, 일부러 당신을 잘못 이끌었을 공산이 자못 크오.\"\n\n\"당신은 그 술집에서의 일을 이해하오?\" 하고 나는 물었다. \"그 직공처럼 차려입은 사내는 물론 인도인들에게 부려져 움직이고 있었소. 허나 나는 그의 별안간의 취한 척함을 설명하는 데에 구스베리 자신만큼이나 어리둥절하오.\"\n\n\"나는 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 짐작해 볼 수 있을 것 같소, 선생,\" 하고 그 부장이 말했다. \"당신이 곰곰이 생각하면, 당신은 그 사내가 인도인들에게서 자못 엄한 지시를 받았음이 틀림없음을 알 것이오. 그들 자신은 그 은행이나 그 술집에서 보이는 위험을 무릅쓰기에 너무나 눈에 띄었소—그들은 온갖 것을 그들의 대리인에게 맡기지 않을 수 없었소. 좋소. 그들의 대리인은 그 술집에서 어떤 번호가, 그 뱃사람이 그 밤을 위해 잡을 방의 번호로 불리는 것을 듣소—그것은 또한 (우리 생각이 죄 틀리지 않다면) 그 다이아몬드가 그 밤을 위해 잡을 방이기도 하오. 그런 상황에서, 인도인들은, 믿어도 좋소만, 그 방에 대한 서술—그것의 그 집에서의 위치, 그것이 밖에서 다가가질 수 있는지 따위를 얻기를 우겼을 것이오. 그 사내는 이런 명령을 받고, 무엇을 해야 했겠소? 바로 그가 한 것이오! 그는 그 뱃사람이 그 방으로 데려가지기 전에 그 방을 한번 보러 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔소. 그는 거기서, 그의 관찰을 하다가 발각되었소—그리고 그는 그 어려움에서 빠져나오는 가장 손쉬운 방도로, 취한 시늉을 했소. 그것이 내가 그 수수께끼를 읽어 내는 방식이오. 그가 그 술집에서 내쫓긴 뒤, 그는 아마 그의 보고를 가지고 그의 고용주들이 그를 기다리는 곳으로 갔을 것이오. 그리고 그의 고용주들은, 두말할 것 없이, 그 뱃사람이 정말로 이튿날 아침까지 그 술집에 자리 잡았는지 확실히 하러 그를 도로 보냈을 것이오. 그 소년이 떠난 뒤 '운명의 수레바퀴'에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지로 말하자면—우리는 그것을 지난밤에 알아냈어야 했소. 지금은 아침 열한 시요. 우리는 최선을 바라고, 우리가 할 수 있는 것을 알아내야 하오.\"\n\n십오 분이 더 지나, 그 삯마차가 쇼어 레인에 멈추었고, 구스베리가 우리가 내리도록 그 문을 열었다.\n\n\"괜찮으냐?\" 하고 그 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"괜찮습니다,\" 하고 그 소년이 대답했다.\n\n우리가 \"운명의 수레바퀴\"에 들어선 순간 그 집에 무언가 잘못된 것이 있음이 내 미숙한 눈에조차 뚜렷했다.\n\n술이 나오는 그 계산대 뒤의 유일한 사람은, 그 일에 완전히 무지한, 어리둥절한 하녀였다. 아침 술을 기다리는 손님 한둘이, 그들의 돈으로 그 계산대를 조바심을 내며 두드리고 있었다. 그 술청 아가씨가 그 응접실의 안쪽 구역에서, 흥분하여 딴생각에 잠긴 채 나타났다. 그녀는 커프 부장의 그 주인에 대한 물음에, 그녀의 주인이 위층에 있으며, 아무에게도 방해받아서는 안 된다고 날카롭게 말함으로써 답했다.\n\n\"나와 함께 갑시다, 선생,\" 하고 커프 부장이, 태연히 위층으로 앞장서 가고, 그 소년에게 그를 따라오라 손짓하며 말했다.\n\n그 술청 아가씨가 그녀의 주인을 불러, 낯선 이들이 그 집에 밀고 들어오고 있다고 그에게 경고했다. 이 층에서 우리는, 무슨 일인지 보러, 자못 부아 난 상태로 서둘러 내려오는 그 주인과 마주쳤다.\n\n\"당신들은 대체 누구요? 그리고 여기서 무엇을 원하오?\" 하고 그가 물었다.\n\n\"성미를 다스리시오,\" 하고 그 부장이 태연히 말했다. \"우선 내가 누구인지 당신에게 말하겠소. 나는 커프 부장이오.\"\n\n그 이름난 이름이 대뜸 그 효과를 냈다. 그 성난 주인은 응접실 문을 활짝 열고, 그 부장의 용서를 빌었다.\n\n\"저는 부아가 나고 심사가 뒤틀렸습니다, 선생—그것이 사실입니다,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 그 집에서 불쾌한 무언가가 일어났습니다. 제 장사를 하는 사람에게는 그의 성미를 뒤집을 것이 많습니다, 커프 부장.\"\n\n\"조금도 의심할 바 없소,\" 하고 그 부장이 말했다. \"당신이 허락한다면, 나는 대뜸 무엇이 우리를 여기 오게 했는지로 넘어가겠소. 이 신사와 나는 우리 둘 다에게 얼마쯤 관심 있는 일로, 당신에게 몇 가지 물음의 수고를 끼치고 싶소.\"\n\n\"무엇에 관련된 것입니까, 선생?\" 하고 그 주인이 물었다.\n\n\"지난밤 여기서 잔, 뱃사람처럼 차려입은 거무스름한 사내에 관련된 것이오.\"\n\n\"맙소사! 그것이 바로 이 순간 온 집을 뒤집어 놓고 있는 그 사내입니다!\" 하고 그 주인이 외쳤다. \"당신이, 또는 이 신사가 그에 대해 무언가 아십니까?\"\n\n\"우리는 그를 볼 때까지 확신할 수 없소,\" 하고 그 부장이 대답했다.\n\n\"그를 봐요?\" 하고 그 주인이 되뇌었다. \"그것이 바로 오늘 아침 일곱 시 이래 아무도 할 수 없었던 그 한 가지입니다. 그것이, 그가 지난밤, 자기가 불려야 한다고 말을 남긴 그 시각이었습니다. 그는 정녕 불렸습니다—그런데 그에게서 아무 답도 얻어지지 않았고, 무슨 일인지 보러 그의 문을 여는 것도 안 됐습니다. 그들은 여덟 시에 다시 해 봤고, 아홉 시에 다시 해 봤습니다. 소용없었습니다! 그 문은 여전히 잠긴 채였고—그 방에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않았습니다! 저는 오늘 아침 나가 있었습니다—그리고 저는 겨우 십오 분 전에 돌아왔습니다. 저 스스로 그 문을 두드려 봤습니다—그리고 죄 헛일이었습니다. 급사 아이가 목수를 데리러 갔습니다. 만일 당신들이 몇 분 기다려 주신다면, 신사분들, 우리는 그 문을 열어, 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 볼 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그 사내가 지난밤 취해 있었소?\" 하고 커프 부장이 물었다.\n\n\"완전히 말짱했습니다, 선생—그렇지 않았다면 저는 결코 그를 제 집에서 재우지 않았을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그가 미리 그의 잠자리 값을 치렀소?\"\n\n\"아니요.\"\n\n\"그가 그 문으로 나가지 않고, 어떤 식으로든 그 방을 떠날 수 있었소?\"\n\n\"그 방은 다락입니다,\" 하고 그 주인이 말했다. \"허나 천장에, 지붕으로 나가는 뚜껑문이 하나 있습니다—그리고 그 거리 조금 아래에, 수리 중인 빈집이 하나 있습니다. 부장, 당신은 그 악당이 값을 치르지 않고 그런 식으로 달아났다고 생각하십니까?\"\n\n\"뱃사람이라면,\" 하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"그것을 할 수 있었을 것이오—이른 아침에, 거리가 움직이기 전에. 그는 기어오르는 데에 익숙할 것이고, 집 지붕 위에서 그의 머리가 어질하지 않을 것이오.\"\n\n그가 말하는 사이, 그 목수의 다다름이 알려졌다. 우리는 죄, 대뜸, 그 꼭대기 층으로 올라갔다. 나는 그 부장이, 그로서도, 유달리 근엄한 것을 알아챘다. 또한 그가 그 소년에게 (앞서 그로 하여금 우리를 따라오도록 부추긴 뒤에) 우리가 다시 내려올 때까지 아래층 방에서 기다리라고 말한 것이 이상하게 나에게 와닿았다.\n\n그 목수의 망치와 끌이 몇 분 만에 그 문의 저항을 처리했다. 허나 어떤 세간 하나가 그 안쪽에서 그것에 대어져, 바리케이드로 세워져 있었다. 그 문을 밀어, 우리는 이 장애물을 옆으로 밀치고, 그리하여 그 방에 들어갈 수 있었다. 그 주인이 먼저 들어갔다. 그 부장이 둘째로. 그리고 내가 셋째로. 자리한 다른 사람들이 우리를 뒤따랐다.\n\n우리는 죄 그 침상을 향해 보았고, 죄 소스라쳤다.\n\n그 사내는 그 방을 떠나지 않은 채였다. 그는, 옷을 입은 채, 그 침상에 누워 있었다—그의 얼굴 위에, 그것을 완전히 눈에서 감추는 흰 베개를 얹은 채.\n\n\"저것이 무슨 뜻입니까?\" 하고 그 주인이 그 베개를 가리키며 말했다.\n\n커프 부장은, 답하지 않고, 그 침상으로 앞장서 가, 그 베개를 치웠다.\n\n그 사내의 거무스름한 얼굴은 평온하고 고요했다. 그의 검은 머리칼과 수염은 조금, 아주 조금 흐트러져 있었다. 그의 눈은, 흐릿하고 텅 빈 채로, 그 천장을 향해 크게 뜬 채 노려보고 있었다. 그것들의 그 뿌연 눈길과 그 고정된 표정이 나를 소름 끼치게 했다. 나는 몸을 돌려, 그 열린 창으로 갔다. 그들 나머지는, 커프 부장이 남은 곳, 그 침상에 남았다.\n\n\"그가 발작을 일으켰소!\" 하고 나는 그 주인이 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"그는 죽었소,\" 하고 그 부장이 대답했다. \"가장 가까운 의사를 부르러, 그리고 경찰을 부르러 보내시오.\"\n\n그 급사가 그 두 심부름에 보내졌다. 어떤 이상한 홀림이 커프 부장을 그 침상에 붙드는 듯했다. 어떤 이상한 호기심이 그들 나머지를, 그 부장이 다음에 무엇을 하는지 보려 기다리게 하는 듯했다.\n\n나는 다시 그 창으로 돌아섰다. 다음 순간, 나는 내 웃옷 자락에 부드러운 잡아당김을 느꼈고, 자그마한 목소리가 속삭였다, \"여기 보십시오, 나리!\"\n\n구스베리가 우리를 따라 그 방으로 들어와 있었다. 그의 헐거운 눈이 무섭게 굴렀다—공포에서가 아니라, 의기양양함에서. 그는 제 나름으로 탐정의 발견을 한 것이었다. \"여기 보십시오, 나리,\" 하고 그가 되뇌었다—그리고 나를 그 방 모퉁이의 탁자로 이끌었다.\n\n그 탁자에는, 열리고 빈, 자그마한 나무 상자 하나가 서 있었다. 그 상자 한쪽에 얼마쯤의 보석상용 솜이 놓여 있었다. 다른 쪽에는, 그 위에 봉인이 일부 부서진 채로 있고, 여전히 완벽히 읽을 수 있는 글씨로 된 새김이 있는, 찢어진 흰 종이 한 장이 있었다. 그 새김은 이런 말로 되어 있었다.\n\n\"부시, 라이소트, 앤 부시 상회에, 램버스의 미들섹스 플레이스의 셉티머스 루커 씨에 의해, 이 봉투에 봉인되고, 큰 값어치의 귀중품을 담은 자그마한 나무 상자 하나가 예치됨. 그 상자는, 청구될 때, 오직 루커 씨의 직접 신청에 의해서만 부시 앤 코 상회에 의해 내어질 것.\"\n\n그 글줄들은, 적어도 한 가지 점에서는, 온갖 더 이상의 의심을 없앴다. 그 뱃사람은, 그가 그 전날 그 은행을 떠났을 때, 그 월장석을 지니고 있었던 것이다.\n\n나는 내 웃옷 자락에 또 한 번 잡아당김을 느꼈다. 구스베리는 아직 나와 볼일을 다하지 않은 채였다.\n\n\"강도예요!\" 하고 그 소년이, 크게 기뻐하며, 그 빈 상자를 가리키며 속삭였다.\n\n\"너는 아래층에서 기다리라고 일러졌다,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"저리 가라!\"\n\n\"그리고 살인이에요!\" 하고 구스베리가, 한층 더 예리하게 즐기며, 그 침상 위의 사내를 가리키며 덧붙였다.\n\n그 소년이 그 장면의 그 끔찍함을 즐기는 데에 어찌나 흉측한 무언가가 있었던지, 나는 그를 두 어깨로 잡아 그 방 밖으로 내보냈다.\n\n내가 그 문의 문지방을 넘는 그 순간, 나는 커프 부장의 목소리가, 내가 어디 있는지 묻는 것을 들었다. 그는, 내가 그 방으로 돌아가면서, 나를 맞았고, 나로 하여금 그와 함께 그 침상 곁으로 돌아가게 했다.\n\n\"블레이크 씨!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그 사내의 얼굴을 보시오. 그것은 변장한 얼굴이오—그리고 여기 그 증거가 있소!\"\n\n그는 그의 손가락으로, 그 죽은 사내의 이마에서 뒤로, 그 거무스름한 낯빛과 그 조금 흐트러진 검은 머리칼 사이로 뻗은, 검푸르뎅뎅한 흰 가느다란 선을 따라 짚었다. \"이 아래에 무엇이 있는지 봅시다,\" 하고 그 부장이, 별안간 그의 손으로 그 검은 머리칼을 단단히 움켜쥐며 말했다.\n\n내 신경은 그것을 견딜 만큼 튼튼하지 못했다. 나는 다시 그 침상에서 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n그 방의 다른 쪽 끝에서, 내 눈에 든 첫 광경은, 의자에 올라서서, 그의 웃어른들의 머리 너머로, 그 부장의 일을 숨죽인 관심으로 보고 있는, 그 억누를 수 없는 구스베리였다.\n\n\"그가 그의 가발을 벗기고 있어요!\" 하고 구스베리가, 그 방에서 아무것도 볼 수 없는 유일한 사람인 나의 처지를 가엾이 여겨 속삭였다.\n\n멈춤이 있었다—그런 다음 그 침상 둘레의 사람들 사이에 놀라움의 외침이 있었다.\n\n\"그가 그의 수염을 벗겼어요!\" 하고 구스베리가 외쳤다.\n\n또 한 번 멈춤이 있었다—커프 부장이 무언가를 청했다. 그 주인이 세면대로 가, 물 한 대야와 수건을 들고 그 침상으로 돌아왔다.\n\n구스베리가 그 의자 위에서 흥분하여 춤추었다. \"이리 올라와요, 저와 함께요, 나리! 그가 이제 그의 낯빛을 씻어 내고 있어요!\"\n\n그 부장이 별안간 그의 둘레의 사람들을 뚫고 나와, 얼굴에 소름을 담고, 곧장 내가 서 있는 곳으로 왔다.\n\n\"그 침상으로 돌아오시오, 선생!\" 하고 그가 입을 뗐다. 그는 나를 더 가까이 보고, 스스로를 멈추었다. \"아니오!\" 하고 그가 다시 이었다. \"먼저 그 봉인된 편지를 여시오—오늘 아침 내가 당신에게 준 그 편지를.\"\n\n나는 그 편지를 열었다.\n\n\"그 이름을 읽으시오, 블레이크 씨, 내가 안에 적어 둔.\"\n\n나는 그가 적어 둔 그 이름을 읽었다. 그것은—고드프리 에이블화이트였다.\n\n\"이제,\" 하고 그 부장이 말했다. \"나와 함께 와서, 그 침상 위의 사내를 보시오.\"\n\n나는 그와 함께 가서, 그 침상 위의 사내를 보았다. 고드프리 에이블화이트였다!\n\n커프 부장이 기고함.\n\n# I\n\n서리주 도킹, 1849년 7월 30일. 프랭클린 블레이크 님께. 선생—제가 당신께 드리기로 한 그 보고서를 내는 데에 생긴 지체에 대해 사과드립니다. 저는 그것을 온전한 보고서로 만들려 기다렸습니다. 그리고 저는, 이곳저곳에서, 얼마쯤의 인내와 시간을 들여야만 치울 수 있는 장애에 부딪혔습니다.\n\n제가 저 자신에게 세운 그 목표는 이제, 바라건대, 이루어졌습니다. 당신은, 이 쪽들에서, 제가 지난번 당신을 뵙는 영예를 누렸을 때 당신의 마음에 떠오른, 고인이 된 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 관한 물음의 전부는 아니더라도—태반에 대한 답을 찾으실 것입니다.\n\n저는 당신께 말씀드릴 작정입니다—먼저—당신의 사촌이 그의 죽음을 맞은 방식에 대해 알려진 것을. 그 진술에, (제 견해에 따라) 우리가 그 사실에서 이끌어 냄이 정당한 그런 미루어 짐작과 결론을 덧붙이면서.\n\n저는 그런 다음 애쓰겠습니다—둘째로—고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 행동에 관하여, 당신과 그가 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 시골집에서 손님으로 만난 그때 전과, 그동안과, 그 뒤에 대해, 제가 한 그런 발견을 당신께 지니게 해 드리는 것을.\n\n그러면, 당신의 사촌의 죽음에 대해, 먼저.\n\n어떤 합당한 의심의 너머로, 그가 (그가 잠든 동안, 또는 그가 깨어나는 즉시) 그의 침상의 베개로 질식되어 죽임을 당했다는 것—그를 살해한 죄지은 사람들이 그 인도인 셋이라는 것—그리고 그 범죄로 궁리된 (그리고 이루어진) 목표가, 월장석이라 불리는 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣는 것이었다는 것이 확정된 듯합니다.\n\n이 결론이 이끌어 내지는 그 사실들은, 얼마쯤은 그 술집의 그 방에 대한 조사에서, 얼마쯤은 검시 심문에서 얻어진 증거에서 나옵니다.\n\n그 방의 문을 억지로 열자, 그 고인이 된 신사가, 죽어, 그 침상의 베개를 그의 얼굴 위에 얹은 채 발견되었습니다. 그를 검안한 의사가, 이 정황을 전해 듣고, 그 사후의 소견이 질식으로 인한 살인과—다시 말해, 어떤 사람, 또는 사람들이, 폐의 울혈로 죽음이 이르기까지 그 고인의 코와 입 위에 그 베개를 누른, 그런 살인과 완벽히 부합한다고 여겼습니다.\n\n다음으로, 그 범죄의 동기에 대해.\n\n봉인된 종이가 (그 종이는 새김을 담고 있습니다) 뜯긴 자그마한 상자 하나가, 그 방의 탁자 위에 열리고 빈 채로 발견되었습니다. 루커 씨는 몸소 그 상자와, 그 봉인과, 그 새김을 확인했습니다. 그는 그 상자가 정말로 월장석이라 불리는 그 다이아몬드를 담고 있었다고 선언했습니다. 그리고 그는 지난 유월 이십육일 오후에, (그렇게 봉인된) 그 상자를 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 (그때 변장 아래 숨은) 주었음을 인정했습니다. 이 모든 것에서 나오는 정당한 미루어 짐작은, 그 월장석을 훔치는 것이 그 범죄의 동기였다는 것입니다.\n\n다음으로, 그 범죄가 저질러진 방식에 대해.\n\n(높이가 겨우 일곱 자인) 그 방을 조사하니, 천장에, 그 집의 지붕으로 나가는 뚜껑문 하나가 열려 있는 것이 발견되었습니다. 그 뚜껑문에 다다르는 데에 쓰이는 (그리고 침상 아래 보관된) 그 짧은 사다리가, 그 방 안의 어떤 사람이나 사람들이 다시 쉽게 그것을 떠날 수 있게끔, 그 열린 곳에 놓인 채로 발견되었습니다. 그 뚜껑문 자체에는, 그 문을 안쪽에서 잠그는 그 빗장 바로 뒤에, 겉보기에 어떤 지극히 날카로운 도구로 그 나무에 잘린 네모난 구멍이 발견되었습니다. 이런 식으로, 밖의 어떤 사람이든 그 빗장을 도로 당겨, 그 문을 열고, 그 방으로 떨어질 (또는 공범에 의해 소리 없이 내려질) 수 있었을 것입니다—그것의 높이는, 이미 눈여겨보아졌듯, 겨우 일곱 자입니다. 어떤 사람이나 사람들이 이런 식으로 들어갔음이 틀림없다는 것은, 그 구멍이 거기 있다는 사실로 뚜렷한 듯합니다. 그가 (또는 그들이) 그 술집의 지붕에 다다른 방식에 대해서는, 그 거리 조금 아래의 셋째 집이 비고, 수리 중이었다는 것—긴 사다리 하나가 그 인도(人道)에서 그 집 꼭대기로 이어지도록 일꾼들에 의해 남겨져 있었다는 것—그리고 이십칠일 아침에 그들의 일로 돌아오면서, 그 일꾼들이, 그들이 없는 동안 아무도 그것을 쓰지 못하게 그 사다리에 매어 두었던 그 널판이 치워져, 바닥에 놓인 것을 발견했다는 것이 짚어져야 합니다. 이 사다리로 올라가, 집들의 지붕 위를 지나, 도로 지나와, 다시 눈에 띄지 않고 내려올 가능성에 대해서는—야간 경관의 증거로 보아, 그가 순찰을 도는 동안 한 시간에 오직 두 번만 쇼어 레인을 지난다는 것이 밝혀졌습니다. 주민들의 증언도, 쇼어 레인이, 자정 뒤에는, 런던에서 가장 조용하고 가장 외진 거리의 하나임을 밝힙니다. 그러니, 여기서도 다시, 여느 조심과 침착만 있으면, 어떤 사람이나 사람들이 그 사다리로 올라가, 다시 눈에 띄지 않고 내려올 수 있었으리라 미루어 짐작함이 정당해 보입니다. 일단 그 술집의 지붕 위에 오르면, 사람이 그 위에 엎드린 채 그 뚜껑문을 뚫을 수 있고, 그런 자세에서, 그 집 앞의 난간이 그를 거리를 지나는 누구의 눈에서든 가려 주리라는 것이, 실험으로 증명되었습니다.\n\n끝으로, 그 범죄를 저지른 사람, 또는 사람들에 대해.\n\n(1) 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣는 데에 관심이 있었음이 알려져 있습니다. (2) 옥타비어스 가이가 그 삯마차의 창에서, 그 직공처럼 차려입은 사내에게 이야기하는 것을 본, 인도인처럼 보이던 그 사내가, 그 힌두인 음모자 셋 중 하나였음이 적어도 있을 법합니다. (3) 이 같은 직공처럼 차려입은 사내가, 이십육일의 온 저녁 내내 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 눈에 두고 있는 것이 보였고, (에이블화이트 씨가 그 침실로 안내되기 전에) 그가 그 방을 살피고 있었다는 의심으로 이어지는 상황에서 그 침실에서 발견되었음이 확실합니다. (4) 찢어진 금실 조각 하나가 그 침실에서 주워졌는데, 그런 일에 정통한 사람들이, 그것을 인도산이며, 잉글랜드에 알려지지 않은 종류의 금실이라 선언합니다. (5) 이십칠일 아침, 그 인도인 셋의 서술에 들어맞는 세 사내가, 로어 템스가에서 목격되어, 타워 부두까지 추적되었고, 로테르담행 증기선으로 런던을 떠나는 것이 보였습니다.\n\n여기에, 법률적이지는 않더라도, 도덕적인 증거가 있으니, 그 살인이 인도인들에 의해 저질러졌다는 것입니다.\n\n그 직공을 흉내 낸 사내가 그 범죄의 공범이었는지 아니었는지는, 말하기 불가능합니다. 그가 홀로 그 살인을 저지를 수 있었으리라는 것은, 있음 직함의 한계 너머로 보입니다. 홀로 움직여서는, 그가 좀처럼, 몸싸움이 벌어지거나 외침이 들리지 않고서, 에이블화이트 씨를—그 둘 중 더 크고 더 힘센 사내를—질식시킬 수 없었을 것입니다. 옆방에서 자던 하녀는 아무것도 듣지 못했습니다. 아래층 방에서 자던 주인은 아무것도 듣지 못했습니다. 온 증거는, 한 사내 넘게가 이 범죄에 관련되었다는 미루어 짐작을 가리킵니다—그리고 그 상황은, 거듭 말하거니와, 인도인들이 그것을 저질렀다는 결론을 도덕적으로 정당화합니다.\n\n저는 다만, 그 검시 심문에서의 평결이 이름 모를 어떤 사람, 또는 사람들에 대한 고의적 살인이었음을 덧붙이면 됩니다. 에이블화이트 씨의 집안은 현상금을 내걸었고, 그 죄지은 사람들을 알아내려 시도되지 않은 애씀이 없었습니다. 그 직공처럼 차려입은 사내는 온갖 조사를 피했습니다. 그 인도인들은 추적되었습니다. 이 뒤엣사람들을 끝내 붙잡을 전망에 대해서는, 제가 이 보고서의 끝에 이르렀을 때, 그 대목에 대해 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있겠습니다.\n\n그동안, 이제 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 죽음이라는 주제에 대해 필요한 것을 죄 썼으니, 저는 다음으로, 당신과 그가 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 집에서 만난 그때 전과, 그동안과, 그 뒤의 그의 행동의 이야기로 넘어가겠습니다.\n\n# III\n\n지금 다루는 그 주제에 관하여, 저는, 첫머리에, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 삶에는 두 면이 있었음을 진술하겠습니다.\n\n세간의 눈에 드러난 그 면은, 자선 모임에서의 연사로서 상당한 명성을 지니고, 갖가지 자선 협회—대개 여성의 부류의 협회에 그가 마음대로 쓰도록 내놓은 행정 능력을 타고난 신사의 광경을 내놓았습니다. 세간의 눈에서 감추어진 그 면은, 이 같은 신사를, 그 자신의 이름으로 잡히지 않은 교외의 별장 하나와, 역시 그 자신의 이름으로 잡히지 않은, 그 별장의 부인 하나를 둔, 쾌락의 사람이라는 전혀 다른 인물로 내보였습니다.\n\n그 별장에 대한 저의 조사는 저에게 몇 점의 훌륭한 그림과 조각상—고상하게 골라, 훌륭하게 만들어진 세간—그리고 온 런던에서 그 짝을 찾기가 쉽지 않을, 가장 진귀한 꽃들의 온실을 보여 주었습니다. 그 부인에 대한 저의 조사는, 그 꽃들과 어깨를 나란히 할 만한 보석과, (마땅히) 공원에서, 그 하나의 만듦새와, 그 다른 것들의 혈통을 판단하기에 넉넉히 자격 있는 사람들 사이에 화젯거리를 자아낸 마차와 말들의 발견으로 이어졌습니다.\n\n이 모든 것은, 여기까지는, 자못 흔한 것입니다. 그 별장과 그 부인은 런던 삶에서 그토록 낯익은 대상이라, 저는 그것들을 눈길에 소개하는 것에 대해 사과해야겠습니다. 허나 흔하지 않고 낯익지 않은 것은 (제 경험으로는), 이 온갖 훌륭한 것들이 주문되었을 뿐 아니라, 값이 치러졌다는 것입니다. 그 그림, 조각상, 꽃, 보석, 마차, 그리고 말들—조사는, 저의 이루 말할 수 없는 놀라움으로, 그것들 어느 것에도 육 펜스의 빚도 지지 않았음을 증명했습니다. 그 별장으로 말하자면, 그것은 통째로 사들여져, 그 부인에게 물려졌습니다.\n\n저는 이 수수께끼의 옳은 읽기를 찾으려 했으나, 헛되이 했을 것입니다—고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 죽음이, 그의 일의 상태에 대한 조사가 이루어지게 하지 않았다면.\n\n그 조사는 이 사실들을 이끌어 냈습니다—\n\n고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 이만 파운드의 돈의 관리를 맡았다는 것—천팔백사십팔 년에 아직 미성년이던 어느 젊은 신사를 위한 두 신탁인 중 하나로서. 그 신탁이 소멸하기로 되어 있었고, 그 젊은 신사가 천팔백오십 년 이월에, 그가 성년이 되는 날 그 이만 파운드를 받기로 되어 있었다는 것. 이 시기가 다다르기까지, 육백 파운드의 소득이 그의 두 신탁인에 의해 그에게, 반년마다—성탄절과 하지에 치러지기로 되어 있었다는 것. 이 소득이 그 발 벗고 나선 신탁인, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 의해 어김없이 치러졌다는 것. (그 소득이 나온다고 여겨진) 그 이만 파운드가, 천팔백사십칠 년 말로 끝나는 여러 시기에, 그 한 푼도 남김없이 그 기금에서 팔려 나갔다는 것. 그 은행가들에게 그 주식을 팔도록 권한을 주는 위임장과, 그들에게 얼마의 액수를 팔지 이르는 갖가지 글로 된 명령이, 그 두 신탁인 모두에 의해 정식으로 서명되었다는 것. 그 둘째 신탁인의 (시골에 사는 퇴역 육군 장교의) 서명이, 어느 경우에나, 그 발 벗고 나선 신탁인—다시 말해 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 의해 위조된 서명이었다는 것.\n\n이 사실들에, 그 부인과 그 별장을 위해 진 빚을 갚은 고드프리 씨의 명예로운 처신에 대한—그리고 (당신이 곧 보시겠지만) 그 밖의 더 많은 것에 대한 설명이 놓여 있습니다.\n\n우리는 이제 버린더 아가씨의 생일—(천팔백사십팔 년의) 유월 이십일일의 날짜로 나아가도 되겠습니다.\n\n그 전날, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그의 아버지의 집에 다다라, (제가 손위 에이블화이트 씨 자신에게서 아는 대로) 삼백 파운드의 대여를 청했습니다. 그 액수를 눈여겨보십시오. 그리고 동시에, 그 젊은 신사에게의 반년치 지급이 그달 이십사일에 예정되어 있었음을 기억하십시오. 또한, 그 젊은 신사의 재산 전부가 그의 신탁인에 의해 사십칠 년 말로 다 쓰였음을.\n\n손위 에이블화이트 씨는 그의 아들에게 한 푼도 빌려주기를 마다했습니다.\n\n이튿날 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 당신과 함께 레이디 버린더의 집으로 말을 몰았습니다. 몇 시간 뒤, 고드프리 씨가 (당신 자신이 저에게 말했듯이) 버린더 아가씨에게 혼인의 청을 했습니다. 여기서, 그는 두말할 것 없이—받아들여진다면—그의 현재와 미래의 온갖 돈 걱정의 끝으로 가는 그의 길을 보았습니다. 허나, 사건이 실제로 돌아간 대로, 무슨 일이 일어났습니까? 버린더 아가씨가 그를 거절했습니다.\n\n따라서, 그 생일 밤에, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 금전적 처지는 이러했습니다. 그는 그달 이십사일에 마련할 삼백 파운드와, 천팔백오십 년 이월에 마련할 이만 파운드가 있었습니다. 이 액수들을, 이 시기들에, 마련하는 데에 실패하면, 그는 파멸한 사람이었습니다.\n\n그런 상황에서, 다음으로 무슨 일이 일어납니까?\n\n당신이 그 의사 캔디 씨를, 그의 직업이라는 아픈 주제로 발끈하게 합니다. 그리고 그가, 답으로, 아편팅크 한 모금으로 당신에게 실질적인 장난을 부립니다. 그는 자그마한 약병에 채비된 그 약의 투여를, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 맡깁니다—그 자신이 그 일에서 그가 맡은 몫을, 곧 당신께 이야기될 상황에서 털어놓았습니다. 고드프리 씨는 그 음모에 들 그만큼 더 마음이 내켰으니, 그 자신이 그 저녁 동안 당신의 날카로운 혀에 시달렸기 때문입니다. 그는, 당신이 잠자리에 들기 전에 브랜디 물을 좀 마시라고 당신을 설득하는 데에 베터리지에게 합류합니다. 그는 사사로이 그 아편팅크 약을 당신의 찬 그로그에 떨어뜨립니다. 그리고 당신은 그 섞인 것을 마십니다.\n\n우리는 이제 장면을, 부디, 램버스의 루커 씨의 집으로 옮깁시다. 그리고, 머리말 삼아, 브러프 씨와 제가, 함께, 그 돈놀이꾼으로 하여금 죄 털어놓게 만들 수단을 찾아냈음을 짚어 두도록 허락해 주십시오. 우리는 그가 우리에게 부친 그 진술을 조심스레 가려냈습니다. 그리고 여기 그것이 당신을 위해 있습니다.\n\n(사십팔 년) 유월 이십삼일 금요일 저녁 늦게, 루커 씨는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 방문에 놀랐습니다. 고드프리 씨가 그 월장석을 꺼냈을 때, 그는 놀란 것 이상이었습니다. (루커 씨의 경험에 따르면) 유럽의 어떤 개인도 그런 다이아몬드를 지니고 있지 않았습니다.\n\n고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 이 화려한 보석과 관련하여, 조촐한 두 가지 제안을 했습니다. 첫째, 루커 씨가 친절히 그것을 사 주겠는가? 둘째, 루커 씨가 (그 매입으로 갈 길이 보이지 않을 경우) 그것을 위탁으로 팔기를 맡아, 그 예상되는 결과에 대해 얼마쯤을 미리 치러 주겠는가?\n\n루커 씨는, 한마디 답하기 전에, 그 다이아몬드를 시험하고, 그 다이아몬드의 무게를 달고, 그 다이아몬드의 값어치를 어림했습니다. 그의 어림은 (그 돌의 흠을 감안하여) 삼만 파운드였습니다.\n\n그 결과에 이르러, 루커 씨는 그의 입을 열어, 물음 하나를 던졌습니다. \"이것을 어떻게 손에 넣었소?\" 겨우 여섯 마디! 허나 그 안에 얼마나 많은 뜻이 담겨 있는지!\n\n고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 이야기를 시작했습니다. 루커 씨는 다시 그의 입을 열어, 이번에는 오직 세 마디만 했습니다. \"그건 안 되오!\"\n\n고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 또 하나의 이야기를 시작했습니다. 루커 씨는 그에게 더는 말을 허비하지 않았습니다. 그는 일어나, 그 신사를 배웅하도록 하인을 부르는 종을 울렸습니다.\n\n이 강요를 받아, 고드프리 씨는 한 번 애를 써, 다음과 같은 취지로, 새롭고 고쳐진 그 일의 판(版)을 털어놓았습니다.\n\n당신의 브랜디 물에 몰래 그 아편팅크를 흘려 넣은 뒤, 그는 당신에게 잘 자라 인사하고, 그 자신의 방으로 들어갔습니다. 그것은 당신의 방 옆방이었고, 그 둘은 그 사이에 통용문이 있었습니다. 그 자신의 방에 들어가면서 고드프리 씨는 (그가 여긴 대로) 그의 문을 닫았습니다. 그의 돈 걱정이 그를 깨어 있게 했습니다. 그는, 실내복과 슬리퍼 차림으로, 거의 한 시간 동안, 그의 처지를 곰곰이 생각하며 앉아 있었습니다. 막 잠자리에 들 채비를 하던 참에, 그는 당신이, 당신 자신의 방에서, 혼잣말하는 것을 들었고, 그 통용문으로 가서, 그가 여긴 대로 그것을 닫지 않았음을 알았습니다.\n\n그는 무슨 일인지 보러 당신의 방을 들여다보았습니다. 그는 당신이 손에 초를 들고, 막 당신의 침실을 떠나는 것을 알아챘습니다. 그는 당신이, 당신 자신의 목소리와 자못 다른 목소리로, 혼잣말하는 것을 들었습니다, \"내가 어찌 알겠어? 그 인도인들이 그 집에 숨어 있을지도 몰라.\"\n\n그때까지, 그는 (당신에게 그 아편팅크를 주면서) 그저 자기가 당신을 해롭지 않은 실질적인 장난의 희생물로 만드는 것을 돕고 있다고 여겼습니다. 이제 그에게, 그 아편팅크가 당신에게, 그 자신에게도 그렇듯 그 의사에게도 예견되지 않은 어떤 효과를 냈다는 것이 떠올랐습니다. 사고가 일어날까 두려워 그는 당신이 무엇을 할지 보려 당신을 사뿐히 뒤따랐습니다.\n\n그는 당신을 버린더 아가씨의 응접실까지 뒤따라, 당신이 들어가는 것을 보았습니다. 당신은 그 문을 열어 두었습니다. 그는 그 자신이 감히 그 방으로 들어가기 전에, 그리하여 그 문과 문설주 사이에 생긴 그 틈으로 들여다보았습니다.\n\n그 자리에서, 그는 당신이 그 서랍에서 그 다이아몬드를 꺼내는 것을 알아챘을 뿐 아니라—그는 또한 버린더 아가씨가, 그녀의 열린 문을 통해, 그녀의 침실에서 말없이 당신을 지켜보는 것을 알아챘습니다. 그 자신의 눈이, 그녀도 당신이 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 보았음을 그에게 흡족히 여기게 했습니다.\n\n당신이 다시 그 응접실을 떠나기 전에, 당신은 조금 망설였습니다. 고드프리 씨는 이 망설임을 틈타, 당신이 나와 그를 알아채기 전에 다시 그의 침실로 돌아갔습니다. 그가 겨우 돌아가자마자, 당신도 돌아갔습니다. 당신은 (그가 여기기로) 그가 막 그 통용문을 지나가는 참에 그를 보았습니다. 어쨌든, 당신은 이상하고, 졸린 목소리로 그를 불렀습니다.\n\n그는 당신에게 돌아왔습니다. 당신은 흐리멍덩하고 졸린 태로 그를 보았습니다. 당신은 그의 손에 그 다이아몬드를 쥐여 주었습니다. 당신은 그에게 말했습니다, \"이걸 도로 가져가게, 고드프리, 자네 아버지의 은행으로. 그건 거기서 안전해—여기서는 안전하지 않아.\" 당신은 불안하게 몸을 돌려, 당신의 실내복을 걸쳤습니다. 당신은 당신의 방의 그 큰 안락의자에 앉았습니다. 당신은 말했습니다, \"나는 그걸 은행으로 도로 가져갈 수 없어. 내 머리가 납덩이 같아—그리고 내 발이 나를 받치는 게 느껴지지 않아.\" 당신의 머리가 그 의자 등받이에 내려앉았습니다—당신은 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬었습니다—그리고 당신은 잠들었습니다.\n\n고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 그 다이아몬드를 지니고, 그 자신의 방으로 돌아갔습니다. 그의 진술은, 그가, 그때는, 아침에 무슨 일이 일어나는지 기다려 보겠다는 것 말고는—아무 결론에도 이르지 않았다는 것입니다.\n\n아침이 오자, 당신의 말과 처신은 당신이 밤사이 당신이 말하고 한 것을 절대적으로 모름을 보여 주었습니다. 동시에, 버린더 아가씨의 말과 처신은 그녀가 (당신에 대한 자비로) 그녀 편에서 아무 말도 하지 않기로 마음먹었음을 보여 주었습니다. 만일 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그 다이아몬드를 간직하기로 정한다면, 그는 완전히 벌 받지 않고 그리할 수 있었습니다. 그 월장석이 그와 파멸 사이에 서 있었습니다. 그는 그 월장석을 그의 주머니에 넣었습니다.\n\n# V\n\n이것이 (필요의 압박 아래) 당신의 사촌이 루커 씨에게 들려준 이야기였습니다.\n\n루커 씨는 그 이야기가, 온갖 주요한 알맹이로 보아, 참이라고 믿었습니다—이런 근거에서, 곧 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그것을 지어냈다기엔 너무나 큰 바보였다는 것입니다. 브러프 씨와 저는, 그 이야기의 참됨에 대한 이 검증을 더없이 믿을 만한 것으로 여기는 데에, 루커 씨와 뜻을 같이합니다.\n\n다음 물음은, 루커 씨가 그 월장석의 일에서 무엇을 할지의 물음이었습니다. 그는 다음의 조건을, (그의 장사 방면에서조차) 미심쩍고 위태로운 거래인 것에 자신을 얽어 넣는 데에 동의할 유일한 조건으로 제안했습니다.\n\n루커 씨는, 그 월장석이 그에게 저당으로 예치된다는 조건으로, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 이천 파운드의 돈을 빌려주는 데에 동의하겠습니다. 만일, 그 날짜로부터 한 해의 만기에, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 루커 씨에게 삼천 파운드를 치른다면, 그는 되찾아진 저당물로서 그 다이아몬드를 도로 받기로 되어 있었습니다. 만일 그가 그 해의 만기에 그 돈을 내는 데에 실패하면, 그 저당물은 (다시 말해 그 월장석은) 루커 씨에게 몰수된 것으로 여겨지기로 되어 있었습니다—이 뒤엣경우에, 그는 너그럽게 고드프리 씨에게, 그때 그 돈놀이꾼의 소유에 있던 그의 (예전 거래에 관련된) 어떤 약속 어음들을 선물로 줄 터였습니다.\n\n고드프리 씨가 이 괴물 같은 조건을 들으려조차 분개하여 마다한 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없습니다. 이에 루커 씨는 그에게 그 다이아몬드를 도로 건네고, 그에게 잘 자라 인사했습니다.\n\n당신의 사촌은 그 문으로 갔다가, 다시 돌아왔습니다. 그 저녁의 대화가 그의 벗과 그 자신 사이에 엄격히 비밀로 지켜지리라는 것을 그가 어떻게 확신할 수 있는가?\n\n루커 씨는 어떻게인지 안다고 짐짓 나서지 않았습니다. 만일 고드프리 씨가 그의 조건을 받아들였다면, 고드프리 씨는 그를 공범으로 만들었을 것이고, 그의 침묵을 확실한 것으로 헤아릴 수 있었을 것입니다. 사정이 이러하니, 루커 씨는 그 자신의 이해에 따라 움직여야 했습니다. 만일 거북한 조사가 이루어진다면, 그와 거래하기를 마다한 사람을 위해, 그가 어찌 자신을 위태롭게 하기를 기대받겠는가?\n\n이 답을 받고, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 온갖 짐승들이 (사람이든 아니든) 덫에 걸린 것을 알았을 때 하는 것을 했습니다. 그는 어쩔 도리 없는 절망의 상태로 그의 둘레를 둘러보았습니다. 그 돈놀이꾼의 벽난로 위의 상자 속의 단정한 자그마한 카드에 적힌 그달의 날짜가 우연히 그의 눈을 끌었습니다. 그것은 유월 이십삼일이었습니다. 이십사일에 그는 자기가 신탁인 노릇을 하는 그 젊은 신사에게 치를 삼백 파운드가 있었고, 루커 씨가 그에게 내놓은 그 기회 말고는 그 돈을 마련할 아무 기회도 없었습니다. 이 비참한 장애만 아니었다면, 그는 그 다이아몬드를 암스테르담으로 가져가, 그것을 여러 개의 돌로 쪼갬으로써 팔 만한 상품으로 만들 수 있었을 것입니다. 사정이 그러하니, 그는 루커 씨의 조건을 받아들이는 것 말고는 도리가 없었습니다. 어쨌든, 그에게는 그 삼천 파운드를 마련할 한 해가 마음대로 있었습니다—그리고 한 해는 긴 시간입니다.\n\n루커 씨는 그 자리에서 필요한 문서를 작성했습니다. 그것들이 서명되자, 그는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 수표 두 장을 주었습니다. 하나는, 유월 이십삼일 자로, 삼백 파운드짜리. 다른 하나는, 일주일 뒤 자로, 남은 잔액—천칠백 파운드짜리.\n\n그 월장석이 어떻게 루커 씨의 은행가의 보관에 맡겨졌는지, 그리고 (그것이 이루어진 뒤) 인도인들이 어떻게 루커 씨와 고드프리 씨를 다루었는지는, 당신이 이미 아십니다.\n\n당신의 사촌의 삶에서 다음 사건은 다시 버린더 아가씨에 관련됩니다. 그는 두 번째로 그녀에게 혼인의 청을 했습니다—그리고 (받아들여진 뒤) 그녀의 청으로, 그 혼인을 깨진 것으로 여기는 데에 동의했습니다. 이 양보를 한 그의 이유 중 하나를 브러프 씨가 꿰뚫어 보았습니다. 버린더 아가씨는 그녀의 어머니의 재산에 오직 종신 수익권만 있었을 뿐입니다—그리고 그것으로는 그 이만 파운드를 마련할 길이 없었습니다.\n\n허나 당신은, 그가 혼인했다면 그 저당 잡힌 다이아몬드를 되찾을 그 삼천 파운드를 아낄 수 있었을 것이라고 말할 것입니다. 그는 정녕 그리할 수 있었을 것입니다—그의 아내도, 그녀의 후견인과 신탁인들도, 그가 혼인 첫해에 어떤 알 수 없는 목적으로 그가 마음대로 쓸 소득의 절반 넘게를 앞당겨 쓰는 것에 반대하지 않는다고 치면 말입니다. 허나 그가 이 장애를 넘는다 해도, 배경에서 그를 기다리는 또 하나가 있었습니다. 그 별장의 부인이 그가 궁리하는 혼인을 들은 것입니다. 함부로 대할 수 없는 부류의—밝은 낯빛과 로마인 코를 한 부류의, 더없이 당당한 여자였습니다, 블레이크 씨. 그녀는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 더없는 경멸을 느꼈습니다. 만일 그가 그녀에게 넉넉한 살림을 마련해 준다면, 그것은 말 없는 경멸일 터였습니다. 그렇지 않으면, 그것은 혀 달린 경멸일 터였습니다. 버린더 아가씨의 종신 수익권은 그에게 그 \"살림\"을 마련할 희망을 그 이만 파운드를 마련할 희망보다 조금도 더 주지 않았습니다. 그는 혼인할 수 없었습니다—그는 온갖 상황에서, 정말로 혼인할 수 없었습니다.\n\n그가 어떻게 다른 아가씨와 다시 그의 운을 시험했는지, 그리고 그 혼인도 어떻게 돈 문제로 깨졌는지는, 당신이 이미 아십니다. 당신은 또한, 이 매혹적인 사내가 그 호의를 얻어 낸, 그 무른 성(性)의 숱한 흠모자 가운데 하나에 의해, 그 뒤 곧 그에게 남겨진 오천 파운드의 유산에 대해서도 아십니다. 그 유산이 (사건이 증명했듯이) 그를 그의 죽음으로 이끌었습니다.\n\n저는, 그가 그의 오천 파운드를 얻어 외국으로 갔을 때, 그가 암스테르담으로 갔음을 알아냈습니다. 거기서 그는 그 다이아몬드를 여러 개의 돌로 쪼개는 데에 필요한 온갖 채비를 했습니다. 그는 (변장하여) 돌아와, 그 정해진 날에 그 월장석을 되찾았습니다. 그 보석이 실제로 그 은행에서 꺼내지기 전에 (양편이 합의한 방비로서) 며칠이 흐르도록 두어졌습니다. 만일 그가 그것을 지니고 암스테르담에 무사히 이르렀다면, 사십구 년 칠월과, (그 젊은 신사가 성년이 되는) 오십 년 이월 사이에, 그 다이아몬드를 쪼개어, 그 여러 개의 돌로 (연마되었든 안 되었든) 팔 만한 상품을 만들 딱 그만한 시간이 있었을 것입니다. 이것으로, 그가 실제로 무릅쓴 그 위험을 무릅쓴 데에 어떤 동기가 있었는지 판단해 보십시오. 그에게 그것은 \"죽기 아니면 살기\"였습니다—여태 어떤 사람에게 \"죽기 아니면 살기\"였던 적이 있다면 말입니다.\n\n저는 다만, 이 보고서를 닫기 전에, 아직 그 인도인들에게 손을 얹고, 그 월장석을 되찾을 기회가 있음을 당신께 일깨워 드리면 됩니다. 그들은 지금 (믿을 만한 온갖 근거가 있습니다) 동인도 무역선으로, 봄베이로 가는 항해 중에 있습니다. 그 배는 (사고가 없는 한) 나가는 길에 다른 어떤 항구에도 들르지 않을 것입니다. 그리고 봄베이의 당국은 (이미 육로로 편지로 연락되었습니다) 그 배가 항구에 들어서는 순간, 그 배에 오를 채비를 하고 있을 것입니다.\n\n저는 삼가, 친애하는 선생, 당신의 순종하는 종으로 남는 영예를 지닙니다, 리처드 커프 (런던 스코틀랜드 야드 탐정대의 전 부장).*\n\n* 주(註).—이 보고서가 그 생일의, 또는 그것에 뒤이은 사흘의 사건들을 건드리는 곳은 어디든, 베터리지 이야기의 여덟째에서 열셋째 장과 견주어 보십시오.\n\n캔디 씨의 편지에서.\n\n프리징홀, 1849년 9월 26일 수요일.—친애하는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨, 당신은, 에즈라 제닝스에게 보낸 당신의 편지가 뜯기지 않은 채 이 봉투에 담겨 당신께 돌아온 것을 보고, 제가 당신께 전할 그 서글픈 소식을 미리 짐작하실 것입니다. 그는 지난 수요일 해 뜰 녘에, 제 품에서 죽었습니다.\n\n그의 끝이 임박했음을 당신께 경고하지 못한 데에 대해 저는 탓받을 것이 없습니다. 그는 제가 당신께 편지하는 것을 딱 잘라 금했습니다. \"나는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게,\" 하고 그가 말했습니다. \"얼마쯤의 행복한 날들을 본 것을 빚졌소. 그를 괴롭히지 마시오, 캔디 씨—그를 괴롭히지 마시오.\"\n\n그의 괴로움은, 그의 삶의 마지막 여섯 시간까지, 보기에 끔찍했습니다. 뜸해지는 사이에, 그의 마음이 또렷할 때, 저는 그에게 제가 편지할 수 있을 그의 친척이 있으면 저에게 말해 달라고 간청했습니다. 그는 저에게 무엇이든 마다하는 것을 용서받기를 청했습니다. 그리고 그런 다음 그는—쓰라리지 않게—자기가 산 대로, 잊히고 알려지지 않은 채 죽겠다고 말했습니다. 그는 그 결심을 끝까지 지켰습니다. 이제 그에 관해 무슨 발견을 할 희망은 없습니다. 그의 이야기는 백지입니다.\n\n그가 죽기 전날, 그는 저에게 그의 온갖 서류를 어디서 찾을지 말해 주었습니다. 저는 그것들을 그의 침상의 그에게 가져다주었습니다. 그가 옆으로 밀어 놓은 낡은 편지 한 묶음이 있었습니다. 그의 미완성 책이 있었습니다. 그의 일기가—숱한 잠긴 권으로 있었습니다. 그는 올해의 권을 펴, 당신과 그가 함께 있던 그때에 관련된 쪽들을 하나씩 뜯어냈습니다. \"그것들을,\" 하고 그가 말했습니다. \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 주시오. 앞으로 오는 세월에, 그는 거기 적힌 것을 되돌아보는 데에 관심을 느낄지도 모르오.\" 그런 다음 그는 두 손을 맞잡고, 당신과, 당신에게 소중한 이들을 축복하시기를 하느님께 열렬히 기도했습니다. 그는 당신을 다시 보고 싶다고 말했습니다. 허나 다음 순간 그는 마음을 바꾸었습니다. \"아니오,\" 하고 그는 제가 편지하겠다고 하자 대답했습니다. \"나는 그를 괴롭히지 않겠소! 나는 그를 괴롭히지 않겠소!\"\n\n그의 청으로 저는 다음으로 다른 서류들을—다시 말해, 그 편지 묶음과, 그 미완성 책과, 그 일기의 여러 권을 모아—그것들을 죄 한 싸개에 넣어, 저 자신의 봉인으로 봉했습니다. \"약속해 주시오,\" 하고 그가 말했습니다. \"당신 자신의 손으로 이것을 나의 관에 넣어 주겠다고. 그리고 그 뒤로 다른 어떤 손도 그것에 닿지 않게 지켜보겠다고.\"\n\n저는 그에게 저의 약속을 주었습니다. 그리고 그 약속은 지켜졌습니다.\n\n그는 저에게 그를 위해 다른 한 가지 일을 해 달라고 청했습니다—그것을 따르는 데에 저는 힘든 씨름을 치렀습니다. 그가 말했습니다, \"나의 무덤이 잊히게 하시오. 어떤 종류의 기념비도—가장 흔한 묘비조차—나의 묻힌 자리를 표시하도록 허락하지 않겠다고 나에게 명예를 건 약속을 주시오. 나를 이름 없이 잠들게 하시오. 나를 알려지지 않은 채 쉬게 하시오.\" 제가 그에게 그 결심을 바꾸라고 사정하려 하자, 그는 처음이자 유일하게, 격렬히 동요했습니다. 저는 그것을 차마 볼 수 없었습니다. 그래서 저는 굴복했습니다. 자그마한 풀 봉분 말고는 아무것도 그의 안식의 자리를 표시하지 않습니다. 세월이 흐르면, 그 둘레에 묘비들이 솟아오를 것입니다. 그리고 우리 뒤에 오는 사람들은 그 이름 없는 무덤을 보고 의아해할 것입니다.\n\n당신께 말씀드렸듯이, 그의 죽음 전 여섯 시간 동안 그의 괴로움이 그쳤습니다. 그는 조금 졸았습니다. 제 생각에 그는 꿈을 꾸었습니다. 한두 번 그는 미소 지었습니다. 한 여자의 이름이, 제가 짐작하기로—\"엘라\"라는 이름이—이때 자주 그의 입술에 올랐습니다. 끝 몇 분 전에 그는 저에게, 그 창을 통해 해가 뜨는 것을 보게, 그를 그의 베개 위로 일으켜 달라고 청했습니다. 그는 아주 약했습니다. 그의 머리가 제 어깨에 떨어졌습니다. 그가 속삭였습니다, \"그것이 오고 있어요!\" 그런 다음 그가 말했습니다, \"나에게 입 맞춰 주시오!\" 저는 그의 이마에 입 맞추었습니다. 별안간 그가 그의 머리를 들었습니다. 그 햇빛이 그의 얼굴에 닿았습니다. 아름다운 표정이, 천사 같은 표정이 그 위에 번졌습니다. 그가 세 번 외쳤습니다, \"평화! 평화! 평화!\" 그의 머리가 다시 제 어깨에 내려앉았고, 그의 삶의 그 긴 고난이 끝났습니다.\n\n그리하여 그는 우리에게서 갔습니다. 이 사람은, 제 생각에, 위대한 사람이었습니다—세상은 결코 그를 알지 못했지만. 그는 제가 여태 만난 가장 다정한 성미를 지녔습니다. 그를 잃은 것이 저를 아주 외롭게 느끼게 합니다. 어쩌면 저는 저의 병 이래 아주 저 자신인 적이 없었는지도 모릅니다. 때로, 저는 저의 개업을 접고, 떠나서, 어떤 외국의 온천과 물이 저에게 무엇을 해 줄지 시험해 볼까 생각합니다.\n\n여기서는, 당신과 버린더 아가씨가 다음 달에 혼인하기로 되어 있다는 소문이 돕니다. 부디 저의 진심 어린 축하를 받아 주십시오.\n\n저의 가엾은 벗의 일기의 그 쪽들이 저의 집에서 당신을 기다리고 있습니다—봉해져, 그 싸개에 당신의 이름을 적은 채로. 저는 그것들을 우편에 맡기기가 두려웠습니다.\n\n저의 진심 어린 존경과 좋은 바람이 버린더 아가씨께 함께합니다. 저는, 친애하는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨, 참으로 당신의 것으로 남습니다, 토머스 캔디.\n\n게이브리얼 베터리지가 기고함.\n\n저는 (두말할 것 없이 당신이 기억하듯이) 이 쪽들에서 앞장서서, 그 이야기를 연 사람입니다. 저는 또한, 말하자면, 그 이야기를 닫도록 뒤에 남겨진 사람입니다.\n\n제가 여기서 그 인도 다이아몬드에 관해 무슨 마지막 말을 할 것이 있다고는 아무도 여기지 마십시오. 저는 그 불운한 보석을 혐오하여 붙듭니다—그리고 지금으로서 당신이 받으리라 기대될 만한 그 월장석에 대한 소식으로는, 저 말고 다른 권위에게로 당신을 넘깁니다. 이 자리에서의 저의 목적은, 모두에게 지나쳐진, 그리고 저는 그런 식으로 무례하게 묻혀 버리도록 두지 않을, 그 집안의 내력의 한 사실을 진술하는 것입니다. 제가 넌지시 말하는 그 사실은—레이철 아가씨와 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 혼인입니다. 이 흥미로운 사건은 우리 요크셔의 집에서, 천팔백사십구 년 시월 구일 화요일에 일어났습니다. 저는 이 자리에 새 정장 한 벌을 입었습니다. 그리고 그 부부는 스코틀랜드에서 신혼여행을 보내러 갔습니다.\n\n저의 가엾은 여주인의 죽음 이래 우리 집에서 집안 잔치가 자못 드물었던지라, 저는—이 혼인의 자리에—(그날의 후반 무렵) 그것을 빌미로 한 모금 지나치게 마셨음을 인정합니다.\n\n만일 당신이 여태 스스로 같은 종류의 일을 해 본 적이 있다면 당신은 이해하고 저를 헤아려 줄 것입니다. 만일 그렇지 않다면, 당신은 십중팔구 이렇게 말할 것입니다, \"역겨운 늙은이! 어째서 그는 우리에게 이것을 말하는가?\" 그 까닭이 이제 나옵니다.\n\n그러니, 저의 한 모금을 마시고 (원 참! 당신에게도 당신이 좋아하는 나쁜 버릇이 있지요. 다만 당신의 버릇이 제 것이 아니고, 제 것이 당신 것이 아닐 뿐입니다), 저는 다음으로 그 하나의 틀림없는 약을 썼습니다—그 약은, 아시다시피, 「로빈슨 크루소」입니다. 제가 그 견줄 데 없는 책을 어디서 폈는지는, 저는 말할 수 없습니다. 허나 그 인쇄된 글줄들이 마침내 서로 뒤섞여 흐르기를 그친 곳은, 저는 완벽히 잘 압니다. 그것은 삼백열여덟 쪽이었습니다—로빈슨 크루소의 혼인에 관한, 다음과 같은 집안일의 한 대목이었습니다.\n\n\"그러한 생각과 더불어, 나는 나의 새로운 언약을, 곧 나에게 아내가 있다는 것을 헤아렸다\"—(눈여겨보십시오! 프랭클린 나리께도 그러했습니다!)—\"태어난 아이 하나가\"—(다시 눈여겨보십시오! 그것도 아직 프랭클린 나리의 경우가 될 수 있었습니다!)—\"그리고 그때 나의 아내가\"—로빈슨 크루소의 아내가 \"그때\" 했거나 하지 않은 것을, 저는 알아낼 아무 바람도 느끼지 못했습니다. 저는 그 아이에 관한 그 대목을 제 연필로 표시하고, 그 자리를 지키려 종잇조각 하나를 표로 끼웠습니다. \"너는 거기 누워 있어라,\" 하고 저는 말했습니다. \"프랭클린 나리와 레이철 아가씨의 혼인이 몇 달 더 묵을 때까지—그런 다음 우리가 보자!\"\n\n그 달들이 지났습니다 (제가 셈한 것보다 더 많이). 그리고 그 책의 그 표를 건드릴 아무 계제도 나타나지 않았습니다. 이 지금의 달, 천팔백오십 년 십일월에 이르러서야, 프랭클린 나리가 기분이 아주 좋아, 제 방으로 들어와, 말했습니다, \"베터리지! 자네에게 알릴 소식이 있네! 우리가 몇 달 더 묵기 전에, 그 집에서 무슨 일이 일어날 참일세.\"\n\n\"그것이 그 집안에 관련됩니까, 나리?\" 하고 저는 물었습니다.\n\n\"그것은 정녕 그 집안에 관련되네,\" 하고 프랭클린 나리가 말합니다.\n\n\"부디, 나리, 나리의 착한 부인이 그것과 무슨 상관이 있습니까?\"\n\n\"그녀가 그것과 아주 큰 상관이 있네,\" 하고 프랭클린 나리가, 조금 놀란 낯빛을 하기 시작하며 말합니다.\n\n\"한마디도 더 하실 것 없습니다, 나리,\" 하고 저는 대답했습니다. \"두 분을 하느님이 축복하시기를! 저는 그것을 듣게 되어 진심으로 기쁩니다.\"\n\n프랭클린 나리는 벼락 맞은 사람처럼 빤히 쳐다보았습니다. \"감히 자네가 어디서 그 정보를 얻었는지 물어봐도 되겠나?\" 하고 그가 물었습니다. \"나는 겨우 오 분 전에 (더없이 엄격한 비밀로 전해져) 내 것을 얻었네.\"\n\n여기 「로빈슨 크루소」를 내놓을 기회가 있었습니다! 여기 제가 프랭클린 나리의 혼인의 날에 표시해 둔 그 아이에 관한 그 집안일의 대목을 읽을 기회가 있었습니다! 저는 그 기적 같은 말들을, 그것들에 마땅한 힘을 주어 읽고, 그런 다음 그를 엄히 얼굴을 마주 보았습니다. \"이제, 나리, 나리는 「로빈슨 크루소」를 믿으십니까?\" 하고 저는 그 자리에 어울리는 엄숙함으로 물었습니다.\n\n\"베터리지!\" 하고 프랭클린 나리가, 못지않은 엄숙함으로 말합니다. \"나는 마침내 확신하네.\" 그는 저와 악수했습니다—그리고 저는 제가 그를 개종시켰음을 느꼈습니다.\n\n이 유별난 정황의 이야기와 더불어, 이 쪽들에서의 저의 다시 나타남이 끝에 이릅니다. 여기 이야기된 그 유일무이한 일화를 아무도 비웃지 마십시오. 당신은 제가 쓴 다른 온갖 것에 대해서는 마음껏 즐거워해도 좋습니다. 허나 제가 「로빈슨 크루소」에 대해 쓸 때는, 맹세코 그것은 진지합니다—그리고 저는 당신이 그것을 그에 따라 받아들이기를 청합니다!\n\n이것이 말해졌으니, 온갖 것이 말해졌습니다. 신사 숙녀 여러분, 저는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 그 이야기를 닫습니다.\n\n지난 유월 이십칠일, 저는 커프 부장에게서, 살인의 혐의를 받고, 인도인으로 서술된 세 사내를 뒤따르라는 지시를 받았습니다. 그들은 그날 아침 타워 부두에서, 로테르담행 증기선에 오르는 것이 목격된 터였습니다.\n\n저는 다른 회사에 속한 증기선으로 런던을 떠났는데, 그것은 이십팔일 목요일 아침에 출항했습니다. 로테르담에 다다라, 저는 그 수요일 증기선의 선장을 찾는 데에 성공했습니다. 그는 저에게, 그 인도인들이 정녕 그의 배의 승객이었으나—오직 그레이브젠드까지였을 뿐이라고 알려 주었습니다. 그곳 앞바다에서, 그 셋 중 하나가 그들이 몇 시에 칼레에 다다르는지 물었습니다. 그 증기선이 로테르담으로 가는 것임을 알게 되자, 그 일행의 대변인이 그와 그의 두 벗이 저지른 그 잘못에 더없는 놀라움과 괴로움을 나타냈습니다. 그들은 죄 (그가 말하기로), 만일 그 증기선의 선장이 그저 그들을 뭍에 내려 주기만 한다면, 그들의 뱃삯을 버리려 했습니다. 낯선 땅의 외국인으로서의 그들의 처지를 딱하게 여기고, 그들을 붙들 아무 이유도 알지 못하여, 그 선장은 뭍배에 신호했고, 그 세 사내가 그 배를 떠났습니다.\n\n인도인들의 이 행동이, 그들이 추적되는 것을 막을 수단으로, 미리 뚜렷이 정해진 것이었으므로, 저는 지체 없이 잉글랜드로 돌아왔습니다. 저는 그레이브젠드에서 그 증기선을 떠나, 그 인도인들이 그곳에서 런던으로 갔음을 알아냈습니다. 거기서, 저는 다시 그들이 플리머스로 떠났음을 추적했습니다. 플리머스에서 이루어진 조사는, 그들이 마흔여덟 시간 전에, 봄베이로 곧장 가는 동인도 무역선 《뷸리 캐슬》호로 출항했음을 증명했습니다.\n\n이 정보를 받고, 커프 부장은 봄베이의 당국이 육로로 연락되게 했습니다—그 배가 항구에 들어서는 즉시 경찰에 의해 그 배에 오름이 있을 수 있도록. 이 조치가 취해졌으니, 그 일과 저의 연은 끝에 이르렀습니다. 저는 그때 이래 그것에 대해 더는 아무것도 듣지 못했습니다.\n\n저는 커프 부장에게, 지난여름, 제 지휘 아래 봄베이로 곧장 가는 배 《뷸리 캐슬》호의 승객이던, (힌두인으로 여겨지는) 세 사내에 관한 어떤 사실들을 글로 적으라는 청을 받았습니다.\n\n그 힌두인들은 플리머스에서 우리와 합류했습니다. 나가는 항해에서 저는 그들의 처신에 대한 아무 불평도 듣지 못했습니다. 그들은 그 배의 앞쪽에 침상을 잡았습니다. 저 자신은 그들을 몸소 눈여겨볼 계제가 몇 번밖에 없었습니다.\n\n그 항해의 후반부에, 우리는 인도 해안 앞바다에서 사흘 낮과 밤 동안 바람이 자는 불운을 겪었습니다. 저는 참조할 배의 일지를 가지고 있지 않고, 저는 이제 그 위도와 경도를 떠올릴 수 없습니다. 따라서, 우리의 위치에 대해서는, 저는 다만, 조류가 우리를 뭍 쪽으로 떠밀었다는 것과, 바람이 우리를 다시 찾았을 때, 우리가 그 뒤 스물네 시간 만에 우리 항구에 다다랐다는 것만 대략 진술할 수 있습니다.\n\n배의 규율은 (온갖 뱃사람이 알듯) 긴 무풍 속에서 느슨해집니다. 제 배의 규율이 느슨해졌습니다. 승객 가운데 어떤 신사들이 그 더 작은 배 몇 척을 내리게 하여, 저녁때 해가 그런 식으로 스스로를 즐기게 할 만큼 서늘할 때, 노 저어 돌아다니고, 헤엄치며, 스스로를 즐겼습니다. 그 배들은 볼일이 끝나면 그 자리에 다시 달아 올려져야 했습니다. 이 대신에 그것들은 그 배의 옆구리에 매인 채로 두어졌습니다. 더위 탓인지, 날씨의 짜증 탓인지, 그 무풍이 이어지는 동안 관원도 선원도 그들의 의무에 마음이 없는 듯했습니다.\n\n셋째 밤에, 갑판의 당직이 이상한 아무것도 듣거나 보지 못했습니다. 아침이 오자, 그 배들 중 가장 작은 것이 사라졌습니다—그리고 다음으로 그 세 힌두인도 사라졌다고 보고되었습니다.\n\n만일 이 사내들이 어둠이 진 직후에 그 배를 훔쳤다면 (저는 그들이 그리했음을 조금도 의심치 않습니다), 우리는 아침에 그 발견이 이루어졌을 때 그들을 뒤쫓아 보내는 것이 헛되게 할 만큼 뭍에 가까이 있었습니다. 저는 그들이, 그 바람 잔 날씨에 (피로와 서투른 노 젓기를 온갖 마땅히 감안하더라도), 동트기 전에 뭍에 올랐음을 조금도 의심치 않습니다.\n\n우리 항구에 다다라, 저는 거기서, 처음으로, 이 승객들이 그 배에서 달아날 기회를 붙잡은 이유를 알게 되었습니다. 저는 당국에, 제가 여기서 한 것과 같은 진술만 할 수 있었습니다. 그들은 그 배의 규율이 느슨해지도록 둔 데에 저를 탓받을 만하다고 여겼습니다. 저는 이 점에 대한 저의 유감을 그들에게, 그리고 저의 선주들에게 나타냈습니다.\n\n그때 이래, 제가 아는 한 그 세 힌두인에 대해 아무것도 들리지 않았습니다. 저는 여기 적힌 것에 더 보탤 것이 없습니다.\n\n(브러프 씨에게 보내는 편지에서.)\n\n제 친애하는 선생, 당신은 사십팔 년 가을에 런던에서 만찬에서 만난 어떤 반쯤 야만인 같은 사람을 기억하십니까? 그 사람의 이름이 머스웨이트였고, 당신과 그가 저녁 뒤에 함께 긴 대화를 나눴음을 당신께 일깨워 드리는 것을 허락해 주십시오. 그 이야기는 월장석이라 불리는 인도 다이아몬드에 관한 것과, 그때 그 보석을 손에 넣으려 존재하던 음모에 관한 것이었습니다.\n\n그때 이래, 저는 중앙아시아를 떠돌았습니다. 거기서 저는 인도 북부와 북서부의 저의 지난 모험 몇몇의 무대로 도로 흘러들었습니다. 두 주쯤 전에, 저는 카티아와르라 불리는 (허나 유럽인에게는 거의 알려지지 않은) 어떤 지역, 곧 주(州)에 있는 저 자신을 보았습니다.\n\n여기서 저에게, 그 안에서 (믿기 어려워 보일지 몰라도) 당신이 몸소 관련된 모험이 닥쳤습니다.\n\n카티아와르의 그 거친 지역에서 (그리고 그것들이 얼마나 거친지는, 심지어 농사꾼조차 이를 데까지 무장하고 땅을 간다고 제가 당신께 말하면, 당신이 이해하실 것입니다), 그 주민은 광신적으로 그 옛 힌두 종교에—브라마와 비슈누의 그 오랜 숭배에 바쳐져 있습니다. 그 내지의 마을들에 드문드문 흩어진 몇 안 되는 이슬람교도 집안은, 어떤 종류의 고기도 맛보기를 두려워합니다. 그 신성한 짐승인 소를 죽였다는 의심을 받기만 해도, 이슬람교도는, 당연한 일로, 이 지역에서 그를 둘러싼 독실한 힌두 이웃들에 의해 인정사정없이 죽임을 당합니다. 그 사람들의 종교적 열정을 굳히려, 힌두 순례의 가장 이름난 성소 둘이 카티아와르의 경계 안에 담겨 있습니다. 그중 하나는 드와르카로, 신 크리슈나의 태어난 곳입니다. 다른 하나는 솜나트의 신성한 도시로—일찍이 십일 세기에, 이슬람 정복자 가즈니의 마무드에 의해 노략질되고 파괴되었습니다.\n\n두 번째로, 이 낭만적인 지역에 있는 저 자신을 보고, 저는 솜나트의 그 장엄한 황폐를 한 번 더 보지 않고서는 카티아와르를 떠나지 않기로 마음먹었습니다. 제가 이것을 하려 계획한 곳에서, 저는 (제가 헤아릴 수 있는 한 가까이) 그 신성한 도시에서, 걸어서, 사흘쯤 떨어져 있었습니다.\n\n제가 그 길에 오른 지 오래지 않아, 저는 다른 사람들이—둘씩 셋씩—저와 같은 방향으로 여행하는 듯한 것을 알아챘습니다.\n\n저에게 말을 건 이들에게는, 저는 저를 먼 주에서 온, 순례길에 오른 힌두-불교도로 내세웠습니다. 저의 옷차림이 이 서술을 뒷받침할 부류의 것이었음은 굳이 말할 것도 없습니다. 여기에다, 제가 그 말을 제 것만큼 잘 알고, 제가 저의 유럽 태생을 알아채는 것을 쉽지 않은 일로 만들 만큼 여위고 볕에 그을렸다는 것을 더하면—당신은 제가 그 사람들에게 어렵잖게 무사히 통했음을 이해하실 것입니다. 그들 중 하나로서가 아니라, 그들 자신의 나라의 먼 지역에서 온 낯선 이로서.\n\n둘째 날에, 저의 방향으로 여행하는 힌두인의 수가 쉰씩 백씩으로 불었습니다. 셋째 날에, 그 무리가 수천으로 불어났습니다. 죄 천천히 한 지점—솜나트의 도시로 모여들며.\n\n셋째 날의 여정 동안, 제가 제 동료 순례자 하나에게 베풀 수 있었던 하찮은 도움이, 저를 어떤 높은 계급의 힌두인들에게 소개하는 수단으로 드러났습니다. 이 사람들에게서 저는 그 무리가 솜나트에서 조금 떨어진 언덕에서 열리기로 되어 있던 큰 종교 의식으로 가는 길임을 알게 되었습니다. 그 의식은 달의 신을 기려서였고, 그것은 밤에 열리기로 되어 있었습니다.\n\n우리가 그 거행의 자리에 가까이 다가가면서 그 무리가 우리를 붙들었습니다. 우리가 그 언덕에 다다랐을 무렵 그 달이 하늘에 높이 떠 있었습니다. 저의 힌두 벗들은 그 성소에 다다를 수 있게 하는 어떤 각별한 특권을 지니고 있었습니다. 그들은 친절히 저를 동행하게 해 주었습니다. 우리가 그 자리에 다다랐을 때, 우리는 그 성소가 두 그루의 장엄한 나무 사이에 걸린 커튼에 우리 눈에서 가려진 것을 보았습니다. 그 나무들 아래에 바위의 평평한 돌출부가 튀어나와, 일종의 자연스러운 단(壇)을 이루었습니다. 이 아래에, 저는, 저의 힌두 벗들과 함께 섰습니다.\n\n그 언덕 아래를 되돌아보니, 그 경치는 제가 여태 본, 자연과 사람이 어우러진 가장 웅장한 광경을 내놓았습니다. 그 높은 곳의 낮은 비탈이 어느새 풀밭의 평야로 녹아들었는데, 세 강이 만나는 곳이었습니다. 한쪽으로, 그 물의 우아한 굽이침이, 이제는 보이고, 이제는 나무들에 가려지며, 눈이 볼 수 있는 데까지 뻗어 있었습니다. 다른 쪽으로, 물결 없는 대양이 그 밤의 고요 속에 잠들어 있었습니다. 이 사랑스러운 장면을, 죄 흰 옷을 입고, 그 언덕의 옆을 따라 뻗어 내리고, 그 평야로 넘쳐흐르고, 그 굽이치는 강의 더 가까운 기슭을 두른, 수만의 인간 존재로 채우십시오. 순례자들의 이 멈춤을, 그 헤아릴 수 없는 무리의 온갖 부분에서 이따금 솟구쳐 오르는, 화톳불과 횃불의 거친 붉은 불꽃으로 밝히십시오. 그 위에 온통, 구름 한 점 없는 영광으로 쏟아지는 동방의 달빛을 상상하십시오—그러면 당신은 제가 그 언덕 꼭대기에서 앞을 내다보았을 때 저를 맞은 그 경치에 대한 얼마쯤의 생각을 그리게 될 것입니다.\n\n현악기와 피리로 연주된 애처로운 가락 한 줄기가, 저의 주의를 그 가려진 성소로 도로 불렀습니다.\n\n저는 돌아서서, 그 바위 단 위에서 세 사내의 모습을 보았습니다. 그 셋 중 가운데 모습에서 저는, 그 인도인들이 레이디 버린더의 집의 테라스에 나타났을 때, 잉글랜드에서 제가 이야기한 그 사내를 알아보았습니다. 그때 그의 동무였던 다른 둘도 두말할 것 없이 이번에도 그의 동무였습니다.\n\n제가 곁에 서 있던 구경꾼 하나가 제가 소스라치는 것을 보았습니다. 속삭임으로, 그는 저에게 그 바위 단 위의 세 모습의 나타남을 풀이해 주었습니다.\n\n그들은 (그가 말하기로) 그 신을 섬기다 그들의 계급을 잃은 브라만들이었습니다. 그 신은 그들의 정화가 순례에 의한 정화여야 한다고 명했습니다. 그 밤에, 그 세 사내는 헤어지기로 되어 있었습니다. 세 갈래의 다른 방향으로, 그들은 인도의 성소들로 가는 순례자로 길을 나서기로 되어 있었습니다. 다시는 그들은 서로의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었습니다. 다시는 그들은 그들의 헤어짐을 지켜본 그날로부터, 그들의 죽음을 지켜볼 그날까지, 그들의 떠돎에서 쉴 수 없었습니다.\n\n그 말들이 저에게 속삭여지자, 그 애처로운 가락이 그쳤습니다. 그 세 사내는, 그 성소를 가린 그 커튼 앞에서, 그 바위에 엎드렸습니다. 그들은 일어섰습니다—그들은 서로를 보았습니다—그들은 끌어안았습니다. 그런 다음 그들은 그 사람들 사이로 따로따로 내려갔습니다. 그 사람들이 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에 그들에게 길을 내주었습니다. 세 갈래의 다른 방향으로 저는 그 무리가, 하나의 같은 순간에 갈라지는 것을 보았습니다. 천천히 그 사람들의 웅장한 흰 덩어리가 다시 함께 닫혔습니다. 그 죽음이 정해진 사내들의, 그들의 동료 인간의 대열을 지나는 그 자취가 지워졌습니다. 우리는 그들을 다시 보지 못했습니다.\n\n크고 환희에 찬 새로운 가락이, 그 가려진 성소에서 솟아올랐습니다. 제 둘레의 무리가 몸서리치며, 함께 밀착했습니다.\n\n그 나무들 사이의 그 커튼이 옆으로 젖혀졌고, 그 성소가 눈에 드러났습니다.\n\n거기, 왕좌 위에 높이 올려져—그의 상징인 영양 위에 앉아, 그의 네 팔을 땅의 네 귀퉁이를 향해 뻗은 채—거기, 하늘의 그 신비로운 빛 속에 어둡고 무섭게, 우리 위로 솟아오른, 달의 신이 있었습니다. 그리고 거기, 그 신의 이마에, 그 광채가 잉글랜드에서, 한 여인의 드레스 가슴께에서, 저에게 마지막으로 빛났던 그 노란 다이아몬드가 어른거렸습니다!\n\n그렇습니다! 여덟 세기가 흐른 뒤, 그 월장석이, 그 이야기가 처음 시작된 그 신성한 도시의 벽 너머로, 다시 한 번 앞을 내다봅니다. 그것이 어떻게 그 거친 고향 땅으로 도로 돌아가는 길을 찾았는지—어떤 우연으로, 또는 어떤 범죄로, 인도인들이 그들의 신성한 보석을 도로 손에 넣었는지는, 당신의 앎에는 있을지 몰라도, 저의 앎에는 없습니다. 당신은 잉글랜드에서 그것을 시야에서 잃었고, (제가 이 사람들에 대해 무언가 안다면) 당신은 그것을 영영 시야에서 잃었습니다.\n\n그리하여 세월이 지나고, 서로를 되풀이합니다. 그리하여 같은 사건들이 시간의 순환 속에 돌아옵니다. 그 월장석의 다음 모험은 무엇일까요? 누가 알 수 있으랴?";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-155"]=[[["# I", "# I"]],[["I address these lines—written in India—to my relatives in England.", "(어느 가문의 문서에서 발췌한 것이다.)"]], [["My object is to explain the motive which has induced me to refuse the right hand of friendship to my cousin, John Herncastle.", "인도에서 쓰는 이 글을 나는 영국에 있는 내 친척들에게 부친다. 내 목적은, 사촌 존 헌캐슬에게 우정의 손을 내밀기를 거부하게 된 그 동기를 밝히려는 데 있다."], ["The reserve which I have hitherto maintained in this matter has been misinterpreted by members of my family whose good opinion I cannot consent to forfeit.", "지금까지 내가 이 문제에 관해 지켜 온 침묵은, 그 호의를 차마 잃고 싶지 않은 가족들에게 오해를 사 왔다."], ["I request them to suspend their decision until they have read my narrative.", "나는 그들에게, 내 이야기를 다 읽기 전에는 판단을 미루어 달라고 청한다."], ["And I declare, on my word of honour, that what I am now about to write is, strictly and literally, the truth.", "그리고 나는 명예를 걸고 선언하거니와, 이제부터 쓰려는 것은 엄밀히, 그리고 글자 그대로 진실이다."]], [["The private difference between my cousin and me took its rise in a great public event in which we were both concerned—the storming of Seringapatam, under General Baird, on the 4th of May, 1799.", "사촌과 나 사이의 사사로운 불화는, 우리 둘 다 얽혀 있던 큰 공적 사건에서 비롯되었다—1799년 5월 4일, 베어드 장군의 지휘 아래 감행된 세링가파탐 공략이 그것이다."]], [["In order that the circumstances may be clearly understood, I must revert for a moment to the period before the assault, and to the stories current in our camp of the treasure in jewels and gold stored up in the Palace of Seringapatam.", "그 정황을 분명히 이해시키기 위해, 나는 잠시 공격 이전의 시기로, 그리고 세링가파탐 궁전에 쌓여 있다는 보석과 황금의 보물에 관하여 우리 진영에 떠돌던 이야기들로 거슬러 올라가야겠다."]], [["# II", "# II"]], [["One of the wildest of these stories related to a Yellow Diamond—a famous gem in the native annals of India.", "그 이야기들 가운데 가장 터무니없는 것 하나가 어느 노란 다이아몬드에 관한 것이었다—인도 토착의 연대기에 이름 높은 보석이었다."]], [["The earliest known traditions describe the stone as having been set in the forehead of the four-handed Indian god who typifies the Moon.", "알려진 가장 오랜 전승은, 그 돌이 달을 상징하는 네 팔 달린 인도 신의 이마에 박혀 있었다고 전한다."], ["Partly from its peculiar colour, partly from a superstition which represented it as feeling the influence of the deity whom it adorned, and growing and lessening in lustre with the waxing and waning of the moon, it first gained the name by which it continues to be known in India to this day—the name of THE MOONSTONE.", "한편으로는 그 독특한 빛깔 때문에, 또 한편으로는 그것이 제 몸을 장식한 신의 감응을 받아 달이 차고 이지러짐에 따라 광채가 더해지고 스러진다고 여긴 미신 때문에, 그 돌은 오늘날까지도 인도에서 통용되는 이름을 처음으로 얻었다—바로 '월장석(月長石)'이라는 이름이다."], ["A similar superstition was once prevalent, as I have heard, in ancient Greece and Rome; not applying, however (as in India), to a diamond devoted to the service of a god, but to a semi-transparent stone of the inferior order of gems, supposed to be affected by the lunar influences—the moon, in this latter case also, giving the name by which the stone is still known to collectors in our own time.", "내가 듣기로는, 이와 비슷한 미신이 옛 그리스와 로마에도 한때 널리 퍼져 있었다. 다만 그것은 (인도에서처럼) 신을 섬기는 데 바쳐진 다이아몬드가 아니라, 하급 보석에 속하는 어느 반투명한 돌에 관한 것이었으니, 그 돌이 달의 감응을 받는다고들 믿었던 것이다—이 경우에도 역시 달이, 오늘날 수집가들 사이에서 여전히 통하는 그 돌의 이름을 지어 주었다."]], [["The adventures of the Yellow Diamond begin with the eleventh century of the Christian era.", "이 노란 다이아몬드의 기구한 내력은 기독교 기원 11세기부터 시작된다."]], [["At that date, the Mohammedan conqueror, Mahmoud of Ghizni, crossed India; seized on the holy city of Somnauth; and stripped of its treasures the famous temple, which had stood for centuries—the shrine of Hindoo pilgrimage, and the wonder of the Eastern world.", "그 무렵, 이슬람의 정복자 가즈니의 마무드가 인도를 가로질러 쳐들어와, 성도(聖都) 솜나트를 함락하고, 여러 세기 동안 서 있던 그 이름난 신전에서 보물을 남김없이 약탈했다—힌두 순례의 성소요, 동방 세계의 경이이던 신전이었다."]], [["Of all the deities worshipped in the temple, the moon-god alone escaped the rapacity of the conquering Mohammedans.", "그 신전에서 섬겨지던 모든 신들 가운데, 오직 달의 신만이 정복자 이슬람교도들의 탐욕을 면했다."], ["Preserved by three Brahmins, the inviolate deity, bearing the Yellow Diamond in its forehead, was removed by night, and was transported to the second of the sacred cities of India—the city of Benares.", "세 명의 브라만이 지켜 낸 그 침범할 수 없는 신상은, 이마에 노란 다이아몬드를 박은 채 밤을 틈타 옮겨져, 인도의 두 번째 성도(聖都)—베나레스 시로 운반되었다."]], [["Here, in a new shrine—in a hall inlaid with precious stones, under a roof supported by pillars of gold—the moon-god was set up and worshipped.", "이곳, 새로운 성소에서—보석으로 아로새긴 전당, 황금 기둥이 떠받친 지붕 아래—달의 신은 모셔지고 섬겨졌다."], ["Here, on the night when the shrine was completed, Vishnu the Preserver appeared to the three Brahmins in a dream.", "이곳에서, 성소가 완성되던 날 밤, 수호신 비슈누가 세 브라만의 꿈에 나타났다."]], [["The deity breathed the breath of his divinity on the Diamond in the forehead of the god.", "그 신은 신의 이마에 박힌 다이아몬드에 자신의 신성한 숨결을 불어넣었다."], ["And the Brahmins knelt and hid their faces in their robes.", "그러자 브라만들은 무릎 꿇고 옷자락에 얼굴을 파묻었다."], ["The deity commanded that the Moonstone should be watched, from that time forth, by three priests in turn, night and day, to the end of the generations of men.", "신은 명하기를, 그때부터 세 사제가 번갈아, 밤낮으로, 인간의 대(代)가 다하는 그날까지 월장석을 지키라 하였다."], ["And the Brahmins heard, and bowed before his will.", "브라만들은 그 말씀을 듣고 그 뜻 앞에 머리를 조아렸다."], ["The deity predicted certain disaster to the presumptuous mortal who laid hands on the sacred gem, and to all of his house and name who received it after him.", "신은 예언하기를, 그 신성한 보석에 감히 손을 대는 오만한 인간에게, 그리고 그 뒤로 그것을 물려받는 그 집안과 이름의 모든 자에게 반드시 재앙이 내리리라 하였다."], ["And the Brahmins caused the prophecy to be written over the gates of the shrine in letters of gold.", "브라만들은 그 예언을 성소의 문 위에 황금 글자로 새겨 두게 하였다."]], [["One age followed another—and still, generation after generation, the successors of the three Brahmins watched their priceless Moonstone, night and day.", "한 시대가 가고 또 한 시대가 왔다—그러고도 여전히, 대를 이어 가며, 세 브라만의 후예들은 값을 매길 수 없는 그들의 월장석을 밤낮으로 지켰다."], ["One age followed another until the first years of the eighteenth Christian century saw the reign of Aurungzebe, Emperor of the Moguls.", "한 시대가 가고 또 한 시대가 오기를 거듭하여, 마침내 기독교 기원 18세기 초엽에 이르러 무굴의 황제 아우랑제브의 치세를 맞았다."], ["At his command havoc and rapine were let loose once more among the temples of the worship of Brahmah.", "그의 명령으로, 브라마를 섬기는 신전들에 또다시 파괴와 약탈이 풀려났다."], ["The shrine of the four-handed god was polluted by the slaughter of sacred animals; the images of the deities were broken in pieces; and the Moonstone was seized by an officer of rank in the army of Aurungzebe.", "네 팔 달린 신의 성소는 신성한 짐승들을 도살함으로써 더럽혀졌고, 신들의 상은 산산이 부서졌으며, 월장석은 아우랑제브 군대의 어느 지체 높은 장교의 손에 빼앗겼다."]], [["Powerless to recover their lost treasure by open force, the three guardian priests followed and watched it in disguise.", "잃어버린 보물을 드러내 놓고 힘으로 되찾을 길이 없었으므로, 세 수호 사제는 변장한 채 그것을 뒤쫓으며 지켜보았다. 대가 거듭 바뀌었다."], ["The generations succeeded each other; the warrior who had committed the sacrilege perished miserably; the Moonstone passed (carrying its curse with it) from one lawless Mohammedan hand to another; and still, through all chances and changes, the successors of the three guardian priests kept their watch, waiting the day when the will of Vishnu the Preserver should restore to them their sacred gem.", "그 신성모독을 저지른 장수는 비참하게 죽었고, 월장석은 (제 저주를 지닌 채) 무법한 이슬람교도의 손에서 손으로 넘어갔다. 그러면서도 여전히, 온갖 우여곡절을 겪는 동안, 세 수호 사제의 후예들은 수호신 비슈누의 뜻이 그들의 신성한 보석을 되돌려 줄 그날을 기다리며 파수를 늦추지 않았다."], ["Time rolled on from the first to the last years of the eighteenth Christian century.", "세월은 기독교 기원 18세기의 첫 해로부터 마지막 해로 흘러갔다."], ["The Diamond fell into the possession of Tippoo, Sultan of Seringapatam, who caused it to be placed as an ornament in the handle of a dagger, and who commanded it to be kept among the choicest treasures of his armoury.", "다이아몬드는 세링가파탐의 술탄 티푸의 손에 들어갔으니, 그는 그것을 단검 자루에 장식으로 박게 하고, 자기 병기고의 가장 귀한 보물들 틈에 간수하라 명하였다."], ["Even then—in the palace of the Sultan himself—the three guardian priests still kept their watch in secret.", "그때에도—바로 그 술탄의 궁전 안에서—세 수호 사제는 여전히 남몰래 파수를 서고 있었다."], ["There were three officers of Tippoo’s household, strangers to the rest, who had won their master’s confidence by conforming, or appearing to conform, to the Mussulman faith; and to those three men report pointed as the three priests in disguise.", "티푸의 가솔 가운데 세 사람이 있었는데, 다른 이들과는 낯선 자들로서, 이슬람교로 개종하거나 개종한 체함으로써 주인의 신임을 얻은 자들이었다. 소문은 바로 그 세 사람을 변장한 세 사제라 지목하였다."]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["So, as told in our camp, ran the fanciful story of the Moonstone.", "우리 진영에서 전해진 월장석의 허황된 이야기는 대략 이러하였다."], ["It made no serious impression on any of us except my cousin—whose love of the marvellous induced him to believe it.", "그 이야기는 내 사촌 하나를 빼고는 우리 누구에게도 대수로운 인상을 주지 못했다—그런데 기이한 것을 좋아하던 그 사촌은 그것을 곧이곧대로 믿었다."], ["On the night before the assault on Seringapatam, he was absurdly angry with me, and with others, for treating the whole thing as a fable.", "세링가파탐 공격 전날 밤, 그는 온통 그 일을 한낱 옛이야기로 치부한다고 하여 나와 다른 이들에게 어처구니없이 화를 냈다."], ["A foolish wrangle followed; and Herncastle’s unlucky temper got the better of him.", "어리석은 언쟁이 뒤따랐고, 헌캐슬은 그 못된 성미에 그만 자신을 잃고 말았다."], ["He declared, in his boastful way, that we should see the Diamond on his finger, if the English army took Seringapatam.", "그는 제 자랑삼는 투로, 영국군이 세링가파탐을 함락하면 우리가 그의 손가락에 낀 그 다이아몬드를 보게 되리라 큰소리쳤다."], ["The sally was saluted by a roar of laughter, and there, as we all thought that night, the thing ended.", "그 호언은 한바탕 폭소로 맞이되었고, 그날 밤 우리 모두가 여긴 대로, 그것으로 일은 끝난 듯싶었다."]], [["Let me now take you on to the day of the assault.", "이제 나는 당신들을 그 공격의 날로 데려가려 한다."]], [["My cousin and I were separated at the outset.", "사촌과 나는 처음부터 서로 떨어졌다."], ["I never saw him when we forded the river; when we planted the English flag in the first breach; when we crossed the ditch beyond; and, fighting every inch of our way, entered the town.", "우리가 강을 건널 때에도, 첫 번째 돌파구에 영국 국기를 꽂을 때에도, 그 너머의 참호를 건널 때에도, 한 걸음 한 걸음 싸워 가며 시내로 들어설 때에도, 나는 그를 보지 못했다."], ["It was only at dusk, when the place was ours, and after General Baird himself had found the dead body of Tippoo under a heap of the slain, that Herncastle and I met.", "그곳이 우리 손에 들어오고, 베어드 장군이 몸소 시체 더미 아래에서 티푸의 주검을 찾아낸 뒤, 땅거미가 질 무렵에야 비로소 헌캐슬과 나는 마주쳤다."]], [["We were each attached to a party sent out by the general’s orders to prevent the plunder and confusion which followed our conquest.", "우리는 각기, 우리의 정복에 뒤이은 약탈과 혼란을 막으라는 장군의 명으로 파견된 부대에 배속되어 있었다."], ["The camp-followers committed deplorable excesses; and, worse still, the soldiers found their way, by an unguarded door, into the treasury of the Palace, and loaded themselves with gold and jewels.", "종군하던 무리는 개탄스러운 난동을 부렸고, 더욱 나쁜 것은, 병사들이 지키는 이 없는 문으로 궁전의 보물고에 들어가 황금과 보석을 잔뜩 챙긴 일이었다."], ["It was in the court outside the treasury that my cousin and I met, to enforce the laws of discipline on our own soldiers.", "사촌과 내가 마주친 곳은 바로 그 보물고 바깥뜰, 우리 편 병사들에게 군율을 세우려던 자리였다."], ["Herncastle’s fiery temper had been, as I could plainly see, exasperated to a kind of frenzy by the terrible slaughter through which we had passed.", "내가 똑똑히 보건대, 헌캐슬의 불같은 성미는 우리가 지나온 그 끔찍한 살육으로 하여 일종의 광기에 이를 만큼 격앙되어 있었다."], ["He was very unfit, in my opinion, to perform the duty that had been entrusted to him.", "내 생각에 그는 자기에게 맡겨진 임무를 수행하기에 지극히 부적당한 자였다."]], [["There was riot and confusion enough in the treasury, but no violence that I saw.", "보물고 안에는 소란과 혼란이 넘쳤으나, 내가 본 바로는 폭행은 없었다."], ["The men (if I may use such an expression) disgraced themselves good-humouredly.", "병사들은 (이런 표현을 써도 좋다면) 흥청거리며 스스로를 욕되게 하고 있었다."], ["All sorts of rough jests and catchwords were bandied about among them; and the story of the Diamond turned up again unexpectedly, in the form of a mischievous joke.", "온갖 상스러운 농지거리와 후렴 같은 말들이 그들 사이에 오갔고, 다이아몬드 이야기가 뜻밖에도 짓궂은 농담의 꼴로 다시 튀어나왔다."], ["“Who’s got the Moonstone?” was the rallying cry which perpetually caused the plundering, as soon as it was stopped in one place, to break out in another.", "\"월장석은 누가 차지했나?\"—이것이 그 함성이었으니, 한곳에서 약탈이 그치기가 무섭게 다른 곳에서 다시 터지게 만들곤 하였다."], ["While I was still vainly trying to establish order, I heard a frightful yelling on the other side of the courtyard, and at once ran towards the cries, in dread of finding some new outbreak of the pillage in that direction.", "내가 여전히 부질없이 질서를 잡으려 애쓰고 있을 때, 안뜰 저편에서 소름 끼치는 고함이 들려왔고, 나는 그쪽에서 또 무슨 새로운 약탈이 벌어졌을까 두려워 곧장 그 소리를 향해 달려갔다."]], [["I got to an open door, and saw the bodies of two Indians (by their dress, as I guessed, officers of the palace) lying across the entrance, dead.", "나는 열린 문 앞에 이르러, 인도인 둘의 주검이 (그 복색으로 미루어 짐작건대 궁전의 관리들이었다) 입구를 가로질러 죽어 널브러져 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["A cry inside hurried me into a room, which appeared to serve as an armoury.", "안에서 터진 비명에 나는 서둘러 어느 방으로 들어섰는데, 병기고로 쓰이는 듯한 곳이었다."], ["A third Indian, mortally wounded, was sinking at the feet of a man whose back was towards me.", "치명상을 입은 세 번째 인도인이, 등을 내 쪽으로 돌린 한 사내의 발치에 스러져 가고 있었다."], ["The man turned at the instant when I came in, and I saw John Herncastle, with a torch in one hand, and a dagger dripping with blood in the other.", "그 사내는 내가 들어서는 순간 몸을 돌렸고, 나는 존 헌캐슬을, 한 손에는 횃불을, 다른 손에는 피가 뚝뚝 듣는 단검을 든 그를 보았다."], ["A stone, set like a pommel, in the end of the dagger’s handle, flashed in the torchlight, as he turned on me, like a gleam of fire.", "단검 자루 끝에 손잡이 머리처럼 박힌 돌 하나가, 그가 나를 향해 돌아설 때 횃불빛을 받아 불꽃처럼 번쩍였다."], ["The dying Indian sank to his knees, pointed to the dagger in Herncastle’s hand, and said, in his native language—“The Moonstone will have its vengeance yet on you and yours!”", "죽어 가던 인도인은 무릎을 꿇으며, 헌캐슬의 손에 든 단검을 가리키고는, 제 나라 말로 이렇게 말했다—\"월장석은 반드시 너와 네 핏줄에게 그 원한을 갚으리라!\""], ["He spoke those words, and fell dead on the floor.", "그는 그 말을 남기고 바닥에 쓰러져 숨졌다."]], [["Before I could stir in the matter, the men who had followed me across the courtyard crowded in.", "내가 이 일에 손쓸 겨를도 없이, 안뜰을 가로질러 나를 뒤따라온 병사들이 우르르 몰려들었다."], ["My cousin rushed to meet them, like a madman.", "사촌은 미친 사람처럼 그들에게 달려들었다."], ["“Clear the room!”", "\"이 방을 비워라!\""], ["he shouted to me, “and set a guard on the door!”", "그가 내게 소리쳤다. \"그리고 문에 파수를 세워라!\""], ["The men fell back as he threw himself on them with his torch and his dagger.", "그가 횃불과 단검을 휘두르며 덤벼들자 병사들은 물러섰다."], ["I put two sentinels of my own company, on whom I could rely, to keep the door.", "나는 내가 믿을 만한 우리 중대의 보초 둘을 세워 문을 지키게 했다."], ["Through the remainder of the night, I saw no more of my cousin.", "그날 밤이 다하도록 나는 다시는 사촌을 보지 못했다."]], [["Early in the morning, the plunder still going on, General Baird announced publicly by beat of drum, that any thief detected in the fact, be he whom he might, should be hung.", "이튿날 이른 아침, 약탈이 여전히 이어지는 가운데, 베어드 장군은 북을 울려, 현장에서 붙잡힌 도둑은 그가 누구이든 교수형에 처하겠노라 공공연히 포고했다."], ["The provost-marshal was in attendance, to prove that the General was in earnest; and in the throng that followed the proclamation, Herncastle and I met again.", "장군의 진심을 증명하려는 듯 헌병대장이 배석했다. 그 포고를 뒤따른 인파 속에서 헌캐슬과 나는 다시 마주쳤다."]], [["He held out his hand, as usual, and said, “Good morning.”", "그는 여느 때처럼 손을 내밀며 \"안녕하시오\" 하고 말했다."]], [["I waited before I gave him my hand in return.", "나는 답으로 손을 내밀기 전에 잠시 기다렸다."]], [["“Tell me first,”", "\"먼저 말해 주시오.\""], ["I said, “how the Indian in the armoury met his death, and what those last words meant, when he pointed to the dagger in your hand.”", "내가 말했다. \"병기고의 그 인도인이 어떻게 죽었는지, 그리고 그가 당신 손의 단검을 가리키며 남긴 그 마지막 말이 무슨 뜻이었는지를.\""]], [["“The Indian met his death, as I suppose, by a mortal wound,”", "\"그 인도인은, 내 짐작으로는, 치명상을 입어 죽은 것이오.\""], ["said Herncastle.", "헌캐슬이 말했다."], ["“What his last words meant I know no more than you do.”", "\"그의 마지막 말이 무슨 뜻인지는 나도 당신만큼밖에 모르오.\""]], [["I looked at him narrowly.", "나는 그를 유심히 뜯어보았다."], ["His frenzy of the previous day had all calmed down.", "전날의 광기는 온통 가라앉아 있었다."], ["I determined to give him another chance.", "나는 그에게 한 번 더 기회를 주기로 마음먹었다."]], [["“Is that all you have to tell me?”", "\"내게 할 말이 그것뿐이오?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["He answered, “That is all.”", "그는 \"그것뿐이오\" 하고 대답했다."]], [["I turned my back on him; and we have not spoken since.", "나는 그에게 등을 돌렸다. 그리고 우리는 그 뒤로 말을 나눈 일이 없다."]], [["# IV", "# IV"]], [["I beg it to be understood that what I write here about my cousin (unless some necessity should arise for making it public) is for the information of the family only.", "내가 여기 사촌에 관하여 적는 것은 (그것을 공개해야 할 어떤 필요가 생기지 않는 한) 오직 집안에 알리기 위함임을 밝혀 두고자 한다."], ["Herncastle has said nothing that can justify me in speaking to our commanding officer.", "헌캐슬은 내가 우리 지휘관에게 고할 만한 어떤 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He has been taunted more than once about the Diamond, by those who recollect his angry outbreak before the assault; but, as may easily be imagined, his own remembrance of the circumstances under which I surprised him in the armoury has been enough to keep him silent.", "공격 전의 그 격분을 기억하는 이들이 다이아몬드를 두고 한두 번 그를 비웃은 적은 있으나, 능히 짐작할 수 있듯이, 내가 병기고에서 그를 불시에 맞닥뜨린 그 정황에 대한 그 자신의 기억만으로도 그의 입을 다물게 하기에 충분했다."], ["It is reported that he means to exchange into another regiment, avowedly for the purpose of separating himself from me.", "들리는 말로는, 그는 다른 연대로 옮길 작정이라는데, 대놓고 내세우는 이유가 바로 나에게서 떨어지려는 것이라 한다."]], [["Whether this be true or not, I cannot prevail upon myself to become his accuser—and I think with good reason.", "이것이 사실이든 아니든, 나는 도저히 그를 고발하는 자가 될 마음이 나지 않는다—그리고 거기에는 그럴 만한 까닭이 있다고 여긴다."], ["If I made the matter public, I have no evidence but moral evidence to bring forward.", "이 일을 공개한다 한들, 나로서는 정황상의 심증 말고는 내놓을 증거가 없다."], ["I have not only no proof that he killed the two men at the door; I cannot even declare that he killed the third man inside—for I cannot say that my own eyes saw the deed committed.", "그가 문간의 두 사람을 죽였다는 증거가 없을뿐더러, 안에 있던 세 번째 사람을 죽였다고조차 단언할 수 없다—내 두 눈으로 그 행위가 저질러지는 것을 보았다고 말할 수 없기 때문이다."], ["It is true that I heard the dying Indian’s words; but if those words were pronounced to be the ravings of delirium, how could I contradict the assertion from my own knowledge?", "죽어 가던 인도인의 말을 내가 들은 것은 사실이나, 만일 그 말이 헛것에 들린 자의 광언(狂言)이었다고 판정된다면, 내가 무슨 수로 내 아는 바를 들어 그 단정을 반박하겠는가?"], ["Let our relatives, on either side, form their own opinion on what I have written, and decide for themselves whether the aversion I now feel towards this man is well or ill founded.", "양쪽 우리 친척들은 내가 적은 바에 대하여 저마다 나름의 판단을 내리고, 지금 내가 이 사람에게 느끼는 혐오가 근거 있는 것인지 아닌지를 스스로 정하기 바란다."]], [["Although I attach no sort of credit to the fantastic Indian legend of the gem, I must acknowledge, before I conclude, that I am influenced by a certain superstition of my own in this matter.", "그 보석에 얽힌 황당한 인도 전설을 나는 조금도 믿지 않지만, 이 글을 맺기 전에 고백해 두어야겠다. 이 일에 관하여 나 역시 나름의 어떤 미신에 사로잡혀 있음을."], ["It is my conviction, or my delusion, no matter which, that crime brings its own fatality with it.", "그것이 내 확신이든 내 망상이든—어느 쪽이라도 상관없다—죄는 제 안에 제 몫의 재앙을 품고 온다는 것이 그 미신이다."], ["I am not only persuaded of Herncastle’s guilt; I am even fanciful enough to believe that he will live to regret it, if he keeps the Diamond; and that others will live to regret taking it from him, if he gives the Diamond away.", "나는 헌캐슬이 죄를 지었음을 확신할 뿐 아니라, 그가 그 다이아몬드를 계속 지닌다면 살아생전에 그것을 뉘우치게 되리라 믿고, 또 그가 그 다이아몬드를 남에게 넘긴다면 그것을 받은 다른 이들이 살아생전에 그것을 뉘우치게 되리라 믿을 만큼 공상에 빠져 있기까지 하다."]], [["The Events related by Gabriel Betteredge, house-steward in the service of Julia, Lady Verinder.", "여기 실린 사건들은, 줄리아 곧 레이디 버린더를 섬기는 집사(執事) 게이브리얼 베터리지가 이야기한 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["In the first part of Robinson Crusoe, at page one hundred and twenty-nine, you will find it thus written:", "「로빈슨 크루소」제1부 백스물아홉 쪽을 펼치면, 여러분은 이렇게 적힌 대목을 보게 될 것이다."]], [["“Now I saw, though too late, the Folly of beginning a Work before we count the Cost, and before we judge rightly of our own Strength to go through with it.”", "\"이제야 나는, 너무 늦게나마 깨달았다. 치를 대가를 미리 헤아려 보지도 않고, 그 일을 끝까지 해낼 제 힘을 옳게 가늠해 보지도 않은 채 일을 벌이는 것이 얼마나 어리석은 짓인가를.\""]], [["Only yesterday, I opened my Robinson Crusoe at that place.", "바로 어제, 나는 내 「로빈슨 크루소」를 하필 그 대목에서 펼쳤다."], ["Only this morning (May twenty-first, eighteen hundred and fifty), came my lady’s nephew, Mr. Franklin Blake, and held a short conversation with me, as follows:—", "그리고 바로 오늘 아침(1850년 5월 스무하룻날), 마님의 조카이신 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이 오셔서, 나와 이런 짤막한 이야기를 나누셨다."]], [["“Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지,\""], ["says Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말씀하셨다."], ["Franklin, “I have been to the lawyer’s about some family matters; and, among other things, we have been talking of the loss of the Indian Diamond, in my aunt’s house in Yorkshire, two years since. Mr.", "\"집안일로 변호사한테 다녀오는 길인데, 이런저런 얘기 끝에 이 년 전 요크셔의 이모님 댁에서 인도 다이아몬드를 잃어버린 일까지 나왔다네."], ["Bruff thinks as I think, that the whole story ought, in the interests of truth, to be placed on record in writing—and the sooner the better.”", "브러프 씨도 나와 같은 생각이야—진실을 위해서라도 그 이야기를 죄다 글로 적어 남겨 두어야 한다는 거지. 그것도 이를수록 좋고.\""]], [["Not perceiving his drift yet, and thinking it always desirable for the sake of peace and quietness to be on the lawyer’s side, I said I thought so too.", "아직 그 속뜻을 알아채지 못한 데다, 무릇 조용하고 편안하게 지내자면 변호사 편을 드는 게 상책이라 여겨, 나도 그렇게 생각한다고 대답했다."], ["Mr. Franklin went on.", "프랭클린 도련님은 말을 이으셨다."]], [["“In this matter of the Diamond,”", "\"이 다이아몬드 일로 말하자면,\""], ["he said, “the characters of innocent people have suffered under suspicion already—as you know.", "그가 말씀하셨다. \"자네도 알다시피, 죄 없는 사람들이 벌써 의심을 받아 명예가 상했네."], ["The memories of innocent people may suffer, hereafter, for want of a record of the facts to which those who come after us can appeal.", "앞으로도, 뒷사람들이 기댈 만한 사실의 기록이 없어서 죄 없는 사람들의 기억이 욕을 볼지도 몰라."], ["There can be no doubt that this strange family story of ours ought to be told.", "우리 집안의 이 야릇한 이야기를 마땅히 남겨 두어야 한다는 건 두말할 나위가 없지."], ["And I think, Betteredge, Mr. Bruff and I together have hit on the right way of telling it.”", "그런데 베터리지, 브러프 씨와 나 둘이서 그걸 남기는 옳은 방법을 찾아낸 것 같단 말이야.\""]], [["Very satisfactory to both of them, no doubt.", "두 분한테야 더할 나위 없이 흡족한 방법이었겠지."], ["But I failed to see what I myself had to do with it, so far.", "하지만 나로서는, 여태 그게 나하고 무슨 상관이 있는지 도무지 알 수가 없었다."]], [["“We have certain events to relate,” Mr.", "\"우리한테는 이야기해야 할 사건들이 있고,\""], ["Franklin proceeded; “and we have certain persons concerned in those events who are capable of relating them.", "프랭클린 도련님이 계속하셨다. \"그 사건들에 얽힌, 그것을 이야기할 만한 사람들도 있지."], ["Starting from these plain facts, the idea is that we should all write the story of the Moonstone in turn—as far as our own personal experience extends, and no farther.", "이 뻔한 사실에서 출발해서, 우리 모두가 돌아가며 월장석 이야기를 쓰자는 거야—저마다 제가 몸소 겪은 데까지만, 그 이상은 말고."], ["We must begin by showing how the Diamond first fell into the hands of my uncle Herncastle, when he was serving in India fifty years since.", "우선 오십 년 전 헌캐슬 외삼촌이 인도에서 복무할 적에 그 다이아몬드가 어떻게 처음 그분 손에 들어왔는지부터 보여야 해."], ["This prefatory narrative I have already got by me in the form of an old family paper, which relates the necessary particulars on the authority of an eye-witness.", "이 머리말 격의 이야기는 내가 이미 오래된 가문 문서 형태로 갖고 있네—목격자의 증언에 기대어 필요한 사정을 죄다 적어 둔 것이지."], ["The next thing to do is to tell how the Diamond found its way into my aunt’s house in Yorkshire, two years ago, and how it came to be lost in little more than twelve hours afterwards.", "그다음 할 일은, 이 년 전 그 다이아몬드가 어떻게 요크셔의 이모님 댁으로 흘러들었고, 그러고 나서 열두 시간 남짓 만에 어떻게 없어졌는지를 이야기하는 거야."], ["Nobody knows as much as you do, Betteredge, about what went on in the house at that time.", "그때 그 집 안에서 무슨 일이 있었는지는 자네만큼 잘 아는 사람이 없어, 베터리지."], ["So you must take the pen in hand, and start the story.”", "그러니 자네가 펜을 들고 이야기의 첫머리를 떼야겠네.\""]], [["In those terms I was informed of what my personal concern was with the matter of the Diamond.", "이런 식으로 나는 그 다이아몬드 일에 내가 무슨 관계가 있는지를 통고받은 셈이었다."], ["If you are curious to know what course I took under the circumstances, I beg to inform you that I did what you would probably have done in my place.", "그런 처지에서 내가 어떻게 처신했는지 궁금하시다면, 여러분이 내 자리에 있었어도 아마 그리했을 법한 그대로 했다고 아뢰겠다."], ["I modestly declared myself to be quite unequal to the task imposed upon me—and I privately felt, all the time, that I was quite clever enough to perform it, if I only gave my own abilities a fair chance.", "나는 겸손하게, 나한테 지워진 그 일을 감당할 재간이 도무지 없노라 잡아뗐다—그러면서도 속으로는 내내, 내 능력에 한 번 공평한 기회만 준다면 그쯤이야 넉넉히 해낼 만큼 영리하다고 여기고 있었다."], ["Mr. Franklin, I imagine, must have seen my private sentiments in my face.", "짐작건대 프랭클린 도련님은 내 속마음을 얼굴에서 읽으셨던 모양이다."], ["He declined to believe in my modesty; and he insisted on giving my abilities a fair chance.", "그분은 내 겸손을 곧이듣기를 마다하시고, 기어이 내 능력에 공평한 기회를 주겠노라 우기셨다."]], [["Two hours have passed since Mr. Franklin left me.", "프랭클린 도련님이 나를 떠나신 지 두 시간이 흘렀다."], ["As soon as his back was turned, I went to my writing-desk to start the story.", "그분이 등을 돌리기가 무섭게, 나는 이야기를 시작하려고 책상 앞에 앉았다."], ["There I have sat helpless (in spite of my abilities) ever since; seeing what Robinson Crusoe saw, as quoted above—namely, the folly of beginning a work before we count the cost, and before we judge rightly of our own strength to go through with it.", "그러고는 (내 능력이 무색하게도) 여태껏 속수무책으로 앉아만 있다. 앞에서 옮긴 대로, 로빈슨 크루소가 깨친 그것—곧 치를 대가를 미리 헤아려 보지도 않고, 그 일을 끝까지 해낼 제 힘을 옳게 가늠해 보지도 않은 채 일을 벌이는 어리석음을 나 역시 눈앞에 보면서 말이다."], ["Please to remember, I opened the book by accident, at that bit, only the day before I rashly undertook the business now in hand; and, allow me to ask—if that isn’t prophecy, what is?", "부디 잊지 마시라, 내가 지금 벌인 이 일을 경솔하게 떠맡기 바로 전날, 나는 우연히 그 책을 하필 그 대목에서 펼쳤던 것이다. 그러니 감히 여쭙거니와—그게 예언이 아니라면, 대체 무엇이 예언이란 말인가?"]], [["I am not superstitious; I have read a heap of books in my time; I am a scholar in my own way.", "나는 미신을 믿는 사람이 아니다. 살아오면서 책도 산더미같이 읽었고, 내 나름으로는 학식깨나 갖춘 사람이다."], ["Though turned seventy, I possess an active memory, and legs to correspond.", "일흔을 넘겼어도 기억력은 여태 팔팔하고, 다리도 그에 걸맞게 성하다."], ["You are not to take it, if you please, as the saying of an ignorant man, when I express my opinion that such a book as Robinson Crusoe never was written, and never will be written again.", "그러니 「로빈슨 크루소」같은 책은 여태 쓰인 적도 없고 앞으로도 다시는 쓰이지 못하리라는 게 내 소견이라 하여, 부디 무식쟁이의 헛소리로 듣지는 마시라."], ["I have tried that book for years—generally in combination with a pipe of tobacco—and I have found it my friend in need in all the necessities of this mortal life.", "나는 여러 해 동안 그 책을 시험 삼아 읽어 왔는데—대개는 담배 한 대와 곁들여서였다—이 덧없는 인생의 온갖 고비마다 그 책이 곤경 속의 벗임을 알았다."], ["When my spirits are bad—Robinson Crusoe.", "심사가 사나울 때면—「로빈슨 크루소」."], ["When I want advice—Robinson Crusoe.", "조언이 아쉬울 때면—「로빈슨 크루소」."], ["In past times when my wife plagued me; in present times when I have had a drop too much—Robinson Crusoe.", "지난날 아내가 나를 들볶을 때에도, 요즈음 술이 한 잔 과했을 때에도—「로빈슨 크루소」."], ["I have worn out six stout Robinson Crusoes with hard work in my service.", "나는 여태 부려 온 세월 동안 튼튼한 「로빈슨 크루소」를 여섯 권이나 닳도록 읽어 없앴다."], ["On my lady’s last birthday she gave me a seventh.", "지난번 마님 생신에 마님은 내게 일곱 번째 것을 주셨다."], ["I took a drop too much on the strength of it; and Robinson Crusoe put me right again.", "나는 그게 반가워 술을 한 잔 과하게 걸쳤고—그러자 「로빈슨 크루소」가 또다시 나를 바로잡아 주었다."], ["Price four shillings and sixpence, bound in blue, with a picture into the bargain.", "값은 네 실링 육 펜스, 파란 표지에, 덤으로 그림 한 장까지 딸린 책이다."]], [["Still, this don’t look much like starting the story of the Diamond—does it?", "그건 그렇고, 이래서야 다이아몬드 이야기를 시작하는 꼴이 영 아니지 않은가?"], ["I seem to be wandering off in search of Lord knows what, Lord knows where.", "나는 어디서 무엇을 찾겠다는 건지 저도 모르게 자꾸 딴 데로 새고 있는 성싶다."], ["We will take a new sheet of paper, if you please, and begin over again, with my best respects to you.", "자, 괜찮으시다면 새 종이를 한 장 꺼내 들고, 여러분께 삼가 예를 갖추어 처음부터 다시 시작하기로 하자."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["I spoke of my lady a line or two back.", "두어 줄 앞에서 나는 마님 이야기를 꺼냈다."], ["Now the Diamond could never have been in our house, where it was lost, if it had not been made a present of to my lady’s daughter; and my lady’s daughter would never have been in existence to have the present, if it had not been for my lady who (with pain and travail) produced her into the world.", "그런데 그 다이아몬드가, 잃어버린 그 장소인 우리 집에 있게 된 것은, 애초에 그것이 마님의 따님에게 선물로 주어진 덕분이요, 마님의 따님이 그 선물을 받으려고 세상에 나와 있게 된 것은, (산고와 진통을 겪어 가며) 그분을 이 세상에 낳아 주신 마님이 계셨던 덕분이다."], ["Consequently, if we begin with my lady, we are pretty sure of beginning far enough back.", "그러니 마님에서부터 시작한다면, 우리는 넉넉히 거슬러 올라간 데서 시작하는 셈이 틀림없다."], ["And that, let me tell you, when you have got such a job as mine in hand, is a real comfort at starting.", "그리고 말이지, 나 같은 일감을 손에 쥐어 보면 알겠지만, 그게 바로 첫머리를 떼는 데 여간 큰 위안이 아니다."]], [["If you know anything of the fashionable world, you have heard tell of the three beautiful Miss Herncastles.", "상류 사교계를 조금이라도 아신다면, 여러분은 아름다운 헌캐슬 세 자매 아가씨에 관해 들어 보셨을 것이다. 애덜레이드 아가씨, 캐럴라인 아가씨, 그리고 줄리아 아가씨—이 막내가 세 자매 가운데 가장 어리면서도, 내 소견으로는 가장 훌륭했다."], ["Miss Adelaide; Miss Caroline; and Miss Julia—this last being the youngest and the best of the three sisters, in my opinion; and I had opportunities of judging, as you shall presently see.", "그리고 그 점을 가늠할 기회가 내게 있었음은, 곧 보시게 될 것이다. 나는 그분들의 부친인 노(老)영주 밑에서 일하게 되었는데(다행히도 이 다이아몬드 사건에서 그분과 우리는 아무 상관이 없다."], ["I went into the service of the old lord, their father (thank God, we have got nothing to do with him, in this business of the Diamond; he had the longest tongue and the shortest temper of any man, high or low, I ever met with)—I say, I went into the service of the old lord, as page-boy in waiting on the three honourable young ladies, at the age of fifteen years.", "그분으로 말할 것 같으면, 지체가 높든 낮든 내가 여태 만나 본 어느 누구보다도 혀가 길고 성미가 짧은 양반이었다)—아무튼, 나는 열다섯 나이에, 그 세 귀하신 아가씨들의 시중을 드는 시동(侍童)으로 노영주 밑에 들어갔다. 그러고는 줄리아 아가씨가 지금은 고인이 되신 존 버린더 경과 혼인하실 때까지 그곳에서 지냈다."], ["There I lived till Miss Julia married the late Sir John Verinder.", "존 경은 훌륭한 분이었으되, 다만 자기를 다스려 줄 누군가가 있어야 할 분이었다."], ["An excellent man, who only wanted somebody to manage him; and, between ourselves, he found somebody to do it; and what is more, he throve on it and grew fat on it, and lived happy and died easy on it, dating from the day when my lady took him to church to be married, to the day when she relieved him of his last breath, and closed his eyes for ever.", "그리고 우리끼리 하는 말이지만, 그분은 그 누군가를 찾아내셨다. 뿐만 아니라 그분은 그 덕에 잘되고 그 덕에 살이 올랐으며, 마님이 그분을 성당으로 데려가 혼인하시던 날부터, 마님이 그분의 마지막 숨을 거두어 주고 그 눈을 영영 감겨 드리던 날까지, 그 덕에 행복하게 살다 편안하게 돌아가셨다."]], [["I have omitted to state that I went with the bride to the bride’s husband’s house and lands down here.", "내가 신부를 따라, 신랑 되는 분의 이 아랫녘 저택과 땅으로 함께 내려왔다는 이야기를 빼먹었다."], ["“Sir John,”", "\"존 경,\""], ["she says, “I can’t do without Gabriel Betteredge.”", "아가씨가 말씀하셨다. \"저는 게이브리얼 베터리지 없이는 못 살아요.\""], ["“My lady,”", "\"마님,\""], ["says Sir John, “I can’t do without him, either.”", "존 경이 말씀하셨다. \"나도 저 사람 없이는 못 살겠소.\""], ["That was his way with her—and that was how I went into his service.", "그것이 아가씨를 대하는 그분의 방식이었다—그리고 그렇게 해서 나는 그분 밑에서 일하게 되었다."], ["It was all one to me where I went, so long as my mistress and I were together.", "마님과 나만 함께 있을 수 있다면, 내가 어디로 가든 내게는 매한가지였다."]], [["Seeing that my lady took an interest in the out-of-door work, and the farms, and such like, I took an interest in them too—with all the more reason that I was a small farmer’s seventh son myself.", "마님이 바깥일이며 농장이며 그런 일들에 관심을 두시는 것을 보고, 나도 덩달아 관심을 두게 되었다—더구나 나 자신이 자그마한 소작농의 일곱째 아들이었으니 관심을 둘 까닭이야 충분했다."], ["My lady got me put under the bailiff, and I did my best, and gave satisfaction, and got promotion accordingly.", "마님은 나를 마름 밑에 딸리게 해 주셨고, 나는 힘껏 애를 써서 만족을 드렸으며, 그에 따라 승진도 했다."], ["Some years later, on the Monday as it might be, my lady says, “Sir John, your bailiff is a stupid old man.", "몇 해 뒤, 이를테면 어느 월요일에, 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"존 경, 당신네 마름은 어리석은 늙은이예요."], ["Pension him liberally, and let Gabriel Betteredge have his place.”", "후하게 연금을 주어 내보내고, 게이브리얼 베터리지에게 그 자리를 주도록 하세요.\""], ["On the Tuesday as it might be, Sir John says, “My lady, the bailiff is pensioned liberally; and Gabriel Betteredge has got his place.”", "이를테면 그 화요일에, 존 경이 말씀하셨다. \"마님, 마름은 후하게 연금을 받아 물러났고, 게이브리얼 베터리지가 그 자리를 차지했소.\""], ["You hear more than enough of married people living together miserably.", "부부가 함께 살며 서로 못 잡아먹어 안달인 이야기라면 여러분도 지겹도록 들으셨을 것이다."], ["Here is an example to the contrary.", "여기 그와는 정반대의 본보기가 있다."], ["Let it be a warning to some of you, and an encouragement to others.", "이것을 어떤 이에게는 경계로, 또 어떤 이에게는 격려로 삼으시기 바란다."], ["In the meantime, I will go on with my story.", "그동안 나는 내 이야기나 계속하겠다."]], [["Well, there I was in clover, you will say.", "자, 그쯤 되면 팔자가 늘어졌구나, 여러분은 그렇게 말씀하실 것이다."], ["Placed in a position of trust and honour, with a little cottage of my own to live in, with my rounds on the estate to occupy me in the morning, and my accounts in the afternoon, and my pipe and my Robinson Crusoe in the evening—what more could I possibly want to make me happy?", "믿음직하고 명예로운 자리에 앉아, 몸 뉠 조그만 오두막을 하나 얻고, 아침이면 영지를 도는 순찰로, 오후면 장부 정리로, 저녁이면 담뱃대와 「로빈슨 크루소」로 소일하니—대체 무엇이 더 있어야 내가 행복해지겠는가?"], ["Remember what Adam wanted when he was alone in the Garden of Eden; and if you don’t blame it in Adam, don’t blame it in me.", "아담이 에덴동산에 홀로 있을 적에 무엇을 아쉬워했는지 떠올려 보시라. 그것을 두고 아담을 나무라지 않으신다면, 나 또한 나무라지 마시라."]], [["The woman I fixed my eye on, was the woman who kept house for me at my cottage.", "내가 눈독을 들인 여자는, 내 오두막에서 살림을 돌봐 주던 여자였다."], ["Her name was Selina Goby.", "이름은 셀리나 고비였다."], ["I agree with the late William Cobbett about picking a wife.", "아내를 고르는 일에 관해서라면 나는 지금은 고인이 된 윌리엄 코빗의 말에 동의한다."], ["See that she chews her food well and sets her foot down firmly on the ground when she walks, and you’re all right.", "음식을 잘 씹어 먹는지, 걸을 때 땅을 야무지게 딛는지만 보면 그것으로 됐다는 것이다."], ["Selina Goby was all right in both these respects, which was one reason for marrying her.", "셀리나 고비는 그 두 가지 점에서 다 나무랄 데가 없었으니, 그것이 그녀와 혼인한 한 가지 까닭이었다."], ["I had another reason, likewise, entirely of my own discovering.", "나한테는 또 다른 까닭도 있었는데, 이건 순전히 나 혼자 궁리해 낸 것이었다."], ["Selina, being a single woman, made me pay so much a week for her board and services.", "셀리나가 처녀로 있을 때는, 그녀의 식비며 품삯이며 하여 내가 그녀에게 주급을 얼마씩 치러야 했다."], ["Selina, being my wife, couldn’t charge for her board, and would have to give me her services for nothing.", "그런데 셀리나가 내 아내가 되면, 식비를 청구할 수도 없거니와, 품도 공짜로 내게 바쳐야 할 터였다."], ["That was the point of view I looked at it from.", "내가 그 일을 바라본 관점이 바로 그러했다."], ["Economy—with a dash of love.", "절약—거기에 사랑도 한 자밤."], ["I put it to my mistress, as in duty bound, just as I had put it to myself.", "나는 마땅히 그래야 할 도리대로, 나 자신에게 내놓았던 그대로 그 이야기를 마님께도 아뢰었다."]], [["“I have been turning Selina Goby over in my mind,”", "\"셀리나 고비 문제를 곰곰이 굴려 보았습니다만,\" 하고 나는 아뢰었다."], ["I said, “and I think, my lady, it will be cheaper to marry her than to keep her.”", "\"마님, 아무래도 그 여자를 데리고 사는 것보다 아예 혼인하는 편이 싸게 먹힐 성싶습니다.\""]], [["My lady burst out laughing, and said she didn’t know which to be most shocked at—my language or my principles.", "마님은 웃음을 터뜨리시더니, 내 말투에 더 놀라야 할지 내 소신에 더 놀라야 할지 모르겠다고 하셨다."], ["Some joke tickled her, I suppose, of the sort that you can’t take unless you are a person of quality.", "짐작건대, 지체 높은 분이 아니고서는 알아듣지 못할 종류의 어떤 우스갯거리가 마님의 심사를 간질였던 모양이다."], ["Understanding nothing myself but that I was free to put it next to Selina, I went and put it accordingly.", "나로 말하면, 이제 셀리나한테 그 말을 꺼내도 좋다는 것 말고는 아무것도 알아듣지 못한 채, 냉큼 가서 그대로 말을 꺼냈다."], ["And what did Selina say? Lord!", "그래서 셀리나가 뭐라고 했느냐고?"], ["how little you must know of women, if you ask that.", "원, 그런 걸 물으시다니, 여러분은 여자에 대해 어지간히도 모르시는구려."], ["Of course she said, Yes.", "물론 그녀는 \"네\" 하고 대답했다."]], [["As my time drew nearer, and there got to be talk of my having a new coat for the ceremony, my mind began to misgive me.", "그날이 가까워 오고, 예식에 입을 새 외투를 마련해야 한다는 이야기가 나오자, 내 마음에 슬며시 불안이 일기 시작했다."], ["I have compared notes with other men as to what they felt while they were in my interesting situation; and they have all acknowledged that, about a week before it happened, they privately wished themselves out of it.", "나는 나와 같은 흥미로운 처지에 놓였던 다른 사내들과 심경을 견주어 본 적이 있는데, 그들은 하나같이, 그 일이 있기 한 주 전쯤에는 속으로 이 일에서 발을 빼고 싶어 했노라 실토했다."], ["I went a trifle further than that myself; I actually rose up, as it were, and tried to get out of it.", "나 자신은 거기서 한 발짝 더 나아갔다. 나는 실제로, 이를테면 벌떡 일어나서, 이 일에서 빠져나오려고 애를 썼다."], ["Not for nothing!", "물론 거저는 아니었다!"], ["I was too just a man to expect she would let me off for nothing.", "나는 그녀가 나를 거저 놓아주리라 기대할 만큼 뻔뻔한 사람은 못 되었다."], ["Compensation to the woman when the man gets out of it, is one of the laws of England.", "사내가 발을 뺄 적에 여자에게 위자료를 물어 주는 것은, 영국 법도의 하나다."], ["In obedience to the laws, and after turning it over carefully in my mind, I offered Selina Goby a feather-bed and fifty shillings to be off the bargain.", "그 법도를 좇아, 그리고 마음속으로 신중히 저울질한 끝에, 나는 셀리나 고비에게 이 혼약을 무르는 대가로 깃털 이불 하나와 오십 실링을 내놓았다."], ["You will hardly believe it, but it is nevertheless true—she was fool enough to refuse.", "여러분은 좀처럼 믿지 못하시겠지만, 그래도 이것은 엄연한 사실이다—그녀는 어리석게도 그것을 마다했다."]], [["After that it was all over with me, of course.", "그다음엔, 물론, 나는 만사 끝장이었다."], ["I got the new coat as cheap as I could, and I went through all the rest of it as cheap as I could.", "나는 새 외투를 되도록 싸게 장만했고, 나머지 일도 죄다 되도록 싸게 치렀다."], ["We were not a happy couple, and not a miserable couple.", "우리는 행복한 부부도 아니요, 불행한 부부도 아니었다."], ["We were six of one and half-a-dozen of the other.", "이것이나 저것이나 그게 그거인, 도토리 키 재기였다."], ["How it was I don’t understand, but we always seemed to be getting, with the best of motives, in one another’s way.", "어찌 된 노릇인지 나도 모르겠으나, 우리는 더없이 좋은 뜻을 품고서도 늘 서로의 앞길을 가로막기 일쑤였다."], ["When I wanted to go upstairs, there was my wife coming down; or when my wife wanted to go down, there was I coming up.", "내가 위층으로 올라가려 하면 아내가 내려오고, 아내가 아래층으로 내려가려 하면 내가 올라가는 식이었다."], ["That is married life, according to my experience of it.", "내 겪어 본 바로는, 그게 바로 부부살이라는 것이다."]], [["After five years of misunderstandings on the stairs, it pleased an all-wise Providence to relieve us of each other by taking my wife.", "계단에서 오 년 동안 엇갈린 끝에, 전지(全知)하신 섭리께서 기꺼이 내 아내를 거두어 가심으로써 우리를 서로에게서 놓아주셨다."], ["I was left with my little girl Penelope, and with no other child.", "내게는 어린 딸 페넬로피 하나가 남고, 다른 자식은 없었다."], ["Shortly afterwards Sir John died, and my lady was left with her little girl, Miss Rachel, and no other child.", "그 얼마 뒤 존 경이 돌아가시니, 마님께는 어린 따님 레이철 아가씨 하나가 남고, 다른 자식은 없었다."], ["I have written to very poor purpose of my lady, if you require to be told that my little Penelope was taken care of, under my good mistress’s own eye, and was sent to school and taught, and made a sharp girl, and promoted, when old enough, to be Miss Rachel’s own maid.", "우리 어진 마님의 손수 보살핌 아래 내 어린 페넬로피가 자랐고, 학교에 보내져 배웠고, 야무진 처녀로 컸으며, 나이가 차자 레이철 아가씨의 몸종으로 발탁되었다는 것을—여러분께 굳이 일러 드려야 한다면, 나는 마님에 관해 참으로 형편없이 적어 온 셈이 될 것이다."]], [["As for me, I went on with my business as bailiff year after year up to Christmas 1847, when there came a change in my life.", "나로 말하면, 해가 바뀌고 또 바뀌도록 마름 일을 이어 가다가, 1847년 성탄절에 이르러 내 삶에 한 변화가 찾아왔다."], ["On that day, my lady invited herself to a cup of tea alone with me in my cottage.", "그날, 마님이 몸소 내 오두막을 찾아 나와 단둘이 차 한 잔을 청하셨다."], ["She remarked that, reckoning from the year when I started as page-boy in the time of the old lord, I had been more than fifty years in her service, and she put into my hands a beautiful waistcoat of wool that she had worked herself, to keep me warm in the bitter winter weather.", "마님은, 노영주 시절에 내가 시동으로 첫발을 디딘 그해부터 헤아리면, 내가 오십 년 넘도록 당신을 섬겨 왔노라 이르시고는, 살을 에는 겨울 추위에 나를 따뜻이 감싸라고, 손수 짜신 아름다운 양모 조끼를 내 손에 쥐여 주셨다."]], [["I received this magnificent present quite at a loss to find words to thank my mistress with for the honour she had done me.", "나는 이 훌륭한 선물을 받고서, 마님이 베풀어 주신 그 영광에 무슨 말로 감사를 올려야 할지 몰라 그저 어쩔 줄을 몰랐다."], ["To my great astonishment, it turned out, however, that the waistcoat was not an honour, but a bribe.", "그런데 놀랍게도, 알고 보니 그 조끼는 영광이 아니라 뇌물이었다."], ["My lady had discovered that I was getting old before I had discovered it myself, and she had come to my cottage to wheedle me (if I may use such an expression) into giving up my hard out-of-door work as bailiff, and taking my ease for the rest of my days as steward in the house.", "마님은 내가 미처 깨닫기도 전에 내가 늙어 간다는 것을 알아채셨고, (이런 표현을 써도 좋다면) 나를 살살 구슬려, 고된 바깥일인 마름 노릇을 그만두고 남은 날들일랑 저택의 집사로서 편히 지내게 하시려고 내 오두막을 찾아오셨던 것이다."], ["I made as good a fight of it against the indignity of taking my ease as I could.", "나는 편히 지내야 한다는 그 치욕에 맞서 있는 힘껏 버텨 보았다."], ["But my mistress knew the weak side of me; she put it as a favour to herself.", "하지만 마님은 내 약한 구석을 아셨으니, 그것을 당신을 위한 청으로 돌려 말씀하셨다."], ["The dispute between us ended, after that, in my wiping my eyes, like an old fool, with my new woollen waistcoat, and saying I would think about it.", "그렇게 해서 우리 사이의 실랑이는, 내가 늙은 바보처럼 새 양모 조끼로 눈물을 훔치며 한번 생각해 보겠노라 말하는 것으로 끝이 났다."]], [["The perturbation in my mind, in regard to thinking about it, being truly dreadful after my lady had gone away, I applied the remedy which I have never yet found to fail me in cases of doubt and emergency.", "마님이 돌아가신 뒤, 그 일을 생각해 본다는 것을 두고 내 마음이 심란하기가 이루 말할 수 없이 끔찍했으므로, 나는 의혹과 위급의 순간마다 여태 한 번도 나를 저버린 적이 없는 그 처방을 썼다."], ["I smoked a pipe and took a turn at Robinson Crusoe.", "담뱃대를 한 대 피우고 「로빈슨 크루소」를 한 대목 들춘 것이다."], ["Before I had occupied myself with that extraordinary book five minutes, I came on a comforting bit (page one hundred and fifty-eight), as follows: “Today we love, what tomorrow we hate.”", "그 비범한 책에 파묻힌 지 오 분도 채 지나지 않아, 나는 마음을 달래 주는 한 구절(백쉰여덟 쪽)을 만났으니, 이러하였다. \"오늘 우리가 사랑하는 것을, 내일 우리는 미워한다.\""], ["I saw my way clear directly.", "나는 대뜸 갈 길이 훤히 보였다."], ["Today I was all for continuing to be farm-bailiff; tomorrow, on the authority of Robinson Crusoe, I should be all the other way.", "오늘의 나는 한사코 농장 마름으로 남으려 한다. 그러나 내일의 나는, 「로빈슨 크루소」의 권위를 빌리자면, 정반대로 마음이 기울 터였다."], ["Take myself tomorrow while in tomorrow’s humour, and the thing was done.", "내일 내일의 기분에 잠겼을 때의 나를 붙잡으면, 일은 다 된 것이었다."], ["My mind being relieved in this manner, I went to sleep that night in the character of Lady Verinder’s farm-bailiff, and I woke up the next morning in the character of Lady Verinder’s house-steward.", "이렇게 마음을 놓은 나는, 그날 밤 레이디 버린더의 농장 마름이라는 신분으로 잠이 들었고, 이튿날 아침 레이디 버린더의 집사라는 신분으로 잠에서 깨었다."], ["All quite comfortable, and all through Robinson Crusoe!", "만사가 아주 편안하게, 그것도 죄다 「로빈슨 크루소」 덕분에!"]], [["My daughter Penelope has just looked over my shoulder to see what I have done so far.", "방금 내 딸 페넬로피가 내 어깨너머로 여태 내가 써 놓은 것을 들여다보았다."], ["She remarks that it is beautifully written, and every word of it true.", "이 애 말이, 글이 참으로 곱게 쓰였고 한 마디 한 마디가 죄다 사실이라고 한다."], ["But she points out one objection.", "그러면서도 한 가지 트집을 잡는다."], ["She says what I have done so far isn’t in the least what I was wanted to do.", "여태 내가 써 놓은 것이, 나한테 시킨 일과는 전혀 딴판이라는 것이다."], ["I am asked to tell the story of the Diamond and, instead of that, I have been telling the story of my own self.", "나는 다이아몬드 이야기를 하라고 부탁받았는데, 그 대신 내 신세타령이나 늘어놓고 있었다는 게다."], ["Curious, and quite beyond me to account for.", "희한한 노릇이요, 나로서는 도무지 까닭을 알 길이 없다."], ["I wonder whether the gentlemen who make a business and a living out of writing books, ever find their own selves getting in the way of their subjects, like me?", "책 쓰는 것으로 밥벌이하고 생업을 삼는 양반들도, 나처럼 저 자신이 제 주제(主題)를 가로막고 나서는 일을 겪는 적이 있을까 궁금하다."], ["If they do, I can feel for them.", "만일 그렇다면, 나는 그분들의 심정을 헤아릴 수 있겠다."], ["In the meantime, here is another false start, and more waste of good writing-paper.", "그건 그렇고, 여기 또 한 번의 헛발질과, 아까운 원고지 낭비가 있다."], ["What’s to be done now?", "이제 어쩐다?"], ["Nothing that I know of, except for you to keep your temper, and for me to begin it all over again for the third time.", "여러분은 부디 성을 삭이시고, 나는 세 번째로 처음부터 모조리 다시 시작하는 수밖에, 내가 아는 도리로는 달리 방도가 없다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["The question of how I am to start the story properly I have tried to settle in two ways.", "이야기를 제대로 시작하려면 어떻게 해야 하나, 이 문제를 나는 두 가지 방법으로 풀어 보려 했다."], ["First, by scratching my head, which led to nothing.", "첫째는 머리통을 긁적이는 것이었는데, 이것은 아무 소득이 없었다."], ["Second, by consulting my daughter Penelope, which has resulted in an entirely new idea.", "둘째는 내 딸 페넬로피와 의논하는 것이었는데, 이것이 아주 새로운 생각으로 열매를 맺었다."]], [["Penelope’s notion is that I should set down what happened, regularly day by day, beginning with the day when we got the news that Mr. Franklin Blake was expected on a visit to the house.", "페넬로피의 생각인즉, 일어난 일을 날마다 차례차례 적어 나가되, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 우리 집에 오시기로 되어 있다는 소식을 받은 그날부터 시작하라는 것이다."], ["When you come to fix your memory with a date in this way, it is wonderful what your memory will pick up for you upon that compulsion.", "이런 식으로 날짜를 딱 박아 기억을 붙들어 매 놓으면, 그 날짜에 걸려 기억이 무엇무엇을 끌어올려 주는지 참으로 놀라울 지경이다."], ["The only difficulty is to fetch out the dates, in the first place.", "다만 한 가지 어려움은, 애당초 그 날짜들을 캐내는 일이다."], ["This Penelope offers to do for me by looking into her own diary, which she was taught to keep when she was at school, and which she has gone on keeping ever since.", "이 일을 페넬로피가 제 일기장을 들춰 대신해 주겠다고 나선다. 그 애는 학교에 다닐 적에 일기 쓰는 법을 배웠고, 그때부터 지금껏 줄곧 써 오고 있다."], ["In answer to an improvement on this notion, devised by myself, namely, that she should tell the story instead of me, out of her own diary, Penelope observes, with a fierce look and a red face, that her journal is for her own private eye, and that no living creature shall ever know what is in it but herself.", "이 생각을 한층 더 낫게 손질한답시고, 나 스스로—그러니까 나 대신 그 애가 제 일기장을 밑천 삼아 이야기를 하면 어떻겠느냐고—궁리해 내놓았더니, 페넬로피는 눈을 사납게 치뜨고 얼굴을 붉히며 하는 말이, 제 일기는 저 혼자 보려고 쓴 것이요 그 안에 무엇이 적혔는지는 살아 있는 그 어떤 것도 오직 저 말고는 결코 알지 못하리라는 것이다."], ["When I inquire what this means, Penelope says, “Fiddlesticks!”", "그게 무슨 소리냐고 내가 물으니, 페넬로피는 \"쓸데없는 소리!\" 한다."], ["I say, Sweethearts.", "나는 \"아이고, 요 귀여운 것.\" 한다."]], [["Beginning, then, on Penelope’s plan, I beg to mention that I was specially called one Wednesday morning into my lady’s own sitting-room, the date being the twenty-fourth of May, eighteen hundred and forty-eight.", "그러면, 페넬로피의 방식대로 시작하기로 하고, 나는 어느 수요일 아침에 특별히 마님의 거실로 불려 들어갔음을 아뢰는 바이다. 날짜는 1848년 5월 24일이었다."]], [["“Gabriel,”", "\"게이브리얼.\""], ["says my lady, “here is news that will surprise you.", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"자네가 놀랄 만한 소식이 있네."], ["Franklin Blake has come back from abroad.", "프랭클린 블레이크가 외국에서 돌아왔어."], ["He has been staying with his father in London, and he is coming to us tomorrow to stop till next month, and keep Rachel’s birthday.”", "런던에서 제 아버지와 함께 지내다가, 내일 우리한테 와서 다음 달까지 묵으며 레이철의 생일을 함께 쇨 참이라네.\""]], [["If I had had a hat in my hand, nothing but respect would have prevented me from throwing that hat up to the ceiling.", "만일 그때 내 손에 모자가 들려 있었던들, 오직 예의 하나가 나를 말렸을 뿐, 나는 그 모자를 천장까지 냅다 던져 올렸을 것이다."], ["I had not seen Mr. Franklin since he was a boy, living along with us in this house.", "나는 프랭클린 도련님이 소년이던 시절, 우리와 함께 이 집에서 살던 그 뒤로 그분을 뵌 적이 없었다."], ["He was, out of all sight (as I remember him), the nicest boy that ever spun a top or broke a window.", "내 기억으로 그분은, 팽이를 돌리거나 유리창을 깨뜨린 어느 사내아이보다도 단연코 가장 사랑스러운 아이였다."], ["Miss Rachel, who was present, and to whom I made that remark, observed, in return, that she remembered him as the most atrocious tyrant that ever tortured a doll, and the hardest driver of an exhausted little girl in string harness that England could produce.", "마침 그 자리에 있던 레이철 아가씨는, 내가 그 말을 건네자 되받아 이르시기를, 아가씨 당신은 그분을, 인형을 못살게 군 것으로 치면 세상에 둘도 없는 흉악한 폭군이요 노끈 마구를 씌워 지쳐 빠진 어린 계집아이를 몰아댄 것으로 치면 온 영국이 낳을 수 있는 가장 모진 마부로 기억한다고 하셨다."], ["“I burn with indignation, and I ache with fatigue,”", "\"나는 분해서 몸이 타오르고, 고단해서 삭신이 쑤신다니까.\""], ["was the way Miss Rachel summed it up, “when I think of Franklin Blake.”", "레이철 아가씨는 프랭클린 블레이크를 떠올릴 때의 심경을 그렇게 요약하셨다."]], [["Hearing what I now tell you, you will naturally ask how it was that Mr.", "지금 내가 하는 이야기를 듣고서, 여러분은 자연히 물으실 것이다."], ["Franklin should have passed all the years, from the time when he was a boy to the time when he was a man, out of his own country.", "프랭클린 도련님이 소년이던 때부터 어른이 된 때까지 그 세월을 어찌하여 죄다 제 나라 밖에서 보내게 되었느냐고. 나는 이렇게 답하겠다."], ["I answer, because his father had the misfortune to be next heir to a Dukedom, and not to be able to prove it.", "그분의 부친께서 공작 작위의 다음 상속자가 되고서도 그것을 증명해 내지 못하는 불운을 겪으셨기 때문이라고."]], [["In two words, this was how the thing happened:", "두 마디로 하자면, 일은 이렇게 된 것이다."]], [["My lady’s eldest sister married the celebrated Mr. Blake—equally famous for his great riches, and his great suit at law.", "마님의 맏언니께서 그 유명한 블레이크 씨와 혼인하셨다—어마어마한 재산으로도 유명하고 어마어마한 소송으로도 똑같이 유명한 분이었다."], ["How many years he went on worrying the tribunals of his country to turn out the Duke in possession, and to put himself in the Duke’s place—how many lawyer’s purses he filled to bursting, and how many otherwise harmless people he set by the ears together disputing whether he was right or wrong—is more by a great deal than I can reckon up.", "그분이 작위를 차지하고 있는 공작을 몰아내고 그 자리에 자기가 들어앉겠노라며 나라의 법정들을 몇 해나 들볶았는지, 변호사들의 주머니를 몇 개나 터지도록 채워 주었는지, 그리고 그분이 옳으니 그르니 다투게 만들어 서로 멱살을 잡게 한 애먼 사람들이 몇이나 되는지는—내가 헤아릴 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 많다."], ["His wife died, and two of his three children died, before the tribunals could make up their minds to show him the door and take no more of his money.", "법정이 마침내 그분을 문밖으로 내치고 그 돈을 더는 받지 않기로 마음을 정하기도 전에, 그분의 아내가 죽고 세 자녀 가운데 둘이 죽었다."], ["When it was all over, and the Duke in possession was left in possession, Mr. Blake discovered that the only way of being even with his country for the manner in which it had treated him, was not to let his country have the honour of educating his son.", "만사가 다 끝나 작위를 차지한 공작이 그대로 작위를 지니게 되자, 블레이크 씨는 자기를 그런 식으로 대접한 조국에 앙갚음하는 유일한 길이, 조국으로 하여금 제 아들을 교육하는 영예를 누리지 못하게 하는 것임을 깨달았다."], ["“How can I trust my native institutions,” was the form in which he put it, “after the way in which my native institutions have behaved to me?”", "\"내 나라의 제도가 나한테 이 따위로 굴었는데, 내가 어찌 그것을 믿을 수 있겠는가?\""], ["Add to this, that Mr.", "그분은 그런 식으로 말했다."], ["Blake disliked all boys, his own included, and you will admit that it could only end in one way.", "여기에다, 블레이크 씨가 제 자식을 포함해 사내아이라면 죄다 질색했다는 점까지 보태면, 여러분도 일이 한 가지 결말로밖에 끝날 수 없었음을 인정하시리라."], ["Master Franklin was taken from us in England, and was sent to institutions which his father could trust, in that superior country, Germany; Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님은 영국의 우리 곁에서 떨어져 나가, 그 잘난 나라 독일, 즉 부친께서 믿을 수 있는 제도들이 있는 곳으로 보내졌다."], ["Blake himself, you will observe, remaining snug in England, to improve his fellow-countrymen in the Parliament House, and to publish a statement on the subject of the Duke in possession, which has remained an unfinished statement from that day to this.", "정작 블레이크 씨 본인은, 보시다시피, 영국에 아늑히 눌러앉아 의사당에서 동포들을 계도하고 작위를 차지한 공작에 관한 성명서를 펴내는 일에 몰두하셨으니, 그 성명서는 그날 이후 오늘날까지 미완성인 채로 남아 있다."]], [["There!", "자!"], ["thank God, that’s told!", "하느님 감사합니다, 그 이야기는 다 했다!"], ["Neither you nor I need trouble our heads any more about Mr. Blake, senior.", "여러분도 나도 이제 블레이크 씨, 그 어른에 관해서는 더 골머리를 앓을 것이 없다."], ["Leave him to the Dukedom; and let you and I stick to the Diamond.", "그분은 공작 작위에나 맡겨 두고, 여러분과 나는 다이아몬드에나 매달리기로 하자."]], [["The Diamond takes us back to Mr. Franklin, who was the innocent means of bringing that unlucky jewel into the house.", "다이아몬드 이야기가 나오니 다시 프랭클린 도련님께로 돌아가게 되는데, 그 불길한 보석을 이 집으로 들여온 죄 없는 장본인이 바로 그분이었다."]], [["Our nice boy didn’t forget us after he went abroad.", "우리 착한 도련님은 외국으로 떠난 뒤에도 우리를 잊지 않았다."], ["He wrote every now and then; sometimes to my lady, sometimes to Miss Rachel, and sometimes to me.", "그분은 이따금 편지를 보내오셨다. 어떤 때는 마님께, 어떤 때는 레이철 아가씨께, 또 어떤 때는 내게."], ["We had had a transaction together, before he left, which consisted in his borrowing of me a ball of string, a four-bladed knife, and seven-and-sixpence in money—the colour of which last I have not seen, and never expect to see again.", "그분이 떠나기 전에 우리 사이에는 거래가 하나 있었으니, 그분이 내게서 노끈 한 뭉치와 날이 넷 달린 칼 한 자루, 그리고 돈 칠 실링 육 펜스를 꾸어 간 것이었다—그 돈으로 말할 것 같으면, 나는 그 빛깔을 여태 다시 본 적이 없고 앞으로도 다시 보리라 기대하지 않는다."], ["His letters to me chiefly related to borrowing more.", "내게 오는 그분의 편지는 주로 더 꾸어 달라는 내용이었다."], ["I heard, however, from my lady, how he got on abroad, as he grew in years and stature.", "그렇지만 그분이 나이와 키가 자라 감에 따라 외국에서 어찌 지내는지는 마님께 전해 들었다."], ["After he had learnt what the institutions of Germany could teach him, he gave the French a turn next, and the Italians a turn after that.", "그분은 독일의 제도가 가르쳐 줄 수 있는 것을 다 배우고 나서, 다음으로는 프랑스를 한 차례 겪고, 그다음으로는 이탈리아를 한 차례 겪었다."], ["They made him among them a sort of universal genius, as well as I could understand it.", "내가 이해한 바로는, 그 나라들이 그분을 이를테면 만능 천재 비슷한 것으로 만들어 놓은 모양이었다."], ["He wrote a little; he painted a little; he sang and played and composed a little—borrowing, as I suspect, in all these cases, just as he had borrowed from me.", "그분은 글도 좀 쓰고, 그림도 좀 그리고, 노래도 좀 하고 연주도 좀 하고 작곡도 좀 했다—짐작건대 이 모든 경우에도 나한테서 꾸어 갔듯 여기저기서 꾸어다 한 것이렷다."], ["His mother’s fortune (seven hundred a year) fell to him when he came of age, and ran through him, as it might be through a sieve.", "그분이 성년이 되자 어머니의 유산(연 700파운드)이 그분 몫으로 떨어졌는데, 그것은 마치 체를 빠져나가듯 그분을 술술 빠져나갔다. 돈이 많아질수록 그분은 더 많이 바랐다."], ["The more money he had, the more he wanted; there was a hole in Mr. Franklin’s pocket that nothing would sew up.", "프랭클린 도련님의 주머니에는 무엇으로도 기워지지 않는 구멍이 하나 뚫려 있었던 것이다."], ["Wherever he went, the lively, easy way of him made him welcome.", "어디를 가든 그분의 발랄하고 스스럼없는 됨됨이가 그분을 반갑게 만들었다."], ["He lived here, there, and everywhere; his address (as he used to put it himself) being “Post Office, Europe—to be left till called for.”", "그분은 여기저기, 어디에서나 살았다. 그분의 주소는 (본인이 즐겨 이르던 대로 하면) \"유럽 우체국—찾으러 올 때까지 보관 요망\"이었다."], ["Twice over, he made up his mind to come back to England and see us; and twice over (saving your presence), some unmentionable woman stood in the way and stopped him.", "두 차례나 그분은 영국으로 돌아와 우리를 보러 오리라 마음먹었으나, 두 차례 다 (외람된 말씀이오나) 입에 올리지 못할 어떤 여자가 앞을 가로막아 그분을 붙들어 세웠다."], ["His third attempt succeeded, as you know already from what my lady told me.", "세 번째 시도가 성공했다는 것은, 마님이 내게 이르신 바로 여러분도 이미 아시는 대로다."], ["On Thursday the twenty-fifth of May, we were to see for the first time what our nice boy had grown to be as a man.", "5월 25일 목요일에, 우리는 우리 착한 도련님이 어른이 되어 어떤 모습으로 자랐는지 처음으로 보게 될 참이었다."], ["He came of good blood; he had a high courage; and he was five-and-twenty years of age, by our reckoning. Now you know as much of Mr.", "그분은 좋은 혈통을 타고났고, 드높은 용기를 지녔으며, 우리 셈으로 스물다섯 살이었다."], ["Franklin Blake as I did—before Mr. Franklin Blake came down to our house.", "자, 이제 여러분은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에 관해, 그분이 우리 집에 내려오기 전의 내가 알던 만큼은 아시게 된 셈이다."]], [["The Thursday was as fine a summer’s day as ever you saw: and my lady and Miss Rachel (not expecting Mr.", "그 목요일은 여러분이 여태 본 그 어떤 날 못지않게 화창한 여름날이었다."], ["Franklin till dinner-time) drove out to lunch with some friends in the neighbourhood.", "그리고 마님과 레이철 아가씨는 (프랭클린 도련님이 저녁 식사 때나 되어야 오시리라 여기고서) 이웃에 사는 벗들과 점심을 들러 마차를 몰고 나가셨다."]], [["When they were gone, I went and had a look at the bedroom which had been got ready for our guest, and saw that all was straight.", "두 분이 떠나신 뒤, 나는 손님을 맞으려고 채비해 둔 침실을 둘러보러 가서, 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있는지 살폈다."], ["Then, being butler in my lady’s establishment, as well as steward (at my own particular request, mind, and because it vexed me to see anybody but myself in possession of the key of the late Sir John’s cellar)—then, I say, I fetched up some of our famous Latour claret, and set it in the warm summer air to take off the chill before dinner.", "그러고 나서, 마님 댁에서 마름일 뿐 아니라 집사 노릇도 겸하고 있던 터라—이건 (똑똑히 새겨 두시라) 순전히 내 특별한 청에 따른 것으로, 지금은 고인이 되신 존 경의 술 창고 열쇠를 나 아닌 다른 누가 쥐고 있는 꼴을 보면 심사가 뒤틀렸기 때문이다—아무튼 내 말이지, 나는 우리 집의 그 유명한 라투르 클라레 포도주를 몇 병 꺼내 와, 저녁 식사 전에 냉기를 가시게 하려고 따뜻한 여름 공기 속에 놓아두었다."], ["Concluding to set myself in the warm summer air next—seeing that what is good for old claret is equally good for old age—I took up my beehive chair to go out into the back court, when I was stopped by hearing a sound like the soft beating of a drum, on the terrace in front of my lady’s residence.", "오래 묵은 클라레에 좋은 것은 늙은이한테도 매한가지로 좋으렷다 싶어, 다음은 나 자신을 그 따뜻한 여름 공기 속에 놓아두기로 하고, 뒤뜰로 나가려 벌집 모양 등받이 의자를 집어 드는데, 마님 저택 앞 테라스에서 마치 북을 나직이 두드리는 듯한 소리가 들려 나는 멈칫 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["Going round to the terrace, I found three mahogany-coloured Indians, in white linen frocks and trousers, looking up at the house.", "테라스 쪽으로 돌아가 보니, 흰 아마포 웃옷과 바지를 걸친 마호가니 빛 살갗의 인도인 셋이 우리 집을 올려다보고 서 있었다."]], [["The Indians, as I saw on looking closer, had small hand-drums slung in front of them.", "가까이서 보니 그 인도인들은 저마다 조그만 손북을 앞에 매달고 있었다."], ["Behind them stood a little delicate-looking light-haired English boy carrying a bag.", "그들 뒤에는 가방을 든, 여리여리하고 살결이 고운 금발의 영국 소년 하나가 서 있었다."], ["I judged the fellows to be strolling conjurors, and the boy with the bag to be carrying the tools of their trade.", "나는 이 사내들을 떠돌이 요술쟁이로, 가방을 든 그 소년을 그들 장사 밑천을 나르는 아이로 짐작했다."], ["One of the three, who spoke English and who exhibited, I must own, the most elegant manners, presently informed me that my judgment was right.", "셋 가운데 영어를 하는 한 명은, 실토하건대 여간 우아한 태도를 지닌 자였는데, 이내 내 짐작이 옳았음을 일러 주었다."], ["He requested permission to show his tricks in the presence of the lady of the house.", "그는 이 집 마님 앞에서 자기 재주를 보여 드릴 수 있게 허락해 달라고 청했다."]], [["Now I am not a sour old man.", "자, 나는 심술궂은 늙은이가 아니다."], ["I am generally all for amusement, and the last person in the world to distrust another person because he happens to be a few shades darker than myself.", "나는 웬만하면 다 흥겨운 것을 좋아하는 사람이요, 누군가가 나보다 살빛이 몇 겹 검다는 까닭만으로 그 사람을 못 믿을 인간은 결코 아니다."], ["But the best of us have our weaknesses—and my weakness, when I know a family plate-basket to be out on a pantry-table, is to be instantly reminded of that basket by the sight of a strolling stranger whose manners are superior to my own.", "하지만 우리 중 아무리 잘난 사람에게도 약점은 있는 법이라—내 약점인즉, 집안의 은 접시 바구니가 식기실 탁자에 나와 있는 줄 알고 있을 적에, 나보다 태도가 점잖은 떠돌이 낯선 자를 보기만 하면 그 바구니가 대뜸 떠오른다는 것이다."], ["I accordingly informed the Indian that the lady of the house was out; and I warned him and his party off the premises.", "그리하여 나는 그 인도인에게 이 집 마님은 출타 중이라 이르고, 그와 그 일행을 이 집 안에서 나가라 경고했다."], ["He made me a beautiful bow in return; and he and his party went off the premises.", "그는 답례로 내게 아름다운 절을 한 번 하더니, 자기 일행과 함께 집 밖으로 나갔다."], ["On my side, I returned to my beehive chair, and set myself down on the sunny side of the court, and fell (if the truth must be owned), not exactly into a sleep, but into the next best thing to it.", "나는 나대로 벌집 모양 의자로 돌아가 뜰의 양지바른 쪽에 자리를 잡고 앉아, (사실대로 털어놓자면) 잠이라고까지는 할 수 없으되 그다음으로 좋은 상태에 빠져들었다."]], [["I was roused up by my daughter Penelope running out at me as if the house was on fire.", "나를 흔들어 깨운 것은, 집에 불이라도 난 듯 내게로 뛰쳐나온 내 딸 페넬로피였다. 이 애가 무엇을 바랐는지 여러분은 아시겠는가?"], ["What do you think she wanted?", "이 애는 그 인도인 요술쟁이 셋을 당장 붙잡아 가두기를 바랐다."], ["She wanted to have the three Indian jugglers instantly taken up; for this reason, namely, that they knew who was coming from London to visit us, and that they meant some mischief to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "그 까닭인즉, 그자들이 런던에서 누가 우리를 찾아오는지를 알고 있으며 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 무슨 해코지를 하려 든다는 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Franklin’s name roused me.", "프랭클린 도련님의 이름에 나는 정신이 번쩍 들었다."], ["I opened my eyes, and made my girl explain herself.", "나는 눈을 뜨고, 내 딸에게 무슨 소린지 똑똑히 말해 보라 했다."]], [["It appeared that Penelope had just come from our lodge, where she had been having a gossip with the lodge-keeper’s daughter.", "알고 보니 페넬로피는 방금 우리 문지기 오두막에서 오는 길이었는데, 거기서 문지기 딸과 수다를 떨고 있었더란다."], ["The two girls had seen the Indians pass out, after I had warned them off, followed by their little boy.", "두 처녀는, 내가 인도인들을 쫓아낸 뒤에 그자들이 어린 소년을 뒤딸린 채 나가는 것을 보았다."], ["Taking it into their heads that the boy was ill-used by the foreigners—for no reason that I could discover, except that he was pretty and delicate-looking—the two girls had stolen along the inner side of the hedge between us and the road, and had watched the proceedings of the foreigners on the outer side.", "그 소년이 외국인들에게 학대받고 있다고 지레짐작하고—내가 알아낼 수 있는 까닭이라곤 그저 그 아이가 곱상하고 여리여리해 보인다는 것뿐이었지만—두 처녀는 우리 집과 한길 사이의 산울타리 안쪽을 따라 몰래 다가가서는, 그 바깥쪽에서 외국인들이 하는 짓을 지켜보았다."], ["Those proceedings resulted in the performance of the following extraordinary tricks.", "그자들이 한 짓이란 다음과 같은 괴상한 재주로 이어졌다."]], [["They first looked up the road, and down the road, and made sure that they were alone.", "그자들은 먼저 한길 위쪽을 보고 아래쪽을 보며, 자기들뿐임을 확인했다."], ["Then they all three faced about, and stared hard in the direction of our house.", "그러고는 셋이 다 몸을 돌려, 우리 집 쪽을 뚫어져라 노려보았다."], ["Then they jabbered and disputed in their own language, and looked at each other like men in doubt.", "그러고는 저희끼리 저희 말로 지껄이며 옥신각신하더니, 갈피를 못 잡는 사람들처럼 서로를 쳐다보았다."], ["Then they all turned to their little English boy, as if they expected him to help them.", "그러고는 셋이 다 저희 어린 영국 소년에게로 돌아서서, 마치 그 아이가 저희를 도와주기를 바라는 듯했다."], ["And then the chief Indian, who spoke English, said to the boy, “Hold out your hand.”", "그러더니 영어를 하는 우두머리 인도인이 소년에게 말했다. \"손을 내밀어라.\""]], [["On hearing those dreadful words, my daughter Penelope said she didn’t know what prevented her heart from flying straight out of her.", "그 무시무시한 말을 듣는 순간, 내 딸 페넬로피는 제 심장이 곧장 몸 밖으로 튀어나가지 않은 게 무슨 조화인지 모르겠더란다."], ["I thought privately that it might have been her stays.", "나는 속으로, 그거야 그 애가 조인 코르셋 덕이었으려니 했다."], ["All I said, however, was, “You make my flesh creep.”", "그러나 내가 입 밖에 낸 말은 그저 \"네 말을 들으니 소름이 다 끼치는구나.\"뿐이었다."], ["(Nota bene: Women like these little compliments.)", "(참고삼아 이르는 말이오나, 여자들은 이런 자잘한 치사를 좋아한다.)"]], [["Well, when the Indian said, “Hold out your hand,”", "자, 그 인도인이 \"손을 내밀어라.\""], ["the boy shrunk back, and shook his head, and said he didn’t like it.", "하자, 소년은 움찔 뒤로 물러나 고개를 젓고는 그게 싫다고 했다."], ["The Indian, thereupon, asked him (not at all unkindly), whether he would like to be sent back to London, and left where they had found him, sleeping in an empty basket in a market—a hungry, ragged, and forsaken little boy.", "그러자 인도인은 (조금도 모질지 않게) 아이에게 물었다. 다시 런던으로 돌려보내져, 저희가 처음 아이를 주워 왔던 곳, 그러니까 저잣거리의 빈 바구니 속에서 잠자던 그곳, 굶주리고 누더기를 걸친 채 버림받은 어린아이로 되돌아가고 싶으냐고."], ["This, it seems, ended the difficulty.", "이것으로 실랑이는 끝난 모양이었다."], ["The little chap unwillingly held out his hand.", "어린 녀석은 마지못해 손을 내밀었다."], ["Upon that, the Indian took a bottle from his bosom, and poured out of it some black stuff, like ink, into the palm of the boy’s hand.", "그러자 인도인은 품에서 병을 하나 꺼내, 그 속에서 잉크 같은 검은 것을 소년의 손바닥에 부었다."], ["The Indian—first touching the boy’s head, and making signs over it in the air—then said, “Look.”", "인도인은—먼저 소년의 머리에 손을 대고 그 위 허공에다 무슨 표시를 그리더니—이윽고 말했다."], ["The boy became quite stiff, and stood like a statue, looking into the ink in the hollow of his hand.", "\"보아라.\" 소년은 아주 뻣뻣하게 굳어, 조각상처럼 서서 제 손바닥 오목한 곳의 잉크를 들여다보았다."]], [["(So far, it seemed to me to be juggling, accompanied by a foolish waste of ink.", "(여기까지는, 내가 보기에 어리석게 잉크나 축내는 요술로만 여겨졌다."], ["I was beginning to feel sleepy again, when Penelope’s next words stirred me up.)", "나는 다시 슬슬 졸음이 오려는 참이었는데, 페넬로피의 다음 말이 나를 흔들어 깨웠다.)"]], [["The Indians looked up the road and down the road once more—and then the chief Indian said these words to the boy; “See the English gentleman from foreign parts.”", "인도인들은 한 번 더 한길 위쪽을 보고 아래쪽을 보았다—그러고는 우두머리 인도인이 소년에게 이런 말을 했다. \"외국에서 오는 그 영국 신사를 보아라.\""]], [["The boy said, “I see him.”", "소년이 말했다. \"보입니다.\""]], [["The Indian said, “Is it on the road to this house, and on no other, that the English gentleman will travel today?”", "인도인이 말했다. \"그 영국 신사가 오늘 여행할 길이, 다른 어느 길도 아닌 바로 이 집으로 오는 길이더냐?\""]], [["The boy said, “It is on the road to this house, and on no other, that the English gentleman will travel today.”", "소년이 말했다. \"그 영국 신사가 오늘 여행할 길은, 다른 어느 길도 아닌 바로 이 집으로 오는 길입니다.\""]], [["The Indian put a second question—after waiting a little first.", "인도인은—먼저 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤에—두 번째 물음을 던졌다."], ["He said: “Has the English gentleman got It about him?”", "그가 말했다. \"그 영국 신사가 그것을 몸에 지니고 있더냐?\""]], [["The boy answered—also, after waiting a little first—“Yes.”", "소년은—역시 먼저 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤에—대답했다. \"예.\""]], [["The Indian put a third and last question: “Will the English gentleman come here, as he has promised to come, at the close of day?”", "인도인이 세 번째이자 마지막 물음을 던졌다. \"그 영국 신사가, 약속한 대로, 해 질 무렵에 이리로 오겠더냐?\""]], [["The boy said, “I can’t tell.”", "소년이 말했다. \"그건 모르겠습니다.\""]], [["The Indian asked why.", "인도인이 까닭을 물었다."]], [["The boy said, “I am tired.", "소년이 말했다. \"저는 지쳤습니다."], ["The mist rises in my head, and puzzles me.", "머릿속에 안개가 피어올라 저를 어지럽힙니다."], ["I can see no more today.”", "오늘은 더 볼 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["With that the catechism ended.", "그것으로 그 문답은 끝났다."], ["The chief Indian said something in his own language to the other two, pointing to the boy, and pointing towards the town, in which (as we afterwards discovered) they were lodged.", "우두머리 인도인은 소년을 가리키고, 또 그들이 (뒤에 우리가 알아낸 바로는) 묵고 있던 읍내 쪽을 가리키며, 저희 말로 다른 두 사람에게 무어라 일렀다."], ["He then, after making more signs on the boy’s head, blew on his forehead, and so woke him up with a start.", "그러고는 소년의 머리에 다시 몇 가지 표시를 한 뒤, 그 이마에 입김을 훅 불어 소년을 화들짝 깨웠다."], ["After that, they all went on their way towards the town, and the girls saw them no more.", "그런 다음 그들은 다 함께 읍내 쪽으로 제 갈 길을 갔고, 처녀들은 그들을 더는 보지 못했다."]], [["Most things they say have a moral, if you only look for it.", "사람들이 하는 대개의 일에는, 찾아보려고만 들면 교훈이 담겨 있는 법이다."], ["What was the moral of this?", "그렇다면 이 일의 교훈은 무엇이었을까?"]], [["The moral was, as I thought: First, that the chief juggler had heard Mr.", "교훈인즉, 내 생각으로는 이러했다."], ["Franklin’s arrival talked of among the servants out-of-doors, and saw his way to making a little money by it.", "첫째, 그 우두머리 요술쟁이가 바깥의 하인들 사이에서 프랭클린 도련님의 도착 이야기가 오가는 것을 듣고 그것으로 돈푼깨나 벌 궁리를 했다는 것."], ["Second, that he and his men and boy (with a view to making the said money) meant to hang about till they saw my lady drive home, and then to come back, and foretell Mr. Franklin’s arrival by magic.", "둘째, 그자가 (그 돈을 벌 셈으로) 제 사내들과 소년을 데리고, 마님이 마차를 몰고 집으로 돌아오시는 것을 볼 때까지 근처를 어정거리다가, 그런 다음 다시 와서 프랭클린 도련님의 도착을 요술로 알아맞히려 했다는 것."], ["Third, that Penelope had heard them rehearsing their hocus-pocus, like actors rehearsing a play.", "셋째, 페넬로피가 들은 것은, 배우들이 연극을 연습하듯 그자들이 저희 요술 눈속임을 미리 익히는 소리였다는 것."], ["Fourth, that I should do well to have an eye, that evening, on the plate-basket.", "넷째, 나는 그날 저녁에 그 은 접시 바구니를 잘 지켜보는 편이 좋으리라는 것."], ["Fifth, that Penelope would do well to cool down, and leave me, her father, to doze off again in the sun.", "다섯째, 페넬로피는 흥분을 가라앉히고 제 아비인 나를 두고 물러가, 다시 볕 아래서 졸게 내버려 두는 편이 좋으리라는 것."]], [["That appeared to me to be the sensible view.", "내가 보기에는 그것이 사리에 맞는 견해였다."], ["If you know anything of the ways of young women, you won’t be surprised to hear that Penelope wouldn’t take it.", "젊은 여자들의 성미를 조금이라도 아신다면, 페넬로피가 그 견해를 받아들이지 않았다는 이야기를 들으셔도 놀라지 않으실 것이다."], ["The moral of the thing was serious, according to my daughter.", "내 딸의 말로는, 이 일의 교훈은 심상치 않은 것이었다."], ["She particularly reminded me of the Indian’s third question, Has the English gentleman got It about him?", "그 애는 특히 인도인의 세 번째 물음, 곧 그 영국 신사가 그것을 몸에 지니고 있더냐, 하던 것을 내게 새삼 일깨웠다."], ["“Oh, father!”", "\"아, 아버지!\""], ["says Penelope, clasping her hands, “don’t joke about this.", "페넬로피가 두 손을 마주 잡으며 말했다. \"이 일로 농담하지 마세요."], ["What does ‘It’ mean?”", "'그것'이 무슨 뜻일까요?\""]], [["“We’ll ask Mr. Franklin, my dear,”", "\"프랭클린 도련님께 여쭤보자꾸나, 얘야.\""], ["I said, “if you can wait till Mr.", "나는 말했다. \"네가 프랭클린 도련님이 오실 때까지 기다릴 수만 있다면 말이다.\""], ["Franklin comes.” I winked to show I meant that in joke.", "나는 그게 농담이라는 뜻으로 눈을 찡긋해 보였다."], ["Penelope took it quite seriously.", "페넬로피는 그것을 아주 진지하게 받아들였다."], ["My girl’s earnestness tickled me.", "내 딸의 그 진지함이 나를 우습게 간질였다."], ["“What on earth should Mr. Franklin know about it?”", "\"대체 프랭클린 도련님이 그 일에 대해 무엇을 아신단 말이냐?\""], ["I inquired.", "내가 물었다."], ["“Ask him,”", "\"여쭤보세요.\""], ["says Penelope.", "페넬로피가 말했다."], ["“And see whether he thinks it a laughing matter, too.”", "\"그러곤 그분도 이걸 웃음거리로 여기시는지 두고 보자고요.\""], ["With that parting shot, my daughter left me.", "그 한마디를 이별의 화살처럼 쏘아 놓고, 내 딸은 나를 떠났다."]], [["I settled it with myself, when she was gone, that I really would ask Mr. Franklin—mainly to set Penelope’s mind at rest.", "그 애가 가고 난 뒤, 나는 정말로 프랭클린 도련님께 여쭤보리라고 혼자 마음을 정했다—주로 페넬로피의 마음을 가라앉혀 주기 위해서였다."], ["What was said between us, when I did ask him, later on that same day, you will find set out fully in its proper place.", "그날 늦게 내가 실제로 여쭈었을 때 우리 사이에 오간 말은, 마땅한 자리에 이르러 낱낱이 적어 놓은 것을 보시게 될 것이다."], ["But as I don’t wish to raise your expectations and then disappoint them, I will take leave to warn you here—before we go any further—that you won’t find the ghost of a joke in our conversation on the subject of the jugglers.", "그러나 나는 여러분의 기대를 잔뜩 부풀려 놓았다가 저버리고 싶지는 않으므로, 여기서—더 나아가기 전에—미리 일러두는 것을 허락받고자 한다. 그 요술쟁이들에 관한 우리 대화에서는 농담의 그림자조차 찾아보지 못하시리라는 것을."], ["To my great surprise, Mr. Franklin, like Penelope, took the thing seriously.", "참으로 놀랍게도, 프랭클린 도련님은 페넬로피와 마찬가지로 그 일을 진지하게 받아들이셨다."], ["How seriously, you will understand, when I tell you that, in his opinion, “It” meant the Moonstone.", "얼마나 진지했는가 하면, 그분의 소견으로는 '그것'이 곧 월장석을 뜻했다고 내가 말씀드리면 여러분도 아시게 되리라."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["I am truly sorry to detain you over me and my beehive chair.", "여러분을 나와 내 벌집 의자 곁에 이렇게 붙들어 두어 참으로 미안하다."], ["A sleepy old man, in a sunny back yard, is not an interesting object, I am well aware.", "볕바른 뒤뜰에서 꾸벅거리는 늙은이란 흥미로운 구경거리가 못 된다는 것쯤은 나도 잘 안다."], ["But things must be put down in their places, as things actually happened—and you must please to jog on a little while longer with me, in expectation of Mr. Franklin Blake’s arrival later in the day.", "그러나 일이란 실제로 일어난 그대로 마땅한 제자리에 적어 두어야 하는 법이니—부디 조금만 더 나와 함께 터덜터덜 걸으시며, 이날 늦게 있을 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 도착을 기다려 주시기 바란다."]], [["Before I had time to doze off again, after my daughter Penelope had left me, I was disturbed by a rattling of plates and dishes in the servants’ hall, which meant that dinner was ready.", "내 딸 페넬로피가 나를 떠난 뒤, 미처 다시 졸음에 빠져들 새도 없이, 나는 하인들 식당에서 접시와 그릇이 달그락거리는 소리에 방해를 받았으니, 그것은 곧 저녁 식사가 준비되었다는 뜻이었다."], ["Taking my own meals in my own sitting-room, I had nothing to do with the servants’ dinner, except to wish them a good stomach to it all round, previous to composing myself once more in my chair.", "나는 내 식사를 내 거실에서 따로 드는지라, 하인들 저녁과는 아무 상관이 없었고, 그저 다시 의자에 몸을 편안히 누이기 전에 다들 맛있게 잘 먹으라고 빌어 주는 것이 고작이었다."], ["I was just stretching my legs, when out bounced another woman on me.", "막 다리를 쭉 뻗으려는데, 또 다른 여자 하나가 내게로 튀어나왔다."], ["Not my daughter again; only Nancy, the kitchen-maid, this time.", "이번엔 딸이 아니라, 부엌데기 낸시였다."], ["I was straight in her way out; and I observed, as she asked me to let her by, that she had a sulky face—a thing which, as head of the servants, I never allow, on principle, to pass me without inquiry.", "나는 마침 그녀가 나가는 길목에 딱 서 있었는데, 지나가게 비켜 달라는 그녀의 얼굴을 보니 뾰로통한 낯빛이었다—하인들의 우두머리인 나로서는, 원칙상 그런 낯빛을 캐묻지 않고 그냥 지나쳐 보내는 법이 결코 없다."]], [["“What are you turning your back on your dinner for?”", "\"저녁밥을 마다하고 등을 돌리다니 무슨 일이냐?\" 나는 물었다."], ["I asked. “What’s wrong now, Nancy?”", "\"지금 무엇이 못마땅한 게냐, 낸시?\""]], [["Nancy tried to push by, without answering; upon which I rose up, and took her by the ear.", "낸시는 대답도 없이 나를 밀치고 지나가려 했다. 그래서 나는 몸을 일으켜 그녀의 귀를 붙잡았다."], ["She is a nice plump young lass, and it is customary with me to adopt that manner of showing that I personally approve of a girl.", "그녀는 통통하니 잘생긴 젊은 처녀인데, 나는 한 계집아이가 개인적으로 마음에 든다는 표시로 그런 식을 취하는 것이 예사다."]], [["“What’s wrong now?”", "\"지금 무엇이 못마땅하냐고?\""], ["I said once more.", "나는 다시 한번 말했다."]], [["“Rosanna’s late again for dinner,”", "\"로재나가 또 저녁 시간에 늦었어요.\""], ["says Nancy.", "낸시가 말한다."], ["“And I’m sent to fetch her in.", "\"그래서 제가 그 애를 데리러 가라고 시킴을 당했지 뭐예요."], ["All the hard work falls on my shoulders in this house.", "이 집에선 힘든 일이 죄다 제 어깨에 떨어진다니까요."], ["Let me alone, Mr. Betteredge!”", "놓아주세요, 베터리지 씨!\""]], [["The person here mentioned as Rosanna was our second housemaid.", "여기서 로재나라고 일컬은 사람은 우리 집 둘째 하녀였다."], ["Having a kind of pity for our second housemaid (why, you shall presently know), and seeing in Nancy’s face, that she would fetch her fellow-servant in with more hard words than might be needful under the circumstances, it struck me that I had nothing particular to do, and that I might as well fetch Rosanna myself; giving her a hint to be punctual in future, which I knew she would take kindly from me.", "우리 둘째 하녀가 나는 어쩐지 가엾게 여겨지던 터라 (그 까닭은 이제 곧 아시게 될 것이다), 그리고 낸시의 낯빛을 보아하니 형편상 필요한 것보다 더 모진 말을 퍼부으며 제 동료를 데려올 성싶기에, 문득 나는 딱히 할 일도 없겠다 로재나를 내가 손수 데려오는 편이 낫겠다는 생각이 들었다. 앞으로는 시간을 지키라고 넌지시 일러 줄 참이었는데, 그 말이 다른 사람 아닌 나한테서 나온다면 그 애가 고이 받아들이리라는 것을 나는 알고 있었다."]], [["“Where is Rosanna?”", "\"로재나는 어디 있느냐?\""], ["I inquired.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“At the sands, of course!”", "\"그야 물론 모래펄이죠!\""], ["says Nancy, with a toss of her head.", "낸시가 고개를 홱 젖히며 말한다."], ["“She had another of her fainting fits this morning, and she asked to go out and get a breath of fresh air.", "\"오늘 아침에도 그 기절 발작을 또 일으키더니, 바깥에 나가 바람 좀 쐬고 오게 해 달라지 뭐예요."], ["I have no patience with her!”", "저는 정말이지 그 애한테 진절머리가 나요!\""]], [["“Go back to your dinner, my girl,”", "\"저녁밥이나 먹으러 돌아가거라, 얘야.\""], ["I said.", "나는 말했다."], ["“I have patience with her, and I’ll fetch her in.”", "\"나는 그 애한테 진절머리가 나지 않으니, 내가 데려오마.\""]], [["Nancy (who has a fine appetite) looked pleased.", "낸시는 (식성이 좋은 아이라) 흐뭇한 낯빛이 되었다."], ["When she looks pleased, she looks nice.", "그 애가 흐뭇한 낯빛이 되면, 예뻐 보인다."], ["When she looks nice, I chuck her under the chin.", "그 애가 예뻐 보이면, 나는 그 턱을 살짝 쓸어 준다."], ["It isn’t immorality—it’s only habit.", "이건 부도덕이 아니라—그저 버릇일 뿐이다."]], [["Well, I took my stick, and set off for the sands.", "자, 나는 지팡이를 챙겨 들고 모래펄로 길을 나섰다."]], [["No!", "아니!"], ["it won’t do to set off yet.", "아직 나서서는 안 되겠다."], ["I am sorry again to detain you; but you really must hear the story of the sands, and the story of Rosanna—for this reason, that the matter of the Diamond touches them both nearly.", "또 여러분을 붙들어 두어 미안하다. 하지만 여러분은 정말이지 이 모래펄 이야기와 로재나 이야기를 들으셔야 한다—그 까닭인즉, 다이아몬드 사건이 그 둘 다와 밀접히 얽혀 있기 때문이다."], ["How hard I try to get on with my statement without stopping by the way, and how badly I succeed!", "곁길로 새지 않고 내 이야기를 밀고 나가려고 내가 얼마나 애를 쓰는지, 그리고 얼마나 번번이 실패하는지!"], ["But, there!—Persons and Things do turn up so vexatiously in this life, and will in a manner insist on being noticed.", "그러나 어쩌겠는가!—사람이며 사물이며 이 세상살이에서 어찌나 성가시게 불쑥불쑥 튀어나와, 이를테면 저를 좀 봐 달라고 기어이 우겨 대는 것을."], ["Let us take it easy, and let us take it short; we shall be in the thick of the mystery soon, I promise you!", "우리 느긋이, 그리고 짤막하게 넘어가기로 하자. 곧 수수께끼 한복판에 들어서게 될 테니, 내가 약속하리다!"]], [["Rosanna (to put the Person before the Thing, which is but common politeness) was the only new servant in our house.", "로재나로 말할 것 같으면 (사물보다 사람을 앞세우는 것이 그저 인지상정이니), 우리 집의 유일한 새 하인이었다."], ["About four months before the time I am writing of, my lady had been in London, and had gone over a Reformatory, intended to save forlorn women from drifting back into bad ways, after they had got released from prison.", "내가 지금 적고 있는 시점으로부터 넉 달쯤 전에, 마님이 런던에 가셨다가 한 갱생원을 둘러보셨다. 그곳은 감옥에서 풀려난 가엾은 여자들이 다시 나쁜 길로 떠내려가지 않도록 구하려는 뜻으로 세워진 곳이었다."], ["The matron, seeing my lady took an interest in the place, pointed out a girl to her, named Rosanna Spearman, and told her a most miserable story, which I haven’t the heart to repeat here; for I don’t like to be made wretched without any use, and no more do you.", "원장은 마님이 그곳에 관심을 두시는 것을 보고, 로재나 스피어먼이라는 이름의 한 처녀를 가리켜 보이며, 참으로 애처로운 사연을 들려주었다. 그 사연을 나는 여기서 되풀이할 엄두가 나지 않는다."], ["The upshot of it was, that Rosanna Spearman had been a thief, and not being of the sort that get up Companies in the City, and rob from thousands, instead of only robbing from one, the law laid hold of her, and the prison and the reformatory followed the lead of the law.", "아무 소용도 없이 마음이 비참해지는 것을 나는 좋아하지 않으며, 여러분도 그러시리라. 그 사연의 요지인즉, 로재나 스피어먼이 도둑이었다는 것이다."], ["The matron’s opinion of Rosanna was (in spite of what she had done) that the girl was one in a thousand, and that she only wanted a chance to prove herself worthy of any Christian woman’s interest in her.", "다만 그녀는, 도시에서 회사를 차려 한 사람이 아니라 수천 명에게서 훔쳐 내는 그런 부류가 못 되었던 탓에, 법에 붙들렸고, 감옥과 갱생원이 그 법의 뒤를 따랐다. 로재나에 대한 원장의 소견은 (그녀가 저지른 일에도 불구하고) 이 처녀가 천에 하나 나올까 말까 한 아이요, 그저 어떤 기독교인 여성의 관심을 받을 만한 사람임을 스스로 증명해 보일 기회 하나가 필요할 뿐이라는 것이었다."], ["My lady (being a Christian woman, if ever there was one yet) said to the matron, upon that, “Rosanna Spearman shall have her chance, in my service.”", "마님은 (세상에 참된 기독교인 여성이 있다면 바로 그분이셨으니) 그 말에, 원장에게 이르셨다. \"로재나 스피어먼에게 내 밑에서 그 기회를 주겠어요.\""], ["In a week afterwards, Rosanna Spearman entered this establishment as our second housemaid.", "그로부터 일주일 뒤, 로재나 스피어먼은 우리 둘째 하녀로 이 집에 들어왔다."]], [["Not a soul was told the girl’s story, excepting Miss Rachel and me.", "레이철 아가씨와 나 말고는, 이 처녀의 사연을 아는 이가 한 사람도 없었다."], ["My lady, doing me the honour to consult me about most things, consulted me about Rosanna.", "마님은 대개의 일을 내게 물어 의논하시는 영예를 베풀어 주셨는데, 로재나에 관해서도 내게 물으셨다."], ["Having fallen a good deal latterly into the late Sir John’s way of always agreeing with my lady, I agreed with her heartily about Rosanna Spearman.", "요즈음 들어 나는, 지금은 고인이 되신 존 경의, 매사에 마님 뜻을 따르는 방식에 제법 물들어 있던 터라, 로재나 스피어먼에 관해서도 마님 뜻에 진심으로 동의했다."]], [["A fairer chance no girl could have had than was given to this poor girl of ours.", "이 가엾은 우리 처녀에게 주어진 것보다 더 공평한 기회를 누린 처녀는 없었을 것이다."], ["None of the servants could cast her past life in her teeth, for none of the servants knew what it had been.", "하인들 가운데 아무도 그녀의 지난 삶을 들먹여 면박할 수 없었으니, 그것이 어떠했는지 아는 하인이 하나도 없었기 때문이다."], ["She had her wages and her privileges, like the rest of them; and every now and then a friendly word from my lady, in private, to encourage her.", "그녀는 다른 이들과 마찬가지로 제 품삯과 제 특혜를 받았고, 이따금 마님이 남몰래 건네주시는 다정한 격려의 말도 받았다."], ["In return, she showed herself, I am bound to say, well worthy of the kind treatment bestowed upon her.", "그 답례로 그녀는, 나로서는 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없는데, 저에게 베풀어진 그 친절한 대접을 받을 만한 사람임을 넉넉히 보여 주었다."], ["Though far from strong, and troubled occasionally with those fainting-fits already mentioned, she went about her work modestly and uncomplainingly, doing it carefully, and doing it well.", "몸은 결코 튼튼하지 못하여 이따금 앞서 말한 그 기절 발작에 시달리면서도, 그녀는 제 일을 얌전하고 군말 없이, 정성껏, 그리고 훌륭하게 해냈다."], ["But, somehow, she failed to make friends among the other women servants, excepting my daughter Penelope, who was always kind to Rosanna, though never intimate with her.", "그러나 어쩐 일인지, 그녀는 다른 여자 하인들 사이에서는 좀처럼 벗을 사귀지 못했다. 다만 내 딸 페넬로피만은 예외였으니, 그 애는 로재나와 각별히 친하게 지내지는 않았어도 늘 그녀에게 다정했다."]], [["I hardly know what the girl did to offend them.", "그 처녀가 대체 무엇으로 그들의 비위를 거슬렀는지 나는 도무지 모르겠다."], ["There was certainly no beauty about her to make the others envious; she was the plainest woman in the house, with the additional misfortune of having one shoulder bigger than the other.", "남들의 시샘을 살 만한 미모가 그녀에게 있었던 것도 아니다. 그녀는 이 집에서 가장 못생긴 여자였고, 게다가 한쪽 어깨가 다른 쪽보다 크다는 불운까지 겹쳐 있었다."], ["What the servants chiefly resented, I think, was her silent tongue and her solitary ways.", "하인들이 무엇보다 못마땅해한 것은, 내 생각에, 그녀의 과묵한 혀와 외톨이 같은 처신이었다."], ["She read or worked in leisure hours when the rest gossiped.", "그녀는 남들이 수다를 떠는 틈틈이 책을 읽거나 일을 했다."], ["And when it came to her turn to go out, nine times out of ten she quietly put on her bonnet, and had her turn by herself.", "그리고 제 차례가 되어 바깥나들이를 할 때면, 열에 아홉은 조용히 챙 모자를 눌러쓰고 혼자서 그 차례를 보냈다."], ["She never quarrelled, she never took offence; she only kept a certain distance, obstinately and civilly, between the rest of them and herself.", "그녀는 결코 다투는 법이 없었고, 결코 성을 내는 법이 없었다. 다만 남들과 저 사이에, 어떤 일정한 거리를, 고집스럽게, 그러나 예의 바르게 두었을 뿐이다."], ["Add to this that, plain as she was, there was just a dash of something that wasn’t like a housemaid, and that was like a lady, about her.", "여기에다, 그토록 못생겼으면서도 그녀에게는 하녀 같지 않고 오히려 귀부인 같은 무언가가 살짝 감돌았다는 점까지 보태자."], ["It might have been in her voice, or it might have been in her face.", "그것은 그녀의 목소리에 있었는지도 모르고, 그녀의 얼굴에 있었는지도 모른다."], ["All I can say is, that the other women pounced on it like lightning the first day she came into the house, and said (which was most unjust) that Rosanna Spearman gave herself airs.", "내가 말할 수 있는 것은 다만, 그녀가 이 집에 들어온 첫날부터 다른 여자들이 번갯불처럼 그 점을 낚아채더니, (참으로 부당하게도) 로재나 스피어먼이 잘난 체를 한다고 수군거렸다는 것뿐이다."]], [["Having now told the story of Rosanna, I have only to notice one of the many queer ways of this strange girl to get on next to the story of the sands.", "이제 로재나 이야기를 했으니, 이 별난 처녀의 여러 괴상한 버릇 가운데 하나만 짚어 두고서 다음으로 모래펄 이야기로 넘어가면 되겠다."]], [["Our house is high up on the Yorkshire coast, and close by the sea.", "우리 집은 요크셔 해안 높다란 곳, 바다에 바싹 붙어 있다."], ["We have got beautiful walks all round us, in every direction but one.", "우리 집 사방으로는, 딱 한 방향만 빼고, 어느 쪽으로든 아름다운 산책길이 나 있다."], ["That one I acknowledge to be a horrid walk.", "그 한 방향만은, 나도 인정하거니와, 지긋지긋한 길이다."], ["It leads, for a quarter of a mile, through a melancholy plantation of firs, and brings you out between low cliffs on the loneliest and ugliest little bay on all our coast.", "그 길은 사분의 일 마일가량 음산한 전나무 조림지를 뚫고 나아가다가, 나지막한 벼랑 사이로 여러분을 데려내어, 우리 해안을 통틀어 가장 외지고 가장 볼썽사나운 조그만 만(灣)에 이르게 한다."]], [["The sandhills here run down to the sea, and end in two spits of rock jutting out opposite each other, till you lose sight of them in the water.", "여기 모래언덕들은 바다까지 흘러내리다가, 서로 마주 보며 삐죽 튀어나온 두 갈래 바위 곶으로 끝나는데, 그 곶들은 물속에서 시야를 벗어날 때까지 뻗어 나간다."], ["One is called the North Spit, and one the South.", "하나는 북쪽 곶, 하나는 남쪽 곶이라 불린다."], ["Between the two, shifting backwards and forwards at certain seasons of the year, lies the most horrible quicksand on the shores of Yorkshire.", "그 둘 사이에, 해마다 철에 따라 앞뒤로 자리를 옮겨 가며, 요크셔 해안을 통틀어 가장 무시무시한 유사(流沙)가 자리하고 있다."], ["At the turn of the tide, something goes on in the unknown deeps below, which sets the whole face of the quicksand shivering and trembling in a manner most remarkable to see, and which has given to it, among the people in our parts, the name of the Shivering Sand.", "물때가 바뀔 무렵이면, 저 아래 알 수 없는 깊은 곳에서 무언가가 일어나, 유사의 온 얼굴을 보기에도 참으로 놀랍도록 부르르 떨고 부들부들 흔들리게 만든다. 그 때문에 우리 고장 사람들 사이에서 그곳은 떠는 모래펄이라는 이름을 얻었다."], ["A great bank, half a mile out, nigh the mouth of the bay, breaks the force of the main ocean coming in from the offing.", "만 어귀 가까이, 반 마일쯤 나간 곳에 커다란 모래톱이 하나 있어, 먼바다에서 밀려 들어오는 큰 물결의 힘을 꺾어 준다."], ["Winter and summer, when the tide flows over the quicksand, the sea seems to leave the waves behind it on the bank, and rolls its waters in smoothly with a heave, and covers the sand in silence.", "겨울이나 여름이나, 밀물이 그 유사 위로 흘러들 때면, 바다는 제 물결들을 그 모래톱에 남겨 둔 채, 물을 넘실넘실 매끄럽게 밀어 넣어, 소리 없이 모래를 덮어 버리는 듯하다."], ["A lonesome and a horrid retreat, I can tell you!", "외롭고도 지긋지긋한 피난처라고 내 장담하리다!"], ["No boat ever ventures into this bay.", "이 만에는 배 한 척 감히 들어오지 않는다."], ["No children from our fishing-village, called Cobb’s Hole, ever come here to play.", "코브스 홀이라 불리는 우리 어촌의 아이들도 여기 놀러 오는 법이 없다."], ["The very birds of the air, as it seems to me, give the Shivering Sand a wide berth.", "내가 보기엔 하늘을 나는 새들조차 이 떠는 모래펄을 멀찍이 에돌아 간다."], ["That a young woman, with dozens of nice walks to choose from, and company to go with her, if she only said “Come!”, should prefer this place, and should sit and work or read in it, all alone, when it’s her turn out, I grant you, passes belief.", "골라 걸을 근사한 산책길이 수십 군데요, \"같이 가!\" 한마디만 하면 함께 가 줄 동무도 있는 젊은 여자가, 하필 이런 곳을 좋아하여, 제 나들이 차례가 오면 여기 홀로 앉아 일을 하거나 책을 읽는다는 것은—나도 인정하거니와, 도무지 믿기지 않는 노릇이다."], ["It’s true, nevertheless, account for it as you may, that this was Rosanna Spearman’s favourite walk, except when she went once or twice to Cobb’s Hole, to see the only friend she had in our neighbourhood, of whom more anon.", "그러나 여러분이 어떻게 갖다 붙이든 간에, 이것이 로재나 스피어먼이 가장 좋아하는 산책길이었다는 것만은 엄연한 사실이다. 다만 그녀가 한두 번 코브스 홀에 간 적은 있었으니, 우리 동네에서 그녀에게 있는 단 하나뿐인 벗을 만나러 간 것이었는데, 그에 관해서는 이따가 더 이야기하겠다."], ["It’s also true that I was now setting out for this same place, to fetch the girl in to dinner, which brings us round happily to our former point, and starts us fair again on our way to the sands.", "그리고 내가 지금 그 처녀를 저녁 먹으러 데려오려고 바로 그 같은 곳으로 나서고 있었다는 것도 엄연한 사실이니, 이로써 우리는 다행히 아까 그 대목으로 되돌아와, 다시 모래펄로 가는 길에 산뜻이 오르게 되었다."]], [["I saw no sign of the girl in the plantation.", "조림지에서는 그 처녀의 그림자도 보이지 않았다."], ["When I got out, through the sandhills, on to the beach, there she was, in her little straw bonnet, and her plain grey cloak that she always wore to hide her deformed shoulder as much as might be—there she was, all alone, looking out on the quicksand and the sea.", "모래언덕을 지나 바닷가로 나서니, 거기 그녀가 있었다. 조그만 밀짚 챙 모자를 쓰고, 기형인 어깨를 되도록 감추려고 늘 걸치던 그 수수한 잿빛 망토를 두른 채—거기 그녀가, 오롯이 홀로 서서, 유사와 바다를 내다보고 있었다."]], [["She started when I came up with her, and turned her head away from me.", "내가 다가서자 그녀는 흠칫 놀라, 나에게서 고개를 돌려 버렸다."], ["Not looking me in the face being another of the proceedings, which, as head of the servants, I never allow, on principle, to pass without inquiry—I turned her round my way, and saw that she was crying.", "사람의 얼굴을 마주 보지 않는 것 역시, 하인들의 우두머리인 내가 원칙상 캐묻지 않고 그냥 지나쳐 보내는 법이 결코 없는 처사이기에—나는 그녀를 내 쪽으로 돌려세웠고, 그녀가 울고 있음을 알았다."], ["My bandanna handkerchief—one of six beauties given to me by my lady—was handy in my pocket.", "마님이 내게 주신 여섯 장의 고운 손수건 가운데 하나인 반다나 손수건이 마침 내 주머니에 있었다."], ["I took it out, and I said to Rosanna, “Come and sit down, my dear, on the slope of the beach along with me.", "나는 그것을 꺼내며 로재나에게 말했다. \"이리 와서 앉으렴, 얘야, 나와 함께 여기 비탈진 바닷가에."], ["I’ll dry your eyes for you first, and then I’ll make so bold as to ask what you have been crying about.”", "우선 내가 네 눈물부터 닦아 주고, 그러고 나서 무엇 때문에 울고 있었는지 주제넘게 한번 물어보마.\""]], [["When you come to my age, you will find sitting down on the slope of a beach a much longer job than you think it now.", "여러분도 내 나이가 되어 보면, 비탈진 바닷가에 앉는 일이 지금 생각하시는 것보다 훨씬 오래 걸리는 일임을 아시게 되리라."], ["By the time I was settled, Rosanna had dried her own eyes with a very inferior handkerchief to mine—cheap cambric.", "내가 자리를 다 잡았을 무렵, 로재나는 내 것보다 한참 못한 손수건—값싼 무명—으로 제 눈물을 다 훔친 뒤였다."], ["She looked very quiet, and very wretched; but she sat down by me like a good girl, when I told her.", "그녀는 몹시 조용하고 몹시 가엾어 보였다. 그러나 내가 말하니 착한 아이처럼 내 곁에 앉았다."], ["When you want to comfort a woman by the shortest way, take her on your knee.", "여자를 가장 빠른 길로 달래고 싶거든, 무릎 위에 앉히라."], ["I thought of this golden rule.", "나는 이 금과옥조를 떠올렸다."], ["But there!", "그러나 이런!"], ["Rosanna wasn’t Nancy, and that’s the truth of it!", "로재나는 낸시가 아니었으니, 그것이 사실이렷다!"]], [["“Now, tell me, my dear,”", "\"자, 말해 보렴, 얘야.\""], ["I said, “what are you crying about?”", "나는 말했다. \"무엇 때문에 울고 있느냐?\""]], [["“About the years that are gone, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"흘러가 버린 세월 때문이에요, 베터리지 씨.\""], ["says Rosanna quietly.", "로재나가 조용히 말한다."], ["“My past life still comes back to me sometimes.”", "\"제 지난 삶이 아직도 이따금 제게 되돌아와요.\""]], [["“Come, come, my girl,”", "\"이런, 이런, 얘야.\""], ["I said, “your past life is all sponged out.", "나는 말했다. \"네 지난 삶은 죄다 말끔히 씻겨 나갔단다."], ["Why can’t you forget it?”", "어째서 그걸 잊지 못하는 게냐?\""]], [["She took me by one of the lappets of my coat.", "그녀는 내 외투 자락 한쪽을 붙잡았다."], ["I am a slovenly old man, and a good deal of my meat and drink gets splashed about on my clothes.", "나는 칠칠치 못한 늙은이라, 먹고 마시는 것이 옷에 곧잘 튄다."], ["Sometimes one of the women, and sometimes another, cleans me of my grease.", "이 여자 저 여자가 번갈아 내 몸의 기름때를 닦아 준다."], ["The day before, Rosanna had taken out a spot for me on the lappet of my coat, with a new composition, warranted to remove anything.", "바로 전날, 로재나는 무엇이든 지워 준다고 장담하는 새 약제로 내 외투 자락의 얼룩 하나를 빼 주었다."], ["The grease was gone, but there was a little dull place left on the nap of the cloth where the grease had been.", "기름때는 사라졌으나, 기름이 묻었던 자리에 천 보풀이 살짝 칙칙해진 데가 남아 있었다."], ["The girl pointed to that place, and shook her head.", "그 처녀는 그 자리를 가리키며 고개를 저었다."]], [["“The stain is taken off,”", "\"얼룩은 빠졌어요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“But the place shows, Mr. Betteredge—the place shows!”", "\"하지만 자국은 남아요, 베터리지 씨—자국은 남는다고요!\""]], [["A remark which takes a man unawares by means of his own coat is not an easy remark to answer.", "제 외투를 빌미로 삼아 사람의 허를 찌르는 그런 말에는 대꾸하기가 쉽지 않다."], ["Something in the girl herself, too, made me particularly sorry for her just then.", "게다가 그 처녀 자신의 무언가가, 바로 그 순간 그녀를 유난히 가엾게 여기게 만들었다."], ["She had nice brown eyes, plain as she was in other ways—and she looked at me with a sort of respect for my happy old age and my good character, as things for ever out of her own reach, which made my heart heavy for our second housemaid.", "다른 점으로는 그토록 못생겼어도 그녀에게는 고운 갈색 눈이 있었는데—그 눈으로 그녀는, 내 행복한 노년과 내 훌륭한 인품을, 마치 저 자신에게는 영영 닿을 수 없는 것을 바라보듯, 어떤 존경 어린 눈길로 나를 바라보았으니, 그것이 우리 둘째 하녀를 생각하는 내 마음을 무겁게 했다."], ["Not feeling myself able to comfort her, there was only one other thing to do.", "내가 스스로 그녀를 달랠 능력이 없다고 느껴지니, 남은 것은 오직 한 가지뿐이었다."], ["That thing was—to take her in to dinner.", "그것은—그녀를 데리고 저녁을 먹으러 들어가는 일이었다."]], [["“Help me up,” I said.", "\"나 좀 일으켜다오.\""], ["“You’re late for dinner, Rosanna—and I have come to fetch you in.”", "나는 말했다. \"너 저녁 시간에 늦었다, 로재나—그래서 내가 데리러 온 게야.\""]], [["“You, Mr. Betteredge!”", "\"당신이요, 베터리지 씨!\""], ["says she.", "그녀가 말한다."]], [["“They told Nancy to fetch you,”", "\"낸시더러 너를 데려오라 했더랬다.\""], ["I said.", "나는 말했다."], ["“But I thought you might like your scolding better, my dear, if it came from me.”", "\"하지만 어차피 꾸중을 들을 바에야, 얘야, 그게 내 입에서 나오는 편이 네게 나으리라 싶었지.\""]], [["Instead of helping me up, the poor thing stole her hand into mine, and gave it a little squeeze.", "가엾은 것은 나를 일으켜 주기는커녕, 제 손을 슬며시 내 손안에 밀어 넣고 살짝 쥐어 왔다."], ["She tried hard to keep from crying again, and succeeded—for which I respected her.", "그녀는 다시 울지 않으려고 무진 애를 썼고, 마침내 참아 냈으니—나는 그 점을 갸륵히 여겼다."], ["“You’re very kind, Mr.", "\"참 다정하세요, 베터리지 씨.\""], ["Betteredge,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “I don’t want any dinner today—let me bide a little longer here.”", "\"저는 오늘 저녁은 먹고 싶지 않아요—여기 조금만 더 있게 해 주세요.\""]], [["“What makes you like to be here?” I asked.", "\"무엇이 그리 이곳을 좋아하게 하느냐?\""], ["“What is it that brings you everlastingly to this miserable place?”", "나는 물었다. \"대체 무엇이 너를 이 처량한 곳으로 노상 끌어들이는 게냐?\""]], [["“Something draws me to it,”", "\"무언가가 저를 이곳으로 끌어당겨요.\""], ["says the girl, making images with her finger in the sand.", "그 처녀가 손가락으로 모래에 그림을 그리며 말한다."], ["“I try to keep away from it, and I can’t.", "\"여기서 떨어져 있으려고 애를 써도, 그럴 수가 없어요."], ["Sometimes,”", "이따금,\""], ["says she in a low voice, as if she was frightened at her own fancy, “sometimes, Mr.", "그녀가, 마치 제 공상에 저 스스로 겁이라도 먹은 듯, 나직한 목소리로 말한다."], ["Betteredge, I think that my grave is waiting for me here.”", "\"이따금, 베터리지 씨, 저는 제 무덤이 여기서 저를 기다리고 있는 것만 같아요.\""]], [["“There’s roast mutton and suet pudding waiting for you!”", "\"널 기다리는 건 양고기 구이와 쇠기름 푸딩이니라!\" 하고 나는 말한다."], ["says I. “Go in to dinner directly.", "\"당장 들어가 저녁을 먹어라."], ["This is what comes, Rosanna, of thinking on an empty stomach!”", "로재나야, 빈속에 생각에 잠기면 바로 이 꼴이 나는 게다!\""], ["I spoke severely, being naturally indignant (at my time of life) to hear a young woman of five-and-twenty talking about her latter end!", "나는 준엄하게 말했으니, (내 나잇살에) 스물다섯 젊은 여자가 제 마지막을 입에 올리는 소리를 들으매 자연히 부아가 치밀었던 것이다!"]], [["She didn’t seem to hear me: she put her hand on my shoulder, and kept me where I was, sitting by her side.", "그녀는 내 말이 들리지도 않는 듯했다. 그녀는 내 어깨에 손을 얹어, 나를 제 곁에 앉은 그대로 붙들어 두었다."]], [["“I think the place has laid a spell on me,”", "\"이곳이 제게 마법을 걸어 놓은 것만 같아요.\""], ["she said.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“I dream of it night after night; I think of it when I sit stitching at my work.", "\"밤이면 밤마다 이곳 꿈을 꾸어요. 앉아서 바느질을 할 때도 이곳을 생각해요."], ["You know I am grateful, Mr. Betteredge—you know I try to deserve your kindness, and my lady’s confidence in me.", "제가 고마워하는 줄은 아시잖아요, 베터리지 씨—제가 당신의 친절과, 저를 믿어 주시는 마님의 신임에 값하려 애쓰는 줄은 아시잖아요."], ["But I wonder sometimes whether the life here is too quiet and too good for such a woman as I am, after all I have gone through, Mr. Betteredge—after all I have gone through.", "하지만 이따금 저는, 제가 겪어 온 그 모든 일을 겪고 난 저 같은 여자한테는 여기 삶이 너무 조용하고 너무 과분한 게 아닐까 싶어요, 베터리지 씨—제가 겪어 온 그 모든 일을 겪고 나서요."], ["It’s more lonely to me to be among the other servants, knowing I am not what they are, than it is to be here.", "제가 저들과 같은 사람이 아님을 알면서 다른 하인들 틈에 끼어 있는 것이, 제게는 이곳에 있는 것보다 더 외로워요."], ["My lady doesn’t know, the matron at the reformatory doesn’t know, what a dreadful reproach honest people are in themselves to a woman like me.", "마님도 모르시고, 갱생원 원장님도 모르세요, 정직한 사람들이 저 같은 여자한테는 그저 그 존재만으로도 얼마나 무서운 꾸짖음이 되는지를요."], ["Don’t scold me, there’s a dear good man.", "저를 꾸짖지 마세요, 다정하고 착한 어른이시니."], ["I do my work, don’t I?", "저는 제 할 일은 하잖아요, 그렇죠?"], ["Please not to tell my lady I am discontented—I am not.", "부디 제가 불만스러워한다고 마님께 이르지 마세요—저는 불만스럽지 않아요."], ["My mind’s unquiet, sometimes, that’s all.”", "그저 이따금 마음이 뒤숭숭할 뿐이에요.\""], ["She snatched her hand off my shoulder, and suddenly pointed down to the quicksand.", "그녀는 내 어깨에서 제 손을 홱 떼더니, 느닷없이 유사를 손가락으로 가리켰다."], ["“Look!”", "\"보세요!\""], ["she said “Isn’t it wonderful?", "그녀가 말했다. \"놀랍지 않아요?"], ["isn’t it terrible?", "무섭지 않아요?"], ["I have seen it dozens of times, and it’s always as new to me as if I had never seen it before!”", "벌써 수십 번은 봤는데도, 마치 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 것처럼 볼 때마다 늘 새로워요!\""]], [["I looked where she pointed.", "나는 그녀가 가리키는 곳을 보았다."], ["The tide was on the turn, and the horrid sand began to shiver.", "물때가 바뀌는 참이라, 그 지긋지긋한 모래가 부르르 떨기 시작했다."], ["The broad brown face of it heaved slowly, and then dimpled and quivered all over.", "그 널따란 갈색 얼굴이 서서히 부풀어 오르더니, 이내 온통 옴폭옴폭 패며 부들부들 떨었다."], ["“Do you know what it looks like to me?”", "\"저것이 제 눈엔 어떻게 보이는지 아세요?\""], ["says Rosanna, catching me by the shoulder again.", "로재나가 다시 내 어깨를 붙잡으며 말한다."], ["“It looks as if it had hundreds of suffocating people under it—all struggling to get to the surface, and all sinking lower and lower in the dreadful deeps!", "\"마치 그 밑에 숨 막혀 죽어 가는 사람이 수백 명이나 있어서—죄다 수면 위로 올라오려고 발버둥 치면서, 죄다 저 무서운 심연 속으로 점점 더 깊이 가라앉는 것만 같아요!"], ["Throw a stone in, Mr. Betteredge!", "돌을 하나 던져 보세요, 베터리지 씨!"], ["Throw a stone in, and let’s see the sand suck it down!”", "돌을 하나 던져서, 모래가 그걸 쭉 빨아들이는 걸 봐요!\""]], [["Here was unwholesome talk!", "이 무슨 해괴한 소리인가!"], ["Here was an empty stomach feeding on an unquiet mind!", "빈속이 뒤숭숭한 마음을 파먹고 있는 꼴이라니!"], ["My answer—a pretty sharp one, in the poor girl’s own interests, I promise you!—was at my tongue’s end, when it was snapped short off on a sudden by a voice among the sandhills shouting for me by my name.", "내 대답이—장담하건대, 이 가엾은 처녀 자신을 위한 제법 매서운 대답이!—혀끝에서 막 나오려는 참에, 모래언덕 사이에서 내 이름을 부르며 나를 찾는 목소리에 그 대답이 별안간 뚝 끊겨 버렸다."], ["“Betteredge!”", "\"베터리지!\""], ["cries the voice, “where are you?”", "목소리가 외친다. \"어디 있나?\""], ["“Here!”", "\"여기요!\""], ["I shouted out in return, without a notion in my mind of who it was.", "나도 마주 외쳤으나, 그게 누구인지는 짐작조차 가지 않았다."], ["Rosanna started to her feet, and stood looking towards the voice.", "로재나가 벌떡 일어서더니, 그 목소리 쪽을 바라보고 섰다."], ["I was just thinking of getting on my own legs next, when I was staggered by a sudden change in the girl’s face.", "나도 이번엔 내 두 다리로 일어서 볼까 하던 참인데, 그 처녀의 얼굴에 별안간 일어난 변화에 나는 그만 얼떨떨해졌다."]], [["Her complexion turned of a beautiful red, which I had never seen in it before; she brightened all over with a kind of speechless and breathless surprise.", "그녀의 낯빛이, 여태 한 번도 본 적 없는 아름다운 붉은빛으로 물들었다. 그녀는 말도 숨도 못 이룰 듯한 놀라움에 사로잡혀 온몸이 환히 밝아졌다."], ["“Who is it?”", "\"누구지?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."], ["Rosanna gave me back my own question.", "로재나는 내 물음을 도로 내게 되돌려주었다."], ["“Oh!", "\"아!"], ["who is it?”", "누구지?\""], ["she said softly, more to herself than to me.", "그녀는, 내게라기보다 저 자신에게, 나직이 말했다."], ["I twisted round on the sand and looked behind me.", "나는 모래 위에서 몸을 비틀어 뒤를 돌아보았다."], ["There, coming out on us from among the hills, was a bright-eyed young gentleman, dressed in a beautiful fawn-coloured suit, with gloves and hat to match, with a rose in his button-hole, and a smile on his face that might have set the Shivering Sand itself smiling at him in return.", "거기, 언덕 사이에서 우리에게로 나오고 있는 것은, 눈빛이 초롱초롱한 젊은 신사였으니, 아름다운 담황색 양복에 그에 어울리는 장갑과 모자를 갖추고, 단춧구멍에는 장미 한 송이를 꽂고, 얼굴에는 저 떠는 모래펄마저도 마주 웃게 만들 법한 미소를 띠고 있었다."], ["Before I could get on my legs, he plumped down on the sand by the side of me, put his arm round my neck, foreign fashion, and gave me a hug that fairly squeezed the breath out of my body.", "내가 미처 일어서기도 전에, 그는 내 곁 모래 위로 털썩 주저앉더니, 외국식으로 제 팔을 내 목에 두르고, 내 몸에서 숨이 다 빠져나갈 만큼 나를 와락 끌어안았다. \"그리운 베터리지 영감!\""], ["“Dear old Betteredge!”", "그가 말한다."], ["says he. “I owe you seven-and-sixpence.", "\"내가 자네한테 칠 실링 육 펜스 빚이 있지."], ["Now do you know who I am?”", "자, 내가 누군지 알겠나?\""]], [["Lord bless us and save us!", "우리를 굽어살피사 지켜 주소서!"], ["Here—four good hours before we expected him—was Mr. Franklin Blake!", "여기—우리가 그분을 기다리던 때보다 실히 네 시간이나 앞서—프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 와 있었던 것이다!"]], [["Before I could say a word, I saw Mr. Franklin, a little surprised to all appearance, look up from me to Rosanna.", "내가 미처 한마디 하기도 전에, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이, 겉보기에 조금 놀란 낯으로, 나에게서 로재나에게로 눈길을 들어 올리는 것을 보았다."], ["Following his lead, I looked at the girl too.", "그분을 따라, 나도 그 처녀를 보았다."], ["She was blushing of a deeper red than ever, seemingly at having caught Mr.", "그녀는, 프랭클린 도련님과 눈이 마주친 탓인 듯, 그 어느 때보다 더 짙은 붉은빛으로 얼굴을 붉히더니, 몸을 돌려 홀연 우리 곁을 떠나갔다."], ["Franklin’s eye; and she turned and left us suddenly, in a confusion quite unaccountable to my mind, without either making her curtsey to the gentleman or saying a word to me.", "그 신사에게 절을 올리지도, 내게 한마디 건네지도 않은 채, 내 머리로는 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 당황함에 휩싸여서. 여느 때의 그녀와는 영 딴판이었다."], ["Very unlike her usual self: a civiller and better-behaved servant, in general, you never met with.", "대체로 그녀보다 더 예의 바르고 몸가짐 반듯한 하인은 여러분도 좀처럼 만나 보지 못하셨을 것이다."]], [["“That’s an odd girl,”", "\"별난 아가씨로군.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“I wonder what she sees in me to surprise her?”", "\"저 사람이 내게서 무얼 보았기에 저리 놀라는 걸까?\""]], [["“I suppose, sir,”", "\"제 짐작으로는, 도련님,\""], ["I answered, drolling on our young gentleman’s Continental education, “it’s the varnish from foreign parts.”", "하고 나는 우리 젊은 신사의 대륙풍 교육을 살짝 놀리며 대답했다. \"외국물이 든 그 광택이 아닐는지요.\""]], [["I set down here Mr. Franklin’s careless question, and my foolish answer, as a consolation and encouragement to all stupid people—it being, as I have remarked, a great satisfaction to our inferior fellow-creatures to find that their betters are, on occasions, no brighter than they are.", "나는 프랭클린 도련님의 무심한 물음과 나의 어리석은 대답을 여기 적어 두거니와, 이는 모든 아둔한 사람들에게 위안과 격려가 되라는 뜻에서다—앞서도 말했듯이, 저보다 나은 사람들도 때로는 저희만큼이나 별수 없다는 것을 알게 되는 것이, 우리네 못난 동류들에게는 크나큰 만족이 되는 법이니 말이다."], ["Neither Mr. Franklin, with his wonderful foreign training, nor I, with my age, experience, and natural mother-wit, had the ghost of an idea of what Rosanna Spearman’s unaccountable behaviour really meant.", "그 놀라운 외국 수련을 쌓은 프랭클린 도련님도, 나이와 경험과 타고난 어수룩한 잔꾀를 지닌 나도, 로재나 스피어먼의 그 까닭 모를 행동이 실제로 무엇을 뜻하는지는 눈곱만큼도 짐작하지 못했다."], ["She was out of our thoughts, poor soul, before we had seen the last flutter of her little grey cloak among the sandhills.", "그 가엾은 것은, 우리가 모래언덕 사이로 그 조그만 잿빛 망토가 마지막으로 나풀거리는 것을 미처 다 보기도 전에, 우리 생각에서 잊히고 말았다."], ["And what of that?", "그래서 그게 어쨌다는 거냐?"], ["you will ask, naturally enough.", "여러분은 자연히 그렇게 물으실 것이다."], ["Read on, good friend, as patiently as you can, and perhaps you will be as sorry for Rosanna Spearman as I was, when I found out the truth.", "계속 읽어 나가시라, 착한 벗이여, 되도록 참을성 있게. 그러면 여러분도 아마, 내가 진상을 알아냈을 때 그러했듯이, 로재나 스피어먼을 가엾이 여기게 되시리라."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["The first thing I did, after we were left together alone, was to make a third attempt to get up from my seat on the sand.", "우리 둘만 남게 된 뒤, 내가 맨 처음 한 일은, 모래 위 내 자리에서 일어나 보려고 세 번째로 시도해 본 것이었다."], ["Mr. Franklin stopped me.", "프랭클린 도련님이 나를 말렸다."]], [["“There is one advantage about this horrid place,”", "\"이 지긋지긋한 곳에도 한 가지 이점은 있군.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said; “we have got it all to ourselves.", "\"이곳을 온통 우리끼리 차지했으니 말이야."], ["Stay where you are, Betteredge; I have something to say to you.”", "그대로 있게, 베터리지. 자네한테 할 말이 있어.\""]], [["While he was speaking, I was looking at him, and trying to see something of the boy I remembered, in the man before me.", "그가 말하는 동안, 나는 그를 바라보며, 내 앞에 선 이 사내에게서 내가 기억하는 그 소년의 모습을 조금이나마 찾아보려 애썼다. 그런데 이 사내가 나를 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["The man put me out. Look as I might, I could see no more of his boy’s rosy cheeks than of his boy’s trim little jacket.", "아무리 뜯어보아도, 소년 시절 그 말쑥한 조그만 저고리를 찾을 수 없듯 소년 시절 그 발그레한 뺨도 찾아볼 수가 없었다."], ["His complexion had got pale: his face, at the lower part was covered, to my great surprise and disappointment, with a curly brown beard and moustachios.", "그의 낯빛은 창백해져 있었다. 얼굴 아래쪽은, 나로서는 여간 놀랍고 실망스럽게도, 곱슬곱슬한 갈색 턱수염과 콧수염으로 덮여 있었다."], ["He had a lively touch-and-go way with him, very pleasant and engaging, I admit; but nothing to compare with his free-and-easy manners of other times.", "그에게는 활기차고 시원시원한 태도가 있어, 인정하거니와 여간 유쾌하고 사람을 끄는 데가 있었다. 그러나 옛 시절의 그 스스럼없고 편안하던 몸가짐에는 견줄 바가 못 되었다."], ["To make matters worse, he had promised to be tall, and had not kept his promise.", "설상가상으로, 그는 키가 커지겠거니 싶더니만 그 약속을 지키지 않았다."], ["He was neat, and slim, and well made; but he wasn’t by an inch or two up to the middle height.", "그는 말끔하고 호리호리하고 몸매가 좋았으나, 중키에서 한두 치는 모자랐다."], ["In short, he baffled me altogether.", "한마디로, 그는 나를 온통 갈피 못 잡게 했다."], ["The years that had passed had left nothing of his old self, except the bright, straightforward look in his eyes.", "흘러간 세월은 그의 옛 모습이라곤 아무것도 남겨 두지 않았다. 다만 그 눈에 어린 밝고 솔직한 눈빛만은 예외였다."], ["There I found our nice boy again, and there I concluded to stop in my investigation.", "거기서 나는 우리 착한 도련님을 다시 찾아냈고, 거기서 내 살펴보기를 그만 멈추기로 했다."]], [["“Welcome back to the old place, Mr.", "\"옛집으로 돌아오신 것을 환영합니다, 프랭클린 도련님.\""], ["Franklin,”", "나는 말했다."], ["I said. “All the more welcome, sir, that you have come some hours before we expected you.”", "\"더구나, 도련님, 우리가 기다리던 것보다 몇 시간이나 일찍 오셨으니 그만큼 더 반갑습니다요.\""]], [["“I have a reason for coming before you expected me,”", "\"자네들이 기다리던 것보다 일찍 온 데는 까닭이 있네.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다."], ["answered Mr. Franklin.", "\"베터리지, 아무래도 나는 지난 사나흘 동안 런던에서 미행을 당하고 감시를 받은 것 같아."], ["“I suspect, Betteredge, that I have been followed and watched in London, for the last three or four days; and I have travelled by the morning instead of the afternoon train, because I wanted to give a certain dark-looking stranger the slip.”", "그래서 오후 기차 대신 아침 기차로 온 걸세. 어떤 거무튀튀한 낯선 자를 따돌리고 싶었거든.\""]], [["Those words did more than surprise me.", "그 말은 나를 놀라게 하는 데 그치지 않았다."], ["They brought back to my mind, in a flash, the three jugglers, and Penelope’s notion that they meant some mischief to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "그 말은 내 머릿속에, 번개처럼, 그 요술쟁이 셋과, 그들이 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 무슨 해코지를 하려 든다던 페넬로피의 짐작을 되살려 놓았다."]], [["“Who’s watching you, sir,—and why?”", "\"누가 도련님을 감시한단 말입니까—또 어째서요?\""], ["I inquired.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“Tell me about the three Indians you have had at the house today,”", "\"오늘 이 집에 왔었다는 그 인도인 셋에 대해 말해 주게.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin, without noticing my question.", "프랭클린 도련님이, 내 물음에는 아랑곳없이 말한다."], ["“It’s just possible, Betteredge, that my stranger and your three jugglers may turn out to be pieces of the same puzzle.”", "\"베터리지, 어쩌면 내 그 낯선 자와 자네네 그 요술쟁이 셋이, 한 수수께끼의 조각들로 밝혀질지도 모르는 일이야.\""]], [["“How do you come to know about the jugglers, sir?”", "\"도련님은 어떻게 그 요술쟁이들에 대해 아십니까?\""], ["I asked, putting one question on the top of another, which was bad manners, I own.", "나는, 물음 위에 또 물음을 얹으며 물었으니, 실토하건대 이건 무례한 짓이었다."], ["But you don’t expect much from poor human nature—so don’t expect much from me.", "그러나 여러분도 가련한 인간 본성에서 너무 많은 것을 기대하지는 않으실 터이니—나한테서도 너무 많은 것을 기대하지는 마시라."]], [["“I saw Penelope at the house,” says Mr.", "\"이 집에서 페넬로피를 보았지.\""], ["Franklin; “and Penelope told me.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["Your daughter promised to be a pretty girl, Betteredge, and she has kept her promise.", "\"그리고 페넬로피가 내게 말해 주었어. 베터리지, 자네 딸은 예쁜 처녀가 되겠거니 싶더니 그 약속을 지켰더군."], ["Penelope has got a small ear and a small foot.", "페넬로피는 조그만 귀와 조그만 발을 지녔어."], ["Did the late Mrs. Betteredge possess those inestimable advantages?”", "지금은 고인이 된 베터리지 부인도 그런 값진 장점을 지녔었나?\""]], [["“The late Mrs. Betteredge possessed a good many defects, sir,”", "\"지금은 고인이 된 베터리지 부인은 흠이라면 여럿 지녔었지요, 도련님.\""], ["says I.", "하고 나는 말한다."], ["“One of them (if you will pardon my mentioning it) was never keeping to the matter in hand.", "\"그중 하나가 (이런 말씀을 드려도 용서하신다면) 도무지 당면한 일에 붙어 있질 못하는 것이었습니다요."], ["She was more like a fly than a woman: she couldn’t settle on anything.”", "그 사람은 여자라기보다 파리에 가까웠어요. 무엇 하나에도 진득이 내려앉질 못했으니까요.\""]], [["“She would just have suited me,”", "\"그렇다면 그 사람은 딱 내게 어울렸겠군.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“I never settle on anything either.", "\"나 역시 무엇 하나에도 진득이 눌러앉질 못하거든."], ["Betteredge, your edge is better than ever.", "베터리지, 자네 그 재치는 여전하다 못해 예전보다 더 날이 섰군그래."], ["Your daughter said as much, when I asked for particulars about the jugglers.", "자네 딸도 내가 요술쟁이 이야기를 자세히 물었을 때 꼭 그렇게 말하더군."], ["‘Father will tell you, sir.", "'아버지가 말씀해 주실 거예요, 도련님."], ["He’s a wonderful man for his age; and he expresses himself beautifully.’ Penelope’s own words—blushing divinely.", "연세에 비해 참으로 대단한 분이시고, 말씀도 참 곱게 하시거든요.' 페넬로피가 한 말 그대로일세—고운 얼굴을 발그레 붉히면서."], ["Not even my respect for you prevented me from—never mind; I knew her when she was a child, and she’s none the worse for it.", "내가 자네를 우러르는 마음조차 나를 말리지 못했으니—아니, 됐네. 나는 그 애가 어린애일 적부터 알았고, 그 애도 그만한 일로 나빠질 것은 없지."], ["Let’s be serious.", "자, 진지해지세."], ["What did the jugglers do?”", "그래, 그 요술쟁이들이 무슨 짓을 했나?\""]], [["I was something dissatisfied with my daughter—not for letting Mr. Franklin kiss her; Mr.", "나는 내 딸이 조금 못마땅했다—프랭클린 도련님이 그 애에게 입 맞추게 놔두어서가 아니라 (그거야 프랭클린 도련님이라면 얼마든지 환영이었다)—그 애의 어리석은 이야기를 나더러 건너 전해 옮기게 만들었기 때문이다."], ["Franklin was welcome to that—but for forcing me to tell her foolish story at second hand.", "그러나 이제 와서는 그 일을 이야기하는 것 말고는 달리 도리가 없었다."], ["However, there was no help for it now but to mention the circumstances.", "내가 이야기를 이어 가는 동안 프랭클린 도련님의 그 유쾌함은 죄다 스러졌다."], ["Mr. Franklin’s merriment all died away as I went on.", "그는 눈썹을 잔뜩 찌푸리고 턱수염을 배배 꼬며 앉아 있었다."], ["He sat knitting his eyebrows, and twisting his beard. When I had done, he repeated after me two of the questions which the chief juggler had put to the boy—seemingly for the purpose of fixing them well in his mind.", "내 이야기가 끝나자, 그는 우두머리 요술쟁이가 소년에게 던졌던 물음 가운데 둘을 나를 따라 되뇌었다—아무래도 그것들을 제 머릿속에 단단히 박아 두려는 속셈인 듯했다."]], [["“‘Is it on the road to this house, and on no other, that the English gentleman will travel today?’ ‘Has the English gentleman got It about him?’ I suspect,”", "\"'그 영국 신사가 오늘 여행할 길이, 다른 어느 길도 아닌 바로 이 집으로 오는 길이더냐?' '그 영국 신사가 그것을 몸에 지니고 있더냐?'"], ["says Mr.", "아무래도,\""], ["Franklin, pulling a little sealed paper parcel out of his pocket, “that ‘It’ means this.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제 주머니에서 봉인된 조그만 종이 꾸러미를 하나 꺼내며 말한다. \"그 '그것'이란 바로 이것을 뜻하는 것 같아."], ["And ‘this,’ Betteredge, means my uncle Herncastle’s famous Diamond.”", "그리고 '이것'이란, 베터리지, 내 외숙부 헌캐슬의 그 유명한 다이아몬드를 뜻하지.\""]], [["“Good Lord, sir!”", "\"아이고 맙소사, 도련님!\""], ["I broke out, “how do you come to be in charge of the wicked Colonel’s Diamond?”", "나는 불쑥 내뱉었다. \"어쩌다 도련님이 그 사악한 대령의 다이아몬드를 맡게 되셨답니까?\""]], [["“The wicked Colonel’s will has left his Diamond as a birthday present to my cousin Rachel,”", "\"그 사악한 대령의 유언이, 그 다이아몬드를 내 사촌 레이철에게 생일 선물로 남겼다네.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“And my father, as the wicked Colonel’s executor, has given it in charge to me to bring down here.”", "\"그리고 그 사악한 대령의 유언 집행인인 내 아버지가, 그것을 이리로 가져오라고 내게 맡기신 게지.\""]], [["If the sea, then oozing in smoothly over the Shivering Sand, had been changed into dry land before my own eyes, I doubt if I could have been more surprised than I was when Mr. Franklin spoke those words.", "그때 떠는 모래펄 위로 매끄럽게 스며들던 그 바다가, 내 눈앞에서 마른 땅으로 변했던들, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 그 말을 했을 때보다 더 놀랄 수 있었을까 싶다."]], [["“The Colonel’s Diamond left to Miss Rachel!”", "\"대령의 다이아몬드가 레이철 아가씨께 남겨졌다!\" 하고 나는 말한다."], ["says I. “And your father, sir, the Colonel’s executor!", "\"게다가 도련님 아버님이, 도련님, 그 대령의 유언 집행인이시라니!"], ["Why, I would have laid any bet you like, Mr. Franklin, that your father wouldn’t have touched the Colonel with a pair of tongs!”", "아니, 프랭클린 도련님, 저는 도련님 아버님이 그 대령을 부젓가락으로도 건드리려 하지 않으실 거라는 데 무슨 내기든 걸었을 겁니다요!\""]], [["“Strong language, Betteredge!", "\"험한 소리로군, 베터리지!"], ["What was there against the Colonel?", "그 대령한테 무슨 흠이 있었기에?"], ["He belonged to your time, not to mine.", "그 사람은 내 시절이 아니라 자네 시절 사람이잖나."], ["Tell me what you know about him, and I’ll tell you how my father came to be his executor, and more besides.", "자네가 그에 대해 아는 것을 내게 말해 주게. 그러면 나도 내 아버지가 어떻게 그의 유언 집행인이 되셨는지, 그 밖의 것들도 자네한테 말해 주지."], ["I have made some discoveries in London about my uncle Herncastle and his Diamond, which have rather an ugly look to my eyes; and I want you to confirm them.", "나는 런던에서 내 외숙부 헌캐슬과 그 다이아몬드에 관해 몇 가지를 알아냈는데, 내 눈에는 그게 꽤나 흉하게 보인단 말이야. 자네가 그걸 확인해 주었으면 하네."], ["You called him the ‘wicked Colonel’ just now.", "자네 방금 그를 '사악한 대령'이라고 불렀지."], ["Search your memory, my old friend, and tell me why.”", "기억을 더듬어 보게, 이 사람아, 그리고 그 까닭을 내게 말해 주게.\""]], [["I saw he was in earnest, and I told him.", "그가 진지하다는 것을 알았기에, 나는 그에게 말해 주었다."]], [["Here follows the substance of what I said, written out entirely for your benefit.", "이하는 내가 한 이야기의 골자로, 순전히 여러분을 위해 죄다 적어 놓은 것이다. 여기에 주의를 기울이시라."], ["Pay attention to it, or you will be all abroad, when we get deeper into the story.", "그러지 않으면 이야기가 깊어졌을 때 여러분은 온통 갈피를 잃고 헤매게 될 것이다."], ["Clear your mind of the children, or the dinner, or the new bonnet, or what not.", "아이들이며 저녁밥이며 새 챙 모자며 하는 것들을 머릿속에서 말끔히 비워 내시라."], ["Try if you can’t forget politics, horses, prices in the City, and grievances at the club.", "정치며 말(馬)이며 도시의 시세며 클럽에서의 불평 따위를 잊을 수 있거든 어디 한번 잊어 보시라."], ["I hope you won’t take this freedom on my part amiss; it’s only a way I have of appealing to the gentle reader.", "내 이런 허물없음을 부디 언짢게 여기지 마시기를. 이건 그저 내가 점잖으신 독자께 호소하는 한 방식일 뿐이다."], ["Lord!", "원 참!"], ["haven’t I seen you with the greatest authors in your hands, and don’t I know how ready your attention is to wander when it’s a book that asks for it, instead of a person?", "나는 여러분이 손에 그 위대한 작가들의 책을 들고 있는 것을 보아 왔거니와, 사람이 아니라 책이 주의를 청할 때면 여러분의 주의가 얼마나 냉큼 딴 데로 새어 나가려 하는지를 내가 어찌 모르겠는가?"]], [["I spoke, a little way back, of my lady’s father, the old lord with the short temper and the long tongue.", "나는 조금 앞에서, 마님의 부친이신, 성미는 짧고 혀는 긴 그 노영주 이야기를 했다. 그분에게는 자녀가 모두 다섯 있었다."], ["He had five children in all.", "우선 아들이 둘."], ["Two sons to begin with; then, after a long time, his wife broke out breeding again, and the three young ladies came briskly one after the other, as fast as the nature of things would permit; my mistress, as before mentioned, being the youngest and best of the three.", "그러고는 한참 뒤에, 그 아내가 다시 아이를 배기 시작하더니, 세 아가씨가, 이치가 허락하는 한 부지런히, 잇달아 태어났다. 앞서 말했듯이 내 여주인이 그 셋 가운데 가장 어리고 가장 훌륭했다."], ["Of the two sons, the eldest, Arthur, inherited the title and estates.", "두 아들 중 맏이 아서는 작위와 영지를 물려받았다."], ["The second, the Honourable John, got a fine fortune left him by a relative, and went into the army.", "둘째인 존 도련님은 한 친척이 남긴 넉넉한 재산을 얻어, 군에 들어갔다."]], [["It’s an ill bird, they say, that fouls its own nest.", "제 둥지를 더럽히는 새는 못된 새라는 말이 있다."], ["I look on the noble family of the Herncastles as being my nest; and I shall take it as a favour if I am not expected to enter into particulars on the subject of the Honourable John.", "나는 저 고귀한 헌캐슬 가문을 내 둥지로 여기고 있다. 그러니 존 도련님에 관한 이야기를 시시콜콜 늘어놓으라고 나한테 바라지 않으신다면, 나는 그것을 고맙게 여기겠다."], ["He was, I honestly believe, one of the greatest blackguards that ever lived.", "그는, 내가 정직히 믿기로, 세상에 나온 가장 몹쓸 망나니 가운데 하나였다."], ["I can hardly say more or less for him than that.", "그에 대해서라면 나는 그 이상도 그 이하도 좀처럼 말하기 어렵다."], ["He went into the army, beginning in the Guards.", "그는 근위대에서 시작하여 군에 들어갔다."], ["He had to leave the Guards before he was two-and-twenty—never mind why.", "그는 스물두 살도 되기 전에 근위대를 떠나야 했다—까닭은 묻지 마시라."], ["They are very strict in the army, and they were too strict for the Honourable John.", "군은 몹시 엄격한데, 존 도련님한테는 지나치게 엄격했던 것이다."], ["He went out to India to see whether they were equally strict there, and to try a little active service.", "그는 인도로 나가, 거기서도 똑같이 엄격한지 알아보고, 실전(實戰)을 조금 겪어 볼 셈이었다."], ["In the matter of bravery (to give him his due), he was a mixture of bull-dog and game-cock, with a dash of the savage.", "용맹으로 말하면 (그의 몫은 돌려주자면), 그는 불도그와 싸움닭을 섞고 거기에 야만의 기운을 한 자밤 뿌린 사내였다."], ["He was at the taking of Seringapatam.", "그는 세링가파탐 공략에 참가했다."], ["Soon afterwards he changed into another regiment, and, in course of time, changed into a third.", "얼마 뒤 그는 다른 연대로 옮겼고, 세월이 흐르는 사이에 세 번째 연대로 또 옮겼다."], ["In the third he got his last step as lieutenant-colonel, and, getting that, got also a sunstroke, and came home to England.", "그 세 번째 연대에서 그는 중령으로 마지막 진급을 했고, 그것을 얻으면서 일사병도 얻어, 영국으로 돌아왔다."]], [["He came back with a character that closed the doors of all his family against him, my lady (then just married) taking the lead, and declaring (with Sir John’s approval, of course) that her brother should never enter any house of hers.", "그는 온 집안의 문을 죄다 제게서 닫아 걸게 만드는 평판을 안고 돌아왔으니, (그때 막 혼인하신) 마님이 앞장서서, 당신 오라비는 결코 당신 집 어느 문도 들어서지 못하리라 선언하셨다 (물론 존 경의 동의를 얻어)."], ["There was more than one slur on the Colonel that made people shy of him; but the blot of the Diamond is all I need mention here.", "대령에게는 사람들이 그를 꺼리게 만드는 오점이 한둘이 아니었으나, 여기서는 그 다이아몬드의 오점 하나만 짚어 두면 되겠다."]], [["It was said he had got possession of his Indian jewel by means which, bold as he was, he didn’t dare acknowledge.", "들리는 말로는, 그가 그 인도 보석을, 아무리 대담한 그일지언정 감히 실토하지 못할 방법으로 손에 넣었다고 했다."], ["He never attempted to sell it—not being in need of money, and not (to give him his due again) making money an object.", "그는 결코 그것을 팔려 하지 않았다—돈이 궁하지도 않았거니와, (다시 그의 몫을 돌려주자면) 돈을 목적으로 삼지도 않았으니까."], ["He never gave it away; he never even showed it to any living soul.", "그는 결코 그것을 남에게 주지 않았고, 살아 있는 그 어떤 사람에게도 그것을 보여 준 적조차 없었다."], ["Some said he was afraid of its getting him into a difficulty with the military authorities; others (very ignorant indeed of the real nature of the man) said he was afraid, if he showed it, of its costing him his life.", "어떤 이들은 그가, 그것 때문에 군 당국과 곤란한 일에 얽힐까 봐 두려워한다고 했고, 또 어떤 이들은 (그 사내의 본바탕을 참으로 몰라도 한참 몰랐던 자들이었는데) 그가, 그것을 보여 주었다가는 목숨을 잃을까 봐 두려워한다고 했다."]], [["There was perhaps a grain of truth mixed up with this last report.", "이 마지막 소문에는 어쩌면 한 톨의 진실이 섞여 있었는지도 모른다."], ["It was false to say that he was afraid; but it was a fact that his life had been twice threatened in India; and it was firmly believed that the Moonstone was at the bottom of it.", "그가 두려워한다는 것은 거짓말이었으나, 인도에서 그의 목숨이 두 번이나 위협당했다는 것은 사실이었다. 그리고 그 밑바닥에 월장석이 있다는 것이 굳게 믿어졌다."], ["When he came back to England, and found himself avoided by everybody, the Moonstone was thought to be at the bottom of it again.", "그가 영국으로 돌아와, 모든 사람이 저를 피하는 것을 알게 되었을 때도, 그 밑바닥에 또 월장석이 있다고들 여겼다."], ["The mystery of the Colonel’s life got in the Colonel’s way, and outlawed him, as you may say, among his own people.", "대령의 삶에 드리운 수수께끼가 대령의 앞길을 가로막아, 이를테면 그를 제 겨레붙이 사이에서 무법자로 내몰았다."], ["The men wouldn’t let him into their clubs; the women—more than one—whom he wanted to marry, refused him; friends and relations got too near-sighted to see him in the street.", "사내들은 그를 저희 클럽에 들이려 하지 않았고, 그가 혼인하고자 했던 여자들은—한둘이 아니었는데—그를 거절했으며, 벗과 친척들은 거리에서 그를 알아보지 못할 만큼 눈이 어두워졌다."]], [["Some men in this mess would have tried to set themselves right with the world.", "이런 곤경에 처한 사내라면 세상과 화해하려고 애써 볼 법도 하다."], ["But to give in, even when he was wrong, and had all society against him, was not the way of the Honourable John.", "그러나 제가 그르고 온 사회가 저를 적대할 때조차 굽히고 드는 것은, 존 도련님의 방식이 아니었다."], ["He had kept the Diamond, in flat defiance of assassination, in India.", "그는 인도에서, 암살에 대놓고 맞서며 그 다이아몬드를 지켰다."], ["He kept the Diamond, in flat defiance of public opinion, in England.", "그는 영국에서, 세상 여론에 대놓고 맞서며 그 다이아몬드를 지켰다."], ["There you have the portrait of the man before you, as in a picture: a character that braved everything; and a face, handsome as it was, that looked possessed by the devil.", "여기, 한 폭의 그림처럼, 그 사내의 초상이 여러분 앞에 놓여 있다. 모든 것에 맞서는 성정, 그리고 잘생겼으되 악마에게 들린 듯한 얼굴."]], [["We heard different rumours about him from time to time.", "우리는 이따금 그에 관한 갖가지 소문을 들었다."], ["Sometimes they said he was given up to smoking opium and collecting old books; sometimes he was reported to be trying strange things in chemistry; sometimes he was seen carousing and amusing himself among the lowest people in the lowest slums of London.", "어떤 때는 그가 아편을 피우고 고서(古書)를 모으는 일에 빠져 있다고 했고, 어떤 때는 화학으로 괴상한 것들을 시험해 보고 있다고 전해졌으며, 또 어떤 때는 런던의 가장 낮은 빈민굴에서 가장 낮은 사람들 틈에 끼어 진탕 마시고 흥청거리는 것이 목격되었다고도 했다."], ["Anyhow, a solitary, vicious, underground life was the life the Colonel led.", "아무튼 외롭고 사악하고 음침한 삶이, 그 대령이 살아간 삶이었다."], ["Once, and once only, after his return to England, I myself saw him, face to face.", "영국으로 돌아온 뒤 단 한 번, 오직 한 번, 나 자신이 그를 얼굴을 맞대고 본 적이 있다."]], [["About two years before the time of which I am now writing, and about a year and a half before the time of his death, the Colonel came unexpectedly to my lady’s house in London.", "지금 내가 적고 있는 시점으로부터 두 해쯤 전, 그리고 그가 죽기 한 해 반쯤 전에, 대령이 뜻밖에도 런던에 있는 마님 댁에 찾아왔다."], ["It was the night of Miss Rachel’s birthday, the twenty-first of June; and there was a party in honour of it, as usual.", "그날은 레이철 아가씨의 생일밤, 6월 21일이었고, 여느 때처럼 그것을 기리는 잔치가 열리고 있었다."], ["I received a message from the footman to say that a gentleman wanted to see me.", "나는 하인에게서, 어떤 신사분이 나를 보고자 한다는 전갈을 받았다."], ["Going up into the hall, there I found the Colonel, wasted, and worn, and old, and shabby, and as wild and as wicked as ever.", "현관으로 올라가 보니, 거기 대령이 있었다. 여위고, 초췌하고, 늙고, 남루한 데다, 여전히 사납고 여전히 사악한 모습으로."]], [["“Go up to my sister,”", "\"내 누이한테 올라가게.\""], ["says he; “and say that I have called to wish my niece many happy returns of the day.”", "그가 말한다. \"그리고 내 조카에게 생일을 두고두고 경하하고 싶어 내가 찾아왔다고 전하게.\""]], [["He had made attempts by letter, more than once already, to be reconciled with my lady, for no other purpose, I am firmly persuaded, than to annoy her.", "그는 이미 여러 차례 편지로, 마님과 화해해 보려고 시도한 바 있었으나, 내가 굳게 믿기로, 그것은 오직 마님을 성가시게 하려는 것 말고는 다른 목적이 없었다."], ["But this was the first time he had actually come to the house.", "그러나 실제로 이 집에 몸소 찾아온 것은 이번이 처음이었다."], ["I had it on the tip of my tongue to say that my mistress had a party that night.", "나는 오늘 밤 내 여주인께서 잔치를 열고 계신다고 말하려고 혀끝까지 그 말이 나와 있었다."], ["But the devilish look of him daunted me.", "그러나 그의 그 악마 같은 낯빛이 나를 주춤하게 했다."], ["I went upstairs with his message, and left him, by his own desire, waiting in the hall.", "나는 그의 전갈을 들고 위층으로 올라갔고, 그 자신이 바라는 대로 그를 현관에 세워 둔 채 기다리게 했다."], ["The servants stood staring at him, at a distance, as if he was a walking engine of destruction, loaded with powder and shot, and likely to go off among them at a moment’s notice.", "하인들은 멀찍이 떨어져 서서 그를 빤히 쳐다보았으니, 마치 그가 화약과 탄환을 잔뜩 채워 넣은 걸어 다니는 파괴 병기라서, 언제라도 대뜸 저희 틈에서 터져 버릴 것만 같다는 듯했다."]], [["My lady had a dash—no more—of the family temper.", "마님께는 그 집안 성미가 한 가닥—그 이상은 아니고—있었다."], ["“Tell Colonel Herncastle,”", "\"헌캐슬 대령한테 이르게.\""], ["she said, when I gave her her brother’s message, “that Miss Verinder is engaged, and that I decline to see him.”", "내가 그 오라비의 전갈을 전해 드리니 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"버린더 아가씨는 볼일이 있고, 나는 그를 만나기를 거절한다고.\""], ["I tried to plead for a civiller answer than that; knowing the Colonel’s constitutional superiority to the restraints which govern gentlemen in general.", "나는 그보다 좀 더 점잖은 대답을 올려 보시라고 아뢰어 보려 했으니, 여느 신사들을 다스리는 예법 따위를 대령이 천성적으로 대수롭잖게 여긴다는 것을 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Quite useless!", "아주 헛일이었다!"], ["The family temper flashed out at me directly.", "그 집안 성미가 대뜸 내게로 번쩍 튀었다."], ["“When I want your advice,”", "\"내가 자네 조언이 필요할 때는,\""], ["says my lady, “you know that I always ask for it.", "마님이 말씀하신다. \"늘 자네한테 청한다는 걸 알지 않나."], ["I don’t ask for it now.”", "지금은 청하지 않네.\""], ["I went downstairs with the message, of which I took the liberty of presenting a new and amended edition of my own contriving, as follows: “My lady and Miss Rachel regret that they are engaged, Colonel; and beg to be excused having the honour of seeing you.”", "나는 그 전갈을 들고 아래층으로 내려갔는데, 주제넘게도 내 딴에 새로 손질한 개정판을 하나 지어내어 이렇게 올렸다. \"마님과 레이철 아가씨께서는 볼일이 있어 유감스러워하시며, 대령님, 뵙는 영광을 누리지 못함을 부디 양해해 주십사 하십니다.\""]], [["I expected him to break out, even at that polite way of putting it.", "그토록 점잖게 둘러 말했는데도, 나는 그가 벌컥 화를 내리라 여겼다."], ["To my surprise he did nothing of the sort; he alarmed me by taking the thing with an unnatural quiet.", "놀랍게도 그는 전혀 그러지 않았다. 그는 그 일을 부자연스러울 만큼 잠잠하게 받아들여 나를 오싹하게 했다."], ["His eyes, of a glittering bright grey, just settled on me for a moment; and he laughed, not out of himself, like other people, but into himself, in a soft, chuckling, horridly mischievous way.", "번뜩이는 밝은 잿빛 눈이 잠깐 내게 머무는가 싶더니, 그는 웃었다. 남들처럼 밖으로 터뜨려 웃는 것이 아니라, 안으로 삼켜, 나직이 킥킥거리며, 소름 끼치도록 심술궂게."], ["“Thank you, Betteredge,”", "\"고맙네, 베터리지.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“I shall remember my niece’s birthday.”", "\"내 조카의 생일을 잊지 않으마.\""], ["With that, he turned on his heel, and walked out of the house.", "그러고는 발꿈치를 홱 돌려, 집을 걸어 나갔다."]], [["The next birthday came round, and we heard he was ill in bed.", "이듬해 생일이 돌아왔을 때, 우리는 그가 병들어 자리에 누웠다는 소식을 들었다."], ["Six months afterwards—that is to say, six months before the time I am now writing of—there came a letter from a highly respectable clergyman to my lady.", "여섯 달 뒤에—그러니까 지금 내가 적고 있는 시점으로부터 여섯 달 전에—더없이 지체 높은 어느 목사가 마님께 보낸 편지 한 통이 당도했다."], ["It communicated two wonderful things in the way of family news.", "그것은 집안 소식 삼아 두 가지 놀라운 일을 알려 왔다."], ["First, that the Colonel had forgiven his sister on his death-bed.", "첫째, 대령이 임종의 자리에서 제 누이를 용서했다는 것."], ["Second, that he had forgiven everybody else, and had made a most edifying end.", "둘째, 그가 그 밖의 모든 사람도 용서했으며, 더없이 교화적인 최후를 맞았다는 것."], ["I have myself (in spite of the bishops and the clergy) an unfeigned respect for the Church; but I am firmly persuaded, at the same time, that the devil remained in undisturbed possession of the Honourable John, and that the last abominable act in the life of that abominable man was (saving your presence) to take the clergyman in!", "나 자신은 (주교들과 성직자들에도 불구하고) 교회에 대해 거짓 없는 존경을 품고 있다. 그러나 동시에 나는, 악마가 여전히 그 존 도련님을 아무 방해도 받지 않고 차지하고 있었으며, 그 몹쓸 사내의 삶에서 마지막 몹쓸 짓이란 (외람된 말씀이오나) 그 목사를 감쪽같이 속여 넘긴 것이었다고 굳게 믿는다!"]], [["This was the sum-total of what I had to tell Mr. Franklin.", "이것이 내가 프랭클린 도련님께 들려줄 이야기의 전부였다."], ["I remarked that he listened more and more eagerly the longer I went on.", "나는 이야기가 길어질수록 그가 점점 더 열심히 귀 기울이는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["Also, that the story of the Colonel being sent away from his sister’s door, on the occasion of his niece’s birthday, seemed to strike Mr. Franklin like a shot that had hit the mark.", "또한, 대령이 제 조카의 생일을 맞아 누이의 문전에서 쫓겨났다는 그 대목이, 프랭클린 도련님을 마치 과녁에 명중한 총알처럼 후려치는 듯한 것도 눈여겨보았다."], ["Though he didn’t acknowledge it, I saw that I had made him uneasy, plainly enough, in his face.", "그는 인정하지는 않았으나, 내가 그를 불안하게 만들었음을 그의 얼굴에서 똑똑히 읽을 수 있었다."]], [["“You have said your say, Betteredge,”", "\"자네는 할 말을 다 했군, 베터리지.\""], ["he remarked.", "그가 말했다."], ["“It’s my turn now.", "\"이제 내 차례일세."], ["Before, however, I tell you what discoveries I have made in London, and how I came to be mixed up in this matter of the Diamond, I want to know one thing.", "하지만, 내가 런던에서 무엇을 알아냈는지, 그리고 내가 어쩌다 이 다이아몬드 사건에 얽히게 되었는지 자네한테 말하기 전에, 한 가지 알고 싶은 게 있어."], ["You look, my old friend, as if you didn’t quite understand the object to be answered by this consultation of ours.", "이 사람아, 자네 얼굴을 보아하니, 우리의 이 의논으로 대체 무엇을 밝히려는 건지 통 갈피를 못 잡은 것 같군그래."], ["Do your looks belie you?”", "자네 얼굴이 거짓을 말하고 있는 건가?\""]], [["“No, sir,” I said.", "\"아닙니다요, 도련님.\""], ["“My looks, on this occasion at any rate, tell the truth.”", "나는 말했다. \"제 얼굴은, 적어도 이번만큼은, 사실을 말하고 있습니다요.\""]], [["“In that case,” says Mr.", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["Franklin, “suppose I put you up to my point of view, before we go any further.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"더 나아가기 전에, 자네한테 내 관점을 일러 주기로 하지."], ["I see three very serious questions involved in the Colonel’s birthday-gift to my cousin Rachel.", "나는 대령이 내 사촌 레이철에게 남긴 그 생일 선물에, 아주 중대한 물음이 셋 걸려 있다고 보네."], ["Follow me carefully, Betteredge; and count me off on your fingers, if it will help you,”", "잘 따라오게, 베터리지. 그리고 도움이 되겠거든, 손가락을 하나씩 꼽으며 헤아려 보게.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin, with a certain pleasure in showing how clear-headed he could be, which reminded me wonderfully of old times when he was a boy.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제가 얼마나 명석해질 수 있는지 뽐내는 데서 어떤 즐거움을 느끼며 말하는데, 그것이 나로 하여금 그가 소년이던 옛 시절을 신기하리만치 떠오르게 했다."], ["“Question the first: Was the Colonel’s Diamond the object of a conspiracy in India?", "\"첫 번째 물음. 대령의 다이아몬드가 인도에서 어떤 음모의 표적이었는가?"], ["Question the second: Has the conspiracy followed the Colonel’s Diamond to England?", "두 번째 물음. 그 음모가 대령의 다이아몬드를 따라 영국까지 왔는가?"], ["Question the third: Did the Colonel know the conspiracy followed the Diamond; and has he purposely left a legacy of trouble and danger to his sister, through the innocent medium of his sister’s child?", "세 번째 물음. 대령은 그 음모가 다이아몬드를 뒤쫓는 줄 알고 있었는가—그리하여, 제 누이의 자식이라는 죄 없는 매개를 통해, 누이에게 골칫거리와 위험이라는 유산을 일부러 남긴 것인가?"], ["That is what I am driving at, Betteredge.", "바로 그것이 내가 겨누고 있는 바일세, 베터리지."], ["Don’t let me frighten you.”", "내가 자네를 겁주게 두진 말게.\""]], [["It was all very well to say that, but he had frightened me.", "그렇게 말하는 거야 좋았지만, 그는 이미 나를 겁주고 만 터였다."]], [["If he was right, here was our quiet English house suddenly invaded by a devilish Indian Diamond—bringing after it a conspiracy of living rogues, set loose on us by the vengeance of a dead man.", "만일 그의 말이 옳다면, 여기 우리의 조용한 영국 집이 별안간 악마 같은 인도 다이아몬드에 침범당한 셈이었다—죽은 자의 복수가 우리에게 풀어놓은, 시퍼렇게 살아 있는 악당들의 음모를 뒤에 거느린 채로."], ["There was our situation as revealed to me in Mr. Franklin’s last words!", "프랭클린 도련님의 마지막 말이 내게 드러내 보인 우리의 처지가 바로 그러했다!"], ["Who ever heard the like of it—in the nineteenth century, mind; in an age of progress, and in a country which rejoices in the blessings of the British constitution?", "이런 일을 대체 누가 들어 본 적 있단 말인가—그것도, 새겨 두시라, 십구 세기에. 진보의 시대에, 그리고 영국 헌정의 축복을 누리며 기뻐하는 나라에서?"], ["Nobody ever heard the like of it, and, consequently, nobody can be expected to believe it.", "이런 일은 여태 아무도 들어 본 적이 없고, 따라서 아무에게도 이것을 믿으라고 바랄 수는 없는 노릇이다."], ["I shall go on with my story, however, in spite of that.", "그렇지만 나는 그럼에도 내 이야기를 계속해 나가겠다."]], [["When you get a sudden alarm, of the sort that I had got now, nine times out of ten the place you feel it in is your stomach.", "지금 내가 당한 그런 종류의 갑작스러운 놀라움이 닥치면, 열에 아홉은 그것을 느끼는 자리가 배 속이다."], ["When you feel it in your stomach, your attention wanders, and you begin to fidget.", "배 속에서 그것을 느끼면, 주의가 산만해지고, 안절부절못하기 시작한다. 나는 모래 위 내 자리에서 소리 없이 안절부절못했다."], ["I fidgeted silently in my place on the sand. Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님은 내가 뒤숭숭한 배—아니면 마음, 어느 쪽이든 좋으시다."], ["Franklin noticed me, contending with a perturbed stomach or mind—which you please; they mean the same thing—and, checking himself just as he was starting with his part of the story, said to me sharply, “What do you want?”", "그게 그거니까—와 씨름하고 있는 것을 눈치채고는, 막 제 몫의 이야기를 시작하려다 스스로 멈추고, 내게 퉁명스레 말했다. \"자네 뭐가 필요한가?\""]], [["What did I want?", "내가 무엇이 필요했느냐고?"], ["I didn’t tell him; but I’ll tell you, in confidence.", "그에게는 말하지 않았다. 하지만 여러분께는, 은밀히, 말씀드리겠다."], ["I wanted a whiff of my pipe, and a turn at Robinson Crusoe.", "나는 내 담뱃대 한 모금과, 「로빈슨 크루소」 한 대목이 필요했다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["Keeping my private sentiments to myself, I respectfully requested Mr.", "내 속마음은 나 혼자 간직한 채, 나는 프랭클린 도련님께 공손히 계속하시라 청했다."], ["Franklin to go on. Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님은 \"안절부절못하지 말게, 베터리지.\""], ["Franklin replied, “Don’t fidget, Betteredge,” and went on.", "하고 대꾸하더니, 이야기를 계속했다."]], [["Our young gentleman’s first words informed me that his discoveries, concerning the wicked Colonel and the Diamond, had begun with a visit which he had paid (before he came to us) to the family lawyer, at Hampstead.", "우리 젊은 신사가 맨 처음 한 말로 나는 알게 되었으니, 그 사악한 대령과 다이아몬드에 관한 그의 알아냄은, 그가 (우리에게 오기 전에) 햄스테드에 사는 집안 변호사를 찾아간 데서 비롯되었다는 것이다."], ["A chance word dropped by Mr. Franklin, when the two were alone, one day, after dinner, revealed that he had been charged by his father with a birthday present to be taken to Miss Rachel.", "어느 날 저녁 식사 뒤 둘만 있을 때 프랭클린 도련님이 무심코 흘린 한마디가, 그가 제 아버지로부터 레이철 아가씨께 전할 생일 선물을 맡았다는 사실을 드러냈다."], ["One thing led to another; and it ended in the lawyer mentioning what the present really was, and how the friendly connexion between the late Colonel and Mr. Blake, senior, had taken its rise.", "한 가지가 다른 한 가지로 이어졌고, 마침내 변호사가, 그 선물이 실제로 무엇인지, 그리고 지금은 고인이 되신 대령과 블레이크 씨, 그 어른 사이의 그 화기로운 인연이 어떻게 시작되었는지를 이야기하기에 이르렀다."], ["The facts here are really so extraordinary, that I doubt if I can trust my own language to do justice to them. I prefer trying to report Mr.", "여기 그 사실들은 참으로 하도 기이하여, 나는 그것들을 온당히 옮겨 낼 만큼 내 말솜씨를 믿을 수 있을지 의심스럽다."], ["Franklin’s discoveries, as nearly as may be, in Mr. Franklin’s own words.", "나는 차라리 프랭클린 도련님이 알아낸 것을, 되도록 프랭클린 도련님 자신의 말 그대로 전해 보려 한다."]], [["“You remember the time, Betteredge,”", "\"자네도 기억하겠지, 베터리지.\""], ["he said, “when my father was trying to prove his title to that unlucky Dukedom?", "그가 말했다. \"내 아버지가 그 불운한 공작 작위에 대한 당신 권리를 증명하려 애쓰던 그 무렵을?"], ["Well!", "그런데!"], ["that was also the time when my uncle Herncastle returned from India.", "바로 그 무렵이, 내 외숙부 헌캐슬이 인도에서 돌아온 때이기도 했어."], ["My father discovered that his brother-in-law was in possession of certain papers which were likely to be of service to him in his lawsuit.", "내 아버지는, 처남이 당신 소송에 도움이 될 법한 어떤 문서들을 가지고 있다는 것을 알아냈지. 그래서 아버지는, 영국으로 돌아온 것을 환영한다는 구실로 대령을 찾아갔어."], ["He called on the Colonel, on pretence of welcoming him back to England.", "대령은 그런 식으로 속아 넘어갈 사람이 아니었지."], ["The Colonel was not to be deluded in that way.", "'자네 뭔가 바라는 게 있군.' 그가 말했어."], ["‘You want something,’ he said, ‘or you would never have compromised your reputation by calling on me.’ My father saw that the one chance for him was to show his hand; he admitted, at once, that he wanted the papers.", "'아니면 나 같은 사람을 찾아와 자네 체면을 구길 리가 없지.' 내 아버지는, 자기에게 남은 단 하나의 승산이 패를 까 보이는 것임을 알았어."], ["The Colonel asked for a day to consider his answer.", "그래서 대뜸, 그 문서들을 원한다고 인정했지."], ["His answer came in the shape of a most extraordinary letter, which my friend the lawyer showed me.", "대령은 대답을 궁리할 하루 말미를 청했어. 그의 대답은 더없이 기이한 편지 한 통의 형태로 왔는데, 내 벗인 그 변호사가 내게 그 편지를 보여 주었어."], ["The Colonel began by saying that he wanted something of my father, and that he begged to propose an exchange of friendly services between them.", "대령은 먼저, 자기도 내 아버지에게서 바라는 것이 있으며, 두 사람 사이에 서로 돕는 우호적 교환을 제안하고 싶다는 말로 시작했지."], ["The fortune of war (that was the expression he used) had placed him in possession of one of the largest Diamonds in the world; and he had reason to believe that neither he nor his precious jewel was safe in any house, in any quarter of the globe, which they occupied together.", "전쟁의 운(그가 쓴 표현이 그러했다네)이 자기를 세상에서 가장 큰 다이아몬드 가운데 하나의 임자로 만들어 주었노라고. 그런데 자기든 그 귀한 보석이든, 그 둘이 함께 머무는 집이라면 지구상 어느 구석에 있든 안전하지 못하다고 믿을 만한 까닭이 있노라고."], ["Under these alarming circumstances, he had determined to place his Diamond in the keeping of another person.", "이 불안한 형편에서, 자기는 그 다이아몬드를 다른 사람의 보관에 맡기기로 마음먹었다고."], ["That person was not expected to run any risk.", "그 사람은 아무 위험도 무릅쓸 필요가 없다고."], ["He might deposit the precious stone in any place especially guarded and set apart—like a banker’s or jeweller’s strongroom—for the safe custody of valuables of high price.", "그저 그 귀한 보석을, 은행 금고나 보석상 금고처럼—값비싼 귀중품을 안전히 보관하도록 각별히 지키고 따로 마련해 둔—그런 아무 곳에나 맡겨 두면 된다고."], ["His main personal responsibility in the matter was to be of the passive kind.", "이 일에서 그 사람이 몸소 질 주된 책임이란 소극적인 종류의 것이라고."], ["He was to undertake either by himself, or by a trustworthy representative—to receive at a prearranged address, on certain prearranged days in every year, a note from the Colonel, simply stating the fact that he was a living man at that date.", "그 사람은, 몸소든 아니면 자기가 세운 믿을 만한 대리인을 통해서든—미리 정한 주소로, 해마다 미리 정한 며칠에, 대령이 그 날짜에 살아 있는 사람임을 그저 알리는 쪽지 한 장을 받기만 하면 된다고."], ["In the event of the date passing over without the note being received, the Colonel’s silence might be taken as a sure token of the Colonel’s death by murder.", "만일 그 쪽지를 받지 못한 채 그 날짜가 넘어가면, 대령의 침묵은 대령이 살해되어 죽었다는 확실한 표지로 여겨도 좋다고."], ["In that case, and in no other, certain sealed instructions relating to the disposal of the Diamond, and deposited with it, were to be opened, and followed implicitly.", "그런 경우에, 오직 그런 경우에만, 다이아몬드의 처분에 관한, 그것과 함께 맡겨 둔 어떤 봉인된 지시서를 열어, 한 치도 어김없이 따르라고."], ["If my father chose to accept this strange charge, the Colonel’s papers were at his disposal in return.", "만일 내 아버지가 이 기이한 소임을 맡기로 한다면, 그 답례로 대령의 문서들을 아버지 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 하겠노라고."], ["That was the letter.”", "그 편지가 그러했어.\""]], [["“What did your father do, sir?”", "\"도련님 아버님은 어찌하셨습니까요?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“Do?” says Mr.", "\"어찌했느냐고?\""], ["Franklin. “I’ll tell you what he did.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["He brought the invaluable faculty, called common sense, to bear on the Colonel’s letter.", "\"무얼 하셨는지 말해 주지. 아버지는, 상식이라 불리는 그 값을 매길 수 없는 능력을 대령의 편지에 들이대셨어."], ["The whole thing, he declared, was simply absurd.", "이 모든 게 그저 터무니없는 소리라고 아버지는 단언하셨지."], ["Somewhere in his Indian wanderings, the Colonel had picked up with some wretched crystal which he took for a diamond.", "대령이 인도를 떠돌던 어디쯤에서, 다이아몬드로 착각한 어떤 형편없는 수정 조각을 하나 주워 왔으리라는 게야."], ["As for the danger of his being murdered, and the precautions devised to preserve his life and his piece of crystal, this was the nineteenth century, and any man in his senses had only to apply to the police.", "살해당할 위험이니, 제 목숨과 제 수정 조각을 지키려고 궁리해 낸 방비책이니 하는 것으로 말하면, 지금은 십구 세기요, 제정신인 사람이라면 그저 경찰에 신고하기만 하면 될 노릇이라고."], ["The Colonel had been a notorious opium-eater for years past; and, if the only way of getting at the valuable papers he possessed was by accepting a matter of opium as a matter of fact, my father was quite willing to take the ridiculous responsibility imposed on him—all the more readily that it involved no trouble to himself.", "대령은 여러 해 전부터 소문난 아편쟁이였으니, 그가 가진 그 귀중한 문서들을 손에 넣는 단 하나의 길이 아편쟁이의 헛소리를 엄연한 사실인 양 받아들이는 것이라면, 내 아버지는 자기에게 지워진 그 우스꽝스러운 책임을 기꺼이 떠맡을 용의가 있었어—더구나 그것이 자기한테는 아무 수고도 끼치지 않는 일이었으니 더욱 선뜻."], ["The Diamond and the sealed instructions went into his banker’s strongroom, and the Colonel’s letters, periodically reporting him a living man, were received and opened by our family lawyer, Mr. Bruff, as my father’s representative.", "다이아몬드와 그 봉인된 지시서는 아버지 거래 은행의 금고로 들어갔고, 대령이 살아 있는 사람임을 주기적으로 알려 오는 그 편지들은, 아버지 대리인인 우리 집안 변호사 브러프 씨가 받아 열어 보았지."], ["No sensible person, in a similar position, could have viewed the matter in any other way.", "비슷한 처지에 놓인 분별 있는 사람이라면 누구도 이 일을 달리 볼 수는 없었을 게야."], ["Nothing in this world, Betteredge, is probable unless it appeals to our own trumpery experience; and we only believe in a romance when we see it in a newspaper.”", "베터리지, 이 세상 무엇도 우리 자신의 하찮은 경험에 들어맞지 않는 한 그럴듯해 보이지 않는 법이고, 우리는 신문에서 읽어야 비로소 어떤 기담(奇談)을 믿는 법이니까.\""]], [["It was plain to me from this, that Mr. Franklin thought his father’s notion about the Colonel hasty and wrong.", "이로써 나는, 프랭클린 도련님이 그 대령에 관한 제 아버지의 생각을 성급하고 그릇된 것으로 여긴다는 것을 뻔히 알 수 있었다."]], [["“What is your own private opinion about the matter, sir?”", "\"그럼 이 일에 대한 도련님 자신의 속생각은 어떻습니까요?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“Let’s finish the story of the Colonel first,”", "\"우선 대령 이야기부터 마저 끝내세.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“There is a curious want of system, Betteredge, in the English mind; and your question, my old friend, is an instance of it.", "\"베터리지, 영국인의 머리에는 묘하게 체계가 없단 말이야. 그리고 이 사람아, 자네 그 물음이 바로 그 한 본보기일세."], ["When we are not occupied in making machinery, we are (mentally speaking) the most slovenly people in the universe.”", "우리는 기계를 만드느라 바쁘지 않을 때면, (정신으로 말하자면) 온 우주에서 가장 칠칠치 못한 사람들이거든.\""]], [["“So much,” I thought to myself, “for a foreign education!", "\"외국 교육이란 게 고작 이 모양이로군.\""], ["He has learned that way of girding at us in France, I suppose.”", "나는 속으로 생각했다. \"우리를 이렇게 빈정대는 법을 프랑스에서 배워 오셨겠지, 아마.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin took up the lost thread, and went on.", "프랭클린 도련님은 놓쳤던 이야기의 실마리를 도로 집어 들고 계속했다."]], [["“My father,”", "\"내 아버지는,\""], ["he said, “got the papers he wanted, and never saw his brother-in-law again from that time.", "그가 말했다. \"원하던 문서들을 얻고서, 그때부터 다시는 처남을 만나지 않으셨어."], ["Year after year, on the prearranged days, the prearranged letter came from the Colonel, and was opened by Mr. Bruff.", "해마다, 미리 정한 며칠에, 미리 정한 그 편지가 대령에게서 왔고, 브러프 씨가 그것을 열어 보았지."], ["I have seen the letters, in a heap, all of them written in the same brief, business-like form of words: ‘Sir,—This is to certify that I am still a living man.", "나도 그 편지들을 한 무더기로 본 적이 있는데, 죄다 똑같이 짧고 사무적인 문투로 쓰여 있었어. '귀하—본인이 아직 살아 있는 사람임을 이로써 증명하는 바입니다."], ["Let the Diamond be.", "다이아몬드는 그대로 두시오. 존 헌캐슬.'"], ["John Herncastle.’ That was all he ever wrote, and that came regularly to the day; until some six or eight months since, when the form of the letter varied for the first time.", "그가 쓴 것이라곤 늘 그게 다였고, 그게 날짜에 맞춰 어김없이 왔지. 그러다 여섯 달인가 여덟 달쯤 전에, 그 편지의 문투가 처음으로 달라졌어."], ["It ran now: ‘Sir,—They tell me I am dying.", "이번엔 이랬지. '귀하—사람들이 내가 죽어 간다고 하오."], ["Come to me, and help me to make my will.’ Mr.", "내게 와서, 유언장 작성을 도와주시오.'"], ["Bruff went, and found him, in the little suburban villa, surrounded by its own grounds, in which he had lived alone, ever since he had left India.", "브러프 씨가 갔고, 대령이, 인도를 떠난 뒤로 줄곧 저 혼자 살아온, 제 땅에 둘러싸인 그 조그만 교외 별장에 있는 것을 보았어."], ["He had dogs, cats, and birds to keep him company; but no human being near him, except the person who came daily to do the house-work, and the doctor at the bedside.", "그에게는 벗 삼을 개와 고양이와 새들은 있었으나, 곁에 가까이 있는 사람이라곤 날마다 와서 집안일을 해 주는 사람과 머리맡의 의사뿐이었지."], ["The will was a very simple matter.", "유언장은 아주 단순한 것이었어."], ["The Colonel had dissipated the greater part of his fortune in his chemical investigations.", "대령은 제 재산의 대부분을 화학 연구로 탕진해 버렸거든."], ["His will began and ended in three clauses, which he dictated from his bed, in perfect possession of his faculties.", "그의 유언은 세 조항으로 시작해서 세 조항으로 끝났는데, 정신을 온전히 지닌 채 침상에서 그것을 받아쓰게 했어."], ["The first clause provided for the safe keeping and support of his animals.", "첫째 조항은 제 짐승들을 안전히 건사하고 부양하는 데 관한 것이었어."], ["The second founded a professorship of experimental chemistry at a northern university.", "둘째 조항은 북부의 한 대학에 실험화학 강좌를 하나 세우는 것이었지."], ["The third bequeathed the Moonstone as a birthday present to his niece, on condition that my father would act as executor.", "셋째 조항은, 내 아버지가 유언 집행인 노릇을 한다는 조건으로, 월장석을 제 조카에게 생일 선물로 물려주는 것이었어."], ["My father at first refused to act.", "내 아버지는 처음에는 맡기를 거절하셨어."], ["On second thoughts, however, he gave way, partly because he was assured that the executorship would involve him in no trouble; partly because Mr. Bruff suggested, in Rachel’s interest, that the Diamond might be worth something, after all.”", "그러나 다시 생각한 끝에 뜻을 굽히셨는데, 한편으로는 그 집행인 노릇이 자기한테 아무 골칫거리도 끼치지 않으리라는 다짐을 받았기 때문이고, 또 한편으로는 브러프 씨가, 레이철을 위해서 하는 말이라며, 그 다이아몬드가 그래도 얼마간 값어치가 있을지도 모른다고 넌지시 일렀기 때문이야.\""]], [["“Did the Colonel give any reason, sir,”", "\"대령이, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 물었다."], ["I inquired, “why he left the Diamond to Miss Rachel?”", "\"어째서 그 다이아몬드를 레이철 아가씨께 남겼는지 무슨 까닭이라도 댔습니까요?\""]], [["“He not only gave the reason—he had the reason written in his will,”", "\"까닭을 댔을 뿐 아니라—그 까닭을 유언장에 적어 놓기까지 했어.\""], ["said Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말했다."], ["“I have got an extract, which you shall see presently.", "\"그 발췌본을 내가 하나 갖고 있으니, 이따가 자네도 보게 될 걸세."], ["Don’t be slovenly-minded, Betteredge!", "칠칠치 못하게 굴지 말게, 베터리지!"], ["One thing at a time.", "한 번에 하나씩."], ["You have heard about the Colonel’s Will; now you must hear what happened after the Colonel’s death.", "자네는 대령의 유언장에 관해 들었으니, 이제 대령이 죽은 뒤에 무슨 일이 있었는지 들어야 하네."], ["It was formally necessary to have the Diamond valued, before the Will could be proved.", "유언장을 검인받으려면, 먼저 그 다이아몬드의 값을 매겨 두는 것이 절차상 필요했어."], ["All the jewellers consulted, at once confirmed the Colonel’s assertion that he possessed one of the largest diamonds in the world.", "자문을 구한 보석상들은 하나같이, 세상에서 가장 큰 다이아몬드 가운데 하나를 지녔다던 대령의 장담을 대뜸 확인해 주었지."], ["The question of accurately valuing it presented some serious difficulties.", "그것의 값을 정확히 매기는 문제에는 여간 심각한 어려움이 따랐어."], ["Its size made it a phenomenon in the diamond market; its colour placed it in a category by itself; and, to add to these elements of uncertainty, there was a defect, in the shape of a flaw, in the very heart of the stone.", "그 크기는 그것을 다이아몬드 시장의 진기한 물건으로 만들었고, 그 빛깔은 그것을 그것만의 부류에 따로 세워 두었으며, 게다가 이 불확실한 요소들에 더하여, 그 돌 한복판에 흠 모양의 결함이 하나 있었거든."], ["Even with this last serious draw-back, however, the lowest of the various estimates given was twenty thousand pounds.", "그렇지만 이 마지막의 심각한 흠에도 불구하고, 매겨진 여러 감정가 가운데 가장 낮은 것이 이만 파운드였어."], ["Conceive my father’s astonishment!", "내 아버지가 얼마나 놀라셨을지 헤아려 보게!"], ["He had been within a hair’s-breadth of refusing to act as executor, and of allowing this magnificent jewel to be lost to the family.", "아버지는 하마터면 집행인 맡기를 거절하여, 이 굉장한 보석을 집안에서 잃어버리게 할 뻔했으니까."], ["The interest he took in the matter now, induced him to open the sealed instructions which had been deposited with the Diamond.", "이제 이 일에 품게 된 관심이, 아버지로 하여금 그 다이아몬드와 함께 맡겨 두었던 봉인된 지시서를 열어 보게 했어."], ["Mr. Bruff showed this document to me, with the other papers; and it suggests (to my mind) a clue to the nature of the conspiracy which threatened the Colonel’s life.”", "브러프 씨가 다른 문서들과 함께 이 서류를 내게 보여 주었는데, 그것은 (내 보기엔) 대령의 목숨을 위협하던 그 음모의 정체에 대한 한 실마리를 넌지시 던져 주고 있어.\""]], [["“Then you do believe, sir,”", "\"그럼 도련님은, 도련님,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said, “that there was a conspiracy?”", "\"정말로 음모가 있었다고 믿으시는 겁니까요?\""]], [["“Not possessing my father’s excellent common sense,” answered Mr.", "\"내 아버지의 그 훌륭한 상식을 지니지 못한지라,\""], ["Franklin, “I believe the Colonel’s life was threatened, exactly as the Colonel said.", "프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"나는 대령의 목숨이, 바로 대령이 말한 그대로, 위협당했다고 믿네."], ["The sealed instructions, as I think, explain how it was that he died, after all, quietly in his bed.", "그 봉인된 지시서가, 내 생각엔, 그가 그럼에도 결국 제 침상에서 조용히 죽은 것이 어찌 된 일인지를 설명해 주지."], ["In the event of his death by violence (that is to say, in the absence of the regular letter from him at the appointed date), my father was then directed to send the Moonstone secretly to Amsterdam.", "그가 만일 폭력으로 죽는 경우에는(그러니까, 정한 날짜에 그에게서 정례 편지가 오지 않는 경우에는), 내 아버지더러 그 월장석을 은밀히 암스테르담으로 보내라고 지시해 두었어."], ["It was to be deposited in that city with a famous diamond-cutter, and it was to be cut up into from four to six separate stones.", "그 도시의 어느 이름난 다이아몬드 세공사에게 그것을 맡겨, 넷에서 여섯 개의 낱낱 돌로 쪼개게 하라고."], ["The stones were then to be sold for what they would fetch, and the proceeds were to be applied to the founding of that professorship of experimental chemistry, which the Colonel has since endowed by his Will.", "그런 다음 그 돌들을 받을 수 있는 값에 팔아, 그 판 돈을, 대령이 그 뒤 유언장으로 기부한 바로 그 실험화학 강좌를 세우는 데 쓰라고 했지."], ["Now, Betteredge, exert those sharp wits of yours, and observe the conclusion to which the Colonel’s instructions point!”", "자, 베터리지, 그 날카로운 재치를 어디 좀 부려서, 대령의 지시가 가리키는 결론을 살펴보게나!\""]], [["I instantly exerted my wits.", "나는 대뜸 내 재치를 부렸다."], ["They were of the slovenly English sort; and they consequently muddled it all, until Mr. Franklin took them in hand, and pointed out what they ought to see.", "그런데 그것은 칠칠치 못한 영국식이라, 결국 온통 뒤죽박죽으로 만들어 놓고 말았으니, 프랭클린 도련님이 그것을 붙들어 마땅히 보아야 할 바를 짚어 주고서야 겨우 정리가 되었다."]], [["“Remark,” says Mr. Franklin, “that the integrity of the Diamond, as a whole stone, is here artfully made dependent on the preservation from violence of the Colonel’s life. He is not satisfied with saying to the enemies he dreads, ‘Kill me—and you will be no nearer to the Diamond than you are now; it is where you can’t get at it—in the guarded strongroom of a bank.’ He says instead, ‘Kill me—and the Diamond will be the Diamond no longer; its identity will be destroyed.’ What does that mean?”", "\"잘 보게.\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"여기서 그 다이아몬드가 온전한 한 돌로서 지니는 완전함이, 대령의 목숨이 폭력에서 지켜지느냐에 교묘히 매여 있단 말이야. 그는 자기가 두려워하는 적들에게 이렇게 말하는 것으로 만족하지 않았어. '나를 죽여라—그래 봐야 너희는 지금보다 그 다이아몬드에 조금도 더 가까워지지 못한다. 그것은 너희가 손댈 수 없는 곳, 삼엄히 지키는 은행 금고 안에 있으니까.' 그는 그 대신 이렇게 말한 거지. '나를 죽여라—그러면 그 다이아몬드는 더 이상 그 다이아몬드가 아니게 된다. 그 정체가 파괴되고 말 테니까.' 그게 무슨 뜻이겠나?\""]], [["Here I had (as I thought) a flash of the wonderful foreign brightness.", "여기서 나는 (내 딴에는) 그 놀라운 외국식 명민함이 한 줄기 번쩍하는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I know,” I said.", "\"알겠습니다.\""], ["“It means lowering the value of the stone, and cheating the rogues in that way!”", "나는 말했다. \"그 돌의 값어치를 떨어뜨려서, 그런 식으로 그 악당들을 골탕 먹이겠다는 뜻이군요!\""]], [["“Nothing of the sort,”", "\"전혀 그렇지 않아.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“I have inquired about that.", "\"내가 그 점을 알아봤거든."], ["The flawed Diamond, cut up, would actually fetch more than the Diamond as it now is; for this plain reason—that from four to six perfect brilliants might be cut from it, which would be, collectively, worth more money than the large—but imperfect single stone.", "흠 있는 그 다이아몬드는, 쪼개 놓으면, 지금 그대로일 때보다 실제로 더 값을 받을 걸세. 그 뻔한 까닭인즉—거기서 넷에서 여섯 개의 완벽한 광채석을 잘라 낼 수 있는데, 그것들을 다 합치면 크되 흠 있는 낱돌 하나보다 더 많은 돈이 될 테니까."], ["If robbery for the purpose of gain was at the bottom of the conspiracy, the Colonel’s instructions absolutely made the Diamond better worth stealing.", "만일 이득을 노린 강탈이 그 음모의 밑바닥에 있었다면, 대령의 지시는 그야말로 그 다이아몬드를 훔칠 값어치가 더 나가도록 만든 셈이야."], ["More money could have been got for it, and the disposal of it in the diamond market would have been infinitely easier, if it had passed through the hands of the workmen of Amsterdam.”", "그것으로 더 많은 돈을 받을 수 있었을 테고, 암스테르담 직공들의 손을 거쳤더라면 그것을 다이아몬드 시장에서 처분하기도 한없이 더 쉬웠을 테니까.\""]], [["“Lord bless us, sir!”", "\"아이고 맙소사, 도련님!\""], ["I burst out.", "나는 불쑥 내뱉었다."], ["“What was the plot, then?”", "\"그럼 대체 그 흉계가 무엇이었단 말입니까요?\""]], [["“A plot organised among the Indians who originally owned the jewel,”", "\"애초에 그 보석을 소유했던 인도인들 사이에서 꾸며진 흉계지.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin—“a plot with some old Hindoo superstition at the bottom of it.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다—\"그 밑바닥에 어떤 오래된 힌두교 미신이 깔린 흉계 말일세."], ["That is my opinion, confirmed by a family paper which I have about me at this moment.”", "그게 내 생각인데, 지금 이 순간 내가 몸에 지니고 있는 한 집안 문서가 그것을 뒷받침해 주고 있어.\""]], [["I saw, now, why the appearance of the three Indian jugglers at our house had presented itself to Mr. Franklin in the light of a circumstance worth noting.", "나는 이제야, 우리 집에 그 인도인 요술쟁이 셋이 나타난 것이 어째서 프랭클린 도련님에게 눈여겨볼 만한 정황으로 비쳤는지 알 수 있었다."]], [["“I don’t want to force my opinion on you,”", "\"내 생각을 자네한테 억지로 들이밀 생각은 없네.\""], ["Mr. Franklin went on.", "프랭클린 도련님이 계속했다."], ["“The idea of certain chosen servants of an old Hindoo superstition devoting themselves, through all difficulties and dangers, to watching the opportunity of recovering their sacred gem, appears to me to be perfectly consistent with everything that we know of the patience of Oriental races, and the influence of Oriental religions.", "\"어떤 오래된 힌두교 미신을 받드는, 뽑힌 종복들이, 온갖 어려움과 위험을 무릅쓰고, 저희 성스러운 보석을 되찾을 기회를 엿보는 데 몸을 바친다는 발상은, 내가 보기엔, 동방 민족의 그 끈기와 동방 종교의 그 위력에 대해 우리가 아는 모든 것과 완벽하게 들어맞는단 말이야."], ["But then I am an imaginative man; and the butcher, the baker, and the tax-gatherer, are not the only credible realities in existence to my mind.", "하지만 뭐, 나는 상상력이 풍부한 사람이니까. 그리고 내 머릿속에서는, 푸주한이며 빵집 주인이며 세금 걷는 이만이 믿을 만한 현실인 것도 아니고."], ["Let the guess I have made at the truth in this matter go for what it is worth, and let us get on to the only practical question that concerns us.", "이 일의 진상에 대해 내가 해 본 짐작은 그저 그 값어치대로 여기기로 하고, 우리와 관계된 단 하나의 실제적인 물음으로 넘어가세."], ["Does the conspiracy against the Moonstone survive the Colonel’s death?", "월장석을 노린 그 음모가 대령의 죽음 뒤에도 살아남아 있는가?"], ["And did the Colonel know it, when he left the birthday gift to his niece?”", "그리고 대령은, 제 조카에게 그 생일 선물을 남길 때, 그 사실을 알고 있었는가?\""]], [["I began to see my lady and Miss Rachel at the end of it all, now.", "나는 이제야, 이 모든 것의 끄트머리에 마님과 레이철 아가씨가 계신 것을 보기 시작했다."], ["Not a word he said escaped me.", "그가 하는 말은 한마디도 내게서 놓치지 않았다."]], [["“I was not very willing, when I discovered the story of the Moonstone,”", "\"나는, 월장석의 그 내력을 알아냈을 때,\" 프랭클린 도련님이 말했다."], ["said Mr. Franklin, “to be the means of bringing it here.", "\"그것을 이리로 가져오는 노릇을 맡고 싶은 마음이 그리 없었어."], ["But Mr. Bruff reminded me that somebody must put my cousin’s legacy into my cousin’s hands—and that I might as well do it as anybody else.", "하지만 브러프 씨가, 누군가는 내 사촌의 유산을 내 사촌의 손에 쥐여 주어야 하는데—그 일을 남이 하나 내가 하나 매한가지 아니겠느냐고 일깨워 주더군."], ["After taking the Diamond out of the bank, I fancied I was followed in the streets by a shabby, dark-complexioned man.", "은행에서 그 다이아몬드를 찾아 나온 뒤, 나는 거리에서 남루하고 거무튀튀한 사내 하나에게 미행당하는 것 같았어."], ["I went to my father’s house to pick up my luggage, and found a letter there, which unexpectedly detained me in London.", "짐을 챙기러 아버지 댁에 갔더니 거기 편지가 한 통 있었는데, 그게 뜻밖에 나를 런던에 붙들어 두었지."], ["I went back to the bank with the Diamond, and thought I saw the shabby man again.", "나는 그 다이아몬드를 도로 은행에 갖다 두었는데, 그 남루한 사내를 또 본 것 같았어."], ["Taking the Diamond once more out of the bank this morning, I saw the man for the third time, gave him the slip, and started (before he recovered the trace of me) by the morning instead of the afternoon train.", "오늘 아침 다시 한번 은행에서 그 다이아몬드를 찾아 나오다가, 나는 그 사내를 세 번째로 보았고, 그를 따돌린 뒤, (그가 내 자취를 되찾기 전에) 오후 기차 대신 아침 기차로 출발한 거야."], ["Here I am, with the Diamond safe and sound—and what is the first news that meets me?", "이렇게 나는, 다이아몬드를 멀쩡히 잘 간수한 채 여기 와 있어—그런데 나를 맞이한 첫 소식이 무엇인 줄 아나?"], ["I find that three strolling Indians have been at the house, and that my arrival from London, and something which I am expected to have about me, are two special objects of investigation to them when they believe themselves to be alone.", "알고 보니 떠돌이 인도인 셋이 이 집에 다녀갔고, 내가 런던에서 오는 일과, 내가 몸에 지니고 있으리라 여겨지는 어떤 것이, 저희끼리만 있다고 믿을 때 그자들이 캐고 드는 두 가지 특별한 표적이라지 뭔가."], ["I don’t waste time and words on their pouring the ink into the boy’s hand, and telling him to look in it for a man at a distance, and for something in that man’s pocket.", "그자들이 소년의 손에 잉크를 붓고, 그 속을 들여다보아 멀리 있는 사람과 그 사람 주머니 속 무언가를 찾아보라고 시켰다는 것에는, 나도 굳이 시간과 말을 허비하지 않겠네."], ["The thing (which I have often seen done in the East) is ‘hocus-pocus’ in my opinion, as it is in yours.", "그런 짓은 (동방에서 여러 번 행해지는 것을 봤지만) 내 생각에도, 자네 생각과 마찬가지로, '눈속임'이니까."], ["The present question for us to decide is, whether I am wrongly attaching a meaning to a mere accident?", "지금 우리가 판가름해야 할 물음은 이걸세. 내가 한낱 우연에 부질없이 어떤 의미를 갖다 붙이고 있는 건가?"], ["or whether we really have evidence of the Indians being on the track of the Moonstone, the moment it is removed from the safe keeping of the bank?”", "아니면 우리는, 그 다이아몬드가 은행의 안전한 보관에서 벗어나는 바로 그 순간 인도인들이 월장석의 자취를 뒤쫓고 있다는 진짜 증거를 쥔 건가?\""]], [["Neither he nor I seemed to fancy dealing with this part of the inquiry.", "그도 나도, 이 조사의 이 대목을 다루고 싶은 마음은 없어 보였다."], ["We looked at each other, and then we looked at the tide, oozing in smoothly, higher and higher, over the Shivering Sand.", "우리는 서로를 마주 보았고, 그러고는, 떠는 모래펄 위로 점점 더 높이 매끄럽게 스며드는 밀물을 바라보았다."]], [["“What are you thinking of?”", "\"무슨 생각을 하고 있나?\""], ["says Mr. Franklin, suddenly.", "프랭클린 도련님이 불쑥 말한다."]], [["“I was thinking, sir,”", "\"저는 생각하고 있었습니다요, 도련님.\""], ["I answered, “that I should like to shy the Diamond into the quicksand, and settle the question in that way.”", "나는 대답했다. \"그 다이아몬드를 저 유사에 던져 넣어, 그런 식으로 이 문제를 매듭지어 버리면 좋겠다고요.\""]], [["“If you have got the value of the stone in your pocket,”", "\"그 돌의 값어치를 자네 주머니에서 물어 낼 수 있거든,\""], ["answered Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다."], ["Franklin, “say so, Betteredge, and in it goes!”", "\"그렇다고 말하게, 베터리지. 그러면 냅다 던져 넣지!\""]], [["It’s curious to note, when your mind’s anxious, how very far in the way of relief a very small joke will go.", "마음이 불안할 때, 아주 조그만 농담 하나가 마음을 덜어 주는 데 얼마나 멀리까지 가 닿는지는 눈여겨볼 만한 노릇이다."], ["We found a fund of merriment, at the time, in the notion of making away with Miss Rachel’s lawful property, and getting Mr. Blake, as executor, into dreadful trouble—though where the merriment was, I am quite at a loss to discover now.", "우리는 그때, 레이철 아가씨의 정당한 재산을 없애 버려 유언 집행인인 블레이크 씨를 끔찍한 곤경에 빠뜨린다는 생각에 한바탕 신명이 났으니—대체 그 신명 날 거리가 어디 있었는지는, 지금 와서는 나도 도무지 찾아낼 길이 없다만."]], [["Mr. Franklin was the first to bring the talk back to the talk’s proper purpose.", "이야기를 그 본래 목적으로 도로 끌어온 것은 프랭클린 도련님이 먼저였다."], ["He took an envelope out of his pocket, opened it, and handed to me the paper inside.", "그는 제 주머니에서 봉투를 하나 꺼내 열더니, 그 안에 든 종이를 내게 건넸다."]], [["“Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지.\""], ["he said, “we must face the question of the Colonel’s motive in leaving this legacy to his niece, for my aunt’s sake.", "그가 말했다. \"우리는 내 이모님을 위해서라도, 대령이 제 조카에게 이 유산을 남긴 동기라는 물음을 마주해야 하네."], ["Bear in mind how Lady Verinder treated her brother from the time when he returned to England, to the time when he told you he should remember his niece’s birthday.", "대령이 영국으로 돌아온 때부터, 제 조카의 생일을 잊지 않겠노라 자네한테 말한 때까지, 레이디 버린더가 그 오라비를 어떻게 대했는지 되새겨 보게."], ["And read that.”", "그러고 나서 이걸 읽어 보게.\""]], [["He gave me the extract from the Colonel’s Will.", "그는 대령의 유언장에서 뽑은 발췌본을 내게 주었다."], ["I have got it by me while I write these words; and I copy it, as follows, for your benefit:", "나는 이 글을 쓰는 지금도 그것을 곁에 두고 있으며, 여러분을 위해 그것을 아래와 같이 옮겨 적는다."]], [["“Thirdly, and lastly, I give and bequeath to my niece, Rachel Verinder, daughter and only child of my sister, Julia Verinder, widow—if her mother, the said Julia Verinder, shall be living on the said Rachel Verinder’s next Birthday after my death—the yellow Diamond belonging to me, and known in the East by the name of The Moonstone: subject to this condition, that her mother, the said Julia Verinder, shall be living at the time.", "\"셋째로, 그리고 마지막으로, 나는 내 조카 레이철 버린더, 곧 내 누이인 미망인 줄리아 버린더의 딸이자 외동 자식인 그에게—만일 그 어머니, 곧 앞서 말한 줄리아 버린더가 내가 죽은 뒤 앞서 말한 레이철 버린더의 다음 생일에 살아 있다면—나에게 속한, 그리고 동방에서 월장석이라는 이름으로 알려진 그 노란 다이아몬드를 주고 물려준다. 다만 그 어머니, 곧 앞서 말한 줄리아 버린더가 그때에 살아 있어야 한다는 이 조건에 따른다."], ["And I hereby desire my executor to give my Diamond, either by his own hands or by the hands of some trustworthy representative whom he shall appoint, into the personal possession of my said niece Rachel, on her next birthday after my death, and in the presence, if possible, of my sister, the said Julia Verinder.", "그리고 나는 이로써 내 유언 집행인에게, 내가 죽은 뒤 다음 생일에, 되도록이면 내 누이, 곧 앞서 말한 줄리아 버린더가 지켜보는 가운데, 내 다이아몬드를 제 손으로든 아니면 제가 세운 어떤 믿을 만한 대리인의 손으로든, 앞서 말한 내 조카 레이철의 몸소 소유로 넘겨줄 것을 바란다."], ["And I desire that my said sister may be informed, by means of a true copy of this, the third and last clause of my Will, that I give the Diamond to her daughter Rachel, in token of my free forgiveness of the injury which her conduct towards me has been the means of inflicting on my reputation in my lifetime; and especially in proof that I pardon, as becomes a dying man, the insult offered to me as an officer and a gentleman, when her servant, by her orders, closed the door of her house against me, on the occasion of her daughter’s birthday.”", "또한 나는, 내가 그 딸 레이철에게 이 다이아몬드를 주는 것이, 그의 행실이 내 생전에 내 명예에 입힌 상처를 내가 기꺼이 용서하는 표시임을—특히, 그 딸의 생일을 맞아 그가 제 하인을 시켜 제 집 문을 내 앞에서 닫아걸었을 때, 한 사람의 장교이자 신사인 나에게 가한 그 모욕을, 죽어 가는 사람답게 내가 용서한다는 증거임을—내 유언의 이 셋째이자 마지막 조항의 진정한 사본을 통해, 앞서 말한 내 누이에게 알려 주기를 바란다.\""]], [["More words followed these, providing if my lady was dead, or if Miss Rachel was dead, at the time of the testator’s decease, for the Diamond being sent to Holland, in accordance with the sealed instructions originally deposited with it.", "이 뒤로 몇 마디가 더 이어졌는데, 만일 유언자가 죽을 때 마님이 이미 죽었거나 레이철 아가씨가 이미 죽은 경우에는, 애초에 그 다이아몬드와 함께 맡겨 둔 봉인된 지시서에 따라 그것을 네덜란드로 보내라는 내용이었다."], ["The proceeds of the sale were, in that case, to be added to the money already left by the Will for the professorship of chemistry at the university in the north.", "그 경우, 판 돈은 이미 유언장으로 북부 대학의 화학 강좌에 남긴 돈에 보태도록 되어 있었다."]], [["I handed the paper back to Mr. Franklin, sorely troubled what to say to him.", "나는 그 종이를 프랭클린 도련님께 도로 건네주며, 그에게 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몹시 난감했다."], ["Up to that moment, my own opinion had been (as you know) that the Colonel had died as wickedly as he had lived.", "그 순간까지, 내 나름의 생각은 (여러분도 아시다시피) 대령이 살아온 그대로 사악하게 죽었으리라는 것이었다."], ["I don’t say the copy from his Will actually converted me from that opinion: I only say it staggered me.", "그 유언장 사본이 그 생각에서 나를 실제로 돌아서게 했다고까지는 말하지 않겠다. 다만 그것이 나를 휘청하게 했다고만 말하겠다."]], [["“Well,” says Mr.", "\"자.\""], ["Franklin, “now you have read the Colonel’s own statement, what do you say?", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"이제 자네도 대령 자신의 진술을 읽었으니, 어떻게 생각하나?"], ["In bringing the Moonstone to my aunt’s house, am I serving his vengeance blindfold, or am I vindicating him in the character of a penitent and Christian man?”", "그 월장석을 내 이모님 댁으로 가져오는 것으로, 나는 눈을 가린 채 그의 복수에 이바지하고 있는 건가, 아니면 뉘우치는 기독교인이라는 사람됨으로 그의 명예를 되세우고 있는 건가?\""]], [["“It seems hard to say, sir,”", "\"말하기 어려워 보입니다요, 도련님.\""], ["I answered, “that he died with a horrid revenge in his heart, and a horrid lie on his lips.", "나는 대답했다. \"그가 가슴에는 끔찍한 복수심을, 입술에는 끔찍한 거짓말을 담은 채 죽었다고요."], ["God alone knows the truth.", "진상은 오직 하느님만이 아십니다."], ["Don’t ask me.”", "저한테는 묻지 마십시오.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin sat twisting and turning the extract from the Will in his fingers, as if he expected to squeeze the truth out of it in that manner.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 마치 그런 식으로 진상을 쥐어짜 낼 수 있으리라 여기는 듯, 그 유언장 발췌본을 손가락으로 배배 돌리고 뒤집으며 앉아 있었다."], ["He altered quite remarkably, at the same time.", "그와 동시에 그는 사뭇 눈에 띄게 달라졌다."], ["From being brisk and bright, he now became, most unaccountably, a slow, solemn, and pondering young man.", "활기차고 명민하던 데서, 이제 그는, 참으로 알 수 없게도, 굼뜨고 근엄하고 골똘한 젊은이가 되어 버렸다."]], [["“This question has two sides,”", "\"이 물음에는 두 측면이 있어.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“An Objective side, and a Subjective side.", "\"객관적 측면과, 주관적 측면 말이야."], ["Which are we to take?”", "우리는 어느 쪽을 택할까?\""]], [["He had had a German education as well as a French.", "그는 프랑스식뿐 아니라 독일식 교육도 받은 터였다."], ["One of the two had been in undisturbed possession of him (as I supposed) up to this time.", "(내 짐작으로는) 그 둘 중 하나가 이때까지 그를 아무 방해 없이 차지하고 있었다."], ["And now (as well as I could make out) the other was taking its place.", "그런데 이제 (내가 짐작할 수 있는 한) 다른 하나가 그 자리를 대신 차지하는 참이었다."], ["It is one of my rules in life, never to notice what I don’t understand.", "내가 이해하지 못하는 것에는 결코 아랑곳하지 않는 것이, 내 인생의 규칙 가운데 하나다."], ["I steered a middle course between the Objective side and the Subjective side.", "나는 객관적 측면과 주관적 측면 사이에서 중도를 잡았다."], ["In plain English I stared hard, and said nothing.", "쉬운 우리말로 하면, 나는 눈만 멀뚱멀뚱 뜨고 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["“Let’s extract the inner meaning of this,”", "\"이것의 속뜻을 뽑아내 보세.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“Why did my uncle leave the Diamond to Rachel?", "\"어째서 내 외숙부는 그 다이아몬드를 레이철에게 남겼을까?"], ["Why didn’t he leave it to my aunt?”", "어째서 그것을 내 이모님께 남기지 않았을까?\""]], [["“That’s not beyond guessing, sir, at any rate,” I said.", "\"그거야 적어도 짐작 못 할 일은 아니지요, 도련님.\""], ["“Colonel Herncastle knew my lady well enough to know that she would have refused to accept any legacy that came to her from him.”", "나는 말했다. \"헌캐슬 대령은, 그에게서 오는 유산이라면 무엇이든 마님이 받기를 거절하시리라는 것을 알 만큼 마님을 잘 알고 있었으니까요.\""]], [["“How did he know that Rachel might not refuse to accept it, too?”", "\"레이철도 그것을 받기를 거절할지 모른다는 걸, 그는 어떻게 알았을까?\""]], [["“Is there any young lady in existence, sir, who could resist the temptation of accepting such a birthday present as The Moonstone?”", "\"세상에, 도련님, 월장석 같은 생일 선물을 받고 싶은 유혹을 이겨 낼 수 있는 젊은 아가씨가 어디 있겠습니까요?\""]], [["“That’s the Subjective view,”", "\"그게 바로 주관적 관점일세.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“It does you great credit, Betteredge, to be able to take the Subjective view.", "\"주관적 관점을 취할 줄 알다니, 베터리지, 자네 참 대단하군그래."], ["But there’s another mystery about the Colonel’s legacy which is not accounted for yet.", "하지만 대령의 그 유산에는 아직 설명되지 않은 또 다른 수수께끼가 있어."], ["How are we to explain his only giving Rachel her birthday present conditionally on her mother being alive?”", "그가 레이철에게 생일 선물을 주되 오직 그 어머니가 살아 있어야 한다는 조건을 단 것을, 우리는 어떻게 설명해야 할까?\""]], [["“I don’t want to slander a dead man, sir,”", "\"죽은 사람을 헐뜯고 싶지는 않습니다요, 도련님.\""], ["I answered.", "나는 대답했다."], ["“But if he has purposely left a legacy of trouble and danger to his sister, by the means of her child, it must be a legacy made conditional on his sister’s being alive to feel the vexation of it.”", "\"하지만 그가 만일, 제 누이의 자식을 매개 삼아, 골칫거리와 위험이라는 유산을 누이에게 일부러 남긴 것이라면, 그건 틀림없이 누이가 살아서 그 속을 썩일 수 있어야 한다는 조건을 단 유산이겠지요.\""]], [["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["That’s your interpretation of his motive, is it?", "그게 그의 동기에 대한 자네 풀이란 말이지?"], ["The Subjective interpretation again!", "또 그 주관적 풀이로군!"], ["Have you ever been in Germany, Betteredge?”", "자네 독일에 가 본 적 있나, 베터리지?\""]], [["“No, sir.", "\"없습니다요, 도련님."], ["What’s your interpretation, if you please?”", "그럼 도련님의 풀이는 어떻습니까, 괜찮으시다면?\""]], [["“I can see,” says Mr.", "\"내가 보기엔,\""], ["Franklin, “that the Colonel’s object may, quite possibly, have been—not to benefit his niece, whom he had never even seen—but to prove to his sister that he had died forgiving her, and to prove it very prettily by means of a present made to her child.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"대령의 목적이, 얼마든지 이럴 수도 있어—얼굴 한번 본 적조차 없는 제 조카에게 은혜를 베풀려던 게 아니라, 제가 누이를 용서하며 죽었음을 누이에게 증명해 보이려던 것, 그것도 누이의 자식에게 준 선물을 통해 아주 곱게 증명해 보이려던 것 말이야."], ["There is a totally different explanation from yours, Betteredge, taking its rise in a Subjective-Objective point of view.", "이건 자네 것과는 전혀 다른 설명일세, 베터리지. 주관적-객관적 관점에서 비롯된 설명이지."], ["From all I can see, one interpretation is just as likely to be right as the other.”", "내가 볼 수 있는 한, 어느 한쪽 풀이나 다른 한쪽 풀이나 옳을 법하기는 매한가지야.\""]], [["Having brought matters to this pleasant and comforting issue, Mr. Franklin appeared to think that he had completed all that was required of him.", "이렇게 일을 이 유쾌하고 위안이 되는 결말로 이끌어 놓고서, 프랭클린 도련님은 제게 요구된 바를 죄다 마쳤다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["He laid down flat on his back on the sand, and asked what was to be done next.", "그는 모래 위에 벌렁 나자빠져 등을 대고 눕더니, 다음엔 무엇을 하면 되느냐고 물었다."]], [["He had been so clever, and clear-headed (before he began to talk the foreign gibberish), and had so completely taken the lead in the business up to the present time, that I was quite unprepared for such a sudden change as he now exhibited in this helpless leaning upon me.", "그는 (그 외국식 횡설수설을 늘어놓기 전까지는) 어찌나 영리하고 명민했던지, 또 지금껏 이 일에서 어찌나 완전히 앞장서 왔던지, 이제 이렇게 나한테 속수무책으로 기대는 이 갑작스러운 변화에 나는 전혀 마음의 채비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["It was not till later that I learned—by assistance of Miss Rachel, who was the first to make the discovery—that these puzzling shifts and transformations in Mr. Franklin were due to the effect on him of his foreign training.", "프랭클린 도련님의 이 갈피 못 잡을 변덕과 뒤바뀜이 그가 받은 외국 수련이 그에게 미친 영향 탓임을—레이철 아가씨의 도움으로 알게 되었는데, 이 사실을 맨 먼저 알아낸 이가 아가씨였다—내가 알게 된 것은 한참 뒤의 일이었다."], ["At the age when we are all of us most apt to take our colouring, in the form of a reflection from the colouring of other people, he had been sent abroad, and had been passed on from one nation to another, before there was time for any one colouring more than another to settle itself on him firmly.", "우리가 저마다 가장 쉽게, 남들의 물이 비치듯 그 물을 받아 제 물을 들이는 그 나이에, 그는 외국으로 보내졌고, 어느 한 가지 물이 다른 물보다 그에게 단단히 자리 잡을 겨를도 없이 이 나라에서 저 나라로 넘겨졌다."], ["As a consequence of this, he had come back with so many different sides to his character, all more or less jarring with each other, that he seemed to pass his life in a state of perpetual contradiction with himself.", "그 탓에 그는, 저마다 서로 얼마간 어긋나는 하도 여러 다른 면을 제 됨됨이에 지닌 채 돌아왔으니, 마치 저 자신과 끝없이 모순을 이루는 상태로 평생을 사는 듯했다. 그는 부지런한 사람일 수도, 게으른 사람일 수도 있었다."], ["He could be a busy man, and a lazy man; cloudy in the head, and clear in the head; a model of determination, and a spectacle of helplessness, all together.", "머리가 흐릿할 수도, 머리가 맑을 수도 있었다. 결단의 본보기일 수도, 속수무책의 볼거리일 수도 있었으니, 그 모든 게 한꺼번에였다."], ["He had his French side, and his German side, and his Italian side—the original English foundation showing through, every now and then, as much as to say, “Here I am, sorely transmogrified, as you see, but there’s something of me left at the bottom of him still.”", "그에게는 프랑스식 면이 있고, 독일식 면이 있고, 이탈리아식 면이 있었다—그리고 그 밑바탕의 본디 영국식이 이따금 비쳐 나와, 이를테면 이렇게 말하는 듯했다. \"나 여기 있소."], ["Miss Rachel used to remark that the Italian side of him was uppermost, on those occasions when he unexpectedly gave in, and asked you in his nice sweet-tempered way to take his own responsibilities on your shoulders.", "보다시피 지독히 뒤바뀌긴 했지만, 그래도 이 사람 밑바닥엔 아직 내 것이 얼마쯤 남아 있다오.\" 레이철 아가씨는 늘 말씀하시기를, 그가 뜻밖에 굽히고 들며 그 곱고 상냥한 투로 제 책임을 남의 어깨에 지워 달라 청할 때면, 그의 이탈리아식 면이 가장 위로 올라온 것이라 하셨다."], ["You will do him no injustice, I think, if you conclude that the Italian side of him was uppermost now.", "지금 그의 이탈리아식 면이 가장 위로 올라와 있었다고 여기신들, 내 생각엔, 여러분이 그를 부당하게 여기는 것은 아니리라."]], [["“Isn’t it your business, sir,”", "\"다음에 무엇을 할지 아는 게, 도련님,\""], ["I asked, “to know what to do next?", "하고 나는 물었다. \"도련님이 하실 일 아닙니까요?"], ["Surely it can’t be mine?”", "설마 그게 제 일일 리는 없겠지요?\""]], [["Mr. Franklin didn’t appear to see the force of my question—not being in a position, at the time, to see anything but the sky over his head.", "프랭클린 도련님은 내 물음의 무게를 알아채지 못하는 듯했다—그때 그는 제 머리 위 하늘 말고는 아무것도 볼 처지가 아니었으니까."]], [["“I don’t want to alarm my aunt without reason,”", "\"까닭 없이 이모님을 놀라게 해 드리고 싶진 않아.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“And I don’t want to leave her without what may be a needful warning.", "\"그렇다고 어쩌면 꼭 필요할지도 모를 경고 없이 이모님을 내버려 두고 싶지도 않고."], ["If you were in my place, Betteredge, tell me, in one word, what would you do?”", "자네가 내 처지라면, 베터리지, 한마디로 말해 보게, 자네라면 어찌하겠나?\""]], [["In one word, I told him: “Wait.”", "한마디로, 나는 그에게 말했다. \"기다리시지요.\""]], [["“With all my heart,”", "\"내 진심으로 그러겠네.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“How long?”", "\"얼마나?\""]], [["I proceeded to explain myself.", "나는 내 뜻을 풀어 설명해 나갔다."]], [["“As I understand it, sir,”", "\"제가 이해하기로는, 도련님,\""], ["I said, “somebody is bound to put this plaguy Diamond into Miss Rachel’s hands on her birthday—and you may as well do it as another.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"누군가는 이 성가신 다이아몬드를 레이철 아가씨의 생일에 아가씨 손에 쥐여 드려야 하는데—그 일을 남이 하나 도련님이 하나 매한가지지요."], ["Very good.", "아주 좋습니다."], ["This is the twenty-fifth of May, and the birthday is on the twenty-first of June.", "오늘이 5월 25일이고, 생일은 6월 21일입니다."], ["We have got close on four weeks before us.", "우리한테는 넉 주 가까운 시간이 있어요."], ["Let’s wait and see what happens in that time; and let’s warn my lady, or not, as the circumstances direct us.”", "그동안 무슨 일이 일어나는지 기다려 보시지요. 그리고 마님께 경고를 드리든 안 드리든, 형편이 이끄는 대로 하시고요.\""]], [["“Perfect, Betteredge, as far as it goes!”", "\"완벽해, 베터리지, 거기까지는!\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“But between this and the birthday, what’s to be done with the Diamond?”", "\"하지만 지금부터 그 생일까지, 그 다이아몬드는 어떻게 해 둔다지?\""]], [["“What your father did with it, to be sure, sir!”", "\"도련님 아버님이 그것으로 하신 대로 하셔야지요, 그야!\""], ["I answered.", "나는 대답했다."], ["“Your father put it in the safe keeping of a bank in London.", "\"도련님 아버님은 그것을 런던의 한 은행에 안전히 맡겨 두셨어요."], ["You put in the safe keeping of the bank at Frizinghall.”", "도련님은 그것을 프리징홀의 은행에 안전히 맡겨 두시고요.\""], ["(Frizinghall was our nearest town, and the Bank of England wasn’t safer than the bank there.) “If I were you, sir,”", "(프리징홀은 우리 집에서 가장 가까운 읍이었고, 잉글랜드 은행이라 한들 거기 은행보다 더 안전할 것도 없었다.) \"제가 도련님이라면요, 도련님,\""], ["I added, “I would ride straight away with it to Frizinghall before the ladies come back.”", "하고 나는 덧붙였다. \"숙녀분들이 돌아오시기 전에 그것을 가지고 곧장 프리징홀로 말을 달려가겠습니다요.\""]], [["The prospect of doing something—and, what is more, of doing that something on a horse—brought Mr. Franklin up like lightning from the flat of his back.", "무언가를 한다는 것—더구나, 그것도 말을 타고 한다는 것—의 전망이, 프랭클린 도련님을 등을 대고 누운 자리에서 번갯불처럼 벌떡 일으켜 세웠다."], ["He sprang to his feet, and pulled me up, without ceremony, on to mine.", "그는 두 발로 벌떡 뛰어 일어나더니, 다짜고짜 나를 붙들어 내 두 발로 일으켜 세웠다."], ["“Betteredge, you are worth your weight in gold,”", "\"베터리지, 자네는 제 몸무게만큼 금값을 하는 사람이야.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Come along, and saddle the best horse in the stables directly.”", "\"자, 어서 가서 마구간에서 가장 좋은 말에 당장 안장을 얹게.\""]], [["Here (God bless it!) was the original English foundation of him showing through all the foreign varnish at last!", "여기 (하느님 감사합니다!) 그 온갖 외국물의 광택을 뚫고 마침내 그의 그 본디 영국식 밑바탕이 비쳐 나오고 있었다!"], ["Here was the Master Franklin I remembered, coming out again in the good old way at the prospect of a ride, and reminding me of the good old times!", "여기, 내가 기억하는 그 프랭클린 도련님이, 말을 탄다는 전망에 그 정겨운 옛 모습으로 다시 튀어나와, 내게 그 정겨운 옛 시절을 떠올려 주고 있었다!"], ["Saddle a horse for him?", "그에게 말 한 필에 안장을 얹어 주라고?"], ["I would have saddled a dozen horses, if he could only have ridden them all!", "그가 그 말을 죄다 탈 수만 있다면, 나는 열두 필에라도 안장을 얹어 주었으리라!"]], [["We went back to the house in a hurry; we had the fleetest horse in the stables saddled in a hurry; and Mr.", "우리는 부랴부랴 집으로 돌아갔다. 우리는 부랴부랴 마구간에서 가장 날랜 말에 안장을 얹었다."], ["Franklin rattled off in a hurry, to lodge the cursed Diamond once more in the strongroom of a bank.", "그리고 프랭클린 도련님은, 그 저주받은 다이아몬드를 또 한 번 은행 금고에 맡겨 두려고, 부랴부랴 말을 달려 떠나갔다."], ["When I heard the last of his horse’s hoofs on the drive, and when I turned about in the yard and found I was alone again, I felt half inclined to ask myself if I hadn’t woke up from a dream.", "그의 말발굽 소리가 마찻길에서 마지막으로 잦아드는 것을 듣고, 마당에서 몸을 돌려 다시 나 홀로임을 알았을 때, 나는 내가 혹 꿈에서 깨어난 것은 아닌지 나 자신에게 물어보고 싶은 심정이 반쯤 들었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["While I was in this bewildered frame of mind, sorely needing a little quiet time by myself to put me right again, my daughter Penelope got in my way (just as her late mother used to get in my way on the stairs), and instantly summoned me to tell her all that had passed at the conference between Mr. Franklin and me.", "이렇게 얼떨떨한 심경에 잠겨, 나 자신을 도로 추스르려면 나 혼자 조용한 시간이 좀 절실히 필요하던 참에, 내 딸 페넬로피가 내 앞을 가로막고서 (꼭 제 죽은 어미가 계단에서 내 앞을 가로막곤 하던 것처럼) 대뜸, 프랭클린 도련님과 나 사이의 그 담판에서 오간 이야기를 죄다 저한테 들려 달라고 졸랐다."], ["Under present circumstances, the one thing to be done was to clap the extinguisher upon Penelope’s curiosity on the spot.", "지금 형편에서 할 수 있는 단 한 가지는, 페넬로피의 호기심에 그 자리에서 뚜껑을 덮어 버리는 것이었다."], ["I accordingly replied that Mr. Franklin and I had both talked of foreign politics, till we could talk no longer, and had then mutually fallen asleep in the heat of the sun.", "그리하여 나는, 프랭클린 도련님과 내가 둘 다 외국 정치 이야기를 하도 오래 나누다가 더는 할 말이 없어져서, 그다음엔 볕이 뜨거워 서로 잠이 들어 버렸다고 대답했다."], ["Try that sort of answer when your wife or your daughter next worries you with an awkward question at an awkward time, and depend on the natural sweetness of women for kissing and making it up again at the next opportunity.", "다음번에 여러분의 아내나 딸이 곤란한 때에 곤란한 물음으로 여러분을 들볶거든 그런 식의 대답을 한번 써 보시라. 그리고 다음 기회에 입 맞추며 다시 화해하는 일일랑 여자의 그 타고난 상냥함에 맡겨 두시라."]], [["The afternoon wore on, and my lady and Miss Rachel came back.", "오후가 저물어 가고, 마님과 레이철 아가씨가 돌아오셨다."]], [["Needless to say how astonished they were, when they heard that Mr. Franklin Blake had arrived, and had gone off again on horseback. Needless also to say, that they asked awkward questions directly, and that the “foreign politics” and the “falling asleep in the sun” wouldn’t serve a second time over with them. Being at the end of my invention, I said Mr. Franklin’s arrival by the early train was entirely attributable to one of Mr. Franklin’s freaks. Being asked, upon that, whether his galloping off again on horseback was another of Mr. Franklin’s freaks, I said, “Yes, it was;” and slipped out of it—I think very cleverly—in that way.", "프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 왔다가 다시 말을 타고 떠나 버렸다는 말을 들었을 때 두 분이 얼마나 놀라셨는지는 굳이 말할 것도 없다. 또한, 그 두 분이 대뜸 곤란한 물음들을 던지셨다는 것도, 그리고 \"외국 정치\"니 \"볕에 잠이 들었다\"느니 하는 것이 그 두 분한테는 두 번씩이나 통하지 않았다는 것도, 굳이 말할 것도 없다. 내 꾀가 바닥이 나서, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 이른 기차로 온 것은 순전히 프랭클린 도련님 특유의 변덕 탓이라고 둘러댔다. 그러자, 그가 다시 말을 타고 내달려 간 것도 프랭클린 도련님의 또 다른 변덕이냐고 물으시기에, 나는 \"예, 그렇습니다.\" 하고는—내 생각엔 아주 영리하게—그런 식으로 슬쩍 빠져나갔다."]], [["Having got over my difficulties with the ladies, I found more difficulties waiting for me when I went back to my own room.", "숙녀분들과의 곤경을 넘기고 나니, 내 방으로 돌아왔을 때 더 많은 곤경이 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["In came Penelope—with the natural sweetness of women—to kiss and make it up again; and—with the natural curiosity of women—to ask another question.", "페넬로피가 들어왔다—여자의 그 타고난 상냥함으로 다시 입 맞추며 화해하려고, 그리고—여자의 그 타고난 호기심으로 또 하나의 물음을 던지려고."], ["This time she only wanted me to tell her what was the matter with our second housemaid, Rosanna Spearman.", "이번엔 그저, 우리 둘째 하녀 로재나 스피어먼이 대체 무슨 일이냐고 내게 말해 달라는 것이었다."]], [["After leaving Mr. Franklin and me at the Shivering Sand, Rosanna, it appeared, had returned to the house in a very unaccountable state of mind.", "떠는 모래펄에서 프랭클린 도련님과 나를 두고 떠난 뒤, 로재나는, 알고 보니, 참으로 알 수 없는 심경으로 집에 돌아왔더란다."], ["She had turned (if Penelope was to be believed) all the colours of the rainbow.", "그녀는 (페넬로피의 말을 믿는다면) 무지개 빛깔을 죄다 띠었다."], ["She had been merry without reason, and sad without reason.", "까닭 없이 들떴다가, 까닭 없이 시무룩했다."], ["In one breath she asked hundreds of questions about Mr. Franklin Blake, and in another breath she had been angry with Penelope for presuming to suppose that a strange gentleman could possess any interest for her.", "한 숨결로는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에 관해 수백 가지 물음을 던지다가, 또 한 숨결로는, 낯선 신사가 저한테 무슨 관심거리가 될 수 있으리라 지레짐작한다고 페넬로피에게 화를 냈다."], ["She had been surprised, smiling, and scribbling Mr. Franklin’s name inside her workbox.", "놀란 얼굴로 미소를 지으며 제 반짇고리 속에 프랭클린 도련님의 이름을 끄적거리기도 했다."], ["She had been surprised again, crying and looking at her deformed shoulder in the glass.", "또 놀란 얼굴로 울면서 거울에 비친 제 기형인 어깨를 들여다보기도 했다."], ["Had she and Mr. Franklin known anything of each other before today?", "그녀와 프랭클린 도련님이 오늘 이전에 서로에 대해 무언가 알고 있었을까?"], ["Quite impossible!", "도무지 있을 수 없는 일이었다!"], ["Had they heard anything of each other?", "서로에 대해 무언가 들은 적이 있었을까?"], ["Impossible again! I could speak to Mr.", "이 또한 있을 수 없는 일이었다!"], ["Franklin’s astonishment as genuine, when he saw how the girl stared at him.", "나는, 그 처녀가 저를 빤히 쳐다보는 것을 보았을 때 프랭클린 도련님이 지은 놀라움이 진짜였다고 증언할 수 있다."], ["Penelope could speak to the girl’s inquisitiveness as genuine, when she asked questions about Mr. Franklin.", "페넬로피는, 그녀가 프랭클린 도련님에 관해 물었을 때 그 캐묻는 마음이 진짜였다고 증언할 수 있었다."], ["The conference between us, conducted in this way, was tiresome enough, until my daughter suddenly ended it by bursting out with what I thought the most monstrous supposition I had ever heard in my life.", "이런 식으로 이어진 우리 사이의 담판은 여간 지겨운 것이 아니었는데, 마침내 내 딸이, 내 평생 들어 본 것 중 가장 터무니없다 싶은 억측을 불쑥 내뱉으며 그것을 끝장내 버렸다."]], [["“Father!”", "\"아버지!\""], ["says Penelope, quite seriously, “there’s only one explanation of it.", "페넬로피가, 자못 진지하게 말한다. \"이건 설명이 딱 하나뿐이에요."], ["Rosanna has fallen in love with Mr. Franklin Blake at first sight!”", "로재나가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨한테 첫눈에 반해 버린 거라고요!\""]], [["You have heard of beautiful young ladies falling in love at first sight, and have thought it natural enough.", "아름다운 젊은 아가씨가 첫눈에 사랑에 빠졌다는 이야기라면 여러분도 들어 보셨을 테고, 그것을 제법 자연스러운 일로 여기셨을 것이다."], ["But a housemaid out of a reformatory, with a plain face and a deformed shoulder, falling in love, at first sight, with a gentleman who comes on a visit to her mistress’s house, match me that, in the way of an absurdity, out of any story-book in Christendom, if you can!", "그러나 갱생원 출신에, 못생긴 얼굴에, 기형인 어깨를 가진 하녀가, 제 여주인 집에 손님으로 온 신사한테 첫눈에 사랑에 빠졌다니—이만한 황당함을, 온 기독교 세계의 그 어떤 이야기책에서든 어디 한번 나한테 견주어 대 보시라, 하실 수 있거든!"], ["I laughed till the tears rolled down my cheeks.", "나는 두 뺨에 눈물이 흘러내리도록 웃었다."], ["Penelope resented my merriment, in rather a strange way.", "페넬로피는 내 웃음을, 자못 묘한 식으로 언짢아했다."], ["“I never knew you cruel before, father,”", "\"아버지가 이렇게 모진 분인 줄은 여태 몰랐어요.\""], ["she said, very gently, and went out.", "그 애는 아주 나직이 말하고는, 나가 버렸다."]], [["My girl’s words fell upon me like a splash of cold water.", "내 딸의 말이 마치 찬물 한 바가지처럼 내게 끼얹어졌다."], ["I was savage with myself, for feeling uneasy in myself the moment she had spoken them—but so it was.", "그 애가 그 말을 내뱉은 순간 내 속이 어쩐지 편치 않아지는 것에, 나는 나 자신에게 화가 났다—그러나 사실이 그러했다."], ["We will change the subject, if you please.", "여러분이 괜찮으시다면, 우리 이 이야기는 그만 접기로 하자."], ["I am sorry I drifted into writing about it; and not without reason, as you will see when we have gone on together a little longer.", "내가 어쩌다 이 이야기를 쓰게 되어 미안하다. 그리고 그것이 까닭 없는 미안함이 아님은, 우리가 조금 더 함께 나아가 보면 여러분도 아시게 될 것이다."]], [["The evening came, and the dressing-bell for dinner rang, before Mr. Franklin returned from Frizinghall.", "저녁이 되었고, 프랭클린 도련님이 프리징홀에서 돌아오기 전에 저녁 식사를 알리는 옷 갈아입을 종이 울렸다."], ["I took his hot water up to his room myself, expecting to hear, after this extraordinary delay, that something had happened.", "나는 그의 세숫물을 손수 그의 방으로 들고 올라가며, 이 유별난 지체 끝에, 무슨 일이라도 일어났다는 말을 듣게 되려니 여겼다."], ["To my great disappointment (and no doubt to yours also), nothing had happened.", "나로서는 여간 실망스럽게도 (그리고 틀림없이 여러분에게도), 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["He had not met with the Indians, either going or returning.", "그는 갈 때도 올 때도 그 인도인들과 마주치지 않았다."], ["He had deposited the Moonstone in the bank—describing it merely as a valuable of great price—and he had got the receipt for it safe in his pocket.", "그는 그 월장석을 은행에 맡겼고—그저 값비싼 귀중품이라고만 이르고서—그 보관증을 제 주머니에 안전히 챙겨 넣었다."], ["I went downstairs, feeling that this was rather a flat ending, after all our excitement about the Diamond earlier in the day.", "나는 아래층으로 내려가며, 낮에 그 다이아몬드를 두고 그토록 들떴던 것에 비하면 이건 어지간히 맥 빠지는 결말이라는 생각이 들었다."]], [["How the meeting between Mr. Franklin and his aunt and cousin went off, is more than I can tell you.", "프랭클린 도련님과 그 이모, 그리고 사촌 사이의 만남이 어떻게 흘러갔는지는, 내가 여러분께 말씀드릴 수 있는 바를 넘어선다."]], [["I would have given something to have waited at table that day.", "그날 식탁 시중을 들 수만 있었다면 나는 무엇이라도 내놓았을 것이다."], ["But, in my position in the household, waiting at dinner (except on high family festivals) was letting down my dignity in the eyes of the other servants—a thing which my lady considered me quite prone enough to do already, without seeking occasions for it.", "그러나 이 집에서 내 자리로 말하면, 저녁 식사 시중을 드는 일은 (집안의 큰 잔치가 아니고서는) 다른 하인들 눈에 내 위엄을 떨어뜨리는 짓이었다—마님이 여기시기로, 내가 굳이 그럴 기회를 찾아 나서지 않아도 이미 넉넉히 그러기 쉬운 사람이었으니 말이다."], ["The news brought to me from the upper regions, that evening, came from Penelope and the footman.", "그날 저녁 위쪽 세상에서 내게 전해진 소식은, 페넬로피와 하인에게서 온 것이었다."], ["Penelope mentioned that she had never known Miss Rachel so particular about the dressing of her hair, and had never seen her look so bright and pretty as she did when she went down to meet Mr. Franklin in the drawing-room.", "페넬로피의 말이, 레이철 아가씨가 그토록 머리 손질에 까다롭게 구시는 것은 여태 본 적이 없고, 응접실로 프랭클린 도련님을 맞으러 내려가실 때만큼 그토록 환하고 어여쁘신 것도 여태 본 적이 없다는 것이었다."], ["The footman’s report was, that the preservation of a respectful composure in the presence of his betters, and the waiting on Mr. Franklin Blake at dinner, were two of the hardest things to reconcile with each other that had ever tried his training in service.", "하인의 보고인즉, 윗분들 앞에서 공손한 침착함을 지키는 일과, 저녁 식탁에서 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 시중을 드는 일, 이 둘을 서로 아우르는 것이야말로 여태껏 제 시중 수련을 시험한 가장 어려운 일 축에 든다는 것이었다."], ["Later in the evening, we heard them singing and playing duets, Mr. Franklin piping high, Miss Rachel piping higher, and my lady, on the piano, following them as it were over hedge and ditch, and seeing them safe through it in a manner most wonderful and pleasant to hear through the open windows, on the terrace at night.", "저녁이 이슥해지자, 우리는 그분들이 노래하고 이중주를 연주하는 소리를 들었으니, 프랭클린 도련님은 높이 소리를 뽑고, 레이철 아가씨는 더 높이 뽑고, 마님은 피아노로 그분들을 이를테면 산울타리와 도랑을 넘도록 뒤따르며, 밤의 테라스에서 열린 창을 통해 듣기에 더없이 놀랍고 유쾌하게, 그분들을 무사히 그 곡을 헤쳐 나가게 이끄셨다."], ["Later still, I went to Mr. Franklin in the smoking-room, with the soda water and brandy, and found that Miss Rachel had put the Diamond clean out of his head.", "그보다 더 이슥해서, 나는 소다수와 브랜디를 들고 흡연실의 프랭클린 도련님께 갔는데, 레이철 아가씨가 그 다이아몬드를 그의 머릿속에서 말끔히 몰아내 버린 것을 알았다."], ["“She’s the most charming girl I have seen since I came back to England!” was all I could extract from him, when I endeavoured to lead the conversation to more serious things.", "내가 이야기를 좀 더 진지한 쪽으로 이끌어 보려 애썼을 때 그에게서 끄집어낼 수 있었던 것이라곤 \"그녀는 내가 영국에 돌아온 뒤로 본 가장 매혹적인 아가씨야!\"라는 말뿐이었다."]], [["Towards midnight, I went round the house to lock up, accompanied by my second in command (Samuel, the footman), as usual.", "자정 무렵, 나는 여느 때처럼 내 부관(하인 새뮤얼)을 데리고, 문단속을 하러 집을 한 바퀴 돌았다."], ["When all the doors were made fast, except the side door that opened on the terrace, I sent Samuel to bed, and stepped out for a breath of fresh air before I too went to bed in my turn.", "테라스로 통하는 옆문만 빼고 모든 문을 단단히 잠근 뒤, 나는 새뮤얼을 자러 보내고, 나도 내 차례로 잠자리에 들기 전에 바깥바람을 좀 쐬려고 밖으로 나섰다."]], [["The night was still and close, and the moon was at the full in the heavens.", "밤은 고요하고 후텁지근했으며, 하늘에는 보름달이 걸려 있었다."], ["It was so silent out of doors, that I heard from time to time, very faint and low, the fall of the sea, as the ground-swell heaved it in on the sand-bank near the mouth of our little bay.", "바깥이 어찌나 괴괴하던지, 나는 이따금, 아주 희미하고 나직하게, 너울이 우리 작은 만 어귀 가까이 모래톱으로 물을 밀어 올릴 때 바닷물이 내려앉는 소리를 들었다."], ["As the house stood, the terrace side was the dark side; but the broad moonlight showed fair on the gravel walk that ran along the next side to the terrace.", "집이 놓인 모양새로 보면, 테라스 쪽이 어두운 쪽이었으나, 넓은 달빛이 테라스 다음 쪽을 따라 난 자갈길 위에 훤히 비치고 있었다."], ["Looking this way, after looking up at the sky, I saw the shadow of a person in the moonlight thrown forward from behind the corner of the house.", "하늘을 올려다본 뒤 이쪽을 바라보니, 집 모퉁이 뒤에서 앞으로 드리워진 사람 그림자 하나가 달빛 속에 보였다."]], [["Being old and sly, I forbore to call out; but being also, unfortunately, old and heavy, my feet betrayed me on the gravel.", "나이 든 데다 능글맞은지라, 나는 소리쳐 부르는 것을 참았다. 그러나 불행히도 나이 든 데다 몸도 무거운지라, 자갈길에서 내 발이 나를 배신했다."], ["Before I could steal suddenly round the corner, as I had proposed, I heard lighter feet than mine—and more than one pair of them as I thought—retreating in a hurry.", "내가 애초 작정한 대로 그 모퉁이를 슬며시 돌아 나가기도 전에, 나는 내 것보다 가벼운 발소리가—게다가 내 생각엔 한 사람이 아닌 여럿의 발소리가—부랴부랴 물러나는 것을 들었다."], ["By the time I had got to the corner, the trespassers, whoever they were, had run into the shrubbery at the off side of the walk, and were hidden from sight among the thick trees and bushes in that part of the grounds.", "내가 그 모퉁이에 이르렀을 무렵, 그 무단 침입자들은, 그게 누구든 간에, 길 저쪽 관목숲으로 뛰어들어, 영지 그 언저리의 우거진 나무와 덤불 사이로 자취를 감춘 뒤였다."], ["From the shrubbery, they could easily make their way, over our fence into the road.", "그 관목숲에서라면, 그들은 우리 울타리를 넘어 손쉽게 한길로 빠져나갈 수 있었다."], ["If I had been forty years younger, I might have had a chance of catching them before they got clear of our premises.", "내가 마흔 살만 젊었던들, 그들이 우리 집 경내를 벗어나기 전에 붙잡을 승산이 있었을지도 모른다."], ["As it was, I went back to set a-going a younger pair of legs than mine.", "실상은 그러하여, 나는 내 것보다 젊은 두 다리를 부리려고 되돌아갔다."], ["Without disturbing anybody, Samuel and I got a couple of guns, and went all round the house and through the shrubbery.", "아무도 깨우지 않은 채, 새뮤얼과 나는 총을 두 자루 챙겨 들고, 집을 한 바퀴 돌고 관목숲을 헤집었다."], ["Having made sure that no persons were lurking about anywhere in our grounds, we turned back.", "우리 영지 어디에도 숨어 있는 자가 없음을 확인하고서, 우리는 발길을 돌렸다."], ["Passing over the walk where I had seen the shadow, I now noticed, for the first time, a little bright object, lying on the clean gravel, under the light of the moon.", "아까 그 그림자를 보았던 길을 지나다가, 나는 이제야 처음으로, 달빛 아래 깨끗한 자갈 위에 놓인 조그맣고 반짝이는 물건 하나를 알아보았다."], ["Picking the object up, I discovered it was a small bottle, containing a thick sweet-smelling liquor, as black as ink.", "그 물건을 집어 들어 보니, 그것은 잉크처럼 새까맣고 걸쭉하며 달콤한 냄새가 나는 액체가 담긴 조그만 병이었다."]], [["I said nothing to Samuel.", "나는 새뮤얼에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["But, remembering what Penelope had told me about the jugglers, and the pouring of the little pool of ink into the palm of the boy’s hand, I instantly suspected that I had disturbed the three Indians, lurking about the house, and bent, in their heathenish way, on discovering the whereabouts of the Diamond that night.", "그러나 페넬로피가 그 요술쟁이들에 관해, 그리고 소년의 손바닥에 조그맣게 잉크를 고이도록 부었다던 이야기를 내게 들려준 것을 떠올리며, 나는 대뜸 의심했다. 내가, 집 언저리에 숨어, 저희 이교도다운 방식으로 그날 밤 그 다이아몬드가 어디 있는지 알아내려 벼르던 그 인도인 셋을 방해한 것이라고."]], [["Here, for one moment, I find it necessary to call a halt.", "여기서, 잠깐, 나는 한 번 멈춰 서야겠다."]], [["On summoning up my own recollections—and on getting Penelope to help me, by consulting her journal—I find that we may pass pretty rapidly over the interval between Mr. Franklin Blake’s arrival and Miss Rachel’s birthday.", "내 나름의 기억을 더듬어 보고—그리고 페넬로피더러 제 일기를 뒤져 나를 돕게 한 결과—나는, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 도착과 레이철 아가씨의 생일 사이의 그 사이 기간은 제법 빠르게 지나쳐도 되겠다는 것을 알았다."], ["For the greater part of that time the days passed, and brought nothing with them worth recording.", "그 시간의 대부분은 날들이 그저 지나갔을 뿐, 적어 둘 만한 것을 하나도 데려오지 않았다."], ["With your good leave, then, and with Penelope’s help, I shall notice certain dates only in this place; reserving to myself to tell the story day by day, once more, as soon as we get to the time when the business of the Moonstone became the chief business of everybody in our house.", "그러니 여러분이 허락하신다면, 그리고 페넬로피의 도움을 받아, 나는 이 대목에서는 몇몇 날짜만 짚어 두려 한다. 월장석 사건이 우리 집 모든 사람의 첫째가는 큰일이 된 그 시점에 이르면, 다시 한번 날마다 하나하나 이야기해 나가는 몫은 나 자신을 위해 남겨 두기로 하고."]], [["This said, we may now go on again—beginning, of course, with the bottle of sweet-smelling ink which I found on the gravel walk at night.", "이 말을 해 두었으니, 이제 우리 다시 나아가도 되겠다—물론, 내가 밤에 자갈길에서 주운 그 달콤한 냄새 나는 잉크병에서부터 시작하기로 하고."]], [["On the next morning (the morning of the twenty-sixth) I showed Mr. Franklin this article of jugglery, and told him what I have already told you.", "이튿날 아침(26일 아침)에 나는 프랭클린 도련님께 이 요술 물건을 보여 주며, 내가 이미 여러분께 들려드린 것을 그에게 이야기했다."], ["His opinion was, not only that the Indians had been lurking about after the Diamond, but also that they were actually foolish enough to believe in their own magic—meaning thereby the making of signs on a boy’s head, and the pouring of ink into a boy’s hand, and then expecting him to see persons and things beyond the reach of human vision. In our country, as well as in the East, Mr.", "그의 생각인즉, 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 노려 언저리에 숨어 있었을 뿐 아니라, 실제로 어리석게도 저희 요술을 스스로 믿을 만큼이었다는 것이다—여기서 요술이란, 소년의 머리에 표시를 하고 소년의 손에 잉크를 붓고서, 그런 다음 그 아이가 사람의 눈이 미치지 못하는 곳의 사람과 사물을 보아 주기를 기대하는 것을 말한다."], ["Franklin informed me, there are people who practise this curious hocus-pocus (without the ink, however); and who call it by a French name, signifying something like brightness of sight.", "프랭클린 도련님이 일러 주기로, 동방뿐 아니라 우리나라에도 이 괴상한 눈속임을 부리는 사람들이 있는데 (다만 잉크는 없이), 그들은 그것을 프랑스말로 부르며, 그것은 이를테면 밝은 눈이라는 뜻이라고 했다. \"틀림없어.\""], ["“Depend upon it,” says Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["Franklin, “the Indians took it for granted that we should keep the Diamond here; and they brought their clairvoyant boy to show them the way to it, if they succeeded in getting into the house last night.”", "\"인도인들은 우리가 그 다이아몬드를 여기 둘 것이라고 당연시했어. 그래서 어젯밤 집 안에 들어드는 데 성공하면 그 투시하는 소년더러 다이아몬드로 가는 길을 알려 달라고 데려온 거지.\""]], [["“Do you think they’ll try again, sir?”", "\"그자들이 또 시도할 것 같습니까요, 도련님?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“It depends,” says Mr.", "\"그거야 달렸지.\""], ["Franklin, “on what the boy can really do.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["If he can see the Diamond through the iron safe of the bank at Frizinghall, we shall be troubled with no more visits from the Indians for the present.", "\"그 소년이 실제로 무엇을 할 수 있느냐에. 만일 그 애가 프리징홀 은행의 그 철제 금고를 뚫고 다이아몬드를 볼 수 있다면, 우리는 당분간 인도인들의 방문에 더는 시달리지 않을 걸세."], ["If he can’t, we shall have another chance of catching them in the shrubbery, before many more nights are over our heads.”", "만일 못 본다면, 우리 머리 위로 여러 밤이 더 지나가기 전에, 관목숲에서 그자들을 붙잡을 또 한 번의 기회가 있을 게고.\""]], [["I waited pretty confidently for that latter chance; but, strange to relate, it never came.", "나는 그 뒤엣것, 곧 붙잡을 기회를 제법 자신 있게 기다렸다. 그러나, 이상하게도, 그것은 끝내 오지 않았다."]], [["Whether the jugglers heard, in the town, of Mr. Franklin having been seen at the bank, and drew their conclusions accordingly; or whether the boy really did see the Diamond where the Diamond was now lodged (which I, for one, flatly disbelieve); or whether, after all, it was a mere effect of chance, this at any rate is the plain truth—not the ghost of an Indian came near the house again, through the weeks that passed before Miss Rachel’s birthday.", "요술쟁이들이 읍내에서 프랭클린 도련님이 은행에 나타났다는 말을 듣고 그에 맞춰 결론을 내렸는지, 아니면 그 소년이 정말로 그 다이아몬드가 지금 맡겨져 있는 곳에서 그것을 보았는지(나로 말하면 이것은 딱 잘라 못 믿겠다만), 아니면 결국 한낱 우연의 조화였는지—어쨌거나 이것만은 엄연한 사실이다."], ["The jugglers remained in and about the town plying their trade; and Mr.", "레이철 아가씨의 생일에 앞선 그 몇 주 동안, 인도인의 그림자조차 다시는 집 근처에 얼씬하지 않았다는 것 말이다."], ["Franklin and I remained waiting to see what might happen, and resolute not to put the rogues on their guard by showing our suspicions of them too soon.", "요술쟁이들은 읍내와 그 언저리에 머물며 저희 장사를 이어 갔고, 프랭클린 도련님과 나는, 무슨 일이 일어날지 두고 보며 기다리되, 우리의 의심을 너무 일찍 내비쳐 그 악당들에게 경계심을 심어 주지 않기로 굳게 마음먹고 있었다."], ["With this report of the proceedings on either side, ends all that I have to say about the Indians for the present.", "양쪽에서 벌어진 일에 대한 이 보고를 끝으로, 인도인들에 관해 당분간 내가 할 이야기는 다 마친다."]], [["On the twenty-ninth of the month, Miss Rachel and Mr. Franklin hit on a new method of working their way together through the time which might otherwise have hung heavy on their hands.", "그달 29일에, 레이철 아가씨와 프랭클린 도련님은, 그러지 않았으면 손안에서 지루하게 늘어졌을 그 시간을 함께 헤쳐 나갈 새로운 방편을 하나 찾아냈다."], ["There are reasons for taking particular notice here of the occupation that amused them.", "여기서 그 두 사람을 재미나게 한 그 소일거리를 각별히 눈여겨 두어야 할 까닭이 있다."], ["You will find it has a bearing on something that is still to come.", "그것이 아직 오지 않은 어떤 일과 관련이 있음을 여러분은 알게 되실 것이다."]], [["Gentlefolks in general have a very awkward rock ahead in life—the rock ahead of their own idleness.", "대체로 지체 높은 분들에게는 인생에 아주 성가신 암초가 하나 앞에 놓여 있다—바로 저 자신의 무료함이라는 암초다."], ["Their lives being, for the most part, passed in looking about them for something to do, it is curious to see—especially when their tastes are of what is called the intellectual sort—how often they drift blindfold into some nasty pursuit.", "그분들의 삶은 대개, 무언가 할 일이 없나 하고 주위를 두리번거리며 지나가는지라, 보고 있노라면 참으로 묘하다—더구나 그 취향이 이른바 지적이라는 부류일 때면—얼마나 자주 그분들이 눈을 가린 채 어떤 고약한 소일거리로 떠내려가는지 말이다."], ["Nine times out of ten they take to torturing something, or to spoiling something—and they firmly believe they are improving their minds, when the plain truth is, they are only making a mess in the house.", "열에 아홉은 무언가를 못살게 굴거나 무언가를 망가뜨리는 데 손을 대고서는—그러면서 저희가 제 정신을 갈고닦고 있다고 굳게 믿는데, 엄연한 사실인즉 그저 집안을 어지럽히고 있을 따름이다."], ["I have seen them (ladies, I am sorry to say, as well as gentlemen) go out, day after day, for example, with empty pill-boxes, and catch newts, and beetles, and spiders, and frogs, and come home and stick pins through the miserable wretches, or cut them up, without a pang of remorse, into little pieces.", "나는 그분들이 (숙녀분들도, 이런 말씀 드리기 안됐습니다만, 신사분들 못지않게) 이를테면 날마다 빈 알약통을 들고 나가, 영원(蠑螈)이며 딱정벌레며 거미며 개구리를 잡아 와서는, 그 가엾은 것들의 몸에 핀을 꿰거나, 눈곱만한 뉘우침도 없이 조각조각 잘라 놓는 것을 보아 왔다."], ["You see my young master, or my young mistress, poring over one of their spiders’ insides with a magnifying-glass; or you meet one of their frogs walking downstairs without his head—and when you wonder what this cruel nastiness means, you are told that it means a taste in my young master or my young mistress for natural history.", "우리 젊은 도련님이나 우리 젊은 아가씨가 확대경을 들이대고 그분들 거미 중 하나의 뱃속을 들여다보고 있는 꼴을 여러분은 보게 되고, 아니면 그분들 개구리 중 하나가 제 머리도 없이 계단을 걸어 내려오는 것과 마주치게 된다—그리고 이 잔인한 고약함이 무슨 뜻이냐고 여러분이 의아해하면, 그것이 우리 젊은 도련님이나 우리 젊은 아가씨의 박물학 취미를 뜻하는 것이라는 대답을 듣게 된다."], ["Sometimes, again, you see them occupied for hours together in spoiling a pretty flower with pointed instruments, out of a stupid curiosity to know what the flower is made of.", "또 어떤 때는, 그 꽃이 무엇으로 이루어졌는지 알고 싶다는 어리석은 호기심에서, 뾰족한 연장으로 예쁜 꽃 한 송이를 망가뜨리며 몇 시간이고 매달려 있는 것을 보게 된다."], ["Is its colour any prettier, or its scent any sweeter, when you do know? But there!", "막상 알고 나면 그 빛깔이 조금이라도 더 예뻐지던가, 그 향기가 조금이라도 더 향긋해지던가?"], ["the poor souls must get through the time, you see—they must get through the time.", "하지만 어쩌겠는가! 가엾은 분들은, 보시다시피, 그 시간을 어떻게든 때워야 하는 것을—그 시간을 어떻게든 때워야 하는 것을."], ["You dabbled in nasty mud, and made pies, when you were a child; and you dabble in nasty science, and dissect spiders, and spoil flowers, when you grow up.", "여러분도 어릴 적엔 고약한 진흙을 주무르며 흙떡을 빚었고, 어른이 되어서는 고약한 과학을 주무르며 거미를 해부하고 꽃을 망가뜨리는 것이다."], ["In the one case and in the other, the secret of it is, that you have got nothing to think of in your poor empty head, and nothing to do with your poor idle hands.", "이 경우나 저 경우나, 그 속내인즉, 여러분의 가엾고 텅 빈 머리에는 생각할 것이 아무것도 없고, 여러분의 가엾고 한가한 손에는 할 것이 아무것도 없다는 것이다."], ["And so it ends in your spoiling canvas with paints, and making a smell in the house; or in keeping tadpoles in a glass box full of dirty water, and turning everybody’s stomach in the house; or in chipping off bits of stone here, there, and everywhere, and dropping grit into all the victuals in the house; or in staining your fingers in the pursuit of photography, and doing justice without mercy on everybody’s face in the house.", "그리하여 그것은 결국, 물감으로 화폭을 망치며 집안에 냄새를 피우는 것으로, 아니면 더러운 물이 가득한 유리 상자에 올챙이를 길러 온 집안 식구의 속을 뒤집어 놓는 것으로, 아니면 여기저기, 아무 데서나 돌 조각을 쪼아 내어 집안의 온갖 먹을거리에 모래알을 떨어뜨리는 것으로, 아니면 사진 놀음을 좇느라 손가락에 얼룩을 묻히고 온 집안 식구의 얼굴을 사정없이 있는 그대로 박아 내는 것으로 끝이 난다."], ["It often falls heavy enough, no doubt, on people who are really obliged to get their living, to be forced to work for the clothes that cover them, the roof that shelters them, and the food that keeps them going.", "제 밥벌이를 정말로 해야만 하는 사람들, 곧 제 몸을 덮을 옷과 제 몸을 가릴 지붕과 제 목숨을 이어 줄 밥을 위해 억지로 일해야만 하는 사람들에게는, 그 일이 흔히 어지간히 고되게 내리누르는 것도 틀림없다."], ["But compare the hardest day’s work you ever did with the idleness that splits flowers and pokes its way into spiders’ stomachs, and thank your stars that your head has got something it must think of, and your hands something that they must do.", "그러나 여러분이 여태 해 본 가장 고된 하루 노동을, 꽃을 쪼개고 거미 뱃속을 쑤셔 대는 그 무료함과 견주어 보고서, 여러분의 머리에는 반드시 생각해야만 할 무언가가 있고 여러분의 손에는 반드시 해야만 할 무언가가 있음을 여러분의 별들에게 감사하시라."]], [["As for Mr. Franklin and Miss Rachel, they tortured nothing, I am glad to say.", "프랭클린 도련님과 레이철 아가씨로 말하면, 다행히도 그분들은 아무것도 못살게 굴지 않았다."], ["They simply confined themselves to making a mess; and all they spoilt, to do them justice, was the panelling of a door.", "그분들은 그저 어지르는 것으로 그쳤고, 그분들이 망가뜨린 것이라곤, 공평히 말하자면, 문짝 하나의 널판때기뿐이었다."]], [["Mr. Franklin’s universal genius, dabbling in everything, dabbled in what he called “decorative painting.”", "무엇에나 손을 대는 프랭클린 도련님의 그 만능 재주가, 이번엔 그가 \"장식 그림\"이라 부르는 것에 손을 댔다."], ["He had invented, he informed us, a new mixture to moisten paint with, which he described as a “vehicle.”", "그는, 물감을 개는 데 쓰는 새로운 혼합물을 발명했노라 우리에게 일러 주며, 그것을 \"매재(媒材)\"라고 일컬었다."], ["What it was made of, I don’t know.", "그것이 무엇으로 만들어졌는지 나는 모른다."], ["What it did, I can tell you in two words—it stank. Miss Rachel being wild to try her hand at the new process, Mr.", "그것이 무슨 일을 했는지는 두 마디로 말씀드릴 수 있다—고약한 악취를 풍겼다."], ["Franklin sent to London for the materials; mixed them up, with accompaniment of a smell which made the very dogs sneeze when they came into the room; put an apron and a bib over Miss Rachel’s gown, and set her to work decorating her own little sitting-room—called, for want of English to name it in, her “boudoir.”", "레이철 아가씨가 그 새 기법에 손수 도전해 보고 싶어 안달이 나자, 프랭클린 도련님은 런던으로 재료를 주문했다. 그러고는 방에 들어오는 개들마저 재채기하게 만드는 냄새를 곁들여 그것들을 개어, 레이철 아가씨의 겉옷 위에 앞치마와 턱받이를 두르게 하고, 아가씨를 앉혀 아가씨 자신의 조그만 거실을—우리말로 부를 마땅한 말이 없어 그저 아가씨의 \"부두아르(규방)\"라고 불렀는데—꾸미는 일을 시켰다."], ["They began with the inside of the door.", "그분들은 문 안쪽부터 시작했다."], ["Mr. Franklin scraped off all the nice varnish with pumice-stone, and made what he described as a surface to work on.", "프랭클린 도련님은 속돌로 그 고운 광택칠을 죄다 벗겨 내어, 그가 이르기로 작업할 바탕을 만들었다."], ["Miss Rachel then covered the surface, under his directions and with his help, with patterns and devices—griffins, birds, flowers, cupids, and such like—copied from designs made by a famous Italian painter, whose name escapes me: the one, I mean, who stocked the world with Virgin Maries, and had a sweetheart at the baker’s.", "그러자 레이철 아가씨는, 그의 지시에 따라 그의 도움을 받아 가며, 그 바탕을 온갖 무늬와 도안으로—그리핀이며 새며 꽃이며 큐피드며 그런 것들로—뒤덮었으니, 이름은 잊었으나 어느 유명한 이탈리아 화가가 그린 도안을 본뜬 것이었다. 내 말은, 세상을 성모 마리아상으로 가득 채우고 빵집에 애인을 두었다던 바로 그 사람 말이다."], ["Viewed as work, this decoration was slow to do, and dirty to deal with.", "일로 보자면, 이 장식은 하는 데 더디고 다루기에 지저분했다."], ["But our young lady and gentleman never seemed to tire of it.", "그러나 우리 젊은 아가씨와 신사분은 도무지 그것에 물릴 줄을 몰랐다."], ["When they were not riding, or seeing company, or taking their meals, or piping their songs, there they were with their heads together, as busy as bees, spoiling the door.", "말을 타거나, 손님을 보거나, 끼니를 들거나, 노래를 뽑는 때가 아니면, 그분들은 늘 거기서 머리를 맞대고, 꿀벌처럼 부지런히, 그 문을 망가뜨리고 있었다."], ["Who was the poet who said that Satan finds some mischief still for idle hands to do?", "게으른 손에게는 사탄이 여전히 무슨 못된 짓거리를 마련해 준다고 말한 시인이 누구였던가?"], ["If he had occupied my place in the family, and had seen Miss Rachel with her brush, and Mr. Franklin with his vehicle, he could have written nothing truer of either of them than that.", "그가 만일 이 집에서 내 자리에 있어, 붓을 든 레이철 아가씨와 매재를 든 프랭클린 도련님을 보았던들, 그 두 사람 어느 쪽에 대해서도 그보다 더 참된 것은 쓸 수 없었으리라."]], [["The next date worthy of notice is Sunday the fourth of June.", "눈여겨 둘 만한 다음 날짜는 6월 4일 일요일이다."]], [["On that evening we, in the servants’ hall, debated a domestic question for the first time, which, like the decoration of the door, has its bearing on something that is still to come.", "그날 저녁 우리는, 하인들 식당에서, 한 집안일을 놓고 처음으로 갑론을박했는데, 그것 또한 그 문 장식과 마찬가지로, 아직 오지 않은 어떤 일과 관련이 있다."]], [["Seeing the pleasure which Mr. Franklin and Miss Rachel took in each other’s society, and noting what a pretty match they were in all personal respects, we naturally speculated on the chance of their putting their heads together with other objects in view besides the ornamenting of a door.", "프랭클린 도련님과 레이철 아가씨가 서로의 벗이 되어 즐거워하는 것을 보고, 또 그분들이 됨됨이의 온갖 면에서 얼마나 어울리는 한 쌍인지를 눈여겨보고서, 우리는 자연히, 그분들이 문 하나 꾸미는 것 말고 다른 목적을 염두에 두고 머리를 맞댈 가망은 없을까 어림해 보았다."], ["Some of us said there would be a wedding in the house before the summer was over.", "우리 중 더러는 여름이 가기 전에 이 집에 혼례가 있으리라 했다."], ["Others (led by me) admitted it was likely enough Miss Rachel might be married; but we doubted (for reasons which will presently appear) whether her bridegroom would be Mr. Franklin Blake.", "다른 이들은 (내가 앞장서서) 레이철 아가씨가 혼인하실 법하다는 데는 동의하면서도, 다만 그 신랑이 프랭클린 블레이크 씨일지는 (이제 곧 드러날 까닭들로) 미심쩍어했다."]], [["That Mr. Franklin was in love, on his side, nobody who saw and heard him could doubt.", "프랭클린 도련님이, 그 나름으로는, 사랑에 빠져 있다는 것은, 그를 보고 그의 말을 들은 이라면 아무도 의심할 수 없었다."], ["The difficulty was to fathom Miss Rachel.", "어려운 것은 레이철 아가씨의 속을 헤아리는 일이었다."], ["Let me do myself the honour of making you acquainted with her; after which, I will leave you to fathom for yourself—if you can.", "여러분께 아가씨를 소개하는 영예를 내가 누리게 해 주시라. 그러고 나서는, 여러분 스스로 헤아려 보시라고—하실 수 있거든—맡겨 두겠다."]], [["My young lady’s eighteenth birthday was the birthday now coming, on the twenty-first of June.", "우리 젊은 아가씨의 열여덟 번째 생일이, 6월 21일에 이제 곧 다가올 그 생일이었다."], ["If you happen to like dark women (who, I am informed, have gone out of fashion latterly in the gay world), and if you have no particular prejudice in favour of size, I answer for Miss Rachel as one of the prettiest girls your eyes ever looked on.", "혹 여러분이 살빛 검은 여자를 좋아하시고 (그런 여자들은 요즈음 화려한 사교계에서 유행이 지났다고 들었습니다만), 그리고 몸집에 대해 딱히 이렇다 할 편애가 없으시다면, 나는 레이철 아가씨가 여러분 눈이 여태 본 가장 어여쁜 처녀 가운데 하나임을 보증하겠다."], ["She was small and slim, but all in fine proportion from top to toe.", "아가씨는 자그맣고 호리호리했으나, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 죄다 고른 균형을 이루고 있었다."], ["To see her sit down, to see her get up, and specially to see her walk, was enough to satisfy any man in his senses that the graces of her figure (if you will pardon me the expression) were in her flesh and not in her clothes.", "아가씨가 앉는 것을 보고, 일어서는 것을 보고, 특히 걷는 것을 보노라면, 제정신인 사내라면 누구든, (이런 표현을 용서하신다면) 그 몸매의 우아함이 옷이 아니라 살에 깃들어 있음에 넉넉히 흡족해할 만했다."], ["Her hair was the blackest I ever saw. Her eyes matched her hair.", "아가씨의 머리칼은 내가 여태 본 것 중 가장 검었다."], ["Her nose was not quite large enough, I admit.", "아가씨의 눈은 그 머리칼과 짝을 이루었다."], ["Her mouth and chin were (to quote Mr.", "코는, 인정하거니와, 그리 크지는 못했다."], ["Franklin) morsels for the gods; and her complexion (on the same undeniable authority) was as warm as the sun itself, with this great advantage over the sun, that it was always in nice order to look at.", "입과 턱은 (프랭클린 도련님의 말을 빌리자면) 신들이 탐낼 만한 진미였고, 낯빛은 (똑같이 부인할 수 없는 그 권위에 따르면) 해 그 자체만큼이나 따뜻했으되, 해보다 이 큰 이점이 있었으니, 언제 보아도 늘 곱게 갈무리되어 있다는 점이었다."], ["Add to the foregoing that she carried her head as upright as a dart, in a dashing, spirited, thoroughbred way—that she had a clear voice, with a ring of the right metal in it, and a smile that began very prettily in her eyes before it got to her lips—and there behold the portrait of her, to the best of my painting, as large as life!", "앞엣것에다, 아가씨가 그 머리를 화살처럼 꼿꼿이, 늠름하고 활기차고 순종(純種) 같은 태로 이고 다녔다는 것—맑은 목소리에는 제대로 된 쇠붙이의 울림이 서려 있었다는 것—그리고 입술에 이르기 전에 먼저 눈에서 아주 예쁘게 피어나는 미소가 있었다는 것을 보태면—자, 여기, 내 그림 솜씨가 미치는 데까지 실물만 한 크기로 그린 아가씨의 초상이 있소이다!"]], [["And what about her disposition next?", "그럼 다음으로 아가씨의 성품은 어떠했느냐고?"], ["Had this charming creature no faults?", "이 매혹적인 존재에게는 흠이 하나도 없었느냐고?"], ["She had just as many faults as you have, ma’am—neither more nor less.", "아가씨에게도 흠은 꼭 여러분만큼 있었소이다, 부인—더도 덜도 아니고."]], [["To put it seriously, my dear pretty Miss Rachel, possessing a host of graces and attractions, had one defect, which strict impartiality compels me to acknowledge.", "진지하게 말하자면, 나의 사랑스럽고 어여쁜 레이철 아가씨는, 수많은 우아함과 매력을 지녔으되, 한 가지 결함이 있었으니, 엄정한 공평함이 나로 하여금 그것을 인정하게 만든다."], ["She was unlike most other girls of her age, in this—that she had ideas of her own, and was stiff-necked enough to set the fashions themselves at defiance, if the fashions didn’t suit her views.", "아가씨는 또래의 다른 여느 처녀들과 이 점에서 달랐으니—제 나름의 생각을 지녔고, 유행이 제 소견에 맞지 않으면 유행 그 자체마저 거스를 만큼 고집이 셌다는 것이다."], ["In trifles, this independence of hers was all well enough; but in matters of importance, it carried her (as my lady thought, and as I thought) too far.", "사소한 일에서는 이 독립심이 다 좋았다. 그러나 중대한 일에서는, 그것이 아가씨를 (마님이 생각하시기에도, 또 내가 생각하기에도) 지나치게 멀리 몰아갔다."], ["She judged for herself, as few women of twice her age judge in general; never asked your advice; never told you beforehand what she was going to do; never came with secrets and confidences to anybody, from her mother downwards.", "아가씨는, 제 나이의 곱절 되는 여자들도 좀처럼 그러지 못하는 만큼, 제 스스로 판단했다. 결코 남의 조언을 구하지 않았고, 무엇을 할 참인지 미리 일러 주는 법이 없었으며, 제 어머니에서부터 그 아래로 그 누구에게도 비밀이며 속마음을 털어놓으러 오는 법이 없었다."], ["In little things and great, with people she loved, and people she hated (and she did both with equal heartiness), Miss Rachel always went on a way of her own, sufficient for herself in the joys and sorrows of her life.", "크고 작은 일에서, 사랑하는 사람에게나 미워하는 사람에게나 (아가씨는 그 둘 다 똑같이 진심으로 했다), 레이철 아가씨는 언제나 제 나름의 길을 갔으니, 제 삶의 기쁨과 슬픔 속에서 스스로에게 족했다."], ["Over and over again I have heard my lady say, “Rachel’s best friend and Rachel’s worst enemy are, one and the other—Rachel herself.”", "나는 마님이 이렇게 말씀하시는 것을 몇 번이고 거듭 들었다. \"레이철의 가장 좋은 벗과 레이철의 가장 나쁜 원수는, 이것도 저것도 다—레이철 자신이야.\""]], [["Add one thing more to this, and I have done.", "여기에 한 가지만 더 보태면, 내 할 말은 다 한다."]], [["With all her secrecy, and self-will, there was not so much as the shadow of anything false in her.", "그 온갖 은밀함과 고집에도 불구하고, 아가씨에게는 거짓된 것이라곤 그 그림자조차 없었다. 나는 아가씨가 약속을 어기는 것을 본 기억이 없다."], ["I never remember her breaking her word; I never remember her saying No, and meaning Yes.", "아가씨가 아니오, 라고 말하면서 속으로 예, 라고 여기는 것을 본 기억이 없다."], ["I can call to mind, in her childhood, more than one occasion when the good little soul took the blame, and suffered the punishment, for some fault committed by a playfellow whom she loved.", "어린 시절, 그 착한 어린것이 제가 사랑하는 놀이동무가 저지른 잘못을 두고 대신 탓을 뒤집어쓰고 벌을 받은 일이 한두 번이 아니었음을 나는 떠올릴 수 있다."], ["Nobody ever knew her to confess to it, when the thing was found out, and she was charged with it afterwards.", "그 일이 들통나서 나중에 아가씨가 추궁을 받았을 때, 아가씨가 그것을 자백하는 것을 본 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["But nobody ever knew her to lie about it, either.", "그러나 아가씨가 그것을 두고 거짓말하는 것을 본 사람 또한 아무도 없었다."], ["She looked you straight in the face, and shook her little saucy head, and said plainly, “I won’t tell you!”", "아가씨는 여러분의 얼굴을 똑바로 쳐다보며, 그 앙큼한 조그만 머리를 젓고는, 딱 잘라 말했다. \"말 안 할래요!\""], ["Punished again for this, she would own to being sorry for saying “won’t;”", "이 일로 또 벌을 받으면, 아가씨는 \"안 할래요\"라고 말한 것은 미안하다고 인정했다."], ["but, bread and water notwithstanding, she never told you.", "그러나 빵과 물만 주는 벌에도 아랑곳없이, 끝내 말하지 않았다."], ["Self-willed—devilish self-willed sometimes—I grant; but the finest creature, nevertheless, that ever walked the ways of this lower world.", "고집이 세고—때로는 지독히도 고집이 세고—그건 인정한다. 그러나 그럼에도 아가씨는, 이 낮은 세상의 길을 걸어 본 이들 가운데 가장 훌륭한 존재였다."], ["Perhaps you think you see a certain contradiction here?", "여러분은 여기서 어떤 모순을 보시는 듯싶은가?"], ["In that case, a word in your ear.", "그렇다면, 귓속말 한마디 하겠다."], ["Study your wife closely, for the next four-and-twenty hours.", "앞으로 스물네 시간 동안 여러분의 아내를 찬찬히 살펴보시라."], ["If your good lady doesn’t exhibit something in the shape of a contradiction in that time, Heaven help you!—you have married a monster.", "만일 여러분의 어지신 부인께서 그동안 모순이랄 만한 것을 조금도 내보이지 않으신다면, 하느님이 여러분을 도우시기를!—여러분은 괴물과 혼인하신 것이니."]], [["I have now brought you acquainted with Miss Rachel, which you will find puts us face to face, next, with the question of that young lady’s matrimonial views.", "이제 나는 여러분께 레이철 아가씨를 소개해 드렸으니, 이로써 우리는 다음으로, 그 젊은 아가씨의 혼삿길에 관한 물음과 마주 서게 되었음을 여러분도 아시게 될 것이다."]], [["On June the twelfth, an invitation from my mistress was sent to a gentleman in London, to come and help to keep Miss Rachel’s birthday.", "6월 12일에, 내 여주인의 초대장이 런던의 한 신사분께 보내졌으니, 오셔서 레이철 아가씨의 생일 쇠는 것을 도와 달라는 것이었다."], ["This was the fortunate individual on whom I believed her heart to be privately set!", "이분이 바로, 아가씨가 남몰래 마음을 두고 있다고 내가 믿었던 그 복 받은 사람이었다!"], ["Like Mr. Franklin, he was a cousin of hers.", "프랭클린 도련님과 마찬가지로, 그도 아가씨의 사촌이었다."], ["His name was Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "이름은 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨였다."]], [["My lady’s second sister (don’t be alarmed; we are not going very deep into family matters this time)—my lady’s second sister, I say, had a disappointment in love; and taking a husband afterwards, on the neck or nothing principle, made what they call a misalliance.", "마님의 둘째 언니께서는 (놀라지 마시라. 이번엔 집안 사정을 아주 깊이까지 파고들 것은 아니니)—마님의 둘째 언니, 내 말은, 그분께서 사랑에 실연하시고, 그 뒤 될 대로 되라는 심정으로 남편을 얻어, 이른바 격에 안 맞는 혼인을 하셨다."], ["There was terrible work in the family when the Honourable Caroline insisted on marrying plain Mr. Ablewhite, the banker at Frizinghall.", "캐럴라인 아가씨께서 그저 평범한 에이블화이트 씨, 곧 프리징홀의 그 은행가와 혼인하겠다고 우기셨을 때, 집안에는 한바탕 난리가 났다."], ["He was very rich and very respectable, and he begot a prodigious large family—all in his favour, so far.", "그는 매우 부유하고 매우 점잖았으며, 엄청나게 많은 자식을 두었다—여기까지는 죄다 그에게 유리한 점이다."], ["But he had presumed to raise himself from a low station in the world—and that was against him.", "그러나 그는 세상의 낮은 신분에서 감히 제 몸을 끌어올린 사람이었다—그리고 그 점이 그에게 불리했다."], ["However, Time and the progress of modern enlightenment put things right; and the misalliance passed muster very well.", "그렇지만 세월과 근대 계몽의 진보가 일을 바로잡아, 그 격에 안 맞는 혼인은 아주 무난히 받아들여졌다."], ["We are all getting liberal now; and (provided you can scratch me, if I scratch you) what do I care, in or out of Parliament, whether you are a Dustman or a Duke? That’s the modern way of looking at it—and I keep up with the modern way.", "이제 우리는 다들 자유주의자가 되어 가고 있으니, (여러분이 내 등을 긁어 주면 나도 여러분 등을 긁어 준다는 조건 아래) 의회 안에서든 밖에서든, 여러분이 청소부이든 공작이든 내가 무슨 상관이겠는가?"], ["The Ablewhites lived in a fine house and grounds, a little out of Frizinghall.", "그것이 요즈음 세상의 관점이요—나도 요즈음 세상에 발맞추고 있다."], ["Very worthy people, and greatly respected in the neighbourhood.", "에이블화이트 집안은 프리징홀에서 조금 벗어난, 훌륭한 저택과 정원에서 살았다."], ["We shall not be much troubled with them in these pages—excepting Mr.", "매우 훌륭한 사람들이요, 이웃에서 크게 존경받았다."], ["Godfrey, who was Mr. Ablewhite’s second son, and who must take his proper place here, if you please, for Miss Rachel’s sake.", "이 지면에서 그들 때문에 우리가 크게 시달릴 일은 없을 것이다—다만 에이블화이트 씨의 둘째 아들인 고드프리 씨만은 예외이니, 그는, 여러분이 괜찮으시다면, 레이철 아가씨를 위해서라도 여기서 제 마땅한 자리를 차지해야 한다."]], [["With all his brightness and cleverness and general good qualities, Mr. Franklin’s chance of topping Mr. Godfrey in our young lady’s estimation was, in my opinion, a very poor chance indeed.", "그 온갖 총명함과 영리함과 두루 좋은 자질에도 불구하고, 우리 젊은 아가씨의 눈에 프랭클린 도련님이 고드프리 씨를 능가할 가망은, 내 생각에, 참으로 지극히 희박한 가망이었다."]], [["In the first place, Mr. Godfrey was, in point of size, the finest man by far of the two.", "우선 첫째로, 고드프리 씨는, 몸집으로 치면, 두 사람 가운데 단연코 더 훤칠한 사내였다."], ["He stood over six feet high; he had a beautiful red and white colour; a smooth round face, shaved as bare as your hand; and a head of lovely long flaxen hair, falling negligently over the poll of his neck.", "그는 키가 6피트를 넘었고, 아름다운 붉고 흰 낯빛에, 여러분 손바닥처럼 말끔히 면도한 매끈하고 둥근 얼굴에, 목덜미 위로 아무렇게나 흘러내리는 고운 긴 아마빛 머리칼을 지녔다."], ["But why do I try to give you this personal description of him?", "그런데 내가 어째서 그의 이런 생김새를 여러분께 굳이 그려 드리려 애쓰는가?"], ["If you ever subscribed to a Ladies’ Charity in London, you know Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite as well as I do.", "여러분이 혹 런던에서 어느 부인 자선회에 기부해 보신 적이 있다면, 여러분도 나만큼이나 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 잘 아신다."], ["He was a barrister by profession; a ladies’ man by temperament; and a good Samaritan by choice.", "그는 직업으로는 법정 변호사요, 기질로는 여자들의 사내요, 스스로 택하기로는 착한 사마리아인이었다."], ["Female benevolence and female destitution could do nothing without him.", "여성의 자선과 여성의 궁핍은 그가 없이는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["Maternal societies for confining poor women; Magdalen societies for rescuing poor women; strong-minded societies for putting poor women into poor men’s places, and leaving the men to shift for themselves;—he was vice-president, manager, referee to them all.", "가난한 여자들의 해산을 돕는 산모회, 가난한 여자들을 구제하는 막달레나회, 가난한 여자들을 가난한 남자들의 자리에 앉히고 남자들일랑 저 알아서 살라고 내버려 두자는 억센 마음의 모임들—그는 그 모든 것의 부회장이요, 간사요, 심판이었다."], ["Wherever there was a table with a committee of ladies sitting round it in council there was Mr. Godfrey at the bottom of the board, keeping the temper of the committee, and leading the dear creatures along the thorny ways of business, hat in hand.", "어디든 숙녀 위원들이 빙 둘러앉아 의논하는 탁자가 있는 곳이면, 거기 그 판의 맨 끄트머리에 고드프리 씨가 있어, 위원회의 성미를 다독이며, 그 어여쁜 분들을 모자를 손에 들고 사무의 가시밭길로 이끌었다."], ["I do suppose this was the most accomplished philanthropist (on a small independence) that England ever produced.", "나는 이 사람이야말로 (얼마 안 되는 제 재산으로) 영국이 여태 낳은 가장 완숙한 박애가라고 생각한다."], ["As a speaker at charitable meetings the like of him for drawing your tears and your money was not easy to find.", "자선 모임의 연설가로서, 여러분의 눈물과 여러분의 돈을 끌어내는 데 그만한 사람은 좀처럼 찾기 어려웠다."], ["He was quite a public character.", "그는 그야말로 공인이었다."], ["The last time I was in London, my mistress gave me two treats.", "지난번 내가 런던에 있을 때, 내 여주인이 내게 두 가지 대접을 베푸셨다."], ["She sent me to the theatre to see a dancing woman who was all the rage; and she sent me to Exeter Hall to hear Mr. Godfrey.", "마님은 나를 극장에 보내 장안의 화제이던 어느 춤추는 여자를 보게 하셨고, 나를 엑서터 홀에 보내 고드프리 씨의 연설을 듣게 하셨다."], ["The lady did it, with a band of music.", "그 여자는 악단을 곁들여 그 일을 했다."], ["The gentleman did it, with a handkerchief and a glass of water.", "그 신사는 손수건 한 장과 물 한 잔을 곁들여 그 일을 했다."], ["Crowds at the performance with the legs.", "다리로 하는 그 공연에도 인파가 몰렸다."], ["Ditto at the performance with the tongue.", "혀로 하는 그 공연에도 마찬가지였다."], ["And with all this, the sweetest tempered person (I allude to Mr. Godfrey)—the simplest and pleasantest and easiest to please—you ever met with.", "그리고 이 모든 것에 더해, 그는 (내 말은 고드프리 씨 말이다) 여러분이 여태 만나 본 가장 상냥한 성미의 사람—가장 소탈하고 가장 유쾌하고 가장 비위 맞추기 쉬운 사람이었다."], ["He loved everybody.", "그는 모든 사람을 사랑했다."], ["And everybody loved him. What chance had Mr.", "그리고 모든 사람이 그를 사랑했다."], ["Franklin—what chance had anybody of average reputation and capacities—against such a man as this?", "프랭클린 도련님한테—평범한 평판과 재간을 지닌 그 누구한테—이런 사람에 맞설 무슨 가망이 있었겠는가?"]], [["On the fourteenth, came Mr. Godfrey’s answer.", "14일에, 고드프리 씨의 답장이 왔다."]], [["He accepted my mistress’s invitation, from the Wednesday of the birthday to the evening of Friday—when his duties to the Ladies’ Charities would oblige him to return to town.", "그는 내 여주인의 초대를, 생일인 수요일부터 금요일 저녁까지—그때가 되면 부인 자선회에 대한 그의 소임이 그를 도로 도시로 불러들일 참이라며—받아들였다."], ["He also enclosed a copy of verses on what he elegantly called his cousin’s “natal day.”", "그는 또한 그가 우아하게 제 사촌의 \"탄신일\"이라 일컬은 것을 노래한 시 한 편의 사본을 동봉했다."], ["Miss Rachel, I was informed, joined Mr. Franklin in making fun of the verses at dinner; and Penelope, who was all on Mr.", "내가 전해 들으니, 레이철 아가씨는 저녁 식탁에서 프랭클린 도련님과 어울려 그 시를 놀림감으로 삼았다고 한다."], ["Franklin’s side, asked me, in great triumph, what I thought of that.", "그리고 죄다 프랭클린 도련님 편이던 페넬로피가, 자못 의기양양하게, 그걸 어떻게 생각하시느냐고 내게 물었다."], ["“Miss Rachel has led you off on a false scent, my dear,”", "\"레이철 아가씨가 너를 헛다리 짚게 끌고 갔구나, 얘야.\" 나는 대꾸했다."], ["I replied; “but my nose is not so easily mystified. Wait till Mr.", "\"하지만 내 코는 그리 쉽사리 홀리지 않는단다."], ["Ablewhite’s verses are followed by Mr. Ablewhite himself.”", "에이블화이트 씨의 시 뒤에 에이블화이트 씨 본인이 따라올 때까지 기다려 보렴.\""]], [["My daughter replied, that Mr. Franklin might strike in, and try his luck, before the verses were followed by the poet.", "내 딸은, 그 시 뒤에 그 시인이 따라오기 전에, 프랭클린 도련님이 끼어들어 제 운을 시험해 볼지도 모른다고 대꾸했다."], ["In favour of this view, I must acknowledge that Mr. Franklin left no chance untried of winning Miss Rachel’s good graces.", "이 견해에 유리하게, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 레이철 아가씨의 총애를 얻으려고 시도해 보지 않은 기회가 하나도 없었음을 인정하지 않을 수 없다."]], [["Though one of the most inveterate smokers I ever met with, he gave up his cigar, because she said, one day, she hated the stale smell of it in his clothes.", "내가 여태 만나 본 가장 골수 골초 가운데 하나였으면서도, 그는 시가를 끊었다. 어느 날 아가씨가, 그의 옷에서 나는 그 케케묵은 담배 냄새가 싫다고 말했기 때문이다."], ["He slept so badly, after this effort of self-denial, for want of the composing effect of the tobacco to which he was used, and came down morning after morning looking so haggard and worn, that Miss Rachel herself begged him to take to his cigars again. No!", "이 극기의 노력 뒤로, 그는, 익숙해져 있던 담배의 그 마음을 가라앉히는 효험이 없으니 잠을 하도 설쳐, 아침마다 그토록 초췌하고 지친 몰골로 내려오기에, 급기야 레이철 아가씨가 몸소 그에게 시가를 다시 피우라고 청할 지경이었다."], ["he would take to nothing again that could cause her a moment’s annoyance; he would fight it out resolutely, and get back his sleep, sooner or later, by main force of patience in waiting for it.", "아니 될 말! 그는 아가씨에게 한순간이라도 언짢음을 끼칠 수 있는 것이라면 무엇도 다시는 손대지 않으려 했다."], ["Such devotion as this, you may say (as some of them said downstairs), could never fail of producing the right effect on Miss Rachel—backed up, too, as it was, by the decorating work every day on the door.", "그는 그것을 결연히 견뎌 내어, 이르든 늦든, 그것을 기다리는 참을성의 순전한 힘으로 제 잠을 되찾으려 했다. 이만한 헌신이라면 (아래층의 더러가 그렇게 말했듯이) 레이철 아가씨에게 마땅한 효과를 내지 못할 리 없다고 여러분은 말씀하실 것이다—더구나 날마다 그 문에 하는 장식 일까지 뒷받침해 주고 있었으니 말이다."], ["All very well—but she had a photograph of Mr.", "다 좋다—그러나 아가씨는 제 침실에 고드프리 씨의 사진을 한 장 두고 있었다."], ["Godfrey in her bedroom; represented speaking at a public meeting, with all his hair blown out by the breath of his own eloquence, and his eyes, most lovely, charming the money out of your pockets.", "어느 공개 모임에서 연설하는 모습으로, 제 웅변의 입김에 머리칼이 죄다 흩날리고, 그 더없이 사랑스러운 눈은 여러분 주머니에서 돈을 홀려 내는 사진이었다. 여러분은 그것에 대해 뭐라 하시겠는가?"], ["What do you say to that? Every morning—as Penelope herself owned to me—there was the man whom the women couldn’t do without, looking on, in effigy, while Miss Rachel was having her hair combed.", "아침마다—페넬로피 자신이 내게 실토한 바로—레이철 아가씨가 머리를 빗기는 동안, 여자들이 없이는 못 사는 그 사내가, 초상이 되어, 지켜보고 있었던 것이다."], ["He would be looking on, in reality, before long—that was my opinion of it.", "머잖아 그는 실물로 지켜보게 되리라—그것이 그 일에 대한 내 생각이었다."]], [["June the sixteenth brought an event which made Mr. Franklin’s chance look, to my mind, a worse chance than ever.", "6월 16일에는, 내 보기에 프랭클린 도련님의 가망을 그 어느 때보다 더 나빠 보이게 만든 한 사건이 일어났다."]], [["A strange gentleman, speaking English with a foreign accent, came that morning to the house, and asked to see Mr.", "영어를 외국식 억양으로 하는 어떤 낯선 신사가, 그날 아침 집에 찾아와, 볼일이 있어 프랭클린 블레이크 씨를 뵙고 싶다 청했다."], ["Franklin Blake on business. The business could not possibly have been connected with the Diamond, for these two reasons—first, that Mr.", "그 볼일은 도무지 그 다이아몬드와는 관계가 있을 수 없었으니, 그 까닭은 두 가지다—첫째, 프랭클린 도련님이 그것에 관해 내게 아무 말도 하지 않았다는 것."], ["Franklin told me nothing about it; secondly, that he communicated it (when the gentleman had gone, as I suppose) to my lady.", "둘째, 그가 그것을 (그 신사가 돌아간 뒤, 짐작건대) 마님께 알렸다는 것."], ["She probably hinted something about it next to her daughter.", "마님은 아마 그다음에 그것에 관해 무언가를 당신 따님께 넌지시 비치셨을 것이다."], ["At any rate, Miss Rachel was reported to have said some severe things to Mr. Franklin, at the piano that evening, about the people he had lived among, and the principles he had adopted in foreign parts.", "어쨌든, 그날 저녁 피아노 앞에서 레이철 아가씨가 프랭클린 도련님에게, 그가 함께 어울려 지낸 사람들과 그가 외국에서 받아들인 신조에 관해 몇 가지 매서운 말을 했다고 전해졌다."], ["The next day, for the first time, nothing was done towards the decoration of the door.", "이튿날, 처음으로, 그 문 장식을 향해서는 아무것도 이루어지지 않았다."], ["I suspect some imprudence of Mr. Franklin’s on the Continent—with a woman or a debt at the bottom of it—had followed him to England.", "나는 대륙에서의 프랭클린 도련님의 어떤 경솔한 짓이—그 밑바닥에 여자 아니면 빚을 깔고서—그를 따라 영국까지 온 것이 아닐까 의심한다."], ["But that is all guesswork.", "그러나 그것은 죄다 짐작일 뿐이다."], ["In this case, not only Mr. Franklin, but my lady too, for a wonder, left me in the dark.", "이번 경우엔, 프랭클린 도련님뿐 아니라 마님까지도, 웬일로, 나를 캄캄한 채로 내버려 두셨다."]], [["On the seventeenth, to all appearance, the cloud passed away again.", "17일에는, 겉보기로는, 그 먹구름이 다시 걷혔다."], ["They returned to their decorating work on the door, and seemed to be as good friends as ever.", "그분들은 문에 하는 장식 일로 돌아갔고, 여느 때처럼 좋은 벗으로 보였다."], ["If Penelope was to be believed, Mr. Franklin had seized the opportunity of the reconciliation to make an offer to Miss Rachel, and had neither been accepted nor refused.", "페넬로피의 말을 믿는다면, 프랭클린 도련님이 그 화해의 기회를 붙들어 레이철 아가씨에게 청혼을 했는데, 받아들여지지도 거절당하지도 않았다는 것이다."], ["My girl was sure (from signs and tokens which I need not trouble you with) that her young mistress had fought Mr. Franklin off by declining to believe that he was in earnest, and had then secretly regretted treating him in that way afterwards.", "내 딸은 (여러분을 굳이 성가시게 할 것 없는 이런저런 낌새와 표지로 미루어) 확신하기를, 제 젊은 여주인이 그가 진심이라는 것을 믿기를 거부하는 식으로 프랭클린 도련님을 물리쳐 놓고는, 그 뒤 그런 식으로 그를 대한 것을 남몰래 후회했다는 것이었다."], ["Though Penelope was admitted to more familiarity with her young mistress than maids generally are—for the two had been almost brought up together as children—still I knew Miss Rachel’s reserved character too well to believe that she would show her mind to anybody in this way.", "페넬로피가 여느 하녀들보다 제 젊은 여주인과 더 허물없이 지내는 것이 허락되어 있기는 했으나—그 둘은 어린 시절 거의 함께 자라다시피 했으므로—그래도 나는, 아가씨가 그런 식으로 제 속마음을 누구에게든 내보이리라 믿기에는 레이철 아가씨의 그 내성적인 성품을 너무 잘 알고 있었다."], ["What my daughter told me, on the present occasion, was, as I suspected, more what she wished than what she really knew.", "이번 경우에 내 딸이 내게 한 말은, 내가 의심한 대로, 그 애가 실제로 아는 것이라기보다 그 애가 바라는 것에 가까웠다."]], [["On the nineteenth another event happened.", "19일에는 또 다른 사건이 일어났다."], ["We had the doctor in the house professionally.", "우리는 의사를 진료차 집에 들였다."], ["He was summoned to prescribe for a person whom I have had occasion to present to you in these pages—our second housemaid, Rosanna Spearman.", "그는, 내가 이 지면에서 여러분께 소개할 기회가 있었던 한 사람—우리 둘째 하녀 로재나 스피어먼을 진찰하러 불려 왔다."]], [["This poor girl—who had puzzled me, as you know already, at the Shivering Sand—puzzled me more than once again, in the interval time of which I am now writing.", "이 가엾은 처녀는—여러분도 이미 아시다시피 떠는 모래펄에서 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 그 처녀는—지금 내가 적고 있는 그 사이 기간에도, 다시 한두 번 나를 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["Penelope’s notion that her fellow-servant was in love with Mr. Franklin (which my daughter, by my orders, kept strictly secret) seemed to be just as absurd as ever.", "제 동료 하인이 프랭클린 도련님을 사랑한다는 페넬로피의 생각은 (내 명에 따라 내 딸이 철저히 비밀에 부친 것이었는데) 여전히 예전만큼이나 터무니없어 보였다."], ["But I must own that what I myself saw, and what my daughter saw also, of our second housemaid’s conduct, began to look mysterious, to say the least of it.", "그러나 나 자신이 본 것, 그리고 내 딸도 본, 우리 둘째 하녀의 처신이, 아무리 좋게 보아도 알쏭달쏭해 보이기 시작했음은 나도 인정해야겠다."]], [["For example, the girl constantly put herself in Mr. Franklin’s way—very slyly and quietly, but she did it.", "이를테면, 그 처녀는 끊임없이 프랭클린 도련님의 앞길에 제 몸을 갖다 놓았다—아주 슬며시 그리고 조용히 그랬으나, 아무튼 그랬다."], ["He took about as much notice of her as he took of the cat; it never seemed to occur to him to waste a look on Rosanna’s plain face.", "그는 고양이한테 기울일 만큼의 관심을 그녀에게 기울였을 뿐이다. 로재나의 못생긴 얼굴에 눈길 한 번 허비할 생각은 도무지 떠오르지 않는 듯했다."], ["The poor thing’s appetite, never much, fell away dreadfully; and her eyes in the morning showed plain signs of waking and crying at night.", "그 가엾은 것의, 본디 많지도 않던 입맛이 무섭게 떨어졌고, 아침이면 그 눈이, 밤에 잠 못 이루고 운 것이 뚜렷한 흔적을 드러냈다."], ["One day Penelope made an awkward discovery, which we hushed up on the spot.", "어느 날 페넬로피가 난처한 발견을 하나 했는데, 우리는 그것을 그 자리에서 쉬쉬해 두었다."], ["She caught Rosanna at Mr. Franklin’s dressing-table, secretly removing a rose which Miss Rachel had given him to wear in his button-hole, and putting another rose like it, of her own picking, in its place.", "그 애가, 프랭클린 도련님의 화장대에서, 레이철 아가씨가 그더러 단춧구멍에 꽂으라고 준 장미 한 송이를 로재나가 남몰래 빼내고, 그 자리에 제가 손수 꺾은 그와 비슷한 다른 장미를 꽂아 놓는 것을 붙잡은 것이다."], ["She was, after that, once or twice impudent to me, when I gave her a well-meant general hint to be careful in her conduct; and, worse still, she was not over-respectful now, on the few occasions when Miss Rachel accidentally spoke to her.", "그 뒤로 그녀는, 내가 처신을 조심하라고 좋은 뜻에서 두루뭉술하게 넌지시 일러 줄 때, 한두 번 내게 건방지게 굴었다. 그리고 더 나쁘게는, 레이철 아가씨가 어쩌다 그녀에게 말을 거는 그 몇 안 되는 경우에도, 이제 그리 공손하지가 않았다."]], [["My lady noticed the change, and asked me what I thought about it.", "마님이 그 변화를 눈치채시고, 그것에 대해 어찌 생각하느냐고 내게 물으셨다."], ["I tried to screen the girl by answering that I thought she was out of health; and it ended in the doctor being sent for, as already mentioned, on the nineteenth.", "나는 그 처녀를 감싸 주려고, 그녀가 몸이 편찮은 것 같다고 대답했다. 그리고 그것은 앞서 말했듯이 19일에 의사를 부르는 것으로 이어졌다."], ["He said it was her nerves, and doubted if she was fit for service.", "의사는 그녀의 신경 탓이라며, 그녀가 일할 만한 몸인지 미심쩍어했다."], ["My lady offered to remove her for change of air to one of our farms, inland.", "마님은 바람이라도 쐬게 그녀를 내륙에 있는 우리 농장 중 하나로 옮겨 주겠다고 하셨다."], ["She begged and prayed, with the tears in her eyes, to be let to stop; and, in an evil hour, I advised my lady to try her for a little longer.", "그녀는 눈에 눈물을 담고서, 여기 머물게 해 달라고 빌고 또 빌었다. 그리고 불행한 시각에, 나는 마님께 그녀를 좀 더 두고 보시라 권했다."], ["As the event proved, and as you will soon see, this was the worst advice I could have given.", "뒤에 일이 드러낸 대로, 그리고 여러분도 곧 보시게 될 대로, 이것은 내가 할 수 있었던 가장 나쁜 권고였다."], ["If I could only have looked a little way into the future, I would have taken Rosanna Spearman out of the house, then and there, with my own hand.", "내가 앞날을 조금이라도 내다볼 수만 있었던들, 나는 로재나 스피어먼을 그 자리에서 당장, 내 손으로 이 집에서 내보냈으리라."]], [["On the twentieth, there came a note from Mr. Godfrey.", "20일에는, 고드프리 씨에게서 쪽지가 하나 왔다."], ["He had arranged to stop at Frizinghall that night, having occasion to consult his father on business.", "그는 볼일이 있어 제 아버지와 의논할 일이 생겨, 그날 밤은 프리징홀에 묵기로 정했다고 했다."], ["On the afternoon of the next day, he and his two eldest sisters would ride over to us on horseback, in good time before dinner.", "이튿날 오후에, 그와 그의 손위 두 누이가 저녁 식사에 넉넉히 앞서 우리에게 말을 타고 오리라는 것이었다."], ["An elegant little casket in china accompanied the note, presented to Miss Rachel, with her cousin’s love and best wishes.", "그 쪽지에는 우아한 도자기 상자 하나가 딸려 왔는데, 사촌의 사랑과 더없는 축원을 담아 레이철 아가씨께 드리는 것이었다."], ["Mr. Franklin had only given her a plain locket not worth half the money.", "프랭클린 도련님은 그저, 그 돈의 반값도 안 되는 밋밋한 로켓 하나를 아가씨께 드렸을 뿐이다."], ["My daughter Penelope, nevertheless—such is the obstinacy of women—still backed him to win.", "그렇지만 내 딸 페넬로피는—여자의 고집이란 그런 것이라—여전히 그가 이기는 데 걸었다."]], [["Thanks be to Heaven, we have arrived at the eve of the birthday at last!", "하늘에 감사하게도, 우리는 마침내 그 생일의 전야에 이르렀다!"], ["You will own, I think, that I have got you over the ground this time, without much loitering by the way.", "이번엔 내가 여러분을, 길에서 그리 어정거리지 않고 이 대목까지 넘어오게 해 드렸다고, 여러분도 아마 인정하시리라."], ["Cheer up!", "기운을 내시라!"], ["I’ll ease you with another new chapter here—and, what is more, that chapter shall take you straight into the thick of the story.", "여기서 또 하나 새 장(章)으로 여러분의 짐을 덜어 드리겠다—더구나, 그 장은 여러분을 곧장 이야기 한복판으로 데려다줄 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# CHAPTER IX"]], [["June twenty-first, the day of the birthday, was cloudy and unsettled at sunrise, but towards noon it cleared up bravely.", "생일날인 6월 21일은, 해가 뜰 무렵엔 흐리고 변덕스러웠으나, 정오 무렵엔 씩씩하게 개었다."]], [["We, in the servants’ hall, began this happy anniversary, as usual, by offering our little presents to Miss Rachel, with the regular speech delivered annually by me as the chief.", "우리는, 하인들 식당에서, 여느 때처럼 이 기쁜 기념일을 시작했으니, 우리네 조촐한 선물을 레이철 아가씨께 바치고, 우두머리인 내가 해마다 하는 그 판에 박힌 연설을 올리는 것이었다."], ["I follow the plan adopted by the Queen in opening Parliament—namely, the plan of saying much the same thing regularly every year.", "나는 여왕께서 의회를 개회하실 때 취하시는 방식을 따른다—곧, 해마다 판에 박힌 듯 거의 똑같은 이야기를 하는 방식 말이다."], ["Before it is delivered, my speech (like the Queen’s) is looked for as eagerly as if nothing of the kind had ever been heard before.", "그 연설이 나오기 전에는, 내 연설도 (여왕의 연설과 마찬가지로) 마치 그런 것을 여태 한 번도 들어 본 적 없다는 듯 잔뜩 기다려진다."], ["When it is delivered, and turns out not to be the novelty anticipated, though they grumble a little, they look forward hopefully to something newer next year.", "그것이 나오고 나서, 기대하던 새로운 것이 아님이 드러나면, 다들 조금 투덜대기는 해도, 이듬해엔 무언가 더 새로운 것이 있으려니 하고 희망차게 고대한다."], ["An easy people to govern, in the Parliament and in the Kitchen—that’s the moral of it.", "의회에서든 부엌에서든, 다스리기 쉬운 백성이라—그것이 그 교훈이다."]], [["After breakfast, Mr. Franklin and I had a private conference on the subject of the Moonstone—the time having now come for removing it from the bank at Frizinghall, and placing it in Miss Rachel’s own hands.", "아침 식사 뒤, 프랭클린 도련님과 나는 월장석을 두고 남몰래 담판을 벌였다—이제 그것을 프리징홀 은행에서 찾아 내어 레이철 아가씨 손에 직접 쥐여 드릴 때가 되었기 때문이다."]], [["Whether he had been trying to make love to his cousin again, and had got a rebuff—or whether his broken rest, night after night, was aggravating the queer contradictions and uncertainties in his character—I don’t know.", "그가 다시 제 사촌에게 사랑을 고백해 보려다 퇴짜를 맞은 것인지—아니면 밤이면 밤마다 설친 잠이 그 됨됨이의 그 묘한 모순과 종잡을 수 없음을 더 부추긴 것인지—나는 모른다."], ["But certain it is, that Mr. Franklin failed to show himself at his best on the morning of the birthday.", "그러나 분명한 것은, 프랭클린 도련님이 생일날 아침에 제 가장 나은 모습을 보여 주지 못했다는 것이다."], ["He was in twenty different minds about the Diamond in as many minutes.", "그는 몇 분 사이에 그 다이아몬드를 두고 스무 가지나 다른 마음을 먹었다."], ["For my part, I stuck fast by the plain facts as we knew them.", "나로 말하면, 우리가 아는 대로의 그 엄연한 사실들에 딱 붙어 있었다."], ["Nothing had happened to justify us in alarming my lady on the subject of the jewel; and nothing could alter the legal obligation that now lay on Mr. Franklin to put it in his cousin’s possession.", "그 보석을 두고 마님을 놀라게 해 드릴 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았고, 이제 그것을 제 사촌의 소유로 넘겨줄 프랭클린 도련님에게 지워진 그 법적 의무를 바꿀 수 있는 것도 아무것도 없었다."], ["That was my view of the matter; and, twist and turn it as he might, he was forced in the end to make it his view too.", "그것이 이 일에 대한 내 견해였고, 그가 아무리 이리 비틀고 저리 돌려 보아도, 그는 결국 그것을 제 견해로도 삼지 않을 수 없었다."], ["We arranged that he was to ride over, after lunch, to Frizinghall, and bring the Diamond back, with Mr. Godfrey and the two young ladies, in all probability, to keep him company on the way home again.", "우리는 그가 점심 뒤에 프리징홀로 말을 달려가, 그 다이아몬드를 가지고 오되, 아마도 고드프리 씨와 두 젊은 아가씨를 데리고 와 집으로 돌아오는 길에 벗을 삼기로 정했다."]], [["This settled, our young gentleman went back to Miss Rachel.", "이렇게 매듭짓고서, 우리 젊은 신사는 레이철 아가씨께로 돌아갔다."]], [["They consumed the whole morning, and part of the afternoon, in the everlasting business of decorating the door, Penelope standing by to mix the colours, as directed; and my lady, as luncheon time drew near, going in and out of the room, with her handkerchief to her nose (for they used a deal of Mr.", "그분들은 아침 내내, 그리고 오후의 얼마간을, 그 끝없는 문 장식 일로 다 써 버렸다. 페넬로피는 곁에 서서 지시받은 대로 물감을 개었고, 마님은 점심때가 가까워지자, 손수건으로 코를 막은 채 (그날 그분들이 프랭클린 도련님의 그 매재를 어지간히 많이 썼기 때문이다) 방을 들락날락하시며, 그 두 화가를 일에서 떼어 내려고 헛되이 애쓰셨다."], ["Franklin’s vehicle that day), and trying vainly to get the two artists away from their work. It was three o’clock before they took off their aprons, and released Penelope (much the worse for the vehicle), and cleaned themselves of their mess.", "그분들이 앞치마를 벗고, (그 매재 탓에 훨씬 더 축난) 페넬로피를 놓아주고, 제 몸에 묻은 지저분한 것을 씻어 낸 것은 세 시가 되어서였다."], ["But they had done what they wanted—they had finished the door on the birthday, and proud enough they were of it.", "그러나 그분들은 원하던 것을 해낸 터였다—그분들은 생일날 그 문을 완성했고, 그 일을 여간 자랑스러워하지 않았다."], ["The griffins, cupids, and so on, were, I must own, most beautiful to behold; though so many in number, so entangled in flowers and devices, and so topsy-turvy in their actions and attitudes, that you felt them unpleasantly in your head for hours after you had done with the pleasure of looking at them.", "그리핀이며 큐피드며 그런 것들은, 인정하거니와, 바라보기에 더없이 아름다웠다. 다만 그 수가 하도 많고, 꽃과 도안 속에 하도 뒤엉켜 있으며, 그 몸짓과 자세가 하도 뒤죽박죽이라, 그것들을 바라보는 즐거움을 다한 뒤에도 몇 시간이나 그것들이 머릿속에서 불쾌하게 어른거렸다."], ["If I add that Penelope ended her part of the morning’s work by being sick in the back-kitchen, it is in no unfriendly spirit towards the vehicle.", "페넬로피가 그날 아침 일에서 제 몫을 뒤쪽 부엌에서 토하는 것으로 끝맺었다고 내가 덧붙인다면, 그것은 그 매재에 대한 아무 악의 없이 하는 말이다. 아니!"], ["No! no!", "아니고말고!"], ["It left off stinking when it dried; and if Art requires these sort of sacrifices—though the girl is my own daughter—I say, let Art have them!", "그것은 마르고 나니 악취가 가셨다. 그리고 예술이 이런 종류의 희생을 요구한다면—그 애가 내 딸이긴 하다만—나는 말하겠다, 예술더러 그것을 가져가라고!"]], [["Mr. Franklin snatched a morsel from the luncheon-table, and rode off to Frizinghall—to escort his cousins, as he told my lady.", "프랭클린 도련님은 점심 식탁에서 한 입 낚아채 먹고는, 프리징홀로 말을 달려갔다—마님께 이르기로는, 제 사촌들을 데려오려고."], ["To fetch the Moonstone, as was privately known to himself and to me.", "그러나 그 월장석을 가지러 간다는 것은, 그 자신과 나만 남몰래 아는 바였다."]], [["This being one of the high festivals on which I took my place at the side-board, in command of the attendance at table, I had plenty to occupy my mind while Mr. Franklin was away.", "그날은 내가 식기대 곁에 자리 잡고 식탁 시중을 지휘하는 그 큰 잔치 가운데 하나였던지라, 프랭클린 도련님이 나가 있는 동안 나는 마음을 쏟을 일이 넉넉했다."], ["Having seen to the wine, and reviewed my men and women who were to wait at dinner, I retired to collect myself before the company came.", "포도주를 살피고, 저녁 식탁에서 시중들 내 사내와 여자들을 점검하고 나서, 나는 손님들이 오기 전에 마음을 추스르려고 물러났다."], ["A whiff of—you know what, and a turn at a certain book which I have had occasion to mention in these pages, composed me, body and mind.", "그—여러분도 아시는 것—한 모금과, 이 지면에서 내가 언급할 기회가 있었던 어떤 책 한 대목이, 내 몸과 마음을 가라앉혀 주었다."], ["I was aroused from what I am inclined to think must have been, not a nap, but a reverie, by the clatter of horses’ hoofs outside; and, going to the door, received a cavalcade comprising Mr.", "나는, 내 생각에 아무래도 낮잠이 아니라 공상이었지 싶은 그 상태에서, 바깥에서 나는 말발굽 소리에 깨어났다."], ["Franklin and his three cousins, escorted by one of old Mr. Ablewhite’s grooms.", "그리고 문간으로 가서, 프랭클린 도련님과 그의 세 사촌으로 이루어진 마상(馬上) 행렬을, 노(老)에이블화이트 씨의 마부 한 사람이 딸려 온 그 행렬을 맞이했다."]], [["Mr. Godfrey struck me, strangely enough, as being like Mr.", "고드프리 씨가, 참 이상하게도, 이 점에서 프랭클린 도련님과 닮았다는 인상을 내게 주었다—그도 여느 때의 기운을 차리지 못한 듯 보였다는 것이다."], ["Franklin in this respect—that he did not seem to be in his customary spirits. He kindly shook hands with me as usual, and was most politely glad to see his old friend Betteredge wearing so well.", "그는 여느 때처럼 다정하게 내게 악수를 청하고, 옛 벗 베터리지가 그토록 정정한 것을 뵈니 더없이 반갑다고 정중히 말했다."], ["But there was a sort of cloud over him, which I couldn’t at all account for; and when I asked how he had found his father in health, he answered rather shortly, “Much as usual.”", "그러나 그에게는 내가 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 어떤 먹구름 같은 것이 드리워 있었고, 부친께서 강녕하시더냐고 내가 묻자, 그는 자못 짧게 \"여느 때와 다름없으시네.\" 하고 대답했다."], ["However, the two Miss Ablewhites were cheerful enough for twenty, which more than restored the balance.", "그렇지만 두 에이블화이트 아가씨는 스무 사람 몫으로 명랑하여, 그 균형을 넉넉히 되돌려 놓고도 남았다."], ["They were nearly as big as their brother; spanking, yellow-haired, rosy lasses, overflowing with super-abundant flesh and blood; bursting from head to foot with health and spirits.", "그들은 제 오라비만큼이나 덩치가 컸다. 팔팔하고, 머리가 노랗고, 발그레한 처녀들로, 넘쳐 나는 살과 피가 그득하고, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 건강과 기운이 터질 듯했다."], ["The legs of the poor horses trembled with carrying them; and when they jumped from their saddles (without waiting to be helped), I declare they bounced on the ground as if they were made of india-rubber.", "그들을 태운 가엾은 말들은 다리가 후들거렸다. 그리고 그들이 (부축을 기다리지도 않고) 안장에서 뛰어내릴 때, 정말이지 그들은 마치 인도 고무로 만들어지기라도 한 듯 땅에서 통통 튀었다."], ["Everything the Miss Ablewhites said began with a large O; everything they did was done with a bang; and they giggled and screamed, in season and out of season, on the smallest provocation.", "에이블화이트 아가씨들이 하는 말은 죄다 커다란 \"오!\"로 시작했고, 그들이 하는 짓은 죄다 쾅 소리와 함께였으며, 그들은 때를 가리지 않고, 아주 사소한 자극에도 킥킥거리고 꺅꺅거렸다."], ["Bouncers—that’s what I call them.", "통통이들—그것이 내가 그들을 부르는 이름이다."]], [["Under cover of the noise made by the young ladies, I had an opportunity of saying a private word to Mr. Franklin in the hall.", "젊은 아가씨들이 내는 소란을 틈타, 나는 현관에서 프랭클린 도련님께 남몰래 한마디 건넬 기회를 얻었다."]], [["“Have you got the Diamond safe, sir?”", "\"그 다이아몬드는 안전히 챙겨 오셨습니까요, 도련님?\""]], [["He nodded, and tapped the breast-pocket of his coat.", "그는 고개를 끄덕이며, 제 외투 가슴 주머니를 톡톡 쳤다."]], [["“Have you seen anything of the Indians?”", "\"인도인들은 뭐 좀 보이던가요?\""]], [["“Not a glimpse.”", "\"그림자도 없었어.\""], ["With that answer, he asked for my lady, and, hearing she was in the small drawing-room, went there straight.", "그 대답과 함께, 그는 마님을 찾았고, 마님이 작은 응접실에 계시다는 말을 듣자 곧장 그리로 갔다."], ["The bell rang, before he had been a minute in the room, and Penelope was sent to tell Miss Rachel that Mr. Franklin Blake wanted to speak to her.", "그가 그 방에 든 지 일 분도 채 되기 전에 종이 울렸고, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 이야기를 나누고자 하신다는 것을 레이철 아가씨께 알리라고 페넬로피가 보내졌다."]], [["Crossing the hall, about half an hour afterwards, I was brought to a sudden standstill by an outbreak of screams from the small drawing-room.", "그로부터 반 시간쯤 뒤, 현관을 가로지르다가, 나는 작은 응접실에서 터져 나온 비명 소리에 우뚝 걸음을 멈췄다. 내가 조금이라도 놀랐다고는 할 수 없다."], ["I can’t say I was at all alarmed; for I recognised in the screams the favourite large O of the Miss Ablewhites.", "그 비명 속에서 나는 에이블화이트 아가씨들이 즐겨 내는 그 커다란 \"오!\"를 알아차렸으니까."], ["However, I went in (on pretence of asking for instructions about the dinner) to discover whether anything serious had really happened.", "그렇지만 나는 (저녁 식사에 관한 분부를 여쭙는다는 구실로) 안으로 들어가, 정말로 무슨 심상찮은 일이 일어났는지 알아보았다."]], [["There stood Miss Rachel at the table, like a person fascinated, with the Colonel’s unlucky Diamond in her hand.", "거기, 레이철 아가씨가 탁자 곁에, 마치 홀린 사람처럼, 대령의 그 불길한 다이아몬드를 손에 들고 서 있었다."], ["There, on either side of her, knelt the two Bouncers, devouring the jewel with their eyes, and screaming with ecstasy every time it flashed on them in a new light. There, at the opposite side of the table, stood Mr.", "거기, 아가씨 양옆에는, 두 통통이가 무릎을 꿇고 앉아, 그 보석을 눈으로 집어삼킬 듯 바라보며, 그것이 새로운 빛으로 저희에게 번쩍일 때마다 황홀하여 비명을 질렀다."], ["Godfrey, clapping his hands like a large child, and singing out softly, “Exquisite! exquisite!”", "거기, 탁자 맞은편에는, 고드프리 씨가 커다란 어린애처럼 손뼉을 치며, \"빼어나!"], ["There sat Mr.", "빼어나!\""], ["Franklin in a chair by the bookcase, tugging at his beard, and looking anxiously towards the window.", "하고 나직이 노래하듯 외치고 서 있었다. 거기, 책장 옆 의자에는 프랭클린 도련님이 앉아, 제 턱수염을 잡아당기며, 근심스레 창 쪽을 바라보고 있었다."], ["And there, at the window, stood the object he was contemplating—my lady, having the extract from the Colonel’s Will in her hand, and keeping her back turned on the whole of the company.", "그리고 거기, 그 창가에는, 그가 바라보던 그 대상이 서 있었으니—마님이, 대령의 유언장 발췌본을 손에 든 채, 온 좌중에 등을 돌리고 계셨다."]], [["She faced me, when I asked for my instructions; and I saw the family frown gathering over her eyes, and the family temper twitching at the corners of her mouth.", "내가 분부를 여쭙자 마님이 내 쪽으로 돌아서셨다. 그리고 나는, 그 집안 특유의 찌푸림이 마님의 눈 위로 모여드는 것과, 그 집안 특유의 성미가 마님의 입가에서 씰룩거리는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Come to my room in half an hour,”", "\"반 시간 뒤에 내 방으로 오게.\""], ["she answered.", "마님이 대답하셨다."], ["“I shall have something to say to you then.”", "\"그때 자네한테 할 말이 있을 게야.\""]], [["With those words, she went out.", "그 말을 남기고, 마님은 나가셨다."], ["It was plain enough that she was posed by the same difficulty which had posed Mr. Franklin and me in our conference at the Shivering Sand.", "마님이, 떠는 모래펄에서 프랭클린 도련님과 나를 담판 중에 난감하게 했던 바로 그 똑같은 어려움에 난감해하고 계심은 뻔했다."], ["Was the legacy of the Moonstone a proof that she had treated her brother with cruel injustice?", "월장석의 유증(遺贈)은, 마님이 당신 오라비를 잔인하고 부당하게 대했다는 증거였을까?"], ["or was it a proof that he was worse than the worst she had ever thought of him?", "아니면 그가, 마님이 여태껏 그에 대해 생각해 온 그 가장 나쁜 것보다도 더 나빴다는 증거였을까?"], ["Serious questions those for my lady to determine, while her daughter, innocent of all knowledge of the Colonel’s character, stood there with the Colonel’s birthday gift in her hand.", "대령의 사람됨에 대해서는 아무것도 모르는 마님의 따님이, 대령의 생일 선물을 손에 들고 거기 서 있는 동안, 마님이 판가름해야 할 그 물음들은 심각한 것이었다."]], [["Before I could leave the room in my turn, Miss Rachel, always considerate to the old servant who had been in the house when she was born, stopped me.", "내 차례로 그 방을 나서기도 전에, 늘 제가 태어날 적부터 이 집에 있던 늙은 하인을 살뜰히 챙기는 레이철 아가씨가 나를 붙들었다."], ["“Look, Gabriel!”", "\"보세요, 게이브리얼!\""], ["she said, and flashed the jewel before my eyes in a ray of sunlight that poured through the window.", "아가씨가 말하며, 창으로 쏟아져 드는 한 줄기 햇살 속에 그 보석을 내 눈앞에서 번쩍 비춰 보였다."]], [["Lord bless us!", "우리를 굽어살피소서!"], ["it was a Diamond!", "그것은 정말이지 다이아몬드였다!"], ["As large, or nearly, as a plover’s egg!", "물떼새 알만큼, 아니 거의 그만큼 큰!"], ["The light that streamed from it was like the light of the harvest moon.", "그것에서 흘러나오는 빛은 마치 한가위 보름달 빛 같았다."], ["When you looked down into the stone, you looked into a yellow deep that drew your eyes into it so that they saw nothing else.", "그 돌 속을 들여다보면, 여러분은 여러분의 눈을 그 속으로 끌어들여 다른 아무것도 보이지 않게 만드는 어떤 노란 심연을 들여다보는 것이었다."], ["It seemed unfathomable; this jewel, that you could hold between your finger and thumb, seemed unfathomable as the heavens themselves.", "그것은 헤아릴 길이 없어 보였다. 여러분이 엄지와 검지 사이에 쥘 수 있는 이 보석이, 하늘 그 자체만큼이나 헤아릴 길이 없어 보였다."], ["We set it in the sun, and then shut the light out of the room, and it shone awfully out of the depths of its own brightness, with a moony gleam, in the dark.", "우리가 그것을 햇빛 속에 두었다가 방 안의 빛을 차단하니, 그것은 어둠 속에서, 달빛 같은 미광(微光)으로, 제 자신의 밝음의 심연에서 무시무시하게 빛을 뿜었다."], ["No wonder Miss Rachel was fascinated: no wonder her cousins screamed.", "레이철 아가씨가 홀린 것도 무리가 아니었다. 아가씨의 사촌들이 비명을 지른 것도 무리가 아니었다."], ["The Diamond laid such a hold on me that I burst out with as large an “O” as the Bouncers themselves.", "그 다이아몬드가 나까지 어찌나 사로잡던지, 나도 그 통통이들 못지않게 커다란 \"오!\"를 터뜨리고 말았다."], ["The only one of us who kept his senses was Mr. Godfrey.", "우리 중 홀로 제정신을 지킨 이는 고드프리 씨뿐이었다."], ["He put an arm round each of his sisters’ waists, and, looking compassionately backwards and forwards between the Diamond and me, said, “Carbon, Betteredge!", "그는 제 누이 둘의 허리에 한 팔씩 두르고서, 그 다이아몬드와 나를 번갈아 딱하다는 듯 바라보며 말했다. \"탄소일세, 베터리지!"], ["mere carbon, my good friend, after all!”", "그저 탄소야, 이 사람아, 따지고 보면!\""]], [["His object, I suppose, was to instruct me.", "그의 뜻은, 짐작건대, 나를 깨우쳐 주려는 것이었으리라."], ["All he did, however, was to remind me of the dinner.", "그러나 그가 한 일이라곤, 내게 저녁 식사를 떠올려 준 것뿐이었다."], ["I hobbled off to my army of waiters downstairs. As I went out, Mr.", "나는 아래층의 내 시중꾼 군대를 향해 절뚝절뚝 걸어갔다."], ["Godfrey said, “Dear old Betteredge, I have the truest regard for him!”", "내가 나가는데, 고드프리 씨가 말했다. \"그리운 베터리지 영감, 나는 그를 진심으로 아낀다니까!\""], ["He was embracing his sisters, and ogling Miss Rachel, while he honoured me with that testimony of affection.", "그는 그 애정의 증언으로 나를 치켜세우면서, 제 누이들을 얼싸안고, 레이철 아가씨에게 추파를 던지고 있었다."], ["Something like a stock of love to draw on there! Mr.", "저기 저 사람한테는 끌어다 쓸 사랑이 무슨 밑천처럼 그득한 모양이지!"], ["Franklin was a perfect savage by comparison with him.", "그에 견주면 프랭클린 도련님은 그야말로 야만인이었다."]], [["At the end of half an hour, I presented myself, as directed, in my lady’s room.", "반 시간이 지나, 나는 분부받은 대로 마님의 방에 나타났다."]], [["What passed between my mistress and me, on this occasion, was, in the main, a repetition of what had passed between Mr. Franklin and me at the Shivering Sand—with this difference, that I took care to keep my own counsel about the jugglers, seeing that nothing had happened to justify me in alarming my lady on this head.", "이 자리에서 내 여주인과 나 사이에 오간 것은, 대체로, 떠는 모래펄에서 프랭클린 도련님과 나 사이에 오간 것의 되풀이였다—다만 이 차이가 있었으니, 나는 그 요술쟁이들에 관해서는 내 속내를 감추어 두려 조심했다는 것이다."], ["When I received my dismissal, I could see that she took the blackest view possible of the Colonel’s motives, and that she was bent on getting the Moonstone out of her daughter’s possession at the first opportunity.", "이 대목을 두고 마님을 놀라게 해 드릴 만한 일은 아무것도 일어나지 않았음을 알고 있었으니까. 물러가라는 분부를 받았을 때, 나는 마님이 대령의 동기에 대해 있을 수 있는 가장 새까만 견해를 취하고 계심을, 그리고 마님이 첫 기회가 오는 대로 그 월장석을 당신 따님의 소유에서 빼내기로 작정하셨음을 알 수 있었다."]], [["On my way back to my own part of the house, I was encountered by Mr. Franklin.", "내 쪽 집으로 돌아가는 길에, 나는 프랭클린 도련님과 마주쳤다."], ["He wanted to know if I had seen anything of his cousin Rachel.", "그는 제 사촌 레이철을 뭐 좀 보았느냐고 알고 싶어 했다."], ["I had seen nothing of her.", "나는 아가씨를 전혀 보지 못했다."], ["Could I tell him where his cousin Godfrey was?", "제 사촌 고드프리가 어디 있는지 일러 줄 수 있느냐고."], ["I didn’t know; but I began to suspect that cousin Godfrey might not be far away from cousin Rachel.", "나는 몰랐다. 다만 나는 사촌 고드프리가 사촌 레이철에게서 그리 멀지 않은 데 있을지도 모른다고 의심하기 시작했다."], ["Mr. Franklin’s suspicions apparently took the same turn.", "프랭클린 도련님의 의심도 겉보기에 같은 쪽으로 돌아간 모양이었다."], ["He tugged hard at his beard, and went and shut himself up in the library with a bang of the door that had a world of meaning in it.", "그는 제 턱수염을 세게 잡아당기더니, 가서 서재에 틀어박히며 문을, 온갖 뜻이 담긴 그 문을, 쾅 닫아 버렸다."]], [["I was interrupted no more in the business of preparing for the birthday dinner till it was time for me to smarten myself up for receiving the company.", "생일 저녁 식사 채비하는 일에서 나는 더는 방해받지 않았고, 마침내 손님들을 맞으려고 내 몸을 말끔히 단장할 때가 되었다."], ["Just as I had got my white waistcoat on, Penelope presented herself at my toilet, on pretence of brushing what little hair I have got left, and improving the tie of my white cravat.", "내가 막 흰 조끼를 입었을 때, 페넬로피가, 내 몇 오라기 안 남은 머리칼을 빗기고 내 흰 넥타이 매듭을 손봐 준다는 구실로, 내 화장대 앞에 나타났다."], ["My girl was in high spirits, and I saw she had something to say to me.", "내 딸은 잔뜩 들떠 있었고, 나는 그 애가 내게 할 말이 있음을 알았다."], ["She gave me a kiss on the top of my bald head, and whispered, “News for you, father!", "그 애는 내 벗어진 머리 꼭대기에 입을 맞추고는 속삭였다. \"아버지, 소식이 있어요!"], ["Miss Rachel has refused him.”", "레이철 아가씨가 그 사람을 거절했대요.\""]], [["“Who’s ‘him’?”", "\"그 '사람'이 누구냐?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“The ladies’ committee-man, father,”", "\"그 부인회 위원 나리요, 아버지.\""], ["says Penelope.", "페넬로피가 말한다."], ["“A nasty sly fellow!", "\"고약하고 능글맞은 작자예요!"], ["I hate him for trying to supplant Mr. Franklin!”", "프랭클린 도련님 자리를 가로채려 들다니, 저는 그 사람이 미워요!\""]], [["If I had had breath enough, I should certainly have protested against this indecent way of speaking of an eminent philanthropic character.", "내게 숨이 넉넉했더라면, 나는 틀림없이, 저명한 박애가를 두고 이토록 무엄하게 말하는 것에 항의했을 것이다."], ["But my daughter happened to be improving the tie of my cravat at that moment, and the whole strength of her feelings found its way into her fingers.", "그러나 그 순간 마침 내 딸이 내 넥타이 매듭을 손보고 있었고, 그 감정의 온 힘이 그 애 손가락으로 쏠려 들었다."], ["I never was more nearly strangled in my life.", "내 평생 그토록 목이 졸릴 뻔한 적은 없었다."]], [["“I saw him take her away alone into the rose-garden,”", "\"그 사람이 아가씨를 혼자 장미 정원으로 데리고 가는 걸 봤어요.\""], ["says Penelope.", "페넬로피가 말한다."], ["“And I waited behind the holly to see how they came back.", "\"그래서 저는 그분들이 어떻게 돌아오나 보려고 호랑가시나무 뒤에서 기다렸죠."], ["They had gone out arm-in-arm, both laughing.", "나갈 땐 둘이 팔짱을 끼고, 둘 다 웃으면서 갔어요."], ["They came back, walking separate, as grave as grave could be, and looking straight away from each other in a manner which there was no mistaking.", "돌아올 땐, 따로따로 걸으며, 더없이 근엄하게, 그리고 서로에게서 고개를 딱 돌린 채로였는데, 그건 오해할 여지가 없는 모양새였어요."], ["I never was more delighted, father, in my life!", "저는 제 평생 그토록 기뻤던 적이 없어요, 아버지!"], ["There’s one woman in the world who can resist Mr.", "세상에 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 이겨 낼 수 있는 여자가 적어도 하나는 있는 거예요."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite, at any rate; and, if I was a lady, I should be another!”", "그리고 제가 만일 숙녀라면, 저도 또 하나가 될 거고요!\""]], [["Here I should have protested again.", "여기서 나는 또 항의했어야 마땅하다."], ["But my daughter had got the hair-brush by this time, and the whole strength of her feelings had passed into that.", "그러나 이때쯤 내 딸은 머리빗을 들고 있었고, 그 감정의 온 힘이 그리로 옮아가 있었다."], ["If you are bald, you will understand how she sacrificed me.", "여러분이 대머리라면, 그 애가 나를 어떻게 제물 삼았는지 아시리라."], ["If you are not, skip this bit, and thank God you have got something in the way of a defence between your hair-brush and your head.", "대머리가 아니시라면, 이 대목은 건너뛰시고, 여러분의 머리빗과 여러분의 머리 사이에 방패 노릇을 할 무언가가 있음을 하느님께 감사하시라."]], [["“Just on the other side of the holly,”", "\"바로 그 호랑가시나무 저쪽 편에서,\" 페넬로피가 계속했다."], ["Penelope went on, “Mr.", "\"고드프리 씨가 우뚝 멈춰 섰어요. '그럼 넌,'"], ["Godfrey came to a standstill.", "하고 그가 말해요. '내가 아무 일도 없었다는 듯 여기 이대로 머물기를 바라는 거니?'"], ["‘You prefer,’ says he, ‘that I should stop here as if nothing had happened?’ Miss Rachel turned on him like lightning.", "레이철 아가씨가 번갯불처럼 그에게 홱 돌아섰어요. '오라버니는 우리 어머니의 초대를 받아들였잖아요.'"], ["‘You have accepted my mother’s invitation,’ she said; ‘and you are here to meet her guests.", "아가씨가 말했어요. '그리고 어머니의 손님들을 맞으러 여기 온 거고요."], ["Unless you wish to make a scandal in the house, you will remain, of course!’ She went on a few steps, and then seemed to relent a little.", "이 집안에 추문을 일으키고 싶은 게 아니라면, 물론 머무셔야죠!' 아가씨가 몇 걸음 더 가더니, 조금 누그러진 듯했어요."], ["‘Let us forget what has passed, Godfrey,’ she said, ‘and let us remain cousins still.’ She gave him her hand.", "'지난 일은 잊기로 해요, 고드프리.' 아가씨가 말했어요."], ["He kissed it, which I should have considered taking a liberty, and then she left him.", "'그리고 우린 여전히 사촌으로 남기로 해요.' 아가씨가 그에게 손을 내밀었어요."], ["He waited a little by himself, with his head down, and his heel grinding a hole slowly in the gravel walk; you never saw a man look more put out in your life.", "그가 그 손에 입을 맞췄는데, 저 같으면 그걸 주제넘은 짓으로 여겼을 거예요. 그러고 나서 아가씨가 그를 두고 떠났어요."], ["‘Awkward!’ he said between his teeth, when he looked up, and went on to the house—‘very awkward!’ If that was his opinion of himself, he was quite right.", "그는 고개를 떨군 채, 발꿈치로 자갈길에 천천히 구멍을 갈아 파며, 잠시 혼자 서 있었어요. 그렇게 낭패한 얼굴을 한 사내는 평생 보신 적 없으실 거예요."], ["Awkward enough, I’m sure.", "'난처하군!' 그가 고개를 들며 이 사이로 중얼거리더니, 집 쪽으로 가면서—'아주 난처해!'"], ["And the end of it is, father, what I told you all along,”", "그게 자기 자신에 대한 그의 생각이었다면, 그는 아주 옳았어요. 여간 난처하지 않았을 테니까요, 틀림없이."], ["cries Penelope, finishing me off with a last scarification, the hottest of all.", "그리고 그 결말은, 아버지, 제가 내내 아버지께 말씀드린 그대로예요.\" 하고 페넬로피가, 마지막 생채기를, 그중에서도 가장 뜨거운 것을 내며 나를 끝장내며 외친다."], ["“Mr. Franklin’s the man!”", "\"프랭클린 도련님이 바로 그 사람이라니까요!\""]], [["I got possession of the hair-brush, and opened my lips to administer the reproof which, you will own, my daughter’s language and conduct richly deserved.", "나는 머리빗을 손에 넣고서, 여러분도 인정하시겠지만 내 딸의 말과 처신이 넉넉히 받아 마땅한 그 꾸지람을 내리려고 입술을 열었다."]], [["Before I could say a word, the crash of carriage-wheels outside struck in, and stopped me.", "내가 미처 한마디 하기도 전에, 바깥에서 마차 바퀴가 요란하게 굴러드는 소리가 끼어들어 나를 멈춰 세웠다."], ["The first of the dinner-company had come.", "저녁 손님의 첫 무리가 온 것이다."], ["Penelope instantly ran off.", "페넬로피가 대뜸 달아났다."], ["I put on my coat, and looked in the glass.", "나는 외투를 걸치고 거울을 들여다보았다."], ["My head was as red as a lobster; but, in other respects, I was as nicely dressed for the ceremonies of the evening as a man need be.", "내 머리는 바닷가재처럼 새빨갰다. 그러나 그 밖의 점으로는, 나는 그 저녁 예식을 위해 사내가 갖춰 입어야 할 만큼 말끔히 차려입은 모습이었다."], ["I got into the hall just in time to announce the two first of the guests.", "나는 마침맞게 현관에 들어서서 첫 두 손님을 알렸다."], ["You needn’t feel particularly interested about them. Only the philanthropist’s father and mother—Mr.", "그 두 분에 대해서는 여러분이 딱히 관심을 두실 것 없다."], ["and Mrs. Ablewhite.", "그저 그 박애가의 아버지와 어머니—에이블화이트 씨 부부일 뿐이니."]], [["# CHAPTER X", "# CHAPTER X"]], [["One on the top of the other the rest of the company followed the Ablewhites, till we had the whole tale of them complete.", "에이블화이트 씨네 뒤로 나머지 손님들이 꼬리를 물고 잇달아 들어와, 마침내 그들 무리가 죄다 채워졌다."], ["Including the family, they were twenty-four in all.", "집안 식구까지 넣어, 그들은 모두 스물넷이었다."], ["It was a noble sight to see, when they were settled in their places round the dinner-table, and the Rector of Frizinghall (with beautiful elocution) rose and said grace.", "그들이 저녁 식탁을 빙 둘러 제자리에 자리 잡고, 프리징홀 교구 목사가 (고운 낭독 솜씨로) 일어나 감사 기도를 올리는 광경은, 보기에 훌륭한 것이었다."]], [["There is no need to worry you with a list of the guests.", "손님 명단으로 여러분을 성가시게 할 것은 없다."], ["You will meet none of them a second time—in my part of the story, at any rate—with the exception of two.", "그들 가운데 여러분이 두 번째로 다시 만나실 이는—적어도 내 몫의 이야기에서는—단 둘을 빼고는 아무도 없을 테니 말이다."]], [["Those two sat on either side of Miss Rachel, who, as queen of the day, was naturally the great attraction of the party.", "그 둘은 레이철 아가씨의 양옆에 앉았는데, 그날의 여왕인 아가씨는 당연히 그 잔치의 큰 볼거리였다."], ["On this occasion she was more particularly the centre-point towards which everybody’s eyes were directed; for (to my lady’s secret annoyance) she wore her wonderful birthday present, which eclipsed all the rest—the Moonstone.", "이 자리에서 아가씨는 특히나 모든 이의 눈길이 쏠리는 중심점이었으니, (마님이 남몰래 언짢아하시게도) 아가씨가 그 놀라운 생일 선물, 곧 나머지 모든 것을 무색하게 하는 그 월장석을 걸치고 있었기 때문이다."], ["It was without any setting when it had been placed in her hands; but that universal genius, Mr. Franklin, had contrived, with the help of his neat fingers and a little bit of silver wire, to fix it as a brooch in the bosom of her white dress.", "그것은 아가씨 손에 쥐여졌을 때는 아무 테두리 장식이 없었으나, 저 만능 재주꾼 프랭클린 도련님이, 제 야무진 손가락과 은철사 한 오라기의 도움으로, 그것을 아가씨의 흰 드레스 가슴에 브로치로 고정해 놓았다."], ["Everybody wondered at the prodigious size and beauty of the Diamond, as a matter of course.", "모두가 그 다이아몬드의 엄청난 크기와 아름다움에 감탄한 것은 물론이었다."], ["But the only two of the company who said anything out of the common way about it were those two guests I have mentioned, who sat by Miss Rachel on her right hand and her left.", "그러나 그것을 두고 예사롭지 않은 말을 한 사람은 좌중에서 오직 둘뿐이었으니, 곧 내가 언급한, 레이철 아가씨의 오른편과 왼편에 앉은 그 두 손님이었다."]], [["The guest on her left was Mr. Candy, our doctor at Frizinghall.", "아가씨 왼편의 손님은 캔디 씨, 곧 프리징홀에 있는 우리 의사였다."]], [["This was a pleasant, companionable little man, with the drawback, however, I must own, of being too fond, in season and out of season, of his joke, and of his plunging in rather a headlong manner into talk with strangers, without waiting to feel his way first.", "이 사람은 유쾌하고 붙임성 있는 자그마한 사내였으나, 다만 내가 인정해야 할 흠이 있었으니, 때를 가리지 않고 제 농담을 지나치게 즐기고, 우선 조심스레 실마리를 더듬어 볼 것도 없이 자못 무모하게 낯선 이들과의 이야기에 뛰어드는 것이었다."], ["In society, he was constantly making mistakes, and setting people unintentionally by the ears together.", "사교장에서 그는 끊임없이 실수를 저지르고, 뜻하지 않게 사람들을 서로 다투게 만들었다. 의술을 펼 때 그는 더 신중한 사람이었다."], ["In his medical practice he was a more prudent man; picking up his discretion (as his enemies said) by a kind of instinct, and proving to be generally right where more carefully conducted doctors turned out to be wrong.", "(그의 적들의 말로는) 일종의 본능으로 분별을 주워 담아, 더 조심스레 진료하는 의사들이 그르다고 드러날 자리에서 대개는 옳다고 판명되곤 했다. 그가 레이철 아가씨께 그 다이아몬드에 대해 한 말은, 여느 때처럼, 어리둥절하게 하거나 농담하는 식으로 한 것이었다."], ["What he said about the Diamond to Miss Rachel was said, as usual, by way of a mystification or joke.", "그는 (과학을 위해서라며) 그것을 제집으로 가져가 태우게 해 달라고 짐짓 엄숙히 아가씨께 청했다."], ["He gravely entreated her (in the interests of science) to let him take it home and burn it.", "\"우선 그것을 이만저만한 온도로 달구겠습니다, 레이철 아가씨.\" 하고 의사가 말한다."], ["“We will first heat it, Miss Rachel,”", "\"그런 다음 그것을 공기의 흐름에 쐬는 겁니다."], ["says the doctor, “to such and such a degree; then we will expose it to a current of air; and, little by little—puff!—we evaporate the Diamond, and spare you a world of anxiety about the safe keeping of a valuable precious stone!”", "그러면, 조금씩 조금씩—확!—우리는 그 다이아몬드를 증발시켜, 값비싼 귀한 돌을 안전히 간수하는 일에 대한 온갖 근심에서 아가씨를 벗어나게 해 드리는 겁니다요!\""], ["My lady, listening with rather a careworn expression on her face, seemed to wish that the doctor had been in earnest, and that he could have found Miss Rachel zealous enough in the cause of science to sacrifice her birthday gift.", "얼굴에 자못 근심스러운 빛을 띠고 듣고 계시던 마님은, 그 의사가 진심이었더라면, 그리고 레이철 아가씨가 제 생일 선물을 희생할 만큼 과학의 대의에 열성적임을 그가 알아냈더라면 좋았으련만, 하고 바라시는 듯했다."]], [["The other guest, who sat on my young lady’s right hand, was an eminent public character—being no other than the celebrated Indian traveller, Mr.", "우리 젊은 아가씨의 오른편에 앉은 다른 손님은 저명한 공인이었으니—다름 아닌 그 유명한 인도 여행가 머스웨이트 씨였다."], ["Murthwaite, who, at risk of his life, had penetrated in disguise where no European had ever set foot before.", "그는, 제 목숨을 걸고, 여태 어느 유럽인도 발을 들인 적 없는 곳에 변장하고 뚫고 들어간 인물이었다."]], [["This was a long, lean, wiry, brown, silent man. He had a weary look, and a very steady, attentive eye.", "이 사람은 키가 크고, 여위고, 근골이 질기고, 살빛이 검은, 과묵한 사내였다."], ["It was rumoured that he was tired of the humdrum life among the people in our parts, and longing to go back and wander off on the tramp again in the wild places of the East.", "그에게는 지친 기색과, 아주 흔들림 없고 주의 깊은 눈이 있었다. 소문으로는, 그가 우리 고장 사람들 사이의 단조로운 삶에 진력이 나서, 다시 돌아가 동방의 거친 땅을 정처 없이 떠돌고 싶어 못 견딘다는 것이었다."], ["Except what he said to Miss Rachel about her jewel, I doubt if he spoke six words or drank so much as a single glass of wine, all through the dinner.", "그가 레이철 아가씨께 그 보석에 대해 한 말을 빼면, 나는 그가 저녁 식사 내내 여섯 마디나 했는지, 포도주를 한 잔이라도 마셨는지조차 의심스럽다."], ["The Moonstone was the only object that interested him in the smallest degree.", "월장석만이 그가 조금이라도 관심을 둔 유일한 대상이었다."], ["The fame of it seemed to have reached him, in some of those perilous Indian places where his wanderings had lain.", "그것의 명성이, 그의 방랑길이 뻗어 있던 그 위험한 인도의 어느 곳들에서, 그의 귀에까지 닿았던 모양이다."], ["After looking at it silently for so long a time that Miss Rachel began to get confused, he said to her in his cool immovable way, “If you ever go to India, Miss Verinder, don’t take your uncle’s birthday gift with you.", "그가 하도 오래 말없이 그것을 바라보아 레이철 아가씨가 거북해지기 시작할 무렵, 그는 그 차갑고 요지부동한 투로 아가씨께 말했다. \"혹 인도에 가시거든, 버린더 아가씨, 외숙부의 생일 선물은 지니고 가지 마십시오."], ["A Hindoo diamond is sometimes part of a Hindoo religion.", "힌두교 다이아몬드는 때로 힌두교 종교의 일부입니다."], ["I know a certain city, and a certain temple in that city, where, dressed as you are now, your life would not be worth five minutes’ purchase.”", "나는 어떤 도시를, 그리고 그 도시의 어떤 사원을 알고 있는데, 거기서라면 지금 아가씨 차림 그대로는 아가씨 목숨이 오 분어치도 못 될 겁니다.\""], ["Miss Rachel, safe in England, was quite delighted to hear of her danger in India.", "영국에 안전히 있는 레이철 아가씨는, 인도에서의 제 위험 이야기를 듣고 자못 신이 났다."], ["The Bouncers were more delighted still; they dropped their knives and forks with a crash, and burst out together vehemently, “O!", "통통이들은 한술 더 떠 신이 났다. 그들은 나이프와 포크를 쨍그랑 떨어뜨리며, 한목소리로 격하게 터뜨렸다."], ["how interesting!”", "\"오! 어쩜 그렇게 흥미로울까!\""], ["My lady fidgeted in her chair, and changed the subject.", "마님은 의자에서 안절부절못하시며, 화제를 바꾸셨다."]], [["As the dinner got on, I became aware, little by little, that this festival was not prospering as other like festivals had prospered before it.", "저녁 식사가 진행되면서, 나는 조금씩 조금씩, 이 잔치가 그 전의 여느 비슷한 잔치들이 잘 흘러간 것처럼 잘 흘러가고 있지 않음을 알아차렸다."]], [["Looking back at the birthday now, by the light of what happened afterwards, I am half inclined to think that the cursed Diamond must have cast a blight on the whole company.", "이제 와서, 그 뒤에 일어난 일에 비추어 그 생일을 되돌아보면, 나는, 그 저주받은 다이아몬드가 온 좌중에 무슨 마름병을 드리운 것이 틀림없다는 생각이 반쯤 든다."], ["I plied them well with wine; and being a privileged character, followed the unpopular dishes round the table, and whispered to the company confidentially, “Please to change your mind and try it; for I know it will do you good.”", "나는 그들에게 포도주를 넉넉히 권했고, 특권을 지닌 사람인지라, 인기 없는 요리를 들고 식탁을 돌며 좌중에게 은밀히 속삭였다. \"부디 마음을 바꾸어 이걸 좀 드셔 보십시오."], ["Nine times out of ten they changed their minds—out of regard for their old original Betteredge, they were pleased to say—but all to no purpose.", "몸에 좋을 줄 제가 아니까요.\" 열에 아홉은 마음을 바꾸었으나—저희 오래되고 한결같은 베터리지를 봐서 그런다고 기꺼이 말해 주면서—죄다 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["There were gaps of silence in the talk, as the dinner got on, that made me feel personally uncomfortable.", "저녁 식사가 진행되면서, 이야기에 침묵의 틈이 생겨, 나 자신이 다 거북해질 지경이었다."], ["When they did use their tongues again, they used them innocently, in the most unfortunate manner and to the worst possible purpose.", "그들이 다시 혀를 놀릴 때면, 아무 악의 없이, 더없이 불운한 방식으로, 있을 수 있는 가장 나쁜 목적으로 그것을 놀렸다."], ["Mr. Candy, the doctor, for instance, said more unlucky things than I ever knew him to say before.", "이를테면 의사 캔디 씨는, 내가 그가 여태껏 하는 것을 본 것보다 더 불길한 소리들을 했다."], ["Take one sample of the way in which he went on, and you will understand what I had to put up with at the sideboard, officiating as I was in the character of a man who had the prosperity of the festival at heart.", "그가 어떤 식으로 굴었는지 한 가지 본보기만 들어 보시면, 잔치의 흥성을 마음에 두고 그 소임을 맡은 사람으로서 내가 식기대 곁에서 무엇을 견뎌 내야 했는지 여러분도 아시게 될 것이다."]], [["One of our ladies present at dinner was worthy Mrs. Threadgall, widow of the late Professor of that name.", "저녁 식사에 자리한 우리 숙녀분들 가운데, 지금은 고인이 된 같은 이름의 교수의 미망인, 훌륭한 스레드골 부인이 있었다."], ["Talking of her deceased husband perpetually, this good lady never mentioned to strangers that he was deceased.", "이 어지신 부인은 돌아가신 남편 이야기를 노상 하면서도, 낯선 이들에게는 그가 고인이라는 것을 결코 입에 올리지 않았다."], ["She thought, I suppose, that every able-bodied adult in England ought to know as much as that.", "짐작건대 부인은, 영국의 성한 몸을 가진 성인이라면 누구나 그쯤은 알고 있어야 마땅하다고 여겼던 모양이다."], ["In one of the gaps of silence, somebody mentioned the dry and rather nasty subject of human anatomy; whereupon good Mrs.", "침묵의 틈 하나에서, 누군가가 인체 해부라는 무미건조하고 자못 께름칙한 화제를 꺼냈다."], ["Threadgall straightway brought in her late husband as usual, without mentioning that he was dead.", "그러자 어지신 스레드골 부인은 여느 때처럼 대뜸 돌아가신 남편을 끌어들였는데, 그가 죽은 사람이라는 말은 하지 않았다."], ["Anatomy she described as the Professor’s favourite recreation in his leisure hours.", "부인은 해부학을 두고, 교수가 여가에 즐기던 취미라고 묘사했다."], ["As ill-luck would have it, Mr. Candy, sitting opposite (who knew nothing of the deceased gentleman), heard her.", "불운하게도, 맞은편에 앉은 캔디 씨는 (그 돌아가신 신사에 대해 아무것도 몰랐는데) 부인의 말을 들었다."], ["Being the most polite of men, he seized the opportunity of assisting the Professor’s anatomical amusements on the spot.", "더없이 예의 바른 사람인지라, 그는 그 자리에서 그 교수의 해부학적 취미를 거들 기회를 냉큼 붙잡았다."]], [["“They have got some remarkably fine skeletons lately at the College of Surgeons,”", "\"요즈음 외과 의사 학교에 유난히 훌륭한 골격 표본이 몇 점 들어왔답니다.\""], ["says Mr. Candy, across the table, in a loud cheerful voice.", "하고 캔디 씨가 식탁 너머로, 크고 명랑한 목소리로 말한다."], ["“I strongly recommend the Professor, ma’am, when he next has an hour to spare, to pay them a visit.”", "\"부인, 교수님께 강력히 권합니다, 다음번에 한 시간 짬이 나거든 꼭 한번 가서 그것들을 구경하시라고요.\""]], [["You might have heard a pin fall.", "핀 떨어지는 소리라도 들렸을 것이다."], ["The company (out of respect to the Professor’s memory) all sat speechless.", "좌중은 (교수의 넋을 기려) 죄다 말없이 앉아 있었다."], ["I was behind Mrs. Threadgall at the time, plying her confidentially with a glass of hock.", "나는 그때 스레드골 부인 뒤에서, 부인께 백포도주 한 잔을 은밀히 권하고 있었다."], ["She dropped her head, and said in a very low voice, “My beloved husband is no more.”", "부인은 고개를 떨구고, 아주 나직한 목소리로 말했다. \"내 사랑하는 남편은 이미 세상에 없어요.\""]], [["Unluckily Mr. Candy, hearing nothing, and miles away from suspecting the truth, went on across the table louder and politer than ever.", "불운하게도 캔디 씨는, 아무것도 못 듣고, 진상을 짐작하기에는 십만 리나 떨어져 있는지라, 식탁 너머로 그 어느 때보다 더 크고 더 정중하게 이어 갔다."]], [["“The Professor may not be aware,”", "\"교수님은 아마 모르실 겁니다.\""], ["says he, “that the card of a member of the College will admit him, on any day but Sunday, between the hours of ten and four.”", "하고 그가 말한다. \"학교 회원의 명함이 있으면, 일요일만 빼고 아무 날에나, 열 시부터 네 시 사이에 입장하실 수 있다는 것을요.\""]], [["Mrs.", "스레드골 부인은 고개를 제 옷깃 안으로 푹 떨구고, 더욱 나직한 목소리로, 그 엄숙한 말을 되풀이했다."], ["Threadgall dropped her head right into her tucker, and, in a lower voice still, repeated the solemn words, “My beloved husband is no more.”", "\"내 사랑하는 남편은 이미 세상에 없어요.\""]], [["I winked hard at Mr. Candy across the table.", "나는 식탁 너머로 캔디 씨에게 눈을 힘껏 찡긋했다."], ["Miss Rachel touched his arm.", "레이철 아가씨는 그의 팔을 건드렸다."], ["My lady looked unutterable things at him.", "마님은 그에게 이루 말 못 할 눈길을 보내셨다."], ["Quite useless!", "아주 헛일이었다!"], ["On he went, with a cordiality that there was no stopping anyhow.", "그는, 도무지 멈출 수 없는 다정함으로, 계속해 나갔다. \"기꺼이 교수님께 제 명함을 보내 드리겠습니다.\""], ["“I shall be delighted,”", "하고 그가 말한다."], ["says he, “to send the Professor my card, if you will oblige me by mentioning his present address.”", "\"지금 계신 주소를 일러 주시는 은혜를 베풀어 주신다면요.\""]], [["“His present address, sir, is the grave,” says Mrs.", "\"그이가 지금 있는 주소는요, 선생, 바로 무덤이랍니다.\""], ["Threadgall, suddenly losing her temper, and speaking with an emphasis and fury that made the glasses ring again.", "하고 스레드골 부인이, 별안간 성을 내며, 술잔들이 다시 쨍 울릴 만큼 힘주어 노기 띤 목소리로 말한다."], ["“The Professor has been dead these ten years.”", "\"그 교수는 죽은 지 십 년이 되었어요.\""]], [["“Oh, good Heavens!”", "\"오, 맙소사!\""], ["says Mr. Candy.", "하고 캔디 씨가 말한다."], ["Excepting the Bouncers, who burst out laughing, such a blank now fell on the company, that they might all have been going the way of the Professor, and hailing as he did from the direction of the grave.", "웃음을 터뜨린 통통이들만 빼고, 이제 좌중에 어찌나 허탈한 정적이 내려앉던지, 그들 모두가 그 교수의 뒤를 따라, 그처럼 무덤 쪽에서 부름을 받고 있기라도 한 것 같았다."]], [["So much for Mr. Candy.", "캔디 씨 이야기는 이쯤 해 두자."], ["The rest of them were nearly as provoking in their different ways as the doctor himself.", "나머지 사람들도 저마다 다른 식으로 그 의사 못지않게 사람 속을 뒤집어 놓았다."], ["When they ought to have spoken, they didn’t speak; or when they did speak they were perpetually at cross purposes.", "마땅히 말해야 할 때 그들은 말하지 않았고, 말을 할 때면 노상 서로 딴 데를 겨누었다."], ["Mr. Godfrey, though so eloquent in public, declined to exert himself in private.", "고드프리 씨는, 사람들 앞에서는 그토록 능변이면서도, 사석에서는 애써 나서기를 마다했다."], ["Whether he was sulky, or whether he was bashful, after his discomfiture in the rose-garden, I can’t say.", "장미 정원에서 낭패를 본 뒤 그가 뾰로통한 것인지, 아니면 수줍은 것인지는 나로선 알 수 없다."], ["He kept all his talk for the private ear of the lady (a member of our family) who sat next to him.", "그는 제 곁에 앉은 부인(우리 집안 사람이었다)의 귀에만 대고 온갖 이야기를 도맡아 했다."], ["She was one of his committee-women—a spiritually-minded person, with a fine show of collar-bone and a pretty taste in champagne; liked it dry, you understand, and plenty of it.", "부인은 그의 위원회 여인 중 하나로—신앙심이 도타운 사람이었는데, 쇄골을 곱게 드러내고, 샴페인에 제법 취미가 있었다. 아시겠지만, 달지 않은 것으로, 그것도 넉넉히 좋아했다."], ["Being close behind these two at the sideboard, I can testify, from what I heard pass between them, that the company lost a good deal of very improving conversation, which I caught up while drawing the corks, and carving the mutton, and so forth.", "식기대 곁에서 이 두 사람 바로 뒤에 있던지라, 나는 그들 사이에 오간 것을 들은 바로 증언할 수 있으니, 좌중은 여간 유익한 대화를 많이 놓친 것이 아니었다. 나는 그것을, 코르크 마개를 뽑고 양고기를 저미고 하는 사이에 주워들었다."], ["What they said about their Charities I didn’t hear.", "저희 자선회에 대해 그들이 무슨 말을 했는지는 나는 못 들었다."], ["When I had time to listen to them, they had got a long way beyond their women to be confined, and their women to be rescued, and were disputing on serious subjects.", "내가 그들 말에 귀 기울일 짬이 났을 때는, 그들은 저희가 해산을 도울 여자들이며 구제할 여자들 이야기에서 한참 멀리 나아가, 자못 진지한 주제를 놓고 갑론을박하고 있었다."], ["Religion (I understand Mr. Godfrey to say, between the corks and the carving) meant love.", "(코르크와 저미기 사이에서 고드프리 씨가 하는 말을 내가 알아들은 바로는) 종교란 사랑을 뜻했다."], ["And love meant religion.", "그리고 사랑이란 종교를 뜻했다."], ["And earth was heaven a little the worse for wear.", "그리고 이 땅은 조금 낡은 천국이었다."], ["And heaven was earth, done up again to look like new.", "그리고 천국은 새것처럼 다시 매만진 이 땅이었다."], ["Earth had some very objectionable people in it; but, to make amends for that, all the women in heaven would be members of a prodigious committee that never quarrelled, with all the men in attendance on them as ministering angels.", "이 땅에는 여간 아니꼬운 사람들이 좀 있었으나, 그것을 벌충하느라, 천국의 모든 여자는 결코 다투는 법 없는 어마어마한 위원회의 회원이 될 터이고, 모든 남자는 시중드는 천사가 되어 그들을 곁에서 받들 터였다."], ["Beautiful!", "아름답구려!"], ["beautiful!", "아름답구려!"], ["But why the mischief did Mr. Godfrey keep it all to his lady and himself?", "그런데 대체 무엇 때문에 고드프리 씨는 그 이야기를 죄다 제 부인과 저 자신한테만 담아 두었을까?"]], [["Mr. Franklin again—surely, you will say, Mr. Franklin stirred the company up into making a pleasant evening of it?", "그럼 프랭클린 도련님은—여러분은 필시 이렇게 말씀하시리라, 프랭클린 도련님이 좌중을 부추겨 그 저녁을 유쾌하게 만들었으리라고?"]], [["Nothing of the sort!", "천만에!"], ["He had quite recovered himself, and he was in wonderful force and spirits, Penelope having informed him, I suspect, of Mr. Godfrey’s reception in the rose-garden.", "그는 아주 기운을 되찾았고, 놀랍도록 힘차고 기운이 넘쳤으니, 짐작건대 페넬로피가 그에게 장미 정원에서의 고드프리 씨의 대접에 관해 일러 주었기 때문이다."], ["But, talk as he might, nine times out of ten he pitched on the wrong subject, or he addressed himself to the wrong person; the end of it being that he offended some, and puzzled all of them.", "그러나 아무리 이야기를 해도, 열에 아홉은 그가 엉뚱한 화제를 골랐거나, 엉뚱한 사람에게 말을 건넸으니, 그 끝은 그가 몇몇의 비위를 거스르고 모두를 어리둥절하게 만드는 것이었다."], ["That foreign training of his—those French and German and Italian sides of him, to which I have already alluded—came out, at my lady’s hospitable board, in a most bewildering manner.", "앞서 내가 언급한 그의 그 외국 수련—그의 프랑스식 면과 독일식 면과 이탈리아식 면이, 마님의 넉넉한 식탁에서, 더없이 얼떨떨한 방식으로 튀어나왔다."]], [["What do you think, for instance, of his discussing the lengths to which a married woman might let her admiration go for a man who was not her husband, and putting it in his clear-headed witty French way to the maiden aunt of the Vicar of Frizinghall?", "이를테면, 여러분은 어떻게 생각하시는가, 그가 유부녀가 제 남편 아닌 사내에게 얼마만큼까지 흠모를 품어도 되느냐 하는 정도를 논하며, 그것을 그 명석하고 재치 있는 프랑스식 투로, 프리징홀 교구 부목사의 노처녀 이모께 늘어놓은 것을?"], ["What do you think, when he shifted to the German side, of his telling the lord of the manor, while that great authority on cattle was quoting his experience in the breeding of bulls, that experience, properly understood, counted for nothing, and that the proper way to breed bulls was to look deep into your own mind, evolve out of it the idea of a perfect bull, and produce him?", "여러분은 어떻게 생각하시는가, 그가 독일식 면으로 옮아가, 소에 관한 그 대단한 권위자인 이 영지의 지주가 황소 사육에 관한 제 경험을 늘어놓는 동안, 그에게, 경험이란 것은 제대로 이해하면 아무것도 아니며, 황소를 사육하는 올바른 길은 제 마음속을 깊이 들여다보아 거기서 완벽한 황소의 관념을 뽑아내어 그놈을 낳는 것이라고 말한 것을?"], ["What do you say, when our county member, growing hot, at cheese and salad time, about the spread of democracy in England, burst out as follows: “If we once lose our ancient safeguards, Mr.", "여러분은 뭐라 하시겠는가, 치즈와 샐러드가 나올 무렵, 영국에서의 민주주의 확산을 놓고 열이 오른 우리 고을 의원이 이렇게 터뜨렸을 때—\"우리가 우리의 유서 깊은 방벽을 한번 잃고 나면, 블레이크 씨, 감히 여쭙습니다만, 우리에게 대체 무엇이 남습니까?\"—프랭클린 도련님이 이탈리아식 관점에서 이렇게 대답한 것에 대해서 말이오."], ["Blake, I beg to ask you, what have we got left?”—what do you say to Mr.", "\"우리한텐 세 가지가 남지요, 의원님—사랑, 음악, 그리고 샐러드요.\""], ["Franklin answering, from the Italian point of view: “We have got three things left, sir—Love, Music, and Salad”?", "그는 이런 돌출로 좌중을 질겁하게 했을 뿐 아니라, 때가 되어 그의 영국식 면이 튀어나오자, 그 외국식의 매끈함을 잃어버렸다."], ["He not only terrified the company with such outbreaks as these, but, when the English side of him turned up in due course, he lost his foreign smoothness; and, getting on the subject of the medical profession, said such downright things in ridicule of doctors, that he actually put good-humoured little Mr. Candy in a rage.", "그리고 의술이라는 주제에 들어서서는, 의사들을 조롱하는 그런 노골적인 말들을 해 대는 통에, 급기야 붙임성 좋고 자그마한 캔디 씨를 발끈하게 만들고 말았다."]], [["The dispute between them began in Mr. Franklin being led—I forget how—to acknowledge that he had latterly slept very badly at night.", "그 둘 사이의 언쟁은, 프랭클린 도련님이—어쩌다 그리 되었는지는 잊었으나—요즈음 밤에 잠을 몹시 설친다고 인정하도록 이끌린 데서 비롯됐다."], ["Mr. Candy thereupon told him that his nerves were all out of order and that he ought to go through a course of medicine immediately.", "그러자 캔디 씨는 그에게, 신경이 죄다 어긋나 있으니 당장 약물 치료를 한 차례 받아야 한다고 일렀다."], ["Mr. Franklin replied that a course of medicine, and a course of groping in the dark, meant, in his estimation, one and the same thing.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 약물 치료 한 차례나 어둠 속을 더듬는 것 한 차례나, 자기가 보기엔 그게 그거라고 대꾸했다."], ["Mr. Candy, hitting back smartly, said that Mr.", "캔디 씨는 날렵하게 되받아, 프랭클린 도련님 자신이야말로, 체질로 말하자면, 어둠 속에서 잠을 더듬고 있는 것이며, 약물 말고는 무엇도 그가 그것을 찾도록 도와줄 수 없다고 말했다."], ["Franklin himself was, constitutionally speaking, groping in the dark after sleep, and that nothing but medicine could help him to find it. Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 제 쪽에서 공을 계속 받아넘기며, 소경이 소경을 인도한다는 말은 여러 번 들었으나, 그것이 무슨 뜻인지는 이제 처음으로 알겠다고 말했다."], ["Franklin, keeping the ball up on his side, said he had often heard of the blind leading the blind, and now, for the first time, he knew what it meant. In this way, they kept it going briskly, cut and thrust, till they both of them got hot—Mr.", "이런 식으로 그들은, 치고 찌르며, 활발하게 그것을 이어 가다가, 둘 다 열이 올랐다—특히 캔디 씨는, 제 직업을 옹호하느라 어찌나 완전히 자제심을 잃던지, 마님이 끼어들어 그 언쟁을 그만두게 하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Candy, in particular, so completely losing his self-control, in defence of his profession, that my lady was obliged to interfere, and forbid the dispute to go on.", "이 부득이한 권위의 행사가 좌중의 흥에 마지막 불끄개를 덮어 버렸다."], ["This necessary act of authority put the last extinguisher on the spirits of the company.", "이야기가 여기저기서 한두 번씩 잠깐 다시 솟구치기는 했으나, 거기엔 생기와 반짝임이 처량하리만치 없었다."], ["The talk spurted up again here and there, for a minute or two at a time; but there was a miserable lack of life and sparkle in it.", "그 저녁 잔치를 악마가 (아니면 그 다이아몬드가) 사로잡고 있었다."], ["The Devil (or the Diamond) possessed that dinner-party; and it was a relief to everybody when my mistress rose, and gave the ladies the signal to leave the gentlemen over their wine.", "그리고 내 여주인이 일어서서, 숙녀분들에게 신사분들을 포도주 곁에 남겨 두고 물러나자는 신호를 보냈을 때, 모두에게 그것은 하나의 안도였다."]], [["I had just ranged the decanters in a row before old Mr. Ablewhite (who represented the master of the house), when there came a sound from the terrace which startled me out of my company manners on the instant.", "내가 막 노에이블화이트 씨(집주인을 대신하고 있었다) 앞에 유리병들을 한 줄로 늘어놓았을 때, 테라스에서 소리가 하나 들려와, 그 즉시 나를 손님 앞의 예법에서 화들짝 벗어나게 했다."], ["Mr. Franklin and I looked at each other; it was the sound of the Indian drum.", "프랭클린 도련님과 나는 서로를 마주 보았다."], ["As I live by bread, here were the jugglers returning to us with the return of the Moonstone to the house!", "그것은 인도 북소리였다. 내가 빵을 먹고 사는 것이 확실하듯, 여기, 월장석이 이 집으로 돌아온 것과 때를 맞추어 요술쟁이들이 우리에게 돌아오고 있었던 것이다!"]], [["As they rounded the corner of the terrace, and came in sight, I hobbled out to warn them off.", "그들이 테라스 모퉁이를 돌아 시야에 들어오자, 나는 그들을 쫓아 보내려고 절뚝절뚝 걸어 나갔다."], ["But, as ill-luck would have it, the two Bouncers were beforehand with me.", "그러나 불운하게도, 두 통통이가 나보다 한발 앞섰다."], ["They whizzed out on to the terrace like a couple of skyrockets, wild to see the Indians exhibit their tricks.", "그들은 인도인들이 재주 부리는 것을 보고 싶어 안달이 나서, 한 쌍의 쏘아 올린 불꽃처럼 테라스로 쌩 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["The other ladies followed; the gentlemen came out on their side.", "다른 숙녀분들이 뒤를 따랐고, 신사분들도 그들 쪽에서 나왔다."], ["Before you could say, “Lord bless us!”", "\"우리를 굽어살피소서!\""], ["the rogues were making their salaams; and the Bouncers were kissing the pretty little boy.", "소리를 채 하기도 전에, 그 악당들은 저희 인사(살람)를 올리고 있었고, 통통이들은 그 예쁘장한 어린 소년에게 입을 맞추고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Franklin got on one side of Miss Rachel, and I put myself behind her.", "프랭클린 도련님은 레이철 아가씨의 한쪽 곁에 붙어 섰고, 나는 아가씨 뒤에 자리 잡았다."], ["If our suspicions were right, there she stood, innocent of all knowledge of the truth, showing the Indians the Diamond in the bosom of her dress!", "우리의 의심이 옳다면, 거기 아가씨가, 진상은 아무것도 모른 채, 제 드레스 가슴의 그 다이아몬드를 인도인들에게 내보이며 서 있는 셈이었다!"]], [["I can’t tell you what tricks they performed, or how they did it.", "그들이 무슨 재주를 부렸는지, 어떻게 했는지는 여러분께 말씀드릴 수 없다."], ["What with the vexation about the dinner, and what with the provocation of the rogues coming back just in the nick of time to see the jewel with their own eyes, I own I lost my head.", "저녁 식사에 대한 언짢음에다, 그 악당들이 하필 절묘한 순간에 돌아와 제 눈으로 그 보석을 보게 된 것에 대한 부아까지 겹쳐, 나는, 실토하건대, 정신을 놓아 버렸다."], ["The first thing that I remember noticing was the sudden appearance on the scene of the Indian traveller, Mr. Murthwaite.", "내가 알아차린 것으로 맨 처음 기억나는 것은, 그 자리에 인도 여행가 머스웨이트 씨가 별안간 나타난 것이었다."], ["Skirting the half-circle in which the gentlefolks stood or sat, he came quietly behind the jugglers and spoke to them on a sudden in the language of their own country.", "지체 높은 분들이 서고 앉아 있던 그 반원을 빙 돌아, 그는 요술쟁이들 뒤로 조용히 다가가더니, 별안간 저희 나라 말로 그들에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["If he had pricked them with a bayonet, I doubt if the Indians could have started and turned on him with a more tigerish quickness than they did, on hearing the first words that passed his lips.", "그가 그들을 총검으로 찔렀던들, 인도인들이, 그의 입에서 나온 첫마디를 들었을 때 그리한 것보다 더 호랑이 같은 날램으로 흠칫 놀라 그에게 홱 돌아설 수 있었을까 싶다."], ["The next moment they were bowing and salaaming to him in their most polite and snaky way.", "다음 순간 그들은, 더없이 정중하고 뱀 같은 방식으로 그에게 절하고 인사를 올리고 있었다."], ["After a few words in the unknown tongue had passed on either side, Mr. Murthwaite withdrew as quietly as he had approached.", "그 알 수 없는 말로 양쪽에서 몇 마디가 오간 뒤, 머스웨이트 씨는 다가올 때와 마찬가지로 조용히 물러났다."], ["The chief Indian, who acted as interpreter, thereupon wheeled about again towards the gentlefolks.", "통역 노릇을 하던 우두머리 인도인은, 그러자 다시 지체 높은 분들 쪽으로 홱 돌아섰다."], ["I noticed that the fellow’s coffee-coloured face had turned grey since Mr. Murthwaite had spoken to him.", "나는 그 사내의 커피빛 얼굴이, 머스웨이트 씨가 그에게 말을 건넨 뒤로 잿빛이 된 것을 알아차렸다."], ["He bowed to my lady, and informed her that the exhibition was over.", "그는 마님께 절하고, 공연이 끝났다고 아뢰었다."], ["The Bouncers, indescribably disappointed, burst out with a loud “O!” directed against Mr.", "통통이들은, 이루 말 못 하게 실망하여, 공연을 중단시킨 머스웨이트 씨를 겨냥해 큰 소리로 \"오!\"를 터뜨렸다."], ["Murthwaite for stopping the performance. The chief Indian laid his hand humbly on his breast, and said a second time that the juggling was over.", "우두머리 인도인은 제 가슴에 겸손히 손을 얹고, 요술이 끝났다고 두 번째로 말했다."], ["The little boy went round with the hat.", "어린 소년이 모자를 들고 돌아다녔다."], ["The ladies withdrew to the drawing-room; and the gentlemen (excepting Mr. Franklin and Mr.", "숙녀분들은 응접실로 물러났고, 신사분들은 (프랭클린 도련님과 머스웨이트 씨만 빼고) 저희 포도주로 돌아갔다."], ["Murthwaite) returned to their wine. I and the footman followed the Indians, and saw them safe off the premises.", "나와 하인은 인도인들을 따라가, 그들이 무사히 경내를 벗어나는 것을 지켜보았다."]], [["Going back by way of the shrubbery, I smelt tobacco, and found Mr. Franklin and Mr. Murthwaite (the latter smoking a cheroot) walking slowly up and down among the trees. Mr. Franklin beckoned to me to join them.", "관목숲을 지나 돌아오다가, 나는 담배 냄새를 맡고, 프랭클린 도련님과 머스웨이트 씨가 (후자는 여송연을 피우며) 나무들 사이를 천천히 오르내리며 거니는 것을 보았다. 프랭클린 도련님이 내게 저희와 합류하라고 손짓했다."]], [["“This,” says Mr.", "\"이 사람이,\""], ["Franklin, presenting me to the great traveller, “is Gabriel Betteredge, the old servant and friend of our family of whom I spoke to you just now.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 그 위대한 여행가에게 나를 소개하며 말한다. \"게이브리얼 베터리지, 방금 제가 말씀드린 우리 집안의 오랜 하인이자 벗입니다."], ["Tell him, if you please, what you have just told me.”", "부디, 방금 저한테 말씀하신 것을 이 사람한테도 말씀해 주시지요.\""]], [["Mr. Murthwaite took his cheroot out of his mouth, and leaned, in his weary way, against the trunk of a tree.", "머스웨이트 씨는 여송연을 입에서 떼고, 그 지친 투로, 나무 둥치에 기댔다."]], [["“Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지 씨.\""], ["he began, “those three Indians are no more jugglers than you and I are.”", "그가 입을 열었다. \"저 인도인 셋은 당신이나 나만큼이나 요술쟁이가 아니올시다.\""]], [["Here was a new surprise!", "이거야말로 새로운 놀라움이었다!"], ["I naturally asked the traveller if he had ever met with the Indians before.", "나는 자연히 그 여행가에게, 전에 그 인도인들을 만난 적이 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“Never,” says Mr.", "\"전혀 없소.\""], ["Murthwaite; “but I know what Indian juggling really is.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말한다. \"하지만 나는 인도의 요술이 실제로 어떤 것인지 아오."], ["All you have seen tonight is a very bad and clumsy imitation of it.", "오늘 밤 당신이 본 것은 죄다 그것을 아주 서투르고 어설프게 흉내 낸 것일 뿐이오."], ["Unless, after long experience, I am utterly mistaken, those men are high-caste Brahmins.", "오랜 경험 끝의 내가 아주 크게 잘못 본 것이 아니라면, 저 사내들은 높은 계급의 브라만이오."], ["I charged them with being disguised, and you saw how it told on them, clever as the Hindoo people are in concealing their feelings.", "내가 그들에게 변장하고 있다고 몰아붙였더니, 힌두 사람들이 제 감정을 감추는 데 그토록 능한데도, 그것이 그들에게 어떻게 작용했는지 당신도 보셨소."], ["There is a mystery about their conduct that I can’t explain.", "그들의 행실에는 내가 설명할 수 없는 수수께끼가 있소."], ["They have doubly sacrificed their caste—first, in crossing the sea; secondly, in disguising themselves as jugglers.", "그들은 두 겹으로 제 계급을 희생했으니—첫째, 바다를 건넌 것이요, 둘째, 요술쟁이로 변장한 것이오."], ["In the land they live in that is a tremendous sacrifice to make.", "그들이 사는 땅에서 그것은 어마어마한 희생이오."], ["There must be some very serious motive at the bottom of it, and some justification of no ordinary kind to plead for them, in recovery of their caste, when they return to their own country.”", "그 밑바닥에는 여간 심각한 동기가 있는 게 틀림없고, 그들이 제 나라로 돌아가 제 계급을 되찾을 때 그들을 변호할 예사 아닌 어떤 명분이 있는 게 틀림없소.\""]], [["I was struck dumb. Mr.", "나는 벙어리가 되었다."], ["Murthwaite went on with his cheroot. Mr.", "머스웨이트 씨는 제 여송연을 계속 피웠다."], ["Franklin, after what looked to me like a little private veering about between the different sides of his character, broke the silence as follows:", "프랭클린 도련님은, 내 보기엔 제 됨됨이의 서로 다른 면들 사이에서 잠시 남몰래 방향을 트는 듯싶더니, 이렇게 침묵을 깼다."]], [["“I feel some hesitation, Mr. Murthwaite, in troubling you with family matters, in which you can have no interest and which I am not very willing to speak of out of our own circle.", "\"저는 좀 망설여집니다, 머스웨이트 씨, 당신께 아무 흥미도 없으실, 그리고 제가 우리 울타리 밖에서는 그리 이야기하고 싶지 않은 집안일로 당신을 성가시게 해 드리는 것이요."], ["But, after what you have said, I feel bound, in the interests of Lady Verinder and her daughter, to tell you something which may possibly put the clue into your hands.", "하지만 당신 말씀을 듣고 나니, 저는, 레이디 버린더와 그 따님을 위해서, 어쩌면 당신 손에 실마리를 쥐여 드릴지도 모를 무언가를 당신께 말씀드려야 한다는 의무를 느낍니다."], ["I speak to you in confidence; you will oblige me, I am sure, by not forgetting that?”", "저는 은밀히 말씀드리는 것이니, 부디 그 점을 잊지 말아 주시는 은혜를 베풀어 주시리라 믿습니다.\""]], [["With this preface, he told the Indian traveller all that he had told me at the Shivering Sand.", "이 서두와 함께, 그는 떠는 모래펄에서 내게 들려주었던 모든 것을 그 인도 여행가에게 이야기했다."], ["Even the immovable Mr. Murthwaite was so interested in what he heard, that he let his cheroot go out.", "그 요지부동한 머스웨이트 씨조차 듣는 이야기에 어찌나 흥미를 느꼈던지, 제 여송연이 꺼지도록 내버려 두었다."]], [["“Now,” says Mr.", "\"자.\""], ["Franklin, when he had done, “what does your experience say?”", "하고 이야기를 마친 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"당신의 경험은 뭐라고 합니까?\""]], [["“My experience,”", "\"내 경험은,\""], ["answered the traveller, “says that you have had more narrow escapes of your life, Mr.", "하고 그 여행가가 대답했다. \"당신이, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨, 내가 내 목숨을 아슬아슬하게 건진 것보다 더 여러 번 당신 목숨을 아슬아슬하게 건졌다고 말하는구려."], ["Franklin Blake, than I have had of mine; and that is saying a great deal.”", "그리고 그건 여간한 말이 아니오.\""]], [["It was Mr. Franklin’s turn to be astonished now.", "이제 놀랄 차례는 프랭클린 도련님이었다."]], [["“Is it really as serious as that?”", "\"정말로 그렇게까지 심각합니까?\""], ["he asked.", "그가 물었다."]], [["“In my opinion it is,”", "\"내 생각엔 그렇소.\""], ["answered Mr. Murthwaite.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 대답했다."], ["“I can’t doubt, after what you have told me, that the restoration of the Moonstone to its place on the forehead of the Indian idol, is the motive and the justification of that sacrifice of caste which I alluded to just now.", "\"당신이 내게 말한 것을 듣고 나니, 나는 그 월장석을 인도 우상의 이마에 있던 제자리로 되돌려 놓는 것이, 방금 내가 언급한 그 계급 희생의 동기이자 명분임을 의심할 수가 없소."], ["Those men will wait their opportunity with the patience of cats, and will use it with the ferocity of tigers.", "저 사내들은 고양이의 끈기로 제 기회를 기다릴 것이며, 호랑이의 사나움으로 그것을 쓸 것이오."], ["How you have escaped them I can’t imagine,”", "당신이 어떻게 그들에게서 벗어났는지 나는 짐작이 안 가는구려.\""], ["says the eminent traveller, lighting his cheroot again, and staring hard at Mr. Franklin.", "하고 그 저명한 여행가가, 제 여송연에 다시 불을 붙이고, 프랭클린 도련님을 뚫어져라 쳐다보며 말한다."], ["“You have been carrying the Diamond backwards and forwards, here and in London, and you are still a living man!", "\"당신은 그 다이아몬드를 여기와 런던에서 이리저리 가지고 다녔는데도, 아직 살아 있는 사람이오!"], ["Let us try and account for it.", "어디 한번 그 까닭을 대 봅시다."], ["It was daylight, both times, I suppose, when you took the jewel out of the bank in London?”", "런던에서 그 보석을 은행에서 찾아 나올 때, 두 번 다, 낮이었겠지요?\""]], [["“Broad daylight,”", "\"훤한 대낮이었지요.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."]], [["“And plenty of people in the streets?”", "\"그리고 거리엔 사람이 많았고요?\""]], [["“Plenty.”", "\"많았지요.\""]], [["“You settled, of course, to arrive at Lady Verinder’s house at a certain time?", "\"당신은, 물론, 레이디 버린더의 집에 어느 시각에 도착하기로 정해 두었겠지요?"], ["It’s a lonely country between this and the station.", "여기와 역 사이는 인적 드문 벌판이잖소."], ["Did you keep your appointment?”", "그 약속을 지켰소?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["I arrived four hours earlier than my appointment.”", "저는 약속보다 네 시간 일찍 도착했지요.\""]], [["“I beg to congratulate you on that proceeding!", "\"그 처사에 축하를 드리는 바요!"], ["When did you take the Diamond to the bank at the town here?”", "여기 이 읍내 은행에는 그 다이아몬드를 언제 가져갔소?\""]], [["“I took it an hour after I had brought it to this house—and three hours before anybody was prepared for seeing me in these parts.”", "\"이 집에 그것을 가져온 지 한 시간 뒤에 가져갔지요—그리고 이 근방에서 누가 저를 볼 채비를 하기 세 시간 전에요.\""]], [["“I beg to congratulate you again!", "\"다시 한번 축하를 드리는 바요!"], ["Did you bring it back here alone?”", "그것을 여기로 도로 가져올 때는 혼자였소?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["I happened to ride back with my cousins and the groom.”", "마침 제 사촌들과 마부와 함께 말을 타고 왔지요.\""]], [["“I beg to congratulate you for the third time!", "\"세 번째로 축하를 드리는 바요!"], ["If you ever feel inclined to travel beyond the civilised limits, Mr.", "혹 문명의 경계 너머로 여행하고 싶은 마음이 들거든, 블레이크 씨, 내게 알려 주시오."], ["Blake, let me know, and I will go with you.", "내가 함께 가리다."], ["You are a lucky man.”", "당신은 운 좋은 사람이오.\""]], [["Here I struck in.", "여기서 나는 끼어들었다."], ["This sort of thing didn’t at all square with my English ideas.", "이런 식의 이야기는 내 영국식 생각과는 도무지 맞아떨어지지 않았다."]], [["“You don’t really mean to say, sir,”", "\"정말로, 선생,\" 하고 나는 물었다."], ["I asked, “that they would have taken Mr. Franklin’s life, to get their Diamond, if he had given them the chance?”", "\"그자들이 저희 다이아몬드를 손에 넣으려고, 프랭클린 도련님이 틈을 주었더라면, 도련님 목숨을 빼앗았으리라는 말씀입니까?\""]], [["“Do you smoke, Mr. Betteredge?”", "\"당신은 담배를 피우시오, 베터리지 씨?\""], ["says the traveller.", "하고 그 여행가가 말한다."]], [["“Yes, sir.", "\"예, 그렇습니다요.\""]], [["“Do you care much for the ashes left in your pipe when you empty it?”", "\"담뱃대를 비울 때 그 속에 남은 재를 당신은 대수롭게 여기시오?\""]], [["“No, sir.”", "\"아닙니다요, 선생.\""]], [["“In the country those men came from, they care just as much about killing a man, as you care about emptying the ashes out of your pipe.", "\"저 사내들이 온 나라에서는, 사람을 죽이는 일을, 당신이 담뱃대에서 재를 털어 내는 일만큼이나 대수롭게 여긴다오."], ["If a thousand lives stood between them and the getting back of their Diamond—and if they thought they could destroy those lives without discovery—they would take them all.", "만일 천 명의 목숨이 그들과 저희 다이아몬드를 되찾는 일 사이를 가로막고 있다면—그리고 만일 그들이 발각되지 않고 그 목숨들을 없앨 수 있다고 여긴다면—그들은 그것을 죄다 빼앗을 것이오."], ["The sacrifice of caste is a serious thing in India, if you like.", "계급의 희생은, 정 그러시다면, 인도에서 심각한 일이오."], ["The sacrifice of life is nothing at all.”", "목숨의 희생은 아무것도 아니오.\""]], [["I expressed my opinion upon this, that they were a set of murdering thieves. Mr.", "나는 이에 대해 내 의견을 밝혔으니, 그들이 사람 죽이는 도둑 떼라는 것이었다."], ["Murthwaite expressed his opinion that they were a wonderful people.", "머스웨이트 씨는 그들이 놀라운 민족이라는 자기 의견을 밝혔다."], ["Mr. Franklin, expressing no opinion at all, brought us back to the matter in hand.", "아무 의견도 밝히지 않은 프랭클린 도련님이, 우리를 당면한 문제로 도로 데려왔다."]], [["“They have seen the Moonstone on Miss Verinder’s dress,”", "\"그들은 버린더 아가씨의 드레스에서 그 월장석을 봤습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“What is to be done?”", "\"어찌해야 합니까?\""]], [["“What your uncle threatened to do,”", "\"당신 외숙부가 하겠다고 으름장 놓은 대로 하시오.\""], ["answered Mr. Murthwaite.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 대답했다."], ["“Colonel Herncastle understood the people he had to deal with.", "\"헌캐슬 대령은 자기가 상대해야 할 사람들을 잘 알고 있었소."], ["Send the Diamond tomorrow (under guard of more than one man) to be cut up at Amsterdam.", "그 다이아몬드를 내일 (한 사람 이상의 호위를 붙여) 암스테르담으로 보내 쪼개게 하시오."], ["Make half a dozen diamonds of it, instead of one.", "그것을 하나 대신 여섯 개의 다이아몬드로 만드시오."], ["There is an end of its sacred identity as The Moonstone—and there is an end of the conspiracy.”", "그러면 월장석으로서의 그 성스러운 정체도 끝이 나고—그 음모도 끝이 나는 것이오.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin turned to me.", "프랭클린 도련님이 나를 돌아보았다."]], [["“There is no help for it,”", "\"달리 도리가 없군.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“We must speak to Lady Verinder tomorrow.”", "\"우리는 내일 레이디 버린더께 말씀드려야 하네.\""]], [["“What about tonight, sir?”", "\"오늘 밤은요, 도련님?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."], ["“Suppose the Indians come back?”", "\"만일 인도인들이 다시 온다면요?\""]], [["Mr. Murthwaite answered me before Mr. Franklin could speak.", "머스웨이트 씨가, 프랭클린 도련님이 미처 말하기 전에, 내게 대답했다."]], [["“The Indians won’t risk coming back tonight,” he said.", "\"인도인들은 오늘 밤 다시 오는 모험은 하지 않을 것이오.\""], ["“The direct way is hardly ever the way they take to anything—let alone a matter like this, in which the slightest mistake might be fatal to their reaching their end.”", "그가 말했다. \"곧장 가는 길은 그들이 무슨 일에든 좀처럼 택하지 않는 길이오—더구나 이런 일에서야, 아주 사소한 실수 하나가 저희 목적에 이르는 데 치명적일 수 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["“But suppose the rogues are bolder than you think, sir?”", "\"하지만 그 악당들이 선생 생각보다 더 대담하다면요?\""], ["I persisted.", "나는 물고 늘어졌다."]], [["“In that case,” says Mr.", "\"그럴 경우엔,\""], ["Murthwaite, “let the dogs loose.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말한다."], ["Have you got any big dogs in the yard?”", "\"개들을 풀어놓으시오. 마당에 큰 개가 있소?\""]], [["“Two, sir.", "\"둘 있습니다요, 선생."], ["A mastiff and a bloodhound.”", "마스티프 한 마리와 블러드하운드 한 마리요.\""]], [["“They will do.", "\"그거면 되겠소."], ["In the present emergency, Mr. Betteredge, the mastiff and the bloodhound have one great merit—they are not likely to be troubled with your scruples about the sanctity of human life.”", "지금 이 위급한 때에, 베터리지 씨, 그 마스티프와 블러드하운드에게는 큰 장점이 하나 있소—그놈들은 사람 목숨의 신성함에 대한 당신의 그 거리낌 따위로 골머리를 앓을 것 같지가 않다는 점 말이오.\""]], [["The strumming of the piano reached us from the drawing-room, as he fired that shot at me.", "그가 그 화살을 내게 쏘아 붙일 때, 응접실에서 피아노 퉁기는 소리가 우리에게 들려왔다."], ["He threw away his cheroot, and took Mr. Franklin’s arm, to go back to the ladies.", "그는 제 여송연을 내던지고, 숙녀분들에게로 돌아가려고 프랭클린 도련님의 팔을 잡았다."], ["I noticed that the sky was clouding over fast, as I followed them to the house.", "그들을 따라 집으로 가는데, 나는 하늘이 빠르게 흐려지고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["Mr. Murthwaite noticed it too.", "머스웨이트 씨도 그것을 알아차렸다."], ["He looked round at me, in his dry, droning way, and said:", "그는 그 무미건조하고 웅얼거리는 투로 나를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“The Indians will want their umbrellas, Mr. Betteredge, tonight!”", "\"인도인들은 오늘 밤 우산이 필요하겠구려, 베터리지 씨!\""]], [["It was all very well for him to joke.", "그야 그로서는 농담해도 좋았다."], ["But I was not an eminent traveller—and my way in this world had not led me into playing ducks and drakes with my own life, among thieves and murderers in the outlandish places of the earth.", "그러나 나는 저명한 여행가가 아니었고—이 세상에서 내가 걸어온 길은, 땅끝의 낯선 곳들에서 도둑과 살인자들 틈에 끼어 내 목숨을 물수제비 뜨듯 하는 데로 나를 이끌지 않았다."], ["I went into my own little room, and sat down in my chair in a perspiration, and wondered helplessly what was to be done next.", "나는 내 조그만 방으로 들어가, 땀에 젖은 채 의자에 주저앉아, 다음엔 무엇을 해야 하나 하고 속수무책으로 골똘했다."], ["In this anxious frame of mind, other men might have ended by working themselves up into a fever; I ended in a different way.", "이런 근심스러운 심경이면, 다른 사내들은 결국 저 스스로를 부추겨 열병이 나고 말았을지도 모른다. 나는 다른 식으로 끝을 맺었다."], ["I lit my pipe, and took a turn at Robinson Crusoe.", "나는 담뱃대에 불을 붙이고, 「로빈슨 크루소」를 한 대목 들췄다."]], [["Before I had been at it five minutes, I came to this amazing bit—page one hundred and sixty-one—as follows:", "그것에 매달린 지 오 분도 채 되기 전에, 나는 이 놀라운 대목—백예순한 쪽—에 이르렀으니, 이러하였다."]], [["“Fear of Danger is ten thousand times more terrifying than Danger itself, when apparent to the Eyes; and we find the Burthen of Anxiety greater, by much, than the Evil which we are anxious about.”", "\"위험에 대한 두려움은, 눈에 뻔히 보이는 위험 그 자체보다 만 곱절이나 더 무섭다. 그리고 우리는 근심의 짐이, 우리가 근심하는 그 화(禍)보다 훨씬 더 무거움을 알게 된다.\""]], [["The man who doesn’t believe in Robinson Crusoe, after that, is a man with a screw loose in his understanding, or a man lost in the mist of his own self-conceit!", "이러고도 「로빈슨 크루소」를 믿지 않는 사람은, 그 머릿속 나사 하나가 풀린 사람이거나, 제 자만의 안개 속에서 길을 잃은 사람이다!"], ["Argument is thrown away upon him; and pity is better reserved for some person with a livelier faith.", "그런 사람에게 논변은 부질없는 것이요, 딱한 마음일랑 좀 더 발랄한 믿음을 지닌 다른 사람을 위해 아껴 두는 편이 낫다."]], [["I was far on with my second pipe, and still lost in admiration of that wonderful book, when Penelope (who had been handing round the tea) came in with her report from the drawing-room.", "내가 두 번째 담뱃대에 한창 빠져, 여전히 그 놀라운 책에 감탄하여 넋을 잃고 있는데, (차를 돌리던) 페넬로피가 응접실에서의 보고를 들고 들어왔다."], ["She had left the Bouncers singing a duet—words beginning with a large “O,” and music to correspond.", "그 애는 통통이들이 이중창을 부르는 것을 두고 나왔는데—커다란 \"오!\"로 시작하는 노랫말에, 거기에 맞춘 곡이었다."], ["She had observed that my lady made mistakes in her game of whist for the first time in our experience of her.", "그 애가 눈여겨본 바로, 마님이 우리가 여태껏 모신 경험으로는 처음으로, 휘스트 놀이에서 실수를 하셨다."], ["She had seen the great traveller asleep in a corner. She had overheard Mr.", "그 애는 그 위대한 여행가가 한구석에서 잠든 것을 보았다."], ["Franklin sharpening his wits on Mr. Godfrey, at the expense of Ladies’ Charities in general; and she had noticed that Mr.", "그 애는 프랭클린 도련님이, 대체로 부인 자선회를 도마에 올려 고드프리 씨에게 제 재치를 벼리는 것을 어깨너머로 들었다."], ["Godfrey hit him back again rather more smartly than became a gentleman of his benevolent character. She had detected Miss Rachel, apparently engaged in appeasing Mrs.", "그리고 고드프리 씨가, 제 그 자비로운 성품에 어울리는 것보다는 자못 날렵하게 그에게 되받아치는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["Threadgall by showing her some photographs, and really occupied in stealing looks at Mr. Franklin, which no intelligent lady’s maid could misinterpret for a single instant.", "그 애는 레이철 아가씨가, 겉으로는 스레드골 부인께 사진 몇 장을 보여 주며 부인을 달래는 척하면서, 실은 프랭클린 도련님에게 몰래 눈길을 훔치는 일에 골몰해 있는 것을 알아챘으니, 그것은 총명한 하녀라면 단 한순간도 잘못 해석할 수 없는 눈길이었다."], ["Finally, she had missed Mr. Candy, the doctor, who had mysteriously disappeared from the drawing-room, and had then mysteriously returned, and entered into conversation with Mr.", "끝으로, 그 애는 의사 캔디 씨가 안 보이는 것을 알아챘는데, 그는 응접실에서 알 수 없이 사라졌다가, 그러고는 알 수 없이 돌아와, 고드프리 씨와 이야기에 들어간 것이었다."], ["Godfrey. Upon the whole, things were prospering better than the experience of the dinner gave us any right to expect.", "대체로, 일은 저녁 식사의 경험이 우리에게 기대할 자격을 준 것보다는 더 잘 흘러가고 있었다."], ["If we could only hold on for another hour, old Father Time would bring up their carriages, and relieve us of them altogether.", "우리가 한 시간만 더 버틸 수 있다면, 늙은 시간 노인이 그들의 마차를 몰고 와, 그들을 죄다 우리에게서 덜어 줄 터였다."]], [["Everything wears off in this world; and even the comforting effect of Robinson Crusoe wore off, after Penelope left me.", "이 세상 무엇이나 닳아 없어지는 법이니, 「로빈슨 크루소」의 그 위안이 되는 효험조차, 페넬로피가 나를 두고 나간 뒤로는 닳아 없어졌다."], ["I got fidgety again, and resolved on making a survey of the grounds before the rain came.", "나는 다시 안절부절못하게 되어, 비가 오기 전에 영지를 한 바퀴 살펴보기로 마음먹었다."], ["Instead of taking the footman, whose nose was human, and therefore useless in any emergency, I took the bloodhound with me.", "코가 사람의 것이라 어떤 위급한 때에도 쓸모없는 그 하인을 데려가는 대신, 나는 블러드하운드를 데리고 갔다."], ["His nose for a stranger was to be depended on.", "낯선 자를 알아채는 그놈의 코라면 믿을 만했다."], ["We went all round the premises, and out into the road—and returned as wise as we went, having discovered no such thing as a lurking human creature anywhere.", "우리는 경내를 죄다 돌고, 한길로 나갔다가—숨어 있는 사람 따위는 어디서도 찾아내지 못한 채, 나갈 때만큼이나 아는 것 없이 돌아왔다."]], [["The arrival of the carriages was the signal for the arrival of the rain.", "마차들의 도착은 곧 비의 도착을 알리는 신호였다."], ["It poured as if it meant to pour all night.", "밤새 퍼부을 작정인 듯 비가 쏟아졌다."], ["With the exception of the doctor, whose gig was waiting for him, the rest of the company went home snugly, under cover, in close carriages.", "이륜마차가 기다리고 있던 그 의사만 빼고, 나머지 손님들은 지붕 있는 밀폐 마차를 타고 아늑하게 집으로 갔다."], ["I told Mr. Candy that I was afraid he would get wet through.", "나는 캔디 씨에게 흠뻑 젖으실까 걱정된다고 말했다."], ["He told me, in return, that he wondered I had arrived at my time of life, without knowing that a doctor’s skin was waterproof.", "그는 그 답례로, 의사의 살갗이 방수라는 것도 모르고 내가 이 나잇살을 먹은 것이 놀랍다고 내게 말했다."], ["So he drove away in the rain, laughing over his own little joke; and so we got rid of our dinner company.", "그러고는 제 조그만 농담에 껄껄 웃으며 빗속으로 마차를 몰아갔다. 그리하여 우리는 저녁 손님들을 다 치웠다."]], [["The next thing to tell is the story of the night.", "다음으로 할 이야기는 그 밤의 이야기다."]], [["# CHAPTER XI", "# CHAPTER XI"]], [["When the last of the guests had driven away, I went back into the inner hall and found Samuel at the side-table, presiding over the brandy and soda water.", "마지막 손님이 마차를 몰고 떠난 뒤, 나는 안쪽 현관으로 돌아가, 새뮤얼이 곁 탁자에서 브랜디와 소다수를 맡아 지키고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["My lady and Miss Rachel came out of the drawing-room, followed by the two gentlemen. Mr.", "마님과 레이철 아가씨가 응접실에서 나오셨고, 두 신사가 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["Godfrey had some brandy and soda water, Mr. Franklin took nothing.", "고드프리 씨는 브랜디에 소다수를 타 마셨고, 프랭클린 도련님은 아무것도 들지 않았다."], ["He sat down, looking dead tired; the talking on this birthday occasion had, I suppose, been too much for him.", "그는 죽도록 지친 얼굴로 앉았다. 짐작건대 이 생일 자리에서의 이야기가 그에게 너무 벅찼던 모양이다."]], [["My lady, turning round to wish them good-night, looked hard at the wicked Colonel’s legacy shining in her daughter’s dress.", "마님이 그분들께 잘 자라는 인사를 하려고 돌아서시다가, 당신 따님의 드레스에서 빛나고 있는 그 사악한 대령의 유산을 뚫어져라 바라보셨다."]], [["“Rachel,”", "\"레이철.\""], ["she asked, “where are you going to put your Diamond tonight?”", "마님이 물으셨다. \"네 다이아몬드를 오늘 밤 어디에 둘 참이냐?\""]], [["Miss Rachel was in high good spirits, just in that humour for talking nonsense, and perversely persisting in it as if it was sense, which you may sometimes have observed in young girls, when they are highly wrought up, at the end of an exciting day.", "레이철 아가씨는 잔뜩 들뜬 기분이었고, 마침 허튼소리를 늘어놓으며 그것을 사리에 맞는 말인 양 짐짓 우기고 드는 그런 심사였으니, 여러분도 젊은 처녀들이 신나는 하루의 끝에 잔뜩 흥분했을 때 이따금 그런 모습을 보신 적이 있으리라."], ["First, she declared she didn’t know where to put the Diamond.", "처음에 아가씨는 그 다이아몬드를 어디 둬야 할지 모르겠다고 잘라 말했다."], ["Then she said, “on her dressing-table, of course, along with her other things.”", "그러더니 \"그야 물론 다른 것들과 함께 제 화장대에\" 두겠다고 했다."], ["Then she remembered that the Diamond might take to shining of itself, with its awful moony light in the dark—and that would terrify her in the dead of night.", "그러더니 그 다이아몬드가 어둠 속에서 그 무시무시한 달빛으로 저 혼자 빛을 내기 시작할지도 모른다는 것을—그리고 그것이 한밤중에 저를 질겁하게 하리라는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["Then she bethought herself of an Indian cabinet which stood in her sitting-room; and instantly made up her mind to put the Indian diamond in the Indian cabinet, for the purpose of permitting two beautiful native productions to admire each other.", "그러더니 제 거실에 놓인 인도식 캐비닛을 생각해 냈다. 그러고는 대뜸, 두 아름다운 토산품이 서로 감탄하도록 해 주려는 뜻에서, 그 인도 다이아몬드를 그 인도식 캐비닛에 넣기로 마음먹었다."], ["Having let her little flow of nonsense run on as far as that point, her mother interposed and stopped her.", "아가씨의 그 자그마한 허튼소리의 물줄기가 거기까지 흘러가도록 놔두시던 그 어머니가, 끼어들어 아가씨를 멈추셨다."]], [["“My dear!", "\"얘야!"], ["your Indian cabinet has no lock to it,”", "네 인도식 캐비닛은 자물쇠가 없잖니.\""], ["says my lady.", "하고 마님이 말씀하신다."]], [["“Good Heavens, mamma!”", "\"어머, 엄마!\""], ["cried Miss Rachel, “is this an hotel?", "하고 레이철 아가씨가 외쳤다. \"여기가 무슨 여관이에요?"], ["Are there thieves in the house?”", "이 집에 도둑이라도 있어요?\""]], [["Without taking notice of this fantastic way of talking, my lady wished the gentlemen good-night.", "이 엉뚱한 말투에는 아랑곳없이, 마님은 신사분들께 잘 자라는 인사를 하셨다."], ["She next turned to Miss Rachel, and kissed her.", "그러고는 레이철 아가씨께 돌아서서, 아가씨에게 입을 맞추셨다."], ["“Why not let me keep the Diamond for you tonight?”", "\"오늘 밤 그 다이아몬드를 내가 대신 맡아 주면 안 되겠니?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 물으셨다."]], [["Miss Rachel received that proposal as she might, ten years since, have received a proposal to part her from a new doll.", "레이철 아가씨는 그 제안을, 십 년 전이었다면 제게서 새 인형을 떼어 놓겠다는 제안을 받았을 법한 그 심정으로 받아들였다."], ["My lady saw there was no reasoning with her that night.", "마님은 그날 밤 아가씨와는 이치를 따질 수 없음을 아셨다."], ["“Come into my room, Rachel, the first thing tomorrow morning,”", "\"내일 아침 맨 먼저 내 방으로 오너라, 레이철.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다."], ["she said. “I shall have something to say to you.”", "\"너한테 할 말이 있을 게다.\""], ["With those last words she left us slowly; thinking her own thoughts, and, to all appearance, not best pleased with the way by which they were leading her.", "그 마지막 말을 남기고 마님은 천천히 우리를 떠나셨다. 당신 나름의 생각에 잠긴 채, 그리고 겉보기에, 그 생각이 당신을 이끌어 가는 그 방향이 영 마뜩잖으신 채로."]], [["Miss Rachel was the next to say good-night. She shook hands first with Mr.", "다음으로 잘 자라는 인사를 한 것은 레이철 아가씨였다."], ["Godfrey, who was standing at the other end of the hall, looking at a picture.", "아가씨는 먼저, 현관 저쪽 끝에서 그림 한 점을 보고 서 있던 고드프리 씨와 악수했다."], ["Then she turned back to Mr. Franklin, still sitting weary and silent in a corner.", "그러고는 여전히 한구석에 지치고 말없이 앉아 있는 프랭클린 도련님께로 돌아섰다."]], [["What words passed between them I can’t say.", "그 두 사람 사이에 무슨 말이 오갔는지는 나는 알 수 없다."], ["But standing near the old oak frame which holds our large looking-glass, I saw her reflected in it, slyly slipping the locket which Mr.", "그러나 우리 큰 거울을 끼운 그 낡은 참나무 틀 가까이 서 있던 나는, 거울에 비친 아가씨가, 프랭클린 도련님이 아가씨께 준 그 로켓을 제 드레스 가슴에서 슬며시 빼내어, 잠시 그에게 내보이는 것을 보았다."], ["Franklin had given to her, out of the bosom of her dress, and showing it to him for a moment, with a smile which certainly meant something out of the common, before she tripped off to bed.", "잠자리로 총총 떠나기 전에, 분명 예사롭지 않은 무언가를 뜻하는 미소와 함께."], ["This incident staggered me a little in the reliance I had previously felt on my own judgment.", "이 일은, 내가 여태 내 판단에 품고 있던 그 믿음을 조금 휘청하게 했다."], ["I began to think that Penelope might be right about the state of her young lady’s affections, after all.", "나는, 어쩌면 제 젊은 여주인의 마음 상태에 관해 페넬로피가 결국 옳았을지도 모른다는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["As soon as Miss Rachel left him eyes to see with, Mr. Franklin noticed me.", "레이철 아가씨가 그의 볼 눈을 남겨 두고 떠나자마자, 프랭클린 도련님이 나를 알아보았다."], ["His variable humour, shifting about everything, had shifted about the Indians already.", "무엇에나 이리저리 옮아 다니는 그의 변덕스러운 기분이, 이미 그 인도인들 쪽으로도 옮아가 있었다."]], [["“Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지.\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “I’m half inclined to think I took Mr. Murthwaite too seriously, when we had that talk in the shrubbery.", "\"우리가 관목숲에서 그 이야기를 나눌 때, 내가 머스웨이트 씨를 너무 진지하게 받아들인 게 아닌가 하는 생각이 반쯤 드는군."], ["I wonder whether he has been trying any of his traveller’s tales on us?", "그가 우리한테 제 여행담 나부랭이를 시험해 본 게 아닌가 싶어."], ["Do you really mean to let the dogs loose?”", "자네 정말로 개들을 풀어놓을 참인가?\""]], [["“I’ll relieve them of their collars, sir,”", "\"그놈들 목줄을 벗겨 주겠습니다요, 도련님.\""], ["I answered, “and leave them free to take a turn in the night, if they smell a reason for it.”", "나는 대답했다. \"그러고는 그놈들이 무슨 낌새를 맡거든 밤중에 한 바퀴 돌 수 있게 풀어 두겠습니다.\""]], [["“All right,”", "\"좋아.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“We’ll see what is to be done tomorrow.", "\"무엇을 할지는 내일 보기로 하지."], ["I am not at all disposed to alarm my aunt, Betteredge, without a very pressing reason for it.", "나는, 베터리지, 아주 절박한 까닭이 없이는 이모님을 놀라게 해 드릴 마음이 조금도 없네."], ["Good-night.”", "잘 자게.\""]], [["He looked so worn and pale as he nodded to me, and took his candle to go upstairs, that I ventured to advise his having a drop of brandy-and-water, by way of night-cap. Mr.", "그가 내게 고개를 끄덕이고 위층으로 가려고 제 촛불을 집어 들 때 어찌나 초췌하고 창백해 보이던지, 나는 자기 전에 잠자리 술로 브랜디에 물을 조금 타 드시라고 주제넘게 권해 보았다."], ["Godfrey, walking towards us from the other end of the hall, backed me.", "현관 저쪽 끝에서 우리 쪽으로 걸어오던 고드프리 씨가 내 편을 들었다."], ["He pressed Mr. Franklin, in the friendliest manner, to take something, before he went to bed.", "그는 더없이 다정한 태도로, 자기 전에 뭐라도 좀 들라고 프랭클린 도련님을 채근했다."]], [["I only note these trifling circumstances, because, after all I had seen and heard, that day, it pleased me to observe that our two gentlemen were on just as good terms as ever.", "내가 이 자질구레한 정황들을 굳이 적어 두는 것은, 그날 내가 보고 들은 모든 것에도 불구하고, 우리 두 신사가 여느 때와 다름없이 좋은 사이임을 보게 되어 흐뭇했기 때문이다."], ["Their warfare of words (heard by Penelope in the drawing-room), and their rivalry for the best place in Miss Rachel’s good graces, seemed to have set no serious difference between them.", "(페넬로피가 응접실에서 들은) 그들의 말싸움과, 레이철 아가씨의 총애 속에서 가장 좋은 자리를 차지하려는 그들의 경쟁도, 그들 사이에 이렇다 할 골을 남기지는 않은 듯했다. 하지만 어쩌겠는가!"], ["But there! they were both good-tempered, and both men of the world.", "그들은 둘 다 성미가 좋았고, 둘 다 세상 물정에 밝은 사람들이었다."], ["And there is certainly this merit in people of station, that they are not nearly so quarrelsome among each other as people of no station at all.", "그리고 지체 있는 사람들에게는 분명 이 장점이 있으니, 그들은 지체라곤 전혀 없는 사람들만큼 저희끼리 그리 잘 다투지 않는다는 것이다."]], [["Mr. Franklin declined the brandy-and-water, and went upstairs with Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님은 브랜디 물을 마다하고, 고드프리 씨와 함께 위층으로 올라갔으니, 두 사람 방이 서로 나란히 붙어 있었다."], ["Godfrey, their rooms being next door to each other. On the landing, however, either his cousin persuaded him, or he veered about and changed his mind as usual.", "그러나 층계참에서, 사촌이 그를 설득했든지, 아니면 그가 여느 때처럼 방향을 틀어 마음을 바꿨든지 했다."], ["“Perhaps I may want it in the night,”", "\"어쩌면 밤중에 그게 당길지도 모르지.\""], ["he called down to me.", "그가 내게 아래로 대고 외쳤다."], ["“Send up some brandy-and-water into my room.”", "\"브랜디에 물 좀 타서 내 방으로 올려 보내 주게.\""]], [["I sent up Samuel with the brandy-and-water; and then went out and unbuckled the dogs’ collars.", "나는 새뮤얼 편에 브랜디 물을 올려 보냈다. 그러고는 밖으로 나가 개들의 목줄을 끌렀다."], ["They both lost their heads with astonishment on being set loose at that time of night, and jumped upon me like a couple of puppies!", "그 두 놈은 그런 밤 시각에 풀려나자 놀라움에 정신을 잃고, 한 쌍의 강아지처럼 내게 껑충 뛰어올랐다!"], ["However, the rain soon cooled them down again: they lapped a drop of water each, and crept back into their kennels.", "그렇지만 비가 곧 그놈들을 다시 가라앉혔다. 그놈들은 저마다 물 한 모금씩을 핥아 마시고는, 슬금슬금 제 개집으로 기어들어 갔다."], ["As I went into the house, I noticed signs in the sky which betokened a break in the weather for the better.", "집으로 들어가면서, 나는 날씨가 개는 쪽으로 바뀔 조짐을 하늘에서 알아차렸다."], ["For the present, it still poured heavily, and the ground was in a perfect sop.", "당장은, 여전히 비가 세차게 퍼붓고, 땅은 온통 질척였다."]], [["Samuel and I went all over the house, and shut up as usual.", "새뮤얼과 나는 집을 죄다 돌며 여느 때처럼 문단속을 했다."], ["I examined everything myself, and trusted nothing to my deputy on this occasion.", "나는 모든 것을 손수 살폈고, 이번만큼은 내 부관에게 아무것도 맡기지 않았다."], ["All was safe and fast when I rested my old bones in bed, between midnight and one in the morning.", "내가 자정과 새벽 한 시 사이에 늙은 뼈를 침상에 뉘었을 때, 모든 것이 안전하고 단단했다."]], [["The worries of the day had been a little too much for me, I suppose.", "짐작건대, 그날의 온갖 근심이 내게 조금 벅찼던 모양이다."], ["At any rate, I had a touch of Mr. Franklin’s malady that night.", "아무튼, 나는 그날 밤 프랭클린 도련님의 그 병을 조금 앓았다."], ["It was sunrise before I fell off at last into a sleep.", "마침내 잠에 빠져든 것은 해가 뜰 무렵이었다."], ["All the time I lay awake the house was as quiet as the grave.", "내가 깨어 누워 있는 동안 내내 집은 무덤처럼 고요했다."], ["Not a sound stirred but the splash of the rain, and the sighing of the wind among the trees as a breeze sprang up with the morning.", "빗물이 철벅이는 소리와, 아침과 함께 산들바람이 일면서 나무들 사이로 바람이 한숨짓는 소리 말고는, 아무 소리도 움직이지 않았다."]], [["About half-past seven I woke, and opened my window on a fine sunshiny day.", "일곱 시 반쯤에 나는 깨어, 화창한 햇살이 비치는 날에 창을 열었다."], ["The clock had struck eight, and I was just going out to chain up the dogs again, when I heard a sudden whisking of petticoats on the stairs behind me.", "시계가 여덟 시를 쳤고, 나는 막 다시 개들을 사슬에 매러 나가려던 참인데, 내 뒤 계단에서 별안간 페티코트가 휙휙 스치는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["I turned about, and there was Penelope flying down after me like mad.", "내가 돌아서니, 거기 페넬로피가 미친 듯이 나를 뒤쫓아 뛰어 내려오고 있었다."], ["“Father!”", "\"아버지!\""], ["she screamed, “come upstairs, for God’s sake!", "그 애가 비명을 질렀다. \"제발 위층으로 올라오세요!"], ["The Diamond is gone!”", "다이아몬드가 사라졌어요!\""]], [["“Are you out of your mind?”", "\"너 정신이 나갔니?\""], ["I asked her.", "나는 그 애에게 물었다."]], [["“Gone!”", "\"사라졌어요!\""], ["says Penelope.", "하고 페넬로피가 말한다."], ["“Gone, nobody knows how!", "\"어떻게 된 건지 아무도 몰라요!"], ["Come up and see.”", "올라와서 보세요.\""]], [["She dragged me after her into our young lady’s sitting-room, which opened into her bedroom.", "그 애가 나를 끌고 우리 젊은 아가씨의 거실로 들어갔는데, 그 방은 아가씨의 침실로 통해 있었다."], ["There, on the threshold of her bedroom door, stood Miss Rachel, almost as white in the face as the white dressing-gown that clothed her.", "거기, 침실 문지방에, 레이철 아가씨가, 제가 걸친 흰 실내복만큼이나 낯빛이 하얗게 질려 서 있었다."], ["There also stood the two doors of the Indian cabinet, wide open.", "거기 또, 인도식 캐비닛의 두 문짝이 활짝 열려 있었다."], ["One of the drawers inside was pulled out as far as it would go.", "그 안의 서랍 하나가 끝까지 잡아 빼내진 채였다."]], [["“Look!”", "\"보세요!\""], ["says Penelope.", "하고 페넬로피가 말한다."], ["“I myself saw Miss Rachel put the Diamond into that drawer last night.”", "\"어젯밤 레이철 아가씨가 그 서랍에 다이아몬드를 넣으시는 걸 제가 이 눈으로 봤다니까요.\""], ["I went to the cabinet.", "나는 캐비닛으로 갔다."], ["The drawer was empty.", "서랍은 텅 비어 있었다."]], [["“Is this true, miss?”", "\"이게 사실입니까, 아가씨?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."]], [["With a look that was not like herself, with a voice that was not like her own, Miss Rachel answered as my daughter had answered:", "제 자신 같지 않은 표정으로, 제 자신 같지 않은 목소리로, 레이철 아가씨는 내 딸이 대답했던 그대로 대답했다."]], [["“The Diamond is gone!”", "\"다이아몬드가 사라졌어요!\""]], [["Having said those words, she withdrew into her bedroom, and shut and locked the door.", "그 말을 하고서, 아가씨는 제 침실로 물러나, 문을 닫고 잠갔다."]], [["Before we knew which way to turn next, my lady came in, hearing my voice in her daughter’s sitting-room, and wondering what had happened.", "우리가 다음엔 어느 쪽으로 몸을 돌려야 할지 알기도 전에, 당신 따님의 거실에서 나는 내 목소리를 듣고 무슨 일인가 싶으셨던 마님이 들어오셨다."], ["The news of the loss of the Diamond seemed to petrify her.", "다이아몬드를 잃어버렸다는 소식이 마님을 돌처럼 굳게 하는 듯했다."], ["She went straight to Miss Rachel’s bedroom, and insisted on being admitted.", "마님은 곧장 레이철 아가씨의 침실로 가시더니, 들여보내 달라고 우기셨다."], ["Miss Rachel let her in.", "레이철 아가씨가 마님을 들여보냈다."]], [["The alarm, running through the house like fire, caught the two gentlemen next.", "불길처럼 온 집안을 내달린 그 놀라움이, 다음으로 두 신사에게 옮아붙었다."]], [["Mr. Godfrey was the first to come out of his room.", "고드프리 씨가 제 방에서 맨 먼저 나왔다."], ["All he did when he heard what had happened was to hold up his hands in a state of bewilderment, which didn’t say much for his natural strength of mind.", "무슨 일이 있었는지 듣고서 그가 한 일이라곤, 어리둥절하여 두 손을 치켜드는 것뿐이었으니, 그것은 그의 타고난 심지의 굳음을 그리 대변해 주지 못했다."], ["Mr. Franklin, whose clear head I had confidently counted on to advise us, seemed to be as helpless as his cousin when he heard the news in his turn.", "우리에게 조언을 해 주리라 내가 자신 있게 기대던 그 맑은 머리의 프랭클린 도련님도, 제 차례로 그 소식을 듣고서는, 제 사촌만큼이나 속수무책인 듯했다."], ["For a wonder, he had had a good night’s rest at last; and the unaccustomed luxury of sleep had, as he said himself, apparently stupefied him.", "웬일로, 그는 마침내 하룻밤 푹 잤던 것이다. 그리고 그 낯선 잠의 호사가, 제 말로는, 그를 얼떨떨하게 만들어 놓은 모양이었다."], ["However, when he had swallowed his cup of coffee—which he always took, on the foreign plan, some hours before he ate any breakfast—his brains brightened; the clear-headed side of him turned up, and he took the matter in hand, resolutely and cleverly, much as follows:", "그렇지만 그가 커피 한 잔을 마시고 나자—그는 외국식으로 늘 아침을 먹기 몇 시간 전에 커피를 마셨다—그의 머리가 환해졌다. 그의 명민한 면이 튀어나왔고, 그는 다음과 같이, 결연하고 영리하게, 그 일을 손에 잡았다."]], [["He first sent for the servants, and told them to leave all the lower doors and windows (with the exception of the front door, which I had opened) exactly as they had been left when we locked up over night.", "그는 먼저 하인들을 불러, 아래층의 모든 문과 창을 (내가 열어 둔 현관문만 빼고) 밤에 우리가 문단속했을 때 그대로 놔두라고 일렀다."], ["He next proposed to his cousin and to me to make quite sure, before we took any further steps, that the Diamond had not accidentally dropped somewhere out of sight—say at the back of the cabinet, or down behind the table on which the cabinet stood.", "그다음으로 그는, 우리가 더 나아간 조처를 취하기에 앞서, 그 다이아몬드가 어쩌다 어딘가 눈에 안 띄는 데로 떨어진 것은 아닌지—이를테면 캐비닛 뒤쪽이나, 캐비닛이 놓인 탁자 뒤로—확실히 해 두자고 제 사촌과 내게 제안했다."], ["Having searched in both places, and found nothing—having also questioned Penelope, and discovered from her no more than the little she had already told me—Mr. Franklin suggested next extending our inquiries to Miss Rachel, and sent Penelope to knock at her bedroom door.", "그 두 군데를 다 뒤져 보고 아무것도 찾지 못한 뒤—또한 페넬로피에게 물어보았으나 그 애가 이미 내게 말한 그 얼마 말고는 더 알아내지 못한 뒤—프랭클린 도련님은 다음으로 우리 조사를 레이철 아가씨께로 넓히자고 제안하며, 페넬로피를 보내 아가씨 침실 문을 두드리게 했다."]], [["My lady answered the knock, and closed the door behind her.", "마님이 그 두드림에 대답하시고, 뒤로 문을 닫으셨다."], ["The moment after we heard it locked inside by Miss Rachel.", "그다음 순간 우리는 레이철 아가씨가 안에서 그것을 잠그는 소리를 들었다."], ["My mistress came out among us, looking sorely puzzled and distressed.", "내 여주인이 우리 가운데로 나오셨는데, 몹시 난감하고 괴로운 낯빛이셨다."], ["“The loss of the Diamond seems to have quite overwhelmed Rachel,”", "\"다이아몬드를 잃은 것이 레이철을 아주 압도해 버린 모양이야.\""], ["she said, in reply to Mr. Franklin.", "하고 마님이 프랭클린 도련님께 대답하여 말씀하셨다."], ["“She shrinks, in the strangest manner, from speaking of it, even to me.", "\"그 애가, 참으로 이상하게도, 나한테조차 그 이야기를 하기를 움츠리는구나."], ["It is impossible you can see her for the present.”", "지금은 도저히 그 애를 볼 수가 없겠어.\""]], [["Having added to our perplexities by this account of Miss Rachel, my lady, after a little effort, recovered her usual composure, and acted with her usual decision.", "레이철 아가씨에 관한 이 이야기로 우리의 곤혹스러움을 더해 놓으시고서, 마님은, 잠시 애쓴 끝에, 여느 때의 침착함을 되찾아, 여느 때의 결단으로 움직이셨다."]], [["“I suppose there is no help for it?”", "\"달리 도리가 없겠지?\""], ["she said, quietly.", "하고 마님이 조용히 말씀하셨다."], ["“I suppose I have no alternative but to send for the police?”", "\"경찰을 부르는 것 말고는 내게 다른 방도가 없겠지?\""]], [["“And the first thing for the police to do,” added Mr.", "\"그리고 경찰이 맨 먼저 할 일은,\""], ["Franklin, catching her up, “is to lay hands on the Indian jugglers who performed here last night.”", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 마님의 말을 받아 덧붙였다. \"어젯밤 여기서 재주를 부린 그 인도인 요술쟁이들을 잡아들이는 것입니다.\""]], [["My lady and Mr. Godfrey (not knowing what Mr. Franklin and I knew) both started, and both looked surprised.", "마님과 고드프리 씨는 (프랭클린 도련님과 내가 아는 것을 알지 못한지라) 둘 다 흠칫 놀라, 둘 다 어리둥절한 낯빛이 되었다."]], [["“I can’t stop to explain myself now,”", "\"지금은 제가 다 설명해 드릴 겨를이 없습니다.\""], ["Mr. Franklin went on.", "프랭클린 도련님이 이어 갔다."], ["“I can only tell you that the Indians have certainly stolen the Diamond.", "\"다만, 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 훔친 게 틀림없다는 것만은 말씀드릴 수 있어요."], ["Give me a letter of introduction,”", "소개장을 하나 써 주십시오.\" 하고 그가 마님께 말한다."], ["says he, addressing my lady, “to one of the magistrates at Frizinghall—merely telling him that I represent your interests and wishes, and let me ride off with it instantly.", "\"프리징홀의 치안판사 중 한 사람 앞으로—그저, 제가 이모님의 이익과 뜻을 대신한다고만 적어서요. 그러면 제가 그것을 들고 당장 말을 달려가겠습니다."], ["Our chance of catching the thieves may depend on our not wasting one unnecessary minute.”", "도둑을 잡을 우리 승산이, 쓸데없는 일 분도 허비하지 않는 데 달렸을지도 모르니까요.\""], ["(Nota bene: Whether it was the French side or the English, the right side of Mr. Franklin seemed to be uppermost now.", "(참고삼아 이르는 말이오나, 그것이 프랑스식 면이었든 영국식 면이었든, 프랭클린 도련님의 옳은 면이 이제 가장 위로 올라온 듯했다."], ["The only question was, How long would it last?)", "다만 물음은 하나, 그것이 얼마나 오래갈 것인가였다.)"]], [["He put pen, ink, and paper before his aunt, who (as it appeared to me) wrote the letter he wanted a little unwillingly.", "그는 이모 앞에 펜과 잉크와 종이를 내놓았고, 마님은 (내가 보기엔) 그가 원하는 편지를 자못 마지못해 쓰셨다."], ["If it had been possible to overlook such an event as the loss of a jewel worth twenty thousand pounds, I believe—with my lady’s opinion of her late brother, and her distrust of his birthday-gift—it would have been privately a relief to her to let the thieves get off with the Moonstone scot free.", "만일 이만 파운드짜리 보석을 잃어버린 그런 일을 못 본 척 넘길 수만 있었던들, 나는 믿거니와—돌아가신 오라비에 대한 마님의 생각과, 그의 생일 선물에 대한 마님의 불신을 헤아리면—그 도둑들이 월장석을 가지고 아무 벌도 안 받고 달아나게 놔두는 것이 마님께는 남몰래 오히려 안도가 되었을 것이다."]], [["I went out with Mr. Franklin to the stables, and took the opportunity of asking him how the Indians (whom I suspected, of course, as shrewdly as he did) could possibly have got into the house.", "나는 프랭클린 도련님과 함께 마구간으로 나가, 그 인도인들이 (물론 나도 그만큼이나 예리하게 의심하고 있었다) 대체 어떻게 집 안에 들어올 수 있었겠느냐고 그에게 물어볼 기회를 잡았다."]], [["“One of them might have slipped into the hall, in the confusion, when the dinner company were going away,”", "\"저녁 손님들이 돌아갈 때 그 혼란을 틈타, 그중 하나가 현관으로 슬쩍 들어왔을 수 있지.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“The fellow may have been under the sofa while my aunt and Rachel were talking about where the Diamond was to be put for the night.", "\"이모님과 레이철이 그 다이아몬드를 밤에 어디에 둘지 이야기하는 동안, 그 녀석이 소파 밑에 숨어 있었을 수도 있고."], ["He would only have to wait till the house was quiet, and there it would be in the cabinet, to be had for the taking.”", "그러면 집이 조용해질 때까지 기다리기만 하면 되는 거지. 그러면 그게 캐비닛 안에, 그냥 집어 가기만 하면 되게 놓여 있을 테니까.\""], ["With those words, he called to the groom to open the gate, and galloped off.", "그 말과 함께, 그는 마부에게 문을 열라고 외치고는, 말을 달려 떠났다."]], [["This seemed certainly to be the only rational explanation.", "이것이 분명 유일하게 이치에 닿는 설명인 듯했다."], ["But how had the thief contrived to make his escape from the house?", "그러나 그 도둑은 어떻게 집에서 빠져나갈 수 있었을까?"], ["I had found the front door locked and bolted, as I had left it at night, when I went to open it, after getting up.", "나는 아침에 일어나 현관문을 열러 갔을 때, 그것이 밤에 내가 놔둔 그대로 잠기고 빗장까지 걸려 있는 것을 보았다."], ["As for the other doors and windows, there they were still, all safe and fast, to speak for themselves.", "다른 문과 창들로 말하면, 거기 그것들이 여전히, 죄다 안전하고 단단히 잠긴 채, 스스로 증언하고 있었다."], ["The dogs, too?", "개들 또한?"], ["Suppose the thief had got away by dropping from one of the upper windows, how had he escaped the dogs?", "그 도둑이 위층 창 하나에서 뛰어내려 달아났다 치자, 그럼 그가 어떻게 개들에게서 벗어났단 말인가?"], ["Had he come provided for them with drugged meat?", "그가 개들을 위해 약을 탄 고기를 마련해 왔던가?"], ["As the doubt crossed my mind, the dogs themselves came galloping at me round a corner, rolling each other over on the wet grass, in such lively health and spirits that it was with no small difficulty I brought them to reason, and chained them up again.", "그 의심이 내 머리를 스치는 바로 그때, 개들 자신이 모퉁이를 돌아 내게로 껑충껑충 달려오더니, 젖은 풀 위에서 서로 뒹굴며, 어찌나 팔팔하게 건강과 기운이 넘치던지, 나는 여간 애를 먹고서야 그놈들을 얌전하게 만들어 다시 사슬에 맬 수 있었다."], ["The more I turned it over in my mind, the less satisfactory Mr. Franklin’s explanation appeared to be.", "그것을 마음속으로 곱씹으면 곱씹을수록, 프랭클린 도련님의 설명은 점점 더 미덥지 못해 보였다."]], [["We had our breakfasts—whatever happens in a house, robbery or murder, it doesn’t matter, you must have your breakfast.", "우리는 아침을 먹었다—집에서 무슨 일이 일어나든, 강도든 살인이든, 그런 건 아무래도 좋고, 아침은 먹어야 하는 법이다."], ["When we had done, my lady sent for me; and I found myself compelled to tell her all that I had hitherto concealed, relating to the Indians and their plot.", "우리가 다 먹고 나자, 마님이 나를 부르셨다. 그리고 나는, 그 인도인들과 그들의 흉계에 관해 여태 내가 감추어 온 모든 것을 마님께 말씀드리지 않을 수 없게 되었다."], ["Being a woman of a high courage, she soon got over the first startling effect of what I had to communicate.", "담이 큰 여인인지라, 마님은 내가 알린 것의 그 첫 충격을 곧 이겨 내셨다."], ["Her mind seemed to be far more perturbed about her daughter than about the heathen rogues and their conspiracy.", "마님의 마음은 그 이교도 악당들과 그들의 음모보다도 당신 따님을 두고 훨씬 더 어지러운 듯했다."], ["“You know how odd Rachel is, and how differently she behaves sometimes from other girls,”", "\"자네도 레이철이 얼마나 별난지, 그리고 이따금 다른 처녀들과 얼마나 다르게 구는지 알잖나.\""], ["my lady said to me.", "마님이 내게 말씀하셨다."], ["“But I have never, in all my experience, seen her so strange and so reserved as she is now.", "\"하지만 나는, 여태 겪은 모든 경험을 통틀어도, 그 애가 지금처럼 그렇게 이상하고 그렇게 속을 닫는 것을 본 적이 없어."], ["The loss of her jewel seems almost to have turned her brain.", "제 보석을 잃은 것이 그 애 머리를 거의 돌게 만든 것 같아."], ["Who would have thought that horrible Diamond could have laid such a hold on her in so short a time?”", "그 끔찍한 다이아몬드가 그토록 짧은 사이에 그 애를 그토록 사로잡을 줄 누가 생각이나 했겠나?\""]], [["It was certainly strange.", "그것은 분명 이상한 일이었다."], ["Taking toys and trinkets in general, Miss Rachel was nothing like so mad after them as most young girls.", "대체로 장난감이며 노리개로 말하면, 레이철 아가씨는 여느 젊은 처녀들만큼 그런 것에 사족을 못 쓰는 편은 결코 아니었다."], ["Yet there she was, still locked up inconsolably in her bedroom.", "그런데도 아가씨는, 여전히 제 침실에 틀어박혀 달랠 길 없이 있었다."], ["It is but fair to add that she was not the only one of us in the house who was thrown out of the regular groove.", "공평을 위해 덧붙이자면, 이 집에서 여느 궤도 밖으로 튕겨 나간 사람이 아가씨만은 아니었다."], ["Mr. Godfrey, for instance—though professionally a sort of consoler-general—seemed to be at a loss where to look for his own resources.", "이를테면 고드프리 씨는—직업상 이를테면 만인의 위로자 같은 사람이면서도—제 밑천을 어디서 찾아야 할지 갈피를 못 잡는 듯했다."], ["Having no company to amuse him, and getting no chance of trying what his experience of women in distress could do towards comforting Miss Rachel, he wandered hither and thither about the house and gardens in an aimless uneasy way.", "즐겁게 해 줄 친구도 없고, 곤경에 처한 여자들에 관한 제 경험이 레이철 아가씨를 달래는 데 무엇을 할 수 있을지 시험해 볼 기회도 얻지 못한지라, 그는 목적 없이 불안한 걸음으로 집과 정원 이곳저곳을 배회했다."], ["He was in two different minds about what it became him to do, after the misfortune that had happened to us.", "우리에게 닥친 그 불행 뒤에, 자기가 어떻게 처신해야 마땅한지를 두고 그는 두 마음이었다."], ["Ought he to relieve the family, in their present situation, of the responsibility of him as a guest, or ought he to stay on the chance that even his humble services might be of some use?", "지금 이 형편에서, 손님으로서의 자기라는 짐을 이 집안에서 덜어 드려야 할까, 아니면 제 하찮은 시중이나마 얼마간 쓸모가 있을지 모른다는 가망을 걸고 눌러 있어야 할까?"], ["He decided ultimately that the last course was perhaps the most customary and considerate course to take, in such a very peculiar case of family distress as this was.", "그는 마침내, 이렇게 여간 유별난 집안의 곤경에서는 아마 마지막 쪽이 가장 관례에 맞고 사려 깊은 처사이리라고 결정했다."], ["Circumstances try the metal a man is really made of. Mr.", "정황은 사내가 실제로 어떤 쇠붙이로 만들어졌는지를 시험한다."], ["Godfrey, tried by circumstances, showed himself of weaker metal than I had thought him to be.", "정황에 시험받은 고드프리 씨는, 내가 여겼던 것보다 더 무른 쇠붙이임을 스스로 드러냈다."], ["As for the women-servants excepting Rosanna Spearman, who kept by herself—they took to whispering together in corners, and staring at nothing suspiciously, as is the manner of that weaker half of the human family, when anything extraordinary happens in a house.", "여자 하인들로 말하면—홀로 지내던 로재나 스피어먼만 빼고—그들은 구석에서 서로 수군거리며, 아무것도 아닌 것을 미심쩍게 노려보기 시작했으니, 집에 뭔가 예사롭지 않은 일이 일어났을 때 인간이라는 무리의 그 여린 절반이 으레 그러하듯 말이다."], ["I myself acknowledge to have been fidgety and ill-tempered.", "나 자신도 안절부절못하고 심사가 사나웠음을 인정한다."], ["The cursed Moonstone had turned us all upside down.", "그 저주받은 월장석이 우리 모두를 발칵 뒤집어 놓았던 것이다."]], [["A little before eleven Mr. Franklin came back.", "열한 시가 조금 못 되어 프랭클린 도련님이 돌아왔다."], ["The resolute side of him had, to all appearance, given way, in the interval since his departure, under the stress that had been laid on it.", "그가 떠난 뒤로 그 사이에, 겉보기에, 그의 결연한 면이, 거기에 지워진 중압 아래 무너져 버린 터였다."], ["He had left us at a gallop; he came back to us at a walk.", "그는 말을 달려 우리를 떠났으나, 걸음걸이로 우리에게 돌아왔다."], ["When he went away, he was made of iron.", "떠날 때 그는 쇠로 만들어져 있었다."], ["When he returned, he was stuffed with cotton, as limp as limp could be.", "돌아올 때 그는 솜으로 채워져, 더없이 흐늘흐늘했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["says my lady, “are the police coming?”", "하고 마님이 말씀하신다. \"경찰이 오고 있느냐?\""]], [["“Yes,” says Mr.", "\"예.\""], ["Franklin; “they said they would follow me in a fly.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"삯마차로 저를 뒤따라오겠다고 했습니다."], ["Superintendent Seegrave, of your local police force, and two of his men.", "이곳 관할 경찰의 시그레이브 경정과 그 부하 둘이요. 그저 형식일 뿐입니다!"], ["A mere form! The case is hopeless.”", "이 사건은 가망이 없어요.\""]], [["“What!", "\"아니!"], ["have the Indians escaped, sir?”", "인도인들이 달아났답니까, 도련님?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“The poor ill-used Indians have been most unjustly put in prison,”", "\"그 가엾은, 억울한 누명을 쓴 인도인들은 더없이 부당하게 감옥에 갇혔다네.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“They are as innocent as the babe unborn.", "\"그들은 아직 태어나지 않은 갓난아이만큼이나 결백해."], ["My idea that one of them was hidden in the house has ended, like all the rest of my ideas, in smoke.", "그들 중 하나가 집 안에 숨어 있었다던 내 생각은, 내 다른 모든 생각들과 마찬가지로, 연기처럼 스러졌지."], ["It’s been proved,” says Mr.", "그건 그야말로 불가능한 일임이 밝혀졌어.\""], ["Franklin, dwelling with great relish on his own incapacity, “to be simply impossible.”", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제 무능함을 자못 흡족해하며 곱씹으며 말한다."]], [["After astonishing us by announcing this totally new turn in the matter of the Moonstone, our young gentleman, at his aunt’s request, took a seat, and explained himself.", "월장석 사건에서 이 전혀 새로운 국면을 알려 우리를 놀라게 하고서, 우리 젊은 신사는, 이모의 청에 따라, 자리에 앉아 사정을 설명했다."]], [["It appeared that the resolute side of him had held out as far as Frizinghall.", "겉보기에 그의 결연한 면은 프리징홀까지는 버텼던 모양이다."], ["He had put the whole case plainly before the magistrate, and the magistrate had at once sent for the police.", "그는 치안판사 앞에 그 사건 전부를 낱낱이 펼쳐 놓았고, 치안판사는 대뜸 경찰을 불렀다."], ["The first inquiries instituted about the Indians showed that they had not so much as attempted to leave the town.", "인도인들에 관해 처음 벌인 조사에서, 그들이 읍내를 떠나려는 시도조차 하지 않았음이 드러났다."], ["Further questions addressed to the police, proved that all three had been seen returning to Frizinghall with their boy, on the previous night between ten and eleven—which (regard being had to hours and distances) also proved that they had walked straight back after performing on our terrace.", "경찰에 던진 더 자세한 물음들로, 셋 다 지난밤 열 시에서 열한 시 사이에 제 소년을 데리고 프리징홀로 돌아가는 것이 목격되었음이 밝혀졌으니—이는 (시각과 거리를 헤아려 보면) 그들이 우리 테라스에서 재주를 부린 뒤 곧장 걸어 돌아갔음도 밝혀 주는 것이었다."], ["Later still, at midnight, the police, having occasion to search the common lodging-house where they lived, had seen them all three again, and their little boy with them, as usual.", "그보다 더 늦게, 자정에, 경찰이 그들이 사는 공동 숙박소를 뒤질 일이 있어 갔을 때, 셋을 다시, 그리고 그들과 함께 있는 어린 소년을, 여느 때처럼 다 보았다."], ["Soon after midnight I myself had safely shut up the house.", "자정 조금 뒤에 나 자신이 집을 안전히 잠갔다."], ["Plainer evidence than this, in favour of the Indians, there could not well be.", "인도인들에게 유리한 이보다 더 명백한 증거는 좀처럼 있을 수 없었다."], ["The magistrate said there was not even a case of suspicion against them so far.", "치안판사는 여태껏 그들에게 의심을 둘 건덕지조차 없다고 말했다."], ["But, as it was just possible, when the police came to investigate the matter, that discoveries affecting the jugglers might be made, he would contrive, by committing them as rogues and vagabonds, to keep them at our disposal, under lock and key, for a week.", "그러나, 경찰이 이 일을 조사하러 오면 그 요술쟁이들에게 걸리는 무언가가 드러날 수도 있는 만큼, 그는 그들을 부랑아이자 뜨내기로 구금하는 식으로 하여, 일주일 동안 우리 뜻대로 쓸 수 있게 자물쇠를 채워 붙들어 두도록 손을 쓰겠다고 했다."], ["They had ignorantly done something (I forget what) in the town, which barely brought them within the operation of the law.", "그들은 읍내에서 뭔가 (뭔지는 잊었다) 멋모르고 저지른 것이 있어, 간신히 법의 적용 범위에 들었다."], ["Every human institution (justice included) will stretch a little, if you only pull it the right way.", "인간의 온갖 제도는 (정의도 포함해서) 올바른 쪽으로 잡아당기기만 하면 얼마간 늘어나는 법이다."], ["The worthy magistrate was an old friend of my lady’s, and the Indians were “committed” for a week, as soon as the court opened that morning.", "그 훌륭한 치안판사는 마님의 오랜 벗이었고, 그리하여 인도인들은 그날 아침 법정이 열리자마자 일주일간 \"구금\"되었다."]], [["Such was Mr. Franklin’s narrative of events at Frizinghall.", "프리징홀에서 있었던 일에 대한 프랭클린 도련님의 이야기는 그러했다."], ["The Indian clue to the mystery of the lost jewel was now, to all appearance, a clue that had broken in our hands.", "잃어버린 보석의 수수께끼로 가는 인도인이라는 실마리는, 이제 겉보기에, 우리 손안에서 끊어져 버린 실마리였다."], ["If the jugglers were innocent, who, in the name of wonder, had taken the Moonstone out of Miss Rachel’s drawer?", "만일 그 요술쟁이들이 결백하다면, 대체 세상에 누가 레이철 아가씨의 서랍에서 그 월장석을 가져갔단 말인가?"]], [["Ten minutes later, to our infinite relief, Superintendent Seegrave arrived at the house.", "십 분 뒤, 우리로서는 이루 말 못 하게 안도스럽게도, 시그레이브 경정이 집에 도착했다."], ["He reported passing Mr. Franklin on the terrace, sitting in the sun (I suppose with the Italian side of him uppermost), and warning the police, as they went by, that the investigation was hopeless, before the investigation had begun.", "그는 테라스에서 (짐작건대 이탈리아식 면이 가장 위로 올라온 채) 볕을 쬐며 앉아 있던 프랭클린 도련님을 지나쳐 왔는데, 그가 지나가는 경찰에게, 조사가 시작되기도 전에 그 조사는 가망이 없다고 경고하더라고 보고했다."]], [["For a family in our situation, the Superintendent of the Frizinghall police was the most comforting officer you could wish to see.", "우리 같은 처지의 집안에게, 프리징홀 경찰 경정은 여러분이 보고 싶어 할 만한 가장 든든한 관리였다."], ["Mr. Seegrave was tall and portly, and military in his manners.", "시그레이브 씨는 키가 크고 풍채가 좋았으며, 태도가 군인다웠다."], ["He had a fine commanding voice, and a mighty resolute eye, and a grand frock-coat which buttoned beautifully up to his leather stock. “I’m the man you want!”", "그에게는 우렁차게 호령하는 목소리와, 여간 결연한 눈과, 가죽 목깃까지 곱게 단추를 잠근 훌륭한 프록코트가 있었다."], ["was written all over his face; and he ordered his two inferior police men about with a severity which convinced us all that there was no trifling with him.", "\"당신들이 찾던 사람이 바로 나요!\"라는 말이 그의 얼굴 곳곳에 쓰여 있었다. 그리고 그는 그 아래 두 순경을 어찌나 준엄하게 부리던지, 우리 모두는 그와는 함부로 장난칠 수 없음을 뼈저리게 느꼈다."]], [["He began by going round the premises, outside and in; the result of that investigation proving to him that no thieves had broken in upon us from outside, and that the robbery, consequently, must have been committed by some person in the house.", "그는 먼저 경내를, 밖과 안을, 한 바퀴 도는 것으로 시작했다. 그 조사의 결과로 그에게 드러난 바, 바깥에서 도둑이 우리에게 침입한 것이 아니며, 따라서 그 절도는 집 안의 누군가가 저지른 것이 틀림없다는 것이었다."], ["I leave you to imagine the state the servants were in when this official announcement first reached their ears.", "이 관(官)의 선언이 처음 그들 귀에 닿았을 때 하인들이 어떤 꼴이었는지는 여러분 상상에 맡기겠다."], ["The Superintendent decided to begin by examining the boudoir, and, that done, to examine the servants next.", "경정은 먼저 그 부두아르를 살펴보고, 그것이 끝나면 다음으로 하인들을 심문하기로 정했다."], ["At the same time, he posted one of his men on the staircase which led to the servants’ bedrooms, with instructions to let nobody in the house pass him, till further orders.", "그와 동시에 그는 하인들 침실로 이어지는 계단에 제 부하 하나를 배치하여, 다음 지시가 있을 때까지 집안 누구도 저를 지나쳐 가지 못하게 하라고 일렀다."]], [["At this latter proceeding, the weaker half of the human family went distracted on the spot.", "이 뒤엣 조처에, 인간이라는 무리의 그 여린 절반이 그 자리에서 이성을 잃었다."], ["They bounced out of their corners, whisked upstairs in a body to Miss Rachel’s room (Rosanna Spearman being carried away among them this time), burst in on Superintendent Seegrave, and, all looking equally guilty, summoned him to say which of them he suspected, at once.", "그들은 저희 구석에서 튀어나와, 떼를 지어 위층 레이철 아가씨의 방으로 휙 몰려가서는 (이번엔 로재나 스피어먼도 그들 틈에 휩쓸려 갔다), 시그레이브 경정에게 들이닥쳐, 죄다 똑같이 죄지은 얼굴로, 그가 누구를 의심하는지 당장 말하라고 다그쳤다."]], [["Mr.", "경정 나리는 그 자리에 걸맞은 사람임을 입증했다."], ["Superintendent proved equal to the occasion; he looked at them with his resolute eye, and he cowed them with his military voice.", "그는 제 결연한 눈으로 그들을 바라보고, 제 군인다운 목소리로 그들을 주눅 들게 했다."]], [["“Now, then, you women, go downstairs again, every one of you.", "\"자, 그럼, 여자들, 다들 다시 아래층으로 내려가시오. 하나도 빠짐없이."], ["I won’t have you here. Look!”", "여기 있는 걸 두고 볼 수 없소."], ["says Mr.", "보시오!\""], ["Superintendent, suddenly pointing to a little smear of the decorative painting on Miss Rachel’s door, at the outer edge, just under the lock.", "하고 경정 나리가, 별안간 레이철 아가씨 문의 바깥쪽 가장자리, 자물쇠 바로 밑에 묻은 장식 그림의 조그만 얼룩을 가리키며 말한다."], ["“Look what mischief the petticoats of some of you have done already.", "\"당신들 중 누군가의 페티코트가 벌써 무슨 짓을 저질렀는지 보시오."], ["Clear out!", "나가시오!"], ["clear out!”", "나가!\""], ["Rosanna Spearman, who was nearest to him, and nearest to the little smear on the door, set the example of obedience, and slipped off instantly to her work.", "그에게서 가장 가깝고, 문에 묻은 그 조그만 얼룩에서도 가장 가깝던 로재나 스피어먼이, 복종의 본을 보이며, 대뜸 제 일터로 슬쩍 빠져나갔다."], ["The rest followed her out.", "나머지가 그 뒤를 따라 나갔다."], ["The Superintendent finished his examination of the room, and, making nothing of it, asked me who had first discovered the robbery.", "경정은 그 방 조사를 마치고, 아무 소득도 얻지 못하자, 누가 그 절도를 맨 처음 발견했느냐고 내게 물었다."], ["My daughter had first discovered it.", "내 딸이 맨 먼저 발견했다."], ["My daughter was sent for.", "내 딸이 불려 왔다."]], [["Mr. Superintendent proved to be a little too sharp with Penelope at starting.", "경정 나리는 처음부터 페넬로피에게 좀 지나치게 날카롭게 굴었다."], ["“Now, young woman, attend to me, and mind you speak the truth.”", "\"자, 젊은 처자, 내 말 잘 듣고, 진실을 말하도록 하시오.\""], ["Penelope fired up instantly.", "페넬로피가 대뜸 발끈했다."], ["“I’ve never been taught to tell lies Mr. Policeman!—and if father can stand there and hear me accused of falsehood and thieving, and my own bedroom shut against me, and my character taken away, which is all a poor girl has left, he’s not the good father I take him for!”", "\"저는 거짓말하라고 배운 적 없어요, 순경 나리!—그리고 아버지가 저기 서서 제가 거짓과 도둑질로 몰리는 걸 듣고도, 제 침실이 저한테 잠기고, 가난한 계집한테 남은 전부인 제 인격이 짓밟히는 걸 두고 보신다면, 제가 여긴 그 좋은 아버지가 아니시죠!\""], ["A timely word from me put Justice and Penelope on a pleasanter footing together.", "내가 때맞춰 한마디 하여 정의와 페넬로피를 좀 더 유쾌한 사이로 만들어 놓았다."], ["The questions and answers went swimmingly, and ended in nothing worth mentioning.", "물음과 대답이 술술 나아가, 언급할 만한 것 없이 끝났다."], ["My daughter had seen Miss Rachel put the Diamond in the drawer of the cabinet the last thing at night.", "내 딸은 레이철 아가씨가 밤에 맨 마지막으로 그 다이아몬드를 캐비닛 서랍에 넣는 것을 보았다."], ["She had gone in with Miss Rachel’s cup of tea at eight the next morning, and had found the drawer open and empty.", "그 애는 이튿날 아침 여덟 시에 레이철 아가씨의 찻잔을 들고 들어갔다가, 서랍이 열려 텅 빈 것을 발견했다."], ["Upon that, she had alarmed the house—and there was an end of Penelope’s evidence.", "그러자 그 애가 집안에 알린 것이었다—그것으로 페넬로피의 증언은 끝이었다."]], [["Mr. Superintendent next asked to see Miss Rachel herself.", "경정 나리는 다음으로 레이철 아가씨를 직접 보게 해 달라 청했다."], ["Penelope mentioned his request through the door.", "페넬로피가 문 너머로 그 청을 전했다."], ["The answer reached us by the same road: “I have nothing to tell the policeman—I can’t see anybody.”", "대답이 같은 길로 우리에게 왔다. \"그 순경한테 할 말은 아무것도 없어요—누구도 볼 수 없어요.\""], ["Our experienced officer looked equally surprised and offended when he heard that reply.", "우리의 노련한 관리는 그 대답을 듣고 놀라기도 하고 언짢기도 한 낯빛이 되었다."], ["I told him my young lady was ill, and begged him to wait a little and see her later. We thereupon went downstairs again, and were met by Mr.", "나는 그에게 우리 젊은 아가씨가 편찮으시다고 말하고, 조금 기다렸다가 이따 뵙기를 청했다."], ["Godfrey and Mr. Franklin crossing the hall.", "그리하여 우리는 다시 아래층으로 내려갔고, 현관을 가로지르던 고드프리 씨와 프랭클린 도련님을 만났다."]], [["The two gentlemen, being inmates of the house, were summoned to say if they could throw any light on the matter.", "두 신사는 집에 묵는 사람인지라, 이 일에 무슨 실마리라도 던져 줄 수 있는지 말하라고 불려 왔다."], ["Neither of them knew anything about it.", "둘 다 그것에 관해 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["Had they heard any suspicious noises during the previous night?", "지난밤 무슨 수상한 소리라도 들었는가?"], ["They had heard nothing but the pattering of the rain.", "그들은 빗소리 말고는 아무것도 듣지 못했다."], ["Had I, lying awake longer than either of them, heard nothing either? Nothing!", "그 둘 어느 쪽보다 더 오래 깨어 누워 있던 나도, 아무것도 못 들었는가?"], ["Released from examination, Mr.", "아무것도!"], ["Franklin, still sticking to the helpless view of our difficulty, whispered to me: “That man will be of no earthly use to us.", "심문에서 풀려나며, 여전히 우리 곤경을 속수무책으로 보는 프랭클린 도련님이 내게 속삭였다. \"저 사람은 우리한테 눈곱만큼도 쓸모가 없겠어."], ["Superintendent Seegrave is an ass.”", "시그레이브 경정은 얼간이야.\""], ["Released in his turn, Mr. Godfrey whispered to me—“Evidently a most competent person.", "제 차례로 풀려나며, 고드프리 씨가 내게 속삭였다—\"보아하니 여간 유능한 사람이야."], ["Betteredge, I have the greatest faith in him!”", "베터리지, 나는 그를 크게 믿네!\""], ["Many men, many opinions, as one of the ancients said, before my time.", "옛사람 하나가 내 시절보다 앞서 말했듯이, 사람이 여럿이면 의견도 여럿이다."]], [["Mr. Superintendent’s next proceeding took him back to the “boudoir”", "경정 나리의 다음 조처는 그를 다시 그 \"부두아르\"로 데려갔고, 내 딸과 나도 그 뒤를 바싹 따랐다."], ["again, with my daughter and me at his heels. His object was to discover whether any of the furniture had been moved, during the night, out of its customary place—his previous investigation in the room having, apparently, not gone quite far enough to satisfy his mind on this point.", "그의 목적은, 밤사이에 가구 가운데 무엇이 여느 제자리에서 옮겨진 것은 없는지 알아내는 것이었다—겉보기에, 그 방에서의 앞선 조사가 이 점에 대해 그의 마음을 흡족하게 할 만큼 충분히 나아가지 못했던 모양이다."]], [["While we were still poking about among the chairs and tables, the door of the bedroom was suddenly opened.", "우리가 아직 의자며 탁자 사이를 쑤시고 다니는데, 별안간 침실 문이 열렸다."], ["After having denied herself to everybody, Miss Rachel, to our astonishment, walked into the midst of us of her own accord.", "모두에게 저를 안 보이겠다고 하던 레이철 아가씨가, 우리를 놀라게도, 제 발로 우리 한가운데로 걸어 들어왔다."], ["She took up her garden hat from a chair, and then went straight to Penelope with this question:—", "아가씨는 의자에서 제 정원용 모자를 집어 들고, 그러고는 곧장 페넬로피에게 가서 이렇게 물었다.—"]], [["“Mr. Franklin Blake sent you with a message to me this morning?”", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 오늘 아침 너를 내게 전갈을 들려 보냈지?\""]], [["“Yes, miss.”", "\"예, 아가씨.\""]], [["“He wished to speak to me, didn’t he?”", "\"나와 이야기하고 싶어 하셨어, 그렇지?\""]], [["“Yes, miss.”", "\"예, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Where is he now?”", "\"그분 지금 어디 계시니?\""]], [["Hearing voices on the terrace below, I looked out of window, and saw the two gentlemen walking up and down together.", "아래 테라스에서 목소리가 들리기에, 나는 창밖을 내다보았고, 두 신사가 함께 오르내리며 거니는 것을 보았다."], ["Answering for my daughter, I said, “Mr.", "내 딸을 대신하여, 나는 말했다."], ["Franklin is on the terrace, miss.”", "\"프랭클린 도련님은 테라스에 계십니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["Without another word, without heeding Mr. Superintendent, who tried to speak to her, pale as death, and wrapped up strangely in her own thoughts, she left the room, and went down to her cousins on the terrace.", "더는 한마디도 없이, 저에게 말을 건네려는 경정 나리에는 아랑곳없이, 죽은 사람처럼 창백하게, 그리고 이상하게 제 생각에 파묻힌 채, 아가씨는 방을 나가, 테라스의 사촌들에게로 내려갔다."]], [["It showed a want of due respect, it showed a breach of good manners, on my part, but, for the life of me, I couldn’t help looking out of window when Miss Rachel met the gentlemen outside. She went up to Mr.", "그것은 마땅한 예의를 저버린 짓이요, 예법을 어긴 짓이었으나, 내 쪽에서 말이오, 나는 죽어도, 레이철 아가씨가 밖에서 신사들을 만날 때 창밖을 내다보지 않을 수가 없었다."], ["Franklin without appearing to notice Mr. Godfrey, who thereupon drew back and left them by themselves.", "아가씨는 고드프리 씨는 알아채지도 못한 채 프랭클린 도련님께 다가갔고, 그러자 고드프리 씨는 물러나 두 사람을 저희끼리 있게 했다."], ["What she said to Mr. Franklin appeared to be spoken vehemently.", "아가씨가 프랭클린 도련님께 하는 말은 격하게 하는 듯했다."], ["It lasted but for a short time, and, judging by what I saw of his face from the window, seemed to astonish him beyond all power of expression.", "그것은 잠깐밖에 이어지지 않았고, 창에서 내가 본 그의 얼굴로 미루어 보건대, 그를 이루 표현할 길 없이 놀라게 하는 듯했다."], ["While they were still together, my lady appeared on the terrace.", "그들이 아직 함께 있는데, 마님이 테라스에 나타나셨다."], ["Miss Rachel saw her—said a few last words to Mr. Franklin—and suddenly went back into the house again, before her mother came up with her.", "레이철 아가씨가 마님을 보고—프랭클린 도련님께 마지막 몇 마디를 하고는—제 어머니가 다다르기 전에, 별안간 다시 집 안으로 돌아갔다."], ["My lady, surprised herself, and noticing Mr. Franklin’s surprise, spoke to him.", "당신도 놀라시고, 프랭클린 도련님의 놀람을 알아채신 마님이, 그에게 말을 거셨다."], ["Mr. Godfrey joined them, and spoke also.", "고드프리 씨가 그들에게 합류하여, 또 말을 했다."], ["Mr. Franklin walked away a little between the two, telling them what had happened I suppose, for they both stopped short, after taking a few steps, like persons struck with amazement.", "프랭클린 도련님이 두 사람 사이에서 조금 걸어가며, 짐작건대 무슨 일이 있었는지 그들에게 이야기하는 듯했으니, 그들 둘 다 몇 걸음 옮기다가, 놀라움에 얻어맞은 사람들처럼 우뚝 멈춰 섰다."], ["I had just seen as much as this, when the door of the sitting-room was opened violently.", "내가 딱 이만큼 보았을 때, 거실 문이 거칠게 열렸다."], ["Miss Rachel walked swiftly through to her bedroom, wild and angry, with fierce eyes and flaming cheeks. Mr.", "레이철 아가씨가 침실로 잰걸음으로 걸어갔으니, 사납고 성난 채, 눈에는 불꽃이 일고 뺨은 타올랐다."], ["Superintendent once more attempted to question her.", "경정 나리가 다시 한번 아가씨에게 물으려 했다."], ["She turned round on him at her bedroom door.", "아가씨는 제 침실 문에서 그에게 홱 돌아섰다."], ["“I have not sent for you!”", "\"나는 당신을 부른 적 없어요!\""], ["she cried out vehemently.", "아가씨가 격하게 외쳤다."], ["“I don’t want you.", "\"나는 당신이 필요 없어요."], ["My Diamond is lost.", "내 다이아몬드는 사라졌어요."], ["Neither you nor anybody else will ever find it!”", "당신도 그 누구도 그걸 영영 못 찾을 거예요!\""], ["With those words she went in, and locked the door in our faces.", "그 말과 함께 아가씨는 안으로 들어가, 우리 코앞에서 문을 잠갔다."], ["Penelope, standing nearest to it, heard her burst out crying the moment she was alone again.", "그 문에서 가장 가까이 섰던 페넬로피가, 아가씨가 다시 혼자가 되자마자 울음을 터뜨리는 소리를 들었다."]], [["In a rage, one moment; in tears, the next!", "한순간엔 노발대발, 그다음 순간엔 눈물바다!"], ["What did it mean?", "그게 무슨 뜻이었을까?"]], [["I told the Superintendent it meant that Miss Rachel’s temper was upset by the loss of her jewel.", "나는 경정에게, 그것은 레이철 아가씨의 성미가 제 보석을 잃어 뒤틀린 것을 뜻한다고 말했다."], ["Being anxious for the honour of the family, it distressed me to see my young lady forget herself—even with a police-officer—and I made the best excuse I could, accordingly.", "집안의 명예가 걱정스러운지라, 우리 젊은 아가씨가—경찰관 앞에서까지—제 몸가짐을 잊는 것을 보매 나는 괴로웠고, 그래서 나는 그에 맞춰 되도록 그럴싸한 변명을 지어냈다."], ["In my own private mind I was more puzzled by Miss Rachel’s extraordinary language and conduct than words can tell.", "내 속마음으로는, 레이철 아가씨의 그 유별난 말과 행동에, 말로 다 할 수 없이 어리둥절했다."], ["Taking what she had said at her bedroom door as a guide to guess by, I could only conclude that she was mortally offended by our sending for the police, and that Mr. Franklin’s astonishment on the terrace was caused by her having expressed herself to him (as the person chiefly instrumental in fetching the police) to that effect.", "아가씨가 제 침실 문에서 한 말을 짐작의 실마리로 삼으면, 나는 다만, 아가씨가 우리가 경찰을 부른 것에 죽도록 언짢아하며, 테라스에서의 프랭클린 도련님의 놀람이, 아가씨가 그에게 (경찰을 데려오는 데 주로 앞장선 사람으로서) 그런 뜻을 내비쳤기 때문에 일어난 것이라고 결론지을 수밖에 없었다."], ["If this guess was right, why—having lost her Diamond—should she object to the presence in the house of the very people whose business it was to recover it for her?", "만일 이 짐작이 옳다면, 어째서—제 다이아몬드를 잃고서—그것을 저를 위해 되찾아 주는 게 소임인 바로 그 사람들이 집에 있는 것을 아가씨는 마다한단 말인가?"], ["And how, in Heaven’s name, could she know that the Moonstone would never be found again?", "그리고 대체 어떻게 아가씨가, 그 월장석을 영영 다시 못 찾으리라는 것을 알 수 있었단 말인가?"]], [["As things stood, at present, no answer to those questions was to be hoped for from anybody in the house. Mr.", "지금 형편으로는, 그 물음들에 대한 대답을 집안 누구에게서도 바랄 수 없었다."], ["Franklin appeared to think it a point of honour to forbear repeating to a servant—even to so old a servant as I was—what Miss Rachel had said to him on the terrace. Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 레이철 아가씨가 테라스에서 그에게 한 말을 하인에게—나만큼이나 오래된 하인에게까지도—되풀이하지 않는 것을 명예로운 일로 여기는 듯했다."], ["Godfrey, who, as a gentleman and a relative, had been probably admitted into Mr. Franklin’s confidence, respected that confidence as he was bound to do.", "신사이자 친척으로서, 아마 프랭클린 도련님의 속내에 끼워졌을 고드프리 씨는, 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로 그 속내를 지켰다."], ["My lady, who was also in the secret no doubt, and who alone had access to Miss Rachel, owned openly that she could make nothing of her.", "마님 역시 틀림없이 그 비밀을 아셨고, 홀로 레이철 아가씨께 드나들 수 있었으나, 그 애를 도무지 어떻게 할 수 없다고 솔직히 인정하셨다."], ["“You madden me when you talk of the Diamond!”", "\"다이아몬드 이야기를 하면 네가 나를 미치게 하는구나!\""], ["All her mother’s influence failed to extract from her a word more than that.", "어머니의 온갖 힘도 아가씨에게서 그 이상의 말은 한마디도 끌어내지 못했다."]], [["Here we were, then, at a dead-lock about Miss Rachel—and at a dead-lock about the Moonstone.", "그리하여 우리는, 레이철 아가씨에 관해서도 꽉 막혔고—월장석에 관해서도 꽉 막혔다."], ["In the first case, my lady was powerless to help us.", "앞엣것에서는, 마님이 우리를 도우실 힘이 없으셨다."], ["In the second (as you shall presently judge), Mr. Seegrave was fast approaching the condition of a superintendent at his wits’ end.", "뒤엣것에서는 (여러분도 곧 판단하시겠지만), 시그레이브 씨가 재간이 다한 경정의 처지에 빠르게 다가가고 있었다."]], [["Having ferreted about all over the “boudoir,”", "그 \"부두아르\""], ["without making any discoveries among the furniture, our experienced officer applied to me to know, whether the servants in general were or were not acquainted with the place in which the Diamond had been put for the night.", "곳곳을 샅샅이 뒤지고도 가구 가운데서 아무것도 발견하지 못한 우리의 노련한 관리는, 하인들이 대체로 그 다이아몬드를 밤에 넣어 둔 자리를 알고 있었는지 아닌지 내게 물어 왔다."]], [["“I knew where it was put, sir,”", "\"저는 그게 어디에 놓였는지 알았습니다요, 선생.\""], ["I said, “to begin with.", "나는 말했다."], ["Samuel, the footman, knew also—for he was present in the hall, when they were talking about where the Diamond was to be kept that night.", "\"우선 저부터가요. 하인 새뮤얼도 알았지요—그날 밤 다이아몬드를 어디 둘지 이야기할 때 그가 현관에 있었으니까요."], ["My daughter knew, as she has already told you.", "제 딸도, 이미 선생께 말씀드린 대로, 알았고요."], ["She or Samuel may have mentioned the thing to the other servants—or the other servants may have heard the talk for themselves, through the side-door of the hall, which might have been open to the back staircase.", "그 애나 새뮤얼이 다른 하인들한테 그 일을 말했을 수도 있고—아니면 다른 하인들이, 뒤쪽 계단으로 열려 있었을지도 모를 현관의 옆문을 통해, 그 이야기를 저희끼리 들었을 수도 있습니다."], ["For all I can tell, everybody in the house may have known where the jewel was, last night.”", "제가 아는 한, 어젯밤 그 보석이 어디 있는지 이 집 사람 누구나 알고 있었을 수 있어요.\""]], [["My answer presenting rather a wide field for Mr. Superintendent’s suspicions to range over, he tried to narrow it by asking about the servants’ characters next.", "내 대답이 경정 나리의 의심이 뻗칠 자못 넓은 벌판을 내놓은지라, 그는 다음으로 하인들의 인품에 대해 물으며 그것을 좁혀 보려 했다."]], [["I thought directly of Rosanna Spearman.", "나는 대뜸 로재나 스피어먼이 떠올랐다."], ["But it was neither my place nor my wish to direct suspicion against a poor girl, whose honesty had been above all doubt as long as I had known her.", "그러나 내가 그녀를 알아 온 내내 그 정직함이 온갖 의심을 넘어서 있던 가엾은 처녀에게 의심을 돌리는 것은, 내 자리도 내 뜻도 아니었다."], ["The matron at the Reformatory had reported her to my lady as a sincerely penitent and thoroughly trustworthy girl.", "갱생원의 원장은 그녀를 진심으로 뉘우치고 더없이 믿음직한 처녀로 마님께 보고한 바 있었다."], ["It was the Superintendent’s business to discover reason for suspecting her first—and then, and not till then, it would be my duty to tell him how she came into my lady’s service.", "그녀를 먼저 의심할 까닭을 찾아내는 것은 경정의 소임이었다—그러고 나서, 그러기 전에는 아니고, 그녀가 어떻게 마님의 시중을 들게 되었는지 그에게 말하는 것이 내 도리일 터였다."], ["“All our people have excellent characters,” I said.", "\"우리 사람들은 죄다 인품이 훌륭합니다요.\""], ["“And all have deserved the trust their mistress has placed in them.”", "나는 말했다. \"그리고 죄다, 여주인이 저희에게 둔 그 믿음을 받을 만합니다.\""], ["After that, there was but one thing left for Mr. Seegrave to do—namely, to set to work, and tackle the servants’ characters himself.", "그 뒤로, 시그레이브 씨가 할 수 있는 일은 딱 하나 남아 있었다—곧, 팔을 걷어붙이고 몸소 하인들의 인품과 씨름하는 것이었다."]], [["One after another, they were examined.", "하나씩 하나씩, 그들이 심문받았다."], ["One after another, they proved to have nothing to say—and said it (so far as the women were concerned) at great length, and with a very angry sense of the embargo laid on their bedrooms.", "하나씩 하나씩, 그들은 할 말이 아무것도 없음이 드러났다—그리고 (여자들로 말하면) 아주 길게, 그리고 제 침실에 놓인 그 통행 금지에 대한 여간한 노기를 담아, 그것을 말했다."], ["The rest of them being sent back to their places downstairs, Penelope was then summoned, and examined separately a second time.", "나머지가 아래층 제 자리로 돌려보내진 뒤, 다음으로 페넬로피가 불려 나와, 따로 두 번째로 심문받았다."]], [["My daughter’s little outbreak of temper in the “boudoir,” and her readiness to think herself suspected, appeared to have produced an unfavourable impression on Superintendent Seegrave.", "그 \"부두아르\"에서 내 딸이 조금 발끈했던 것과, 저를 의심받는다고 냉큼 여기던 것이, 시그레이브 경정에게 좋지 않은 인상을 남긴 모양이었다."], ["It seemed also to dwell a little on his mind, that she had been the last person who saw the Diamond at night.", "또한, 그 애가 밤에 그 다이아몬드를 본 마지막 사람이라는 것도 그의 마음에 조금 걸리는 듯했다."], ["When the second questioning was over, my girl came back to me in a frenzy.", "두 번째 심문이 끝나자, 내 딸이 광분하여 내게 돌아왔다."], ["There was no doubt of it any longer—the police-officer had almost as good as told her she was the thief!", "이제 더는 의심할 여지가 없었다—그 경찰관이 대놓고 그 애더러 도둑이라고 말한 것이나 진배없었던 것이다!"], ["I could scarcely believe him (taking Mr. Franklin’s view) to be quite such an ass as that.", "나는 (프랭클린 도련님의 견해를 좇아) 그가 그토록까지 얼간이라고는 좀처럼 믿을 수 없었다."], ["But, though he said nothing, the eye with which he looked at my daughter was not a very pleasant eye to see.", "그러나 그가 아무 말도 하지 않았어도, 그가 내 딸을 바라보던 그 눈은 그리 유쾌히 볼 만한 눈이 아니었다."], ["I laughed it off with poor Penelope, as something too ridiculous to be treated seriously—which it certainly was.", "나는 가엾은 페넬로피와 함께 그것을, 진지하게 다루기엔 너무 우스꽝스러운 것으로—실제로 그러했다—웃어넘겼다."], ["Secretly, I am afraid I was foolish enough to be angry too.", "남몰래, 나도 어리석게도 화가 났던 것 같아 걱정이다."], ["It was a little trying—it was, indeed.", "그것은 좀 사람을 시험하는 일이었다—정말이지 그러했다."], ["My girl sat down in a corner, with her apron over her head, quite broken-hearted.", "내 딸은 앞치마를 머리에 뒤집어쓴 채 구석에 앉아, 완전히 상심하여 있었다."], ["Foolish of her, you will say.", "그 애가 어리석다고 여러분은 말씀하시리라."], ["She might have waited till he openly accused her.", "그가 대놓고 저를 고발할 때까지 기다렸어도 됐을 것이라고."], ["Well, being a man of just an equal temper, I admit that.", "그야, 나도 그만큼 침착한 사람인지라, 그건 인정한다."], ["Still Mr. Superintendent might have remembered—never mind what he might have remembered.", "그래도 경정 나리는 떠올렸어야 했다—그가 무엇을 떠올렸어야 했는지는 아무래도 좋다."], ["The devil take him!", "그를 악마가 잡아가기를!"]], [["The next and last step in the investigation brought matters, as they say, to a crisis.", "조사의 다음이자 마지막 단계가, 이른바, 일을 위기로 몰고 갔다."], ["The officer had an interview (at which I was present) with my lady.", "그 관리가 (내가 배석한 자리에서) 마님과 면담을 했다."], ["After informing her that the Diamond must have been taken by somebody in the house, he requested permission for himself and his men to search the servants’ rooms and boxes on the spot.", "그 다이아몬드가 집 안의 누군가에게 틀림없이 훔쳐졌음을 마님께 알린 뒤, 그는 저와 제 부하들이 하인들의 방과 상자를 그 자리에서 뒤질 수 있게 허락해 달라 청했다."], ["My good mistress, like the generous high-bred woman she was, refused to let us be treated like thieves.", "나의 어지신 여주인은, 본디 그 관대하고 지체 높은 여인답게, 우리가 도둑처럼 취급받게 놔두기를 거절하셨다."], ["“I will never consent to make such a return as that,”", "\"나는 결코 그런 갚음을 하는 데 동의하지 않겠어요.\""], ["she said, “for all I owe to the faithful servants who are employed in my house.”", "마님이 말씀하셨다. \"내 집에서 일하는 그 충실한 하인들에게 내가 진 모든 것에 대해서 말이에요.\""]], [["Mr. Superintendent made his bow, with a look in my direction, which said plainly, “Why employ me, if you are to tie my hands in this way?”", "경정 나리는 절을 하며, 내 쪽으로 눈길을 던졌는데, 그것은 뻔히 이렇게 말하고 있었다."], ["As head of the servants, I felt directly that we were bound, in justice to all parties, not to profit by our mistress’s generosity.", "\"이런 식으로 내 손을 묶으실 거면, 나를 왜 부르셨소?\" 하인들의 우두머리로서, 나는 대뜸, 모든 당사자를 위한 정의를 생각해, 우리가 여주인의 관대함을 이용해서는 안 된다고 느꼈다."], ["“We gratefully thank your ladyship,”", "\"마님의 은혜에 감사드립니다요.\""], ["I said; “but we ask your permission to do what is right in this matter by giving up our keys.", "나는 말했다. \"하지만 저희 열쇠를 내드려, 이 일에서 옳은 바를 행하도록 허락해 주십사 청합니다."], ["When Gabriel Betteredge sets the example,”", "게이브리얼 베터리지가 본을 보이면,\""], ["says I, stopping Superintendent Seegrave at the door, “the rest of the servants will follow, I promise you.", "하고 나는, 문간에서 시그레이브 경정을 붙들어 세우며 말한다. \"나머지 하인들도 따를 겁니다요, 제가 약속하지요."], ["There are my keys, to begin with!”", "우선, 여기 제 열쇠올시다!\""], ["My lady took me by the hand, and thanked me with the tears in her eyes.", "마님이 내 손을 잡고, 눈에 눈물을 담아 내게 고마워하셨다."], ["Lord!", "아이고!"], ["what would I not have given, at that moment, for the privilege of knocking Superintendent Seegrave down!", "그 순간, 시그레이브 경정을 때려눕히는 특권을 위해서라면 내가 무엇인들 내놓지 않았으랴!"]], [["As I had promised for them, the other servants followed my lead, sorely against the grain, of course, but all taking the view that I took.", "내가 그들을 대신해 약속한 대로, 다른 하인들도 내 본을 따랐으니, 물론 몹시 마지못해서였으나, 죄다 내가 취한 견해를 취했다."], ["The women were a sight to see, while the police-officers were rummaging among their things.", "경찰관들이 저희 물건을 뒤지는 동안, 그 여자들은 볼만한 구경거리였다."], ["The cook looked as if she could grill Mr. Superintendent alive on a furnace, and the other women looked as if they could eat him when he was done.", "요리사는 마치 경정 나리를 화덕에 산 채로 구울 수 있을 듯한 얼굴이었고, 다른 여자들은 마치 그가 다 구워지면 그를 먹어 치울 수 있을 듯한 얼굴이었다."]], [["The search over, and no Diamond or sign of a Diamond being found, of course, anywhere, Superintendent Seegrave retired to my little room to consider with himself what he was to do next.", "수색이 끝나고, 물론, 어디서도 다이아몬드나 다이아몬드의 흔적이 나오지 않자, 시그레이브 경정은 다음엔 무엇을 할지 홀로 궁리하려고 내 조그만 방으로 물러났다."], ["He and his men had now been hours in the house, and had not advanced us one inch towards a discovery of how the Moonstone had been taken, or of whom we were to suspect as the thief.", "그와 그 부하들은 이제 몇 시간이나 집에 있었으면서도, 그 월장석이 어떻게 훔쳐졌는지, 아니면 우리가 누구를 도둑으로 의심해야 하는지를 밝히는 데는 우리를 한 치도 나아가게 하지 못했다."]], [["While the police-officer was still pondering in solitude, I was sent for to see Mr. Franklin in the library.", "그 경찰관이 아직 홀로 골똘히 궁리하는 동안, 나는 서재에서 프랭클린 도련님을 뵈러 오라는 부름을 받았다."], ["To my unutterable astonishment, just as my hand was on the door, it was suddenly opened from the inside, and out walked Rosanna Spearman!", "이루 말 못 하게 놀랍게도, 내 손이 막 문에 닿았을 때, 그것이 별안간 안에서 열리더니, 로재나 스피어먼이 걸어 나왔다!"]], [["After the library had been swept and cleaned in the morning, neither first nor second housemaid had any business in that room at any later period of the day.", "아침에 서재를 쓸고 닦은 뒤로는, 첫째 하녀든 둘째 하녀든 그날 그 뒤 시각에 그 방에 볼일이 있을 리 없었다."], ["I stopped Rosanna Spearman, and charged her with a breach of domestic discipline on the spot.", "나는 로재나 스피어먼을 붙들어 세우고, 그 자리에서 집안 규율을 어겼다고 나무랐다."]], [["“What might you want in the library at this time of day?”", "\"이 시각에 서재에서 대체 무슨 볼일이냐?\""], ["I inquired.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“Mr. Franklin Blake dropped one of his rings upstairs,”", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 위층에서 반지 하나를 떨어뜨리셔서요.\""], ["says Rosanna; “and I have been into the library to give it to him.”", "하고 로재나가 말한다. \"그걸 갖다드리려고 서재에 들어갔던 거예요.\""], ["The girl’s face was all in a flush as she made me that answer; and she walked away with a toss of her head and a look of self-importance which I was quite at a loss to account for.", "그렇게 대답하는 그 처녀의 얼굴은 온통 붉게 달아올라 있었다. 그리고 그녀는 고개를 홱 젖히며, 내가 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 잘난 체하는 낯빛으로 걸어가 버렸다."], ["The proceedings in the house had doubtless upset all the women-servants more or less; but none of them had gone clean out of their natural characters, as Rosanna, to all appearance, had now gone out of hers.", "집안에서 벌어진 일들이 여자 하인들을 죄다 얼마쯤은 뒤흔들어 놓은 것이 틀림없었으나, 겉보기에 로재나가 지금 제 타고난 성품에서 벗어난 것만큼 완전히 제 타고난 성품에서 벗어난 이는 아무도 없었다."]], [["I found Mr. Franklin writing at the library-table.", "나는 프랭클린 도련님이 서재 탁자에서 글을 쓰고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He asked for a conveyance to the railway station the moment I entered the room.", "그는 내가 방에 들어서기가 무섭게 기차역까지 갈 탈것을 청했다."], ["The first sound of his voice informed me that we now had the resolute side of him uppermost once more.", "그 목소리의 첫 울림이 내게 일러 주기로, 이제 우리에게는 그의 결연한 면이 다시 한번 가장 위로 올라와 있었다."], ["The man made of cotton had disappeared; and the man made of iron sat before me again.", "솜으로 만든 사내는 사라졌고, 쇠로 만든 사내가 다시 내 앞에 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Going to London, sir?”", "\"런던으로 가십니까, 도련님?\""], ["I asked.", "나는 물었다."]], [["“Going to telegraph to London,”", "\"런던에 전보를 치러 가는 걸세.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."], ["“I have convinced my aunt that we must have a cleverer head than Superintendent Seegrave’s to help us; and I have got her permission to despatch a telegram to my father.", "\"나는 이모님께, 우리한테는 시그레이브 경정보다 더 영리한 머리가 도와주어야 한다고 납득시켰네. 그리고 내 아버지께 전보를 보낼 허락을 받았지."], ["He knows the Chief Commissioner of Police, and the Commissioner can lay his hand on the right man to solve the mystery of the Diamond.", "아버지는 경찰 총감을 알고, 그 총감은 이 다이아몬드의 수수께끼를 풀 적임자를 짚어 낼 수 있어. 그런데, 수수께끼 이야기가 나왔으니 말인데,\""], ["Talking of mysteries, by-the-bye,” says Mr.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 목소리를 낮추며 말한다."], ["Franklin, dropping his voice, “I have another word to say to you before you go to the stables.", "\"자네가 마구간에 가기 전에 한마디 더 할 게 있네. 아직은 아무한테도 한마디도 흘리지 말게."], ["Don’t breathe a word of it to anybody as yet; but either Rosanna Spearman’s head is not quite right, or I am afraid she knows more about the Moonstone than she ought to know.”", "로재나 스피어먼의 머리가 좀 어떻게 된 게 아니라면, 아무래도 그녀가 그 월장석에 대해 마땅히 알아야 할 것보다 더 많이 아는 것 같아서 걱정이야.\""]], [["I can hardly tell whether I was more startled or distressed at hearing him say that.", "그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 들으매 내가 놀랐는지 괴로웠는지는 나도 좀처럼 가리기 어렵다."], ["If I had been younger, I might have confessed as much to Mr. Franklin.", "내가 더 젊었더라면, 나도 그만큼을 프랭클린 도련님께 털어놓았을지도 모른다."], ["But when you are old, you acquire one excellent habit.", "그러나 늙으면 훌륭한 버릇을 하나 익히게 된다."], ["In cases where you don’t see your way clearly, you hold your tongue.", "갈 길이 뚜렷이 보이지 않는 경우엔, 혀를 놀리지 않는 것이다."]], [["“She came in here with a ring I dropped in my bedroom,”", "\"그녀가 내가 침실에서 떨어뜨린 반지를 들고 여기 들어왔어.\""], ["Mr. Franklin went on.", "프랭클린 도련님이 이어 갔다."], ["“When I had thanked her, of course I expected her to go.", "\"내가 고맙다고 했으니, 물론 나는 그녀가 나가려니 했지."], ["Instead of that, she stood opposite to me at the table, looking at me in the oddest manner—half frightened, and half familiar—I couldn’t make it out.", "그런데 그러기는커녕, 그녀가 탁자를 사이에 두고 내 맞은편에 서서, 더없이 이상한 태로 나를 바라보는 게야—반쯤 겁먹고, 반쯤 허물없이—나는 도무지 영문을 알 수 없었어."], ["‘This is a strange thing about the Diamond, sir,’ she said, in a curiously sudden, headlong way.", "'이 다이아몬드는 참 이상한 일이에요, 도련님.' 그녀가 묘하게 별안간, 무모한 투로 말하더군."], ["I said, ‘Yes, it was,’ and wondered what was coming next.", "나는 '그래, 이상하지.' 하고는, 다음에 무슨 말이 나올까 싶었어."], ["Upon my honour, Betteredge, I think she must be wrong in the head!", "내 명예를 걸고, 베터리지, 나는 그녀의 머리가 어떻게 된 게 틀림없다고 생각해!"], ["She said, ‘They will never find the Diamond, sir, will they?", "그녀가 이러는 게야. '그들은 그 다이아몬드를 영영 못 찾을 거예요, 도련님, 그렇죠?"], ["No!", "그럼요!"], ["nor the person who took it—I’ll answer for that.’ She actually nodded and smiled at me!", "그걸 가져간 사람도 못 찾을 거고요—제가 장담해요.' 그녀가 실제로 내게 고개를 끄덕이며 웃어 보이더군!"], ["Before I could ask her what she meant, we heard your step outside.", "내가 무슨 뜻이냐고 미처 묻기도 전에, 우리는 바깥에서 자네 발소리를 들었어."], ["I suppose she was afraid of your catching her here.", "짐작건대 그녀는 자네가 저를 여기서 붙잡을까 봐 겁이 났던 게지."], ["At any rate, she changed colour, and left the room.", "아무튼, 그녀는 낯빛이 변하더니, 방을 나갔어."], ["What on earth does it mean?”", "대체 그게 무슨 뜻일까?\""]], [["I could not bring myself to tell him the girl’s story, even then.", "나는, 그때조차도, 차마 그에게 그 처녀의 사연을 말할 수가 없었다."], ["It would have been almost as good as telling him that she was the thief.", "그것은 그에게 그녀가 도둑이라고 말하는 것이나 거의 진배없었을 것이다."], ["Besides, even if I had made a clean breast of it, and even supposing she was the thief, the reason why she should let out her secret to Mr. Franklin, of all the people in the world, would have been still as far to seek as ever.", "게다가, 설령 내가 그것을 죄다 털어놓았다 한들, 또 설령 그녀가 도둑이라 한들, 하필 세상 모든 사람 가운데 프랭클린 도련님에게 그녀가 제 비밀을 흘려야 할 까닭은 여전히 오리무중이었을 것이다."]], [["“I can’t bear the idea of getting the poor girl into a scrape, merely because she has a flighty way with her, and talks very strangely,”", "\"그 가엾은 처녀가 그저 들뜬 데가 있고 아주 이상하게 말한다는 이유만으로 그녀를 곤경에 빠뜨린다는 건 견딜 수가 없어.\""], ["Mr. Franklin went on.", "프랭클린 도련님이 이어 갔다."], ["“And yet if she had said to the Superintendent what she said to me, fool as he is, I’m afraid——”", "\"그렇지만 만일 그녀가 나한테 한 말을 그 경정한테 했다면, 그가 아무리 얼간이라도, 아무래도——\""], ["He stopped there, and left the rest unspoken.", "그는 거기서 멈추고, 나머지는 말하지 않은 채로 두었다."]], [["“The best way, sir,”", "\"가장 좋은 방법은요, 도련님,\""], ["I said, “will be for me to say two words privately to my mistress about it at the first opportunity.", "나는 말했다. \"첫 기회가 오는 대로 제가 그것에 대해 여주인께 남몰래 두어 마디 아뢰는 겁니다."], ["My lady has a very friendly interest in Rosanna; and the girl may only have been forward and foolish, after all.", "마님은 로재나에게 아주 다정한 관심을 두고 계시니까요. 그리고 그 처녀는 결국 그저 주제넘고 어리석었던 것뿐인지도 모르고요."], ["When there’s a mess of any kind in a house, sir, the women-servants like to look at the gloomy side—it gives the poor wretches a kind of importance in their own eyes.", "집에 무슨 소동이 나면요, 도련님, 여자 하인들은 어두운 쪽을 보고 싶어 합니다—그게 그 가엾은 것들에게 제 눈에 일종의 무게를 실어 주거든요."], ["If there’s anybody ill, trust the women for prophesying that the person will die.", "누가 병들면, 여자들이 그 사람이 죽으리라 예언하는 데 걸어 보세요."], ["If it’s a jewel lost, trust them for prophesying that it will never be found again.”", "보석을 잃으면, 그것을 영영 다시 못 찾으리라 예언하는 데 걸어 보시고요.\""]], [["This view (which I am bound to say, I thought a probable view myself, on reflection) seemed to relieve Mr.", "이 견해가 (거듭 생각하니 나 스스로도 있음 직한 견해라 여겼음을 밝혀 둔다) 프랭클린 도련님을 대단히 안심시키는 듯했다."], ["Franklin mightily: he folded up his telegram, and dismissed the subject.", "그는 제 전보를 접어 넣고, 그 이야기를 접었다."], ["On my way to the stables, to order the pony-chaise, I looked in at the servants’ hall, where they were at dinner.", "조랑말 이륜마차를 대령시키러 마구간으로 가는 길에, 나는 하인들 식당을 들여다보았는데, 그들이 저녁을 먹고 있었다."], ["Rosanna Spearman was not among them.", "로재나 스피어먼은 그들 틈에 없었다."], ["On inquiry, I found that she had been suddenly taken ill, and had gone upstairs to her own room to lie down.", "물어보니, 그녀가 별안간 앓아누워, 눕겠다고 제 방으로 위층에 올라갔다는 것이었다."]], [["“Curious!", "\"이상하네!"], ["She looked well enough when I saw her last,”", "내가 마지막으로 봤을 땐 멀쩡해 보였는데.\""], ["I remarked.", "나는 한마디 했다."]], [["Penelope followed me out.", "페넬로피가 나를 따라 나왔다."], ["“Don’t talk in that way before the rest of them, father,”", "\"다른 사람들 앞에서 그런 식으로 말씀하지 마세요, 아버지.\""], ["she said.", "그 애가 말했다."], ["“You only make them harder on Rosanna than ever.", "\"아버지는 그저 그들이 로재나한테 더 모질게 굴게 만들 뿐이에요."], ["The poor thing is breaking her heart about Mr. Franklin Blake.”", "그 가엾은 것은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨 때문에 애간장이 다 타고 있다고요.\""]], [["Here was another view of the girl’s conduct.", "여기 그 처녀의 행동에 대한 또 다른 견해가 있었다."], ["If it was possible for Penelope to be right, the explanation of Rosanna’s strange language and behaviour might have been all in this—that she didn’t care what she said, so long as she could surprise Mr. Franklin into speaking to her.", "만일 페넬로피가 옳을 수 있다면, 로재나의 그 이상한 말과 행동에 대한 설명은 죄다 이런 것이었을지도 모른다—프랭클린 도련님을 놀래어 저한테 말을 걸게 할 수만 있다면, 그녀는 제가 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 않았다는 것 말이다."], ["Granting that to be the right reading of the riddle, it accounted, perhaps, for her flighty, self-conceited manner when she passed me in the hall.", "그것이 그 수수께끼의 옳은 풀이라 치면, 그것은 아마, 그녀가 현관에서 나를 지나칠 때 그 들뜨고 잘난 체하던 태를 설명해 주었다."], ["Though he had only said three words, still she had carried her point, and Mr. Franklin had spoken to her.", "그가 겨우 세 마디밖에 하지 않았어도, 어쨌든 그녀는 제 목적을 이룬 것이었으니, 프랭클린 도련님이 그녀에게 말을 걸었던 것이다."]], [["I saw the pony harnessed myself.", "나는 조랑말에 마구가 채워지는 것을 손수 지켜보았다."], ["In the infernal network of mysteries and uncertainties that now surrounded us, I declare it was a relief to observe how well the buckles and straps understood each other!", "이제 우리를 에워싼 그 지옥 같은 수수께끼와 종잡을 수 없음의 그물 속에서, 정말이지, 그 죔쇠와 가죽끈이 서로를 그토록 잘 알아듣는 것을 보는 것은 하나의 안도였다!"], ["When you had seen the pony backed into the shafts of the chaise, you had seen something there was no doubt about.", "조랑말이 이륜마차의 채 안으로 뒷걸음쳐 들어가는 것을 보고 나면, 여러분은 의심할 여지없는 무언가를 본 것이었다."], ["And that, let me tell you, was becoming a treat of the rarest kind in our household.", "그리고 그것은, 내 말하건대, 우리 집안에서 더없이 진귀한 종류의 대접이 되어 가고 있었다."]], [["Going round with the chaise to the front door, I found not only Mr. Franklin, but Mr. Godfrey and Superintendent Seegrave also waiting for me on the steps.", "이륜마차를 몰고 현관으로 돌아가 보니, 프랭클린 도련님뿐 아니라 고드프리 씨와 시그레이브 경정까지도 층계에서 나를 기다리고 있었다."]], [["Mr. Superintendent’s reflections (after failing to find the Diamond in the servants’ rooms or boxes) had led him, it appeared, to an entirely new conclusion.", "경정 나리의 궁리는 (하인들의 방과 상자에서 그 다이아몬드를 찾는 데 실패한 뒤) 그를 전혀 새로운 결론으로 이끈 모양이었다."], ["Still sticking to his first text, namely, that somebody in the house had stolen the jewel, our experienced officer was now of the opinion that the thief (he was wise enough not to name poor Penelope, whatever he might privately think of her!) had been acting in concert with the Indians; and he accordingly proposed shifting his inquiries to the jugglers in the prison at Frizinghall.", "여전히 제 첫 명제, 곧 집 안의 누군가가 그 보석을 훔쳤다는 것에 매달린 채, 우리의 노련한 관리는, 이제 그 도둑이 (그가 페넬로피를 속으로 어찌 여기든, 그 가엾은 페넬로피의 이름을 대지 않을 만큼은 지혜로웠다!) 인도인들과 짜고 움직였다는 견해였다."], ["Hearing of this new move, Mr.", "그리하여 그는 제 조사를 프리징홀 감옥의 요술쟁이들에게로 옮기자고 제안했다."], ["Franklin had volunteered to take the Superintendent back to the town, from which he could telegraph to London as easily as from our station. Mr.", "이 새 움직임을 듣고, 프랭클린 도련님이, 그 경정을 읍내로 다시 데려다주겠다고 자원했으니, 거기서라면 우리 역에서만큼이나 손쉽게 런던에 전보를 칠 수 있었다."], ["Godfrey, still devoutly believing in Mr. Seegrave, and greatly interested in witnessing the examination of the Indians, had begged leave to accompany the officer to Frizinghall.", "여전히 시그레이브 씨를 독실히 믿고, 인도인들의 심문을 참관하는 데 크게 흥미를 느낀 고드프리 씨는, 그 관리를 따라 프리징홀에 가게 해 달라 청했다."], ["One of the two inferior policemen was to be left at the house, in case anything happened.", "두 하급 순경 중 하나는, 무슨 일이 생길 경우에 대비해 집에 남기로 했다."], ["The other was to go back with the Superintendent to the town.", "다른 하나는 경정과 함께 읍내로 돌아가기로 했다."], ["So the four places in the pony-chaise were just filled.", "그리하여 조랑말 이륜마차의 네 자리가 딱 채워졌다."]], [["Before he took the reins to drive off, Mr. Franklin walked me away a few steps out of hearing of the others.", "고삐를 잡고 몰아 떠나기 전에, 프랭클린 도련님이 나를 다른 이들에게 안 들리는 데로 몇 걸음 데리고 갔다."]], [["“I will wait to telegraph to London,” he said, “till I see what comes of our examination of the Indians.", "\"나는 런던에 전보 치는 것을, 우리의 인도인 심문이 어찌 되는지 볼 때까지 미루겠네.\""], ["My own conviction is, that this muddle-headed local police-officer is as much in the dark as ever, and is simply trying to gain time.", "그가 말했다. \"내 나름의 확신으로는, 이 얼빠진 시골 경찰관은 여전히 오리무중이고, 그저 시간을 벌려는 것뿐이야."], ["The idea of any of the servants being in league with the Indians is a preposterous absurdity, in my opinion.", "하인 중 누군가가 인도인들과 한패라는 발상은, 내 생각에, 얼토당토않은 헛소리일세."], ["Keep about the house, Betteredge, till I come back, and try what you can make of Rosanna Spearman.", "내가 돌아올 때까지 집 언저리에 있게, 베터리지. 그리고 로재나 스피어먼에게서 무엇을 알아낼 수 있는지 해 보게."], ["I don’t ask you to do anything degrading to your own self-respect, or anything cruel towards the girl.", "나는 자네더러 자네 자존심을 깎는 짓이나, 그 처녀에게 모진 짓을 하라고 청하는 게 아니야."], ["I only ask you to exercise your observation more carefully than usual.", "다만 여느 때보다 더 조심스레 자네 관찰력을 부려 보라고 청하는 걸세."], ["We will make as light of it as we can before my aunt—but this is a more important matter than you may suppose.”", "이모님 앞에서는 되도록 대수롭잖게 넘기겠지만—이건 자네가 짐작하는 것보다 더 중요한 문제야.\""]], [["“It is a matter of twenty thousand pounds, sir,”", "\"그건 이만 파운드짜리 문제입지요, 도련님.\""], ["I said, thinking of the value of the Diamond.", "나는 그 다이아몬드의 값어치를 생각하며 말했다."]], [["“It’s a matter of quieting Rachel’s mind,”", "\"그건 레이철의 마음을 가라앉히는 문제일세.\""], ["answered Mr. Franklin gravely.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 근엄하게 대답했다."], ["“I am very uneasy about her.”", "\"나는 그 애가 몹시 걱정스러워.\""]], [["He left me suddenly; as if he desired to cut short any further talk between us.", "그는 별안간 나를 떠났다. 마치 우리 사이의 더 이상의 이야기를 잘라 버리고 싶어 하는 듯이."], ["I thought I understood why.", "나는 그 까닭을 알 것 같았다."], ["Further talk might have let me into the secret of what Miss Rachel had said to him on the terrace.", "더 이야기하다 보면, 레이철 아가씨가 테라스에서 그에게 한 말의 비밀을 내가 알게 될 수도 있었으니까."]], [["So they drove away to Frizinghall.", "그리하여 그들은 프리징홀로 마차를 몰아 떠났다."], ["I was ready enough, in the girl’s own interest, to have a little talk with Rosanna in private.", "나는 그 처녀 자신을 위해서라도, 로재나와 남몰래 조금 이야기를 나눌 마음이 넉넉했다."], ["But the needful opportunity failed to present itself.", "그러나 그럴 만한 기회가 좀처럼 나지 않았다."], ["She only came downstairs again at tea-time.", "그녀는 찻시간에야 다시 아래층으로 내려왔다."], ["When she did appear, she was flighty and excited, had what they call an hysterical attack, took a dose of sal-volatile by my lady’s order, and was sent back to her bed.", "막상 나타났을 때, 그녀는 들떠 흥분해 있었고, 이른바 히스테리 발작을 일으켜, 마님의 분부로 각성제를 한 모금 먹고, 다시 잠자리로 보내졌다."]], [["The day wore on to its end drearily and miserably enough, I can tell you.", "그날은, 내 말하건대, 여간 음울하고 비참하게 끝을 향해 저물어 갔다."], ["Miss Rachel still kept her room, declaring that she was too ill to come down to dinner that day.", "레이철 아가씨는 여전히 제 방을 지키며, 몸이 너무 아파 그날은 저녁 식사에 내려올 수 없다고 잘라 말했다."], ["My lady was in such low spirits about her daughter, that I could not bring myself to make her additionally anxious, by reporting what Rosanna Spearman had said to Mr. Franklin.", "마님은 당신 따님을 두고 어찌나 심기가 가라앉으셨던지, 나는 차마, 로재나 스피어먼이 프랭클린 도련님께 한 말을 알려 마님을 한층 더 근심스럽게 해 드릴 수가 없었다."], ["Penelope persisted in believing that she was to be forthwith tried, sentenced, and transported for theft.", "페넬로피는, 그녀가 이제 곧 절도죄로 재판받고, 선고받고, 유배당하리라 굳게 믿고 있었다."], ["The other women took to their Bibles and hymn-books, and looked as sour as verjuice over their reading—a result, which I have observed, in my sphere of life, to follow generally on the performance of acts of piety at unaccustomed periods of the day.", "다른 여자들은 저희 성경과 찬송가책을 집어 들고, 그것을 읽으며 시큼한 풋과일처럼 뚱한 얼굴을 하고 있었다—이는, 내가 내 처지에서 관찰한 바로, 하루 중 익숙지 않은 시각에 경건한 짓을 행하면 대개 뒤따르는 결과다."], ["As for me, I hadn’t even heart enough to open my Robinson Crusoe.", "나로 말하면, 나는 「로빈슨 크루소」를 펼칠 마음조차 없었다."], ["I went out into the yard, and, being hard up for a little cheerful society, set my chair by the kennels, and talked to the dogs.", "나는 마당으로 나가, 조금 즐거운 벗이 아쉬운지라, 개집 곁에 내 의자를 놓고, 개들에게 말을 걸었다."]], [["Half an hour before dinner-time, the two gentlemen came back from Frizinghall, having arranged with Superintendent Seegrave that he was to return to us the next day.", "저녁 식사 시간 반 시간 전에, 두 신사가 프리징홀에서 돌아왔는데, 시그레이브 경정과, 그가 이튿날 우리에게 돌아오기로 약조를 해 둔 터였다."], ["They had called on Mr. Murthwaite, the Indian traveller, at his present residence, near the town.", "그들은 인도 여행가 머스웨이트 씨를, 읍내 가까운 그의 현 거처로 찾아갔었다."], ["At Mr. Franklin’s request, he had kindly given them the benefit of his knowledge of the language, in dealing with those two, out of the three Indians, who knew nothing of English.", "프랭클린 도련님의 청에 따라, 그는 친절히, 셋 중 영어를 전혀 모르는 그 두 인도인을 상대하는 데, 제 언어 지식의 도움을 그들에게 베풀어 주었다."], ["The examination, conducted carefully, and at great length, had ended in nothing; not the shadow of a reason being discovered for suspecting the jugglers of having tampered with any of our servants.", "조심스레, 그리고 아주 길게 진행된 그 심문은 아무 소득 없이 끝났다. 그 요술쟁이들이 우리 하인 누구와 손을 댔다고 의심할 만한 까닭은 그림자조차 발견되지 않았다."], ["On reaching that conclusion, Mr. Franklin had sent his telegraphic message to London, and there the matter now rested till tomorrow came.", "그 결론에 이르자, 프랭클린 도련님은 제 전보를 런던으로 보냈고, 이제 그 일은 내일이 올 때까지 그렇게 걸려 있었다."]], [["So much for the history of the day that followed the birthday.", "생일 다음 날의 이야기는 이쯤 해 두자."], ["Not a glimmer of light had broken in on us, so far.", "여태껏, 우리에게 한 줄기 빛도 새어 들지 않았다."], ["A day or two after, however, the darkness lifted a little.", "그러나 하루 이틀 뒤에, 그 어둠이 조금 걷혔다."], ["How, and with what result, you shall presently see.", "어떻게, 그리고 어떤 결과로였는지는, 여러분도 곧 보시게 될 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XII", "# CHAPTER XII"]], [["The Thursday night passed, and nothing happened.", "목요일 밤이 지나갔으나, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다."], ["With the Friday morning came two pieces of news.", "금요일 아침과 함께 두 가지 소식이 들어왔다."]], [["Item the first: the baker’s man declared he had met Rosanna Spearman, on the previous afternoon, with a thick veil on, walking towards Frizinghall by the foot-path way over the moor. It seemed strange that anybody should be mistaken about Rosanna, whose shoulder marked her out pretty plainly, poor thing—but mistaken the man must have been; for Rosanna, as you know, had been all the Thursday afternoon ill upstairs in her room.", "첫 번째 소식. 빵집 사내가 딱 잘라 말하기를, 자기가 전날 오후에 로재나 스피어먼을 만났다는 것이다. 두툼한 베일을 쓰고, 황야를 가로지르는 오솔길을 따라 프리징홀 쪽으로 걸어가고 있더라는 것이었다. 로재나로 말할 것 같으면, 그 딱한 것의 어깨가 그 사람됨을 제법 뚜렷이 드러내 주는 터라, 누군가 그를 잘못 알아본다는 것은 이상한 노릇으로 보였다—그러나 그 사내가 잘못 본 것임에 틀림없었다. 여러분도 아시다시피, 로재나는 목요일 오후 내내 위층 제 방에서 앓아누워 있었으니 말이다."]], [["Item the second came through the postman.", "두 번째 소식은 우편배달부를 통해 왔다."], ["Worthy Mr. Candy had said one more of his many unlucky things, when he drove off in the rain on the birthday night, and told me that a doctor’s skin was waterproof.", "그 착한 캔디 씨는, 생일날 밤 빗속으로 마차를 몰고 떠나며 나더러 의사의 살갗은 방수라고 말했을 때, 제 그 숱한 실없는 소리에 한마디를 더 보탠 셈이었다."], ["In spite of his skin, the wet had got through him.", "그 살갗이 무색하게도, 빗물이 그를 뚫고 스며들었으니 말이다."], ["He had caught a chill that night, and was now down with a fever.", "그는 그날 밤 오한이 들었고, 이제 열병으로 앓아누웠다."], ["The last accounts, brought by the postman, represented him to be light-headed—talking nonsense as glibly, poor man, in his delirium as he often talked it in his sober senses.", "우편배달부가 가져온 가장 최근의 전갈에 따르면, 그는 정신이 혼미하다는 것이었다—그 딱한 양반이, 헛소리 속에서도, 맑은 정신일 때 곧잘 지껄이던 그 허튼소리를 어찌나 술술 늘어놓던지."], ["We were all sorry for the little doctor; but Mr.", "우리는 다들 그 자그마한 의사를 딱하게 여겼다."], ["Franklin appeared to regret his illness, chiefly on Miss Rachel’s account.", "그러나 프랭클린 도련님은, 주로 레이철 아가씨 때문에, 그의 병을 안타까워하는 눈치였다."], ["From what he said to my lady, while I was in the room at breakfast-time, he appeared to think that Miss Rachel—if the suspense about the Moonstone was not soon set at rest—might stand in urgent need of the best medical advice at our disposal.", "아침 식사 때 내가 방에 있는 동안 그가 마님께 한 말로 미루어 보건대, 그는 이렇게 생각하는 듯했다—만일 월장석을 둘러싼 이 애타는 마음이 곧 가라앉지 않으면, 레이철 아가씨가 우리가 마련할 수 있는 최선의 의술을 다급히 필요로 하게 될지도 모른다고."]], [["Breakfast had not been over long, when a telegram from Mr. Blake, the elder, arrived, in answer to his son.", "아침 식사가 끝난 지 얼마 되지 않아, 아버지 되는 블레이크 씨가 제 아들에게 답으로 보낸 전보가 도착했다."], ["It informed us that he had laid hands (by help of his friend, the Commissioner) on the right man to help us.", "그 전보는 우리에게 알리기를, 그가 (제 친구인 경찰국장의 도움으로) 우리를 도와줄 적임자를 붙잡았다는 것이었다."], ["The name of him was Sergeant Cuff; and the arrival of him from London might be expected by the morning train.", "그 사람의 이름은 커프 부장이며, 그가 런던에서 오전 기차 편으로 도착할 것으로 예상하면 되리라는 것이었다."]], [["At reading the name of the new police-officer, Mr. Franklin gave a start.", "그 새 경찰관의 이름을 읽고, 프랭클린 도련님은 흠칫했다."], ["It seems that he had heard some curious anecdotes about Sergeant Cuff, from his father’s lawyer, during his stay in London.", "아무래도 그는 런던에 머무는 동안, 제 아버지의 변호사에게서 커프 부장에 관한 기이한 일화를 몇 가지 들은 모양이었다."]], [["“I begin to hope we are seeing the end of our anxieties already,” he said.", "\"우리 근심의 끝이 벌써 보이기 시작하는 것 같아 슬며시 기대가 생기는군.\""], ["“If half the stories I have heard are true, when it comes to unravelling a mystery, there isn’t the equal in England of Sergeant Cuff!”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"내가 들은 이야기의 절반만 사실이라 해도, 수수께끼를 푸는 일에 관한 한, 온 잉글랜드를 통틀어 커프 부장에 견줄 자가 없다는 거야!\""]], [["We all got excited and impatient as the time drew near for the appearance of this renowned and capable character.", "이 이름난 유능한 인물이 나타날 시각이 가까워질수록 우리는 모두 들뜨고 조바심이 났다."], ["Superintendent Seegrave, returning to us at his appointed time, and hearing that the Sergeant was expected, instantly shut himself up in a room, with pen, ink, and paper, to make notes of the Report which would be certainly expected from him.", "정해진 시각에 우리에게 돌아온 시그레이브 경정은, 그 부장이 오기로 되어 있다는 말을 듣자, 곧바로 방 하나에 틀어박혀 펜과 잉크와 종이를 갖추고서, 저에게서 분명 요구될 그 보고서의 메모를 작성하기 시작했다."], ["I should have liked to have gone to the station myself, to fetch the Sergeant.", "나로서는 내가 직접 역으로 나가 그 부장을 모셔 오고 싶었다."], ["But my lady’s carriage and horses were not to be thought of, even for the celebrated Cuff; and the pony-chaise was required later for Mr. Godfrey.", "그러나 그 이름난 커프를 위해서라 한들, 마님의 마차와 말은 언감생심이었고, 조랑말 이륜마차는 나중에 고드프리 씨께 필요했다."], ["He deeply regretted being obliged to leave his aunt at such an anxious time; and he kindly put off the hour of his departure till as late as the last train, for the purpose of hearing what the clever London police-officer thought of the case.", "그분은 이런 애타는 때에 이모님 곁을 떠나야 하는 것을 몹시 안타까워했다. 그래서 그 영리한 런던 경찰관이 이 사건을 어찌 보는지 들어 보려는 뜻에서, 친절하게도 제 출발 시각을 막차 시각까지 늦추어 놓았다."], ["But on Friday night he must be in town, having a Ladies’ Charity, in difficulties, waiting to consult him on Saturday morning.", "그러나 금요일 밤에는 시내에 있어야만 했으니, 어려움에 처한 어느 부인 자선회가 토요일 아침에 그와 의논하려고 기다리고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["When the time came for the Sergeant’s arrival, I went down to the gate to look out for him.", "그 부장이 도착할 시각이 되자, 나는 그를 맞으러 대문께로 내려갔다."]], [["A fly from the railway drove up as I reached the lodge; and out got a grizzled, elderly man, so miserably lean that he looked as if he had not got an ounce of flesh on his bones in any part of him.", "내가 문지기 오두막에 이르렀을 때 역에서 온 삯마차 한 대가 다가와 섰다. 그리고 백발이 희끗한 나이 든 사내가 내렸는데, 어찌나 딱하게 여위었던지 온몸 어디에도 뼈에 살점 한 냥 붙어 있지 않은 듯 보였다."], ["He was dressed all in decent black, with a white cravat round his neck.", "그는 온통 점잖은 검은 옷차림에, 목에는 흰 크라바트를 두르고 있었다."], ["His face was as sharp as a hatchet, and the skin of it was as yellow and dry and withered as an autumn leaf.", "그의 얼굴은 손도끼처럼 날카로웠고, 그 살갗은 가을 낙엽처럼 누렇고 메마르고 시들어 있었다."], ["His eyes, of a steely light grey, had a very disconcerting trick, when they encountered your eyes, of looking as if they expected something more from you than you were aware of yourself.", "강철빛이 도는 옅은 잿빛 눈은, 여러분의 눈과 마주칠 때, 여러분 자신이 미처 깨닫지 못한 무언가를 여러분에게서 더 바라는 듯 보이는, 몹시 당혹스러운 버릇이 있었다."], ["His walk was soft; his voice was melancholy; his long lanky fingers were hooked like claws.", "그의 걸음은 나긋했고, 목소리는 우울했으며, 길고 여윈 손가락은 갈퀴처럼 구부러져 있었다."], ["He might have been a parson, or an undertaker—or anything else you like, except what he really was.", "그는 목사일 수도, 장의사일 수도—아니, 여러분 좋으실 대로 그 무엇이라 해도 좋았으나, 오직 그가 정말로 그것인 바로 그것만은 아닌 듯했다."], ["A more complete opposite to Superintendent Seegrave than Sergeant Cuff, and a less comforting officer to look at, for a family in distress, I defy you to discover, search where you may.", "커프 부장보다 시그레이브 경정과 더 완벽하게 상반된 인물, 그리고 곤경에 처한 집안이 바라보기에 이보다 덜 위안이 되는 관리는, 아무리 이 잡듯 뒤진들 결코 찾아내지 못하리라고 나는 장담한다."]], [["“Is this Lady Verinder’s?”", "\"여기가 버린더 마님 댁이오?\""], ["he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"예, 그렇습니다.\""]], [["“I am Sergeant Cuff.”", "\"나는 커프 부장이오.\""]], [["“This way, sir, if you please.”", "\"이리로 오시지요, 나리.\""]], [["On our road to the house, I mentioned my name and position in the family, to satisfy him that he might speak to me about the business on which my lady was to employ him.", "집으로 가는 길에, 나는 마님이 그를 부린 그 용건에 관해 그가 내게 이야기해도 좋다는 것을 그에게 납득시키려고, 내 이름과 이 집안에서의 내 위치를 밝혔다."], ["Not a word did he say about the business, however, for all that.", "그러나 그럼에도 그는 그 용건에 관해서는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["He admired the grounds, and remarked that he felt the sea air very brisk and refreshing.", "그는 이 저택 부지를 감탄해 마지않았고, 바닷바람이 아주 상쾌하고 시원하게 느껴진다고 말했다."], ["I privately wondered, on my side, how the celebrated Cuff had got his reputation.", "나는 나대로, 이 이름난 커프가 대체 어떻게 그런 명성을 얻었는지 속으로 의아해했다."], ["We reached the house, in the temper of two strange dogs, coupled up together for the first time in their lives by the same chain.", "우리는 마치 난생처음 한 사슬에 함께 묶인 낯선 개 두 마리 같은 심사로 집에 다다랐다."]], [["Asking for my lady, and hearing that she was in one of the conservatories, we went round to the gardens at the back, and sent a servant to seek her.", "마님을 찾으니 온실 중 한 곳에 계시다는 대답이라, 우리는 집 뒤편 정원으로 돌아가서, 하인 하나를 보내 마님을 모셔 오게 했다."], ["While we were waiting, Sergeant Cuff looked through the evergreen arch on our left, spied out our rosery, and walked straight in, with the first appearance of anything like interest that he had shown yet.", "기다리는 동안, 커프 부장은 우리 왼편의 상록수 아치 너머를 들여다보다가, 우리 장미원을 발견하고는, 여태 그가 보인 것 중 처음으로 관심 비슷한 기색을 띠며 곧장 그 안으로 걸어 들어갔다."], ["To the gardener’s astonishment, and to my disgust, this celebrated policeman proved to be quite a mine of learning on the trumpery subject of rose-gardens.", "정원사가 놀라고 내가 못마땅해하게도, 이 이름난 경찰관은 장미원이라는 그 하찮은 주제에 관한 한 그야말로 지식의 보고임이 드러났다."]], [["“Ah, you’ve got the right exposure here to the south and sou’-west,”", "\"아, 여기는 남쪽과 남서쪽으로 향한 방향이 딱 알맞구려.\""], ["says the Sergeant, with a wag of his grizzled head, and a streak of pleasure in his melancholy voice.", "하고 부장이 그 희끗한 머리를 끄덕이며, 우울한 목소리에 한 줄기 기쁨을 실어 말한다."], ["“This is the shape for a rosery—nothing like a circle set in a square.", "\"이게 바로 장미원에 걸맞은 모양이지—네모 안에 동그라미를 앉힌 것만 한 게 없다오."], ["Yes, yes; with walks between all the beds.", "그렇지, 그렇지. 화단마다 사이로 길을 낸 것도 그렇고."], ["But they oughtn’t to be gravel walks like these. Grass, Mr.", "하지만 이렇게 자갈길이어서는 안 되지."], ["Gardener—grass walks between your roses; gravel’s too hard for them.", "잔디요, 정원사 양반—장미 사이로는 잔디 길을 내야지. 자갈은 장미한테 너무 딱딱하니까."], ["That’s a sweet pretty bed of white roses and blush roses.", "저기 저 흰 장미와 연분홍 장미를 심은 화단은 참 곱기도 하구려."], ["They always mix well together, don’t they?", "이 둘은 늘 잘 어울리지 않소?"], ["Here’s the white musk rose, Mr. Betteredge—our old English rose holding up its head along with the best and the newest of them.", "여기 이건 흰 사향장미로군, 베터리지 양반—우리네 오래된 잉글랜드 장미가 저 가장 빼어나고 가장 새로운 것들 틈에서도 고개를 꼿꼿이 쳐들고 있구려."], ["Pretty dear!”", "어여쁜 것!\""], ["says the Sergeant, fondling the Musk Rose with his lanky fingers, and speaking to it as if he was speaking to a child.", "하고 부장은 그 여윈 손가락으로 사향장미를 어루만지며, 마치 어린아이에게 말하듯 그것에게 말을 건넨다."]], [["This was a nice sort of man to recover Miss Rachel’s Diamond, and to find out the thief who stole it!", "이런 자가 레이철 아가씨의 다이아몬드를 되찾고, 그것을 훔친 도둑을 알아낼 위인이라니, 참으로 그럴싸한 노릇이었다!"]], [["“You seem to be fond of roses, Sergeant?”", "\"장미를 무척 좋아하시나 봅니다, 부장님?\""], ["I remarked.", "하고 내가 말했다."]], [["“I haven’t much time to be fond of anything,”", "\"나는 무언가를 좋아할 겨를이 그리 많지 않소.\""], ["says Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말한다."], ["“But when I have a moment’s fondness to bestow, most times, Mr. Betteredge, the roses get it.", "\"허나 내게 잠시라도 쏟을 애정이 생기면, 베터리지 양반, 그건 대개 장미 차지가 되지."], ["I began my life among them in my father’s nursery garden, and I shall end my life among them, if I can. Yes.", "나는 아버지의 묘목밭에서 장미 틈에 파묻혀 삶을 시작했고, 할 수만 있다면 장미 틈에서 삶을 끝맺을 작정이오."], ["One of these days (please God) I shall retire from catching thieves, and try my hand at growing roses.", "그렇소. 언젠가는 (하느님 뜻이라면) 도둑 잡는 일에서 물러나, 장미 기르는 일에 손을 대 볼 참이지."], ["There will be grass walks, Mr. Gardener, between my beds,”", "내 화단 사이에는 잔디 길을 낼 거요, 정원사 양반.\""], ["says the Sergeant, on whose mind the gravel paths of our rosery seemed to dwell unpleasantly.", "하고 부장은 말하는데, 우리 장미원의 자갈길이 그의 마음에 못내 거슬리게 남아 있는 모양이었다."]], [["“It seems an odd taste, sir,”", "\"그거 좀 별난 취향으로 보입니다, 나리.\""], ["I ventured to say, “for a man in your line of life.”", "하고 나는 조심스레 말을 꺼냈다. \"나리 같은 직업에 몸담은 분치고는 말입니다.\""]], [["“If you will look about you (which most people won’t do),”", "\"당신이 주위를 한번 둘러보기만 한다면 (하기야 사람들 대부분은 그러지 않지만),\""], ["says Sergeant Cuff, “you will see that the nature of a man’s tastes is, most times, as opposite as possible to the nature of a man’s business.", "하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"사람의 취향이라는 것이, 대개는, 그 사람의 생업과 될 수 있는 한 정반대라는 걸 알게 될 거요."], ["Show me any two things more opposite one from the other than a rose and a thief; and I’ll correct my tastes accordingly—if it isn’t too late at my time of life.", "장미와 도둑보다 서로 더 정반대되는 두 가지를 내게 대 보시오. 그러면 그에 맞춰 내 취향을 바로잡으리다—이 나이에 너무 늦은 게 아니라면 말이지."], ["You find the damask rose a goodish stock for most of the tender sorts, don’t you, Mr. Gardener?", "정원사 양반, 다마스크 장미가 저 여린 품종 대부분한테 제법 쓸 만한 대목(臺木)이라는 걸 아시지 않소?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I thought so.", "그럴 줄 알았소."], ["Here’s a lady coming.", "여기 한 부인이 오고 있구려."], ["Is it Lady Verinder?”", "저분이 버린더 마님이오?\""]], [["He had seen her before either I or the gardener had seen her, though we knew which way to look, and he didn’t.", "그는, 나나 정원사보다 먼저 마님을 알아보았다. 우리는 어느 쪽을 봐야 하는지 알고 있었고 그는 몰랐는데도 말이다."], ["I began to think him rather a quicker man than he appeared to be at first sight.", "나는 그가 첫눈에 보이는 것보다는 꽤나 눈치 빠른 사람이라는 생각이 들기 시작했다."]], [["The Sergeant’s appearance, or the Sergeant’s errand—one or both—seemed to cause my lady some little embarrassment.", "그 부장의 겉모습이었는지, 아니면 그의 용무였는지—그 하나 때문인지 둘 다 때문인지—마님은 다소 당혹스러워하시는 듯했다."], ["She was, for the first time in all my experience of her, at a loss what to say at an interview with a stranger.", "마님은, 내가 마님을 겪어 온 그 오랜 세월에 처음으로, 낯선 이와의 면담에서 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰라 어쩔 줄 몰라 하셨다."], ["Sergeant Cuff put her at her ease directly.", "커프 부장은 곧바로 마님의 마음을 편하게 해 드렸다."], ["He asked if any other person had been employed about the robbery before we sent for him; and hearing that another person had been called in, and was now in the house, begged leave to speak to him before anything else was done.", "그는 우리가 저를 부르기 전에 이 도난 사건에 다른 사람이 쓰인 적이 있는지를 물었다. 그리고 다른 사람이 불려 와 지금 이 집에 있다는 말을 듣고는, 다른 무슨 일에 착수하기 전에 먼저 그 사람과 이야기하게 해 달라고 청했다."]], [["My lady led the way back.", "마님이 앞장서 돌아가셨다."], ["Before he followed her, the Sergeant relieved his mind on the subject of the gravel walks by a parting word to the gardener.", "부장은 마님을 뒤따르기 전에, 정원사에게 작별의 말 한마디를 남겨 그 자갈길에 관한 제 마음속 응어리를 풀었다."], ["“Get her ladyship to try grass,”", "\"마님을 구슬려 잔디를 한번 써 보시게.\""], ["he said, with a sour look at the paths.", "하고 그는 그 길들을 못마땅하게 쏘아보며 말했다. \"자갈은 안 돼!"], ["“No gravel! no gravel!”", "자갈은 안 된다니까!\""]], [["Why Superintendent Seegrave should have appeared to be several sizes smaller than life, on being presented to Sergeant Cuff, I can’t undertake to explain.", "커프 부장에게 소개되었을 때, 어째서 시그레이브 경정이 제 실물보다 몇 치수는 작아 보였는지, 나로서는 나서서 설명할 재간이 없다."], ["I can only state the fact.", "그저 그 사실만을 말할 수 있을 뿐이다."], ["They retired together; and remained a weary long time shut up from all mortal intrusion. When they came out, Mr.", "두 사람은 함께 물러갔고, 사람이라면 누구도 끼어들 수 없게 문을 걸어 잠근 채 지겹도록 오랜 시간을 머물렀다."], ["Superintendent was excited, and Mr. Sergeant was yawning.", "그들이 나왔을 때, 경정 나리는 잔뜩 흥분해 있었고, 부장 나리는 하품을 하고 있었다."]], [["“The Sergeant wishes to see Miss Verinder’s sitting-room,”", "\"부장께서 버린더 아가씨의 거실을 보고 싶어 하시네.\""], ["says Mr. Seegrave, addressing me with great pomp and eagerness.", "하고 시그레이브 씨가 잔뜩 위엄을 부리며 열을 올려 내게 이른다."], ["“The Sergeant may have some questions to ask.", "\"부장께서 물으실 것이 좀 있으실 게야."], ["Attend the Sergeant, if you please!”", "부장을 잘 모시게, 알겠나!\""]], [["While I was being ordered about in this way, I looked at the great Cuff.", "이런 식으로 내가 이래라저래라 부림을 받는 동안, 나는 그 위대한 커프를 바라보았다."], ["The great Cuff, on his side, looked at Superintendent Seegrave in that quietly expecting way which I have already noticed.", "그 위대한 커프는, 저대로, 내가 앞서 눈여겨본 그 조용히 무언가를 기다리는 눈길로 시그레이브 경정을 바라보고 있었다."], ["I can’t affirm that he was on the watch for his brother officer’s speedy appearance in the character of an Ass—I can only say that I strongly suspected it.", "그가 제 동료 관리가 머잖아 얼간이 꼴을 드러내기를 노리고 있었다고 딱 잘라 말할 수는 없다—다만 나는 그러리라고 강하게 짐작했다고는 말할 수 있다."]], [["I led the way upstairs.", "나는 앞장서 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["The Sergeant went softly all over the Indian cabinet and all round the “boudoir;”", "부장은 그 인도식 캐비닛 구석구석과 \"규방\""], ["asking questions (occasionally only of Mr. Superintendent, and continually of me), the drift of which I believe to have been equally unintelligible to both of us.", "사방을 나긋나긋 훑어보며, (경정 나리께는 이따금씩만, 내게는 끊임없이) 이런저런 것을 물었는데, 그 물음의 갈피는 우리 둘 다에게 똑같이 알 수 없는 것이었다고 나는 생각한다."], ["In due time, his course brought him to the door, and put him face to face with the decorative painting that you know of.", "이윽고 그의 발길은 그 문께에 이르러, 여러분도 아시는 그 장식 그림과 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다."], ["He laid one lean inquiring finger on the small smear, just under the lock, which Superintendent Seegrave had already noticed, when he reproved the women-servants for all crowding together into the room.", "그는 여윈 손가락 하나를 캐묻듯이, 자물쇠 바로 아래의 그 작은 얼룩에 갖다 댔는데, 그것은 시그레이브 경정이 여자 하인들이 죄다 방 안으로 몰려든 것을 나무랄 때 이미 눈여겨보았던 바로 그 얼룩이었다."]], [["“That’s a pity,”", "\"그거 안됐군.\""], ["says Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말한다."], ["“How did it happen?”", "\"어쩌다 이리됐소?\""]], [["He put the question to me.", "그는 그 물음을 내게 던졌다."], ["I answered that the women-servants had crowded into the room on the previous morning, and that some of their petticoats had done the mischief, “Superintendent Seegrave ordered them out, sir,”", "나는, 여자 하인들이 전날 아침 그 방으로 몰려들었고, 그 가운데 몇몇의 속치마가 이런 낭패를 저질렀다고 대답했다. \"시그레이브 경정께서 그 여자들을 내보내셨습니다, 나리.\""], ["I added, “before they did any more harm.”", "하고 나는 덧붙였다. \"더 해코지를 하기 전에 말입니다.\""]], [["“Right!”", "\"옳거니!\""], ["says Mr. Superintendent in his military way.", "하고 경정 나리가 그 군인다운 투로 말한다."], ["“I ordered them out.", "\"내가 그들을 내보냈지."], ["The petticoats did it, Sergeant—the petticoats did it.”", "속치마들이 저지른 짓이오, 부장—속치마들이 저지른 짓이라오.\""]], [["“Did you notice which petticoat did it?”", "\"어느 속치마가 저지른 짓인지 눈여겨보셨소?\""], ["asked Sergeant Cuff, still addressing himself, not to his brother-officer, but to me.", "하고 커프 부장이 물었는데, 여전히 그 동료 관리가 아니라 내게 말을 건네고 있었다."]], [["“No, sir.”", "\"아닙니다, 나리.\""]], [["He turned to Superintendent Seegrave upon that, and said, “You noticed, I suppose?”", "그러자 그는 시그레이브 경정께로 돌아서서 말했다. \"경정께서는 눈여겨보셨겠지요?\""]], [["Mr. Superintendent looked a little taken aback; but he made the best of it.", "경정 나리는 좀 당황한 낯빛이었으나, 애써 태연한 척했다."], ["“I can’t charge my memory, Sergeant,”", "\"내 기억을 다 짜낼 수야 없지 않소, 부장.\""], ["he said, “a mere trifle—a mere trifle.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그깟 하찮은 일—그저 하찮은 일이니 말이오.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff looked at Mr. Seegrave, as he had looked at the gravel walks in the rosery, and gave us, in his melancholy way, the first taste of his quality which we had had yet.", "커프 부장은, 장미원의 자갈길을 바라보았을 때처럼 시그레이브 씨를 바라보았고, 그 우울한 투로, 우리가 여태 맛본 적 없던 그의 진면목을 처음으로 우리에게 슬쩍 보여 주었다."]], [["“I made a private inquiry last week, Mr.", "\"지난주에 나는 은밀히 한 사건을 조사했소, 경정 나리.\""], ["Superintendent,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “At one end of the inquiry there was a murder, and at the other end there was a spot of ink on a table cloth that nobody could account for.", "\"그 사건의 한쪽 끝에는 살인이 있었고, 다른 쪽 끝에는 아무도 그 까닭을 대지 못하는, 식탁보에 묻은 잉크 한 방울이 있었지."], ["In all my experience along the dirtiest ways of this dirty little world, I have never met with such a thing as a trifle yet.", "이 더러운 조그만 세상의 더없이 지저분한 길바닥을 헤매고 다닌 내 온 경험을 통틀어, 나는 여태 하찮은 일 따위란 것과 마주친 적이 한 번도 없소."], ["Before we go a step further in this business we must see the petticoat that made the smear, and we must know for certain when that paint was wet.”", "이 일에서 한 걸음이라도 더 나아가기 전에, 우리는 그 얼룩을 낸 속치마를 봐야 하고, 그 페인트가 언제 젖어 있었는지를 확실히 알아야 하오.\""]], [["Mr. Superintendent—taking his set-down rather sulkily—asked if he should summon the women.", "경정 나리는—면박당한 것을 다소 뾰로통하게 받아들이며—그 여자들을 불러올까요, 하고 물었다."], ["Sergeant Cuff, after considering a minute, sighed, and shook his head.", "커프 부장은 잠시 생각에 잠긴 뒤, 한숨을 쉬고는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“No,” he said, “we’ll take the matter of the paint first.", "\"아니오.\""], ["It’s a question of Yes or No with the paint—which is short.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"페인트 문제를 먼저 다룹시다."], ["It’s a question of petticoats with the women—which is long.", "페인트로 말하면 예냐 아니냐의 문제라—간단하지."], ["What o’clock was it when the servants were in this room yesterday morning?", "여자들로 말하면 속치마의 문제라—길고 말이오. 어제 아침 하인들이 이 방에 있었던 게 몇 시였소?"], ["Eleven o’clock—eh?", "열한 시—응?"], ["Is there anybody in the house who knows whether that paint was wet or dry, at eleven yesterday morning?”", "어제 아침 열한 시에 그 페인트가 젖어 있었는지 말라 있었는지 아는 사람이 이 집에 누구 없소?\""]], [["“Her ladyship’s nephew, Mr. Franklin Blake, knows,”", "\"마님의 조카 되시는 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이 아십니다.\""], ["I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."]], [["“Is the gentleman in the house?”", "\"그 신사분이 이 집에 계시오?\""]], [["Mr. Franklin was as close at hand as could be—waiting for his first chance of being introduced to the great Cuff.", "프랭클린 도련님은 더할 나위 없이 가까이 있었다—그 위대한 커프에게 소개될 첫 기회를 기다리면서 말이다."], ["In half a minute he was in the room, and was giving his evidence as follows:", "반 분도 안 되어 그는 방 안에 들어와, 다음과 같이 제 증언을 하고 있었다."]], [["“That door, Sergeant,”", "\"저 문은요, 부장님.\""], ["he said, “has been painted by Miss Verinder, under my inspection, with my help, and in a vehicle of my own composition.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"버린더 아가씨가, 제 감독 아래, 제 도움을 받아, 제가 손수 조제한 용매로 칠한 것입니다."], ["The vehicle dries whatever colours may be used with it, in twelve hours.”", "그 용매는 그것과 함께 쓴 물감이 어떤 것이든 열두 시간이면 말립니다.\""]], [["“Do you remember when the smeared bit was done, sir?”", "\"그 얼룩진 부분을 언제 칠하셨는지 기억하시오, 나리?\""], ["asked the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 물었다."]], [["“Perfectly,”", "\"똑똑히 기억합니다.\""], ["answered Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다."], ["“That was the last morsel of the door to be finished.", "\"거기가 그 문에서 맨 마지막으로 마무리한 자리였지요."], ["We wanted to get it done, on Wednesday last—and I myself completed it by three in the afternoon, or soon after.”", "우리는 지난 수요일에 그걸 끝내고 싶었고—제가 손수 오후 세 시 무렵, 아니 그 조금 뒤에 마무리했습니다.\""]], [["“Today is Friday,”", "\"오늘이 금요일이오.\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, addressing himself to Superintendent Seegrave.", "하고 커프 부장이 시그레이브 경정께 말을 건네며 말했다."], ["“Let us reckon back, sir.", "\"거슬러 셈해 봅시다, 나리."], ["At three on the Wednesday afternoon, that bit of the painting was completed.", "수요일 오후 세 시에, 그 그림의 그 부분이 마무리되었소."], ["The vehicle dried it in twelve hours—that is to say, dried it by three o’clock on Thursday morning.", "용매가 열두 시간이면 그걸 말렸으니—다시 말해, 목요일 새벽 세 시면 말랐다는 얘기지."], ["At eleven on Thursday morning you held your inquiry here.", "목요일 아침 열한 시에 경정께서는 여기서 조사를 벌였고."], ["Take three from eleven, and eight remains.", "열한에서 셋을 빼면, 여덟이 남소."], ["That paint had been eight hours dry, Mr. Superintendent, when you supposed that the women-servants’ petticoats smeared it.”", "그 페인트는 이미 여덟 시간을 마른 상태였던 거요, 경정 나리, 여자 하인들의 속치마가 그걸 문질렀다고 짐작하셨을 그때 말이오.\""]], [["First knock-down blow for Mr. Seegrave!", "시그레이브 씨가 얻어맞은 첫 번째 결정타였다!"], ["If he had not suspected poor Penelope, I should have pitied him.", "그가 그 딱한 페넬로피를 의심하지만 않았던들, 나는 그를 가엾이 여겼으련만."]], [["Having settled the question of the paint, Sergeant Cuff, from that moment, gave his brother-officer up as a bad job—and addressed himself to Mr. Franklin, as the more promising assistant of the two.", "페인트 문제를 매듭짓고 나자, 커프 부장은 그 순간부터, 제 동료 관리를 가망 없는 자로 아주 단념해 버리고—둘 중 더 미더운 조력자인 프랭클린 도련님께로 말을 건넸다."]], [["“It’s quite on the cards, sir,”", "\"충분히 있을 법한 일입니다, 나리.\""], ["he said, “that you have put the clue into our hands.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"도련님이 그 실마리를 우리 손에 쥐여 주셨다는 것이 말입니다.\""]], [["As the words passed his lips, the bedroom door opened, and Miss Rachel came out among us suddenly.", "그 말이 그의 입에서 떨어지기가 무섭게, 침실 문이 열리고, 레이철 아가씨가 불쑥 우리 가운데로 나왔다."]], [["She addressed herself to the Sergeant, without appearing to notice (or to heed) that he was a perfect stranger to her.", "아가씨는, 그가 저와는 생판 낯선 사람이라는 것을 알아채는 (혹은 개의하는) 기색도 없이, 부장에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Did you say,”", "\"방금 말씀하셨나요,\""], ["she asked, pointing to Mr.", "하고 아가씨가 프랭클린 도련님을 가리키며 물었다."], ["Franklin, “that he had put the clue into your hands?”", "\"저 사람이 그 실마리를 당신 손에 쥐여 주었다고요?\""]], [["(“This is Miss Verinder,”", "(\"이분이 버린더 아가씨입니다.\""], ["I whispered, behind the Sergeant.)", "하고 나는 부장의 등 뒤에서 속삭였다.)"]], [["“That gentleman, miss,”", "\"저 신사분이 말이지요, 아가씨,\""], ["says the Sergeant—with his steely-grey eyes carefully studying my young lady’s face—“has possibly put the clue into our hands.”", "하고 부장이—그 강철빛 잿빛 눈으로 우리 아가씨의 얼굴을 찬찬히 살피며—말한다. \"어쩌면 그 실마리를 우리 손에 쥐여 주셨을지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["She turned for one moment, and tried to look at Mr.", "아가씨는 한순간 돌아서서, 프랭클린 도련님을 바라보려 했다."], ["Franklin. I say, tried, for she suddenly looked away again before their eyes met.", "바라보려 했다고 말하는 것은, 두 사람의 눈길이 마주치기 전에 아가씨가 별안간 다시 시선을 돌려 버렸기 때문이다."], ["There seemed to be some strange disturbance in her mind.", "아가씨의 마음속에 무언가 야릇한 동요가 이는 듯했다."], ["She coloured up, and then she turned pale again.", "아가씨는 얼굴이 붉어졌다가, 이내 다시 파리해졌다."], ["With the paleness, there came a new look into her face—a look which it startled me to see.", "그 파리함과 함께, 아가씨의 얼굴에 새로운 기색이 떠올랐다—보고 있자니 나를 흠칫하게 하는 그런 기색이었다."]], [["“Having answered your question, miss,”", "\"아가씨의 물음에 답해 드렸으니, 아가씨,\""], ["says the Sergeant, “I beg leave to make an inquiry in my turn.", "하고 부장이 말한다. \"이번에는 제가 여쭙게 해 주시지요."], ["There is a smear on the painting of your door, here.", "여기, 아가씨 문의 그림에 얼룩이 하나 있습니다."], ["Do you happen to know when it was done?", "혹 언제 그리되었는지 아십니까?"], ["or who did it?”", "아니면 누가 그랬는지요?\""]], [["Instead of making any reply, Miss Rachel went on with her questions, as if he had not spoken, or as if she had not heard him.", "아무런 대답도 하지 않고, 레이철 아가씨는, 그가 말을 하지 않았다는 듯, 아니면 아가씨가 그 말을 듣지 못했다는 듯, 제 물음을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Are you another police-officer?”", "\"당신도 또 한 사람의 경찰관인가요?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 아가씨가 물었다."]], [["“I am Sergeant Cuff, miss, of the Detective Police.”", "\"저는 커프 부장입니다, 아가씨, 형사과 소속이지요.\""]], [["“Do you think a young lady’s advice worth having?”", "\"젊은 처녀의 충고도 새겨들을 만하다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["“I shall be glad to hear it, miss.”", "\"기꺼이 듣겠습니다, 아가씨.\""]], [["“Do your duty by yourself—and don’t allow Mr. Franklin Blake to help you!”", "\"당신 혼자 힘으로 당신 할 일을 하세요—그리고 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 당신을 돕게 놔두지 마세요!\""]], [["She said those words so spitefully, so savagely, with such an extraordinary outbreak of ill-will towards Mr. Franklin, in her voice and in her look, that—though I had known her from a baby, though I loved and honoured her next to my lady herself—I was ashamed of Miss Rachel for the first time in my life.", "아가씨는 그 말을 어찌나 앙심 서리게, 어찌나 사납게, 그 목소리와 그 눈빛에 프랭클린 도련님을 향한 그토록 유별난 적의를 터뜨리며 내뱉던지—비록 내가 아가씨를 갓난아기 적부터 알아 왔고, 비록 내가 마님 바로 다음으로 아가씨를 아끼고 우러러 왔건만—나는 난생처음으로 레이철 아가씨가 부끄러웠다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff’s immovable eyes never stirred from off her face.", "커프 부장의 꿈쩍 않는 눈은 아가씨의 얼굴에서 조금도 떨어지지 않았다."], ["“Thank you, miss,” he said.", "\"고맙습니다, 아가씨.\""], ["“Do you happen to know anything about the smear?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"혹 그 얼룩에 대해 뭔가 아시는 게 있습니까?"], ["Might you have done it by accident yourself?”", "아가씨 자신이 실수로 그리한 건 아니고요?\""]], [["“I know nothing about the smear.”", "\"저는 그 얼룩에 대해 아무것도 몰라요.\""]], [["With that answer, she turned away, and shut herself up again in her bedroom.", "그 대답을 남기고, 아가씨는 돌아서서, 다시 제 침실에 틀어박혔다."], ["This time, I heard her—as Penelope had heard her before—burst out crying as soon as she was alone again.", "이번에는 나도 들었다—앞서 페넬로피가 들었던 것처럼—아가씨가 다시 혼자가 되자마자 왈칵 울음을 터뜨리는 소리를."]], [["I couldn’t bring myself to look at the Sergeant—I looked at Mr. Franklin, who stood nearest to me.", "나는 차마 부장을 바라볼 수가 없었다—나는 내게서 가장 가까이 서 있던 프랭클린 도련님을 바라보았다."], ["He seemed to be even more sorely distressed at what had passed than I was.", "그는 방금 일어난 일에 나보다도 더 애타게 괴로워하는 듯 보였다."]], [["“I told you I was uneasy about her,”", "\"내가 아가씨 일로 마음이 편치 않다고 자네한테 말했잖나.\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“And now you see why.”", "\"이제 그 까닭을 알겠지.\""]], [["“Miss Verinder appears to be a little out of temper about the loss of her Diamond,”", "\"버린더 아가씨는 다이아몬드를 잃은 일로 심기가 좀 상하신 모양이군요.\""], ["remarked the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“It’s a valuable jewel.", "\"값진 보석이니 말입니다."], ["Natural enough!", "그럴 만도 하지요!"], ["natural enough!”", "그럴 만도 하고말고요!\""]], [["Here was the excuse that I had made for her (when she forgot herself before Superintendent Seegrave, on the previous day) being made for her over again, by a man who couldn’t have had my interest in making it—for he was a perfect stranger!", "여기, 내가 (전날 아가씨가 시그레이브 경정 앞에서 자제심을 잃었을 때) 아가씨를 위해 둘러댔던 바로 그 변명이, 나 같은 사심을 품었을 리 없는 사람의 입에서—그도 그럴 것이 그는 생판 낯선 이였으니!—다시 한번 아가씨를 위해 둘러대지고 있었다."], ["A kind of cold shudder ran through me, which I couldn’t account for at the time.", "일종의 차가운 전율이 나를 훑고 지나갔는데, 그 까닭을 그때는 알 수 없었다."], ["I know, now, that I must have got my first suspicion, at that moment, of a new light (and horrid light) having suddenly fallen on the case, in the mind of Sergeant Cuff—purely and entirely in consequence of what he had seen in Miss Rachel, and heard from Miss Rachel, at that first interview between them.", "이제 와서 나는 안다, 바로 그 순간 나는 처음으로 낌새를 챘음이 틀림없다는 것을—커프 부장의 마음속에 이 사건을 비추는 어떤 새로운 (그리고 소름 끼치는) 빛이 별안간 드리웠다는 낌새를 말이다. 그것은 오로지, 그리고 온전히, 그가 그 첫 면담에서 레이철 아가씨에게서 본 것과 레이철 아가씨에게서 들은 것의 결과였다."]], [["“A young lady’s tongue is a privileged member, sir,”", "\"젊은 처녀의 혀란 것은 특권을 누리는 지체이지요, 나리.\""], ["says the Sergeant to Mr. Franklin.", "하고 부장이 프랭클린 도련님께 말한다."], ["“Let us forget what has passed, and go straight on with this business.", "\"방금 일은 잊어버리고, 이 일을 곧장 밀고 나갑시다."], ["Thanks to you, we know when the paint was dry.", "도련님 덕분에, 우리는 페인트가 언제 말랐는지를 알지요."], ["The next thing to discover is when the paint was last seen without that smear.", "다음으로 알아내야 할 것은, 그 얼룩이 없는 페인트를 마지막으로 본 것이 언제냐 하는 겁니다."], ["You have got a head on your shoulders—and you understand what I mean.”", "도련님은 어깨 위에 제대로 된 머리를 이고 계시니—제 말뜻을 아실 겁니다.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin composed himself, and came back with an effort from Miss Rachel to the matter in hand.", "프랭클린 도련님은 마음을 가다듬고, 애를 써서 레이철 아가씨 생각에서 벗어나 지금 눈앞의 일로 돌아왔다."]], [["“I think I do understand,” he said.", "\"알 것 같습니다.\""], ["“The more we narrow the question of time, the more we also narrow the field of inquiry.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"시간의 문제를 좁히면 좁힐수록, 조사의 범위도 그만큼 좁혀지는 거로군요.\""]], [["“That’s it, sir,”", "\"바로 그겁니다, 나리.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“Did you notice your work here, on the Wednesday afternoon, after you had done it?”", "\"수요일 오후에, 이 일을 끝낸 뒤에, 여기 이 손질한 자리를 눈여겨보셨습니까?\""]], [["Mr.", "프랭클린 도련님은 고개를 저으며 대답했다."], ["Franklin shook his head, and answered, “I can’t say I did.”", "\"봤다고는 할 수 없군요.\""]], [["“Did you?”", "\"자네는 봤나?\""], ["inquired Sergeant Cuff, turning to me.", "하고 커프 부장이 내게 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["“I can’t say I did either, sir.”", "\"저 역시 봤다고는 할 수 없습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“Who was the last person in the room, the last thing on Wednesday night?”", "\"수요일 밤, 맨 마지막으로 그 방에 있었던 사람은 누구요?\""]], [["“Miss Rachel, I suppose, sir.”", "\"레이철 아가씨일 겁니다, 나리.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin struck in there, “Or possibly your daughter, Betteredge.”", "거기서 프랭클린 도련님이 끼어들었다."], ["He turned to Sergeant Cuff, and explained that my daughter was Miss Verinder’s maid.", "\"아니면 어쩌면 자네 딸일지도 모르지, 베터리지.\" 그는 커프 부장께로 돌아서서, 내 딸이 버린더 아가씨의 몸종이라고 설명했다."]], [["“Mr. Betteredge, ask your daughter to step up.", "\"베터리지 양반, 따님더러 올라오라 하시오."], ["Stop!”", "잠깐!\""], ["says the Sergeant, taking me away to the window, out of earshot.", "하고 부장이, 남의 귀에 들리지 않게 나를 창가로 데려가며 말한다."], ["“Your Superintendent here,”", "\"여기 이 댁 경정께서는,\""], ["he went on, in a whisper, “has made a pretty full report to me of the manner in which he has managed this case.", "하고 그가 속삭임으로 말을 이었다. \"이 사건을 어떻게 다뤄 왔는지를 내게 제법 소상히 보고했소."], ["Among other things, he has, by his own confession, set the servants’ backs up.", "그중에서도, 그는 제 입으로 실토한 대로, 하인들의 심기를 잔뜩 건드려 놨더군."], ["It’s very important to smooth them down again.", "그들을 다시 다독여 놓는 것이 아주 중요하오."], ["Tell your daughter, and tell the rest of them, these two things, with my compliments: First, that I have no evidence before me, yet, that the Diamond has been stolen; I only know that the Diamond has been lost.", "따님한테, 그리고 나머지 사람들한테도, 내 인사와 함께 이 두 가지를 전하시오. 첫째, 나는 아직 내 앞에 다이아몬드가 도둑맞았다는 증거가 하나도 없다는 것."], ["Second, that my business here with the servants is simply to ask them to lay their heads together and help me to find it.”", "나는 다만 다이아몬드가 없어졌다는 것만 알 뿐이오. 둘째, 여기서 하인들을 상대로 하는 내 볼일이란, 그저 그들더러 다 같이 머리를 맞대어 그것을 찾도록 나를 도와 달라고 청하는 것뿐이라는 것.\""]], [["My experience of the women-servants, when Superintendent Seegrave laid his embargo on their rooms, came in handy here.", "시그레이브 경정이 여자 하인들의 방에 출입 금지령을 내렸을 적에 겪은 그 경험이, 여기서 요긴하게 쓰였다."]], [["“May I make so bold, Sergeant, as to tell the women a third thing?” I asked.", "\"부장님, 제가 외람되나마 그 여자들한테 세 번째 것을 하나 더 일러도 될까요?\""], ["“Are they free (with your compliments) to fidget up and downstairs, and whisk in and out of their bedrooms, if the fit takes them?”", "하고 내가 물었다. \"그들이 (부장님 인사와 함께) 마음 내키는 대로 위아래층을 들락날락 부산을 떨고, 제 침실을 휙휙 드나들어도 좋다고 말입니다.\""]], [["“Perfectly free,”", "\"얼마든지 마음대로.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."]], [["“That will smooth them down, sir,”", "\"그 말이면 그들을 다독여 놓을 겁니다, 나리.\""], ["I remarked, “from the cook to the scullion.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"요리사에서 부엌데기에 이르기까지 말입니다.\""]], [["“Go, and do it at once, Mr. Betteredge.”", "\"가서, 당장 그리하시오, 베터리지 양반.\""]], [["I did it in less than five minutes.", "나는 그 일을 오 분도 안 되어 해치웠다."], ["There was only one difficulty when I came to the bit about the bedrooms.", "침실 대목에 이르렀을 때 딱 한 가지 어려움이 있었다."], ["It took a pretty stiff exertion of my authority, as chief, to prevent the whole of the female household from following me and Penelope upstairs, in the character of volunteer witnesses in a burning fever of anxiety to help Sergeant Cuff.", "여자 식솔 전부가, 커프 부장을 돕겠다는 안달로 애가 타서, 자원 증인 노릇을 하겠다며 나와 페넬로피를 뒤따라 위층으로 올라오려는 것을 막느라, 우두머리로서 내 권위를 제법 뻣뻣하게 부려야만 했다."]], [["The Sergeant seemed to approve of Penelope.", "부장은 페넬로피가 마음에 드는 눈치였다."], ["He became a trifle less dreary; and he looked much as he had looked when he noticed the white musk rose in the flower-garden.", "그는 조금은 덜 음침해졌고, 화단에서 흰 사향장미를 눈여겨보았을 때와 사뭇 비슷한 낯빛이 되었다. 여기, 부장이 내 딸에게서 이끌어 낸 그 증언이 있다."], ["Here is my daughter’s evidence, as drawn off from her by the Sergeant.", "딸아이는, 내 생각에, 아주 어여쁘게 그것을 진술했다—허나, 저런!"], ["She gave it, I think, very prettily—but, there!", "그 아이는 어디로 보나 영락없는 내 자식이다."], ["she is my child all over: nothing of her mother in her; Lord bless you, nothing of her mother in her!", "제 어미를 닮은 구석이라곤 하나도 없으니. 아이고 맙소사, 제 어미를 닮은 데라곤 눈곱만큼도 없다니까!"]], [["Penelope examined: Took a lively interest in the painting on the door, having helped to mix the colours. Noticed the bit of work under the lock, because it was the last bit done. Had seen it, some hours afterwards, without a smear. Had left it, as late as twelve at night, without a smear. Had, at that hour, wished her young lady good-night in the bedroom; had heard the clock strike in the “boudoir”; had her hand at the time on the handle of the painted door; knew the paint was wet (having helped to mix the colours, as aforesaid); took particular pains not to touch it; could swear that she held up the skirts of her dress, and that there was no smear on the paint then; could not swear that her dress mightn’t have touched it accidentally in going out; remembered the dress she had on, because it was new, a present from Miss Rachel; her father remembered, and could speak to it, too; could, and would, and did fetch it; dress recognised by her father as the dress she wore that night; skirts examined, a long job from the size of them; not the ghost of a paint-stain discovered anywhere. End of Penelope’s evidence—and very pretty and convincing, too. Signed, Gabriel Betteredge.", "페넬로피 신문: 물감 섞는 일을 거들었던 터라, 문의 그 그림에 생생한 관심을 두었음. 자물쇠 아래의 그 손질한 자리를 눈여겨보았으니, 그것이 맨 마지막으로 칠한 자리였기 때문임. 그로부터 몇 시간 뒤에, 얼룩 없는 그것을 보았음. 밤 열두 시나 되어서까지도, 얼룩 없는 그대로 두고 나왔음. 그 시각에, 침실에서 제 아가씨께 잘 자라는 인사를 드렸음. \"규방\"에서 시계가 치는 소리를 들었음. 그때 제 손을 그 칠한 문의 손잡이에 얹고 있었음. 그 페인트가 젖어 있음을 알고 있었음 (앞서 말한 대로 물감 섞는 일을 거들었으므로). 그것에 닿지 않으려고 각별히 조심했음. 제 드레스 자락을 치켜들고 있었으며 그때 페인트에 아무 얼룩도 없었음을 맹세할 수 있음. 나가는 길에 제 드레스가 어쩌다 그것에 닿지 않았노라고는 맹세할 수 없음. 제가 입고 있던 드레스를 기억하니, 그것이 새것이요 레이철 아가씨께 받은 선물이었기 때문임. 제 아비도 그것을 기억하며, 그에 대해 증언할 수 있음. 그것을 가져올 수 있고, 가져오려 했고, 실제로 가져왔음. 제 아비가 그것을 그날 밤 딸아이가 입었던 드레스로 알아봄. 그 자락을 살폈으니, 크기가 큰 탓에 오래 걸리는 일이었음. 그 어디에서도 페인트 얼룩의 그림자조차 발견되지 않음. 페넬로피의 증언 끝—그리고 아주 어여쁘고, 게다가 아주 설득력 있는 것이었음. 서명, 게이브리얼 베터리지."]], [["The Sergeant’s next proceeding was to question me about any large dogs in the house who might have got into the room, and done the mischief with a whisk of their tails.", "부장이 다음으로 한 일은, 혹 그 방에 들어가 꼬리를 휙 쳐서 이런 낭패를 저질렀을 법한 큰 개가 이 집에 있는지를 내게 캐묻는 것이었다."], ["Hearing that this was impossible, he next sent for a magnifying-glass, and tried how the smear looked, seen that way.", "그런 일은 있을 수 없다는 말을 듣고, 그는 다음으로 확대경을 가져오라 하여, 그것으로 들여다보면 그 얼룩이 어찌 보이는지를 살폈다."], ["No skin-mark (as of a human hand) printed off on the paint.", "(사람 손 같은) 살갗 자국은 페인트에 찍혀 있지 않았다."], ["All the signs visible—signs which told that the paint had been smeared by some loose article of somebody’s dress touching it in going by.", "눈에 보이는 온갖 흔적들은—누군가의 드레스에 달린 어떤 헐거운 것이 지나가다 스치는 바람에 페인트가 문질러졌음을 말해 주는 흔적들이었다."], ["That somebody (putting together Penelope’s evidence and Mr. Franklin’s evidence) must have been in the room, and done the mischief, between midnight and three o’clock on the Thursday morning.", "그 누군가는 (페넬로피의 증언과 프랭클린 도련님의 증언을 맞추어 보건대) 목요일 새벽 자정과 세 시 사이에 그 방에 있었고, 이 낭패를 저지른 것이 틀림없었다."]], [["Having brought his investigation to this point, Sergeant Cuff discovered that such a person as Superintendent Seegrave was still left in the room, upon which he summed up the proceedings for his brother-officer’s benefit, as follows:", "조사를 이 지점까지 끌고 온 뒤에야, 커프 부장은 시그레이브 경정이라는 위인이 아직 방 안에 남아 있음을 알아챘고, 그리하여 그 동료 관리를 위해 지금까지의 경과를 다음과 같이 간추려 주었다."]], [["“This trifle of yours, Mr. Superintendent,”", "\"경정 나리의 이 하찮은 일이 말입니다,\""], ["says the Sergeant, pointing to the place on the door, “has grown a little in importance since you noticed it last.", "하고 부장이 문의 그 자리를 가리키며 말한다. \"지난번 나리가 눈여겨본 이래로 그 중요함이 조금 자라났습니다."], ["At the present stage of the inquiry there are, as I take it, three discoveries to make, starting from that smear.", "지금 이 조사 단계에서는, 내가 보기에, 그 얼룩에서 출발하여 밝혀내야 할 것이 세 가지 있소."], ["Find out (first) whether there is any article of dress in this house with the smear of the paint on it.", "(첫째) 이 집에 페인트 얼룩이 묻은 옷가지가 있는지를 알아낼 것."], ["Find out (second) who that dress belongs to.", "(둘째) 그 옷이 누구 것인지를 알아낼 것."], ["Find out (third) how the person can account for having been in this room, and smeared the paint, between midnight and three in the morning.", "(셋째) 그 사람이 자정과 새벽 세 시 사이에 이 방에 있으면서 페인트를 문지른 일을 어떻게 해명하는지를 알아낼 것."], ["If the person can’t satisfy you, you haven’t far to look for the hand that has got the Diamond.", "만일 그 사람이 나리를 흡족하게 대지 못한다면, 다이아몬드를 손에 넣은 그 손을 찾느라 멀리 헤맬 것 없소."], ["I’ll work this by myself, if you please, and detain you no longer from your regular business in the town.", "괜찮으시다면 이 일은 나 혼자 해 나가리다. 그러니 나리를 시내에서의 본연의 업무에서 더는 붙들어 두지 않겠소."], ["You have got one of your men here, I see.", "보아하니 나리의 부하 하나가 여기 있구려."], ["Leave him here at my disposal, in case I want him—and allow me to wish you good morning.”", "혹 내가 그가 필요할 때를 대비해, 그를 여기 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 남겨 두시오—그럼 나리께 좋은 아침 인사를 드리게 해 주시오.\""]], [["Superintendent Seegrave’s respect for the Sergeant was great; but his respect for himself was greater still.", "시그레이브 경정이 그 부장에게 품은 존경은 컸다. 그러나 저 자신에게 품은 존경은 그보다도 더 컸다."], ["Hit hard by the celebrated Cuff, he hit back smartly, to the best of his ability, on leaving the room.", "그 이름난 커프에게 호되게 얻어맞은 그는, 방을 나서면서, 제 딴에 있는 재간을 다해 야무지게 되받아쳤다."]], [["“I have abstained from expressing any opinion, so far,”", "\"나는 여태 아무런 의견도 내지 않고 삼가 왔소.\""], ["says Mr. Superintendent, with his military voice still in good working order.", "하고 경정 나리가, 그 군인다운 목소리를 여전히 멀쩡히 굴리며 말한다."], ["“I have now only one remark to offer on leaving this case in your hands.", "\"이제 이 사건을 당신 손에 넘기며 딱 한마디만 하겠소."], ["There is such a thing, Sergeant, as making a mountain out of a molehill.", "부장, 두더지 흙두둑을 가지고 산을 만드는 일이란 게 세상엔 있는 법이오."], ["Good morning.”", "좋은 아침이오.\""]], [["“There is also such a thing as making nothing out of a molehill, in consequence of your head being too high to see it.”", "\"제 머리가 너무 높이 있어 그것이 눈에 뵈지 않는 통에, 두더지 흙두둑을 가지고 아무것도 만들어 내지 못하는 일이란 것도 세상엔 있는 법이지요.\""], ["Having returned his brother-officer’s compliments in those terms, Sergeant Cuff wheeled about, and walked away to the window by himself.", "그런 말로 제 동료 관리의 인사를 되돌려 준 뒤, 커프 부장은 홱 돌아서서, 혼자 창가로 걸어갔다."]], [["Mr. Franklin and I waited to see what was coming next.", "프랭클린 도련님과 나는 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질지 기다렸다."], ["The Sergeant stood at the window with his hands in his pockets, looking out, and whistling the tune of “The Last Rose of Summer”", "부장은 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고 창가에 서서, 밖을 내다보며, \"여름날의 마지막 장미\" 가락을 혼자 나직이 휘파람으로 불고 있었다."], ["softly to himself. Later in the proceedings, I discovered that he only forgot his manners so far as to whistle, when his mind was hard at work, seeing its way inch by inch to its own private ends, on which occasions “The Last Rose of Summer”", "뒷날 일이 진행되는 가운데 나는 알게 되었는데, 그가 예의를 잊고 휘파람을 부는 것은 오직 그의 머리가 저만의 은밀한 목적을 향해 한 치 한 치 길을 더듬으며 골똘히 돌아갈 때뿐이었고, 그럴 때면 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"가 그를 거드는 것이 분명했다."], ["evidently helped and encouraged him. I suppose it fitted in somehow with his character.", "짐작건대 그것이 어떻게든 그의 성미에 들어맞았던 모양이다."], ["It reminded him, you see, of his favourite roses, and, as he whistled it, it was the most melancholy tune going.", "아시다시피 그것은 그에게 가장 아끼는 장미를 떠올리게 했고, 게다가 그가 그것을 휘파람으로 불면, 그것은 세상에 다시없이 우울한 가락이 되었다."]], [["Turning from the window, after a minute or two, the Sergeant walked into the middle of the room, and stopped there, deep in thought, with his eyes on Miss Rachel’s bedroom door.", "일이 분쯤 뒤, 부장은 창가에서 돌아서서, 방 한복판으로 걸어 들어가 거기 멈춰 서더니, 레이철 아가씨의 침실 문에 눈을 둔 채 깊은 생각에 잠겼다."], ["After a little he roused himself, nodded his head, as much as to say, “That will do,” and, addressing me, asked for ten minutes’ conversation with my mistress, at her ladyship’s earliest convenience.", "조금 있다가 그는 정신을 차리고, \"그만하면 됐다\"는 듯 고개를 끄덕이고는, 내게 말을 건네어, 마님이 형편 닿는 대로 되도록 빨리 십 분간 이야기를 나눌 수 있게 해 달라고 청했다."]], [["Leaving the room with this message, I heard Mr. Franklin ask the Sergeant a question, and stopped to hear the answer also at the threshold of the door.", "이 전갈을 안고 방을 나서던 나는, 프랭클린 도련님이 부장에게 무언가 묻는 소리를 듣고, 나 역시 그 대답을 들으려고 문지방께에 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“Can you guess yet,”", "\"이제 짐작이 가시오,\""], ["inquired Mr.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 물었다."], ["Franklin, “who has stolen the Diamond?”", "\"누가 그 다이아몬드를 훔쳤는지 말이오?\""]], [["“Nobody has stolen the Diamond,”", "\"아무도 그 다이아몬드를 훔치지 않았습니다.\""], ["answered Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 대답했다."]], [["We both started at that extraordinary view of the case, and both earnestly begged him to tell us what he meant.", "우리 둘 다 그 사건에 대한 그 놀라운 견해에 흠칫했고, 둘 다 그게 무슨 뜻인지 말해 달라고 간곡히 청했다."]], [["“Wait a little,”", "\"좀 기다려 보시오.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“The pieces of the puzzle are not all put together yet.”", "\"그 수수께끼의 조각들이 아직 다 맞춰지지 않았으니 말이오.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XIII", "# CHAPTER XIII"]], [["I found my lady in her own sitting room.", "나는 마님을 마님의 거실에서 뵈었다."], ["She started and looked annoyed when I mentioned that Sergeant Cuff wished to speak to her.", "커프 부장이 마님과 이야기하고 싶어 한다고 아뢰자, 마님은 흠칫하시며 언짢은 낯빛이 되셨다."]], [["“Must I see him?”", "\"내가 꼭 그 사람을 만나야 하느냐?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 마님이 물으셨다."], ["“Can’t you represent me, Gabriel?”", "\"네가 나를 대신할 수는 없겠니, 게이브리얼?\""]], [["I felt at a loss to understand this, and showed it plainly, I suppose, in my face.", "나는 이 말을 어찌 헤아려야 할지 몰라 난감했고, 짐작건대 그 난감함이 내 얼굴에 고스란히 드러났던 모양이다."], ["My lady was so good as to explain herself.", "마님은 고맙게도 당신 마음을 풀어 설명해 주셨다."]], [["“I am afraid my nerves are a little shaken,” she said.", "\"아무래도 내 신경이 좀 곤두선 것 같구나.\""], ["“There is something in that police-officer from London which I recoil from—I don’t know why.", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"저 런던에서 온 경찰관에게는 어쩐지 내가 움츠러들게 하는 무언가가 있어—왜인지는 나도 모르겠다."], ["I have a presentiment that he is bringing trouble and misery with him into the house.", "그가 이 집에 근심과 불행을 안고 들어오고 있다는 예감이 드는구나."], ["Very foolish, and very unlike me—but so it is.”", "참으로 어리석고, 나답지 않은 노릇이다만—그래도 그런 것을 어쩌겠느냐.\""]], [["I hardly knew what to say to this.", "나는 이 말에 무어라 대답해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["The more I saw of Sergeant Cuff, the better I liked him.", "커프 부장을 볼수록, 나는 그가 더욱 마음에 들었다."], ["My lady rallied a little after having opened her heart to me—being, naturally, a woman of a high courage, as I have already told you.", "마님은 내게 당신 속내를 털어놓고 나자 조금 기운을 차리셨다—앞서 여러분께 말씀드린 대로, 본디 담대한 분이시니 말이다."]], [["“If I must see him, I must,” she said.", "\"만나야 한다면, 만나야겠지.\""], ["“But I can’t prevail on myself to see him alone.", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"허나 그를 혼자서 대면할 엄두가 도무지 나지 않는구나."], ["Bring him in, Gabriel, and stay here as long as he stays.”", "그를 들여보내고, 게이브리얼, 그가 머무는 동안 너도 여기 함께 있거라.\""]], [["This was the first attack of the megrims that I remembered in my mistress since the time when she was a young girl.", "이것이, 마님이 아직 어린 처녀였던 시절 이래로 내가 기억하는, 마님이 겪으신 첫 신경 발작이었다."], ["I went back to the “boudoir.” Mr.", "나는 \"규방\"으로 돌아갔다."], ["Franklin strolled out into the garden, and joined Mr. Godfrey, whose time for departure was now drawing near.", "프랭클린 도련님은 정원으로 어슬렁어슬렁 나가, 이제 떠날 시각이 가까워 오던 고드프리 씨와 어울렸다."], ["Sergeant Cuff and I went straight to my mistress’s room.", "커프 부장과 나는 곧장 마님의 방으로 갔다."]], [["I declare my lady turned a shade paler at the sight of him!", "단언컨대 마님은 그를 보시자 안색이 한 겹 더 파리해지셨다!"], ["She commanded herself, however, in other respects, and asked the Sergeant if he had any objection to my being present.", "그러나 그 밖의 점에서는 마님은 자제하셨고, 부장에게 내가 자리에 있어도 괜찮겠냐고 물으셨다."], ["She was so good as to add, that I was her trusted adviser, as well as her old servant, and that in anything which related to the household I was the person whom it might be most profitable to consult.", "마님은 고맙게도, 내가 당신의 오랜 하인일 뿐 아니라 믿음직한 조언자이기도 하며, 집안일에 관한 것이라면 상의하기에 가장 이로울 사람이 바로 나라고 덧붙이셨다."], ["The Sergeant politely answered that he would take my presence as a favour, having something to say about the servants in general, and having found my experience in that quarter already of some use to him.", "부장은 정중히 대답하기를, 하인들 전반에 대해 할 말이 있고 또 그 방면에서 내 경험이 이미 제게 다소 도움이 되었던 터라, 내가 자리에 있는 것을 오히려 고맙게 여기겠노라고 했다."], ["My lady pointed to two chairs, and we set in for our conference immediately.", "마님이 의자 두 개를 가리키셨고, 우리는 곧바로 회의에 들어갔다."]], [["“I have already formed an opinion on this case,”", "\"저는 이미 이 사건에 대해 한 가지 의견을 세웠습니다.\""], ["says Sergeant Cuff, “which I beg your ladyship’s permission to keep to myself for the present.", "하고 커프 부장이 말한다. \"그것은 마님께서 허락해 주신다면 당분간은 제 안에 담아 두고자 합니다."], ["My business now is to mention what I have discovered upstairs in Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, and what I have decided (with your ladyship’s leave) on doing next.”", "지금 제 볼일은, 제가 위층 버린더 아가씨의 거실에서 알아낸 것과, 그리고 (마님의 윤허 아래) 다음에 하기로 정한 것을 말씀드리는 것입니다.\""]], [["He then went into the matter of the smear on the paint, and stated the conclusions he drew from it—just as he had stated them (only with greater respect of language) to Superintendent Seegrave.", "이어 그는 페인트에 묻은 얼룩 문제로 들어가, 거기서 이끌어 낸 결론들을 진술했다—시그레이브 경정에게 진술했던 그대로였다 (다만 말씨는 한결 정중했다)."], ["“One thing,”", "\"한 가지는,\""], ["he said, in conclusion, “is certain.", "하고 그가 결론지어 말했다. \"확실합니다."], ["The Diamond is missing out of the drawer in the cabinet.", "다이아몬드가 캐비닛 서랍에서 없어졌다는 것이지요."], ["Another thing is next to certain.", "또 한 가지는 확실한 것이나 다름없습니다."], ["The marks from the smear on the door must be on some article of dress belonging to somebody in this house.", "문의 그 얼룩에서 묻어난 자국이, 이 집 누군가에게 딸린 어떤 옷가지에 남아 있는 게 틀림없다는 것입니다."], ["We must discover that article of dress before we go a step further.”", "우리는 한 걸음 더 나아가기 전에 그 옷가지를 찾아내야 합니다.\""]], [["“And that discovery,”", "\"그리고 그것을 찾아낸다는 것은,\""], ["remarked my mistress, “implies, I presume, the discovery of the thief?”", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"짐작건대, 도둑을 찾아낸다는 뜻이겠지요?\""]], [["“I beg your ladyship’s pardon—I don’t say the Diamond is stolen.", "\"송구하오나 마님—저는 다이아몬드가 도둑맞았다고 말씀드리지 않습니다."], ["I only say, at present, that the Diamond is missing.", "지금으로서는, 다만 다이아몬드가 없어졌다고만 말씀드릴 뿐입니다."], ["The discovery of the stained dress may lead the way to finding it.”", "그 얼룩진 옷을 찾아내는 일이, 그것을 찾는 길로 이어질지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["Her ladyship looked at me.", "마님이 나를 바라보셨다."], ["“Do you understand this?”", "\"너는 이 말을 알아듣겠느냐?\""], ["she said.", "하고 물으셨다."]], [["“Sergeant Cuff understands it, my lady,”", "\"커프 부장께서 알아들으시는 겁니다, 마님.\""], ["I answered.", "하고 내가 대답했다."]], [["“How do you propose to discover the stained dress?”", "\"그 얼룩진 옷을 어떻게 찾아낼 작정이시오?\""], ["inquired my mistress, addressing herself once more to the Sergeant.", "하고 마님이 다시 한번 부장에게 말을 건네며 물으셨다."], ["“My good servants, who have been with me for years, have, I am ashamed to say, had their boxes and rooms searched already by the other officer.", "\"부끄러운 말이오만, 오랜 세월 나와 함께해 온 내 선량한 하인들은, 이미 저 다른 관리에게 제 상자와 방을 뒤짐당했소."], ["I can’t and won’t permit them to be insulted in that way a second time!”", "나는 그들이 그런 식으로 두 번씩이나 모욕당하는 것을 허락할 수도 없고, 하지도 않겠소!\""]], [["(There was a mistress to serve!", "(이 얼마나 섬길 만한 마님인가!"], ["There was a woman in ten thousand, if you like!)", "이야말로 만 사람 가운데 하나 나올까 말까 한 여인이 아닌가!)"]], [["“That is the very point I was about to put to your ladyship,”", "\"그것이 바로 제가 마님께 여쭈려던 요점입니다.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“The other officer has done a world of harm to this inquiry, by letting the servants see that he suspected them.", "\"저 다른 관리는, 하인들에게 제가 그들을 의심한다는 것을 내보임으로써, 이 조사에 이만저만 해를 끼친 게 아닙니다."], ["If I give them cause to think themselves suspected a second time, there’s no knowing what obstacles they may not throw in my way—the women especially.", "만일 제가 그들에게 두 번째로 의심받는다고 여길 빌미를 준다면, 그들이 제 앞길에 어떤 훼방을 놓을지 알 수 없지요—특히나 여자들이 그렇습니다."], ["At the same time, their boxes must be searched again—for this plain reason, that the first investigation only looked for the Diamond, and that the second investigation must look for the stained dress.", "하나 그와 동시에, 그들의 상자는 반드시 다시 뒤져야 합니다—그야말로 뻔한 이유에서지요. 첫 번째 조사는 다이아몬드만 찾았지만, 두 번째 조사는 그 얼룩진 옷을 찾아야 하니 말입니다."], ["I quite agree with you, my lady, that the servants’ feelings ought to be consulted.", "하인들의 심정을 헤아려야 한다는 데는 저도 전적으로 마님과 뜻을 같이합니다, 마님."], ["But I am equally clear that the servants’ wardrobes ought to be searched.”", "허나 하인들의 옷장을 뒤져야 한다는 것 또한 저는 못지않게 분명히 압니다.\""]], [["This looked very like a dead-lock.", "이것은 영락없이 막다른 골목처럼 보였다."], ["My lady said so, in choicer language than mine.", "마님도, 나보다 한결 고른 말씨로, 그렇게 말씀하셨다."]], [["“I have got a plan to meet the difficulty,”", "\"이 난처함을 풀 방책이 제게 하나 있습니다.\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, “if your ladyship will consent to it.", "하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"마님께서 승낙만 해 주신다면요."], ["I propose explaining the case to the servants.”", "저는 하인들에게 이 사정을 설명하려 합니다.\""]], [["“The women will think themselves suspected directly,”", "\"여자들은 대뜸 제가 의심받는다고 여길 겁니다.\""], ["I said, interrupting him.", "하고 나는 그의 말을 가로막으며 말했다."]], [["“The women won’t, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"여자들은 그러지 않을 겁니다, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["answered the Sergeant, “if I can tell them I am going to examine the wardrobes of everybody—from her ladyship downwards—who slept in the house on Wednesday night.", "하고 부장이 대답했다. \"제가 그들에게, 수요일 밤 이 집에서 잔 사람이라면 누구든—마님으로부터 아래로 죄다—그 옷장을 살펴볼 참이라고 말할 수 있다면 말이지요."], ["It’s a mere formality,”", "그저 형식일 뿐입니다.\""], ["he added, with a side look at my mistress; “but the servants will accept it as even dealing between them and their betters; and, instead of hindering the investigation, they will make a point of honour of assisting it.”", "하고 그는 마님을 곁눈질하며 덧붙였다. \"허나 하인들은 그것을 저희와 저희 윗분들 사이의 공평한 처사로 받아들일 것이고, 그리하여 조사를 훼방하기는커녕, 그것을 돕는 것을 명예로 삼으려 들 것입니다.\""]], [["I saw the truth of that.", "나는 그 말이 옳음을 알아보았다."], ["My lady, after her first surprise was over, saw the truth of it also.", "마님도, 처음의 놀라움이 가시고 나자, 역시 그 말이 옳음을 알아보셨다."]], [["“You are certain the investigation is necessary?”", "\"그 조사가 꼭 필요하다고 확신하시오?\""], ["she said.", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다."]], [["“It’s the shortest way that I can see, my lady, to the end we have in view.”", "\"제가 보기에는, 우리가 눈앞에 둔 목적에 이르는 가장 빠른 길입니다, 마님.\""]], [["My mistress rose to ring the bell for her maid.", "마님이 몸종을 부르는 종을 울리려고 자리에서 일어나셨다."], ["“You shall speak to the servants,”", "\"그대는 하인들에게 이야기하시오.\""], ["she said, “with the keys of my wardrobe in your hand.”", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"내 옷장 열쇠를 손에 쥐고서 말이오.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff stopped her by a very unexpected question.", "커프 부장이 아주 뜻밖의 물음으로 마님을 멈춰 세웠다."]], [["“Hadn’t we better make sure first,”", "\"먼저 확실히 해 두는 편이 낫지 않겠습니까,\""], ["he asked, “that the other ladies and gentlemen in the house will consent, too?”", "하고 그가 물었다. \"이 집의 다른 숙녀분들과 신사분들도 함께 승낙하실지를 말입니다.\""]], [["“The only other lady in the house is Miss Verinder,”", "\"이 집에 다른 숙녀라고는 버린더 아가씨뿐이오.\""], ["answered my mistress, with a look of surprise.", "하고 마님이 놀란 낯빛으로 대답하셨다."], ["“The only gentlemen are my nephews, Mr. Blake and Mr.", "\"신사라고는 내 조카들인 블레이크 도련님과 에이블화이트 조카뿐이고."], ["Ablewhite. There is not the least fear of a refusal from any of the three.”", "그 셋 중 누구에게서도 거절이 나올 염려는 조금도 없소.\""]], [["I reminded my lady here that Mr. Godfrey was going away.", "나는 여기서 마님께 고드프리 씨가 떠나려던 참임을 일깨워 드렸다."], ["As I said the words, Mr. Godfrey himself knocked at the door to say good-bye, and was followed in by Mr.", "내가 그 말을 하는 참에, 고드프리 씨 본인이 작별 인사를 하려고 문을 두드렸고, 그를 역까지 배웅하려던 프랭클린 도련님이 뒤따라 들어왔다."], ["Franklin, who was going with him to the station.", "마님이 그 난처함을 설명하셨다."], ["My lady explained the difficulty. Mr.", "고드프리 씨는 곧바로 그것을 매듭지었다."], ["Godfrey settled it directly. He called to Samuel, through the window, to take his portmanteau upstairs again, and he then put the key himself into Sergeant Cuff’s hand.", "그는 창문 너머로 새뮤얼을 불러 제 여행 가방을 다시 위층으로 올려 두라 이르고는, 손수 그 열쇠를 커프 부장의 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["“My luggage can follow me to London,”", "\"제 짐은 이 조사가 끝나면,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “when the inquiry is over.”", "\"저를 따라 런던으로 부치면 되겠지요.\""], ["The Sergeant received the key with a becoming apology.", "부장은 격에 맞는 사과와 함께 그 열쇠를 받아 들었다."], ["“I am sorry to put you to any inconvenience, sir, for a mere formality; but the example of their betters will do wonders in reconciling the servants to this inquiry.”", "\"한낱 형식 때문에 폐를 끼치게 되어 송구합니다, 나리. 하나 윗분들이 보이시는 본보기는, 하인들이 이 조사를 순순히 받아들이게 하는 데 놀라운 힘을 낼 것입니다.\""], ["Mr. Godfrey, after taking leave of my lady, in a most sympathising manner, left a farewell message for Miss Rachel, the terms of which made it clear to my mind that he had not taken No for an answer, and that he meant to put the marriage question to her once more, at the next opportunity.", "고드프리 씨는, 더없이 마음을 헤아리는 태도로 마님께 작별을 고한 뒤, 레이철 아가씨께 작별의 전언을 남겼는데, 그 말투로 미루어 내 마음속에는 그가 거절을 순순히 받아들이지 않았으며, 다음 기회에 아가씨께 그 혼담을 한 번 더 꺼낼 작정임이 분명해졌다."], ["Mr. Franklin, on following his cousin out, informed the Sergeant that all his clothes were open to examination, and that nothing he possessed was kept under lock and key.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 제 사촌을 따라 나서면서, 부장에게 제 옷은 죄다 살펴봐도 좋으며, 제가 지닌 것 중에 자물쇠로 잠가 둔 것이라곤 하나도 없다고 일러 주었다."], ["Sergeant Cuff made his best acknowledgments.", "커프 부장은 더없이 정중히 사의를 표했다."], ["His views, you will observe, had been met with the utmost readiness by my lady, by Mr. Godfrey, and by Mr.", "여러분도 보시다시피, 그의 뜻은 마님과, 고드프리 씨와, 프랭클린 도련님에게서 더할 나위 없이 흔쾌하게 받아들여진 셈이었다."], ["Franklin. There was only Miss Rachel now wanting to follow their lead, before we called the servants together, and began the search for the stained dress.", "이제 하인들을 불러 모아 그 얼룩진 옷을 찾기 시작하기에 앞서, 그들의 본을 따르는 데 남은 이라고는 오직 레이철 아가씨 한 사람뿐이었다."]], [["My lady’s unaccountable objection to the Sergeant seemed to make our conference more distasteful to her than ever, as soon as we were left alone again.", "부장에 대한 마님의 그 알 수 없는 거부감은, 우리 둘만 다시 남게 되자마자, 이 회의를 마님께 그 어느 때보다 더 언짢은 것으로 만드는 듯했다."], ["“If I send you down Miss Verinder’s keys,”", "\"내가 버린더 아가씨의 열쇠를 그대에게 내려보내면,\""], ["she said to him, “I presume I shall have done all you want of me for the present?”", "하고 마님이 그에게 말씀하셨다. \"짐작건대 당분간 내게 바라는 바는 다 이룬 셈이겠지요?\""]], [["“I beg your ladyship’s pardon,”", "\"송구하오나 마님.\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말했다."], ["“Before we begin, I should like, if convenient, to have the washing-book.", "\"시작하기에 앞서, 괜찮으시다면, 세탁 장부를 봤으면 합니다."], ["The stained article of dress may be an article of linen.", "그 얼룩진 옷가지가 아마포 종류일 수도 있으니까요."], ["If the search leads to nothing, I want to be able to account next for all the linen in the house, and for all the linen sent to the wash.", "만일 이 수색이 아무 소득 없이 끝난다면, 저는 다음으로 이 집에 있는 모든 아마포와, 세탁에 내보낸 모든 아마포를 낱낱이 셈할 수 있기를 바랍니다."], ["If there is an article missing, there will be at least a presumption that it has got the paint-stain on it, and that it has been purposely made away with, yesterday or today, by the person owning it.", "만일 없어진 물건이 하나라도 있다면, 적어도 그것에 페인트 얼룩이 묻어 있었으며, 그 임자 되는 사람이 어제나 오늘, 일부러 그것을 없애 버렸으리라는 짐작은 서게 될 테지요."], ["Superintendent Seegrave,”", "시그레이브 경정께서는,\""], ["added the Sergeant, turning to me, “pointed the attention of the women-servants to the smear, when they all crowded into the room on Thursday morning.", "하고 부장은 나를 돌아보며 덧붙였다. \"목요일 아침 여자 하인들이 죄다 방으로 몰려들었을 때, 그들의 주의를 그 얼룩으로 이끌었지요."], ["That may turn out, Mr. Betteredge, to have been one more of Superintendent Seegrave’s many mistakes.”", "그것이, 베터리지 양반, 시그레이브 경정의 그 숱한 실수에 하나 더 보태진 것으로 드러날지도 모릅니다.\""]], [["My lady desired me to ring the bell, and order the washing-book.", "마님은 내게 종을 울려 세탁 장부를 가져오라 이르셨다."], ["She remained with us until it was produced, in case Sergeant Cuff had any further request to make of her after looking at it.", "마님은 그것이 나올 때까지 우리와 함께 계셨는데, 커프 부장이 그것을 살펴본 뒤 마님께 또 다른 청을 할 경우에 대비해서였다."]], [["The washing-book was brought in by Rosanna Spearman.", "세탁 장부는 로재나 스피어먼이 들여왔다."], ["The girl had come down to breakfast that morning miserably pale and haggard, but sufficiently recovered from her illness of the previous day to do her usual work.", "그 아이는 그날 아침 딱하게도 파리하고 초췌한 낯으로 아침 식사에 내려왔으나, 전날의 병에서는 제 여느 일을 할 만큼 웬만큼 회복되어 있었다."], ["Sergeant Cuff looked attentively at our second housemaid—at her face, when she came in; at her crooked shoulder, when she went out.", "커프 부장은 우리 집 둘째 하녀를 유심히 바라보았다—들어올 때는 그 얼굴을, 나갈 때는 그 굽은 어깨를."]], [["“Have you anything more to say to me?”", "\"내게 더 할 말이 있소?\""], ["asked my lady, still as eager as ever to be out of the Sergeant’s society.", "하고 마님이, 여전히 부장의 곁을 벗어나고 싶어 안달하며 물으셨다."]], [["The great Cuff opened the washing-book, understood it perfectly in half a minute, and shut it up again.", "그 위대한 커프는 세탁 장부를 펼쳐, 반 분 만에 그것을 완벽히 파악하고는, 다시 덮었다."], ["“I venture to trouble your ladyship with one last question,”", "\"마님께 감히 마지막 질문 하나로 폐를 끼치겠습니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Has the young woman who brought us this book been in your employment as long as the other servants?”", "\"우리에게 이 장부를 가져다준 저 젊은 여자는, 다른 하인들만큼 오래 마님 밑에서 일해 왔습니까?\""]], [["“Why do you ask?”", "\"어째서 그것을 묻소?\""], ["said my lady.", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다."]], [["“The last time I saw her,”", "\"제가 그를 마지막으로 보았을 때,\""], ["answered the Sergeant, “she was in prison for theft.”", "하고 부장이 대답했다. \"그는 절도죄로 감옥에 있었습니다.\""]], [["After that, there was no help for it, but to tell him the truth.", "그다음에는, 그에게 사실대로 이르는 수밖에 도리가 없었다."], ["My mistress dwelt strongly on Rosanna’s good conduct in her service, and on the high opinion entertained of her by the matron at the Reformatory.", "마님은 로재나가 당신 밑에서 지내며 보인 착실한 품행과, 갱생원 여감독관이 그를 높이 평가했다는 사실을 힘주어 말씀하셨다."], ["“You don’t suspect her, I hope?”", "\"설마 그 아이를 의심하는 건 아니겠지요?\""], ["my lady added, in conclusion, very earnestly.", "하고 마님은 끝으로 아주 간곡히 덧붙이셨다."]], [["“I have already told your ladyship that I don’t suspect any person in the house of thieving—up to the present time.”", "\"저는 이미 마님께 말씀드렸습니다, 이 집의 그 누구도 도둑질을 저질렀다고 의심하지 않는다고요—지금 이 순간까지는 말입니다.\""]], [["After that answer, my lady rose to go upstairs, and ask for Miss Rachel’s keys.", "그 대답을 듣고, 마님은 위층으로 올라가 레이철 아가씨의 열쇠를 청하려고 자리에서 일어나셨다."], ["The Sergeant was beforehand with me in opening the door for her.", "부장은 나보다 앞질러 마님을 위해 문을 열었다."], ["He made a very low bow.", "그는 아주 깊이 허리를 굽혀 절했다."], ["My lady shuddered as she passed him.", "마님은 그의 곁을 지나치시며 몸서리를 치셨다."]], [["We waited, and waited, and no keys appeared.", "우리는 기다리고, 또 기다렸으나, 열쇠는 나타나지 않았다."], ["Sergeant Cuff made no remark to me.", "커프 부장은 내게 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He turned his melancholy face to the window; he put his lanky hands into his pockets; and he whistled “The Last Rose of Summer” softly to himself.", "그는 그 우울한 얼굴을 창 쪽으로 돌리고, 여윈 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고는, \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 혼자 나직이 휘파람으로 불었다."]], [["At last, Samuel came in, not with the keys, but with a morsel of paper for me.", "마침내 새뮤얼이 들어왔는데, 열쇠가 아니라, 내게 전할 쪽지 한 조각을 들고 있었다."], ["I got at my spectacles, with some fumbling and difficulty, feeling the Sergeant’s dismal eyes fixed on me all the time.", "나는 부장의 그 음침한 눈이 내내 나를 지켜보고 있음을 느끼며, 얼마쯤 더듬거리고 애를 먹은 끝에 안경을 찾아 썼다."], ["There were two or three lines on the paper, written in pencil by my lady.", "그 쪽지에는 마님이 연필로 적으신 두어 줄이 있었다."], ["They informed me that Miss Rachel flatly refused to have her wardrobe examined.", "그 글은 내게 알리기를, 레이철 아가씨가 제 옷장을 살펴보게 하는 것을 딱 잘라 거절했다는 것이었다."], ["Asked for her reasons, she had burst out crying.", "그 까닭을 묻자, 아가씨는 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Asked again, she had said: “I won’t, because I won’t.", "다시 묻자, 아가씨는 이렇게 말했다는 것이었다. \"싫으니까 싫은 거예요."], ["I must yield to force if you use it, but I will yield to nothing else.”", "강제로 하신다면 그 힘에는 굽히겠지만, 그 밖의 어떤 것에도 저는 굽히지 않겠어요.\""], ["I understood my lady’s disinclination to face Sergeant Cuff with such an answer from her daughter as that.", "나는 마님이 당신 따님에게서 나온 그런 대답을 들고 커프 부장을 대면하기 꺼리시는 심정을 헤아렸다."], ["If I had not been too old for the amiable weaknesses of youth, I believe I should have blushed at the notion of facing him myself.", "만일 내가 젊은이의 사랑스러운 약점을 지니기에는 너무 늙어 버린 처지가 아니었던들, 나 역시 그를 대면할 생각에 얼굴을 붉혔으리라 여겨진다."]], [["“Any news of Miss Verinder’s keys?”", "\"버린더 아가씨의 열쇠에 대해 무슨 소식이 있소?\""], ["asked the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 물었다."]], [["“My young lady refuses to have her wardrobe examined.”", "\"우리 아가씨께서 제 옷장을 살펴보게 하는 것을 거절하십니다.\""]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."]], [["His voice was not quite in such a perfect state of discipline as his face.", "그의 목소리는 그의 얼굴만큼 그리 완벽하게 다스려진 상태는 아니었다."], ["When he said “Ah!”", "그가 \"아!\""], ["he said it in the tone of a man who had heard something which he expected to hear.", "하고 말했을 때, 그것은 듣게 되리라 예상하던 무언가를 들은 사람의 어조였다."], ["He half angered and half frightened me—why, I couldn’t tell, but he did it.", "그것은 나를 반은 골나게 하고 반은 겁나게 했다—왜인지는 나도 알 수 없었으나, 그가 그렇게 만들었다."]], [["“Must the search be given up?”", "\"수색을 그만두어야 합니까?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said the Sergeant, “the search must be given up, because your young lady refuses to submit to it like the rest.", "하고 부장이 말했다. \"수색은 그만두어야 하오."], ["We must examine all the wardrobes in the house or none. Send Mr.", "아가씨가 나머지 사람들처럼 그것에 응하기를 거절하시니 말이오."], ["Ablewhite’s portmanteau to London by the next train, and return the washing-book, with my compliments and thanks, to the young woman who brought it in.”", "우리는 이 집의 옷장을 죄다 살펴보든지, 아니면 하나도 살펴보지 말든지 해야 하오. 에이블화이트 씨의 여행 가방은 다음 기차 편으로 런던에 부치고, 세탁 장부는 그것을 가져온 젊은 여자에게, 내 인사와 감사와 함께 돌려주시오.\""]], [["He laid the washing-book on the table, and taking out his penknife, began to trim his nails.", "그는 세탁 장부를 탁자 위에 내려놓고는, 주머니칼을 꺼내 제 손톱을 다듬기 시작했다."]], [["“You don’t seem to be much disappointed,”", "\"그리 실망하신 것 같지 않군요.\""], ["I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff; “I am not much disappointed.”", "하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"나는 그리 실망하지 않았소.\""]], [["I tried to make him explain himself.", "나는 그에게 속내를 털어놓게 하려고 애썼다."]], [["“Why should Miss Rachel put an obstacle in your way?”", "\"어째서 레이철 아가씨가 당신 앞길에 훼방을 놓으시는 걸까요?\""], ["I inquired.", "하고 내가 캐물었다."], ["“Isn’t it her interest to help you?”", "\"당신을 돕는 것이 아가씨께 이로운 일 아닙니까?\""]], [["“Wait a little, Mr. Betteredge—wait a little.”", "\"좀 기다려 보시오, 베터리지 양반—좀 기다려 보시오.\""]], [["Cleverer heads than mine might have seen his drift.", "나보다 영리한 머리라면 그의 속셈을 알아보았을지도 모른다."], ["Or a person less fond of Miss Rachel than I was, might have seen his drift.", "아니면 나만큼 레이철 아가씨를 아끼지 않는 사람이라면 그의 속셈을 알아보았을지도 모른다."], ["My lady’s horror of him might (as I have since thought) have meant that she saw his drift (as the scripture says) “in a glass darkly.”", "그에 대한 마님의 그 두려움은 (그 뒤로 내가 생각해 온 바로는) 마님께서는 그의 속셈을 (성경 말씀처럼) \"희미하게 거울로 보듯\" 알아보셨음을 뜻하는 것일 수도 있었다."], ["I didn’t see it yet—that’s all I know.", "나는 아직 그것을 알아보지 못했다—내가 아는 것은 그뿐이다."]], [["“What’s to be done next?”", "\"이제 다음엔 무얼 해야 합니까?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff finished the nail on which he was then at work, looked at it for a moment with a melancholy interest, and put up his penknife.", "커프 부장은 그때 다듬고 있던 손톱을 마저 다듬고는, 그것을 우울한 흥미로 잠시 들여다보다가, 주머니칼을 도로 넣었다."]], [["“Come out into the garden,”", "\"정원으로 나갑시다.\""], ["he said, “and let’s have a look at the roses.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 그 장미나 한번 보러 갑시다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER XIV", "# CHAPTER XIV"]], [["The nearest way to the garden, on going out of my lady’s sitting-room, was by the shrubbery path, which you already know of.", "마님의 거실을 나서서 정원으로 가는 가장 가까운 길은, 여러분도 이미 아시는 그 관목 숲 오솔길이었다."], ["For the sake of your better understanding of what is now to come, I may add to this, that the shrubbery path was Mr. Franklin’s favourite walk.", "앞으로 벌어질 일을 여러분이 한결 잘 헤아리시도록, 여기에 한 가지 덧붙이자면, 그 관목 숲 오솔길은 프랭클린 도련님이 즐겨 걷는 산책길이었다."], ["When he was out in the grounds, and when we failed to find him anywhere else, we generally found him here.", "그가 부지에 나와 있을 때, 다른 어디서도 그를 찾지 못하면, 우리는 대개 여기서 그를 찾아냈다."]], [["I am afraid I must own that I am rather an obstinate old man.", "부끄러운 말이나, 나는 아무래도 좀 고집스러운 늙은이임을 인정해야겠다."], ["The more firmly Sergeant Cuff kept his thoughts shut up from me, the more firmly I persisted in trying to look in at them.", "커프 부장이 제 속생각을 내게서 굳게 걸어 잠글수록, 나는 더욱 굳게 그 속을 들여다보려 애썼다."], ["As we turned into the shrubbery path, I attempted to circumvent him in another way.", "관목 숲 오솔길로 접어들면서, 나는 또 다른 수로 그를 넘겨짚어 보려 했다."]], [["“As things are now,”", "\"지금 사정이 이러니,\""], ["I said, “if I was in your place, I should be at my wits’ end.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"제가 만일 나리 처지라면, 저는 도무지 갈피를 못 잡았을 겁니다.\""]], [["“If you were in my place,”", "\"당신이 만일 내 처지라면,\""], ["answered the Sergeant, “you would have formed an opinion—and, as things are now, any doubt you might previously have felt about your own conclusions would be completely set at rest.", "하고 부장이 대답했다. \"당신은 이미 한 가지 의견을 세웠을 것이오—그리고 지금 사정이 이러하니, 당신이 앞서 제 결론에 대해 품었을 법한 어떤 의심도 말끔히 가셨을 테지."], ["Never mind for the present what those conclusions are, Mr. Betteredge.", "그 결론이 무엇인지는 당분간 신경 쓸 것 없소, 베터리지 양반."], ["I haven’t brought you out here to draw me like a badger; I have brought you out here to ask for some information.", "나는 당신을 여기로 데려온 것이 나를 오소리 잡듯 캐내라는 것이 아니오. 나는 얼마쯤 알아둘 것이 있어 당신을 여기로 데려온 거요."], ["You might have given it to me no doubt, in the house, instead of out of it.", "물론 당신은 그것을 집 밖이 아니라 집 안에서도 내게 일러 줄 수 있었겠지."], ["But doors and listeners have a knack of getting together; and, in my line of life, we cultivate a healthy taste for the open air.”", "허나 문과 엿듣는 귀란 것은 곧잘 한데 어울리는 재간이 있고, 우리네 직업에서는, 바깥 공기에 대한 건전한 취향을 기르는 법이라오.\""]], [["Who was to circumvent this man?", "이런 사람을 누가 넘겨짚을 수 있으랴?"], ["I gave in—and waited as patiently as I could to hear what was coming next.", "나는 두 손 들었다—그리고 다음에 무슨 말이 나올지 되도록 참을성 있게 기다렸다."]], [["“We won’t enter into your young lady’s motives,”", "\"당신 아가씨의 속뜻에까지 파고들지는 맙시다.\" 하고 부장이 말을 이었다."], ["the Sergeant went on; “we will only say it’s a pity she declines to assist me, because, by so doing, she makes this investigation more difficult than it might otherwise have been.", "\"다만 아가씨가 나를 돕기를 마다하시니 딱한 노릇이라고만 해 둡시다. 그리함으로써, 아가씨는 이 조사를 본디보다 한결 더 어렵게 만드셨으니 말이오."], ["We must now try to solve the mystery of the smear on the door—which, you may take my word for it, means the mystery of the Diamond also—in some other way.", "우리는 이제 그 문의 얼룩이라는 수수께끼를—내 말을 믿으시오, 그것은 곧 다이아몬드의 수수께끼이기도 하니—다른 어떤 방도로 풀어 보아야 하오."], ["I have decided to see the servants, and to search their thoughts and actions, Mr. Betteredge, instead of searching their wardrobes.", "나는 하인들의 옷장을 뒤지는 대신, 그들을 만나 그들의 생각과 행동을 살피기로 정했소, 베터리지 양반."], ["Before I begin, however, I want to ask you a question or two.", "그러나 시작하기 전에, 당신에게 한두 가지 묻고 싶은 것이 있소."], ["You are an observant man—did you notice anything strange in any of the servants (making due allowance, of course, for fright and fluster), after the loss of the Diamond was found out?", "당신은 눈썰미 있는 사람이니—다이아몬드가 없어진 것이 밝혀진 뒤에, (물론 놀라고 허둥댔을 것은 감안해서) 하인들 가운데 누구에게서든 무언가 이상한 낌새를 눈치챈 것이 있소?"], ["Any particular quarrel among them?", "그들 사이에 무슨 유별난 다툼이 있었다든가?"], ["Anyone of them not in his or her usual spirits?", "누구 하나 여느 때 같지 않은 기색이었다든가?"], ["Unexpectedly out of temper, for instance?", "이를테면, 뜻밖에 심기가 상했다든가?"], ["or unexpectedly taken ill?”", "아니면 뜻밖에 몸져누웠다든가?\""]], [["I had just time to think of Rosanna Spearman’s sudden illness at yesterday’s dinner—but not time to make any answer—when I saw Sergeant Cuff’s eyes suddenly turn aside towards the shrubbery; and I heard him say softly to himself, “Hullo!”", "나는 어제 저녁 식사 때 로재나 스피어먼이 별안간 앓았던 일을 떠올릴 겨를은 있었으나—미처 아무 대답도 하기 전에—커프 부장의 눈이 별안간 관목 숲 쪽으로 돌아가는 것을 보았다. 그리고 그가 혼자 나직이 \"이런!\" 하고 말하는 소리를 들었다."]], [["“What’s the matter?”", "\"무슨 일입니까?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“A touch of the rheumatics in my back,”", "\"등에 신경통이 좀 도지는군.\""], ["said the Sergeant, in a loud voice, as if he wanted some third person to hear us.", "하고 부장이, 마치 제삼자가 우리 말을 들어 주기를 바라기라도 하듯, 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["“We shall have a change in the weather before long.”", "\"머잖아 날씨가 바뀔 모양이오.\""]], [["A few steps further brought us to the corner of the house.", "몇 걸음 더 가자 우리는 집 모퉁이에 이르렀다."], ["Turning off sharp to the right, we entered on the terrace, and went down, by the steps in the middle, into the garden below.", "오른편으로 홱 꺾어, 우리는 테라스로 들어섰고, 한복판의 계단을 따라, 아래쪽 정원으로 내려갔다."], ["Sergeant Cuff stopped there, in the open space, where we could see round us on every side.", "커프 부장은 거기, 사방을 둘러볼 수 있는 툭 트인 자리에서 멈춰 섰다."]], [["“About that young person, Rosanna Spearman?” he said.", "\"그 젊은 사람 말인데, 로재나 스피어먼 말이오.\""], ["“It isn’t very likely, with her personal appearance, that she has got a lover.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그 생김새로 보아, 정인이 있을 성싶지는 않소."], ["But, for the girl’s own sake, I must ask you at once whether she has provided herself with a sweetheart, poor wretch, like the rest of them?”", "허나, 그 아이 자신을 위해서라도, 나는 당장 당신에게 묻지 않을 수 없구려. 그 딱한 것도, 나머지 여자들처럼, 제게 사랑하는 이를 하나 마련해 두었소?\""]], [["What on earth did he mean, under present circumstances, by putting such a question to me as that?", "지금 이런 마당에, 대체 무슨 뜻으로 그가 내게 그런 물음을 던지는지 알 수가 없었다."], ["I stared at him, instead of answering him.", "나는 그에게 대답하는 대신 그를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“I saw Rosanna Spearman hiding in the shrubbery as we went by,”", "\"우리가 지나갈 때, 로재나 스피어먼이 관목 숲에 숨어 있는 것을 보았소.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."]], [["“When you said ‘Hullo’?”", "\"'이런!' 하고 말씀하셨을 때 말입니까?\""]], [["“Yes—when I said ‘Hullo!’ If there’s a sweetheart in the case, the hiding doesn’t much matter.", "\"그렇소—내가 '이런!' 하고 말했을 때 말이오."], ["If there isn’t—as things are in this house—the hiding is a highly suspicious circumstance, and it will be my painful duty to act on it accordingly.”", "만일 이 일에 사랑하는 이가 얽혀 있다면, 그 숨은 것은 그리 대수롭지 않소. 만일 그렇지 않다면—이 집 사정이 이러한 마당에—그 숨은 것은 몹시 수상쩍은 정황이며, 그에 따라 손을 쓰는 것이 나의 괴로운 임무가 될 것이오.\""]], [["What, in God’s name, was I to say to him?", "맙소사, 나는 그에게 무어라 말해야 했을까?"], ["I knew the shrubbery was Mr.", "나는 그 관목 숲이 프랭클린 도련님이 즐겨 걷는 산책길임을 알고 있었다."], ["Franklin’s favourite walk; I knew he would most likely turn that way when he came back from the station; I knew that Penelope had over and over again caught her fellow-servant hanging about there, and had always declared to me that Rosanna’s object was to attract Mr.", "나는 그가 역에서 돌아오면 십중팔구 그리로 향하리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 나는 페넬로피가 제 동료 하인이 그곳을 서성이는 것을 몇 번이고 붙잡았으며, 로재나의 속셈이 프랭클린 도련님의 눈길을 끄는 데 있다고 내게 늘 딱 잘라 말해 왔음을 알고 있었다."], ["Franklin’s attention. If my daughter was right, she might well have been lying in wait for Mr.", "만일 내 딸의 말이 옳다면, 부장이 그를 눈여겨보았을 때 그는 프랭클린 도련님이 돌아오기를 숨어 기다리고 있었던 것일 수도 있었다."], ["Franklin’s return when the Sergeant noticed her. I was put between the two difficulties of mentioning Penelope’s fanciful notion as if it was mine, or of leaving an unfortunate creature to suffer the consequences, the very serious consequences, of exciting the suspicion of Sergeant Cuff.", "나는 두 갈래 곤경 사이에 놓였다—페넬로피의 그 지어낸 억측을 마치 내 것인 양 말할 것인가, 아니면 한 불행한 것을 내버려 두어 커프 부장의 의심을 산 결과를, 그것도 아주 중대한 결과를 고스란히 겪게 할 것인가."], ["Out of pure pity for the girl—on my soul and my character, out of pure pity for the girl—I gave the Sergeant the necessary explanations, and told him that Rosanna had been mad enough to set her heart on Mr. Franklin Blake.", "그 아이가 순전히 가여운 마음에서—내 영혼과 내 인격을 걸고, 순전히 그 아이가 가여운 마음에서—나는 부장에게 필요한 설명을 해 주었고, 로재나가 어리석게도 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 마음을 두었다고 일러 주었다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff never laughed.", "커프 부장은 결코 소리 내어 웃는 법이 없었다."], ["On the few occasions when anything amused him, he curled up a little at the corners of the lips, nothing more.", "무언가가 그를 즐겁게 하는 드문 경우에도, 그는 입아귀를 조금 말아 올릴 뿐, 그 이상은 없었다."], ["He curled up now.", "그가 이제 입아귀를 말아 올렸다."]], [["“Hadn’t you better say she’s mad enough to be an ugly girl and only a servant?” he asked.", "\"차라리, 그 아이가 어리석게도 못생긴 데다 한낱 하인 신세라고 말하는 편이 낫지 않겠소?\""], ["“The falling in love with a gentleman of Mr.", "하고 그가 물었다."], ["Franklin Blake’s manners and appearance doesn’t seem to me to be the maddest part of her conduct by any means.", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 도련님 같은 몸가짐과 풍채를 지닌 신사에게 반한 것이, 내가 보기엔 그 아이의 처신 중 결코 가장 어리석은 대목은 아닌 듯싶소."], ["However, I’m glad the thing is cleared up: it relieves one’s mind to have things cleared up.", "아무튼, 이 일이 밝혀져서 다행이오. 일이란 밝혀지고 나면 마음이 놓이는 법이지."], ["Yes, I’ll keep it a secret, Mr. Betteredge.", "그렇소, 이건 비밀로 해 두리다, 베터리지 양반."], ["I like to be tender to human infirmity—though I don’t get many chances of exercising that virtue in my line of life.", "나는 사람의 약함에 너그럽고 싶은 사람이오—하기야 우리네 직업에서 그 덕을 발휘할 기회가 그리 많지는 않소만."], ["You think Mr. Franklin Blake hasn’t got a suspicion of the girl’s fancy for him?", "당신은 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이 그 아이가 저를 마음에 둔 것을 눈치조차 못 챘다고 여기시오?"], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["he would have found it out fast enough if she had been nice-looking.", "그 아이가 곱상했더라면 그는 냉큼 알아챘을 거요."], ["The ugly women have a bad time of it in this world; let’s hope it will be made up to them in another.", "못생긴 여자들은 이 세상에서 고달픈 신세지—저세상에서라도 그 값을 치러 받기를 바라 봅시다."], ["You have got a nice garden here, and a well-kept lawn.", "여기 정원이 참 좋고, 잔디도 잘 손질돼 있구려."], ["See for yourself how much better the flowers look with grass about them instead of gravel.", "자갈 대신 잔디를 둘러 놓으니 꽃들이 얼마나 더 고와 보이는지 당신 눈으로 직접 보시오."], ["No, thank you.", "아니, 됐소."], ["I won’t take a rose.", "나는 장미를 꺾지 않으리다."], ["It goes to my heart to break them off the stem.", "그것을 줄기에서 꺾어 내는 것은 내 마음을 아프게 하니 말이오."], ["Just as it goes to your heart, you know, when there’s something wrong in the servants’ hall.", "마치, 아시다시피, 하인 방에서 무언가 잘못된 일이 있을 때 당신 마음이 아픈 것처럼 말이오."], ["Did you notice anything you couldn’t account for in any of the servants when the loss of the Diamond was first found out?”", "다이아몬드가 없어진 것이 처음 밝혀졌을 때, 하인들 가운데 누구에게서든 도무지 까닭을 알 수 없는 무언가를 눈치챈 것이 있소?\""]], [["I had got on very fairly well with Sergeant Cuff so far.", "여기까지는 나도 커프 부장과 제법 무난히 지내 왔다."], ["But the slyness with which he slipped in that last question put me on my guard.", "그러나 그가 그 마지막 물음을 슬며시 끼워 넣는 그 능청스러움이 나를 경계하게 만들었다."], ["In plain English, I didn’t at all relish the notion of helping his inquiries, when those inquiries took him (in the capacity of snake in the grass) among my fellow-servants.", "쉬운 말로 하자면, 나는 그의 조사를 돕는다는 생각이 도무지 내키지 않았다. 그 조사가 그를 (풀숲에 도사린 뱀의 자격으로) 내 동료 하인들 틈으로 끌고 들어갈 때는 말이다."]], [["“I noticed nothing,”", "\"저는 아무것도 눈치채지 못했습니다.\""], ["I said, “except that we all lost our heads together, myself included.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"우리 모두가, 저까지 포함해서, 한꺼번에 넋을 잃은 것 말고는요.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"허,\""], ["says the Sergeant, “that’s all you have to tell me, is it?”", "하고 부장이 말한다. \"내게 일러 줄 것이 그게 전부요?\""]], [["I answered, with (as I flattered myself) an unmoved countenance, “That is all.”", "나는 (스스로 우쭐하기로는) 태연한 낯빛으로 대답했다. \"그게 전부입니다.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff’s dismal eyes looked me hard in the face.", "커프 부장의 그 음침한 눈이 내 얼굴을 뚫어져라 들여다보았다."]], [["“Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지 양반,\""], ["he said, “have you any objection to oblige me by shaking hands?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"미안하지만 나와 악수 한번 해 주지 않겠소?"], ["I have taken an extraordinary liking to you.”", "나는 당신이 유별나게 마음에 드오.\""]], [["(Why he should have chosen the exact moment when I was deceiving him to give me that proof of his good opinion, is beyond all comprehension!", "(하필 내가 그를 속이고 있던 바로 그 순간을 골라, 나를 좋게 본다는 그런 증거를 내게 보이다니, 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 노릇이다!"], ["I felt a little proud—I really did feel a little proud of having been one too many at last for the celebrated Cuff!)", "나는 조금 우쭐했다—마침내 저 이름난 커프에게 한 수 앞섰다는 생각에 정말이지 조금 우쭐했다!)"]], [["We went back to the house; the Sergeant requesting that I would give him a room to himself, and then send in the servants (the indoor servants only), one after another, in the order of their rank, from first to last.", "우리는 집으로 돌아갔다. 부장은 제게 방 하나를 따로 내어 주고, 그런 다음 하인들을 (집 안 하인들만) 지위 순서대로, 위에서부터 아래까지, 하나씩 차례로 들여보내 달라고 청했다."]], [["I showed Sergeant Cuff into my own room, and then called the servants together in the hall.", "나는 커프 부장을 내 방으로 안내했고, 그런 다음 하인들을 큰방에 불러 모았다."], ["Rosanna Spearman appeared among them, much as usual.", "로재나 스피어먼도 그들 틈에 여느 때와 별반 다름없는 모습으로 나타났다."], ["She was as quick in her way as the Sergeant in his, and I suspect she had heard what he said to me about the servants in general, just before he discovered her.", "그는 제 나름대로 부장이 제 나름으로 그러하듯 눈치가 빨랐고, 짐작건대 부장이 그를 발견하기 바로 전에 하인들 전반에 관해 내게 한 말을 엿들었던 것 같다."], ["There she was, at any rate, looking as if she had never heard of such a place as the shrubbery in her life.", "아무튼 그는 거기, 마치 평생 관목 숲이라는 데가 있다는 소리조차 들어 본 적 없는 얼굴로 서 있었다."]], [["I sent them in, one by one, as desired.", "나는 청한 대로 그들을 하나씩 들여보냈다."], ["The cook was the first to enter the Court of Justice, otherwise my room.", "요리사가 그 재판정, 다시 말해 내 방에 맨 먼저 들어갔다."], ["She remained but a short time.", "그는 잠깐 머물렀을 뿐이다."], ["Report, on coming out: “Sergeant Cuff is depressed in his spirits; but Sergeant Cuff is a perfect gentleman.”", "나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장은 기가 죽어 있긴 하지만, 완벽한 신사이십니다.\""], ["My lady’s own maid followed.", "다음은 마님의 몸종이 뒤를 이었다."], ["Remained much longer.", "훨씬 오래 머물렀다."], ["Report, on coming out: “If Sergeant Cuff doesn’t believe a respectable woman, he might keep his opinion to himself, at any rate!”", "나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장이 점잖은 여자를 못 믿겠다면, 하다못해 그 속내는 혼자 담아 두시는 게 좋겠어요!\""], ["Penelope went next.", "다음은 페넬로피가 들어갔다."], ["Remained only a moment or two.", "겨우 한두 순간 머물렀다."], ["Report, on coming out: “Sergeant Cuff is much to be pitied.", "나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장은 참 딱한 분이에요."], ["He must have been crossed in love, father, when he was a young man.”", "아버지, 저분은 젊었을 적에 사랑에 실패하셨던 게 틀림없어요.\""], ["The first housemaid followed Penelope.", "첫째 하녀가 페넬로피의 뒤를 이었다."], ["Remained, like my lady’s maid, a long time.", "마님의 몸종처럼, 오래 머물렀다. 나오면서 하는 보고."], ["Report, on coming out: “I didn’t enter her ladyship’s service, Mr. Betteredge, to be doubted to my face by a low police-officer!”", "\"저는 한낱 하급 경찰관에게 대놓고 의심받으려고 마님 밑에 들어온 게 아니에요, 베터리지 씨!\""], ["Rosanna Spearman went next.", "다음은 로재나 스피어먼이 들어갔다."], ["Remained longer than any of them.", "그들 누구보다도 오래 머물렀다."], ["No report on coming out—dead silence, and lips as pale as ashes.", "나오면서 아무 보고도 없었다—죽은 듯한 침묵과, 잿빛처럼 파리한 입술뿐이었다."], ["Samuel, the footman, followed Rosanna.", "하인 새뮤얼이 로재나의 뒤를 이었다."], ["Remained a minute or two.", "한두 순간 머물렀다."], ["Report, on coming out: “Whoever blacks Sergeant Cuff’s boots ought to be ashamed of himself.”", "나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장의 구두를 닦는 사람이 누구든 제 신세를 부끄러워해야 마땅합니다.\""], ["Nancy, the kitchen-maid, went last.", "부엌데기 낸시가 맨 마지막으로 들어갔다."], ["Remained a minute or two.", "한두 순간 머물렀다."], ["Report, on coming out: “Sergeant Cuff has a heart; he doesn’t cut jokes, Mr.", "나오면서 하는 보고. \"커프 부장은 마음이 따뜻한 분이에요."], ["Betteredge, with a poor hard-working girl.”", "저분은, 베터리지 씨, 고되게 일하는 가엾은 계집아이를 두고 농담 따위 하지 않으세요.\""]], [["Going into the Court of Justice, when it was all over, to hear if there were any further commands for me, I found the Sergeant at his old trick—looking out of window, and whistling “The Last Rose of Summer” to himself.", "일이 죄다 끝난 뒤, 내게 더 이를 분부가 있는지 들으러 그 재판정에 들어가 보니, 부장이 그 옛 버릇대로—창밖을 내다보며 혼자 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 휘파람으로 불고 있었다."]], [["“Any discoveries, sir?”", "\"뭐 좀 알아내셨습니까, 나리?\""], ["I inquired.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“If Rosanna Spearman asks leave to go out,”", "\"로재나 스피어먼이 밖에 나가게 해 달라 청하면,\" 하고 부장이 말했다."], ["said the Sergeant, “let the poor thing go; but let me know first.”", "\"그 딱한 것을 내보내 주시오. 다만 먼저 내게 알려 주고.\""]], [["I might as well have held my tongue about Rosanna and Mr.", "로재나와 프랭클린 도련님 이야기 따위, 차라리 입을 다물고 있을 것을!"], ["Franklin!", "이제 뻔했다."], ["It was plain enough; the unfortunate girl had fallen under Sergeant Cuff’s suspicions, in spite of all I could do to prevent it.", "그 불행한 아이는, 그것을 막으려고 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 짓을 다 했건만, 기어이 커프 부장의 의심 아래 떨어지고 만 것이다."]], [["“I hope you don’t think Rosanna is concerned in the loss of the Diamond?”", "\"설마 로재나가 다이아몬드 실종에 얽혀 있다고 여기시는 건 아니겠지요?\""], ["I ventured to say.", "하고 나는 조심스레 말을 꺼냈다."]], [["The corners of the Sergeant’s melancholy mouth curled up, and he looked hard in my face, just as he had looked in the garden.", "부장의 그 우울한 입아귀가 말려 올라갔고, 그는, 정원에서 그러했던 것과 똑같이, 내 얼굴을 뚫어져라 들여다보았다."]], [["“I think I had better not tell you, Mr.", "\"당신에게는 말하지 않는 편이 나을 것 같소, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["Betteredge,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “You might lose your head, you know, for the second time.”", "\"당신은, 아시다시피, 두 번째로 또 넋을 잃을지도 모르니 말이오.\""]], [["I began to doubt whether I had been one too many for the celebrated Cuff, after all!", "나는 과연 내가 저 이름난 커프에게 한 수 앞섰던 것인지 새삼 의심스러워지기 시작했다!"], ["It was rather a relief to me that we were interrupted here by a knock at the door, and a message from the cook.", "여기서 문 두드리는 소리와 요리사가 전하는 전갈에 우리 이야기가 끊긴 것이, 나로서는 오히려 다행이었다."], ["Rosanna Spearman had asked to go out, for the usual reason, that her head was bad, and she wanted a breath of fresh air.", "로재나 스피어먼이, 여느 때와 같은 이유로, 곧 머리가 아프고 바깥바람을 좀 쐬고 싶다며, 밖에 나가게 해 달라 청했던 것이다."], ["At a sign from the Sergeant, I said, Yes.", "부장이 눈짓하기에, 나는 그러라고 했다."], ["“Which is the servants’ way out?”", "\"하인들이 드나드는 문이 어느 쪽이오?\""], ["he asked, when the messenger had gone.", "하고 그가 심부름꾼이 가고 난 뒤에 물었다."], ["I showed him the servants’ way out.", "나는 그에게 하인들이 드나드는 문을 일러 주었다."], ["“Lock the door of your room,”", "\"당신 방문을 잠가 두시오.\""], ["says the Sergeant; “and if anybody asks for me, say I’m in there, composing my mind.”", "하고 부장이 말한다. \"그리고 누가 나를 찾거든, 내가 그 안에서 마음을 가다듬고 있다고 하시오.\""], ["He curled up again at the corners of the lips, and disappeared.", "그는 입아귀를 다시 말아 올리고는, 사라졌다."]], [["Left alone, under those circumstances, a devouring curiosity pushed me on to make some discoveries for myself.", "그런 처지에 혼자 남겨지니, 좀이 쑤시는 호기심이 나를 부추겨 나 스스로 무언가를 알아내 보게끔 몰아갔다."]], [["It was plain that Sergeant Cuff’s suspicions of Rosanna had been roused by something that he had found out at his examination of the servants in my room.", "커프 부장의 로재나에 대한 의심이, 내 방에서 하인들을 신문하는 동안 그가 알아낸 무언가에서 비롯되었음은 뻔했다."], ["Now, the only two servants (excepting Rosanna herself) who had remained under examination for any length of time, were my lady’s own maid and the first housemaid, those two being also the women who had taken the lead in persecuting their unfortunate fellow-servant from the first.", "그런데 (로재나 자신을 빼고) 얼마쯤 긴 시간 신문 아래 머문 하인은 오직 둘뿐이었으니, 곧 마님의 몸종과 첫째 하녀였다. 그리고 이 둘은 또한, 처음부터 그 불행한 동료 하인을 못살게 구는 데 앞장선 여자들이기도 했다."], ["Reaching these conclusions, I looked in on them, casually as it might be, in the servants’ hall, and, finding tea going forward, instantly invited myself to that meal.", "이런 결론에 다다른 나는, 짐짓 별일 아닌 척, 하인 방에 있는 그들을 들여다보았고, 마침 차 시간이 벌어지고 있는 것을 보고, 냉큼 그 자리에 나 자신을 끼워 넣었다."], ["(For, nota bene, a drop of tea is to a woman’s tongue what a drop of oil is to a wasting lamp.)", "(왜냐하면, 눈여겨 두시라, 차 한 방울이 여자의 혀에 하는 노릇은 기름 한 방울이 사위어 가는 등잔에 하는 노릇과 같으니 말이다.)"]], [["My reliance on the tea-pot, as an ally, did not go unrewarded.", "동맹군으로서의 찻주전자에 건 내 기대는 헛되지 않았다."], ["In less than half an hour I knew as much as the Sergeant himself.", "반 시간도 채 안 되어 나는 부장 자신만큼이나 많은 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["My lady’s maid and the housemaid, had, it appeared, neither of them believed in Rosanna’s illness of the previous day.", "마님의 몸종과 하녀는, 알고 보니, 둘 다 전날 로재나가 앓았다는 것을 믿지 않았다."], ["These two devils—I ask your pardon; but how else can you describe a couple of spiteful women?—had stolen upstairs, at intervals during the Thursday afternoon; had tried Rosanna’s door, and found it locked; had knocked, and not been answered; had listened, and not heard a sound inside.", "이 두 악마는—용서를 구하나, 심술궂은 두 여자를 달리 어찌 이르겠는가?—목요일 오후 동안 틈틈이 위층으로 살금살금 올라가, 로재나의 문을 밀어 보고는 잠겨 있음을 알아냈고, 두드려도 대답이 없었으며, 귀 기울여도 안에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않았다."], ["When the girl had come down to tea, and had been sent up, still out of sorts, to bed again, the two devils aforesaid had tried her door once more, and found it locked; had looked at the keyhole, and found it stopped up; had seen a light under the door at midnight, and had heard the crackling of a fire (a fire in a servant’s bedroom in the month of June!) at four in the morning.", "그 아이가 차를 마시러 내려왔다가, 여전히 몸이 안 좋아 다시 잠자리에 들라고 올려 보내진 뒤, 앞서 말한 그 두 악마는 그 문을 다시 한번 밀어 보고 잠겨 있음을 알아냈고, 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보고는 그것이 틀어막혀 있음을 알아냈으며, 자정에 문 아래로 새어 나오는 불빛을 보았고, 새벽 네 시에는 불이 타닥거리는 소리를 (유월에 하인 침실에 불이라니!) 들었다."], ["All this they had told Sergeant Cuff, who, in return for their anxiety to enlighten him, had eyed them with sour and suspicious looks, and had shown them plainly that he didn’t believe either one or the other.", "이 모든 것을 그들은 커프 부장에게 일러바쳤는데, 부장은 저를 깨우쳐 주려는 그들의 안달에 대한 보답으로, 시큰둥하고 미심쩍은 눈길로 그들을 훑어보며, 이쪽도 저쪽도 도무지 믿지 않는다는 것을 그들에게 뻔히 내보였다."], ["Hence, the unfavourable reports of him which these two women had brought out with them from the examination.", "그리하여, 이 두 여자가 신문을 마치고 나오며 내놓은 그에 대한 그 언짢은 보고가 나온 것이다."], ["Hence, also (without reckoning the influence of the tea-pot), their readiness to let their tongues run to any length on the subject of the Sergeant’s ungracious behaviour to them.", "그리하여, 또한 (찻주전자의 영향력은 셈에 넣지 않더라도) 그들에게 부장이 보인 그 무례한 처사를 두고 실컷 혀를 놀려 대는 그 신바람도 나온 것이다."]], [["Having had some experience of the great Cuff’s roundabout ways, and having last seen him evidently bent on following Rosanna privately when she went out for her walk, it seemed clear to me that he had thought it unadvisable to let the lady’s maid and the housemaid know how materially they had helped him.", "저 위대한 커프의 에두르는 수법을 얼마쯤 겪어 본 데다, 로재나가 산책하러 나갈 때 그를 남몰래 뒤밟을 작정임이 뚜렷한 그를 마지막으로 본 터라, 그가 몸종과 하녀에게 그들이 얼마나 결정적으로 저를 도왔는지 알리는 것을 마땅치 않게 여겼음이 내게는 분명해 보였다."], ["They were just the sort of women, if he had treated their evidence as trustworthy, to have been puffed up by it, and to have said or done something which would have put Rosanna Spearman on her guard.", "만일 그가 그들의 증언을 미덥게 여기는 척했다면, 그들은 그것에 우쭐하여 로재나 스피어먼이 경계하게끔 만들 무슨 말이나 짓을 하고도 남을, 딱 그런 부류의 여자들이었으니 말이다."]], [["I walked out in the fine summer afternoon, very sorry for the poor girl, and very uneasy in my mind at the turn things had taken.", "나는 그 화창한 여름 오후에 밖으로 나갔다. 그 딱한 아이가 몹시 가엾고, 일이 흘러가는 판국에 마음이 몹시 편치 않은 채로였다."], ["Drifting towards the shrubbery, some time later, there I met Mr. Franklin.", "얼마쯤 뒤 관목 숲 쪽으로 발길이 흘러갔는데, 거기서 나는 프랭클린 도련님을 만났다."], ["After returning from seeing his cousin off at the station, he had been with my lady, holding a long conversation with her.", "역에서 제 사촌을 배웅하고 돌아온 뒤, 그는 마님과 함께 오래도록 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["She had told him of Miss Rachel’s unaccountable refusal to let her wardrobe be examined; and had put him in such low spirits about my young lady that he seemed to shrink from speaking on the subject.", "마님은 레이철 아가씨가 도무지 까닭 모를 이유로 제 옷장을 살펴보게 하기를 거절한 일을 그에게 이르셨고, 그리하여 그를 우리 아가씨에 대해 어찌나 풀 죽게 만드셨던지 그는 그 이야기를 꺼내는 것조차 꺼리는 듯했다."], ["The family temper appeared in his face that evening, for the first time in my experience of him.", "그 집안 특유의 성미가 그날 저녁 그의 얼굴에 처음으로—내가 그를 겪어 온 이래 처음으로—드러났다."]], [["“Well, Betteredge,”", "\"저런, 베터리지,\""], ["he said, “how does the atmosphere of mystery and suspicion in which we are all living now, agree with you?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"지금 우리 모두가 그 속에서 살아가고 있는 이 수수께끼와 의심의 공기가, 자네한테는 어떤가?"], ["Do you remember that morning when I first came here with the Moonstone?", "내가 월장석을 안고 여기 처음 왔던 그 아침을 기억하나?"], ["I wish to God we had thrown it into the quicksand!”", "맙소사, 우리가 그것을 그 유사(流沙) 속에 던져 버렸더라면 좋았을 것을!\""]], [["After breaking out in that way, he abstained from speaking again until he had composed himself.", "그런 식으로 터뜨리고 나서, 그는 마음을 가라앉힐 때까지 다시 입을 열지 않았다."], ["We walked silently, side by side, for a minute or two, and then he asked me what had become of Sergeant Cuff.", "우리는 나란히, 한두 순간 말없이 걸었고, 그런 다음 그는 내게 커프 부장이 어찌 되었느냐고 물었다."], ["It was impossible to put Mr. Franklin off with the excuse of the Sergeant being in my room, composing his mind.", "프랭클린 도련님을 부장이 내 방에서 마음을 가다듬고 있다는 핑계로 따돌릴 수는 없는 노릇이었다."], ["I told him exactly what had happened, mentioning particularly what my lady’s maid and the house-maid had said about Rosanna Spearman.", "나는 그에게 일어난 일을 있는 그대로 일러 주었는데, 특히 마님의 몸종과 하녀가 로재나 스피어먼에 대해 한 말을 짚어 이야기했다."]], [["Mr. Franklin’s clear head saw the turn the Sergeant’s suspicions had taken, in the twinkling of an eye.", "프랭클린 도련님의 명석한 머리는, 눈 깜짝할 사이에 부장의 의심이 향한 방향을 알아보았다."]], [["“Didn’t you tell me this morning,”", "\"자네, 오늘 아침에 내게 말하지 않았나,\""], ["he said, “that one of the tradespeople declared he had met Rosanna yesterday, on the footway to Frizinghall, when we supposed her to be ill in her room?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"우리가 로재나를 제 방에서 앓고 있으려니 여기던 어제, 그를 프리징홀로 가는 오솔길에서 만났노라고 어느 장사꾼이 딱 잘라 말하더라고 말이야?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"예, 그렇습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“If my aunt’s maid and the other woman have spoken the truth, you may depend upon it the tradesman did meet her.", "\"내 이모님의 몸종과 그 다른 여자가 사실을 말한 것이라면, 그 장사꾼이 그를 만난 것이 틀림없다고 믿어도 좋아."], ["The girl’s attack of illness was a blind to deceive us.", "그 아이가 앓았던 것은 우리를 속이려는 눈속임이었어."], ["She had some guilty reason for going to the town secretly.", "그는 무언가 켕기는 이유가 있어 몰래 시내로 갔던 거야."], ["The paint-stained dress is a dress of hers; and the fire heard crackling in her room at four in the morning was a fire lit to destroy it.", "그 페인트 얼룩진 옷은 그의 옷이고, 새벽 네 시에 그 방에서 타닥거리는 소리가 들렸다는 그 불은 그것을 태워 없애려고 지핀 불이야."], ["Rosanna Spearman has stolen the Diamond.", "로재나 스피어먼이 그 다이아몬드를 훔친 거야."], ["I’ll go in directly, and tell my aunt the turn things have taken.”", "내 곧장 들어가서, 이모님께 일이 이렇게 돌아갔다고 아뢰야겠어.\""]], [["“Not just yet, if you please, sir,”", "\"괜찮으시다면, 아직은 하지 마시지요, 나리.\""], ["said a melancholy voice behind us.", "하고 우리 등 뒤에서 우울한 목소리가 말했다."]], [["We both turned about, and found ourselves face to face with Sergeant Cuff.", "우리 둘 다 홱 돌아섰고, 커프 부장과 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다."]], [["“Why not just yet?”", "\"어째서 아직은 안 된다는 거요?\""], ["asked Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 물었다."]], [["“Because, sir, if you tell her ladyship, her ladyship will tell Miss Verinder.”", "\"왜냐하면, 나리, 나리가 마님께 아뢰면, 마님은 버린더 아가씨께 이르실 테니까요.\""]], [["“Suppose she does.", "\"이르신다 칩시다."], ["What then?” Mr.", "그러면 어떻단 말이오?\""], ["Franklin said those words with a sudden heat and vehemence, as if the Sergeant had mortally offended him.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 마치 부장이 자기를 죽도록 모욕하기라도 한 듯, 별안간 발끈 성을 내어 그 말을 내뱉었다."]], [["“Do you think it’s wise, sir,”", "\"나리, 그것이 현명한 일이라고 여기십니까,\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, quietly, “to put such a question as that to me—at such a time as this?”", "하고 커프 부장이 나직이 말했다. \"지금 이런 마당에—내게 그런 물음을 던지시는 것이 말입니다?\""]], [["There was a moment’s silence between them: Mr.", "두 사람 사이에 한순간 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Franklin walked close up to the Sergeant.", "프랭클린 도련님이 부장에게 바짝 다가섰다."], ["The two looked each other straight in the face.", "두 사람은 서로의 얼굴을 똑바로 마주 보았다."], ["Mr. Franklin spoke first, dropping his voice as suddenly as he had raised it.", "프랭클린 도련님이, 목소리를 높였을 때만큼이나 별안간 그것을 낮추며, 먼저 입을 열었다."]], [["“I suppose you know, Mr. Cuff,”", "\"짐작건대 당신도 아시겠지, 커프 씨,\""], ["he said, “that you are treading on delicate ground?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"지금 당신이 아슬아슬한 자리를 밟고 있다는 것을 말이오?\""]], [["“It isn’t the first time, by a good many hundreds, that I find myself treading on delicate ground,”", "\"내가 아슬아슬한 자리를 밟고 있음을 깨닫는 것이, 이번이 처음은 아니오—몇백 번은 족히 되지.\""], ["answered the other, as immovable as ever.", "하고 상대가, 여전히 꿈쩍도 않고 대답했다."]], [["“I am to understand that you forbid me to tell my aunt what has happened?”", "\"그러니까 당신은, 무슨 일이 있었는지 이모님께 이르는 것을 내게 금하는 거요?\""]], [["“You are to understand, if you please, sir, that I throw up the case, if you tell Lady Verinder, or tell anybody, what has happened, until I give you leave.”", "\"괜찮으시다면 이렇게 알아 두시지요, 나리. 만일 나리가, 내가 허락하기 전에, 버린더 마님께든 그 누구에게든, 무슨 일이 있었는지 이른다면, 나는 이 사건을 손 떼겠습니다.\""]], [["That settled it.", "그것으로 판가름이 났다."], ["Mr. Franklin had no choice but to submit.", "프랭클린 도련님으로서는 따르는 수밖에 도리가 없었다."], ["He turned away in anger—and left us.", "그는 성이 나서 홱 돌아서더니—우리를 두고 가 버렸다."]], [["I had stood there listening to them, all in a tremble; not knowing whom to suspect, or what to think next.", "나는 온몸을 떨며 거기 서서 그들의 말을 듣고 있었다—누구를 의심해야 할지, 다음엔 무엇을 생각해야 할지도 모른 채로."], ["In the midst of my confusion, two things, however, were plain to me.", "그러나 내 혼란의 한복판에서도, 두 가지는 내게 뚜렷했다."], ["First, that my young lady was, in some unaccountable manner, at the bottom of the sharp speeches that had passed between them.", "첫째, 우리 아가씨가, 도무지 알 수 없는 어떤 식으로, 그들 사이에 오간 그 날 선 말다툼의 밑바닥에 있다는 것."], ["Second, that they thoroughly understood each other, without having previously exchanged a word of explanation on either side.", "둘째, 그들이, 앞서 어느 쪽도 설명이랄 것을 한마디도 주고받은 적 없이, 서로를 속속들이 알아듣고 있다는 것."]], [["“Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지 양반,\""], ["says the Sergeant, “you have done a very foolish thing in my absence.", "하고 부장이 말한다. \"당신은 내가 없는 동안 아주 어리석은 짓을 저질렀소."], ["You have done a little detective business on your own account.", "당신 딴에 조그만 탐정 노릇을 좀 한 거요."], ["For the future, perhaps you will be so obliging as to do your detective business along with me.”", "앞으로는, 부디, 당신 탐정 노릇을 나와 함께해 주는 아량을 베풀어 주시겠소.\""]], [["He took me by the arm, and walked me away with him along the road by which he had come.", "그는 내 팔을 잡고, 제가 왔던 길을 따라 나를 데리고 걸었다."], ["I dare say I had deserved his reproof—but I was not going to help him to set traps for Rosanna Spearman, for all that.", "그의 나무람을 들어 마땅했을지도 모른다—그렇다 해도, 나는 로재나 스피어먼에게 덫을 놓는 일에 그를 도울 생각은 없었다."], ["Thief or no thief, legal or not legal, I don’t care—I pitied her.", "도둑이든 아니든, 법에 걸리든 안 걸리든, 나는 아랑곳없다—나는 그가 가여웠다."]], [["“What do you want of me?”", "\"제게 무얼 바라시는 겁니까?\""], ["I asked, shaking him off, and stopping short.", "하고 나는 그를 뿌리치고 우뚝 멈춰 서며 물었다."]], [["“Only a little information about the country round here,”", "\"이 근방 고장에 대해 얼마쯤 알고 싶을 뿐이오.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."]], [["I couldn’t well object to improve Sergeant Cuff in his geography.", "커프 부장의 지리 지식을 넓혀 주는 것을 굳이 마다할 수도 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["“Is there any path, in that direction, leading to the sea-beach from this house?”", "\"저쪽 방향으로, 이 집에서 바닷가로 이어지는 길이 어디 있소?\""], ["asked the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 물었다."], ["He pointed, as he spoke, to the fir-plantation which led to the Shivering Sand.", "그는 말하면서, 그 흔들리는 모래로 이어지는 전나무 숲을 가리켰다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"예,\""], ["I said, “there is a path.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"길이 하나 있습니다.\""]], [["“Show it to me.”", "\"내게 보여 주시오.\""]], [["Side by side, in the grey of the summer evening, Sergeant Cuff and I set forth for the Shivering Sand.", "여름 저녁의 어스름 속에서, 나란히, 커프 부장과 나는 그 흔들리는 모래를 향해 길을 나섰다."]], [["# CHAPTER XV", "# CHAPTER XV"]], [["The Sergeant remained silent, thinking his own thoughts, till we entered the plantation of firs which led to the quicksand.", "부장은 저만의 생각에 잠긴 채, 우리가 그 유사(流沙)로 이어지는 전나무 숲에 들어설 때까지 잠자코 있었다."], ["There he roused himself, like a man whose mind was made up, and spoke to me again.", "거기서 그는, 마음을 굳힌 사람처럼 정신을 차리더니, 다시 내게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지 양반,\""], ["he said, “as you have honoured me by taking an oar in my boat, and as you may, I think, be of some assistance to me before the evening is out, I see no use in our mystifying one another any longer, and I propose to set you an example of plain speaking on my side.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 내 배에서 노 한 자루를 잡아 주는 영광을 베풀었고, 또 내 생각에 오늘 저녁이 다 가기 전에 당신이 내게 얼마쯤 도움이 될 성싶으니, 우리가 더 이상 서로를 헷갈리게 하는 것은 아무 소용이 없다고 보오."], ["You are determined to give me no information to the prejudice of Rosanna Spearman, because she has been a good girl to you, and because you pity her heartily.", "그러니 내 쪽에서는 당신에게 솔직히 털어놓는 본을 보이려 하오. 당신은 로재나 스피어먼에게 해가 될 정보는 내게 한 조각도 주지 않기로 마음을 굳혔소."], ["Those humane considerations do you a world of credit, but they happen in this instance to be humane considerations clean thrown away.", "그 아이가 당신에게는 착한 아이였고, 또 당신이 진심으로 그를 가여워하니 말이오. 그런 인정 어린 헤아림은 당신에게 이만저만한 명예가 아니오만, 이 경우에는 공교롭게도 그것이 헛되이 내던져진 인정 어린 헤아림이 되고 말았소."], ["Rosanna Spearman is not in the slightest danger of getting into trouble—no, not if I fix her with being concerned in the disappearance of the Diamond, on evidence which is as plain as the nose on your face!”", "로재나 스피어먼이 곤경에 빠질 위험은 조금도 없소—그렇소, 내가 그 아이를, 당신 코가 얼굴에 붙어 있는 것만큼이나 뻔한 증거로, 다이아몬드 실종에 연루된 자로 못 박는다 해도 말이오!\""]], [["“Do you mean that my lady won’t prosecute?”", "\"마님께서 그 아이를 고발하지 않으시리라는 말씀입니까?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“I mean that your lady can’t prosecute,”", "\"마님께서 고발하실 수가 없다는 말이오.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“Rosanna Spearman is simply an instrument in the hands of another person, and Rosanna Spearman will be held harmless for that other person’s sake.”", "\"로재나 스피어먼은 그저 다른 사람 손에 쥐인 도구일 뿐이고, 로재나 스피어먼은 그 다른 사람을 위해 화를 면하게 될 것이오.\""]], [["He spoke like a man in earnest—there was no denying that.", "그는 진심인 사람처럼 말했다—그것은 부인할 수 없었다."], ["Still, I felt something stirring uneasily against him in my mind.", "그래도, 내 마음속에서는 무언가가 그를 향해 께름칙하게 꿈틀거리는 것이 느껴졌다."], ["“Can’t you give that other person a name?”", "\"그 다른 사람에게 이름을 붙여 주실 수는 없습니까?\""], ["I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."]], [["“Can’t you, Mr. Betteredge?”", "\"당신은 못 붙이겠소, 베터리지 양반?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"못 붙입니다.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff stood stock-still, and surveyed me with a look of melancholy interest.", "커프 부장은 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 우울한 흥미가 어린 눈길로 나를 찬찬히 훑어보았다."]], [["“It’s always a pleasure to me to be tender towards human infirmity,”", "\"사람의 약함에 너그럽게 구는 것은 내게는 늘 즐거운 일이오.\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “I feel particularly tender at the present moment, Mr.", "\"지금 이 순간, 나는 당신에게 유별나게 너그러운 마음이 드는구려, 베터리지 양반."], ["Betteredge, towards you. And you, with the same excellent motive, feel particularly tender towards Rosanna Spearman, don’t you?", "그리고 당신은, 그와 똑같은 갸륵한 동기에서, 로재나 스피어먼에게 유별나게 너그러운 마음이 들지, 그렇지 않소?"], ["Do you happen to know whether she has had a new outfit of linen lately?”", "혹 그 아이가 요즘 아마포 옷 한 벌을 새로 장만한 적이 있는지 아시오?\""]], [["What he meant by slipping in this extraordinary question unawares, I was at a total loss to imagine.", "이 뜬금없는 물음을 슬며시 끼워 넣은 그의 속뜻이 무엇인지, 나는 도무지 짐작할 길이 없었다."], ["Seeing no possible injury to Rosanna if I owned the truth, I answered that the girl had come to us rather sparely provided with linen, and that my lady, in recompense for her good conduct (I laid a stress on her good conduct), had given her a new outfit not a fortnight since.", "사실대로 밝힌들 로재나에게 해가 될 리 없다고 보아, 나는 그 아이가 아마포 옷을 제법 변변찮게 갖춘 채 우리에게 왔으며, 마님이 그 착실한 품행에 대한 보답으로 (나는 그 착실한 품행에 힘을 주어 말했다) 채 보름도 안 되어 새 옷 한 벌을 내려 주셨다고 대답했다."]], [["“This is a miserable world,”", "\"참으로 서글픈 세상이오.\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."], ["“Human life, Mr. Betteredge, is a sort of target—misfortune is always firing at it, and always hitting the mark.", "\"사람의 목숨이란, 베터리지 양반, 일종의 과녁 같은 것이지—불운이 늘 그것을 향해 쏘아 대고, 늘 명중시키니 말이오."], ["But for that outfit, we should have discovered a new nightgown or petticoat among Rosanna’s things, and have nailed her in that way.", "그 새 옷만 아니었다면, 우리는 로재나의 물건 틈에서 새 잠옷이나 속치마를 발견하고, 그런 식으로 그 아이를 못 박았을 거요."], ["You’re not at a loss to follow me, are you?", "내 말을 못 따라오는 건 아니겠지?"], ["You have examined the servants yourself, and you know what discoveries two of them made outside Rosanna’s door.", "당신은 손수 하인들을 신문했으니, 그중 둘이 로재나의 문밖에서 무엇을 알아냈는지 알고 있소."], ["Surely you know what the girl was about yesterday, after she was taken ill?", "그러니 그 아이가 몸져누운 뒤 어제 무슨 짓을 했는지도 분명 알겠지?"], ["You can’t guess?", "짐작이 안 가시오?"], ["Oh dear me, it’s as plain as that strip of light there, at the end of the trees.", "아이고 저런, 저기 나무들 끝에 비치는 저 한 줄기 빛만큼이나 뻔한 일이오."], ["At eleven, on Thursday morning, Superintendent Seegrave (who is a mass of human infirmity) points out to all the women servants the smear on the door.", "목요일 아침 열한 시에, (사람의 약함이 뭉쳐진 덩어리 같은) 시그레이브 경정이 여자 하인들 모두에게 그 문의 얼룩을 가리켜 보였소."], ["Rosanna has her own reasons for suspecting her own things; she takes the first opportunity of getting to her room, finds the paint-stain on her night-gown, or petticoat, or what not, shams ill and slips away to the town, gets the materials for making a new petticoat or nightgown, makes it alone in her room on the Thursday night, lights a fire (not to destroy it; two of her fellow-servants are prying outside her door, and she knows better than to make a smell of burning, and to have a lot of tinder to get rid of)—lights a fire, I say, to dry and iron the substitute dress after wringing it out, keeps the stained dress hidden (probably on her), and is at this moment occupied in making away with it, in some convenient place, on that lonely bit of beach ahead of us.", "로재나는 제 물건을 의심할 저만의 이유가 있었고, 제 방으로 갈 첫 기회를 붙잡아, 제 잠옷이나 속치마나 뭐 그런 것에서 페인트 얼룩을 발견하고는, 앓는 시늉을 하며 슬며시 시내로 빠져나가, 새 속치마나 잠옷을 지을 감을 구해, 목요일 밤 제 방에서 혼자 그것을 짓고, 불을 지폈소 (그것을 태우려는 게 아니오. 제 동료 하인 둘이 문밖에서 엿보고 있었으니, 타는 냄새를 피우고 없애야 할 잿더미를 잔뜩 남기는 어리석은 짓은 안 할 만큼 그 아이는 영리했지)—불을 지폈다는 것은, 짜낸 대체 옷을 말리고 다림질하려던 것이고, 그 얼룩진 옷은 (아마도 제 몸에) 숨겨 둔 채, 지금 이 순간 우리 앞 저 외진 바닷가 어딘가 편한 자리에서 그것을 없애느라 바쁜 것이오."], ["I have traced her this evening to your fishing village, and to one particular cottage, which we may possibly have to visit, before we go back.", "나는 오늘 저녁 그 아이를 당신네 어촌까지, 그리고 어느 한 오두막까지 뒤밟았는데, 그 오두막은 우리가 돌아가기 전에 어쩌면 들러야 할지도 모르오."], ["She stopped in the cottage for some time, and she came out with (as I believe) something hidden under her cloak.", "그 아이는 그 오두막에 얼마간 머물렀다가, (내가 믿기로는) 무언가를 제 망토 아래 감춘 채 나왔소."], ["A cloak (on a woman’s back) is an emblem of charity—it covers a multitude of sins.", "망토란 (여자의 등에 걸치면) 자비의 상징이지—숱한 죄를 덮어 주니까 말이오."], ["I saw her set off northwards along the coast, after leaving the cottage.", "나는 그 아이가 오두막을 나선 뒤 해안을 따라 북쪽으로 떠나는 것을 보았소."], ["Is your sea-shore here considered a fine specimen of marine landscape, Mr. Betteredge?”", "여기 당신네 바닷가는 훌륭한 해양 경관의 본보기로 쳐 주는 곳이오, 베터리지 양반?\""]], [["I answered, “Yes,”", "나는 될 수 있는 대로 짤막하게 \"예.\""], ["as shortly as might be.", "하고 대답했다."]], [["“Tastes differ,”", "\"취향이란 제각각이지.\""], ["says Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말한다."], ["“Looking at it from my point of view, I never saw a marine landscape that I admired less.", "\"내 관점에서 보자면, 이보다 덜 감탄스러운 해양 경관은 여태 본 적이 없소."], ["If you happen to be following another person along your sea-coast, and if that person happens to look round, there isn’t a scrap of cover to hide you anywhere.", "만일 당신이 이런 바닷가를 따라 어떤 사람을 뒤밟고 있는데, 그 사람이 마침 뒤를 돌아본다면, 당신을 숨겨 줄 가림막이라곤 어디에도 한 조각 없으니 말이오."], ["I had to choose between taking Rosanna in custody on suspicion, or leaving her, for the time being, with her little game in her own hands.", "나는 로재나를 혐의로 붙잡아 두느냐, 아니면 당분간 그 아이가 제 잔꾀를 제 손에 쥔 채로 내버려 두느냐, 그 둘 중 하나를 골라야 했소."], ["For reasons which I won’t trouble you with, I decided on making any sacrifice rather than give the alarm as soon as tonight to a certain person who shall be nameless between us.", "당신을 성가시게 하지 않으려고 이유는 접어 두겠소만, 나는 우리 사이에서 이름을 밝히지 않을 어떤 사람에게 당장 오늘 밤 경종을 울리느니, 차라리 어떤 희생이라도 치르는 편이 낫겠다고 마음먹었소."], ["I came back to the house to ask you to take me to the north end of the beach by another way.", "나는 당신에게 다른 길로 나를 바닷가 북쪽 끝까지 데려가 달라고 청하러 집으로 돌아왔던 거요."], ["Sand—in respect of its printing off people’s footsteps—is one of the best detective officers I know.", "모래란—사람의 발자국을 찍어 내는 점에서는—내가 아는 가장 뛰어난 형사 나리 중 하나요."], ["If we don’t meet with Rosanna Spearman by coming round on her in this way, the sand may tell us what she has been at, if the light only lasts long enough.", "이런 식으로 그 아이를 에둘러 덮쳐도 로재나 스피어먼과 마주치지 못한다면, 빛만 넉넉히 남아 있어 준다면, 그 모래가 그 아이가 무슨 짓을 했는지 우리에게 일러 줄지도 모르오."], ["Here is the sand.", "자, 여기가 그 모래요."], ["If you will excuse my suggesting it—suppose you hold your tongue, and let me go first?”", "내 이런 제안을 하는 것을 너그러이 봐 주신다면—당신은 입을 다물고, 내가 먼저 가게 해 주는 것이 어떻겠소?\""]], [["If there is such a thing known at the doctor’s shop as a detective-fever, that disease had now got fast hold of your humble servant.", "만일 의원(醫院)에 탐정 열병이라는 것이 알려져 있다면, 그 병이 이제 이 미천한 종을 단단히 사로잡은 셈이었다."], ["Sergeant Cuff went on between the hillocks of sand, down to the beach.", "커프 부장은 모래 언덕들 사이로 나아가, 바닷가로 내려갔다."], ["I followed him (with my heart in my mouth); and waited at a little distance for what was to happen next.", "나는 (가슴이 목구멍까지 치받쳐 오른 채) 그를 뒤따랐고, 조금 떨어진 자리에서 다음에 무슨 일이 벌어질지 기다렸다."]], [["As it turned out, I found myself standing nearly in the same place where Rosanna Spearman and I had been talking together when Mr. Franklin suddenly appeared before us, on arriving at our house from London.", "알고 보니, 나는 프랭클린 도련님이 런던에서 우리 집에 당도하여 우리 앞에 별안간 나타났을 때, 로재나 스피어먼과 내가 함께 이야기를 나누던 바로 그 자리 가까이에 서 있었다."], ["While my eyes were watching the Sergeant, my mind wandered away in spite of me to what had passed, on that former occasion, between Rosanna and me.", "내 눈은 부장을 지켜보고 있었으나, 내 마음은 나도 모르게 그 지난번에 로재나와 나 사이에 오갔던 일로 흘러갔다."], ["I declare I almost felt the poor thing slip her hand again into mine, and give it a little grateful squeeze to thank me for speaking kindly to her.", "단언컨대 나는 그 딱한 것이 다시 제 손을 내 손에 밀어 넣어, 제게 다정하게 말해 준 것을 고마워하며 살짝 감사의 뜻으로 쥐던 것을 거의 느끼는 듯했다."], ["I declare I almost heard her voice telling me again that the Shivering Sand seemed to draw her to it against her own will, whenever she went out—almost saw her face brighten again, as it brightened when she first set eyes upon Mr. Franklin coming briskly out on us from among the hillocks.", "단언컨대 나는 그 아이의 목소리가, 밖에 나갈 때마다 흔들리는 모래가 제 뜻과는 달리 저를 끌어당기는 것 같다고 다시 내게 말하는 것을 거의 듣는 듯했다—프랭클린 도련님이 언덕들 사이에서 우리 쪽으로 성큼 나오는 것을 처음 보았을 때 환해졌던 것처럼, 그 얼굴이 다시 환해지는 것을 거의 보는 듯했다."], ["My spirits fell lower and lower as I thought of these things—and the view of the lonesome little bay, when I looked about to rouse myself, only served to make me feel more uneasy still.", "이런 일들을 떠올리자 내 마음은 갈수록 가라앉았다—그리고 정신을 차리려 주위를 둘러보았을 때, 그 쓸쓸한 조그만 만(灣)의 풍경은 나를 한층 더 불안하게 만들 뿐이었다."]], [["The last of the evening light was fading away; and over all the desolate place there hung a still and awful calm.", "저녁 빛의 마지막 자락이 스러지고 있었다. 그리고 그 황량한 곳 전체 위로 고요하고 무시무시한 정적이 드리워 있었다."], ["The heave of the main ocean on the great sandbank out in the bay, was a heave that made no sound.", "만 저편 큰 모래톱에 부딪는 넓은 바다의 너울은, 아무 소리도 내지 않는 너울이었다."], ["The inner sea lay lost and dim, without a breath of wind to stir it.", "안쪽 바다는 그것을 흔들 바람 한 점 없이, 아득하고 흐릿하게 누워 있었다."], ["Patches of nasty ooze floated, yellow-white, on the dead surface of the water.", "역겨운 진창 덩어리들이, 누르스름한 흰빛으로, 죽은 듯한 물 표면에 떠 있었다."], ["Scum and slime shone faintly in certain places, where the last of the light still caught them on the two great spits of rock jutting out, north and south, into the sea.", "바다로 남북으로 삐져나온 두 큰 바위 곶 위에, 마지막 남은 빛이 여태 걸려 있는 어떤 자리들에서는, 물때와 점액이 어렴풋이 번들거렸다. 이제 물때가 바뀔 무렵이었다."], ["It was now the time of the turn of the tide: and even as I stood there waiting, the broad brown face of the quicksand began to dimple and quiver—the only moving thing in all the horrid place.", "그리하여 내가 거기 서서 기다리는 바로 그 순간에도, 유사의 넓적한 갈색 얼굴이 잔물결을 일으키며 떨리기 시작했다—그 소름 끼치는 곳 전체에서 유일하게 움직이는 것이었다."]], [["I saw the Sergeant start as the shiver of the sand caught his eye.", "나는 그 모래의 떨림이 부장의 눈에 들자 그가 흠칫하는 것을 보았다."], ["After looking at it for a minute or so, he turned and came back to me.", "일 분쯤 그것을 바라보다가, 그는 돌아서서 내게로 왔다."]], [["“A treacherous place, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"믿을 수 없는 곳이오, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["he said; “and no signs of Rosanna Spearman anywhere on the beach, look where you may.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 아무리 둘러봐도, 바닷가 어디에도 로재나 스피어먼의 흔적이라곤 없구려.\""]], [["He took me down lower on the shore, and I saw for myself that his footsteps and mine were the only footsteps printed off on the sand.", "그는 나를 바닷가 더 낮은 데로 데려갔고, 나는 그의 발자국과 내 발자국만이 모래에 찍혀 있는 것을 내 눈으로 직접 보았다."]], [["“How does the fishing village bear, standing where we are now?”", "\"지금 우리가 선 자리에서 보면, 그 어촌이 어느 방향이오?\""], ["asked Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 물었다."]], [["“Cobb’s Hole,”", "\"코브스 홀은,\""], ["I answered (that being the name of the place), “bears as near as may be, due south.”", "하고 나는 (그것이 그곳의 이름이었으므로) 대답했다. \"될 수 있는 대로 가까이 잡아, 정남쪽에 있습니다.\""]], [["“I saw the girl this evening, walking northward along the shore, from Cobb’s Hole,”", "\"나는 오늘 저녁 그 아이가 코브스 홀에서부터 바닷가를 따라 북쪽으로 걸어가는 것을 보았소.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“Consequently, she must have been walking towards this place.", "\"그러니, 그 아이는 이곳을 향해 걸어오고 있었던 게 틀림없소."], ["Is Cobb’s Hole on the other side of that point of land there?", "코브스 홀이 저기 저 곶 반대편에 있소?"], ["And can we get to it—now it’s low water—by the beach?”", "그리고 우리가—지금은 썰물이니—바닷가를 따라 거기 갈 수 있소?\""]], [["I answered, “Yes,”", "나는 그 두 물음에 다 \"예.\""], ["to both those questions.", "하고 대답했다."]], [["“If you’ll excuse my suggesting it, we’ll step out briskly,”", "\"내 이런 제안을 하는 것을 너그러이 봐 주신다면, 걸음을 서둘러 봅시다.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“I want to find the place where she left the shore, before it gets dark.”", "\"날이 어두워지기 전에, 그 아이가 바닷가를 벗어난 자리를 찾고 싶소.\""]], [["We had walked, I should say, a couple of hundred yards towards Cobb’s Hole, when Sergeant Cuff suddenly went down on his knees on the beach, to all appearance seized with a sudden frenzy for saying his prayers.", "우리가, 대충 잡아, 코브스 홀 쪽으로 이백 야드쯤 걸었을 때, 커프 부장이 별안간 바닷가에 무릎을 꿇었는데, 겉보기로는 마치 느닷없이 기도를 올리고 싶은 광기에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯했다."]], [["“There’s something to be said for your marine landscape here, after all,”", "\"여기 당신네 해양 경관도 그리 나쁘게만 볼 것은 아니로군, 결국은 말이오.\""], ["remarked the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“Here are a woman’s footsteps, Mr. Betteredge!", "\"여기 여자의 발자국이 있소, 베터리지 양반!"], ["Let us call them Rosanna’s footsteps, until we find evidence to the contrary that we can’t resist.", "도저히 거스를 수 없는 반대 증거를 찾기 전까지는, 이것을 로재나의 발자국이라 부릅시다."], ["Very confused footsteps, you will please to observe—purposely confused, I should say.", "아주 어지럽혀진 발자국이라는 걸 부디 눈여겨보시오—일부러 어지럽힌 것이라고 나는 보오."], ["Ah, poor soul, she understands the detective virtues of sand as well as I do!", "아, 딱한 것, 그 아이는 모래의 그 형사다운 재간을 나만큼이나 잘 아는군!"], ["But hasn’t she been in rather too great a hurry to tread out the marks thoroughly?", "허나 그 자국을 말끔히 밟아 지우기에는 너무 서둘렀던 게 아닐까?"], ["I think she has.", "나는 그렇게 보오."], ["Here’s one footstep going from Cobb’s Hole; and here is another going back to it.", "여기 코브스 홀에서 멀어지는 발자국이 하나 있고, 여기 다시 그리로 돌아가는 발자국이 또 하나 있소."], ["Isn’t that the toe of her shoe pointing straight to the water’s edge?", "저것이 곧장 물가를 가리키는 그 아이 신발 앞코가 아니오?"], ["And don’t I see two heel-marks further down the beach, close at the water’s edge also?", "그리고 바닷가 더 아래, 역시 물가 바로 곁에, 발뒤꿈치 자국 둘이 내 눈에 보이지 않소?"], ["I don’t want to hurt your feelings, but I’m afraid Rosanna is sly.", "당신 심기를 상하게 하고 싶지는 않소만, 아무래도 로재나는 잔꾀가 있는 아이 같소."], ["It looks as if she had determined to get to that place you and I have just come from, without leaving any marks on the sand to trace her by.", "마치 그 아이가, 저를 뒤밟을 아무 자국도 모래에 남기지 않고, 당신과 내가 방금 다녀온 그 자리에 이르기로 작정했던 것처럼 보이오."], ["Shall we say that she walked through the water from this point till she got to that ledge of rocks behind us, and came back the same way, and then took to the beach again where those two heel marks are still left?", "그 아이가 이 지점에서부터 물속을 걸어 우리 뒤편 저 바위 턱에 이르렀다가, 같은 길로 되돌아와, 저 발뒤꿈치 자국 둘이 아직 남아 있는 그 자리에서 다시 바닷가로 올라섰다고—그렇게 봐도 되겠소?"], ["Yes, we’ll say that.", "그렇소, 그렇게 봅시다."], ["It seems to fit in with my notion that she had something under her cloak, when she left the cottage. No!", "그 아이가 오두막을 나설 때 제 망토 아래 무언가를 지니고 있었다는 내 짐작과 아귀가 맞는 것 같소."], ["not something to destroy—for, in that case, where would have been the need of all these precautions to prevent my tracing the place at which her walk ended?", "아니! 없애 버릴 무언가는 아니오—만일 그런 것이라면, 제 걸음이 끝난 자리를 내가 뒤밟지 못하게 하려고 이 온갖 조심을 다 부릴 까닭이 어디 있었겠소?"], ["Something to hide is, I think, the better guess of the two.", "숨길 무언가라는 것이, 내 생각엔, 그 둘 중 더 나은 짐작이오."], ["Perhaps, if we go on to the cottage, we may find out what that something is?”", "어쩌면, 우리가 그 오두막까지 가 보면, 그 무언가가 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있을지도 모르겠소?\""]], [["At this proposal, my detective-fever suddenly cooled.", "이 제안에, 내 탐정 열병이 별안간 식어 버렸다. \"저는 필요 없으시잖습니까.\""], ["“You don’t want me,”", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “What good can I do?”", "\"제가 무슨 도움이 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“The longer I know you, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"당신을 오래 알수록, 베터리지 양반,\""], ["said the Sergeant, “the more virtues I discover.", "하고 부장이 말했다. \"나는 더 많은 미덕을 발견하는구려."], ["Modesty—oh dear me, how rare modesty is in this world!", "겸손이라니—아이고 저런, 이 세상에 겸손이란 게 얼마나 드문 것인데!"], ["and how much of that rarity you possess!", "그리고 당신은 그 드문 것을 얼마나 많이 지녔는지!"], ["If I go alone to the cottage, the people’s tongues will be tied at the first question I put to them.", "만일 내가 혼자 그 오두막에 간다면, 사람들은 내가 던지는 첫 물음에 그만 입을 다물어 버릴 거요."], ["If I go with you, I go introduced by a justly respected neighbour, and a flow of conversation is the necessary result.", "만일 내가 당신과 함께 간다면, 나는 마땅히 존경받는 이웃의 소개를 받고 가는 셈이니, 술술 이야기가 흘러나오는 것이 그 당연한 결과지."], ["It strikes me in that light; how does it strike you?”", "내게는 그렇게 보이오. 당신에게는 어떻게 보이오?\""]], [["Not having an answer of the needful smartness as ready as I could have wished, I tried to gain time by asking him what cottage he wanted to go to.", "내가 바라던 만큼 재치 있는 대답이 얼른 준비되어 있지 않아, 나는 그가 가려는 오두막이 어느 것인지 물어 시간을 벌어 보려 했다."]], [["On the Sergeant describing the place, I recognised it as a cottage inhabited by a fisherman named Yolland, with his wife and two grown-up children, a son and a daughter.", "부장이 그곳을 묘사하자, 나는 그것이 욜랜드라는 어부가 제 아내와 다 자란 두 아이, 곧 아들 하나 딸 하나와 함께 사는 오두막임을 알아보았다."], ["If you will look back, you will find that, in first presenting Rosanna Spearman to your notice, I have described her as occasionally varying her walk to the Shivering Sand, by a visit to some friends of hers at Cobb’s Hole.", "앞을 돌이켜 보시면, 내가 로재나 스피어먼을 처음 여러분께 소개할 때, 그가 이따금 흔들리는 모래로 가는 산책길을 바꾸어 코브스 홀에 사는 제 벗 몇을 찾아가곤 했다고 묘사한 것을 아실 것이다."], ["Those friends were the Yollands—respectable, worthy people, a credit to the neighbourhood.", "그 벗들이 바로 욜랜드네였다—점잖고 훌륭한 사람들로, 그 이웃의 자랑거리였다."], ["Rosanna’s acquaintance with them had begun by means of the daughter, who was afflicted with a misshapen foot, and who was known in our parts by the name of Limping Lucy.", "로재나가 그들과 알게 된 것은 그 딸을 통해서였는데, 그 딸은 발이 기형이라, 우리 고장에서는 절름발이 루시라는 이름으로 통했다."], ["The two deformed girls had, I suppose, a kind of fellow-feeling for each other.", "짐작건대 그 두 불구의 처녀는 서로에게 일종의 동병상련을 느꼈던 모양이다."], ["Anyway, the Yollands and Rosanna always appeared to get on together, at the few chances they had of meeting, in a pleasant and friendly manner.", "아무튼 욜랜드네와 로재나는, 어쩌다 만날 몇 안 되는 기회에, 늘 화기애애하고 다정하게 어울리는 듯 보였다."], ["The fact of Sergeant Cuff having traced the girl to their cottage, set the matter of my helping his inquiries in quite a new light.", "커프 부장이 그 아이를 그들의 오두막까지 뒤밟았다는 사실은, 그의 조사를 돕는다는 문제를 사뭇 새로운 빛 아래 놓았다."], ["Rosanna had merely gone where she was in the habit of going; and to show that she had been in company with the fisherman and his family was as good as to prove that she had been innocently occupied so far, at any rate.", "로재나는 그저 늘 다니던 곳에 갔을 뿐이었다. 그리고 그가 그 어부와 그 식구들과 함께 있었음을 밝히는 것은, 적어도 거기까지는 그가 아무 죄 없이 지냈음을 증명하는 것이나 다름없었다."], ["It would be doing the girl a service, therefore, instead of an injury, if I allowed myself to be convinced by Sergeant Cuff’s logic.", "그러니 내가 커프 부장의 논리에 설득당하기로 한다면, 그것은 그 아이에게 해를 끼치는 것이 아니라 오히려 도움을 주는 일이 될 터였다."], ["I professed myself convinced by it accordingly.", "그리하여 나는 그 논리에 설득당했노라고 표명했다."]], [["We went on to Cobb’s Hole, seeing the footsteps on the sand, as long as the light lasted.", "우리는 코브스 홀로 나아갔고, 빛이 남아 있는 동안은 모래 위의 발자국을 보며 갔다."]], [["On reaching the cottage, the fisherman and his son proved to be out in the boat; and Limping Lucy, always weak and weary, was resting on her bed upstairs.", "오두막에 다다르니, 어부와 그 아들은 배를 타고 나가 있었고, 늘 약하고 지쳐 있는 절름발이 루시는 위층 제 침대에서 쉬고 있었다."], ["Good Mrs. Yolland received us alone in her kitchen.", "마음씨 좋은 욜랜드 부인이 제 부엌에서 우리를 혼자 맞았다."], ["When she heard that Sergeant Cuff was a celebrated character in London, she clapped a bottle of Dutch gin and a couple of clean pipes on the table, and stared as if she could never see enough of him.", "커프 부장이 런던에서 이름난 인물이라는 말을 듣자, 부인은 네덜란드 진 한 병과 깨끗한 담뱃대 두 개를 탁자에 탁 내려놓고는, 아무리 봐도 성이 안 찬다는 듯 그를 뚫어져라 쳐다보았다."]], [["I sat quiet in a corner, waiting to hear how the Sergeant would find his way to the subject of Rosanna Spearman.", "나는 부장이 로재나 스피어먼이라는 화제로 어떻게 길을 터 갈지 들으려고 한구석에 잠자코 앉아 있었다."], ["His usual roundabout manner of going to work proved, on this occasion, to be more roundabout than ever.", "그가 일에 착수하는 그 여느 에두르는 방식이, 이번만은, 그 어느 때보다도 더 에둘렀다."], ["How he managed it is more than I could tell at the time, and more than I can tell now.", "그가 그것을 어떻게 해냈는지는 그때도 내가 이를 수 없는 노릇이었고, 지금도 이를 수 없는 노릇이다. 허나 이것만은 분명하다."], ["But this is certain, he began with the Royal Family, the Primitive Methodists, and the price of fish; and he got from that (in his dismal, underground way) to the loss of the Moonstone, the spitefulness of our first house-maid, and the hard behaviour of the women-servants generally towards Rosanna Spearman.", "그는 왕실 이야기와, 원시감리교도와, 생선값으로 말문을 열었다. 그리고 거기서 (그 음침하고 땅속 같은 방식으로) 월장석의 실종과, 우리 집 첫째 하녀의 심술과, 여자 하인들이 로재나 스피어먼에게 보인 대체로 모진 처사로 옮겨 갔다."], ["Having reached his subject in this fashion, he described himself as making his inquiries about the lost Diamond, partly with a view to find it, and partly for the purpose of clearing Rosanna from the unjust suspicions of her enemies in the house.", "이런 식으로 제 화제에 이른 그는, 자기가 저 잃어버린 다이아몬드에 관해 조사하는 것이, 한편으로는 그것을 찾으려는 뜻에서요, 다른 한편으로는 그 집안에서 로재나를 원수처럼 여기는 자들의 부당한 의심에서 그를 벗겨 주려는 뜻에서라고 스스로를 소개했다."], ["In about a quarter of an hour from the time when we entered the kitchen, good Mrs. Yolland was persuaded that she was talking to Rosanna’s best friend, and was pressing Sergeant Cuff to comfort his stomach and revive his spirits out of the Dutch bottle.", "우리가 그 부엌에 들어선 지 십오 분쯤 되었을 무렵, 마음씨 좋은 욜랜드 부인은 자기가 로재나의 가장 좋은 벗과 이야기하고 있다고 굳게 믿게 되었고, 커프 부장에게 그 네덜란드 병으로 뱃속을 달래고 기운을 되살리라고 자꾸 권하고 있었다."]], [["Being firmly persuaded that the Sergeant was wasting his breath to no purpose on Mrs. Yolland, I sat enjoying the talk between them, much as I have sat, in my time, enjoying a stage play.", "부장이 욜랜드 부인에게 아무 소득도 없이 헛기운을 쓰고 있다고 굳게 믿은 나는, 왕년에 무대극 한 편을 즐기며 앉아 있었던 것과 사뭇 비슷하게, 두 사람의 이야기를 즐기며 앉아 있었다."], ["The great Cuff showed a wonderful patience; trying his luck drearily this way and that way, and firing shot after shot, as it were, at random, on the chance of hitting the mark.", "저 위대한 커프는 놀라운 참을성을 보였다. 이리 찔러 보고 저리 찔러 보며 처량하게 운을 시험했고, 이를테면 과녁을 맞힐 요행을 바라며 아무렇게나 총알을 연방 쏘아 댔다."], ["Everything to Rosanna’s credit, nothing to Rosanna’s prejudice—that was how it ended, try as he might; with Mrs.", "죄다 로재나에게 이로운 것뿐, 로재나에게 해로운 것이라곤 하나도 없었다—아무리 애를 써도 결국은 그렇게 끝났다."], ["Yolland talking nineteen to the dozen, and placing the most entire confidence in him.", "욜랜드 부인은 쉴 새 없이 나불거리며, 그에게 더할 나위 없는 믿음을 두었다."], ["His last effort was made, when we had looked at our watches, and had got on our legs previous to taking leave.", "그의 마지막 시도는, 우리가 시계를 보고, 작별을 고하려고 자리에서 일어섰을 때 이루어졌다."]], [["“I shall now wish you good-night, ma’am,”", "\"이제 그만 안녕히 주무시라 인사드리겠소, 부인.\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."], ["“And I shall only say, at parting, that Rosanna Spearman has a sincere well-wisher in myself, your obedient servant.", "\"그리고 헤어지면서 딱 한마디만 하자면, 로재나 스피어먼에게는 저, 그대의 충직한 종이라는 진심 어린 후원자가 하나 있다는 거요."], ["But, oh dear me!", "허나, 아이고 저런!"], ["she will never get on in her present place; and my advice to her is—leave it.”", "그 아이는 지금 있는 그 자리에서는 결코 잘 풀리지 못할 거요. 그러니 그 아이에게 주는 내 조언은—거길 떠나라, 이거요.\""]], [["“Bless your heart alive!", "\"어머 저런, 세상에나!"], ["she is going to leave it!” cries Mrs.", "그 애가 바로 떠나려는 참인걸요!\""], ["Yolland. (Nota bene—I translate Mrs.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 외친다."], ["Yolland out of the Yorkshire language into the English language.", "(눈여겨 두시라—나는 욜랜드 부인의 말을 요크셔 말에서 표준 영어로 옮겨 전하는 것이다."], ["When I tell you that the all-accomplished Cuff was every now and then puzzled to understand her until I helped him, you will draw your own conclusions as to what your state of mind would be if I reported her in her native tongue.)", "그 온갖 재주를 다 갖춘 커프조차 내가 거들어 주기 전까지는 이따금 그의 말을 알아듣지 못해 쩔쩔맸다고 내가 이르면, 만일 내가 그의 말을 그 본디 말투 그대로 전한다면 여러분의 정신 상태가 어찌 될지에 대해 여러분 나름의 결론을 내리실 것이다.)"]], [["Rosanna Spearman going to leave us!", "로재나 스피어먼이 우리를 떠나려 한다니!"], ["I pricked up my ears at that.", "나는 그 말에 귀가 쫑긋해졌다."], ["It seemed strange, to say the least of it, that she should have given no warning, in the first place, to my lady or to me.", "아무리 좋게 봐도, 그 아이가 애당초 마님께든 내게든 아무런 언질조차 주지 않았다는 것이 이상했다."], ["A certain doubt came up in my mind whether Sergeant Cuff’s last random shot might not have hit the mark.", "커프 부장의 그 마지막 아무렇게나 쏜 총알이 어쩌면 과녁을 맞힌 게 아닐까 하는 일말의 의심이 내 마음속에 떠올랐다."], ["I began to question whether my share in the proceedings was quite as harmless a one as I had thought it.", "나는 이 일에서 내가 맡은 몫이 과연 내가 여기던 만큼 그리 해롭지 않은 것이었는지 새삼 의심하기 시작했다."], ["It might be all in the way of the Sergeant’s business to mystify an honest woman by wrapping her round in a network of lies but it was my duty to have remembered, as a good Protestant, that the father of lies is the Devil—and that mischief and the Devil are never far apart.", "정직한 여자를 거짓의 그물로 칭칭 둘러 헷갈리게 하는 것이야 죄다 부장의 일 처리 방식일 수 있겠으나, 착실한 신교도로서 나는, 거짓의 아비는 마귀이며—또 재앙과 마귀는 결코 멀리 떨어져 있는 법이 없음을 마땅히 기억했어야 했다."], ["Beginning to smell mischief in the air, I tried to take Sergeant Cuff out.", "공기 중에서 재앙의 냄새가 나기 시작하는 것을 느낀 나는, 커프 부장을 밖으로 데리고 나가려 해 보았다."], ["He sat down again instantly, and asked for a little drop of comfort out of the Dutch bottle.", "그는 곧바로 도로 앉더니, 그 네덜란드 병으로 위안 한 모금을 청했다."], ["Mrs. Yolland sat down opposite to him, and gave him his nip.", "욜랜드 부인은 그의 맞은편에 앉아, 그에게 한 잔을 따라 주었다."], ["I went on to the door, excessively uncomfortable, and said I thought I must bid them good-night—and yet I didn’t go.", "나는 몹시 불편한 마음으로 문께로 갔고, 이제 그만 안녕히 주무시라 인사드려야겠다고 말했다—그러면서도 정작 나가지는 못했다."]], [["“So she means to leave?”", "\"그럼 그 아이가 떠날 작정이라 이거요?\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."], ["“What is she to do when she does leave?", "\"떠나면 무얼 하려고? 딱하기도 하지, 딱하기도 해!"], ["Sad, sad! The poor creature has got no friends in the world, except you and me.”", "그 가여운 것은 이 세상에 벗이라곤, 당신과 나 말고는 하나도 없는데.\""]], [["“Ah, but she has though!”", "\"아이고, 그렇지가 않다니까요!\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다."], ["“She came in here, as I told you, this evening; and, after sitting and talking a little with my girl Lucy and me she asked to go upstairs by herself, into Lucy’s room.", "\"말씀드렸다시피, 그 애가 오늘 저녁 여기 왔었어요. 그리고 우리 딸 루시랑 저랑 앉아서 얘기 좀 나누다가, 혼자 위층으로, 루시 방에 좀 올라가게 해 달라더군요."], ["It’s the only room in our place where there’s pen and ink.", "우리 집에서 펜이랑 잉크가 있는 방은 거기뿐이거든요. '벗한테 편지를 한 장 쓰고 싶은데,'"], ["‘I want to write a letter to a friend,’ she says ‘and I can’t do it for the prying and peeping of the servants up at the house.’ Who the letter was written to I can’t tell you: it must have been a mortal long one, judging by the time she stopped upstairs over it.", "그 애가 그러더라고요, '저 위 집에선 하인들이 엿보고 훔쳐보고 하니 도무지 쓸 수가 없어요.' 편지가 누구한테 쓴 건지는 말씀 못 드리겠어요."], ["I offered her a postage-stamp when she came down.", "그걸 붙들고 위층에 머문 시간으로 미루어 보건대, 엄청나게 긴 편지였던 게 틀림없어요."], ["She hadn’t got the letter in her hand, and she didn’t accept the stamp.", "내려왔을 때 제가 우표 한 장을 건네줬어요. 손에 편지는 안 들려 있었고, 우표도 안 받더군요."], ["A little close, poor soul (as you know), about herself and her doings.", "그 딱한 것이, (아시다시피) 제 일이랑 제 하는 짓에 대해선 좀 입이 무거운 애라서요."], ["But a friend she has got somewhere, I can tell you; and to that friend you may depend upon it, she will go.”", "하지만 어디엔가 벗이 하나 있는 건 분명해요, 장담해요. 그리고 그 벗한테, 틀림없이, 그 애가 갈 거예요.\""]], [["“Soon?”", "\"곧 말이오?\""], ["asked the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 물었다."]], [["“As soon as she can,”", "\"될 수 있는 대로 빨리요.\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다."]], [["Here I stepped in again from the door.", "여기서 나는 다시 문께에서 걸어 들어갔다."], ["As chief of my lady’s establishment, I couldn’t allow this sort of loose talk about a servant of ours going, or not going, to proceed any longer in my presence, without noticing it.", "마님 살림의 우두머리로서, 우리 하인 하나가 떠나느니 안 떠나느니 하는 이런 실없는 이야기가 내가 있는 자리에서 더 이상 오가는 것을, 아무 말 없이 그냥 둘 수는 없는 노릇이었다."]], [["“You must be mistaken about Rosanna Spearman,” I said.", "\"당신이 로재나 스피어먼에 대해 잘못 아신 게 틀림없습니다.\""], ["“If she had been going to leave her present situation, she would have mentioned it, in the first place, to me.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"만일 그 아이가 지금 있는 자리를 떠나려 했다면, 애당초 그 말을 내게 했을 겁니다.\""]], [["“Mistaken?”", "\"잘못 알아요?\""], ["cries Mrs. Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 외친다."], ["“Why, only an hour ago she bought some things she wanted for travelling—of my own self, Mr. Betteredge, in this very room.", "\"아니, 바로 한 시간 전에 그 애가 여행에 필요한 물건 몇 가지를 샀는걸요—다른 사람도 아닌 저한테서, 베터리지 씨, 바로 이 방에서요."], ["And that reminds me,”", "그러고 보니 생각나네요,\""], ["says the wearisome woman, suddenly beginning to feel in her pocket, “of something I have got it on my mind to say about Rosanna and her money.", "하고 이 성가신 여자가, 별안간 제 주머니를 뒤지기 시작하며 말한다. \"로재나랑 그 애 돈에 대해 마음에 걸려 하던 게 하나 있었어요."], ["Are you either of you likely to see her when you go back to the house?”", "두 분 중 누구든 집에 돌아가서 그 애를 볼 것 같으세요?\""]], [["“I’ll take a message to the poor thing, with the greatest pleasure,”", "\"내가 그 딱한 것에게 더없이 기꺼이 전갈을 전해 주리다.\""], ["answered Sergeant Cuff, before I could put in a word edgewise.", "하고 커프 부장이, 내가 한마디 비집고 넣기도 전에 대답했다."]], [["Mrs. Yolland produced out of her pocket, a few shillings and sixpences, and counted them out with a most particular and exasperating carefulness in the palm of her hand.", "욜랜드 부인은 제 주머니에서 실링과 육펜스 몇 닢을 꺼내, 더없이 꼼꼼하고 사람 애태우는 조심스러움으로 제 손바닥 위에 세어 놓았다."], ["She offered the money to the Sergeant, looking mighty loth to part with it all the while.", "부인은 그 돈을 부장에게 내밀었는데, 그러는 내내 그것을 죄다 내놓기가 여간 아까운 낯이 아니었다."]], [["“Might I ask you to give this back to Rosanna, with my love and respects?”", "\"부디 이걸 로재나한테, 내 사랑과 안부와 함께 돌려주시겠어요?\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다."], ["“She insisted on paying me for the one or two things she took a fancy to this evening—and money’s welcome enough in our house, I don’t deny it.", "\"그 애가 오늘 저녁 마음에 든 물건 한두 가지 값을 기어이 치르겠다고 우겨서요—돈이야 우리 집엔 반갑기 그지없죠, 그건 부정 안 해요."], ["Still, I’m not easy in my mind about taking the poor thing’s little savings.", "그래도, 그 딱한 것의 쥐꼬리만 한 저금을 받는 게 영 마음이 편치가 않아요."], ["And to tell you the truth, I don’t think my man would like to hear that I had taken Rosanna Spearman’s money, when he comes back tomorrow morning from his work.", "그리고 사실대로 말씀드리면, 우리 양반이 내일 아침 일 마치고 돌아와서, 내가 로재나 스피어먼의 돈을 받았다는 얘길 들으면 좋아하지 않을 것 같아요."], ["Please say she’s heartily welcome to the things she bought of me—as a gift.", "그 애가 저한테서 산 물건은—선물로—얼마든지 가지라고 부디 전해 주세요."], ["And don’t leave the money on the table,” says Mrs.", "그리고 그 돈을 탁자에 두고 가진 마세요,\""], ["Yolland, putting it down suddenly before the Sergeant, as if it burnt her fingers—“don’t, there’s a good man!", "하고 욜랜드 부인이, 마치 그것이 제 손가락을 데기라도 하는 듯, 별안간 그것을 부장 앞에 내려놓으며 말한다. \"제발요, 착한 양반!"], ["For times are hard, and flesh is weak; and I might feel tempted to put it back in my pocket again.”", "세상살이는 팍팍하고 사람 마음은 여리니까요. 안 그럼 저도 모르게 다시 그걸 주머니에 도로 넣고 싶어질지도 모르잖아요.\""]], [["“Come along!”", "\"자, 갑시다!\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “I can’t wait any longer: I must go back to the house.”", "\"더는 못 기다리겠습니다. 집으로 돌아가야 해요.\""]], [["“I’ll follow you directly,”", "\"내 곧 따라가리다.\""], ["says Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말한다."]], [["For the second time, I went to the door; and, for the second time, try as I might, I couldn’t cross the threshold.", "두 번째로, 나는 문께로 갔다. 그리고 두 번째로, 아무리 애를 써도, 나는 그 문지방을 넘지 못했다."]], [["“It’s a delicate matter, ma’am,”", "\"돈을 돌려준다는 게, 부인, 미묘한 일이구려.\""], ["I heard the Sergeant say, “giving money back.", "하고 부장이 말하는 것이 들렸다."], ["You charged her cheap for the things, I’m sure?”", "\"그 물건들 값을 싸게 받으신 게 틀림없죠?\""]], [["“Cheap!”", "\"싸게요!\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다."], ["“Come and judge for yourself.”", "\"와서 직접 판단해 보세요.\""]], [["She took up the candle and led the Sergeant to a corner of the kitchen.", "부인은 촛불을 집어 들고 부장을 부엌 한구석으로 데려갔다."], ["For the life of me, I couldn’t help following them.", "나는 도무지 그들을 따라가지 않고는 못 배겼다."], ["Shaken down in the corner was a heap of odds and ends (mostly old metal), which the fisherman had picked up at different times from wrecked ships, and which he hadn’t found a market for yet, to his own mind.", "그 구석에는 잡동사니 한 무더기(거의가 낡은 쇠붙이였다)가 쌓여 있었는데, 그것은 어부가 이런저런 때에 난파선에서 주워 온 것으로, 그의 마음에 차게 팔아 치울 자리를 아직 못 찾은 물건들이었다."], ["Mrs. Yolland dived into this rubbish, and brought up an old japanned tin case, with a cover to it, and a hasp to hang it up by—the sort of thing they use, on board ship, for keeping their maps and charts, and such-like, from the wet.", "욜랜드 부인은 이 잡동사니 속으로 파고들더니, 뚜껑이 달리고 걸어 두는 걸쇠가 붙은 낡은 옻칠 양철 상자 하나를 꺼내 올렸다—뱃사람들이 배 위에서 제 지도며 해도(海圖)며 그런 것들을 물기에서 지키느라 쓰는 그런 물건이었다."]], [["“There!”", "\"자!\""], ["says she.", "하고 부인이 말한다."], ["“When Rosanna came in this evening, she bought the fellow to that.", "\"로재나가 오늘 저녁 왔을 때, 이거랑 똑같은 걸 하나 샀어요. '이거면 딱 좋겠어요,'"], ["‘It will just do,’ she says, ‘to put my cuffs and collars in, and keep them from being crumpled in my box.’ One and ninepence, Mr.", "그 애가 그러더라고요, '제 소맷동이랑 깃을 넣어서, 상자 안에서 구겨지지 않게 하는 데요.' 일 실링 구 펜스예요, 커프 씨."], ["Cuff. As I live by bread, not a halfpenny more!”", "밥 먹고 사는 목숨을 걸고, 반 페니도 더 안 받았어요!\""]], [["“Dirt cheap!”", "\"거저나 다름없구려!\""], ["says the Sergeant, with a heavy sigh.", "하고 부장이 무거운 한숨과 함께 말한다."]], [["He weighed the case in his hand.", "그는 그 상자를 손으로 가늠해 보았다."], ["I thought I heard a note or two of “The Last Rose of Summer” as he looked at it.", "그가 그것을 들여다보며 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 한두 소절 흥얼거리는 소리를 들은 것 같았다."], ["There was no doubt now!", "이제 의심할 여지가 없었다!"], ["He had made another discovery to the prejudice of Rosanna Spearman, in the place of all others where I thought her character was safest, and all through me!", "그는 로재나 스피어먼에게 해가 될 또 하나의 사실을, 내가 그 아이의 결백이 가장 안전하리라 여기던 바로 그곳에서, 그것도 죄다 내 탓으로 알아낸 것이다!"], ["I leave you to imagine what I felt, and how sincerely I repented having been the medium of introduction between Mrs. Yolland and Sergeant Cuff.", "내가 무슨 심정이었는지, 그리고 욜랜드 부인과 커프 부장 사이를 잇는 매개가 되어 준 것을 얼마나 진심으로 후회했는지는, 여러분의 상상에 맡기겠다."]], [["“That will do,”", "\"이만하면 됐습니다.\""], ["I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["“We really must go.”", "\"우린 정말 가야 해요.\""]], [["Without paying the least attention to me, Mrs. Yolland took another dive into the rubbish, and came up out of it, this time, with a dog-chain.", "내게는 눈곱만큼도 아랑곳하지 않고, 욜랜드 부인은 그 잡동사니 속으로 또 한 번 파고들더니, 이번에는 개 사슬 하나를 꺼내 올렸다."]], [["“Weigh it in your hand, sir,”", "\"손으로 무게를 한번 가늠해 보세요, 나리.\""], ["she said to the Sergeant.", "하고 부인이 부장에게 말했다."], ["“We had three of these; and Rosanna has taken two of them.", "\"우리 집에 이게 세 개 있었는데, 로재나가 그중 둘을 가져갔어요."], ["‘What can you want, my dear, with a couple of dog’s chains?’ says I.", "'아니 얘야, 개 사슬을 두 개나 뭐에 쓰려고?' 하고 제가 그랬죠."], ["‘If I join them together they’ll go round my box nicely,’ says she.", "'둘을 이으면 제 상자를 딱 알맞게 두를 수 있거든요,' 하고 그 애가 그러더군요."], ["‘Rope’s cheapest,’ says I.", "'밧줄이 제일 싼데,' 하고 제가 그랬죠."], ["‘Chain’s surest,’ says she.", "'사슬이 제일 튼튼해요,' 하고 그 애가 그러고요."], ["‘Who ever heard of a box corded with chain,’ says I.", "'아니 세상에 사슬로 상자를 동여맨다는 소리를 누가 들어 봤다니,'"], ["‘Oh, Mrs.", "하고 제가 그랬죠."], ["Yolland, don’t make objections!’ says she; ‘let me have my chains!’ A strange girl, Mr.", "'어머, 욜랜드 아주머니, 자꾸 토 달지 마세요!' 하고 그 애가 그러더군요."], ["Cuff—good as gold, and kinder than a sister to my Lucy—but always a little strange.", "'그냥 사슬 주세요!' 참 별난 애예요, 커프 씨—금덩이처럼 착하고, 우리 루시한테는 친자매보다 더 다정하고—그래도 늘 어딘가 좀 별나요."], ["There!", "자!"], ["I humoured her.", "제가 그 애 비위를 맞춰 줬죠."], ["Three and sixpence.", "삼 실링 육 펜스요."], ["On the word of an honest woman, three and sixpence, Mr. Cuff!”", "정직한 여자의 말을 걸고, 삼 실링 육 펜스예요, 커프 씨!\""]], [["“Each?”", "\"하나에 말이오?\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."]], [["“Both together!”", "\"둘 합쳐서요!\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다."], ["“Three and sixpence for the two.”", "\"둘 다 해서 삼 실링 육 펜스요.\""]], [["“Given away, ma’am,”", "\"거저 주신 셈이오, 부인.\""], ["says the Sergeant, shaking his head.", "하고 부장이 고개를 저으며 말한다."], ["“Clean given away!”", "\"아주 거저 주신 셈이오!\""]], [["“There’s the money,”", "\"돈은 여기 있어요,\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland, getting back sideways to the little heap of silver on the table, as if it drew her in spite of herself.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이, 마치 그것이 제 뜻과는 달리 저를 끌어당기기라도 하는 듯, 탁자 위 그 조그만 은화 무더기 쪽으로 옆걸음으로 도로 다가가며 말한다."], ["“The tin case and the dog chains were all she bought, and all she took away.", "\"양철 상자랑 개 사슬이 그 애가 산 전부고, 가져간 전부예요."], ["One and ninepence and three and sixpence—total, five and three.", "일 실링 구 펜스에 삼 실링 육 펜스—합해서 오 실링 삼 펜스죠."], ["With my love and respects—and I can’t find it in my conscience to take a poor girl’s savings, when she may want them herself.”", "내 사랑과 안부와 함께요—그리고 가여운 계집애가 저 쓸 데 있을지도 모르는 저금을, 제 양심상 도저히 못 받겠어요.\""]], [["“I can’t find it in my conscience, ma’am, to give the money back,”", "\"부인, 나도 내 양심상 도저히 그 돈을 돌려줄 수가 없구려.\""], ["says Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말한다."], ["“You have as good as made her a present of the things—you have indeed.”", "\"그 물건들을 그 아이에게 선물로 주신 것이나 진배없소—정말이지 그렇소.\""]], [["“Is that your sincere opinion, sir?”", "\"그게 진심이세요, 나리?\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland brightening up wonderfully.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 놀랍도록 낯빛이 환해지며 말한다."]], [["“There can’t be a doubt about it,”", "\"의심할 여지가 있을 리가 있소.\""], ["answered the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 대답했다."], ["“Ask Mr. Betteredge.”", "\"베터리지 양반에게 물어보시구려.\""]], [["It was no use asking me.", "내게 물어봐야 아무 소용 없었다."], ["All they got out of me was, “Good-night.”", "그들이 내게서 얻어 낸 것이라곤 \"안녕히 주무십시오.\"뿐이었다."]], [["“Bother the money!”", "\"돈이고 뭐고!\""], ["says Mrs. Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드 부인이 말한다."], ["With these words, she appeared to lose all command over herself; and, making a sudden snatch at the heap of silver, put it back, holus-bolus, in her pocket.", "이 말과 함께, 부인은 제 자제심을 죄다 잃은 듯했다. 그러고는 그 은화 무더기를 별안간 낚아채, 몽땅 제 주머니에 도로 넣었다."], ["“It upsets one’s temper, it does, to see it lying there, and nobody taking it,”", "\"저기 저러고 놓여 있는데 아무도 안 가져가는 걸 보고 있자니, 사람 성미가 뒤집힌다니까요, 정말이지,\""], ["cries this unreasonable woman, sitting down with a thump, and looking at Sergeant Cuff, as much as to say, “It’s in my pocket again now—get it out if you can!”", "하고 이 도무지 말이 안 통하는 여자가, 털썩 주저앉으며, 마치 \"이제 다시 내 주머니에 들어갔으니—꺼낼 수 있으면 어디 꺼내 봐요!\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯, 커프 부장을 바라보며 외친다."]], [["This time, I not only went to the door, but went fairly out on the road back.", "이번에는, 나는 문께로 갔을 뿐 아니라, 아주 돌아가는 길로 나와 버렸다."], ["Explain it how you may, I felt as if one or both of them had mortally offended me.", "어떻게 설명하든, 나는 그 둘 중 하나가, 아니 둘 다가 나를 죽도록 모욕하기라도 한 것 같은 심정이었다."], ["Before I had taken three steps down the village, I heard the Sergeant behind me.", "내가 마을을 세 걸음도 채 내려가기 전에, 등 뒤에서 부장의 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Thank you for your introduction, Mr.", "\"소개해 줘서 고맙소, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["Betteredge,” he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I am indebted to the fisherman’s wife for an entirely new sensation.", "\"저 어부의 아내 덕분에 나는 아주 새로운 느낌 하나를 얻었소."], ["Mrs. Yolland has puzzled me.”", "욜랜드 부인이 나를 헷갈리게 했구려.\""]], [["It was on the tip of my tongue to have given him a sharp answer, for no better reason than this—that I was out of temper with him, because I was out of temper with myself.", "내 혀끝에는 그에게 쏘아붙일 대꾸가 걸려 있었으니, 그 까닭이란 다름 아니라—내가 나 자신에게 심기가 상해 있는 통에 그에게도 심기가 상해 있었기 때문이었다."], ["But when he owned to being puzzled, a comforting doubt crossed my mind whether any great harm had been done after all.", "그러나 그가 헷갈렸다고 실토하자, 결국 별 큰 해는 없었던 게 아닐까 하는 위안이 되는 의심이 내 마음을 스쳤다."], ["I waited in discreet silence to hear more.", "나는 더 들으려고 조심스레 잠자코 기다렸다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["says the Sergeant, as if he was actually reading my thoughts in the dark.", "하고 부장이, 마치 어둠 속에서 내 속생각을 정말로 읽어 내기라도 하듯 말한다."], ["“Instead of putting me on the scent, it may console you to know, Mr. Betteredge (with your interest in Rosanna), that you have been the means of throwing me off.", "\"내게 냄새를 맡게 해 주기는커녕, (로재나에게 마음을 쓰는) 당신에게는 이 말이 위안이 될지도 모르겠소, 베터리지 양반, 당신이 나를 그 자취에서 벗어나게 하는 구실을 했다는 것이 말이오."], ["What the girl has done, tonight, is clear enough, of course.", "그 아이가 오늘 밤 무슨 짓을 했는지야, 물론, 뻔하기 그지없소."], ["She has joined the two chains, and has fastened them to the hasp in the tin case.", "그 아이는 두 사슬을 이어, 그것을 그 양철 상자 걸쇠에 붙들어 맸소."], ["She has sunk the case, in the water or in the quicksand.", "그리고 그 상자를 물속이나 유사 속에 가라앉혔지."], ["She has made the loose end of the chain fast to some place under the rocks, known only to herself.", "사슬의 풀린 쪽 끝은 저만 아는 바위 아래 어딘가에 단단히 붙들어 맸고."], ["And she will leave the case secure at its anchorage till the present proceedings have come to an end; after which she can privately pull it up again out of its hiding-place, at her own leisure and convenience.", "그리고 이번 일이 다 끝날 때까지 그 상자를 그 닻자리에 안전하게 놔둘 거요. 그 뒤에는 저 좋을 때 편할 때, 남몰래 그것을 숨은 자리에서 도로 끌어 올릴 수 있지."], ["All perfectly plain, so far.", "여기까지는 죄다 더없이 뻔하오."], ["But,”", "허나,\""], ["says the Sergeant, with the first tone of impatience in his voice that I had heard yet, “the mystery is—what the devil has she hidden in the tin case?”", "하고 부장이, 내가 여태 들어 본 중 처음으로 그 목소리에 조바심의 기색을 실어 말한다. \"수수께끼는—그 아이가 대체 그 양철 상자에 무엇을 숨겼느냐, 이거요.\""]], [["I thought to myself, “The Moonstone!”", "나는 속으로 \"월장석!\" 하고 생각했다."], ["But I only said to Sergeant Cuff, “Can’t you guess?”", "그러나 커프 부장에게는 그저 \"짐작이 안 가십니까?\" 하고만 말했다."]], [["“It’s not the Diamond,”", "\"그건 다이아몬드가 아니오.\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."], ["“The whole experience of my life is at fault, if Rosanna Spearman has got the Diamond.”", "\"로재나 스피어먼이 그 다이아몬드를 가졌다면, 내 평생의 온 경험이 다 틀린 것이지.\""]], [["On hearing those words, the infernal detective-fever began, I suppose, to burn in me again.", "그 말을 듣자, 그 지긋지긋한 탐정 열병이, 짐작건대, 내 안에서 다시 타오르기 시작했다."], ["At any rate, I forgot myself in the interest of guessing this new riddle.", "아무튼 나는 이 새 수수께끼를 알아맞히는 재미에 그만 나를 잊었다."], ["I said rashly, “The stained dress!”", "나는 경솔하게도 \"그 얼룩진 옷!\" 하고 말했다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff stopped short in the dark, and laid his hand on my arm.", "커프 부장이 어둠 속에서 우뚝 멈춰 서더니, 내 팔에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Is anything thrown into that quicksand of yours, ever thrown up on the surface again?”", "\"저 당신네 유사에 무언가를 던져 넣으면, 그것이 다시 표면으로 떠오른 적이 있소?\""], ["he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."]], [["“Never,” I answered.", "\"결코 없습니다.\""], ["“Light or heavy, whatever goes into the Shivering Sand is sucked down, and seen no more.”", "하고 내가 대답했다. \"가볍든 무겁든, 저 흔들리는 모래에 들어가는 것은 무엇이든 빨려 들어가, 다시는 보이지 않습니다.\""]], [["“Does Rosanna Spearman know that?”", "\"로재나 스피어먼도 그것을 아오?\""]], [["“She knows it as well as I do.”", "\"저만큼이나 잘 압니다.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["says the Sergeant, “what on earth has she got to do but to tie up a bit of stone in the stained dress and throw it into the quicksand?", "하고 부장이 말한다. \"대체 그 아이가, 그 얼룩진 옷에 돌멩이 하나를 싸서 유사에 던져 넣는 것 말고, 달리 무슨 할 일이 있단 말이오?"], ["There isn’t the shadow of a reason why she should have hidden it—and yet she must have hidden it.", "그 아이가 그것을 숨겼어야 할 이유는 그림자만큼도 없소—그런데도 그 아이는 그것을 숨긴 게 틀림없소."], ["Query,”", "문제는,\""], ["says the Sergeant, walking on again, “is the paint-stained dress a petticoat or a night-gown?", "하고 부장이, 다시 걸으며 말한다."], ["or is it something else which there is a reason for preserving at any risk? Mr.", "\"그 페인트 얼룩진 옷이 속치마냐 잠옷이냐—아니면 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 간직할 이유가 있는 다른 무언가냐, 이거요."], ["Betteredge, if nothing occurs to prevent it, I must go to Frizinghall tomorrow, and discover what she bought in the town, when she privately got the materials for making the substitute dress.", "베터리지 양반, 훼방 놓을 일이 생기지 않는 한, 나는 내일 프리징홀에 가서, 그 아이가 대체 옷을 지을 감을 남몰래 구할 때 시내에서 무엇을 샀는지를 알아내야겠소."], ["It’s a risk to leave the house, as things are now—but it’s a worse risk still to stir another step in this matter in the dark.", "지금 이런 마당에 집을 비우는 것은 모험이오만—이 일에서 캄캄한 채로 한 걸음 더 내딛는 것은 그보다 더한 모험이지."], ["Excuse my being a little out of temper; I’m degraded in my own estimation—I have let Rosanna Spearman puzzle me.”", "내 심기가 좀 상한 것을 너그러이 봐 주시오. 나는 내 스스로 보기에 체면을 구겼소—로재나 스피어먼이 나를 헷갈리게 하도록 내버려 뒀으니 말이오.\""]], [["When we got back, the servants were at supper.", "우리가 돌아왔을 때, 하인들은 저녁을 먹고 있었다."], ["The first person we saw in the outer yard was the policeman whom Superintendent Seegrave had left at the Sergeant’s disposal.", "바깥뜰에서 우리가 처음 마주친 사람은, 시그레이브 경정이 부장의 마음대로 쓰라고 남겨 둔 그 순경이었다."], ["The Sergeant asked if Rosanna Spearman had returned.", "부장은 로재나 스피어먼이 돌아왔는지 물었다. 그렇다고."], ["Yes. When?", "언제?"], ["Nearly an hour since.", "거의 한 시간 전에."], ["What had she done?", "무얼 했나?"], ["She had gone upstairs to take off her bonnet and cloak—and she was now at supper quietly with the rest.", "위층으로 올라가 보닛과 망토를 벗었고—지금은 나머지 사람들과 조용히 저녁을 먹고 있다고."]], [["Without making any remark, Sergeant Cuff walked on, sinking lower and lower in his own estimation, to the back of the house.", "아무 말도 없이, 커프 부장은, 제 스스로 보기에 갈수록 더 체면을 구긴 채, 집 뒤편으로 걸어갔다."], ["Missing the entrance in the dark, he went on (in spite of my calling to him) till he was stopped by a wicket-gate which led into the garden.", "어둠 속에서 입구를 놓친 그는, (내가 불러 세우는데도) 계속 걸어가다가, 정원으로 이어지는 쪽문에 막혀서야 멈춰 섰다."], ["When I joined him to bring him back by the right way, I found that he was looking up attentively at one particular window, on the bedroom floor, at the back of the house.", "내가 그를 바른길로 데려오려고 다가가 보니, 그는 집 뒤편 침실 층의 어느 한 창문을 유심히 올려다보고 있었다."]], [["Looking up, in my turn, I discovered that the object of his contemplation was the window of Miss Rachel’s room, and that lights were passing backwards and forwards there as if something unusual was going on.", "나도 이번에는 따라 올려다보고서, 그가 골똘히 바라보던 대상이 레이철 아가씨 방의 창문이며, 마치 무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 벌어지고 있기라도 하듯 그곳에서 불빛이 이리저리 오가고 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["“Isn’t that Miss Verinder’s room?”", "\"저것이 버린더 아가씨의 방 아니오?\""], ["asked Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 물었다."]], [["I replied that it was, and invited him to go in with me to supper.", "나는 그렇다고 대꾸하고, 나와 함께 안으로 들어가 저녁을 들자고 청했다."], ["The Sergeant remained in his place, and said something about enjoying the smell of the garden at night.", "부장은 제자리에 그대로 서서, 밤중 정원의 냄새를 즐기는 것에 대해 무어라 말했다."], ["I left him to his enjoyment.", "나는 그를 그 즐거움에 맡겨 두었다."], ["Just as I was turning in at the door, I heard “The Last Rose of Summer” at the wicket-gate.", "막 문 안으로 들어서려던 참에, 나는 그 쪽문께에서 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 들었다."], ["Sergeant Cuff had made another discovery!", "커프 부장이 또 하나를 알아낸 것이다!"], ["And my young lady’s window was at the bottom of it this time!", "그리고 이번에는 우리 아가씨의 창문이 그 밑바닥에 있었다!"]], [["The latter reflection took me back again to the Sergeant, with a polite intimation that I could not find it in my heart to leave him by himself.", "그 뒤엣것에 생각이 미치자 나는, 차마 그를 혼자 내버려 둘 마음이 나지 않는다는 정중한 뜻과 함께, 다시 부장에게로 돌아갔다."], ["“Is there anything you don’t understand up there?”", "\"저 위에 무언가 이해가 안 가는 게 있으십니까?\""], ["I added, pointing to Miss Rachel’s window.", "하고 나는 레이철 아가씨의 창문을 가리키며 덧붙였다."]], [["Judging by his voice, Sergeant Cuff had suddenly risen again to the right place in his own estimation.", "그 목소리로 미루어, 커프 부장은 별안간 다시 제 스스로 보기에 걸맞은 자리로 올라선 모양이었다."], ["“You are great people for betting in Yorkshire, are you not?”", "\"요크셔 사람들은 내기를 대단히 좋아하지 않소?\""], ["he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["“Suppose we are?”", "\"우리가 그렇다 칩시다?\""]], [["“If I was a Yorkshireman,”", "\"내가 만일 요크셔 사람이라면,\""], ["proceeded the Sergeant, taking my arm, “I would lay you an even sovereign, Mr.", "하고 부장이 내 팔을 잡으며 말을 이었다."], ["Betteredge, that your young lady has suddenly resolved to leave the house.", "\"당신에게 소버린 금화 한 닢을 대등하게 걸겠소, 베터리지 양반, 당신 아가씨가 별안간 이 집을 떠나기로 마음먹으셨다는 데 말이오."], ["If I won on that event, I should offer to lay another sovereign, that the idea has occurred to her within the last hour.”", "만일 그 내기에서 이긴다면, 나는 소버린 금화 한 닢을 더 걸겠다고 나서겠소, 그 생각이 아가씨께 지난 한 시간 안에 떠올랐다는 데 말이오.\""], ["The first of the Sergeant’s guesses startled me.", "부장의 첫 번째 짐작이 나를 흠칫하게 했다."], ["The second mixed itself up somehow in my head with the report we had heard from the policeman, that Rosanna Spearman had returned from the sands within the last hour.", "두 번째 짐작은, 로재나 스피어먼이 지난 한 시간 안에 그 모래밭에서 돌아왔다는, 우리가 순경에게서 들은 그 보고와 내 머릿속에서 어쩐지 뒤섞였다."], ["The two together had a curious effect on me as we went in to supper.", "우리가 저녁을 들러 들어갈 때, 그 둘이 함께 내게 야릇한 영향을 미쳤다."], ["I shook off Sergeant Cuff’s arm, and, forgetting my manners, pushed by him through the door to make my own inquiries for myself.", "나는 커프 부장의 팔을 뿌리치고는, 예의도 잊은 채, 내 나름으로 직접 알아보려고 그를 밀치고 문을 지나갔다."]], [["Samuel, the footman, was the first person I met in the passage.", "하인 새뮤얼이, 복도에서 내가 처음 마주친 사람이었다."]], [["“Her ladyship is waiting to see you and Sergeant Cuff,”", "\"마님께서 어르신과 커프 부장을 뵙자고 기다리고 계십니다.\""], ["he said, before I could put any questions to him.", "하고 그가, 내가 무어라 물어보기도 전에 말했다."]], [["“How long has she been waiting?”", "\"얼마나 오래 기다리셨나?\""], ["asked the Sergeant’s voice behind me.", "하고 내 등 뒤에서 부장의 소리가 물었다."]], [["“For the last hour, sir.”", "\"지난 한 시간째입니다, 나리.\""]], [["There it was again!", "또 그거였다!"], ["Rosanna had come back; Miss Rachel had taken some resolution out of the common; and my lady had been waiting to see the Sergeant—all within the last hour!", "로재나가 돌아왔고, 레이철 아가씨가 예사롭지 않은 무슨 결심을 했으며, 마님이 부장을 뵈려고 기다리고 계셨다—죄다 지난 한 시간 안에 말이다!"], ["It was not pleasant to find these very different persons and things linking themselves together in this way.", "이토록 서로 다른 사람들과 일들이 이런 식으로 서로 얽혀 드는 것을 보는 것은 유쾌한 노릇이 아니었다."], ["I went on upstairs, without looking at Sergeant Cuff, or speaking to him.", "나는 커프 부장을 쳐다보지도, 그에게 말을 건네지도 않고, 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["My hand took a sudden fit of trembling as I lifted it to knock at my mistress’s door.", "마님의 방문을 두드리려고 손을 들어 올리는데, 내 손이 별안간 부들부들 떨리는 발작을 일으켰다."]], [["“I shouldn’t be surprised,”", "\"놀랄 일도 아니오,\""], ["whispered the Sergeant over my shoulder, “if a scandal was to burst up in the house tonight.", "하고 부장이 내 어깨 너머로 속삭였다. \"오늘 밤 이 집에서 추문이 터진다 해도 말이오."], ["Don’t be alarmed!", "놀라지 마시오!"], ["I have put the muzzle on worse family difficulties than this, in my time.”", "나는 왕년에 이보다 더한 집안의 골칫거리도 입막음시켜 본 사람이오.\""]], [["As he said the words I heard my mistress’s voice calling to us to come in.", "그가 그 말을 하는데, 마님이 우리더러 들어오라 부르시는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVI", "# CHAPTER XVI"]], [["We found my lady with no light in the room but the reading-lamp.", "우리는 방에 독서등 말고는 아무 불도 없는 채로 마님을 뵈었다."], ["The shade was screwed down so as to overshadow her face.", "갓은 마님의 얼굴에 그늘이 지도록 아래로 돌려 내려져 있었다."], ["Instead of looking up at us in her usual straightforward way, she sat close at the table, and kept her eyes fixed obstinately on an open book.", "여느 때처럼 우리를 똑바로 올려다보시는 대신, 마님은 탁자에 바짝 붙어 앉아, 펼쳐 놓은 책 한 권에 눈길을 고집스레 박고 계셨다."]], [["“Officer,”", "\"관리 양반,\""], ["she said, “is it important to the inquiry you are conducting, to know beforehand if any person now in this house wishes to leave it?”", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그대가 벌이고 있는 조사에 있어, 지금 이 집에 있는 누군가가 이곳을 떠나고자 하는지를 미리 아는 것이 중요하오?\""]], [["“Most important, my lady.”", "\"더없이 중요합니다, 마님.\""]], [["“I have to tell you, then, that Miss Verinder proposes going to stay with her aunt, Mrs.", "\"그렇다면, 그대에게 이르지 않을 수 없구려. 버린더 아가씨가 제 이모 되는 프리징홀의 에이블화이트 부인 댁에 가 지내겠다 하오."], ["Ablewhite, of Frizinghall. She has arranged to leave us the first thing tomorrow morning.”", "내일 아침 일찍 우리를 떠나기로 정했소.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff looked at me.", "커프 부장이 나를 바라보았다."], ["I made a step forward to speak to my mistress—and, feeling my heart fail me (if I must own it), took a step back again, and said nothing.", "나는 마님께 말씀드리려고 한 걸음 앞으로 나아갔다가—(굳이 인정하자면) 가슴이 오그라드는 것을 느끼고, 다시 한 걸음 뒤로 물러나, 아무 말도 하지 못했다."]], [["“May I ask your ladyship when Miss Verinder informed you that she was going to her aunt’s?”", "\"버린더 아가씨가 이모 댁에 가시겠다고 마님께 언제 아뢰었는지 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""], ["inquired the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 물었다."]], [["“About an hour since,”", "\"한 시간쯤 전에 말이오.\""], ["answered my mistress.", "하고 마님이 대답하셨다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff looked at me once more.", "커프 부장이 다시 한번 나를 바라보았다."], ["They say old people’s hearts are not very easily moved.", "늙은이의 심장은 그리 쉽게 흔들리지 않는다고들 한다."], ["My heart couldn’t have thumped much harder than it did now, if I had been five-and-twenty again!", "내가 다시 스물다섯이 되었다 한들, 내 심장이 지금보다 더 세차게 쿵쾅거릴 수는 없었으리라!"]], [["“I have no claim, my lady,”", "\"마님, 저에게는,\" 하고 부장이 말한다."], ["says the Sergeant, “to control Miss Verinder’s actions.", "\"버린더 아가씨의 행동을 좌우할 아무런 권한도 없습니다."], ["All I can ask you to do is to put off her departure, if possible, till later in the day.", "제가 마님께 청할 수 있는 것이라곤, 될 수 있으면, 아가씨의 출발을 그날 좀 더 늦은 시각으로 미루어 달라는 것뿐입니다."], ["I must go to Frizinghall myself tomorrow morning—and I shall be back by two o’clock, if not before.", "저는 내일 아침 직접 프리징홀에 가야 합니다—그리고 더 이르지 않더라도, 두 시까지는 돌아오겠습니다."], ["If Miss Verinder can be kept here till that time, I should wish to say two words to her—unexpectedly—before she goes.”", "만일 버린더 아가씨를 그때까지 여기 붙들어 둘 수 있다면, 저는 아가씨가 떠나시기 전에—뜻밖에—두어 마디 여쭙고 싶습니다.\""]], [["My lady directed me to give the coachman her orders, that the carriage was not to come for Miss Rachel until two o’clock.", "마님은 내게, 두 시가 되기 전에는 마차를 레이철 아가씨께 대령하지 말라는 분부를 마부에게 전하라 이르셨다."], ["“Have you more to say?”", "\"더 할 말이 있소?\""], ["she asked of the Sergeant, when this had been done.", "하고 이 일이 끝나자 마님이 부장에게 물으셨다."]], [["“Only one thing, your ladyship.", "\"딱 한 가지입니다, 마님."], ["If Miss Verinder is surprised at this change in the arrangements, please not to mention Me as being the cause of putting off her journey.”", "만일 버린더 아가씨가 이 채비의 변경에 놀라시거든, 부디 아가씨의 여정을 미룬 원인으로 저를 들먹이지 말아 주십시오.\""]], [["My mistress lifted her head suddenly from her book as if she was going to say something—checked herself by a great effort—and, looking back again at the open page, dismissed us with a sign of her hand.", "마님은 무슨 말씀을 하시려는 듯 별안간 책에서 고개를 드셨다가—크게 애를 써서 스스로를 억누르시고는—다시 그 펼쳐진 쪽으로 눈길을 돌리시며, 손짓으로 우리를 물러가게 하셨다."]], [["“That’s a wonderful woman,”", "\"참으로 놀라운 여인이오.\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, when we were out in the hall again.", "하고 우리가 다시 큰방으로 나왔을 때 커프 부장이 말했다."], ["“But for her self-control, the mystery that puzzles you, Mr. Betteredge, would have been at an end tonight.”", "\"저 자제심만 아니었다면, 당신을 헷갈리게 하는 그 수수께끼는, 베터리지 양반, 오늘 밤 끝장이 났을 거요.\""]], [["At those words, the truth rushed at last into my stupid old head.", "그 말에, 마침내 그 진실이 나의 우둔한 늙은 머릿속으로 왈칵 밀려들었다."], ["For the moment, I suppose I must have gone clean out of my senses.", "그 순간, 짐작건대 나는 제정신을 아주 놓아 버렸던 게 틀림없다."], ["I seized the Sergeant by the collar of his coat, and pinned him against the wall.", "나는 부장의 저고리 깃을 움켜쥐고, 그를 벽에 밀어붙였다."]], [["“Damn you!”", "\"이런 빌어먹을!\""], ["I cried out, “there’s something wrong about Miss Rachel—and you have been hiding it from me all this time!”", "하고 나는 소리쳤다. \"레이철 아가씨한테 뭔가 잘못된 게 있고—당신은 여태 그걸 내게 숨겨 온 거요!\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff looked up at me—flat against the wall—without stirring a hand, or moving a muscle of his melancholy face.", "커프 부장은—벽에 납작하게 밀린 채—손 하나 까딱하지 않고, 그 우울한 얼굴의 근육 하나 움직이지 않고, 나를 올려다보았다."]], [["“Ah,”", "\"아,\""], ["he said, “you’ve guessed it at last.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"마침내 알아채셨구려.\""]], [["My hand dropped from his collar, and my head sunk on my breast.", "내 손이 그의 깃에서 떨어졌고, 내 고개가 가슴께로 떨궈졌다."], ["Please to remember, as some excuse for my breaking out as I did, that I had served the family for fifty years.", "내가 그런 식으로 발끈한 데 대한 얼마간의 변명으로, 부디 기억해 주시기 바란다. 나는 이 집안을 오십 년이나 섬겨 왔다는 것을."], ["Miss Rachel had climbed upon my knees, and pulled my whiskers, many and many a time when she was a child.", "레이철 아가씨는 어린 시절 몇 번이고, 또 몇 번이고 내 무릎에 기어올라, 내 구레나룻을 잡아당기곤 했다."], ["Miss Rachel, with all her faults, had been, to my mind, the dearest and prettiest and best young mistress that ever an old servant waited on, and loved.", "레이철 아가씨는, 그 온갖 흠에도 불구하고, 내 마음속에서, 늙은 하인이 여태 시중들고 사랑한 그 어떤 아가씨보다도 가장 애틋하고 가장 어여쁘고 가장 훌륭한 젊은 여주인이었다."], ["I begged Sergeant’s Cuff’s pardon, but I am afraid I did it with watery eyes, and not in a very becoming way.", "나는 커프 부장에게 용서를 빌었으나, 아무래도 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채, 그리 격에 맞지 않는 꼴로 빌었던 것 같다."]], [["“Don’t distress yourself, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"너무 상심 마시오, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["says the Sergeant, with more kindness than I had any right to expect from him.", "하고 부장이, 내가 그에게 바랄 자격이라곤 없는 다정함으로 말한다."], ["“In my line of life if we were quick at taking offence, we shouldn’t be worth salt to our porridge.", "\"우리네 직업에서, 걸핏하면 발끈하는 사람이라면, 죽 한 그릇에 칠 소금값어치도 못 되지."], ["If it’s any comfort to you, collar me again.", "위로가 좀 된다면, 다시 내 멱살을 잡으시오."], ["You don’t in the least know how to do it; but I’ll overlook your awkwardness in consideration of your feelings.”", "당신은 그걸 도무지 할 줄 모르오만, 당신 심정을 헤아려 그 서투름은 눈감아 주리다.\""]], [["He curled up at the corners of his lips, and, in his own dreary way, seemed to think he had delivered himself of a very good joke.", "그는 입아귀를 말아 올리고는, 제 나름의 그 처량한 방식으로, 아주 그럴듯한 농담 한마디를 던졌다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["I led him into my own little sitting-room, and closed the door.", "나는 그를 내 조그만 거실로 데리고 들어가, 문을 닫았다."]], [["“Tell me the truth, Sergeant,”", "\"사실대로 말해 주시오, 부장.\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “What do you suspect?", "\"무엇을 의심하는 거요?"], ["It’s no kindness to hide it from me now.”", "이제 와서 그걸 내게 숨기는 건 친절도 아니오.\""]], [["“I don’t suspect,”", "\"나는 의심하는 게 아니오.\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말했다."], ["“I know.”", "\"나는 아는 거요.\""]], [["My unlucky temper began to get the better of me again.", "내 그 불운한 성미가 다시 나를 이기기 시작했다."]], [["“Do you mean to tell me, in plain English,”", "\"그러니까 당신은, 쉬운 말로,\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “that Miss Rachel has stolen her own Diamond?”", "\"레이철 아가씨가 당신 다이아몬드를 손수 훔쳤다고 내게 말하려는 거요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["says the Sergeant; “that is what I mean to tell you, in so many words.", "하고 부장이 말한다. \"그것이, 이만큼 여러 말로, 내가 당신에게 하려는 말이오."], ["Miss Verinder has been in secret possession of the Moonstone from first to last; and she has taken Rosanna Spearman into her confidence, because she has calculated on our suspecting Rosanna Spearman of the theft.", "버린더 아가씨는 처음부터 끝까지 남몰래 월장석을 지니고 계셨소. 그리고 로재나 스피어먼을 제 심복으로 끌어들이셨지."], ["There is the whole case in a nutshell.", "우리가 로재나 스피어먼을 그 절도의 범인으로 의심하리라 셈하셨기 때문이오."], ["Collar me again, Mr. Betteredge.", "이 사건의 전모가 여기 한마디로 다 들었소."], ["If it’s any vent to your feelings, collar me again.”", "다시 멱살을 잡으시오, 베터리지 양반. 당신 심정을 풀어 주는 데 조금이라도 도움이 된다면, 다시 멱살을 잡으시오.\""]], [["God help me!", "하느님 저를 도우소서!"], ["my feelings were not to be relieved in that way.", "내 심정은 그런 식으로 풀릴 것이 아니었다."], ["“Give me your reasons!”", "\"그 이유를 대 보시오!\""], ["That was all I could say to him.", "그것이 내가 그에게 할 수 있는 말의 전부였다."]], [["“You shall hear my reasons tomorrow,”", "\"내 이유는 내일 들으실 거요.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“If Miss Verinder refuses to put off her visit to her aunt (which you will find Miss Verinder will do), I shall be obliged to lay the whole case before your mistress tomorrow.", "\"만일 버린더 아가씨가 이모 댁 방문을 미루기를 거절하신다면 (아가씨가 그러시리라는 것을 당신은 알게 될 거요), 나는 내일 이 사건의 전모를 마님 앞에 늘어놓지 않을 수 없소."], ["And, as I don’t know what may come of it, I shall request you to be present, and to hear what passes on both sides.", "그리고 그 결과가 어찌 될지 나로서는 알 수 없으니, 당신에게 그 자리에 있어, 양편에서 오가는 말을 들어 달라고 청하겠소."], ["Let the matter rest for tonight.", "이 일은 오늘 밤은 접어 둡시다."], ["No, Mr. Betteredge, you don’t get a word more on the subject of the Moonstone out of me.", "아니오, 베터리지 양반, 당신은 월장석에 관한 한 나에게서 더는 한마디도 못 얻어 낼 거요."], ["There is your table spread for supper.", "저기 당신 탁자에 저녁상이 차려져 있구려."], ["That’s one of the many human infirmities which I always treat tenderly.", "저것이 내가 늘 너그러이 대하는 그 숱한 사람의 약함 가운데 하나요."], ["If you will ring the bell, I’ll say grace.", "종을 울려 주시면, 내 감사 기도를 올리리다."], ["‘For what we are going to receive——’”", "'이제 저희가 받으려 하는 이 음식을 위하여—'\""]], [["“I wish you a good appetite to it, Sergeant,”", "\"부장, 맛있게 드시길 비오.\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “My appetite is gone.", "\"내 입맛은 가셨소."], ["I’ll wait and see you served, and then I’ll ask you to excuse me, if I go away, and try to get the better of this by myself.”", "나는 당신 상 차려지는 것까지 지켜본 다음, 자리를 뜨는 것을 양해해 달라 청하고, 이 심정을 나 혼자 다스려 보려 하오.\""]], [["I saw him served with the best of everything—and I shouldn’t have been sorry if the best of everything had choked him. The head gardener (Mr.", "나는 그에게 온갖 것 중 가장 좋은 것으로 상이 차려지는 것을 지켜보았다—그리고 그 온갖 가장 좋은 것이 그의 목을 막히게 했더라도 나는 조금도 애석하지 않았으리라."], ["Begbie) came in at the same time, with his weekly account.", "마침 그때 우두머리 정원사(벡비 씨)가 제 주간 장부를 들고 들어왔다."], ["The Sergeant got on the subject of roses and the merits of grass walks and gravel walks immediately.", "부장은 곧바로 장미 이야기와 잔디 길과 자갈 길의 우열에 관한 화제로 접어들었다."], ["I left the two together, and went out with a heavy heart.", "나는 그 둘을 내버려 두고, 무거운 마음으로 나갔다."], ["This was the first trouble I remember for many a long year which wasn’t to be blown off by a whiff of tobacco, and which was even beyond the reach of Robinson Crusoe.", "이것은 내가 여러 해 동안 기억하는 근심 가운데 처음으로, 담배 한 모금으로 날려 버릴 수 없는 것이었고, 심지어 『로빈슨 크루소』의 손길로도 미치지 못하는 것이었다."]], [["Being restless and miserable, and having no particular room to go to, I took a turn on the terrace, and thought it over in peace and quietness by myself.", "안절부절못하고 비참한 데다, 딱히 갈 방도 없어, 나는 테라스를 한 바퀴 돌며, 혼자 조용하고 고요하게 그 일을 곰곰이 되씹었다."], ["It doesn’t much matter what my thoughts were.", "내 생각이 무엇이었는지는 그리 대수롭지 않다."], ["I felt wretchedly old, and worn out, and unfit for my place—and began to wonder, for the first time in my life, when it would please God to take me.", "나는 처량하게 늙고, 지쳐 빠지고, 내 자리에 걸맞지 않은 처지라는 것을 느꼈다—그리고 평생 처음으로, 하느님께서 언제쯤 나를 데려가 주실지를 헤아려 보기 시작했다. 이 모든 것에도 불구하고, 나는 레이철 아가씨에 대한 내 믿음을 굳게 지켰다."], ["With all this, I held firm, notwithstanding, to my belief in Miss Rachel.", "커프 부장이 그 온갖 영화를 누리던 솔로몬이었고, 또 우리 아가씨가 비열하고 죄스러운 음모에 발을 담갔다고 내게 말했다 한들, 나는 아무리 지혜로운 솔로몬에게라도 단 하나의 대답밖에 없었으리라."], ["If Sergeant Cuff had been Solomon in all his glory, and had told me that my young lady had mixed herself up in a mean and guilty plot, I should have had but one answer for Solomon, wise as he was, “You don’t know her; and I do.”", "\"당신은 아가씨를 모르오. 나는 알고 말이오.\""]], [["My meditations were interrupted by Samuel.", "내 묵상은 새뮤얼에게 끊겼다."], ["He brought me a written message from my mistress.", "그는 내게 마님의 서면 전갈을 가져왔다."]], [["Going into the house to get a light to read it by, Samuel remarked that there seemed a change coming in the weather.", "그것을 읽을 불을 얻으려고 집 안으로 들어가면서, 새뮤얼이 날씨가 바뀔 것 같다고 말했다."], ["My troubled mind had prevented me from noticing it before.", "심란한 마음 탓에 나는 그 전까지 그것을 눈치채지 못했던 것이다."], ["But, now my attention was roused, I heard the dogs uneasy, and the wind moaning low.", "그러나 이제 주의가 일깨워지고 보니, 개들이 불안해하는 소리와, 바람이 낮게 신음하는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Looking up at the sky, I saw the rack of clouds getting blacker and blacker, and hurrying faster and faster over a watery moon.", "하늘을 올려다보니, 조각구름이 갈수록 시커메지며, 물기 어린 달 위로 갈수록 빠르게 휘몰려 가는 것이 보였다."], ["Wild weather coming—Samuel was right, wild weather coming.", "사나운 날씨가 오고 있었다—새뮤얼의 말이 옳았다, 사나운 날씨가 오고 있었다."]], [["The message from my lady informed me, that the magistrate at Frizinghall had written to remind her about the three Indians.", "마님의 전갈은 내게 알리기를, 프리징홀의 치안판사가 그 인도인 셋에 관해 마님께 상기시키는 편지를 보내왔다는 것이었다."], ["Early in the coming week, the rogues must needs be released, and left free to follow their own devices.", "다가오는 주 초에는, 그 악당들을 부득이 풀어 주어, 저희 마음대로 하게끔 놓아주어야 했다."], ["If we had any more questions to ask them, there was no time to lose.", "만일 우리가 그들에게 더 물어볼 것이 있다면, 지체할 겨를이 없었다."], ["Having forgotten to mention this, when she had last seen Sergeant Cuff, my mistress now desired me to supply the omission.", "마님이 지난번 커프 부장을 뵈었을 때 이 이야기를 하는 것을 잊으셨던 터라, 이제 내게 그 빠뜨린 것을 채우라 이르신 것이다."], ["The Indians had gone clean out of my head (as they have, no doubt, gone clean out of yours).", "그 인도인들은 내 머릿속에서 깨끗이 사라져 있었다 (틀림없이 여러분의 머릿속에서도 깨끗이 사라졌으리라)."], ["I didn’t see much use in stirring that subject again.", "그 화제를 다시 들쑤셔 무슨 소용이 있을까 싶었다."], ["However, I obeyed my orders on the spot, as a matter of course.", "그래도 나는, 당연한 노릇으로, 그 자리에서 분부에 따랐다."]], [["I found Sergeant Cuff and the gardener, with a bottle of Scotch whisky between them, head over ears in an argument on the growing of roses.", "나는 커프 부장과 정원사가, 그 둘 사이에 스카치 위스키 한 병을 놓고, 장미 재배를 두고 벌인 논쟁에 귀밑까지 푹 빠져 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The Sergeant was so deeply interested that he held up his hand, and signed to me not to interrupt the discussion, when I came in.", "부장은 어찌나 깊이 빠져 있던지, 내가 들어오자 손을 들어, 그 토론에 끼어들지 말라는 시늉을 했다."], ["As far as I could understand it, the question between them was, whether the white moss rose did, or did not, require to be budded on the dog-rose to make it grow well.", "내가 알아들은 바로는, 그들 사이의 문제란, 흰 이끼장미를 잘 자라게 하려면 그것을 들장미에 접붙여야 하느냐 마느냐 하는 것이었다."], ["Mr. Begbie said, Yes; and Sergeant Cuff said, No.", "벡비 씨는 그래야 한다고 하고, 커프 부장은 그럴 것 없다고 했다."], ["They appealed to me, as hotly as a couple of boys.", "그들은 사내아이 둘처럼 열을 올리며 내게 판정을 청했다."], ["Knowing nothing whatever about the growing of roses, I steered a middle course—just as her Majesty’s judges do, when the scales of justice bother them by hanging even to a hair.", "장미 재배에 관해서라곤 아무것도 모르는 나는, 정의의 저울이 머리카락 한 올만큼도 기울지 않고 팽팽하여 여왕 폐하의 재판관들을 애먹일 때 그들이 하는 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 가운뎃길을 잡았다."], ["“Gentlemen,”", "\"두 분,\""], ["I remarked, “there is much to be said on both sides.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"양편 다 할 말이 많은 노릇입니다.\""], ["In the temporary lull produced by that impartial sentence, I laid my lady’s written message on the table, under the eyes of Sergeant Cuff.", "그 공평무사한 판결이 자아낸 잠시의 소강상태를 틈타, 나는 마님의 서면 전갈을 커프 부장의 눈앞, 탁자 위에 내려놓았다."]], [["I had got by this time, as nearly as might be, to hate the Sergeant.", "이 무렵 나는, 될 수 있는 대로 가깝게 말하자면, 그 부장을 미워하기에 이르러 있었다."], ["But truth compels me to acknowledge that, in respect of readiness of mind, he was a wonderful man.", "그러나 진실을 위해 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없으니, 기지의 민첩함이라는 점에서, 그는 놀라운 사람이었다."]], [["In half a minute after he had read the message, he had looked back into his memory for Superintendent Seegrave’s report; had picked out that part of it in which the Indians were concerned; and was ready with his answer.", "그 전갈을 읽고 반 분도 안 되어, 그는 제 기억 속에서 시그레이브 경정의 보고를 되짚어, 그중 인도인들이 얽힌 대목을 골라냈고, 답을 낼 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["A certain great traveller, who understood the Indians and their language, had figured in Mr. Seegrave’s report, hadn’t he?", "인도인들과 그들의 말을 아는 어떤 대단한 여행가가 시그레이브 씨의 보고에 등장했었지, 그렇지 않소?"], ["Very well.", "좋소."], ["Did I know the gentleman’s name and address?", "그 신사분의 이름과 주소를 아시오?"], ["Very well again.", "그것도 좋소."], ["Would I write them on the back of my lady’s message?", "그것을 마님 전갈 뒷면에 적어 주시겠소?"], ["Much obliged to me.", "참으로 고맙소."], ["Sergeant Cuff would look that gentleman up, when he went to Frizinghall in the morning.", "커프 부장은 아침에 프리징홀에 가면 그 신사분을 찾아볼 참이었다."]], [["“Do you expect anything to come of it?” I asked.", "\"거기서 뭐라도 나올 걸로 보시오?\""], ["“Superintendent Seegrave found the Indians as innocent as the babe unborn.”", "하고 내가 물었다. \"시그레이브 경정은 그 인도인들이 갓난아기처럼 결백하다고 봤소만.\""]], [["“Superintendent Seegrave has been proved wrong, up to this time, in all his conclusions,”", "\"시그레이브 경정은, 지금까지, 제 온갖 결론에서 죄다 틀렸음이 드러났소.\""], ["answered the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 대답했다."], ["“It may be worth while to find out tomorrow whether Superintendent Seegrave was wrong about the Indians as well.”", "\"시그레이브 경정이 그 인도인들에 대해서도 틀렸는지 아닌지, 내일 알아볼 만한 노릇일지도 모르지.\""], ["With that he turned to Mr. Begbie, and took up the argument again exactly at the place where it had left off.", "그러고는 벡비 씨에게로 돌아서서, 논쟁이 끊겼던 바로 그 대목에서 다시 그것을 이어 갔다."], ["“This question between us is a question of soils and seasons, and patience and pains, Mr. Gardener.", "\"우리 사이의 이 문제는 흙과 철에 관한 문제요, 또 참을성과 공에 관한 문제지, 정원사 양반."], ["Now let me put it to you from another point of view.", "자, 다른 관점에서 그것을 짚어 봅시다."], ["You take your white moss rose——”", "당신이 흰 이끼장미를 든다 치면—\""]], [["By that time, I had closed the door on them, and was out of hearing of the rest of the dispute.", "그 무렵 나는, 그들에게 문을 닫아, 나머지 논쟁이 들리지 않는 데로 나와 있었다."]], [["In the passage, I met Penelope hanging about, and asked what she was waiting for.", "복도에서, 나는 페넬로피가 서성거리는 것을 만나, 무엇을 기다리느냐고 물었다."]], [["She was waiting for her young lady’s bell, when her young lady chose to call her back to go on with the packing for the next day’s journey.", "그 아이는 제 아가씨의 종을 기다리고 있었다. 아가씨가 다음 날 여정을 위한 짐 꾸리기를 마저 하러 저를 도로 부르실 때 울릴 그 종을 말이다."], ["Further inquiry revealed to me, that Miss Rachel had given it as a reason for wanting to go to her aunt at Frizinghall, that the house was unendurable to her, and that she could bear the odious presence of a policeman under the same roof with herself no longer.", "더 물어보고서야 나는, 레이철 아가씨가 프리징홀의 이모 댁에 가고 싶어 하는 이유로, 이 집이 당신께 견딜 수 없는 곳이 되었고, 한 지붕 아래 경찰관이 있다는 그 역겨운 존재를 더는 참을 수 없노라고 댄 것을 알게 되었다."], ["On being informed, half an hour since, that her departure would be delayed till two in the afternoon, she had flown into a violent passion.", "반 시간 전에 출발이 오후 두 시로 늦춰지리라는 말을 듣고, 아가씨는 격렬한 노여움을 터뜨렸다."], ["My lady, present at the time, had severely rebuked her, and then (having apparently something to say, which was reserved for her daughter’s private ear) had sent Penelope out of the room.", "그때 그 자리에 계시던 마님이 아가씨를 호되게 꾸짖으신 다음, (아무래도 따님만의 귀에 남겨 둘 무슨 하실 말씀이 있었던 듯) 페넬로피를 방에서 내보내셨다."], ["My girl was in wretchedly low spirits about the changed state of things in the house.", "내 딸아이는 집안이 이렇게 뒤바뀐 형편에 처량하게 풀이 죽어 있었다."], ["“Nothing goes right, father; nothing is like what it used to be.", "\"만사가 어그러져요, 아버지. 아무것도 예전 같지 않아요."], ["I feel as if some dreadful misfortune was hanging over us all.”", "무슨 무시무시한 불행이 우리 모두 위에 드리워져 있는 것만 같아요.\""]], [["That was my feeling too.", "그것은 나의 심정이기도 했다."], ["But I put a good face on it, before my daughter.", "그러나 나는 딸 앞에서는 태연한 낯을 지어 보였다."], ["Miss Rachel’s bell rang while we were talking.", "우리가 이야기하는 동안 레이철 아가씨의 종이 울렸다."], ["Penelope ran up the back stairs to go on with the packing.", "페넬로피는 짐 꾸리기를 마저 하러 뒤쪽 계단을 뛰어 올라갔다."], ["I went by the other way to the hall, to see what the glass said about the change in the weather.", "나는 다른 길로 큰방으로 가서, 날씨 변화에 대해 청우계가 뭐라 하는지 보려 했다."]], [["Just as I approached the swing-door leading into the hall from the servants’ offices, it was violently opened from the other side, and Rosanna Spearman ran by me, with a miserable look of pain in her face, and one of her hands pressed hard over her heart, as if the pang was in that quarter.", "하인들의 방에서 큰방으로 이어지는 그 회전문에 막 다가서던 참에, 그것이 반대편에서 거칠게 열리더니, 로재나 스피어먼이 내 곁을 지나 뛰어갔다. 얼굴에는 딱한 고통의 빛이 서려 있고, 한 손은, 마치 그 쥐어짜는 아픔이 그 어름에 있기라도 하듯, 제 가슴을 꽉 누른 채였다."], ["“What’s the matter, my girl?”", "\"무슨 일이냐, 얘야?\""], ["I asked, stopping her.", "하고 나는 그를 붙잡으며 물었다."], ["“Are you ill?”", "\"어디 아프냐?\""], ["“For God’s sake, don’t speak to me,”", "\"제발, 저한테 말 걸지 마세요.\""], ["she answered, and twisted herself out of my hands, and ran on towards the servants’ staircase.", "하고 그가 대답하고는, 몸을 비틀어 내 손을 빠져나와, 하인용 계단 쪽으로 뛰어갔다."], ["I called to the cook (who was within hearing) to look after the poor girl.", "나는 (들을 만한 데 있던) 요리사를 불러 그 딱한 아이를 살펴 달라 했다."], ["Two other persons proved to be within hearing, as well as the cook.", "요리사 말고도 다른 두 사람이 들을 만한 데 있었음이 드러났다."], ["Sergeant Cuff darted softly out of my room, and asked what was the matter.", "커프 부장이 내 방에서 소리 없이 튀어나와, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."], ["I answered, “Nothing.” Mr.", "나는 \"아무것도 아니오.\""], ["Franklin, on the other side, pulled open the swing-door, and beckoning me into the hall, inquired if I had seen anything of Rosanna Spearman.", "하고 대답했다. 다른 편에서는, 프랭클린 도련님이 그 회전문을 홱 열어젖히고, 나를 큰방으로 손짓해 부르며, 로재나 스피어먼을 본 적이 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“She has just passed me, sir, with a very disturbed face, and in a very odd manner.”", "\"방금 제 곁을 지나갔습니다, 나리. 몹시 심란한 얼굴로, 아주 이상한 모양으로요.\""]], [["“I am afraid I am innocently the cause of that disturbance, Betteredge.”", "\"아무래도 내가 죄 없이 그 심란함의 원인인 것 같네, 베터리지.\""]], [["“You, sir!”", "\"나리께서요!\""]], [["“I can’t explain it,” says Mr.", "\"나도 그걸 설명할 수가 없어.\""], ["Franklin; “but, if the girl is concerned in the loss of the Diamond, I do really believe she was on the point of confessing everything—to me, of all the people in the world—not two minutes since.”", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다. \"하지만, 만일 그 아이가 다이아몬드 실종에 얽혀 있는 것이라면, 나는 정말이지, 그 아이가 채 이 분도 안 되기 전에, 세상 모든 사람 중에서도 하필 나에게—모든 것을 털어놓기 직전이었다고 믿네.\""]], [["Looking towards the swing-door, as he said those last words, I fancied I saw it opened a little way from the inner side.", "그가 그 마지막 말을 하면서 회전문 쪽을 바라보는데, 나는 그것이 안쪽에서 조금 열리는 것을 본 듯한 착각이 들었다."]], [["Was there anybody listening?", "누가 엿듣고 있었던 걸까?"], ["The door fell to, before I could get to it.", "내가 거기 다다르기도 전에 그 문은 도로 닫혔다."], ["Looking through, the moment after, I thought I saw the tails of Sergeant Cuff’s respectable black coat disappearing round the corner of the passage.", "그 바로 다음 순간 안을 들여다보니, 커프 부장의 그 점잖은 검은 저고리 자락이 복도 모퉁이를 돌아 사라지는 것을 본 듯싶었다."], ["He knew, as well as I did, that he could expect no more help from me, now that I had discovered the turn which his investigations were really taking.", "그는, 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있었다. 그의 조사가 실은 어느 방향으로 향하고 있는지를 내가 알아챈 지금, 그가 나에게서 더는 도움을 바랄 수 없다는 것을."], ["Under those circumstances, it was quite in his character to help himself, and to do it by the underground way.", "그런 처지에서, 스스로 알아서 하되, 그것을 땅속 같은 은밀한 방식으로 하는 것은 그야말로 그다운 노릇이었다."]], [["Not feeling sure that I had really seen the Sergeant—and not desiring to make needless mischief, where, Heaven knows, there was mischief enough going on already—I told Mr. Franklin that I thought one of the dogs had got into the house—and then begged him to describe what had happened between Rosanna and himself.", "내가 정말로 부장을 본 것인지 확신이 서지 않는 데다—하늘도 아시다시피, 이미 재앙이 넘치도록 벌어지고 있는 마당에 공연히 재앙을 더 만들고 싶지도 않아, 나는 프랭클린 도련님에게 개 한 마리가 집 안에 들어온 것 같다고 말했다—그러고는 그에게 로재나와 그 자신 사이에 무슨 일이 있었는지 이야기해 달라고 청했다."]], [["“Were you passing through the hall, sir?”", "\"큰방을 지나가시던 중이었습니까, 나리?\" 하고 내가 물었다."], ["I asked. “Did you meet her accidentally, when she spoke to you?”", "\"그 아이가 나리께 말을 걸었을 때, 우연히 마주치신 겁니까?\""]], [["Mr. Franklin pointed to the billiard-table.", "프랭클린 도련님이 당구대를 가리켰다."]], [["“I was knocking the balls about,”", "\"나는 공이나 이리저리 쳐 대며,\""], ["he said, “and trying to get this miserable business of the Diamond out of my mind.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"이 지긋지긋한 다이아몬드 일을 머릿속에서 떨쳐 내려 하고 있었네."], ["I happened to look up—and there stood Rosanna Spearman at the side of me, like a ghost!", "마침 고개를 들었는데—내 곁에 로재나 스피어먼이 유령처럼 서 있지 뭔가!"], ["Her stealing on me in that way was so strange, that I hardly knew what to do at first.", "그런 식으로 내게 살금살금 다가온 것이 어찌나 야릇하던지, 처음엔 뭘 어째야 할지 도무지 알 수가 없었어."], ["Seeing a very anxious expression in her face, I asked her if she wished to speak to me.", "그 얼굴에 몹시 안달하는 기색이 서린 것을 보고, 나는 그 아이에게 내게 할 말이 있느냐고 물었지. 그 아이가 대답하기를, '예, 감히 그래도 된다면요.'"], ["She answered, ‘Yes, if I dare.’ Knowing what suspicion attached to her, I could only put one construction on such language as that.", "하더군. 그 아이에게 어떤 혐의가 걸려 있는지 아는 터라, 나는 그런 말을 오직 한 가지로밖에 새길 수 없었어."], ["I confess it made me uncomfortable.", "솔직히 그 말이 나를 불편하게 했네."], ["I had no wish to invite the girl’s confidence.", "나는 그 아이의 속내를 청해 들일 마음이 없었어."], ["At the same time, in the difficulties that now beset us, I could hardly feel justified in refusing to listen to her, if she was really bent on speaking to me.", "그와 동시에, 지금 우리를 에워싼 이 곤경 속에서, 만일 그 아이가 정말로 내게 말하기로 작정한 것이라면, 그것을 들어 주기를 거절하는 것도 도무지 떳떳할 수가 없었지. 난처한 처지였네."], ["It was an awkward position; and I dare say I got out of it awkwardly enough.", "그리고 아마 나는 그 처지를 제법 서투르게 빠져나왔을 거야. 나는 그 아이에게, '네 말을 잘 모르겠구나."], ["I said to her, ‘I don’t quite understand you.", "내가 해 주었으면 하는 게 뭐라도 있느냐?'"], ["Is there anything you want me to do?’ Mind, Betteredge, I didn’t speak unkindly!", "하고 말했어. 이보게, 베터리지, 나는 매정하게 말한 게 아니었네!"], ["The poor girl can’t help being ugly—I felt that, at the time.", "그 딱한 아이가 못생긴 거야 어쩔 수 없는 노릇이지—그때 나는 그걸 느꼈어."], ["The cue was still in my hand, and I went on knocking the balls about, to take off the awkwardness of the thing.", "큐대가 여전히 내 손에 들려 있어서, 나는 이 일의 어색함을 덜어 보려고 계속 공을 쳐 댔지."], ["As it turned out, I only made matters worse still.", "그런데 알고 보니, 나는 사태를 한층 더 나쁘게 만들었을 뿐이었어."], ["I’m afraid I mortified her without meaning it!", "아무래도 나는 뜻하지 않게 그 아이의 자존심을 상하게 한 것 같아!"], ["She suddenly turned away.", "그 아이가 별안간 돌아서더군."], ["‘He looks at the billiard balls,’ I heard her say.", "'저이는 당구공을 보네,' 하고 그 아이가 말하는 것이 들렸어."], ["‘Anything rather than look at me!’ Before I could stop her, she had left the hall.", "'나를 보느니 뭐든 다른 걸 보는 게 낫다는 거지!' 내가 그를 붙잡기도 전에, 그 아이는 큰방을 떠나 버렸어."], ["I am not quite easy about it, Betteredge.", "나는 그게 영 마음이 편치 않네, 베터리지."], ["Would you mind telling Rosanna that I meant no unkindness?", "미안하지만 로재나에게 내가 매정하게 굴 뜻은 없었다고 전해 주겠나?"], ["I have been a little hard on her, perhaps, in my own thoughts—I have almost hoped that the loss of the Diamond might be traced to her.", "내 속생각으로는, 어쩌면 내가 그 아이에게 좀 모질었는지도 몰라—나는 그 다이아몬드의 실종이 그 아이에게로 거슬러 밝혀지기를 은근히 바라기까지 했으니 말이야."], ["Not from any ill-will to the poor girl: but——”", "그 딱한 아이에게 무슨 악의가 있어서가 아니라, 다만—\""], ["He stopped there, and going back to the billiard-table, began to knock the balls about once more.", "그는 거기서 말을 멈추고, 당구대로 돌아가, 다시 한번 공을 이리저리 쳐 대기 시작했다."]], [["After what had passed between the Sergeant and me, I knew what it was that he had left unspoken as well as he knew it himself.", "부장과 나 사이에 오간 일을 겪은 뒤라, 나는 그가 입 밖에 내지 않고 남겨 둔 것이 무엇인지를 그 자신이 아는 것만큼이나 잘 알았다."]], [["Nothing but the tracing of the Moonstone to our second housemaid could now raise Miss Rachel above the infamous suspicion that rested on her in the mind of Sergeant Cuff.", "이제 오직 월장석이 우리 집 둘째 하녀에게로 거슬러 밝혀지는 것만이, 커프 부장의 마음속에서 레이철 아가씨를 짓누르고 있는 그 치욕스러운 혐의 위로 아가씨를 끌어올릴 수 있었다."], ["It was no longer a question of quieting my young lady’s nervous excitement; it was a question of proving her innocence.", "이제 그것은 우리 아가씨의 초조한 흥분을 가라앉히는 문제가 아니었다. 그것은 아가씨의 결백을 증명하는 문제였다."], ["If Rosanna had done nothing to compromise herself, the hope which Mr. Franklin confessed to having felt would have been hard enough on her in all conscience.", "만일 로재나가 제 몸에 혐의를 씌울 아무 짓도 하지 않았더라면, 프랭클린 도련님이 품었노라 실토한 그 바람은, 양심에 비추어 그 아이에게 이만저만 모진 것이 아니었으리라."], ["But this was not the case.", "그러나 사정은 그렇지가 않았다."], ["She had pretended to be ill, and had gone secretly to Frizinghall.", "그 아이는 앓는 척했고, 남몰래 프리징홀에 다녀왔다."], ["She had been up all night, making something or destroying something, in private.", "그 아이는 밤을 꼬박 새우며, 남모르게 무언가를 짓거나 무언가를 없애 버렸다."], ["And she had been at the Shivering Sand, that evening, under circumstances which were highly suspicious, to say the least of them.", "그리고 그 아이는 그날 저녁, 아무리 좋게 봐도 몹시 수상쩍은 정황 아래, 그 흔들리는 모래에 가 있었다."], ["For all these reasons (sorry as I was for Rosanna) I could not but think that Mr. Franklin’s way of looking at the matter was neither unnatural nor unreasonable, in Mr.", "이 모든 이유에서 (로재나가 딱하기는 했으나) 나는, 이 일을 바라보는 프랭클린 도련님의 방식이, 프랭클린 도련님의 처지에서는, 부자연스럽지도 이치에 어긋나지도 않는다고 생각하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Franklin’s position. I said a word to him to that effect.", "나는 그런 취지의 말을 그에게 한마디 했다."]], [["“Yes, yes!”", "\"그래, 그래!\""], ["he said in return.", "하고 그가 대꾸했다."], ["“But there is just a chance—a very poor one, certainly—that Rosanna’s conduct may admit of some explanation which we don’t see at present.", "\"하지만 딱 한 가닥 가망은 있어—물론 아주 희박한 것이지만—로재나의 처신에, 지금 우리 눈에는 안 보이는 무슨 설명이 있을지도 모른다는 가망 말이야."], ["I hate hurting a woman’s feelings, Betteredge!", "나는 여자의 마음을 아프게 하는 게 질색이네, 베터리지!"], ["Tell the poor creature what I told you to tell her.", "그 딱한 것에게, 내가 자네더러 전하라 한 그 말을 전해 주게."], ["And if she wants to speak to me—I don’t care whether I get into a scrape or not—send her to me in the library.”", "그리고 만일 그 아이가 내게 말하고 싶어 하거든—내가 곤경에 빠지든 말든 나는 아랑곳없으니—서재로 그를 보내 주게.\""], ["With those kind words he laid down the cue and left me.", "이 다정한 말과 함께 그는 큐대를 내려놓고 나를 떠났다."]], [["Inquiry at the servants’ offices informed me that Rosanna had retired to her own room.", "하인들의 방에 물어보니, 로재나가 제 방으로 물러갔다는 것이었다."], ["She had declined all offers of assistance with thanks, and had only asked to be left to rest in quiet.", "그 아이는 도와주겠다는 온갖 제안을 고마워하며 물리쳤고, 그저 조용히 쉬게 내버려 달라고만 청했다는 것이었다."], ["Here, therefore, was an end of any confession on her part (supposing she really had a confession to make) for that night.", "그러니, 여기서 그 아이 쪽에서 나올 어떤 고백도 (설령 정말로 할 고백이 있었다손 치더라도) 그날 밤은 끝난 셈이었다."], ["I reported the result to Mr. Franklin, who, thereupon, left the library, and went up to bed.", "나는 그 결과를 프랭클린 도련님께 알렸고, 그는 그러자 서재를 떠나, 잠자리에 들러 올라갔다."]], [["I was putting the lights out, and making the windows fast, when Samuel came in with news of the two guests whom I had left in my room.", "내가 불을 끄고 창문을 단단히 잠그고 있는데, 새뮤얼이 내가 내 방에 남겨 둔 두 손님에 관한 소식을 가지고 들어왔다."]], [["The argument about the white moss rose had apparently come to an end at last.", "흰 이끼장미에 관한 논쟁은 아무래도 마침내 끝난 모양이었다."], ["The gardener had gone home, and Sergeant Cuff was nowhere to be found in the lower regions of the house.", "정원사는 집으로 돌아갔고, 커프 부장은 집 아래층 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다."]], [["I looked into my room.", "나는 내 방을 들여다보았다."], ["Quite true—nothing was to be discovered there but a couple of empty tumblers and a strong smell of hot grog.", "과연 그러했다—거기서 발견할 수 있는 것이라곤 빈 텀블러 두 개와 뜨거운 그로그 술의 짙은 냄새뿐이었다."], ["Had the Sergeant gone of his own accord to the bedchamber that was prepared for him?", "부장이 제 발로 저를 위해 마련된 침실로 간 것일까?"], ["I went upstairs to see.", "나는 알아보러 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["After reaching the second landing, I thought I heard a sound of quiet and regular breathing on my left-hand side.", "두 번째 층계참에 이른 뒤, 나는 왼편에서 조용하고 고른 숨소리가 들리는 것 같았다."], ["My left-hand side led to the corridor which communicated with Miss Rachel’s room.", "내 왼편은 레이철 아가씨의 방과 통하는 복도로 이어져 있었다."], ["I looked in, and there, coiled up on three chairs placed right across the passage—there, with a red handkerchief tied round his grizzled head, and his respectable black coat rolled up for a pillow, lay and slept Sergeant Cuff!", "안을 들여다보니, 거기, 복도를 가로질러 나란히 놓인 의자 세 개 위에 몸을 웅크린 채—거기, 희끗한 머리에 붉은 손수건을 둘러 매고, 제 점잖은 검은 저고리를 말아 베개로 삼은 채, 커프 부장이 누워 자고 있는 게 아닌가!"]], [["He woke, instantly and quietly, like a dog, the moment I approached him.", "내가 그에게 다가서는 그 순간, 그는 개처럼, 곧바로 조용히 잠에서 깼다."]], [["“Good-night, Mr.", "\"안녕히 주무시오, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["Betteredge,” he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“And mind, if you ever take to growing roses, the white moss rose is all the better for not being budded on the dog-rose, whatever the gardener may say to the contrary!”", "\"그리고 명심하시오, 만일 당신이 언젠가 장미를 기르게 되거든, 흰 이끼장미는 들장미에 접붙이지 않는 편이 도리어 낫소, 정원사가 아무리 그 반대로 말하더라도 말이오!\""]], [["“What are you doing here?”", "\"여기서 뭘 하는 거요?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."], ["“Why are you not in your proper bed?”", "\"어째서 당신의 제 침대에 있지 않고?\""]], [["“I am not in my proper bed,”", "\"내가 내 제 침대에 있지 않은 것은,\""], ["answered the Sergeant, “because I am one of the many people in this miserable world who can’t earn their money honestly and easily at the same time.", "하고 부장이 대답했다. \"내가, 이 서글픈 세상에서, 제 밥벌이를 정직하게, 그러면서 동시에 편안하게 할 수는 없는 그 숱한 사람 가운데 하나이기 때문이오."], ["There was a coincidence, this evening, between the period of Rosanna Spearman’s return from the Sands and the period when Miss Verinder stated her resolution to leave the house.", "오늘 저녁, 로재나 스피어먼이 그 모래밭에서 돌아온 무렵과, 버린더 아가씨가 이 집을 떠나기로 한 결심을 밝힌 무렵이 우연히 겹쳤소."], ["Whatever Rosanna may have hidden, it’s clear to my mind that your young lady couldn’t go away until she knew that it was hidden.", "로재나가 무엇을 숨겼든 간에, 당신 아가씨는 그것이 숨겨졌다는 것을 알기 전에는 떠나실 수 없었으리라는 것이 내게는 뻔하오."], ["The two must have communicated privately once already tonight.", "그 둘은 오늘 밤 이미 한 번 남몰래 통했음이 틀림없소."], ["If they try to communicate again, when the house is quiet, I want to be in the way, and stop it.", "만일 그들이 집이 조용해졌을 때 다시 통하려 한다면, 나는 그 길목에 있다가, 그것을 막고 싶은 거요."], ["Don’t blame me for upsetting your sleeping arrangements, Mr. Betteredge—blame the Diamond.”", "당신 잠자리 채비를 어질러 놓았다고 나를 탓하지 마시오, 베터리지 양반—그 다이아몬드를 탓하시오.\""]], [["“I wish to God the Diamond had never found its way into this house!”", "\"맙소사, 그 다이아몬드가 이 집에 발을 들여놓는 일이 애초에 없었더라면 좋았을 것을!\""], ["I broke out.", "하고 나는 터뜨렸다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff looked with a rueful face at the three chairs on which he had condemned himself to pass the night.", "커프 부장은 제가 하룻밤을 지새우도록 스스로를 처넣은 그 의자 셋을 서글픈 얼굴로 바라보았다."]], [["“So do I,”", "\"나도 그렇소.\""], ["he said, gravely.", "하고 그가 무겁게 말했다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVII", "# CHAPTER XVII"]], [["Nothing happened in the night; and (I am happy to add) no attempt at communication between Miss Rachel and Rosanna rewarded the vigilance of Sergeant Cuff.", "밤에는 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다. 그리고 (기쁘게 덧붙이자면) 레이철 아가씨와 로재나 사이에 통하려는 어떤 시도도 커프 부장의 경계에 걸려들지 않았다."]], [["I had expected the Sergeant to set off for Frizinghall the first thing in the morning.", "나는 부장이 아침에 맨 먼저 프리징홀로 떠날 줄 알았다. 그러나 그는, 마치 먼저 할 다른 일이라도 있는 듯, 이리저리 서성거렸다."], ["He waited about, however, as if he had something else to do first.", "나는 그를 제 마음대로 하게 내버려 두었다."], ["I left him to his own devices; and going into the grounds shortly after, met Mr. Franklin on his favourite walk by the shrubbery side.", "그러고는 얼마 뒤 부지로 나갔다가, 관목 숲 곁 그 즐겨 걷는 산책길에서 프랭클린 도련님을 만났다."]], [["Before we had exchanged two words, the Sergeant unexpectedly joined us.", "우리가 두 마디도 채 나누기 전에, 부장이 뜻밖에 우리에게 합류했다."], ["He made up to Mr. Franklin, who received him, I must own, haughtily enough.", "그는 프랭클린 도련님께 다가갔는데, 도련님은, 인정하건대, 제법 도도하게 그를 맞았다."], ["“Have you anything to say to me?”", "\"내게 할 말이라도 있소?\""], ["was all the return he got for politely wishing Mr. Franklin good morning.", "하는 것이, 프랭클린 도련님께 정중히 좋은 아침 인사를 건넨 것에 대해 부장이 받은 대꾸의 전부였다."]], [["“I have something to say to you, sir,”", "\"나리께 드릴 말씀이 있습니다, 나리.\""], ["answered the Sergeant, “on the subject of the inquiry I am conducting here.", "하고 부장이 대답했다. \"제가 여기서 벌이고 있는 조사에 관해서 말입니다."], ["You detected the turn that inquiry was really taking, yesterday.", "나리는 어제 그 조사가 실은 어느 방향으로 향하고 있는지를 알아채셨지요."], ["Naturally enough, in your position, you are shocked and distressed.", "나리의 처지에서는, 지극히 당연하게도, 충격을 받고 괴로우실 겁니다."], ["Naturally enough, also, you visit your own angry sense of your own family scandal upon Me.”", "또한 지극히 당연하게도, 나리는 나리 집안의 추문에 대한 나리 자신의 노여운 심정을 저에게 쏟아 내시는 거고요.\""]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭘 바라는 거요?\""], ["Mr. Franklin broke in, sharply enough.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 제법 날카롭게 끼어들었다."]], [["“I want to remind you, sir, that I have at any rate, thus far, not been proved to be wrong.", "\"나리께 일깨워 드리고 싶습니다, 나리. 어쨌거나, 지금까지, 제가 틀렸다고 증명된 적은 없다는 것을요."], ["Bearing that in mind, be pleased to remember, at the same time, that I am an officer of the law acting here under the sanction of the mistress of the house.", "그것을 마음에 새기시되, 동시에, 부디 기억해 주십시오. 저는 이 댁 마님의 재가 아래 여기서 일하는 법의 관리라는 것을 말입니다."], ["Under these circumstances, is it, or is it not, your duty as a good citizen, to assist me with any special information which you may happen to possess?”", "이런 처지에서, 나리가 마침 지니고 계실지도 모르는 어떤 특별한 정보로 저를 돕는 것이, 선량한 시민으로서 나리의 의무입니까, 아닙니까?\""]], [["“I possess no special information,”", "\"나는 아무런 특별한 정보도 지니고 있지 않소.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff put that answer by him, as if no answer had been made.", "커프 부장은, 마치 아무 대답도 나오지 않았다는 듯, 그 대답을 옆으로 밀쳐 두었다."]], [["“You may save my time, sir, from being wasted on an inquiry at a distance,”", "\"나리는 제 시간이 먼 데서의 조사에 헛되이 쓰이는 것을 아껴 주실 수 있습니다, 나리.\""], ["he went on, “if you choose to understand me and speak out.”", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"만일 나리가 제 뜻을 알아차리고 툭 터놓기로 하신다면 말입니다.\""]], [["“I don’t understand you,” answered Mr.", "\"나는 당신 뜻을 못 알아듣겠소.\""], ["Franklin; “and I have nothing to say.”", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"그리고 나는 할 말이 없소.\""]], [["“One of the female servants (I won’t mention names) spoke to you privately, sir, last night.”", "\"여자 하인 하나가 (이름은 대지 않겠습니다) 어젯밤 나리께 남몰래 말을 걸었지요, 나리.\""]], [["Once more Mr. Franklin cut him short; once more Mr.", "또 한 번 프랭클린 도련님이 그의 말을 잘랐다."], ["Franklin answered, “I have nothing to say.”", "또 한 번 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"나는 할 말이 없소.\""]], [["Standing by in silence, I thought of the movement in the swing-door on the previous evening, and of the coat-tails which I had seen disappearing down the passage.", "잠자코 곁에 서서, 나는 전날 저녁 그 회전문이 움직이던 것과, 복도 저편으로 사라지던 것을 내가 본 그 저고리 자락을 떠올렸다."], ["Sergeant Cuff had, no doubt, just heard enough, before I interrupted him, to make him suspect that Rosanna had relieved her mind by confessing something to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "커프 부장은, 틀림없이, 내가 그의 말을 가로막기 직전에, 로재나가 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 무언가를 고백함으로써 제 마음의 짐을 덜었으리라 의심할 만큼은 충분히 엿들었던 것이다."]], [["This notion had barely struck me—when who should appear at the end of the shrubbery walk but Rosanna Spearman in her own proper person!", "이 생각이 내 머리를 스치기가 무섭게—관목 숲 산책길 끝에 나타난 것이 다름 아닌 로재나 스피어먼 본인이지 뭔가!"], ["She was followed by Penelope, who was evidently trying to make her retrace her steps to the house. Seeing that Mr.", "그 뒤로는 페넬로피가 따라오고 있었는데, 아무래도 그를 집으로 되돌려 세우려 애쓰는 눈치였다."], ["Franklin was not alone, Rosanna came to a standstill, evidently in great perplexity what to do next.", "프랭클린 도련님이 혼자가 아닌 것을 보고, 로재나는 우뚝 멈춰 섰다. 이제 어찌해야 할지 몰라 크게 당황한 기색이 역력했다."], ["Penelope waited behind her. Mr.", "페넬로피는 그 뒤에서 기다렸다."], ["Franklin saw the girls as soon as I saw them.", "프랭클린 도련님도 내가 그 아이들을 본 것과 동시에 그들을 보았다."], ["The Sergeant, with his devilish cunning, took on not to have noticed them at all.", "부장은, 그 악마 같은 교활함으로, 그들을 아예 눈치채지 못한 척했다."], ["All this happened in an instant.", "이 모든 일이 순식간에 벌어졌다."], ["Before either Mr. Franklin or I could say a word, Sergeant Cuff struck in smoothly, with an appearance of continuing the previous conversation.", "프랭클린 도련님이나 나나 미처 한마디 하기도 전에, 커프 부장이, 앞서의 대화를 이어 가는 척하며, 매끄럽게 끼어들었다."]], [["“You needn’t be afraid of harming the girl, sir,” he said to Mr.", "\"그 아이에게 해를 끼칠까 봐 두려워하실 것 없습니다, 나리.\""], ["Franklin, speaking in a loud voice, so that Rosanna might hear him.", "하고 그가, 로재나가 들을 수 있도록 큰 소리로 프랭클린 도련님께 말했다."], ["“On the contrary, I recommend you to honour me with your confidence, if you feel any interest in Rosanna Spearman.”", "\"오히려, 나리가 로재나 스피어먼에게 조금이라도 관심이 있으시다면, 저를 믿고 털어놓는 영광을 베풀어 주시기를 권합니다.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin instantly took on not to have noticed the girls either.", "프랭클린 도련님도 곧바로 그 아이들을 눈치채지 못한 척했다."], ["He answered, speaking loudly on his side:", "그도 제 쪽에서 큰 소리로 대답했다."]], [["“I take no interest whatever in Rosanna Spearman.”", "\"나는 로재나 스피어먼에게 아무런 관심도 없소.\""]], [["I looked towards the end of the walk.", "나는 산책길 끝 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["All I saw at the distance was that Rosanna suddenly turned round, the moment Mr. Franklin had spoken.", "그 거리에서 내가 본 것이라곤, 프랭클린 도련님이 말을 마치는 그 순간 로재나가 별안간 홱 돌아서는 것뿐이었다."], ["Instead of resisting Penelope, as she had done the moment before, she now let my daughter take her by the arm and lead her back to the house.", "바로 조금 전에 그러했던 것처럼 페넬로피에게 버티는 대신, 그는 이제 내 딸이 제 팔을 잡고 저를 집으로 도로 데려가도록 순순히 맡겼다."]], [["The breakfast-bell rang as the two girls disappeared—and even Sergeant Cuff was now obliged to give it up as a bad job!", "두 아이가 사라지는 것과 동시에 아침 식사를 알리는 종이 울렸다—그리고 이제는 커프 부장마저 이것을 가망 없는 일로 단념하지 않을 수 없었다!"], ["He said to me quietly, “I shall go to Frizinghall, Mr.", "그는 내게 나직이 말했다. \"나는 프리징홀에 가겠소, 베터리지 양반."], ["Betteredge; and I shall be back before two.”", "그리고 두 시 전에 돌아오겠소.\""], ["He went his way without a word more—and for some few hours we were well rid of him.", "그는 더 말 한마디 없이 제 갈 길을 갔다—그리하여 몇 시간 동안 우리는 그를 잘 떨쳐 낸 셈이었다."]], [["“You must make it right with Rosanna,”", "\"자네가 로재나와의 일을 바로잡아 줘야겠네.\""], ["Mr. Franklin said to me, when we were alone.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 우리 둘만 남았을 때 내게 말했다."], ["“I seem to be fated to say or do something awkward, before that unlucky girl.", "\"나는 저 불운한 아이 앞에서는 무슨 서툰 말이나 짓을 하도록 운명 지어진 것만 같아."], ["You must have seen yourself that Sergeant Cuff laid a trap for both of us.", "커프 부장이 우리 둘 다에게 덫을 놓은 것을, 자네도 직접 봤을 거야."], ["If he could confuse me, or irritate her into breaking out, either she or I might have said something which would answer his purpose.", "만일 그가 나를 헷갈리게 하거나, 그 아이를 자극해 무언가 터뜨리게 할 수 있었다면, 그 아이든 나든 그의 목적에 들어맞을 무슨 말을 했을지도 몰라."], ["On the spur of the moment, I saw no better way out of it than the way I took.", "그 순간의 기지로는, 내가 택한 그 방도 말고는 거기서 빠져나갈 더 나은 길이 보이지 않았어."], ["It stopped the girl from saying anything, and it showed the Sergeant that I saw through him.", "그것이 그 아이가 무슨 말을 하는 것을 막았고, 또 부장에게는 내가 그 속을 꿰뚫어 봤다는 것을 보여 줬지."], ["He was evidently listening, Betteredge, when I was speaking to you last night.”", "그가 엿듣고 있었던 게 분명해, 베터리지, 내가 어젯밤 자네에게 말할 때 말이야.\""]], [["He had done worse than listen, as I privately thought to myself. He had remembered my telling him that the girl was in love with Mr.", "그는 엿듣는 것보다 더한 짓을 했다고, 나는 속으로 생각했다."], ["Franklin; and he had calculated on that, when he appealed to Mr. Franklin’s interest in Rosanna—in Rosanna’s hearing.", "그는 그 아이가 프랭클린 도련님께 반해 있다고 내가 그에게 말한 것을 기억해 두었고, 로재나가 듣는 데서 프랭클린 도련님의 로재나에 대한 관심에 호소할 때, 바로 그것을 셈에 넣었던 것이다."]], [["“As to listening, sir,”", "\"엿듣는 것으로 말하자면, 나리,\""], ["I remarked (keeping the other point to myself), “we shall all be rowing in the same boat if this sort of thing goes on much longer.", "하고 나는 (그 다른 대목은 속에 묻어 둔 채) 말했다. \"이런 일이 이대로 더 오래 이어진다면, 우리 모두가 한배를 젓는 신세가 될 겁니다."], ["Prying, and peeping, and listening are the natural occupations of people situated as we are.", "훔쳐보고, 엿보고, 엿듣는 것이 우리 같은 처지에 놓인 사람들의 자연스러운 소일거리지요."], ["In another day or two, Mr. Franklin, we shall all be struck dumb together—for this reason, that we shall all be listening to surprise each other’s secrets, and all know it.", "하루 이틀만 더 지나면, 프랭클린 도련님, 우리는 다 함께 벙어리가 되고 말 겁니다—그 까닭인즉, 우리가 다들 서로의 비밀을 불시에 캐내려고 엿듣고 있을 것이요, 또 다들 그것을 알고 있을 테니까요."], ["Excuse my breaking out, sir.", "제가 발끈한 것을 용서하십시오, 나리."], ["The horrid mystery hanging over us in this house gets into my head like liquor, and makes me wild.", "이 집에 우리 위로 드리워진 그 소름 끼치는 수수께끼가 술처럼 제 머릿속에 스며들어, 저를 사납게 만듭니다."], ["I won’t forget what you have told me.", "나리가 제게 이르신 것을 잊지 않겠습니다."], ["I’ll take the first opportunity of making it right with Rosanna Spearman.”", "첫 기회를 잡아 로재나 스피어먼과의 일을 바로잡겠습니다.\""]], [["“You haven’t said anything to her yet about last night, have you?”", "\"자네, 어젯밤 일에 대해서는 아직 그 아이에게 아무 말도 안 했지, 그렇지?\""], ["Mr. Franklin asked.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 물었다."]], [["“No, sir.”", "\"안 했습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“Then say nothing now.", "\"그럼 지금은 아무 말도 말게."], ["I had better not invite the girl’s confidence, with the Sergeant on the look-out to surprise us together.", "부장이 우리 둘을 함께 덮치려고 지켜보는 마당에, 나는 그 아이의 속내를 청해 듣지 않는 편이 낫겠어."], ["My conduct is not very consistent, Betteredge—is it?", "내 처신이 그리 앞뒤가 맞지는 않지, 베터리지—안 그런가?"], ["I see no way out of this business, which isn’t dreadful to think of, unless the Diamond is traced to Rosanna.", "나는 이 일에서, 생각만 해도 끔찍하지 않은 빠져나갈 길이라곤, 그 다이아몬드가 로재나에게로 거슬러 밝혀지는 것 말고는 도무지 안 보여."], ["And yet I can’t, and won’t, help Sergeant Cuff to find the girl out.”", "그러면서도 나는 커프 부장이 그 아이를 캐내는 것을 도울 수가 없고, 돕지도 않겠어.\""]], [["Unreasonable enough, no doubt.", "의심할 여지 없이, 어지간히 이치에 안 맞는 노릇이었다."], ["But it was my state of mind as well.", "그러나 그것은 나의 심정이기도 했다."], ["I thoroughly understood him.", "나는 그를 속속들이 이해했다."], ["If you will, for once in your life, remember that you are mortal, perhaps you will thoroughly understand him too.", "만일 여러분이, 평생에 한 번만이라도, 여러분도 죽을 목숨임을 기억한다면, 아마 여러분도 그를 속속들이 이해하시리라."]], [["The state of things, indoors and out, while Sergeant Cuff was on his way to Frizinghall, was briefly this:", "커프 부장이 프리징홀로 가는 동안, 집 안팎의 형편은, 간추리자면 이러했다."]], [["Miss Rachel waited for the time when the carriage was to take her to her aunt’s, still obstinately shut up in her own room.", "레이철 아가씨는, 여전히 고집스레 제 방에 틀어박힌 채, 마차가 저를 이모 댁으로 데려갈 시각을 기다렸다."], ["My lady and Mr. Franklin breakfasted together.", "마님과 프랭클린 도련님은 함께 아침을 들었다."], ["After breakfast, Mr. Franklin took one of his sudden resolutions, and went out precipitately to quiet his mind by a long walk.", "아침 식사 뒤, 프랭클린 도련님은 그 느닷없는 결심 가운데 하나를 내리고는, 오래 걸으며 마음을 달래려고 부랴부랴 밖으로 나갔다."], ["I was the only person who saw him go; and he told me he should be back before the Sergeant returned.", "그가 나가는 것을 본 사람은 나뿐이었다. 그리고 그는 내게 부장이 돌아오기 전에 돌아오겠다고 말했다."], ["The change in the weather, foreshadowed overnight, had come.", "밤사이 예고되었던 날씨의 변화가 찾아왔다."], ["Heavy rain had been followed soon after dawn, by high wind.", "새벽 직후 세찬 비가 내리더니, 뒤이어 거센 바람이 불었다."], ["It was blowing fresh, as the day got on.", "날이 밝아 갈수록 바람은 상쾌하게 불었다."], ["But though the clouds threatened more than once, the rain still held off.", "그러나 구름이 몇 번이고 을러댔음에도, 비는 여전히 참고 있었다."], ["It was not a bad day for a walk, if you were young and strong, and could breast the great gusts of wind which came sweeping in from the sea.", "젊고 튼튼하여, 바다에서 휘몰아쳐 오는 그 큰 돌풍을 가슴으로 맞받을 수 있는 사람이라면, 산책하기에 나쁜 날은 아니었다."]], [["I attended my lady after breakfast, and assisted her in the settlement of our household accounts.", "나는 아침 식사 뒤 마님을 모시고, 집안 살림 장부를 셈하시는 것을 거들었다."], ["She only once alluded to the matter of the Moonstone, and that was in the way of forbidding any present mention of it between us.", "마님은 월장석 이야기를 딱 한 번 넌지시 꺼내셨는데, 그것도 우리 사이에서 지금은 그 이야기를 하지 못하게 하려는 뜻에서였다."], ["“Wait till that man comes back,”", "\"저 사람이 돌아올 때까지 기다려라.\""], ["she said, meaning the Sergeant.", "하고 마님은, 부장을 두고 말씀하셨다."], ["“We must speak of it then: we are not obliged to speak of it now.”", "\"그때는 그것을 이야기해야만 할 게다. 지금은 그것을 이야기할 까닭이 없느니라.\""]], [["After leaving my mistress, I found Penelope waiting for me in my room.", "마님 곁을 떠난 뒤, 나는 페넬로피가 내 방에서 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“I wish, father, you would come and speak to Rosanna,”", "\"아버지, 아버지가 좀 와서 로재나한테 말 좀 걸어 주셨으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["she said.", "하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["“I am very uneasy about her.”", "\"그 애 때문에 마음이 몹시 안 좋아요.\""]], [["I suspected what was the matter readily enough.", "무슨 일인지 나는 냉큼 짐작이 갔다."], ["But it is a maxim of mine that men (being superior creatures) are bound to improve women—if they can.", "그러나 사내란 (우월한 존재이니만큼) 여자를 나아지게 할 의무가 있다는 것이—할 수만 있다면 말이다—나의 격언이다."], ["When a woman wants me to do anything (my daughter, or not, it doesn’t matter), I always insist on knowing why.", "여자가 내게 무언가를 해 주기를 바랄 때는 (내 딸이든 아니든 상관없다), 나는 늘 그 까닭을 아는 것을 고집한다."], ["The oftener you make them rummage their own minds for a reason, the more manageable you will find them in all the relations of life.", "여자들에게 까닭을 찾느라 제 마음속을 더 자주 뒤지게 만들수록, 삶의 온갖 관계에서 그들을 더 다루기 쉬워지는 것을 알게 되리라."], ["It isn’t their fault (poor wretches!) that they act first and think afterwards; it’s the fault of the fools who humour them.", "그들이 (딱한 것들!) 먼저 저지르고 나중에 생각하는 것은 그들의 잘못이 아니다. 그들의 비위를 맞춰 주는 바보들의 잘못이다."]], [["Penelope’s reason why, on this occasion, may be given in her own words.", "이번 경우에 페넬로피가 댄 까닭은 그 아이 자신의 말로 옮겨 볼 수 있다. \"아버지, 저는요,\""], ["“I am afraid, father,”", "하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["she said, “Mr. Franklin has hurt Rosanna cruelly, without intending it.”", "\"프랭클린 도련님이 뜻하지 않게 로재나를 모질게 아프게 하신 것 같아 걱정이에요.\""]], [["“What took Rosanna into the shrubbery walk?”", "\"무엇 때문에 로재나가 관목 숲 산책길로 갔던 거냐?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“Her own madness,”", "\"제 광기 때문이죠.\""], ["says Penelope; “I can call it nothing else.", "하고 페넬로피가 말한다. \"달리 뭐라 부를 수가 없어요."], ["She was bent on speaking to Mr. Franklin, this morning, come what might of it.", "그 앤 오늘 아침, 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 기어이 프랭클린 도련님께 말하려고 작정했어요."], ["I did my best to stop her; you saw that.", "저는 그 앨 막으려고 애를 다 썼어요."], ["If I could only have got her away before she heard those dreadful words——”", "아버지도 그건 보셨잖아요. 그 애가 그 무시무시한 말을 듣기 전에 제가 그 앨 데리고 가기만 했더라도—\""]], [["“There!", "\"저런!"], ["there!”", "저런!\""], ["I said, “don’t lose your head.", "하고 내가 말했다. \"정신 놓지 마라."], ["I can’t call to mind that anything happened to alarm Rosanna.”", "로재나를 놀래킬 무슨 일이 있었는지 나는 도무지 떠오르질 않는구나.\""]], [["“Nothing to alarm her, father.", "\"놀래킬 건 없었어요, 아버지."], ["But Mr. Franklin said he took no interest whatever in her—and, oh, he said it in such a cruel voice!”", "하지만 프랭클린 도련님이 그 애한테 아무런 관심도 없다고 하셨잖아요—게다가, 아, 어찌나 모진 목소리로 그러시던지요!\""]], [["“He said it to stop the Sergeant’s mouth,”", "\"그건 부장의 입을 막으려고 하신 말이다.\""], ["I answered.", "하고 내가 대답했다."]], [["“I told her that,” says Penelope.", "\"그건 저도 그 애한테 말해 줬어요.\""], ["“But you see, father (though Mr.", "하고 페넬로피가 말한다."], ["Franklin isn’t to blame), he’s been mortifying and disappointing her for weeks and weeks past; and now this comes on the top of it all!", "\"하지만 보세요, 아버지 (프랭클린 도련님을 탓할 건 아니지만), 도련님은 몇 주 몇 주째 그 애 자존심을 상하게 하고 실망시켜 오셨어요. 그런데 이제 그 모든 것 위에 이 일까지 겹친 거예요!"], ["She has no right, of course, to expect him to take any interest in her.", "물론 그 애가 도련님이 저한테 관심을 가져 주기를 바랄 자격은 없죠."], ["It’s quite monstrous that she should forget herself and her station in that way.", "그 애가 그런 식으로 저 자신과 제 신분을 잊는다는 건 정말 터무니없는 일이에요."], ["But she seems to have lost pride, and proper feeling, and everything.", "하지만 그 앤 자존심이며, 온당한 분별이며, 죄다 잃어버린 것 같아요."], ["She frightened me, father, when Mr. Franklin said those words.", "프랭클린 도련님이 그 말을 하셨을 때, 아버지, 그 애가 저를 겁나게 했어요."], ["They seemed to turn her into stone.", "그 말이 그 애를 돌덩이로 바꿔 놓는 것 같았어요."], ["A sudden quiet came over her, and she has gone about her work, ever since, like a woman in a dream.”", "별안간 그 애한테 고요함이 내려앉더니, 그때부터 죽 그 앤 마치 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 제 일을 해요.\""]], [["I began to feel a little uneasy.", "나는 조금 마음이 편치 않아지기 시작했다."], ["There was something in the way Penelope put it which silenced my superior sense.", "페넬로피가 그것을 늘어놓는 그 태도에는, 나의 우월한 분별을 잠재우는 무언가가 있었다."], ["I called to mind, now my thoughts were directed that way, what had passed between Mr.", "이제 내 생각이 그리로 향하고 보니, 나는 밤사이 프랭클린 도련님과 로재나 사이에 오간 일을 떠올렸다."], ["Franklin and Rosanna overnight.", "그때 그 아이는 가슴이 도려내진 듯한 얼굴이었다."], ["She looked cut to the heart on that occasion; and now, as ill-luck would have it, she had been unavoidably stung again, poor soul, on the tender place.", "그리고 이제, 불운하게도, 그 딱한 것은 어쩔 수 없이 그 여린 자리를 또 한 번 찔린 것이다. 딱하기도 하지!"], ["Sad! sad!—all the more sad because the girl had no reason to justify her, and no right to feel it.", "딱하기도 해!—그 아이에게 저를 정당화할 아무런 까닭도 없고, 그것을 서러워할 아무런 권리도 없다는 데서, 한층 더 딱한 노릇이었다."]], [["I had promised Mr. Franklin to speak to Rosanna, and this seemed the fittest time for keeping my word.", "나는 프랭클린 도련님께 로재나에게 말을 걸겠다고 약속한 터였고, 지금이 그 약속을 지키기에 가장 알맞은 때인 듯했다."]], [["We found the girl sweeping the corridor outside the bedrooms, pale and composed, and neat as ever in her modest print dress.", "우리는 그 아이가 침실들 바깥 복도를 쓸고 있는 것을 보았는데, 파리하고 차분한 데다, 그 수수한 무명 드레스 차림이 여느 때처럼 말끔했다."], ["I noticed a curious dimness and dullness in her eyes—not as if she had been crying but as if she had been looking at something too long.", "나는 그 눈에 어린 야릇한 흐릿함과 멍함을 눈여겨보았다—운 것 같은 기색이 아니라, 무언가를 너무 오래 바라본 것 같은 기색이었다."], ["Possibly, it was a misty something raised by her own thoughts.", "어쩌면 그것은 제 생각이 자아낸 어떤 안개 같은 것이었는지도 모른다."], ["There was certainly no object about her to look at which she had not seen already hundreds on hundreds of times.", "그 언저리에는 확실히, 그 아이가 이미 수백 번 수백 번 보지 않은, 바라볼 만한 대상이라곤 하나도 없었으니 말이다."]], [["“Cheer up, Rosanna!” I said.", "\"기운 내라, 로재나!\""], ["“You mustn’t fret over your own fancies.", "하고 내가 말했다. \"네 부질없는 공상으로 애태우면 못써."], ["I have got something to say to you from Mr. Franklin.”", "프랭클린 도련님께서 전하라 하신 말씀이 있단다.\""]], [["I thereupon put the matter in the right view before her, in the friendliest and most comforting words I could find.", "그리하여 나는, 찾아낼 수 있는 가장 다정하고 가장 위로가 되는 말로, 그 일을 그 아이 앞에 바르게 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["My principles, in regard to the other sex, are, as you may have noticed, very severe.", "이성(異性)에 관한 한, 여러분도 눈치채셨겠지만, 나의 원칙은 아주 엄격하다."], ["But somehow or other, when I come face to face with the women, my practice (I own) is not conformable.", "그러나 어쩐 일인지, 막상 여자들과 얼굴을 맞대고 보면, 나의 실천은 (인정하건대) 그에 걸맞지가 않다."]], [["“Mr. Franklin is very kind and considerate.", "\"프랭클린 도련님은 참 자상하고 사려 깊으세요."], ["Please to thank him.”", "부디 도련님께 고맙다고 전해 주세요.\""], ["That was all the answer she made me.", "그것이 그 아이가 내게 한 대답의 전부였다."]], [["My daughter had already noticed that Rosanna went about her work like a woman in a dream.", "내 딸은 이미 로재나가 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 제 일을 한다고 눈여겨본 터였다."], ["I now added to this observation, that she also listened and spoke like a woman in a dream.", "나는 이제 이 관찰에, 그 아이가 또한 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 듣고 말한다는 것을 덧붙였다."], ["I doubted if her mind was in a fit condition to take in what I had said to her.", "나는 그 아이의 정신이 내가 그에게 한 말을 받아들일 만한 상태인지 미심쩍었다."]], [["“Are you quite sure, Rosanna, that you understand me?”", "\"정말 확실하냐, 로재나, 내 말을 알아들었어?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“Quite sure.”", "\"정말 확실해요.\""]], [["She echoed me, not like a living woman, but like a creature moved by machinery.", "그 아이는 내 말을 되뇌었으나, 살아 있는 여자처럼이 아니라, 기계 장치로 움직이는 존재처럼 그리했다."], ["She went on sweeping all the time.", "그러는 내내 그 아이는 계속 비질을 했다."], ["I took away the broom as gently and as kindly as I could.", "나는 될 수 있는 대로 부드럽고 다정하게 그 빗자루를 치웠다."]], [["“Come, come, my girl!”", "\"자, 자, 얘야!\""], ["I said, “this is not like yourself.", "하고 내가 말했다. \"이건 네 답지가 않구나."], ["You have got something on your mind.", "뭔가 마음에 걸리는 게 있지."], ["I’m your friend—and I’ll stand your friend, even if you have done wrong.", "나는 네 벗이다—그리고 나는, 네가 설령 잘못을 저질렀더라도, 네 벗으로 있어 주마."], ["Make a clean breast of it, Rosanna—make a clean breast of it!”", "죄다 털어놓으렴, 로재나—죄다 털어놓으렴!\""]], [["The time had been, when my speaking to her in that way would have brought the tears into her eyes.", "내가 그런 식으로 말하면 그 아이의 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁해지던 때가 있었다."], ["I could see no change in them now.", "이제 그 눈에서 나는 아무 변화도 볼 수 없었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"예,\""], ["she said, “I’ll make a clean breast of it.”", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"죄다 털어놓을게요.\""]], [["“To my lady?”", "\"마님께?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“To Mr. Franklin?”", "\"프랭클린 도련님께?\""]], [["“Yes; to Mr. Franklin.”", "\"예, 프랭클린 도련님께요.\""]], [["I hardly knew what to say to that.", "나는 거기에 무어라 말해야 할지 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["She was in no condition to understand the caution against speaking to him in private, which Mr. Franklin had directed me to give her.", "그 아이는 프랭클린 도련님이 내게 그에게 주라고 이르신, 그와 은밀히 이야기하지 말라는 그 주의를 알아들을 상태가 아니었다."], ["Feeling my way, little by little, I only told her Mr. Franklin had gone out for a walk.", "나는 조금씩 조심조심 더듬어 가며, 그저 프랭클린 도련님이 산책하러 나가셨다고만 일러 주었다."]], [["“It doesn’t matter,”", "\"괜찮아요.\""], ["she answered.", "하고 그 아이가 대답했다."], ["“I shan’t trouble Mr. Franklin, today.”", "\"오늘은 프랭클린 도련님을 성가시게 하지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Why not speak to my lady?” I said.", "\"어째서 마님께 말씀드리지 않고?\""], ["“The way to relieve your mind is to speak to the merciful and Christian mistress who has always been kind to you.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"네 마음을 더는 길은, 늘 네게 다정하셨던 그 자비롭고 신앙심 깊은 마님께 말씀드리는 거란다.\""]], [["She looked at me for a moment with a grave and steady attention, as if she was fixing what I said in her mind.", "그 아이는 잠시, 마치 내 말을 제 마음속에 새겨 두려는 듯, 진지하고 흔들림 없는 눈길로 나를 바라보았다."], ["Then she took the broom out of my hands and moved off with it slowly, a little way down the corridor.", "그러더니 내 손에서 빗자루를 빼앗아 들고, 그것을 든 채 복도를 따라 조금 떨어진 데로 천천히 옮겨 갔다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["she said, going on with her sweeping, and speaking to herself; “I know a better way of relieving my mind than that.”", "하고 그 아이가, 비질을 이어 가며, 저 혼자 말했다. \"저는 그것보다 제 마음을 더는 더 나은 길을 알아요.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"그게 뭐냐?\""]], [["“Please to let me go on with my work.”", "\"부디 제 일을 계속하게 놔둬 주세요.\""]], [["Penelope followed her, and offered to help her.", "페넬로피가 그를 따라가, 도와주겠다고 나섰다."]], [["She answered, “No.", "그 아이가 대답했다. \"아니."], ["I want to do my work.", "나는 내 일을 하고 싶어."], ["Thank you, Penelope.”", "고마워, 페넬로피.\""], ["She looked round at me.", "그러고는 나를 돌아보았다."], ["“Thank you, Mr. Betteredge.”", "\"고맙습니다, 베터리지 씨.\""]], [["There was no moving her—there was nothing more to be said.", "그 아이를 움직일 도리가 없었다—더는 할 말이 없었다."], ["I signed to Penelope to come away with me.", "나는 페넬로피에게 나와 함께 물러가자고 손짓했다."], ["We left her, as we had found her, sweeping the corridor, like a woman in a dream.", "우리는 그 아이를, 우리가 발견했을 때 그대로, 꿈속을 헤매는 여자처럼 복도를 쓸고 있는 채로 두고 떠났다."]], [["“This is a matter for the doctor to look into,”", "\"이건 의사가 들여다봐야 할 일이다.\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “It’s beyond me.”", "\"내 힘으로는 어림없어.\""]], [["My daughter reminded me of Mr. Candy’s illness, owing (as you may remember) to the chill he had caught on the night of the dinner-party.", "내 딸은 캔디 씨의 병을 내게 일깨워 주었는데, 그것은 (여러분도 기억하시겠지만) 그가 만찬 날 밤 걸린 오한 탓이었다."], ["His assistant—a certain Mr. Ezra Jennings—was at our disposal, to be sure.", "그의 조수—에즈라 제닝스라는 어떤 사람—를 부를 수야 있었다, 물론."], ["But nobody knew much about him in our parts. He had been engaged by Mr.", "그러나 우리 고장에서는 아무도 그에 대해 아는 것이 별로 없었다."], ["Candy under rather peculiar circumstances; and, right or wrong, we none of us liked him or trusted him.", "그는 자못 별난 사정 아래 캔디 씨에게 고용된 터였고, 옳든 그르든, 우리 중 누구도 그를 좋아하지도 미더워하지도 않았다."], ["There were other doctors at Frizinghall.", "프리징홀에는 다른 의사들도 있었다."], ["But they were strangers to our house; and Penelope doubted, in Rosanna’s present state, whether strangers might not do her more harm than good.", "그러나 그들은 우리 집에 낯선 이들이었다. 그리고 페넬로피는, 로재나가 지금 그런 상태에서, 낯선 이들이 그에게 득보다 해를 끼치지나 않을지 미심쩍어했다."]], [["I thought of speaking to my lady.", "나는 마님께 말씀드릴까 생각했다."], ["But, remembering the heavy weight of anxiety which she already had on her mind, I hesitated to add to all the other vexations this new trouble.", "그러나 마님이 이미 마음에 짊어지고 계신 그 근심의 무거운 짐을 떠올리니, 그 온갖 다른 시름에 이 새로운 골칫거리를 더하기가 망설여졌다."], ["Still, there was a necessity for doing something.", "그래도, 무언가를 하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The girl’s state was, to my thinking, downright alarming—and my mistress ought to be informed of it.", "그 아이의 상태는, 내 보기에, 여간 걱정스러운 것이 아니었다—그러니 마님께 그것을 알려 드려야 마땅했다."], ["Unwilling enough, I went to her sitting-room.", "어지간히 내키지 않는 걸음으로, 나는 마님의 거실로 갔다."], ["No one was there.", "거기엔 아무도 없었다."], ["My lady was shut up with Miss Rachel.", "마님은 레이철 아가씨와 함께 틀어박혀 계셨다."], ["It was impossible for me to see her till she came out again.", "마님이 다시 나오시기 전에는 내가 마님을 뵐 도리가 없었다."]], [["I waited in vain till the clock on the front staircase struck the quarter to two.", "나는 앞쪽 층계의 시계가 두 시 십오 분 전을 칠 때까지 부질없이 기다렸다."], ["Five minutes afterwards, I heard my name called, from the drive outside the house.", "그로부터 오 분 뒤, 나는 집 밖 진입로에서 내 이름을 부르는 소리를 들었다."], ["I knew the voice directly.", "나는 그 목소리를 대번에 알아챘다."], ["Sergeant Cuff had returned from Frizinghall.", "커프 부장이 프리징홀에서 돌아온 것이다."]], [["# CHAPTER XVIII", "# CHAPTER XVIII"]], [["Going down to the front door, I met the Sergeant on the steps.", "현관으로 내려가다가, 나는 층계에서 부장을 만났다."]], [["It went against the grain with me, after what had passed between us, to show him that I felt any sort of interest in his proceedings.", "우리 사이에 오간 일을 겪은 뒤라, 그에게 내가 그의 일 처리에 무슨 관심이라도 있는 티를 내는 것이 나로서는 영 마음에 거슬렸다."], ["In spite of myself, however, I felt an interest that there was no resisting.", "그러나 나도 어쩔 수 없이, 도무지 거스를 수 없는 관심이 일었다."], ["My sense of dignity sank from under me, and out came the words: “What news from Frizinghall?”", "내 위신은 발밑에서 무너져 내렸고, 이런 말이 튀어나왔다. \"프리징홀에서는 무슨 소식이오?\""]], [["“I have seen the Indians,”", "\"인도인들을 만나 봤소.\""], ["answered Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 대답했다."], ["“And I have found out what Rosanna bought privately in the town, on Thursday last.", "\"그리고 로재나가 지난 목요일 시내에서 남몰래 무엇을 샀는지도 알아냈고."], ["The Indians will be set free on Wednesday in next week.", "인도인들은 다음 주 수요일에 풀려날 거요."], ["There isn’t a doubt on my mind, and there isn’t a doubt on Mr. Murthwaite’s mind, that they came to this place to steal the Moonstone.", "그들이 월장석을 훔치러 이곳에 왔다는 것은, 내 마음속에도 한 점 의심이 없고, 머스웨이트 씨의 마음속에도 한 점 의심이 없소."], ["Their calculations were all thrown out, of course, by what happened in the house on Wednesday night; and they have no more to do with the actual loss of the jewel than you have.", "물론, 그들의 셈은 수요일 밤 이 집에서 일어난 일로 죄다 어그러졌지. 그러니 그 보석의 실제 실종에 대해서는 그들도 당신만큼이나 아무 상관이 없소."], ["But I can tell you one thing, Mr. Betteredge—if we don’t find the Moonstone, they will.", "허나 한 가지는 일러 둘 수 있소, 베터리지 양반—만일 우리가 월장석을 찾지 못하면, 저들이 찾아낼 거요."], ["You have not heard the last of the three jugglers yet.”", "당신은 그 세 요술쟁이의 이야기를 아직 다 들은 게 아니오.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin came back from his walk as the Sergeant said those startling words.", "부장이 그 섬뜩한 말을 하는 참에 프랭클린 도련님이 산책에서 돌아왔다."], ["Governing his curiosity better than I had governed mine, he passed us without a word, and went on into the house.", "내가 내 호기심을 다스린 것보다 제 호기심을 더 잘 다스리며, 그는 한마디 없이 우리를 지나쳐, 집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["As for me, having already dropped my dignity, I determined to have the whole benefit of the sacrifice.", "나로 말하자면, 이미 위신을 떨어뜨린 터라, 그 희생의 값어치를 온전히 뽑아내기로 마음먹었다. \"인도인 이야기는 그쯤 하고.\""], ["“So much for the Indians,”", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “What about Rosanna next?”", "\"그다음 로재나는 어찌 됐소?\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff shook his head.", "커프 부장이 고개를 저었다."]], [["“The mystery in that quarter is thicker than ever,”", "\"그쪽 수수께끼는 그 어느 때보다 더 짙어졌소.\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I have traced her to a shop at Frizinghall, kept by a linen draper named Maltby.", "\"나는 그 아이를 프리징홀의 한 가게까지 뒤밟았소. 몰트비라는 아마포 상인이 하는 가게였지."], ["She bought nothing whatever at any of the other drapers’ shops, or at any milliners’ or tailors’ shops; and she bought nothing at Maltby’s but a piece of long cloth.", "그 아이는 다른 어느 포목점에서도, 어느 모자점이나 양복점에서도 아무것도 사지 않았소. 그리고 몰트비네에서 산 것이라곤 롱클로스 한 필뿐이었소."], ["She was very particular in choosing a certain quality.", "그 아이는 어떤 특정한 품질을 고르는 데 아주 까다로웠소."], ["As to quantity, she bought enough to make a nightgown.”", "양으로 말하자면, 잠옷 한 벌을 지을 만큼 샀지.\""]], [["“Whose nightgown?”", "\"누구 잠옷 말이오?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“Her own, to be sure.", "\"제 것이지, 물론."], ["Between twelve and three, on the Thursday morning, she must have slipped down to your young lady’s room, to settle the hiding of the Moonstone while all the rest of you were in bed.", "목요일 새벽 열두 시에서 세 시 사이에, 그 아이는 당신네 나머지 사람들이 죄다 잠자리에 든 사이, 월장석을 숨기는 일을 매듭지으려고 당신 아가씨의 방으로 살며시 내려간 게 틀림없소."], ["In going back to her own room, her nightgown must have brushed the wet paint on the door.", "제 방으로 돌아가는 길에, 그 아이의 잠옷이 문의 젖은 페인트를 스쳤을 테고."], ["She couldn’t wash out the stain; and she couldn’t safely destroy the night-gown without first providing another like it, to make the inventory of her linen complete.”", "그 아이는 그 얼룩을 씻어 낼 수도 없었고, 제 아마포 목록을 온전히 채워 둘, 그것과 똑같은 다른 것을 먼저 마련하지 않고서는 그 잠옷을 안전하게 없애 버릴 수도 없었던 거요.\""]], [["“What proves that it was Rosanna’s nightgown?”", "\"그것이 로재나의 잠옷이었다는 걸 무엇으로 증명하오?\""], ["I objected.", "하고 내가 이의를 달았다."]], [["“The material she bought for making the substitute dress,”", "\"그 아이가 대체 옷을 지으려고 산 그 감이지.\""], ["answered the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 대답했다."], ["“If it had been Miss Verinder’s nightgown, she would have had to buy lace, and frilling, and Lord knows what besides; and she wouldn’t have had time to make it in one night.", "\"만일 그것이 버린더 아가씨의 잠옷이었다면, 그 아이는 레이스며, 주름 장식이며, 그 밖에 뭔지 모를 것들을 사야 했을 테고, 그것을 하룻밤에 지을 시간도 없었을 거요."], ["Plain long cloth means a plain servant’s nightgown.", "소박한 롱클로스는 소박한 하인의 잠옷을 뜻하지."], ["No, no, Mr. Betteredge—all that is clear enough.", "아니, 아니오, 베터리지 양반—그 모든 게 어지간히 뻔하오."], ["The pinch of the question is—why, after having provided the substitute dress, does she hide the smeared nightgown, instead of destroying it?", "이 문제의 요점은—어째서, 대체 옷을 마련해 놓고서도, 그 아이는 그 얼룩진 잠옷을 없애 버리는 대신 숨기느냐 하는 거요."], ["If the girl won’t speak out, there is only one way of settling the difficulty.", "만일 그 아이가 툭 터놓지 않는다면, 이 난제를 매듭지을 길은 하나뿐이오."], ["The hiding-place at the Shivering Sand must be searched—and the true state of the case will be discovered there.”", "흔들리는 모래의 그 숨긴 자리를 뒤져야 하오—그러면 이 사건의 참된 실상이 거기서 드러날 거요.\""]], [["“How are you to find the place?”", "\"그 자리를 어떻게 찾아낼 셈이오?\""], ["I inquired.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“I am sorry to disappoint you,”", "\"당신을 실망시켜 미안하오만,\""], ["said the Sergeant—“but that’s a secret which I mean to keep to myself.”", "하고 부장이 말했다. \"그건 내 혼자만 간직할 작정인 비밀이오.\""]], [["(Not to irritate your curiosity, as he irritated mine, I may here inform you that he had come back from Frizinghall provided with a search-warrant.", "(그가 내 호기심을 애태웠던 것처럼 여러분의 호기심을 애태우지 않으려고, 여기서 여러분께 알려 드릴 수 있으니, 그는 수색 영장을 갖추어 프리징홀에서 돌아온 터였다."], ["His experience in such matters told him that Rosanna was in all probability carrying about her a memorandum of the hiding-place, to guide her, in case she returned to it, under changed circumstances and after a lapse of time.", "그런 일에 대한 그의 경험이 그에게 일러 주기를, 로재나는 십중팔구 그 숨긴 자리를 적은 비망록을 몸에 지니고 다니고 있으리라는 것이었다. 형편이 바뀌고 얼마쯤 시간이 흐른 뒤에 다시 그리로 돌아갈 경우에 저를 인도해 줄 그런 비망록 말이다."], ["Possessed of this memorandum, the Sergeant would be furnished with all that he could desire.)", "이 비망록을 손에 넣으면, 부장은 저가 바랄 수 있는 모든 것을 갖추게 될 터였다.)"]], [["“Now, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"자, 베터리지 양반,\""], ["he went on, “suppose we drop speculation, and get to business.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"이제 억측은 그만 접고, 일에 착수합시다."], ["I told Joyce to have an eye on Rosanna.", "나는 조이스에게 로재나를 지켜보라고 일러 뒀소."], ["Where is Joyce?”", "조이스는 어디 있소?\""]], [["Joyce was the Frizinghall policeman, who had been left by Superintendent Seegrave at Sergeant Cuff’s disposal.", "조이스는, 시그레이브 경정이 커프 부장의 마음대로 쓰라고 남겨 둔 그 프리징홀 순경이었다."], ["The clock struck two, as he put the question; and, punctual to the moment, the carriage came round to take Miss Rachel to her aunt’s.", "그가 그 물음을 던지는 참에 시계가 두 시를 쳤다. 그리고 시각에 딱 맞추어, 레이철 아가씨를 이모 댁으로 데려갈 마차가 돌아 나왔다."]], [["“One thing at a time,”", "\"한 번에 한 가지씩.\""], ["said the Sergeant, stopping me as I was about to send in search of Joyce.", "하고 부장이, 내가 조이스를 찾으러 사람을 보내려던 참에 나를 멈춰 세우며 말했다."], ["“I must attend to Miss Verinder first.”", "\"먼저 버린더 아가씨부터 챙겨야 하오.\""]], [["As the rain was still threatening, it was the close carriage that had been appointed to take Miss Rachel to Frizinghall.", "비가 여전히 을러대고 있던 터라, 레이철 아가씨를 프리징홀로 데려가기로 정해진 것은 지붕 있는 마차였다."], ["Sergeant Cuff beckoned Samuel to come down to him from the rumble behind.", "커프 부장이 뒤쪽 하인석에 있던 새뮤얼에게 저에게로 내려오라고 손짓했다."]], [["“You will see a friend of mine waiting among the trees, on this side of the lodge gate,” he said.", "\"문지기 대문 이쪽 편, 나무들 사이에 내 친구 하나가 기다리고 있는 것이 보일 거요.\""], ["“My friend, without stopping the carriage, will get up into the rumble with you.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"내 친구는, 마차를 세우지 않고, 자네와 함께 그 하인석에 올라탈 거요."], ["You have nothing to do but to hold your tongue, and shut your eyes.", "자네가 할 일이라곤 입을 다물고 눈을 감는 것뿐이오."], ["Otherwise, you will get into trouble.”", "안 그러면, 자네가 곤경에 처하게 될 테니까.\""]], [["With that advice, he sent the footman back to his place.", "이런 당부와 함께, 그는 하인을 제자리로 돌려보냈다."], ["What Samuel thought I don’t know.", "새뮤얼이 어찌 생각했는지는 나도 모른다."], ["It was plain, to my mind, that Miss Rachel was to be privately kept in view from the time when she left our house—if she did leave it.", "내 마음에는, 레이철 아가씨가 우리 집을 떠나는 그 순간부터—정말로 떠난다면—은밀히 감시받으리라는 것이 뻔했다."], ["A watch set on my young lady!", "우리 아가씨께 감시가 붙다니!"], ["A spy behind her in the rumble of her mother’s carriage!", "제 어머니 마차 하인석에, 등 뒤에 첩자가 붙다니!"], ["I could have cut my own tongue out for having forgotten myself so far as to speak to Sergeant Cuff.", "나는 커프 부장에게 말을 걸 만큼 나를 잊었던 것에 내 혀를 잘라 버리고 싶은 심정이었다."]], [["The first person to come out of the house was my lady.", "집에서 맨 처음 나온 사람은 마님이었다."], ["She stood aside, on the top step, posting herself there to see what happened.", "마님은 맨 위 층계 한쪽으로 비켜서서, 무슨 일이 벌어지는지 보려고 거기 자리를 잡으셨다."], ["Not a word did she say, either to the Sergeant or to me.", "부장에게든 내게든, 마님은 한마디도 하지 않으셨다."], ["With her lips closed, and her arms folded in the light garden cloak which she had wrapped round her on coming into the air, there she stood, as still as a statue, waiting for her daughter to appear.", "입술을 굳게 다물고, 바깥공기를 쐬러 나오며 몸에 두르신 그 가벼운 정원용 망토 속에 두 팔을 낀 채, 거기 마님은, 조각상처럼 미동도 없이, 당신 따님이 나타나기를 기다리며 서 계셨다."]], [["In a minute more, Miss Rachel came downstairs—very nicely dressed in some soft yellow stuff, that set off her dark complexion, and clipped her tight (in the form of a jacket) round the waist.", "다시 일 분쯤 뒤, 레이철 아가씨가 아래층으로 내려왔다—아가씨의 가무스름한 살빛을 돋보이게 하고, (재킷 모양으로) 허리께를 꼭 죄어 주는 어떤 부드러운 노란 감으로 아주 곱게 차려입은 채였다."], ["She had a smart little straw hat on her head, with a white veil twisted round it.", "아가씨는 흰 베일을 둘러 감은 맵시 있는 조그만 밀짚모자를 머리에 쓰고 있었다."], ["She had primrose-coloured gloves that fitted her hands like a second skin.", "앵초 빛깔 장갑이 마치 또 하나의 살갗처럼 아가씨 손에 꼭 맞았다."], ["Her beautiful black hair looked as smooth as satin under her hat.", "아가씨의 아름다운 검은 머리칼은 모자 아래에서 공단처럼 매끄러워 보였다."], ["Her little ears were like rosy shells—they had a pearl dangling from each of them.", "아가씨의 조그만 귀는 장밋빛 조개껍데기 같았다—그 각각에 진주가 하나씩 달랑거렸다."], ["She came swiftly out to us, as straight as a lily on its stem, and as lithe and supple in every movement she made as a young cat.", "아가씨는, 줄기 위의 백합처럼 꼿꼿하게, 그리고 하는 몸짓마다 어린 고양이처럼 나긋나긋하고 매끄럽게, 우리 쪽으로 쏜살같이 나왔다."], ["Nothing that I could discover was altered in her pretty face, but her eyes and her lips.", "아가씨의 그 어여쁜 얼굴에서 내가 알아볼 수 있는 것 가운데 달라진 것이라곤, 그 눈과 그 입술뿐이었다."], ["Her eyes were brighter and fiercer than I liked to see; and her lips had so completely lost their colour and their smile that I hardly knew them again.", "아가씨의 눈은 내가 보고 싶은 것보다 더 밝고 더 사나웠다. 그리고 아가씨의 입술은 그 빛깔과 그 미소를 어찌나 남김없이 잃어버렸던지 나는 그것을 좀처럼 다시 알아보지 못했다."], ["She kissed her mother in a hasty and sudden manner on the cheek.", "아가씨는 서두르는 느닷없는 몸짓으로 제 어머니의 뺨에 입을 맞추었다."], ["She said, “Try to forgive me, mamma”—and then pulled down her veil over her face so vehemently that she tore it.", "아가씨는 \"저를 용서하려 애써 주세요, 엄마.\" 하고 말했다—그러고는 제 베일을 얼굴 위로 어찌나 거세게 끌어내렸던지 그것을 찢고 말았다."], ["In another moment she had run down the steps, and had rushed into the carriage as if it was a hiding-place.", "다시 한순간에 아가씨는 층계를 뛰어 내려가, 마치 그것이 숨을 자리라도 되는 듯 마차 안으로 뛰어들었다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff was just as quick on his side.", "커프 부장도 저대로 꼭 그만큼 재발랐다."], ["He put Samuel back, and stood before Miss Rachel, with the open carriage-door in his hand, at the instant when she settled herself in her place.", "그는 새뮤얼을 뒤로 물리고, 아가씨가 제자리에 앉는 바로 그 순간, 열린 마차 문을 손에 쥔 채 레이철 아가씨 앞에 섰다."]], [["“What do you want?”", "\"뭘 바라죠?\""], ["says Miss Rachel, from behind her veil.", "하고 레이철 아가씨가 제 베일 뒤에서 말한다."]], [["“I want to say one word to you, miss,”", "\"아가씨께 한마디 여쭙고 싶습니다, 아가씨.\""], ["answered the Sergeant, “before you go.", "하고 부장이 대답했다. \"떠나시기 전에요."], ["I can’t presume to stop your paying a visit to your aunt.", "저는 감히 아가씨가 이모 댁을 방문하시는 것을 막을 수 없습니다."], ["I can only venture to say that your leaving us, as things are now, puts an obstacle in the way of my recovering your Diamond.", "다만 이 말씀만은 감히 드리지요, 지금 이런 마당에 아가씨가 우리를 떠나시는 것은, 제가 아가씨의 다이아몬드를 되찾는 길에 걸림돌을 놓는 것이라고요."], ["Please to understand that; and now decide for yourself whether you go or stay.”", "부디 그것을 헤아려 주십시오. 그러고 나서, 가실지 머무실지는 아가씨 스스로 정하십시오.\""]], [["Miss Rachel never even answered him.", "레이철 아가씨는 그에게 대꾸조차 하지 않았다."], ["“Drive on, James!”", "\"출발해요, 제임스!\""], ["she called out to the coachman.", "하고 아가씨가 마부에게 외쳤다."]], [["Without another word, the Sergeant shut the carriage-door.", "더 말 한마디 없이, 부장은 마차 문을 닫았다."], ["Just as he closed it, Mr. Franklin came running down the steps.", "그가 그것을 막 닫는 참에, 프랭클린 도련님이 층계를 뛰어 내려왔다."], ["“Good-bye, Rachel,”", "\"잘 가, 레이철.\""], ["he said, holding out his hand.", "하고 그가 손을 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Drive on!”", "\"출발해요!\""], ["cried Miss Rachel, louder than ever, and taking no more notice of Mr.", "하고 레이철 아가씨가, 그 어느 때보다 큰 소리로 외쳤다."], ["Franklin than she had taken of Sergeant Cuff.", "커프 부장에게 그랬던 것 이상으로 프랭클린 도련님께도 아랑곳하지 않고 말이다."]], [["Mr. Franklin stepped back thunderstruck, as well he might be.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 그럴 만도 했으니, 벼락 맞은 듯 뒷걸음질 쳤다."], ["The coachman, not knowing what to do, looked towards my lady, still standing immovable on the top step.", "마부는, 어찌해야 할지 몰라, 여전히 맨 위 층계에 꿈쩍 않고 서 계시던 마님 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["My lady, with anger and sorrow and shame all struggling together in her face, made him a sign to start the horses, and then turned back hastily into the house. Mr.", "마님은, 노여움과 서러움과 부끄러움이 얼굴에서 죄다 뒤엉키는 채로, 마부에게 말을 몰라는 신호를 보내시고는, 부랴부랴 집 안으로 돌아 들어가셨다."], ["Franklin, recovering the use of his speech, called after her, as the carriage drove off, “Aunt!", "프랭클린 도련님은, 말문을 되찾고, 마차가 떠나갈 때 마님을 향해 뒤에서 외쳤다. \"이모님!"], ["you were quite right.", "이모님 말씀이 정말 옳았습니다."], ["Accept my thanks for all your kindness—and let me go.”", "이모님의 그 온갖 자상함에 대한 제 감사를 받아 주십시오—그리고 저를 떠나게 해 주십시오.\""]], [["My lady turned as though to speak to him.", "마님이 그에게 무슨 말씀을 하시려는 듯 돌아서셨다."], ["Then, as if distrusting herself, waved her hand kindly.", "그러더니, 마치 당신 자신을 못 미더워하듯, 다정하게 손을 흔드셨다."], ["“Let me see you, before you leave us, Franklin,”", "\"떠나기 전에 나를 보고 가거라, 프랭클린.\""], ["she said, in a broken voice—and went on to her own room.", "하고 마님이 잠긴 목소리로 말씀하시고는—당신 방으로 가셨다."]], [["“Do me a last favour, Betteredge,”", "\"내게 마지막 부탁 하나만 들어주게, 베터리지.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin, turning to me, with the tears in his eyes.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이, 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁한 채 내게 돌아서며 말한다."], ["“Get me away to the train as soon as you can!”", "\"될 수 있는 대로 빨리 나를 기차 편으로 떠나보내 주게!\""]], [["He too went his way into the house.", "그도 제 갈 길로 집 안에 들어갔다."], ["For the moment, Miss Rachel had completely unmanned him.", "그 순간, 레이철 아가씨가 그의 사내다움을 아주 무너뜨려 놓은 것이다."], ["Judge from that, how fond he must have been of her!", "그것으로 미루어 보시라, 그가 얼마나 아가씨를 아꼈던 것인지를!"]], [["Sergeant Cuff and I were left face to face, at the bottom of the steps.", "커프 부장과 나만이, 층계 밑에서 얼굴을 마주하고 남겨졌다."], ["The Sergeant stood with his face set towards a gap in the trees, commanding a view of one of the windings of the drive which led from the house.", "부장은, 집에서 뻗어 나온 그 진입로의 굽이 하나가 내다보이는 나무들 사이의 틈을 향해 얼굴을 두고 서 있었다."], ["He had his hands in his pockets, and he was softly whistling “The Last Rose of Summer” to himself.", "그는 두 손을 호주머니에 찔러 넣고, 혼자 나직이 \"여름날의 마지막 장미\"를 휘파람으로 불고 있었다."]], [["“There’s a time for everything,”", "\"매사에는 때가 있는 법이오.\""], ["I said savagely enough.", "하고 나는 제법 사납게 말했다."], ["“This isn’t a time for whistling.”", "\"지금은 휘파람 불 때가 아니오.\""]], [["At that moment, the carriage appeared in the distance, through the gap, on its way to the lodge-gate.", "바로 그 순간, 마차가 그 틈 사이로, 저 멀리, 문지기 대문을 향해 가는 모습으로 나타났다."], ["There was another man, besides Samuel, plainly visible in the rumble behind.", "뒤쪽 하인석에는, 새뮤얼 말고도, 또 한 사내가 뚜렷이 보였다."]], [["“All right!”", "\"됐군!\""], ["said the Sergeant to himself.", "하고 부장이 혼자 말했다."], ["He turned round to me.", "그가 내게로 돌아섰다."], ["“It’s no time for whistling, Mr. Betteredge, as you say.", "\"당신 말대로, 휘파람 불 때가 아니오, 베터리지 양반."], ["It’s time to take this business in hand, now, without sparing anybody.", "이제, 누구도 봐주지 않고, 이 일에 착수할 때요."], ["We’ll begin with Rosanna Spearman.", "로재나 스피어먼부터 시작합시다."], ["Where is Joyce?”", "조이스는 어디 있소?\""]], [["We both called for Joyce, and received no answer.", "우리 둘 다 조이스를 불렀으나, 아무 대답도 없었다."], ["I sent one of the stable-boys to look for him.", "나는 마구간지기 아이 하나를 보내 그를 찾게 했다."]], [["“You heard what I said to Miss Verinder?”", "\"내가 버린더 아가씨께 뭐라 했는지 들었지요?\""], ["remarked the Sergeant, while we were waiting.", "하고 부장이, 우리가 기다리는 동안 말했다."], ["“And you saw how she received it?", "\"그리고 아가씨가 그것을 어떻게 받아들이는지 봤고?"], ["I tell her plainly that her leaving us will be an obstacle in the way of my recovering her Diamond—and she leaves, in the face of that statement!", "나는 아가씨께, 우리를 떠나시는 것이 내가 아가씨 다이아몬드를 되찾는 길에 걸림돌이 되리라고 뻔히 말했소—그런데도 아가씨는 그 말을 뻔히 듣고서 떠나셨소!"], ["Your young lady has got a travelling companion in her mother’s carriage, Mr. Betteredge—and the name of it is, the Moonstone.”", "당신 아가씨는 제 어머니 마차에 길동무를 하나 두셨소, 베터리지 양반—그리고 그 이름은, 월장석이오.\""]], [["I said nothing.", "나는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["I only held on like death to my belief in Miss Rachel.", "나는 그저 죽기 살기로 레이철 아가씨에 대한 내 믿음을 붙들고 있었다."]], [["The stable-boy came back, followed—very unwillingly, as it appeared to me—by Joyce.", "마구간지기 아이가 돌아왔는데, 그 뒤로—내 보기엔 아주 마지못한 걸음으로—조이스가 따라왔다."]], [["“Where is Rosanna Spearman?”", "\"로재나 스피어먼은 어디 있소?\""], ["asked Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 물었다."]], [["“I can’t account for it, sir,”", "\"영문을 모르겠습니다, 나리.\""], ["Joyce began; “and I am very sorry.", "하고 조이스가 말문을 열었다. \"정말 죄송합니다."], ["But somehow or other——”", "하지만 어쩌다 보니—\""]], [["“Before I went to Frizinghall,”", "\"내가 프리징홀에 가기 전에,\""], ["said the Sergeant, cutting him short, “I told you to keep your eyes on Rosanna Spearman, without allowing her to discover that she was being watched.", "하고 부장이 그의 말을 자르며 말했다. \"나는 자네에게, 로재나 스피어먼이 감시받고 있다는 것을 눈치채지 못하게 하면서, 그를 지켜보라고 일렀소."], ["Do you mean to tell me that you have let her give you the slip?”", "그러니까 자네는, 그가 자네를 따돌리게 놔뒀다고 내게 말하려는 거요?\""]], [["“I am afraid, sir,”", "\"아무래도, 나리,\""], ["says Joyce, beginning to tremble, “that I was perhaps a little too careful not to let her discover me.", "하고 조이스가 떨기 시작하며 말한다. \"제가 그에게 들키지 않으려고 어쩌면 좀 지나치게 조심했던 것 같습니다."], ["There are such a many passages in the lower parts of this house——”", "이 집 아래층에는 통로가 어찌나 많은지—\""]], [["“How long is it since you missed her?”", "\"그를 놓친 지 얼마나 됐소?\""]], [["“Nigh on an hour since, sir.”", "\"거의 한 시간쯤 됐습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“You can go back to your regular business at Frizinghall,”", "\"자네는 프리징홀에서 자네 본연의 업무로 돌아가도 좋소.\""], ["said the Sergeant, speaking just as composedly as ever, in his usual quiet and dreary way.", "하고 부장이, 그 여느 조용하고 처량한 투로, 여전히 꼭 그대로 태연하게 말했다."], ["“I don’t think your talents are at all in our line, Mr. Joyce.", "\"내 보기에 자네 재능은 우리 쪽 일과는 도무지 맞지 않소, 조이스 씨."], ["Your present form of employment is a trifle beyond you.", "지금 자네가 맡은 이 일은 자네에게 조금 벅차구려."], ["Good morning.”", "좋은 아침이오.\""]], [["The man slunk off.", "그 사내는 슬금슬금 물러갔다."], ["I find it very difficult to describe how I was affected by the discovery that Rosanna Spearman was missing.", "로재나 스피어먼이 사라졌다는 사실에 내가 어떤 심정이었는지는 참으로 묘사하기 어렵다."], ["I seemed to be in fifty different minds about it, all at the same time.", "나는 그것에 대해, 죄다 한꺼번에, 쉰 가지 서로 다른 마음이 든 듯싶었다."], ["In that state, I stood staring at Sergeant Cuff—and my powers of language quite failed me.", "그런 상태로, 나는 커프 부장을 빤히 쳐다보며 서 있었다—그리고 나의 말재간은 아주 나를 저버렸다."]], [["“No, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"아니오, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["said the Sergeant, as if he had discovered the uppermost thought in me, and was picking it out to be answered, before all the rest.", "하고 부장이, 마치 내 안의 맨 위에 있는 생각을 알아채고, 그것을 나머지 모두에 앞서 답해 주려고 골라낸 듯 말했다."], ["“Your young friend, Rosanna, won’t slip through my fingers so easy as you think.", "\"당신의 젊은 벗, 로재나는, 당신이 여기는 것처럼 그리 쉽게 내 손가락 사이로 빠져나가지 못할 거요."], ["As long as I know where Miss Verinder is, I have the means at my disposal of tracing Miss Verinder’s accomplice.", "버린더 아가씨가 어디 계신지를 내가 아는 한, 나는 버린더 아가씨의 공범을 뒤밟을 방도를 손에 쥐고 있는 셈이오."], ["I prevented them from communicating last night.", "나는 어젯밤 그들이 통하는 것을 막았소."], ["Very good.", "아주 좋소."], ["They will get together at Frizinghall, instead of getting together here.", "그들은 여기서 만나는 대신, 프리징홀에서 만날 거요."], ["The present inquiry must be simply shifted (rather sooner than I had anticipated) from this house, to the house at which Miss Verinder is visiting.", "지금 이 조사는 그저 (내가 예상했던 것보다는 다소 이르게) 이 집에서, 버린더 아가씨가 방문하고 계신 그 집으로 옮겨 가야 하는 것뿐이오."], ["In the meantime, I’m afraid I must trouble you to call the servants together again.”", "그러는 동안, 미안하오만 당신에게 하인들을 다시 불러 모으는 수고를 끼쳐야겠소.\""]], [["I went round with him to the servants’ hall.", "나는 그와 함께 하인 방으로 돌아갔다."], ["It is very disgraceful, but it is not the less true, that I had another attack of the detective-fever, when he said those last words.", "참으로 창피한 노릇이나, 그렇다고 덜 참된 것도 아니니, 그가 그 마지막 말을 했을 때 나는 또 한 번 탐정 열병 발작을 일으켰다."], ["I forgot that I hated Sergeant Cuff.", "나는 내가 커프 부장을 미워한다는 것을 잊었다."], ["I seized him confidentially by the arm.", "나는 은밀히 그의 팔을 움켜쥐었다."], ["I said, “For goodness’ sake, tell us what you are going to do with the servants now?”", "나는 말했다. \"제발 부탁이오, 이제 하인들을 데리고 뭘 하려는 건지 우리에게 말해 주시오.\""]], [["The great Cuff stood stock-still, and addressed himself in a kind of melancholy rapture to the empty air.", "그 위대한 커프는 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 일종의 우울한 황홀경에 잠겨 텅 빈 허공을 향해 말했다."]], [["“If this man,”", "\"이 사람이,\""], ["said the Sergeant (apparently meaning me), “only understood the growing of roses he would be the most completely perfect character on the face of creation!”", "하고 부장이 (아무래도 나를 두고 하는 말이었다) \"장미 재배만 이해한다면 천지 만물의 얼굴 위에서 가장 완벽하게 온전한 인물이 될 텐데!\""], ["After that strong expression of feeling, he sighed, and put his arm through mine.", "그런 강렬한 감정 표현을 하고 나서, 그는 한숨을 쉬고, 제 팔을 내 팔에 꿰었다."], ["“This is how it stands,”", "\"사정은 이렇소.\""], ["he said, dropping down again to business.", "하고 그가, 다시 일 이야기로 내려앉으며 말했다."], ["“Rosanna has done one of two things.", "\"로재나는 둘 중 하나를 한 거요."], ["She has either gone direct to Frizinghall (before I can get there), or she has gone first to visit her hiding-place at the Shivering Sand.", "곧장 프리징홀로 갔든지 (내가 거기 도착하기 전에), 아니면 먼저 흔들리는 모래에 있는 제 숨긴 자리를 살피러 갔든지."], ["The first thing to find out is, which of the servants saw the last of her before she left the house.”", "맨 먼저 알아내야 할 것은, 그 아이가 집을 떠나기 전에 하인들 중 누가 그를 마지막으로 봤느냐 하는 거요.\""]], [["On instituting this inquiry, it turned out that the last person who had set eyes on Rosanna was Nancy, the kitchenmaid.", "이 조사를 벌이고 보니, 로재나를 마지막으로 눈에 담은 사람은 부엌데기 낸시임이 드러났다."]], [["Nancy had seen her slip out with a letter in her hand, and stop the butcher’s man who had just been delivering some meat at the back door.", "낸시는 그가 손에 편지 한 장을 들고 살며시 나가, 마침 뒷문으로 고기를 얼마쯤 배달하고 있던 푸줏간 사내를 붙잡는 것을 보았다."], ["Nancy had heard her ask the man to post the letter when he got back to Frizinghall.", "낸시는 그가 그 사내에게 프리징홀에 돌아가거든 그 편지를 부쳐 달라고 청하는 소리를 들었다."], ["The man had looked at the address, and had said it was a roundabout way of delivering a letter directed to Cobb’s Hole, to post it at Frizinghall—and that, moreover, on a Saturday, which would prevent the letter from getting to its destination until Monday morning.", "그 사내는 겉봉을 보더니, 코브스 홀로 가는 편지를 프리징홀에서 부친다는 건 에두르는 배달 방식이라고—게다가, 토요일이니, 이 편지가 월요일 아침에나 목적지에 닿게 될 거라고 말했다."], ["Rosanna had answered that the delivery of the letter being delayed till Monday was of no importance.", "로재나는 편지 배달이 월요일까지 늦어지는 것은 대수롭지 않다고 대답했다."], ["The only thing she wished to be sure of was that the man would do what she told him.", "그가 확실히 해 두고 싶은 단 한 가지는, 그 사내가 저가 이른 대로 해 주리라는 것뿐이었다."], ["The man had promised to do it, and had driven away.", "그 사내는 그러겠다고 약속하고, 마차를 몰고 떠났다."], ["Nancy had been called back to her work in the kitchen.", "낸시는 부엌의 제 일로 도로 불려 갔다."], ["And no other person had seen anything afterwards of Rosanna Spearman.", "그리고 그 뒤로 다른 어느 누구도 로재나 스피어먼을 다시 보지 못했다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["I asked, when we were alone again.", "하고 나는 우리 둘만 다시 남았을 때 물었다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."], ["“I must go to Frizinghall.”", "\"나는 프리징홀에 가야 하오.\""]], [["“About the letter, sir?”", "\"그 편지 때문에요, 나리?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["The memorandum of the hiding-place is in that letter.", "그 숨긴 자리의 비망록이 그 편지 안에 있소."], ["I must see the address at the post-office.", "나는 우체국에서 그 겉봉을 봐야 하오."], ["If it is the address I suspect, I shall pay our friend, Mrs. Yolland, another visit on Monday next.”", "만일 그것이 내가 짐작하는 그 겉봉이라면, 나는 다음 월요일에 우리 벗 욜랜드 부인을 다시 한번 찾아갈 거요.\""]], [["I went with the Sergeant to order the pony-chaise.", "나는 부장과 함께 조랑말 이륜마차를 대령하라 이르러 갔다."], ["In the stable-yard we got a new light thrown on the missing girl.", "마구간 뜰에서 우리는 그 사라진 아이에 대한 새로운 실마리를 얻었다."]], [["# CHAPTER XIX", "# CHAPTER XIX"]], [["The news of Rosanna’s disappearance had, as it appeared, spread among the out-of-door servants.", "로재나가 사라졌다는 소식은, 보아하니, 바깥일을 하는 하인들 사이에도 이미 퍼져 있었다."], ["They too had made their inquiries; and they had just laid hands on a quick little imp, nicknamed “Duffy”—who was occasionally employed in weeding the garden, and who had seen Rosanna Spearman as lately as half-an-hour since.", "그들 역시 나름대로 수소문을 해 본 터라, 마침 \"더피\"라는 별명으로 불리는 잽싼 꼬마 녀석 하나를 붙들어 온 참이었다—이따금 정원의 잡초를 뽑는 일에 부려지곤 하던 아이로, 불과 반 시간 전에 로재나 스피어먼을 본 아이였다."], ["Duffy was certain that the girl had passed him in the fir-plantation, not walking, but running, in the direction of the sea-shore.", "더피는 그 아이가 전나무 숲에서 저를 지나쳤는데, 걷는 것이 아니라 바닷가 쪽으로 뛰어가고 있었다고 딱 잘라 말했다."]], [["“Does this boy know the coast hereabouts?”", "\"이 아이가 이 근방 해안을 아오?\""], ["asked Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 물었다."]], [["“He has been born and bred on the coast,”", "\"이 해안에서 나고 자란 아이오.\""], ["I answered.", "하고 내가 대답했다."]], [["“Duffy!”", "\"더피!\""], ["says the Sergeant, “do you want to earn a shilling?", "하고 부장이 말한다. \"너, 한 실링 벌고 싶으냐?"], ["If you do, come along with me.", "그러거든, 나를 따라오너라."], ["Keep the pony-chaise ready, Mr. Betteredge, till I come back.”", "베터리지 양반, 내가 돌아올 때까지 조랑말 이륜마차를 대기시켜 두시오.\""]], [["He started for the Shivering Sand, at a rate that my legs (though well enough preserved for my time of life) had no hope of matching.", "그는 흔들리는 모래를 향해, 내 다리로는 (내 나이치고는 제법 잘 간수해 온 다리이건만) 도무지 따라잡을 가망이 없는 속도로 내달렸다."], ["Little Duffy, as the way is with the young savages in our parts when they are in high spirits, gave a howl, and trotted off at the Sergeant’s heels.", "꼬마 더피는, 우리 고장의 어린 야만족들이 신이 났을 때 흔히 그러하듯, 한바탕 괴성을 지르고는 부장의 발뒤꿈치를 좇아 종종걸음으로 달려 나갔다."]], [["Here again, I find it impossible to give anything like a clear account of the state of my mind in the interval after Sergeant Cuff had left us.", "여기서도 또, 커프 부장이 우리를 떠난 뒤의 그 동안 내 마음이 어떤 상태였는지를 조금이라도 또렷이 적어 낸다는 것은 나로서는 도저히 못 할 노릇이다."], ["A curious and stupefying restlessness got possession of me.", "야릇하고 얼을 빼는 안절부절이 나를 사로잡았다."], ["I did a dozen different needless things in and out of the house, not one of which I can now remember.", "나는 집 안팎에서 열두 가지도 넘는 부질없는 짓들을 했는데, 그중 단 하나도 지금은 떠올릴 수가 없다."], ["I don’t even know how long it was after the Sergeant had gone to the sands, when Duffy came running back with a message for me.", "부장이 모래펄로 떠난 지 얼마나 지났을 때 더피가 내게 전할 말을 들고 뛰어 돌아왔는지조차 나는 모른다."], ["Sergeant Cuff had given the boy a leaf torn out of his pocket-book, on which was written in pencil, “Send me one of Rosanna Spearman’s boots, and be quick about it.”", "커프 부장은 그 아이에게 제 수첩에서 찢어 낸 종잇장 하나를 주었는데, 거기에는 연필로 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"로재나 스피어먼의 장화 한 짝을 내게 보내 주시오, 그리고 서두르시오.\""]], [["I despatched the first woman-servant I could find to Rosanna’s room; and I sent the boy back to say that I myself would follow him with the boot.", "나는 맨 처음 눈에 띈 하녀를 로재나의 방으로 급히 보냈다. 그리고 그 아이는, 내가 몸소 그 장화를 들고 뒤따라가겠다는 말을 전하라고 돌려보냈다."]], [["This, I am well aware, was not the quickest way to take of obeying the directions which I had received.", "이것이, 나도 잘 알거니와, 내가 받은 지시를 따르는 데 가장 빠른 길은 아니었다."], ["But I was resolved to see for myself what new mystification was going on before I trusted Rosanna’s boot in the Sergeant’s hands.", "그러나 나는 로재나의 장화를 부장의 손에 맡기기 전에, 이번에는 또 무슨 알쏭달쏭한 일이 벌어지고 있는지를 내 눈으로 직접 보리라 마음먹었다."], ["My old notion of screening the girl, if I could, seemed to have come back on me again, at the eleventh hour.", "할 수만 있다면 그 아이를 감싸 주려던 내 오랜 심산이, 이 막판에 이르러 다시금 내게로 되돌아온 듯싶었다."], ["This state of feeling (to say nothing of the detective-fever) hurried me off, as soon as I had got the boot, at the nearest approach to a run which a man turned seventy can reasonably hope to make.", "이런 심사가 (탐정 열병은 말할 것도 없고) 나로 하여금, 장화를 손에 넣기가 무섭게, 일흔 고개를 넘긴 사람이 그런대로 바랄 수 있는 가장 뜀박질에 가까운 걸음으로 부랴부랴 떠나게 했다."]], [["As I got near the shore, the clouds gathered black, and the rain came down, drifting in great white sheets of water before the wind.", "해안 가까이 다다르자, 구름이 시커멓게 몰려들었고, 비가 쏟아져 내리며, 바람 앞에서 거대한 흰 물의 장막이 되어 흩날렸다."], ["I heard the thunder of the sea on the sand-bank at the mouth of the bay.", "만 어귀의 모래톱에 부딪는 바다의 우렛소리가 들려왔다."], ["A little further on, I passed the boy crouching for shelter under the lee of the sandhills.", "조금 더 나아가서, 나는 모래 언덕의 바람 그늘에 웅크려 비를 피하고 있는 그 아이를 지나쳤다."], ["Then I saw the raging sea, and the rollers tumbling in on the sand-bank, and the driven rain sweeping over the waters like a flying garment, and the yellow wilderness of the beach with one solitary black figure standing on it—the figure of Sergeant Cuff.", "그러고 나서 나는 미쳐 날뛰는 바다와, 모래톱으로 밀려 굴러드는 큰 물결과, 흩날리는 옷자락처럼 물 위를 휩쓸어 가는 몰아치는 빗줄기와, 그 위에 홀로 검은 형상 하나가 서 있는 그 누런 황무지 같은 백사장을 보았다—커프 부장의 형상이었다."]], [["He waved his hand towards the north, when he first saw me.", "그는 나를 처음 알아보고는, 북쪽을 향해 손을 내저었다."], ["“Keep on that side!”", "\"그쪽으로 오시오!\""], ["he shouted.", "하고 그가 소리쳤다."], ["“And come on down here to me!”", "\"그리고 여기 내게로 내려오시오!\""]], [["I went down to him, choking for breath, with my heart leaping as if it was like to leap out of me.", "나는, 숨이 턱턱 막히고, 심장이 마치 몸 밖으로 튀어나올 듯 펄떡거리는 채로, 그에게로 내려갔다."], ["I was past speaking.", "나는 말이 나오지 않는 지경이었다."], ["I had a hundred questions to put to him; and not one of them would pass my lips.", "그에게 물을 것이 백 가지나 있었건만, 그중 단 하나도 내 입 밖으로 나오려 하지 않았다."], ["His face frightened me.", "그의 얼굴이 나를 오싹하게 했다."], ["I saw a look in his eyes which was a look of horror.", "나는 그의 눈에서 어떤 표정을 보았는데, 그것은 공포의 표정이었다."], ["He snatched the boot out of my hand, and set it in a footmark on the sand, bearing south from us as we stood, and pointing straight towards the rocky ledge called the South Spit.", "그는 내 손에서 장화를 낚아채더니, 우리가 선 자리에서 남쪽으로 뻗어, 사우스 스핏이라 불리는 그 바위 턱을 곧장 가리키고 있는 모래 위의 한 발자국에 그것을 맞추어 놓았다."], ["The mark was not yet blurred out by the rain—and the girl’s boot fitted it to a hair.", "그 자국은 아직 빗물에 지워지지 않은 채였다—그리고 그 아이의 장화가 거기에 털끝만큼도 어긋남 없이 꼭 들어맞았다."]], [["The Sergeant pointed to the boot in the footmark, without saying a word.", "부장은, 한마디 말도 없이, 그 발자국에 놓인 장화를 손으로 가리켰다."]], [["I caught at his arm, and tried to speak to him, and failed as I had failed when I tried before.", "나는 그의 팔을 붙들고, 그에게 말을 걸어 보려 했으나, 아까 애썼을 때 그러했던 것처럼 또 뜻을 이루지 못했다."], ["He went on, following the footsteps down and down to where the rocks and the sand joined.", "그는, 그 발자국을 좇아, 바위와 모래가 맞닿는 곳까지 자꾸자꾸 내려갔다."], ["The South Spit was just awash with the flowing tide; the waters heaved over the hidden face of the Shivering Sand.", "사우스 스핏은 밀려드는 밀물에 막 잠기려는 참이었다. 물결이 흔들리는 모래의 그 숨은 낯 위로 넘실거렸다."], ["Now this way and now that, with an obstinate patience that was dreadful to see, Sergeant Cuff tried the boot in the footsteps, and always found it pointing the same way—straight to the rocks.", "이쪽으로 갔다 저쪽으로 갔다, 보기에도 소름 끼치는 그 끈질긴 참을성으로, 커프 부장은 그 발자국마다 장화를 맞추어 보았고, 그때마다 그것이 한결같이 같은 방향을—곧장 바위 쪽을 가리키고 있음을 알아냈다."], ["Hunt as he might, no sign could he find anywhere of the footsteps walking from them.", "아무리 뒤져 보아도, 그 바위에서 걸어 나온 발자국의 자취는 어디서도 찾을 수 없었다."]], [["He gave it up at last.", "그는 마침내 단념했다."], ["Still keeping silence, he looked again at me; and then he looked out at the waters before us, heaving in deeper and deeper over the quicksand.", "여전히 잠자코, 그는 다시 나를 바라보았다. 그러고 나서, 우리 앞에서 유사 위로 점점 더 깊이 넘실거리며 밀려드는 바닷물을 내다보았다."], ["I looked where he looked—and I saw his thought in his face.", "나는 그가 보는 곳을 보았다—그리고 나는 그의 얼굴에서 그의 생각을 읽었다."], ["A dreadful dumb trembling crawled all over me on a sudden.", "소름 끼치는 말없는 떨림이 별안간 내 온몸을 타고 기어올랐다."], ["I fell upon my knees on the beach.", "나는 백사장 위에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다."]], [["“She has been back at the hiding-place,”", "\"그 아이가 숨긴 자리로 되돌아갔던 거로군.\""], ["I heard the Sergeant say to himself.", "하고 부장이 혼잣말하는 소리가 내게 들렸다."], ["“Some fatal accident has happened to her on those rocks.”", "\"저 바위에서 무슨 끔찍한 변을 당한 게야.\""]], [["The girl’s altered looks, and words, and actions—the numbed, deadened way in which she listened to me, and spoke to me—when I had found her sweeping the corridor but a few hours since, rose up in my mind, and warned me, even as the Sergeant spoke, that his guess was wide of the dreadful truth.", "불과 몇 시간 전 복도를 쓸고 있던 그 아이를 찾아냈을 때의, 그 달라진 낯빛과, 말과, 몸짓—얼이 빠지고 넋이 나간 듯 내 말을 듣고 내게 말하던 그 모양이 내 마음속에 떠올라, 부장이 그 말을 하는 바로 그 순간에도, 그의 짐작이 그 끔찍한 진실과는 한참 어긋나 있음을 내게 일러 주었다."], ["I tried to tell him of the fear that had frozen me up.", "나는 나를 얼어붙게 한 그 두려움을 그에게 말하려 했다."], ["I tried to say, “The death she has died, Sergeant, was a death of her own seeking.”", "나는 \"그 아이가 맞은 죽음은, 부장, 제 스스로 찾아 나선 죽음이오.\" 하고 말하려 했다."], ["No!", "아니!"], ["the words wouldn’t come.", "말이 나오지 않았다."], ["The dumb trembling held me in its grip.", "그 말없는 떨림이 나를 그 손아귀에 옭아쥐고 있었다."], ["I couldn’t feel the driving rain.", "나는 몰아치는 비를 느낄 수도 없었다."], ["I couldn’t see the rising tide.", "나는 차오르는 밀물을 볼 수도 없었다."], ["As in the vision of a dream, the poor lost creature came back before me.", "마치 꿈속의 환영처럼, 그 가엾이 스러진 것이 내 앞에 되돌아왔다."], ["I saw her again as I had seen her in the past time—on the morning when I went to fetch her into the house.", "나는 지난 시절 보았던 그대로 그 아이를 다시 보았다—내가 그 아이를 집으로 데려오려고 갔던 그 아침의 모습을."], ["I heard her again, telling me that the Shivering Sand seemed to draw her to it against her will, and wondering whether her grave was waiting for her there.", "나는, 흔들리는 모래가 제 뜻과는 어긋나게 저를 그리로 끌어당기는 것만 같다고, 그리고 제 무덤이 저기서 저를 기다리고 있는 것은 아닐까 하던 그 아이의 말을 다시 들었다."], ["The horror of it struck at me, in some unfathomable way, through my own child.", "그 소름 끼침이, 어떤 헤아릴 길 없는 방식으로, 내 제 자식을 통해 나를 후려쳤다."], ["My girl was just her age.", "내 딸도 꼭 그 아이 나이였다."], ["My girl, tried as Rosanna was tried, might have lived that miserable life, and died this dreadful death.", "내 딸도, 로재나가 시달린 것처럼 시달렸더라면, 그 비참한 삶을 살고, 이 끔찍한 죽음을 맞았을지도 모를 일이었다."]], [["The Sergeant kindly lifted me up, and turned me away from the sight of the place where she had perished.", "부장은 다정하게 나를 일으켜 세우고는, 그 아이가 스러진 그 자리가 보이지 않도록 나를 돌려세웠다."]], [["With that relief, I began to fetch my breath again, and to see things about me, as things really were.", "그 덕에 한숨 돌리자, 나는 다시 숨을 고르고, 내 둘레의 것들을, 있는 그대로 보기 시작했다."], ["Looking towards the sandhills, I saw the men-servants from out-of-doors, and the fisherman, named Yolland, all running down to us together; and all, having taken the alarm, calling out to know if the girl had been found.", "모래 언덕 쪽을 바라보니, 바깥일을 하는 사내 하인들과, 욜랜드라는 이름의 그 어부가, 다 함께 우리 쪽으로 뛰어 내려오는 것이 보였다. 그리고 다들, 소동에 놀라, 그 아이를 찾았느냐고 외쳐 묻고 있었다."], ["In the fewest words, the Sergeant showed them the evidence of the footmarks, and told them that a fatal accident must have happened to her.", "아주 짧은 몇 마디로, 부장은 그들에게 그 발자국이라는 증거를 보여 주고, 그 아이가 끔찍한 변을 당한 게 틀림없다고 일러 주었다."], ["He then picked out the fisherman from the rest, and put a question to him, turning about again towards the sea: “Tell me,”", "그러고 나서 그는 그 나머지 가운데서 그 어부를 골라내어, 다시 바다 쪽으로 몸을 돌리며, 그에게 물음 하나를 던졌다."], ["he said.", "\"말해 보시오.\""], ["“Could a boat have taken her off, in such weather as this, from those rocks where her footmarks stop?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"이런 날씨에, 그 아이의 발자국이 멎은 저 바위에서, 배 한 척이 그 아이를 태워 갈 수 있었겠소?\""]], [["The fisherman pointed to the rollers tumbling in on the sand-bank, and to the great waves leaping up in clouds of foam against the headlands on either side of us.", "그 어부는 모래톱으로 밀려 굴러드는 큰 물결과, 우리 양편의 곶에 부딪혀 물거품 구름을 일으키며 솟구쳐 오르는 커다란 파도를 손으로 가리켰다."]], [["“No boat that ever was built,”", "\"여태 만들어진 그 어떤 배도,\""], ["he answered, “could have got to her through that.”", "하고 그가 대답했다. \"저것을 뚫고 그 아이에게 다다르지는 못했을 겁니다.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff looked for the last time at the foot-marks on the sand, which the rain was now fast blurring out.", "커프 부장은, 비가 이제 빠르게 지워 가고 있는 모래 위의 그 발자국을 마지막으로 한 번 바라보았다."]], [["“There,”", "\"저기,\""], ["he said, “is the evidence that she can’t have left this place by land.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"저것이 그 아이가 뭍으로는 이곳을 떠날 수 없었다는 증거요."], ["And here,”", "그리고 여기,\""], ["he went on, looking at the fisherman, “is the evidence that she can’t have got away by sea.”", "하고 그가, 그 어부를 바라보며 말을 이었다. \"여기 있는 것이 그 아이가 바다로는 빠져나갈 수 없었다는 증거고.\""], ["He stopped, and considered for a minute.", "그는 말을 멈추고, 잠시 헤아려 보았다."], ["“She was seen running towards this place, half an hour before I got here from the house,”", "\"그 아이가 이곳으로 뛰어오는 것이 목격된 것이, 내가 집에서 여기 다다르기 반 시간 전이오.\""], ["he said to Yolland.", "하고 그가 욜랜드에게 말했다."], ["“Some time has passed since then.", "\"그 뒤로 얼마쯤 시간이 흘렀고."], ["Call it, altogether, an hour ago.", "다 쳐서, 한 시간 전이라고 칩시다."], ["How high would the water be, at that time, on this side of the rocks?”", "그때, 이쪽 바위 편의 물이 얼마나 높았겠소?\""], ["He pointed to the south side—otherwise, the side which was not filled up by the quicksand.", "그는 남쪽 편을—다시 말해, 유사에 메워지지 않은 그 편을 가리켰다."]], [["“As the tide makes today,”", "\"오늘 밀물 드는 형편으로 보아서는,\""], ["said the fisherman, “there wouldn’t have been water enough to drown a kitten on that side of the Spit, an hour since.”", "하고 그 어부가 말했다. \"한 시간 전이라면, 스핏 그쪽 편에는 새끼 고양이 한 마리 빠뜨려 죽일 만한 물도 없었을 겁니다.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff turned about northward, towards the quicksand.", "커프 부장은 북쪽으로, 유사 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["“How much on this side?”", "\"이쪽 편은 얼마나 됐겠소?\""], ["he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."]], [["“Less still,”", "\"그보다 더 적었지요.\""], ["answered Yolland.", "하고 욜랜드가 대답했다."], ["“The Shivering Sand would have been just awash, and no more.”", "\"흔들리는 모래는 그저 물에 겨우 잠길락 말락 했을 뿐, 그 이상은 아니었을 겁니다.\""]], [["The Sergeant turned to me, and said that the accident must have happened on the side of the quicksand.", "부장은 내게로 돌아서서, 그 변고가 유사 편에서 일어난 게 틀림없다고 말했다. 그 말에 내 혀가 풀렸다."], ["My tongue was loosened at that.", "\"변고가 아니오!\""], ["“No accident!” I told him.", "하고 내가 그에게 말했다."], ["“When she came to this place, she came weary of her life, to end it here.”", "\"그 아이가 이곳에 왔을 때, 그 아이는 제 삶에 지쳐, 여기서 그것을 끝내려고 온 거요.\""]], [["He started back from me.", "그는 나로부터 흠칫 물러섰다."], ["“How do you know?”", "\"어떻게 아시오?\""], ["he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."], ["The rest of them crowded round.", "나머지 사람들이 우르르 둘러쌌다."], ["The Sergeant recovered himself instantly.", "부장은 이내 마음을 다잡았다."], ["He put them back from me; he said I was an old man; he said the discovery had shaken me; he said, “Let him alone a little.”", "그는 그들을 내게서 물러서게 하고, 나더러 늙은이라고 했고, 이 발견이 나를 뒤흔들어 놓은 거라고 했고, \"잠시 이 사람을 가만 놔두시오.\" 하고 말했다."], ["Then he turned to Yolland, and asked, “Is there any chance of finding her, when the tide ebbs again?”", "그러고 나서 그는 욜랜드에게로 돌아서서 물었다. \"밀물이 다시 빠지면, 그 아이를 찾아낼 가망이 조금이라도 있소?\""], ["And Yolland answered, “None.", "그러자 욜랜드가 대답했다. \"없습니다."], ["What the Sand gets, the Sand keeps for ever.”", "저 모래가 삼킨 것은, 저 모래가 영영 간직하지요.\""], ["Having said that, the fisherman came a step nearer, and addressed himself to me.", "그 말을 하고 나서, 그 어부는 한 걸음 더 가까이 다가와, 내게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지 양반,\""], ["he said, “I have a word to say to you about the young woman’s death.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그 젊은 여자의 죽음에 대해 한마디 드릴 말씀이 있소."], ["Four foot out, broadwise, along the side of the Spit, there’s a shelf of rock, about half fathom down under the sand.", "스핏 옆으로, 옆으로 나란히 네 자쯤 나간 곳에, 모래 밑으로 반 길쯤 되는 데에 바위 선반이 하나 있소."], ["My question is—why didn’t she strike that?", "내 물음은 이거요—어째서 그 여자는 거기에 부딪지 않았느냐 하는 거요."], ["If she slipped, by accident, from off the Spit, she fell in where there’s foothold at the bottom, at a depth that would barely cover her to the waist.", "만일 그 여자가, 어쩌다, 스핏에서 미끄러졌다면, 밑바닥에 발 디딜 데가 있는 곳으로, 기껏해야 허리께나 잠길 깊이에 빠졌을 거요."], ["She must have waded out, or jumped out, into the Deeps beyond—or she wouldn’t be missing now.", "그 여자는 그 너머의 깊은 데로 걸어 들어갔거나, 뛰어들었음이 틀림없소—안 그랬다면 지금 이렇게 자취가 없을 리가 없지."], ["No accident, sir!", "변고가 아니오, 양반!"], ["The Deeps of the Quicksand have got her.", "유사의 깊은 데가 그 여자를 삼킨 거요."], ["And they have got her by her own act.”", "그리고 제 스스로 한 짓으로 그리된 거요.\""]], [["After that testimony from a man whose knowledge was to be relied on, the Sergeant was silent.", "그 방면의 앎을 믿고 기댈 만한 사람의 그런 증언이 있고 나자, 부장은 말이 없었다."], ["The rest of us, like him, held our peace.", "우리 나머지도, 그와 마찬가지로, 입을 다물었다."], ["With one accord, we all turned back up the slope of the beach.", "한마음으로, 우리는 다 함께 백사장 비탈을 되짚어 올라갔다."]], [["At the sandhillocks we were met by the under-groom, running to us from the house.", "그 조그만 모래 언덕들께에서 우리는, 집에서 우리 쪽으로 뛰어오는 아래 마부와 마주쳤다."], ["The lad is a good lad, and has an honest respect for me.", "그 젊은이는 착한 젊은이로, 나를 참되게 우러르는 아이였다."], ["He handed me a little note, with a decent sorrow in his face.", "그는, 얼굴에 얌전한 슬픔을 띤 채, 내게 조그만 쪽지 하나를 건넸다."], ["“Penelope sent me with this, Mr.", "\"페넬로피가 이걸 저더러 갖다 드리라 했습니다, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["Betteredge,” he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“She found it in Rosanna’s room.”", "\"로재나의 방에서 찾아냈다고요.\""]], [["It was her last farewell word to the old man who had done his best—thank God, always done his best—to befriend her.", "그것은, 그 아이의 벗이 되어 주려고 제 힘을 다한—하느님 고맙게도, 늘 제 힘을 다한—그 늙은이에게 남긴 그 아이의 마지막 작별 인사였다."]], [["“You have often forgiven me, Mr. Betteredge, in past times.", "\"지난날, 베터리지 양반은 저를 여러 번 용서해 주셨지요."], ["When you next see the Shivering Sand, try to forgive me once more.", "다음번에 흔들리는 모래를 보시거든, 부디 한 번만 더 저를 용서해 주세요."], ["I have found my grave where my grave was waiting for me.", "저는 제 무덤이 저를 기다리고 있던 그 자리에서 제 무덤을 찾았습니다."], ["I have lived, and died, sir, grateful for your kindness.”", "저는, 양반의 그 자상함에 고마워하며, 살았고, 또 죽습니다.\""]], [["There was no more than that.", "그것뿐, 더는 없었다."], ["Little as it was, I hadn’t manhood enough to hold up against it.", "그 짧은 것에도, 나는 견뎌 낼 만한 사내다움이 없었다."], ["Your tears come easy, when you’re young, and beginning the world.", "젊어서, 세상을 막 시작할 적에는, 눈물이 쉬이 나는 법이다."], ["Your tears come easy, when you’re old, and leaving it.", "늙어서, 세상을 떠나갈 적에도, 눈물이 쉬이 나는 법이다."], ["I burst out crying.", "나는 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff took a step nearer to me—meaning kindly, I don’t doubt.", "커프 부장이 내게로 한 걸음 다가섰다—다정한 마음에서였음을, 나는 의심치 않는다."], ["I shrank back from him.", "나는 그로부터 움츠러들어 물러섰다."], ["“Don’t touch me,”", "\"내게 손대지 마시오.\""], ["I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["“It’s the dread of you, that has driven her to it.”", "\"그 아이를 이 지경으로 내몬 것은, 바로 당신에 대한 두려움이오.\""]], [["“You are wrong, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"당신이 틀렸소, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["he answered, quietly.", "하고 그가 조용히 대답했다."], ["“But there will be time enough to speak of it when we are indoors again.”", "\"허나 그 이야기는 우리가 다시 집 안에 들거든 할 겨를이 넉넉할 거요.\""]], [["I followed the rest of them, with the help of the groom’s arm.", "나는, 그 마부의 팔에 의지하여, 나머지 사람들을 뒤따랐다."], ["Through the driving rain we went back—to meet the trouble and the terror that were waiting for us at the house.", "몰아치는 빗속을 뚫고 우리는 되돌아갔다—집에서 우리를 기다리고 있던 그 근심과 그 공포를 맞이하러."]], [["# CHAPTER XX", "# CHAPTER XX"]], [["Those in front had spread the news before us.", "앞서 간 사람들이 우리보다 먼저 그 소식을 퍼뜨려 놓은 터였다."], ["We found the servants in a state of panic.", "우리가 돌아와 보니 하인들은 겁에 질려 우왕좌왕하고 있었다."], ["As we passed my lady’s door, it was thrown open violently from the inner side.", "우리가 마님의 방문 앞을 지나는데, 그 문이 안쪽에서 거칠게 벌컥 열렸다."], ["My mistress came out among us (with Mr. Franklin following, and trying vainly to compose her), quite beside herself with the horror of the thing.", "마님이 (프랭클린 도련님이 뒤따르며 헛되이 마님을 진정시키려 애쓰는 채로) 우리 가운데로 나오셨는데, 이 일의 그 끔찍함에 아주 제정신이 아니셨다."]], [["“You are answerable for this!”", "\"이 일은 그대에게 책임이 있소!\""], ["she cried out, threatening the Sergeant wildly with her hand.", "하고 마님이, 손으로 부장을 사납게 위협하며 외치셨다."], ["“Gabriel!", "\"게이브리얼!"], ["give that wretch his money—and release me from the sight of him!”", "저 몹쓸 사람에게 삯을 치러 주게—그리고 저 사람 꼴을 내 눈앞에서 치워 주게!\""]], [["The Sergeant was the only one among us who was fit to cope with her—being the only one among us who was in possession of himself.", "우리 가운데 마님을 감당할 만한 사람은 부장뿐이었다—우리 가운데 저를 온전히 다스리고 있는 사람이 그뿐이었으니."]], [["“I am no more answerable for this distressing calamity, my lady, than you are,” he said.", "\"이 애처로운 참사에 대해 제게 책임이 없기로는, 마님, 마님께 책임이 없는 것과 매한가지입니다.\""], ["“If, in half an hour from this, you still insist on my leaving the house, I will accept your ladyship’s dismissal, but not your ladyship’s money.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"만일, 지금부터 반 시간 뒤에도, 마님께서 여전히 제가 이 집을 떠나기를 고집하신다면, 저는 마님의 해고는 받아들이겠으나, 마님의 돈은 받지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["It was spoken very respectfully, but very firmly at the same time—and it had its effect on my mistress as well as on me.", "그것은 아주 공손하게, 그러나 동시에 아주 단호하게 뱉어진 말이었다—그리고 그것은 나에게 그러하였듯 마님께도 효험을 냈다."], ["She suffered Mr. Franklin to lead her back into the room.", "마님은 프랭클린 도련님이 당신을 방 안으로 도로 이끌어 들이도록 순순히 따르셨다."], ["As the door closed on the two, the Sergeant, looking about among the women-servants in his observant way, noticed that while all the rest were merely frightened, Penelope was in tears.", "그 둘 위로 문이 닫히자, 부장은, 그 눈여겨보는 버릇으로 하녀들 사이를 둘러보다가, 나머지가 모두 그저 겁을 먹고 있을 뿐인데 페넬로피만은 눈물을 흘리고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["“When your father has changed his wet clothes,”", "\"네 아버지가 젖은 옷을 갈아입거든,\""], ["he said to her, “come and speak to us, in your father’s room.”", "하고 그가 그 아이에게 말했다. \"네 아버지 방으로 와서, 우리와 이야기 좀 하자.\""]], [["Before the half-hour was out, I had got my dry clothes on, and had lent Sergeant Cuff such change of dress as he required.", "반 시간이 다 가기 전에, 나는 마른 옷을 갈아입었고, 커프 부장에게도 그가 필요로 하는 만큼 갈아입을 옷을 빌려주었다."], ["Penelope came in to us to hear what the Sergeant wanted with her.", "페넬로피가, 부장이 저에게 무슨 볼일이 있는지 들으러, 우리에게로 들어왔다."], ["I don’t think I ever felt what a good dutiful daughter I had, so strongly as I felt it at that moment.", "나는 그 순간만큼 내가 얼마나 착하고 효성스러운 딸을 두었는지를 그토록 사무치게 느껴 본 적이 없는 듯싶다."], ["I took her and sat her on my knee and I prayed God bless her.", "나는 그 아이를 데려다 내 무릎에 앉히고, 하느님께서 그 아이에게 복 내리시기를 빌었다."], ["She hid her head on my bosom, and put her arms round my neck—and we waited a little while in silence.", "그 아이는 내 가슴에 제 머리를 파묻고, 두 팔로 내 목을 감았다—그리고 우리는 잠시 말없이 기다렸다."], ["The poor dead girl must have been at the bottom of it, I think, with my daughter and with me.", "그 가엾이 죽은 아이가, 내 딸과 내 마음 밑바닥에 함께 자리하고 있었으리라, 나는 생각한다."], ["The Sergeant went to the window, and stood there looking out.", "부장은 창가로 가서, 거기 서서 바깥을 내다보았다."], ["I thought it right to thank him for considering us both in this way—and I did.", "나는 그가 이렇게 우리 둘을 헤아려 준 것에 대해 고마움을 표하는 것이 도리라 여겼다—그래서 그리했다."]], [["People in high life have all the luxuries to themselves—among others, the luxury of indulging their feelings.", "지체 높은 사람들은 온갖 호사를 저희끼리만 누린다—그중에서도, 제 감정을 마음껏 풀어 놓는 호사 말이다."], ["People in low life have no such privilege.", "지체 낮은 사람들에게는 그런 특권이 없다."], ["Necessity, which spares our betters, has no pity on us.", "우리보다 나은 이들은 봐주는 그 필연이라는 것이, 우리에게는 도무지 자비가 없다."], ["We learn to put our feelings back into ourselves, and to jog on with our duties as patiently as may be.", "우리는 제 감정을 도로 제 안으로 밀어 넣고, 될 수 있는 대로 참을성 있게 제 소임을 꾸역꾸역 해 나가는 법을 배운다."], ["I don’t complain of this—I only notice it.", "나는 이를 두고 불평하는 것이 아니다—다만 이를 짚어 둘 뿐이다."], ["Penelope and I were ready for the Sergeant, as soon as the Sergeant was ready on his side.", "페넬로피와 나는, 부장이 저쪽 채비를 갖추기가 무섭게, 부장을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["Asked if she knew what had led her fellow-servant to destroy herself, my daughter answered (as you will foresee) that it was for love of Mr. Franklin Blake.", "무엇이 제 동료 하인을 스스로 목숨을 끊게 했는지 아느냐는 물음에, 내 딸은 (여러분도 미리 짐작하시겠지만) 그것이 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님에 대한 사랑 때문이었다고 대답했다."], ["Asked next, if she had mentioned this notion of hers to any other person, Penelope answered, “I have not mentioned it, for Rosanna’s sake.”", "이어서, 이 제 생각을 다른 누구에게 말한 적이 있느냐는 물음에, 페넬로피는 \"저는 로재나를 생각해서, 말하지 않았습니다.\" 하고 대답했다."], ["I felt it necessary to add a word to this.", "나는 여기에 한마디 보태는 것이 마땅하다고 느꼈다."], ["I said, “And for Mr.", "나는 말했다."], ["Franklin’s sake, my dear, as well.", "\"그리고 프랭클린 도련님을 생각해서도 그런 거지, 얘야."], ["If Rosanna has died for love of him, it is not with his knowledge or by his fault.", "만일 로재나가 정말로 그분을 사랑해서 죽은 거라 해도, 그것은 그분이 아셔서도 아니고 그분 잘못으로도 아니다."], ["Let him leave the house today, if he does leave it, without the useless pain of knowing the truth.”", "그분이 오늘 이 집을 떠나신다면, 그 진실을 아는 부질없는 아픔 없이 떠나시게 하자꾸나.\""], ["Sergeant Cuff said, “Quite right,”", "커프 부장은 \"지당한 말이오.\""], ["and fell silent again; comparing Penelope’s notion (as it seemed to me) with some other notion of his own which he kept to himself.", "하고 말하고는, 다시 잠잠해졌다. 페넬로피의 생각을 (내 보기에) 저 혼자만 간직한 어떤 딴 생각과 견주어 보면서 말이다."]], [["At the end of the half-hour, my mistress’s bell rang.", "반 시간이 다 될 무렵, 마님의 초인종이 울렸다."]], [["On my way to answer it, I met Mr. Franklin coming out of his aunt’s sitting-room.", "그것에 응하러 가는 길에, 나는 이모님의 거실에서 나오는 프랭클린 도련님과 마주쳤다."], ["He mentioned that her ladyship was ready to see Sergeant Cuff—in my presence as before—and he added that he himself wanted to say two words to the Sergeant first.", "그는 마님께서 커프 부장을 만날—전처럼 내가 배석한 가운데—채비가 되셨다고 알려 주었고, 그러면서 저 자신도 먼저 부장에게 두어 마디 하고 싶다고 덧붙였다."], ["On our way back to my room, he stopped, and looked at the railway time-table in the hall.", "내 방으로 되돌아가는 길에, 그는 걸음을 멈추고, 현관에 걸린 기차 시각표를 들여다보았다."]], [["“Are you really going to leave us, sir?” I asked.", "\"정말로 저희를 떠나시렵니까, 도련님?\""], ["“Miss Rachel will surely come right again, if you only give her time?”", "하고 내가 물었다. \"시간만 좀 드리면, 레이철 아가씨도 분명 다시 마음을 돌리실 텐데요?\""]], [["“She will come right again,” answered Mr.", "\"레이철은 다시 마음을 돌릴 걸세.\""], ["Franklin, “when she hears that I have gone away, and that she will see me no more.”", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다. \"내가 떠나 버렸다는 것을, 그리고 다시는 나를 볼 일이 없다는 것을 그녀가 듣게 되면 말이야.\""]], [["I thought he spoke in resentment of my young lady’s treatment of him.", "나는 그가 우리 아가씨가 저를 대한 처사에 대한 노여움에서 그리 말하는 줄로 알았다."], ["But it was not so.", "그러나 그렇지가 않았다."], ["My mistress had noticed, from the time when the police first came into the house, that the bare mention of him was enough to set Miss Rachel’s temper in a flame.", "마님은, 경찰이 처음 이 집에 든 그때부터, 그의 이름을 그저 입에 올리는 것만으로도 레이철 아가씨의 성미를 불같이 돋우기에 넉넉하다는 것을 눈여겨보아 오셨다."], ["He had been too fond of his cousin to like to confess this to himself, until the truth had been forced on him, when she drove off to her aunt’s.", "그는 제 사촌 누이를 너무나 아꼈던 터라, 아가씨가 이모 댁으로 마차를 몰고 떠나 그 진실이 억지로 그에게 들이밀어질 때까지는, 이것을 저 자신에게 털어놓기를 꺼렸다."], ["His eyes once opened in that cruel way which you know of, Mr. Franklin had taken his resolution—the one resolution which a man of any spirit could take—to leave the house.", "여러분도 아시는 그 모진 방식으로 그의 눈이 한번 뜨이고 나자, 프랭클린 도련님은 결심을 굳혔다—조금이라도 기개 있는 사내라면 능히 내릴 수 있는 그 유일한 결심을—이 집을 떠나겠다는 결심을."]], [["What he had to say to the Sergeant was spoken in my presence.", "그가 부장에게 하고자 한 말은 내가 배석한 가운데 뱉어졌다."], ["He described her ladyship as willing to acknowledge that she had spoken over-hastily.", "그는 마님께서 당신이 너무 성급하게 말씀하셨음을 기꺼이 인정하려 하신다고 전했다."], ["And he asked if Sergeant Cuff would consent—in that case—to accept his fee, and to leave the matter of the Diamond where the matter stood now.", "그리고 그는 커프 부장이—그렇다면—제 삯을 받고, 다이아몬드 일은 지금 그대로 놓아두는 데 동의해 주겠느냐고 물었다."], ["The Sergeant answered, “No, sir.", "부장이 대답했다. \"아니오, 도련님."], ["My fee is paid me for doing my duty.", "제 삯은 제 소임을 다한 값으로 치러지는 겁니다."], ["I decline to take it, until my duty is done.”", "제 소임이 끝나기 전에는, 저는 그것을 받기를 사양하겠습니다.\""]], [["“I don’t understand you,”", "\"무슨 말인지 모르겠소.\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 도련님이 말한다."]], [["“I’ll explain myself, sir,”", "\"제 뜻을 풀어 드리지요, 도련님.\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."], ["“When I came here, I undertook to throw the necessary light on the matter of the missing Diamond.", "\"제가 이곳에 왔을 때, 저는 사라진 다이아몬드 일에 필요한 빛을 비추어 밝히겠노라 떠맡았습니다."], ["I am now ready, and waiting to redeem my pledge.", "저는 이제 채비가 되었고, 제 약조를 지키려고 기다리고 있는 참입니다."], ["When I have stated the case to Lady Verinder as the case now stands, and when I have told her plainly what course of action to take for the recovery of the Moonstone, the responsibility will be off my shoulders.", "지금 사정이 놓인 그대로 이 사건을 레이디 버린더께 아뢰고, 월장석을 되찾으려면 어떤 행보를 취해야 하는지를 마님께 명백히 말씀드리고 나면, 그 책임은 제 어깨에서 벗어납니다."], ["Let her ladyship decide, after that, whether she does, or does not, allow me to go on.", "그 뒤에, 제가 계속하도록 두실지 마실지는, 마님께서 정하시게 하십시오."], ["I shall then have done what I undertook to do—and I’ll take my fee.”", "그러면 저는 제가 떠맡은 일을 다한 것이 됩니다—그리고 저는 제 삯을 받겠습니다.\""]], [["In those words Sergeant Cuff reminded us that, even in the Detective Police, a man may have a reputation to lose.", "그 말로써 커프 부장은, 탐정 경찰에게조차, 사내에게는 잃을 명성이라는 것이 있을 수 있음을 우리에게 일깨워 주었다."]], [["The view he took was so plainly the right one, that there was no more to be said.", "그가 취한 견해가 어찌나 뻔히 옳은 것이었던지, 더 할 말이 없었다."], ["As I rose to conduct him to my lady’s room, he asked if Mr.", "내가 그를 마님의 방으로 안내하려고 일어서자, 그는 프랭클린 도련님이 배석하기를 바라느냐고 물었다. 프랭클린 도련님이 대답했다."], ["Franklin wished to be present. Mr.", "\"레이디 버린더께서 바라지 않으신다면, 그럴 것 없소.\""], ["Franklin answered, “Not unless Lady Verinder desires it.”", "그는, 내가 부장을 따라 나가려는 참에, 나직이 내 귀에 대고 덧붙였다."], ["He added, in a whisper to me, as I was following the Sergeant out, “I know what that man is going to say about Rachel; and I am too fond of her to hear it, and keep my temper.", "\"저 사람이 레이철에 대해 무슨 말을 할지 나는 알아. 그런데 나는 그녀를 너무 아껴서, 그 말을 듣고도 성미를 다스릴 수가 없어."], ["Leave me by myself.”", "나를 혼자 있게 놔두게.\""]], [["I left him, miserable enough, leaning on the sill of my window, with his face hidden in his hands and Penelope peeping through the door, longing to comfort him.", "나는 그를, 넉넉히 비참한 채로, 두 손에 얼굴을 파묻고 내 창턱에 기대어 있게 두고 나왔다. 페넬로피는 문틈으로 엿보며, 그를 위로해 주고 싶어 안달이었다."], ["In Mr. Franklin’s place, I should have called her in.", "내가 프랭클린 도련님 처지였다면, 나는 그 아이를 불러들였을 것이다."], ["When you are ill-used by one woman, there is great comfort in telling it to another—because, nine times out of ten, the other always takes your side.", "한 여인에게 모진 대접을 받았을 때는, 그것을 또 다른 여인에게 털어놓는 데 큰 위안이 있는 법이다—열 번에 아홉 번은, 그 또 다른 여인이 늘 여러분 편을 들어 주니까 말이다."], ["Perhaps, when my back was turned, he did call her in?", "어쩌면, 내가 등을 돌린 사이, 그가 그 아이를 불러들였을까?"], ["In that case it is only doing my daughter justice to declare that she would stick at nothing, in the way of comforting Mr. Franklin Blake.", "그렇다고 하면, 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님을 위로하는 일에 있어서라면 그 아이가 무슨 짓이든 마다하지 않았으리라고 선언해 두는 것이야말로 내 딸에게 마땅한 정을 베푸는 일이렷다."]], [["In the meantime, Sergeant Cuff and I proceeded to my lady’s room.", "그러는 동안, 커프 부장과 나는 마님의 방으로 나아갔다."]], [["At the last conference we had held with her, we had found her not over willing to lift her eyes from the book which she had on the table.", "마님과 마지막으로 가졌던 그 대담에서, 우리는 마님이 탁자 위에 놓아둔 책에서 눈을 좀처럼 들려 하지 않는 것을 보았었다."], ["On this occasion there was a change for the better.", "이번에는 좀 나아진 쪽으로 변화가 있었다."], ["She met the Sergeant’s eye with an eye that was as steady as his own.", "마님은 부장의 눈을, 그의 눈만큼이나 흔들림 없는 눈으로 마주하셨다."], ["The family spirit showed itself in every line of her face; and I knew that Sergeant Cuff would meet his match, when a woman like my mistress was strung up to hear the worst he could say to her.", "그 가문의 기개가 마님 얼굴의 주름 하나하나에서 드러났다. 그리고 나는, 우리 마님 같은 여인이 저에게 퍼부어질 수 있는 가장 나쁜 말을 들으려고 잔뜩 벼르고 있는 마당에는, 커프 부장도 제 맞수를 만나게 되리라는 것을 알았다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXI", "# CHAPTER XXI"]], [["The first words, when we had taken our seats, were spoken by my lady.", "우리가 자리를 잡고 앉았을 때, 첫마디를 뗀 것은 마님이었다."]], [["“Sergeant Cuff,”", "\"커프 부장.\""], ["she said, “there was perhaps some excuse for the inconsiderate manner in which I spoke to you half an hour since.", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"반 시간 전에 내가 그대에게 함부로 말한 것에는, 어쩌면 얼마쯤 변명거리가 있을지도 모르오."], ["I have no wish, however, to claim that excuse.", "허나 나는 그 변명거리를 내세우고 싶지 않소."], ["I say, with perfect sincerity, that I regret it, if I wronged you.”", "나는, 아주 진심으로 말하거니와, 만일 내가 그대를 억울하게 했다면, 그것을 뉘우치오.\""]], [["The grace of voice and manner with which she made him that atonement had its due effect on the Sergeant.", "마님이 그에게 그런 사죄를 건네는 그 목소리와 몸가짐의 기품은 부장에게 마땅한 효험을 냈다. 그는 저를 변호할 것을 청했다—제 변호를 마님께 바치는 예의의 행위로 삼으면서 말이다."], ["He requested permission to justify himself—putting his justification as an act of respect to my mistress.", "그가 우리 모두를 충격에 빠뜨린 그 참사에 조금이라도 책임이 있다는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이라고, 그가 말했다."], ["It was impossible, he said, that he could be in any way responsible for the calamity, which had shocked us all, for this sufficient reason, that his success in bringing his inquiry to its proper end depended on his neither saying nor doing anything that could alarm Rosanna Spearman.", "이런 넉넉한 이유에서였다. 곧, 그가 제 조사를 마땅한 결말에 이르게 하는 데 성공하려면, 로재나 스피어먼을 놀라게 할 만한 어떤 말도, 어떤 행동도 하지 않는 데 달려 있었기 때문이라는 것이었다."], ["He appealed to me to testify whether he had, or had not, carried that object out.", "그는 그가 그 목적을 지켜 냈는지 어쨌는지를 증언해 달라고 내게 호소했다."], ["I could, and did, bear witness that he had.", "나는, 그가 그리했노라 능히 증언할 수 있었고, 또 그리했다."], ["And there, as I thought, the matter might have been judiciously left to come to an end.", "그리고 거기서, 내 생각으로는, 이 일이 슬기롭게 마무리되도록 놓아둘 수도 있었을 것이다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff, however, took it a step further, evidently (as you shall now judge) with the purpose of forcing the most painful of all possible explanations to take place between her ladyship and himself.", "그러나 커프 부장은, (여러분도 이제 판단하시게 되겠지만) 있을 수 있는 온갖 해명 가운데 가장 고통스러운 해명이 마님과 저 사이에 벌어지도록 몰아갈 심산으로, 그것을 한 걸음 더 끌고 나갔다."]], [["“I have heard a motive assigned for the young woman’s suicide,”", "\"그 젊은 여자의 자살에 대해 어떤 동기가 붙여진 것을 저도 들었습니다.\""], ["said the Sergeant, “which may possibly be the right one.", "하고 부장이 말했다. \"그것이 어쩌면 옳은 동기일 수도 있지요."], ["It is a motive quite unconnected with the case which I am conducting here.", "그것은 제가 여기서 맡아 하고 있는 사건과는 도무지 이어지지 않는 동기입니다."], ["I am bound to add, however, that my own opinion points the other way.", "허나, 이 말은 덧붙여야겠군요, 제 나름의 견해는 그 반대쪽을 가리키고 있다고요."], ["Some unbearable anxiety in connexion with the missing Diamond, has, I believe, driven the poor creature to her own destruction.", "사라진 다이아몬드와 관련된 어떤 견딜 수 없는 근심이, 제가 믿기로는, 그 가엾은 것을 제 손으로 목숨을 끊는 데로 내몬 것입니다."], ["I don’t pretend to know what that unbearable anxiety may have been.", "그 견딜 수 없는 근심이 대체 무엇이었을지, 저는 안다고 꾸며 대지 않겠습니다."], ["But I think (with your ladyship’s permission) I can lay my hand on a person who is capable of deciding whether I am right or wrong.”", "허나 저는 (마님의 허락하에) 제가 옳은지 그른지를 능히 판가름할 수 있는 사람에게 손을 얹을 수 있으리라 생각합니다.\""]], [["“Is the person now in the house?”", "\"그 사람이 지금 이 집 안에 있소?\""], ["my mistress asked, after waiting a little.", "하고 마님이, 잠시 뜸을 들이신 뒤에 물으셨다."]], [["“The person has left the house, my lady.”", "\"그 사람은 이 집을 떠났습니다, 마님.\""]], [["That answer pointed as straight to Miss Rachel as straight could be.", "그 대답은 더할 나위 없이 곧장 레이철 아가씨를 가리키고 있었다."], ["A silence dropped on us which I thought would never come to an end.", "결코 끝날 것 같지 않은 침묵이 우리 위로 내려앉았다."], ["Lord!", "아이고!"], ["how the wind howled, and how the rain drove at the window, as I sat there waiting for one or other of them to speak again!", "내가 거기 앉아 그 둘 가운데 누군가가 다시 입을 열기를 기다리는 사이, 바람은 어찌 그리 울부짖고, 비는 어찌 그리 창문을 두들겨 대던지!"]], [["“Be so good as to express yourself plainly,”", "\"부디 분명하게 말해 주시오.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다."], ["said my lady. “Do you refer to my daughter?”", "\"내 딸을 두고 하는 말이오?\""]], [["“I do,”", "\"그렇습니다.\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, in so many words.", "하고 커프 부장이, 딱 그 몇 마디로 말했다."]], [["My mistress had her cheque-book on the table when we entered the room—no doubt to pay the Sergeant his fee.", "우리가 방에 들어섰을 때 마님은 탁자 위에 수표책을 놓아두고 계셨다—의심할 것 없이 부장에게 삯을 치르려는 것이었다."], ["She now put it back in the drawer.", "마님은 이제 그것을 서랍 속에 도로 넣으셨다."], ["It went to my heart to see how her poor hand trembled—the hand that had loaded her old servant with benefits; the hand that, I pray God, may take mine, when my time comes, and I leave my place for ever!", "그 가엾은 손이 어찌나 떨리던지 보고 있자니 내 가슴이 미어졌다—당신의 늙은 하인에게 온갖 은혜를 베풀어 주신 그 손이. 하느님께 비오니, 나의 때가 와서 내가 내 자리를 영영 떠날 적에, 부디 나의 손을 잡아 주었으면 하는 그 손이 말이다."]], [["“I had hoped,”", "\"나는 바랐소.\""], ["said my lady, very slowly and quietly, “to have recompensed your services, and to have parted with you without Miss Verinder’s name having been openly mentioned between us as it has been mentioned now.", "하고 마님이, 아주 천천히 그리고 조용히 말씀하셨다. \"그대의 수고에 값을 치르고, 지금 이렇게 오간 것처럼 버린더 아가씨의 이름이 우리 사이에 드러내 놓고 오르는 일 없이 그대와 헤어지기를 말이오."], ["My nephew has probably said something of this, before you came into my room?”", "아마 내 조카가, 그대가 내 방에 들어오기 전에, 이에 대해 무슨 말을 했겠지?\""]], [["“Mr. Blake gave his message, my lady.", "\"블레이크 도련님께서 전할 말씀을 주셨습니다, 마님."], ["And I gave Mr. Blake a reason——”", "그리고 제가 블레이크 도련님께 한 가지 이유를 말씀드렸고요—\""]], [["“It is needless to tell me your reason.", "\"그대의 이유는 내게 말할 것 없소."], ["After what you have just said, you know as well as I do that you have gone too far to go back.", "방금 그대가 한 말을 듣고 나니, 그대도 나만큼이나 잘 알 것이오, 그대가 이미 되돌아가기에는 너무 멀리 나아갔다는 것을."], ["I owe it to myself, and I owe it to my child, to insist on your remaining here, and to insist on your speaking out.”", "나는 나 자신에 대한 도리로서도, 내 자식에 대한 도리로서도, 그대가 여기 남아 있기를 고집하고, 그대가 툭 터놓고 말하기를 고집해야겠소.\""]], [["The Sergeant looked at his watch.", "부장은 제 회중시계를 들여다보았다."]], [["“If there had been time, my lady,”", "\"시간이 있었더라면, 마님.\""], ["he answered, “I should have preferred writing my report, instead of communicating it by word of mouth.", "하고 그가 대답했다. \"저는 입으로 아뢰는 대신, 제 보고서를 써서 올리는 편이 나았을 겁니다."], ["But, if this inquiry is to go on, time is of too much importance to be wasted in writing.", "허나, 만일 이 조사가 계속되어야 한다면, 시간이라는 것이 글을 쓰느라 허비하기에는 너무나 소중합니다."], ["I am ready to go into the matter at once.", "저는 당장이라도 이 문제로 들어갈 채비가 되어 있습니다."], ["It is a very painful matter for me to speak of, and for you to hear.”", "그것은 제가 말하기에도, 마님이 들으시기에도, 아주 고통스러운 문제입니다.\""]], [["There my mistress stopped him once more.", "거기서 마님이 다시 한번 그를 멈춰 세우셨다."]], [["“I may possibly make it less painful to you, and to my good servant and friend here,” she said, “if I set the example of speaking boldly, on my side.", "\"내가 내 쪽에서 대범하게 말하는 본을 보이면, 어쩌면 그것이 그대에게도, 여기 있는 나의 착한 하인이자 벗에게도, 덜 고통스러워질지 모르겠소.\""], ["You suspect Miss Verinder of deceiving us all, by secreting the Diamond for some purpose of her own?", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그대는 버린더 아가씨가, 제 나름의 어떤 속셈으로 다이아몬드를 감춤으로써, 우리 모두를 속이고 있다고 의심하는 것이오?"], ["Is that true?”", "그것이 사실이오?\""]], [["“Quite true, my lady.”", "\"틀림없는 사실입니다, 마님.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋소."], ["Now, before you begin, I have to tell you, as Miss Verinder’s mother, that she is absolutely incapable of doing what you suppose her to have done.", "자, 그대가 시작하기에 앞서, 나는 버린더 아가씨의 어미로서 그대에게 일러 두어야겠소, 그 아이는 그대가 짐작하는 그런 짓을 저지를 능력이 전혀 없다는 것을."], ["Your knowledge of her character dates from a day or two since.", "그 아이의 됨됨이에 대한 그대의 앎은 하루 이틀 전으로 거슬러 올라갈 뿐이오."], ["My knowledge of her character dates from the beginning of her life.", "그 아이의 됨됨이에 대한 나의 앎은 그 아이가 태어난 처음으로 거슬러 올라가오."], ["State your suspicion of her as strongly as you please—it is impossible that you can offend me by doing so.", "그 아이에 대한 그대의 의심을 좋을 대로 얼마든지 세게 말해 보시오—그런다고 그대가 나를 언짢게 할 수는 없을 것이오."], ["I am sure, beforehand, that (with all your experience) the circumstances have fatally misled you in this case. Mind!", "나는, 미리부터, (그대의 그 온갖 경험에도 불구하고) 이 사건에서는 정황이 그대를 돌이킬 수 없이 그르쳐 놓았다고 확신하오."], ["I am in possession of no private information.", "명심하시오! 나는 어떤 은밀한 정보도 쥐고 있지 않소."], ["I am as absolutely shut out of my daughter’s confidence as you are.", "나는 그대만큼이나 내 딸의 속내에서 온전히 밀려나 있소."], ["My one reason for speaking positively, is the reason you have heard already.", "내가 단언하여 말하는 그 한 가지 근거는, 그대가 이미 들은 그 근거요."], ["I know my child.”", "나는 내 자식을 아오.\""]], [["She turned to me, and gave me her hand.", "마님은 내게로 돌아서서, 당신 손을 내게 내미셨다."], ["I kissed it in silence.", "나는 말없이 그 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["“You may go on,”", "\"이어서 하시오.\""], ["she said, facing the Sergeant again as steadily as ever.", "하고 마님이, 여느 때처럼 흔들림 없이 부장을 다시 마주하며 말씀하셨다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff bowed.", "커프 부장은 고개를 숙였다."], ["My mistress had produced but one effect on him.", "마님은 그에게 단 한 가지 효험만을 내셨다."], ["His hatchet-face softened for a moment, as if he was sorry for her.", "그의 손도끼 같은 얼굴이 한순간 누그러졌다, 마치 마님이 딱하다는 듯이."], ["As to shaking him in his own conviction, it was plain to see that she had not moved him by a single inch.", "그러나 그 자신의 확신을 흔들어 놓는 데 있어서는, 마님이 그를 한 치도 움직이지 못했음이 뻔히 보였다."], ["He settled himself in his chair; and he began his vile attack on Miss Rachel’s character in these words:", "그는 제 의자에 자리를 고쳐 앉았다. 그리고 그는 레이철 아가씨의 됨됨이에 대한 그 비열한 공격을 이런 말로 시작했다."]], [["“I must ask your ladyship,”", "\"저는 마님께 청하는 바입니다.\""], ["he said, “to look this matter in the face, from my point of view as well as from yours.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"이 문제를, 마님의 관점에서뿐만 아니라 제 관점에서도 정면으로 바라봐 주시기를요."], ["Will you please to suppose yourself coming down here, in my place, and with my experience?", "마님께서 제 처지가 되어, 제 경험을 지니고 이곳에 내려오셨다고 여겨 봐 주시겠습니까?"], ["and will you allow me to mention very briefly what that experience has been?”", "그리고 그 경험이 어떤 것이었는지를 제가 아주 간략히 말씀드리는 것을 허락해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["My mistress signed to him that she would do this.", "마님은 그리하겠노라는 뜻을 그에게 보이셨다."], ["The Sergeant went on:", "부장은 말을 이었다."]], [["“For the last twenty years,”", "\"지난 이십 년 동안,\""], ["he said, “I have been largely employed in cases of family scandal, acting in the capacity of confidential man.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 은밀한 심부름꾼 격으로 나서서, 집안의 추문에 얽힌 사건들에 크게 부려져 왔습니다."], ["The one result of my domestic practice which has any bearing on the matter now in hand, is a result which I may state in two words.", "지금 다루고 있는 이 문제와 조금이라도 관련이 있는, 제 그 집안일 경험의 한 가지 결론은, 제가 딱 두 마디로 말씀드릴 수 있는 결론입니다."], ["It is well within my experience, that young ladies of rank and position do occasionally have private debts which they dare not acknowledge to their nearest relatives and friends.", "곧, 지체 있고 신분 있는 젊은 아가씨들이 이따금 제 가장 가까운 친척이나 벗들에게도 감히 털어놓지 못하는 사사로운 빚을 진다는 것은, 넉넉히 제 경험 안에 있는 일입니다."], ["Sometimes, the milliner and the jeweller are at the bottom of it.", "어떤 때는, 양장점 여자와 보석상이 그 밑바탕에 있지요."], ["Sometimes, the money is wanted for purposes which I don’t suspect in this case, and which I won’t shock you by mentioning.", "어떤 때는, 이번 사건에서는 제가 의심하지 않는, 그리고 입에 올려 마님을 놀라게 하고 싶지 않은 그런 용도에 그 돈이 필요한 것이고요."], ["Bear in mind what I have said, my lady—and now let us see how events in this house have forced me back on my own experience, whether I liked it or not!”", "제가 한 말을 마음에 새겨 두십시오, 마님—그리고 이제, 이 집에서 벌어진 일들이 어떻게, 제가 좋든 싫든, 저를 제 경험으로 되돌려 놓았는지 함께 봅시다!\""]], [["He considered with himself for a moment, and went on—with a horrid clearness that obliged you to understand him; with an abominable justice that favoured nobody.", "그는 잠시 혼자 헤아려 보고는, 말을 이어 갔다—여러분으로 하여금 그를 이해하지 않을 수 없게 하는 그 소름 끼치는 또렷함으로, 아무도 편들지 않는 그 얄미운 공평함으로."]], [["“My first information relating to the loss of the Moonstone,”", "\"월장석의 분실에 관한 저의 첫 정보는,\""], ["said the Sergeant, “came to me from Superintendent Seegrave.", "하고 부장이 말했다. \"시그레이브 경정에게서 제게로 왔습니다."], ["He proved to my complete satisfaction that he was perfectly incapable of managing the case.", "그는 저로 하여금 그가 이 사건을 다룰 능력이 도무지 없음을 아주 흡족하게 납득하게 해 주었지요."], ["The one thing he said which struck me as worth listening to, was this—that Miss Verinder had declined to be questioned by him, and had spoken to him with a perfectly incomprehensible rudeness and contempt.", "그가 한 말 가운데 제가 귀담아들을 만하다 여긴 단 한 가지는 이것이었습니다—버린더 아가씨가 그의 신문을 받기를 거절했고, 도무지 알 수 없는 무례함과 경멸로 그에게 말했다는 것이었지요."], ["I thought this curious—but I attributed it mainly to some clumsiness on the Superintendent’s part which might have offended the young lady.", "저는 이것을 야릇하게 여겼습니다—허나 저는 그것을, 그 젊은 아가씨의 비위를 거슬렀을지도 모를 경정 쪽의 어떤 서투름 탓으로 주로 돌렸습니다."], ["After that, I put it by in my mind, and applied myself, single-handed, to the case.", "그러고는, 저는 그것을 마음속에 밀쳐 두고, 혼자 힘으로 이 사건에 매달렸습니다."], ["It ended, as you are aware, in the discovery of the smear on the door, and in Mr. Franklin Blake’s evidence satisfying me, that this same smear, and the loss of the Diamond, were pieces of the same puzzle.", "그것은, 마님도 아시다시피, 문에 묻은 얼룩을 발견하는 것으로, 그리고 이 똑같은 얼룩과 다이아몬드의 분실이 같은 수수께끼의 조각들이라는 것을 저로 하여금 흡족히 납득하게 한 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님의 증언으로 끝났습니다."], ["So far, if I suspected anything, I suspected that the Moonstone had been stolen, and that one of the servants might prove to be the thief.", "거기까지는, 제가 무언가를 의심했다면, 저는 월장석이 도둑맞았고, 하인들 가운데 하나가 그 도둑으로 드러날지 모른다고 의심했지요."], ["Very good.", "아주 좋습니다."], ["In this state of things, what happens?", "이런 형편에서, 무슨 일이 벌어졌겠습니까?"], ["Miss Verinder suddenly comes out of her room, and speaks to me.", "버린더 아가씨가 별안간 제 방에서 나와, 제게 말을 겁니다."], ["I observe three suspicious appearances in that young lady.", "저는 그 젊은 아가씨에게서 세 가지 수상쩍은 낌새를 알아챕니다."], ["She is still violently agitated, though more than four-and-twenty hours have passed since the Diamond was lost.", "다이아몬드를 잃은 지 스물네 시간도 넘게 지났건만, 아가씨는 여전히 격렬하게 흥분해 있습니다."], ["She treats me as she has already treated Superintendent Seegrave.", "아가씨는 이미 시그레이브 경정을 대했던 것처럼 저를 대합니다."], ["And she is mortally offended with Mr. Franklin Blake.", "그리고 아가씨는 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님에게 죽도록 골이 나 있습니다."], ["Very good again.", "다시 아주 좋습니다."], ["Here (I say to myself) is a young lady who has lost a valuable jewel—a young lady, also, as my own eyes and ears inform me, who is of an impetuous temperament. Under these circumstances, and with that character, what does she do?", "여기 (하고 저는 혼잣말합니다) 값진 보석을 잃은 젊은 아가씨가 있다—또한, 제 눈과 귀가 제게 일러 주는 바로는, 성미가 불같은 젊은 아가씨가."], ["She betrays an incomprehensible resentment against Mr. Blake, Mr.", "이런 정황에서, 그런 됨됨이를 지닌 아가씨가, 무엇을 합니까?"], ["Superintendent, and myself—otherwise, the very three people who have all, in their different ways, been trying to help her to recover her lost jewel.", "아가씨는 블레이크 도련님과, 경정과, 저에게—다시 말해, 저마다 서로 다른 방식으로 아가씨가 잃은 보석을 되찾도록 도우려 애써 온 바로 그 세 사람에게—도무지 알 수 없는 원망을 드러냅니다."], ["Having brought my inquiry to that point—then, my lady, and not till then, I begin to look back into my own mind for my own experience.", "제 조사를 그 지점까지 끌고 오고 나서야—그때, 마님, 그때에 이르러서야 비로소, 저는 제 경험을 찾아 제 마음속을 되돌아보기 시작합니다."], ["My own experience explains Miss Verinder’s otherwise incomprehensible conduct.", "제 경험이 버린더 아가씨의 달리 이해할 길 없는 행동을 풀어 줍니다."], ["It associates her with those other young ladies that I know of.", "그것이 아가씨를 제가 아는 그 다른 젊은 아가씨들과 하나로 묶어 줍니다."], ["It tells me she has debts she daren’t acknowledge, that must be paid.", "그것이 제게 이르기를, 아가씨에게는 감히 털어놓지 못하는, 갚아야만 하는 빚이 있다고 합니다."], ["And it sets me asking myself, whether the loss of the Diamond may not mean—that the Diamond must be secretly pledged to pay them.", "그리고 그것이 저로 하여금 스스로에게 묻게 합니다, 다이아몬드의 분실이 뜻하는 바가—그 빚을 갚기 위해 다이아몬드를 남몰래 저당 잡혀야만 한다는 것은 아닐지를 말입니다."], ["That is the conclusion which my experience draws from plain facts.", "그것이 바로 제 경험이 명백한 사실들로부터 이끌어 낸 결론입니다."], ["What does your ladyship’s experience say against it?”", "마님의 경험은 그에 맞서 무어라 하십니까?\""]], [["“What I have said already,”", "\"내가 이미 한 말대로요.\""], ["answered my mistress.", "하고 마님이 대답하셨다."], ["“The circumstances have misled you.”", "\"정황이 그대를 그르쳐 놓았소.\""]], [["I said nothing on my side.", "나는 내 쪽에서 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Robinson Crusoe—God knows how—had got into my muddled old head.", "「로빈슨 크루소」가—하느님만 아시게 어찌 된 노릇인지—내 어리둥절한 늙은 머릿속에 들어와 있었다."], ["If Sergeant Cuff had found himself, at that moment, transported to a desert island, without a man Friday to keep him company, or a ship to take him off—he would have found himself exactly where I wished him to be!", "만일 커프 부장이, 바로 그 순간, 저와 벗해 줄 프라이데이 하나 없이, 저를 태워 갈 배 한 척 없이, 어느 무인도로 실려 갔다면—그는 딱 내가 그를 있게 하고 싶은 그 자리에 놓인 셈이었으련만!"], ["(Nota bene:—I am an average good Christian, when you don’t push my Christianity too far.", "(부기: 나는, 여러분이 내 기독교 신앙을 너무 몰아붙이지만 않는다면, 여느 만큼은 착한 기독교인이다."], ["And all the rest of you—which is a great comfort—are, in this respect, much the same as I am.)", "그리고 여러분 나머지도 모두—이것이 큰 위안이거니와—이 점에 있어서는, 나와 어지간히 매한가지렷다.)"]], [["Sergeant Cuff went on:", "커프 부장은 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Right or wrong, my lady,”", "\"옳든 그르든, 마님.\""], ["he said, “having drawn my conclusion, the next thing to do was to put it to the test.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"제 결론을 이끌어 냈으니, 그다음에 할 일은 그것을 시험대에 올리는 것이었습니다."], ["I suggested to your ladyship the examination of all the wardrobes in the house.", "저는 마님께 이 집 안의 옷장을 죄다 조사할 것을 제안했지요."], ["It was a means of finding the article of dress which had, in all probability, made the smear; and it was a means of putting my conclusion to the test.", "그것은, 십중팔구, 그 얼룩을 낸 옷가지를 찾아내는 방편이었고, 또한 제 결론을 시험대에 올리는 방편이었습니다."], ["How did it turn out?", "그것이 어떻게 되었습니까?"], ["Your ladyship consented; Mr. Blake consented; Mr.", "마님도 승낙하셨고, 블레이크 도련님도 승낙하셨고, 에이블화이트 씨도 승낙하셨습니다."], ["Ablewhite consented. Miss Verinder alone stopped the whole proceeding by refusing point-blank.", "오직 버린더 아가씨만이 딱 잘라 거절함으로써 그 온 절차를 멈춰 세웠습니다."], ["That result satisfied me that my view was the right one.", "그 결말이 제게 제 견해가 옳다는 것을 납득시켰습니다."], ["If your ladyship and Mr. Betteredge persist in not agreeing with me, you must be blind to what happened before you this very day.", "마님과 베터리지 양반이 저와 뜻을 같이하지 않기를 고집하신다면, 바로 오늘 두 분 눈앞에서 벌어진 일에 눈을 감고 계신 것임에 틀림없습니다."], ["In your hearing, I told the young lady that her leaving the house (as things were then) would put an obstacle in the way of my recovering her jewel.", "두 분이 듣는 데서, 저는 그 젊은 아가씨께, (그때 형편으로) 아가씨가 이 집을 떠나시는 것이 제가 아가씨의 보석을 되찾는 길에 걸림돌을 놓는 것이라고 말했습니다."], ["You saw yourselves that she drove off in the face of that statement.", "두 분은 아가씨가 그 말에도 아랑곳없이 마차를 몰고 떠나는 것을 몸소 보셨습니다."], ["You saw yourself that, so far from forgiving Mr. Blake for having done more than all the rest of you to put the clue into my hands, she publicly insulted Mr.", "두 분은 아가씨가, 두 분 모두를 합친 것보다 더 애써 그 실마리를 제 손에 쥐여 준 블레이크 도련님을 용서하기는커녕, 제 어머니 집 층계에서 드러내 놓고 블레이크 도련님을 모욕하는 것을 몸소 보셨습니다."], ["Blake, on the steps of her mother’s house.", "이런 일들이 무엇을 뜻합니까?"], ["What do these things mean? If Miss Verinder is not privy to the suppression of the Diamond, what do these things mean?”", "만일 버린더 아가씨가 다이아몬드를 감춘 일에 관여하고 있지 않다면, 이런 일들이 대체 무엇을 뜻한단 말입니까?\""]], [["This time he looked my way.", "이번에는 그가 내 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["It was downright frightful to hear him piling up proof after proof against Miss Rachel, and to know, while one was longing to defend her, that there was no disputing the truth of what he said.", "그가 레이철 아가씨에게 불리한 증거를 하나하나 쌓아 올리는 것을 듣고 있자니, 그리고 그 아가씨를 감싸 주고 싶어 애가 타는 와중에도, 그가 하는 말의 진실됨에는 다툴 여지가 없음을 알고 있자니, 그야말로 소름이 끼쳤다."], ["I am (thank God!) constitutionally superior to reason.", "나는 (하느님 고맙게도!) 타고나기를 이치를 넘어서게 태어났다."], ["This enabled me to hold firm to my lady’s view, which was my view also.", "이 덕에 나는, 나의 견해이기도 한 마님의 견해를 굳게 붙들 수 있었다."], ["This roused my spirit, and made me put a bold face on it before Sergeant Cuff.", "이것이 내 기개를 북돋아, 커프 부장 앞에서 뻔뻔한 낯을 지을 수 있게 했다."], ["Profit, good friends, I beseech you, by my example.", "착한 벗들이여, 부디, 나의 본을 보고 덕을 보시라."], ["It will save you from many troubles of the vexing sort.", "그것이 여러분을 성가신 부류의 숱한 근심에서 구해 줄 것이다."], ["Cultivate a superiority to reason, and see how you pare the claws of all the sensible people when they try to scratch you for your own good!", "이치를 넘어서는 기질을 갈고닦으시라, 그리고 그 온갖 사리 밝은 사람들이 여러분의 이로움을 위한답시고 여러분을 할퀴려 들 때 그들의 발톱을 어떻게 잘라 내는지 보시라!"]], [["Finding that I made no remark, and that my mistress made no remark, Sergeant Cuff proceeded.", "내가 아무 말도 하지 않고, 마님도 아무 말씀도 하지 않으심을 알고서, 커프 부장은 계속했다."], ["Lord!", "아이고!"], ["how it did enrage me to notice that he was not in the least put out by our silence!", "그가 우리의 침묵에 조금도 당황하지 않는 것을 알아채는 것이 어찌나 나를 화나게 하던지!"]], [["“There is the case, my lady, as it stands against Miss Verinder alone,” he said.", "\"버린더 아가씨 한 사람에게만 불리하게 놓여 있는, 지금 그대로의 사건은 그러합니다, 마님.\""], ["“The next thing is to put the case as it stands against Miss Verinder and the deceased Rosanna Spearman taken together.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그다음은, 버린더 아가씨와 죽은 로재나 스피어먼을 한데 묶었을 때 놓이는 그대로의 사건을 아뢰는 것입니다."], ["We will go back for a moment, if you please, to your daughter’s refusal to let her wardrobe be examined.", "괜찮으시다면, 잠시 아가씨가 제 옷장을 조사하도록 하기를 거절한 그 대목으로 되돌아가지요."], ["My mind being made up, after that circumstance, I had two questions to consider next.", "그 정황이 있고 나서 제 마음이 정해지자, 저는 그다음으로 두 가지 물음을 헤아려야 했습니다."], ["First, as to the right method of conducting my inquiry.", "첫째는, 제 조사를 이끌어 갈 옳은 방법에 관한 것이었습니다."], ["Second, as to whether Miss Verinder had an accomplice among the female servants in the house.", "둘째는, 버린더 아가씨에게 이 집 안의 하녀들 가운데 공범이 있느냐 하는 것이었습니다."], ["After carefully thinking it over, I determined to conduct the inquiry in, what we should call at our office, a highly irregular manner.", "그것을 곰곰이 따져 본 끝에, 저는, 우리 사무소에서 이르는 말로 하자면, 아주 격식에 어긋난 방식으로 이 조사를 이끌어 가기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["For this reason: I had a family scandal to deal with, which it was my business to keep within the family limits.", "이런 까닭에서였습니다. 저는 다루어야 할 집안 추문이 있었는데, 그것을 집안의 울타리 안에 묶어 두는 것이 제 소임이었지요."], ["The less noise made, and the fewer strangers employed to help me, the better.", "소란은 덜 일으킬수록, 돕는 데 부려지는 외부인은 적을수록 좋았습니다."], ["As to the usual course of taking people in custody on suspicion, going before the magistrate, and all the rest of it—nothing of the sort was to be thought of, when your ladyship’s daughter was (as I believed) at the bottom of the whole business.", "혐의로 사람들을 붙잡아 가두고, 치안판사 앞에 세우고, 그 밖의 온갖 일을 벌이는 여느 절차로 말하자면—마님의 따님이 (제가 믿기로는) 이 온 일의 밑바탕에 있는 마당에, 그런 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었습니다."], ["In this case, I felt that a person of Mr. Betteredge’s character and position in the house—knowing the servants as he did, and having the honour of the family at heart—would be safer to take as an assistant than any other person whom I could lay my hand on.", "이 경우에, 저는 베터리지 양반 같은 됨됨이와, 이 집에서의 지위를 지닌 사람이라면—하인들을 그이만큼 잘 알고, 집안의 명예를 마음에 두고 있으니—제가 손댈 수 있는 다른 어떤 사람보다 조수로 삼기에 더 미더우리라 느꼈습니다."], ["I should have tried Mr. Blake as well—but for one obstacle in the way.", "저는 블레이크 도련님도 시험해 보려 했습니다—허나 길을 가로막는 한 가지 걸림돌이 있었지요."], ["He saw the drift of my proceedings at a very early date; and, with his interest in Miss Verinder, any mutual understanding was impossible between him and me.", "그이는 아주 이른 때에 제 하는 일의 낌새를 알아챘습니다. 그리고, 버린더 아가씨에 대한 그이의 마음으로 보아, 그이와 저 사이에 어떤 서로 간의 양해도 있을 수 없었습니다."], ["I trouble your ladyship with these particulars to show you that I have kept the family secret within the family circle.", "제가 이런 자잘한 것들로 마님을 성가시게 하는 것은, 제가 이 집안의 비밀을 집안의 테두리 안에 지켜 왔음을 보여 드리기 위해서입니다."], ["I am the only outsider who knows it—and my professional existence depends on holding my tongue.”", "그것을 아는 외부인은 저 하나뿐이고요—그리고 제 입을 다무는 데에 저의 직업적 목숨이 달려 있습니다.\""]], [["Here I felt that my professional existence depended on not holding my tongue.", "여기서 나는, 나의 직업적 목숨은 나의 입을 다물지 않는 데에 달려 있음을 느꼈다."], ["To be held up before my mistress, in my old age, as a sort of deputy-policeman, was, once again, more than my Christianity was strong enough to bear.", "이 늘그막에, 마님 앞에서, 일종의 보조 순경 노릇을 한 사람으로 치켜세워지는 것은, 다시 한번, 나의 기독교 신앙이 견뎌 낼 만큼 굳세지 못한 노릇이었다."]], [["“I beg to inform your ladyship,”", "\"마님께 아뢰옵기를 청하옵니다.\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “that I never, to my knowledge, helped this abominable detective business, in any way, from first to last; and I summon Sergeant Cuff to contradict me, if he dares!”", "\"저는, 제가 아는 한, 처음부터 끝까지 이 얄미운 탐정질을 어떤 식으로도 결코 돕지 않았사옵니다. 그리고 저는 커프 부장에게 요구하는 바입니다, 감히 그럴 배짱이 있거든, 저를 반박해 보라고요!\""]], [["Having given vent in those words, I felt greatly relieved.", "그 말로써 속을 후련히 풀고 나니, 나는 크게 홀가분해졌다."], ["Her ladyship honoured me by a little friendly pat on the shoulder.", "마님은 내 어깨를 다정하게 살짝 두드려 주심으로써 나를 높여 주셨다."], ["I looked with righteous indignation at the Sergeant, to see what he thought of such a testimony as that!", "나는, 부장이 그런 증언을 어찌 여기는지 보려고, 정의로운 노여움에 차 그를 바라보았다!"], ["The Sergeant looked back like a lamb, and seemed to like me better than ever.", "부장은 어린 양처럼 마주 바라보았고, 그 어느 때보다 나를 더 마음에 들어 하는 듯싶었다."]], [["My lady informed him that he might continue his statement.", "마님은 그가 진술을 계속해도 좋다고 그에게 이르셨다."], ["“I understand,”", "\"나는 이해하오.\""], ["she said, “that you have honestly done your best, in what you believe to be my interest.", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그대가, 내 이로움이라 믿는 바를 위해, 정직하게 그대의 최선을 다했다는 것을."], ["I am ready to hear what you have to say next.”", "나는 그대가 다음에 할 말을 들을 채비가 되어 있소.\""]], [["“What I have to say next,”", "\"제가 다음에 아뢸 것은,\""], ["answered Sergeant Cuff, “relates to Rosanna Spearman.", "하고 커프 부장이 대답했다. \"로재나 스피어먼에 관한 것입니다."], ["I recognised the young woman, as your ladyship may remember, when she brought the washing-book into this room.", "마님도 어쩌면 기억하시겠지만, 저는 그 젊은 여자가 이 방에 세탁 장부를 가지고 들어왔을 때 그를 알아보았습니다."], ["Up to that time I was inclined to doubt whether Miss Verinder had trusted her secret to anyone.", "그때까지 저는 버린더 아가씨가 제 비밀을 누구에게든 맡겼을지 어떨지를 미심쩍어하는 쪽이었지요."], ["When I saw Rosanna, I altered my mind.", "로재나를 보았을 때, 저는 마음을 고쳐먹었습니다."], ["I suspected her at once of being privy to the suppression of the Diamond.", "저는 대뜸 그가 다이아몬드를 감춘 일에 관여하고 있다고 의심했습니다."], ["The poor creature has met her death by a dreadful end, and I don’t want your ladyship to think, now she’s gone, that I was unduly hard on her.", "그 가엾은 것이 끔찍한 종말로 제 죽음을 맞았으니, 저는 마님께서, 이제 그가 갔다고 해서, 제가 그를 지나치게 모질게 대했다고 여기시기를 바라지 않습니다."], ["If this had been a common case of thieving, I should have given Rosanna the benefit of the doubt just as freely as I should have given it to any of the other servants in the house.", "만일 이것이 여느 도둑질 사건이었다면, 저는 이 집의 다른 어느 하인에게든 그리했을 것과 꼭 마찬가지로 선선히 로재나에게 의심의 이로움을 베풀었을 것입니다."], ["Our experience of the Reformatory women is, that when tried in service—and when kindly and judiciously treated—they prove themselves in the majority of cases to be honestly penitent, and honestly worthy of the pains taken with them.", "감화원 출신 여자들에 대한 우리의 경험은, 그들이 하인살이로 시험받을 때—그리고 다정하고 슬기롭게 다루어질 때—태반의 경우에 정직하게 뉘우치고, 그들에게 들인 수고에 정직하게 값하는 사람임을 스스로 증명해 보인다는 것입니다."], ["But this was not a common case of thieving.", "허나 이것은 여느 도둑질 사건이 아니었습니다."], ["It was a case—in my mind—of a deeply planned fraud, with the owner of the Diamond at the bottom of it.", "그것은—제 마음속으로는—다이아몬드의 주인이 그 밑바탕에 있는, 깊이 꾸며진 사기 사건이었습니다."], ["Holding this view, the first consideration which naturally presented itself to me, in connection with Rosanna, was this: Would Miss Verinder be satisfied (begging your ladyship’s pardon) with leading us all to think that the Moonstone was merely lost?", "이런 견해를 지니고 보니, 로재나와 관련하여 제게 자연스레 떠오른 첫 헤아림은 이것이었습니다. 곧, 버린더 아가씨는 (마님, 송구하오나) 우리 모두로 하여금 월장석이 그저 없어졌을 뿐이라고 여기게 하는 것으로 만족했을까요?"], ["Or would she go a step further, and delude us into believing that the Moonstone was stolen?", "아니면 아가씨는 한 걸음 더 나아가, 우리를 홀려 월장석이 도둑맞았다고 믿게 했을까요?"], ["In the latter event there was Rosanna Spearman—with the character of a thief—ready to her hand; the person of all others to lead your ladyship off, and to lead me off, on a false scent.”", "뒤엣것이라면, 거기에 로재나 스피어먼이—도둑이라는 이력을 지닌 채—아가씨 손안에 마련되어 있었지요. 다른 누구보다도, 마님을 그릇된 자취로 이끌고, 저를 그릇된 자취로 이끌기에 알맞은 사람이 말입니다.\""]], [["Was it possible (I asked myself) that he could put his case against Miss Rachel and Rosanna in a more horrid point of view than this?", "(하고 나는 스스로에게 물었다) 그가 레이철 아가씨와 로재나에게 불리한 그의 사건을 이보다 더 소름 끼치는 관점으로 세울 수 있을까?"], ["It was possible, as you shall now see.", "세울 수 있었다, 여러분도 이제 보시게 되겠지만."]], [["“I had another reason for suspecting the deceased woman,”", "\"저는 그 죽은 여자를 의심할 또 다른 이유가 있었습니다.\""], ["he said, “which appears to me to have been stronger still.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"제게는 그것이 오히려 더 강력했던 것으로 여겨집니다."], ["Who would be the very person to help Miss Verinder in raising money privately on the Diamond?", "버린더 아가씨가 다이아몬드로 남몰래 돈을 마련하는 것을 도울, 바로 그 적임자가 누구였겠습니까?"], ["Rosanna Spearman.", "로재나 스피어먼입니다."], ["No young lady in Miss Verinder’s position could manage such a risky matter as that by herself.", "버린더 아가씨 같은 처지의 젊은 아가씨는 그처럼 위험한 일을 혼자 힘으로 꾸려 나갈 수 없습니다."], ["A go-between she must have, and who so fit, I ask again, as Rosanna Spearman?", "아가씨에게는 중개인이 하나 있어야만 했는데, 다시 여쭙거니와, 로재나 스피어먼만큼 알맞은 이가 어디 있겠습니까?"], ["Your ladyship’s deceased housemaid was at the top of her profession when she was a thief.", "마님의 죽은 하녀는 도둑이던 시절에 제 직업의 정점에 있던 사람입니다."], ["She had relations, to my certain knowledge, with one of the few men in London (in the money-lending line) who would advance a large sum on such a notable jewel as the Moonstone, without asking awkward questions, or insisting on awkward conditions.", "그는, 제가 확실히 아는 바로는, 월장석 같은 이름난 보석에 대해 껄끄러운 질문을 던지거나 껄끄러운 조건을 내걸지 않고 큰돈을 내어줄, 런던의 (돈놀이 쪽) 몇 안 되는 사내들 가운데 하나와 왕래가 있었습니다. 이것을 마음에 새겨 두십시오, 마님."], ["Bear this in mind, my lady; and now let me show you how my suspicions have been justified by Rosanna’s own acts, and by the plain inferences to be drawn from them.”", "그리고 이제 제 의심이 로재나 자신의 행위로써, 그리고 그로부터 이끌어 낼 수 있는 명백한 추론으로써 어떻게 정당화되었는지 보여 드리겠습니다.\""]], [["He thereupon passed the whole of Rosanna’s proceedings under review.", "그러고서 그는 로재나의 온갖 행적을 낱낱이 훑어 냈다."], ["You are already as well acquainted with those proceedings as I am; and you will understand how unanswerably this part of his report fixed the guilt of being concerned in the disappearance of the Moonstone on the memory of the poor dead girl.", "여러분은 그 행적들을 이미 나만큼이나 잘 알고 계신다. 그러니 여러분은 그의 보고의 이 대목이 어떻게, 월장석의 실종에 관여했다는 죄를 그 가엾이 죽은 아이의 기억 위에 반박할 길 없이 못 박아 놓았는지 아실 것이다."], ["Even my mistress was daunted by what he said now.", "마님조차 그가 이제 하는 말에 기가 꺾이셨다."], ["She made him no answer when he had done.", "마님은 그가 말을 마쳤을 때 그에게 아무 대꾸도 하지 않으셨다."], ["It didn’t seem to matter to the Sergeant whether he was answered or not.", "대꾸를 받든 못 받든 부장에게는 대수롭지 않은 듯싶었다."], ["On he went (devil take him!), just as steady as ever.", "그는 계속 나아갔다 (빌어먹을 인간 같으니!), 여느 때처럼 꼭 그대로 태연하게."]], [["“Having stated the whole case as I understand it,”", "\"제가 이해하는 그대로 온 사건을 아뢰었으니,\""], ["he said, “I have only to tell your ladyship, now, what I propose to do next.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 이제 마님께, 제가 다음에 무엇을 하려는지만 말씀드리면 됩니다."], ["I see two ways of bringing this inquiry successfully to an end.", "저는 이 조사를 성공리에 끝맺을 두 가지 길이 보입니다."], ["One of those ways I look upon as a certainty.", "그 두 길 가운데 하나를 저는 확실한 것으로 봅니다."], ["The other, I admit, is a bold experiment, and nothing more.", "다른 하나는, 인정하거니와, 대담한 시도일 뿐, 그 이상은 아닙니다."], ["Your ladyship shall decide.", "마님께서 정하시게 됩니다."], ["Shall we take the certainty first?”", "확실한 것부터 취할까요?\""]], [["My mistress made him a sign to take his own way, and choose for himself.", "마님은 그에게, 그가 제 방식대로 하고, 스스로 고르라는 뜻을 보이셨다."]], [["“Thank you,”", "\"고맙습니다.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“We’ll begin with the certainty, as your ladyship is so good as to leave it to me.", "\"마님께서 이렇게 제게 맡겨 주시니, 확실한 것부터 시작하지요."], ["Whether Miss Verinder remains at Frizinghall, or whether she returns here, I propose, in either case, to keep a careful watch on all her proceedings—on the people she sees, on the rides and walks she may take, and on the letters she may write and receive.”", "버린더 아가씨가 프리징홀에 머무시든, 아니면 이곳으로 돌아오시든, 저는 어느 경우에든, 아가씨의 온갖 행보를—아가씨가 만나는 사람들과, 아가씨가 나서는 승마와 산책과, 아가씨가 쓰고 받는 편지들을 낱낱이 세심하게 지켜볼 작정입니다.\""]], [["“What next?”", "\"그다음은?\""], ["asked my mistress.", "하고 마님이 물으셨다."]], [["“I shall next,”", "\"저는 그다음으로,\""], ["answered the Sergeant, “request your ladyship’s leave to introduce into the house, as a servant in the place of Rosanna Spearman, a woman accustomed to private inquiries of this sort, for whose discretion I can answer.”", "하고 부장이 대답했다. \"로재나 스피어먼의 자리를 대신하는 하인으로서, 이런 종류의 은밀한 조사에 익은, 제가 그 신중함을 보증할 수 있는 여자 하나를 이 집에 들이는 것을 허락해 주십사 마님께 청하겠습니다.\""]], [["“What next?”", "\"그다음은?\""], ["repeated my mistress.", "하고 마님이 되물으셨다."]], [["“Next,”", "\"그다음으로,\""], ["proceeded the Sergeant, “and last, I propose to send one of my brother-officers to make an arrangement with that money-lender in London, whom I mentioned just now as formerly acquainted with Rosanna Spearman—and whose name and address, your ladyship may rely on it, have been communicated by Rosanna to Miss Verinder.", "하고 부장이 이어 갔다. \"그리고 마지막으로, 저는 제 동료 하나를 보내어, 방금 로재나 스피어먼과 예전에 안면이 있다고 말씀드린 그 런던의 돈놀이꾼과 약조를 맺게 할 작정입니다—그리고 그자의 이름과 주소는, 마님, 로재나가 버린더 아가씨께 알려 준 것이 틀림없다고 믿으셔도 좋습니다."], ["I don’t deny that the course of action I am now suggesting will cost money, and consume time.", "제가 지금 제안하는 이 행보가 돈이 들고 시간이 걸리리라는 것을 저는 부인하지 않습니다."], ["But the result is certain.", "허나 그 결과는 확실합니다."], ["We run a line round the Moonstone, and we draw that line closer and closer till we find it in Miss Verinder’s possession, supposing she decides to keep it.", "우리는 월장석 둘레에 한 줄을 두르고, 그 줄을 점점 더 바싹 조여, 마침내 그것을 버린더 아가씨가 지니고 계신 데서 찾아내는 것입니다, 아가씨가 그것을 간직하기로 정하신다면 말입니다."], ["If her debts press, and she decides on sending it away, then we have our man ready, and we meet the Moonstone on its arrival in London.”", "만일 빚이 아가씨를 몰아붙여, 아가씨가 그것을 부쳐 보내기로 정하신다면, 그때는 우리 사람이 대기하고 있다가, 월장석이 런던에 다다르는 것을 맞이하는 것이지요.\""]], [["To hear her own daughter made the subject of such a proposal as this, stung my mistress into speaking angrily for the first time.", "당신의 친딸이 그런 제안의 대상이 되는 것을 듣자, 마님은 처음으로 노기 띤 말씀을 하시게 되었다."]], [["“Consider your proposal declined, in every particular,” she said.", "\"그대의 제안은, 낱낱이, 거절된 것으로 아시오.\""], ["“And go on to your other way of bringing the inquiry to an end.”", "하고 마님이 말씀하셨다. \"그리고 조사를 끝맺을 그대의 다른 길로 넘어가시오.\""]], [["“My other way,”", "\"제 다른 길은,\""], ["said the Sergeant, going on as easy as ever, “is to try that bold experiment to which I have alluded.", "하고 부장이, 여느 때처럼 태연하게 이어 가며 말했다. \"제가 넌지시 말씀드린 그 대담한 시도를 해 보는 것입니다."], ["I think I have formed a pretty correct estimate of Miss Verinder’s temperament.", "저는 버린더 아가씨의 성미에 대해 제법 정확한 어림을 지니게 되었다고 생각합니다."], ["She is quite capable (according to my belief) of committing a daring fraud.", "아가씨는 (제 믿음으로는) 대담한 사기를 저지를 만한 사람이 넉넉히 됩니다."], ["But she is too hot and impetuous in temper, and too little accustomed to deceit as a habit, to act the hypocrite in small things, and to restrain herself under all provocations.", "허나 아가씨는 성미가 너무 뜨겁고 불같으며, 속임수를 몸에 밴 버릇으로 삼는 데 너무 익지 않아, 자잘한 일에까지 위선을 부리고 온갖 부아 돋우는 일에도 저를 억누르지는 못합니다."], ["Her feelings, in this case, have repeatedly got beyond her control, at the very time when it was plainly her interest to conceal them.", "아가씨의 감정은, 이번 사건에서, 그것을 감추는 것이 뻔히 아가씨의 이로움이던 바로 그 순간에, 거듭거듭 아가씨의 다스림을 벗어났습니다."], ["It is on this peculiarity in her character that I now propose to act.", "제가 이제 발판으로 삼으려는 것은 아가씨의 됨됨이에 있는 바로 이 별난 점입니다."], ["I want to give her a great shock suddenly, under circumstances that will touch her to the quick.", "저는 아가씨의 아픈 데를 찌를 정황 아래, 별안간 아가씨에게 큰 충격을 주고 싶습니다."], ["In plain English, I want to tell Miss Verinder, without a word of warning, of Rosanna’s death—on the chance that her own better feelings will hurry her into making a clean breast of it.", "쉬운 말로 하자면, 저는 버린더 아가씨께, 아무런 미리 알림도 없이, 로재나의 죽음을 말하고 싶습니다—아가씨 자신의 더 나은 심성이 아가씨를 부추겨 죄다 툭 털어놓게 할지 모른다는 요행을 노리고 말입니다."], ["Does your ladyship accept that alternative?”", "마님께서는 그 대안을 받아들이시겠습니까?\""]], [["My mistress astonished me beyond all power of expression.", "마님은 이루 말할 수 없이 나를 놀라게 하셨다."], ["She answered him on the instant:", "마님은 그 자리에서 대뜸 그에게 대답하셨다."]], [["“Yes; I do.”", "\"그러겠소. 받아들이겠소.\""]], [["“The pony-chaise is ready,”", "\"조랑말 이륜마차가 준비되어 있습니다.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“I wish your ladyship good morning.”", "\"마님, 안녕히 계십시오.\""]], [["My lady held up her hand, and stopped him at the door.", "마님은 손을 들어, 문가에서 그를 멈춰 세우셨다."]], [["“My daughter’s better feelings shall be appealed to, as you propose,”", "\"내 딸의 더 나은 심성에, 그대가 제안한 대로 호소해 보겠소.\" 하고 마님이 말씀하셨다."], ["she said. “But I claim the right, as her mother, of putting her to the test myself.", "\"허나 나는, 그 아이의 어미로서, 그 아이를 몸소 시험대에 올릴 권리를 내세우겠소."], ["You will remain here, if you please; and I will go to Frizinghall.”", "괜찮다면, 그대는 여기 남아 있으시오. 그리고 프리징홀에는 내가 가겠소.\""]], [["For once in his life, the great Cuff stood speechless with amazement, like an ordinary man.", "평생에 단 한 번, 그 위대한 커프가 여느 사람처럼 넋을 잃고 말문이 막힌 채 서 있었다."]], [["My mistress rang the bell, and ordered her waterproof things.", "마님은 초인종을 울려, 당신의 방수 우비를 가져오라 이르셨다."], ["It was still pouring with rain; and the close carriage had gone, as you know, with Miss Rachel to Frizinghall.", "여전히 비가 억수같이 쏟아지고 있었다. 그리고 그 지붕 있는 마차는, 여러분도 아시다시피, 레이철 아가씨를 태우고 프리징홀로 가 버린 터였다."], ["I tried to dissuade her ladyship from facing the severity of the weather.", "나는 마님께 그 모진 날씨를 무릅쓰지 마시라 말리려 했다."], ["Quite useless!", "아주 부질없었다!"], ["I asked leave to go with her, and hold the umbrella.", "나는 함께 가서 우산을 받쳐 들게 해 달라 청했다."], ["She wouldn’t hear of it.", "마님은 들으려 하지 않으셨다."], ["The pony-chaise came round, with the groom in charge.", "조랑말 이륜마차가, 마부가 맡아 몰고, 돌아 나왔다."], ["“You may rely on two things,”", "\"그대는 두 가지를 믿어도 좋소.\""], ["she said to Sergeant Cuff, in the hall.", "하고 마님이 현관에서 커프 부장에게 말씀하셨다."], ["“I will try the experiment on Miss Verinder as boldly as you could try it yourself.", "\"나는 그대 자신이 능히 시험해 볼 수 있을 만큼 대담하게 버린더 아가씨에게 그 시도를 해 보겠소."], ["And I will inform you of the result, either personally or by letter, before the last train leaves for London tonight.”", "그리고 그 결과를, 몸소든 편지로든, 오늘 밤 런던행 마지막 기차가 떠나기 전에 그대에게 알리겠소.\""]], [["With that, she stepped into the chaise, and, taking the reins herself, drove off to Frizinghall.", "그 말과 함께, 마님은 이륜마차에 올라, 몸소 고삐를 잡고, 프리징홀로 몰고 떠나셨다."]], [["# CHAPTER XXII", "# CHAPTER XXII"]], [["My mistress having left us, I had leisure to think of Sergeant Cuff.", "마님이 우리를 떠나시고 나자, 나는 커프 부장을 생각할 겨를이 생겼다."], ["I found him sitting in a snug corner of the hall, consulting his memorandum book, and curling up viciously at the corners of the lips.", "나는 그가 현관의 아늑한 구석에 앉아, 제 비망록을 들여다보며, 입가를 짓궂게 말아 올리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“Making notes of the case?”", "\"사건에 대한 기록을 하시오?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“No,” said the Sergeant.", "\"아니오.\""], ["“Looking to see what my next professional engagement is.”", "하고 부장이 말했다. \"내 다음번 일거리가 무엇인지 살펴보고 있소.\""]], [["“Oh!”", "\"허!\""], ["I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["“You think it’s all over then, here?”", "\"그럼 여기 일은 죄다 끝났다고 여기는 거요?\""]], [["“I think,”", "\"내 생각에는,\""], ["answered Sergeant Cuff, “that Lady Verinder is one of the cleverest women in England.", "하고 커프 부장이 대답했다. \"레이디 버린더는 잉글랜드에서 가장 영리한 여인 가운데 하나요."], ["I also think a rose much better worth looking at than a diamond.", "그리고 내 생각에는, 다이아몬드보다 장미 한 송이가 훨씬 더 들여다볼 값어치가 있고."], ["Where is the gardener, Mr. Betteredge?”", "정원사는 어디 있소, 베터리지 양반?\""]], [["There was no getting a word more out of him on the matter of the Moonstone.", "월장석 일에 대해서는 그에게서 더는 한마디도 끌어낼 수가 없었다."], ["He had lost all interest in his own inquiry; and he would persist in looking for the gardener.", "그는 제 조사에 대한 온갖 흥미를 잃어버렸고, 한사코 정원사를 찾겠다는 것이었다."], ["An hour afterwards, I heard them at high words in the conservatory, with the dog-rose once more at the bottom of the dispute.", "한 시간 뒤, 나는 그 둘이 온실에서 언성을 높이는 소리를 들었는데, 그 다툼의 밑바탕에는 다시 한번 들장미가 자리하고 있었다."]], [["In the meantime, it was my business to find out whether Mr. Franklin persisted in his resolution to leave us by the afternoon train.", "그러는 동안, 프랭클린 도련님이 오후 기차로 우리를 떠나겠다는 결심을 여전히 굳게 지키고 있는지 알아보는 것이 내 일이었다."], ["After having been informed of the conference in my lady’s room, and of how it had ended, he immediately decided on waiting to hear the news from Frizinghall.", "마님의 방에서 있었던 그 대담과, 그것이 어떻게 끝났는지에 대해 전해 듣고 나자, 그는 대뜸 프리징홀에서 오는 소식을 기다려 듣기로 마음먹었다."], ["This very natural alteration in his plans—which, with ordinary people, would have led to nothing in particular—proved, in Mr. Franklin’s case, to have one objectionable result.", "계획에 있어 이 아주 자연스러운 변경은—여느 사람이라면 딱히 아무 일로도 이어지지 않았을 것이나—프랭클린 도련님의 경우에는, 한 가지 달갑잖은 결과를 낳는 것으로 드러났다."], ["It left him unsettled, with a legacy of idle time on his hands, and, in so doing, it let out all the foreign sides of his character, one on the top of another, like rats out of a bag.", "그것은 그를 어수선하게 붙들어 놓아, 손에 한가한 시간이라는 유산을 안겼고, 그렇게 함으로써, 그의 됨됨이의 온갖 외국물 든 면모를, 자루에서 쏟아져 나오는 쥐 떼처럼, 하나 위에 또 하나씩, 죄다 풀어놓았다."]], [["Now as an Italian-Englishman, now as a German-Englishman, and now as a French-Englishman, he drifted in and out of all the sitting-rooms in the house, with nothing to talk of but Miss Rachel’s treatment of him; and with nobody to address himself to but me.", "이탈리아식 잉글랜드인이 되었다가, 독일식 잉글랜드인이 되었다가, 또 프랑스식 잉글랜드인이 되어, 그는 레이철 아가씨가 저를 대한 처사 말고는 이야기할 거리라곤 없이, 또 나 말고는 하소연할 상대라곤 없이, 집 안의 온갖 거실을 들락날락 떠돌았다."], ["I found him (for example) in the library, sitting under the map of Modern Italy, and quite unaware of any other method of meeting his troubles, except the method of talking about them.", "나는 (이를테면) 서재에서, 근대 이탈리아 지도 아래 앉아, 제 근심을 마주하는 데에 그 근심에 대해 이야기하는 방법 말고는 다른 어떤 방법도 도무지 알지 못하는 그를 보았다."], ["“I have several worthy aspirations, Betteredge; but what am I to do with them now?", "\"나는 여러 가지 훌륭한 포부가 있어, 베터리지. 그런데 이제 그것들을 가지고 어쩌란 말인가?"], ["I am full of dormant good qualities, if Rachel would only have helped me to bring them out!”", "나는 잠들어 있는 좋은 자질들로 가득 차 있는데, 레이철이 그것들을 끌어내는 것을 도와주기만 했더라면!\""], ["He was so eloquent in drawing the picture of his own neglected merits, and so pathetic in lamenting over it when it was done, that I felt quite at my wits’ end how to console him, when it suddenly occurred to me that here was a case for the wholesome application of a bit of Robinson Crusoe.", "그는 제 등한시된 미덕을 그려 보이는 데에 어찌나 청산유수던지, 그리고 그것을 다 그리고 나서 그것을 두고 한탄하는 데에 어찌나 애처롭던지, 그를 어떻게 위로해야 할지 나는 그야말로 어찌할 바를 몰랐는데, 문득 여기야말로 「로빈슨 크루소」한 대목을 몸에 이롭게 발라 줄 사례라는 생각이 떠올랐다."], ["I hobbled out to my own room, and hobbled back with that immortal book.", "나는 절뚝거리며 내 방으로 나가, 그 불멸의 책을 들고 절뚝거리며 돌아왔다."], ["Nobody in the library!", "서재에는 아무도 없었다!"], ["The map of Modern Italy stared at me; and I stared at the map of Modern Italy.", "근대 이탈리아 지도가 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다. 그리고 나는 근대 이탈리아 지도를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["I tried the drawing-room.", "나는 응접실을 살펴보았다."], ["There was his handkerchief on the floor, to prove that he had drifted in.", "마룻바닥에 그의 손수건이 떨어져 있어, 그가 그리로 떠들어 왔음을 증명해 주었다."], ["And there was the empty room to prove that he had drifted out again.", "그리고 텅 빈 방이 있어, 그가 다시 떠들어 나갔음을 증명해 주었다."]], [["I tried the dining-room, and discovered Samuel with a biscuit and a glass of sherry, silently investigating the empty air.", "나는 식당을 살펴보았다가, 새뮤얼이 비스킷 한 조각과 셰리주 한 잔을 든 채, 말없이 텅 빈 허공을 살피고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["A minute since, Mr. Franklin had rung furiously for a little light refreshment.", "조금 전, 프랭클린 도련님이 가벼운 요깃거리를 가져오라고 사납게 초인종을 울렸다."], ["On its production, in a violent hurry, by Samuel, Mr. Franklin had vanished before the bell downstairs had quite done ringing with the pull he had given to it.", "그것이, 새뮤얼에 의해, 몹시 부산스럽게 마련되어 나오자, 프랭클린 도련님은, 그가 잡아당긴 그 초인종이 아래층에서 채 다 울리기도 전에, 사라져 버렸다는 것이었다."]], [["I tried the morning-room, and found him at last.", "나는 아침 방을 살펴보았고, 마침내 그를 찾아냈다."], ["There he was at the window, drawing hieroglyphics with his finger in the damp on the glass.", "그는 거기 창가에서, 유리에 서린 김에 손가락으로 상형 문자를 그리고 있었다."]], [["“Your sherry is waiting for you, sir,”", "\"셰리주가 기다리고 있습니다, 도련님.\""], ["I said to him.", "하고 나는 그에게 말했다."], ["I might as well have addressed myself to one of the four walls of the room; he was down in the bottomless deep of his own meditations, past all pulling up.", "방의 네 벽 가운데 하나에 대고 말하는 편이 차라리 나았으리라. 그는 제 명상의 바닥 모를 깊은 데 잠겨, 도무지 끌어 올릴 길이 없었다."], ["“How do you explain Rachel’s conduct, Betteredge?” was the only answer I received.", "\"자네는 레이철의 행동을 어떻게 설명하나, 베터리지?\"가 내가 받은 유일한 대답이었다."], ["Not being ready with the needful reply, I produced Robinson Crusoe, in which I am firmly persuaded some explanation might have been found, if we had only searched long enough for it.", "마땅한 대꾸를 얼른 내놓지 못한 채, 나는 「로빈슨 크루소」를 꺼내 들었는데, 그 안에서라면, 우리가 그것을 넉넉히 오래 찾기만 했더라면, 어떤 설명이든 찾아낼 수 있었으리라고 나는 굳게 믿는다."], ["Mr. Franklin shut up Robinson Crusoe, and floundered into his German-English gibberish on the spot.", "프랭클린 도련님은 「로빈슨 크루소」를 탁 덮고는, 그 자리에서 그의 독일식 잉글랜드 횡설수설로 허우적거리며 빠져들었다."], ["“Why not look into it?”", "\"왜 그것을 파고들어 보지 않는 건가?\""], ["he said, as if I had personally objected to looking into it.", "하고 그가, 마치 내가 몸소 그것을 파고드는 데 반대하기라도 한 듯 말했다."], ["“Why the devil lose your patience, Betteredge, when patience is all that’s wanted to arrive at the truth?", "\"빌어먹을, 진실에 다다르는 데 필요한 것이라곤 참을성뿐인데, 어째서 참을성을 잃는단 말인가, 베터리지?"], ["Don’t interrupt me.", "내 말을 끊지 말게."], ["Rachel’s conduct is perfectly intelligible, if you will only do her the common justice to take the Objective view first, and the Subjective view next, and the Objective-Subjective view to wind up with.", "레이철의 행동은, 자네가 그저 그녀에게 마땅한 공정을 베풀어, 먼저 객관적 관점을 취하고, 그다음에 주관적 관점을 취하고, 그리고 마무리로 객관적-주관적 관점을 취하기만 한다면, 아주 또렷이 이해될 수 있어."], ["What do we know?", "우리가 무엇을 아는가?"], ["We know that the loss of the Moonstone, on Thursday morning last, threw her into a state of nervous excitement, from which she has not recovered yet.", "우리는 지난 목요일 아침의 월장석 분실이 그녀를, 아직도 헤어나지 못한 신경 흥분 상태에 빠뜨렸다는 것을 알지."], ["Do you mean to deny the Objective view, so far?", "자네는 여기까지의 객관적 관점을 부인하려는 건가?"], ["Very well, then—don’t interrupt me.", "좋아, 그렇다면—내 말을 끊지 말게."], ["Now, being in a state of nervous excitement, how are we to expect that she should behave as she might otherwise have behaved to any of the people about her?", "자, 신경 흥분 상태에 있으니, 그녀가 제 둘레의 어느 누구에게든 달리 처신했을 법하게 처신하기를 우리가 어찌 기대할 수 있겠나?"], ["Arguing in this way, from within-outwards, what do we reach?", "이런 식으로, 안에서 바깥으로 따져 나가면, 우리는 무엇에 다다르는가?"], ["We reach the Subjective view.", "우리는 주관적 관점에 다다르지."], ["I defy you to controvert the Subjective view.", "자네가 주관적 관점을 뒤집을 수 있거든 어디 해 보게."], ["Very well then—what follows?", "좋아, 그렇다면—무엇이 뒤따르는가?"], ["Good Heavens!", "맙소사!"], ["the Objective-Subjective explanation follows, of course!", "물론, 객관적-주관적 설명이 뒤따르지!"], ["Rachel, properly speaking, is not Rachel, but Somebody Else.", "레이철은, 엄밀히 말하면, 레이철이 아니라, 다른 어떤 사람이야."], ["Do I mind being cruelly treated by Somebody Else?", "다른 어떤 사람에게 모질게 대접받는 것을 내가 마음에 두겠나?"], ["You are unreasonable enough, Betteredge; but you can hardly accuse me of that.", "자네는 넉넉히 억지스럽네, 베터리지. 허나 자네가 나더러 그렇다고 나무랄 수는 없지."], ["Then how does it end?", "그러면 그것은 어떻게 끝나는가?"], ["It ends, in spite of your confounded English narrowness and prejudice, in my being perfectly happy and comfortable.", "그것은, 자네의 그 지긋지긋한 잉글랜드식 옹졸함과 편견에도 불구하고, 내가 아주 행복하고 편안해지는 것으로 끝난다네."], ["Where’s the sherry?”", "셰리주는 어디 있나?\""]], [["My head was by this time in such a condition, that I was not quite sure whether it was my own head, or Mr. Franklin’s.", "이때쯤 내 머리는, 그것이 내 머리인지 프랭클린 도련님의 머리인지 도무지 확신이 서지 않을 지경이 되었다."], ["In this deplorable state, I contrived to do, what I take to have been, three Objective things.", "이 딱한 처지에서, 나는, 내가 세 가지 객관적인 일이라 여기는 것을 용케 해냈다."], ["I got Mr. Franklin his sherry; I retired to my own room; and I solaced myself with the most composing pipe of tobacco I ever remember to have smoked in my life.", "나는 프랭클린 도련님께 셰리주를 갖다 드렸고, 내 방으로 물러갔고, 그리고 내 평생 피워 본 것 가운데 가장 마음을 가라앉혀 주는 담배 한 대로 내 마음을 달랬다."]], [["Don’t suppose, however, that I was quit of Mr. Franklin on such easy terms as these.", "허나, 내가 이토록 손쉬운 조건으로 프랭클린 도련님에게서 벗어났으리라고는 여기지 마시라."], ["Drifting again, out of the morning-room into the hall, he found his way to the offices next, smelt my pipe, and was instantly reminded that he had been simple enough to give up smoking for Miss Rachel’s sake.", "다시 떠돌아, 아침 방에서 현관으로 나온 그는, 이번에는 하인방 쪽으로 길을 잡아, 내 담배 냄새를 맡고는, 대뜸 저가 레이철 아가씨를 위해 담배를 끊는 어리석음을 부렸었다는 것을 떠올렸다."], ["In the twinkling of an eye, he burst in on me with his cigar-case, and came out strong on the one everlasting subject, in his neat, witty, unbelieving, French way.", "눈 깜짝할 새, 그는 제 여송연갑을 들고 내게 들이닥쳐, 그의 그 말쑥하고, 재치 있고, 곧이듣지 않는, 프랑스식 태도로, 그 하나뿐인 영원한 화제를 힘차게 늘어놓았다."], ["“Give me a light, Betteredge.", "\"불 좀 붙여 주게, 베터리지."], ["Is it conceivable that a man can have smoked as long as I have without discovering that there is a complete system for the treatment of women at the bottom of his cigar-case?", "사내가 나만큼 오래 담배를 피우고서도, 제 여송연갑 밑바탕에 여자를 다루는 완벽한 체계가 들어 있다는 것을 알아채지 못했다는 게 있을 법한 일인가?"], ["Follow me carefully, and I will prove it in two words.", "내 말을 잘 따라오게, 그러면 두 마디로 그것을 증명해 보이지."], ["You choose a cigar, you try it, and it disappoints you.", "자네는 여송연 하나를 고르고, 그것을 피워 보는데, 그것이 자네를 실망시켜."], ["What do you do upon that?", "그러면 자네는 어찌하나?"], ["You throw it away and try another.", "자네는 그것을 던져 버리고 다른 것을 피워 보지."], ["Now observe the application!", "자, 이 적용을 눈여겨보게!"], ["You choose a woman, you try her, and she breaks your heart. Fool!", "자네는 여자 하나를 고르고, 그녀를 시험해 보는데, 그녀가 자네의 가슴을 찢어 놓아."], ["take a lesson from your cigar-case.", "이 어리석은 사람아! 자네 여송연갑에서 교훈을 얻게."], ["Throw her away, and try another!”", "그녀를 던져 버리고, 다른 여자를 시험해 보라고!\""]], [["I shook my head at that.", "나는 그 말에 고개를 저었다."], ["Wonderfully clever, I dare say, but my own experience was dead against it.", "놀랍도록 영리하렷다, 허나 내 나름의 경험은 딱 그와 어긋났다."], ["“In the time of the late Mrs.", "\"돌아가신 베터리지 안사람이 살아 있던 시절에는,\""], ["Betteredge,”", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said, “I felt pretty often inclined to try your philosophy, Mr. Franklin.", "\"저도 도련님의 그 철학을 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 꽤나 자주 들었습지요, 프랭클린 도련님."], ["But the law insists on your smoking your cigar, sir, when you have once chosen it.”", "허나 법이라는 것이, 한번 여송연을 골랐거든, 그것을 끝까지 피우라고 우기지 뭡니까, 도련님.\""], ["I pointed that observation with a wink. Mr.", "나는 그 소견에 한쪽 눈을 찡긋하는 것으로 방점을 찍었다."], ["Franklin burst out laughing—and we were as merry as crickets, until the next new side of his character turned up in due course.", "프랭클린 도련님은 왈칵 웃음을 터뜨렸다—그리고 우리는, 이윽고 그의 됨됨이의 그다음 새 면모가 순서대로 튀어나올 때까지, 귀뚜라미처럼 흥겨웠다."], ["So things went on with my young master and me; and so (while the Sergeant and the gardener were wrangling over the roses) we two spent the interval before the news came back from Frizinghall.", "나의 젊은 주인과 나 사이의 일은 그렇게 흘러갔다. 그리고 그렇게 (부장과 정원사가 장미를 두고 옥신각신하는 동안) 우리 둘은 프리징홀에서 소식이 돌아오기 전의 그 사이를 보냈다."]], [["The pony-chaise returned a good half hour before I had ventured to expect it.", "조랑말 이륜마차는 내가 감히 예상했던 것보다 족히 반 시간은 일찍 돌아왔다."], ["My lady had decided to remain for the present, at her sister’s house.", "마님은 당분간, 언니 되시는 분의 집에 머물기로 정하신 터였다."], ["The groom brought two letters from his mistress; one addressed to Mr. Franklin, and the other to me.", "마부가 제 여주인에게서 편지 두 통을 가져왔는데, 하나는 프랭클린 도련님 앞으로, 다른 하나는 내 앞으로 온 것이었다."]], [["Mr. Franklin’s letter I sent to him in the library—into which refuge his driftings had now taken him for the second time.", "프랭클린 도련님의 편지는 서재로 그에게 보냈다—그의 떠돌이 걸음이 이제 두 번째로 그를 피신시켜 놓은 그 은신처로 말이다."], ["My own letter, I read in my own room.", "내 편지는, 내 방에서 읽었다."], ["A cheque, which dropped out when I opened it, informed me (before I had mastered the contents) that Sergeant Cuff’s dismissal from the inquiry after the Moonstone was now a settled thing.", "그것을 뜯었을 때 툭 떨어진 수표 한 장이, (내가 그 내용을 다 헤아리기도 전에) 월장석을 좇는 조사에서 커프 부장을 해임하는 것이 이제 정해진 일임을 내게 알려 주었다."]], [["I sent to the conservatory to say that I wished to speak to the Sergeant directly.", "나는 온실로 사람을 보내, 부장에게 곧바로 할 말이 있다고 전했다."], ["He appeared, with his mind full of the gardener and the dog-rose, declaring that the equal of Mr. Begbie for obstinacy never had existed yet, and never would exist again.", "그는, 정원사와 들장미로 머릿속이 가득한 채, 베그비 씨만큼 고집스러운 자는 여태 존재한 적도 없고, 앞으로도 결코 존재하지 않으리라고 선언하며 나타났다."], ["I requested him to dismiss such wretched trifling as this from our conversation, and to give his best attention to a really serious matter.", "나는 그에게, 이런 하잘것없는 시시한 소리는 우리 이야기에서 물리치고, 정말로 진지한 문제에 온 정신을 쏟아 달라고 청했다."], ["Upon that he exerted himself sufficiently to notice the letter in my hand.", "그러자 그는, 내 손에 든 편지를 알아챌 만큼은 애를 썼다."], ["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["he said in a weary way, “you have heard from her ladyship.", "하고 그가 지친 투로 말했다. \"마님에게서 기별이 왔구려."], ["Have I anything to do with it, Mr. Betteredge?”", "나와 무슨 상관이 있는 거요, 베터리지 양반?\""]], [["“You shall judge for yourself, Sergeant.”", "\"직접 판단해 보시오, 부장.\""], ["I thereupon read him the letter (with my best emphasis and discretion), in the following words:", "그러고서 나는 그에게 그 편지를 (내 나름 가장 잘 힘주고 신중하게) 다음과 같은 말로 읽어 주었다."]], [["“MY GOOD GABRIEL,—I request that you will inform Sergeant Cuff, that I have performed the promise I made to him; with this result, so far as Rosanna Spearman is concerned.", "\"나의 착한 게이브리얼에게,—커프 부장에게 알려 주기를 부탁하오, 내가 그에게 한 약속을 이행했으며, 로재나 스피어먼에 관한 한 이런 결과가 나왔다고 말이오."], ["Miss Verinder solemnly declares, that she has never spoken a word in private to Rosanna, since that unhappy woman first entered my house.", "버린더 아가씨는, 그 불행한 여자가 처음 내 집에 들어온 이래, 로재나와 단둘이서 단 한마디도 나눈 적이 없다고 엄숙히 단언하오."], ["They never met, even accidentally, on the night when the Diamond was lost; and no communication of any sort whatever took place between them, from the Thursday morning when the alarm was first raised in the house, to this present Saturday afternoon, when Miss Verinder left us.", "그들은, 다이아몬드를 잃은 그 밤에도, 우연히라도 마주친 적이 없소. 그리고 이 집에서 처음 소동이 벌어진 목요일 아침부터, 버린더 아가씨가 우리를 떠난 오늘 이 토요일 오후까지, 그들 사이에는 어떤 종류의 왕래도 전혀 없었소."], ["After telling my daughter suddenly, and in so many words, of Rosanna Spearman’s suicide—this is what has come of it.”", "내 딸에게 별안간, 그 몇 마디로, 로재나 스피어먼의 자살을 말한 뒤에—이런 결과가 나온 것이오.\""]], [["Having reached that point, I looked up, and asked Sergeant Cuff what he thought of the letter, so far?", "거기까지 다다르자, 나는 눈을 들어, 커프 부장에게 여기까지의 편지를 어찌 여기느냐고 물었다."]], [["“I should only offend you if I expressed my opinion,”", "\"내가 내 의견을 밝힌다면 그저 당신을 언짢게 할 뿐이겠지.\""], ["answered the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 대답했다."], ["“Go on, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"계속하시오, 베터리지 양반.\""], ["he said, with the most exasperating resignation, “go on.”", "하고 그가, 더할 나위 없이 부아 돋우는 체념으로 말했다. \"계속하시오.\""]], [["When I remembered that this man had had the audacity to complain of our gardener’s obstinacy, my tongue itched to “go on”", "이 사내가 뻔뻔스럽게도 우리 정원사의 고집을 불평했던 것을 떠올리자, 내 혀는 마님의 말씀 아닌 다른 말로 \"계속하고\""], ["in other words than my mistress’s.", "싶어 근질거렸다."], ["This time, however, my Christianity held firm.", "허나, 이번에는, 나의 기독교 신앙이 굳게 버텨 주었다."], ["I proceeded steadily with her ladyship’s letter:", "나는 마님의 편지를 꾸준히 이어 갔다."]], [["“Having appealed to Miss Verinder in the manner which the officer thought most desirable, I spoke to her next in the manner which I myself thought most likely to impress her.", "\"그 형사가 가장 바람직하다 여긴 방식으로 버린더 아가씨에게 호소한 뒤, 나는 이어서 나 자신이 그 아이에게 가장 감명을 줄 성싶다 여긴 방식으로 그 아이에게 말했소."], ["On two different occasions, before my daughter left my roof, I privately warned her that she was exposing herself to suspicion of the most unendurable and most degrading kind.", "두 차례에 걸쳐, 내 딸이 내 지붕 아래를 떠나기 전에, 나는 그 아이에게, 가장 견딜 수 없고 가장 수치스러운 종류의 의심에 스스로를 내맡기고 있노라고 남몰래 일렀소."], ["I have now told her, in the plainest terms, that my apprehensions have been realised.", "나는 이제 그 아이에게, 더없이 명백한 말로, 나의 우려가 현실이 되었다고 말했소."]], [["“Her answer to this, on her own solemn affirmation, is as plain as words can be.", "\"이에 대한 그 아이의 대답은, 그 아이 자신의 엄숙한 확언으로써, 더할 나위 없이 분명하오."], ["In the first place, she owes no money privately to any living creature.", "첫째로, 그 아이는 살아 있는 그 어떤 이에게도 남몰래 진 빚이 없소."], ["In the second place, the Diamond is not now, and never has been, in her possession, since she put it into her cabinet on Wednesday night.", "둘째로, 다이아몬드는, 그 아이가 수요일 밤 그것을 제 장롱에 넣어 둔 이래, 지금 그 아이가 지니고 있지도 않고, 여태 지닌 적도 없소."]], [["“The confidence which my daughter has placed in me goes no further than this.", "\"내 딸이 내게 준 속내는 이 이상으로 더 나아가지 않소."], ["She maintains an obstinate silence, when I ask her if she can explain the disappearance of the Diamond.", "다이아몬드의 실종을 설명할 수 있느냐고 내가 물으면, 그 아이는 고집스러운 침묵을 지키오."], ["She refuses, with tears, when I appeal to her to speak out for my sake.", "나를 위해 툭 터놓고 말해 달라 내가 호소하면, 그 아이는 눈물을 흘리며 거절하오."], ["‘The day will come when you will know why I am careless about being suspected, and why I am silent even to you.", "'제가 어째서 의심받는 것에 개의치 않는지, 그리고 어째서 엄마에게조차 입을 다무는지, 엄마가 알게 될 날이 올 거예요."], ["I have done much to make my mother pity me—nothing to make my mother blush for me.’ Those are my daughter’s own words.", "저는 제 어머니가 저를 가엾이 여기게 할 일은 많이 했어도—제 어머니가 저 때문에 낯을 붉히게 할 일은 아무것도 하지 않았어요.' 그것이 내 딸 자신의 말이오."]], [["“After what has passed between the officer and me, I think—stranger as he is—that he should be made acquainted with what Miss Verinder has said, as well as you.", "\"그 형사와 나 사이에 오간 일로 미루어, 나는—그가 외부인이긴 하나—그에게도 그대에게처럼 버린더 아가씨가 한 말을 알려 주어야 마땅하다고 생각하오."], ["Read my letter to him, and then place in his hands the cheque which I enclose.", "내 편지를 그에게 읽어 주고, 그런 뒤에 내가 동봉하는 수표를 그의 손에 쥐여 주시오."], ["In resigning all further claim on his services, I have only to say that I am convinced of his honesty and his intelligence; but I am more firmly persuaded than ever, that the circumstances, in this case, have fatally misled him.”", "그의 수고에 대한 이후의 모든 청구를 그만두면서, 나는 다만 이 말만 하겠소, 나는 그의 정직함과 그의 총명함을 확신한다고. 허나 나는 그 어느 때보다 더 굳게 믿게 되었소, 이 사건에서는, 정황이 그를 돌이킬 수 없이 그르쳐 놓았다고 말이오.\""]], [["There the letter ended.", "거기서 편지가 끝났다."], ["Before presenting the cheque, I asked Sergeant Cuff if he had any remark to make.", "수표를 건네기 전에, 나는 커프 부장에게 무슨 할 말이 있느냐고 물었다."]], [["“It’s no part of my duty, Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"사건을 다 끝낸 마당에, 그 사건에 대해 이러쿵저러쿵하는 것은, 베터리지 양반,\""], ["he answered, “to make remarks on a case, when I have done with it.”", "하고 그가 대답했다. \"내 소임의 몫이 아니오.\""]], [["I tossed the cheque across the table to him.", "나는 탁자 너머로 그에게 수표를 툭 던졌다."], ["“Do you believe in that part of her ladyship’s letter?”", "\"마님 편지의 그 대목을 당신은 믿소?\""], ["I said, indignantly.", "하고 내가 분개하여 말했다."]], [["The Sergeant looked at the cheque, and lifted up his dismal eyebrows in acknowledgment of her ladyship’s liberality.", "부장은 수표를 들여다보더니, 마님의 후함을 인정하는 뜻으로 제 침울한 눈썹을 치켜올렸다."]], [["“This is such a generous estimate of the value of my time,”", "\"이것은 내 시간의 값어치를 어찌나 후하게 쳐준 것인지,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “that I feel bound to make some return for it.", "\"나로서는 그에 얼마쯤 보답을 하지 않을 수 없다는 느낌이 드는구려."], ["I’ll bear in mind the amount in this cheque, Mr. Betteredge, when the occasion comes round for remembering it.”", "이 수표의 액수를 마음에 새겨 두겠소, 베터리지 양반, 그것을 떠올릴 때가 돌아오거든 말이오.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“Her ladyship has smoothed matters over for the present very cleverly,”", "\"마님은 당장은 일을 아주 영리하게 얼버무려 놓으셨소.\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말했다."], ["“But this family scandal is of the sort that bursts up again when you least expect it.", "\"허나 이런 집안 추문이라는 것은 당신이 가장 뜻하지 않을 때 다시 터져 나오는 종류의 것이오."], ["We shall have more detective-business on our hands, sir, before the Moonstone is many months older.”", "우리는 손에 더 많은 탐정 일거리를 쥐게 될 거요, 양반, 월장석이 몇 달 더 나이를 먹기 전에 말이오.\""]], [["If those words meant anything, and if the manner in which he spoke them meant anything—it came to this.", "그 말에 무슨 뜻이 있다면, 그리고 그가 그 말을 하는 태도에 무슨 뜻이 있다면—그것은 이런 것이 되었다."], ["My mistress’s letter had proved, to his mind, that Miss Rachel was hardened enough to resist the strongest appeal that could be addressed to her, and that she had deceived her own mother (good God, under what circumstances!) by a series of abominable lies.", "마님의 편지는, 그의 마음속에, 레이철 아가씨가 저에게 던져질 수 있는 가장 강력한 호소에도 버틸 만큼 모질어졌으며, 일련의 얄미운 거짓말로 (아이고 하느님, 어떤 정황 아래서!) 제 친어머니를 속였음을 증명해 주었다는 것이었다."], ["How other people, in my place, might have replied to the Sergeant, I don’t know.", "다른 사람들이라면, 내 처지에서, 그 부장에게 어떻게 대꾸했을지 나는 모른다."], ["I answered what he said in these plain terms:", "나는 그가 한 말에 이런 명백한 말로 대답했다."]], [["“Sergeant Cuff, I consider your last observation as an insult to my lady and her daughter!”", "\"커프 부장, 나는 당신의 그 마지막 소견을 마님과 그 따님에 대한 모욕으로 여기겠소!\""]], [["“Mr. Betteredge, consider it as a warning to yourself, and you will be nearer the mark.”", "\"베터리지 양반, 그것을 당신 자신에 대한 경고로 여긴다면, 당신은 정곡에 더 가까워질 거요.\""]], [["Hot and angry as I was, the infernal confidence with which he gave me that answer closed my lips.", "내가 아무리 달아오르고 노여웠어도, 그가 그 대답을 내놓는 그 지옥 같은 자신감은 내 입을 다물게 했다."]], [["I walked to the window to compose myself.", "나는 마음을 가라앉히려 창가로 걸어갔다."], ["The rain had given over; and, who should I see in the court-yard, but Mr.", "비가 그친 터였다."], ["Begbie, the gardener, waiting outside to continue the dog-rose controversy with Sergeant Cuff.", "그런데 뜰에서 내가 본 것은, 다름 아닌, 커프 부장과 들장미 논쟁을 이어 가려고 밖에서 기다리고 있는 정원사 베그비 씨였다."]], [["“My compliments to the Sairgent,”", "\"부장 나리께 안부 여쭙습니다요.\""], ["said Mr. Begbie, the moment he set eyes on me.", "하고 베그비 씨가, 나를 보자마자 말했다."], ["“If he’s minded to walk to the station, I’m agreeable to go with him.”", "\"나리가 역까지 걸어갈 마음이 있으시다면, 지도 기꺼이 함께 가겠습니다요.\""]], [["“What!”", "\"뭐요!\""], ["cries the Sergeant, behind me, “are you not convinced yet?”", "하고 내 뒤에서 부장이 소리친다. \"당신 아직도 납득이 안 됐소?\""]], [["“The de’il a bit I’m convinced!”", "\"납득은 무슨 얼어 죽을!\""], ["answered Mr. Begbie.", "하고 베그비 씨가 대답했다."]], [["“Then I’ll walk to the station!”", "\"그럼 나는 역까지 걸어가겠소!\""], ["says the Sergeant.", "하고 부장이 말한다."]], [["“Then I’ll meet you at the gate!”", "\"그럼 지는 대문에서 나리를 맞겠습니다요!\""], ["says Mr. Begbie.", "하고 베그비 씨가 말한다."]], [["I was angry enough, as you know—but how was any man’s anger to hold out against such an interruption as this?", "여러분도 아시다시피, 나는 넉넉히 노여웠다—허나 이런 훼방에 어느 누구의 노여움인들 어찌 버텨 낼 수 있었으랴?"], ["Sergeant Cuff noticed the change in me, and encouraged it by a word in season.", "커프 부장은 내 안에 인 변화를 알아채고는, 때맞춘 한마디로 그것을 부추겼다."], ["“Come!", "\"자!"], ["come!”", "자!\""], ["he said, “why not treat my view of the case as her ladyship treats it?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"어째서 이 사건에 대한 내 견해를, 마님이 그것을 대하시듯 대하지 않는 거요?"], ["Why not say, the circumstances have fatally misled me?”", "어째서, 정황이 나를 돌이킬 수 없이 그르쳐 놓았다고 말하지 않는 거요?\""]], [["To take anything as her ladyship took it was a privilege worth enjoying—even with the disadvantage of its having been offered to me by Sergeant Cuff.", "무엇이든 마님이 그것을 대하시듯 대하는 것은 누릴 값어치가 있는 특권이었다—그것이 커프 부장에게서 내게 건네진 것이라는 흠이 있음에도 말이다."], ["I cooled slowly down to my customary level.", "나는 서서히 여느 때 상태로 식어 갔다."], ["I regarded any other opinion of Miss Rachel, than my lady’s opinion or mine, with a lofty contempt.", "나는 레이철 아가씨에 대한, 마님의 견해나 나의 견해 아닌 다른 어떤 견해도, 도도한 경멸로 여겼다."], ["The only thing I could not do, was to keep off the subject of the Moonstone!", "내가 도무지 할 수 없었던 단 한 가지는, 월장석이라는 화제에서 손을 떼는 것이었다!"], ["My own good sense ought to have warned me, I know, to let the matter rest—but, there!", "나도 알거니와, 나의 분별력이 마땅히 나를 일깨워 그 일을 그냥 놓아두게 했어야 했다—허나, 저런!"], ["the virtues which distinguish the present generation were not invented in my time.", "요즘 세대를 돋보이게 하는 그 미덕들은 내 시대에는 아직 발명되지 않았던 것이다."], ["Sergeant Cuff had hit me on the raw, and, though I did look down upon him with contempt, the tender place still tingled for all that.", "커프 부장이 내 생살을 건드려 놓은 터라, 내가 그를 경멸로 내려다보긴 했어도, 그럼에도 그 여린 데는 여전히 얼얼했다."], ["The end of it was that I perversely led him back to the subject of her ladyship’s letter.", "그 끝은, 내가 심술궂게 그를 마님 편지라는 화제로 도로 이끌었다는 것이다. \"나 자신은 아주 흡족하오.\""], ["“I am quite satisfied myself,”", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “But never mind that!", "\"허나 그건 아무래도 좋소!"], ["Go on, as if I was still open to conviction.", "마치 내가 아직 설득에 열려 있기라도 한 듯 계속해 보시오."], ["You think Miss Rachel is not to be believed on her word; and you say we shall hear of the Moonstone again.", "당신은 레이철 아가씨의 말을 곧이들을 수 없다고 여기지. 그리고 당신은 우리가 월장석에 대해 다시 듣게 되리라고 말하고."], ["Back your opinion, Sergeant,”", "당신 견해를 뒷받침해 보시오, 부장.\""], ["I concluded, in an airy way.", "하고 나는, 가벼운 투로 맺었다."], ["“Back your opinion.”", "\"당신 견해를 뒷받침해 보시오.\""]], [["Instead of taking offence, Sergeant Cuff seized my hand, and shook it till my fingers ached again.", "부장은 언짢아하기는커녕, 내 손을 움켜쥐고, 내 손가락이 다시 아파 올 때까지 흔들어 댔다."]], [["“I declare to heaven,”", "\"하늘에 대고 맹세하거니와,\""], ["says this strange officer solemnly, “I would take to domestic service tomorrow, Mr.", "하고 이 별난 형사가 엄숙히 말한다."], ["Betteredge, if I had a chance of being employed along with You!", "\"당신과 함께 부려질 기회만 있다면, 나는 당장 내일이라도 하인살이에 나서겠소, 베터리지 양반!"], ["To say you are as transparent as a child, sir, is to pay the children a compliment which nine out of ten of them don’t deserve.", "당신더러 아이처럼 속이 훤히 들여다보인다고 말하는 것은, 열에 아홉은 그런 찬사를 받을 자격도 없는 아이들에게 찬사를 바치는 셈이오."], ["There!", "저런!"], ["there!", "저런!"], ["we won’t begin to dispute again.", "우리 다시 다투지는 맙시다."], ["You shall have it out of me on easier terms than that.", "그보다 손쉬운 조건으로 내게서 그것을 들려 드리지."], ["I won’t say a word more about her ladyship, or about Miss Verinder—I’ll only turn prophet, for once in a way, and for your sake.", "마님에 대해서도, 버린더 아가씨에 대해서도 더는 한마디도 하지 않겠소—그저, 이번 한 번만, 그리고 당신을 위해, 예언자 노릇을 해 보겠소."], ["I have warned you already that you haven’t done with the Moonstone yet.", "나는 이미 당신에게 당신이 아직 월장석 일을 끝낸 게 아니라고 일러 두었소."], ["Very well.", "아주 좋소."], ["Now I’ll tell you, at parting, of three things which will happen in the future, and which, I believe, will force themselves on your attention, whether you like it or not.”", "이제 나는 헤어지는 마당에, 앞으로 일어날, 그리고 내가 믿기로는, 당신이 좋든 싫든 억지로 당신의 눈길을 끌어당길 세 가지 일을 당신에게 일러 주겠소.\""]], [["“Go on!”", "\"계속해 보시오!\""], ["I said, quite unabashed, and just as airy as ever.", "하고 나는, 조금도 기죽지 않고, 여느 때처럼 가볍게 말했다."]], [["“First,”", "\"첫째,\""], ["said the Sergeant, “you will hear something from the Yollands—when the postman delivers Rosanna’s letter at Cobb’s Hole, on Monday next.”", "하고 부장이 말했다. \"당신은 욜랜드네에서 무슨 소식을 듣게 될 거요—우체부가 다음 월요일에 코브스 홀로 로재나의 편지를 배달할 때 말이오.\""]], [["If he had thrown a bucket of cold water over me, I doubt if I could have felt it much more unpleasantly than I felt those words.", "그가 내게 찬물 한 통을 끼얹었다 한들, 그 말을 느꼈던 것보다 훨씬 더 언짢게 느낄 수 있었을지 의심스럽다."], ["Miss Rachel’s assertion of her innocence had left Rosanna’s conduct—the making the new nightgown, the hiding the smeared nightgown, and all the rest of it—entirely without explanation.", "레이철 아가씨가 제 결백을 단언한 탓에, 로재나의 행동은—새 잠옷을 지은 것, 얼룩진 잠옷을 숨긴 것, 그리고 그 밖의 온갖 것들은—온전히 설명되지 않은 채 남아 있었다."], ["And this had never occurred to me, till Sergeant Cuff forced it on my mind all in a moment!", "그런데 이것이, 커프 부장이 순식간에 그것을 내 마음에 들이밀 때까지, 내게는 한 번도 떠오른 적이 없었던 것이다!"]], [["“In the second place,”", "\"둘째로,\""], ["proceeded the Sergeant, “you will hear of the three Indians again.", "하고 부장이 이어 갔다. \"당신은 그 인도인 셋에 대해 다시 듣게 될 거요."], ["You will hear of them in the neighbourhood, if Miss Rachel remains in the neighbourhood.", "레이철 아가씨가 이 근방에 머무신다면, 당신은 이 근방에서 그들에 대해 듣게 될 거요."], ["You will hear of them in London, if Miss Rachel goes to London.”", "레이철 아가씨가 런던으로 가신다면, 당신은 런던에서 그들에 대해 듣게 될 거고.\""]], [["Having lost all interest in the three jugglers, and having thoroughly convinced myself of my young lady’s innocence, I took this second prophecy easily enough.", "그 세 요술쟁이에 대한 온갖 흥미를 잃어버렸고, 우리 아가씨의 결백을 나 자신에게 철저히 납득시킨 터라, 나는 이 두 번째 예언을 넉넉히 대수롭잖게 받아들였다."], ["“So much for two of the three things that are going to happen,”", "\"일어날 세 가지 일 가운데 두 가지는 그쯤 됐고.\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said. “Now for the third!”", "\"이제 세 번째요!\""]], [["“Third, and last,”", "\"셋째로, 그리고 마지막으로,\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, “you will, sooner or later, hear something of that money-lender in London, whom I have twice taken the liberty of mentioning already.", "하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"당신은, 이르든 늦든, 내가 이미 두 번이나 주제넘게 입에 올린 그 런던의 돈놀이꾼에 대해 무슨 소식을 듣게 될 거요."], ["Give me your pocket-book, and I’ll make a note for you of his name and address—so that there may be no mistake about it if the thing really happens.”", "당신 수첩을 이리 주시오, 그러면 당신을 위해 그자의 이름과 주소를 적어 두겠소—그래야 그 일이 정말로 일어날 때 착오가 없을 테니.\""]], [["He wrote accordingly on a blank leaf—“Mr. Septimus Luker, Middlesex-place, Lambeth, London.”", "그리하여 그는 빈 종잇장에 이렇게 적었다—\"셉티머스 루커 씨, 미들섹스플레이스, 램버스, 런던.\""]], [["“There,”", "\"자,\""], ["he said, pointing to the address, “are the last words, on the subject of the Moonstone, which I shall trouble you with for the present.", "하고 그가, 그 주소를 가리키며 말했다. \"이것이 당분간 월장석이라는 화제에 대해 내가 당신을 성가시게 할 마지막 말이오."], ["Time will show whether I am right or wrong.", "내가 옳은지 그른지는 시간이 보여 줄 거요."], ["In the meanwhile, sir, I carry away with me a sincere personal liking for you, which I think does honour to both of us.", "그러는 동안, 양반, 나는 당신에 대한 진심 어린 개인적 호감을 지니고 떠나오, 그리고 그것이 우리 둘 다에게 명예로운 일이라고 나는 생각하오."], ["If we don’t meet again before my professional retirement takes place, I hope you will come and see me in a little house near London, which I have got my eye on.", "만일 내 직업적 은퇴가 이루어지기 전에 우리가 다시 만나지 못한다면, 부디 내가 점찍어 둔 런던 근교의 조그만 집으로 나를 보러 와 주시오."], ["There will be grass walks, Mr. Betteredge, I promise you, in my garden.", "풀밭 오솔길이 있을 거요, 베터리지 양반, 내 장담하리다, 나의 정원에는 말이오."], ["And as for the white moss rose——”", "그리고 그 흰 이끼장미로 말하자면——\""]], [["“The de’il a bit ye’ll get the white moss rose to grow, unless you bud him on the dogue-rose first,”", "\"흰 이끼장미는, 먼저 들장미에 접붙이지 않고서는, 어림 반 푼어치도 자라게 못 할 겁니다요!\""], ["cried a voice at the window.", "하고 창가에서 한 목소리가 외쳤다."]], [["We both turned round.", "우리는 둘 다 돌아보았다."], ["There was the everlasting Mr. Begbie, too eager for the controversy to wait any longer at the gate.", "거기에는 그 지긋지긋한 베그비 씨가, 논쟁이 하고 싶어 안달이 나 더는 대문에서 기다리지 못하고 서 있었다."], ["The Sergeant wrung my hand, and darted out into the court-yard, hotter still on his side.", "부장은 내 손을 힘껏 쥐었다가, 제 쪽에서 한층 더 달아올라, 뜰로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["“Ask him about the moss rose, when he comes back, and see if I have left him a leg to stand on!”", "\"저 사람이 돌아오거든 이끼장미에 대해 물어보시오, 그리고 내가 저 사람에게 딛고 설 다리 한쪽이라도 남겨 뒀는지 보시오!\""], ["cried the great Cuff, hailing me through the window in his turn.", "하고 그 위대한 커프가, 이번에는 제가 창문 너머로 나를 부르며 외쳤다."], ["“Gentlemen, both!”", "\"두 분 다, 신사분들이오!\""], ["I answered, moderating them again as I had moderated them once already.", "하고 나는, 이미 한 번 그들을 진정시켰던 것처럼 다시 그들을 진정시키며 대답했다."], ["“In the matter of the moss rose there is a great deal to be said on both sides!”", "\"이끼장미 문제에 있어서는 양쪽 다 할 말이 많소이다!\""], ["I might as well (as the Irish say) have whistled jigs to a milestone.", "(아일랜드 사람들 말마따나) 이정표에 대고 지그 가락을 휘파람으로 부는 편이 차라리 나았으리라."], ["Away they went together, fighting the battle of the roses without asking or giving quarter on either side.", "그들은 함께 떠나갔다, 양쪽 어느 편도 봐주기를 청하지도 베풀지도 않으며 장미 전쟁을 벌이면서 말이다."], ["The last I saw of them, Mr. Begbie was shaking his obstinate head, and Sergeant Cuff had got him by the arm like a prisoner in charge.", "내가 그들을 마지막으로 본 모습은, 베그비 씨가 제 고집스러운 머리를 절레절레 흔들고 있고, 커프 부장은 죄인을 붙든 간수처럼 그의 팔을 붙들고 있는 것이었다."], ["Ah, well!", "아, 저런!"], ["well!", "저런!"], ["I own I couldn’t help liking the Sergeant—though I hated him all the time.", "나는 인정하거니와 그 부장을 좋아하지 않을 수가 없었다—내내 그를 미워하면서도 말이다."]], [["Explain that state of mind, if you can.", "할 수 있거든, 그런 마음의 상태를 설명해 보시라."], ["You will soon be rid, now, of me and my contradictions. When I have reported Mr.", "여러분은 이제, 곧, 나와 나의 모순들에게서 벗어나게 될 것이다."], ["Franklin’s departure, the history of the Saturday’s events will be finished at last.", "프랭클린 도련님의 출발을 아뢰고 나면, 그 토요일에 있었던 일들의 기록은 마침내 끝날 것이다."], ["And when I have next described certain strange things that happened in the course of the new week, I shall have done my part of the Story, and shall hand over the pen to the person who is appointed to follow my lead.", "그리고 새로운 한 주 사이에 일어난 어떤 기이한 일들을 그다음에 이야기하고 나면, 나는 이 이야기의 내 몫을 다한 것이 되며, 내 뒤를 잇도록 정해진 사람에게 붓을 넘기게 될 것이다."], ["If you are as tired of reading this narrative as I am of writing it—Lord, how we shall enjoy ourselves on both sides a few pages further on!", "만일 여러분이 이 이야기를 읽는 데에 내가 그것을 쓰는 데에 지친 것만큼 지쳐 있다면—아이고, 몇 쪽 더 나아가면 우리 양쪽 다 얼마나 신이 날 것인가!"]], [["# CHAPTER XXIII", "# CHAPTER XXIII"]], [["I had kept the pony-chaise ready, in case Mr. Franklin persisted in leaving us by the train that night.", "나는, 프랭클린 도련님이 그날 밤 기차로 우리를 떠나겠다는 결심을 굳게 지킬 경우에 대비하여, 조랑말 이륜마차를 대기시켜 두었다."], ["The appearance of the luggage, followed downstairs by Mr. Franklin himself, informed me plainly enough that he had held firm to a resolution for once in his life.", "짐이 나타나고, 그 뒤로 프랭클린 도련님이 몸소 층계를 내려오는 것을 보니, 그가 평생에 단 한 번 어떤 결심을 굳게 지켰음이 넉넉히 뻔했다."]], [["“So you have really made up your mind, sir?”", "\"그럼 정말로 마음을 굳히신 겁니까, 도련님?\""], ["I said, as we met in the hall.", "하고 우리가 현관에서 마주쳤을 때 내가 말했다."], ["“Why not wait a day or two longer, and give Miss Rachel another chance?”", "\"하루 이틀 더 기다리셔서, 레이철 아가씨께 다시 한번 기회를 드리시는 게 어떻습니까?\""]], [["The foreign varnish appeared to have all worn off Mr. Franklin, now that the time had come for saying good-bye.", "이제 작별을 고할 때가 되고 보니, 그 외국물 든 겉치레는 프랭클린 도련님에게서 죄다 벗겨진 듯싶었다."], ["Instead of replying to me in words, he put the letter which her ladyship had addressed to him into my hand.", "그는 내게 말로 대답하는 대신, 마님이 그에게 보내신 편지를 내 손에 쥐여 주었다."], ["The greater part of it said over again what had been said already in the other communication received by me.", "그 대부분은 내게 온 다른 편지에서 이미 이야기된 것을 다시 이야기하고 있었다."], ["But there was a bit about Miss Rachel added at the end, which will account for the steadiness of Mr. Franklin’s determination, if it accounts for nothing else.", "허나 끝에 레이철 아가씨에 대한 대목이 얼마쯤 덧붙어 있었는데, 그것은, 다른 아무것도 설명해 주지 못한다 해도, 프랭클린 도련님의 그 흔들림 없는 결심만은 설명해 줄 것이다."]], [["“You will wonder, I dare say”", "\"자네는 아마 의아해하겠지\""], ["(her ladyship wrote), “at my allowing my own daughter to keep me perfectly in the dark.", "(하고 마님은 쓰셨다) \"내가 내 친딸이 나를 온전히 깜깜한 데 두도록 내버려 두는 것을 말이야."], ["A Diamond worth twenty thousand pounds has been lost—and I am left to infer that the mystery of its disappearance is no mystery to Rachel, and that some incomprehensible obligation of silence has been laid on her, by some person or persons utterly unknown to me, with some object in view at which I cannot even guess.", "이만 파운드 값어치의 다이아몬드가 없어졌는데—나는 그 실종의 수수께끼가 레이철에게는 수수께끼가 아니라는 것, 그리고 내게는 전혀 알려지지 않은 어떤 사람 혹은 사람들에 의해, 내가 짐작조차 할 수 없는 어떤 목적을 위해, 무언가 알 수 없는 침묵의 의무가 그 아이에게 지워졌다는 것을 미루어 헤아릴 수밖에 없는 처지에 남겨졌네."], ["Is it conceivable that I should allow myself to be trifled with in this way?", "내가 이런 식으로 놀림감이 되는 것을 스스로 용납한다는 것이 있을 법한 일인가?"], ["It is quite conceivable, in Rachel’s present state.", "지금 레이철의 상태로 보아서는, 넉넉히 있을 법한 일이지."], ["She is in a condition of nervous agitation pitiable to see.", "그 아이는 보기에 딱한 신경 흥분 상태에 있어."], ["I dare not approach the subject of the Moonstone again until time has done something to quiet her.", "시간이 그 아이를 얼마쯤 가라앉혀 놓을 때까지, 나는 감히 월장석이라는 화제에 다시 다가갈 수가 없네."], ["To help this end, I have not hesitated to dismiss the police-officer.", "이 목적을 돕고자, 나는 서슴없이 그 경찰관을 해임했어."], ["The mystery which baffles us, baffles him too.", "우리를 쩔쩔매게 하는 그 수수께끼는, 그 사람도 쩔쩔매게 하니까."], ["This is not a matter in which any stranger can help us.", "이것은 어떤 외부인도 우리를 도울 수 있는 일이 아니야."], ["He adds to what I have to suffer; and he maddens Rachel if she only hears his name.", "그 사람은 내가 겪어야 할 고통에 짐을 더할 뿐이고, 레이철은 그 사람 이름만 들어도 미쳐 날뛴다네."]], [["“My plans for the future are as well settled as they can be.", "\"앞날에 대한 나의 계획은 정해질 수 있는 만큼은 정해졌네."], ["My present idea is to take Rachel to London—partly to relieve her mind by a complete change, partly to try what may be done by consulting the best medical advice.", "지금 나의 생각은 레이철을 런던으로 데려가는 것이야—한편으로는 온전한 환경의 변화로 그 아이의 마음을 풀어 주려는 것이고, 한편으로는 가장 뛰어난 의료 조언을 구하면 무엇을 할 수 있을지 시험해 보려는 것이지."], ["Can I ask you to meet us in town?", "자네에게 도회지에서 우리를 만나 달라고 청해도 되겠나?"], ["My dear Franklin, you, in your way, must imitate my patience, and wait, as I do, for a fitter time.", "나의 사랑하는 프랭클린, 자네도, 자네 나름의 방식으로, 나의 참을성을 본받아, 나처럼, 더 알맞은 때를 기다려야 하네."], ["The valuable assistance which you rendered to the inquiry after the lost jewel is still an unpardoned offence, in the present dreadful state of Rachel’s mind.", "자네가 그 잃어버린 보석을 좇는 조사에 베푼 그 값진 도움은, 지금 레이철의 이 끔찍한 마음 상태에서는, 여전히 용서받지 못한 죄야."], ["Moving blindfold in this matter, you have added to the burden of anxiety which she has had to bear, by innocently threatening her secret with discovery, through your exertions.", "이 일에서 눈을 가린 채 움직이면서, 자네는, 그 애씀을 통해, 순진하게도 그 아이의 비밀을 발각의 위협에 빠뜨림으로써, 그 아이가 짊어져야 했던 근심의 짐을 더해 놓았네."], ["It is impossible for me to excuse the perversity that holds you responsible for consequences which neither you nor I could imagine or foresee.", "자네도 나도 상상하거나 내다볼 수 없었던 결과에 대해 자네에게 책임을 지우는 그 억지를, 나로서는 도무지 두둔할 수가 없어."], ["She is not to be reasoned with—she can only be pitied.", "그 아이는 사리로 타이를 수 있는 상태가 아니야—그저 가엾이 여길 수 있을 뿐이지."], ["I am grieved to have to say it, but for the present, you and Rachel are better apart.", "이런 말을 해야 하는 것이 서글프네만, 당분간은, 자네와 레이철은 떨어져 있는 편이 나아."], ["The only advice I can offer you is, to give her time.”", "내가 자네에게 건넬 수 있는 유일한 조언은, 그 아이에게 시간을 주라는 것이야.\""]], [["I handed the letter back, sincerely sorry for Mr.", "나는 프랭클린 도련님이 진심으로 딱해서, 그 편지를 도로 건넸다."], ["Franklin, for I knew how fond he was of my young lady; and I saw that her mother’s account of her had cut him to the heart.", "나는 그가 우리 아가씨를 얼마나 아끼는지 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 그 어머니가 아가씨에 대해 전한 그 사연이 그의 가슴을 도려냈음을 보았다."], ["“You know the proverb, sir,”", "\"속담을 아시지요, 도련님.\""], ["was all I said to him.", "하는 것이 내가 그에게 한 말의 전부였다."], ["“When things are at the worst, they’re sure to mend.", "\"일이 가장 나쁠 때는, 반드시 나아지게 마련이라잖습니까."], ["Things can’t be much worse, Mr. Franklin, than they are now.”", "일이 지금보다 훨씬 더 나빠질 수야 없겠지요, 프랭클린 도련님.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin folded up his aunt’s letter, without appearing to be much comforted by the remark which I had ventured on addressing to him.", "프랭클린 도련님은, 내가 감히 그에게 건네 본 그 소견에 별로 위로를 받은 기색도 없이, 이모님의 편지를 접었다."]], [["“When I came here from London with that horrible Diamond,”", "\"내가 그 끔찍한 다이아몬드를 가지고 런던에서 이곳으로 왔을 때,\""], ["he said, “I don’t believe there was a happier household in England than this.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"잉글랜드에 이 집보다 더 행복한 가정은 없었으리라 나는 믿네."], ["Look at the household now!", "지금 이 가정을 보게!"], ["Scattered, disunited—the very air of the place poisoned with mystery and suspicion!", "뿔뿔이 흩어지고, 갈라져서—이곳의 공기마저 수수께끼와 의심으로 독이 올랐어!"], ["Do you remember that morning at the Shivering Sand, when we talked about my uncle Herncastle, and his birthday gift?", "우리가 흔들리는 모래에서, 내 외숙부 헌캐슬과, 그의 생일 선물에 대해 이야기하던 그날 아침을 기억하나?"], ["The Moonstone has served the Colonel’s vengeance, Betteredge, by means which the Colonel himself never dreamt of!”", "월장석은 대령 자신도 결코 꿈에도 그리지 못한 방식으로, 대령의 복수를 이루어 주었네, 베터리지!\""]], [["With that he shook me by the hand, and went out to the pony-chaise.", "그 말과 함께 그는 내 손을 잡고 흔들고는, 조랑말 이륜마차로 나갔다."]], [["I followed him down the steps.", "나는 층계를 내려 그를 따라갔다."], ["It was very miserable to see him leaving the old place, where he had spent the happiest years of his life, in this way.", "그가 제 인생의 가장 행복한 시절을 보낸 그 오래된 집을, 이런 식으로 떠나는 것을 보는 것은 몹시 서글펐다."], ["Penelope (sadly upset by all that had happened in the house) came round crying, to bid him good-bye.", "페넬로피가 (이 집에서 벌어진 온갖 일로 몹시 심란해져서) 그에게 작별을 고하려고 울면서 돌아 나왔다."], ["Mr. Franklin kissed her.", "프랭클린 도련님은 그 아이에게 입을 맞추었다."], ["I waved my hand as much as to say, “You’re heartily welcome, sir.”", "나는 \"진심으로 반가운 인사이옵니다, 도련님.\" 하고 말하기라도 하듯 손을 흔들었다."], ["Some of the other female servants appeared, peeping after him round the corner.", "다른 하녀들 몇몇이 나타나, 모퉁이를 돌아 그를 배웅하며 엿보았다."], ["He was one of those men whom the women all like.", "그는 여자들이 모두 좋아하는 그런 사내들 가운데 하나였다."], ["At the last moment, I stopped the pony-chaise, and begged as a favour that he would let us hear from him by letter.", "마지막 순간에, 나는 조랑말 이륜마차를 멈춰 세우고, 편지로나마 그의 소식을 듣게 해 달라고 청을 넣었다."], ["He didn’t seem to heed what I said—he was looking round from one thing to another, taking a sort of farewell of the old house and grounds.", "그는 내 말을 귀담아듣는 기색이 아니었다—그는 이것저것 둘러보며, 그 오래된 집과 뜰에 일종의 작별을 고하고 있었다."], ["“Tell us where you are going to, sir!”", "\"어디로 가시는지 저희에게 말씀해 주십시오, 도련님!\""], ["I said, holding on by the chaise, and trying to get at his future plans in that way.", "하고 나는, 이륜마차를 붙든 채, 그런 식으로나마 그의 앞날 계획을 캐내 보려 애쓰며 말했다."], ["Mr. Franklin pulled his hat down suddenly over his eyes.", "프랭클린 도련님은 별안간 제 모자를 눈 위로 푹 눌러 내렸다."], ["“Going?”", "\"가느냐고?\""], ["says he, echoing the word after me.", "하고 그가, 내 말을 따라 되뇌며 말한다."], ["“I am going to the devil!”", "\"나는 지옥으로 가네!\""], ["The pony started at the word, as if he had felt a Christian horror of it.", "조랑말이 그 말에 흠칫했다, 마치 그 말에 기독교인다운 공포를 느끼기라도 한 듯이."], ["“God bless you, sir, go where you may!”", "\"하느님께서 축복하시기를, 도련님, 어디로 가시든지요!\""], ["was all I had time to say, before he was out of sight and hearing.", "하는 것이, 그가 눈에서도 귀에서도 사라지기 전에 내가 할 수 있었던 말의 전부였다."], ["A sweet and pleasant gentleman!", "다정하고 유쾌한 신사분!"], ["With all his faults and follies, a sweet and pleasant gentleman!", "그 온갖 흠과 어리석음에도 불구하고, 다정하고 유쾌한 신사분!"], ["He left a sad gap behind him, when he left my lady’s house.", "그는 마님의 집을 떠나면서, 뒤에 서글픈 빈자리를 남겼다."]], [["It was dull and dreary enough, when the long summer evening closed in, on that Saturday night.", "그 토요일 밤, 긴 여름 저녁이 저물어 들 무렵에는, 넉넉히 따분하고 을씨년스러웠다."]], [["I kept my spirits from sinking by sticking fast to my pipe and my Robinson Crusoe.", "나는 내 담뱃대와 내 「로빈슨 크루소」에 바싹 붙어 있음으로써 내 기운이 처지지 않게 붙들었다."], ["The women (excepting Penelope) beguiled the time by talking of Rosanna’s suicide.", "(페넬로피만 빼고) 여자들은 로재나의 자살에 대해 이야기하며 시간을 보냈다."], ["They were all obstinately of opinion that the poor girl had stolen the Moonstone, and that she had destroyed herself in terror of being found out.", "그들은 하나같이, 그 가엾은 아이가 월장석을 훔쳤으며, 발각될까 두려워 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 의견을 고집스럽게 붙들고 있었다."], ["My daughter, of course, privately held fast to what she had said all along.", "내 딸은, 물론, 저 혼자 내내 해 온 말을 굳게 지켰다."], ["Her notion of the motive which was really at the bottom of the suicide failed, oddly enough, just where my young lady’s assertion of her innocence failed also.", "그 자살의 정말 밑바탕에 있던 동기에 대한 그 아이의 생각은, 참으로 묘하게도, 우리 아가씨가 제 결백을 단언한 그것이 어그러지는 바로 그 지점에서 똑같이 어그러졌다."], ["It left Rosanna’s secret journey to Frizinghall, and Rosanna’s proceedings in the matter of the nightgown entirely unaccounted for.", "그것은 로재나가 프리징홀로 남몰래 다녀온 일과, 잠옷 문제에 있어서의 로재나의 행적을 온전히 설명되지 않은 채로 남겨 두었다."], ["There was no use in pointing this out to Penelope; the objection made about as much impression on her as a shower of rain on a waterproof coat.", "페넬로피에게 이것을 짚어 주어 봐야 소용이 없었다. 그 반론은 방수 외투에 소나기 한 줄기가 미치는 만큼의 감명밖에 그 아이에게 주지 못했다."], ["The truth is, my daughter inherits my superiority to reason—and, in respect to that accomplishment, has got a long way ahead of her own father.", "사실인즉, 내 딸은 이치를 넘어서는 나의 기질을 물려받았다—그리고, 그 재간에 있어서는, 제 친아비를 한참 앞질러 있다."]], [["On the next day (Sunday), the close carriage, which had been kept at Mr. Ablewhite’s, came back to us empty.", "그 이튿날 (일요일), 에이블화이트 씨 댁에 두었던 지붕 있는 마차가 우리에게로 텅 빈 채 돌아왔다."], ["The coachman brought a message for me, and written instructions for my lady’s own maid and for Penelope.", "마부가 나에게 전할 말과, 마님의 몸종과 페넬로피에게 줄 문서로 된 지시를 가져왔다."]], [["The message informed me that my mistress had determined to take Miss Rachel to her house in London, on the Monday.", "그 전언은, 마님이 월요일에 레이철 아가씨를 런던에 있는 당신 집으로 데려가기로 정하셨음을 내게 알려 주었다."], ["The written instructions informed the two maids of the clothing that was wanted, and directed them to meet their mistresses in town at a given hour.", "그 문서로 된 지시는, 두 하녀에게 어떤 옷가지가 필요한지를 알리고, 정해진 시각에 도회지에서 제 여주인들을 만나라고 일러 두었다."], ["Most of the other servants were to follow.", "다른 하인들도 대부분 뒤따르기로 되어 있었다."], ["My lady had found Miss Rachel so unwilling to return to the house, after what had happened in it, that she had decided on going to London direct from Frizinghall.", "마님은, 이 집에서 그런 일이 벌어진 뒤로, 레이철 아가씨가 이 집으로 돌아오기를 어찌나 꺼리는지를 아시고, 프리징홀에서 곧장 런던으로 가기로 정하신 터였다."], ["I was to remain in the country, until further orders, to look after things indoors and out.", "나는, 안팎의 일들을 돌보기 위해, 다음 분부가 있을 때까지 시골에 남아 있기로 되어 있었다."], ["The servants left with me were to be put on board wages.", "나와 함께 남은 하인들은 식대만 받는 형편에 놓이게 되었다."]], [["Being reminded, by all this, of what Mr. Franklin had said about our being a scattered and disunited household, my mind was led naturally to Mr. Franklin himself. The more I thought of him, the more uneasy I felt about his future proceedings. It ended in my writing, by the Sunday’s post, to his father’s valet, Mr. Jeffco (whom I had known in former years) to beg he would let me know what Mr. Franklin had settled to do, on arriving in London.", "이 모든 것으로 인해, 우리가 뿔뿔이 흩어지고 갈라진 가정이 되었다던 프랭클린 도련님의 말이 떠오르자, 내 마음은 자연히 프랭클린 도련님 그 자신에게로 이끌렸다. 그를 생각하면 할수록, 나는 그의 앞날 행보에 대해 더욱 마음이 편치 않았다. 그것은, 내가 일요일 우편으로, 그의 아버지의 시종인 제프코 씨에게 (내가 지난날 알고 지내던 사람이다) 편지를 써서, 프랭클린 도련님이 런던에 다다르거든 무엇을 하기로 정했는지 알려 달라고 청하는 것으로 끝났다."]], [["The Sunday evening was, if possible, duller even than the Saturday evening.", "일요일 저녁은, 그럴 수만 있다면, 토요일 저녁보다도 더 따분했다."], ["We ended the day of rest, as hundreds of thousands of people end it regularly, once a week, in these islands—that is to say, we all anticipated bedtime, and fell asleep in our chairs.", "우리는 이 섬나라에서 수십만 사람이 일주일에 한 번씩 어김없이 안식일을 끝맺듯, 그렇게 그 쉬는 날을 끝맺었다—다시 말해, 우리는 모두 잠자리에 들 때를 앞질러, 제 의자에 앉은 채 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["How the Monday affected the rest of the household I don’t know.", "월요일이 나머지 식구들에게 어떤 영향을 미쳤는지 나는 모른다."], ["The Monday gave me a good shake up.", "월요일은 나를 단단히 뒤흔들어 놓았다."], ["The first of Sergeant Cuff’s prophecies of what was to happen—namely, that I should hear from the Yollands—came true on that day.", "앞으로 일어날 일에 대한 커프 부장의 예언들 가운데 첫 번째—곧, 내가 욜랜드네에서 소식을 듣게 되리라는 것이—그날 들어맞았다."]], [["I had seen Penelope and my lady’s maid off in the railway with the luggage for London, and was pottering about the grounds, when I heard my name called.", "나는 페넬로피와 마님의 몸종을 런던행 짐과 함께 기차로 떠나보내고, 뜰을 어정거리고 있었는데, 그때 내 이름을 부르는 소리가 들렸다."], ["Turning round, I found myself face to face with the fisherman’s daughter, Limping Lucy.", "돌아보니, 나는 그 어부의 딸, 절름발이 루시와 얼굴을 마주하고 있었다."], ["Bating her lame foot and her leanness (this last a horrid draw-back to a woman, in my opinion), the girl had some pleasing qualities in the eye of a man.", "그 아이의 저는 발과 야윈 몸(이 뒤엣것은, 내 생각에는, 여자에게 끔찍한 흠이다)을 빼면, 그 아이는 사내의 눈에 들 만한 몇 가지 어여쁜 데가 있었다."], ["A dark, keen, clever face, and a nice clear voice, and a beautiful brown head of hair counted among her merits.", "가무잡잡하고, 예리하고, 영리한 얼굴과, 곱고 맑은 목소리와, 아름다운 갈색 머릿결이 그 아이의 미덕에 꼽혔다."], ["A crutch appeared in the list of her misfortunes.", "목발이 그 아이의 불운 목록에 나타났다."], ["And a temper reckoned high in the sum total of her defects.", "그리고 성깔이 그 아이의 결점 총합에서 큰 몫을 차지했다."]], [["“Well, my dear,”", "\"그래, 얘야,\""], ["I said, “what do you want with me?”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"내게 무슨 볼일이냐?\""]], [["“Where’s the man you call Franklin Blake?”", "\"당신들이 프랭클린 블레이크라고 부르는 그 사내는 어디 있어요?\""], ["says the girl, fixing me with a fierce look, as she rested herself on her crutch.", "하고 그 아이가, 목발에 몸을 기댄 채, 사나운 눈길로 나를 쏘아보며 말한다."]], [["“That’s not a respectful way to speak of any gentleman,”", "\"어느 신사분을 두고 그리 말하는 것은 예의 바른 말투가 아니다.\""], ["I answered.", "하고 내가 대답했다."], ["“If you wish to inquire for my lady’s nephew, you will please to mention him as Mr. Franklin Blake.”", "\"마님의 조카분에 대해 알고 싶거든, 그분을 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이라 일컫는 것이 좋을 게다.\""]], [["She limped a step nearer to me, and looked as if she could have eaten me alive.", "그 아이가 내게로 절뚝절뚝 한 걸음 다가서더니, 나를 산 채로 잡아먹을 듯한 낯빛을 지었다."], ["“Mr. Franklin Blake?”", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 도련님이라고요?\""], ["she repeated after me.", "하고 그 아이가 내 말을 되뇌었다."], ["“Murderer Franklin Blake would be a fitter name for him.”", "\"살인자 프랭클린 블레이크가 그자에게 더 알맞은 이름이겠죠.\""]], [["My practice with the late Mrs. Betteredge came in handy here.", "돌아가신 베터리지 안사람과 겪은 내 경험이 여기서 요긴하게 쓰였다."], ["Whenever a woman tries to put you out of temper, turn the tables, and put her out of temper instead.", "여자가 여러분의 성미를 돋우려 들 때는, 언제든지 판을 뒤집어, 대신 그 여자의 성미를 돋우어 놓으시라."], ["They are generally prepared for every effort you can make in your own defence, but that.", "그들은 대체로 여러분이 제 방어를 위해 할 수 있는 온갖 애씀에는 대비가 되어 있지만, 그것만은 아니다."], ["One word does it as well as a hundred; and one word did it with Limping Lucy.", "한 마디가 백 마디만큼이나 효험을 낸다. 그리고 절름발이 루시에게는 한 마디가 효험을 냈다."], ["I looked her pleasantly in the face; and I said—“Pooh!”", "나는 그 아이의 얼굴을 유쾌하게 들여다보았다. 그리고 나는 말했다—\"흥!\""]], [["The girl’s temper flamed out directly.", "그 아이의 성깔이 대뜸 활활 타올랐다."], ["She poised herself on her sound foot, and she took her crutch, and beat it furiously three times on the ground.", "그 아이는 제 성한 발로 몸을 가누고, 제 목발을 들어, 땅바닥을 세 번 사납게 내리쳤다."], ["“He’s a murderer!", "\"저자는 살인자예요!"], ["he’s a murderer!", "저자는 살인자예요!"], ["he’s a murderer!", "저자는 살인자라고요!"], ["He has been the death of Rosanna Spearman!”", "저자가 로재나 스피어먼을 죽인 거예요!\""], ["She screamed that answer out at the top of her voice.", "그 아이는 그 대답을 목청껏 소리 질러 댔다."], ["One or two of the people at work in the grounds near us looked up—saw it was Limping Lucy—knew what to expect from that quarter—and looked away again.", "우리 가까이 뜰에서 일하던 사람 한둘이 고개를 들어—그것이 절름발이 루시임을 보고는—저쪽에서 무엇을 예상해야 할지 알아채고는—다시 눈길을 돌렸다."]], [["“He has been the death of Rosanna Spearman?”", "\"저 도련님이 로재나 스피어먼을 죽였다고?\""], ["I repeated.", "하고 내가 되뇌었다."], ["“What makes you say that, Lucy?”", "\"무엇 때문에 그런 말을 하느냐, 루시?\""]], [["“What do you care?", "\"당신이 무슨 상관이에요?"], ["What does any man care?", "사내가 무슨 상관이냐고요?"], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["if she had only thought of the men as I think, she might have been living now!”", "그 애가 나처럼 사내들을 생각하기만 했어도, 지금 살아 있었을지 몰라요!\""]], [["“She always thought kindly of me, poor soul,”", "\"그 아이는 늘 나를 다정하게 생각해 주었단다, 가엾은 것.\""], ["I said; “and, to the best of my ability, I always tried to act kindly by her.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"그리고, 내 힘닿는 한, 나는 늘 그 아이에게 다정하게 대하려 애썼고.\""]], [["I spoke those words in as comforting a manner as I could.", "나는 그 말을 될 수 있는 대로 위로가 되게끔 뱉었다."], ["The truth is, I hadn’t the heart to irritate the girl by another of my smart replies.", "사실인즉, 나는 또 한 번 내 약삭빠른 대꾸로 그 아이를 성나게 할 마음이 없었다."], ["I had only noticed her temper at first.", "처음에 나는 그 아이의 성깔만을 알아챘었다."], ["I noticed her wretchedness now—and wretchedness is not uncommonly insolent, you will find, in humble life.", "이제 나는 그 아이의 비참함을 알아챘다—그리고 비참함이라는 것은, 여러분도 아시게 되겠지만, 미천한 살림에서는 드물지 않게 무례한 법이다."], ["My answer melted Limping Lucy.", "나의 대답이 절름발이 루시를 누그러뜨렸다."], ["She bent her head down, and laid it on the top of her crutch.", "그 아이는 고개를 숙여, 그것을 제 목발 꼭대기에 얹었다."]], [["“I loved her,” the girl said softly.", "\"저는 그 애를 사랑했어요.\""], ["“She had lived a miserable life, Mr.", "하고 그 아이가 나직이 말했다."], ["Betteredge—vile people had ill-treated her and led her wrong—and it hadn’t spoiled her sweet temper.", "\"그 애는 비참한 삶을 살았어요, 베터리지 아저씨—몹쓸 사람들이 그 애를 학대하고 그릇된 길로 이끌었지만—그것이 그 애의 다정한 성품을 망쳐 놓지는 못했어요."], ["She was an angel.", "그 애는 천사였어요."], ["She might have been happy with me.", "그 애는 저와 함께 행복할 수도 있었어요."], ["I had a plan for our going to London together like sisters, and living by our needles.", "저는 우리 둘이 자매처럼 함께 런던으로 가서, 바느질로 먹고살 계획을 세웠었죠."], ["That man came here, and spoilt it all.", "그 사내가 이곳에 와서, 그것을 죄다 망쳐 놓았어요."], ["He bewitched her.", "그자가 그 애를 홀렸어요."], ["Don’t tell me he didn’t mean it, and didn’t know it.", "그자가 그럴 뜻이 없었다느니, 몰랐다느니 하는 말은 마세요."], ["He ought to have known it.", "그자는 마땅히 알았어야 했어요."], ["He ought to have taken pity on her.", "그자는 마땅히 그 애를 가엾이 여겼어야 했어요."], ["‘I can’t live without him—and, oh, Lucy, he never even looks at me.’ That’s what she said.", "'그이 없이는 살 수가 없어—그런데, 아, 루시, 그이는 나를 쳐다보지도 않아.' 그 애가 한 말이 그거예요."], ["Cruel, cruel, cruel.", "잔인해요, 잔인해요, 잔인해요."], ["I said, ‘No man is worth fretting for in that way.’ And she said, ‘There are men worth dying for, Lucy, and he is one of them.’ I had saved up a little money.", "저는 말했죠, '그런 식으로 애태울 값어치가 있는 사내는 없어.' 그러니까 그 애가 말했어요, '목숨을 바칠 값어치가 있는 사내들도 있어, 루시, 그리고 그이가 그런 사람이야.'"], ["I had settled things with father and mother.", "저는 돈을 조금 모아 두었었어요. 저는 아버지 어머니와 이야기를 매듭지어 놓았고요."], ["I meant to take her away from the mortification she was suffering here.", "저는 그 애를 이곳에서 겪고 있던 그 굴욕에서 데리고 떠날 작정이었어요."], ["We should have had a little lodging in London, and lived together like sisters.", "우리는 런던에 조그만 셋방을 얻어, 자매처럼 함께 살았을 거예요."], ["She had a good education, sir, as you know, and she wrote a good hand.", "그 애는, 아저씨도 아시다시피, 배움이 넉넉했고, 글씨도 잘 썼어요."], ["She was quick at her needle.", "바느질도 잽쌌고요."], ["I have a good education, and I write a good hand.", "저도 배움이 넉넉하고, 글씨도 잘 써요."], ["I am not as quick at my needle as she was—but I could have done.", "저는 그 애만큼 바느질이 잽싸지는 못해도—그럭저럭 해낼 수 있었을 거예요."], ["We might have got our living nicely.", "우리는 그런대로 먹고살 수 있었을 거예요."], ["And, oh!", "그런데, 아!"], ["what happens this morning?", "오늘 아침에 무슨 일이 일어났나요?"], ["what happens this morning?", "오늘 아침에 무슨 일이 일어났느냐고요?"], ["Her letter comes and tells me that she has done with the burden of her life.", "그 애의 편지가 와서 제게 이르기를, 그 애가 제 삶의 짐을 끝냈다는 거예요."], ["Her letter comes, and bids me good-bye for ever.", "그 애의 편지가 와서, 제게 영영 작별을 고하는 거예요."], ["Where is he?”", "그자는 어디 있죠?\""], ["cries the girl, lifting her head from the crutch, and flaming out again through her tears.", "하고 그 아이가, 목발에서 고개를 들어, 눈물 사이로 다시 활활 타오르며 외친다."], ["“Where’s this gentleman that I mustn’t speak of, except with respect? Ha, Mr.", "\"제가 예의를 갖추지 않고서는 입에 올려서도 안 된다는 그 신사분은 어디 있느냐고요?"], ["Betteredge, the day is not far off when the poor will rise against the rich.", "하, 베터리지 아저씨, 가난한 자들이 부유한 자들에게 들고일어날 날이 머지않았어요."], ["I pray Heaven they may begin with him.", "부디 하늘이 그자부터 시작하게 하시기를."], ["I pray Heaven they may begin with him.”", "부디 하늘이 그자부터 시작하게 하시기를.\""]], [["Here was another of your average good Christians, and here was the usual break-down, consequent on that same average Christianity being pushed too far!", "여기 또 하나의 여느 만큼 착한 기독교인이 있었고, 여기 바로 그 여느 만큼의 기독교 신앙이 너무 몰아붙여진 데서 비롯된 그 흔한 무너짐이 있었다!"], ["The parson himself (though I own this is saying a great deal) could hardly have lectured the girl in the state she was in now.", "목사님 자신도 (이렇게 말하는 것이 대단한 말임을 나도 인정하지만) 지금 그 아이가 놓인 상태로는 그 아이를 좀처럼 타이를 수 없었으리라."], ["All I ventured to do was to keep her to the point—in the hope of something turning up which might be worth hearing.", "내가 감히 할 수 있었던 것이라곤, 무언가 들을 값어치가 있는 것이 튀어나오리라는 바람으로, 그 아이를 요점에 붙들어 두는 것뿐이었다."]], [["“What do you want with Mr.", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 무슨 볼일이냐?\""], ["Franklin Blake?” I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“I want to see him.”", "\"그자를 만나고 싶어요.\""]], [["“For anything particular?”", "\"무슨 특별한 일로?\""]], [["“I have got a letter to give him.”", "\"그자에게 건넬 편지가 있어요.\""]], [["“From Rosanna Spearman?”", "\"로재나 스피어먼에게서 온?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Sent to you in your own letter?”", "\"네 편지에 함께 부쳐 온?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["Was the darkness going to lift?", "어둠이 걷히려는 것인가?"], ["Were all the discoveries that I was dying to make, coming and offering themselves to me of their own accord?", "내가 몹시도 알아내고 싶던 그 온갖 것들이, 제 발로 다가와 내게 제 몸을 내맡기려는 것인가?"], ["I was obliged to wait a moment.", "나는 잠시 뜸을 들여야 했다."], ["Sergeant Cuff had left his infection behind him.", "커프 부장이 제 병균을 뒤에 남겨 놓고 간 것이다."], ["Certain signs and tokens, personal to myself, warned me that the detective-fever was beginning to set in again.", "나 자신에게만 나타나는 어떤 조짐과 낌새가, 탐정 열병이 다시 도지기 시작했음을 내게 일러 주었다."]], [["“You can’t see Mr.", "\"프랭클린 도련님은 만날 수 없다.\""], ["Franklin,” I said.", "하고 내가 말했다."]], [["“I must, and will, see him.”", "\"저는 반드시, 기어이, 그자를 만날 거예요.\""]], [["“He went to London last night.”", "\"그분은 어젯밤 런던으로 가셨다.\""]], [["Limping Lucy looked me hard in the face, and saw that I was speaking the truth.", "절름발이 루시가 내 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보더니, 내가 진실을 말하고 있음을 알아챘다."], ["Without a word more, she turned about again instantly towards Cobb’s Hole.", "더 말 한마디 없이, 그 아이는 대뜸 다시 코브스 홀 쪽으로 돌아섰다."]], [["“Stop!” I said.", "\"멈춰라!\""], ["“I expect news of Mr.", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["Franklin Blake tomorrow.", "\"내일 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님의 소식을 받게 될 게다."], ["Give me your letter, and I’ll send it on to him by the post.”", "네 편지를 내게 주면, 우편으로 그분께 부쳐 주마.\""]], [["Limping Lucy steadied herself on her crutch and looked back at me over her shoulder.", "절름발이 루시가 제 목발에 몸을 가누고, 어깨 너머로 나를 돌아보았다."]], [["“I am to give it from my hands into his hands,”", "\"저는 이걸 제 손에서 그자의 손으로 직접 건네야 해요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["she said. “And I am to give it to him in no other way.”", "\"그리고 저는 이걸 그자에게 다른 어떤 식으로도 건네지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Shall I write, and tell him what you have said?”", "\"내가 편지를 써서, 네가 한 말을 그분께 전해 줄까?\""]], [["“Tell him I hate him.", "\"제가 그자를 미워한다고 전해요."], ["And you will tell him the truth.”", "그럼 당신은 진실을 전하는 거예요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes.", "\"그래, 그래."], ["But about the letter——?”", "그런데 편지 말이다만——?\""]], [["“If he wants the letter, he must come back here, and get it from Me.”", "\"그자가 편지를 원하거든, 이곳으로 돌아와서, 저에게서 받아 가야 해요.\""]], [["With those words she limped off on the way to Cobb’s Hole.", "그 말과 함께 그 아이는 코브스 홀로 가는 길로 절뚝절뚝 떠나갔다."], ["The detective-fever burnt up all my dignity on the spot.", "탐정 열병이 그 자리에서 내 온갖 위신을 태워 버렸다."], ["I followed her, and tried to make her talk.", "나는 그 아이를 뒤따라가, 그 아이를 말하게 하려 애썼다."], ["All in vain.", "죄다 헛일이었다."], ["It was my misfortune to be a man—and Limping Lucy enjoyed disappointing me.", "사내라는 것이 내 불운이었다—그리고 절름발이 루시는 나를 실망시키는 것을 즐겼다."], ["Later in the day, I tried my luck with her mother.", "그날 늦게, 나는 그 아이의 어머니에게 운을 걸어 보았다."], ["Good Mrs. Yolland could only cry, and recommend a drop of comfort out of the Dutch bottle.", "착한 욜랜드 부인은 그저 울면서, 그 네덜란드 병에서 나오는 위안 한 방울을 권할 뿐이었다."], ["I found the fisherman on the beach.", "나는 그 어부를 바닷가에서 찾아냈다."], ["He said it was “a bad job,” and went on mending his net.", "그는 \"고약한 일\"이라고 말하고는, 제 그물을 계속 손질했다."], ["Neither father nor mother knew more than I knew.", "아비도 어미도 나보다 더 아는 것이 없었다."], ["The one way left to try was the chance, which might come with the morning, of writing to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "시험해 볼 남은 한 가지 길은, 아침과 함께 올지도 모를 요행—곧, 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님께 편지를 쓰는 것이었다."]], [["I leave you to imagine how I watched for the postman on Tuesday morning.", "내가 화요일 아침에 우체부를 어떻게 지켜보았을지는 여러분의 상상에 맡기겠다."], ["He brought me two letters.", "그는 내게 편지 두 통을 가져왔다."], ["One, from Penelope (which I had hardly patience enough to read), announced that my lady and Miss Rachel were safely established in London.", "하나는, 페넬로피에게서 온 것으로 (나는 그것을 읽어 낼 참을성도 거의 없었다), 마님과 레이철 아가씨가 런던에 무사히 자리 잡았음을 알렸다."], ["The other, from Mr. Jeffco, informed me that his master’s son had left England already.", "다른 하나는, 제프코 씨에게서 온 것으로, 그 주인의 아드님이 이미 잉글랜드를 떠났음을 내게 알려 주었다."]], [["On reaching the metropolis, Mr. Franklin had, it appeared, gone straight to his father’s residence.", "그 대도회지에 다다르자, 프랭클린 도련님은, 보아하니, 곧장 제 아버지의 저택으로 갔다."], ["He arrived at an awkward time. Mr.", "그는 곤란한 때에 다다랐다."], ["Blake, the elder, was up to his eyes in the business of the House of Commons, and was amusing himself at home that night with the favourite parliamentary plaything which they call “a private bill.” Mr.", "아버지 되는 블레이크 씨는 하원 일에 눈코 뜰 새가 없었고, 그날 밤에는 집에서 그들이 \"개인 법안\"이라 부르는 그 가장 좋아하는 의회 장난감을 가지고 소일하고 있었다."], ["Jeffco himself showed Mr. Franklin into his father’s study.", "제프코 씨 자신이 프랭클린 도련님을 제 아버지의 서재로 안내해 들였다."], ["“My dear Franklin!", "\"얘야 프랭클린!"], ["why do you surprise me in this way?", "어째서 이런 식으로 나를 놀라게 하느냐?"], ["Anything wrong?”", "무슨 일이 잘못됐느냐?\""], ["“Yes; something wrong with Rachel; I am dreadfully distressed about it.”", "\"네, 레이철에게 무언가 잘못된 일이 있어요. 저는 그것 때문에 몹시 괴롭습니다.\""], ["“Grieved to hear it.", "\"그 말을 들으니 서글프구나."], ["But I can’t listen to you now.”", "허나 나는 지금 네 말을 들어 줄 수가 없다.\""], ["“When can you listen?”", "\"언제쯤 들어 주실 수 있어요?\""], ["“My dear boy!", "\"얘야!"], ["I won’t deceive you.", "너를 속이지는 않으마."], ["I can listen at the end of the session, not a moment before.", "나는 회기가 끝나야 들어 줄 수 있다, 한순간도 그 전에는 안 되고."], ["Good-night.”", "잘 자거라.\""], ["“Thank you, sir.", "\"고맙습니다, 아버지."], ["Good-night.”", "안녕히 주무세요.\""]], [["Such was the conversation, inside the study, as reported to me by Mr. Jeffco.", "제프코 씨가 내게 전해 준 바로는, 서재 안에서 오간 대화는 그러했다."], ["The conversation outside the study, was shorter still.", "서재 밖에서 오간 대화는, 그보다도 더 짧았다."], ["“Jeffco, see what time the tidal train starts tomorrow morning.”", "\"제프코, 내일 아침 조수 맞춰 떠나는 기차가 몇 시에 출발하는지 알아봐 주게.\""], ["“At six-forty, Mr. Franklin.”", "\"여섯 시 사십 분입니다, 프랭클린 도련님.\""], ["“Have me called at five.”", "\"다섯 시에 나를 깨워 주게.\""], ["“Going abroad, sir?”", "\"외국으로 가시는 겁니까, 도련님?\""], ["“Going, Jeffco, wherever the railway chooses to take me.”", "\"가네, 제프코, 철도가 나를 데려가겠다는 곳이 어디든 말이야.\""], ["“Shall I tell your father, sir?”", "\"아버님께 말씀드릴까요, 도련님?\""], ["“Yes; tell him at the end of the session.”", "\"그래, 회기가 끝나거든 말씀드리게.\""]], [["The next morning Mr. Franklin had started for foreign parts.", "이튿날 아침 프랭클린 도련님은 외국 땅을 향해 떠났다."], ["To what particular place he was bound, nobody (himself included) could presume to guess.", "그가 어느 특정한 곳으로 향하는지는 아무도 (그 자신을 포함하여) 감히 짐작할 수 없었다."], ["We might hear of him next in Europe, Asia, Africa, or America.", "우리는 그다음번에 유럽에서, 아시아에서, 아프리카에서, 아니면 아메리카에서 그의 소식을 들을지 몰랐다."], ["The chances were as equally divided as possible, in Mr. Jeffco’s opinion, among the four quarters of the globe.", "제프코 씨의 견해로는, 그 승산이 지구의 네 방위에 걸쳐 될 수 있는 한 고르게 나뉘어 있었다."]], [["This news—by closing up all prospects of my bringing Limping Lucy and Mr. Franklin together—at once stopped any further progress of mine on the way to discovery.", "이 소식은—절름발이 루시와 프랭클린 도련님을 한자리에 모을 온갖 가망을 막아 버림으로써—발견으로 나아가는 내 걸음의 모든 진전을 대뜸 멈춰 세웠다."], ["Penelope’s belief that her fellow-servant had destroyed herself through unrequited love for Mr. Franklin Blake, was confirmed—and that was all.", "제 동료 하인이 프랭클린 블레이크 도련님을 향한 짝사랑 때문에 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 페넬로피의 믿음은 굳어졌다—그리고 그것이 전부였다."], ["Whether the letter which Rosanna had left to be given to him after her death did, or did not, contain the confession which Mr. Franklin had suspected her of trying to make to him in her life-time, it was impossible to say.", "로재나가 제 죽음 뒤에 그에게 건네지도록 남긴 그 편지가, 그가 그 아이의 생전에 그 아이가 그에게 하려 애썼으리라 의심한 그 고백을 담고 있었는지 아닌지는, 도무지 알 길이 없었다."], ["It might be only a farewell word, telling nothing but the secret of her unhappy fancy for a person beyond her reach.", "그것은 그저, 제 손이 닿지 않는 사람을 향한 불행한 연정의 비밀 말고는 아무것도 이르지 않는, 작별의 말일 뿐일지도 몰랐다."], ["Or it might own the whole truth about the strange proceedings in which Sergeant Cuff had detected her, from the time when the Moonstone was lost, to the time when she rushed to her own destruction at the Shivering Sand.", "아니면 그것은, 월장석을 잃은 때부터 흔들리는 모래에서 제 스스로의 파멸로 달려든 때까지, 커프 부장이 그 아이에게서 알아챈 그 기이한 행적들에 대한 온전한 진실을 털어놓은 것일지도 몰랐다."], ["A sealed letter it had been placed in Limping Lucy’s hand, and a sealed letter it remained to me and to every one about the girl, her own parents included.", "그것은 봉인된 편지로 절름발이 루시의 손에 놓였고, 나에게도, 그 아이의 친부모를 포함하여 그 아이 둘레의 모든 이에게도, 봉인된 편지로 남았다."], ["We all suspected her of having been in the dead woman’s confidence; we all tried to make her speak; we all failed.", "우리는 모두 그 아이가 그 죽은 여자의 속내를 알고 있었다고 의심했다. 우리는 모두 그 아이를 말하게 하려 애썼다."], ["Now one, and now another, of the servants—still holding to the belief that Rosanna had stolen the Diamond and had hidden it—peered and poked about the rocks to which she had been traced, and peered and poked in vain.", "우리는 모두 실패했다. 하인들이, 이번엔 이 사람이, 이번엔 저 사람이—여전히 로재나가 다이아몬드를 훔쳐 숨겨 두었다는 믿음을 붙든 채—그 아이의 자취가 이어졌던 그 바위들을 들여다보고 쑤셔 보았으나, 들여다보고 쑤셔 봐도 헛일이었다."], ["The tide ebbed, and the tide flowed; the summer went on, and the autumn came.", "밀물이 빠지고, 밀물이 밀려들었다. 여름이 흘러가고, 가을이 왔다."], ["And the Quicksand, which hid her body, hid her secret too.", "그리고 그 아이의 몸을 감춘 유사는, 그 아이의 비밀도 함께 감추었다."]], [["The news of Mr. Franklin’s departure from England on the Sunday morning, and the news of my lady’s arrival in London with Miss Rachel on the Monday afternoon, had reached me, as you are aware, by the Tuesday’s post.", "일요일 아침 프랭클린 도련님이 잉글랜드를 떠났다는 소식과, 월요일 오후 마님이 레이철 아가씨와 함께 런던에 다다랐다는 소식은, 여러분도 아시다시피, 화요일 우편으로 내게 이르렀다."], ["The Wednesday came, and brought nothing.", "수요일이 와서는, 아무것도 가져오지 않았다."], ["The Thursday produced a second budget of news from Penelope.", "목요일이 페넬로피에게서 온 두 번째 소식 꾸러미를 내놓았다."]], [["My girl’s letter informed me that some great London doctor had been consulted about her young lady, and had earned a guinea by remarking that she had better be amused.", "내 딸의 편지는, 어떤 대단한 런던 의사가 우리 아가씨에 대해 진찰을 받았는데, 아가씨를 즐겁게 해 드리는 편이 낫겠다고 소견을 낸 값으로 일 기니를 벌어 갔다고 내게 알려 주었다."], ["Flower-shows, operas, balls—there was a whole round of gaieties in prospect; and Miss Rachel, to her mother’s astonishment, eagerly took to it all.", "꽃 전시회, 오페라, 무도회—온갖 흥겨운 놀이가 줄줄이 앞에 놓여 있었다. 그리고 레이철 아가씨는, 그 어머니가 놀랍게도, 그 모든 것에 열심히 빠져들었다."], ["Mr. Godfrey had called; evidently as sweet as ever on his cousin, in spite of the reception he had met with, when he tried his luck on the occasion of the birthday.", "고드프리 씨가 다녀갔는데, 생일 때 운을 걸어 보았다가 겪은 그 홀대에도 불구하고, 뻔히 제 사촌 누이에게 여느 때처럼 다정했다."], ["To Penelope’s great regret, he had been most graciously received, and had added Miss Rachel’s name to one of his Ladies’ Charities on the spot.", "페넬로피가 몹시 아쉬워하게도, 그는 더할 나위 없이 정중하게 맞아들여졌고, 그 자리에서 제가 맡은 부인 자선회 가운데 하나에 레이철 아가씨의 이름을 올렸다."], ["My mistress was reported to be out of spirits, and to have held two long interviews with her lawyer.", "마님은 기운이 없으시고, 당신 변호사와 두 차례 긴 면담을 가지셨다고 전해졌다."], ["Certain speculations followed, referring to a poor relation of the family—one Miss Clack, whom I have mentioned in my account of the birthday dinner, as sitting next to Mr. Godfrey, and having a pretty taste in champagne.", "이어서 어떤 억측들이 뒤따랐는데, 그것은 그 집안의 어느 가난한 친척—내가 생일 만찬에 대한 내 이야기에서, 고드프리 씨 옆자리에 앉아, 샴페인에 제법 취향이 있다고 언급한 바 있는 어느 클랙 양에 관한 것이었다."], ["Penelope was astonished to find that Miss Clack had not called yet.", "페넬로피는 클랙 양이 아직 찾아오지 않은 것을 알고 놀랐다."], ["She would surely not be long before she fastened herself on my lady as usual—and so forth, and so forth, in the way women have of girding at each other, on and off paper.", "그 여자는 분명 오래지 않아 여느 때처럼 마님에게 찰싹 달라붙을 터였다—어쩌고저쩌고, 이러쿵저러쿵, 여자들이 종이 위에서나 밖에서나 서로 헐뜯는 그 방식으로 말이다."], ["This would not have been worth mentioning, I admit, but for one reason.", "나도 인정하거니와, 이것은 한 가지 이유가 아니었다면 언급할 값어치가 없었으리라."], ["I hear you are likely to be turned over to Miss Clack, after parting with me.", "듣자 하니 여러분은 나와 헤어진 뒤에 클랙 양에게 넘겨질 성싶다."], ["In that case, just do me the favour of not believing a word she says, if she speaks of your humble servant.", "그렇게 되거든, 그 여자가 여러분의 이 미천한 하인에 대해 말하거든, 부디 그 여자 말은 한마디도 믿지 말아 주는 호의를 내게 베풀어 주시라."]], [["On Friday, nothing happened—except that one of the dogs showed signs of a breaking out behind the ears.", "금요일에는, 아무 일도 일어나지 않았다—개 한 마리가 귀 뒤에 무언가 헐기 시작하는 낌새를 보인 것만 빼고."], ["I gave him a dose of syrup of buckthorn, and put him on a diet of pot-liquor and vegetables till further orders.", "나는 그놈에게 갈매나무 시럽 한 첩을 먹이고, 다음 분부가 있을 때까지 고깃국물과 채소로 식이를 조절해 주었다."], ["Excuse my mentioning this.", "이것을 언급하는 것을 용서하시라."], ["It has slipped in somehow.", "어쩌다 보니 슬그머니 끼어들었다."], ["Pass it over please.", "부디 그냥 넘어가 주시라."], ["I am fast coming to the end of my offences against your cultivated modern taste.", "나는 여러분의 세련된 현대적 취향에 대한 나의 죄과의 끝에 빠르게 다다르고 있다."], ["Besides, the dog was a good creature, and deserved a good physicking; he did indeed.", "게다가, 그 개는 착한 짐승이었고, 정성 어린 치료를 받을 만했다. 정말로 그러했다."]], [["Saturday, the last day of the week, is also the last day in my narrative.", "한 주의 마지막 날인 토요일은, 나의 이야기에서도 마지막 날이다."]], [["The morning’s post brought me a surprise in the shape of a London newspaper.", "그날 아침 우편이 런던 신문 한 부의 모습으로 내게 뜻밖의 것을 가져왔다."], ["The handwriting on the direction puzzled me.", "그 겉봉의 글씨가 나를 어리둥절하게 했다."], ["I compared it with the money-lender’s name and address as recorded in my pocket-book, and identified it at once as the writing of Sergeant Cuff.", "나는 그것을 내 수첩에 적힌 그 돈놀이꾼의 이름과 주소와 견주어 보고는, 대뜸 그것이 커프 부장의 글씨임을 알아냈다."]], [["Looking through the paper eagerly enough, after this discovery, I found an ink-mark drawn round one of the police reports.", "이 발견 뒤에, 넉넉히 열심히 그 신문을 훑어보다가, 나는 경찰 보도 가운데 하나에 잉크로 두른 표시가 그어져 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["Here it is, at your service.", "여기, 여러분께 바친다."], ["Read it as I read it, and you will set the right value on the Sergeant’s polite attention in sending me the news of the day:", "내가 읽은 대로 그것을 읽어 보시라, 그러면 여러분은 그날의 소식을 내게 보내 준 그 부장의 정중한 배려에 마땅한 값을 매기게 될 것이다."]], [["“LAMBETH—Shortly before the closing of the court, Mr. Septimus Luker, the well-known dealer in ancient gems, carvings, intagli, &c., &c., applied to the sitting magistrate for advice.", "\"램버스—법정이 문을 닫기 조금 전, 고대 보석과 조각과 음각 세공 등등의 이름난 거래상인 셉티머스 루커 씨가, 재판 중인 치안판사에게 조언을 구했다."], ["The applicant stated that he had been annoyed, at intervals throughout the day, by the proceedings of some of those strolling Indians who infest the streets.", "신청인이 진술하기로는, 그는 온종일 틈틈이, 거리를 들끓게 하는 그 떠돌이 인도인 몇몇의 행패로 시달렸다는 것이다."], ["The persons complained of were three in number.", "하소연 대상이 된 자들은 셋이었다."], ["After having been sent away by the police, they had returned again and again, and had attempted to enter the house on pretence of asking for charity.", "경찰에 의해 쫓겨난 뒤에도, 그들은 몇 번이고 다시 돌아와, 자선을 청하는 척하며 집 안에 들어오려 시도했다."], ["Warned off in the front, they had been discovered again at the back of the premises.", "앞쪽에서 쫓겨나면, 그들은 다시 그 건물 뒤쪽에서 발견되곤 했다."], ["Besides the annoyance complained of, Mr. Luker expressed himself as being under some apprehension that robbery might be contemplated.", "하소연한 그 행패 말고도, 루커 씨는 절도가 꾀해지고 있을지 모른다는 얼마간의 우려에 사로잡혀 있다고 밝혔다."], ["His collection contained many unique gems, both classical and Oriental, of the highest value.", "그의 소장품에는 최고 값어치의 고전적이고 동양적인 진귀한 보석이 많이 들어 있었다."], ["He had only the day before been compelled to dismiss a skilled workman in ivory carving from his employment (a native of India, as we understood), on suspicion of attempted theft; and he felt by no means sure that this man and the street jugglers of whom he complained, might not be acting in concert.", "그는 바로 그 전날, 절도 미수 혐의로 상아 조각에 숙련된 직공 하나를 (우리가 이해하기로는, 인도 태생이었다) 마지못해 제 밑에서 내보내야 했다. 그리고 그는 이 사내와 그가 하소연한 그 거리의 요술쟁이들이 서로 짜고 움직이고 있지는 않은지 결코 확신할 수 없다고 느꼈다."], ["It might be their object to collect a crowd, and create a disturbance in the street, and, in the confusion thus caused, to obtain access to the house.", "사람들을 모아, 거리에서 소동을 일으키고, 그렇게 빚어진 혼란을 틈타, 집 안에 발을 들이는 것이 그들의 목적일지도 몰랐다."], ["In reply to the magistrate, Mr. Luker admitted that he had no evidence to produce of any attempt at robbery being in contemplation.", "치안판사에게 답하며, 루커 씨는 절도가 꾀해지고 있다는 어떤 시도에 대해서도 내놓을 증거가 없음을 인정했다."], ["He could speak positively to the annoyance and interruption caused by the Indians, but not to anything else.", "그는 그 인도인들이 일으킨 행패와 훼방에 대해서는 딱 잘라 말할 수 있어도, 그 밖의 어떤 것에 대해서도 그럴 수는 없었다."], ["The magistrate remarked that, if the annoyance were repeated, the applicant could summon the Indians to that court, where they might easily be dealt with under the Act. As to the valuables in Mr.", "치안판사는, 만일 그 행패가 되풀이되거든, 신청인이 그 인도인들을 그 법정으로 소환할 수 있으며, 거기서 그들은 법령에 따라 손쉽게 다루어질 수 있으리라고 소견을 밝혔다."], ["Luker’s possession, Mr. Luker himself must take the best measures for their safe custody.", "루커 씨가 지닌 귀중품에 대해서는, 루커 씨 자신이 그것들의 안전한 보관을 위한 최선의 방책을 강구해야 할 것이었다."], ["He would do well perhaps to communicate with the police, and to adopt such additional precautions as their experience might suggest.", "그는 아마 경찰과 연락을 취하고, 그들의 경험이 일러 줄 만한 추가적인 대비책을 채택하는 것이 좋으리라."], ["The applicant thanked his worship, and withdrew.”", "신청인은 판사에게 감사하고, 물러갔다.\""]], [["One of the wise ancients is reported (I forget on what occasion) as having recommended his fellow-creatures to “look to the end.”", "옛 현인 가운데 하나가 (무슨 자리에서였는지는 잊었다) 제 동료 인간들에게 \"끝을 바라보라\"고 권한 것으로 전해진다."], ["Looking to the end of these pages of mine, and wondering for some days past how I should manage to write it, I find my plain statement of facts coming to a conclusion, most appropriately, of its own self.", "나의 이 여러 쪽의 끝을 바라보며, 그리고 며칠 전부터 그것을 어떻게 써낼까 궁리하며, 나는 나의 이 담담한 사실 진술이, 더없이 알맞게, 저절로 결말에 다다르고 있음을 알아챈다."], ["We have gone on, in this matter of the Moonstone, from one marvel to another; and here we end with the greatest marvel of all—namely, the accomplishment of Sergeant Cuff’s three predictions in less than a week from the time when he had made them.", "우리는, 이 월장석 일에 있어서, 하나의 경이에서 또 하나의 경이로 나아왔다. 그리고 여기서 우리는 그 모든 것 가운데 가장 큰 경이로 끝맺는다—곧, 커프 부장이 그 세 가지 예언을 한 때로부터 일주일도 채 안 되어 그것들이 이루어졌다는 것이다."]], [["After hearing from the Yollands on the Monday, I had now heard of the Indians, and heard of the money-lender, in the news from London—Miss Rachel herself remember, being also in London at the time.", "월요일에 욜랜드네에서 소식을 들은 뒤, 나는 이제 그 인도인들에 대해 들었고, 런던발 소식에서 그 돈놀이꾼에 대해 들었다—레이철 아가씨 자신도, 기억하시라, 그때 마침 런던에 있었다는 것을."], ["You see, I put things at their worst, even when they tell dead against my own view.", "보시다시피, 나는, 그것이 나 자신의 견해에 딱 어긋나게 말할 때조차, 일들을 가장 나쁘게 놓는다."], ["If you desert me, and side with the Sergeant, on the evidence before you—if the only rational explanation you can see is, that Miss Rachel and Mr. Luker must have got together, and that the Moonstone must be now in pledge in the money-lender’s house—I own, I can’t blame you for arriving at that conclusion.", "만일 여러분이 나를 저버리고, 여러분 앞에 놓인 증거로 보아 부장 편을 든다면—만일 여러분이 볼 수 있는 유일하게 사리에 맞는 설명이, 레이철 아가씨와 루커 씨가 서로 손을 잡았음이 틀림없고, 월장석이 지금 그 돈놀이꾼의 집에 저당 잡혀 있음이 틀림없다는 것이라면—인정하거니와, 나는 여러분이 그 결론에 다다르는 것을 나무랄 수가 없다."], ["In the dark, I have brought you thus far.", "어둠 속에서, 나는 여러분을 이만큼 데려왔다."], ["In the dark I am compelled to leave you, with my best respects.", "어둠 속에서 나는, 최고의 경의를 담아, 여러분을 남겨 두고 떠날 수밖에 없다."]], [["Why compelled? it may be asked.", "어째서 그럴 수밖에 없느냐고, 물음이 나올 법하다."], ["Why not take the persons who have gone along with me, so far, up into those regions of superior enlightenment in which I sit myself?", "어째서 이만큼 나와 함께 걸어온 사람들을, 내가 몸소 앉아 있는 그 더 높은 깨달음의 경지로 데려가지 않느냐고 말이다."]], [["In answer to this, I can only state that I am acting under orders, and that those orders have been given to me (as I understand) in the interests of truth.", "이에 대한 답으로, 나는 그저, 내가 명령을 받아 움직이고 있으며, 그 명령이 (내가 이해하기로는) 진실을 위하여 내게 내려졌다는 것만 말할 수 있다."], ["I am forbidden to tell more in this narrative than I knew myself at the time.", "나는 이 이야기에서 그 당시 나 자신이 알던 것 이상을 이야기하는 것이 금지되어 있다."], ["Or, to put it plainer, I am to keep strictly within the limits of my own experience, and am not to inform you of what other persons told me—for the very sufficient reason that you are to have the information from those other persons themselves, at first hand.", "다시 말해, 더 쉽게 이르자면, 나는 나 자신의 경험의 테두리 안에 엄격히 머물러야 하며, 다른 사람들이 내게 말해 준 것을 여러분에게 알려서는 안 된다—여러분이 그 정보를 그 다른 사람들 자신에게서, 직접, 얻게 되어 있다는 아주 넉넉한 이유에서다."], ["In this matter of the Moonstone the plan is, not to present reports, but to produce witnesses.", "이 월장석 일에 있어서 그 방침은, 보고를 내놓는 것이 아니라, 증인을 내세우는 것이다."], ["I picture to myself a member of the family reading these pages fifty years hence.", "나는 이 집안의 한 사람이 오십 년 뒤에 이 쪽들을 읽는 모습을 마음속에 그려 본다."], ["Lord!", "아이고!"], ["what a compliment he will feel it, to be asked to take nothing on hear-say, and to be treated in all respects like a Judge on the bench.", "아무것도 전해 들은 대로 받아들이지 말라는 청을 받고, 모든 점에서 법정의 판사처럼 대접받는 것을, 그가 얼마나 큰 영예로 느낄 것인가."]], [["At this place, then, we part—for the present, at least—after long journeying together, with a companionable feeling, I hope, on both sides.", "그러면, 이 자리에서, 우리는 헤어진다—적어도, 당분간은—오래 함께 여행한 끝에, 부디, 양쪽 다 벗다운 정을 품고서 말이다."], ["The devil’s dance of the Indian Diamond has threaded its way to London; and to London you must go after it, leaving me at the country house.", "그 인도 다이아몬드의 악마의 춤은 제 길을 더듬어 런던까지 다다랐다. 그리고 여러분은 그것을 좇아 런던으로 가야 하며, 나는 시골집에 남는다."], ["Please to excuse the faults of this composition—my talking so much of myself, and being too familiar, I am afraid, with you.", "이 글의 흠들을—내가 나 자신에 대해 너무 많이 지껄인 것과, 여러분에게 너무 허물없이 군 것이 아닌가 싶은 것을—부디 용서하시라."], ["I mean no harm; and I drink most respectfully (having just done dinner) to your health and prosperity, in a tankard of her ladyship’s ale.", "나는 해코지할 뜻이 없다. 그리고 나는 (막 저녁을 마친 참이라) 마님의 맥주 한 잔으로, 더없이 정중하게, 여러분의 건강과 번영을 위해 마신다."], ["May you find in these leaves of my writing, what Robinson Crusoe found in his experience on the desert island—namely, “something to comfort yourselves from, and to set in the Description of Good and Evil, on the Credit Side of the Account.”—Farewell.", "부디 여러분이 나의 이 글줄들 속에서, 「로빈슨 크루소」가 그 무인도에서의 제 경험 속에서 찾아낸 것을 찾아내기를—곧, \"스스로를 위로할 무언가를, 그리고 선과 악의 명세 가운데 계정의 대변(貸邊) 쪽에 적어 넣을 무언가를.\"—안녕히."]], [["The Events related in several Narratives.", "여러 편의 이야기로 서술된 사건들."]], [["# FIRST NARRATIVE.", "# FIRST NARRATIVE."]], [["Contributed by Miss Clack; niece of the late Sir John Verinder", "고(故) 존 버린더 경의 조카딸, 클랙 양이 기고함."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["I am indebted to my dear parents (both now in heaven) for having had habits of order and regularity instilled into me at a very early age.", "나는 나의 사랑하는 부모님(두 분 다 이제는 천국에 계신다)께, 아주 어린 나이에 질서와 규칙의 습관을 내 안에 심어 주신 데 대해 큰 은혜를 입고 있다."]], [["In that happy bygone time, I was taught to keep my hair tidy at all hours of the day and night, and to fold up every article of my clothing carefully, in the same order, on the same chair, in the same place at the foot of the bed, before retiring to rest.", "그 행복했던 지난 시절에, 나는 낮이든 밤이든 어느 때고 머리를 단정히 하도록, 그리고 잠자리에 들기 전에 내 옷가지를 하나하나, 똑같은 순서로, 똑같은 의자 위에, 침대 발치의 똑같은 자리에, 정성껏 개어 두도록 배웠다."], ["An entry of the day’s events in my little diary invariably preceded the folding up.", "나의 작은 일기장에 그날의 일들을 적는 것이 언제나 그 개는 일에 앞섰다."], ["The “Evening Hymn” (repeated in bed) invariably followed the folding up.", "(잠자리에서 되뇌는) '저녁 찬송'이 언제나 그 개는 일 뒤에 따랐다."], ["And the sweet sleep of childhood invariably followed the “Evening Hymn.”", "그리고 어린 시절의 그 달콤한 잠이 언제나 '저녁 찬송' 뒤에 따랐다."]], [["In later life (alas!) the Hymn has been succeeded by sad and bitter meditations; and the sweet sleep has been but ill exchanged for the broken slumbers which haunt the uneasy pillow of care.", "훗날의 삶에서 (아아!) 그 찬송은 서글프고 쓰라린 명상으로 이어졌다. 그리고 그 달콤한 잠은 근심으로 편치 못한 베개를 맴도는 그 끊긴 선잠과 고약하게 맞바꾸어졌을 뿐이다."], ["On the other hand, I have continued to fold my clothes, and to keep my little diary.", "다른 한편으로, 나는 내 옷을 개고, 나의 작은 일기를 적는 일을 계속해 왔다."], ["The former habit links me to my happy childhood—before papa was ruined.", "앞엣것의 습관은 나를 나의 행복했던 어린 시절로—아빠가 파산하시기 전으로 이어 준다."], ["The latter habit—hitherto mainly useful in helping me to discipline the fallen nature which we all inherit from Adam—has unexpectedly proved important to my humble interests in quite another way.", "뒤엣것의 습관은—여태껏은 주로 우리 모두가 아담에게서 물려받은 그 타락한 본성을 다스리는 데 도움이 되어 왔거니와—전혀 다른 방식으로, 뜻밖에도 나의 미천한 이익에 중요한 것으로 드러났다."], ["It has enabled poor Me to serve the caprice of a wealthy member of the family into which my late uncle married.", "그것이 이 가엾은 나로 하여금, 내 돌아가신 외숙부가 혼인으로 맺어진 그 집안의 어느 부유한 일원의 변덕에 봉사할 수 있게 해 준 것이다."], ["I am fortunate enough to be useful to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "나는 다행스럽게도 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 쓸모가 있게 되었다."]], [["I have been cut off from all news of my relatives by marriage for some time past.", "나는 지난 얼마 동안 혼인으로 맺어진 친척들의 온갖 소식에서 끊겨 있었다."], ["When we are isolated and poor, we are not infrequently forgotten.", "우리가 외따로 떨어지고 가난할 때, 우리는 드물지 않게 잊힌다."], ["I am now living, for economy’s sake, in a little town in Brittany, inhabited by a select circle of serious English friends, and possessed of the inestimable advantages of a Protestant clergyman and a cheap market.", "나는 지금, 아껴 쓰기 위하여, 브르타뉴의 어느 작은 마을에 살고 있는데, 그곳에는 진지한 영국인 벗들의 선택받은 무리가 거주하며, 개신교 목사님 한 분과 값싼 장터라는 값을 매길 수 없는 이점을 지니고 있다."]], [["In this retirement—a Patmos amid the howling ocean of popery that surrounds us—a letter from England has reached me at last.", "이 은거지에서—우리를 에워싼 저 울부짖는 교황 숭배의 대양 한가운데 있는 파트모스에서—마침내 잉글랜드에서 온 편지 한 통이 내게 다다랐다."], ["I find my insignificant existence suddenly remembered by Mr. Franklin Blake.", "나는 나의 보잘것없는 존재가 별안간 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 기억되었음을 알게 되었다."], ["My wealthy relative—would that I could add my spiritually-wealthy relative!—writes, without even an attempt at disguising that he wants something of me.", "나의 부유한 친척은—그가 영적으로 부유한 친척이라고 덧붙일 수 있다면 얼마나 좋으랴!—내게 무언가 바라는 바가 있음을 숨기려는 시도조차 없이 편지를 쓴다."], ["The whim has seized him to stir up the deplorable scandal of the Moonstone: and I am to help him by writing the account of what I myself witnessed while visiting at Aunt Verinder’s house in London.", "월장석이라는 그 개탄스러운 추문을 들쑤셔 보려는 변덕이 그를 사로잡았다. 그리하여 나는, 런던의 버린더 이모님 댁에 머무는 동안 나 자신이 목격한 것에 대한 기록을 씀으로써 그를 도와야 한다."], ["Pecuniary remuneration is offered to me—with the want of feeling peculiar to the rich.", "내게 금전적 보상이 제시된다—부유한 자들 특유의 무정함과 더불어 말이다."], ["I am to re-open wounds that Time has barely closed; I am to recall the most intensely painful remembrances—and this done, I am to feel myself compensated by a new laceration, in the shape of Mr.", "나는 세월이 겨우 아물려 놓은 상처들을 다시 열어야 한다. 나는 더없이 사무치게 고통스러운 기억들을 떠올려야 한다—그리고 이 일을 마치고 나면, 나는 블레이크 씨의 수표라는 모양을 한 새로운 생채기로 보상받는 셈이 된다."], ["Blake’s cheque.", "나의 천성은 약하다."], ["My nature is weak. It cost me a hard struggle, before Christian humility conquered sinful pride, and self-denial accepted the cheque.", "기독교인다운 겸손이 죄 많은 자존심을 이기고, 극기가 그 수표를 받아들이기까지, 그것은 내게 힘겨운 씨름을 치르게 했다."]], [["Without my diary, I doubt—pray let me express it in the grossest terms!—if I could have honestly earned my money.", "나의 일기가 없었더라면, 나는—부디 가장 노골적인 말로 그것을 표현하게 해 다오!—과연 정직하게 내 돈을 벌 수 있었을지 의심스럽다."], ["With my diary, the poor labourer (who forgives Mr. Blake for insulting her) is worthy of her hire.", "나의 일기와 더불어, 이 가엾은 품팔이꾼은 (자기를 모욕한 블레이크 씨를 용서하는 이 여자는) 제 삯을 받을 자격이 있다."], ["Nothing escaped me at the time I was visiting dear Aunt Verinder.", "사랑하는 버린더 이모님 댁에 머물던 그 시절, 그 무엇도 나를 벗어나지 못했다."], ["Everything was entered (thanks to my early training) day by day as it happened; and everything, down to the smallest particular, shall be told here.", "모든 것이 (나의 어린 시절 훈련 덕분에) 날마다 일어나는 그대로 적혔다. 그리고 모든 것이, 가장 사소한 세부까지, 여기에 이야기될 것이다."], ["My sacred regard for truth is (thank God) far above my respect for persons. It will be easy for Mr.", "진실에 대한 나의 성스러운 존중은 (하느님 고맙게도) 사람에 대한 나의 존중보다 훨씬 위에 있다."], ["Blake to suppress what may not prove to be sufficiently flattering in these pages to the person chiefly concerned in them.", "이 쪽들 가운데, 여기에 주로 관련된 사람에게 충분히 아첨이 되지 못할 만한 것을 억누르는 일은, 블레이크 씨에게 손쉬운 일이리라."], ["He has purchased my time, but not even his wealth can purchase my conscience too.*", "그는 나의 시간은 샀으나, 그의 그 재물조차 나의 양심까지 살 수는 없다.*"]], [["[*Note.", "[*주."], ["Added by Franklin Blake.—Miss Clack may make her mind quite easy on this point.", "프랭클린 블레이크가 덧붙임.—클랙 양은 이 점에 대해 마음을 아주 편히 놓아도 좋다."], ["Nothing will be added, altered or removed, in her manuscript, or in any of the other manuscripts which pass through my hands.", "그녀의 원고에서든, 내 손을 거치는 다른 어느 원고에서든, 아무것도 덧붙여지거나, 고쳐지거나, 지워지지 않을 것이다."], ["Whatever opinions any of the writers may express, whatever peculiarities of treatment may mark, and perhaps in a literary sense, disfigure the narratives which I am now collecting, not a line will be tampered with anywhere, from first to last.", "어느 필자가 어떤 견해를 밝히든, 어떤 별난 처리 방식이 내가 지금 모으고 있는 이야기들을 특징짓든, 그리고 어쩌면 문학적 의미에서 그것들을 망가뜨리든, 처음부터 끝까지 어느 한 줄도 어디에서도 손대어지지 않을 것이다."], ["As genuine documents they are sent to me—and as genuine documents I shall preserve them, endorsed by the attestations of witnesses who can speak to the facts.", "진짜 문서로서 그것들은 내게 보내진다—그리고 진짜 문서로서 나는 그것들을, 사실을 증언할 수 있는 증인들의 확인을 곁들여, 보존할 것이다."], ["It only remains to be added that “the person chiefly concerned” in Miss Clack’s narrative, is happy enough at the present moment, not only to brave the smartest exercise of Miss Clack’s pen, but even to recognise its unquestionable value as an instrument for the exhibition of Miss Clack’s character.]", "다만 덧붙여 둘 것은, 클랙 양의 이야기에 \"주로 관련된 사람\"은, 지금 이 순간, 클랙 양의 붓이 부리는 가장 매서운 놀림을 꿋꿋이 견뎌 낼 뿐만 아니라, 심지어 그것을 클랙 양의 됨됨이를 드러내 보이는 도구로서 의심할 여지 없는 값어치를 지녔음을 알아볼 만큼 충분히 행복하다는 것이다.]"]], [["My diary informs me, that I was accidentally passing Aunt Verinder’s house in Montagu Square, on Monday, 3rd July, 1848.", "나의 일기는, 내가 1848년 7월 3일 월요일에, 몬터규 스퀘어에 있는 버린더 이모님 댁을 우연히 지나가고 있었음을 내게 일러 준다."]], [["Seeing the shutters opened, and the blinds drawn up, I felt that it would be an act of polite attention to knock, and make inquiries.", "덧문이 열리고, 차양이 걷혀 올라간 것을 보고, 나는 문을 두드려 안부를 여쭙는 것이 예의 바른 배려의 행위이리라 느꼈다."], ["The person who answered the door, informed me that my aunt and her daughter (I really cannot call her my cousin!) had arrived from the country a week since, and meditated making some stay in London.", "문을 열어 준 사람이 내게 알려 주기로는, 이모님과 그 따님이 (나는 정말이지 그 아이를 내 사촌이라고 부를 수가 없다!) 일주일 전에 시골에서 올라와, 런던에 얼마간 머물 작정을 하고 있다는 것이었다."], ["I sent up a message at once, declining to disturb them, and only begging to know whether I could be of any use.", "나는 대뜸 위층으로 전언을 올려, 그들을 방해하기를 사양하고, 다만 내가 무슨 도움이 될 수 있을지만 알고 싶다고 청했다."]], [["The person who answered the door, took my message in insolent silence, and left me standing in the hall.", "문을 열어 준 그 사람은, 무례한 침묵 속에 내 전언을 받아 들고, 나를 현관에 세워 둔 채 가 버렸다."], ["She is the daughter of a heathen old man named Betteredge—long, too long, tolerated in my aunt’s family.", "그 여자는 베터리지라는 이름의 이교도 늙은이의 딸로—오래도록, 너무도 오래도록, 이모님 집안에서 참아 준 자다."], ["I sat down in the hall to wait for my answer—and, having always a few tracts in my bag, I selected one which proved to be quite providentially applicable to the person who answered the door.", "나는 대답을 기다리려고 현관에 앉았다—그리고, 언제나 가방에 소책자 몇 부를 지니고 다니는 터라, 나는 문을 열어 준 그 사람에게 참으로 하느님의 섭리처럼 꼭 들어맞는 것으로 드러난 한 부를 골라냈다."], ["The hall was dirty, and the chair was hard; but the blessed consciousness of returning good for evil raised me quite above any trifling considerations of that kind.", "현관은 지저분했고, 의자는 딱딱했다. 허나 악을 선으로 갚는다는 그 복된 자각이 나를 그런 하잘것없는 헤아림 따위로부터 넉넉히 높이 들어 올려 주었다."], ["The tract was one of a series addressed to young women on the sinfulness of dress.", "그 소책자는 젊은 여자들에게 옷치레의 죄스러움에 대해 이르는 한 연작 가운데 하나였다."], ["In style it was devoutly familiar.", "문체에 있어서 그것은 경건하게 친근했다."], ["Its title was, “A Word With You On Your Cap-Ribbons.”", "그 제목은 '그대의 모자 리본에 관하여 한 말씀'이었다."]], [["“My lady is much obliged, and begs you will come and lunch tomorrow at two.”", "\"마님께서 대단히 고마워하시며, 내일 두 시에 점심 드시러 오시라 청하십니다.\""]], [["I passed over the manner in which she gave her message, and the dreadful boldness of her look.", "나는 그 여자가 그 전언을 건네는 태도와, 그 끔찍하게 뻔뻔한 눈초리를 그냥 넘겼다. 나는 이 젊은 버림받은 자에게 감사했다."], ["I thanked this young castaway; and I said, in a tone of Christian interest, “Will you favour me by accepting a tract?”", "그리고 나는 기독교인다운 관심의 어조로 말했다. \"소책자 한 부를 받아 주는 호의를 베풀어 주겠니?\""]], [["She looked at the title.", "그 여자는 제목을 들여다보았다."], ["“Is it written by a man or a woman, Miss?", "\"이거 남자가 썼나요, 여자가 썼나요, 아가씨?"], ["If it’s written by a woman, I had rather not read it on that account.", "여자가 쓴 거라면, 그런 이유로 저는 읽지 않는 편이 낫겠어요."], ["If it’s written by a man, I beg to inform him that he knows nothing about it.”", "남자가 쓴 거라면, 그 사람에게 아무것도 모르면서 썼다고 전해 주시길 청합니다.\""], ["She handed me back the tract, and opened the door.", "그 여자는 소책자를 내게 도로 건네고는, 문을 열었다."], ["We must sow the good seed somehow.", "우리는 어떻게든 좋은 씨앗을 뿌려야 한다."], ["I waited till the door was shut on me, and slipped the tract into the letter-box.", "나는 문이 내 뒤로 닫힐 때까지 기다렸다가, 소책자를 우편함에 슬며시 밀어 넣었다."], ["When I had dropped another tract through the area railings, I felt relieved, in some small degree, of a heavy responsibility towards others.", "또 한 부의 소책자를 지하실 난간 사이로 떨어뜨리고 나자, 나는 타인들을 향한 무거운 책임에서, 얼마쯤이나마, 홀가분해진 느낌이 들었다."]], [["We had a meeting that evening of the Select Committee of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes-Conversion-Society.", "그날 저녁 우리는 '어머니회 아동복 개조 협회'의 선정 위원회 모임이 있었다."], ["The object of this excellent Charity is—as all serious people know—to rescue unredeemed fathers’ trousers from the pawnbroker, and to prevent their resumption, on the part of the irreclaimable parent, by abridging them immediately to suit the proportions of the innocent son.", "이 훌륭한 자선회의 목적은—진지한 사람이라면 다 알거니와—구원받지 못한 아버지들의 바지를 전당포에서 구해 내고, 그것을 즉시 죄 없는 아들의 몸에 맞도록 줄임으로써, 그 개심할 길 없는 어버이 편에서 그것을 되찾아 가는 일을 막는 것이다. 나는, 그 무렵, 그 선정 위원회의 일원이었다."], ["I was a member, at that time, of the select committee; and I mention the Society here, because my precious and admirable friend, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, was associated with our work of moral and material usefulness.", "그리고 내가 여기서 그 협회를 언급하는 것은, 나의 소중하고 감탄스러운 벗, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 우리의 도덕적이고 물질적인 유익의 일에 함께하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["I had expected to see him in the boardroom, on the Monday evening of which I am now writing, and had proposed to tell him, when we met, of dear Aunt Verinder’s arrival in London.", "나는 지금 내가 적고 있는 그 월요일 저녁에 이사회 방에서 그를 보리라 기대하고 있었고, 우리가 만나거든, 사랑하는 버린더 이모님이 런던에 도착하셨음을 그에게 알리려 작정하고 있었다."], ["To my great disappointment he never appeared.", "나의 큰 실망스럽게도 그는 끝내 나타나지 않았다."], ["On my expressing a feeling of surprise at his absence, my sisters of the Committee all looked up together from their trousers (we had a great pressure of business that night), and asked in amazement, if I had not heard the news.", "그의 부재에 놀라움의 심정을 내비치자, 위원회의 자매들이 다 함께 저마다의 바지에서 (그날 밤 우리는 일감이 크게 밀려 있었다) 고개를 들고는, 놀라며, 내가 그 소식을 듣지 못했느냐고 물었다."], ["I acknowledged my ignorance, and was then told, for the first time, of an event which forms, so to speak, the starting-point of this narrative.", "나는 나의 무지를 인정했고, 그러자 나는, 이를테면, 이 이야기의 출발점을 이루는 어떤 사건에 대해, 처음으로 전해 들었다."], ["On the previous Friday, two gentlemen—occupying widely-different positions in society—had been the victims of an outrage which had startled all London.", "그 전 금요일에, 두 신사—사회에서 크게 다른 지위를 차지하고 있는—가 온 런던을 깜짝 놀라게 한 어떤 폭행의 희생자가 되었다는 것이었다."], ["One of the gentlemen was Mr. Septimus Luker, of Lambeth.", "그 신사들 가운데 하나는 램버스의 셉티머스 루커 씨였다."], ["The other was Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "다른 하나는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨였다."]], [["Living in my present isolation, I have no means of introducing the newspaper-account of the outrage into my narrative.", "지금 이 외딴 처지에 살고 있는 터라, 나는 그 폭행에 대한 신문 기사를 나의 이야기에 들여올 방도가 없다."], ["I was also deprived, at the time, of the inestimable advantage of hearing the events related by the fervid eloquence of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "나는 또한, 그 당시, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 그 열띤 웅변으로 이야기되는 사건들을 들을 값을 매길 수 없는 이점을 누리지도 못했다."], ["All I can do is to state the facts as they were stated, on that Monday evening, to me; proceeding on the plan which I have been taught from infancy to adopt in folding up my clothes.", "내가 할 수 있는 것이라곤, 그 월요일 저녁 내게 이야기된 그대로 사실들을 적는 것뿐이다. 어려서부터 옷을 개는 데에 취하도록 배워 온 그 방침에 따라 나아가면서 말이다."], ["Everything shall be put neatly, and everything shall be put in its place.", "모든 것이 단정히 놓일 것이며, 모든 것이 제자리에 놓일 것이다."], ["These lines are written by a poor weak woman.", "이 글줄들은 가엾고 연약한 한 여자에 의해 쓰인다."], ["From a poor weak woman who will be cruel enough to expect more?", "가엾고 연약한 한 여자에게서 누가 그 이상을 기대할 만큼 모질겠는가?"]], [["The date—thanks to my dear parents, no dictionary that ever was written can be more particular than I am about dates—was Friday, June 30th, 1848.", "그 날짜는—나의 사랑하는 부모님 덕분에, 여태껏 쓰인 그 어떤 사전도 날짜에 관한 한 나보다 더 꼼꼼할 수는 없거니와—1848년 6월 30일 금요일이었다."]], [["Early on that memorable day, our gifted Mr. Godfrey happened to be cashing a cheque at a banking-house in Lombard Street.", "그 잊지 못할 날의 이른 시각에, 우리의 재능 있는 고드프리 씨는 마침 롬바드 스트리트의 어느 은행에서 수표 한 장을 현금으로 바꾸고 있었다."], ["The name of the firm is accidentally blotted in my diary, and my sacred regard for truth forbids me to hazard a guess in a matter of this kind.", "그 회사의 이름은 나의 일기에서 어쩌다 얼룩져 지워졌고, 진실에 대한 나의 성스러운 존중이 나로 하여금 이런 종류의 일에서 함부로 짐작을 무릅쓰는 것을 금한다."], ["Fortunately, the name of the firm doesn’t matter.", "다행히, 그 회사의 이름은 대수롭지 않다."], ["What does matter is a circumstance that occurred when Mr. Godfrey had transacted his business.", "대수로운 것은 고드프리 씨가 제 볼일을 마쳤을 때 일어난 어떤 정황이다."], ["On gaining the door, he encountered a gentleman—a perfect stranger to him—who was accidentally leaving the office exactly at the same time as himself.", "문에 다다르자, 그는 어느 신사와—그에게는 전혀 낯선 이와—맞닥뜨렸는데, 그이는 마침 그와 꼭 같은 순간에 그 사무소를 나서고 있었다."], ["A momentary contest of politeness ensued between them as to who should be the first to pass through the door of the bank.", "은행 문을 누가 먼저 지날 것이냐를 두고 그들 사이에 한순간의 예의 겨루기가 벌어졌다."], ["The stranger insisted on making Mr. Godfrey precede him; Mr.", "그 낯선 이는 고드프리 씨를 먼저 지나게 하겠다고 우겼다."], ["Godfrey said a few civil words; they bowed, and parted in the street.", "고드프리 씨가 정중한 말 몇 마디를 건넸다. 그들은 고개 숙여 인사하고, 거리에서 헤어졌다."]], [["Thoughtless and superficial people may say, Here is surely a very trumpery little incident related in an absurdly circumstantial manner.", "생각 없고 얄팍한 사람들은 말할지 모른다, 여기 참으로 하잘것없는 조그만 일 하나가 우스꽝스러울 만큼 시시콜콜하게 이야기되고 있다고."], ["Oh, my young friends and fellow-sinners!", "오, 나의 젊은 벗들이여, 그리고 함께 죄 지은 이들이여!"], ["beware of presuming to exercise your poor carnal reason.", "그대들의 가엾은 육신의 이성을 감히 부리려 들지 않도록 조심하라."], ["Oh, be morally tidy.", "오, 도덕적으로 단정하라."], ["Let your faith be as your stockings, and your stockings as your faith.", "그대들의 믿음이 그대들의 양말과 같게 하고, 그대들의 양말이 그대들의 믿음과 같게 하라."], ["Both ever spotless, and both ready to put on at a moment’s notice!", "둘 다 언제나 티끌 하나 없이, 그리고 둘 다 한순간의 통지에도 걸칠 채비가 되어 있게!"]], [["I beg a thousand pardons.", "수천 번 용서를 빈다."], ["I have fallen insensibly into my Sunday-school style.", "나는 나도 모르게 나의 주일학교 문체에 빠져들고 말았다."], ["Most inappropriate in such a record as this.", "이런 기록에는 더없이 어울리지 않는 것을."], ["Let me try to be worldly—let me say that trifles, in this case as in many others, led to terrible results. Merely premising that the polite stranger was Mr.", "세속적이 되어 보도록 애써 보리라—다른 많은 경우에서와 마찬가지로 이 경우에도, 사소한 것들이 끔찍한 결과로 이어졌다고 말해 보리라."], ["Luker, of Lambeth, we will now follow Mr. Godfrey home to his residence at Kilburn.", "그 정중한 낯선 이가 램버스의 루커 씨였다는 것만 미리 밝혀 두고, 우리는 이제 고드프리 씨를 따라 킬번에 있는 그의 저택으로 가 보리라."]], [["He found waiting for him, in the hall, a poorly clad but delicate and interesting-looking little boy.", "그는, 현관에서, 초라한 옷차림이지만 곱상하고 흥미로워 보이는 어린 사내아이 하나가 자기를 기다리고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["The boy handed him a letter, merely mentioning that he had been entrusted with it by an old lady whom he did not know, and who had given him no instructions to wait for an answer.", "그 아이는 그에게 편지 한 통을 건네며, 다만 자기가 알지 못하는 어느 노부인에게서 그것을 맡았는데, 그분이 답장을 기다리라는 아무 지시도 주지 않았다고만 말했다."], ["Such incidents as these were not uncommon in Mr. Godfrey’s large experience as a promoter of public charities.", "이런 일들은 공공 자선의 후원자로서 고드프리 씨의 넓은 경험에서 드물지 않았다."], ["He let the boy go, and opened the letter.", "그는 그 아이를 보내고, 편지를 뜯었다."]], [["The handwriting was entirely unfamiliar to him.", "그 필적은 그에게 전혀 낯익지 않았다."], ["It requested his attendance, within an hour’s time, at a house in Northumberland Street, Strand, which he had never had occasion to enter before.", "그것은 한 시간 안에, 그가 여태 들어가 볼 일이 없었던 스트랜드의 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에 있는 어느 집으로 와 달라고 청하고 있었다."], ["The object sought was to obtain from the worthy manager certain details on the subject of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes-Conversion-Society, and the information was wanted by an elderly lady who proposed adding largely to the resources of the charity, if her questions were met by satisfactory replies.", "구하는 바는, 그 훌륭한 관리자에게서 '어머니회 아동복 개조 협회'라는 화제에 대한 어떤 세부 사항들을 얻는 것이었고, 그 정보를 원하는 이는, 만일 제 물음들이 흡족한 답으로 채워진다면, 그 자선회의 재원에 크게 보태겠다는 어느 나이 지긋한 부인이었다."], ["She mentioned her name, and she added that the shortness of her stay in London prevented her from giving any longer notice to the eminent philanthropist whom she addressed.", "그녀는 제 이름을 밝히고, 런던에 머무는 기간이 짧아 자기가 편지를 보내는 그 저명한 자선가에게 더 길게 미리 알릴 수가 없었다고 덧붙였다."]], [["Ordinary people might have hesitated before setting aside their own engagements to suit the convenience of a stranger.", "여느 사람들이라면 낯선 이의 편의를 봐주려고 제 볼일을 제쳐 두기 전에 망설였을지 모른다."], ["The Christian Hero never hesitates where good is to be done. Mr.", "기독교적 영웅은 선을 행할 일이 있는 곳에서 결코 망설이지 않는다."], ["Godfrey instantly turned back, and proceeded to the house in Northumberland Street.", "고드프리 씨는 대뜸 발길을 돌려, 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에 있는 그 집으로 나아갔다."], ["A most respectable though somewhat corpulent man answered the door, and, on hearing Mr. Godfrey’s name, immediately conducted him into an empty apartment at the back, on the drawing-room floor.", "더없이 점잖으나 다소 뚱뚱한 사내가 문을 열어 주었고, 고드프리 씨의 이름을 듣고는, 즉시 그를 응접실 층 뒤편의 텅 빈 방으로 안내해 들였다."], ["He noticed two unusual things on entering the room.", "그는 그 방에 들어서면서 예사롭지 않은 두 가지를 알아챘다."], ["One of them was a faint odour of musk and camphor.", "그 하나는 사향과 장뇌의 희미한 냄새였다."], ["The other was an ancient Oriental manuscript, richly illuminated with Indian figures and devices, that lay open to inspection on a table.", "다른 하나는, 인도풍의 인물과 문양으로 화려하게 장식되어, 탁자 위에 들여다보라는 듯 펼쳐져 있는 어느 고대 동양 필사본이었다."]], [["He was looking at the book, the position of which caused him to stand with his back turned towards the closed folding doors communicating with the front room, when, without the slightest previous noise to warn him, he felt himself suddenly seized round the neck from behind.", "그는 그 책을 들여다보고 있었는데, 그 책이 놓인 위치 탓에 그는 앞방으로 통하는 닫힌 접이문에 등을 돌린 채 서 있게 되었고, 그때, 그를 일깨울 아무런 낌새의 소리도 앞서 없이, 그는 뒤에서 별안간 제 목이 휘감기는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He had just time to notice that the arm round his neck was naked and of a tawny-brown colour, before his eyes were bandaged, his mouth was gagged, and he was thrown helpless on the floor by (as he judged) two men.", "그는 제 목을 휘감은 그 팔이 벌거벗겨 있고 황갈색을 띠고 있음을 알아챌 겨를밖에 없었는데, 그 순간 그의 눈이 가리어지고, 그의 입에 재갈이 물리고, 그는 (그가 헤아리기로는) 두 사내에 의해 속절없이 바닥에 내동댕이쳐졌다."], ["A third rifled his pockets, and—if, as a lady, I may venture to use such an expression—searched him, without ceremony, through and through to his skin.", "세 번째 자가 그의 호주머니를 털어—그리고, 숙녀로서 감히 이런 표현을 써도 된다면—격식도 없이, 그의 살갗에 이르기까지 샅샅이 그를 뒤졌다."]], [["Here I should greatly enjoy saying a few cheering words on the devout confidence which could alone have sustained Mr. Godfrey in an emergency so terrible as this.", "여기서 나는 이토록 끔찍한 위급함 속에서 고드프리 씨를 홀로 지탱해 줄 수 있었을 그 경건한 믿음에 대해 몇 마디 격려의 말을 하는 것을 크게 즐길 만하다."], ["Perhaps, however, the position and appearance of my admirable friend at the culminating period of the outrage (as above described) are hardly within the proper limits of female discussion.", "허나 어쩌면, (위에서 묘사한 바) 그 폭행의 절정기에 있어서의 나의 감탄스러운 벗의 자세와 몰골은, 여자가 논할 마땅한 테두리 안에 좀처럼 들지 않는 것이리라."], ["Let me pass over the next few moments, and return to Mr. Godfrey at the time when the odious search of his person had been completed.", "그다음 몇 순간은 그냥 넘기고, 그의 몸에 대한 그 역겨운 수색이 끝난 때의 고드프리 씨에게로 돌아가리라."], ["The outrage had been perpetrated throughout in dead silence.", "그 폭행은 처음부터 끝까지 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에서 저질러졌다."], ["At the end of it some words were exchanged, among the invisible wretches, in a language which he did not understand, but in tones which were plainly expressive (to his cultivated ear) of disappointment and rage.", "그것이 끝날 무렵 그 보이지 않는 몹쓸 자들 사이에, 그가 알아듣지 못하는 어떤 언어로, 허나 (그의 교양 있는 귀에는) 실망과 분노를 뚜렷이 나타내는 어조로, 몇 마디 말이 오갔다."], ["He was suddenly lifted from the ground, placed in a chair, and bound there hand and foot.", "그는 별안간 바닥에서 들어 올려져, 의자에 앉혀지고, 거기에 손발이 묶였다."], ["The next moment he felt the air flowing in from the open door, listened, and concluded that he was alone again in the room.", "그다음 순간 그는 열린 문에서 공기가 흘러 들어오는 것을 느끼고, 귀 기울였고, 자기가 그 방에 다시 혼자 남았다고 결론지었다."]], [["An interval elapsed, and he heard a sound below like the rustling sound of a woman’s dress.", "한동안이 흐르고, 그는 아래층에서 여자의 옷자락이 스치는 소리 같은 것을 들었다."], ["It advanced up the stairs, and stopped.", "그것이 층계를 올라와, 멈췄다."], ["A female scream rent the atmosphere of guilt.", "여자의 비명이 그 죄스러운 공기를 찢었다."], ["A man’s voice below exclaimed “Hullo!”", "아래층에서 사내의 목소리가 \"어이!\""], ["A man’s feet ascended the stairs.", "하고 외쳤다. 사내의 발이 층계를 올라왔다."], ["Mr. Godfrey felt Christian fingers unfastening his bandage, and extracting his gag.", "고드프리 씨는 기독교인의 손가락이 제 눈가리개를 풀고, 제 재갈을 빼내는 것을 느꼈다."], ["He looked in amazement at two respectable strangers, and faintly articulated, “What does it mean?”", "그는 놀라며 점잖은 두 낯선 이를 바라보고, 힘없이 또렷이 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻입니까?\""], ["The two respectable strangers looked back, and said, “Exactly the question we were going to ask you.”", "그 점잖은 두 낯선 이가 마주 바라보며 말했다. \"바로 우리가 당신에게 물으려던 물음이오.\""]], [["The inevitable explanation followed.", "피할 수 없는 해명이 뒤따랐다. 아니!"], ["No! Let me be scrupulously particular.", "나로 하여금 빈틈없이 꼼꼼하게 하도록 해 다오."], ["Sal volatile and water followed, to compose dear Mr. Godfrey’s nerves.", "사랑하는 고드프리 씨의 신경을 가라앉히기 위한 각성용 암모니아수와 물이 뒤따랐다."], ["The explanation came next.", "해명은 그다음에 왔다."]], [["It appeared from the statement of the landlord and landlady of the house (persons of good repute in the neighbourhood), that their first and second floor apartments had been engaged, on the previous day, for a week certain, by a most respectable-looking gentleman—the same who has been already described as answering the door to Mr. Godfrey’s knock.", "그 집의 남녀 주인(이웃에서 평판 좋은 사람들)의 진술로 미루어 드러난 바는, 그들의 이층과 삼층 방이, 그 전날, 확실히 일주일간, 더없이 점잖아 보이는 어느 신사에 의해—고드프리 씨의 문 두드림에 문을 열어 준 자로 이미 묘사된 바로 그 자에 의해—세내어졌다는 것이었다."], ["The gentleman had paid the week’s rent and all the week’s extras in advance, stating that the apartments were wanted for three Oriental noblemen, friends of his, who were visiting England for the first time.", "그 신사는 일주일 치 방세와 일주일 치 온갖 별도 비용을 미리 치르면서, 그 방들이 잉글랜드를 처음으로 방문하는 제 친구인 세 동양 귀족을 위해 필요하다고 밝혔다."], ["Early on the morning of the outrage, two of the Oriental strangers, accompanied by their respectable English friend, took possession of the apartments.", "그 폭행의 날 이른 아침에, 그 동양 낯선 이들 가운데 둘이, 그들의 점잖은 영국인 벗을 대동하고, 그 방들을 차지했다."], ["The third was expected to join them shortly; and the luggage (reported as very bulky) was announced to follow when it had passed through the Custom-house, late in the afternoon.", "세 번째 자는 오래지 않아 그들에게 합류할 것으로 예상되었다. 그리고 그 짐은 (아주 부피가 크다고 전해졌다) 그날 오후 늦게 세관을 통과하거든 뒤따라오는 것으로 알려졌다."], ["Not more than ten minutes previous to Mr. Godfrey’s visit, the third foreigner had arrived.", "고드프리 씨가 방문하기 채 십 분도 전에, 그 세 번째 외국인이 도착했다."], ["Nothing out of the common had happened, to the knowledge of the landlord and landlady downstairs, until within the last five minutes—when they had seen the three foreigners, accompanied by their respectable English friend, all leave the house together, walking quietly in the direction of the Strand.", "아래층의 남녀 주인이 아는 한, 지난 오 분 사이까지는—그 세 외국인이, 그들의 점잖은 영국인 벗을 대동하고, 죄다 함께 그 집을 나서, 스트랜드 방향으로 조용히 걸어가는 것을 그들이 본 그때까지는—예사롭지 않은 일이라곤 아무것도 일어나지 않았다."], ["Remembering that a visitor had called, and not having seen the visitor also leave the house, the landlady had thought it rather strange that the gentleman should be left by himself upstairs.", "방문객 하나가 다녀갔음을 기억하고, 그 방문객이 함께 그 집을 나서는 것은 보지 못한 터라, 그 안주인은 그 신사가 위층에 홀로 남겨졌다는 것이 다소 이상하다고 여겼다."], ["After a short discussion with her husband, she had considered it advisable to ascertain whether anything was wrong.", "제 남편과 잠깐 의논한 뒤, 그녀는 무언가 잘못된 것은 아닌지 확인해 보는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼다."], ["The result had followed, as I have already attempted to describe it; and there the explanation of the landlord and the landlady came to an end.", "그 결과가, 내가 이미 묘사하려 애쓴 대로, 뒤따랐다. 그리고 거기서 남녀 주인의 해명이 끝났다."]], [["An investigation was next made in the room.", "그다음으로 그 방에서 조사가 이루어졌다."], ["Dear Mr. Godfrey’s property was found scattered in all directions.", "사랑하는 고드프리 씨의 소지품이 사방에 흩어져 있는 것이 발견되었다."], ["When the articles were collected, however, nothing was missing; his watch, chain, purse, keys, pocket-handkerchief, note-book, and all his loose papers had been closely examined, and had then been left unharmed to be resumed by the owner.", "허나, 그 물건들을 모아 보니, 없어진 것은 아무것도 없었다. 그의 회중시계, 시곗줄, 지갑, 열쇠, 손수건, 수첩, 그리고 낱장의 종이들이 죄다 꼼꼼히 살펴진 뒤, 주인이 되찾도록 손상 없이 남겨져 있었다."], ["In the same way, not the smallest morsel of property belonging to the proprietors of the house had been abstracted.", "마찬가지로, 그 집 주인들에게 딸린 소지품은 아주 조그만 부스러기 하나도 훔쳐지지 않았다."], ["The Oriental noblemen had removed their own illuminated manuscript, and had removed nothing else.", "그 동양 귀족들은 저희 자신의 장식된 필사본을 치워 갔을 뿐, 그 밖에는 아무것도 치워 가지 않았다."]], [["What did it mean?", "그것은 무슨 뜻이었을까?"], ["Taking the worldly point of view, it appeared to mean that Mr. Godfrey had been the victim of some incomprehensible error, committed by certain unknown men.", "세속적 관점을 취하자면, 그것은 고드프리 씨가 어떤 알 수 없는 자들이 저지른 어떤 이해할 수 없는 착오의 희생자가 되었다는 뜻인 듯싶었다."], ["A dark conspiracy was on foot in the midst of us; and our beloved and innocent friend had been entangled in its meshes.", "어두운 음모가 우리 한복판에서 진행되고 있었다. 그리고 우리의 사랑스럽고 죄 없는 벗이 그 그물코에 얽혀들었던 것이다."], ["When the Christian hero of a hundred charitable victories plunges into a pitfall that has been dug for him by mistake, oh, what a warning it is to the rest of us to be unceasingly on our guard!", "백 번의 자선의 승리를 거둔 기독교적 영웅이, 착오로 파인 함정에 곤두박질칠 때, 오, 그것은 우리 나머지에게 끊임없이 경계하라는 얼마나 큰 경고인가!"], ["How soon may our own evil passions prove to be Oriental noblemen who pounce on us unawares!", "우리 자신의 사악한 정념이 얼마나 이내 방심한 우리를 덮치는 동양 귀족으로 드러날 수 있는가!"]], [["I could write pages of affectionate warning on this one theme, but (alas!) I am not permitted to improve—I am condemned to narrate.", "나는 이 한 가지 주제에 대해 애정 어린 경고를 몇 쪽이고 쓸 수 있으련만, (아아!) 나는 교화하는 것이 허락되지 않는다—나는 서술하도록 선고받았다."], ["My wealthy relative’s cheque—henceforth, the incubus of my existence—warns me that I have not done with this record of violence yet. We must leave Mr.", "나의 부유한 친척의 수표가—이제부터, 나의 존재를 짓누르는 악몽이거니와—내게 아직 이 폭력의 기록을 끝내지 못했음을 일깨운다."], ["Godfrey to recover in Northumberland Street, and must follow the proceedings of Mr. Luker at a later period of the day.", "우리는 고드프리 씨를 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서 회복하도록 남겨 두고, 그날 늦은 시각의 루커 씨의 행적을 따라가야 한다."]], [["After leaving the bank, Mr. Luker had visited various parts of London on business errands.", "은행을 나선 뒤, 루커 씨는 볼일로 런던의 여러 곳을 다녔다."], ["Returning to his own residence, he found a letter waiting for him, which was described as having been left a short time previously by a boy. In this case, as in Mr.", "제 저택으로 돌아오니, 그는 자기를 기다리는 편지 한 통을 발견했는데, 그것은 얼마 전 어느 사내아이가 두고 갔다고 전해졌다."], ["Godfrey’s case, the handwriting was strange; but the name mentioned was the name of one of Mr.", "이 경우에도, 고드프리 씨의 경우에서와 마찬가지로, 그 필적은 낯설었다."], ["Luker’s customers. His correspondent announced (writing in the third person—apparently by the hand of a deputy) that he had been unexpectedly summoned to London.", "허나 거기 적힌 이름은 루커 씨의 고객 가운데 하나의 이름이었다."], ["He had just established himself in lodgings in Alfred Place, Tottenham Court Road; and he desired to see Mr.", "그의 편지 상대는 (삼인칭으로 쓰면서—보아하니 대필자의 손을 빌려) 자기가 뜻밖에 런던으로 불려 왔다고 알렸다."], ["Luker immediately, on the subject of a purchase which he contemplated making.", "그는 방금 토트넘 코트 로드의 앨프리드 플레이스에 숙소를 정한 참이었다."], ["The gentleman was an enthusiastic collector of Oriental antiquities, and had been for many years a liberal patron of the establishment in Lambeth.", "그리고 그는, 자기가 하려는 어떤 구매라는 화제에 관하여, 루커 씨를 즉시 만나고 싶어 했다. 그 신사는 동양 골동품의 열렬한 수집가였고, 여러 해 동안 램버스의 그 상점의 후한 단골이었다."], ["Oh, when shall we wean ourselves from the worship of Mammon!", "오, 우리는 언제쯤 맘몬 숭배에서 젖을 뗄 것인가!"], ["Mr. Luker called a cab, and drove off instantly to his liberal patron.", "루커 씨는 삯마차를 불러, 대뜸 제 후한 단골에게로 몰고 갔다."]], [["Exactly what had happened to Mr. Godfrey in Northumberland Street now happened to Mr.", "노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서 고드프리 씨에게 일어났던 바로 그 일이 이제 앨프리드 플레이스에서 루커 씨에게 일어났다."], ["Luker in Alfred Place. Once more the respectable man answered the door, and showed the visitor upstairs into the back drawing-room.", "다시 한번 그 점잖은 사내가 문을 열어 주고, 방문객을 위층 뒤편 응접실로 안내했다."], ["There, again, lay the illuminated manuscript on a table. Mr.", "거기에, 또, 그 장식된 필사본이 탁자 위에 놓여 있었다."], ["Luker’s attention was absorbed, as Mr. Godfrey’s attention had been absorbed, by this beautiful work of Indian art.", "루커 씨의 관심은, 고드프리 씨의 관심이 사로잡혔던 것처럼, 이 아름다운 인도 예술품에 사로잡혔다."], ["He too was aroused from his studies by a tawny naked arm round his throat, by a bandage over his eyes, and by a gag in his mouth.", "그도 제 목을 휘감는 황갈색의 벌거벗은 팔에, 제 눈을 덮는 눈가리개에, 그리고 제 입에 물리는 재갈에 제 궁리에서 화들짝 깨어났다."], ["He too was thrown prostrate and searched to the skin.", "그도 엎어뜨려져 살갗까지 뒤져졌다."], ["A longer interval had then elapsed than had passed in the experience of Mr.", "그런 뒤 고드프리 씨의 경험에서 지났던 것보다 더 긴 동안이 흘렀다."], ["Godfrey; but it had ended as before, in the persons of the house suspecting something wrong, and going upstairs to see what had happened.", "허나 그것도 앞서처럼, 그 집 사람들이 무언가 잘못되었다고 의심하고, 무슨 일이 일어났는지 보려고 위층으로 올라가는 것으로 끝났다."], ["Precisely the same explanation which the landlord in Northumberland Street had given to Mr. Godfrey, the landlord in Alfred Place now gave to Mr.", "노섬벌랜드 스트리트의 주인이 고드프리 씨에게 했던 바로 그 해명을, 앨프리드 플레이스의 주인이 이제 루커 씨에게 했다."], ["Luker. Both had been imposed on in the same way by the plausible address and well-filled purse of the respectable stranger, who introduced himself as acting for his foreign friends.", "둘 다, 제 외국인 벗들을 대신해 행세한다고 자기를 소개한 그 점잖은 낯선 이의 그럴듯한 언사와 두둑한 지갑에 똑같은 식으로 속아 넘어간 것이었다."], ["The one point of difference between the two cases occurred when the scattered contents of Mr. Luker’s pockets were being collected from the floor.", "두 경우 사이의 단 하나의 차이점은 루커 씨의 호주머니에서 쏟아진 것들이 바닥에서 그러모아질 때 나타났다."], ["His watch and purse were safe, but (less fortunate than Mr. Godfrey) one of the loose papers that he carried about him had been taken away.", "그의 회중시계와 지갑은 무사했으나, (고드프리 씨보다는 운이 덜 좋게도) 그가 몸에 지니고 다니던 낱장의 종이 하나가 없어졌다."], ["The paper in question acknowledged the receipt of a valuable of great price which Mr. Luker had that day left in the care of his bankers.", "문제의 그 종이는 루커 씨가 그날 제 은행에 맡겨 둔, 값이 대단히 비싼 어느 귀중품의 수령을 증명하는 것이었다."], ["This document would be useless for purposes of fraud, inasmuch as it provided that the valuable should only be given up on the personal application of the owner.", "이 문서는, 그 귀중품이 오직 주인 본인이 직접 청할 때에만 내어진다고 규정하고 있었으므로, 사기의 목적으로는 쓸모가 없을 터였다."], ["As soon as he recovered himself, Mr. Luker hurried to the bank, on the chance that the thieves who had robbed him might ignorantly present themselves with the receipt.", "정신을 차리자마자, 루커 씨는, 자기를 턴 그 도둑들이 아무것도 모르고 그 수령증을 들고 나타날지 모른다는 요행을 노리고, 은행으로 서둘러 갔다."], ["Nothing had been seen of them when he arrived at the establishment, and nothing was seen of them afterwards.", "그가 그 은행에 다다랐을 때 그들의 자취는 보이지 않았고, 그 뒤로도 그들의 자취는 보이지 않았다."], ["Their respectable English friend had (in the opinion of the bankers) looked the receipt over before they attempted to make use of it, and had given them the necessary warning in good time.", "그들의 점잖은 영국인 벗이 (은행 사람들의 견해로는) 그들이 그것을 써먹으려 시도하기 전에 그 수령증을 훑어보고, 제때에 그들에게 필요한 경고를 해 준 것이었다."]], [["Information of both outrages was communicated to the police, and the needful investigations were pursued, I believe, with great energy.", "두 폭행에 대한 정보가 경찰에 전해졌고, 필요한 조사가, 내가 믿기로는, 아주 힘차게 이루어졌다."], ["The authorities held that a robbery had been planned, on insufficient information received by the thieves.", "당국은 그 도둑들이 불충분한 정보를 받아 절도를 꾸몄다고 보았다."], ["They had been plainly not sure whether Mr. Luker had, or had not, trusted the transmission of his precious gem to another person; and poor polite Mr.", "그들은 루커 씨가 제 귀한 보석의 이송을 다른 사람에게 맡겼는지 아닌지를 뻔히 확신하지 못했던 것이다."], ["Godfrey had paid the penalty of having been seen accidentally speaking to him. Add to this, that Mr.", "그리고 가엾은 정중한 고드프리 씨는 우연히 그와 이야기하는 것이 목격된 대가를 치른 것이었다."], ["Godfrey’s absence from our Monday evening meeting had been occasioned by a consultation of the authorities, at which he was requested to assist—and all the explanations required being now given, I may proceed with the simpler story of my own little personal experiences in Montagu Square.", "여기에 덧붙이자면, 고드프리 씨가 우리의 월요일 저녁 모임에 나오지 못한 것은 당국의 협의 탓이었는데, 그는 그 자리에서 협조해 달라는 청을 받았던 것이다—그리고 필요한 온갖 해명을 이제 다 했으니, 나는 몬터규 스퀘어에서의 나 자신의 조그만 개인적 경험이라는 더 단순한 이야기로 나아가도 되겠다."]], [["I was punctual to the luncheon hour on Tuesday.", "나는 화요일 점심 시각에 어김없이 갔다."], ["Reference to my diary shows this to have been a chequered day—much in it to be devoutly regretted, much in it to be devoutly thankful for.", "나의 일기를 살펴보니 이날은 이런저런 일이 뒤섞인 날이었음을 알 수 있다—그 안에 경건히 뉘우칠 일도 많고, 그 안에 경건히 감사할 일도 많은 날이었다."]], [["Dear Aunt Verinder received me with her usual grace and kindness.", "사랑하는 버린더 이모님은 여느 때의 우아함과 다정함으로 나를 맞으셨다."], ["But I noticed, after a little while, that something was wrong.", "허나 나는, 잠시 뒤, 무언가 잘못되었음을 알아챘다."], ["Certain anxious looks escaped my aunt, all of which took the direction of her daughter.", "이모님에게서 어떤 근심 어린 눈길이 새어 나오곤 했는데, 그 모두가 당신 따님 쪽을 향했다."], ["I never see Rachel myself without wondering how it can be that so insignificant-looking a person should be the child of such distinguished parents as Sir John and Lady Verinder.", "나는 레이철을 볼 때마다 저토록 보잘것없어 보이는 사람이 어떻게 존 버린더 경과 레이디 버린더 같은 그토록 저명한 부모의 자식일 수 있는지 의아해하지 않은 적이 없다."], ["On this occasion, however, she not only disappointed—she really shocked me.", "허나 이번에는, 그 아이가 나를 실망시켰을 뿐만 아니라—정말로 나를 충격에 빠뜨렸다."], ["There was an absence of all lady-like restraint in her language and manner most painful to see.", "그 아이의 말과 몸가짐에는 숙녀다운 절제라곤 죄다 없어서 보기에 더없이 고통스러웠다."], ["She was possessed by some feverish excitement which made her distressingly loud when she laughed, and sinfully wasteful and capricious in what she ate and drank at lunch.", "그 아이는 어떤 열병 같은 흥분에 사로잡혀 있어, 웃을 때는 딱하게도 목소리가 컸고, 점심에 먹고 마시는 것에 있어서는 죄스럽게도 헤프고 변덕스러웠다."], ["I felt deeply for her poor mother, even before the true state of the case had been confidentially made known to me.", "나는, 이 일의 참된 실상이 내게 은밀히 알려지기도 전에, 그 아이의 가엾은 어머니를 위해 깊이 마음 아파했다."]], [["Luncheon over, my aunt said: “Remember what the doctor told you, Rachel, about quieting yourself with a book after taking your meals.”", "점심이 끝나자, 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"레이철, 의사가 네게 이른 말을 기억하렴, 밥 먹은 뒤에는 책으로 마음을 가라앉히라던 그 말 말이야.\""]], [["“I’ll go into the library, mamma,”", "\"서재로 갈게요, 엄마.\""], ["she answered.", "하고 그 아이가 대답했다."], ["“But if Godfrey calls, mind I am told of it.", "\"그런데 고드프리가 오거든, 제게 꼭 알려 주세요."], ["I am dying for more news of him, after his adventure in Northumberland Street.”", "노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서의 그 봉변 뒤로, 저는 그이의 소식을 더 듣고 싶어 죽겠어요.\""], ["She kissed her mother on the forehead, and looked my way.", "그 아이는 제 어머니의 이마에 입을 맞추고, 내 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["“Good-bye, Clack,”", "\"잘 있어요, 클랙.\""], ["she said, carelessly.", "하고 그 아이가 아무렇지도 않게 말했다."], ["Her insolence roused no angry feeling in me; I only made a private memorandum to pray for her.", "그 아이의 무례함은 내 안에 아무런 노여운 심정도 불러일으키지 않았다. 나는 다만 그 아이를 위해 기도하리라고 남몰래 마음속에 적어 두었다."]], [["When we were left by ourselves, my aunt told me the whole horrible story of the Indian Diamond, which, I am happy to know, it is not necessary to repeat here.", "우리 둘만 남겨지자, 이모님은 내게 그 인도 다이아몬드에 대한 그 온갖 끔찍한 이야기를 들려주셨는데, 나는 그것을 여기서 되풀이할 필요가 없다는 것을 알아 기쁘다."], ["She did not conceal from me that she would have preferred keeping silence on the subject.", "이모님은 그 화제에 대해 침묵을 지키는 편을 좋아하셨으리라는 것을 내게 숨기지 않으셨다."], ["But when her own servants all knew of the loss of the Moonstone, and when some of the circumstances had actually found their way into the newspapers—when strangers were speculating whether there was any connection between what had happened at Lady Verinder’s country house, and what had happened in Northumberland Street and Alfred Place—concealment was not to be thought of; and perfect frankness became a necessity as well as a virtue.", "허나 당신 하인들이 죄다 월장석의 분실을 알고 있고, 그 정황 가운데 얼마쯤이 실제로 신문에까지 흘러들어 간 마당에—낯선 이들이, 레이디 버린더의 시골집에서 일어난 일과 노섬벌랜드 스트리트와 앨프리드 플레이스에서 일어난 일 사이에 무슨 관련이 있는지 억측하고 있는 마당에—감추는 것은 생각조차 할 수 없었다. 그리고 온전한 솔직함이 미덕이자 필요가 되었다."]], [["Some persons, hearing what I now heard, would have been probably overwhelmed with astonishment.", "지금 내가 들은 것을 들었다면, 어떤 사람들은 아마 놀라움에 압도되었으리라."], ["For my own part, knowing Rachel’s spirit to have been essentially unregenerate from her childhood upwards, I was prepared for whatever my aunt could tell me on the subject of her daughter.", "나로 말하자면, 레이철의 성정이 어린 시절부터 죽 본질적으로 거듭나지 못한 것임을 알고 있는 터라, 나는 이모님이 당신 따님이라는 화제에 대해 내게 이야기할 수 있는 그 무엇에도 마음의 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["It might have gone on from bad to worse till it ended in Murder; and I should still have said to myself, The natural result!", "그것이 나쁜 데서 더 나쁜 데로 나아가 마침내 살인으로 끝났을 수도 있으련만, 그래도 나는 여전히 혼잣말했으리라, 당연한 결과지!"], ["oh, dear, dear, the natural result!", "오, 저런, 저런, 당연한 결과야!"], ["The one thing that did shock me was the course my aunt had taken under the circumstances.", "정말로 나를 충격에 빠뜨린 단 한 가지는 그 정황 아래 이모님이 취한 방침이었다."], ["Here surely was a case for a clergyman, if ever there was one yet!", "여기야말로, 여태껏 그런 경우가 있었다면, 참으로 목사님이 필요한 경우가 아니겠는가!"], ["Lady Verinder had thought it a case for a physician.", "레이디 버린더는 그것을 의사가 필요한 경우로 여기셨다."], ["All my poor aunt’s early life had been passed in her father’s godless household.", "나의 가엾은 이모님의 어린 시절이 죄다 당신 아버지의 신을 모르는 집안에서 지나갔던 것이다."], ["The natural result again!", "또다시 당연한 결과!"], ["Oh, dear, dear, the natural result again!", "오, 저런, 저런, 또다시 당연한 결과!"]], [["“The doctors recommend plenty of exercise and amusement for Rachel, and strongly urge me to keep her mind as much as possible from dwelling on the past,”", "\"의사들은 레이철에게 운동과 놀이를 많이 하라고 권하고, 그 아이의 마음이 될 수 있는 대로 지난 일에 머물지 않게 하라고 내게 강하게 당부하는구나.\""], ["said Lady Verinder.", "하고 레이디 버린더가 말씀하셨다."]], [["“Oh, what heathen advice!”", "\"오, 이 무슨 이교도 같은 조언인가!\""], ["I thought to myself.", "하고 나는 혼잣말했다."], ["“In this Christian country, what heathen advice!”", "\"이 기독교의 나라에서, 이 무슨 이교도 같은 조언인가!\""]], [["My aunt went on, “I do my best to carry out my instructions.", "이모님이 말을 이으셨다. \"나는 그 당부를 지키려고 최선을 다한단다."], ["But this strange adventure of Godfrey’s happens at a most unfortunate time.", "그런데 고드프리의 이 기이한 봉변이 더없이 불운한 때에 일어났어."], ["Rachel has been incessantly restless and excited since she first heard of it.", "레이철은 그 소식을 처음 들은 뒤로 쉼 없이 안절부절못하고 흥분해 있어."], ["She left me no peace till I had written and asked my nephew Ablewhite to come here.", "그 아이는 내가 조카 에이블화이트에게 편지를 써서 이리로 와 달라 청할 때까지 나를 가만두지 않았지."], ["She even feels an interest in the other person who was roughly used—Mr. Luker, or some such name—though the man is, of course, a total stranger to her.”", "그 아이는 심지어 거칠게 다루어진 다른 사람에게도—루커 씨라던가, 그런 이름의 사람에게도—관심을 느껴, 물론 그 사내가 그 아이에게는 전혀 낯선 사람인데도 말이야.\""]], [["“Your knowledge of the world, dear aunt, is superior to mine,”", "\"세상에 대한 이모님의 앎이 저의 앎보다 나으시지요, 사랑하는 이모님.\""], ["I suggested diffidently.", "하고 나는 조심스레 넌지시 말했다."], ["“But there must be a reason surely for this extraordinary conduct on Rachel’s part.", "\"허나 레이철 편의 이 예사롭지 않은 행동에는 분명 무슨 까닭이 있을 거예요."], ["She is keeping a sinful secret from you and from everybody.", "그 아이는 이모님과 모두에게 죄스러운 비밀을 감추고 있어요."], ["May there not be something in these recent events which threatens her secret with discovery?”", "이 최근의 일들 가운데, 그 아이의 비밀을 발각의 위협에 빠뜨리는 무언가가 있는 것은 아닐까요?\""]], [["“Discovery?”", "\"발각이라고?\""], ["repeated my aunt.", "하고 이모님이 되뇌셨다."], ["“What can you possibly mean?", "\"대체 무슨 뜻이니?"], ["Discovery through Mr. Luker?", "루커 씨를 통한 발각?"], ["Discovery through my nephew?”", "내 조카를 통한 발각?\""]], [["As the word passed her lips, a special providence occurred.", "그 말이 이모님의 입술을 지나는 순간, 특별한 섭리가 일어났다."], ["The servant opened the door, and announced Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "하인이 문을 열고, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 방문을 알렸다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["Mr. Godfrey followed the announcement of his name—as Mr.", "고드프리 씨는 제 이름이 불리는 것을 뒤따라—고드프리 씨가 다른 모든 일을 하듯이—꼭 알맞은 때에 들어섰다."], ["Godfrey does everything else—exactly at the right time. He was not so close on the servant’s heels as to startle us.", "그는 우리를 놀라게 할 만큼 하인의 발뒤꿈치에 바싹 붙어 있지도 않았다."], ["He was not so far behind as to cause us the double inconvenience of a pause and an open door.", "그는 우리에게 멈칫하는 순간과 열린 문이라는 이중의 불편을 끼칠 만큼 뒤처져 있지도 않았다."], ["It is in the completeness of his daily life that the true Christian appears.", "참된 기독교인이 드러나는 것은 그 나날의 삶의 완벽함에서다."], ["This dear man was very complete.", "이 사랑스러운 사람은 아주 완벽했다."]], [["“Go to Miss Verinder,”", "\"버린더 아가씨께 가서,\""], ["said my aunt, addressing the servant, “and tell her Mr.", "하고 이모님이 하인에게 이르셨다."], ["Ablewhite is here.”", "\"에이블화이트 씨가 오셨다고 전하렴.\""]], [["We both inquired after his health.", "우리는 둘 다 그의 건강을 물었다."], ["We both asked him together whether he felt like himself again, after his terrible adventure of the past week.", "우리는 둘 다 함께, 지난주의 그 끔찍한 봉변 뒤로, 그가 다시 제 몸 같은지를 물었다."], ["With perfect tact, he contrived to answer us at the same moment.", "더없는 재치로, 그는 용케 우리 둘에게 동시에 답했다."], ["Lady Verinder had his reply in words.", "레이디 버린더는 그의 대답을 말로 받으셨다."], ["I had his charming smile.", "나는 그의 매혹적인 미소를 받았다."]], [["“What,”", "\"제가,\""], ["he cried, with infinite tenderness, “have I done to deserve all this sympathy?", "하고 그가, 한없는 다정함으로 외쳤다. \"이 온갖 동정을 받을 무슨 일을 했다는 겁니까?"], ["My dear aunt!", "사랑하는 이모님!"], ["my dear Miss Clack!", "사랑하는 클랙 양!"], ["I have merely been mistaken for somebody else.", "저는 그저 다른 누군가로 잘못 여겨졌을 뿐입니다."], ["I have only been blindfolded; I have only been strangled; I have only been thrown flat on my back, on a very thin carpet, covering a particularly hard floor.", "저는 그저 눈이 가려졌을 뿐이고, 저는 그저 목이 졸렸을 뿐이고, 저는 그저 유난히 딱딱한 바닥을 덮은 아주 얇은 양탄자 위에 벌렁 나자빠졌을 뿐입니다."], ["Just think how much worse it might have been!", "그것이 얼마나 더 나빴을 수 있었을지 한번 생각해 보세요!"], ["I might have been murdered; I might have been robbed.", "저는 살해되었을 수도 있고, 저는 강도를 당했을 수도 있습니다."], ["What have I lost?", "제가 무엇을 잃었나요?"], ["Nothing but Nervous Force—which the law doesn’t recognise as property; so that, strictly speaking, I have lost nothing at all.", "신경의 기운 말고는 아무것도 잃지 않았지요—그리고 그것은 법이 재산으로 인정하지 않으니, 엄밀히 말하면, 저는 아무것도 잃지 않은 셈입니다."], ["If I could have had my own way, I would have kept my adventure to myself—I shrink from all this fuss and publicity. But Mr.", "제 마음대로 할 수 있었다면, 저는 제 봉변을 저 혼자만 간직했을 겁니다—저는 이 온갖 소동과 세상의 이목이 질색이거든요."], ["Luker made his injuries public, and my injuries, as the necessary consequence, have been proclaimed in their turn.", "허나 루커 씨가 제 피해를 세상에 알렸고, 그리하여 제 피해도, 그 당연한 결과로, 제 차례가 되어 알려진 것이지요."], ["I have become the property of the newspapers, until the gentle reader gets sick of the subject.", "저는 점잖은 독자들이 이 화제에 물릴 때까지, 신문의 소유물이 되었습니다."], ["I am very sick indeed of it myself.", "저 자신도 참으로 그것에 물렸고요."], ["May the gentle reader soon be like me!", "부디 점잖은 독자들도 어서 저처럼 되기를!"], ["And how is dear Rachel?", "그런데 사랑하는 레이철은 어떻습니까?"], ["Still enjoying the gaieties of London?", "여전히 런던의 흥겨움을 즐기고 있고요?"], ["So glad to hear it!", "그 말을 들으니 참 기쁩니다!"], ["Miss Clack, I need all your indulgence.", "클랙 양, 저는 당신의 온갖 너그러움이 필요합니다."], ["I am sadly behind-hand with my Committee Work and my dear Ladies.", "저는 제 위원회 일과 제 사랑하는 부인들 일에 서글프게도 뒤처져 있어요."], ["But I really do hope to look in at the Mothers’-Small-Clothes next week.", "허나 저는 다음 주에는 정말 '어머니회 아동복' 모임에 들러 볼 셈입니다."], ["Did you make cheering progress at Monday’s Committee?", "월요일 위원회에서는 기쁜 진전이 있었습니까?"], ["Was the Board hopeful about future prospects?", "이사회는 앞날의 전망에 대해 희망적이었고요?"], ["And are we nicely off for Trousers?”", "그리고 우리는 바지 형편이 넉넉한가요?\""]], [["The heavenly gentleness of his smile made his apologies irresistible.", "그의 미소의 그 천상 같은 부드러움이 그의 사과를 거스를 수 없게 만들었다."], ["The richness of his deep voice added its own indescribable charm to the interesting business question which he had just addressed to me.", "그의 굵은 목소리의 풍성함이, 그가 방금 내게 건넨 그 흥미로운 볼일에 관한 물음에 저 나름의 형언할 수 없는 매력을 더했다."], ["In truth, we were almost too nicely off for Trousers; we were quite overwhelmed by them.", "사실인즉, 우리는 바지 형편이 거의 지나칠 만큼 넉넉했다. 우리는 그것들에 아주 파묻힐 지경이었다."], ["I was just about to say so, when the door opened again, and an element of worldly disturbance entered the room, in the person of Miss Verinder.", "나는 막 그렇게 말하려던 참이었는데, 그때 문이 다시 열리고, 세속적 소란의 한 요소가, 버린더 아가씨라는 사람의 모습으로, 방에 들어왔다."]], [["She approached dear Mr. Godfrey at a most unladylike rate of speed, with her hair shockingly untidy, and her face, what I should call, unbecomingly flushed.", "그 아이는 더없이 숙녀답지 못한 속도로 사랑하는 고드프리 씨에게 다가갔는데, 머리는 충격적으로 헝클어져 있고, 얼굴은, 내가 말하자면, 볼썽사납게 달아올라 있었다."]], [["“I am charmed to see you, Godfrey,”", "\"만나서 반가워요, 고드프리.\""], ["she said, addressing him, I grieve to add, in the off-hand manner of one young man talking to another.", "하고 그 아이가, 덧붙이기 서글프거니와, 한 젊은 사내가 다른 젊은 사내에게 말하는 그 스스럼없는 태도로 그에게 말했다."], ["“I wish you had brought Mr. Luker with you.", "\"루커 씨를 함께 데려왔으면 좋았을걸."], ["You and he (as long as our present excitement lasts) are the two most interesting men in all London.", "당신하고 그이는 (지금 우리의 이 흥분이 이어지는 한) 온 런던에서 가장 흥미로운 두 사내예요. 이런 말을 하는 건 병적이지요."], ["It’s morbid to say this; it’s unhealthy; it’s all that a well-regulated mind like Miss Clack’s most instinctively shudders at.", "건강하지 못하고요. 클랙 양 같은 반듯한 마음이 본능적으로 몸서리치는 그 모든 것이고요."], ["Never mind that.", "그런 건 아무래도 좋아요."], ["Tell me the whole of the Northumberland Street story directly.", "노섬벌랜드 스트리트 이야기를 죄다 당장 들려줘요."], ["I know the newspapers have left some of it out.”", "신문들이 그중 얼마쯤을 빼놓았다는 걸 저는 알거든요.\""]], [["Even dear Mr. Godfrey partakes of the fallen nature which we all inherit from Adam—it is a very small share of our human legacy, but, alas!", "사랑하는 고드프리 씨조차 우리 모두가 아담에게서 물려받은 그 타락한 본성을 나누어 지녔다—그것은 우리 인간 유산의 아주 작은 몫이지만, 아아!"], ["he has it.", "그도 그것을 지녔다."], ["I confess it grieved me to see him take Rachel’s hand in both of his own hands, and lay it softly on the left side of his waistcoat.", "그가 레이철의 손을 제 두 손으로 잡아, 제 조끼 왼편에 부드럽게 갖다 대는 것을 보고 마음이 아팠음을 나는 고백한다."], ["It was a direct encouragement to her reckless way of talking, and her insolent reference to me.", "그것은 그 아이의 무모한 말투와, 나를 향한 무례한 언급에 대한 직접적인 부추김이었다."]], [["“Dearest Rachel,”", "\"더없이 사랑하는 레이철,\""], ["he said, in the same voice which had thrilled me when he spoke of our prospects and our trousers, “the newspapers have told you everything—and they have told it much better than I can.”", "하고 그가, 우리의 전망과 우리의 바지에 대해 말할 때 나를 전율케 했던 바로 그 목소리로 말했다. \"신문들이 당신에게 죄다 이야기했고—내가 할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 이야기했어요.\""]], [["“Godfrey thinks we all make too much of the matter,”", "\"고드프리는 우리 모두가 이 일을 너무 크게 여긴다고 생각한단다.\""], ["my aunt remarked.", "하고 이모님이 짚으셨다."], ["“He has just been saying that he doesn’t care to speak of it.”", "\"그 애는 방금 그것을 이야기하고 싶지 않다고 말하던 참이야.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["She put the question with a sudden flash in her eyes, and a sudden look up into Mr. Godfrey’s face.", "그 아이는 눈에 별안간 번득임을 띠고, 별안간 고드프리 씨의 얼굴을 올려다보며 그 물음을 던졌다."], ["On his side, he looked down at her with an indulgence so injudicious and so ill-deserved, that I really felt called on to interfere.", "그의 쪽에서는, 어찌나 분별없고 어찌나 받을 자격 없는 너그러움으로 그 아이를 내려다보던지, 나는 정말이지 끼어들어야겠다는 느낌이 들었다."]], [["“Rachel, darling!”", "\"레이철, 얘야!\""], ["I remonstrated gently, “true greatness and true courage are ever modest.”", "하고 나는 부드럽게 타일렀다. \"참된 위대함과 참된 용기는 언제나 겸손하단다.\""]], [["“You are a very good fellow in your way, Godfrey,”", "\"당신은 당신 나름으로 아주 좋은 사람이에요, 고드프리.\""], ["she said—not taking the smallest notice, observe, of me, and still speaking to her cousin as if she was one young man addressing another.", "하고 그 아이가—보시라, 나는 조금도 아랑곳하지 않고, 여전히 한 젊은 사내가 다른 젊은 사내에게 말하듯 제 사촌에게 말하며—말했다. \"하지만 당신이 위대하지 않다는 건 제가 아주 확실히 알아요."], ["“But I am quite sure you are not great; I don’t believe you possess any extraordinary courage; and I am firmly persuaded—if you ever had any modesty—that your lady-worshippers relieved you of that virtue a good many years since.", "당신이 무슨 대단한 용기를 지녔다고는 믿지 않고요. 그리고 저는 굳게 믿어요—당신에게 겸손이라는 게 조금이라도 있었다면—당신의 그 부인 숭배자들이 여러 해 전에 그 미덕을 당신에게서 벗겨 갔으리라고요."], ["You have some private reason for not talking of your adventure in Northumberland Street; and I mean to know it.”", "당신은 노섬벌랜드 스트리트의 봉변을 이야기하지 않을 무슨 남모를 까닭이 있어요. 그리고 저는 그걸 알아낼 셈이에요.\""]], [["“My reason is the simplest imaginable, and the most easily acknowledged,”", "\"제 까닭은 상상할 수 있는 가장 단순한 것이고, 가장 손쉽게 인정할 수 있는 것이에요.\""], ["he answered, still bearing with her.", "하고 그가, 여전히 그 아이를 참아 주며 대답했다."], ["“I am tired of the subject.”", "\"저는 그 화제에 물렸어요.\""]], [["“You are tired of the subject?", "\"당신이 그 화제에 물렸다고요?"], ["My dear Godfrey, I am going to make a remark.”", "사랑하는 고드프리, 제가 소견을 하나 말할게요.\""]], [["“What is it?”", "\"뭔데요?\""]], [["“You live a great deal too much in the society of women.", "\"당신은 너무 지나치게 여자들 무리 속에서 살아요."], ["And you have contracted two very bad habits in consequence.", "그리고 그 결과로 아주 나쁜 버릇 두 가지가 들었고요."], ["You have learnt to talk nonsense seriously, and you have got into a way of telling fibs for the pleasure of telling them.", "당신은 헛소리를 진지하게 하는 걸 익혔고, 재미 삼아 거짓말을 늘어놓는 버릇이 들었어요."], ["You can’t go straight with your lady-worshippers.", "당신은 그 부인 숭배자들에게는 곧이곧대로 갈 수가 없죠."], ["I mean to make you go straight with me.", "저는 당신이 저에게만은 곧이곧대로 가게 만들 셈이에요."], ["Come, and sit down.", "이리 와서, 앉아요."], ["I am brimful of downright questions; and I expect you to be brimful of downright answers.”", "저는 대놓고 묻는 물음들로 가득 차 있고, 당신은 대놓고 답하는 대답들로 가득 차 있기를 바라요.\""]], [["She actually dragged him across the room to a chair by the window, where the light would fall on his face.", "그 아이는 실제로 그를 방 저편, 빛이 그의 얼굴에 떨어질 창가의 의자로 끌고 갔다."], ["I deeply feel being obliged to report such language, and to describe such conduct.", "나는 이런 말을 옮기고, 이런 행동을 묘사해야 하는 것을 깊이 마음 아파한다."], ["But, hemmed in, as I am, between Mr. Franklin Blake’s cheque on one side and my own sacred regard for truth on the other, what am I to do?", "허나, 한쪽에는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 수표가, 다른 쪽에는 진실에 대한 나 자신의 성스러운 존중이 있어, 이렇게 옴짝달싹 못하게 끼여 있는 마당에, 나로서는 어쩌란 말인가?"], ["I looked at my aunt.", "나는 이모님을 바라보았다."], ["She sat unmoved; apparently in no way disposed to interfere.", "이모님은 꿈쩍도 않고 앉아 계셨다. 보아하니 조금도 끼어들 뜻이 없으신 듯했다."], ["I had never noticed this kind of torpor in her before.", "나는 여태껏 이모님에게서 이런 종류의 무기력을 본 적이 없었다."], ["It was, perhaps, the reaction after the trying time she had had in the country.", "그것은, 어쩌면, 시골에서 겪으신 그 힘겨운 시절 뒤의 반작용이었으리라."], ["Not a pleasant symptom to remark, be it what it might, at dear Lady Verinder’s age, and with dear Lady Verinder’s autumnal exuberance of figure.", "그것이 무엇이든 간에, 사랑하는 레이디 버린더의 연세에, 그리고 사랑하는 레이디 버린더의 그 무르익은 풍만한 몸매로 보아, 짚어 두기에 유쾌한 징후는 아니었다."]], [["In the meantime, Rachel had settled herself at the window with our amiable and forbearing—our too forbearing—Mr. Godfrey.", "그러는 동안, 레이철은 우리의 상냥하고 잘 참아 주는—너무 잘 참아 주는—고드프리 씨와 함께 창가에 자리를 잡았다."], ["She began the string of questions with which she had threatened him, taking no more notice of her mother, or of myself, than if we had not been in the room.", "그 아이는, 마치 우리가 그 방에 있지도 않은 듯 제 어머니나 나를 더는 아랑곳하지 않고, 그를 위협했던 그 줄줄이 이어지는 물음들을 시작했다."]], [["“Have the police done anything, Godfrey?”", "\"경찰이 뭐라도 했어요, 고드프리?\""]], [["“Nothing whatever.”", "\"아무것도 못 했어요.\""]], [["“It is certain, I suppose, that the three men who laid the trap for you were the same three men who afterwards laid the trap for Mr. Luker?”", "\"당신에게 덫을 놓은 그 세 사내가, 나중에 루커 씨에게 덫을 놓은 바로 그 세 사내라는 건, 틀림없겠죠?\""]], [["“Humanly speaking, my dear Rachel, there can be no doubt of it.”", "\"사람의 앎으로 말하자면, 사랑하는 레이철, 그건 의심할 여지가 없어요.\""]], [["“And not a trace of them has been discovered?”", "\"그런데 그들의 자취라곤 하나도 발견되지 않았고요?\""]], [["“Not a trace.”", "\"자취 하나 없어요.\""]], [["“It is thought—is it not?—that these three men are the three Indians who came to our house in the country.”", "\"그 세 사내가 시골 우리 집에 왔던 그 인도인 셋이라고—여겨지죠, 안 그래요?\""]], [["“Some people think so.”", "\"그리 여기는 사람들도 있어요.\""]], [["“Do you think so?”", "\"당신은 그리 여겨요?\""]], [["“My dear Rachel, they blindfolded me before I could see their faces.", "\"사랑하는 레이철, 그들은 제가 그들 얼굴을 보기도 전에 제 눈을 가렸어요."], ["I know nothing whatever of the matter.", "저는 그 일에 대해 아무것도 몰라요."], ["How can I offer an opinion on it?”", "제가 어떻게 그것에 대해 의견을 낼 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["Even the angelic gentleness of Mr. Godfrey was, you see, beginning to give way at last under the persecution inflicted on him.", "보시다시피, 고드프리 씨의 그 천사 같은 부드러움조차, 그에게 가해지는 그 시달림 아래 마침내 무너지기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Whether unbridled curiosity, or ungovernable dread, dictated Miss Verinder’s questions I do not presume to inquire.", "고삐 풀린 호기심이 버린더 아가씨의 물음을 몰았는지, 아니면 다스릴 수 없는 두려움이 몰았는지, 나는 감히 캐묻지 않으련다."], ["I only report that, on Mr. Godfrey’s attempting to rise, after giving her the answer just described, she actually took him by the two shoulders, and pushed him back into his chair—Oh, don’t say this was immodest!", "나는 다만, 방금 묘사한 그 대답을 그 아이에게 준 뒤 고드프리 씨가 일어서려 하자, 그 아이가 실제로 그의 두 어깨를 붙들어, 그를 의자에 도로 밀어 넣었다는 것만 옮긴다—오, 이것이 정숙하지 못했다고 말하지 마시라!"], ["don’t even hint that the recklessness of guilty terror could alone account for such conduct as I have described!", "죄지은 자의 공포에서 오는 무모함만이 내가 묘사한 그런 행동을 설명할 수 있으리라고 넌지시라도 말하지 마시라!"], ["We must not judge others.", "우리는 남을 판단해서는 안 된다."], ["My Christian friends, indeed, indeed, indeed, we must not judge others!", "나의 기독교인 벗들이여, 정말로, 정말로, 정말로, 우리는 남을 판단해서는 안 된다!"]], [["She went on with her questions, unabashed.", "그 아이는 기죽지 않고 제 물음을 이어 갔다."], ["Earnest Biblical students will perhaps be reminded—as I was reminded—of the blinded children of the devil, who went on with their orgies, unabashed, in the time before the Flood.", "진지한 성경 학도라면 어쩌면 떠올릴 것이다—나도 떠올렸듯이—대홍수 이전 시절에, 기죽지 않고 제 광란의 잔치를 이어 가던 그 눈먼 악마의 자식들을 말이다."]], [["“I want to know something about Mr. Luker, Godfrey.”", "\"루커 씨에 대해 좀 알고 싶어요, 고드프리.\""]], [["“I am again unfortunate, Rachel.", "\"저는 또다시 운이 없군요, 레이철."], ["No man knows less of Mr. Luker than I do.”", "저보다 루커 씨를 덜 아는 사람은 없어요.\""]], [["“You never saw him before you and he met accidentally at the bank?”", "\"당신과 그이가 은행에서 우연히 마주치기 전에는 그이를 본 적이 없어요?\""]], [["“Never.”", "\"한 번도 없어요.\""]], [["“You have seen him since?”", "\"그 뒤로는 그이를 봤고요?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["We have been examined together, as well as separately, to assist the police.”", "경찰을 돕느라, 우리는 따로도, 함께도 조사를 받았어요.\""]], [["“Mr. Luker was robbed of a receipt which he had got from his banker’s—was he not?", "\"루커 씨는 제 은행에서 받은 수령증을 빼앗겼어요—그렇죠?"], ["What was the receipt for?”", "그 수령증은 무엇에 대한 거였죠?\""]], [["“For a valuable gem which he had placed in the safe keeping of the bank.”", "\"그가 은행에 안전하게 맡겨 둔 어느 값진 보석에 대한 것이었어요.\""]], [["“That’s what the newspapers say.", "\"그건 신문들이 하는 말이에요."], ["It may be enough for the general reader; but it is not enough for me.", "그건 여느 독자에게는 넉넉할지 모르지만, 저에게는 넉넉하지 않아요."], ["The banker’s receipt must have mentioned what the gem was?”", "그 은행 수령증에는 분명 그 보석이 무엇인지 적혀 있었을 텐데요?\""]], [["“The banker’s receipt, Rachel—as I have heard it described—mentioned nothing of the kind.", "\"그 은행 수령증에는, 레이철—내가 들어 묘사된 바로는—그런 건 아무것도 적혀 있지 않았어요."], ["A valuable gem, belonging to Mr.", "루커 씨에게 딸린 값진 보석 하나."], ["Luker; deposited by Mr.", "루커 씨가 맡김."], ["Luker; sealed with Mr.", "루커 씨의 봉인으로 봉해짐."], ["Luker’s seal; and only to be given up on Mr.", "그리고 오직 루커 씨 본인이 직접 청할 때에만 내어짐."], ["Luker’s personal application. That was the form, and that is all I know about it.”", "그것이 그 양식이었고, 그것이 내가 그에 대해 아는 전부예요.\""]], [["She waited a moment, after he had said that.", "그가 그 말을 하고 나서, 그 아이는 잠시 뜸을 들였다."], ["She looked at her mother, and sighed.", "그 아이는 제 어머니를 바라보고, 한숨지었다."], ["She looked back again at Mr. Godfrey, and went on.", "그 아이는 다시 고드프리 씨를 바라보고, 말을 이었다."]], [["“Some of our private affairs, at home,”", "\"우리 집안의 사사로운 일 가운데 얼마쯤이,\""], ["she said, “seem to have got into the newspapers?”", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"신문에 들어간 것 같은데요?\""]], [["“I grieve to say, it is so.”", "\"그렇다고 말하기 서글프지만, 그래요.\""]], [["“And some idle people, perfect strangers to us, are trying to trace a connexion between what happened at our house in Yorkshire and what has happened since, here in London?”", "\"그리고 우리에게는 전혀 낯선 어떤 한가한 사람들이, 요크셔의 우리 집에서 일어난 일과 그 뒤 여기 런던에서 일어난 일 사이의 관련을 캐내려 하고 있고요?\""]], [["“The public curiosity, in certain quarters, is, I fear, taking that turn.”", "\"세상의 호기심이, 어떤 부류에서는, 그렇게 방향을 트는 것 같아 걱정이에요.\""]], [["“The people who say that the three unknown men who ill-used you and Mr. Luker are the three Indians, also say that the valuable gem——”", "\"당신과 루커 씨를 험하게 다룬 그 낯선 세 사내가 그 인도인 셋이라고 말하는 사람들은, 또한 그 값진 보석이——\""]], [["There she stopped.", "거기서 그 아이는 멈췄다."], ["She had become gradually, within the last few moments, whiter and whiter in the face.", "그 아이는 지난 몇 순간 사이에 점점 더, 얼굴이 하얘지고 하얘졌다."], ["The extraordinary blackness of her hair made this paleness, by contrast, so ghastly to look at, that we all thought she would faint, at the moment when she checked herself in the middle of her question.", "그 아이의 유별나게 검은 머리칼이 이 창백함을, 그 대비로, 어찌나 섬뜩하게 보이게 하던지, 우리는 모두 그 아이가, 제 물음 한복판에서 스스로 말을 멈춘 그 순간, 기절하리라 여겼다."], ["Dear Mr. Godfrey made a second attempt to leave his chair.", "사랑하는 고드프리 씨가 두 번째로 의자를 떠나려 시도했다."], ["My aunt entreated her to say no more.", "이모님이 그 아이에게 더는 말하지 말라고 애원하셨다."], ["I followed my aunt with a modest medicinal peace-offering, in the shape of a bottle of salts.", "나는 각성제 한 병이라는 모양을 한 겸손한 약물의 화해 예물을 들고 이모님을 뒤따랐다."], ["We none of us produced the slightest effect on her.", "우리 가운데 누구도 그 아이에게 조금의 효험도 내지 못했다."], ["“Godfrey, stay where you are.", "\"고드프리, 그 자리에 그대로 있어요."], ["Mamma, there is not the least reason to be alarmed about me.", "엄마, 저에 대해선 조금도 놀라실 까닭이 없어요."], ["Clack, you’re dying to hear the end of it—I won’t faint, expressly to oblige you.”", "클랙, 당신은 이 끝을 듣고 싶어 죽겠지요—당신 비위를 딱히 맞춰 주려고, 저는 기절하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["Those were the exact words she used—taken down in my diary the moment I got home.", "그것이 그 아이가 쓴 바로 그 말이었다—내가 집에 다다르는 순간 나의 일기에 받아 적은 것이다."], ["But, oh, don’t let us judge!", "허나, 오, 우리 판단하지 말자!"], ["My Christian friends, don’t let us judge!", "나의 기독교인 벗들이여, 우리 판단하지 말자!"]], [["She turned once more to Mr. Godfrey.", "그 아이는 다시 한번 고드프리 씨에게로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["With an obstinacy dreadful to see, she went back again to the place where she had checked herself, and completed her question in these words:", "보기에 소름 끼치는 고집으로, 그 아이는 스스로 말을 멈추었던 그 자리로 도로 돌아가, 이런 말로 제 물음을 마무리했다."]], [["“I spoke to you, a minute since, about what people were saying in certain quarters.", "\"조금 전에, 어떤 부류에서 사람들이 뭐라 하는지에 대해 당신에게 말했죠."], ["Tell me plainly, Godfrey, do they any of them say that Mr. Luker’s valuable gem is—the Moonstone?”", "분명하게 말해 줘요, 고드프리, 그들 중 누구든, 루커 씨의 그 값진 보석이—월장석이라고 말하나요?\""]], [["As the name of the Indian Diamond passed her lips, I saw a change come over my admirable friend.", "그 인도 다이아몬드의 이름이 그 아이의 입술을 지나는 순간, 나는 나의 감탄스러운 벗에게 변화가 이는 것을 보았다."], ["His complexion deepened.", "그의 낯빛이 짙어졌다."], ["He lost the genial suavity of manner which is one of his greatest charms.", "그는 그의 가장 큰 매력 가운데 하나인 그 상냥하고 부드러운 태도를 잃었다."], ["A noble indignation inspired his reply.", "고결한 분노가 그의 대답을 불러일으켰다."]], [["“They do say it,” he answered.", "\"그들은 정말 그리 말해요.\""], ["“There are people who don’t hesitate to accuse Mr.", "하고 그가 대답했다."], ["Luker of telling a falsehood to serve some private interests of his own.", "\"루커 씨가 저 나름의 어떤 남모를 잇속을 위해 거짓을 말하고 있다고 서슴없이 몰아붙이는 사람들이 있어요."], ["He has over and over again solemnly declared that, until this scandal assailed him, he had never even heard of the Moonstone.", "그는 몇 번이고 거듭, 이 추문이 자기를 덮치기 전까지는 월장석에 대해 들어 본 적조차 없다고 엄숙히 단언했어요."], ["And these vile people reply, without a shadow of proof to justify them, He has his reasons for concealment; we decline to believe him on his oath.", "그런데 이 몹쓸 사람들은, 저희를 정당화할 증거의 그림자 하나 없이, 이렇게 대꾸하지요, 그가 감출 까닭이 있는 거다, 우리는 그의 맹세를 믿기를 거부한다고."], ["Shameful!", "수치스러워요!"], ["shameful!”", "수치스러워요!\""]], [["Rachel looked at him very strangely—I can’t well describe how—while he was speaking.", "레이철은 그가 말하는 동안 그를 아주 이상하게—어떻게라고 잘 묘사할 수가 없는데—바라보았다."], ["When he had done, she said, “Considering that Mr.", "그가 말을 마치자, 그 아이가 말했다."], ["Luker is only a chance acquaintance of yours, you take up his cause, Godfrey, rather warmly.”", "\"루커 씨가 그저 당신의 우연한 지인일 뿐임을 헤아려 보면, 당신은 그이의 편을, 고드프리, 제법 뜨겁게 드는군요.\""]], [["My gifted friend made her one of the most truly evangelical answers I ever heard in my life.", "나의 재능 있는 벗은 그 아이에게, 내가 내 평생 들어 본 가장 참으로 복음적인 대답 가운데 하나를 건넸다."]], [["“I hope, Rachel, I take up the cause of all oppressed people rather warmly,”", "\"저는, 레이철, 억눌린 모든 사람들의 편을 제법 뜨겁게 들기를 바라요.\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["The tone in which those words were spoken might have melted a stone.", "그 말이 뱉어진 그 어조는 돌이라도 녹였으리라."], ["But, oh dear, what is the hardness of stone?", "허나, 아 저런, 돌의 굳음이 무엇인가?"], ["Nothing, compared to the hardness of the unregenerate human heart!", "거듭나지 못한 인간 마음의 굳음에 견주면, 아무것도 아니다! 그 아이는 비웃었다."], ["She sneered. I blush to record it—she sneered at him to his face.", "이것을 적기가 낯 뜨겁지만—그 아이는 그의 얼굴에 대고 비웃었다."]], [["“Keep your noble sentiments for your Ladies’ Committees, Godfrey.", "\"당신의 고결한 감정은 당신의 부인 위원회들을 위해 간직해 둬요, 고드프리."], ["I am certain that the scandal which has assailed Mr. Luker, has not spared You.”", "루커 씨를 덮친 그 추문이, 당신도 봐주지 않았다는 걸 저는 확신해요.\""]], [["Even my aunt’s torpor was roused by those words.", "이모님의 무기력조차 그 말에 일깨워졌다."]], [["“My dear Rachel,”", "\"얘 레이철,\""], ["she remonstrated, “you have really no right to say that!”", "하고 이모님이 타이르셨다. \"너는 정말이지 그런 말을 할 자격이 없다!\""]], [["“I mean no harm, mamma—I mean good.", "\"해코지할 뜻은 없어요, 엄마—좋은 뜻이에요."], ["Have a moment’s patience with me, and you will see.”", "저에게 잠깐만 참을성을 가져 주세요, 그럼 아시게 될 거예요.\""]], [["She looked back at Mr. Godfrey, with what appeared to be a sudden pity for him.", "그 아이는, 별안간 그가 딱해진 듯한 낯빛으로, 고드프리 씨를 다시 바라보았다."], ["She went the length—the very unladylike length—of taking him by the hand.", "그 아이는 그의 손을 잡는—더없이 숙녀답지 못한—지경에까지 이르렀다."]], [["“I am certain,”", "\"저는 확신해요,\""], ["she said, “that I have found out the true reason of your unwillingness to speak of this matter before my mother and before me.", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"제 어머니 앞에서, 그리고 제 앞에서 이 일을 이야기하지 않으려는 당신의 마음의 참된 까닭을 알아냈다고요."], ["An unlucky accident has associated you in people’s minds with Mr.", "어떤 불운한 우연이 사람들의 마음속에서 당신을 루커 씨와 엮어 놓았어요."], ["Luker. You have told me what scandal says of him.", "당신은 그이에 대해 추문이 뭐라 하는지 저에게 말했죠."], ["What does scandal say of you?”", "당신에 대해서는 추문이 뭐라 하나요?\""]], [["Even at the eleventh hour, dear Mr. Godfrey—always ready to return good for evil—tried to spare her.", "이 막판에 이르러서까지, 사랑하는 고드프리 씨는—언제나 악을 선으로 갚을 채비가 되어 있는 그는—그 아이를 봐주려 애썼다."]], [["“Don’t ask me!”", "\"제게 묻지 말아요!\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“It’s better forgotten, Rachel—it is, indeed.”", "\"그건 잊는 편이 나아요, 레이철—정말로 그래요.\""]], [["“I will hear it!”", "\"저는 기어이 그걸 듣겠어요!\""], ["she cried out, fiercely, at the top of her voice.", "하고 그 아이가, 목청껏, 사납게 외쳤다."]], [["“Tell her, Godfrey!”", "\"말해 주게, 고드프리!\""], ["entreated my aunt.", "하고 이모님이 애원하셨다."], ["“Nothing can do her such harm as your silence is doing now!”", "\"지금 자네의 침묵이 저 아이에게 끼치는 만큼의 해를 끼칠 수 있는 건 아무것도 없네!\""]], [["Mr. Godfrey’s fine eyes filled with tears.", "고드프리 씨의 고운 눈에 눈물이 그렁그렁 고였다."], ["He cast one last appealing look at her—and then he spoke the fatal words:", "그는 마지막으로 한 번 애원하는 눈길을 그 아이에게 던지고는—그러고 나서 그 운명적인 말을 했다."]], [["“If you will have it, Rachel—scandal says that the Moonstone is in pledge to Mr. Luker, and that I am the man who has pawned it.”", "\"정 그러겠다면, 레이철—추문은 월장석이 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혀 있고, 내가 그것을 저당 잡힌 사내라고 말해요.\""]], [["She started to her feet with a scream. She looked backwards and forwards from Mr.", "그 아이는 비명과 함께 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["Godfrey to my aunt, and from my aunt to Mr. Godfrey, in such a frantic manner that I really thought she had gone mad.", "그 아이는 고드프리 씨에게서 이모님으로, 이모님에게서 고드프리 씨로, 어찌나 미친 듯이 앞뒤로 눈길을 옮기던지 나는 정말로 그 아이가 미쳐 버린 줄 알았다."]], [["“Don’t speak to me!", "\"저에게 말 걸지 마세요!"], ["Don’t touch me!”", "저를 건드리지 마세요!\""], ["she exclaimed, shrinking back from all of us (I declare like some hunted animal!) into a corner of the room.", "하고 그 아이가, (선언하거니와 마치 어떤 쫓기는 짐승처럼!) 우리 모두에게서 움츠러들어 물러나며, 방 한구석으로 외쳤다."], ["“This is my fault!", "\"이건 제 잘못이에요!"], ["I must set it right.", "제가 바로잡아야 해요."], ["I have sacrificed myself—I had a right to do that, if I liked.", "저는 저 자신을 희생했어요—원한다면 그럴 권리가 저에겐 있었죠."], ["But to let an innocent man be ruined; to keep a secret which destroys his character for life—Oh, good God, it’s too horrible!", "하지만 죄 없는 사내가 파멸하도록 두다니. 그이의 됨됨이를 평생 무너뜨리는 비밀을 지키다니—오, 하느님 맙소사, 너무 끔찍해요!"], ["I can’t bear it!”", "저는 견딜 수 없어요!\""]], [["My aunt half rose from her chair, then suddenly sat down again.", "이모님이 의자에서 반쯤 일어나셨다가, 별안간 다시 앉으셨다."], ["She called to me faintly, and pointed to a little phial in her work-box.", "이모님은 힘없이 나를 부르시고, 당신 반짇고리 안의 작은 약병 하나를 가리키셨다."]], [["“Quick!”", "\"어서!\""], ["she whispered.", "하고 이모님이 속삭이셨다."], ["“Six drops, in water.", "\"여섯 방울, 물에."], ["Don’t let Rachel see.”", "레이철이 못 보게 하렴.\""]], [["Under other circumstances, I should have thought this strange.", "다른 정황이었다면, 나는 이것을 이상하게 여겼으리라."], ["There was no time now to think—there was only time to give the medicine.", "지금은 생각할 겨를이 없었다—그저 약을 드릴 겨를밖에 없었다."], ["Dear Mr. Godfrey unconsciously assisted me in concealing what I was about from Rachel, by speaking composing words to her at the other end of the room.", "사랑하는 고드프리 씨는, 방 저편에서 그 아이에게 마음을 가라앉히는 말을 건넴으로써, 내가 하는 일을 레이철에게서 감추도록 저도 모르게 나를 도왔다."]], [["“Indeed, indeed, you exaggerate,” I heard him say.", "\"정말이지, 정말이지, 당신은 과장하고 있어요.\""], ["“My reputation stands too high to be destroyed by a miserable passing scandal like this.", "하는 그의 말이 들렸다. \"제 평판은 이런 지나가는 비참한 추문 따위에 무너지기에는 너무 높이 서 있어요."], ["It will be all forgotten in another week.", "그건 한 주만 더 지나면 죄다 잊힐 거예요."], ["Let us never speak of it again.”", "우리 다시는 그것을 이야기하지 맙시다.\""], ["She was perfectly inaccessible, even to such generosity as this.", "그 아이는, 이런 너그러움에조차, 도무지 다가갈 수가 없었다."], ["She went on from bad to worse.", "그 아이는 나쁜 데서 더 나쁜 데로 나아갔다."]], [["“I must, and will, stop it,”", "\"저는 반드시, 기어이, 그걸 멈추겠어요.\""], ["she said. “Mamma!", "하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["hear what I say.", "\"엄마! 제 말을 들어 주세요."], ["Miss Clack!", "클랙 양!"], ["hear what I say.", "제 말을 들어 줘요."], ["I know the hand that took the Moonstone.", "저는 월장석을 가져간 손을 알아요."], ["I know—”", "저는 알아요—\""], ["she laid a strong emphasis on the words; she stamped her foot in the rage that possessed her—“I know that Godfrey Ablewhite is innocent!", "그 아이는 그 말에 세게 힘을 주었다. 그 아이는 저를 사로잡은 격분에 발을 굴렀다—\"저는 고드프리 에이블화이트가 죄 없음을 알아요!"], ["Take me to the magistrate, Godfrey!", "저를 치안판사에게 데려가 줘요, 고드프리!"], ["Take me to the magistrate, and I will swear it!”", "저를 치안판사에게 데려가 줘요, 그럼 제가 그것을 맹세하겠어요!\""]], [["My aunt caught me by the hand, and whispered, “Stand between us for a minute or two.", "이모님이 내 손을 붙드시고, 속삭이셨다. \"일이 분만 우리 사이에 서 있으렴."], ["Don’t let Rachel see me.”", "레이철이 나를 못 보게 하고.\""], ["I noticed a bluish tinge in her face which alarmed me.", "나는 이모님 얼굴에 나를 놀라게 하는 푸르스름한 빛이 도는 것을 알아챘다."], ["She saw I was startled.", "이모님은 내가 놀란 것을 보셨다."], ["“The drops will put me right in a minute or two,”", "\"그 방울이 일이 분이면 나를 낫게 할 거야.\""], ["she said, and so closed her eyes, and waited a little.", "하고 이모님이 말씀하시고는, 그렇게 눈을 감으시고, 잠시 기다리셨다."]], [["While this was going on, I heard dear Mr. Godfrey still gently remonstrating.", "이 일이 벌어지는 동안, 사랑하는 고드프리 씨가 여전히 부드럽게 타이르는 소리가 들렸다."]], [["“You must not appear publicly in such a thing as this,”", "\"당신은 이런 일에 세상 사람들 앞에 나서서는 안 돼요.\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Your reputation, dearest Rachel, is something too pure and too sacred to be trifled with.”", "\"당신의 평판은, 더없이 사랑하는 레이철, 함부로 다루기에는 너무 순결하고 너무 성스러운 것이에요.\""]], [["“My reputation!”", "\"제 평판이요!\""], ["She burst out laughing.", "그 아이는 왈칵 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“Why, I am accused, Godfrey, as well as you.", "\"아니, 저도, 고드프리, 당신처럼 고발당했어요."], ["The best detective officer in England declares that I have stolen my own Diamond.", "잉글랜드 최고의 형사가 제가 제 다이아몬드를 훔쳤다고 선언한다고요."], ["Ask him what he thinks—and he will tell you that I have pledged the Moonstone to pay my private debts!”", "그이에게 뭐라 생각하는지 물어봐요—그럼 그이는 제가 제 사사로운 빚을 갚으려고 월장석을 저당 잡혔다고 말할 거예요!\""], ["She stopped, ran across the room—and fell on her knees at her mother’s feet.", "그 아이는 멈추더니, 방을 가로질러 달려가—제 어머니 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["“Oh mamma!", "\"오 엄마!"], ["mamma!", "엄마!"], ["mamma!", "엄마!"], ["I must be mad—mustn’t I?—not to own the truth now!”", "지금 진실을 털어놓지 않다니—저는 미친 게 틀림없어요—그렇지 않아요?\""], ["She was too vehement to notice her mother’s condition—she was on her feet again, and back with Mr. Godfrey, in an instant.", "그 아이는 너무 격해서 제 어머니의 상태를 알아채지 못했다—그 아이는 어느새 다시 일어나, 순식간에 고드프리 씨에게 도로 가 있었다."], ["“I won’t let you—I won’t let any innocent man—be accused and disgraced through my fault.", "\"저는 당신이—어떤 죄 없는 사내가—제 잘못으로 고발당하고 망신당하도록 두지 않겠어요."], ["If you won’t take me before the magistrate, draw out a declaration of your innocence on paper, and I will sign it.", "당신이 저를 치안판사 앞에 데려가지 않겠다면, 당신의 결백을 종이에 선언서로 적어 줘요, 그럼 제가 그것에 서명하겠어요."], ["Do as I tell you, Godfrey, or I’ll write it to the newspapers—I’ll go out, and cry it in the streets!”", "제가 이르는 대로 해요, 고드프리, 안 그러면 제가 그걸 신문에 써 보내겠어요—제가 밖으로 나가, 그걸 거리에서 외치겠어요!\""]], [["We will not say this was the language of remorse—we will say it was the language of hysterics.", "이것이 뉘우침의 말이었다고는 말하지 않으련다—우리는 그것이 히스테리의 말이었다고 말하련다."], ["Indulgent Mr. Godfrey pacified her by taking a sheet of paper, and drawing out the declaration.", "너그러운 고드프리 씨가 종이 한 장을 꺼내, 그 선언서를 적어 내려감으로써 그 아이를 달랬다."], ["She signed it in a feverish hurry.", "그 아이는 열병 같은 다급함으로 그것에 서명했다."], ["“Show it everywhere—don’t think of me,”", "\"그걸 어디에나 보여 줘요—저는 생각하지 말고요.\""], ["she said, as she gave it to him.", "하고 그 아이가 그것을 그에게 주며 말했다."], ["“I am afraid, Godfrey, I have not done you justice, hitherto, in my thoughts.", "\"제가, 고드프리, 여태껏, 제 마음속에서 당신을 정당하게 대하지 못한 것 같아 걱정이에요."], ["You are more unselfish—you are a better man than I believed you to be.", "당신은 제가 믿었던 것보다 더 사심이 없어요—더 나은 사람이에요."], ["Come here when you can, and I will try and repair the wrong I have done you.”", "올 수 있을 때 이리 와요, 그럼 제가 당신에게 저지른 잘못을 바로잡으려 애써 볼게요.\""]], [["She gave him her hand.", "그 아이는 그에게 제 손을 내밀었다."], ["Alas, for our fallen nature!", "아아, 우리의 타락한 본성이여!"], ["Alas, for Mr. Godfrey!", "아아, 고드프리 씨여!"], ["He not only forgot himself so far as to kiss her hand—he adopted a gentleness of tone in answering her which, in such a case, was little better than a compromise with sin.", "그는 그 아이의 손에 입 맞출 만큼 스스로를 잊었을 뿐만 아니라—이런 경우에는 죄와의 타협보다 나을 것이 거의 없는 부드러운 어조를 취해 그 아이에게 답했다. \"가겠어요, 더없이 사랑하는 이.\""], ["“I will come, dearest,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “on condition that we don’t speak of this hateful subject again.”", "\"우리가 이 밉살스러운 화제를 다시는 이야기하지 않는다는 조건으로요.\""], ["Never had I seen and heard our Christian Hero to less advantage than on this occasion.", "나는 우리의 기독교적 영웅을 이때만큼 못한 모습으로 보고 들은 적이 없었다."]], [["Before another word could be said by anybody, a thundering knock at the street door startled us all.", "누구든 한마디를 더 할 수 있기도 전에, 현관문을 우레같이 두드리는 소리가 우리 모두를 놀라게 했다."], ["I looked through the window, and saw the World, the Flesh, and the Devil waiting before the house—as typified in a carriage and horses, a powdered footman, and three of the most audaciously dressed women I ever beheld in my life.", "나는 창밖을 내다보았고, 세상과 육신과 악마가 그 집 앞에서 기다리는 것을 보았다—마차와 말들과, 분을 바른 하인과, 내 평생 본 가운데 가장 뻔뻔스럽게 차려입은 세 여자로 표상된 채로 말이다."]], [["Rachel started, and composed herself.", "레이철은 흠칫하더니, 마음을 다잡았다."], ["She crossed the room to her mother.", "그 아이는 방을 가로질러 제 어머니에게로 갔다."]], [["“They have come to take me to the flower-show,”", "\"저를 꽃 전시회에 데려가려고 왔어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["she said. “One word, mamma, before I go.", "\"가기 전에 한마디만요, 엄마."], ["I have not distressed you, have I?”", "제가 엄마를 괴롭히진 않았죠, 그렇죠?\""]], [["(Is the bluntness of moral feeling which could ask such a question as that, after what had just happened, to be pitied or condemned?", "(방금 있었던 일 뒤에, 그런 물음을 던질 수 있는 그 도덕적 감각의 무딤은, 가엾이 여겨야 할 것인가 나무라야 할 것인가?"], ["I like to lean towards mercy.", "나는 자비 쪽으로 기우는 편이 좋다."], ["Let us pity it.)", "그것을 가엾이 여기자.)"]], [["The drops had produced their effect.", "그 방울들이 효험을 냈다."], ["My poor aunt’s complexion was like itself again.", "나의 가엾은 이모님의 낯빛이 다시 본디대로 돌아와 있었다."], ["“No, no, my dear,” she said.", "\"아니, 아니야, 얘야.\""], ["“Go with our friends, and enjoy yourself.”", "하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"우리 벗들과 함께 가서, 즐겁게 지내렴.\""]], [["Her daughter stooped, and kissed her.", "그 따님은 몸을 굽혀, 이모님께 입 맞추었다."], ["I had left the window, and was near the door, when Rachel approached it to go out.", "나는 창가를 떠나, 문 가까이에 있었는데, 그때 레이철이 나가려고 그 문으로 다가왔다."], ["Another change had come over her—she was in tears.", "그 아이에게 또 한 번 변화가 일었다—그 아이는 눈물을 흘리고 있었다."], ["I looked with interest at the momentary softening of that obdurate heart.", "나는 그 완고한 마음의 순간적인 누그러짐을 흥미롭게 바라보았다."], ["I felt inclined to say a few earnest words.", "나는 간곡한 말 몇 마디를 하고 싶은 마음이 들었다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["my well-meant sympathy only gave offence.", "나의 선의의 동정이 그저 비위를 거스르고 말았다."], ["“What do you mean by pitying me?”", "\"저를 가엾이 여긴다니 무슨 뜻이에요?\""], ["she asked in a bitter whisper, as she passed to the door.", "하고 그 아이가, 문으로 지나가며, 쓰라린 속삭임으로 물었다."], ["“Don’t you see how happy I am?", "\"제가 얼마나 행복한지 안 보여요?"], ["I’m going to the flower-show, Clack; and I’ve got the prettiest bonnet in London.”", "저는 꽃 전시회에 가요, 클랙. 그리고 저는 런던에서 가장 예쁜 보닛을 썼고요.\""], ["She completed the hollow mockery of that address by blowing me a kiss—and so left the room.", "그 아이는 내게 손 키스를 날려 보냄으로써 그 속 빈 조롱의 인사를 마무리했다—그러고는 방을 나섰다."]], [["I wish I could describe in words the compassion I felt for this miserable and misguided girl.", "나는 이 비참하고 그릇 인도된 아이에게 느낀 그 연민을 말로 묘사할 수 있으면 좋겠다."], ["But I am almost as poorly provided with words as with money.", "허나 나는 돈만큼이나 말도 넉넉지 못하다."], ["Permit me to say—my heart bled for her.", "이 말만은 하게 해 다오—나의 가슴이 그 아이를 위해 피 흘렸다."]], [["Returning to my aunt’s chair, I observed dear Mr. Godfrey searching for something softly, here and there, in different parts of the room.", "이모님의 의자로 돌아오다가, 나는 사랑하는 고드프리 씨가 방의 여기저기, 이곳저곳에서 무언가를 부드럽게 찾고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Before I could offer to assist him he had found what he wanted.", "내가 그를 돕겠다고 나서기도 전에 그는 원하는 것을 찾아냈다."], ["He came back to my aunt and me, with his declaration of innocence in one hand, and with a box of matches in the other.", "그는 한 손에는 제 결백의 선언서를, 다른 손에는 성냥갑을 든 채, 이모님과 내게로 돌아왔다."]], [["“Dear aunt, a little conspiracy!” he said.", "\"사랑하는 이모님, 조그만 모의 하나!\""], ["“Dear Miss Clack, a pious fraud which even your high moral rectitude will excuse!", "하고 그가 말했다. \"사랑하는 클랙 양, 당신의 그 높은 도덕적 올곧음조차 눈감아 줄 경건한 속임수 하나!"], ["Will you leave Rachel to suppose that I accept the generous self-sacrifice which has signed this paper?", "레이철이, 이 종이에 서명한 그 갸륵한 자기희생을 제가 받아들이는 줄로 여기도록 놓아두시겠습니까?"], ["And will you kindly bear witness that I destroy it in your presence, before I leave the house?”", "그리고 제가 이 집을 떠나기 전에, 두 분 앞에서 이것을 없애는 것을 친절히 증언해 주시겠습니까?\""], ["He kindled a match, and, lighting the paper, laid it to burn in a plate on the table.", "그는 성냥을 켜, 그 종이에 불을 붙여, 탁자 위의 접시에 담아 태웠다."], ["“Any trifling inconvenience that I may suffer is as nothing,”", "\"제가 겪을 수 있는 그 어떤 사소한 불편도,\" 하고 그가 짚었다."], ["he remarked, “compared with the importance of preserving that pure name from the contaminating contact of the world.", "\"저 순결한 이름을 세상의 오염시키는 접촉으로부터 지켜 내는 일의 중대함에 견주면, 아무것도 아닙니다."], ["There!", "자!"], ["We have reduced it to a little harmless heap of ashes; and our dear impulsive Rachel will never know what we have done!", "우리는 그것을 조그맣고 해롭잖은 잿더미로 만들어 버렸습니다. 그리고 우리의 사랑스러운 충동적인 레이철은 우리가 무슨 일을 했는지 결코 알지 못할 겁니다!"], ["How do you feel?", "기분이 어떠십니까?"], ["My precious friends, how do you feel?", "나의 소중한 벗들이여, 기분이 어떠십니까?"], ["For my poor part, I am as light-hearted as a boy!”", "이 가엾은 저로 말하자면, 저는 소년처럼 마음이 가볍습니다!\""]], [["He beamed on us with his beautiful smile; he held out a hand to my aunt, and a hand to me.", "그는 그 아름다운 미소로 우리를 향해 환히 빛냈다. 그는 한 손을 이모님께, 한 손을 내게 내밀었다."], ["I was too deeply affected by his noble conduct to speak.", "나는 그의 고결한 행동에 너무 깊이 감동해 말을 할 수가 없었다."], ["I closed my eyes; I put his hand, in a kind of spiritual self-forgetfulness, to my lips.", "나는 눈을 감았다. 나는, 일종의 영적인 자기 망각 속에서, 그의 손을 내 입술에 갖다 대었다."], ["He murmured a soft remonstrance.", "그가 부드럽게 나무라는 말을 웅얼거렸다."], ["Oh the ecstasy, the pure, unearthly ecstasy of that moment!", "오 그 순간의 황홀함, 그 순결하고, 이 세상 것이 아닌 황홀함이여!"], ["I sat—I hardly know on what—quite lost in my own exalted feelings.", "나는 앉아—무엇에 앉았는지도 잘 모르겠다—나 자신의 드높여진 감정에 아주 넋을 잃었다."], ["When I opened my eyes again, it was like descending from heaven to earth.", "다시 눈을 떴을 때, 그것은 천국에서 땅으로 내려오는 것과 같았다."], ["There was nobody but my aunt in the room.", "방에는 이모님밖에 없었다."], ["He had gone.", "그는 가고 없었다."]], [["I should like to stop here—I should like to close my narrative with the record of Mr. Godfrey’s noble conduct.", "나는 여기서 멈추고 싶다—나는 고드프리 씨의 그 고결한 행동의 기록으로 나의 이야기를 맺고 싶다."], ["Unhappily there is more, much more, which the unrelenting pecuniary pressure of Mr. Blake’s cheque obliges me to tell.", "불행히도 더, 훨씬 더 많은 것이 있어, 블레이크 씨의 수표라는 그 가차 없는 금전적 압박이 나로 하여금 이야기하지 않을 수 없게 한다."], ["The painful disclosures which were to reveal themselves in my presence, during that Tuesday’s visit to Montagu Square, were not at an end yet.", "그 화요일의 몬터규 스퀘어 방문 동안, 내 앞에서 저를 드러내게 될 그 고통스러운 폭로들은 아직 끝나지 않은 것이었다."]], [["Finding myself alone with Lady Verinder, I turned naturally to the subject of her health; touching delicately on the strange anxiety which she had shown to conceal her indisposition, and the remedy applied to it, from the observation of her daughter.", "레이디 버린더와 단둘이 남은 나 자신을 발견하고, 나는 자연스레 이모님의 건강이라는 화제로 돌아섰다. 이모님이 당신의 병환과, 그에 쓴 약을 당신 따님의 눈에서 감추려 보이신 그 이상한 근심을 조심스레 건드리면서 말이다."]], [["My aunt’s reply greatly surprised me.", "이모님의 대답은 나를 크게 놀라게 했다."]], [["“Drusilla,”", "\"드루실라,\""], ["she said (if I have not already mentioned that my Christian name is Drusilla, permit me to mention it now), “you are touching—quite innocently, I know—on a very distressing subject.”", "하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다 (내 세례명이 드루실라라는 것을 아직 언급하지 않았다면, 지금 그것을 언급하게 해 다오). \"너는 아주 순진하게, 나도 알거니와—더없이 마음 아픈 화제를 건드리고 있구나.\""]], [["I rose immediately.", "나는 즉시 일어섰다."], ["Delicacy left me but one alternative—the alternative, after first making my apologies, of taking my leave.", "조심성이 내게는 오직 하나의 선택지를 남겼다—먼저 사과를 드린 뒤, 물러가는 선택지 말이다."], ["Lady Verinder stopped me, and insisted on my sitting down again.", "레이디 버린더는 나를 멈춰 세우시고, 다시 앉으라고 우기셨다."]], [["“You have surprised a secret,”", "\"너는 어떤 비밀을 뜻하지 않게 알아냈구나,\" 하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다."], ["she said, “which I had confided to my sister Mrs. Ablewhite, and to my lawyer Mr.", "\"내가 내 언니 에이블화이트 부인과, 내 변호사 브러프 씨에게만 털어놓고, 그 밖에는 아무에게도 털어놓지 않은 비밀을 말이야."], ["Bruff, and to no one else.", "나는 그들의 신중함을 믿을 수 있다."], ["I can trust in their discretion; and I am sure, when I tell you the circumstances, I can trust in yours.", "그리고 네게 그 정황을 이야기하면, 나는 너의 신중함도 믿을 수 있으리라 확신한다."], ["Have you any pressing engagement, Drusilla?", "무슨 급한 볼일이라도 있니, 드루실라?"], ["or is your time your own this afternoon?”", "아니면 오늘 오후 시간은 네 마음대로 쓸 수 있고?\""]], [["It is needless to say that my time was entirely at my aunt’s disposal.", "내 시간이 온전히 이모님 뜻에 달려 있었음은 말할 것도 없다."]], [["“Keep me company then,”", "\"그럼 나와 함께 있어 다오,\""], ["she said, “for another hour.", "하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다."], ["I have something to tell you which I believe you will be sorry to hear.", "\"한 시간만 더. 나는 네게, 네가 듣기 서글퍼하리라 믿는 무언가를 이야기해야겠다."], ["And I shall have a service to ask of you afterwards, if you don’t object to assist me.”", "그리고 그 뒤에, 네가 나를 돕기를 마다하지 않는다면, 네게 부탁할 일이 하나 있을 게다.\""]], [["It is again needless to say that, so far from objecting, I was all eagerness to assist her.", "마다하기는커녕, 내가 이모님을 도우려는 열의로 가득했음은 다시 말할 것도 없다."]], [["“You can wait here,”", "\"너는 여기서 기다리면 된다,\""], ["she went on, “till Mr.", "하고 이모님이 말을 이으셨다."], ["Bruff comes at five.", "\"다섯 시에 브러프 씨가 올 때까지 말이야."], ["And you can be one of the witnesses, Drusilla, when I sign my Will.”", "그리고 너는, 드루실라, 내가 내 유언장에 서명할 때, 증인 가운데 하나가 되어 주면 된다.\""]], [["Her Will!", "이모님의 유언장이라니!"], ["I thought of the drops which I had seen in her work-box.", "나는 이모님 반짇고리에서 본 그 방울들을 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of the bluish tinge which I had noticed in her complexion.", "나는 이모님 낯빛에서 알아챈 그 푸르스름한 빛을 떠올렸다."], ["A light which was not of this world—a light shining prophetically from an unmade grave—dawned on my mind.", "이 세상 것이 아닌 빛이—아직 만들어지지 않은 무덤에서 예언처럼 비쳐 오는 빛이—내 마음에 밝아 왔다."], ["My aunt’s secret was a secret no longer.", "이모님의 비밀은 더는 비밀이 아니었다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["Consideration for poor Lady Verinder forbade me even to hint that I had guessed the melancholy truth, before she opened her lips.", "가엾은 레이디 버린더에 대한 배려가, 이모님이 입을 여시기 전에 내가 그 서글픈 진실을 짐작했다는 것을 넌지시라도 비치는 것을 내게 금했다."], ["I waited her pleasure in silence; and, having privately arranged to say a few sustaining words at the first convenient opportunity, felt prepared for any duty that could claim me, no matter how painful it might be.", "나는 말없이 이모님의 뜻을 기다렸다. 그리고, 첫 알맞은 기회에 몇 마디 지탱해 주는 말을 하리라 남몰래 마련해 둔 채, 그것이 아무리 고통스러울지라도, 나를 부를 수 있는 그 어떤 소임에도 마음의 채비가 된 느낌이었다."]], [["“I have been seriously ill, Drusilla, for some time past,”", "\"나는 지난 얼마 동안, 드루실라, 심하게 앓아 왔단다.\""], ["my aunt began.", "하고 이모님이 말문을 여셨다."], ["“And, strange to say, without knowing it myself.”", "\"그런데, 이상하게도, 나 자신은 그것을 모른 채 말이야.\""]], [["I thought of the thousands and thousands of perishing human creatures who were all at that moment spiritually ill, without knowing it themselves.", "나는 바로 그 순간 저 자신은 그것을 모른 채 영적으로 앓고 있는 수천수만의 스러져 가는 인간들을 떠올렸다."], ["And I greatly feared that my poor aunt might be one of the number.", "그리고 나는 나의 가엾은 이모님도 그 무리 가운데 하나일까 봐 몹시 두려웠다. \"네, 이모님.\""], ["“Yes, dear,” I said, sadly.", "하고 나는 서글피 말했다."], ["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“I brought Rachel to London, as you know, for medical advice,”", "\"너도 알다시피, 나는 의료 조언을 구하러 레이철을 런던으로 데려왔지.\""], ["she went on.", "하고 이모님이 말을 이으셨다."], ["“I thought it right to consult two doctors.”", "\"나는 의사 둘에게 진찰받는 것이 옳다고 여겼어.\""]], [["Two doctors!", "의사 둘!"], ["And, oh me (in Rachel’s state), not one clergyman!", "그리고, 오 저런, (레이철의 상태에서) 목사님은 한 분도 없이!"], ["“Yes, dear?”", "\"네, 이모님?\""], ["I said once more.", "하고 나는 다시 한번 말했다."], ["“Yes?”", "\"네?\""]], [["“One of the two medical men,”", "\"그 두 의사 가운데 하나는,\""], ["proceeded my aunt, “was a stranger to me.", "하고 이모님이 이어 가셨다. \"내게 낯선 사람이었어."], ["The other had been an old friend of my husband’s, and had always felt a sincere interest in me for my husband’s sake.", "다른 하나는 내 남편의 오랜 벗이었고, 언제나 내 남편을 봐서 나에게 진심 어린 관심을 느껴 온 사람이었지."], ["After prescribing for Rachel, he said he wished to speak to me privately in another room.", "레이철에게 처방을 내린 뒤, 그는 다른 방에서 나와 단둘이 이야기하고 싶다고 했어."], ["I expected, of course, to receive some special directions for the management of my daughter’s health.", "나는, 물론, 내 딸의 건강을 돌보는 데 대한 어떤 특별한 지시를 받으리라 예상했지."], ["To my surprise, he took me gravely by the hand, and said, ‘I have been looking at you, Lady Verinder, with a professional as well as a personal interest.", "놀랍게도, 그는 진지하게 내 손을 잡고, 이렇게 말했어. '저는, 레이디 버린더, 직업적 관심으로도 개인적 관심으로도 마님을 살펴보고 있었습니다."], ["You are, I am afraid, far more urgently in need of medical advice than your daughter.’ He put some questions to me, which I was at first inclined to treat lightly enough, until I observed that my answers distressed him.", "마님은, 유감스럽게도, 따님보다 훨씬 더 절박하게 의료 조언이 필요하십니다.' 그는 내게 몇 가지 물음을 던졌는데, 나는 처음에는 그것들을 넉넉히 가볍게 다루려는 편이었어, 내 대답이 그를 괴롭게 하는 것을 알아채기 전까지는."], ["It ended in his making an appointment to come and see me, accompanied by a medical friend, on the next day, at an hour when Rachel would not be at home.", "그것은 그가 이튿날, 레이철이 집에 없을 시각에, 어느 의사 벗을 대동하고 나를 보러 오겠다는 약속을 잡는 것으로 끝났지."], ["The result of that visit—most kindly and gently conveyed to me—satisfied both the physicians that there had been precious time lost, which could never be regained, and that my case had now passed beyond the reach of their art.", "그 방문의 결과는—더없이 친절하고 부드럽게 내게 전해졌거니와—두 의사 모두에게, 결코 되찾을 수 없는 귀중한 시간이 허비되었으며, 내 병이 이제 그들의 의술이 미치는 데를 지나 버렸음을 납득시켰어."], ["For more than two years I have been suffering under an insidious form of heart disease, which, without any symptoms to alarm me, has, by little and little, fatally broken me down.", "이 년 넘게 나는 은밀한 형태의 심장병을 앓아 왔는데, 그것은, 나를 놀라게 할 아무 증상도 없이, 조금씩 조금씩, 나를 돌이킬 수 없이 허물어뜨려 왔지."], ["I may live for some months, or I may die before another day has passed over my head—the doctors cannot, and dare not, speak more positively than this.", "나는 몇 달을 더 살 수도 있고, 아니면 내 머리 위로 하루가 더 지나기 전에 죽을 수도 있어—의사들도 이 이상으로 딱 잘라 말할 수는, 또 감히 그럴 수는 없다는구나."], ["It would be vain to say, my dear, that I have not had some miserable moments since my real situation has been made known to me.", "내 실상이 내게 알려진 뒤로 내가 얼마간 비참한 순간을 겪지 않았다고 말하는 것은, 얘야, 부질없는 노릇이겠지."], ["But I am more resigned than I was, and I am doing my best to set my worldly affairs in order.", "허나 나는 예전보다는 마음을 다잡았고, 내 세속의 일들을 정리하는 데 최선을 다하고 있어."], ["My one great anxiety is that Rachel should be kept in ignorance of the truth.", "나의 한 가지 큰 근심은 레이철이 그 진실을 모른 채로 있어야 한다는 거야."], ["If she knew it, she would at once attribute my broken health to anxiety about the Diamond, and would reproach herself bitterly, poor child, for what is in no sense her fault.", "만일 그 아이가 그것을 안다면, 그 아이는 대뜸 내 허물어진 건강을 다이아몬드에 대한 근심 탓으로 돌리고, 결코 그 아이 잘못이 아닌 일로, 가엾은 것, 저 자신을 쓰라리게 나무랄 테니까."], ["Both the doctors agree that the mischief began two, if not three years since.", "두 의사 모두 그 화근이 이 년, 어쩌면 삼 년 전에 시작되었다는 데 뜻을 같이해."], ["I am sure you will keep my secret, Drusilla—for I am sure I see sincere sorrow and sympathy for me in your face.”", "나는 네가 내 비밀을 지켜 주리라 확신한다, 드루실라—네 얼굴에서 나를 향한 진심 어린 슬픔과 동정이 보인다고 확신하니 말이야.\""]], [["Sorrow and sympathy!", "슬픔과 동정이라니!"], ["Oh, what Pagan emotions to expect from a Christian Englishwoman anchored firmly on her faith!", "오, 제 믿음에 굳게 닻을 내린 기독교인 영국 여자에게서 기대하기에 이 무슨 이교도 같은 감정인가!"]], [["Little did my poor aunt imagine what a gush of devout thankfulness thrilled through me as she approached the close of her melancholy story.", "나의 가엾은 이모님은, 당신의 그 서글픈 이야기의 끝에 다가가시는 동안 얼마나 벅찬 경건한 감사가 나를 전율케 하는지 조금도 짐작하지 못하셨다."], ["Here was a career of usefulness opened before me!", "여기 쓸모의 길이 내 앞에 열려 있었던 것이다!"], ["Here was a beloved relative and perishing fellow-creature, on the eve of the great change, utterly unprepared; and led, providentially led, to reveal her situation to Me!", "여기, 사랑하는 친척이자 스러져 가는 동료 인간이, 그 큰 변화의 문턱에서, 전혀 채비가 되지 않은 채 있었고—섭리의 인도로 이끌려, 제 처지를 내게 드러낸 것이다!"], ["How can I describe the joy with which I now remembered that the precious clerical friends on whom I could rely, were to be counted, not by ones or twos, but by tens and twenties.", "이제 내가, 내가 믿고 기댈 수 있는 그 소중한 성직자 벗들이, 하나둘로가 아니라, 열, 스물로 헤아려진다는 것을 떠올린 그 기쁨을 어찌 묘사할 수 있으랴."], ["I took my aunt in my arms—my overflowing tenderness was not to be satisfied, now, with anything less than an embrace.", "나는 이모님을 내 품에 안았다—넘쳐 흐르는 내 다정함이, 이제는, 포옹 아닌 무엇으로도 흡족해질 수 없었다."], ["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["I said to her, fervently, “the indescribable interest with which you inspire me!", "하고 나는 뜨겁게 이모님께 말했다. \"이모님이 제 안에 불러일으키는 그 형언할 수 없는 관심이라니!"], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["the good I mean to do you, dear, before we part!”", "우리가 헤어지기 전에, 이모님, 제가 이모님께 하려는 그 선이라니!\""], ["After another word or two of earnest prefatory warning, I gave her her choice of three precious friends, all plying the work of mercy from morning to night in her own neighbourhood; all equally inexhaustible in exhortation; all affectionately ready to exercise their gifts at a word from me.", "몇 마디 더 간곡한 서두의 경고를 건넨 뒤, 나는 이모님께 세 소중한 벗 가운데 골라잡으시게 했는데, 다들 이모님의 이웃에서 아침부터 밤까지 자비의 일에 힘쓰고, 다들 똑같이 지칠 줄 모르고 권면하며, 다들 나의 한마디에 다정하게 제 은사를 베풀 채비가 되어 있는 이들이었다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["the result was far from encouraging.", "그 결과는 도무지 신통치 못했다."], ["Poor Lady Verinder looked puzzled and frightened, and met everything I could say to her with the purely worldly objection that she was not strong enough to face strangers.", "가엾은 레이디 버린더는 어리둥절하고 겁먹은 낯빛으로, 내가 이모님께 할 수 있는 온갖 말을, 낯선 이들을 마주할 만큼 기운이 넉넉지 못하다는 순전히 세속적인 이의로 받으셨다."], ["I yielded—for the moment only, of course.", "나는 물러섰다—물론, 그저 그때만이다."], ["My large experience (as Reader and Visitor, under not less, first and last, than fourteen beloved clerical friends) informed me that this was another case for preparation by books.", "(여태껏 열넷도 넘는 사랑하는 성직자 벗들 밑에서, 낭독자이자 방문자로서 쌓은) 나의 넓은 경험이, 이것이 또 하나의 책으로 준비할 경우임을 내게 일러 주었다."], ["I possessed a little library of works, all suitable to the present emergency, all calculated to arouse, convince, prepare, enlighten, and fortify my aunt.", "나는 저작들의 조그만 서고를 지니고 있었는데, 죄다 지금의 이 위급함에 알맞고, 죄다 이모님을 일깨우고, 납득시키고, 준비시키고, 깨우치고, 굳세게 하도록 헤아려진 것들이었다."], ["“You will read, dear, won’t you?”", "\"읽으실 거죠, 이모님, 그렇죠?\""], ["I said, in my most winning way.", "하고 나는 더없이 사근사근하게 말했다."], ["“You will read, if I bring you my own precious books?", "\"제가 저의 소중한 책들을 가져다드리면, 읽으실 거죠?"], ["Turned down at all the right places, aunt.", "알맞은 데는 죄다 페이지 귀퉁이를 접어서요, 이모님."], ["And marked in pencil where you are to stop and ask yourself, ‘Does this apply to me?’”", "그리고 '이것이 내게 해당되는가?' 하고 멈춰 서서 스스로에게 물어야 할 데는 연필로 표시해서요.\""], ["Even that simple appeal—so absolutely heathenising is the influence of the world—appeared to startle my aunt.", "그런 소박한 호소조차—세상의 영향이 어찌나 철저히 사람을 이교화하는지—이모님을 놀라게 하는 듯싶었다."], ["She said, “I will do what I can, Drusilla, to please you,”", "이모님은 \"내가 할 수 있는 것을 하마, 드루실라, 너를 기쁘게 하려고.\""], ["with a look of surprise, which was at once instructive and terrible to see.", "하고, 놀란 듯한 낯빛으로 말씀하셨는데, 그것은 교훈적이면서도 동시에 보기에 끔찍했다."], ["Not a moment was to be lost.", "한순간도 허비할 수 없었다."], ["The clock on the mantelpiece informed me that I had just time to hurry home; to provide myself with a first series of selected readings (say a dozen only); and to return in time to meet the lawyer, and witness Lady Verinder’s Will.", "벽난로 위의 시계가, 내가 서둘러 집에 가서, 골라낸 읽을거리의 첫 묶음을 (이를테면 열두어 부만) 마련해 오고, 변호사를 만나 레이디 버린더의 유언장을 증언하기에 딱 알맞을 겨를이 있음을 내게 일러 주었다."], ["Promising faithfully to be back by five o’clock, I left the house on my errand of mercy.", "다섯 시까지 반드시 돌아오겠노라 약속하고, 나는 자비의 심부름을 나서 그 집을 떠났다."]], [["When no interests but my own are involved, I am humbly content to get from place to place by the omnibus.", "나 자신의 잇속밖에 걸려 있지 않을 때는, 나는 겸손히 합승 마차로 이곳에서 저곳으로 다니는 것에 만족한다."], ["Permit me to give an idea of my devotion to my aunt’s interests by recording that, on this occasion, I committed the prodigality of taking a cab.", "이번에는, 삯마차를 타는 낭비를 저질렀음을 적음으로써, 이모님의 잇속에 대한 나의 헌신을 여러분이 짐작하게 해 다오."]], [["I drove home, selected and marked my first series of readings, and drove back to Montagu Square, with a dozen works in a carpet-bag, the like of which, I firmly believe, are not to be found in the literature of any other country in Europe.", "나는 마차로 집에 가서, 읽을거리의 첫 묶음을 골라 표시하고, 융단 가방에 열두어 부의 저작을 담아 몬터규 스퀘어로 다시 몰고 왔는데, 그런 것들은, 내가 굳게 믿거니와, 유럽의 다른 어느 나라 문헌에서도 찾아볼 수 없는 것들이다."], ["I paid the cabman exactly his fare.", "나는 마부에게 삯을 꼭 그만큼만 치렀다."], ["He received it with an oath; upon which I instantly gave him a tract.", "그는 그것을 욕설과 함께 받았다. 그래서 나는 대뜸 그에게 소책자 한 부를 주었다."], ["If I had presented a pistol at his head, this abandoned wretch could hardly have exhibited greater consternation.", "내가 그의 머리에 권총을 겨누었던들, 이 막된 몹쓸 자는 그보다 더 큰 경악을 좀처럼 드러내지 못했으리라."], ["He jumped up on his box, and, with profane exclamations of dismay, drove off furiously.", "그는 제 마부석에 뛰어올라, 낭패의 불경한 외침과 함께, 사납게 몰아 떠나갔다."], ["Quite useless, I am happy to say!", "아주 부질없었다, 말할 수 있어 기쁘거니와!"], ["I sowed the good seed, in spite of him, by throwing a second tract in at the window of the cab.", "나는 그에게도 아랑곳없이, 마차 창 안으로 두 번째 소책자를 던져 넣음으로써, 좋은 씨앗을 뿌렸다."]], [["The servant who answered the door—not the person with the cap-ribbons, to my great relief, but the footman—informed me that the doctor had called, and was still shut up with Lady Verinder. Mr.", "문을 열어 준 하인은—그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 아니라, 나의 큰 안도스럽게도, 그 남자 하인이었는데—의사가 다녀갔고, 여전히 레이디 버린더와 함께 방에 들어앉아 있다고 내게 알려 주었다."], ["Bruff, the lawyer, had arrived a minute since and was waiting in the library.", "변호사 브러프 씨가 조금 전에 도착해 서재에서 기다리고 있었다."], ["I was shown into the library to wait too.", "나도 기다리도록 서재로 안내되었다."]], [["Mr. Bruff looked surprised to see me.", "브러프 씨는 나를 보고 놀란 듯했다."], ["He is the family solicitor, and we had met more than once, on previous occasions, under Lady Verinder’s roof.", "그는 그 집안의 사무 변호사로, 우리는 예전에 레이디 버린더의 지붕 아래서 여러 번 만난 적이 있었다."], ["A man, I grieve to say, grown old and grizzled in the service of the world.", "말하기 서글프거니와, 세상을 섬기다 늙어 반백이 된 사내다."], ["A man who, in his hours of business, was the chosen prophet of Law and Mammon; and who, in his hours of leisure, was equally capable of reading a novel and of tearing up a tract.", "볼일을 보는 시간에는, 법과 맘몬의 뽑힌 예언자였고, 한가한 시간에는, 소설을 읽는 것도 소책자를 찢는 것도 똑같이 능히 해내는 사내였다."]], [["“Have you come to stay here, Miss Clack?”", "\"여기 머무르러 오셨습니까, 클랙 양?\""], ["he asked, with a look at my carpet-bag.", "하고 그가, 내 융단 가방을 흘긋 보며 물었다."]], [["To reveal the contents of my precious bag to such a person as this would have been simply to invite an outburst of profanity.", "이런 사람에게 나의 소중한 가방의 내용물을 드러내는 것은 그저 불경의 폭발을 부르는 노릇이었으리라."], ["I lowered myself to his own level, and mentioned my business in the house.", "나는 그 자신의 수준으로 나를 낮추어, 이 집에서의 내 볼일을 언급했다."]], [["“My aunt has informed me that she is about to sign her Will,”", "\"이모님이 곧 유언장에 서명하실 거라고 제게 알려 주셨어요.\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“She has been so good as to ask me to be one of the witnesses.”", "\"고맙게도 저더러 증인 가운데 하나가 되어 달라 청하셨고요.\""]], [["“Aye? aye?", "\"그래요?"], ["Well, Miss Clack, you will do.", "그래요? 음, 클랙 양, 당신이면 되겠군요."], ["You are over twenty-one, and you have not the slightest pecuniary interest in Lady Verinder’s Will.”", "당신은 스물한 살이 넘었고, 레이디 버린더의 유언장에 조금의 금전적 이해관계도 없으니 말입니다.\""]], [["Not the slightest pecuniary interest in Lady Verinder’s Will.", "레이디 버린더의 유언장에 조금의 금전적 이해관계도 없다니."], ["Oh, how thankful I felt when I heard that!", "오, 그 말을 들었을 때 나는 얼마나 감사했던지!"], ["If my aunt, possessed of thousands, had remembered poor Me, to whom five pounds is an object—if my name had appeared in the Will, with a little comforting legacy attached to it—my enemies might have doubted the motive which had loaded me with the choicest treasures of my library, and had drawn upon my failing resources for the prodigal expenses of a cab.", "만일 수천 파운드를 지닌 이모님이, 오 파운드도 큰돈인 이 가엾은 나를 기억하셨더라면—만일 내 이름이, 조그마한 위안이 될 유산과 함께, 그 유언장에 나타났더라면—나의 원수들은 나로 하여금 내 서고의 가장 빼어난 보물들을 짊어지게 하고, 삯마차의 헤픈 비용을 위해 내 쪼그라드는 살림을 축내게 한 그 동기를 의심했을지 모른다."], ["Not the cruellest scoffer of them all could doubt now.", "이제는 그들 가운데 가장 모진 조롱꾼도 의심할 수 없었다."], ["Much better as it was!", "이대로가 훨씬 나았다!"], ["Oh, surely, surely, much better as it was!", "오, 분명, 분명, 이대로가 훨씬 나았다!"]], [["I was aroused from these consoling reflections by the voice of Mr.", "나는 브러프 씨의 목소리에 이 위안이 되는 상념에서 깨어났다."], ["Bruff. My meditative silence appeared to weigh upon the spirits of this worldling, and to force him, as it were, into talking to me against his own will.", "나의 명상에 잠긴 침묵이 이 속물의 마음을 짓눌러, 이를테면, 그 자신의 뜻과는 어긋나게 나에게 말을 걸도록 그를 몰아붙이는 듯싶었다."]], [["“Well, Miss Clack, what’s the last news in the charitable circles?", "\"음, 클랙 양, 자선계의 최근 소식은 무엇입니까?"], ["How is your friend Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, after the mauling he got from the rogues in Northumberland Street?", "당신의 벗 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 노섬벌랜드 스트리트에서 그 악당들에게 두들겨 맞은 뒤로 어떻습니까?"], ["Egad!", "이런!"], ["they’re telling a pretty story about that charitable gentleman at my club!”", "우리 클럽에서는 그 자선가 신사분에 대해 제법 재미난 이야기를 하고들 있답니다!\""]], [["I had passed over the manner in which this person had remarked that I was more than twenty-one, and that I had no pecuniary interest in my aunt’s Will.", "나는 이 사람이 내가 스물한 살이 넘었다는 것과, 이모님 유언장에 금전적 이해관계가 없다는 것을 짚은 그 태도는 그냥 넘겼다."], ["But the tone in which he alluded to dear Mr. Godfrey was too much for my forbearance.", "허나 그가 사랑하는 고드프리 씨를 넌지시 이르는 그 어조는 내 참을성으로 감당하기에 너무 지나쳤다."], ["Feeling bound, after what had passed in my presence that afternoon, to assert the innocence of my admirable friend, whenever I found it called in question—I own to having also felt bound to include in the accomplishment of this righteous purpose, a stinging castigation in the case of Mr. Bruff.", "그날 오후 내 앞에서 벌어진 일 뒤로, 나의 감탄스러운 벗의 결백이 의문시되는 것을 볼 때마다 그것을 주장할 의무가 있다고 느끼는 터라—나는 또한 이 정의로운 목적을 이루는 데에, 브러프 씨의 경우에는 따끔한 벌을 곁들일 의무가 있다고도 느꼈음을 인정한다."]], [["“I live very much out of the world,”", "\"저는 아주 세상 밖에서 삽니다.\""], ["I said; “and I don’t possess the advantage, sir, of belonging to a club.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"그리고, 선생, 저는 클럽에 속하는 이점을 지니고 있지 않아요."], ["But I happen to know the story to which you allude; and I also know that a viler falsehood than that story never was told.”", "하지만 저는 마침 선생이 넌지시 이르는 그 이야기를 알고 있어요. 그리고 저는 또한, 그 이야기보다 더 비열한 거짓은 여태껏 있은 적이 없다는 것도 알고요.\""]], [["“Yes, yes, Miss Clack—you believe in your friend.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 클랙 양—당신은 당신 벗을 믿는군요."], ["Natural enough. Mr.", "넉넉히 자연스러운 일이지요."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite won’t find the world in general quite so easy to convince as a committee of charitable ladies.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 자선하는 부인들의 위원회만큼 세상 일반을 그리 쉽게 납득시키지는 못할 겁니다."], ["Appearances are dead against him.", "겉모습이 죄다 그에게 불리하거든요."], ["He was in the house when the Diamond was lost.", "그는 다이아몬드를 잃었을 때 그 집에 있었어요."], ["And he was the first person in the house to go to London afterwards.", "그리고 그 뒤 그 집에서 맨 처음 런던으로 간 사람이 그였고요."], ["Those are ugly circumstances, ma’am, viewed by the light of later events.”", "그것들은 고약한 정황입니다, 부인, 나중의 일들에 비추어 보자면 말이지요.\""]], [["I ought, I know, to have set him right before he went any farther.", "나도 알거니와, 나는 그가 더 나아가기 전에 그를 바로잡았어야 했다."], ["I ought to have told him that he was speaking in ignorance of a testimony to Mr. Godfrey’s innocence, offered by the only person who was undeniably competent to speak from a positive knowledge of the subject.", "나는 그에게, 그가 이 화제에 대한 확실한 앎으로부터 말할 수 있음이 부인할 수 없는 유일한 사람이 내놓은, 고드프리 씨의 결백에 대한 증언을 모른 채 말하고 있다고 일러 주었어야 했다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["the temptation to lead the lawyer artfully on to his own discomfiture was too much for me.", "그 변호사를 교묘히 이끌어 그 자신의 낭패로 몰아넣으려는 유혹이 나로서는 너무 지나쳤다."], ["I asked what he meant by “later events”—with an appearance of the utmost innocence.", "나는 그가 \"나중의 일들\"로 무엇을 뜻하느냐고—더없이 순진한 낯빛으로—물었다."]], [["“By later events, Miss Clack, I mean events in which the Indians are concerned,”", "\"나중의 일들로, 클랙 양, 저는 그 인도인들이 관련된 일들을 뜻합니다.\""], ["proceeded Mr. Bruff, getting more and more superior to poor Me, the longer he went on.", "하고 브러프 씨가, 말을 이어 갈수록 이 가엾은 나보다 점점 더 잘난 체하며 이어 갔다."], ["“What do the Indians do, the moment they are let out of the prison at Frizinghall?", "\"그 인도인들이 프리징홀 감옥에서 풀려나는 그 순간 무엇을 합니까?"], ["They go straight to London, and fix on Mr.", "그들은 곧장 런던으로 가서, 루커 씨를 점찍습니다."], ["Luker. What follows?", "무엇이 뒤따르나요?"], ["Mr. Luker feels alarmed for the safety of ‘a valuable of great price,’ which he has got in the house.", "루커 씨는 제가 집에 지닌 '값이 대단히 비싼 귀중품'의 안전을 걱정합니다."], ["He lodges it privately (under a general description) in his bankers’ strongroom.", "그는 그것을 (뭉뚱그린 묘사로) 제 은행의 금고실에 남몰래 맡깁니다."], ["Wonderfully clever of him: but the Indians are just as clever on their side.", "놀랍도록 영리하지요. 허나 인도인들도 저희 편에서 꼭 그만큼 영리합니다."], ["They have their suspicions that the ‘valuable of great price’ is being shifted from one place to another; and they hit on a singularly bold and complete way of clearing those suspicions up.", "그들은 그 '값이 대단히 비싼 귀중품'이 이곳에서 저곳으로 옮겨지고 있다는 의심을 품습니다. 그리고 그들은 그 의심을 풀 유난히 대담하고 빈틈없는 방도를 찾아냅니다."], ["Whom do they seize and search? Not Mr.", "그들이 누구를 붙잡아 뒤지나요?"], ["Luker only—which would be intelligible enough—but Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite as well.", "루커 씨만이 아니라—그거라면 넉넉히 알아들을 만하지요—고드프리 에이블화이트 씨까지입니다."], ["Why? Mr.", "왜죠?"], ["Ablewhite’s explanation is, that they acted on blind suspicion, after seeing him accidentally speaking to Mr.", "에이블화이트 씨의 해명은, 그가 우연히 루커 씨와 이야기하는 것을 본 뒤, 그들이 눈먼 의심에서 그리했다는 겁니다."], ["Luker. Absurd!", "어림없어요!"], ["Half-a-dozen other people spoke to Mr. Luker that morning.", "그날 아침 예닐곱 명의 다른 사람들도 루커 씨와 이야기했어요."], ["Why were they not followed home too, and decoyed into the trap?", "어째서 그들은 집까지 미행당하고 덫으로 꾀어지지 않았나요?"], ["No! no!", "아니!"], ["The plain inference is, that Mr.", "아니에요!"], ["Ablewhite had his private interest in the ‘valuable’ as well as Mr. Luker, and that the Indians were so uncertain as to which of the two had the disposal of it, that there was no alternative but to search them both.", "명백한 추론은, 에이블화이트 씨도 루커 씨처럼 그 '귀중품'에 남모를 잇속이 있었으며, 인도인들이 그 둘 중 누가 그것을 처분할 수 있는지 어찌나 확신하지 못했던지, 둘 다 뒤지는 것 말고는 다른 방도가 없었다는 겁니다."], ["Public opinion says that, Miss Clack.", "여론이 그리 말합니다, 클랙 양."], ["And public opinion, on this occasion, is not easily refuted.”", "그리고 여론은, 이 경우에, 쉽게 반박되지 않지요.\""]], [["He said those last words, looking so wonderfully wise in his own worldly conceit, that I really (to my shame be it spoken) could not resist leading him a little farther still, before I overwhelmed him with the truth.", "그는 그 마지막 말을, 저 나름의 세속적 자만 속에서 놀랍도록 현명해 보이는 낯빛으로 뱉었는데, 그래서 나는 정말로 (낯 뜨겁게도 하는 말이지만) 진실로 그를 압도하기 전에 그를 조금 더 이끌고 나가는 것을 참을 수가 없었다."]], [["“I don’t presume to argue with a clever lawyer like you,” I said.", "\"저는 선생 같은 영리한 변호사와 논쟁할 주제가 못 됩니다.\""], ["“But is it quite fair, sir, to Mr.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["Ablewhite to pass over the opinion of the famous London police officer who investigated this case?", "\"하지만, 선생, 이 사건을 조사한 그 이름난 런던 경찰관의 견해를 그냥 넘기는 것이 에이블화이트 씨에게 온당한 일일까요?"], ["Not the shadow of a suspicion rested upon anybody but Miss Verinder, in the mind of Sergeant Cuff.”", "커프 부장의 마음속에는, 버린더 아가씨 말고는 그 누구에게도 의심의 그림자 하나 드리워지지 않았어요.\""]], [["“Do you mean to tell me, Miss Clack, that you agree with the Sergeant?”", "\"당신 지금 나더러, 클랙 양, 당신이 그 부장에게 동의한다고 말하는 겁니까?\""]], [["“I judge nobody, sir, and I offer no opinion.”", "\"저는 누구도 판단하지 않아요, 선생, 그리고 저는 아무 의견도 내지 않고요.\""]], [["“And I commit both those enormities, ma’am.", "\"그런데 나는 그 두 가지 죄악을 다 저지릅니다, 부인."], ["I judge the Sergeant to have been utterly wrong; and I offer the opinion that, if he had known Rachel’s character as I know it, he would have suspected everybody in the house but her.", "나는 그 부장이 완전히 틀렸다고 판단합니다. 그리고 나는, 만일 그가 내가 아는 만큼 레이철의 됨됨이를 알았더라면, 그 집에서 그 아이만 빼고 모두를 의심했으리라는 의견을 냅니다."], ["I admit that she has her faults—she is secret, and self-willed; odd and wild, and unlike other girls of her age.", "나는 그 아이에게 흠이 있음을 인정해요—그 아이는 속을 감추고, 고집이 세지요. 별나고 사납고, 제 또래의 다른 아이들과 다르고요."], ["But true as steel, and high-minded and generous to a fault.", "하지만 강철처럼 참되고, 흠잡을 데 없이 고결하고 너그럽습니다."], ["If the plainest evidence in the world pointed one way, and if nothing but Rachel’s word of honour pointed the other, I would take her word before the evidence, lawyer as I am!", "만일 세상에서 가장 명백한 증거가 한쪽을 가리키고, 오직 레이철의 명예를 건 말만이 다른 쪽을 가리킨다 해도, 나는, 변호사인 나로서도, 그 증거보다 그 아이의 말을 택하겠습니다!"], ["Strong language, Miss Clack; but I mean it.”", "센 말이지요, 클랙 양. 허나 나는 진심입니다.\""]], [["“Would you object to illustrate your meaning, Mr. Bruff, so that I may be sure I understand it?", "\"선생의 뜻을 예를 들어 설명해 주시겠어요, 브러프 씨, 제가 그것을 확실히 이해하도록요?"], ["Suppose you found Miss Verinder quite unaccountably interested in what has happened to Mr. Ablewhite and Mr.", "만일 선생이 버린더 아가씨가 에이블화이트 씨와 루커 씨에게 일어난 일에 아주 영문 모르게 관심을 두는 것을 발견했다고 해 봐요."], ["Luker? Suppose she asked the strangest questions about this dreadful scandal, and displayed the most ungovernable agitation when she found out the turn it was taking?”", "만일 그 아이가 이 끔찍한 추문에 대해 더없이 이상한 물음들을 던지고, 그것이 트는 방향을 알아냈을 때 더없이 다스릴 수 없는 흥분을 드러냈다고 해 봐요.\""]], [["“Suppose anything you please, Miss Clack, it wouldn’t shake my belief in Rachel Verinder by a hair’s-breadth.”", "\"당신 좋을 대로 무엇이든 가정해 보세요, 클랙 양, 그것이 레이철 버린더에 대한 내 믿음을 털끝만큼도 흔들지 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["“She is so absolutely to be relied on as that?”", "\"그렇게까지 온전히 믿고 기댈 만하다고요?\""]], [["“So absolutely to be relied on as that.”", "\"그렇게까지 온전히 믿고 기댈 만합니다.\""]], [["“Then permit me to inform you, Mr. Bruff, that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was in this house not two hours since, and that his entire innocence of all concern in the disappearance of the Moonstone was proclaimed by Miss Verinder herself, in the strongest language I ever heard used by a young lady in my life.”", "\"그럼 선생께 알려 드리게 해 주세요, 브러프 씨, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 채 두 시간도 안 되어 이 집에 있었고, 월장석의 실종과 관련한 온갖 혐의에 대한 그의 온전한 결백이, 내 평생 젊은 아가씨가 쓴 것을 들어 본 가운데 가장 센 말로, 버린더 아가씨 자신에 의해 선언되었다는 것을요.\""]], [["I enjoyed the triumph—the unholy triumph, I fear I must admit—of seeing Mr. Bruff utterly confounded and overthrown by a few plain words from Me.", "나는 이 가엾은 나의 몇 마디 명백한 말에 브러프 씨가 완전히 당황하고 무너지는 것을 보는 승리감을—낯 뜨겁게도 인정하거니와, 그 거룩하지 못한 승리감을—맛보았다."], ["He started to his feet, and stared at me in silence.", "그는 벌떡 일어서서, 말없이 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["I kept my seat, undisturbed, and related the whole scene as it had occurred.", "나는 흔들림 없이 내 자리를 지키고, 그 온 장면을 일어난 그대로 이야기했다."], ["“And what do you say about Mr. Ablewhite now?”", "\"그럼 이제 에이블화이트 씨에 대해 뭐라 하시겠어요?\""], ["I asked, with the utmost possible gentleness, as soon as I had done.", "하고 나는, 이야기를 마치자마자, 될 수 있는 대로 더없이 부드럽게 물었다."]], [["“If Rachel has testified to his innocence, Miss Clack, I don’t scruple to say that I believe in his innocence as firmly as you do.", "\"레이철이 그의 결백을 증언했다면, 클랙 양, 나는 서슴없이 말하겠습니다, 나도 당신만큼 굳게 그의 결백을 믿는다고요."], ["I have been misled by appearances, like the rest of the world; and I will make the best atonement I can, by publicly contradicting the scandal which has assailed your friend wherever I meet with it.", "나도 세상 나머지처럼 겉모습에 속아 넘어갔던 겁니다. 그리고 나는 할 수 있는 최선의 속죄를, 당신 벗을 덮친 그 추문을 어디서 마주치든 드러내 놓고 반박함으로써 하겠습니다."], ["In the meantime, allow me to congratulate you on the masterly manner in which you have opened the full fire of your batteries on me at the moment when I least expected it.", "그러는 동안, 당신이 내가 가장 뜻하지 않은 순간에 나를 향해 당신 포대의 전면적인 포화를 열어젖힌 그 능란한 솜씨에 대해 축하하게 해 주세요."], ["You would have done great things in my profession, ma’am, if you had happened to be a man.”", "당신이 마침 사내였다면, 부인, 당신은 내 직업에서 대단한 일을 해냈을 겁니다.\""]], [["With those words he turned away from me, and began walking irritably up and down the room.", "그 말과 함께 그는 나에게서 몸을 돌려, 짜증스럽게 방 안을 오락가락 걷기 시작했다."]], [["I could see plainly that the new light I had thrown on the subject had greatly surprised and disturbed him.", "내가 그 화제에 던진 새 빛이 그를 크게 놀라게 하고 심란하게 했음을 나는 뻔히 알 수 있었다."], ["Certain expressions dropped from his lips, as he became more and more absorbed in his own thoughts, which suggested to my mind the abominable view that he had hitherto taken of the mystery of the lost Moonstone.", "그가 그 자신의 생각에 점점 더 빠져들면서, 어떤 표현들이 그의 입술에서 새어 나왔는데, 그것들은 그가 여태껏 잃어버린 월장석의 수수께끼에 대해 취해 온 그 얄미운 견해를 내 마음에 넌지시 알려 주었다."], ["He had not scrupled to suspect dear Mr. Godfrey of the infamy of stealing the Diamond, and to attribute Rachel’s conduct to a generous resolution to conceal the crime.", "그는 서슴없이 사랑하는 고드프리 씨를 다이아몬드를 훔친 파렴치의 혐의로 의심하고, 레이철의 행동을 그 죄를 감추려는 갸륵한 결심 탓으로 돌린 것이었다."], ["On Miss Verinder’s own authority—a perfectly unassailable authority, as you are aware, in the estimation of Mr. Bruff—that explanation of the circumstances was now shown to be utterly wrong.", "버린더 아가씨 자신의 확언으로—여러분도 아시다시피, 브러프 씨의 평가에서는 더없이 반박할 수 없는 확언으로—그 정황에 대한 그 설명이 이제 완전히 틀린 것으로 드러났다."], ["The perplexity into which I had plunged this high legal authority was so overwhelming that he was quite unable to conceal it from notice.", "내가 이 높은 법률적 권위자를 빠뜨려 넣은 그 당혹감이 어찌나 압도적이던지 그는 그것을 남의 눈에서 도무지 감출 수가 없었다."], ["“What a case!”", "\"이 무슨 사건인가!\""], ["I heard him say to himself, stopping at the window in his walk, and drumming on the glass with his fingers.", "하고 그가, 오락가락하다 창가에 멈춰 서서, 손가락으로 유리를 두드리며 혼잣말하는 소리가 들렸다."], ["“It not only defies explanation, it’s even beyond conjecture.”", "\"이건 설명을 거스를 뿐만 아니라, 억측조차 넘어서는군.\""]], [["There was nothing in these words which made any reply at all needful, on my part—and yet, I answered them!", "이 말들에는 내 쪽에서 어떤 대꾸도 조금도 필요하게 만드는 것이 없었다—그런데도, 나는 그 말들에 대꾸했다!"], ["It seems hardly credible that I should not have been able to let Mr. Bruff alone, even now.", "이제 와서까지도 내가 브러프 씨를 가만 놓아둘 수 없었다는 것은 좀처럼 믿기지 않는 노릇인 듯싶다."], ["It seems almost beyond mere mortal perversity that I should have discovered, in what he had just said, a new opportunity of making myself personally disagreeable to him.", "그가 방금 한 말 속에서, 그에게 나 자신을 개인적으로 밉살스럽게 굴 새로운 기회를 발견했다는 것은 한낱 인간의 심술을 거의 넘어서는 노릇인 듯싶다."], ["But—ah, my friends!", "허나—아, 나의 벗들이여!"], ["nothing is beyond mortal perversity; and anything is credible when our fallen natures get the better of us!", "무엇도 인간의 심술을 넘어서지 못한다. 그리고 우리의 타락한 본성이 우리를 이길 때는 무엇이든 믿을 만하다!"]], [["“Pardon me for intruding on your reflections,”", "\"선생의 상념을 방해해서 죄송해요.\""], ["I said to the unsuspecting Mr. Bruff.", "하고 나는 아무것도 눈치채지 못한 브러프 씨에게 말했다."], ["“But surely there is a conjecture to make which has not occurred to us yet.”", "\"하지만 우리에게 아직 떠오르지 않은, 해 봄 직한 억측이 하나 분명 있어요.\""]], [["“Maybe, Miss Clack.", "\"어쩌면요, 클랙 양."], ["I own I don’t know what it is.”", "그게 무엇인지 나는 모르겠습니다만.\""]], [["“Before I was so fortunate, sir, as to convince you of Mr. Ablewhite’s innocence, you mentioned it as one of the reasons for suspecting him, that he was in the house at the time when the Diamond was lost.", "\"제가, 선생, 다행히도 선생께 에이블화이트 씨의 결백을 납득시키기 전에, 선생은 그를 의심하는 까닭 가운데 하나로, 다이아몬드를 잃었을 때 그가 그 집에 있었다는 것을 언급하셨어요."], ["Permit me to remind you that Mr. Franklin Blake was also in the house at the time when the Diamond was lost.”", "프랭클린 블레이크 씨도 다이아몬드를 잃었을 때 그 집에 있었다는 것을 상기시켜 드리게 해 주세요.\""]], [["The old worldling left the window, took a chair exactly opposite to mine, and looked at me steadily, with a hard and vicious smile.", "그 늙은 속물은 창가를 떠나, 내 맞은편에 딱 마주 보이는 의자를 잡아 앉고는, 딱딱하고 짓궂은 미소로 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."]], [["“You are not so good a lawyer, Miss Clack,”", "\"당신은 내가 생각했던 것만큼, 클랙 양,\""], ["he remarked in a meditative manner, “as I supposed.", "하고 그가 명상에 잠긴 투로 짚었다. \"훌륭한 변호사는 못 되는군요."], ["You don’t know how to let well alone.”", "당신은 좋은 것을 그냥 놓아둘 줄을 몰라요.\""]], [["“I am afraid I fail to follow you, Mr.", "\"선생 말씀을 못 따라가겠어요, 브러프 씨.\""], ["Bruff,” I said, modestly.", "하고 나는 겸손히 말했다."]], [["“It won’t do, Miss Clack—it really won’t do a second time.", "\"그건 안 통해요, 클랙 양—두 번째로는 정말 안 통합니다."], ["Franklin Blake is a prime favourite of mine, as you are well aware.", "프랭클린 블레이크는, 당신도 잘 알다시피, 내가 아주 아끼는 사람입니다."], ["But that doesn’t matter.", "하지만 그건 대수롭지 않아요."], ["I’ll adopt your view, on this occasion, before you have time to turn round on me.", "나는 이번에는, 당신이 나를 향해 돌아설 겨를도 없이, 당신 견해를 받아들이겠습니다."], ["You’re quite right, ma’am.", "당신 말이 아주 옳아요, 부인."], ["I have suspected Mr. Ablewhite, on grounds which abstractedly justify suspecting Mr.", "나는 에이블화이트 씨를, 원리로 따지면 블레이크 씨도 의심하는 것이 정당해지는 근거에서 의심했습니다."], ["Blake too. Very good—let’s suspect them together.", "아주 좋아요—그 둘을 함께 의심합시다."], ["It’s quite in his character, we will say, to be capable of stealing the Moonstone.", "그가 월장석을 훔칠 만하다는 것은, 우리가 말하자면, 그의 됨됨이에 넉넉히 들어맞습니다."], ["The only question is, whether it was his interest to do so.”", "유일한 물음은, 그리하는 것이 그의 잇속이었느냐 하는 겁니다.\""]], [["“Mr. Franklin Blake’s debts,”", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 빚은,\""], ["I remarked, “are matters of family notoriety.”", "하고 나는 짚었다. \"집안에 널리 알려진 일이지요.\""]], [["“And Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s debts have not arrived at that stage of development yet.", "\"그런데 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 빚은 아직 그런 단계에 이르지 않았지요."], ["Quite true.", "아주 옳은 말입니다."], ["But there happen to be two difficulties in the way of your theory, Miss Clack.", "하지만 당신 이론에는 마침 두 가지 걸림돌이 있어요, 클랙 양."], ["I manage Franklin Blake’s affairs, and I beg to inform you that the vast majority of his creditors (knowing his father to be a rich man) are quite content to charge interest on their debts, and to wait for their money.", "나는 프랭클린 블레이크의 일을 관리하는데, 당신께 알려 드리지요, 그의 채권자 대다수가 (그의 아버지가 부자임을 아는 터라) 제 빚에 이자를 물리고 제 돈을 기다리는 것에 아주 만족한다고요."], ["There is the first difficulty—which is tough enough.", "거기 첫 번째 걸림돌이 있어요—이것도 넉넉히 벅차지요."], ["You will find the second tougher still.", "두 번째는 더 벅차다는 것을 알게 될 겁니다."], ["I have it on the authority of Lady Verinder herself, that her daughter was ready to marry Franklin Blake, before that infernal Indian Diamond disappeared from the house.", "나는 레이디 버린더 자신의 확언으로, 그 지긋지긋한 인도 다이아몬드가 집에서 사라지기 전에, 그 따님이 프랭클린 블레이크와 혼인할 채비가 되어 있었다는 것을 알고 있습니다."], ["She had drawn him on and put him off again, with the coquetry of a young girl.", "그 아이는 젊은 아가씨의 요염함으로 그를 끌어들였다 다시 밀어냈다 했지요."], ["But she had confessed to her mother that she loved cousin Franklin, and her mother had trusted cousin Franklin with the secret.", "하지만 그 아이는 사촌 프랭클린을 사랑한다고 제 어머니에게 털어놓았고, 그 어머니는 사촌 프랭클린에게 그 비밀을 맡겼습니다."], ["So there he was, Miss Clack, with his creditors content to wait, and with the certain prospect before him of marrying an heiress.", "그러니 거기 그가 있었던 겁니다, 클랙 양, 채권자들은 기다리는 것에 만족하고, 앞에는 상속녀와 혼인할 확실한 전망이 놓인 채로 말이지요."], ["By all means consider him a scoundrel; but tell me, if you please, why he should steal the Moonstone?”", "얼마든지 그를 악당으로 여기세요. 하지만 말해 주세요, 부디, 어째서 그가 월장석을 훔쳐야 한단 말입니까?\""]], [["“The human heart is unsearchable,”", "\"사람의 마음은 헤아릴 수 없지요.\""], ["I said gently.", "하고 나는 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“Who is to fathom it?”", "\"누가 그것을 가늠하겠어요?\""]], [["“In other words, ma’am—though he hadn’t the shadow of a reason for taking the Diamond—he might have taken it, nevertheless, through natural depravity.", "\"다시 말해, 부인—그가 다이아몬드를 가져갈 이유의 그림자 하나 없었을지라도—그럼에도 그가, 타고난 사악함으로, 그것을 가져갔을 수도 있다는 거군요. 아주 좋아요."], ["Very well. Say he did.", "그가 그랬다고 칩시다."], ["Why the devil——”", "그럼 대체 어째서——\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr. Bruff.", "\"죄송합니다만, 브러프 씨."], ["If I hear the devil referred to in that manner, I must leave the room.”", "악마가 그런 식으로 입에 오르는 것을 들으면, 저는 이 방을 떠나야겠어요.\""]], [["“I beg your pardon, Miss Clack—I’ll be more careful in my choice of language for the future.", "\"죄송합니다, 클랙 양—앞으로는 말을 고르는 데 더 조심하지요."], ["All I meant to ask was this.", "내가 묻고자 한 것은 다만 이것입니다."], ["Why—even supposing he did take the Diamond—should Franklin Blake make himself the most prominent person in the house in trying to recover it?", "어째서—그가 다이아몬드를 가져갔다고 가정하더라도—프랭클린 블레이크가 그것을 되찾는 데에 그 집에서 가장 두드러진 사람으로 나섰겠느냐 하는 겁니다."], ["You may tell me he cunningly did that to divert suspicion from himself.", "당신은 나더러, 그가 저 자신에게서 의심을 돌리려고 교활하게 그랬다고 말할지 모르지요."], ["I answer that he had no need to divert suspicion—because nobody suspected him.", "나는 그가 의심을 돌릴 필요가 없었다고 답합니다—아무도 그를 의심하지 않았으니까요."], ["He first steals the Moonstone (without the slightest reason) through natural depravity; and he then acts a part, in relation to the loss of the jewel, which there is not the slightest necessity to act, and which leads to his mortally offending the young lady who would otherwise have married him.", "그는 먼저 (조금의 이유도 없이) 타고난 사악함으로 월장석을 훔치고, 그런 다음 그 보석의 분실과 관련하여 조금도 할 필요가 없는 역할을 연기하는데, 그 역할이 그로 하여금, 안 그랬다면 그와 혼인했을 그 젊은 아가씨의 비위를 죽도록 거스르게 만듭니다."], ["That is the monstrous proposition which you are driven to assert, if you attempt to associate the disappearance of the Moonstone with Franklin Blake.", "그것이, 당신이 월장석의 실종을 프랭클린 블레이크와 엮으려 시도한다면, 당신이 어쩔 수 없이 주장하게 되는 그 터무니없는 명제입니다."], ["No, no, Miss Clack!", "아니, 아니에요, 클랙 양!"], ["After what has passed here today, between us two, the dead-lock, in this case, is complete.", "오늘 여기서, 우리 둘 사이에 오간 일 뒤로, 이 사건에서, 그 교착은 완전합니다."], ["Rachel’s own innocence is (as her mother knows, and as I know) beyond a doubt.", "레이철 자신의 결백은 (그 어머니가 알고, 내가 알듯이) 의심할 여지가 없어요."], ["Mr. Ablewhite’s innocence is equally certain—or Rachel would never have testified to it.", "에이블화이트 씨의 결백도 똑같이 확실하고요—안 그랬다면 레이철이 결코 그것을 증언하지 않았을 겁니다."], ["And Franklin Blake’s innocence, as you have just seen, unanswerably asserts itself.", "그리고 프랭클린 블레이크의 결백은, 당신이 방금 보았듯이, 반박할 수 없이 스스로를 주장합니다."], ["On the one hand, we are morally certain of all these things.", "한편으로, 우리는 이 모든 것을 도덕적으로 확신합니다."], ["And, on the other hand, we are equally sure that somebody has brought the Moonstone to London, and that Mr. Luker, or his banker, is in private possession of it at this moment.", "그리고, 다른 한편으로, 우리는 누군가가 월장석을 런던으로 가져왔으며, 루커 씨나 그의 은행이 바로 이 순간 그것을 남몰래 지니고 있다는 것도 똑같이 확신하고요."], ["What is the use of my experience, what is the use of any person’s experience, in such a case as that?", "그런 사건에서, 내 경험이 무슨 소용입니까, 어느 누구의 경험인들 무슨 소용이고요? 그것은 나를 쩔쩔매게 합니다."], ["It baffles me; it baffles you, it baffles everybody.”", "그것은 당신을 쩔쩔매게 하고, 그것은 모두를 쩔쩔매게 하지요.\""]], [["No—not everybody.", "아니—모두는 아니었다."], ["It had not baffled Sergeant Cuff.", "그것은 커프 부장을 쩔쩔매게 하지 못했다."], ["I was about to mention this, with all possible mildness, and with every necessary protest against being supposed to cast a slur upon Rachel—when the servant came in to say that the doctor had gone, and that my aunt was waiting to receive us.", "나는, 될 수 있는 대로 더없이 부드럽게, 그리고 레이철에게 흠집을 내려는 것으로 여겨지는 데 대한 온갖 필요한 항변과 더불어, 이것을 언급하려던 참이었는데—그때 하인이 들어와, 의사가 갔으며, 이모님이 우리를 맞으려 기다리신다고 전했다."]], [["This stopped the discussion. Mr.", "이것이 그 논쟁을 멈추게 했다."], ["Bruff collected his papers, looking a little exhausted by the demands which our conversation had made on him.", "브러프 씨는, 우리 대화가 그에게 요구한 것들에 다소 지친 듯한 낯빛으로, 제 서류를 그러모았다."], ["I took up my bag-full of precious publications, feeling as if I could have gone on talking for hours.", "나는 소중한 간행물로 가득 찬 내 가방을 집어 들었는데, 몇 시간이라도 계속 이야기할 수 있을 것 같은 느낌이었다."], ["We proceeded in silence to Lady Verinder’s room.", "우리는 말없이 레이디 버린더의 방으로 나아갔다."]], [["Permit me to add here, before my narrative advances to other events, that I have not described what passed between the lawyer and me, without having a definite object in view.", "나의 이야기가 다른 일들로 나아가기 전에, 여기에 덧붙이게 해 다오, 내가 그 변호사와 나 사이에 오간 일을 묘사한 것이 뚜렷한 목적 없이 한 것이 아님을."], ["I am ordered to include in my contribution to the shocking story of the Moonstone a plain disclosure, not only of the turn which suspicion took, but even of the names of the persons on whom suspicion rested, at the time when the Indian Diamond was believed to be in London.", "나는 월장석의 그 충격적인 이야기에 대한 나의 기고에, 그 인도 다이아몬드가 런던에 있다고 여겨지던 그때, 의심이 튼 방향뿐만 아니라, 의심이 드리워진 사람들의 이름까지 명백히 밝히도록 명령받았다."], ["A report of my conversation in the library with Mr. Bruff appeared to me to be exactly what was wanted to answer this purpose—while, at the same time, it possessed the great moral advantage of rendering a sacrifice of sinful self-esteem essentially necessary on my part.", "서재에서의 브러프 씨와의 대화에 대한 기록이, 이 목적에 답하기에 딱 필요한 것으로 내게 여겨졌다—그러면서, 동시에, 그것은 나의 편에서 죄스러운 자존심의 희생을 본질적으로 필요하게 만드는 그 큰 도덕적 이점을 지녔다."], ["I have been obliged to acknowledge that my fallen nature got the better of me.", "나는 나의 타락한 본성이 나를 이겼음을 인정할 수밖에 없었다."], ["In making that humiliating confession, I get the better of my fallen nature.", "그 창피한 고백을 함으로써, 나는 나의 타락한 본성을 이긴다."], ["The moral balance is restored; the spiritual atmosphere feels clear once more.", "도덕의 저울이 회복된다. 영적인 공기가 다시 한번 맑아지는 느낌이다."], ["Dear friends, we may go on again.", "사랑하는 벗들이여, 우리는 다시 나아가도 되겠다."]], [["# CHAPTER IV", "# CHAPTER IV"]], [["The signing of the Will was a much shorter matter than I had anticipated.", "유언장에 서명하는 일은 내가 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 짧게 끝났다."], ["It was hurried over, to my thinking, in indecent haste.", "그것은, 내 생각으로는, 볼썽사나울 만큼 서둘러 후딱 해치워졌다."], ["Samuel, the footman, was sent for to act as second witness—and the pen was put at once into my aunt’s hand.", "두 번째 증인 노릇을 하도록 남자 하인 새뮤얼이 불려 왔다—그리고 펜이 대뜸 이모님 손에 쥐어졌다."], ["I felt strongly urged to say a few appropriate words on this solemn occasion.", "나는 이 엄숙한 자리에 몇 마디 알맞은 말을 하고 싶은 강한 충동을 느꼈다."], ["But Mr. Bruff’s manner convinced me that it was wisest to check the impulse while he was in the room.", "허나 브러프 씨의 태도가 나로 하여금, 그가 방에 있는 동안에는 그 충동을 억누르는 것이 가장 슬기롭다고 확신하게 했다."], ["In less than two minutes it was all over—and Samuel (unbenefited by what I might have said) had gone downstairs again.", "채 이 분도 안 되어 그것은 죄다 끝났다—그리고 새뮤얼은 (내가 했을 법한 말의 덕은 보지 못한 채) 다시 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["Mr. Bruff folded up the Will, and then looked my way; apparently wondering whether I did or did not mean to leave him alone with my aunt.", "브러프 씨는 유언장을 접고는, 내 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["I had my mission of mercy to fulfil, and my bag of precious publications ready on my lap.", "보아하니 내가 그를 이모님과 단둘이 남겨 둘 뜻이 있는지 없는지 궁금해하는 듯했다. 나에게는 이루어야 할 자비의 사명이 있었고, 소중한 간행물이 든 내 가방이 무릎 위에 준비되어 있었다."], ["He might as well have expected to move St. Paul’s Cathedral by looking at it, as to move Me.", "그가 나를 움직이려 하느니, 세인트 폴 대성당을 바라봄으로써 그것을 움직이려 기대하는 편이 나았으리라."], ["There was one merit about him (due no doubt to his worldly training) which I have no wish to deny.", "그에게는 (의심할 것 없이 그의 세속적 훈련 덕분인) 한 가지 장점이 있었는데, 나는 그것을 부인할 뜻이 없다."], ["He was quick at seeing things.", "그는 사물을 재빨리 알아챘다."], ["I appeared to produce almost the same impression on him which I had produced on the cabman.", "나는 그에게, 내가 그 마부에게 냈던 것과 거의 똑같은 인상을 내는 듯싶었다."], ["He too uttered a profane expression, and withdrew in a violent hurry, and left me mistress of the field.", "그도 불경한 표현을 뱉고, 몹시 부산스럽게 물러가, 나를 이 싸움터의 주인으로 남겨 두었다."]], [["As soon as we were alone, my aunt reclined on the sofa, and then alluded, with some appearance of confusion, to the subject of her Will.", "우리 둘만 남자마자, 이모님은 소파에 기대어 누우시고는, 얼마쯤 당혹스러운 낯빛으로, 당신 유언장이라는 화제를 넌지시 이르셨다."]], [["“I hope you won’t think yourself neglected, Drusilla,” she said.", "\"네가 소홀히 대접받았다고 여기지 않았으면 좋겠구나, 드루실라.\""], ["“I mean to give you your little legacy, my dear, with my own hand.”", "하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"나는 너의 그 조그만 유산을, 얘야, 내 손으로 직접 주려는 거란다.\""]], [["Here was a golden opportunity!", "여기 황금 같은 기회가 있었다!"], ["I seized it on the spot.", "나는 그 자리에서 그것을 붙잡았다."], ["In other words, I instantly opened my bag, and took out the top publication.", "다시 말해, 나는 대뜸 내 가방을 열어, 맨 위의 간행물을 꺼냈다."], ["It proved to be an early edition—only the twenty-fifth—of the famous anonymous work (believed to be by precious Miss Bellows), entitled The Serpent at Home. The design of the book—with which the worldly reader may not be acquainted—is to show how the Evil One lies in wait for us in all the most apparently innocent actions of our daily lives.", "그것은 「집 안의 뱀」이라는 제목의 그 이름난 익명 저작(소중한 벨로스 양의 것으로 여겨진다)의 초기 판—겨우 스물다섯 번째 판—으로 드러났다."], ["The chapters best adapted to female perusal are “Satan in the Hair Brush;” “Satan behind the Looking Glass;”", "그 책의 취지는—세속적 독자는 익숙하지 않을지 모르나—악한 자가 우리의 나날의 삶에서 겉보기에 가장 죄 없어 보이는 온갖 행동 속에 어떻게 우리를 노리고 도사리는지를 보여 주는 것이다."], ["“Satan under the Tea Table;” “Satan out of the Window”—and many others.", "여자가 읽기에 가장 알맞은 장들은 '머리빗 속의 사탄', '거울 뒤의 사탄', '찻상 밑의 사탄', '창밖의 사탄'—그 밖에도 여럿이다."]], [["“Give your attention, dear aunt, to this precious book—and you will give me all I ask.”", "\"이 소중한 책에 정신을 쏟아 주세요, 사랑하는 이모님—그러면 이모님은 제가 청하는 모든 것을 제게 주시는 거예요.\""], ["With those words, I handed it to her open, at a marked passage—one continuous burst of burning eloquence!", "그 말과 함께, 나는 그것을, 표시해 둔 대목에 펼쳐서 이모님께 건넸다—타오르는 웅변의 끊임없는 폭발 한 줄기였다!"], ["Subject: Satan among the Sofa Cushions.", "주제는, 소파 방석 사이의 사탄."]], [["Poor Lady Verinder (reclining thoughtlessly on her own sofa cushions) glanced at the book, and handed it back to me looking more confused than ever.", "가엾은 레이디 버린더는 (당신 자신의 소파 방석에 아무 생각 없이 기대어 누우신 채) 그 책을 흘긋 보시고는, 그 어느 때보다 더 당혹스러운 낯빛으로 그것을 내게 도로 건네셨다."]], [["“I’m afraid, Drusilla,”", "\"미안하다만, 드루실라,\""], ["she said, “I must wait till I am a little better, before I can read that.", "하고 이모님이 말씀하셨다. \"그것을 읽으려면 내가 조금 더 나아질 때까지 기다려야겠구나."], ["The doctor——”", "의사가——\""]], [["The moment she mentioned the doctor’s name, I knew what was coming.", "이모님이 의사의 이름을 언급하시는 그 순간, 나는 무엇이 올지 알았다."], ["Over and over again in my past experience among my perishing fellow-creatures, the members of the notoriously infidel profession of Medicine had stepped between me and my mission of mercy—on the miserable pretence that the patient wanted quiet, and that the disturbing influence of all others which they most dreaded, was the influence of Miss Clack and her Books.", "스러져 가는 동료 인간들 사이에서 겪은 나의 지난 경험 속에서 몇 번이고 거듭, 그 악명 높이 불신앙적인 직업인 의료의 종사자들이 나와 나의 자비의 사명 사이에 끼어들곤 했다—환자에게 안정이 필요하며, 그들이 다른 무엇보다 가장 두려워하는 그 방해가 되는 영향이란 곧 클랙 양과 그녀의 책들의 영향이라는 비참한 핑계로 말이다."], ["Precisely the same blinded materialism (working treacherously behind my back) now sought to rob me of the only right of property that my poverty could claim—my right of spiritual property in my perishing aunt.", "바로 그 똑같은 눈먼 유물론이 (내 등 뒤에서 음험하게 작동하며) 이제 나의 가난이 내세울 수 있는 유일한 소유권을—나의 스러져 가는 이모님에 대한 나의 영적 소유권을—내게서 빼앗으려 들었다."]], [["“The doctor tells me,”", "\"의사가 내게 이르기를,\""], ["my poor misguided relative went on, “that I am not so well today.", "하고 나의 가엾고 그릇 인도된 친척이 말을 이으셨다."], ["He forbids me to see any strangers; and he orders me, if I read at all, only to read the lightest and the most amusing books.", "\"오늘은 내가 그리 좋지 않다는구나. 그는 내가 낯선 이를 만나는 것을 금하고, 만일 읽는다면, 가장 가볍고 가장 재미난 책만 읽으라고 이르는구나."], ["‘Do nothing, Lady Verinder, to weary your head, or to quicken your pulse’—those were his last words, Drusilla, when he left me today.”", "'아무것도 하지 마십시오, 레이디 버린더, 머리를 지치게 하거나, 맥박을 빠르게 할 만한 것은요'—그것이, 드루실라, 그가 오늘 나를 떠날 때 남긴 마지막 말이었어.\""]], [["There was no help for it but to yield again—for the moment only, as before.", "다시 물러서는 수밖에 없었다—전처럼, 그저 그때만이다."], ["Any open assertion of the infinitely superior importance of such a ministry as mine, compared with the ministry of the medical man, would only have provoked the doctor to practise on the human weakness of his patient, and to threaten to throw up the case.", "의료인의 봉직에 견주어, 나의 봉직 같은 것이 한없이 더 중대하다고 드러내 놓고 주장하는 것은, 그저 그 의사를 부추겨 제 환자의 인간적 나약함을 이용하게 하고, 그 사건을 내던지겠다고 으름장 놓게 할 뿐이었으리라."], ["Happily, there are more ways than one of sowing the good seed, and few persons are better versed in those ways than myself.", "다행히도, 좋은 씨앗을 뿌리는 데는 한 가지 이상의 길이 있고, 그 길들에 나보다 더 밝은 사람은 드물다."]], [["“You might feel stronger, dear, in an hour or two,” I said.", "\"한두 시간 뒤면 기운이 나실지도 몰라요, 이모님.\""], ["“Or you might wake, tomorrow morning, with a sense of something wanting, and even this unpretending volume might be able to supply it.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"아니면 내일 아침, 무언가 부족한 느낌으로 깨어나실지도 모르고요, 그때는 이 수수한 책 한 권이 그것을 채워 드릴 수 있을지도 몰라요."], ["You will let me leave the book, aunt?", "책을 두고 가게 해 주실 거죠, 이모님?"], ["The doctor can hardly object to that!”", "의사도 그것만은 좀처럼 반대하지 못할 거예요!\""]], [["I slipped it under the sofa cushions, half in, and half out, close by her handkerchief, and her smelling-bottle.", "나는 그것을, 반은 안으로 반은 밖으로, 이모님의 손수건과 냄새 맡는 약병 바로 곁, 소파 방석 밑에 슬며시 밀어 넣었다."], ["Every time her hand searched for either of these, it would touch the book; and, sooner or later (who knows?) the book might touch her.", "이모님 손이 그 둘 중 하나를 찾을 때마다, 그것은 그 책에 닿을 터였다. 그리고, 이르든 늦든 (누가 알랴?) 그 책이 이모님께 닿을지도 몰랐다."], ["After making this arrangement, I thought it wise to withdraw.", "이렇게 마련해 놓고, 나는 물러가는 것이 슬기롭다고 여겼다."], ["“Let me leave you to repose, dear aunt; I will call again tomorrow.”", "\"쉬시게 두고 가겠어요, 사랑하는 이모님. 내일 다시 들르겠어요.\""], ["I looked accidentally towards the window as I said that.", "나는 그 말을 하며 우연히 창 쪽을 바라보았다."], ["It was full of flowers, in boxes and pots.", "그것은 상자와 화분에 담긴 꽃들로 가득했다."], ["Lady Verinder was extravagantly fond of these perishable treasures, and had a habit of rising every now and then, and going to look at them and smell them.", "레이디 버린더는 이 스러지기 쉬운 보물들을 유난히 좋아하셔서, 이따금 일어나, 그것들을 보고 냄새 맡으러 가는 버릇이 있으셨다."], ["A new idea flashed across my mind.", "새로운 생각 하나가 내 마음을 스쳤다."], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["may I take a flower?”", "꽃 한 송이 가져가도 될까요?\""], ["I said—and got to the window unsuspected, in that way.", "하고 나는 말했다—그리고 그런 식으로, 의심받지 않고 창가로 갔다."], ["Instead of taking away a flower, I added one, in the shape of another book from my bag, which I left, to surprise my aunt, among the geraniums and roses.", "꽃 한 송이를 가져가는 대신, 나는 하나를 보탰는데, 내 가방에서 꺼낸 또 다른 책이라는 모양으로, 이모님을 놀래 드리려고, 그것을 제라늄과 장미 사이에 남겨 두었다."], ["The happy thought followed, “Why not do the same for her, poor dear, in every other room that she enters?”", "이런 기특한 생각이 뒤따랐다, \"어째서 이모님이, 가엾은 이가, 들어가는 다른 모든 방에서도 똑같이 해 드리지 않으랴?\""], ["I immediately said good-bye; and, crossing the hall, slipped into the library.", "나는 즉시 작별을 고했다. 그리고, 현관을 가로질러, 서재로 슬며시 들어갔다."], ["Samuel, coming up to let me out, and supposing I had gone, went downstairs again.", "나를 내보내 주려고 올라온 새뮤얼은, 내가 갔다고 여기고, 다시 아래층으로 내려갔다."], ["On the library table I noticed two of the “amusing books”", "서재 탁자 위에서 나는 그 불신앙적인 의사가 권한 '재미난 책'"], ["which the infidel doctor had recommended.", "두 권을 알아챘다."], ["I instantly covered them from sight with two of my own precious publications.", "나는 즉시 그것들을 나 자신의 소중한 간행물 두 권으로 보이지 않게 덮었다."], ["In the breakfast-room I found my aunt’s favourite canary singing in his cage.", "아침 식사방에서 나는 이모님이 가장 아끼는 카나리아가 새장 안에서 노래하는 것을 발견했다."], ["She was always in the habit of feeding the bird herself.", "이모님은 언제나 그 새에게 손수 모이를 주는 버릇이 있으셨다."], ["Some groundsel was strewed on a table which stood immediately under the cage.", "새장 바로 아래 놓인 탁자 위에 개쑥갓이 뿌려져 있었다."], ["I put a book among the groundsel.", "나는 그 개쑥갓 사이에 책 한 권을 놓았다."], ["In the drawing-room I found more cheering opportunities of emptying my bag.", "응접실에서 나는 내 가방을 비울 더 기쁜 기회들을 발견했다."], ["My aunt’s favourite musical pieces were on the piano.", "이모님이 가장 아끼는 악보들이 피아노 위에 있었다."], ["I slipped in two more books among the music.", "나는 그 악보 사이에 책 두 권을 더 슬며시 끼워 넣었다."], ["I disposed of another in the back drawing-room, under some unfinished embroidery, which I knew to be of Lady Verinder’s working.", "나는 또 한 권을, 레이디 버린더의 솜씨임을 내가 아는 어떤 끝나지 않은 자수 밑, 뒤편 응접실에 두었다."], ["A third little room opened out of the back drawing-room, from which it was shut off by curtains instead of a door.", "뒤편 응접실에서 셋째 방이 이어졌는데, 그것은 문 대신 커튼으로 가려져 있었다."], ["My aunt’s plain old-fashioned fan was on the chimney-piece.", "이모님의 소박한 구식 부채가 벽난로 선반 위에 있었다."], ["I opened my ninth book at a very special passage, and put the fan in as a marker, to keep the place.", "나는 나의 아홉 번째 책을 아주 특별한 대목에 펼쳐, 그 자리를 표시해 두려고, 그 부채를 책갈피로 끼워 넣었다."], ["The question then came, whether I should go higher still, and try the bedroom floor—at the risk, undoubtedly, of being insulted, if the person with the cap-ribbons happened to be in the upper regions of the house, and to find me out.", "그다음 물음이 왔으니, 내가 더 높이 올라가, 침실 층까지 시험해 볼 것인가—만일 그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 마침 이 집의 위층에 있다가 나를 발견한다면, 의심할 것 없이, 모욕당할 위험을 무릅쓰고 말이다."], ["But oh, what of that?", "허나 오, 그것이 무슨 대수랴?"], ["It is a poor Christian that is afraid of being insulted.", "모욕당하는 것을 두려워한다면 가엾은 기독교인이다."], ["I went upstairs, prepared to bear anything.", "나는 무엇이든 견딜 채비를 하고 위층으로 올라갔다."], ["All was silent and solitary—it was the servants’ tea-time, I suppose.", "온통 고요하고 인적이 없었다—하인들의 차 마시는 시간이었으리라."], ["My aunt’s room was in front.", "이모님의 방이 앞쪽에 있었다."], ["The miniature of my late dear uncle, Sir John, hung on the wall opposite the bed.", "내 돌아가신 사랑하는 외숙부 존 경의 소형 초상화가 침대 맞은편 벽에 걸려 있었다."], ["It seemed to smile at me; it seemed to say, “Drusilla!", "그것은 내게 미소 짓는 듯했다. 그것은 이렇게 말하는 듯했다, \"드루실라!"], ["deposit a book.”", "책을 하나 두렴.\""], ["There were tables on either side of my aunt’s bed.", "이모님 침대 양편에 탁자가 있었다."], ["She was a bad sleeper, and wanted, or thought she wanted, many things at night.", "이모님은 잠을 잘 못 이루셔서, 밤에 이것저것 필요로 하시거나, 필요로 한다고 여기셨다."], ["I put a book near the matches on one side, and a book under the box of chocolate drops on the other.", "나는 한쪽에는 성냥 곁에 책을 하나, 다른 쪽에는 초콜릿 방울 상자 밑에 책을 하나 두었다."], ["Whether she wanted a light, or whether she wanted a drop, there was a precious publication to meet her eye, or to meet her hand, and to say with silent eloquence, in either case, “Come, try me!", "이모님이 불을 원하시든, 방울을 원하시든, 이모님 눈에 들거나, 이모님 손에 닿을, 그리고 어느 경우에든 말없는 웅변으로 \"자, 저를 시험해 보세요!"], ["try me!”", "저를 시험해 보세요!\""], ["But one book was now left at the bottom of my bag, and but one apartment was still unexplored—the bath-room, which opened out of the bedroom.", "하고 말할 소중한 간행물이 있었다. 허나 이제 내 가방 밑바닥에는 책 한 권만이 남았고, 아직 답사되지 않은 방은 하나뿐이었다—침실에서 이어지는 그 욕실 말이다."], ["I peeped in; and the holy inner voice that never deceives, whispered to me, “You have met her, Drusilla, everywhere else; meet her at the bath, and the work is done.”", "나는 안을 엿보았다. 그리고 결코 속이지 않는 그 거룩한 내면의 목소리가 내게 속삭였다, \"너는 다른 모든 곳에서 이모님을 만났다, 드루실라."], ["I observed a dressing-gown thrown across a chair.", "욕실에서 이모님을 만나면, 그 일이 다 이루어진다.\""], ["It had a pocket in it, and in that pocket I put my last book.", "나는 의자에 걸쳐진 실내복을 보았다."], ["Can words express my exquisite sense of duty done, when I had slipped out of the house, unsuspected by any of them, and when I found myself in the street with my empty bag under my arm?", "그것에는 주머니가 하나 있었고, 그 주머니에 나는 나의 마지막 책을 넣었다. 내가, 그들 가운데 누구에게도 의심받지 않고, 그 집을 슬며시 빠져나와, 빈 가방을 팔에 끼고 거리에 서 있는 나 자신을 발견했을 때의, 소임을 다했다는 그 더없는 느낌을 어찌 말로 표현할 수 있으랴?"], ["Oh, my worldly friends, pursuing the phantom, Pleasure, through the guilty mazes of Dissipation, how easy it is to be happy, if you will only be good!", "오, 방탕의 죄스러운 미로를 지나, 쾌락이라는 환영을 좇는 나의 세속적 벗들이여, 그저 착하게만 굴려 한다면, 행복해지기가 얼마나 쉬운가!"]], [["When I folded up my things that night—when I reflected on the true riches which I had scattered with such a lavish hand, from top to bottom of the house of my wealthy aunt—I declare I felt as free from all anxiety as if I had been a child again.", "그날 밤 내 옷들을 갤 때—내가 나의 부유한 이모님 집 위에서 아래까지 그토록 아낌없는 손으로 흩뿌려 놓은 그 참된 재물을 되새길 때—나는 선언하거니와 마치 다시 어린아이가 된 듯 온갖 근심에서 벗어난 느낌이었다."], ["I was so light-hearted that I sang a verse of the Evening Hymn.", "나는 어찌나 마음이 가볍던지 '저녁 찬송'의 한 절을 불렀다."], ["I was so light-hearted that I fell asleep before I could sing another.", "나는 어찌나 마음이 가볍던지 또 한 절을 부르기도 전에 잠이 들었다."], ["Quite like a child again!", "아주 다시 어린아이가 된 듯!"], ["quite like a child again!", "아주 다시 어린아이가 된 듯!"]], [["So I passed that blissful night.", "그렇게 나는 그 더없이 행복한 밤을 보냈다."], ["On rising the next morning, how young I felt!", "이튿날 아침 일어날 때, 나는 얼마나 젊은 느낌이었던지!"], ["I might add, how young I looked, if I were capable of dwelling on the concerns of my own perishable body.", "내가 나 자신의 스러지기 쉬운 몸의 일들에 마음을 쏟을 수 있는 사람이라면, 내가 얼마나 젊어 보였던지도 덧붙일 수 있으련만."], ["But I am not capable—and I add nothing.", "허나 나는 그럴 수 있는 사람이 못 된다—그리고 나는 아무것도 덧붙이지 않는다."]], [["Towards luncheon time—not for the sake of the creature-comforts, but for the certainty of finding dear aunt—I put on my bonnet to go to Montagu Square.", "점심 시각 무렵—몸의 안락을 위해서가 아니라, 사랑하는 이모님을 확실히 뵙기 위해—나는 몬터규 스퀘어로 가려고 보닛을 썼다."], ["Just as I was ready, the maid at the lodgings in which I then lived looked in at the door, and said, “Lady Verinder’s servant, to see Miss Clack.”", "막 채비가 되었을 때, 그 무렵 내가 살던 셋집의 하녀가 문 안을 들여다보며 말했다, \"레이디 버린더의 하인이, 클랙 양을 뵈러 왔습니다.\""]], [["I occupied the parlour-floor, at that period of my residence in London.", "나는 그 무렵 런던 거주 시절에, 응접실 층을 쓰고 있었다."], ["The front parlour was my sitting-room.", "앞쪽 응접실이 나의 거실이었다."], ["Very small, very low in the ceiling, very poorly furnished—but, oh, so neat!", "아주 작고, 천장이 아주 낮고, 세간이 아주 초라한—허나, 오, 어찌나 말끔한지!"], ["I looked into the passage to see which of Lady Verinder’s servants had asked for me.", "나는 레이디 버린더의 하인 가운데 누가 나를 찾는지 보려고 통로를 내다보았다."], ["It was the young footman, Samuel—a civil fresh-coloured person, with a teachable look and a very obliging manner.", "그것은 그 젊은 남자 하인 새뮤얼이었다—배우려는 낯빛과 아주 싹싹한 태도를 지닌, 얌전하고 혈색 좋은 사람이었다."], ["I had always felt a spiritual interest in Samuel, and a wish to try him with a few serious words.", "나는 언제나 새뮤얼에게 영적 관심을 느껴 왔고, 몇 마디 진지한 말로 그를 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 있었다."], ["On this occasion, I invited him into my sitting-room.", "이번에는, 나는 그를 내 거실로 청해 들였다."]], [["He came in, with a large parcel under his arm.", "그는 큰 꾸러미를 팔에 끼고 들어왔다."], ["When he put the parcel down, it appeared to frighten him.", "그가 그 꾸러미를 내려놓을 때, 그것이 그를 겁먹게 하는 듯했다."], ["“My lady’s love, Miss; and I was to say that you would find a letter inside.”", "\"마님께서 안부 전하십니다, 아가씨. 그리고 안에 편지가 있다고 말씀드리라 하셨습니다.\""], ["Having given that message, the fresh-coloured young footman surprised me by looking as if he would have liked to run away.", "그 전언을 건네고 나서, 그 혈색 좋은 젊은 하인은 마치 달아나고 싶은 듯한 낯빛을 지어 나를 놀라게 했다."]], [["I detained him to make a few kind inquiries.", "나는 몇 마디 다정한 질문을 하려고 그를 붙들었다."], ["Could I see my aunt, if I called in Montagu Square?", "몬터규 스퀘어에 들르면 이모님을 뵐 수 있을까?"], ["No; she had gone out for a drive.", "아니, 이모님은 마차 산책을 나가셨다."], ["Miss Rachel had gone with her, and Mr. Ablewhite had taken a seat in the carriage, too.", "레이철 아가씨도 함께 가셨고, 에이블화이트 씨도 마차에 자리를 잡으셨다."], ["Knowing how sadly dear Mr. Godfrey’s charitable work was in arrear, I thought it odd that he should be going out driving, like an idle man.", "사랑하는 고드프리 씨의 자선 일이 얼마나 서글프게 밀려 있는지 아는 터라, 나는 그가 한가한 사내처럼 마차 산책을 나간다는 것이 이상하다고 여겼다."], ["I stopped Samuel at the door, and made a few more kind inquiries.", "나는 문가에서 새뮤얼을 멈춰 세우고, 몇 마디 더 다정한 질문을 했다."], ["Miss Rachel was going to a ball that night, and Mr. Ablewhite had arranged to come to coffee, and go with her.", "레이철 아가씨는 그날 밤 무도회에 가실 참이었고, 에이블화이트 씨는 커피를 마시러 와서 그 아이와 함께 가기로 약속되어 있었다."], ["There was a morning concert advertised for tomorrow, and Samuel was ordered to take places for a large party, including a place for Mr.", "내일 오전 음악회가 광고되어 있었고, 새뮤얼은 에이블화이트 씨의 자리를 포함해 여럿의 자리를 잡으라는 분부를 받았다."], ["Ablewhite. “All the tickets may be gone, Miss,”", "\"표가 죄다 나가 버릴지도 모릅니다, 아가씨,\""], ["said this innocent youth, “if I don’t run and get them at once!”", "하고 이 순진한 젊은이가 말했다. \"제가 얼른 달려가 당장 그것들을 구하지 않으면요!\""], ["He ran as he said the words—and I found myself alone again, with some anxious thoughts to occupy me.", "그는 그 말을 하며 달려갔다—그리고 나는, 나를 사로잡을 몇 가지 근심스러운 생각과 함께, 다시 혼자가 되었다."]], [["We had a special meeting of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes-Conversion Society that night, summoned expressly with a view to obtaining Mr. Godfrey’s advice and assistance.", "그날 밤 우리는 고드프리 씨의 조언과 도움을 얻으려는 뚜렷한 뜻으로 특별히 소집된, '어머니회 아동복 개조 협회'의 특별 모임이 있었다."], ["Instead of sustaining our sisterhood, under an overwhelming flow of Trousers which quite prostrated our little community, he had arranged to take coffee in Montagu Square, and to go to a ball afterwards!", "우리의 작은 공동체를 아주 짓눌러 버리는 그 압도적인 바지의 물결 아래 우리 자매회를 떠받치는 대신, 그는 몬터규 스퀘어에서 커피를 마시고, 그다음에 무도회에 가기로 약속해 놓은 것이었다!"], ["The afternoon of the next day had been selected for the Festival of the British-Ladies’-Servants’-Sunday-Sweetheart-Supervision Society.", "이튿날 오후는 '영국 부인회 하인 주일 애인 감독 협회'의 축제일로 잡혀 있었다."], ["Instead of being present, the life and soul of that struggling Institution, he had engaged to make one of a party of worldlings at a morning concert!", "그 고전하는 기관의 생명이자 영혼으로 참석하는 대신, 그는 오전 음악회에서 세속적인 자들의 무리 가운데 하나가 되기로 약조한 것이었다!"], ["I asked myself what did it mean?", "나는 스스로에게 물었다, 이것이 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["it meant that our Christian Hero was to reveal himself to me in a new character, and to become associated in my mind with one of the most awful backslidings of modern times.", "그것은 우리의 기독교적 영웅이 새로운 됨됨이로 내게 저를 드러내리라는 뜻이었고, 내 마음속에서 현대의 가장 끔찍한 타락 가운데 하나와 엮이게 되리라는 뜻이었다."]], [["To return, however, to the history of the passing day.", "허나, 지나가는 그날의 이야기로 돌아가자."], ["On finding myself alone in my room, I naturally turned my attention to the parcel which appeared to have so strangely intimidated the fresh-coloured young footman.", "내 방에 혼자 남은 나 자신을 발견하고, 나는 자연히 그 혈색 좋은 젊은 하인을 그토록 이상하게 겁먹게 한 듯한 그 꾸러미에 관심을 돌렸다."], ["Had my aunt sent me my promised legacy?", "이모님이 내게 약속하신 유산을 보내신 걸까?"], ["and had it taken the form of cast-off clothes, or worn-out silver spoons, or unfashionable jewellery, or anything of that sort?", "그리고 그것이 헌 옷가지나, 닳은 은수저나, 유행 지난 장신구나, 그런 종류의 것이라는 모양을 취한 걸까?"], ["Prepared to accept all, and to resent nothing, I opened the parcel—and what met my view?", "무엇이든 받아들이고, 무엇에도 발끈하지 않을 채비를 하고, 나는 그 꾸러미를 열었다—그러자 무엇이 내 눈에 들어왔던가?"], ["The twelve precious publications which I had scattered through the house, on the previous day; all returned to me by the doctor’s orders!", "내가 그 전날 온 집 안에 흩뿌려 놓았던 그 열두 권의 소중한 간행물이, 의사의 분부로 죄다 내게 되돌려진 것이었다!"], ["Well might the youthful Samuel shrink when he brought his parcel into my room!", "젊은 새뮤얼이 제 꾸러미를 내 방으로 들여올 때 움츠러들 만도 했구나!"], ["Well might he run when he had performed his miserable errand!", "그 비참한 심부름을 마치고 나서 그가 달아날 만도 했구나!"], ["As to my aunt’s letter, it simply amounted, poor soul, to this—that she dare not disobey her medical man.", "이모님의 편지로 말하자면, 그것은 그저, 가엾은 이가, 이런 것에 이르렀다—당신이 감히 당신 의료인의 말을 거스를 수 없다는 것이었다."]], [["What was to be done now?", "이제 어찌해야 했던가?"], ["With my training and my principles, I never had a moment’s doubt.", "나의 훈련과 나의 원칙으로 보아, 나는 한순간의 망설임도 없었다."]], [["Once self-supported by conscience, once embarked on a career of manifest usefulness, the true Christian never yields.", "한번 양심에 의해 스스로 지탱되고, 한번 명백한 쓸모의 길에 나서면, 참된 기독교인은 결코 굴하지 않는다."], ["Neither public nor private influences produce the slightest effect on us, when we have once got our mission.", "우리가 한번 우리의 사명을 얻으면, 공적 영향도 사적 영향도 우리에게 조금의 효험도 내지 못한다."], ["Taxation may be the consequence of a mission; riots may be the consequence of a mission; wars may be the consequence of a mission: we go on with our work, irrespective of every human consideration which moves the world outside us.", "과세가 사명의 결과일 수 있고, 폭동이 사명의 결과일 수 있고, 전쟁이 사명의 결과일 수 있다. 우리는, 우리 바깥의 세상을 움직이는 온갖 인간적 헤아림과 상관없이, 우리의 일을 이어 간다."], ["We are above reason; we are beyond ridicule; we see with nobody’s eyes, we hear with nobody’s ears, we feel with nobody’s hearts, but our own.", "우리는 이치를 넘어서 있고, 우리는 조롱을 넘어서 있다. 우리는 그 누구의 눈으로도 보지 않고, 그 누구의 귀로도 듣지 않고, 그 누구의 마음으로도 느끼지 않는다, 우리 자신의 것 말고는."], ["Glorious, glorious privilege!", "영광스럽고, 영광스러운 특권이여!"], ["And how is it earned?", "그리고 그것은 어떻게 얻어지는가?"], ["Ah, my friends, you may spare yourselves the useless inquiry!", "아, 나의 벗들이여, 그 부질없는 물음은 삼가도 좋다!"], ["We are the only people who can earn it—for we are the only people who are always right.", "그것을 얻을 수 있는 것은 오직 우리뿐이다—우리야말로 언제나 옳은 유일한 사람들이니까."]], [["In the case of my misguided aunt, the form which pious perseverance was next to take revealed itself to me plainly enough.", "나의 그릇 인도된 이모님의 경우에, 경건한 끈기가 다음으로 취할 모양이 넉넉히 뻔하게 내게 드러났다."]], [["Preparation by clerical friends had failed, owing to Lady Verinder’s own reluctance.", "성직자 벗들에 의한 준비는, 레이디 버린더 자신의 꺼림 탓에, 실패했다."], ["Preparation by books had failed, owing to the doctor’s infidel obstinacy.", "책들에 의한 준비는, 의사의 불신앙적인 고집 탓에, 실패했다. 그렇다면 좋다!"], ["So be it! What was the next thing to try?", "다음으로 시험해 볼 것은 무엇이었나?"], ["The next thing to try was—Preparation by Little Notes.", "다음으로 시험해 볼 것은—작은 쪽지들에 의한 준비였다."], ["In other words, the books themselves having been sent back, select extracts from the books, copied by different hands, and all addressed as letters to my aunt, were, some to be sent by post, and some to be distributed about the house on the plan I had adopted on the previous day.", "다시 말해, 책 자체는 되돌려졌으니, 그 책들에서 골라낸 발췌문을, 서로 다른 손으로 베껴, 죄다 이모님께 보내는 편지로 부친 뒤, 그중 얼마쯤은 우편으로 보내고, 얼마쯤은 내가 그 전날 취한 방침대로 온 집 안에 뿌리는 것이었다."], ["As letters they would excite no suspicion; as letters they would be opened—and, once opened, might be read.", "편지로서 그것들은 아무 의심도 일으키지 않을 터였다. 편지로서 그것들은 뜯길 터였다—그리고, 한번 뜯기면, 읽힐지도 몰랐다."], ["Some of them I wrote myself.", "그중 얼마쯤은 내가 직접 썼다."], ["“Dear aunt, may I ask your attention to a few lines?”", "\"사랑하는 이모님, 몇 줄에 이모님의 관심을 청해도 될까요?\""], ["&c.", "어쩌고."], ["“Dear aunt, I was reading last night, and I chanced on the following passage,” &c.", "\"사랑하는 이모님, 저는 어젯밤 책을 읽다가, 마침 다음 대목을 만났어요,\""], ["Other letters were written for me by my valued fellow-workers, the sisterhood at the Mothers’-Small-Clothes.", "어쩌고. 다른 편지들은 나의 소중한 동료들, '어머니회 아동복'의 자매들이 나를 대신해 써 주었다."], ["“Dear madam, pardon the interest taken in you by a true, though humble, friend.”", "\"친애하는 부인, 참되지만 미천한 벗이 부인께 두는 관심을 용서하소서.\""], ["“Dear madam, may a serious person surprise you by saying a few cheering words?”", "\"친애하는 부인, 진지한 사람이 몇 마디 기운 나는 말씀을 드려 부인을 놀래도 될까요?\""], ["Using these and other similar forms of courteous appeal, we reintroduced all my precious passages under a form which not even the doctor’s watchful materialism could suspect.", "이러한, 그리고 그 밖의 비슷한 정중한 호소의 양식을 써서, 우리는 나의 그 온갖 소중한 대목들을, 의사의 그 방심 없는 유물론조차 의심할 수 없는 모양으로 다시 들여왔다."], ["Before the shades of evening had closed around us, I had a dozen awakening letters for my aunt, instead of a dozen awakening books.", "저녁 어스름이 우리 둘레에 닫히기 전에, 나는 이모님을 위해 열두 권의 일깨우는 책 대신, 열두 통의 일깨우는 편지를 마련했다."], ["Six I made immediate arrangements for sending through the post, and six I kept in my pocket for personal distribution in the house the next day.", "여섯 통은 즉시 우편으로 보내도록 마련하고, 여섯 통은 이튿날 집에서 직접 뿌리려고 내 주머니에 간직했다."]], [["Soon after two o’clock I was again on the field of pious conflict, addressing more kind inquiries to Samuel at Lady Verinder’s door.", "두 시 조금 지나 나는 다시, 레이디 버린더의 문에서 새뮤얼에게 더 다정한 질문을 건네며, 경건한 싸움의 마당에 나섰다."]], [["My aunt had had a bad night.", "이모님은 밤을 나쁘게 지내셨다."], ["She was again in the room in which I had witnessed her Will, resting on the sofa, and trying to get a little sleep.", "이모님은 다시, 내가 당신 유언장을 증언했던 그 방에서, 소파에 기대어, 잠을 좀 청해 보려 하고 계셨다."]], [["I said I would wait in the library, on the chance of seeing her.", "나는 이모님을 뵐 요행을 노리고, 서재에서 기다리겠다고 말했다."], ["In the fervour of my zeal to distribute the letters, it never occurred to me to inquire about Rachel.", "편지를 뿌리려는 열의의 뜨거움 속에서, 나는 레이철에 대해 물어볼 생각을 도무지 하지 못했다."], ["The house was quiet, and it was past the hour at which the musical performance began.", "집은 조용했고, 음악 공연이 시작되는 시각이 지나 있었다."], ["I took it for granted that she and her party of pleasure-seekers (Mr.", "나는 그 아이와 그 아이의 쾌락 좇는 무리가 (아아!"], ["Godfrey, alas! included) were all at the concert, and eagerly devoted myself to my good work, while time and opportunity were still at my own disposal.", "고드프리 씨도 포함하여) 죄다 음악회에 있으려니 여기고, 시간과 기회가 아직 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있는 동안, 나의 좋은 일에 열심히 매달렸다."]], [["My aunt’s correspondence of the morning—including the six awakening letters which I had posted overnight—was lying unopened on the library table.", "그날 아침의 이모님 앞으로 온 편지들이—내가 간밤에 부친 그 여섯 통의 일깨우는 편지를 포함하여—서재 탁자 위에 뜯기지 않은 채 놓여 있었다."], ["She had evidently not felt herself equal to dealing with a large mass of letters—and she might be daunted by the number of them, if she entered the library later in the day.", "이모님은 뻔히 많은 편지 더미를 감당할 만한 기운이 없다고 느끼신 듯했다—그리고 그날 늦게 서재에 들어오시면 그 수에 기가 꺾이실지도 몰랐다."], ["I put one of my second set of six letters on the chimney-piece by itself; leaving it to attract her curiosity, by means of its solitary position, apart from the rest.", "나는 나의 두 번째 여섯 통 가운데 하나를 벽난로 선반 위에 따로 놓았다. 나머지와 떨어진 그 외로운 자리로써, 이모님의 호기심을 끌도록 그것을 남겨 두면서 말이다."], ["A second letter I put purposely on the floor in the breakfast-room.", "두 번째 편지는 일부러 아침 식사방 바닥에 놓았다."], ["The first servant who went in after me would conclude that my aunt had dropped it, and would be specially careful to restore it to her.", "나 다음으로 들어가는 첫 하인이 이모님이 그것을 떨어뜨렸다고 여기고, 그것을 이모님께 도로 갖다드리려고 각별히 조심할 터였다."], ["The field thus sown on the basement story, I ran lightly upstairs to scatter my mercies next over the drawing-room floor.", "이렇게 지하층에 씨를 뿌리고, 나는 다음으로 응접실 층에 나의 자비를 흩뿌리려고 가볍게 위층으로 달려 올라갔다."]], [["Just as I entered the front room, I heard a double knock at the street-door—a soft, fluttering, considerate little knock.", "막 앞쪽 방에 들어서는데, 현관문을 두 번 두드리는 소리가 들렸다—부드럽고, 파닥거리는 듯한, 조심스러운 조그만 두드림이었다."], ["Before I could think of slipping back to the library (in which I was supposed to be waiting), the active young footman was in the hall, answering the door.", "내가 (내가 기다리고 있으리라 여겨지는) 서재로 슬며시 돌아갈 생각을 하기도 전에, 그 재빠른 젊은 하인이 현관에서 문을 열고 있었다."], ["It mattered little, as I thought.", "그건 대수롭지 않다고, 나는 생각했다."], ["In my aunt’s state of health, visitors in general were not admitted.", "이모님의 건강 상태로 보아, 방문객은 대체로 들이지 않았으니까."], ["To my horror and amazement, the performer of the soft little knock proved to be an exception to general rules.", "나의 두려움과 놀라움에도, 그 부드러운 조그만 두드림을 한 자가 여느 규칙의 예외로 드러났다."], ["Samuel’s voice below me (after apparently answering some questions which I did not hear) said, unmistakably, “Upstairs, if you please, sir.”", "내 아래쪽에서 새뮤얼의 목소리가 (내가 듣지 못한 어떤 질문에 답한 뒤인 듯) 틀림없이 말했다, \"위층으로 올라가시지요, 나리.\""], ["The next moment I heard footsteps—a man’s footsteps—approaching the drawing-room floor.", "다음 순간 나는 발소리를—사내의 발소리를—응접실 층으로 다가오는 것을 들었다."], ["Who could this favoured male visitor possibly be?", "이 총애받는 남자 방문객이 대체 누구일 수 있었나?"], ["Almost as soon as I asked myself the question, the answer occurred to me.", "내가 스스로에게 그 물음을 던지자마자, 거의 그와 동시에, 대답이 떠올랐다."], ["Who could it be but the doctor?", "그 의사 말고 누구일 수 있었으랴?"]], [["In the case of any other visitor, I should have allowed myself to be discovered in the drawing-room.", "다른 어느 방문객의 경우였다면, 나는 나 자신이 응접실에서 발견되도록 놓아두었으리라."], ["There would have been nothing out of the common in my having got tired of the library, and having gone upstairs for a change.", "내가 서재에 싫증이 나, 기분 전환으로 위층에 올라왔다는 데는 예사롭지 않을 것이 아무것도 없었을 테니까."], ["But my own self-respect stood in the way of my meeting the person who had insulted me by sending me back my books.", "허나 나 자신의 자존심이, 내게 내 책들을 되돌려 보냄으로써 나를 모욕한 그 사람을 만나는 것을 가로막았다."], ["I slipped into the little third room, which I have mentioned as communicating with the back drawing-room, and dropped the curtains which closed the open doorway.", "나는, 뒤편 응접실과 통한다고 언급한 그 조그만 셋째 방으로 슬며시 들어가, 그 열린 문간을 가리는 커튼을 늘어뜨렸다."], ["If I only waited there for a minute or two, the usual result in such cases would take place.", "내가 거기서 일이 분만 기다린다면, 그런 경우의 여느 결과가 벌어질 터였다."], ["That is to say, the doctor would be conducted to his patient’s room.", "다시 말해, 그 의사는 제 환자의 방으로 안내될 터였다."]], [["I waited a minute or two, and more than a minute or two.", "나는 일이 분, 그리고 일이 분 이상을 기다렸다."], ["I heard the visitor walking restlessly backwards and forwards.", "나는 그 방문객이 안절부절 앞뒤로 걷는 소리를 들었다."], ["I also heard him talking to himself.", "나는 또한 그가 혼잣말하는 소리도 들었다."], ["I even thought I recognised the voice.", "나는 심지어 그 목소리를 알아보는 것도 같았다."], ["Had I made a mistake?", "내가 착각한 걸까?"], ["Was it not the doctor, but somebody else?", "그것은 의사가 아니라, 다른 누군가였나?"], ["Mr. Bruff, for instance?", "이를테면, 브러프 씨?"], ["No!", "아니!"], ["an unerring instinct told me it was not Mr. Bruff.", "어긋남 없는 본능이 내게 그것이 브러프 씨가 아니라고 일러 주었다."], ["Whoever he was, he was still talking to himself.", "그가 누구든, 그는 여전히 혼잣말하고 있었다."], ["I parted the heavy curtains the least little morsel in the world, and listened.", "나는 그 무거운 커튼을 세상에서 가장 조그만 부스러기만큼 벌리고, 귀 기울였다."]], [["The words I heard were, “I’ll do it today!”", "내가 들은 말은, \"오늘 그것을 하겠어!\"였다."], ["And the voice that spoke them was Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s.", "그리고 그 말을 한 목소리는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 것이었다."]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["My hand dropped from the curtain.", "내 손이 커튼에서 떨어졌다."], ["But don’t suppose—oh, don’t suppose—that the dreadful embarrassment of my situation was the uppermost idea in my mind!", "허나 여기지 마시라—오, 여기지 마시라—내 처지의 그 끔찍한 곤혹스러움이 내 마음속의 가장 앞선 생각이었다고는!"], ["So fervent still was the sisterly interest I felt in Mr. Godfrey, that I never stopped to ask myself why he was not at the concert.", "고드프리 씨에게 느끼는 자매다운 관심이 여전히 어찌나 뜨겁던지, 나는 그가 어째서 음악회에 있지 않은지 스스로에게 물어볼 겨를도 없었다."], ["No!", "아니!"], ["I thought only of the words—the startling words—which had just fallen from his lips.", "나는 오직 그 말들만을—그의 입술에서 방금 떨어진 그 놀라운 말들만을 생각했다."], ["He would do it today.", "그는 오늘 그것을 하겠다고 했다."], ["He had said, in a tone of terrible resolution, he would do it today.", "그는, 끔찍한 결의의 어조로, 오늘 그것을 하겠다고 말했다."], ["What, oh what, would he do?", "무엇을, 오 무엇을, 그는 하려는 걸까?"], ["Something even more deplorably unworthy of him than what he had done already?", "그가 이미 한 것보다도 더 개탄스럽게 그답지 못한 무언가를?"], ["Would he apostatise from the faith?", "그가 신앙을 저버리려는 걸까?"], ["Would he abandon us at the Mothers’-Small-Clothes?", "그가 '어머니회 아동복'에서 우리를 저버리려는 걸까?"], ["Had we seen the last of his angelic smile in the committee-room?", "우리는 위원회 방에서 그의 천사 같은 미소를 마지막으로 본 걸까?"], ["Had we heard the last of his unrivalled eloquence at Exeter Hall?", "우리는 엑서터 홀에서 그의 견줄 데 없는 웅변을 마지막으로 들은 걸까?"], ["I was so wrought up by the bare idea of such awful eventualities as these in connection with such a man, that I believe I should have rushed from my place of concealment, and implored him in the name of all the Ladies’ Committees in London to explain himself—when I suddenly heard another voice in the room.", "나는 그런 사람과 관련하여 이런 끔찍한 사태를 떠올리는 것만으로도 어찌나 격해졌던지, 나는 내 숨은 자리에서 뛰쳐나가, 런던의 모든 부인 위원회의 이름으로 그에게 해명하기를 애원했으리라—그때 나는 별안간 그 방에서 또 다른 목소리를 들었다."], ["It penetrated through the curtains; it was loud, it was bold, it was wanting in every female charm.", "그것은 커튼을 뚫고 들어왔다. 그것은 크고, 대담하고, 온갖 여자다운 매력이 없었다."], ["The voice of Rachel Verinder.", "레이철 버린더의 목소리였다."]], [["“Why have you come up here, Godfrey?” she asked.", "\"어째서 여기 올라온 거예요, 고드프리?\""], ["“Why didn’t you go into the library?”", "하고 그 아이가 물었다. \"어째서 서재로 들어가지 않고요?\""]], [["He laughed softly, and answered, “Miss Clack is in the library.”", "그는 부드럽게 웃고, 대답했다. \"클랙 양이 서재에 있어요.\""]], [["“Clack in the library!”", "\"클랙이 서재에요!\""], ["She instantly seated herself on the ottoman in the back drawing-room.", "그 아이는 대뜸 뒤편 응접실의 등받이 없는 긴 의자에 앉았다."], ["“You are quite right, Godfrey.", "\"당신 말이 아주 옳아요, 고드프리."], ["We had much better stop here.”", "우리는 여기 머무는 편이 훨씬 낫겠어요.\""]], [["I had been in a burning fever, a moment since, and in some doubt what to do next.", "나는, 조금 전만 해도, 타는 듯한 열병에 시달리며, 다음에 무엇을 할지 얼마쯤 갈피를 못 잡고 있었다."], ["I became extremely cold now, and felt no doubt whatever.", "나는 이제 몹시 싸늘해졌고, 조금의 갈피도 못 잡을 것이 없었다."], ["To show myself, after what I had heard, was impossible.", "내가 들은 것을 들은 뒤에, 내 모습을 드러내는 것은 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["To retreat—except into the fireplace—was equally out of the question.", "물러나는 것은—벽난로 속으로가 아니고서는—똑같이 생각할 수 없었다."], ["A martyrdom was before me.", "순교가 내 앞에 놓여 있었다."], ["In justice to myself, I noiselessly arranged the curtains so that I could both see and hear.", "나 자신을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 나는 볼 수도 들을 수도 있도록 소리 없이 커튼을 매만졌다."], ["And then I met my martyrdom, with the spirit of a primitive Christian.", "그리고 나서 나는, 초기 기독교인의 정신으로, 나의 순교를 맞았다."]], [["“Don’t sit on the ottoman,”", "\"등받이 없는 의자에 앉지 마세요.\""], ["the young lady proceeded.", "하고 그 젊은 아가씨가 이어 갔다."], ["“Bring a chair, Godfrey.", "\"의자를 가져와요, 고드프리."], ["I like people to be opposite to me when I talk to them.”", "저는 사람들과 이야기할 때 그들이 제 맞은편에 있는 게 좋아요.\""]], [["He took the nearest seat.", "그는 가장 가까운 자리를 잡았다."], ["It was a low chair.", "그것은 낮은 의자였다."], ["He was very tall, and many sizes too large for it.", "그는 아주 키가 컸고, 그것에 비해 몇 치수나 너무 컸다."], ["I never saw his legs to such disadvantage before.", "나는 여태껏 그의 다리가 그토록 볼썽사나운 것을 본 적이 없었다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"자?\""], ["she went on.", "하고 그 아이가 이어 갔다."], ["“What did you say to them?”", "\"그들에게 뭐라고 말했어요?\""]], [["“Just what you said, dear Rachel, to me.”", "\"당신이 나에게 말한 그대로예요, 사랑하는 레이철.\""]], [["“That mamma was not at all well today?", "\"엄마가 오늘 도무지 좋지 않다고요?"], ["And that I didn’t quite like leaving her to go to the concert?”", "그리고 제가 그런 엄마를 두고 음악회에 가는 게 썩 내키지 않는다고요?\""]], [["“Those were the words.", "\"그 말 그대로였어요."], ["They were grieved to lose you at the concert, but they quite understood.", "그들은 음악회에서 당신을 잃는 것을 서글퍼했지만, 아주 이해했어요."], ["All sent their love; and all expressed a cheering belief that Lady Verinder’s indisposition would soon pass away.”", "다들 안부를 전했고요. 그리고 다들 레이디 버린더의 병환이 곧 나으리라는 기운 나는 믿음을 내비쳤어요.\""]], [["“You don’t think it’s serious, do you, Godfrey?”", "\"당신은 그게 심각하다고 여기진 않죠, 그렇죠, 고드프리?\""]], [["“Far from it!", "\"천만에요!"], ["In a few days, I feel quite sure, all will be well again.”", "며칠이면, 저는 아주 확신하는데, 죄다 다시 괜찮아질 거예요.\""]], [["“I think so, too.", "\"저도 그렇게 여겨요."], ["I was a little frightened at first, but I think so too.", "처음엔 조금 겁이 났지만, 저도 그렇게 여겨요."], ["It was very kind to go and make my excuses for me to people who are almost strangers to you.", "당신에게 거의 낯선 사람들에게 저를 대신해 변명해 주러 간 건 아주 친절한 일이었어요."], ["But why not have gone with them to the concert?", "그런데 어째서 그들과 함께 음악회에 가지 않았어요?"], ["It seems very hard that you should miss the music too.”", "당신까지 음악을 놓치다니 아주 안됐어요.\""]], [["“Don’t say that, Rachel!", "\"그런 말 말아요, 레이철!"], ["If you only knew how much happier I am—here, with you!”", "제가 얼마나 더 행복한지 당신이 알기만 한다면요—여기, 당신과 함께!\""]], [["He clasped his hands, and looked at her.", "그는 두 손을 맞잡고, 그 아이를 바라보았다."], ["In the position which he occupied, when he did that, he turned my way.", "그가 그리할 때 그가 있던 자세로, 그는 내 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸다."], ["Can words describe how I sickened when I noticed exactly the same pathetic expression on his face, which had charmed me when he was pleading for destitute millions of his fellow-creatures on the platform at Exeter Hall!", "엑서터 홀 연단에서 그가 헐벗은 수백만 동료 인간들을 위해 호소할 때 나를 매혹했던 바로 그 애처로운 표정을 그의 얼굴에서 알아챘을 때 내가 얼마나 역겨웠는지 어찌 말로 묘사할 수 있으랴!"]], [["“It’s hard to get over one’s bad habits, Godfrey.", "\"나쁜 버릇을 고치기란 어렵지요, 고드프리."], ["But do try to get over the habit of paying compliments—do, to please me.”", "하지만 부디 그 아첨하는 버릇은 고쳐 봐요—부디, 저를 기쁘게 해 주려면요.\""]], [["“I never paid you a compliment, Rachel, in my life.", "\"저는 여태껏 당신에게, 레이철, 제 평생 아첨을 한 적이 없어요."], ["Successful love may sometimes use the language of flattery, I admit.", "이루어진 사랑은 때로 아첨의 말을 쓸 수 있음을, 저도 인정해요."], ["But hopeless love, dearest, always speaks the truth.”", "하지만 가망 없는 사랑은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 언제나 진실을 말하지요.\""]], [["He drew his chair close, and took her hand, when he said “hopeless love.”", "그는 \"가망 없는 사랑\"이라고 말할 때, 제 의자를 바싹 당기고, 그 아이의 손을 잡았다."], ["There was a momentary silence.", "한순간의 침묵이 있었다."], ["He, who thrilled everybody, had doubtless thrilled her.", "모두를 전율케 하는 그가, 의심할 것 없이 그 아이도 전율케 했던 것이다."], ["I thought I now understood the words which had dropped from him when he was alone in the drawing-room, “I’ll do it today.”", "나는 이제야 그가 응접실에서 혼자 있을 때 그에게서 떨어진 그 말, \"오늘 그것을 하겠어\"를 이해한 듯싶었다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["the most rigid propriety could hardly have failed to discover that he was doing it now.", "더없이 엄격한 예절인들, 그가 지금 그것을 하고 있음을 좀처럼 알아채지 못할 리 없었으리라."]], [["“Have you forgotten what we agreed on, Godfrey, when you spoke to me in the country?", "\"우리가 합의한 것을 잊었어요, 고드프리, 당신이 시골에서 저에게 말했을 때요?"], ["We agreed that we were to be cousins, and nothing more.”", "우리는 사촌일 뿐, 그 이상은 아니기로 합의했잖아요.\""]], [["“I break the agreement, Rachel, every time I see you.”", "\"저는 그 합의를 깨요, 레이철, 당신을 볼 때마다요.\""]], [["“Then don’t see me.”", "\"그럼 저를 보지 마세요.\""]], [["“Quite useless!", "\"아주 부질없어요!"], ["I break the agreement every time I think of you.", "저는 그 합의를 깨요, 당신을 생각할 때마다요."], ["Oh, Rachel!", "오, 레이철!"], ["how kindly you told me, only the other day, that my place in your estimation was a higher place than it had ever been yet!", "바로 며칠 전에, 당신은 저에 대한 당신의 평가에서 제 자리가 여태껏 있었던 것보다 더 높은 자리라고 얼마나 다정하게 말해 주었던가요!"], ["Am I mad to build the hopes I do on those dear words?", "제가 그 사랑스러운 말에 이런 희망을 걸다니 미친 걸까요?"], ["Am I mad to dream of some future day when your heart may soften to me?", "언젠가 당신의 마음이 저에게 누그러질 어느 앞날을 꿈꾸다니 미친 걸까요?"], ["Don’t tell me so, if I am!", "만일 그렇다면, 그렇다고 저에게 말하지 말아요!"], ["Leave me my delusion, dearest!", "저에게 저의 망상을 남겨 주세요, 더없이 사랑하는 이여!"], ["I must have that to cherish, and to comfort me, if I have nothing else!”", "저에게 다른 아무것도 없다면, 저는 그것만은 소중히 간직하고, 저를 위로할 그것만은 가져야 해요!\""]], [["His voice trembled, and he put his white handkerchief to his eyes.", "그의 목소리가 떨렸고, 그는 제 흰 손수건을 눈에 갖다 댔다."], ["Exeter Hall again!", "또다시 엑서터 홀이었다!"], ["Nothing wanting to complete the parallel but the audience, the cheers, and the glass of water.", "그 대비를 완성하는 데에 청중과, 갈채와, 물 한 잔밖에 빠진 것이 없었다."]], [["Even her obdurate nature was touched.", "그 아이의 완고한 성정조차 감동했다."], ["I saw her lean a little nearer to him.", "나는 그 아이가 그에게 조금 더 가까이 기대는 것을 보았다."], ["I heard a new tone of interest in her next words.", "나는 그 아이의 다음 말에서 새로운 관심의 어조를 들었다."]], [["“Are you really sure, Godfrey, that you are so fond of me as that?”", "\"정말 확실해요, 고드프리, 당신이 그토록 저를 아낀다는 게?\""]], [["“Sure! You know what I was, Rachel.", "\"확실하고말고요!"], ["Let me tell you what I am.", "당신은 제가 어떤 사람이었는지 알지요, 레이철."], ["I have lost every interest in life, but my interest in you.", "제가 어떤 사람인지 당신에게 말하게 해 주세요."], ["A transformation has come over me which I can’t account for, myself.", "저는 삶의 온갖 흥미를 잃었어요, 당신에 대한 제 관심만 빼고요. 저 자신도 설명할 수 없는 변모가 저에게 일어났어요."], ["Would you believe it?", "믿어지겠어요?"], ["My charitable business is an unendurable nuisance to me; and when I see a Ladies’ Committee now, I wish myself at the uttermost ends of the earth!”", "제 자선 일이 저에게 견딜 수 없는 성가심이 되었어요. 그리고 이제 부인 위원회를 보면, 저는 제가 땅끝에 가 있기를 바라요!\""]], [["If the annals of apostasy offer anything comparable to such a declaration as that, I can only say that the case in point is not producible from the stores of my reading.", "만일 배교(背敎)의 기록에 그런 선언에 견줄 만한 것이 조금이라도 있다면, 나는 다만 그 사례가 나의 독서의 창고에서 내놓을 수 없는 것이라고밖에 말할 수 없다."], ["I thought of the Mothers’-Small-Clothes.", "나는 '어머니회 아동복'을 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of the Sunday-Sweetheart-Supervision.", "나는 '주일 애인 감독'을 떠올렸다."], ["I thought of the other Societies, too numerous to mention, all built up on this man as on a tower of strength. I thought of the struggling Female Boards, who, so to speak, drew the breath of their business-life through the nostrils of Mr.", "나는, 일일이 언급하기에 너무 많은, 죄다 이 사내를 힘의 탑으로 삼아 세워진 그 밖의 협회들을 떠올렸다."], ["Godfrey—of that same Mr. Godfrey who had just reviled our good work as a “nuisance”—and just declared that he wished he was at the uttermost ends of the earth when he found himself in our company!", "나는, 이를테면 고드프리 씨의 콧구멍을 통해 제 사업적 생명의 숨을 들이쉬던 그 고전하는 부인 이사회들을—우리의 좋은 일을 방금 \"성가심\"이라 헐뜯고—우리와 함께 있는 저 자신을 발견하면 땅끝에 가 있기를 바란다고 방금 선언한 바로 그 고드프리 씨를 떠올렸다!"], ["My young female friends will feel encouraged to persevere, when I mention that it tried even my discipline before I could devour my own righteous indignation in silence.", "나의 젊은 여자 벗들은, 내가 나 자신의 정의로운 분노를 말없이 삼켜 낼 수 있기까지 그것이 나의 수양조차 시험했다고 말하면, 견뎌 나갈 용기를 얻으리라."], ["At the same time, it is only justice to myself to add, that I didn’t lose a syllable of the conversation.", "동시에, 나 자신을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 내가 그 대화의 한 음절도 놓치지 않았음을 덧붙여 둘 뿐이다."], ["Rachel was the next to speak.", "다음으로 말한 것은 레이철이었다."]], [["“You have made your confession,”", "\"당신은 고백을 했어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["she said. “I wonder whether it would cure you of your unhappy attachment to me, if I made mine?”", "\"제가 제 고백을 하면, 그게 저에 대한 당신의 그 불행한 애착을 낫게 할지 궁금하네요.\""]], [["He started.", "그는 흠칫했다."], ["I confess I started too.", "나도 흠칫했음을 고백한다."], ["He thought, and I thought, that she was about to divulge the mystery of the Moonstone.", "그도, 나도, 그 아이가 월장석의 수수께끼를 털어놓으려 한다고 생각했다."]], [["“Would you think, to look at me,”", "\"저를 보고,\" 하고 그 아이가 이어 갔다."], ["she went on, “that I am the wretchedest girl living?", "\"제가 살아 있는 가장 비참한 아이라고 여기겠어요?"], ["It’s true, Godfrey.", "정말이에요, 고드프리."], ["What greater wretchedness can there be than to live degraded in your own estimation?", "제 자신의 평가에서 격이 떨어져 사는 것보다 더 큰 비참함이 어디 있겠어요?"], ["That is my life now.”", "그게 지금 제 삶이에요.\""]], [["“My dear Rachel!", "\"사랑하는 레이철!"], ["it’s impossible you can have any reason to speak of yourself in that way!”", "당신이 자신에 대해 그런 식으로 말할 무슨 이유가 있을 리 없어요!\""]], [["“How do you know I have no reason?”", "\"제게 이유가 없다는 걸 당신이 어떻게 알아요?\""]], [["“Can you ask me the question!", "\"어떻게 저에게 그런 물음을 던질 수 있어요!"], ["I know it, because I know you.", "저는 알아요, 저는 당신을 아니까요."], ["Your silence, dearest, has never lowered you in the estimation of your true friends.", "당신의 침묵은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 당신의 참된 벗들의 평가에서 결코 당신을 낮춘 적이 없어요."], ["The disappearance of your precious birthday gift may seem strange; your unexplained connection with that event may seem stranger still——”", "당신의 소중한 생일 선물이 사라진 것은 이상해 보일지 모르지요. 그 사건과 당신의 설명되지 않은 관련은 더 이상해 보일지 모르고요——\""]], [["“Are you speaking of the Moonstone, Godfrey?”", "\"당신 지금 월장석 이야기를 하는 거예요, 고드프리?\""]], [["“I certainly thought that you referred——”", "\"저는 분명 당신이 이르는 것이——\""]], [["“I referred to nothing of the sort.", "\"저는 그런 걸 이른 게 아니에요."], ["I can hear of the loss of the Moonstone, let who will speak of it, without feeling degraded in my own estimation.", "저는 누가 그것을 이야기하든, 월장석의 분실에 대해 들어도, 제 자신의 평가에서 격이 떨어지는 느낌 없이 들을 수 있어요."], ["If the story of the Diamond ever comes to light, it will be known that I accepted a dreadful responsibility; it will be known that I involved myself in the keeping of a miserable secret—but it will be as clear as the sun at noon-day that I did nothing mean!", "만일 그 다이아몬드의 사연이 언젠가 드러난다면, 제가 끔찍한 책임을 떠맡았음이 알려질 거예요. 제가 비참한 비밀을 지키는 일에 저를 얽어 넣었음이 알려질 거예요—하지만 제가 비열한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았음이 한낮의 태양처럼 환히 드러날 거예요!"], ["You have misunderstood me, Godfrey.", "당신은 저를 오해했어요, 고드프리."], ["It’s my fault for not speaking more plainly.", "더 분명하게 말하지 않은 제 잘못이에요."], ["Cost me what it may, I will be plainer now.", "무슨 대가를 치르든, 저는 이제 더 분명하게 말하겠어요."], ["Suppose you were not in love with me?", "당신이 저를 사랑하지 않는다고 해 봐요."], ["Suppose you were in love with some other woman?”", "당신이 어떤 다른 여자를 사랑한다고 해 봐요.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"네?\""]], [["“Suppose you discovered that woman to be utterly unworthy of you?", "\"당신이 그 여자가 도무지 당신에게 어울리지 않는다는 걸 알아냈다고 해 봐요."], ["Suppose you were quite convinced that it was a disgrace to you to waste another thought on her?", "그 여자에게 또 한 번 생각을 허비하는 것이 당신에게 수치라고 아주 확신했다고 해 봐요."], ["Suppose the bare idea of ever marrying such a person made your face burn, only with thinking of it?”", "그런 사람과 언젠가 혼인한다는 생각만으로도, 그저 그것을 생각하는 것만으로도 당신의 얼굴이 화끈거렸다고 해 봐요.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"네?\""]], [["“And, suppose, in spite of all that—you couldn’t tear her from your heart?", "\"그리고, 그 모든 것에도 불구하고—당신이 그 여자를 당신의 마음에서 떼어 낼 수 없었다고 해 봐요."], ["Suppose the feeling she had roused in you (in the time when you believed in her) was not a feeling to be hidden?", "그 여자가 (당신이 그 여자를 믿던 시절에) 당신 안에 불러일으킨 감정이 감출 수 있는 감정이 아니었다고 해 봐요."], ["Suppose the love this wretch had inspired in you——?", "이 몹쓸 여자가 당신 안에 불러일으킨 사랑이——?"], ["Oh, how can I find words to say it in!", "오, 그것을 말할 무슨 말을 제가 어찌 찾겠어요!"], ["How can I make a man understand that a feeling which horrifies me at myself, can be a feeling that fascinates me at the same time?", "저 자신에게 소름 끼치는 감정이 동시에 저를 매혹하는 감정일 수 있다는 것을 어찌 사내에게 이해시킬 수 있겠어요?"], ["It’s the breath of my life, Godfrey, and it’s the poison that kills me—both in one!", "그건 제 삶의 숨결이에요, 고드프리, 그리고 그건 저를 죽이는 독이에요—둘이 하나로!"], ["Go away!", "가세요!"], ["I must be out of my mind to talk as I am talking now. No!", "지금 제가 말하듯 말하다니 저는 제정신이 아닌 게 틀림없어요."], ["you mustn’t leave me—you mustn’t carry away a wrong impression.", "아니에요! 당신은 저를 떠나면 안 돼요—당신은 잘못된 인상을 안고 가면 안 돼요."], ["I must say what is to be said in my own defence.", "저는 제 자신을 변호하기 위해 할 말은 해야겠어요."], ["Mind this!", "이걸 명심해요!"], ["He doesn’t know—he never will know, what I have told you.", "그이는 몰라요—그이는 결코 알지 못할 거예요, 제가 당신에게 말한 것을요."], ["I will never see him—I don’t care what happens—I will never, never, never see him again! Don’t ask me his name!", "저는 결코 그이를 보지 않겠어요—무슨 일이 일어나든 상관없어요—저는 결코, 결코, 결코 그이를 다시 보지 않겠어요!"], ["Don’t ask me any more!", "저에게 그이의 이름을 묻지 마세요!"], ["Let’s change the subject.", "저에게 더는 묻지 마세요!"], ["Are you doctor enough, Godfrey, to tell me why I feel as if I was stifling for want of breath?", "화제를 바꿔요. 당신은, 고드프리, 제가 어째서 숨이 막혀 죽을 것 같은 느낌인지 말해 줄 만큼 의사인가요?"], ["Is there a form of hysterics that bursts into words instead of tears?", "눈물 대신 말로 터져 나오는 히스테리의 형태가 있나요?"], ["I dare say!", "아마 있겠죠!"], ["What does it matter?", "그게 무슨 대수예요?"], ["You will get over any trouble I have caused you, easily enough now.", "당신은 이제 제가 당신에게 끼친 어떤 괴로움도 넉넉히 쉽게 이겨 낼 거예요."], ["I have dropped to my right place in your estimation, haven’t I?", "저는 당신의 평가에서 제 마땅한 자리로 떨어졌죠, 안 그래요? 저를 아랑곳하지 마세요!"], ["Don’t notice me! Don’t pity me!", "저를 가엾이 여기지 마세요!"], ["For God’s sake, go away!”", "제발 부탁이에요, 가세요!\""]], [["She turned round on a sudden, and beat her hands wildly on the back of the ottoman.", "그 아이는 별안간 돌아서서, 두 손으로 그 긴 의자의 등받이를 사납게 두들겼다."], ["Her head dropped on the cushions; and she burst out crying.", "그 아이의 머리가 방석 위로 떨어졌다."], ["Before I had time to feel shocked at this, I was horror-struck by an entirely unexpected proceeding on the part of Mr.", "그리고 그 아이는 왈칵 울음을 터뜨렸다. 내가 이것에 충격받을 겨를도 없이, 나는 고드프리 씨 편의 전혀 뜻밖의 행동에 소름이 끼쳤다."], ["Godfrey. Will it be credited that he fell on his knees at her feet?—on both knees, I solemnly declare!", "그가 그 아이 발치에 무릎을 꿇었다면 믿어지겠는가?—두 무릎을 다, 나는 엄숙히 선언한다!"], ["May modesty mention that he put his arms round her next?", "그가 그다음에 그 아이에게 두 팔을 둘렀음을 정숙함이 언급해도 될까?"], ["And may reluctant admiration acknowledge that he electrified her with two words?", "그리고 그가 두 마디로 그 아이를 감전시켰음을 마지못한 감탄이 인정해도 될까?"]], [["“Noble creature!”", "\"고귀한 사람이여!\""]], [["No more than that!", "그것뿐이었다!"], ["But he did it with one of the bursts which have made his fame as a public speaker.", "허나 그는 그를 대중 연설가로서 이름나게 한 그 격정의 폭발 가운데 하나로 그것을 했다."], ["She sat, either quite thunderstruck, or quite fascinated—I don’t know which—without even making an effort to put his arms back where his arms ought to have been.", "그 아이는, 아주 넋을 잃었는지, 아주 매혹되었는지—어느 쪽인지 모르겠으나—그의 팔을 마땅히 있어야 할 자리로 도로 밀어내려는 애조차 쓰지 않고 앉아 있었다."], ["As for me, my sense of propriety was completely bewildered.", "나로 말하자면, 나의 예절 감각이 완전히 어리둥절해졌다."], ["I was so painfully uncertain whether it was my first duty to close my eyes, or to stop my ears, that I did neither.", "눈을 감는 것이 나의 첫 의무인지, 아니면 귀를 막는 것이 나의 첫 의무인지 어찌나 괴롭게도 갈피를 못 잡던지, 나는 둘 다 하지 못했다."], ["I attribute my being still able to hold the curtain in the right position for looking and listening, entirely to suppressed hysterics.", "내가 여전히 보고 듣기에 알맞은 자리에 커튼을 붙들고 있을 수 있었던 것을, 나는 온전히 억눌린 히스테리 탓으로 돌린다."], ["In suppressed hysterics, it is admitted, even by the doctors, that one must hold something.", "억눌린 히스테리에 있어서는, 의사들조차 인정하거니와, 사람이 무언가를 붙들고 있어야 한다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["he said, with all the fascination of his evangelical voice and manner, “you are a noble creature!", "하고 그가, 그의 복음적인 목소리와 태도의 온갖 매혹으로 말했다. \"당신은 고귀한 사람이에요!"], ["A woman who can speak the truth, for the truth’s own sake—a woman who will sacrifice her pride, rather than sacrifice an honest man who loves her—is the most priceless of all treasures.", "진실을, 진실 그 자체를 위해 말할 수 있는 여자—자기를 사랑하는 정직한 사내를 희생하느니, 차라리 제 자존심을 희생할 여자는—온갖 보물 가운데 가장 값을 매길 수 없는 것이에요."], ["When such a woman marries, if her husband only wins her esteem and regard, he wins enough to ennoble his whole life.", "그런 여자가 혼인할 때, 그 남편이 그 여자의 존경과 존중만 얻어도, 그는 제 온 삶을 고귀하게 할 만큼을 얻는 겁니다."], ["You have spoken, dearest, of your place in my estimation.", "당신은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 저의 평가에서 당신의 자리에 대해 말했지요."], ["Judge what that place is—when I implore you on my knees, to let the cure of your poor wounded heart be my care.", "그 자리가 어떤 자리인지 헤아려 봐요—제가 무릎 꿇고, 당신의 가엾은 상처 입은 마음을 치유하는 일을 저의 몫으로 삼게 해 달라 애원할 때 말이에요."], ["Rachel!", "레이철!"], ["will you honour me, will you bless me, by being my wife?”", "저의 아내가 됨으로써, 저를 영광되게 해 주시겠어요, 저를 축복해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["By this time I should certainly have decided on stopping my ears, if Rachel had not encouraged me to keep them open, by answering him in the first sensible words I had ever heard fall from her lips.", "이때쯤 나는 분명 귀를 막기로 마음먹었으련만, 레이철이, 내가 여태껏 그 아이의 입술에서 떨어지는 것을 들어 본 가운데 가장 분별 있는 말로 그에게 대답함으로써, 내가 귀를 열어 두도록 부추기지 않았더라면 말이다."]], [["“Godfrey!”", "\"고드프리!\""], ["she said, “you must be mad!”", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"당신 미친 게 틀림없어요!\""]], [["“I never spoke more reasonably, dearest—in your interests, as well as in mine. Look for a moment to the future.", "\"저는 여태껏 이보다 더 사리에 맞게 말한 적이 없어요, 더없이 사랑하는 이여—저의 잇속뿐만 아니라, 당신의 잇속으로도요."], ["Is your happiness to be sacrificed to a man who has never known how you feel towards him, and whom you are resolved never to see again?", "잠깐 앞날을 바라봐요. 당신의 행복이, 당신이 자기에게 어떤 감정을 품는지 결코 알지 못했고, 당신이 결코 다시 보지 않기로 결심한 사내에게 희생되어야 하나요?"], ["Is it not your duty to yourself to forget this ill-fated attachment?", "이 기구한 애착을 잊는 것이 당신 자신에 대한 당신의 의무가 아닌가요?"], ["and is forgetfulness to be found in the life you are leading now?", "그리고 당신이 지금 이끄는 삶에서 그 잊음을 찾을 수 있나요?"], ["You have tried that life, and you are wearying of it already.", "당신은 그 삶을 시험해 봤고, 벌써 그것에 싫증 내고 있어요."], ["Surround yourself with nobler interests than the wretched interests of the world.", "세상의 그 비참한 잇속보다 더 고귀한 관심들로 당신을 에워싸요."], ["A heart that loves and honours you; a home whose peaceful claims and happy duties win gently on you day by day—try the consolation, Rachel, which is to be found there!", "당신을 사랑하고 존경하는 마음, 그 평화로운 요구와 행복한 소임이 날마다 부드럽게 당신을 사로잡는 가정—레이철, 거기서 찾을 수 있는 그 위안을 시험해 봐요!"], ["I don’t ask for your love—I will be content with your affection and regard.", "저는 당신의 사랑을 청하는 게 아니에요—저는 당신의 애정과 존중으로 만족하겠어요."], ["Let the rest be left, confidently left, to your husband’s devotion, and to Time that heals even wounds as deep as yours.”", "나머지는, 당신의 상처만큼 깊은 상처조차 낫게 하는 세월과, 당신 남편의 헌신에, 믿고 맡겨 두어요.\""]], [["She began to yield already.", "그 아이는 벌써 넘어가기 시작했다."], ["Oh, what a bringing-up she must have had!", "오, 그 아이가 어떤 양육을 받았기에!"], ["Oh, how differently I should have acted in her place!", "오, 그 아이 처지였다면 나는 얼마나 다르게 처신했으랴!"]], [["“Don’t tempt me, Godfrey,”", "\"저를 유혹하지 마세요, 고드프리.\""], ["she said; “I am wretched enough and reckless enough as it is.", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"저는 이대로도 넉넉히 비참하고 넉넉히 무모해요."], ["Don’t tempt me to be more wretched and more wreckless still!”", "저를 더 비참하고 더 무모해지도록 유혹하지 마세요!\""]], [["“One question, Rachel.", "\"한 가지 물음이에요, 레이철."], ["Have you any personal objection to me?”", "당신은 저에 대해 개인적으로 못마땅한 것이 있나요?\""]], [["“I! I always liked you.", "\"저요!"], ["After what you have just said to me, I should be insensible indeed if I didn’t respect and admire you as well.”", "저는 언제나 당신을 좋아했어요. 당신이 방금 저에게 한 말 뒤로는, 제가 당신을 존경하고 감탄하기까지 하지 않는다면 저는 정말이지 무딘 사람이겠죠.\""]], [["“Do you know many wives, my dear Rachel, who respect and admire their husbands?", "\"당신은, 사랑하는 레이철, 제 남편을 존경하고 감탄하는 아내를 많이 아나요?"], ["And yet they and their husbands get on very well.", "그런데도 그들과 그들의 남편은 아주 잘 지내요."], ["How many brides go to the altar with hearts that would bear inspection by the men who take them there?", "얼마나 많은 신부가, 저를 그리로 데려가는 사내들의 검사를 견뎌 낼 만한 마음을 지니고 제단으로 가나요?"], ["And yet it doesn’t end unhappily—somehow or other the nuptial establishment jogs on.", "그런데도 그것이 불행하게 끝나지는 않아요—어떻게든 그 혼인 살림은 꾸역꾸역 굴러가지요."], ["The truth is, that women try marriage as a Refuge, far more numerously than they are willing to admit; and, what is more, they find that marriage has justified their confidence in it.", "사실인즉, 여자들은, 저희가 기꺼이 인정하려는 것보다 훨씬 더 많이, 혼인을 피난처로 삼아 시험해요. 그리고, 더욱이, 저희는 혼인이 그에 대한 저희의 믿음을 정당화해 주었음을 알게 되지요."], ["Look at your own case once again.", "당신 자신의 경우를 다시 한번 봐요."], ["At your age, and with your attractions, is it possible for you to sentence yourself to a single life?", "당신의 나이에, 당신의 매력으로, 당신이 자신에게 독신의 삶을 선고하는 것이 있을 법한 일인가요?"], ["Trust my knowledge of the world—nothing is less possible.", "세상에 대한 제 앎을 믿어요—그보다 덜 있을 법한 일은 없어요."], ["It is merely a question of time.", "그건 그저 시간 문제예요."], ["You may marry some other man, some years hence.", "당신은 몇 해 뒤에, 어떤 다른 사내와 혼인할지도 모르지요."], ["Or you may marry the man, dearest, who is now at your feet, and who prizes your respect and admiration above the love of any other woman on the face of the earth.”", "아니면 당신은, 더없이 사랑하는 이여, 지금 당신 발치에 있는, 그리고 이 땅 위의 다른 어떤 여자의 사랑보다 당신의 존경과 감탄을 더 값지게 여기는 이 사내와 혼인할지도 모르고요.\""]], [["“Gently, Godfrey!", "\"천천히요, 고드프리!"], ["you are putting something into my head which I never thought of before.", "당신은 제가 여태껏 생각해 본 적 없는 무언가를 제 머릿속에 넣고 있어요."], ["You are tempting me with a new prospect, when all my other prospects are closed before me.", "당신은, 저의 다른 온갖 앞날이 제 앞에서 닫혔을 때, 새로운 앞날로 저를 유혹하고 있어요."], ["I tell you again, I am miserable enough and desperate enough, if you say another word, to marry you on your own terms.", "다시 말하지만, 저는, 당신이 한마디만 더 하면, 당신 조건대로 당신과 혼인할 만큼 넉넉히 비참하고 넉넉히 절박해요."], ["Take the warning, and go!”", "그 경고를 받아들이고, 가세요!\""]], [["“I won’t even rise from my knees, till you have said yes!”", "\"당신이 그러겠다고 말하기 전까지는, 저는 무릎에서 일어서지도 않겠어요!\""]], [["“If I say yes you will repent, and I shall repent, when it is too late!”", "\"제가 그러겠다고 말하면 당신은 뉘우칠 거예요, 그리고 저도 뉘우칠 거예요, 너무 늦었을 때요!\""]], [["“We shall both bless the day, darling, when I pressed, and when you yielded.”", "\"우리는 둘 다 그날을 축복할 거예요, 사랑하는 이여, 제가 밀어붙이고, 당신이 넘어간 그날을요.\""]], [["“Do you feel as confidently as you speak?”", "\"당신은 말하는 만큼 확신하나요?\""]], [["“You shall judge for yourself.", "\"직접 판단해 봐요."], ["I speak from what I have seen in my own family.", "저는 제 자신의 집안에서 본 것을 말하는 거예요."], ["Tell me what you think of our household at Frizinghall.", "프리징홀의 우리 가정을 어떻게 생각하는지 말해 봐요."], ["Do my father and mother live unhappily together?”", "제 아버지와 어머니가 함께 불행하게 사시나요?\""]], [["“Far from it—so far as I can see.”", "\"천만에요—제가 보기에는요.\""]], [["“When my mother was a girl, Rachel (it is no secret in the family), she had loved as you love—she had given her heart to a man who was unworthy of her.", "\"제 어머니가 아가씨였을 때, 레이철 (집안에 비밀도 아니지만), 그분은 당신이 사랑하듯 사랑했어요—그분은 자기에게 어울리지 않는 사내에게 마음을 주었지요."], ["She married my father, respecting him, admiring him, but nothing more.", "그분은 제 아버지와, 그분을 존경하고, 감탄하며, 하지만 그 이상은 없이 혼인했어요."], ["Your own eyes have seen the result.", "당신 자신의 눈이 그 결과를 봤고요."], ["Is there no encouragement in it for you and for me?”*", "그 안에 당신과 저에게 아무 용기도 없나요?*"]], [["* See Betteredge’s Narrative, chapter viii.", "* 베터리지의 이야기, 8장 참조."]], [["“You won’t hurry me, Godfrey?”", "\"저를 서두르게 하지 않을 거죠, 고드프리?\""]], [["“My time shall be yours.”", "\"제 시간은 당신의 것이에요.\""]], [["“You won’t ask me for more than I can give?”", "\"제가 줄 수 있는 것보다 더는 청하지 않을 거죠?\""]], [["“My angel!", "\"저의 천사여!"], ["I only ask you to give me yourself.”", "저는 다만 당신 자신을 저에게 달라고 청할 뿐이에요.\""]], [["“Take me!”", "\"저를 가져요!\""]], [["In those two words, she accepted him!", "그 두 마디로, 그 아이는 그를 받아들였다!"]], [["He had another burst—a burst of unholy rapture this time.", "그는 또 한 번 격정을 터뜨렸다—이번에는 거룩하지 못한 환희의 폭발이었다."], ["He drew her nearer and nearer to him till her face touched his; and then—No!", "그는 그 아이의 얼굴이 제 얼굴에 닿을 때까지 그 아이를 점점 더 가까이 끌어당겼다. 그리고 나서는—아니!"], ["I really cannot prevail upon myself to carry this shocking disclosure any farther.", "나는 정말이지 이 충격적인 폭로를 더 이어 갈 마음을 나 자신에게서 도무지 끌어낼 수가 없다."], ["Let me only say, that I tried to close my eyes before it happened, and that I was just one moment too late.", "다만, 나는 그 일이 벌어지기 전에 눈을 감으려 했으나, 딱 한순간 너무 늦었다는 것만 말하겠다."], ["I had calculated, you see, on her resisting.", "나는, 보시다시피, 그 아이가 저항하리라 셈했던 것이다."], ["She submitted.", "그 아이는 순순히 따랐다."], ["To every right-feeling person of my own sex, volumes could say no more.", "나와 같은 성(性)의 올바르게 느끼는 모든 사람에게라면, 여러 권의 책인들 이 이상 말할 수 없으리라."]], [["Even my innocence in such matters began to see its way to the end of the interview now.", "이런 일에 대한 나의 순진함조차 이제 그 면담의 끝으로 가는 길이 보이기 시작했다."], ["They understood each other so thoroughly by this time, that I fully expected to see them walk off together, arm in arm, to be married. There appeared, however, judging by Mr.", "그들은 이때쯤 서로를 어찌나 철저히 알아버렸던지, 나는 그들이 혼인하러 팔짱을 끼고 함께 걸어 나가는 것을 보게 되리라 넉넉히 예상했다."], ["Godfrey’s next words, to be one more trifling formality which it was necessary to observe.", "허나, 고드프리 씨의 다음 말로 미루어 보건대, 지켜야 할 사소한 격식이 하나 더 있는 듯했다."], ["He seated himself—unforbidden this time—on the ottoman by her side.", "그는—이번에는 금지되지 않은 채—그 아이 곁 긴 의자에 앉았다."], ["“Shall I speak to your dear mother?”", "\"제가 당신의 사랑하는 어머니께 말할까요?\" 하고 그가 물었다."], ["he asked. “Or will you?”", "\"아니면 당신이 하겠어요?\""]], [["She declined both alternatives.", "그 아이는 두 가지 다 마다했다."]], [["“Let my mother hear nothing from either of us, until she is better.", "\"제 어머니가 나으실 때까지는, 우리 둘 중 누구에게서도 아무것도 듣지 않게 해요."], ["I wish it to be kept a secret for the present, Godfrey.", "저는 당분간 그것을 비밀로 하고 싶어요, 고드프리."], ["Go now, and come back this evening.", "이제 가요, 그리고 오늘 저녁에 돌아와요."], ["We have been here alone together quite long enough.”", "우리는 여기 단둘이서 넉넉히 오래 있었어요.\""]], [["She rose, and, in rising, looked for the first time towards the little room in which my martyrdom was going on.", "그 아이는 일어섰고, 일어서면서, 처음으로 나의 순교가 벌어지고 있는 그 조그만 방 쪽을 바라보았다."]], [["“Who has drawn those curtains?”", "\"누가 저 커튼을 쳐 놨지?\""], ["she exclaimed.", "하고 그 아이가 외쳤다."], ["“The room is close enough, as it is, without keeping the air out of it in that way.”", "\"이 방은 이대로도 넉넉히 답답한데, 그런 식으로 공기를 막다니.\""]], [["She advanced to the curtains.", "그 아이는 커튼으로 다가갔다."], ["At the moment when she laid her hand on them—at the moment when the discovery of me appeared to be quite inevitable—the voice of the fresh-coloured young footman, on the stairs, suddenly suspended any further proceedings on her side or on mine.", "그 아이가 커튼에 손을 얹은 순간—내가 발각되는 것이 아주 피할 수 없어 보이던 순간—층계에서 들려온 그 혈색 좋은 젊은 하인의 목소리가, 별안간 그 아이 편의 그리고 내 편의 어떤 다음 행동도 멈춰 세웠다."], ["It was unmistakably the voice of a man in great alarm.", "그것은 틀림없이 몹시 놀란 사내의 목소리였다."]], [["“Miss Rachel!”", "\"레이철 아가씨!\""], ["he called out, “where are you, Miss Rachel?”", "하고 그가 소리쳐 불렀다. \"어디 계세요, 레이철 아가씨?\""]], [["She sprang back from the curtains, and ran to the door.", "그 아이는 커튼에서 물러나 뛰어, 문으로 달려갔다."]], [["The footman came just inside the room.", "그 하인이 방 안쪽으로 막 들어섰다."], ["His ruddy colour was all gone.", "그의 불그레한 혈색이 죄다 사라져 있었다."], ["He said, “Please to come downstairs, Miss!", "그가 말했다. \"아래층으로 내려오세요, 아가씨!"], ["My lady has fainted, and we can’t bring her to again.”", "마님이 기절하셨는데, 저희가 도무지 깨워 드릴 수가 없습니다.\""]], [["In a moment more I was alone, and free to go downstairs in my turn, quite unobserved.", "한 순간 더 지나 나는 혼자가 되었고, 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않고, 내 차례로 아래층에 내려가도 되었다."]], [["Mr. Godfrey passed me in the hall, hurrying out, to fetch the doctor.", "고드프리 씨가 의사를 부르러 서둘러 나가며, 현관에서 나를 지나쳤다."], ["“Go in, and help them!”", "\"들어가서, 그들을 도와요!\""], ["he said, pointing to the room.", "하고 그가, 그 방을 가리키며 말했다."], ["I found Rachel on her knees by the sofa, with her mother’s head on her bosom.", "나는 레이철이 소파 곁에 무릎 꿇고, 제 어머니의 머리를 제 가슴에 안고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["One look at my aunt’s face (knowing what I knew) was enough to warn me of the dreadful truth.", "이모님의 얼굴을 한 번 보는 것으로 (내가 아는 것을 아는 터라) 그 끔찍한 진실을 알아채기에 넉넉했다."], ["I kept my thoughts to myself till the doctor came in.", "나는 의사가 들어올 때까지 내 생각을 내 안에 간직했다."], ["It was not long before he arrived.", "그가 도착하기까지는 오래지 않았다."], ["He began by sending Rachel out of the room—and then he told the rest of us that Lady Verinder was no more.", "그는 레이철을 방에서 내보내는 것으로 시작했다—그러고 나서 그는 우리 나머지에게 레이디 버린더가 세상을 떠났다고 말했다."], ["Serious persons, in search of proofs of hardened scepticism, may be interested in hearing that he showed no signs of remorse when he looked at Me.", "완고한 회의(懷疑)의 증거를 찾는 진지한 사람들은, 그가 나를 바라볼 때 아무런 뉘우침의 기색도 보이지 않았다는 것을 들으면 흥미로워할지 모른다."]], [["At a later hour I peeped into the breakfast-room, and the library.", "한참 뒤 나는 아침 식사방과 서재를 엿보았다."], ["My aunt had died without opening one of the letters which I had addressed to her.", "이모님은 내가 이모님 앞으로 쓴 편지들 가운데 한 통도 뜯지 않은 채 돌아가신 것이었다."], ["I was so shocked at this, that it never occurred to me, until some days afterwards, that she had also died without giving me my little legacy.", "나는 이것에 어찌나 충격받았던지, 이모님이 또한 나에게 나의 조그만 유산을 주지 않은 채 돌아가셨다는 것은, 며칠 뒤에야 비로소 내게 떠올랐다."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["(1.) “Miss Clack presents her compliments to Mr. Franklin Blake; and, in sending him the fifth chapter of her humble narrative, begs to say that she feels quite unequal to enlarge as she could wish on an event so awful, under the circumstances, as Lady Verinder’s death.", "(1.) \"클랙 양은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨께 문안 인사를 올리며, 그에게 자신의 미천한 이야기의 다섯째 장을 보내면서, 이런 정황에서 레이디 버린더의 죽음처럼 그토록 참혹한 사건에 대해서는 자신이 바라는 만큼 상세히 서술할 능력이 도무지 없다고 느낀다는 말씀을 감히 드립니다."], ["She has, therefore, attached to her own manuscripts, copious Extracts from precious publications in her possession, all bearing on this terrible subject.", "그리하여 클랙 양은 자신의 원고에, 자신이 지닌 소중한 간행물에서 뽑은, 죄다 이 끔찍한 주제와 관련된 넉넉한 발췌문을 붙였습니다."], ["And may those Extracts (Miss Clack fervently hopes) sound as the blast of a trumpet in the ears of her respected kinsman, Mr. Franklin Blake.”", "그리고 부디 그 발췌문이 (클랙 양은 뜨겁게 바랍니다) 자신이 존경하는 친척 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 귀에 나팔의 울림처럼 울려 퍼지기를 빕니다.\""]], [["(2.) “Mr. Franklin Blake presents his compliments to Miss Clack, and begs to thank her for the fifth chapter of her narrative.", "(2.) \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 클랙 양께 문안 인사를 올리며, 그녀의 이야기 다섯째 장에 대해 감사드리는 바입니다."], ["In returning the extracts sent with it, he will refrain from mentioning any personal objection which he may entertain to this species of literature, and will merely say that the proposed additions to the manuscript are not necessary to the fulfilment of the purpose that he has in view.”", "그것과 함께 보내진 발췌문을 돌려보내면서, 그는 이런 종류의 문헌에 대해 자신이 품을 수 있는 어떤 개인적 반감도 언급하기를 삼가고, 다만 그 원고에 덧붙이자고 제안된 것들이 그가 뜻하는 목적을 이루는 데에는 필요하지 않다고만 말씀드리겠습니다.\""]], [["(3.) “Miss Clack begs to acknowledge the return of her Extracts. She affectionately reminds Mr.", "(3.) \"클랙 양은 자신의 발췌문이 반송되었음을 확인하는 바입니다."], ["Franklin Blake that she is a Christian, and that it is, therefore, quite impossible for him to offend her. Miss C.", "그녀는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게, 자신이 기독교인이며, 따라서 그가 자신의 비위를 거스르는 것은 도무지 있을 수 없는 일임을 다정하게 상기시켜 드립니다."], ["persists in feeling the deepest interest in Mr. Blake, and pledges herself, on the first occasion when sickness may lay him low, to offer him the use of her Extracts for the second time.", "클랙 양은 여전히 블레이크 씨에게 더없이 깊은 관심을 느끼며, 병환이 그를 눕혀 놓게 될 첫 기회에, 자신의 발췌문을 두 번째로 그에게 쓰시도록 내드릴 것을 스스로 다짐합니다."], ["In the meanwhile she would be glad to know, before beginning the final chapters of her narrative, whether she may be permitted to make her humble contribution complete, by availing herself of the light which later discoveries have thrown on the mystery of the Moonstone.”", "그러는 동안 그녀는, 자신의 이야기의 마지막 장들을 시작하기에 앞서, 나중의 발견들이 월장석의 수수께끼에 던진 그 빛을 이용하여, 자신의 미천한 기고를 온전하게 만들어도 되는지 알고 싶습니다.\""]], [["(4.) “Mr. Franklin Blake is sorry to disappoint Miss Clack.", "(4.) \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 클랙 양을 실망시켜 유감스럽습니다."], ["He can only repeat the instructions which he had the honour of giving her when she began her narrative.", "그는 그녀가 이야기를 시작할 때 그녀에게 삼가 드린 지시를 되풀이할 수 있을 뿐입니다."], ["She is requested to limit herself to her own individual experience of persons and events, as recorded in her diary.", "그녀는, 자신의 일기에 기록된 대로, 사람과 사건에 대한 자기 자신의 개인적 경험에만 국한하기를 청받습니다."], ["Later discoveries she will be good enough to leave to the pens of those persons who can write in the capacity of actual witnesses.”", "나중의 발견들은 실제 증인의 자격으로 쓸 수 있는 사람들의 붓에 맡겨 주시면 고맙겠습니다.\""]], [["(5.) “Miss Clack is extremely sorry to trouble Mr. Franklin Blake with another letter.", "(5.) \"클랙 양은 또 한 통의 편지로 프랭클린 블레이크 씨를 성가시게 해 몹시 유감스럽습니다."], ["Her Extracts have been returned, and the expression of her matured views on the subject of the Moonstone has been forbidden.", "그녀의 발췌문은 반송되었고, 월장석이라는 주제에 대한 그녀의 무르익은 견해를 표하는 것은 금지되었습니다."], ["Miss Clack is painfully conscious that she ought (in the worldly phrase) to feel herself put down.", "클랙 양은 자신이 (세속적 표현으로) 묵살당했다고 느껴야 마땅함을 고통스럽게 자각합니다."], ["But, no—Miss C. has learnt Perseverance in the School of Adversity.", "허나, 아니지요—클랙 양은 역경의 학교에서 인내를 배웠습니다."], ["Her object in writing is to know whether Mr. Blake (who prohibits everything else) prohibits the appearance of the present correspondence in Miss Clack’s narrative?", "그녀가 편지를 쓰는 뜻은, 블레이크 씨가 (다른 모든 것을 금하시는 그가) 이 서신 왕복을 클랙 양의 이야기에 싣는 것도 금하시는지를 알기 위함입니다."], ["Some explanation of the position in which Mr. Blake’s interference has placed her as an authoress, seems due on the ground of common justice.", "블레이크 씨의 간섭이 저술가로서의 그녀를 놓은 그 처지에 대한 얼마쯤의 해명이, 마땅한 공정의 견지에서 필요한 듯싶습니다."], ["And Miss Clack, on her side, is most anxious that her letters should be produced to speak for themselves.”", "그리고 클랙 양은, 그녀 편에서, 자신의 편지들이 스스로 말하도록 내놓이기를 더없이 간절히 바랍니다.\""]], [["(6.) “Mr. Franklin Blake agrees to Miss Clack’s proposal, on the understanding that she will kindly consider this intimation of his consent as closing the correspondence between them.”", "(6.) \"프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 클랙 양의 제안에 동의하는 바이며, 다만 그녀가 이 동의의 통지를 두 사람 사이의 서신 왕복을 끝맺는 것으로 친절히 여겨 주시리라는 양해하에서입니다.\""]], [["(7.) “Miss Clack feels it an act of Christian duty (before the correspondence closes) to inform Mr. Franklin Blake that his last letter—evidently intended to offend her—has not succeeded in accomplishing the object of the writer. She affectionately requests Mr. Blake to retire to the privacy of his own room, and to consider with himself whether the training which can thus elevate a poor weak woman above the reach of insult, be not worthy of greater admiration than he is now disposed to feel for it. On being favoured with an intimation to that effect, Miss C. solemnly pledges herself to send back the complete series of her Extracts to Mr. Franklin Blake.”", "(7.) \"클랙 양은 (서신 왕복이 끝나기 전에) 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게, 그의 마지막 편지가—뻔히 그녀의 비위를 거스르려는 뜻이었던 그 편지가—그 필자의 목적을 이루는 데 성공하지 못했음을 알리는 것이 기독교인의 의무라고 느낍니다. 그녀는 블레이크 씨에게, 자기 방의 조용한 데로 물러가, 이렇듯 가엾고 연약한 한 여자를 모욕이 미치는 데 너머로 높여 줄 수 있는 그 수양이, 그가 지금 그것에 대해 느끼려는 것보다 더 큰 감탄을 받을 만하지 않은지 스스로 헤아려 보시기를 다정하게 청합니다. 그런 뜻의 통지를 받게 되면, 클랙 양은 자신의 발췌문 온전한 한 벌을 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 되돌려 보낼 것을 엄숙히 다짐합니다.\""]], [["[To this letter no answer was received.", "[이 편지에 대해서는 아무 답장도 받지 못했다. 논평은 부질없다."], ["Comment is needless.", "(서명) 드루실라 클랙.]"]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["The foregoing correspondence will sufficiently explain why no choice is left to me but to pass over Lady Verinder’s death with the simple announcement of the fact which ends my fifth chapter.", "앞의 서신 왕복이, 어째서 내게 나의 다섯째 장을 끝맺는 그 사실의 담담한 알림만으로 레이디 버린더의 죽음을 넘길 수밖에 다른 선택지가 없는지를 넉넉히 설명해 줄 것이다."]], [["Keeping myself for the future strictly within the limits of my own personal experience, I have next to relate that a month elapsed from the time of my aunt’s decease before Rachel Verinder and I met again.", "앞으로 나 자신의 개인적 경험의 테두리 안에 엄격히 머물면서, 나는 다음으로, 이모님이 돌아가신 때로부터 레이철 버린더와 내가 다시 만나기까지 한 달이 흘렀음을 이야기해야겠다."], ["That meeting was the occasion of my spending a few days under the same roof with her.", "그 만남은 내가 그 아이와 한 지붕 아래서 며칠을 보내는 계기가 되었다."], ["In the course of my visit, something happened, relative to her marriage-engagement with Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, which is important enough to require special notice in these pages.", "그 방문 중에, 그 아이의 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와의 혼약과 관련하여 어떤 일이 일어났는데, 그것은 이 쪽들에서 각별히 다룰 만큼 넉넉히 중요하다."], ["When this last of many painful family circumstances has been disclosed, my task will be completed; for I shall then have told all that I know, as an actual (and most unwilling) witness of events.", "이 많은 고통스러운 집안 정황 가운데 마지막이 밝혀지고 나면, 나의 소임은 완수될 것이다. 그때는 내가, 사건들의 실제 (그리고 더없이 마지못한) 증인으로서, 내가 아는 모든 것을 이야기한 셈이 될 테니까."]], [["My aunt’s remains were removed from London, and were buried in the little cemetery attached to the church in her own park.", "이모님의 유해는 런던에서 옮겨져, 당신 자신의 정원에 딸린 교회의 그 조그만 묘지에 묻혔다."], ["I was invited to the funeral with the rest of the family.", "나는 나머지 식구들과 함께 장례에 초대받았다."], ["But it was impossible (with my religious views) to rouse myself in a few days only from the shock which this death had caused me.", "허나 (나의 종교적 견해로 보아) 겨우 며칠 만에 이 죽음이 내게 일으킨 그 충격에서 나 자신을 추스르기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["I was informed, moreover, that the rector of Frizinghall was to read the service.", "게다가, 나는 프리징홀의 교구 목사가 그 예배를 집전하기로 되어 있다고 전해 들었다."], ["Having myself in past times seen this clerical castaway making one of the players at Lady Verinder’s whist-table, I doubt, even if I had been fit to travel, whether I should have felt justified in attending the ceremony.", "지난날 이 성직에서 버림받은 자가 레이디 버린더의 휘스트 탁자에서 노름꾼 가운데 하나가 되어 있는 것을 몸소 본 터라, 나는, 설령 내가 여행할 만한 상태였더라도, 그 의식에 참석하는 것이 정당하다고 느꼈을지 의심스럽다."]], [["Lady Verinder’s death left her daughter under the care of her brother-in-law, Mr. Ablewhite the elder.", "레이디 버린더의 죽음은 그 따님을 형부 되는 노(老) 에이블화이트 씨의 보살핌 아래 남겼다."], ["He was appointed guardian by the will, until his niece married, or came of age.", "그는 유언장에 의해, 조카딸이 혼인하거나 성년이 될 때까지, 후견인으로 임명되었다."], ["Under these circumstances, Mr. Godfrey informed his father, I suppose, of the new relation in which he stood towards Rachel.", "이런 정황에서, 고드프리 씨는, 내 짐작으로는, 저가 레이철에 대해 놓인 그 새로운 관계를 제 아버지에게 알렸다."], ["At any rate, in ten days from my aunt’s death, the secret of the marriage-engagement was no secret at all within the circle of the family, and the grand question for Mr. Ablewhite senior—another confirmed castaway!—was how to make himself and his authority most agreeable to the wealthy young lady who was going to marry his son.", "어쨌든, 이모님이 돌아가신 지 열흘 만에, 그 혼약의 비밀은 집안 테두리 안에서는 도무지 비밀이 아니게 되었고, 노 에이블화이트 씨에게—또 한 사람의 판박이 버림받은 자!—큰 물음은, 어떻게 저 자신과 제 권위를 제 아들과 혼인할 그 부유한 젊은 아가씨에게 더없이 마음에 들게 만드느냐 하는 것이었다."]], [["Rachel gave him some trouble at the outset, about the choice of a place in which she could be prevailed upon to reside.", "레이철은 처음부터, 그 아이가 살도록 설득될 만한 곳을 고르는 일로 그에게 얼마간 애를 먹였다."], ["The house in Montagu Square was associated with the calamity of her mother’s death.", "몬터규 스퀘어의 집은 제 어머니의 죽음이라는 참사와 엮여 있었다."], ["The house in Yorkshire was associated with the scandalous affair of the lost Moonstone.", "요크셔의 집은 잃어버린 월장석이라는 추문거리와 엮여 있었다."], ["Her guardian’s own residence at Frizinghall was open to neither of these objections.", "프리징홀에 있는 후견인 자신의 저택은 이 두 가지 반론 어느 쪽에도 걸리지 않았다."], ["But Rachel’s presence in it, after her recent bereavement, operated as a check on the gaieties of her cousins, the Miss Ablewhites—and she herself requested that her visit might be deferred to a more favourable opportunity.", "허나 최근의 사별 뒤 그곳에 있는 레이철의 존재가, 그 아이의 사촌들인 에이블화이트 아가씨들의 흥겨움에 제동을 걸었다—그리고 그 아이 자신이 제 방문을 더 알맞은 기회로 미루자고 청했다."], ["It ended in a proposal, emanating from old Mr. Ablewhite, to try a furnished house at Brighton.", "그것은 노 에이블화이트 씨에게서 나온, 브라이턴에 가구 딸린 집을 얻어 보자는 제안으로 끝났다."], ["His wife, an invalid daughter, and Rachel were to inhabit it together, and were to expect him to join them later in the season.", "그의 아내와, 병약한 딸과, 레이철이 함께 그 집에 살고, 그가 철이 좀 지나 그들에게 합류하기를 기다리기로 했다."], ["They would see no society but a few old friends, and they would have his son Godfrey, travelling backwards and forwards by the London train, always at their disposal.", "그들은 몇몇 오랜 벗 말고는 아무 교제도 하지 않을 터였고, 런던행 기차로 오락가락하는 그의 아들 고드프리를 언제든 마음대로 부릴 수 있을 터였다."]], [["I describe this aimless flitting about from one place of residence to another—this insatiate restlessness of body and appalling stagnation of soul—merely with the view to arriving at results.", "내가 이 목적 없는, 이 거처에서 저 거처로의 옮겨 다님을—이 채울 길 없는 몸의 안절부절과 소름 끼치는 영혼의 정체를—묘사하는 것은, 그저 결과에 이르기 위함이다."], ["The event which (under Providence) proved to be the means of bringing Rachel Verinder and myself together again, was no other than the hiring of the house at Brighton.", "(섭리 아래) 레이철 버린더와 나를 다시 한자리에 모으는 방편이 된 그 사건은, 다름 아닌 그 브라이턴 집을 얻는 일이었다."]], [["My Aunt Ablewhite is a large, silent, fair-complexioned woman, with one noteworthy point in her character.", "나의 에이블화이트 이모는 몸집 크고, 말 없고, 살빛 흰 여자로, 그 됨됨이에 눈여겨볼 만한 점이 하나 있다."], ["From the hour of her birth she has never been known to do anything for herself.", "태어난 그 시각부터 그녀는 여태껏 무엇 하나 제 손으로 한 것으로 알려진 적이 없다."], ["She has gone through life, accepting everybody’s help, and adopting everybody’s opinions.", "그녀는 모든 이의 도움을 받아들이고, 모든 이의 의견을 취하며 삶을 지나왔다."], ["A more hopeless person, in a spiritual point of view, I have never met with—there is absolutely, in this perplexing case, no obstructive material to work upon.", "영적인 관점에서, 나는 여태껏 더 가망 없는 사람을 만나 본 적이 없다—이 난감한 경우에는, 붙들고 씨름할 저항하는 재료가 전혀 없다."], ["Aunt Ablewhite would listen to the Grand Lama of Thibet exactly as she listens to Me, and would reflect his views quite as readily as she reflects mine.", "에이블화이트 이모는 티베트의 달라이 라마가 하는 말을 딱 나의 말을 듣듯 들을 것이고, 그의 견해를 나의 견해를 비추는 것만큼이나 선선히 비출 것이다."], ["She found the furnished house at Brighton by stopping at an hotel in London, composing herself on a sofa, and sending for her son.", "그녀는 런던의 어느 호텔에 묵으며, 소파에 몸을 뉘고, 제 아들을 부름으로써, 브라이턴의 그 가구 딸린 집을 찾아냈다."], ["She discovered the necessary servants by breakfasting in bed one morning (still at the hotel), and giving her maid a holiday on condition that the girl “would begin enjoying herself by fetching Miss Clack.”", "그녀는 어느 아침 (여전히 그 호텔에서) 침대에서 아침을 들면서, 제 하녀에게 \"클랙 양을 데려오는 것으로 즐거움을 시작하는\" 조건으로 휴가를 줌으로써, 필요한 하인들을 알아냈다."], ["I found her placidly fanning herself in her dressing-gown at eleven o’clock.", "나는 열한 시에 그녀가 실내복 차림으로 태평하게 부채질하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["“Drusilla, dear, I want some servants.", "\"드루실라, 얘야, 나는 하인이 몇 필요하구나."], ["You are so clever—please get them for me.”", "너는 어찌나 영리한지—부디 나를 위해 그들을 구해 다오.\""], ["I looked round the untidy room.", "나는 어수선한 방을 둘러보았다."], ["The church-bells were going for a week-day service; they suggested a word of affectionate remonstrance on my part.", "평일 예배를 알리는 교회 종이 울리고 있었다. 그것이 내게 다정한 훈계 한마디를 넌지시 일러 주었다."], ["“Oh, aunt!”", "\"오, 이모님!\""], ["I said sadly.", "하고 나는 서글피 말했다."], ["“Is this worthy of a Christian Englishwoman?", "\"이것이 기독교인 영국 여자에게 어울리는 일인가요?"], ["Is the passage from time to eternity to be made in this manner?”", "시간에서 영원으로 건너가는 것을 이런 식으로 해야 하나요?\""], ["My aunt answered, “I’ll put on my gown, Drusilla, if you will be kind enough to help me.”", "이모는 대답했다. \"가운을 입으마, 드루실라, 네가 친절히 도와준다면.\""], ["What was to be said after that?", "그 뒤에 무슨 말을 할 수 있었으랴?"], ["I have done wonders with murderesses—I have never advanced an inch with Aunt Ablewhite.", "나는 살인한 여자들을 상대로 놀라운 일들을 해냈다—나는 에이블화이트 이모를 상대로는 한 치도 나아가지 못했다. \"이모님이 필요로 하시는 하인들의,\""], ["“Where is the list,”", "하고 나는 물었다."], ["I asked, “of the servants whom you require?”", "\"명단은 어디 있어요?\" 이모는 고개를 저었다."], ["My aunt shook her head; she hadn’t even energy enough to keep the list.", "그녀에게는 명단을 간수할 기운조차 없었다."], ["“Rachel has got it, dear,”", "\"레이철이 갖고 있단다, 얘야,\""], ["she said, “in the next room.”", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"옆방에.\""], ["I went into the next room, and so saw Rachel again for the first time since we had parted in Montagu Square.", "나는 옆방으로 들어갔고, 그렇게 우리가 몬터규 스퀘어에서 헤어진 뒤 처음으로 레이철을 다시 보았다."]], [["She looked pitiably small and thin in her deep mourning.", "그 아이는 짙은 상복 차림으로 딱하리만치 작고 여위어 보였다."], ["If I attached any serious importance to such a perishable trifle as personal appearance, I might be inclined to add that hers was one of those unfortunate complexions which always suffer when not relieved by a border of white next the skin.", "만일 내가 용모 같은 그토록 스러지기 쉬운 하찮은 것에 조금이라도 진지한 무게를 둔다면, 그 아이의 살빛이, 살갗에 잇닿은 흰 테두리로 돋보이지 않을 때는 언제나 손해를 보는 그 불운한 살빛 가운데 하나라고 덧붙일 마음이 들지도 모른다."], ["But what are our complexions and our looks?", "허나 우리의 살빛과 우리의 용모가 무엇인가?"], ["Hindrances and pitfalls, dear girls, which beset us on our way to higher things!", "더 높은 것들로 가는 우리 길목에 도사린 걸림돌이자 함정이다, 사랑하는 아이들이여!"], ["Greatly to my surprise, Rachel rose when I entered the room, and came forward to meet me with outstretched hand.", "나의 큰 놀라움에도, 레이철은 내가 방에 들어서자 일어나, 손을 내밀고 나를 맞으러 앞으로 나왔다."]], [["“I am glad to see you,” she said.", "\"만나서 반가워요.\""], ["“Drusilla, I have been in the habit of speaking very foolishly and very rudely to you, on former occasions.", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"드루실라, 저는 예전에 여러 번, 당신에게 아주 어리석고 아주 무례하게 말하는 버릇이 있었죠."], ["I beg your pardon.", "용서를 빌어요."], ["I hope you will forgive me.”", "부디 저를 용서해 주면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["My face, I suppose, betrayed the astonishment I felt at this.", "내 얼굴이, 짐작건대, 이것에 내가 느낀 놀라움을 드러냈으리라."], ["She coloured up for a moment, and then proceeded to explain herself.", "그 아이는 한순간 얼굴을 붉히고는, 이어서 제 뜻을 설명했다."]], [["“In my poor mother’s lifetime,”", "\"제 가엾은 어머니가 살아 계실 때는,\""], ["she went on, “her friends were not always my friends, too.", "하고 그 아이가 이어 갔다. \"그분의 벗들이 늘 제 벗이기도 했던 건 아니었어요."], ["Now I have lost her, my heart turns for comfort to the people she liked.", "이제 그분을 잃고 나니, 제 마음이 위안을 얻으려고 그분이 좋아하던 사람들에게로 돌아서네요."], ["She liked you.", "그분은 당신을 좋아하셨죠."], ["Try to be friends with me, Drusilla, if you can.”", "저와 벗이 되어 주려 애써 줘요, 드루실라, 그럴 수 있다면요.\""]], [["To any rightly-constituted mind, the motive thus acknowledged was simply shocking.", "올바르게 짜인 마음이라면 누구에게든, 이렇게 인정된 그 동기는 그저 충격적이었다."], ["Here in Christian England was a young woman in a state of bereavement, with so little idea of where to look for true comfort, that she actually expected to find it among her mother’s friends!", "여기 기독교의 잉글랜드에, 사별의 처지에 놓인 젊은 여자가, 참된 위안을 어디서 찾아야 할지에 대한 생각이 어찌나 없던지, 그것을 실제로 제 어머니의 벗들 사이에서 찾으리라 예상하다니!"], ["Here was a relative of mine, awakened to a sense of her shortcomings towards others, under the influence, not of conviction and duty, but of sentiment and impulse!", "여기 나의 친척이, 타인들에 대한 제 모자람의 자각에 눈떴는데, 확신과 의무의 영향 아래서가 아니라, 감상과 충동의 영향 아래서였다니!"], ["Most deplorable to think of—but, still, suggestive of something hopeful, to a person of my experience in plying the good work.", "생각하기에 더없이 개탄스럽거니와—허나, 그럼에도, 좋은 일에 힘쓰는 나 같은 경험 있는 사람에게는, 무언가 희망적인 것을 넌지시 알려 주는 노릇이었다."], ["There could be no harm, I thought, in ascertaining the extent of the change which the loss of her mother had wrought in Rachel’s character.", "나는, 그 어머니의 죽음이 레이철의 됨됨이에 일으킨 그 변화의 정도를 확인하는 데 해로울 것이 없다고 생각했다."], ["I decided, as a useful test, to probe her on the subject of her marriage-engagement to Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "나는, 쓸모 있는 시험으로, 그 아이의 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와의 혼약이라는 화제에 대해 그 아이를 떠보기로 마음먹었다."]], [["Having first met her advances with all possible cordiality, I sat by her on the sofa, at her own request.", "먼저 그 아이의 다가옴을 될 수 있는 대로 더없이 살갑게 맞아 준 뒤, 나는 그 아이 자신의 청에 따라, 소파에서 그 아이 곁에 앉았다."], ["We discussed family affairs and future plans—always excepting that one future plan which was to end in her marriage.", "우리는 집안일과 앞날의 계획을 논했다—그 아이의 혼인으로 끝나게 될 그 한 가지 앞날의 계획만은 언제나 빼고서."], ["Try as I might to turn the conversation that way, she resolutely declined to take the hint.", "아무리 그쪽으로 이야기를 돌리려 애써도, 그 아이는 그 낌새를 알아채기를 한사코 마다했다."], ["Any open reference to the question, on my part, would have been premature at this early stage of our reconciliation.", "그 물음을 내 쪽에서 드러내 놓고 이르는 것은, 우리 화해의 이 이른 단계에서는 너무 성급한 노릇이었으리라."], ["Besides, I had discovered all I wanted to know.", "게다가, 나는 알고 싶은 것을 죄다 알아냈다."], ["She was no longer the reckless, defiant creature whom I had heard and seen, on the occasion of my martyrdom in Montagu Square.", "그 아이는 더는, 몬터규 스퀘어에서의 나의 순교 때 내가 듣고 본 그 무모하고 반항적인 것이 아니었다."], ["This was, of itself, enough to encourage me to take her future conversion in hand—beginning with a few words of earnest warning directed against the hasty formation of the marriage tie, and so getting on to higher things.", "이것만으로도, 그 아이의 앞날의 개심을 손에 맡을 만큼—혼인의 매듭을 성급히 짓는 데 맞선 몇 마디 간곡한 경고로 시작하여, 그렇게 더 높은 것들로 나아갈 만큼—넉넉히 나를 부추겼다."], ["Looking at her, now, with this new interest—and calling to mind the headlong suddenness with which she had met Mr. Godfrey’s matrimonial views—I felt the solemn duty of interfering with a fervour which assured me that I should achieve no common results.", "이제, 이 새로운 관심으로 그 아이를 바라보며—그리고 그 아이가 고드프리 씨의 혼인 견해를 맞은 그 곤두박질 같은 느닷없음을 떠올리며—나는, 내가 예사롭지 않은 결과를 이루어 내리라 확신케 하는 뜨거움으로, 끼어드는 그 엄숙한 의무를 느꼈다."], ["Rapidity of proceeding was, as I believed, of importance in this case.", "이 경우에는, 내가 믿기로는, 일을 빨리 처리하는 것이 중요했다."], ["I went back at once to the question of the servants wanted for the furnished house.", "나는 대뜸 그 가구 딸린 집에 필요한 하인들이라는 물음으로 돌아갔다."]], [["“Where is the list, dear?”", "\"명단이 어디 있니, 얘야?\""]], [["Rachel produced it.", "레이철이 그것을 내놓았다."]], [["“Cook, kitchen-maid, housemaid, and footman,” I read.", "\"요리사, 부엌 하녀, 하녀, 그리고 남자 하인이라.\""], ["“My dear Rachel, these servants are only wanted for a term—the term during which your guardian has taken the house.", "하고 나는 읽었다. \"얘 레이철, 이 하인들은 한 기간 동안만—네 후견인이 그 집을 얻은 기간 동안만 필요한 거지."], ["We shall have great difficulty in finding persons of character and capacity to accept a temporary engagement of that sort, if we try in London.", "런던에서 구하려 들면, 우리는 그런 종류의 한시적 자리를 받아들일, 됨됨이와 능력을 갖춘 사람들을 찾는 데 큰 어려움을 겪을 게다."], ["Has the house in Brighton been found yet?”", "브라이턴 집은 벌써 구해졌니?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"네."], ["Godfrey has taken it; and persons in the house wanted him to hire them as servants.", "고드프리가 얻었어요. 그리고 그 집 사람들이 그더러 저희를 하인으로 고용해 달라 했고요."], ["He thought they would hardly do for us, and came back having settled nothing.”", "그는 그들이 우리에게 좀처럼 어울리지 않으리라 여기고, 아무것도 정하지 않은 채 돌아왔어요.\""]], [["“And you have no experience yourself in these matters, Rachel?”", "\"그리고 너 자신은 이런 일에 아무 경험도 없고, 레이철?\""]], [["“None whatever.”", "\"전혀 없어요.\""]], [["“And Aunt Ablewhite won’t exert herself?”", "\"그리고 에이블화이트 이모는 몸을 움직이려 하지 않으실 테고?\""]], [["“No, poor dear.", "\"안 하시죠, 가엾은 이."], ["Don’t blame her, Drusilla.", "그분을 나무라지 마세요, 드루실라."], ["I think she is the only really happy woman I have ever met with.”", "제 생각에 그분은 제가 여태껏 만나 본 가운데 정말로 유일하게 행복한 여자예요.\""]], [["“There are degrees in happiness, darling.", "\"행복에도 정도가 있단다, 얘야."], ["We must have a little talk, some day, on that subject.", "우리 언젠가 그 화제에 대해 좀 이야기 나눠야겠구나."], ["In the meantime I will undertake to meet the difficulty about the servants.", "그러는 동안 하인들에 대한 그 어려움은 내가 맡으마."], ["Your aunt will write a letter to the people of the house——”", "네 이모가 그 집 사람들에게 편지를 쓰시면——\""]], [["“She will sign a letter, if I write it for her, which comes to the same thing.”", "\"제가 그분을 위해 써 드리면 그분이 서명하실 거예요, 그게 같은 얘기죠.\""]], [["“Quite the same thing.", "\"딱 같은 얘기지."], ["I shall get the letter, and I will go to Brighton tomorrow.”", "내가 그 편지를 받아서, 내일 브라이턴으로 가마.\""]], [["“How extremely kind of you!", "\"어쩌면 그렇게 친절하세요!"], ["We will join you as soon as you are ready for us.", "저희도 당신 채비가 되는 대로 곧 당신과 합류할게요."], ["And you will stay, I hope, as my guest.", "그리고 부디, 제 손님으로 머물러 주세요."], ["Brighton is so lively; you are sure to enjoy it.”", "브라이턴은 아주 활기차거든요. 당신도 분명 즐거우실 거예요.\""]], [["In those words the invitation was given, and the glorious prospect of interference was opened before me.", "그 말로 초대가 건네졌고, 끼어듦의 영광스러운 앞날이 내 앞에 열렸다."]], [["It was then the middle of the week.", "그때는 주중이었다."], ["By Saturday afternoon the house was ready for them.", "토요일 오후면 그 집은 그들을 맞을 채비가 되었다."], ["In that short interval I had sifted, not the characters only, but the religious views as well, of all the disengaged servants who applied to me, and had succeeded in making a selection which my conscience approved.", "그 짧은 사이에 나는, 내게 지원한 일 없는 하인들 죄다의 됨됨이뿐만 아니라 종교적 견해까지 가려내어, 내 양심이 승인하는 선택을 해내는 데 성공했다."], ["I also discovered, and called on two serious friends of mine, residents in the town, to whom I knew I could confide the pious object which had brought me to Brighton.", "나는 또한, 브라이턴에 사는 나의 진지한 벗 둘을 알아내어 찾아갔는데, 나는 그들에게 나를 브라이턴으로 데려온 그 경건한 목적을 털어놓을 수 있음을 알고 있었다."], ["One of them—a clerical friend—kindly helped me to take sittings for our little party in the church in which he himself ministered.", "그중 하나는—성직자 벗이었는데—우리 작은 무리를 위해 저 자신이 봉직하는 교회에 자리를 잡는 것을 친절히 도와주었다."], ["The other—a single lady, like myself—placed the resources of her library (composed throughout of precious publications) entirely at my disposal.", "다른 하나는—나처럼 미혼의 부인이었는데—제 서고(온통 소중한 간행물로 이루어진)의 자원을 온전히 내 뜻대로 쓰게 해 주었다."], ["I borrowed half-a-dozen works, all carefully chosen with a view to Rachel.", "나는 예닐곱 권의 저작을, 죄다 레이철을 염두에 두고 정성껏 골라, 빌렸다."], ["When these had been judiciously distributed in the various rooms she would be likely to occupy, I considered that my preparations were complete.", "이것들을 그 아이가 있음 직한 여러 방에 슬기롭게 뿌려 놓고 나서, 나는 나의 준비가 완료되었다고 여겼다."], ["Sound doctrine in the servants who waited on her; sound doctrine in the minister who preached to her; sound doctrine in the books that lay on her table—such was the treble welcome which my zeal had prepared for the motherless girl!", "그 아이를 시중드는 하인들 안의 건전한 교리, 그 아이에게 설교하는 목사 안의 건전한 교리, 그 아이 탁자에 놓인 책들 안의 건전한 교리—그것이, 나의 열의가 그 어미 없는 아이를 위해 마련한 세 겹의 환영이었다!"], ["A heavenly composure filled my mind, on that Saturday afternoon, as I sat at the window waiting the arrival of my relatives.", "그 토요일 오후, 내가 창가에 앉아 나의 친척들이 도착하기를 기다리는 동안, 천상의 평온이 내 마음을 채웠다."], ["The giddy throng passed and repassed before my eyes.", "어지러운 무리가 내 눈앞을 지나갔다 다시 지나갔다 했다."], ["Alas!", "아아!"], ["how many of them felt my exquisite sense of duty done?", "그들 가운데 얼마나 많은 이가 소임을 다했다는 나의 더없는 느낌을 느꼈던가?"], ["An awful question.", "끔찍한 물음이다."], ["Let us not pursue it.", "우리 그것을 파고들지 말자."]], [["Between six and seven the travellers arrived. To my indescribable surprise, they were escorted, not by Mr.", "여섯 시와 일곱 시 사이에 그 여행자들이 도착했다."], ["Godfrey (as I had anticipated), but by the lawyer, Mr. Bruff.", "나의 이루 말할 수 없는 놀라움에도, 그들을 호위한 것은 (내가 예상했던 대로) 고드프리 씨가 아니라, 변호사 브러프 씨였다."]], [["“How do you do, Miss Clack?”", "\"안녕하십니까, 클랙 양?\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I mean to stay this time.”", "\"이번에는 머무를 작정입니다.\""]], [["That reference to the occasion on which I had obliged him to postpone his business to mine, when we were both visiting in Montagu Square, satisfied me that the old worldling had come to Brighton with some object of his own in view.", "우리 둘 다 몬터규 스퀘어에 방문했을 때 내가 그로 하여금 제 볼일을 내 볼일 뒤로 미루게 했던 그 일을 이르는 그 말이, 그 늙은 속물이 저 나름의 어떤 목적을 품고 브라이턴에 왔음을 내게 납득시켰다."], ["I had prepared quite a little Paradise for my beloved Rachel—and here was the Serpent already!", "나는 나의 사랑하는 레이철을 위해 넉넉히 조그만 낙원을 마련해 두었는데—여기 벌써 뱀이 있었던 것이다!"]], [["“Godfrey was very much vexed, Drusilla, not to be able to come with us,”", "\"고드프리가 우리와 함께 오지 못해 아주 속상해했어, 드루실라.\""], ["said my Aunt Ablewhite.", "하고 에이블화이트 이모가 말했다."], ["“There was something in the way which kept him in town.", "\"그를 도회지에 붙들어 놓는 무언가가 있었나 봐."], ["Mr. Bruff volunteered to take his place, and make a holiday of it till Monday morning.", "브러프 씨가 자청해서 그의 자리를 대신하고, 월요일 아침까지 휴가로 삼겠다고 했지."], ["By-the-bye, Mr. Bruff, I’m ordered to take exercise, and I don’t like it.", "그런데 참, 브러프 씨, 나는 운동을 하라는 분부를 받았는데, 나는 그게 싫어요."], ["That,”", "저것이,\""], ["added Aunt Ablewhite, pointing out of window to an invalid going by in a chair on wheels, drawn by a man, “is my idea of exercise.", "하고 에이블화이트 이모가, 창밖으로 사내가 끄는 바퀴 달린 의자에 실려 가는 어느 병자를 가리키며 덧붙였다. \"내가 생각하는 운동이에요."], ["If it’s air you want, you get it in your chair.", "바람을 쐬고 싶으면, 의자에 앉아 쐬면 되죠."], ["And if it’s fatigue you want, I am sure it’s fatigue enough to look at the man.”", "그리고 고단해지고 싶으면, 저 사내를 보는 것만으로도 넉넉히 고단하고요.\""]], [["Rachel stood silent, at a window by herself, with her eyes fixed on the sea.", "레이철은 저 혼자 어느 창가에, 두 눈을 바다에 고정한 채, 말없이 서 있었다."]], [["“Tired, love?”", "\"고단하니, 얘야?\""], ["I inquired.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["Only a little out of spirits,”", "그저 기운이 좀 없어요.\""], ["she answered.", "하고 그 아이가 대답했다."], ["“I have often seen the sea, on our Yorkshire coast, with that light on it.", "\"저는 우리 요크셔 해안에서, 저런 빛이 어린 바다를 자주 봤어요."], ["And I was thinking, Drusilla, of the days that can never come again.”", "그리고 저는, 드루실라, 결코 다시 올 수 없는 그 나날을 생각하고 있었어요.\""]], [["Mr. Bruff remained to dinner, and stayed through the evening.", "브러프 씨는 저녁 식사에 남았고, 저녁 내내 머물렀다."], ["The more I saw of him, the more certain I felt that he had some private end to serve in coming to Brighton.", "그를 볼수록, 나는 그가 브라이턴에 오면서 봉사할 어떤 남모를 목적이 있다고 더욱 확신했다."], ["I watched him carefully.", "나는 그를 유심히 지켜보았다."], ["He maintained the same appearance of ease, and talked the same godless gossip, hour after hour, until it was time to take leave.", "그는 떠날 때가 될 때까지, 시간이 가고 또 가도, 똑같은 태연한 모양새를 유지하고, 똑같은 신을 모르는 잡담을 늘어놓았다."], ["As he shook hands with Rachel, I caught his hard and cunning eyes resting on her for a moment with a peculiar interest and attention.", "그가 레이철과 악수할 때, 나는 그의 딱딱하고 교활한 눈이 한순간 유별난 관심과 주의로 그 아이에게 머무는 것을 포착했다."], ["She was plainly concerned in the object that he had in view.", "그 아이는 뻔히 그가 품은 그 목적에 관련되어 있었다."], ["He said nothing out of the common to her or to anyone on leaving.", "그는 떠나면서 그 아이에게든 누구에게든 예사롭지 않은 말은 아무것도 하지 않았다."], ["He invited himself to luncheon the next day, and then he went away to his hotel.", "그는 이튿날 점심에 저 자신을 초대하고는, 제 호텔로 갔다."]], [["It was impossible the next morning to get my Aunt Ablewhite out of her dressing-gown in time for church.", "이튿날 아침 에이블화이트 이모를 교회에 갈 시간에 맞춰 실내복에서 벗어나게 하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["Her invalid daughter (suffering from nothing, in my opinion, but incurable laziness, inherited from her mother) announced that she meant to remain in bed for the day.", "그 병약한 딸은 (내 생각에는, 제 어머니에게서 물려받은 낫지 않는 게으름 말고는 아무것도 앓지 않으면서) 그날은 침대에 있을 작정이라고 알렸다."], ["Rachel and I went alone together to church.", "레이철과 나는 단둘이서 교회에 갔다."], ["A magnificent sermon was preached by my gifted friend on the heathen indifference of the world to the sinfulness of little sins.", "나의 재능 있는 벗이 작은 죄들의 죄스러움에 대한 세상의 이교도 같은 무관심에 대해 훌륭한 설교를 했다."], ["For more than an hour his eloquence (assisted by his glorious voice) thundered through the sacred edifice.", "한 시간 넘게 그의 웅변이 (그의 영광스러운 목소리에 도움받아) 그 성스러운 건물에 우렁차게 울려 퍼졌다. 나는 우리가 나올 때 레이철에게 말했다."], ["I said to Rachel, when we came out, “Has it found its way to your heart, dear?”", "\"그것이 네 마음에 가닿았니, 얘야?\" 그러자 그 아이가 대답했다."], ["And she answered, “No; it has only made my head ache.”", "\"아니요. 그저 머리가 아플 뿐이에요.\""], ["This might have been discouraging to some people; but, once embarked on a career of manifest usefulness, nothing discourages Me.", "이것은 어떤 사람들에게는 맥 빠지는 노릇이었을지 모른다. 허나, 한번 명백한 쓸모의 길에 나서면, 아무것도 나를 맥 빠지게 하지 못한다."]], [["We found Aunt Ablewhite and Mr. Bruff at luncheon.", "우리는 에이블화이트 이모와 브러프 씨가 점심을 들고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["When Rachel declined eating anything, and gave as a reason for it that she was suffering from a headache, the lawyer’s cunning instantly saw, and seized, the chance that she had given him.", "레이철이 아무것도 먹기를 마다하며, 머리가 아파 그런다고 이유를 대자, 그 변호사의 교활함이 대뜸, 그 아이가 그에게 준 그 기회를 보고, 붙잡았다."]], [["“There is only one remedy for a headache,”", "\"머리 아픈 데는 약이 하나뿐입니다.\""], ["said this horrible old man.", "하고 이 끔찍한 늙은이가 말했다."], ["“A walk, Miss Rachel, is the thing to cure you.", "\"산책이, 레이철 아가씨, 아가씨를 낫게 할 것입니다."], ["I am entirely at your service, if you will honour me by accepting my arm.”", "제 팔을 받아 주는 영광을 베풀어 주신다면, 저는 온전히 아가씨의 뜻에 따르겠습니다.\""]], [["“With the greatest pleasure.", "\"더없이 기쁘게요."], ["A walk is the very thing I was longing for.”", "산책이야말로 제가 바로 갈망하던 거예요.\""]], [["“It’s past two,”", "\"두 시가 지났어.\""], ["I gently suggested.", "하고 나는 부드럽게 넌지시 일렀다."], ["“And the afternoon service, Rachel, begins at three.”", "\"그리고 오후 예배가, 레이철, 세 시에 시작한단다.\""]], [["“How can you expect me to go to church again,”", "\"이런 두통을 가지고 어떻게 저더러 또 교회에 가라는 거예요?\""], ["she asked, petulantly, “with such a headache as mine?”", "하고 그 아이가 짜증스레 물었다."]], [["Mr. Bruff officiously opened the door for her.", "브러프 씨가 주제넘게 그 아이를 위해 문을 열어 주었다."], ["In another minute more they were both out of the house.", "한 순간 더 지나 그들은 둘 다 집 밖에 있었다."], ["I don’t know when I have felt the solemn duty of interfering so strongly as I felt it at that moment.", "나는 그 순간 느낀 것만큼 끼어드는 엄숙한 의무를 그토록 강하게 느껴 본 적이 언제였는지 모르겠다."], ["But what was to be done?", "허나 어찌해야 했던가?"], ["Nothing was to be done but to interfere at the first opportunity, later in the day.", "그날 늦게, 첫 기회에 끼어드는 것 말고는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."]], [["On my return from the afternoon service I found that they had just got back.", "오후 예배에서 돌아오니 나는 그들이 막 돌아온 것을 알았다."], ["One look at them told me that the lawyer had said what he wanted to say.", "그들을 한 번 보는 것으로 나는 그 변호사가 하고 싶은 말을 했음을 알았다."], ["I had never before seen Rachel so silent and so thoughtful.", "나는 여태껏 레이철이 그토록 말이 없고 그토록 생각에 잠긴 것을 본 적이 없었다."], ["I had never before seen Mr. Bruff pay her such devoted attention, and look at her with such marked respect.", "나는 여태껏 브러프 씨가 그 아이에게 그토록 헌신적인 주의를 기울이고, 그토록 두드러진 존경으로 그 아이를 바라보는 것을 본 적이 없었다."], ["He had (or pretended that he had) an engagement to dinner that day—and he took an early leave of us all; intending to go back to London by the first train the next morning.", "그는 그날 저녁 식사 약속이 있었다 (또는 있는 척했다)—그리고 그는 우리 모두에게 이른 작별을 고했다. 이튿날 아침 첫 기차로 런던에 돌아갈 작정으로 말이다."]], [["“Are you sure of your own resolution?”", "\"당신 자신의 결심을 확신합니까?\""], ["he said to Rachel at the door.", "하고 그가 문가에서 레이철에게 말했다."]], [["“Quite sure,”", "\"아주 확신해요.\""], ["she answered—and so they parted.", "하고 그 아이가 대답했다—그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다."]], [["The moment his back was turned, Rachel withdrew to her own room.", "그가 등을 돌리는 순간, 레이철은 제 방으로 물러갔다."], ["She never appeared at dinner.", "그 아이는 저녁 식사에 도무지 나타나지 않았다."], ["Her maid (the person with the cap-ribbons) was sent downstairs to announce that her headache had returned.", "그 아이의 하녀 (그 모자 리본을 단 사람)가 아래층으로 보내져, 그 아이의 두통이 도졌다고 알렸다."], ["I ran up to her and made all sorts of sisterly offers through the door.", "나는 그 아이에게로 뛰어 올라가, 문 너머로 온갖 자매다운 도움을 내밀었다."], ["It was locked, and she kept it locked.", "문은 잠겨 있었고, 그 아이는 그것을 잠근 채 두었다."], ["Plenty of obstructive material to work on here!", "여기 붙들고 씨름할 저항하는 재료가 넉넉했다!"], ["I felt greatly cheered and stimulated by her locking the door.", "나는 그 아이가 문을 잠근 것에 크게 기운이 나고 북돋아졌다."]], [["When her cup of tea went up to her the next morning, I followed it in.", "이튿날 아침 그 아이의 차 한 잔이 위층으로 올라갈 때, 나는 그것을 뒤따라 들어갔다."], ["I sat by her bedside and said a few earnest words.", "나는 그 아이 침대맡에 앉아 몇 마디 간곡한 말을 했다."], ["She listened with languid civility.", "그 아이는 나른한 정중함으로 들었다."], ["I noticed my serious friend’s precious publications huddled together on a table in a corner.", "나는 나의 진지한 벗의 소중한 간행물들이 한구석 탁자 위에 뒤섞여 쌓여 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["Had she chanced to look into them?—I asked.", "그 아이가 우연히 그것들을 들여다본 적이 있는지?—나는 물었다."], ["Yes—and they had not interested her.", "그렇다—그리고 그것들은 그 아이의 흥미를 끌지 못했다."], ["Would she allow me to read a few passages of the deepest interest, which had probably escaped her eye?", "그 아이 눈을 아마 스쳐 지나갔을, 더없이 깊은 흥미로운 대목 몇 개를 내가 읽어 주는 것을 허락하겠는지?"], ["No, not now—she had other things to think of.", "아니, 지금은 아니에요—그 아이는 생각할 다른 일들이 있었다."], ["She gave these answers, with her attention apparently absorbed in folding and refolding the frilling on her nightgown.", "그 아이는, 뻔히 제 잠옷의 주름 장식을 접었다 다시 접었다 하는 데 정신을 팔며, 이 대답들을 했다."], ["It was plainly necessary to rouse her by some reference to those worldly interests which she still had at heart.", "그 아이가 여전히 마음에 두고 있는 그 세속적 잇속에 대한 어떤 언급으로 그 아이를 일깨우는 것이 뻔히 필요했다."]], [["“Do you know, love,”", "\"그거 아니, 얘야,\""], ["I said, “I had an odd fancy, yesterday, about Mr.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 어제, 브러프 씨에 대해 이상한 생각이 들었단다."], ["Bruff? I thought, when I saw you after your walk with him, that he had been telling you some bad news.”", "나는, 네가 그와 산책한 뒤에 너를 봤을 때, 그가 네게 무슨 나쁜 소식을 전한 거라고 생각했지.\""]], [["Her fingers dropped from the frilling of her nightgown, and her fierce black eyes flashed at me.", "그 아이의 손가락이 제 잠옷 주름 장식에서 떨어졌고, 그 아이의 사나운 검은 눈이 나를 향해 번득였다."]], [["“Quite the contrary!”", "\"정반대예요!\""], ["she said.", "하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["“It was news I was interested in hearing—and I am deeply indebted to Mr. Bruff for telling me of it.”", "\"그건 제가 듣기에 관심 있던 소식이었어요—그리고 저는 그것을 제게 전해 준 브러프 씨에게 깊이 감사하고 있고요.\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래?\""], ["I said, in a tone of gentle interest.", "하고 나는 부드러운 관심의 어조로 말했다."]], [["Her fingers went back to the frilling, and she turned her head sullenly away from me.", "그 아이의 손가락이 주름 장식으로 돌아갔고, 그 아이는 뾰로통하게 나에게서 고개를 돌렸다."], ["I had been met in this manner, in the course of plying the good work, hundreds of times.", "좋은 일에 힘쓰는 동안, 나는 이런 식으로 수백 번 마주쳐졌다."], ["She merely stimulated me to try again.", "그것은 그저 나로 하여금 다시 시도하도록 북돋았을 뿐이다."], ["In my dauntless zeal for her welfare, I ran the great risk, and openly alluded to her marriage engagement.", "그 아이의 안녕을 위한 나의 굴하지 않는 열의로, 나는 큰 위험을 무릅쓰고, 그 아이의 혼약을 드러내 놓고 넌지시 일렀다."]], [["“News you were interested in hearing?”", "\"네가 듣기에 관심 있던 소식이라?\""], ["I repeated.", "하고 나는 되뇌었다."], ["“I suppose, my dear Rachel, that must be news of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite?”", "\"짐작건대, 얘 레이철, 그건 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 소식임이 틀림없겠구나?\""]], [["She started up in the bed, and turned deadly pale.", "그 아이는 침대에서 벌떡 일어나, 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["It was evidently on the tip of her tongue to retort on me with the unbridled insolence of former times.", "지난날의 고삐 풀린 무례함으로 나에게 쏘아붙이려는 것이 뻔히 그 아이 혀끝에 걸려 있었다."], ["She checked herself—laid her head back on the pillow—considered a minute—and then answered in these remarkable words:", "그 아이는 스스로를 억눌렀다—제 머리를 베개에 도로 뉘고—잠시 헤아리고는—그런 다음 이 놀라운 말로 대답했다."]], [["“I shall never marry Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.”", "\"저는 결코 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와 혼인하지 않겠어요.\""]], [["It was my turn to start at that.", "이번에는 내가 흠칫할 차례였다."]], [["“What can you possibly mean?”", "\"대체 무슨 뜻이니?\""], ["I exclaimed.", "하고 나는 외쳤다."], ["“The marriage is considered by the whole family as a settled thing!”", "\"그 혼인은 온 집안이 정해진 일로 여기는데!\""]], [["“Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite is expected here today,”", "\"고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 오늘 여기 오기로 되어 있어요.\""], ["she said doggedly.", "하고 그 아이가 고집스레 말했다."], ["“Wait till he comes—and you will see.”", "\"그가 올 때까지 기다려요—그럼 알게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“But my dear Rachel——”", "\"하지만 얘 레이철——\""]], [["She rang the bell at the head of her bed.", "그 아이는 제 침대 머리맡의 초인종을 울렸다."], ["The person with the cap-ribbons appeared.", "그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 나타났다."]], [["“Penelope!", "\"페넬로피!"], ["my bath.”", "내 목욕물.\""]], [["Let me give her her due.", "그 아이에게 마땅한 값을 쳐주자."], ["In the state of my feelings at that moment, I do sincerely believe that she had hit on the only possible way of forcing me to leave the room.", "그 순간의 내 심정의 상태로 보아, 나는 그 아이가 나로 하여금 그 방을 떠나게 만들 유일하게 가능한 방도를 찾아냈다고 진심으로 믿는다."]], [["By the mere worldly mind my position towards Rachel might have been viewed as presenting difficulties of no ordinary kind.", "한낱 세속적 마음으로 보자면 레이철에 대한 나의 처지는 예사롭지 않은 종류의 어려움을 드러내는 것으로 보였을지 모른다."], ["I had reckoned on leading her to higher things by means of a little earnest exhortation on the subject of her marriage.", "나는 그 아이의 혼인이라는 화제에 대한 조그마한 간곡한 훈계로 그 아이를 더 높은 것들로 이끌 셈이었다."], ["And now, if she was to be believed, no such event as her marriage was to take place at all.", "그런데 이제, 그 아이의 말을 믿는다면, 그 아이의 혼인 같은 사건은 도무지 벌어지지 않을 것이었다."], ["But ah, my friends!", "허나 아, 나의 벗들이여!"], ["a working Christian of my experience (with an evangelising prospect before her) takes broader views than these.", "나 같은 경험 있는 일하는 기독교인은 (전도의 앞날을 앞에 두고) 이런 것보다 더 넓은 견해를 취한다."], ["Supposing Rachel really broke off the marriage, on which the Ablewhites, father and son, counted as a settled thing, what would be the result?", "만일 레이철이, 에이블화이트 부자가 정해진 일로 셈하던 그 혼인을 정말로 깨뜨린다고 하면, 그 결과는 어떻게 될까?"], ["It could only end, if she held firm, in an exchanging of hard words and bitter accusations on both sides.", "그 아이가 굳게 버틴다면, 그것은 양편에서 모진 말과 쓰라린 비난을 주고받는 것으로밖에 끝날 수 없었다."], ["And what would be the effect on Rachel when the stormy interview was over?", "그리고 그 폭풍 같은 면담이 끝났을 때 레이철에게 미칠 영향은 어떤 것일까?"], ["A salutary moral depression would be the effect.", "이로운 도덕적 침체가 그 영향일 터였다."], ["Her pride would be exhausted, her stubbornness would be exhausted, by the resolute resistance which it was in her character to make under the circumstances.", "그 아이의 자존심은 지쳐 버릴 것이고, 그 아이의 고집은, 그런 정황에서 그 아이의 됨됨이가 부릴 그 완강한 저항으로 인해, 지쳐 버릴 것이었다."], ["She would turn for sympathy to the nearest person who had sympathy to offer.", "그 아이는 동정을 베풀 가장 가까운 사람에게 동정을 얻으려 돌아설 것이었다."], ["And I was that nearest person—brimful of comfort, charged to overflowing with seasonable and reviving words.", "그리고 내가 그 가장 가까운 사람이었다—위안으로 가득 차고, 때맞고 되살리는 말로 넘치도록 채워진."], ["Never had the evangelising prospect looked brighter, to my eyes, than it looked now.", "전도의 앞날이 나의 눈에, 지금 보이는 것만큼 밝아 보인 적은 없었다."]], [["She came down to breakfast, but she ate nothing, and hardly uttered a word.", "그 아이는 아침 식사에 내려왔으나, 아무것도 먹지 않았고, 한마디도 거의 하지 않았다."]], [["After breakfast she wandered listlessly from room to room—then suddenly roused herself, and opened the piano.", "아침 식사 뒤 그 아이는 맥없이 이 방 저 방을 어정거렸다—그러다 별안간 스스로를 추스르고, 피아노를 열었다."], ["The music she selected to play was of the most scandalously profane sort, associated with performances on the stage which it curdles one’s blood to think of.", "그 아이가 골라 친 그 곡은 더없이 추문거리로 불경한 종류의 것으로, 생각만 해도 피가 얼어붙는 무대 공연과 엮인 것이었다."], ["It would have been premature to interfere with her at such a time as this.", "이런 때에 그 아이에게 끼어드는 것은 너무 성급한 노릇이었으리라."], ["I privately ascertained the hour at which Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was expected, and then I escaped the music by leaving the house.", "나는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 오기로 되어 있는 시각을 남몰래 확인하고는, 집을 떠남으로써 그 음악에서 벗어났다."]], [["Being out alone, I took the opportunity of calling upon my two resident friends.", "혼자 나온 김에, 나는 나의 그곳에 사는 두 벗을 찾아가는 기회를 잡았다."], ["It was an indescribable luxury to find myself indulging in earnest conversation with serious persons.", "진지한 사람들과 간곡한 대화에 빠져 있는 나 자신을 발견하는 것은 이루 말할 수 없는 호사였다."], ["Infinitely encouraged and refreshed, I turned my steps back again to the house, in excellent time to await the arrival of our expected visitor.", "한없이 북돋아지고 상쾌해져서, 나는, 우리가 기다리는 방문객의 도착을 기다리기에 아주 알맞은 때에, 집으로 발길을 돌렸다."], ["I entered the dining-room, always empty at that hour of the day, and found myself face to face with Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite!", "나는, 하루 중 그 시각에는 언제나 비어 있는 식당에 들어갔고, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨와 얼굴을 마주하고 있는 나 자신을 발견했다!"]], [["He made no attempt to fly the place. Quite the contrary.", "그는 그 자리를 피하려는 시도를 조금도 하지 않았다."], ["He advanced to meet me with the utmost eagerness.", "정반대였다. 그는 더없는 열의로 나를 맞으러 나아왔다."]], [["“Dear Miss Clack, I have been only waiting to see you!", "\"사랑하는 클랙 양, 저는 다만 당신을 보려고 기다리고 있었어요!"], ["Chance set me free of my London engagements today sooner than I had expected, and I have got here, in consequence, earlier than my appointed time.”", "뜻밖의 우연이 오늘 저를 런던 약속에서 예상보다 일찍 풀어 주었고, 그 결과 저는 정해진 시각보다 일찍 여기 다다랐답니다.\""]], [["Not the slightest embarrassment encumbered his explanation, though this was his first meeting with me after the scene in Montagu Square.", "몬터규 스퀘어의 그 장면 뒤 이것이 그와 나의 첫 만남이었건만, 조금의 곤혹스러움도 그의 해명을 짓누르지 않았다."], ["He was not aware, it is true, of my having been a witness of that scene.", "그가 그 장면의 증인이 나였음을 몰랐던 것은 사실이다."], ["But he knew, on the other hand, that my attendances at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes, and my relations with friends attached to other charities, must have informed me of his shameless neglect of his Ladies and of his Poor.", "허나 그는, 다른 한편으로, 내가 '어머니회 아동복'에 나가는 것과, 다른 자선회에 딸린 벗들과의 관계로 미루어, 그가 제 부인들과 제 가난한 이들을 파렴치하게 등한시한 것을 내가 알았음이 틀림없다는 것은 알고 있었다."], ["And yet there he was before me, in full possession of his charming voice and his irresistible smile!", "그런데도 거기 그가 내 앞에 있었다, 그의 매혹적인 목소리와 그의 거스를 수 없는 미소를 온전히 지닌 채로!"]], [["“Have you seen Rachel yet?”", "\"레이철은 벌써 봤나요?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["He sighed gently, and took me by the hand.", "그는 부드럽게 한숨짓고, 내 손을 잡았다."], ["I should certainly have snatched my hand away, if the manner in which he gave his answer had not paralysed me with astonishment.", "그가 제 대답을 하는 태도가 나를 놀라움으로 마비시키지 않았더라면, 나는 분명 내 손을 홱 잡아 뺐으리라."]], [["“I have seen Rachel,”", "\"레이철을 봤어요.\""], ["he said with perfect tranquillity.", "하고 그가 더없는 평온함으로 말했다."], ["“You are aware, dear friend, that she was engaged to me?", "\"당신도 아시지요, 사랑하는 벗, 그 아이가 저와 약혼했다는 것을요?"], ["Well, she has taken a sudden resolution to break the engagement.", "그런데, 그 아이가 별안간 그 약혼을 깨기로 결심했어요."], ["Reflection has convinced her that she will best consult her welfare and mine by retracting a rash promise, and leaving me free to make some happier choice elsewhere.", "곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에 그 아이는, 성급한 약속을 물리고, 제가 다른 데서 더 행복한 선택을 할 수 있도록 자유롭게 놓아주는 것이, 제 안녕과 저의 안녕을 가장 잘 살피는 길이라고 확신하게 되었지요."], ["That is the only reason she will give, and the only answer she will make to every question that I can ask of her.”", "그것이 그 아이가 댈 유일한 이유이고, 제가 그 아이에게 던질 수 있는 온갖 물음에 그 아이가 할 유일한 대답이에요.\""]], [["“What have you done on your side?”", "\"당신은 당신 편에서 무엇을 했나요?\""], ["I inquired.", "하고 나는 물었다."], ["“Have you submitted?”", "\"순순히 따랐나요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["he said with the most unruffled composure, “I have submitted.”", "하고 그가 더없이 흐트러지지 않은 평온함으로 말했다. \"저는 순순히 따랐어요.\""]], [["His conduct, under the circumstances, was so utterly inconceivable, that I stood bewildered with my hand in his.", "그 정황 아래 그의 처신이 어찌나 도무지 상상할 수 없는 것이던지, 나는 내 손을 그의 손에 맡긴 채 어리둥절해 서 있었다."], ["It is a piece of rudeness to stare at anybody, and it is an act of indelicacy to stare at a gentleman.", "누구를 빤히 쳐다보는 것은 무례한 짓이고, 신사를 빤히 쳐다보는 것은 정숙하지 못한 짓이다."], ["I committed both those improprieties.", "나는 그 두 가지 무례를 다 저질렀다."], ["And I said, as if in a dream, “What does it mean?”", "그리고 나는, 마치 꿈속에서인 듯, 말했다. \"이게 무슨 뜻인가요?\""]], [["“Permit me to tell you,”", "\"당신에게 말씀드리게 해 주세요.\""], ["he replied.", "하고 그가 대답했다."], ["“And suppose we sit down?”", "\"그리고 우리 앉을까요?\""]], [["He led me to a chair.", "그는 나를 의자로 이끌었다."], ["I have an indistinct remembrance that he was very affectionate.", "나는 그가 아주 다정했다는 어렴풋한 기억이 있다."], ["I don’t think he put his arm round my waist to support me—but I am not sure.", "나를 부축하려고 그가 내 허리에 팔을 둘렀던 것 같지는 않다—허나 확실치는 않다."], ["I was quite helpless, and his ways with ladies were very endearing.", "나는 아주 속수무책이었고, 부인들을 대하는 그의 태도는 아주 사랑스러웠다."], ["At any rate, we sat down.", "어쨌든, 우리는 앉았다."], ["I can answer for that, if I can answer for nothing more.", "다른 것은 아무것도 장담할 수 없어도, 그것만은 장담할 수 있다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# CHAPTER VIII"]], [["“I have lost a beautiful girl, an excellent social position, and a handsome income,”", "\"저는 아름다운 아가씨와, 훌륭한 사회적 지위와, 두둑한 수입을 잃었어요.\" 하고 고드프리 씨가 말문을 열었다."], ["Mr. Godfrey began; “and I have submitted to it without a struggle.", "\"그리고 저는 아무 다툼도 없이 그것에 순순히 따랐고요."], ["What can be the motive for such extraordinary conduct as that?", "그런 예사롭지 않은 처신의 동기가 무엇일 수 있겠어요?"], ["My precious friend, there is no motive.”", "나의 소중한 벗, 동기라는 게 없어요.\""]], [["“No motive?”", "\"동기가 없다고요?\""], ["I repeated.", "하고 나는 되뇌었다."]], [["“Let me appeal, my dear Miss Clack, to your experience of children,” he went on.", "\"저의 사랑하는 클랙 양, 아이들에 대한 당신의 경험에 호소하게 해 주세요.\""], ["“A child pursues a certain course of conduct.", "하고 그가 이어 갔다. \"아이가 어떤 행동을 해요."], ["You are greatly struck by it, and you attempt to get at the motive.", "당신은 그것에 크게 마음이 끌려, 그 동기를 캐내려 하고요."], ["The dear little thing is incapable of telling you its motive.", "그 사랑스러운 조그만 것은 당신에게 제 동기를 말할 능력이 없어요."], ["You might as well ask the grass why it grows, or the birds why they sing.", "풀에게 왜 자라느냐고, 새에게 왜 노래하느냐고 묻는 편이 차라리 나을 거예요."], ["Well!", "자!"], ["in this matter, I am like the dear little thing—like the grass—like the birds.", "이 일에서, 저는 그 사랑스러운 조그만 것과 같아요—풀과 같고—새와 같고요."], ["I don’t know why I made a proposal of marriage to Miss Verinder.", "저는 제가 왜 버린더 아가씨에게 청혼했는지 모르겠어요."], ["I don’t know why I have shamefully neglected my dear Ladies.", "저는 제가 왜 부끄럽게도 저의 사랑하는 부인들을 등한시했는지 모르겠어요."], ["I don’t know why I have apostatised from the Mothers’ Small-Clothes.", "저는 제가 왜 '어머니회 아동복'에서 저버렸는지 모르겠어요."], ["You say to the child, Why have you been naughty?", "당신이 아이에게 물어요, 왜 말을 안 들었니?"], ["And the little angel puts its finger into its mouth, and doesn’t know.", "그러면 그 조그만 천사는 제 손가락을 입에 넣고, 모른다고 해요."], ["My case exactly, Miss Clack!", "딱 제 경우예요, 클랙 양!"], ["I couldn’t confess it to anybody else.", "저는 다른 누구에게도 그것을 고백할 수 없었어요."], ["I feel impelled to confess it to you!”", "저는 당신에게 그것을 고백하지 않을 수 없다는 느낌이 들어요!\""]], [["I began to recover myself.", "나는 정신을 차리기 시작했다."], ["A mental problem was involved here.", "여기에는 마음의 문제 하나가 걸려 있었다."], ["I am deeply interested in mental problems—and I am not, it is thought, without some skill in solving them.", "나는 마음의 문제에 깊이 관심이 있다—그리고 나는, 그것을 푸는 데에 얼마쯤 솜씨가 없지 않다고들 여겨진다."]], [["“Best of friends, exert your intellect, and help me,” he proceeded.", "\"더없이 좋은 벗이여, 당신의 지력을 발휘해서, 저를 도와줘요.\""], ["“Tell me—why does a time come when these matrimonial proceedings of mine begin to look like something done in a dream?", "하고 그가 이어 갔다. \"말해 줘요—어째서 저의 이 혼인 절차가 꿈속에서 한 무언가처럼 보이기 시작하는 때가 오는 걸까요?"], ["Why does it suddenly occur to me that my true happiness is in helping my dear Ladies, in going my modest round of useful work, in saying my few earnest words when called on by my Chairman?", "어째서 저의 참된 행복이 저의 사랑하는 부인들을 돕는 데에, 쓸모 있는 일의 수수한 순회를 도는 데에, 저의 의장께서 부르실 때 몇 마디 간곡한 말을 하는 데에 있다는 생각이 별안간 드는 걸까요?"], ["What do I want with a position?", "제게 지위가 무슨 소용인가요?"], ["I have got a position!", "저는 지위가 있는걸요!"], ["What do I want with an income?", "제게 수입이 무슨 소용인가요?"], ["I can pay for my bread and cheese, and my nice little lodging, and my two coats a year.", "저는 제 빵과 치즈와, 제 아담한 조그만 셋방과, 한 해 두 벌의 외투 값을 치를 수 있는걸요."], ["What do I want with Miss Verinder?", "제게 버린더 아가씨가 무슨 소용인가요?"], ["She has told me with her own lips (this, dear lady, is between ourselves) that she loves another man, and that her only idea in marrying me is to try and put that other man out of her head.", "그 아이는 제 입으로 저에게 (이건, 사랑하는 부인, 우리끼리 얘긴데요) 다른 사내를 사랑하며, 저와 혼인하려는 유일한 생각은 그 다른 사내를 제 머릿속에서 몰아내 보려는 것이라고 말했어요."], ["What a horrid union is this!", "이 얼마나 끔찍한 결합인가요!"], ["Oh, dear me, what a horrid union is this!", "오, 저런, 이 얼마나 끔찍한 결합인가요!"], ["Such are my reflections, Miss Clack, on my way to Brighton.", "그것이, 클랙 양, 브라이턴으로 오는 길에 든 저의 상념이에요."], ["I approach Rachel with the feeling of a criminal who is going to receive his sentence.", "저는 판결을 받으러 가는 죄인의 심정으로 레이철에게 다가가요."], ["When I find that she has changed her mind too—when I hear her propose to break the engagement—I experience (there is no sort of doubt about it) a most overpowering sense of relief.", "그 아이도 마음을 바꿨다는 것을 알아냈을 때—그 아이가 약혼을 깨자고 제안하는 것을 들었을 때—저는 (그것에 대해서는 어떤 종류의 의심도 없이) 더없이 압도적인 안도감을 느껴요."], ["A month ago I was pressing her rapturously to my bosom.", "한 달 전에 저는 황홀하게 그 아이를 제 가슴에 끌어안고 있었어요."], ["An hour ago, the happiness of knowing that I shall never press her again, intoxicates me like strong liquor.", "한 시간 전에는, 다시는 그 아이를 끌어안지 않으리라는 것을 아는 그 행복이, 독한 술처럼 저를 취하게 해요."], ["The thing seems impossible—the thing can’t be.", "그 일은 있을 수 없어 보여요—그 일은 있을 수 없어요."], ["And yet there are the facts, as I had the honour of stating them when we first sat down together in these two chairs.", "그런데도 사실들이 저기 있어요, 우리가 이 두 의자에 처음 함께 앉았을 때 제가 삼가 아뢴 그대로요."], ["I have lost a beautiful girl, an excellent social position, and a handsome income; and I have submitted to it without a struggle.", "저는 아름다운 아가씨와, 훌륭한 사회적 지위와, 두둑한 수입을 잃었어요. 그리고 저는 아무 다툼도 없이 그것에 순순히 따랐고요."], ["Can you account for it, dear friend?", "당신은 그것을 설명할 수 있어요, 사랑하는 벗?"], ["It’s quite beyond me.”", "그건 도무지 저로서는 넘어서는 일이에요.\""]], [["His magnificent head sank on his breast, and he gave up his own mental problem in despair.", "그의 훌륭한 머리가 제 가슴으로 떨어졌고, 그는 절망 속에서 제 자신의 마음의 문제를 포기했다."]], [["I was deeply touched.", "나는 깊이 감동했다."], ["The case (if I may speak as a spiritual physician) was now quite plain to me.", "그 경우는 (내가 영적 의사로서 말해도 된다면) 이제 내게 아주 명백했다."], ["It is no uncommon event, in the experience of us all, to see the possessors of exalted ability occasionally humbled to the level of the most poorly-gifted people about them.", "뛰어난 재능을 지닌 이들이 이따금 제 둘레의 가장 재능이 빈약한 사람들의 수준으로 낮추어지는 것을 보는 것은, 우리 모두의 경험에서 드물지 않은 일이다."], ["The object, no doubt, in the wise economy of Providence, is to remind greatness that it is mortal and that the power which has conferred it can also take it away.", "그 뜻은, 의심할 것 없이, 섭리의 슬기로운 경륜 속에서, 위대함에게 그것이 죽을 것이며 그것을 부여한 그 힘이 또한 그것을 거두어 갈 수도 있음을 일깨우는 것이다."], ["It was now—to my mind—easy to discern one of these salutary humiliations in the deplorable proceedings on dear Mr. Godfrey’s part, of which I had been the unseen witness.", "이제—내 마음으로는—사랑하는 고드프리 씨 편의 그 개탄스러운 행동, 내가 그 보이지 않는 증인이었던 그 행동 속에서, 이런 이로운 낮추어짐 가운데 하나를 알아보기가 쉬웠다."], ["And it was equally easy to recognise the welcome reappearance of his own finer nature in the horror with which he recoiled from the idea of a marriage with Rachel, and in the charming eagerness which he showed to return to his Ladies and his Poor.", "그리고 그가 레이철과의 혼인이라는 생각에서 움츠러들며 물러선 그 소름 끼침 속에서, 그리고 제 부인들과 제 가난한 이들에게로 돌아가려고 그가 보인 그 매혹적인 열의 속에서, 그 자신의 더 고운 본성의 반가운 재등장을 알아보기도 꼭 그만큼 쉬웠다."]], [["I put this view before him in a few simple and sisterly words.", "나는 몇 마디 소박하고 자매다운 말로 이 견해를 그의 앞에 내놓았다."], ["His joy was beautiful to see.", "그의 기쁨은 보기에 아름다웠다."], ["He compared himself, as I went on, to a lost man emerging from the darkness into the light.", "그는, 내가 말을 이어 가는 동안, 저 자신을 어둠에서 빛으로 나오는 길 잃은 사람에 견주었다."], ["When I answered for a loving reception of him at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes, the grateful heart of our Christian Hero overflowed.", "내가 '어머니회 아동복'에서 그를 다정하게 맞아 주리라 장담하자, 우리의 기독교적 영웅의 감사에 찬 마음이 넘쳐 흘렀다."], ["He pressed my hands alternately to his lips.", "그는 내 두 손을 번갈아 제 입술에 갖다 댔다."], ["Overwhelmed by the exquisite triumph of having got him back among us, I let him do what he liked with my hands.", "그를 우리에게로 도로 데려왔다는 그 더없는 승리감에 압도되어, 나는 그가 내 손을 마음대로 하도록 놓아두었다."], ["I closed my eyes.", "나는 눈을 감았다."], ["I felt my head, in an ecstasy of spiritual self-forgetfulness, sinking on his shoulder.", "나는 나의 머리가, 영적인 자기 망각의 황홀 속에서, 그의 어깨로 떨어지는 것을 느꼈다."], ["In a moment more I should certainly have swooned away in his arms, but for an interruption from the outer world, which brought me to myself again.", "한 순간 더 지나면 나는 분명 그의 팔 안에서 까무러쳤으련만, 바깥세상에서 온 훼방이 나를 다시 정신 차리게 했다."], ["A horrid rattling of knives and forks sounded outside the door, and the footman came in to lay the table for luncheon.", "문밖에서 나이프와 포크가 끔찍하게 덜그럭거리는 소리가 났고, 그 남자 하인이 점심 상을 차리러 들어왔다."]], [["Mr. Godfrey started up, and looked at the clock on the mantelpiece.", "고드프리 씨는 벌떡 일어나, 벽난로 선반의 시계를 보았다."]], [["“How time flies with you!”", "\"당신과 함께 있으니 시간이 어찌나 잘 가는지!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "하고 그가 외쳤다."], ["“I shall barely catch the train.”", "\"저는 기차를 겨우 잡겠어요.\""]], [["I ventured on asking why he was in such a hurry to get back to town.", "나는 그가 어째서 그토록 서둘러 도회지로 돌아가려는지 감히 물어보았다."], ["His answer reminded me of family difficulties that were still to be reconciled, and of family disagreements that were yet to come.", "그의 대답은 내게, 아직 풀려야 할 집안의 어려움들과, 아직 올 집안의 불화들을 떠올리게 했다."]], [["“I have heard from my father,” he said.", "\"저는 아버지에게서 기별을 받았어요.\""], ["“Business obliges him to leave Frizinghall for London today, and he proposes coming on here, either this evening or tomorrow.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"볼일 때문에 아버지가 오늘 프리징홀을 떠나 런던으로 가시게 되었는데, 오늘 저녁이나 내일 이곳으로 오시겠다는 거예요."], ["I must tell him what has happened between Rachel and me.", "저는 레이철과 저 사이에 있었던 일을 아버지께 말씀드려야 해요."], ["His heart is set on our marriage—there will be great difficulty, I fear, in reconciling him to the breaking-off of the engagement.", "아버지는 우리 혼인에 마음을 쏟고 계세요—약혼이 깨진 것을 받아들이시게 하는 데에 큰 어려움이 있을까 봐 걱정이에요."], ["I must stop him, for all our sakes, from coming here till he is reconciled.", "저는, 우리 모두를 위해, 아버지가 받아들이시기 전에는 이곳으로 오시는 것을 막아야 해요."], ["Best and dearest of friends, we shall meet again!”", "더없이 좋고 사랑하는 벗이여, 우리 다시 만나요!\""]], [["With those words he hurried out.", "그 말과 함께 그는 서둘러 나갔다."], ["In equal haste on my side, I ran upstairs to compose myself in my own room before meeting Aunt Ablewhite and Rachel at the luncheon-table.", "내 쪽에서도 똑같이 서둘러, 나는 점심 상에서 에이블화이트 이모와 레이철을 만나기 전에 마음을 가라앉히려고 내 방으로 뛰어 올라갔다."]], [["I am well aware—to dwell for a moment yet on the subject of Mr. Godfrey—that the all-profaning opinion of the world has charged him with having his own private reasons for releasing Rachel from her engagement, at the first opportunity she gave him.", "나는 잘 안다—고드프리 씨라는 화제에 잠깐 더 머물자면—온갖 것을 더럽히는 세상의 견해가 그를, 레이철이 그에게 준 첫 기회에 그 아이를 약혼에서 풀어 준 데에 저 나름의 남모를 이유가 있었다고 몰아붙였음을."], ["It has also reached my ears, that his anxiety to recover his place in my estimation has been attributed in certain quarters, to a mercenary eagerness to make his peace (through me) with a venerable committee-woman at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes, abundantly blessed with the goods of this world, and a beloved and intimate friend of my own.", "또한 내 귀에 들어오기로는, 나의 평가에서 제 자리를 되찾으려는 그의 안달이, 어떤 부류에서는, 이 세상 재물로 넉넉히 복 받은, 그리고 나 자신의 사랑하고 가까운 벗인, '어머니회 아동복'의 어느 존경받는 위원회 부인과 (나를 통해) 화해하려는 잇속 밝은 열의 탓으로 돌려졌다는 것이다."], ["I only notice these odious slanders for the sake of declaring that they never had a moment’s influence on my mind.", "내가 이 얄미운 비방을 짚는 것은 오로지, 그것이 결코 한순간도 내 마음에 영향을 미치지 못했음을 선언하기 위해서다."], ["In obedience to my instructions, I have exhibited the fluctuations in my opinion of our Christian Hero, exactly as I find them recorded in my diary.", "나의 지시에 따라, 나는 우리의 기독교적 영웅에 대한 나의 견해의 흔들림을, 나의 일기에 기록된 그대로 꼭 그렇게 드러내 보였다."], ["In justice to myself, let me here add that, once reinstated in his place in my estimation, my gifted friend never lost that place again.", "나 자신을 위해 공정을 기하자면, 여기에 덧붙이게 해 다오, 나의 평가에서 한번 제 자리를 되찾은 뒤로, 나의 재능 있는 벗은 결코 다시 그 자리를 잃지 않았음을."], ["I write with the tears in my eyes, burning to say more.", "나는 눈에 눈물을 담고, 더 말하고 싶어 애타며 쓴다."], ["But no—I am cruelly limited to my actual experience of persons and things.", "허나 아니—나는 사람과 사물에 대한 나의 실제 경험에 모질게도 국한되어 있다."], ["In less than a month from the time of which I am now writing, events in the money-market (which diminished even my miserable little income) forced me into foreign exile, and left me with nothing but a loving remembrance of Mr. Godfrey which the slander of the world has assailed, and assailed in vain.", "내가 지금 쓰고 있는 그때로부터 채 한 달도 안 되어, 금융 시장의 사건들이 (나의 그 비참한 조그만 수입마저 줄여 놓아) 나를 외국 유배로 몰아넣었고, 세상의 비방이 덮쳤으나 헛되이 덮친 고드프리 씨에 대한 다정한 기억 말고는 아무것도 내게 남기지 않았다."]], [["Let me dry my eyes, and return to my narrative.", "나의 눈물을 닦고, 나의 이야기로 돌아가자."]], [["I went downstairs to luncheon, naturally anxious to see how Rachel was affected by her release from her marriage engagement.", "나는 점심을 들러 아래층으로 내려갔다, 자연히 레이철이 제 혼약에서 풀려난 것에 어떻게 영향받았는지 보고 싶어 마음 졸이며."]], [["It appeared to me—but I own I am a poor authority in such matters—that the recovery of her freedom had set her thinking again of that other man whom she loved, and that she was furious with herself for not being able to control a revulsion of feeling of which she was secretly ashamed.", "내게 보이기로는—허나 나는 이런 일에 서투른 권위자임을 인정한다—제 자유를 되찾은 것이 그 아이로 하여금 다시 그 사랑하던 다른 사내를 생각하게 만들었고, 그 아이가 남몰래 부끄러워하는 그 감정의 반전을 다스리지 못하는 것에 저 자신에게 몹시 화가 나 있는 듯했다."], ["Who was the man?", "그 사내는 누구였을까?"], ["I had my suspicions—but it was needless to waste time in idle speculation.", "나는 나름의 의심이 있었다—허나 부질없는 억측으로 시간을 허비할 것 없었다."], ["When I had converted her, she would, as a matter of course, have no concealments from Me.", "내가 그 아이를 개심시키면, 그 아이는, 당연히, 나에게 아무 감춤도 없을 터였다."], ["I should hear all about the man; I should hear all about the Moonstone.", "나는 그 사내에 대해 죄다 듣게 될 것이었다. 나는 월장석에 대해 죄다 듣게 될 것이었다."], ["If I had had no higher object in stirring her up to a sense of spiritual things, the motive of relieving her mind of its guilty secrets would have been enough of itself to encourage me to go on.", "그 아이를 일깨워 영적인 것들을 자각하게 하는 데에 내게 더 높은 목적이 없었더라도, 그 아이의 마음을 죄스러운 비밀에서 풀어 준다는 동기만으로도 나로 하여금 계속하도록 넉넉히 북돋았으리라."]], [["Aunt Ablewhite took her exercise in the afternoon in an invalid chair.", "에이블화이트 이모는 오후에 병자용 의자에서 운동을 했다."], ["Rachel accompanied her.", "레이철이 함께했다."], ["“I wish I could drag the chair,”", "\"제가 그 의자를 끌 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["she broke out, recklessly.", "하고 그 아이가 무모하게 터뜨렸다."], ["“I wish I could fatigue myself till I was ready to drop.”", "\"쓰러질 지경이 될 때까지 저 자신을 고단하게 만들 수 있으면 좋겠어요.\""]], [["She was in the same humour in the evening.", "그 아이는 저녁에도 똑같은 심기였다."], ["I discovered in one of my friend’s precious publications—the Life, Letters, and Labours of Miss Jane Ann Stamper, forty-fourth edition—passages which bore with a marvellous appropriateness on Rachel’s present position.", "나는 나의 벗의 소중한 간행물 가운데 하나—「제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 생애, 편지, 그리고 노고」 사십사 판—에서 레이철의 지금 처지에 놀랍도록 꼭 들어맞는 대목들을 찾아냈다."], ["Upon my proposing to read them, she went to the piano.", "내가 그것들을 읽어 주자고 제안하자, 그 아이는 피아노로 갔다."], ["Conceive how little she must have known of serious people, if she supposed that my patience was to be exhausted in that way!", "나의 참을성이 그런 식으로 지쳐 버리리라 여겼다면, 그 아이가 진지한 사람들을 얼마나 몰랐음이 틀림없을지 헤아려 보시라!"], ["I kept Miss Jane Ann Stamper by me, and waited for events with the most unfaltering trust in the future.", "나는 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양을 내 곁에 두고, 앞날에 대한 더없이 흔들림 없는 믿음으로 사건들을 기다렸다."]], [["Old Mr. Ablewhite never made his appearance that night.", "노 에이블화이트 씨는 그날 밤 끝내 나타나지 않았다."], ["But I knew the importance which his worldly greed attached to his son’s marriage with Miss Verinder—and I felt a positive conviction (do what Mr. Godfrey might to prevent it) that we should see him the next day.", "허나 나는 그의 세속적 탐욕이 제 아들과 버린더 아가씨의 혼인에 두는 그 중대함을 알고 있었다—그리고 나는 (고드프리 씨가 그것을 막으려 무엇을 하든) 우리가 이튿날 그를 보게 되리라는 확실한 확신이 들었다."], ["With his interference in the matter, the storm on which I had counted would certainly come, and the salutary exhaustion of Rachel’s resisting powers would as certainly follow.", "그가 그 일에 끼어들면, 내가 셈했던 그 폭풍이 분명 올 것이고, 레이철의 저항력의 이로운 소진이 꼭 그만큼 분명 뒤따를 터였다."], ["I am not ignorant that old Mr. Ablewhite has the reputation generally (especially among his inferiors) of being a remarkably good-natured man.", "나는 노 에이블화이트 씨가 (특히 제 아랫사람들 사이에서) 놀랍도록 사람 좋은 사내라는 평판을 지녔다는 것을 모르지 않는다."], ["According to my observation of him, he deserves his reputation as long as he has his own way, and not a moment longer.", "그에 대한 나의 관찰로 보아, 그는 제 뜻대로 되는 동안에는 제 평판에 값하고, 그 이상은 한순간도 아니다."]], [["The next day, exactly as I had foreseen, Aunt Ablewhite was as near to being astonished as her nature would permit, by the sudden appearance of her husband.", "이튿날, 딱 내가 내다본 대로, 에이블화이트 이모는 제 남편의 별안간 나타남에, 그녀의 성정이 허락하는 한 놀라움에 가까운 상태가 되었다."], ["He had barely been a minute in the house, before he was followed, to my astonishment this time, by an unexpected complication in the shape of Mr. Bruff.", "그가 그 집에 든 지 겨우 일 분 만에, 이번에는 나의 놀라움에도, 브러프 씨라는 모양을 한 뜻밖의 복잡함이 그를 뒤따랐다."]], [["I never remember feeling the presence of the lawyer to be more unwelcome than I felt it at that moment.", "나는 그 순간 느낀 것만큼 그 변호사의 존재를 달갑잖게 느낀 적이 여태껏 있었는지 기억나지 않는다."], ["He looked ready for anything in the way of an obstructive proceeding—capable even of keeping the peace with Rachel for one of the combatants!", "그는 방해하는 행동이라면 무엇이든 할 채비가 된 듯 보였다—싸움꾼 가운데 하나를 위해 레이철과 평화를 지키는 것마저 능히 할 만하게!"]], [["“This is a pleasant surprise, sir,” said Mr.", "\"이거 반가운 뜻밖의 일이군요, 선생.\""], ["Ablewhite, addressing himself with his deceptive cordiality to Mr. Bruff.", "하고 에이블화이트 씨가, 제 속임수 같은 살가움으로 브러프 씨에게 말했다."], ["“When I left your office yesterday, I didn’t expect to have the honour of seeing you at Brighton today.”", "\"어제 당신 사무소를 떠날 때, 저는 오늘 브라이턴에서 당신을 뵙는 영광을 누리리라곤 예상 못 했어요.\""]], [["“I turned over our conversation in my mind, after you had gone,”", "\"당신이 가신 뒤, 저는 우리 대화를 마음속에서 되짚어 보았습니다.\""], ["replied Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 대답했다."], ["“And it occurred to me that I might perhaps be of some use on this occasion.", "\"그리고 제가 이번 일에 어쩌면 얼마쯤 쓸모가 있을지 모른다는 생각이 들었지요."], ["I was just in time to catch the train, and I had no opportunity of discovering the carriage in which you were travelling.”", "저는 기차를 겨우 맞춰 잡았고, 당신이 타고 계신 객차를 찾아낼 기회가 없었습니다.\""]], [["Having given that explanation, he seated himself by Rachel.", "그 해명을 하고 나서, 그는 레이철 곁에 앉았다."], ["I retired modestly to a corner—with Miss Jane Ann Stamper on my lap, in case of emergency.", "나는—만약을 대비하여 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양을 무릎에 얹고—겸손히 한구석으로 물러났다."], ["My aunt sat at the window; placidly fanning herself as usual.", "이모는 창가에 앉아, 여느 때처럼 태평하게 부채질했다."], ["Mr. Ablewhite stood up in the middle of the room, with his bald head much pinker than I had ever seen it yet, and addressed himself in the most affectionate manner to his niece.", "에이블화이트 씨는 방 한가운데 서서, 그의 벗어진 머리를 내가 여태껏 본 것보다 훨씬 더 분홍빛으로 물들인 채, 더없이 다정한 태도로 제 조카딸에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Rachel, my dear,”", "\"레이철, 얘야,\""], ["he said, “I have heard some very extraordinary news from Godfrey.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 고드프리에게서 아주 예사롭지 않은 소식을 들었다."], ["And I am here to inquire about it.", "그래서 그것에 대해 물어보러 여기 왔지."], ["You have a sitting-room of your own in this house.", "너는 이 집에 네 전용 거실이 있잖니."], ["Will you honour me by showing me the way to it?”", "나를 그리로 안내하는 영광을 베풀어 주겠니?\""]], [["Rachel never moved.", "레이철은 도무지 움직이지 않았다."], ["Whether she was determined to bring matters to a crisis, or whether she was prompted by some private sign from Mr. Bruff, is more than I can tell.", "그 아이가 일을 위기로 몰아갈 작정이었는지, 아니면 브러프 씨에게서 온 어떤 남모를 신호에 부추겨졌는지는 내가 알 수 있는 바가 아니다."], ["She declined doing old Mr. Ablewhite the honour of conducting him into her sitting-room.", "그 아이는 노 에이블화이트 씨를 제 거실로 안내하는 영광을 베풀기를 마다했다."]], [["“Whatever you wish to say to me,”", "\"저에게 하실 말씀이 무엇이든,\""], ["she answered, “can be said here—in the presence of my relatives, and in the presence”", "하고 그 아이가 대답했다. \"여기서 하실 수 있어요—제 친척들 앞에서, 그리고\""], ["(she looked at Mr. Bruff) “of my mother’s trusted old friend.”", "(그 아이는 브러프 씨를 바라보았다) \"제 어머니가 믿던 오랜 벗 앞에서요.\""]], [["“Just as you please, my dear,”", "\"네 좋을 대로 하렴, 얘야.\""], ["said the amiable Mr. Ablewhite.", "하고 상냥한 에이블화이트 씨가 말했다."], ["He took a chair.", "그는 의자를 잡았다."], ["The rest of them looked at his face—as if they expected it, after seventy years of worldly training, to speak the truth.", "나머지 사람들은 그의 얼굴을 바라보았다—마치 칠십 년의 세속적 훈련 뒤에, 그것이 진실을 말하기를 기대하기라도 하듯이."], ["I looked at the top of his bald head; having noticed on other occasions that the temper which was really in him had a habit of registering itself there.", "나는 그의 벗어진 머리 꼭대기를 바라보았다. 다른 경우에, 정말로 그의 안에 있는 성미가 바로 거기에 저를 기록하는 버릇이 있음을 알아챈 터라서."]], [["“Some weeks ago,”", "\"몇 주 전에,\""], ["pursued the old gentleman, “my son informed me that Miss Verinder had done him the honour to engage herself to marry him.", "하고 그 늙은 신사가 이어 갔다. \"내 아들이 나에게, 버린더 아가씨가 저와 혼인하기로 약혼하는 영광을 베풀었다고 알렸다."], ["Is it possible, Rachel, that he can have misinterpreted—or presumed upon—what you really said to him?”", "있을 수 있는 일이냐, 레이철, 그 아이가 네가 정말로 저에게 한 말을 잘못 해석했거나—넘겨짚었을 수가?\""]], [["“Certainly not,”", "\"결코 아니에요.\""], ["she replied.", "하고 그 아이가 대답했다."], ["“I did engage myself to marry him.”", "\"저는 정말로 그와 혼인하기로 약혼했어요.\""]], [["“Very frankly answered!”", "\"아주 솔직한 대답이구나!\""], ["said Mr. Ablewhite.", "하고 에이블화이트 씨가 말했다."], ["“And most satisfactory, my dear, so far.", "\"그리고 더없이 흡족하다, 얘야, 거기까지는."], ["In respect to what happened some weeks since, Godfrey has made no mistake.", "몇 주 전에 있었던 일에 관해서는, 고드프리가 아무 잘못을 하지 않았어."], ["The error is evidently in what he told me yesterday.", "그 착오는 뻔히 그 애가 어제 나에게 말한 데에 있구나."], ["I begin to see it now.", "나는 이제 그것이 보이기 시작한다."], ["You and he have had a lovers’ quarrel—and my foolish son has interpreted it seriously.", "너와 그 애가 연인끼리의 다툼을 한 거야—그리고 나의 어리석은 아들이 그것을 진지하게 해석한 거고."], ["Ah!", "아!"], ["I should have known better than that at his age.”", "나라면 그 애 나이에 그보다는 잘 알았을 텐데.\""]], [["The fallen nature in Rachel—the mother Eve, so to speak—began to chafe at this.", "레이철 안의 타락한 본성이—이를테면, 이브 어머니가—이것에 발끈하기 시작했다."]], [["“Pray let us understand each other, Mr.", "\"부디 우리 서로를 분명히 이해해요, 에이블화이트 씨.\""], ["Ablewhite,” she said.", "하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["“Nothing in the least like a quarrel took place yesterday between your son and me.", "\"어제 당신 아들과 저 사이에 다툼 비슷한 것은 조금도 없었어요."], ["If he told you that I proposed breaking off our marriage engagement, and that he agreed on his side—he told you the truth.”", "만일 그가 당신에게, 제가 우리 혼약을 깨자고 제안했고, 그가 제 편에서 동의했다고 말했다면—그는 당신에게 진실을 말한 거예요.\""]], [["The self-registering thermometer at the top of Mr. Ablewhite’s bald head began to indicate a rise of temper.", "에이블화이트 씨의 벗어진 머리 꼭대기의 그 스스로 기록하는 온도계가 성미의 상승을 나타내기 시작했다."], ["His face was more amiable than ever—but there was the pink at the top of his face, a shade deeper already!", "그의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 다정했으나—바로 거기에, 그의 얼굴 꼭대기의 그 분홍빛이, 벌써 한 단계 더 짙어져 있었다!"]], [["“Come, come, my dear!”", "\"자, 자, 얘야!\""], ["he said, in his most soothing manner, “now don’t be angry, and don’t be hard on poor Godfrey!", "하고 그가 더없이 달래는 태도로 말했다. \"자, 화내지 말고, 가엾은 고드프리에게 모질게 굴지 마라!"], ["He has evidently said some unfortunate thing.", "그 애가 뻔히 무슨 불운한 말을 한 게로구나."], ["He was always clumsy from a child—but he means well, Rachel, he means well!”", "그 애는 어려서부터 늘 서툴렀지—하지만 뜻은 좋단다, 레이철, 뜻은 좋아!\""]], [["“Mr. Ablewhite, I have either expressed myself very badly, or you are purposely mistaking me.", "\"에이블화이트 씨, 제가 제 뜻을 아주 서투르게 표현했거나, 아니면 당신이 일부러 저를 오해하시는 거예요."], ["Once for all, it is a settled thing between your son and myself that we remain, for the rest of our lives, cousins and nothing more.", "단번에 말하지만, 우리가 남은 평생, 사촌으로, 그 이상은 아닌 채로 남는다는 것은 당신 아들과 저 사이에 정해진 일이에요."], ["Is that plain enough?”", "그건 충분히 분명한가요?\""]], [["The tone in which she said those words made it impossible, even for old Mr. Ablewhite, to mistake her any longer.", "그 아이가 그 말을 한 그 어조가, 노 에이블화이트 씨조차도, 더는 그 아이를 오해하는 것을 있을 수 없게 만들었다."], ["His thermometer went up another degree, and his voice when he next spoke, ceased to be the voice which is appropriate to a notoriously good-natured man.", "그의 온도계가 한 눈금 더 올라갔고, 그가 다음에 말할 때의 그 목소리는, 소문난 사람 좋은 사내에게 어울리는 그 목소리이기를 그쳤다."]], [["“I am to understand, then,”", "\"그럼, 나는 이렇게 이해해야겠구나,\""], ["he said, “that your marriage engagement is broken off?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"네 혼약이 깨졌다고?\""]], [["“You are to understand that, Mr. Ablewhite, if you please.”", "\"그렇게 이해하시면 돼요, 에이블화이트 씨, 괜찮으시다면요.\""]], [["“I am also to take it as a matter of fact that the proposal to withdraw from the engagement came, in the first instance, from you?”", "\"나는 또한, 약혼에서 물러나자는 제안이, 처음에, 너에게서 왔다는 것도 사실로 받아들여야 하고?\""]], [["“It came, in the first instance, from me.", "\"그것은, 처음에, 저에게서 왔어요."], ["And it met, as I have told you, with your son’s consent and approval.”", "그리고 그것은, 제가 말씀드렸듯이, 당신 아들의 동의와 승인을 받았고요.\""]], [["The thermometer went up to the top of the register.", "그 온도계가 눈금 꼭대기까지 올라갔다."], ["I mean, the pink changed suddenly to scarlet.", "다시 말해, 그 분홍빛이 별안간 진홍빛으로 바뀌었다."]], [["“My son is a mean-spirited hound!”", "\"내 아들은 비열한 개다!\""], ["cried this furious old worldling.", "하고 이 격분한 늙은 속물이 외쳤다."], ["“In justice to myself as his father—not in justice to him—I beg to ask you, Miss Verinder, what complaint you have to make of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite?”", "\"그 애 아비로서의 나 자신을 위한 공정으로—그 애를 위한 공정이 아니라—나는 너에게 묻는 바다, 버린더 아가씨, 고드프리 에이블화이트에게 무슨 불만이 있느냐?\""]], [["Here Mr. Bruff interfered for the first time.", "여기서 브러프 씨가 처음으로 끼어들었다."]], [["“You are not bound to answer that question,”", "\"당신은 그 물음에 답할 의무가 없습니다.\""], ["he said to Rachel.", "하고 그가 레이철에게 말했다."]], [["Old Mr. Ablewhite fastened on him instantly.", "노 에이블화이트 씨가 대뜸 그에게 달려들었다."]], [["“Don’t forget, sir,”", "\"잊지 마시오, 선생.\""], ["he said, “that you are a self-invited guest here.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은 여기 스스로 자청한 손님이오."], ["Your interference would have come with a better grace if you had waited until it was asked for.”", "당신의 끼어듦은 그것이 청해질 때까지 기다렸더라면 더 볼품 있게 왔을 거요.\""]], [["Mr. Bruff took no notice.", "브러프 씨는 아랑곳하지 않았다."], ["The smooth varnish on his wicked old face never cracked.", "그의 그 사악한 늙은 얼굴의 매끄러운 겉치레는 도무지 금이 가지 않았다."], ["Rachel thanked him for the advice he had given to her, and then turned to old Mr. Ablewhite—preserving her composure in a manner which (having regard to her age and her sex) was simply awful to see.", "레이철은 그가 자기에게 준 조언에 감사하고는, 노 에이블화이트 씨에게로 돌아섰다—(그 아이의 나이와 성을 헤아리면) 그저 소름 끼치는 방식으로 제 평온을 지키면서."]], [["“Your son put the same question to me which you have just asked,”", "\"당신 아들이 저에게 방금 당신이 하신 그 똑같은 물음을 던졌어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["she said. “I had only one answer for him, and I have only one answer for you.", "\"저는 그에게 줄 대답이 하나뿐이었고, 당신에게 줄 대답도 하나뿐이에요."], ["I proposed that we should release each other, because reflection had convinced me that I should best consult his welfare and mine by retracting a rash promise, and leaving him free to make his choice elsewhere.”", "저는 우리가 서로를 놓아주자고 제안했어요, 왜냐하면 곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에, 성급한 약속을 물리고, 그가 다른 데서 제 선택을 할 수 있도록 자유롭게 놓아주는 것이, 그의 안녕과 저의 안녕을 가장 잘 살피는 길이라고 확신하게 되었기 때문이에요.\""]], [["“What has my son done?”", "\"내 아들이 무엇을 했느냐?\""], ["persisted Mr. Ablewhite.", "하고 에이블화이트 씨가 우겼다."], ["“I have a right to know that.", "\"나는 그것을 알 권리가 있다."], ["What has my son done?”", "내 아들이 무엇을 했느냐?\""]], [["She persisted just as obstinately on her side.", "그 아이도 제 편에서 꼭 그만큼 고집스럽게 우겼다."]], [["“You have had the only explanation which I think it necessary to give to you, or to him,”", "\"당신에게든, 그에게든, 제가 드릴 필요가 있다고 여기는 유일한 해명을 당신은 들으셨어요.\""], ["she answered.", "하고 그 아이가 대답했다."]], [["“In plain English, it’s your sovereign will and pleasure, Miss Verinder, to jilt my son?”", "\"쉬운 말로 하면, 내 아들을 차는 것이 너의 지엄하신 뜻이자 마음이렷다, 버린더 아가씨?\""]], [["Rachel was silent for a moment.", "레이철은 한순간 잠잠했다."], ["Sitting close behind her, I heard her sigh. Mr.", "그 아이 바로 뒤에 앉아, 나는 그 아이가 한숨짓는 소리를 들었다."], ["Bruff took her hand, and gave it a little squeeze.", "브러프 씨가 그 아이의 손을 잡고, 그것을 살짝 쥐어 주었다."], ["She recovered herself, and answered Mr. Ablewhite as boldly as ever.", "그 아이는 스스로를 추스르고, 그 어느 때보다 대담하게 에이블화이트 씨에게 대답했다."]], [["“I have exposed myself to worse misconstruction than that,”", "\"저는 그보다 더 고약한 오해에 저를 내맡겨 왔어요.\" 하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["she said. “And I have borne it patiently.", "\"그리고 저는 그것을 참을성 있게 견뎌 왔고요."], ["The time has gone by, when you could mortify me by calling me a jilt.”", "당신이 저를 차는 여자라 불러 저를 굴욕스럽게 할 수 있던 때는 이미 지났어요.\""]], [["She spoke with a bitterness of tone which satisfied me that the scandal of the Moonstone had been in some way recalled to her mind.", "그 아이는 어조에 쓰라림을 담아 말했는데, 그것이 나로 하여금 월장석의 추문이 어떤 식으로 그 아이의 마음에 떠올랐음을 납득하게 했다."], ["“I have no more to say,”", "\"저는 더 할 말이 없어요.\""], ["she added, wearily, not addressing the words to anyone in particular, and looking away from us all, out of the window that was nearest to her.", "하고 그 아이가, 딱히 누구에게랄 것 없이, 그리고 우리 모두에게서 눈길을 돌려, 저에게 가장 가까운 창밖을 바라보며, 지친 듯 덧붙였다."]], [["Mr. Ablewhite got upon his feet, and pushed away his chair so violently that it toppled over and fell on the floor.", "에이블화이트 씨는 일어서서, 제 의자를 어찌나 사납게 밀쳐 냈던지 그것이 넘어가 바닥에 쓰러졌다."]], [["“I have something more to say on my side,”", "\"내 편에서 더 할 말이 있다.\""], ["he announced, bringing down the flat of his hand on the table with a bang.", "하고 그가, 제 손바닥을 탁자에 쾅 내리치며 알렸다."], ["“I have to say that if my son doesn’t feel this insult, I do!”", "\"내 아들이 이 모욕을 느끼지 못한다면, 나는 느낀다고 말해야겠다!\""]], [["Rachel started, and looked at him in sudden surprise.", "레이철은 흠칫하며, 별안간 놀라 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“Insult?”", "\"모욕이라고요?\""], ["she repeated.", "하고 그 아이가 되뇌었다."], ["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이에요?\""]], [["“Insult!”", "\"모욕이다!\""], ["reiterated Mr. Ablewhite.", "하고 에이블화이트 씨가 되풀이했다."], ["“I know your motive, Miss Verinder, for breaking your promise to my son!", "\"나는 안다, 버린더 아가씨, 네가 내 아들에게 한 약속을 깨는 그 동기를!"], ["I know it as certainly as if you had confessed it in so many words.", "나는 네가 그 몇 마디로 그것을 고백하기라도 한 것처럼 확실히 안다."], ["Your cursed family pride is insulting Godfrey, as it insulted me when I married your aunt.", "너의 그 저주받은 집안 자존심이 고드프리를 모욕하고 있다, 내가 네 이모와 혼인했을 때 나를 모욕했던 것처럼!"], ["Her family—her beggarly family—turned their backs on her for marrying an honest man, who had made his own place and won his own fortune.", "그녀의 집안이—그녀의 거지 같은 집안이—제 자리를 스스로 만들고 제 재산을 스스로 얻은 정직한 사내와 혼인했다는 이유로 그녀에게 등을 돌렸지."], ["I had no ancestors.", "나는 조상이 없었다."], ["I wasn’t descended from a set of cut-throat scoundrels who lived by robbery and murder.", "나는 강도와 살인으로 먹고산 목 베는 악당 무리의 후손이 아니었어."], ["I couldn’t point to the time when the Ablewhites hadn’t a shirt to their backs, and couldn’t sign their own names.", "나는 에이블화이트 집안이 등에 걸칠 셔츠 한 장 없고 제 이름도 못 쓰던 때를 짚어 보일 수가 없었지."], ["Ha!", "하!"], ["ha!", "하!"], ["I wasn’t good enough for the Herncastles, when I married.", "내가 혼인했을 때 나는 헌캐슬 집안에는 어울릴 만큼 훌륭하지 못했다."], ["And now it comes to the pinch, my son isn’t good enough for you.", "그런데 이제 막판에 이르니, 내 아들이 너에게는 어울릴 만큼 훌륭하지 못하구나."], ["I suspected it, all along.", "나는 그것을, 내내, 의심했다."], ["You have got the Herncastle blood in you, my young lady!", "너에게는 헌캐슬 피가 흐르고 있어, 젊은 아가씨!"], ["I suspected it all along.”", "나는 그것을 내내 의심했다.\""]], [["“A very unworthy suspicion,”", "\"아주 못난 의심이군요.\""], ["remarked Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 짚었다."], ["“I am astonished that you have the courage to acknowledge it.”", "\"당신이 그것을 인정할 배짱이 있다니 놀랍습니다.\""]], [["Before Mr. Ablewhite could find words to answer in, Rachel spoke in a tone of the most exasperating contempt.", "에이블화이트 씨가 대답할 말을 찾기도 전에, 레이철이 더없이 부아 돋우는 경멸의 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Surely,”", "\"분명,\""], ["she said to the lawyer, “this is beneath notice.", "하고 그 아이가 그 변호사에게 말했다. \"이건 상대할 가치도 없어요."], ["If he can think in that way, let us leave him to think as he pleases.”", "저이가 그런 식으로 생각할 수 있다면, 저이가 좋을 대로 생각하도록 내버려 둬요.\""]], [["From scarlet, Mr. Ablewhite was now becoming purple.", "진홍빛에서, 에이블화이트 씨는 이제 자줏빛이 되어 가고 있었다."], ["He gasped for breath; he looked backwards and forwards from Rachel to Mr.", "그는 숨을 헐떡였다."], ["Bruff in such a frenzy of rage with both of them that he didn’t know which to attack first.", "그는 레이철에서 브러프 씨로, 앞뒤로, 그 둘 다에게 어찌나 격분해 미쳐 날뛰던지 누구를 먼저 공격해야 할지 몰랐다."], ["His wife, who had sat impenetrably fanning herself up to this time, began to be alarmed, and attempted, quite uselessly, to quiet him.", "그의 아내는, 이때까지 뚫을 수 없이 부채질하며 앉아 있다가, 놀라기 시작해, 아주 부질없게도, 그를 진정시키려 했다."], ["I had, throughout this distressing interview, felt more than one inward call to interfere with a few earnest words, and had controlled myself under a dread of the possible results, very unworthy of a Christian Englishwoman who looks, not to what is meanly prudent, but to what is morally right.", "나는, 이 괴로운 면담 내내, 몇 마디 간곡한 말로 끼어들라는 안으로부터의 부름을 한 번 이상 느꼈고, 있을 수 있는 결과에 대한 두려움 아래 나 자신을 억눌러 왔는데, 그것은 비열하게 신중한 것이 아니라 도덕적으로 옳은 것을 바라보는 기독교인 영국 여자에게는 아주 못난 노릇이었다."], ["At the point at which matters had now arrived, I rose superior to all considerations of mere expediency.", "이제 일이 이른 그 지점에서, 나는 한낱 편의의 온갖 헤아림 위로 높이 일어섰다."], ["If I had contemplated interposing any remonstrance of my own humble devising, I might possibly have still hesitated.", "만일 내가 나의 미천한 궁리로 무슨 훈계를 끼워 넣으려 생각했다면, 나는 어쩌면 여전히 망설였을지 모른다."], ["But the distressing domestic emergency which now confronted me, was most marvellously and beautifully provided for in the Correspondence of Miss Jane Ann Stamper—Letter one thousand and one, on “Peace in Families.”", "허나 이제 나를 마주한 그 괴로운 집안의 위급함은, 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 서신집—'가정의 평화'에 관한 천한 번째 편지—속에 더없이 놀랍고 아름답게 마련되어 있었다."], ["I rose in my modest corner, and I opened my precious book.", "나는 나의 수수한 한구석에서 일어섰고, 나의 소중한 책을 펼쳤다."]], [["“Dear Mr. Ablewhite,”", "\"사랑하는 에이블화이트 씨,\""], ["I said, “one word!”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"한마디만요!\""]], [["When I first attracted the attention of the company by rising, I could see that he was on the point of saying something rude to me.", "내가 일어섬으로써 처음 그 자리에 있는 이들의 주의를 끌었을 때, 나는 그가 나에게 무슨 무례한 말을 하려는 참임을 알 수 있었다."], ["My sisterly form of address checked him.", "나의 자매다운 부름의 형식이 그를 멈춰 세웠다."], ["He stared at me in heathen astonishment.", "그는 이교도 같은 놀라움으로 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다."]], [["“As an affectionate well-wisher and friend,”", "\"다정하게 잘되기를 비는 이이자 벗으로서,\""], ["I proceeded, “and as one long accustomed to arouse, convince, prepare, enlighten, and fortify others, permit me to take the most pardonable of all liberties—the liberty of composing your mind.”", "하고 나는 이어 갔다. \"그리고 오래도록 타인들을 일깨우고, 납득시키고, 준비시키고, 깨우치고, 굳세게 하는 데 익은 이로서, 온갖 무례 가운데 가장 용서받을 만한 무례를—당신의 마음을 가라앉히는 무례를 저지르게 해 주세요.\""]], [["He began to recover himself; he was on the point of breaking out—he would have broken out, with anybody else.", "그는 정신을 차리기 시작했다. 그는 터뜨릴 참이었다—다른 누구에게라면 그는 터뜨렸으리라."], ["But my voice (habitually gentle) possesses a high note or so, in emergencies.", "허나 나의 목소리는 (습관적으로 부드럽지만) 위급할 때는, 얼마쯤 높은 음을 지니고 있다."], ["In this emergency, I felt imperatively called upon to have the highest voice of the two.", "이 위급함에서, 나는 그 둘 가운데 더 높은 목소리를 내라는 절박한 부름을 받은 느낌이었다."]], [["I held up my precious book before him; I rapped the open page impressively with my forefinger.", "나는 나의 소중한 책을 그의 앞에 들어 올렸다. 나는 펼쳐진 쪽을 나의 집게손가락으로 인상 깊게 톡톡 두드렸다."], ["“Not my words!”", "\"나의 말이 아니에요!\""], ["I exclaimed, in a burst of fervent interruption.", "하고 나는 뜨거운 훼방의 폭발로 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, don’t suppose that I claim attention for My humble words!", "\"오, 이 미천한 나의 말에 주의를 청한다고 여기지 마세요!"], ["Manna in the wilderness, Mr. Ablewhite!", "광야의 만나예요, 에이블화이트 씨!"], ["Dew on the parched earth!", "메마른 땅 위의 이슬이에요!"], ["Words of comfort, words of wisdom, words of love—the blessed, blessed, blessed words of Miss Jane Ann Stamper!”", "위안의 말, 지혜의 말, 사랑의 말—제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 복되고, 복되고, 복된 말이에요!\""]], [["I was stopped there by a momentary impediment of the breath.", "나는 거기서 한순간 숨이 막혀 멈추었다."], ["Before I could recover myself, this monster in human form shouted out furiously,", "내가 정신을 차리기도 전에, 인간의 모습을 한 이 괴물이 격분해 소리쳤다."]], [["“Miss Jane Ann Stamper be ——!”", "\"제인 앤 스탬퍼 양은 ——이나 먹으라지!\""]], [["It is impossible for me to write the awful word, which is here represented by a blank.", "여기서 빈칸으로 나타낸 그 끔찍한 말을 나로서는 쓸 수가 없다. 나는 그것이 그의 입술을 지날 때 비명을 질렀다."], ["I shrieked as it passed his lips; I flew to my little bag on the side table; I shook out all my tracts; I seized the one particular tract on profane swearing, entitled, “Hush, for Heaven’s Sake!”; I handed it to him with an expression of agonised entreaty.", "나는 곁상 위의 내 조그만 가방으로 날아갔다. 나는 내 소책자를 죄다 털어냈다."], ["He tore it in two, and threw it back at me across the table.", "나는 불경한 욕설에 관한 그 한 특정한 소책자, '제발, 조용히 하세요!'라는 제목의 그것을 붙잡았다."], ["The rest of them rose in alarm, not knowing what might happen next.", "나는 그것을 애타는 애원의 낯빛으로 그에게 건넸다. 그는 그것을 둘로 찢어, 탁자 너머로 내게 도로 던졌다."], ["I instantly sat down again in my corner.", "나머지 사람들이 다음에 무슨 일이 일어날지 몰라, 놀라 일어섰다."], ["There had once been an occasion, under somewhat similar circumstances, when Miss Jane Ann Stamper had been taken by the two shoulders and turned out of a room.", "나는 대뜸 다시 내 구석에 앉았다. 예전에, 얼마쯤 비슷한 정황에서, 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양이 두 어깨를 잡힌 채 어느 방에서 쫓겨난 일이 한 번 있었다."], ["I waited, inspired by her spirit, for a repetition of her martyrdom.", "나는, 그녀의 정신에 힘입어, 그녀의 순교의 되풀이를 기다렸다."]], [["But no—it was not to be.", "허나 아니—그렇게 되지는 않았다."], ["His wife was the next person whom he addressed.", "그가 다음으로 말을 건넨 것은 제 아내였다."], ["“Who—who—who,”", "\"누가—누가—누가,\""], ["he said, stammering with rage, “who asked this impudent fanatic into the house?", "하고 그가 격분에 말을 더듬으며 말했다. \"누가 이 뻔뻔한 광신자를 집에 들였소?"], ["Did you?”", "당신이오?\""]], [["Before Aunt Ablewhite could say a word, Rachel answered for her.", "에이블화이트 이모가 한마디 하기도 전에, 레이철이 그녀를 대신해 대답했다."]], [["“Miss Clack is here,”", "\"클랙 양은 여기,\""], ["she said, “as my guest.”", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"제 손님으로 있어요.\""]], [["Those words had a singular effect on Mr.", "그 말이 에이블화이트 씨에게 유별난 효과를 냈다."], ["Ablewhite. They suddenly changed him from a man in a state of red-hot anger to a man in a state of icy-cold contempt.", "그것은 별안간 그를, 벌겋게 달아오른 분노의 상태에 있던 사내에서, 얼음처럼 싸늘한 경멸의 상태에 있는 사내로 바꾸어 놓았다."], ["It was plain to everybody that Rachel had said something—short and plain as her answer had been—which gave him the upper hand of her at last.", "레이철이 무언가—그 아이의 대답이 짧고 명백했음에도—마침내 그로 하여금 그 아이보다 우위에 서게 하는 무언가를 말했음이 모두에게 뻔했다."]], [["“Oh?”", "\"오?\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“Miss Clack is here as your guest—in my house?”", "\"클랙 양이 여기 너의 손님으로 있다—나의 집에?\""]], [["It was Rachel’s turn to lose her temper at that.", "이번에는 레이철이 성미를 잃을 차례였다."], ["Her colour rose, and her eyes brightened fiercely.", "그 아이의 낯빛이 달아올랐고, 그 아이의 눈이 사납게 빛났다."], ["She turned to the lawyer, and, pointing to Mr.", "그 아이는 그 변호사에게 돌아서서, 에이블화이트 씨를 가리키며, 거만하게 물었다."], ["Ablewhite, asked haughtily, “What does he mean?”", "\"저이가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말이죠?\""]], [["Mr. Bruff interfered for the third time.", "브러프 씨가 세 번째로 끼어들었다."]], [["“You appear to forget,”", "\"당신은 잊으신 듯합니다.\""], ["he said, addressing Mr.", "하고 그가 에이블화이트 씨에게 말했다."], ["Ablewhite, “that you took this house as Miss Verinder’s guardian, for Miss Verinder’s use.”", "\"당신이 이 집을 버린더 아가씨의 후견인으로서, 버린더 아가씨가 쓰도록 얻었다는 것을요.\""]], [["“Not quite so fast,”", "\"그렇게 빨리는 안 되지.\""], ["interposed Mr. Ablewhite.", "하고 에이블화이트 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["“I have a last word to say, which I should have said some time since, if this——”", "\"나에게는 마지막 할 말이 있소, 얼마 전에 했어야 할 말인데, 만일 이——\""], ["He looked my way, pondering what abominable name he should call me—“if this Rampant Spinster had not interrupted us.", "그는 나를 나더러 무슨 얄미운 이름으로 부를까 궁리하며 바라보았다—\"만일 이 설쳐대는 노처녀가 우리를 훼방하지 않았더라면 말이오."], ["I beg to inform you, sir, that, if my son is not good enough to be Miss Verinder’s husband, I cannot presume to consider his father good enough to be Miss Verinder’s guardian.", "당신께 알려 드리는 바요, 선생, 만일 내 아들이 버린더 아가씨의 남편이 될 만큼 훌륭하지 못하다면, 나는 감히 그 아비가 버린더 아가씨의 후견인이 될 만큼 훌륭하다고 여길 수가 없소."], ["Understand, if you please, that I refuse to accept the position which is offered to me by Lady Verinder’s will.", "부디, 이렇게 이해하시오, 나는 레이디 버린더의 유언장이 나에게 제시한 그 지위를 받기를 거부하겠소."], ["In your legal phrase, I decline to act.", "당신네 법률 용어로, 나는 직무를 맡기를 사양하오."], ["This house has necessarily been hired in my name.", "이 집은 마땅히 내 이름으로 얻어졌소."], ["I take the entire responsibility of it on my shoulders.", "나는 그것의 온전한 책임을 내 어깨에 지겠소."], ["It is my house.", "이건 내 집이오."], ["I can keep it, or let it, just as I please.", "나는 그것을 간직하든, 세놓든, 내 좋을 대로 할 수 있소."], ["I have no wish to hurry Miss Verinder.", "나는 버린더 아가씨를 서두르게 할 뜻이 없소."], ["On the contrary, I beg her to remove her guest and her luggage, at her own entire convenience.”", "오히려, 나는 그녀에게 청하는 바요, 그녀의 온전한 편의에 따라, 그녀의 손님과 그녀의 짐을 치워 달라고.\""], ["He made a low bow, and walked out of the room.", "그는 깊이 고개 숙여 절하고는, 방을 나갔다."]], [["That was Mr. Ablewhite’s revenge on Rachel, for refusing to marry his son!", "그것이, 제 아들과 혼인하기를 마다한 데 대한, 레이철에 대한 에이블화이트 씨의 복수였다!"]], [["The instant the door closed, Aunt Ablewhite exhibited a phenomenon which silenced us all.", "문이 닫히는 순간, 에이블화이트 이모가 우리 모두를 잠잠하게 하는 현상을 드러내 보였다."], ["She became endowed with energy enough to cross the room!", "그녀는 방을 가로지를 만큼의 기운을 부여받게 된 것이다!"]], [["“My dear,”", "\"얘야,\""], ["she said, taking Rachel by the hand, “I should be ashamed of my husband, if I didn’t know that it is his temper which has spoken to you, and not himself.", "하고 그녀가 레이철의 손을 잡으며 말했다. \"저것이 네게 말한 것이 그이 자신이 아니라 그이의 성미라는 것을 내가 몰랐다면, 나는 내 남편이 부끄러웠을 게다."], ["You,”", "당신은,\""], ["continued Aunt Ablewhite, turning on me in my corner with another endowment of energy, in her looks this time instead of her limbs—“you are the mischievous person who irritated him.", "하고 에이블화이트 이모가, 이번에는 팔다리가 아니라 눈빛에 또 한 번의 기운을 담아, 내 구석의 나를 향해 돌아서며 이어 갔다. \"당신은 그이를 성나게 한 그 심술궂은 사람이에요."], ["I hope I shall never see you or your tracts again.”", "나는 당신도 당신의 소책자도 다시는 안 봤으면 좋겠어요.\""], ["She went back to Rachel and kissed her.", "그녀는 레이철에게로 돌아가 그 아이에게 입 맞추었다."], ["“I beg your pardon, my dear,”", "\"미안하다, 얘야,\""], ["she said, “in my husband’s name.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"내 남편을 대신해서."], ["What can I do for you?”", "내가 너를 위해 무엇을 해 줄까?\""]], [["Consistently perverse in everything—capricious and unreasonable in all the actions of her life—Rachel melted into tears at those commonplace words, and returned her aunt’s kiss in silence.", "무엇에서나 한결같이 심술궂고—제 삶의 온갖 행동에서 변덕스럽고 불합리한—레이철이 그 흔해 빠진 말에 눈물을 쏟으며 녹아, 말없이 제 이모의 입맞춤에 응했다."]], [["“If I may be permitted to answer for Miss Verinder,” said Mr.", "\"제가 버린더 아가씨를 대신해 답해도 된다면,\""], ["Bruff, “might I ask you, Mrs.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["Ablewhite, to send Penelope down with her mistress’s bonnet and shawl.", "\"에이블화이트 부인, 페넬로피를 아래로 보내 그 여주인의 보닛과 숄을 가져오게 해 주시겠습니까?"], ["Leave us ten minutes together,”", "우리 둘이 십 분만 있게 해 주세요.\""], ["he added, in a lower tone, “and you may rely on my setting matters right, to your satisfaction as well as to Rachel’s.”", "하고 그가 더 낮은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그러면 제가 일들을, 레이철뿐만 아니라 당신도 흡족하게, 바로잡으리라 믿으셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["The trust of the family in this man was something wonderful to see.", "이 사내에 대한 그 집안의 믿음은 보기에 놀라운 것이었다."], ["Without a word more, on her side, Aunt Ablewhite left the room.", "제 편에서 더 말 한마디 없이, 에이블화이트 이모는 방을 떠났다."]], [["“Ah!”", "\"아!\""], ["said Mr. Bruff, looking after her.", "하고 브러프 씨가, 그녀를 눈으로 좇으며 말했다."], ["“The Herncastle blood has its drawbacks, I admit.", "\"헌캐슬 피에는 흠이 있지요, 인정합니다."], ["But there is something in good breeding after all!”", "하지만 좋은 혈통에는 결국 무언가가 있긴 있어요!\""]], [["Having made that purely worldly remark, he looked hard at my corner, as if he expected me to go.", "그 순전히 세속적인 소견을 내고 나서, 그는, 마치 내가 가기를 기대하는 듯, 내 구석을 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["My interest in Rachel—an infinitely higher interest than his—riveted me to my chair.", "레이철에 대한 나의 관심—그의 것보다 한없이 더 높은 관심—이 나를 내 의자에 붙들어 매었다."]], [["Mr. Bruff gave it up, exactly as he had given it up at Aunt Verinder’s, in Montagu Square.", "브러프 씨는, 몬터규 스퀘어의 버린더 이모님 댁에서 그랬던 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그것을 단념했다."], ["He led Rachel to a chair by the window, and spoke to her there.", "그는 레이철을 창가의 의자로 이끌어, 거기서 그 아이에게 말했다."]], [["“My dear young lady,”", "\"나의 사랑하는 젊은 아가씨,\""], ["he said, “Mr.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["Ablewhite’s conduct has naturally shocked you, and taken you by surprise.", "\"에이블화이트 씨의 처신은 자연히 당신을 충격에 빠뜨리고, 놀라게 했어요."], ["If it was worth while to contest the question with such a man, we might soon show him that he is not to have things all his own way.", "만일 그런 사내와 그 문제를 다툴 만한 가치가 있다면, 우리는 이내 그에게 그가 만사를 제 뜻대로 할 수는 없다는 것을 보여 줄 수 있을 겁니다."], ["But it isn’t worth while.", "하지만 그럴 만한 가치가 없어요."], ["You were quite right in what you said just now; he is beneath our notice.”", "당신이 방금 한 말은 아주 옳았어요. 그는 우리가 상대할 가치도 없어요.\""]], [["He stopped, and looked round at my corner.", "그는 멈추고, 내 구석을 둘러보았다."], ["I sat there quite immovable, with my tracts at my elbow and with Miss Jane Ann Stamper on my lap.", "나는 소책자를 팔꿈치에 두고 제인 앤 스탬퍼 양을 무릎에 얹은 채, 도무지 꿈쩍 않고 거기 앉아 있었다."]], [["“You know,”", "\"당신도 알잖아요,\""], ["he resumed, turning back again to Rachel, “that it was part of your poor mother’s fine nature always to see the best of the people about her, and never the worst.", "하고 그가, 다시 레이철에게로 돌아서며 말을 이었다. \"제 둘레의 사람들에게서 언제나 가장 좋은 점을 보고, 결코 가장 나쁜 점은 보지 않는 것이 당신의 가엾은 어머니의 고운 본성의 일부였다는 것을요."], ["She named her brother-in-law your guardian because she believed in him, and because she thought it would please her sister.", "그녀는 형부를 당신의 후견인으로 지명했는데, 그를 믿었기 때문이고, 그것이 제 언니를 기쁘게 하리라 여겼기 때문이지요."], ["I had never liked Mr. Ablewhite myself, and I induced your mother to let me insert a clause in the will, empowering her executors, in certain events, to consult with me about the appointment of a new guardian.", "나 자신은 여태껏 에이블화이트 씨를 좋아한 적이 없었고, 나는 당신 어머니를 설득해, 어떤 사건이 생길 경우, 새 후견인의 임명에 대해 나와 상의할 권한을 그녀의 유언 집행인들에게 부여하는 조항을 유언장에 넣게 했지요."], ["One of those events has happened today; and I find myself in a position to end all these dry business details, I hope agreeably, with a message from my wife.", "그 사건들 가운데 하나가 오늘 일어났어요. 그리고 나는, 이 온갖 무미건조한 사무적 세부를, 부디 기분 좋게, 내 아내의 전언과 함께 끝맺을 수 있는 처지에 있게 되었고요."], ["Will you honour Mrs. Bruff by becoming her guest?", "브러프 부인의 손님이 되어 주는 영광을 베풀어 주겠어요?"], ["And will you remain under my roof, and be one of my family, until we wise people have laid our heads together, and have settled what is to be done next?”", "그리고 우리 슬기로운 사람들이 머리를 맞대고, 다음에 무엇을 할지 정할 때까지, 내 지붕 아래 머물러, 내 식구 가운데 하나가 되어 주겠어요?\""]], [["At those words, I rose to interfere. Mr.", "그 말에, 나는 끼어들려고 일어섰다."], ["Bruff had done exactly what I had dreaded he would do, when he asked Mrs. Ablewhite for Rachel’s bonnet and shawl.", "브러프 씨는, 에이블화이트 부인에게 레이철의 보닛과 숄을 청했을 때 내가 그가 하리라 두려워했던 바로 그것을 한 것이었다."]], [["Before I could interpose a word, Rachel had accepted his invitation in the warmest terms.", "내가 한마디 끼워 넣기도 전에, 레이철이 더없이 따뜻한 말로 그의 초대를 받아들였다."], ["If I suffered the arrangement thus made between them to be carried out—if she once passed the threshold of Mr. Bruff’s door—farewell to the fondest hope of my life, the hope of bringing my lost sheep back to the fold!", "만일 내가 그들 사이에 그렇게 맺어진 그 약정이 이루어지도록 놓아둔다면—만일 그 아이가 한번 브러프 씨의 문지방을 넘는다면—내 삶의 가장 애틋한 희망, 나의 길 잃은 양을 우리로 도로 데려오려는 그 희망은 안녕이었다!"], ["The bare idea of such a calamity as this quite overwhelmed me.", "이런 참사라는 생각만으로도 나는 아주 압도되었다."], ["I cast the miserable trammels of worldly discretion to the winds, and spoke with the fervour that filled me, in the words that came first.", "나는 세속적 신중함이라는 그 비참한 굴레를 바람에 내던지고, 나를 채운 그 뜨거움으로, 처음 떠오르는 말로 말했다."]], [["“Stop!”", "\"멈춰요!\""], ["I said—“stop!", "하고 나는 말했다—\"멈춰요!"], ["I must be heard.", "제 말을 들어야 해요."], ["Mr. Bruff!", "브러프 씨!"], ["you are not related to her, and I am.", "당신은 그 아이의 친척이 아니고, 저는 친척이에요."], ["I invite her—I summon the executors to appoint me guardian.", "제가 그 아이를 청해요—저는 유언 집행인들에게 저를 후견인으로 임명하라 요구해요."], ["Rachel, dearest Rachel, I offer you my modest home; come to London by the next train, love, and share it with me!”", "레이철, 더없이 사랑하는 레이철, 저는 너에게 나의 수수한 집을 내주마. 다음 기차로 런던으로 오렴, 얘야, 그리고 그것을 나와 함께 나누자!\""]], [["Mr. Bruff said nothing.", "브러프 씨는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Rachel looked at me with a cruel astonishment which she made no effort to conceal.", "레이철은, 감추려는 애조차 하지 않는 모진 놀라움으로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“You are very kind, Drusilla,”", "\"참 친절하시네요, 드루실라.\""], ["she said.", "하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["“I shall hope to visit you whenever I happen to be in London. But I have accepted Mr.", "\"저는 런던에 있게 될 때마다 당신을 찾아뵙기를 바랄게요."], ["Bruff’s invitation, and I think it will be best, for the present, if I remain under Mr. Bruff’s care.”", "하지만 저는 브러프 씨의 초대를 받아들였고, 당분간은, 제가 브러프 씨의 보살핌 아래 머무는 것이 가장 좋으리라 생각해요.\""]], [["“Oh, don’t say so!”", "\"오, 그런 말 말아라!\""], ["I pleaded.", "하고 나는 간청했다."], ["“I can’t part with you, Rachel—I can’t part with you!”", "\"나는 너와 헤어질 수 없어, 레이철—나는 너와 헤어질 수 없어!\""]], [["I tried to fold her in my arms.", "나는 그 아이를 내 품에 안으려 했다."], ["But she drew back.", "허나 그 아이는 물러섰다."], ["My fervour did not communicate itself; it only alarmed her.", "나의 뜨거움은 옮겨 가지 않았다. 그것은 그저 그 아이를 놀라게 했을 뿐이다."]], [["“Surely,”", "\"분명,\""], ["she said, “this is a very unnecessary display of agitation?", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"이건 아주 부질없는 흥분의 과시죠?"], ["I don’t understand it.”", "저는 이해가 안 돼요.\""]], [["“No more do I,”", "\"나도 마찬가지예요.\""], ["said Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."]], [["Their hardness—their hideous, worldly hardness—revolted me.", "그들의 모짊이—그들의 소름 끼치는, 세속적인 모짊이—나를 역겹게 했다."]], [["“Oh, Rachel!", "\"오, 레이철!"], ["Rachel!”", "레이철!\""], ["I burst out.", "하고 나는 터뜨렸다."], ["“Haven’t you seen yet, that my heart yearns to make a Christian of you?", "\"너는 아직도 못 봤니, 나의 마음이 너를 기독교인으로 만들고자 애타는 것을?"], ["Has no inner voice told you that I am trying to do for you, what I was trying to do for your dear mother when death snatched her out of my hands?”", "죽음이 네 사랑하는 어머니를 내 손에서 낚아채 갔을 때 내가 그분을 위해 하려던 것을, 내가 너를 위해 하려 애쓰고 있다고, 어떤 내면의 목소리도 너에게 일러 주지 않았니?\""]], [["Rachel advanced a step nearer, and looked at me very strangely.", "레이철이 한 걸음 더 가까이 나아와, 나를 아주 이상하게 바라보았다."]], [["“I don’t understand your reference to my mother,” she said.", "\"저는 제 어머니에 대한 당신의 언급을 이해할 수 없어요.\""], ["“Miss Clack, will you have the goodness to explain yourself?”", "하고 그 아이가 말했다. \"클랙 양, 부디 당신 뜻을 설명해 주시겠어요?\""]], [["Before I could answer, Mr. Bruff came forward, and offering his arm to Rachel, tried to lead her out of the room.", "내가 대답하기도 전에, 브러프 씨가 앞으로 나와, 레이철에게 제 팔을 내밀며, 그 아이를 방 밖으로 이끌어 내려 했다."]], [["“You had better not pursue the subject, my dear,”", "\"당신은 그 화제를 더 파고들지 않는 편이 낫습니다, 얘야.\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“And Miss Clack had better not explain herself.”", "\"그리고 클랙 양도 제 뜻을 설명하지 않는 편이 낫고요.\""]], [["If I had been a stock or a stone, such an interference as this must have roused me into testifying to the truth.", "만일 내가 나무토막이나 돌덩이였다면, 이런 끼어듦은 분명 나를 일깨워 진실을 증언하게 했으리라."], ["I put Mr. Bruff aside indignantly with my own hand, and, in solemn and suitable language, I stated the view with which sound doctrine does not scruple to regard the awful calamity of dying unprepared.", "나는 나의 손으로 브러프 씨를 분개하여 밀쳐 내고, 엄숙하고 알맞은 말로, 채비 없이 죽는다는 그 끔찍한 참사를 건전한 교리가 서슴없이 여기는 그 견해를 밝혔다."]], [["Rachel started back from me—I blush to write—with a scream of horror.", "레이철이 나에게서 흠칫 물러섰다—적기가 낯 뜨겁거니와—소름 끼치는 비명과 함께."]], [["“Come away!”", "\"가요!\""], ["she said to Mr. Bruff.", "하고 그 아이가 브러프 씨에게 말했다."], ["“Come away, for God’s sake, before that woman can say any more!", "\"가요, 제발, 저 여자가 더 말할 수 있기 전에요!"], ["Oh, think of my poor mother’s harmless, useful, beautiful life!", "오, 제 가엾은 어머니의 해롭잖고, 쓸모 있고, 아름다운 삶을 생각해요!"], ["You were at the funeral, Mr.", "당신은 장례에 있었죠, 브러프 씨."], ["Bruff; you saw how everybody loved her; you saw the poor helpless people crying at her grave over the loss of their best friend.", "당신은 모두가 그분을 얼마나 사랑했는지 보았죠. 당신은 그 가엾고 힘없는 사람들이 제 가장 좋은 벗을 잃은 것을 두고 그분 무덤에서 우는 것을 보았죠."], ["And that wretch stands there, and tries to make me doubt that my mother, who was an angel on earth, is an angel in heaven now!", "그런데 저 몹쓸 사람이 저기 서서, 이 땅 위의 천사였던 제 어머니가 지금 천국의 천사인지를 제가 의심하게 만들려 해요!"], ["Don’t stop to talk about it! Come away!", "그것에 대해 이야기하느라 멈추지 말아요!"], ["It stifles me to breathe the same air with her!", "가요! 저 여자와 같은 공기를 마시는 게 저를 숨 막히게 해요!"], ["It frightens me to feel that we are in the same room together!”", "우리가 같은 방에 함께 있다는 걸 느끼는 게 저를 겁나게 해요!\""]], [["Deaf to all remonstrance, she ran to the door.", "온갖 훈계에 귀먹은 채, 그 아이는 문으로 달려갔다."]], [["At the same moment, her maid entered with her bonnet and shawl.", "바로 그 순간, 그 아이의 하녀가 그 아이의 보닛과 숄을 들고 들어왔다."], ["She huddled them on anyhow.", "그 아이는 그것들을 아무렇게나 걸쳤다. \"제 짐을 꾸려요,\""], ["“Pack my things,”", "하고 그 아이가 말했다."], ["she said, “and bring them to Mr. Bruff’s.”", "\"그리고 그것들을 브러프 씨 댁으로 가져와요.\""], ["I attempted to approach her—I was shocked and grieved, but, it is needless to say, not offended.", "나는 그 아이에게 다가가려 했다—나는 충격받고 마음 아팠으나, 다시 말할 것도 없이, 비위가 상하지는 않았다."], ["I only wished to say to her, “May your hard heart be softened!", "나는 그저 그 아이에게 말하고 싶었을 뿐이다, \"부디 너의 모진 마음이 누그러지기를!"], ["I freely forgive you!”", "나는 기꺼이 너를 용서한다!\""], ["She pulled down her veil, and tore her shawl away from my hand, and, hurrying out, shut the door in my face.", "그 아이는 제 베일을 끌어내리고, 내 손에서 제 숄을 홱 잡아채고는, 서둘러 나가며, 내 얼굴에 대고 문을 닫았다."], ["I bore the insult with my customary fortitude.", "나는 여느 때의 꿋꿋함으로 그 모욕을 견뎠다."], ["I remember it now with my customary superiority to all feeling of offence.", "나는 그것을 지금 온갖 비위 상하는 느낌을 넘어서는 여느 때의 우월함으로 떠올린다."]], [["Mr. Bruff had his parting word of mockery for me, before he too hurried out, in his turn.", "브러프 씨도, 제 차례로 서둘러 나가기 전에, 나에게 건넬 작별의 조롱의 말이 있었다."]], [["“You had better not have explained yourself, Miss Clack,”", "\"당신은 제 뜻을 설명하지 않는 편이 나았어요, 클랙 양.\""], ["he said, and bowed, and left the room.", "하고 그가 말하고는, 고개 숙여 절하고, 방을 나갔다."]], [["The person with the cap-ribbons followed.", "그 모자 리본을 단 사람이 뒤따랐다."]], [["“It’s easy to see who has set them all by the ears together,”", "\"저들을 다 서로 아웅다웅하게 만든 게 누군지는 뻔히 보이네요.\" 하고 그 여자가 말했다."], ["she said. “I’m only a poor servant—but I declare I’m ashamed of you!”", "\"저는 그저 가엾은 하녀일 뿐이지만—당신이 부끄럽다고 선언해요!\""], ["She too went out, and banged the door after her.", "그 여자도 나가, 제 뒤로 문을 쾅 닫았다."]], [["I was left alone in the room.", "나는 그 방에 혼자 남겨졌다."], ["Reviled by them all, deserted by them all, I was left alone in the room.", "그들 모두에게 욕먹고, 그들 모두에게 버림받고, 나는 그 방에 혼자 남겨졌다."]], [["Is there more to be added to this plain statement of facts—to this touching picture of a Christian persecuted by the world?", "이 담담한 사실 진술에—세상에 박해받는 한 기독교인의 이 가슴 뭉클한 그림에—덧붙일 것이 더 있을까? 아니!"], ["No! my diary reminds me that one more of the many chequered chapters in my life ends here.", "나의 일기가 내게, 나의 삶의 그 숱한 얼룩덜룩한 장들 가운데 또 하나가 여기서 끝난다는 것을 일깨운다."], ["From that day forth, I never saw Rachel Verinder again.", "그날 이후로, 나는 레이철 버린더를 다시 보지 못했다."], ["She had my forgiveness at the time when she insulted me.", "그 아이는 나를 모욕한 그때 나의 용서를 받았다."], ["She has had my prayerful good wishes ever since.", "그 아이는 그 뒤로 죽 나의 기도 어린 축복을 받아 왔다."], ["And when I die—to complete the return on my part of good for evil—she will have the Life, Letters, and Labours of Miss Jane Ann Stamper left her as a legacy by my will.", "그리고 내가 죽을 때—악을 선으로 갚는 나의 편의 그 되갚음을 완성하고자—그 아이는 나의 유언에 의해 「제인 앤 스탬퍼 양의 생애, 편지, 그리고 노고」를 유산으로 남겨 받을 것이다."]], [["# SECOND NARRATIVE.", "# SECOND NARRATIVE."]], [["Contributed by Mathew Bruff, Solicitor, of Gray’s Inn Square.", "그레이스 인 스퀘어의 사무 변호사, 매슈 브러프가 기고함."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["My fair friend, Miss Clack, having laid down the pen, there are two reasons for my taking it up next, in my turn.", "나의 어여쁜 벗 클랙 양이 붓을 내려놓았으니, 다음으로 내가 내 차례로 그것을 집어 드는 데는 두 가지 이유가 있다."]], [["In the first place, I am in a position to throw the necessary light on certain points of interest which have thus far been left in the dark.", "첫째로, 나는 여태껏 어둠 속에 남겨져 온 몇몇 흥미로운 점에 필요한 빛을 비출 처지에 있다."], ["Miss Verinder had her own private reason for breaking her marriage engagement—and I was at the bottom of it. Mr.", "버린더 아가씨에게는 제 혼약을 깨뜨릴 저 나름의 남모를 이유가 있었다—그리고 내가 그 밑바탕에 있었다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite had his own private reason for withdrawing all claim to the hand of his charming cousin—and I discovered what it was.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게는 제 매혹적인 사촌 누이의 손에 대한 온갖 청구를 물릴 저 나름의 남모를 이유가 있었다—그리고 나는 그것이 무엇인지 알아냈다."]], [["In the second place, it was my good or ill fortune, I hardly know which, to find myself personally involved—at the period of which I am now writing—in the mystery of the Indian Diamond.", "둘째로, 내가 지금 쓰고 있는 그때에, 나 자신이 그 인도 다이아몬드의 수수께끼에 몸소 얽혀들게 된 것이 나의 행운이었는지 불운이었는지, 나는 좀처럼 모르겠다."], ["I had the honour of an interview, at my own office, with an Oriental stranger of distinguished manners, who was no other, unquestionably, than the chief of the three Indians.", "나는 나 자신의 사무소에서, 품위 있는 태도를 지닌 어느 동양 낯선 이와 면담하는 영광을 누렸는데, 그는 의심할 것 없이, 다름 아닌 그 인도인 셋의 우두머리였다."], ["Add to this, that I met with the celebrated traveller, Mr. Murthwaite, the day afterwards, and that I held a conversation with him on the subject of the Moonstone, which has a very important bearing on later events.", "여기에 덧붙이자면, 나는 그 이튿날 이름난 여행가 머스웨이트 씨를 만났고, 그와 월장석이라는 화제에 대해 대화를 나누었는데, 그것은 나중의 일들에 아주 중요하게 관련된다."], ["And there you have the statement of my claims to fill the position which I occupy in these pages.", "그리고 그것으로 여러분은, 내가 이 쪽들에서 차지하는 자리를 채우는 데 대한 나의 자격의 진술을 갖게 되는 셈이다."]], [["The true story of the broken marriage engagement comes first in point of time, and must therefore take the first place in the present narrative.", "깨진 혼약의 참된 이야기가 시간상 먼저 오니, 따라서 그것이 지금의 이야기에서 첫 자리를 차지해야 한다."], ["Tracing my way back along the chain of events, from one end to the other, I find it necessary to open the scene, oddly enough as you will think, at the bedside of my excellent client and friend, the late Sir John Verinder.", "사건들의 사슬을 따라, 한쪽 끝에서 다른 쪽 끝으로, 내 길을 되짚어 가다 보니, 나는, 여러분이 생각하기에 참으로 이상하게도, 나의 훌륭한 의뢰인이자 벗인 고(故) 존 버린더 경의 병상에서 그 장면을 열 필요를 느낀다."]], [["Sir John had his share—perhaps rather a large share—of the more harmless and amiable of the weaknesses incidental to humanity.", "존 경은 인간에게 딸린 약점 가운데 더 해롭잖고 상냥한 것들을 제 몫으로—어쩌면 다소 큰 몫으로—지니고 있었다."], ["Among these, I may mention as applicable to the matter in hand, an invincible reluctance—so long as he enjoyed his usual good health—to face the responsibility of making his will.", "그중에서, 지금 다루는 일에 해당하는 것으로 내가 언급할 만한 것은, 그가—여느 때의 건강을 누리는 동안에는—제 유언장을 만드는 책임을 마주하기를 이길 수 없이 꺼렸다는 것이다."], ["Lady Verinder exerted her influence to rouse him to a sense of duty in this matter; and I exerted my influence.", "레이디 버린더가 이 일에서 그를 일깨워 의무를 자각하게 하려고 제 영향력을 발휘했다. 그리고 나도 내 영향력을 발휘했다."], ["He admitted the justice of our views—but he went no further than that, until he found himself afflicted with the illness which ultimately brought him to his grave.", "그는 우리 견해의 옳음을 인정했다—허나 결국 그를 무덤으로 데려간 그 병에 걸린 자신을 발견하기 전까지는, 그 이상으로 나아가지 않았다."], ["Then, I was sent for at last, to take my client’s instructions on the subject of his will.", "그때에야, 마침내, 나는 유언장이라는 화제에 대한 내 의뢰인의 지시를 받으러 불려 갔다."], ["They proved to be the simplest instructions I had ever received in the whole of my professional career.", "그것은 내 직업 이력 전체에서 여태껏 받아 본 가장 단순한 지시로 드러났다."]], [["Sir John was dozing, when I entered the room.", "내가 방에 들어섰을 때 존 경은 꾸벅꾸벅 졸고 있었다."], ["He roused himself at the sight of me.", "그는 나를 보고 정신을 차렸다."]], [["“How do you do, Mr.", "\"안녕하시오, 브러프 씨?\""], ["Bruff?” he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I sha’n’t be very long about this.", "\"이 일에 그리 오래 걸리지는 않을 거요."], ["And then I’ll go to sleep again.”", "그러고 나면 나는 다시 잠들겠소.\""], ["He looked on with great interest while I collected pens, ink, and paper.", "그는 내가 펜과 잉크와 종이를 챙기는 동안 큰 관심을 가지고 지켜보았다. \"준비됐소?\""], ["“Are you ready?”", "하고 그가 물었다."], ["he asked. I bowed and took a dip of ink, and waited for my instructions.", "나는 고개 숙여 인사하고 잉크를 찍고는, 그의 지시를 기다렸다."]], [["“I leave everything to my wife,”", "\"모든 것을 내 아내에게 남기오.\""], ["said Sir John. “That’s all.”", "하고 존 경이 말했다."], ["He turned round on his pillow, and composed himself to sleep again.", "\"그게 다요.\" 그는 베개 위에서 돌아누워, 다시 잠들 채비를 했다."]], [["I was obliged to disturb him.", "나는 그를 방해할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“Am I to understand,”", "\"이렇게 이해해야 합니까,\""], ["I asked, “that you leave the whole of the property, of every sort and description, of which you die possessed, absolutely to Lady Verinder?”", "하고 나는 물었다. \"경께서 돌아가실 때 지니고 계신, 온갖 종류와 종별의 재산 전부를, 온전히 레이디 버린더께 남기신다고요?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소.\""], ["said Sir John.", "하고 존 경이 말했다."], ["“Only, I put it shorter.", "\"다만, 나는 그것을 더 짧게 말했을 뿐이오."], ["Why can’t you put it shorter, and let me go to sleep again?", "당신은 어째서 그것을 더 짧게 말하고, 나를 다시 잠들게 해 주지 못하오?"], ["Everything to my wife.", "모든 것을 내 아내에게."], ["That’s my Will.”", "그것이 나의 유언이오.\""]], [["His property was entirely at his own disposal, and was of two kinds.", "그의 재산은 온전히 그 자신의 뜻대로 처분할 수 있는 것이었고, 두 가지 종류였다."], ["Property in land (I purposely abstain from using technical language), and property in money.", "토지 재산(나는 일부러 전문 용어를 쓰기를 삼간다)과, 금전 재산이었다."], ["In the majority of cases, I am afraid I should have felt it my duty to my client to ask him to reconsider his Will.", "태반의 경우에, 나는 내 의뢰인에게 유언장을 다시 헤아려 보라 청하는 것이 그에 대한 나의 의무라고 느꼈으리라."], ["In the case of Sir John, I knew Lady Verinder to be, not only worthy of the unreserved trust which her husband had placed in her (all good wives are worthy of that)—but to be also capable of properly administering a trust (which, in my experience of the fair sex, not one in a thousand of them is competent to do).", "존 경의 경우에는, 나는 레이디 버린더가, 그 남편이 그녀에게 둔 그 아낌없는 신뢰에 값할 만할 (모든 좋은 아내가 그럴 만하다) 뿐만 아니라, 신탁을 제대로 운영할 능력도 있음을 (내가 여자들을 겪은 바로는, 그럴 능력이 있는 이는 천에 하나도 없다) 알고 있었다."], ["In ten minutes, Sir John’s Will was drawn, and executed, and Sir John himself, good man, was finishing his interrupted nap.", "십 분 만에, 존 경의 유언장은 작성되고, 집행되었으며, 존 경 그 자신은, 착한 이여, 그 끊긴 선잠을 마저 자고 있었다."]], [["Lady Verinder amply justified the confidence which her husband had placed in her.", "레이디 버린더는 그 남편이 그녀에게 둔 그 믿음에 넉넉히 값했다."], ["In the first days of her widowhood, she sent for me, and made her Will.", "과부가 된 첫 며칠에, 그녀는 나를 불러, 제 유언장을 만들었다."], ["The view she took of her position was so thoroughly sound and sensible, that I was relieved of all necessity for advising her.", "그녀가 제 처지에 대해 취한 견해가 어찌나 철저히 건전하고 분별 있던지, 나는 그녀에게 조언할 온갖 필요에서 벗어났다."], ["My responsibility began and ended with shaping her instructions into the proper legal form.", "나의 책임은 그녀의 지시를 알맞은 법적 형식으로 다듬는 데서 시작되어 거기서 끝났다."], ["Before Sir John had been a fortnight in his grave, the future of his daughter had been most wisely and most affectionately provided for.", "존 경이 무덤에 든 지 채 두 주도 되기 전에, 그 딸의 앞날이 더없이 슬기롭고 더없이 다정하게 마련되었다."]], [["The Will remained in its fireproof box at my office, through more years than I like to reckon up.", "그 유언장은 내 사무소의 방화 상자 안에, 내가 헤아려 보고 싶지 않을 만큼 여러 해 동안 머물러 있었다."], ["It was not till the summer of eighteen hundred and forty-eight that I found occasion to look at it again under very melancholy circumstances.", "내가 다시 그것을 들여다볼 일을 찾은 것은, 아주 서글픈 정황 아래, 1848년 여름이 되어서였다."]], [["At the date I have mentioned, the doctors pronounced the sentence on poor Lady Verinder, which was literally a sentence of death.", "내가 언급한 그 시기에, 의사들은 가엾은 레이디 버린더에게 선고를 내렸는데, 그것은 문자 그대로 죽음의 선고였다."], ["I was the first person whom she informed of her situation; and I found her anxious to go over her Will again with me.", "나는 그녀가 제 처지를 알린 첫 사람이었다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 나와 다시 제 유언장을 살펴보고 싶어 안달임을 알았다."]], [["It was impossible to improve the provisions relating to her daughter.", "그 딸에 관한 조항들은 손볼 수가 없었다."], ["But, in the lapse of time, her wishes in regard to certain minor legacies, left to different relatives, had undergone some modification; and it became necessary to add three or four Codicils to the original document.", "허나, 세월이 흐르며, 여러 친척에게 남긴 몇몇 자잘한 유산에 관한 그녀의 뜻이 얼마쯤 바뀌었다. 그리하여 원래 문서에 서너 개의 부칙을 덧붙일 필요가 생겼다."], ["Having done this at once, for fear of accident, I obtained her ladyship’s permission to embody her recent instructions in a second Will.", "만일의 사고를 염려하여 이것을 대뜸 해 놓고 나서, 나는 그녀의 최근 지시를 두 번째 유언장에 담아도 된다는 마님의 허락을 얻었다."], ["My object was to avoid certain inevitable confusions and repetitions which now disfigured the original document, and which, to own the truth, grated sadly on my professional sense of the fitness of things.", "나의 목적은, 이제 원래 문서를 볼썽사납게 하는, 그리고, 진실을 인정하자면, 사물의 알맞음에 대한 나의 직업적 감각을 서글피 거스르는, 어떤 피할 수 없는 혼란과 되풀이를 없애는 것이었다."]], [["The execution of this second Will has been described by Miss Clack, who was so obliging as to witness it.", "이 두 번째 유언장의 집행은 클랙 양이 묘사했는데, 그녀는 친절하게도 그것을 증언해 주었다."], ["So far as regarded Rachel Verinder’s pecuniary interests, it was, word for word, the exact counterpart of the first Will.", "레이철 버린더의 금전적 이익에 관한 한, 그것은, 한 자 한 자, 첫 번째 유언장의 꼭 그대로의 짝이었다."], ["The only changes introduced related to the appointment of a guardian, and to certain provisions concerning that appointment, which were made under my advice.", "들여온 유일한 변경은 후견인의 임명과, 그 임명에 관한 어떤 조항들—나의 조언 아래 만들어진 것들—에 관련되었다."], ["On Lady Verinder’s death, the Will was placed in the hands of my proctor to be “proved” (as the phrase is) in the usual way.", "레이디 버린더가 죽자, 그 유언장은 여느 방식대로 (그 말마따나) \"검인\"되도록 나의 검인 대리인의 손에 놓였다."]], [["In about three weeks from that time—as well as I can remember—the first warning reached me of something unusual going on under the surface.", "그때로부터 세 주쯤 지나—내가 기억할 수 있는 한—무언가 예사롭지 않은 일이 겉면 아래 진행되고 있다는 첫 경고가 내게 다다랐다."], ["I happened to be looking in at my friend the proctor’s office, and I observed that he received me with an appearance of greater interest than usual.", "나는 마침 내 벗인 그 검인 대리인의 사무소에 들렀는데, 나는 그가 여느 때보다 더 큰 관심의 낯빛으로 나를 맞는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["“I have some news for you,” he said.", "\"당신께 드릴 소식이 좀 있습니다.\""], ["“What do you think I heard at Doctors’ Commons this morning?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"제가 오늘 아침 닥터스 코먼스에서 무슨 소리를 들었는지 아세요?"], ["Lady Verinder’s Will has been asked for, and examined, already!”", "레이디 버린더의 유언장이 벌써 조회되고, 열람되었답니다!\""]], [["This was news indeed!", "이것은 참으로 소식이었다!"], ["There was absolutely nothing which could be contested in the Will; and there was nobody I could think of who had the slightest interest in examining it.", "그 유언장에는 다툴 만한 것이라곤 도무지 아무것도 없었다. 그리고 그것을 열람하는 데 조금이라도 관심이 있을 만한 사람으로 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 이는 아무도 없었다."], ["(I shall perhaps do well if I explain in this place, for the benefit of the few people who don’t know it already, that the law allows all Wills to be examined at Doctors’ Commons by anybody who applies, on the payment of a shilling fee.)", "(아직 그것을 모르는 몇몇 사람을 위해, 법이 모든 유언장을 닥터스 코먼스에서, 일 실링의 수수료를 내면 누구든 신청하는 이가 열람할 수 있게 허용한다는 것을 여기서 설명해 두는 것이 좋겠다.)"]], [["“Did you hear who asked for the Will?”", "\"누가 그 유언장을 조회했는지 들었소?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Yes; the clerk had no hesitation in telling me.", "\"네. 서기가 나에게 서슴없이 말해 주더군요."], ["Mr. Smalley, of the firm of Skipp and Smalley, asked for it.", "스킵 앤드 스몰리 회사의 스몰리 씨가 그것을 조회했답니다."], ["The Will has not been copied yet into the great Folio Registers.", "그 유언장은 아직 그 큰 이절판 등록부에 옮겨 적히지 않았지요."], ["So there was no alternative but to depart from the usual course, and to let him see the original document.", "그래서 여느 절차에서 벗어나, 그에게 원래 문서를 보여 주는 수밖에 없었답니다."], ["He looked it over carefully, and made a note in his pocket-book.", "그는 그것을 꼼꼼히 훑어보고, 제 수첩에 무언가 적었다는군요."], ["Have you any idea of what he wanted with it?”", "그가 그것으로 무엇을 하려는지 짐작이 가세요?\""]], [["I shook my head.", "나는 고개를 저었다."], ["“I shall find out,”", "\"알아내겠소.\""], ["I answered, “before I am a day older.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"하루가 더 가기 전에.\""], ["With that I went back at once to my own office.", "그 말과 함께 나는 대뜸 나 자신의 사무소로 돌아갔다."]], [["If any other firm of solicitors had been concerned in this unaccountable examination of my deceased client’s Will, I might have found some difficulty in making the necessary discovery.", "만일 다른 어떤 사무 변호사 회사가 나의 죽은 의뢰인의 유언장에 대한 이 영문 모를 열람에 관련되었다면, 나는 필요한 발견을 하는 데 얼마쯤 어려움을 겪었을지 모른다."], ["But I had a hold over Skipp and Smalley which made my course in this matter a comparatively easy one.", "허나 나는 스킵 앤드 스몰리에 대해, 이 일에서 나의 행보를 견주어 손쉽게 만드는 어떤 쥔 것이 있었다."], ["My common-law clerk (a most competent and excellent man) was a brother of Mr.", "나의 관습법 서기(더없이 유능하고 훌륭한 사람)가 스몰리 씨의 형제였다."], ["Smalley’s; and, owing to this sort of indirect connection with me, Skipp and Smalley had, for some years past, picked up the crumbs that fell from my table, in the shape of cases brought to my office, which, for various reasons, I did not think it worth while to undertake.", "그리고 나와의 이런 종류의 간접적 연줄 덕분에, 스킵 앤드 스몰리는, 지난 몇 해 동안, 여러 이유로 내가 맡을 값어치가 없다고 여긴, 내 사무소에 들어온 사건이라는 모양으로, 내 상에서 떨어지는 부스러기를 주워 왔다."], ["My professional patronage was, in this way, of some importance to the firm.", "나의 직업적 후원이, 이런 식으로, 그 회사에 얼마쯤 중요했다."], ["I intended, if necessary, to remind them of that patronage, on the present occasion.", "나는, 필요하다면, 이번 경우에 그들에게 그 후원을 상기시킬 작정이었다."]], [["The moment I got back I spoke to my clerk; and, after telling him what had happened, I sent him to his brother’s office, “with Mr.", "돌아오는 즉시 나는 내 서기에게 말했다."], ["Bruff’s compliments, and he would be glad to know why Messrs. Skipp and Smalley had found it necessary to examine Lady Verinder’s will.”", "그리고, 그에게 무슨 일이 있었는지 이른 뒤, 나는 그를 제 형제의 사무소로 보냈다, \"브러프 씨의 안부와 함께, 스킵 앤드 스몰리 씨들이 어째서 레이디 버린더의 유언장을 열람할 필요가 있다고 여겼는지 알고 싶다\"고."]], [["This message brought Mr. Smalley back to my office in company with his brother.", "이 전언이 스몰리 씨를 제 형제와 함께 내 사무소로 데려왔다."], ["He acknowledged that he had acted under instructions received from a client.", "그는 어느 의뢰인에게서 받은 지시에 따라 움직였음을 인정했다."], ["And then he put it to me, whether it would not be a breach of professional confidence on his part to say more.", "그러고 나서 그는 내게, 더 말하는 것이 제 편에서 직업적 비밀을 저버리는 것이 아니겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["We had a smart discussion upon that.", "우리는 그것을 두고 날카로운 논쟁을 벌였다."], ["He was right, no doubt; and I was wrong.", "그가 옳았다, 의심할 것 없이. 그리고 내가 틀렸다."], ["The truth is, I was angry and suspicious—and I insisted on knowing more.", "사실인즉, 나는 화가 나고 미심쩍었다—그래서 나는 더 알기를 우겼다."], ["Worse still, I declined to consider any additional information offered me, as a secret placed in my keeping: I claimed perfect freedom to use my own discretion.", "더 나쁘게도, 나는 내게 제시되는 어떤 추가 정보도 내가 간직하도록 맡겨진 비밀로 여기기를 마다했다. 나는 나 자신의 재량을 쓸 온전한 자유를 주장했다."], ["Worse even than that, I took an unwarrantable advantage of my position.", "그보다 더 나쁘게도, 나는 내 처지를 부당하게 이용했다. \"고르시오, 선생,\""], ["“Choose, sir,” I said to Mr.", "하고 나는 스몰리 씨에게 말했다."], ["Smalley, “between the risk of losing your client’s business and the risk of losing Mine.”", "\"당신 의뢰인의 일감을 잃을 위험과, 나의 일감을 잃을 위험 사이에서.\""], ["Quite indefensible, I admit—an act of tyranny, and nothing less.", "아주 변명할 여지가 없다, 인정하거니와—폭정의 행위요, 그 이하가 아니다."], ["Like other tyrants, I carried my point. Mr.", "다른 폭군들처럼, 나는 내 뜻을 관철했다."], ["Smalley chose his alternative, without a moment’s hesitation.", "스몰리 씨는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 제 선택지를 골랐다."]], [["He smiled resignedly, and gave up the name of his client:", "그는 체념한 듯 미소 짓고, 제 의뢰인의 이름을 내주었다."]], [["Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨였다."]], [["That was enough for me—I wanted to know no more.", "그것으로 내게는 넉넉했다—나는 더 알 필요가 없었다."]], [["Having reached this point in my narrative, it now becomes necessary to place the reader of these lines—so far as Lady Verinder’s Will is concerned—on a footing of perfect equality, in respect of information, with myself.", "나의 이야기에서 이 지점에 다다랐으니, 이제—레이디 버린더의 유언장에 관한 한—이 글줄들을 읽는 이를, 정보에 관해, 나 자신과 온전히 대등한 처지에 놓을 필요가 생긴다."]], [["Let me state, then, in the fewest possible words, that Rachel Verinder had nothing but a life-interest in the property.", "그러면, 될 수 있는 대로 가장 적은 말로 밝히자면, 레이철 버린더는 그 재산에 대해 종신 수익권밖에 없었다."], ["Her mother’s excellent sense, and my long experience, had combined to relieve her of all responsibility, and to guard her from all danger of becoming the victim in the future of some needy and unscrupulous man.", "그녀의 어머니의 훌륭한 분별과, 나의 오랜 경험이 어우러져, 그녀를 온갖 책임에서 벗어나게 하고, 앞으로 궁핍하고 파렴치한 어떤 사내의 희생물이 될 온갖 위험에서 그녀를 지켰다."], ["Neither she, nor her husband (if she married), could raise sixpence, either on the property in land, or on the property in money.", "그녀도, 그녀의 남편도 (그녀가 혼인한다면), 토지 재산으로도, 금전 재산으로도, 육 펜스 한 푼 마련할 수 없었다."], ["They would have the houses in London and in Yorkshire to live in, and they would have the handsome income—and that was all.", "그들은 런던과 요크셔의 집을 살 곳으로 갖게 되고, 두둑한 수입을 갖게 될 것이었다—그리고 그것이 전부였다."]], [["When I came to think over what I had discovered, I was sorely perplexed what to do next.", "내가 알아낸 것을 곰곰이 헤아려 보게 되자, 나는 다음에 무엇을 할지 몹시 갈피를 못 잡았다."]], [["Hardly a week had passed since I had heard (to my surprise and distress) of Miss Verinder’s proposed marriage.", "내가 (놀라움과 괴로움 속에서) 버린더 아가씨의 예정된 혼인에 대해 들은 지 채 한 주도 지나지 않은 터였다."], ["I had the sincerest admiration and affection for her; and I had been inexpressibly grieved when I heard that she was about to throw herself away on Mr.", "나는 그녀를 진심으로 감탄하고 아꼈다. 그리고 나는 그녀가 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 저를 내던지려 한다는 것을 들었을 때 이루 말할 수 없이 마음 아팠다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite. And now, here was the man—whom I had always believed to be a smooth-tongued impostor—justifying the very worst that I had thought of him, and plainly revealing the mercenary object of the marriage, on his side!", "그런데 이제, 여기, 그 사내가—내가 언제나 매끄러운 혀를 놀리는 사기꾼이라 믿어 온 그 사내가—내가 그에 대해 생각한 바로 그 가장 나쁜 것을 입증하며, 제 편에서 그 혼인의 잇속 밝은 목적을 뻔히 드러내고 있었던 것이다!"], ["And what of that?—you may reply—the thing is done every day.", "그래서 그것이 어떻다는 거냐?—여러분은 대꾸할지 모른다—그런 일은 날마다 일어난다고."], ["Granted, my dear sir.", "그렇다 치자, 나의 선생."], ["But would you think of it quite as lightly as you do, if the thing was done (let us say) with your own sister?", "허나 만일 그 일이 (이를테면) 여러분 자신의 누이에게 벌어진다면, 여러분은 그것을 지금 여기는 만큼 가볍게 여기겠는가?"]], [["The first consideration which now naturally occurred to me was this.", "이제 자연히 내게 떠오른 첫 헤아림은 이것이었다."], ["Would Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite hold to his engagement, after what his lawyer had discovered for him?", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 제 변호사가 저를 위해 알아낸 것 뒤에도, 제 약혼을 지킬 것인가?"]], [["It depended entirely on his pecuniary position, of which I knew nothing.", "그것은 온전히 그의 금전적 처지에 달려 있었는데, 나는 그것에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다."], ["If that position was not a desperate one, it would be well worth his while to marry Miss Verinder for her income alone.", "만일 그 처지가 절박한 것이 아니라면, 오직 그 수입만을 위해서라도 버린더 아가씨와 혼인하는 것이 그로서는 넉넉히 값어치 있는 일일 터였다."], ["If, on the other hand, he stood in urgent need of realising a large sum by a given time, then Lady Verinder’s Will would exactly meet the case, and would preserve her daughter from falling into a scoundrel’s hands.", "만일, 다른 한편으로, 그가 정해진 때까지 큰돈을 마련해야 할 절박한 필요에 처해 있다면, 그때는 레이디 버린더의 유언장이 딱 그 경우에 들어맞아, 그 딸이 악당의 손에 떨어지는 것을 막아 줄 터였다."]], [["In the latter event, there would be no need for me to distress Miss Rachel, in the first days of her mourning for her mother, by an immediate revelation of the truth.", "뒤엣경우라면, 내가 레이철 아가씨를, 제 어머니를 애도하는 첫 며칠에, 즉각적인 진실의 폭로로 괴롭힐 필요가 없을 터였다."], ["In the former event, if I remained silent, I should be conniving at a marriage which would make her miserable for life.", "앞엣경우라면, 만일 내가 잠자코 있으면, 나는 그녀를 평생 비참하게 만들 혼인을 눈감아 주는 셈이 될 터였다."]], [["My doubts ended in my calling at the hotel in London, at which I knew Mrs. Ablewhite and Miss Verinder to be staying.", "나의 망설임은, 에이블화이트 부인과 버린더 아가씨가 머물고 있음을 내가 아는 런던의 그 호텔에 들르는 것으로 끝났다."], ["They informed me that they were going to Brighton the next day, and that an unexpected obstacle prevented Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite from accompanying them.", "그들은 내게, 이튿날 브라이턴으로 갈 것이며, 뜻밖의 걸림돌이 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그들과 동행하는 것을 막는다고 알려 주었다."], ["I at once proposed to take his place.", "나는 대뜸 그의 자리를 대신하겠다고 제안했다."], ["While I was only thinking of Rachel Verinder, it was possible to hesitate.", "내가 레이철 버린더를 생각하기만 할 때는, 망설이는 것이 가능했다."], ["When I actually saw her, my mind was made up directly, come what might of it, to tell her the truth.", "내가 실제로 그녀를 보자, 나의 마음은 대뜸, 그것으로 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 그녀에게 진실을 말하기로 정해졌다."]], [["I found my opportunity, when I was out walking with her, on the day after my arrival.", "나는 도착한 이튿날, 그녀와 산책을 나갔을 때 나의 기회를 찾았다."]], [["“May I speak to you,”", "\"당신에게 말해도 되겠소,\""], ["I asked, “about your marriage engagement?”", "하고 나는 물었다. \"당신의 혼약에 대해?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["she said, indifferently, “if you have nothing more interesting to talk about.”", "하고 그녀가 무심하게 말했다. \"더 흥미로운 이야깃거리가 없으시다면요.\""]], [["“Will you forgive an old friend and servant of your family, Miss Rachel, if I venture on asking whether your heart is set on this marriage?”", "\"당신 집안의 오랜 벗이자 하인을 용서해 주겠소, 레이철 아가씨, 만일 내가 감히, 당신의 마음이 이 혼인에 쏠려 있는지 물어본다면?\""]], [["“I am marrying in despair, Mr. Bruff—on the chance of dropping into some sort of stagnant happiness which may reconcile me to my life.”", "\"저는 절망 속에서 혼인하는 거예요, 브러프 씨—저를 제 삶과 화해시켜 줄지 모를 어떤 종류의 정체된 행복에 빠져들 요행을 노리고요.\""]], [["Strong language!", "센 말이었다!"], ["and suggestive of something below the surface, in the shape of a romance.", "그리고 어떤 사랑 이야기라는 모양으로, 겉면 아래 무언가가 있음을 넌지시 알려 주는 말이었다."], ["But I had my own object in view, and I declined (as we lawyers say) to pursue the question into its side issues.", "허나 나에게는 나 나름의 목적이 있었고, 나는 (우리 변호사들 말마따나) 그 물음을 그 곁가지들로 좇기를 마다했다."]], [["“Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite can hardly be of your way of thinking,”", "\"고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 좀처럼 당신과 같은 생각일 수 없겠구려.\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["“His heart must be set on the marriage at any rate?”", "\"그의 마음은 어쨌든 그 혼인에 쏠려 있음이 틀림없겠지요?\""]], [["“He says so, and I suppose I ought to believe him.", "\"그이는 그렇다고 하고, 저는 그이를 믿어야 마땅하겠죠."], ["He would hardly marry me, after what I have owned to him, unless he was fond of me.”", "제가 그이에게 털어놓은 것 뒤에도, 저를 아끼지 않는다면 그이는 좀처럼 저와 혼인하지 않을 테니까요.\""]], [["Poor thing!", "가엾은 것!"], ["the bare idea of a man marrying her for his own selfish and mercenary ends had never entered her head.", "어떤 사내가 저 자신의 이기적이고 잇속 밝은 목적으로 그녀와 혼인한다는 생각만은 그녀의 머릿속에 도무지 들어온 적이 없었던 것이다."], ["The task I had set myself began to look like a harder task than I had bargained for.", "내가 나 자신에게 지운 그 과업이 내가 어림했던 것보다 더 벅찬 과업으로 보이기 시작했다."]], [["“It sounds strangely,”", "\"이상하게 들리는구려,\""], ["I went on, “in my old-fashioned ears——”", "하고 나는 이어 갔다. \"나의 구식 귀에는——\""]], [["“What sounds strangely?”", "\"뭐가 이상하게 들려요?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“To hear you speak of your future husband as if you were not quite sure of the sincerity of his attachment.", "\"당신이 당신의 앞날의 남편에 대해, 마치 그의 애정의 진심을 아주 확신하지는 못하는 듯 말하는 것을 듣는 것이."], ["Are you conscious of any reason in your own mind for doubting him?”", "당신은 당신 자신의 마음속에서 그를 미심쩍어할 무슨 이유라도 자각하고 있소?\""]], [["Her astonishing quickness of perception, detected a change in my voice, or my manner, when I put that question, which warned her that I had been speaking all along with some ulterior object in view.", "그녀의 놀라운 지각의 예리함이, 내가 그 물음을 던질 때, 나의 목소리, 또는 나의 태도에 인 변화를 알아챘고, 그것이 그녀에게 내가 내내 어떤 숨은 목적을 품고 말해 왔음을 일깨웠다."], ["She stopped, and taking her arm out of mine, looked me searchingly in the face.", "그녀는 멈추더니, 제 팔을 내 팔에서 빼고, 나의 얼굴을 살피듯 들여다보았다."]], [["“Mr. Bruff,”", "\"브러프 씨,\""], ["she said, “you have something to tell me about Godfrey Ablewhite.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 고드프리 에이블화이트에 대해 저에게 할 말이 있어요."], ["Tell it.”", "말해 줘요.\""]], [["I knew her well enough to take her at her word.", "나는 그녀의 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들일 만큼 그녀를 잘 알았다."], ["I told it.", "나는 그것을 말했다."]], [["She put her arm again into mine, and walked on with me slowly.", "그녀는 제 팔을 다시 내 팔에 끼고, 나와 함께 천천히 걸어 나갔다."], ["I felt her hand tightening its grasp mechanically on my arm, and I saw her getting paler and paler as I went on—but, not a word passed her lips while I was speaking.", "나는 그녀의 손이 기계적으로 내 팔을 붙드는 힘을 조이는 것을 느꼈고, 내가 말을 이어 가는 동안 그녀가 점점 더 창백해지는 것을 보았다—허나, 내가 말하는 동안 그녀의 입술에서는 한마디도 나오지 않았다."], ["When I had done, she still kept silence.", "내가 말을 마쳤을 때도, 그녀는 여전히 침묵을 지켰다."], ["Her head drooped a little, and she walked by my side, unconscious of my presence, unconscious of everything about her; lost—buried, I might almost say—in her own thoughts.", "그녀의 머리가 조금 수그러졌고, 그녀는 내 존재도, 제 둘레의 모든 것도 의식하지 못한 채, 내 곁에서 걸었다. 제 자신의 생각에 잠긴—거의 파묻힌, 이라고 해도 되겠다."]], [["I made no attempt to disturb her.", "나는 그녀를 방해하려는 시도를 하지 않았다."], ["My experience of her disposition warned me, on this, as on former occasions, to give her time.", "그녀의 성향에 대한 나의 경험이, 이번에도, 예전 경우들에서처럼, 그녀에게 시간을 주라고 내게 일러 주었다."]], [["The first instinct of girls in general, on being told of anything which interests them, is to ask a multitude of questions, and then to run off, and talk it all over with some favourite friend.", "여자들 일반이, 저희에게 흥미로운 무언가를 들었을 때의 첫 본능은, 숱한 물음을 던지고, 그런 다음 달려가, 어떤 마음에 드는 벗과 그것을 죄다 이야기하는 것이다."], ["Rachel Verinder’s first instinct, under similar circumstances, was to shut herself up in her own mind, and to think it over by herself.", "비슷한 정황에서 레이철 버린더의 첫 본능은, 제 자신의 마음속에 틀어박혀, 저 혼자 그것을 곰곰이 헤아리는 것이었다."], ["This absolute self-dependence is a great virtue in a man.", "이 온전한 자립은 사내에게는 큰 미덕이다."], ["In a woman it has a serious drawback of morally separating her from the mass of her sex, and so exposing her to misconstruction by the general opinion.", "여자에게 그것은, 그녀를 도덕적으로 제 성(性)의 무리에서 갈라놓고, 그리하여 그녀를 세상 일반의 견해에 의한 오해에 내맡기는, 심각한 흠을 지닌다."], ["I strongly suspect myself of thinking as the rest of the world think in this matter—except in the case of Rachel Verinder.", "나는 이 일에서 세상 나머지가 생각하는 대로 생각하는 나 자신을 강하게 의심한다—레이철 버린더의 경우만은 빼고."], ["The self-dependence in her character, was one of its virtues in my estimation; partly, no doubt, because I sincerely admired and liked her; partly, because the view I took of her connexion with the loss of the Moonstone was based on my own special knowledge of her disposition.", "그녀의 됨됨이에 있는 그 자립은, 나의 평가에서는, 그 미덕 가운데 하나였다. 얼마쯤은, 의심할 것 없이, 내가 그녀를 진심으로 감탄하고 좋아했기 때문이고, 얼마쯤은, 월장석의 분실에 대한 그녀의 관련에 대해 내가 취한 견해가 그녀의 성향에 대한 나 자신의 각별한 앎에 바탕을 두었기 때문이다."], ["Badly as appearances might look, in the matter of the Diamond—shocking as it undoubtedly was to know that she was associated in any way with the mystery of an undiscovered theft—I was satisfied nevertheless that she had done nothing unworthy of her, because I was also satisfied that she had not stirred a step in the business, without shutting herself up in her own mind, and thinking it over first.", "그 다이아몬드 문제에서 겉모습이 아무리 고약해 보여도—그녀가 어떤 식으로든 발각되지 않은 도둑질의 수수께끼에 엮여 있음을 아는 것이 의심할 것 없이 아무리 충격적이어도—그럼에도 나는 그녀가 저답지 못한 짓은 아무것도 하지 않았다고 흡족히 여겼는데, 나는 또한 그녀가 그 일에서 한 걸음도 움직이지 않되, 먼저 제 자신의 마음속에 틀어박혀, 그것을 곰곰이 헤아리지 않고서는 그리하지 않았다고 흡족히 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["We had walked on, for nearly a mile I should say, before Rachel roused herself.", "우리는, 내가 말하기로 거의 일 마일을 걸어왔을 때에야, 레이철이 스스로를 추슬렀다."], ["She suddenly looked up at me with a faint reflection of her smile of happier times—the most irresistible smile I have ever seen on a woman’s face.", "그녀는 별안간 나를 올려다보았는데, 더 행복했던 시절의 미소가 희미하게 어렸다—내가 여태껏 여자의 얼굴에서 본 가장 거스를 수 없는 미소였다."]], [["“I owe much already to your kindness,”", "\"저는 이미 당신의 친절에 많은 빚을 졌어요.\""], ["she said.", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["“And I feel more deeply indebted to it now than ever.", "\"그리고 저는 이제 그 어느 때보다 더 깊이 그것에 빚졌음을 느껴요."], ["If you hear any rumours of my marriage when you get back to London, contradict them at once, on my authority.”", "만일 당신이 런던에 돌아가서 제 혼인에 대한 어떤 소문이라도 듣거든, 제 이름을 내세워, 대뜸 그것을 반박해 줘요.\""]], [["“Have you resolved to break your engagement?”", "\"당신은 당신의 약혼을 깨기로 결심했소?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Can you doubt it?”", "\"의심할 수 있어요?\""], ["she returned proudly, “after what you have told me!”", "하고 그녀가 자랑스레 되받았다. \"당신이 저에게 말해 준 것 뒤에!\""]], [["“My dear Miss Rachel, you are very young—and you may find more difficulty in withdrawing from your present position than you anticipate.", "\"나의 사랑하는 레이철 아가씨, 당신은 아주 젊소—그리고 당신은 당신의 지금 처지에서 물러나는 데에 당신이 어림하는 것보다 더 큰 어려움을 겪을지 모르오."], ["Have you no one—I mean a lady, of course—whom you could consult?”", "당신에게는 아무도 없소—물론 부인 말이오—당신이 상의할 수 있는?\""]], [["“No one,”", "\"아무도 없어요.\""], ["she answered.", "하고 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["It distressed me, it did indeed distress me, to hear her say that.", "그녀가 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣는 것이 나를 괴롭혔다, 정말로 나를 괴롭혔다."], ["She was so young and so lonely—and she bore it so well!", "그녀는 어찌나 젊고 어찌나 외로운지—그리고 그것을 어찌나 잘 견디는지!"], ["The impulse to help her got the better of any sense of my own unfitness which I might have felt under the circumstances; and I stated such ideas on the subject as occurred to me on the spur of the moment, to the best of my ability.", "그녀를 도우려는 충동이, 그 정황에서 내가 느꼈을 법한 나 자신의 부적격의 느낌을 이겼다. 그리고 나는 그 화제에 대해 그 순간 문득 떠오르는 생각들을 내 힘닿는 한 밝혔다."], ["I have advised a prodigious number of clients, and have dealt with some exceedingly awkward difficulties, in my time.", "나는 내 시절에 엄청난 수의 의뢰인에게 조언했고, 몇몇 지극히 껄끄러운 어려움을 다루어 왔다."], ["But this was the first occasion on which I had ever found myself advising a young lady how to obtain her release from a marriage engagement.", "허나 이것은 젊은 아가씨에게 혼약에서 벗어날 방도를 조언하는 나 자신을 발견한 첫 경우였다."], ["The suggestion I offered amounted briefly to this.", "내가 내놓은 제안은 간단히 이런 것이었다."], ["I recommended her to tell Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite—at a private interview, of course—that he had, to her certain knowledge, betrayed the mercenary nature of the motive on his side.", "나는 그녀에게, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게—물론, 단둘의 면담에서—그가, 그녀가 확실히 아는 바로, 제 편의 그 잇속 밝은 동기의 본색을 드러냈다고 말하라 권했다."], ["She was then to add that their marriage, after what she had discovered, was a simple impossibility—and she was to put it to him, whether he thought it wisest to secure her silence by falling in with her views, or to force her, by opposing them, to make the motive under which she was acting generally known.", "그런 다음 그녀는, 그녀가 알아낸 것 뒤에, 그들의 혼인은 그저 있을 수 없는 일이라고 덧붙이고—그에게, 그녀의 견해에 따름으로써 그녀의 침묵을 확보하는 것이 가장 슬기롭다고 여기는지, 아니면 그것에 맞섬으로써 그녀로 하여금 그녀가 따르는 그 동기를 세상에 널리 알리게 하는 것이 가장 슬기롭다고 여기는지 물어야 했다."], ["If he attempted to defend himself, or to deny the facts, she was, in that event, to refer him to me.", "만일 그가 저를 변호하려 하거나, 그 사실들을 부인하려 하면, 그녀는, 그 경우에, 그를 나에게 보내야 했다."]], [["Miss Verinder listened attentively till I had done.", "버린더 아가씨는 내가 마칠 때까지 주의 깊게 들었다."], ["She then thanked me very prettily for my advice, but informed me at the same time that it was impossible for her to follow it.", "그런 다음 그녀는 나의 조언에 아주 어여쁘게 감사했으나, 동시에 그것을 따르기가 저로서는 있을 수 없는 일이라고 내게 알렸다."]], [["“May I ask,”", "\"물어봐도 되겠소,\""], ["I said, “what objection you see to following it?”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"그것을 따르는 데에 무슨 반대되는 점이 보이는지?\""]], [["She hesitated—and then met me with a question on her side.", "그녀는 망설였다—그런 다음 제 편에서 물음으로 나를 맞았다."]], [["“Suppose you were asked to express your opinion of Mr.", "\"만일 당신이 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 처신에 대해 당신의 의견을 밝히라는 청을 받는다고 해 봐요.\""], ["Godfrey Ablewhite’s conduct?” she began.", "하고 그녀가 말문을 열었다."]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래서요?\""]], [["“What would you call it?”", "\"당신은 그것을 뭐라 부르겠어요?\""]], [["“I should call it the conduct of a meanly deceitful man.”", "\"나는 그것을 비열하게 남을 속이는 사내의 처신이라 부르겠소.\""]], [["“Mr. Bruff!", "\"브러프 씨!"], ["I have believed in that man.", "저는 그 사내를 믿었어요."], ["I have promised to marry that man.", "저는 그 사내와 혼인하겠다고 약속했고요."], ["How can I tell him he is mean, how can I tell him he has deceived me, how can I disgrace him in the eyes of the world after that?", "그런데 어떻게 제가 그이에게 비열하다고 말하겠어요, 어떻게 그이가 저를 속였다고 말하겠어요, 어떻게 제가 그 뒤에 세상의 눈앞에서 그이를 망신시키겠어요?"], ["I have degraded myself by ever thinking of him as my husband.", "저는 그이를 제 남편으로 생각한 것만으로도 저 자신을 격 떨어뜨렸어요."], ["If I say what you tell me to say to him—I am owning that I have degraded myself to his face.", "만일 제가 당신이 그이에게 말하라는 것을 말한다면—저는 제가 저 자신을 그이의 면전에서 격 떨어뜨렸음을 인정하는 거예요."], ["I can’t do that.", "저는 그럴 수 없어요."], ["After what has passed between us, I can’t do that!", "우리 사이에 오간 것 뒤에, 저는 그럴 수 없어요!"], ["The shame of it would be nothing to him.", "그것의 수치는 그이에게는 아무것도 아니겠죠."], ["But the shame of it would be unendurable to me.”", "하지만 그것의 수치는 저로서는 견딜 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["Here was another of the marked peculiarities in her character disclosing itself to me without reserve.", "여기 그녀의 됨됨이에 있는 그 두드러진 별난 점들 가운데 또 하나가 거리낌 없이 내게 저를 드러내고 있었다."], ["Here was her sensitive horror of the bare contact with anything mean, blinding her to every consideration of what she owed to herself, hurrying her into a false position which might compromise her in the estimation of all her friends!", "여기 비열한 무엇과의 그저 스치는 접촉에 대한 그녀의 예민한 소름 끼침이, 그녀가 자신에게 진 것에 대한 온갖 헤아림에 그녀를 눈멀게 하고, 그녀를 그녀의 온 벗들의 평가에서 그녀에게 해가 될지 모를 그릇된 처지로 몰아넣고 있었다!"], ["Up to this time, I had been a little diffident about the propriety of the advice I had given to her.", "이때까지, 나는 내가 그녀에게 준 조언의 온당함에 대해 조금 자신이 없었다."], ["But, after what she had just said, I had no sort of doubt that it was the best advice that could have been offered; and I felt no sort of hesitation in pressing it on her again.", "허나, 그녀가 방금 한 말 뒤로, 나는 그것이 내놓을 수 있었던 가장 좋은 조언이라는 데 아무런 의심도 없었다. 그리고 나는 그것을 그녀에게 다시 밀어붙이는 데 아무런 망설임도 느끼지 않았다."]], [["She only shook her head, and repeated her objection in other words.", "그녀는 그저 고개를 젓고, 다른 말로 제 반대를 되풀이했다."]], [["“He has been intimate enough with me to ask me to be his wife.", "\"그이는 저에게 제 아내가 되어 달라 청할 만큼 저와 가까웠어요."], ["He has stood high enough in my estimation to obtain my consent.", "그이는 제 승낙을 얻을 만큼 제 평가에서 높이 서 있었고요."], ["I can’t tell him to his face that he is the most contemptible of living creatures, after that!”", "그런데 그 뒤에 그이의 면전에서, 그이가 살아 있는 것들 가운데 가장 경멸스러운 자라고 말할 수는 없어요!\""]], [["“But, my dear Miss Rachel,”", "\"하지만, 나의 사랑하는 레이철 아가씨,\""], ["I remonstrated, “it’s equally impossible for you to tell him that you withdraw from your engagement without giving some reason for it.”", "하고 나는 타일렀다. \"그것에 대한 아무 이유도 대지 않고 당신의 약혼에서 물러나겠다고 그에게 말하는 것도 꼭 그만큼 있을 수 없는 일이오.\""]], [["“I shall say that I have thought it over, and that I am satisfied it will be best for both of us if we part.”", "\"저는 곰곰이 헤아려 보았으며, 우리가 헤어지는 것이 우리 둘 다에게 가장 좋으리라 흡족히 여긴다고 말하겠어요.\""]], [["“No more than that?”", "\"그 이상은 아니고?\""]], [["“No more.”", "\"그 이상은 아니고요.\""]], [["“Have you thought of what he may say, on his side?”", "\"그가 제 편에서 무슨 말을 할지 생각해 봤소?\""]], [["“He may say what he pleases.”", "\"그이는 제 좋을 대로 말하면 돼요.\""]], [["It was impossible not to admire her delicacy and her resolution, and it was equally impossible not to feel that she was putting herself in the wrong.", "그녀의 섬세함과 그녀의 결의에 감탄하지 않기란 있을 수 없는 일이었고, 그녀가 자신을 그릇된 처지에 놓고 있음을 느끼지 않기도 꼭 그만큼 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["I entreated her to consider her own position.", "나는 그녀에게 그녀 자신의 처지를 헤아려 보라고 간청했다."], ["I reminded her that she would be exposing herself to the most odious misconstruction of her motives.", "나는 그녀에게, 그녀가 자신의 동기에 대한 더없이 얄미운 오해에 저를 내맡기게 되리라는 것을 상기시켰다."], ["“You can’t brave public opinion,”", "\"당신은 세상의 견해에 맞설 수 없소,\""], ["I said, “at the command of private feeling.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"사사로운 감정의 명령에 따라서는.\""]], [["“I can,”", "\"할 수 있어요.\""], ["she answered.", "하고 그녀가 대답했다."], ["“I have done it already.”", "\"저는 이미 그리했는걸요.\""]], [["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["“You have forgotten the Moonstone, Mr.", "\"당신은 월장석을 잊으셨어요, 브러프 씨."], ["Bruff. Have I not braved public opinion, there, with my own private reasons for it?”", "제가 거기서, 그것에 대한 저 나름의 남모를 이유를 품고, 세상의 견해에 맞서지 않았던가요?\""]], [["Her answer silenced me for the moment.", "그녀의 대답이 그 순간 나를 잠잠하게 했다."], ["It set me trying to trace the explanation of her conduct, at the time of the loss of the Moonstone, out of the strange avowal which had just escaped her.", "그것은 나로 하여금, 방금 그녀에게서 새어 나온 그 이상한 실토에서, 월장석 분실 때의 그녀의 처신에 대한 설명을 더듬어 찾으려 애쓰게 했다."], ["I might perhaps have done it when I was younger.", "나는 더 젊었을 때라면 어쩌면 그것을 해냈으리라."], ["I certainly couldn’t do it now.", "나는 이제는 분명 그것을 해낼 수 없었다."]], [["I tried a last remonstrance before we returned to the house.", "나는 우리가 집으로 돌아가기 전에 마지막 훈계를 시도했다."], ["She was just as immovable as ever.", "그녀는 그 어느 때처럼 꿈쩍도 하지 않았다."], ["My mind was in a strange conflict of feelings about her when I left her that day.", "그날 내가 그녀를 떠날 때 나의 마음은 그녀에 대한 이상한 감정의 갈등 속에 있었다. 그녀는 고집스러웠다."], ["She was obstinate; she was wrong.", "그녀는 틀렸다. 그녀는 흥미로웠다."], ["She was interesting; she was admirable; she was deeply to be pitied.", "그녀는 감탄스러웠다. 그녀는 깊이 가엾이 여길 만했다."], ["I made her promise to write to me the moment she had any news to send.", "나는 그녀에게, 보낼 소식이 생기는 즉시 나에게 편지를 쓰겠다고 약속하게 했다."], ["And I went back to my business in London, with a mind exceedingly ill at ease.", "그리고 나는 마음이 지극히 편치 못한 채, 런던의 내 볼일로 돌아갔다."]], [["On the evening of my return, before it was possible for me to receive my promised letter, I was surprised by a visit from Mr. Ablewhite the elder, and was informed that Mr. Godfrey had got his dismissal—and had accepted it—that very day.", "내가 돌아온 그날 저녁, 내가 약속받은 편지를 받는 것이 가능하기도 전에, 나는 노 에이블화이트 씨의 방문에 놀랐고, 고드프리 씨가 바로 그날 해고당했으며—그것을 받아들였다는 것을 전해 들었다."]], [["With the view I already took of the case, the bare fact stated in the words that I have underlined, revealed Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s motive for submission as plainly as if he had acknowledged it himself.", "내가 그 경우에 대해 이미 취한 견해로 보아, 내가 밑줄 친 그 말로 밝혀진 그 담담한 사실은, 마치 그가 몸소 그것을 인정한 것처럼 뻔하게, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 순순히 따른 동기를 드러냈다."], ["He needed a large sum of money; and he needed it by a given time.", "그는 큰돈이 필요했다. 그리고 그는 그것이 정해진 때까지 필요했다."], ["Rachel’s income, which would have helped him to anything else, would not help him here; and Rachel had accordingly released herself, without encountering a moment’s serious opposition on his part.", "다른 무엇에라면 그를 도왔을 레이철의 수입이, 여기서는 그를 돕지 못할 터였다. 그리하여 레이철은, 그의 편에서 한순간의 진지한 반대도 마주치지 않고, 스스로를 풀어 준 것이었다."], ["If I am told that this is a mere speculation, I ask, in my turn, what other theory will account for his giving up a marriage which would have maintained him in splendour for the rest of his life?", "만일 누군가 이것이 한낱 억측이라고 나에게 말한다면, 나는, 내 차례로, 다른 어떤 이론이 그가 제 남은 평생을 호화롭게 지탱해 주었을 혼인을 포기한 것을 설명해 주겠느냐고 묻는다."]], [["Any exultation I might otherwise have felt at the lucky turn which things had now taken, was effectually checked by what passed at my interview with old Mr. Ablewhite.", "일들이 이제 취한 그 다행스러운 방향 전환에 내가 안 그랬다면 느꼈을 법한 어떤 득의양양함도, 노 에이블화이트 씨와의 면담에서 오간 것으로 인해 효과 있게 억눌러졌다."]], [["He came, of course, to know whether I could give him any explanation of Miss Verinder’s extraordinary conduct.", "그는, 물론, 버린더 아가씨의 예사롭지 않은 처신에 대해 내가 무슨 설명이라도 해 줄 수 있는지 알러 왔다."], ["It is needless to say that I was quite unable to afford him the information he wanted.", "내가 그가 원하는 정보를 도무지 줄 수 없었음은 말할 것도 없다."], ["The annoyance which I thus inflicted, following on the irritation produced by a recent interview with his son, threw Mr.", "내가 이렇게 끼친 언짢음이, 제 아들과의 최근 면담이 자아낸 짜증에 뒤이어, 에이블화이트 씨의 경계를 무너뜨렸다."], ["Ablewhite off his guard. Both his looks and his language convinced me that Miss Verinder would find him a merciless man to deal with, when he joined the ladies at Brighton the next day.", "그의 낯빛도 그의 말도, 버린더 아가씨가 이튿날 브라이턴에서 그 부인들과 합류하면 그를 상대하기에 무자비한 사내로 알게 되리라는 것을 나에게 납득시켰다."]], [["I had a restless night, considering what I ought to do next.", "나는 다음에 내가 무엇을 해야 할지 헤아리며, 편치 못한 밤을 보냈다."], ["How my reflections ended, and how thoroughly well founded my distrust of Mr. Ablewhite proved to be, are items of information which (as I am told) have already been put tidily in their proper places, by that exemplary person, Miss Clack.", "나의 상념이 어떻게 끝났는지, 그리고 에이블화이트 씨에 대한 나의 불신이 얼마나 철저히 근거 있는 것으로 드러났는지는 (내가 듣기로는) 그 모범적인 사람 클랙 양에 의해 이미 알맞은 자리에 말끔히 놓인 정보의 항목들이다."], ["I have only to add—in completion of her narrative—that Miss Verinder found the quiet and repose which she sadly needed, poor thing, in my house at Hampstead.", "나는 다만—그녀의 이야기를 마무리하며—버린더 아가씨가 제 서글피 필요로 하던 고요와 안식을, 가엾은 것, 햄스테드의 내 집에서 찾았음을 덧붙일 뿐이다."], ["She honoured us by making a long stay.", "그녀는 오래 머묾으로써 우리를 영예롭게 했다."], ["My wife and daughters were charmed with her; and, when the executors decided on the appointment of a new guardian, I feel sincere pride and pleasure in recording that my guest and my family parted like old friends, on either side.", "나의 아내와 딸들은 그녀에게 매혹되었다. 그리고 유언 집행인들이 새 후견인의 임명을 정했을 때, 나는 나의 손님과 나의 식구가 양편에서 오랜 벗처럼 헤어졌음을 적는 데에 진심 어린 자랑과 기쁨을 느낀다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["The next thing I have to do, is to present such additional information as I possess on the subject of the Moonstone, or, to speak more correctly, on the subject of the Indian plot to steal the Diamond.", "내가 다음으로 해야 할 일은, 월장석이라는 화제에 대해—더 정확히 말하자면, 그 다이아몬드를 훔치려는 인도인의 음모라는 화제에 대해—내가 지닌 그런 추가 정보를 내놓는 것이다."], ["The little that I have to tell is (as I think I have already said) of some importance, nevertheless, in respect of its bearing very remarkably on events which are still to come.", "내가 이야기할 것은 비록 얼마 안 되나 (내 생각에 이미 말했듯이), 그럼에도, 아직 다가올 일들에 아주 두드러지게 관련된다는 점에서, 얼마쯤 중요하다."]], [["About a week or ten days after Miss Verinder had left us, one of my clerks entered the private room at my office, with a card in his hand, and informed me that a gentleman was below, who wanted to speak to me.", "버린더 아가씨가 우리를 떠난 지 한 주쯤, 또는 열흘쯤 지났을 때, 내 서기 하나가 손에 명함 한 장을 들고 내 사무소의 개인실로 들어와, 아래층에 나와 이야기하고 싶어 하는 신사 한 분이 와 있다고 알렸다."]], [["I looked at the card.", "나는 그 명함을 보았다."], ["There was a foreign name written on it, which has escaped my memory.", "그 위에는 어느 외국 이름이 적혀 있었는데, 그것은 내 기억에서 사라졌다."], ["It was followed by a line written in English at the bottom of the card, which I remember perfectly well:", "그 뒤로, 명함 아래쪽에 영어로 적힌 한 줄이 이어졌는데, 그것을 나는 아주 또렷이 기억한다."]], [["“Recommended by Mr. Septimus Luker.”", "\"셉티머스 루커 씨의 추천으로.\""]], [["The audacity of a person in Mr. Luker’s position presuming to recommend anybody to me, took me so completely by surprise, that I sat silent for the moment, wondering whether my own eyes had not deceived me.", "루커 씨 같은 처지의 사람이 감히 누군가를 나에게 추천하다니, 그 뻔뻔함이 나를 어찌나 완전히 놀라게 했던지, 나는 잠시 잠자코 앉아, 내 두 눈이 나를 속인 것은 아닌가 의아해했다."], ["The clerk, observing my bewilderment, favoured me with the result of his own observation of the stranger who was waiting downstairs.", "그 서기는, 나의 어리둥절함을 알아채고, 아래층에서 기다리는 그 낯선 이에 대한 제 나름의 관찰의 결과를 나에게 베풀어 주었다."]], [["“He is rather a remarkable-looking man, sir.", "\"제법 눈에 띄는 생김새의 분입니다, 나리."], ["So dark in the complexion that we all set him down in the office for an Indian, or something of that sort.”", "낯빛이 어찌나 거무스름한지 우리는 사무소에서 죄다 그를 인도인이거나, 그 비슷한 무엇으로 여겼습니다.\""]], [["Associating the clerk’s idea with the line inscribed on the card in my hand, I thought it possible that the Moonstone might be at the bottom of Mr. Luker’s recommendation, and of the stranger’s visit at my office.", "내 손에 든 명함에 적힌 그 줄에 그 서기의 생각을 이어 붙여, 나는 월장석이 루커 씨의 추천과, 그 낯선 이의 내 사무소 방문의 밑바탕에 있을지도 모른다고 생각했다."], ["To the astonishment of my clerk, I at once decided on granting an interview to the gentleman below.", "내 서기가 깜짝 놀라게도, 나는 대뜸 아래층의 그 신사에게 면담을 허락하기로 정했다."]], [["In justification of the highly unprofessional sacrifice to mere curiosity which I thus made, permit me to remind anybody who may read these lines, that no living person (in England, at any rate) can claim to have had such an intimate connexion with the romance of the Indian Diamond as mine has been.", "내가 이렇듯 한낱 호기심에 바친 그 지극히 비직업적인 희생을 변명하고자, 이 글줄들을 읽을지 모를 누구에게든 상기시키는 것을 허락해 주기 바란다. 살아 있는 어떤 사람도 (적어도 잉글랜드에서는) 그 인도 다이아몬드의 기담과 나만큼 그리도 가까운 연을 맺었다고 주장할 수 없다는 것을."], ["I was trusted with the secret of Colonel Herncastle’s plan for escaping assassination.", "나는 헌캐슬 대령의 암살 모면 계획의 비밀을 맡았다."], ["I received the Colonel’s letters, periodically reporting himself a living man.", "나는 대령이 제가 살아 있는 사람임을 주기적으로 알려 오는 편지들을 받았다."], ["I drew his Will, leaving the Moonstone to Miss Verinder.", "나는 월장석을 버린더 아가씨에게 남기는 그의 유언장을 작성했다."], ["I persuaded his executor to act, on the chance that the jewel might prove to be a valuable acquisition to the family.", "나는 그 보석이 그 집안에 값진 소득으로 드러날 요행을 노려, 그 유언 집행인을 움직이도록 설득했다."], ["And, lastly, I combated Mr. Franklin Blake’s scruples, and induced him to be the means of transporting the Diamond to Lady Verinder’s house.", "그리고, 끝으로, 나는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 망설임에 맞서, 그가 그 다이아몬드를 레이디 버린더의 집으로 옮기는 수단이 되게끔 이끌었다."], ["If anyone can claim a prescriptive right of interest in the Moonstone, and in everything connected with it, I think it is hardly to be denied that I am the man.", "만일 누군가 월장석에 대해, 그리고 그것에 얽힌 모든 것에 대해 시효로 얻은 관심의 권리를 주장할 수 있다면, 그것이 나라는 것은 좀처럼 부인될 수 없으리라고 나는 생각한다."]], [["The moment my mysterious client was shown in, I felt an inner conviction that I was in the presence of one of the three Indians—probably of the chief.", "나의 수수께끼 같은 의뢰인이 안으로 안내되어 든 그 순간, 나는 내가 그 인도인 셋 가운데 하나—아마도 그 우두머리—의 앞에 있다는 속으로부터의 확신을 느꼈다."], ["He was carefully dressed in European costume.", "그는 유럽식 복장을 정성 들여 차려입고 있었다."], ["But his swarthy complexion, his long lithe figure, and his grave and graceful politeness of manner were enough to betray his Oriental origin to any intelligent eyes that looked at him.", "허나 그의 거무스름한 낯빛, 그의 길고 나긋한 몸매, 그리고 그의 무겁고도 우아한 몸가짐의 정중함은, 그를 바라보는 어떤 지각 있는 눈에게든 그의 동양 태생을 드러내기에 넉넉했다."]], [["I pointed to a chair, and begged to be informed of the nature of his business with me.", "나는 의자 하나를 가리키고, 그가 나와 볼 일의 성격을 알려 주기를 청했다."]], [["After first apologising—in an excellent selection of English words—for the liberty which he had taken in disturbing me, the Indian produced a small parcel the outer covering of which was of cloth of gold.", "먼저—더없이 잘 고른 영어 낱말들로—나를 방해하는 무람없음을 저질렀음을 사과한 뒤, 그 인도인은 겉을 금실 천으로 감싼 작은 꾸러미 하나를 꺼냈다."], ["Removing this and a second wrapping of some silken fabric, he placed a little box, or casket, on my table, most beautifully and richly inlaid in jewels, on an ebony ground.", "이것과, 어떤 비단 천으로 된 두 번째 싸개를 벗기고서, 그는 내 탁자 위에 작은 상자, 곧 함(函) 하나를 놓았는데, 그것은 흑단 바탕에 보석이 더없이 아름답고 화려하게 박혀 있었다."]], [["“I have come, sir,”", "\"저는 왔습니다, 선생.\""], ["he said, “to ask you to lend me some money.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신께 얼마간의 돈을 빌려 달라고 청하러요."], ["And I leave this as an assurance to you that my debt will be paid back.”", "그리고 저는 제 빚이 갚아지리라는 보증으로 이것을 당신께 맡겨 두겠습니다.\""]], [["I pointed to his card.", "나는 그의 명함을 가리켰다."], ["“And you apply to me,”", "\"그래서 당신은 나에게 청하는 거요,\""], ["I rejoined, “at Mr.", "하고 나는 되받았다."], ["Luker’s recommendation?”", "\"루커 씨의 추천으로?\""]], [["The Indian bowed.", "그 인도인은 고개 숙여 인사했다."]], [["“May I ask how it is that Mr. Luker himself did not advance the money that you require?”", "\"물어봐도 되겠소, 어찌하여 루커 씨 자신은 당신이 필요로 하는 돈을 내주지 않았는지?\""]], [["“Mr. Luker informed me, sir, that he had no money to lend.”", "\"루커 씨는 저에게, 선생, 빌려줄 돈이 없다고 알렸습니다.\""]], [["“And so he recommended you to come to me?”", "\"그래서 그가 당신더러 나에게 오라고 추천했다?\""]], [["The Indian, in his turn, pointed to the card.", "그 인도인은, 제 차례로, 그 명함을 가리켰다."], ["“It is written there,”", "\"거기에 적혀 있습니다.\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["Briefly answered, and thoroughly to the purpose!", "짧게 대답하되, 더없이 요점에 들어맞았다!"], ["If the Moonstone had been in my possession, this Oriental gentleman would have murdered me, I am well aware, without a moment’s hesitation.", "만일 월장석이 내 수중에 있었더라면, 이 동양 신사는 한순간의 망설임도 없이 나를 죽였으리라는 것을, 나는 잘 안다."], ["At the same time, and barring that slight drawback, I am bound to testify that he was the perfect model of a client.", "동시에, 그 자잘한 흠 하나만 빼면, 나는 그가 완벽한 의뢰인의 본보기였음을 증언하지 않을 수 없다."], ["He might not have respected my life.", "그는 나의 목숨은 존중하지 않았을지 모른다."], ["But he did what none of my own countrymen had ever done, in all my experience of them—he respected my time.", "허나 그는, 내가 그들을 겪은 온 경험에서 내 동포 가운데 아무도 여태껏 하지 않은 일을 했다—그는 나의 시간을 존중했다."]], [["“I am sorry,”", "\"미안하오,\""], ["I said, “that you should have had the trouble of coming to me.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"당신이 나에게 오는 수고를 겪어야 했다니."], ["Mr. Luker is quite mistaken in sending you here.", "루커 씨가 당신을 여기로 보낸 것은 아주 잘못한 일이오."], ["I am trusted, like other men in my profession, with money to lend.", "나는, 내 직업의 다른 사람들처럼, 빌려줄 돈을 맡고 있소."], ["But I never lend it to strangers, and I never lend it on such a security as you have produced.”", "허나 나는 결코 그것을 낯선 이에게 빌려주지 않으며, 결코 당신이 내놓은 그런 담보로 빌려주지 않소.\""]], [["Far from attempting, as other people would have done, to induce me to relax my own rules, the Indian only made me another bow, and wrapped up his box in its two coverings without a word of protest.", "다른 사람들이라면 그랬을 법하게, 내가 내 규칙을 늦추도록 꾀려 애쓰기는커녕, 그 인도인은 나에게 다시 한 번 고개 숙여 인사했을 뿐이고, 한마디 항변도 없이 제 상자를 그 두 싸개로 도로 쌌다."], ["He rose—this admirable assassin rose to go, the moment I had answered him!", "그는 일어섰다—이 감탄스러운 자객은, 내가 그에게 대답한 그 순간, 떠나려고 일어섰다!"]], [["“Will your condescension towards a stranger, excuse my asking one question,”", "\"낯선 이에게 베푸시는 당신의 너그러움이, 제가 한 가지 물음을 여쭙는 것을 용서해 주겠습니까,\""], ["he said, “before I take my leave?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"제가 물러가기 전에?\""]], [["I bowed on my side.", "나도 내 편에서 고개 숙여 인사했다."], ["Only one question at parting!", "헤어질 때 물음이 오직 하나라니!"], ["The average in my experience was fifty.", "내 경험에서 그 평균은 쉰이었다."]], [["“Supposing, sir, it had been possible (and customary) for you to lend me the money,”", "\"이를테면, 선생, 당신이 저에게 그 돈을 빌려주는 것이 가능했다(그리고 관례였다)고 한다면,\""], ["he said, “in what space of time would it have been possible (and customary) for me to pay it back?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"제가 그것을 갚는 것이 가능한(그리고 관례인) 것은 얼마만 한 기간 안이겠습니까?\""]], [["“According to the usual course pursued in this country,”", "\"이 나라에서 밟는 통상의 절차에 따르면,\""], ["I answered, “you would have been entitled to pay the money back (if you liked) in one year’s time from the date at which it was first advanced to you.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"당신은 그 돈이 당신에게 처음 내주어진 날로부터 한 해 안에 (당신이 원한다면) 그것을 갚을 권리가 있게 될 거요.\""]], [["The Indian made me a last bow, the lowest of all—and suddenly and softly walked out of the room.", "그 인도인은 나에게 마지막 인사를, 그중 가장 깊은 인사를 하고는—별안간, 그리고 소리 없이 방을 걸어 나갔다."]], [["It was done in a moment, in a noiseless, supple, cat-like way, which a little startled me, I own.", "그것은 한순간에, 소리 없고, 나긋하고, 고양이 같은 몸놀림으로 이루어졌는데, 그것이 나를 조금 놀라게 했음을 나는 인정한다."], ["As soon as I was composed enough to think, I arrived at one distinct conclusion in reference to the otherwise incomprehensible visitor who had favoured me with a call.", "생각할 만큼 마음을 추스르자마자, 나는 그러지 않고서는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는, 나를 찾아 준 그 방문객에 관해 한 가지 또렷한 결론에 다다랐다."]], [["His face, voice, and manner—while I was in his company—were under such perfect control that they set all scrutiny at defiance.", "그의 얼굴, 목소리, 그리고 몸가짐은—내가 그와 함께 있는 동안—어찌나 완벽히 통제되어 있던지, 온갖 뜯어봄을 무색게 했다."], ["But he had given me one chance of looking under the smooth outer surface of him, for all that.", "허나 그럼에도 그는 나에게 그의 매끄러운 겉면 아래를 들여다볼 기회를 한 번 주었다."], ["He had not shown the slightest sign of attempting to fix anything that I had said to him in his mind, until I mentioned the time at which it was customary to permit the earliest repayment, on the part of a debtor, of money that had been advanced as a loan.", "그는, 빚진 이 편에서 대여로 내준 돈을 가장 이르게 갚는 것을 허락하는 것이 관례인 때를 내가 언급하기 전까지는, 내가 그에게 한 말을 무엇 하나 제 마음에 새겨 두려 애쓰는 기색을 조금도 보이지 않았다."], ["When I gave him that piece of information, he looked me straight in the face, while I was speaking, for the first time.", "내가 그에게 그 정보를 주었을 때, 그는, 내가 말하는 동안, 처음으로 나의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보았다."], ["The inference I drew from this was—that he had a special purpose in asking me his last question, and a special interest in hearing my answer to it.", "이것에서 내가 이끌어 낸 추론은—그가 그 마지막 물음을 나에게 던진 데에 각별한 목적이 있었고, 그 답을 듣는 데에 각별한 관심이 있었다는 것이다."], ["The more carefully I reflected on what had passed between us, the more shrewdly I suspected the production of the casket, and the application for the loan, of having been mere formalities, designed to pave the way for the parting inquiry addressed to me.", "우리 사이에 오간 것을 더 꼼꼼히 곱씹을수록, 나는 그 함을 꺼낸 것과, 그 대여의 청이, 나에게 던져진 그 이별의 물음으로 가는 길을 닦으려고 꾀한 한낱 형식에 지나지 않았음을 더 날카롭게 의심했다."]], [["I had satisfied myself of the correctness of this conclusion—and was trying to get on a step further, and penetrate the Indian’s motives next—when a letter was brought to me, which proved to be from no less a person that Mr. Septimus Luker himself.", "나는 이 결론의 옳음을 스스로 흡족히 여겼고—한 걸음 더 나아가, 다음으로 그 인도인의 동기를 꿰뚫어 보려 애쓰고 있었는데—그때 나에게 편지 한 통이 왔고, 그것은 다름 아닌 셉티머스 루커 씨 자신에게서 온 것으로 드러났다."], ["He asked my pardon in terms of sickening servility, and assured me that he could explain matters to my satisfaction, if I would honour him by consenting to a personal interview.", "그는 역겨우리만치 굽실거리는 말투로 나의 용서를 구했고, 만일 내가 직접 만나는 데 동의하는 영예를 베풀어 준다면 자기가 나를 흡족하게끔 일들을 설명할 수 있다고 장담했다."]], [["I made another unprofessional sacrifice to mere curiosity.", "나는 한낱 호기심에 또 한 번 비직업적인 희생을 바쳤다."], ["I honoured him by making an appointment at my office, for the next day.", "나는 이튿날 내 사무소에서 약속을 잡음으로써 그에게 영예를 베풀었다."]], [["Mr. Luker was, in every respect, such an inferior creature to the Indian—he was so vulgar, so ugly, so cringing, and so prosy—that he is quite unworthy of being reported, at any length, in these pages.", "루커 씨는, 온갖 면에서, 그 인도인에 견주면 어찌나 못난 인간이던지—어찌나 상스럽고, 어찌나 흉하고, 어찌나 굽실대고, 어찌나 따분하던지—그는 이 쪽들에서 조금이라도 길게 전할 값어치가 도무지 없다."], ["The substance of what he had to tell me may be fairly stated as follows:", "그가 나에게 이야기해야 했던 것의 알맹이는 대체로 다음과 같이 밝힐 수 있다."]], [["The day before I had received the visit of the Indian, Mr. Luker had been favoured with a call from that accomplished gentleman.", "내가 그 인도인의 방문을 받기 전날, 루커 씨는 그 능란한 신사의 방문을 받았었다."], ["In spite of his European disguise, Mr. Luker had instantly identified his visitor with the chief of the three Indians, who had formerly annoyed him by loitering about his house, and who had left him no alternative but to consult a magistrate.", "그의 유럽식 변장에도, 루커 씨는 대뜸 제 방문객을, 예전에 제 집 둘레를 어슬렁거리며 그를 성가시게 했고, 그로 하여금 치안판사에게 상의하는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없게 만든 그 인도인 셋의 우두머리로 알아보았다."], ["From this startling discovery he had rushed to the conclusion (naturally enough I own) that he must certainly be in the company of one of the three men, who had blindfolded him, gagged him, and robbed him of his banker’s receipt.", "이 소스라칠 발견에서 그는 (당연하게도, 나는 인정한다) 자기가 틀림없이, 저의 눈을 가리고, 저의 입을 틀어막고, 저의 은행 영수증을 빼앗은 그 세 사내 가운데 하나와 함께 있는 것이 분명하다는 결론으로 내달았다."], ["The result was that he became quite paralysed with terror, and that he firmly believed his last hour had come.", "그 결과 그는 공포로 아주 얼이 빠졌고, 저의 마지막 시각이 왔다고 굳게 믿었다."]], [["On his side, the Indian preserved the character of a perfect stranger.", "제 편에서, 그 인도인은 온전한 낯선 이의 모습을 지켰다."], ["He produced the little casket, and made exactly the same application which he had afterwards made to me.", "그는 그 작은 함을 꺼냈고, 뒤에 나에게 한 것과 꼭 같은 청을 했다."], ["As the speediest way of getting rid of him, Mr. Luker had at once declared that he had no money.", "그를 떼어 내는 가장 빠른 방편으로, 루커 씨는 대뜸 자기에게는 돈이 없다고 잘라 말했다."], ["The Indian had thereupon asked to be informed of the best and safest person to apply to for the loan he wanted. Mr.", "그러자 그 인도인은 자기가 원하는 대여를 청하기에 가장 좋고 가장 안전한 사람을 알려 달라 청했다."], ["Luker had answered that the best and safest person, in such cases, was usually a respectable solicitor.", "루커 씨는, 그런 경우에 가장 좋고 가장 안전한 사람은 대개 점잖은 사무 변호사라고 대답했다."], ["Asked to name some individual of that character and profession, Mr. Luker had mentioned me—for the one simple reason that, in the extremity of his terror, mine was the first name which occurred to him.", "그런 성격과 직업의 어떤 개인의 이름을 대라는 청을 받자, 루커 씨는 나를 언급했다—오직 한 가지 단순한 이유에서, 곧 그의 공포의 극단에서, 나의 이름이 그에게 맨 처음 떠오른 이름이었기 때문이다."], ["“The perspiration was pouring off me like rain, sir,”", "\"땀이 저에게서 비처럼 쏟아지고 있었습니다, 선생.\""], ["the wretched creature concluded.", "하고 그 가련한 인간이 말을 맺었다."], ["“I didn’t know what I was talking about.", "\"저는 제가 무슨 말을 하는지도 몰랐지요."], ["And I hope you’ll look over it, Mr. Bruff, sir, in consideration of my having been really and truly frightened out of my wits.”", "그리고 저는 당신께서, 브러프 씨, 선생, 제가 정말이지 참으로 넋이 빠지도록 겁에 질렸던 것을 헤아리시어, 그것을 눈감아 주시기를 바랍니다.\""]], [["I excused the fellow graciously enough.", "나는 그 친구를 제법 너그러이 용서해 주었다."], ["It was the readiest way of releasing myself from the sight of him.", "그것이 그의 꼴에서 나 자신을 풀어내는 가장 손쉬운 방편이었다."], ["Before he left me, I detained him to make one inquiry.", "그가 나를 떠나기 전에, 나는 한 가지 물음을 던지려고 그를 붙들었다."]], [["Had the Indian said anything noticeable, at the moment of quitting Mr. Luker’s house?", "그 인도인이 루커 씨의 집을 떠나던 그 순간에, 무언가 눈에 띄는 말을 했던가?"]], [["Yes!", "그랬다!"], ["The Indian had put precisely the same question to Mr. Luker, at parting, which he had put to me; receiving of course, the same answer as the answer which I had given him.", "그 인도인은, 헤어질 때, 나에게 던진 것과 정확히 같은 물음을 루커 씨에게 던졌고, 물론, 내가 그에게 준 답과 같은 답을 받았다."]], [["What did it mean? Mr.", "그것은 무엇을 뜻했는가?"], ["Luker’s explanation gave me no assistance towards solving the problem.", "루커 씨의 설명은 그 수수께끼를 푸는 데에 나에게 아무런 도움도 주지 못했다."], ["My own unaided ingenuity, consulted next, proved quite unequal to grapple with the difficulty.", "다음으로 기댄 나 자신의 홀로의 재간도, 그 어려움과 맞붙기에 아주 모자란 것으로 드러났다."], ["I had a dinner engagement that evening; and I went upstairs, in no very genial frame of mind, little suspecting that the way to my dressing-room and the way to discovery, meant, on this particular occasion, one and the same thing.", "나는 그날 저녁 만찬 약속이 있었다. 그리고 나는 그리 유쾌하지 못한 심사로 위층으로 올라갔는데, 나의 화장실로 가는 길과 발견으로 가는 길이, 바로 이 경우에는, 하나의 같은 것을 뜻한다고는 좀처럼 의심하지 못한 채였다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["The prominent personage among the guests at the dinner party I found to be Mr. Murthwaite.", "그 만찬 자리의 손님들 가운데 가장 두드러진 인물은, 알고 보니 머스웨이트 씨였다."]], [["On his appearance in England, after his wanderings, society had been greatly interested in the traveller, as a man who had passed through many dangerous adventures, and who had escaped to tell the tale.", "떠돌이 방랑을 마치고 잉글랜드에 모습을 드러냈을 때, 사교계는 그 여행가에게 지대한 관심을 보였다—숱한 위험한 모험을 헤치고 지나와, 그 이야기를 전하려 살아 돌아온 사람으로서 말이다."], ["He had now announced his intention of returning to the scene of his exploits, and of penetrating into regions left still unexplored.", "그는 이제 자기의 그 무용담의 무대로 돌아가, 아직도 미답으로 남은 지역들 속으로 파고들 작정임을 밝힌 터였다."], ["This magnificent indifference to placing his safety in peril for the second time, revived the flagging interest of the worshippers in the hero.", "두 번째로 제 안전을 위험에 내던지는 이 장엄한 무심함이, 그 영웅을 흠모하는 이들의 시들해진 관심을 되살려 놓았다."], ["The law of chances was clearly against his escaping on this occasion.", "이번만은 그가 무사히 벗어나기가 확률의 법칙상 명백히 어려웠다."], ["It is not every day that we can meet an eminent person at dinner, and feel that there is a reasonable prospect of the news of his murder being the news that we hear of him next.", "저명한 인물과 만찬에서 마주 앉아, 그에 관해 우리가 다음번에 듣게 될 소식이 바로 그의 피살 소식일 상당한 개연성이 있다고 느끼는 일은, 날마다 있는 일이 아니다."]], [["When the gentlemen were left by themselves in the dining-room, I found myself sitting next to Mr. Murthwaite.", "신사들끼리만 식당에 남게 되었을 때, 나는 어느새 머스웨이트 씨의 옆자리에 앉아 있었다."], ["The guests present being all English, it is needless to say that, as soon as the wholesome check exercised by the presence of the ladies was removed, the conversation turned on politics as a necessary result.", "자리한 손님들이 모두 잉글랜드 사람이었으니, 숙녀들의 임석이 발휘하던 그 건전한 제어가 사라지자마자 화제가 필연적인 결과로 정치로 흘렀다는 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다."]], [["In respect to this all-absorbing national topic, I happen to be one of the most un-English Englishmen living.", "이 온 나라를 사로잡는 화제에 관한 한, 나는 세상에 살아 있는 가장 잉글랜드인답지 않은 잉글랜드인의 하나임을 자인한다."], ["As a general rule, political talk appears to me to be of all talk the most dreary and the most profitless.", "대체로 나에게 정치 이야기는 온갖 이야기 가운데 가장 따분하고 가장 무익한 것으로 여겨진다."], ["Glancing at Mr. Murthwaite, when the bottles had made their first round of the table, I found that he was apparently of my way of thinking.", "병들이 식탁을 한 바퀴 돌고 났을 때 머스웨이트 씨를 흘긋 보니, 그도 겉보기에 나와 같은 생각을 지닌 이였다."], ["He was doing it very dexterously—with all possible consideration for the feelings of his host—but it is not the less certain that he was composing himself for a nap.", "그는 아주 능란하게 그리하고 있었다—주인의 심기를 헤아릴 수 있는 온갖 배려를 다하면서—허나 그가 한숨 자려고 몸을 가다듬고 있다는 것만은 못지않게 분명했다."], ["It struck me as an experiment worth attempting, to try whether a judicious allusion to the subject of the Moonstone would keep him awake, and, if it did, to see what he thought of the last new complication in the Indian conspiracy, as revealed in the prosaic precincts of my office.", "월장석이라는 화제를 지혜롭게 넌지시 꺼내면 그를 깨어 있게 할 수 있을지, 그리고 만일 그리된다면, 나의 사무소의 그 무미건조한 울타리 안에서 드러난 그 인도인 음모의 마지막 새 얽힘을 그가 어찌 생각하는지 보아 두는 것이, 해 볼 만한 실험으로 나에게 떠올랐다."]], [["“If I am not mistaken, Mr. Murthwaite,”", "\"제가 잘못 안 것이 아니라면, 머스웨이트 씨,\""], ["I began, “you were acquainted with the late Lady Verinder, and you took some interest in the strange succession of events which ended in the loss of the Moonstone?”", "하고 나는 말을 뗐다. \"당신은 고인이 되신 레이디 버린더와 아는 사이셨고, 월장석의 분실로 끝난 그 기이한 사건들의 잇따름에 얼마쯤 관심을 두셨지요?\""]], [["The eminent traveller did me the honour of waking up in an instant, and asking me who I was.", "그 저명한 여행가는 그 자리에서 대뜸 깨어나, 내가 누구냐고 물어 오는 영예를 나에게 베풀었다."]], [["I informed him of my professional connection with the Herncastle family, not forgetting the curious position which I had occupied towards the Colonel and his Diamond in the bygone time.", "나는 헌캐슬 집안과의 나의 직업상의 연을 그에게 알렸는데, 지난 시절 그 대령과 그의 다이아몬드를 향해 내가 차지했던 그 묘한 처지도 잊지 않고 곁들였다."]], [["Mr. Murthwaite shifted round in his chair, so as to put the rest of the company behind him (Conservatives and Liberals alike), and concentrated his whole attention on plain Mr. Bruff, of Gray’s Inn Square.", "머스웨이트 씨는 나머지 일행(보수당원이든 자유당원이든 매한가지로)을 제 등 뒤로 돌리려는 듯 의자에서 몸을 틀어, 그레이스 인 스퀘어의 한낱 평범한 브러프 씨에게 온 정신을 쏟았다."]], [["“Have you heard anything, lately, of the Indians?”", "\"근래에 그 인도인들에 관해 무언가 들으신 것이 있소?\""], ["he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."]], [["“I have every reason to believe,”", "\"믿을 만한 온갖 근거가 있소,\""], ["I answered, “that one of them had an interview with me, in my office, yesterday.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"그들 가운데 하나가 어제, 내 사무소에서, 나와 면담을 가졌다고 여길 만한 근거가요.\""]], [["Mr. Murthwaite was not an easy man to astonish; but that last answer of mine completely staggered him.", "머스웨이트 씨는 좀처럼 놀라게 하기 어려운 사람이었다."], ["I described what had happened to Mr.", "허나 나의 그 마지막 대답은 그를 완전히 휘청거리게 했다."], ["Luker, and what had happened to myself, exactly as I have described it here.", "나는 루커 씨에게 일어난 일과, 나 자신에게 일어난 일을, 바로 여기 적어 온 그대로 그에게 이야기해 주었다."], ["“It is clear that the Indian’s parting inquiry had an object,” I added.", "\"그 인도인이 헤어지며 던진 물음에 목적이 있었던 것은 분명하오,\""], ["“Why should he be so anxious to know the time at which a borrower of money is usually privileged to pay the money back?”", "하고 나는 덧붙였다. \"어찌하여 그가, 돈을 빌린 이가 통상 그 돈을 되갚도록 허락받는 시기를 그리도 애타게 알고 싶어 했겠소?\""]], [["“Is it possible that you don’t see his motive, Mr. Bruff?”", "\"당신은 정녕 그의 동기를 모르시겠소, 브러프 씨?\""]], [["“I am ashamed of my stupidity, Mr. Murthwaite—but I certainly don’t see it.”", "\"나의 아둔함이 부끄럽소, 머스웨이트 씨—허나 나는 정말로 그것을 모르겠소.\""]], [["The great traveller became quite interested in sounding the immense vacuity of my dulness to its lowest depths.", "그 위대한 여행가는 나의 우둔함의 그 광대한 공허를 가장 밑바닥까지 재어 보는 데에 자못 흥미가 동한 참이었다."]], [["“Let me ask you one question,” he said.", "\"당신에게 한 가지 물음을 던지게 해 주시오,\""], ["“In what position does the conspiracy to seize the Moonstone now stand?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"월장석을 손에 넣으려는 음모는 지금 어떤 처지에 놓여 있소?\""]], [["“I can’t say,”", "\"나는 알 수 없소,\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“The Indian plot is a mystery to me.”", "\"그 인도인의 음모는 나에게 하나의 수수께끼요.\""]], [["“The Indian plot, Mr. Bruff, can only be a mystery to you, because you have never seriously examined it.", "\"그 인도인의 음모가, 브러프 씨, 당신에게 수수께끼일 수 있는 것은 오직 당신이 그것을 진지하게 살펴본 적이 없기 때문이오."], ["Shall we run it over together, from the time when you drew Colonel Herncastle’s Will, to the time when the Indian called at your office?", "우리 그것을 함께 한번 훑어봅시다, 당신이 헌캐슬 대령의 유언장을 작성한 때로부터, 그 인도인이 당신의 사무소를 찾아온 때까지 말이오."], ["In your position, it may be of very serious importance to the interests of Miss Verinder, that you should be able to take a clear view of this matter in case of need.", "당신의 처지에서는, 필요할 때에 이 문제를 또렷이 내다볼 수 있다는 것이, 버린더 아가씨의 이해(利害)에 아주 막중하게 중요할지도 모르오."], ["Tell me, bearing that in mind, whether you will penetrate the Indian’s motive for yourself?", "그것을 마음에 두고서, 나에게 말해 보시오, 당신 스스로 그 인도인의 동기를 꿰뚫어 보시겠소?"], ["or whether you wish me to save you the trouble of making any inquiry into it?”", "아니면 내가 당신을 대신해 그것을 캐어 보는 수고를 덜어 드리기를 바라시오?\""]], [["It is needless to say that I thoroughly appreciated the practical purpose which I now saw that he had in view, and that the first of the two alternatives was the alternative I chose.", "이제 그가 품고 있음을 내가 알아본 그 실질적인 목적을 내가 낱낱이 헤아렸다는 것과, 그 두 갈래 가운데 첫째가 내가 고른 갈래였다는 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다."]], [["“Very good,”", "\"아주 좋소,\""], ["said Mr. Murthwaite.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말했다."], ["“We will take the question of the ages of the three Indians first.", "\"우리는 먼저 그 인도인 셋의 나이 문제를 다루겠소."], ["I can testify that they all look much about the same age—and you can decide for yourself, whether the man whom you saw was, or was not, in the prime of life.", "나는 그들이 모두 엇비슷한 나이로 보인다고 증언할 수 있소—그리고 당신이 본 그 사내가 한창 나이였는지 아니었는지는 당신 스스로 판단할 수 있소."], ["Not forty, you think? My idea too.", "마흔은 안 되었다, 그리 여기시오?"], ["We will say not forty.", "나의 짐작도 그렇소."], ["Now look back to the time when Colonel Herncastle came to England, and when you were concerned in the plan he adopted to preserve his life.", "마흔은 안 되었다고 해 둡시다. 자, 헌캐슬 대령이 잉글랜드로 왔던 때, 그리고 당신이 그가 제 목숨을 지키려 채택한 계획에 관여했던 때로 거슬러 봅시다."], ["I don’t want you to count the years.", "나는 당신더러 햇수를 세라는 것이 아니오."], ["I will only say, it is clear that these present Indians, at their age, must be the successors of three other Indians (high caste Brahmins all of them, Mr. Bruff, when they left their native country!) who followed the Colonel to these shores.", "나는 다만 이렇게만 말하겠소, 지금의 이 인도인들이, 그 나이로 보아, 대령을 뒤따라 이 해안까지 온 다른 인도인 셋(모두 제 고국을 떠날 때는 높은 계급의 브라만이었소, 브러프 씨!)의 후계자들임에 틀림없다는 것은 명백하다고 말이오."], ["Very well.", "아주 좋소."], ["These present men of ours have succeeded to the men who were here before them.", "지금 우리의 이 사내들은 그들에 앞서 여기 있던 사내들의 자리를 이어받았소."], ["If they had only done that, the matter would not have been worth inquiring into.", "만일 그들이 그것만 했다면, 이 일은 캐어 볼 값어치조차 없었을 거요."], ["But they have done more.", "허나 그들은 그 이상을 했소."], ["They have succeeded to the organisation which their predecessors established in this country.", "그들은 그들의 선대가 이 나라에 세운 조직을 이어받았소."], ["Don’t start!", "놀라지 마시오!"], ["The organisation is a very trumpery affair, according to our ideas, I have no doubt.", "그 조직은, 나는 조금도 의심치 않소만, 우리의 관념으로는 아주 하찮은 물건이오."], ["I should reckon it up as including the command of money; the services, when needed, of that shady sort of Englishman, who lives in the byways of foreign life in London; and, lastly, the secret sympathy of such few men of their own country, and (formerly, at least) of their own religion, as happen to be employed in ministering to some of the multitudinous wants of this great city.", "나라면 그것을 이렇게 헤아리겠소—돈을 부리는 힘, 필요할 때 부릴 수 있는, 런던의 외국 삶의 뒷골목에 사는 그 수상쩍은 부류의 잉글랜드 사람의 도움, 그리고, 끝으로, 이 큰 도시의 그 숱한 필요 가운데 얼마쯤을 채워 주는 일에 우연히 종사하는, 저희 나라 사람이자 (적어도 예전에는) 저희 종교를 지닌 몇 안 되는 사람들의 은밀한 동조 말이오."], ["Nothing very formidable, as you see!", "보시다시피, 그리 대단할 것은 없소!"], ["But worth notice at starting, because we may find occasion to refer to this modest little Indian organisation as we go on.", "허나 처음에 눈여겨 둘 값어치는 있소, 우리가 나아가는 동안 이 조촐하고 자그마한 인도인 조직을 다시 언급할 계제를 만날지도 모르기 때문이오."], ["Having now cleared the ground, I am going to ask you a question; and I expect your experience to answer it.", "이제 바탕을 닦았으니, 나는 당신에게 한 가지 물음을 던지려 하오. 그리고 나는 당신의 경험이 그것에 답해 주기를 바라오."], ["What was the event which gave the Indians their first chance of seizing the Diamond?”", "그 인도인들에게 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 첫 기회를 준 사건은 무엇이었소?\""]], [["I understood the allusion to my experience.", "나는 나의 경험을 가리키는 그 말뜻을 알아들었다."]], [["“The first chance they got,”", "\"그들이 얻은 첫 기회는,\""], ["I replied, “was clearly offered to them by Colonel Herncastle’s death.", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"명백히 헌캐슬 대령의 죽음이 그들에게 안겨 준 것이오."], ["They would be aware of his death, I suppose, as a matter of course?”", "짐작건대 그들은 당연한 일로 그의 죽음을 알았겠지요?\""]], [["“As a matter of course.", "\"당연한 일로 알았소."], ["And his death, as you say, gave them their first chance.", "그리고 그의 죽음은, 당신이 말하듯, 그들에게 그 첫 기회를 주었소."], ["Up to that time the Moonstone was safe in the strongroom of the bank.", "그때까지 월장석은 은행의 금고실에 안전히 있었소."], ["You drew the Colonel’s Will leaving his jewel to his niece; and the Will was proved in the usual way.", "당신은 대령의 보석을 그의 조카딸에게 남기는 그 유언장을 작성했소. 그리고 그 유언장은 통상의 절차로 검인되었소."], ["As a lawyer, you can be at no loss to know what course the Indians would take (under English advice) after that.”", "변호사로서, 당신은 그 뒤에 인도인들이 (잉글랜드 사람의 조언을 받아) 어떤 방도를 취할지를 어렵잖게 알 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["“They would provide themselves with a copy of the Will from Doctors’ Commons,”", "\"그들은 닥터스 코먼스에서 그 유언장의 사본을 마련하겠지요,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“Exactly.", "\"바로 그렇소."], ["One or other of those shady Englishmen to whom I have alluded, would get them the copy you have described.", "내가 넌지시 말한 그 수상쩍은 잉글랜드 사람들 가운데 이런저런 이가 그들에게 당신이 방금 말한 그 사본을 구해다 줄 거요."], ["That copy would inform them that the Moonstone was bequeathed to the daughter of Lady Verinder, and that Mr. Blake the elder, or some person appointed by him, was to place it in her hands.", "그 사본은 그들에게, 월장석이 레이디 버린더의 딸에게 유증되었다는 것과, 손위 블레이크 씨, 또는 그가 지명한 어떤 이가 그것을 그녀의 손에 쥐여 주기로 되어 있다는 것을 알려 줄 거요."], ["You will agree with me that the necessary information about persons in the position of Lady Verinder and Mr. Blake, would be perfectly easy information to obtain.", "레이디 버린더와 블레이크 씨 같은 처지의 사람들에 관해 필요한 정보를 얻는 것이 더없이 손쉬운 일이라는 데에는 당신도 나와 뜻을 같이할 거요."], ["The one difficulty for the Indians would be to decide whether they should make their attempt on the Diamond when it was in course of removal from the keeping of the bank, or whether they should wait until it was taken down to Yorkshire to Lady Verinder’s house.", "인도인들에게 오직 하나의 어려움은, 그 다이아몬드를 은행의 보관에서 옮기는 도중에 노릴지, 아니면 그것이 요크셔의 레이디 버린더의 집으로 내려가기까지 기다릴지를 정하는 일이었을 거요."], ["The second way would be manifestly the safest way—and there you have the explanation of the appearance of the Indians at Frizinghall, disguised as jugglers, and waiting their time.", "둘째 방도가 명백히 가장 안전한 방도였을 것이오—그리고 바로 거기에, 요술쟁이로 변장하고 프리징홀에 나타나 제 때를 기다린 그 인도인들의 출현에 대한 설명이 있소."], ["In London, it is needless to say, they had their organisation at their disposal to keep them informed of events. Two men would do it.", "런던에서, 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 그들은 사건의 소식을 계속 얻으려고 제 조직을 부릴 수 있었소."], ["One to follow anybody who went from Mr. Blake’s house to the bank.", "두 사람이면 될 일이었소."], ["And one to treat the lower men servants with beer, and to hear the news of the house. These commonplace precautions would readily inform them that Mr.", "하나는 블레이크 씨의 집에서 은행으로 가는 이는 누구든 뒤따르는 일, 그리고 하나는 아랫사람 하인들에게 맥주를 사 대며 그 집의 소식을 듣는 일."], ["Franklin Blake had been to the bank, and that Mr. Franklin Blake was the only person in the house who was going to visit Lady Verinder.", "이 흔해빠진 방비만으로도 그들은, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 은행에 다녀왔다는 것과, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 그 집에서 레이디 버린더를 찾아가려는 유일한 사람이라는 것을 어렵잖게 알았을 거요."], ["What actually followed upon that discovery, you remember, no doubt, quite as correctly as I do.”", "그 발견에 뒤이어 실제로 일어난 일은, 당신도 나만큼이나 아주 또렷이 기억하고 있을 것이 틀림없소.\""]], [["I remembered that Franklin Blake had detected one of the spies, in the street—that he had, in consequence, advanced the time of his arrival in Yorkshire by some hours—and that (thanks to old Betteredge’s excellent advice) he had lodged the Diamond in the bank at Frizinghall, before the Indians were so much as prepared to see him in the neighbourhood.", "나는 기억했다. 프랭클린 블레이크가 그 밀정 가운데 하나를 길거리에서 알아챈 것, 그리하여 그가 요크셔에 다다르는 시각을 몇 시간 앞당긴 것, 그리고 (늙은 베터리지의 훌륭한 조언 덕분에) 인도인들이 그를 그 근방에서 볼 채비를 채 갖추기도 전에 그가 프리징홀의 은행에 그 다이아몬드를 맡겨 둔 것을 말이다."], ["All perfectly clear so far.", "여기까지는 모두 더없이 또렷했다."], ["But the Indians being ignorant of the precautions thus taken, how was it that they had made no attempt on Lady Verinder’s house (in which they must have supposed the Diamond to be) through the whole of the interval that elapsed before Rachel’s birthday?", "허나 인도인들이 이렇듯 취해진 방비를 몰랐다고 한들, 어찌하여 그들은 레이철의 생일에 앞서 흘러간 그 온 기간 내내 (그들이 다이아몬드가 있으리라 여겼음이 틀림없는) 레이디 버린더의 집에 아무런 시도도 하지 않았단 말인가?"]], [["In putting this difficulty to Mr. Murthwaite, I thought it right to add that I had heard of the little boy, and the drop of ink, and the rest of it, and that any explanation based on the theory of clairvoyance was an explanation which would carry no conviction whatever with it, to my mind.", "이 난제를 머스웨이트 씨에게 내놓으면서, 나는 그 어린 소년과, 잉크 한 방울과, 그 나머지의 이야기를 들은 바 있다는 것, 그리고 천리안이라는 이론에 바탕을 둔 어떤 설명도 나의 마음에는 조금도 확신을 주지 못하는 설명이라는 것을 덧붙이는 것이 옳다고 여겼다."]], [["“Nor to mine either,”", "\"나의 마음에도 마찬가지요,\""], ["said Mr. Murthwaite.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말했다."], ["“The clairvoyance in this case is simply a development of the romantic side of the Indian character.", "\"이 경우의 천리안이란 그저 인도인 기질의 낭만적인 면이 뻗어 나온 것에 지나지 않소."], ["It would be refreshment and an encouragement to those men—quite inconceivable, I grant you, to the English mind—to surround their wearisome and perilous errand in this country with a certain halo of the marvellous and the supernatural.", "그들에게는—당신네 잉글랜드 사람의 마음으로는 도무지 헤아릴 수 없는 일임을 나도 인정하오만—이 나라에서 벌이는 저희의 지루하고 위태로운 심부름을 어떤 경이롭고 초자연한 후광으로 두르는 것이, 하나의 위안이자 격려일 것이오."], ["Their boy is unquestionably a sensitive subject to the mesmeric influence—and, under that influence, he has no doubt reflected what was already in the mind of the person mesmerising him.", "그들의 소년은 두말할 것 없이 최면술의 영향에 예민한 대상이오—그리고, 그 영향 아래에서, 그는 그에게 최면을 거는 사람의 마음속에 이미 들어 있던 것을 비추어 냈을 뿐임에 틀림없소."], ["I have tested the theory of clairvoyance—and I have never found the manifestations get beyond that point.", "나는 천리안이라는 이론을 시험해 보았소—그리고 나는 그 나타남이 그 지점을 넘어서는 것을 결코 본 적이 없소."], ["The Indians don’t investigate the matter in this way; the Indians look upon their boy as a Seer of things invisible to their eyes—and, I repeat, in that marvel they find the source of a new interest in the purpose that unites them.", "인도인들은 이런 식으로 그 일을 캐지 않소. 인도인들은 그들의 소년을 저희 눈에 보이지 않는 것들을 보는 예언자로 우러러보오—그리고, 거듭 말하거니와, 그 경이 속에서 그들은 저희를 하나로 묶는 목적에 대한 새로운 관심의 원천을 찾소."], ["I only notice this as offering a curious view of human character, which must be quite new to you.", "나는 다만 이것을, 당신에게는 아주 새로울 것이 틀림없는, 인간 기질의 한 흥미로운 면을 보여 주는 것으로서 짚어 둘 뿐이오."], ["We have nothing whatever to do with clairvoyance, or with mesmerism, or with anything else that is hard of belief to a practical man, in the inquiry that we are now pursuing.", "우리가 지금 좇고 있는 이 탐구에서는, 천리안도, 최면술도, 실질적인 사람으로서는 믿기 어려운 그 무엇도, 우리와는 아무런 상관이 없소."], ["My object in following the Indian plot, step by step, is to trace results back, by rational means, to natural causes.", "그 인도인의 음모를 한 걸음 한 걸음 좇는 나의 목적은, 결과를 이성적인 방편으로 자연스러운 원인들로 거슬러 밝히는 것이오."], ["Have I succeeded to your satisfaction so far?”", "여기까지 내가 당신을 흡족하게 해 드렸소?\""]], [["“Not a doubt of it, Mr. Murthwaite!", "\"조금도 의심할 바 없소, 머스웨이트 씨!"], ["I am waiting, however, with some anxiety, to hear the rational explanation of the difficulty which I have just had the honour of submitting to you.”", "다만 나는, 얼마쯤 애를 태우며, 내가 방금 당신께 내놓는 영예를 누린 그 난제에 대한 이성적인 설명을 듣기를 기다리고 있소.\""]], [["Mr. Murthwaite smiled.", "머스웨이트 씨는 미소 지었다."], ["“It’s the easiest difficulty to deal with of all,”", "\"그것은 온갖 난제 가운데 다루기 가장 쉬운 것이오,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“Permit me to begin by admitting your statement of the case as a perfectly correct one. The Indians were undoubtedly not aware of what Mr.", "\"먼저 이 사건에 대한 당신의 진술이 더없이 옳다는 것을 인정하는 데서 시작하도록 허락해 주시오."], ["Franklin Blake had done with the Diamond—for we find them making their first mistake, on the first night of Mr. Blake’s arrival at his aunt’s house.”", "인도인들은 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지를 두말할 것 없이 몰랐소—그들이 블레이크 씨가 이모의 집에 다다른 그 첫날 밤에 첫 실수를 저지르는 것을 우리가 보게 되니 말이오.\""]], [["“Their first mistake?”", "\"그들의 첫 실수라니요?\""], ["I repeated.", "하고 나는 되물었다."]], [["“Certainly!", "\"물론이오!"], ["The mistake of allowing themselves to be surprised, lurking about the terrace at night, by Gabriel Betteredge.", "밤에 그 테라스 둘레를 어슬렁대다가 게이브리얼 베터리지에게 들키는 낭패를 자초한 실수 말이오."], ["However, they had the merit of seeing for themselves that they had taken a false step—for, as you say, again, with plenty of time at their disposal, they never came near the house for weeks afterwards.”", "그러나 그들에게는 스스로 자기들이 잘못된 걸음을 내디뎠음을 알아차리는 미덕이 있었소—당신이 다시 말하듯, 손에 넉넉한 시간을 쥐고서도, 그들은 그 뒤로 몇 주 동안 그 집 근처에 얼씬도 하지 않았으니 말이오.\""]], [["“Why, Mr. Murthwaite?", "\"어찌하여요, 머스웨이트 씨?"], ["That’s what I want to know!", "그것이 바로 내가 알고 싶은 것이오!"], ["Why?”", "어찌하여?\""]], [["“Because no Indian, Mr. Bruff, ever runs an unnecessary risk.", "\"어떤 인도인도, 브러프 씨, 결코 쓸데없는 위험을 무릅쓰지 않기 때문이오."], ["The clause you drew in Colonel Herncastle’s Will, informed them (didn’t it?) that the Moonstone was to pass absolutely into Miss Verinder’s possession on her birthday.", "당신이 헌캐슬 대령의 유언장에 작성한 그 조항은 그들에게, 월장석이 버린더 아가씨의 생일에 그녀의 소유로 완전히 넘어가리라는 것을 알려 주었소 (그렇지 않소?)."], ["Very well.", "아주 좋소."], ["Tell me which was the safest course for men in their position?", "그들의 처지에 놓인 사람들에게 가장 안전한 방도가 어느 쪽이었는지 나에게 말해 보시오."], ["To make their attempt on the Diamond while it was under the control of Mr. Franklin Blake, who had shown already that he could suspect and outwit them?", "이미 저희를 의심하고 따돌릴 수 있음을 보여 준 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 손아귀에 그 다이아몬드가 있는 동안에 노리는 것이겠소?"], ["Or to wait till the Diamond was at the disposal of a young girl, who would innocently delight in wearing the magnificent jewel at every possible opportunity?", "아니면 그 화려한 보석을 틈만 나면 몸에 지니려 천진하게 즐거워할 어린 아가씨의 손에 그 다이아몬드가 놓일 때까지 기다리는 것이겠소?"], ["Perhaps you want a proof that my theory is correct?", "어쩌면 당신은 나의 이론이 옳다는 증거를 원할지도 모르오?"], ["Take the conduct of the Indians themselves as the proof.", "그 인도인들 자신의 처신을 그 증거로 삼으시오."], ["They appeared at the house, after waiting all those weeks, on Miss Verinder’s birthday; and they were rewarded for the patient accuracy of their calculations by seeing the Moonstone in the bosom of her dress!", "그들은 그 여러 주를 다 기다린 끝에, 버린더 아가씨의 생일에 그 집에 나타났소. 그리고 그들은 그녀의 드레스 가슴께에 있는 월장석을 봄으로써 저희 계산의 참을성 있는 정확함에 대한 보답을 받았소!"], ["When I heard the story of the Colonel and the Diamond, later in the evening, I felt so sure about the risk Mr. Franklin Blake had run (they would have certainly attacked him, if he had not happened to ride back to Lady Verinder’s in the company of other people); and I was so strongly convinced of the worse risk still, in store for Miss Verinder, that I recommended following the Colonel’s plan, and destroying the identity of the gem by having it cut into separate stones.", "그날 저녁 늦게 그 대령과 그 다이아몬드의 이야기를 들었을 때, 나는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 무릅쓴 그 위험을 어찌나 확신했던지 (만일 그가 마침 다른 사람들과 함께 무리 지어 레이디 버린더의 집으로 말을 몰아 돌아오지 않았더라면, 그들은 틀림없이 그를 덮쳤을 것이오), 그리고 버린더 아가씨에게 앞으로 닥칠 더한 위험을 어찌나 굳게 믿었던지, 나는 그 대령의 계획을 따라, 그 보석을 여러 개의 돌로 쪼개어 그 정체를 없애 버리기를 권했소."], ["How its extraordinary disappearance that night, made my advice useless, and utterly defeated the Hindoo plot—and how all further action on the part of the Indians was paralysed the next day by their confinement in prison as rogues and vagabonds—you know as well as I do.", "그날 밤 그것의 그 기이한 사라짐이 어찌하여 나의 조언을 쓸모없게 만들고 그 힌두인의 음모를 완전히 무너뜨렸는지—그리고 인도인들 편의 온갖 다음 행동이 어찌하여 이튿날 그들이 부랑자이자 무뢰한으로 옥에 갇힘으로써 마비되었는지—당신도 나만큼이나 잘 알고 있소."], ["The first act in the conspiracy closes there.", "그 음모의 첫 막(幕)은 거기서 닫히오."], ["Before we go on to the second, may I ask whether I have met your difficulty, with an explanation which is satisfactory to the mind of a practical man?”", "우리가 둘째 막으로 넘어가기 전에, 내가 당신의 난제를, 실질적인 사람의 마음에 흡족한 설명으로써 풀어 드렸는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["It was impossible to deny that he had met my difficulty fairly; thanks to his superior knowledge of the Indian character—and thanks to his not having had hundreds of other Wills to think of since Colonel Herncastle’s time!", "그가 나의 난제를 정정당당히 풀어 주었음은 부인할 도리가 없었다. 인도인 기질에 대한 그의 뛰어난 식견 덕분에—그리고 헌캐슬 대령의 시절 이후로 그가 생각해야 할 다른 유언장을 수백 건이나 떠안지 않은 덕분에 말이다!"]], [["“So far, so good,”", "\"여기까지는 잘되었소,\""], ["resumed Mr. Murthwaite.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 말을 이었다."], ["“The first chance the Indians had of seizing the Diamond was a chance lost, on the day when they were committed to the prison at Frizinghall.", "\"인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 첫 기회는, 그들이 프리징홀의 옥에 갇히던 날 잃어버린 기회였소."], ["When did the second chance offer itself?", "둘째 기회는 언제 찾아왔소?"], ["The second chance offered itself—as I am in a condition to prove—while they were still in confinement.”", "그 둘째 기회는—내가 증명할 수 있는 처지에 있소만—그들이 아직 갇혀 있는 동안에 찾아왔소.\""]], [["He took out his pocket-book, and opened it at a particular leaf, before he went on.", "그는 말을 잇기에 앞서 제 수첩을 꺼내어 어느 한 낱장을 펼쳤다."]], [["“I was staying,”", "\"나는 그때,\""], ["he resumed, “with some friends at Frizinghall, at the time.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"프리징홀의 몇몇 벗과 함께 묵고 있었소."], ["A day or two before the Indians were set free (on a Monday, I think), the governor of the prison came to me with a letter.", "그 인도인들이 풀려나기 하루나 이틀 전에 (내 생각에 월요일이었소), 그 옥의 소장이 편지 한 통을 들고 나를 찾아왔소."], ["It had been left for the Indians by one Mrs. Macann, of whom they had hired the lodging in which they lived; and it had been delivered at Mrs.", "그것은 그 인도인들이 세 들어 살던 숙소의 주인인 어떤 매캔 부인이라는 이가 그들 앞으로 남겨 둔 것이었소."], ["Macann’s door, in ordinary course of post, on the previous morning.", "그리고 그것은 그 전날 아침, 통상의 우편 절차로 매캔 부인의 집 문 앞에 배달된 것이었소."], ["The prison authorities had noticed that the postmark was ‘Lambeth,’ and that the address on the outside, though expressed in correct English, was, in form, oddly at variance with the customary method of directing a letter.", "옥의 당국은 소인이 '램버스'인 것과, 겉면의 주소가, 비록 올바른 영어로 적혀 있기는 하나, 그 꼴이 편지 겉봉을 쓰는 관례의 방식과는 묘하게 어긋난다는 것을 눈여겨보았소."], ["On opening it, they had found the contents to be written in a foreign language, which they rightly guessed at as Hindustani.", "그것을 뜯어 보고, 그들은 그 내용이 어떤 외국어로 적혀 있음을 알아냈는데, 그들은 그것을 힌두스타니어라고 옳게 짐작했소."], ["Their object in coming to me was, of course, to have the letter translated to them.", "그들이 나를 찾아온 목적은, 물론, 그 편지를 그들에게 번역해 주도록 하는 것이었소."], ["I took a copy in my pocket-book of the original, and of my translation—and there they are at your service.”", "나는 그 원문과 나의 번역을 내 수첩에 베껴 두었소—그리고 그것들이 여기 당신을 위해 있소.\""]], [["He handed me the open pocket-book.", "그는 그 펼쳐진 수첩을 나에게 건넸다."], ["The address on the letter was the first thing copied.", "그 편지의 주소가 맨 먼저 베껴져 있었다."], ["It was all written in one paragraph, without any attempt at punctuation, thus: “To the three Indian men living with the lady called Macann at Frizinghall in Yorkshire.”", "그것은 온통 한 문단에, 구두점을 찍으려는 아무런 시도도 없이, 이렇게 적혀 있었다. \"요크셔의 프리징홀에서 매캔이라 불리는 부인과 함께 사는 세 명의 인도 사내에게.\""], ["The Hindoo characters followed; and the English translation appeared at the end, expressed in these mysterious words:", "뒤이어 힌두 글자들이 있었고, 그 영어 번역이 끝에 나타났는데, 다음의 알쏭달쏭한 말로 적혀 있었다."]], [["“In the name of the Regent of the Night, whose seat is on the Antelope, whose arms embrace the four corners of the earth.", "\"밤의 섭정, 그 자리가 영양(羚羊) 위에 있고, 그 팔이 땅의 네 귀퉁이를 끌어안는 이의 이름으로."]], [["“Brothers, turn your faces to the south, and come to me in the street of many noises, which leads down to the muddy river.", "\"형제들이여, 그대들의 얼굴을 남쪽으로 돌려, 진흙탕 강으로 내려가는 소란한 거리로 나에게 오라."]], [["“The reason is this.", "\"까닭은 이러하다."]], [["“My own eyes have seen it.”", "\"내 두 눈이 그것을 보았노라.\""]], [["There the letter ended, without either date or signature.", "거기서 그 편지는, 날짜도 서명도 없이, 끝나 있었다."], ["I handed it back to Mr. Murthwaite, and owned that this curious specimen of Hindoo correspondence rather puzzled me.", "나는 그것을 머스웨이트 씨에게 도로 건네며, 이 기묘한 힌두 서신의 표본이 나를 자못 어리둥절하게 한다고 털어놓았다."]], [["“I can explain the first sentence to you,”", "\"나는 당신에게 그 첫 문장을 풀이해 드릴 수 있소,\""], ["he said; “and the conduct of the Indians themselves will explain the rest.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 나머지는 그 인도인들 자신의 처신이 풀이해 줄 거요."], ["The god of the moon is represented, in the Hindoo mythology, as a four-armed deity, seated on an antelope; and one of his titles is the regent of the night.", "달의 신은, 힌두 신화에서, 영양 위에 앉은, 팔이 넷 달린 신으로 그려지오. 그리고 그의 칭호 가운데 하나가 밤의 섭정이오."], ["Here, then, to begin with, is something which looks suspiciously like an indirect reference to the Moonstone.", "그러니, 우선 여기에, 월장석에 대한 에두른 언급인 양 수상쩍게 보이는 무언가가 있는 셈이오."], ["Now, let us see what the Indians did, after the prison authorities had allowed them to receive their letter.", "자, 옥의 당국이 그들에게 그 편지를 받도록 허락한 뒤에 그 인도인들이 무엇을 했는지 봅시다."], ["On the very day when they were set free they went at once to the railway station, and took their places in the first train that started for London.", "그들은 풀려난 바로 그날, 곧장 철도역으로 가서 런던으로 떠나는 첫 기차에 자리를 잡았소."], ["We all thought it a pity at Frizinghall that their proceedings were not privately watched.", "우리는 프리징홀에서 그들의 행동이 은밀히 감시되지 않은 것을 모두 애석하게 여겼소."], ["But, after Lady Verinder had dismissed the police-officer, and had stopped all further inquiry into the loss of the Diamond, no one else could presume to stir in the matter.", "허나 레이디 버린더가 그 경찰관을 물리치고, 그 다이아몬드의 분실에 대한 온갖 더 이상의 조사를 그만두게 한 뒤로는, 아무도 감히 그 일에 나설 수 없었소."], ["The Indians were free to go to London, and to London they went.", "인도인들은 마음대로 런던으로 갈 수 있었고, 런던으로 그들은 갔소."], ["What was the next news we heard of them, Mr. Bruff?”", "우리가 다음으로 그들에 관해 들은 소식은 무엇이었소, 브러프 씨?\""]], [["“They were annoying Mr. Luker,”", "\"그들은 루커 씨를 성가시게 하고 있었소,\""], ["I answered, “by loitering about the house at Lambeth.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"램버스의 그 집 둘레를 어슬렁거리며 말이오.\""]], [["“Did you read the report of Mr. Luker’s application to the magistrate?”", "\"당신은 루커 씨가 치안판사에게 낸 신청의 보고서를 읽어 보았소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“In the course of his statement he referred, if you remember, to a foreign workman in his employment, whom he had just dismissed on suspicion of attempted theft, and whom he also distrusted as possibly acting in collusion with the Indians who had annoyed him. The inference is pretty plain, Mr.", "\"그의 진술 가운데, 당신도 기억한다면, 그는 자기가 부리던 어느 외국인 일꾼을 언급했소—그가 절도 미수의 혐의로 방금 내쫓은, 그리고 자기를 성가시게 한 그 인도인들과 어쩌면 짜고 놀아났을지 모른다고 미심쩍어한 그 일꾼을 말이오."], ["Bruff, as to who wrote that letter which puzzled you just now, and as to which of Mr. Luker’s Oriental treasures the workman had attempted to steal.”", "방금 당신을 어리둥절하게 한 그 편지를 누가 썼는지, 그리고 루커 씨의 동양 보물들 가운데 어느 것을 그 일꾼이 훔치려 했는지에 대해서는, 브러프 씨, 그 미루어 짐작함이 자못 뚜렷하오.\""]], [["The inference (as I hastened to acknowledge) was too plain to need being pointed out.", "그 미루어 짐작함은 (내가 서둘러 인정했듯) 굳이 짚어 줄 것도 없이 너무나 뚜렷했다."], ["I had never doubted that the Moonstone had found its way into Mr. Luker’s hands, at the time Mr.", "나는 머스웨이트 씨가 넌지시 말한 그 무렵에 월장석이 루커 씨의 손에 흘러 들어갔다는 것을 한 번도 의심한 적이 없었다."], ["Murthwaite alluded to. My only question had been, How had the Indians discovered the circumstance?", "나의 유일한 물음은, 어떻게 그 인도인들이 그 정황을 알아냈는가 하는 것이었다."], ["This question (the most difficult to deal with of all, as I had thought) had now received its answer, like the rest.", "이 물음은 (내 생각에 온갖 것 가운데 다루기 가장 어려운 것이었는데) 이제 나머지처럼 그 답을 얻은 셈이었다."], ["Lawyer as I was, I began to feel that I might trust Mr. Murthwaite to lead me blindfold through the last windings of the labyrinth, along which he had guided me thus far.", "변호사인 나였으나, 나는 머스웨이트 씨가 여기까지 나를 이끌어 온 그 미로의 마지막 굽이들을 지나 눈먼 나를 데려가 주리라 믿어도 좋겠다고 느끼기 시작했다."], ["I paid him the compliment of telling him this, and found my little concession very graciously received.", "나는 그에게 이렇게 말하는 찬사를 바쳤고, 나의 그 자그마한 양보가 아주 정중하게 받아들여지는 것을 보았다."]], [["“You shall give me a piece of information in your turn before we go on,” he said.", "\"우리가 나아가기 전에 이번에는 당신이 나에게 한 가지 정보를 주어야겠소,\""], ["“Somebody must have taken the Moonstone from Yorkshire to London.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"누군가 그 월장석을 요크셔에서 런던으로 가져갔을 것이오."], ["And somebody must have raised money on it, or it would never have been in Mr. Luker’s possession.", "그리고 누군가 그것을 담보로 돈을 마련했을 것이오, 그러지 않았다면 그것이 결코 루커 씨의 수중에 있지 않았을 테니 말이오."], ["Has there been any discovery made of who that person was?”", "그 사람이 누구였는지에 대해 무슨 밝혀진 것이 있소?\""]], [["“None that I know of.”", "\"내가 아는 바로는 아무것도 없소.\""]], [["“There was a story (was there not?) about Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "\"고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 관한 소문이 (있지 않았소?) 있었소."], ["I am told he is an eminent philanthropist—which is decidedly against him, to begin with.”", "나는 그가 저명한 자선가라고 들었소—그것부터가, 우선 그에게 결정적으로 불리한 점이오.\""]], [["I heartily agreed in this with Mr. Murthwaite.", "나는 이 점에서 머스웨이트 씨에게 진심으로 뜻을 같이했다."], ["At the same time, I felt bound to inform him (without, it is needless to say, mentioning Miss Verinder’s name) that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite had been cleared of all suspicion, on evidence which I could answer for as entirely beyond dispute.", "동시에, 나는 그에게 (굳이 말할 것도 없이, 버린더 아가씨의 이름은 들먹이지 않고) 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가, 내가 전적으로 다툼의 여지가 없다고 장담할 수 있는 증거로써, 온갖 혐의를 벗었음을 알려 줄 의무를 느꼈다."]], [["“Very well,” said Mr.", "\"아주 좋소,\""], ["Murthwaite, quietly, “let us leave it to time to clear the matter up.", "하고 머스웨이트 씨가 조용히 말했다. \"그 일은 시간에 맡겨 밝혀지게 둡시다."], ["In the meanwhile, Mr. Bruff, we must get back again to the Indians, on your account.", "그동안, 브러프 씨, 우리는 당신을 위해 다시 그 인도인들에게로 돌아가야 하오."], ["Their journey to London simply ended in their becoming the victims of another defeat.", "그들의 런던행은 그저 그들이 또 한 번의 낭패의 희생물이 되는 것으로 끝나고 말았소."], ["The loss of their second chance of seizing the Diamond is mainly attributable, as I think, to the cunning and foresight of Mr. Luker—who doesn’t stand at the top of the prosperous and ancient profession of usury for nothing!", "그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 그들의 둘째 기회를 잃은 것은, 내 생각에, 주로 루커 씨의 교활함과 앞을 내다보는 눈 덕분이오—그가 공연히 그 번창하고 유서 깊은 고리대업의 꼭대기에 앉아 있는 것이 아니오!"], ["By the prompt dismissal of the man in his employment, he deprived the Indians of the assistance which their confederate would have rendered them in getting into the house.", "자기가 부리던 그 사내를 즉각 내쫓음으로써, 그는 인도인들에게서 그들의 공범이 그 집으로 들어가는 데에 베풀었을 도움을 앗아 버렸소."], ["By the prompt transport of the Moonstone to his banker’s, he took the conspirators by surprise before they were prepared with a new plan for robbing him.", "월장석을 즉각 제 은행가에게로 옮김으로써, 그는 그 음모자들이 자기를 털 새 계획을 채 갖추기도 전에 그들의 허를 찔렀소."], ["How the Indians, in this latter case, suspected what he had done, and how they contrived to possess themselves of his banker’s receipt, are events too recent to need dwelling on.", "이 뒤엣일에서, 인도인들이 어떻게 그가 한 일을 미심쩍어했고, 어떻게 그들이 그의 은행가 영수증을 손에 넣으려 꾀했는지는, 너무나 근래의 일이라 새삼 늘어놓을 것도 없소."], ["Let it be enough to say that they know the Moonstone to be once more out of their reach; deposited (under the general description of ‘a valuable of great price’) in a banker’s strong room.", "그들이 이제 월장석이 다시 한 번 저희 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있음을—('큰 값어치의 귀중품'이라는 두루뭉술한 표현으로) 어느 은행가의 금고실에 맡겨져 있음을 안다는 것만 말해 두면 넉넉하오."], ["Now, Mr. Bruff, what is their third chance of seizing the Diamond?", "자, 브러프 씨, 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣을 그들의 셋째 기회는 무엇이오?"], ["and when will it come?”", "그리고 그것은 언제 오겠소?\""]], [["As the question passed his lips, I penetrated the motive of the Indian’s visit to my office at last!", "그 물음이 그의 입술을 스쳐 나오는 순간, 나는 마침내 그 인도인이 내 사무소를 찾아온 동기를 꿰뚫어 보았다!"]], [["“I see it!”", "\"알겠소!\""], ["I exclaimed.", "하고 나는 외쳤다."], ["“The Indians take it for granted, as we do, that the Moonstone has been pledged; and they want to be certainly informed of the earliest period at which the pledge can be redeemed—because that will be the earliest period at which the Diamond can be removed from the safe keeping of the bank!”", "\"그 인도인들은, 우리처럼, 월장석이 저당 잡혔음을 당연한 일로 여기고 있소. 그리고 그들은 그 저당물을 되찾을 수 있는 가장 이른 시기를 틀림없이 알고 싶어 하오—그것이 곧 그 다이아몬드가 은행의 안전한 보관에서 나올 수 있는 가장 이른 시기일 테니 말이오!\""]], [["“I told you you would find it out for yourself, Mr. Bruff, if I only gave you a fair chance.", "\"내가 당신에게 기회만 정당히 주면 당신 스스로 알아내리라고 말했지요, 브러프 씨."], ["In a year from the time when the Moonstone was pledged, the Indians will be on the watch for their third chance. Mr.", "그 월장석이 저당 잡힌 때로부터 한 해가 지나면, 인도인들은 그들의 셋째 기회를 노리고 지켜볼 것이오."], ["Luker’s own lips have told them how long they will have to wait, and your respectable authority has satisfied them that Mr. Luker has spoken the truth.", "그들이 얼마나 오래 기다려야 하는지는 루커 씨 자신의 입이 그들에게 일러 주었고, 당신의 존경할 만한 권위가 루커 씨의 말이 참임을 그들에게 확인해 주었소."], ["When do we suppose, at a rough guess, that the Diamond found its way into the money-lender’s hands?”", "우리가 얼추 어림잡아, 그 다이아몬드가 그 돈놀이꾼의 손에 흘러 들어간 것이 언제라고 보오?\""]], [["“Towards the end of last June,”", "\"지난 유월 말경이오,\" 하고 나는 대답했다."], ["I answered, “as well as I can reckon it.”", "\"내가 헤아릴 수 있는 한으로는 말이오.\""]], [["“And we are now in the year ’forty-eight.", "\"그리고 지금 우리는 사십팔 년에 있소."], ["Very good.", "아주 좋소."], ["If the unknown person who has pledged the Moonstone can redeem it in a year, the jewel will be in that person’s possession again at the end of June, ’forty-nine.", "만일 그 월장석을 저당 잡힌 그 알 수 없는 사람이 한 해 안에 그것을 되찾을 수 있다면, 그 보석은 사십구 년 유월 말에 다시 그 사람의 수중에 있게 될 것이오."], ["I shall be thousands of miles from England and English news at that date.", "그 무렵 나는 잉글랜드와 잉글랜드 소식에서 수천 마일 떨어진 곳에 있을 거요."], ["But it may be worth your while to take a note of it, and to arrange to be in London at the time.”", "허나 그것을 적어 두고, 그때 런던에 있도록 채비하는 것이 당신에게는 애써 볼 값어치가 있을지도 모르오.\""]], [["“You think something serious will happen?”", "\"당신은 무언가 심상찮은 일이 일어나리라 여기시오?\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“I think I shall be safer,”", "\"나는 내가,\" 하고 그가 대답했다."], ["he answered, “among the fiercest fanatics of Central Asia than I should be if I crossed the door of the bank with the Moonstone in my pocket.", "\"그 월장석을 주머니에 넣고 은행의 문을 넘느니, 차라리 중앙아시아의 가장 사나운 광신자들 틈에 있는 편이 더 안전하리라 여기오."], ["The Indians have been defeated twice running, Mr.", "인도인들은 잇달아 두 번 낭패를 보았소, 브러프 씨."], ["Bruff. It’s my firm belief that they won’t be defeated a third time.”", "그들이 세 번째로는 낭패를 보지 않으리라는 것이 나의 굳은 믿음이오.\""]], [["Those were the last words he said on the subject.", "그것이 그 화제에 관해 그가 한 마지막 말이었다. 커피가 들어왔다."], ["The coffee came in; the guests rose, and dispersed themselves about the room; and we joined the ladies of the dinner-party upstairs.", "손님들은 자리에서 일어나 방 안 이곳저곳으로 흩어졌다. 그리고 우리는 위층에서 그 만찬 자리의 숙녀들과 어울렸다."]], [["I made a note of the date, and it may not be amiss if I close my narrative by repeating that note here:", "나는 그 날짜를 적어 두었다. 그리고 그 적어 둔 것을 여기 다시 옮기며 나의 이야기를 맺더라도 그리 나쁘지는 않으리라."]], [["June, ’forty-nine.", "사십구 년 유월."], ["Expect news of the Indians, towards the end of the month.", "유월 말경, 인도인들의 소식을 기다릴 것."]], [["And that done, I hand the pen, which I have now no further claim to use, to the writer who follows me next.", "그리고 그것을 마쳤으니, 나는 이제 더는 쓸 자격이 없는 이 펜을, 나의 다음을 잇는 글쓴이에게 넘긴다."]], [["Contributed by Franklin Blake.", "프랭클린 블레이크가 기고함."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["In the spring of the year eighteen hundred and forty-nine I was wandering in the East, and had then recently altered the travelling plans which I had laid out some months before, and which I had communicated to my lawyer and my banker in London.", "천팔백사십구 년 봄에 나는 동방을 떠돌고 있었는데, 그 무렵 나는 몇 달 전에 세워 두었던, 그리고 런던의 내 변호사와 내 은행가에게 알려 두었던 여행 계획을 얼마 전 바꾼 참이었다."]], [["This change made it necessary for me to send one of my servants to obtain my letters and remittances from the English consul in a certain city, which was no longer included as one of my resting-places in my new travelling scheme.", "이 변경으로 인해 나는 내 하인 하나를 어느 도시의 잉글랜드 영사에게 보내어 내 편지와 송금을 받아 오게 할 필요가 생겼는데, 그 도시는 나의 새 여행 일정에서는 더 이상 내가 머무를 곳의 하나로 들어 있지 않았다."], ["The man was to join me again at an appointed place and time.", "그 사내는 정해진 곳과 정해진 때에 나와 다시 합류하기로 되어 있었다."], ["An accident, for which he was not responsible, delayed him on his errand.", "그의 탓이 아닌 사고 하나가 그 심부름길에서 그를 지체시켰다."], ["For a week I and my people waited, encamped on the borders of a desert.", "일주일 동안 나와 내 사람들은 어느 사막의 언저리에 진을 치고 기다렸다."], ["At the end of that time the missing man made his appearance, with the money and the letters, at the entrance of my tent.", "그 기간이 끝날 무렵, 그 사라졌던 사내가 그 돈과 편지들을 지니고, 내 천막 어귀에 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“I am afraid I bring you bad news, sir,”", "\"안됐지만 나쁜 소식을 가져온 것 같습니다, 나리.\""], ["he said, and pointed to one of the letters, which had a mourning border round it, and the address on which was in the handwriting of Mr. Bruff.", "하고 그가 말하며, 편지 하나를 가리켰는데, 그것은 둘레에 상장(喪章)의 검은 테가 둘려 있었고, 그 위의 주소는 브러프 씨의 필적이었다."]], [["I know nothing, in a case of this kind, so unendurable as suspense.", "이런 경우에, 나는 마음 졸이는 조바심만큼 견디기 어려운 것을 알지 못한다."], ["The letter with the mourning border was the letter that I opened first.", "그 검은 테가 둘린 편지가 내가 맨 먼저 뜯은 편지였다."]], [["It informed me that my father was dead, and that I was heir to his great fortune.", "그것은 나에게, 내 아버지가 돌아가셨다는 것과, 내가 그의 막대한 재산의 상속인이라는 것을 알려 주었다."], ["The wealth which had thus fallen into my hands brought its responsibilities with it, and Mr. Bruff entreated me to lose no time in returning to England.", "이렇듯 내 손에 떨어진 그 재물은 그와 더불어 그 책임을 함께 가져왔고, 브러프 씨는 나에게 지체 없이 잉글랜드로 돌아오기를 간청했다."]], [["By daybreak the next morning, I was on my way back to my own country.", "이튿날 동틀 무렵, 나는 내 조국으로 돌아가는 길에 올라 있었다."]], [["The picture presented of me, by my old friend Betteredge, at the time of my departure from England, is (as I think) a little overdrawn.", "내가 잉글랜드를 떠날 무렵의 나를 그려 낸, 내 옛 벗 베터리지의 그 초상은 (내 생각에) 조금 과장되어 있다."], ["He has, in his own quaint way, interpreted seriously one of his young mistress’s many satirical references to my foreign education; and has persuaded himself that he actually saw those French, German, and Italian sides to my character, which my lively cousin only professed to discover in jest, and which never had any real existence, except in our good Betteredge’s own brain.", "그는, 제 나름의 예스럽고 별난 투로, 그의 젊은 여주인이 내 외국 물든 교육을 두고 던진 그 숱한 빈정거림 가운데 하나를 진지하게 새겨듣고 말았다. 그리하여 그는, 내 활달한 사촌이 다만 우스개로 찾아냈다고 짐짓 말했을 뿐이고, 우리의 착한 베터리지 자신의 머릿속 말고는 실은 아무런 실체도 없었던, 내 성품의 그 프랑스식·독일식·이탈리아식 면들을 자기가 정말로 보았노라고 스스로 믿어 버렸다."], ["But, barring this drawback, I am bound to own that he has stated no more than the truth in representing me as wounded to the heart by Rachel’s treatment, and as leaving England in the first keenness of suffering caused by the bitterest disappointment of my life.", "허나, 이 흠만 빼면, 나는 인정하지 않을 수 없다—그가 나를, 레이철의 처사에 가슴 깊이 상처 입고, 내 생애의 가장 쓰라린 실망이 안긴 고통이 가장 날카로운 그 첫머리에 잉글랜드를 떠나는 사람으로 그려 낸 것은, 진실을 넘어선 무엇도 말한 것이 아니라는 것을."]], [["I went abroad, resolved—if change and absence could help me—to forget her.", "나는, 마음먹고—만일 변화와 부재가 나에게 도움이 될 수 있다면—그녀를 잊으려, 외국으로 갔다."], ["It is, I am persuaded, no true view of human nature which denies that change and absence do help a man under these circumstances; they force his attention away from the exclusive contemplation of his own sorrow.", "이런 처지에 놓인 사람에게 변화와 부재가 정녕 도움이 된다는 것을 부인하는 것은, 나는 확신하거니와, 인간 본성에 대한 참된 견해가 아니다. 그것들은 그의 주의를 오직 제 슬픔만을 곱씹는 데에서 억지로 떼어 놓는다."], ["I never forgot her; but the pang of remembrance lost its worst bitterness, little by little, as time, distance, and novelty interposed themselves more and more effectually between Rachel and me.", "나는 결코 그녀를 잊지 못했다. 허나 기억의 그 아릿한 아픔은, 시간과 거리와 새로움이 레이철과 나 사이에 점점 더 효과적으로 끼어들면서, 조금씩 그 가장 모진 쓰라림을 잃어 갔다."]], [["On the other hand, it is no less certain that, with the act of turning homeward, the remedy which had gained its ground so steadily, began now, just as steadily, to drop back.", "한편으로, 못지않게 분명한 것은, 고향으로 발길을 돌리는 그 행위와 더불어, 그토록 꾸준히 자리를 넓혀 오던 그 약이, 이제 꼭 그만큼 꾸준히 도로 물러나기 시작했다는 것이다."], ["The nearer I drew to the country which she inhabited, and to the prospect of seeing her again, the more irresistibly her influence began to recover its hold on me.", "그녀가 사는 그 나라에, 그리고 그녀를 다시 볼 전망에 가까이 다가갈수록, 그녀의 영향력은 그만큼 더 뿌리치기 어렵게 나에 대한 제 손아귀를 되찾기 시작했다."], ["On leaving England she was the last person in the world whose name I would have suffered to pass my lips.", "잉글랜드를 떠날 적에 그녀는 내가 그 이름이 내 입술을 스치도록 허락했을 세상에서 가장 마지막 사람이었다."], ["On returning to England, she was the first person I inquired after, when Mr. Bruff and I met again.", "잉글랜드로 돌아오자, 브러프 씨와 내가 다시 만났을 때, 그녀는 내가 맨 먼저 안부를 물은 사람이었다."]], [["I was informed, of course, of all that had happened in my absence; in other words, of all that has been related here in continuation of Betteredge’s narrative—one circumstance only being excepted.", "나는, 물론, 내가 없는 동안에 일어난 온갖 일을 전해 들었다. 다시 말해, 베터리지의 이야기에 이어 여기 적혀 온 모든 것을 말이다—오직 한 가지 정황만은 예외로 하고."], ["Mr. Bruff did not, at that time, feel himself at liberty to inform me of the motives which had privately influenced Rachel and Godfrey Ablewhite in recalling the marriage promise, on either side.", "브러프 씨는, 그때는, 레이철과 고드프리 에이블화이트로 하여금 양쪽에서 그 혼약을 물리게끔 은밀히 움직인 그 동기들을 나에게 알려 줄 자유가 자기에게 있다고 여기지 않았다."], ["I troubled him with no embarrassing questions on this delicate subject.", "나는 이 미묘한 화제에 관해 그를 난처하게 할 물음으로 그를 괴롭히지 않았다."], ["It was relief enough to me, after the jealous disappointment caused by hearing that she had ever contemplated being Godfrey’s wife, to know that reflection had convinced her of acting rashly, and that she had effected her own release from her marriage engagement.", "그녀가 한때 고드프리의 아내가 될 것을 생각했다는 것을 듣고 일어난 질투 어린 실망 뒤에, 곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에 그녀 자신이 경솔하게 처신했음을 깨달았다는 것과, 그녀가 스스로 제 혼약에서 풀려났다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 나에게는 넉넉한 위안이었다."]], [["Having heard the story of the past, my next inquiries (still inquiries after Rachel!) advanced naturally to the present time.", "지난 일의 이야기를 들었으니, 나의 다음 물음은 (여전히 레이철에 대한 물음이었다!) 자연스럽게 현재로 나아갔다."], ["Under whose care had she been placed after leaving Mr.", "그녀는 브러프 씨의 집을 떠난 뒤 누구의 보살핌에 맡겨졌는가?"], ["Bruff’s house? and where was she living now?", "그리고 그녀는 지금 어디에 살고 있는가?"]], [["She was living under the care of a widowed sister of the late Sir John Verinder—one Mrs. Merridew—whom her mother’s executors had requested to act as guardian, and who had accepted the proposal.", "그녀는 고인이 된 존 버린더 경의, 홀로된 누이 되는 어느 분—메리듀 부인이라는 이의 보살핌 아래 살고 있었는데, 그녀의 어머니의 유언 집행인들이 그 부인에게 후견인 노릇을 해 주기를 청했고, 그 부인이 그 제안을 받아들였던 것이다."], ["They were reported to me as getting on together admirably well, and as being now established, for the season, in Mrs. Merridew’s house in Portland Place.", "그들은 서로 더없이 잘 지내고 있으며, 지금은 사교철을 나려고 포틀랜드 플레이스의 메리듀 부인의 집에 자리 잡고 있다고 나에게 전해졌다."]], [["Half an hour after receiving this information, I was on my way to Portland Place—without having had the courage to own it to Mr. Bruff!", "이 소식을 받은 지 반 시간 만에, 나는 포틀랜드 플레이스로 가는 길에 올라 있었다—브러프 씨에게 그것을 털어놓을 용기는 차마 내지 못한 채로!"]], [["The man who answered the door was not sure whether Miss Verinder was at home or not.", "문을 열어 준 사내는 버린더 아가씨가 집에 있는지 없는지 확실히 알지 못했다."], ["I sent him upstairs with my card, as the speediest way of setting the question at rest.", "나는 그 물음을 매듭짓는 가장 빠른 방편으로, 내 명함을 들려 그를 위층으로 올려 보냈다."], ["The man came down again with an impenetrable face, and informed me that Miss Verinder was out.", "그 사내는 속을 헤아릴 수 없는 낯빛으로 다시 내려와, 버린더 아가씨가 외출 중이라고 나에게 알렸다."]], [["I might have suspected other people of purposely denying themselves to me.", "다른 사람들 같으면 나에게 일부러 없는 척한 것이 아닌가 의심했을지도 모른다."], ["But it was impossible to suspect Rachel.", "허나 레이철을 의심하기란 있을 수 없는 일이었다."], ["I left word that I would call again at six o’clock that evening.", "나는 그날 저녁 여섯 시에 다시 찾아오겠다는 말을 남겼다."]], [["At six o’clock I was informed for the second time that Miss Verinder was not at home.", "여섯 시에, 나는 두 번째로 버린더 아가씨가 집에 없다는 말을 들었다."], ["Had any message been left for me?", "나에게 남겨진 전갈이 있는가?"], ["No message had been left for me.", "나에게 남겨진 전갈은 없었다."], ["Had Miss Verinder not received my card?", "버린더 아가씨가 내 명함을 받지 못했는가?"], ["The servant begged my pardon—Miss Verinder had received it.", "그 하인은 나에게 용서를 빌었다—버린더 아가씨는 그것을 받으셨다고."]], [["The inference was too plain to be resisted.", "그 미루어 짐작함은 너무나 뚜렷하여 물리칠 수 없었다."], ["Rachel declined to see me.", "레이철은 나를 만나기를 마다한 것이다."]], [["On my side, I declined to be treated in this way, without making an attempt, at least, to discover a reason for it.", "내 편에서, 나는 적어도 그 까닭을 알아내려는 시도조차 없이 이런 대접을 받기를 마다했다."], ["I sent up my name to Mrs. Merridew, and requested her to favour me with a personal interview at any hour which it might be most convenient to her to name.", "나는 메리듀 부인에게 내 이름을 올려 보내, 그녀가 편한 시각을 아무 때나 정하여 나에게 직접 면담의 은혜를 베풀어 주기를 청했다."]], [["Mrs. Merridew made no difficulty about receiving me at once.", "메리듀 부인은 나를 곧바로 맞아들이는 데에 아무 어려움을 두지 않았다."], ["I was shown into a comfortable little sitting-room, and found myself in the presence of a comfortable little elderly lady.", "나는 아늑하고 자그마한 응접실로 안내되어 들었고, 아늑하고 자그마한 늙은 부인 앞에 서 있는 나 자신을 보았다."], ["She was so good as to feel great regret and much surprise, entirely on my account.", "그녀는 오로지 나를 위하여 큰 유감과 적잖은 놀라움을 느낄 만큼 마음씨가 고왔다."], ["She was at the same time, however, not in a position to offer me any explanation, or to press Rachel on a matter which appeared to relate to a question of private feeling alone.", "허나 그녀는 동시에, 나에게 아무런 설명을 해 줄 처지도, 오로지 개인의 감정 문제에만 관련되는 듯싶은 사안을 두고 레이철을 다그칠 처지도 아니었다. 이것은, 무엇으로도 지치게 할 수 없는 예의 바른 참을성으로, 거듭거듭 되풀이되었다."], ["This was said over and over again, with a polite patience that nothing could tire; and this was all I gained by applying to Mrs. Merridew.", "그리고 이것이 내가 메리듀 부인에게 청하여 얻은 전부였다."]], [["My last chance was to write to Rachel.", "나의 마지막 방편은 레이철에게 편지를 쓰는 것이었다."], ["My servant took a letter to her the next day, with strict instructions to wait for an answer.", "내 하인은 이튿날 답장을 기다리라는 엄한 분부와 함께 그녀에게 편지를 가져갔다."]], [["The answer came back, literally in one sentence.", "답장은, 글자 그대로 단 한 문장으로, 돌아왔다."]], [["“Miss Verinder begs to decline entering into any correspondence with Mr. Franklin Blake.”", "\"버린더 아가씨는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨와 어떠한 서신 왕래도 나누기를 삼가고자 합니다.\""]], [["Fond as I was of her, I felt indignantly the insult offered to me in that reply. Mr.", "그녀를 그토록 사랑했으면서도, 나는 그 답장에 담긴 나에 대한 모욕을 분노에 차 느꼈다."], ["Bruff came in to speak to me on business, before I had recovered possession of myself.", "내가 채 제정신을 되찾기도 전에, 브러프 씨가 볼일로 나에게 이야기하러 들어왔다."], ["I dismissed the business on the spot, and laid the whole case before him.", "나는 그 볼일을 그 자리에서 물리치고, 그 온 사연을 그의 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["He proved to be as incapable of enlightening me as Mrs. Merridew herself.", "그는 메리듀 부인 자신과 다름없이 나를 밝혀 줄 능력이 없는 것으로 드러났다."], ["I asked him if any slander had been spoken of me in Rachel’s hearing. Mr.", "나는 그에게 레이철이 듣는 데에서 나에 대한 어떤 험담이 나온 적이 있는지 물었다."], ["Bruff was not aware of any slander of which I was the object.", "브러프 씨는 내가 그 표적이 된 어떤 험담도 아는 바가 없었다."], ["Had she referred to me in any way while she was staying under Mr.", "그녀가 브러프 씨의 지붕 아래 머무는 동안 어떤 식으로든 나를 언급한 적이 있는가?"], ["Bruff’s roof? Never.", "한 번도 없었다."], ["Had she not so much as asked, during all my long absence, whether I was living or dead?", "나의 그 오랜 부재 내내, 그녀가 내가 살아 있는지 죽었는지조차 물은 적이 있는가?"], ["No such question had ever passed her lips.", "그런 물음은 그녀의 입술을 한 번도 스친 적이 없었다."], ["I took out of my pocket-book the letter which poor Lady Verinder had written to me from Frizinghall, on the day when I left her house in Yorkshire.", "나는 내 수첩에서, 가엾은 레이디 버린더가 내가 요크셔의 그녀의 집을 떠나던 날 프리징홀에서 나에게 써 보낸 그 편지를 꺼냈다."], ["And I pointed Mr. Bruff’s attention to these two sentences in it:", "그리고 나는 그 안의 이 두 문장에 브러프 씨의 주의를 돌렸다."]], [["“The valuable assistance which you rendered to the inquiry after the lost jewel is still an unpardoned offence, in the present dreadful state of Rachel’s mind.", "\"그 잃어버린 보석의 조사에 자네가 베푼 그 값진 도움은, 지금 레이철의 마음의 이 끔찍한 상태에서는, 아직도 용서받지 못한 잘못일세."], ["Moving blindfold in this matter, you have added to the burden of anxiety which she has had to bear, by innocently threatening her secret with discovery through your exertions.”", "이 일에서 눈을 가린 채 움직이며, 자네는 자네의 애씀을 통해 그 아이의 비밀이 드러날 위협을 저도 모르게 가함으로써, 그 아이가 짊어져야 했던 그 근심의 무게를 더해 놓았네.\""]], [["“Is it possible,”", "\"있을 수 있는 일이오,\""], ["I asked, “that the feeling towards me which is there described, is as bitter as ever against me now?”", "하고 나는 물었다. \"거기 그려진 나에 대한 그 감정이, 지금도 나에 대해 여전히 그토록 쓰라리다는 것이?\""]], [["Mr. Bruff looked unaffectedly distressed.", "브러프 씨는 꾸밈없이 괴로워하는 낯빛이었다."]], [["“If you insist on an answer,”", "\"당신이 굳이 대답을 바란다면,\""], ["he said, “I own I can place no other interpretation on her conduct than that.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 그녀의 처신에 그것 말고 다른 해석을 붙일 수 없음을 인정하오.\""]], [["I rang the bell, and directed my servant to pack my portmanteau, and to send out for a railway guide.", "나는 종을 울려, 내 하인에게 내 여행 가방을 꾸리고 철도 안내서를 사 오도록 분부했다."], ["Mr. Bruff asked, in astonishment, what I was going to do.", "브러프 씨가, 놀라움에 차, 내가 무엇을 하려는 것이냐고 물었다."]], [["“I am going to Yorkshire,”", "\"나는 요크셔로 가려 하오,\""], ["I answered, “by the next train.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"다음 기차로 말이오.\""]], [["“May I ask for what purpose?”", "\"무슨 목적으로인지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Mr. Bruff, the assistance I innocently rendered to the inquiry after the Diamond was an unpardoned offence, in Rachel’s mind, nearly a year since; and it remains an unpardoned offence still.", "\"브러프 씨, 그 다이아몬드의 조사에 내가 저도 모르게 베푼 그 도움은, 거의 한 해 전에, 레이철의 마음속에서 용서받지 못한 잘못이었소."], ["I won’t accept that position!", "그리고 그것은 지금도 여전히 용서받지 못한 잘못으로 남아 있소."], ["I am determined to find out the secret of her silence towards her mother, and her enmity towards me.", "나는 그 처지를 받아들이지 않겠소! 나는 그녀가 제 어머니에게 침묵한 그 비밀과, 나에 대한 그 적의의 비밀을 기어이 알아내고야 말겠소."], ["If time, pains, and money can do it, I will lay my hand on the thief who took the Moonstone!”", "만일 시간과 애씀과 돈으로 그것을 할 수 있다면, 나는 월장석을 훔친 그 도둑에게 내 손을 얹고야 말겠소!\""]], [["The worthy old gentleman attempted to remonstrate—to induce me to listen to reason—to do his duty towards me, in short.", "그 인품 있는 노신사는 나를 타이르려—나에게 이치에 귀 기울이게 하려—요컨대, 나를 향한 제 도리를 다하려 애썼다."], ["I was deaf to everything that he could urge.", "나는 그가 다그칠 수 있는 온갖 말에 귀먹은 채였다."], ["No earthly consideration would, at that moment, have shaken the resolution that was in me.", "그 순간, 이 세상의 어떤 사려도 내 안에 든 그 결심을 흔들어 놓지 못했을 것이다."]], [["“I shall take up the inquiry again,”", "\"나는 그 조사를 다시 이어 나가겠소,\" 하고 나는 말을 이었다."], ["I went on, “at the point where I dropped it; and I shall follow it onwards, step by step, till I come to the present time.", "\"내가 그것을 놓아 버린 그 지점에서 말이오. 그리고 나는 그것을 한 걸음 한 걸음 앞으로 좇아, 현재에 다다르기까지 나아가겠소."], ["There are missing links in the evidence, as I left it, which Gabriel Betteredge can supply, and to Gabriel Betteredge I go!”", "내가 남겨 둔 그대로의 그 증거에는 빠진 고리들이 있는데, 그것을 게이브리얼 베터리지가 채워 줄 수 있소. 그러니 게이브리얼 베터리지에게로 나는 가오!\""]], [["Towards sunset that evening I stood again on the well-remembered terrace, and looked once more at the peaceful old country house.", "그날 저녁 해 질 녘에, 나는 다시 그 잊지 못할 테라스에 서서, 그 평화롭고 오래된 시골집을 한 번 더 바라보았다."], ["The gardener was the first person whom I saw in the deserted grounds.", "그 인적 없는 뜰에서 내가 맨 처음 본 사람은 정원사였다."], ["He had left Betteredge, an hour since, sunning himself in the customary corner of the back yard.", "그는 한 시간 전에, 뒤뜰의 늘 그 자리에서 볕을 쬐고 있는 베터리지를 두고 온 참이었다."], ["I knew it well; and I said I would go and seek him myself.", "나는 그곳을 잘 알았다. 그래서 나는 몸소 가서 그를 찾겠노라고 말했다."]], [["I walked round by the familiar paths and passages, and looked in at the open gate of the yard.", "나는 그 낯익은 오솔길과 통로를 돌아 걸어, 그 뜰의 열린 문 안을 들여다보았다."]], [["There he was—the dear old friend of the happy days that were never to come again—there he was in the old corner, on the old beehive chair, with his pipe in his mouth, and his Robinson Crusoe on his lap, and his two friends, the dogs, dozing on either side of him!", "그가 거기 있었다—다시는 돌아오지 않을 그 행복한 날들의, 정다운 옛 벗이—그가 거기, 그 옛 모퉁이에, 그 옛 벌통 모양 의자에, 입에 파이프를 물고, 무릎에 그의 「로빈슨 크루소」를 얹고, 그의 두 벗인 개들을 양옆에 두어 꾸벅꾸벅 졸게 한 채로 있었다!"], ["In the position in which I stood, my shadow was projected in front of me by the last slanting rays of the sun.", "내가 서 있던 그 자리에서, 저무는 해의 마지막 비스듬한 빛살이 내 그림자를 내 앞으로 드리웠다."], ["Either the dogs saw it, or their keen scent informed them of my approach; they started up with a growl.", "그 개들이 그것을 보았거나, 아니면 그것들의 날카로운 후각이 내가 다가옴을 그것들에게 알렸거나 했다."], ["Starting in his turn, the old man quieted them by a word, and then shaded his failing eyes with his hand, and looked inquiringly at the figure at the gate.", "그것들은 으르렁거리며 벌떡 일어섰다. 제 차례로 소스라친 그 노인은 한마디 말로 그것들을 달래고는, 흐릿해진 제 눈을 손으로 가려 그늘을 지우고, 그 문가의 사람 모습을 미심쩍은 듯 바라보았다."]], [["My own eyes were full of tears.", "내 두 눈은 눈물로 가득했다."], ["I was obliged to wait a moment before I could trust myself to speak to him.", "나는 그에게 말을 건넬 만큼 나 자신을 다잡을 수 있기까지 잠시 기다려야만 했다."]], [["# CHAPTER II", "# CHAPTER II"]], [["“Betteredge!”", "\"베터리지!\""], ["I said, pointing to the well-remembered book on his knee, “has Robinson Crusoe informed you, this evening, that you might expect to see Franklin Blake?”", "하고 나는 그의 무릎 위의 그 잊지 못할 책을 가리키며 말했다. \"「로빈슨 크루소」가 오늘 저녁 자네에게, 프랭클린 블레이크를 보게 되리라 여겨도 좋다고 일러 주던가?\""]], [["“By the lord Harry, Mr. Franklin!”", "\"어이쿠 맙소사, 프랭클린 나리!\""], ["cried the old man, “that’s exactly what Robinson Crusoe has done!”", "하고 그 노인이 부르짖었다. \"바로 그것이 「로빈슨 크루소」가 한 일입지요!\""]], [["He struggled to his feet with my assistance, and stood for a moment, looking backwards and forwards between Robinson Crusoe and me, apparently at a loss to discover which of us had surprised him most.", "그는 내 부축을 받아 간신히 일어서서, 잠시 「로빈슨 크루소」와 나 사이를 이리저리 번갈아 보며 서 있었는데, 겉보기에 우리 둘 가운데 어느 쪽이 자기를 가장 놀라게 했는지 가려내지 못하는 눈치였다."], ["The verdict ended in favour of the book.", "그 판정은 그 책 편을 들어 주는 것으로 끝났다."], ["Holding it open before him in both hands, he surveyed the wonderful volume with a stare of unutterable anticipation—as if he expected to see Robinson Crusoe himself walk out of the pages, and favour us with a personal interview.", "그것을 두 손으로 제 앞에 펼쳐 든 채, 그는 그 놀라운 책을 이루 말할 수 없는 기대에 찬 뚫어진 눈길로 살폈다—마치 로빈슨 크루소 그 사람이 책장 밖으로 걸어 나와, 우리에게 몸소 면담의 영예를 베풀어 주기라도 기대하는 것처럼."]], [["“Here’s the bit, Mr. Franklin!”", "\"바로 이 대목입니다, 프랭클린 나리!\""], ["he said, as soon as he had recovered the use of his speech.", "하고 그는 말할 힘을 되찾자마자 말했다."], ["“As I live by bread, sir, here’s the bit I was reading, the moment before you came in!", "\"이 몸이 빵을 먹고 사는 것이 진실이듯, 나리, 이것이 나리께서 들어오시기 바로 그 순간에 제가 읽고 있던 대목입지요!"], ["Page one hundred and fifty-six as follows:—‘I stood like one Thunderstruck, or as if I had seen an Apparition.’ If that isn’t as much as to say: ‘Expect the sudden appearance of Mr.", "백쉰여섯 쪽에 이렇게 나와 있습니다요—'나는 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼, 또는 유령이라도 본 사람처럼 서 있었다.' 만일 그것이 '프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 느닷없는 출현을 기다리라'는 말이나 진배없는 것이 아니라면—영어라는 말에는 아무 뜻도 없는 것입지요!\""], ["Franklin Blake’—there’s no meaning in the English language!” said Betteredge, closing the book with a bang, and getting one of his hands free at last to take the hand which I offered him.", "하고 베터리지는 그 책을 탁 소리 나게 덮고, 마침내 한 손을 자유롭게 놓아 내가 그에게 내민 손을 잡으며 말했다."]], [["I had expected him, naturally enough under the circumstances, to overwhelm me with questions.", "나는, 그런 상황에서라면 지극히 당연하게도, 그가 나에게 물음을 퍼부어 댈 줄 알았다."], ["But no—the hospitable impulse was the uppermost impulse in the old servant’s mind, when a member of the family appeared (no matter how!) as a visitor at the house.", "허나 아니었다—그 집안의 한 사람이 (어찌 되었든 간에!) 그 집의 방문객으로 나타났을 때, 그 늙은 하인의 마음속에서 가장 위에 있는 충동은 손님을 대접하려는 충동이었다."]], [["“Walk in, Mr. Franklin,”", "\"들어오시지요, 프랭클린 나리.\""], ["he said, opening the door behind him, with his quaint old-fashioned bow.", "하고 그는 제 뒤의 문을 열며, 그 예스럽고 별난 몸짓의 인사와 함께 말했다."], ["“I’ll ask what brings you here afterwards—I must make you comfortable first.", "\"무엇이 나리를 여기까지 오게 했는지는 나중에 여쭙겠습니다—우선 나리를 편히 모셔야지요."], ["There have been sad changes, since you went away.", "나리께서 떠나신 뒤로 서글픈 변화가 있었습니다."], ["The house is shut up, and the servants are gone.", "집은 닫혔고, 하인들은 떠났습지요."], ["Never mind that!", "그런 건 마음 쓰지 마십시오!"], ["I’ll cook your dinner; and the gardener’s wife will make your bed—and if there’s a bottle of our famous Latour claret left in the cellar, down your throat, Mr.", "제가 나리의 저녁을 지어 올리겠습니다. 그리고 정원사의 아내가 나리의 잠자리를 봐 드리지요—그리고 지하실에 우리의 그 이름난 라투르 클라레 한 병이 남아 있다면, 나리, 그 병은 나리의 목구멍으로 넘어갈 것입니다."], ["Franklin, that bottle shall go.", "나리를 환영합니다요, 나리!"], ["I bid you welcome, sir!", "진심으로 환영합니다요!\""], ["I bid you heartily welcome!” said the poor old fellow, fighting manfully against the gloom of the deserted house, and receiving me with the sociable and courteous attention of the bygone time.", "하고 그 가엾은 노인은, 그 인적 없는 집의 음울함에 맞서 사내답게 싸우며, 지난 시절의 그 정겹고 정중한 대접으로 나를 맞으며 말했다."]], [["It vexed me to disappoint him.", "그를 실망시키는 것이 나에게는 괴로웠다."], ["But the house was Rachel’s house, now.", "허나 그 집은, 이제, 레이철의 집이었다."], ["Could I eat in it, or sleep in it, after what had happened in London?", "런던에서 일어난 일 뒤에, 내가 그 안에서 밥을 먹거나 잠을 잘 수 있단 말인가?"], ["The commonest sense of self-respect forbade me—properly forbade me—to cross the threshold.", "자존이라는 지극히 흔한 분별이 나에게—마땅히도—그 문지방을 넘는 것을 금했다."]], [["I took Betteredge by the arm, and led him out into the garden.", "나는 베터리지의 팔을 잡고, 그를 이끌어 정원으로 나갔다."], ["There was no help for it.", "달리 도리가 없었다."], ["I was obliged to tell him the truth.", "나는 그에게 진실을 말해 주지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Between his attachment to Rachel, and his attachment to me, he was sorely puzzled and distressed at the turn things had taken.", "레이철에 대한 애착과 나에 대한 애착 사이에서, 그는 일이 이렇게 돌아간 데에 몹시 어리둥절해하고 괴로워했다."], ["His opinion, when he expressed it, was given in his usual downright manner, and was agreeably redolent of the most positive philosophy I know—the philosophy of the Betteredge school.", "그의 견해는, 그가 그것을 내놓았을 때, 그의 여느 거침없는 투로 주어졌고, 내가 아는 가장 단정적인 철학—베터리지 학파의 철학의 향취를 기분 좋게 물씬 풍겼다."]], [["“Miss Rachel has her faults—I’ve never denied it,” he began.", "\"레이철 아가씨께는 흠이 있습지요—저는 그것을 한 번도 부인한 적이 없습니다.\""], ["“And riding the high horse, now and then, is one of them.", "하고 그가 말을 뗐다. \"그리고 이따금 콧대를 세우고 내닫는 것이 그중 하나입니다요."], ["She has been trying to ride over you—and you have put up with it.", "그 아가씨는 나리를 타 넘으려 애써 오셨습니다—그리고 나리는 그것을 참아 오셨지요."], ["Lord, Mr. Franklin, don’t you know women by this time better than that?", "원 참, 프랭클린 나리, 이 나이 되도록 여자를 그보다는 잘 아셔야지요?"], ["You have heard me talk of the late Mrs. Betteredge?”", "고인이 된 베터리지 마누라 이야기를 제가 하는 걸 들으신 적 있으시지요?\""]], [["I had heard him talk of the late Mrs. Betteredge pretty often—invariably producing her as his one undeniable example of the inbred frailty and perversity of the other sex.", "나는 그가 고인이 된 베터리지 부인 이야기를 하는 것을 꽤 자주 들었었다—그는 어김없이 그녀를, 그 다른 성(性)의 타고난 무름과 어깃장의, 그의 부인할 수 없는 유일한 본보기로 내세우곤 했다."], ["In that capacity he exhibited her now.", "그 자격으로 그는 이제 그녀를 다시 끄집어냈다."]], [["“Very well, Mr.", "\"자, 프랭클린 나리."], ["Franklin. Now listen to me.", "이제 제 말을 들어 보십시오."], ["Different women have different ways of riding the high horse.", "여자마다 콧대 세우고 내닫는 방식이 다 다릅니다요."], ["The late Mrs. Betteredge took her exercise on that favourite female animal whenever I happened to deny her anything that she had set her heart on.", "고인이 된 베터리지 마누라는, 제가 마침 그 여편네가 마음에 둔 무언가를 안 들어줄 때면 언제든, 바로 그 여자들이 즐겨 타는 짐승 위에 올라 그 운동을 즐겼습지요."], ["So sure as I came home from my work on these occasions, so sure was my wife to call to me up the kitchen stairs, and to say that, after my brutal treatment of her, she hadn’t the heart to cook me my dinner.", "이럴 때 제가 일을 마치고 집에 돌아오기가 무섭게, 어김없이 제 마누라는 부엌 계단 위로 저를 불러, 제가 저를 그리 모질게 다루었으니 저에게 저녁을 지어 줄 마음이 없다고 말하곤 했습니다요."], ["I put up with it for some time—just as you are putting up with it now from Miss Rachel.", "저는 한동안 그것을 참았습지요—꼭 나리께서 지금 레이철 아가씨한테 참고 계신 것처럼요."], ["At last my patience wore out.", "마침내 제 인내가 바닥났습니다."], ["I went downstairs, and I took Mrs. Betteredge—affectionately, you understand—up in my arms, and carried her, holus-bolus, into the best parlour where she received her company.", "저는 아래층으로 내려가, 베터리지 마누라를—애정을 담아서요, 아시겠지요—두 팔로 안아 들고, 통째로 번쩍, 그 여자가 손님을 맞던 가장 좋은 응접실로 데려갔습지요."], ["I said ‘That’s the right place for you, my dear,’ and so went back to the kitchen.", "저는 '이곳이 임자에게 딱 맞는 자리요, 여보,' 하고는, 그길로 부엌으로 돌아갔습니다."], ["I locked myself in, and took off my coat, and turned up my shirt-sleeves, and cooked my own dinner.", "저는 안에서 문을 걸어 잠그고, 웃옷을 벗고, 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올리고, 제 저녁을 손수 지었습지요."], ["When it was done, I served it up in my best manner, and enjoyed it most heartily.", "다 되자, 저는 제가 할 수 있는 가장 훌륭한 솜씨로 그것을 차려 내어, 더없이 흐뭇하게 즐겼습니다."], ["I had my pipe and my drop of grog afterwards; and then I cleared the table, and washed the crockery, and cleaned the knives and forks, and put the things away, and swept up the hearth.", "뒤이어 저는 제 파이프와 저의 그로그 한 모금을 즐겼지요. 그러고는 상을 치우고, 그릇을 씻고, 나이프와 포크를 닦고, 물건들을 제자리에 넣고, 벽난로 아궁이를 쓸었습니다."], ["When things were as bright and clean again, as bright and clean could be, I opened the door and let Mrs.", "온갖 것이 다시, 반들반들 깨끗할 수 있는 만큼 반들반들 깨끗해졌을 때, 저는 문을 열어 베터리지 마누라를 들였습지요. '나는 저녁을 다 먹었소, 여보,'"], ["Betteredge in.", "하고 저는 말했습니다."], ["‘I’ve had my dinner, my dear,’ I said; ‘and I hope you will find that I have left the kitchen all that your fondest wishes can desire.’ For the rest of that woman’s life, Mr.", "'그리고 임자의 가장 살가운 소원이 바랄 수 있는 그 모든 대로 부엌을 남겨 둔 것을 임자가 알아주기 바라오.' 그 여자가 사는 남은 평생 동안, 프랭클린 나리, 저는 다시는 제 저녁을 지을 일이 없었습지요!"], ["Franklin, I never had to cook my dinner again!", "교훈인즉—나리는 런던에서 레이철 아가씨를 참으셨습니다."], ["Moral: You have put up with Miss Rachel in London; don’t put up with her in Yorkshire.", "요크셔에서는 참지 마십시오."], ["Come back to the house!”", "집으로 돌아오시지요!\""]], [["Quite unanswerable!", "도무지 대꾸할 수 없었다!"], ["I could only assure my good friend that even his powers of persuasion were, in this case, thrown away on me.", "나는 다만 나의 그 착한 벗에게, 이 경우에는 그의 그 설득의 힘조차 나에게 헛되이 쓰인 것이라고 다짐해 줄 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“It’s a lovely evening,”", "\"참으로 좋은 저녁일세.\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["“I shall walk to Frizinghall, and stay at the hotel, and you must come tomorrow morning and breakfast with me.", "\"나는 프리징홀까지 걸어가, 그 호텔에 묵겠네. 그러니 자네는 내일 아침 와서 나와 함께 아침을 들게."], ["I have something to say to you.”", "자네에게 할 이야기가 있네.\""]], [["Betteredge shook his head gravely.", "베터리지는 무겁게 고개를 저었다."]], [["“I am heartily sorry for this,” he said.", "\"이것 참 진심으로 안됐습니다요.\""], ["“I had hoped, Mr.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["Franklin, to hear that things were all smooth and pleasant again between you and Miss Rachel.", "\"저는, 프랭클린 나리, 나리와 레이철 아가씨 사이가 다시 모두 매끄럽고 즐겁게 되었다는 말을 듣기를 바랐습지요."], ["If you must have your own way, sir,”", "나리께서 굳이 나리 뜻대로 하셔야겠다면, 나리,\""], ["he continued, after a moment’s reflection, “there is no need to go to Frizinghall tonight for a bed.", "하고 그는 잠시 곰곰 생각하고서 말을 이었다. \"잠자리 때문에 오늘 밤 프리징홀까지 가실 것은 없습니다."], ["It’s to be had nearer than that.", "그보다 더 가까운 데서 얻을 수 있습지요."], ["There’s Hotherstone’s Farm, barely two miles from here.", "여기서 채 이 마일도 안 되는 곳에 호더스톤 농장이 있습니다."], ["You can hardly object to that on Miss Rachel’s account,”", "그거라면 나리께서 레이철 아가씨 때문에 마다하시기도 어려울 겁니다요,\""], ["the old man added slily.", "하고 그 노인은 능청스레 덧붙였다."], ["“Hotherstone lives, Mr. Franklin, on his own freehold.”", "\"호더스톤은요, 프랭클린 나리, 제 소유의 자유보유지에서 삽지요.\""]], [["I remembered the place the moment Betteredge mentioned it.", "베터리지가 그곳을 입에 올리는 순간 나는 그 자리를 떠올렸다."], ["The farm-house stood in a sheltered inland valley, on the banks of the prettiest stream in that part of Yorkshire: and the farmer had a spare bedroom and parlour, which he was accustomed to let to artists, anglers, and tourists in general.", "그 농가는 요크셔의 그 지역에서 가장 어여쁜 개울가의, 바람 자는 내륙의 골짜기에 서 있었다. 그리고 그 농부에게는 남는 침실과 응접실이 하나씩 있었는데, 그는 그것을 화가와 낚시꾼과 여느 나그네들에게 세놓곤 했다."], ["A more agreeable place of abode, during my stay in the neighbourhood, I could not have wished to find.", "그 근방에 머무는 동안 그보다 더 마음에 드는 거처를, 나는 도무지 찾기를 바랄 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["“Are the rooms to let?”", "\"그 방들을 세놓는가?\""], ["I inquired.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Mrs. Hotherstone herself, sir, asked for my good word to recommend the rooms, yesterday.”", "\"바로 어제, 나리, 호더스톤 부인이 몸소 그 방들을 추천해 달라고 제 좋은 말을 청했습지요.\""]], [["“I’ll take them, Betteredge, with the greatest pleasure.”", "\"그 방들을 얻겠네, 베터리지, 더없이 기쁜 마음으로.\""]], [["We went back to the yard, in which I had left my travelling-bag.", "우리는, 내가 내 여행 가방을 두고 온 그 뜰로 돌아갔다."], ["After putting a stick through the handle, and swinging the bag over his shoulder, Betteredge appeared to relapse into the bewilderment which my sudden appearance had caused, when I surprised him in the beehive chair.", "손잡이에 막대기를 꿰어 그 가방을 어깨에 둘러멘 뒤, 베터리지는, 내가 그 벌통 의자에서 그를 놀라게 했을 때 나의 느닷없는 출현이 자아냈던 그 어리둥절함으로 도로 빠져드는 듯했다."], ["He looked incredulously at the house, and then he wheeled about, and looked more incredulously still at me.", "그는 못 믿겠다는 듯 그 집을 보았고, 그러고는 홱 돌아서, 못 믿겠다는 눈길로 나를 한층 더 오래 보았다."]], [["“I’ve lived a goodish long time in the world,”", "\"이 몸이 세상을 제법 오래 살아 왔습니다요.\""], ["said this best and dearest of all old servants—“but the like of this, I never did expect to see.", "하고 온 늙은 하인들 가운데 가장 착하고 가장 정다운 이 사람이 말했다."], ["There stands the house, and here stands Mr.", "\"허나 이런 일은, 저는 여태 보게 되리라 여겨 본 적이 없습지요."], ["Franklin Blake—and, Damme, if one of them isn’t turning his back on the other, and going to sleep in a lodging!”", "저기 집이 서 있고, 여기 프랭클린 블레이크 나리가 서 있는데—에잇, 빌어먹을, 그중 하나가 다른 하나에 등을 돌리고, 셋방에 자러 간다니 말입니다!\""]], [["He led the way out, wagging his head and growling ominously.", "그는 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 불길하게 투덜대면서 앞장서 나갔다."], ["“There’s only one more miracle that can happen,”", "\"이제 일어날 수 있는 기적이 딱 하나 더 남았습지요.\""], ["he said to me, over his shoulder.", "하고 그가 어깨 너머로 나에게 말했다."], ["“The next thing you’ll do, Mr. Franklin, will be to pay me back that seven-and-sixpence you borrowed of me when you were a boy.”", "\"나리께서 다음으로 하실 일은, 프랭클린 나리, 어릴 적에 저한테서 꾸어 가신 그 칠 실링 육 펜스를 저에게 갚으시는 것일 겁니다요.\""]], [["This stroke of sarcasm put him in a better humour with himself and with me.", "이 빈정거림의 한 방이 그를 그 자신에 대해서도, 나에 대해서도 한결 나은 기분으로 만들어 놓았다."], ["We left the house, and passed through the lodge gates.", "우리는 그 집을 떠나, 관리인 오두막의 문을 지나갔다."], ["Once clear of the grounds, the duties of hospitality (in Betteredge’s code of morals) ceased, and the privileges of curiosity began.", "일단 그 뜰을 벗어나자, (베터리지의 도덕률에서는) 손님 대접의 의무가 그치고, 호기심의 특권이 시작되었다."]], [["He dropped back, so as to let me get on a level with him.", "그는, 내가 그와 나란히 서게끔, 뒤로 처졌다."], ["“Fine evening for a walk, Mr. Franklin,”", "\"산책하기 좋은 저녁입니다요, 프랭클린 나리.\""], ["he said, as if we had just accidentally encountered each other at that moment.", "하고 그는, 마치 우리가 방금 그 순간 우연히 서로 마주치기라도 한 것처럼 말했다."], ["“Supposing you had gone to the hotel at Frizinghall, sir?”", "\"가령 나리께서 프리징홀의 그 호텔로 가셨다면 말입니다, 나리?\""]], [["“Yes?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“I should have had the honour of breakfasting with you, tomorrow morning.”", "\"저는 내일 아침 나리와 함께 아침을 드는 영예를 누렸겠지요.\""]], [["“Come and breakfast with me at Hotherstone’s Farm, instead.”", "\"그러지 말고 호더스톤 농장으로 와서 나와 아침을 들게.\""]], [["“Much obliged to you for your kindness, Mr. Franklin.", "\"친절히 대해 주시니 참으로 고맙습니다요, 프랭클린 나리."], ["But it wasn’t exactly breakfast that I was driving at.", "허나 제가 노린 것이 딱히 아침은 아니었습지요."], ["I think you mentioned that you had something to say to me?", "나리께서 저에게 하실 이야기가 있다고 말씀하신 것 같은데요?"], ["If it’s no secret, sir,”", "만일 비밀이 아니라면, 나리,\""], ["said Betteredge, suddenly abandoning the crooked way, and taking the straight one, “I’m burning to know what’s brought you down here, if you please, in this sudden way.”", "하고 베터리지는, 별안간 그 에두르는 길을 버리고 곧은 길로 들어서며 말했다. \"무엇이 나리를, 부디 말씀해 주십시오, 이리 느닷없이 여기까지 내려오게 했는지 저는 몹시 궁금해 죽겠습니다요.\""]], [["“What brought me here before?”", "\"전에는 무엇이 나를 여기 오게 했던가?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“The Moonstone, Mr.", "\"월장석입지요, 프랭클린 나리."], ["Franklin. But what brings you now, sir?”", "허나 지금은 무엇이 나리를 오게 했습니까, 나리?\""]], [["“The Moonstone again, Betteredge.”", "\"다시 월장석일세, 베터리지.\""]], [["The old man suddenly stood still, and looked at me in the grey twilight as if he suspected his own ears of deceiving him.", "그 노인은 별안간 우뚝 멈춰 서서, 그 잿빛 땅거미 속에서, 마치 제 두 귀가 저를 속이는 것은 아닌가 의심하는 듯 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“If that’s a joke, sir,”", "\"만일 그것이 농담이라면, 나리,\""], ["he said, “I’m afraid I’m getting a little dull in my old age.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 이 늙은 나이에 머리가 좀 둔해지나 봅니다요."], ["I don’t take it.”", "저는 그걸 못 알아듣겠습니다.\""]], [["“It’s no joke,”", "\"농담이 아닐세.\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“I have come here to take up the inquiry which was dropped when I left England.", "\"나는 내가 잉글랜드를 떠날 때 놓아 버린 그 조사를 다시 이어 가려고 여기 왔네."], ["I have come here to do what nobody has done yet—to find out who took the Diamond.”", "나는 아직 아무도 하지 못한 일을 하려고—누가 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔는지 알아내려고 여기 왔네.\""]], [["“Let the Diamond be, Mr. Franklin!", "\"그 다이아몬드는 내버려 두십시오, 프랭클린 나리!"], ["Take my advice, and let the Diamond be!", "제 말을 들으시고, 그 다이아몬드는 내버려 두십시오!"], ["That cursed Indian jewel has misguided everybody who has come near it.", "그 저주받은 인도 보석은 그것에 가까이 다가간 누구든 죄 그르쳐 놓았습지요."], ["Don’t waste your money and your temper—in the fine spring time of your life, sir—by meddling with the Moonstone.", "나리의 삶의 그 고운 봄철에, 나리—월장석에 참견하다가 나리의 돈과 나리의 심기를 허비하지 마십시오."], ["How can you hope to succeed (saving your presence), when Sergeant Cuff himself made a mess of it?", "커프 부장 자신이 그 일을 죽 쒀 놨는데, 어찌 나리가 (외람되오나) 성공하기를 바라시겠습니까?"], ["Sergeant Cuff!”", "커프 부장이요!\""], ["repeated Betteredge, shaking his forefinger at me sternly.", "하고 베터리지는, 나를 향해 집게손가락을 엄히 흔들며 되뇌었다."], ["“The greatest policeman in England!”", "\"잉글랜드에서 가장 위대한 경관 말입니다!\""]], [["“My mind is made up, my old friend.", "\"내 마음은 정해졌네, 나의 오랜 벗이여."], ["Even Sergeant Cuff doesn’t daunt me.", "커프 부장조차 나를 주눅 들게 하지 못하네."], ["By-the-bye, I may want to speak to him, sooner or later.", "그러고 보니, 나는 조만간 그와 이야기해야 할지도 모르겠군."], ["Have you heard anything of him lately?”", "근래에 그에 관해 무슨 들은 것이 있는가?\""]], [["“The Sergeant won’t help you, Mr. Franklin.”", "\"그 부장은 나리를 돕지 못합니다요, 프랭클린 나리.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"어째서?\""]], [["“There has been an event, sir, in the police-circles, since you went away.", "\"나리께서 떠나신 뒤로, 나리, 경찰 바닥에 한 사건이 있었습지요."], ["The great Cuff has retired from business.", "그 위대한 커프가 일에서 물러났습니다."], ["He has got a little cottage at Dorking; and he’s up to his eyes in the growing of roses.", "그는 도킹에 자그마한 오두막 하나를 얻었습지요. 그리고 그는 장미 기르는 일에 눈코 뜰 새가 없습니다."], ["I have it in his own handwriting, Mr. Franklin.", "저는 그것을 그 사람 자신의 필적으로 받았습니다요, 프랭클린 나리."], ["He has grown the white moss rose, without budding it on the dog-rose first.", "그는 흰 이끼장미를, 먼저 들장미에 접붙이지 않고 길러 냈습지요."], ["And Mr. Begbie the gardener is to go to Dorking, and own that the Sergeant has beaten him at last.”", "그리고 정원사 베그비 씨가 도킹으로 가서, 그 부장이 마침내 자기를 이겼음을 인정하기로 되어 있습니다.\""]], [["“It doesn’t much matter,” I said.", "\"그것은 그리 대수롭지 않네.\""], ["“I must do without Sergeant Cuff’s help.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 커프 부장의 도움 없이 해내야 하네."], ["And I must trust to you, at starting.”", "그리고 나는, 우선, 자네를 믿어야 하네.\""]], [["It is likely enough that I spoke rather carelessly.", "내가 다소 무심하게 말했을 법도 하다."]], [["At any rate, Betteredge seemed to be piqued by something in the reply which I had just made to him.", "어쨌든, 베터리지는 내가 방금 그에게 한 그 대답 속의 무언가에 마음이 상한 듯했다."], ["“You might trust to worse than me, Mr. Franklin—I can tell you that,”", "\"저보다 못한 이를 믿으실 수도 있습지요, 프랭클린 나리—그것만은 제가 말씀드릴 수 있습니다,\""], ["he said a little sharply.", "하고 그가 다소 날카롭게 말했다."]], [["The tone in which he retorted, and a certain disturbance, after he had spoken, which I detected in his manner, suggested to me that he was possessed of some information which he hesitated to communicate.", "그가 되받은 그 어조와, 말을 마친 뒤 그의 태도에서 내가 알아챈 어떤 동요가, 나에게 그가 전하기를 망설이는 어떤 정보를 지니고 있음을 넌지시 일러 주었다."]], [["“I expect you to help me,”", "\"나는 자네가 나를 도와주기를 바라네.\""], ["I said, “in picking up the fragments of evidence which Sergeant Cuff has left behind him.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"커프 부장이 뒤에 남기고 간 그 증거의 조각들을 주워 모으는 데에 말일세."], ["I know you can do that.", "나는 자네가 그것을 할 수 있음을 아네."], ["Can you do no more?”", "자네는 그 이상은 못 하는가?\""]], [["“What more can you expect from me, sir?”", "\"저에게서 그 이상 무엇을 바라실 수 있겠습니까, 나리?\""], ["asked Betteredge, with an appearance of the utmost humility.", "하고 베터리지는 더없이 겸손한 낯빛으로 물었다."]], [["“I expect more—from what you said just now.”", "\"나는 그 이상을 바라네—자네가 방금 한 말로 보아서 말일세.\""]], [["“Mere boasting, Mr. Franklin,”", "\"한낱 허풍입지요, 프랭클린 나리,\""], ["returned the old man obstinately.", "하고 그 노인은 고집스레 되받았다."], ["“Some people are born boasters, and they never get over it to their dying day.", "\"어떤 사람들은 허풍쟁이로 태어나, 죽는 날까지 그것을 못 고칩니다요."], ["I’m one of them.”", "저도 그중 하납니다.\""]], [["There was only one way to take with him.", "그에게 취할 방도는 오직 하나였다."], ["I appealed to his interest in Rachel, and his interest in me.", "나는 레이철에 대한 그의 마음과 나에 대한 그의 마음에 호소했다."]], [["“Betteredge, would you be glad to hear that Rachel and I were good friends again?”", "\"베터리지, 레이철과 내가 다시 좋은 벗이 되었다는 말을 들으면 자네는 기쁘겠는가?\""]], [["“I have served your family, sir, to mighty little purpose, if you doubt it!”", "\"만일 그것을 의심하신다면, 나리, 제가 나리의 집안을 섬긴 것이 참으로 하찮은 보람밖에 없는 것입지요!\""]], [["“Do you remember how Rachel treated me, before I left England?”", "\"내가 잉글랜드를 떠나기 전에 레이철이 나를 어찌 대했는지 기억하는가?\""]], [["“As well as if it was yesterday!", "\"바로 어제 일인 양 똑똑히요!"], ["My lady herself wrote you a letter about it; and you were so good as to show the letter to me.", "마님께서 몸소 그것에 관해 나리께 편지를 쓰셨습지요. 그리고 나리는 친절하게도 그 편지를 저에게 보여 주셨습니다."], ["It said that Miss Rachel was mortally offended with you, for the part you had taken in trying to recover her jewel.", "그 편지에는, 레이철 아가씨가 그 보석을 되찾으려 애쓴 데에 나리께서 맡은 몫 때문에 나리께 죽도록 마음이 상하셨다고 적혀 있었습니다."], ["And neither my lady, nor you, nor anybody else could guess why.", "그리고 마님도, 나리도, 다른 그 누구도 어째서인지 짐작할 수 없었습지요.\""]], [["“Quite true, Betteredge!", "\"바로 그렇네, 베터리지!"], ["And I come back from my travels, and find her mortally offended with me still.", "그런데 나는 여행에서 돌아와, 그녀가 여전히 나에게 죽도록 마음 상해 있는 것을 보네."], ["I knew that the Diamond was at the bottom of it, last year, and I know that the Diamond is at the bottom of it now.", "나는 작년에 그 다이아몬드가 그 밑바탕에 있음을 알았고, 지금도 그 다이아몬드가 그 밑바탕에 있음을 아네."], ["I have tried to speak to her, and she won’t see me.", "나는 그녀에게 말을 걸어 보려 했으나, 그녀는 나를 만나려 하지 않네."], ["I have tried to write to her, and she won’t answer me.", "나는 그녀에게 편지를 써 보려 했으나, 그녀는 나에게 답하려 하지 않네."], ["How, in Heaven’s name, am I to clear the matter up?", "대체, 도무지, 내가 어떻게 이 일을 밝혀낸단 말인가?"], ["The chance of searching into the loss of the Moonstone, is the one chance of inquiry that Rachel herself has left me.”", "월장석의 분실을 캐어 볼 그 기회가, 레이철 자신이 나에게 남겨 준 유일한 조사의 기회일세.\""]], [["Those words evidently put the case before him, as he had not seen it yet.", "그 말들은 분명, 그가 아직 보지 못했던 대로, 그 사건을 그의 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["He asked a question which satisfied me that I had shaken him.", "그는 내가 그를 흔들어 놓았음을 나에게 흡족히 확인시켜 주는 물음 하나를 던졌다."]], [["“There is no ill-feeling in this, Mr. Franklin, on your side—is there?”", "\"이 일에, 프랭클린 나리, 나리 편에는 아무 나쁜 감정이 없는 것입지요—그렇지요?\""]], [["“There was some anger,”", "\"약간의 노여움은 있었네.\""], ["I answered, “when I left London.", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"런던을 떠날 적에 말일세."], ["But that is all worn out now.", "허나 그것은 이제 죄 닳아 없어졌네."], ["I want to make Rachel come to an understanding with me—and I want nothing more.”", "나는 레이철이 나와 서로 이해에 이르게 되기를 바라네—그리고 나는 그 이상 아무것도 바라지 않네.\""]], [["“You don’t feel any fear, sir—supposing you make any discoveries—in regard to what you may find out about Miss Rachel?”", "\"나리는 아무 두려움도 느끼지 않으십니까, 나리—가령 나리께서 무언가 알아내신다 치고—레이철 아가씨에 관해 나리께서 알아내실지 모를 것에 대해서요?\""]], [["I understood the jealous belief in his young mistress which prompted those words.", "나는 그 말들을 자아낸, 그의 젊은 여주인에 대한 그 질투 어린 믿음을 헤아렸다."]], [["“I am as certain of her as you are,”", "\"나는 자네만큼이나 그녀를 굳게 믿네.\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“The fullest disclosure of her secret will reveal nothing that can alter her place in your estimation, or in mine.”", "\"그녀의 비밀이 낱낱이 드러난들, 자네의 평가에서든 나의 평가에서든 그녀의 자리를 바꿔 놓을 만한 것은 아무것도 드러나지 않을 걸세.\""]], [["Betteredge’s last-left scruples vanished at that.", "베터리지의 마지막 남은 망설임이 그 말에 사라졌다."]], [["“If I am doing wrong to help you, Mr.", "\"만일 제가 나리를 돕는 것이 잘못하는 것이라면, 프랭클린 나리,\""], ["Franklin,”", "하고 그가 외쳤다."], ["he exclaimed, “all I can say is—I am as innocent of seeing it as the babe unborn!", "\"제가 말씀드릴 수 있는 것은 오직—저는 그것을 아직 세상에 나지 않은 갓난아기만큼이나 알아채지 못하고 있다는 것뿐입니다요!"], ["I can put you on the road to discovery, if you can only go on by yourself.", "나리께서 홀로 나아가시기만 한다면, 저는 나리를 그 발견으로 가는 길에 올려 드릴 수 있습니다."], ["You remember that poor girl of ours—Rosanna Spearman?”", "우리의 그 가엾은 아이—로재나 스피어먼을 기억하시지요?\""]], [["“Of course!”", "\"물론이지!\""]], [["“You always thought she had some sort of confession in regard to this matter of the Moonstone, which she wanted to make to you?”", "\"나리는 늘, 그 아이가 이 월장석의 일에 관해 나리께 털어놓고 싶어 하는 어떤 고백 같은 것을 지녔다고 여기셨지요?\""]], [["“I certainly couldn’t account for her strange conduct in any other way.”", "\"나는 정녕 그녀의 그 이상한 처신을 달리는 도무지 설명할 수 없었네.\""]], [["“You may set that doubt at rest, Mr. Franklin, whenever you please.”", "\"나리는 그 의심을, 프랭클린 나리, 언제든 나리가 원하실 때 쉬게 하실 수 있습니다요.\""]], [["It was my turn to come to a standstill now.", "이제는 내가 우뚝 멈춰 설 차례였다."], ["I tried vainly, in the gathering darkness, to see his face.", "나는, 짙어 가는 어둠 속에서, 그의 얼굴을 보려 했으나 헛일이었다."], ["In the surprise of the moment, I asked a little impatiently what he meant.", "그 순간의 놀라움에, 나는 다소 조바심을 내며 그것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었다."]], [["“Steady, sir!”", "\"진정하십시오, 나리!\""], ["proceeded Betteredge.", "하고 베터리지가 말을 이었다."], ["“I mean what I say.", "\"저는 제가 말하는 그대로를 뜻합니다."], ["Rosanna Spearman left a sealed letter behind her—a letter addressed to you.”", "로재나 스피어먼은 봉인된 편지 한 통을 뒤에 남겼습지요—나리 앞으로 된 편지 말입니다.\""]], [["“Where is it?”", "\"그것이 어디 있는가?\""]], [["“In the possession of a friend of hers, at Cobb’s Hole.", "\"코브스 홀에 있는 그 아이의 벗 하나가 지니고 있습니다요."], ["You must have heard tell, when you were here last, sir, of Limping Lucy—a lame girl with a crutch.”", "나리께서 지난번 여기 계실 때, 나리, 절름발이 루시 이야기를 들으셨을 겁니다—목발을 짚은 저는 아이 말입니다.\""]], [["“The fisherman’s daughter?”", "\"그 어부의 딸 말인가?\""]], [["“The same, Mr. Franklin.”", "\"바로 그 아이입지요, 프랭클린 나리.\""]], [["“Why wasn’t the letter forwarded to me?”", "\"어째서 그 편지가 나에게 부쳐지지 않았는가?\""]], [["“Limping Lucy has a will of her own, sir.", "\"절름발이 루시는 제 나름의 고집이 있습니다, 나리."], ["She wouldn’t give it into any hands but yours.", "그 아이는 그것을 나리의 손이 아닌 어떤 손에도 내주려 하지 않았습지요."], ["And you had left England before I could write to you.”", "그리고 나리는 제가 나리께 편지를 쓸 수 있기도 전에 잉글랜드를 떠나셨습니다.\""]], [["“Let’s go back, Betteredge, and get it at once!”", "\"돌아가세, 베터리지, 지금 당장 그것을 받으러 가세!\""]], [["“Too late, sir, tonight.", "\"오늘 밤은 너무 늦었습니다요, 나리."], ["They’re great savers of candles along our coast; and they go to bed early at Cobb’s Hole.”", "우리 해안 사람들은 초를 무척 아끼는지라, 코브스 홀에서는 일찍 잠자리에 듭지요.\""]], [["“Nonsense!", "\"당치 않은 소리!"], ["We might get there in half an hour.”", "우리는 반 시간이면 거기 갈 수 있을 걸세.\""]], [["“You might, sir.", "\"나리라면 그럴 수 있겠지요, 나리."], ["And when you did get there, you would find the door locked.”", "그리고 나리가 거기 다다르셨을 때, 나리는 문이 잠긴 것을 보시게 될 겁니다.\""], ["He pointed to a light, glimmering below us; and, at the same moment, I heard through the stillness of the evening the bubbling of a stream.", "그는 우리 아래쪽에서 가물거리는 불빛 하나를 가리켰다. 그리고, 바로 그 순간, 나는 그 저녁의 고요를 뚫고 개울이 졸졸 흐르는 소리를 들었다."], ["“There’s the Farm, Mr. Franklin!", "\"저기 그 농장입니다, 프랭클린 나리!"], ["Make yourself comfortable for tonight, and come to me tomorrow morning—if you’ll be so kind?”", "오늘 밤은 편히 쉬시고, 내일 아침 저에게로 오십시오—그리 해 주시겠지요?\""]], [["“You will go with me to the fisherman’s cottage?”", "\"자네가 나와 함께 그 어부의 오두막에 가 주겠는가?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["“Early?”", "\"이른 아침에?\""]], [["“As early, Mr. Franklin, as you like.”", "\"나리가 원하시는 만큼 이른 아침에요, 프랭클린 나리.\""]], [["We descended the path that led to the Farm.", "우리는 그 농장으로 이어지는 그 오솔길을 내려갔다."]], [["# CHAPTER III", "# CHAPTER III"]], [["I have only the most indistinct recollection of what happened at Hotherstone’s Farm.", "호더스톤 농장에서 일어난 일에 대해 나는 그저 더없이 흐릿한 기억밖에 없다."]], [["I remember a hearty welcome; a prodigious supper, which would have fed a whole village in the East; a delightfully clean bedroom, with nothing in it to regret but that detestable product of the folly of our forefathers—a feather-bed; a restless night, with much kindling of matches, and many lightings of one little candle; and an immense sensation of relief when the sun rose, and there was a prospect of getting up.", "나는 기억한다—정성 어린 환대, 동방에서라면 온 마을을 다 먹였을 어마어마한 저녁, 더없이 정갈한 침실—그 안에 아쉬운 것이라고는 우리 조상들의 어리석음이 낳은 저 몹쓸 산물, 곧 깃털 침대뿐이었던, 성냥을 여러 번 그어 대고 작은 초 하나에 여러 번 불을 붙이며 지새운 뒤척인 밤, 그리고 해가 떠올라 이제 일어날 전망이 생겼을 때의 그 어마어마한 안도감을."]], [["It had been arranged over-night with Betteredge, that I was to call for him, on our way to Cobb’s Hole, as early as I liked—which, interpreted by my impatience to get possession of the letter, meant as early as I could.", "밤사이 베터리지와, 코브스 홀로 가는 길에 내가 원하는 만큼 이른 아침에 그를 부르러 들르기로 정해 두었었는데—그것은 그 편지를 손에 넣으려는 나의 조바심으로 헤아리면, 내가 그럴 수 있는 한 가장 이른 아침을 뜻했다."], ["Without waiting for breakfast at the Farm, I took a crust of bread in my hand, and set forth, in some doubt whether I should not surprise the excellent Betteredge in his bed.", "농장에서 아침을 기다리지 않고, 나는 빵 한 조각을 손에 쥐고 길을 나섰는데, 그 훌륭한 베터리지를 잠자리에서 깜짝 놀라게 하는 것은 아닐까 다소 미심쩍었다."], ["To my great relief he proved to be quite as excited about the coming event as I was.", "나의 큰 안도로, 그는 나만큼이나 다가올 일에 들떠 있는 것으로 드러났다."], ["I found him ready, and waiting for me, with his stick in his hand.", "나는 그가 채비를 갖추고, 손에 지팡이를 든 채 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“How are you this morning, Betteredge?”", "\"오늘 아침 몸은 어떤가, 베터리지?\""]], [["“Very poorly, sir.”", "\"아주 형편없습니다요, 나리.\""]], [["“Sorry to hear it.", "\"안됐군."], ["What do you complain of?”", "무엇이 괴로운가?\""]], [["“I complain of a new disease, Mr. Franklin, of my own inventing.", "\"저는 새로운 병 하나로 괴로워하고 있습지요, 프랭클린 나리, 제가 몸소 지어낸 병 말입니다."], ["I don’t want to alarm you, but you’re certain to catch it before the morning is out.”", "나리를 놀라게 하고 싶지는 않습니다만, 나리도 아침이 다 가기 전에 틀림없이 그것에 걸리실 겁니다요.\""]], [["“The devil I am!”", "\"내가 그런단 말인가, 원 참!\""]], [["“Do you feel an uncomfortable heat at the pit of your stomach, sir?", "\"명치께에 거북한 열이 느껴지십니까, 나리?"], ["and a nasty thumping at the top of your head?", "그리고 정수리께가 고약하게 쿵쿵 뛰는 것이? 아!"], ["Ah! not yet?", "아직 아니시라고요?"], ["It will lay hold of you at Cobb’s Hole, Mr. Franklin.", "그것은 코브스 홀에서 나리를 덮칠 겁니다요, 프랭클린 나리."], ["I call it the detective-fever; and I first caught it in the company of Sergeant Cuff.”", "저는 그것을 탐정 열병이라 부릅지요. 그리고 이 몸은 커프 부장과 함께 있으면서 그것에 처음 걸렸습니다.\""]], [["“Aye! aye!", "\"옳거니!"], ["and the cure in this instance is to open Rosanna Spearman’s letter, I suppose?", "옳거니! 그런데 이 경우의 치료약은, 짐작건대, 로재나 스피어먼의 편지를 여는 것이렷다?"], ["Come along, and let’s get it.”", "자 가세, 그것을 손에 넣도록 하세.\""]], [["Early as it was, we found the fisherman’s wife astir in her kitchen.", "이른 시각이었건만, 우리는 그 어부의 아내가 제 부엌에서 부산히 움직이고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["On my presentation by Betteredge, good Mrs. Yolland performed a social ceremony, strictly reserved (as I afterwards learnt) for strangers of distinction.", "베터리지가 나를 소개하자, 착한 욜랜드 부인은 (내가 나중에 알게 된 바로는) 오직 지체 높은 손님을 위해서만 엄격히 아껴 두는 사교의 의례를 치렀다."], ["She put a bottle of Dutch gin and a couple of clean pipes on the table, and opened the conversation by saying, “What news from London, sir?”", "그녀는 네덜란드 진 한 병과 깨끗한 파이프 두 개를 탁자에 올려놓고, \"런던에서는 무슨 소식이 있습니까, 나리?\" 하고 말하며 대화의 문을 열었다."]], [["Before I could find an answer to this immensely comprehensive question, an apparition advanced towards me, out of a dark corner of the kitchen.", "이 어마어마하게 두루뭉술한 물음에 미처 답을 찾기도 전에, 그 부엌의 어두운 구석에서 한 유령 같은 형체가 나를 향해 다가왔다."], ["A wan, wild, haggard girl, with remarkably beautiful hair, and with a fierce keenness in her eyes, came limping up on a crutch to the table at which I was sitting, and looked at me as if I was an object of mingled interest and horror, which it quite fascinated her to see.", "파리하고, 사납고, 초췌한 소녀가, 유난히 아름다운 머리칼을 하고, 두 눈에는 매서운 날카로움을 담은 채, 목발을 짚고 절뚝이며 내가 앉아 있는 탁자로 올라와, 마치 내가 그녀로서는 보는 것만으로도 자못 홀리는, 관심과 공포가 뒤섞인 대상이라도 되는 양 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Mr. Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지 씨,\""], ["she said, without taking her eyes off me, “mention his name again, if you please.”", "하고 그녀가 나에게서 눈을 떼지 않은 채 말했다. \"부디, 저이의 이름을 다시 한 번 대 주세요.\""]], [["“This gentleman’s name,”", "\"이 신사분의 이름은,\""], ["answered Betteredge (with a strong emphasis on gentleman), “is Mr.", "하고 베터리지가 (신사라는 낱말에 힘을 잔뜩 주어) 대답했다."], ["Franklin Blake.”", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨란다.\""]], [["The girl turned her back on me, and suddenly left the room.", "그 소녀는 나에게 등을 돌리고, 별안간 방을 나가 버렸다."], ["Good Mrs. Yolland—as I believe—made some apologies for her daughter’s odd behaviour, and Betteredge (probably) translated them into polite English.", "착한 욜랜드 부인은—내가 믿기로는—제 딸의 그 괴이한 행동에 대해 얼마쯤 사과를 했고, 베터리지는 (아마도) 그것을 정중한 영어로 옮겼다."], ["I speak of this in complete uncertainty.", "나는 이것을 온전히 확신 없이 말한다."], ["My attention was absorbed in following the sound of the girl’s crutch.", "나의 주의는 그 소녀의 목발 소리를 좇는 데에 온통 쏠려 있었다."], ["Thump-thump, up the wooden stairs; thump-thump across the room above our heads; thump-thump down the stairs again—and there stood the apparition at the open door, with a letter in its hand, beckoning me out!", "쿵-쿵, 나무 계단을 오르고, 쿵-쿵, 우리 머리 위의 방을 가로지르고, 쿵-쿵, 다시 계단을 내려오고—그러더니 그 유령 같은 형체가 열린 문가에, 손에 편지 한 통을 든 채, 나에게 밖으로 나오라 손짓하며 서 있는 것이었다!"]], [["I left more apologies in course of delivery behind me, and followed this strange creature—limping on before me, faster and faster—down the slope of the beach.", "나는 미처 다 전해지지 못한 사과들을 뒤에 남겨 두고, 이 이상한 존재를 따라갔다—내 앞에서 절뚝이며, 점점 더 빠르게—그 해변의 비탈을 내려갔다."], ["She led me behind some boats, out of sight and hearing of the few people in the fishing-village, and then stopped, and faced me for the first time.", "그녀는 나를 몇 척의 배 뒤로 이끌어, 그 어촌의 몇 안 되는 사람들의 눈과 귀가 닿지 않는 곳으로 데려가더니, 걸음을 멈추고, 처음으로 나를 마주 보았다."]], [["“Stand there,”", "\"거기 서세요,\""], ["she said, “I want to look at you.”", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신을 보고 싶어요.\""]], [["There was no mistaking the expression on her face.", "그녀의 얼굴에 떠오른 표정은 잘못 볼 여지가 없었다."], ["I inspired her with the strongest emotions of abhorrence and disgust.", "나는 그녀에게 혐오와 역겨움의 가장 강렬한 감정을 불러일으켰다."], ["Let me not be vain enough to say that no woman had ever looked at me in this manner before.", "여태 어떤 여자도 나를 이런 식으로 본 적이 없었노라고 말할 만큼 내가 우쭐하지는 않으련다."], ["I will only venture on the more modest assertion that no woman had ever let me perceive it yet.", "나는 다만, 여태 어떤 여자도 나로 하여금 그것을 알아채게 한 적이 없었노라는, 좀 더 겸손한 주장에 그치련다."], ["There is a limit to the length of the inspection which a man can endure, under certain circumstances.", "어떤 상황에서는, 한 사내가 견딜 수 있는 뜯어봄의 길이에도 한계가 있다."], ["I attempted to direct Limping Lucy’s attention to some less revolting object than my face.", "나는 절름발이 루시의 주의를 내 얼굴보다 덜 역겨운 어떤 대상으로 돌려 보려 했다."]], [["“I think you have got a letter to give me,” I began.", "\"자네가 나에게 줄 편지를 가지고 있는 것 같은데,\""], ["“Is it the letter there, in your hand?”", "하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"저기, 자네 손에 든 그 편지가 그것인가?\""]], [["“Say that again,”", "\"그 말을 다시 해 보세요,\""], ["was the only answer I received.", "하는 것이 내가 받은 유일한 대답이었다."]], [["I repeated the words, like a good child learning its lesson.", "나는, 제 공부를 익히는 착한 아이처럼, 그 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니야,\""], ["said the girl, speaking to herself, but keeping her eyes still mercilessly fixed on me.", "하고 그 소녀는 혼잣말로 말했으나, 그 눈은 여전히 인정사정없이 나에게 못 박힌 채였다."], ["“I can’t find out what she saw in his face.", "\"나는 그 아이가 저이의 얼굴에서 무엇을 보았는지 도무지 알 수가 없어."], ["I can’t guess what she heard in his voice.”", "나는 그 아이가 저이의 목소리에서 무엇을 들었는지 도무지 짐작할 수가 없어.\""], ["She suddenly looked away from me, and rested her head wearily on the top of her crutch.", "그녀는 별안간 나에게서 눈을 돌려, 지친 듯 제 목발 끝에 머리를 기댔다."], ["“Oh, my poor dear!”", "\"오, 나의 가엾은 벗아!\""], ["she said, in the first soft tones which had fallen from her, in my hearing.", "하고 그녀는, 내가 듣는 데에서 그녀에게서 흘러나온 첫 부드러운 어조로 말했다."], ["“Oh, my lost darling!", "\"오, 나의 잃어버린 님아!"], ["what could you see in this man?”", "대체 이 사내에게서 무엇을 볼 수 있었단 말이냐?\""], ["She lifted her head again fiercely, and looked at me once more.", "그녀는 다시 사납게 머리를 들어, 나를 한 번 더 바라보았다."], ["“Can you eat and drink?”", "\"당신은 먹고 마실 수 있나요?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["I did my best to preserve my gravity, and answered, “Yes.”", "나는 애써 근엄함을 지키며, \"그렇네,\" 하고 대답했다."]], [["“Can you sleep?”", "\"당신은 잠잘 수 있나요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇네.\""]], [["“When you see a poor girl in service, do you feel no remorse?”", "\"당신은 하인살이하는 가엾은 소녀를 볼 때, 아무런 뉘우침도 느끼지 않나요?\""]], [["“Certainly not.", "\"물론 느끼지 않네."], ["Why should I?”", "어째서 내가 느껴야 하는가?\""]], [["She abruptly thrust the letter (as the phrase is) into my face.", "그녀는 별안간 그 편지를 (흔히 하는 말대로) 내 얼굴에 들이밀었다."]], [["“Take it!”", "\"받아요!\""], ["she exclaimed furiously.", "하고 그녀가 노기등등하게 외쳤다."], ["“I never set eyes on you before.", "\"나는 이제껏 당신을 본 적이 없어요."], ["God Almighty forbid I should ever set eyes on you again.”", "전능하신 하느님이시여, 제가 다시는 이 사람을 보게 되지 않게 하소서.\""]], [["With those parting words she limped away from me at the top of her speed.", "그 이별의 말과 함께 그녀는 있는 힘껏 빠르게 나에게서 절뚝이며 멀어져 갔다."], ["The one interpretation that I could put on her conduct has, no doubt, been anticipated by everybody.", "그녀의 처신에 내가 붙일 수 있는 그 한 가지 해석은, 두말할 것 없이, 누구나 이미 짐작했을 것이다."], ["I could only suppose that she was mad.", "나는 다만 그녀가 미쳤으리라고 여길 수밖에 없었다."]], [["Having reached that inevitable conclusion, I turned to the more interesting object of investigation which was presented to me by Rosanna Spearman’s letter.", "그 피할 수 없는 결론에 이르러, 나는 로재나 스피어먼의 편지가 나에게 내놓은, 더 흥미로운 조사의 대상으로 눈을 돌렸다."], ["The address was written as follows:—“For Franklin Blake, Esq.", "그 주소는 다음과 같이 적혀 있었다—\"프랭클린 블레이크 님께."], ["To be given into his own hands (and not to be trusted to anyone else), by Lucy Yolland.”", "루시 욜랜드가, (다른 누구에게도 맡기지 말고) 그분 본인의 손에 직접 전할 것.\""]], [["I broke the seal.", "나는 그 봉인을 뜯었다."], ["The envelope contained a letter: and this, in its turn, contained a slip of paper.", "그 봉투 안에는 편지 한 통이 들어 있었다. 그리고 이것은, 그 안에 또다시, 종이쪽지 한 장을 담고 있었다."], ["I read the letter first:—", "나는 먼저 그 편지를 읽었다—"]], [["“Sir,—If you are curious to know the meaning of my behaviour to you, whilst you were staying in the house of my mistress, Lady Verinder, do what you are told to do in the memorandum enclosed with this—and do it without any person being present to overlook you.", "\"선생님—만일 선생님께서, 저의 주인이신 레이디 버린더의 집에 머무시는 동안 제가 선생님께 한 행동의 뜻을 궁금해하신다면, 이것에 동봉된 각서에 적힌 대로 하십시오—그리고 선생님을 지켜볼 어떤 사람도 곁에 없이 그것을 하십시오."], ["Your humble servant,", "선생님의 미천한 하녀,"]], [["“ROSANNA SPEARMAN.”", "로재나 스피어먼.\""]], [["I turned to the slip of paper next.", "나는 다음으로 그 종이쪽지로 눈을 돌렸다."], ["Here is the literal copy of it, word for word:", "다음은 그것을 한 글자 한 글자 그대로 옮긴 것이다."]], [["“Memorandum:—To go to the Shivering Sand at the turn of the tide.", "\"각서—조수(潮水)가 바뀔 무렵 흔들리는 모래로 갈 것."], ["To walk out on the South Spit, until I get the South Spit Beacon, and the flagstaff at the Coast-guard station above Cobb’s Hole in a line together.", "남쪽 사주(砂洲) 위로 걸어 나가, 남쪽 사주 등대와, 코브스 홀 위쪽 해안 경비대 초소의 깃대가 한 줄로 나란히 놓일 때까지 갈 것."], ["To lay down on the rocks, a stick, or any straight thing to guide my hand, exactly in the line of the beacon and the flagstaff.", "그 바위 위에, 막대기든 무엇이든 곧은 물건 하나를, 내 손을 이끌도록, 꼭 그 등대와 그 깃대의 선에 맞추어 놓을 것."], ["To take care, in doing this, that one end of the stick shall be at the edge of the rocks, on the side of them which overlooks the quicksand.", "이것을 할 때, 그 막대기의 한쪽 끝이, 그 바위의 늪 모래를 내려다보는 쪽 가장자리에 오도록 조심할 것."], ["To feel along the stick, among the seaweed (beginning from the end of the stick which points towards the beacon), for the Chain.", "그 막대기를 따라, 해초 사이로 (등대 쪽을 가리키는 막대기 끝에서부터 시작하여), 쇠사슬을 더듬어 찾을 것."], ["To run my hand along the Chain, when found, until I come to the part of it which stretches over the edge of the rocks, down into the quicksand.", "찾거든, 그 쇠사슬을 따라 손을 옮겨, 그것이 바위 가장자리를 넘어 늪 모래 속으로 뻗어 내린 부분에 이를 때까지 갈 것."], ["And then, to pull the chain.”", "그러고 나서, 그 사슬을 잡아당길 것.\""]], [["Just as I had read the last words—underlined in the original—I heard the voice of Betteredge behind me.", "내가 그 마지막 말들—원문에는 밑줄이 쳐진—을 다 읽은 바로 그때, 나는 내 뒤에서 베터리지의 목소리를 들었다."], ["The inventor of the detective-fever had completely succumbed to that irresistible malady.", "그 탐정 열병의 창안자는 그 뿌리칠 수 없는 병에 완전히 굴복해 버린 참이었다."], ["“I can’t stand it any longer, Mr.", "\"저는 더는 못 참겠습니다, 프랭클린 나리."], ["Franklin. What does her letter say?", "그 아이의 편지에 뭐라 적혀 있습니까?"], ["For mercy’s sake, sir, tell us, what does her letter say?”", "제발, 나리, 저희에게 말씀해 주십시오, 그 아이의 편지에 뭐라 적혀 있습니까?\""]], [["I handed him the letter, and the memorandum.", "나는 그에게 그 편지와 그 각서를 건넸다."], ["He read the first without appearing to be much interested in it.", "그는 그 첫째 것을 그리 흥미로워하는 기색 없이 읽었다."], ["But the second—the memorandum—produced a strong impression on him.", "허나 둘째 것—그 각서—은 그에게 강한 인상을 남겼다."]], [["“The Sergeant said it!”", "\"부장이 그리 말했습지요!\""], ["cried Betteredge.", "하고 베터리지가 부르짖었다."], ["“From first to last, sir, the Sergeant said she had got a memorandum of the hiding-place.", "\"처음부터 끝까지, 나리, 부장은 그 아이가 그 은닉처의 각서를 가지고 있다고 말했습니다."], ["And here it is! Lord save us, Mr.", "그리고 여기 그것이 있습니다!"], ["Franklin, here is the secret that puzzled everybody, from the great Cuff downwards, ready and waiting, as one may say, to show itself to you!", "맙소사, 프랭클린 나리, 여기, 그 위대한 커프를 비롯해 온 사람을 어리둥절하게 했던 그 비밀이, 이를테면, 나리께 제 모습을 드러내려고 채비를 갖추고 기다리고 있습니다요!"], ["It’s the ebb now, sir, as anybody may see for themselves.", "지금은 썰물입니다, 나리, 누구든 스스로 보면 알 수 있듯이요."], ["How long will it be till the turn of the tide?”", "조수가 바뀌기까지 얼마나 남았을까요?\""], ["He looked up, and observed a lad at work, at some little distance from us, mending a net.", "그는 고개를 들어, 우리에게서 얼마쯤 떨어진 데서 그물을 손질하고 있는 젊은이 하나를 눈여겨보았다."], ["“Tammie Bright!”", "\"태미 브라이트!\""], ["he shouted at the top of his voice.", "하고 그가 목청껏 소리쳤다."]], [["“I hear you!”", "\"들려요!\""], ["Tammie shouted back.", "하고 태미가 되받아 소리쳤다."]], [["“When’s the turn of the tide?”", "\"조수가 언제 바뀌지?\""]], [["“In an hour’s time.”", "\"한 시간 뒤에요.\""]], [["We both looked at our watches.", "우리는 둘 다 우리의 시계를 보았다."]], [["“We can go round by the coast, Mr. Franklin,”", "\"해안을 돌아서 갈 수 있습니다, 프랭클린 나리,\""], ["said Betteredge; “and get to the quicksand in that way with plenty of time to spare.", "하고 베터리지가 말했다. \"그리고 그렇게 하면 시간이 넉넉히 남는 채로 그 늪 모래에 다다를 수 있습지요."], ["What do you say, sir?”", "어떻게 하시겠습니까, 나리?\""]], [["“Come along!”", "\"자 가세!\""]], [["On our way to the Shivering Sand, I applied to Betteredge to revive my memory of events (as affecting Rosanna Spearman) at the period of Sergeant Cuff’s inquiry.", "흔들리는 모래로 가는 길에, 나는 베터리지에게 청하여 커프 부장의 조사 무렵의 (로재나 스피어먼에 관련되는 한에서의) 사건들에 대한 나의 기억을 되살렸다."], ["With my old friend’s help, I soon had the succession of circumstances clearly registered in my mind.", "나의 옛 벗의 도움으로, 나는 곧 그 정황들의 잇따름을 또렷이 내 마음에 새겨 넣었다."], ["Rosanna’s journey to Frizinghall, when the whole household believed her to be ill in her own room—Rosanna’s mysterious employment of the night-time with her door locked, and her candle burning till the morning—Rosanna’s suspicious purchase of the japanned tin case, and the two dog’s chains from Mrs. Yolland—the Sergeant’s positive conviction that Rosanna had hidden something at the Shivering Sand, and the Sergeant’s absolute ignorance as to what that something might be—all these strange results of the abortive inquiry into the loss of the Moonstone were clearly present to me again, when we reached the quicksand, and walked out together on the low ledge of rocks called the South Spit.", "온 집안이 그녀가 제 방에서 앓고 있는 줄로 믿었을 때의 로재나의 프리징홀행—문을 걸어 잠근 채, 초를 아침까지 태우며 밤 시간을 보낸 로재나의 알 수 없는 일—로재나가 욜랜드 부인에게서 옻칠한 양철상자와 개사슬 두 개를 수상쩍게 사들인 것—로재나가 흔들리는 모래에 무언가를 숨겼다는 부장의 단정적인 확신과, 그 무언가가 무엇인지에 대한 부장의 전적인 무지—월장석의 분실을 캐다 만 그 조사의 이 온갖 이상한 결과들이, 우리가 그 늪 모래에 다다라, 남쪽 사주라 불리는 그 낮은 바위 턱 위로 함께 걸어 나갔을 때, 다시 또렷이 내 앞에 있었다."]], [["With Betteredge’s help, I soon stood in the right position to see the Beacon and the Coast-guard flagstaff in a line together.", "베터리지의 도움으로, 나는 곧 그 등대와 해안 경비대의 깃대가 한 줄로 나란히 보이는 알맞은 자리에 섰다."], ["Following the memorandum as our guide, we next laid my stick in the necessary direction, as neatly as we could, on the uneven surface of the rocks.", "그 각서를 우리의 길잡이로 삼아, 우리는 다음으로 내 막대기를 그 바위의 울퉁불퉁한 바닥 위에, 우리가 할 수 있는 한 정확히, 필요한 방향으로 놓았다."], ["And then we looked at our watches once more.", "그러고 나서 우리는 우리의 시계를 한 번 더 보았다."]], [["It wanted nearly twenty minutes yet of the turn of the tide.", "조수가 바뀌기까지 아직 이십 분쯤이 모자랐다."], ["I suggested waiting through this interval on the beach, instead of on the wet and slippery surface of the rocks.", "나는 이 사이의 시간을, 젖고 미끄러운 바위 바닥 위가 아니라, 해변에서 기다리자고 했다."], ["Having reached the dry sand, I prepared to sit down; and, greatly to my surprise, Betteredge prepared to leave me.", "마른 모래에 이르러, 나는 앉을 채비를 했다. 그런데 나의 큰 놀라움으로, 베터리지는 나를 떠날 채비를 하는 것이었다."]], [["“What are you going away for?”", "\"어디 가려는 겐가?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Look at the letter again, sir, and you will see.”", "\"편지를 다시 보십시오, 나리, 그러면 아실 겁니다.\""]], [["A glance at the letter reminded me that I was charged, when I made my discovery, to make it alone.", "편지를 흘긋 보니, 내가 나의 발견을 할 때에 그것을 홀로 하도록 당부받았음이 떠올랐다."]], [["“It’s hard enough for me to leave you, at such a time as this,”", "\"이런 때에 나리를 두고 가는 것이 저에게는 참으로 괴롭습니다요,\""], ["said Betteredge.", "하고 베터리지가 말했다."], ["“But she died a dreadful death, poor soul—and I feel a kind of call on me, Mr. Franklin, to humour that fancy of hers.", "\"허나 그 아이는 끔찍한 죽음을 맞았습지요, 가엾은 것이—그리고 저는, 프랭클린 나리, 그 아이의 그 소원을 들어주라는 어떤 부름 같은 것을 저에게 느낍니다."], ["Besides,”", "게다가,\""], ["he added, confidentially, “there’s nothing in the letter against your letting out the secret afterwards.", "하고 그는 은근히 덧붙였다. \"그 편지에는 나리께서 나중에 그 비밀을 털어놓으시는 것을 막는 것은 아무것도 없습니다."], ["I’ll hang about in the fir-plantation, and wait till you pick me up.", "저는 전나무 숲 언저리에서 어정거리며, 나리께서 저를 데리러 오실 때까지 기다리겠습니다."], ["Don’t be longer than you can help, sir.", "되도록 오래 걸리지 않으시길, 나리."], ["The detective-fever isn’t an easy disease to deal with, under these circumstances.”", "탐정 열병은, 이런 상황에서는, 다루기 쉬운 병이 아닙지요.\""]], [["With that parting caution, he left me.", "그 이별의 당부와 함께, 그는 나를 떠났다."]], [["The interval of expectation, short as it was when reckoned by the measure of time, assumed formidable proportions when reckoned by the measure of suspense.", "그 기다림의 시간은, 시간의 잣대로 재면 짧았건만, 조바심의 잣대로 재면 어마어마한 크기를 띠었다."], ["This was one of the occasions on which the invaluable habit of smoking becomes especially precious and consolatory.", "이것은 담배 피우는 그 더없이 값진 버릇이 각별히 소중하고 위안이 되는 그런 경우의 하나였다."], ["I lit a cigar, and sat down on the slope of the beach.", "나는 여송연 하나에 불을 붙이고, 그 해변의 비탈에 앉았다."]], [["The sunlight poured its unclouded beauty on every object that I could see.", "햇빛은 내가 볼 수 있는 온갖 대상 위에 그 구름 한 점 없는 아름다움을 쏟아부었다."], ["The exquisite freshness of the air made the mere act of living and breathing a luxury.", "더없이 상쾌한 공기가 그저 살아 숨 쉬는 행위마저 하나의 호사로 만들었다."], ["Even the lonely little bay welcomed the morning with a show of cheerfulness; and the bared wet surface of the quicksand itself, glittering with a golden brightness, hid the horror of its false brown face under a passing smile.", "그 외로운 자그마한 만(灣)조차 명랑한 낯빛으로 아침을 맞았다. 그리고 그 늪 모래 자체의 드러난 젖은 표면마저, 황금빛 밝음으로 반짝이며, 그 지나가는 미소 아래 제 거짓된 갈색 얼굴의 공포를 감추었다."], ["It was the finest day I had seen since my return to England.", "그것은 내가 잉글랜드로 돌아온 뒤로 본 가장 화창한 날이었다."]], [["The turn of the tide came, before my cigar was finished.", "조수는 내 여송연이 다 타기 전에 바뀌었다."], ["I saw the preliminary heaving of the Sand, and then the awful shiver that crept over its surface—as if some spirit of terror lived and moved and shuddered in the fathomless deeps beneath.", "나는 그 모래의 예비적인 들썩임을 보았고, 뒤이어 그 표면 위로 기어드는 그 무시무시한 떨림을 보았다—마치 어떤 공포의 정령이 그 밑의 헤아릴 수 없는 심연 속에서 살아 움직이며 몸서리치기라도 하는 듯이."], ["I threw away my cigar, and went back again to the rocks.", "나는 여송연을 던져 버리고, 다시 그 바위로 돌아갔다."]], [["My directions in the memorandum instructed me to feel along the line traced by the stick, beginning with the end which was nearest to the beacon.", "그 각서 속의 나에 대한 지시는, 등대에 가장 가까운 끝에서 시작하여, 그 막대기가 그은 선을 따라 더듬으라고 나에게 이르고 있었다."]], [["I advanced, in this manner, more than half way along the stick, without encountering anything but the edges of the rocks.", "나는, 이런 식으로, 그 바위의 가장자리 말고는 아무것도 만나지 못한 채, 그 막대기를 따라 절반을 넘어 나아갔다."], ["An inch or two further on, however, my patience was rewarded.", "허나 한두 치 더 나아가자, 나의 참을성이 보답을 받았다."], ["In a narrow little fissure, just within reach of my forefinger, I felt the chain.", "내 집게손가락이 겨우 닿을락 말락 한, 좁고 자그마한 틈 속에서, 나는 그 쇠사슬을 만졌다."], ["Attempting, next, to follow it, by touch, in the direction of the quicksand, I found my progress stopped by a thick growth of seaweed—which had fastened itself into the fissure, no doubt, in the time that had elapsed since Rosanna Spearman had chosen her hiding-place.", "다음으로, 촉감으로, 그것을 늪 모래 쪽으로 따라가려 하자, 나는 나의 나아감이 두툼하게 자란 해초에 가로막힌 것을 알았다—그것은, 두말할 것 없이, 로재나 스피어먼이 제 은닉처를 고른 뒤로 흘러간 시간 동안, 그 틈 속에 스스로 얽혀 든 것이었다."]], [["It was equally impossible to pull up the seaweed, or to force my hand through it.", "그 해초를 뽑아내는 것도, 그 사이로 내 손을 밀어 넣는 것도 똑같이 불가능했다."], ["After marking the spot indicated by the end of the stick which was placed nearest to the quicksand, I determined to pursue the search for the chain on a plan of my own. My idea was to “sound”", "늪 모래에 가장 가까이 놓인 막대기 끝이 가리키는 지점을 표시해 둔 뒤, 나는 나 나름의 방책으로 그 쇠사슬 찾기를 이어 가기로 정했다."], ["immediately under the rocks, on the chance of recovering the lost trace of the chain at the point at which it entered the sand.", "나의 생각은, 그 쇠사슬이 모래로 들어가는 지점에서 그 잃어버린 자취를 되찾을 요행을 노려, 그 바위 바로 아래를 곧장 \"재어\" 보는 것이었다."], ["I took up the stick, and knelt down on the brink of the South Spit.", "나는 그 막대기를 집어 들고, 그 남쪽 사주의 벼랑 끝에 무릎을 꿇었다."]], [["In this position, my face was within a few feet of the surface of the quicksand.", "이 자세에서, 내 얼굴은 그 늪 모래의 표면에서 몇 자 안 되는 거리에 있었다."], ["The sight of it so near me, still disturbed at intervals by its hideous shivering fit, shook my nerves for the moment.", "그 흉측한 떨림의 발작으로 이따금 여전히 요동치는 그것이 나에게 그토록 가까이 있는 광경이, 그 순간 나의 신경을 뒤흔들었다."], ["A horrible fancy that the dead woman might appear on the scene of her suicide, to assist my search—an unutterable dread of seeing her rise through the heaving surface of the sand, and point to the place—forced itself into my mind, and turned me cold in the warm sunlight.", "그 죽은 여인이, 나의 찾기를 도우려, 제 자살의 현장에 나타날지도 모른다는 끔찍한 상상—그녀가 그 들썩이는 모래의 표면을 뚫고 솟아올라, 그 자리를 가리키는 것을 보게 될지도 모른다는 이루 말할 수 없는 공포가—내 마음속에 억지로 밀고 들어와, 그 따스한 햇빛 속에서 나를 오싹 얼어붙게 했다."], ["I own I closed my eyes at the moment when the point of the stick first entered the quicksand.", "나는 인정한다, 그 막대기 끝이 처음으로 그 늪 모래로 들어가던 그 순간, 내가 두 눈을 감았음을."]], [["The instant afterwards, before the stick could have been submerged more than a few inches, I was free from the hold of my own superstitious terror, and was throbbing with excitement from head to foot.", "그 바로 다음 순간, 그 막대기가 채 몇 치도 잠기기 전에, 나는 나 자신의 미신적인 공포의 손아귀에서 벗어나, 머리끝부터 발끝까지 흥분으로 두근거리고 있었다."], ["Sounding blindfold, at my first attempt—at that first attempt I had sounded right!", "눈을 감고 재어 보아, 나의 첫 시도에—그 첫 시도에 나는 바로 맞게 재었던 것이다!"], ["The stick struck the chain.", "그 막대기가 그 쇠사슬에 부딪쳤다."]], [["Taking a firm hold of the roots of the seaweed with my left hand, I laid myself down over the brink, and felt with my right hand under the overhanging edges of the rock.", "왼손으로 그 해초의 뿌리를 단단히 움켜쥔 채, 나는 그 벼랑 끝 너머로 몸을 눕히고, 오른손으로 그 튀어나온 바위 가장자리 아래를 더듬었다."], ["My right hand found the chain.", "나의 오른손이 그 쇠사슬을 찾아냈다."]], [["I drew it up without the slightest difficulty.", "나는 그것을 조금의 어려움도 없이 끌어 올렸다."], ["And there was the japanned tin case fastened to the end of it.", "그리고 거기, 그 끝에 그 옻칠한 양철상자가 매달려 있었다."]], [["The action of the water had so rusted the chain, that it was impossible for me to unfasten it from the hasp which attached it to the case.", "물의 작용이 그 쇠사슬을 어찌나 녹슬게 했던지, 나는 그것을 그 상자에 매어 두는 걸쇠에서 풀어낼 수가 없었다."], ["Putting the case between my knees and exerting my utmost strength, I contrived to draw off the cover.", "그 상자를 두 무릎 사이에 끼우고 나의 온 힘을 다하여, 나는 간신히 그 뚜껑을 벗겨 냈다."], ["Some white substance filled the whole interior when I looked in.", "안을 들여다보니 어떤 흰 물체가 그 속을 온통 채우고 있었다."], ["I put in my hand, and found it to be linen.", "나는 손을 넣어, 그것이 아마포임을 알았다."]], [["In drawing out the linen, I also drew out a letter crumpled up with it.", "그 아마포를 끌어내면서, 나는 그것과 함께 구겨진 편지 한 통도 끌어냈다."], ["After looking at the direction, and discovering that it bore my name, I put the letter in my pocket, and completely removed the linen.", "그 겉면을 보고, 그것이 내 이름을 지니고 있음을 알아채고서, 나는 그 편지를 주머니에 넣고, 그 아마포를 온전히 끄집어냈다."], ["It came out in a thick roll, moulded, of course, to the shape of the case in which it had been so long confined, and perfectly preserved from any injury by the sea.", "그것은, 물론, 그토록 오래 갇혀 있던 그 상자의 모양에 맞추어 빚어진, 두툼한 두루마리 꼴로 나왔고, 바닷물로 인한 어떤 상함으로부터도 완벽히 지켜져 있었다."]], [["I carried the linen to the dry sand of the beach, and there unrolled and smoothed it out.", "나는 그 아마포를 그 해변의 마른 모래로 가져가, 거기서 그것을 펼쳐 반반히 폈다."], ["There was no mistaking it as an article of dress.", "그것이 하나의 옷가지라는 것은 잘못 볼 여지가 없었다."], ["It was a nightgown.", "그것은 잠옷이었다."]], [["The uppermost side, when I spread it out, presented to view innumerable folds and creases, and nothing more.", "내가 그것을 펼쳤을 때, 그 위로 향한 면은, 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 주름과 구김살을 눈에 드러낼 뿐, 그 이상은 아무것도 없었다."], ["I tried the undermost side, next—and instantly discovered the smear of the paint from the door of Rachel’s boudoir!", "나는 다음으로 그 아래로 향한 면을 살폈다—그러자 대뜸 레이철의 규방 문에서 묻은 그 페인트 얼룩을 알아챈 것이다!"]], [["My eyes remained riveted on the stain, and my mind took me back at a leap from present to past.", "내 두 눈은 그 얼룩에 못 박힌 채였고, 나의 마음은 나를 단숨에 현재에서 과거로 데려갔다."], ["The very words of Sergeant Cuff recurred to me, as if the man himself was at my side again, pointing to the unanswerable inference which he drew from the smear on the door.", "커프 부장의 바로 그 말들이, 마치 그 사람 자신이 다시 내 곁에서, 그가 그 문의 얼룩에서 이끌어 낸 그 반박할 수 없는 추론을 가리키기라도 하는 듯, 나에게 되살아났다."]], [["“Find out whether there is any article of dress in this house with the stain of paint on it.", "\"이 집에 페인트 얼룩이 묻은 옷가지가 있는지 알아내시오."], ["Find out who that dress belongs to.", "그 옷이 누구의 것인지 알아내시오."], ["Find out how the person can account for having been in the room, and smeared the paint between midnight and three in the morning.", "그 사람이 자정과 새벽 세 시 사이에 그 방에 들어가 그 페인트를 묻힌 것을 어떻게 해명하는지 알아내시오."], ["If the person can’t satisfy you, you haven’t far to look for the hand that took the Diamond.”", "만일 그 사람이 당신을 흡족하게 하지 못한다면, 그 다이아몬드를 가져간 손을 찾으려 멀리 볼 것도 없소.\""]], [["One after another those words travelled over my memory, repeating themselves again and again with a wearisome, mechanical reiteration.", "그 말들이 하나하나 나의 기억 위를 지나가며, 지겹고 기계적인 되풀이로 몇 번이고 거듭 저를 되뇌었다."], ["I was roused from what felt like a trance of many hours—from what was really, no doubt, the pause of a few moments only—by a voice calling to me.", "나는, 여러 시간의 무아지경처럼 느껴진 것에서—실은, 두말할 것 없이, 겨우 몇 순간의 멈춤에 지나지 않았던 것에서—나를 부르는 목소리에 깨어났다."], ["I looked up, and saw that Betteredge’s patience had failed him at last.", "나는 고개를 들어, 베터리지의 참을성이 마침내 바닥나 버렸음을 보았다."], ["He was just visible between the sandhills, returning to the beach.", "그는 그 모래언덕 사이로 겨우 보일락 말락 하게, 해변으로 돌아오고 있었다."]], [["The old man’s appearance recalled me, the moment I perceived it, to my sense of present things, and reminded me that the inquiry which I had pursued thus far still remained incomplete.", "그 노인의 모습은, 내가 그것을 알아챈 순간, 나를 현재의 일들에 대한 감각으로 불러들였고, 여기까지 내가 좇아 온 그 조사가 아직 마무리되지 않은 채 남아 있음을 나에게 일깨웠다."], ["I had discovered the smear on the nightgown.", "나는 그 잠옷의 얼룩을 발견했다."], ["To whom did the nightgown belong?", "그 잠옷은 누구의 것이었던가?"]], [["My first impulse was to consult the letter in my pocket—the letter which I had found in the case.", "나의 첫 충동은 내 주머니 속의 편지—내가 그 상자 속에서 찾은 그 편지를 살펴보는 것이었다."]], [["As I raised my hand to take it out, I remembered that there was a shorter way to discovery than this.", "그것을 꺼내려 손을 드는데, 나는 이보다 발견으로 가는 더 빠른 길이 있음을 떠올렸다."], ["The nightgown itself would reveal the truth, for, in all probability, the nightgown was marked with its owner’s name.", "그 잠옷 자체가 진실을 드러내 줄 터였다, 십중팔구, 그 잠옷에 그 주인의 이름이 새겨져 있을 것이므로."]], [["I took it up from the sand, and looked for the mark.", "나는 그것을 모래에서 집어 들고, 그 표시를 찾았다."]], [["I found the mark, and read—MY OWN NAME.", "나는 그 표시를 찾아, 읽었다—바로 나 자신의 이름을."]], [["There were the familiar letters which told me that the nightgown was mine.", "거기 그 잠옷이 나의 것임을 나에게 일러 주는 그 낯익은 글자들이 있었다. 나는 그것들에서 눈을 들었다."], ["I looked up from them.", "거기 해가 있었다."], ["There was the sun; there were the glittering waters of the bay; there was old Betteredge, advancing nearer and nearer to me.", "거기 그 만의 반짝이는 물이 있었다. 거기 늙은 베터리지가, 점점 더 가까이 나에게 다가오고 있었다."], ["I looked back again at the letters.", "나는 다시 그 글자들을 돌아보았다. 바로 나 자신의 이름."], ["My own name. Plainly confronting me—my own name.", "뚜렷이 나를 마주하고 있는—바로 나 자신의 이름을."]], [["“If time, pains, and money can do it, I will lay my hand on the thief who took the Moonstone.”—I had left London, with those words on my lips.", "\"만일 시간과 애씀과 돈으로 그것을 할 수 있다면, 나는 월장석을 훔친 그 도둑에게 내 손을 얹고야 말겠소.\"—나는 그 말들을 입에 담고 런던을 떠났었다."], ["I had penetrated the secret which the quicksand had kept from every other living creature.", "나는 그 늪 모래가 다른 온갖 살아 있는 존재에게서 감추어 온 그 비밀을 꿰뚫어 보았다."], ["And, on the unanswerable evidence of the paint-stain, I had discovered Myself as the Thief.", "그리고, 그 페인트 얼룩의 반박할 수 없는 증거로써, 나는 바로 나 자신을 그 도둑으로 발견한 것이다."]], [["I have not a word to say about my own sensations.", "나 자신의 심경에 대해서는 한마디도 할 말이 없다."]], [["My impression is that the shock inflicted on me completely suspended my thinking and feeling power.", "내 느낌으로는, 나에게 가해진 그 충격이 나의 생각하고 느끼는 힘을 완전히 멎게 했던 것 같다."], ["I certainly could not have known what I was about when Betteredge joined me—for I have it on his authority that I laughed, when he asked what was the matter, and putting the nightgown into his hands, told him to read the riddle for himself.", "베터리지가 나에게 합류했을 때 나는 정녕 내가 무엇을 하고 있는지 알 수 없었을 것이다—무슨 일이냐고 그가 물었을 때 내가 웃었고, 그 잠옷을 그의 손에 쥐여 주며 그 수수께끼를 스스로 읽어 보라고 그에게 말했다는 것을, 나는 그의 증언에 기대어 알고 있으니 말이다."]], [["Of what was said between us on the beach, I have not the faintest recollection.", "그 해변에서 우리 사이에 오간 말에 대해서는, 나에게 아주 흐릿한 기억조차 없다."], ["The first place in which I can now see myself again plainly is the plantation of firs.", "이제 내가 다시 나 자신을 또렷이 볼 수 있는 첫 장소는 그 전나무 숲이다."], ["Betteredge and I are walking back together to the house; and Betteredge is telling me that I shall be able to face it, and he will be able to face it, when we have had a glass of grog.", "베터리지와 나는 함께 그 집으로 걸어 돌아가고 있다. 그리고 베터리지는 나에게, 우리가 그로그 한 잔을 마시고 나면, 내가 그것에 맞설 수 있을 것이고, 그도 그것에 맞설 수 있을 것이라고 말하고 있다."]], [["The scene shifts from the plantation, to Betteredge’s little sitting-room.", "장면이 그 숲에서, 베터리지의 자그마한 응접실로 옮겨 간다."], ["My resolution not to enter Rachel’s house is forgotten.", "레이철의 집에 들지 않겠다던 나의 결심은 잊혔다."], ["I feel gratefully the coolness and shadiness and quiet of the room.", "나는 그 방의 서늘함과 그늘짐과 고요함을 고맙게 느낀다."], ["I drink the grog (a perfectly new luxury to me, at that time of day), which my good old friend mixes with icy-cold water from the well.", "나는 그로그를 (그 시각에는 나에게 아주 새로운 호사였다) 마신다—나의 착한 옛 벗이 우물에서 길어 온 얼음처럼 찬 물에 타 주는 그것을."], ["Under any other circumstances, the drink would simply stupefy me.", "여느 다른 상황에서라면, 그 술은 그저 나를 멍하게 만들었을 것이다."], ["As things are, it strings up my nerves.", "지금 사정이 사정인지라, 그것은 나의 신경을 팽팽히 죈다."], ["I begin to “face it,” as Betteredge has predicted.", "나는 베터리지가 예언한 대로, \"그것에 맞서기\""], ["And Betteredge, on his side, begins to “face it,”", "시작한다. 그리고 베터리지도, 제 편에서, \"그것에 맞서기\""], ["too.", "시작한다."]], [["The picture which I am now presenting of myself, will, I suspect, be thought a very strange one, to say the least of it.", "내가 지금 나 자신에 대해 내놓고 있는 이 초상은, 짐작건대, 아무리 좋게 말해도, 아주 이상한 것으로 여겨지리라."], ["Placed in a situation which may, I think, be described as entirely without parallel, what is the first proceeding to which I resort?", "내 생각에 전혀 유례가 없다고 이를 만한 처지에 놓여, 내가 맨 먼저 취하는 조치는 무엇인가?"], ["Do I seclude myself from all human society?", "나는 온갖 인간의 사회로부터 나 자신을 격리하는가?"], ["Do I set my mind to analyse the abominable impossibility which, nevertheless, confronts me as an undeniable fact?", "나는, 그럼에도 부인할 수 없는 사실로 나를 마주하는 그 가증스러운 불가능을 분석하는 데에 나의 마음을 쏟는가?"], ["Do I hurry back to London by the first train to consult the highest authorities, and to set a searching inquiry on foot immediately?", "나는 최고의 권위자들과 상의하고 당장 철저한 조사에 착수하려, 첫 기차로 서둘러 런던으로 돌아가는가? 아니다."], ["No. I accept the shelter of a house which I had resolved never to degrade myself by entering again; and I sit, tippling spirits and water in the company of an old servant, at ten o’clock in the morning.", "나는 다시는 들어가 나 자신을 욕되게 하지 않겠다고 결심했던 그 집의 비호를 받아들인다."], ["Is this the conduct that might have been expected from a man placed in my horrible position?", "그리고 나는, 아침 열 시에, 늙은 하인과 함께, 술과 물을 홀짝이며 앉아 있다."], ["I can only answer that the sight of old Betteredge’s familiar face was an inexpressible comfort to me, and that the drinking of old Betteredge’s grog helped me, as I believe nothing else would have helped me, in the state of complete bodily and mental prostration into which I had fallen.", "이것이 나의 그 끔찍한 처지에 놓인 사람에게서 기대되었을 법한 처신인가? 나는 다만, 늙은 베터리지의 낯익은 얼굴을 보는 것이 나에게 이루 말할 수 없는 위안이었다는 것과, 늙은 베터리지의 그로그를 마시는 것이, 내가 빠져든 그 온전한 몸과 마음의 탈진 상태에서, 다른 어떤 것도 나를 돕지 못했으리라 믿는 만큼 나를 도왔다는 것으로써 답할 수밖에 없다."], ["I can only offer this excuse for myself; and I can only admire that invariable preservation of dignity, and that strictly logical consistency of conduct which distinguish every man and woman who may read these lines, in every emergency of their lives from the cradle to the grave.", "나는 다만 나 자신을 위해 이 변명을 내놓을 수밖에 없다. 그리고 나는, 이 글줄을 읽을지 모를 온갖 남녀가, 요람에서 무덤에 이르기까지 그들 삶의 온갖 위급한 때에 보여 주는, 그 한결같은 위엄의 지킴과, 그 엄격히 논리적인 처신의 일관됨을 그저 감탄할 수밖에 없다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Franklin, there’s one thing certain, at any rate,”", "\"자, 프랭클린 나리, 어쨌든 한 가지만은 확실합니다요,\""], ["said Betteredge, throwing the nightgown down on the table between us, and pointing to it as if it was a living creature that could hear him.", "하고 베터리지가, 그 잠옷을 우리 사이의 탁자 위에 툭 던지고, 마치 그것이 제 말을 들을 수 있는 살아 있는 존재이기라도 한 양 그것을 가리키며 말했다."], ["“He’s a liar, to begin with.”", "\"우선, 저놈은 거짓말쟁이입니다.\""]], [["This comforting view of the matter was not the view that presented itself to my mind.", "이 위안이 되는 견해는 나의 마음에 떠오른 견해가 아니었다."]], [["“I am as innocent of all knowledge of having taken the Diamond as you are,”", "\"나는 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔다는 온갖 앎에 대해 자네만큼이나 결백하네.\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said. “But there is the witness against me!", "\"허나 저기 나에게 불리한 증인이 있네!"], ["The paint on the nightgown, and the name on the nightgown are facts.”", "그 잠옷의 페인트와, 그 잠옷의 이름은 엄연한 사실이야.\""]], [["Betteredge lifted my glass, and put it persuasively into my hand.", "베터리지는 내 잔을 들어, 구슬리듯 그것을 내 손에 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“Facts?”", "\"사실이라고요?\""], ["he repeated.", "하고 그가 되뇌었다."], ["“Take a drop more grog, Mr. Franklin, and you’ll get over the weakness of believing in facts!", "\"그로그를 한 모금 더 드십시오, 프랭클린 나리, 그러면 사실 따위를 믿는 그 약한 마음을 이겨 내실 겁니다요!"], ["Foul play, sir!”", "흉계입니다, 나리!\""], ["he continued, dropping his voice confidentially.", "하고 그는 은근히 목소리를 낮추며 말을 이었다."], ["“That is how I read the riddle.", "\"그것이 제가 이 수수께끼를 읽어 내는 방식입지요."], ["Foul play somewhere—and you and I must find it out.", "어딘가에 흉계가 있습니다—그리고 나리와 저는 그것을 밝혀내야 합니다."], ["Was there nothing else in the tin case, when you put your hand into it?”", "그 양철상자에 손을 넣으셨을 때, 그 안에 다른 것은 아무것도 없었습니까?\""]], [["The question instantly reminded me of the letter in my pocket.", "그 물음은 대뜸 나에게 내 주머니 속의 편지를 떠올리게 했다."], ["I took it out, and opened it.", "나는 그것을 꺼내어 폈다."], ["It was a letter of many pages, closely written.", "그것은 빽빽이 적힌, 여러 쪽에 걸친 편지였다."], ["I looked impatiently for the signature at the end.", "나는 조바심을 내며 그 끝의 서명을 찾았다."], ["“Rosanna Spearman.”", "\"로재나 스피어먼.\""]], [["As I read the name, a sudden remembrance illuminated my mind, and a sudden suspicion rose out of the new light.", "그 이름을 읽자, 별안간 떠오른 기억이 내 마음을 밝혔고, 그 새로운 빛에서 별안간 하나의 의심이 일었다."]], [["“Stop!”", "\"멈추게!\""], ["I exclaimed.", "하고 나는 외쳤다."], ["“Rosanna Spearman came to my aunt out of a reformatory?", "\"로재나 스피어먼은 감화원에서 나와 내 이모에게 온 것이지?"], ["Rosanna Spearman had once been a thief?”", "로재나 스피어먼은 한때 도둑이었지?\""]], [["“There’s no denying that, Mr.", "\"그것은 부인할 수 없습지요, 프랭클린 나리."], ["Franklin. What of it now, if you please?”", "그런데 그것이 이제 와서 어쨌다는 겁니까, 부디?\""]], [["“What of it now?", "\"그것이 이제 와서 어쨌냐고?"], ["How do we know she may not have stolen the Diamond after all?", "결국 그녀가 그 다이아몬드를 훔치지 않았다고 우리가 어찌 아는가?"], ["How do we know she may not have smeared my nightgown purposely with the paint?”", "그녀가 일부러 내 잠옷에 그 페인트를 묻히지 않았다고 우리가 어찌 아는가?\""]], [["Betteredge laid his hand on my arm, and stopped me before I could say any more.", "베터리지는 내 팔에 손을 얹어, 내가 더 말하기 전에 나를 멈추게 했다."]], [["“You will be cleared of this, Mr. Franklin, beyond all doubt.", "\"나리는 이 일에서 벗으실 겁니다, 프랭클린 나리, 조금의 의심할 여지도 없이요."], ["But I hope you won’t be cleared in that way.", "허나 저는 나리가 그런 식으로 벗으시지는 않기를 바랍니다요."], ["See what the letter says, sir.", "그 편지가 뭐라 하는지 보십시오, 나리."], ["In justice to the girl’s memory, see what it says.”", "그 아이의 넋에 대한 도리로라도, 그것이 뭐라 하는지 보십시오.\""]], [["I felt the earnestness with which he spoke—felt it as a friendly rebuke to me.", "나는 그가 말하는 그 간곡함을 느꼈다—그것을 나에 대한 다정한 꾸짖음으로 느꼈다."], ["“You shall form your own judgment on her letter,”", "\"자네가 그녀의 편지에 대해 자네 나름의 판단을 세우게.\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said. “I will read it out.”", "\"내가 그것을 소리 내어 읽겠네.\""]], [["I began—and read these lines:", "나는 시작했다—그리고 이 글줄들을 읽었다."]], [["“Sir—I have something to own to you.", "\"선생님—저는 선생님께 털어놓을 것이 있습니다."], ["A confession which means much misery, may sometimes be made in very few words.", "큰 비참을 뜻하는 고백이, 때로는 아주 몇 마디 말로 이루어질 수 있습니다."], ["This confession can be made in three words.", "이 고백은 세 마디로 이루어질 수 있습니다."], ["I love you.”", "저는 당신을 사랑합니다.\""]], [["The letter dropped from my hand.", "그 편지가 내 손에서 떨어졌다."], ["I looked at Betteredge.", "나는 베터리지를 보았다."], ["“In the name of Heaven,”", "\"맙소사,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said, “what does it mean?”", "\"이것이 무슨 뜻인가?\""]], [["He seemed to shrink from answering the question.", "그는 그 물음에 답하기를 꺼리는 듯했다."]], [["“You and Limping Lucy were alone together this morning, sir,”", "\"나리는 오늘 아침 절름발이 루시와 단둘이 있으셨지요, 나리,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “Did she say nothing about Rosanna Spearman?”", "\"그 아이가 로재나 스피어먼에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않던가요?\""]], [["“She never even mentioned Rosanna Spearman’s name.”", "\"그녀는 로재나 스피어먼의 이름조차 입에 올리지 않았네.\""]], [["“Please to go back to the letter, Mr. Franklin.", "\"부디 그 편지로 돌아가 주십시오, 프랭클린 나리."], ["I tell you plainly, I can’t find it in my heart to distress you, after what you have had to bear already.", "솔직히 말씀드리는데, 나리께서 이미 겪으셔야 했던 일 뒤에, 저는 도무지 나리를 괴롭힐 마음이 나지 않습니다요."], ["Let her speak for herself, sir.", "그 아이가 스스로 말하게 두십시오, 나리."], ["And get on with your grog.", "그리고 그로그를 마저 드십시오."], ["For your own sake, get on with your grog.”", "나리 자신을 위해서라도, 그로그를 마저 드십시오.\""]], [["I resumed the reading of the letter.", "나는 그 편지 읽기를 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["“It would be very disgraceful to me to tell you this, if I was a living woman when you read it.", "\"선생님께서 이것을 읽으실 때 제가 살아 있는 여자라면, 이런 말씀을 드리는 것이 저에게 몹시 부끄러운 일이겠지요."], ["I shall be dead and gone, sir, when you find my letter.", "선생님께서 제 편지를 찾으실 때, 저는, 선생님, 죽어 사라지고 없을 것입니다."], ["It is that which makes me bold.", "그것이 저를 대담하게 만듭니다."], ["Not even my grave will be left to tell of me.", "저에 대해 말해 줄 무덤조차 저에게는 남지 않을 것입니다."], ["I may own the truth—with the quicksand waiting to hide me when the words are written.", "이 말들이 다 적히면 저를 감추려 기다리는 그 늪 모래와 더불어—저는 진실을 털어놓아도 되겠지요."]], [["“Besides, you will find your nightgown in my hiding-place, with the smear of the paint on it; and you will want to know how it came to be hidden by me?", "\"게다가, 선생님께서는 저의 은닉처에서, 페인트 얼룩이 묻은 채로, 선생님의 잠옷을 찾으실 것입니다. 그러면 선생님께서는, 그것이 어쩌다 저에게 감추어지게 되었는지, 그리고 어째서 제가 살아생전 그것에 대해 선생님께 아무 말도 하지 않았는지 알고 싶으실 것입니다."], ["and why I said nothing to you about it in my life-time? I have only one reason to give.", "저에게는 내놓을 이유가 오직 하나 있습니다."], ["I did these strange things, because I loved you.", "저는 이 이상한 일들을, 선생님을 사랑했기에 했습니다."]], [["“I won’t trouble you with much about myself, or my life, before you came to my lady’s house.", "\"선생님께서 저의 마님 댁에 오시기 전의, 저 자신에 대해서나, 저의 삶에 대해서 많은 것으로 선생님을 성가시게 하지는 않겠습니다."], ["Lady Verinder took me out of a reformatory.", "레이디 버린더께서는 저를 감화원에서 데려오셨습니다."], ["I had gone to the reformatory from the prison.", "저는 옥에서 감화원으로 갔습니다."], ["I was put in the prison, because I was a thief.", "저는 옥에 갇혔는데, 제가 도둑이었기 때문입니다."], ["I was a thief, because my mother went on the streets when I was quite a little girl.", "저는 도둑이었는데, 제가 아주 어린 계집아이일 때 제 어머니가 거리로 나섰기 때문입니다."], ["My mother went on the streets, because the gentleman who was my father deserted her.", "제 어머니가 거리로 나선 것은, 제 아버지였던 그 신사가 그녀를 버렸기 때문입니다."], ["There is no need to tell such a common story as this, at any length.", "이처럼 흔한 이야기를 길게 늘어놓을 것은 없습니다."], ["It is told quite often enough in the newspapers.", "그것은 신문에 아주 자주 실립니다."]], [["“Lady Verinder was very kind to me, and Mr. Betteredge was very kind to me.", "\"레이디 버린더께서는 저에게 아주 다정하셨고, 베터리지 씨도 저에게 아주 다정하셨습니다."], ["Those two, and the matron at the reformatory, are the only good people I have ever met with in all my life.", "그 두 분과, 감화원의 그 사감님이, 제 온 생애에서 제가 만난 유일한 좋은 분들입니다."], ["I might have got on in my place—not happily—but I might have got on, if you had not come visiting.", "저는 제 자리에서 그럭저럭 지낼 수 있었을지도 모릅니다—행복하지는 않게라도—선생님께서 오셔서 머물지만 않으셨다면, 저는 그럭저럭 지낼 수 있었을지도 모릅니다."], ["I don’t blame you, sir.", "저는 선생님을 탓하지 않습니다, 선생님."], ["It’s my fault—all my fault.", "그것은 제 잘못입니다—죄다 제 잘못입니다."]], [["“Do you remember when you came out on us from among the sandhills, that morning, looking for Mr. Betteredge?", "\"그날 아침, 선생님께서 베터리지 씨를 찾으시며 그 모래언덕 사이에서 저희 앞으로 나오셨을 때를 기억하십니까?"], ["You were like a prince in a fairy-story.", "선생님은 마치 동화 속의 왕자님 같았습니다."], ["You were like a lover in a dream.", "선생님은 마치 꿈속의 연인 같았습니다."], ["You were the most adorable human creature I had ever seen.", "선생님은 제가 여태 본 가장 사랑스러운 인간이었습니다."], ["Something that felt like the happy life I had never led yet, leapt up in me at the instant I set eyes on you.", "제가 여태 살아 본 적 없는 그 행복한 삶처럼 느껴지는 무언가가, 제가 선생님께 눈길을 준 그 순간 제 안에서 솟구쳤습니다."], ["Don’t laugh at this if you can help it.", "하실 수만 있다면 이것을 비웃지 말아 주십시오."], ["Oh, if I could only make you feel how serious it is to me!", "오, 이것이 저에게 얼마나 진지한 것인지 선생님께 느끼게 해 드릴 수만 있다면!"]], [["“I went back to the house, and wrote your name and mine in my work-box, and drew a true lovers’ knot under them.", "\"저는 그 집으로 돌아가, 제 반짇고리에 선생님의 이름과 저의 이름을 적고, 그 아래에 진실한 연인의 매듭을 그렸습니다."], ["Then, some devil—no, I ought to say some good angel—whispered to me, ‘Go and look in the glass.’ The glass told me—never mind what.", "그러자 어떤 악마가—아니, 어떤 착한 천사라고 해야겠지요—저에게 속삭였습니다, '가서 거울을 보아라.' 그 거울이 저에게 일러 주었습니다—무엇을 일러 주었는지는 아무래도 좋습니다."], ["I was too foolish to take the warning.", "저는 그 경고를 새겨듣기에는 너무 어리석었습니다."], ["I went on getting fonder and fonder of you, just as if I was a lady in your own rank of life, and the most beautiful creature your eyes ever rested on.", "저는, 마치 제가 선생님과 같은 신분의 귀부인이자, 선생님의 눈이 여태 머문 가장 아름다운 존재이기라도 한 양, 선생님을 점점 더 사랑하게 되었습니다."], ["I tried—oh, dear, how I tried—to get you to look at me.", "저는 애썼습니다—오, 아아, 제가 얼마나 애썼는지—선생님이 저를 보시게 하려고요."], ["If you had known how I used to cry at night with the misery and the mortification of your never taking any notice of me, you would have pitied me perhaps, and have given me a look now and then to live on.", "선생님이 저를 조금도 알아봐 주지 않으시는 그 비참과 굴욕으로 제가 밤마다 어찌 울곤 했는지 아셨더라면, 선생님은 어쩌면 저를 가엾이 여겨, 제가 그것으로 살아갈 눈길을 이따금 저에게 주셨을지도 모릅니다."]], [["“It would have been no very kind look, perhaps, if you had known how I hated Miss Rachel.", "\"제가 레이철 아가씨를 얼마나 미워했는지 아셨더라면, 그것은 아마 그리 다정한 눈길은 아니었겠지요."], ["I believe I found out you were in love with her, before you knew it yourself.", "저는 선생님이 그녀를 사랑하고 계심을, 선생님 자신이 아시기도 전에 알아챘다고 믿습니다."], ["She used to give you roses to wear in your button-hole.", "그녀는 선생님께 단춧구멍에 꽂으실 장미를 주곤 했습니다."], ["Ah, Mr. Franklin, you wore my roses oftener than either you or she thought!", "아, 프랭클린 선생님, 선생님은 선생님도 그녀도 여기신 것보다 더 자주 저의 장미를 꽂으셨답니다!"], ["The only comfort I had at that time, was putting my rose secretly in your glass of water, in place of hers—and then throwing her rose away.", "그 무렵 제가 가진 유일한 위안은, 그녀의 것 대신 저의 장미를 몰래 선생님의 물잔에 꽂아 두고—그런 다음 그녀의 장미를 던져 버리는 것이었습니다."]], [["“If she had been really as pretty as you thought her, I might have borne it better.", "\"만일 그녀가 정말로 선생님이 여기신 만큼 어여뻤다면, 저는 그것을 더 잘 견뎠을지도 모릅니다."], ["No; I believe I should have been more spiteful against her still.", "아니요. 저는 그녀에게 더욱더 앙심을 품었으리라 믿습니다."], ["Suppose you put Miss Rachel into a servant’s dress, and took her ornaments off?", "레이철 아가씨에게 하녀의 옷을 입히고, 그녀의 장신구를 벗겨 놓았다고 쳐 보세요?"], ["I don’t know what is the use of my writing in this way.", "제가 어째서 이런 식으로 쓰고 있는지 모르겠습니다."], ["It can’t be denied that she had a bad figure; she was too thin.", "그녀의 몸매가 볼품없었다는 것은 부인할 수 없습니다. 그녀는 너무 말랐습니다."], ["But who can tell what the men like?", "허나 사내들이 무엇을 좋아하는지 누가 알겠습니까?"], ["And young ladies may behave in a manner which would cost a servant her place.", "그리고 젊은 아가씨는 하녀 같으면 제 자리를 잃을 만한 행실을 부려도 됩니다."], ["It’s no business of mine.", "그것은 저와 상관없는 일이지요."], ["I can’t expect you to read my letter, if I write it in this way.", "제가 이런 식으로 쓴다면, 선생님이 제 편지를 읽어 주시기를 바랄 수 없습니다."], ["But it does stir one up to hear Miss Rachel called pretty, when one knows all the time that it’s her dress does it, and her confidence in herself.", "허나, 내내 그것이 그녀의 옷과, 그녀의 제 자신에 대한 자신감이 만든 것임을 알면서도, 레이철 아가씨가 어여쁘다고 불리는 것을 듣는 것은 정말이지 사람의 속을 뒤집어 놓습니다."]], [["“Try not to lose patience with me, sir.", "\"저에게 참을성을 잃지 말아 주십시오, 선생님."], ["I will get on as fast as I can to the time which is sure to interest you—the time when the Diamond was lost.", "저는 선생님께서 틀림없이 관심 두실 그때—그 다이아몬드가 없어진 그때로 되도록 빨리 나아가겠습니다."]], [["“But there is one thing which I have got it on my mind to tell you first.", "\"허나 먼저 선생님께 말씀드려야겠다고 마음먹은 것이 한 가지 있습니다."]], [["“My life was not a very hard life to bear, while I was a thief.", "\"제가 도둑이던 동안에는, 제 삶이 견디기에 그리 힘든 삶이 아니었습니다."], ["It was only when they had taught me at the reformatory to feel my own degradation, and to try for better things, that the days grew long and weary.", "감화원에서 저에게 제 자신의 타락을 느끼도록, 그리고 더 나은 것을 좇도록 가르치고 나서야, 비로소 나날이 길고 고달파졌습니다."], ["Thoughts of the future forced themselves on me now.", "이제 미래에 대한 생각들이 저에게 밀려들었습니다."], ["I felt the dreadful reproach that honest people—even the kindest of honest people—were to me in themselves.", "저는 정직한 사람들이—가장 다정한 정직한 사람들조차—그저 그 자체로 저에게 얼마나 무서운 꾸짖음인지를 느꼈습니다."], ["A heart-breaking sensation of loneliness kept with me, go where I might, and do what I might, and see what persons I might.", "어디를 가든, 무엇을 하든, 어떤 사람들을 보든, 가슴이 미어지는 외로움이 저에게서 떠나지 않았습니다."], ["It was my duty, I know, to try and get on with my fellow-servants in my new place.", "새 자리에서 제 동료 하인들과 그럭저럭 어울려 지내려 애쓰는 것이 저의 도리임을, 저도 압니다."], ["Somehow, I couldn’t make friends with them.", "어쩐지 저는 그들과 벗이 될 수 없었습니다."], ["They looked (or I thought they looked) as if they suspected what I had been.", "그들은 (또는 그렇게 보인다고 제가 여겼습니다) 제가 무엇이었는지를 의심하는 듯 보였습니다."], ["I don’t regret, far from it, having been roused to make the effort to be a reformed woman—but, indeed, indeed it was a weary life.", "저는, 개과천선한 여자가 되려는 애씀에 눈뜬 것을 후회하지 않습니다, 결코 후회하지 않습니다—허나, 정말이지, 정말이지 그것은 고달픈 삶이었습니다."], ["You had come across it like a beam of sunshine at first—and then you too failed me.", "선생님은 처음에는 한 줄기 햇살처럼 그 삶을 가로질러 오셨습니다—그러고는 선생님마저 저를 저버리셨습니다."], ["I was mad enough to love you; and I couldn’t even attract your notice.", "저는 선생님을 사랑할 만큼 미쳐 있었습니다. 그런데 저는 선생님의 눈길조차 끌 수 없었습니다."], ["There was great misery—there really was great misery in that.", "거기에는 큰 비참이 있었습니다—정말로 거기에는 큰 비참이 있었습니다."]], [["“Now I am coming to what I wanted to tell you.", "\"이제 저는 제가 말씀드리고 싶었던 것에 이릅니다."], ["In those days of bitterness, I went two or three times, when it was my turn to go out, to my favourite place—the beach above the Shivering Sand.", "그 쓰라린 나날에, 저는 외출할 차례가 되면, 저의 가장 좋아하는 곳—흔들리는 모래 위쪽의 그 해변으로 두세 번 갔습니다."], ["And I said to myself, ‘I think it will end here.", "그리고 저는 스스로에게 말했습니다, '나는 이것이 여기서 끝나리라 생각해."], ["When I can bear it no longer, I think it will end here.’ You will understand, sir, that the place had laid a kind of spell on me before you came.", "내가 더는 견딜 수 없게 되면, 나는 이것이 여기서 끝나리라 생각해.' 아시겠지만, 선생님, 그곳은 선생님이 오시기 전부터 저에게 일종의 마법을 걸어 두었습니다."], ["I had always had a notion that something would happen to me at the quicksand.", "저는 언제나 그 늪 모래에서 저에게 무슨 일이 일어나리라는 생각을 품고 있었습니다."], ["But I had never looked at it, with the thought of its being the means of my making away with myself, till the time came of which I am now writing.", "허나 저는, 지금 제가 쓰고 있는 그때가 오기까지는, 그것을 제 자신을 없애는 수단이 되리라는 생각으로 바라본 적은 없었습니다."], ["Then I did think that here was a place which would end all my troubles for me in a moment or two—and hide me for ever afterwards.", "그때 저는 정녕, 여기가 저의 온갖 괴로움을 한두 순간에 끝내 줄—그리고 그 뒤로 영영 저를 감추어 줄 곳이라고 생각했습니다."]], [["“This is all I have to say about myself, reckoning from the morning when I first saw you, to the morning when the alarm was raised in the house that the Diamond was lost.", "\"이것이, 제가 선생님을 처음 본 그 아침부터, 그 다이아몬드가 없어졌다는 소동이 그 집에서 일어난 그 아침까지 헤아려, 저 자신에 대해 드릴 말씀의 전부입니다."]], [["“I was so aggravated by the foolish talk among the women servants, all wondering who was to be suspected first; and I was so angry with you (knowing no better at that time) for the pains you took in hunting for the jewel, and sending for the police, that I kept as much as possible away by myself, until later in the day, when the officer from Frizinghall came to the house.", "\"저는 누가 맨 먼저 의심받을까 죄 궁금해하는 하녀들 사이의 그 어리석은 수다에 몹시 짜증이 났습니다. 그리고 저는 (그때는 더 잘 알지 못한 채) 선생님이 그 보석을 찾느라 애쓰고 경찰을 부르시는 것에 몹시 화가 나서, 그날 늦게 프리징홀에서 그 경관이 그 집에 올 때까지, 되도록 저 혼자 떨어져 있었습니다."]], [["“Mr. Seegrave began, as you may remember, by setting a guard on the women’s bedrooms; and the women all followed him upstairs in a rage, to know what he meant by the insult he had put on them.", "\"기억하시겠지만, 시그레이브 씨는 먼저 하녀들의 침실에 파수를 세우는 것으로 시작했습니다."], ["I went with the rest, because if I had done anything different from the rest, Mr.", "그러자 하녀들은 그가 자기들에게 준 그 모욕이 무슨 뜻인지 따지려, 죄 성이 나서 그를 따라 위층으로 올라갔습니다."], ["Seegrave was the sort of man who would have suspected me directly.", "저도 나머지와 함께 갔는데, 만일 제가 나머지와 다른 무언가를 했다면, 시그레이브 씨는 대뜸 저를 의심했을 그런 부류의 사람이었기 때문입니다."], ["We found him in Miss Rachel’s room.", "우리는 그가 레이철 아가씨의 방에 있는 것을 보았습니다."], ["He told us he wouldn’t have a lot of women there; and he pointed to the smear on the painted door, and said some of our petticoats had done the mischief, and sent us all downstairs again.", "그는 여자들을 잔뜩 그곳에 두지 않겠다고 우리에게 말했습니다. 그리고 그는 그 칠해진 문의 얼룩을 가리키며, 우리 페티코트 자락 가운데 하나가 그 사달을 냈다고 말하고는, 우리를 죄 다시 아래층으로 보냈습니다."]], [["“After leaving Miss Rachel’s room, I stopped a moment on one of the landings, by myself, to see if I had got the paint-stain by any chance on my gown.", "\"레이철 아가씨의 방을 나온 뒤, 저는 혹시 저의 옷에 그 페인트 얼룩이 묻지는 않았는지 보려고, 층계참 하나에서 혼자 잠시 멈춰 섰습니다."], ["Penelope Betteredge (the only one of the women with whom I was on friendly terms) passed, and noticed what I was about.", "페넬로피 베터리지가 (제가 사이좋게 지낸 유일한 하녀였습니다) 지나가다, 제가 무엇을 하는지 알아챘습니다."]], [["“‘You needn’t trouble yourself, Rosanna,’ she said.", "\"'애태울 것 없어, 로재나,' 하고 그녀가 말했습니다."], ["‘The paint on Miss Rachel’s door has been dry for hours.", "'레이철 아가씨 방의 그 페인트는 몇 시간 전에 말랐는걸."], ["If Mr. Seegrave hadn’t set a watch on our bedrooms, I might have told him as much.", "시그레이브 씨가 우리 침실에 파수를 세우지만 않았어도, 내가 그에게 그만큼 말해 줬을 텐데."], ["I don’t know what you think—I was never so insulted before in my life!’", "네 생각은 모르겠지만—나는 평생 이렇게 모욕당한 적이 없어!'"]], [["“Penelope was a hot-tempered girl.", "\"페넬로피는 성미가 급한 아이였습니다."], ["I quieted her, and brought her back to what she had said about the paint on the door having been dry for hours.", "저는 그녀를 달래, 그 문의 페인트가 몇 시간 전에 말랐다고 한 그녀의 말로 그녀를 되돌렸습니다."]], [["“‘How do you know that?’ I asked.", "\"'그걸 어떻게 알아?' 하고 저는 물었습니다."]], [["“‘I was with Miss Rachel, and Mr.", "\"'나는 어제 아침 내내 레이철 아가씨랑 프랭클린 나리랑 함께 있었잖아,'"], ["Franklin, all yesterday morning,’ Penelope said, ‘mixing the colours, while they finished the door.", "하고 페넬로피가 말했습니다. '그분들이 그 문을 마저 칠하시는 동안 물감을 개면서 말이야."], ["I heard Miss Rachel ask whether the door would be dry that evening, in time for the birthday company to see it.", "레이철 아가씨가 그 문이 그날 저녁, 생일 손님들이 볼 수 있게 제때 마를지 여쭙는 걸 나는 들었어."], ["And Mr. Franklin shook his head, and said it wouldn’t be dry in less than twelve hours.", "그러자 프랭클린 나리가 고개를 저으시며, 열두 시간 안에는 마르지 않을 거라고 하셨지."], ["It was long past luncheon-time—it was three o’clock before they had done.", "점심때가 한참 지나서였어—그분들이 마치신 게 세 시였으니까."], ["What does your arithmetic say, Rosanna?", "네 셈으로는 어떻게 나오니, 로재나?"], ["Mine says the door was dry by three this morning.’", "내 셈으로는 그 문이 오늘 아침 세 시쯤엔 말랐어.'"]], [["“‘Did some of the ladies go upstairs yesterday evening to see it?’ I asked.", "\"'어제 저녁 그걸 보러 위층에 올라간 부인들이 있었어?' 하고 저는 물었습니다."], ["‘I thought I heard Miss Rachel warning them to keep clear of the door.’", "'레이철 아가씨가 그 문에 가까이 가지 말라고 그분들께 이르시는 걸 들은 것 같은데.'"]], [["“‘None of the ladies made the smear,’ Penelope answered.", "\"'그 얼룩을 낸 부인은 아무도 없어,' 하고 페넬로피가 대답했습니다."], ["‘I left Miss Rachel in bed at twelve last night.", "'나는 어젯밤 열두 시에 레이철 아가씨를 잠자리에 뉘어 드리고 나왔어."], ["And I noticed the door, and there was nothing wrong with it then.’", "그리고 그 문을 눈여겨봤는데, 그때는 그것에 아무 이상도 없었어.'"]], [["“‘Oughtn’t you to mention this to Mr. Seegrave, Penelope?’", "\"'이걸 시그레이브 씨에게 말해야 하지 않겠니, 페넬로피?'"]], [["“‘I wouldn’t say a word to help Mr. Seegrave for anything that could be offered to me!’", "\"'나한테 무엇을 주더라도 시그레이브 씨를 돕는 말은 한마디도 안 할 거야!'"]], [["“She went to her work, and I went to mine.", "\"그녀는 제 일을 하러 갔고, 저는 제 일을 하러 갔습니다."]], [["“My work, sir, was to make your bed, and to put your room tidy.", "\"저의 일은, 선생님, 선생님의 잠자리를 봐 드리고, 선생님의 방을 정돈하는 것이었습니다."], ["It was the happiest hour I had in the whole day.", "그것은 온종일 중에 제가 가진 가장 행복한 시간이었습니다."], ["I used to kiss the pillow on which your head had rested all night.", "저는 밤새 선생님의 머리가 놓였던 그 베개에 입을 맞추곤 했습니다."], ["No matter who has done it since, you have never had your clothes folded as nicely as I folded them for you.", "그 뒤로 누가 그 일을 했든, 선생님은 제가 선생님을 위해 갠 것만큼 곱게 옷이 개어진 적이 없으셨을 것입니다."], ["Of all the little knick-knacks in your dressing-case, there wasn’t one that had so much as a speck on it.", "선생님의 화장 상자 안의 온갖 자잘한 소품들 가운데, 티끌 하나라도 앉은 것은 없었습니다."], ["You never noticed it, any more than you noticed me.", "선생님은 저를 알아보지 않으신 것과 마찬가지로, 그것도 알아보지 않으셨습니다."], ["I beg your pardon; I am forgetting myself.", "용서하십시오, 제가 저를 잊고 있습니다."], ["I will make haste, and go on again.", "서둘러, 다시 나아가겠습니다."]], [["“Well, I went in that morning to do my work in your room.", "\"자, 그날 아침 저는 선생님의 방에서 제 일을 하러 들어갔습니다."], ["There was your nightgown tossed across the bed, just as you had thrown it off.", "거기, 선생님이 벗어 던지신 그대로, 선생님의 잠옷이 침대에 걸쳐져 있었습니다."], ["I took it up to fold it—and I saw the stain of the paint from Miss Rachel’s door!", "저는 그것을 개려고 집어 들었습니다—그리고 저는 레이철 아가씨의 문에서 묻은 그 페인트 얼룩을 보았습니다!"]], [["“I was so startled by the discovery that I ran out with the nightgown in my hand, and made for the back stairs, and locked myself into my own room, to look at it in a place where nobody could intrude and interrupt me.", "\"저는 그 발견에 어찌나 소스라쳤던지, 그 잠옷을 손에 든 채 뛰쳐나가, 뒤쪽 계단으로 가서, 아무도 끼어들어 저를 방해할 수 없는 곳에서 그것을 보려고, 제 방에 저를 가두었습니다."]], [["“As soon as I got my breath again, I called to mind my talk with Penelope, and I said to myself, ‘Here’s the proof that he was in Miss Rachel’s sitting-room between twelve last night, and three this morning!’", "\"다시 숨을 돌리자마자, 저는 페넬로피와의 그 이야기를 떠올리고, 스스로에게 말했습니다, '여기 그분이 어젯밤 열두 시와 오늘 아침 세 시 사이에 레이철 아가씨의 응접실에 계셨다는 증거가 있어!'"]], [["“I shall not tell you in plain words what was the first suspicion that crossed my mind, when I had made that discovery.", "\"제가 그 발견을 했을 때 제 마음을 스친 첫 의심이 무엇이었는지는, 대놓고 말씀드리지 않겠습니다."], ["You would only be angry—and, if you were angry, you might tear my letter up and read no more of it.", "선생님은 그저 화만 나실 테니까요—그리고, 화가 나시면, 선생님은 제 편지를 찢어 버리시고 더는 읽지 않으실지도 모릅니다."]], [["“Let it be enough, if you please, to say only this.", "\"부디, 오직 이것만 말씀드리는 것으로 넉넉하다고 해 주십시오."], ["After thinking it over to the best of my ability, I made it out that the thing wasn’t likely, for a reason that I will tell you.", "제 능력껏 그것을 곰곰이 헤아려 본 끝에, 저는 그 일이 있음 직하지 않다고, 제가 말씀드릴 한 가지 이유에서, 결론지었습니다."], ["If you had been in Miss Rachel’s sitting-room, at that time of night, with Miss Rachel’s knowledge (and if you had been foolish enough to forget to take care of the wet door) she would have reminded you—she would never have let you carry away such a witness against her, as the witness I was looking at now!", "만일 선생님이 그 밤 시각에, 레이철 아가씨가 아시는 채로 그녀의 응접실에 계셨다면 (그리고 만일 선생님이 그 젖은 문을 조심할 것을 잊을 만큼 어리석으셨다면) 그녀가 선생님께 일러 드렸을 것입니다—그녀가 결코, 지금 제가 보고 있는 그 증인 같은, 자신에게 불리한 증인을 선생님이 가지고 나가시게 두지 않았을 것입니다!"], ["At the same time, I own I was not completely certain in my own mind that I had proved my own suspicion to be wrong.", "동시에, 저는 제 의심이 틀렸음을 증명했다고 제 마음속으로 온전히 확신한 것은 아니었음을 털어놓습니다."], ["You will not have forgotten that I have owned to hating Miss Rachel.", "제가 레이철 아가씨를 미워했다고 털어놓은 것을 잊지 않으셨겠지요."], ["Try to think, if you can, that there was a little of that hatred in all this.", "하실 수 있다면, 이 모든 것에 그 미움이 얼마쯤 섞여 있었다고 생각해 주십시오."], ["It ended in my determining to keep the nightgown, and to wait, and watch, and see what use I might make of it.", "그것은, 제가 그 잠옷을 간직하고, 기다리며, 지켜보며, 그것으로 제가 무슨 쓸모를 얻을 수 있을지 보기로 마음먹는 것으로 끝났습니다."], ["At that time, please to remember, not the ghost of an idea entered my head that you had stolen the Diamond.”", "그때, 부디 기억해 주십시오, 선생님이 그 다이아몬드를 훔치셨으리라는 생각은 그 유령조차 제 머릿속에 들어오지 않았습니다.\""]], [["There, I broke off in the reading of the letter for the second time.", "거기서, 나는 그 편지 읽기를 두 번째로 멈추었다."]], [["I had read those portions of the miserable woman’s confession which related to myself, with unaffected surprise, and, I can honestly add, with sincere distress.", "나는 그 가엾은 여인의 고백 가운데 나 자신과 관련된 부분들을, 꾸밈없는 놀라움과, 정직하게 덧붙이자면, 진심 어린 괴로움을 안고 읽었다."], ["I had regretted, truly regretted, the aspersion which I had thoughtlessly cast on her memory, before I had seen a line of her letter.", "나는, 그녀의 편지를 한 줄도 보기 전에 내가 무심히 그녀의 넋에 던진 그 험담을, 참으로 뉘우쳤다."], ["But when I had advanced as far as the passage which is quoted above, I own I felt my mind growing bitterer and bitterer against Rosanna Spearman as I went on.", "허나 위에 인용된 그 대목까지 나아갔을 때, 나는 인정한다, 읽어 나갈수록 로재나 스피어먼에 대한 내 마음이 점점 더 쓰라려지는 것을 느꼈음을."], ["“Read the rest for yourself,”", "\"나머지는 자네가 스스로 읽게.\""], ["I said, handing the letter to Betteredge across the table.", "하고 나는, 그 편지를 탁자 너머로 베터리지에게 건네며 말했다."], ["“If there is anything in it that I must look at, you can tell me as you go on.”", "\"그 안에 내가 꼭 보아야 할 것이 있거든, 자네가 읽어 가며 나에게 일러 주게.\""]], [["“I understand you, Mr.", "\"알겠습니다, 프랭클린 나리,\""], ["Franklin,” he answered.", "하고 그가 대답했다."], ["“It’s natural, sir, in you.", "\"그것은 나리로서는 당연한 일입지요."], ["And, God help us all!”", "그리고, 우리 모두를 하느님이 도우시기를!\""], ["he added, in a lower tone, “it’s no less natural in her.”", "하고 그는 한결 낮은 어조로 덧붙였다. \"그것은 그 아이로서도 못지않게 당연한 일입니다.\""]], [["I proceed to copy the continuation of the letter from the original, in my own possession:—", "나는 내가 지니고 있는 원본에서 그 편지의 이어지는 부분을 옮겨 적어 나간다—"]], [["“Having determined to keep the nightgown, and to see what use my love, or my revenge (I hardly know which) could turn it to in the future, the next thing to discover was how to keep it without the risk of being found out.", "\"그 잠옷을 간직하고, 저의 사랑, 또는 저의 복수가 (어느 쪽인지 저도 잘 모르겠습니다) 앞으로 그것을 무슨 쓸모로 돌릴 수 있을지 보기로 마음먹었으니, 다음으로 알아내야 할 것은 들킬 위험 없이 그것을 간직할 방도였습니다."]], [["“There was only one way—to make another nightgown exactly like it, before Saturday came, and brought the laundry-woman and her inventory to the house.", "\"길은 오직 하나였습니다—토요일이 와서 세탁하는 여자와 그녀의 물목 명세를 그 집에 데려오기 전에, 그것과 꼭 같은 잠옷을 또 하나 만드는 것이었습니다."]], [["“I was afraid to put it off till next day (the Friday); being in doubt lest some accident might happen in the interval.", "\"저는 그것을 이튿날(금요일)까지 미루기가 두려웠습니다. 그 사이에 무슨 사고가 일어날까 봐 걱정스러웠기 때문입니다."], ["I determined to make the new nightgown on that same day (the Thursday), while I could count, if I played my cards properly, on having my time to myself.", "저는, 제가 패를 제대로 놀리기만 하면 제 시간을 저 자신에게 쓸 수 있으리라 셈할 수 있는, 바로 그날(목요일)에 그 새 잠옷을 만들기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["The first thing to do (after locking up your nightgown in my drawer) was to go back to your bedroom—not so much to put it to rights (Penelope would have done that for me, if I had asked her) as to find out whether you had smeared off any of the paint-stain from your nightgown, on the bed, or on any piece of furniture in the room.", "맨 먼저 할 일은 (선생님의 잠옷을 제 서랍에 넣고 잠근 뒤) 선생님의 침실로 돌아가는 것이었는데—그것을 정돈하기 위해서라기보다는 (제가 청했다면 페넬로피가 저를 위해 그것을 해 주었을 것입니다) 선생님이 선생님의 잠옷에서 그 페인트 얼룩을 침대나 방 안의 어떤 가구에 묻혀 놓지는 않으셨는지 알아내기 위해서였습니다."]], [["“I examined everything narrowly, and at last, I found a few streaks of the paint on the inside of your dressing-gown—not the linen dressing-gown you usually wore in that summer season, but a flannel dressing-gown which you had with you also.", "\"저는 온갖 것을 샅샅이 살폈고, 마침내, 선생님의 실내복 안쪽에서 페인트 몇 줄기를 찾아냈습니다—그해 여름철에 선생님이 늘 입으시던 그 아마포 실내복이 아니라, 선생님이 함께 가지고 계셨던 플란넬 실내복이었습니다."], ["I suppose you felt chilly after walking to and fro in nothing but your nightdress, and put on the warmest thing you could find.", "짐작건대 선생님은 잠옷만 입고 이리저리 걸으신 뒤 으슬으슬 추위를 느끼시어, 찾을 수 있는 가장 따뜻한 것을 걸치셨겠지요."], ["At any rate, there were the stains, just visible, on the inside of the dressing-gown.", "어쨌든, 거기, 그 실내복 안쪽에, 그 얼룩이 겨우 보일락 말락 있었습니다."], ["I easily got rid of these by scraping away the stuff of the flannel.", "저는 그 플란넬의 보풀을 긁어내어 이것들을 쉽게 없앴습니다."], ["This done, the only proof left against you was the proof locked up in my drawer.", "이렇게 하고 나니, 선생님에게 불리하게 남은 유일한 증거는 제 서랍에 넣고 잠근 그 증거뿐이었습니다."]], [["“I had just finished your room when I was sent for to be questioned by Mr. Seegrave, along with the rest of the servants.", "\"제가 막 선생님의 방을 다 정돈했을 때, 저는 나머지 하인들과 함께 시그레이브 씨에게 신문받으라는 부름을 받았습니다."], ["Next came the examination of all our boxes.", "다음으로 우리 모두의 상자에 대한 검사가 이어졌습니다."], ["And then followed the most extraordinary event of the day—to me—since I had found the paint on your nightgown.", "그러고는, 제가 선생님의 잠옷에서 그 페인트를 찾아낸 뒤로, 그날의—저에게—가장 놀라운 사건이 뒤따랐습니다."], ["This event came out of the second questioning of Penelope Betteredge by Superintendent Seegrave.", "이 사건은 시그레이브 슈퍼린텐던트의 페넬로피 베터리지에 대한 두 번째 신문에서 나왔습니다."]], [["“Penelope returned to us quite beside herself with rage at the manner in which Mr. Seegrave had treated her.", "\"페넬로피는 시그레이브 씨가 자기를 다룬 그 태도에 아주 넋이 나갈 만큼 성이 나서 우리에게 돌아왔습니다."], ["He had hinted, beyond the possibility of mistaking him, that he suspected her of being the thief.", "그는, 잘못 알아들을 여지가 조금도 없이, 그녀가 그 도둑이 아닐까 의심한다고 넌지시 비쳤던 것입니다."], ["We were all equally astonished at hearing this, and we all asked, Why?", "우리는 이 말을 듣고 죄 똑같이 놀라, 죄 물었습니다, 어째서냐고."]], [["“‘Because the Diamond was in Miss Rachel’s sitting-room,’ Penelope answered.", "\"'그 다이아몬드가 레이철 아가씨의 응접실에 있었으니까,' 하고 페넬로피가 대답했습니다."], ["‘And because I was the last person in the sitting-room at night!’", "'그리고 내가 밤에 그 응접실에 있었던 마지막 사람이었으니까!'"]], [["“Almost before the words had left her lips, I remembered that another person had been in the sitting-room later than Penelope.", "\"그 말이 그녀의 입술을 채 떠나기도 전에, 저는 또 다른 사람이 페넬로피보다 더 늦게 그 응접실에 있었음을 떠올렸습니다."], ["That person was yourself.", "그 사람은 바로 선생님이었습니다."], ["My head whirled round, and my thoughts were in dreadful confusion.", "제 머리가 핑핑 돌았고, 제 생각은 끔찍한 혼란에 빠졌습니다."], ["In the midst of it all, something in my mind whispered to me that the smear on your nightgown might have a meaning entirely different to the meaning which I had given to it up to that time.", "그 온갖 것의 한복판에서, 제 마음속의 무언가가 저에게, 선생님의 잠옷에 묻은 그 얼룩이 그때까지 제가 그것에 부여해 온 뜻과는 전혀 다른 뜻을 지녔을지도 모른다고 속삭였습니다. '만일 그 방에 있었던 마지막 사람이 의심받을 사람이라면,'"], ["‘If the last person who was in the room is the person to be suspected,’ I thought to myself, ‘the thief is not Penelope, but Mr. Franklin Blake!’", "하고 저는 속으로 생각했습니다, '그 도둑은 페넬로피가 아니라, 프랭클린 블레이크 나리야!'"]], [["“In the case of any other gentleman, I believe I should have been ashamed of suspecting him of theft, almost as soon as the suspicion had passed through my mind.", "\"여느 다른 신사의 경우라면, 저는 그를 절도로 의심한 것을, 그 의심이 제 마음을 스치기가 무섭게 부끄러워했으리라 믿습니다."]], [["“But the bare thought that you had let yourself down to my level, and that I, in possessing myself of your nightgown, had also possessed myself of the means of shielding you from being discovered, and disgraced for life—I say, sir, the bare thought of this seemed to open such a chance before me of winning your good will, that I passed blindfold, as one may say, from suspecting to believing.", "\"허나 선생님이 저의 수준으로 몸을 낮추셨다는, 그리고 제가, 선생님의 잠옷을 손에 넣음으로써, 선생님이 발각되어 평생 욕되게 되는 것으로부터 선생님을 지켜 드릴 수단도 손에 넣었다는—그 헐벗은 생각만으로도, 선생님의 호의를 얻을 그런 기회가 제 앞에 열리는 듯싶어, 저는, 이를테면, 눈을 감은 채 의심에서 믿음으로 건너갔습니다."], ["I made up my mind, on the spot, that you had shown yourself the busiest of anybody in fetching the police, as a blind to deceive us all; and that the hand which had taken Miss Rachel’s jewel could by no possibility be any other hand than yours.", "저는 그 자리에서, 선생님이 우리 모두를 속이려는 눈가림으로, 경찰을 부르는 데에 누구보다 부산히 나서는 모습을 보이셨다고, 그리고 레이철 아가씨의 보석을 가져간 그 손은 도저히 선생님의 손 말고 다른 손일 수 없다고 마음을 굳혔습니다."]], [["“The excitement of this new discovery of mine must, I think, have turned my head for a while.", "\"저의 이 새로운 발견의 흥분이, 제 생각에, 한동안 제 머리를 돌게 했음이 틀림없습니다."], ["I felt such a devouring eagerness to see you—to try you with a word or two about the Diamond, and to make you look at me, and speak to me, in that way—that I put my hair tidy, and made myself as nice as I could, and went to you boldly in the library where I knew you were writing.", "저는 선생님을 보고 싶은—그 다이아몬드에 관해 한두 마디로 선생님을 떠보고, 선생님이 저를 그런 식으로 보시고, 저에게 말을 거시게 만들고 싶은 어찌나 애타는 열망을 느꼈던지—저는 제 머리를 매만지고, 되도록 곱게 저를 꾸미고, 선생님이 글을 쓰고 계신 줄 아는 그 서재로 대담하게 선생님께 갔습니다."]], [["“You had left one of your rings upstairs, which made as good an excuse for my intrusion as I could have desired.", "\"선생님은 반지 하나를 위층에 두고 오셨는데, 그것이 제가 바랐을 만한 좋은 침입의 핑계가 되어 주었습니다."], ["But, oh, sir!", "허나, 오, 선생님!"], ["if you have ever loved, you will understand how it was that all my courage cooled, when I walked into the room, and found myself in your presence.", "만일 선생님이 사랑을 해 보신 적이 있다면, 제가 그 방으로 걸어 들어가 선생님 앞에 서게 되었을 때, 어찌하여 저의 온 용기가 식어 버렸는지 이해하실 것입니다."], ["And then, you looked up at me so coldly, and you thanked me for finding your ring in such an indifferent manner, that my knees trembled under me, and I felt as if I should drop on the floor at your feet.", "그러고는, 선생님이 저를 어찌나 차갑게 올려다보시고, 어찌나 무심한 태도로 반지를 찾아 준 것에 대해 저에게 고마워하셨던지, 제 무릎이 저를 지탱하지 못하고 떨렸고, 저는 마치 선생님 발치의 바닥에 쓰러질 것만 같았습니다. 저에게 고마워하시고서, 기억하신다면, 선생님은 다시 선생님의 글로 눈을 돌리셨습니다."], ["When you had thanked me, you looked back, if you remember, at your writing.", "저는 이런 식으로 대접받은 것이 어찌나 굴욕스러웠던지, 말할 만큼의 기운을 가까스로 냈습니다. 저는 말했습니다, '이 다이아몬드 일은 참 이상하네요, 나리.'"], ["I was so mortified at being treated in this way, that I plucked up spirit enough to speak.", "그러자 선생님은 다시 올려다보시며, '그래, 그렇지!' 하고 말씀하셨습니다."], ["I said, ‘This is a strange thing about the Diamond, sir.’ And you looked up again, and said, ‘Yes, it is!’ You spoke civilly (I can’t deny that); but still you kept a distance—a cruel distance between us.", "선생님은 점잖게 말씀하셨습니다 (그것은 부인할 수 없습니다). 허나 그럼에도 선생님은 거리를—우리 사이에 잔인한 거리를 두셨습니다."], ["Believing, as I did, that you had got the lost Diamond hidden about you, while you were speaking, your coolness so provoked me that I got bold enough, in the heat of the moment, to give you a hint.", "선생님이 말씀하시는 동안, 선생님이 그 잃어버린 다이아몬드를 몸에 감추고 계신다고 믿었기에, 선생님의 그 차가움이 저를 어찌나 부아 나게 했던지, 저는 그 북받침에 겨워 대담해져서 선생님께 넌지시 비쳤습니다. 저는 말했습니다, '그들은 결코 그 다이아몬드를 못 찾을 거예요, 나리, 그렇지요?"], ["I said, ‘They will never find the Diamond, sir, will they?", "아무렴요! 그것을 가져간 사람도 못 찾을 거예요—그건 제가 장담해요.'"], ["No! nor the person who took it—I’ll answer for that.’ I nodded, and smiled at you, as much as to say, ‘I know!’ This time, you looked up at me with something like interest in your eyes; and I felt that a few more words on your side and mine might bring out the truth.", "저는 고개를 끄덕이고, 마치 '난 알아요!'"], ["Just at that moment, Mr.", "하고 말하는 것처럼 선생님께 미소 지었습니다."], ["Betteredge spoilt it all by coming to the door.", "이번에는, 선생님이 눈에 얼마쯤 관심 같은 것을 담고 저를 올려다보셨습니다."], ["I knew his footstep, and I also knew that it was against his rules for me to be in the library at that time of day—let alone being there along with you.", "그리고 저는 선생님 편과 제 편의 몇 마디만 더 있으면 진실이 드러날지도 모른다고 느꼈습니다. 바로 그 순간, 베터리지 씨가 그 문으로 옴으로써 그 모든 것을 망쳐 놓았습니다."], ["I had only just time to get out of my own accord, before he could come in and tell me to go.", "저는 그의 발소리를 알았고, 또한 그 시각에 제가 서재에 있는 것이—선생님과 함께 거기 있는 것은 말할 것도 없이—그의 규율에 어긋난다는 것도 알았습니다. 저는 그가 들어와 저더러 가라고 이르기 전에, 제 발로 나올 겨우 그만큼의 짬밖에 없었습니다."], ["I was angry and disappointed; but I was not entirely without hope for all that.", "저는 화가 나고 실망했습니다. 허나 그래도 저는 아주 희망이 없지는 않았습니다."], ["The ice, you see, was broken between us—and I thought I would take care, on the next occasion, that Mr. Betteredge was out of the way.", "아시겠지만, 우리 사이의 얼음이 깨진 것이었습니다—그리고 저는 다음번에는, 베터리지 씨가 자리에 없도록 조심하리라 생각했습니다."]], [["“When I got back to the servants’ hall, the bell was going for our dinner.", "\"제가 하인 방으로 돌아왔을 때, 우리의 저녁을 알리는 종이 울리고 있었습니다."], ["Afternoon already!", "벌써 오후였습니다!"], ["and the materials for making the new nightgown were still to be got!", "그런데 그 새 잠옷을 만들 재료는 아직 마련해야 했습니다!"], ["There was but one chance of getting them.", "그것을 마련할 기회는 오직 하나였습니다."], ["I shammed ill at dinner; and so secured the whole of the interval from then till tea-time to my own use.", "저는 저녁 때 앓는 시늉을 했습니다. 그리하여 그때부터 다과 시간까지의 사이 전부를 저 자신의 몫으로 확보했습니다."]], [["“What I was about, while the household believed me to be lying down in my own room; and how I spent the night, after shamming ill again at tea-time, and having been sent up to bed, there is no need to tell you.", "\"온 집안이 제가 제 방에 누워 있는 줄로 믿는 동안 제가 무엇을 했는지, 그리고 다과 시간에 다시 앓는 시늉을 하고 잠자리로 올려 보내진 뒤 제가 밤을 어떻게 보냈는지는, 선생님께 말씀드릴 것도 없습니다."], ["Sergeant Cuff discovered that much, if he discovered nothing more. And I can guess how.", "커프 부장은, 더는 알아내지 못했더라도, 그만큼은 알아냈습니다."], ["I was detected (though I kept my veil down) in the draper’s shop at Frizinghall.", "그리고 저는 어떻게인지 짐작할 수 있습니다. 저는 (베일을 내리고 있었건만) 프리징홀의 포목점에서 들켰습니다."], ["There was a glass in front of me, at the counter where I was buying the longcloth; and—in that glass—I saw one of the shopmen point to my shoulder and whisper to another.", "제가 옥양목을 사고 있던 그 계산대 앞에 거울이 하나 있었습니다. 그리고—그 거울 속에서—저는 점원 하나가 제 어깨를 가리키며 다른 점원에게 소곤대는 것을 보았습니다."], ["At night again, when I was secretly at work, locked into my room, I heard the breathing of the women servants who suspected me, outside my door.", "밤에 다시, 제가 방에 갇혀 몰래 일하고 있을 때, 저는 저를 의심하는 하녀들이 제 문밖에서 숨 쉬는 소리를 들었습니다."]], [["“It didn’t matter then; it doesn’t matter now.", "\"그때는 그것이 대수롭지 않았고, 지금도 그것은 대수롭지 않습니다."], ["On the Friday morning, hours before Sergeant Cuff entered the house, there was the new nightgown—to make up your number in place of the nightgown that I had got—made, wrung out, dried, ironed, marked, and folded as the laundry woman folded all the others, safe in your drawer.", "금요일 아침, 커프 부장이 그 집에 들기 몇 시간 전에, 거기 그 새 잠옷이—제가 손에 넣은 잠옷 대신 선생님의 벌수를 채우려고—만들어지고, 짜지고, 말려지고, 다려지고, 표시되고, 세탁하는 여자가 나머지 온갖 것을 갠 대로 개어져, 선생님의 서랍에 안전히 있었습니다."], ["There was no fear (if the linen in the house was examined) of the newness of the nightgown betraying me.", "(그 집의 아마포가 검사되더라도) 그 잠옷의 새것 티가 저를 배신할 두려움은 없었습니다."], ["All your underclothing had been renewed, when you came to our house—I suppose on your return home from foreign parts.", "선생님의 온갖 속옷은, 선생님이 우리 집에 오셨을 때 죄 새것으로 바꾸어진 터였습니다—짐작건대 외국 땅에서 귀국하시면서요."]], [["“The next thing was the arrival of Sergeant Cuff; and the next great surprise was the announcement of what he thought about the smear on the door.", "\"다음 일은 커프 부장의 도착이었습니다. 그리고 다음의 큰 놀라움은 그 문의 얼룩에 대해 그가 어찌 생각하는지의 발표였습니다."]], [["“I had believed you to be guilty (as I have owned), more because I wanted you to be guilty than for any other reason.", "\"저는 (털어놓았듯이) 선생님이 죄지으셨다고 믿었는데, 다른 어떤 이유에서라기보다 제가 선생님이 죄지으셨기를 바랐기 때문이었습니다."], ["And now, the Sergeant had come round by a totally different way to the same conclusion (respecting the nightgown) as mine!", "그런데 이제, 그 부장이 전혀 다른 길을 돌아, (그 잠옷에 관하여) 저와 같은 결론에 이른 것이었습니다!"], ["And I had got the dress that was the only proof against you!", "그리고 저는 선생님에게 불리한 유일한 증거인 그 옷을 가지고 있었습니다!"], ["And not a living creature knew it—yourself included!", "그리고 살아 있는 어떤 존재도—선생님 자신을 포함해—그것을 알지 못했습니다!"], ["I am afraid to tell you how I felt when I called these things to mind—you would hate my memory for ever afterwards.”", "이것들을 떠올렸을 때 제가 어떤 심정이었는지 선생님께 말씀드리기가 두렵습니다—선생님은 그 뒤로 영영 저의 넋을 미워하실 테니까요.\""]], [["At that place, Betteredge looked up from the letter.", "그 대목에서, 베터리지는 그 편지에서 눈을 들었다."]], [["“Not a glimmer of light so far, Mr. Franklin,”", "\"여기까지는 빛 한 줄기 없습니다요, 프랭클린 나리,\""], ["said the old man, taking off his heavy tortoiseshell spectacles, and pushing Rosanna Spearman’s confession a little away from him.", "하고 그 노인은, 그의 두툼한 대모갑 안경을 벗고, 로재나 스피어먼의 고백을 제게서 조금 밀어 놓으며 말했다."], ["“Have you come to any conclusion, sir, in your own mind, while I have been reading?”", "\"제가 읽는 동안, 나리는 나리 마음속으로 어떤 결론에 이르셨습니까, 나리?\""]], [["“Finish the letter first, Betteredge; there may be something to enlighten us at the end of it.", "\"먼저 그 편지를 끝내게, 베터리지. 그 끝에 우리를 밝혀 줄 무언가가 있을지도 모르네."], ["I shall have a word or two to say to you after that.”", "그다음에 자네에게 한두 마디 할 말이 있겠네.\""]], [["“Very good, sir.", "\"알겠습니다, 나리."], ["I’ll just rest my eyes, and then I’ll go on again.", "잠깐 눈을 좀 쉬고, 그런 다음 다시 나아가겠습니다."], ["In the meantime, Mr. Franklin—I don’t want to hurry you—but would you mind telling me, in one word, whether you see your way out of this dreadful mess yet?”", "그동안, 프랭클린 나리—나리를 재촉하고 싶지는 않습니다만—한마디로, 나리께서 이 끔찍한 난장판을 빠져나갈 길을 아직 보시는지 저에게 말씀해 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“I see my way back to London,”", "\"나는 런던으로 돌아갈 길이 보이네.\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said, “to consult Mr.", "\"브러프 씨와 상의하러 말일세."], ["Bruff. If he can’t help me——”", "만일 그가 나를 돕지 못한다면——\""]], [["“Yes, sir?”", "\"예, 나리?\""]], [["“And if the Sergeant won’t leave his retirement at Dorking——”", "\"그리고 만일 그 부장이 도킹의 은거를 떠나려 하지 않는다면——\""]], [["“He won’t, Mr. Franklin!”", "\"떠나지 않을 겁니다, 프랭클린 나리!\""]], [["“Then, Betteredge—as far as I can see now—I am at the end of my resources.", "\"그렇다면, 베터리지—지금 내가 보는 한으로는—나는 나의 방책이 다한 것일세."], ["After Mr. Bruff and the Sergeant, I don’t know of a living creature who can be of the slightest use to me.”", "브러프 씨와 그 부장 다음으로는, 나는 나에게 조금이라도 쓸모가 있을 만한 살아 있는 존재를 알지 못하네.\""]], [["As the words passed my lips, some person outside knocked at the door of the room.", "그 말이 내 입술을 스칠 때, 밖에서 누군가가 그 방의 문을 두드렸다."]], [["Betteredge looked surprised as well as annoyed by the interruption.", "베터리지는 그 방해에 짜증이 나기도 하고 놀라기도 한 낯빛이었다."]], [["“Come in,”", "\"들어오시오,\""], ["he called out, irritably, “whoever you are!”", "하고 그가 짜증스레 소리쳤다. \"누구시든 간에!\""]], [["The door opened, and there entered to us, quietly, the most remarkable-looking man that I had ever seen.", "문이 열리고, 우리에게로, 조용히, 내가 여태 본 가장 눈에 띄는 생김새의 사내가 들어왔다."], ["Judging him by his figure and his movements, he was still young.", "그의 몸매와 그의 몸놀림으로 판단하면, 그는 아직 젊었다."], ["Judging him by his face, and comparing him with Betteredge, he looked the elder of the two.", "그의 얼굴로 판단하고, 그를 베터리지와 견주면, 그는 둘 중 더 나이 든 쪽으로 보였다."], ["His complexion was of a gipsy darkness; his fleshless cheeks had fallen into deep hollows, over which the bone projected like a penthouse.", "그의 낯빛은 집시처럼 거무스름했다. 그의 살 없는 두 뺨은 깊은 우묵함으로 꺼져 있었고, 그 위로 뼈가 차양처럼 튀어나와 있었다."], ["His nose presented the fine shape and modelling so often found among the ancient people of the East, so seldom visible among the newer races of the West.", "그의 코는 동방의 옛 종족들 사이에서는 그토록 자주 보이나, 서방의 더 새로운 인종들 사이에서는 그토록 드물게 보이는, 그 고운 모양새와 조형을 지니고 있었다."], ["His forehead rose high and straight from the brow.", "그의 이마는 눈썹에서부터 높고 곧게 솟아 있었다."], ["His marks and wrinkles were innumerable.", "그의 반점과 주름은 헤아릴 수 없이 많았다."], ["From this strange face, eyes, stranger still, of the softest brown—eyes dreamy and mournful, and deeply sunk in their orbits—looked out at you, and (in my case, at least) took your attention captive at their will.", "이 이상한 얼굴에서, 더욱더 이상한, 더없이 부드러운 갈색 눈이—꿈꾸는 듯하고 애처로운, 그 안와에 깊이 꺼진 눈이—당신을 내다보며, (적어도 나의 경우에는) 제멋대로 당신의 주의를 사로잡았다."], ["Add to this a quantity of thick closely-curling hair, which, by some freak of Nature, had lost its colour in the most startlingly partial and capricious manner.", "여기에다, 자연의 어떤 변덕으로, 더없이 놀랍도록 얼룩덜룩하고 제멋대로인 방식으로 그 빛깔을 잃어버린, 빽빽하고 촘촘히 곱슬거리는 숱 많은 머리칼을 더하라."], ["Over the top of his head it was still of the deep black which was its natural colour.", "그의 머리 꼭대기 위로는 그것이 여전히, 그 본래의 빛깔인 짙은 검정이었다."], ["Round the sides of his head—without the slightest gradation of grey to break the force of the extraordinary contrast—it had turned completely white.", "그의 머리 옆으로 빙 둘러서는—그 놀라운 대비의 기세를 누그러뜨릴 회색의 옅은 층 하나 없이—그것이 완전히 하얗게 세어 있었다."], ["The line between the two colours preserved no sort of regularity.", "그 두 빛깔 사이의 경계는 아무런 규칙도 지키지 않았다."], ["At one place, the white hair ran up into the black; at another, the black hair ran down into the white.", "어느 곳에서는 흰 머리칼이 검은 머리칼 속으로 치달아 올랐고, 다른 곳에서는 검은 머리칼이 흰 머리칼 속으로 흘러내렸다."], ["I looked at the man with a curiosity which, I am ashamed to say, I found it quite impossible to control.", "나는, 부끄럽지만 도무지 억누를 수 없다고 느낀 호기심을 안고, 그 사내를 바라보았다."], ["His soft brown eyes looked back at me gently; and he met my involuntary rudeness in staring at him, with an apology which I was conscious that I had not deserved.", "그의 부드러운 갈색 눈이 다정하게 나를 마주 보았다. 그리고 그는, 그를 빤히 쳐다보는 나의 무심결의 무례함을, 내가 받을 자격이 없다고 자각한 사과로써 맞이했다."]], [["“I beg your pardon,”", "\"용서하십시오,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I had no idea that Mr. Betteredge was engaged.”", "\"베터리지 씨에게 손님이 계신 줄 몰랐습니다.\""], ["He took a slip of paper from his pocket, and handed it to Betteredge.", "그는 주머니에서 종이쪽지 하나를 꺼내어, 베터리지에게 건넸다. \"다음 주 목록입니다,\""], ["“The list for next week,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. His eyes just rested on me again—and he left the room as quietly as he had entered it.", "그의 눈이 다시 나에게 잠시 머물렀다—그러고는 그는 그가 들어온 것과 똑같이 조용히 방을 나갔다."]], [["“Who is that?”", "\"저 사람은 누구인가?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Mr. Candy’s assistant,”", "\"캔디 씨의 조수입지요,\""], ["said Betteredge.", "하고 베터리지가 말했다."], ["“By-the-bye, Mr. Franklin, you will be sorry to hear that the little doctor has never recovered that illness he caught, going home from the birthday dinner.", "\"그러고 보니, 프랭클린 나리, 나리께서 들으시면 안타까우실 텐데, 그 자그마한 의사 선생은 생일 만찬에서 돌아가는 길에 걸린 그 병에서 끝내 회복하지 못했습니다."], ["He’s pretty well in health; but he lost his memory in the fever, and he has never recovered more than the wreck of it since.", "건강은 제법 괜찮습니다요. 허나 그는 그 열병에 기억을 잃었고, 그 뒤로 그것의 잔해 이상은 끝내 되찾지 못했습니다."], ["The work all falls on his assistant.", "일이 죄 그의 조수에게 떨어집지요."], ["Not much of it now, except among the poor.", "지금은 그리 많지도 않습니다, 가난한 사람들 사이에서 말고는요."], ["They can’t help themselves, you know.", "그들은, 아시다시피, 어쩔 도리가 없지요."], ["They must put up with the man with the piebald hair, and the gipsy complexion—or they would get no doctoring at all.”", "그들은 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼과 집시 같은 낯빛의 사내로 참을 수밖에 없습니다—그러지 않으면 진료를 아예 못 받을 테니까요.\""]], [["“You don’t seem to like him, Betteredge?”", "\"자네는 그를 좋아하지 않는 듯하군, 베터리지?\""]], [["“Nobody likes him, sir.”", "\"아무도 그를 좋아하지 않습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“Why is he so unpopular?”", "\"어째서 그가 그리 인기가 없는가?\""]], [["“Well, Mr. Franklin, his appearance is against him, to begin with.", "\"글쎄요, 프랭클린 나리, 우선, 그의 생김새가 그에게 불리합니다."], ["And then there’s a story that Mr. Candy took him with a very doubtful character.", "그리고 캔디 씨가 그를 아주 미심쩍은 신원으로 받아들였다는 소문이 있습지요."], ["Nobody knows who he is—and he hasn’t a friend in the place.", "아무도 그가 누구인지 모릅니다—그리고 그는 이곳에 벗 하나 없습니다."], ["How can you expect one to like him, after that?”", "그러고서야, 어찌 누가 그를 좋아하기를 바라겠습니까?\""]], [["“Quite impossible, of course!", "\"물론, 아주 불가능하지!"], ["May I ask what he wanted with you, when he gave you that bit of paper?”", "그가 자네에게 그 종잇조각을 줄 때, 자네에게 무슨 볼일이 있었는지 물어봐도 되겠는가?\""]], [["“Only to bring me the weekly list of the sick people about here, sir, who stand in need of a little wine.", "\"그저 이 근방의, 포도주가 좀 필요한 병자들의 주간 목록을 저에게 가져다주는 것뿐입니다, 나리."], ["My lady always had a regular distribution of good sound port and sherry among the infirm poor; and Miss Rachel wishes the custom to be kept up.", "마님께서는 늘 병약한 가난한 이들에게 좋고 실한 포트와 셰리를 정기적으로 나눠 주셨습니다. 그리고 레이철 아가씨는 그 관례가 이어지기를 바라십니다."], ["Times have changed!", "세월이 변했습니다!"], ["times have changed!", "세월이 변했어요!"], ["I remember when Mr. Candy himself brought the list to my mistress.", "저는 캔디 씨가 몸소 그 목록을 저의 여주인께 가져오던 때를 기억합니다."], ["Now it’s Mr. Candy’s assistant who brings the list to me.", "이제는 캔디 씨의 조수가 그 목록을 저에게 가져옵니다."], ["I’ll go on with the letter, if you will allow me, sir,”", "허락하신다면, 그 편지를 이어 나가겠습니다, 나리,\""], ["said Betteredge, drawing Rosanna Spearman’s confession back to him.", "하고 베터리지는 로재나 스피어먼의 고백을 제게로 도로 끌어당기며 말했다."], ["“It isn’t lively reading, I grant you.", "\"명랑한 읽을거리가 아닌 것은 인정합니다요."], ["But, there!", "허나, 원 참!"], ["it keeps me from getting sour with thinking of the past.”", "그것이 저를 지난 일 생각으로 심사가 뒤틀리지 않게 지켜 줍니다.\""], ["He put on his spectacles, and wagged his head gloomily.", "그는 안경을 쓰고, 침울하게 고개를 저었다."], ["“There’s a bottom of good sense, Mr. Franklin, in our conduct to our mothers, when they first start us on the journey of life.", "\"우리가 태어날 때, 우리를 처음으로 삶의 여정에 내보내는 우리 어머니들을 향한 우리의 처신에는, 프랭클린 나리, 밑바탕에 깔린 지혜가 있습지요."], ["We are all of us more or less unwilling to be brought into the world.", "우리는 죄, 다소간 이 세상에 나오기를 꺼립니다."], ["And we are all of us right.”", "그리고 우리는 죄 옳습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Candy’s assistant had produced too strong an impression on me to be immediately dismissed from my thoughts.", "캔디 씨의 조수는 나에게 너무나 강한 인상을 남겨, 당장 내 생각에서 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다."], ["I passed over the last unanswerable utterance of the Betteredge philosophy; and returned to the subject of the man with the piebald hair.", "나는 베터리지 철학의 그 마지막 반박할 수 없는 발언을 지나쳐 흘려버리고, 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼의 사내라는 화제로 돌아갔다."]], [["“What is his name?”", "\"그의 이름은 무엇인가?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“As ugly a name as need be,”", "\"더없이 흉한 이름입지요,\""], ["Betteredge answered gruffly.", "하고 베터리지가 퉁명스레 대답했다."], ["“Ezra Jennings.”", "\"에즈라 제닝스입니다.\""]], [["# CHAPTER V", "# CHAPTER V"]], [["Having told me the name of Mr. Candy’s assistant, Betteredge appeared to think that we had wasted enough of our time on an insignificant subject.", "캔디 씨의 조수의 이름을 나에게 말해 주고서, 베터리지는 우리가 하찮은 화제에 시간을 넉넉히 허비했다고 여기는 듯했다."], ["He resumed the perusal of Rosanna Spearman’s letter.", "그는 로재나 스피어먼의 편지 읽기를 다시 이어 갔다."]], [["On my side, I sat at the window, waiting until he had done.", "내 편에서, 나는 그가 다 읽기를 기다리며 창가에 앉아 있었다."], ["Little by little, the impression produced on me by Ezra Jennings—it seemed perfectly unaccountable, in such a situation as mine, that any human being should have produced an impression on me at all!—faded from my mind.", "조금씩, 에즈라 제닝스가 나에게 남긴 그 인상은—나 같은 처지에서, 어떤 인간 존재가 대체 나에게 인상을 남겼다는 것 자체가 도무지 설명할 수 없는 일로 보였다!—내 마음에서 바래져 갔다."], ["My thoughts flowed back into their former channel.", "나의 생각은 도로 그 이전의 물길로 흘러들었다."], ["Once more, I forced myself to look my own incredible position resolutely in the face.", "다시 한 번, 나는 나 자신의 그 믿기 어려운 처지를 단호히 똑바로 마주하도록 나를 다잡았다."], ["Once more, I reviewed in my own mind the course which I had at last summoned composure enough to plan out for the future.", "다시 한 번, 나는 마침내 미래를 위해 짜낼 만큼의 침착함을 그러모아 세운 그 방책을 내 마음속으로 되짚었다."]], [["To go back to London that day; to put the whole case before Mr. Bruff; and, last and most important, to obtain (no matter by what means or at what sacrifice) a personal interview with Rachel—this was my plan of action, so far as I was capable of forming it at the time.", "그날 런던으로 돌아가는 것, 그 온 사건을 브러프 씨 앞에 펼쳐 놓는 것, 그리고, 마지막이자 가장 중요하게, (어떤 수단으로든 어떤 희생을 치르든) 레이철과의 직접 면담을 얻어 내는 것—이것이, 그때 내가 짤 수 있는 한으로는, 나의 행동 방책이었다."], ["There was more than an hour still to spare before the train started.", "기차가 떠나기까지는 아직 한 시간 넘게 남아 있었다."], ["And there was the bare chance that Betteredge might discover something in the unread portion of Rosanna Spearman’s letter, which it might be useful for me to know before I left the house in which the Diamond had been lost.", "그리고 내가 그 다이아몬드가 없어졌던 집을 떠나기 전에 알아 두면 쓸모가 있을 만한 무언가를, 베터리지가 로재나 스피어먼의 편지의 아직 안 읽은 부분에서 찾아낼지도 모를 실낱같은 요행이 있었다."], ["For that chance I was now waiting.", "그 요행을 나는 이제 기다리고 있었다."]], [["The letter ended in these terms:", "그 편지는 이런 말로 끝났다."]], [["“You have no need to be angry, Mr. Franklin, even if I did feel some little triumph at knowing that I held all your prospects in life in my own hands.", "\"화내실 것 없습니다, 프랭클린 선생님, 제가 선생님의 온 앞날을 제 손에 쥐고 있음을 아는 데에서 약간의 승리감을 얼마쯤 느꼈다 하더라도요."], ["Anxieties and fears soon came back to me.", "근심과 두려움이 이내 저에게 돌아왔습니다."], ["With the view Sergeant Cuff took of the loss of the Diamond, he would be sure to end in examining our linen and our dresses.", "커프 부장이 그 다이아몬드의 분실을 보는 그 시각으로 보아, 그는 틀림없이 결국 우리의 아마포와 우리의 옷을 검사하게 될 터였습니다."], ["There was no place in my room—there was no place in the house—which I could feel satisfied would be safe from him.", "제 방에는—그 집에는—그로부터 안전하리라고 제가 흡족히 여길 만한 곳이 없었습니다."], ["How to hide the nightgown so that not even the Sergeant could find it?", "그 부장조차 찾아내지 못하도록 그 잠옷을 어떻게 감출까?"], ["and how to do that without losing one moment of precious time?—these were not easy questions to answer.", "그리고 그것을, 그 소중한 시간을 한 순간도 잃지 않고서 어떻게 할까?—이것들은 답하기 쉬운 물음이 아니었습니다."], ["My uncertainties ended in my taking a way that may make you laugh.", "저의 갈팡질팡은, 선생님을 웃게 만들지도 모를 방도를 취하는 것으로 끝났습니다."], ["I undressed, and put the nightgown on me.", "저는 옷을 벗고, 그 잠옷을 제 몸에 걸쳤습니다."], ["You had worn it—and I had another little moment of pleasure in wearing it after you.", "선생님이 그것을 입으셨었지요—그리고 저는 선생님 뒤에 그것을 입는 데에서 또 한 번 자그마한 기쁨의 순간을 누렸습니다."]], [["“The next news that reached us in the servants’ hall showed that I had not made sure of the nightgown a moment too soon.", "\"하인 방에 다음으로 우리에게 다다른 소식은, 제가 그 잠옷을 챙긴 것이 한순간도 이르지 않았음을 보여 주었습니다."], ["Sergeant Cuff wanted to see the washing-book.", "커프 부장이 세탁 장부를 보고 싶어 했던 것입니다."]], [["“I found it, and took it to him in my lady’s sitting-room.", "\"저는 그것을 찾아, 마님의 응접실에 있는 그에게 가져갔습니다."], ["The Sergeant and I had come across each other more than once in former days.", "그 부장과 저는 지난날 한 번 넘게 서로 마주친 적이 있었습니다."], ["I was certain he would know me again—and I was not certain of what he might do when he found me employed as servant in a house in which a valuable jewel had been lost.", "저는 그가 저를 다시 알아보리라 확신했습니다—그리고 저는 그가, 값진 보석이 없어진 집에 제가 하녀로 일하고 있는 것을 알았을 때 무엇을 할지 확신할 수 없었습니다."], ["In this suspense, I felt it would be a relief to me to get the meeting between us over, and to know the worst of it at once.", "이 마음 졸임 속에서, 저는 우리 사이의 만남을 끝내 버리고, 그 최악의 것을 대뜸 아는 것이 저에게 차라리 안도가 되리라 느꼈습니다."]], [["“He looked at me as if I was a stranger, when I handed him the washing-book; and he was very specially polite in thanking me for bringing it.", "\"제가 그에게 세탁 장부를 건넸을 때, 그는 마치 제가 낯선 사람이기라도 한 양 저를 보았습니다. 그리고 그는 그것을 가져다준 것에 대해 저에게 각별히 아주 정중하게 고마워했습니다."], ["I thought those were both bad signs.", "저는 그 둘 다 나쁜 징조라고 생각했습니다."], ["There was no knowing what he might say of me behind my back; there was no knowing how soon I might not find myself taken in custody on suspicion, and searched.", "그가 제 등 뒤에서 저에 대해 무슨 말을 할지 알 수 없었습니다. 제가 얼마나 곧 의심을 받아 붙들려, 몸수색을 당하게 될지 알 수 없었습니다."], ["It was then time for your return from seeing Mr.", "그때는 선생님이 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 철도역까지 배웅하고 돌아오실 무렵이었습니다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite off by the railway; and I went to your favourite walk in the shrubbery, to try for another chance of speaking to you—the last chance, for all I knew to the contrary, that I might have.", "그래서 저는 선생님께 말을 걸 또 한 번의 기회를—제가 아는 한, 제가 가질 수 있는 마지막 기회를 노리고, 선생님이 즐겨 거니시는 관목숲으로 갔습니다."]], [["“You never appeared; and, what was worse still, Mr.", "\"선생님은 끝내 나타나지 않으셨습니다."], ["Betteredge and Sergeant Cuff passed by the place where I was hiding—and the Sergeant saw me.", "그리고, 더 나쁘게도, 베터리지 씨와 커프 부장이 제가 숨어 있던 그곳을 지나갔습니다—그리고 그 부장이 저를 보았습니다."]], [["“I had no choice, after that, but to return to my proper place and my proper work, before more disasters happened to me.", "\"그 뒤로, 저에게 더 많은 재앙이 닥치기 전에, 저는 제 마땅한 자리와 제 마땅한 일로 돌아가는 것 말고는 다른 도리가 없었습니다."], ["Just as I was going to step across the path, you came back from the railway.", "제가 막 그 오솔길을 가로질러 발을 내디디려는 참에, 선생님이 철도역에서 돌아오셨습니다."], ["You were making straight for the shrubbery, when you saw me—I am certain, sir, you saw me—and you turned away as if I had got the plague, and went into the house.*", "선생님은 곧장 그 관목숲으로 향하시다가, 저를 보셨습니다—틀림없이, 선생님, 선생님은 저를 보셨습니다—그리고 선생님은 마치 제가 흑사병에라도 걸린 양 몸을 돌리시어, 그 집으로 들어가셨습니다.*"]], [["* NOTE; by Franklin Blake.—The writer is entirely mistaken, poor creature.", "* 주(註); 프랭클린 블레이크가 붙임.—글쓴이는 온전히 잘못 알고 있다, 가엾은 사람이."], ["I never noticed her.", "나는 결코 그녀를 알아채지 못했다."], ["My intention was certainly to have taken a turn in the shrubbery.", "나의 뜻은 정녕 그 관목숲을 한 바퀴 거닐려는 것이었다."], ["But, remembering at the same moment that my aunt might wish to see me, after my return from the railway, I altered my mind, and went into the house.", "허나 그와 동시에, 철도역에서 돌아온 뒤 이모가 나를 보고 싶어 할지도 모른다는 것이 떠올라, 나는 마음을 바꾸어, 그 집으로 들어갔다."]], [["“I made the best of my way indoors again, returning by the servants’ entrance.", "\"저는 하인용 출입구로 돌아, 다시 서둘러 안으로 들어갔습니다."], ["There was nobody in the laundry-room at that time; and I sat down there alone.", "그때 세탁방에는 아무도 없었습니다. 그래서 저는 거기 홀로 앉았습니다."], ["I have told you already of the thoughts which the Shivering Sand put into my head.", "흔들리는 모래가 제 머릿속에 넣어 준 그 생각들을 저는 이미 선생님께 말씀드렸습니다."], ["Those thoughts came back to me now.", "그 생각들이 이제 저에게 돌아왔습니다."], ["I wondered in myself which it would be harder to do, if things went on in this manner—to bear Mr. Franklin Blake’s indifference to me, or to jump into the quicksand and end it for ever in that way?", "저는 속으로, 일이 이런 식으로 계속된다면—프랭클린 블레이크 나리의 저에 대한 무심함을 견디는 것과, 그 늪 모래로 뛰어들어 그런 식으로 그것을 영영 끝내는 것 가운데 어느 쪽이 하기 더 어려울까 궁금해했습니다."]], [["“It’s useless to ask me to account for my own conduct, at this time.", "\"이때의 제 처신을 해명하라고 저에게 청해 봐야 헛일입니다."], ["I try—and I can’t understand it myself.", "저는 애써 봅니다—그리고 저 자신도 그것을 이해할 수 없습니다."]], [["“Why didn’t I stop you, when you avoided me in that cruel manner? Why didn’t I call out, ‘Mr.", "\"선생님이 그 잔인한 태도로 저를 피하셨을 때, 어째서 저는 선생님을 멈춰 세우지 않았을까요?"], ["Franklin, I have got something to say to you; it concerns yourself, and you must, and shall, hear it?’ You were at my mercy—I had got the whip-hand of you, as they say.", "어째서 저는 '프랭클린 나리, 저는 나리께 드릴 말씀이 있어요. 그것은 나리 자신에 관한 것이니, 나리는 그것을 들으셔야 하고, 또 들으실 거예요'"], ["And better than that, I had the means (if I could only make you trust me) of being useful to you in the future.", "하고 외치지 않았을까요? 선생님은 제 손아귀에 있었습니다—흔히 하는 말로, 저는 선생님을 쥐고 있었습니다."], ["Of course, I never supposed that you—a gentleman—had stolen the Diamond for the mere pleasure of stealing it.", "그리고 그보다 나은 것으로, 저는 (제가 선생님을 저를 믿게 만들 수만 있다면) 앞으로 선생님께 쓸모가 될 수단을 지니고 있었습니다."], ["No. Penelope had heard Miss Rachel, and I had heard Mr.", "물론, 저는 선생님이—신사이신 선생님이—그저 훔치는 재미로 그 다이아몬드를 훔치셨다고는 결코 여기지 않았습니다."], ["Betteredge, talk about your extravagance and your debts.", "아니요. 페넬로피는 레이철 아가씨가, 저는 베터리지 씨가, 선생님의 낭비와 선생님의 빚에 대해 이야기하는 것을 들었습니다."], ["It was plain enough to me that you had taken the Diamond to sell it, or pledge it, and so to get the money of which you stood in need.", "선생님이 그 다이아몬드를 팔거나 저당 잡혀, 그리하여 선생님이 필요로 하시는 돈을 마련하려고 그것을 가져가셨다는 것은 저에게 아주 뚜렷했습니다. 그렇지요!"], ["Well! I could have told you of a man in London who would have advanced a good large sum on the jewel, and who would have asked no awkward questions about it either.", "저는 선생님께, 그 보석에 상당히 큰 금액을 내줄, 그리고 그것에 대해 거북한 물음도 하지 않을, 런던의 한 사내를 일러 드릴 수 있었을 것입니다."]], [["“Why didn’t I speak to you!", "\"어째서 저는 선생님께 말하지 않았을까요!"], ["why didn’t I speak to you!", "어째서 저는 선생님께 말하지 않았을까요!"]], [["“I wonder whether the risks and difficulties of keeping the nightgown were as much as I could manage, without having other risks and difficulties added to them?", "\"그 잠옷을 간직하는 위험과 어려움이, 다른 위험과 어려움이 거기 더해지는 것을 감당할 수 없을 만큼 제가 겨우 감당할 수 있는 것이었을까 궁금합니다."], ["This might have been the case with some women—but how could it be the case with me?", "어떤 여자들에게는 이것이 사실이었을지도 모릅니다—허나 어떻게 그것이 저에게 사실일 수 있겠습니까?"], ["In the days when I was a thief, I had run fifty times greater risks, and found my way out of difficulties to which this difficulty was mere child’s play.", "제가 도둑이던 시절에, 저는 쉰 배는 더 큰 위험을 무릅썼고, 이 어려움 따위는 한낱 애들 장난인 그런 어려움에서 빠져나오는 길을 찾았습니다."], ["I had been apprenticed, as you may say, to frauds and deceptions—some of them on such a grand scale, and managed so cleverly, that they became famous, and appeared in the newspapers.", "저는, 이를테면, 사기와 속임수의 도제 노릇을 했습니다—그중 어떤 것은 어찌나 큰 규모로, 어찌나 교묘하게 부려졌던지, 이름이 나서 신문에까지 실렸습니다."], ["Was such a little thing as the keeping of the nightgown likely to weigh on my spirits, and to set my heart sinking within me, at the time when I ought to have spoken to you?", "그 잠옷을 간직하는 것 같은 그런 자잘한 일이, 제가 선생님께 말했어야 할 그때에, 제 마음을 짓누르고 제 가슴을 제 안에서 내려앉게 할 만한 것이었을까요?"], ["What nonsense to ask the question!", "그런 물음을 하다니 얼마나 어이없는 일입니까!"], ["The thing couldn’t be.", "그럴 리 없었습니다."]], [["“Where is the use of my dwelling in this way on my own folly?", "\"제 자신의 어리석음을 이런 식으로 곱씹은들 무슨 소용이 있겠습니까?"], ["The plain truth is plain enough, surely?", "분명한 진실은, 정말이지, 충분히 분명하지 않은가요?"], ["Behind your back, I loved you with all my heart and soul.", "선생님의 등 뒤에서, 저는 온 마음과 온 넋으로 선생님을 사랑했습니다. 선생님의 얼굴 앞에서는—그것은 부인할 수 없습니다—저는 선생님이 무서웠습니다."], ["Before your face—there’s no denying it—I was frightened of you; frightened of making you angry with me; frightened of what you might say to me (though you had taken the Diamond) if I presumed to tell you that I had found it out.", "선생님을 저에게 화나시게 할까 무서웠습니다. 제가 감히 그것을 알아냈다고 선생님께 말씀드린다면 선생님이 저에게 무슨 말씀을 하실지 (선생님이 그 다이아몬드를 가져가셨는데도) 무서웠습니다."], ["I had gone as near to it as I dared when I spoke to you in the library.", "저는 서재에서 선생님께 말했을 때 감히 그럴 수 있는 만큼 그것에 가까이 갔었습니다."], ["You had not turned your back on me then.", "그때는 선생님이 저에게 등을 돌리지 않으셨습니다."], ["You had not started away from me as if I had got the plague.", "그때는 선생님이 마치 제가 흑사병에 걸린 양 저에게서 소스라쳐 물러나지 않으셨습니다."], ["I tried to provoke myself into feeling angry with you, and to rouse up my courage in that way.", "저는 선생님께 화가 나도록 저를 부추기고, 그런 식으로 제 용기를 일으켜 세우려 애썼습니다."], ["No!", "아니요!"], ["I couldn’t feel anything but the misery and the mortification of it.", "저는 그것의 비참과 굴욕밖에는 아무것도 느낄 수 없었습니다. '너는 못난 계집이야."], ["‘You’re a plain girl; you have got a crooked shoulder; you’re only a housemaid—what do you mean by attempting to speak to Me?’ You never uttered a word of that, Mr.", "너는 어깨가 굽었어. 너는 한낱 하녀일 뿐이야—무슨 낯으로 감히 나에게 말을 걸려 드는 게냐?'"], ["Franklin; but you said it all to me, nevertheless!", "선생님은 그런 말을 한마디도 입에 담지 않으셨습니다, 프랭클린 나리. 허나 선생님은 그럼에도 그 모든 것을 저에게 말씀하셨습니다!"], ["Is such madness as this to be accounted for?", "이런 미친 짓이 해명될 수 있는 것일까요? 아니요."], ["No. There is nothing to be done but to confess it, and let it be.", "그것을 고백하고, 그대로 두는 것 말고는 달리 할 것이 없습니다."]], [["“I ask your pardon, once more, for this wandering of my pen.", "\"제 펜의 이 헤맴에 대해, 다시 한 번 선생님의 용서를 구합니다."], ["There is no fear of its happening again.", "그것이 다시 일어날 두려움은 없습니다."], ["I am close at the end now.", "저는 이제 끝에 다다랐습니다."]], [["“The first person who disturbed me by coming into the empty room was Penelope.", "\"그 빈방에 들어와 저를 방해한 첫 사람은 페넬로피였습니다."], ["She had found out my secret long since, and she had done her best to bring me to my senses—and done it kindly too.", "그녀는 오래전에 제 비밀을 알아챘고, 저를 제정신으로 돌려놓으려 애를 다했습니다—그것도 다정하게 그리했습니다."]], [["“‘Ah!’ she said, ‘I know why you’re sitting here, and fretting, all by yourself.", "\"'아!' 하고 그녀가 말했습니다."], ["The best thing that can happen for your advantage, Rosanna, will be for Mr.", "'나는 네가 왜 여기 혼자 앉아, 애태우는지 알아."], ["Franklin’s visit here to come to an end.", "로재나, 너에게 가장 좋은 일은 프랭클린 나리의 이곳 방문이 끝나는 걸 거야."], ["It’s my belief that he won’t be long now before he leaves the house.”", "내 믿기로는 그분이 이제 곧 그 집을 떠나실 거야.'"]], [["“In all my thoughts of you I had never thought of your going away.", "\"선생님에 대한 저의 온갖 생각 중에서 저는 선생님이 떠나신다는 것을 한 번도 생각해 본 적이 없었습니다."], ["I couldn’t speak to Penelope.", "저는 페넬로피에게 말을 할 수 없었습니다."], ["I could only look at her.", "저는 그저 그녀를 바라볼 수밖에 없었습니다."]], [["“‘I’ve just left Miss Rachel,’ Penelope went on.", "\"'나는 방금 레이철 아가씨 곁을 떠나왔어,'"], ["‘And a hard matter I have had of it to put up with her temper.", "하고 페넬로피가 말을 이었습니다. '그런데 그분 성미를 견디느라 참 애를 먹었지."], ["She says the house is unbearable to her with the police in it; and she’s determined to speak to my lady this evening, and to go to her Aunt Ablewhite tomorrow.", "그분은 경찰이 든 이 집이 견딜 수 없다고 하셔. 그리고 그분은 오늘 저녁 마님께 말씀드리고, 내일 에이블화이트 이모님께 가시기로 마음을 굳히셨어."], ["If she does that, Mr. Franklin will be the next to find a reason for going away, you may depend on it!’", "그분이 그리하시면, 프랭클린 나리가 다음으로 떠날 이유를 찾으실 거야, 그건 틀림없어!'"]], [["“I recovered the use of my tongue at that.", "\"저는 그 말에 다시 혀를 놀릴 수 있게 되었습니다."], ["‘Do you mean to say Mr.", "'프랭클린 나리가 그분과 함께 가신다는 말이야?'"], ["Franklin will go with her?’ I asked.", "하고 저는 물었습니다."]], [["“‘Only too gladly, if she would let him; but she won’t.", "\"'그분이 허락만 하시면야 더없이 기꺼이 그러시겠지. 허나 그분은 허락 안 하실 거야."], ["He has been made to feel her temper; he is in her black books too—and that after having done all he can to help her, poor fellow!", "그분도 그분의 성미를 겪으셨어. 그분도 그분의 미움을 사고 계셔—그것도 그분을 도우려 할 수 있는 걸 다 하시고서 말이야, 가엾은 분!"], ["No! no!", "아니!"], ["If they don’t make it up before tomorrow, you will see Miss Rachel go one way, and Mr.", "아니야! 그분들이 내일 전에 화해하지 않으시면, 너는 레이철 아가씨가 이 길로 가시고, 프랭클린 나리가 저 길로 가시는 걸 보게 될 거야."], ["Franklin another. Where he may betake himself to I can’t say.", "그분이 어디로 몸을 두실지 나는 말할 수 없어."], ["But he will never stay here, Rosanna, after Miss Rachel has left us.’", "허나 레이철 아가씨가 우리를 떠나신 뒤에, 그분은 결코 여기 머무르지 않으실 거야, 로재나.'"]], [["“I managed to master the despair I felt at the prospect of your going away.", "\"저는 선생님이 떠나신다는 전망에 제가 느낀 절망을 가까스로 억눌렀습니다."], ["To own the truth, I saw a little glimpse of hope for myself if there was really a serious disagreement between Miss Rachel and you.", "진실을 털어놓자면, 만일 레이철 아가씨와 선생님 사이에 정말로 심각한 불화가 있다면, 저는 저 자신을 위한 희망의 실낱같은 빛을 보았습니다. '너는,'"], ["‘Do you know,’ I asked, ‘what the quarrel is between them?’", "하고 저는 물었습니다. '그분들 사이의 다툼이 무엇인지 아니?'"]], [["“‘It is all on Miss Rachel’s side,’ Penelope said.", "\"'그건 죄 레이철 아가씨 쪽에서 비롯된 거야,'"], ["‘And, for anything I know to the contrary, it’s all Miss Rachel’s temper, and nothing else.", "하고 페넬로피가 말했습니다. '그리고, 내가 달리 아는 게 없는 한, 그건 죄 레이철 아가씨 성미일 뿐, 다른 아무것도 아니야."], ["I am loth to distress you, Rosanna; but don’t run away with the notion that Mr.", "나는 너를 괴롭게 하고 싶지 않아, 로재나."], ["Franklin is ever likely to quarrel with her.", "허나 프랭클린 나리가 언젠가 그분과 다투실 것 같다는 생각일랑 품지 마."], ["He’s a great deal too fond of her for that!’", "그러기엔 그분이 그분을 너무나도 좋아하시니까!'"]], [["“She had only just spoken those cruel words when there came a call to us from Mr. Betteredge.", "\"그녀가 그 잔인한 말을 막 마쳤을 때, 베터리지 씨에게서 우리에게 부름이 왔습니다."], ["All the indoor servants were to assemble in the hall.", "온 안살림 하인들은 현관홀에 모이라는 것이었습니다."], ["And then we were to go in, one by one, and be questioned in Mr. Betteredge’s room by Sergeant Cuff.", "그런 다음 우리는 하나씩 들어가, 베터리지 씨의 방에서 커프 부장에게 신문받기로 되어 있었습니다."]], [["“It came to my turn to go in, after her ladyship’s maid and the upper housemaid had been questioned first.", "\"마님의 몸종과 웃길 하녀가 먼저 신문받은 뒤, 제가 들어갈 차례가 되었습니다."], ["Sergeant Cuff’s inquiries—though he wrapped them up very cunningly—soon showed me that those two women (the bitterest enemies I had in the house) had made their discoveries outside my door, on the Tuesday afternoon, and again on the Thursday night.", "커프 부장의 물음은—그가 그것을 아주 교묘하게 감쌌건만—곧 저에게, 그 두 여자가 (그 집에서 제가 가진 가장 지독한 원수들이었습니다) 화요일 오후에, 그리고 다시 목요일 밤에 제 문밖에서 저의 일을 알아챘음을 보여 주었습니다."], ["They had told the Sergeant enough to open his eyes to some part of the truth.", "그들은 그 부장에게, 그의 눈이 진실의 얼마쯤에 뜨일 만큼 충분히 일러바쳤습니다."], ["He rightly believed me to have made a new nightgown secretly, but he wrongly believed the paint-stained nightgown to be mine.", "그는 제가 몰래 새 잠옷을 만들었다고 옳게 믿었으나, 페인트 얼룩진 잠옷이 저의 것이라고 그릇되게 믿었습니다."], ["I felt satisfied of another thing, from what he said, which it puzzled me to understand.", "저는 그가 한 말에서, 이해하기에 저를 어리둥절하게 하는 또 한 가지를 흡족히 느꼈습니다."], ["He suspected me, of course, of being concerned in the disappearance of the Diamond.", "그는, 물론, 제가 그 다이아몬드의 사라짐에 관련되어 있다고 의심했습니다."], ["But, at the same time, he let me see—purposely, as I thought—that he did not consider me as the person chiefly answerable for the loss of the jewel.", "허나 동시에 그는—일부러, 라고 저는 생각했습니다—저를 그 보석의 분실에 주로 책임 있는 사람으로는 여기지 않음을 저에게 내비쳤습니다."], ["He appeared to think that I had been acting under the direction of somebody else.", "그는 제가 다른 누군가의 지시 아래 움직여 왔다고 여기는 듯했습니다."], ["Who that person might be, I couldn’t guess then, and can’t guess now.", "그 사람이 누구일지, 저는 그때도 짐작할 수 없었고, 지금도 짐작할 수 없습니다."]], [["“In this uncertainty, one thing was plain—that Sergeant Cuff was miles away from knowing the whole truth.", "\"이 갈팡질팡 속에서, 한 가지는 뚜렷했습니다—커프 부장이 온전한 진실을 아는 데에서 아득히 멀리 있다는 것이었습니다."], ["You were safe as long as the nightgown was safe—and not a moment longer.", "그 잠옷이 안전한 한 선생님은 안전했습니다—그리고 그 이상 한 순간도 안전하지 않았습니다."]], [["“I quite despair of making you understand the distress and terror which pressed upon me now.", "\"이제 저를 짓누르던 그 괴로움과 공포를 선생님이 이해하시게 만드는 것은 정녕 가망 없는 일입니다."], ["It was impossible for me to risk wearing your nightgown any longer.", "저로서는 더 이상 선생님의 잠옷을 입는 위험을 무릅쓰는 것이 불가능했습니다."], ["I might find myself taken off, at a moment’s notice, to the police court at Frizinghall, to be charged on suspicion, and searched accordingly.", "저는 당장이라도, 의심으로 고발되어 그에 따라 몸수색을 당하려, 프리징홀의 경찰서로 끌려가게 될지도 몰랐습니다."], ["While Sergeant Cuff still left me free, I had to choose—and at once—between destroying the nightgown, or hiding it in some safe place, at some safe distance from the house.", "커프 부장이 아직 저를 자유롭게 놓아둔 동안, 저는 골라야 했습니다—그것도 당장에—그 잠옷을 없애 버리는 것과, 그 집에서 안전한 거리만큼 떨어진 어느 안전한 곳에 그것을 감추는 것 가운데서 말입니다."]], [["“If I had only been a little less fond of you, I think I should have destroyed it.", "\"만일 제가 선생님을 조금만 덜 사랑했더라면, 저는 그것을 없애 버렸으리라 생각합니다."], ["But oh!", "허나 오!"], ["how could I destroy the only thing I had which proved that I had saved you from discovery?", "제가 선생님을 발각에서 구했음을 증명하는, 제가 가진 유일한 그것을 어찌 제가 없앨 수 있겠습니까?"], ["If we did come to an explanation together, and if you suspected me of having some bad motive, and denied it all, how could I win upon you to trust me, unless I had the nightgown to produce?", "만일 우리가 정말로 함께 해명에 이르게 되고, 만일 선생님이 저에게 무슨 나쁜 동기가 있다고 의심하시어 그 모든 것을 부인하신다면, 제가 내놓을 그 잠옷을 가지고 있지 않고서야, 어찌 제가 선생님이 저를 믿게 만들 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Was it wronging you to believe, as I did and do still, that you might hesitate to let a poor girl like me be the sharer of your secret, and your accomplice in the theft which your money-troubles had tempted you to commit?", "선생님의 돈 걱정이 저지르도록 선생님을 꾄 그 절도의, 저 같은 가엾은 계집이 선생님의 비밀을 함께 나누는 자, 선생님의 공범이 되게 하는 것을 선생님이 망설이실지도 모른다고, 제가 그때 믿었고 지금도 믿듯이 믿는 것이, 선생님께 잘못하는 일일까요?"], ["Think of your cold behaviour to me, sir, and you will hardly wonder at my unwillingness to destroy the only claim on your confidence and your gratitude which it was my fortune to possess.", "선생님의 저에 대한 그 차가운 처신을 생각해 보십시오, 선생님, 그러면 선생님은, 제가 지니게 된 행운인 그 유일한, 선생님의 믿음과 선생님의 고마움에 대한 권리를 없애기를 제가 꺼린 것에 좀처럼 놀라지 않으실 것입니다."]], [["“I determined to hide it; and the place I fixed on was the place I knew best—the Shivering Sand.", "\"저는 그것을 감추기로 마음먹었습니다. 그리고 제가 정한 곳은 제가 가장 잘 아는 곳—흔들리는 모래였습니다."]], [["“As soon as the questioning was over, I made the first excuse that came into my head, and got leave to go out for a breath of fresh air.", "\"신문이 끝나자마자, 저는 제 머리에 떠오른 첫 핑계를 대고, 바깥바람을 좀 쐬러 나가도 좋다는 허락을 얻었습니다."], ["I went straight to Cobb’s Hole, to Mr. Yolland’s cottage.", "저는 곧장 코브스 홀로, 욜랜드 씨의 오두막으로 갔습니다."], ["His wife and daughter were the best friends I had.", "그의 아내와 딸은 제가 가진 가장 좋은 벗이었습니다."], ["Don’t suppose I trusted them with your secret—I have trusted nobody.", "제가 그들에게 선생님의 비밀을 맡겼다고는 여기지 마십시오—저는 아무에게도 맡기지 않았습니다."], ["All I wanted was to write this letter to you, and to have a safe opportunity of taking the nightgown off me.", "제가 원한 것은 오직 이 편지를 선생님께 쓰는 것과, 그 잠옷을 제 몸에서 벗을 안전한 기회를 갖는 것뿐이었습니다."], ["Suspected as I was, I could do neither of those things with any sort of security, at the house.", "의심받고 있는 처지라, 저는 그 집에서는 그 두 가지 중 어느 것도 조금도 안전하게 할 수 없었습니다."]], [["“And now I have nearly got through my long letter, writing it alone in Lucy Yolland’s bedroom.", "\"그리고 이제 저는 루시 욜랜드의 침실에서 홀로 이 편지를 쓰며, 저의 긴 편지를 거의 다 마쳤습니다."], ["When it is done, I shall go downstairs with the nightgown rolled up, and hidden under my cloak.", "그것이 끝나면, 저는 그 잠옷을 둘둘 말아 제 망토 아래 감추고 아래층으로 내려가겠습니다."], ["I shall find the means I want for keeping it safe and dry in its hiding-place, among the litter of old things in Mrs. Yolland’s kitchen.", "저는 욜랜드 부인의 부엌에 있는 낡은 잡동사니 사이의 그 은닉처에, 그것을 안전하고 마른 채로 간직하는 데 필요한 수단을 찾을 것입니다."], ["And then I shall go to the Shivering Sand—don’t be afraid of my letting my footmarks betray me!—and hide the nightgown down in the sand, where no living creature can find it without being first let into the secret by myself.", "그런 다음 저는 흔들리는 모래로 가서—제 발자국이 저를 배신하도록 두지 않을 테니 두려워 마십시오!—그 잠옷을, 제가 먼저 그 비밀을 알려 주지 않고서는 살아 있는 어떤 존재도 찾을 수 없는, 그 모래 속에 감출 것입니다."]], [["“And, when that’s done, what then?", "\"그리고, 그것이 끝나면, 그다음은?"]], [["“Then, Mr. Franklin, I shall have two reasons for making another attempt to say the words to you which I have not said yet.", "\"그다음, 프랭클린 나리, 저는 아직 하지 못한 그 말들을 선생님께 하려는 또 한 번의 시도를 할 두 가지 이유를 갖게 될 것입니다."], ["If you leave the house, as Penelope believes you will leave it, and if I haven’t spoken to you before that, I shall lose my opportunity forever.", "만일 선생님이, 페넬로피가 믿는 대로, 그 집을 떠나신다면, 그리고 만일 제가 그 전에 선생님께 말하지 못한다면, 저는 제 기회를 영영 잃을 것입니다."], ["That is one reason.", "그것이 한 가지 이유입니다."], ["Then, again, there is the comforting knowledge—if my speaking does make you angry—that I have got the nightgown ready to plead my cause for me as nothing else can.", "그리고, 또, 만일 저의 말함이 선생님을 화나시게 만든다면—다른 어떤 것도 할 수 없는 방식으로 저를 위해 제 사정을 변호해 줄 그 잠옷을 제가 채비해 두었다는, 그 위안이 되는 앎이 있습니다."], ["That is my other reason.", "그것이 저의 다른 이유입니다."], ["If these two together don’t harden my heart against the coldness which has hitherto frozen it up (I mean the coldness of your treatment of me), there will be the end of my efforts—and the end of my life.", "만일 이 둘이 함께, 여태 저를 얼어붙게 해 온 그 차가움(선생님의 저에 대한 처신의 차가움을 말합니다)에 맞서 제 마음을 굳게 만들지 못한다면, 거기서 저의 애씀은 끝날 것입니다—그리고 저의 삶도 끝날 것입니다."]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇습니다."], ["If I miss my next opportunity—if you are as cruel as ever, and if I feel it again as I have felt it already—good-bye to the world which has grudged me the happiness that it gives to others.", "만일 제가 다음 기회를 놓친다면—만일 선생님이 여느 때처럼 잔인하시고, 만일 제가 이미 느낀 대로 그것을 다시 느낀다면—남들에게는 베푸는 그 행복을 저에게는 인색하게 굴어 온 이 세상이여, 안녕히."], ["Good-bye to life, which nothing but a little kindness from you can ever make pleasurable to me again.", "오직 선생님의 작은 다정함만이 다시 저에게 즐거운 것으로 만들어 줄 수 있는 그 삶이여, 안녕히."], ["Don’t blame yourself, sir, if it ends in this way.", "만일 그것이 이런 식으로 끝나거든, 선생님, 선생님 자신을 탓하지 마십시오."], ["But try—do try—to feel some forgiving sorrow for me!", "허나 애써—부디 애써—저를 위해 얼마쯤 용서하는 슬픔을 느껴 주십시오!"], ["I shall take care that you find out what I have done for you, when I am past telling you of it myself.", "저는, 제가 선생님께 직접 말할 수 없게 되었을 때, 선생님이 제가 선생님을 위해 한 일을 알아내시도록 마음 쓰겠습니다."], ["Will you say something kind of me then—in the same gentle way that you have when you speak to Miss Rachel?", "그때 선생님은 저에 대해 뭔가 다정한 말씀을 해 주시겠습니까—선생님이 레이철 아가씨께 말씀하실 때의 그 다감한 투로요?"], ["If you do that, and if there are such things as ghosts, I believe my ghost will hear it, and tremble with the pleasure of it.", "만일 선생님이 그리하신다면, 그리고 만일 유령 같은 것이 있다면, 저는 저의 유령이 그것을 듣고, 그 기쁨에 떨리라 믿습니다."]], [["“It’s time I left off.", "\"이제 그만 멈춰야 할 때입니다."], ["I am making myself cry.", "제가 저를 울리고 있습니다."], ["How am I to see my way to the hiding-place if I let these useless tears come and blind me?", "이 쓸데없는 눈물이 나와 저를 멀게 하도록 둔다면, 어떻게 제가 그 은닉처로 가는 길을 보겠습니까?"]], [["“Besides, why should I look at the gloomy side?", "\"게다가, 어째서 제가 그 음울한 쪽을 보아야 합니까?"], ["Why not believe, while I can, that it will end well after all?", "할 수 있는 동안, 결국 그것이 잘 끝나리라고 믿지 못할 것이 무엇입니까?"], ["I may find you in a good humour tonight—or, if not, I may succeed better tomorrow morning.", "저는 오늘 밤 기분 좋으신 선생님을 뵐지도 모릅니다—또는, 그렇지 않다면, 저는 내일 아침에 더 잘 해낼지도 모릅니다."], ["I sha’n’t improve my plain face by fretting—shall I?", "저는 애태운다고 저의 못난 얼굴을 나아지게 하지는 못할 겁니다—그렇지요?"], ["Who knows but I may have filled all these weary long pages of paper for nothing?", "제가 이 고달프고 긴 종이쪽들을 죄 헛되이 채웠을지 누가 알겠습니까?"], ["They will go, for safety’s sake (never mind now for what other reason) into the hiding-place along with the nightgown.", "그것들은, 안전을 위하여 (다른 무슨 이유에서인지는 이제 아무래도 좋습니다) 그 잠옷과 함께 그 은닉처로 들어갈 것입니다."], ["It has been hard, hard work writing my letter.", "제 편지를 쓰는 것은 고되고, 고된 일이었습니다."], ["Oh!", "오!"], ["if we only end in understanding each other, how I shall enjoy tearing it up!", "우리가 서로 이해하는 것으로 끝나기만 한다면, 제가 이것을 찢어 버리는 것을 얼마나 즐기겠습니까!"]], [["“I beg to remain, sir, your true lover and humble servant,", "\"삼가, 선생님, 선생님의 진실한 연인이자 미천한 하녀로 남습니다,"]], [["“ROSANNA SPEARMAN.”", "로재나 스피어먼.\""]], [["The reading of the letter was completed by Betteredge in silence.", "그 편지 읽기는 베터리지에 의해 말없이 마쳐졌다."], ["After carefully putting it back in the envelope, he sat thinking, with his head bowed down, and his eyes on the ground.", "그것을 조심스레 봉투에 도로 넣고서, 그는 고개를 숙이고, 눈을 바닥에 둔 채, 생각에 잠겨 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Betteredge,”", "\"베터리지,\""], ["I said, “is there any hint to guide me at the end of the letter?”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"그 편지 끝에 나를 이끌어 줄 무슨 실마리가 있는가?\""]], [["He looked up slowly, with a heavy sigh.", "그는 무거운 한숨과 함께, 천천히 눈을 들었다."]], [["“There is nothing to guide you, Mr. Franklin,”", "\"나리를 이끌어 줄 것은 아무것도 없습니다, 프랭클린 나리,\""], ["he answered.", "하고 그가 대답했다."], ["“If you take my advice you will keep the letter in the cover till these present anxieties of yours have come to an end.", "\"제 말을 들으신다면 나리는 나리의 지금 이 근심들이 끝날 때까지 그 편지를 봉투 안에 넣어 두시는 게 좋겠습니다."], ["It will sorely distress you, whenever you read it.", "그것은 나리가 읽으실 때마다 나리를 몹시 괴롭게 할 것입니다."], ["Don’t read it now.”", "지금은 그것을 읽지 마십시오.\""]], [["I put the letter away in my pocket-book.", "나는 그 편지를 내 수첩에 넣어 두었다."]], [["A glance back at the sixteenth and seventeenth chapters of Betteredge’s Narrative will show that there really was a reason for my thus sparing myself, at a time when my fortitude had been already cruelly tried.", "베터리지 이야기의 열여섯째와 열일곱째 장을 되돌아보면, 이미 나의 꿋꿋함이 잔인하게 시험받은 그때에 내가 이렇듯 나 자신을 아낀 데에 정녕 이유가 있었음을 알 수 있으리라."], ["Twice over, the unhappy woman had made her last attempt to speak to me.", "두 번에 걸쳐, 그 불행한 여인은 나에게 말을 걸려는 마지막 시도를 했었다."], ["And twice over, it had been my misfortune (God knows how innocently!) to repel the advances she had made to me.", "그리고 두 번에 걸쳐, 그녀가 나에게 건넨 그 다가섬을 밀어낸 것이 (하느님은 그것이 얼마나 무심결이었는지 아신다!) 나의 불운이었다."], ["On the Friday night, as Betteredge truly describes it, she had found me alone at the billiard-table.", "금요일 밤에, 베터리지가 참되이 그리는 대로, 그녀는 내가 당구대에 홀로 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Her manner and language suggested to me and would have suggested to any man, under the circumstances—that she was about to confess a guilty knowledge of the disappearance of the Diamond.", "그녀의 태도와 말은 나에게, 그리고 그런 상황에서라면 어떤 사내에게든—그녀가 그 다이아몬드의 사라짐에 대한 죄지은 앎을 고백하려는 참임을 넌지시 일러 주었다."], ["For her own sake, I had purposely shown no special interest in what was coming; for her own sake, I had purposely looked at the billiard-balls, instead of looking at her—and what had been the result?", "그녀 자신을 위하여, 나는 일부러 다가오는 것에 아무 각별한 관심도 보이지 않았다. 그녀 자신을 위하여, 나는 일부러 그녀를 보는 대신 당구공들을 보았다—그리고 그 결과는 어떠했던가?"], ["I had sent her away from me, wounded to the heart!", "나는 그녀를, 가슴 깊이 상처 입은 채, 나에게서 떠나보냈던 것이다!"], ["On the Saturday again—on the day when she must have foreseen, after what Penelope had told her, that my departure was close at hand—the same fatality still pursued us.", "토요일에 다시—페넬로피가 그녀에게 말해 준 뒤로, 나의 떠남이 임박했음을 그녀가 내다보았음이 틀림없는 그날에—똑같은 숙명이 여전히 우리를 좇았다."], ["She had once more attempted to meet me in the shrubbery walk, and she had found me there in company with Betteredge and Sergeant Cuff.", "그녀는 다시 한 번 그 관목숲 길에서 나를 만나려 했고, 그녀는 내가 베터리지와 커프 부장과 함께 있는 그곳에서 나를 보았다."], ["In her hearing, the Sergeant, with his own underhand object in view, had appealed to my interest in Rosanna Spearman.", "그녀가 듣는 데에서, 그 부장은, 제 나름의 은밀한 목적을 품고, 로재나 스피어먼에 대한 나의 관심에 호소했다."], ["Again for the poor creature’s own sake, I had met the police-officer with a flat denial, and had declared—loudly declared, so that she might hear me too—that I felt “no interest whatever in Rosanna Spearman.”", "다시 그 가엾은 존재 자신을 위하여, 나는 그 경관을 딱 잘라 부인함으로써 맞았고—그녀도 나를 듣도록—큰 소리로 선언했다, 내가 \"로재나 스피어먼에게 아무런 관심도 없다\"고."], ["At those words, solely designed to warn her against attempting to gain my private ear, she had turned away and left the place: cautioned of her danger, as I then believed; self-doomed to destruction, as I know now.", "그녀가 나의 사적인 귀를 얻으려 시도하는 것을 막으려는 뜻으로만 던진 그 말에, 그녀는 몸을 돌려 그곳을 떠났다—그때 내가 믿기로는, 제 위험을 경계한 채로. 지금 내가 아는 대로는, 파멸로 스스로를 몰아넣은 채로."], ["From that point, I have already traced the succession of events which led me to the astounding discovery at the quicksand.", "그 지점에서부터, 나는 나를 그 늪 모래에서의 그 아연할 발견으로 이끈 사건들의 잇따름을 이미 좇아 왔다."], ["The retrospect is now complete.", "그 회고는 이제 마무리되었다."], ["I may leave the miserable story of Rosanna Spearman—to which, even at this distance of time, I cannot revert without a pang of distress—to suggest for itself all that is here purposely left unsaid.", "나는 로재나 스피어먼의 그 비참한 이야기를—이만한 세월이 흐른 지금도 괴로움의 아릿함 없이는 돌이킬 수 없는 그 이야기를—여기 일부러 말하지 않은 채 남겨 둔 온갖 것을 스스로 넌지시 일러 주도록 둘 수 있으리라."], ["I may pass from the suicide at the Shivering Sand, with its strange and terrible influence on my present position and future prospects, to interests which concern the living people of this narrative, and to events which were already paving my way for the slow and toilsome journey from the darkness to the light.", "나는 흔들리는 모래에서의 그 자살을, 나의 현재 처지와 미래 전망에 대한 그 이상하고도 무서운 영향과 더불어 지나쳐, 이 이야기의 살아 있는 사람들에 관련되는 관심사로, 그리고 어둠에서 빛으로 가는 그 더디고 고된 여정을 위해 이미 나의 길을 닦고 있던 사건들로 넘어갈 수 있으리라."]], [["# CHAPTER VI", "# CHAPTER VI"]], [["I walked to the railway station accompanied, it is needless to say, by Gabriel Betteredge.", "나는, 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 게이브리얼 베터리지를 동무 삼아, 그 철도역까지 걸었다."], ["I had the letter in my pocket, and the nightgown safely packed in a little bag—both to be submitted, before I slept that night, to the investigation of Mr. Bruff.", "나는 그 편지를 주머니에 지녔고, 그 잠옷을 자그마한 가방에 안전히 챙겼다—둘 다, 그날 밤 내가 잠들기 전에, 브러프 씨의 조사에 부쳐질 것이었다."]], [["We left the house in silence.", "우리는 말없이 그 집을 떠났다."], ["For the first time in my experience of him, I found old Betteredge in my company without a word to say to me.", "내가 그를 겪은 이래 처음으로, 나는 늙은 베터리지가 나에게 할 말 한마디 없이 내 곁에 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Having something to say on my side, I opened the conversation as soon as we were clear of the lodge gates.", "내 편에서는 할 말이 있었으므로, 나는 우리가 그 관리인 문을 벗어나자마자 대화의 문을 열었다."]], [["“Before I go to London,”", "\"내가 런던으로 가기 전에,\""], ["I began, “I have two questions to ask you.", "하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"자네에게 물을 것이 두 가지 있네."], ["They relate to myself, and I believe they will rather surprise you.”", "그것들은 나 자신에 관한 것인데, 아마 자네를 자못 놀라게 할 걸세.\""]], [["“If they will put that poor creature’s letter out of my head, Mr. Franklin, they may do anything else they like with me.", "\"그것들이 그 가엾은 것의 편지를 제 머리에서 몰아내 준다면, 프랭클린 나리, 그것들이 저를 어찌하든 좋습니다."], ["Please to begin surprising me, sir, as soon as you can.”", "부디 되도록 빨리 저를 놀라게 하기 시작해 주십시오, 나리.\""]], [["“My first question, Betteredge, is this.", "\"나의 첫 물음은, 베터리지, 이것일세."], ["Was I drunk on the night of Rachel’s Birthday?”", "레이철의 생일 밤에 내가 취해 있었는가?\""]], [["“You drunk!”", "\"나리가 취하셨다니요!\""], ["exclaimed the old man.", "하고 그 노인이 외쳤다."], ["“Why it’s the great defect of your character, Mr. Franklin that you only drink with your dinner, and never touch a drop of liquor afterwards!”", "\"원 참, 나리는 저녁에만 술을 드시고 그 뒤로는 술 한 방울 입에 대지 않으신다는 것이야말로 나리의 성품의 큰 흠입지요, 프랭클린 나리!\""]], [["“But the birthday was a special occasion.", "\"허나 그 생일은 특별한 날이었네."], ["I might have abandoned my regular habits, on that night of all others.”", "다른 어느 밤도 아닌 그 밤에, 내가 나의 여느 버릇을 저버렸을지도 모르지.\""]], [["Betteredge considered for a moment.", "베터리지는 잠시 곰곰 생각했다."]], [["“You did go out of your habits, sir,”", "\"나리는 정녕 나리의 버릇에서 벗어나셨습니다, 나리,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “And I’ll tell you how.", "\"그리고 제가 어찌 된 일인지 말씀드리지요."], ["You looked wretchedly ill—and we persuaded you to have a drop of brandy and water to cheer you up a little.”", "나리가 몹시 편찮아 보이셨습니다—그래서 우리는 나리를 조금 기운 나게 해 드리려고 브랜디 물을 좀 드시라고 나리를 구슬렸습니다.\""]], [["“I am not used to brandy and water.", "\"나는 브랜디 물에 익숙하지 않아."], ["It is quite possible——”", "아주 있을 법한 일이지——\""]], [["“Wait a bit, Mr. Franklin.", "\"잠깐만요, 프랭클린 나리."], ["I knew you were not used, too.", "저도 나리가 익숙하지 않으신 걸 알았습니다."], ["I poured you out half a wineglass-full of our fifty year old Cognac; and (more shame for me!) I drowned that noble liquor in nigh on a tumbler-full of cold water.", "저는 나리께 우리의 오십 년 묵은 코냑을 포도주잔으로 반 잔 따라 드렸습니다. 그리고 (더 부끄러운 일이지요!) 저는 그 고귀한 술을 거의 큰 잔 하나 가득한 찬물에 빠뜨려 놓았습니다."], ["A child couldn’t have got drunk on it—let alone a grown man!”", "어린아이도 그것으로는 취할 수 없었을 겁니다—다 큰 어른은 말할 것도 없이요!\""]], [["I knew I could depend on his memory, in a matter of this kind.", "나는 이런 종류의 일에서는 그의 기억에 기대어도 좋음을 알았다."], ["It was plainly impossible that I could have been intoxicated.", "내가 취해 있었을 수는 명백히 없었다."], ["I passed on to the second question.", "나는 두 번째 물음으로 넘어갔다."]], [["“Before I was sent abroad, Betteredge, you saw a great deal of me when I was a boy?", "\"내가 외국으로 보내지기 전에, 베터리지, 자네는 내가 소년이었을 때 나를 많이 보았지?"], ["Now tell me plainly, do you remember anything strange of me, after I had gone to bed at night?", "자, 솔직히 말해 주게, 밤에 내가 잠자리에 든 뒤에 나에게서 무슨 이상한 것을 기억하는가?"], ["Did you ever discover me walking in my sleep?”", "자네는 언젠가 내가 잠결에 걷는 것을 본 적이 있는가?\""]], [["Betteredge stopped, looked at me for a moment, nodded his head, and walked on again.", "베터리지는 걸음을 멈추고, 잠시 나를 바라보고, 고개를 끄덕이고, 다시 걸어갔다."]], [["“I see your drift now, Mr. Franklin!”", "\"이제 나리의 속셈을 알겠습니다, 프랭클린 나리!\""], ["he said “You’re trying to account for how you got the paint on your nightgown, without knowing it yourself.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"나리는 나리 자신도 모르게 어찌하여 나리의 잠옷에 그 페인트가 묻었는지를 해명하려 하고 계십니다."], ["It won’t do, sir.", "그건 안 됩니다, 나리."], ["You’re miles away still from getting at the truth.", "나리는 진실에 다다르는 데에서 아직 아득히 멀리 계십니다."], ["Walk in your sleep?", "잠결에 걷는다고요?"], ["You never did such a thing in your life!”", "나리는 평생 그런 짓을 하신 적이 없습니다!\""]], [["Here again, I felt that Betteredge must be right.", "여기서도 다시, 나는 베터리지가 옳음이 틀림없다고 느꼈다."], ["Neither at home nor abroad had my life ever been of the solitary sort.", "집에서든 외국에서든 나의 삶은 여태 혼자만의 부류였던 적이 없었다."], ["If I had been a sleep-walker, there were hundreds on hundreds of people who must have discovered me, and who, in the interest of my own safety, would have warned me of the habit, and have taken precautions to restrain it.", "만일 내가 몽유병자였다면, 나를 발견했음이 틀림없는, 그리고 나 자신의 안전을 위하여 그 버릇을 나에게 알려 주고 그것을 막을 방비를 취했을, 수백에 수백 명의 사람들이 있었을 것이다."]], [["Still, admitting all this, I clung—with an obstinacy which was surely natural and excusable, under the circumstances—to one or other of the only two explanations that I could see which accounted for the unendurable position in which I then stood.", "그럼에도, 이 모든 것을 인정하면서도, 나는—그런 상황에서라면 정녕 당연하고 용서받을 만한 고집으로—그때 내가 놓여 있던 그 견딜 수 없는 처지를 설명해 주는, 내가 볼 수 있는 오직 두 가지 설명 가운데 이런저런 것에 매달렸다."], ["Observing that I was not yet satisfied, Betteredge shrewdly adverted to certain later events in the history of the Moonstone; and scattered both my theories to the wind at once and for ever.", "내가 아직 만족하지 않았음을 알아채고, 베터리지는 월장석의 내력에서 어떤 나중의 사건들을 슬기롭게 들추었다. 그리하여 나의 두 이론을 대뜸, 그리고 영영, 바람에 날려 버렸다."]], [["“Let’s try it another way, sir,” he said.", "\"이걸 다른 식으로 해 봅시다, 나리,\""], ["“Keep your own opinion, and see how far it will take you towards finding out the truth.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"나리 나름의 견해를 지키시고, 그것이 진실을 알아내는 데로 나리를 얼마나 멀리 데려가는지 보십시오."], ["If we are to believe the nightgown—which I don’t for one—you not only smeared off the paint from the door, without knowing it, but you also took the Diamond without knowing it.", "만일 우리가 그 잠옷을 믿는다면—저는 조금도 안 믿습니다만—나리는 나리 자신도 모르게 그 문에서 페인트를 묻히셨을 뿐 아니라, 나리 자신도 모르게 그 다이아몬드도 가져가신 것입니다."], ["Is that right, so far?”", "여기까지 맞습니까?\""]], [["“Quite right.", "\"아주 맞네."], ["Go on.”", "계속하게.\""]], [["“Very good, sir.", "\"좋습니다, 나리."], ["We’ll say you were drunk, or walking in your sleep, when you took the jewel.", "나리가 그 보석을 가져가셨을 때 취해 계셨거나, 잠결에 걸으셨다고 해 둡시다."], ["That accounts for the night and morning, after the birthday.", "그것이 그 생일 뒤의 밤과 아침을 설명해 줍니다."], ["But how does it account for what has happened since that time?", "허나 그것이 그때 이후로 일어난 일을 어떻게 설명합니까?"], ["The Diamond has been taken to London, since that time.", "그때 이후로 그 다이아몬드는 런던으로 가져가졌습니다."], ["The Diamond has been pledged to Mr. Luker, since that time.", "그때 이후로 그 다이아몬드는 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔습니다."], ["Did you do those two things, without knowing it, too?", "나리는 그 두 가지 일도, 나리 자신도 모르게 하셨습니까?"], ["Were you drunk when I saw you off in the pony-chaise on that Saturday evening?", "그 토요일 저녁에 제가 나리를 조랑말 마차에 태워 배웅했을 때 나리는 취해 계셨습니까?"], ["And did you walk in your sleep to Mr. Luker’s, when the train had brought you to your journey’s end?", "그리고 기차가 나리를 여정의 끝에 데려다주었을 때, 나리는 루커 씨의 집으로 잠결에 걸어가셨습니까?"], ["Excuse me for saying it, Mr. Franklin, but this business has so upset you, that you’re not fit yet to judge for yourself.", "이런 말씀 드리는 걸 용서하십시오, 프랭클린 나리, 허나 이 일이 나리를 어찌나 심란하게 했던지, 나리는 아직 나리 스스로 판단하실 만한 상태가 못 됩니다."], ["The sooner you lay your head alongside Mr. Bruff’s head, the sooner you will see your way out of the dead-lock that has got you now.”", "나리의 머리를 브러프 씨의 머리 곁에 나란히 대실수록, 나리는 지금 나리를 옭아맨 그 막다른 처지에서 빠져나갈 길을 더 빨리 보시게 될 것입니다.\""]], [["We reached the station, with only a minute or two to spare.", "우리는 겨우 일이 분을 남기고 그 역에 다다랐다."]], [["I hurriedly gave Betteredge my address in London, so that he might write to me, if necessary; promising, on my side, to inform him of any news which I might have to communicate.", "나는, 필요하다면 그가 나에게 편지할 수 있도록, 서둘러 베터리지에게 런던의 내 주소를 주었다. 내 편에서는, 내가 전할 만한 무슨 소식이든 그에게 알리겠다고 약속하면서."], ["This done, and just as I was bidding him farewell, I happened to glance towards the book-and-newspaper stall.", "이것을 마치고, 막 그에게 작별을 고하려는데, 나는 우연히 그 책과 신문 가판대 쪽을 흘긋 보았다."], ["There was Mr. Candy’s remarkable-looking assistant again, speaking to the keeper of the stall!", "거기, 다시 캔디 씨의 그 눈에 띄는 생김새의 조수가, 그 가판대 주인에게 이야기하고 있는 것이었다!"], ["Our eyes met at the same moment.", "우리의 눈이 같은 순간에 마주쳤다."], ["Ezra Jennings took off his hat to me.", "에즈라 제닝스는 나에게 모자를 벗어 인사했다."], ["I returned the salute, and got into a carriage just as the train started.", "나는 그 인사에 답하고, 기차가 막 떠나는 참에 객차에 올랐다."], ["It was a relief to my mind, I suppose, to dwell on any subject which appeared to be, personally, of no sort of importance to me.", "짐작건대, 나에게 개인적으로 아무런 중요성도 없어 보이는 어떤 화제에 마음을 두는 것이, 나의 마음에 안도가 되었다."], ["At all events, I began the momentous journey back which was to take me to Mr. Bruff, wondering—absurdly enough, I admit—that I should have seen the man with the piebald hair twice in one day!", "어쨌든, 나는—꽤나 어이없게도, 인정하거니와—내가 하루에 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼의 사내를 두 번이나 보았다는 것을 의아해하며, 나를 브러프 씨에게로 데려갈 그 중대한 귀로에 올랐다!"]], [["The hour at which I arrived in London precluded all hope of my finding Mr. Bruff at his place of business.", "내가 런던에 다다른 시각은 브러프 씨를 그의 일터에서 볼 온갖 희망을 앗아 갔다."], ["I drove from the railway to his private residence at Hampstead, and disturbed the old lawyer dozing alone in his dining-room, with his favourite pug-dog on his lap, and his bottle of wine at his elbow.", "나는 철도역에서 햄스테드의 그의 사저로 마차를 몰았고, 그 늙은 변호사가 무릎에는 그가 아끼는 퍼그 개를, 팔꿈치께에는 포도주 병을 두고, 홀로 그의 식당에서 꾸벅꾸벅 조는 것을 방해했다."]], [["I shall best describe the effect which my story produced on the mind of Mr. Bruff by relating his proceedings when he had heard it to the end.", "나의 이야기가 브러프 씨의 마음에 남긴 그 효과는, 그가 그것을 끝까지 듣고 나서 취한 조치들을 이야기하는 것으로 가장 잘 그려 낼 수 있으리라."], ["He ordered lights, and strong tea, to be taken into his study; and he sent a message to the ladies of his family, forbidding them to disturb us on any pretence whatever.", "그는 그의 서재로 불과 진한 차를 들이라 일렀다. 그리고 그는 그의 가족의 부인들에게, 그 무슨 핑계로도 우리를 방해하지 말라는 전갈을 보냈다."], ["These preliminaries disposed of, he first examined the nightgown, and then devoted himself to the reading of Rosanna Spearman’s letter.", "이 사전 채비를 마치고, 그는 먼저 그 잠옷을 살폈고, 그런 다음 로재나 스피어먼의 편지 읽기에 몰두했다."]], [["The reading completed, Mr. Bruff addressed me for the first time since we had been shut up together in the seclusion of his own room.", "그 읽기가 끝나자, 브러프 씨는, 우리가 그의 방의 은밀함 속에 함께 들어앉은 이래 처음으로 나에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Franklin Blake,”", "\"프랭클린 블레이크,\""], ["said the old gentleman, “this is a very serious matter, in more respects than one.", "하고 그 노신사가 말했다. \"이것은 한 가지 이상의 면에서 아주 중대한 일이오."], ["In my opinion, it concerns Rachel quite as nearly as it concerns you.", "내 생각으로는, 이것은 당신에게 관련되는 것 못지않게 레이철에게도 가까이 관련되오."], ["Her extraordinary conduct is no mystery now.", "그녀의 그 유별난 처신은 이제 수수께끼가 아니오."], ["She believes you have stolen the Diamond.”", "그녀는 당신이 그 다이아몬드를 훔쳤다고 믿는 것이오.\""]], [["I had shrunk from reasoning my own way fairly to that revolting conclusion.", "나는 나 자신의 논리로 그 역겨운 결론에 정직하게 이르기를 움츠려 피해 왔다."], ["But it had forced itself on me, nevertheless.", "허나 그것은 그럼에도 나에게 억지로 밀고 들어와 있었다."], ["My resolution to obtain a personal interview with Rachel, rested really and truly on the ground just stated by Mr. Bruff.", "레이철과의 직접 면담을 얻으려는 나의 결심은, 참으로 정녕, 브러프 씨가 방금 밝힌 그 근거 위에 놓여 있었다."]], [["“The first step to take in this investigation,”", "\"이 조사에서 취할 첫걸음은,\""], ["the lawyer proceeded, “is to appeal to Rachel.", "하고 그 변호사가 말을 이었다. \"레이철에게 호소하는 것이오."], ["She has been silent all this time, from motives which I (who know her character) can readily understand.", "그녀는 여태 내내, 내가 (그녀의 성품을 아는 사람으로서) 어렵잖게 이해할 수 있는 동기에서, 침묵해 왔소."], ["It is impossible, after what has happened, to submit to that silence any longer.", "이런 일이 일어난 뒤에는, 그 침묵을 더 이상 참는 것이 불가능하오."], ["She must be persuaded to tell us, or she must be forced to tell us, on what grounds she bases her belief that you took the Moonstone.", "그녀가, 당신이 월장석을 가져갔다는 믿음을 어떤 근거 위에 두고 있는지, 우리에게 말하도록 설득되든지, 아니면 억지로라도 말하게 되든지 해야 하오."], ["The chances are, that the whole of this case, serious as it seems now, will tumble to pieces, if we can only break through Rachel’s inveterate reserve, and prevail upon her to speak out.”", "만일 우리가 레이철의 그 뿌리 깊은 과묵함을 뚫고, 그녀가 털어놓도록 이겨 낼 수만 있다면, 지금은 이토록 중대해 보이는 이 온 사건이, 산산조각으로 무너져 내릴 공산이 크오.\""]], [["“That is a very comforting opinion for me,” I said.", "\"그것은 나에게 아주 위안이 되는 견해요,\""], ["“I own I should like to know——”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 정녕 알고 싶소——\""]], [["“You would like to know how I can justify it,” interposed Mr.", "\"당신은 내가 그것을 어떻게 정당화할 수 있는지 알고 싶겠지,\""], ["Bruff. “I can tell you in two minutes.", "하고 브러프 씨가 끼어들었다."], ["Understand, in the first place, that I look at this matter from a lawyer’s point of view.", "\"이 분 안에 말해 줄 수 있소. 우선, 내가 이 일을 변호사의 관점에서 본다는 것을 이해하시오."], ["It’s a question of evidence, with me.", "나에게 이것은 증거의 문제요."], ["Very well.", "좋소."], ["The evidence breaks down, at the outset, on one important point.”", "그 증거는, 첫머리에서, 한 가지 중요한 점에서 무너지오.\""]], [["“On what point?”", "\"어떤 점에서요?\""]], [["“You shall hear.", "\"들어 보시오."], ["I admit that the mark of the name proves the nightgown to be yours.", "나는 그 이름 표시가 그 잠옷이 당신의 것임을 증명한다는 것을 인정하오."], ["I admit that the mark of the paint proves the nightgown to have made the smear on Rachel’s door.", "나는 그 페인트 자국이 그 잠옷이 레이철의 문에 그 얼룩을 냈음을 증명한다는 것을 인정하오."], ["But what evidence is there to prove that you are the person who wore it, on the night when the Diamond was lost?”", "허나 그 다이아몬드가 없어진 그 밤에 그것을 입은 사람이 당신이라는 것을 증명할 증거가 어디 있소?\""]], [["The objection struck me, all the more forcibly that it reflected an objection which I had felt myself.", "그 반론은, 그것이 나 자신도 느꼈던 반론을 되비치는 것이었던 만큼, 한층 더 세게 나를 때렸다."]], [["“As to this,”", "\"이것에 관해서는,\""], ["pursued the lawyer taking up Rosanna Spearman’s confession, “I can understand that the letter is a distressing one to you.", "하고 그 변호사는 로재나 스피어먼의 고백을 집어 들며 말을 이었다. \"나는 그 편지가 당신에게 괴로운 편지임을 이해할 수 있소."], ["I can understand that you may hesitate to analyse it from a purely impartial point of view.", "나는 당신이 그것을 순전히 공정한 관점에서 분석하기를 망설일 수 있음을 이해할 수 있소."], ["But I am not in your position.", "허나 나는 당신의 처지에 있지 않소."], ["I can bring my professional experience to bear on this document, just as I should bring it to bear on any other.", "나는 나의 직업적 경험을, 다른 어떤 문서에든 적용하듯, 바로 이 문서에 적용할 수 있소."], ["Without alluding to the woman’s career as a thief, I will merely remark that her letter proves her to have been an adept at deception, on her own showing; and I argue from that, that I am justified in suspecting her of not having told the whole truth.", "그 여인의 도둑으로서의 이력은 들먹이지 않고, 나는 다만 그녀의 편지가 그녀 자신이 내보인 대로 그녀가 속임수에 능한 사람이었음을 증명한다는 것만 짚어 두겠소. 그리고 나는 그것으로부터, 그녀가 온전한 진실을 말하지 않았다고 의심하는 것이 정당하다고 논하오."], ["I won’t start any theory, at present, as to what she may or may not have done.", "나는 지금으로서는, 그녀가 무엇을 했을지 안 했을지에 대해 어떤 이론도 세우지 않겠소."], ["I will only say that, if Rachel has suspected you on the evidence of the nightgown only, the chances are ninety-nine to a hundred that Rosanna Spearman was the person who showed it to her.", "나는 다만 이렇게 말하겠소, 만일 레이철이 오직 그 잠옷의 증거만으로 당신을 의심했다면, 로재나 스피어먼이 그것을 그녀에게 보여 준 사람일 공산이 백에 아흔아홉이라고."], ["In that case, there is the woman’s letter, confessing that she was jealous of Rachel, confessing that she changed the roses, confessing that she saw a glimpse of hope for herself, in the prospect of a quarrel between Rachel and you.", "그런 경우, 여기 그 여인의 편지가 있소, 자기가 레이철을 질투했음을 고백하고, 자기가 그 장미를 바꿔치기했음을 고백하고, 레이철과 당신 사이의 다툼의 전망에서 자기 자신을 위한 희망의 실낱같은 빛을 보았음을 고백하는 편지 말이오."], ["I don’t stop to ask who took the Moonstone (as a means to her end, Rosanna Spearman would have taken fifty Moonstones)—I only say that the disappearance of the jewel gave this reclaimed thief who was in love with you, an opportunity of setting you and Rachel at variance for the rest of your lives.", "나는 누가 월장석을 가져갔는지 물으려 멈추지 않겠소 (제 목적의 수단으로라면, 로재나 스피어먼은 월장석 쉰 개라도 가져갔을 거요)—나는 다만, 그 보석의 사라짐이, 당신을 사랑한 이 개과천선한 도둑에게, 당신과 레이철을 남은 평생 갈라놓을 기회를 주었다고 말하는 것이오."], ["She had not decided on destroying herself, then, remember; and, having the opportunity, I distinctly assert that it was in her character, and in her position at the time, to take it.", "기억하시오, 그녀는 그때는 아직 자기 자신을 없애기로 정하지 않았소. 그리고, 그 기회를 가진 채로, 그것을 붙잡는 것이 그녀의 성품에, 그리고 그때 그녀의 처지에 있었다고 나는 또렷이 단언하오."], ["What do you say to that?”", "그것에 대해 당신은 뭐라 하겠소?\""]], [["“Some such suspicion,”", "\"그와 비슷한 의심이,\""], ["I answered, “crossed my own mind, as soon as I opened the letter.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"그 편지를 열자마자 내 마음도 스쳤소.\""]], [["“Exactly!", "\"바로 그렇소!"], ["And when you had read the letter, you pitied the poor creature, and couldn’t find it in your heart to suspect her.", "그리고 그 편지를 다 읽었을 때, 당신은 그 가엾은 존재를 가엾이 여겨, 도무지 그녀를 의심할 마음이 나지 않았지."], ["Does you credit, my dear sir—does you credit!”", "당신을 돋보이게 하오, 나의 친애하는 이여—당신을 돋보이게 하오!\""]], [["“But suppose it turns out that I did wear the nightgown?", "\"허나 결국 내가 그 잠옷을 입었던 것으로 드러난다고 쳐 봅시다?"], ["What then?”", "그러면 어찌 되오?\""]], [["“I don’t see how the fact can be proved,”", "\"나는 그 사실이 어떻게 증명될 수 있는지 모르겠소,\""], ["said Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["“But assuming the proof to be possible, the vindication of your innocence would be no easy matter.", "\"허나 그 증명이 가능하다고 가정하면, 당신의 결백을 밝히는 것은 결코 쉬운 일이 아닐 거요."], ["We won’t go into that, now.", "지금은 그 문제에 들어가지 맙시다."], ["Let us wait and see whether Rachel hasn’t suspected you on the evidence of the nightgown only.”", "레이철이 오직 그 잠옷의 증거만으로 당신을 의심한 것은 아닌지 기다려 봅시다.\""]], [["“Good God, how coolly you talk of Rachel suspecting me!”", "\"맙소사, 레이철이 나를 의심한다는 이야기를 어찌 그리 태연히 하시오!\""], ["I broke out.", "하고 나는 터뜨렸다."], ["“What right has she to suspect Me, on any evidence, of being a thief?”", "\"그녀가 무슨 권리로, 그 무슨 증거로, 나를 도둑으로 의심한단 말이오?\""]], [["“A very sensible question, my dear sir.", "\"아주 분별 있는 물음이오, 나의 친애하는 이여."], ["Rather hotly put—but well worth considering for all that.", "다소 격하게 던져졌으나—그래도 곰곰이 새겨 볼 값어치가 넉넉하오."], ["What puzzles you, puzzles me too.", "당신을 어리둥절하게 하는 것이, 나도 어리둥절하게 하오."], ["Search your memory, and tell me this.", "당신의 기억을 뒤져, 나에게 이것을 말해 보시오."], ["Did anything happen while you were staying at the house—not, of course, to shake Rachel’s belief in your honour—but, let us say, to shake her belief (no matter with how little reason) in your principles generally?”", "당신이 그 집에 머무는 동안 무슨 일이 일어났소—물론, 당신의 명예에 대한 레이철의 믿음을 뒤흔들 만한 것이 아니라—이를테면, (그 근거가 아무리 하찮든) 당신의 사람됨 전반에 대한 그녀의 믿음을 뒤흔들 만한 무슨 일이?\""]], [["I started, in ungovernable agitation, to my feet.", "나는, 걷잡을 수 없는 동요에 휩싸여, 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["The lawyer’s question reminded me, for the first time since I had left England, that something had happened.", "그 변호사의 물음은, 내가 잉글랜드를 떠난 이래 처음으로, 정녕 무슨 일이 일어났었음을 나에게 일깨웠다."]], [["In the eighth chapter of Betteredge’s Narrative, an allusion will be found to the arrival of a foreigner and a stranger at my aunt’s house, who came to see me on business.", "베터리지 이야기의 여덟째 장에, 볼일로 나를 보러 온, 어느 외국인이자 낯선 이의 내 이모 집 방문에 대한 언급이 있을 것이다."], ["The nature of his business was this.", "그의 볼일의 성격은 이러했다."]], [["I had been foolish enough (being, as usual, straitened for money at the time) to accept a loan from the keeper of a small restaurant in Paris, to whom I was well known as a customer.", "나는 어리석게도 (여느 때처럼 그 무렵 돈에 쪼들려) 파리의 어느 작은 식당 주인에게서 빚을 받아들였는데, 나는 그에게 손님으로 잘 알려져 있었다. 그 돈을 되갚을 때가 우리 사이에 정해졌다."], ["A time was settled between us for paying the money back; and when the time came, I found it (as thousands of other honest men have found it) impossible to keep my engagement.", "그리고 그때가 오자, 나는 (다른 수천의 정직한 사내들이 그러했듯이) 나의 약속을 지키는 것이 불가능함을 알았다. 나는 그 사내에게 어음 한 장을 보냈다."], ["I sent the man a bill.", "안타깝게도 그런 문서에 나의 이름은 너무나 잘 알려져 있었다."], ["My name was unfortunately too well known on such documents: he failed to negotiate it.", "그는 그것을 유통하는 데에 실패했다. 내가 그에게서 빌린 이래 흘러간 사이에 그의 형편은 어지러워졌다."], ["His affairs had fallen into disorder, in the interval since I had borrowed of him; bankruptcy stared him in the face; and a relative of his, a French lawyer, came to England to find me, and to insist upon the payment of my debt.", "파산이 그의 눈앞에 닥쳤다. 그리고 그의 친척 하나인 어느 프랑스인 변호사가, 나를 찾아 나의 빚의 상환을 강경히 요구하러 잉글랜드로 왔다."], ["He was a man of violent temper; and he took the wrong way with me.", "그는 성미가 사나운 사내였다. 그리고 그는 나에게 잘못된 방도를 취했다."], ["High words passed on both sides; and my aunt and Rachel were unfortunately in the next room, and heard us.", "양쪽에서 거친 말이 오갔다. 그리고 안타깝게도 내 이모와 레이철이 옆방에 있어, 우리 소리를 들었다."], ["Lady Verinder came in, and insisted on knowing what was the matter.", "레이디 버린더가 들어와, 무슨 일인지 알아야겠다고 다그쳤다."], ["The Frenchman produced his credentials, and declared me to be responsible for the ruin of a poor man, who had trusted in my honour.", "그 프랑스인은 제 신임장을 내보이며, 내가, 나의 명예를 믿었던 한 가엾은 사내의 파멸에 책임이 있다고 선언했다."], ["My aunt instantly paid him the money, and sent him off.", "내 이모는 대뜸 그에게 그 돈을 갚아, 그를 돌려보냈다."], ["She knew me better of course than to take the Frenchman’s view of the transaction.", "그녀는 물론 그 거래에 대해 그 프랑스인의 견해를 취할 만큼 나를 몰랐던 것이 아니다."], ["But she was shocked at my carelessness, and justly angry with me for placing myself in a position, which, but for her interference, might have become a very disgraceful one.", "허나 그녀는 나의 부주의에 충격을 받았고, 그녀의 개입이 아니었다면 아주 욕된 처지가 될 수도 있었던 처지에 나 자신을 몰아넣은 것에 대해 나에게 마땅히 화를 냈다."], ["Either her mother told her, or Rachel heard what passed—I can’t say which.", "그녀의 어머니가 그녀에게 말했든지, 아니면 레이철이 오간 말을 들었든지—어느 쪽인지 나는 말할 수 없다."], ["She took her own romantic, high-flown view of the matter.", "그녀는 그 일에 대해 제 나름의 낭만적이고 드높은 견해를 취했다."], ["I was “heartless”; I was “dishonourable”; I had “no principle”; there was “no knowing what I might do next”—in short, she said some of the severest things to me which I had ever heard from a young lady’s lips.", "나는 \"무정\"했고, 나는 \"명예를 모르는\" 사람이었고, 나는 \"지조가 없\"었으며, \"내가 다음에 무슨 짓을 할지 알 수 없\"었다—요컨대, 그녀는 젊은 아가씨의 입술에서 내가 여태 들은 가장 모진 말들을 나에게 했다."], ["The breach between us lasted for the whole of the next day.", "우리 사이의 그 틈은 이튿날 온종일 이어졌다."], ["The day after, I succeeded in making my peace, and thought no more of it.", "그다음 날, 나는 가까스로 화해를 이루었고, 그것을 더 마음에 두지 않았다."], ["Had Rachel reverted to this unlucky accident, at the critical moment when my place in her estimation was again, and far more seriously, assailed? Mr.", "나의 그녀의 평가 속의 자리가 다시, 그것도 훨씬 더 심각하게 공격받은 그 위태로운 순간에, 레이철이 이 불운한 사건으로 되돌아갔던 것일까?"], ["Bruff, when I had mentioned the circumstances to him, answered the question at once in the affirmative.", "브러프 씨는, 내가 그 정황을 그에게 이야기하자, 대뜸 그 물음에 그렇다고 답했다."]], [["“It would have its effect on her mind,”", "\"그것은 그녀의 마음에 영향을 미쳤을 거요,\""], ["he said gravely.", "하고 그가 무겁게 말했다."], ["“And I wish, for your sake, the thing had not happened.", "\"그리고 나는, 당신을 위해, 그 일이 일어나지 않았기를 바라오."], ["However, we have discovered that there was a predisposing influence against you—and there is one uncertainty cleared out of our way, at any rate.", "어쨌든, 우리는 정녕 당신에게 불리하게 미리 마음을 기울인 영향이 있었음을 알아냈소—그리고 어쨌든 한 가지 불확실함이 우리 길에서 치워졌소."], ["I see nothing more that we can do now.", "이제 우리가 할 수 있는 것이 더는 보이지 않소."], ["Our next step in this inquiry must be the step that takes us to Rachel.”", "이 조사에서 우리의 다음 걸음은 우리를 레이철에게로 데려가는 걸음이어야 하오.\""]], [["He rose, and began walking thoughtfully up and down the room.", "그는 일어서서, 생각에 잠긴 채 방 안을 오르내리며 걷기 시작했다."], ["Twice, I was on the point of telling him that I had determined on seeing Rachel personally; and twice, having regard to his age and his character, I hesitated to take him by surprise at an unfavourable moment.", "두 번, 나는 그에게 레이철을 직접 만나기로 마음먹었다고 말하려는 참이었다. 그리고 두 번, 그의 나이와 그의 성품을 헤아려, 나는 좋지 않은 순간에 그를 깜짝 놀라게 하기를 망설였다."]], [["“The grand difficulty is,”", "\"큰 어려움은,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he resumed, “how to make her show her whole mind in this matter, without reserve.", "\"그녀가 이 일에서 제 온 마음을 감춤 없이 드러내도록 어떻게 만드느냐 하는 것이오."], ["Have you any suggestions to offer?”", "당신은 내놓을 무슨 제안이 있소?\""]], [["“I have made up my mind, Mr. Bruff, to speak to Rachel myself.”", "\"나는 마음을 굳혔소, 브러프 씨, 내가 몸소 레이철에게 말하기로.\""]], [["“You!”", "\"당신이!\""], ["He suddenly stopped in his walk, and looked at me as if he thought I had taken leave of my senses.", "그는 별안간 걸음을 멈추고, 마치 내가 제정신을 놓았다고 여기는 듯 나를 바라보았다."], ["“You, of all the people in the world!”", "\"세상 온갖 사람 가운데 하필 당신이!\""], ["He abruptly checked himself, and took another turn in the room.", "그는 갑자기 스스로를 다잡고, 방 안을 한 바퀴 더 돌았다. \"잠깐만,\""], ["“Wait a little,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “In cases of this extraordinary kind, the rash way is sometimes the best way.”", "\"이런 유별난 종류의 경우에는, 무모한 방도가 때로 가장 좋은 방도이기도 하오.\""], ["He considered the question for a moment or two, under that new light, and ended boldly by a decision in my favour.", "그는 그 새로운 빛 아래에서 그 문제를 한두 순간 곰곰이 헤아리고는, 대담하게 나에게 유리한 결정으로 끝맺었다."], ["“Nothing venture, nothing have,”", "\"부딪쳐 보지 않으면 얻는 것도 없지,\""], ["the old gentleman resumed.", "하고 그 노신사가 말을 이었다."], ["“You have a chance in your favour which I don’t possess—and you shall be the first to try the experiment.”", "\"당신에게는 내가 지니지 못한, 당신에게 유리한 기회가 있소—그러니 당신이 먼저 그 실험을 해 보시오.\""]], [["“A chance in my favour?”", "\"나에게 유리한 기회라니요?\""], ["I repeated, in the greatest surprise.", "하고 나는 크나큰 놀라움에 되뇌었다."]], [["Mr. Bruff’s face softened, for the first time, into a smile.", "브러프 씨의 얼굴이, 처음으로, 미소로 부드러워졌다."]], [["“This is how it stands,”", "\"사정은 이렇소,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “I tell you fairly, I don’t trust your discretion, and I don’t trust your temper.", "\"당신에게 솔직히 말하는데, 나는 당신의 신중함을 믿지 않고, 당신의 성미를 믿지 않소."], ["But I do trust in Rachel’s still preserving, in some remote little corner of her heart, a certain perverse weakness for you.", "허나 나는, 레이철이 제 마음의 어느 외진 자그마한 구석에 여전히 당신에 대한 어떤 삐딱한 약함을 간직하고 있음은 믿소."], ["Touch that—and trust to the consequences for the fullest disclosures that can flow from a woman’s lips!", "그것을 건드리시오—그리고 여인의 입술에서 흘러나올 수 있는 가장 온전한 털어놓음을 그 결과에 맡기시오!"], ["The question is—how are you to see her?”", "문제는—어떻게 그녀를 만나느냐 하는 것이오.\""]], [["“She has been a guest of yours at this house,”", "\"그녀는 이 집에서 당신의 손님이었소,\" 하고 나는 대답했다."], ["I answered. “May I venture to suggest—if nothing was said about me beforehand—that I might see her here?”", "\"감히 제안해도 되겠소—만일 미리 나에 대해 아무 말도 없다면—내가 여기서 그녀를 만날 수 있으리라는 것을?\""]], [["“Cool!”", "\"뻔뻔하군!\""], ["said Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["With that one word of comment on the reply that I had made to him, he took another turn up and down the room.", "내가 그에게 한 그 대답에 대한 그 한마디 논평과 함께, 그는 방 안을 한 바퀴 더 오르내렸다."]], [["“In plain English,”", "\"쉬운 말로 하면,\""], ["he said, “my house is to be turned into a trap to catch Rachel; with a bait to tempt her, in the shape of an invitation from my wife and daughters.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"내 집이 레이철을 잡는 덫으로 바뀌는 것이오."], ["If you were anybody else but Franklin Blake, and if this matter was one atom less serious than it really is, I should refuse point-blank.", "그녀를 꾈 미끼로, 내 아내와 딸들의 초대라는 모양을 하고서 말이오. 만일 당신이 프랭클린 블레이크 아닌 다른 누구였다면, 그리고 만일 이 일이 실제보다 티끌만큼이라도 덜 중대했다면, 나는 딱 잘라 거절했을 거요."], ["As things are, I firmly believe Rachel will live to thank me for turning traitor to her in my old age.", "사정이 사정인지라, 나는 레이철이 내가 늘그막에 그녀를 배신한 것에 대해 언젠가 나에게 고마워하며 살리라 굳게 믿소."], ["Consider me your accomplice.", "나를 당신의 공범으로 여기시오."], ["Rachel shall be asked to spend the day here; and you shall receive due notice of it.”", "레이철은 여기서 하루를 보내도록 청함을 받을 것이오. 그리고 당신은 그에 대한 마땅한 통지를 받을 것이오.\""]], [["“When?", "\"언제요?"], ["Tomorrow?”", "내일이오?\""]], [["“Tomorrow won’t give us time enough to get her answer.", "\"내일은 그녀의 답을 받을 만한 시간을 주지 못하오."], ["Say the day after.”", "모레라고 해 둡시다.\""]], [["“How shall I hear from you?”", "\"당신에게서 어떻게 소식을 듣소?\""]], [["“Stay at home all the morning and expect me to call on you.”", "\"오전 내내 집에 계시고, 내가 당신을 찾아오기를 기다리시오.\""]], [["I thanked him for the inestimable assistance which he was rendering to me, with the gratitude that I really felt; and, declining a hospitable invitation to sleep that night at Hampstead, returned to my lodgings in London.", "나는 그가 나에게 베풀고 있는 그 값을 헤아릴 수 없는 도움에 대해, 내가 참으로 느낀 고마움으로 그에게 감사했다. 그리고 그날 밤 햄스테드에서 자고 가라는 후한 초대를 사양하고, 런던의 내 하숙으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Of the day that followed, I have only to say that it was the longest day of my life.", "뒤이은 그날에 대해, 나는 그것이 내 생애에서 가장 긴 하루였다고 말할 수밖에 없다."], ["Innocent as I knew myself to be, certain as I was that the abominable imputation which rested on me must sooner or later be cleared off, there was nevertheless a sense of self-abasement in my mind which instinctively disinclined me to see any of my friends.", "내가 스스로 결백함을 알았고, 나에게 씌워진 그 가증스러운 혐의가 조만간 반드시 벗겨지리라 확신했건만, 그럼에도 나의 마음에는, 본능적으로 나로 하여금 나의 어떤 벗도 만나고 싶지 않게 만드는 자기 비하의 느낌이 있었다."], ["We often hear (almost invariably, however, from superficial observers) that guilt can look like innocence.", "우리는 죄가 결백처럼 보일 수 있다는 말을 흔히 듣는다 (허나, 거의 어김없이, 겉만 보는 이들에게서다)."], ["I believe it to be infinitely the truer axiom of the two that innocence can look like guilt.", "나는 그 둘 가운데, 결백이 죄처럼 보일 수 있다는 것이 한없이 더 참된 격언이라 믿는다."], ["I caused myself to be denied all day, to every visitor who called; and I only ventured out under cover of the night.", "나는 온종일, 찾아오는 온갖 방문객에게 없다는 말을 하게 했다. 그리고 나는 오직 밤의 장막 아래에서만 감히 밖에 나갔다."]], [["The next morning, Mr. Bruff surprised me at the breakfast-table.", "이튿날 아침, 브러프 씨가 아침 식탁에서 나를 깜짝 놀라게 했다."], ["He handed me a large key, and announced that he felt ashamed of himself for the first time in his life.", "그는 나에게 큼직한 열쇠 하나를 건네며, 제 생애에서 처음으로 스스로가 부끄럽게 느껴진다고 알렸다."]], [["“Is she coming?”", "\"그녀가 오는 것이오?\""]], [["“She is coming today, to lunch and spend the afternoon with my wife and my girls.”", "\"그녀는 오늘 오오, 점심을 들고 내 아내와 내 딸들과 함께 오후를 보내려고.\""]], [["“Are Mrs. Bruff, and your daughters, in the secret?”", "\"브러프 부인과 당신의 딸들은 그 비밀을 아오?\""]], [["“Inevitably.", "\"불가피하게도."], ["But women, as you may have observed, have no principles.", "허나 당신도 알아챘겠지만, 여자들은 지조가 없소."], ["My family don’t feel my pangs of conscience.", "내 식구들은 내가 느끼는 양심의 아릿함을 느끼지 않소."], ["The end being to bring you and Rachel together again, my wife and daughters pass over the means employed to gain it, as composedly as if they were Jesuits.”", "당신과 레이철을 다시 함께 맺어 주는 것이 목적이니, 내 아내와 딸들은 그것을 이루려 쓰이는 수단을, 마치 예수회 수사라도 되는 양 태연히 눈감아 버리오.\""]], [["“I am infinitely obliged to them.", "\"그들에게 한없이 고맙소."], ["What is this key?”", "이 열쇠는 무엇이오?\""]], [["“The key of the gate in my back-garden wall.", "\"내 뒷마당 담의 문 열쇠요."], ["Be there at three this afternoon.", "오늘 오후 세 시에 거기 가 계시오."], ["Let yourself into the garden, and make your way in by the conservatory door.", "그 문으로 마당에 드시어, 온실 문으로 안에 들어가시오."], ["Cross the small drawing-room, and open the door in front of you which leads into the music-room.", "작은 응접실을 가로질러, 당신 앞의, 음악실로 이어지는 문을 여시오."], ["There, you will find Rachel—and find her, alone.”", "거기서 당신은 레이철을 보게 될 것이오—그리고 그녀를, 홀로 있는 그녀를 보게 될 것이오.\""]], [["“How can I thank you!”", "\"어찌 당신에게 감사하리까!\""]], [["“I will tell you how.", "\"어찌 감사할지 내가 말해 주겠소."], ["Don’t blame me for what happens afterwards.”", "그다음에 일어나는 일에 대해 나를 탓하지 마시오.\""]], [["With those words, he went out.", "그 말과 함께, 그는 나갔다."]], [["I had many weary hours still to wait through.", "나는 아직 지새워야 할 고달픈 시간이 많았다."], ["To while away the time, I looked at my letters.", "시간을 때우려, 나는 내 편지들을 보았다."], ["Among them was a letter from Betteredge.", "그중에 베터리지에게서 온 편지가 있었다."]], [["I opened it eagerly.", "나는 그것을 간절히 뜯었다."], ["To my surprise and disappointment, it began with an apology warning me to expect no news of any importance.", "나의 놀라움과 실망으로, 그것은 아무런 중요한 소식도 기대하지 말라 미리 이르는 사과로 시작했다."], ["In the next sentence the everlasting Ezra Jennings appeared again!", "그다음 문장에서 그 지긋지긋한 에즈라 제닝스가 다시 나타났다!"], ["He had stopped Betteredge on the way out of the station, and had asked who I was.", "그는 역을 나서는 길에 베터리지를 붙들어, 내가 누구냐고 물었던 것이다."], ["Informed on this point, he had mentioned having seen me to his master Mr. Candy.", "이 점에 대해 알게 되자, 그는 자기 주인인 캔디 씨에게 나를 본 것을 언급했다."], ["Mr. Candy hearing of this, had himself driven over to Betteredge, to express his regret at our having missed each other.", "이것을 들은 캔디 씨는, 우리가 서로 엇갈린 것에 대한 유감을 나타내려, 몸소 베터리지에게 마차를 몰고 왔다."], ["He had a reason for wishing particularly to speak to me; and when I was next in the neighbourhood of Frizinghall, he begged I would let him know.", "그는 각별히 나에게 이야기하고 싶은 이유가 있었다. 그리고 내가 다음에 프리징홀 근방에 오게 되면, 자기에게 알려 주기를 청했다."], ["Apart from a few characteristic utterances of the Betteredge philosophy, this was the sum and substance of my correspondent’s letter.", "베터리지 철학의 몇몇 특유한 발언들 말고는, 이것이 나의 편지 상대의 편지의 알맹이의 전부였다."], ["The warm-hearted, faithful old man acknowledged that he had written “mainly for the pleasure of writing to me.”", "그 따뜻한 마음씨의, 충직한 노인은 자기가 \"주로 나에게 편지하는 즐거움 때문에\" 그것을 썼음을 인정했다."]], [["I crumpled up the letter in my pocket, and forgot it the moment after, in the all-absorbing interest of my coming interview with Rachel.", "나는 그 편지를 주머니에 구겨 넣고, 곧이어 레이철과의 다가올 면담에 대한 온통 마음을 사로잡는 관심 속에, 그것을 잊어버렸다."]], [["As the clock of Hampstead church struck three, I put Mr. Bruff’s key into the lock of the door in the wall.", "햄스테드 교회의 시계가 세 시를 칠 때, 나는 그 담의 문 자물쇠에 브러프 씨의 열쇠를 넣었다."], ["When I first stepped into the garden, and while I was securing the door again on the inner side, I own to having felt a certain guilty doubtfulness about what might happen next.", "내가 처음 그 마당으로 발을 들이고, 안쪽에서 다시 그 문을 잠그는 동안, 나는 그다음에 무슨 일이 일어날지에 대해 어떤 죄지은 듯한 미심쩍음을 느꼈음을 인정한다."], ["I looked furtively on either side of me; suspicious of the presence of some unexpected witness in some unknown corner of the garden.", "나는 내 양옆을 몰래 흘긋거렸다. 그 마당의 어느 알 수 없는 구석에, 뜻밖의 어떤 증인이 있지 않을까 미심쩍어하면서."], ["Nothing appeared, to justify my apprehensions.", "나의 염려를 정당화해 줄 것은 아무것도 나타나지 않았다."], ["The walks were, one and all, solitudes; and the birds and the bees were the only witnesses.", "그 길들은, 하나같이 죄, 인적 없었다. 그리고 새들과 벌들만이 유일한 증인이었다."]], [["I passed through the garden; entered the conservatory; and crossed the small drawing-room.", "나는 그 마당을 지나, 온실에 들어, 작은 응접실을 가로질렀다."], ["As I laid my hand on the door opposite, I heard a few plaintive chords struck on the piano in the room within.", "내가 맞은편 문에 손을 얹었을 때, 나는 안쪽 방의 피아노에서 몇몇 애처로운 화음이 울리는 것을 들었다."], ["She had often idled over the instrument in this way, when I was staying at her mother’s house.", "그녀는 내가 그녀의 어머니의 집에 머물 때, 이런 식으로 그 악기를 만지작거리며 자주 시간을 보내곤 했다."], ["I was obliged to wait a little, to steady myself.", "나는 나를 다잡으려, 잠시 기다리지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The past and present rose side by side, at that supreme moment—and the contrast shook me.", "그 지고한 순간에, 과거와 현재가 나란히 솟아올랐다—그리고 그 대비가 나를 뒤흔들었다."]], [["After the lapse of a minute, I roused my manhood, and opened the door.", "일 분이 지난 뒤, 나는 나의 사내다움을 일깨워, 그 문을 열었다."]], [["# CHAPTER VII", "# CHAPTER VII"]], [["At the moment when I showed myself in the doorway, Rachel rose from the piano.", "내가 그 문간에 모습을 드러낸 그 순간, 레이철이 피아노에서 일어섰다."]], [["I closed the door behind me.", "나는 내 뒤의 문을 닫았다."], ["We confronted each other in silence, with the full length of the room between us.", "우리는, 방의 온 길이를 사이에 두고, 말없이 서로를 마주했다."], ["The movement she had made in rising appeared to be the one exertion of which she was capable.", "그녀가 일어서며 한 그 동작이, 그녀가 할 수 있는 유일한 안간힘인 듯했다."], ["All use of every other faculty, bodily or mental, seemed to be merged in the mere act of looking at me.", "몸이든 마음이든, 다른 온갖 능력의 온갖 쓰임이, 그저 나를 바라보는 행위 하나에 녹아든 것 같았다."]], [["A fear crossed my mind that I had shown myself too suddenly.", "내가 너무 느닷없이 모습을 드러낸 것은 아닌가 하는 두려움이 내 마음을 스쳤다."], ["I advanced a few steps towards her.", "나는 그녀를 향해 몇 걸음 나아갔다."], ["I said gently, “Rachel!”", "나는 부드럽게 말했다, \"레이철!\""]], [["The sound of my voice brought the life back to her limbs, and the colour to her face.", "내 목소리의 울림이 그녀의 팔다리에 생기를, 그녀의 얼굴에 핏기를 되돌려 놓았다. 그녀는, 제 편에서, 여전히 말없이 나아왔다."], ["She advanced, on her side, still without speaking.", "천천히, 마치 제 뜻과는 별개의 어떤 영향 아래 움직이는 듯, 그녀는 나에게 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다."], ["Slowly, as if acting under some influence independent of her own will, she came nearer and nearer to me; the warm dusky colour flushing her cheeks, the light of reviving intelligence brightening every instant in her eyes.", "따스한 거뭇한 홍조가 그녀의 두 뺨을 붉혔고, 되살아나는 지각의 빛이 순간순간 그녀의 눈 속에서 밝아졌다. 나는 나를 그녀 앞에 데려온 그 목적을 잊었다."], ["I forgot the object that had brought me into her presence; I forgot the vile suspicion that rested on my good name; I forgot every consideration, past, present, and future, which I was bound to remember.", "나는 나의 명예에 얹힌 그 비열한 의심을 잊었다. 나는 내가 마땅히 기억해야 할 온갖 사려를, 과거의 것도, 현재의 것도, 미래의 것도 잊었다."], ["I saw nothing but the woman I loved coming nearer and nearer to me.", "나는 내가 사랑하는 그 여인이 나에게 점점 더 가까이 다가오는 것 말고는 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["She trembled; she stood irresolute.", "그녀는 떨었다. 그녀는 망설이며 섰다."], ["I could resist it no longer—I caught her in my arms, and covered her face with kisses.", "나는 더는 그것을 견딜 수 없었다—나는 그녀를 내 품에 끌어안고, 그녀의 얼굴을 입맞춤으로 뒤덮었다."]], [["There was a moment when I thought the kisses were returned; a moment when it seemed as if she, too, might have forgotten.", "그 입맞춤이 되돌려지는 것 같은 순간이 있었다. 그녀도, 잊었을지 모른다고 여겨지는 순간이."], ["Almost before the idea could shape itself in my mind, her first voluntary action made me feel that she remembered.", "그 생각이 내 마음속에서 미처 꼴을 갖추기도 전에, 그녀의 첫 자발적인 행동이 나로 하여금 그녀가 기억하고 있음을 느끼게 했다."], ["With a cry which was like a cry of horror—with a strength which I doubt if I could have resisted if I had tried—she thrust me back from her.", "공포의 외침 같은 외침과 함께—내가 애를 썼더라도 저항할 수 있었을지 의심스러운 힘과 함께—그녀는 나를 제게서 밀쳐 냈다."], ["I saw merciless anger in her eyes; I saw merciless contempt on her lips.", "나는 그녀의 눈에서 인정사정없는 노여움을 보았다. 나는 그녀의 입술에서 인정사정없는 경멸을 보았다."], ["She looked me over, from head to foot, as she might have looked at a stranger who had insulted her.", "그녀는, 마치 자기를 모욕한 낯선 이를 훑어보듯, 나를 머리끝부터 발끝까지 훑어보았다."]], [["“You coward!”", "\"이 비겁한 사람!\""], ["she said.", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["“You mean, miserable, heartless coward!”", "\"이 치사하고, 비참하고, 무정한 비겁한 사람!\""]], [["Those were her first words!", "그것이 그녀의 첫마디였다!"], ["The most unendurable reproach that a woman can address to a man, was the reproach that she picked out to address to Me.", "여자가 남자에게 던질 수 있는 가장 견디기 어려운 꾸짖음이, 그녀가 나에게 던지려 골라낸 그 꾸짖음이었다."]], [["“I remember the time, Rachel,”", "\"나는 그때를 기억하오, 레이철,\""], ["I said, “when you could have told me that I had offended you, in a worthier way than that.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"당신이 나에게, 내가 당신의 마음을 상하게 했다고, 그것보다 더 값진 방식으로 말해 줄 수 있었던 그때를 말이오."], ["I beg your pardon.”", "용서하시오.\""]], [["Something of the bitterness that I felt may have communicated itself to my voice.", "내가 느낀 쓰라림의 얼마쯤이 내 목소리에 옮아 갔을지도 모른다."], ["At the first words of my reply, her eyes, which had been turned away the moment before, looked back at me unwillingly.", "내 대답의 첫마디에, 조금 전 딴 데로 돌려져 있던 그녀의 눈이, 마지못해 나를 되돌아보았다."], ["She answered in a low tone, with a sullen submission of manner which was quite new in my experience of her.", "그녀는, 내가 그녀를 겪은 중에 아주 새로운, 부루퉁하게 순응하는 태도로, 낮은 어조로 대답했다."]], [["“Perhaps there is some excuse for me,” she said.", "\"어쩌면 저에게도 얼마쯤 변명거리가 있을지 몰라요,\""], ["“After what you have done, is it a manly action, on your part, to find your way to me as you have found it today?", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신이 한 일을 겪고서, 오늘 당신이 찾아온 그런 식으로 저에게 오는 것이, 당신 편에서 사내다운 짓인가요?"], ["It seems a cowardly experiment, to try an experiment on my weakness for you.", "당신에 대한 저의 약함을 시험하려 실험을 하다니, 그것은 비겁한 실험 같아요."], ["It seems a cowardly surprise, to surprise me into letting you kiss me.", "저를 깜짝 놀라게 하여 당신이 입 맞추도록 두게 만들다니, 그것은 비겁한 기습 같아요."], ["But that is only a woman’s view.", "허나 그것은 그저 한 여자의 견해일 뿐이지요."], ["I ought to have known it couldn’t be your view.", "그것이 당신의 견해일 수 없음을 저는 알았어야 했어요."], ["I should have done better if I had controlled myself, and said nothing.”", "제가 저를 다잡고 아무 말도 하지 않았더라면 더 나았을 거예요.\""]], [["The apology was more unendurable than the insult.", "그 사과는 그 모욕보다 더 견디기 어려웠다."], ["The most degraded man living would have felt humiliated by it.", "살아 있는 가장 비천한 사람이라도 그것에 굴욕을 느꼈으리라."]], [["“If my honour was not in your hands,”", "\"만일 나의 명예가 당신 손에 있지 않다면,\""], ["I said, “I would leave you this instant, and never see you again.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 이 순간 당신을 떠나, 다시는 당신을 보지 않을 것이오."], ["You have spoken of what I have done.", "당신은 내가 한 일에 대해 말했소."], ["What have I done?”", "내가 무엇을 했소?\""]], [["“What have you done!", "\"당신이 무엇을 했느냐고요!"], ["You ask that question of me?”", "당신이 그 물음을 저에게 하는 건가요?\""]], [["“I ask it.”", "\"내가 그것을 묻소.\""]], [["“I have kept your infamy a secret,” she answered.", "\"저는 당신의 그 파렴치함을 비밀로 지켜 왔어요,\""], ["“And I have suffered the consequences of concealing it.", "하고 그녀가 대답했다. \"그리고 저는 그것을 감춘 대가를 치러 왔어요."], ["Have I no claim to be spared the insult of your asking me what you have done?", "당신이 한 일이 무엇이냐고 저에게 묻는 그 모욕만은 면할 자격이 저에게 없단 말인가요?"], ["Is all sense of gratitude dead in you?", "온갖 고마움의 마음이 당신 안에서 다 죽어 버린 건가요?"], ["You were once a gentleman.", "당신은 한때 신사였어요."], ["You were once dear to my mother, and dearer still to me——”", "당신은 한때 저의 어머니께 소중한 사람이었고, 저에게는 더욱더 소중한 사람이었어요——\""]], [["Her voice failed her.", "그녀의 목소리가 그녀를 저버렸다."], ["She dropped into a chair, and turned her back on me, and covered her face with her hands.", "그녀는 의자에 털썩 주저앉아, 나에게 등을 돌리고, 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."]], [["I waited a little before I trusted myself to say any more.", "나는 더 무슨 말을 할 만큼 나 자신을 믿기 전에 잠시 기다렸다."], ["In that moment of silence, I hardly know which I felt most keenly—the sting which her contempt had planted in me, or the proud resolution which shut me out from all community with her distress.", "그 침묵의 순간에, 나는 어느 쪽을 가장 날카롭게 느꼈는지 좀처럼 알 수 없다—그녀의 경멸이 내 안에 심어 놓은 그 가시인지, 아니면 나를 그녀의 괴로움과의 온갖 교감에서 몰아낸 그 오만한 결의인지."]], [["“If you will not speak first,”", "\"만일 당신이 먼저 말하지 않겠다면,\""], ["I said, “I must.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["I have come here with something serious to say to you.", "\"내가 말해야겠소. 나는 당신에게 할 중대한 이야기를 지니고 여기 왔소."], ["Will you do me the common justice of listening while I say it?”", "내가 그것을 말하는 동안 들어 주는, 그 예사로운 도리라도 나에게 베풀어 주겠소?\""]], [["She neither moved, nor answered.", "그녀는 움직이지도, 대답하지도 않았다."], ["I made no second appeal to her; I never advanced an inch nearer to her chair.", "나는 그녀에게 두 번 청하지 않았다. 나는 그녀의 의자로 한 치도 더 다가가지 않았다."], ["With a pride which was as obstinate as her pride, I told her of my discovery at the Shivering Sand, and of all that had led to it.", "그녀의 오만만큼이나 고집스러운 오만을 안고, 나는 그녀에게 흔들리는 모래에서의 나의 발견과, 그것에 이르게 한 온갖 것을 이야기했다."], ["The narrative, of necessity, occupied some little time.", "그 이야기는, 필연적으로, 얼마쯤의 시간이 걸렸다."], ["From beginning to end, she never looked round at me, and she never uttered a word.", "처음부터 끝까지, 그녀는 결코 나를 돌아보지 않았고, 결코 한마디도 입에 담지 않았다."]], [["I kept my temper.", "나는 성미를 다스렸다."], ["My whole future depended, in all probability, on my not losing possession of myself at that moment.", "나의 온 앞날은, 십중팔구, 그 순간 내가 나 자신을 잃지 않는 데에 달려 있었다."], ["The time had come to put Mr. Bruff’s theory to the test.", "브러프 씨의 이론을 시험대에 올릴 때가 온 것이다."], ["In the breathless interest of trying that experiment, I moved round so as to place myself in front of her.", "그 실험을 해 보려는 숨죽인 관심 속에, 나는 나 자신을 그녀 앞에 두려 빙 돌아 움직였다."]], [["“I have a question to ask you,”", "\"당신에게 물을 것이 있소,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said. “It obliges me to refer again to a painful subject.", "\"그것은 내가 다시 괴로운 화제를 들추지 않을 수 없게 하오."], ["Did Rosanna Spearman show you the nightgown?", "로재나 스피어먼이 당신에게 그 잠옷을 보여 주었소?"], ["Yes, or No?”", "그렇소, 아니오?\""]], [["She started to her feet; and walked close up to me of her own accord.", "그녀는 벌떡 일어섰다. 그리고 제 발로 나에게 바싹 다가와 걸어왔다."], ["Her eyes looked me searchingly in the face, as if to read something there which they had never read yet.", "그녀의 눈이, 마치 여태 읽어 본 적 없는 무언가를 거기서 읽으려는 듯, 나의 얼굴을 뚫어지게 들여다보았다."]], [["“Are you mad?”", "\"당신 미쳤어요?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["I still restrained myself.", "나는 여전히 나를 억눌렀다."], ["I said quietly, “Rachel, will you answer my question?”", "나는 조용히 말했다, \"레이철, 내 물음에 답해 주겠소?\""]], [["She went on, without heeding me.", "그녀는, 나에게 아랑곳없이, 말을 이어 갔다."]], [["“Have you some object to gain which I don’t understand?", "\"제가 이해하지 못하는 무슨 얻을 목적이 있나요?"], ["Some mean fear about the future, in which I am concerned?", "제가 관련된, 미래에 대한 무슨 치사한 두려움이?"], ["They say your father’s death has made you a rich man.", "당신 아버지의 죽음이 당신을 부자로 만들었다더군요."], ["Have you come here to compensate me for the loss of my Diamond?", "당신은 저의 다이아몬드의 손실을 저에게 보상해 주려고 여기 온 건가요?"], ["And have you heart enough left to feel ashamed of your errand?", "그리고 당신은 당신의 볼일이 부끄럽다고 느낄 만큼의 마음이 아직 남아 있나요?"], ["Is that the secret of your pretence of innocence, and your story about Rosanna Spearman?", "그것이 당신의 결백한 척과, 로재나 스피어먼에 대한 당신의 이야기의 비밀인가요?"], ["Is there a motive of shame at the bottom of all the falsehood, this time?”", "이번에는, 그 온갖 거짓의 밑바탕에 부끄러움의 동기가 있는 건가요?\""]], [["I stopped her there.", "나는 거기서 그녀를 멈추게 했다."], ["I could control myself no longer.", "나는 더는 나를 다스릴 수 없었다."]], [["“You have done me an infamous wrong!”", "\"당신은 나에게 파렴치한 잘못을 저질렀소!\""], ["I broke out hotly.", "하고 나는 격하게 터뜨렸다."], ["“You suspect me of stealing your Diamond.", "\"당신은 내가 당신의 다이아몬드를 훔쳤다고 의심하고 있소."], ["I have a right to know, and I will know, the reason why!”", "나는 그 까닭을 알 권리가 있고, 나는 기어이 알고야 말겠소!\""]], [["“Suspect you!”", "\"당신을 의심한다고요!\""], ["she exclaimed, her anger rising with mine.", "하고 그녀가 외쳤다, 그녀의 노여움이 나의 노여움과 함께 치솟으며."], ["“You villain, I saw you take the Diamond with my own eyes!”", "\"이 악당아, 나는 내 두 눈으로 당신이 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 보았어요!\""]], [["The revelation which burst upon me in those words, the overthrow which they instantly accomplished of the whole view of the case on which Mr. Bruff had relied, struck me helpless.", "그 말과 함께 나에게 터져 나온 그 폭로, 그것들이 브러프 씨가 기대었던 그 온 사건관을 대뜸 뒤엎어 버린 그 전복이, 나를 속수무책으로 때렸다."], ["Innocent as I was, I stood before her in silence.", "결백했건만, 나는 그녀 앞에 말없이 서 있었다."], ["To her eyes, to any eyes, I must have looked like a man overwhelmed by the discovery of his own guilt.", "그녀의 눈에는, 어떤 눈에든, 나는 제 자신의 죄의 발각에 압도된 사내처럼 보였음이 틀림없다."]], [["She drew back from the spectacle of my humiliation and of her triumph.", "그녀는 나의 굴욕과 그녀의 승리의 그 광경에서 물러났다."], ["The sudden silence that had fallen upon me seemed to frighten her.", "나에게 내려앉은 그 별안간의 침묵이 그녀를 겁먹게 하는 듯했다. \"저는 그때 당신을 봐줬어요,\""], ["“I spared you, at the time,”", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “I would have spared you now, if you had not forced me to speak.”", "\"만일 당신이 저더러 말하도록 몰아붙이지만 않았다면, 저는 지금도 당신을 봐주었을 거예요.\""], ["She moved away as if to leave the room—and hesitated before she got to the door.", "그녀는 마치 방을 나가려는 듯 물러났다—그리고 문에 다다르기 전에 망설였다."], ["“Why did you come here to humiliate yourself?”", "\"어째서 당신은 여기 와서 당신 자신을 욕되게 하나요?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 그녀가 물었다."], ["“Why did you come here to humiliate me?”", "\"어째서 당신은 여기 와서 저를 욕되게 하나요?\""], ["She went on a few steps, and paused once more.", "그녀는 몇 걸음 더 가더니, 다시 한 번 멈춰 섰다."], ["“For God’s sake, say something!”", "\"제발, 무슨 말이든 좀 해요!\""], ["she exclaimed, passionately.", "하고 그녀가 격정에 차 외쳤다."], ["“If you have any mercy left, don’t let me degrade myself in this way!", "\"당신에게 자비가 조금이라도 남았다면, 제가 이런 식으로 저 자신을 깎아내리게 두지 말아요!"], ["Say something—and drive me out of the room!”", "무슨 말이든 해요—그리고 저를 이 방에서 몰아내요!\""]], [["I advanced towards her, hardly conscious of what I was doing.", "나는, 내가 무엇을 하는지도 거의 자각하지 못한 채, 그녀를 향해 나아갔다."], ["I had possibly some confused idea of detaining her until she had told me more.", "나는 아마도, 그녀가 나에게 더 말해 줄 때까지 그녀를 붙들어 두려는 어떤 어렴풋한 생각을 품었던 것 같다."], ["From the moment when I knew that the evidence on which I stood condemned in Rachel’s mind, was the evidence of her own eyes, nothing—not even my conviction of my own innocence—was clear to my mind.", "레이철의 마음속에서 나를 단죄하는 그 증거가 그녀 자신의 눈의 증거임을 안 그 순간부터, 아무것도—심지어 나 자신의 결백에 대한 나의 확신조차—내 마음에 또렷하지 않았다."], ["I took her by the hand; I tried to speak firmly and to the purpose.", "나는 그녀의 손을 잡았다. 나는 굳게, 그리고 요점에 맞게 말하려 애썼다."], ["All I could say was, “Rachel, you once loved me.”", "내가 할 수 있었던 말은 오직, \"레이철, 당신은 한때 나를 사랑했소,\" 하는 것뿐이었다."]], [["She shuddered, and looked away from me.", "그녀는 몸서리치며, 나에게서 눈을 돌렸다."], ["Her hand lay powerless and trembling in mine.", "그녀의 손이 힘없이, 떨며 내 손 안에 놓여 있었다."], ["“Let go of it,”", "\"그것을 놓아요,\""], ["she said faintly.", "하고 그녀가 가냘프게 말했다."]], [["My touch seemed to have the same effect on her which the sound of my voice had produced when I first entered the room.", "나의 손길은, 내가 처음 그 방에 들어섰을 때 내 목소리의 울림이 자아냈던 것과 같은 효과를 그녀에게 미치는 듯했다."], ["After she had said the word which called me a coward, after she had made the avowal which branded me as a thief—while her hand lay in mine I was her master still!", "나를 비겁한 사람이라 부른 그 말을 하고 나서도, 나를 도둑으로 낙인찍은 그 폭로를 하고 나서도—그녀의 손이 내 손 안에 놓여 있는 동안 나는 여전히 그녀의 주인이었다!"]], [["I drew her gently back into the middle of the room.", "나는 그녀를 부드럽게 방 한가운데로 도로 이끌었다."], ["I seated her by the side of me.", "나는 그녀를 내 곁에 앉혔다."], ["“Rachel,”", "\"레이철,\""], ["I said, “I can’t explain the contradiction in what I am going to tell you.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 하려는 말 속의 그 모순을 나는 설명할 수 없소."], ["I can only speak the truth as you have spoken it.", "나는 다만 당신이 진실을 말한 대로 진실을 말할 수 있을 뿐이오."], ["You saw me—with your own eyes, you saw me take the Diamond.", "당신은 나를 보았소—당신 자신의 눈으로, 당신은 내가 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 보았소."], ["Before God who hears us, I declare that I now know I took it for the first time!", "우리 말을 듣고 계신 하느님 앞에서, 나는 선언하오, 내가 그것을 가져갔음을 지금 처음으로 안다는 것을!"], ["Do you doubt me still?”", "당신은 아직도 나를 의심하오?\""]], [["She had neither heeded nor heard me.", "그녀는 나에게 아랑곳하지도, 나를 듣지도 않았다."], ["“Let go of my hand,”", "\"내 손을 놓아요,\""], ["she repeated faintly.", "하고 그녀가 가냘프게 되뇌었다."], ["That was her only answer.", "그것이 그녀의 유일한 대답이었다."], ["Her head sank on my shoulder; and her hand unconsciously closed on mine, at the moment when she asked me to release it.", "그녀의 머리가 내 어깨에 내려앉았다. 그리고 그녀의 손은, 나에게 그것을 놓아 달라고 청하는 그 순간에, 나도 모르게 내 손을 꼭 쥐었다."]], [["I refrained from pressing the question.", "나는 그 물음을 다그치기를 삼갔다."], ["But there my forbearance stopped.", "허나 나의 참음은 거기서 그쳤다."], ["My chance of ever holding up my head again among honest men depended on my chance of inducing her to make her disclosure complete.", "다시 정직한 사람들 사이에서 고개를 들 나의 가망은, 그녀가 그 털어놓음을 온전히 하도록 이끌 나의 가망에 달려 있었다."], ["The one hope left for me was the hope that she might have overlooked something in the chain of evidence—some mere trifle, perhaps, which might nevertheless, under careful investigation, be made the means of vindicating my innocence in the end.", "나에게 남은 유일한 희망은, 그녀가 그 증거의 사슬에서 무언가를—어쩌면 한낱 사소한 것을, 그럼에도 꼼꼼히 살피면 끝내 나의 결백을 밝히는 수단이 될 수 있을 무언가를 놓쳤을지도 모른다는 희망이었다."], ["I own I kept possession of her hand.", "나는 인정한다, 내가 그녀의 손을 계속 쥐고 있었음을."], ["I own I spoke to her with all that I could summon back of the sympathy and confidence of the bygone time.", "나는 인정한다, 내가 지난 시절의 교감과 믿음 중에서 그러모을 수 있는 온갖 것을 담아 그녀에게 말했음을."]], [["“I want to ask you something,” I said.", "\"당신에게 뭔가 묻고 싶소,\""], ["“I want you to tell me everything that happened, from the time when we wished each other good-night, to the time when you saw me take the Diamond.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 서로 잘 자라 인사한 그때부터, 당신이 내가 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 본 그때까지, 일어난 모든 것을 나에게 말해 주었으면 하오.\""]], [["She lifted her head from my shoulder, and made an effort to release her hand.", "그녀는 내 어깨에서 머리를 들고, 제 손을 빼려 애썼다. \"오, 어째서 그것으로 돌아가나요!\""], ["“Oh, why go back to it!”", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “Why go back to it!”", "\"어째서 그것으로 돌아가나요!\""]], [["“I will tell you why, Rachel.", "\"어째서인지 내가 말해 주겠소, 레이철."], ["You are the victim, and I am the victim, of some monstrous delusion which has worn the mask of truth.", "당신은 희생자요, 나도 희생자요, 진실의 가면을 쓴 어떤 괴물 같은 착각의."], ["If we look at what happened on the night of your birthday together, we may end in understanding each other yet.”", "만일 우리가 당신의 생일 밤에 일어난 일을 함께 본다면, 우리는 아직 서로 이해하는 것으로 끝날 수 있을지도 모르오.\""]], [["Her head dropped back on my shoulder.", "그녀의 머리가 내 어깨에 도로 내려앉았다."], ["The tears gathered in her eyes, and fell slowly over her cheeks.", "눈물이 그녀의 눈에 고여, 천천히 그녀의 뺨 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["“Oh!”", "\"오!\""], ["she said, “have I never had that hope?", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그 희망을 한 번도 품지 않았을 것 같아요?"], ["Have I not tried to see it, as you are trying now?”", "지금 당신이 애쓰듯, 제가 그것을 보려 애쓰지 않았을 것 같아요?\""]], [["“You have tried by yourself,”", "\"당신은 혼자서 애썼소,\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“You have not tried with me to help you.”", "\"당신은 당신을 도울 나와 함께 애쓰지는 않았소.\""]], [["Those words seemed to awaken in her something of the hope which I felt myself when I uttered them.", "그 말은, 내가 그것을 입에 담을 때 나 자신이 느낀 그 희망의 얼마쯤을 그녀 안에서 일깨우는 듯했다."], ["She replied to my questions with more than docility—she exerted her intelligence; she willingly opened her whole mind to me.", "그녀는 나의 물음에 고분고분함 이상으로 답했다—그녀는 제 지각을 발휘했다. 그녀는 기꺼이 제 온 마음을 나에게 열었다."]], [["“Let us begin,”", "\"시작합시다,\""], ["I said, “with what happened after we had wished each other good-night.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 서로 잘 자라 인사하고 나서 일어난 일부터."], ["Did you go to bed?", "당신은 잠자리에 들었소?"], ["or did you sit up?”", "아니면 앉아 있었소?\""]], [["“I went to bed.”", "\"저는 잠자리에 들었어요.\""]], [["“Did you notice the time?", "\"시각을 눈여겨보았소?"], ["Was it late?”", "늦었소?\""]], [["“Not very.", "\"아주 늦지는 않았어요."], ["About twelve o’clock, I think.”", "열두 시쯤이었던 것 같아요.\""]], [["“Did you fall asleep?”", "\"잠이 들었소?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["I couldn’t sleep that night.”", "그날 밤 저는 잠들 수 없었어요.\""]], [["“You were restless?”", "\"뒤척였소?\""]], [["“I was thinking of you.”", "\"당신을 생각하고 있었어요.\""]], [["The answer almost unmanned me.", "그 대답은 나를 거의 무너뜨렸다."], ["Something in the tone, even more than in the words, went straight to my heart.", "그 말보다도 더, 그 어조 속의 무언가가 곧장 내 가슴에 와닿았다."], ["It was only after pausing a little first that I was able to go on.", "나는 먼저 잠시 멈추고 나서야 비로소 계속할 수 있었다."]], [["“Had you any light in your room?”", "\"당신 방에 무슨 불이 있었소?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“None—until I got up again, and lit my candle.”", "\"없었어요—제가 다시 일어나 초에 불을 붙이기 전까지는.\""]], [["“How long was that, after you had gone to bed?”", "\"그것은, 당신이 잠자리에 든 지 얼마 뒤였소?\""]], [["“About an hour after, I think.", "\"한 시간쯤 뒤였던 것 같아요."], ["About one o’clock.”", "한 시쯤.\""]], [["“Did you leave your bedroom?”", "\"당신은 침실을 떠났소?\""]], [["“I was going to leave it.", "\"떠나려던 참이었어요."], ["I had put on my dressing-gown; and I was going into my sitting-room to get a book——”", "저는 실내복을 걸쳤고, 책을 가지러 응접실로 들어가려던 참이었어요——\""]], [["“Had you opened your bedroom door?”", "\"침실 문을 열었소?\""]], [["“I had just opened it.”", "\"막 열었어요.\""]], [["“But you had not gone into the sitting-room?”", "\"허나 응접실로는 들어가지 않았소?\""]], [["“No—I was stopped from going into it.”", "\"아니요—저는 그리 들어가는 것을 저지당했어요.\""]], [["“What stopped you?", "\"무엇이 당신을 저지했소?\""]], [["“I saw a light, under the door; and I heard footsteps approaching it.”", "\"저는 문 아래로 불빛을 보았어요. 그리고 그것으로 다가오는 발소리를 들었어요.\""]], [["“Were you frightened?”", "\"겁이 났소?\""]], [["“Not then.", "\"그때는 아니었어요."], ["I knew my poor mother was a bad sleeper; and I remembered that she had tried hard, that evening, to persuade me to let her take charge of my Diamond.", "저는 저의 가엾은 어머니가 잠귀가 여림을 알았어요. 그리고 저는 그분이 그날 저녁, 제 다이아몬드를 자기가 맡게 해 달라고 저를 몹시 설득하려 애쓰신 것을 떠올렸어요."], ["She was unreasonably anxious about it, as I thought; and I fancied she was coming to me to see if I was in bed, and to speak to me about the Diamond again, if she found that I was up.”", "제 생각에 그분은 그것에 대해 지나치게 걱정하셨어요. 그래서 저는 그분이, 제가 잠자리에 들었는지 보려고, 그리고 제가 안 자고 있으면 다시 그 다이아몬드에 대해 저에게 말씀하려고, 저에게 오시는 것이라고 짐작했어요.\""]], [["“What did you do?”", "\"당신은 어찌했소?\""]], [["“I blew out my candle, so that she might think I was in bed.", "\"저는 그분이 제가 잠자리에 든 줄로 여기시도록, 제 초를 불어 껐어요."], ["I was unreasonable, on my side—I was determined to keep my Diamond in the place of my own choosing.”", "저도, 제 편에서 지나쳤지요—저는 제 다이아몬드를 제가 고른 곳에 두기로 마음을 굳혔어요.\""]], [["“After blowing out the candle, did you go back to bed?”", "\"초를 불어 끄고 나서, 당신은 잠자리로 돌아갔소?\""]], [["“I had no time to go back.", "\"돌아갈 짬이 없었어요."], ["At the moment when I blew the candle out, the sitting-room door opened, and I saw——”", "제가 초를 불어 끈 그 순간에, 응접실 문이 열렸고, 저는 보았어요——\""]], [["“You saw?”", "\"당신은 보았소?\""]], [["“You.”", "\"당신을요.\""]], [["“Dressed as usual?”", "\"여느 때처럼 옷을 입고?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“In my nightgown?”", "\"내 잠옷 차림으로?\""]], [["“In your nightgown—with your bedroom candle in your hand.”", "\"당신의 잠옷 차림으로—당신의 침실 초를 손에 들고서요.\""]], [["“Alone?”", "\"혼자서?\""]], [["“Alone.”", "\"혼자서요.\""]], [["“Could you see my face?”", "\"내 얼굴을 볼 수 있었소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Plainly?”", "\"또렷이?\""]], [["“Quite plainly.", "\"아주 또렷이요."], ["The candle in your hand showed it to me.”", "당신 손의 그 초가 그것을 저에게 보여 줬어요.\""]], [["“Were my eyes open?”", "\"내 눈이 떠져 있었소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"네.\""]], [["“Did you notice anything strange in them?", "\"그 눈에서 무슨 이상한 것을 눈여겨보았소?"], ["Anything like a fixed, vacant expression?”", "뭔가 고정된, 텅 빈 표정 같은 것을?\""]], [["“Nothing of the sort.", "\"그런 것은 없었어요."], ["Your eyes were bright—brighter than usual.", "당신의 눈은 밝았어요—여느 때보다 더 밝았어요."], ["You looked about in the room, as if you knew you were where you ought not to be, and as if you were afraid of being found out.”", "당신은, 마치 자기가 있어서는 안 될 곳에 있음을 아는 듯, 그리고 마치 들킬까 두려워하는 듯, 방 안을 두리번거렸어요.\""]], [["“Did you observe one thing when I came into the room—did you observe how I walked?”", "\"내가 그 방에 들어왔을 때 한 가지를 눈여겨보았소—내가 어떻게 걸었는지 눈여겨보았소?\""]], [["“You walked as you always do.", "\"당신은 늘 걷는 대로 걸었어요."], ["You came in as far as the middle of the room—and then you stopped and looked about you.”", "당신은 방 한가운데까지 들어왔어요—그러고는 멈춰 서서 주위를 둘러봤어요.\""]], [["“What did you do, on first seeing me?”", "\"나를 처음 보았을 때, 당신은 어찌했소?\""]], [["“I could do nothing.", "\"저는 아무것도 할 수 없었어요."], ["I was petrified.", "저는 돌처럼 굳어 버렸어요."], ["I couldn’t speak, I couldn’t call out, I couldn’t even move to shut my door.”", "저는 말할 수도, 소리칠 수도, 심지어 제 문을 닫으러 움직일 수도 없었어요.\""]], [["“Could I see you, where you stood?”", "\"당신이 서 있던 그곳에서, 내가 당신을 볼 수 있었겠소?\""]], [["“You might certainly have seen me.", "\"당신은 틀림없이 저를 볼 수 있었을 거예요."], ["But you never looked towards me.", "허나 당신은 결코 제 쪽을 보지 않았어요."], ["It’s useless to ask the question.", "그 물음은 물어봐야 헛일이에요."], ["I am sure you never saw me.”", "저는 당신이 결코 저를 보지 못했음을 확신해요.\""]], [["“How are you sure?”", "\"어떻게 확신하오?\""]], [["“Would you have taken the Diamond? would you have acted as you did afterwards?", "\"만일 당신이, 제가 깨어서 당신을 보고 있음을 보았다면—당신이 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔겠어요?"], ["would you be here now—if you had seen that I was awake and looking at you?", "당신이 그 뒤에 한 대로 처신했겠어요? 당신이 지금 여기 있겠어요?"], ["Don’t make me talk of that part of it!", "그 부분에 대해 저더러 말하게 하지 말아요!"], ["I want to answer you quietly.", "저는 당신에게 조용히 답하고 싶어요."], ["Help me to keep as calm as I can.", "제가 될 수 있는 한 침착하게 있도록 도와줘요."], ["Go on to something else.”", "다른 것으로 넘어가요.\""]], [["She was right—in every way, right.", "그녀가 옳았다—온갖 면에서, 옳았다."], ["I went on to other things.", "나는 다른 것들로 넘어갔다."]], [["“What did I do, after I had got to the middle of the room, and had stopped there?”", "\"내가 방 한가운데에 이르러, 거기서 멈춰 선 뒤에, 나는 무엇을 했소?\""]], [["“You turned away, and went straight to the corner near the window—where my Indian cabinet stands.”", "\"당신은 몸을 돌려, 곧장 창가 모퉁이로 갔어요—제 인도식 장식장이 서 있는 곳으로요.\""]], [["“When I was at the cabinet, my back must have been turned towards you.", "\"내가 그 장식장에 있었을 때, 내 등이 당신 쪽으로 돌려져 있었음이 틀림없소."], ["How did you see what I was doing?”", "당신은 내가 무엇을 하는지 어떻게 보았소?\""]], [["“When you moved, I moved.”", "\"당신이 움직이면, 저도 움직였어요.\""]], [["“So as to see what I was about with my hands?”", "\"내가 내 손으로 무엇을 하는지 볼 수 있도록?\""]], [["“There are three glasses in my sitting-room.", "\"제 응접실에는 거울이 세 개 있어요."], ["As you stood there, I saw all that you did, reflected in one of them.”", "당신이 거기 서 있는 동안, 저는 그중 하나에 비친, 당신이 한 모든 것을 봤어요.\""]], [["“What did you see?”", "\"당신은 무엇을 보았소?\""]], [["“You put your candle on the top of the cabinet.", "\"당신은 당신의 초를 그 장식장 위에 놓았어요."], ["You opened, and shut, one drawer after another, until you came to the drawer in which I had put my Diamond.", "당신은 서랍을 하나씩 열었다 닫았다 하며, 제가 제 다이아몬드를 넣어 둔 그 서랍에 이르렀어요."], ["You looked at the open drawer for a moment.", "당신은 잠시 그 열린 서랍을 들여다봤어요."], ["And then you put your hand in, and took the Diamond out.”", "그러고는 손을 넣어, 그 다이아몬드를 꺼냈어요.\""]], [["“How do you know I took the Diamond out?”", "\"내가 그 다이아몬드를 꺼낸 것을 당신이 어떻게 아오?\""]], [["“I saw your hand go into the drawer.", "\"저는 당신 손이 그 서랍으로 들어가는 것을 봤어요."], ["And I saw the gleam of the stone between your finger and thumb, when you took your hand out.”", "그리고 저는 당신이 손을 뺄 때, 당신의 검지와 엄지 사이에서 그 돌이 반짝이는 것을 봤어요.\""]], [["“Did my hand approach the drawer again—to close it, for instance?”", "\"내 손이 다시 그 서랍으로 다가갔소—이를테면, 그것을 닫으려고?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["You had the Diamond in your right hand; and you took the candle from the top of the cabinet with your left hand.”", "당신은 오른손에 그 다이아몬드를 쥐고 있었어요. 그리고 당신은 왼손으로 그 장식장 위에서 그 초를 집었어요.\""]], [["“Did I look about me again, after that?”", "\"그 뒤에 내가 다시 주위를 둘러보았소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“Did I leave the room immediately?”", "\"내가 곧바로 방을 떠났소?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["You stood quite still, for what seemed a long time.", "당신은, 긴 시간처럼 느껴진 동안, 아주 가만히 서 있었어요."], ["I saw your face sideways in the glass.", "저는 그 거울 속에서 당신의 옆얼굴을 봤어요."], ["You looked like a man thinking, and dissatisfied with his own thoughts.”", "당신은 무언가 생각하는, 그리고 제 자신의 생각에 흡족해하지 않는 사내처럼 보였어요.\""]], [["“What happened next?”", "\"그다음에 무슨 일이 있었소?\""]], [["“You roused yourself on a sudden, and you went straight out of the room.”", "\"당신은 별안간 정신을 차리더니, 곧장 방을 걸어 나갔어요.\""]], [["“Did I close the door after me?”", "\"내가 나가면서 문을 닫았소?\""]], [["“No.", "\"아니요."], ["You passed out quickly into the passage, and left the door open.”", "당신은 재빨리 복도로 나가, 문을 열어 둔 채였어요.\""]], [["“And then?”", "\"그러고는?\""]], [["“Then, your light disappeared, and the sound of your steps died away, and I was left alone in the dark.”", "\"그러고는, 당신의 불빛이 사라졌고, 당신의 발소리가 잦아들었고, 저는 어둠 속에 홀로 남겨졌어요.\""]], [["“Did nothing happen—from that time, to the time when the whole house knew that the Diamond was lost?”", "\"아무 일도 없었소—그때부터, 온 집이 그 다이아몬드가 없어졌음을 안 그때까지?\""]], [["“Nothing.”", "\"아무 일도요.\""]], [["“Are you sure of that?", "\"그것을 확신하오?"], ["Might you not have been asleep a part of the time?”", "그 사이 얼마쯤 잠들지는 않았소?\""]], [["“I never slept.", "\"저는 결코 잠들지 않았어요."], ["I never went back to my bed.", "저는 결코 제 잠자리로 돌아가지 않았어요."], ["Nothing happened until Penelope came in, at the usual time in the morning.”", "아침의 여느 때에 페넬로피가 들어올 때까지 아무 일도 없었어요.\""]], [["I dropped her hand, and rose, and took a turn in the room.", "나는 그녀의 손을 놓고, 일어나, 방 안을 한 바퀴 돌았다."], ["Every question that I could put had been answered.", "내가 던질 수 있는 온갖 물음에 답이 주어졌다."], ["Every detail that I could desire to know had been placed before me.", "내가 알고 싶어 할 수 있는 온갖 세세한 것이 내 앞에 놓였다."], ["I had even reverted to the idea of sleep-walking, and the idea of intoxication; and, again, the worthlessness of the one theory and the other had been proved—on the authority, this time, of the witness who had seen me.", "나는 심지어 몽유의 생각과 취중의 생각으로 되돌아가 보았다. 그리고, 다시, 그 두 이론의 무가치함이 증명되었다—이번에는, 나를 본 그 증인의 권위로써."], ["What was to be said next?", "다음으로 무슨 말을 해야 하는가?"], ["what was to be done next?", "다음으로 무엇을 해야 하는가?"], ["There rose the horrible fact of the Theft—the one visible, tangible object that confronted me, in the midst of the impenetrable darkness which enveloped all besides!", "거기 그 절도라는 끔찍한 사실이 솟아 있었다—다른 온갖 것을 뒤덮은 그 뚫을 수 없는 어둠의 한복판에서 나를 마주하는, 유일하게 눈에 보이고 만질 수 있는 대상이!"], ["Not a glimpse of light to guide me, when I had possessed myself of Rosanna Spearman’s secret at the Shivering Sand.", "흔들리는 모래에서 로재나 스피어먼의 비밀을 손에 넣었을 때에도 나를 이끌어 줄 빛 한 줄기 없었다."], ["And not a glimpse of light now, when I had appealed to Rachel herself, and had heard the hateful story of the night from her own lips.", "그리고 지금, 레이철 자신에게 호소하여, 그녀 자신의 입술에서 그 밤의 가증스러운 이야기를 들었을 때에도 빛 한 줄기 없었다."]], [["She was the first, this time, to break the silence.", "이번에는, 그 침묵을 먼저 깬 것이 그녀였다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"자?\""], ["she said, “you have asked, and I have answered.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"당신은 물었고, 저는 답했어요."], ["You have made me hope something from all this, because you hoped something from it.", "당신이 이 모든 것에서 무언가를 바랐기에, 당신은 저로 하여금 이 모든 것에서 무언가를 바라게 만들었어요."], ["What have you to say now?”", "이제 당신은 할 말이 뭐가 있나요?\""]], [["The tone in which she spoke warned me that my influence over her was a lost influence once more.", "그녀가 말하는 그 어조가, 그녀에 대한 나의 영향력이 다시 한 번 잃어버린 영향력임을 나에게 일러 주었다."]], [["“We were to look at what happened on my birthday night, together,”", "\"우리는 나의 생일 밤에 일어난 일을 함께 보기로 했지요,\""], ["she went on; “and we were then to understand each other.", "하고 그녀가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 우리는 그때 서로를 이해하기로 했지요."], ["Have we done that?”", "우리가 그것을 했나요?\""]], [["She waited pitilessly for my reply.", "그녀는 인정사정없이 나의 대답을 기다렸다."], ["In answering her I committed a fatal error—I let the exasperating helplessness of my situation get the better of my self-control.", "그녀에게 답하면서 나는 치명적인 잘못을 저질렀다—나는 내 처지의 그 부아 나는 속수무책함이 나의 자제심을 이기도록 두었다."], ["Rashly and uselessly, I reproached her for the silence which had kept me until that moment in ignorance of the truth.", "경솔하고 쓸데없이, 나는 그 순간까지 나를 진실에 대한 무지에 붙들어 둔 그 침묵에 대해 그녀를 나무랐다."]], [["“If you had spoken when you ought to have spoken,”", "\"만일 당신이 말했어야 할 때에 말했다면,\" 하고 나는 입을 뗐다."], ["I began; “if you had done me the common justice to explain yourself——”", "\"만일 당신이 스스로 해명하는, 그 예사로운 도리를 나에게 베풀었다면——\""]], [["She broke in on me with a cry of fury.", "그녀가 격노의 외침으로 나의 말을 끊었다."], ["The few words I had said seemed to have lashed her on the instant into a frenzy of rage.", "내가 한 그 몇 마디가 그 자리에서 그녀를 격분의 광란으로 몰아넣은 듯했다."]], [["“Explain myself!”", "\"스스로 해명한다고요!\""], ["she repeated.", "하고 그녀가 되뇌었다."], ["“Oh!", "\"오!"], ["is there another man like this in the world?", "세상에 이런 남자가 또 있을까요? 내 가슴이 찢어질 때 나는 그를 봐줬어요."], ["I spare him, when my heart is breaking; I screen him when my own character is at stake; and he—of all human beings, he—turns on me now, and tells me that I ought to have explained myself!", "내 자신의 명예가 걸렸을 때 나는 그를 감싸 줬어요. 그런데 그가—온갖 인간 가운데 하필 그가—이제 나에게 등을 돌려, 나더러 스스로 해명했어야 한다고 말하는군요!"], ["After believing in him as I did, after loving him as I did, after thinking of him by day, and dreaming of him by night—he wonders I didn’t charge him with his disgrace the first time we met: ‘My heart’s darling, you are a Thief!", "그를 그토록 믿고서, 그를 그토록 사랑하고서, 낮에는 그를 생각하고, 밤에는 그를 꿈꾸고서—그는 어째서 내가 우리가 처음 만났을 때 그의 그 파렴치를 그에게 들이대지 않았는지 의아해하네요. '내 마음의 님이여, 당신은 도둑이에요!"], ["My hero whom I love and honour, you have crept into my room under cover of the night, and stolen my Diamond!’ That is what I ought to have said.", "내가 사랑하고 우러르는 나의 영웅이여, 당신은 밤을 틈타 내 방에 숨어들어, 내 다이아몬드를 훔쳤어요!' 그것이 내가 말했어야 할 것이라니."], ["You villain, you mean, mean, mean villain, I would have lost fifty diamonds, rather than see your face lying to me, as I see it lying now!”", "이 악당아, 이 치사하고, 치사하고, 치사한 악당아, 나는 다이아몬드 쉰 개를 잃을지언정, 지금 내가 보고 있듯 당신의 얼굴이 나에게 거짓말하는 것을 보지는 않았을 거예요!\""]], [["I took up my hat.", "나는 내 모자를 집어 들었다."], ["In mercy to her—yes!", "그녀에 대한 자비로—그렇다!"], ["I can honestly say it—in mercy to her, I turned away without a word, and opened the door by which I had entered the room.", "나는 정직하게 그리 말할 수 있다—그녀에 대한 자비로, 나는 한마디 없이 몸을 돌려, 내가 그 방에 들어온 그 문을 열었다."]], [["She followed, and snatched the door out of my hand; she closed it, and pointed back to the place that I had left.", "그녀가 뒤따라와, 내 손에서 그 문을 낚아챘다. 그녀는 그것을 닫고, 내가 떠나온 그 자리를 도로 가리켰다."]], [["“No!” she said.", "\"안 돼요!\""], ["“Not yet!", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["It seems that I owe a justification of my conduct to you.", "\"아직은! 아무래도 내가 당신에게 내 처신에 대한 해명을 빚진 것 같군요."], ["You shall stay and hear it.", "당신은 남아서 그것을 들어요."], ["Or you shall stoop to the lowest infamy of all, and force your way out.”", "아니면 당신은 온갖 것 가운데 가장 낮은 파렴치로 몸을 굽혀, 억지로 나가 버리든가요.\""]], [["It wrung my heart to see her; it wrung my heart to hear her.", "그녀를 보는 것이 내 가슴을 쥐어짰다. 그녀를 듣는 것이 내 가슴을 쥐어짰다."], ["I answered by a sign—it was all I could do—that I submitted myself to her will.", "나는 하나의 몸짓으로 답했다—그것이 내가 할 수 있는 전부였다—내가 그녀의 뜻에 따르겠노라는."]], [["The crimson flush of anger began to fade out of her face, as I went back, and took my chair in silence.", "내가 돌아가, 말없이 내 의자를 잡자, 노여움의 진홍빛 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에서 바래기 시작했다."], ["She waited a little, and steadied herself.", "그녀는 잠시 기다리며, 자신을 다잡았다."], ["When she went on, but one sign of feeling was discernible in her.", "그녀가 말을 이었을 때, 그녀에게서 알아볼 수 있는 감정의 표시는 단 하나뿐이었다."], ["She spoke without looking at me.", "그녀는 나를 보지 않고 말했다."], ["Her hands were fast clasped in her lap, and her eyes were fixed on the ground.", "그녀의 두 손은 무릎에 꼭 맞잡혀 있었고, 그녀의 눈은 바닥에 못 박혀 있었다."]], [["“I ought to have done you the common justice to explain myself,”", "\"나는 당신에게 스스로 해명하는, 그 예사로운 도리를 베풀었어야 했어요,\""], ["she said, repeating my own words.", "하고 그녀가, 내 자신의 말을 되뇌며 말했다."], ["“You shall see whether I did try to do you justice, or not.", "\"내가 당신에게 도리를 다하려 애썼는지 아닌지 당신이 보게 될 거예요."], ["I told you just now that I never slept, and never returned to my bed, after you had left my sitting-room.", "나는 방금 당신에게, 당신이 내 응접실을 떠난 뒤로 내가 결코 잠들지 않았고, 결코 내 잠자리로 돌아가지 않았다고 말했어요."], ["It’s useless to trouble you by dwelling on what I thought—you would not understand my thoughts—I will only tell you what I did, when time enough had passed to help me to recover myself.", "내가 무엇을 생각했는지를 곱씹어 당신을 괴롭히는 것은 헛일이에요—당신은 내 생각을 이해하지 못할 테니까요—나는 다만, 나를 추스르는 데 도움이 될 만큼 시간이 충분히 지났을 때, 내가 무엇을 했는지만 당신에게 말하겠어요."], ["I refrained from alarming the house, and telling everybody what had happened—as I ought to have done.", "나는—마땅히 그랬어야 했듯이—온 집을 깨워 무슨 일이 일어났는지 모두에게 알리기를 삼갔어요."], ["In spite of what I had seen, I was fond enough of you to believe—no matter what!—any impossibility, rather than admit it to my own mind that you were deliberately a thief.", "내가 본 것에도 불구하고, 나는, 당신이 일부러 도둑이라고 내 마음속에 인정하느니, 차라리—무엇이든!—어떤 불가능이든 믿을 만큼 당신을 사랑했어요."], ["I thought and thought—and I ended in writing to you.”", "나는 생각하고 또 생각했어요—그리고 나는 당신에게 편지 쓰는 것으로 끝냈어요.\""]], [["“I never received the letter.”", "\"나는 그 편지를 결코 받지 못했소.\""]], [["“I know you never received it.", "\"당신이 그것을 결코 받지 못한 것을 나는 알아요."], ["Wait a little, and you shall hear why.", "잠시 기다려요, 그러면 어째서인지 듣게 될 거예요."], ["My letter would have told you nothing openly.", "내 편지는 당신에게 아무것도 드러내 놓고 말하지 않았을 거예요."], ["It would not have ruined you for life, if it had fallen into some other person’s hands.", "그것이 다른 어떤 사람의 손에 들어갔더라도, 그것은 당신을 평생 파멸시키지 않았을 거예요."], ["It would only have said—in a manner which you yourself could not possibly have mistaken—that I had reason to know you were in debt, and that it was in my experience and in my mother’s experience of you, that you were not very discreet, or very scrupulous about how you got money when you wanted it.", "그것은 다만—당신 자신은 도저히 잘못 알 수 없는 방식으로—내가 당신이 빚졌음을 알 만한 근거가 있다는 것과, 당신이 돈이 필요할 때 그것을 어떻게 마련하는지에 대해 그리 신중하지도, 그리 조심스럽지도 않다는 것이 나의, 그리고 나의 어머니의 당신에 대한 경험이라는 것을 말했을 뿐이에요."], ["You would have remembered the visit of the French lawyer, and you would have known what I referred to.", "당신은 그 프랑스인 변호사의 방문을 떠올리고, 내가 무엇을 가리키는지 알았을 거예요."], ["If you had read on with some interest after that, you would have come to an offer I had to make to you—the offer, privately (not a word, mind, to be said openly about it between us!), of the loan of as large a sum of money as I could get.—And I would have got it!”", "만일 당신이 그다음에 얼마쯤 관심을 가지고 계속 읽었다면, 당신은 내가 당신에게 할 제안에 이르렀을 거예요—사사로이 (명심해요, 우리 사이에 그것에 대해 드러내 놓고 한마디도 말하지 않기로 하고!), 내가 마련할 수 있는 만큼 큰 금액을 빌려주겠다는 제안에요.—그리고 나는 그것을 마련했을 거예요!\""], ["she exclaimed, her colour beginning to rise again, and her eyes looking up at me once more.", "하고 그녀가 외쳤다, 그녀의 핏기가 다시 오르기 시작하고, 그녀의 눈이 다시 한 번 나를 올려다보며."], ["“I would have pledged the Diamond myself, if I could have got the money in no other way!", "\"만일 다른 어떤 방도로도 그 돈을 마련할 수 없었다면, 나는 그 다이아몬드를 내 손으로 저당 잡혔을 거예요!"], ["In those words I wrote to you.", "그런 말로 나는 당신에게 편지했어요."], ["Wait!", "잠깐!"], ["I did more than that.", "나는 그 이상을 했어요."], ["I arranged with Penelope to give you the letter when nobody was near.", "나는 아무도 가까이 없을 때 당신에게 그 편지를 주도록 페넬로피와 짰어요."], ["I planned to shut myself into my bedroom, and to have the sitting-room left open and empty all the morning.", "나는 내 침실에 나를 가두고, 응접실을 오전 내내 열어 둔 채 비워 두기로 짰어요."], ["And I hoped—with all my heart and soul I hoped!—that you would take the opportunity, and put the Diamond back secretly in the drawer.”", "그리고 나는 바랐어요—온 마음과 온 넋으로 나는 바랐어요!—당신이 그 기회를 붙잡아, 그 다이아몬드를 몰래 그 서랍에 도로 넣기를.\""]], [["I attempted to speak.", "나는 말하려 했다."], ["She lifted her hand impatiently, and stopped me.", "그녀가 조바심을 내며 손을 들어, 나를 멈추게 했다."], ["In the rapid alternations of her temper, her anger was beginning to rise again.", "그녀의 성미의 빠른 오르내림 속에서, 그녀의 노여움이 다시 치솟기 시작하고 있었다."], ["She got up from her chair, and approached me.", "그녀는 제 의자에서 일어나, 나에게 다가왔다."]], [["“I know what you are going to say,”", "\"나는 당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 알아요,\""], ["she went on.", "하고 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“You are going to remind me again that you never received my letter.", "\"당신은 다시 나에게, 당신이 내 편지를 결코 받지 못했음을 일깨우려는 거지요."], ["I can tell you why.", "어째서인지 내가 말해 줄게요."], ["I tore it up.", "나는 그것을 찢어 버렸어요.\""]], [["“For what reason?”", "\"무슨 이유로요?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“For the best of reasons.", "\"더없이 마땅한 이유로요."], ["I preferred tearing it up to throwing it away upon such a man as you!", "나는 그것을 당신 같은 사내에게 던져 버리느니 찢어 버리는 편을 택했어요!"], ["What was the first news that reached me in the morning?", "아침에 나에게 다다른 첫 소식이 무엇이었을까요?"], ["Just as my little plan was complete, what did I hear?", "나의 그 자그마한 계획이 막 완성되었을 때, 내가 무엇을 들었을까요?"], ["I heard that you—you!!!—were the foremost person in the house in fetching the police.", "나는 당신이—당신이!!!—경찰을 부르는 데에 그 집에서 가장 앞장선 사람이라는 것을 들었어요. 당신이 그 발 벗고 나선 사내였어요."], ["You were the active man; you were the leader; you were working harder than any of them to recover the jewel!", "당신이 그 주동자였어요. 당신이 그 보석을 되찾으려 그들 중 누구보다 더 열심히 애쓰고 있었어요!"], ["You even carried your audacity far enough to ask to speak to me about the loss of the Diamond—the Diamond which you yourself had stolen; the Diamond which was all the time in your own hands!", "당신은 심지어 뻔뻔함을 한껏 부려, 그 다이아몬드의 분실에 대해 나에게 이야기하기를 청하기까지 했어요—당신 자신이 훔친 그 다이아몬드, 내내 당신 자신의 손안에 있던 그 다이아몬드에 대해서요!"], ["After that proof of your horrible falseness and cunning, I tore up my letter.", "당신의 그 끔찍한 거짓됨과 교활함의 그 증거를 겪고서, 나는 내 편지를 찢어 버렸어요."], ["But even then—even when I was maddened by the searching and questioning of the policeman, whom you had sent in—even then, there was some infatuation in my mind which wouldn’t let me give you up.", "허나 그때조차—당신이 들여보낸 그 경찰관의 뒤짐과 신문에 내가 미쳐 버릴 지경이었을 때조차—그때조차, 내 마음속에는 당신을 포기하지 못하게 하는 어떤 홀림이 있었어요."], ["I said to myself, ‘He has played his vile farce before everybody else in the house.", "나는 스스로에게 말했어요, '그는 집안의 다른 모든 사람 앞에서 그의 그 비열한 광대극을 벌였어."], ["Let me try if he can play it before me.’ Somebody told me you were on the terrace.", "그가 내 앞에서 그것을 벌일 수 있는지 시험해 보자.' 누군가 나에게 당신이 그 테라스에 있다고 말했어요."], ["I went down to the terrace.", "나는 그 테라스로 내려갔어요."], ["I forced myself to look at you; I forced myself to speak to you.", "나는 억지로 당신을 보았어요. 나는 억지로 당신에게 말했어요."], ["Have you forgotten what I said?”", "당신은 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 잊었나요?\""]], [["I might have answered that I remembered every word of it.", "나는 그 한마디 한마디를 다 기억한다고 답할 수도 있었으리라."], ["But what purpose, at that moment, would the answer have served?", "허나 그 순간, 그 답이 무슨 소용에 닿았겠는가?"]], [["How could I tell her that what she had said had astonished me, had distressed me, had suggested to me that she was in a state of dangerous nervous excitement, had even roused a moment’s doubt in my mind whether the loss of the jewel was as much a mystery to her as to the rest of us—but had never once given me so much as a glimpse at the truth?", "그녀가 한 말이 나를 놀라게 했고, 나를 괴롭게 했고, 그녀가 위태로운 신경의 흥분 상태에 있음을 나에게 넌지시 일러 주었고, 심지어 그 보석의 분실이 우리 나머지에게만큼이나 그녀에게도 수수께끼인지 나의 마음에 한순간의 의심마저 불러일으켰다는 것을—허나 진실에 대한 실낱같은 엿봄조차 나에게 한 번도 준 적이 없었다는 것을, 내가 어찌 그녀에게 말할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Without the shadow of a proof to produce in vindication of my innocence, how could I persuade her that I knew no more than the veriest stranger could have known of what was really in her thoughts when she spoke to me on the terrace?", "나의 결백을 밝히려 내놓을 증거의 그림자 하나 없이, 그녀가 그 테라스에서 나에게 말했을 때 그녀의 생각 속에 정말로 무엇이 있었는지를, 내가 그야말로 온전한 낯선 이가 알 수 있었을 것보다 조금도 더 알지 못했음을, 내가 어찌 그녀를 설득할 수 있었겠는가?"]], [["“It may suit your convenience to forget; it suits my convenience to remember,”", "\"잊는 것이 당신 편할지 모르지요. 기억하는 것이 나는 편해요,\""], ["she went on.", "하고 그녀가 말을 이었다."], ["“I know what I said—for I considered it with myself, before I said it.", "\"나는 내가 무슨 말을 했는지 알아요—그것을 말하기 전에 나 스스로 곰곰이 헤아렸으니까요."], ["I gave you one opportunity after another of owning the truth.", "나는 당신에게 진실을 털어놓을 기회를 하나 또 하나 주었어요."], ["I left nothing unsaid that I could say—short of actually telling you that I knew you had committed the theft.", "나는 내가 할 수 있는 말은 하나도 안 한 채 남겨 두지 않았어요—당신이 그 절도를 저질렀음을 내가 안다고 실제로 말하는 것만 빼고요."], ["And all the return you made, was to look at me with your vile pretence of astonishment, and your false face of innocence—just as you have looked at me today; just as you are looking at me now!", "그런데 당신이 한 보답이라고는, 당신의 그 비열한 놀란 척과, 당신의 그 거짓된 결백의 얼굴로 나를 바라보는 것뿐이었어요—꼭 당신이 오늘 나를 바라본 것처럼요. 꼭 당신이 지금 나를 바라보고 있는 것처럼요!"], ["I left you, that morning, knowing you at last for what you were—for what you are—as base a wretch as ever walked the earth!”", "나는 그날 아침, 마침내 당신이 어떤 사람인지를—당신이 어떤 사람인지를—여태 이 땅 위를 걸은 가장 비열한 몹쓸 것임을 알고서, 당신을 떠났어요!\""]], [["“If you had spoken out at the time, you might have left me, Rachel, knowing that you had cruelly wronged an innocent man.”", "\"만일 당신이 그때 털어놓았다면, 당신은 자기가 결백한 사람에게 잔인하게 잘못했음을 알고서, 나를 떠날 수 있었을 것이오, 레이철.\""]], [["“If I had spoken out before other people,”", "\"만일 내가 다른 사람들 앞에서 털어놓았다면,\""], ["she retorted, with another burst of indignation, “you would have been disgraced for life!", "하고 그녀가 또 한 차례의 분노의 터짐과 함께 되받았다. \"당신은 평생 욕되게 되었을 거예요!"], ["If I had spoken out to no ears but yours, you would have denied it, as you are denying it now!", "만일 내가 당신 귀에만 털어놓았다면, 당신은, 지금 부인하고 있듯, 그것을 부인했을 거예요!"], ["Do you think I should have believed you?", "내가 당신을 믿었을 것 같아요?"], ["Would a man hesitate at a lie, who had done what I saw you do—who had behaved about it afterwards, as I saw you behave?", "내가 당신이 하는 것을 본 짓을 한 사내가—그 뒤에 그것에 대해 내가 당신이 처신하는 것을 본 대로 처신한 사내가—거짓말 앞에 망설였을까요?"], ["I tell you again, I shrank from the horror of hearing you lie, after the horror of seeing you thieve.", "다시 말하지만, 나는 당신이 훔치는 것을 본 그 끔찍함 뒤에, 당신이 거짓말하는 것을 듣는 그 끔찍함이 무서워 움츠러들었어요."], ["You talk as if this was a misunderstanding which a few words might have set right!", "당신은 마치 이것이 몇 마디 말로 바로잡힐 수 있었을 오해이기라도 한 양 말하는군요!"], ["Well!", "그래요!"], ["the misunderstanding is at an end.", "그 오해는 끝났어요."], ["Is the thing set right? No!", "그것이 바로잡혔나요?"], ["the thing is just where it was.", "아니요! 그것은 여전히 그대로예요."], ["I don’t believe you now!", "나는 지금도 당신을 안 믿어요!"], ["I don’t believe you found the nightgown, I don’t believe in Rosanna Spearman’s letter, I don’t believe a word you have said.", "나는 당신이 그 잠옷을 찾았다는 것을 안 믿어요. 나는 로재나 스피어먼의 편지를 안 믿어요."], ["You stole it—I saw you!", "나는 당신이 한 말 한마디도 안 믿어요."], ["You affected to help the police—I saw you!", "당신이 그것을 훔쳤어요—나는 당신을 봤어요!"], ["You pledged the Diamond to the money-lender in London—I am sure of it!", "당신이 경찰을 돕는 척했어요—나는 당신을 봤어요! 당신이 런던의 그 돈놀이꾼에게 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡혔어요—나는 그것을 확신해요!"], ["You cast the suspicion of your disgrace (thanks to my base silence!) on an innocent man!", "당신이 당신의 파렴치의 그 의심을 (나의 비열한 침묵 덕분에!) 결백한 사람에게 씌웠어요!"], ["You fled to the Continent with your plunder the next morning!", "당신이 이튿날 아침 당신의 노획물을 가지고 대륙으로 달아났어요!"], ["After all that vileness, there was but one thing more you could do.", "그 온갖 비열함을 저지르고, 당신이 할 수 있는 것이 딱 한 가지 더 남았지요."], ["You could come here with a last falsehood on your lips—you could come here, and tell me that I have wronged you!”", "당신은 입술에 마지막 거짓을 담고 여기 올 수 있었어요—당신은 여기 와서, 내가 당신에게 잘못했다고 나에게 말할 수 있었어요!\""]], [["If I had stayed a moment more, I know not what words might have escaped me which I should have remembered with vain repentance and regret.", "만일 내가 한순간 더 머물렀다면, 나는 뒤에 헛된 뉘우침과 후회로 기억했을 그 무슨 말이 내 입에서 새어 나왔을지 알지 못한다."], ["I passed by her, and opened the door for the second time.", "나는 그녀를 지나쳐, 두 번째로 그 문을 열었다."], ["For the second time—with the frantic perversity of a roused woman—she caught me by the arm, and barred my way out.", "두 번째로—격앙된 여자의 광란 어린 어깃장으로—그녀가 내 팔을 붙들어, 내가 나가는 길을 막았다."]], [["“Let me go, Rachel” I said.", "\"나를 가게 해 주오, 레이철,\""], ["“It will be better for both of us.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"그것이 우리 둘 다에게 더 나을 것이오."], ["Let me go.”", "나를 가게 해 주오.\""]], [["The hysterical passion swelled in her bosom—her quickened convulsive breathing almost beat on my face, as she held me back at the door.", "발작적인 격정이 그녀의 가슴속에서 부풀어 올랐다—그녀가 그 문에서 나를 막아서는 동안, 그녀의 가빠진 경련하는 숨결이 거의 내 얼굴에 부딪쳤다."]], [["“Why did you come here?”", "\"어째서 당신은 여기 왔나요?\""], ["she persisted, desperately.", "하고 그녀가 필사적으로 다그쳤다."], ["“I ask you again—why did you come here?", "\"다시 물을게요—어째서 당신은 여기 왔나요?"], ["Are you afraid I shall expose you?", "내가 당신을 폭로할까 두려운 건가요?"], ["Now you are a rich man, now you have got a place in the world, now you may marry the best lady in the land—are you afraid I shall say the words which I have never said yet to anybody but you?", "이제 당신은 부자가 되었고, 이제 당신은 세상에서 자리를 얻었고, 이제 당신은 이 나라에서 가장 좋은 규수와 혼인할 수 있어요—내가 여태 당신 말고는 아무에게도 하지 않은 그 말을 할까 두려운 건가요?"], ["I can’t say the words!", "나는 그 말을 못 해요!"], ["I can’t expose you!", "나는 당신을 폭로할 수 없어요!"], ["I am worse, if worse can be, than you are yourself.”", "나는, 더 나쁠 수 있다면, 당신 자신보다 더 나빠요.\""], ["Sobs and tears burst from her.", "흐느낌과 눈물이 그녀에게서 터져 나왔다."], ["She struggled with them fiercely; she held me more and more firmly.", "그녀는 그것들과 사납게 싸웠다. 그녀는 나를 점점 더 굳게 붙들었다."], ["“I can’t tear you out of my heart,”", "\"나는 당신을 내 마음에서 뜯어낼 수 없어요,\""], ["she said, “even now!", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["You may trust in the shameful, shameful weakness which can only struggle against you in this way!”", "\"지금조차도요! 오직 이런 식으로만 당신에게 맞서 발버둥 칠 수 있는 이 부끄럽고, 부끄러운 약함을 믿어도 좋아요!\""], ["She suddenly let go of me—she threw up her hands, and wrung them frantically in the air.", "그녀가 별안간 나를 놓았다—그녀는 두 손을 치켜들어, 허공에 대고 미친 듯이 비틀었다."], ["“Any other woman living would shrink from the disgrace of touching him!”", "\"살아 있는 다른 어떤 여자라도 그를 만지는 그 수치를 꺼렸을 거예요!\""], ["she exclaimed.", "하고 그녀가 외쳤다."], ["“Oh, God!", "\"오, 하느님!"], ["I despise myself even more heartily than I despise him!”", "나는 그를 경멸하는 것보다 나 자신을 더욱더 진심으로 경멸해요!\""]], [["The tears were forcing their way into my eyes in spite of me—the horror of it was to be endured no longer.", "눈물이, 나도 모르게, 내 눈으로 밀고 들어오고 있었다—그 끔찍함은 더는 견딜 수 없었다."]], [["“You shall know that you have wronged me, yet,”", "\"당신은 아직, 당신이 나에게 잘못했음을 알게 될 것이오,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["“Or you shall never see me again!”", "\"아니면 당신은 다시는 나를 보지 못할 것이오!\""]], [["With those words, I left her.", "그 말과 함께, 나는 그녀를 떠났다."], ["She started up from the chair on which she had dropped the moment before: she started up—the noble creature!—and followed me across the outer room, with a last merciful word at parting.", "그녀는 조금 전 털썩 주저앉았던 그 의자에서 벌떡 일어났다. 그녀는 벌떡 일어나—그 고귀한 존재는!—바깥방을 가로질러 나를 뒤따라오며, 헤어질 때의 마지막 자비로운 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Franklin!”", "\"프랭클린!\""], ["she said, “I forgive you!", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"나는 당신을 용서해요!"], ["Oh, Franklin, Franklin!", "오, 프랭클린, 프랭클린!"], ["we shall never meet again.", "우리는 다시는 만나지 못할 거예요."], ["Say you forgive me!”", "당신도 나를 용서한다고 말해 줘요!\""]], [["I turned, so as to let my face show her that I was past speaking—I turned, and waved my hand, and saw her dimly, as in a vision, through the tears that had conquered me at last.", "나는, 내가 말할 수 없는 지경임을 내 얼굴이 그녀에게 보이도록 몸을 돌렸다—나는 몸을 돌려, 손을 흔들었고, 마침내 나를 이겨 버린 그 눈물 너머로, 마치 환영 속에서인 양, 흐릿하게 그녀를 보았다."]], [["The next moment, the worst bitterness of it was over.", "그다음 순간, 그것의 가장 모진 쓰라림이 지나갔다."], ["I was out in the garden again.", "나는 다시 그 정원에 나와 있었다."], ["I saw her, and heard her, no more.", "나는 그녀를 다시는 보지도, 듣지도 못했다."]], [["# CHAPTER VIII", "# CHAPTER VIII"]], [["Late that evening, I was surprised at my lodgings by a visit from Mr. Bruff.", "그날 저녁 늦게, 나는 내 하숙에서 브러프 씨의 방문에 놀랐다."]], [["There was a noticeable change in the lawyer’s manner.", "그 변호사의 태도에는 눈에 띄는 변화가 있었다."], ["It had lost its usual confidence and spirit.", "그것은 여느 때의 자신감과 기운을 잃은 채였다."], ["He shook hands with me, for the first time in his life, in silence.", "그는, 제 생애에서 처음으로, 말없이 나와 악수했다."]], [["“Are you going back to Hampstead?”", "\"햄스테드로 돌아가는 길이오?\""], ["I asked, by way of saying something.", "하고 나는 무슨 말이든 하려는 셈으로 물었다."]], [["“I have just left Hampstead,”", "\"나는 방금 햄스테드를 떠나왔소,\""], ["he answered. “I know, Mr.", "하고 그가 대답했다."], ["Franklin, that you have got at the truth at last.", "\"나는 아오, 프랭클린 씨, 당신이 마침내 진실에 다다랐음을."], ["But, I tell you plainly, if I could have foreseen the price that was to be paid for it, I should have preferred leaving you in the dark.”", "허나, 솔직히 말하는데, 만일 그것을 위해 치러질 대가를 내가 미리 내다볼 수 있었다면, 나는 차라리 당신을 어둠 속에 남겨 두는 편을 택했을 거요.\""]], [["“You have seen Rachel?”", "\"당신은 레이철을 보았소?\""]], [["“I have come here after taking her back to Portland Place; it was impossible to let her return in the carriage by herself.", "\"나는 그녀를 포틀랜드 플레이스로 데려다주고서 여기 왔소. 그녀를 마차에 혼자 태워 돌려보내기란 불가능했소."], ["I can hardly hold you responsible—considering that you saw her in my house and by my permission—for the shock that this unlucky interview has inflicted on her.", "나는 좀처럼 당신에게 책임을 물을 수 없소—당신이 내 집에서, 내 허락으로 그녀를 보았음을 헤아리면 말이오—이 불운한 면담이 그녀에게 가한 그 충격에 대해서."], ["All I can do is to provide against a repetition of the mischief.", "내가 할 수 있는 것은 다만 그 화의 되풀이를 막는 것뿐이오."], ["She is young—she has a resolute spirit—she will get over this, with time and rest to help her.", "그녀는 젊소—그녀는 굳센 기상이 있소—그녀는, 시간과 안식이 그녀를 도우면, 이것을 이겨 낼 것이오."], ["I want to be assured that you will do nothing to hinder her recovery.", "나는 당신이 그녀의 회복을 방해할 아무 짓도 하지 않으리라는 것을 다짐받고 싶소."], ["May I depend on your making no second attempt to see her—except with my sanction and approval?”", "내가 당신이, 나의 재가와 승인이 있는 경우 말고는, 그녀를 만나려는 두 번째 시도를 하지 않으리라 믿어도 되겠소?\""]], [["“After what she has suffered, and after what I have suffered,”", "\"그녀가 겪은 일 뒤에, 그리고 내가 겪은 일 뒤에,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said, “you may rely on me.”", "\"당신은 나를 믿어도 좋소.\""]], [["“I have your promise?”", "\"당신의 약속을 받은 것이오?\""]], [["“You have my promise.”", "\"나의 약속을 받은 것이오.\""]], [["Mr. Bruff looked relieved.", "브러프 씨는 안도한 낯빛이었다."], ["He put down his hat, and drew his chair nearer to mine.", "그는 제 모자를 내려놓고, 제 의자를 내 의자 가까이 끌어당겼다."]], [["“That’s settled!” he said.", "\"그것은 정해졌소!\""], ["“Now, about the future—your future, I mean.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"이제, 미래에 대해서인데—당신의 미래 말이오."], ["To my mind, the result of the extraordinary turn which the matter has now taken is briefly this.", "내 생각으로는, 이 일이 이제 취한 그 유별난 전환의 결과는 간추리면 이렇소."], ["In the first place, we are sure that Rachel has told you the whole truth, as plainly as words can tell it.", "우선, 우리는 레이철이, 말로 할 수 있는 한 또렷이, 당신에게 온 진실을 말했음을 확신하오."], ["In the second place—though we know that there must be some dreadful mistake somewhere—we can hardly blame her for believing you to be guilty, on the evidence of her own senses; backed, as that evidence has been, by circumstances which appear, on the face of them, to tell dead against you.”", "둘째로—어딘가에 무슨 끔찍한 잘못이 있음이 틀림없음을 우리가 알기는 하나—우리는 그녀가, 제 자신의 감각의 증거로, 그 증거가, 겉으로 보기에 당신에게 완전히 불리하게 말하는 정황들로 뒷받침되었으니, 당신을 죄지었다고 믿는 것을 좀처럼 탓할 수 없소.\""]], [["There I interposed.", "거기서 나는 끼어들었다."], ["“I don’t blame Rachel,” I said.", "\"나는 레이철을 탓하지 않소,\""], ["“I only regret that she could not prevail on herself to speak more plainly to me at the time.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 다만 그녀가 그때 나에게 더 또렷이 말하도록 스스로를 이겨 내지 못한 것이 안타까울 뿐이오.\""]], [["“You might as well regret that Rachel is not somebody else,”", "\"그것은 레이철이 다른 누군가가 아닌 것을 안타까워하는 것이나 마찬가지요,\""], ["rejoined Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다."], ["“And even then, I doubt if a girl of any delicacy, whose heart had been set on marrying you, could have brought herself to charge you to your face with being a thief.", "\"그리고 그렇다 한들, 당신과 혼인하는 데에 제 마음을 쏟았던, 얼마쯤이라도 섬세한 아가씨가, 당신의 면전에 대고 당신이 도둑이라고 들이댈 마음을 스스로 낼 수 있었을지 나는 의심스럽소."], ["Anyhow, it was not in Rachel’s nature to do it.", "어쨌든, 그것은 레이철의 천성에 없는 일이었소."], ["In a very different matter to this matter of yours—which placed her, however, in a position not altogether unlike her position towards you—I happen to know that she was influenced by a similar motive to the motive which actuated her conduct in your case.", "이 당신의 일과는 아주 다른 어떤 일에서—허나 그것은 그녀를, 당신을 향한 처지와 아주 다르지만은 않은 처지에 놓았소—나는 마침, 그녀가 당신의 경우에 그녀의 처신을 움직인 동기와 비슷한 동기에 영향받았음을 아오."], ["Besides, as she told me herself, on our way to town this evening, if she had spoken plainly, she would no more have believed your denial then than she believes it now.", "게다가, 그녀 자신이 오늘 저녁 시내로 오는 길에 나에게 말했듯이, 만일 그녀가 정녕 또렷이 말했더라도, 그녀는 그때 당신의 부인을, 지금 그것을 믿지 않는 것보다 조금도 더 믿지 않았을 거요."], ["What answer can you make to that?", "그것에 당신은 무슨 대답을 할 수 있소?"], ["There is no answer to be made to it.", "그것에는 할 대답이 없소."], ["Come, come, Mr. Franklin!", "자, 자, 프랭클린 씨!"], ["my view of the case has been proved to be all wrong, I admit—but, as things are now, my advice may be worth having for all that.", "이 사건에 대한 나의 견해가 죄 틀린 것으로 증명되었음을 나는 인정하오—허나, 사정이 지금 이러하니, 나의 조언은 그럼에도 들어 둘 값어치가 있을지 모르오."], ["I tell you plainly, we shall be wasting our time, and cudgelling our brains to no purpose, if we attempt to try back, and unravel this frightful complication from the beginning.", "솔직히 말하는데, 만일 우리가 거꾸로 되짚어, 이 무시무시한 얽힘을 처음부터 풀어내려 한다면, 우리는 우리의 시간을 허비하고, 아무 소용 없이 우리의 머리를 쥐어짜는 것이 될 거요."], ["Let us close our minds resolutely to all that happened last year at Lady Verinder’s country house; and let us look to what we can discover in the future, instead of to what we can not discover in the past.”", "지난해에 레이디 버린더의 시골집에서 일어난 온갖 것에 우리의 마음을 단호히 닫읍시다. 그리고 우리가 과거에서 알아낼 수 없는 것 대신에, 우리가 미래에서 알아낼 수 있는 것을 봅시다.\""]], [["“Surely you forget,”", "\"당신은 정녕 잊고 있소,\""], ["I said, “that the whole thing is essentially a matter of the past—so far as I am concerned?”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"이 온 일이, 나에게 관련되는 한, 본질적으로 과거의 일이라는 것을?\""]], [["“Answer me this,”", "\"이것에 답해 보시오,\""], ["retorted Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다."], ["“Is the Moonstone at the bottom of all the mischief—or is it not?”", "\"월장석이 이 온갖 화의 밑바탕에 있소—아니오?\""]], [["“It is—of course.”", "\"있소—물론이오.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"좋소."], ["What do we believe was done with the Moonstone, when it was taken to London?”", "그 월장석이 런던으로 가져가졌을 때, 그것이 어찌되었다고 우리는 믿소?\""]], [["“It was pledged to Mr. Luker.”", "\"그것은 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔소.\""]], [["“We know that you are not the person who pledged it.", "\"우리는 그것을 저당 잡힌 사람이 당신이 아님을 아오."], ["Do we know who did?”", "우리는 누가 그리했는지 아오?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니오.\""]], [["“Where do we believe the Moonstone to be now?”", "\"우리는 그 월장석이 지금 어디 있다고 믿소?\""]], [["“Deposited in the keeping of Mr. Luker’s bankers.”", "\"루커 씨의 은행가의 보관에 맡겨져 있소.\""]], [["“Exactly.", "\"바로 그렇소."], ["Now observe.", "자 눈여겨보시오."], ["We are already in the month of June.", "우리는 이미 유월에 들어와 있소."], ["Towards the end of the month (I can’t be particular to a day) a year will have elapsed from the time when we believe the jewel to have been pledged.", "이달 말경이면 (하루까지 꼭 집을 수는 없소) 우리가 그 보석이 저당 잡혔다고 믿는 그때로부터 한 해가 흘러가 있을 거요."], ["There is a chance—to say the least—that the person who pawned it, may be prepared to redeem it when the year’s time has expired.", "실낱같은 가능성이나마—적어도—그것을 저당 잡힌 사람이, 그 한 해의 기한이 다하면 그것을 되찾을 채비를 하고 있을 가능성이 있소."], ["If he redeems it, Mr. Luker must himself—according to the terms of his own arrangement—take the Diamond out of his banker’s hands.", "만일 그가 그것을 되찾는다면, 루커 씨는 몸소—제 자신의 약정 조건에 따라—그 다이아몬드를 제 은행가의 손에서 꺼내야 하오."], ["Under these circumstances, I propose setting a watch at the bank, as the present month draws to an end, and discovering who the person is to whom Mr. Luker restores the Moonstone.", "이런 상황에서, 나는 이달이 끝나 갈 무렵 그 은행에 파수를 세워, 루커 씨가 그 월장석을 되돌려 주는 사람이 누구인지 알아낼 것을 제안하오."], ["Do you see it now?”", "이제 그것이 보이오?\""]], [["I admitted (a little unwillingly) that the idea was a new one, at any rate.", "나는 (다소 마지못해) 그 생각이 어쨌든 새로운 것임을 인정했다."]], [["“It’s Mr. Murthwaite’s idea quite as much as mine,”", "\"그것은 나의 생각인 것 못지않게 머스웨이트 씨의 생각이기도 하오,\""], ["said Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["“It might have never entered my head, but for a conversation we had together some time since.", "\"얼마 전 우리가 함께 나눈 대화가 아니었다면, 그것은 결코 내 머리에 떠오르지 않았을지도 모르오."], ["If Mr. Murthwaite is right, the Indians are likely to be on the lookout at the bank, towards the end of the month too—and something serious may come of it.", "만일 머스웨이트 씨가 옳다면, 그 인도인들도 이달 말경 그 은행을 노리고 지켜볼 공산이 크오—그리고 거기서 무언가 심상찮은 일이 나올지도 모르오."], ["What comes of it doesn’t matter to you and me except as it may help us to lay our hands on the mysterious Somebody who pawned the Diamond.", "거기서 무엇이 나오든, 그것이 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡힌 그 알 수 없는 누군가에게 우리 손을 얹는 데에 도움이 되는 한에서 말고는, 당신과 나에게 대수롭지 않소."], ["That person, you may rely on it, is responsible (I don’t pretend to know how) for the position in which you stand at this moment; and that person alone can set you right in Rachel’s estimation.”", "그 사람이, 믿어도 좋소, 당신이 이 순간 처한 그 처지에 (나는 어떻게인지 안다고 짐짓 말하지는 않겠소) 책임이 있소. 그리고 오직 그 사람만이 레이철의 평가 속에서 당신을 바로잡아 줄 수 있소.\""]], [["“I can’t deny,”", "\"나는 부인할 수 없소,\""], ["I said, “that the plan you propose meets the difficulty in a way that is very daring, and very ingenious, and very new.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"당신이 제안하는 그 계획이 아주 대담하고, 아주 기발하고, 아주 새로운 방식으로 그 어려움에 맞선다는 것을."], ["But——”", "허나——\""]], [["“But you have an objection to make?”", "\"허나 당신은 내놓을 반론이 있군?\""]], [["“Yes.", "\"그렇소."], ["My objection is, that your proposal obliges us to wait.”", "나의 반론은, 당신의 제안이 우리로 하여금 기다리게 한다는 것이오.\""]], [["“Granted.", "\"인정하오."], ["As I reckon the time, it requires you to wait about a fortnight—more or less.", "내가 시간을 헤아리기로는, 그것은 당신에게 두 주쯤—더도 덜도—기다리기를 요구하오."], ["Is that so very long?”", "그것이 그리 오래오?\""]], [["“It’s a life-time, Mr. Bruff, in such a situation as mine.", "\"그것은 나 같은 처지에서는, 브러프 씨, 한평생이오."], ["My existence will be simply unendurable to me, unless I do something towards clearing my character at once.”", "나는 당장 나의 명예를 씻는 무언가를 하지 않고서는, 나의 삶이 나에게 그저 견딜 수 없을 것이오.\""]], [["“Well, well, I understand that.", "\"그래, 그래, 나도 그것을 이해하오."], ["Have you thought yet of what you can do?”", "당신이 무엇을 할 수 있을지 아직 생각해 보았소?\""]], [["“I have thought of consulting Sergeant Cuff.”", "\"나는 커프 부장과 상의할 생각을 했소.\""]], [["“He has retired from the police.", "\"그는 경찰에서 물러났소."], ["It’s useless to expect the Sergeant to help you.”", "그 부장이 당신을 돕기를 바라는 것은 헛일이오.\""]], [["“I know where to find him; and I can but try.”", "\"나는 그를 어디서 찾을지 아오. 그리고 나는 해 볼 수밖에 없소.\""]], [["“Try,” said Mr.", "\"해 보시오,\""], ["Bruff, after a moment’s consideration.", "하고 브러프 씨가 잠시 생각하고 나서 말했다."], ["“The case has assumed such an extraordinary aspect since Sergeant Cuff’s time, that you may revive his interest in the inquiry.", "\"이 사건은 커프 부장의 시절 이래 어찌나 유별난 양상을 띠었던지, 당신이 정녕 그 조사에 대한 그의 관심을 되살릴 수 있을지도 모르오."], ["Try, and let me hear the result.", "해 보시고, 나에게 그 결과를 들려주시오."], ["In the meanwhile,”", "그동안,\""], ["he continued, rising, “if you make no discoveries between this, and the end of the month, am I free to try, on my side, what can be done by keeping a lookout at the bank?”", "하고 그는 일어서며 말을 이었다. \"만일 당신이 지금과 이달 말 사이에 아무것도 알아내지 못한다면, 나는 내 편에서, 그 은행을 지켜봄으로써 무엇을 할 수 있을지 해 봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이오,\""], ["I answered—“unless I relieve you of all necessity for trying the experiment in the interval.”", "하고 나는 대답했다—\"내가 그 사이에 당신이 그 실험을 해 볼 온갖 필요를 덜어 주지 않는 한 말이오.\""]], [["Mr. Bruff smiled, and took up his hat.", "브러프 씨는 미소 지으며, 제 모자를 집어 들었다."]], [["“Tell Sergeant Cuff,”", "\"커프 부장에게 전하시오,\""], ["he rejoined, “that I say the discovery of the truth depends on the discovery of the person who pawned the Diamond.", "하고 그가 되받았다. \"진실의 발견이 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡힌 사람의 발견에 달려 있다고 내가 말한다고."], ["And let me hear what the Sergeant’s experience says to that.”", "그리고 그것에 대해 그 부장의 경험이 뭐라 하는지 나에게 들려주시오.\""]], [["So we parted.", "그리하여 우리는 헤어졌다."]], [["Early the next morning, I set forth for the little town of Dorking—the place of Sergeant Cuff’s retirement, as indicated to me by Betteredge.", "이튿날 아침 일찍, 나는 도킹이라는 작은 읍으로 길을 나섰다—베터리지가 나에게 일러 준 대로, 커프 부장의 은거지였다."]], [["Inquiring at the hotel, I received the necessary directions for finding the Sergeant’s cottage.", "그 호텔에서 물어, 나는 그 부장의 오두막을 찾는 데 필요한 안내를 받았다."], ["It was approached by a quiet by-road, a little way out of the town, and it stood snugly in the middle of its own plot of garden ground, protected by a good brick wall at the back and the sides, and by a high quickset hedge in front.", "그것은 읍에서 조금 벗어난, 조용한 샛길로 다가가게 되어 있었고, 그것은 뒤와 옆으로는 튼튼한 벽돌담에, 앞으로는 높은 산울타리에 둘러싸여, 제 나름의 정원 뙈기 한가운데에 아늑히 서 있었다."], ["The gate, ornamented at the upper part by smartly-painted trellis-work, was locked.", "위쪽이 산뜻하게 칠해진 격자 세공으로 꾸며진 그 문은, 잠겨 있었다."], ["After ringing at the bell, I peered through the trellis-work, and saw the great Cuff’s favourite flower everywhere; blooming in his garden, clustering over his door, looking in at his windows.", "종을 울리고서, 나는 그 격자 세공 사이로 들여다보았고, 그 위대한 커프가 아끼는 꽃이 곳곳에 있는 것을 보았다. 그의 정원에 피어나고, 그의 문 위로 무리 지고, 그의 창을 들여다보며."], ["Far from the crimes and the mysteries of the great city, the illustrious thief-taker was placidly living out the last Sybarite years of his life, smothered in roses!", "그 큰 도시의 범죄와 수수께끼에서 멀리 떨어져, 그 이름난 도둑잡이는 장미에 파묻혀, 제 생애의 마지막 향락의 나날을 고요히 살아 내고 있었다!"]], [["A decent elderly woman opened the gate to me, and at once annihilated all the hopes I had built on securing the assistance of Sergeant Cuff.", "점잖은 나이 든 여자가 나에게 그 문을 열어 주었고, 대뜸 커프 부장의 도움을 얻는 데에 내가 쌓아 올린 온갖 희망을 무너뜨렸다."], ["He had started, only the day before, on a journey to Ireland.", "그는, 겨우 그 전날, 아일랜드로 가는 여행길에 올랐던 것이다."]], [["“Has he gone there on business?”", "\"그가 볼일로 거기 갔소?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["The woman smiled.", "그 여자는 미소 지었다."], ["“He has only one business now, sir,”", "\"그분에게는 이제 볼일이 딱 하나뿐입니다, 나리,\""], ["she said; “and that’s roses.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 장미지요."], ["Some great man’s gardener in Ireland has found out something new in the growing of roses—and Mr. Cuff’s away to inquire into it.”", "아일랜드의 어느 대단한 분의 정원사가 장미 기르는 데에서 무언가 새로운 것을 알아냈답니다—그래서 커프 씨는 그것을 알아보러 떠나셨어요.\""]], [["“Do you know when he will be back?”", "\"그가 언제 돌아올지 아오?\""]], [["“It’s quite uncertain, sir.", "\"아주 알 수 없습니다, 나리."], ["Mr. Cuff said he should come back directly, or be away some time, just according as he found the new discovery worth nothing, or worth looking into.", "커프 씨는, 그 새 발견이 아무 값어치가 없다고 보시는지, 아니면 살펴볼 값어치가 있다고 보시는지에 꼭 따라, 곧 돌아오시거나 한동안 떠나 계시거나 하겠다고 하셨어요."], ["If you have any message to leave for him, I’ll take care, sir, that he gets it.”", "만일 그분께 남길 전갈이 있으시면, 제가 그분이 그것을 받으시도록 마음 쓰겠습니다, 나리.\""]], [["I gave her my card, having first written on it in pencil: “I have something to say about the Moonstone.", "나는, 먼저 그 위에 연필로 이렇게 써서, 그녀에게 내 명함을 주었다. \"월장석에 관해 드릴 말씀이 있습니다."], ["Let me hear from you as soon as you get back.”", "돌아오시는 대로 연락 주십시오.\""], ["That done, there was nothing left but to submit to circumstances, and return to London.", "그것을 마치니, 상황에 따르는 것과, 런던으로 돌아가는 것 말고는 남은 것이 아무것도 없었다."]], [["In the irritable condition of my mind, at the time of which I am now writing, the abortive result of my journey to the Sergeant’s cottage simply aggravated the restless impulse in me to be doing something.", "지금 내가 쓰고 있는 그 무렵의, 나의 마음의 짜증스러운 상태에서, 그 부장의 오두막으로의 나의 여행의 헛된 결과는 그저 무언가를 하려는 내 안의 그 안절부절못하는 충동을 부추길 뿐이었다."], ["On the day of my return from Dorking, I determined that the next morning should find me bent on a new effort at forcing my way, through all obstacles, from the darkness to the light.", "도킹에서 돌아온 날, 나는 이튿날 아침에는 내가, 온갖 장애를 뚫고 어둠에서 빛으로 억지로 나아가려는 새로운 애씀에 마음을 쏟고 있게끔 하리라 마음먹었다."]], [["What form was my next experiment to take?", "나의 다음 실험은 무슨 꼴을 띠어야 하는가?"]], [["If the excellent Betteredge had been present while I was considering that question, and if he had been let into the secret of my thoughts, he would, no doubt, have declared that the German side of me was, on this occasion, my uppermost side.", "그 물음을 헤아리는 동안 그 훌륭한 베터리지가 곁에 있었더라면, 그리고 그가 내 생각의 비밀에 들어왔더라면, 그는 두말할 것 없이, 이번에는 나의 독일식 면이 나의 가장 위에 있는 면이라고 선언했으리라."], ["To speak seriously, it is perhaps possible that my German training was in some degree responsible for the labyrinth of useless speculations in which I now involved myself.", "진지하게 말하자면, 나의 독일식 훈련이, 내가 이제 나 자신을 얽어 넣은 그 쓸데없는 사변의 미로에 얼마쯤 책임이 있었는지도 모른다."], ["For the greater part of the night, I sat smoking, and building up theories, one more profoundly improbable than another.", "밤의 태반 동안, 나는 앉아서 담배를 피우며, 하나가 다른 하나보다 더 깊이 있음 직하지 않은 이론들을 쌓아 올렸다."], ["When I did get to sleep, my waking fancies pursued me in dreams.", "정작 잠이 들자, 나의 깨어 있을 적의 공상이 꿈속에서 나를 좇았다."], ["I rose the next morning, with Objective-Subjective and Subjective-Objective inextricably entangled together in my mind; and I began the day which was to witness my next effort at practical action of some kind, by doubting whether I had any sort of right (on purely philosophical grounds) to consider any sort of thing (the Diamond included) as existing at all.", "나는 이튿날 아침, 객관-주관과 주관-객관이 내 마음속에 풀 수 없이 뒤엉킨 채로 일어났다. 그리고 나는, 무슨 종류든 실질적인 행동을 향한 나의 다음 애씀을 지켜볼 그날을, 내가 (순전히 철학적인 근거에서) 어떤 종류의 것이든 (그 다이아몬드를 포함하여) 도무지 존재하는 것으로 여길 무슨 권리가 있는지 의심하는 것으로 시작했다."]], [["How long I might have remained lost in the mist of my own metaphysics, if I had been left to extricate myself, it is impossible for me to say.", "만일 나 스스로 헤쳐 나오도록 내버려 두어졌더라면, 내가 얼마나 오래 나 자신의 형이상학의 안개 속에 길 잃은 채 있었을지, 나는 말할 수 없다."], ["As the event proved, accident came to my rescue, and happily delivered me.", "결과가 증명했듯이, 우연이 나를 구하러 와서, 다행히 나를 건져 냈다."], ["I happened to wear, that morning, the same coat which I had worn on the day of my interview with Rachel.", "나는 그날 아침, 마침 레이철과 면담한 그날 입었던 그 웃옷을 입고 있었다."], ["Searching for something else in one of the pockets, I came upon a crumpled piece of paper, and, taking it out, found Betteredge’s forgotten letter in my hand.", "주머니 하나에서 다른 무언가를 찾다가, 나는 구겨진 종잇조각 하나에 손이 닿았고, 그것을 꺼내니, 내 손에 잊고 있던 베터리지의 편지가 있었다."]], [["It seemed hard on my good old friend to leave him without a reply.", "나의 착한 옛 벗을 답장 없이 두는 것은 그에게 야박한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["I went to my writing-table, and read his letter again.", "나는 내 필기 탁자로 가서, 그의 편지를 다시 읽었다."]], [["A letter which has nothing of the slightest importance in it, is not always an easy letter to answer.", "조금도 중요할 것이 없는 편지가, 늘 답하기 쉬운 편지인 것은 아니다."], ["Betteredge’s present effort at corresponding with me came within this category. Mr.", "나와 편지를 주고받으려는 베터리지의 이번 애씀은 이 부류에 들었다."], ["Candy’s assistant, otherwise Ezra Jennings, had told his master that he had seen me; and Mr. Candy, in his turn, wanted to see me and say something to me, when I was next in the neighbourhood of Frizinghall.", "캔디 씨의 조수, 다시 말해 에즈라 제닝스가 제 주인에게 나를 보았다고 말했고, 캔디 씨는, 제 차례로, 내가 다음에 프리징홀 근방에 오면 나를 보고 나에게 무언가 말하고 싶어 했다."], ["What was to be said in answer to that, which would be worth the paper it was written on?", "그것에 대한 답으로, 그것이 적힌 종이 값어치라도 할 무슨 말을 할 수 있단 말인가?"], ["I sat idly drawing likenesses from memory of Mr. Candy’s remarkable-looking assistant, on the sheet of paper which I had vowed to dedicate to Betteredge—until it suddenly occurred to me that here was the irrepressible Ezra Jennings getting in my way again!", "나는 베터리지에게 바치기로 다짐한 그 종이 위에, 캔디 씨의 눈에 띄는 생김새의 조수의 얼굴을 기억에서 이리저리 그리며 한가로이 앉아 있었다—문득, 여기 그 억누를 수 없는 에즈라 제닝스가 다시 내 길을 가로막고 있다는 것이 떠오르기까지!"], ["I threw a dozen portraits, at least, of the man with the piebald hair (the hair in every case, remarkably like), into the waste-paper basket—and then and there, wrote my answer to Betteredge.", "나는 그 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼의 사내의 초상을 (그 머리칼은 어느 경우에나 놀랍도록 비슷했다) 적어도 열두 장은 휴지통에 던져 넣었다—그러고는 그 자리에서, 베터리지에게 내 답장을 썼다."], ["It was a perfectly commonplace letter—but it had one excellent effect on me.", "그것은 더없이 평범한 편지였다—허나 그것은 나에게 한 가지 훌륭한 효과를 냈다."], ["The effort of writing a few sentences, in plain English, completely cleared my mind of the cloudy nonsense which had filled it since the previous day.", "몇 문장을, 쉬운 영어로 쓰는 그 애씀이, 전날부터 내 마음을 채웠던 그 안개 낀 헛소리를 완전히 걷어 냈다."]], [["Devoting myself once more to the elucidation of the impenetrable puzzle which my own position presented to me, I now tried to meet the difficulty by investigating it from a plainly practical point of view.", "나 자신의 처지가 나에게 내놓은 그 뚫을 수 없는 수수께끼의 해명에 다시 한 번 몰두하며, 나는 이제 그것을 명백히 실질적인 관점에서 살핌으로써 그 어려움에 맞서려 했다."], ["The events of the memorable night being still unintelligible to me, I looked a little farther back, and searched my memory of the earlier hours of the birthday for any incident which might prove of some assistance to me in finding the clue.", "그 잊지 못할 밤의 사건들이 여전히 나에게 알쏭달쏭했으므로, 나는 조금 더 뒤로 돌아가, 그 실마리를 찾는 데에 나에게 얼마쯤 도움이 될 만한 어떤 사건이든 찾으려, 그 생일의 이른 시각들에 대한 나의 기억을 뒤졌다."]], [["Had anything happened while Rachel and I were finishing the painted door?", "레이철과 내가 그 칠한 문을 마무리하는 동안 무슨 일이 일어났던가?"], ["or, later, when I rode over to Frizinghall?", "또는, 나중에, 내가 프리징홀로 말을 몰고 갔을 때?"], ["or afterwards, when I went back with Godfrey Ablewhite and his sisters?", "또는 그 뒤에, 내가 고드프리 에이블화이트와 그의 누이들과 함께 돌아왔을 때?"], ["or, later again, when I put the Moonstone into Rachel’s hands?", "또는, 다시 나중에, 내가 그 월장석을 레이철의 손에 쥐여 주었을 때?"], ["or, later still, when the company came, and we all assembled round the dinner-table?", "또는, 더 나중에, 손님들이 오고, 우리 모두가 만찬 식탁에 둘러 모였을 때?"], ["My memory disposed of that string of questions readily enough, until I came to the last.", "나의 기억은 그 일련의 물음들을, 마지막 것에 이르기까지, 어렵잖게 처리했다."], ["Looking back at the social event of the birthday dinner, I found myself brought to a standstill at the outset of the inquiry.", "그 생일 만찬이라는 그 사교 행사를 되돌아보니, 나는 그 조사의 첫머리에서 우뚝 멈추어 섰다."], ["I was not even capable of accurately remembering the number of the guests who had sat at the same table with me.", "나는 심지어 나와 같은 식탁에 앉았던 손님들의 수조차 정확히 기억할 수 없었다."]], [["To feel myself completely at fault here, and to conclude, thereupon, that the incidents of the dinner might especially repay the trouble of investigating them, formed parts of the same mental process, in my case.", "여기서 내가 완전히 막혔음을 느끼는 것과, 그리하여 그 만찬의 사건들이 각별히 그것들을 조사하는 수고를 갚아 줄지 모른다고 결론짓는 것은, 나의 경우에, 같은 사고 과정의 부분을 이루었다."], ["I believe other people, in a similar situation, would have reasoned as I did.", "나는 비슷한 처지에 놓인 다른 사람들도 나처럼 헤아렸으리라 믿는다."], ["When the pursuit of our own interests causes us to become objects of inquiry to ourselves, we are naturally suspicious of what we don’t know.", "우리 자신의 이해를 좇는 것이 우리로 하여금 우리 자신에게 조사의 대상이 되게 할 때, 우리는 자연히 우리가 모르는 것을 미심쩍어한다."], ["Once in possession of the names of the persons who had been present at the dinner, I resolved—as a means of enriching the deficient resources of my own memory—to appeal to the memory of the rest of the guests; to write down all that they could recollect of the social events of the birthday; and to test the result, thus obtained, by the light of what had happened afterwards, when the company had left the house.", "그 만찬에 있었던 사람들의 이름을 일단 손에 넣으면, 나는—나 자신의 부족한 기억의 밑천을 넉넉히 하는 수단으로—나머지 손님들의 기억에 호소하기로, 그들이 그 생일의 사교적 사건들에 대해 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것을 적어 두기로, 그리고 이렇게 얻은 결과를, 손님들이 그 집을 떠난 뒤에 일어난 일의 빛으로 검증하기로 마음먹었다."]], [["This last and newest of my many contemplated experiments in the art of inquiry—which Betteredge would probably have attributed to the clear-headed, or French, side of me being uppermost for the moment—may fairly claim record here, on its own merits.", "조사의 기술에서 내가 궁리한 그 숱한 실험 가운데 이 마지막이자 가장 새로운 것은—베터리지라면 아마 이 순간 나의 명석한, 곧 프랑스식 면이 가장 위에 있는 탓으로 돌렸을 것이다—그 자체의 값어치로, 여기 기록될 자격이 넉넉하다."], ["Unlikely as it may seem, I had now actually groped my way to the root of the matter at last.", "있음 직하지 않아 보일지 모르나, 나는 이제 마침내 정녕 그 일의 뿌리로 더듬어 나아간 것이었다."], ["All I wanted was a hint to guide me in the right direction at starting.", "내가 원한 것은 다만 시작에서 나를 옳은 방향으로 이끌어 줄 실마리 하나뿐이었다."], ["Before another day had passed over my head, that hint was given me by one of the company who had been present at the birthday feast!", "하루가 더 내 머리 위로 지나가기 전에, 그 실마리가 그 생일 잔치에 있었던 손님 가운데 하나에 의해 나에게 주어졌다!"]], [["With the plan of proceeding which I now had in view, it was first necessary to possess the complete list of the guests.", "내가 이제 염두에 둔 그 진행의 계획으로는, 먼저 그 손님들의 온전한 명단을 지녀야 했다."], ["This I could easily obtain from Gabriel Betteredge.", "이것을 나는 게이브리얼 베터리지에게서 쉽게 얻을 수 있었다."], ["I determined to go back to Yorkshire on that day, and to begin my contemplated investigation the next morning.", "나는 그날 요크셔로 돌아가, 이튿날 아침 내가 궁리한 조사를 시작하기로 마음먹었다."]], [["It was just too late to start by the train which left London before noon.", "정오 전에 런던을 떠나는 그 기차로 출발하기에는 딱 너무 늦었다."], ["There was no alternative but to wait, nearly three hours, for the departure of the next train.", "다음 기차의 출발까지 거의 세 시간을 기다리는 것 말고는 다른 방도가 없었다."], ["Was there anything I could do in London, which might usefully occupy this interval of time?", "이 사이의 시간을 쓸모 있게 채울 만한, 내가 런던에서 할 수 있는 무언가가 있었던가?"]], [["My thoughts went back again obstinately to the birthday dinner.", "나의 생각은 다시 고집스레 그 생일 만찬으로 돌아갔다."]], [["Though I had forgotten the numbers, and, in many cases, the names of the guests, I remembered readily enough that by far the larger proportion of them came from Frizinghall, or from its neighbourhood.", "나는 그 수를, 그리고, 많은 경우, 손님들의 이름을 잊었건만, 그들 가운데 훨씬 더 큰 비율이 프리징홀이나 그 근방에서 왔다는 것은 어렵잖게 기억했다. 허나 그 더 큰 비율이 전부는 아니었다."], ["But the larger proportion was not all.", "우리 중 몇몇은 그 지방의 상주자가 아니었다."], ["Some few of us were not regular residents in the country.", "나 자신이 그 몇몇 중 하나였다."], ["I myself was one of the few.", "머스웨이트 씨가 또 하나였다."], ["Mr. Murthwaite was another.", "고드프리 에이블화이트가 셋째였다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite was a third. Mr.", "브러프 씨는—아니다."], ["Bruff—no: I called to mind that business had prevented Mr. Bruff from making one of the party.", "나는 볼일이 브러프 씨가 그 자리의 하나가 되는 것을 막았음을 떠올렸다."], ["Had any ladies been present, whose usual residence was in London?", "여느 거처가 런던인 부인이 있었던가?"], ["I could only remember Miss Clack as coming within this latter category.", "나는 오직 클랙 양만이 이 뒤엣 부류에 드는 것으로 기억할 수 있었다."], ["However, here were three of the guests, at any rate, whom it was clearly advisable for me to see before I left town. I drove off at once to Mr.", "어쨌든, 여기, 어쨌든, 내가 시내를 떠나기 전에 만나는 것이 명백히 이로울 손님이 셋 있었다."], ["Bruff’s office; not knowing the addresses of the persons of whom I was in search, and thinking it probable that he might put me in the way of finding them.", "나는 대뜸 브러프 씨의 사무소로 마차를 몰았다. 내가 찾는 그 사람들의 주소를 알지 못했고, 그가 나에게 그들을 찾는 길을 일러 줄지도 모른다고 여겨서."]], [["Mr. Bruff proved to be too busy to give me more than a minute of his valuable time.", "브러프 씨는 그의 값진 시간을 나에게 일 분 넘게 내주기에는 너무 바쁜 것으로 드러났다."], ["In that minute, however, he contrived to dispose—in the most discouraging manner—of all the questions I had to put to him.", "허나 그 일 분 안에, 그는—더없이 맥 빠지게 하는 방식으로—내가 그에게 물을 온갖 물음을 처리해 냈다."]], [["In the first place, he considered my newly-discovered method of finding a clue to the mystery as something too purely fanciful to be seriously discussed. In the second, third, and fourth places, Mr.", "우선, 그는 그 수수께끼의 실마리를 찾는 나의 새로 발견한 방법을, 진지하게 논하기에는 너무나 순전히 공상적인 무엇으로 여겼다."], ["Murthwaite was now on his way back to the scene of his past adventures; Miss Clack had suffered losses, and had settled, from motives of economy, in France; Mr.", "둘째로, 셋째로, 넷째로, 머스웨이트 씨는 이제 제 지난 모험의 무대로 돌아가는 길에 있었다. 클랙 양은 손실을 겪었고, 절약의 동기에서, 프랑스에 자리 잡았다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite might, or might not, be discoverable somewhere in London.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 런던 어딘가에서 찾을 수 있을지도, 없을지도 몰랐다."], ["Suppose I inquired at his club?", "그의 클럽에서 물어보면 어떻겠소?"], ["And suppose I excused Mr. Bruff, if he went back to his business and wished me good morning?", "그리고 만일 브러프 씨가 제 볼일로 돌아가 나에게 안녕을 고한다면 양해해 주겠소?"]], [["The field of inquiry in London, being now so narrowed as only to include the one necessity of discovering Godfrey’s address, I took the lawyer’s hint, and drove to his club.", "런던에서의 조사의 마당이, 이제 오직 고드프리의 주소를 알아내는 그 한 가지 필요만을 담게끔 그토록 좁혀졌으므로, 나는 그 변호사의 귀띔을 따라, 그의 클럽으로 마차를 몰았다."]], [["In the hall, I met with one of the members, who was an old friend of my cousin’s, and who was also an acquaintance of my own.", "그 현관홀에서, 나는 내 사촌의 옛 벗이자 나 자신과도 아는 사이인 회원 하나와 마주쳤다."], ["This gentleman, after enlightening me on the subject of Godfrey’s address, told me of two recent events in his life, which were of some importance in themselves, and which had not previously reached my ears.", "이 신사는, 고드프리의 주소에 관해 나를 밝혀 준 뒤, 그의 삶에서의 최근의 두 사건에 대해 나에게 이야기해 주었는데, 그것들은 그 자체로 얼마쯤 중요했고, 그때껏 나의 귀에 다다르지 않은 것이었다."]], [["It appeared that Godfrey, far from being discouraged by Rachel’s withdrawal from her engagement to him had made matrimonial advances soon afterwards to another young lady, reputed to be a great heiress.", "알고 보니 고드프리는, 레이철이 그에 대한 혼약에서 물러난 것에 낙심하기는커녕, 그 뒤 곧 크나큰 상속녀로 알려진 다른 젊은 아가씨에게 혼담을 건넸던 것이다."], ["His suit had prospered, and his marriage had been considered as a settled and certain thing.", "그의 구애는 순조로웠고, 그의 혼인은 정해지고 확실한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["But, here again, the engagement had been suddenly and unexpectedly broken off—owing, it was said, on this occasion, to a serious difference of opinion between the bridegroom and the lady’s father, on the question of settlements.", "허나, 여기서도 다시, 그 혼약은 별안간 뜻밖에 깨지고 말았다—이번에는, 재산 분배의 문제를 두고 신랑과 그 아가씨의 아버지 사이의 심각한 견해차 때문이었다는 소문이었다."]], [["As some compensation for this second matrimonial disaster, Godfrey had soon afterwards found himself the object of fond pecuniary remembrance, on the part of one of his many admirers.", "이 두 번째 혼인의 낭패에 대한 얼마쯤의 보상으로, 고드프리는 그 뒤 곧 제 숱한 흠모자 가운데 하나의, 다정한 금전적 기억의 대상이 된 것을 알았다."], ["A rich old lady—highly respected at the Mothers’ Small-Clothes-Conversion-Society, and a great friend of Miss Clack’s (to whom she left nothing but a mourning ring)—had bequeathed to the admirable and meritorious Godfrey a legacy of five thousand pounds.", "어느 부유한 노부인이—'어머니회 어린이옷 개조 협회'에서 크게 존경받고, 클랙 양의 좋은 벗이었던 (그 클랙 양에게는 상장 반지 하나 말고는 아무것도 남기지 않았다) 부인이—그 감탄스럽고 갸륵한 고드프리에게 오천 파운드의 유산을 유증했던 것이다."], ["After receiving this handsome addition to his own modest pecuniary resources, he had been heard to say that he felt the necessity of getting a little respite from his charitable labours, and that his doctor prescribed “a run on the Continent, as likely to be productive of much future benefit to his health.”", "제 자신의 조촐한 금전 밑천에 이 큼직한 보탬을 받고서, 그는 자기가 자선 사업에서 잠시 벗어나 숨 돌릴 필요를 느낀다고, 그리고 자기 의사가 \"앞으로 그의 건강에 크게 이로울 법한 것으로서, 대륙 여행\"을 처방했다고 말하는 것이 들렸다."], ["If I wanted to see him, it would be advisable to lose no time in paying my contemplated visit.", "만일 내가 그를 보고 싶다면, 지체 없이 내가 궁리한 방문을 하는 것이 이로울 터였다."]], [["I went, then and there, to pay my visit.", "나는, 그 자리에서, 나의 방문을 하러 갔다."]], [["The same fatality which had made me just one day too late in calling on Sergeant Cuff, made me again one day too late in calling on Godfrey.", "내가 커프 부장을 찾아가는 데에 딱 하루 너무 늦게 했던 그 똑같은 숙명이, 내가 고드프리를 찾아가는 데에도 다시 딱 하루 너무 늦게 했다."], ["He had left London, on the previous morning, by the tidal train, for Dover.", "그는, 그 전날 아침, 조석(潮汐) 기차로, 도버로 떠났다."], ["He was to cross to Ostend; and his servant believed he was going on to Brussels.", "그는 오스텐트로 건너가기로 되어 있었다. 그리고 그의 하인은 그가 브뤼셀로 계속 가는 것이라 믿었다."], ["The time of his return was rather uncertain; but I might be sure he would be away at least three months.", "그가 돌아올 때는 자못 알 수 없었다. 허나 그가 적어도 석 달은 떠나 있으리라는 것만은 확실했다."]], [["I went back to my lodgings a little depressed in spirits.", "나는 다소 풀 죽은 심사로 내 하숙으로 돌아갔다."], ["Three of the guests at the birthday dinner—and those three all exceptionally intelligent people—were out of my reach, at the very time when it was most important to be able to communicate with them.", "그 생일 만찬의 손님 셋이—그것도 그 셋이 죄 유달리 총명한 사람들이—그들과 연락할 수 있는 것이 가장 중요한 바로 그때에, 내 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 있었다."], ["My last hopes now rested on Betteredge, and on the friends of the late Lady Verinder whom I might still find living in the neighbourhood of Rachel’s country house.", "나의 마지막 희망은 이제 베터리지에게, 그리고 레이철의 시골집 근방에서 아직 살아 있는 것을 볼 수 있을 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 벗들에게 놓였다."]], [["On this occasion, I travelled straight to Frizinghall—the town being now the central point in my field of inquiry.", "이번에는, 나는 곧장 프리징홀로 갔다—그 읍이 이제 내 조사의 마당에서 중심점이었으므로."], ["I arrived too late in the evening to be able to communicate with Betteredge.", "나는 저녁에 너무 늦게 다다라 베터리지와 연락할 수 없었다."], ["The next morning, I sent a messenger with a letter, requesting him to join me at the hotel, at his earliest convenience.", "이튿날 아침, 나는 편지를 든 심부름꾼을 보내어, 되도록 편한 때에 그 호텔로 나에게 와 달라고 청했다."]], [["Having taken the precaution—partly to save time, partly to accommodate Betteredge—of sending my messenger in a fly, I had a reasonable prospect, if no delays occurred, of seeing the old man within less than two hours from the time when I had sent for him.", "시간을 아끼려, 또 얼마쯤은 베터리지를 배려하려, 내 심부름꾼을 삯마차로 보내는 조심을 했으므로, 나는, 아무 지체도 없다면, 그를 부르러 보낸 때로부터 두 시간이 채 못 되어 그 노인을 볼 만한 상당한 전망이 있었다."], ["During this interval, I arranged to employ myself in opening my contemplated inquiry, among the guests present at the birthday dinner who were personally known to me, and who were easily within my reach.", "이 사이에, 나는 내가 몸소 아는, 그리고 내 손이 쉽게 닿는, 그 생일 만찬에 있었던 손님들 사이에서 내가 궁리한 조사를 시작하는 데에 나 자신을 쓰기로 정했다."], ["These were my relatives, the Ablewhites, and Mr. Candy.", "이들은 나의 친척인 에이블화이트 집안 사람들과, 캔디 씨였다."], ["The doctor had expressed a special wish to see me, and the doctor lived in the next street.", "그 의사는 나를 보고 싶다는 각별한 바람을 나타낸 터였고, 그 의사는 다음 거리에 살았다."], ["So to Mr. Candy I went first.", "그래서 나는 먼저 캔디 씨에게로 갔다."]], [["After what Betteredge had told me, I naturally anticipated finding traces in the doctor’s face of the severe illness from which he had suffered.", "베터리지가 나에게 말해 준 것을 겪고서, 나는 자연히 그 의사의 얼굴에서 그가 앓은 그 심한 병의 자취를 볼 것을 예상했다."], ["But I was utterly unprepared for such a change as I saw in him when he entered the room and shook hands with me.", "허나 나는 그가 방에 들어와 나와 악수했을 때 그에게서 본 그런 변화에는 전혀 채비가 되어 있지 않았다."], ["His eyes were dim; his hair had turned completely grey; his face was wizen; his figure had shrunk.", "그의 눈은 흐릿했고, 그의 머리칼은 완전히 세었고, 그의 얼굴은 쭈그러들었고, 그의 몸은 오그라들었다."], ["I looked at the once lively, rattlepated, humorous little doctor—associated in my remembrance with the perpetration of incorrigible social indiscretions and innumerable boyish jokes—and I saw nothing left of his former self, but the old tendency to vulgar smartness in his dress.", "나는, 나의 기억 속에서 고칠 수 없는 사교적 경솔과 헤아릴 수 없는 어린애 같은 농담을 저지르는 것과 결부된, 한때 활달하고, 수다스럽고, 익살맞던 그 자그마한 의사를 보았다—그리고 나는 그의 이전 자신에게서 남은 것을, 그의 옷차림의 저속한 멋에 대한 옛 성벽 말고는, 아무것도 보지 못했다."], ["The man was a wreck; but his clothes and his jewellery—in cruel mockery of the change in him—were as gay and as gaudy as ever.", "그 사람은 잔해였다. 허나 그의 옷과 그의 장신구는—그에게 일어난 변화에 대한 잔인한 조롱인 양—여느 때와 다름없이 화사하고 야했다."]], [["“I have often thought of you, Mr. Blake,”", "\"나는 당신을 자주 생각했소, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["he said; “and I am heartily glad to see you again at last.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그리고 나는 마침내 당신을 다시 보게 되어 진심으로 기쁘오."], ["If there is anything I can do for you, pray command my services, sir—pray command my services!”", "만일 내가 당신을 위해 할 수 있는 것이 있다면, 부디 내 도움을 부리시오, 선생—부디 내 도움을 부리시오!\""]], [["He said those few commonplace words with needless hurry and eagerness, and with a curiosity to know what had brought me to Yorkshire, which he was perfectly—I might say childishly—incapable of concealing from notice.", "그는 그 몇 마디 평범한 말을 쓸데없이 서두르며 안달하는 태도로, 그리고 무엇이 나를 요크셔로 데려왔는지 알고 싶어 하는—그가 완전히, 어린애처럼이라 이를 만큼, 남의 눈에서 감출 수 없는—호기심을 안고 말했다."]], [["With the object that I had in view, I had of course foreseen the necessity of entering into some sort of personal explanation, before I could hope to interest people, mostly strangers to me, in doing their best to assist my inquiry.", "내가 염두에 둔 그 목적으로, 나는 물론, 대개 나에게 낯선 그 사람들이 나의 조사를 돕는 데에 최선을 다하도록 관심을 두게 하기를 바라기 전에, 얼마쯤의 개인적 설명에 들어갈 필요를 미리 내다본 터였다."], ["On the journey to Frizinghall I had arranged what my explanation was to be—and I seized the opportunity now offered to me of trying the effect of it on Mr. Candy.", "프리징홀로 오는 여행길에 나는 나의 설명이 어떠할지를 짜 두었다—그리고 나는 이제 나에게 주어진 그 기회를 붙잡아, 그것의 효과를 캔디 씨에게 시험해 보았다."]], [["“I was in Yorkshire, the other day, and I am in Yorkshire again now, on rather a romantic errand,” I said.", "\"나는 요전 날 요크셔에 있었고, 지금 다시 요크셔에 있소, 자못 낭만적인 볼일로,\""], ["“It is a matter, Mr.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["Candy, in which the late Lady Verinder’s friends all took some interest.", "\"그것은, 캔디 씨, 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 벗들이 죄 얼마쯤 관심을 둔 일이오."], ["You remember the mysterious loss of the Indian Diamond, now nearly a year since?", "이제 거의 한 해 전인, 그 인도 다이아몬드의 알 수 없는 분실을 기억하시오?"], ["Circumstances have lately happened which lead to the hope that it may yet be found—and I am interesting myself, as one of the family, in recovering it.", "근래에, 그것이 아직 찾아질지도 모른다는 희망으로 이어지는 정황들이 일어났소—그리고 나는, 그 집안의 한 사람으로서, 그것을 되찾는 데에 나 자신의 관심을 두고 있소."], ["Among the obstacles in my way, there is the necessity of collecting again all the evidence which was discovered at the time, and more if possible.", "내 길의 장애물 가운데, 그때 발견된 온갖 증거를, 그리고 가능하다면 그 이상을, 다시 모아야 할 필요가 있소."], ["There are peculiarities in this case which make it desirable to revive my recollection of everything that happened in the house, on the evening of Miss Verinder’s birthday.", "이 사건에는, 버린더 아가씨의 생일 저녁에 그 집에서 일어난 모든 것에 대한 나의 기억을 되살리는 것을 바람직하게 만드는 특이한 점들이 있소."], ["And I venture to appeal to her late mother’s friends who were present on that occasion, to lend me the assistance of their memories——”", "그리고 나는 감히, 그 자리에 있었던 그녀의 고인이 된 어머니의 벗들에게, 그들의 기억의 도움을 나에게 빌려주기를 호소하오——\""]], [["I had got as far as that in rehearsing my explanatory phrases, when I was suddenly checked by seeing plainly in Mr. Candy’s face that my experiment on him was a total failure.", "나의 설명하는 말들을 되뇌는 데에 그만큼 나아갔을 때, 나는 캔디 씨의 얼굴에서 그에 대한 나의 실험이 완전한 실패임을 또렷이 보고 별안간 멈추었다."]], [["The little doctor sat restlessly picking at the points of his fingers all the time I was speaking.", "그 자그마한 의사는 내가 말하는 내내 안절부절못하며 제 손끝을 만지작거리며 앉아 있었다."], ["His dim watery eyes were fixed on my face with an expression of vacant and wistful inquiry very painful to see.", "그의 흐릿하고 물기 어린 눈은 보기에 아주 애처로운, 텅 비고 아쉬워하는 물음의 표정을 담고 나의 얼굴에 못 박혀 있었다."], ["What he was thinking of, it was impossible to divine.", "그가 무엇을 생각하는지는 헤아릴 도리가 없었다."], ["The one thing clearly visible was that I had failed, after the first two or three words, in fixing his attention.", "또렷이 보이는 한 가지는, 내가 처음 두세 마디 뒤로, 그의 주의를 붙드는 데에 실패했다는 것이었다."], ["The only chance of recalling him to himself appeared to lie in changing the subject.", "그를 제정신으로 불러들일 유일한 가망은 화제를 바꾸는 데에 있는 듯했다."], ["I tried a new topic immediately.", "나는 대뜸 새 화제를 시도했다."]], [["“So much,”", "\"무엇이 나를 프리징홀로 데려왔는지는,\""], ["I said, gaily, “for what brings me to Frizinghall!", "하고 나는 명랑하게 말했다. \"그쯤 해 둡시다!"], ["Now, Mr. Candy, it’s your turn.", "자, 캔디 씨, 이번엔 당신 차례요."], ["You sent me a message by Gabriel Betteredge——”", "당신은 게이브리얼 베터리지를 통해 나에게 전갈을 보냈지요——\""]], [["He left off picking at his fingers, and suddenly brightened up.", "그는 제 손가락 만지작거리기를 그치고, 별안간 환해졌다."]], [["“Yes!", "\"그렇소!"], ["yes!", "그렇소!"], ["yes!”", "그렇소!\""], ["he exclaimed eagerly.", "하고 그가 간절히 외쳤다."], ["“That’s it!", "\"바로 그거요!"], ["I sent you a message!”", "내가 당신에게 전갈을 보냈소!\""]], [["“And Betteredge duly communicated it by letter,” I went on.", "\"그리고 베터리지가 그것을 편지로 어김없이 전했소,\""], ["“You had something to say to me, the next time I was in your neighbourhood.", "하고 나는 말을 이었다. \"당신은 내가 다음에 당신 근방에 오면 나에게 할 말이 있었소."], ["Well, Mr. Candy, here I am!”", "자, 캔디 씨, 내가 여기 왔소!\""]], [["“Here you are!”", "\"당신이 여기 왔군!\""], ["echoed the doctor.", "하고 그 의사가 되뇌었다."], ["“And Betteredge was quite right.", "\"그리고 베터리지가 아주 옳았소."], ["I had something to say to you.", "나는 당신에게 할 말이 있었소."], ["That was my message.", "그것이 나의 전갈이었소."], ["Betteredge is a wonderful man.", "베터리지는 놀라운 사람이오."], ["What a memory!", "그 무슨 기억력이오!"], ["At his age, what a memory!”", "그 나이에, 그 무슨 기억력이오!\""]], [["He dropped back into silence, and began picking at his fingers again.", "그는 도로 침묵에 빠져, 다시 제 손가락을 만지작거리기 시작했다."], ["Recollecting what I had heard from Betteredge about the effect of the fever on his memory, I went on with the conversation, in the hope that I might help him at starting.", "그 열병이 그의 기억에 미친 효과에 대해 베터리지에게서 들은 것을 떠올리며, 나는 그가 시작하는 것을 도울 수 있을까 하는 희망으로 대화를 이어 갔다."]], [["“It’s a long time since we met,” I said.", "\"우리가 만난 지 오래되었소,\""], ["“We last saw each other at the last birthday dinner my poor aunt was ever to give.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"우리는 나의 가엾은 이모가 여태 열 마지막 생일 만찬에서 서로를 마지막으로 보았소.\""]], [["“That’s it!” cried Mr.", "\"바로 그거요!\""], ["Candy. “The birthday dinner!”", "하고 캔디 씨가 부르짖었다."], ["He started impulsively to his feet, and looked at me.", "\"그 생일 만찬!\" 그는 충동적으로 벌떡 일어나, 나를 바라보았다."], ["A deep flush suddenly overspread his faded face, and he abruptly sat down again, as if conscious of having betrayed a weakness which he would fain have concealed.", "깊은 홍조가 별안간 그의 바랜 얼굴에 번졌고, 그는, 마치 기꺼이 감추었을 약함을 드러냈음을 자각한 듯, 갑자기 다시 앉았다."], ["It was plain, pitiably plain, that he was aware of his own defect of memory, and that he was bent on hiding it from the observation of his friends.", "그가 제 자신의 기억의 결함을 알고 있다는 것, 그리고 그가 그것을 벗들의 눈에서 감추려 애쓰고 있다는 것이, 뚜렷했다, 가엾도록 뚜렷했다."]], [["Thus far he had appealed to my compassion only.", "여기까지 그는 오직 나의 연민에만 호소했다."], ["But the words he had just said—few as they were—roused my curiosity instantly to the highest pitch.", "허나 그가 방금 한 말은—몇 마디 안 되었건만—대뜸 나의 호기심을 가장 높은 정점으로 일으켜 세웠다."], ["The birthday dinner had already become the one event in the past, at which I looked back with strangely-mixed feelings of hope and distrust.", "그 생일 만찬은 이미, 내가 희망과 불신의 이상하게 뒤섞인 느낌으로 되돌아보는, 과거의 그 유일한 사건이 되어 있었다."], ["And here was the birthday dinner unmistakably proclaiming itself as the subject on which Mr. Candy had something important to say to me!", "그런데 여기 그 생일 만찬이, 캔디 씨가 나에게 할 중요한 말이 있는 그 화제임을 잘못 볼 여지 없이 선언하고 있었던 것이다!"]], [["I attempted to help him out once more.", "나는 다시 한 번 그를 거들려 했다."], ["But, this time, my own interests were at the bottom of my compassionate motive, and they hurried me on a little too abruptly, to the end I had in view.", "허나, 이번에는, 나 자신의 이해가 나의 연민 어린 동기의 밑바탕에 있었고, 그것들이 나를, 내가 염두에 둔 그 끝으로, 조금 너무 갑작스레 재촉했다."]], [["“It’s nearly a year now,”", "\"이제 거의 한 해가 되었소,\""], ["I said, “since we sat at that pleasant table.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 그 즐거운 식탁에 앉은 지."], ["Have you made any memorandum—in your diary, or otherwise—of what you wanted to say to me?”", "당신은 나에게 하고 싶었던 말을—당신의 일기에나, 아니면 달리—무슨 각서로 적어 두었소?\""]], [["Mr. Candy understood the suggestion, and showed me that he understood it, as an insult.", "캔디 씨는 그 제안을 이해했고, 그가 그것을 하나의 모욕으로 이해했음을 나에게 보였다."]], [["“I require no memorandum, Mr. Blake,”", "\"나는 아무 각서도 필요치 않소, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["he said, stiffly enough.", "하고 그가 자못 뻣뻣하게 말했다."], ["“I am not such a very old man, yet—and my memory (thank God) is to be thoroughly depended on!”", "\"나는 아직 그리 늙은이가 아니오—그리고 나의 기억은 (하느님께 감사하게도) 완전히 믿어도 좋소!\""]], [["It is needless to say that I declined to understand that he was offended with me.", "그가 나에게 언짢아졌음을 내가 이해하기를 마다한 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다."]], [["“I wish I could say the same of my memory,” I answered.", "\"나의 기억에 대해서도 그리 말할 수 있으면 좋으련만,\""], ["“When I try to think of matters that are a year old, I seldom find my remembrance as vivid as I could wish it to be.", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"내가 한 해 묵은 일들을 생각하려 하면, 나는 좀처럼 나의 기억이 내가 바랄 만큼 생생함을 보지 못하오."], ["Take the dinner at Lady Verinder’s, for instance——”", "이를테면 레이디 버린더의 그 만찬을 들어 봅시다——\""]], [["Mr. Candy brightened up again, the moment the allusion passed my lips.", "캔디 씨는, 그 말이 내 입술을 스치는 순간, 다시 환해졌다."]], [["“Ah!", "\"아!"], ["the dinner, the dinner at Lady Verinder’s!”", "그 만찬, 레이디 버린더의 그 만찬!\""], ["he exclaimed, more eagerly than ever.", "하고 그가 여느 때보다 더 간절히 외쳤다."], ["“I have got something to say to you about that.”", "\"나는 그것에 대해 당신에게 할 말이 있소.\""]], [["His eyes looked at me again with the painful expression of inquiry, so wistful, so vacant, so miserably helpless to see.", "그의 눈이 다시 그 애처로운 물음의 표정을 담고 나를 보았다, 그토록 아쉬워하고, 그토록 텅 비고, 그토록 비참하도록 어쩔 줄 몰라 하며."], ["He was evidently trying hard, and trying in vain, to recover the lost recollection.", "그는 뚜렷이, 그 잃어버린 기억을 되찾으려 애쓰고 있었으나, 헛되이 애쓰고 있었다."], ["“It was a very pleasant dinner,”", "\"그것은 아주 즐거운 만찬이었소,\""], ["he burst out suddenly, with an air of saying exactly what he wanted to say.", "하고 그가, 꼭 하고 싶은 말을 하는 척하는 태도로, 별안간 터뜨렸다."], ["“A very pleasant dinner, Mr. Blake, wasn’t it?”", "\"아주 즐거운 만찬이었소, 블레이크 씨, 그렇지 않소?\""], ["He nodded and smiled, and appeared to think, poor fellow, that he had succeeded in concealing the total failure of his memory, by a well-timed exertion of his own presence of mind.", "그는 고개를 끄덕이고 미소 지으며, 가엾게도, 자기가 제 침착함을 알맞게 발휘함으로써 제 기억의 완전한 실패를 감추는 데에 성공했다고 여기는 듯했다."]], [["It was so distressing that I at once shifted the talk—deeply as I was interested in his recovering the lost remembrance—to topics of local interest.", "그것이 어찌나 괴로웠던지 나는—그가 그 잃어버린 기억을 되찾는 데에 그토록 깊이 관심을 두었건만—대뜸 이야기를 그 고장의 관심사로 옮겼다."]], [["Here, he got on glibly enough.", "여기서, 그는 자못 술술 나아갔다."], ["Trumpery little scandals and quarrels in the town, some of them as much as a month old, appeared to recur to his memory readily.", "그 읍의 하찮은 자잘한 뒷공론과 다툼이, 그중 얼마쯤은 한 달이나 묵은 것이었건만, 어렵잖게 그의 기억에 되살아나는 듯했다."], ["He chattered on, with something of the smooth gossiping fluency of former times.", "그는, 지난 시절의 매끄러운 수다의 유창함을 얼마쯤 담고, 재잘거려 나갔다."], ["But there were moments, even in the full flow of his talkativeness, when he suddenly hesitated—looked at me for a moment with the vacant inquiry once more in his eyes—controlled himself—and went on again.", "허나, 그의 수다스러움이 한창 흐를 때조차, 그가 별안간 망설이는—다시 한 번 그 눈에 텅 빈 물음을 담고 잠시 나를 보고—자신을 다잡고—다시 나아가는 순간들이 있었다."], ["I submitted patiently to my martyrdom (it is surely nothing less than martyrdom to a man of cosmopolitan sympathies, to absorb in silent resignation the news of a country town?) until the clock on the chimney-piece told me that my visit had been prolonged beyond half an hour.", "나는 참을성 있게 나의 순교를 견뎠다 (온 세상에 마음이 열린 사람에게, 어느 시골 읍의 소식을 말없는 체념 속에 빨아들이는 것은, 정녕 순교나 다름없지 않은가?)—벽난로 위의 시계가 나의 방문이 반 시간을 넘어 늘어졌음을 나에게 일러 줄 때까지."], ["Having now some right to consider the sacrifice as complete, I rose to take leave.", "이제 그 희생을 온전한 것으로 여길 얼마쯤의 권리가 생겼으니, 나는 작별하려 일어섰다."], ["As we shook hands, Mr. Candy reverted to the birthday festival of his own accord.", "우리가 악수할 때, 캔디 씨는 제 발로 그 생일 잔치로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“I am so glad we have met again,”", "\"우리가 다시 만나 참으로 기쁘오,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I had it on my mind—I really had it on my mind, Mr. Blake, to speak to you.", "\"나는 그것을 마음에 두고 있었소—나는 정말이지 그것을 마음에 두고 있었소, 블레이크 씨, 당신에게 말하려고."], ["About the dinner at Lady Verinder’s, you know?", "레이디 버린더의 그 만찬에 대해서 말이오, 아시겠소?"], ["A pleasant dinner—really a pleasant dinner now, wasn’t it?”", "즐거운 만찬—정말이지 즐거운 만찬이었소, 그렇지 않소?\""]], [["On repeating the phrase, he seemed to feel hardly as certain of having prevented me from suspecting his lapse of memory, as he had felt on the first occasion.", "그 말을 되뇌면서, 그는 제 기억의 실수를 내가 의심하는 것을 자기가 막았다는 것을, 처음 때만큼 확신하는 것 같지는 않았다."], ["The wistful look clouded his face again; and, after apparently designing to accompany me to the street door, he suddenly changed his mind, rang the bell for the servant, and remained in the drawing-room.", "그 아쉬워하는 표정이 다시 그의 얼굴을 흐렸다. 그리고, 겉보기에 나를 현관문까지 배웅하려 하다가, 그는 별안간 마음을 바꾸어, 하인을 부르는 종을 울리고, 그 응접실에 남았다."]], [["I went slowly down the doctor’s stairs, feeling the disheartening conviction that he really had something to say which it was vitally important to me to hear, and that he was morally incapable of saying it.", "나는 그 의사의 계단을 천천히 내려왔다, 그가 정녕 내가 듣는 것이 나에게 아주 중요한 무언가를 말할 것이 있으나, 그가 도덕적으로 그것을 말할 능력이 없다는, 맥 빠지는 확신을 느끼면서."], ["The effort of remembering that he wanted to speak to me was, but too evidently, the only effort that his enfeebled memory was now able to achieve.", "그가 나에게 말하고 싶어 했음을 기억하는 그 애씀이, 너무나 뚜렷이, 그의 약해진 기억이 이제 이룰 수 있는 유일한 애씀이었다."]], [["Just as I reached the bottom of the stairs, and had turned a corner on my way to the outer hall, a door opened softly somewhere on the ground floor of the house, and a gentle voice said behind me:—", "내가 막 그 계단 밑에 다다라, 바깥 현관홀로 가는 길에 모퉁이를 돌았을 때, 그 집의 아래층 어딘가에서 문 하나가 사뿐히 열리고, 부드러운 목소리가 내 뒤에서 말했다—"]], [["“I am afraid, sir, you find Mr. Candy sadly changed?”", "\"안됐지만, 선생, 당신은 캔디 씨가 서글피 변한 것을 보시겠지요?\""]], [["I turned round, and found myself face to face with Ezra Jennings.", "나는 돌아섰고, 에즈라 제닝스와 얼굴을 마주하게 되었다."]], [["# CHAPTER IX", "# CHAPTER IX"]], [["The doctor’s pretty housemaid stood waiting for me, with the street door open in her hand.", "그 의사의 어여쁜 하녀가, 현관문을 손에 연 채, 나를 기다리며 서 있었다."], ["Pouring brightly into the hall, the morning light fell full on the face of Mr. Candy’s assistant when I turned, and looked at him.", "그 현관홀로 환히 쏟아져 드는 아침 빛이, 내가 돌아서서 그를 보았을 때, 캔디 씨의 조수의 얼굴에 가득 떨어졌다."]], [["It was impossible to dispute Betteredge’s assertion that the appearance of Ezra Jennings, speaking from a popular point of view, was against him.", "에즈라 제닝스의 모습이, 세간의 관점에서 말하자면, 그에게 불리하다는 베터리지의 주장에는 반박할 도리가 없었다."], ["His gipsy-complexion, his fleshless cheeks, his gaunt facial bones, his dreamy eyes, his extraordinary parti-coloured hair, the puzzling contradiction between his face and figure which made him look old and young both together—were all more or less calculated to produce an unfavourable impression of him on a stranger’s mind.", "그의 집시 같은 낯빛, 그의 살 없는 두 뺨, 그의 앙상한 얼굴뼈, 그의 꿈꾸는 듯한 눈, 그의 유별난 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼, 그를 늙어 보이는 동시에 젊어 보이게 하는 그의 얼굴과 몸매 사이의 어리둥절한 모순—이 모든 것이 다소간, 낯선 이의 마음에 그에 대한 좋지 않은 인상을 자아내게끔 되어 있었다."], ["And yet—feeling this as I certainly did—it is not to be denied that Ezra Jennings made some inscrutable appeal to my sympathies, which I found it impossible to resist.", "그럼에도—내가 정녕 이것을 느끼면서도—에즈라 제닝스가 나의 연민에 어떤 헤아릴 수 없는 호소를 했고, 내가 그것에 저항하기가 불가능했음은 부인할 수 없다."], ["While my knowledge of the world warned me to answer the question which he had put, acknowledging that I did indeed find Mr. Candy sadly changed, and then to proceed on my way out of the house—my interest in Ezra Jennings held me rooted to the place, and gave him the opportunity of speaking to me in private about his employer, for which he had been evidently on the watch.", "세상에 대한 나의 앎이, 그가 던진 물음에 답하여, 내가 정녕 캔디 씨가 서글피 변한 것을 보았음을 인정하고, 그런 다음 그 집을 나서는 내 길을 가라고 나에게 일렀건만—에즈라 제닝스에 대한 나의 관심이 나를 그 자리에 붙박아 두어, 그가 뚜렷이 노리고 있던, 그의 고용주에 대해 나에게 사사로이 이야기할 기회를 그에게 주었다."]], [["“Are you walking my way, Mr. Jennings?”", "\"내 길과 같은 길을 걸으시오, 제닝스 씨?\""], ["I said, observing that he held his hat in his hand.", "하고 나는, 그가 모자를 손에 든 것을 보고 말했다."], ["“I am going to call on my aunt, Mrs. Ablewhite.”", "\"나는 내 이모 에이블화이트 부인을 찾아가는 길이오.\""]], [["Ezra Jennings replied that he had a patient to see, and that he was walking my way.", "에즈라 제닝스는 자기가 볼 환자가 있으며, 자기가 내 길과 같은 길을 걷는다고 답했다."]], [["We left the house together.", "우리는 함께 그 집을 떠났다."], ["I observed that the pretty servant girl—who was all smiles and amiability, when I wished her good morning on my way out—received a modest little message from Ezra Jennings, relating to the time at which he might be expected to return, with pursed-up lips, and with eyes which ostentatiously looked anywhere rather than look in his face.", "나는 그 어여쁜 하녀가—내가 나가는 길에 안녕을 빌었을 때는 온통 미소와 상냥함이었건만—입술을 오므린 채, 그의 얼굴을 보느니 차라리 어디든 딴 데를 보란 듯 보는 눈으로, 그가 언제쯤 돌아올지에 관련된 에즈라 제닝스의 조촐한 자그마한 전갈을 받는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["The poor wretch was evidently no favourite in the house.", "그 가엾은 몹쓸 사람은 뚜렷이 그 집에서 아무에게도 사랑받지 못했다."], ["Out of the house, I had Betteredge’s word for it that he was unpopular everywhere.", "그 집 밖에서는, 그가 어디서든 인기가 없다는 베터리지의 말이 있었다. \"이 무슨 삶인가!\""], ["“What a life!” I thought to myself, as we descended the doctor’s doorsteps.", "하고 나는, 우리가 그 의사의 현관 계단을 내려오면서, 속으로 생각했다."]], [["Having already referred to Mr. Candy’s illness on his side, Ezra Jennings now appeared determined to leave it to me to resume the subject.", "이미 제 편에서 캔디 씨의 병을 언급했으므로, 에즈라 제닝스는 이제 그 화제를 다시 꺼내는 것을 나에게 맡기기로 정한 듯했다."], ["His silence said significantly, “It’s your turn now.”", "그의 침묵은 뜻깊게 말하고 있었다, \"이제 당신 차례요.\""], ["I, too, had my reasons for referring to the doctor’s illness: and I readily accepted the responsibility of speaking first.", "나도, 그 의사의 병을 언급할 나 나름의 이유가 있었다. 그래서 나는 먼저 말할 책임을 기꺼이 받아들였다."]], [["“Judging by the change I see in him,”", "\"그에게서 내가 보는 그 변화로 판단하건대,\""], ["I began, “Mr.", "하고 나는 입을 뗐다."], ["Candy’s illness must have been far more serious than I had supposed?”", "\"캔디 씨의 병은 내가 짐작했던 것보다 훨씬 더 심각했음이 틀림없소?\""]], [["“It is almost a miracle,”", "\"그가 그것을 견뎌 내고 살아남은 것은,\""], ["said Ezra Jennings, “that he lived through it.”", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"거의 기적이오.\""]], [["“Is his memory never any better than I have found it today?", "\"그의 기억은 오늘 내가 본 것보다 조금도 더 나아진 적이 없소?"], ["He has been trying to speak to me——”", "그가 나에게 무언가 말하려 애쓰고 있었소——\""]], [["“Of something which happened before he was taken ill?”", "\"그가 병에 걸리기 전에 일어난 무언가에 대해서요?\""], ["asked the assistant, observing that I hesitated.", "하고 그 조수가, 내가 망설이는 것을 보고 물었다."]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“His memory of events, at that past time, is hopelessly enfeebled,”", "\"그 지난 시절의 사건에 대한 그의 기억은, 가망 없이 약해졌소,\""], ["said Ezra Jennings.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다."], ["“It is almost to be deplored, poor fellow, that even the wreck of it remains.", "\"가엾은 사람, 그것의 잔해라도 남아 있다는 것이 오히려 애석할 지경이오."], ["While he remembers dimly plans that he formed—things, here and there, that he had to say or do before his illness—he is perfectly incapable of recalling what the plans were, or what the thing was that he had to say or do.", "그는 자기가 세운 계획들—병에 걸리기 전에 이러저러하게 말하거나 해야 했던 것들을 어렴풋이 기억하면서도—그 계획이 무엇이었는지, 또는 그가 말하거나 해야 했던 그 일이 무엇이었는지를 떠올리는 것은 완전히 불가능하오."], ["He is painfully conscious of his own deficiency, and painfully anxious, as you must have seen, to hide it from observation.", "그는 제 자신의 결함을 아프도록 자각하고, 당신도 보았겠지만, 그것을 남의 눈에서 감추려 아프도록 안달하오."], ["If he could only have recovered in a complete state of oblivion as to the past, he would have been a happier man.", "만일 그가 과거에 대한 온전한 망각의 상태로 회복될 수만 있었다면, 그는 더 행복한 사람이었을 거요."], ["Perhaps we should all be happier,”", "어쩌면 우리 모두가 더 행복할지도 모르오,\""], ["he added, with a sad smile, “if we could but completely forget!”", "하고 그는 서글픈 미소와 함께 덧붙였다. \"우리가 완전히 잊을 수만 있다면!\""]], [["“There are some events surely in all men’s lives,”", "\"모든 사람의 삶에는 정녕 어떤 사건들이 있지 않겠소,\""], ["I replied, “the memory of which they would be unwilling entirely to lose?”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"그것의 기억을 온전히 잃기를 꺼릴 만한?\""]], [["“That is, I hope, to be said of most men, Mr. Blake.", "\"그것은, 바라건대, 대부분의 사람에 대해 할 수 있는 말이겠지요, 블레이크 씨."], ["I am afraid it cannot truly be said of all. Have you any reason to suppose that the lost remembrance which Mr.", "안됐지만 그것이 모든 사람에 대해 참되이 할 수 있는 말은 아닐 것이오."], ["Candy tried to recover—while you were speaking to him just now—was a remembrance which it was important to you that he should recall?”", "캔디 씨가 되찾으려 애쓴 그 잃어버린 기억이—방금 당신이 그와 이야기하는 동안 말이오—그가 그것을 떠올리는 것이 당신에게 중요한 기억이었다고 여길 무슨 근거가 당신에게 있소?\""]], [["In saying those words, he had touched, of his own accord, on the very point upon which I was anxious to consult him.", "그 말을 하면서, 그는, 제 발로, 내가 그와 상의하고 싶어 하던 바로 그 점을 건드린 것이었다."], ["The interest I felt in this strange man had impelled me, in the first instance, to give him the opportunity of speaking to me; reserving what I might have to say, on my side, in relation to his employer, until I was first satisfied that he was a person in whose delicacy and discretion I could trust.", "이 이상한 사람에게 내가 느낀 관심이, 나로 하여금, 우선, 그에게 나에게 이야기할 기회를 주도록 몰아붙였다—그의 고용주에 관해 내 편에서 할 말이 있으면, 그가 내가 그 섬세함과 신중함을 믿을 수 있는 사람임을 먼저 흡족히 여기기까지 미뤄 두면서 말이다."], ["The little that he had said, thus far, had been sufficient to convince me that I was speaking to a gentleman.", "그가 여태 한 그 얼마 안 되는 말이, 내가 신사와 이야기하고 있음을 나에게 확신시키기에 넉넉했다."], ["He had what I may venture to describe as the unsought self-possession, which is a sure sign of good breeding, not in England only, but everywhere else in the civilised world.", "그에게는, 잉글랜드에서뿐 아니라 문명 세계의 다른 어디에서든, 좋은 가문의 확실한 표시인, 내가 감히 구하지 않은 침착함이라 이를 만한 것이 있었다."], ["Whatever the object which he had in view, in putting the question that he had just addressed to me, I felt no doubt that I was justified—so far—in answering him without reserve.", "그가 방금 나에게 던진 그 물음을 던짐에 있어 무슨 목적을 염두에 두었든, 나는 내가—여기까지는—감춤 없이 그에게 답하는 것이 정당하다고 조금도 의심하지 않았다."]], [["“I believe I have a strong interest,”", "\"나는 내가 강한 관심을 지녔다고 믿소,\""], ["I said, “in tracing the lost remembrance which Mr.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"캔디 씨가 떠올리지 못한 그 잃어버린 기억을 좇는 데에."], ["Candy was unable to recall. May I ask whether you can suggest to me any method by which I might assist his memory?”", "내가 그의 기억을 도울 만한 무슨 방법을 당신이 나에게 일러 줄 수 있는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["Ezra Jennings looked at me, with a sudden flash of interest in his dreamy brown eyes.", "에즈라 제닝스는, 그의 꿈꾸는 듯한 갈색 눈에 별안간 관심의 번뜩임을 담고, 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“Mr. Candy’s memory is beyond the reach of assistance,”", "\"캔디 씨의 기억은 도움의 손길이 닿을 수 없는 곳에 있소,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I have tried to help it often enough since his recovery, to be able to speak positively on that point.”", "\"나는 그의 회복 이래 그것을 자못 자주 도우려 애써 왔소, 그 점에 대해 단정적으로 말할 수 있을 만큼.\""]], [["This disappointed me; and I owned it.", "이것이 나를 실망시켰다. 그리고 나는 그것을 털어놓았다."]], [["“I confess you led me to hope for a less discouraging answer than that,”", "\"솔직히 당신은 나로 하여금 그것보다 덜 맥 빠지는 답을 바라게 했소,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["Ezra Jennings smiled. “It may not, perhaps, be a final answer, Mr.", "에즈라 제닝스는 미소 지었다."], ["Blake. It may be possible to trace Mr.", "\"그것이, 어쩌면, 최종의 답은 아닐지도 모르오, 블레이크 씨."], ["Candy’s lost recollection, without the necessity of appealing to Mr. Candy himself.”", "캔디 씨 자신에게 호소할 필요 없이, 캔디 씨의 잃어버린 기억을 좇는 것이 가능할지도 모르오.\""]], [["“Indeed?", "\"정말이오?"], ["Is it an indiscretion, on my part, to ask how?”", "어떻게인지 묻는 것이, 내 편에서 무람없는 일이오?\""]], [["“By no means.", "\"결코 아니오."], ["My only difficulty in answering your question, is the difficulty of explaining myself.", "당신의 물음에 답하는 데에 나의 유일한 어려움은, 나 자신을 설명하는 어려움이오."], ["May I trust to your patience, if I refer once more to Mr. Candy’s illness: and if I speak of it this time without sparing you certain professional details?”", "만일 내가 다시 한 번 캔디 씨의 병을 언급하되, 이번에는 어떤 전문적인 세세한 것들을 당신에게 아끼지 않고 말한다면, 당신의 참을성에 기대어도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Pray go on!", "\"부디 계속하시오!"], ["You have interested me already in hearing the details.”", "당신은 그 세세한 것들을 듣는 데에 이미 나의 관심을 끌었소.\""]], [["My eagerness seemed to amuse—perhaps, I might rather say, to please him.", "나의 간절함이 그를 재미있어하게—어쩌면, 차라리, 기뻐하게 하는 듯했다."], ["He smiled again.", "그는 다시 미소 지었다."], ["We had by this time left the last houses in the town behind us.", "우리는 이때쯤 그 읍의 마지막 집들을 뒤로한 터였다."], ["Ezra Jennings stopped for a moment, and picked some wild flowers from the hedge by the roadside.", "에즈라 제닝스는 잠시 걸음을 멈추고, 길가의 산울타리에서 들꽃 몇 송이를 꺾었다."], ["“How beautiful they are!”", "\"얼마나 아름다운가!\""], ["he said, simply, showing his little nosegay to me.", "하고 그는, 그의 자그마한 꽃다발을 나에게 보이며, 소박하게 말했다."], ["“And how few people in England seem to admire them as they deserve!”", "\"그리고 잉글랜드에서 얼마나 적은 사람들만이 이것들을 마땅한 만큼 감탄하는 것 같은가!\""]], [["“You have not always been in England?”", "\"당신이 늘 잉글랜드에 있었던 것은 아니오?\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“No.", "\"아니오."], ["I was born, and partly brought up, in one of our colonies.", "나는 우리 식민지의 하나에서 태어나, 얼마쯤 거기서 자랐소."], ["My father was an Englishman; but my mother—We are straying away from our subject, Mr.", "내 아버지는 잉글랜드 사람이었소. 허나 내 어머니는—우리가 우리 화제에서 벗어나고 있소, 블레이크 씨."], ["Blake; and it is my fault.", "그리고 그것은 나의 잘못이오."], ["The truth is, I have associations with these modest little hedgeside flowers—It doesn’t matter; we were speaking of Mr.", "실은, 나는 이 조촐하고 자그마한 산울타리 꽃들과 얽힌 추억이 있소—아무래도 좋소."], ["Candy. To Mr.", "우리는 캔디 씨에 대해 이야기하던 참이었소."], ["Candy let us return.”", "캔디 씨에게로 돌아갑시다.\""]], [["Connecting the few words about himself which thus reluctantly escaped him, with the melancholy view of life which led him to place the conditions of human happiness in complete oblivion of the past, I felt satisfied that the story which I had read in his face was, in two particulars at least, the story that it really told.", "이렇게 마지못해 그에게서 새어 나온, 그 자신에 대한 그 몇 마디를, 과거에 대한 온전한 망각 속에 인간 행복의 조건을 두게 한 그의 우울한 인생관과 이어 보고, 나는 그의 얼굴에서 내가 읽은 그 이야기가, 적어도 두 가지 점에서는, 정녕 그것이 말하는 이야기임을 흡족히 여겼다."], ["He had suffered as few men suffer; and there was the mixture of some foreign race in his English blood.", "그는 좀처럼 없을 만큼 괴로움을 겪은 사람이었다. 그리고 그의 잉글랜드 피에는 어떤 이방 종족의 섞임이 있었다."]], [["“You have heard, I dare say, of the original cause of Mr.", "\"당신은, 짐작건대, 캔디 씨의 병의 애초의 원인을 들었겠지요?\""], ["Candy’s illness?” he resumed.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다."], ["“The night of Lady Verinder’s dinner-party was a night of heavy rain.", "\"레이디 버린더의 만찬 밤은 비가 억수같이 쏟아지는 밤이었소."], ["My employer drove home through it in his gig, and reached the house wetted to the skin.", "나의 고용주는 그것을 뚫고 이륜마차로 집에 돌아와, 살갗까지 젖은 채로 그 집에 다다랐소."], ["He found an urgent message from a patient, waiting for him; and he most unfortunately went at once to visit the sick person, without stopping to change his clothes.", "그는 어느 환자에게서 온 급한 전갈이 자기를 기다리고 있음을 알았소. 그리고 그는 더없이 안타깝게도, 옷을 갈아입으러 멈추지 않고, 대뜸 그 병자를 찾아갔소."], ["I was myself professionally detained, that night, by a case at some distance from Frizinghall.", "나 자신은, 그날 밤, 프리징홀에서 얼마쯤 떨어진 곳의 어느 환자에게 볼일로 붙들려 있었소."], ["When I got back the next morning, I found Mr. Candy’s groom waiting in great alarm to take me to his master’s room.", "이튿날 아침 돌아오니, 나는 캔디 씨의 마부가 크게 놀라, 나를 제 주인의 방으로 데려가려 기다리고 있는 것을 보았소."], ["By that time the mischief was done; the illness had set in.”", "그때는 이미 화가 저질러진 뒤였소. 병이 시작되어 있었소.\""]], [["“The illness has only been described to me, in general terms, as a fever,”", "\"그 병은 나에게 그저, 두루뭉술하게, 열병으로만 이야기되었소,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“I can add nothing which will make the description more accurate,”", "\"나는 그 설명을 더 정확하게 만들 것을 아무것도 덧붙일 수 없소,\""], ["answered Ezra Jennings.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 대답했다."], ["“From first to last the fever assumed no specific form.", "\"처음부터 끝까지 그 열병은 어떤 특정한 꼴도 띠지 않았소."], ["I sent at once to two of Mr. Candy’s medical friends in the town, both physicians, to come and give me their opinion of the case.", "나는 대뜸 그 읍의 캔디 씨의 의사 벗 둘, 둘 다 내과의였는데, 그들에게 와서 그 병에 대한 그들의 견해를 달라고 사람을 보냈소."], ["They agreed with me that it looked serious; but they both strongly dissented from the view I took of the treatment.", "그들은 그것이 심각해 보인다는 데에는 나와 뜻을 같이했소. 허나 그들은 둘 다, 그 치료에 대해 내가 취한 견해에는 강하게 반대했소."], ["We differed entirely in the conclusions which we drew from the patient’s pulse.", "우리는 그 환자의 맥에서 우리가 이끌어 낸 결론에서 완전히 갈렸소."], ["The two doctors, arguing from the rapidity of the beat, declared that a lowering treatment was the only treatment to be adopted.", "그 두 의사는, 그 박동의 빠름으로 논하여, 열을 내리는 치료가 채택할 유일한 치료라고 선언했소."], ["On my side, I admitted the rapidity of the pulse, but I also pointed to its alarming feebleness as indicating an exhausted condition of the system, and as showing a plain necessity for the administration of stimulants.", "내 편에서는, 나는 그 맥의 빠름은 인정했으나, 나는 또한 그것의 놀라운 미약함을 몸의 지친 상태를 가리키는 것으로, 그리고 흥분제의 투여의 명백한 필요를 보여 주는 것으로 짚었소."], ["The two doctors were for keeping him on gruel, lemonade, barley-water, and so on.", "그 두 의사는 그를 죽과 레모네이드와 보리차 따위로 지키려 했소."], ["I was for giving him champagne, or brandy, ammonia, and quinine.", "나는 그에게 샴페인이나 브랜디, 암모니아와 키니네를 주려 했소."], ["A serious difference of opinion, as you see!", "보다시피, 심각한 견해차였소!"], ["A difference between two physicians of established local repute, and a stranger who was only an assistant in the house.", "그 고장의 확고한 명성을 지닌 두 내과의와, 그 집의 한낱 조수일 뿐인 낯선 이 사이의 차이였소."], ["For the first few days, I had no choice but to give way to my elders and betters; the patient steadily sinking all the time.", "처음 며칠 동안, 나는 나의 어른이자 윗사람에게 양보하는 것 말고는 도리가 없었소. 그동안 환자는 꾸준히 가라앉았소."], ["I made a second attempt to appeal to the plain, undeniably plain, evidence of the pulse.", "나는 그 명백한, 부인할 수 없이 명백한 맥의 증거에 호소하려 두 번째 시도를 했소."], ["Its rapidity was unchecked, and its feebleness had increased.", "그것의 빠름은 억눌리지 않았고, 그것의 미약함은 더해 있었소."], ["The two doctors took offence at my obstinacy.", "그 두 의사는 나의 고집에 언짢아했소."], ["They said, ‘Mr. Jennings, either we manage this case, or you manage it.", "그들은 말했소, '제닝스 씨, 우리가 이 병을 다루든지, 당신이 다루든지 하시오."], ["Which is it to be?’ I said, ‘Gentlemen, give me five minutes to consider, and that plain question shall have a plain reply.’ When the time expired, I was ready with my answer.", "어느 쪽이오?' 나는 말했소, '선생들, 나에게 헤아릴 오 분을 주시오, 그러면 그 명백한 물음에 명백한 대답이 있을 것이오.'"], ["I said, ‘You positively refuse to try the stimulant treatment?’ They refused in so many words.", "그 시간이 다하자, 나는 내 대답을 준비하고 있었소. 나는 말했소, '당신들은 흥분제 치료를 해 보기를 단호히 거절하오?'"], ["‘I mean to try it at once, gentlemen.’—‘Try it, Mr.", "그들은 그야말로 거절했소."], ["Jennings, and we withdraw from the case.’ I sent down to the cellar for a bottle of champagne; and I administered half a tumbler-full of it to the patient with my own hand.", "'나는 그것을 당장 해 보겠소, 선생들.'—'해 보시오, 제닝스 씨, 그러면 우리는 이 병에서 손을 떼겠소.' 나는 지하실로 샴페인 한 병을 가져오라고 보냈소."], ["The two physicians took up their hats in silence, and left the house.”", "그리고 나는 내 손으로 그것을 큰 잔의 반쯤 환자에게 투여했소. 그 두 내과의는 말없이 제 모자를 집어 들고, 그 집을 떠났소.\""]], [["“You had assumed a serious responsibility,”", "\"당신은 심각한 책임을 떠맡았소,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said. “In your place, I am afraid I should have shrunk from it.”", "\"당신 처지였다면, 안됐지만 나는 그것을 움츠려 피했을 것 같소.\""]], [["“In my place, Mr. Blake, you would have remembered that Mr.", "\"나의 처지였다면, 블레이크 씨, 당신은 캔디 씨가, 당신을 평생 그의 은인의 빚진 자로 만든 상황에서, 당신을 제 밑에 거두었음을 떠올렸을 것이오."], ["Candy had taken you into his employment, under circumstances which made you his debtor for life.", "나의 처지였다면, 당신은 그가 시시각각 가라앉는 것을 보았을 것이오."], ["In my place, you would have seen him sinking, hour by hour; and you would have risked anything, rather than let the one man on earth who had befriended you, die before your eyes.", "그리고 당신은, 이 땅에서 당신을 도운 유일한 사람이 당신의 눈앞에서 죽도록 두느니, 차라리 무엇이든 무릅썼을 것이오."], ["Don’t suppose that I had no sense of the terrible position in which I had placed myself!", "내가 나 자신을 놓아둔 그 끔찍한 처지에 대한 아무 감각도 없었다고 여기지 마시오!"], ["There were moments when I felt all the misery of my friendlessness, all the peril of my dreadful responsibility.", "나의 벗 없음의 온갖 비참을, 나의 그 끔찍한 책임의 온갖 위태로움을 느낀 순간들이 있었소."], ["If I had been a happy man, if I had led a prosperous life, I believe I should have sunk under the task I had imposed on myself.", "만일 내가 행복한 사람이었다면, 만일 내가 번창한 삶을 살아왔다면, 나는 내가 나 자신에게 지운 그 과업 아래 가라앉았으리라 믿소."], ["But I had no happy time to look back at, no past peace of mind to force itself into contrast with my present anxiety and suspense—and I held firm to my resolution through it all.", "허나 나에게는 되돌아볼 행복한 시절이 없었고, 나의 현재의 근심과 마음 졸임에 억지로 대비될 지난 마음의 평안이 없었소—그리고 나는 그 모든 것을 견디며 나의 결의를 굳게 지켰소."], ["I took an interval in the middle of the day, when my patient’s condition was at its best, for the repose I needed.", "나는 내 환자의 상태가 가장 좋은 한낮에, 내가 필요로 하는 안식을 위해 짬을 냈소."], ["For the rest of the four-and-twenty hours, as long as his life was in danger, I never left his bedside.", "남은 스물네 시간 동안은, 그의 목숨이 위태로운 한, 나는 결코 그의 잠자리를 떠나지 않았소."], ["Towards sunset, as usual in such cases, the delirium incidental to the fever came on.", "해 질 녘에, 그런 경우에 흔히 그렇듯, 그 열병에 딸린 섬망이 왔소."], ["It lasted more or less through the night; and then intermitted, at that terrible time in the early morning—from two o’clock to five—when the vital energies even of the healthiest of us are at their lowest.", "그것은 밤새 다소간 이어지다가, 이른 아침의 그 끔찍한 시각—두 시부터 다섯 시까지, 우리 중 가장 건강한 이의 생명력조차 가장 낮은 그때에—뜸해졌소."], ["It is then that Death gathers in his human harvest most abundantly.", "죽음이 제 인간의 수확을 가장 넉넉히 거두어들이는 것이 바로 그때요."], ["It was then that Death and I fought our fight over the bed, which should have the man who lay on it.", "죽음과 내가, 그 위에 누운 사람을 누가 차지할지, 그 침상을 두고 우리의 싸움을 벌인 것이 바로 그때요."], ["I never hesitated in pursuing the treatment on which I had staked everything.", "나는 내가 온갖 것을 건 그 치료를 밀고 나감에 있어 결코 망설이지 않았소."], ["When wine failed, I tried brandy.", "포도주가 듣지 않으면, 나는 브랜디를 시도했소."], ["When the other stimulants lost their influence, I doubled the dose.", "다른 흥분제가 그 힘을 잃으면, 나는 그 양을 두 배로 했소."], ["After an interval of suspense—the like of which I hope to God I shall never feel again—there came a day when the rapidity of the pulse slightly, but appreciably, diminished; and, better still, there came also a change in the beat—an unmistakable change to steadiness and strength.", "마음 졸임의 사이—다시는 결코 느끼지 않기를 하느님께 바라는 그런—뒤에, 그 맥의 빠름이 조금이나마, 허나 알아볼 만하게 줄어든 날이 왔소. 그리고, 더 좋게도, 그 박동에도 변화가 왔소—꾸준함과 힘으로의, 잘못 볼 여지 없는 변화가."], ["Then, I knew that I had saved him; and then I own I broke down.", "그때, 나는 내가 그를 구했음을 알았소. 그리고 그때 나는, 인정하거니와, 무너졌소."], ["I laid the poor fellow’s wasted hand back on the bed, and burst out crying.", "나는 그 가엾은 사람의 여윈 손을 침상에 도로 뉘고, 울음을 터뜨렸소."], ["An hysterical relief, Mr. Blake—nothing more!", "발작적인 안도였소, 블레이크 씨—그 이상 아무것도 아니었소!"], ["Physiology says, and says truly, that some men are born with female constitutions—and I am one of them!”", "생리학은 말하오, 그리고 참되이 말하오, 어떤 사람들은 여성의 체질을 타고난다고—그리고 나는 그중 하나요!\""]], [["He made that bitterly professional apology for his tears, speaking quietly and unaffectedly, as he had spoken throughout.", "그는 그의 눈물에 대해 그 씁쓸하게 전문적인 변명을, 내내 말해 온 대로, 조용하고 꾸밈없이 말했다."], ["His tone and manner, from beginning to end, showed him to be especially, almost morbidly, anxious not to set himself up as an object of interest to me.", "그의 어조와 태도는, 처음부터 끝까지, 그가 각별히, 거의 병적으로, 나에게 관심의 대상으로 자신을 내세우지 않으려 안달함을 보여 주었다."]], [["“You may well ask, why I have wearied you with all these details?” he went on.", "\"당신은 정녕, 내가 어째서 당신을 이 온갖 세세한 것들로 지치게 했는지 물을 만하오,\""], ["“It is the only way I can see, Mr.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다."], ["Blake, of properly introducing to you what I have to say next. Now you know exactly what my position was, at the time of Mr.", "\"그것이, 블레이크 씨, 내가 다음으로 할 말을 당신에게 제대로 소개할, 내가 보는 유일한 길이오."], ["Candy’s illness, you will the more readily understand the sore need I had of lightening the burden on my mind by giving it, at intervals, some sort of relief.", "이제 당신은 캔디 씨의 병 무렵에 나의 처지가 정확히 어떠했는지 아니, 당신은, 이따금, 내 마음의 짐에 무슨 안도를 줌으로써 그것을 덜어야 할 그 절실한 필요를 더 어렵잖게 이해할 것이오."], ["I have had the presumption to occupy my leisure, for some years past, in writing a book, addressed to the members of my profession—a book on the intricate and delicate subject of the brain and the nervous system.", "나는 몇 해 전부터, 나의 여가를, 나의 직업의 사람들에게 부치는 책—뇌와 신경계라는 그 복잡하고 섬세한 주제에 대한 책을 쓰는 데에 쓰는 주제넘음을 부려 왔소. 나의 저작은 아마 결코 끝나지 않을 것이오."], ["My work will probably never be finished; and it will certainly never be published.", "그리고 그것은 확실히 결코 출판되지 않을 것이오. 그럼에도 그것은 숱한 외로운 시간의 벗이었소."], ["It has none the less been the friend of many lonely hours; and it helped me to while away the anxious time—the time of waiting, and nothing else—at Mr.", "그리고 그것은 내가 캔디 씨의 잠자리에서 그 애타는 시간—기다림의, 그 밖의 아무것도 아닌 시간을 때우는 것을 도왔소."], ["Candy’s bedside. I told you he was delirious, I think?", "내가 그가 섬망 상태였다고 당신에게 말한 것 같소?"], ["And I mentioned the time at which his delirium came on?”", "그리고 내가 그의 섬망이 온 시각을 언급했지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Well, I had reached a section of my book, at that time, which touched on this same question of delirium.", "\"자, 나는 그때, 나의 책에서 바로 이 섬망의 문제를 건드린 한 부분에 이른 참이었소."], ["I won’t trouble you at any length with my theory on the subject—I will confine myself to telling you only what it is your present interest to know.", "그 주제에 대한 나의 이론으로 당신을 길게 성가시게 하지는 않겠소—나는 나 자신을, 오직 당신이 지금 알 관심이 있는 것만 당신에게 말하는 데에 국한하겠소."], ["It has often occurred to me in the course of my medical practice, to doubt whether we can justifiably infer—in cases of delirium—that the loss of the faculty of speaking connectedly, implies of necessity the loss of the faculty of thinking connectedly as well. Poor Mr.", "나는 나의 의료 행위 가운데 자주, 섬망의 경우에—조리 있게 말하는 능력의 상실이 필연적으로 조리 있게 생각하는 능력의 상실도 뜻한다고, 우리가 정당하게 미루어 짐작할 수 있는지 의심하곤 했소."], ["Candy’s illness gave me an opportunity of putting this doubt to the test.", "가엾은 캔디 씨의 병이 이 의심을 시험대에 올릴 기회를 나에게 주었소. 나는 속기의 기술을 아오."], ["I understand the art of writing in shorthand; and I was able to take down the patient’s ‘wanderings’, exactly as they fell from his lips.—Do you see, Mr. Blake, what I am coming to at last?”", "그리고 나는 그 환자의 '헛소리'를, 그것이 그의 입술에서 떨어지는 그대로 정확히 받아 적을 수 있었소.—보이오, 블레이크 씨, 내가 마침내 무엇에 이르고 있는지?\""]], [["I saw it clearly, and waited with breathless interest to hear more.", "나는 그것을 또렷이 보았고, 더 듣기를 숨죽인 관심으로 기다렸다."]], [["“At odds and ends of time,”", "\"자투리 시간에,\" 하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말을 이었다."], ["Ezra Jennings went on, “I reproduced my shorthand notes, in the ordinary form of writing—leaving large spaces between the broken phrases, and even the single words, as they had fallen disconnectedly from Mr. Candy’s lips.", "\"나는 나의 속기 기록을, 여느 글자의 꼴로 옮겼소—캔디 씨의 입술에서 조각조각 떨어진 그대로, 끊어진 어구들 사이에, 심지어 낱낱의 낱말들 사이에도, 큰 빈칸을 남겨 두면서."], ["I then treated the result thus obtained, on something like the principle which one adopts in putting together a child’s ‘puzzle.’ It is all confusion to begin with; but it may be all brought into order and shape, if you can only find the right way.", "그런 다음 나는 이렇게 얻은 결과를, 아이의 '조각 맞추기'를 맞출 때 쓰는 것과 비슷한 원리로 다루었소. 처음에는 죄 뒤죽박죽이오."], ["Acting on this plan, I filled in each blank space on the paper, with what the words or phrases on either side of it suggested to me as the speaker’s meaning; altering over and over again, until my additions followed naturally on the spoken words which came before them, and fitted naturally into the spoken words which came after them.", "허나 당신이 옳은 길만 찾을 수 있다면, 그것은 죄 질서와 꼴로 이끌어질 수 있소. 이 방책에 따라, 나는 종이 위의 빈칸 하나하나를, 그 양편의 낱말이나 어구가 나에게 말하는 이의 뜻으로 넌지시 일러 준 것으로 채웠소."], ["The result was, that I not only occupied in this way many vacant and anxious hours, but that I arrived at something which was (as it seemed to me) a confirmation of the theory that I held.", "나의 보탬이, 그것들 앞에 오는 말해진 낱말들에 자연스레 이어지고, 그것들 뒤에 오는 말해진 낱말들에 자연스레 들어맞기까지, 몇 번이고 고치면서."], ["In plainer words, after putting the broken sentences together I found the superior faculty of thinking going on, more or less connectedly, in my patient’s mind, while the inferior faculty of expression was in a state of almost complete incapacity and confusion.”", "그 결과는, 내가 이런 식으로 숱한 텅 비고 애타는 시간을 채웠을 뿐 아니라, 내가 (나에게 보이기로는) 내가 지닌 이론의 확증인 무언가에 이르렀다는 것이었소. 더 쉬운 말로 하면, 그 끊어진 문장들을 맞춘 뒤, 나는 내 환자의 마음속에서, 생각하는 그 우월한 능력이 다소간 조리 있게 이어지고 있는 것을 보았소, 표현하는 그 열등한 능력은 거의 완전한 무능과 혼란의 상태에 있는 동안에 말이오.\""]], [["“One word!”", "\"한마디만!\""], ["I interposed eagerly.", "하고 나는 간절히 끼어들었다."], ["“Did my name occur in any of his wanderings?”", "\"그의 헛소리 어딘가에 내 이름이 나왔소?\""]], [["“You shall hear, Mr. Blake.", "\"들어 보시오, 블레이크 씨."], ["Among my written proofs of the assertion which I have just advanced—or, I ought to say, among the written experiments, tending to put my assertion to the proof—there is one, in which your name occurs.", "내가 방금 내놓은 그 주장의 나의 글로 된 증거들 가운데—또는, 내 주장을 증명하는 데로 향하는, 글로 된 실험들이라 해야겠소만—당신의 이름이 나오는 것이 하나 있소."], ["For nearly the whole of one night, Mr. Candy’s mind was occupied with something between himself and you.", "거의 온 하룻밤 동안, 캔디 씨의 마음은 그 자신과 당신 사이의 무언가에 사로잡혀 있었소."], ["I have got the broken words, as they dropped from his lips, on one sheet of paper.", "나는 그 끊어진 낱말들을, 그것들이 그의 입술에서 떨어진 그대로, 한 장의 종이에 지니고 있소."], ["And I have got the links of my own discovering which connect those words together, on another sheet of paper.", "그리고 나는 그 낱말들을 이어 주는, 나 자신이 발견한 그 고리들을 다른 한 장의 종이에 지니고 있소."], ["The product (as the arithmeticians would say) is an intelligible statement—first, of something actually done in the past; secondly, of something which Mr. Candy contemplated doing in the future, if his illness had not got in the way, and stopped him.", "그 곱셈의 결과는 (산술가들이라면 그리 말하겠지요) 알아들을 수 있는 진술이오—첫째로, 과거에 실제로 이루어진 무언가에 대한, 둘째로, 만일 그의 병이 길을 막아 그를 멈추게 하지 않았다면 캔디 씨가 미래에 하려고 궁리한 무언가에 대한."], ["The question is whether this does, or does not, represent the lost recollection which he vainly attempted to find when you called on him this morning?”", "문제는, 이것이, 당신이 오늘 아침 그를 찾아왔을 때 그가 헛되이 찾으려 애쓴 그 잃어버린 기억을 나타내느냐, 아니냐 하는 것이오.\""]], [["“Not a doubt of it!” I answered.", "\"조금도 의심할 바 없소!\""], ["“Let us go back directly, and look at the papers!”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"당장 돌아가, 그 종이들을 봅시다!\""]], [["“Quite impossible, Mr. Blake.”", "\"아주 불가능하오, 블레이크 씨.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Put yourself in my position for a moment,”", "\"잠시 내 처지에 서 보시오,\""], ["said Ezra Jennings.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다."], ["“Would you disclose to another person what had dropped unconsciously from the lips of your suffering patient and your helpless friend, without first knowing that there was a necessity to justify you in opening your lips?”", "\"당신이라면, 당신의 입을 여는 것을 정당화할 무슨 필요가 있는지를 먼저 알지도 못한 채, 당신의 앓는 환자이자 어쩔 도리 없는 벗의 입술에서 무심결에 떨어진 것을 다른 사람에게 털어놓겠소?\""]], [["I felt that he was unanswerable, here; but I tried to argue the question, nevertheless.", "나는 그가 여기서 반박할 여지가 없음을 느꼈다. 허나 나는 그럼에도 그 문제를 따져 보려 했다."]], [["“My conduct in such a delicate matter as you describe,”", "\"당신이 말한 그런 섬세한 일에서의 나의 처신은,\""], ["I replied, “would depend greatly on whether the disclosure was of a nature to compromise my friend or not.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"그 털어놓음이 나의 벗을 위태롭게 할 성질의 것인지 아닌지에 크게 달렸을 것이오.\""]], [["“I have disposed of all necessity for considering that side of the question, long since,”", "\"나는 그 문제의 그 면을 헤아릴 온갖 필요를, 오래전에 처리했소,\""], ["said Ezra Jennings.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다."], ["“Wherever my notes included anything which Mr. Candy might have wished to keep secret, those notes have been destroyed.", "\"나의 기록이 캔디 씨가 비밀로 지키고 싶어 했을 무언가를 담은 곳은 어디든, 그 기록은 없애 버렸소."], ["My manuscript experiments at my friend’s bedside, include nothing, now, which he would have hesitated to communicate to others, if he had recovered the use of his memory.", "내 벗의 잠자리에서의 나의 원고 실험은, 이제, 그가 제 기억의 쓰임을 되찾았다면 남들에게 전하기를 망설였을 것을 아무것도 담고 있지 않소."], ["In your case, I have every reason to suppose that my notes contain something which he actually wished to say to you.”", "당신의 경우에는, 나의 기록이 그가 정녕 당신에게 말하고 싶어 했던 무언가를 담고 있다고 여길 온갖 근거가 나에게 있소.\""]], [["“And yet, you hesitate?”", "\"그런데도, 당신은 망설이오?\""]], [["“And yet, I hesitate.", "\"그런데도, 나는 망설이오."], ["Remember the circumstances under which I obtained the information which I possess!", "내가 지닌 그 정보를 얻은 상황을 기억하시오!"], ["Harmless as it is, I cannot prevail upon myself to give it up to you, unless you first satisfy me that there is a reason for doing so.", "그것이 아무리 해롭지 않다 한들, 나는, 당신이 먼저 그리할 이유가 있음을 나에게 흡족히 보여 주지 않는 한, 그것을 당신에게 내줄 마음을 나 자신에게 내키게 할 수 없소."], ["He was so miserably ill, Mr. Blake!", "그는 어찌나 비참하게 앓았던지, 블레이크 씨!"], ["and he was so helplessly dependent upon Me!", "그리고 그는 어찌나 어쩔 도리 없이 나에게 의지했던지!"], ["Is it too much to ask, if I request you only to hint to me what your interest is in the lost recollection—or what you believe that lost recollection to be?”", "만일 내가 당신에게, 그 잃어버린 기억에 당신의 관심이 무엇인지—또는 당신이 그 잃어버린 기억을 무엇이라 믿는지 그저 넌지시 일러 주기만을 청한다면, 그것이 너무한 부탁이오?\""]], [["To have answered him with the frankness which his language and his manner both claimed from me, would have been to commit myself to openly acknowledging that I was suspected of the theft of the Diamond.", "그의 말과 그의 태도가 둘 다 나에게 요구하는 그 솔직함으로 그에게 답하는 것은, 내가 그 다이아몬드의 절도로 의심받고 있음을 드러내 놓고 인정하는 데에 나 자신을 매어 두는 것이었으리라."], ["Strongly as Ezra Jennings had intensified the first impulsive interest which I had felt in him, he had not overcome my unconquerable reluctance to disclose the degrading position in which I stood.", "에즈라 제닝스가 내가 그에게 느낀 그 첫 충동적 관심을 아무리 강하게 북돋웠어도, 그는 내가 놓여 있는 그 욕된 처지를 털어놓기를 꺼리는 나의 이겨 낼 수 없는 마음을 이겨 내지는 못했다."], ["I took refuge once more in the explanatory phrases with which I had prepared myself to meet the curiosity of strangers.", "나는 다시 한 번, 낯선 이들의 호기심을 맞으려 나 자신을 채비시킨 그 설명하는 말들에 몸을 숨겼다."]], [["This time I had no reason to complain of a want of attention on the part of the person to whom I addressed myself.", "이번에는, 나는 내가 말을 건네는 그 사람 편에서 주의가 모자라다고 불평할 이유가 없었다."], ["Ezra Jennings listened patiently, even anxiously, until I had done.", "에즈라 제닝스는, 내가 마칠 때까지, 참을성 있게, 애타게까지 들었다."]], [["“I am sorry to have raised your expectations, Mr. Blake, only to disappoint them,”", "\"당신의 기대를 부풀려 놓고, 그것을 실망시키기만 하게 되어 안됐소, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["he said. “Throughout the whole period of Mr.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["Candy’s illness, from first to last, not one word about the Diamond escaped his lips.", "\"캔디 씨의 병의 온 기간 내내, 처음부터 끝까지, 그 다이아몬드에 대해서는 한마디도 그의 입술에서 새어 나오지 않았소."], ["The matter with which I heard him connect your name has, I can assure you, no discoverable relation whatever with the loss or the recovery of Miss Verinder’s jewel.”", "내가 당신의 이름과 이어지는 것을 들은 그 일은, 장담하건대, 버린더 아가씨의 보석의 분실이나 되찾음과는 조금도 알아볼 만한 관련이 없소.\""]], [["We arrived, as he said those words, at a place where the highway along which we had been walking branched off into two roads.", "우리는, 그가 그 말을 하면서, 우리가 걸어오던 그 큰길이 두 갈래 길로 갈라지는 곳에 다다랐다."], ["One led to Mr. Ablewhite’s house, and the other to a moorland village some two or three miles off.", "하나는 에이블화이트 씨의 집으로 이어졌고, 다른 하나는 두세 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 황야의 마을로 이어졌다."], ["Ezra Jennings stopped at the road which led to the village.", "에즈라 제닝스는 그 마을로 이어지는 길에서 멈추었다."]], [["“My way lies in this direction,”", "\"나의 길은 이 방향에 있소,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“I am really and truly sorry, Mr. Blake, that I can be of no use to you.”", "\"나는 정말이지 참으로 안됐소, 블레이크 씨, 내가 당신에게 아무 쓸모도 될 수 없어서.\""]], [["His voice told me that he spoke sincerely.", "그의 목소리는 나에게 그가 진심으로 말함을 일러 주었다."], ["His soft brown eyes rested on me for a moment with a look of melancholy interest.", "그의 부드러운 갈색 눈이 우울한 관심의 눈길로 잠시 나에게 머물렀다."], ["He bowed, and went, without another word, on his way to the village.", "그는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 한마디 없이, 그 마을로 가는 제 길을 갔다."]], [["For a minute or more I stood and watched him, walking farther and farther away from me; carrying farther and farther away with him what I now firmly believed to be the clue of which I was in search.", "일 분 남짓, 나는 서서 그가 나에게서 점점 더 멀리 걸어가는 것을 지켜보았다. 내가 이제 굳게 찾고 있는 그 실마리라 믿는 것을 그와 함께 점점 더 멀리 가져가는 것을."], ["He turned, after walking on a little way, and looked back.", "그는 얼마쯤 걸어가고 나서, 돌아서, 뒤돌아보았다."], ["Seeing me still standing at the place where we had parted, he stopped, as if doubting whether I might not wish to speak to him again.", "내가 여전히 우리가 헤어진 그 자리에 서 있는 것을 보고, 그는, 마치 내가 그와 다시 이야기하고 싶어 하는 것은 아닌가 미심쩍어하듯, 걸음을 멈추었다."], ["There was no time for me to reason out my own situation—to remind myself that I was losing my opportunity, at what might be the turning point of my life, and all to flatter nothing more important than my own self-esteem!", "나 자신의 처지를 따져 볼—내 생애의 전환점일지도 모를 때에 내가 나의 기회를 잃고 있으며, 그것이 죄 나 자신의 자존심보다 조금도 더 중요할 것 없는 것을 만족시키려는 것임을 나 자신에게 일깨울 짬은 없었다!"], ["There was only time to call him back first, and to think afterwards.", "먼저 그를 도로 부르고, 나중에 생각할 짬밖에 없었다."], ["I suspect I am one of the rashest of existing men.", "나는 살아 있는 사람 가운데 가장 경솔한 이의 하나인 것 같다."], ["I called him back—and then I said to myself, “Now there is no help for it.", "나는 그를 도로 불렀다—그러고는 나 자신에게 말했다, \"이제 어쩔 도리가 없다."], ["I must tell him the truth!”", "나는 그에게 진실을 말해야 한다!\""]], [["He retraced his steps directly.", "그는 대뜸 제 걸음을 되짚어 왔다."], ["I advanced along the road to meet him.", "나는 그를 맞으러 그 길을 따라 나아갔다."]], [["“Mr. Jennings,”", "\"제닝스 씨,\""], ["I said. “I have not treated you quite fairly.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["My interest in tracing Mr.", "\"나는 당신을 아주 공정하게 대하지 않았소."], ["Candy’s lost recollection is not the interest of recovering the Moonstone.", "캔디 씨의 잃어버린 기억을 좇는 데에 대한 나의 관심은 그 월장석을 되찾는 관심이 아니오."], ["A serious personal matter is at the bottom of my visit to Yorkshire.", "심각한 개인적 일이 나의 요크셔 방문의 밑바탕에 있소."], ["I have but one excuse for not having dealt frankly with you in this matter.", "이 일에서 당신에게 솔직히 대하지 않은 데에 나는 오직 한 가지 변명거리가 있소."], ["It is more painful to me than I can say, to mention to anybody what my position really is.”", "나의 처지가 정말로 어떠한지를 누구에게든 말하는 것이, 나에게는 이루 말할 수 없이 괴롭소.\""]], [["Ezra Jennings looked at me with the first appearance of embarrassment which I had seen in him yet.", "에즈라 제닝스는, 내가 여태 그에게서 본 처음의 당혹한 기색을 띠고, 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“I have no right, Mr. Blake, and no wish,”", "\"나에게는 아무 권리도 없고, 아무 바람도 없소, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["he said, “to intrude myself into your private affairs.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 사사로운 일에 나를 밀어 넣을."], ["Allow me to ask your pardon, on my side, for having (most innocently) put you to a painful test.”", "내 편에서, (더없이 무심결에) 당신을 괴로운 시험에 들게 한 데에 대해, 당신의 용서를 구하도록 허락해 주시오.\""]], [["“You have a perfect right,”", "\"당신에게는 온전한 권리가 있소,\""], ["I rejoined, “to fix the terms on which you feel justified in revealing what you heard at Mr.", "하고 나는 되받았다. \"캔디 씨의 잠자리에서 들은 것을 드러내는 것이 정당하다고 느낄 조건을 정할."], ["Candy’s bedside. I understand and respect the delicacy which influences you in this matter.", "나는 이 일에서 당신을 움직이는 그 섬세함을 이해하고 존중하오."], ["How can I expect to be taken into your confidence if I decline to admit you into mine?", "내가 당신을 나의 믿음 안에 들이기를 마다한다면, 어찌 내가 당신의 믿음 안에 받아들여지기를 바라겠소?"], ["You ought to know, and you shall know, why I am interested in discovering what Mr. Candy wanted to say to me.", "당신은 마땅히 알아야 하고, 알게 될 것이오, 어째서 내가 캔디 씨가 나에게 말하고 싶어 한 것을 알아내는 데에 관심을 두는지."], ["If I turn out to be mistaken in my anticipations, and if you prove unable to help me when you are really aware of what I want, I shall trust to your honour to keep my secret—and something tells me that I shall not trust in vain.”", "만일 내가 나의 예상에서 틀린 것으로 드러나고, 만일 당신이 내가 원하는 것을 정말로 알게 되었을 때 나를 도울 수 없는 것으로 드러난다면, 나는 나의 비밀을 지켜 줄 당신의 명예를 믿겠소—그리고 무언가가 나에게, 내가 헛되이 믿는 것이 아니리라고 일러 주오.\""]], [["“Stop, Mr. Blake.", "\"멈추시오, 블레이크 씨."], ["I have a word to say, which must be said before you go any farther.”", "나는 당신이 조금이라도 더 나아가기 전에 해야만 할 말이 있소.\""]], [["I looked at him in astonishment.", "나는 놀라움에 그를 바라보았다."], ["The grip of some terrible emotion seemed to have seized him, and shaken him to the soul.", "어떤 끔찍한 감정의 움켜쥠이 그를 사로잡아, 그를 넋까지 뒤흔들어 놓은 듯했다. 그의 집시 같은 낯빛은 검푸르뎅뎅한 잿빛 창백함으로 변해 있었다."], ["His gipsy complexion had altered to a livid greyish paleness; his eyes had suddenly become wild and glittering; his voice had dropped to a tone—low, stern, and resolute—which I now heard for the first time.", "그의 눈은 별안간 사납고 번들거리게 되었다. 그의 목소리는 어떤 어조로—낮고, 준엄하고, 단호한, 내가 이제 처음으로 듣는 어조로 떨어졌다."], ["The latent resources in the man, for good or for evil—it was hard, at that moment, to say which—leapt up in him and showed themselves to me, with the suddenness of a flash of light.", "그 사람 안의 잠재된 힘이, 선을 향한 것인지 악을 향한 것인지—그 순간에는 어느 쪽인지 말하기 어려웠다—그의 안에서 솟구쳐, 한 줄기 섬광의 느닷없음으로 나에게 제 모습을 드러냈다."]], [["“Before you place any confidence in me,”", "\"당신이 나에게 조금이라도 믿음을 두기 전에,\""], ["he went on, “you ought to know, and you must know, under what circumstances I have been received into Mr.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"당신은 마땅히 알아야 하고, 알아야만 하오, 내가 어떤 상황에서 캔디 씨의 집에 거두어졌는지."], ["Candy’s house. It won’t take long.", "오래 걸리지 않을 것이오."], ["I don’t profess, sir, to tell my story (as the phrase is) to any man.", "나는, 선생, (흔히 하는 말로) 어떤 사람에게든 나의 이야기를 하겠다고 짐짓 나서지 않소."], ["My story will die with me.", "나의 이야기는 나와 함께 죽을 것이오."], ["All I ask, is to be permitted to tell you, what I have told Mr. Candy.", "내가 청하는 것은 다만, 내가 캔디 씨에게 말한 것을 당신에게 말하도록 허락받는 것뿐이오."], ["If you are still in the mind, when you have heard that, to say what you have proposed to say, you will command my attention and command my services.", "만일 당신이, 그것을 듣고 나서도, 당신이 하겠다고 한 말을 할 마음이면, 당신은 나의 주의를 부리고 나의 도움을 부릴 것이오."], ["Shall we walk on?”", "걸으면서 이야기할까요?\""]], [["The suppressed misery in his face silenced me.", "그의 얼굴의 억눌린 비참함이 나를 잠잠하게 했다."], ["I answered his question by a sign.", "나는 하나의 몸짓으로 그의 물음에 답했다."], ["We walked on.", "우리는 걸어 나아갔다."]], [["After advancing a few hundred yards, Ezra Jennings stopped at a gap in the rough stone wall which shut off the moor from the road, at this part of it.", "몇백 야드 나아가고서, 에즈라 제닝스는, 이 부분에서 그 황야를 길과 갈라놓는 거친 돌담의 터진 곳에서 멈추었다."]], [["“Do you mind resting a little, Mr.", "\"조금 쉬어도 괜찮겠소, 블레이크 씨?\""], ["Blake?” he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."], ["“I am not what I was—and some things shake me.”", "\"나는 예전의 내가 아니오—그리고 어떤 것들은 나를 뒤흔드오.\""]], [["I agreed, of course.", "나는, 물론, 그러자고 했다."], ["He led the way through the gap to a patch of turf on the heathy ground, screened by bushes and dwarf trees on the side nearest to the road, and commanding in the opposite direction a grandly desolate view over the broad brown wilderness of the moor.", "그는 그 터진 곳을 지나, 길과 가장 가까운 쪽으로는 덤불과 왜소한 나무들에 가려지고, 맞은편 방향으로는 그 황야의 드넓은 갈색 벌판 너머로 장엄하도록 황량한 경치가 내다보이는, 그 히스 우거진 땅의 잔디밭 한 뙈기로 앞장서 갔다."], ["The clouds had gathered, within the last half hour.", "지난 반 시간 사이에 구름이 몰려든 터였다."], ["The light was dull; the distance was dim.", "빛은 흐렸다. 먼 곳은 어슴푸레했다."], ["The lovely face of Nature met us, soft and still colourless—met us without a smile.", "자연의 그 사랑스러운 얼굴이, 부드럽고 여전히 빛깔 없이, 우리를 맞았다—미소 없이 우리를 맞았다."]], [["We sat down in silence.", "우리는 말없이 앉았다."], ["Ezra Jennings laid aside his hat, and passed his hand wearily over his forehead, wearily through his startling white and black hair.", "에즈라 제닝스는 제 모자를 벗어 놓고, 지친 듯 제 이마를, 지친 듯 제 놀라운 흑백의 머리칼을 손으로 쓸었다."], ["He tossed his little nosegay of wild flowers away from him, as if the remembrances which it recalled were remembrances which hurt him now.", "그는, 마치 그것이 되살리는 추억이 이제 그를 아프게 하는 추억이기라도 한 양, 그의 자그마한 들꽃 꽃다발을 제게서 던져 버렸다."]], [["“Mr. Blake!”", "\"블레이크 씨!\""], ["he said, suddenly.", "하고 그가 별안간 말했다."], ["“You are in bad company.", "\"당신은 나쁜 벗과 함께 있소."], ["The cloud of a horrible accusation has rested on me for years.", "어떤 끔찍한 혐의의 먹구름이 여러 해 동안 나에게 얹혀 있었소."], ["I tell you the worst at once.", "나는 대뜸 최악을 당신에게 말하오."], ["I am a man whose life is a wreck, and whose character is gone.”", "나는 삶이 잔해이고, 명예가 사라진 사람이오.\""]], [["I attempted to speak.", "나는 말하려 했다."], ["He stopped me.", "그가 나를 멈추게 했다."]], [["“No,” he said.", "\"아니오,\""], ["“Pardon me; not yet.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"용서하시오."], ["Don’t commit yourself to expressions of sympathy which you may afterwards wish to recall.", "아직은. 당신이 나중에 도로 거두고 싶어 할지 모를 동정의 표현에 당신 자신을 매어 두지 마시오."], ["I have mentioned an accusation which has rested on me for years.", "나는 여러 해 동안 나에게 얹혀 있는 혐의를 언급했소."], ["There are circumstances in connexion with it that tell against me.", "그것과 이어진, 나에게 불리하게 말하는 상황들이 있소."], ["I cannot bring myself to acknowledge what the accusation is.", "나는 그 혐의가 무엇인지 인정할 마음을 나 자신에게 낼 수 없소."], ["And I am incapable, perfectly incapable, of proving my innocence.", "그리고 나는 나의 결백을 증명할 능력이 없소, 완전히 없소."], ["I can only assert my innocence.", "나는 다만 나의 결백을 주장할 수 있을 뿐이오."], ["I assert it, sir, on my oath, as a Christian.", "나는 그것을, 선생, 그리스도인으로서, 나의 맹세를 걸고 주장하오."], ["It is useless to appeal to my honour as a man.”", "사내로서의 나의 명예에 호소하는 것은 헛일이오.\""]], [["He paused again.", "그는 다시 멈추었다."], ["I looked round at him.", "나는 그를 돌아보았다."], ["He never looked at me in return.", "그는 답으로 결코 나를 보지 않았다."], ["His whole being seemed to be absorbed in the agony of recollecting, and in the effort to speak.", "그의 온 존재가, 떠올리는 고통에, 그리고 말하려는 애씀에 온통 사로잡힌 듯했다."]], [["“There is much that I might say,”", "\"내가 말할 수 있는 것이 많소,\""], ["he went on, “about the merciless treatment of me by my own family, and the merciless enmity to which I have fallen a victim.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"나 자신의 가족의 나에 대한 인정사정없는 처사와, 내가 그 희생물로 떨어진 그 인정사정없는 적의에 대해."], ["But the harm is done; the wrong is beyond all remedy.", "허나 해는 저질러졌소. 그 잘못은 온갖 바로잡음의 너머에 있소."], ["I decline to weary or distress you, sir, if I can help it.", "나는, 할 수만 있다면, 당신을 지치게 하거나 괴롭히기를 마다하오, 선생."], ["At the outset of my career in this country, the vile slander to which I have referred struck me down at once and for ever.", "이 나라에서 나의 이력의 첫머리에, 내가 언급한 그 비열한 중상이 나를 대뜸, 그리고 영영 쓰러뜨렸소."], ["I resigned my aspirations in my profession—obscurity was the only hope left for me.", "나는 나의 직업에서의 나의 포부를 버렸소—무명이 나에게 남은 유일한 희망이었소."], ["I parted with the woman I loved—how could I condemn her to share my disgrace?", "나는 내가 사랑하는 여인과 헤어졌소—어찌 내가 그녀에게 나의 치욕을 함께 나누라고 선고할 수 있었겠소?"], ["A medical assistant’s place offered itself, in a remote corner of England.", "잉글랜드의 어느 외진 구석에, 의사 조수의 자리 하나가 났소."], ["I got the place.", "나는 그 자리를 얻었소."], ["It promised me peace; it promised me obscurity, as I thought.", "그것은 나에게 평안을 약속했소. 그것은, 내 생각으로는, 나에게 무명을 약속했소."], ["I was wrong.", "나는 틀렸소."], ["Evil report, with time and chance to help it, travels patiently, and travels far.", "나쁜 소문은, 시간과 우연이 그것을 도우면, 참을성 있게, 그리고 멀리 나아가오."], ["The accusation from which I had fled followed me.", "내가 달아난 그 혐의가 나를 뒤따라왔소."], ["I got warning of its approach.", "나는 그것이 다가옴을 경고받았소."], ["I was able to leave my situation voluntarily, with the testimonials that I had earned.", "나는 내가 얻은 추천장과 함께, 나의 자리를 제 발로 떠날 수 있었소."], ["They got me another situation in another remote district.", "그것들이 다른 외진 지역의 다른 자리를 나에게 구해 주었소. 다시 세월이 흘렀소."], ["Time passed again; and again the slander that was death to my character found me out.", "그리고 다시 나의 명예에 죽음인 그 중상이 나를 찾아냈소. 이번에는 나에게 아무 경고가 없었소."], ["On this occasion I had no warning. My employer said, ‘Mr.", "나의 고용주가 말했소, '제닝스 씨, 나는 당신에게 아무 불만이 없소."], ["Jennings, I have no complaint to make against you; but you must set yourself right, or leave me.’ I had but one choice—I left him.", "허나 당신은 당신의 결백을 밝히든지, 나를 떠나든지 해야 하오.' 나에게는 오직 한 가지 선택뿐이었소—나는 그를 떠났소."], ["It’s useless to dwell on what I suffered after that.", "그 뒤에 내가 겪은 것을 곱씹어 봐야 헛일이오."], ["I am only forty years old now.", "나는 이제 겨우 마흔 살이오."], ["Look at my face, and let it tell for me the story of some miserable years.", "내 얼굴을 보시오, 그리고 그것이 나를 대신해 어떤 비참한 세월의 이야기를 말하게 두시오."], ["It ended in my drifting to this place, and meeting with Mr. Candy.", "그것은 내가 이곳으로 흘러들어, 캔디 씨와 만나는 것으로 끝났소."], ["He wanted an assistant.", "그는 조수를 원했소."], ["I referred him, on the question of capacity, to my last employer.", "나는 그를, 능력의 문제에 대해서는, 나의 지난 고용주에게 알아보게 했소. 명예의 문제가 남았소."], ["The question of character remained.", "나는 그에게 내가 당신에게 말한 것을—그리고 그 이상을 말했소."], ["I told him what I have told you—and more.", "나는 그에게, 그가 나를 믿더라도, 길에 어려움이 있음을 경고했소."], ["I warned him that there were difficulties in the way, even if he believed me.", "'여기서도, 다른 데서처럼,' 하고 나는 말했소, '나는 가명으로 사는 그 죄지은 회피를 경멸하오."], ["‘Here, as elsewhere,’ I said ‘I scorn the guilty evasion of living under an assumed name: I am no safer at Frizinghall than at other places from the cloud that follows me, go where I may.’ He answered, ‘I don’t do things by halves—I believe you, and I pity you.", "나는 프리징홀에서도, 어디를 가든 나를 뒤따르는 그 먹구름으로부터, 다른 곳에서보다 조금도 더 안전하지 않소.' 그가 대답했소, '나는 일을 어중간하게 하지 않소—나는 당신을 믿고, 당신을 가엾이 여기오."], ["If you will risk what may happen, I will risk it too.’ God Almighty bless him!", "만일 당신이 무슨 일이 일어날지를 무릅쓰겠다면, 나도 그것을 무릅쓰겠소.'"], ["He has given me shelter, he has given me employment, he has given me rest of mind—and I have the certain conviction (I have had it for some months past) that nothing will happen now to make him regret it.”", "전능하신 하느님이 그를 축복하시기를! 그는 나에게 비호를 주었고, 나에게 일자리를 주었고, 나에게 마음의 안식을 주었소—그리고 나는 이제 (지난 몇 달 동안 지녀 온) 확실한 확신이 있소, 그가 그것을 후회하게 만들 아무 일도 이제 일어나지 않으리라는.\""]], [["“The slander has died out?”", "\"그 중상은 사그라졌소?\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“The slander is as active as ever.", "\"그 중상은 여느 때와 다름없이 살아 있소."], ["But when it follows me here, it will come too late.”", "허나 그것이 여기로 나를 뒤따라올 때에는, 너무 늦게 올 것이오.\""]], [["“You will have left the place?”", "\"당신이 그 자리를 떠나 있을 테니?\""]], [["“No, Mr. Blake—I shall be dead.", "\"아니오, 블레이크 씨—나는 죽어 있을 것이오."], ["For ten years past I have suffered from an incurable internal complaint.", "지난 십 년 동안 나는 고칠 수 없는 몸속의 병으로 앓아 왔소."], ["I don’t disguise from you that I should have let the agony of it kill me long since, but for one last interest in life, which makes my existence of some importance to me still.", "나는 당신에게 숨기지 않소, 나의 삶을 아직 나에게 얼마쯤 중요하게 만드는, 삶에 대한 한 가지 마지막 관심이 아니었다면, 나는 그것의 고통이 오래전에 나를 죽이도록 두었으리라는 것을."], ["I want to provide for a person—very dear to me—whom I shall never see again.", "나는 한 사람을—나에게 아주 소중한, 내가 다시는 보지 못할 사람을 돌보아 주고 싶소."], ["My own little patrimony is hardly sufficient to make her independent of the world.", "나 자신의 자그마한 유산은 그녀를 세상으로부터 독립하게 하기에 좀처럼 넉넉지 않소."], ["The hope, if I could only live long enough, of increasing it to a certain sum, has impelled me to resist the disease by such palliative means as I could devise.", "내가 오래 살 수만 있다면 그것을 어떤 액수까지 불릴 그 희망이, 나로 하여금 내가 궁리할 수 있는 완화 수단으로 그 병에 맞서게 몰아붙였소."], ["The one effectual palliative in my case, is—opium.", "나의 경우에 그 하나의 효과 있는 완화제는—아편이오."], ["To that all-potent and all-merciful drug I am indebted for a respite of many years from my sentence of death.", "그 전능하고 자비로운 약에, 나는 나의 사형 선고로부터 여러 해의 유예를 빚졌소."], ["But even the virtues of opium have their limit.", "허나 아편의 미덕조차 그 한계가 있소."], ["The progress of the disease has gradually forced me from the use of opium to the abuse of it.", "그 병의 진행이 나를 점점, 아편의 쓰임에서 그것의 남용으로 억지로 몰아넣었소."], ["I am feeling the penalty at last.", "나는 마침내 그 벌을 받고 있소."], ["My nervous system is shattered; my nights are nights of horror.", "나의 신경계는 산산조각 났소. 나의 밤은 공포의 밤이오."], ["The end is not far off now.", "이제 그 끝이 멀지 않소."], ["Let it come—I have not lived and worked in vain.", "그것이 오게 두시오—나는 헛되이 살고 일한 것이 아니오."], ["The little sum is nearly made up; and I have the means of completing it, if my last reserves of life fail me sooner than I expect.", "그 자그마한 액수는 거의 마련되었소. 그리고 나에게는, 나의 마지막 생명의 밑천이 내가 예상하는 것보다 더 빨리 나를 저버리더라도, 그것을 채울 수단이 있소."], ["I hardly know how I have wandered into telling you this.", "나는 내가 어쩌다 이것을 당신에게 말하는 데로 헤매어 들었는지 좀처럼 모르겠소."], ["I don’t think I am mean enough to appeal to your pity.", "나는 내가 당신의 연민에 호소할 만큼 비열하다고는 생각지 않소."], ["Perhaps, I fancy you may be all the readier to believe me, if you know that what I have said to you, I have said with the certain knowledge in me that I am a dying man.", "어쩌면, 내가 당신에게 한 말이, 내가 죽어 가는 사람임을 내 안에 확실히 알고서 한 말임을 당신이 안다면, 당신은 그만큼 더 어렵잖게 나를 믿어 줄지도 모른다고 나는 짐작하오."], ["There is no disguising, Mr. Blake, that you interest me.", "숨길 것도 없소, 블레이크 씨, 당신이 나의 관심을 끈다는 것을."], ["I have attempted to make my poor friend’s loss of memory the means of bettering my acquaintance with you.", "나는 나의 가엾은 벗의 기억 상실을, 당신과의 사귐을 나아지게 하는 수단으로 삼으려 했소."], ["I have speculated on the chance of your feeling a passing curiosity about what he wanted to say, and of my being able to satisfy it.", "나는 당신이 그가 말하고 싶어 한 것에 대해 지나가는 호기심을 느끼고, 내가 그것을 만족시킬 수 있을 요행을 노렸소."], ["Is there no excuse for my intruding myself on you?", "내가 나 자신을 당신에게 밀어 넣은 데에 아무 변명거리가 없소?"], ["Perhaps there is some excuse.", "어쩌면 얼마쯤 변명거리가 있소."], ["A man who has lived as I have lived has his bitter moments when he ponders over human destiny.", "나처럼 살아온 사람은 인간의 운명을 곰곰이 헤아릴 때 쓰라린 순간들이 있소."], ["You have youth, health, riches, a place in the world, a prospect before you.", "당신에게는 젊음, 건강, 부, 세상에서의 자리, 앞에 놓인 전망이 있소."], ["You, and such as you, show me the sunny side of human life, and reconcile me with the world that I am leaving, before I go.", "당신과, 당신 같은 이들이, 나에게 인간 삶의 볕 드는 쪽을 보여 주고, 내가 떠나기 전에 내가 떠나는 그 세상과 나를 화해시켜 주오."], ["However this talk between us may end, I shall not forget that you have done me a kindness in doing that.", "우리 사이의 이 이야기가 어떻게 끝나든, 나는 당신이 그리함으로써 나에게 친절을 베풀었음을 잊지 않을 것이오."], ["It rests with you, sir, to say what you proposed saying, or to wish me good morning.”", "이제 당신에게 달렸소, 선생, 당신이 하겠다고 한 말을 하든지, 아니면 나에게 안녕을 고하든지.\""]], [["I had but one answer to make to that appeal.", "나는 그 호소에 할 대답이 오직 하나뿐이었다."], ["Without a moment’s hesitation I told him the truth, as unreservedly as I have told it in these pages.", "한순간의 망설임도 없이 나는 그에게 진실을, 내가 이 쪽들에서 그것을 말해 온 것만큼 감춤 없이 말했다."]], [["He started to his feet, and looked at me with breathless eagerness as I approached the leading incident of my story.", "그는 내가 나의 이야기의 그 핵심 사건에 다가서자, 벌떡 일어나, 숨죽인 간절함으로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“It is certain that I went into the room,”", "\"내가 그 방에 들어간 것은 확실하오,\""], ["I said; “it is certain that I took the Diamond.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"내가 그 다이아몬드를 가져간 것은 확실하오."], ["I can only meet those two plain facts by declaring that, do what I might, I did it without my own knowledge——”", "나는 그 두 명백한 사실을, 내가 무엇을 했든, 나는 그것을 나 자신도 모르게 했다고 선언함으로써 맞설 수밖에 없소——\""]], [["Ezra Jennings caught me excitedly by the arm.", "에즈라 제닝스가 흥분하여 내 팔을 붙들었다."]], [["“Stop!”", "\"멈추시오!\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“You have suggested more to me than you suppose.", "\"당신은 당신이 짐작하는 것보다 더 많은 것을 나에게 넌지시 일러 주었소."], ["Have you ever been accustomed to the use of opium?”", "당신은 여태 아편의 쓰임에 익숙했던 적이 있소?\""]], [["“I never tasted it in my life.”", "\"나는 평생 그것을 맛본 적이 없소.\""]], [["“Were your nerves out of order, at this time last year?", "\"이때쯤 지난해에 당신의 신경이 어긋나 있었소?"], ["Were you unusually restless and irritable?”", "당신은 유달리 안절부절못하고 짜증스러웠소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그랬소.\""]], [["“Did you sleep badly?”", "\"당신은 잠을 잘 못 잤소?\""]], [["“Wretchedly.", "\"형편없었소."], ["Many nights I never slept at all.”", "숱한 밤 나는 아예 잠들지 못했소.\""]], [["“Was the birthday night an exception?", "\"그 생일 밤은 예외였소?"], ["Try, and remember.", "애써, 기억해 보시오."], ["Did you sleep well on that one occasion?”", "당신은 그 한 번은 잘 잤소?\""]], [["“I do remember!", "\"정녕 기억하오!"], ["I slept soundly.”", "나는 곤히 잤소.\""]], [["He dropped my arm as suddenly as he had taken it—and looked at me with the air of a man whose mind was relieved of the last doubt that rested on it.", "그는 내 팔을 잡았던 것만큼 별안간 놓았다—그리고 제 마음에 얹혀 있던 마지막 의심을 던 사람의 태도로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“This is a marked day in your life, and in mine,” he said, gravely.", "\"이것은 당신의 삶에서, 그리고 나의 삶에서 특기할 날이오,\""], ["“I am absolutely certain, Mr.", "하고 그가 무겁게 말했다."], ["Blake, of one thing—I have got what Mr. Candy wanted to say to you this morning, in the notes that I took at my patient’s bedside.", "\"나는 한 가지를 절대적으로 확신하오, 블레이크 씨—나는 캔디 씨가 오늘 아침 당신에게 말하고 싶어 한 것을, 내 환자의 잠자리에서 받아 적은 그 기록 속에 지니고 있소."], ["Wait!", "잠깐!"], ["that is not all.", "그것이 전부가 아니오."], ["I am firmly persuaded that I can prove you to have been unconscious of what you were about, when you entered the room and took the Diamond.", "나는, 당신이 그 방에 들어가 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔을 때 당신이 당신 자신이 무엇을 하는지 자각하지 못하고 있었음을 내가 증명할 수 있다고 굳게 믿소."], ["Give me time to think, and time to question you.", "나에게 생각할 시간과, 당신에게 물을 시간을 주시오."], ["I believe the vindication of your innocence is in my hands!”", "나는 당신의 결백을 밝히는 것이 내 손안에 있다고 믿소!\""]], [["“Explain yourself, for God’s sake!", "\"제발, 당신 자신을 설명하시오!"], ["What do you mean?”", "무슨 뜻이오?\""]], [["In the excitement of our colloquy, we had walked on a few steps, beyond the clump of dwarf trees which had hitherto screened us from view.", "우리의 이야기의 흥분 속에서, 우리는 그때껏 우리를 남의 눈에서 가려 주던 그 왜소한 나무들의 무리 너머로, 몇 걸음 걸어 나온 터였다."], ["Before Ezra Jennings could answer me, he was hailed from the high road by a man, in great agitation, who had been evidently on the look-out for him.", "에즈라 제닝스가 나에게 답하기도 전에, 그는 그 큰길에서, 뚜렷이 그를 지켜보고 있던 한 사내에 의해, 크게 안달하며 불렸다."]], [["“I am coming,”", "\"가오,\""], ["he called back; “I am coming as fast as I can!”", "하고 그가 되받아 소리쳤다. \"되도록 빨리 가오!\""], ["He turned to me.", "그는 나에게로 돌아섰다."], ["“There is an urgent case waiting for me at the village yonder; I ought to have been there half an hour since—I must attend to it at once.", "\"저기 저 마을에 나를 기다리는 급한 환자가 있소. 나는 반 시간 전에 거기 있어야 했소—나는 당장 그것을 돌봐야 하오."], ["Give me two hours from this time, and call at Mr. Candy’s again—and I will engage to be ready for you.”", "지금부터 두 시간을 나에게 주고, 캔디 씨에게 다시 들르시오—그러면 나는 당신을 위해 채비하고 있을 것을 약속하오.\""]], [["“How am I to wait!”", "\"내가 어찌 기다린단 말이오!\""], ["I exclaimed, impatiently.", "하고 나는 조바심을 내며 외쳤다."], ["“Can’t you quiet my mind by a word of explanation before we part?”", "\"우리가 헤어지기 전에 한마디 설명으로 내 마음을 잠재워 줄 수 없소?\""]], [["“This is far too serious a matter to be explained in a hurry, Mr.", "\"이것은 서둘러 설명하기에는 너무나 심각한 일이오, 블레이크 씨."], ["Blake. I am not wilfully trying your patience—I should only be adding to your suspense, if I attempted to relieve it as things are now.", "나는 일부러 당신의 참을성을 시험하는 것이 아니오—사정이 지금 이러하니, 내가 당신의 마음 졸임을 덜려 한다면 나는 그것을 더할 뿐일 것이오."], ["At Frizinghall, sir, in two hours’ time!”", "프리징홀에서, 선생, 두 시간 뒤에!\""]], [["The man on the high road hailed him again.", "그 큰길의 사내가 그를 다시 불렀다."], ["He hurried away, and left me.", "그는 서둘러 가 버리고, 나를 남겼다."]], [["# CHAPTER X", "# CHAPTER X"]], [["How the interval of suspense in which I was now condemned might have affected other men in my position, I cannot pretend to say.", "내가 이제 처해진 그 마음 졸임의 사이가, 나의 처지에 놓인 다른 사람들에게 어찌 영향을 미쳤을지, 나는 짐짓 말할 수 없다."], ["The influence of the two hours’ probation upon my temperament was simply this.", "그 두 시간의 유예가 나의 기질에 미친 영향은 그저 이러했다."], ["I felt physically incapable of remaining still in any one place, and morally incapable of speaking to any one human being, until I had first heard all that Ezra Jennings had to say to me.", "나는, 에즈라 제닝스가 나에게 할 말을 죄 먼저 듣기까지는, 어느 한 자리에 가만히 있는 것이 몸으로 불가능함을, 그리고 어떤 한 인간에게든 말하는 것이 마음으로 불가능함을 느꼈다."]], [["In this frame of mind, I not only abandoned my contemplated visit to Mrs. Ablewhite—I even shrank from encountering Gabriel Betteredge himself.", "이런 심사로, 나는 궁리했던 에이블화이트 부인에 대한 방문을 버렸을 뿐 아니라—심지어 게이브리얼 베터리지 자신과 마주치는 것조차 움츠려 피했다."]], [["Returning to Frizinghall, I left a note for Betteredge, telling him that I had been unexpectedly called away for a few hours, but that he might certainly expect me to return towards three o’clock in the afternoon.", "프리징홀로 돌아와, 나는 베터리지에게 쪽지 하나를 남겨, 내가 뜻밖에 몇 시간 불려 나갔으나, 오후 세 시경에는 틀림없이 내가 돌아오기를 기대해도 좋다고 일렀다."], ["I requested him, in the interval, to order his dinner at the usual hour, and to amuse himself as he pleased.", "나는 그에게, 그 사이에, 여느 때에 저녁을 시키고, 마음껏 즐기라고 청했다."], ["He had, as I well knew, hosts of friends in Frizinghall; and he would be at no loss how to fill up his time until I returned to the hotel.", "그에게는, 내가 잘 알거니와, 프리징홀에 벗들이 숱했다. 그리고 그는 내가 그 호텔로 돌아올 때까지 제 시간을 채우는 데에 조금도 막힘이 없을 터였다."]], [["This done, I made the best of my way out of the town again, and roamed the lonely moorland country which surrounds Frizinghall, until my watch told me that it was time, at last, to return to Mr. Candy’s house.", "이것을 마치고, 나는 다시 서둘러 그 읍을 나서서, 나의 시계가 마침내 캔디 씨의 집으로 돌아갈 때임을 나에게 일러 줄 때까지, 프리징홀을 둘러싼 그 외로운 황야의 지방을 헤맸다."]], [["I found Ezra Jennings ready and waiting for me.", "나는 에즈라 제닝스가 채비를 갖추고 나를 기다리고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["He was sitting alone in a bare little room, which communicated by a glazed door with a surgery.", "그는, 유리를 낀 문으로 진료실과 통하는, 휑뎅그렁한 자그마한 방에 홀로 앉아 있었다."], ["Hideous coloured diagrams of the ravages of hideous diseases decorated the barren buff-coloured walls.", "흉측한 병들이 앗아 간 참상을 그린 흉측한 채색 도해들이 그 삭막한 담황색 벽을 장식하고 있었다."], ["A bookcase filled with dingy medical works, and ornamented at the top with a skull, in place of the customary bust; a large deal table copiously splashed with ink; wooden chairs of the sort that are seen in kitchens and cottages; a threadbare drugget in the middle of the floor; a sink of water, with a basin and waste-pipe roughly let into the wall, horribly suggestive of its connection with surgical operations—comprised the entire furniture of the room.", "칙칙한 의학서들로 가득 차고, 그 위쪽에 여느 흉상 대신 두개골로 꾸며진 책장 하나, 잉크가 흥건히 튄 큰 전나무 탁자 하나, 부엌과 오두막에서 보이는 부류의 나무 의자들, 그 방바닥 한가운데의 올이 다 드러난 융단 한 장, 대야와 배수관이 벽에 거칠게 박혀, 그것이 외과 수술과 이어져 있음을 끔찍하게 넌지시 일러 주는 물이 든 개수대 하나—가 그 방의 온 세간을 이루었다. 벌들이 그 창밖 화분에 놓인 몇 송이 꽃 사이에서 붕붕거렸다."], ["The bees were humming among a few flowers placed in pots outside the window; the birds were singing in the garden, and the faint intermittent jingle of a tuneless piano in some neighbouring house forced itself now and again on the ear.", "새들이 그 정원에서 노래했다. 그리고 어느 이웃집의 가락 없는 피아노의 희미하고 이따금씩의 짤랑거림이 이따금 귀에 억지로 밀려들었다."], ["In any other place, these everyday sounds might have spoken pleasantly of the everyday world outside.", "여느 다른 곳에서라면, 이 나날의 소리들이 바깥의 나날의 세상을 즐겁게 이야기했을지도 모른다."], ["Here, they came in as intruders on a silence which nothing but human suffering had the privilege to disturb.", "여기서, 그것들은, 오직 인간의 괴로움만이 어지럽힐 특권을 가진 그 고요에 침입자로 들어왔다."], ["I looked at the mahogany instrument case, and at the huge roll of lint, occupying places of their own on the bookshelves, and shuddered inwardly as I thought of the sounds, familiar and appropriate to the everyday use of Ezra Jennings’ room.", "나는, 그 책장에 제 나름의 자리를 차지한 그 마호가니 기구 상자와 그 커다란 붕대 두루마리를 보았고, 에즈라 제닝스의 방의 나날의 쓰임에 익숙하고 걸맞은 그 소리들을 생각하며 속으로 몸서리쳤다."]], [["“I make no apology, Mr. Blake, for the place in which I am receiving you,”", "\"내가 당신을 맞는 이 자리에 대해, 블레이크 씨, 나는 아무 변명도 하지 않겠소,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“It is the only room in the house, at this hour of the day, in which we can feel quite sure of being left undisturbed.", "\"이것이 그 집에서, 하루의 이 시각에, 우리가 방해받지 않고 있을 것을 아주 확신할 수 있는 유일한 방이오."], ["Here are my papers ready for you; and here are two books to which we may have occasion to refer, before we have done.", "여기 당신을 위해 채비된 나의 종이들이 있소. 그리고 여기 우리가 마치기 전에 참조할 계제가 있을지도 모를 책 두 권이 있소."], ["Bring your chair to the table, and we shall be able to consult them together.”", "당신의 의자를 탁자로 가져오시오, 그러면 우리가 함께 그것들을 참조할 수 있을 것이오.\""]], [["I drew up to the table; and Ezra Jennings handed me his manuscript notes.", "나는 탁자로 다가갔다. 그리고 에즈라 제닝스는 나에게 그의 원고 기록을 건넸다."], ["They consisted of two large folio leaves of paper.", "그것들은 두 장의 큰 이절판 종이로 이루어져 있었다."], ["One leaf contained writing which only covered the surface at intervals.", "한 장은 오직 이따금씩만 표면을 덮은 글씨를 담고 있었다."], ["The other presented writing, in red and black ink, which completely filled the page from top to bottom.", "다른 한 장은, 붉은 잉크와 검은 잉크로 된, 위에서 아래까지 그 쪽을 완전히 채운 글씨를 내보였다."], ["In the irritated state of my curiosity, at that moment, I laid aside the second sheet of paper in despair.", "그 순간, 나의 호기심의 애태워진 상태에서, 나는 절망하여 그 둘째 장을 옆으로 밀어 놓았다."]], [["“Have some mercy on me!”", "\"나에게 좀 자비를 베푸시오!\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["“Tell me what I am to expect, before I attempt to read this.”", "\"내가 이것을 읽으려 하기 전에, 내가 무엇을 예상해야 할지 나에게 말해 주시오.\""]], [["“Willingly, Mr. Blake!", "\"기꺼이, 블레이크 씨!"], ["Do you mind my asking you one or two more questions?”", "내가 당신에게 한두 가지 물음을 더 물어도 괜찮겠소?\""]], [["“Ask me anything you like!”", "\"당신이 원하는 것은 무엇이든 나에게 물으시오!\""]], [["He looked at me with the sad smile on his lips, and the kindly interest in his soft brown eyes.", "그는 입술에 그 서글픈 미소를, 부드러운 갈색 눈에 다정한 관심을 담고 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“You have already told me,”", "\"당신은 이미 나에게 말했소,\""], ["he said, “that you have never—to your knowledge—tasted opium in your life.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 여태껏—당신이 아는 한—평생 아편을 맛본 적이 없다고.\""]], [["“To my knowledge,”", "\"내가 아는 한,\""], ["I repeated.", "하고 나는 되뇌었다."]], [["“You will understand directly why I speak with that reservation.", "\"당신은 내가 어째서 그 유보를 달아 말하는지 곧 이해할 것이오."], ["Let us go on.", "계속합시다."], ["You are not aware of ever having taken opium.", "당신은 아편을 먹은 적이 있음을 자각하지 못하오."], ["At this time, last year, you were suffering from nervous irritation, and you slept wretchedly at night.", "이때쯤, 지난해에, 당신은 신경의 짜증으로 괴로워했고, 밤에 잠을 형편없이 잤소."], ["On the night of the birthday, however, there was an exception to the rule—you slept soundly.", "허나 그 생일 밤에는, 그 상례에 예외가 있었소—당신은 곤히 잤소."], ["Am I right, so far?”", "여기까지 내가 옳소?\""]], [["“Quite right!”", "\"아주 옳소!\""]], [["“Can you assign any cause for the nervous suffering, and your want of sleep?”", "\"그 신경의 괴로움과, 당신의 잠 못 이룸에 대해 무슨 원인이든 댈 수 있소?\""]], [["“I can assign no cause.", "\"나는 아무 원인도 댈 수 없소."], ["Old Betteredge made a guess at the cause, I remember.", "늙은 베터리지가 그 원인을 짐작해 본 것이, 기억나오."], ["But that is hardly worth mentioning.”", "허나 그것은 좀처럼 언급할 값어치가 없소.\""]], [["“Pardon me.", "\"용서하시오."], ["Anything is worth mentioning in such a case as this.", "이런 경우에는 무엇이든 언급할 값어치가 있소."], ["Betteredge attributed your sleeplessness to something.", "베터리지가 당신의 잠 못 이룸을 무언가의 탓으로 돌렸소."], ["To what?”", "무엇의?\""]], [["“To my leaving off smoking.”", "\"내가 담배를 끊은 것의.\""]], [["“Had you been an habitual smoker?”", "\"당신은 습관적인 흡연가였소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Did you leave off the habit suddenly?”", "\"당신은 그 버릇을 별안간 끊었소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Betteredge was perfectly right, Mr. Blake.", "\"베터리지가 완전히 옳았소, 블레이크 씨."], ["When smoking is a habit a man must have no common constitution who can leave it off suddenly without some temporary damage to his nervous system.", "흡연이 버릇일 때, 그것을 별안간 끊고도 제 신경계에 얼마쯤의 일시적 손상이 없을 수 있는 사람은 여느 체질이 아님이 틀림없소."], ["Your sleepless nights are accounted for, to my mind.", "당신의 잠 못 이룬 밤은, 내 생각으로는, 설명되오."], ["My next question refers to Mr. Candy.", "나의 다음 물음은 캔디 씨에 관한 것이오."], ["Do you remember having entered into anything like a dispute with him—at the birthday dinner, or afterwards—on the subject of his profession?”", "당신은 그와—그 생일 만찬에서든, 그 뒤든—그의 직업이라는 주제로 다툼 같은 것을 벌인 것을 기억하오?\""]], [["The question instantly awakened one of my dormant remembrances in connection with the birthday festival.", "그 물음은 대뜸 그 생일 잔치와 이어진 나의 잠든 기억 하나를 일깨웠다."], ["The foolish wrangle which took place, on that occasion, between Mr. Candy and myself, will be found described at much greater length than it deserves in the tenth chapter of Betteredge’s Narrative.", "그 자리에서 캔디 씨와 나 사이에 일어난 그 어리석은 언쟁은, 베터리지 이야기의 열째 장에 그것이 마땅한 것보다 훨씬 더 길게 그려진 것으로 나올 것이다."], ["The details there presented of the dispute—so little had I thought of it afterwards—entirely failed to recur to my memory.", "거기 내놓인 그 다툼의 세세한 것들은—그 뒤에 내가 그것을 어찌나 조금밖에 생각하지 않았던지—나의 기억에 온전히 되살아나지 않았다."], ["All that I could now recall, and all that I could tell Ezra Jennings was, that I had attacked the art of medicine at the dinner-table with sufficient rashness and sufficient pertinacity to put even Mr. Candy out of temper for the moment.", "이제 내가 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 것, 그리고 내가 에즈라 제닝스에게 말할 수 있는 온갖 것은, 내가 그 만찬 식탁에서, 캔디 씨조차 그 순간 발끈하게 할 만큼의 경솔함과 끈질김으로 의술을 공격했다는 것이었다."], ["I also remembered that Lady Verinder had interfered to stop the dispute, and that the little doctor and I had “made it up again,” as the children say, and had become as good friends as ever, before we shook hands that night.", "나는 또한, 레이디 버린더가 그 다툼을 멈추려 끼어들었다는 것과, 그 자그마한 의사와 내가, 아이들 말로 하듯, \"다시 화해\"하여, 그날 밤 우리가 악수하기 전에 여느 때와 다름없이 좋은 벗이 되었다는 것을 기억했다."]], [["“There is one thing more,”", "\"한 가지가 더 있소,\""], ["said Ezra Jennings, “which it is very important I should know.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"내가 아는 것이 아주 중요한."], ["Had you any reason for feeling any special anxiety about the Diamond, at this time last year?”", "이때쯤 지난해에, 당신은 그 다이아몬드에 대해 어떤 각별한 걱정을 느낄 무슨 이유가 있었소?\""]], [["“I had the strongest reasons for feeling anxiety about the Diamond.", "\"나는 그 다이아몬드에 대해 걱정을 느낄 더없이 강한 이유가 있었소."], ["I knew it to be the object of a conspiracy; and I was warned to take measures for Miss Verinder’s protection, as the possessor of the stone.”", "나는 그것이 어떤 음모의 대상임을 알았소. 그리고 나는 그 돌의 소유자인 버린더 아가씨를 지킬 방책을 취하라고 경고받았소.\""]], [["“Was the safety of the Diamond the subject of conversation between you and any other person, immediately before you retired to rest on the birthday night?”", "\"그 다이아몬드의 안전이, 당신이 그 생일 밤에 잠자리에 들기 바로 전에, 당신과 다른 어떤 사람 사이의 대화의 주제였소?\""]], [["“It was the subject of a conversation between Lady Verinder and her daughter——”", "\"그것은 레이디 버린더와 그녀의 딸 사이의 대화의 주제였소——\""]], [["“Which took place in your hearing?”", "\"그것이 당신이 듣는 데에서 일어났소?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["Ezra Jennings took up his notes from the table, and placed them in my hands.", "에즈라 제닝스는 탁자에서 그의 기록을 집어 들어, 그것을 내 손에 쥐여 주었다."]], [["“Mr. Blake,”", "\"블레이크 씨,\""], ["he said, “if you read those notes now, by the light which my questions and your answers have thrown on them, you will make two astounding discoveries concerning yourself.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"만일 당신이 지금, 나의 물음과 당신의 대답이 그것들 위에 던진 그 빛으로, 그 기록을 읽는다면, 당신은 당신 자신에 관한 두 가지 아연할 발견을 하게 될 것이오."], ["You will find:—First, that you entered Miss Verinder’s sitting-room and took the Diamond, in a state of trance, produced by opium.", "당신은 알게 될 것이오—첫째로, 당신이 아편이 자아낸 무아지경의 상태로, 버린더 아가씨의 응접실에 들어가 그 다이아몬드를 가져갔음을."], ["Secondly, that the opium was given to you by Mr. Candy—without your own knowledge—as a practical refutation of the opinions which you had expressed to him at the birthday dinner.”", "둘째로, 그 아편이 캔디 씨에 의해 당신에게—당신 자신도 모르게—당신이 그 생일 만찬에서 그에게 내뱉은 견해에 대한 실질적인 반박으로 주어졌음을.\""]], [["I sat with the papers in my hand completely stupefied.", "나는 그 종이들을 손에 든 채 완전히 얼이 빠져 앉아 있었다."]], [["“Try and forgive poor Mr. Candy,”", "\"가엾은 캔디 씨를 부디 용서하시오,\""], ["said the assistant gently.", "하고 그 조수가 부드럽게 말했다."], ["“He has done dreadful mischief, I own; but he has done it innocently.", "\"그가 끔찍한 화를 저지른 것은 나도 인정하오. 허나 그는 그것을 무심결에 저질렀소."], ["If you will look at the notes, you will see that—but for his illness—he would have returned to Lady Verinder’s the morning after the party, and would have acknowledged the trick that he had played you.", "만일 당신이 그 기록을 본다면, 당신은 알 것이오—그의 병이 아니었다면—그가 그 잔치 이튿날 아침 레이디 버린더의 집으로 돌아가, 그가 당신에게 부린 그 장난을 인정했으리라는 것을."], ["Miss Verinder would have heard of it, and Miss Verinder would have questioned him—and the truth which has laid hidden for a year would have been discovered in a day.”", "버린더 아가씨가 그것을 들었을 것이고, 버린더 아가씨가 그를 캐물었을 것이고—한 해 동안 숨겨져 있던 그 진실이 하루 만에 밝혀졌을 것이오.\""]], [["I began to regain my self-possession.", "나는 나의 침착함을 되찾기 시작했다."], ["“Mr. Candy is beyond the reach of my resentment,”", "\"캔디 씨는 나의 원한이 닿을 수 없는 곳에 있소,\""], ["I said angrily.", "하고 나는 성이 나서 말했다."], ["“But the trick that he played me is not the less an act of treachery, for all that.", "\"허나 그가 나에게 부린 그 장난은, 그럼에도, 하나의 배신 행위임에 조금도 다름이 없소."], ["I may forgive, but I shall not forget it.”", "나는 용서할지언정, 그것을 잊지는 않겠소.\""]], [["“Every medical man commits that act of treachery, Mr. Blake, in the course of his practice.", "\"모든 의사가 제 의료 행위 가운데 그 배신 행위를 저지르오, 블레이크 씨."], ["The ignorant distrust of opium (in England) is by no means confined to the lower and less cultivated classes.", "(잉글랜드에서) 아편에 대한 무지한 불신은 결코 하층의, 덜 교양 있는 계급에 국한되지 않소."], ["Every doctor in large practice finds himself, every now and then, obliged to deceive his patients, as Mr. Candy deceived you.", "크게 개업한 모든 의사가, 이따금, 캔디 씨가 당신을 속인 것처럼, 제 환자를 속이지 않을 수 없음을 알게 되오."], ["I don’t defend the folly of playing you a trick under the circumstances.", "나는 그 상황에서 당신에게 장난을 부린 그 어리석음을 두둔하지 않소."], ["I only plead with you for a more accurate and more merciful construction of motives.”", "나는 다만 당신에게 동기에 대한 더 정확하고 더 자비로운 해석을 청할 뿐이오.\""]], [["“How was it done?” I asked.", "\"그것이 어떻게 이루어졌소?\""], ["“Who gave me the laudanum, without my knowing it myself?”", "하고 나는 물었다. \"누가, 나 자신도 모르게, 나에게 그 아편팅크를 주었소?\""]], [["“I am not able to tell you.", "\"나는 당신에게 말할 수 없소."], ["Nothing relating to that part of the matter dropped from Mr. Candy’s lips, all through his illness.", "그 일의 그 부분에 관련되는 것은 아무것도, 그의 온 병 내내, 캔디 씨의 입술에서 새어 나오지 않았소."], ["Perhaps your own memory may point to the person to be suspected.”", "어쩌면 당신 자신의 기억이 의심받을 사람을 가리킬지도 모르오.\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니오.\""]], [["“It is useless, in that case, to pursue the inquiry.", "\"그런 경우, 그 조사를 좇는 것은 헛일이오."], ["The laudanum was secretly given to you in some way.", "그 아편팅크는 어떤 식으로든 몰래 당신에게 주어졌소."], ["Let us leave it there, and go on to matters of more immediate importance.", "그것은 거기 두고, 더 당장 중요한 일로 넘어갑시다."], ["Read my notes, if you can.", "할 수 있거든, 나의 기록을 읽으시오."], ["Familiarise your mind with what has happened in the past.", "과거에 일어난 일에 당신의 마음을 익히시오."], ["I have something very bold and very startling to propose to you, which relates to the future.”", "나에게는 미래에 관련되는, 당신에게 제안할 아주 대담하고 아주 놀라운 무언가가 있소.\""]], [["Those last words roused me.", "그 마지막 말이 나를 일깨웠다."]], [["I looked at the papers, in the order in which Ezra Jennings had placed them in my hands.", "나는 에즈라 제닝스가 그것들을 내 손에 쥐여 준 순서대로 그 종이들을 보았다."], ["The paper which contained the smaller quantity of writing was the uppermost of the two.", "더 적은 양의 글씨를 담은 종이가 그 둘 중 맨 위에 있었다."], ["On this, the disconnected words, and fragments of sentences, which had dropped from Mr. Candy in his delirium, appeared as follows:", "여기에, 캔디 씨가 그의 섬망 속에서 떨어뜨린, 이어지지 않는 낱말들과 문장의 조각들이, 다음과 같이 나타났다."]], [["“... Mr. Franklin Blake ... and agreeable ... down a peg ... medicine ... confesses ... sleep at night ... tell him ... out of order ... medicine ... he tells me ... and groping in the dark mean one and the same thing ... all the company at the dinner-table ... I say ... groping after sleep ... nothing but medicine ... he says ... leading the blind ... know what it means ... witty ... a night’s rest in spite of his teeth ... wants sleep ... Lady Verinder’s medicine chest ... five-and-twenty minims ... without his knowing it ... tomorrow morning ... Well, Mr. Blake ... medicine today ... never ... without it ... out, Mr. Candy ... excellent ... without it ... down on him ... truth ... something besides ... excellent ... dose of laudanum, sir ... bed ... what ... medicine now.”", "\"…프랭클린 블레이크 씨…그리고 상냥한…한 등급 낮추어…약…털어놓는다…밤에 잠…그에게 말해…어긋난…약…그가 나에게 말한다…그리고 어둠 속을 더듬는 것은 하나의 같은 것을 뜻한다…만찬 식탁의 온 손님들…내가 말한다…잠을 더듬어 찾는…약 말고는 아무것도…그가 말한다…눈먼 이를 이끄는…무슨 뜻인지 안다…재치 있는…제 고집에도 하룻밤 안식을…잠을 원한다…레이디 버린더의 약장…스물다섯 미님…그가 모르게…내일 아침…자, 블레이크 씨…오늘 약…결코…그것 없이…틀렸소, 캔디 씨…훌륭한…그것 없이…그를 몰아붙여…진실…그 밖에 무언가…훌륭한…아편팅크 한 모금, 선생…잠자리…무슨…이제 약.\""]], [["There, the first of the two sheets of paper came to an end.", "거기서, 그 두 장의 종이 가운데 첫째 장이 끝났다."], ["I handed it back to Ezra Jennings.", "나는 그것을 에즈라 제닝스에게 도로 건넸다."]], [["“That is what you heard at his bedside?”", "\"그것이 당신이 그의 잠자리에서 들은 것이오?\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“Literally and exactly what I heard,”", "\"글자 그대로, 정확히 내가 들은 것이오,\""], ["he answered—“except that the repetitions are not transferred here from my short-hand notes.", "하고 그가 대답했다—\"다만 그 되풀이들이 나의 속기 기록에서 여기 옮겨지지 않았을 뿐이오."], ["He reiterated certain words and phrases a dozen times over, fifty times over, just as he attached more or less importance to the idea which they represented.", "그는 어떤 낱말들과 어구들을, 그것들이 나타내는 생각에 얼마쯤 더, 또는 덜 중요성을 두는 데에 꼭 따라, 열두 번씩, 쉰 번씩 되뇌었소."], ["The repetitions, in this sense, were of some assistance to me in putting together those fragments.", "그 되풀이들은, 이런 뜻에서, 그 조각들을 맞추는 데에 나에게 얼마쯤 도움이 되었소."], ["Don’t suppose,”", "여기지 마시오,\""], ["he added, pointing to the second sheet of paper, “that I claim to have reproduced the expressions which Mr.", "하고 그는 그 둘째 장의 종이를 가리키며 덧붙였다."], ["Candy himself would have used if he had been capable of speaking connectedly.", "\"내가 캔디 씨 자신이, 조리 있게 말할 능력이 있었다면 썼을 그 표현들을 되살렸다고 짐짓 주장하는 것으로."], ["I only say that I have penetrated through the obstacle of the disconnected expression, to the thought which was underlying it connectedly all the time.", "나는 다만, 내가 그 이어지지 않는 표현의 장애를 뚫고, 내내 그 밑에 조리 있게 깔려 있던 그 생각에 이르렀다고 말할 뿐이오."], ["Judge for yourself.”", "당신 스스로 판단하시오.\""]], [["I turned to the second sheet of paper, which I now knew to be the key to the first.", "나는 이제 첫째 장의 열쇠임을 알게 된 그 둘째 장의 종이로 눈을 돌렸다."]], [["Once more, Mr.", "다시 한 번, 캔디 씨의 헛소리가, 검은 잉크로 옮겨져 나타났다."], ["Candy’s wanderings appeared, copied in black ink; the intervals between the phrases being filled up by Ezra Jennings in red ink.", "그 어구들 사이의 빈틈은 에즈라 제닝스가 붉은 잉크로 채운 채였다."], ["I reproduce the result here, in one plain form; the original language and the interpretation of it coming close enough together in these pages to be easily compared and verified.", "나는 그 결과를 여기 하나의 평이한 꼴로 옮긴다. 원래의 말과 그것의 해석이 이 쪽들에서 쉽게 견주어 확인될 만큼 충분히 가까이 오도록."]], [["“... Mr.", "\"…프랭클린 블레이크 씨는 영리하고 상냥하나, 그가 약 이야기를 할 때는 한 등급 낮출 필요가 있다."], ["Franklin Blake is clever and agreeable, but he wants taking down a peg when he talks of medicine. He confesses that he has been suffering from want of sleep at night.", "그는 자기가 밤에 잠이 모자라 괴로워해 왔음을 털어놓는다."], ["I tell him that his nerves are out of order, and that he ought to take medicine.", "나는 그에게, 그의 신경이 어긋났으니, 약을 먹어야 한다고 말한다."], ["He tells me that taking medicine and groping in the dark mean one and the same thing.", "그는 나에게, 약을 먹는 것과 어둠 속을 더듬는 것은 하나의 같은 것을 뜻한다고 말한다."], ["This before all the company at the dinner-table.", "이것을 만찬 식탁의 온 손님들 앞에서 한다."], ["I say to him, you are groping after sleep, and nothing but medicine can help you to find it.", "나는 그에게 말한다, 당신은 잠을 더듬어 찾고 있으며, 약 말고는 아무것도 당신이 그것을 찾도록 도울 수 없다고."], ["He says to me, I have heard of the blind leading the blind, and now I know what it means.", "그는 나에게 말한다, 나는 눈먼 이가 눈먼 이를 이끈다는 말을 들은 적이 있는데, 이제 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 안다고."], ["Witty—but I can give him a night’s rest in spite of his teeth.", "재치 있는 말이다—허나 나는 그의 고집에도 그에게 하룻밤 안식을 줄 수 있다."], ["He really wants sleep; and Lady Verinder’s medicine chest is at my disposal.", "그는 정말로 잠을 원한다. 그리고 레이디 버린더의 약장이 내 마음대로 쓸 수 있게 있다."], ["Give him five-and-twenty minims of laudanum tonight, without his knowing it; and then call tomorrow morning.", "오늘 밤, 그가 모르게, 그에게 아편팅크 스물다섯 미님을 주자. 그런 다음 내일 아침 찾아가자."], ["‘Well, Mr. Blake, will you try a little medicine today?", "'자, 블레이크 씨, 오늘 약을 좀 드셔 보시겠소?"], ["You will never sleep without it.’—‘There you are out, Mr.", "그것 없이는 결코 잠들지 못할 것이오.'—'거기서 틀렸소, 캔디 씨."], ["Candy: I have had an excellent night’s rest without it.’ Then, come down on him with the truth!", "나는 그것 없이도 훌륭히 하룻밤 안식을 취했소.' 그러거든, 진실로 그를 몰아붙여라!"], ["‘You have had something besides an excellent night’s rest; you had a dose of laudanum, sir, before you went to bed.", "'당신은 훌륭한 하룻밤 안식 말고 그 밖에 무언가를 취했소. 당신은 잠자리에 들기 전에, 선생, 아편팅크 한 모금을 취했소."], ["What do you say to the art of medicine, now?’”", "이제 의술에 대해 뭐라 하겠소?'\""]], [["Admiration of the ingenuity which had woven this smooth and finished texture out of the ravelled skein was naturally the first impression that I felt, on handing the manuscript back to Ezra Jennings.", "이 엉킨 실타래에서 이 매끄럽고 다듬어진 짜임을 짜 낸 그 기발함에 대한 감탄이, 그 원고를 에즈라 제닝스에게 도로 건네면서 내가 느낀, 자연스럽게 첫 인상이었다."], ["He modestly interrupted the first few words in which my sense of surprise expressed itself, by asking me if the conclusion which he had drawn from his notes was also the conclusion at which my own mind had arrived.", "그는 나의 놀라움의 느낌이 스스로를 나타낸 그 처음 몇 마디를, 그가 그의 기록에서 이끌어 낸 결론이, 나 자신의 마음이 이른 결론이기도 한지 나에게 물음으로써, 겸손히 끊었다."]], [["“Do you believe as I believe,”", "\"당신은 나처럼 믿소,\""], ["he said, “that you were acting under the influence of the laudanum in doing all that you did, on the night of Miss Verinder’s birthday, in Lady Verinder’s house?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 레이디 버린더의 집에서, 버린더 아가씨의 생일 밤에, 당신이 한 그 온갖 것을 하면서, 그 아편팅크의 영향 아래 움직이고 있었다고?\""]], [["“I am too ignorant of the influence of laudanum to have an opinion of my own,”", "\"나는 아편팅크의 영향에 너무 무지하여 나 자신의 견해를 가질 수 없소,\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“I can only follow your opinion, and feel convinced that you are right.”", "\"나는 다만 당신의 견해를 따르고, 당신이 옳음을 확신할 수 있을 뿐이오.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋소."], ["The next question is this.", "다음 물음은 이것이오."], ["You are convinced; and I am convinced—how are we to carry our conviction to the minds of other people?”", "당신은 확신하오. 그리고 나도 확신하오—우리는 어떻게 우리의 확신을 다른 사람들의 마음에 전할 것이오?\""]], [["I pointed to the two manuscripts, lying on the table between us.", "나는 우리 사이의 탁자 위에 놓인 그 두 원고를 가리켰다."], ["Ezra Jennings shook his head.", "에즈라 제닝스는 고개를 저었다."]], [["“Useless, Mr. Blake!", "\"헛일이오, 블레이크 씨!"], ["Quite useless, as they stand now for three unanswerable reasons.", "그것들이 지금 있는 그대로로는, 세 가지 반박할 수 없는 이유에서, 아주 헛일이오."], ["In the first place, those notes have been taken under circumstances entirely out of the experience of the mass of mankind.", "우선, 그 기록은 인류 대중의 경험에서 온전히 벗어난 상황에서 받아 적혔소."], ["Against them, to begin with!", "우선 그것부터가 그것들에게 불리하오!"], ["In the second place, those notes represent a medical and metaphysical theory.", "둘째로, 그 기록은 하나의 의학적이고 형이상학적인 이론을 나타내오."], ["Against them, once more!", "다시 한 번 그것들에게 불리하오!"], ["In the third place, those notes are of my making; there is nothing but my assertion to the contrary, to guarantee that they are not fabrications.", "셋째로, 그 기록은 나의 손으로 만든 것이오. 그것들이 날조가 아님을 보장할, 그렇지 않다는 나의 주장 말고는 아무것도 없소."], ["Remember what I told you on the moor—and ask yourself what my assertion is worth. No!", "내가 그 황야에서 당신에게 말한 것을 기억하시오—그리고 나의 주장이 무슨 값어치가 있는지 스스로에게 물어보시오."], ["my notes have but one value, looking to the verdict of the world outside.", "아니오! 나의 기록은, 바깥 세상의 평결을 보자면, 오직 한 가지 값어치가 있소."], ["Your innocence is to be vindicated; and they show how it can be done.", "당신의 결백이 밝혀져야 하오. 그리고 그것들은 그것이 어떻게 이루어질 수 있는지 보여 주오."], ["We must put our conviction to the proof—and You are the man to prove it!”", "우리는 우리의 확신을 증명해야 하오—그리고 당신이 그것을 증명할 사람이오!\""]], [["“How?”", "\"어떻게요?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["He leaned eagerly nearer to me across the table that divided us.", "그는 우리를 갈라놓은 그 탁자 너머로 간절히 나에게 더 가까이 몸을 기울였다."]], [["“Are you willing to try a bold experiment?”", "\"당신은 대담한 실험을 해 볼 마음이 있소?\""]], [["“I will do anything to clear myself of the suspicion that rests on me now.”", "\"나는 이제 나에게 얹혀 있는 그 의심에서 나를 씻기 위해서라면 무엇이든 하겠소.\""]], [["“Will you submit to some personal inconvenience for a time?”", "\"당신은 한동안 얼마쯤의 개인적 불편을 감수하겠소?\""]], [["“To any inconvenience, no matter what it may be.”", "\"어떤 불편이든, 그것이 무엇이든 간에.\""]], [["“Will you be guided implicitly by my advice?", "\"당신은 나의 조언에 온전히 따르겠소?"], ["It may expose you to the ridicule of fools; it may subject you to the remonstrances of friends whose opinions you are bound to respect.”", "그것은 당신을 바보들의 비웃음에 드러낼지도 모르오. 그것은 당신을, 당신이 마땅히 존중해야 할 견해를 지닌 벗들의 항변에 처하게 할지도 모르오.\""]], [["“Tell me what to do!”", "\"무엇을 할지 나에게 말하시오!\""], ["I broke out impatiently.", "하고 나는 조바심을 내며 터뜨렸다."], ["“And, come what may, I’ll do it.”", "\"그리고, 무슨 일이 있어도, 나는 그것을 하겠소.\""]], [["“You shall do this, Mr.", "\"당신은 이것을 할 것이오, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["Blake,” he answered.", "하고 그가 대답했다."], ["“You shall steal the Diamond, unconsciously, for the second time, in the presence of witnesses whose testimony is beyond dispute.”", "\"당신은, 두 번째로, 무의식적으로, 그 증언이 다툼의 여지가 없는 증인들의 앞에서, 그 다이아몬드를 훔칠 것이오.\""]], [["I started to my feet.", "나는 벌떡 일어섰다."], ["I tried to speak.", "나는 말하려 했다."], ["I could only look at him.", "나는 그를 바라볼 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“I believe it can be done,” he went on.", "\"나는 그것이 정녕 이루어질 수 있다고 믿소,\""], ["“And it shall be done—if you will only help me.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"그리고 그것은 정녕 이루어질 것이오—당신이 나를 돕기만 한다면."], ["Try to compose yourself—sit down, and hear what I have to say to you.", "진정하려 애쓰시오—앉으시오, 그리고 내가 당신에게 할 말을 들으시오."], ["You have resumed the habit of smoking; I have seen that for myself.", "당신은 담배 피우는 버릇을 다시 들였소. 나는 그것을 내 눈으로 보았소."], ["How long have you resumed it.”", "언제부터 그것을 다시 들였소?\""]], [["“For nearly a year.”", "\"거의 한 해 전부터.\""]], [["“Do you smoke more or less than you did?”", "\"당신은 예전보다 더 많이 피우오, 덜 피우오?\""]], [["“More.”", "\"더 많이.\""]], [["“Will you give up the habit again?", "\"당신은 그 버릇을 다시 끊겠소?"], ["Suddenly, mind!—as you gave it up before.”", "별안간, 명심하시오!—당신이 전에 그것을 끊었듯이.\""]], [["I began dimly to see his drift.", "나는 그의 속셈을 어렴풋이 보기 시작했다."], ["“I will give it up, from this moment,”", "\"나는 이 순간부터 그것을 끊겠소,\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."]], [["“If the same consequences follow, which followed last June,”", "\"만일 지난 유월에 뒤따른 것과 같은 결과가 뒤따른다면,\""], ["said Ezra Jennings—“if you suffer once more as you suffered then, from sleepless nights, we shall have gained our first step.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다—\"만일 당신이 그때 괴로워한 것처럼, 잠 못 이룬 밤으로 다시 한 번 괴로워한다면, 우리는 우리의 첫걸음을 이룬 것이오."], ["We shall have put you back again into something assimilating to your nervous condition on the birthday night.", "우리는 당신을 그 생일 밤의 당신의 신경 상태와 비슷한 무언가로 다시 되돌려 놓은 것이오."], ["If we can next revive, or nearly revive, the domestic circumstances which surrounded you; and if we can occupy your mind again with the various questions concerning the Diamond which formerly agitated it, we shall have replaced you, as nearly as possible in the same position, physically and morally, in which the opium found you last year.", "만일 우리가 다음으로, 당신을 둘러쌌던 그 집안의 상황을 되살리거나, 거의 되살릴 수 있다면, 그리고 만일 우리가 예전에 당신의 마음을 어지럽혔던 그 다이아몬드에 관한 갖가지 물음으로 당신의 마음을 다시 사로잡을 수 있다면, 우리는 당신을, 몸으로도 마음으로도, 지난해에 그 아편이 당신을 찾아냈던 그 처지와 되도록 가까운 처지에 도로 놓은 것이오."], ["In that case we may fairly hope that a repetition of the dose will lead, in a greater or lesser degree, to a repetition of the result.", "그런 경우 우리는, 그 약을 되풀이하는 것이, 더하든 덜하든, 그 결과의 되풀이로 이어지리라고 정녕 바랄 수 있소."], ["There is my proposal, expressed in a few hasty words.", "이것이, 몇 마디 서두른 말로 나타낸 나의 제안이오."], ["You shall now see what reasons I have to justify me in making it.”", "이제 당신은 내가 그것을 하는 데에 나를 정당화할 무슨 이유가 있는지 보게 될 것이오.\""]], [["He turned to one of the books at his side, and opened it at a place marked by a small slip of paper.", "그는 그의 곁의 책 하나로 눈을 돌려, 작은 종이쪽지로 표시된 곳에서 그것을 폈다."]], [["“Don’t suppose that I am going to weary you with a lecture on physiology,”", "\"내가 당신을 생리학 강의로 지치게 하려는 것으로 여기지 마시오,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “I think myself bound to prove, in justice to both of us, that I am not asking you to try this experiment in deference to any theory of my own devising.", "\"나는 우리 둘 다에 대한 도리로, 내가 나 자신이 궁리한 어떤 이론에 대한 존중에서 이 실험을 해 보라고 당신에게 청하는 것이 아님을 증명해야겠다고 여기오."], ["Admitted principles, and recognised authorities, justify me in the view that I take.", "인정된 원리들과, 널리 알려진 권위들이, 내가 취하는 그 견해에 나를 정당화하오."], ["Give me five minutes of your attention; and I will undertake to show you that Science sanctions my proposal, fanciful as it may seem.", "나에게 당신의 주의의 오 분을 주시오. 그러면 나는, 그것이 아무리 공상적으로 보일지라도, 과학이 나의 제안을 승인함을 당신에게 보여 드리겠소."], ["Here, in the first place, is the physiological principle on which I am acting, stated by no less a person than Dr.", "여기, 우선, 내가 따라 움직이는 그 생리학적 원리가, 다른 아닌 카펜터 박사만 한 사람에 의해 진술되어 있소."], ["Carpenter. Read it for yourself.”", "당신 스스로 그것을 읽으시오.\""]], [["He handed me the slip of paper which had marked the place in the book.", "그는 나에게 그 책에서 그 곳을 표시했던 그 종이쪽지를 건넸다."], ["It contained a few lines of writing, as follows:—", "그것은 몇 줄의 글씨를 담고 있었는데, 다음과 같았다—"]], [["“There seems much ground for the belief, that every sensory impression which has once been recognised by the perceptive consciousness, is registered (so to speak) in the brain, and may be reproduced at some subsequent time, although there may be no consciousness of its existence in the mind during the whole intermediate period.”", "\"지각 의식에 의해 한 번 인식된 모든 감각 인상은 (이를테면) 뇌에 등록되며, 비록 그 사이의 온 기간 동안 그것의 존재에 대한 의식이 마음에 없을지라도, 그 뒤 어느 때에 되살아날 수 있다는 믿음에 상당한 근거가 있는 듯하다.\""]], [["“Is that plain, so far?”", "\"여기까지 평이하오?\""], ["asked Ezra Jennings.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 물었다."]], [["“Perfectly plain.”", "\"완전히 평이하오.\""]], [["He pushed the open book across the table to me, and pointed to a passage, marked by pencil lines.", "그는 그 펼쳐진 책을 탁자 너머로 나에게 밀고, 연필 줄로 표시된 한 대목을 가리켰다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"자,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “read that account of a case, which has—as I believe—a direct bearing on your own position, and on the experiment which I am tempting you to try. Observe, Mr.", "\"당신 자신의 처지와, 그리고 내가 당신에게 해 보라고 꾀고 있는 그 실험에—내가 믿기로는—직접 관련되는 어떤 경우에 대한 그 서술을 읽으시오."], ["Blake, before you begin, that I am now referring you to one of the greatest of English physiologists.", "시작하기 전에 눈여겨보시오, 블레이크 씨, 내가 지금 당신에게 잉글랜드의 가장 위대한 생리학자 가운데 하나를 가리키고 있음을. 당신 손에 든 그 책은 엘리엇슨 박사의 《인체 생리학》이오."], ["The book in your hand is Doctor Elliotson’s Human Physiology; and the case which the doctor cites rests on the well-known authority of Mr. Combe.”", "그리고 그 박사가 인용한 그 경우는 콤 씨의 널리 알려진 권위에 기대고 있소.\""]], [["The passage pointed out to me was expressed in these terms:—", "나에게 가리켜진 그 대목은 이런 말로 나타나 있었다—"]], [["“Dr. Abel informed me,”", "\"에이벌 박사가 나에게 알려 주었다,\""], ["says Mr.", "하고 콤 씨는 말한다."], ["Combe, “of an Irish porter to a warehouse, who forgot, when sober, what he had done when drunk; but, being drunk, again recollected the transactions of his former state of intoxication.", "\"어느 창고의 아일랜드인 짐꾼에 대해, 그는 술이 깨면, 술 취했을 때 자기가 한 일을 잊었으나, 다시 술 취하면, 자기의 이전 취한 상태의 일들을 도로 떠올렸다."], ["On one occasion, being drunk, he had lost a parcel of some value, and in his sober moments could give no account of it.", "한번은, 술 취해, 그가 얼마쯤 값나가는 꾸러미 하나를 잃어버렸고, 그의 술 깬 순간에는 그것에 대해 아무것도 댈 수 없었다."], ["Next time he was intoxicated, he recollected that he had left the parcel at a certain house, and there being no address on it, it had remained there safely, and was got on his calling for it.”", "다음에 그가 취했을 때, 그는 자기가 그 꾸러미를 어느 집에 두었음을 떠올렸고, 그 위에 주소가 없었던 터라, 그것은 거기 안전히 남아 있어, 그가 그것을 찾으러 가매 되찾아졌다.\""]], [["“Plain again?”", "\"다시 평이하오?\""], ["asked Ezra Jennings.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 물었다."]], [["“As plain as need be.”", "\"더없이 평이하오.\""]], [["He put back the slip of paper in its place, and closed the book.", "그는 그 종이쪽지를 제자리에 도로 넣고, 그 책을 덮었다."]], [["“Are you satisfied that I have not spoken without good authority to support me?”", "\"내가 나를 뒷받침할 좋은 권위 없이 말한 것이 아님을 흡족히 여기오?\" 하고 그가 물었다."], ["he asked. “If not, I have only to go to those bookshelves, and you have only to read the passages which I can point out to you.”", "\"만일 그렇지 않다면, 나는 저 책장으로 가기만 하면 되고, 당신은 내가 당신에게 가리켜 줄 수 있는 대목들을 읽기만 하면 되오.\""]], [["“I am quite satisfied,”", "\"나는 아주 흡족하오,\""], ["I said, “without reading a word more.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"한마디 더 읽지 않아도.\""]], [["“In that case, we may return to your own personal interest in this matter.", "\"그런 경우, 우리는 이 일에서의 당신 자신의 개인적 이해로 돌아가도 되겠소."], ["I am bound to tell you that there is something to be said against the experiment as well as for it.", "나는 당신에게, 그 실험에 대해 찬성뿐 아니라 반대해서도 할 말이 있음을 말해야겠소."], ["If we could, this year, exactly reproduce, in your case, the conditions as they existed last year, it is physiologically certain that we should arrive at exactly the same result.", "만일 우리가, 올해, 당신의 경우에, 지난해에 있었던 그대로의 조건을 정확히 되살릴 수 있다면, 우리가 정확히 같은 결과에 이르리라는 것은 생리학적으로 확실하오."], ["But this—there is no denying it—is simply impossible.", "허나 이것은—부인할 수 없거니와—그저 불가능하오."], ["We can only hope to approximate to the conditions; and if we don’t succeed in getting you nearly enough back to what you were, this venture of ours will fail.", "우리는 다만 그 조건에 가까이 다가가기를 바랄 수 있을 뿐이오. 그리고 만일 우리가 당신을 당신이던 상태에 충분히 가까이 되돌려 놓는 데에 성공하지 못한다면, 우리의 이 모험은 실패할 것이오."], ["If we do succeed—and I am myself hopeful of success—you may at least so far repeat your proceedings on the birthday night, as to satisfy any reasonable person that you are guiltless, morally speaking, of the theft of the Diamond. I believe, Mr.", "만일 우리가 성공한다면—그리고 나 자신은 성공을 바라고 있소—당신은 적어도, 어떤 분별 있는 사람에게든 당신이 그 다이아몬드의 절도에 대해, 도덕적으로 말하여, 죄 없음을 흡족히 여기게 할 만큼은, 그 생일 밤의 당신의 행동을 되풀이할 수 있을 것이오."], ["Blake, I have now stated the question, on both sides of it, as fairly as I can, within the limits that I have imposed on myself.", "나는, 블레이크 씨, 내가 이제 그 문제를, 내가 나 자신에게 지운 한계 안에서, 그 양편으로, 되도록 공정하게 진술한 것 같소."], ["If there is anything that I have not made clear to you, tell me what it is—and if I can enlighten you, I will.”", "만일 내가 당신에게 또렷이 하지 못한 무언가가 있다면, 그것이 무엇인지 나에게 말하시오—그리고 내가 당신을 밝혀 줄 수 있다면, 그리하겠소.\""]], [["“All that you have explained to me,”", "\"당신이 나에게 설명한 온갖 것을,\""], ["I said, “I understand perfectly.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 완전히 이해하오."], ["But I own I am puzzled on one point, which you have not made clear to me yet.”", "허나 나는, 당신이 아직 나에게 또렷이 하지 못한 한 가지 점에서 어리둥절함을 인정하오.\""]], [["“What is the point?”", "\"그 점이 무엇이오?\""]], [["“I don’t understand the effect of the laudanum on me.", "\"나는 그 아편팅크가 나에게 미친 영향을 이해하지 못하오."], ["I don’t understand my walking downstairs, and along corridors, and my opening and shutting the drawers of a cabinet, and my going back again to my own room.", "나는 내가 아래층으로, 그리고 복도를 따라 걸은 것과, 내가 장식장의 서랍을 열고 닫은 것과, 내가 다시 내 방으로 돌아간 것을 이해하지 못하오."], ["All these are active proceedings.", "이 모든 것은 능동적인 행동이오."], ["I thought the influence of opium was first to stupefy you, and then to send you to sleep.”", "나는 아편의 영향이 먼저 당신을 멍하게 하고, 그런 다음 당신을 잠재우는 것이라고 생각했소.\""]], [["“The common error about opium, Mr. Blake!", "\"아편에 대한 흔한 오해요, 블레이크 씨!"], ["I am, at this moment, exerting my intelligence (such as it is) in your service, under the influence of a dose of laudanum, some ten times larger than the dose Mr. Candy administered to you.", "나는, 이 순간, 캔디 씨가 당신에게 투여한 양보다 열 배쯤 더 많은 아편팅크 한 모금의 영향 아래, 당신을 위해 나의 (그런 정도의) 지력을 발휘하고 있소."], ["But don’t trust to my authority—even on a question which comes within my own personal experience.", "허나 나의 권위에 기대지 마시오—나 자신의 개인적 경험에 드는 물음에 대해서조차."], ["I anticipated the objection you have just made: and I have again provided myself with independent testimony which will carry its due weight with it in your own mind, and in the minds of your friends.”", "나는 당신이 방금 한 그 반론을 미리 내다보았소. 그리고 나는 다시, 당신 자신의 마음에서, 그리고 당신의 벗들의 마음에서 마땅한 무게를 지닐, 독립적인 증언을 나 자신에게 마련해 두었소.\""]], [["He handed me the second of the two books which he had by him on the table.", "그는 나에게 그가 탁자 위에 두었던 그 두 책 가운데 둘째 것을 건넸다."]], [["“There,”", "\"저기,\""], ["he said, “are the far-famed Confessions of an English Opium Eater!", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그 널리 이름난 《어느 잉글랜드 아편 상용자의 고백》이오!"], ["Take the book away with you, and read it.", "그 책을 가져가서, 읽으시오."], ["At the passage which I have marked, you will find that when De Quincey had committed what he calls ‘a debauch of opium,’ he either went to the gallery at the Opera to enjoy the music, or he wandered about the London markets on Saturday night, and interested himself in observing all the little shifts and bargainings of the poor in providing their Sunday’s dinner.", "내가 표시한 그 대목에서, 당신은 드퀸시가 자기가 '아편의 방탕'이라 부르는 것을 저질렀을 때, 그가 음악을 즐기러 오페라의 특별석에 가든지, 아니면 그가 토요일 밤 런던의 장터를 돌아다니며, 가난한 이들이 저희 일요일 저녁을 마련하는 온갖 자잘한 궁리와 흥정을 살피는 데에 관심을 두든지 했음을 알게 될 것이오."], ["So much for the capacity of a man to occupy himself actively, and to move about from place to place under the influence of opium.”", "아편의 영향 아래 능동적으로 스스로를 쓰고, 이곳저곳으로 옮겨 다니는 사람의 능력에 대해서는 그쯤 해 둡시다.\""]], [["“I am answered so far,”", "\"거기까지는 답을 얻었소,\""], ["I said; “but I am not answered yet as to the effect produced by the opium on myself.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"허나 나는 그 아편이 나 자신에게 미친 그 영향에 대해서는 아직 답을 얻지 못했소.\""]], [["“I will try to answer you in a few words,” said Ezra Jennings.", "\"내가 몇 마디로 당신에게 답하려 애써 보겠소,\""], ["“The action of opium is comprised, in the majority of cases, in two influences—a stimulating influence first, and a sedative influence afterwards.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말했다. \"아편의 작용은, 대부분의 경우, 두 가지 영향에 담겨 있소—먼저 흥분시키는 영향, 그리고 뒤이어 진정시키는 영향."], ["Under the stimulating influence, the latest and most vivid impressions left on your mind—namely, the impressions relating to the Diamond—would be likely, in your morbidly sensitive nervous condition, to become intensified in your brain, and would subordinate to themselves your judgment and your will exactly as an ordinary dream subordinates to itself your judgment and your will.", "그 흥분시키는 영향 아래, 당신의 마음에 남겨진 가장 나중의, 가장 생생한 인상들—곧 그 다이아몬드에 관련되는 인상들이, 당신의 병적으로 예민한 신경 상태에서, 당신의 뇌 속에서 격해져, 꼭 여느 꿈이 당신의 판단과 당신의 의지를 제 아래 둠과 똑같이, 당신의 판단과 당신의 의지를 제 아래 두었을 공산이 크오."], ["Little by little, under this action, any apprehensions about the safety of the Diamond which you might have felt during the day would be liable to develop themselves from the state of doubt to the state of certainty—would impel you into practical action to preserve the jewel—would direct your steps, with that motive in view, into the room which you entered—and would guide your hand to the drawers of the cabinet, until you had found the drawer which held the stone.", "조금씩, 이 작용 아래, 당신이 낮 동안 느꼈을지 모를 그 다이아몬드의 안전에 대한 어떤 염려든 의심의 상태에서 확신의 상태로 발전하기 쉬웠을 것이고—당신을 그 보석을 지키려는 실질적인 행동으로 몰아붙였을 것이고—그 동기를 염두에 두고, 당신의 걸음을 당신이 들어간 그 방으로 이끌었을 것이고—당신이 그 돌을 담은 서랍을 찾기까지, 당신의 손을 그 장식장의 서랍으로 인도했을 것이오."], ["In the spiritualised intoxication of opium, you would do all that.", "아편의 그 영적으로 고양된 취기 속에서, 당신은 그 온갖 것을 했을 것이오."], ["Later, as the sedative action began to gain on the stimulant action, you would slowly become inert and stupefied.", "나중에, 그 진정시키는 작용이 그 흥분시키는 작용을 이기기 시작하면서, 당신은 서서히 무기력하고 멍해졌을 것이오."], ["Later still you would fall into a deep sleep.", "더 나중에 당신은 깊은 잠에 빠졌을 것이오."], ["When the morning came, and the effect of the opium had been all slept off, you would wake as absolutely ignorant of what you had done in the night as if you had been living at the Antipodes.", "아침이 오고, 그 아편의 영향이 죄 잠으로 씻겨 나갔을 때, 당신은 마치 당신이 지구 반대편에서 살고 있었기라도 한 양, 밤에 당신이 한 일에 대해 절대적으로 무지한 채로 깨어났을 것이오."], ["Have I made it tolerably clear to you so far?”", "여기까지 내가 당신에게 그것을 웬만큼 또렷이 했소?\""]], [["“You have made it so clear,”", "\"당신은 그것을 어찌나 또렷이 했던지,\""], ["I said, “that I want you to go farther.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 당신이 더 나아가기를 바라오."], ["You have shown me how I entered the room, and how I came to take the Diamond.", "당신은 나에게 내가 어떻게 그 방에 들어갔고, 어떻게 그 다이아몬드를 가져가게 되었는지 보여 주었소."], ["But Miss Verinder saw me leave the room again, with the jewel in my hand.", "허나 버린더 아가씨는 내가 그 보석을 손에 들고 다시 그 방을 떠나는 것을 보았소."], ["Can you trace my proceedings from that moment?", "당신은 그 순간부터의 나의 행동을 좇을 수 있소?"], ["Can you guess what I did next?”", "당신은 내가 다음에 무엇을 했는지 짐작할 수 있소?\""]], [["“That is the very point I was coming to,” he rejoined.", "\"그것이 바로 내가 이르려던 그 점이오,\""], ["“It is a question with me whether the experiment which I propose as a means of vindicating your innocence, may not also be made a means of recovering the lost Diamond as well.", "하고 그가 되받았다. \"나에게는, 당신의 결백을 밝히는 수단으로 내가 제안하는 그 실험이, 그 잃어버린 다이아몬드를 되찾는 수단으로도 될 수 있지 않을까 하는 물음이 있소."], ["When you left Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, with the jewel in your hand, you went back in all probability to your own room——”", "당신이 그 보석을 손에 들고 버린더 아가씨의 응접실을 떠났을 때, 당신은 십중팔구 당신 자신의 방으로 돌아갔을 것이오——\""]], [["“Yes?", "\"그래서요?"], ["and what then?”", "그러고는요?\""]], [["“It is possible, Mr. Blake—I dare not say more—that your idea of preserving the Diamond led, by a natural sequence, to the idea of hiding the Diamond, and that the place in which you hid it was somewhere in your bedroom.", "\"있을 법한 일이오, 블레이크 씨—나는 감히 더 말하지 못하겠소만—그 다이아몬드를 지키려는 당신의 생각이, 자연스러운 잇따름으로, 그 다이아몬드를 감추려는 생각으로 이어졌고, 당신이 그것을 감춘 그 자리가 당신의 침실 어딘가였다는 것이."], ["In that event, the case of the Irish porter may be your case.", "그런 경우, 그 아일랜드인 짐꾼의 경우가 당신의 경우일지도 모르오."], ["You may remember, under the influence of the second dose of opium, the place in which you hid the Diamond under the influence of the first.”", "당신은, 그 둘째 번 아편의 영향 아래, 당신이 첫째 번 영향 아래 그 다이아몬드를 감춘 그 자리를 떠올릴지도 모르오.\""]], [["It was my turn, now, to enlighten Ezra Jennings.", "이제, 에즈라 제닝스를 밝혀 줄 차례는 나였다."], ["I stopped him, before he could say any more.", "나는 그가 더 말하기 전에 그를 멈추게 했다."]], [["“You are speculating,”", "\"당신은 짐작하고 있소,\""], ["I said, “on a result which cannot possibly take place.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"도저히 일어날 수 없는 결과를."], ["The Diamond is, at this moment, in London.”", "그 다이아몬드는, 이 순간, 런던에 있소.\""]], [["He started, and looked at me in great surprise.", "그는 소스라치며, 크나큰 놀라움에 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“In London?”", "\"런던에?\""], ["he repeated.", "하고 그가 되뇌었다."], ["“How did it get to London from Lady Verinder’s house?”", "\"그것이 레이디 버린더의 집에서 어떻게 런던으로 갔소?\""]], [["“Nobody knows.”", "\"아무도 모르오.\""]], [["“You removed it with your own hand from Miss Verinder’s room.", "\"당신은 당신 자신의 손으로 그것을 버린더 아가씨의 방에서 옮겼소."], ["How was it taken out of your keeping?”", "그것이 어떻게 당신의 보관에서 빠져나갔소?\""]], [["“I have no idea how it was taken out of my keeping.”", "\"나는 그것이 어떻게 나의 보관에서 빠져나갔는지 짐작조차 못 하오.\""]], [["“Did you see it, when you woke in the morning?”", "\"당신은 아침에 깨어났을 때 그것을 보았소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니오.\""]], [["“Has Miss Verinder recovered possession of it?”", "\"버린더 아가씨가 그것을 도로 손에 넣었소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니오.\""]], [["“Mr. Blake!", "\"블레이크 씨!"], ["there seems to be something here which wants clearing up.", "여기 밝혀져야 할 무언가가 있는 듯하오."], ["May I ask how you know that the Diamond is, at this moment, in London?”", "당신이, 그 다이아몬드가 이 순간 런던에 있음을 어떻게 아는지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["I had put precisely the same question to Mr. Bruff when I made my first inquiries about the Moonstone, on my return to England.", "나는, 내가 잉글랜드로 돌아와 그 월장석에 대한 나의 첫 조사를 했을 때, 바로 똑같은 물음을 브러프 씨에게 던진 터였다."], ["In answering Ezra Jennings, I accordingly repeated what I had myself heard from the lawyer’s own lips—and what is already familiar to the readers of these pages.", "에즈라 제닝스에게 답하면서, 나는 그에 따라, 내가 그 변호사 자신의 입술에서 들은 것을—그리고 이 쪽들의 독자들에게 이미 익숙한 것을 되뇌었다."]], [["He showed plainly that he was not satisfied with my reply.", "그는 그가 나의 대답에 흡족하지 않음을 또렷이 내보였다."]], [["“With all deference to you,”", "\"당신에 대한 온갖 존중과,\""], ["he said, “and with all deference to your legal adviser, I maintain the opinion which I expressed just now.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 법률 고문에 대한 온갖 존중을 담아, 나는 방금 내가 나타낸 그 견해를 고수하오."], ["It rests, I am well aware, on a mere assumption.", "그것은, 내가 잘 아는 바로는, 한낱 가정에 기대고 있소."], ["Pardon me for reminding you, that your opinion also rests on a mere assumption as well.”", "당신의 견해도 마찬가지로 한낱 가정에 기대고 있음을 당신에게 일깨워 드리는 것을 용서하시오.\""]], [["The view he took of the matter was entirely new to me.", "그가 그 일에 대해 취한 그 견해는 나에게 온전히 새로운 것이었다."], ["I waited anxiously to hear how he would defend it.", "나는 그가 그것을 어떻게 옹호할지 애태우며 들으려 기다렸다."]], [["“I assume,”", "\"나는 가정하오,\""], ["pursued Ezra Jennings, “that the influence of the opium—after impelling you to possess yourself of the Diamond, with the purpose of securing its safety—might also impel you, acting under the same influence and the same motive, to hide it somewhere in your own room.", "하고 에즈라 제닝스가 말을 이었다. \"그 아편의 영향이—당신을, 그것의 안전을 확보할 목적으로, 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣도록 몰아붙인 뒤에—또한 당신을, 같은 영향과 같은 동기 아래 움직여, 그것을 당신 자신의 방 어딘가에 감추도록 몰아붙였을지도 모른다고."], ["You assume that the Hindoo conspirators could by no possibility commit a mistake. The Indians went to Mr.", "당신은 가정하오, 그 힌두인 음모자들이 도저히 잘못을 저지를 수 없다고."], ["Luker’s house after the Diamond—and, therefore, in Mr. Luker’s possession the Diamond must be!", "그 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 찾아 루커 씨의 집으로 갔고—따라서, 그 다이아몬드가 루커 씨의 소유에 있음이 틀림없다고!"], ["Have you any evidence to prove that the Moonstone was taken to London at all?", "당신은 그 월장석이 애초에 런던으로 가져가지기라도 했다는 것을 증명할 무슨 증거라도 있소?"], ["You can’t even guess how, or by whom, it was removed from Lady Verinder’s house!", "당신은 그것이 어떻게, 또는 누구에 의해 레이디 버린더의 집에서 옮겨졌는지 짐작조차 못 하오!"], ["Have you any evidence that the jewel was pledged to Mr. Luker?", "당신은 그 보석이 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔다는 무슨 증거라도 있소?"], ["He declares that he never heard of the Moonstone; and his bankers’ receipt acknowledges nothing but the deposit of a valuable of great price.", "그는 자기가 그 월장석에 대해 들은 적이 없다고 선언하오. 그리고 그의 은행가 영수증은 큰 값어치의 귀중품의 예치 말고는 아무것도 인정하지 않소."], ["The Indians assume that Mr. Luker is lying—and you assume again that the Indians are right.", "그 인도인들은 루커 씨가 거짓말하고 있다고 가정하오—그리고 당신은 다시 그 인도인들이 옳다고 가정하오."], ["All I say, in differing with you, is—that my view is possible.", "당신과 견해를 달리하면서, 내가 하는 말의 전부는—나의 견해가 가능하다는 것이오."], ["What more, Mr. Blake, either logically, or legally, can be said for yours?”", "그 이상, 블레이크 씨, 논리적으로든 법률적으로든, 당신의 견해를 위해 무슨 말을 할 수 있소?\""]], [["It was put strongly; but there was no denying that it was put truly as well.", "그것은 강하게 던져졌다. 허나 그것이 마찬가지로 참되이 던져졌음도 부인할 수 없었다."]], [["“I confess you stagger me,”", "\"솔직히 당신이 나를 휘청거리게 하오,\""], ["I replied.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“Do you object to my writing to Mr. Bruff, and telling him what you have said?”", "\"내가 브러프 씨에게 편지하여, 당신이 말한 것을 그에게 이야기하는 것에 반대하오?\""]], [["“On the contrary, I shall be glad if you will write to Mr.", "\"오히려, 당신이 브러프 씨에게 편지한다면 나는 기쁘겠소."], ["Bruff. If we consult his experience, we may see the matter under a new light.", "만일 우리가 그의 경험을 참조한다면, 우리는 그 일을 새로운 빛 아래 볼지도 모르오."], ["For the present, let us return to our experiment with the opium.", "지금으로서는, 우리의 아편 실험으로 돌아갑시다."], ["We have decided that you leave off the habit of smoking from this moment.”", "우리는 당신이 이 순간부터 담배 피우는 버릇을 끊기로 정했소.\""]], [["“From this moment.”", "\"이 순간부터.\""]], [["“That is the first step.", "\"그것이 첫걸음이오."], ["The next step is to reproduce, as nearly as we can, the domestic circumstances which surrounded you last year.”", "다음 걸음은, 우리가 할 수 있는 한 가까이, 지난해에 당신을 둘러쌌던 그 집안의 상황을 되살리는 것이오.\""]], [["How was this to be done?", "이것이 어떻게 이루어질 것인가?"], ["Lady Verinder was dead.", "레이디 버린더는 죽었다."], ["Rachel and I, so long as the suspicion of theft rested on me, were parted irrevocably.", "레이철과 나는, 절도의 의심이 나에게 얹혀 있는 한, 돌이킬 수 없이 갈라져 있었다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite was away travelling on the Continent.", "고드프리 에이블화이트는 대륙으로 여행을 떠나 있었다."], ["It was simply impossible to reassemble the people who had inhabited the house, when I had slept in it last.", "내가 지난번 그 집에서 잤을 때 그것에 살던 사람들을 다시 불러 모으는 것은 그저 불가능했다."], ["The statement of this objection did not appear to embarrass Ezra Jennings.", "이 반론을 진술해도 에즈라 제닝스는 난처해하는 듯싶지 않았다."], ["He attached very little importance, he said, to reassembling the same people—seeing that it would be vain to expect them to reassume the various positions which they had occupied towards me in the past times.", "그는 같은 사람들을 다시 모으는 데에는 아주 조금밖에 중요성을 두지 않는다고 말했다—그들이 지난 시절 나를 향해 차지했던 갖가지 처지를 다시 취하기를 기대하는 것은 헛되리라고 보았으므로."], ["On the other hand, he considered it essential to the success of the experiment, that I should see the same objects about me which had surrounded me when I was last in the house.", "다른 한편으로, 그는 그 실험의 성공에, 내가 지난번 그 집에 있었을 때 나를 둘러쌌던 것과 같은 물건들을 내 둘레에서 보는 것이 필수라고 여겼다."]], [["“Above all things,”", "\"무엇보다도,\""], ["he said, “you must sleep in the room which you slept in, on the birthday night, and it must be furnished in the same way.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신은 당신이 그 생일 밤에 잔 그 방에서 자야 하오."], ["The stairs, the corridors, and Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, must also be restored to what they were when you saw them last.", "그리고 그것은 같은 식으로 갖추어져야 하오. 그 계단과, 복도와, 버린더 아가씨의 응접실도, 당신이 지난번 그것들을 보았을 때의 그대로로 되돌려져야 하오."], ["It is absolutely necessary, Mr. Blake, to replace every article of furniture in that part of the house which may now be put away.", "블레이크 씨, 그 집의 그 부분에서 이제 치워졌을지도 모를 온갖 세간을 도로 놓는 것이 절대적으로 필요하오."], ["The sacrifice of your cigars will be useless, unless we can get Miss Verinder’s permission to do that.”", "당신의 여송연의 희생은, 우리가 버린더 아가씨의 허락을 얻어 그것을 할 수 없다면, 헛될 것이오.\""]], [["“Who is to apply to her for permission?”", "\"누가 그녀에게 허락을 청할 것이오?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Is it not possible for you to apply?”", "\"당신이 청하는 것이 가능하지 않소?\""]], [["“Quite out of the question.", "\"아주 당치 않소."], ["After what has passed between us on the subject of the lost Diamond, I can neither see her, nor write to her, as things are now.”", "그 잃어버린 다이아몬드라는 주제로 우리 사이에 오간 일을 겪고서, 나는, 사정이 지금 이러하니, 그녀를 만날 수도, 그녀에게 편지할 수도 없소.\""]], [["Ezra Jennings paused, and considered for a moment.", "에즈라 제닝스는 멈추어, 잠시 곰곰이 생각했다."]], [["“May I ask you a delicate question?”", "\"내가 당신에게 섬세한 물음을 하나 해도 되겠소?\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["I signed to him to go on.", "나는 그에게 계속하라고 몸짓했다."]], [["“Am I right, Mr. Blake, in fancying (from one or two things which have dropped from you) that you felt no common interest in Miss Verinder, in former times?”", "\"내가 옳소, 블레이크 씨, (당신에게서 새어 나온 한두 가지로) 당신이 지난 시절 버린더 아가씨에게 여느 것 아닌 관심을 느꼈다고 짐작하는 것이?\""]], [["“Quite right.”", "\"아주 옳소.\""]], [["“Was the feeling returned?”", "\"그 감정이 되돌려졌소?\""]], [["“It was.”", "\"그랬소.\""]], [["“Do you think Miss Verinder would be likely to feel a strong interest in the attempt to prove your innocence?”", "\"당신은 버린더 아가씨가 당신의 결백을 증명하려는 그 시도에 강한 관심을 느낄 공산이 크다고 생각하오?\""]], [["“I am certain of it.”", "\"나는 그것을 확신하오.\""]], [["“In that case, I will write to Miss Verinder—if you will give me leave.”", "\"그런 경우, 내가 버린더 아가씨에게 편지하겠소—당신이 나에게 허락한다면.\""]], [["“Telling her of the proposal that you have made to me?”", "\"당신이 나에게 한 그 제안을 그녀에게 이야기하고?\""]], [["“Telling her of everything that has passed between us today.”", "\"오늘 우리 사이에 오간 모든 것을 그녀에게 이야기하고.\""]], [["It is needless to say that I eagerly accepted the service which he had offered to me.", "내가 그가 나에게 내놓은 그 도움을 간절히 받아들인 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없다."]], [["“I shall have time to write by today’s post,”", "\"오늘 우편으로 편지할 짬이 있겠소,\""], ["he said, looking at his watch.", "하고 그가 그의 시계를 보며 말했다."], ["“Don’t forget to lock up your cigars, when you get back to the hotel!", "\"호텔로 돌아가거든, 당신의 여송연을 넣고 잠그는 것을 잊지 마시오!"], ["I will call tomorrow morning and hear how you have passed the night.”", "나는 내일 아침 찾아와 당신이 밤을 어떻게 보냈는지 듣겠소.\""]], [["I rose to take leave of him; and attempted to express the grateful sense of his kindness which I really felt.", "나는 그에게 작별하려 일어섰다. 그리고 내가 정말로 느낀, 그의 친절에 대한 고마운 느낌을 나타내려 했다."]], [["He pressed my hand gently.", "그는 부드럽게 내 손을 쥐었다."], ["“Remember what I told you on the moor,”", "\"그 황야에서 내가 당신에게 말한 것을 기억하시오,\""], ["he answered.", "하고 그가 대답했다."], ["“If I can do you this little service, Mr. Blake, I shall feel it like a last gleam of sunshine, falling on the evening of a long and clouded day.”", "\"만일 내가 당신에게 이 자그마한 도움이 될 수 있다면, 블레이크 씨, 나는 그것을, 길고 흐렸던 하루의 저녁에 떨어지는 마지막 한 줄기 햇살처럼 느낄 것이오.\""]], [["We parted.", "우리는 헤어졌다."], ["It was then the fifteenth of June.", "그때는 유월 십오일이었다."], ["The events of the next ten days—every one of them more or less directly connected with the experiment of which I was the passive object—are all placed on record, exactly as they happened, in the Journal habitually kept by Mr.", "뒤이은 열흘의 사건들은—그 하나하나가 다소간 곧바로, 내가 그 수동적인 대상이었던 그 실험과 이어진—캔디 씨의 조수가 늘 써 온 일기에, 그것들이 일어난 그대로 정확히, 죄 기록되어 있다."], ["Candy’s assistant. In the pages of Ezra Jennings nothing is concealed, and nothing is forgotten.", "에즈라 제닝스의 쪽들에는 아무것도 숨겨지지 않고, 아무것도 잊히지 않는다."], ["Let Ezra Jennings tell how the venture with the opium was tried, and how it ended.", "그 아편의 모험이 어떻게 시도되었고, 어떻게 끝났는지, 에즈라 제닝스가 이야기하게 두자."]], [["Extracted from the Journal of Ezra Jennings.", "에즈라 제닝스의 일기에서 발췌함."]], [["1849.—June 15....", "1849년.—유월 15일.…"], ["With some interruption from patients, and some interruption from pain, I finished my letter to Miss Verinder in time for today’s post.", "환자들로 인한 얼마쯤의 방해와, 통증으로 인한 얼마쯤의 방해에도, 나는 오늘 우편에 대도록 버린더 아가씨에게 보내는 나의 편지를 마쳤다."], ["I failed to make it as short a letter as I could have wished.", "나는 그것을 내가 바랐을 만큼 짧은 편지로 만드는 데에 실패했다."], ["But I think I have made it plain.", "허나 나는 그것을 평이하게 만든 것 같다."], ["It leaves her entirely mistress of her own decision.", "그것은 그녀를 온전히 자기 자신의 결정의 주인으로 남겨 둔다."], ["If she consents to assist the experiment, she consents of her own free will, and not as a favour to Mr. Franklin Blake or to me.", "만일 그녀가 그 실험을 돕는 데에 동의한다면, 그녀는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨나 나에 대한 호의로가 아니라, 제 자유로운 뜻으로 동의하는 것이다."]], [["June 16th.—Rose late, after a dreadful night; the vengeance of yesterday’s opium, pursuing me through a series of frightful dreams.", "유월 16일.—끔찍한 밤을 지새운 뒤, 늦게 일어났다. 어제의 아편의 앙갚음이, 일련의 무시무시한 꿈을 통해 나를 좇았다."], ["At one time I was whirling through empty space with the phantoms of the dead, friends and enemies together.", "어느 때는 내가 죽은 이들의, 벗과 원수가 함께 있는 환영들과 더불어 텅 빈 허공을 소용돌이치며 날았다."], ["At another, the one beloved face which I shall never see again, rose at my bedside, hideously phosphorescent in the black darkness, and glared and grinned at me.", "다른 때는, 내가 다시는 보지 못할 그 하나의 사랑하는 얼굴이, 내 잠자리 곁에 솟아올라, 그 캄캄한 어둠 속에서 흉측하게 인광을 뿜으며, 나를 노려보고 나를 향해 이를 드러내며 웃었다."], ["A slight return of the old pain, at the usual time in the early morning, was welcome as a change.", "이른 아침의 여느 때에 그 옛 통증이 조금 돌아온 것이, 변화로서 반가웠다."], ["It dispelled the visions—and it was bearable because it did that.", "그것은 그 환영들을 흩어 놓았다—그리고 그것이 그리했기에 견딜 만했다."]], [["My bad night made it late in the morning, before I could get to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "나의 나쁜 밤이, 내가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 갈 수 있기 전에, 아침을 늦게 만들었다."], ["I found him stretched on the sofa, breakfasting on brandy and soda water, and a dry biscuit.", "나는 그가 소파에 몸을 뻗고, 브랜디와 소다수와 마른 비스킷으로 아침을 들고 있는 것을 보았다."]], [["“I am beginning, as well as you could possibly wish,”", "\"당신이 정녕 바랄 만큼 잘, 나는 시작하고 있소,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“A miserable, restless night; and a total failure of appetite this morning.", "\"비참하고, 안절부절못한 밤. 그리고 오늘 아침 완전한 입맛의 실종."], ["Exactly what happened last year, when I gave up my cigars.", "지난해에 내가 여송연을 끊었을 때 일어난 것과 정확히 같소."], ["The sooner I am ready for my second dose of laudanum, the better I shall be pleased.”", "내가 나의 둘째 번 아편팅크를 위해 채비될수록, 나는 그만큼 더 흡족할 것이오.\""]], [["“You shall have it on the earliest possible day,” I answered.", "\"당신은 되도록 이른 날에 그것을 얻을 것이오,\""], ["“In the meantime, we must be as careful of your health as we can.", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"그동안, 우리는 되도록 당신의 건강을 조심해야 하오."], ["If we allow you to become exhausted, we shall fail in that way.", "만일 우리가 당신을 지치게 둔다면, 우리는 그런 식으로 실패할 것이오."], ["You must get an appetite for your dinner.", "당신은 당신의 저녁을 위한 입맛을 얻어야 하오."], ["In other words, you must get a ride or a walk this morning, in the fresh air.”", "다시 말해, 당신은 오늘 아침, 맑은 공기 속에서 승마나 산책을 해야 하오.\""]], [["“I will ride, if they can find me a horse here.", "\"여기서 나에게 말을 구해 줄 수 있다면, 나는 말을 타겠소."], ["By-the-bye, I wrote to Mr. Bruff, yesterday.", "그러고 보니, 나는 어제 브러프 씨에게 편지했소."], ["Have you written to Miss Verinder?”", "당신은 버린더 아가씨에게 편지했소?\""]], [["“Yes—by last night’s post.”", "\"그렇소—지난밤 우편으로.\""]], [["“Very good.", "\"아주 좋소."], ["We shall have some news worth hearing, to tell each other tomorrow.", "우리는 내일 서로에게 이야기할, 들을 값어치 있는 소식을 얼마쯤 갖게 될 것이오."], ["Don’t go yet!", "아직 가지 마시오!"], ["I have a word to say to you.", "당신에게 할 말이 있소."], ["You appeared to think, yesterday, that our experiment with the opium was not likely to be viewed very favourably by some of my friends.", "당신은 어제, 우리의 아편 실험이 나의 어떤 벗들에게 그리 좋게 여겨질 성싶지 않다고 생각하는 듯했소."], ["You were quite right.", "당신은 아주 옳았소."], ["I call old Gabriel Betteredge one of my friends; and you will be amused to hear that he protested strongly when I saw him yesterday.", "나는 늙은 게이브리얼 베터리지를 나의 벗들 가운데 하나로 부르오. 그리고 당신은, 내가 어제 그를 보았을 때 그가 강하게 항변한 것을 들으면 재미있어할 것이오."], ["‘You have done a wonderful number of foolish things in the course of your life, Mr. Franklin, but this tops them all!’ There is Betteredge’s opinion!", "'나리는 나리의 생애 가운데 놀라울 만큼 숱한 어리석은 일을 하셨습니다요, 프랭클린 나리, 허나 이것은 그 모두의 으뜸입니다!'"], ["You will make allowance for his prejudices, I am sure, if you and he happen to meet?”", "그것이 베터리지의 견해요! 만일 당신과 그가 마주치게 된다면, 당신은 틀림없이 그의 편견을 헤아려 주겠지요?\""]], [["I left Mr.", "나는, 내 환자들 사이의 나의 왕진을 돌러, 블레이크 씨를 떠났다."], ["Blake, to go my rounds among my patients; feeling the better and the happier even for the short interview that I had had with him.", "내가 그와 가진 그 짧은 면담만으로도 더 나아지고 더 행복해진 것을 느끼면서."]], [["What is the secret of the attraction that there is for me in this man?", "이 사람에게 내가 느끼는 그 끌림의 비밀은 무엇인가?"], ["Does it only mean that I feel the contrast between the frankly kind manner in which he has allowed me to become acquainted with him, and the merciless dislike and distrust with which I am met by other people?", "그것은 그저, 그가 나로 하여금 그와 사귀게 해 준 그 솔직하고 다정한 태도와, 다른 사람들이 나를 맞는 그 인정사정없는 미움과 불신 사이의 대비를 내가 느낀다는 것을 뜻할 뿐인가?"], ["Or is there really something in him which answers to the yearning that I have for a little human sympathy—the yearning, which has survived the solitude and persecution of many years; which seems to grow keener and keener, as the time comes nearer and nearer when I shall endure and feel no more?", "아니면 그에게, 내가 얼마쯤의 인간적 교감에 대해 지닌 그 갈망에—여러 해의 고독과 박해를 견디고 살아남은, 내가 더는 견디지도 느끼지도 않게 될 그때가 점점 더 가까이 다가올수록 더욱더 날카로워지는 듯한 그 갈망에 응답하는 무언가가 정말로 있는가?"], ["How useless to ask these questions!", "이 물음들을 하는 것이 얼마나 헛된가!"], ["Mr. Blake has given me a new interest in life.", "블레이크 씨는 나에게 삶에 대한 새로운 관심을 주었다."], ["Let that be enough, without seeking to know what the new interest is.", "그 새로운 관심이 무엇인지 알려 하지 않고, 그것으로 넉넉하다 하자."]], [["June 17th.—Before breakfast, this morning, Mr. Candy informed me that he was going away for a fortnight, on a visit to a friend in the south of England.", "유월 17일.—오늘 아침, 아침 식사 전에, 캔디 씨가 나에게, 자기가 잉글랜드 남부의 어느 벗을 찾아, 두 주 동안 떠나 있겠다고 알렸다."], ["He gave me as many special directions, poor fellow, about the patients, as if he still had the large practice which he possessed before he was taken ill.", "그는 나에게, 가엾은 사람, 마치 그가 아직도 그가 병에 걸리기 전에 지녔던 그 큰 개업을 하고 있기라도 한 양, 그 환자들에 대해 숱한 각별한 지시를 주었다."], ["The practice is worth little enough now!", "그 개업은 이제 값어치가 얼마 안 된다!"], ["Other doctors have superseded him; and nobody who can help it will employ me.", "다른 의사들이 그를 밀어냈다. 그리고 어쩔 수 있는 사람이면 아무도 나를 부리지 않을 것이다."]], [["It is perhaps fortunate that he is to be away just at this time.", "그가 바로 이때에 떠나 있게 되는 것이 어쩌면 다행이다."], ["He would have been mortified if I had not informed him of the experiment which I am going to try with Mr.", "만일 내가 그에게 내가 블레이크 씨와 해 보려는 그 실험을 알리지 않았다면 그는 언짢아했을 것이다."], ["Blake. And I hardly know what undesirable results might not have happened, if I had taken him into my confidence.", "그리고 만일 내가 그를 나의 믿음 안에 들였다면 무슨 바람직하지 못한 결과가 일어났을지 나는 좀처럼 모르겠다."], ["Better as it is.", "지금 이대로가 더 낫다."], ["Unquestionably, better as it is.", "두말할 것 없이, 지금 이대로가 더 낫다."]], [["The post brought me Miss Verinder’s answer, after Mr. Candy had left the house.", "우편이, 캔디 씨가 그 집을 떠난 뒤에, 나에게 버린더 아가씨의 답장을 가져다주었다."]], [["A charming letter!", "매혹적인 편지!"], ["It gives me the highest opinion of her.", "그것은 나로 하여금 그녀를 더없이 높이 여기게 한다."], ["There is no attempt to conceal the interest that she feels in our proceedings.", "우리의 일에 그녀가 느끼는 관심을 감추려는 아무 시도도 없다."], ["She tells me, in the prettiest manner, that my letter has satisfied her of Mr. Blake’s innocence, without the slightest need (so far as she is concerned) of putting my assertion to the proof.", "그녀는, 더없이 어여쁜 태도로, 나에게, 나의 편지가 (그녀에 관한 한) 나의 주장을 증명에 부칠 조금의 필요도 없이, 그녀에게 블레이크 씨의 결백을 흡족히 여기게 했다고 말한다."], ["She even upbraids herself—most undeservedly, poor thing!—for not having divined at the time what the true solution of the mystery might really be.", "그녀는 심지어—더없이 부당하게도, 가엾은 것!—그 수수께끼의 참된 해답이 정말로 무엇일지를 그때 알아채지 못한 데에 대해 자신을 나무란다."], ["The motive underlying all this proceeds evidently from something more than a generous eagerness to make atonement for a wrong which she has innocently inflicted on another person.", "이 모든 것의 밑에 깔린 동기는 뚜렷이, 그녀가 무심결에 다른 사람에게 가한 잘못을 속죄하려는 너그러운 간절함 이상의 무언가에서 비롯한다."], ["It is plain that she has loved him, throughout the estrangement between them.", "그녀가, 그들 사이의 소원함 내내, 그를 사랑해 온 것이 뚜렷하다."], ["In more than one place the rapture of discovering that he has deserved to be loved, breaks its way innocently through the stoutest formalities of pen and ink, and even defies the stronger restraint still of writing to a stranger.", "한 곳 넘게에서, 그가 사랑받을 자격이 있었음을 발견한 그 황홀함이, 펜과 잉크의 더없이 굳센 격식을 뚫고 무심결에 제 길을 내고, 심지어 낯선 이에게 편지하는 더 강한 제약마저 무릅쓴다."], ["Is it possible (I ask myself, in reading this delightful letter) that I, of all men in the world, am chosen to be the means of bringing these two young people together again?", "가능한 일인가 (나는 이 기쁨 어린 편지를 읽으며 스스로에게 묻는다), 세상 온갖 사람 가운데 하필 내가, 이 두 젊은이를 다시 함께 맺어 주는 수단으로 뽑힌 것이?"], ["My own happiness has been trampled under foot; my own love has been torn from me.", "나 자신의 행복은 발밑에 짓밟혔다. 나 자신의 사랑은 나에게서 뜯겨 나갔다."], ["Shall I live to see a happiness of others, which is of my making—a love renewed, which is of my bringing back?", "나는, 내가 만드는 남들의 행복을—내가 되돌리는, 되살아난 사랑을 살아서 보게 될 것인가?"], ["Oh merciful Death, let me see it before your arms enfold me, before your voice whispers to me, “Rest at last!”", "오 자비로운 죽음이여, 그대의 팔이 나를 감싸기 전에, 그대의 목소리가 나에게 \"마침내 쉬어라!\" 하고 속삭이기 전에, 나로 하여금 그것을 보게 하라."]], [["There are two requests contained in the letter.", "그 편지에는 두 가지 청이 담겨 있다."], ["One of them prevents me from showing it to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "그중 하나가 나로 하여금 그것을 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 보이지 못하게 한다."], ["I am authorised to tell him that Miss Verinder willingly consents to place her house at our disposal; and, that said, I am desired to add no more.", "나는 버린더 아가씨가 기꺼이 자기 집을 우리 마음대로 쓰게 하는 데에 동의한다고 그에게 말할 권한이 있고—그것을 말하고 나서, 나는 더는 아무것도 덧붙이지 말라는 청을 받았다."]], [["So far, it is easy to comply with her wishes.", "여기까지는, 그녀의 바람에 따르기가 쉽다."], ["But the second request embarrasses me seriously.", "허나 그 둘째 청이 나를 심각하게 난처하게 한다."]], [["Not content with having written to Mr. Betteredge, instructing him to carry out whatever directions I may have to give, Miss Verinder asks leave to assist me, by personally superintending the restoration of her own sitting-room.", "베터리지 씨에게 편지하여, 내가 줄 어떤 지시든 이행하라고 이른 것으로 만족하지 못하고, 버린더 아가씨는 제 응접실의 복원을 몸소 감독함으로써 나를 돕게 해 달라고 청한다."], ["She only waits a word of reply from me to make the journey to Yorkshire, and to be present as one of the witnesses on the night when the opium is tried for the second time.", "그녀는 다만 나의 한마디 답을 기다려, 요크셔로 여행하여, 그 아편이 두 번째로 시도되는 그 밤에 증인 가운데 하나로 자리하려 한다."]], [["Here, again, there is a motive under the surface; and, here again, I fancy that I can find it out.", "여기서도, 다시, 표면 아래 동기가 있다. 그리고, 여기서도, 다시, 나는 그것을 알아낼 수 있을 것 같다."]], [["What she has forbidden me to tell Mr. Franklin Blake, she is (as I interpret it) eager to tell him with her own lips, before he is put to the test which is to vindicate his character in the eyes of other people.", "그녀가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 말하는 것을 나에게 금한 것을, 그녀는 (내가 그것을 해석하기로는) 그가, 다른 사람들의 눈에 그의 명예를 밝힐 그 시험에 부쳐지기 전에, 제 입술로 그에게 말하기를 간절히 바란다."], ["I understand and admire this generous anxiety to acquit him, without waiting until his innocence may, or may not, be proved.", "나는, 그의 결백이 증명되든 안 되든, 그것을 기다리지 않고 그를 무죄로 여기려는 이 너그러운 안달을 이해하고 감탄한다."], ["It is the atonement that she is longing to make, poor girl, after having innocently and inevitably wronged him.", "그것은, 그녀가, 무심결에, 그리고 어쩔 수 없이 그에게 잘못한 뒤에, 하기를 갈망하는 속죄다, 가엾은 아가씨."], ["But the thing cannot be done.", "허나 그 일은 이루어질 수 없다."], ["I have no sort of doubt that the agitation which a meeting between them would produce on both sides—reviving dormant feelings, appealing to old memories, awakening new hopes—would, in their effect on the mind of Mr. Blake, be almost certainly fatal to the success of our experiment.", "나는, 그들 사이의 만남이 양편에 자아낼 그 동요가—잠든 감정을 되살리고, 옛 기억에 호소하고, 새 희망을 일깨우며—블레이크 씨의 마음에 미치는 그 효과에 있어, 우리 실험의 성공에 거의 틀림없이 치명적이리라는 것을 조금도 의심하지 않는다."], ["It is hard enough, as things are, to reproduce in him the conditions as they existed, or nearly as they existed, last year.", "사정이 이러하니, 지난해에 있었던, 또는 거의 있었던 그대로의 조건을 그에게 되살리는 것만으로도 충분히 어렵다."], ["With new interests and new emotions to agitate him, the attempt would be simply useless.", "그를 뒤흔들 새 관심과 새 감정이 있다면, 그 시도는 그저 헛될 것이다."]], [["And yet, knowing this, I cannot find it in my heart to disappoint her.", "그럼에도, 이것을 알면서도, 나는 그녀를 실망시킬 마음이 도무지 나지 않는다."], ["I must try if I can discover some new arrangement, before post-time, which will allow me to say Yes to Miss Verinder, without damage to the service which I have bound myself to render to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "나는, 우편 시간 전에, 내가 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 하기로 나를 맨 그 도움에 해를 끼치지 않고, 버린더 아가씨에게 그렇다고 말할 수 있게 해 줄 어떤 새로운 방안을 찾아낼 수 있을지 애써 봐야 한다."]], [["Two o’clock.—I have just returned from my round of medical visits; having begun, of course, by calling at the hotel.", "두 시.—나는 방금 나의 왕진을 돌고 돌아왔다—물론, 그 호텔에 들르는 것으로 시작하여."]], [["Mr. Blake’s report of the night is the same as before.", "블레이크 씨의 그 밤에 대한 보고는 이전과 같다."], ["He has had some intervals of broken sleep, and no more.", "그는 얼마쯤의 끊긴 잠의 사이가 있었을 뿐, 그 이상은 없었다."], ["But he feels it less today, having slept after yesterday’s dinner.", "허나 그는 어제 저녁을 든 뒤 잤으므로, 오늘은 그것을 덜 느낀다."], ["This after-dinner sleep is the result, no doubt, of the ride which I advised him to take.", "이 저녁 식사 뒤의 잠은, 두말할 것 없이, 내가 그에게 권한 그 승마의 결과다."], ["I fear I shall have to curtail his restorative exercise in the fresh air.", "안됐지만 나는 맑은 공기 속에서의 그의 회복 운동을 줄여야 할 것 같다."], ["He must not be too well; he must not be too ill.", "그는 너무 건강해서도 안 되고, 너무 아파서도 안 된다."], ["It is a case (as a sailor would say) of very fine steering.", "이것은 (뱃사람이라면 그리 말하듯) 아주 섬세한 키잡이가 필요한 경우다."]], [["He has not heard yet from Mr. Bruff.", "그는 아직 브러프 씨에게서 소식을 듣지 못했다."], ["I found him eager to know if I had received any answer from Miss Verinder.", "나는 그가 내가 버린더 아가씨에게서 무슨 답을 받았는지 알고 싶어 안달하는 것을 보았다."]], [["I told him exactly what I was permitted to tell, and no more.", "나는 그에게, 내가 말하도록 허락받은 것을 정확히, 그 이상은 아무것도 말하지 않았다."], ["It was quite needless to invent excuses for not showing him the letter.", "그에게 그 편지를 보이지 않는 데에 대한 핑계를 지어낼 것은 아주 없었다."], ["He told me bitterly enough, poor fellow, that he understood the delicacy which disinclined me to produce it.", "그는, 가엾은 사람, 자기가 내가 그것을 내놓기를 꺼리게 하는 그 섬세함을 이해한다고, 자못 쓰라리게 나에게 말했다."], ["“She consents, of course, as a matter of common courtesy and common justice,”", "\"그녀는, 물론, 예사로운 예의와 예사로운 도리에서 동의하는 것이오,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “But she keeps her own opinion of me, and waits to see the result.”", "\"허나 그녀는 나에 대한 제 견해를 지킨 채, 그 결과를 보려 기다리는 것이오.\""], ["I was sorely tempted to hint that he was now wronging her as she had wronged him.", "나는 그에게, 그가 이제 그녀가 그에게 잘못한 것처럼 그녀에게 잘못하고 있다고 넌지시 비칠 심한 유혹을 느꼈다."], ["On reflection, I shrank from forestalling her in the double luxury of surprising and forgiving him.", "곰곰이 생각한 끝에, 나는 그를 놀라게 하고 용서하는 그 이중의 호사에서 그녀를 앞지르기를 움츠려 피했다."]], [["My visit was a very short one.", "나의 방문은 아주 짧았다."], ["After the experience of the other night, I have been compelled once more to give up my dose of opium.", "요전 날 밤의 겪음 뒤에, 나는 다시 한 번 나의 아편 한 모금을 끊지 않을 수 없었다."], ["As a necessary result, the agony of the disease that is in me has got the upper hand again.", "필연적인 결과로, 내 안의 그 병의 고통이 다시 우위를 차지했다."], ["I felt the attack coming on, and left abruptly, so as not to alarm or distress him.", "나는 그 발작이 다가옴을 느끼고, 그를 놀라게 하거나 괴롭히지 않으려, 갑자기 떠났다."], ["It only lasted a quarter of an hour this time, and it left me strength enough to go on with my work.", "이번에는 그것이 겨우 십오 분 이어졌고, 나에게 나의 일을 계속할 만큼의 힘을 남겼다."]], [["Five o’clock.—I have written my reply to Miss Verinder.", "다섯 시.—나는 버린더 아가씨에게 나의 답장을 썼다."]], [["The arrangement I have proposed reconciles the interests on both sides, if she will only consent to it.", "내가 제안한 그 방안은, 그녀가 그것에 동의하기만 한다면, 양편의 이해를 조화시킨다."], ["After first stating the objections that there are to a meeting between Mr. Blake and herself, before the experiment is tried, I have suggested that she should so time her journey as to arrive at the house privately, on the evening when we make the attempt.", "먼저 그 실험이 시도되기 전에 블레이크 씨와 그녀 자신 사이의 만남에 있는 반론들을 진술하고서, 나는 그녀가, 우리가 그 시도를 하는 그 저녁에 남몰래 그 집에 다다르도록 여행 시간을 잡을 것을 제안했다."], ["Travelling by the afternoon train from London, she would delay her arrival until nine o’clock.", "런던에서 오후 기차로 여행하면, 그녀는 그녀의 다다름을 아홉 시까지 늦출 것이다."], ["At that hour, I have undertaken to see Mr.", "그 시각에, 나는 블레이크 씨를 그의 침실로 안전히 들이기로 했다."], ["Blake safely into his bedchamber; and so to leave Miss Verinder free to occupy her own rooms until the time comes for administering the laudanum.", "그리하여 그 아편팅크를 투여할 때가 올 때까지 버린더 아가씨가 자유로이 제 방을 쓰도록 남겨 두기로."], ["When that has been done, there can be no objection to her watching the result, with the rest of us.", "그것이 이루어지고 나면, 그녀가 우리 나머지와 함께 그 결과를 지켜보는 데에 아무 반론도 있을 수 없다."], ["On the next morning, she shall show Mr. Blake (if she likes) her correspondence with me, and shall satisfy him in that way that he was acquitted in her estimation, before the question of his innocence was put to the proof.", "이튿날 아침, 그녀는 (원한다면) 블레이크 씨에게 나와의 그녀의 편지 왕래를 보여, 그가 그의 결백의 문제가 증명에 부쳐지기 전에 그녀의 평가에서 무죄로 여겨졌음을 그런 식으로 그에게 흡족히 여기게 할 것이다."]], [["In that sense, I have written to her.", "그런 뜻으로, 나는 그녀에게 편지했다."], ["This is all that I can do today.", "이것이 오늘 내가 할 수 있는 전부다."], ["Tomorrow I must see Mr. Betteredge, and give the necessary directions for re-opening the house.", "내일 나는 베터리지 씨를 보고, 그 집을 다시 여는 데에 필요한 지시를 주어야 한다."]], [["June 18th.—Late again, in calling on Mr. Franklin Blake.", "유월 18일.—다시 늦게, 프랭클린 블레이크 씨를 찾아갔다."], ["More of that horrible pain in the early morning; followed, this time, by complete prostration, for some hours.", "이른 아침의 그 끔찍한 통증이 더 있었고, 이번에는, 몇 시간 동안의 완전한 탈진이 뒤따랐다."], ["I foresee, in spite of the penalties which it exacts from me, that I shall have to return to the opium for the hundredth time.", "나는, 그것이 나에게 물리는 벌에도, 백 번째로 그 아편으로 돌아가야 할 것을 내다본다."], ["If I had only myself to think of, I should prefer the sharp pains to the frightful dreams.", "만일 나 자신만 생각하면 된다면, 나는 그 무시무시한 꿈보다 그 날카로운 통증을 택하련다."], ["But the physical suffering exhausts me.", "허나 그 몸의 괴로움이 나를 지치게 한다."], ["If I let myself sink, it may end in my becoming useless to Mr. Blake at the time when he wants me most.", "만일 내가 나 자신을 가라앉게 둔다면, 그것은 블레이크 씨가 나를 가장 필요로 하는 그때에 내가 그에게 쓸모없게 되는 것으로 끝날지도 모른다."]], [["It was nearly one o’clock before I could get to the hotel today.", "오늘 내가 그 호텔에 갈 수 있기 전에 거의 한 시가 되었다."], ["The visit, even in my shattered condition, proved to be a most amusing one—thanks entirely to the presence on the scene of Gabriel Betteredge.", "그 방문은, 나의 산산조각 난 상태에서조차, 더없이 재미있는 것으로 드러났다—온전히 게이브리얼 베터리지가 그 자리에 있어 준 덕분에."]], [["I found him in the room, when I went in.", "내가 들어갔을 때, 나는 그가 방에 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He withdrew to the window and looked out, while I put my first customary question to my patient.", "내가 나의 환자에게 나의 첫 여느 물음을 던지는 동안, 그는 창가로 물러나 밖을 내다보았다."], ["Mr. Blake had slept badly again, and he felt the loss of rest this morning more than he had felt it yet.", "블레이크 씨는 다시 잠을 잘 못 잤고, 그는 오늘 아침 그 안식의 손실을 여태껏보다 더 느꼈다."]], [["I asked next if he had heard from Mr. Bruff.", "나는 다음으로 그가 브러프 씨에게서 소식을 들었는지 물었다."]], [["A letter had reached him that morning. Mr.", "그날 아침 그에게 편지 한 통이 다다랐다."], ["Bruff expressed the strongest disapproval of the course which his friend and client was taking under my advice.", "브러프 씨는 그의 벗이자 의뢰인이 나의 조언에 따라 취하고 있는 그 방책에 더없이 강한 반대를 나타냈다."], ["It was mischievous—for it excited hopes that might never be realised.", "그것은 해로웠다—결코 이루어지지 않을지도 모를 희망을 부추기니 말이다."], ["It was quite unintelligible to his mind, except that it looked like a piece of trickery, akin to the trickery of mesmerism, clairvoyance, and the like.", "그것은, 그것이 최면술, 천리안 따위의 술수와 비슷한 한 조각 술수처럼 보인다는 것 말고는, 그의 마음에는 아주 알쏭달쏭했다."], ["It unsettled Miss Verinder’s house, and it would end in unsettling Miss Verinder herself.", "그것은 버린더 아가씨의 집을 어수선하게 했고, 그것은 버린더 아가씨 자신을 어수선하게 하는 것으로 끝날 터였다."], ["He had put the case (without mentioning names) to an eminent physician; and the eminent physician had smiled, had shaken his head, and had said—nothing.", "그는 그 경우를 (이름은 대지 않고) 어느 저명한 내과의에게 내놓았다. 그리고 그 저명한 내과의는 미소 짓고, 고개를 젓고, 말했다—아무것도."], ["On these grounds, Mr. Bruff entered his protest, and left it there.", "이런 근거에서, 브러프 씨는 그의 항변을 제기하고, 그것을 거기 두었다."]], [["My next inquiry related to the subject of the Diamond.", "나의 다음 물음은 그 다이아몬드라는 주제에 관련되었다."], ["Had the lawyer produced any evidence to prove that the jewel was in London?", "그 변호사가 그 보석이 런던에 있음을 증명할 무슨 증거라도 내놓았던가?"]], [["No, the lawyer had simply declined to discuss the question.", "아니, 그 변호사는 그저 그 문제를 논하기를 마다했다."], ["He was himself satisfied that the Moonstone had been pledged to Mr. Luker.", "그 자신은 그 월장석이 루커 씨에게 저당 잡혔음을 흡족히 여겼다."], ["His eminent absent friend, Mr. Murthwaite (whose consummate knowledge of the Indian character no one could deny), was satisfied also.", "그의 저명한 자리를 비운 벗, 머스웨이트 씨(그의 인도인 기질에 대한 더없이 완숙한 앎을 아무도 부인할 수 없었다)도 마찬가지로 흡족히 여겼다."], ["Under these circumstances, and with the many demands already made on him, he must decline entering into any disputes on the subject of evidence.", "이런 상황에서, 그리고 이미 그에게 걸린 숱한 요구가 있으니, 그는 증거라는 주제에 대한 어떤 다툼에도 들기를 마다해야 했다."], ["Time would show; and Mr.", "시간이 밝혀 줄 터였다."], ["Bruff was willing to wait for time.", "그리고 브러프 씨는 기꺼이 시간을 기다렸다."]], [["It was quite plain—even if Mr. Blake had not made it plainer still by reporting the substance of the letter, instead of reading what was actually written—that distrust of me was at the bottom of all this.", "그것은 아주 뚜렷했다—블레이크 씨가, 실제로 적힌 것을 읽는 대신 그 편지의 알맹이를 전함으로써 그것을 한층 더 뚜렷이 하지 않았더라도—나에 대한 불신이 이 모든 것의 밑바탕에 있음이."], ["Having myself foreseen that result, I was neither mortified nor surprised.", "나 자신이 그 결과를 미리 내다본 터라, 나는 언짢지도 놀랍지도 않았다."], ["I asked Mr. Blake if his friend’s protest had shaken him.", "나는 블레이크 씨에게 그의 벗의 항변이 그를 흔들어 놓았는지 물었다."], ["He answered emphatically, that it had not produced the slightest effect on his mind.", "그는 힘주어, 그것이 그의 마음에 조금의 영향도 미치지 않았다고 대답했다."], ["I was free after that to dismiss Mr. Bruff from consideration—and I did dismiss him accordingly.", "나는 그 뒤로 브러프 씨를 헤아림에서 물릴 수 있었다—그리고 나는 그에 따라 그를 물렸다."]], [["A pause in the talk between us, followed—and Gabriel Betteredge came out from his retirement at the window.", "우리 사이의 이야기에 잠시 멈춤이 뒤따랐다—그리고 게이브리얼 베터리지가 창가의 그의 은둔에서 나왔다."]], [["“Can you favour me with your attention, sir?”", "\"저에게 주의를 좀 베풀어 주시겠습니까, 선생?\""], ["he inquired, addressing himself to me.", "하고 그가 나에게 말을 건네며 물었다."]], [["“I am quite at your service,”", "\"나는 아주 당신을 위해 있소,\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."]], [["Betteredge took a chair and seated himself at the table.", "베터리지는 의자 하나를 가져와 그 탁자에 앉았다."], ["He produced a huge old-fashioned leather pocket-book, with a pencil of dimensions to match.", "그는 그에 걸맞은 크기의 연필과 함께, 커다란 예스러운 가죽 수첩을 꺼냈다."], ["Having put on his spectacles, he opened the pocket-book, at a blank page, and addressed himself to me once more.", "안경을 쓰고, 그는 빈 쪽에서 그 수첩을 펴고, 다시 한 번 나에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I have lived,”", "\"저는 살아왔습니다,\""], ["said Betteredge, looking at me sternly, “nigh on fifty years in the service of my late lady.", "하고 베터리지가 나를 엄히 보며 말했다. \"고인이 되신 저의 마님을 섬기며 거의 오십 년을."], ["I was page-boy before that, in the service of the old lord, her father.", "그 전에는, 그분의 아버님이신 옛 나리를 섬기며 시동이었습지요."], ["I am now somewhere between seventy and eighty years of age—never mind exactly where!", "저는 이제 일흔과 여든 사이 어딘가입니다—정확히 어디인지는 아무래도 좋습니다!"], ["I am reckoned to have got as pretty a knowledge and experience of the world as most men.", "저는 대부분의 사람만큼 세상에 대한 제법 훌륭한 앎과 경험을 얻은 것으로 여겨집니다."], ["And what does it all end in? It ends, Mr.", "그런데 그 모든 것이 무엇으로 끝나나요?"], ["Ezra Jennings, in a conjuring trick being performed on Mr. Franklin Blake, by a doctor’s assistant with a bottle of laudanum—and by the living jingo, I’m appointed, in my old age, to be conjurer’s boy!”", "그것은, 에즈라 제닝스 씨, 프랭클린 블레이크 나리에게, 아편팅크 한 병을 든 의사의 조수에 의해 요술 부리기가 행해지는 것으로 끝납니다—그리고 살아 계신 하느님을 걸고, 저는, 이 늙은 나이에, 요술쟁이의 조수 노릇을 하도록 임명됩니다!\""]], [["Mr. Blake burst out laughing.", "블레이크 씨가 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["I attempted to speak.", "나는 말하려 했다."], ["Betteredge held up his hand, in token that he had not done yet.", "베터리지가 아직 안 끝났다는 표시로 손을 들었다."]], [["“Not a word, Mr. Jennings!”", "\"한마디도 마십시오, 제닝스 씨!\""], ["he said, “It don’t want a word, sir, from you.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그건 당신에게서 한마디도 필요치 않습니다, 선생."], ["I have got my principles, thank God.", "저에게는, 하느님 덕분에, 저의 원칙이 있습니다."], ["If an order comes to me, which is own brother to an order come from Bedlam, it don’t matter.", "만일 저에게, 정신병원에서 나온 명령과 친형제뻘인 명령이 온다 해도, 그건 상관없습니다."], ["So long as I get it from my master or mistress, as the case may be, I obey it.", "제가 그것을, 경우에 따라, 저의 주인이나 여주인에게서 받는 한, 저는 그것에 복종합니다."], ["I may have my own opinion, which is also, you will please to remember, the opinion of Mr.", "저에게는 저 나름의 견해가 있을지도 모릅니다, 그것은 또한, 기억해 주시기 바랍니다만, 브러프 씨의—그 위대하신 브러프 씨의 견해이기도 합지요!\" 하고 베터리지가, 목소리를 높이고, 엄숙하게 나를 향해 고개를 저으며 말했다."], ["Bruff—the Great Mr. Bruff!”", "\"그건 상관없습니다."], ["said Betteredge, raising his voice, and shaking his head at me solemnly.", "저는 그럼에도 저의 견해를 거둡니다. 저의 젊은 아가씨께서 말씀하십니다, '그것을 하라.'"], ["“It don’t matter; I withdraw my opinion, for all that.", "그리고 저는 말합니다, '아가씨, 그것은 이루어질 것입니다.'"], ["My young lady says, ‘Do it.’ And I say, ‘Miss, it shall be done.’ Here I am, with my book and my pencil—the latter not pointed so well as I could wish, but when Christians take leave of their senses, who is to expect that pencils will keep their points?", "여기 제가, 저의 수첩과 저의 연필을 들고 있습니다—뒤엣것은 제가 바랄 만큼 잘 뾰족하지는 않습니다만, 기독교인들이 제정신을 놓을 때, 연필이 그 뾰족함을 지키기를 누가 기대하겠습니까?"], ["Give me your orders, Mr. Jennings.", "저에게 당신의 명령을 주십시오, 제닝스 씨."], ["I’ll have them in writing, sir.", "저는 그것을 글로 받겠습니다, 선생."], ["I’m determined not to be behind ’em, or before ’em, by so much as a hair’s breadth.", "저는 그것들에 머리카락 한 올 너비만큼도 뒤처지거나 앞서지 않기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["I’m a blind agent—that’s what I am.", "저는 눈먼 대리인입니다—그것이 바로 저입니다."], ["A blind agent!”", "눈먼 대리인!\""], ["repeated Betteredge, with infinite relish of his own description of himself.", "하고 베터리지가, 제 자신에 대한 제 서술을 한없이 흡족해하며 되뇌었다."]], [["“I am very sorry,”", "\"참으로 안됐소,\""], ["I began, “that you and I don’t agree——”", "하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"당신과 내가 뜻을 같이하지 못해서——\""]], [["“Don’t bring me, into it!”", "\"저를 거기 끌어들이지 마십시오!\""], ["interposed Betteredge.", "하고 베터리지가 끼어들었다."], ["“This is not a matter of agreement, it’s a matter of obedience.", "\"이것은 뜻을 같이하고 말고의 문제가 아니라, 복종의 문제입니다."], ["Issue your directions, sir—issue your directions!”", "당신의 지시를 내리십시오, 선생—당신의 지시를 내리십시오!\""]], [["Mr. Blake made me a sign to take him at his word.", "블레이크 씨가 나에게 그의 말을 곧이곧대로 받으라고 몸짓했다."], ["I “issued my directions” as plainly and as gravely as I could.", "나는 되도록 평이하고 무겁게 \"나의 지시를 내렸다.\""]], [["“I wish certain parts of the house to be re-opened,”", "\"나는 그 집의 어떤 부분들이 다시 열리기를 바라오,\""], ["I said, “and to be furnished, exactly as they were furnished at this time last year.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"그리고 이때쯤 지난해에 갖추어졌던 그대로 정확히 갖추어지기를.\""]], [["Betteredge gave his imperfectly-pointed pencil a preliminary lick with his tongue.", "베터리지는 그의 불완전하게 뾰족한 연필을 그의 혀로 한 번 예비적으로 핥았다."], ["“Name the parts, Mr. Jennings!”", "\"그 부분들을 대시오, 제닝스 씨!\""], ["he said loftily.", "하고 그가 거만하게 말했다."]], [["“First, the inner hall, leading to the chief staircase.”", "\"먼저, 그 중앙 계단으로 이어지는 안쪽 현관홀.\""]], [["“‘First, the inner hall,’”", "\"'먼저, 그 안쪽 현관홀,'\""], ["Betteredge wrote.", "하고 베터리지가 적었다."], ["“Impossible to furnish that, sir, as it was furnished last year—to begin with.”", "\"우선, 그것을 지난해에 갖추어졌던 대로 갖추기란 불가능합니다, 선생.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"어째서요?\""]], [["“Because there was a stuffed buzzard, Mr. Jennings, in the hall last year.", "\"지난해에 그 현관홀에 박제된 말똥가리가 있었기 때문입지요, 제닝스 씨."], ["When the family left, the buzzard was put away with the other things.", "그 집안이 떠날 때, 그 말똥가리는 다른 물건들과 함께 치워졌습니다."], ["When the buzzard was put away—he burst.”", "그 말똥가리가 치워질 때—그것이 터졌습니다.\""]], [["“We will except the buzzard then.”", "\"그러면 그 말똥가리는 제외하겠소.\""]], [["Betteredge took a note of the exception.", "베터리지는 그 예외를 적어 두었다."], ["“‘The inner hall to be furnished again, as furnished last year.", "\"'그 안쪽 현관홀은 지난해에 갖추어진 대로 다시 갖출 것. 터진 말똥가리 하나만 제외함.'"], ["A burst buzzard alone excepted.’ Please to go on, Mr. Jennings.”", "계속하시지요, 제닝스 씨.\""]], [["“The carpet to be laid down on the stairs, as before.”", "\"그 계단에 융단을 전처럼 깔 것.\""]], [["“‘The carpet to be laid down on the stairs, as before.’ Sorry to disappoint you, sir.", "\"'그 계단에 융단을 전처럼 깔 것.' 실망시켜 드려 안됐습니다, 선생."], ["But that can’t be done either.”", "허나 그것도 할 수 없습니다.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"어째서 안 되오?\""]], [["“Because the man who laid that carpet down is dead, Mr. Jennings—and the like of him for reconciling together a carpet and a corner, is not to be found in all England, look where you may.”", "\"그 융단을 깐 그 사람이 죽었기 때문입지요, 제닝스 씨—그리고 융단과 모퉁이를 서로 잘 맞추는 데에 그만한 사람은, 어디를 보든, 온 잉글랜드에 없습니다.\""]], [["“Very well.", "\"좋소."], ["We must try the next best man in England.”", "우리는 잉글랜드에서 그다음으로 가장 뛰어난 사람을 시켜야겠소.\""]], [["Betteredge took another note; and I went on issuing my directions.", "베터리지는 또 하나를 적어 두었다. 그리고 나는 나의 지시를 계속 내렸다."]], [["“Miss Verinder’s sitting-room to be restored exactly to what it was last year.", "\"버린더 아가씨의 응접실은 지난해의 그대로 정확히 되돌릴 것."], ["Also, the corridor leading from the sitting-room to the first landing.", "또한, 그 응접실에서 첫째 층계참으로 이어지는 복도."], ["Also, the second corridor, leading from the second landing to the best bedrooms.", "또한, 둘째 층계참에서 그 가장 좋은 침실들로 이어지는 둘째 복도."], ["Also, the bedroom occupied last June by Mr. Franklin Blake.”", "또한, 지난 유월 프랭클린 블레이크 씨가 쓴 침실.\""]], [["Betteredge’s blunt pencil followed me conscientiously, word by word.", "베터리지의 뭉툭한 연필이 한 자 한 자 성실히 나를 뒤따랐다. \"계속하시지요, 선생,\""], ["“Go on, sir,” he said, with sardonic gravity.", "하고 그가 빈정대는 무거움으로 말했다."], ["“There’s a deal of writing left in the point of this pencil yet.”", "\"이 연필 촉에는 아직 쓸 것이 잔뜩 남아 있습니다.\""]], [["I told him that I had no more directions to give.", "나는 그에게 더는 내릴 지시가 없다고 말했다."], ["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["said Betteredge, “in that case, I have a point or two to put on my own behalf.”", "하고 베터리지가 말했다. \"그런 경우, 저에게는 저 나름으로 짚어 둘 점이 한둘 있습니다.\""], ["He opened the pocket-book at a new page, and gave the inexhaustible pencil another preliminary lick.", "그는 새 쪽에서 그 수첩을 펴고, 그 다함 없는 연필을 다시 한 번 예비적으로 핥았다."]], [["“I wish to know,”", "\"저는 알고 싶습니다,\""], ["he began, “whether I may, or may not, wash my hands——”", "하고 그가 입을 뗐다. \"제가, 어떤 책임들에서 손을 씻어도 되는지 아닌지를——\""]], [["“You may decidedly,”", "\"당신은 정녕 그래도 되오,\""], ["said Mr. Blake.", "하고 블레이크 씨가 말했다."], ["“I’ll ring for the waiter.”", "\"내가 급사를 부르러 종을 울리겠소.\""]], [["“——of certain responsibilities,”", "\"——어떤 책임들에서 말입니다,\""], ["pursued Betteredge, impenetrably declining to see anybody in the room but himself and me.", "하고 베터리지가, 그 방에 자기와 나 말고는 아무도 없는 척, 흔들림 없이 이어 갔다."], ["“As to Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, to begin with.", "\"우선, 버린더 아가씨의 응접실에 관해서요."], ["When we took up the carpet last year, Mr. Jennings, we found a surprising quantity of pins.", "우리가 지난해에 그 융단을 걷었을 때, 제닝스 씨, 우리는 놀라운 양의 핀을 발견했습니다."], ["Am I responsible for putting back the pins?”", "제가 그 핀을 도로 놓는 데에 책임이 있습니까?\""]], [["“Certainly not.”", "\"물론 아니오.\""]], [["Betteredge made a note of that concession, on the spot.", "베터리지는 그 자리에서 그 양보를 적어 두었다."]], [["“As to the first corridor next,”", "\"다음으로 그 첫째 복도에 관해서요,\""], ["he resumed.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다."], ["“When we moved the ornaments in that part, we moved a statue of a fat naked child—profanely described in the catalogue of the house as ‘Cupid, god of Love.’ He had two wings last year, in the fleshy part of his shoulders.", "\"우리가 그 부분의 장식물을 옮겼을 때, 우리는 살진 벌거벗은 아이의 상 하나를 옮겼습니다—그 집의 목록에는 불경하게도 '큐피드, 사랑의 신'이라 적혀 있었습지요. 그는 지난해에 그의 어깨의 살진 부분에 날개가 둘 있었습니다."], ["My eye being off him, for the moment, he lost one of them.", "제 눈이 잠시 그에게서 떨어진 사이, 그는 그중 하나를 잃었습니다."], ["Am I responsible for Cupid’s wing?”", "제가 큐피드의 날개에 책임이 있습니까?\""]], [["I made another concession, and Betteredge made another note.", "나는 또 하나의 양보를 했고, 베터리지는 또 하나를 적어 두었다."]], [["“As to the second corridor,”", "\"그 둘째 복도에 관해서는,\""], ["he went on.", "하고 그가 계속했다."], ["“There having been nothing in it, last year, but the doors of the rooms (to every one of which I can swear, if necessary), my mind is easy, I admit, respecting that part of the house only.", "\"지난해에 거기에 그 방들의 문 말고는 (그 하나하나에 대해, 필요하다면, 제가 맹세할 수 있습니다) 아무것도 없었던 터라, 저는, 인정하거니와, 그 집의 그 부분에 관해서만은 마음이 편합니다."], ["But, as to Mr. Franklin’s bedroom (if that is to be put back to what it was before), I want to know who is responsible for keeping it in a perpetual state of litter, no matter how often it may be set right—his trousers here, his towels there, and his French novels everywhere.", "허나, 프랭클린 나리의 침실에 관해서는 (만일 그것이 전의 그대로로 되돌려져야 한다면), 저는 알고 싶습니다, 그것이 아무리 자주 정돈되더라도, 그것을 영구히 어질러진 상태로 지키는 데에 누가 책임이 있는지를—그의 바지는 여기, 그의 수건은 저기, 그리고 그의 프랑스 소설은 어디에나 널려 있으니 말입니다."], ["I say, who is responsible for untidying the tidiness of Mr. Franklin’s room, him or me?”", "저는 묻습니다, 프랭클린 나리의 방의 정돈됨을 어지럽히는 데에 누가 책임이 있는지, 그입니까 저입니까?\""]], [["Mr. Blake declared that he would assume the whole responsibility with the greatest pleasure.", "블레이크 씨는 자기가 더없이 기쁜 마음으로 그 온 책임을 떠맡겠다고 선언했다."], ["Betteredge obstinately declined to listen to any solution of the difficulty, without first referring it to my sanction and approval. I accepted Mr.", "베터리지는, 먼저 그것을 나의 재가와 승인에 부치지 않고서는, 그 어려움의 어떤 해결도 들으려 고집스레 마다했다."], ["Blake’s proposal; and Betteredge made a last entry in the pocket-book to that effect.", "나는 블레이크 씨의 제안을 받아들였다. 그리고 베터리지는 그 취지로 그 수첩에 마지막 항목을 적어 넣었다."]], [["“Look in when you like, Mr. Jennings, beginning from tomorrow,”", "\"내일부터, 원하실 때 들르십시오, 제닝스 씨,\""], ["he said, getting on his legs.", "하고 그가 다리를 세워 일어서며 말했다."], ["“You will find me at work, with the necessary persons to assist me.", "\"당신은 제가, 저를 도울 필요한 사람들과 함께, 일하고 있는 것을 보실 겁니다."], ["I respectfully beg to thank you, sir, for overlooking the case of the stuffed buzzard, and the other case of the Cupid’s wing—as also for permitting me to wash my hands of all responsibility in respect of the pins on the carpet, and the litter in Mr. Franklin’s room.", "저는 삼가 당신께 감사드립니다, 선생, 그 박제된 말똥가리의 경우와, 그 큐피드의 날개의 다른 경우를 눈감아 주신 데에 대해—또한 그 융단의 핀과, 프랭클린 나리의 방의 어질러짐에 관한 온갖 책임에서 제가 손을 씻도록 허락해 주신 데에 대해서도."], ["Speaking as a servant, I am deeply indebted to you.", "하인으로서 말하자면, 저는 당신께 깊이 빚졌습니다."], ["Speaking as a man, I consider you to be a person whose head is full of maggots, and I take up my testimony against your experiment as a delusion and a snare.", "사람으로서 말하자면, 저는 당신을 머릿속이 구더기로 가득한 사람으로 여기며, 저는 당신의 실험이 하나의 미혹이자 덫이라는 저의 증언을 제기합니다."], ["Don’t be afraid, on that account, of my feelings as a man getting in the way of my duty as a servant!", "그렇다고 해서, 하인으로서의 저의 의무를 사람으로서의 저의 감정이 가로막을까 두려워 마십시오!"], ["You shall be obeyed.", "당신은 복종받으실 겁니다."], ["The maggots notwithstanding, sir, you shall be obeyed.", "그 구더기에도 불구하고, 선생, 당신은 복종받으실 겁니다."], ["If it ends in your setting the house on fire, Damme if I send for the engines, unless you ring the bell and order them first!”", "만일 그것이 당신이 그 집에 불을 지르는 것으로 끝난다면, 당신이 먼저 종을 울려 그것들을 부르지 않는 한, 제가 소방차를 부른다면 빌어먹을 놈입니다!\""]], [["With that farewell assurance, he made me a bow, and walked out of the room.", "그 작별의 다짐과 함께, 그는 나에게 고개 숙여 인사하고, 그 방을 걸어 나갔다."]], [["“Do you think we can depend on him?”", "\"우리가 그를 믿어도 될 것 같소?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Implicitly,”", "\"온전히,\""], ["answered Mr. Blake.", "하고 블레이크 씨가 대답했다."], ["“When we go to the house, we shall find nothing neglected, and nothing forgotten.”", "\"우리가 그 집에 가면, 우리는 아무것도 소홀히 되지 않고, 아무것도 잊히지 않은 것을 볼 것이오.\""]], [["June 19th.—Another protest against our contemplated proceedings!", "유월 19일.—우리가 궁리한 일에 대한 또 하나의 항변!"], ["From a lady this time.", "이번에는 한 부인에게서."]], [["The morning’s post brought me two letters.", "그날 아침 우편이 나에게 편지 두 통을 가져다주었다."], ["One from Miss Verinder, consenting, in the kindest manner, to the arrangement that I have proposed.", "하나는 버린더 아가씨에게서 온 것으로, 더없이 다정하게, 내가 제안한 그 방안에 동의하는 것이었다."], ["The other from the lady under whose care she is living—one Mrs. Merridew.", "다른 하나는 그녀가 그 보살핌 아래 살고 있는 그 부인—어느 메리듀 부인에게서 온 것이었다."]], [["Mrs. Merridew presents her compliments, and does not pretend to understand the subject on which I have been corresponding with Miss Verinder, in its scientific bearings.", "메리듀 부인은 인사를 전하며, 내가 버린더 아가씨와 편지를 주고받아 온 그 주제를, 그것의 과학적 측면에서 이해하는 척하지 않는다."], ["Viewed in its social bearings, however, she feels free to pronounce an opinion.", "허나, 그것의 사교적 측면에서 보면, 그녀는 마음 놓고 견해를 밝힌다."], ["I am probably, Mrs. Merridew thinks, not aware that Miss Verinder is barely nineteen years of age.", "짐작건대 나는, 메리듀 부인이 생각하기로, 버린더 아가씨가 겨우 열아홉 살임을 자각하지 못한다."], ["To allow a young lady, at her time of life, to be present (without a “chaperone”) in a house full of men among whom a medical experiment is being carried on, is an outrage on propriety which Mrs.", "젊은 아가씨를, 그 나이에, 의학 실험이 행해지고 있는 사내들로 가득한 집에 (\"샤프롱\" 없이) 자리하게 두는 것은, 메리듀 부인이 도저히 허락할 수 없는 예절에 대한 능욕이다."], ["Merridew cannot possibly permit. If the matter is allowed to proceed, she will feel it to be her duty—at a serious sacrifice of her own personal convenience—to accompany Miss Verinder to Yorkshire.", "만일 그 일이 진행되도록 허락된다면, 그녀는—제 자신의 개인적 편의의 심각한 희생을 무릅쓰고—버린더 아가씨를 요크셔로 동행하는 것을 자기의 도리로 느낄 것이다."], ["Under these circumstances, she ventures to request that I will kindly reconsider the subject; seeing that Miss Verinder declines to be guided by any opinion but mine.", "이런 상황에서, 그녀는 감히, 내가 부디 그 주제를 다시 헤아려 주기를 청한다. 버린더 아가씨가 나의 견해 말고는 어떤 견해에도 이끌리기를 마다하는 것을 보아서 말이다."], ["Her presence cannot possibly be necessary; and a word from me, to that effect, would relieve both Mrs.", "그녀의 자리함은 도저히 필요할 수 없다."], ["Merridew and myself of a very unpleasant responsibility.", "그리고 그런 취지의, 나로부터의 한마디가, 메리듀 부인과 나 자신을 아주 불쾌한 책임에서 덜어 줄 것이다."]], [["Translated from polite commonplace into plain English, the meaning of this is, as I take it, that Mrs. Merridew stands in mortal fear of the opinion of the world.", "정중한 상투어에서 쉬운 영어로 옮기면, 이것의 뜻은, 내가 그것을 새기기로는, 메리듀 부인이 세상의 견해를 죽도록 두려워한다는 것이다."], ["She has unfortunately appealed to the very last man in existence who has any reason to regard that opinion with respect.", "그녀는 안타깝게도, 그 견해를 존중으로 여길 아무 이유도 없는, 살아 있는 그야말로 마지막 사람에게 호소한 것이다."], ["I won’t disappoint Miss Verinder; and I won’t delay a reconciliation between two young people who love each other, and who have been parted too long already.", "나는 버린더 아가씨를 실망시키지 않겠다."], ["Translated from plain English into polite commonplace, this means that Mr.", "그리고 나는 서로 사랑하고, 이미 너무 오래 갈라져 있던 두 젊은이 사이의 화해를 미루지 않겠다."], ["Jennings presents his compliments to Mrs. Merridew, and regrets that he cannot feel justified in interfering any farther in the matter.", "쉬운 영어에서 정중한 상투어로 옮기면, 이것의 뜻은, 제닝스 씨가 메리듀 부인에게 인사를 전하며, 그가 그 일에 조금이라도 더 끼어드는 것이 정당하다고 느낄 수 없어 유감이라는 것이다."]], [["Mr. Blake’s report of himself, this morning, was the same as before.", "블레이크 씨의 오늘 아침 제 자신에 대한 보고는 이전과 같았다."], ["We determined not to disturb Betteredge by overlooking him at the house today.", "우리는 오늘 그 집에서 베터리지를 살펴봄으로써 그를 방해하지 않기로 정했다."], ["Tomorrow will be time enough for our first visit of inspection.", "내일이면 우리의 첫 점검 방문에 넉넉히 이를 것이다."]], [["June 20th.—Mr. Blake is beginning to feel his continued restlessness at night.", "유월 20일.—블레이크 씨가 밤의 그의 계속되는 안절부절못함을 느끼기 시작하고 있다."], ["The sooner the rooms are refurnished, now, the better.", "이제, 그 방들이 다시 갖추어질수록, 그만큼 좋다."]], [["On our way to the house, this morning, he consulted me, with some nervous impatience and irresolution, about a letter (forwarded to him from London) which he had received from Sergeant Cuff.", "오늘 아침, 그 집으로 가는 길에, 그는, 얼마쯤의 신경질적인 조바심과 우유부단함을 안고, (런던에서 그에게 부쳐진) 그가 커프 부장에게서 받은 편지에 대해 나와 상의했다."]], [["The Sergeant writes from Ireland.", "그 부장은 아일랜드에서 쓴다."], ["He acknowledges the receipt (through his housekeeper) of a card and message which Mr. Blake left at his residence near Dorking, and announces his return to England as likely to take place in a week or less.", "그는 블레이크 씨가 도킹 근처의 그의 거처에 남긴 명함과 전갈의 (그의 가정부를 통한) 수령을 인정하며, 그의 잉글랜드로의 귀환이 일주일이나 그 이내에 이루어질 공산이 크다고 알린다."], ["In the meantime, he requests to be favoured with Mr. Blake’s reasons for wishing to speak to him (as stated in the message) on the subject of the Moonstone.", "그동안, 그는 블레이크 씨가 (그 전갈에 진술된 대로) 그 월장석이라는 주제로 그와 이야기하고 싶어 하는 이유를 알려 주기를 청한다."], ["If Mr. Blake can convict him of having made any serious mistake, in the course of his last year’s inquiry concerning the Diamond, he will consider it a duty (after the liberal manner in which he was treated by the late Lady Verinder) to place himself at that gentleman’s disposal.", "만일 블레이크 씨가, 그의 지난해의 그 다이아몬드에 관한 조사 과정에서, 그가 무슨 심각한 잘못을 저질렀음을 입증할 수 있다면, 그는 (고인이 된 레이디 버린더에게 후하게 대접받은 뒤로) 그 신사가 마음대로 부리도록 자신을 내놓는 것을 도리로 여길 것이다."], ["If not, he begs permission to remain in his retirement, surrounded by the peaceful horticultural attractions of a country life.", "그렇지 않다면, 그는 시골 삶의 평화로운 원예의 매력에 둘러싸여, 그의 은거에 남아 있기를 청한다."]], [["After reading the letter, I had no hesitation in advising Mr. Blake to inform Sergeant Cuff, in reply, of all that had happened since the inquiry was suspended last year, and to leave him to draw his own conclusions from the plain facts.", "그 편지를 읽고서, 나는 블레이크 씨에게, 답으로, 지난해에 그 조사가 중단된 이래 일어난 온갖 것을 커프 부장에게 알리고, 그로 하여금 그 명백한 사실에서 스스로 결론을 이끌어 내도록 두라고 조언하는 데에 아무 망설임이 없었다."]], [["On second thoughts I also suggested inviting the Sergeant to be present at the experiment, in the event of his returning to England in time to join us.", "곰곰이 다시 생각한 끝에 나는 또한, 그 부장이 우리와 합류할 만큼 제때 잉글랜드로 돌아올 경우에, 그를 그 실험에 자리하도록 청할 것을 제안했다. 그는, 어쨌든, 두어 값진 증인이 될 터였다."], ["He would be a valuable witness to have, in any case; and, if I proved to be wrong in believing the Diamond to be hidden in Mr. Blake’s room, his advice might be of great importance, at a future stage of the proceedings over which I could exercise no control.", "그리고, 만일 그 다이아몬드가 블레이크 씨의 방에 감춰져 있다고 믿는 데에서 내가 틀린 것으로 드러난다면, 내가 아무런 통제도 발휘할 수 없는, 그 일의 나중 단계에서 그의 조언이 크게 중요할지도 몰랐다."], ["This last consideration appeared to decide Mr.", "이 마지막 헤아림이 블레이크 씨를 결심하게 하는 듯했다."], ["Blake. He promised to follow my advice.", "그는 나의 조언을 따르겠다고 약속했다."]], [["The sound of the hammer informed us that the work of refurnishing was in full progress, as we entered the drive that led to the house.", "망치 소리가, 우리가 그 집으로 이어지는 그 진입로에 들어서면서, 다시 갖추는 일이 한창임을 우리에게 알렸다."]], [["Betteredge, attired for the occasion in a fisherman’s red cap, and an apron of green baize, met us in the outer hall.", "이 날을 위해 어부의 붉은 모자와 초록 나사 앞치마 차림을 한 베터리지가, 그 바깥 현관홀에서 우리를 맞았다."], ["The moment he saw me, he pulled out the pocket-book and pencil, and obstinately insisted on taking notes of everything that I said to him.", "그가 나를 본 순간, 그는 그 수첩과 연필을 꺼내어, 내가 그에게 말하는 온갖 것을 적어 두겠다고 고집스레 우겼다."], ["Look where we might, we found, as Mr. Blake had foretold, that the work was advancing as rapidly and as intelligently as it was possible to desire.", "어디를 보든, 우리는, 블레이크 씨가 예언한 대로, 그 일이 바랄 수 있는 만큼 빠르고 지혜롭게 진척되고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["But there was still much to be done in the inner hall, and in Miss Verinder’s room.", "허나 그 안쪽 현관홀과, 버린더 아가씨의 방에는 아직 할 일이 많았다."], ["It seemed doubtful whether the house would be ready for us before the end of the week.", "그 집이 이번 주말 전에 우리를 위해 채비될지는 미심쩍은 듯했다."]], [["Having congratulated Betteredge on the progress that he had made (he persisted in taking notes every time I opened my lips; declining, at the same time, to pay the slightest attention to anything said by Mr. Blake); and having promised to return for a second visit of inspection in a day or two, we prepared to leave the house, going out by the back way.", "베터리지에게 그가 이룬 진척에 대해 축하하고 (그는 내가 입을 열 때마다 적어 두기를 고집했으며, 동시에, 블레이크 씨가 하는 말에는 조금의 주의도 기울이기를 마다했다), 하루나 이틀 안에 둘째 점검 방문을 하러 돌아오겠다고 약속하고서, 우리는 뒷길로 나가, 그 집을 떠날 채비를 했다."], ["Before we were clear of the passages downstairs, I was stopped by Betteredge, just as I was passing the door which led into his own room.", "우리가 아래층 통로를 미처 벗어나기 전에, 나는 막 그 자신의 방으로 이어지는 문을 지나가는데, 베터리지에게 붙들렸다."]], [["“Could I say two words to you in private?”", "\"당신께 사사로이 두어 마디 드려도 될까요?\""], ["he asked, in a mysterious whisper.", "하고 그가, 알쏭달쏭한 속삭임으로 물었다."]], [["I consented of course. Mr.", "나는 물론 그러자고 했다."], ["Blake walked on to wait for me in the garden, while I accompanied Betteredge into his room.", "블레이크 씨는 정원에서 나를 기다리려 걸어갔고, 나는 베터리지를 따라 그의 방으로 들어갔다."], ["I fully anticipated a demand for certain new concessions, following the precedent already established in the cases of the stuffed buzzard, and the Cupid’s wing.", "나는, 그 박제된 말똥가리와 큐피드의 날개의 경우에 이미 세워진 선례를 좇아, 어떤 새로운 양보에 대한 요구를 온전히 예상했다."], ["To my great surprise, Betteredge laid his hand confidentially on my arm, and put this extraordinary question to me:", "나의 큰 놀라움으로, 베터리지는 은근히 내 팔에 손을 얹고, 이 유별난 물음을 나에게 던졌다."]], [["“Mr. Jennings, do you happen to be acquainted with Robinson Crusoe?”", "\"제닝스 씨, 당신은 혹시 「로빈슨 크루소」를 아십니까?\""]], [["I answered that I had read Robinson Crusoe when I was a child.", "나는 어렸을 때 「로빈슨 크루소」를 읽었다고 대답했다."]], [["“Not since then?”", "\"그 뒤로는 안 읽으셨고요?\""], ["inquired Betteredge.", "하고 베터리지가 물었다."]], [["“Not since then.”", "\"그 뒤로는 안 읽었소.\""]], [["He fell back a few steps, and looked at me with an expression of compassionate curiosity, tempered by superstitious awe.", "그는 몇 걸음 물러나, 미신적인 경외로 누그러진, 가엾이 여기는 호기심의 표정으로 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“He has not read Robinson Crusoe since he was a child,”", "\"그는 어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽었어,\""], ["said Betteredge, speaking to himself—not to me.", "하고 베터리지가, 나에게가 아니라 자기 자신에게 말했다."], ["“Let’s try how Robinson Crusoe strikes him now!”", "\"「로빈슨 크루소」가 이제 그에게 어떻게 와닿는지 시험해 보자!\""]], [["He unlocked a cupboard in a corner, and produced a dirty and dog’s-eared book, which exhaled a strong odour of stale tobacco as he turned over the leaves.", "그는 한 모퉁이의 찬장을 열고, 더럽고 귀퉁이가 접힌 책 한 권을 꺼냈는데, 그것은 그가 책장을 넘길 때 케케묵은 담배의 강한 냄새를 뿜었다."], ["Having found a passage of which he was apparently in search, he requested me to join him in the corner; still mysteriously confidential, and still speaking under his breath.", "겉보기에 그가 찾던 한 대목을 찾고서, 그는 나에게 그 모퉁이에서 그와 합류하기를 청했다. 여전히 알쏭달쏭하게 은근하고, 여전히 숨죽여 말하며."]], [["“In respect to this hocus-pocus of yours, sir, with the laudanum and Mr.", "\"당신의, 아편팅크와 프랭클린 블레이크 나리에 관한 이 요술에 관해서 말입니다, 선생,\""], ["Franklin Blake,” he began.", "하고 그가 입을 뗐다."], ["“While the workpeople are in the house, my duty as a servant gets the better of my feelings as a man.", "\"일꾼들이 그 집에 있는 동안은, 하인으로서의 저의 의무가 사람으로서의 저의 감정을 이깁니다."], ["When the workpeople are gone, my feelings as a man get the better of my duty as a servant.", "일꾼들이 가고 나면, 사람으로서의 저의 감정이 하인으로서의 저의 의무를 이깁지요."], ["Very good.", "좋습니다."], ["Last night, Mr. Jennings, it was borne in powerfully on my mind that this new medical enterprise of yours would end badly.", "지난밤에, 제닝스 씨, 이 당신의 새로운 의학적 사업이 나쁘게 끝나리라는 것이 저의 마음에 세차게 밀려들었습니다."], ["If I had yielded to that secret Dictate, I should have put all the furniture away again with my own hand, and have warned the workmen off the premises when they came the next morning.”", "만일 제가 그 은밀한 명령에 굴복했다면, 저는 제 손으로 온갖 세간을 다시 치워 버리고, 이튿날 아침 일꾼들이 왔을 때 그들을 그 집 밖으로 내쫓았을 것입니다.\""]], [["“I am glad to find, from what I have seen upstairs,”", "\"내가 위층에서 본 것으로 보아,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said, “that you resisted the secret Dictate.”", "\"당신이 그 은밀한 명령에 맞선 것을 알게 되어 기쁘오.\""]], [["“Resisted isn’t the word,”", "\"맞섰다는 것은 옳은 말이 아닙니다,\""], ["answered Betteredge.", "하고 베터리지가 대답했다."], ["“Wrostled is the word.", "\"씨름했다가 옳은 말입지요."], ["I wrostled, sir, between the silent orders in my bosom pulling me one way, and the written orders in my pocket-book pushing me the other, until (saving your presence) I was in a cold sweat.", "저는 씨름했습니다, 선생, 제 가슴속에서 저를 한쪽으로 당기는 그 말 없는 명령과, 제 수첩 속에서 저를 다른 쪽으로 미는 그 글로 된 명령 사이에서, (외람되오나) 제가 식은땀이 날 때까지요."], ["In that dreadful perturbation of mind and laxity of body, to what remedy did I apply?", "그 끔찍한 마음의 소란과 몸의 늘어짐 속에서, 저는 무슨 약에 기댔을까요?"], ["To the remedy, sir, which has never failed me yet for the last thirty years and more—to This Book!”", "지난 삼십 년 넘게 여태 저를 저버린 적이 없는 그 약에요, 선생—이 책에요!\""]], [["He hit the book a sounding blow with his open hand, and struck out of it a stronger smell of stale tobacco than ever.", "그는 그 책을 편 손으로 소리 나게 한 대 치더니, 그것에서 여느 때보다 더 강한 케케묵은 담배 냄새를 쳐 냈다."]], [["“What did I find here,”", "\"제가 여기서 무엇을 찾았을까요,\""], ["pursued Betteredge, “at the first page I opened?", "하고 베터리지가 이어 갔다. \"제가 편 첫 쪽에서?"], ["This awful bit, sir, page one hundred and seventy-eight, as follows:—‘Upon these, and many like Reflections, I afterwards made it a certain rule with me, That whenever I found those secret Hints or Pressings of my Mind, to doing, or not doing any Thing that presented; or to going this Way, or that Way, I never failed to obey the secret Dictate.’ As I live by bread, Mr.", "이 무시무시한 대목입니다, 선생, 백일흔여덟 쪽에, 이렇게—'이러한, 그리고 이와 비슷한 숱한 성찰에 따라, 나는 그 뒤로 이것을 나의 확고한 규칙으로 삼았다, 곧 내가 눈앞에 나타난 어떤 일을 하거나 안 하도록, 또는 이 길로 가거나 저 길로 가도록 하는 그 은밀한 암시나 마음의 재촉을 느낄 때는 언제든, 나는 어김없이 그 은밀한 명령에 복종했다.'"], ["Jennings, those were the first words that met my eye, exactly at the time when I myself was setting the secret Dictate at defiance!", "이 몸이 빵을 먹고 사는 것이 진실이듯, 제닝스 씨, 그것이 바로 제가 그 은밀한 명령을 거스르던 그때에 정확히, 제 눈에 든 첫마디였습니다!"], ["You don’t see anything at all out of the common in that, do you, sir?”", "당신은 거기서 조금이라도 예사롭지 않은 무언가가 안 보이시나요, 선생?\""]], [["“I see a coincidence—nothing more.”", "\"나는 우연의 일치가 보이오—그 이상은 아무것도.\""]], [["“You don’t feel at all shaken, Mr. Jennings, in respect to this medical enterprise of yours?", "\"당신은 이 당신의 의학적 사업에 관해, 조금도, 제닝스 씨, 흔들리지 않으시나요?\""]], [["“Not the least in the world.”", "\"세상없어도 조금도.\""]], [["Betteredge stared hard at me, in dead silence.", "베터리지는 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에, 나를 뚫어지게 노려보았다. 그는 아주 신중하게 그 책을 덮었다."], ["He closed the book with great deliberation; he locked it up again in the cupboard with extraordinary care; he wheeled round, and stared hard at me once more.", "그는 유별난 조심으로 그것을 다시 찬장에 넣고 잠갔다. 그는 홱 돌아서, 다시 한 번 나를 뚫어지게 노려보았다."], ["Then he spoke.", "그러고는 그가 말했다."]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생,\""], ["he said gravely, “there are great allowances to be made for a man who has not read Robinson Crusoe since he was a child.", "하고 그가 무겁게 말했다. \"어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽은 사람에게는 크게 헤아려 줄 것이 있지요."], ["I wish you good morning.”", "저는 당신께 안녕을 빕니다.\""]], [["He opened his door with a low bow, and left me at liberty to find my own way into the garden.", "그는 나직이 고개 숙여 인사하며 그의 문을 열고, 내가 정원으로 가는 나의 길을 스스로 찾도록 놓아주었다."], ["I met Mr. Blake returning to the house.", "나는 그 집으로 돌아오는 블레이크 씨와 마주쳤다."]], [["“You needn’t tell me what has happened,”", "\"무슨 일이 있었는지 나에게 말할 것 없소,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“Betteredge has played his last card: he has made another prophetic discovery in Robinson Crusoe.", "\"베터리지가 그의 마지막 패를 내밀었소. 그는 「로빈슨 크루소」에서 또 하나의 예언적 발견을 한 것이오."], ["Have you humoured his favourite delusion?", "당신은 그가 아끼는 그 미혹을 받아 주었소? 아니라고?"], ["No? You have let him see that you don’t believe in Robinson Crusoe?", "당신은 그로 하여금 당신이 「로빈슨 크루소」를 믿지 않음을 알게 했소?"], ["Mr. Jennings!", "제닝스 씨!"], ["you have fallen to the lowest possible place in Betteredge’s estimation.", "당신은 베터리지의 평가에서 있을 수 있는 가장 낮은 자리로 떨어졌소."], ["Say what you like, and do what you like, for the future.", "앞으로는, 원하는 대로 말하고, 원하는 대로 하시오."], ["You will find that he won’t waste another word on you now.”", "당신은 그가 이제 당신에게 한마디도 더 허비하지 않으리라는 것을 알게 될 것이오.\""]], [["June 21st.—A short entry must suffice in my journal today.", "유월 21일.—오늘은 나의 일기에 짧은 항목으로 넉넉해야겠다."]], [["Mr. Blake has had the worst night that he has passed yet.", "블레이크 씨는 여태 지낸 중 가장 나쁜 밤을 지냈다."], ["I have been obliged, greatly against my will, to prescribe for him.", "나는, 몹시 내키지 않았으나, 그에게 약을 처방하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Men of his sensitive organisation are fortunately quick in feeling the effect of remedial measures.", "그의 예민한 체질의 사람들은 다행히 약의 효과를 빨리 느낀다."], ["Otherwise, I should be inclined to fear that he will be totally unfit for the experiment when the time comes to try it.", "그렇지 않았다면, 나는 그것을 시도할 때가 왔을 때 그가 그 실험에 온전히 부적합할까 두려워하는 쪽으로 기울었을 것이다."]], [["As for myself, after some little remission of my pains for the last two days I had an attack this morning, of which I shall say nothing but that it has decided me to return to the opium.", "나 자신으로 말하자면, 지난 이틀 동안 나의 통증이 얼마쯤 뜸해진 뒤, 나는 오늘 아침 발작이 있었는데, 그것에 대해서는, 그것이 나로 하여금 그 아편으로 돌아가기로 정하게 했다는 것 말고는 아무 말도 하지 않겠다."], ["I shall close this book, and take my full dose—five hundred drops.", "나는 이 책을 덮고, 나의 온전한 양—오백 방울을 취하겠다."]], [["June 22nd.—Our prospects look better today. Mr.", "유월 22일.—우리의 전망이 오늘 더 나아 보인다."], ["Blake’s nervous suffering is greatly allayed.", "블레이크 씨의 신경의 괴로움이 크게 가라앉았다."], ["He slept a little last night.", "그는 지난밤 조금 잤다."], ["My night, thanks to the opium, was the night of a man who is stunned.", "나의 밤은, 그 아편 덕분에, 얼이 빠진 사람의 밤이었다."], ["I can’t say that I woke this morning; the fitter expression would be, that I recovered my senses.", "나는 오늘 아침 깨어났다고 말할 수 없다. 더 걸맞은 표현은, 내가 나의 지각을 되찾았다는 것이겠다."]], [["We drove to the house to see if the refurnishing was done.", "우리는 그 다시 갖추기가 끝났는지 보러 그 집으로 마차를 몰았다."], ["It will be completed tomorrow—Saturday.", "그것은 내일—토요일이면 마쳐질 것이다."], ["As Mr. Blake foretold, Betteredge raised no further obstacles.", "블레이크 씨가 예언한 대로, 베터리지는 더는 아무 장애도 세우지 않았다."], ["From first to last, he was ominously polite, and ominously silent.", "처음부터 끝까지, 그는 불길하게 정중했고, 불길하게 말이 없었다."]], [["My medical enterprise (as Betteredge calls it) must now, inevitably, be delayed until Monday next.", "나의 의학적 사업은 (베터리지가 그것을 그리 부른다) 이제, 어쩔 수 없이, 다음 월요일까지 미뤄져야 한다."], ["Tomorrow evening the workmen will be late in the house.", "내일 저녁 일꾼들이 그 집에서 늦게까지 있을 것이다."], ["On the next day, the established Sunday tyranny which is one of the institutions of this free country, so times the trains as to make it impossible to ask anybody to travel to us from London.", "그 이튿날은, 이 자유로운 나라의 제도의 하나인 그 굳어진 일요일의 폭정이, 기차 시간을 어찌나 잡아 놓았던지 누구에게든 런던에서 우리에게로 여행해 달라고 청하기가 불가능하다."], ["Until Monday comes, there is nothing to be done but to watch Mr. Blake carefully, and to keep him, if possible, in the same state in which I find him today.", "월요일이 올 때까지, 블레이크 씨를 조심스레 지켜보고, 가능하다면, 그를 오늘 내가 그를 보는 것과 같은 상태로 지키는 것 말고는 할 일이 없다."]], [["In the meanwhile, I have prevailed on him to write to Mr. Bruff, making a point of it that he shall be present as one of the witnesses.", "그동안, 나는 그를 설득하여, 브러프 씨에게, 그가 증인 가운데 하나로 자리하는 것을 요점으로 삼아 편지하게 했다."], ["I especially choose the lawyer, because he is strongly prejudiced against us.", "나는 그 변호사를 각별히 고르는데, 그가 우리에게 강하게 편견을 지녔기 때문이다."], ["If we convince him, we place our victory beyond the possibility of dispute.", "만일 우리가 그를 납득시킨다면, 우리는 우리의 승리를 다툼의 여지 너머에 두는 것이다."]], [["Mr. Blake has also written to Sergeant Cuff; and I have sent a line to Miss Verinder.", "블레이크 씨는 커프 부장에게도 편지했다."], ["With these, and with old Betteredge (who is really a person of importance in the family) we shall have witnesses enough for the purpose—without including Mrs.", "그리고 나는 버린더 아가씨에게 몇 줄을 보냈다."], ["Merridew, if Mrs. Merridew persists in sacrificing herself to the opinion of the world.", "이들과, 그리고 늙은 베터리지(그는 정말로 그 집안에서 중요한 사람이다)와 함께, 우리는 그 목적에 넉넉한 증인을 갖게 될 것이다—메리듀 부인이, 만일 메리듀 부인이 세상의 견해에 자신을 희생하기를 고집한다면, 그녀를 넣지 않고서라도."]], [["June 23rd.—The vengeance of the opium overtook me again last night.", "유월 23일.—그 아편의 앙갚음이 지난밤 다시 나를 덮쳤다. 아무래도 좋다."], ["No matter; I must go on with it now till Monday is past and gone.", "나는 월요일이 지나 갈 때까지 이제 그것을 계속해야 한다."]], [["Mr. Blake is not so well again today.", "블레이크 씨가 오늘 다시 그리 좋지 않다."], ["At two this morning, he confesses that he opened the drawer in which his cigars are put away.", "오늘 아침 두 시에, 그는 자기가 여송연을 넣어 둔 서랍을 열었음을 털어놓는다."], ["He only succeeded in locking it up again by a violent effort.", "그는 격렬한 안간힘으로 겨우 그것을 다시 잠갔다."], ["His next proceeding, in case of temptation, was to throw the key out of window.", "유혹에 대비한 그의 다음 조치는, 그 열쇠를 창밖으로 던지는 것이었다."], ["The waiter brought it in this morning, discovered at the bottom of an empty cistern—such is Fate!", "급사가 오늘 아침 그것을, 빈 물통 바닥에서 찾아내어 들고 왔다—운명이란 이런 것이다!"], ["I have taken possession of the key until Tuesday next.", "나는 다음 화요일까지 그 열쇠를 맡았다."]], [["June 24th.—Mr. Blake and I took a long drive in an open carriage.", "유월 24일.—블레이크 씨와 나는 뚜껑 없는 마차로 오래 마차를 탔다."], ["We both felt beneficially the blessed influence of the soft summer air.", "우리는 둘 다 그 부드러운 여름 공기의 축복 어린 영향을 이롭게 느꼈다."], ["I dined with him at the hotel.", "나는 그 호텔에서 그와 저녁을 들었다."], ["To my great relief—for I found him in an over-wrought, over-excited state this morning—he had two hours’ sound sleep on the sofa after dinner.", "나의 큰 안도로—나는 오늘 아침 그가 과로하고 과도하게 흥분한 상태에 있음을 보았으므로—그는 저녁 뒤에 소파에서 두 시간 곤히 잤다."], ["If he has another bad night, now—I am not afraid of the consequences.", "만일 그가 이제 또 한 번 나쁜 밤을 지낸다면—나는 그 결과가 두렵지 않다."]], [["June 25th, Monday.—The day of the experiment!", "유월 25일, 월요일.—그 실험의 날!"], ["It is five o’clock in the afternoon.", "오후 다섯 시다."], ["We have just arrived at the house.", "우리는 방금 그 집에 다다랐다."]], [["The first and foremost question, is the question of Mr. Blake’s health.", "첫째이자 무엇보다도 앞선 물음은, 블레이크 씨의 건강의 물음이다."]], [["So far as it is possible for me to judge, he promises (physically speaking) to be quite as susceptible to the action of the opium tonight as he was at this time last year.", "내가 판단할 수 있는 한으로는, 그는 (몸으로 말하여) 오늘 밤 이때쯤 지난해에 그랬던 만큼이나 아편의 작용에 예민하리라 약속한다."], ["He is, this afternoon, in a state of nervous sensitiveness which just stops short of nervous irritation.", "그는, 오늘 오후, 신경의 짜증에 딱 못 미친 신경의 예민함의 상태에 있다. 그는 어렵잖게 낯빛이 변한다."], ["He changes colour readily; his hand is not quite steady; and he starts at chance noises, and at unexpected appearances of persons and things.", "그의 손은 아주 흔들림 없지는 않다. 그리고 그는 우연한 소리에, 그리고 사람과 물건의 뜻밖의 나타남에 소스라친다."]], [["These results have all been produced by deprivation of sleep, which is in its turn the nervous consequence of a sudden cessation in the habit of smoking, after that habit has been carried to an extreme.", "이 결과들은 죄 잠의 결핍으로 자아내진 것인데, 그것은 다시, 그 버릇이 극단으로 밀린 뒤의, 담배 피우는 버릇의 별안간의 멈춤의 신경적 결과다."], ["Here are the same causes at work again, which operated last year; and here are, apparently, the same effects.", "여기 지난해에 작용한 것과 같은 원인들이 다시 작용하고 있다. 그리고 여기, 겉보기에, 같은 결과들이 있다."], ["Will the parallel still hold good, when the final test has been tried?", "그 최종의 시험이 시도되었을 때, 그 나란함이 여전히 들어맞을 것인가?"], ["The events of the night must decide.", "그 밤의 사건들이 결정해야 한다."]], [["While I write these lines, Mr. Blake is amusing himself at the billiard table in the inner hall, practising different strokes in the game, as he was accustomed to practise them when he was a guest in this house in June last.", "내가 이 글줄들을 쓰는 동안, 블레이크 씨는 그 안쪽 현관홀의 당구대에서, 지난 유월 그가 이 집의 손님이었을 때 그것들을 연습하던 대로, 그 놀이의 갖가지 타법을 연습하며, 스스로를 즐기고 있다."], ["I have brought my journal here, partly with a view to occupying the idle hours which I am sure to have on my hands between this and tomorrow morning; partly in the hope that something may happen which it may be worth my while to place on record at the time.", "나는 나의 일기를 여기 가져왔는데, 얼마쯤은 내가 지금부터 내일 아침 사이에 틀림없이 손에 갖게 될 그 한가한 시간을 채울 셈에서, 얼마쯤은 그때에 내가 기록해 둘 값어치가 있을 무언가가 일어날지도 모른다는 희망에서다."]], [["Have I omitted anything, thus far?", "여기까지 내가 무언가 빠뜨렸는가?"], ["A glance at yesterday’s entry shows me that I have forgotten to note the arrival of the morning’s post.", "어제의 항목을 흘긋 보니, 내가 아침 우편의 다다름을 적어 두는 것을 잊었음을 알겠다."], ["Let me set this right before I close these leaves for the present, and join Mr. Blake.", "지금으로서는 이 낱장들을 덮고 블레이크 씨와 합류하기 전에, 이것을 바로잡아 두자."]], [["I received a few lines then, yesterday, from Miss Verinder.", "그러니, 나는 어제, 버린더 아가씨에게서 몇 줄을 받았다."], ["She has arranged to travel by the afternoon train, as I recommended.", "그녀는, 내가 권한 대로, 오후 기차로 여행하기로 했다."], ["Mrs. Merridew has insisted on accompanying her.", "메리듀 부인이 그녀를 동행하겠다고 우겼다."], ["The note hints that the old lady’s generally excellent temper is a little ruffled, and requests all due indulgence for her, in consideration of her age and her habits.", "그 쪽지는, 그 노부인의 대체로 훌륭한 성미가 조금 뒤틀렸음을 넌지시 일러 주며, 그녀의 나이와 그녀의 버릇을 헤아려, 그녀에게 온갖 마땅한 너그러움을 베풀어 주기를 청한다."], ["I will endeavour, in my relations with Mrs. Merridew, to emulate the moderation which Betteredge displays in his relations with me.", "나는, 메리듀 부인과의 나의 관계에서, 베터리지가 나와의 그의 관계에서 내보이는 그 절제를 본받으려 애쓰겠다."], ["He received us today, portentously arrayed in his best black suit, and his stiffest white cravat.", "그는 오늘, 불길하게도 그의 가장 좋은 검은 정장과, 그의 가장 뻣뻣한 흰 넥타이 차림으로 우리를 맞았다."], ["Whenever he looks my way, he remembers that I have not read Robinson Crusoe since I was a child, and he respectfully pities me.", "그는 내 쪽을 볼 때마다, 내가 어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽었음을 떠올리고, 삼가 나를 가엾이 여긴다."]], [["Yesterday, also, Mr. Blake had the lawyer’s answer.", "어제, 또한, 블레이크 씨는 그 변호사의 답을 받았다."], ["Mr. Bruff accepts the invitation—under protest.", "브러프 씨는 그 초대를—항변과 함께—받아들인다."], ["It is, he thinks, clearly necessary that a gentleman possessed of the average allowance of common sense, should accompany Miss Verinder to the scene of, what we will venture to call, the proposed exhibition.", "그가 생각하기로, 예사로운 분별의 평균치를 지닌 신사가, 우리가 감히 그리 부르자면, 그 제안된 구경거리의 현장으로 버린더 아가씨를 동행하는 것이 명백히 필요하다."], ["For want of a better escort, Mr. Bruff himself will be that gentleman.—So here is poor Miss Verinder provided with two “chaperones.”", "더 나은 호위가 없어, 브러프 씨 자신이 그 신사가 될 것이다.—그리하여 여기 가엾은 버린더 아가씨에게 두 \"샤프롱\"이 갖추어졌다."], ["It is a relief to think that the opinion of the world must surely be satisfied with this!", "세상의 견해가 이것으로는 틀림없이 만족되리라 생각하니 안도가 된다!"]], [["Nothing has been heard of Sergeant Cuff.", "커프 부장에게서는 아무 소식도 들리지 않았다."], ["He is no doubt still in Ireland.", "그는 두말할 것 없이 아직 아일랜드에 있다."], ["We must not expect to see him tonight.", "우리는 오늘 밤 그를 볼 것을 기대하지 말아야 한다."]], [["Betteredge has just come in, to say that Mr. Blake has asked for me.", "베터리지가 방금 들어와, 블레이크 씨가 나를 찾는다고 말했다."], ["I must lay down my pen for the present.", "나는 지금으로서는 나의 펜을 내려놓아야 한다."]], [["Seven o’clock.—We have been all over the refurnished rooms and staircases again; and we have had a pleasant stroll in the shrubbery, which was Mr.", "일곱 시.—우리는 다시 그 새로 갖춘 방들과 계단들을 죄 둘러보았다. 그리고 우리는, 블레이크 씨가 지난번 여기 있었을 때 그가 즐기던 산책이었던 그 관목숲에서 기분 좋은 산책을 했다."], ["Blake’s favourite walk when he was here last. In this way, I hope to revive the old impressions of places and things as vividly as possible in his mind.", "이런 식으로, 나는 그의 마음속에 장소와 물건들의 옛 인상을 되도록 생생하게 되살리기를 바란다."]], [["We are now going to dine, exactly at the hour at which the birthday dinner was given last year.", "우리는 이제, 지난해에 그 생일 만찬이 열린 바로 그 시각에, 저녁을 들려 한다."], ["My object, of course, is a purely medical one in this case.", "나의 목적은, 물론, 이 경우에는 순전히 의학적인 것이다."], ["The laudanum must find the process of digestion, as nearly as may be, where the laudanum found it last year.", "그 아편팅크는, 되도록 가까이, 그것이 지난해에 소화 과정을 찾았던 그 지점에서 소화 과정을 찾아야 한다."]], [["At a reasonable time after dinner I propose to lead the conversation back again—as inartificially as I can—to the subject of the Diamond, and of the Indian conspiracy to steal it.", "저녁 뒤의 알맞은 때에 나는 그 대화를 다시—되도록 꾸밈없이—그 다이아몬드라는 주제로, 그리고 그것을 훔치려는 인도인의 음모라는 주제로 도로 이끌 작정이다."], ["When I have filled his mind with these topics, I shall have done all that it is in my power to do, before the time comes for giving him the second dose.", "내가 그의 마음을 이 화제들로 채우고 나면, 나는 그에게 그 둘째 번 약을 주는 때가 오기 전에 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 온갖 것을 다한 셈일 것이다."]], [["Half-past eight.—I have only this moment found an opportunity of attending to the most important duty of all; the duty of looking in the family medicine chest, for the laudanum which Mr.", "여덟 시 반.—나는 바로 이 순간에야 가장 중요한 그 온갖 의무에 마음 쓸 기회를 찾았다."], ["Candy used last year.", "곧, 지난해에 캔디 씨가 쓴 그 아편팅크를 찾아, 그 집안의 약장을 들여다볼 의무를."]], [["Ten minutes since, I caught Betteredge at an unoccupied moment, and told him what I wanted.", "십 분 전에, 나는 손 비어 있는 순간의 베터리지를 붙들어, 그에게 내가 원하는 것을 말했다."], ["Without a word of objection, without so much as an attempt to produce his pocket-book, he led the way (making allowances for me at every step) to the store-room in which the medicine chest is kept.", "한마디 반대도 없이, 그의 수첩을 꺼내려는 시도조차 없이, 그는 (걸음마다 나를 헤아려 주며) 그 약장이 보관된 그 저장실로 앞장서 갔다."]], [["I discovered the bottle, carefully guarded by a glass stopper tied over with leather.", "나는 그 병을, 가죽으로 덮어 묶인 유리 마개로 조심스레 지켜진 채 찾아냈다."], ["The preparation which it contained was, as I had anticipated, the common Tincture of Opium.", "그것이 담은 그 조제약은, 내가 예상한 대로, 여느 아편팅크였다."], ["Finding the bottle still well filled, I have resolved to use it, in preference to employing either of the two preparations with which I had taken care to provide myself, in case of emergency.", "그 병이 아직 잘 차 있는 것을 보고, 나는, 위급에 대비하여 내가 마련해 둔 그 두 조제약 중 어느 것을 쓰기보다, 그것을 쓰기로 정했다."]], [["The question of the quantity which I am to administer presents certain difficulties.", "내가 투여할 그 양의 물음은 어떤 어려움을 내놓는다."], ["I have thought it over, and have decided on increasing the dose.", "나는 그것을 곰곰이 헤아려, 그 양을 늘리기로 정했다."]], [["My notes inform me that Mr. Candy only administered twenty-five minims.", "나의 기록은 나에게 캔디 씨가 겨우 스물다섯 미님을 투여했음을 알려 준다."], ["This is a small dose to have produced the results which followed—even in the case of a person so sensitive as Mr. Blake.", "이것은 그 뒤따른 결과를—블레이크 씨처럼 예민한 사람의 경우에조차—자아냈다기엔 적은 양이다."], ["I think it highly probable that Mr. Candy gave more than he supposed himself to have given—knowing, as I do, that he has a keen relish of the pleasures of the table, and that he measured out the laudanum on the birthday, after dinner.", "나는, 내가 아는 대로, 그가 식탁의 즐거움을 예리하게 좋아하고, 그가 그 생일에 저녁 뒤에 그 아편팅크를 재어 냈음을 아니, 캔디 씨가 자기가 준 것으로 여긴 것보다 더 주었을 공산이 크다고 생각한다."], ["In any case, I shall run the risk of enlarging the dose to forty minims.", "어쨌든, 나는 그 양을 마흔 미님으로 늘리는 위험을 무릅쓰겠다."], ["On this occasion, Mr. Blake knows beforehand that he is going to take the laudanum—which is equivalent, physiologically speaking, to his having (unconsciously to himself) a certain capacity in him to resist the effects.", "이번에는, 블레이크 씨가 자기가 그 아편팅크를 취하려 함을 미리 아는데—그것은, 생리학적으로 말하여, 그가 (그 자신은 자각하지 못한 채) 그 효과에 저항할 어떤 능력을 제 안에 지니는 것과 맞먹는다."], ["If my view is right, a larger quantity is therefore imperatively required, this time, to repeat the results which the smaller quantity produced, last year.", "만일 나의 견해가 옳다면, 따라서, 이번에는, 지난해에 그 적은 양이 자아낸 결과를 되풀이하려면 더 많은 양이 반드시 필요하다."]], [["Ten o’clock.—The witnesses, or the company (which shall I call them?) reached the house an hour since.", "열 시.—그 증인들, 또는 그 손님들이 (나는 그들을 무엇이라 부를까?) 한 시간 전에 그 집에 다다랐다."]], [["A little before nine o’clock, I prevailed on Mr.", "아홉 시 조금 전에, 나는 블레이크 씨를 설득하여 나와 함께 그의 침실로 가게 했다."], ["Blake to accompany me to his bedroom; stating, as a reason, that I wished him to look round it, for the last time, in order to make quite sure that nothing had been forgotten in the refurnishing of the room.", "이유로, 그 방을 다시 갖추는 데에 아무것도 잊히지 않았음을 아주 확실히 하려고, 그가 그것을 마지막으로 한 번 둘러보기를 바란다고 대며."], ["I had previously arranged with Betteredge, that the bedchamber prepared for Mr. Bruff should be the next room to Mr.", "나는 미리 베터리지와, 브러프 씨를 위해 채비된 그 침실이 블레이크 씨의 방 옆방이어야 한다는 것과, 문을 두드림으로써 나에게 그 변호사의 다다름이 알려져야 한다는 것을 짜 두었다."], ["Blake’s, and that I should be informed of the lawyer’s arrival by a knock at the door.", "그 현관홀의 시계가 아홉 시를 친 지 오 분 뒤에, 나는 그 두드림을 들었다."], ["Five minutes after the clock in the hall had struck nine, I heard the knock; and, going out immediately, met Mr. Bruff in the corridor.", "그리고 대뜸 나가서, 그 복도에서 브러프 씨와 마주쳤다."]], [["My personal appearance (as usual) told against me. Mr.", "나의 겉모습이 (여느 때처럼) 나에게 불리하게 작용했다."], ["Bruff’s distrust looked at me plainly enough out of Mr. Bruff’s eyes.", "브러프 씨의 불신이 브러프 씨의 눈에서 자못 또렷이 나를 바라보았다."], ["Being well used to producing this effect on strangers, I did not hesitate a moment in saying what I wanted to say, before the lawyer found his way into Mr. Blake’s room.", "낯선 이들에게 이 효과를 자아내는 데에 익숙한지라, 나는 그 변호사가 블레이크 씨의 방으로 들어가기 전에, 내가 하고 싶은 말을 하는 데에 한순간도 망설이지 않았다."]], [["“You have travelled here, I believe, in company with Mrs.", "\"당신은, 내가 믿기로, 메리듀 부인과 버린더 아가씨와 함께 여기 여행해 왔지요?\""], ["Merridew and Miss Verinder?” I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["answered Mr. Bruff, as drily as might be.", "하고 브러프 씨가 되도록 무뚝뚝하게 대답했다."]], [["“Miss Verinder has probably told you, that I wish her presence in the house (and Mrs. Merridew’s presence of course) to be kept a secret from Mr. Blake, until my experiment on him has been tried first?”", "\"버린더 아가씨가 아마 당신에게, 내가 그녀의 그 집에 있음이 (그리고 물론 메리듀 부인의 있음이), 그에 대한 나의 실험이 먼저 시도되기까지, 블레이크 씨에게 비밀로 지켜지기를 바란다고 말했겠지요?\""]], [["“I know that I am to hold my tongue, sir!”", "\"나는 내가 입을 다물어야 함을 아오, 선생!\""], ["said Mr. Bruff, impatiently.", "하고 브러프 씨가 조바심을 내며 말했다."], ["“Being habitually silent on the subject of human folly, I am all the readier to keep my lips closed on this occasion.", "\"인간의 어리석음이라는 주제에 대해서는 늘 입을 다무는지라, 나는 이 경우에 그만큼 더 어렵잖게 나의 입술을 다문 채로 지킬 수 있소."], ["Does that satisfy you?”", "그것이 당신을 만족시키오?\""]], [["I bowed, and left Betteredge to show him to his room.", "나는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 베터리지에게 그를 그의 방으로 안내하도록 두었다."], ["Betteredge gave me one look at parting, which said, as if in so many words, “You have caught a Tartar, Mr. Jennings—and the name of him is Bruff.”", "베터리지는 헤어질 때 나에게 한 눈길을 주었는데, 그것은, 마치 그야말로 이런 말로 하듯, 이렇게 말했다, \"당신은 만만찮은 사람을 붙들었소, 제닝스 씨—그리고 그의 이름은 브러프요.\""]], [["It was next necessary to get the meeting over with the two ladies.", "다음으로 그 두 부인과의 만남을 끝내야 했다."], ["I descended the stairs—a little nervously, I confess—on my way to Miss Verinder’s sitting-room.", "나는—인정하거니와, 조금 신경이 곤두선 채로—버린더 아가씨의 응접실로 가는 길에 그 계단을 내려갔다."]], [["The gardener’s wife (charged with looking after the accommodation of the ladies) met me in the first-floor corridor.", "(그 부인들의 시중을 맡은) 정원사의 아내가 그 이 층 복도에서 나를 맞았다."], ["This excellent woman treats me with an excessive civility which is plainly the offspring of down-right terror.", "이 훌륭한 여자는 나를, 뚜렷이 그야말로 공포에서 태어난 지나친 정중함으로 대한다."], ["She stares, trembles, and curtseys, whenever I speak to her.", "그녀는 내가 말할 때마다 빤히 쳐다보고, 떨고, 무릎을 굽혀 인사한다."], ["On my asking for Miss Verinder, she stared, trembled, and would no doubt have curtseyed next, if Miss Verinder herself had not cut that ceremony short, by suddenly opening her sitting-room door.", "내가 버린더 아가씨를 청하자, 그녀는 빤히 쳐다보고, 떨고, 두말할 것 없이 다음으로 무릎을 굽혀 인사했을 것이나, 버린더 아가씨 자신이 별안간 제 응접실 문을 엶으로써 그 의례를 짧게 끊지 않았다면 말이다."]], [["“Is that Mr.", "\"제닝스 씨인가요?\""], ["Jennings?” she asked.", "하고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["Before I could answer, she came out eagerly to speak to me in the corridor.", "내가 미처 답하기도 전에, 그녀는 그 복도에서 나에게 이야기하려 간절히 나왔다."], ["We met under the light of a lamp on a bracket.", "우리는 벽걸이 등의 불빛 아래에서 마주했다."], ["At the first sight of me, Miss Verinder stopped, and hesitated.", "나를 처음 본 순간, 버린더 아가씨는 멈추어, 망설였다."], ["She recovered herself instantly, coloured for a moment—and then, with a charming frankness, offered me her hand.", "그녀는 대뜸 자신을 다잡고, 잠시 낯을 붉혔다—그러고는, 매혹적인 솔직함으로, 나에게 그녀의 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“I can’t treat you like a stranger, Mr.", "\"나는 당신을 낯선 이처럼 대할 수 없어요, 제닝스 씨,\""], ["Jennings,” she said.", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Oh, if you only knew how happy your letters have made me!”", "\"오, 당신의 편지가 나를 얼마나 행복하게 했는지 당신이 알기만 한다면!\""]], [["She looked at my ugly wrinkled face, with a bright gratitude so new to me in my experience of my fellow-creatures, that I was at a loss how to answer her.", "그녀는 나의 못난 주름진 얼굴을, 내 동료 인간들에 대한 나의 경험에서 그토록 새로운 밝은 고마움을 담고 바라보았는데, 나는 그녀에게 어찌 답해야 할지 막막했다."], ["Nothing had prepared me for her kindness and her beauty.", "그 무엇도 나를 그녀의 친절과 그녀의 아름다움에 대해 채비시켜 놓지 않았었다."], ["The misery of many years has not hardened my heart, thank God.", "여러 해의 비참함이 나의 마음을 굳히지는 못했다, 하느님께 감사하게도."], ["I was as awkward and as shy with her, as if I had been a lad in my teens.", "나는, 마치 내가 십 대의 소년이기라도 한 양, 그녀와 함께 있으니 어색하고 수줍었다."]], [["“Where is he now?”", "\"그가 지금 어디 있어요?\""], ["she asked, giving free expression to her one dominant interest—the interest in Mr.", "하고 그녀가, 그녀의 하나의 압도적인 관심—블레이크 씨에 대한 관심을 거리낌 없이 나타내며 물었다."], ["Blake. “What is he doing?", "\"그가 무엇을 하고 있어요?"], ["Has he spoken of me?", "그가 나에 대해 말했어요?"], ["Is he in good spirits?", "그가 기분이 좋아요?"], ["How does he bear the sight of the house, after what happened in it last year?", "그가 지난해에 그 집에서 일어난 일을 겪고서, 그 집을 보는 것을 어떻게 견뎌요?"], ["When are you going to give him the laudanum?", "언제 그에게 그 아편팅크를 줄 거예요?"], ["May I see you pour it out?", "당신이 그것을 따르는 것을 내가 봐도 될까요?"], ["I am so interested; I am so excited—I have ten thousand things to say to you, and they all crowd together so that I don’t know what to say first.", "나는 어찌나 관심이 이는지, 어찌나 흥분되는지—당신에게 할 말이 만 가지나 있는데, 그것들이 죄 한데 몰려서 나는 무엇을 먼저 말해야 할지 모르겠어요."], ["Do you wonder at the interest I take in this?”", "내가 이것에 두는 관심에 당신은 놀라나요?\""]], [["“No,” I said.", "\"아니요,\""], ["“I venture to think that I thoroughly understand it.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 감히 내가 그것을 낱낱이 이해한다고 생각하오.\""]], [["She was far above the paltry affectation of being confused.", "그녀는 어리둥절해하는 척하는 그 하찮은 꾸밈보다 훨씬 위에 있었다."], ["She answered me as she might have answered a brother or a father.", "그녀는 마치 오빠나 아버지에게 답하듯 나에게 답했다."]], [["“You have relieved me of indescribable wretchedness; you have given me a new life.", "\"당신은 나를 이루 말할 수 없는 비참함에서 덜어 주었어요. 당신은 나에게 새 삶을 주었어요."], ["How can I be ungrateful enough to have any concealment from you?", "어찌 내가, 당신에게 무언가를 감출 만큼 은혜를 모를 수 있겠어요?"], ["I love him,”", "나는 그를 사랑해요,\""], ["she said simply, “I have loved him from first to last—even when I was wronging him in my own thoughts; even when I was saying the hardest and the cruellest words to him.", "하고 그녀가 소박하게 말했다. \"나는 처음부터 끝까지 그를 사랑해 왔어요—내가 내 자신의 생각 속에서 그에게 잘못하고 있을 때조차, 내가 그에게 가장 모질고 가장 잔인한 말을 하고 있을 때조차요."], ["Is there any excuse for me, in that?", "거기에, 나에게 무슨 변명거리가 있을까요?"], ["I hope there is—I am afraid it is the only excuse I have.", "나는 있기를 바라요—안됐지만 그것이 내가 가진 유일한 변명거리 같아요."], ["When tomorrow comes, and he knows that I am in the house, do you think——”", "내일이 와서, 그가 내가 그 집에 있음을 알면, 당신은 생각하나요——\""]], [["She stopped again, and looked at me very earnestly.", "그녀는 다시 멈추어, 아주 간절히 나를 바라보았다."]], [["“When tomorrow comes,”", "\"내일이 오면,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said, “I think you have only to tell him what you have just told me.”", "\"당신은 방금 나에게 말한 것을 그에게 말하기만 하면 된다고 나는 생각하오.\""]], [["Her face brightened; she came a step nearer to me.", "그녀의 얼굴이 환해졌다. 그녀는 나에게 한 걸음 더 다가왔다."], ["Her fingers trifled nervously with a flower which I had picked in the garden, and which I had put into the button-hole of my coat.", "그녀의 손가락이, 내가 정원에서 꺾어 내 웃옷의 단춧구멍에 꽂았던 꽃 한 송이를 신경질적으로 만지작거렸다."]], [["“You have seen a great deal of him lately,”", "\"당신은 근래에 그를 많이 봤어요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “Have you, really and truly, seen that?”", "\"당신은, 정말이지 참으로, 그것을 봤나요?\""]], [["“Really and truly,”", "\"정말이지 참으로,\""], ["I answered.", "하고 나는 대답했다."], ["“I am quite certain of what will happen tomorrow.", "\"나는 내일 무슨 일이 일어날지 아주 확신하오."], ["I wish I could feel as certain of what will happen tonight.”", "나는 오늘 밤 무슨 일이 일어날지도 그만큼 확신할 수 있으면 좋으련만.\""]], [["At that point in the conversation, we were interrupted by the appearance of Betteredge with the tea-tray.", "그 대화의 그 지점에서, 우리는 다과 쟁반을 든 베터리지의 나타남에 방해받았다."], ["He gave me another significant look as he passed on into the sitting-room.", "그는 그 응접실로 들어가면서 나에게 또 한 번 뜻깊은 눈길을 주었다."], ["“Aye! aye!", "\"옳지!"], ["make your hay while the sun shines.", "옳지! 해가 비칠 때 건초를 만드시오."], ["The Tartar’s upstairs, Mr. Jennings—the Tartar’s upstairs!”", "그 만만찮은 사람이 위층에 있소, 제닝스 씨—그 만만찮은 사람이 위층에 있소!\""]], [["We followed him into the room.", "우리는 그를 따라 그 방으로 들어갔다."], ["A little old lady, in a corner, very nicely dressed, and very deeply absorbed over a smart piece of embroidery, dropped her work in her lap, and uttered a faint little scream at the first sight of my gipsy complexion and my piebald hair.", "한 모퉁이의, 아주 곱게 차려입고, 세련된 자수 한 조각에 아주 깊이 빠져 있던 자그마한 노부인이, 나의 집시 같은 낯빛과 나의 얼룩덜룩한 머리칼을 처음 본 순간, 무릎에 제 일감을 떨어뜨리고, 희미하고 자그마한 비명을 내질렀다."]], [["“Mrs. Merridew,”", "\"메리듀 부인,\""], ["said Miss Verinder, “this is Mr.", "하고 버린더 아가씨가 말했다."], ["Jennings.”", "\"이분이 제닝스 씨예요.\""]], [["“I beg Mr. Jennings’s pardon,”", "\"제닝스 씨의 용서를 빕니다,\""], ["said the old lady, looking at Miss Verinder, and speaking at me.", "하고 그 노부인이, 버린더 아가씨를 보며, 나에게 말했다."], ["“Railway travelling always makes me nervous.", "\"기차 여행은 늘 저를 초조하게 만듭니다."], ["I am endeavouring to quiet my mind by occupying myself as usual.", "저는 여느 때처럼 스스로를 바쁘게 함으로써 제 마음을 잠재우려 애쓰는 중이에요."], ["I don’t know whether my embroidery is out of place, on this extraordinary occasion.", "이 유별난 자리에서, 제 자수가 어울리지 않는 것인지 저는 모르겠어요."], ["If it interferes with Mr. Jennings’s medical views, I shall be happy to put it away of course.”", "만일 그것이 제닝스 씨의 의학적 견해를 방해한다면, 저는 물론 기꺼이 그것을 치우겠어요.\""]], [["I hastened to sanction the presence of the embroidery, exactly as I had sanctioned the absence of the burst buzzard and the Cupid’s wing. Mrs.", "나는, 꼭 내가 그 터진 말똥가리와 그 큐피드의 날개의 부재를 승인했듯이, 서둘러 그 자수의 자리함을 승인했다."], ["Merridew made an effort—a grateful effort—to look at my hair.", "메리듀 부인은 나의 머리칼을 보려 한 번 애를 썼다—고마운 애씀을. 아니!"], ["No! it was not to be done.", "그것은 될 일이 아니었다."], ["Mrs. Merridew looked back again at Miss Verinder.", "메리듀 부인은 다시 버린더 아가씨를 돌아보았다."]], [["“If Mr. Jennings will permit me,”", "\"만일 제닝스 씨가 저에게 허락해 주신다면,\""], ["pursued the old lady, “I should like to ask a favour.", "하고 그 노부인이 이어 갔다. \"저는 청 하나를 하고 싶어요."], ["Mr. Jennings is about to try a scientific experiment tonight.", "제닝스 씨는 오늘 밤 과학 실험을 하려 하십니다."], ["I used to attend scientific experiments when I was a girl at school.", "저는 학교 다니는 계집아이였을 때 과학 실험에 참관하곤 했어요."], ["They invariably ended in an explosion.", "그것들은 어김없이 폭발로 끝났지요."], ["If Mr. Jennings will be so very kind, I should like to be warned of the explosion this time.", "만일 제닝스 씨가 아주 친절히 해 주신다면, 저는 이번에는 그 폭발을 미리 경고받고 싶어요."], ["With a view to getting it over, if possible, before I go to bed.”", "가능하다면, 제가 잠자리에 들기 전에 그것을 끝내 버릴 셈으로요.\""]], [["I attempted to assure Mrs. Merridew that an explosion was not included in the programme on this occasion.", "나는 메리듀 부인에게, 이번에는 폭발이 그 순서에 들어 있지 않다고 다짐하려 했다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["said the old lady.", "하고 그 노부인이 말했다."], ["“I am much obliged to Mr. Jennings—I am aware that he is only deceiving me for my own good.", "\"저는 제닝스 씨께 아주 고마워요—저는 그가 오직 저 자신의 좋음을 위해 저를 속이고 있음을 알아요."], ["I prefer plain dealing.", "저는 솔직한 대함을 좋아해요."], ["I am quite resigned to the explosion—but I do want to get it over, if possible, before I go to bed.”", "저는 그 폭발에 아주 체념했어요—허나 저는 정말로, 가능하다면, 제가 잠자리에 들기 전에 그것을 끝내 버리고 싶어요.\""]], [["Here the door opened, and Mrs. Merridew uttered another little scream.", "여기서 그 문이 열렸고, 메리듀 부인이 또 한 번 자그마한 비명을 질렀다."], ["The advent of the explosion?", "그 폭발의 도래인가?"], ["No: only the advent of Betteredge.", "아니, 그저 베터리지의 도래일 뿐."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr. Jennings,”", "\"용서하십시오, 제닝스 씨,\""], ["said Betteredge, in his most elaborately confidential manner.", "하고 베터리지가, 그의 더없이 공들여 은근한 태도로 말했다."], ["“Mr. Franklin wishes to know where you are.", "\"프랭클린 나리가 당신이 어디 계신지 알고 싶어 하십니다."], ["Being under your orders to deceive him, in respect to the presence of my young lady in the house, I have said I don’t know.", "저의 젊은 아가씨가 그 집에 있음에 관하여 그를 속이라는 당신의 명령을 받은지라, 저는 모른다고 말했습니다."], ["That, you will please to observe, was a lie.", "그것은, 눈여겨봐 주시기 바랍니다만, 거짓말이었습지요."], ["Having one foot already in the grave, sir, the fewer lies you expect me to tell, the more I shall be indebted to you, when my conscience pricks me and my time comes.”", "이미 한 발을 무덤에 들여놓은지라, 선생, 당신이 저에게 하기를 바라시는 거짓말이 적을수록, 저의 양심이 저를 찌르고 저의 때가 올 때, 저는 그만큼 당신께 빚지게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["There was not a moment to be wasted on the purely speculative question of Betteredge’s conscience.", "베터리지의 양심이라는 순전히 사변적인 물음에 허비할 순간은 없었다."], ["Mr. Blake might make his appearance in search of me, unless I went to him at once in his own room.", "만일 내가 대뜸 그의 방에서 그에게 가지 않는다면, 블레이크 씨가 나를 찾아 나타날지도 몰랐다."], ["Miss Verinder followed me out into the corridor.", "버린더 아가씨가 나를 따라 그 복도로 나왔다."]], [["“They seem to be in a conspiracy to persecute you,”", "\"그들이 당신을 못살게 굴려고 짜기라도 한 것 같아요,\""], ["she said.", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["“What does it mean?”", "\"그게 무슨 뜻일까요?\""]], [["“Only the protest of the world, Miss Verinder—on a very small scale—against anything that is new.”", "\"그저 세상의 항변이에요, 버린더 아가씨—아주 작은 규모의—새로운 것은 무엇이든에 대한.\""]], [["“What are we to do with Mrs. Merridew?”", "\"우리가 메리듀 부인을 어떻게 하죠?\""]], [["“Tell her the explosion will take place at nine tomorrow morning.”", "\"그녀에게 그 폭발이 내일 아침 아홉 시에 일어날 거라고 말하시오.\""]], [["“So as to send her to bed?”", "\"그녀를 잠자리로 보내려고요?\""]], [["“Yes—so as to send her to bed.”", "\"그렇소—그녀를 잠자리로 보내려고.\""]], [["Miss Verinder went back to the sitting-room, and I went upstairs to Mr. Blake.", "버린더 아가씨는 그 응접실로 돌아갔고, 나는 블레이크 씨에게로 위층으로 올라갔다."]], [["To my surprise I found him alone; restlessly pacing his room, and a little irritated at being left by himself.", "나의 놀라움으로 나는 그가 홀로 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 안절부절못하며 그의 방을 서성이고 있었고, 홀로 남겨진 것에 조금 짜증이 나 있었다."]], [["“Where is Mr.", "\"브러프 씨는 어디 있소?\""], ["Bruff?” I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["He pointed to the closed door of communication between the two rooms. Mr.", "그는 그 두 방 사이의 닫힌 통용문을 가리켰다."], ["Bruff had looked in on him, for a moment; had attempted to renew his protest against our proceedings; and had once more failed to produce the smallest impression on Mr. Blake.", "브러프 씨가 잠시 그를 들여다보았고, 우리 일에 대한 그의 항변을 되풀이하려 했고, 다시 한 번 블레이크 씨에게 조금의 인상도 남기는 데에 실패했다."], ["Upon this, the lawyer had taken refuge in a black leather bag, filled to bursting with professional papers.", "이에, 그 변호사는 직업상의 서류로 터질 듯 채워진 검은 가죽 가방에 몸을 숨겼다."], ["“The serious business of life,” he admitted, “was sadly out of place on such an occasion as the present.", "\"삶의 진지한 일\"이, 그가 인정하기를, \"현재 같은 자리에는 서글피 어울리지 않소."], ["But the serious business of life must be carried on, for all that. Mr.", "허나 삶의 진지한 일은 그럼에도 계속되어야 하오."], ["Blake would perhaps kindly make allowance for the old-fashioned habits of a practical man. Time was money—and, as for Mr.", "블레이크 씨는 어쩌면 친절히, 실질적인 사람의 예스러운 버릇을 헤아려 줄 거요."], ["Jennings, he might depend on it that Mr. Bruff would be forthcoming when called upon.”", "시간은 돈이오—그리고, 제닝스 씨로 말하자면, 그는 브러프 씨가 부름을 받으면 나서리라 믿어도 좋소.\""], ["With that apology, the lawyer had gone back to his own room, and had immersed himself obstinately in his black bag.", "그 변명과 함께, 그 변호사는 제 방으로 돌아가, 그의 검은 가방에 고집스레 파묻혔다."]], [["I thought of Mrs. Merridew and her embroidery, and of Betteredge and his conscience.", "나는 메리듀 부인과 그녀의 자수를, 그리고 베터리지와 그의 양심을 생각했다."], ["There is a wonderful sameness in the solid side of the English character—just as there is a wonderful sameness in the solid expression of the English face.", "잉글랜드인의 성품의 그 굳건한 면에는 놀라운 한결같음이 있다—꼭 잉글랜드인의 얼굴의 그 굳건한 표정에 놀라운 한결같음이 있듯이."]], [["“When are you going to give me the laudanum?”", "\"언제 나에게 그 아편팅크를 줄 것이오?\""], ["asked Mr. Blake impatiently.", "하고 블레이크 씨가 조바심을 내며 물었다."]], [["“You must wait a little longer,” I said.", "\"당신은 조금 더 기다려야 하오,\""], ["“I will stay and keep you company till the time comes.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 남아서 그때가 올 때까지 당신의 벗이 되어 드리겠소.\""]], [["It was then not ten o’clock.", "그때는 아직 열 시가 아니었다."], ["Inquiries which I had made, at various times, of Betteredge and Mr. Blake, had led me to the conclusion that the dose of laudanum given by Mr.", "내가 여러 때에, 베터리지와 블레이크 씨에게 한 물음들이, 나로 하여금 캔디 씨가 준 그 아편팅크 약이 도저히 열한 시 전에 투여되었을 수 없다는 결론에 이르게 했다."], ["Candy could not possibly have been administered before eleven. I had accordingly determined not to try the second dose until that time.", "나는 그에 따라 그때까지 그 둘째 번 약을 시도하지 않기로 정한 터였다."]], [["We talked a little; but both our minds were preoccupied by the coming ordeal.", "우리는 조금 이야기했다. 허나 우리 둘의 마음 모두 다가올 시련에 사로잡혀 있었다."], ["The conversation soon flagged—then dropped altogether.", "그 대화는 곧 시들해졌다—그러고는 아예 그쳤다."], ["Mr. Blake idly turned over the books on his bedroom table.", "블레이크 씨는 그의 침실 탁자 위의 책들을 한가로이 뒤적였다."], ["I had taken the precaution of looking at them, when we first entered the room.", "나는 우리가 처음 그 방에 들어섰을 때, 그것들을 보아 두는 조심을 한 터였다. 《가디언》, 《태틀러》, 리처드슨의 《패멀라》, 매켄지의 《감정의 사람》, 로스코의 《로렌초 데 메디치》, 그리고 로버트슨의 《카를 오세》—죄 고전 작품들이다."], ["The Guardian; The Tatler; Richardson’s Pamela; Mackenzie’s Man of Feeling; Roscoe’s Lorenzo de’ Medici; and Robertson’s Charles the Fifth—all classical works; all (of course) immeasurably superior to anything produced in later times; and all (from my present point of view) possessing the one great merit of enchaining nobody’s interest, and exciting nobody’s brain.", "죄 (물론) 나중 시대에 나온 무엇보다 헤아릴 수 없이 뛰어나다. 그리고 죄 (나의 지금 관점에서) 아무의 관심도 사로잡지 않고, 아무의 머리도 흥분시키지 않는다는 그 하나의 큰 장점을 지녔다."], ["I left Mr. Blake to the composing influence of Standard Literature, and occupied myself in making this entry in my journal.", "나는 블레이크 씨를 그 표준 문학의 진정시키는 영향에 맡겨 두고, 나의 일기에 이 항목을 적는 데에 나를 썼다."]], [["My watch informs me that it is close on eleven o’clock.", "나의 시계가 나에게 열한 시가 다 되었음을 알린다."], ["I must shut up these leaves once more.", "나는 다시 한 번 이 낱장들을 덮어야 한다."]], [["Two o’clock A.M.—The experiment has been tried.", "오전 두 시.—그 실험이 시도되었다."], ["With what result, I am now to describe.", "무슨 결과로 그러했는지, 나는 이제 서술하려 한다."]], [["At eleven o’clock, I rang the bell for Betteredge, and told Mr. Blake that he might at last prepare himself for bed.", "열한 시에, 나는 베터리지를 부르는 종을 울리고, 블레이크 씨에게 그가 마침내 잠자리에 들 채비를 해도 좋다고 말했다."]], [["I looked out of the window at the night.", "나는 창밖으로 그 밤을 내다보았다."], ["It was mild and rainy, resembling, in this respect, the night of the birthday—the twenty-first of June, last year.", "그것은 부드럽고 비가 왔는데, 이 점에서, 그 생일 밤—지난해 유월 이십일일과 닮았다."], ["Without professing to believe in omens, it was at least encouraging to find no direct nervous influences—no stormy or electric perturbations—in the atmosphere.", "조짐을 믿는 척하지 않고도, 적어도 그 대기 속에 아무 직접적인 신경적 영향—아무 사나운, 또는 전기적인 소란도 없음을 알게 된 것은 격려가 되었다."], ["Betteredge joined me at the window, and mysteriously put a little slip of paper into my hand.", "베터리지가 그 창가에서 나에게 합류하여, 알쏭달쏭하게 자그마한 종잇조각 하나를 내 손에 넣어 주었다."], ["It contained these lines:", "그것은 이 글줄들을 담고 있었다."]], [["“Mrs. Merridew has gone to bed, on the distinct understanding that the explosion is to take place at nine tomorrow morning, and that I am not to stir out of this part of the house until she comes and sets me free.", "\"메리듀 부인은, 그 폭발이 내일 아침 아홉 시에 일어난다는 것과, 그녀가 와서 나를 놓아줄 때까지 내가 그 집의 이 부분을 벗어나 움직이지 않는다는 뚜렷한 이해로, 잠자리에 들었어요."], ["She has no idea that the chief scene of the experiment is my sitting-room—or she would have remained in it for the whole night!", "그녀는 그 실험의 주된 무대가 나의 응접실이라는 것을 알지 못해요—그렇지 않았다면 그녀는 온 밤 내내 그 안에 남아 있었을 거예요!"], ["I am alone, and very anxious.", "나는 홀로 있고, 아주 애가 타요."], ["Pray let me see you measure out the laudanum; I want to have something to do with it, even in the unimportant character of a mere looker-on.—R.V.”", "부디 당신이 그 아편팅크를 재어 내는 것을 내가 보게 해 줘요. 나는, 한낱 구경꾼이라는 대수롭지 않은 자격으로라도, 그것에 무언가 관여하고 싶어요.—R.V.\""]], [["I followed Betteredge out of the room, and told him to remove the medicine-chest into Miss Verinder’s sitting-room.", "나는 베터리지를 따라 그 방을 나가, 그에게 그 약장을 버린더 아가씨의 응접실로 옮기라고 말했다."]], [["The order appeared to take him completely by surprise.", "그 명령은 그를 완전히 깜짝 놀라게 하는 듯했다."], ["He looked as if he suspected me of some occult medical design on Miss Verinder!", "그는 마치 내가 버린더 아가씨에게 어떤 은밀한 의학적 속셈을 품었다고 의심하는 것 같은 낯빛이었다!"], ["“Might I presume to ask,”", "\"감히 여쭤봐도 될까요,\""], ["he said, “what my young lady and the medicine-chest have got to do with each other?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"저의 젊은 아가씨와 그 약장이 서로 무슨 상관이 있는지를?\""]], [["“Stay in the sitting-room, and you will see.”", "\"그 응접실에 있어 보시오, 그러면 알게 될 것이오.\""]], [["Betteredge appeared to doubt his own unaided capacity to superintend me effectually, on an occasion when a medicine-chest was included in the proceedings.", "베터리지는, 약장이 그 일에 들어 있는 자리에서, 나를 효과적으로 감독할 제 혼자만의 능력을 미심쩍어하는 듯했다."]], [["“Is there any objection, sir”", "\"반대가 있으신가요, 선생,\""], ["he asked, “to taking Mr.", "하고 그가 물었다."], ["Bruff into this part of the business?”", "\"브러프 씨를 이 부분의 일에 끌어들이는 것에?\""]], [["“Quite the contrary!", "\"오히려 정반대요!"], ["I am now going to ask Mr. Bruff to accompany me downstairs.”", "나는 이제 브러프 씨에게 나와 함께 아래층으로 가기를 청하려 하오.\""]], [["Betteredge withdrew to fetch the medicine-chest, without another word.", "베터리지는 한마디 없이 그 약장을 가지러 물러났다."], ["I went back into Mr. Blake’s room, and knocked at the door of communication.", "나는 블레이크 씨의 방으로 돌아가, 그 통용문을 두드렸다."], ["Mr. Bruff opened it, with his papers in his hand—immersed in Law; impenetrable to Medicine.", "브러프 씨가, 손에 그의 서류를 들고 그것을 열었다—법률에 파묻혀, 의술에는 뚫리지 않는 채로."]], [["“I am sorry to disturb you,”", "\"방해해서 안됐소,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said. “But I am going to prepare the laudanum for Mr.", "\"허나 나는 블레이크 씨를 위해 그 아편팅크를 채비하려 하오."], ["Blake; and I must request you to be present, and to see what I do.”", "그리고 나는 당신이 자리하여, 내가 무엇을 하는지 보기를 청해야겠소.\""]], [["“Yes?” said Mr.", "\"그래서요?\""], ["Bruff, with nine-tenths of his attention riveted on his papers, and with one-tenth unwillingly accorded to me.", "하고 브러프 씨가, 그의 주의의 십 분의 구를 그의 서류에 못 박고, 십 분의 일을 마지못해 나에게 내주며 말했다."], ["“Anything else?”", "\"그 밖에 또 있소?\""]], [["“I must trouble you to return here with me, and to see me administer the dose.”", "\"나는 당신에게 나와 함께 여기로 돌아와, 내가 그 약을 투여하는 것을 보는 수고를 끼쳐야겠소.\""]], [["“Anything else?”", "\"그 밖에 또 있소?\""]], [["“One thing more.", "\"한 가지 더."], ["I must put you to the inconvenience of remaining in Mr. Blake’s room, and of waiting to see what happens.”", "나는 당신에게 블레이크 씨의 방에 남아, 무슨 일이 일어나는지 보려 기다리는 불편을 끼쳐야겠소.\""]], [["“Oh, very good!” said Mr.", "\"오, 아주 좋소!\""], ["Bruff. “My room, or Mr.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["Blake’s room—it doesn’t matter which; I can go on with my papers anywhere.", "\"내 방이든, 블레이크 씨의 방이든—어느 쪽이든 상관없소. 나는 어디서든 나의 서류를 계속할 수 있소."], ["Unless you object, Mr. Jennings, to my importing that amount of common sense into the proceedings?”", "제닝스 씨, 당신이 내가 그만한 양의 예사로운 분별을 이 일에 들여오는 것에 반대하지 않는다면 말이오?\""]], [["Before I could answer, Mr. Blake addressed himself to the lawyer, speaking from his bed.", "내가 미처 답하기도 전에, 블레이크 씨가 그의 침상에서 말하며 그 변호사에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“Do you really mean to say that you don’t feel any interest in what we are going to do?”", "\"당신은 정말로 우리가 하려는 것에 아무 관심도 느끼지 않는다는 말이오?\""], ["he asked. “Mr.", "하고 그가 물었다."], ["Bruff, you have no more imagination than a cow!”", "\"브러프 씨, 당신은 소만큼의 상상력도 없구려!\""]], [["“A cow is a very useful animal, Mr.", "\"소는 아주 쓸모 있는 짐승이오, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["Blake,” said the lawyer.", "하고 그 변호사가 말했다."], ["With that reply he followed me out of the room, still keeping his papers in his hand.", "그 대답과 함께 그는, 여전히 그의 서류를 손에 든 채, 나를 따라 그 방을 나왔다."]], [["We found Miss Verinder, pale and agitated, restlessly pacing her sitting-room from end to end.", "우리는 버린더 아가씨가, 창백하고 동요한 채, 안절부절못하며 제 응접실을 이 끝에서 저 끝으로 서성이는 것을 보았다."], ["At a table in a corner stood Betteredge, on guard over the medicine-chest.", "한 모퉁이의 탁자에 베터리지가, 그 약장을 지키며 서 있었다."], ["Mr. Bruff sat down on the first chair that he could find, and (emulating the usefulness of the cow) plunged back again into his papers on the spot.", "브러프 씨는 그가 찾을 수 있는 첫 의자에 앉아, (그 소의 쓸모 있음을 본받아) 그 자리에서 다시 그의 서류에 파묻혔다."]], [["Miss Verinder drew me aside, and reverted instantly to her one all-absorbing interest—her interest in Mr. Blake.", "버린더 아가씨는 나를 한쪽으로 끌어, 대뜸 그녀의 하나의 온통 마음을 사로잡는 관심—블레이크 씨에 대한 관심으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“How is he now?”", "\"그가 지금 어때요?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 그녀가 물었다."], ["“Is he nervous?", "\"그가 초조해해요?"], ["is he out of temper?", "그가 성이 났어요?"], ["Do you think it will succeed?", "당신은 그것이 성공할 거라 생각해요?"], ["Are you sure it will do no harm?”", "당신은 그것이 아무 해도 끼치지 않을 것을 확신해요?\""]], [["“Quite sure.", "\"아주 확신하오."], ["Come, and see me measure it out.”", "자, 와서 내가 그것을 재어 내는 것을 보시오.\""]], [["“One moment!", "\"잠깐만요!"], ["It is past eleven now.", "이제 열한 시가 지났어요."], ["How long will it be before anything happens?”", "무슨 일이 일어나기까지 얼마나 걸릴까요?\""]], [["“It is not easy to say.", "\"말하기 쉽지 않소."], ["An hour perhaps.”", "어쩌면 한 시간.\""]], [["“I suppose the room must be dark, as it was last year?”", "\"짐작건대 그 방은, 지난해에 그랬듯이, 어두워야겠지요?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론이오.\""]], [["“I shall wait in my bedroom—just as I did before.", "\"나는 내 침실에서 기다릴게요—꼭 전에 그랬듯이."], ["I shall keep the door a little way open.", "나는 문을 조금 열어 둘게요."], ["It was a little way open last year.", "지난해에 그것이 조금 열려 있었어요."], ["I will watch the sitting-room door; and the moment it moves, I will blow out my light.", "나는 그 응접실 문을 지켜볼게요. 그리고 그것이 움직이는 순간, 나는 내 불을 불어 끌게요."], ["It all happened in that way, on my birthday night.", "그것이 죄 그런 식으로 일어났어요, 나의 생일 밤에."], ["And it must all happen again in the same way, musn’t it?”", "그리고 그것이 죄 같은 식으로 다시 일어나야 해요, 그렇지 않아요?\""]], [["“Are you sure you can control yourself, Miss Verinder?”", "\"당신은 스스로를 다잡을 수 있음을 확신하오, 버린더 아가씨?\""]], [["“In his interests, I can do anything!”", "\"그를 위해서라면, 나는 무엇이든 할 수 있어요!\""], ["she answered fervently.", "하고 그녀가 열렬히 대답했다."]], [["One look at her face told me that I could trust her.", "그녀의 얼굴을 한 번 보니 나는 그녀를 믿어도 좋음을 알았다."], ["I addressed myself again to Mr. Bruff.", "나는 다시 브러프 씨에게 말을 건넸다."]], [["“I must trouble you to put your papers aside for a moment,”", "\"나는 당신에게 잠시 당신의 서류를 옆에 놓는 수고를 끼쳐야겠소,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“Oh, certainly!”", "\"오, 물론이오!\""], ["He got up with a start—as if I had disturbed him at a particularly interesting place—and followed me to the medicine-chest.", "그는 소스라치며 일어났다—마치 내가 그를 각별히 흥미로운 대목에서 방해하기라도 한 양—그리고 나를 따라 그 약장으로 왔다."], ["There, deprived of the breathless excitement incidental to the practice of his profession, he looked at Betteredge—and yawned wearily.", "거기서, 그의 직업의 행위에 딸린 그 숨죽인 흥분을 빼앗긴 채, 그는 베터리지를 보았다—그리고 지친 듯 하품했다."]], [["Miss Verinder joined me with a glass jug of cold water, which she had taken from a side-table.", "버린더 아가씨가, 곁상에서 집어 온 찬물 유리 주전자를 들고 나에게 합류했다. \"물은 내가 따르게 해 줘요,\""], ["“Let me pour out the water,”", "하고 그녀가 속삭였다."], ["she whispered. “I must have a hand in it!”", "\"나는 정말로 그것에 손을 대고 싶어요!\""]], [["I measured out the forty minims from the bottle, and poured the laudanum into a medicine glass.", "나는 그 병에서 마흔 미님을 재어 내어, 그 아편팅크를 약 잔에 따랐다."], ["“Fill it till it is three parts full,”", "\"그것이 사분의 삼쯤 차도록 채우시오,\""], ["I said, and handed the glass to Miss Verinder.", "하고 나는 말하며, 그 잔을 버린더 아가씨에게 건넸다."], ["I then directed Betteredge to lock up the medicine chest; informing him that I had done with it now.", "그런 다음 나는 베터리지에게 그 약장을 넣고 잠그라고 지시했다. 그에게 이제 그것으로 볼일을 다했다고 알리며."], ["A look of unutterable relief overspread the old servant’s countenance.", "이루 말할 수 없는 안도의 표정이 그 늙은 하인의 낯에 번졌다."], ["He had evidently suspected me of a medical design on his young lady!", "그는 뚜렷이 내가 그의 젊은 아가씨에게 어떤 의학적 속셈을 품었다고 의심했던 것이다!"]], [["After adding the water as I had directed, Miss Verinder seized a moment—while Betteredge was locking the chest, and while Mr. Bruff was looking back to his papers—and slyly kissed the rim of the medicine glass.", "내가 지시한 대로 그 물을 더한 뒤, 버린더 아가씨는 한순간을 붙잡아—베터리지가 그 약장을 잠그는 동안, 그리고 브러프 씨가 그의 서류를 돌아보는 동안—슬며시 그 약 잔의 가장자리에 입을 맞추었다."], ["“When you give it to him,”", "\"그것을 그에게 줄 때,\""], ["said the charming girl, “give it to him on that side!”", "하고 그 매혹적인 아가씨가 말했다. \"그것을 저쪽으로 그에게 줘요!\""]], [["I took the piece of crystal which was to represent the Diamond from my pocket, and gave it to her.", "나는 그 다이아몬드를 나타낼 그 수정 조각을 내 주머니에서 꺼내어, 그것을 그녀에게 주었다."]], [["“You must have a hand in this, too,”", "\"당신은 이것에도 손을 대야 하오,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["I said. “You must put it where you put the Moonstone last year.”", "\"당신은 그것을 지난해에 그 월장석을 놓았던 곳에 놓아야 하오.\""]], [["She led the way to the Indian cabinet, and put the mock Diamond into the drawer which the real Diamond had occupied on the birthday night.", "그녀는 그 인도식 장식장으로 앞장서 가, 그 진짜 다이아몬드가 그 생일 밤에 차지했던 그 서랍에 그 가짜 다이아몬드를 놓았다."], ["Mr. Bruff witnessed this proceeding, under protest, as he had witnessed everything else.", "브러프 씨는, 그가 다른 온갖 것을 지켜본 것과 같이, 항변과 함께, 이 일을 지켜보았다."], ["But the strong dramatic interest which the experiment was now assuming, proved (to my great amusement) to be too much for Betteredge’s capacity of self-restraint.", "허나 그 실험이 이제 띠어 가는 그 강렬한 극적 관심이, (나의 큰 재미로) 베터리지의 자제 능력에 벅찬 것으로 드러났다."], ["His hand trembled as he held the candle, and he whispered anxiously, “Are you sure, miss, it’s the right drawer?”", "그는 그 초를 든 채 손을 떨며, 애타게 속삭였다, \"아가씨, 그게 옳은 서랍이 확실한가요?\""]], [["I led the way out again, with the laudanum and water in my hand.", "나는 그 아편팅크와 물을 손에 들고, 다시 앞장서 나갔다."], ["At the door, I stopped to address a last word to Miss Verinder.", "그 문에서, 나는 버린더 아가씨에게 마지막 한마디를 건네려 멈추었다."]], [["“Don’t be long in putting out the lights,”", "\"불을 끄는 것을 오래 끌지 마시오,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“I will put them out at once,”", "\"나는 그것들을 대뜸 끌게요,\""], ["she answered.", "하고 그녀가 대답했다."], ["“And I will wait in my bedroom, with only one candle alight.”", "\"그리고 나는 오직 초 하나만 켜 둔 채, 내 침실에서 기다릴게요.\""]], [["She closed the sitting-room door behind us. Followed by Mr.", "그녀는 우리 뒤로 그 응접실 문을 닫았다."], ["Bruff and Betteredge, I went back to Mr. Blake’s room.", "브러프 씨와 베터리지를 뒤에 거느리고, 나는 블레이크 씨의 방으로 돌아갔다."]], [["We found him moving restlessly from side to side of the bed, and wondering irritably whether he was to have the laudanum that night.", "우리는 그가 안절부절못하며 침상의 이쪽저쪽으로 움직이며, 자기가 그날 밤 그 아편팅크를 받게 되는지 짜증스레 궁금해하는 것을 보았다."], ["In the presence of the two witnesses, I gave him the dose, and shook up his pillows, and told him to lie down again quietly and wait.", "그 두 증인의 앞에서, 나는 그에게 그 약을 주고, 그의 베개를 흔들어 매만지고, 다시 조용히 누워 기다리라고 그에게 말했다."]], [["His bed, provided with light chintz curtains, was placed, with the head against the wall of the room, so as to leave a good open space on either side of it.", "가벼운 사라사 커튼을 두른 그의 침상은, 그 방의 벽에 머리를 대고, 그 양옆으로 넉넉히 트인 공간을 남기게끔 놓여 있었다."], ["On one side, I drew the curtains completely—and in the part of the room thus screened from his view, I placed Mr. Bruff and Betteredge, to wait for the result.", "한쪽으로, 나는 그 커튼을 완전히 쳤다—그리고 그의 눈에서 이렇게 가려진 그 방의 부분에, 나는 그 결과를 기다리도록 브러프 씨와 베터리지를 두었다."], ["At the bottom of the bed I half drew the curtains—and placed my own chair at a little distance, so that I might let him see me or not see me, speak to me or not speak to me, just as the circumstances might direct.", "그 침상의 발치에 나는 그 커튼을 반쯤 쳤다—그리고 나 자신의 의자를 조금 떨어진 곳에 두어, 상황이 지시하는 대로, 그가 나를 보게 하거나 못 보게 하고, 나에게 말을 걸게 하거나 못 걸게 할 수 있도록."], ["Having already been informed that he always slept with a light in the room, I placed one of the two lighted candles on a little table at the head of the bed, where the glare of the light would not strike on his eyes.", "그가 늘 방에 불을 켠 채 잔다는 것을 이미 들은 터라, 나는 그 두 켜진 초 중 하나를, 그 불빛의 번쩍임이 그의 눈에 부딪치지 않을, 그 침상 머리맡의 자그마한 탁자에 놓았다."], ["The other candle I gave to Mr.", "다른 초는 브러프 씨에게 주었다."], ["Bruff; the light, in this instance, being subdued by the screen of the chintz curtains.", "그 불빛은, 이 경우에, 그 사라사 커튼의 가림으로 누그러진 채였다."], ["The window was open at the top, so as to ventilate the room.", "그 창은, 방을 환기하려, 위쪽이 열려 있었다."], ["The rain fell softly, the house was quiet.", "비가 부드럽게 내렸고, 그 집은 고요했다."], ["It was twenty minutes past eleven, by my watch, when the preparations were completed, and I took my place on the chair set apart at the bottom of the bed.", "그 채비가 마쳐지고, 내가 그 침상 발치에 따로 놓인 그 의자에 나의 자리를 잡았을 때, 나의 시계로 열한 시 이십 분이었다."]], [["Mr. Bruff resumed his papers, with every appearance of being as deeply interested in them as ever.", "브러프 씨는, 여느 때와 다름없이 그것들에 깊이 관심을 둔 온갖 기색과 함께, 그의 서류를 다시 이어 갔다."], ["But looking towards him now, I saw certain signs and tokens which told me that the Law was beginning to lose its hold on him at last.", "허나 이제 그를 향해 보니, 나는 그 법률이 마침내 그에 대한 손아귀를 잃기 시작하고 있음을 나에게 일러 주는 어떤 표시와 조짐들을 보았다."], ["The suspended interest of the situation in which we were now placed was slowly asserting its influence even on his unimaginative mind.", "우리가 이제 놓인 그 처지의 그 멎은 관심이, 그의 상상력 없는 마음에조차 서서히 그 영향을 발휘하고 있었다."], ["As for Betteredge, consistency of principle and dignity of conduct had become, in his case, mere empty words.", "베터리지로 말하자면, 원칙의 일관됨과 처신의 위엄이, 그의 경우에, 한낱 빈말이 되어 있었다. 그는 내가 프랭클린 블레이크 나리에게 요술을 부리고 있음을 잊었다."], ["He forgot that I was performing a conjuring trick on Mr.", "그는 내가 그 집을 위아래로 뒤집어 놓았음을 잊었다."], ["Franklin Blake; he forgot that I had upset the house from top to bottom; he forgot that I had not read Robinson Crusoe since I was a child.", "그는 내가 어렸을 때 이후로 「로빈슨 크루소」를 안 읽었음을 잊었다. \"제발, 선생,\""], ["“For the Lord’s sake, sir,”", "하고 그가 나에게 속삭였다."], ["he whispered to me, “tell us when it will begin to work.”", "\"그것이 언제 듣기 시작할지 우리에게 말해 주십시오.\""]], [["“Not before midnight,”", "\"자정 전에는 아니오,\""], ["I whispered back.", "하고 나는 속삭여 되받았다."], ["“Say nothing, and sit still.”", "\"아무 말도 말고, 가만히 앉아 있으시오.\""]], [["Betteredge dropped to the lowest depth of familiarity with me, without a struggle to save himself.", "베터리지는, 자신을 지키려는 안간힘 하나 없이, 나와의 스스럼없음의 가장 낮은 밑바닥으로 떨어졌다."], ["He answered by a wink!", "그는 눈을 찡긋함으로써 답했다!"]], [["Looking next towards Mr. Blake, I found him as restless as ever in his bed; fretfully wondering why the influence of the laudanum had not begun to assert itself yet.", "다음으로 블레이크 씨를 향해 보니, 나는 그가 여느 때와 다름없이 그의 침상에서 안절부절못하며, 어째서 그 아편팅크의 영향이 아직 발휘되기 시작하지 않았는지 안달하며 궁금해하는 것을 보았다."], ["To tell him, in his present humour, that the more he fidgeted and wondered, the longer he would delay the result for which we were now waiting, would have been simply useless.", "그의 지금 심사에, 그가 안달하고 궁금해할수록, 우리가 이제 기다리는 그 결과를 그가 그만큼 더 늦추게 되리라고 그에게 말하는 것은 그저 헛되었을 것이다."], ["The wiser course to take was to dismiss the idea of the opium from his mind, by leading him insensibly to think of something else.", "취할 더 슬기로운 방도는, 그로 하여금 어느새 다른 무언가를 생각하게 이끎으로써, 그의 마음에서 그 아편의 생각을 몰아내는 것이었다."]], [["With this view, I encouraged him to talk to me; contriving so to direct the conversation, on my side, as to lead it back again to the subject which had engaged us earlier in the evening—the subject of the Diamond.", "이 뜻으로, 나는 그가 나에게 이야기하도록 부추겼다. 내 편에서, 그것을 그날 저녁 더 이르게 우리를 사로잡았던 그 주제—그 다이아몬드라는 주제로 도로 이끌도록 그 대화를 이끌면서."], ["I took care to revert to those portions of the story of the Moonstone, which related to the transport of it from London to Yorkshire; to the risk which Mr. Blake had run in removing it from the bank at Frizinghall; and to the unexpected appearance of the Indians at the house, on the evening of the birthday.", "나는, 그 월장석의 이야기에서, 그것을 런던에서 요크셔로 옮긴 것에, 블레이크 씨가 그것을 프리징홀의 은행에서 옮기면서 무릅쓴 그 위험에, 그리고 그 생일 저녁에 그 집에 인도인들이 뜻밖에 나타난 것에 관련된 부분들로 되돌아가는 데에 마음 썼다."], ["And I purposely assumed, in referring to these events, to have misunderstood much of what Mr. Blake himself had told me a few hours since.", "그리고 나는, 이 사건들을 언급하면서, 블레이크 씨 자신이 몇 시간 전에 나에게 말해 준 것 중 많은 것을 잘못 알아들은 척 일부러 꾸몄다."], ["In this way, I set him talking on the subject with which it was now vitally important to fill his mind—without allowing him to suspect that I was making him talk for a purpose.", "이런 식으로, 나는 그로 하여금, 이제 그의 마음을 채우는 것이 아주 중요한 그 주제로 이야기하게 만들었다—그가, 내가 어떤 목적으로 그를 이야기하게 만들고 있음을 의심하도록 두지 않으면서."], ["Little by little, he became so interested in putting me right that he forgot to fidget in the bed.", "조금씩, 그는 나를 바로잡는 데에 어찌나 관심이 이는지 그 침상에서 안달하는 것을 잊었다."], ["His mind was far away from the question of the opium, at the all-important time when his eyes first told me that the opium was beginning to lay its hold on his brain.", "그의 눈이 처음으로 나에게 그 아편이 그의 뇌에 손을 얹기 시작하고 있음을 일러 준 그 더없이 중요한 때에, 그의 마음은 그 아편의 물음에서 멀리 떨어져 있었다."]], [["I looked at my watch.", "나는 나의 시계를 보았다."], ["It wanted five minutes to twelve, when the premonitory symptoms of the working of the laudanum first showed themselves to me.", "그 아편팅크가 듣는 것의 전조 증상이 처음으로 나에게 제 모습을 드러냈을 때, 열두 시까지 오 분이 모자랐다."]], [["At this time, no unpractised eyes would have detected any change in him.", "이때, 훈련되지 않은 눈이라면 그에게서 아무 변화도 알아채지 못했을 것이다."], ["But, as the minutes of the new morning wore away, the swiftly-subtle progress of the influence began to show itself more plainly.", "허나, 그 새 아침의 순간들이 흘러가면서, 그 영향의 빠르고도 미묘한 진행이 더 또렷이 제 모습을 드러내기 시작했다."], ["The sublime intoxication of opium gleamed in his eyes; the dew of a stealthy perspiration began to glisten on his face.", "아편의 그 숭고한 취기가 그의 눈에서 어른거렸다. 은밀한 땀의 이슬이 그의 얼굴에서 반짝이기 시작했다."], ["In five minutes more, the talk which he still kept up with me, failed in coherence.", "오 분이 더 지나자, 그가 여전히 나와 이어 가던 그 이야기가, 조리를 잃었다."], ["He held steadily to the subject of the Diamond; but he ceased to complete his sentences.", "그는 그 다이아몬드라는 주제를 꾸준히 붙들었다. 허나 그는 그의 문장들을 마무리하기를 그쳤다."], ["A little later, the sentences dropped to single words.", "조금 뒤, 그 문장들이 낱낱의 낱말로 떨어졌다. 그러고는, 침묵의 사이가 있었다."], ["Then, there was an interval of silence.", "그러고는, 그가 침상에서 일어나 앉았다."], ["Then, he sat up in bed. Then, still busy with the subject of the Diamond, he began to talk again—not to me, but to himself.", "그러고는, 여전히 그 다이아몬드라는 주제로 바쁜 채, 그가 다시 이야기하기 시작했다—나에게가 아니라, 자기 자신에게."], ["That change told me that the first stage in the experiment was reached.", "그 변화가 나에게 그 실험의 첫 단계에 이르렀음을 일러 주었다."], ["The stimulant influence of the opium had got him.", "그 아편의 흥분시키는 영향이 그를 사로잡은 것이다."]], [["The time, now, was twenty-three minutes past twelve.", "그때, 시각은 열두 시 이십삼 분이었다."], ["The next half hour, at most, would decide the question of whether he would, or would not, get up from his bed, and leave the room.", "기껏해야 그다음 반 시간이, 그가 그의 침상에서 일어나 그 방을 떠날 것인지 아닌지의 물음을 결정할 터였다."]], [["In the breathless interest of watching him—in the unutterable triumph of seeing the first result of the experiment declare itself in the manner, and nearly at the time, which I had anticipated—I had utterly forgotten the two companions of my night vigil.", "그를 지켜보는 그 숨죽인 관심 속에서—그 실험의 첫 결과가, 내가 예상한 그 방식으로, 그리고 거의 그 때에 스스로를 드러내는 것을 보는 그 이루 말할 수 없는 승리 속에서—나는 나의 밤샘의 그 두 동무를 온전히 잊고 있었다."], ["Looking towards them now, I saw the Law (as represented by Mr. Bruff’s papers) lying unheeded on the floor.", "이제 그들을 향해 보니, 나는 (브러프 씨의 서류로 나타나는) 그 법률이 바닥에 아랑곳없이 놓인 것을 보았다."], ["Mr. Bruff himself was looking eagerly through a crevice left in the imperfectly-drawn curtains of the bed.", "브러프 씨 자신은 그 불완전하게 쳐진 침상의 커튼에 남겨진 틈으로 간절히 들여다보고 있었다."], ["And Betteredge, oblivious of all respect for social distinctions, was peeping over Mr. Bruff’s shoulder.", "그리고 베터리지는, 신분의 차이에 대한 온갖 존중을 잊은 채, 브러프 씨의 어깨 너머로 엿보고 있었다."]], [["They both started back, on finding that I was looking at them, like two boys caught out by their schoolmaster in a fault.", "그들은 둘 다, 내가 그들을 보고 있음을 알아채고, 마치 잘못을 저지르다 훈장에게 들킨 두 소년처럼 움찔 물러났다."], ["I signed to them to take off their boots quietly, as I was taking off mine. If Mr.", "나는, 내가 내 것을 벗고 있듯, 그들에게 조용히 그들의 부츠를 벗으라고 몸짓했다."], ["Blake gave us the chance of following him, it was vitally necessary to follow him without noise.", "만일 블레이크 씨가 우리에게 그를 뒤따를 기회를 준다면, 소리 없이 그를 뒤따르는 것이 아주 중요했다."]], [["Ten minutes passed—and nothing happened.", "십 분이 지났다—그리고 아무 일도 없었다."], ["Then, he suddenly threw the bed-clothes off him.", "그러고는, 그가 별안간 그의 침구를 벗어 던졌다."], ["He put one leg out of bed.", "그는 한 다리를 침상 밖으로 내밀었다."], ["He waited.", "그는 기다렸다."]], [["“I wish I had never taken it out of the bank,”", "\"내가 그것을 그 은행에서 꺼내지 말았어야 했는데,\""], ["he said to himself.", "하고 그가 자기 자신에게 말했다."], ["“It was safe in the bank.”", "\"그것은 그 은행에서 안전했는데.\""]], [["My heart throbbed fast; the pulses at my temples beat furiously.", "내 심장이 빠르게 뛰었다. 내 관자놀이의 맥이 세차게 뛰었다."], ["The doubt about the safety of the Diamond was, once more, the dominant impression in his brain!", "그 다이아몬드의 안전에 대한 그 의심이, 다시 한 번, 그의 뇌 속의 압도적인 인상이었다!"], ["On that one pivot, the whole success of the experiment turned.", "그 하나의 축(軸) 위에, 그 실험의 온 성공이 돌았다."], ["The prospect thus suddenly opened before me was too much for my shattered nerves.", "이렇게 별안간 내 앞에 열린 그 전망이 나의 산산조각 난 신경에 벅찼다."], ["I was obliged to look away from him—or I should have lost my self-control.", "나는 그에게서 눈을 돌리지 않을 수 없었다—그러지 않았다면 나는 나의 자제심을 잃었을 것이다."]], [["There was another interval of silence.", "또 한 번 침묵의 사이가 있었다."]], [["When I could trust myself to look back at him he was out of his bed, standing erect at the side of it.", "내가 그를 도로 볼 만큼 나 자신을 믿을 수 있게 되었을 때 그는 그의 침상 밖에 나와, 그 곁에 곧추서 있었다."], ["The pupils of his eyes were now contracted; his eyeballs gleamed in the light of the candle as he moved his head slowly to and fro.", "그의 눈동자는 이제 오그라들어 있었다. 그가 그의 머리를 천천히 이리저리 움직이자 그의 눈알이 그 초의 불빛 속에서 어른거렸다."], ["He was thinking; he was doubting—he spoke again.", "그는 생각하고 있었다. 그는 의심하고 있었다—그가 다시 말했다."]], [["“How do I know?”", "\"내가 어찌 알겠어?\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“The Indians may be hidden in the house.”", "\"그 인도인들이 그 집에 숨어 있을지도 몰라.\""]], [["He stopped, and walked slowly to the other end of the room.", "그는 멈추어, 그 방의 다른 쪽 끝으로 천천히 걸어갔다."], ["He turned—waited—came back to the bed.", "그는 돌아섰다—기다렸다—그 침상으로 돌아왔다."]], [["“It’s not even locked up,”", "\"그건 잠겨 있지도 않아,\""], ["he went on.", "하고 그가 이어 갔다."], ["“It’s in the drawer of her cabinet.", "\"그건 그녀의 장식장 서랍에 있어."], ["And the drawer doesn’t lock.”", "그리고 그 서랍은 잠기지 않아.\""]], [["He sat down on the side of the bed.", "그는 그 침상 곁에 앉았다."], ["“Anybody might take it,”", "\"누구든 그것을 가져갈 수 있어,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["He rose again restlessly, and reiterated his first words.", "그는 다시 안절부절못하며 일어나, 그의 첫마디를 되뇌었다."]], [["“How do I know?", "\"내가 어찌 알겠어?"], ["The Indians may be hidden in the house.”", "그 인도인들이 그 집에 숨어 있을지도 몰라.\""]], [["He waited again.", "그는 다시 기다렸다."], ["I drew back behind the half curtain of the bed.", "나는 그 침상의 반쯤 친 커튼 뒤로 물러났다."], ["He looked about the room, with a vacant glitter in his eyes.", "그는 그의 눈에 텅 빈 어른거림을 담고, 그 방을 둘러보았다."], ["It was a breathless moment.", "그것은 숨죽인 순간이었다."], ["There was a pause of some sort.", "어떤 종류의 멈춤이 있었다."], ["A pause in the action of the opium?", "그 아편의 작용의 멈춤인가?"], ["a pause in the action of the brain?", "그 뇌의 작용의 멈춤인가?"], ["Who could tell?", "누가 알 수 있었으랴?"], ["Everything depended, now, on what he did next.", "모든 것이, 이제, 그가 다음에 무엇을 하는지에 달렸다."]], [["He laid himself down again on the bed!", "그가 다시 그 침상에 몸을 눕혔다!"]], [["A horrible doubt crossed my mind.", "끔찍한 의심이 내 마음을 스쳤다."], ["Was it possible that the sedative action of the opium was making itself felt already?", "그 아편의 진정시키는 작용이 이미 느껴지고 있다는 것이 있을 수 있는 일인가?"], ["It was not in my experience that it should do this.", "그것이 이리하는 것은 나의 경험에 없었다."], ["But what is experience, where opium is concerned?", "허나, 아편에 관한 한, 경험이 무엇이랴?"], ["There are probably no two men in existence on whom the drug acts in exactly the same manner.", "그 약이 정확히 같은 방식으로 작용하는 두 사람은 세상에 아마 없을 것이다."], ["Was some constitutional peculiarity in him, feeling the influence in some new way?", "그의 어떤 체질적 특이함이, 그 영향을 어떤 새로운 방식으로 느끼고 있는 것인가?"], ["Were we to fail on the very brink of success?", "우리가 성공의 그야말로 벼랑에서 실패해야 하는가?"]], [["No!", "아니!"], ["He got up again abruptly.", "그가 다시 갑자기 일어났다."], ["“How the devil am I to sleep,”", "\"내가 어찌 잠들겠어,\""], ["he said, “with this on my mind?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"이것을 마음에 두고서?\""]], [["He looked at the light, burning on the table at the head of his bed.", "그는 그의 침상 머리맡 탁자에서 타는 그 불빛을 보았다."], ["After a moment, he took the candle in his hand.", "잠시 뒤, 그가 그 초를 손에 들었다."]], [["I blew out the second candle, burning behind the closed curtains.", "나는 그 닫힌 커튼 뒤에서 타는 그 둘째 초를 불어 껐다."], ["I drew back, with Mr. Bruff and Betteredge, into the farthest corner by the bed.", "나는, 브러프 씨와 베터리지와 함께, 그 침상 곁의 가장 먼 모퉁이로 물러났다."], ["I signed to them to be silent, as if their lives had depended on it.", "나는 그들에게, 마치 그들의 목숨이 거기 달리기라도 한 양, 조용히 하라고 몸짓했다."]], [["We waited—seeing and hearing nothing.", "우리는 기다렸다—아무것도 보지도 듣지도 못한 채."], ["We waited, hidden from him by the curtains.", "우리는 기다렸다, 그 커튼에 그에게서 가려진 채."]], [["The light which he was holding on the other side of us moved suddenly.", "그가 우리 저쪽에서 들고 있던 그 불빛이 별안간 움직였다."], ["The next moment he passed us, swift and noiseless, with the candle in his hand.", "다음 순간 그가, 손에 그 초를 들고, 재빠르고 소리 없이, 우리를 지나쳤다."]], [["He opened the bedroom door, and went out.", "그는 그 침실 문을 열고, 나갔다."]], [["We followed him along the corridor.", "우리는 그를 따라 그 복도를 지나갔다."], ["We followed him down the stairs.", "우리는 그를 따라 그 계단을 내려갔다."], ["We followed him along the second corridor.", "우리는 그를 따라 그 둘째 복도를 지나갔다."], ["He never looked back; he never hesitated.", "그는 결코 뒤돌아보지 않았다. 그는 결코 망설이지 않았다."]], [["He opened the sitting-room door, and went in, leaving it open behind him.", "그는 그 응접실 문을 열고, 그 뒤에 그것을 열어 둔 채, 들어갔다."]], [["The door was hung (like all the other doors in the house) on large old-fashioned hinges.", "그 문은 (그 집의 다른 온갖 문처럼) 큰 예스러운 돌쩌귀에 달려 있었다."], ["When it was opened, a crevice was opened between the door and the post.", "그것이 열리자, 그 문과 문설주 사이에 틈이 열렸다."], ["I signed to my two companions to look through this, so as to keep them from showing themselves.", "나는 나의 두 동무에게, 그들 자신이 드러나지 않게, 이것을 통해 들여다보라고 몸짓했다."], ["I placed myself—outside the door also—on the opposite side.", "나 자신은—역시 그 문 밖에서—그 맞은편에 자리했다."], ["A recess in the wall was at my left hand, in which I could instantly hide myself, if he showed any signs of looking back into the corridor.", "벽의 우묵한 곳이 나의 왼편에 있었는데, 만일 그가 그 복도를 뒤돌아볼 어떤 기색이라도 보이면, 나는 그 안에 대뜸 나를 숨길 수 있었다."]], [["He advanced to the middle of the room, with the candle still in his hand: he looked about him—but he never looked back.", "그는 여전히 손에 그 초를 든 채, 그 방 한가운데로 나아갔다. 그는 주위를 둘러보았다—허나 그는 결코 뒤돌아보지 않았다."]], [["I saw the door of Miss Verinder’s bedroom, standing ajar.", "나는 버린더 아가씨의 침실 문이 빠끔히 열려 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She had put out her light.", "그녀는 그녀의 불을 껐다."], ["She controlled herself nobly.", "그녀는 고귀하게 스스로를 다잡았다."], ["The dim white outline of her summer dress was all that I could see.", "그녀의 여름 옷의 어슴푸레한 흰 윤곽이 내가 볼 수 있는 전부였다."], ["Nobody who had not known it beforehand would have suspected that there was a living creature in the room.", "그것을 미리 알지 못한 사람이라면 그 방에 살아 있는 존재가 있음을 의심하지 않았을 것이다. 그녀는 어둠 속에 물러나 있었다."], ["She kept back, in the dark: not a word, not a movement escaped her.", "한마디 말도, 한 번의 움직임도 그녀에게서 새어 나오지 않았다."]], [["It was now ten minutes past one.", "이제 한 시 십 분이었다."], ["I heard, through the dead silence, the soft drip of the rain and the tremulous passage of the night air through the trees.", "나는, 그 죽은 듯한 침묵을 뚫고, 그 비의 부드러운 떨어짐과, 그 나무들 사이를 지나는 그 밤 공기의 떨리는 지나감을 들었다."]], [["After waiting irresolute, for a minute or more, in the middle of the room, he moved to the corner near the window, where the Indian cabinet stood.", "그 방 한가운데에서, 일 분 남짓, 망설이며 기다린 뒤, 그는 그 인도식 장식장이 서 있는 그 창가 모퉁이로 움직였다."]], [["He put his candle on the top of the cabinet.", "그는 그의 초를 그 장식장 위에 놓았다."], ["He opened, and shut, one drawer after another, until he came to the drawer in which the mock Diamond was put.", "그는 서랍을 하나씩 열었다 닫았다 하며, 그 가짜 다이아몬드가 놓인 그 서랍에 이르렀다."], ["He looked into the drawer for a moment.", "그는 잠시 그 서랍을 들여다보았다."], ["Then he took the mock Diamond out with his right hand.", "그러고는 그의 오른손으로 그 가짜 다이아몬드를 꺼냈다."], ["With the other hand, he took the candle from the top of the cabinet.", "다른 손으로, 그는 그 장식장 위에서 그 초를 집었다."]], [["He walked back a few steps towards the middle of the room, and stood still again.", "그는 그 방 한가운데를 향해 몇 걸음 걸어 돌아와, 다시 가만히 섰다."]], [["Thus far, he had exactly repeated what he had done on the birthday night.", "여기까지, 그는 그 생일 밤에 그가 한 것을 정확히 되풀이한 터였다."], ["Would his next proceeding be the same as the proceeding of last year?", "그의 다음 조치가 지난해의 조치와 같을 것인가?"], ["Would he leave the room?", "그가 그 방을 떠날 것인가?"], ["Would he go back now, as I believed he had gone back then, to his bedchamber?", "그가 이제, 내가 그때 그가 돌아갔다고 믿는 대로, 그의 침실로 돌아갈 것인가?"], ["Would he show us what he had done with the Diamond, when he had returned to his own room?", "그가 그 자신의 방으로 돌아갔을 때 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지 우리에게 보여 줄 것인가?"]], [["His first action, when he moved once more, proved to be an action which he had not performed, when he was under the influence of the opium for the first time.", "그가 다시 한 번 움직였을 때 그의 첫 행동은, 그가 처음으로 그 아편의 영향 아래 있었을 때 그가 하지 않은 행동으로 드러났다."], ["He put the candle down on a table, and wandered on a little towards the farther end of the room.", "그는 그 초를 탁자에 내려놓고, 그 방의 더 먼 쪽 끝을 향해 조금 헤매어 갔다."], ["There was a sofa there.", "거기 소파가 하나 있었다."], ["He leaned heavily on the back of it, with his left hand—then roused himself, and returned to the middle of the room.", "그는 왼손으로 그 등받이에 무겁게 기댔다—그러고는 정신을 차리고, 그 방 한가운데로 돌아왔다. 나는 이제 그의 눈을 볼 수 있었다."], ["I could now see his eyes.", "그것들은 흐릿하고 무거워지고 있었다."], ["They were getting dull and heavy; the glitter in them was fast dying out.", "그것들 속의 그 어른거림이 빠르게 스러져 가고 있었다."]], [["The suspense of the moment proved too much for Miss Verinder’s self-control.", "그 순간의 마음 졸임이 버린더 아가씨의 자제심에 벅찬 것으로 드러났다."], ["She advanced a few steps—then stopped again. Mr.", "그녀는 몇 걸음 나아갔다—그러고는 다시 멈추었다."], ["Bruff and Betteredge looked across the open doorway at me for the first time.", "브러프 씨와 베터리지가 처음으로 그 열린 문간 너머로 나를 건너다보았다."], ["The prevision of a coming disappointment was impressing itself on their minds as well as on mine.", "다가오는 실망에 대한 예감이, 나의 마음에서와 마찬가지로 그들의 마음에서도 새겨지고 있었다."]], [["Still, so long as he stood where he was, there was hope.", "그럼에도, 그가 그가 있는 곳에 서 있는 한, 희망이 있었다."], ["We waited, in unutterable expectation, to see what would happen next.", "우리는, 이루 말할 수 없는 기대 속에, 다음에 무슨 일이 일어나는지 보려 기다렸다."]], [["The next event was decisive.", "다음 사건이 결정적이었다."], ["He let the mock Diamond drop out of his hand.", "그가 그 가짜 다이아몬드를 그의 손에서 떨어뜨렸다."]], [["It fell on the floor, before the doorway—plainly visible to him, and to every one.", "그것은 바닥에, 그 문간 앞에 떨어졌다—그에게도, 모두에게도 또렷이 보이게."], ["He made no effort to pick it up: he looked down at it vacantly, and, as he looked, his head sank on his breast.", "그는 그것을 집으려 아무 애도 쓰지 않았다. 그는 그것을 멍하니 내려다보았고, 내려다보면서, 그의 머리가 그의 가슴에 내려앉았다."], ["He staggered—roused himself for an instant—walked back unsteadily to the sofa—and sat down on it.", "그는 비틀거렸다—한순간 정신을 차렸다—불안하게 그 소파로 걸어 돌아왔다—그리고 그 위에 앉았다."], ["He made a last effort; he tried to rise, and sank back.", "그는 마지막 안간힘을 썼다. 그는 일어서려 했고, 도로 주저앉았다."], ["His head fell on the sofa cushions.", "그의 머리가 그 소파 쿠션에 떨어졌다."], ["It was then twenty-five minutes past one o’clock.", "그때는 한 시 이십오 분이었다."], ["Before I had put my watch back in my pocket, he was asleep.", "내가 나의 시계를 주머니에 도로 넣기 전에, 그는 잠들어 있었다."]], [["It was all over now.", "이제 그것은 죄 끝났다."], ["The sedative influence had got him; the experiment was at an end.", "그 진정시키는 영향이 그를 사로잡았다. 그 실험은 끝난 것이다."]], [["I entered the room, telling Mr. Bruff and Betteredge that they might follow me.", "나는, 브러프 씨와 베터리지에게 그들이 나를 뒤따라도 좋다고 말하며, 그 방으로 들어갔다."], ["There was no fear of disturbing him.", "그를 방해할 두려움은 없었다."], ["We were free to move and speak.", "우리는 자유로이 움직이고 말할 수 있었다."]], [["“The first thing to settle,”", "\"먼저 정할 일은,\""], ["I said, “is the question of what we are to do with him.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 그를 어떻게 하느냐 하는 물음이오."], ["He will probably sleep for the next six or seven hours, at least.", "그는 아마 적어도 그다음 여섯이나 일곱 시간을 잘 것이오."], ["It is some distance to carry him back to his own room.", "그를 그 자신의 방으로 도로 나르기에는 얼마쯤 거리가 있소."], ["When I was younger, I could have done it alone.", "내가 더 젊었을 때는, 나는 그것을 혼자 할 수 있었을 것이오."], ["But my health and strength are not what they were—I am afraid I must ask you to help me.”", "허나 나의 건강과 힘은 예전 같지 않소—안됐지만 나는 당신들에게 나를 돕기를 청해야겠소.\""]], [["Before they could answer, Miss Verinder called to me softly.", "그들이 미처 답하기도 전에, 버린더 아가씨가 나를 나직이 불렀다."], ["She met me at the door of her room, with a light shawl, and with the counterpane from her own bed.", "그녀는 그녀의 방 문에서, 가벼운 숄과, 그녀 자신의 침상에서 가져온 침대보를 들고 나를 맞았다."]], [["“Do you mean to watch him while he sleeps?”", "\"그가 자는 동안 그를 지켜볼 셈인가요?\""], ["she asked.", "하고 그녀가 물었다."]], [["“Yes, I am not sure enough of the action of the opium in his case to be willing to leave him alone.”", "\"그렇소, 나는 그의 경우에 그 아편의 작용을 그를 혼자 두어도 될 만큼 확신하지 못하오.\""]], [["She handed me the shawl and the counterpane.", "그녀는 나에게 그 숄과 그 침대보를 건넸다."]], [["“Why should you disturb him?”", "\"어째서 그를 방해해야 하죠?\""], ["she whispered.", "하고 그녀가 속삭였다."], ["“Make his bed on the sofa.", "\"그의 잠자리를 그 소파에 봐 줘요."], ["I can shut my door, and keep in my room.”", "나는 내 문을 닫고, 내 방에 있을 수 있어요.\""]], [["It was infinitely the simplest and the safest way of disposing of him for the night.", "그것은 한없이, 그날 밤 그를 처리하는 가장 간단하고 가장 안전한 방도였다."], ["I mentioned the suggestion to Mr. Bruff and Betteredge—who both approved of my adopting it.", "나는 그 제안을 브러프 씨와 베터리지에게 말했다—둘 다 내가 그것을 채택하는 것에 찬성했다."], ["In five minutes I had laid him comfortably on the sofa, and had covered him lightly with the counterpane and the shawl.", "오 분 만에 나는 그를 그 소파에 편안히 뉘었고, 그 침대보와 그 숄로 그를 가볍게 덮었다."], ["Miss Verinder wished us good-night, and closed the door.", "버린더 아가씨는 우리에게 잘 자라 인사하고, 그 문을 닫았다."], ["At my request, we three then drew round the table in the middle of the room, on which the candle was still burning, and on which writing materials were placed.", "나의 청으로, 우리 셋은 그런 다음, 그 초가 여전히 타고 있고 필기도구가 놓인, 그 방 한가운데의 그 탁자에 둘러 모였다."]], [["“Before we separate,”", "\"우리가 헤어지기 전에,\""], ["I began, “I have a word to say about the experiment which has been tried tonight.", "하고 나는 입을 뗐다. \"나는 오늘 밤 시도된 그 실험에 대해 한마디 하고 싶소."], ["Two distinct objects were to be gained by it.", "그것으로 얻어야 할 두 가지 뚜렷한 목적이 있었소."], ["The first of these objects was to prove, that Mr. Blake entered this room, and took the Diamond, last year, acting unconsciously and irresponsibly, under the influence of opium.", "이 목적 가운데 첫째는, 블레이크 씨가 지난해에 이 방에 들어와 그 다이아몬드를 가져간 것이, 아편의 영향 아래, 무의식적으로, 책임 없이 한 것임을 증명하는 것이었소."], ["After what you have both seen, are you both satisfied, so far?”", "당신들 둘이 본 것을 겪고서, 당신들 둘은, 여기까지, 흡족하오?\""]], [["They answered me in the affirmative, without a moment’s hesitation.", "그들은 한순간의 망설임도 없이, 나에게 그렇다고 답했다."]], [["“The second object,”", "\"그 둘째 목적은,\""], ["I went on, “was to discover what he did with the Diamond, after he was seen by Miss Verinder to leave her sitting-room with the jewel in his hand, on the birthday night.", "하고 나는 이어 갔다. \"그 생일 밤에, 그가 손에 그 보석을 들고 그녀의 응접실을 떠나는 것을 버린더 아가씨가 본 뒤, 그가 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지 알아내는 것이었소."], ["The gaining of this object depended, of course, on his still continuing exactly to repeat his proceedings of last year.", "이 목적을 얻는 것은, 물론, 그가 여전히 지난해의 그의 행동을 정확히 계속 되풀이하는 것에 달려 있었소."], ["He has failed to do that; and the purpose of the experiment is defeated accordingly.", "그는 그것을 하는 데에 실패했소. 그리고 그에 따라 그 실험의 목적은 좌절되었소."], ["I can’t assert that I am not disappointed at the result—but I can honestly say that I am not surprised by it. I told Mr.", "나는 내가 그 결과에 실망하지 않았다고 우기지는 못하겠소—허나 나는 내가 그것에 놀라지는 않는다고 정직하게 말할 수 있소."], ["Blake from the first, that our complete success in this matter depended on our completely reproducing in him the physical and moral conditions of last year—and I warned him that this was the next thing to a downright impossibility.", "나는 처음부터 블레이크 씨에게, 이 일에서의 우리의 온전한 성공이, 우리가 지난해의 몸과 마음의 조건을 그에게 온전히 되살리는 것에 달렸다고 말했소—그리고 나는 그에게 이것이 그야말로 불가능한 것에 가깝다고 경고했소."], ["We have only partially reproduced the conditions, and the experiment has been only partially successful in consequence.", "우리는 다만 그 조건을 부분적으로만 되살렸고, 그 결과로 그 실험은 다만 부분적으로만 성공했소."], ["It is also possible that I may have administered too large a dose of laudanum.", "내가 그 아편팅크를 너무 많이 투여했을지도 모른다는 것도 있을 법하오."], ["But I myself look upon the first reason that I have given, as the true reason why we have to lament a failure, as well as to rejoice over a success.”", "허나 나 자신은, 우리가 성공을 기뻐할 뿐 아니라 실패를 슬퍼해야 하는 참된 이유로, 내가 든 그 첫 이유를 보오.\""]], [["After saying those words, I put the writing materials before Mr. Bruff, and asked him if he had any objection—before we separated for the night—to draw out, and sign, a plain statement of what he had seen.", "그 말을 하고서, 나는 브러프 씨 앞에 그 필기도구를 놓고, 그에게—우리가 그날 밤 헤어지기 전에—그가 본 것에 대한 평이한 진술을 작성하고 서명하는 것에 반대가 있는지 물었다."], ["He at once took the pen, and produced the statement with the fluent readiness of a practised hand.", "그는 대뜸 그 펜을 들어, 익숙한 손의 유창한 거침없음으로 그 진술을 내놓았다."]], [["“I owe you this,”", "\"나는 당신에게 이것을 빚졌소,\""], ["he said, signing the paper, “as some atonement for what passed between us earlier in the evening.", "하고 그가 그 종이에 서명하며 말했다. \"그날 저녁 더 이르게 우리 사이에 오간 일에 대한 얼마쯤의 속죄로."], ["I beg your pardon, Mr. Jennings, for having doubted you.", "당신을 의심한 데 대해 당신의 용서를 비오, 제닝스 씨."], ["You have done Franklin Blake an inestimable service.", "당신은 프랭클린 블레이크에게 값을 헤아릴 수 없는 도움을 베풀었소."], ["In our legal phrase, you have proved your case.”", "우리의 법률 용어로, 당신은 당신의 주장을 증명했소.\""]], [["Betteredge’s apology was characteristic of the man.", "베터리지의 사과는 그 사람다웠다."]], [["“Mr. Jennings,”", "\"제닝스 씨,\""], ["he said, “when you read Robinson Crusoe again (which I strongly recommend you to do), you will find that he never scruples to acknowledge it, when he turns out to have been in the wrong.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 「로빈슨 크루소」를 다시 읽으시면 (제가 강하게 권하는 바입니다만), 당신은 그가 자기가 틀렸던 것으로 드러날 때, 결코 그것을 인정하기를 망설이지 않는 것을 알게 될 것입니다."], ["Please to consider me, sir, as doing what Robinson Crusoe did, on the present occasion.”", "부디, 선생, 이 경우에 저를, 로빈슨 크루소가 한 것을 하는 사람으로 여겨 주십시오.\""], ["With those words he signed the paper in his turn.", "그 말과 함께 그는 제 차례로 그 종이에 서명했다."]], [["Mr. Bruff took me aside, as we rose from the table.", "브러프 씨가, 우리가 그 탁자에서 일어설 때, 나를 한쪽으로 끌었다."]], [["“One word about the Diamond,” he said.", "\"그 다이아몬드에 대해 한마디,\""], ["“Your theory is that Franklin Blake hid the Moonstone in his room.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신의 이론은 프랭클린 블레이크가 그 월장석을 그의 방에 감췄다는 것이오."], ["My theory is, that the Moonstone is in the possession of Mr. Luker’s bankers in London.", "나의 이론은, 그 월장석이 런던의 루커 씨의 은행가의 소유에 있다는 것이오."], ["We won’t dispute which of us is right.", "우리 중 어느 쪽이 옳은지는 다투지 맙시다."], ["We will only ask, which of us is in a position to put his theory to the test?”", "우리는 다만, 우리 중 어느 쪽이 제 이론을 증명에 부칠 처지에 있는지 물읍시다.\""]], [["“The test, in my case,”", "\"나의 경우의 그 증명은,\""], ["I answered, “has been tried tonight, and has failed.”", "하고 나는 대답했다. \"오늘 밤 시도되어, 실패했소.\""]], [["“The test, in my case,” rejoined Mr.", "\"나의 경우의 그 증명은,\""], ["Bruff, “is still in process of trial.", "하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다. \"아직 시도되는 과정에 있소."], ["For the last two days I have had a watch set for Mr.", "지난 이틀 동안 나는 그 은행에 루커 씨를 위한 파수를 세워 두었소."], ["Luker at the bank; and I shall cause that watch to be continued until the last day of the month.", "그리고 나는 그 파수를 이달의 마지막 날까지 계속하게 하겠소."], ["I know that he must take the Diamond himself out of his bankers’ hands—and I am acting on the chance that the person who has pledged the Diamond may force him to do this by redeeming the pledge.", "나는 그가 몸소 그 다이아몬드를 그의 은행가의 손에서 꺼내야 함을 아오—그리고 나는, 그 다이아몬드를 저당 잡힌 사람이 그 저당물을 되찾음으로써 그로 하여금 이것을 하게 만들지도 모른다는 요행에 따라 움직이고 있소."], ["In that case I may be able to lay my hand on the person.", "그런 경우 나는 그 사람에게 내 손을 얹을 수 있을지 모르오."], ["If I succeed, I clear up the mystery, exactly at the point where the mystery baffles us now!", "만일 내가 성공한다면, 나는 바로 그 수수께끼가 지금 우리를 어리둥절하게 하는 그 지점에서, 그 수수께끼를 밝히는 것이오!"], ["Do you admit that, so far?”", "여기까지 당신은 인정하오?\""]], [["I admitted it readily.", "나는 그것을 어렵잖게 인정했다."]], [["“I am going back to town by the morning train,”", "\"나는 아침 기차로 시내로 돌아가오,\""], ["pursued the lawyer.", "하고 그 변호사가 이어 갔다."], ["“I may hear, when I return, that a discovery has been made—and it may be of the greatest importance that I should have Franklin Blake at hand to appeal to, if necessary.", "\"돌아가면 나는, 어떤 발견이 이루어졌다는 소식을 들을지도 모르오—그리고, 필요하다면, 프랭클린 블레이크를 곁에 두어 그에게 물을 수 있는 것이 더없이 중요할지도 모르오."], ["I intend to tell him, as soon as he wakes, that he must return with me to London.", "나는 그가 깨어나는 대로, 그가 나와 함께 런던으로 돌아가야 한다고 그에게 말할 작정이오."], ["After all that has happened, may I trust to your influence to back me?”", "일어난 온갖 것을 겪은 뒤에, 내가 당신의 영향력에 기대어 나를 뒷받침해 주기를 믿어도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Certainly!”", "\"물론이오!\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["Mr. Bruff shook hands with me, and left the room.", "브러프 씨는 나와 악수하고, 그 방을 떠났다."], ["Betteredge followed him out.", "베터리지가 그를 따라 나갔다."]], [["I went to the sofa to look at Mr. Blake.", "나는 블레이크 씨를 보려 그 소파로 갔다."], ["He had not moved since I had laid him down and made his bed—he lay locked in a deep and quiet sleep.", "그는 내가 그를 뉘고 그의 잠자리를 봐 준 이래 움직이지 않은 채였다—그는 깊고 고요한 잠에 잠겨 누워 있었다."]], [["While I was still looking at him, I heard the bedroom door softly opened.", "내가 아직 그를 보고 있는 동안, 나는 그 침실 문이 사뿐히 열리는 것을 들었다."], ["Once more, Miss Verinder appeared on the threshold, in her pretty summer dress.", "다시 한 번, 버린더 아가씨가, 그녀의 어여쁜 여름 옷 차림으로, 그 문지방에 나타났다."]], [["“Do me a last favour?”", "\"나에게 마지막 은혜를 베풀어 주겠어요?\""], ["she whispered.", "하고 그녀가 속삭였다."], ["“Let me watch him with you.”", "\"당신과 함께 그를 지켜보게 해 줘요.\""]], [["I hesitated—not in the interests of propriety; only in the interest of her night’s rest.", "나는 망설였다—예절을 위해서가 아니라, 오직 그녀의 밤의 안식을 위해서."], ["She came close to me, and took my hand.", "그녀는 나에게 바싹 다가와, 내 손을 잡았다."]], [["“I can’t sleep; I can’t even sit still, in my own room,”", "\"나는 잠들 수 없어요. 나는 내 방에서 가만히 앉아 있을 수조차 없어요,\""], ["she said. “Oh, Mr.", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["Jennings, if you were me, only think how you would long to sit and look at him.", "\"오, 제닝스 씨, 만일 당신이 나라면, 당신이 앉아서 그를 바라보기를 얼마나 갈망할지 생각해 봐요."], ["Say, yes!", "그렇다고 말해 줘요!"], ["Do!”", "부디!\""]], [["Is it necessary to mention that I gave way?", "내가 굴복했음을 언급할 필요가 있는가?"], ["Surely not!", "정녕 없으리라!"]], [["She drew a chair to the foot of the sofa.", "그녀는 그 소파 발치로 의자 하나를 끌어왔다."], ["She looked at him in a silent ecstasy of happiness, till the tears rose in her eyes.", "그녀는 눈에 눈물이 고이기까지, 말없는 행복의 황홀함 속에 그를 바라보았다."], ["She dried her eyes, and said she would fetch her work.", "그녀는 눈물을 닦고, 자기의 일감을 가져오겠다고 말했다."], ["She fetched her work, and never did a single stitch of it.", "그녀는 자기의 일감을 가져왔고, 그것을 한 땀도 뜨지 않았다."], ["It lay in her lap—she was not even able to look away from him long enough to thread her needle.", "그것은 그녀의 무릎에 놓여 있었다—그녀는 자기 바늘에 실을 꿰기에 넉넉할 만큼도 그에게서 눈을 뗄 수 없었다."], ["I thought of my own youth; I thought of the gentle eyes which had once looked love at me.", "나는 나 자신의 젊음을 생각했다. 나는 한때 나를 향해 사랑을 담아 바라보던 그 다정한 눈을 생각했다."], ["In the heaviness of my heart I turned to my Journal for relief, and wrote in it what is written here.", "마음의 무거움 속에서 나는 위안을 얻으려 나의 일기로 눈을 돌려, 그것에 여기 적힌 것을 적었다."]], [["So we kept our watch together in silence.", "그리하여 우리는 말없이 함께 우리의 지킴을 이어 갔다."], ["One of us absorbed in his writing; the other absorbed in her love.", "우리 중 하나는 그의 글쓰기에 빠져, 다른 하나는 그녀의 사랑에 빠져."]], [["Hour after hour he lay in his deep sleep.", "시간이 흐르고 흘러도 그는 그 깊은 잠에 누워 있었다."], ["The light of the new day grew and grew in the room, and still he never moved.", "그 새 날의 빛이 그 방에서 자라고 자랐으나, 그는 여전히 결코 움직이지 않았다."]], [["Towards six o’clock, I felt the warning which told me that my pains were coming back.", "여섯 시경, 나는 나의 통증이 돌아오고 있음을 나에게 일러 주는 그 경고를 느꼈다."], ["I was obliged to leave her alone with him for a little while.", "나는 잠시 그녀를 그와 홀로 남겨 두지 않을 수 없었다."], ["I said I would go upstairs, and fetch another pillow for him out of his room.", "나는 위층으로 가서, 그의 방에서 그를 위한 베개 하나를 더 가져오겠다고 말했다."], ["It was not a long attack, this time.", "이번에는, 긴 발작이 아니었다."], ["In a little while I was able to venture back, and let her see me again.", "조금 뒤 나는 감히 돌아와, 그녀가 나를 다시 볼 수 있게 할 수 있었다."]], [["I found her at the head of the sofa, when I returned.", "내가 돌아왔을 때, 나는 그녀가 그 소파 머리맡에 있는 것을 보았다."], ["She was just touching his forehead with her lips.", "그녀는 막 그녀의 입술로 그의 이마에 닿는 참이었다."], ["I shook my head as soberly as I could, and pointed to her chair.", "나는 되도록 근엄하게 고개를 젓고, 그녀의 의자를 가리켰다."], ["She looked back at me with a bright smile, and a charming colour in her face.", "그녀는 밝은 미소와, 그녀의 얼굴에 매혹적인 홍조를 담고 나를 돌아보았다."], ["“You would have done it,”", "\"당신도 그리했을 거예요,\""], ["she whispered, “in my place!”", "하고 그녀가 속삭였다. \"내 처지였다면!\""]], [["It is just eight o’clock.", "이제 막 여덟 시다."], ["He is beginning to move for the first time.", "그가 처음으로 움직이기 시작하고 있다."]], [["Miss Verinder is kneeling by the side of the sofa.", "버린더 아가씨가 그 소파 곁에 무릎 꿇고 있다."], ["She has so placed herself that when his eyes first open, they must open on her face.", "그녀는, 그의 눈이 처음 떠질 때, 그것이 그녀의 얼굴에 떠지도록 스스로 그렇게 자리를 잡았다."]], [["Shall I leave them together?", "내가 그들을 함께 두고 떠날까?"]], [["Yes!", "그래!"]], [["Eleven o’clock.—The house is empty again.", "열한 시.—그 집이 다시 비었다."], ["They have arranged it among themselves; they have all gone to London by the ten o’clock train.", "그들은 자기들끼리 그것을 정했다. 그들은 죄 열 시 기차로 런던으로 갔다."], ["My brief dream of happiness is over.", "나의 짧은 행복의 꿈이 끝났다."], ["I have awakened again to the realities of my friendless and lonely life.", "나는 다시, 벗 없고 외로운 나의 삶의 현실로 깨어났다."]], [["I dare not trust myself to write down the kind words that have been said to me—especially by Miss Verinder and Mr. Blake.", "나는 감히, 나에게 건네진 그 다정한 말들을—각별히 버린더 아가씨와 블레이크 씨에게서 건네진 말들을 적어 둘 만큼 나 자신을 믿지 못하겠다."], ["Besides, it is needless.", "게다가, 그것은 부질없다."], ["Those words will come back to me in my solitary hours, and will help me through what is left of the end of my life.", "그 말들은 나의 외로운 시간에 나에게 돌아올 것이고, 나의 삶의 끝의 남은 것을 헤쳐 나가는 것을 도울 것이다."], ["Mr. Blake is to write, and tell me what happens in London.", "블레이크 씨는 편지하여, 런던에서 무슨 일이 일어나는지 나에게 이야기하기로 되어 있다."], ["Miss Verinder is to return to Yorkshire in the autumn (for her marriage, no doubt); and I am to take a holiday, and be a guest in the house.", "버린더 아가씨는 가을에 요크셔로 돌아오기로 (두말할 것 없이, 그녀의 혼인을 위해) 되어 있다. 그리고 나는 휴가를 얻어, 그 집의 손님이 되기로 되어 있다."], ["Oh me, how I felt, as the grateful happiness looked at me out of her eyes, and the warm pressure of her hand said, “This is your doing!”", "오 이런, 그 고마운 행복이 그녀의 눈에서 나를 바라보고, 그녀의 손의 따뜻한 쥠이 \"이것은 당신이 한 일이에요!\" 하고 말했을 때, 내가 어떤 심정이었던가."]], [["My poor patients are waiting for me.", "나의 가엾은 환자들이 나를 기다리고 있다."], ["Back again, this morning, to the old routine!", "오늘 아침, 다시 그 옛 상례로!"], ["Back again, tonight, to the dreadful alternative between the opium and the pain!", "오늘 밤, 다시 그 아편과 그 통증 사이의 끔찍한 선택으로!"]], [["God be praised for His mercy!", "그분의 자비에 하느님을 찬미하라!"], ["I have seen a little sunshine—I have had a happy time.", "나는 얼마쯤의 햇살을 보았다—나는 행복한 시간을 가졌다."]], [["The Story resumed by Franklin Blake.", "프랭클린 블레이크가 이어 쓰는 이야기."]], [["# CHAPTER I", "# CHAPTER I"]], [["But few words are needed, on my part, to complete the narrative that has been presented in the Journal of Ezra Jennings.", "허나 에즈라 제닝스의 일기에 내놓인 그 이야기를 마무리하는 데에는, 내 편에서, 몇 마디밖에 필요치 않다."]], [["Of myself, I have only to say that I awoke on the morning of the twenty-sixth, perfectly ignorant of all that I had said and done under the influence of the opium—from the time when the drug first laid its hold on me, to the time when I opened my eyes, in Rachel’s sitting-room.", "나 자신에 대해서는, 나는 다만, 내가 이십육일 아침에, 그 아편의 영향 아래 내가 말하고 한 온갖 것에 대해—그 약이 처음 나에게 손을 얹은 때로부터, 내가 레이철의 응접실에서 눈을 뜬 때까지—완전히 무지한 채로 깨어났다는 것만 말할 수밖에 없다."]], [["Of what happened after my waking, I do not feel called upon to render an account in detail.", "나의 깨어남 뒤에 일어난 일에 대해서는, 나는 세세하게 이야기할 의무가 있다고 느끼지 않는다."], ["Confining myself merely to results, I have to report that Rachel and I thoroughly understood each other, before a single word of explanation had passed on either side.", "오직 결과에만 나 자신을 국한하여, 나는, 양편에서 한마디의 설명도 오가기 전에, 레이철과 내가 서로를 낱낱이 이해했다고 전하는 바다."], ["I decline to account, and Rachel declines to account, for the extraordinary rapidity of our reconciliation.", "나는 우리 화해의 그 유별난 빠름을 설명하기를 마다하며, 레이철도 설명하기를 마다한다."], ["Sir and Madam, look back at the time when you were passionately attached to each other—and you will know what happened, after Ezra Jennings had shut the door of the sitting-room, as well as I know it myself.", "선생과 부인이여, 그대들이 서로에게 열렬히 매여 있던 그때를 되돌아보시라—그러면 그대들은, 에즈라 제닝스가 그 응접실의 문을 닫은 뒤에 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 나 자신이 아는 것만큼 잘 알게 되리라."]], [["I have, however, no objection to add, that we should have been certainly discovered by Mrs. Merridew, but for Rachel’s presence of mind.", "허나, 나는, 레이철의 침착함이 아니었다면 우리가 틀림없이 메리듀 부인에게 들켰으리라는 것을 덧붙이는 데에 반대가 없다."], ["She heard the sound of the old lady’s dress in the corridor, and instantly ran out to meet her; I heard Mrs.", "그녀는 그 복도에서 그 노부인의 옷 소리를 듣고, 대뜸 뛰쳐나가 그녀를 맞았다."], ["Merridew say, “What is the matter?”", "나는 메리듀 부인이 \"무슨 일이야?\""], ["and I heard Rachel answer, “The explosion!” Mrs.", "하고 말하는 것을 들었고, 나는 레이철이 \"그 폭발이에요!\""], ["Merridew instantly permitted herself to be taken by the arm, and led into the garden, out of the way of the impending shock.", "하고 답하는 것을 들었다. 메리듀 부인은 대뜸 팔을 잡히도록 스스로를 내맡겨, 그 임박한 충격의 길에서 벗어나, 정원으로 이끌려 갔다."], ["On her return to the house, she met me in the hall, and expressed herself as greatly struck by the vast improvement in Science, since the time when she was a girl at school.", "그 집으로 돌아오면서, 그녀는 그 현관홀에서 나와 마주쳐, 자기가 학교 다니는 계집아이였을 때 이래 과학이 크게 나아진 것에 아주 놀랐다고 밝혔다."], ["“Explosions, Mr. Blake, are infinitely milder than they were.", "\"폭발은, 블레이크 씨, 예전보다 한없이 순해요."], ["I assure you, I barely heard Mr. Jennings’s explosion from the garden.", "장담하는데, 저는 정원에서 제닝스 씨의 폭발을 겨우 들었어요."], ["And no smell afterwards, that I can detect, now we have come back to the house!", "그리고 그 뒤로, 우리가 그 집으로 돌아온 지금, 제가 알아챌 수 있는 아무 냄새도 없어요!"], ["I must really apologise to your medical friend.", "저는 정말로 당신의 의사 벗께 사과해야겠어요."], ["It is only due to him to say that he has managed it beautifully!”", "그가 그것을 훌륭히 해냈다고 말해 주는 것이 그에게 마땅한 도리예요!\""]], [["So, after vanquishing Betteredge and Mr. Bruff, Ezra Jennings vanquished Mrs.", "그리하여, 베터리지와 브러프 씨를 무찌른 뒤, 에즈라 제닝스는 메리듀 부인 자신마저 무찔렀다."], ["Merridew herself. There is a great deal of undeveloped liberal feeling in the world, after all!", "결국, 세상에는 아직 피어나지 않은 너그러운 정이 자못 많이 있다!"]], [["At breakfast, Mr. Bruff made no secret of his reasons for wishing that I should accompany him to London by the morning train.", "아침 식사에서, 브러프 씨는 내가 아침 기차로 그와 함께 런던으로 가기를 바라는 그의 이유를 조금도 감추지 않았다."], ["The watch kept at the bank, and the result which might yet come of it, appealed so irresistibly to Rachel’s curiosity, that she at once decided (if Mrs. Merridew had no objection) on accompanying us back to town—so as to be within reach of the earliest news of our proceedings.", "그 은행에 세운 파수와, 그것에서 아직 나올지 모를 그 결과가, 레이철의 호기심에 어찌나 뿌리칠 수 없이 호소했던지, 그녀는 대뜸 (메리듀 부인이 반대하지 않는다면) 우리와 함께 시내로 돌아가기로 정했다—우리 일의 가장 이른 소식이 닿는 곳에 있으려고."]], [["Mrs. Merridew proved to be all pliability and indulgence, after the truly considerate manner in which the explosion had conducted itself; and Betteredge was accordingly informed that we were all four to travel back together by the morning train.", "메리듀 부인은, 그 폭발이 진정 사려 깊게 처신한 그 태도 뒤에, 온통 유순하고 너그러운 것으로 드러났다."], ["I fully expected that he would have asked leave to accompany us.", "그리고 그에 따라 베터리지는 우리 넷이 죄 아침 기차로 함께 돌아가리라는 것을 전해 들었다."], ["But Rachel had wisely provided her faithful old servant with an occupation that interested him.", "나는 그가 우리를 동행하게 해 달라고 청할 줄로 온전히 예상했다. 허나 레이철은 슬기롭게 그녀의 충직한 늙은 하인에게 그의 관심을 끄는 일거리를 마련해 준 터였다."], ["He was charged with completing the refurnishing of the house, and was too full of his domestic responsibilities to feel the “detective-fever” as he might have felt it under other circumstances.", "그는 그 집의 다시 갖추기를 마무리하는 것을 맡았고, 그의 집안 책임으로 어찌나 벅찼던지 다른 상황에서라면 느꼈을 법한 \"탐정 열병\"을 느끼지 못했다."]], [["Our one subject of regret, in going to London, was the necessity of parting, more abruptly than we could have wished, with Ezra Jennings.", "런던으로 가면서, 우리의 한 가지 아쉬움의 화제는, 우리가 바랐을 것보다 더 갑작스레, 에즈라 제닝스와 헤어지는 필요였다."], ["It was impossible to persuade him to accompany us.", "그를 우리와 동행하도록 설득하기란 불가능했다."], ["I could only promise to write to him—and Rachel could only insist on his coming to see her when she returned to Yorkshire.", "나는 다만 그에게 편지하겠다고 약속할 수밖에 없었고—레이철은 다만 그녀가 요크셔로 돌아오면 그가 그녀를 보러 오기를 우길 수밖에 없었다."], ["There was every prospect of our meeting again in a few months—and yet there was something very sad in seeing our best and dearest friend left standing alone on the platform, as the train moved out of the station.", "우리가 몇 달 안에 다시 만날 온갖 전망이 있었다—그럼에도 기차가 역에서 나갈 때, 우리의 가장 좋고 가장 정다운 벗이 그 승강장에 홀로 남아 서 있는 것을 보는 데에는 아주 서글픈 무언가가 있었다."]], [["On our arrival in London, Mr. Bruff was accosted at the terminus by a small boy, dressed in a jacket and trousers of threadbare black cloth, and personally remarkable in virtue of the extraordinary prominence of his eyes.", "우리가 런던에 다다르자, 브러프 씨는 그 종착역에서, 올이 다 드러난 검은 천으로 된 재킷과 바지 차림의, 그리고 그의 눈의 유별난 튀어나옴으로 개인적으로 눈에 띄는 어린 소년 하나에게 말을 걸렸다."], ["They projected so far, and they rolled about so loosely, that you wondered uneasily why they remained in their sockets.", "그것들은 어찌나 멀리 튀어나오고, 어찌나 헐겁게 이리저리 굴렀던지, 그것들이 어째서 그 눈구멍에 남아 있는지 불안스레 의아했다."], ["After listening to the boy, Mr. Bruff asked the ladies whether they would excuse our accompanying them back to Portland Place.", "그 소년의 말을 듣고서, 브러프 씨는 그 부인들에게, 우리가 그들을 포틀랜드 플레이스로 배웅하지 못하는 것을 양해해 주겠는지 물었다."], ["I had barely time to promise Rachel that I would return, and tell her everything that had happened, before Mr. Bruff seized me by the arm, and hurried me into a cab.", "나는 겨우, 브러프 씨가 내 팔을 붙들어 나를 삯마차로 서둘러 밀어 넣기 전에, 레이철에게 내가 돌아와, 일어난 모든 것을 그녀에게 이야기하겠다고 약속할 짬밖에 없었다."], ["The boy with the ill-secured eyes took his place on the box by the driver, and the driver was directed to go to Lombard Street.", "그 잘못 박힌 눈의 소년이 마부 곁의 마부석에 제 자리를 잡았고, 마부는 롬바드가로 가라는 지시를 받았다."]], [["“News from the bank?”", "\"그 은행에서 온 소식이오?\""], ["I asked, as we started.", "하고 나는 우리가 출발하면서 물었다."]], [["“News of Mr. Luker,”", "\"루커 씨의 소식이오,\""], ["said Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["“An hour ago, he was seen to leave his house at Lambeth, in a cab, accompanied by two men, who were recognised by my men as police officers in plain clothes.", "\"한 시간 전에, 그가 두 사람을 데리고, 삯마차로 램버스의 그의 집을 떠나는 것이 목격되었소. 그 두 사람은 내 사람들에게 사복 경관으로 알아보아졌소."], ["If Mr. Luker’s dread of the Indians is at the bottom of this precaution, the inference is plain enough.", "만일 인도인들에 대한 루커 씨의 두려움이 이 방비의 밑바탕에 있다면, 그 미루어 짐작함은 자못 뚜렷하오."], ["He is going to take the Diamond out of the bank.”", "그는 그 다이아몬드를 그 은행에서 꺼내려는 것이오.\""]], [["“And we are going to the bank to see what comes of it?”", "\"그리고 우리는 그것에서 무엇이 나오는지 보러 그 은행으로 가는 것이오?\""]], [["“Yes—or to hear what has come of it, if it is all over by this time.", "\"그렇소—또는, 만일 그것이 이때쯤 죄 끝났다면, 그것에서 무엇이 나왔는지 들으러 가는 것이오."], ["Did you notice my boy—on the box, there?”", "당신은 내 소년을 눈여겨봤소—저기, 마부석의?\""]], [["“I noticed his eyes.”", "\"나는 그의 눈을 눈여겨봤소.\""]], [["Mr. Bruff laughed.", "브러프 씨가 웃었다."], ["“They call the poor little wretch ‘Gooseberry’ at the office,” he said.", "\"그 사무소에서는 그 가엾은 자그마한 몹쓸 것을 '구스베리'라 부른다오,\""], ["“I employ him to go on errands—and I only wish my clerks who have nicknamed him were as thoroughly to be depended on as he is.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 그를 심부름에 부리오—그리고 나는 다만 그에게 별명을 붙인 내 서기들이 그만큼이나 낱낱이 믿을 만하면 좋으련만 하오."], ["Gooseberry is one of the sharpest boys in London, Mr. Blake, in spite of his eyes.”", "구스베리는, 블레이크 씨, 그의 눈에도 불구하고, 런던에서 가장 예리한 소년의 하나요.\""]], [["It was twenty minutes to five when we drew up before the bank in Lombard Street.", "우리가 롬바드가의 그 은행 앞에 마차를 세웠을 때는 다섯 시 이십 분 전이었다."], ["Gooseberry looked longingly at his master, as he opened the cab door.", "구스베리는 그 삯마차 문을 열면서, 갈망하는 눈으로 제 주인을 보았다."]], [["“Do you want to come in too?”", "\"너도 들어오고 싶으냐?\""], ["asked Mr. Bruff kindly.", "하고 브러프 씨가 다정하게 물었다."], ["“Come in then, and keep at my heels till further orders.", "\"그럼 들어와서, 다음 명령이 있을 때까지 내 뒤꿈치에 붙어 있어라."], ["He’s as quick as lightning,”", "그는 번개처럼 빠르오,\""], ["pursued Mr. Bruff, addressing me in a whisper.", "하고 브러프 씨가 나에게 속삭이며 말을 이었다."], ["“Two words will do with Gooseberry, where twenty would be wanted with another boy.”", "\"다른 소년이라면 스무 마디가 필요할 데서, 구스베리에게는 두 마디면 되오.\""]], [["We entered the bank.", "우리는 그 은행에 들어갔다."], ["The outer office—with the long counter, behind which the cashiers sat—was crowded with people; all waiting their turn to take money out, or to pay money in, before the bank closed at five o’clock.", "그 바깥 사무실은—긴 계산대와, 그 뒤에 출납원들이 앉은—사람들로 붐볐다. 죄 그 은행이 다섯 시에 닫기 전에, 돈을 찾거나 돈을 넣으려 제 차례를 기다리며."]], [["Two men among the crowd approached Mr. Bruff, as soon as he showed himself.", "그 무리 가운데 두 사람이, 브러프 씨가 모습을 드러내자마자, 그에게 다가왔다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["asked the lawyer.", "하고 그 변호사가 물었다."], ["“Have you seen him?”", "\"그를 봤소?\""]], [["“He passed us here half an hour since, sir, and went on into the inner office.”", "\"그가 반 시간 전에 여기서 우리를 지나쳐, 안쪽 사무실로 들어갔습니다, 나리.\""]], [["“Has he not come out again yet?”", "\"그가 아직 다시 나오지 않았소?\""]], [["“No, sir.”", "\"예, 나리.\""]], [["Mr. Bruff turned to me.", "브러프 씨가 나에게로 돌아섰다."], ["“Let us wait,”", "\"기다립시다,\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["I looked round among the people about me for the three Indians.", "나는 내 둘레의 사람들 사이에서 그 인도인 셋을 찾아 둘러보았다."], ["Not a sign of them was to be seen anywhere.", "그들의 기색은 어디서도 보이지 않았다."], ["The only person present with a noticeably dark complexion was a tall man in a pilot coat, and a round hat, who looked like a sailor.", "자리한 사람들 가운데 눈에 띄게 거무스름한 낯빛의 유일한 사람은, 뱃사람처럼 보이는, 두꺼운 외투와 둥근 모자 차림의 키 큰 사내였다."], ["Could this be one of them in disguise?", "이것이 변장한 그들 중 하나일 수 있을까?"], ["Impossible!", "있을 수 없었다!"], ["The man was taller than any of the Indians; and his face, where it was not hidden by a bushy black beard, was twice the breadth of any of their faces at least.", "그 사내는 인도인들 누구보다 키가 컸다. 그리고 그의 얼굴은, 그 텁수룩한 검은 수염에 가려지지 않은 데서, 적어도 그들 누구의 얼굴보다 너비가 두 배였다."]], [["“They must have their spy somewhere,”", "\"그들은 어딘가에 그들의 밀정을 두었음이 틀림없소,\""], ["said Mr. Bruff, looking at the dark sailor in his turn.", "하고 브러프 씨가, 제 차례로 그 거무스름한 뱃사람을 보며 말했다."], ["“And he may be the man.”", "\"그리고 그가 그 사람일지도 모르오.\""]], [["Before he could say more, his coat-tail was respectfully pulled by his attendant sprite with the gooseberry eyes. Mr.", "그가 미처 더 말하기 전에, 그의 웃옷 자락이, 구스베리 눈을 한 그의 시중드는 요정에게 삼가 잡아당겨졌다."], ["Bruff looked where the boy was looking.", "브러프 씨는 그 소년이 보고 있는 곳을 보았다. \"쉿!\""], ["“Hush!” he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“Here is Mr. Luker!”", "\"여기 루커 씨가 있소!\""]], [["The money-lender came out from the inner regions of the bank, followed by his two guardian policemen in plain clothes.", "그 돈놀이꾼이, 사복 차림의 그의 두 호위 경관을 거느리고, 그 은행의 안쪽 구역에서 나왔다."]], [["“Keep your eye on him,”", "\"그에게서 눈을 떼지 마시오,\""], ["whispered Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 속삭였다."], ["“If he passes the Diamond to anybody, he will pass it here.”", "\"만일 그가 누구에게든 그 다이아몬드를 건넨다면, 그는 그것을 여기서 건넬 것이오.\""]], [["Without noticing either of us, Mr. Luker slowly made his way to the door—now in the thickest, now in the thinnest part of the crowd.", "우리 둘 어느 쪽도 알아채지 못한 채, 루커 씨는—이제는 그 무리의 가장 빽빽한 곳에서, 이제는 가장 성긴 곳에서—천천히 그 문으로 나아갔다."], ["I distinctly saw his hand move, as he passed a short, stout man, respectably dressed in a suit of sober grey.", "나는 그가, 수수한 회색 정장을 점잖게 차려입은 키 작고 다부진 사내를 지나칠 때, 그의 손이 움직이는 것을 또렷이 보았다."], ["The man started a little, and looked after him.", "그 사내는 조금 소스라치며, 그의 뒤를 보았다."], ["Mr. Luker moved on slowly through the crowd.", "루커 씨는 그 무리 사이로 천천히 나아갔다."], ["At the door his guard placed themselves on either side of him.", "그 문에서 그의 호위가 그의 양옆에 자리했다."], ["They were all three followed by one of Mr. Bruff’s men—and I saw them no more.", "그들 셋은 죄 브러프 씨의 사람 하나에게 뒤따라졌다—그리고 나는 그들을 다시 보지 못했다."]], [["I looked round at the lawyer, and then looked significantly towards the man in the suit of sober grey.", "나는 그 변호사를 돌아보았고, 그런 다음 그 수수한 회색 정장의 사내를 향해 뜻깊게 보았다. \"그렇소!\""], ["“Yes!” whispered Mr.", "하고 브러프 씨가 속삭였다."], ["Bruff, “I saw it too!”", "\"나도 그것을 봤소!\""], ["He turned about, in search of his second man.", "그는 그의 둘째 사람을 찾아 돌아섰다."], ["The second man was nowhere to be seen.", "그 둘째 사람은 어디에도 보이지 않았다."], ["He looked behind him for his attendant sprite.", "그는 그의 시중드는 요정을 찾아 그의 뒤를 보았다."], ["Gooseberry had disappeared.", "구스베리가 사라져 있었다."]], [["“What the devil does it mean?”", "\"이게 대체 무슨 뜻이오?\""], ["said Mr. Bruff angrily.", "하고 브러프 씨가 성이 나서 말했다."], ["“They have both left us at the very time when we want them most.”", "\"그들이 둘 다, 우리가 그들을 가장 필요로 하는 바로 그때에 우리를 떠났소.\""]], [["It came to the turn of the man in the grey suit to transact his business at the counter.", "그 회색 정장의 사내가 그 계산대에서 그의 볼일을 볼 차례가 되었다."], ["He paid in a cheque—received a receipt for it—and turned to go out.", "그는 수표 한 장을 넣었다—그것에 대한 영수증을 받았다—그리고 나가려 돌아섰다."]], [["“What is to be done?”", "\"어찌해야 하오?\""], ["asked Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 물었다."], ["“We can’t degrade ourselves by following him.”", "\"우리는 그를 뒤따르는 것으로 우리 자신을 욕되게 할 수 없소.\""]], [["“I can!” I said.", "\"나는 할 수 있소!\""], ["“I wouldn’t lose sight of that man for ten thousand pounds!”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"나는 만 파운드를 준대도 저 사내에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠소!\""]], [["“In that case,”", "\"그런 경우,\""], ["rejoined Mr.", "하고 브러프 씨가 되받았다."], ["Bruff, “I wouldn’t lose sight of you, for twice the money.", "\"나는 그 두 배의 돈을 준대도 당신에게서 눈을 떼지 않겠소."], ["A nice occupation for a man in my position,”", "나 같은 처지의 사람에게 참 좋은 일거리로군,\""], ["he muttered to himself, as we followed the stranger out of the bank.", "하고 그는, 우리가 그 낯선 이를 뒤따라 그 은행을 나서면서, 자기 자신에게 중얼거렸다."], ["“For Heaven’s sake don’t mention it.", "\"제발 그것을 언급하지 마시오."], ["I should be ruined if it was known.”", "그것이 알려지면 나는 파멸이오.\""]], [["The man in the grey suit got into an omnibus, going westward.", "그 회색 정장의 사내는 서쪽으로 가는 승합마차에 올랐다."], ["We got in after him.", "우리는 그를 따라 올랐다."], ["There were latent reserves of youth still left in Mr.", "브러프 씨에게는 아직 잠재된 젊음의 밑천이 남아 있었다."], ["Bruff. I assert it positively—when he took his seat in the omnibus, he blushed!", "나는 그것을 단언한다—그가 그 승합마차에 그의 자리를 잡았을 때, 그는 낯을 붉혔다!"]], [["The man in the grey suit stopped the omnibus, and got out in Oxford Street.", "그 회색 정장의 사내는 그 승합마차를 세우고, 옥스퍼드가에서 내렸다."], ["We followed him again.", "우리는 다시 그를 뒤따랐다."], ["He went into a chemist’s shop.", "그는 약제사의 가게로 들어갔다."]], [["Mr. Bruff started.", "브러프 씨가 소스라쳤다."], ["“My chemist!”", "\"내 약제사요!\""], ["he exclaimed.", "하고 그가 외쳤다."], ["“I am afraid we have made a mistake.”", "\"안됐지만 우리가 잘못한 것 같소.\""]], [["We entered the shop. Mr.", "우리는 그 가게로 들어갔다."], ["Bruff and the proprietor exchanged a few words in private.", "브러프 씨와 그 주인이 사사로이 몇 마디를 나누었다."], ["The lawyer joined me again, with a very crestfallen face.", "그 변호사는 아주 풀 죽은 얼굴로 다시 나에게 합류했다."]], [["“It’s greatly to our credit,”", "\"그것이 크게 우리의 명예요,\""], ["he said, as he took my arm, and led me out—“that’s one comfort!”", "하고 그가, 내 팔을 잡고 나를 이끌어 내며 말했다. \"그것이 하나의 위안이오!\""]], [["“What is to our credit?”", "\"무엇이 우리의 명예요?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Mr. Blake!", "\"블레이크 씨!"], ["you and I are the two worst amateur detectives that ever tried their hands at the trade.", "당신과 나는 여태 이 장사에 손을 댄 가장 형편없는 두 아마추어 탐정이오."], ["The man in the grey suit has been thirty years in the chemist’s service.", "그 회색 정장의 사내는 그 약제사를 삼십 년 섬겼소."], ["He was sent to the bank to pay money to his master’s account—and he knows no more of the Moonstone than the babe unborn.”", "그는 제 주인의 계좌에 돈을 넣으러 그 은행에 보내진 것이오—그리고 그는 아직 세상에 나지 않은 갓난아기만큼도 그 월장석에 대해 아는 것이 없소.\""]], [["I asked what was to be done next.", "나는 다음으로 어찌해야 하는지 물었다."]], [["“Come back to my office,”", "\"내 사무소로 돌아갑시다,\""], ["said Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["“Gooseberry, and my second man, have evidently followed somebody else.", "\"구스베리와, 내 둘째 사람은, 뚜렷이 다른 누군가를 뒤따랐소."], ["Let us hope that they had their eyes about them at any rate!”", "어쨌든 그들이 눈을 밝히고 있었기를 바랍시다!\""]], [["When we reached Gray’s Inn Square, the second man had arrived there before us.", "우리가 그레이스 인 스퀘어에 다다르자, 그 둘째 사람이 우리보다 먼저 거기 다다라 있었다."], ["He had been waiting for more than a quarter of an hour.", "그는 십오 분 넘게 기다린 터였다."]], [["“Well!”", "\"자!\""], ["asked Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 물었다."], ["“What’s your news?”", "\"무슨 소식이오?\""]], [["“I am sorry to say, sir,”", "\"안됐지만, 나리,\""], ["replied the man, “that I have made a mistake.", "하고 그 사람이 대답했다. \"제가 잘못을 저질렀습니다."], ["I could have taken my oath that I saw Mr. Luker pass something to an elderly gentleman, in a light-coloured paletot.", "저는 루커 씨가, 밝은 빛깔의 외투 차림의 나이 든 신사에게 무언가를 건네는 것을 봤다고 맹세할 수 있었습니다."], ["The elderly gentleman turns out, sir, to be a most respectable master iron-monger in Eastcheap.”", "그 나이 든 신사는, 나리, 이스트칩의 더없이 점잖은 철물상으로 드러났습니다.\""]], [["“Where is Gooseberry?”", "\"구스베리는 어디 있소?\""], ["asked Mr. Bruff resignedly.", "하고 브러프 씨가 체념하여 물었다."]], [["The man stared.", "그 사람이 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["“I don’t know, sir.", "\"모르겠습니다, 나리."], ["I have seen nothing of him since I left the bank.”", "저는 그 은행을 떠난 이래 그를 전혀 못 봤습니다.\""]], [["Mr. Bruff dismissed the man.", "브러프 씨가 그 사람을 물렸다."], ["“One of two things,”", "\"둘 중 하나요,\""], ["he said to me.", "하고 그가 나에게 말했다."], ["“Either Gooseberry has run away, or he is hunting on his own account.", "\"구스베리가 달아났든지, 아니면 그가 제 나름으로 쫓고 있든지."], ["What do you say to dining here, on the chance that the boy may come back in an hour or two?", "그 소년이 한두 시간 안에 돌아올지 모른다는 요행을 노려, 여기서 저녁을 드는 것에 대해 어떻게 생각하오?"], ["I have got some good wine in the cellar, and we can get a chop from the coffee-house.”", "나에게 지하실에 좋은 포도주가 얼마쯤 있고, 우리는 그 커피점에서 고기 한 점을 얻을 수 있소.\""]], [["We dined at Mr. Bruff’s chambers.", "우리는 브러프 씨의 방에서 저녁을 들었다."], ["Before the cloth was removed, “a person” was announced as wanting to speak to the lawyer.", "상보가 걷히기 전에, \"어떤 사람\"이 그 변호사와 이야기하고 싶어 한다고 알려졌다."], ["Was the person Gooseberry?", "그 사람이 구스베리였을까?"], ["No: only the man who had been employed to follow Mr. Luker when he left the bank.", "아니, 그저 루커 씨가 그 은행을 떠날 때 그를 뒤따르도록 부려진 그 사람일 뿐이었다."]], [["The report, in this case, presented no feature of the slightest interest.", "이 경우의 그 보고는 조금도 관심 있는 면을 내놓지 않았다."], ["Mr. Luker had gone back to his own house, and had there dismissed his guard.", "루커 씨는 그 자신의 집으로 돌아가, 거기서 그의 호위를 물렸다."], ["He had not gone out again afterwards.", "그는 그 뒤로 다시 나가지 않았다."], ["Towards dusk, the shutters had been put up, and the doors had been bolted.", "땅거미 무렵, 덧문이 세워지고, 문들이 빗장 질러졌다."], ["The street before the house, and the alley behind the house, had been carefully watched.", "그 집 앞의 거리와, 그 집 뒤의 골목이 조심스레 지켜졌다."], ["No signs of the Indians had been visible.", "인도인들의 기색은 보이지 않았다."], ["No person whatever had been seen loitering about the premises.", "그 어떤 사람도 그 집 둘레를 어슬렁거리는 것이 보이지 않았다."], ["Having stated these facts, the man waited to know whether there were any further orders.", "이 사실들을 진술하고서, 그 사람은 무슨 더 이상의 명령이 있는지 알려 기다렸다."], ["Mr. Bruff dismissed him for the night.", "브러프 씨는 그 밤은 그를 물렸다."]], [["“Do you think Mr. Luker has taken the Moonstone home with him?”", "\"당신은 루커 씨가 그 월장석을 자기 집으로 가져갔다고 생각하오?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Not he,”", "\"그럴 리 없소,\""], ["said Mr. Bruff.", "하고 브러프 씨가 말했다."], ["“He would never have dismissed his two policemen, if he had run the risk of keeping the Diamond in his own house again.”", "\"만일 그가 그 다이아몬드를 다시 자기 집에 두는 위험을 무릅썼다면, 그는 결코 그의 두 경관을 물리지 않았을 것이오.\""]], [["We waited another half-hour for the boy, and waited in vain.", "우리는 그 소년을 반 시간 더 기다렸으나, 헛되이 기다렸다."], ["It was then time for Mr. Bruff to go to Hampstead, and for me to return to Rachel in Portland Place.", "그때는 브러프 씨가 햄스테드로 갈, 그리고 내가 포틀랜드 플레이스의 레이철에게로 돌아갈 때였다."], ["I left my card, in charge of the porter at the chambers, with a line written on it to say that I should be at my lodgings at half past ten, that night.", "나는 그 방의 문지기에게 맡겨, 그날 밤 열 시 반에 내 하숙에 있겠다는 한 줄을 적은 내 명함을 남겼다."], ["The card was to be given to the boy, if the boy came back.", "그 명함은, 만일 그 소년이 돌아오면, 그 소년에게 주어질 것이었다."]], [["Some men have a knack of keeping appointments; and other men have a knack of missing them.", "어떤 사람들은 약속을 지키는 재주가 있고, 다른 사람들은 그것을 어기는 재주가 있다."], ["I am one of the other men.", "나는 그 다른 사람들 중 하나다."], ["Add to this, that I passed the evening at Portland Place, on the same seat with Rachel, in a room forty feet long, with Mrs. Merridew at the further end of it.", "여기에다, 내가 그 저녁을, 저 끝에 메리듀 부인을 둔 마흔 자 길이의 방에서, 레이철과 같은 자리에서 보냈음을 더하라."], ["Does anybody wonder that I got home at half past twelve instead of half past ten?", "내가 열 시 반이 아니라 열두 시 반에 집에 다다랐다고 누가 의아해하겠는가?"], ["How thoroughly heartless that person must be!", "그 사람은 얼마나 낱낱이 무정함이 틀림없으랴!"], ["And how earnestly I hope I may never make that person’s acquaintance!", "그리고 나는 내가 결코 그 사람과 아는 사이가 되지 않기를 얼마나 간절히 바라는가!"]], [["My servant handed me a morsel of paper when he let me in.", "내 하인이 나를 들이면서 나에게 종잇조각 하나를 건넸다."]], [["I read, in a neat legal handwriting, these words—“If you please, sir, I am getting sleepy.", "나는, 단정한 법률가풍 필적으로, 이 말들을 읽었다—\"죄송하지만, 나리, 저는 졸리고 있습니다."], ["I will come back tomorrow morning, between nine and ten.”", "저는 내일 아침, 아홉 시와 열 시 사이에 다시 오겠습니다.\""], ["Inquiry proved that a boy, with very extraordinary-looking eyes, had called, and presented my card and message, had waited an hour, had done nothing but fall asleep and wake up again, had written a line for me, and had gone home—after gravely informing the servant that “he was fit for nothing unless he got his night’s rest.”", "물어보니, 아주 유별난 생김새의 눈을 한 소년 하나가 찾아와, 내 명함과 전갈을 내놓고, 한 시간 기다리고, 잠들었다 다시 깨는 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않고, 나에게 한 줄을 적고, 집으로 갔음이 드러났다—그가 하인에게, \"제 밤의 안식을 얻지 못하면 저는 아무 쓸모가 없습니다\" 하고 근엄하게 알린 뒤에."]], [["At nine, the next morning, I was ready for my visitor.", "이튿날 아침 아홉 시에, 나는 내 방문객을 맞을 채비를 했다."], ["At half past nine, I heard steps outside my door.", "아홉 시 반에, 나는 내 문밖의 발소리를 들었다."], ["“Come in, Gooseberry!”", "\"들어와라, 구스베리!\""], ["I called out.", "하고 나는 소리쳤다."], ["“Thank you, sir,”", "\"고맙습니다, 선생,\""], ["answered a grave and melancholy voice.", "하고 근엄하고 우울한 목소리가 답했다."], ["The door opened.", "그 문이 열렸다."], ["I started to my feet, and confronted—Sergeant Cuff!", "나는 벌떡 일어나, 마주했다—커프 부장을!"]], [["“I thought I would look in here, Mr. Blake, on the chance of your being in town, before I wrote to Yorkshire,”", "\"당신이 시내에 있을지 모른다는 요행을 노려, 요크셔로 편지하기 전에, 여기 들여다볼까 했소, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 그 부장이 말했다."]], [["He was as dreary and as lean as ever.", "그는 여느 때와 다름없이 음울하고 여위었다."], ["His eyes had not lost their old trick (so subtly noticed in Betteredge’s Narrative) of “looking as if they expected something more from you than you were aware of yourself.”", "그의 눈은 (베터리지 이야기에서 그토록 미묘하게 눈여겨보아진) \"당신 자신이 자각하는 것보다 당신에게서 무언가를 더 기대하는 듯 보이는\" 그 옛 버릇을 잃지 않은 채였다."], ["But, so far as dress can alter a man, the great Cuff was changed beyond all recognition.", "허나, 옷차림이 사람을 바꿀 수 있는 한으로는, 그 위대한 커프는 알아볼 수 없게 변해 있었다."], ["He wore a broad-brimmed white hat, a light shooting jacket, white trousers, and drab gaiters.", "그는 챙 넓은 흰 모자, 가벼운 사냥 재킷, 흰 바지, 그리고 담갈색 각반을 걸치고 있었다."], ["He carried a stout oak stick.", "그는 튼튼한 참나무 지팡이를 들었다."], ["His whole aim and object seemed to be to look as if he had lived in the country all his life.", "그의 온 목표와 목적은 마치 그가 평생 시골에서 산 것처럼 보이는 것인 듯했다."], ["When I complimented him on his Metamorphosis, he declined to take it as a joke.", "내가 그의 변신을 칭찬하자, 그는 그것을 농담으로 받기를 마다했다."], ["He complained, quite gravely, of the noises and the smells of London.", "그는, 자못 근엄하게, 런던의 소음과 냄새에 대해 불평했다."], ["I declare I am far from sure that he did not speak with a slightly rustic accent!", "나는 정녕 그가 조금 촌스러운 억양으로 말하지 않았다고 결코 장담하지 못하겠다!"], ["I offered him breakfast.", "나는 그에게 아침 식사를 권했다."], ["The innocent countryman was quite shocked.", "그 순진한 시골 사람은 자못 충격을 받았다."], ["His breakfast hour was half-past six—and he went to bed with the cocks and hens!", "그의 아침 시각은 여섯 시 반이었다—그리고 그는 닭들과 함께 잠자리에 들었다!"]], [["“I only got back from Ireland last night,”", "\"나는 겨우 지난밤 아일랜드에서 돌아왔소,\""], ["said the Sergeant, coming round to the practical object of his visit, in his own impenetrable manner.", "하고 그 부장이, 그 자신의 뚫을 수 없는 태도로, 그의 방문의 실질적인 목적으로 돌아오며 말했다."], ["“Before I went to bed, I read your letter, telling me what has happened since my inquiry after the Diamond was suspended last year.", "\"잠자리에 들기 전에, 나는 지난해에 그 다이아몬드에 대한 내 조사가 중단된 이래 일어난 일을 나에게 이야기하는 당신의 편지를 읽었소."], ["There’s only one thing to be said about the matter on my side.", "그 일에 대해 내 편에서 할 말은 오직 하나뿐이오."], ["I completely mistook my case.", "나는 내 사건을 완전히 잘못 알았소."], ["How any man living was to have seen things in their true light, in such a situation as mine was at the time, I don’t profess to know.", "그때의 나 같은 상황에서, 살아 있는 어떤 사람이 사물을 그 참된 빛으로 볼 수 있었을지, 나는 안다고 짐짓 나서지 않소."], ["But that doesn’t alter the facts as they stand.", "허나 그것이 지금 그대로의 사실을 바꾸지는 않소."], ["I own that I made a mess of it. Not the first mess, Mr.", "나는 내가 그것을 죽 쒀 놨음을 인정하오."], ["Blake, which has distinguished my professional career!", "내 직업 이력을 두드러지게 한 첫 죽은 아니오, 블레이크 씨!"], ["It’s only in books that the officers of the detective force are superior to the weakness of making a mistake.”", "탐정대의 관원이 잘못을 저지르는 약함보다 위에 있는 것은 오직 책 속에서뿐이오.\""]], [["“You have come in the nick of time to recover your reputation,”", "\"당신은 당신의 명성을 되찾을 딱 좋은 때에 왔소,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr. Blake,”", "\"용서하시오, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["rejoined the Sergeant.", "하고 그 부장이 되받았다."], ["“Now I have retired from business, I don’t care a straw about my reputation.", "\"이제 나는 일에서 물러났으니, 나는 내 명성에 대해 지푸라기만큼도 개의치 않소."], ["I have done with my reputation, thank God!", "나는 내 명성과 볼일을 다했소, 하느님께 감사하게도!"], ["I am here, sir, in grateful remembrance of the late Lady Verinder’s liberality to me.", "나는 여기, 선생, 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 나에 대한 후함에 대한 고마운 기억에서 왔소."], ["I will go back to my old work—if you want me, and if you will trust me—on that consideration, and on no other.", "나는 나의 옛 일로 돌아가겠소—당신이 나를 원하고, 당신이 나를 믿는다면—그 헤아림에서, 그리고 다른 어떤 것에서도 아니게."], ["Not a farthing of money is to pass, if you please, from you to me.", "부디, 당신에게서 나에게로 한 푼의 돈도 오가지 않기로."], ["This is on honour. Now tell me, Mr.", "이것은 명예를 걸고 하는 것이오."], ["Blake, how the case stands since you wrote to me last.”", "자 나에게 말해 보시오, 블레이크 씨, 당신이 나에게 마지막으로 편지한 이래 그 사건이 어떤 상태인지.\""]], [["I told him of the experiment with the opium, and of what had occurred afterwards at the bank in Lombard Street.", "나는 그에게 그 아편 실험에 대해, 그리고 그 뒤에 롬바드가의 그 은행에서 일어난 일에 대해 말했다."], ["He was greatly struck by the experiment—it was something entirely new in his experience.", "그는 그 실험에 크게 놀랐다—그것은 그의 경험에 온전히 새로운 무엇이었다."], ["And he was particularly interested in the theory of Ezra Jennings, relating to what I had done with the Diamond, after I had left Rachel’s sitting-room, on the birthday night.", "그리고 그는, 내가 그 생일 밤에 레이철의 응접실을 떠난 뒤 그 다이아몬드를 어찌했는지에 관련된, 에즈라 제닝스의 이론에 각별히 관심을 두었다."]], [["“I don’t hold with Mr. Jennings that you hid the Moonstone,”", "\"나는 당신이 그 월장석을 감췄다는 제닝스 씨의 견해에는 동의하지 않소,\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 말했다."], ["“But I agree with him, that you must certainly have taken it back to your own room.”", "\"허나 나는, 당신이 틀림없이 그것을 당신 자신의 방으로 도로 가져갔음이 분명하다는 데에는 그와 뜻을 같이하오.\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서요?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."], ["“And what happened then?”", "\"그러고는 무슨 일이 일어났소?\""]], [["“Have you no suspicion yourself of what happened, sir?”", "\"당신 자신은 무슨 일이 일어났는지 아무 짐작도 없소, 선생?\""]], [["“None whatever.”", "\"전혀 없소.\""]], [["“Has Mr. Bruff no suspicion?”", "\"브러프 씨는 아무 짐작도 없소?\""]], [["“No more than I have.”", "\"나와 마찬가지로 없소.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff rose, and went to my writing-table.", "커프 부장은 일어나, 내 필기 탁자로 갔다."], ["He came back with a sealed envelope.", "그는 봉인된 봉투 하나를 들고 돌아왔다."], ["It was marked “Private;”", "그것은 \"친전\"이라 표시되어 있었다."], ["it was addressed to me; and it had the Sergeant’s signature in the corner.", "그것은 나에게 부쳐진 것이었다. 그리고 그것은 모퉁이에 그 부장의 서명을 지니고 있었다."]], [["“I suspected the wrong person, last year,”", "\"나는 지난해에 엉뚱한 사람을 의심했소,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said: “and I may be suspecting the wrong person now.", "\"그리고 나는 지금 엉뚱한 사람을 의심하고 있을지도 모르오."], ["Wait to open the envelope, Mr. Blake, till you have got at the truth.", "당신이 진실에 다다르기까지, 블레이크 씨, 그 봉투를 여는 것을 기다리시오."], ["And then compare the name of the guilty person, with the name that I have written in that sealed letter.”", "그런 다음 그 죄지은 사람의 이름을, 내가 그 봉인된 편지에 적어 둔 이름과 견주어 보시오.\""]], [["I put the letter into my pocket—and then asked for the Sergeant’s opinion of the measures which we had taken at the bank.", "나는 그 편지를 내 주머니에 넣었다—그런 다음 그 은행에서 우리가 취한 그 방책에 대한 그 부장의 견해를 물었다."]], [["“Very well intended, sir,”", "\"아주 좋은 뜻이었소, 선생,\""], ["he answered, “and quite the right thing to do.", "하고 그가 대답했다. \"그리고 아주 옳게 할 일이었소."], ["But there was another person who ought to have been looked after besides Mr. Luker.”", "허나 루커 씨 말고도 살펴봤어야 할 다른 사람이 있었소.\""]], [["“The person named in the letter you have just given to me?”", "\"당신이 방금 나에게 준 그 편지에 이름이 적힌 그 사람이오?\""]], [["“Yes, Mr. Blake, the person named in the letter.", "\"그렇소, 블레이크 씨, 그 편지에 이름이 적힌 그 사람이오."], ["It can’t be helped now.", "이제 어쩔 수 없소."], ["I shall have something to propose to you and Mr. Bruff, sir, when the time comes.", "나는 때가 오면, 선생, 당신과 브러프 씨에게 제안할 무언가가 있을 것이오."], ["Let’s wait, first, and see if the boy has anything to tell us that is worth hearing.”", "먼저 기다려, 그 소년이 우리에게 들려줄 값어치 있는 무언가가 있는지 봅시다.\""]], [["It was close on ten o’clock, and the boy had not made his appearance.", "거의 열 시가 다 되었으나, 그 소년은 나타나지 않은 채였다."], ["Sergeant Cuff talked of other matters.", "커프 부장은 다른 일들을 이야기했다."], ["He asked after his old friend Betteredge, and his old enemy the gardener.", "그는 그의 옛 벗 베터리지와, 그의 옛 원수인 그 정원사의 안부를 물었다."], ["In a minute more, he would no doubt have got from this, to the subject of his favourite roses, if my servant had not interrupted us by announcing that the boy was below.", "조금만 더 있었으면, 만일 내 하인이 그 소년이 아래에 있다고 알림으로써 우리를 끊지 않았다면, 그는 두말할 것 없이 이것에서 그가 아끼는 장미라는 주제로 넘어갔을 것이다."]], [["On being brought into the room, Gooseberry stopped at the threshold of the door, and looked distrustfully at the stranger who was in my company.", "그 방으로 데려와지자, 구스베리는 그 문지방에서 멈추어, 나와 함께 있는 그 낯선 이를 미심쩍게 보았다."], ["I told the boy to come to me.", "나는 그 소년에게 나에게 오라고 말했다."]], [["“You may speak before this gentleman,”", "\"너는 이 신사 앞에서 말해도 좋다,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["“He is here to assist me; and he knows all that has happened.", "\"그는 나를 돕기 위해 여기 있다. 그리고 그는 일어난 온갖 것을 안다."], ["Sergeant Cuff,”", "커프 부장,\" 하고 나는 덧붙였다."], ["I added, “this is the boy from Mr. Bruff’s office.”", "\"이 아이가 브러프 씨의 사무소에서 온 그 소년이오.\""]], [["In our modern system of civilisation, celebrity (no matter of what kind) is the lever that will move anything.", "우리의 근대 문명 체계에서, 명성은 (그것이 어떤 종류이든) 무엇이든 움직일 지렛대다."], ["The fame of the great Cuff had even reached the ears of the small Gooseberry.", "그 위대한 커프의 명성은 그 자그마한 구스베리의 귀에마저 다다른 터였다."], ["The boy’s ill-fixed eyes rolled, when I mentioned the illustrious name, till I thought they really must have dropped on the carpet.", "그 소년의 잘못 박힌 눈은, 내가 그 이름난 이름을 언급하자, 정녕 그것들이 그 융단에 떨어졌음이 틀림없다고 내가 생각할 만큼 굴렀다."]], [["“Come here, my lad,”", "\"이리 오너라, 얘야,\""], ["said the Sergeant, “and let’s hear what you have got to tell us.”", "하고 그 부장이 말했다. \"그리고 네가 우리에게 들려줄 것을 들어 보자.\""]], [["The notice of the great man—the hero of many a famous story in every lawyer’s office in London—appeared to fascinate the boy.", "그 위대한 사람—런던의 모든 변호사 사무소에서 숱한 이름난 이야기의 주인공인 이의 눈여겨봄이 그 소년을 홀리는 듯했다."], ["He placed himself in front of Sergeant Cuff, and put his hands behind him, after the approved fashion of a neophyte who is examined in his catechism.", "그는 커프 부장 앞에 자리하고, 제 교리문답을 시험받는 초심자의 정해진 방식대로, 그의 손을 뒤로 두었다."]], [["“What is your name?”", "\"네 이름이 무엇이냐?\""], ["said the Sergeant, beginning with the first question in the catechism.", "하고 그 부장이, 그 교리문답의 첫 물음으로 시작하며 말했다."]], [["“Octavius Guy,”", "\"옥타비어스 가이입니다,\""], ["answered the boy.", "하고 그 소년이 대답했다."], ["“They call me Gooseberry at the office because of my eyes.”", "\"그들은 제 눈 때문에 사무소에서 저를 구스베리라 부릅니다.\""]], [["“Octavius Guy, otherwise Gooseberry,”", "\"옥타비어스 가이, 다시 말해 구스베리,\""], ["pursued the Sergeant, with the utmost gravity, “you were missed at the bank yesterday.", "하고 그 부장이 더없는 근엄함으로 이어 갔다. \"너는 어제 그 은행에서 사라졌다."], ["What were you about?”", "무엇을 하고 있었느냐?\""]], [["“If you please, sir, I was following a man.”", "\"죄송하지만, 선생, 저는 한 사내를 뒤따르고 있었습니다.\""]], [["“Who was he?”", "\"그가 누구였느냐?\""]], [["“A tall man, sir, with a big black beard, dressed like a sailor.”", "\"뱃사람처럼 차려입은, 큰 검은 수염의 키 큰 사내였습니다, 선생.\""]], [["“I remember the man!”", "\"나는 그 사내를 기억하오!\""], ["I broke in. “Mr.", "하고 나는 끼어들었다."], ["Bruff and I thought he was a spy employed by the Indians.”", "\"브러프 씨와 나는 그가 인도인들에게 부려진 밀정이라고 생각했소.\""]], [["Sergeant Cuff did not appear to be much impressed by what Mr. Bruff and I had thought.", "커프 부장은 브러프 씨와 내가 생각한 것에 그리 놀라는 듯싶지 않았다."], ["He went on catechising Gooseberry.", "그는 구스베리를 계속 문답했다."]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""], ["he said—“and why did you follow the sailor?”", "하고 그가 말했다—\"그리고 너는 어째서 그 뱃사람을 뒤따랐느냐?\""]], [["“If you please, sir, Mr. Bruff wanted to know whether Mr. Luker passed anything to anybody on his way out of the bank. I saw Mr. Luker pass something to the sailor with the black beard.”", "\"죄송하지만, 선생, 브러프 씨가 루커 씨가 그 은행을 나서는 길에 누구에게든 무언가를 건네는지 알고 싶어 했습니다. 저는 루커 씨가 그 검은 수염의 뱃사람에게 무언가를 건네는 것을 봤습니다.\""]], [["“Why didn’t you tell Mr. Bruff what you saw?”", "\"어째서 네가 본 것을 브러프 씨에게 말하지 않았느냐?\""]], [["“I hadn’t time to tell anybody, sir, the sailor went out in such a hurry.”", "\"저는 아무에게도 말할 짬이 없었습니다, 선생, 그 뱃사람이 어찌나 서둘러 나갔던지요.\""]], [["“And you ran out after him—eh?”", "\"그래서 너는 그를 뒤쫓아 뛰어나갔다—응?\""]], [["“Yes, sir.”", "\"예, 선생.\""]], [["“Gooseberry,”", "\"구스베리,\""], ["said the Sergeant, patting his head, “you have got something in that small skull of yours—and it isn’t cotton-wool.", "하고 그 부장이 그의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 말했다. \"너는 그 자그마한 머릿속에 무언가를 지녔구나—그리고 그것은 솜뭉치가 아니다."], ["I am greatly pleased with you, so far.”", "나는, 여기까지, 너에게 크게 흡족하다.\""]], [["The boy blushed with pleasure.", "그 소년이 기뻐 낯을 붉혔다."], ["Sergeant Cuff went on.", "커프 부장이 이어 갔다."]], [["“Well?", "\"그래서?"], ["and what did the sailor do, when he got into the street?”", "그리고 그 뱃사람이 거리로 나갔을 때 무엇을 했느냐?\""]], [["“He called a cab, sir.”", "\"그가 삯마차를 불렀습니다, 선생.\""]], [["“And what did you do?”", "\"그리고 너는 무엇을 했느냐?\""]], [["“Held on behind, and run after it.”", "\"뒤에 매달려, 그것을 뒤쫓아 뛰었습니다.\""]], [["Before the Sergeant could put his next question, another visitor was announced—the head clerk from Mr. Bruff’s office.", "그 부장이 그의 다음 물음을 던지기 전에, 또 하나의 방문객이 알려졌다—브러프 씨의 사무소에서 온 수석 서기였다."]], [["Feeling the importance of not interrupting Sergeant Cuff’s examination of the boy, I received the clerk in another room.", "커프 부장의 그 소년에 대한 문답을 끊지 않는 것의 중요함을 느끼고, 나는 다른 방에서 그 서기를 맞았다. 그는 그의 고용주에 대한 나쁜 소식을 가지고 왔다."], ["He came with bad news of his employer.", "지난 이틀의 동요와 흥분이 브러프 씨에게 벅찬 것으로 드러났다."], ["The agitation and excitement of the last two days had proved too much for Mr. Bruff.", "그는 그날 아침 통풍의 발작과 함께 깨어났다."], ["He had awoke that morning with an attack of gout; he was confined to his room at Hampstead; and, in the present critical condition of our affairs, he was very uneasy at being compelled to leave me without the advice and assistance of an experienced person.", "그는 햄스테드의 그의 방에 갇혀 있었다. 그리고, 우리 일의 지금의 위태로운 상태에서, 그는 나를 경험 있는 사람의 조언과 도움 없이 두어야 하게 된 것에 아주 불안해했다."], ["The chief clerk had received orders to hold himself at my disposal, and was willing to do his best to replace Mr. Bruff.", "그 수석 서기는 나의 뜻대로 대기하라는 명령을 받았고, 기꺼이 브러프 씨를 대신하려 최선을 다하려 했다."]], [["I wrote at once to quiet the old gentleman’s mind, by telling him of Sergeant Cuff’s visit: adding that Gooseberry was at that moment under examination; and promising to inform Mr.", "나는 대뜸, 커프 부장의 방문을 알림으로써, 그 노신사의 마음을 잠재우려 편지했다. 구스베리가 그 순간 문답받고 있음을 덧붙이며, 그리고 그날 늦게 일어날 무엇이든, 몸소, 또는 편지로, 브러프 씨에게 알리겠다고 약속하며."], ["Bruff, either personally, or by letter, of whatever might occur later in the day. Having despatched the clerk to Hampstead with my note, I returned to the room which I had left, and found Sergeant Cuff at the fireplace, in the act of ringing the bell.", "그 서기를 내 쪽지와 함께 햄스테드로 보내고서, 나는 내가 떠났던 그 방으로 돌아가, 커프 부장이 그 벽난로에서, 막 종을 울리는 참인 것을 보았다."]], [["“I beg your pardon, Mr.", "\"용서하시오, 블레이크 씨,\""], ["Blake,” said the Sergeant.", "하고 그 부장이 말했다."], ["“I was just going to send word by your servant that I wanted to speak to you.", "\"나는 막 당신의 하인을 통해, 내가 당신과 이야기하고 싶다는 말을 보내려던 참이었소."], ["There isn’t a doubt on my mind that this boy—this most meritorious boy,”", "이 소년이—이 더없이 갸륵한 소년이,\" 하고 그 부장이 구스베리의 머리를 쓰다듬으며 덧붙였다."], ["added the Sergeant, patting Gooseberry on the head, “has followed the right man.", "\"옳은 사내를 뒤따랐다는 데에는 내 마음에 아무 의심도 없소."], ["Precious time has been lost, sir, through your unfortunately not being at home at half past ten last night.", "소중한 시간이, 선생, 당신이 안타깝게도 지난밤 열 시 반에 집에 없었던 탓에 잃어졌소."], ["The only thing to do, now, is to send for a cab immediately.”", "이제 할 유일한 일은, 대뜸 삯마차를 부르는 것이오.\""]], [["In five minutes more, Sergeant Cuff and I (with Gooseberry on the box to guide the driver) were on our way eastward, towards the City.", "오 분이 더 지나, 커프 부장과 나는 (마부를 안내하러 마부석에 구스베리를 태우고) 동쪽으로, 시티를 향해 가는 길에 있었다."]], [["“One of these days,”", "\"머잖아,\""], ["said the Sergeant, pointing through the front window of the cab, “that boy will do great things in my late profession.", "하고 그 부장이 그 삯마차의 앞창을 통해 가리키며 말했다. \"저 소년은 나의 옛 직업에서 큰일을 할 것이오."], ["He is the brightest and cleverest little chap I have met with, for many a long year past.", "그는 여러 해 만에 내가 만난 가장 총명하고 가장 영리한 자그마한 녀석이오."], ["You shall hear the substance, Mr. Blake, of what he told me while you were out of the room.", "당신은, 블레이크 씨, 당신이 그 방을 나가 있는 동안 그가 나에게 들려준 것의 알맹이를 듣게 될 것이오."], ["You were present, I think, when he mentioned that he held on behind the cab, and ran after it?”", "내 생각에 당신은, 그가 그 삯마차 뒤에 매달려, 그것을 뒤쫓아 뛰었다고 언급했을 때 자리에 있었지요?\""]], [["“Yes.”", "\"그렇소.\""]], [["“Well, sir, the cab went from Lombard Street to the Tower Wharf.", "\"자, 선생, 그 삯마차는 롬바드가에서 타워 부두로 갔소."], ["The sailor with the black beard got out, and spoke to the steward of the Rotterdam steamboat, which was to start next morning.", "그 검은 수염의 뱃사람이 내려, 이튿날 아침 떠나기로 되어 있던 그 로테르담 증기선의 사무장에게 이야기했소."], ["He asked if he could be allowed to go on board at once, and sleep in his berth over-night.", "그는 자기가 대뜸 승선하여, 하룻밤 자기 침상에서 잘 수 있게 허락받을 수 있는지 물었소."], ["The steward said, No.", "그 사무장은 아니라고 했소."], ["The cabins, and berths, and bedding were all to have a thorough cleaning that evening, and no passenger could be allowed to come on board, before the morning.", "선실과, 침상과, 침구가 그날 저녁 죄 철저히 청소되어야 하고, 어떤 승객도 아침 전에 승선하도록 허락될 수 없다고."], ["The sailor turned round, and left the wharf.", "그 뱃사람은 돌아서서, 그 부두를 떠났소."], ["When he got into the street again, the boy noticed for the first time, a man dressed like a respectable mechanic, walking on the opposite side of the road, and apparently keeping the sailor in view.", "그가 다시 거리로 나갔을 때, 그 소년은 처음으로, 점잖은 직공처럼 차려입은 사내 하나가, 그 길 맞은편에서 걸으며, 겉보기에 그 뱃사람을 눈에 두고 있음을 알아챘소."], ["The sailor stopped at an eating-house in the neighbourhood, and went in.", "그 뱃사람은 그 근방의 어느 식당에서 멈추어, 들어갔소."], ["The boy—not being able to make up his mind, at the moment—hung about among some other boys, staring at the good things in the eating-house window.", "그 소년은—그 순간 마음을 정할 수 없어—다른 소년들 몇몇 사이에서 어정거리며, 그 식당 창의 맛난 것들을 빤히 쳐다보았소."], ["He noticed the mechanic waiting, as he himself was waiting—but still on the opposite side of the street.", "그는 그 직공이, 그 자신이 기다리고 있듯, 기다리는 것을—허나 여전히 그 길 맞은편에서—알아챘소."], ["After a minute, a cab came by slowly, and stopped where the mechanic was standing.", "잠시 뒤, 삯마차 한 대가 천천히 지나와, 그 직공이 서 있는 곳에서 멈추었소."], ["The boy could only see plainly one person in the cab, who leaned forward at the window to speak to the mechanic.", "그 소년은 그 삯마차에서 오직 한 사람만 또렷이 볼 수 있었는데, 그가 그 직공에게 이야기하려 그 창에서 앞으로 몸을 기울였소."], ["He described that person, Mr. Blake, without any prompting from me, as having a dark face, like the face of an Indian.”", "그는 그 사람을, 블레이크 씨, 나에게서 아무 재촉도 없이, 인도인의 얼굴 같은, 거무스름한 얼굴을 지녔다고 서술했소.\""]], [["It was plain, by this time, that Mr. Bruff and I had made another mistake.", "이때쯤, 브러프 씨와 내가 또 하나의 잘못을 저질렀음이 뚜렷했다."], ["The sailor with the black beard was clearly not a spy in the service of the Indian conspiracy.", "그 검은 수염의 뱃사람은 명백히 그 인도인 음모에 부려진 밀정이 아니었다."], ["Was he, by any possibility, the man who had got the Diamond?", "그가, 혹시나, 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣은 사내였을까?"]], [["“After a little,”", "\"조금 뒤,\""], ["pursued the Sergeant, “the cab moved on slowly down the street.", "하고 그 부장이 이어 갔다. \"그 삯마차가 그 거리를 천천히 따라 나아갔소."], ["The mechanic crossed the road, and went into the eating-house.", "그 직공은 그 길을 건너, 그 식당으로 들어갔소."], ["The boy waited outside till he was hungry and tired—and then went into the eating-house, in his turn.", "그 소년은 그가 배고프고 지칠 때까지 밖에서 기다렸소—그런 다음, 제 차례로 그 식당으로 들어갔소."], ["He had a shilling in his pocket; and he dined sumptuously, he tells me, on a black-pudding, an eel-pie, and a bottle of ginger-beer.", "그에게는 주머니에 실링 하나가 있었소. 그리고 그는, 그가 나에게 말하기로, 선지 순대와, 뱀장어 파이와, 생강 맥주 한 병으로 진수성찬을 들었소."], ["What can a boy not digest?", "소년이 소화 못 할 것이 무엇이랴?"], ["The substance in question has never been found yet.”", "그 문제의 알맹이는 아직 찾아지지 않았소.\""]], [["“What did he see in the eating-house?”", "\"그가 그 식당에서 무엇을 봤소?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 나는 물었다."]], [["“Well, Mr. Blake, he saw the sailor reading the newspaper at one table, and the mechanic reading the newspaper at another.", "\"자, 블레이크 씨, 그는 그 뱃사람이 한 탁자에서 신문을 읽고, 그 직공이 다른 탁자에서 신문을 읽는 것을 봤소."], ["It was dusk before the sailor got up, and left the place.", "그 뱃사람이 일어나 그곳을 떠난 것은 땅거미가 진 뒤였소."], ["He looked about him suspiciously when he got out into the street.", "그는 거리로 나갔을 때 미심쩍게 그의 둘레를 둘러보았소."], ["The boy—being a boy—passed unnoticed.", "그 소년은—소년인지라—눈에 띄지 않고 지나쳤소."], ["The mechanic had not come out yet.", "그 직공은 아직 나오지 않은 채였소."], ["The sailor walked on, looking about him, and apparently not very certain of where he was going next.", "그 뱃사람은, 그의 둘레를 둘러보며, 겉보기에 그가 다음에 어디로 가는지 그리 확실하지 않은 채로 걸어 나아갔소."], ["The mechanic appeared once more, on the opposite side of the road.", "그 직공이 다시 한 번, 그 길 맞은편에 나타났소."], ["The sailor went on, till he got to Shore Lane, leading into Lower Thames Street.", "그 뱃사람은, 그가 로어 템스가로 이어지는 쇼어 레인에 이르기까지 나아갔소."], ["There he stopped before a public-house, under the sign of ‘The Wheel of Fortune,’ and, after examining the place outside, went in.", "거기서 그는 '운명의 수레바퀴'라는 간판 아래의 어느 술집 앞에서 멈추어, 그곳을 밖에서 살핀 뒤, 들어갔소."], ["Gooseberry went in too.", "구스베리도 들어갔소."], ["There were a great many people, mostly of the decent sort, at the bar.", "그 술청에는 아주 많은 사람이, 대개 점잖은 부류가 있었소."], ["‘The Wheel of Fortune’ is a very respectable house, Mr.", "'운명의 수레바퀴'는 아주 점잖은 집이오, 블레이크 씨."], ["Blake; famous for its porter and pork-pies.”", "그 흑맥주와 돼지고기 파이로 이름났소.\""]], [["The Sergeant’s digressions irritated me.", "그 부장의 곁길로 새는 것이 나를 부아 나게 했다."], ["He saw it; and confined himself more strictly to Gooseberry’s evidence when he went on.", "그가 그것을 보았다. 그리고 그는 이어 갈 때 더 엄격히 구스베리의 증언에 자신을 국한했다."]], [["“The sailor,”", "\"그 뱃사람은,\""], ["he resumed, “asked if he could have a bed.", "하고 그가 다시 이어 갔다. \"잠자리를 얻을 수 있는지 물었소."], ["The landlord said ‘No; they were full.’ The barmaid corrected him, and said ‘Number Ten was empty.’ A waiter was sent for to show the sailor to Number Ten.", "그 주인은 '아니오, 다 찼소' 하고 말했소."], ["Just before that, Gooseberry had noticed the mechanic among the people at the bar.", "그 술청 아가씨가 그를 바로잡아, '십 호가 비었어요' 하고 말했소."], ["Before the waiter had answered the call, the mechanic had vanished.", "그 뱃사람을 십 호로 안내하러 급사가 불려졌소. 바로 그 전에, 구스베리는 그 술청의 사람들 사이에서 그 직공을 알아챘소."], ["The sailor was taken off to his room.", "그 급사가 그 부름에 답하기 전에, 그 직공이 사라졌소."], ["Not knowing what to do next, Gooseberry had the wisdom to wait and see if anything happened.", "그 뱃사람은 그의 방으로 데려가졌소. 다음에 어찌할지 몰라, 구스베리는 슬기롭게 기다려 무슨 일이 일어나는지 보았소."], ["Something did happen.", "무언가가 정녕 일어났소."], ["The landlord was called for.", "그 주인이 불려졌소."], ["Angry voices were heard upstairs.", "성난 목소리가 위층에서 들렸소."], ["The mechanic suddenly made his appearance again, collared by the landlord, and exhibiting, to Gooseberry’s great surprise, all the signs and tokens of being drunk.", "그 직공이 별안간 다시 나타났는데, 그 주인에게 멱살이 잡히고, 구스베리의 큰 놀라움으로, 술 취한 온갖 표시와 조짐을 내보였소."], ["The landlord thrust him out at the door, and threatened him with the police if he came back.", "그 주인은 그를 문밖으로 밀쳐내고, 그가 다시 오면 경찰을 부르겠다고 으름장을 놓았소."], ["From the altercation between them, while this was going on, it appeared that the man had been discovered in Number Ten, and had declared with drunken obstinacy that he had taken the room.", "이것이 벌어지는 동안 그들 사이의 언쟁으로 보아, 그 사내가 십 호에서 발각되었고, 술 취한 고집으로 자기가 그 방을 잡았다고 우겼음이 드러났소."], ["Gooseberry was so struck by this sudden intoxication of a previously sober person, that he couldn’t resist running out after the mechanic into the street.", "구스베리는 앞서 말짱하던 사람의 이 별안간의 취기에 어찌나 놀랐던지, 그 직공을 뒤쫓아 거리로 뛰어나가는 것을 참을 수 없었소."], ["As long as he was in sight of the public-house, the man reeled about in the most disgraceful manner.", "그가 그 술집이 보이는 데 있는 한, 그 사내는 더없이 창피한 태로 비틀거렸소."], ["The moment he turned the corner of the street, he recovered his balance instantly, and became as sober a member of society as you could wish to see.", "그가 그 거리 모퉁이를 도는 순간, 그는 대뜸 그의 균형을 되찾고, 당신이 바랄 만큼 말짱한 사회의 일원이 되었소."], ["Gooseberry went back to ‘The Wheel of Fortune’ in a very bewildered state of mind.", "구스베리는 아주 어리둥절한 심사로 '운명의 수레바퀴'로 돌아갔소."], ["He waited about again, on the chance of something happening.", "그는 무언가가 일어날 요행을 노려 다시 어정거렸소."], ["Nothing happened; and nothing more was to be heard, or seen, of the sailor.", "아무 일도 일어나지 않았소. 그리고 그 뱃사람에 대해 더는 들리거나 보이는 것이 없었소."], ["Gooseberry decided on going back to the office.", "구스베리는 그 사무소로 돌아가기로 정했소."], ["Just as he came to this conclusion, who should appear, on the opposite side of the street as usual, but the mechanic again!", "그가 이 결론에 이르렀을 때, 여느 때처럼 그 길 맞은편에, 다름 아닌 그 직공이 다시 나타난 것이었소!"], ["He looked up at one particular window at the top of the public-house, which was the only one that had a light in it.", "그는 그 술집 꼭대기의, 유일하게 불이 켜진 한 특정한 창을 올려다보았소."], ["The light seemed to relieve his mind.", "그 불빛이 그의 마음을 놓이게 하는 듯했소."], ["He left the place directly.", "그는 대뜸 그곳을 떠났소."], ["The boy made his way back to Gray’s Inn—got your card and message—called—and failed to find you.", "그 소년은 그레이스 인으로 돌아가는 길을 갔소—당신의 명함과 전갈을 받았소—찾아갔소—그리고 당신을 찾는 데에 실패했소."], ["There you have the state of the case, Mr. Blake, as it stands at the present time.”", "거기에 그 사건의 상태가, 블레이크 씨, 지금 그대로 있소.\""]], [["“What is your own opinion of the case, Sergeant?”", "\"그 사건에 대한 당신 자신의 견해는 무엇이오, 부장?\""]], [["“I think it’s serious, sir.", "\"나는 그것이 심각하다고 생각하오, 선생."], ["Judging by what the boy saw, the Indians are in it, to begin with.”", "그 소년이 본 것으로 판단하건대, 우선, 인도인들이 거기 끼여 있소.\""]], [["“Yes. And the sailor is evidently the person to whom Mr.", "\"그렇소."], ["Luker passed the Diamond. It seems odd that Mr.", "그리고 그 뱃사람은 명백히 루커 씨가 그 다이아몬드를 건넨 그 사람이오."], ["Bruff, and I, and the man in Mr. Bruff’s employment, should all have been mistaken about who the person was.”", "브러프 씨와, 나와, 브러프 씨에게 부려진 그 사람이 죄 그 사람이 누구인지에 대해 잘못 안 것은 이상해 보이오.\""]], [["“Not at all, Mr. Blake.", "\"조금도 이상하지 않소, 블레이크 씨."], ["Considering the risk that person ran, it’s likely enough that Mr. Luker purposely misled you, by previous arrangement between them.”", "그 사람이 무릅쓴 위험을 헤아리면, 루커 씨가 그들 사이의 미리 짠 약속으로, 일부러 당신을 잘못 이끌었을 공산이 자못 크오.\""]], [["“Do you understand the proceedings at the public-house?”", "\"당신은 그 술집에서의 일을 이해하오?\" 하고 나는 물었다."], ["I asked. “The man dressed like a mechanic was acting of course in the employment of the Indians.", "\"그 직공처럼 차려입은 사내는 물론 인도인들에게 부려져 움직이고 있었소."], ["But I am as much puzzled to account for his sudden assumption of drunkenness as Gooseberry himself.”", "허나 나는 그의 별안간의 취한 척함을 설명하는 데에 구스베리 자신만큼이나 어리둥절하오.\""]], [["“I think I can give a guess at what it means, sir,”", "\"나는 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 짐작해 볼 수 있을 것 같소, 선생,\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 그 부장이 말했다."], ["“If you will reflect, you will see that the man must have had some pretty strict instructions from the Indians.", "\"당신이 곰곰이 생각하면, 당신은 그 사내가 인도인들에게서 자못 엄한 지시를 받았음이 틀림없음을 알 것이오."], ["They were far too noticeable themselves to risk being seen at the bank, or in the public-house—they were obliged to trust everything to their deputy.", "그들 자신은 그 은행이나 그 술집에서 보이는 위험을 무릅쓰기에 너무나 눈에 띄었소—그들은 온갖 것을 그들의 대리인에게 맡기지 않을 수 없었소."], ["Very good.", "좋소."], ["Their deputy hears a certain number named in the public-house, as the number of the room which the sailor is to have for the night—that being also the room (unless our notion is all wrong) which the Diamond is to have for the night, too.", "그들의 대리인은 그 술집에서 어떤 번호가, 그 뱃사람이 그 밤을 위해 잡을 방의 번호로 불리는 것을 듣소—그것은 또한 (우리 생각이 죄 틀리지 않다면) 그 다이아몬드가 그 밤을 위해 잡을 방이기도 하오."], ["Under those circumstances, the Indians, you may rely on it, would insist on having a description of the room—of its position in the house, of its capability of being approached from the outside, and so on.", "그런 상황에서, 인도인들은, 믿어도 좋소만, 그 방에 대한 서술—그것의 그 집에서의 위치, 그것이 밖에서 다가가질 수 있는지 따위를 얻기를 우겼을 것이오."], ["What was the man to do, with such orders as these?", "그 사내는 이런 명령을 받고, 무엇을 해야 했겠소?"], ["Just what he did!", "바로 그가 한 것이오!"], ["He ran upstairs to get a look at the room, before the sailor was taken into it.", "그는 그 뱃사람이 그 방으로 데려가지기 전에 그 방을 한번 보러 위층으로 뛰어 올라갔소."], ["He was found there, making his observations—and he shammed drunk, as the easiest way of getting out of the difficulty.", "그는 거기서, 그의 관찰을 하다가 발각되었소—그리고 그는 그 어려움에서 빠져나오는 가장 손쉬운 방도로, 취한 시늉을 했소."], ["That’s how I read the riddle.", "그것이 내가 그 수수께끼를 읽어 내는 방식이오."], ["After he was turned out of the public-house, he probably went with his report to the place where his employers were waiting for him.", "그가 그 술집에서 내쫓긴 뒤, 그는 아마 그의 보고를 가지고 그의 고용주들이 그를 기다리는 곳으로 갔을 것이오."], ["And his employers, no doubt, sent him back to make sure that the sailor was really settled at the public-house till the next morning.", "그리고 그의 고용주들은, 두말할 것 없이, 그 뱃사람이 정말로 이튿날 아침까지 그 술집에 자리 잡았는지 확실히 하러 그를 도로 보냈을 것이오."], ["As for what happened at ‘The Wheel of Fortune,’ after the boy left—we ought to have discovered that last night.", "그 소년이 떠난 뒤 '운명의 수레바퀴'에서 무슨 일이 일어났는지로 말하자면—우리는 그것을 지난밤에 알아냈어야 했소."], ["It’s eleven in the morning, now.", "지금은 아침 열한 시요."], ["We must hope for the best, and find out what we can.”", "우리는 최선을 바라고, 우리가 할 수 있는 것을 알아내야 하오.\""]], [["In a quarter of an hour more, the cab stopped in Shore Lane, and Gooseberry opened the door for us to get out.", "십오 분이 더 지나, 그 삯마차가 쇼어 레인에 멈추었고, 구스베리가 우리가 내리도록 그 문을 열었다."]], [["“All right?”", "\"괜찮으냐?\""], ["asked the Sergeant.", "하고 그 부장이 물었다."]], [["“All right,”", "\"괜찮습니다,\""], ["answered the boy.", "하고 그 소년이 대답했다."]], [["The moment we entered “The Wheel of Fortune” it was plain even to my inexperienced eyes that there was something wrong in the house.", "우리가 \"운명의 수레바퀴\"에 들어선 순간 그 집에 무언가 잘못된 것이 있음이 내 미숙한 눈에조차 뚜렷했다."]], [["The only person behind the counter at which the liquors were served, was a bewildered servant girl, perfectly ignorant of the business.", "술이 나오는 그 계산대 뒤의 유일한 사람은, 그 일에 완전히 무지한, 어리둥절한 하녀였다."], ["One or two customers, waiting for their morning drink, were tapping impatiently on the counter with their money.", "아침 술을 기다리는 손님 한둘이, 그들의 돈으로 그 계산대를 조바심을 내며 두드리고 있었다."], ["The barmaid appeared from the inner regions of the parlour, excited and preoccupied.", "그 술청 아가씨가 그 응접실의 안쪽 구역에서, 흥분하여 딴생각에 잠긴 채 나타났다."], ["She answered Sergeant Cuff’s inquiry for the landlord, by telling him sharply that her master was upstairs, and was not to be bothered by anybody.", "그녀는 커프 부장의 그 주인에 대한 물음에, 그녀의 주인이 위층에 있으며, 아무에게도 방해받아서는 안 된다고 날카롭게 말함으로써 답했다."]], [["“Come along with me, sir,”", "\"나와 함께 갑시다, 선생,\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, coolly leading the way upstairs, and beckoning to the boy to follow him.", "하고 커프 부장이, 태연히 위층으로 앞장서 가고, 그 소년에게 그를 따라오라 손짓하며 말했다."]], [["The barmaid called to her master, and warned him that strangers were intruding themselves into the house.", "그 술청 아가씨가 그녀의 주인을 불러, 낯선 이들이 그 집에 밀고 들어오고 있다고 그에게 경고했다."], ["On the first floor we were encountered by the landlord, hurrying down, in a highly irritated state, to see what was the matter.", "이 층에서 우리는, 무슨 일인지 보러, 자못 부아 난 상태로 서둘러 내려오는 그 주인과 마주쳤다."]], [["“Who the devil are you?", "\"당신들은 대체 누구요?"], ["and what do you want here?”", "그리고 여기서 무엇을 원하오?\""], ["he asked.", "하고 그가 물었다."]], [["“Keep your temper,”", "\"성미를 다스리시오,\""], ["said the Sergeant, quietly.", "하고 그 부장이 태연히 말했다."], ["“I’ll tell you who I am to begin with.", "\"우선 내가 누구인지 당신에게 말하겠소."], ["I am Sergeant Cuff.”", "나는 커프 부장이오.\""]], [["The illustrious name instantly produced its effect.", "그 이름난 이름이 대뜸 그 효과를 냈다."], ["The angry landlord threw open the door of a sitting-room, and asked the Sergeant’s pardon.", "그 성난 주인은 응접실 문을 활짝 열고, 그 부장의 용서를 빌었다."]], [["“I am annoyed and out of sorts, sir—that’s the truth,” he said.", "\"저는 부아가 나고 심사가 뒤틀렸습니다, 선생—그것이 사실입니다,\""], ["“Something unpleasant has happened in the house this morning.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 그 집에서 불쾌한 무언가가 일어났습니다."], ["A man in my way of business has a deal to upset his temper, Sergeant Cuff.”", "제 장사를 하는 사람에게는 그의 성미를 뒤집을 것이 많습니다, 커프 부장.\""]], [["“Not a doubt of it,”", "\"조금도 의심할 바 없소,\""], ["said the Sergeant.", "하고 그 부장이 말했다."], ["“I’ll come at once, if you will allow me, to what brings us here.", "\"당신이 허락한다면, 나는 대뜸 무엇이 우리를 여기 오게 했는지로 넘어가겠소."], ["This gentleman and I want to trouble you with a few inquiries, on a matter of some interest to both of us.”", "이 신사와 나는 우리 둘 다에게 얼마쯤 관심 있는 일로, 당신에게 몇 가지 물음의 수고를 끼치고 싶소.\""]], [["“Relating to what, sir?”", "\"무엇에 관련된 것입니까, 선생?\""], ["asked the landlord.", "하고 그 주인이 물었다."]], [["“Relating to a dark man, dressed like a sailor, who slept here last night.”", "\"지난밤 여기서 잔, 뱃사람처럼 차려입은 거무스름한 사내에 관련된 것이오.\""]], [["“Good God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["that’s the man who is upsetting the whole house at this moment!”", "그것이 바로 이 순간 온 집을 뒤집어 놓고 있는 그 사내입니다!\""], ["exclaimed the landlord.", "하고 그 주인이 외쳤다."], ["“Do you, or does this gentleman know anything about him?”", "\"당신이, 또는 이 신사가 그에 대해 무언가 아십니까?\""]], [["“We can’t be certain till we see him,”", "\"우리는 그를 볼 때까지 확신할 수 없소,\""], ["answered the Sergeant.", "하고 그 부장이 대답했다."]], [["“See him?”", "\"그를 봐요?\""], ["echoed the landlord.", "하고 그 주인이 되뇌었다."], ["“That’s the one thing that nobody has been able to do since seven o’clock this morning.", "\"그것이 바로 오늘 아침 일곱 시 이래 아무도 할 수 없었던 그 한 가지입니다."], ["That was the time when he left word, last night, that he was to be called.", "그것이, 그가 지난밤, 자기가 불려야 한다고 말을 남긴 그 시각이었습니다."], ["He was called—and there was no getting an answer from him, and no opening his door to see what was the matter.", "그는 정녕 불렸습니다—그런데 그에게서 아무 답도 얻어지지 않았고, 무슨 일인지 보러 그의 문을 여는 것도 안 됐습니다."], ["They tried again at eight, and they tried again at nine. No use!", "그들은 여덟 시에 다시 해 봤고, 아홉 시에 다시 해 봤습니다."], ["There was the door still locked—and not a sound to be heard in the room!", "소용없었습니다! 그 문은 여전히 잠긴 채였고—그 방에서 아무 소리도 들리지 않았습니다!"], ["I have been out this morning—and I only got back a quarter of an hour ago.", "저는 오늘 아침 나가 있었습니다—그리고 저는 겨우 십오 분 전에 돌아왔습니다."], ["I have hammered at the door myself—and all to no purpose.", "저 스스로 그 문을 두드려 봤습니다—그리고 죄 헛일이었습니다."], ["The potboy has gone to fetch a carpenter.", "급사 아이가 목수를 데리러 갔습니다."], ["If you can wait a few minutes, gentlemen, we will have the door opened, and see what it means.”", "만일 당신들이 몇 분 기다려 주신다면, 신사분들, 우리는 그 문을 열어, 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 볼 것입니다.\""]], [["“Was the man drunk last night?”", "\"그 사내가 지난밤 취해 있었소?\""], ["asked Sergeant Cuff.", "하고 커프 부장이 물었다."]], [["“Perfectly sober, sir—or I would never have let him sleep in my house.”", "\"완전히 말짱했습니다, 선생—그렇지 않았다면 저는 결코 그를 제 집에서 재우지 않았을 것입니다.\""]], [["“Did he pay for his bed beforehand?”", "\"그가 미리 그의 잠자리 값을 치렀소?\""]], [["“No.”", "\"아니요.\""]], [["“Could he leave the room in any way, without going out by the door?”", "\"그가 그 문으로 나가지 않고, 어떤 식으로든 그 방을 떠날 수 있었소?\""]], [["“The room is a garret,”", "\"그 방은 다락입니다,\""], ["said the landlord.", "하고 그 주인이 말했다."], ["“But there’s a trap-door in the ceiling, leading out on to the roof—and a little lower down the street, there’s an empty house under repair.", "\"허나 천장에, 지붕으로 나가는 뚜껑문이 하나 있습니다—그리고 그 거리 조금 아래에, 수리 중인 빈집이 하나 있습니다."], ["Do you think, Sergeant, the blackguard has got off in that way, without paying?”", "부장, 당신은 그 악당이 값을 치르지 않고 그런 식으로 달아났다고 생각하십니까?\""]], [["“A sailor,”", "\"뱃사람이라면,\""], ["said Sergeant Cuff, “might have done it—early in the morning, before the street was astir.", "하고 커프 부장이 말했다. \"그것을 할 수 있었을 것이오—이른 아침에, 거리가 움직이기 전에."], ["He would be used to climbing, and his head wouldn’t fail him on the roofs of the houses.”", "그는 기어오르는 데에 익숙할 것이고, 집 지붕 위에서 그의 머리가 어질하지 않을 것이오.\""]], [["As he spoke, the arrival of the carpenter was announced.", "그가 말하는 사이, 그 목수의 다다름이 알려졌다."], ["We all went upstairs, at once, to the top story.", "우리는 죄, 대뜸, 그 꼭대기 층으로 올라갔다."], ["I noticed that the Sergeant was unusually grave, even for him.", "나는 그 부장이, 그로서도, 유달리 근엄한 것을 알아챘다."], ["It also struck me as odd that he told the boy (after having previously encouraged him to follow us), to wait in the room below till we came down again.", "또한 그가 그 소년에게 (앞서 그로 하여금 우리를 따라오도록 부추긴 뒤에) 우리가 다시 내려올 때까지 아래층 방에서 기다리라고 말한 것이 이상하게 나에게 와닿았다."]], [["The carpenter’s hammer and chisel disposed of the resistance of the door in a few minutes.", "그 목수의 망치와 끌이 몇 분 만에 그 문의 저항을 처리했다."], ["But some article of furniture had been placed against it inside, as a barricade.", "허나 어떤 세간 하나가 그 안쪽에서 그것에 대어져, 바리케이드로 세워져 있었다."], ["By pushing at the door, we thrust this obstacle aside, and so got admission to the room.", "그 문을 밀어, 우리는 이 장애물을 옆으로 밀치고, 그리하여 그 방에 들어갈 수 있었다. 그 주인이 먼저 들어갔다."], ["The landlord entered first; the Sergeant second; and I third.", "그 부장이 둘째로. 그리고 내가 셋째로."], ["The other persons present followed us.", "자리한 다른 사람들이 우리를 뒤따랐다."]], [["We all looked towards the bed, and all started.", "우리는 죄 그 침상을 향해 보았고, 죄 소스라쳤다."]], [["The man had not left the room.", "그 사내는 그 방을 떠나지 않은 채였다."], ["He lay, dressed, on the bed—with a white pillow over his face, which completely hid it from view.", "그는, 옷을 입은 채, 그 침상에 누워 있었다—그의 얼굴 위에, 그것을 완전히 눈에서 감추는 흰 베개를 얹은 채."]], [["“What does that mean?”", "\"저것이 무슨 뜻입니까?\""], ["said the landlord, pointing to the pillow.", "하고 그 주인이 그 베개를 가리키며 말했다."]], [["Sergeant Cuff led the way to the bed, without answering, and removed the pillow.", "커프 부장은, 답하지 않고, 그 침상으로 앞장서 가, 그 베개를 치웠다."]], [["The man’s swarthy face was placid and still; his black hair and beard were slightly, very slightly, discomposed.", "그 사내의 거무스름한 얼굴은 평온하고 고요했다. 그의 검은 머리칼과 수염은 조금, 아주 조금 흐트러져 있었다."], ["His eyes stared wide-open, glassy and vacant, at the ceiling.", "그의 눈은, 흐릿하고 텅 빈 채로, 그 천장을 향해 크게 뜬 채 노려보고 있었다."], ["The filmy look and the fixed expression of them horrified me.", "그것들의 그 뿌연 눈길과 그 고정된 표정이 나를 소름 끼치게 했다."], ["I turned away, and went to the open window.", "나는 몸을 돌려, 그 열린 창으로 갔다."], ["The rest of them remained, where Sergeant Cuff remained, at the bed.", "그들 나머지는, 커프 부장이 남은 곳, 그 침상에 남았다."]], [["“He’s in a fit!”", "\"그가 발작을 일으켰소!\""], ["I heard the landlord say.", "하고 나는 그 주인이 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“He’s dead,”", "\"그는 죽었소,\""], ["the Sergeant answered.", "하고 그 부장이 대답했다."], ["“Send for the nearest doctor, and send for the police.”", "\"가장 가까운 의사를 부르러, 그리고 경찰을 부르러 보내시오.\""]], [["The waiter was despatched on both errands.", "그 급사가 그 두 심부름에 보내졌다."], ["Some strange fascination seemed to hold Sergeant Cuff to the bed.", "어떤 이상한 홀림이 커프 부장을 그 침상에 붙드는 듯했다."], ["Some strange curiosity seemed to keep the rest of them waiting, to see what the Sergeant would do next.", "어떤 이상한 호기심이 그들 나머지를, 그 부장이 다음에 무엇을 하는지 보려 기다리게 하는 듯했다."]], [["I turned again to the window.", "나는 다시 그 창으로 돌아섰다."], ["The moment afterwards, I felt a soft pull at my coat-tails, and a small voice whispered, “Look here, sir!”", "다음 순간, 나는 내 웃옷 자락에 부드러운 잡아당김을 느꼈고, 자그마한 목소리가 속삭였다, \"여기 보십시오, 나리!\""]], [["Gooseberry had followed us into the room.", "구스베리가 우리를 따라 그 방으로 들어와 있었다."], ["His loose eyes rolled frightfully—not in terror, but in exultation.", "그의 헐거운 눈이 무섭게 굴렀다—공포에서가 아니라, 의기양양함에서."], ["He had made a detective-discovery on his own account.", "그는 제 나름으로 탐정의 발견을 한 것이었다."], ["“Look here, sir,”", "\"여기 보십시오, 나리,\""], ["he repeated—and led me to a table in the corner of the room.", "하고 그가 되뇌었다—그리고 나를 그 방 모퉁이의 탁자로 이끌었다."]], [["On the table stood a little wooden box, open, and empty.", "그 탁자에는, 열리고 빈, 자그마한 나무 상자 하나가 서 있었다."], ["On one side of the box lay some jewellers’ cotton.", "그 상자 한쪽에 얼마쯤의 보석상용 솜이 놓여 있었다."], ["On the other side, was a torn sheet of white paper, with a seal on it, partly destroyed, and with an inscription in writing, which was still perfectly legible.", "다른 쪽에는, 그 위에 봉인이 일부 부서진 채로 있고, 여전히 완벽히 읽을 수 있는 글씨로 된 새김이 있는, 찢어진 흰 종이 한 장이 있었다."], ["The inscription was in these words:", "그 새김은 이런 말로 되어 있었다."]], [["“Deposited with Messrs. Bushe, Lysaught, and Bushe, by Mr. Septimus Luker, of Middlesex Place, Lambeth, a small wooden box, sealed up in this envelope, and containing a valuable of great price. The box, when claimed, to be only given up by Messrs. Bushe and Co. on the personal application of Mr. Luker.”", "\"부시, 라이소트, 앤 부시 상회에, 램버스의 미들섹스 플레이스의 셉티머스 루커 씨에 의해, 이 봉투에 봉인되고, 큰 값어치의 귀중품을 담은 자그마한 나무 상자 하나가 예치됨. 그 상자는, 청구될 때, 오직 루커 씨의 직접 신청에 의해서만 부시 앤 코 상회에 의해 내어질 것.\""]], [["Those lines removed all further doubt, on one point at least.", "그 글줄들은, 적어도 한 가지 점에서는, 온갖 더 이상의 의심을 없앴다."], ["The sailor had been in possession of the Moonstone, when he had left the bank on the previous day.", "그 뱃사람은, 그가 그 전날 그 은행을 떠났을 때, 그 월장석을 지니고 있었던 것이다."]], [["I felt another pull at my coat-tails.", "나는 내 웃옷 자락에 또 한 번 잡아당김을 느꼈다."], ["Gooseberry had not done with me yet.", "구스베리는 아직 나와 볼일을 다하지 않은 채였다."]], [["“Robbery!”", "\"강도예요!\""], ["whispered the boy, pointing, in high delight, to the empty box.", "하고 그 소년이, 크게 기뻐하며, 그 빈 상자를 가리키며 속삭였다."]], [["“You were told to wait downstairs,”", "\"너는 아래층에서 기다리라고 일러졌다,\""], ["I said.", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["“Go away!”", "\"저리 가라!\""]], [["“And Murder!”", "\"그리고 살인이에요!\""], ["added Gooseberry, pointing, with a keener relish still, to the man on the bed.", "하고 구스베리가, 한층 더 예리하게 즐기며, 그 침상 위의 사내를 가리키며 덧붙였다."]], [["There was something so hideous in the boy’s enjoyment of the horror of the scene, that I took him by the two shoulders and put him out of the room.", "그 소년이 그 장면의 그 끔찍함을 즐기는 데에 어찌나 흉측한 무언가가 있었던지, 나는 그를 두 어깨로 잡아 그 방 밖으로 내보냈다."]], [["At the moment when I crossed the threshold of the door, I heard Sergeant Cuff’s voice, asking where I was.", "내가 그 문의 문지방을 넘는 그 순간, 나는 커프 부장의 목소리가, 내가 어디 있는지 묻는 것을 들었다."], ["He met me, as I returned into the room, and forced me to go back with him to the bedside.", "그는, 내가 그 방으로 돌아가면서, 나를 맞았고, 나로 하여금 그와 함께 그 침상 곁으로 돌아가게 했다."]], [["“Mr. Blake!”", "\"블레이크 씨!\""], ["he said.", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["“Look at the man’s face.", "\"그 사내의 얼굴을 보시오."], ["It is a face disguised—and here’s the proof of it!”", "그것은 변장한 얼굴이오—그리고 여기 그 증거가 있소!\""]], [["He traced with his finger a thin line of livid white, running backward from the dead man’s forehead, between the swarthy complexion, and the slightly-disturbed black hair.", "그는 그의 손가락으로, 그 죽은 사내의 이마에서 뒤로, 그 거무스름한 낯빛과 그 조금 흐트러진 검은 머리칼 사이로 뻗은, 검푸르뎅뎅한 흰 가느다란 선을 따라 짚었다."], ["“Let’s see what is under this,”", "\"이 아래에 무엇이 있는지 봅시다,\""], ["said the Sergeant, suddenly seizing the black hair, with a firm grip of his hand.", "하고 그 부장이, 별안간 그의 손으로 그 검은 머리칼을 단단히 움켜쥐며 말했다."]], [["My nerves were not strong enough to bear it.", "내 신경은 그것을 견딜 만큼 튼튼하지 못했다."], ["I turned away again from the bed.", "나는 다시 그 침상에서 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["The first sight that met my eyes, at the other end of the room, was the irrepressible Gooseberry, perched on a chair, and looking with breathless interest, over the heads of his elders, at the Sergeant’s proceedings.", "그 방의 다른 쪽 끝에서, 내 눈에 든 첫 광경은, 의자에 올라서서, 그의 웃어른들의 머리 너머로, 그 부장의 일을 숨죽인 관심으로 보고 있는, 그 억누를 수 없는 구스베리였다."]], [["“He’s pulling off his wig!”", "\"그가 그의 가발을 벗기고 있어요!\""], ["whispered Gooseberry, compassionating my position, as the only person in the room who could see nothing.", "하고 구스베리가, 그 방에서 아무것도 볼 수 없는 유일한 사람인 나의 처지를 가엾이 여겨 속삭였다."]], [["There was a pause—and then a cry of astonishment among the people round the bed.", "멈춤이 있었다—그런 다음 그 침상 둘레의 사람들 사이에 놀라움의 외침이 있었다."]], [["“He’s pulled off his beard!”", "\"그가 그의 수염을 벗겼어요!\""], ["cried Gooseberry.", "하고 구스베리가 외쳤다."]], [["There was another pause—Sergeant Cuff asked for something.", "또 한 번 멈춤이 있었다—커프 부장이 무언가를 청했다."], ["The landlord went to the wash-hand-stand, and returned to the bed with a basin of water and a towel.", "그 주인이 세면대로 가, 물 한 대야와 수건을 들고 그 침상으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Gooseberry danced with excitement on the chair.", "구스베리가 그 의자 위에서 흥분하여 춤추었다."], ["“Come up here, along with me, sir!", "\"이리 올라와요, 저와 함께요, 나리!"], ["He’s washing off his complexion now!”", "그가 이제 그의 낯빛을 씻어 내고 있어요!\""]], [["The Sergeant suddenly burst his way through the people about him, and came, with horror in his face, straight to the place where I was standing.", "그 부장이 별안간 그의 둘레의 사람들을 뚫고 나와, 얼굴에 소름을 담고, 곧장 내가 서 있는 곳으로 왔다."]], [["“Come back to the bed, sir!” he began.", "\"그 침상으로 돌아오시오, 선생!\""], ["He looked at me closer, and checked himself “No!”", "하고 그가 입을 뗐다. 그는 나를 더 가까이 보고, 스스로를 멈추었다."], ["he resumed.", "\"아니오!\""], ["“Open the sealed letter first—the letter I gave you this morning.”", "하고 그가 다시 이었다. \"먼저 그 봉인된 편지를 여시오—오늘 아침 내가 당신에게 준 그 편지를.\""]], [["I opened the letter.", "나는 그 편지를 열었다."]], [["“Read the name, Mr. Blake, that I have written inside.”", "\"그 이름을 읽으시오, 블레이크 씨, 내가 안에 적어 둔.\""]], [["I read the name that he had written.", "나는 그가 적어 둔 그 이름을 읽었다."], ["It was—Godfrey Ablewhite.", "그것은—고드프리 에이블화이트였다."]], [["“Now,”", "\"이제,\""], ["said the Sergeant, “come with me, and look at the man on the bed.”", "하고 그 부장이 말했다. \"나와 함께 와서, 그 침상 위의 사내를 보시오.\""]], [["I went with him, and looked at the man on the bed.", "나는 그와 함께 가서, 그 침상 위의 사내를 보았다. 고드프리 에이블화이트였다!"]], [["Contributed by Sergeant Cuff.", "커프 부장이 기고함."]], [["# I", "# I"]], [["Dorking, Surrey, July 30th, 1849. To Franklin Blake, Esq.", "서리주 도킹, 1849년 7월 30일."], ["Sir,—I beg to apologise for the delay that has occurred in the production of the Report, with which I engaged to furnish you.", "프랭클린 블레이크 님께. 선생—제가 당신께 드리기로 한 그 보고서를 내는 데에 생긴 지체에 대해 사과드립니다."], ["I have waited to make it a complete Report; and I have been met, here and there, by obstacles which it was only possible to remove by some little expenditure of patience and time.", "저는 그것을 온전한 보고서로 만들려 기다렸습니다. 그리고 저는, 이곳저곳에서, 얼마쯤의 인내와 시간을 들여야만 치울 수 있는 장애에 부딪혔습니다."]], [["The object which I proposed to myself has now, I hope, been attained.", "제가 저 자신에게 세운 그 목표는 이제, 바라건대, 이루어졌습니다."], ["You will find, in these pages, answers to the greater part—if not all—of the questions, concerning the late Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, which occurred to your mind when I last had the honour of seeing you.", "당신은, 이 쪽들에서, 제가 지난번 당신을 뵙는 영예를 누렸을 때 당신의 마음에 떠오른, 고인이 된 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 관한 물음의 전부는 아니더라도—태반에 대한 답을 찾으실 것입니다."]], [["I propose to tell you—in the first place—what is known of the manner in which your cousin met his death; appending to the statement such inferences and conclusions as we are justified (according to my opinion) in drawing from the facts.", "저는 당신께 말씀드릴 작정입니다—먼저—당신의 사촌이 그의 죽음을 맞은 방식에 대해 알려진 것을. 그 진술에, (제 견해에 따라) 우리가 그 사실에서 이끌어 냄이 정당한 그런 미루어 짐작과 결론을 덧붙이면서."]], [["I shall then endeavour—in the second place—to put you in possession of such discoveries as I have made, respecting the proceedings of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite, before, during and after the time, when you and he met as guests at the late Lady Verinder’s country house.", "저는 그런 다음 애쓰겠습니다—둘째로—고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 행동에 관하여, 당신과 그가 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 시골집에서 손님으로 만난 그때 전과, 그동안과, 그 뒤에 대해, 제가 한 그런 발견을 당신께 지니게 해 드리는 것을."]], [["As to your cousin’s death, then, first.", "그러면, 당신의 사촌의 죽음에 대해, 먼저."]], [["It appears to be established, beyond any reasonable doubt, that he was killed (while he was asleep, or immediately on his waking) by being smothered with a pillow from his bed—that the persons guilty of murdering him are the three Indians—and that the object contemplated (and achieved) by the crime, was to obtain possession of the diamond, called the Moonstone.", "어떤 합당한 의심의 너머로, 그가 (그가 잠든 동안, 또는 그가 깨어나는 즉시) 그의 침상의 베개로 질식되어 죽임을 당했다는 것—그를 살해한 죄지은 사람들이 그 인도인 셋이라는 것—그리고 그 범죄로 궁리된 (그리고 이루어진) 목표가, 월장석이라 불리는 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣는 것이었다는 것이 확정된 듯합니다."]], [["The facts from which this conclusion is drawn, are derived partly from an examination of the room at the tavern; and partly from the evidence obtained at the Coroner’s Inquest.", "이 결론이 이끌어 내지는 그 사실들은, 얼마쯤은 그 술집의 그 방에 대한 조사에서, 얼마쯤은 검시 심문에서 얻어진 증거에서 나옵니다."]], [["On forcing the door of the room, the deceased gentleman was discovered, dead, with the pillow of the bed over his face.", "그 방의 문을 억지로 열자, 그 고인이 된 신사가, 죽어, 그 침상의 베개를 그의 얼굴 위에 얹은 채 발견되었습니다."], ["The medical man who examined him, being informed of this circumstance, considered the post-mortem appearances as being perfectly compatible with murder by smothering—that is to say, with murder committed by some person, or persons, pressing the pillow over the nose and mouth of the deceased, until death resulted from congestion of the lungs.", "그를 검안한 의사가, 이 정황을 전해 듣고, 그 사후의 소견이 질식으로 인한 살인과—다시 말해, 어떤 사람, 또는 사람들이, 폐의 울혈로 죽음이 이르기까지 그 고인의 코와 입 위에 그 베개를 누른, 그런 살인과 완벽히 부합한다고 여겼습니다."]], [["Next, as to the motive for the crime.", "다음으로, 그 범죄의 동기에 대해."]], [["A small box, with a sealed paper torn off from it (the paper containing an inscription) was found open, and empty, on a table in the room. Mr.", "봉인된 종이가 (그 종이는 새김을 담고 있습니다) 뜯긴 자그마한 상자 하나가, 그 방의 탁자 위에 열리고 빈 채로 발견되었습니다."], ["Luker has himself personally identified the box, the seal, and the inscription.", "루커 씨는 몸소 그 상자와, 그 봉인과, 그 새김을 확인했습니다."], ["He has declared that the box did actually contain the diamond, called the Moonstone; and he has admitted having given the box (thus sealed up) to Mr.", "그는 그 상자가 정말로 월장석이라 불리는 그 다이아몬드를 담고 있었다고 선언했습니다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite (then concealed under a disguise), on the afternoon of the twenty-sixth of June last.", "그리고 그는 지난 유월 이십육일 오후에, (그렇게 봉인된) 그 상자를 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 (그때 변장 아래 숨은) 주었음을 인정했습니다."], ["The fair inference from all this is, that the stealing of the Moonstone was the motive of the crime.", "이 모든 것에서 나오는 정당한 미루어 짐작은, 그 월장석을 훔치는 것이 그 범죄의 동기였다는 것입니다."]], [["Next, as to the manner in which the crime was committed.", "다음으로, 그 범죄가 저질러진 방식에 대해."]], [["On examination of the room (which is only seven feet high), a trap-door in the ceiling, leading out on to the roof of the house, was discovered open.", "(높이가 겨우 일곱 자인) 그 방을 조사하니, 천장에, 그 집의 지붕으로 나가는 뚜껑문 하나가 열려 있는 것이 발견되었습니다."], ["The short ladder, used for obtaining access to the trap-door (and kept under the bed), was found placed at the opening, so as to enable any person or persons, in the room, to leave it again easily.", "그 뚜껑문에 다다르는 데에 쓰이는 (그리고 침상 아래 보관된) 그 짧은 사다리가, 그 방 안의 어떤 사람이나 사람들이 다시 쉽게 그것을 떠날 수 있게끔, 그 열린 곳에 놓인 채로 발견되었습니다."], ["In the trap-door itself was found a square aperture cut in the wood, apparently with some exceedingly sharp instrument, just behind the bolt which fastened the door on the inner side.", "그 뚜껑문 자체에는, 그 문을 안쪽에서 잠그는 그 빗장 바로 뒤에, 겉보기에 어떤 지극히 날카로운 도구로 그 나무에 잘린 네모난 구멍이 발견되었습니다."], ["In this way, any person from the outside could have drawn back the bolt, and opened the door, and have dropped (or have been noiselessly lowered by an accomplice) into the room—its height, as already observed, being only seven feet.", "이런 식으로, 밖의 어떤 사람이든 그 빗장을 도로 당겨, 그 문을 열고, 그 방으로 떨어질 (또는 공범에 의해 소리 없이 내려질) 수 있었을 것입니다—그것의 높이는, 이미 눈여겨보아졌듯, 겨우 일곱 자입니다."], ["That some person, or persons, must have got admission in this way, appears evident from the fact of the aperture being there.", "어떤 사람이나 사람들이 이런 식으로 들어갔음이 틀림없다는 것은, 그 구멍이 거기 있다는 사실로 뚜렷한 듯합니다."], ["As to the manner in which he (or they) obtained access to the roof of the tavern, it is to be remarked that the third house, lower down in the street, was empty, and under repair—that a long ladder was left by the workmen, leading from the pavement to the top of the house—and that, on returning to their work, on the morning of the 27th, the men found the plank which they had tied to the ladder, to prevent anyone from using it in their absence, removed, and lying on the ground.", "그가 (또는 그들이) 그 술집의 지붕에 다다른 방식에 대해서는, 그 거리 조금 아래의 셋째 집이 비고, 수리 중이었다는 것—긴 사다리 하나가 그 인도(人道)에서 그 집 꼭대기로 이어지도록 일꾼들에 의해 남겨져 있었다는 것—그리고 이십칠일 아침에 그들의 일로 돌아오면서, 그 일꾼들이, 그들이 없는 동안 아무도 그것을 쓰지 못하게 그 사다리에 매어 두었던 그 널판이 치워져, 바닥에 놓인 것을 발견했다는 것이 짚어져야 합니다."], ["As to the possibility of ascending by this ladder, passing over the roofs of the houses, passing back, and descending again, unobserved—it is discovered, on the evidence of the night policeman, that he only passes through Shore Lane twice in an hour, when out on his beat.", "이 사다리로 올라가, 집들의 지붕 위를 지나, 도로 지나와, 다시 눈에 띄지 않고 내려올 가능성에 대해서는—야간 경관의 증거로 보아, 그가 순찰을 도는 동안 한 시간에 오직 두 번만 쇼어 레인을 지난다는 것이 밝혀졌습니다."], ["The testimony of the inhabitants also declares, that Shore Lane, after midnight, is one of the quietest and loneliest streets in London.", "주민들의 증언도, 쇼어 레인이, 자정 뒤에는, 런던에서 가장 조용하고 가장 외진 거리의 하나임을 밝힙니다."], ["Here again, therefore, it seems fair to infer that—with ordinary caution, and presence of mind—any man, or men, might have ascended by the ladder, and might have descended again, unobserved.", "그러니, 여기서도 다시, 여느 조심과 침착만 있으면, 어떤 사람이나 사람들이 그 사다리로 올라가, 다시 눈에 띄지 않고 내려올 수 있었으리라 미루어 짐작함이 정당해 보입니다."], ["Once on the roof of the tavern, it has been proved, by experiment, that a man might cut through the trap-door, while lying down on it, and that in such a position, the parapet in front of the house would conceal him from the view of anyone passing in the street.", "일단 그 술집의 지붕 위에 오르면, 사람이 그 위에 엎드린 채 그 뚜껑문을 뚫을 수 있고, 그런 자세에서, 그 집 앞의 난간이 그를 거리를 지나는 누구의 눈에서든 가려 주리라는 것이, 실험으로 증명되었습니다."]], [["Lastly, as to the person, or persons, by whom the crime was committed.", "끝으로, 그 범죄를 저지른 사람, 또는 사람들에 대해."]], [["It is known (1) that the Indians had an interest in possessing themselves of the Diamond.", "(1) 인도인들이 그 다이아몬드를 손에 넣는 데에 관심이 있었음이 알려져 있습니다."], ["(2) It is at least probable that the man looking like an Indian, whom Octavius Guy saw at the window of the cab, speaking to the man dressed like a mechanic, was one of the three Hindoo conspirators. (3) It is certain that this same man dressed like a mechanic, was seen keeping Mr.", "(2) 옥타비어스 가이가 그 삯마차의 창에서, 그 직공처럼 차려입은 사내에게 이야기하는 것을 본, 인도인처럼 보이던 그 사내가, 그 힌두인 음모자 셋 중 하나였음이 적어도 있을 법합니다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite in view, all through the evening of the 26th, and was found in the bedroom (before Mr. Ablewhite was shown into it) under circumstances which lead to the suspicion that he was examining the room.", "(3) 이 같은 직공처럼 차려입은 사내가, 이십육일의 온 저녁 내내 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨를 눈에 두고 있는 것이 보였고, (에이블화이트 씨가 그 침실로 안내되기 전에) 그가 그 방을 살피고 있었다는 의심으로 이어지는 상황에서 그 침실에서 발견되었음이 확실합니다."], ["(4) A morsel of torn gold thread was picked up in the bedroom, which persons expert in such matters, declare to be of Indian manufacture, and to be a species of gold thread not known in England.", "(4) 찢어진 금실 조각 하나가 그 침실에서 주워졌는데, 그런 일에 정통한 사람들이, 그것을 인도산이며, 잉글랜드에 알려지지 않은 종류의 금실이라 선언합니다."], ["(5) On the morning of the 27th, three men, answering to the description of the three Indians, were observed in Lower Thames Street, were traced to the Tower Wharf, and were seen to leave London by the steamer bound for Rotterdam.", "(5) 이십칠일 아침, 그 인도인 셋의 서술에 들어맞는 세 사내가, 로어 템스가에서 목격되어, 타워 부두까지 추적되었고, 로테르담행 증기선으로 런던을 떠나는 것이 보였습니다."]], [["There is here, moral, if not legal, evidence, that the murder was committed by the Indians.", "여기에, 법률적이지는 않더라도, 도덕적인 증거가 있으니, 그 살인이 인도인들에 의해 저질러졌다는 것입니다."]], [["Whether the man personating a mechanic was, or was not, an accomplice in the crime, it is impossible to say.", "그 직공을 흉내 낸 사내가 그 범죄의 공범이었는지 아니었는지는, 말하기 불가능합니다."], ["That he could have committed the murder alone, seems beyond the limits of probability.", "그가 홀로 그 살인을 저지를 수 있었으리라는 것은, 있음 직함의 한계 너머로 보입니다."], ["Acting by himself, he could hardly have smothered Mr. Ablewhite—who was the taller and stronger man of the two—without a struggle taking place, or a cry being heard.", "홀로 움직여서는, 그가 좀처럼, 몸싸움이 벌어지거나 외침이 들리지 않고서, 에이블화이트 씨를—그 둘 중 더 크고 더 힘센 사내를—질식시킬 수 없었을 것입니다."], ["A servant girl, sleeping in the next room, heard nothing.", "옆방에서 자던 하녀는 아무것도 듣지 못했습니다."], ["The landlord, sleeping in the room below, heard nothing.", "아래층 방에서 자던 주인은 아무것도 듣지 못했습니다."], ["The whole evidence points to the inference that more than one man was concerned in this crime—and the circumstances, I repeat, morally justify the conclusion that the Indians committed it.", "온 증거는, 한 사내 넘게가 이 범죄에 관련되었다는 미루어 짐작을 가리킵니다—그리고 그 상황은, 거듭 말하거니와, 인도인들이 그것을 저질렀다는 결론을 도덕적으로 정당화합니다."]], [["I have only to add, that the verdict at the Coroner’s Inquest was Wilful Murder against some person, or persons, unknown.", "저는 다만, 그 검시 심문에서의 평결이 이름 모를 어떤 사람, 또는 사람들에 대한 고의적 살인이었음을 덧붙이면 됩니다."], ["Mr. Ablewhite’s family have offered a reward, and no effort has been left untried to discover the guilty persons.", "에이블화이트 씨의 집안은 현상금을 내걸었고, 그 죄지은 사람들을 알아내려 시도되지 않은 애씀이 없었습니다."], ["The man dressed like a mechanic has eluded all inquiries.", "그 직공처럼 차려입은 사내는 온갖 조사를 피했습니다."], ["The Indians have been traced.", "그 인도인들은 추적되었습니다."], ["As to the prospect of ultimately capturing these last, I shall have a word to say to you on that head, when I reach the end of the present Report.", "이 뒤엣사람들을 끝내 붙잡을 전망에 대해서는, 제가 이 보고서의 끝에 이르렀을 때, 그 대목에 대해 당신께 드릴 말씀이 있겠습니다."]], [["In the meanwhile, having now written all that is needful on the subject of Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s death, I may pass next to the narrative of his proceedings before, during, and after the time, when you and he met at the late Lady Verinder’s house.", "그동안, 이제 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 죽음이라는 주제에 대해 필요한 것을 죄 썼으니, 저는 다음으로, 당신과 그가 고인이 된 레이디 버린더의 집에서 만난 그때 전과, 그동안과, 그 뒤의 그의 행동의 이야기로 넘어가겠습니다."]], [["# III", "# III"]], [["With regard to the subject now in hand, I may state, at the outset, that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s life had two sides to it.", "지금 다루는 그 주제에 관하여, 저는, 첫머리에, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 삶에는 두 면이 있었음을 진술하겠습니다."]], [["The side turned up to the public view, presented the spectacle of a gentleman, possessed of considerable reputation as a speaker at charitable meetings, and endowed with administrative abilities, which he placed at the disposal of various Benevolent Societies, mostly of the female sort.", "세간의 눈에 드러난 그 면은, 자선 모임에서의 연사로서 상당한 명성을 지니고, 갖가지 자선 협회—대개 여성의 부류의 협회에 그가 마음대로 쓰도록 내놓은 행정 능력을 타고난 신사의 광경을 내놓았습니다."], ["The side kept hidden from the general notice, exhibited this same gentleman in the totally different character of a man of pleasure, with a villa in the suburbs which was not taken in his own name, and with a lady in the villa, who was not taken in his own name, either.", "세간의 눈에서 감추어진 그 면은, 이 같은 신사를, 그 자신의 이름으로 잡히지 않은 교외의 별장 하나와, 역시 그 자신의 이름으로 잡히지 않은, 그 별장의 부인 하나를 둔, 쾌락의 사람이라는 전혀 다른 인물로 내보였습니다."]], [["My investigations in the villa have shown me several fine pictures and statues; furniture tastefully selected, and admirably made; and a conservatory of the rarest flowers, the match of which it would not be easy to find in all London.", "그 별장에 대한 저의 조사는 저에게 몇 점의 훌륭한 그림과 조각상—고상하게 골라, 훌륭하게 만들어진 세간—그리고 온 런던에서 그 짝을 찾기가 쉽지 않을, 가장 진귀한 꽃들의 온실을 보여 주었습니다."], ["My investigation of the lady has resulted in the discovery of jewels which are worthy to take rank with the flowers, and of carriages and horses which have (deservedly) produced a sensation in the Park, among persons well qualified to judge of the build of the one, and the breed of the others.", "그 부인에 대한 저의 조사는, 그 꽃들과 어깨를 나란히 할 만한 보석과, (마땅히) 공원에서, 그 하나의 만듦새와, 그 다른 것들의 혈통을 판단하기에 넉넉히 자격 있는 사람들 사이에 화젯거리를 자아낸 마차와 말들의 발견으로 이어졌습니다."]], [["All this is, so far, common enough.", "이 모든 것은, 여기까지는, 자못 흔한 것입니다."], ["The villa and the lady are such familiar objects in London life, that I ought to apologise for introducing them to notice.", "그 별장과 그 부인은 런던 삶에서 그토록 낯익은 대상이라, 저는 그것들을 눈길에 소개하는 것에 대해 사과해야겠습니다."], ["But what is not common and not familiar (in my experience), is that all these fine things were not only ordered, but paid for.", "허나 흔하지 않고 낯익지 않은 것은 (제 경험으로는), 이 온갖 훌륭한 것들이 주문되었을 뿐 아니라, 값이 치러졌다는 것입니다."], ["The pictures, the statues, the flowers, the jewels, the carriages, and the horses—inquiry proved, to my indescribable astonishment, that not a sixpence of debt was owing on any of them.", "그 그림, 조각상, 꽃, 보석, 마차, 그리고 말들—조사는, 저의 이루 말할 수 없는 놀라움으로, 그것들 어느 것에도 육 펜스의 빚도 지지 않았음을 증명했습니다."], ["As to the villa, it had been bought, out and out, and settled on the lady.", "그 별장으로 말하자면, 그것은 통째로 사들여져, 그 부인에게 물려졌습니다."]], [["I might have tried to find the right reading of this riddle, and tried in vain—but for Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s death, which caused an inquiry to be made into the state of his affairs.", "저는 이 수수께끼의 옳은 읽기를 찾으려 했으나, 헛되이 했을 것입니다—고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 죽음이, 그의 일의 상태에 대한 조사가 이루어지게 하지 않았다면."]], [["The inquiry elicited these facts:—", "그 조사는 이 사실들을 이끌어 냈습니다—"]], [["That Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was entrusted with the care of a sum of twenty thousand pounds—as one of two Trustees for a young gentleman, who was still a minor in the year eighteen hundred and forty-eight.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 이만 파운드의 돈의 관리를 맡았다는 것—천팔백사십팔 년에 아직 미성년이던 어느 젊은 신사를 위한 두 신탁인 중 하나로서."], ["That the Trust was to lapse, and that the young gentleman was to receive the twenty thousand pounds on the day when he came of age, in the month of February, eighteen hundred and fifty.", "그 신탁이 소멸하기로 되어 있었고, 그 젊은 신사가 천팔백오십 년 이월에, 그가 성년이 되는 날 그 이만 파운드를 받기로 되어 있었다는 것."], ["That, pending the arrival of this period, an income of six hundred pounds was to be paid to him by his two Trustees, half-yearly—at Christmas and Midsummer Day.", "이 시기가 다다르기까지, 육백 파운드의 소득이 그의 두 신탁인에 의해 그에게, 반년마다—성탄절과 하지에 치러지기로 되어 있었다는 것."], ["That this income was regularly paid by the active Trustee, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "이 소득이 그 발 벗고 나선 신탁인, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 의해 어김없이 치러졌다는 것."], ["That the twenty thousand pounds (from which the income was supposed to be derived) had every farthing of it been sold out of the Funds, at different periods, ending with the end of the year eighteen hundred and forty-seven.", "(그 소득이 나온다고 여겨진) 그 이만 파운드가, 천팔백사십칠 년 말로 끝나는 여러 시기에, 그 한 푼도 남김없이 그 기금에서 팔려 나갔다는 것."], ["That the power of attorney, authorising the bankers to sell out the stock, and the various written orders telling them what amounts to sell out, were formally signed by both the Trustees.", "그 은행가들에게 그 주식을 팔도록 권한을 주는 위임장과, 그들에게 얼마의 액수를 팔지 이르는 갖가지 글로 된 명령이, 그 두 신탁인 모두에 의해 정식으로 서명되었다는 것."], ["That the signature of the second Trustee (a retired army officer, living in the country) was a signature forged, in every case, by the active Trustee—otherwise Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "그 둘째 신탁인의 (시골에 사는 퇴역 육군 장교의) 서명이, 어느 경우에나, 그 발 벗고 나선 신탁인—다시 말해 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에 의해 위조된 서명이었다는 것."]], [["In these facts lies the explanation of Mr. Godfrey’s honourable conduct, in paying the debts incurred for the lady and the villa—and (as you will presently see) of more besides.", "이 사실들에, 그 부인과 그 별장을 위해 진 빚을 갚은 고드프리 씨의 명예로운 처신에 대한—그리고 (당신이 곧 보시겠지만) 그 밖의 더 많은 것에 대한 설명이 놓여 있습니다."]], [["We may now advance to the date of Miss Verinder’s birthday (in the year eighteen hundred and forty-eight)—the twenty-first of June.", "우리는 이제 버린더 아가씨의 생일—(천팔백사십팔 년의) 유월 이십일일의 날짜로 나아가도 되겠습니다."]], [["On the day before, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite arrived at his father’s house, and asked (as I know from Mr.", "그 전날, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그의 아버지의 집에 다다라, (제가 손위 에이블화이트 씨 자신에게서 아는 대로) 삼백 파운드의 대여를 청했습니다."], ["Ablewhite, senior, himself) for a loan of three hundred pounds.", "그 액수를 눈여겨보십시오."], ["Mark the sum; and remember at the same time, that the half-yearly payment to the young gentleman was due on the twenty-fourth of the month.", "그리고 동시에, 그 젊은 신사에게의 반년치 지급이 그달 이십사일에 예정되어 있었음을 기억하십시오."], ["Also, that the whole of the young gentleman’s fortune had been spent by his Trustee, by the end of the year ’forty-seven.", "또한, 그 젊은 신사의 재산 전부가 그의 신탁인에 의해 사십칠 년 말로 다 쓰였음을."]], [["Mr. Ablewhite, senior, refused to lend his son a farthing.", "손위 에이블화이트 씨는 그의 아들에게 한 푼도 빌려주기를 마다했습니다."]], [["The next day Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite rode over, with you, to Lady Verinder’s house.", "이튿날 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 당신과 함께 레이디 버린더의 집으로 말을 몰았습니다."], ["A few hours afterwards, Mr. Godfrey (as you yourself have told me) made a proposal of marriage to Miss Verinder.", "몇 시간 뒤, 고드프리 씨가 (당신 자신이 저에게 말했듯이) 버린더 아가씨에게 혼인의 청을 했습니다."], ["Here, he saw his way no doubt—if accepted—to the end of all his money anxieties, present and future.", "여기서, 그는 두말할 것 없이—받아들여진다면—그의 현재와 미래의 온갖 돈 걱정의 끝으로 가는 그의 길을 보았습니다."], ["But, as events actually turned out, what happened?", "허나, 사건이 실제로 돌아간 대로, 무슨 일이 일어났습니까?"], ["Miss Verinder refused him.", "버린더 아가씨가 그를 거절했습니다."]], [["On the night of the birthday, therefore, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite’s pecuniary position was this.", "따라서, 그 생일 밤에, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 금전적 처지는 이러했습니다."], ["He had three hundred pounds to find on the twenty-fourth of the month, and twenty thousand pounds to find in February eighteen hundred and fifty.", "그는 그달 이십사일에 마련할 삼백 파운드와, 천팔백오십 년 이월에 마련할 이만 파운드가 있었습니다."], ["Failing to raise these sums, at these times, he was a ruined man.", "이 액수들을, 이 시기들에, 마련하는 데에 실패하면, 그는 파멸한 사람이었습니다."]], [["Under those circumstances, what takes place next?", "그런 상황에서, 다음으로 무슨 일이 일어납니까?"]], [["You exasperate Mr. Candy, the doctor, on the sore subject of his profession; and he plays you a practical joke, in return, with a dose of laudanum.", "당신이 그 의사 캔디 씨를, 그의 직업이라는 아픈 주제로 발끈하게 합니다."], ["He trusts the administration of the dose, prepared in a little phial, to Mr.", "그리고 그가, 답으로, 아편팅크 한 모금으로 당신에게 실질적인 장난을 부립니다."], ["Godfrey Ablewhite—who has himself confessed the share he had in the matter, under circumstances which shall presently be related to you. Mr.", "그는 자그마한 약병에 채비된 그 약의 투여를, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 맡깁니다—그 자신이 그 일에서 그가 맡은 몫을, 곧 당신께 이야기될 상황에서 털어놓았습니다."], ["Godfrey is all the readier to enter into the conspiracy, having himself suffered from your sharp tongue in the course of the evening.", "고드프리 씨는 그 음모에 들 그만큼 더 마음이 내켰으니, 그 자신이 그 저녁 동안 당신의 날카로운 혀에 시달렸기 때문입니다."], ["He joins Betteredge in persuading you to drink a little brandy and water before you go to bed.", "그는, 당신이 잠자리에 들기 전에 브랜디 물을 좀 마시라고 당신을 설득하는 데에 베터리지에게 합류합니다."], ["He privately drops the dose of laudanum into your cold grog.", "그는 사사로이 그 아편팅크 약을 당신의 찬 그로그에 떨어뜨립니다."], ["And you drink the mixture.", "그리고 당신은 그 섞인 것을 마십니다."]], [["Let us now shift the scene, if you please to Mr. Luker’s house at Lambeth.", "우리는 이제 장면을, 부디, 램버스의 루커 씨의 집으로 옮깁시다."], ["And allow me to remark, by way of preface, that Mr. Bruff and I, together, have found a means of forcing the money-lender to make a clean breast of it.", "그리고, 머리말 삼아, 브러프 씨와 제가, 함께, 그 돈놀이꾼으로 하여금 죄 털어놓게 만들 수단을 찾아냈음을 짚어 두도록 허락해 주십시오."], ["We have carefully sifted the statement he has addressed to us; and here it is at your service.", "우리는 그가 우리에게 부친 그 진술을 조심스레 가려냈습니다. 그리고 여기 그것이 당신을 위해 있습니다."]], [["Late on the evening of Friday, the twenty-third of June (’forty-eight), Mr. Luker was surprised by a visit from Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite. He was more than surprised, when Mr. Godfrey produced the Moonstone. No such Diamond (according to Mr. Luker’s experience) was in the possession of any private person in Europe.", "(사십팔 년) 유월 이십삼일 금요일 저녁 늦게, 루커 씨는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨의 방문에 놀랐습니다. 고드프리 씨가 그 월장석을 꺼냈을 때, 그는 놀란 것 이상이었습니다. (루커 씨의 경험에 따르면) 유럽의 어떤 개인도 그런 다이아몬드를 지니고 있지 않았습니다."]], [["Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite had two modest proposals to make, in relation to this magnificent gem.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 이 화려한 보석과 관련하여, 조촐한 두 가지 제안을 했습니다."], ["First, Would Mr. Luker be so good as to buy it?", "첫째, 루커 씨가 친절히 그것을 사 주겠는가?"], ["Secondly, Would Mr. Luker (in default of seeing his way to the purchase) undertake to sell it on commission, and to pay a sum down, on the anticipated result?", "둘째, 루커 씨가 (그 매입으로 갈 길이 보이지 않을 경우) 그것을 위탁으로 팔기를 맡아, 그 예상되는 결과에 대해 얼마쯤을 미리 치러 주겠는가?"]], [["Mr. Luker tested the Diamond, weighed the Diamond and estimated the value of the Diamond, before he answered a word.", "루커 씨는, 한마디 답하기 전에, 그 다이아몬드를 시험하고, 그 다이아몬드의 무게를 달고, 그 다이아몬드의 값어치를 어림했습니다."], ["His estimate (allowing for the flaw in the stone) was thirty thousand pounds.", "그의 어림은 (그 돌의 흠을 감안하여) 삼만 파운드였습니다."]], [["Having reached that result, Mr.", "그 결과에 이르러, 루커 씨는 그의 입을 열어, 물음 하나를 던졌습니다."], ["Luker opened his lips, and put a question: “How did you come by this?”", "\"이것을 어떻게 손에 넣었소?\""], ["Only six words!", "겨우 여섯 마디!"], ["But what volumes of meaning in them!", "허나 그 안에 얼마나 많은 뜻이 담겨 있는지!"]], [["Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite began a story.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 이야기를 시작했습니다."], ["Mr. Luker opened his lips again, and only said three words, this time.", "루커 씨는 다시 그의 입을 열어, 이번에는 오직 세 마디만 했습니다."], ["“That won’t do!”", "\"그건 안 되오!\""]], [["Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite began another story.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 또 하나의 이야기를 시작했습니다."], ["Mr. Luker wasted no more words on him.", "루커 씨는 그에게 더는 말을 허비하지 않았습니다."], ["He got up, and rang the bell for the servant to show the gentleman out.", "그는 일어나, 그 신사를 배웅하도록 하인을 부르는 종을 울렸습니다."]], [["Upon this compulsion, Mr. Godfrey made an effort, and came out with a new and amended version of the affair, to the following effect.", "이 강요를 받아, 고드프리 씨는 한 번 애를 써, 다음과 같은 취지로, 새롭고 고쳐진 그 일의 판(版)을 털어놓았습니다."]], [["After privately slipping the laudanum into your brandy and water, he wished you good-night, and went into his own room.", "당신의 브랜디 물에 몰래 그 아편팅크를 흘려 넣은 뒤, 그는 당신에게 잘 자라 인사하고, 그 자신의 방으로 들어갔습니다."], ["It was the next room to yours; and the two had a door of communication between them.", "그것은 당신의 방 옆방이었고, 그 둘은 그 사이에 통용문이 있었습니다."], ["On entering his own room Mr. Godfrey (as he supposed) closed his door.", "그 자신의 방에 들어가면서 고드프리 씨는 (그가 여긴 대로) 그의 문을 닫았습니다."], ["His money troubles kept him awake.", "그의 돈 걱정이 그를 깨어 있게 했습니다."], ["He sat, in his dressing-gown and slippers, for nearly an hour, thinking over his position.", "그는, 실내복과 슬리퍼 차림으로, 거의 한 시간 동안, 그의 처지를 곰곰이 생각하며 앉아 있었습니다."], ["Just as he was preparing to get into bed, he heard you, talking to yourself, in your own room, and going to the door of communication, found that he had not shut it as he supposed.", "막 잠자리에 들 채비를 하던 참에, 그는 당신이, 당신 자신의 방에서, 혼잣말하는 것을 들었고, 그 통용문으로 가서, 그가 여긴 대로 그것을 닫지 않았음을 알았습니다."]], [["He looked into your room to see what was the matter.", "그는 무슨 일인지 보러 당신의 방을 들여다보았습니다."], ["He discovered you with the candle in your hand, just leaving your bedchamber.", "그는 당신이 손에 초를 들고, 막 당신의 침실을 떠나는 것을 알아챘습니다."], ["He heard you say to yourself, in a voice quite unlike your own voice, “How do I know?", "그는 당신이, 당신 자신의 목소리와 자못 다른 목소리로, 혼잣말하는 것을 들었습니다, \"내가 어찌 알겠어?"], ["The Indians may be hidden in the house.”", "그 인도인들이 그 집에 숨어 있을지도 몰라.\""]], [["Up to that time, he had simply supposed himself (in giving you the laudanum) to be helping to make you the victim of a harmless practical joke.", "그때까지, 그는 (당신에게 그 아편팅크를 주면서) 그저 자기가 당신을 해롭지 않은 실질적인 장난의 희생물로 만드는 것을 돕고 있다고 여겼습니다."], ["It now occurred to him, that the laudanum had taken some effect on you, which had not been foreseen by the doctor, any more than by himself.", "이제 그에게, 그 아편팅크가 당신에게, 그 자신에게도 그렇듯 그 의사에게도 예견되지 않은 어떤 효과를 냈다는 것이 떠올랐습니다."], ["In the fear of an accident happening he followed you softly to see what you would do.", "사고가 일어날까 두려워 그는 당신이 무엇을 할지 보려 당신을 사뿐히 뒤따랐습니다."]], [["He followed you to Miss Verinder’s sitting-room, and saw you go in.", "그는 당신을 버린더 아가씨의 응접실까지 뒤따라, 당신이 들어가는 것을 보았습니다."], ["You left the door open.", "당신은 그 문을 열어 두었습니다."], ["He looked through the crevice thus produced, between the door and the post, before he ventured into the room himself.", "그는 그 자신이 감히 그 방으로 들어가기 전에, 그리하여 그 문과 문설주 사이에 생긴 그 틈으로 들여다보았습니다."]], [["In that position, he not only detected you in taking the Diamond out of the drawer—he also detected Miss Verinder, silently watching you from her bedroom, through her open door.", "그 자리에서, 그는 당신이 그 서랍에서 그 다이아몬드를 꺼내는 것을 알아챘을 뿐 아니라—그는 또한 버린더 아가씨가, 그녀의 열린 문을 통해, 그녀의 침실에서 말없이 당신을 지켜보는 것을 알아챘습니다."], ["His own eyes satisfied him that she saw you take the Diamond, too.", "그 자신의 눈이, 그녀도 당신이 그 다이아몬드를 가져가는 것을 보았음을 그에게 흡족히 여기게 했습니다."]], [["Before you left the sitting-room again, you hesitated a little. Mr.", "당신이 다시 그 응접실을 떠나기 전에, 당신은 조금 망설였습니다."], ["Godfrey took advantage of this hesitation to get back again to his bedroom before you came out, and discovered him.", "고드프리 씨는 이 망설임을 틈타, 당신이 나와 그를 알아채기 전에 다시 그의 침실로 돌아갔습니다."], ["He had barely got back, before you got back too.", "그가 겨우 돌아가자마자, 당신도 돌아갔습니다."], ["You saw him (as he supposes) just as he was passing through the door of communication.", "당신은 (그가 여기기로) 그가 막 그 통용문을 지나가는 참에 그를 보았습니다."], ["At any rate, you called to him in a strange, drowsy voice.", "어쨌든, 당신은 이상하고, 졸린 목소리로 그를 불렀습니다."]], [["He came back to you.", "그는 당신에게 돌아왔습니다."], ["You looked at him in a dull sleepy way.", "당신은 흐리멍덩하고 졸린 태로 그를 보았습니다."], ["You put the Diamond into his hand.", "당신은 그의 손에 그 다이아몬드를 쥐여 주었습니다."], ["You said to him, “Take it back, Godfrey, to your father’s bank.", "당신은 그에게 말했습니다, \"이걸 도로 가져가게, 고드프리, 자네 아버지의 은행으로."], ["It’s safe there—it’s not safe here.”", "그건 거기서 안전해—여기서는 안전하지 않아.\""], ["You turned away unsteadily, and put on your dressing-gown.", "당신은 불안하게 몸을 돌려, 당신의 실내복을 걸쳤습니다."], ["You sat down in the large arm-chair in your room.", "당신은 당신의 방의 그 큰 안락의자에 앉았습니다."], ["You said, “I can’t take it back to the bank.", "당신은 말했습니다, \"나는 그걸 은행으로 도로 가져갈 수 없어."], ["My head’s like lead—and I can’t feel my feet under me.”", "내 머리가 납덩이 같아—그리고 내 발이 나를 받치는 게 느껴지지 않아.\""], ["Your head sank on the back of the chair—you heaved a heavy sigh—and you fell asleep.", "당신의 머리가 그 의자 등받이에 내려앉았습니다—당신은 무겁게 한숨을 내쉬었습니다—그리고 당신은 잠들었습니다."]], [["Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite went back, with the Diamond, into his own room.", "고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는 그 다이아몬드를 지니고, 그 자신의 방으로 돌아갔습니다."], ["His statement is, that he came to no conclusion, at that time—except that he would wait, and see what happened in the morning.", "그의 진술은, 그가, 그때는, 아침에 무슨 일이 일어나는지 기다려 보겠다는 것 말고는—아무 결론에도 이르지 않았다는 것입니다."]], [["When the morning came, your language and conduct showed that you were absolutely ignorant of what you had said and done overnight.", "아침이 오자, 당신의 말과 처신은 당신이 밤사이 당신이 말하고 한 것을 절대적으로 모름을 보여 주었습니다."], ["At the same time, Miss Verinder’s language and conduct showed that she was resolved to say nothing (in mercy to you) on her side.", "동시에, 버린더 아가씨의 말과 처신은 그녀가 (당신에 대한 자비로) 그녀 편에서 아무 말도 하지 않기로 마음먹었음을 보여 주었습니다."], ["If Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite chose to keep the Diamond, he might do so with perfect impunity.", "만일 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그 다이아몬드를 간직하기로 정한다면, 그는 완전히 벌 받지 않고 그리할 수 있었습니다."], ["The Moonstone stood between him and ruin.", "그 월장석이 그와 파멸 사이에 서 있었습니다."], ["He put the Moonstone into his pocket.", "그는 그 월장석을 그의 주머니에 넣었습니다."]], [["# V", "# V"]], [["This was the story told by your cousin (under pressure of necessity) to Mr. Luker.", "이것이 (필요의 압박 아래) 당신의 사촌이 루커 씨에게 들려준 이야기였습니다."]], [["Mr. Luker believed the story to be, as to all main essentials, true—on this ground, that Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite was too great a fool to have invented it. Mr. Bruff and I agree with Mr. Luker, in considering this test of the truth of the story to be a perfectly reliable one.", "루커 씨는 그 이야기가, 온갖 주요한 알맹이로 보아, 참이라고 믿었습니다—이런 근거에서, 곧 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 그것을 지어냈다기엔 너무나 큰 바보였다는 것입니다. 브러프 씨와 저는, 그 이야기의 참됨에 대한 이 검증을 더없이 믿을 만한 것으로 여기는 데에, 루커 씨와 뜻을 같이합니다."]], [["The next question, was the question of what Mr. Luker would do in the matter of the Moonstone.", "다음 물음은, 루커 씨가 그 월장석의 일에서 무엇을 할지의 물음이었습니다."], ["He proposed the following terms, as the only terms on which he would consent to mix himself up with, what was (even in his line of business) a doubtful and dangerous transaction.", "그는 다음의 조건을, (그의 장사 방면에서조차) 미심쩍고 위태로운 거래인 것에 자신을 얽어 넣는 데에 동의할 유일한 조건으로 제안했습니다."]], [["Mr. Luker would consent to lend Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite the sum of two thousand pounds, on condition that the Moonstone was to be deposited with him as a pledge. If, at the expiration of one year from that date, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite paid three thousand pounds to Mr. Luker, he was to receive back the Diamond, as a pledge redeemed. If he failed to produce the money at the expiration of the year, the pledge (otherwise the Moonstone) was to be considered as forfeited to Mr. Luker—who would, in this latter case, generously make Mr. Godfrey a present of certain promissory notes of his (relating to former dealings) which were then in the money-lender’s possession.", "루커 씨는, 그 월장석이 그에게 저당으로 예치된다는 조건으로, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 이천 파운드의 돈을 빌려주는 데에 동의하겠습니다. 만일, 그 날짜로부터 한 해의 만기에, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨가 루커 씨에게 삼천 파운드를 치른다면, 그는 되찾아진 저당물로서 그 다이아몬드를 도로 받기로 되어 있었습니다. 만일 그가 그 해의 만기에 그 돈을 내는 데에 실패하면, 그 저당물은 (다시 말해 그 월장석은) 루커 씨에게 몰수된 것으로 여겨지기로 되어 있었습니다—이 뒤엣경우에, 그는 너그럽게 고드프리 씨에게, 그때 그 돈놀이꾼의 소유에 있던 그의 (예전 거래에 관련된) 어떤 약속 어음들을 선물로 줄 터였습니다."]], [["It is needless to say, that Mr. Godfrey indignantly refused to listen to these monstrous terms.", "고드프리 씨가 이 괴물 같은 조건을 들으려조차 분개하여 마다한 것은 굳이 말할 것도 없습니다."], ["Mr. Luker thereupon, handed him back the Diamond, and wished him good-night.", "이에 루커 씨는 그에게 그 다이아몬드를 도로 건네고, 그에게 잘 자라 인사했습니다."]], [["Your cousin went to the door, and came back again.", "당신의 사촌은 그 문으로 갔다가, 다시 돌아왔습니다."], ["How was he to be sure that the conversation of that evening would be kept strictly secret between his friend and himself?", "그 저녁의 대화가 그의 벗과 그 자신 사이에 엄격히 비밀로 지켜지리라는 것을 그가 어떻게 확신할 수 있는가?"]], [["Mr. Luker didn’t profess to know how.", "루커 씨는 어떻게인지 안다고 짐짓 나서지 않았습니다."], ["If Mr. Godfrey had accepted his terms, Mr.", "만일 고드프리 씨가 그의 조건을 받아들였다면, 고드프리 씨는 그를 공범으로 만들었을 것이고, 그의 침묵을 확실한 것으로 헤아릴 수 있었을 것입니다."], ["Godfrey would have made him an accomplice, and might have counted on his silence as on a certainty. As things were, Mr.", "사정이 이러하니, 루커 씨는 그 자신의 이해에 따라 움직여야 했습니다."], ["Luker must be guided by his own interests. If awkward inquiries were made, how could he be expected to compromise himself, for the sake of a man who had declined to deal with him?", "만일 거북한 조사가 이루어진다면, 그와 거래하기를 마다한 사람을 위해, 그가 어찌 자신을 위태롭게 하기를 기대받겠는가?"]], [["Receiving this reply, Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite did, what all animals (human and otherwise) do, when they find themselves caught in a trap.", "이 답을 받고, 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨는, 온갖 짐승들이 (사람이든 아니든) 덫에 걸린 것을 알았을 때 하는 것을 했습니다."], ["He looked about him in a state of helpless despair.", "그는 어쩔 도리 없는 절망의 상태로 그의 둘레를 둘러보았습니다."], ["The day of the month, recorded on a neat little card in a box on the money-lender’s chimney-piece, happened to attract his eye.", "그 돈놀이꾼의 벽난로 위의 상자 속의 단정한 자그마한 카드에 적힌 그달의 날짜가 우연히 그의 눈을 끌었습니다."], ["It was the twenty-third of June.", "그것은 유월 이십삼일이었습니다."], ["On the twenty-fourth he had three hundred pounds to pay to the young gentleman for whom he was trustee, and no chance of raising the money, except the chance that Mr. Luker had offered to him.", "이십사일에 그는 자기가 신탁인 노릇을 하는 그 젊은 신사에게 치를 삼백 파운드가 있었고, 루커 씨가 그에게 내놓은 그 기회 말고는 그 돈을 마련할 아무 기회도 없었습니다."], ["But for this miserable obstacle, he might have taken the Diamond to Amsterdam, and have made a marketable commodity of it, by having it cut up into separate stones.", "이 비참한 장애만 아니었다면, 그는 그 다이아몬드를 암스테르담으로 가져가, 그것을 여러 개의 돌로 쪼갬으로써 팔 만한 상품으로 만들 수 있었을 것입니다."], ["As matters stood, he had no choice but to accept Mr. Luker’s terms.", "사정이 그러하니, 그는 루커 씨의 조건을 받아들이는 것 말고는 도리가 없었습니다."], ["After all, he had a year at his disposal, in which to raise the three thousand pounds—and a year is a long time.", "어쨌든, 그에게는 그 삼천 파운드를 마련할 한 해가 마음대로 있었습니다—그리고 한 해는 긴 시간입니다."]], [["Mr. Luker drew out the necessary documents on the spot.", "루커 씨는 그 자리에서 필요한 문서를 작성했습니다."], ["When they were signed, he gave Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite two cheques.", "그것들이 서명되자, 그는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 수표 두 장을 주었습니다."], ["One, dated June 23rd, for three hundred pounds.", "하나는, 유월 이십삼일 자로, 삼백 파운드짜리."], ["Another, dated a week on, for the remaining balance—seventeen hundred pounds.", "다른 하나는, 일주일 뒤 자로, 남은 잔액—천칠백 파운드짜리."]], [["How the Moonstone was trusted to the keeping of Mr. Luker’s bankers, and how the Indians treated Mr. Luker and Mr. Godfrey (after that had been done) you know already.", "그 월장석이 어떻게 루커 씨의 은행가의 보관에 맡겨졌는지, 그리고 (그것이 이루어진 뒤) 인도인들이 어떻게 루커 씨와 고드프리 씨를 다루었는지는, 당신이 이미 아십니다."]], [["The next event in your cousin’s life refers again to Miss Verinder.", "당신의 사촌의 삶에서 다음 사건은 다시 버린더 아가씨에 관련됩니다."], ["He proposed marriage to her for the second time—and (after having being accepted) he consented, at her request, to consider the marriage as broken off.", "그는 두 번째로 그녀에게 혼인의 청을 했습니다—그리고 (받아들여진 뒤) 그녀의 청으로, 그 혼인을 깨진 것으로 여기는 데에 동의했습니다."], ["One of his reasons for making this concession has been penetrated by Mr.", "이 양보를 한 그의 이유 중 하나를 브러프 씨가 꿰뚫어 보았습니다."], ["Bruff. Miss Verinder had only a life interest in her mother’s property—and there was no raising the twenty thousand pounds on that.", "버린더 아가씨는 그녀의 어머니의 재산에 오직 종신 수익권만 있었을 뿐입니다—그리고 그것으로는 그 이만 파운드를 마련할 길이 없었습니다."]], [["But you will say, he might have saved the three thousand pounds, to redeem the pledged Diamond, if he had married.", "허나 당신은, 그가 혼인했다면 그 저당 잡힌 다이아몬드를 되찾을 그 삼천 파운드를 아낄 수 있었을 것이라고 말할 것입니다."], ["He might have done so certainly—supposing neither his wife, nor her guardians and trustees, objected to his anticipating more than half of the income at his disposal, for some unknown purpose, in the first year of his marriage.", "그는 정녕 그리할 수 있었을 것입니다—그의 아내도, 그녀의 후견인과 신탁인들도, 그가 혼인 첫해에 어떤 알 수 없는 목적으로 그가 마음대로 쓸 소득의 절반 넘게를 앞당겨 쓰는 것에 반대하지 않는다고 치면 말입니다."], ["But even if he got over this obstacle, there was another waiting for him in the background.", "허나 그가 이 장애를 넘는다 해도, 배경에서 그를 기다리는 또 하나가 있었습니다."], ["The lady at the Villa, had heard of his contemplated marriage.", "그 별장의 부인이 그가 궁리하는 혼인을 들은 것입니다."], ["A superb woman, Mr. Blake, of the sort that are not to be trifled with—the sort with the light complexion and the Roman nose.", "함부로 대할 수 없는 부류의—밝은 낯빛과 로마인 코를 한 부류의, 더없이 당당한 여자였습니다, 블레이크 씨."], ["She felt the utmost contempt for Mr. Godfrey Ablewhite.", "그녀는 고드프리 에이블화이트 씨에게 더없는 경멸을 느꼈습니다."], ["It would be silent contempt, if he made a handsome provision for her.", "만일 그가 그녀에게 넉넉한 살림을 마련해 준다면, 그것은 말 없는 경멸일 터였습니다."], ["Otherwise, it would be contempt with a tongue to it.", "그렇지 않으면, 그것은 혀 달린 경멸일 터였습니다."], ["Miss Verinder’s life interest allowed him no more hope of raising the “provision” than of raising the twenty thousand pounds.", "버린더 아가씨의 종신 수익권은 그에게 그 \"살림\"을 마련할 희망을 그 이만 파운드를 마련할 희망보다 조금도 더 주지 않았습니다."], ["He couldn’t marry—he really couldn’t marry, under all the circumstances.", "그는 혼인할 수 없었습니다—그는 온갖 상황에서, 정말로 혼인할 수 없었습니다."]], [["How he tried his luck again with another lady, and how that marriage also broke down on the question of money, you know already.", "그가 어떻게 다른 아가씨와 다시 그의 운을 시험했는지, 그리고 그 혼인도 어떻게 돈 문제로 깨졌는지는, 당신이 이미 아십니다."], ["You also know of the legacy of five thousand pounds, left to him shortly afterwards, by one of those many admirers among the soft sex whose good graces this fascinating man had contrived to win.", "당신은 또한, 이 매혹적인 사내가 그 호의를 얻어 낸, 그 무른 성(性)의 숱한 흠모자 가운데 하나에 의해, 그 뒤 곧 그에게 남겨진 오천 파운드의 유산에 대해서도 아십니다."], ["That legacy (as the event has proved) led him to his death.", "그 유산이 (사건이 증명했듯이) 그를 그의 죽음으로 이끌었습니다."]], [["I have ascertained that when he went abroad, on getting his five thousand pounds, he went to Amsterdam.", "저는, 그가 그의 오천 파운드를 얻어 외국으로 갔을 때, 그가 암스테르담으로 갔음을 알아냈습니다."], ["There he made all the necessary arrangements for having the Diamond cut into separate stones.", "거기서 그는 그 다이아몬드를 여러 개의 돌로 쪼개는 데에 필요한 온갖 채비를 했습니다."], ["He came back (in disguise), and redeemed the Moonstone, on the appointed day.", "그는 (변장하여) 돌아와, 그 정해진 날에 그 월장석을 되찾았습니다."], ["A few days were allowed to elapse (as a precaution agreed to by both parties) before the jewel was actually taken out of the bank.", "그 보석이 실제로 그 은행에서 꺼내지기 전에 (양편이 합의한 방비로서) 며칠이 흐르도록 두어졌습니다."], ["If he had got safe with it to Amsterdam, there would have been just time between July ’forty-nine, and February ’fifty (when the young gentleman came of age) to cut the Diamond, and to make a marketable commodity (polished or unpolished) of the separate stones.", "만일 그가 그것을 지니고 암스테르담에 무사히 이르렀다면, 사십구 년 칠월과, (그 젊은 신사가 성년이 되는) 오십 년 이월 사이에, 그 다이아몬드를 쪼개어, 그 여러 개의 돌로 (연마되었든 안 되었든) 팔 만한 상품을 만들 딱 그만한 시간이 있었을 것입니다."], ["Judge from this, what motives he had to run the risk which he actually ran. It was “neck or nothing”", "이것으로, 그가 실제로 무릅쓴 그 위험을 무릅쓴 데에 어떤 동기가 있었는지 판단해 보십시오."], ["with him—if ever it was “neck or nothing” with a man yet.", "그에게 그것은 \"죽기 아니면 살기\"였습니다—여태 어떤 사람에게 \"죽기 아니면 살기\"였던 적이 있다면 말입니다."]], [["I have only to remind you, before closing this Report, that there is a chance of laying hands on the Indians, and of recovering the Moonstone yet.", "저는 다만, 이 보고서를 닫기 전에, 아직 그 인도인들에게 손을 얹고, 그 월장석을 되찾을 기회가 있음을 당신께 일깨워 드리면 됩니다."], ["They are now (there is every reason to believe) on their passage to Bombay, in an East Indiaman.", "그들은 지금 (믿을 만한 온갖 근거가 있습니다) 동인도 무역선으로, 봄베이로 가는 항해 중에 있습니다."], ["The ship (barring accidents) will touch at no other port on her way out; and the authorities at Bombay (already communicated with by letter, overland) will be prepared to board the vessel, the moment she enters the harbour.", "그 배는 (사고가 없는 한) 나가는 길에 다른 어떤 항구에도 들르지 않을 것입니다. 그리고 봄베이의 당국은 (이미 육로로 편지로 연락되었습니다) 그 배가 항구에 들어서는 순간, 그 배에 오를 채비를 하고 있을 것입니다."]], [["I have the honour to remain, dear sir, your obedient servant, RICHARD CUFF (late sergeant in the Detective Force, Scotland Yard, London).*", "저는 삼가, 친애하는 선생, 당신의 순종하는 종으로 남는 영예를 지닙니다, 리처드 커프 (런던 스코틀랜드 야드 탐정대의 전 부장).*"]], [["* NOTE.—Wherever the Report touches on the events of the birthday, or of the three days that followed it, compare with Betteredge’s Narrative, chapters viii. to xiii.", "* 주(註).—이 보고서가 그 생일의, 또는 그것에 뒤이은 사흘의 사건들을 건드리는 곳은 어디든, 베터리지 이야기의 여덟째에서 열셋째 장과 견주어 보십시오."]], [["In a Letter from Mr. Candy.", "캔디 씨의 편지에서."]], [["Frizinghall, Wednesday, September 26th, 1849.—Dear Mr. Franklin Blake, you will anticipate the sad news I have to tell you, on finding your letter to Ezra Jennings returned to you, unopened, in this enclosure.", "프리징홀, 1849년 9월 26일 수요일.—친애하는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨, 당신은, 에즈라 제닝스에게 보낸 당신의 편지가 뜯기지 않은 채 이 봉투에 담겨 당신께 돌아온 것을 보고, 제가 당신께 전할 그 서글픈 소식을 미리 짐작하실 것입니다."], ["He died in my arms, at sunrise, on Wednesday last.", "그는 지난 수요일 해 뜰 녘에, 제 품에서 죽었습니다."]], [["I am not to blame for having failed to warn you that his end was at hand.", "그의 끝이 임박했음을 당신께 경고하지 못한 데에 대해 저는 탓받을 것이 없습니다."], ["He expressly forbade me to write to you.", "그는 제가 당신께 편지하는 것을 딱 잘라 금했습니다."], ["“I am indebted to Mr.", "\"나는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게,\""], ["Franklin Blake,”", "하고 그가 말했습니다."], ["he said, “for having seen some happy days.", "\"얼마쯤의 행복한 날들을 본 것을 빚졌소."], ["Don’t distress him, Mr. Candy—don’t distress him.”", "그를 괴롭히지 마시오, 캔디 씨—그를 괴롭히지 마시오.\""]], [["His sufferings, up to the last six hours of his life, were terrible to see.", "그의 괴로움은, 그의 삶의 마지막 여섯 시간까지, 보기에 끔찍했습니다."], ["In the intervals of remission, when his mind was clear, I entreated him to tell me of any relatives of his to whom I might write.", "뜸해지는 사이에, 그의 마음이 또렷할 때, 저는 그에게 제가 편지할 수 있을 그의 친척이 있으면 저에게 말해 달라고 간청했습니다."], ["He asked to be forgiven for refusing anything to me.", "그는 저에게 무엇이든 마다하는 것을 용서받기를 청했습니다."], ["And then he said—not bitterly—that he would die as he had lived, forgotten and unknown.", "그리고 그런 다음 그는—쓰라리지 않게—자기가 산 대로, 잊히고 알려지지 않은 채 죽겠다고 말했습니다."], ["He maintained that resolution to the last.", "그는 그 결심을 끝까지 지켰습니다."], ["There is no hope now of making any discoveries concerning him.", "이제 그에 관해 무슨 발견을 할 희망은 없습니다."], ["His story is a blank.", "그의 이야기는 백지입니다."]], [["The day before he died, he told me where to find all his papers.", "그가 죽기 전날, 그는 저에게 그의 온갖 서류를 어디서 찾을지 말해 주었습니다."], ["I brought them to him on his bed.", "저는 그것들을 그의 침상의 그에게 가져다주었습니다."], ["There was a little bundle of old letters which he put aside.", "그가 옆으로 밀어 놓은 낡은 편지 한 묶음이 있었습니다."], ["There was his unfinished book.", "그의 미완성 책이 있었습니다."], ["There was his Diary—in many locked volumes.", "그의 일기가—숱한 잠긴 권으로 있었습니다."], ["He opened the volume for this year, and tore out, one by one, the pages relating to the time when you and he were together.", "그는 올해의 권을 펴, 당신과 그가 함께 있던 그때에 관련된 쪽들을 하나씩 뜯어냈습니다. \"그것들을,\""], ["“Give those,”", "하고 그가 말했습니다."], ["he said, “to Mr. Franklin Blake.", "\"프랭클린 블레이크 씨에게 주시오."], ["In years to come, he may feel an interest in looking back at what is written there.”", "앞으로 오는 세월에, 그는 거기 적힌 것을 되돌아보는 데에 관심을 느낄지도 모르오.\""], ["Then he clasped his hands, and prayed God fervently to bless you, and those dear to you.", "그런 다음 그는 두 손을 맞잡고, 당신과, 당신에게 소중한 이들을 축복하시기를 하느님께 열렬히 기도했습니다."], ["He said he should like to see you again.", "그는 당신을 다시 보고 싶다고 말했습니다."], ["But the next moment he altered his mind.", "허나 다음 순간 그는 마음을 바꾸었습니다. \"아니오,\""], ["“No,” he answered when I offered to write.", "하고 그는 제가 편지하겠다고 하자 대답했습니다."], ["“I won’t distress him!", "\"나는 그를 괴롭히지 않겠소!"], ["I won’t distress him!”", "나는 그를 괴롭히지 않겠소!\""]], [["At his request I next collected the other papers—that is to say, the bundle of letters, the unfinished book and the volumes of the Diary—and enclosed them all in one wrapper, sealed with my own seal.", "그의 청으로 저는 다음으로 다른 서류들을—다시 말해, 그 편지 묶음과, 그 미완성 책과, 그 일기의 여러 권을 모아—그것들을 죄 한 싸개에 넣어, 저 자신의 봉인으로 봉했습니다. \"약속해 주시오,\""], ["“Promise,”", "하고 그가 말했습니다."], ["he said, “that you will put this into my coffin with your own hand; and that you will see that no other hand touches it afterwards.”", "\"당신 자신의 손으로 이것을 나의 관에 넣어 주겠다고. 그리고 그 뒤로 다른 어떤 손도 그것에 닿지 않게 지켜보겠다고.\""]], [["I gave him my promise.", "저는 그에게 저의 약속을 주었습니다."], ["And the promise has been performed.", "그리고 그 약속은 지켜졌습니다."]], [["He asked me to do one other thing for him—which it cost me a hard struggle to comply with.", "그는 저에게 그를 위해 다른 한 가지 일을 해 달라고 청했습니다—그것을 따르는 데에 저는 힘든 씨름을 치렀습니다."], ["He said, “Let my grave be forgotten.", "그가 말했습니다, \"나의 무덤이 잊히게 하시오."], ["Give me your word of honour that you will allow no monument of any sort—not even the commonest tombstone—to mark the place of my burial.", "어떤 종류의 기념비도—가장 흔한 묘비조차—나의 묻힌 자리를 표시하도록 허락하지 않겠다고 나에게 명예를 건 약속을 주시오. 나를 이름 없이 잠들게 하시오."], ["Let me sleep, nameless. Let me rest, unknown.”", "나를 알려지지 않은 채 쉬게 하시오.\""], ["When I tried to plead with him to alter his resolution, he became for the first, and only time, violently agitated.", "제가 그에게 그 결심을 바꾸라고 사정하려 하자, 그는 처음이자 유일하게, 격렬히 동요했습니다. 저는 그것을 차마 볼 수 없었습니다."], ["I could not bear to see it; and I gave way.", "그래서 저는 굴복했습니다."], ["Nothing but a little grass mound marks the place of his rest.", "자그마한 풀 봉분 말고는 아무것도 그의 안식의 자리를 표시하지 않습니다."], ["In time, the tombstones will rise round it.", "세월이 흐르면, 그 둘레에 묘비들이 솟아오를 것입니다."], ["And the people who come after us will look and wonder at the nameless grave.", "그리고 우리 뒤에 오는 사람들은 그 이름 없는 무덤을 보고 의아해할 것입니다."]], [["As I have told you, for six hours before his death his sufferings ceased.", "당신께 말씀드렸듯이, 그의 죽음 전 여섯 시간 동안 그의 괴로움이 그쳤습니다."], ["He dozed a little.", "그는 조금 졸았습니다."], ["I think he dreamed.", "제 생각에 그는 꿈을 꾸었습니다."], ["Once or twice he smiled.", "한두 번 그는 미소 지었습니다."], ["A woman’s name, as I suppose—the name of “Ella”—was often on his lips at this time.", "한 여자의 이름이, 제가 짐작하기로—\"엘라\"라는 이름이—이때 자주 그의 입술에 올랐습니다."], ["A few minutes before the end he asked me to lift him on his pillow, to see the sun rise through the window.", "끝 몇 분 전에 그는 저에게, 그 창을 통해 해가 뜨는 것을 보게, 그를 그의 베개 위로 일으켜 달라고 청했습니다."], ["He was very weak.", "그는 아주 약했습니다."], ["His head fell on my shoulder.", "그의 머리가 제 어깨에 떨어졌습니다."], ["He whispered, “It’s coming!”", "그가 속삭였습니다, \"그것이 오고 있어요!\""], ["Then he said, “Kiss me!” I kissed his forehead.", "그런 다음 그가 말했습니다, \"나에게 입 맞춰 주시오!\""], ["On a sudden he lifted his head.", "저는 그의 이마에 입 맞추었습니다."], ["The sunlight touched his face.", "별안간 그가 그의 머리를 들었습니다."], ["A beautiful expression, an angelic expression, came over it.", "그 햇빛이 그의 얼굴에 닿았습니다. 아름다운 표정이, 천사 같은 표정이 그 위에 번졌습니다."], ["He cried out three times, “Peace!", "그가 세 번 외쳤습니다, \"평화!"], ["peace!", "평화!"], ["peace!”", "평화!\""], ["His head sank back again on my shoulder, and the long trouble of his life was at an end.", "그의 머리가 다시 제 어깨에 내려앉았고, 그의 삶의 그 긴 고난이 끝났습니다."]], [["So he has gone from us.", "그리하여 그는 우리에게서 갔습니다."], ["This was, as I think, a great man—though the world never knew him.", "이 사람은, 제 생각에, 위대한 사람이었습니다—세상은 결코 그를 알지 못했지만."], ["He had the sweetest temper I have ever met with.", "그는 제가 여태 만난 가장 다정한 성미를 지녔습니다."], ["The loss of him makes me feel very lonely.", "그를 잃은 것이 저를 아주 외롭게 느끼게 합니다."], ["Perhaps I have never been quite myself since my illness.", "어쩌면 저는 저의 병 이래 아주 저 자신인 적이 없었는지도 모릅니다."], ["Sometimes, I think of giving up my practice, and going away, and trying what some of the foreign baths and waters will do for me.", "때로, 저는 저의 개업을 접고, 떠나서, 어떤 외국의 온천과 물이 저에게 무엇을 해 줄지 시험해 볼까 생각합니다."]], [["It is reported here, that you and Miss Verinder are to be married next month.", "여기서는, 당신과 버린더 아가씨가 다음 달에 혼인하기로 되어 있다는 소문이 돕니다."], ["Please to accept my best congratulations.", "부디 저의 진심 어린 축하를 받아 주십시오."]], [["The pages of my poor friend’s Journal are waiting for you at my house—sealed up, with your name on the wrapper.", "저의 가엾은 벗의 일기의 그 쪽들이 저의 집에서 당신을 기다리고 있습니다—봉해져, 그 싸개에 당신의 이름을 적은 채로."], ["I was afraid to trust them to the post.", "저는 그것들을 우편에 맡기기가 두려웠습니다."]], [["My best respects and good wishes attend Miss Verinder.", "저의 진심 어린 존경과 좋은 바람이 버린더 아가씨께 함께합니다."], ["I remain, dear Mr. Franklin Blake, truly yours,", "저는, 친애하는 프랭클린 블레이크 씨, 참으로 당신의 것으로 남습니다, 토머스 캔디."]], [["Contributed by Gabriel Betteredge.", "게이브리얼 베터리지가 기고함."]], [["I am the person (as you remember no doubt) who led the way in these pages, and opened the story.", "저는 (두말할 것 없이 당신이 기억하듯이) 이 쪽들에서 앞장서서, 그 이야기를 연 사람입니다."], ["I am also the person who is left behind, as it were, to close the story up.", "저는 또한, 말하자면, 그 이야기를 닫도록 뒤에 남겨진 사람입니다."]], [["Let nobody suppose that I have any last words to say here concerning the Indian Diamond.", "제가 여기서 그 인도 다이아몬드에 관해 무슨 마지막 말을 할 것이 있다고는 아무도 여기지 마십시오."], ["I hold that unlucky jewel in abhorrence—and I refer you to other authority than mine, for such news of the Moonstone as you may, at the present time, be expected to receive.", "저는 그 불운한 보석을 혐오하여 붙듭니다—그리고 지금으로서 당신이 받으리라 기대될 만한 그 월장석에 대한 소식으로는, 저 말고 다른 권위에게로 당신을 넘깁니다."], ["My purpose, in this place, is to state a fact in the history of the family, which has been passed over by everybody, and which I won’t allow to be disrespectfully smothered up in that way.", "이 자리에서의 저의 목적은, 모두에게 지나쳐진, 그리고 저는 그런 식으로 무례하게 묻혀 버리도록 두지 않을, 그 집안의 내력의 한 사실을 진술하는 것입니다."], ["The fact to which I allude is—the marriage of Miss Rachel and Mr. Franklin Blake.", "제가 넌지시 말하는 그 사실은—레이철 아가씨와 프랭클린 블레이크 씨의 혼인입니다."], ["This interesting event took place at our house in Yorkshire, on Tuesday, October ninth, eighteen hundred and forty-nine.", "이 흥미로운 사건은 우리 요크셔의 집에서, 천팔백사십구 년 시월 구일 화요일에 일어났습니다."], ["I had a new suit of clothes on the occasion.", "저는 이 자리에 새 정장 한 벌을 입었습니다."], ["And the married couple went to spend the honeymoon in Scotland.", "그리고 그 부부는 스코틀랜드에서 신혼여행을 보내러 갔습니다."]], [["Family festivals having been rare enough at our house, since my poor mistress’s death, I own—on this occasion of the wedding—to having (towards the latter part of the day) taken a drop too much on the strength of it.", "저의 가엾은 여주인의 죽음 이래 우리 집에서 집안 잔치가 자못 드물었던지라, 저는—이 혼인의 자리에—(그날의 후반 무렵) 그것을 빌미로 한 모금 지나치게 마셨음을 인정합니다."]], [["If you have ever done the same sort of thing yourself you will understand and feel for me.", "만일 당신이 여태 스스로 같은 종류의 일을 해 본 적이 있다면 당신은 이해하고 저를 헤아려 줄 것입니다."], ["If you have not, you will very likely say, “Disgusting old man!", "만일 그렇지 않다면, 당신은 십중팔구 이렇게 말할 것입니다, \"역겨운 늙은이!"], ["why does he tell us this?”", "어째서 그는 우리에게 이것을 말하는가?\""], ["The reason why is now to come.", "그 까닭이 이제 나옵니다."]], [["Having, then, taken my drop (bless you!", "그러니, 저의 한 모금을 마시고 (원 참!"], ["you have got your favourite vice, too; only your vice isn’t mine, and mine isn’t yours), I next applied the one infallible remedy—that remedy being, as you know, Robinson Crusoe.", "당신에게도 당신이 좋아하는 나쁜 버릇이 있지요. 다만 당신의 버릇이 제 것이 아니고, 제 것이 당신 것이 아닐 뿐입니다), 저는 다음으로 그 하나의 틀림없는 약을 썼습니다—그 약은, 아시다시피, 「로빈슨 크루소」입니다."], ["Where I opened that unrivalled book, I can’t say.", "제가 그 견줄 데 없는 책을 어디서 폈는지는, 저는 말할 수 없습니다."], ["Where the lines of print at last left off running into each other, I know, however, perfectly well.", "허나 그 인쇄된 글줄들이 마침내 서로 뒤섞여 흐르기를 그친 곳은, 저는 완벽히 잘 압니다."], ["It was at page three hundred and eighteen—a domestic bit concerning Robinson Crusoe’s marriage, as follows:", "그것은 삼백열여덟 쪽이었습니다—로빈슨 크루소의 혼인에 관한, 다음과 같은 집안일의 한 대목이었습니다."]], [["“With those Thoughts, I considered my new Engagement, that I had a Wife”—(Observe! so had Mr.", "\"그러한 생각과 더불어, 나는 나의 새로운 언약을, 곧 나에게 아내가 있다는 것을 헤아렸다\"—(눈여겨보십시오!"], ["Franklin!)—“one Child born”—(Observe again! that might yet be Mr.", "프랭클린 나리께도 그러했습니다!)—\"태어난 아이 하나가\"—(다시 눈여겨보십시오!"], ["Franklin’s case, too!)—“and my Wife then”—What Robinson Crusoe’s wife did, or did not do, “then,” I felt no desire to discover.", "그것도 아직 프랭클린 나리의 경우가 될 수 있었습니다!)—\"그리고 그때 나의 아내가\"—로빈슨 크루소의 아내가 \"그때\""], ["I scored the bit about the Child with my pencil, and put a morsel of paper for a mark to keep the place; “Lie you there,”", "했거나 하지 않은 것을, 저는 알아낼 아무 바람도 느끼지 못했습니다. 저는 그 아이에 관한 그 대목을 제 연필로 표시하고, 그 자리를 지키려 종잇조각 하나를 표로 끼웠습니다."], ["I said, “till the marriage of Mr.", "\"너는 거기 누워 있어라,\" 하고 저는 말했습니다."], ["Franklin and Miss Rachel is some months older—and then we’ll see!”", "\"프랭클린 나리와 레이철 아가씨의 혼인이 몇 달 더 묵을 때까지—그런 다음 우리가 보자!\""]], [["The months passed (more than I had bargained for), and no occasion presented itself for disturbing that mark in the book.", "그 달들이 지났습니다 (제가 셈한 것보다 더 많이). 그리고 그 책의 그 표를 건드릴 아무 계제도 나타나지 않았습니다."], ["It was not till this present month of November, eighteen hundred and fifty, that Mr. Franklin came into my room, in high good spirits, and said, “Betteredge!", "이 지금의 달, 천팔백오십 년 십일월에 이르러서야, 프랭클린 나리가 기분이 아주 좋아, 제 방으로 들어와, 말했습니다, \"베터리지!"], ["I have got some news for you!", "자네에게 알릴 소식이 있네!"], ["Something is going to happen in the house, before we are many months older.”", "우리가 몇 달 더 묵기 전에, 그 집에서 무슨 일이 일어날 참일세.\""]], [["“Does it concern the family, sir?”", "\"그것이 그 집안에 관련됩니까, 나리?\""], ["I asked.", "하고 저는 물었습니다."]], [["“It decidedly concerns the family,”", "\"그것은 정녕 그 집안에 관련되네,\""], ["says Mr. Franklin.", "하고 프랭클린 나리가 말합니다."]], [["“Has your good lady anything to do with it, if you please, sir?”", "\"부디, 나리, 나리의 착한 부인이 그것과 무슨 상관이 있습니까?\""]], [["“She has a great deal to do with it,”", "\"그녀가 그것과 아주 큰 상관이 있네,\""], ["says Mr. Franklin, beginning to look a little surprised.", "하고 프랭클린 나리가, 조금 놀란 낯빛을 하기 시작하며 말합니다."]], [["“You needn’t say a word more, sir,”", "\"한마디도 더 하실 것 없습니다, 나리,\""], ["I answered.", "하고 저는 대답했습니다."], ["“God bless you both!", "\"두 분을 하느님이 축복하시기를!"], ["I’m heartily glad to hear it.”", "저는 그것을 듣게 되어 진심으로 기쁩니다.\""]], [["Mr. Franklin stared like a person thunderstruck.", "프랭클린 나리는 벼락 맞은 사람처럼 빤히 쳐다보았습니다."], ["“May I venture to inquire where you got your information?” he asked.", "\"감히 자네가 어디서 그 정보를 얻었는지 물어봐도 되겠나?\""], ["“I only got mine (imparted in the strictest secrecy) five minutes since.”", "하고 그가 물었습니다. \"나는 겨우 오 분 전에 (더없이 엄격한 비밀로 전해져) 내 것을 얻었네.\""]], [["Here was an opportunity of producing Robinson Crusoe!", "여기 「로빈슨 크루소」를 내놓을 기회가 있었습니다!"], ["Here was a chance of reading that domestic bit about the child which I had marked on the day of Mr. Franklin’s marriage!", "여기 제가 프랭클린 나리의 혼인의 날에 표시해 둔 그 아이에 관한 그 집안일의 대목을 읽을 기회가 있었습니다!"], ["I read those miraculous words with an emphasis which did them justice, and then I looked him severely in the face.", "저는 그 기적 같은 말들을, 그것들에 마땅한 힘을 주어 읽고, 그런 다음 그를 엄히 얼굴을 마주 보았습니다."], ["“Now, sir, do you believe in Robinson Crusoe?”", "\"이제, 나리, 나리는 「로빈슨 크루소」를 믿으십니까?\""], ["I asked, with a solemnity suitable to the occasion.", "하고 저는 그 자리에 어울리는 엄숙함으로 물었습니다."]], [["“Betteredge!” says Mr.", "\"베터리지!\""], ["Franklin, with equal solemnity, “I’m convinced at last.”", "하고 프랭클린 나리가, 못지않은 엄숙함으로 말합니다. \"나는 마침내 확신하네.\""], ["He shook hands with me—and I felt that I had converted him.", "그는 저와 악수했습니다—그리고 저는 제가 그를 개종시켰음을 느꼈습니다."]], [["With the relation of this extraordinary circumstance, my reappearance in these pages comes to an end.", "이 유별난 정황의 이야기와 더불어, 이 쪽들에서의 저의 다시 나타남이 끝에 이릅니다."], ["Let nobody laugh at the unique anecdote here related.", "여기 이야기된 그 유일무이한 일화를 아무도 비웃지 마십시오."], ["You are welcome to be as merry as you please over everything else I have written.", "당신은 제가 쓴 다른 온갖 것에 대해서는 마음껏 즐거워해도 좋습니다."], ["But when I write of Robinson Crusoe, by the Lord it’s serious—and I request you to take it accordingly!", "허나 제가 「로빈슨 크루소」에 대해 쓸 때는, 맹세코 그것은 진지합니다—그리고 저는 당신이 그것을 그에 따라 받아들이기를 청합니다!"]], [["When this is said, all is said.", "이것이 말해졌으니, 온갖 것이 말해졌습니다."], ["Ladies and gentlemen, I make my bow, and shut up the story.", "신사 숙녀 여러분, 저는 고개 숙여 인사하고, 그 이야기를 닫습니다."]], [["On the twenty-seventh of June last, I received instructions from Sergeant Cuff to follow three men; suspected of murder, and described as Indians.", "지난 유월 이십칠일, 저는 커프 부장에게서, 살인의 혐의를 받고, 인도인으로 서술된 세 사내를 뒤따르라는 지시를 받았습니다."], ["They had been seen on the Tower Wharf that morning, embarking on board the steamer bound for Rotterdam.", "그들은 그날 아침 타워 부두에서, 로테르담행 증기선에 오르는 것이 목격된 터였습니다."]], [["I left London by a steamer belonging to another company, which sailed on the morning of Thursday the twenty-eighth.", "저는 다른 회사에 속한 증기선으로 런던을 떠났는데, 그것은 이십팔일 목요일 아침에 출항했습니다."], ["Arriving at Rotterdam, I succeeded in finding the commander of the Wednesday’s steamer.", "로테르담에 다다라, 저는 그 수요일 증기선의 선장을 찾는 데에 성공했습니다."], ["He informed me that the Indians had certainly been passengers on board his vessel—but as far as Gravesend only.", "그는 저에게, 그 인도인들이 정녕 그의 배의 승객이었으나—오직 그레이브젠드까지였을 뿐이라고 알려 주었습니다."], ["Off that place, one of the three had inquired at what time they would reach Calais.", "그곳 앞바다에서, 그 셋 중 하나가 그들이 몇 시에 칼레에 다다르는지 물었습니다."], ["On being informed that the steamer was bound to Rotterdam, the spokesman of the party expressed the greatest surprise and distress at the mistake which he and his two friends had made.", "그 증기선이 로테르담으로 가는 것임을 알게 되자, 그 일행의 대변인이 그와 그의 두 벗이 저지른 그 잘못에 더없는 놀라움과 괴로움을 나타냈습니다."], ["They were all willing (he said) to sacrifice their passage money, if the commander of the steamer would only put them ashore.", "그들은 죄 (그가 말하기로), 만일 그 증기선의 선장이 그저 그들을 뭍에 내려 주기만 한다면, 그들의 뱃삯을 버리려 했습니다."], ["Commiserating their position, as foreigners in a strange land, and knowing no reason for detaining them, the commander signalled for a shore boat, and the three men left the vessel.", "낯선 땅의 외국인으로서의 그들의 처지를 딱하게 여기고, 그들을 붙들 아무 이유도 알지 못하여, 그 선장은 뭍배에 신호했고, 그 세 사내가 그 배를 떠났습니다."]], [["This proceeding of the Indians having been plainly resolved on beforehand, as a means of preventing their being traced, I lost no time in returning to England.", "인도인들의 이 행동이, 그들이 추적되는 것을 막을 수단으로, 미리 뚜렷이 정해진 것이었으므로, 저는 지체 없이 잉글랜드로 돌아왔습니다."], ["I left the steamer at Gravesend, and discovered that the Indians had gone from that place to London.", "저는 그레이브젠드에서 그 증기선을 떠나, 그 인도인들이 그곳에서 런던으로 갔음을 알아냈습니다."], ["Thence, I again traced them as having left for Plymouth.", "거기서, 저는 다시 그들이 플리머스로 떠났음을 추적했습니다."], ["Inquiries made at Plymouth proved that they had sailed, forty-eight hours previously, in the Bewley Castle, East Indiaman, bound direct to Bombay.", "플리머스에서 이루어진 조사는, 그들이 마흔여덟 시간 전에, 봄베이로 곧장 가는 동인도 무역선 《뷸리 캐슬》호로 출항했음을 증명했습니다."]], [["On receiving this intelligence, Sergeant Cuff caused the authorities at Bombay to be communicated with, overland—so that the vessel might be boarded by the police immediately on her entering the port.", "이 정보를 받고, 커프 부장은 봄베이의 당국이 육로로 연락되게 했습니다—그 배가 항구에 들어서는 즉시 경찰에 의해 그 배에 오름이 있을 수 있도록."], ["This step having been taken, my connection with the matter came to an end.", "이 조치가 취해졌으니, 그 일과 저의 연은 끝에 이르렀습니다."], ["I have heard nothing more of it since that time.", "저는 그때 이래 그것에 대해 더는 아무것도 듣지 못했습니다."]], [["I am requested by Sergeant Cuff to set in writing certain facts, concerning three men (believed to be Hindoos) who were passengers, last summer, in the ship Bewley Castle, bound for Bombay direct, under my command.", "저는 커프 부장에게, 지난여름, 제 지휘 아래 봄베이로 곧장 가는 배 《뷸리 캐슬》호의 승객이던, (힌두인으로 여겨지는) 세 사내에 관한 어떤 사실들을 글로 적으라는 청을 받았습니다."]], [["The Hindoos joined us at Plymouth.", "그 힌두인들은 플리머스에서 우리와 합류했습니다."], ["On the passage out I heard no complaint of their conduct.", "나가는 항해에서 저는 그들의 처신에 대한 아무 불평도 듣지 못했습니다."], ["They were berthed in the forward part of the vessel.", "그들은 그 배의 앞쪽에 침상을 잡았습니다."], ["I had but few occasions myself of personally noticing them.", "저 자신은 그들을 몸소 눈여겨볼 계제가 몇 번밖에 없었습니다."]], [["In the latter part of the voyage, we had the misfortune to be becalmed for three days and nights, off the coast of India.", "그 항해의 후반부에, 우리는 인도 해안 앞바다에서 사흘 낮과 밤 동안 바람이 자는 불운을 겪었습니다."], ["I have not got the ship’s journal to refer to, and I cannot now call to mind the latitude and longitude.", "저는 참조할 배의 일지를 가지고 있지 않고, 저는 이제 그 위도와 경도를 떠올릴 수 없습니다."], ["As to our position, therefore, I am only able to state generally that the currents drifted us in towards the land, and that when the wind found us again, we reached our port in twenty-four hours afterwards.", "따라서, 우리의 위치에 대해서는, 저는 다만, 조류가 우리를 뭍 쪽으로 떠밀었다는 것과, 바람이 우리를 다시 찾았을 때, 우리가 그 뒤 스물네 시간 만에 우리 항구에 다다랐다는 것만 대략 진술할 수 있습니다."]], [["The discipline of a ship (as all seafaring persons know) becomes relaxed in a long calm.", "배의 규율은 (온갖 뱃사람이 알듯) 긴 무풍 속에서 느슨해집니다."], ["The discipline of my ship became relaxed.", "제 배의 규율이 느슨해졌습니다."], ["Certain gentlemen among the passengers got some of the smaller boats lowered, and amused themselves by rowing about, and swimming, when the sun at evening time was cool enough to let them divert themselves in that way.", "승객 가운데 어떤 신사들이 그 더 작은 배 몇 척을 내리게 하여, 저녁때 해가 그런 식으로 스스로를 즐기게 할 만큼 서늘할 때, 노 저어 돌아다니고, 헤엄치며, 스스로를 즐겼습니다."], ["The boats when done with ought to have been slung up again in their places.", "그 배들은 볼일이 끝나면 그 자리에 다시 달아 올려져야 했습니다."], ["Instead of this they were left moored to the ship’s side.", "이 대신에 그것들은 그 배의 옆구리에 매인 채로 두어졌습니다."], ["What with the heat, and what with the vexation of the weather, neither officers nor men seemed to be in heart for their duty while the calm lasted.", "더위 탓인지, 날씨의 짜증 탓인지, 그 무풍이 이어지는 동안 관원도 선원도 그들의 의무에 마음이 없는 듯했습니다."]], [["On the third night, nothing unusual was heard or seen by the watch on deck.", "셋째 밤에, 갑판의 당직이 이상한 아무것도 듣거나 보지 못했습니다."], ["When the morning came, the smallest of the boats was missing—and the three Hindoos were next reported to be missing, too.", "아침이 오자, 그 배들 중 가장 작은 것이 사라졌습니다—그리고 다음으로 그 세 힌두인도 사라졌다고 보고되었습니다."]], [["If these men had stolen the boat shortly after dark (which I have no doubt they did), we were near enough to the land to make it vain to send in pursuit of them, when the discovery was made in the morning.", "만일 이 사내들이 어둠이 진 직후에 그 배를 훔쳤다면 (저는 그들이 그리했음을 조금도 의심치 않습니다), 우리는 아침에 그 발견이 이루어졌을 때 그들을 뒤쫓아 보내는 것이 헛되게 할 만큼 뭍에 가까이 있었습니다."], ["I have no doubt they got ashore, in that calm weather (making all due allowance for fatigue and clumsy rowing), before day-break.", "저는 그들이, 그 바람 잔 날씨에 (피로와 서투른 노 젓기를 온갖 마땅히 감안하더라도), 동트기 전에 뭍에 올랐음을 조금도 의심치 않습니다."]], [["On reaching our port, I there learnt, for the first time, the reason these passengers had for seizing their opportunity of escaping from the ship.", "우리 항구에 다다라, 저는 거기서, 처음으로, 이 승객들이 그 배에서 달아날 기회를 붙잡은 이유를 알게 되었습니다."], ["I could only make the same statement to the authorities which I have made here.", "저는 당국에, 제가 여기서 한 것과 같은 진술만 할 수 있었습니다."], ["They considered me to blame for allowing the discipline of the vessel to be relaxed.", "그들은 그 배의 규율이 느슨해지도록 둔 데에 저를 탓받을 만하다고 여겼습니다."], ["I have expressed my regret on this score to them, and to my owners.", "저는 이 점에 대한 저의 유감을 그들에게, 그리고 저의 선주들에게 나타냈습니다."]], [["Since that time, nothing has been heard to my knowledge of the three Hindoos.", "그때 이래, 제가 아는 한 그 세 힌두인에 대해 아무것도 들리지 않았습니다."], ["I have no more to add to what is here written.", "저는 여기 적힌 것에 더 보탤 것이 없습니다."]], [["(In a letter to Mr. Bruff.)", "(브러프 씨에게 보내는 편지에서.)"]], [["Have you any recollection, my dear sir, of a semi-savage person whom you met out at dinner, in London, in the autumn of ’forty-eight?", "제 친애하는 선생, 당신은 사십팔 년 가을에 런던에서 만찬에서 만난 어떤 반쯤 야만인 같은 사람을 기억하십니까?"], ["Permit me to remind you that the person’s name was Murthwaite, and that you and he had a long conversation together after dinner.", "그 사람의 이름이 머스웨이트였고, 당신과 그가 저녁 뒤에 함께 긴 대화를 나눴음을 당신께 일깨워 드리는 것을 허락해 주십시오."], ["The talk related to an Indian Diamond, called the Moonstone, and to a conspiracy then in existence to get possession of the gem.", "그 이야기는 월장석이라 불리는 인도 다이아몬드에 관한 것과, 그때 그 보석을 손에 넣으려 존재하던 음모에 관한 것이었습니다."]], [["Since that time, I have been wandering in Central Asia.", "그때 이래, 저는 중앙아시아를 떠돌았습니다."], ["Thence I have drifted back to the scene of some of my past adventures in the north and north-west of India.", "거기서 저는 인도 북부와 북서부의 저의 지난 모험 몇몇의 무대로 도로 흘러들었습니다."], ["About a fortnight since, I found myself in a certain district or province (but little known to Europeans) called Kattiawar.", "두 주쯤 전에, 저는 카티아와르라 불리는 (허나 유럽인에게는 거의 알려지지 않은) 어떤 지역, 곧 주(州)에 있는 저 자신을 보았습니다."]], [["Here an adventure befell me, in which (incredible as it may appear) you are personally interested.", "여기서 저에게, 그 안에서 (믿기 어려워 보일지 몰라도) 당신이 몸소 관련된 모험이 닥쳤습니다."]], [["In the wild regions of Kattiawar (and how wild they are, you will understand, when I tell you that even the husbandmen plough the land, armed to the teeth), the population is fanatically devoted to the old Hindoo religion—to the ancient worship of Bramah and Vishnu.", "카티아와르의 그 거친 지역에서 (그리고 그것들이 얼마나 거친지는, 심지어 농사꾼조차 이를 데까지 무장하고 땅을 간다고 제가 당신께 말하면, 당신이 이해하실 것입니다), 그 주민은 광신적으로 그 옛 힌두 종교에—브라마와 비슈누의 그 오랜 숭배에 바쳐져 있습니다."], ["The few Mahometan families, thinly scattered about the villages in the interior, are afraid to taste meat of any kind.", "그 내지의 마을들에 드문드문 흩어진 몇 안 되는 이슬람교도 집안은, 어떤 종류의 고기도 맛보기를 두려워합니다."], ["A Mahometan even suspected of killing that sacred animal, the cow, is, as a matter of course, put to death without mercy in these parts by the pious Hindoo neighbours who surround him.", "그 신성한 짐승인 소를 죽였다는 의심을 받기만 해도, 이슬람교도는, 당연한 일로, 이 지역에서 그를 둘러싼 독실한 힌두 이웃들에 의해 인정사정없이 죽임을 당합니다."], ["To strengthen the religious enthusiasm of the people, two of the most famous shrines of Hindoo pilgrimage are contained within the boundaries of Kattiawar.", "그 사람들의 종교적 열정을 굳히려, 힌두 순례의 가장 이름난 성소 둘이 카티아와르의 경계 안에 담겨 있습니다."], ["One of them is Dwarka, the birthplace of the god Krishna.", "그중 하나는 드와르카로, 신 크리슈나의 태어난 곳입니다."], ["The other is the sacred city of Somnauth—sacked, and destroyed as long since as the eleventh century, by the Mahometan conqueror, Mahmoud of Ghizni.", "다른 하나는 솜나트의 신성한 도시로—일찍이 십일 세기에, 이슬람 정복자 가즈니의 마무드에 의해 노략질되고 파괴되었습니다."]], [["Finding myself, for the second time, in these romantic regions, I resolved not to leave Kattiawar, without looking once more on the magnificent desolation of Somnauth.", "두 번째로, 이 낭만적인 지역에 있는 저 자신을 보고, 저는 솜나트의 그 장엄한 황폐를 한 번 더 보지 않고서는 카티아와르를 떠나지 않기로 마음먹었습니다."], ["At the place where I planned to do this, I was (as nearly as I could calculate it) some three days distant, journeying on foot, from the sacred city.", "제가 이것을 하려 계획한 곳에서, 저는 (제가 헤아릴 수 있는 한 가까이) 그 신성한 도시에서, 걸어서, 사흘쯤 떨어져 있었습니다."]], [["I had not been long on the road, before I noticed that other people—by twos and threes—appeared to be travelling in the same direction as myself.", "제가 그 길에 오른 지 오래지 않아, 저는 다른 사람들이—둘씩 셋씩—저와 같은 방향으로 여행하는 듯한 것을 알아챘습니다."]], [["To such of these as spoke to me, I gave myself out as a Hindoo-Boodhist, from a distant province, bound on a pilgrimage.", "저에게 말을 건 이들에게는, 저는 저를 먼 주에서 온, 순례길에 오른 힌두-불교도로 내세웠습니다."], ["It is needless to say that my dress was of the sort to carry out this description.", "저의 옷차림이 이 서술을 뒷받침할 부류의 것이었음은 굳이 말할 것도 없습니다."], ["Add, that I know the language as well as I know my own, and that I am lean enough and brown enough to make it no easy matter to detect my European origin—and you will understand that I passed muster with the people readily: not as one of themselves, but as a stranger from a distant part of their own country.", "여기에다, 제가 그 말을 제 것만큼 잘 알고, 제가 저의 유럽 태생을 알아채는 것을 쉽지 않은 일로 만들 만큼 여위고 볕에 그을렸다는 것을 더하면—당신은 제가 그 사람들에게 어렵잖게 무사히 통했음을 이해하실 것입니다. 그들 중 하나로서가 아니라, 그들 자신의 나라의 먼 지역에서 온 낯선 이로서."]], [["On the second day, the number of Hindoos travelling in my direction had increased to fifties and hundreds.", "둘째 날에, 저의 방향으로 여행하는 힌두인의 수가 쉰씩 백씩으로 불었습니다."], ["On the third day, the throng had swollen to thousands; all slowly converging to one point—the city of Somnauth.", "셋째 날에, 그 무리가 수천으로 불어났습니다. 죄 천천히 한 지점—솜나트의 도시로 모여들며."]], [["A trifling service which I was able to render to one of my fellow-pilgrims, during the third day’s journey, proved the means of introducing me to certain Hindoos of the higher caste.", "셋째 날의 여정 동안, 제가 제 동료 순례자 하나에게 베풀 수 있었던 하찮은 도움이, 저를 어떤 높은 계급의 힌두인들에게 소개하는 수단으로 드러났습니다."], ["From these men I learnt that the multitude was on its way to a great religious ceremony, which was to take place on a hill at a little distance from Somnauth.", "이 사람들에게서 저는 그 무리가 솜나트에서 조금 떨어진 언덕에서 열리기로 되어 있던 큰 종교 의식으로 가는 길임을 알게 되었습니다."], ["The ceremony was in honour of the god of the Moon; and it was to be held at night.", "그 의식은 달의 신을 기려서였고, 그것은 밤에 열리기로 되어 있었습니다."]], [["The crowd detained us as we drew near to the place of celebration.", "우리가 그 거행의 자리에 가까이 다가가면서 그 무리가 우리를 붙들었습니다."], ["By the time we reached the hill the moon was high in the heaven.", "우리가 그 언덕에 다다랐을 무렵 그 달이 하늘에 높이 떠 있었습니다."], ["My Hindoo friends possessed some special privileges which enabled them to gain access to the shrine.", "저의 힌두 벗들은 그 성소에 다다를 수 있게 하는 어떤 각별한 특권을 지니고 있었습니다."], ["They kindly allowed me to accompany them.", "그들은 친절히 저를 동행하게 해 주었습니다."], ["When we arrived at the place, we found the shrine hidden from our view by a curtain hung between two magnificent trees.", "우리가 그 자리에 다다랐을 때, 우리는 그 성소가 두 그루의 장엄한 나무 사이에 걸린 커튼에 우리 눈에서 가려진 것을 보았습니다."], ["Beneath the trees a flat projection of rock jutted out, and formed a species of natural platform.", "그 나무들 아래에 바위의 평평한 돌출부가 튀어나와, 일종의 자연스러운 단(壇)을 이루었습니다."], ["Below this, I stood, in company with my Hindoo friends.", "이 아래에, 저는, 저의 힌두 벗들과 함께 섰습니다."]], [["Looking back down the hill, the view presented the grandest spectacle of Nature and Man, in combination, that I have ever seen.", "그 언덕 아래를 되돌아보니, 그 경치는 제가 여태 본, 자연과 사람이 어우러진 가장 웅장한 광경을 내놓았습니다."], ["The lower slopes of the eminence melted imperceptibly into a grassy plain, the place of the meeting of three rivers.", "그 높은 곳의 낮은 비탈이 어느새 풀밭의 평야로 녹아들었는데, 세 강이 만나는 곳이었습니다."], ["On one side, the graceful winding of the waters stretched away, now visible, now hidden by trees, as far as the eye could see.", "한쪽으로, 그 물의 우아한 굽이침이, 이제는 보이고, 이제는 나무들에 가려지며, 눈이 볼 수 있는 데까지 뻗어 있었습니다."], ["On the other, the waveless ocean slept in the calm of the night.", "다른 쪽으로, 물결 없는 대양이 그 밤의 고요 속에 잠들어 있었습니다."], ["People this lovely scene with tens of thousands of human creatures, all dressed in white, stretching down the sides of the hill, overflowing into the plain, and fringing the nearer banks of the winding rivers.", "이 사랑스러운 장면을, 죄 흰 옷을 입고, 그 언덕의 옆을 따라 뻗어 내리고, 그 평야로 넘쳐흐르고, 그 굽이치는 강의 더 가까운 기슭을 두른, 수만의 인간 존재로 채우십시오."], ["Light this halt of the pilgrims by the wild red flames of cressets and torches, streaming up at intervals from every part of the innumerable throng.", "순례자들의 이 멈춤을, 그 헤아릴 수 없는 무리의 온갖 부분에서 이따금 솟구쳐 오르는, 화톳불과 횃불의 거친 붉은 불꽃으로 밝히십시오."], ["Imagine the moonlight of the East, pouring in unclouded glory over all—and you will form some idea of the view that met me when I looked forth from the summit of the hill.", "그 위에 온통, 구름 한 점 없는 영광으로 쏟아지는 동방의 달빛을 상상하십시오—그러면 당신은 제가 그 언덕 꼭대기에서 앞을 내다보았을 때 저를 맞은 그 경치에 대한 얼마쯤의 생각을 그리게 될 것입니다."]], [["A strain of plaintive music, played on stringed instruments and flutes, recalled my attention to the hidden shrine.", "현악기와 피리로 연주된 애처로운 가락 한 줄기가, 저의 주의를 그 가려진 성소로 도로 불렀습니다."]], [["I turned, and saw on the rocky platform the figures of three men.", "저는 돌아서서, 그 바위 단 위에서 세 사내의 모습을 보았습니다."], ["In the central figure of the three I recognised the man to whom I had spoken in England, when the Indians appeared on the terrace at Lady Verinder’s house.", "그 셋 중 가운데 모습에서 저는, 그 인도인들이 레이디 버린더의 집의 테라스에 나타났을 때, 잉글랜드에서 제가 이야기한 그 사내를 알아보았습니다."], ["The other two who had been his companions on that occasion were no doubt his companions also on this.", "그때 그의 동무였던 다른 둘도 두말할 것 없이 이번에도 그의 동무였습니다."]], [["One of the spectators, near whom I was standing, saw me start.", "제가 곁에 서 있던 구경꾼 하나가 제가 소스라치는 것을 보았습니다."], ["In a whisper, he explained to me the apparition of the three figures on the platform of rock.", "속삭임으로, 그는 저에게 그 바위 단 위의 세 모습의 나타남을 풀이해 주었습니다."]], [["They were Brahmins (he said) who had forfeited their caste in the service of the god.", "그들은 (그가 말하기로) 그 신을 섬기다 그들의 계급을 잃은 브라만들이었습니다."], ["The god had commanded that their purification should be the purification by pilgrimage.", "그 신은 그들의 정화가 순례에 의한 정화여야 한다고 명했습니다."], ["On that night, the three men were to part.", "그 밤에, 그 세 사내는 헤어지기로 되어 있었습니다."], ["In three separate directions, they were to set forth as pilgrims to the shrines of India.", "세 갈래의 다른 방향으로, 그들은 인도의 성소들로 가는 순례자로 길을 나서기로 되어 있었습니다."], ["Never more were they to look on each other’s faces.", "다시는 그들은 서로의 얼굴을 볼 수 없었습니다."], ["Never more were they to rest on their wanderings, from the day which witnessed their separation, to the day which witnessed their death.", "다시는 그들은 그들의 헤어짐을 지켜본 그날로부터, 그들의 죽음을 지켜볼 그날까지, 그들의 떠돎에서 쉴 수 없었습니다."]], [["As those words were whispered to me, the plaintive music ceased.", "그 말들이 저에게 속삭여지자, 그 애처로운 가락이 그쳤습니다."], ["The three men prostrated themselves on the rock, before the curtain which hid the shrine.", "그 세 사내는, 그 성소를 가린 그 커튼 앞에서, 그 바위에 엎드렸습니다."], ["They rose—they looked on one another—they embraced.", "그들은 일어섰습니다—그들은 서로를 보았습니다—그들은 끌어안았습니다."], ["Then they descended separately among the people.", "그런 다음 그들은 그 사람들 사이로 따로따로 내려갔습니다."], ["The people made way for them in dead silence.", "그 사람들이 죽은 듯한 침묵 속에 그들에게 길을 내주었습니다."], ["In three different directions I saw the crowd part, at one and the same moment.", "세 갈래의 다른 방향으로 저는 그 무리가, 하나의 같은 순간에 갈라지는 것을 보았습니다."], ["Slowly the grand white mass of the people closed together again.", "천천히 그 사람들의 웅장한 흰 덩어리가 다시 함께 닫혔습니다."], ["The track of the doomed men through the ranks of their fellow mortals was obliterated.", "그 죽음이 정해진 사내들의, 그들의 동료 인간의 대열을 지나는 그 자취가 지워졌습니다."], ["We saw them no more.", "우리는 그들을 다시 보지 못했습니다."]], [["A new strain of music, loud and jubilant, rose from the hidden shrine.", "크고 환희에 찬 새로운 가락이, 그 가려진 성소에서 솟아올랐습니다."], ["The crowd around me shuddered, and pressed together.", "제 둘레의 무리가 몸서리치며, 함께 밀착했습니다."]], [["The curtain between the trees was drawn aside, and the shrine was disclosed to view.", "그 나무들 사이의 그 커튼이 옆으로 젖혀졌고, 그 성소가 눈에 드러났습니다."]], [["There, raised high on a throne—seated on his typical antelope, with his four arms stretching towards the four corners of the earth—there, soared above us, dark and awful in the mystic light of heaven, the god of the Moon.", "거기, 왕좌 위에 높이 올려져—그의 상징인 영양 위에 앉아, 그의 네 팔을 땅의 네 귀퉁이를 향해 뻗은 채—거기, 하늘의 그 신비로운 빛 속에 어둡고 무섭게, 우리 위로 솟아오른, 달의 신이 있었습니다."], ["And there, in the forehead of the deity, gleamed the yellow Diamond, whose splendour had last shone on me in England, from the bosom of a woman’s dress!", "그리고 거기, 그 신의 이마에, 그 광채가 잉글랜드에서, 한 여인의 드레스 가슴께에서, 저에게 마지막으로 빛났던 그 노란 다이아몬드가 어른거렸습니다!"]], [["Yes!", "그렇습니다!"], ["after the lapse of eight centuries, the Moonstone looks forth once more, over the walls of the sacred city in which its story first began.", "여덟 세기가 흐른 뒤, 그 월장석이, 그 이야기가 처음 시작된 그 신성한 도시의 벽 너머로, 다시 한 번 앞을 내다봅니다."], ["How it has found its way back to its wild native land—by what accident, or by what crime, the Indians regained possession of their sacred gem, may be in your knowledge, but is not in mine.", "그것이 어떻게 그 거친 고향 땅으로 도로 돌아가는 길을 찾았는지—어떤 우연으로, 또는 어떤 범죄로, 인도인들이 그들의 신성한 보석을 도로 손에 넣었는지는, 당신의 앎에는 있을지 몰라도, 저의 앎에는 없습니다."], ["You have lost sight of it in England, and (if I know anything of this people) you have lost sight of it for ever.", "당신은 잉글랜드에서 그것을 시야에서 잃었고, (제가 이 사람들에 대해 무언가 안다면) 당신은 그것을 영영 시야에서 잃었습니다."]], [["So the years pass, and repeat each other; so the same events revolve in the cycles of time.", "그리하여 세월이 지나고, 서로를 되풀이합니다. 그리하여 같은 사건들이 시간의 순환 속에 돌아옵니다."], ["What will be the next adventures of the Moonstone?", "그 월장석의 다음 모험은 무엇일까요?"], ["Who can tell?", "누가 알 수 있으랴?"]]];
